{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Gittin 2\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Intro to Gittin\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Masechet Overview\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 We begin a new {\i masechet}: Masechet Gittin. It is, in the fullest sense of the phrase, a classic {\i masechet} of the {\i gemara}. Unlike some of the more unusual tractates encountered elsewhere in the cycle \u8212- with non-standard language, absent {\i Rashi}, or unconventional structure \u8212- Gittin belongs to the heart of the {\i Shas} curriculum. Situated within {\i Nashim} and adjacent in spirit to {\i Nezikin}, it carries the full wealth of the {\i rishonim} and has been a staple of {\i yeshiva} education for centuries. And the reason it has remained so central is not merely because it is {\i halacha l\u8217'maaseh} \u8212- though of course the laws of delivering a {\i get} are of great practical consequence \u8212- but because it is extraordinarily rich in conceptual depth.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Conceptual Themes\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Masechet Gittin is packed with some of the deepest and most far-reaching themes in all of {\i Shas}. The laws of {\i shlichus} \u8212- agency \u8212- are explored here at length and in their most complex forms. So too are the laws of {\i shtarot}, legal documents, as well as questions of ownership, and the structure of conditions placed upon transactions. These are not parochial concerns; they are recurring themes throughout {\i Shas}, and Gittin becomes one of the primary sources to which the tradition returns again and again when working through them. It is a {\i masechet} with enormous conceptual reach.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Divorce Context\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 There is, however, something almost melancholy about learning Masechet Gittin in the contemporary context. The {\i gemara}, like any legal document, focuses entirely on process: how does one write a {\i get}, how does one deliver it, what are the technical and conceptual requirements? And throughout, the social reality is simply taken for granted \u8212- of course a husband is giving a {\i get} to his wife. The complications are halachic, not social. Every page assumes that divorce is a natural and available option, and the legal analysis proceeds accordingly.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This stands in sharp contrast to the reality many Jewish women face today, where the process of obtaining a {\i get} is fraught with the possibility of extortion, refusal, and the tragedy of {\i iguna}. Part of what made the system more functional in Talmudic times was the absence of a parallel secular legal system: if you wanted to end a marriage, this was the only path. Just as today one cannot avoid obtaining a civil divorce if one wants legal dissolution, in that world a {\i get} was the sole mechanism. The tight-knit Jewish community, combined with a {\i beis din} that had real authority and the motivation to exercise it, created conditions in which the husband\u8217's cooperation was far more reliably obtained. That said, it is worth noting that in much of Western Europe and America, secular divorce was itself essentially unavailable until well into the twentieth century, owing in large part to Christian legal traditions. The contrast with the Talmudic system is therefore even more pointed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Perakim Structure\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Masechet Gittin spans six {\i perakim} and does something characteristic of several {\i masechtot}: rather than beginning with the most fundamental questions \u8212- how does one write a {\i get}, what constitutes a valid {\i get} \u8212- it opens with what might appear to be a more peripheral topic. The first {\i perek} deals with {\i hameivi get mimedinat hayam}, the agent who delivers a {\i get} from overseas, and the testimony he must provide. The second {\i perek} continues this theme under the same heading, still concerned with the delivery of a {\i get} from afar \u8212- but midway through, it begins to address the specifics of how a {\i get} is written. We encounter {\i hakol kotvin}, the principle that one may write with all types of inks; the question of a {\i get} written by day and signed at night; what material the {\i get} may be written upon; and the rule that {\i ein kotvin bimchubar lakarka} \u8212- one may not write on something still attached to the ground.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is also in this section that we encounter {\i lishmah} \u8212- the requirement that the {\i get} be written with the particular woman in mind. The third {\i perek} continues with questions relating both to the writing and delivery of the {\i get}, including the fascinating question of whether, while the {\i get} is being delivered, we may assume the husband who sent it is still alive. This opens into a broader digression: in general, when a {\i shaliach} has been dispatched to perform an act, what assumptions may he make about the one who sent him?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The fourth {\i perek}, called {\i ha-sholeach}, deals with a scenario of acute halachic danger: a husband who dispatches a {\i shaliach} to deliver a {\i get} to his wife, then subsequently cancels the agency \u8212- but does so not in the presence of the messenger. The messenger proceeds in good faith, the wife believes herself divorced, she remarries, and the children of that remarriage may be {\i mamzerim}. To prevent this catastrophe, the Rabbis intervened \u8212- and here we encounter one of the most celebrated concepts in the {\i masechet}: {\i tikkun haolam}. The Rabbis instituted certain enactments, and in some cases shaped or worked around biblical law, for the betterment of society. The fourth and fifth {\i perakim} are largely devoted to this theme \u8212- a wide-ranging survey, touching areas from {\i nashim} to {\i nezikin}, of things the Rabbis did {\i mipnei tikkun haolam}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The sixth {\i perek} returns to the laws of giving a {\i get}, including the case of a {\i get} given conditionally \u8212- a topic that might sound like a technical curiosity, but which unfolds into a profound analysis of what constitutes a legally valid {\i tnai}. Toward the end of the {\i masechet} we reach the laws of how the woman receives the {\i get} and what constitutes valid receipt. And at the very end, in the final {\i mishnah}, we arrive at what might surprise a reader who has followed the tractate from the beginning: a discussion of under what circumstances a husband should divorce his wife in the first place. It is a rich and diverse {\i masechet} in every sense.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rambam\u8217's 10 Conditions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Before turning to the {\i daf} itself \u8212- which is notably short, with very few lines of {\i gemara} \u8212- it is worth pausing to appreciate a brilliant piece of systematic jurisprudence from the Rambam. His {\i Hilchos Gerushin}, particularly {\i perek} 1, offers a stunning overview of the entire subject. He opens by enumerating ten conditions, all {\i d\u8217'oraisa}, that must be met for a {\i get} to be valid. He then reveals the source: virtually all of them are derived from a single {\i pasuk} in the Torah, specifically from eight words.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The verse is from Devarim: {\i ve-kasav la sefer kerisus ve-nasan beyada ve-shilcha mibeiso} \u8212- \u8220"and he shall write for her a document of severance, and place it in her hand, and send her from his house.\u8221" These eight words, combined with the general principle from {\i al pi shenayim eidim yakum davar}, underpin the entire architecture of {\i geirushin} from the Torah\u8217's perspective.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Conditions Explained\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Rambam proceeds to derive each condition in turn. First, from {\i im lo timtza chen be\u8217'einav} \u8212- \u8220"if she does not find favor in his eyes\u8221" \u8212- we learn that the husband must act willingly: {\i melamed she-eino megaresh ela birtzono}. This distinguishes {\i gerushin} from ordinary transactions, where of course intent is always required; here the requirement of genuine consent carries particular weight. Importantly, the woman is divorced even against her will \u8212- {\i ha-isha mitgereshet birtzonah ve-she-lo birtzonah} \u8212- which is of course the pre-Rabbinic baseline, before the {\i cherem} of Rabbeinu Gershom.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Second, from {\i ve-kasav} \u8212- and he shall write \u8212- we learn that the {\i get} must be a written document: {\i eina mitgereshet ela bichtav}. Third, from {\i la} \u8212- for her \u8212- we derive {\i lishmah}: it must be written specifically with this woman in mind. A generic writ or one written without intention toward the particular woman does not suffice. Fourth, from {\i sefer kerisus} \u8212- a document of severance \u8212- we learn that the {\i get} must fully sever the relationship: {\i davar ha-koret beino u-veinah, she-lo yisha\u8217'er alav rashut}. If the husband appends an ongoing stipulation \u8212- some condition that leaves him with continued authority over her \u8212- the act is not one of complete severance. Fifth, from {\i ve-nasan beyada} \u8212- and he shall place it in her hand \u8212- we learn the obvious: the {\i get} must reach her, whether into her hand, into the hands of her {\i shaliach}, or into her property.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Sixth, from {\i ve-shilcha} \u8212- and he shall send her \u8212- the Rambam derives that the language of the {\i get} must be that {\i she} is being sent, not that {\i he} is leaving. The husband cannot formulate the {\i get} in terms of his own departure; the document must speak of her being divorced from him. Nor can the text be merely a declaration of freedom without reference to the act of divorce itself.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Seventh \u8212- and here the Rambam draws on the combination of {\i ve-kasav} and {\i ve-nasan} together \u8212- there must be no necessary intervening act between the writing and the giving. One may of course allow time to elapse, but there cannot be a situation in which some action is {\i required} between the writing and the delivery. The classic example is {\i mechubar lakarka}: one cannot write on a tree still attached to the ground, because the only way to give it would necessitate cutting the tree down first.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Eighth, from the combination of {\i sefer kerisus ve-natan beyada}, the Rambam derives that the {\i get} must be given explicitly as a {\i get}. One cannot hand a woman a piece of paper under false pretenses \u8212- \u8220"hold this for me\u8221" \u8212- and have that count as delivery of a {\i get}. It must be given as what it is.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The ninth condition \u8212- that there must be {\i eidim}, witnesses \u8212- is the one condition not derived from this {\i pasuk}, but rather from the general Torah principle of {\i al pi shenayim eidim yakum davar}. Whether this means witnesses must sign the document or witnesses must be present at the time of delivery is itself a dispute in the {\i gemara}. Tenth \u8212- and the Rambam closes the list here \u8212- the {\i get} must come from the husband himself, not from a court acting independently.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The elegance of this framework is remarkable: eight words of Torah, one additional {\i pasuk} about testimony, and the Rambam has mapped the entire {\i d\u8217'oraisa} architecture of {\i geirushin}. It is an ideal introduction to the {\i masechet}, and it is worth reading his {\i perek} 1 of {\i Hilchos Gerushin} in full before proceeding through the {\i daf yomi}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Hameivi Get\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Basic Requirement\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} opens: {\i hamevi get mimedinat hayam} \u8212- one who brings a {\i get} from overseas. {\i Tosafos} immediately raises a point about this phrase. {\i Rashi} understands {\i medinat hayam} to mean simply {\i kol chutz la\u8217'aretz} \u8212- anywhere outside of {\i Eretz Yisrael}. {\i Tosafos} disagrees, arguing that if that were the intent, the {\i mishnah} would have said {\i hamevi get michutz la\u8217'aretz}. The use of the phrase {\i medinat hayam} suggests something more distant \u8212- a genuinely far-off place \u8212- and we will return to this distinction shortly.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} states: {\i tzarich she-yomar befanai nechtav uvfanai nechtam} \u8212- the agent delivering the {\i get} must declare, in front of witnesses or a {\i beis din}, \u8220"it was written in my presence and signed in my presence.\u8221" The most natural question is: why? We have a {\i shaliach} standing before us, holding a {\i get}. We do not know who he is. We do not know who sent him. We have no way of verifying that the husband actually authorized this document, that the signatures on it are genuine, or that the {\i get} was written in accordance with {\i halacha}. The husband is not standing in front of us; the {\i shaliach} has not provided any prior affidavit of his agency. In that situation, the {\i shaliach} must testify \u8212- and his testimony that the {\i get} was written and signed in his presence serves as validation of the entire process. The {\i gemara} will go on to distinguish between the {\i nechtav} component and the {\i nechtam} component, attributing to each a different rationale. But at the {\i mishnah} level, both together constitute the required declaration.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Geographic Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} then records a series of positions among the {\i tannaim} about what geographic distance triggers this requirement. {\i Rabban Gamliel} says: {\i af hamevi mi-Rekem umehager} \u8212- even one who brings from Rekem and Hager must make this declaration. These appear to be places very close to the border of {\i Eretz Yisrael}. {\i Tosafos} devotes considerable effort to identifying them, suggesting they may be in the region of the land of the {\i Plishtim}, though acknowledges some ambiguity. {\i Rashi} associates them with the biblical Kadesh and Bered, the location to which Hagar fled when driven out by Sarah \u8212- again pointing to proximity to {\i Eretz Yisrael}. {\i Rabban Gamliel}\u8217's position, then, is that even these nearby locations trigger the requirement; one need not travel to genuinely distant overseas territories.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Eliezer} goes further: {\i afilu mikfar Ludim le-Lud} \u8212- even one who brings from the village of Ludim to Lud. This generates two puzzles. First, Lud is not on the border of {\i Eretz Yisrael} in any straightforward sense; why would its immediate vicinity even be considered {\i chutz la\u8217'aretz}? Second, and more textually puzzling, {\i Tosafos} asks: why does {\i Rabbi Eliezer} state where he is {\i bringing it to}? The point of these {\i tannaitic} positions is to define the place of {\i origin} \u8212- it doesn\u8217't matter where it is being delivered. {\i Tosafos} offers two resolutions. One is that {\i kfar Ludim} must be understood as so closely adjacent to Lud, so much like its suburb, that one might have assumed the two localities share a legal identity for these purposes \u8212- {\i Rabbi Eliezer} teaches that even so, if it technically falls over the line, the requirement applies. The other answer {\i Tosafos} offers is that {\i Rabbi Eliezer} himself was from Lud, and therefore chose this as his illustrative example.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What all three positions \u8212- {\i Rabban Gamliel}, {\i Rabbi Eliezer}, and the following {\i chachamim} \u8212- share is the underlying claim: the {\i mishnah}\u8217's phrase {\i medinat hayam} need not refer to genuinely distant overseas territory. Even proximity to the border triggers the requirement.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Ve-chachamim omrim}: the {\i chachamim} disagree, saying {\i ela hameivi mimedinat hayam} \u8212- only one who brings from genuinely overseas must make the declaration. The phrase {\i medinat hayam} means what it says: a distant place across the sea.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Eretz Yisrael Borders\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} continues with a separate question: how do we define the borders of {\i Eretz Yisrael} itself? This requires its own treatment, since knowing what is inside {\i Eretz Yisrael} is necessary to knowing when the requirement applies. {\i Rabbi Yehuda} provides the coordinates: from Rekem to the east \u8212- with Rekem counting as {\i outside} Israel; from Ashkelon to the south \u8212- with Ashkelon counting as outside; from Akko to the north \u8212- with Akko counting as outside Israel as well. This is immediately striking: Ashkelon is mentioned as one of the cities of Israel, and Akko is likewise associated with {\i Eretz Yisrael} in numerous other contexts.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Meir} takes a different position with respect to Akko: {\i Akko k\u8217'Eretz Yisrael legittin} \u8212- for purposes of the laws of {\i gittin}, Akko has the status of {\i Eretz Yisrael}. This raises a principle that will recur throughout the early {\i dappim} of the {\i masechet}: the borders of {\i Eretz Yisrael} may not be uniform across all areas of {\i halacha}. The borders relevant to {\i tumah} and {\i ma\u8217'asros} are generally those of the {\i Bayit Sheni} era. The borders associated with the {\i kedushah} of the land track the conquest of Yehoshua. But for the purposes of the {\i halacha} of {\i befanai nechtav uvfanai nechtam}, the relevant question may be more practical: is this a place where Jewish communities are established enough that {\i halachos} are reliably known, and where witnesses can be located if needed? If the concern driving the requirement is practical \u8212- that people far away do not know the laws of writing a {\i get}, or that one cannot find witnesses to validate signatures \u8212- then the borders of {\i Eretz Yisrael} for these purposes are drawn based on the cultural and communal realities of Jewish life, not necessarily on historical or sanctity-based demarcations.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Within Eretz Yisrael\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Finally, the {\i mishnah} closes: {\i hamevi get b\u8217'Eretz Yisrael eino tzarich lomar befanai nechtav uvfanai nechtam} \u8212- one who delivers a {\i get} within {\i Eretz Yisrael} does not need to make this declaration. When the entire transaction takes place internally within {\i Eretz Yisrael}, we have sufficient tools to verify everything, and the declaration is unnecessary. However, {\i v\u8217'im yesh alav orerim} \u8212- if a challenge is subsequently raised, if the husband appears and claims the {\i get} was a forgery \u8212- then {\i yitkayem be-chotmav}: the {\i get} is validated through its signatures. So within {\i Eretz Yisrael}, we do not validate proactively, but if and when a challenge arises, the mechanism of signature validation is available and sufficient.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishnah Structure\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This {\i mishnah} offers a remarkable illustration of a broader phenomenon in the structure of {\i mishnayos} \u8212- one that, once noticed, appears throughout the {\i Shas}. The {\i gemara} sometimes refers explicitly to a {\i mishnah rishona} and a {\i mishnah acharona}, cases where an earlier ruling was later superseded. But there is a more subtle and more common phenomenon: a later generation of {\i tannaim} received a simple, early {\i mishnah} containing basic terms, and then proceeded to debate what those terms meant. The apparent structure of {\i tanna kamma} versus {\i Rabbi Yehuda} versus {\i Rabbi Meir} often conceals what was really a simple original teaching, with the named opinions all being subsequent clarifications or debates about the scope of the original terms.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The phenomenon is visible even in the very first {\i mishnah} of Pesachim: {\i kol makom she-ein machnisin bo chametz ein tzarich bdikah} \u8212- wherever one does not bring {\i chametz}, one need not check. The {\i mishnah} then introduces the teaching about the two rows of the wine cellar ({\i shtei shurot ba-martef}), and {\i Beis Shammai} and {\i Beis Hillel} debate what \u8220"two rows\u8221" means. Clearly, there was an early, simple teaching, and the later {\i tannaim} argued about what the term encompassed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Apply this lens to our {\i mishnah}. Strip away every named position, and what remains is a strikingly elegant, minimal teaching: {\i hamevi get mimedinat hayam} \u8212- one who brings a {\i get} from overseas must say {\i befanai nechtav uvfanai nechtam}; and if one brings it from one {\i medinah} to another in {\i medinat hayam}, or from {\i Eretz Yisrael} to {\i medinat hayam}; but {\i hamevi get b\u8217'Eretz Yisrael eino tzarich}. That\u8217's the whole {\i mishnah} in its original form. Crisp, simple, complete.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Then come the named {\i tannaim}, and what are they doing? They are debating the meaning of the term {\i medinat hayam}. Does it mean genuinely distant overseas territory, or does it mean any place outside {\i Eretz Yisrael} \u8212- even places right at the border? {\i Rabban Gamliel} says even Rekem and Hager. {\i Rabbi Eliezer} says even the vicinity of Lud. {\i Chachamim} say no, only genuinely distant places. {\i Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel} takes the term {\i mimedina limedina} and says it applies even to {\i hegmonia le-hegmonia} \u8212- even two jurisdictions within a single city. And {\i Rabbi Yehuda} and {\i Rabbi Meir} are not adding new positions but simply defining, from different angles, what the borders of {\i Eretz Yisrael} are.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This structural observation enriches the reading considerably. Rather than a cacophony of competing voices, we see a simple early ruling, and a later generation systematically working through every ambiguous term. As one participant in the {\i shiur} observed, this is in a sense the same process that the {\i amoraim} perform explicitly in the {\i gemara} \u8212- with the difference that in the {\i gemara} the process of clarification and debate is transparent, while here it is embedded in the {\i mishnah} itself.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Get Convention\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Two brief textual notes are worth recording here. First, the word {\i get}: {\i Tosafos} points out that it is a generic term for a {\i shtar} \u8212- a legal document \u8212- but that without further qualification, it almost always refers to a {\i get nashim}, a bill of divorce. Why the word {\i get} came to be so closely associated with divorce specifically is not entirely clear. Second, {\i Tosafos} notes a widespread {\i minhag} to write a {\i get} with twelve lines. The basis offered is the {\i gematria} of the word {\i get}: {\i gimel} equals 3, {\i tes} equals 9, totaling twelve. An alternative explanation {\i Tosafos} considers is that between each of the five books of the {\i Sefer Torah}, there is a gap of four lines; the three such gaps between the first four books amount to twelve dividing lines in total. To this day, a {\i get} is written so that the text fills exactly twelve lines.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Border Cities\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Returning to the question of Ashkelon and Akko: {\i Tosafos} acknowledges that both cities are, in various sources, counted as part of {\i Eretz Yisrael}, and that Ashkelon is in the region of {\i Eretz Pelishtim}. Yet, {\i Tosafos} argues, even if these cities are technically within the boundaries of {\i Eretz Yisrael}, they may sit at the periphery in a meaningful sense. If the underlying concern of {\i befanai nechtav uvfanai nechtam} is practical \u8212- that people in certain places do not know the {\i halachos} of {\i gittin} or that witnesses cannot be located \u8212- then cities on the extreme margins of Jewish settlement may not enjoy the presumption of halachic reliability that the interior of the land does. The determination of which side of the line they fall on for purposes of this {\i halacha} is therefore not identical with the question of whether they are technically within the borders of {\i Eretz Yisrael}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This question \u8212- whether we use standard definitions of {\i Eretz Yisrael}\u8217's borders, or whether we draw the map based on the specific concerns underlying the law of {\i befanai nechtav} \u8212- will continue to animate the discussion across the first several {\i dappim} of the {\i masechet}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 No Forgery Defense\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafos} notes in its commentary to {\i im yesh alav orer mitkayem be-chotmav} that as long as the husband has not come forward to challenge the {\i get} \u8212- in the case of a {\i get} delivered within {\i Eretz Yisrael} \u8212- the woman may remarry on the strength of the {\i get} alone. We do not, on her behalf, raise the concern that perhaps the {\i get} is forged. One might have thought the opposite: since the husband is absent from the proceedings, should the {\i beis din} not represent his interests and challenge the document\u8217's authenticity? {\i Tosafos} answers: {\i de-mishum iguna akilu bah rabanan} \u8212- because of the concern for {\i iguna}, the Rabbis were lenient and declined to mount the forgery defense on the husband\u8217's behalf. The woman cannot be left in a state of uncertainty indefinitely because of an unsubstantiated concern.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ramban Property\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Ramban} takes a fundamentally different approach to the same question. He argues that the reason the {\i beis din} does not represent the husband\u8217's interests and challenge the {\i get} is not because of any {\i leniency} born of the concern for {\i iguna} \u8212- it is because the entire framing of \u8220"representing the absent party\u8217's interests\u8221" does not apply here in the first place. In monetary matters, if one party is attempting to seize another\u8217's property and the owner is absent, the court will defend the owner\u8217's interests. But the {\i Ramban} says: {\i ein ha-ishah nichsei ha-ba\u8217'al} \u8212- the woman is not the husband\u8217's property. The act of divorce is not the taking of something from the husband. It is the definition of a woman\u8217's legal status \u8212- a question that lies outside the framework of {\i dinei mamonos} entirely.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is a striking conceptual point to encounter on the very first {\i daf} of the {\i masechet}. The entire tractate is, in some sense, structured around the husband\u8217's power \u8212- he writes, he delivers, he sends. And yet here, at the outset, the {\i Ramban} insists that however we frame the halachic mechanics, the woman is not to be understood as her husband\u8217's property, and that framing must be kept entirely separate from the language of monetary law. As one participant noted, this is in some ways consistent with {\i Tosafos}\u8217's position as well: even {\i Tosafos}, who invokes {\i iguna} as the {\i ta\u8217'am}, still presupposes that the husband is seen as a party who could be \u8220"injured\u8221" by the loss of the {\i get} challenge \u8212- something the {\i Ramban} rejects at the conceptual root.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Two Reasons\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbah Lishmah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} opens with a simple question: {\i mai ta\u8217'ama} \u8212- what is the reason for the requirement of {\i befanai nechtav uvfanai nechtam}? Two {\i amoraim} offer two fundamentally different answers, each focusing on a different word of the declaration.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbah} says: {\i lefi she-ein beki\u8217'in lishmah} \u8212- because people in {\i medinat hayam} are not expert in the requirement of {\i lishmah}, the rule that a {\i get} must be written specifically for the sake of this particular woman. Since we are concerned that the {\i get} was not written with proper {\i lishmah} in mind, the {\i shaliach} must testify: {\i befanai nechtav} \u8212- it was written in my presence. {\i Rabbah} is focused on the writing of the {\i get}, and his concern is about whether the {\i get} was produced in accordance with {\i halacha}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafos} raises the obvious question: why single out {\i lishmah} specifically? There are many other requirements for writing a valid {\i get}. The answer {\i Tosafos} gives is that {\i lishmah} was the most commonly neglected requirement \u8212- perhaps because people in distant lands were not fully persuaded of its necessity, or perhaps because it was simply less well-known. But {\i Tosafos} does note other possible framings, including the possibility that in {\i medinat hayam} the problem was not ignorance so much as non-acceptance: people who knew the law of {\i lishmah} but did not reliably observe it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The practical question then arises: how does the {\i shaliach}\u8217's statement {\i befanai nechtav} \u8212- \u8220"it was written in my presence\u8221" \u8212- actually establish that {\i lishmah} was observed? All he is literally saying is that it was written in front of him. {\i Rashi} offers two possibilities. Either the declaration opens a line of inquiry \u8212- we ask him to elaborate on how the {\i get} was written, and through that inquiry we establish that {\i lishmah} was observed \u8212- or, more elegantly, the statement {\i befanai nechtav} functions as an implicit claim that everything was done in accordance with {\i halacha}, without any need for further questioning. The implicit logic is reinforced by the sociological observation: if people in {\i medinat hayam} know they will have to make this declaration before the {\i get} can be received in {\i Eretz Yisrael}, they will know that they are testifying to {\i halachic} compliance, and will take care accordingly.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava Signatures\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rava} focuses on the other half of the declaration: {\i befanai nechtam} \u8212- it was signed in my presence. {\i Rava}\u8217's concern is not about whether the {\i get} was written correctly, but about the practical problem of signature validation. We see at the end of the {\i mishnah} that a {\i get} delivered in {\i Eretz Yisrael} is subject to challenge: if the husband comes forward and claims the {\i get} is a forgery, the mechanism for resolving that challenge is {\i yitkayem be-chotmav} \u8212- signature validation, either through the witnesses themselves or through others who can identify their signatures.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Within {\i Eretz Yisrael}, this is manageable. One can locate witnesses, summon those who know the signatories, produce corroborating documents. But if the {\i get} was signed in a distant land and the challenge arises years later in {\i Eretz Yisrael}, how will one ever find witnesses to validate those signatures? They are back in {\i medinat hayam}, impossible to summon. {\i Rava} therefore says: we require upfront validation by the {\i shaliach} himself. He testifies at the point of delivery that the {\i get} was signed in his presence \u8212- and that testimony, even from a single witness as we will see, suffices to forestall the need for later signature validation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Nafka Mina Cases\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then asks: {\i mai beinaihu} \u8212- what is the practical difference between these two reasons? And it proceeds to spell out several elegant {\i nafka minos}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 First: {\i de\u8217'asu bei trei} \u8212- two people brought the {\i get}. According to {\i Rava}, who is concerned about the ability to validate signatures at a later date, the presence of two witnesses delivering the {\i get} alleviates the concern. The two {\i messengers} together, by the very act of delivering the {\i get}, implicitly testify that it was legitimately dispatched by the husband; a subsequent forgery claim would be answerable through their testimony. But according to {\i Rabbah}, who is concerned about {\i lishmah}, the presence of two messengers solves nothing. The question of whether the {\i get} was written in accordance with {\i halacha} is entirely independent of how many people delivered it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Second: {\i mimedina limedina b\u8217'Eretz Yisrael} \u8212- from one region to another within {\i Eretz Yisrael}, such as from {\i Yehuda} to {\i Galil}. According to {\i Rabbah}, everyone in {\i Eretz Yisrael} knows the requirement of {\i lishmah}, so there is no concern. But according to {\i Rava}, even within {\i Eretz Yisrael}, if the {\i get} was written in one region and delivered in another, the practicalities of locating witnesses for signature validation across those regions may still be difficult \u8212- one might need the declaration even there.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Third: {\i be-osah medinah b\u8217'medinat hayam} \u8212- within a single country in {\i medinat hayam}. According to {\i Rabbah}, who is concerned about {\i lishmah}, the problem exists even within one country abroad, since the concern is one of halachic knowledge. But according to {\i Rava}, if the {\i get} was written and delivered within the same country overseas, one will be able to locate witnesses for signature validation if needed \u8212- they are all in the same place.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 These {\i nafka minos} are elegant because they isolate the exact axis of disagreement between the two {\i amoraim}: {\i Rabbah}\u8217's concern is halachic competence, while {\i Rava}\u8217's concern is logistical access.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 One Witness\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now asks: according to {\i Rabbah}, who says the concern is that people in {\i medinat hayam} do not know the laws of {\i lishmah}, why does one witness suffice? If we genuinely doubt whether the {\i get} was produced in accordance with {\i halacha} \u8212- if we are uncertain of its {\i kashrus} \u8212- surely we require two witnesses to resolve that doubt, as is the case with all testimony in the Torah.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} answers: {\i ed echad ne\u8217'eman b\u8217'issurin} \u8212- a single witness is trusted in matters of {\i kashrus} status. When the question is not one of conflicting factual claims but of an object\u8217's halachic status, the tradition holds that one witness suffices. {\i Rashi} notes the breadth of this principle: the Torah trusted every individual Jew to testify reliably about the separation of {\i terumah}, about the {\i shechita} of an animal, about the removal of {\i gid ha-nasheh} and {\i chelev}. When you purchase meat from a butcher, you do not require two witnesses to confirm it was properly slaughtered; the butcher\u8217's own representation suffices. As {\i Tosafos} puts it: {\i heicha anu somchin al neshoteinu} \u8212- we rely on our wives when they serve us food they have prepared, when they tell us meat was properly salted, when they report that they have attended the {\i mikvah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Challenge Issued\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} immediately raises a sharp challenge. When we say {\i ed echad ne\u8217'eman b\u8217'issurin}, in what circumstances does that apply? The typical case is like a piece of fat: there is a piece of meat before us, and we do not know whether it is {\i chelev} \u8212- forbidden fat \u8212- or {\i shuman} \u8212- permitted fat. There is no prior known status of the piece, and a single witness can establish which it is. But \u8212- and this is the challenge \u8212- {\i aval hacha de-it-chazak issura d\u8217'eshet ish}: here, the woman was known to be married. She entered the situation with an established status of {\i eshet ish}, a forbidden relationship. We are not in a situation of mere uncertainty; we are in a situation of {\i chazakas issur} \u8212- a presumption of prohibition. Can a single witness overturn that?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Moreover, {\i havei davar she-b\u8217'ervah}: this is a matter of {\i arayos} \u8212- sexual prohibitions \u8212- and for those, the Torah\u8217's standard is {\i ein davar she-b\u8217'ervah pachos mishnayim}, not fewer than two witnesses. The woman\u8217's marital status, and therefore her permissibility to others, is a question of {\i arayos}, and {\i arayos} categorically requires two witnesses.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 So there are, in fact, two independent reasons why one witness should {\i not} suffice here: first, the {\i chazakas issur} of being a married woman; second, the inherent requirement of two witnesses for all matters of {\i arayos}. How, then, does the {\i mishnah} permit the single declaration of the {\i shaliach} to suffice?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Beyado Concept\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafos} introduces an important conceptual tool in response to the first problem. The reason one witness suffices to overturn a {\i chazakas issur} in ordinary {\i issurim} \u8212- the piece of fat, the unsalted meat, the question of {\i shechita} \u8212- is the principle of {\i beyado}: the matter lies within the power of the person testifying to change. A butcher {\i can} shecht the animal. A householder {\i can} salt his own meat. A person {\i can} separate {\i terumah}. Because it was within their power to have done the permissible thing, they are believed when they testify that they did so, even against a background presumption of prohibition.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 By contrast, neither the woman nor the {\i shaliach} can bring about a divorce independently. No matter how much effort or money is applied, unless the husband willingly gives the {\i get}, the woman remains married. The divorce is not {\i beyado} \u8212- not within their power to accomplish. And according to {\i Tosafos}, when the matter is {\i lo beyado} \u8212- not within one\u8217's power to change \u8212- a single witness does not suffice to overcome a {\i chazakas issur}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Unresolved Q\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This leaves us with a compounded difficulty. The {\i gemara}\u8217's challenge runs on two tracks: {\i chazakas issur} combined with {\i lo beyado}, and the categorical requirement of two witnesses for {\i arayos}. Even for those authorities who hold that a single witness {\i can} overcome a {\i chazakas issur} even when the matter is {\i lo beyado}, the second problem remains: this is {\i ervah}, and {\i davar she-b\u8217'ervah} always requires two witnesses. The woman was a {\i eshet ish}, and her permissibility to others is a matter of {\i arayos} by definition.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 So the question stands in full force, and the {\i gemara}\u8217's answer will have to address both dimensions simultaneously. The answer is left as a {\i cliffhanger} \u8212- to be resolved at the opening of the next {\i daf}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Gittin 3\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rabbah vs.\u160 ?Rava\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Core Dispute Intro\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} at the opening of {\i Gittin} requires the {\i shaliach} \u8212- the agent delivering the {\i get} \u8212- to declare {\i bifanai nechtav bifanai nechtam}: \u8220"it was written before me and signed before me.\u8221" The {\i gemara} explores this requirement through the lens of a foundational debate between Rabbah and Rava, two {\i amoraim} whose positions have become a touchstone for understanding the entire tractate.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbah\u8217's Lishma Focus\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbah\u8217's concern is {\i lishma} \u8212- that the {\i get} was written according to {\i halacha}, specifically for the sake of this woman and this divorce. His emphasis falls on {\i bifanai nechtav}, \u8220"it was written before me,\u8221" because the writing is the act that must be performed with the proper intent and in accordance with the relevant laws. Rabbah\u8217's declaration is essentially a halachic certification: I witnessed that this document was executed correctly.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava\u8217's Signature Focus\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava\u8217's concern is different. His focus is on {\i eidim metzuyim lekaymo} \u8212- the practical problem that a {\i get} arriving from a distant place cannot easily have its signatures verified. Witnesses who know the signatories may simply not be available. Rava therefore emphasizes {\i bifanai nechtam}, \u8220"it was signed before me,\u8221" because the {\i shaliach\u8217's} testimony addresses the authenticity of the signatures themselves. It preempts the possibility that the {\i get} will later be challenged as a forgery.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Nafka Minot Examples\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} presents two elegant {\i nafka minas} \u8212- practical differences \u8212- that sharpen the distinction between the two positions. First: a {\i get} delivered from one province to another within {\i chutz l\u8217'aretz}, but within the same jurisdiction. Here there is no difficulty locating witnesses to verify signatures, so Rava\u8217's concern does not apply. But because it is {\i chutz l\u8217'aretz}, there remains a legitimate question whether the scribes and signatories were fully versed in the {\i halachos} of {\i lishma}. According to Rabbah, the declaration is required; according to Rava, it is not. Second: a {\i get} traveling from province to province within {\i Eretz Yisrael}. There, the {\i halachic} proficiency of those who wrote and signed the {\i get} is not in question \u8212- but geographic distance makes verification of signatures difficult. According to Rava, the declaration is required; according to Rabbah, it is not. Two positions, two sharply differentiated {\i nafka minas}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Ed Echad Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 One Witness Problem\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having identified what the {\i shaliach\u8217's} declaration accomplishes, the {\i gemara} turns to a deeper structural question: why does a single witness suffice? Whether the {\i shaliach} is certifying {\i lishma} (Rabbah) or authenticating signatures (Rava), this would normally appear to require two witnesses. The {\i gemara} begins to press this question.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ed Echad Principle\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The initial answer offered is the well-established principle of {\i eid echad ne\u8217'eman b\u8217'issurin} \u8212- a single witness is trusted in matters of {\i issur}, religious prohibition. Just as we trust one person to tell us that a piece of meat is {\i kosher}, we may trust one person to say that this {\i get} is halachically valid. The {\i get} is, from this vantage point, a question of permitted and forbidden \u8212- is this woman now permitted to remarry? \u8212- and in that domain, one witness carries sufficient weight.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Itchazek Issura Issue\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} immediately raises a powerful objection. The principle of {\i eid echad ne\u8217'eman b\u8217'issurin} applies when the status of an object is genuinely unknown at the outset. A piece of meat whose identity is unclear \u8212- is it {\i chelev} or permitted fat? \u8212- may be ruled upon by one witness. But here, the woman\u8217's status is not unknown. She is a married woman. Her {\i issur} has been established: {\i itchazek issura d\u8217'eishet ish}. When a forbidden status is already fixed and certain, one witness is ordinarily insufficient to overturn it. This significantly complicates the initial answer.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafot Defense\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafos} raises a further nuance in defense of {\i eid echad}. We do, in fact, trust single witnesses even regarding established forbidden statuses in certain contexts \u8212- the butcher, for instance, or one\u8217's wife who has prepared meat at home. We believe them that the {\i shechita} was performed, the fat removed, the meat properly treated. {\i Tosafos} explains the distinction: in those cases, the matter was {\i b\u8217'yado} \u8212- within the power and ability of that very person to perform or arrange. A butcher could kasher the meat; a wife could ensure it was done correctly. Here, however, no one can divorce this woman except her husband. The act is entirely outside anyone else\u8217's control. That is the {\i gemara\u8217's} point: {\i b\u8217'yado} is the key criterion, and it is absent here.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Davar Shebe\u8217'erva\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Even setting aside {\i itchazek issura}, there is a second, independent obstacle: the principle that {\i ein davar shebe\u8217'erva pachos mishnayim} \u8212- no matter touching on {\i arayos}, on forbidden sexual relationships and personal status, is decided with fewer than two witnesses. A married woman\u8217's status is not merely a standard {\i issur}; it is an {\i issur} rooted in {\i arayos}. The question of whether she is permitted or forbidden to other men always requires two witnesses as an absolute floor. This is the cliffhanger the {\i gemara} leaves us with: both on account of {\i itchazek issura} and on account of {\i davar shebe\u8217'erva}, one witness appears categorically insufficient.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rov Beki\u8217'in Solution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rov Principle\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now offers its answer. The underlying premise of the concern \u8212- that perhaps the {\i get} was not written {\i lishma} \u8212- does not actually rise to the level of a genuine {\i safek} requiring formal resolution. This is because of {\i rov beki\u8217'in}: the majority of people, even in {\i chutz l\u8217'aretz}, are in fact familiar with and committed to the {\i halachos} of {\i lishma}. The majority rule allows us to simply assume that the {\i get} was performed correctly without requiring any formal verification.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Meir Mi\u8217'uta\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A natural challenge arises from Rabbi Meir, who is known throughout {\i Shas} as one who is sensitive to minority possibilities \u8212- {\i de-chayeish le-mi\u8217'uta} \u8212- and does not dismiss them lightly. If there is a minority of people who do not know the relevant {\i halachos}, should Rabbi Meir not require that this concern be explicitly addressed? The {\i gemara} answers: {\i stam sofra de-dayanim migmar g\u8217'miri} \u8212- the scribes of the courts know the {\i halacha}. Who actually writes a {\i get}? Not the general population, but the {\i sofrim}, the professional scribes attached to {\i beis din}. The {\i hamon am} may not know the particulars of {\i lishma}, but the {\i sofrim} overwhelmingly do. As {\i Tosafos} frames it, the ignorant minority constitutes a {\i mi\u8217'uta de-mi\u8217'uta} \u8212- a minority of a minority \u8212- a category so small that even Rabbi Meir is not troubled by it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sofrim Knowledge\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The force of this answer depends on understanding who actually produces a {\i get}. Given the levels of literacy in the relevant era, the universe of those writing {\i gittin} was essentially coextensive with the {\i sofrim de-dayanim}, the court scribes. These are professionals embedded in the court system. Their knowledge of the {\i halachos} of {\i gittin} is virtually certain. The concern that a {\i get} may have been written by someone who did not understand {\i lishma} is therefore exceedingly remote.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mi\u8217'ut Categories\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafos} articulates a formal halachic distinction that underlies this analysis. There is a {\i mi\u8217'ut d\u8217'shchiach} \u8212- a prevalent or significant minority, sometimes quantified as something above approximately five percent \u8212- and a {\i mi\u8217'ut lo shchiach}, a trivial or negligible minority. These two categories are treated fundamentally differently in {\i halacha}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Leidas Hasafek\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i mi\u8217'ut d\u8217'shchiach} generates a {\i leidas hasafek} \u8212- it raises a question that must be addressed. Once such a minority is acknowledged, one must find a way to resolve the doubt: through {\i rov}, {\i chazaka}, investigation, or some other mechanism. A {\i mi\u8217'ut lo shchiach}, by contrast, does not even create a {\i leidas hasafek}. It does not rise to the level of a problem requiring a solution; it is treated as though it simply does not exist as a halachic concern. The {\i gemara} is saying that the possibility of a {\i get} being written by someone unfamiliar with {\i lishma} falls into this latter category \u8212- so negligible that it does not register as a question needing to be addressed.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Aguna Concern\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbis\u8217' Initiative\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Given the foregoing analysis, the {\i gemara} reaches a striking conclusion: from a purely technical standpoint, we should never have needed the {\i shaliach} to say anything at all. The {\i rov} of scribes know the {\i halacha}; the minority is too trivial to generate concern. And yet the Rabbis specifically created the requirement of {\i bifanai nechtav bifanai nechtam}. The {\i gemara} explains: {\i ve-rabbanan hu de-atzrich} \u8212- it is the Rabbis who imposed this additional demand, requiring that even a non-issue be formally addressed. They introduced a concern that strict halachic logic did not require. This is one of the first explicit invocations of the principle that will recur throughout the tractate: {\i mishum aguna akilu bah rabbanan} \u8212- because of the concern for {\i aguna}, a woman who would otherwise be unable to remarry, the Rabbis acted.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Why Higher Standard\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Rabbis\u8217' reasoning is as follows. Even though the {\i rov} of scribes unquestionably know the {\i halachos}, personal status is profoundly consequential. The stakes involve not merely whether one person committed a transgression, but whether any subsequent children might be {\i mamzerim}. In a domain of such severity, the Rabbis felt it appropriate to demand a greater level of certainty \u8212- something beyond a mere inferential reliance on majority. They wanted something concrete, some act of testimony or verification that would stand behind the {\i get}. At the same time, they recognized that if they raised this concern they would need to respond to it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbinic vs.\u160 ?Biblical\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Critically, because the concern is Rabbinic in origin \u8212- it was the Rabbis who created the problem by raising it, not the Torah that mandated it \u8212- the Rabbis retained the latitude to decide how to resolve it. They determined that one witness would suffice. Requiring two witnesses to satisfy what is ultimately a Rabbinically-generated demand would have been disproportionate.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two Witness Problem\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The practical consequences of demanding two witnesses would have been severe. A {\i get} arriving from {\i Medinat HaYam} \u8212- from overseas \u8212- would be blocked pending the production of two witnesses who could confirm the {\i get} was written and signed according to {\i halacha}. Who would those witnesses be? One would have to retrieve additional people from far away, a {\i tircha} \u8212- a burden \u8212- that might prove practically impossible. In many cases, the {\i get} simply would not be deliverable. That outcome is precisely what the Rabbis sought to prevent by accepting one witness.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Defense of One Witness\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Apparent Stringency\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now raises an incisive challenge: {\i hai kulla bu humra hu} \u8212- do you think this is a leniency? It is actually a stringency in disguise. By accepting only one witness rather than two, you have set the woman up for disaster. {\i De-im mas\u8217'rechet trei} \u8212- if you required two witnesses at the outset, a husband would have no opening to later challenge the {\i get} with any success. Two witnesses, properly testified before a {\i beis din}, are close to unassailable. But with only one witness, the {\i gemara} fears: {\i ati ba\u8217'al u\u8217'fasel lei} \u8212- the husband might come later and invalidate it. He might claim the {\i get} was not written {\i lishma}, that the process was flawed, that he had not truly understood what he was doing. If only one person stands behind the {\i get}, such a challenge might succeed. The woman might be left in the worst possible position \u8212- having lived as though divorced, now told the {\i get} is invalid.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shaliach Diligence\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} resolves this through a principle the later {\i rishonim} articulate as {\i me\u8217'ikara mi-dikdaik} \u8212- from the very outset, the {\i shaliach} is extremely careful. The reason is that, as the {\i gemara} notes \u8212- {\i bifnei kama nosna lah}, in front of how many does the {\i shaliach} deliver the {\i get}? \u8212- a debate between Rabbi Yochanan and Rabbi Chanina addresses this question: one holds the {\i shaliach} delivers before two witnesses, the other before three. The point is that the {\i shaliach} knows his declaration must be made before an audience. He is submitting what amounts to formal testimony. A person who knows he will have to formally testify is not cavalier about what he is testifying to; he makes absolutely certain in advance that the facts are as he will state them. Moreover, {\i v\u8217'lo ati l\u8217'irurei nafshei} \u8212- he will not put himself in the position of having his testimony publicly challenged and undermined.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rashi Refinement\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rashi adds an important dimension to what {\i me\u8217'ikara mi-dikdaik} actually entails. One might have assumed the {\i shaliach} is being careful to verify technical compliance \u8212- that the {\i get} was written {\i lishma}, that all the details were in order. But Rashi emphasizes something different. The more realistic concern, as Rashi reads the {\i gemara}, is that a husband who later challenges the {\i get} is typically not raising a genuine halachic defect that he happened to notice. He is almost certainly a husband who had misgivings, who is reconsidering the divorce, who is now motivated by regret. Given that reality, what the {\i shaliach} must be careful about \u8212- what his {\i dikduk} consists of \u8212- is ensuring that the husband was fully committed and resolute at the time of the {\i get}. The {\i shaliach} knows that his word may one day stand against the husband\u8217's, and so he makes certain the husband has no reservations, no hesitations, nothing that could later be dressed up as a halachic challenge.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shaliach Credibility\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rashi adds a second point. Even if a challenge does occur \u8212- whether based on legitimate concerns or on a husband\u8217's change of heart \u8212- {\i eino ne\u8217'eman}: the husband is not believed. {\i De-vadai dak be-milta ve-shapir} \u8212- the {\i shaliach} unquestionably knew what he was saying, and his claim carries more weight than the husband\u8217's belated challenge. This second point is crucial to the {\i gemara\u8217's} overall structure: not only is a challenge unlikely (because the {\i shaliach} has prevented it from arising), but even if it does arise, the {\i get} is protected because the {\i shaliach} will prevail.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishna Support\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafos} points out that this resolution finds implicit support in the {\i mishna} itself. The {\i mishna} states, regarding {\i gittin} in {\i Eretz Yisrael} \u8212- where no initial declaration is required \u8212- {\i im yesh alav orerim yitkayem be-chotmav}: if the {\i get} is challenged, it must be validated through its signatures. The contrast is instructive. In {\i Eretz Yisrael}, without an upfront declaration, a challenge can succeed in forcing signature validation. But in {\i chutz l\u8217'aretz}, where the {\i shaliach} has made the declaration of {\i bifanai nechtav bifanai nechtam}, the {\i mishna} strongly implies that a challenge does not open the door to re-examination. The upfront declaration serves as a shield against future challenge, precisely as the {\i gemara} has been arguing.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rava\u8217's Signature Valid\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Signature Validation Q\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having resolved the question for Rabbah\u8217's position \u8212- why one witness suffices to certify {\i lishma} \u8212- the {\i gemara} turns to the parallel question for Rava. Rava holds that the {\i shaliach\u8217's} function is to authenticate signatures, addressing the absence of {\i eidim metzuyim} who could verify them. Here too, the question immediately arises: {\i liba\u8217'ei trei} \u8212- why not require two? Signature validation is a formal halachic process; since when does one person suffice?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kiyum Shtarot\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The process at issue is {\i kiyum shtaros} \u8212- the standard legal procedure for validating a document. Ordinarily, before a {\i beis din} acts on a document, the signatures must be authenticated. This is done either by the signatories themselves or by two witnesses who recognize the signatures. The number two is structural: it mirrors the requirement for testimony in general. Allowing one person to validate a document\u8217's signatures is not the norm in any other context.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ed Echad Issurin\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} offers the same answer as before: {\i eid echad ne\u8217'eman b\u8217'issurin}. One witness is trusted in matters of {\i issur}. This is a striking application of the principle. The {\i gemara} is not treating the {\i get} as an ordinary {\i shtar} in the domain of civil law \u8212- {\i mammonus} \u8212- where signature validation would clearly require two. Instead, it is recategorizing the authentication of this document as fundamentally an {\i issur v\u8217'heter} question. The {\i shliach} validating the signatures of a {\i get} is not doing {\i kiyum shtaros} in the technical civil-law sense; he is testifying that a document capable of changing a woman\u8217's halachic status is genuine. That is a question of {\i issur}, and in that domain, one witness can in principle suffice.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is a significant {\i chiddush} in how the {\i gemara} frames the {\i get}. The {\i get} is simultaneously a {\i shtar} and an instrument of personal status. The {\i gemara} here chooses to see it through the lens of {\i issur v\u8217'heter} rather than through the lens of documentary law \u8212- and that reframing is what permits the testimony of one witness. The {\i gemara} thus consciously brackets the {\i shtar} identity of the {\i get} and foregrounds its {\i issur} identity.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Woman Status Issue\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} immediately reasserts the same objection it raised for Rabbah\u8217's position: {\i itchazek issura d\u8217'eishet ish}. The woman\u8217's status as a married woman is established and known. {\i Eid echad ne\u8217'eman b\u8217'issurin} applies to cases of genuine uncertainty \u8212- a piece of meat of unknown identity, {\i safeik shel chelev safeik shel shuman}. It does not apply when the forbidden status has already been fixed. A woman who is a married woman is not in an uncertain state; her {\i issur} is established. One witness cannot overturn what is known and certain.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Davar Shebe\u8217'erva\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Beyond {\i itchazek issura}, the {\i gemara} reasserts the second independent obstacle: {\i havei davar shebe\u8217'erva}, and {\i ein davar shebe\u8217'erva pachos mishnayim} \u8212- no fewer than two witnesses suffice for any matter touching on {\i arayos}. Divorcing a woman is precisely such a matter. Whether approached through the {\i issur} angle or the {\i shtar} angle, two witnesses appear to be required on all fronts. The question of how one witness can validate the signatures of a {\i get} has now been posed in full force for Rava\u8217's position, just as it was for Rabbah\u8217's.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Reish Lakish Principle\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Signatures on Document\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} resolves the impasse by invoking the principle of Reish Lakish: {\i edim hachatumim al hashtar} \u8212- witnesses who are signed on a document.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Vetting Presumption\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Reish Lakish states: {\i na\u8217'asu kemi shenachakra edusam beveis din} \u8212- it is as though their testimony has already been received and vetted in a court of law. This is a foundational principle about the nature of signed documents in Jewish law. When two people sign a document, the act of signing is treated as equivalent to appearing before a court and delivering testimony. The {\i chiddush} is that we do not then require a separate procedure to re-examine and validate what they have said.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The deeper implication, as the {\i gemara} develops it, is even more striking. Normally when a witness appears before a {\i beis din}, the court subjects him to {\i drisha ve-chakira} \u8212- rigorous examination and cross-examination. One might think: a person simply wrote something on a document and signed it; where is the cross-examination? Reish Lakish\u8217's principle is that the very act of signing constitutes a waiver of that process. The signed document is treated as testimony already vetted.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chiddush No Validation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} takes this one step further. Not only does Reish Lakish eliminate the need for cross-examination of what the witnesses said \u8212- he also establishes that at a {\i de\u8217'oraisa} level, there is no requirement to independently verify that the signatures on the document are genuine. The presumption is that a document bearing two signatures is authentic. We do not, at a biblical level, suspect forgery. This is the {\i chiddush}: that at the level of Torah law, a signed document is presumed to be what it appears to be. We do not require two witnesses to come and confirm that these really are the signatures of the people named.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This may seem counterintuitive \u8212- forgeries are certainly possible. But the reasoning appears to be that the exposure to challenge and the social and legal consequences of a detected forgery create a sufficient deterrent. The {\i gemara} draws an implicit parallel to how signed instruments function in practice: we presume authenticity unless challenged.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbinic Requirement\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It follows that the requirement to validate signatures before accepting a document \u8212- the entire institution of {\i kiyum shtaros} \u8212- is not a biblical requirement at all. It is a {\i derabbanan}, a Rabbinic enactment. The Rabbis, as a practical matter, imposed the requirement that documents be validated before a court acts on them. They were not willing to rely on the Reish Lakish presumption in practice without some gatekeeping. But at the {\i de\u8217'oraisa} level, none of this is required. The document, by virtue of its signatures, is presumed valid.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Waive Validation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This distinction between {\i de\u8217'oraisa} and {\i derabbanan} is what resolves the difficulty for Rava\u8217's position. The need for two witnesses to validate signatures is Rabbinic. In the case of a {\i get}, the Rabbis chose to waive that Rabbinic requirement \u8212- {\i mishum aguna akilu bah rabbanan}. Rather than demand the full apparatus of {\i kiyum shtaros}, they accepted one witness. Because the underlying requirement is Rabbinic, the Rabbis retained the authority to relax it. One witness satisfies the Rabbinically-created concern without imposing an impossible burden that would prevent {\i get} delivery from distant places.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Woman at Risk\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Husband Challenge\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} raises the same structural objection that it raised for Rabbah\u8217's position: this apparent leniency is in fact a stringency. {\i D\u8217'i atzreicht lah trei} \u8212- if you required two witnesses to validate the signatures, the husband would have no avenue to come later and challenge the {\i get}. {\i Lo ati ba\u8217'al u\u8217'ma\u8217'areer u\u8217'pasel lah} \u8212- he would not be able to come and contest it and render it invalid. But with only one witness standing behind the signature validation, {\i ati ba\u8217'al u\u8217'ma\u8217'areer u\u8217'pasel lah} \u8212- the husband could arrive and challenge the authenticity of the {\i get}, potentially leaving the woman in a devastating situation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 One Witness Protection\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The question sharpens: what exactly protects the woman if the husband does raise a challenge? If we have accepted only one witness\u8217's testimony that the signatures are genuine, what is the legal weight of that testimony when the husband, who is the {\i ba\u8217'al davar} \u8212- the principal and most directly interested party \u8212- appears and contests it?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Delivery Witnesses\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara\u8217's} answer parallels its earlier resolution for Rabbah: {\i bifnei kamma nosna lah} \u8212- in front of how many people does the {\i shaliach} deliver the {\i get}? Rabbi Yochanan and Rabbi Chanina debate whether it must be two or three. Either way, delivery occurs before witnesses. This structural feature of the {\i get} delivery process is what protects against challenge. The {\i shaliach} knows he must deliver before an audience and that his declaration will be part of a formal, witnessed proceeding. Accordingly, {\i meikara meidakdeik} \u8212- from the very outset he takes every care to ensure that the husband was genuinely committed to the {\i get} and has no misgivings.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shaliach Credibility\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Even if a challenge materializes, the {\i shaliach} is believed over the husband. {\i V\u8217'lo ati l\u8217'irurei nafshai} \u8212- the {\i shaliach} will not expose himself to reputational damage by making a claim he cannot support. Having been diligent from the outset, having ensured the husband had no second thoughts, having testified before witnesses, the {\i shaliach\u8217's} credibility prevails. The {\i gemara\u8217's} bottom line is the same as it was for Rabbah: the combination of the {\i shaliach\u8217's} diligence, the near-impossibility of a genuine challenge arising, and the {\i shaliach\u8217's} credibility in the event of a challenge together render the one-witness framework both practically sound and halachically defensible.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Both Phrases Req\u8217'd\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Writing Phrase Q\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having established the theoretical foundation for each position, the {\i gemara} presses further. Rava holds that the {\i shaliach\u8217's} function is to authenticate the signatures. If so, the phrase {\i bifanai nechtam} \u8212- signed before me \u8212- is entirely appropriate. But why say {\i bifanai nechtav} \u8212- written before me \u8212- at all? If the issue is signature validity, the writing process is irrelevant to Rava\u8217's concern. This redundancy is the {\i gemara\u8217's} opening challenge.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Confusion Risk\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} presents Rava\u8217's answer: if the {\i shaliach} said only {\i bifanai nechtam}, people observing this would conclude that one witness can validate signatures in the context of {\i kiyum shtaros} generally. {\i Ati l\u8217'achlufei b\u8217'kiyum shtaros de\u8217'alma b\u8217'eid echad} \u8212- people would confuse this with normal signature validation, conclude that one witness is sufficient for all documents, and halachic standards for document validation would erode. To prevent this confusion, Rava agrees that {\i bifanai nechtav} must be stated as well, signaling that something categorically different from standard {\i kiyum shtaros} is occurring.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Three Factors\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbah pushes back on Rava\u8217's reasoning: are there not already enough distinguishing features to prevent this confusion? Rava himself articulates three. First, the language itself: in normal {\i kiyum shtaros}, witnesses say {\i yadina} \u8212- \u8220"we recognize these signatures.\u8221" The {\i shaliach} says {\i bifanai} \u8212- \u8220"before me\u8221" \u8212- an entirely different formulation that already signals a departure from the norm. Second, women are eligible to serve as {\i shaliach} and make this declaration, whereas women cannot serve as witnesses in ordinary {\i kiyum shtaros}. Third, the {\i ba\u8217'al davar} \u8212- the principal, the interested party \u8212- can serve as {\i shaliach} here; under normal circumstances, an interested party cannot validate signatures. The wife herself could in principle serve as {\i shaliach} in a specially constructed case. These three differences, Rabbah argues, already mark this practice as sui generis, making {\i bifanai nechtav} unnecessary.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbah Counter\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava\u8217's response is that these distinguishing factors are insufficient. The textual difference \u8212- {\i bifanai} versus {\i yadina} \u8212- does not actually preclude confusion, because if the {\i shaliach} were to say {\i yadina} rather than {\i bifanai nechtam}, we would still accept the testimony. As {\i Tosafos} clarifies: Rava\u8217's concern is not that a single {\i shaliach} saying {\i bifanai} would be confused with {\i kiyum shtaros}, but that if two people delivered the {\i get}, they could say {\i yadina} \u8212- and that would genuinely resemble standard signature validation with two witnesses. The public might then draw the wrong lesson. Because in practice the {\i shaliach} might fall back on {\i yadina} rather than {\i bifanai}, and because this would be accepted, there remains a real risk of confusion unless {\i bifanai nechtav} is also stated.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Both Required\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The resolution is that both phrases are necessary, each serving a distinct purpose. {\i Bifanai nechtav} addresses Rabbah\u8217's concern \u8212- certifying {\i lishma} \u8212- and also, for Rava, signals that this is not ordinary {\i kiyum shtaros}. {\i Bifanai nechtam} addresses Rava\u8217's concern \u8212- authenticating signatures \u8212- and also, for Rabbah, confirms that the {\i chasimah} was likewise performed according to {\i halacha}. Together, the two phrases reflect both dimensions of the {\i get\u8217's} validity, and together they make the declaration\u8217's unique character unmistakably clear.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Writing vs.\u160 ?Signing\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Both Lishma Q\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now turns to a foundational question about the nature of a {\i get} as a halachic instrument. If the concern is {\i lishma} and the declaration includes both {\i bifanai nechtav} and {\i bifanai nechtam}, the implication is that both the {\i kesivah} \u8212- the writing \u8212- and the {\i chasimah} \u8212- the signing \u8212- must be performed {\i lishma}. Who is the {\i tanna} who holds this? The {\i gemara} frames this as a search for the author of the {\i mishna\u8217's} formulation, and in doing so, introduces the most fundamental debate of {\i Masechet Gittin}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Meir Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Meir, whose is the presumed view of unattributed {\i mishnayos}, holds that the {\i chasimah} is the essential act. The {\i get} is constituted by its signing, not its writing. The writing alone is merely words on paper; it is the signature that transforms the document into a legal instrument.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Attached Ground\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} derives Rabbi Meir\u8217's position from the rule that {\i ein kosvin b\u8217'mchubar l\u8217'karka} \u8212- one may not write a {\i get} on something still attached to the ground. The rule is that nothing may intervene between the writing and the delivery in a way that could disqualify the {\i get}. Writing on attached material means the document cannot be handed over without first cutting it down \u8212- and that act of cutting intervenes between writing and delivery.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Cutting Down Valid\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 However, if one violated this rule and wrote on something attached to the ground, and then {\i telasho v\u8217'chasamo u\u8217'nesano lah} \u8212- uprooted it, signed it, and delivered it \u8212- the {\i get} is {\i kasher}. This is the critical datum for understanding Rabbi Meir. Nothing occurred between the signing and the delivery. Even though something did intervene between the writing and the delivery \u8212- namely, the cutting down \u8212- this is irrelevant, because what matters is the signing. The {\i chasimah} is the {\i ikar}; it is the act that constitutes the {\i get}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Eliezer Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Eliezer takes the opposite view: {\i kesivah ba\u8217'ei chasimah lo ba\u8217'ei} \u8212- the writing is the essential act; signing is not halachically necessary. The {\i get} is constituted by being written, not by being signed. Signatures are extrinsic to the document\u8217's essential validity.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Three Gittin\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} introduces the position of Rabbi Eliezer by invoking the {\i mishna} of {\i shloshah gittin pesulin v\u8217'im nises ha-velad kasher} \u8212- three {\i gittin} that are rabbinically invalid, yet if the woman remarried, the child is not a {\i mamzer}. The fact that the child is not a {\i mamzer} reveals that the {\i get} is in fact valid at a biblical level; the disqualification is Rabbinic only. The three cases are: a {\i get} in the husband\u8217's own handwriting but without witnesses; a {\i get} with witnesses but without a date; and a {\i get} that is dated but bears only one signature.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 No Sig Valid\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Eliezer\u8217's position within this {\i mishna} goes further than any of those three cases. He states: {\i af al pi she-ein alav edim ella she-nasno bifnei edim} \u8212- even if there are no witnesses signed on the {\i get} at all, as long as it was delivered in the presence of witnesses, the {\i get} is {\i kasher}. She may even collect from encumbered property. The signature requirement is entirely dispensable for Rabbi Eliezer.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Edei Mesirah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The reasoning Rabbi Eliezer offers is encapsulated in the principle the {\i gemara} will call {\i edei mesirah karti} \u8212- the witnesses of delivery effect the divorce. What validates a {\i get} is not the signatures on the document but the witnesses present at the moment of delivery. The witnesses of {\i mesirah} are what transform the act into a legally effective divorce. As a physical document, a {\i get} does not require signatures.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Why then do witnesses ever sign a {\i get}? Rabbi Eliezer explains: {\i she-mipnei she-ein edim chosmin al ha-get ella mipnei tikkun olam} \u8212- signatures on a {\i get} are only a Rabbinic enactment for the smooth functioning of society. If the {\i get} has no signatures and the delivery witnesses are unavailable twenty years later, the woman has no way to prove she was divorced. Signatures are a practical solution to that evidentiary problem \u8212- not a constitutive element of the divorce itself.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This brings the {\i gemara} back to its structural question. Rabbi Meir holds that {\i chasimah} is the {\i ikar} \u8212- so he requires signing {\i lishma} but not necessarily writing {\i lishma}. Rabbi Eliezer holds that {\i kesivah} is the {\i ikar} and signatures are Rabbinically unnecessary \u8212- so he requires writing {\i lishma}, but if signatures happen to be present, there would be no requirement that they be {\i lishma}, as they are superfluous in the first instance. Neither position straightforwardly supports requiring both writing and signing to be {\i lishma} simultaneously. The {\i gemara} pauses here, and this question \u8212- which {\i tanna} is the author of the formulation that demands both \u8212- carries us forward into the next {\i daf}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Gittin 4\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rabbah vs.\u160 ?Rava\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two Explanations\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The discussion continues from the bottom of 3b, where the central debate between Rabbah and Rava is still unfolding \u8212- a debate that will occupy us for several more days. The question is: why does the {\i shaliach} who delivers a {\i get} need to declare {\i bifnei nichtav ubifnei nichtam}, that it was written and signed in his presence? Is this declaration meant to testify that the {\i get} was executed in accordance with the laws of {\i lishma} \u8212- that is, composed with the proper intention directed toward this specific woman \u8212- as Rabbah holds? Or is it meant to validate the signatures on the document, so that if the {\i get} is ever challenged in the future, one need not face the difficulty of locating people who can authenticate those signatures? That is the position of Rava, expressed through the phrase {\i edim metzuyim lekaymo} \u8212- that witnesses are readily available to confirm the document\u8217's validity.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbah: Lishma\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 As the Gemara has already pointed out, the declaration contains two distinct components: the {\i shaliach} testifies both that the {\i get} was {\i written} and that it was {\i signed} in his presence. The term \u8220"signed\u8221" seems naturally to support Rava\u8217's concern with authenticating the signatories, while the term \u8220"written\u8221" seems to emphasize that the proper procedures governing the composition of a {\i get} were followed. The question then becomes: how does each position account for the component that seems to support the other?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava: Signatures\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava, who holds that the concern is with validating the signatures, explains that {\i bifnei nichtav} \u8212- the testimony about the writing \u8212- serves a different purpose altogether. Since this declaration is made by a single individual, which is itself unusual (normally signature validation requires two witnesses), people might become confused about the ordinary rules of authentication. By having the {\i shaliach} add that it was also written in his presence, the declaration signals that this is something distinctive \u8212- a special institution unique to {\i gittin}, not a standard case of {\i kiyum shtarot}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Both Terms\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 For Rabbah, however, who holds that the entire institution is about testifying to the {\i lishma} requirements, the question runs in the opposite direction: if the whole point is the writing, why must the {\i shaliach} also testify about the signing? The Gemara therefore assumes that Rabbah understands the {\i Mishnah} as reflecting a view that demands compliance with the laws of {\i gittin} not only in the writing but equally in the signing. This leads the Gemara into a fundamental debate that will reverberate throughout the entire {\i masechet}: what is it, precisely, that transforms a piece of paper into a {\i get}?\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Get Validity\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The question the Gemara now frames is foundational: is it the writing of the document \u8212- executed according to the proper {\i halachot} \u8212- that creates the {\i get}? Or does the paper remain legally inert until it is signed, with the signatures themselves being the act that constitutes the {\i get} as a valid instrument? The Gemara presents this as a debate between Rabbi Meir and Rabbi Eliezer, and it uses the question of {\i mechubar} \u8212- whether a {\i get} is disqualified if it was written while attached to the ground \u8212- as a testing case to expose the underlying logic of each position.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 R. Meir: Signing\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Meir holds that it is the signatures alone that create the {\i get}. In his terminology, the signing is {\i karti} \u8212- from the root {\i karet}, meaning it \u8220"cuts,\u8221" it effects the severance. Just as {\i koret habrit} refers to the act that seals a covenant, here the {\i chatima} is the act that seals the divorce. The legal consequence of this view is striking: if the {\i get} is nothing until it is signed, then there is no logical basis for demanding anything particular about how it was written. All the {\i halachic} requirements \u8212- including {\i lishma} \u8212- attach only to the signing. Before the signatures are affixed, the document is just paper; one could, in principle, take a pre-printed form off the shelf that happens to bear the correct names and have it signed. It is only the interval between signing and delivery where requirements like {\i mechubar} apply.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 R. Eliezer: Writing\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Eliezer takes the opposite position: the writing is what matters, and the signatures are not required at all. A {\i get} delivered without signatures is valid \u8212- apparently even {\i lechatchila}, at the Torah level, and certainly {\i bediavad}. What is required, however, is that the {\i get} be delivered in the presence of witnesses. Here an important conceptual question arises: is this witness requirement structurally parallel to the witnesses required for {\i kiddushin} \u8212- that is, witnesses who validate the {\i act} of divorce rather than the {\i instrument} \u8212- or do those witnesses themselves form a constitutive part of the {\i get}? In Rabbi Eliezer\u8217's language, this principle is {\i eidei mesira karti}: it is the witnesses to the delivery who \u8220"cut,\u8221" who effect the divorce.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then asks: if Rabbi Eliezer does not require signatures at all, why would {\i bifnei nichtam} \u8212- the testimony about the signing \u8212- ever be relevant to him? The Gemara answers by citing Rabbi Abba: {\i modeh Rabbi Eliezer b\u8217'mezuyaf mitocho shehu pasul} \u8212- Rabbi Eliezer concedes that a document that is \u8220"forged from within\u8221" is invalid. Rashi explains {\i mezuyaf mitocho} as meaning that the document was signed by relatives or otherwise disqualified witnesses. Even though one need not sign the {\i get} at all, if one chooses to sign it, one may not do so with invalid witnesses. The principle is that one should not create something that purports to be a valid legal instrument while building an intrinsic defect into it: if you do not want to have signatures, do not have them; but if you are going to sign, you cannot sign with disqualified witnesses.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 R. Meir: Lenient\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Returning to Rabbi Meir, the Gemara now asks directly: does Rabbi Meir truly impose no requirements whatsoever on the writing? The Gemara cites Rav Nachman quoting Rabbi Meir: if someone found a pre-written {\i get} bearing the husband\u8217's and wife\u8217's names, had it signed by witnesses, and delivered it to the wife, it is {\i kasher}. The Gemara then asks whether this ruling is stated only at the Biblical level, while rabbinically the writing would still need to meet certain standards. The Gemara rejects this qualification: if Rabbi Meir\u8217's ruling were only Biblical, he would have said \u8220"from a Torah perspective it is {\i kasher}.\u8221" Since he simply says it is {\i kasher}, with no qualification, it is clear that Rabbi Meir imposes no requirements on the writing even rabbinically. The practical implication follows directly: there would be no basis for a {\i shaliach} to testify about how the {\i get} was written. We are therefore still searching for the opinion that demands both the writing and the signing follow all the proper requirements.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 R. Eliezer: Conditional\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now revisits Rabbi Eliezer from this angle. It was shown above that Rabbi Eliezer does not require signatures. But if he does not require them, why would there be any demand that they be done {\i lishma} when they are affixed? The Gemara answers: it is precisely the logic of {\i mezuyaf mitocho} that carries this through. If one does sign, the signature must be valid \u8212- not only in the narrow sense that the witnesses must be halachically qualified, but also with regard to {\i lishma}. Rashi adds this point explicitly: {\i o shelo lishma}, or if it was done without the proper intent, that too would constitute a problem for Rabbi Eliezer.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot raises a challenge: how can one compare the case of invalid witnesses \u8212- where the invalidity is intrinsic and obvious \u8212- to the case of signing {\i shelo lishma}, where there is no apparent deficiency? There are valid witnesses; they simply lacked the proper intent. Tosafot\u8217's own answer is somewhat less structurally elegant: the concern is practical \u8212- that people will come to rely on those signatures to permit her to remarry or to collect her {\i ketubah}. His final resolution is that once signing occurs at all, we apply a rabbinic rule: {\i gazrinan chatima atu ketiva} \u8212- we extend the requirements of the writing to the signing, so that slippage in the one domain will not infect the other.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might, however, suggest a more far-reaching approach. There are {\i Rishonim} who hold that according to Rabbi Eliezer, when signatures are affixed to the {\i get}, they do not merely serve as circumstantial evidence that witnesses were present \u8212- they become, at the Torah level, an integral part of the {\i get} itself, carrying the full testimonial weight of a classic {\i shtar}. If this is correct, then once one has chosen to sign the {\i get}, those signatures are recognized by the Torah as constitutive elements of the document, and they therefore require the same criteria \u8212- including {\i lishma} \u8212- as the writing itself. Under this reading, the demand for {\i lishma} in the signatures is not merely rabbinic caution against confusion, but potentially a Torah-level requirement that flows from the act of having incorporated testimony into the document. Tosafot\u8217's answer frames this as a rabbinic fence; but the conceptual logic could extend to the Biblical level.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A further point bears emphasis: the requirement of signatures is, in the final analysis, a {\i tikkun olam} \u8212- a rabbinic institution for the welfare of society. {\i Mideoraita}, Rabbi Eliezer would hold that an unsigned {\i get} takes full legal effect, and the woman is divorced. Practically, however, {\i beit din} will not permit her to remarry without some form of documentation. She cannot simply present herself and claim she was divorced; she bears the status of an {\i eshet ish}, and the court will demand proof. External witnesses could theoretically be summoned, but that is not always feasible or convenient. The practical, prudent course is always to have the document signed. The absence of a signature leaves her divorced in the eyes of Heaven but effectively unable to act on that status in this world. The institution of signed {\i gittin} is therefore the Rabbis\u8217' way of ensuring that {\i mide\u8217'oraita} validity translates into workable legal recognition.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The conclusion of this section: Rabbi Eliezer is the opinion whose framework supports requirements on both the writing and the signing. He does not require the signatures, but once they are affixed, they must follow the same rules as the writing \u8212- whether one understands this rabbinically or, as suggested above, potentially even Biblically.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 R. Yehuda: Both\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then offers a further possibility through Rav Ashi: {\i ha mani Rabbi Yehuda hi} \u8212- the {\i Mishnah} could reflect the position of Rabbi Yehuda. As taught in a {\i baraita}, Rabbi Yehuda invalidates the {\i get} unless both its writing and its signing take place when the document is {\i betalush} \u8212- detached from the ground. While it is somewhat difficult to construct a realistic scenario where a document is written while detached but signed while still attached, the theoretical point is clear: Rabbi Yehuda regards both the writing and the signing as independently essential to the validity of the {\i get}, and the requirement of {\i mechubar} applies to both stages equally. Whether he conceptualizes this as two independent acts that together constitute the {\i get}, or as a unique synthesis in which both the writing and the signing are jointly necessary, is left open, but the practical demand is symmetrical.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Why Not R. Yehuda\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara immediately asks: if Rabbi Yehuda provides the cleanest fit \u8212- requiring both writing and signing \u8212- why did the Gemara not simply begin there? The answer is twofold. First, {\i setam Mishnah Rabbi Meir}: an anonymous {\i Mishnah} is presumptively attributable to Rabbi Meir, and the opening requirement of {\i bifnei nichtav ubifnei nichtam} is stated anonymously. It was therefore natural to first attempt to reconcile it with Rabbi Meir\u8217's position. Second, and more significantly: {\i dekaiyma lan hilchata keva\u8217'tei b\u8217'gittin} \u8212- the {\i halacha} follows Rabbi Eliezer in {\i gittin}. This is a remarkable moment in the {\i sugya}: right at the outset, in what is only the second {\i daf} of the {\i masechet}, the Gemara announces the operative ruling on one of the most fundamental questions in the entire tractate. The {\i halacha} is that signatures on a {\i get} are not required. A {\i get} delivered before witnesses who did not sign the document is Biblically valid. Rabbi Yehuda, whose position is neither the {\i stam Mishnah} nor the accepted ruling, was therefore the option of last resort.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This explains why the {\i sugya} works through the positions in the order it does. The initial anonymous statement is matched first to Rabbi Meir, then to Rabbi Eliezer, and only when both prove problematic does the Gemara reach Rabbi Yehuda. It is a well-constructed opening {\i sugya} that introduces a major conceptual debate, maps out the positions of the {\i Tannaim}, and announces the {\i halacha} \u8212- all within the first pages of the tractate.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Medina HaYam\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now transitions to examining the rest of the {\i Mishnah} through the lens of the Rabbah-Rava debate. The {\i Mishnah} contains an internal dispute about what qualifies as {\i medinat hayam} \u8212- the overseas region or distant diaspora \u8212- that triggers the {\i shaliach}\u8217's obligation to declare {\i bifnei nichtav ubifnei nichtam}. The dispute involves three positions: Rabban Gamliel holds that the requirement applies even to cities {\i semuchot le-Eretz Yisrael} \u8212- adjacent to the Land of Israel \u8212- and Rabbi Eliezer extends this further to cities that are {\i muvlaot b\u8217'tehum Eretz Yisrael} \u8212- geographically enclosed or surrounded by the territory of Israel. The Sages, representing the view that the requirement applies only to genuinely distant locations, take the opposing position. Abaye explains that the debate concerns towns near the border of Israel, some of which are so closely surrounded by Israelite territory that they are described as \u8220"swallowed up\u8221" within it. Rabbah bar bar Hana adds that such a place was as short a distance as from Bei Kubi to Pumbedita.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rashi depicts the geometry as follows: {\i semuchot} refers to a city right at the border; {\i muvlaot} refers to a city that is not merely adjacent but effectively enclosed \u8212- surrounded on multiple sides by Israelite territory. These are different degrees of closeness to Israel, and they are different degrees of removal from the category of {\i medinat hayam}. The Tanna Kamma says that only truly distant locations require the declaration; Rabban Gamliel says that even a city just at the border falls under the requirement; Rabbi Eliezer holds that even a {\i muvlaot} city \u8212- effectively surrounded by Israel \u8212- still requires it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara asks: is this dispute best understood as reflecting the Rabbah-Rava debate? If the concern is {\i lishma} \u8212- cultural and legal awareness of the proper procedures \u8212- then a city right over the border is probably still part of the same cultural orbit and would know the relevant {\i halachot}. But if the concern is the availability of witnesses for {\i kiyum}, then even a nearby city over a political border represents a distinct travel barrier, and we cannot assume that witnesses will be readily accessible across it. On this reading, the debate in the {\i Mishnah} about near versus far is really a debate about which underlying concern governs the institution.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara, however, rejects the premise that this dispute maps directly onto the Rabbah-Rava divide. Instead, it holds that each of Rabbah and Rava can explain the debate internally within their own framework.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rabbah on Lishma\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 According to Rabbah \u8212- who holds the concern is {\i lishma} \u8212- the underlying principle is agreed upon by all: the requirement exists because communities outside Israel may not be adequately familiar with the {\i halachot} of {\i lishma}. The debate is then about where to draw the line.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Tanna Kamma holds that cities so close to Israel are still part of the same cultural and legal sphere; proximity means they have absorbed the relevant knowledge, and no declaration is required. Rabban Gamliel distinguishes: a {\i muvlaot} city, surrounded on multiple sides by Israel, may indeed be culturally integrated enough to know the law \u8212- he will concede that much \u8212- but a {\i semuchot} city, merely adjacent without being enclosed, might not have absorbed the same standards, and he requires the declaration there. Rabbi Eliezer pushes back against any such case-by-case evaluation: we cannot go city by city assessing knowledge levels. A formal, categorical rule is necessary. The rule is simple \u8212- anything over the border requires the declaration \u8212- and Rabbi Eliezer invokes the principle of {\i lo plug}: we do not make distinctions within the category of {\i medinat hayam} based on individual circumstances.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rava on Witnesses\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava\u8217's framework \u8212- that the concern is the availability of witnesses to authenticate the document \u8212- can explain the same dispute with equal coherence. Here too, all {\i Tannaim} agree on the underlying principle: the requirement exists because witnesses may not be readily available to validate signatures across geographic distances. The Tanna Kamma holds that nearby cities have enough cross-border travel that witnesses are sufficiently accessible. Rabban Gamliel distinguishes {\i muvlaot} from {\i semuchot}: a city truly surrounded by Israel will have robust travel and available witnesses, but one merely adjacent will not. Rabbi Eliezer again invokes {\i lo plug} and {\i shelo tachlok b\u8217'medinat hayam} \u8212- we do not parse the category of {\i medinat hayam} into subcategories; the formalist rule applies uniformly to everything across the border.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara\u8217's broader methodological point is significant: one can hold a single principled position and still debate its application. Whether the issue is {\i lishma} or {\i edim metzuyim}, the underlying debate about near and far is really a debate about formalism versus context. A legal system cannot operate through purely contextual, case-by-case determinations. At some point, rules must be categorical. The question is merely where to set the threshold and how rigorously to apply it.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Molich Issue\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara turns to the next clause of the {\i Mishnah}: {\i vehamolich} \u8212- the obligation of a {\i shaliach} who carries a {\i get} outward from Israel to the diaspora, not merely inward. The {\i Chachamim} say that the declaration is required both of one who brings a {\i get} from {\i medinat hayam} to Israel and of one who carries it {\i out} \u8212- {\i hamolich}. The inference is that the Tanna Kamma required the declaration only for {\i hamevi}, bringing it in, and not for {\i hamolich}. The Gemara asks: is this dispute about {\i molich} a direct reflection of the Rabbah-Rava debate?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 On the {\i lishma} theory, the concern is about people in the diaspora who lack the proper knowledge. A {\i shaliach} carrying a {\i get} from Israel outward is departing from a place of expertise; there is no {\i lishma} problem on his end. There would therefore be no intrinsic reason to require the declaration. On the {\i edim} theory, however, travel barriers exist equally in both directions: witnesses in the diaspora are no more accessible to those in Israel than vice versa. The requirement should therefore apply symmetrically, and {\i hamolich} should require the declaration just as {\i hamevi} does.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This appears to be a clean test case for the Rabbah-Rava debate, and the Gemara initially entertains this reading. It then pulls back: each position can explain both the Tanna Kamma and the {\i Chachamim} from within its own logic.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbah explains: {\i dekulei alma lefi she-ein bekiyim b\u8217'lishma}, and the debate about {\i molich} is whether we extend the requirement through a {\i gezerah} of {\i molich atu mevi}. The Tanna Kamma says no such extension is necessary. The {\i Chachamim} say that to avoid confusion \u8212- someone seeing that {\i hamevi} always declares might assume the requirement is universal, and become confused when {\i hamolich} does not \u8212- we institute a symmetric requirement. If people in {\i medinat hayam} observe that every {\i shaliach}, regardless of direction, makes this declaration, this itself serves an educational function consistent with the {\i lishma} concern. The Tanna Kamma is not willing to make that extension; the {\i Rabbanan} are.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava explains more simply: {\i dekulei alma lefi she-ein edim metzuyin lekaymo}, and the {\i Chachamim} who add {\i hamolich} are not introducing a new dispute but merely clarifying what was already implied. When the Tanna Kamma said {\i hamevi}, he meant it to encompass both directions; when the {\i Chachamim} add {\i hamolich} explicitly, {\i rabbanan batrai lefirushei taama d\u8217'Tanna Kamma hu de-atu} \u8212- they are clarifying and unpacking what the earlier statement already included. This parallels the earlier discussion of {\i medinat hayam} itself: just as the debate about {\i semuchot} and {\i muvlaot} was a dispute about the scope of a general term, the addition of {\i hamolich} is similarly an elaboration on the scope of {\i hamevi}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Medina to Medina\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now examines the clause: {\i hamevi get mimedina limedina b\u8217'medinat hayam} \u8212- one who carries a {\i get} from one {\i medina} to another within the diaspora is required to declare {\i bifnei nichtav ubifnei nichtam}. The Gemara asks what can be inferred from this. Both Rabbah\u8217's and Rava\u8217's concerns are clearly present when crossing from one diaspora district to another: {\i lishma} problems exist throughout the diaspora, and witness availability diminishes with each additional border crossed. The question is what the {\i Mishnah}\u8217's language implies about cases it does not explicitly state.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rava\u8217's Challenge\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara notes that a straightforward reading of {\i hamolich mimedina limedina b\u8217'medinat hayam} implies that within a {\i single} diaspora {\i medina}, the declaration would not be required. For Rava, who grounds the requirement in witness availability, this is perfectly intelligible: witnesses are more accessible within a single administrative district, where travel is less restricted. But for Rabbah, who grounds the requirement in {\i lishma}, the implication is more troubling. The {\i lishma} problem does not disappear merely because one stays within a single diaspora {\i medina}; the lack of proper halakhic awareness pervades the entire diaspora regardless of internal administrative divisions.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara also raises a harder challenge for Rabbah. The {\i Mishnah} explicitly states that one who brings a {\i get} within {\i Eretz Yisrael} does not need to make the declaration at all \u8212- including, apparently, when traveling from one region ({\i medina}) to another within Israel. For Rava, who focuses on witness availability, this is easily explained: Israel has special characteristics that ensure witnesses are accessible. But for Rabbah, who focuses on {\i lishma}, why should Israel\u8217's internal geography matter? The {\i lishma} requirements apply everywhere; crossing from the Galil to Yehuda involves a change of district no less than crossing within the diaspora. The distinction between Israel and the diaspora is built into Rabbah\u8217's framework at the macro level, but the internal distinction \u8212- {\i medina} to {\i medina} within Israel \u8212- is harder to derive from {\i lishma} concerns alone.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rabbah\u8217's Answer\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara resolves Rabbah\u8217's difficulty. One might have thought, based on the {\i diyuk}, that staying within a single {\i medina} of {\i medinat hayam} exempts one from the declaration \u8212- which would be inexplicable if the concern is {\i lishma}, since {\i lishma} problems exist uniformly across the diaspora. The Gemara rejects this inference: even within a single diaspora {\i medina} the declaration is required; the {\i Mishnah}\u8217's statement about traveling between {\i medinot} in {\i medinat hayam} is meant to contrast not with same-{\i medina} travel in the diaspora, but with {\i medina}-to-{\i medina} travel within {\i Eretz Yisrael}, which does not require the declaration. The {\i lishma} framework then handles this contrast naturally: Israel as a whole is the domain of proper halakhic knowledge, and the internal structure of Israel is irrelevant to that determination.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 For Rava, the analogous challenge is met by invoking the institution of {\i olei regalim} \u8212- the pilgrimage festivals that brought people to Jerusalem from throughout the land. This constant, large-scale movement across all regions of Israel ensured that witnesses were readily and regularly available throughout the country, making the {\i edim metzuyim} concern inapplicable to any intra-Israel transit. When the Temple no longer stands, the Gemara substitutes {\i batei dinin d\u8217'kevi\u8217'ei} \u8212- the presence of fixed, established courts \u8212- which similarly attracted regular travel and ensured that witnesses from any corner of Israel could be found without undue difficulty. The Gemara acknowledges that this explanation works better in one direction of travel than the other, but the basic answer is that Israel\u8217's unique institutional life \u8212- whether centered on the {\i Beit HaMikdash} or on its network of permanent courts \u8212- provided the structural conditions under which witnesses were always effectively available, and thus the concern animating the requirement of {\i bifnei nichtav ubifnei nichtam} simply did not arise within its borders.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i sugya} of this {\i daf} has thus worked through the core of the {\i Mishnah}: the foundational debate between Rabbah and Rava, the major tannaitic dispute about what constitutes a {\i get}, the announced {\i halacha} following Rabbi Eliezer, and an analysis of how the geographic debates within the {\i Mishnah} \u8212- near versus far, coming in versus going out, one {\i medina} versus another \u8212- each reflect and are illuminated by the two competing rationales. The analysis will continue on the following {\i daf}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Gittin 5\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rabba vs.\u160 ?Rava Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Debate Overview\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The daf begins at the bottom of 4b, still working through the debate between Rabba and Rava concerning the reason the Mishnah requires the messenger who brings a {\i get} from abroad to declare {\i bifnei nichtav u\u8217'bifnei nechtam} \u8212- that the {\i get} was written and signed in his presence. Rabba holds that the requirement addresses {\i lishmah} concerns, ensuring the {\i get} was written with proper intent for this specific couple. Rava holds that the requirement addresses {\i ein eidim metzuyim lekaymo} \u8212- the practical unavailability of witnesses to validate the signatures. The Gemara has already worked through several {\i mishnayot} that could neatly have fallen along these lines, and has managed to explain each according to both positions.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Asasyos Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The outstanding question at this stage concerned why Eretz Yisrael enjoys a special status \u8212- why, within Eretz Yisrael, the messenger need not make this declaration even according to the position that the requirement stems from concerns about witness availability. The answer offered was that travel within Eretz Yisrael is common, whether due to the {\i Beis Hamikdash} or, in later periods, the prevalence of {\i batei dinim}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now advances to a fresh challenge. We learn in the Mishnah that Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel says: even from one {\i hegemonia} to another \u8212- that is, from one jurisdiction to another within a single divided city \u8212- the messenger must make the declaration. Rabbi Yitzchak explains that there was a city in Eretz Yisrael called Asasyos, which was divided between two governments that were at odds with one another ({\i makpidim zeh al zeh}), such that travel from one side of the city to the other was effectively impossible \u8212- like East and West Berlin. Therefore, even within that single city, moving from one political jurisdiction to the other required the declaration.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The critical point is that this case is situated in Eretz Yisrael. Since Eretz Yisrael is generally exempt from the requirement on Rava\u8217's account \u8212- witnesses being available there \u8212- the reason Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel reintroduces the requirement must be the practical impossibility of travel and the resulting unavailability of witnesses. This is {\i nichah l\u8217'Rava} \u8212- it fits Rava\u8217's position well \u8212- but {\i kashya l\u8217'Rabba}, it is difficult for Rabba. If the concern were {\i lishmah}, there would be no reason for Eretz Yisrael\u8217's general exemption to be overridden by a divided city, since {\i lishmah} concerns do not depend on the ease of travel.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabba Concedes\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara concludes: {\i Rabba is lei d\u8217'Rava} \u8212- Rabba concedes Rava\u8217's point. The one who holds {\i lishmah} as his primary rationale also acknowledges that there is a genuine problem of {\i ein eidim metzuyim lekaymo}. Rabba\u8217's position is therefore not simply {\i lishmah} alone, but rather {\i lishmah} plus {\i ein eidim metzuyim lekaymo}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is, in fact, a coherent and elegant position: the declaration has two components \u8212- {\i bifnei nichtav} and {\i bifnei nechtam}. One might understand {\i bifnei nichtav} as addressing the {\i lishmah} concern \u8212- how the {\i get} was written \u8212- and {\i bifnei nechtam} as addressing the witness-validation concern \u8212- how it was signed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two Concerns\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 With Rabba now accepting both rationales, the question becomes: what remains between Rabba and Rava? The answer is that while Rabba agrees to Rava, Rava does not agree to Rabba. Rava maintains that signature validation is the {\i sole} concern; {\i lishmah} adds nothing. The remaining dispute therefore concerns cases where signature validation is not at issue but a {\i lishmah} concern might still apply.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Remaining Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What would constitute such a case? The Gemara identifies two possibilities. The first is where two witnesses bring the {\i get} together. The second is when the {\i get} is brought within the same country in {\i Medinat Hayam}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two Witnesses Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If two witnesses bring the {\i get}, they themselves implicitly testify that the husband dispatched them, thereby eliminating any concern about the signatures being forged. There is no need for further validation. Yet for Rabba, a {\i lishmah} concern may still exist if the {\i get} originated in {\i Chutz La\u8217'aretz} \u8212- if it came from a place where the laws of proper writing were not well known.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Lishmah Concerns\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Similarly, when the {\i get} is brought within the same country of {\i Medinat Hayam}, there is no problem of signature validation since witnesses are accessible within that jurisdiction. But for Rabba, the {\i lishmah} concern persists wherever a {\i Chutz La\u8217'aretz} problem exists \u8212- even within one country, if it is a place where {\i lishmah} was not carefully observed, such as a distant location where Torah was not studied.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 In short: whenever there is a signature-validation concern, everyone agrees the declaration must be made \u8212- {\i Rabba is lei d\u8217'Rava}. What Rabba adds is that even where there is no such concern, if the {\i lishmah} problem of {\i Chutz La\u8217'aretz} applies, he requires the declaration as well.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Mute Messenger Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Aino Yachol Intro\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now turns to a Mishnah that will serve as further evidence in this debate. The Mishnah states: one who brings a {\i get} from {\i Medinat Hayam} and is {\i eino yachol lomar} \u8212- unable to say {\i bifnei nichtav u\u8217'bifnei nechtam} \u8212- may rely instead on having the signatures validated: {\i im yesh alav eidim yiskayem b\u8217'chosamav}, if there are witnesses, let it be confirmed through the signatories. The apparent implication is stark: if you cannot make the declaration, simply validate the signatures, and the {\i get} is valid. This reads as though the entire point of the requirement is signature validation \u8212- nothing more.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Before accepting this reading, however, the Gemara must clarify what {\i eino yachol lomar} \u8212- \u8220"unable to say\u8221" \u8212- actually means. The obvious first answer is that the messenger is a {\i cheresh}, a deaf-mute. But that cannot be right: the Mishnah elsewhere states explicitly that a deaf-mute may not bring a {\i get} in the first place \u8212- {\i hakol k\u8217'sherim l\u8217'havi es haget chutz micheresh shoteh v\u8217'katan} \u8212- since a deaf-mute lacks the legal competence ({\i bar da\u8217'as}) required to serve as a {\i shaliach}. How then could the case be a deaf-mute?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Yosef Explanation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Yosef resolves this: {\i k\u8217'gon shenasno lo k\u8217'shehu pike\u8217'ach} \u8212- the {\i get} was given to a person who was fully competent, but before he could declare {\i bifnei nichtav u\u8217'bifnei nechtam} after delivering the {\i get}, he became a deaf-mute. He had a stroke on the spot. That is why he is unable to say the declaration \u8212- not because he was a deaf-mute at the time of appointment as {\i shaliach}, but because he became one immediately after delivery.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafot Alternative\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This answer strikes Tosafot as unnecessarily extreme. Two far simpler explanations present themselves: the messenger is simply mute ({\i ilem}) \u8212- physically unable to speak but legally competent \u8212- or he was not present at the writing and signing and therefore genuinely cannot testify to it. Either of these would make better sense of the phrase {\i eino yachol lomar}. And if the Gemara wanted to convey a case of physical inability, why not say {\i v\u8217'lo amar} \u8212- \u8220"and he did not say\u8221" \u8212- rather than the charged language of {\i eino yachol}, which seems to emphasize an absolute incapacity?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Signatures Primary\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Whatever the precise identity of the messenger in the Mishnah, the substantive point stands: someone delivered a {\i get} and was unable or failed to make the declaration, and the solution is simply to validate the signatures. This is a clear statement that the primary \u8212- indeed, on this reading, the sole \u8212- operative concern is signature validation. The Gemara therefore notes: {\i l\u8217'Rava nicha, l\u8217'Rabba kashya} \u8212- it is good for Rava, whose entire position rests on the signature concern, but difficult for Rabba, who also insists on a {\i lishmah} concern. What became of {\i lishmah} in this case? The Gemara answers: {\i l\u8217'achar shelamdu} \u8212- this Mishnah addresses the period after the laws of {\i lishmah} became widely known in {\i Chutz La\u8217'aretz}, at which point the {\i lishmah} concern ceased to apply.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Lishmah to Signatures\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Evidence Conflict\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This Gemara is striking in its overall trajectory. What began as a clear, principled debate \u8212- is the requirement of {\i bifnei nichtav u\u8217'bifnei nechtam} about {\i lishmah} or about signature validation? \u8212- has evolved in a way that seems to progressively favor Rava. First, Rabba concedes that signature validation is also a concern. Then, confronted with a Mishnah that implies the {\i lishmah} concern has entirely lapsed, Rabba is forced to concede further: there was a period when the {\i lishmah} issue ceased to be operative.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The argument weighing in Rabba\u8217's favor remains the Mishnah\u8217's fundamental distinction between Eretz Yisrael and {\i Chutz La\u8217'aretz}. If the requirement were purely about signature validation, the geographic distinction should track witness availability \u8212- and witnesses are available in Eretz Yisrael because of the {\i Beis Hamikdash} or the {\i batei dinim}. This indeed explains Eretz Yisrael\u8217's exemption, and the Asasyos case showed that even within Eretz Yisrael, the requirement can be reinstated when travel is impossible. All of this fits Rava\u8217's account. The emphasis on Eretz Yisrael is thus Rabba\u8217's best evidence, and even that is accounted for by Rava.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Yet there is a tension worth noting. The evidence pointing toward {\i lishmah} is the structural emphasis on Eretz Yisrael as the default domain of exemption. The evidence pointing toward signature validation is the repeated pattern by which {\i ein eidim metzuyim lekaymo} explains case after case with precision. And crucially, when the two accounts come apart \u8212- as in the Mishnah of {\i eino yachol} \u8212- the Gemara explains it away as {\i l\u8217'achar shelamdu}, suggesting that the {\i lishmah} concern is temporally contingent in a way that the signature concern is not. This indicates that the parameters of the requirement are contextual, not purely formalistic: if circumstances change, what was once a concern may cease to be one.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gezeirah Yachzor\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This contextual flexibility, however, raises a difficulty. If {\i lishmah} is no longer a concern, why not simply permit the messenger to skip the declaration entirely and rely on signature validation from the outset? Let the Mishnah say: {\i lechatchila}, if you want to validate the signatures instead, be our guest \u8212- that is the better option.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara answers with the principle of {\i gezeirah shema yachzor hadavar lekilkulo} \u8212- we fear that if we abandon the established practice, conditions may revert to the problematic state that originally necessitated it. Things were bad once; they got better; they can get worse again, as indeed we have witnessed in our own times. Therefore, we do not say {\i lechatchila} that the declaration is unnecessary. We maintain the original requirement in its place: the messenger should continue to declare {\i bifnei nichtav u\u8217'bifnei nechtam}. Only when this becomes impossible will we acknowledge changed circumstances and permit signature validation instead.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Lechatachila Bedi\u8217'eved\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This creates a formal distinction between {\i lechatchila} and {\i bedi\u8217'eved}. {\i Lechatchila}, the old practice is preserved \u8212- the messenger must make the declaration. {\i Bedi\u8217'eved}, if that is not possible, we acknowledge that {\i lishmah} is no longer an active concern and signature validation suffices. The {\i gezeirah} is maintained prospectively but not imposed retroactively in impossible cases.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Unusual Cases Exempt\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara presses further: if we maintain the old practice even when circumstances have changed, we should insist on the declaration even in a case like the {\i pike\u8217'ach shenishtatek} \u8212- the person who became a deaf-mute \u8212- where it is genuinely impossible. Should there not be no out? The Gemara responds: {\i milta d\u8217'lo shechicha lo gazru bah rabbanan} \u8212- the Rabbis do not extend their decrees to bizarre and highly unusual circumstances. The case of a person who was fully competent and then became a deaf-mute immediately after delivery is so extraordinarily rare that the {\i gezeirah} does not apply to it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Woman Get Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might think this creates a general exemption for unusual cases. But the Gemara challenges this immediately: what about the woman who brings her own {\i get}? That too is a bizarre scenario \u8212- the Mishnah explains it as a case where the husband appointed her as his {\i shaliach} to deliver the {\i get} to herself upon arriving in a particular place. Yet the Mishnah rules that she must still declare {\i bifnei nichtav u\u8217'bifnei nechtam}. If unusual cases are exempt from the {\i gezeirah}, why must she declare it?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara answers: {\i shelo tachlok b\u8217'shlichus} \u8212- we do not wish to introduce distinctions within the framework of {\i shlichus}. The principle of {\i lo plug} applies: we establish a uniform rule that every {\i shaliach} who is able to make the declaration must make it, regardless of whether the particular circumstances are usual or unusual. The {\i gezeirah} is maintained as a blanket requirement for all functioning {\i shlichim}, precisely to avoid the confusion that exceptions would generate.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Resolution Balance\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This brings the analysis to its resolution. Tosafot captures it concisely: {\i shelo tachlok b\u8217'shlichus}, {\i peirush b\u8217'shlichus d\u8217'yachol} \u8212- the rule applies uniformly when the {\i shaliach} is able to make the declaration. When, however, the {\i shaliach} is genuinely unable \u8212- as in the deaf-mute case \u8212- we do not impose an impossible standard. We acknowledge current realities: {\i lishmah} is no longer an active concern, and signature validation is sufficient. The woman bringing her own {\i get}, unusual as that scenario is, can still make the declaration, so the {\i lo plug} applies and she must.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The overall position, then, is this: {\i lechatchila}, every {\i shaliach} who can make the declaration must do so \u8212- not because {\i lishmah} is still a live concern, but because we maintain the old practice against the possibility of its recurrence. When it is genuinely impossible, we permit signature validation as a {\i bedi\u8217'eved} alternative. And unusual cases that fall entirely outside the normal {\i shlichus} paradigm \u8212- such as two messengers bringing a {\i get} together, as will emerge below \u8212- are treated differently.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Husband Delivers Get\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Husband Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now raises a new question. If the principle is {\i lo plug} \u8212- we maintain the declaration uniformly to preserve the established practice \u8212- then even the husband himself, when he personally delivers the {\i get}, should be required to declare {\i bifnei nichtav u\u8217'bifnei nechtam}. After all, even if he is personally delivering it, and even if there is no concern about forgery, perhaps we should insist on his declaration as a hedge against the {\i lishmah} concern that might someday resurface. Yet a {\i braita} teaches explicitly: {\i hu atzmo sheheivi gito ein tzarich lomar bifnei nichtav u\u8217'bifnei nechtam} \u8212- if the husband himself brought his {\i get}, he need not make the declaration. Why not?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Real Concern\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara answers by clarifying what the underlying concern actually is. One might have thought that the requirement of {\i bifnei nichtav u\u8217'bifnei nechtam} exists because we genuinely worry the {\i get} was written {\i shelo lishmah}. In fact, this is not the case. The genuine concern of {\i shelo lishmah} is a {\i miuta d\u8217'miuta} \u8212- a vanishingly small concern. It is not a real fear that {\i gittin} are being written without proper intent. The actual concern, says the Gemara, is something more social and strategic: {\i dilma ati ba\u8217'al u\u8217'mearer u\u8217'fasil lei} \u8212- we fear that the husband will use {\i lishmah} as a pretext, a basis for later challenging the {\i get} and invalidating it. The declaration closes off that pretext: it establishes a record that the writing and signing were done properly.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Husband Exception\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 With this clarification, the exemption for a husband who delivers his own {\i get} becomes entirely logical. {\i Minka naktei biyadei ve\u8217'ati ka\u8217'mearer lei?} \u8212- if he himself is physically delivering the {\i get} with his own hands, taking personal responsibility for it in public, is he then going to turn around and challenge it? The personal involvement eliminates both the factual basis and any credible pretext for a challenge. It would be absurd: he is the one who brought it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This clarification is significant beyond the immediate case. It reveals that the {\i lishmah} concern was never primarily about the substantive risk that a {\i get} was actually written without proper intent. It was always about protecting the woman against a husband who might exploit a technical pretext. The requirement of {\i bifnei nichtav u\u8217'bifnei nechtam} is thus oriented toward the woman\u8217's interests, not toward a genuine fear that scribal practice was deficient.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Two Messengers Get\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shmuel Query\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara introduces a query posed by Shmuel to Rav Huna: if two people bring a {\i get} from {\i Medinat Hayam} \u8212- {\i shnayim sheheviu get mimedinas hayam} \u8212- must they declare {\i bifnenu nichtav u\u8217'bifnenu nechtam}? Recall that two messengers together constitute one of the key {\i nafka mina} cases in the Rabba-Rava debate. Since two witnesses deliver the {\i get}, their combined testimony implicitly establishes that the husband sent them and that the {\i get} is genuine \u8212- there is no concern about forgery, and thus no {\i ein eidim metzuyim lekaymo} issue. The sole remaining concern, according to Rabba, would be {\i lishmah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Huna answers: they need not declare. He reasons: {\i uma ilu yomru bifnenu gershah mi lo meheimni} \u8212- if they had stated that the divorce was carried out in their presence, they would be believed for everything. Certainly, then, they are believed to establish that the {\i get} was properly dispatched by the husband. This eliminates the signature concern entirely. The Gemara notes: {\i l\u8217'Rabba nicha, l\u8217'Rava kashya} \u8212- good for Rabba, whose position is driven by {\i lishmah}, but difficult for Rava, since on Rava\u8217's account the concern is signature validation, and here the two messengers eliminate that concern. The Gemara\u8217's characteristic response appears once more: {\i delamdu} \u8212- they already knew the laws of {\i lishmah} by this point, so that concern does not apply.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two Unusual\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then asks: if {\i lishmah} is no longer a concern, and we rely on {\i l\u8217'achar shelamdu}, why not simply say that even a single messenger need not declare \u8212- just let the signatures be validated? The answer returns to {\i gezeirah shema yachzor hadavar lekilkulo} \u8212- the fear that conditions may revert. We keep the old practice in place.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But then, why are two messengers exempt? The Gemara answers: {\i bei trei d\u8217'maisei gita milta d\u8217'lo shechicha} \u8212- two people bringing a {\i get} together is an unusual occurrence, and the Rabbis did not apply their decree to unusual circumstances. {\i Milta d\u8217'lo shechicha lo gazru bah rabbanan}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Woman Compare\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara immediately challenges this with the familiar case: the woman who brings her own {\i get} is also unusual \u8212- {\i d\u8217'lo shechicha} \u8212- yet she must still declare. Why is two messengers different?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Testimony Paradigm\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The resolution, spelled out by Tosafot, rests on a fundamental distinction between {\i shlichus} and {\i edus}. In a standard case of one {\i shaliach}, the paradigm is purely one of agency: a single individual is empowered and sent. We do not want to make distinctions within that {\i shlichus} paradigm \u8212- {\i shelo tachlok b\u8217'shlichus d\u8217'yachol} \u8212- so we apply the requirement uniformly to all who are capable. The woman who brings her own {\i get}, unusual as that is, still falls squarely within the {\i shlichus} paradigm, and therefore {\i lo plug} applies.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Two people bringing a {\i get} together is qualitatively different. It is not merely an unusual instantiation of the standard {\i shlichus} model; it has the character of {\i edus} \u8212- testimony \u8212- rather than pure agency. When two people bring a {\i get}, the {\i get} delivery functions more like testimonial validation than like an act of {\i shlichus} alone. They are, to be sure, still {\i shlichim} in the sense that they are empowered to deliver, but the dual presence transforms the act into something that resembles a testimonial proceeding. It falls outside the standard {\i shlichus} paradigm entirely, and therefore {\i gazrinan chad d\u8217'lo shechiach atu chad d\u8217'shechiach \u8212- bei terei atu chad lo gazrinan}: we extend the decree from common cases of one messenger to less common ones, but we do not extend it to the fundamentally different case of two messengers together.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Yehoshua Lishmah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yehoshua Proof\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now identifies the Rabba-Rava debate as a parallel to the {\i machloket} between Rabbi Yochanan and Rabbi Yehoshua ben Levi \u8212- one holding {\i lishmah}, the other holding {\i ein eidim metzuyim lekaymo}. The Gemara seeks to determine which {\i amora} held which position. It concludes that Rabbi Yehoshua ben Levi is the one who said {\i lishmah}, on the basis of the following incident: Rabbi Shimon bar Abba brought a {\i get} before Rabbi Yehoshua ben Levi and asked whether he still needed to declare {\i bifnei nichtav u\u8217'bifnei nechtam}. Rabbi Yehoshua ben Levi replied: you do not need to. {\i Lo amru ela b\u8217'doros harishonim}, it was only required in the earlier generations {\i she\u8217'ein beki\u8217'in lishmah} \u8212- when people were not expert in the laws of {\i lishmah}. But in these later generations, {\i d\u8217'beki\u8217'in lishmah}, when everyone knows the law, no declaration is required. This is the source of the Gemara\u8217's earlier phrase {\i l\u8217'achar shelamdu}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Contradiction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This ruling of Rabbi Yehoshua ben Levi seems to create a problem. We concluded above that everyone \u8212- including Rabba \u8212- agrees that the signature-validation concern is real and cannot be dismissed. If that is so, then even {\i l\u8217'achar shelamdu}, when the {\i lishmah} concern lapses, there should still be a requirement to make the declaration on account of {\i ein eidim metzuyim lekaymo}. And we further concluded that even after {\i l\u8217'achar shelamdu}, the old practice is maintained {\i lechatchila} because of {\i gezeirah shema yachzor}. Yet Rabbi Yehoshua ben Levi told Rabbi Shimon bar Abba simply: you need not declare at all. This sounds like a {\i lishmah}-only position that recognizes no remaining concern once {\i lishmah} is resolved \u8212- which seems to contradict everything the Gemara established.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two Messenger Implicit\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara resolves this in one stroke: {\i Rav Shimon bar Abba inish achrini havah bahadei} \u8212- Rabbi Shimon bar Abba was not alone; there was another person accompanying him. The {\i get} was being brought by two messengers together. The other person\u8217's presence is not mentioned in the account, as the Gemara explains, {\i mishum kevodo d\u8217'Rav Shimon} \u8212- out of respect for Rabbi Shimon bar Abba, the narrative focuses on him rather than noting the unnamed companion.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Resolution Unusual\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This resolves everything. When two people bring a {\i get} together, we already established that this is a {\i milta d\u8217'lo shechicha} \u8212- an unusual case falling outside the standard {\i shlichus} paradigm \u8212- and the Rabbis did not apply their decree to unusual cases. Therefore, Rabbi Yehoshua ben Levi\u8217's ruling that no declaration was needed does not reflect a {\i lishmah}-only position that ignores signature concerns. It reflects the specific exemption for two-messenger cases. Rabbi Yehoshua ben Levi is perfectly consistent with the Gemara\u8217's overall framework: the signature concern remains valid, the old practice is maintained {\i lechatchila}, but two messengers are exempt because they constitute an unusual case that the {\i gezeirah} never covered. The apparent contradiction dissolves.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Witness Numbers\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two vs Three\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now turns to a new question: {\i bifnei kamah notna lah} \u8212- in front of how many people must the {\i shaliach} deliver the {\i get} when making his declaration? Since the {\i shaliach} is testifying, presumably at least two witnesses must be present. But exactly how many? Rabbi Yochanan and Rabbi Chanina debate the matter, one holding {\i bifnei shenayim} \u8212- before two \u8212- and the other holding {\i bifnei sheloshah} \u8212- before three.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rachavei Proof\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara determines which {\i amora} held which position. An incident is cited: Rachavei bar Rav Chisda brought a {\i get} before Rabbi Yochanan, and Rabbi Yochanan instructed him, {\i zil havah lah b\u8217'apei trei} \u8212- go and deliver it to her in the presence of two \u8212- and say to them, {\i bifnei nichtav u\u8217'bifnei nechtam}. This demonstrates clearly that Rabbi Yochanan holds {\i bifnei shenayim} \u8212- two witnesses suffice.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Theory Initial\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The natural inference is that the debate of two versus three maps onto the underlying debate of {\i lishmah} versus signature validation. One might suggest: the position requiring two witnesses corresponds to the {\i lishmah} account, since a simple testimony before two witnesses is all that is needed to establish that proper intent was observed. The position requiring three corresponds to the signature-validation account, since validating signatures ({\i kiyum shtaros}) is done before a {\i beis din} of three.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Theory Rejected\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This mapping, however, does not survive scrutiny. We established above that Rabbi Yochanan himself is the one who holds {\i ein eidim metzuyim lekaymo} \u8212- signature validation \u8212- while Rabbi Yehoshua ben Levi holds {\i lishmah}. Moreover, we concluded that {\i Rabba is lei d\u8217'Rava} \u8212- everyone agrees at some level that signature validation is a concern. If signature validation requires a court of three, how can Rabbi Yochanan, who holds that the requirement is about signatures, rule that two suffice?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shliach Eid Dayan\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara therefore proposes a different basis for the debate. The question is not about the underlying rationale for the requirement, but about the legal mechanics of validation. Everyone agrees that signature validation is necessary. The debate concerns whether {\i shaliach na\u8217'aseh eid v\u8217'eid na\u8217'aseh dayan} \u8212- whether the same individual can first act as the {\i shaliach} delivering the {\i get}, then switch roles to serve as a witness validating the signatures, and then switch roles again to serve as a {\i dayan} on the {\i beis din} that adjudicates his own testimony.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This triple role-shifting is the crux. The {\i shaliach} is delivering the {\i get}: that is his agency role. He simultaneously declares {\i bifnei nichtav u\u8217'bifnei nechtam}: that is his testimonial role \u8212- {\i shaliach na\u8217'aseh eid}. Now if he can also count as a {\i dayan} for the purpose of adjudicating his own testimony \u8212- {\i eid na\u8217'aseh dayan} \u8212- then the three-person {\i beis din} needed for {\i kiyum shtaros} can be constituted with only two additional people: the {\i shaliach} himself counts as one, and the two people before whom he delivers the {\i get} count as two, totaling three. Hence delivery before two people suffices.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If, however, {\i ein eid na\u8217'aseh dayan} \u8212- the witness cannot serve as a {\i dayan} for his own testimony \u8212- then the {\i shaliach}\u8217's counting as a {\i dayan} is impermissible, and three additional people are needed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Eid Dayan Rabbinic\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara notes that the accepted {\i halacha} is: {\i b\u8217'drabanan, eid na\u8217'aseh dayan} \u8212- in the domain of rabbinic enactments, a witness may indeed serve as a {\i dayan} for his own testimony. Since {\i kiyum shtaros} for a {\i get} delivered from abroad is a rabbinic requirement, the ruling should universally permit delivery before two, and the debate should collapse. The Gemara therefore looks for a different basis for the remaining dispute.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Woman Dayan Risk\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The debate, the Gemara concludes, concerns a concern about practical confusion. According to the view that requires three, the reasoning is: since a woman is valid to serve as a {\i shliach} and bring a {\i get}, if we establish a universal practice of delivering before two \u8212- relying on the {\i shaliach} himself counting as the third \u8212- people may eventually forget the nuance and begin relying on a woman to serve in that same counting role. A woman, however, cannot be a {\i dayan}, even for rabbinic proceedings. If she brings the {\i get} and people count her as the {\i shaliach}-turned-{\i eid}-turned-{\i dayan}, the {\i kiyum} will be defective. To prevent this confusion, the view of three insists that the required three people be present exclusively as witnesses and {\i dayanim}, separate from the {\i shaliach}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The opposing view \u8212- Rabbi Yochanan\u8217's view of two \u8212- holds that {\i ishah meida yadiah velo samchi alah}: people know that a woman cannot serve as a {\i dayan} and will not make that error. Therefore, the nuance that the {\i shaliach} counts toward the three does not risk being misapplied.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Failure Remedy\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two Witnesses Accepted\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i braita} teaches the established practice: delivery before two witnesses suffices, in accordance with Rabbi Yochanan. The {\i braita} presents a case in which the {\i shaliach} delivered the {\i get} and gave it to the woman but did not declare {\i bifnei nichtav u\u8217'bifnei nechtam}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Meir Stringency\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Meir rules: {\i yotzi ve-havalad mamzer} \u8212- the husband must divorce her and any child born of a subsequent remarriage is a {\i mamzer}. The failure to declare invalidates the {\i get}, at least rabbinically, and the consequences are correspondingly severe. Rabbi Meir brooks no flexibility: the absence of the declaration, for whatever reason, is an irremediable defect. Even if she has already remarried and has children, the {\i mamzer} status follows.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sages Leniency\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Sages disagree sharply: {\i ein havalad mamzer} \u8212- the child is emphatically not a {\i mamzer}. The failure to declare is a {\i lechatchila} deficiency, not a fundamental invalidation of the {\i get}. The Sages then address the practical question of how to remedy the situation {\i lechatchila} for a woman who has received a {\i get} without the declaration and wishes to remarry: {\i yitlenu mimenu, v\u8217'yachzir v\u8217'yitnena lah bifnei shenayim, v\u8217'yomar bifnei nichtav u\u8217'bifnei nechtam} \u8212- the {\i get} is taken back from her, and the {\i shaliach} redelivers it in the presence of two, this time making the declaration. Since the declaration must accompany the act of delivery, a redelivery with proper declaration remedies the deficiency.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might ask: if she is already divorced by the first delivery \u8212- which is valid {\i d\u8217'oraisa} \u8212- what is the point of redelivering? The answer employs a {\i mima nafshach} logic: if rabbinically the first delivery is not considered fully valid because the declaration was omitted, then rabbinically she is not yet divorced, and a second delivery with the declaration completes the rabbinic requirement. If {\i d\u8217'oraisa} she was already divorced by the first delivery, then certainly the second delivery causes no harm. Either way, the redelivery cures the problem {\i lechatchila}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Timing Declaration\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot raises the question of when precisely the declaration must be made. The {\i braita}\u8217's remedy of {\i yitlenu mimenu} \u8212- taking the {\i get} back from her and redelivering it \u8212- implies that the declaration must be contemporaneous with the act of delivery itself. One cannot simply say the words the next day while the {\i get} sits in the woman\u8217's possession. But how narrowly must \u8220"contemporaneous\u8221" be defined?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot anchors this from the case on the preceding daf: the {\i pike\u8217'ach shenishtatek} case, where the {\i shaliach} gave the {\i get} and became a deaf-mute before completing the declaration. That case implies that a moment after delivery \u8212- but still {\i toch kedei dibbur} \u8212- is acceptable, since the {\i shaliach} was in the process of speaking when he was stricken. Tosafot concludes: during delivery, certainly valid; immediately after delivery within {\i toch kedei dibbur} ({\i peirush b\u8217'shlichus d\u8217'yachol}), also valid; any time after {\i kedei dibbur}, no longer valid as a contemporaneous declaration, and the {\i get} must be redelivered.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 As for the scene suggested by {\i yitlenu mimenu}, Tosafot observes that the language implies the {\i get} is still in her hands at the moment of taking it back \u8212- the parties are apparently still standing together, still engaged in the transaction. Once they have dispersed and the moment of the giving has fully passed, the declaration cannot be retroactively attached to it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What remains unresolved, notes Tosafot, is whether the declaration is valid if made somewhat after {\i kedei dibbur} but while the parties are still actively engaged in discussing the {\i get}. The {\i Ri} remains uncertain \u8212- {\i mesapka lei l\u8217'Ri} \u8212- whether continued engagement with the subject matter of the {\i get} constitutes a prolonged moment of delivery. And similarly, whether a declaration made {\i before} the physical act of giving \u8212- moments before rather than during \u8212- would satisfy the requirement is also left unresolved.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Meir Rationale\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara explains Rabbi Meir\u8217's position by citing a principle attributed to him by Rav Hamnuna in the name of Ulla. Rabbi Meir used to say: {\i kol hameshaneh mi-matbe\u8217'a she-tav\u8217'u chakhamim b\u8217'gittin, yotzi ve-havalad mamzer} \u8212- anyone who deviates from the established mold ({\i matbe\u8217'a}) that the Sages coined for {\i gittin}, must have the woman divorce and any child of remarriage is a {\i mamzer}. For Rabbi Meir, the Sages established a precise and non-negotiable form for the {\i get} process. Any deviation from that form \u8212- including the failure to make the required declaration \u8212- is treated as a fundamental departure from the mold, with all its consequences. There is no {\i bedi\u8217'eved} leniency that mitigates the severity of the deviation. Any step outside the established {\i matbe\u8217'a} carries the full consequence.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Gittin 6\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rabbah & Rava Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 lishmah vs.\u160 ?Signatures\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Our daf begins with a brief but essential summary of the debate developed in the preceding pages. The dispute between Rabbah and Rava concerns the reason for the requirement that a {\i shaliach} delivering a {\i get} from {\i medinat hayam} must declare {\i bifani nechtav u\u8217'bifani nechtam} \u8212- \u8220"it was written in my presence and signed in my presence.\u8221" Rabbah\u8217's position focuses on {\i lishmah}: the declaration ensures that the proper intention required for a {\i get} \u8212- that it be written for the sake of this particular husband and wife \u8212- was indeed observed. This concern is reflected primarily in the phrase {\i bifani nechtav}, in my presence during the writing. Rava, by contrast, understands the declaration as a mechanism for validating the signatures, preventing the husband from later claiming the document was a forgery. His position is reflected more in {\i bifani nechtam}, in my presence during the signing.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Both Positions Align\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} ultimately concludes that the distinction between these two positions is less stark than it initially appears. Rabbah, it emerges, is {\i also} concerned with the validation rationale that Rava emphasizes \u8212- he holds both reasons simultaneously. Rava, for his part, is concerned only with validation. Moreover, the {\i gemara} noted that since the laws of {\i lishmah} had come to be well known even in {\i medinat hayam}, Rabbah\u8217's {\i lishmah} concern was effectively reduced to a {\i lechatchila} standard. The result is that the two positions converge almost entirely in practice. With this summary established, the {\i gemara} now proceeds to examine the concrete details of how {\i bifani nechtav u\u8217'bifani nechtam} operates across a range of circumstances.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Bar Hadaya Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Every Letter Present\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} introduces the case of Bar Hadaya, who wished to bring a {\i get} and came before Rabbi Achi, who had been appointed to oversee the proper execution of {\i gittin} in that city. As Rashi notes, the principle that one who does not know the intricacies of {\i gittin} and {\i kiddushin} should have no dealings with them demanded that a figure of genuine authority supervise the process. Rabbi Achi instructed Bar Hadaya: {\i tzarich at l\u8217'amod al kol ot ve\u8217'ot} \u8212- you must stand present as every single letter of the {\i get} is written, from the first stroke through the final signature. The requirement of {\i bifani nechtav} thus raises an immediate question: what does \u8220"in my presence\u8221" actually demand? How present must the {\i shaliach} be throughout the writing?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Leniency & Chumras\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Bar Hadaya brought this question to Rabbi Ammi and Rabbi Assi, who ruled that Rabbi Achi\u8217's requirement was excessive \u8212- the {\i shaliach} need not be present for every single letter. One might nonetheless argue: even if it is not strictly required, why not adopt Rabbi Achi\u8217's stricter standard as a {\i chumra}? The {\i gemara} answers this decisively: {\i nimtza ata motzi la\u8217'az al gittin harishonim} \u8212- by insisting on a heightened standard, one implicitly casts ill repute upon all earlier {\i gittin} that were executed without such care. People will hear that one was {\i makpid} to supervise every letter and will begin to question the validity of documents delivered by {\i shelichim} who did not observe this practice. This is a critical principle about the real costs of unnecessary stringency: a {\i chumra} is never without consequences, and those consequences often fall upon others.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Earlier Gittin Concern\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The concern for {\i gittin harishonim} \u8212- not besmirching past practice \u8212- serves as an independent brake on the impulse toward stringency in this domain. Where the practical effect of stricter behavior is to impugn what has already been done and accepted, the {\i gemara} treats that as a sufficient reason to refrain.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Partial Presence\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 One Line Suffices\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} next records the case of Rabbah bar bar Chana, who brought a {\i get} in which half had been written in his presence and half had not \u8212- the {\i gemara} does not specify which half. He came before Rabbi Elazar, who ruled: {\i afilu lo katav bo ela shita achat lishmah shuv eino tzarich} \u8212- even if only one line was written {\i lishmah} in his presence, that is sufficient. The ruling is noteworthy for its invocation of {\i lishmah}, addressing the Rabbah dimension of the requirement. The reasoning would seem to be that if the {\i sofer} was observed declaring the proper intent for even one line, one may assume that the {\i sofer} understood and maintained that intent throughout. Tosafot explains the case somewhat differently: the reference to one line means the {\i shaliach} saw the first line being written, and from that beginning one may assume {\i lishmah} was maintained through the end. That reading is perhaps less straightforward than the plain sense of the {\i gemara}, which speaks of \u8220"any one line\u8221" and grants sufficiency regardless of position in the document.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hearing Quill/Klaf\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Ashi goes further still, requiring even less than one line of direct observation. {\i Afilu kan kulmusa vekan megilta} \u8212- even if one merely heard the preparation of the quill and the {\i klaf} being readied, that is sufficient. Rashi\u8217's variant reading is {\i kal kulmusa}, the sound of the quill, and he explains: if one heard the preparation and, in that context, heard them declaring that this was being done {\i lishmah} \u8212- for the sake of the husband and wife \u8212- even that degree of auditory awareness constitutes sufficient presence. The word {\i bifani} is thus stretched considerably: one did not see the writing, one did not witness the writing itself, one may not even have been present during the writing \u8212- and yet the awareness created by hearing the preparation in context suffices.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Medinat Hayam Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i baraita} is cited in support of Rav Ashi\u8217's position. A {\i shaliach} bringing a {\i get} from {\i medinat hayam}: even if he was downstairs and the {\i sofer} was upstairs, or the {\i sofer} downstairs and the {\i shaliach} upstairs, and even if he was going in and out throughout the entire day \u8212- {\i kasher}. The document is valid and the declaration {\i bifani nechtav} may be made.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Bifani Meaning\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} pauses to interrogate the {\i baraita}. If the {\i sofer} is on the upper floor and the {\i shaliach} is below, he cannot see anything. How does {\i bifani} \u8212- in my presence \u8212- apply? The {\i gemara} answers: {\i ela lav d\u8217'shama kan kulmusa vekan megilta} \u8212- it must mean that he could hear the activity upstairs, that there was some form of awareness even without visual contact. {\i Bifani}, in my presence, encompasses this kind of ambient awareness. It is also worth observing that this {\i baraita} does not mention {\i lishmah} at all. Consistent with the conclusion that Rabbah\u8217's concern is ultimately reducible in practical terms, and that the {\i lishmah} issue is primarily {\i lechatchila}, the {\i baraita} appears to address the requirement without any {\i lishmah} qualification.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 In & Out Motion\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sofer Marketplace\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now examines the phrase from the {\i baraita} \u8212- {\i afilu nichnas veyotzei kol hayom kulo kasher}, even going in and out all day, it is valid \u8212- and asks: who is going in and out? If it refers to the {\i shaliach}, the question answers itself: since even physical separation of floors is permitted, surely the case where the {\i shaliach} steps in and out of the room where the {\i sofer} is present poses no difficulty. That case is obvious and needs no ruling. The {\i gemara} therefore concludes that the {\i baraita} must be referring to the {\i sofer} \u8212- the scribe who steps out while the {\i shaliach} remains. The ruling then tells us that even when the {\i sofer} leaves the premises and returns, the {\i shaliach} may still declare {\i bifani nechtav}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Distraction Concern\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But even this seems obvious \u8212- why would the {\i sofer} stepping out invalidate anything? The {\i gemara} answers: {\i lo tzricha} \u8212- the {\i baraita}\u8217's ruling is needed for a specific case. {\i D\u8217'nafik l\u8217'shuka ve\u8217'asa} \u8212- the {\i sofer} went out to the marketplace and returned. One might have thought that while in the marketplace, another person approached him and asked him to write a {\i get} for his own wife. If, when returning, the {\i sofer} carried that second commission in mind, his {\i lishmah} intent may have become confused. The {\i baraita} teaches us that we are not concerned for this possibility. We note that this concern again touches on {\i lishmah} \u8212- suggesting that the question of intent remains relevant in evaluating the {\i sofer}\u8217's conduct throughout the writing, even as the evidentiary threshold for the {\i shaliach}\u8217's own presence has been set quite low.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Bavel\u8217's Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav vs.\u160 ?Shmuel\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now turns from the mechanics of {\i bifani nechtav} to the geographic question of which regions require it at all. The {\i mishnah} distinguished between Eretz Yisrael, where no declaration is needed, and {\i medinat hayam}, where it is. The question now is: what is the status of Bavel? At the time of the {\i Bavli}, Bavel was not a marginal outpost but the center of Jewish life, home to the majority of the Jewish population. The debate is presented thus: {\i Rav amar k\u8217'Eretz Yisrael l\u8217'gittin} \u8212- Rav holds that Bavel has the same status as Eretz Yisrael, and no declaration is required when transmitting a {\i get} from one location to another within Bavel. {\i Shmuel amar k\u8217'chutza la\u8217'aretz} \u8212- Shmuel holds it is like the diaspora, and the declaration is required.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two Explanations\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} initially proposes that this debate maps onto the Rabbah-Rava dispute: perhaps Rav focuses on {\i lishmah} and holds that Bavel\u8217's Torah knowledge is sufficient, while Shmuel focuses on {\i edim metzuyim lekaymo} \u8212- the availability of witnesses to validate signatures \u8212- and holds that travel between Babylonian cities is insufficient to ensure such availability. The {\i gemara} rejects this framing, however, on the grounds that Rabbah himself also holds of Rava\u8217's position. Everyone must address the question of witness availability; the {\i lishmah} issue alone cannot account for the debate. The {\i gemara} therefore reframes: {\i ela d\u8217'kulei alma edim metzuyim lekaymo} \u8212- everyone agrees the concern is about witness availability. The debate is about whether that concern is adequately answered in Bavel. Rav holds that because there are {\i yeshivot} in Bavel, there is constant travel from city to city; an industry of movement grows up around the great academies, and one can assume witnesses to signatures will be available when needed. Shmuel counters: {\i misiftei girsaihu tridi} \u8212- the {\i yeshiva} students are absorbed in their learning. The existence of academies does not translate into the kind of commercial and civic movement through which people become familiar with one another\u8217's signatures. There may be travel, but not the kind that ensures witnesses for {\i kiyum shtaros}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Huna Statement\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} adds a tradition: {\i amar Rabbi Abba amar Rava amar Rav Huna asinu atzmeinu be-Vavel ke-Eretz Yisrael le-gittin} \u8212- we have established in Bavel that we treat ourselves as Eretz Yisrael for matters of {\i gittin}, from the time Rav arrived in Bavel. The same Rav whose ruling opened this section is identified as the pivotal figure: it was his arrival that transformed Bavel\u8217's practical status.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rashi: Yeshivot\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rashi explains: from the time Rav came to Bavel and established a {\i yeshiva} in Sura \u8212- while Shmuel\u8217's {\i yeshiva} was in Nehardea \u8212- there arose a permanent institutional framework driving movement throughout the region. The smaller communities flocked to these centers on days of Torah study; students traveled between Sura and Nehardea; traffic permeated the Babylonian Jewish world. It was this development, in Rashi\u8217's reading, that changed Bavel\u8217's status \u8212- not a pre-existing feature of the landscape, but an institutional transformation that Rav himself created. Although Torah had always been present in Bavel \u8212- Hillel himself came from there, and the exile of Yechanya brought {\i talmidei chachamim} in the aftermath of the first destruction \u8212- what Rav and Shmuel established was something qualitatively new: fixed, high-capacity academies generating the kind of constant movement that ensures witness availability.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Yirmiya Challenge\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Borders & Geography\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yirmiya challenges the foregoing from the {\i mishnah} itself. The {\i mishnah} enumerates the outer borders of Eretz Yisrael: Rekem to the east, Ashkelon to the south, Akko to the north \u8212- each of these border cities being treated as outside Eretz Yisrael for the laws of {\i gittin}. And we know from the {\i pesukim} that Bavel lies to the north of Eretz Yisrael: {\i mi-tzafon tipasach ha-ra\u8217'ah}, the evil shall open from the north \u8212- that was the prophecy of Bavel\u8217's destruction of the {\i Beis HaMikdash}. Moreover, the {\i mishnah} states that Rabbi Meir considers Akko equivalent to Eretz Yisrael for {\i gittin}, and even the sages who disagree with Rabbi Meir do so specifically because Akko is distant from the center \u8212- {\i d\u8217'merachka hi}. If even a border city like Akko requires debate, Bavel \u8212- which is far more distant \u8212- would certainly be treated as {\i chutz la\u8217'aretz}. How, then, can one declare that Bavel has the status of Eretz Yisrael?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Exception Principle\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yirmiya poses his own answer: {\i le-var mi-Bavel} \u8212- the mishnah\u8217's geographic framework applies to everything {\i other than} Bavel. Bavel is a distinct entity unto itself, not simply subsumed under the general category of {\i chutz la\u8217'aretz}. This resolution points to something structurally important. Just as {\i medinat hayam} had its own defined identity in the {\i mishnah}, Bavel \u8212- as Tosafot notes from the opening of the tractate \u8212- has its own category. One might object that since the changed status resulted from Rav\u8217's arrival, which postdates the {\i mishnah}, the {\i mishnah} cannot be used to challenge it. The answer would be that even before Rav, the {\i mishnah}\u8217's framework implicitly allowed for such an exception \u8212- or alternatively, as Tosafot proposes, the status was always there, waiting to be articulated.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Bavel\u8217's Status (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafot Alternative\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot challenges Rashi\u8217's reading of the Rav Huna statement. If Bavel\u8217's status genuinely {\i changed} when Rav arrived \u8212- if it was formerly {\i chutz la\u8217'aretz} and became like Eretz Yisrael \u8212- then why does the {\i gemara} treat a challenge from the {\i mishnah} as meaningful? The {\i mishnah} predates Rav\u8217's arrival; of course it reflects the older reality. Tosafot therefore cites Rabbeinu Tam\u8217's alternative: when Rav arrived in Bavel and taught that Bavel has the status of Eretz Yisrael for {\i gittin}, he was not announcing a change but transmitting a pre-existing {\i halacha} \u8212- grounded in the foundational status of the exile of Yechanya and his {\i talmidei chachamim} \u8212- that Bavel had always carried this distinction. On this reading, there was no change in status, only a clarification of a status that always existed, which is why the {\i mishnah} remains a relevant source to engage. The {\i gemara} sounds more naturally like Rashi\u8217's reading, but the motivation behind Tosafot\u8217's approach is clear: to preserve the coherence of the {\i gemara}\u8217's dialectic.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Bavel Boundaries\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yuchasin Parallel\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having established that Bavel has a distinct status, the {\i gemara} turns to the question of its precise geographic boundaries. {\i Ad heichan hi Bavel?} \u8212- how far does Bavel extend? Rav Papa rules: {\i k\u8217'machloket l\u8217'yuchasin kach machloket l\u8217'gittin} \u8212- whatever debate exists in tractate {\i Kiddushin} about the boundaries of Bavel for purposes of {\i yuchasin} \u8212- personal status and the reliability of genealogical records \u8212- the same boundaries apply for {\i gittin}. The considerations differ: {\i yuchasin} concerns where records were kept and personal status is most assured, while {\i gittin} concerns the availability of witnesses to validate signatures. One might ask whether these two criteria necessarily produce identical borders. Perhaps the {\i gemara}\u8217's point is that for practical coherence \u8212- {\i kedei shelo tachalok} \u8212- a unified standard is preferable even if the underlying rationales do not map perfectly.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Bridge Arch Limit\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Yosef disagrees. The debate in {\i Kiddushin} applies to {\i yuchasin}, but for {\i gittin} there is a different, universally agreed-upon boundary: {\i ad arba tinyana d\u8217'gashra} \u8212- until the second arch of the bridge. The {\i gemara} understood which bridge and which direction this referred to, and that specific landmark demarcates the extent of Bavel for purposes of the {\i bifani nechtav} exemption.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Aktisphon Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 One-Way Requirement\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Chisda introduces an asymmetric case: he required the declaration {\i bifani nechtav} for a {\i get} traveling from Aktisphon to Bei Ardashir, but not in the reverse direction. Two cities are thus in a relationship where one requires the declaration going one way but not the other. At first glance, one might explain this the same way Eretz Yisrael and {\i medinat hayam} differ: perhaps Aktisphon does not know {\i lishmah} and Bei Ardashir does, so when a {\i get} arrives from Aktisphon, Bei Ardashir requires the declaration to ensure proper intent, while the reverse is unnecessary. This explanation would work cleanly if {\i lishmah} were the only concern.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Marketplace Effect\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} rejects this framing, again invoking the established principle that Rava holds of Rabbah \u8212- everyone must address the witness validation concern. The explanation instead focuses on commercial patterns: {\i vahani kimeizali beshuka lehasam hanach yadei bechasimeit yadei dehani} \u8212- the residents of one city go to the marketplace of the other. When people from city A regularly travel to buy and sell in city B, the residents of city B become familiar with the signatures of city A\u8217's residents through the commercial documents \u8212- IOUs, bills of sale \u8212- that the travelers sign while there. The buyers sign; they go home; the sellers of city B are left holding those documents and develop familiarity with those signatures. City B therefore knows city A\u8217's signatures and can perform {\i kiyum shtaros} if necessary. The reverse is not true: {\i vahani behanach lo yadei} \u8212- city A does not accumulate familiarity with city B\u8217's signatures, because city A\u8217's residents are busy with their own transactions when in city B and return home without a comparable record. {\i Mai ta\u8217'ama beshuka\u8217'i teridei} \u8212- they are preoccupied with the marketplace itself. This asymmetry in commercial traffic explains the asymmetry in the {\i halachic} requirement.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Neighborhood Cases\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Reshus Division\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav bar Avuha extends the logic further, requiring the {\i bifani nechtav} declaration even between opposite sides of a {\i reshus harabim} \u8212- a major thoroughfare. The reasoning, as Tosafot explains clearly, is that one is aware of the movements of those in one\u8217's immediate vicinity. If the witnesses to a {\i get} live on the same side of the street as the wife and her community, she and those around her will know if those witnesses are about to relocate, and can arrange {\i kiyum shtaros} in advance. But if the witnesses live across the major thoroughfare \u8212- the other side of the tracks, so to speak \u8212- that awareness is absent. Their departure may go unnoticed until it is too late to validate the signatures. The transience of population, and the limits of what one notices about one\u8217's neighbors, thus drives a requirement that would seem geographically trivial.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Neighborhood Division\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Sheshes goes further: the declaration is required even between different neighborhoods within the same side of such a divide. The principle remains the same \u8212- one tracks the movements of those most proximate \u8212- but the zone of reliable awareness is narrowed further. Even within the same broad residential area, if the witnesses and the wife come from distinct neighborhoods, the risk of unnoticed departure applies.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Transience Principle\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava extends the requirement to within a single neighborhood itself. The transience of urban population is so pronounced that even neighbors may not know one another\u8217's plans, and a witness could depart without warning even to those living in the same {\i shechuna}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mechuza Context\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} qualifies Rava\u8217's position: {\i shani benei Mechuza de\u8217'naidi} \u8212- Rava spoke from his own experience in Mechuza, a cosmopolitan commercial center with an unusually mobile population. His ruling applies specifically to cities of that character. The earlier, less sweeping positions reflect the realities of less transient communities, where one\u8217's neighborhood still provides meaningful notice of departures.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Modern Application\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot draws the practical conclusion directly from Rava\u8217's ruling and its grounding in transience: in all places of the medieval period, where Jewish populations were similarly mobile and cosmopolitan, one should always declare {\i bifani nechtav u\u8217'bifani nechtam}. Even though we rule like Rava that the concern is {\i edim metzuyim lekaymo} rather than {\i lishmah}, and even if the community is learned enough that {\i lishmah} poses no issue, the transience of population means the witness concern always applies. Everywhere in the world of the {\i Rishonim} resembles Mechuza, and the declaration should always be made.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rav Kahana Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 No Need Statement\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} relates that Rav Kahana brought a {\i get} \u8212- Rav Chanin reported the incident \u8212- and did not know whether it had traveled from Sura to Nehardea or from Nehardea to Sura; both were major centers of Torah scholarship and, as established, of constant travel. He came before Rav and asked whether he needed to say {\i bifani nechtav u\u8217'bifani nechtam}. Rav ruled: {\i lo tzarichat} \u8212- you do not need to, because Bavel has the status of Eretz Yisrael for {\i gittin}, and the dense travel between these two great academies ensures that witness availability is not a concern.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 If Said, Works\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} adds: {\i ve\u8217'ee avad mahni} \u8212- and if one did say it, it works. This is not a trivial addendum. Even in cases where the declaration is technically unnecessary, making it protects the woman: if the husband were to later challenge the {\i get}, the declared {\i bifani nechtav u\u8217'bifani nechtam} ensures that his objection will not be entertained. According to Rashi, the risk in the absence of the declaration is that the husband\u8217's challenge could actually succeed in invalidating the {\i get}; according to Tosafot, the concern is rather the {\i la\u8217'az} \u8212- the cloud of suspicion \u8212- that such a challenge would create. Either way, the declaration, when made even unnecessarily, provides the woman with meaningful protection, and its use should be encouraged wherever possible.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Kfar Sisai Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Witness Prevention\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i baraita} is cited: someone came before Rabbi Yishmael with a {\i get} he had brought from Kfar Sisai. He asked whether he needed to say {\i bifani nechtav u\u8217'bifani nechtam}. Rabbi Yishmael asked where he was from; upon learning it was Kfar Sisai, he instructed him: you must say it, {\i shelo tizakak l\u8217'edim} \u8212- so that she should not need to resort to witnesses. The framing is notable: the obligation is presented not as an inherent requirement of Kfar Sisai\u8217's status, but as a protective measure that spares the woman from having to produce witnesses.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ilai Challenge\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 After the {\i shaliach} departed, Rabbi Ilai entered and challenged Rabbi Yishmael: Kfar Sisai is swallowed within the borders of Eretz Yisrael \u8212- it is closer to Tzipori than Akko is, and Tzipori was a central location. The {\i mishnah} records that Rabbi Meir considers Akko itself to be like Eretz Yisrael for {\i gittin}, and even the sages who dispute Rabbi Meir do so only because Akko is distant \u8212- {\i d\u8217'merachka hi} \u8212- while Kfar Sisai is close. If Akko\u8217's proximity was sufficient for Rabbi Meir, Kfar Sisai\u8217's greater proximity should be sufficient for everyone. Why then require the declaration?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Heter Established\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yishmael responded: {\i shtok bni shtok} \u8212- hush, my son, hush. {\i Ho\u8217'il v\u8217'yatza davar b\u8217'heter yatza} \u8212- since the matter went forth as a {\i heter}, as a permission, let it stand. That is: the declaration was instructed not because it was technically required, but because making it activates the protective mechanism. Since the {\i shaliach} was told to say it and it is effective when said, the woman is now protected. Even if it need not have been required, the fact that it was said and is halachically operative means it serves its purpose.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Incomplete Hearing\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} explains Rabbi Ilai\u8217's challenge: {\i lo siyem kamei} \u8212- Rabbi Ilai had not heard the complete statement Rabbi Yishmael made. He heard only the instruction to say {\i bifani nechtav u\u8217'bifani nechtam} and reacted before hearing the rationale \u8212- {\i shelo tizakak l\u8217'edim}, so that she not need witnesses \u8212- which would have clarified that this was protective guidance, not a statement of genuine requirement. Having heard only the ruling and not its explanation, Rabbi Ilai assumed Rabbi Yishmael was declaring Kfar Sisai to require the declaration on its own terms, and challenged accordingly.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Avyasar Statement\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Travel Leniency\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now turns to an entirely different axis of the question: not within Bavel or within Eretz Yisrael, but between them. Rav Avyasar sent a ruling to Rav Chisda: {\i gittin habaim misham l\u8217'kan} \u8212- {\i gittin} coming from there, Bavel, to here, Eretz Yisrael, {\i ein tzarich lomar bifanei nechtav u\u8217'bifanei nechtam}. No declaration is required. One might have thought that since these are two distinct regions \u8212- whatever their internal status \u8212- the movement between them would require the standard declaration applied to {\i medinat hayam}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Travel Rationale\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} again notes that a {\i lishmah}-only explanation is insufficient, since everyone holds of Rava\u8217's position requiring witness availability. The answer: {\i keivan d\u8217'ika rabim d\u8217'salki v\u8217'nechti} \u8212- since there are many who go up and come down, making {\i aliyah} and {\i yerida} between Bavel and Eretz Yisrael \u8212- primarily for purposes of Torah study \u8212- {\i mishkach shchichi}, witnesses are readily available. The volume of travel between the two great centers of Jewish life is so substantial that one can rely on finding someone who can perform {\i kiyum shtaros} when needed. The same logic that makes Bavel equivalent to Eretz Yisrael internally extends to the corridor between them.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Yosef Challenge\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Trust Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Yosef raises a pointed objection to Rav Avyasar\u8217's ruling: {\i man lima lan d\u8217'Rav Avyasar bar samcha hu?} \u8212- who says that Rav Avyasar is a reliable authority? Since his ruling is a {\i kula} \u8212- a leniency, removing a requirement \u8212- the question of his credibility is directly relevant.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Harsh Missive\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Yosef continues: this is the same Rav Avyasar who sent a harsh letter \u8212- apparently from Eretz Yisrael to Rav Yehuda in Bavel \u8212- condemning {\i bnei adam haolim misham l\u8217'kan}, the Torah scholars who came from Bavel to Eretz Yisrael to learn but neglected their responsibilities at home. They left wives effectively abandoned, failed in their obligations to provide {\i mezonot} for their families, and were absent from the responsibilities of building a household and having children.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Verse Critique\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The specific verse Rav Avyasar applied to these scholars was: {\i va\u8217'yitnu ha\u8217'yeled bazonah v\u8217'hayalda machru bayayin va\u8217'yishtu} \u8212- they gave the child to the prostitute and the girl they sold for wine and drank. The harshness of the charge attracted its own commentary. Tosafot understands {\i bazonah} as referring to {\i mezonot} \u8212- sustenance \u8212- meaning the children had to labor to support themselves because their fathers were gone. A tradition from the {\i Yerushalmi} goes further: there was an actual case of a man who sold his daughter into servitude in order to fund his Torah study. Rashi reads the critique differently: these scholars lingered in foreign lands, leaving their wives in a state resembling {\i agunot} \u8212- not refusing a {\i get}, but abandoned and bound \u8212- and neglecting the obligations of {\i pru u\u8217'rvu}, such that children who should have existed were never born. Whatever the precise meaning, the letter was a severe indictment, and its character speaks to the kind of authority Rav Avyasar was willing to project.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Klaf Scoring\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Yosef\u8217's specific technical critique is this: Rav Avyasar wrote the letter {\i b\u8217'lo sirtut} \u8212- without scoring the {\i klaf}, without the preparatory lines grooved into the parchment to guide straight writing, which is required when writing a substantial text. And Rav Yitzchak taught: one may write two words without scoring, but not three. A {\i baraita} has a slightly different tradition \u8212- three words without scoring are permitted, but not four. Either way, Rav Avyasar wrote an extended text without observing this practice, and Rav Yosef takes this as evidence of a gap in his learning: if he is unaware of, or disregarded, this {\i halacha}, why should we trust his rulings on more consequential matters?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Abaye Defense\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Authority Standards\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye responds to Rav Yosef\u8217's critique: {\i atu kuli alma lo yad\u8217'ei ha d\u8217'Rav Yitzchak lav gavra raba hu?} \u8212- does everyone know Rav Yitzchak\u8217's position, and if one does not, does that disqualify him as a major authority? The underlying principle is that gaps in knowledge of specific traditions do not determine overall reliability.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tradition vs.\u160 ?Logic\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye draws a principled distinction: {\i bishlama milta d\u8217'talya b\u8217'savra lechai} \u8212- if the failure were in something that depends on {\i savra}, on reasoning and logical analysis, that would be a valid critique of someone\u8217's reliability as a decisor. If someone cannot reason clearly, that calls into question the quality of his other rulings. But this is different.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tradition Knowledge\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Aval ha gmara gmara hi} \u8212- this is a matter of {\i gemara}, of received tradition. {\i U\u8217'gmara lo shmi\u8217'a lei} \u8212- he simply had not heard this particular tradition. Before the age of printed books and comprehensive libraries, one could not be expected to know every tradition that existed. Rav Yitzchak\u8217's teaching about {\i sirtut} may not have been widely circulated. Failure to have received a specific oral tradition says nothing about someone\u8217's analytical capacity or trustworthiness as a {\i posek}. On this note the {\i shiur} concludes, with the broader discussion of Rav Avyasar\u8217's authority \u8212- and the {\i gemara}\u8217's continuation \u8212- to be taken up in the next session.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Gittin 7\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Bifnei Nichtav Requireme\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Bifnei Nichtav Basics\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The daf begins at the bottom of 6b, picking up a discussion about the practical scope of the {\i shaliach}\u8217's obligation to declare {\i bifnei nichtav uvifnei nechtam} \u8212- that the {\i get} was written and signed in his presence. The Gemara had recently established that even within {\i medinat hayam} one must reassess circumstances: if the population is highly transient or signatures are not recognizable, the declaration is required. This leads Tosafot and the {\i rishonim} to conclude that this is one of those {\i takanot} which do not remain fixed but must be continuously reevaluated based on prevailing conditions.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ramban\u8217's Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Ramban raises a question that bears noting here. The Gemara\u8217's reassessment is framed specifically in terms of {\i medinat hayam}. The Ramban asks: could one ever imagine a scenario in which even in Eretz Yisrael \u8212- if conditions deteriorated to the point where Torah knowledge was scarce and travel was limited, as was the case in the time of the {\i rishonim} \u8212- one would be required to state {\i bifnei nichtav uvifnei nechtam}? Or do we say that the question of whether the declaration is needed simply never applies to Eretz Yisrael, which is always categorically exempt? This remains an open question that the Ramban raises.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Bavel\u8217's Special Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara has already granted Bavel a special status comparable to Eretz Yisrael, such that the {\i shaliach} need not declare {\i bifnei nichtav uvifnei nechtam} within Bavel, owing to the regularity of travel and the widespread familiarity with {\i lishmah}. A further ruling attributed to Rabbi Avyasar held that when traveling between Bavel and Eretz Yisrael the declaration is likewise unnecessary.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Avyasar Authority\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This ruling prompted the Gemara to challenge Rabbi Avyasar\u8217's reliability. The critique rested on the observation that in a letter he wrote \u8212- a blistering denunciation of the practice in Bavel of leaving home, wife, and children to study in Eretz Yisrael \u8212- he quoted a {\i pasuk} without scoring the parchment beneath it. In a {\i Sefer Torah}, the letters are suspended from scored lines indented into the parchment above them; the letters hang from the scoring rather than resting upon it. Failing to score a {\i pasuk} when writing it was taken as evidence that Rabbi Avyasar did not know this {\i halacha}, and therefore his authority could be questioned. The Gemara responds, however, that the absence of this practice is not necessarily disqualifying \u8212- not everyone is expected to know it. Indeed, this is likely the origin of the English word \u8220"underscore\u8221": to place the scoring beneath. In a {\i Sefer Torah} the scoring appears above, but the general concept of marking beneath a passage to highlight it flows from the same tradition.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rabbi Avyasar Defens (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Avyasar Divine Alignment\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara adds a further defense of Rabbi Avyasar. The verse states: {\i vatizneh alav pilegsho} \u8212- his concubine acted faithlessly against him \u8212- which is the opening of the story of {\i pilegeish b\u8217'Givah}. Rabbi Avyasar interpreted {\i vatizneh} not as literal adultery but as referring to a fly ({\i z\u8217'vuv}) found in the food \u8212- the word {\i z\u8217'vuv} possibly connecting etymologically to {\i mezonot}, as Tosafot suggests. Rabbi Yonatan, by contrast, said he found a strand of hair ({\i nima}).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Avyasar then encountered Eliyahu and asked: what is God occupied with at this moment? Eliyahu replied that God is engaged in the {\i sugya} of {\i pilegeish b\u8217'Givah}. And what does He say? {\i Avyasar bni kach hu omer} \u8212- My son Avyasar says thus; {\i Yonatan bni kach hu omer} \u8212- My son Yonatan says thus. Rabbi Avyasar recoiled: {\i chas v\u8217'shalom} \u8212- can there really be a doubt before Heaven? Eliyahu answered: {\i eilu v\u8217'eilu divrei Elokim chayim} \u8212- both are the words of the living God.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Fly vs.\u160 ?Hair Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then clarifies how both positions can be simultaneously correct: {\i hein z\u8217'vuv matza v\u8217'lo hikpid} \u8212- he found the fly and was not particularly disturbed; {\i nima matza v\u8217'hikpid} \u8212- but he found the hair and became angry. The fly was disgusting but understandable; the hair provoked a sharper reaction. Even though Rabbi Yonatan\u8217's position was ultimately more precise, Rabbi Avyasar was nonetheless aligned with something genuinely true, and God\u8217's engagement with his view demonstrates that he cannot simply be dismissed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Negligence Theory\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara elaborates. Rav Yehuda teaches: {\i z\u8217'vuv b\u8217'ke\u8217'ara v\u8217'nima b\u8217'oto makom} \u8212- the fly was in the bowl of food, but the hair was found in an intimate location. Based on a certain passage in the Gemara, it appears there was a practice of removing pubic hair, and the presence of such a hair there indicated either a potential physical danger ({\i sakanta}) or that the matter had not been properly attended to. A second opinion holds that both were found in the food ({\i ika d\u8217'amri idy v\u8217'idy b\u8217'ke\u8217'ara}): the fly was an {\i ones} \u8212- an unavoidable accident, for what can one do about a fly landing in food \u8212- while the hair reflected negligence ({\i pshiyuta}). The husband\u8217's disproportionate anger over a strand of hair, or his fury at finding a fly, drove his {\i pilegesh} from the house and set in motion a catastrophe.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Undue Fear Lesson\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 From here the Gemara draws a broader moral lesson. Rav Chisda teaches: {\i le\u8217'olam al yatil adam eimah yeteirah b\u8217'toch beito} \u8212- a person should never impose excessive fear upon his household. The husband of the {\i pilegeish b\u8217'Givah} imposed such fear upon her, and as a result many myriads of Israel fell in the wars that followed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Yehuda in the name of Rav extends this further: anyone who imposes excessive fear upon his household will ultimately come to three sins \u8212- {\i gilui arayot}, {\i shfichut damim}, and {\i chilul Shabbat}. Rashi explains the mechanism of each. {\i Gilui arayot}: the wife, afraid to admit that she did not immerse in the {\i mikvah} because the water was freezing cold \u8212- and one should not underestimate how arduous those immersions were historically, descending into deep wells in the dead of night in darkness and bitter cold \u8212- remains silent and her husband unknowingly cohabits with her while she is a {\i niddah}. {\i Shfichut damim}: as happened here, she flees from him in terror, falls into a pit or off a bridge, and dies. Or, as Rashi says, it is what the {\i pilegeish b\u8217'Givah} story itself exemplifies. {\i Chilul Shabbat}: she did not light candles and did not cook before nightfall, and out of fear of his reaction she lights the candles and cooks after dark \u8212- and he eats the food cooked on Shabbat without knowing it was forbidden.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot notes that {\i Seder Olam} records that the day of {\i pilegeish b\u8217'Givah} was itself a Shabbat, which would connect {\i chilul Shabbat} directly to that narrative. Rabbeinu Chananel has a variant reading: the three sins are {\i gilui arayot}, {\i shfichut damim}, and {\i chilul Hashem} \u8212- noting that {\i chillul Shabbat} and {\i chillul Hashem} are nearly the same word, and that all three were indeed present in the {\i pilegeish b\u8217'Givah} story.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is striking that this {\i aggadata} appears at the opening of {\i Masechet Gittin}, a tractate deeply concerned with the husband\u8217's unilateral power over the {\i get} and the need to protect the wife\u8217's interests. From the very beginning the Gemara raises the specter of the husband who manufactures claims to make the wife\u8217's life impossible \u8212- and here too the story presents a husband whose disproportionate anger over a fly or a hair sets an entire catastrophe in motion.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Domestic Ethics (III)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Pre-Shabbat Statements\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbah bar bar Chana said in the name of Rabbi Yochanan: the Rabbis have said that a man must say three things in his household \u8212- to his wife \u8212- just as it begins to grow dark on {\i erev Shabbat}: {\i issartem} \u8212- have you taken {\i ma\u8217'aser}? {\i eiravtem} \u8212- have you made the {\i eiruv chatzeiros} or {\i eiruv techumin} (which is itself a dispute between Rashi and Tosafot)? {\i hadliku es haner} \u8212- light the candles. These three must be said {\i benichusa} \u8212- pleasantly, gently, without anger or yelling. The purpose is {\i ki heichi delikablu minei} \u8212- so that the members of the household will actually listen and act accordingly, rather than acting out of fear or being driven to sin precisely through the harshness of the tone.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Abaye\u8217's Derivation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye adds: {\i ana lo shmiyena le\u8217'ha de\u8217'Rabbah bar bar Chana} \u8212- I never heard that teaching \u8212- {\i v\u8217'kiyamtiyah misvara} \u8212- yet I arrived at the same conclusion independently through my own reasoning.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rabbi Avyasar Defens (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Chanina\u8217's Fear\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Abbahu continues the theme: {\i le\u8217'olam al yatil adam eimah yeseirah besoch beiso} \u8212- a person should never impose excessive fear upon his household. A great man once had his household so terrified of him that they nearly fed him something of enormous forbidden consequence: {\i eiver min hachai}, a limb torn from a living animal, because circumstances forced an emergency and they were too afraid to tell him. The Gemara asks: is it really possible that God would allow a {\i tzaddik} to stumble into such a sin? After all, the Gemara teaches elsewhere that {\i behemtan shel tzaddikim} \u8212- the very animals of the righteous \u8212- are protected by God from causing them to stumble, as with the donkey of Pinchas ben Yair, which was prevented from eating produce from which {\i terumos u\u8217'ma\u8217'asros} had not been separated. How much more so would God protect the {\i tzaddikim} themselves?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The answer: {\i ela bikshu le\u8217'ha\u8217'achilo davar gadol} \u8212- they sought to feed him, they were on the verge of it, but did not actually succeed. God intervened. The great man in question is identified as Rabbi Chanina ben Gamliel.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot offers an important clarification here. It is not that {\i tzaddikim} can never stumble into sin. Rather, there is a specific tradition regarding food \u8212- {\i de\u8217'gnai hu le\u8217'tzaddik she\u8217'yochal davar issur} \u8212- that it would be a particular disgrace for a {\i tzaddik} to have a forbidden substance actually enter his body. It is for this reason, Tosafot explains, that the Gemara invokes divine protection specifically in cases of accidentally consuming {\i treif} food, while not making the same argument for other inadvertent transgressions. This concern connects to a broader sensibility about the singular gravity of what enters the body: the idea that feeding even an infant non-{\i kosher} food before the age of {\i chinuch} carries a kind of spiritual weight, the tradition of {\i Moshe Rabbeinu} refusing to nurse from an Egyptian woman, and the considerable hesitation found in various {\i teshuvot} regarding commerce in non-{\i kosher} goods, even in the difficult circumstances of the medieval period.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Silence vs.\u160 ?Adversaries\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mar Ukva\u8217's Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Mar Ukva sent a question to Rabbi Elazar: {\i bnei adam ha\u8217'omedim alai} \u8212- there are people who stand against me and cause me harm \u8212- {\i u\u8217'veyadi le\u8217'mosram la\u8217'malchut} \u8212- and I have the means to hand them over to the government, as I possess information about them. What may I do? This is, of course, the question of {\i mesirah}. He wants to know whether protecting himself through this means is permitted.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Guard Your Mouth\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Elazar scored the parchment and wrote him the following verse \u8212- and here the Gemara notes again the practice of scoring before writing a {\i pasuk}: {\i Amarti eshmerah derachai me\u8217'chato bi\u8217'leshoni, eshmerah le\u8217'fi machsom be\u8217'od rasha le\u8217'negdi} \u8212- I said I would guard my path from sinning with my tongue; I will watch my mouth, keeping it muzzled, while the wicked stands against me. Even though the wicked person is arrayed against you, guard your mouth and do not speak out against him.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Anguish Concern\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Mar Ukva wrote back: {\i ka mitza\u8217'ari li tuva} \u8212- he causes me tremendous anguish \u8212- {\i v\u8217'la mitzina de\u8217'eikum behu} \u8212- and I have no other means of protecting myself against him.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Divine Silence Path\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Elazar wrote back again, scoring and writing: {\i dom l\u8217'Hashem v\u8217'hitcholel lo} \u8212- be silent to God and long for Him. Do not speak out; remain silent; God will cause them to fall, {\i v\u8217'hu yapilem lecha chalalim chalalim}. And if you wish to do something constructive: {\i hashkem v\u8217'haarev aleihem le\u8217'veit ha\u8217'midrash} \u8212- rise early and stay late over them at the {\i Beit ha\u8217'Midrash} \u8212- {\i v\u8217'inan kalin me\u8217'aleihen} \u8212- and they will disappear on their own. Tosafot connects {\i dom} here to {\i dimdumei chamah}, the early and late light of dawn and dusk, as a reading of {\i hashkem v\u8217'haarev}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gneiva\u8217's Punishment\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then records: {\i ha\u8217'davar yatza mi\u8217'pi Rabbi Elazar v\u8217'natnuhu le\u8217'Gneiva be\u8217'kolar} \u8212- the matter had barely left the mouth of Rabbi Elazar when Gneiva \u8212- whose very name is suggestive \u8212- was put in chains. The parallel to {\i Haman chapu} \u8212- the moment in {\i Megillat Esther} when Haman was immediately seized as the words left the king\u8217's mouth \u8212- is notable. God acts swiftly when the righteous are silent and trust in divine justice.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Music Prohibition\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Music Source\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Mar Ukva was then asked a question: {\i zimra minalan de\u8217'assir} \u8212- from where do we know that playing music is forbidden? He scored parchment and wrote: {\i al tismach Yisrael el gil ba\u8217'amim} \u8212- do not rejoice, O Israel, in the rejoicing of nations \u8212- particularly after the {\i Churban ha\u8217'Bayit}. This verse was written even while the first Temple still stood, by the prophet Hoshea.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Voice vs.\u160 ?Instrument\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara asks: why did he not instead send the verse {\i be\u8217'shir lo yishtu yayin, yemar shechar le\u8217'shotav} \u8212- they shall not drink wine with song; the drink shall be bitter to those who drink it? The answer: had he sent that verse alone, one might have thought the prohibition applies only to {\i zimra de\u8217'mana} \u8212- instrumental music \u8212- while {\i de\u8217'puma}, vocal singing, would be permitted. By sending the verse from Hoshea instead, the Gemara establishes {\i ka mashma lan} \u8212- it teaches us \u8212- that even purely vocal music is included in the prohibition.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Drinking Houses Context\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot clarifies what is actually prohibited. Rashi explains that the prohibition is specifically directed at {\i shir be\u8217'veit ha\u8217'mishta\u8217'ot} \u8212- singing in drinking establishments. This reading is supported by the Gemara\u8217's very choice to ask why the verse about wine was not cited, and by the {\i mishnah} at the end of {\i Masechet Sotah} which states: {\i mishe\u8217'batla Sanhedrin batel ha\u8217'shir be\u8217'veit ha\u8217'mishta\u8217'ot} \u8212- once the Sanhedrin was abolished, song in the drinking houses was abolished. Tosafot notes, however, that the Yerushalmi speaks of one who rises and falls asleep to music \u8212- {\i dehavei kaei u\u8217'damich be\u8217'zimra} \u8212- suggesting the prohibition extends beyond the drinking house context to music used purely for pleasure and self-indulgence. {\i Shir shel mitzvah shari} \u8212- music in the context of a {\i mitzvah}, such as at a {\i chuppah} to gladden the groom and bride, or the recitation of {\i zemirot}, remains permitted. This area generated considerable literature among the {\i rishonim} and {\i poskim} regarding precisely where the prohibition applies and how strictly it is to be observed, with added complexity today given the difference between live and recorded music.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Music Prohibition (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbinic Origin\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Huna bar Rav Natan asked Rav Ashi the meaning of the {\i pasuk} in {\i Yehoshua} listing the cities {\i Kinah, Dimonah, v\u8217'Ad\u8217'adah}. Rav Ashi replied that these are simply the cities of Eretz Yisrael being enumerated. Rav Huna responded that of course he knew they were cities \u8212- he was looking for a {\i derasha}. Rav Geviha from Argiza, he said, would offer one: {\i kol sheyesh lo kinah al chaveiro v\u8217'domeh} \u8212- anyone who has a grievance against his fellow and yet remains silent \u8212- {\i Kinah v\u8217'Dimonah} \u8212- and is silent, {\i shochney adei ad oseh lo din} \u8212- the One who dwells forever will execute judgment for him. This connects directly to the story of Gneiva: remain silent, do not hand him over to the government, and God will act. Rav Ashi then challenged Rav Huna bar Rav Natan to extend the {\i derasha} to the next verse: {\i Tziklag, Madmannah, v\u8217'Sansannah}. He replied that if Rav Geviha were present, he would have a {\i derasha} for that too. Rav Acha from Bei Choza\u8217'ah then supplied one: {\i kol mi sheyesh lo tza\u8217'akas legima al chaveiro} \u8212- anyone who has a cry of {\i legima} against his fellow, meaning his livelihood has been compromised to the point where he cannot put food on the table \u8212- {\i v\u8217'domeh} \u8212- and is silent and does not attack \u8212- {\i shocheyn ba\u8217'sneh oseh lo din} \u8212- the One who dwells in the bush will come to his defense.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Wedding Crowns\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Crown Source\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Reish Galuta asked Rav Huna: {\i kelila minalan de\u8217'assur} \u8212- from where do we know that the crown worn by a {\i chassan} at his wedding is forbidden? Rav Huna answered: it is {\i miderabbanan} \u8212- rabbinic \u8212- citing the explicit {\i mishnah} in {\i Masechet Sotah}: {\i be\u8217'fulmus shel Aspasyanos gazru al ataros chassanim v\u8217'al ha\u8217'irus} \u8212- in the wars of Vespasian they decreed against the crowns of grooms and against the drums.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mitre Crown Link\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 While Rav Huna stepped away momentarily, Rav Chisda was left with Reish Galuta and offered a biblical grounding. He cited the verse in {\i Yechezkel}: {\i koh amar Hashem Elokim, hasir ha\u8217'mitznefet v\u8217'hareim ha\u8217'atarah} \u8212- thus says the Lord God, remove the mitre and lift off the crown. {\i Zos lo zos} \u8212- this, not even this. {\i Ha\u8217'shpalah ha\u8217'gavoha v\u8217'ha\u8217'gavoha ha\u8217'shpil} \u8212- raise the lowly and bring down the exalted. What is the connection between the {\i mitznefet}, the High Priest\u8217's mitre, and the {\i atarah}, the crown? The verse teaches: {\i bizman she\u8217'mitznefet b\u8217'rosh kohen gadol, atarah b\u8217'rosh kol adam} \u8212- when the {\i mitznefet} rests upon the head of the {\i Kohen Gadol}, when the {\i Beit ha\u8217'Mikdash} stands, then crowns may rest upon the heads of all people at their weddings. {\i Nistalka mitznefet me\u8217'rosh kohen gadol, nistalka atarah me\u8217'rosh kol adam} \u8212- but once the {\i mitznefet} has been removed from the head of the {\i Kohen Gadol}, once the {\i Churban} has occurred, no one may wear such a crown.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Huna\u8217's Objection\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 When Rav Huna returned and was told what Rav Chisda had said, he declared emphatically: {\i ha\u8217'elohim, miderabbanan} \u8212- by God, it is rabbinic, not a {\i pasuk}. The connection to {\i Yechezkel} is a support and a beautiful one, but it does not transform this into a biblical prohibition. He then added graciously: {\i ella chizer shimcha v\u8217'chazyan milach} \u8212- your name indeed suits you and your words are very pleasing. The point stands: the verse provides an {\i asmachta} and a conceptual underpinning, but the prohibition itself is a rabbinic decree. Tosafot notes that Rashi understood the {\i atarah} as a literal metal crown, but Tosafot challenges this from the first chapter of {\i Sotah}, which indicates the crowns in question were made of roses or myrtle \u8212- that is, wreaths.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Braiding Wreath\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Ravina found Mar bar Rav Ashi braiding a wreath for his daughter for her wedding \u8212- an {\i ateret kallah}, a crown for the bride. Ravina challenged him: does the master not hold by the verse {\i hasir ha\u8217'mitznefet v\u8217'hareim ha\u8217'atarah}, which would seem to prohibit crowns for women as well?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gender Distinction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Mar bar Rav Ashi replied: {\i dumya d\u8217'kohen gadol b\u8217'chasanei} \u8212- the prohibition is drawn by analogy to the {\i Kohen Gadol}, and therefore applies only to grooms ({\i chasanim}), not to women ({\i benashi lo}). Tosafot elaborates: what is prohibited for {\i kallos} according to the {\i mishnah} in {\i Sotah} is specifically an {\i ir shel zahav} \u8212- a golden tiara \u8212- while a wreath made of natural materials is a different matter entirely. The prohibition on the {\i kallah} wearing an {\i ir shel zahav} when she goes out in public derives from the general laws of jewelry on Shabbat treated in the chapter {\i bameh ishah}, not from the specific decree about wedding crowns \u8212- and even there, the prohibition is limited to the wedding context out of concern for {\i simcha yeteirah}, excessive festivity.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Angels Plea Derasha\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Angels\u8217' Argument\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Avira expounded \u8212- sometimes citing the tradition in the name of Rav Ami, sometimes in the name of Rav Assi \u8212- on the phrase {\i zos lo zos}. When God declared to Israel {\i hasir ha\u8217'mitznefet v\u8217'hareim ha\u8217'atarah}, the ministering angels, the {\i malachei ha\u8217'shareis}, spoke before God: {\i Ribbono shel Olam, zos lahem l\u8217'Yisrael she\u8217'hikdimu lefanecha b\u8217'Sinai na\u8217'aseh v\u8217'nishma} \u8212- is this what Israel deserves, the very people who preceded {\i nishma} with {\i na\u8217'aseh} at Sinai?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sinai Connection\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is deeply evocative. The {\i midrashim} describe how at Sinai, when Israel declared {\i na\u8217'aseh v\u8217'nishma}, the {\i malachei ha\u8217'shareis} descended and crowned each Jew with two crowns \u8212- one for {\i na\u8217'aseh} and one for {\i nishma}. When they subsequently sinned with the {\i eigel}, the verse records {\i vayisnazlu b\u8217'nei Yisrael es adyam me\u8217'Har Choreiv} \u8212- Israel stripped off their ornaments from Har Choreiv \u8212- which the {\i midrashim} interpret as the removal of those very crowns. Here, in discussing the removal of the wedding crowns on account of the {\i Churban}, the {\i malachei ha\u8217'shareis} invoke the merit of {\i na\u8217'aseh v\u8217'nishma} as a reason to retain them.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Crowns Removal Reason\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 God\u8217's response to the angels: {\i lo zos im zos} \u8212- is it not this very Israel who {\i hishpila es ha\u8217'gavoha v\u8217'higbiha es ha\u8217'shafel} \u8212- who brought low what was exalted and raised up what was lowly? They engaged in {\i avodah zarah}, they brought down the glory of the {\i Beit ha\u8217'Mikdash}, and they even erected an image in the {\i Heichal}. Therefore the crowns must be removed.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Livelihood Derasha\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Limited Livelihood\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Avira expounded again \u8212- sometimes in the name of Rav Ami, sometimes in the name of Rav Assi \u8212- on the verse: {\i koh amar Hashem im shleimim v\u8217'chen rabbim v\u8217'chen nagozu v\u8217'avar}. If a person sees that his livelihood is {\i mtzumtzamin} \u8212- limited and barely sufficient \u8212- he should give {\i tzedakah} from it; and all the more so if he has abundant resources. The word {\i nagozu} is the key: it suggests shearing, and from it the Gemara derives the obligation to give charity even from constrained means.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Domestic Ethics (III)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Charity Derasha\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tana d\u8217'vei Rabbi Yishmael} teaches: anyone who shears from his property \u8212- {\i kol ha\u8217'gozez mi\u8217'nechasav} \u8212- and gives {\i tzedakah} from it, is saved from the judgment of {\i Gehennom}, and the word {\i v\u8217'avar} \u8212- he passes through \u8212- indicates passage to safety.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Livelihood Derasha (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shearing Property\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tana d\u8217'vei Rabbi Yishmael} continues: anyone who shears from his property and gives {\i tzedakah} is saved from the judgment of {\i Gehennom}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sheep Parable\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is illustrated through a parable of two sheep crossing a body of water. One is shorn and one is not. The shorn sheep passes through without difficulty; the unshorn sheep is weighed down by its waterlogged wool and cannot cross. So too: the one who has given away his wealth is unencumbered and can pass through, while the one who has hoarded it all is dragged down.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Domestic Ethics (III)\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Poor Giving Charity\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The verse concludes {\i v\u8217'iniisicha} \u8212- and I have afflicted you. Mar Zutra teaches: {\i afilu ani ha\u8217'misparnes min ha\u8217'tzedakah ya\u8217'aseh tzedakah} \u8212- even a poor person who himself subsists on charity must give {\i tzedakah}. He too should recognize the blessing of whatever he possesses and share it with those in even greater need.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Poverty Elimination\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Yosef teaches from the word {\i oni} in {\i lo a\u8217'aneich od} \u8212- I will no longer afflict you: {\i shuv ein marin lo simanei aniyus} \u8212- one who gives {\i tzedakah}, even from poverty, will no longer be shown the signs of poverty. With this concludes the lengthy {\i aggadata} that began from the attempt to vindicate Rabbi Avyasar and the principle that one need not declare {\i bifnei nichtav} when traveling between Bavel and {\i chutz la\u8217'aretz}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Eretz Yisrael Borders\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yehuda\u8217's Boundaries\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now turns to the question of the boundaries of Eretz Yisrael as they pertain to {\i bifnei nichtav}. It is worth noting at the outset \u8212- and Tosafot\u8217's extensive discussion earlier in the {\i perek} addresses this \u8212- that the question of boundaries here might either reflect a completely independent definition tailored to the concerns of {\i get} delivery and {\i lishmah}, or it might assume that the boundaries used for {\i terumos u\u8217'ma\u8217'asros} and {\i kedushah} apply here as well. As will become clear, this {\i sugya} appears to adopt the latter approach, at least as a working framework.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yehuda sets the boundaries: from Rekem in the east, Ashkelon in the south, and Akko in the north.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tithes Boundaries\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 These boundaries map onto the standard halakhic definitions used for the laws of {\i terumot u\u8217'ma\u8217'asrot} and {\i shevi\u8217'it}. The Gemara appears to assume, or at least explore, the premise that the same area defined as Eretz Yisrael for those purposes defines it for the laws of {\i gittin} as well \u8212- not necessarily because they are identical definitions by principle, but because the areas of settlement with {\i kedushat Eretz Yisrael} and the areas relevant to {\i bifnei nichtav} can be assumed to overlap unless otherwise demonstrated.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Akko vs.\u160 ?Kziv\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara raises a difficulty: if Akko is the northern boundary of Eretz Yisrael, how do we account for the following {\i baraita}? One who walks from Akko northward to Kziv \u8212- with Kziv being further north than Akko \u8212- is told that the road to his east ({\i mi\u8217'yemino le\u8217'mizrach}, which confirms he is walking northward) is {\i tamei mishum Eretz ha\u8217'Amim} \u8212- impure as foreign territory \u8212- and exempt from {\i ma\u8217'aser} and {\i shevi\u8217'it}, while the road to his west is {\i tehorah} and {\i chayevet b\u8217'ma\u8217'aser u\u8217'v\u8217'shevi\u8217'it}, until Kziv is reached. This implies that the northern boundary extends to Kziv \u8212- beyond Akko. The traveler is walking north from Akko; to his right is the east, which is outside Eretz Yisrael; to his left is the west, a narrow strip between the road and the Mediterranean shore, which retains the sanctity of Eretz Yisrael. The picture becomes clearer when one considers the geography: Akko sits on the sea; the coastal path to Kziv runs along the shore, and to the left of the traveler is indeed a strip of land between the path and the sea that remains within the borders of Eretz Yisrael.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Strip Explanation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye answers: {\i retzu\u8217'ah nafka} \u8212- a small strip extends northward. The primary and substantial settlement of Eretz Yisrael ends at Akko; from Akko to Kziv there is only a narrow coastal strip remaining. When we define the northern border as Akko, we are identifying where the {\i ikkar yishuv} \u8212- the main inhabited region \u8212- ends. The strip beyond, while technically part of Eretz Yisrael, is too marginal to serve as the defining boundary marker. Tosafot raises the question of whether it is appropriate to devote an entire {\i mishnah} to describing the halakhic status of such a narrow strip. The Gemara answers that even a path can serve as a geographic marker, as demonstrated by a verse in the book of {\i Shoftim} that gives directions to Shiloh by reference to a road \u8212- {\i li\u8217'msila ha\u8217'olah mi\u8217'Beit El Shechema} \u8212- and Rav Pappa confirms that {\i lemizrachah shel mesila} means precisely to the east of the path, with the path itself serving as the boundary marker.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The underlying tension throughout this {\i sugya} is worth noting: some discussions in the first {\i perek} tailor the definition of Eretz Yisrael specifically to the concerns of {\i get} delivery \u8212- familiarity with signatures, reliability of witnesses, knowledge of {\i lishmah} \u8212- while this {\i sugya} appears to simply borrow the standard definitions from {\i terumot u\u8217'ma\u8217'asrot}. These two approaches do not seem to be in direct dialogue with one another.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Get by Boat\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Boat Equivalence\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara presents two contradictory {\i beraitot}. One teaches: {\i ha\u8217'meivi get bi\u8217'sfina kemevi b\u8217'Eretz Yisrael} \u8212- one who brings a {\i get} in a boat is like one who brings it in Eretz Yisrael, and need not declare {\i bifnei nichtav}. The other teaches: it is like one who brings it in {\i chutz la\u8217'aretz}, and the declaration is required. The case envisions the {\i get} being written or delivered while the {\i shaliach} is aboard a boat, without having disembarked \u8212- the question being whether his location counts as Eretz Yisrael or not.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Foreign Soil Dispute\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara resolves the contradiction by invoking a parallel dispute regarding {\i terumot u\u8217'ma\u8217'asrot}: the case of foreign soil brought in a boat into Eretz Yisrael \u8212- {\i afar chutz la\u8217'aretz haba bi\u8217'sfina l\u8217'aretz} \u8212- with something growing from that soil aboard the boat. Is the produce {\i chayav b\u8217'ma\u8217'aser u\u8217'v\u8217'shevi\u8217'it}? This is a dispute between the {\i Tanna Kamma} and Rabbi Yehuda.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rashi\u8217's Interpretation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rashi understands the Gemara\u8217's case as involving a boat in one of the rivers of Eretz Yisrael \u8212- not on the open sea. The {\i get} was written in the boat while it was in such a river. The question is whether a boat in a river is considered \u8220"in Eretz Yisrael\u8221" in the relevant sense: {\i k\u8217'ilu mivi derech yavasha} \u8212- as if he were bringing it overland \u8212- or whether {\i ha\u8217'nahar eino tor yavasha l\u8217'chol toras ha\u8217'aretz} \u8212- the river does not count as dry land for all purposes of the Land\u8217's laws.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Scraping Bottom\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yehuda holds: {\i emasy? Bizman she\u8217'ha\u8217'sfina gosheshes} \u8212- when does the boat count as being in Eretz Yisrael? Only when the boat is scraping ({\i gosheshes}) along the riverbed \u8212- that is, when there is physical contact between the boat and the ground of Eretz Yisrael. In that case, the boat is considered connected to the land and the produce is {\i chayav}. If the boat is not scraping the bottom, the produce is {\i patur}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yirmiah resolves the contradiction between the two {\i beraitot} accordingly: one {\i beraita} reflects the view of the {\i Tanna Kamma}, who holds that a boat in Eretz Yisrael is treated as part of the land regardless, while the other reflects Rabbi Yehuda\u8217's position, which requires contact with the riverbed. Abaye sharpens this: the two {\i beraitot} correspond to the two conditions \u8212- when the boat is scraping the bottom, and when it is not.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Perforated Pot\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Zeira draws a comparison to a {\i perforated pot} ({\i atzitz nakuv}) suspended above the ground: just as there is a debate about whether such a pot, with its contents disconnected from the ground by air space, retains the agricultural sanctity of the land ({\i palugta de\u8217'Rabbi Yehuda v\u8217'Rabbanan}), so too the boat case mirrors that same structural question. The boat, like the {\i atzitz nakuv}, may be treated as porous \u8212- either literally, if Rashi\u8217's reading of the boat as made of pottery is accepted, or conceptually \u8212- such that contact with the ground below determines its status.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Boat Movement\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava draws a distinction between the two cases. One cannot simply assume they are parallel. Rabbi Yehuda\u8217's condition of {\i gosheshes} may apply specifically to a boat \u8212- {\i sefina ha\u8217'asuya livroach} \u8212- because a boat is designed to move; it is never truly settled in place. Since it is inherently mobile, the only moment when it can be treated as part of the land is when it is actively dragging along the bottom and thus physically connected. But an {\i atzitz} that stays in one location \u8212- {\i she\u8217'eino asui livroach} \u8212- Rabbi Yehuda might treat differently, granting it the status of connection to the land even without direct contact.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Alternatively, Rava suggests, the {\i Rabbanan} themselves might distinguish between the cases in the opposite direction. When they said the boat counts as part of Eretz Yisrael even without contact, their reasoning was that water is not truly a separation: {\i demaya de\u8217'ar\u8217'a semichta damya} \u8212- water is like thick, dense ground, still physically continuous with the earth beneath. Air, however, is a genuine separation; an {\i atzitz} suspended above ground by an air gap might be treated differently even by the {\i Rabbanan}. The irony throughout this entire {\i sugya} \u8212- and it is worth noting \u8212- is that while the debate about foreign soil in a boat is technically a dispute in the laws of {\i terumos u\u8217'ma\u8217'asros}, the Gemara is applying its resolution directly to the question of {\i get} delivery, even though the conceptual concerns underlying {\i bifnei nichtav} \u8212- familiarity with witnesses, knowledge of {\i lishmah} \u8212- bear no obvious relationship to whether a boat\u8217's hull is scraping the riverbed.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Gittin 8\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Eretz Yisrael Borders\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Boat/Planter Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The daf picks up in the middle of a {\i gemara} discussing the parameters of {\i Eretz Yisrael} for the purposes of {\i gittin}. The question concerns someone who brings a {\i get} while on a boat traveling on a river in {\i Eretz Yisrael} \u8212- is that considered bringing it from within {\i Eretz Yisrael}, or does it have the status of {\i chutz la\u8217'aretz}, thereby requiring the messenger to declare {\i bifnei nichtav uvifnei nechtam}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} ties this debate to a dispute between Rabbi Yehuda and the {\i Chachamim} regarding a boat carrying soil on which crops are growing while the boat travels through a river in {\i Eretz Yisrael} \u8212- are those crops obligated in {\i terumot u\u8217'ma\u8217'asrot}? The {\i Chachamim} say yes; Rabbi Yehuda says only when the boat is actually scraping against the riverbed. The {\i gemara} also draws a comparison to the case of an {\i atzitz}, a flowerpot with a hole in it. Rashi understands the boat to be made of pottery, while Tosafot explains it is a wooden boat, which is considered porous. Regardless, the key question is why this agricultural consideration \u8212- whether the boat is touching the riverbed \u8212- has any bearing on the entirely separate question of {\i bifnei nichtav}, which concerns different practical matters such as {\i lishma} and the ability to validate signatures.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafot: Formal Definiti\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The answer becomes clear from Tosafot\u8217's explanation. The {\i gemara} is not examining whether the pragmatic concerns underlying the {\i takana} of {\i bifnei nichtav} are actually satisfied in any given case. Rather, it is drawing on a pre-existing, formal definition of what constitutes {\i Eretz Yisrael} \u8212- derived from the laws of {\i terumot u\u8217'ma\u8217'asrot} \u8212- and importing that definition wholesale into the context of {\i gittin}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot, in the {\i tosafot} entitled {\i atzitz} toward the end of 7b, makes this point explicitly. He writes: {\i de\u8217'nehei de\u8217'hacha de\u8217'eina gosheses le\u8217'Rabbi Yehuda mi\u8217'de\u8217'hava lei Chutz La\u8217'aretz le\u8217'inyan ma\u8217'aser chashivnan Chutz La\u8217'aretz le\u8217'inyan get} \u8212- if the boat is not scraping the bottom, then since under Rabbi Yehuda\u8217's view the produce would not be obligated in {\i ma\u8217'aser}, it is treated as {\i chutz la\u8217'aretz} for {\i get} purposes as well. The logic is that the place itself is formally labeled {\i chutz la\u8217'aretz}, and Tosafot explains: {\i keivan desheim Chutz La\u8217'aretz al hamakom} \u8212- since that place is called {\i chutz la\u8217'aretz} in the formal sense, one must say {\i bifnei nichtav ubifnei nechtam}, even though from a purely practical standpoint, the {\i edim} are right there on the river, easily accessible, and presumably familiar with the laws of {\i lishma}. Tosafot acknowledges this practical observation and addresses it: {\i kedei shelo tachalok midinas hayam} \u8212- for reasons of formal consistency, the determination follows the label, not the practical reality.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot further clarifies a potentially confusing point. For something to be {\i chayav} in {\i terumot u\u8217'ma\u8217'asrot}, two separate conditions must be met: first, the produce must be growing in {\i Eretz Yisrael}; and second, the soil must be connected to the ground. For the purposes of {\i bifnei nichtav}, however, the second condition \u8212- soil connectivity \u8212- is irrelevant. That is a technical {\i terumot u\u8217'ma\u8217'asrot} concern. The only question that matters for {\i gittin} is the first: is this place formally considered part of {\i Eretz Yisrael} or not? We therefore borrow the {\i terumot u\u8217'ma\u8217'asrot} definition not to resolve questions about dirt connectivity, but as a formal indicator of whether one is standing inside or outside the land. And that formalistic determination, in turn, governs whether {\i bifnei nichtav} must be said.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might ask about the upper floor analogy: does a person writing a {\i get} on a second floor in Israel need to say {\i bifnei nichtav} simply because dirt on an upper story would not be {\i chayav} in {\i terumot u\u8217'ma\u8217'asrot}? Tosafot addresses this: there at least the soil beneath the building is {\i chayav}, whereas on a boat in a river perhaps nothing below would be {\i chayav} \u8212- yet even then, Tosafot\u8217's point stands, because the question about the boat is not really about the dirt at all. It is about whether the location itself is formally designated as {\i Eretz Yisrael}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Nachman: Rivers\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} resumes on 8a. Rav Nachman bar Yitzchak states: {\i benaharot Eretz Yisrael kuli alma lo pligi} \u8212- when it comes to rivers within {\i Eretz Yisrael}, nobody disagrees. A river flowing through the land of Israel is unambiguously within the borders of Israel. Rashi confirms this: {\i kuli alma lo pligi de\u8217'Eretz Yisrael ninhu}, and even if the boat is not scraping the riverbed, that is irrelevant because, as Rashi explains, {\i ta\u8217'ama deget lav mishum yanika min hakarka hu} \u8212- the rationale for {\i gittin} is not about whether the soil is drawing nourishment from the ground of Israel, which is a {\i ma\u8217'aser} and {\i shvi\u8217'it} concern. Therefore, whether it is a river or dry land, {\i lo shna nahar velo shna yabasha}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What, then, is the subject of the debate? {\i Ela ki pligi beyam hagadol} \u8212- the dispute concerns the Mediterranean Sea. If one is carrying a {\i get} on a boat in the Mediterranean, off the coast of Israel, at what point is that boat considered within {\i Eretz Yisrael} and at what point is it in international waters? This is where the {\i gemara} once again borrows a pre-existing formal definition from {\i terumot u\u8217'ma\u8217'asrot} \u8212- but now it is a more appropriate borrowing, because this is not about the technical question of soil connectivity. It is a genuine question about what constitutes the territorial boundaries of {\i Eretz Yisrael}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Turei Amnon Border\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i baraita} is cited: {\i eizehu Eretz Yisrael ve\u8217'eizehu Chutz La\u8217'aretz?} \u8212- which areas belong to {\i Eretz Yisrael} and which to {\i chutz la\u8217'aretz} for purposes of {\i terumah}? {\i Kol she\u8217'shofei\u8217'a v\u8217'yored mi\u8217'Turei Amnon v\u8217'lifnim Eretz Yisrael; mi\u8217'Turei Amnon v\u8217'lachutz Chutz La\u8217'aretz} \u8212- anything that slopes down from {\i Turei Amnon} inward is {\i Eretz Yisrael}; from {\i Turei Amnon} outward is {\i chutz la\u8217'aretz}. Rashi identifies {\i Turei Amnon} as the Aramaic equivalent of {\i Hor HaHar}, the mountain ridge mentioned in the Torah as part of the western border of the Land. The image is that of a mountain ridge: whatever slopes toward the interior of the land is {\i Eretz Yisrael}, and whatever slopes away is outside it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Island Definition\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i baraita} continues with the case of islands: {\i nasin she\u8217'ba\u8217'yam ro\u8217'in otan k\u8217'ilu chut matuach aleihen mi\u8217'Turei Amnon ad Nachal Mitzrayim} \u8212- for islands in the sea, one imagines a string drawn from {\i Turei Amnon} to {\i Nachal Mitzrayim} (understood as Wadi el-Arish at the southern coastal border). Anything within the arc between that string and the coastline is considered {\i Eretz Yisrael}; everything outside that string is {\i chutz la\u8217'aretz}. This is the position of the {\i Tanna Kamma}. Under this view, the northern and southern coastal borders are connected by a diagonal line, and the enclosed area of Mediterranean waters is considered part of the Land.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yehuda disagrees and expands this definition significantly. He derives from the verse {\i u\u8217'gvul yam v\u8217'hayah lachem haYam HaGadol u\u8217'gvul} \u8212- the redundancy of the word {\i u\u8217'gvul} teaches that more of the Mediterranean is to be included. His formulation: {\i kol she\u8217'keneged Eretz Yisrael harei hu k\u8217'Eretz Yisrael} \u8212- anything directly opposite Israel is counted as Israel. And for islands that fall to the sides, rather than drawing the diagonal connecting line of the {\i Tanna Kamma}, Rabbi Yehuda draws two parallel horizontal lines \u8212- one extending westward from {\i Turei Amnon} (which he terms {\i Kaflurya}) to the Atlantic, and one extending westward from {\i Nachal Mitzrayim} to the Atlantic. Everything between those two parallel lines is {\i Eretz Yisrael}, and everything outside them is {\i chutz la\u8217'aretz}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} asks: what does the {\i Tanna Kamma} do with the word {\i u\u8217'gvul} that Rabbi Yehuda uses for his expansion? Answer: the {\i Tanna Kamma} needs it to include islands at all \u8212- without it, one might not know islands are counted. Rabbi Yehuda, by contrast, derives the inclusion of islands from the word {\i yam} itself, and therefore the additional {\i u\u8217'gvul} is available to teach the parallel-line rule.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Under Rabbi Yehuda\u8217's map, the territory between the two parallel lines extending across the entire Mediterranean would be extraordinarily vast \u8212- encompassing islands such as Sicily, Cyprus, and potentially parts of Greece. The debate between the {\i Chachamim} and Rabbi Yehuda about whether a boat is in {\i Eretz Yisrael} or {\i chutz la\u8217'aretz} therefore pertains to boats sailing in the farther regions of the Mediterranean: the {\i Chachamim} would call those waters {\i chutz la\u8217'aretz}, while Rabbi Yehuda would say they remain {\i Eretz Yisrael}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafot: France Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This leads to a question posed by Rabbeinu Peter to Rabbeinu Tam, cited in Tosafot: given Rabbi Yehuda\u8217's position, should communities in France be obligated in {\i ma\u8217'aser}, since they lie to the west of {\i Eretz Yisrael} and might fall within Rabbi Yehuda\u8217's expanded zone? Tosafot records three responses.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The first answer is simply that we do not rule according to Rabbi Yehuda \u8212- {\i delo kaima lan k\u8217'Rabbi Yehuda}. Tosafot then raises an additional question on the side: even granting Rabbi Yehuda\u8217's expansion, what does this have to do with {\i gittin}? Did Rabbi Yehuda really mean to say that all those regions \u8212- including apparently distant lands \u8212- had communities that were experts in {\i lishma} and that would not require {\i bifnei nichtav}? This question itself reinforces the point that this {\i gemara} is working with purely formal, pre-packaged definitions rather than the substantive concerns of the {\i takana}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The second answer \u8212- which Tosafot identifies as closer to the plain sense of the {\i gemara} and as consistent with Rashi\u8217's commentary \u8212- is that Rabbi Yehuda is speaking only about islands in the Mediterranean, not about major continental land masses. {\i Delo havay beyabasha ad Kunas ela banasim shebaYam HaGadol davka} \u8212- his ruling applies only to islands in the sea. Sicily and certain Greek islands might be included, but France, Spain, and other major territories would not be. Tosafot supports this from a series of verses \u8212- {\i ad HaYam HaGadol mevo hashemesh yiheye gevulchem}, {\i veshatati gevulcha miYam Suf ve\u8217'ad Yam Pelishtim}, and so on \u8212- which indicate that the borders are defined in terms of surrounding seas and the territories they enclose, not unlimited projection across entire continents.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The third answer maintains that Rabbi Yehuda did actually know, in his time, that {\i lishma} requirements were being satisfied even in those regions, and that he knew specifically which communities would qualify. This third response, like the first, returns to the practical considerations of {\i lishma} and signature validation. Tosafot\u8217's most substantive resolution remains the middle one: the discussion concerns islands in the sea, and once the formal definition of which islands belong to {\i Eretz Yisrael} is established, that definition is imported wholesale into the context of {\i gittin} \u8212- not because it perfectly mirrors the needs of the {\i takana}, but because the {\i gemara} is working from a formalistic geographic definition.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Three Definitions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Meir: Akko\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now returns to the {\i mishna}: {\i Rabbi Meir omer Akko k\u8217'Eretz Yisrael l\u8217'Gittin} \u8212- Rabbi Meir rules that Akko counts as {\i Eretz Yisrael} for {\i gittin} purposes. This is a more specifically tailored definition \u8212- for {\i gittin} only, not for other purposes \u8212- which already reflects a different approach from the broad formalistic borrowing of the earlier discussion.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Slave to Syria\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then records a question posed to Rabbi Chiyya bar Abba: {\i hamocher avdo l\u8217'Surya k\u8217'mocher b\u8217'chutz la\u8217'aretz dami o lo?} \u8212- if someone sells his slave to {\i Surya} (Syria), is it treated like selling him to {\i chutz la\u8217'aretz}? This question introduces a third legal context in which the definition of {\i Eretz Yisrael} is relevant. There is a {\i halacha} that if a master sells his slave outside of {\i Eretz Yisrael}, the slave goes free as a {\i knas}, because an {\i eved kena\u8217'ani} is obligated in certain {\i mitzvot} tied to the land, and by selling him away the master is effectively determining that the slave will be unable to fulfill those obligations and will be cut off from the Jewish community\u8217's center of life. The question is whether {\i Surya} \u8212- understood as the territory broadly called Syria, which includes {\i Aram Tzova} and which was conquered by King David \u8212- is considered {\i chutz la\u8217'aretz} for this purpose.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Graduated Definitions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} answers by invoking Rabbi Meir\u8217's ruling: {\i l\u8217'Gittin in, l\u8217'avadim lo} \u8212- Akko is like {\i Eretz Yisrael} for {\i gittin} purposes but not for slave purposes. And if even Akko, which is immediately adjacent to the land, is considered {\i chutz la\u8217'aretz} for slave purposes, then {\i kol sheken Surya d\u8217'merachaka tuva} \u8212- how much more so Syria, which is much more distant from the central population of the land.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This establishes three distinct and graduated definitions of {\i Eretz Yisrael}. The first is the {\i terumot u\u8217'ma\u8217'asrot} definition, which turns on the formal question of which land was sanctified through conquest. The second is the {\i gittin} definition, which reflects where there is sufficient Jewish presence, knowledge of {\i lishma}, and ability to validate documents. The third is the {\i avadim} definition, which concerns where the primary Jewish community actually lives \u8212- the {\i ikkar yishuv} \u8212- such that selling a slave away from that center is genuinely disadvantaging him.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Conceptually, {\i Surya} may be {\i chayav} in {\i terumot u\u8217'ma\u8217'asrot} \u8212- because it was formally sanctified through conquest \u8212- yet it is treated as {\i chutz la\u8217'aretz} for both {\i gittin} and for the law of slaves. This is because the {\i gittin} definition asks where there is Jewish travel and communal life sufficient to ensure {\i lishma}, and the {\i avadim} definition asks where the primary Jewish population resides. {\i Surya} satisfies the criterion of formal sanctification but not the criteria of actual Jewish settlement and communal life. The three concentric definitions therefore correspond to three different underlying concerns, and each produces a different geographic result.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Syria\u8217's Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Syria: Three Chutz\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i baraita} articulates the multi-layered status of {\i Surya} systematically. {\i Tanu Rabbanan bishlosha drachim shavsa Surya l\u8217'Eretz Yisrael u\u8217'vishlosha l\u8217'Chutz La\u8217'aretz} \u8212- in three ways {\i Surya} resembles {\i Eretz Yisrael}, and in three ways it resembles {\i chutz la\u8217'aretz}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The three ways it is like {\i chutz la\u8217'aretz}: first, {\i afrah tamei k\u8217'Chutz La\u8217'aretz} \u8212- its soil is {\i tamei} like that of {\i chutz la\u8217'aretz}. The Rabbis decreed that the soil of lands outside {\i Eretz Yisrael} is {\i tamei}, based on the concern that non-Jews buried their dead throughout the land without demarcation, posing a risk of {\i tumah} to {\i kohanim} who traveled there. Beyond the practical concern, this decree also served as a communal mechanism to encourage Jews \u8212- and particularly {\i kohanim} \u8212- to remain in {\i Eretz Yisrael} rather than emigrate, giving {\i halachic} reality to the sanctity of living in the Land. {\i Surya}, despite its formal {\i kedusha} for {\i terumot u\u8217'ma\u8217'asrot}, is treated like {\i chutz la\u8217'aretz} in this regard. Second, {\i v\u8217'hamocher avdo l\u8217'Surya k\u8217'mocher b\u8217'Chutz La\u8217'aretz} \u8212- selling a slave there is like selling to {\i chutz la\u8217'aretz}. Third, {\i v\u8217'hameivi get mi\u8217'Surya k\u8217'meivi mi\u8217'Chutz La\u8217'aretz} \u8212- one who brings a {\i get} from {\i Surya} must declare {\i bifnei nichtav}, as it is treated as {\i chutz la\u8217'aretz} for {\i gittin}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kibush Yachid\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Three ways {\i Surya} is like {\i Eretz Yisrael}: {\i chayavet b\u8217'ma\u8217'aser u\u8217'vishvi\u8217'it k\u8217'Eretz Yisrael} \u8212- it is obligated in {\i ma\u8217'aser} and {\i shvi\u8217'it} like {\i Eretz Yisrael}. The {\i gemara} explains the underlying principle: {\i kasavar kibuish yachid shmo kibuish} \u8212- it holds that a conquest by an individual counts as a valid conquest for purposes of sanctifying land with the {\i kedusha} of {\i Eretz Yisrael}. It is this principle that makes {\i Surya chayav} in {\i terumot u\u8217'ma\u8217'asrot}, since the territories were conquered by David \u8212- an individual acting as king rather than through a full national enterprise.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rashi: Kibush Yachid\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rashi explains {\i kibuish yachid} as follows: {\i shelo hayu kol Yisrael b\u8217'yachad k\u8217'derech shehayu b\u8217'kibuish Yehoshua} \u8212- unlike Joshua\u8217's conquest, which was a fully national undertaking, David\u8217's campaigns were not carried out together with all of Israel. Rashi adds two related points: David\u8217's conquest lacked the participation of all twelve tribes as a national army, and it also lacked {\i Sanhedrin} authorization, which is what would have transformed a military campaign into a nationally sanctioned act of conquest. Additionally, Joshua\u8217's conquest was executed {\i l\u8217'tzorech kol Yisrael} \u8212- for the benefit of all Israel, for the purpose of division among the tribes. David\u8217's conquests, by contrast, were undertaken {\i l\u8217'tzorcho} \u8212- for his own purposes as king, not to serve an obvious national goal. That is what makes them {\i kibuish yachid}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara}\u8217's position here, that {\i kibuish yachid} does confer {\i kedusha} for {\i terumot u\u8217'ma\u8217'asrot}, means David\u8217's conquest of these territories sanctified them even without a full national enterprise. A striking question arises in this context: {\i terumot u\u8217'ma\u8217'asrot} as practiced is based on the sanctity established during the Second Temple period \u8212- we do not generally hold that the sanctity of the First Temple period persists for {\i terumot u\u8217'ma\u8217'asrot} purposes. Why, then, should what David did be the operative consideration? This question is worth noting, though the {\i gemara} proceeds on the assumption that it is.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafot: Kibush Yachid\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot offers a fundamentally different understanding of {\i kibuish yachid}, derived from the {\i Sifrei} at the end of {\i Parshat Va\u8217'yihei Eikev}. For Tosafot, the problem is not that David\u8217's conquest was a private rather than national enterprise. Rather, the issue is that one cannot formally annex territory to {\i Eretz Yisrael} and endow it with {\i kedusha} until the primary land of Israel has itself been fully conquered. The {\i Sifrei} derives this from the verse {\i samuch l\u8217'pator yadcha shelo horashta} \u8212- you have not yet taken possession of that which is near you. Rashi there explains that this refers to the Jebusite enclave near Jerusalem, which had not yet been subdued. If the land adjacent to the capital had not been fully conquered, one cannot go off and conquer {\i Aram Naharayim} and {\i Aram Tzova} and incorporate those territories into the sacred land of Israel.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 However, {\i aval achar shekol Eretz Yisrael kvusha} \u8212- once the entire primary land of Israel is conquered, {\i kol makom asher tidroch kaf raglchem lachem} \u8212- any territory subsequently conquered, even by an individual, attains the sanctity of {\i Eretz Yisrael}. So for Tosafot, {\i kibuish yachid} is not primarily about the absence of national participation; it is about the sequence of conquest. David conquered {\i Surya} before completing the conquest of the primary land, and that timing is what prevented full annexation. The conquered territories received some {\i kedusha} but not the full {\i kedusha} that would have come with a conquest occurring after {\i Eretz Yisrael} itself was fully in hand.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tahara in Syria\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The second way {\i Surya} resembles {\i Eretz Yisrael} in its unique status: {\i v\u8217'harotzeh l\u8217'hikanes lah b\u8217'tahara nichnas} \u8212- if one wishes to enter {\i Surya} while remaining {\i tahor}, it is possible to do so. The {\i gemara} cites a {\i baraita}: one who enters {\i Eretz Ha\u8217'amim} (foreign lands) while enclosed in a box, chest, or tower \u8212- {\i Rebbe} says he becomes {\i tamei}; Rabbi Yose bar Rabbi Yehuda says he remains {\i tahor}. The {\i gemara} then clarifies that even Rebbe only rules stringently in normal {\i chutz la\u8217'aretz}, where the Rabbis decreed {\i tumah} not only on the soil itself but on the very air \u8212- {\i al gusha gazru, avira lo gazru}. In Syria, however, the decree applies only to touching the soil, not to the air above it or to simply being present over the land. Therefore, everyone agrees that in {\i Surya} one can maintain {\i tahara} by avoiding direct contact with the ground, for example by being carried in an enclosed conveyance \u8212- though Tosafot notes that one riding a horse might similarly argue the same point.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The irony of this ruling is notable: the soil of {\i Surya} is formally {\i tamei}, and yet its {\i tumah} is categorically less severe than that of ordinary {\i chutz la\u8217'aretz}. This precisely reflects {\i Surya}\u8217's middle position \u8212- more connected to {\i Eretz Yisrael} than the rest of the world, yet not fully part of it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Purchasing Fields\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The third way {\i Surya} resembles {\i Eretz Yisrael}: {\i v\u8217'hakoneh sadeh b\u8217'Surya k\u8217'koneh b\u8217'parvarei Yerushalayim} \u8212- one who buys a field in {\i Surya} is like one who purchases property in the environs of Jerusalem itself. The {\i gemara} asks: {\i l\u8217'mai hilchasa} \u8212- what is the practical implication of this? Rav Sheshes answers: {\i lomar shekosvin alav ono va\u8217'afilu b\u8217'Shabbos} \u8212- it means that the deed of sale may be drawn up even on {\i Shabbat}. The {\i gemara} immediately asks: surely you do not mean that the Jew himself writes on {\i Shabbat}? The answer, as derived from Rava\u8217's ruling elsewhere, is {\i omer l\u8217'Akum v\u8217'oseh} \u8212- a non-Jew is instructed to draw up the document. Even though {\i amira l\u8217'Akum shvut} is normally prohibited as a Rabbinic prohibition, {\i mishum yishuv Eretz Yisrael lo gazru rabanan} \u8212- the Rabbis did not apply this prohibition when the purpose is settling the Land of Israel.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The tension here is striking. On one hand, the very same {\i Surya} whose dirt is {\i tamei}, whose slaves gain freedom if sold there, and from which a {\i get} requires {\i bifnei nichtav} \u8212- that same territory is valued so highly as an extension of {\i Eretz Yisrael} that purchasing fields there overrides the Rabbinic prohibition against {\i amira l\u8217'goy} on {\i Shabbat}. Perhaps the purchase of agricultural fields ({\i sadeh} suggests planting) in {\i Surya} serves the larger project of {\i yishuv Eretz Yisrael} by providing agricultural resources for the Jewish community in the Land proper. Or perhaps the {\i kedusha} inherent in the sanctified soil, even if Jews do not presently live there in large numbers, is sufficient to make Jewish ownership of that land a religious priority.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot notes the limits of this ruling. {\i Mishum yishuv Eretz Yisrael lo gazru rabanan} \u8212- only for the specific {\i mitzvah} of {\i yishuv Eretz Yisrael} is {\i amira l\u8217'goy} permitted even for a {\i melacha d\u8217'Oraisa}. For any other {\i mitzvah} purpose, {\i amira l\u8217'goy} remains permitted only for a {\i d\u8217'rabbanan} prohibition, not for a {\i d\u8217'Oraisa}. Tosafot does note that the {\i Halachot Gedolot} takes a broader view, permitting {\i amira l\u8217'goy} even for a {\i d\u8217'Oraisa} prohibition whenever it serves a {\i mitzvah} purpose \u8212- citing the case of {\i milah} as an example. We do not rule according to this position, though the Rema is prepared to rely on it {\i b\u8217'sha\u8217'at hadechak}. For {\i yishuv Eretz Yisrael}, however, the ruling of the {\i gemara} is clear and undisputed: {\i amira l\u8217'goy} is permitted.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Slave\u8217's Get Shichrur\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Eved Brings Get\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Since the discussion has introduced the laws of slavery and manumission, the {\i gemara} turns to a related {\i halacha} found in the {\i mishna}: {\i eved she\u8217'heivi gito v\u8217'kasuv bo atzmecha u\u8217'nechasai knuyim lach} \u8212- a slave who brings his own writ of manumission containing the language \u8220"you and all my property are given to you.\u8221" The ruling: {\i atzmo kanah, nechasim lo kanah} \u8212- he acquires his freedom but does not acquire the property.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Get vs.\u160 ?Gift\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rashi explains the underlying reasoning. The slave\u8217's declaration of {\i bifnei nichtav} \u8212- the very statement that the document was written and signed in his presence \u8212- functions as a {\i kiyum}, a validation of the signatures on the document. But it serves as a {\i kiyum} only in its capacity as a {\i get shichrur}, a writ of manumission, and not in its capacity as a {\i matan}, a gift instrument. The {\i gemara} introduced a parallel {\i takana} for {\i gitei shichrur} analogous to the {\i takana} for {\i gitei nashim}: the slave declaring {\i bifnei nichtav} is a special leniency granted to him with respect to validating his own document of freedom. There is, however, no such leniency for gift documents. A gift document requires standard {\i kiyum shtarot} \u8212- validation of its signatures by two witnesses. Since the single document performs two legal functions simultaneously \u8212- manumitting the slave and gifting him property \u8212- the {\i kiyum} achieved through {\i bifnei nichtav} is effective only for the manumission aspect. For the property transfer, the document must be independently validated through normal means.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Abaye vs.\u160 ?Rava\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A question is then raised: the case described above involves two statements \u8212- \u8220"you are given to yourself\u8221" and \u8220"my property is given to you\u8221" \u8212- phrased as two distinct phrases within one document. The {\i bifnei nichtav} can therefore potentially be understood as applying to one phrase and not the other, since there are two separable statements. But what if the document uses a single phrase: {\i kol nechasai k\u8217'nuim lach} \u8212- all my property is given to you? Here, \u8220"all my property\u8221" includes the slave himself, so the freedom and the gift are expressed in a single unified statement rather than two discrete ones. Does the {\i kiyum} achieved by {\i bifnei nichtav} then apply to the entire sentence \u8212- including the property \u8212- or only to the freedom element?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye rules: {\i mitoch she\u8217'kanah atzmo kanah nechasim} \u8212- since the {\i kiyum} validates the sentence for the purpose of acquiring himself, it validates the sentence in its entirety, including the property. The sentence is one undivided legal act; either the {\i kiyum} applies to the whole sentence or it does not.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava disagrees: {\i bishlama atzmo liknei, midi d\u8217'hava a\u8217'get isha} \u8212- it is understandable that the {\i kiyum} works for his own freedom, just as {\i bifnei nichtav} works for a {\i get isha}. {\i Ela nechasim lo liknei, midi d\u8217'hava a\u8217'kiyum shtarot d\u8217'alma} \u8212- but for the property, from the vantage point of a gift document, the sentence requires standard validation of signatures, and {\i bifnei nichtav} does not supply that.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava\u8217's Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The debate continues as Abaye reconsiders. Unwilling to abandon the principle that the sentence is a single unified act, Abaye reverses the logic: {\i mitoch she\u8217'lo kanah nechasim lo kanah atzmo} \u8212- if the {\i kiyum} is insufficient to transfer the property, then by the same token it should not be sufficient to free him either. If the sentence stands or falls as a whole, then either it is fully validated or it is not validated at all.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava responds with equal firmness: {\i bishlama nechasim lo liknei} \u8212- I agree the property is not transferred, since that aspect of the document functions as a gift document requiring ordinary validation. {\i Ela atzmo liknei, midi d\u8217'hava a\u8217'get isha} \u8212- but he should acquire himself. The {\i kiyum} provided by {\i bifnei nichtav} is effective for the manumission aspect of the document, since that is precisely what the {\i takana} of {\i bifnei nichtav} was established to achieve.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 In Rava\u8217's final ruling, {\i echad zeh ve\u8217'echad zeh atzmo kanah nechasim lo kanah} \u8212- whether the document phrases the two elements in two separate clauses or in a single unified clause, the result is identical: he is freed but does not acquire the property. Rava\u8217's position is that the legal effects of a document can be analyzed and split according to the distinct functions the document performs, even when those functions are expressed in a single sentence. A {\i get shichrur} and a {\i matan} are two categorically different types of legal instruments governed by different rules of validation. The fact that they happen to be expressed together \u8212- even in one sentence \u8212- does not merge them into a single legal act for the purposes of {\i kiyum}. Abaye, by contrast, insists that the physical sentence is the irreducible unit: either the {\i kiyum} validates the sentence or it does not, and one cannot selectively apply the {\i kiyum} to some of its legal ramifications while denying it to others.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Gittin 9\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rava vs.\u160 ?Abaye\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Property & Freedom\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The sugya opens with a scenario involving a single document \u8212- a {\i get} \u8212- that simultaneously transfers property to a slave and frees him. The document contains one line stating that all of the master\u8217's property is given to the slave, and the question concerns whether the {\i kiyum} performed through {\i befanai nichtav uvefanai nechtam} \u8212- the declaration required and permitted specifically for a {\i shtar shichrur} \u8212- also validates the property transfer embedded in the same statement.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava: Split Effect\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava holds that it does not need to do both or neither. The effect of the {\i get} can be split: the aspect of the statement that applies to the slave\u8217's {\i shichrur} is validated by the {\i kiyum}, while the aspect that would transfer property to him is not. A single sentence can thus be partially effective.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Abaye: All or Nothing\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye takes the opposing position: it is all or nothing. One cannot apply a {\i kiyum} selectively to one legal effect of a statement while leaving the other unaffected. Though notably, Abaye\u8217's position will shift over the course of the discussion.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Palginan Dibura\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Adda\u8217's Challenge\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Adda bar Matna challenges Rava directly: whose opinion are you following? Is this not the position of Rabbi Shimon, who maintains {\i palginan diburei} \u8212- that we split the words, or rather the legal effects of words?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishnah: Reservation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The source for this principle is a {\i mishnah}: if one writes all his property to his slave, the slave goes free \u8212- because the slave\u8217's own person is included among the property, so the master is effectively giving the slave to himself, rendering the document both a gift and a writ of manumission. However, if the master reserves even a minimal amount of land \u8212- {\i kol she-hu} \u8212- the slave does not go free, because the reservation implies that the transfer is incomplete, that something is being held back, and there is a sense that the slave himself may not be fully released.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Shimon\u8217's Exception\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Shimon qualifies this ruling. He says the slave is {\i always} freed \u8212- {\i le-olam hu ben chorin} \u8212- with only one exception: if the master says \u8220"all my property is given to my slave except for one part in ten thousand of it.\u8221" The difference between this case and a reservation of a specific piece of land is significant. When an abstract, fractional percentage is withheld, we do not know what is being held back. It could be that the thing being withheld is the slave himself. In that case, there is genuine ambiguity as to whether the slave was ever given his own person. But when something concrete like a house is excluded, and there is nothing left to transfer other than the slave himself, the master is effectively just freeing the slave \u8212- and Rabbi Shimon holds that this works.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafot\u8217's Explanation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rashi offers his own reading of how Rabbi Shimon\u8217's position relates to {\i palginan diburei}, but the discussion here follows Tosafot\u8217's explanation, which is more directly illuminating for the issue at hand.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot explains the connection as follows. Suppose the master owns a house and a slave, and he declares: \u8220"I am giving you all my property except for my house.\u8221" According to Rabbi Shimon, the slave goes free. But why should this work? The statement, taken as a whole, is an attempted transfer of property. If we say that excluding the house effectively means there is nothing left to give other than the slave himself, then this statement is really only operative with respect to the slave\u8217's {\i shichrur} \u8212- it cannot function as a gift of property because no property, apart from the slave\u8217's person, is actually being conveyed. The {\i gemara} therefore identifies this as a form of {\i palginan diburei}: you have one statement, \u8220"all my property,\u8221" but it is only going to take legal effect with respect to the slave\u8217's freedom, not with respect to any transfer of assets. That, says Tosafot, is what the {\i gemara} recognizes as analogous to Rava\u8217's position: one statement, {\i kol nechasai konuyim lach}, validated by {\i kiyum} only as a {\i shichrur}, not as a gift. The parallel is precise.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Ruling: R. Meir\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Halacha: R. Meir\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 At this point the {\i gemara} raises a challenge from a ruling attributed to Rav Nachman, as cited by Rav Yosef bar Minyumi: even though Rabbi Yoshiyah praised Rabbi Shimon, the {\i halacha} follows Rabbi Meir \u8212- which here means following the {\i Tanna Kamma} who holds that if anything is held back, if the transfer is in any way incomplete, the slave does not go free. This would appear to reject {\i palginan diburei} entirely, and by extension, to undermine Rava\u8217's position.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Yosei\u8217's Praise\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i baraita} records that when the words of Rabbi Shimon were brought before Rabbi Yosei, he applied to them the verse: {\i sefatayim yishak meshiv devarim nechochim} \u8212- \u8220"lips will kiss the one who returns proper words.\u8221" The verse is a mark of high praise. Tosafot offers a striking reading of what {\i sefatayim yishak} means in this context: when someone says something truly correct and compelling, those around him press their lips together \u8212- that is, they fall silent, because they have nothing to say in response. The brilliance of the answer leaves no room for rebuttal. It is not a warm kiss, but the silence of intellectual defeat.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Despite Praise\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Yet despite this extraordinary praise, the {\i gemara} affirms: we still rule like Rabbi Meir. We do not apply {\i palginan diburei}, and if anything at all is held back within the text, the {\i get} or {\i shichrur} does not take effect. This appears to be a decisive ruling against Rava.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Shachiv Mera\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Dying Donor Recovers\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} immediately presses back. Did Rav Nachman actually rule categorically against {\i palginan diburei}? There is another ruling attributed to him, also via Rav Yosef bar Minyumi: in the case of a {\i shachiv mera} \u8212- a person on his deathbed \u8212- who writes all his property to his slave, and then recovers, what is the outcome?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Split Effect Outcome\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The answer is striking: {\i chozer b\u8217'nechasim v\u8217'eino chozer b\u8217'eved} \u8212- he retracts the property but cannot retract the freeing of the slave. The property reverts because a deathbed gift is given on the assumption that the donor will die from his current illness; if he recovers, the gift returns to him. But the manumission does not revert, because {\i yatza lo shem ben chorin} \u8212- the slave has already been publicly proclaimed free, and once that status has been announced, it cannot be undone.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might ask: if technically speaking the {\i shichrur} was part of a deathbed gift that reverts upon recovery, why should the slave\u8217's status be any different? The {\i gemara}\u8217's invocation of {\i yatza lo shem} \u8212- a kind of proclaimed status \u8212- is admittedly unusual as a basis for {\i halacha}. The parallel offered in the shiur to a mistakenly announced Miss Universe winner captures the intuition: once the declaration has been made publicly, withdrawing it creates impossible social confusion. But how does that translate into hard {\i halachic} consequence?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafot on Institution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot addresses this directly. The entire institution of {\i matnas shachiv mera} is a rabbinic creation, and it operates under the parameters that the rabbis set for it. One of those parameters is that if you free a slave on your deathbed, even if you recover, the {\i shichrur} stands. That is simply how the rabbis structured the institution.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The key point for the present discussion, however, is what this ruling reveals about {\i palginan diburei}. The gift to the slave was made in a single statement \u8212- {\i kol nechasai knuyim lach}, all my property is given to you. And yet the outcome is that the slave receives his freedom but not the property. This is a classic application of {\i palginan diburei}: one sentence, two possible legal effects, and only one of them takes hold. It therefore appears that Rav Nachman himself \u8212- the very authority cited to rule against {\i palginan diburei} \u8212- applied it in the case of {\i shachiv mera}. This contradiction is what forces Abaye to clarify.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Lav Krisus\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 When It Applies\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye resolves the apparent contradiction. He concedes that Rava is correct: we do say {\i palginan diburei}. The {\i kiyum} of a {\i shtar shichrur} can free the slave even if it does not simultaneously effect a property transfer. Similarly, in the {\i shachiv mera} case, the {\i shichrur} holds even though the property reverts. These are genuine applications of {\i palginan diburei}, and they are valid.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Qualified vs.\u160 ?Unqualified\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The question then is: why, according to Rabbi Meir, does the slave {\i not} go free when the master says \u8220"all my property except for this plot of land\u8221"? If we apply {\i palginan diburei}, we should be able to let the {\i shichrur} take effect even if we disregard the property transfer \u8212- just as we do in the {\i shachiv mera} case.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye draws a critical distinction. In the {\i shachiv mera} case, the text of the document is unqualified. The statement {\i kol nechasai knuyim lach} contains no internal reservation \u8212- the split in effect occurs externally, through the operation of the {\i shachiv mera} institution, not through anything embedded in the language of the document itself. Similarly, when Rava speaks of a {\i kiyum} that validates the {\i shichrur} but not the property transfer, the document\u8217's text is clean; it is the {\i kiyum} mechanism that selectively applies. {\i Palginan diburei} means that the effects of an otherwise unqualified statement are split \u8212- one effect is operative, another is not.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Complete Severance\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But when the text itself contains a qualification \u8212- \u8220"all my property {\i except for} this house\u8221" \u8212- the problem is not a failure of {\i palginan diburei}. The problem is {\i lav krisus}: it is not a complete severance. The concept of {\i krisus} \u8212- severance \u8212- which the Torah requires of a {\i sefer krisus}, applies equally to a {\i get isha} and to a {\i get shel eved}. The parallel between divorce and manumission is striking precisely here: just as a {\i get isha} must effect a total and unconditional severance of the marital relationship, so too a {\i shtar shichrur} must effect a complete and unconditional release from servitude.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 When the document itself contains a reservation \u8212- when within the language of the {\i get} something is being held back \u8212- that is not a case of splitting effects externally. That is a document that, on its face, is not a full {\i krisus}. It is an incomplete severance, and no amount of {\i palginan diburei} can remedy that defect. The text itself is defective. This is the reason Rabbi Meir holds that the slave does not go free when a specific property is excepted: not because we reject {\i palginan diburei} in principle, but because the document\u8217's language is internally qualified, and an internally qualified document is {\i lav krisus} \u8212- it is not a {\i get} at all.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Irur & Validation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishnah: Challenge\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} addresses a different scenario: a {\i get} given in {\i Eretz Yisrael}, where the declaration of {\i befanai nichtav uvefanai nechtam} is not required. If such a {\i get} is subsequently challenged \u8212- {\i yatza alav irur} \u8212- its signatures must then be validated.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Number of Challengers\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} asks: how many challengers are needed to trigger this requirement? If only one person challenges the {\i get}, Rabbi Yochanan rules that universally, a single challenger does not constitute a valid {\i irur} \u8212- challenges require at least two people, and so a lone voice of protest should not require any validation. That seems to leave two challengers as the operative case. But if two people challenge the {\i get}, the {\i gemara} notes that we are now facing two witnesses against two witnesses \u8212- {\i trei u\u8217'trei ninhu} \u8212- and validation by an additional two witnesses would merely create an equal and opposite set of testimonies. {\i Mai chazit d\u8217'samchat ahani smoch ahani} \u8212- why should we favor one pair over the other? In that case, even successful validation would not seem sufficient.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Husband\u8217's Challenge\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} resolves both difficulties by identifying the operative case: the challenger is the husband himself. The husband who issued the {\i get} now claims it was a forgery \u8212- that he never wrote it, that the signatures are not authentic. His challenge is weighty enough to require a response, and so we demand that the signatures be validated. But once validation is obtained through proper witnesses, those witnesses overcome even the husband\u8217's own objection. The husband\u8217's challenge demands validation; validation satisfies it.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Get from Overseas\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Unable to Say\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} states: one who brings a {\i get} from {\i medinat hayam} and is unable ({\i eino yachol}) to say {\i befanai nichtav uvefanai nechtam} \u8212- if there are witnesses whose signatures appear on the document, it may be validated through those signatures. This provides an alternative route: though the agent cannot offer the standard oral declaration, the {\i get} may still be delivered once the signatures are confirmed through independent testimony.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} takes note of the language {\i eino yachol} \u8212- literally \u8220"he is unable\u8221" \u8212- and presses on its meaning. If the word is meant literally, suggesting a deaf-mute who is physically incapable of speech, then a prior question arises: can a deaf-mute ({\i cheresh}) serve as a {\i mevi get} at all? The {\i mishnah} in a different context explicitly excludes a {\i cheresh}, {\i shoteh}, and {\i katan} from delivering a {\i get}, since they lack the full intellectual standing \u8212- {\i bar deiah} \u8212- required for this role.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Gittin & Manumission\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Agunah & Iguan\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} states that in this regard \u8212- the requirement of {\i befanai nechtam} when bringing a {\i get} into or out of {\i Eretz Yisrael} \u8212- a {\i get} given to a woman and a {\i shtar shichrur} given to a slave are treated identically. This is introduced as one of the ways in which {\i gittei nashim} and {\i shichrurei avadim} are compared.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot raises an important question here. We understand why the rabbis instituted {\i befanai nechtam} for a woman: without it, there is a serious risk of {\i agunah} \u8212- a woman unable to remarry because her {\i get} cannot be properly authenticated. The concern for the {\i agunah} is a powerful and well-recognized institutional driver. But what parallel concern applies to the slave? What is the slave\u8217's {\i igun}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might argue the concern is marriage-related as well: if the slave has received a {\i shtar shichrur} of uncertain validity, he is caught in between \u8212- he cannot marry a free woman because he may still be a slave, and he cannot properly marry a slave woman because he may already be free. But Tosafot rejects this as a parallel to {\i agunah}, because the solution seems obvious: simply do not hand the {\i shtar shichrur} to the slave until the signatures have been validated. In the interim, the slave remains a slave and may continue living with a slave woman. There is no abandonment, no limbo \u8212- just a delay in delivery. The woman\u8217's situation is different: she is living apart from her husband without access to the {\i get}, with no recourse. That is {\i agunah}. The slave does not face the same predicament.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot answers that {\i igun} for the slave is not primarily about marriage at all. Simply withholding his freedom is itself an {\i igun}. The inability to live a fully free life \u8212- to enter into a normal marriage in the social framework of free people, and to take on the full complement of {\i mitzvot} \u8212- constitutes a deprivation that the rabbis wanted to address. When a master is in the process of freeing his slave, we want to facilitate that {\i shichrur} as smoothly as possible, in the same way that we work to prevent a woman from becoming an {\i agunah}. The slave\u8217's interest in freedom is weighty enough to justify parallel institutional protections.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Get from Overseas (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Deafness/Muteness\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Returning to the question raised by the {\i gemara} regarding {\i eino yachol lomar}: the straightforward reading might be that the agent is mute, physically unable to speak. But as noted, the language of the {\i mishnah} specifically raises the scenario in the context of an agent who is otherwise qualified to bring the {\i get}, while the {\i mishnah} elsewhere explicitly excludes a {\i cheresh} \u8212- a deaf-mute \u8212- from serving in this role.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Given While Able\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Yosef resolves this by clarifying the case: we are dealing with an agent who received the {\i get} and was fully competent \u8212- {\i pikeach} \u8212- at the time he took it. He was present, witnessed the writing and signing, and set out to deliver it. But before he could pronounce the declaration {\i befanai nichtav uvefanai nechtam}, he became a {\i cheresh}. He is therefore {\i eino yachol lomar} in the most literal sense: he was capable, he witnessed everything, but he is now physically unable to give voice to what he knows. In such a case, the {\i mishnah} permits the alternative route of signature validation. This is, admittedly, a peculiar case \u8212- the far simpler reading of {\i eino yachol} would be that the agent simply was not present at the writing. But the {\i gemara}\u8217's insistence on the precise phrasing {\i eino yachol} pushes toward an interpretation of genuine physical incapacity.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Gittin & Manumission (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Befanai Nechtam\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Returning now to the formal comparison between {\i gittei nashim} and {\i shichrurei avadim}, a {\i baraita} enumerates three ways in which they are alike. The first is the requirement of {\i befanai nechtam}: both a {\i get} delivered to a woman and a {\i shtar shichrur} delivered to a slave require the declaration when the document is being brought into or out of {\i Eretz Yisrael}. This was treated at length in the earlier part of the {\i masechet} and is now cited as the first formal point of parallel.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Samaritan Witnesses\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The second similarity \u8212- and a surprising one \u8212- is the rule regarding Samaritan witnesses. The {\i baraita} states: any document bearing a Samaritan ({\i Kuti}) witness is invalid \u8212- {\i except} for a {\i get isha} and a {\i shtar shichrur}. This is striking because these are precisely the documents one would expect to require the most rigorous standards, given that they effect fundamental changes in personal status. They are not merely evidentiary \u8212- they are the operative instruments of the status change itself.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Yet it is exactly this heightened significance that generates the {\i kula}. Because {\i gittei nashim} and {\i shichrurei avadim} are written with such extraordinary care and oversight, under so many more conditions and criteria than an ordinary commercial document, the mere fact that a Samaritan appears as a witness can itself be presumed to reflect carefulness. The assumption is that people involved in such a weighty process knew what they were doing \u8212- that this Samaritan witness was indeed a {\i kasher} witness, someone whose testimony can be relied upon. We will return to the specifics of this analysis, but the structural point is clear: the {\i chumra} of how {\i gittin} are written generates a {\i kula} in the assumptions we make about who signed them.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Non-Jewish Courts\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The third similarity is a {\i chumra}, and one that is entirely expected. As a general rule, documents produced by secular non-Jewish courts \u8212- {\i arkaot shel ovdei kochavim} \u8212- are valid, even when the signatories are non-Jews. For ordinary property documents, such as a bill of sale or a deed, we accept secular legal instruments as binding. However, {\i gittei nashim} and {\i shichrurei avadim} are explicitly excepted. A {\i get} or {\i shtar shichrur} issued by a non-Jewish court, signed by non-Jewish witnesses, is entirely invalid. These documents must conform to the requirements of {\i halacha}, and they cannot simply be processed through the general secular system as though they were property transactions.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Dina D\u8217'Malchuta\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rashi\u8217's explanation of why secular property documents are valid is worth examining closely, because he identifies the conceptual foundation in an illuminating way. Rashi states simply: {\i dina d\u8217'malchuta dina} \u8212- the law of the land is law. In matters of property and commerce, the Jewish legal system defers to the governing civil authority, and therefore a secular document recording a property transaction between two Jews is fully binding under {\i halacha}. There is no requirement to conduct the transaction through Jewish legal mechanisms such as a formal {\i kinyan} under the rules of {\i Choshen Mishpat}; the secular system is sufficient.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But Rashi adds a further dimension. He notes: {\i aval hadinim nitztavu b\u8217'nei Noach} \u8212- matters of law and adjudication are among the {\i sheva mitzvot b\u8217'nei Noach}, the seven Noahide laws binding on all humanity. Among those seven is the commandment to establish courts and a functioning legal order. Rashi appears to be saying that the {\i Torah} itself validates the non-Jewish legal system in matters of property and civil law, precisely because the obligation to maintain such a system is itself divinely mandated for all nations. It is this divine mandate \u8212- the {\i sheva mitzvot b\u8217'nei Noach} \u8212- that provides the conceptual basis for {\i dina d\u8217'malchuta dina}: the secular legal order is not merely tolerated, it is endorsed by the {\i Torah} as a legitimate system for adjudicating property matters.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This, however, has its limits. Non-Jewish legal authority does not extend to matters of {\i halacha} and personal status. {\i Gittin} and {\i shichrurei avadim} are outside the {\i parsha} of non-Jewish law entirely \u8212- {\i lav bnei kritut ninhu}, non-Jews are not within the framework of {\i gittin} and {\i kidushin}, and their documents and signatures cannot effect the changes that only {\i halacha} can effect.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Retraction Rights\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Both Retractable\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Meir identifies a fourth area of comparison between a {\i get} and a {\i shtar shichrur} \u8212- though on this point, Rabbi Meir and the {\i chakhamim} disagree. Rabbi Meir holds that if a man says \u8220"give this {\i get} to my wife\u8221" or \u8220"give this {\i shtar shichrur} to my slave,\u8221" and then before delivery changes his mind, he may retract in both cases. Both a {\i get} and a {\i shtar shichrur} can be recalled before they are actually delivered.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sages\u8217' View\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i chakhamim} disagree on the second half. They agree that a {\i get} can be retracted before delivery \u8212- the act of divorce is not complete until the {\i get} reaches the woman\u8217's hand or domain, and the husband retains the ability to reconsider until that moment. But a {\i shtar shichrur} cannot be retracted once the agent has received it. Why? Because {\i zakhin l\u8217'adam shelo befanav} \u8212- one may represent a person\u8217's interests, and act on his behalf, even without being formally appointed and without the person\u8217's presence, when the act is unambiguously to that person\u8217's benefit.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Zakhin Rationale\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The logic is as follows. When the master hands the {\i shtar shichrur} to an agent and says \u8220"give this to my slave,\u8221" there is no question whatsoever that the slave desires his freedom. Freedom is an obvious and unqualified benefit. Therefore, the moment the agent receives the {\i shtar}, he automatically becomes the slave\u8217's representative \u8212- not by the slave\u8217's appointment, not in the slave\u8217's presence, but by operation of the {\i zakhin} principle: since it is unambiguously in the slave\u8217's interest, the agent acquires it on his behalf. At the instant the agent takes the document, the slave\u8217's interests are already represented, and the {\i shichrur} effectively takes hold. The master can no longer retract.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Meir Dissents\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Meir challenges this reasoning. He argues that one cannot simply assume it is in every slave\u8217's interest to be free. The {\i zakhin} principle applies when the benefit is certain and uncontested. But freedom carries obligations \u8212- a freed slave must now observe all {\i mitzvot}, enters a different social and legal world, and may in fact be leaving a situation of relative security and stability. Rabbi Meir\u8217's position is that we cannot automatically presume every slave wishes to be free, and therefore we cannot apply {\i zakhin} to represent the slave\u8217's presumed desire. Without {\i zakhin}, the agent is no more than the master\u8217's messenger, and the master may recall his messenger \u8212- and his {\i shtar} \u8212- until the moment of actual delivery.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is worth noting the historical resonance of Rabbi Meir\u8217's position. The logic that a slave\u8217's interests are served by remaining enslaved was precisely the argument deployed by slaveholders in another era to justify the institution. That Rabbi Meir invokes a version of this reasoning as a matter of {\i halachic} analysis \u8212- questioning whether freedom is automatically and universally beneficial \u8212- is itself a striking feature of this passage, one that the {\i gemara} leaves for us to sit with.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Gittin 10\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Get & Shtar Similarities\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Intro & Baraita\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 We begin on amud 10b, picking up from the {\i mishna}\u8217's observation that both a {\i get} for a woman and a {\i shtar shichrur} \u8212- a writ of manumission for a slave \u8212- share the requirement of {\i befanai nichtav uvefanai nechtam}. The {\i gemara} had quoted a {\i baraita} listing several points of comparison between these two documents, and that {\i baraita} functions almost as a foreshadowing of the mishnayot that follow, since each case it enumerates will be addressed in turn. It is worth briefly reviewing the list.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Befanai Requirement\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tanu rabbanan}: in three ways are {\i gittei nashim} and {\i shichrurei avadim} alike. The first is {\i shetzarich lomar befanai nichtav uvefanai nechtam} \u8212- the delivering agent must declare that the document was written and signed in his presence.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Samaritan Witnesses\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The second point: any {\i get} \u8212- meaning any {\i shtar} \u8212- that bears the signature of a Samaritan witness, an {\i ed Kuti}, is invalid, {\i chutz migittei nashim u\u8217'shichrurei avadim} \u8212- except for a {\i get} for a woman and a writ of manumission for a slave. That is a striking leniency, and we will return to it in the mishnayot that follow.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Non-Jewish Courts\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The third point: all documents that emerge from non-Jewish courts, {\i arkaot shel akum}, are valid even though non-Jews signed them \u8212- with the exception of a {\i get} for a woman and a {\i shtar shichrur} for a slave. The general validity of secular documents for monetary matters flows from the principles of {\i dinei mamonot} and {\i dina d\u8217'malchuta dina}: such documents function as evidence that a transaction occurred, much as we rely today on bank-processed deeds without requiring a formally executed {\i halachic} instrument. But a {\i get} and a {\i shtar shichrur} are not merely evidentiary; they are instruments that effect a change of personal status. They must therefore be executed according to {\i halacha} in the classical sense.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Meir Retraction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 According to Rabbi Meir, there is a fourth point of comparison: {\i omer ten get ze l\u8217'ishti v\u8217'shtar shichrur ze l\u8217'avdi, ratza lachazor b\u8217'shneihem yachazor} \u8212- if a person hands a messenger a {\i get} for his wife or a {\i shtar shichrur} for his slave for delivery, he may retract either before it reaches its intended recipient.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Zachin Dispute\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Why is this even a question? One might assume that of course the master can retract \u8212- the document has not yet been delivered. The answer is that the {\i chachamim} disagree with Rabbi Meir specifically regarding the {\i shtar shichrur}. Their view is grounded in the principle of {\i zachin l\u8217'adam shelo befanav}: one may act in another person\u8217's interest without their knowledge or consent. Since freedom is unambiguously a {\i zechut} \u8212- a benefit \u8212- for the slave, the moment the master hands the document to the messenger, that messenger automatically becomes an agent on the slave\u8217's behalf. The slave is thereby freed at that moment, even before the document reaches his hands. By a {\i get} for a woman, we cannot assume she wishes to be divorced, so she is not divorced until the {\i get} reaches her; but for the slave\u8217's {\i shtar}, receipt by the messenger suffices. Rabbi Meir disagrees: it is not self-evidently in the slave\u8217's best interest to be freed \u8212- freedom carries its own obligations and uncertainties \u8212- and therefore the slave is not freed until the document actually reaches him. That is Rabbi Meir\u8217's fourth point of comparison between {\i get} and {\i shtar shichrur}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then asks: the {\i chachamim} say there are three ways \u8212- {\i bishlama l\u8217'rabbanan minyana l\u8217'matuhei had\u8217'Rabbi Meir} \u8212- the number three is meant to exclude Rabbi Meir\u8217's position, to say we do not count that fourth case as a shared characteristic, since for the {\i chachamim} the slave is freed the moment the messenger receives the {\i shtar}, making it different from the {\i get}. But what does Rabbi Meir\u8217's number four exclude? {\i L\u8217'matuhei ha d\u8217'tanya} \u8212- it excludes what we learn in the following {\i baraita}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Illiterate Witnesses\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Stencil Method\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i baraita} addresses witnesses who do not know how to sign their own names. {\i Mekayemim lahem niyar chalak u\u8217'memalim et hakera\u8217'im d\u8217'yo}: one tears a blank piece of paper and fills the cutouts with ink \u8212- in other words, one fashions a stencil and has the witnesses fill in the letters of their name. That counts as a valid signature, even though it bears none of the distinctive character of the witness\u8217's own handwriting.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Get Nashim Leniency\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Amar Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel: bameh devarim amurim \u8212- be\u8217'gittei nashim}. Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel holds that this stencil allowance applies only to a {\i get} for a woman. {\i Aval shichrurei avadim u\u8217'she\u8217'ar kol shetarot \u8212- im yode\u8217'im likrot v\u8217'lachtom chotmim v\u8217'im lav ein chotmim}: for a writ of manumission or any other document, if the witnesses know how to read and sign they may do so, and if not they may not sign at all. Remarkably, the leniency is greater precisely for the weightiest personal-status document of all. The reason, as Rashi explains, is {\i mishum takkanat agunos}: the concern that a husband may be about to embark on a sea voyage, or is gravely ill and may soon die, creating a {\i yibbum} situation. The wife needs that {\i get}, and we therefore extend special allowances to make it possible. No such leniency is extended to {\i shichrurei avadim} or any other document.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 So what is Rabbi Meir excluding with his number four? He is saying that the leniency of the stencil and reading assistance is not something shared uniquely by {\i get} and {\i shtar shichrur} \u8212- either it applies to all documents ({\i tanna kamma}\u8217's position) or it applies only to a {\i get} and not to a {\i shtar shichrur} (Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel\u8217's position). In neither case is it a point of symmetry between the two documents, and that is what his count of four is meant to signal.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Cannot Read/Sign\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} pauses on Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel\u8217's formulation \u8212- {\i im yode\u8217'im likrot v\u8217'lachtom} \u8212- and raises a difficulty: {\i kriya man dachar shemei}? Who mentioned reading? The entire prior discussion concerned only signing, not reading. The {\i gemara} answers: {\i chisura mechsara v\u8217'hachi katani} \u8212- the {\i baraita} is abbreviated and must be read as follows. If witnesses do not know how to read, {\i korin lifneihem v\u8217'chotmin} \u8212- the document is read aloud before them and they then sign with their own signatures. If they do not know how to sign, one makes the stencil for them. Both accommodations can be applied simultaneously: the document can be read to witnesses who cannot read it themselves, and a stencil provided to witnesses who cannot write their own names.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ed Mipi Ed Issue\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} raises a pointed question on this arrangement. If someone reads the {\i shtar} to me and I sign my name to it, am I not in the position of {\i ed mi\u8217'pi ed} \u8212- a witness testifying based on another witness\u8217's account rather than direct knowledge? I only know what is in the document because you told me what is in it. How can I affix my signature to something I have not myself read?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} resolves this by noting that the witnesses are not being asked to testify that certain events occurred; they are attesting that what is written in the {\i shtar} is what is written there \u8212- {\i she\u8217'ein tzarich l\u8217'ha\u8217'id al ikkar hama\u8217'aseh ella she\u8217'omer she\u8217'kach katuv bash\u8217'tar}. My signature does not say \u8220"I witnessed a transaction\u8221"; it says \u8220"I affirm that these are the words of this document.\u8221" The distinction matters. Still, {\i Tosafot} acknowledges a degree of awkwardness: you are telling me the contents of the document and I am then signing to them, which carries at least a resemblance to {\i ed mi\u8217'pi ed}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One can press the question further depending on the type of document. A {\i shtar ra\u8217'aya} \u8212- an evidentiary document \u8212- essentially has the witnesses saying: these words in the document represent what occurred. A {\i shtar kinyan}, a document that effects a legal act such as a {\i get}, is somewhat different: the witnesses are not so much reporting past events as they are giving voice to the husband\u8217's expressed will, validating and instantiating it. My signature on a {\i get} is my attestation that this is the intent of the husband \u8212- I am, in a sense, lending my testimony to the act itself rather than to an underlying factual event. Either way, there are two remarkable allowances visible in this {\i baraita}: that a stencil-filled name counts as a signature, and that a witness who was read the document rather than reading it himself can validly attest to its contents. According to the {\i tanna kamma} both allowances are available for all {\i shtarot}, which is quite far-reaching; according to Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel they are restricted to {\i gittei nashim}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Death Before Delivery\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Post-Mortem Invalidity\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now asks: {\i v\u8217'tu lika} \u8212- are there no additional cases in which a {\i get} and a {\i shtar shichrur} are alike? {\i Ve-ha\u8217'ika} \u8212- consider the case: {\i ha\u8217'omer tenu get zeh l\u8217'ishti v\u8217'shtar shichrur zeh l\u8217'avdi umet lo yitnu achar mitah}. If a man says \u8212- using the plural form, {\i tenu}, addressed to witnesses \u8212- \u8220"write and deliver a {\i get} to my wife\u8221" or \u8220"write and deliver a {\i shtar shichrur} to my slave,\u8221" and he then dies, those documents may not be given after his death. One cannot divorce one\u8217's wife posthumously, nor free one\u8217's slave posthumously. Rashi notes the significance of the plural: the man is speaking to multiple people, meaning the document has not yet been written. (If a completed {\i shtar shichrur} had already been handed to a single messenger, the slave would have been freed immediately per the {\i chachamim}\u8217's view \u8212- but here nothing has been written yet, no one can receive it on the slave\u8217's behalf, and the master dies.)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shechiv Merah Property\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The contrast is with a dying man who says {\i tenu maneh l\u8217'ploni} \u8212- \u8220"give such-and-such sum to so-and-so\u8221" \u8212- and dies. In that case, the bequest {\i is} honored after death. This flows from either the principle of {\i mitzvah lekayem divrei hamet} \u8212- the obligation to fulfill the expressed wishes of the deceased \u8212- or, as Rashi frames it, from the principle of {\i divrei shechiv merah kichtuvim uchimsorim dami}: the words of a deathbed declaration are treated as if they were already written and delivered, so that upon his death the property transfer takes effect automatically. The question of which principle governs \u8212- whether the heirs bear an obligation to carry out his wishes, or whether the transfer takes legal effect at the moment of death \u8212- is a broader discussion in {\i halacha}; but either way, money bequeathed in this fashion is distributed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Why does the same reasoning not apply to freeing a slave or divorcing a wife? The answer: freeing a slave is not simply a transfer of property. Even if we say {\i give my slave to Reuven} might resemble a property transfer, {\i free my slave} implicates personal status and requires a specific {\i shtar shichrur} executed by the current master. The wife\u8217's case is even more obvious \u8212- she is already a {\i widow}, an {\i almanah}, and the {\i get} is moot. Neither situation is analogous to straightforward property distribution.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Living Principal\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} presses further. What if the dying man said: write a {\i document} giving a hundred dinars to so-and-so, and then he dies \u8212- may the document be written and delivered posthumously? {\i Shema lo gamar lehaknoto ela beshtar ve\u8217'ein shtar le\u8217'achar mitah}: perhaps he intended the transfer to be effected only through a {\i shtar}, and a {\i shtar} has no validity once the principal has died. A {\i shtar} is, at its core, an expression of the living will of the party who executes it. It cannot be written on behalf of someone already dead, and even if written, it cannot take effect without a living principal behind it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shtar vs Property\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The distinction therefore is this: if a person on his deathbed distributes property without requiring a {\i shtar} \u8212- simply declaring who gets what \u8212- the transfer can take effect at his death, functioning much like an inheritance assignment. But if he stipulates that the transfer is to be accomplished through a {\i shtar}, the {\i shtar} must be executed while he lives. This principle applies equally to all {\i shtarot} \u8212- whether a {\i get}, a {\i shtar shichrur}, or a deed of sale. Since this is not a point of distinction unique to {\i get} and {\i shtar shichrur} but rather a general rule governing all {\i shtarot}, the {\i baraita} does not include it in its list.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Lishmah/Mechubar\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbinic Only\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now asks: {\i v\u8217'tu lika} \u8212- why not include other requirements that apply equally to {\i get} and {\i shtar shichrur}? Specifically, {\i va-ha ika lishmah} \u8212- both require that the document be written {\i lishmah}, for the sake of this particular woman or this particular slave \u8212- and {\i va-ha ika mechubar} \u8212- both must be written on something that is detached from the ground, not still attached ({\i mechubar}), following the principle of {\i ve-katav ve-natan}. These are genuine shared requirements. Why are they absent from the list?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} answers: {\i ki tani pesula de-rabbanan} \u8212- the {\i baraita} lists only rabbinic disqualifications. {\i De-oraita lo tani} \u8212- biblical requirements were not included in the enumeration.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Court Challenge\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This answer is immediately challenged: {\i ve-ha arkaot} \u8212- what about the case of non-Jewish courts, which is listed? That would appear to be a biblical disqualification: a {\i get} executed through a non-Jewish court with non-Jewish signatories is biblically invalid. How can the list be limited to rabbinic matters if this case is included?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Eidei Mesira\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} responds: the case of non-Jewish court documents is only rabbinically problematic, because we are operating within the framework of Rabbi Eliezer\u8217's view \u8212- {\i d\u8217'amar eidim mesira kirti} \u8212- that what effects the {\i get} is the witnesses present at the moment of delivery, not the witnesses who signed the document. If so, a {\i get} delivered before valid Jewish witnesses is biblically valid even if non-Jews signed it. The non-Jewish signatures are only a rabbinic concern. One must imagine the specific scenario in which the document was written in accordance with {\i halacha} \u8212- perhaps by a Jewish scribe \u8212- but subsequently registered and signed in a non-Jewish court. In that case, it was delivered before kosher witnesses, is biblically valid, and the defect of non-Jewish signatures is rabbinic only.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Shimon Agrees\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} supports this by cross-referencing the later {\i mishna}: {\i Rabbi Shimon omer af elu kesherim} \u8212- Rabbi Shimon validates a {\i get} bearing non-Jewish signatures entirely. As Rav Zeira explains: {\i yarad Rabbi Shimon l\u8217'shitato shel Rabbi Eliezer d\u8217'amar eidim mesira kirti} \u8212- Rabbi Shimon aligns himself with Rabbi Eliezer\u8217's position that the witnesses of delivery are what matter. This implies that the {\i tanna kamma} in that {\i mishna}, who invalidates such a {\i get}, must hold that biblical validity depends on the signed witnesses. One might therefore conclude that the {\i tanna kamma} considers the non-Jewish signatures a biblical problem \u8212- which would undercut the {\i gemara}\u8217's claim that the list contains only rabbinic disqualifications.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} deflects: {\i ika beinaihu shemot muvhakin} \u8212- the debate between the {\i tanna kamma} and Rabbi Shimon is not about biblical validity at all. Both may hold like Rabbi Eliezer that it is biblically valid by virtue of the delivery witnesses. The debate is whether it is {\i rabbinically} valid. Even rabbinically, asks the {\i gemara}, why should it be invalid? Because we worry that people will rely on the non-Jewish signatures rather than investigating whether valid delivery witnesses exist. Now, if the signatures are obviously non-Jewish names \u8212- say, recognizably non-Jewish \u8212- then Rabbi Shimon is not concerned that anyone will rely on them, and he validates the {\i get}. The {\i tanna kamma}, however, does not permit any {\i get} bearing non-Jewish names, regardless of how obvious, out of concern for the general principle. Both positions, then, treat the non-Jewish signatures as a rabbinic concern only, and the list is consistent.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Unique to Get\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But then the {\i gemara} raises a fresh challenge: {\i va-hachazara de-oraita ve-katani} \u8212- the case of retraction ({\i chazara}), which Rabbi Meir lists as the fourth point, is a biblical matter. The husband\u8217's ability to retract before delivery is not a rabbinic construct. So the claim that the list is limited to rabbinic invalidations cannot stand.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} therefore abandons that organizing principle entirely and substitutes another: {\i ki ktani milta d\u8217'leisa b\u8217'kiddushin, milta d\u8217'isa b\u8217'kiddushin lo ktani} \u8212- the list enumerates only those features that are unique to {\i get} and {\i shtar shichrur} and do not apply to {\i kiddushin}. Cases that are equally true of a {\i shtar kiddushin} were not included.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Under this rubric, {\i lishmah} and {\i mechubar} are excluded because they apply to {\i shtar kiddushin} as well \u8212- we derive {\i shtar kiddushin}\u8217's requirements from {\i get}, so the same standards apply. By contrast, {\i befanai nichtav} is not required for a {\i shtar kiddushin} because there is no {\i agunah} concern in the context of marriage. The {\i ed Kuti} leniency does not extend to {\i kiddushin}. And the matter of non-Jewish court signatures: Rashi explains that while the same underlying concern about reliance on non-kosher signatures technically exists in {\i kiddushin}, the cost of rabbinically invalidating a {\i shtar kiddushin} bearing non-Jewish signatures would be far too severe \u8212- if we said she is not betrothed, she would go marry another man, which would constitute a grave violation. Therefore the rabbinic disqualification was never applied to {\i kiddushin} in the first place, and the point remains one that distinguishes {\i get} and {\i shtar shichrur} from {\i kiddushin}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Chazara Analysis\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chazara Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having established that the organizing principle is uniqueness relative to {\i kiddushin}, the {\i gemara} immediately challenges whether {\i chazara} \u8212- retraction \u8212- truly qualifies. {\i Ve\u8217'hachazara gufa ita be\u8217'kiddushin}: retraction exists in {\i kiddushin} too. If I send a {\i shaliach} with a ring to betroth a woman and I retract before she receives it, she is obviously not betrothed. So why is {\i chazara} listed as something unique to {\i get} and {\i shtar shichrur}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two-Sided vs One\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} answers that the {\i chiddush} of retraction is not that retraction {\i is possible}, but rather that it is still possible even given the unilateral nature of the act. A {\i get} and a {\i shtar shichrur} are both acts the husband or master may perform against the other party\u8217's will \u8212- {\i bishlichut ba\u8217'al karchah}. In {\i kiddushin}, by contrast, the woman must consent; the entire act is bilateral and depends entirely on her acceptance. It is therefore self-evident that the husband can retract before she receives anything, because nothing is even close to finalized until she agrees. There is no {\i hava amina} that delivery to the {\i shaliach} alone could have effected the marriage.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 With a {\i get} or a {\i shtar shichrur}, however, since the act is unilateral and requires no agreement from the other side, one might have thought that handing the document to the {\i shaliach} already finalizes the matter \u8212- particularly in the case of {\i shtar shichrur}, where the {\i chachamim} argue the slave is freed at that moment through {\i zachin l\u8217'adam shelo befanav}. Rabbi Meir\u8217's teaching is precisely that even in these unilateral transactions, retraction remains possible before the document reaches its recipient. That is a genuine {\i chiddush} \u8212- novel and non-obvious \u8212- which is why it merits inclusion in the list. By {\i kiddushin} no such novel teaching is needed.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Samaritan Leniency\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishna Rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now turns to examine the individual cases from the {\i baraita}, beginning with the {\i mishna}: {\i kol get sheyesh alav ed Kuti pasul} \u8212- any {\i get} (meaning any {\i shtar}) bearing a Samaritan witness is invalid \u8212- {\i chutz migittei nashim u\u8217'shichrurei avadim} \u8212- except for a {\i get} for a woman and a {\i shtar shichrur} for a slave.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} then recounts a {\i maaseh}: a {\i get} was brought before Rabban Gamliel in a village, and its witnesses were both Samaritan witnesses \u8212- {\i edei Kutim} \u8212- and he validated it. This is striking, because until now the formulation {\i ed Kuti} suggested that we are speaking of one Samaritan alongside a Jewish witness. This precedent suggests that even two Samaritan witnesses might suffice, at least for Rabban Gamliel.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tanaic Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Before the {\i gemara} can identify whose opinion underlies the {\i mishna}, it presents a {\i baraita} setting out three distinct positions on the reliability of Samaritans ({\i Kutim}), using the question of {\i matzat Kuti} \u8212- Samaritan-baked {\i matzah} \u8212- as the test case.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i tanna kamma} holds that Samaritan {\i matzah} is not only permissible but one may even fulfill the {\i mitzvah} of eating {\i matzah} on {\i Pesach} with it. This means the {\i tanna kamma} trusts Samaritans to have baked the {\i matzah} with the intention of {\i shimur}, watching for the sake of the {\i mitzvah} \u8212- {\i leshem chovat matzah} \u8212- a requirement drawn from {\i uvaerev tochelu matzot}. Even knowing it was not {\i chametz} would not by itself be enough to fulfill the {\i mitzvah} of {\i matzah}; the {\i matzah} must have been guarded with the proper intent. The fact that the {\i tanna kamma} assumes Samaritans achieve this standard represents a remarkable degree of trust, since this is precisely the kind of detail \u8212- a {\i talmud Torah} she-be\u8217'al peh requirement about intentionality \u8212- that one would not obviously expect Samaritans to follow.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Eliezer disagrees sharply: {\i oser lefi she\u8217'ein beki\u8217'in bedikdukei mitzvot} \u8212- they are not expert in the fine points of the commandments, and one must therefore be concerned not only that the {\i matzah} may fail the {\i mitzvah} requirement but that it may even be {\i chametz}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel takes an intermediate position: {\i kol mitzvah shehechziku bah Kutim harbeh medakdekim bah yoter mi-Yisrael} \u8212- any {\i mitzvah} that the Samaritans have taken hold of, they observe with even greater punctiliousness than natural-born Jews. The practical implication is that one must evaluate each {\i mitzvah} individually: has this particular practice been embraced by the Samaritan community or not? If yes, one may rely on them completely in that area; if not, one cannot rely on them at all.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The relevant background here is that the Samaritans\u8217' status as Jews is itself contested. Their ancestors were resettled in the Land of Israel toward the end of the First Temple period, initially continuing pagan practices, eventually adopting the laws of the land\u8217's God after the famous episode of the lions \u8212- and the question of whether that constituted a valid conversion has been debated ever since. The Samaritans maintained their own {\i Sefer Torah} with significant textual variants and developed their own forms of observance. The debate in the {\i baraita} is not about whether they are considered Jewish \u8212- all three opinions appear to assume they are \u8212- but about how much one can rely on the quality of their observance in any given area.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Opinion ID\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Manni matnitin} \u8212- whose view does our {\i mishna} reflect, given that it is lenient with Samaritan witnesses specifically for {\i get} and {\i shtar shichrur} but not for other documents?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i tanna kamma} cannot be the source: if one trusts Samaritans without qualification, their witnesses should be valid for all documents, not just these two. Rabbi Eliezer cannot be the source: if one never trusts Samaritans, even {\i get} and {\i shtar shichrur} should be excluded. Only Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel, with his context-sensitive approach, can logically support the {\i mishna}\u8217's distinction. And so the {\i gemara} initially proposes: the {\i mishna} follows Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel, who holds that Samaritans have embraced the standards required for {\i get} and {\i shtar shichrur} but not for other documents.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 One Witness Puzzle\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} immediately challenges this: {\i im hachi mai irya chad} \u8212- if Samaritans are trusted in this area, why does the {\i mishna} speak of one Samaritan witness? If they are reliable for {\i get} and {\i shtar shichrur}, two should be equally valid. {\i Afilu terei nami} \u8212- even two of them ought to be acceptable. And the {\i mishna} implies that only one is acceptable alongside a Jewish witness.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Conversely, if we say the {\i mishna} follows Rabbi Eliezer, who does not trust Samaritans at all \u8212- then why is even one {\i ed Kuti} allowed for a {\i get} and a {\i shtar shichrur}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This difficulty renders the easy identification of the {\i mishna}\u8217's author impossible. Moreover, the question of what it even means for Samaritans to have \u8220"embraced\u8221" the practice of signing {\i gittin} as opposed to other documents is conceptually puzzling. Observance of {\i Shabbat} or {\i matzah} is a ritual one can adopt. But signing documents honestly? The question of whether they are {\i ne\u8217'emanim} in signing a {\i get} versus signing a bill of sale seems to reduce to a question about truthfulness in different contexts: do they take personal-status documents more seriously than commercial ones? One could construct such an argument \u8212- similar to observing that even in contemporary society people may treat matters of personal legal standing with greater gravity than commercial paperwork \u8212- but it is admittedly an unusual axis on which to divide trustworthiness.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} also notes a further dimension: a {\i gemara} elsewhere raises the problem of {\i lifnei iver} \u8212- even if Samaritans are personally careful about a given practice, it does not necessarily follow that they will be careful on your behalf. So even if a Samaritan\u8217's own {\i matzah} is beyond reproach, how do you know they guarded {\i your} matzah with sufficient care?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Validation Theory\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Elazar resolves the puzzle about one witness: {\i lo hechshiru bo ela ed echad Kuti bilvad} \u8212- we validated only a single Samaritan alongside a single Jewish witness. And the mechanism is not that Samaritans are trusted in this area, but rather that the presence of the Jewish signature implicitly validates the Samaritan\u8217's. {\i D\u8217'i lav d\u8217'Kuti chaver hava, lo havei machtim lei mikamei} \u8212- if this Samaritan were not a trustworthy individual, the Jewish witness would not have allowed his name to appear above his own, or equivalently, the Jew would not have signed his name below the Samaritan\u8217's. By affixing his signature after the Samaritan\u8217's, the Jewish witness implicitly testifies to the reliability of the Samaritan. The document does not depend on trusting Samaritans as a class; it depends on the individual Jewish signatory vouching, through his subsequent signature, for this particular Samaritan. {\i Tosafot} notes the corollary: biblically, the Samaritan\u8217's signature may in principle be acceptable; the concern about reliability is rabbinic, and the Jewish co-signature resolves it rabbinically.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Validation Problem\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} immediately challenges: {\i ei hachi, afilu she\u8217'ar shtarot nami} \u8212- if this validation mechanism works, why not extend it to all documents? If a Jewish witness signs after a Samaritan, he implicitly endorses the Samaritan\u8217's signature regardless of what kind of document it is. Why is the allowance limited to {\i get} and {\i shtar shichrur}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The answer: {\i ravcha shavik l\u8217'man d\u8217'kashish minei} \u8212- a witness may leave a blank line above his signature as a courtesy, allowing for a more senior or distinguished person to sign in the space above him. If I sign on the second line, it does not necessarily mean I knew who signed above me or implicitly endorsed them. Perhaps I simply left that line open for an honoree, and you later procured a Samaritan to fill it in \u8212- in a different room, at a different time \u8212- without my knowledge. My signing second therefore does not establish that I witnessed the Samaritan\u8217's signature or validated it. This concern applies equally to {\i get} as to any other document: I might have left a blank line on the {\i get} too for a more senior witness, and the Samaritan filled it afterward.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mutual Presence\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Amar Rav Pappa zos omeres} \u8212- this tells us, says Rav Pappa, that {\i eidei ha-get ein chasmin zeh belo zeh} \u8212- witnesses signing a {\i get} must all sign in each other\u8217's presence. This heightened requirement \u8212- which does not apply to other documents \u8212- is what makes the validation mechanism work for {\i get} alone. Because all witnesses must be present simultaneously when the {\i get} is signed, if the Jewish witness signed after the Samaritan, he was necessarily in the room when the Samaritan signed. He cannot claim he left a blank line and someone else filled it in later; the rule is that all signatures must occur in mutual presence. His subsequent signature therefore does constitute an implicit endorsement of the Samaritan\u8217's signature, in a way that is simply not possible with other documents where no such requirement exists.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kulchem Fraud\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Amar Rav Ashi gezeira mishum kulchem} \u8212- Rav Ashi explains the reason for this heightened requirement. A husband may stipulate at the time of the {\i get}\u8217's writing that all present in the room must sign \u8212- {\i kulchem}, all of you. This is consistent with the notion that a {\i get} is, in a sense, the embodiment of the husband\u8217's will, and he may impose more demanding conditions on its validity. If he says {\i kulchem}, then the {\i get} is valid only if every person present signs; if fewer than all of them sign, the {\i get} is {\i pasul}. This creates a real danger: a husband could invoke {\i kulchem}, witnesses could then scatter, and a deliverer could collect signatures from two or three of the original witnesses while leaving others out. The resulting document would appear facially valid \u8212- it bears two {\i kosher} signatures \u8212- but would be {\i pasul} because not all whom the husband designated signed it. The woman would believe she is divorced when she is not.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 To prevent this fraud, the {\i gemara} requires that all witnesses sign in each other\u8217's presence. That way, if {\i kulchem} was invoked, no witness can sign without all others being present to refuse to participate if the full quorum is not in the room. The requirement of {\i ain chasmin zeh belo zeh} is thus a protective enactment to ensure that the {\i kulchem} stipulation cannot be circumvented. And as a byproduct of this rule, a Jewish witness who signs after a Samaritan necessarily witnessed the Samaritan\u8217's signature, lending the validation effect that Rav Elazar described.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Conclusion\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 With this structure in place, the {\i gemara} returns to Rav Elazar\u8217's teaching: {\i lo hechshiru bo ela ed echad Kuti bilvad}. The {\i mishna}\u8217's rule of {\i ed Kuti} refers to one Samaritan, with a later-signing Jewish witness providing the implicit validation. {\i Amar Abaye tani eido} \u8212- Abaye reads the {\i maaseh} of Rabban Gamliel\u8217's case as involving a single Samaritan witness (removing the letter {\i yud} from {\i eidav} to read {\i eido}, \u8220"his witness\u8221"). {\i Rava amar le-olam terei} \u8212- Rava preserves the plural: Rabban Gamliel indeed validated a {\i get} with two Samaritan witnesses. {\i Rabban Gamliel miflag palig} \u8212- Rabban Gamliel takes the more lenient position; the text requires a {\i chisura mechsara} supplement to read: Rabban Gamliel validates even with two Samaritan witnesses. He may hold, consistent with the more trusting of the {\i tana\u8217'ic} views, that Samaritans are generally reliable in the area of {\i gittin} \u8212- whether because they have embraced that domain of practice or because he broadly trusts their testimony.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The overall picture, then, is this: the general rule is that Samaritan witnesses are invalid for signing documents. For {\i get} and {\i shtar shichrur}, a single Samaritan witness is permitted under the standard view, because the Jewish witness who signs afterward necessarily validates the Samaritan\u8217's signature \u8212- a mechanism enabled by the unique requirement of {\i ain chasmin zeh belo zeh}, itself enacted to prevent the {\i kulchem} fraud. Rabban Gamliel goes further, permitting two Samaritan witnesses on a {\i get}, apparently operating from a more expansive trust in Samaritan reliability in matters of personal status.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Gittin 11\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Non-Jewish Courts\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishnah Overview\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} on 10b continues the discussion of ways in which {\i gittin} for women and {\i shtar shichrur} for slaves share similarities \u8212- or possibly differences. The {\i mishna} states: {\i kol shtaros ha\u8217'olim be\u8217'arka\u8217'os shel ovdei kochavim af al pi she\u8217'chosemim ovdei kochavim kesherim} \u8212- all monetary documents that have come out of non-Jewish courts, even though they are signed by non-Jews, are {\i kesherim}. This is striking. The word {\i arka\u8217'os} denotes an established court, and the {\i mishna} is saying that documents processed through such courts are valid.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Get & Manumission\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} then excepts: {\i chutz mi\u8217'gittei nashim u\u8217'shichrurei avadim} \u8212- with the exception of a {\i get} for a woman and a writ of manumission for a slave. This exception is not surprising. Monetary matters are one thing, but a {\i get} and a {\i shtar shichrur} \u8212- the latter derived from and governed by the same laws as {\i gittin} \u8212- change personal status. The entire {\i masechet} is concerned with the precise {\i halachic} requirements for how such documents must be executed, so naturally they cannot be valid when issuing from a non-Jewish court. What is surprising, as we will see, is that even the monetary documents are {\i kasher}, and the {\i Gemara} will explain why.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Shimon\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 For a still greater surprise, {\i Rebbi Shimon} states: {\i af elu kesherim} \u8212- even a {\i get} for a woman and a {\i shtar shichrur} for a slave are {\i kosher} when they come from a non-Jewish court and are signed by non-Jews. {\i Lo huzkeru ela bizman shena\u8217'asu behedyot} \u8212- the only time it is a problem is when they are executed in a private court rather than an established communal court.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 How could this possibly be {\i kosher}? The {\i Gemara} will explain that {\i Rebbi Shimon} follows the position of {\i Rebbi Elazar}, who holds {\i eidi mesirah karshei} \u8212- that the witnesses who matter for a {\i get} and a {\i shtar shichrur} are not the witnesses who sign the document but the witnesses present when it is handed over. The case therefore assumes that the actual writing of the document was done properly by a qualified Jewish {\i sofer} with the correct intent, and all relevant {\i halachos} were followed. The genuine {\i chidush} concerns only the signatures: since {\i eidi mesirah} are what effect the divorce, non-Jewish signatories do not invalidate the document, provided that valid Jewish witnesses are present at the handing over. The document itself must have been composed correctly; it is only the signatures whose disqualification is being set aside.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Monetary Evidence\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sales vs.\u160 ?Gifts\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} opens by noting that the {\i mishna} is categorical \u8212- {\i lo shna mecher lo shna matana} \u8212- it makes no distinction between a document attesting to a sale and one attesting to a gift. {\i Bishlama mecher}: a document of sale is understandable, because a non-Jewish court system would never have written a document of ownership had payment not been made in its presence. The act of acquisition {\i halachically} occurred through the payment of money, and the existence of this court document is absolute evidence that the sale took place. It is not functioning as a formal {\i shtar}; it functions as evidence \u8212- as reliable as a videotape of the transaction. Most {\i Rishonim} understand it precisely this way. Some frame it in terms of a rabbinic {\i takana}, but even without that, one can distinguish between formal testimony and evidence. The {\i Rishonim} in {\i Ashkenaz}, for instance, where women were involved in commerce, would accept a woman\u8217's word in monetary matters not as formal {\i eidus} but as solid evidentiary weight. That is all that is needed here: this document functions as compelling evidence that the transaction occurred, even if it does not satisfy the technical requirements of a formal {\i shtar}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The difficulty arises with a gift \u8212- {\i ella matana, b\u8217'mai kakani?} \u8212- how does one acquire the gifted property? One might say through {\i chazaka}, but there is no reason to think that is what the non-Jewish court is attesting to. The presumption must be that the acquisition is effected by this {\i shtar}. But how can a {\i shtar} function as an instrument of transfer if it was not executed in a Jewish court with Jewish witnesses? The {\i shtar}, in that case, is, as the {\i Gemara} says, {\i chaspa b\u8217'alma hu} \u8212- nothing more than a shard of pottery.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Dina Malchusa\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The answer is {\i Shmuel}\u8217's famous principle: {\i dina d\u8217'malchusa dina} \u8212- the law of the sovereign is the law. Technically, the document might not work as a {\i halachic} instrument, but it works because it is valid under secular law, and secular law is binding. This is of course a major {\i halachic} principle, subject to considerable debate among the {\i Rishonim} and {\i Acharonim} as to its precise scope. But as applied here, if one simply reflects on ordinary life \u8212- when one purchases something in a store, one does not pause to wonder whether {\i halachic} acquisition through {\i meshicha} or {\i hagba\u8217'ah} has occurred \u8212- the underlying assumption is that the modes by which society recognizes ownership transfers are {\i halachically} binding, and {\i dina d\u8217'malchusa dina} is one principled way of grounding that assumption.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Function vs.\u160 ?Instrument\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} offers a further resolution: {\i v\u8217'iba\u8217'is eima tani chutz migitei nashim v\u8217'shichrurei avadim} \u8212- if one prefers not to invoke {\i dina d\u8217'malchusa dina}, or if its scope is understood more narrowly, then the {\i mishna}\u8217's exception for a {\i get} is best read as defining a category. The {\i mishna} does not merely mean a {\i get} literally \u8212- it means the category of {\i shtar} that functions as an instrument. Documents from non-Jewish courts can work when they serve as evidence; they cannot work when they must serve as instruments, as a {\i get} must, and as a {\i shtar matana} must. On this reading, the {\i mishna}\u8217's exception implicitly encompasses any document that must function as a formal legal instrument rather than merely as evidence of an underlying transaction.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Edei Mesira\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Non-Jewish Witnesses\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having explained the {\i mishna}\u8217's first ruling, the {\i Gemara} returns to the position of {\i Rebbi Shimon} \u8212- that even a {\i get} signed by non-Jews and issuing from an established non-Jewish court is {\i kosher}. The {\i Gemara} challenges this immediately: {\i v\u8217'ha lo bnei krisus ninu} \u8212- non-Jews are not in the category of {\i krisus}. When the {\i Torah} speaks of a {\i sefer krisus}, a document of severance in divorce, it implies that those who create and attest to this instrument must themselves be subject to the laws of marriage and divorce. Non-Jews, standing outside this framework, would seem categorically incapable of producing a valid instrument of divorce.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafos} notes that the {\i Gemara} could have raised an even more basic objection \u8212- {\i havi matzi limifrach ha lo bnei eidus ninu} \u8212- that non-Jews are not valid witnesses at all. But the {\i Gemara} presses the more specific point: beyond their general disqualification as witnesses, non-Jews cannot create this type of instrument because they are entirely outside the framework of {\i krisus}. It is worth noting that some {\i Rishonim} raise the theoretical possibility that non-Jews, if one addresses only the concern about truthfulness, might technically qualify as witnesses in some sense \u8212- a striking suggestion that will receive further discussion on 12a.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Witnesses Matter\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rebbi Zeira} explains: {\i amar Rabbi Shimon le-shitato de-Rabbi Elazar de-amar eidi mesirah karshei} \u8212- {\i Rebbi Shimon} has fully embraced the position of {\i Rebbi Elazar}, who holds that it is the witnesses present at the handing over of the {\i get} who effect the divorce, not the signatories. There are two sets of witnesses relevant to a {\i get}: {\i edei chatimah}, those who sign it, and {\i edei mesirah}, those present at its delivery. For {\i Rebbi Elazar}, only the latter are decisive. Accordingly, if one ensures that qualified Jewish witnesses are present at the handing over, the non-Jewish signatures on the document are not an obstacle.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Forgery Risk\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} immediately raises a complication. {\i Ve-ha-amar Rabbi Abba: modeh Rabbi Elazar bi-mzuyaf mi-tocho she-pasul} \u8212- did not {\i Rebbi Abba} say that even {\i Rebbi Elazar} concedes that a document considered {\i mezuyaf mi-tocho}, forged from within, is invalid? The problem here is not that the document is actually forged, but that having invalid signatories on a {\i get} creates a functionally analogous concern: the document may be relied upon improperly.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The danger operates on two levels. Some {\i Rishonim} hold that according to {\i Rebbi Elazar}, while {\i eidi mesirah} are primary, one may in the absence of {\i eidi mesirah} fall back on the {\i edei chatimah}. From that perspective, a {\i get} signed by non-Jews is acutely dangerous: one might hand it over without any Jewish witnesses present, relying instead on the non-Jewish signatories. Even for those who hold that {\i eidi mesirah} are always required and {\i edei chatimah} can never stand alone, a different danger remains: when the {\i get} is handed over, one might look at the signatures \u8212- say, \u8220"John Smith\u8221" \u8212- locate those people and have them stand as witnesses at the handing over, not realizing they are non-Jews. The {\i Gemara} makes clear that in its era, there were no reliable external markers distinguishing Jews from non-Jews, making this a genuine risk.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Clear Names Req.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ambiguous Names\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} therefore asks, at the top of 11a: given this risk of reliance on non-Jewish signatories, how can {\i Rebbi Shimon} permit a {\i get} from a non-Jewish court at all?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Implicit Criteria\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The answer is: {\i bi-shemos muvhakim} \u8212- we are dealing only with cases where the names on the document are unmistakably, unambiguously non-Jewish names. If the signatures are recognizably non-Jewish, no one will mistakenly seek out those signatories as witnesses for the handing over, and no one will rely on the document without ensuring that Jewish witnesses are present.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Name Examples\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rav Pappa} provides illustrations: names like {\i Hurmiz}, {\i Avudina}, {\i bar Shiptai}, {\i bar Kidri}, {\i Bati}, and {\i Nakimuna}. These are examples of names so thoroughly non-Jewish in character that their non-Jewish origin is beyond question.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Both Criteria\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} then presses: if the requirement of clearly non-Jewish names is so central, {\i Rebbi Shimon} should have stated it explicitly when enumerating the conditions for validity. Why does the {\i mishna} instead only mention the private court ({\i hedyot}) as the disqualifying factor? The {\i Gemara} responds: {\i hachi nami ka\u8217'amar} \u8212- the {\i mishna} is indeed implying this. {\i Ba-meh devarim amurim bi-shemos muvhakim} \u8212- when is a {\i get} from a non-Jewish court valid? Only when the names are clearly non-Jewish. {\i Aval shemos she-ein muvhakim} \u8212- where the names are ambiguous, {\i na\u8217'aseh ke-mi-she-na\u8217'asu be-hedyot u-fesulin} \u8212- it is treated as if it were done in a private court, and it is invalid.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is admittedly a strained reading of the {\i mishna}, since one would expect such a central criterion to be stated explicitly rather than folded into the reference to a private court. This is what is sometimes called {\i ikar chaser min ha-sefer} \u8212- the most essential point is not stated outright. The {\i Ran} offers a cleaner reading: there are genuinely two independent criteria. The document must bear clearly non-Jewish names, and it must emanate from a public established court. Both are required because together they make it unmistakably clear that the signatories are non-Jews \u8212- the established court giving the matter a public, well-known character, and the unambiguous names leaving no room for confusion about identity. Either condition alone is insufficient; both together satisfy the standard. On the {\i Ran}\u8217's reading, the private court is problematic for exactly the same reason as an ambiguous name: neither provides sufficient public clarity that the document was signed by non-Jews.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} offers a further alternative reading: {\i seifa ata legitei mammon} \u8212- the final clause of {\i Rebbi Shimon}\u8217's statement, the part about a private court being invalid, is actually not part of {\i Rebbi Shimon}\u8217's statement at all but rather a continuation of the {\i mishna}\u8217's earlier ruling about monetary documents. That is: monetary documents from public {\i arka\u8217'os} are valid; monetary documents from private courts are not. {\i Rebbi Shimon}\u8217's actual statement concerns only {\i gittin}, and he is simply saying those are {\i kosher} in a public court. Under this reading, {\i ve-hachi ka\u8217'amar}: monetary documents ({\i gitei mammon}) in public courts are valid; {\i gittin} in non-Jewish courts are generally not valid \u8212- but {\i Rebbi Shimon} holds they are valid \u8212- and even regarding monetary documents, public courts are required, not private ones.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Public vs.\u160 ?Private\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Public Courts\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i beraita} now elaborates the positions. {\i Rebbi Elazar b\u8217'Rebbi Yose} reports that {\i Rebbi Shimon} said to the {\i Chachamim} in {\i Tzidan}: {\i lo necheleku Rabbi Akiva ve-chachamim al kol ha-shtaros ha-olim be-arkaot shel Akko} \u8212- there was never any disagreement between {\i Rabbi Akiva} and the {\i Chachamim} regarding documents issued by the public established courts of non-Jews. Everyone agreed such documents are {\i kosher} \u8212- and not only monetary documents: even {\i gittin} and {\i shichrurei avadim} are valid, just as {\i Rebbi Shimon} holds in the {\i mishna}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Private Dispute\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The dispute was only regarding documents executed in private courts: {\i sheRabbi Akiva machshir ve-chachamim poslin} \u8212- {\i Rabbi Akiva} validates documents from private non-Jewish courts, while the {\i Chachamim} invalidate them. We are speaking here of monetary documents; the {\i Chachamim} are concerned about the trustworthiness of such documents when they do not emanate from an established public institution.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Chutz mi-gitei nashim ve-shichrurei avadim} \u8212- excepted from the debate are {\i gittin} and {\i shichrurei avadim}. Whether this exception attaches to the {\i Chachamim}\u8217's ruling or to {\i Rabbi Akiva}\u8217's is the subject of a debate between {\i Rashi} and {\i Tosafot} on one side and the {\i Ran} on the other. Following the {\i Ran}, the cleaner reading is: in private courts, {\i Rabbi Akiva} holds monetary documents are valid, and the {\i Chachamim} disagree \u8212- but everyone agrees that in private courts, {\i gittin} are certainly not valid. Conversely, in public courts, everything is valid, even {\i gittin}, as stated above.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ben Gamliel\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel} then goes further: {\i af elu kesherim} \u8212- he will even permit a {\i get} from a private court. The immediate difficulty is that in a private court one could not be certain the document was signed by non-Jews, which reintroduces all the concerns about reliance on invalid signatories.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Clear Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} explains his position: {\i be-makom she-ein Yisrael chotmim} \u8212- he is speaking of municipalities where Jews are not permitted to sign official documents in the secular courts. In such a place, even a private court document\u8217's signatures are self-evidently non-Jewish, so there is no fear of misplaced reliance. {\i Aval be-makom she-Yisrael chotmim lo} \u8212- but where Jews are permitted to sign, even {\i Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel} agrees that a private court creates too much ambiguity.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} asks: even in municipalities where Jews are forbidden to sign, should we not make a {\i gezeira} \u8212- a rabbinic precautionary decree \u8212- prohibiting such documents lest people come to rely on private court documents from places where Jews are permitted to sign? The {\i Gemara} answers: {\i shma bi-shma michlaif, atra be-atra lo michlaif} \u8212- names can be confused with other names, and one might lose track of which names are clearly non-Jewish and which are ambiguous, so such a decree might make sense for names. But a municipality is not confused with another municipality. The rule about whether Jews are permitted to sign in a given location is unambiguous and well-known \u8212- there is no sliding scale, no blurry boundary. {\i Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel}\u8217's position may therefore rest entirely on the criterion of location: where Jews cannot sign, it is simply obvious that all signatures are non-Jewish, regardless of the nature of the names. That is a completely binary criterion and carries no risk of extension by analogy.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Practical Cases\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Communal Gathering\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Ravina} considered permitting a monetary document produced by a large communal gathering of non-Jews \u8212- something resembling an {\i ad hoc} public proceeding, perhaps analogous to the market-day gatherings described in the {\i mishna} when villagers would assemble on market days and courts would convene. It was not an established {\i arka\u8217'os}, but it was public and communal in character, and {\i Ravina} thought it might be close enough.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rafram Rejects\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rafram} pushed back decisively: {\i arkaot tnan} \u8212- the {\i mishna} says {\i arka\u8217'os}, which means a fixed, established court. An {\i ad hoc} gathering, however large and public, does not meet that standard.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Persian Document\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Loan Document\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rava} introduces a practical case: {\i hai shtar parsa\u8217'a de-masri nihalei be-apei sahadei Yisrael} \u8212- a Persian loan document that was handed over in the presence of Jewish witnesses. The document, written in Persian and signed by Persian witnesses, attests to a loan \u8212- say, that {\i Reuven} borrowed a sum of money from {\i Shimon}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A standard {\i halachic} document of this kind serves three functions: it proves the loan occurred, it demonstrates the loan has not been repaid (since one does not ordinarily retain a document after repayment, unless a receipt has been produced), and it creates a lien on the borrower\u8217's property, allowing the lender to collect from real estate the borrower may subsequently sell if he has no unencumbered assets remaining. The third function is the most far-reaching, and the {\i Gemara} will have something to say about it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Witness Testimony\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The question is: if Jewish witnesses were present and can testify to the transaction, why is the document needed at all? The answer is that witnesses present at a transaction might not spontaneously remember the details a year or a decade later \u8212- the amount, the date, the conditions. But when the document is produced and the witnesses see it, their recognition of the document refreshes and grounds their testimony. Their recollection combined with the document gives the document its evidentiary force. That is how a Persian document handed over before Jewish witnesses can function as a valid instrument of evidence.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Requirements\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} challenges this on several grounds. First: {\i ve-ha lo yadei le-mikrarei} \u8212- the Jewish witnesses cannot read Persian and thus do not know what they are witnessing. The {\i Gemara} responds: {\i be-didyei} \u8212- we are speaking of witnesses who do in fact read Persian and who have read and understood the document\u8217's contents.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Second: {\i ve-ha ba\u8217'inan ktav she-eino yachol le-hizayef ve-leika} \u8212- does not {\i halacha} require that the document be written in a manner that cannot be forged, meaning on parchment treated such that any erasure would be visible? Otherwise the creditor could later alter the amount stated. The {\i Gemara} responds: {\i be-dafitza} \u8212- the parchment was treated with gallnut, making it tamper-evident.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Third: {\i ve-ha ba\u8217'inan toretz she-yachzor me-inyano shel shtar be-shita acharona} \u8212- does not a valid {\i shtar} require a closing line restating the essence of the document? The purpose of this requirement, as seen for instance in the concluding formula of a {\i ketubah} \u8212- {\i ve-kanyana mi-kol mah de-ktav u-mforash le-eila be-mana de-chasher le-makanya beih ve-hakol sharir ve-kayam} \u8212- is to prevent anyone from adding text between the end of the document and the witnesses\u8217' signatures. The final line declares itself to be the final line, so anything appearing below the witnesses\u8217' signatures cannot be appended and attributed to the original document. The {\i Gemara} responds: {\i bi-mahadeir} \u8212- this requirement too has been satisfied.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 No Liens\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having confirmed that all technical requirements are met, the {\i Gemara} asks: {\i i hachi, mi-mishababdei nami} \u8212- if this is such a complete and properly formed document, why can it not also create liens, enabling collection from property the borrower has sold? The {\i Gemara} answers: {\i leit lei kala} \u8212- even though {\i eidi mesirah} combined with a technically sound document may suffice {\i halachically}, the document lacks the public resonance of a genuinely Jewish {\i shtar} executed and signed in accordance with Jewish law. Property liens depend on public knowledge \u8212- the buyer of the borrower\u8217's land must be presumed to have been on notice of the prior debt. A Persian document witnessed by Jews in this fashion does not achieve the level of public record that would make it fair to charge third parties with constructive notice. Therefore it may be used to collect from the borrower\u8217's own unencumbered property, but it creates no lien on property he has transferred to others.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Ambiguous Signatures\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Reish Lakish Q.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} returns to the question of {\i gittin} and non-Jewish signatures. {\i Reish Lakish} posed a question to {\i Rabbi Yochanan} \u8212- both of them {\i Eretz Yisrael} {\i Amoraim}. The case: a {\i get} is presented for validation and the signatures on it resemble the names of non-Jews. The question is what to presume. If these are Jews who happen to bear non-Jewish sounding names, the {\i get} is valid. If they are non-Jews, the {\i get} is not valid \u8212- not because non-Jewish signatories are an absolute bar under {\i Rebbi Elazar}\u8217's view, but because a {\i get} signed by non-Jews with ambiguous names is the paradigm of a dangerous document: one cannot tell by looking at it whether the signatories are Jewish or not, making it susceptible to misuse, either by relying on the signatories alone without {\i eidi mesirah}, or by enlisting those very signatories as the witnesses for handing over.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Yochanan\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Yochanan} responded: {\i lo ba le-yadeinu ela Lukis ve-Lus ve-hichsharnu} \u8212- the only case of this kind that came before us was a document signed by someone named {\i Lukis} and {\i Lus}, and we ruled it valid. {\i Tosafot} explains that {\i Lukis} and {\i Lus} were so unmistakably non-Jewish names that the document fell into the category of {\i shemos muvhakim} \u8212- clearly non-Jewish names \u8212- and was therefore treated as valid: clearly non-Jewish, so the rule of {\i Rebbi Shimon} applies, and one ensures that Jewish witnesses are present at the handing over. For ambiguous cases, {\i Rabbi Yochanan} offers no ruling \u8212- signaling that they are not valid.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ambiguity Rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} clarifies: {\i ve-davka Lukos ve-Lus de-la shechichi Yisrael de-maski bishmayhu} \u8212- specifically {\i Lukis} and {\i Lus}, names so foreign to Jewish usage that no Jew is commonly known by them, so that seeing such names one knows immediately they are non-Jewish. {\i Aval shemata achrinei de-shchichi Yisrael de-maski bishmateihu} \u8212- but other names that Jews are also known to use, where there is genuine ambiguity as to whether the signatory is Jewish or not \u8212- in such cases, {\i lo}, they cannot be relied upon. If one knew they were Jews there would be no problem; if one knew they were non-Jews, {\i shemos muvhakim} would render the document safe. But ambiguity itself is the disqualifying factor, since a document that might have been signed by non-Jews with names indistinguishable from Jewish names is too dangerous to validate.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Overseas Gettin\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosefta Rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} raises a challenge from a {\i Tosefta}: {\i gittin ha-ba\u8217'im mi-medinat ha-yam ve-ha-edim chatumim aleihem} \u8212- {\i gittin} that arrive from overseas, bearing witness signatures, {\i af al pi she-shmoteihen ki-shmotei ovdei kochavim kesherim} \u8212- even if the names on those signatures are non-Jewish names, they are valid.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Presumption\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The reason given: {\i mipnei she-rov Yisrael she-be-chutz la-aretz shmoteihen ki-shmotei ovdei kochavim} \u8212- because the majority, or at any rate a very large proportion, of Jews living outside {\i Eretz Yisrael} bear non-Jewish names. In a community where non-Jewish names are so prevalent among Jews, a {\i get} arriving from that community with non-Jewish sounding signatures should obviously be presumed to have been signed by Jews who happen to bear those names, not by actual non-Jews. This seems to contradict {\i Rabbi Yochanan}\u8217's rule, which disqualified ambiguous names.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Geographic Limit\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} resolves the apparent contradiction: {\i hatam ki-detani ta\u8217'ama} \u8212- the {\i Tosefta} itself supplies its reason, and that reason is specific to {\i chutz la-aretz}. Outside {\i Eretz Yisrael} \u8212- and the reference here is to the diaspora more broadly, places of greater integration with the surrounding society and with less dense {\i halachic} infrastructure than {\i Bavel} \u8212- it is so common for Jews to bear non-Jewish names that the presumption straightforwardly favors Jewish signatories. Within {\i Eretz Yisrael}, by contrast, non-Jewish names are far less prevalent among Jews, and therefore the same ambiguity cannot be resolved the same way. There, a document with non-Jewish sounding names cannot enjoy the same presumption, and one must be concerned that the signatories were indeed non-Jews.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} offers an alternative formulation as well: {\i ve-ika de-amri, ki matnita ba\u8217'a mineih pashat lei mi-matnita} \u8212- according to some, {\i Reish Lakish}\u8217's question to {\i Rabbi Yochanan} was specifically about {\i gittin} arriving from {\i chutz la-aretz}, and {\i Rabbi Yochanan}\u8217's answer was the {\i Tosefta} itself: when the documents come from {\i medinat ha-yam}, even names that sound non-Jewish are presumed to be those of Jews, because that is the nature of Jewish life in {\i chutz la-aretz}, and such documents are entirely valid.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Secular Names\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Criticism Q.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What is striking about this entire {\i sugya} is the {\i Gemara}\u8217's matter-of-fact recognition, without any critical editorial comment, of how prevalent it was for Jews to bear non-Jewish names. This prompts a question worth raising: should one criticize Jews for giving their children secular names? The practice was widespread across many generations \u8212- a concern not merely theoretical, as the {\i Gemara} itself treats it as an entirely ordinary feature of diaspora Jewish life.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Moshe Solution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 There is a famous {\i teshuva} in {\i Rav Moshe Feinstein}\u8217's {\i Igrot Moshe} where someone urged that Jews who give their children secular names should be criticized, citing the well-known {\i midrash} that the Israelites were redeemed from Egypt in part because they did not change their names, their dress, or their language. {\i Rav Moshe} goes through the {\i Tannaim}, {\i Amoraim}, {\i Rishonim}, and {\i Acharonim} who bore secular names and demonstrates that the practice was entirely accepted. He then addresses the {\i midrash} directly with a compelling insight: that {\i midrash} was speaking of the period before {\i matan Torah}. Before {\i matan Torah}, the Jewish people had no {\i taryag mitzvos} to sustain their distinct identity; the only markers of that identity were external \u8212- language, names, dress. Holding onto those markers was what preserved them as a people, and it was in that {\i zchus} that they merited redemption. After {\i matan Torah}, however, {\i taryag mitzvos} themselves maintain and sustain Jewish identity. The external markers are no longer the load-bearing structure of {\i am Yisrael}\u8217's distinctiveness, and therefore the question of secular names does not carry the same weight it did before {\i matan Torah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 We will continue with 12a in the next {\i shiur}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Gittin 12\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Non-Jewish Witnesses\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafos Four Reasons\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The discussion opens not with the material formally beginning on daf 12 but with a brief doubling back to a {\i tosafos} on 9b that bears directly on the central question: why are non-Jewish signatures invalid on a {\i get}? The {\i tosafos} there, citing the {\i Ri}, enumerates no fewer than four distinct reasons.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The first is the {\i gemara}\u8217's own formulation: {\i lav bnei kerisus ninhu} \u8212- non-Jews are not within the category of those for whom {\i gittin} apply. Since divorce is a concept that exists only within a community bound by the relevant laws, those standing entirely outside that framework cannot participate in the execution of its documents.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Lav Bnei Kerisus\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The second reason is the requirement of {\i lishma}: the signing must be done with the specific intent that this document serve as a {\i get} for this woman. A non-Jew, the {\i tosafos} reasons, acts {\i ada\u8217'ata d\u8217'nafshei} \u8212- according to his own purposes \u8212- and we cannot presume he directs his mind to the required {\i lishma}, as established in the second chapter.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Non-Jews as Shelichim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Third, {\i tosafos} notes that non-Jews cannot serve as agents: {\i lav bnei shlichus ninhu}. The basis for requiring {\i shlichus} here is that the husband must direct the {\i sofer} to write and the witnesses to sign, which means the entire execution of the {\i get} is mediated through agency. Since non-Jews are categorically excluded from the laws of {\i shelichut}, they cannot function in that role.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Only at this point \u8212- fourth and finally \u8212- does {\i tosafos} add that non-Jews are {\i pesulim l\u8217'edus}, disqualified as witnesses. The fact that this is the last rather than the first reason offered is itself striking. It might seem that disqualification from testimony is the most obvious and straightforward ground for invalidating non-Jewish signatures, yet {\i tosafos} requires three prior reasons before arriving there. The implication, as the lecturer observes, is that none of the four reasons standing alone was felt to be fully airtight, and that together they form a cumulative argument. What is especially remarkable is how the {\i gemara} itself treats the disqualification of non-Jews as witnesses as something less than self-evident \u8212- the {\i tosafos} on 9b is actually working to establish, not simply assert, that non-Jews are {\i pesulim l\u8217'edus}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Get vs.\u160 ?Manumission\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Meir Retracts\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} on 11b introduces a parallel between the {\i get} and the {\i shtar shichrur}, the writ of manumission. If a man says {\i \u8220"ten get ze l\u8217'ishti v\u8217'shtar shichrur ze l\u8217'avdi\u8221"} \u8212- give this {\i get} to my wife and this {\i shtar shichrur} to my slave \u8212- Rabbi Meir holds that he may retract either of them before delivery. The {\i shaliach} does not automatically stand in for the recipient; the transfer is not yet final.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Zachin L\u8217'Adam\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This ruling rests on the principle of {\i zachin l\u8217'adam shelo befanav} \u8212- one may acquire a benefit on behalf of another person even without that person\u8217's knowledge or presence. The corollary is equally important: {\i ein chavin lo ela befanav} \u8212- one may not cause injury to another except in his presence, meaning only the affected party himself, or an agent he has appointed, can receive something that works to his detriment.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chakhamim on Shichrur\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i chakhamim} distinguish between the two cases. For the {\i get}, they agree with Rabbi Meir: since being divorced may well be a detriment to the wife, the {\i get} cannot be delivered by someone acting on her behalf without her appointment of that agent. But for the {\i shtar shichrur}, the {\i chakhamim} hold that we may act on the slave\u8217's behalf without his presence, because freedom is an unqualified benefit \u8212- {\i zachin l\u8217'adam shelo befanav} applies, and the {\i shaliach} may take possession of the document for the slave immediately.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Feeding Obligations\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But is freedom truly an unqualified benefit for the slave? One might argue that the master provides food and shelter, and that this represents a tangible benefit the slave would lose upon manumission. The {\i chakhamim} preempt this argument: {\i she-im ratza shelo lazon et avdo rashai} \u8212- a master may, if he wishes, simply refuse to feed his slave. There is, it appears from the {\i mishna}, no legal obligation to do so. By contrast, a husband may not refuse to feed his wife; that obligation is part of his {\i ketubah} duties. Therefore the slave gains nothing material from his servitude, since the master can always opt out of feeding him, while the wife does enjoy a concrete financial benefit from marriage that her husband cannot unilaterally withdraw.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Meir responds that freedom is nonetheless not an unqualified benefit. {\i Ve-harei hu posel et avdo min ha-terumah} \u8212- a slave owned by a {\i kohen} may eat {\i terumah}, whereas a freed slave may not. This represents a concrete loss. Rabbi Meir\u8217's argument appears to be not that in the aggregate it is better to be a slave, but that the moment any element of concrete harm exists, the act cannot be performed on someone\u8217's behalf without their knowledge. The {\i chakhamim} counter that the slave is simply the master\u8217's property \u8212- {\i mipnei she-hu kinyano} \u8212- and this particular loss is subsumed within the nature of that ownership relationship rather than constituting a free-standing injury that would block the principle of {\i zachin}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Tofes L\u8217'Ba\u8217'al Chov\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Huna Analogy\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 While Rabbi Yirmiyah dozes, his students Rav Huna and Rav Yitzchak bar Yosef continue the discussion. Rav Huna proposes an inference: {\i shma mina midirabbanan} \u8212- from the position of the {\i chakhamim}, who hold that the {\i shaliach} may take the {\i shtar shichrur} on behalf of the slave even before the master has fully resolved to complete the transfer, we may infer that {\i ha-tofes le-ba\u8217'al chov bimkom she-chav le-acherim kana} \u8212- one who seizes property on behalf of a creditor, even in a case where doing so harms third parties, acquires it validly on the creditor\u8217's behalf.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The analogy requires unpacking. In a classic application of {\i zachin l\u8217'adam shelo befanav}, the person acting on behalf of another does so entirely in accordance with the principal\u8217's will \u8212- no one is being overridden. The case of the {\i shtar shichrur} is subtler, because the master, as the {\i mishna} shows, wants to retain the ability to retract. When the {\i shaliach} takes possession of the document on behalf of the slave, he is in some sense jumping ahead before the master is fully ready to release his property. This gives the case a flavor of {\i tofes le-ba\u8217'al chov}, seizing something on behalf of someone even while the current owner is not entirely prepared to let go. Rav Huna reasons that if this works in the {\i mishna}\u8217's case, then seizing property for a creditor must also work even when other parties are harmed in the process.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafot Objection\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} raises a sharp objection to this analogy: in the case of seizing property for a creditor, there is a genuine underlying debt \u8212- the debtor truly owes the money. But the master does not owe the slave his freedom. What basis is there for treating the {\i shaliach}\u8217's taking possession of the {\i shtar shichrur} as analogous to collecting a genuine debt? {\i Tosafot} grapples with this and suggests that perhaps the {\i gemara} understands the master\u8217's expressed will to free the slave as creating some form of obligation or entitlement, even if imperfect \u8212- enough to provide at least a partial analogy. Crucially, the act of saying \u8220"deliver this {\i shtar shichrur}\u8221" has already occurred; the master has expressed his desire, and the {\i shaliach}\u8217's taking possession can be understood as the execution of that expressed will rather than an act hostile to it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Yochanan vs Yosef\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Yirmiyah wakes up and tells his students: {\i dardakei} \u8212- children \u8212- and reports the ruling of Rabbi Yochanan: {\i ha-tofes le-ba\u8217'al chov bimkom she-chav le-acherim lo kana}. When seizing property for a creditor causes harm to third-party creditors, the seizure does not take effect. Rav Yosef bar Yosef had said it does work even in such a case; Rabbi Yochanan rules it does not.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then needs to salvage the ruling of the {\i mishna}: if seizing property at the expense of others does not work, how can the {\i shaliach} validly take possession of the {\i shtar shichrur} on behalf of the slave when the master is not yet ready to release it?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 When One Says Give\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} answers with a fundamental principle: {\i kol ha-omer tnu k\u8217'omer zachi dami} \u8212- when one says \u8220"give this to so-and-so,\u8221" that formulation is legally equivalent to saying \u8220"take possession of it for him right now.\u8221" By handing the document to the {\i shaliach} with the instruction to deliver it, the master is implicitly authorizing the {\i shaliach} to acquire it on the slave\u8217's behalf at that very moment. The act is therefore not against the master\u8217's will \u8212- we interpret his instruction as immediate authorization. This removes the case entirely from the category of {\i tofes le-ba\u8217'al chov}, since there is no seizure against anyone\u8217's will. The {\i shaliach} is not grabbing property ahead of a reluctant owner; the owner, by saying \u8220"give it,\u8221" has already consented.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This principle also explains why the husband can still retract in the case of the {\i get}. Even if we say the master is prepared for the transfer to be immediate, the question remains whether the {\i shaliach} is authorized to represent the {\i other} side \u8212- the wife. The answer is no, because {\i zachin l\u8217'adam shelo befanav} does not apply where the act might constitute a detriment to the recipient. The master\u8217's readiness to proceed does not overcome the requirement that the wife be represented only by someone she herself has appointed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then considers whether the principle of {\i tofes le-ba\u8217'al chov} can be derived from the {\i mishna} in {\i Pe\u8217'ah}: one who harvests {\i pe\u8217'ah} on behalf of a specific poor person \u8212- {\i mi she-likat et ha-pe\u8217'ah ve-amar harei zeh le-ploni ani}. Rabbi Eliezer holds the acquisition is valid; the {\i chakhamim} hold the harvested {\i pe\u8217'ah} must be given to the first poor person who arrives. This seems to map onto the {\i tofes le-ba\u8217'al chov} debate, but Amemar (or Rav Pappa) resists the equation. Rabbi Eliezer may permit the {\i pe\u8217'ah} case for a specific reason: {\i demigo de-ei ba\u8217'ei mafkir le-nichsei ve-havei ani ve-chazi leh} \u8212- since the harvester could himself renounce all his property, become poor, and thereby be personally entitled to the {\i pe\u8217'ah}, he may acquire it for someone else. This logic does not extend to the case of a genuine creditor seizing the debtor\u8217's assets, where the seizing party has no independent entitlement to the property. Conversely, the {\i chakhamim} who prohibit in the {\i pe\u8217'ah} case may rely on the specific verse {\i lo telaket} \u8212- you shall not harvest for the poor \u8212- which creates a particular exclusion not applicable to the general {\i tofes} question. The two debates cannot be straightforwardly equated.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Master\u8217's Obligation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 No Obligation Inference\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 With the {\i tofes} discussion concluded, the {\i gemara} returns to the substantive question raised by the {\i mishna}: does a master have a legal obligation to feed his slave? The {\i mishna} stated that a master may refuse to feed his slave \u8212- {\i she-im ratza shelo lazon et avdo rashai}. The natural inference is {\i shma mina}: {\i yachol ha-rav lomar le-eved asei imi ve-eini zonecha} \u8212- the master may say \u8220"work for me and I will not feed you.\u8221"\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Counter: Must Feed\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} resists this inference. {\i Hacha be-mai askinan} \u8212- the {\i mishna} is not saying there is no obligation. Rather, the case is one where the master says {\i tze ma\u8217'asei yadecha bi-mezonotecha} \u8212- \u8220"go use your own labor to feed yourself.\u8221" The master does have a legal obligation to feed the slave, but he may discharge that obligation indirectly: rather than providing food himself, he may waive his claim to the slave\u8217's labor for the purpose of allowing the slave to earn his own food. The master cannot demand the slave\u8217's labor and simultaneously refuse to feed him; but if he releases the labor, the feeding obligation is effectively transferred.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Wife vs Slave\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A difficulty arises immediately: the same logic seems applicable to a wife. A husband could equally say {\i tze ma\u8217'asei yadecha le-mezonotecha} \u8212- earn your own living \u8212- and thereby avoid his obligation to feed her. If so, why is the wife\u8217's situation treated as superior to the slave\u8217's?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} answers: {\i isha de-la safka} \u8212- the wife\u8217's situation is categorically different because we can easily imagine a case where she cannot earn enough to support herself. In that event, the husband must make up the difference. His obligation is not merely to release her labor; it is to ensure she is actually fed, regardless of her earning capacity. Therefore marriage does confer a genuine financial benefit: if she cannot earn her keep, he is obligated to supplement.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Slave Without Means\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What about a slave who similarly cannot earn his own keep? Here the {\i gemara} responds sharply: {\i avda de-nahom karsei lo shavya le-marei le-martei le-mai mitvabei} \u8212- a slave who cannot earn the food his stomach requires, what use is he to his master? The practical implication, as Rashi explains, is that there is no legal obligation to make up the difference for a slave who cannot earn his own upkeep. Since the entire framework of the master-slave relationship is premised on the slave\u8217's productive labor, where that labor does not cover even basic sustenance, the master has no obligation to supplement. The {\i gemara} does not provide a positive rationale grounded in a {\i pasuk} or contractual principle; it simply asserts the distinction.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The ethical weight of this position is not lost. The moment a slave becomes ill or incapacitated \u8212- precisely when he most needs support \u8212- the master appears to have no legal obligation to provide it. The {\i gemara} arrives at this conclusion without adverting to a verse or a contract, which the lecturer flags as notable.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Cities of Refuge\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now adduces further evidence from a {\i baraita} concerning a slave who has accidentally killed someone and fled to one of the {\i arei miklat}, the cities of refuge. The {\i baraita} states: {\i ein rabo chayav le-zono} \u8212- the master has no obligation to feed him there; {\i ve-lo od, she-ma\u8217'aseh yadav le-rabo} \u8212- moreover, if the slave can perform labor while in the city of refuge, his earnings belong to the master. This seems to resolve the question directly: no feeding obligation, yet full entitlement to earnings.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Slave\u8217's Own Labor\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} again refuses to read this at face value. The master\u8217's lack of obligation to feed means he has arranged that the slave feeds himself from his own earnings \u8212- {\i tze ma\u8217'asei yadecha le-mezonotecha}. The slave\u8217's earnings that \u8220"go to the master\u8221" refer only to the surplus beyond what the slave needs for food. On its face this is a very forced reading of the {\i baraita}, which seems simply to say \u8220"don\u8217't feed him, keep his earnings.\u8221" The {\i gemara} acknowledges this by asking: if the slave keeps his own earnings for food, and any surplus goes to the master, why does the {\i baraita} need to specify that the surplus goes to the master \u8212- is this not obvious? The {\i gemara} answers: {\i mahu deteima} \u8212- one might have thought that since the master has no obligation to make up the difference when the slave falls short, the slave should be entitled to accumulate a rainy-day reserve from his surplus earnings against future days when he cannot earn. The {\i baraita} therefore tells us this is not the case: the arrangement is strictly day-to-day, and any surplus belongs to the master.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} further explains why the {\i baraita} specifically chose the scenario of {\i arei miklat}: one might have thought that the verse commanding the fugitive to {\i live} in the city of refuge \u8212- {\i venas el achat he-arim ha-el ve-chai} \u8212- creates a heightened obligation to ensure the slave\u8217's sustenance there. The {\i baraita} teaches that even in that context, no such obligation exists.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The subsequent discussion in the {\i gemara} involves a {\i baraita} that contrasts the slave with a wife who has fled to a city of refuge: {\i ba\u8217'alah chayav bi-mezonoteiha} \u8212- her husband remains obligated to feed her. The {\i gemara} navigates the inference carefully: since the wife-case explicitly requires feeding only where no special arrangement was made, the slave-case must also be read as involving an arrangement ({\i tze ma\u8217'asei yadecha}) and not as a flat declaration of no obligation. The {\i gemara} then adds a final nuance: even where a wife could theoretically earn enough to feed herself, one might think her dignity prevents us from requiring her to hustle for work \u8212- {\i kol kevuda bat melech penima}. The {\i gemara} rules that we do in fact require her to earn what she can, and only the gap beyond what she can reasonably earn must be made up by the husband.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Throughout this extended passage, the {\i gemara} consistently refuses to accept the plain reading of texts that seem to say the master has no obligation to feed his slave, insisting instead that an obligation exists \u8212- while never grounding that obligation explicitly in a verse or formal legal category. The lecturer notes this is a striking feature of the {\i sugya}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Sanctifying Labor\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hedesh Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now turns to a specific case that illuminates the question from a different angle. Rav rules: {\i ha-makdish yedei avdo} \u8212- if a master declares his slave\u8217's labor {\i hekdesh}, sanctified to the Temple, {\i oto eved loveh ve-ochel ve-oto poreia} \u8212- the slave borrows money to eat and repays from his labor.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The scenario is this: the master has sanctified the slave\u8217's labor, meaning whatever the slave earns passes automatically to {\i hekdesh}. The master, not wishing to feed the slave out of his own pocket now that he is deriving no direct benefit from the labor, refuses to provide food. But the slave\u8217's own earnings also go to {\i hekdesh} and cannot be used for food. The solution the {\i gemara} describes is that the slave borrows money, purchases food with the borrowed funds, and then repays the loan from his subsequent earnings.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The question is how the earnings can go toward repaying the loan if they are {\i hekdesh} the moment they are earned. The {\i gemara} explains the mechanism: the repayment occurs in increments smaller than a {\i prutah}. Since {\i hekdesh} acquires rights only when an amount reaches the threshold of a {\i prutah}, each sub-{\i prutah} increment is transferred to the creditor before {\i hekdesh}\u8217's claim attaches. {\i Hekdesh} never actually gets hold of those amounts, and the creditor is paid through this continuous micro-transfer. {\i Hekdesh} itself consents to this arrangement because a healthier, better-fed slave earns more \u8212- {\i ki heichi d\u8217'lishvach} \u8212- so {\i hekdesh}\u8217's own interests are served.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Borrowing to Eat\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A closer look at this case, however, reveals a tension. The {\i gemara} argues that the master is entitled to sanctify the slave\u8217's labor only because he is in fact feeding the slave \u8212- {\i hacha be-mai askinan be-ma\u8217'aleh lo mezonot}. But if the master is feeding the slave, why does the slave need to borrow money to eat?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} answers: {\i le-hadafah} \u8212- for the extra. The master is providing the bare minimum of food, just sufficient to ground his legal entitlement to the slave\u8217's labor. The slave, however, wants to eat more \u8212- or needs more for his health and productivity. It is for this additional food beyond the bare minimum that he must borrow. The {\i hekdesh} arrangement, with its sub-{\i prutah} mechanism, applies only to the repayment of this supplemental borrowing.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav\u8217's Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This reading is confirmed by Rav\u8217's own language elsewhere: {\i amar Rav ha-makdish yedei avdo, oto eved oseh ve-ochel} \u8212- the slave works and eats. The slave, in Rav\u8217's formulation, is entitled to eat from his labor. Rav\u8217's underlying principle is that if the master is not feeding the slave, he is not entitled to the slave\u8217's labor. If he has sanctified the labor while refusing to feed the slave, the sanctification is invalid and the slave is entitled to work and feed himself directly.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Bare Minimum Feeding\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} draws these threads together. In the case where the sanctification is valid \u8212- the master is feeding the slave \u8212- the master provides the bare minimum, the slave may borrow for additional food and repay from earnings via the sub-{\i prutah} mechanism, and {\i hekdesh} acquiesces because the slave\u8217's productivity benefits it. In the case Rav addresses where the sanctification is invalid, the master was not feeding the slave, and therefore he never had the entitlement to sanctify the labor in the first place. The {\i gemara} affirms: {\i shma mina eino yachol} \u8212- one may not demand a slave\u8217's labor while refusing to feed him. This is Rav\u8217's clear ruling.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rabbi Yochanan Dissent\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Severing Hand\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Against Rav\u8217's position, the {\i gemara} introduces a teaching of Rabbi Yochanan. If someone severs the hand of another person\u8217's slave, Rabbi Yochanan rules: {\i noten shevet ve-refuah le-rabo} \u8212- the tortfeasor pays {\i shevet} and {\i refuah} to the master. {\i Shevet} is the compensation for lost labor during the period of incapacitation \u8212- not the permanent diminishment of the slave\u8217's value, which is addressed by the main injury payment, but the specific loss of labor during the recovery period: work the slave could have done, such as light tasks not requiring the severed hand, that he did not do because he was hospitalized. {\i Refuah} is the payment for medical treatment.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Charity Food\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The remarkable element of Rabbi Yochanan\u8217's ruling is the next clause: {\i ve-oto eved nizon min ha-tzedaka} \u8212- and the slave himself is fed from charity. The master is collecting {\i shevet} payments representing the slave\u8217's foregone labor, yet the slave\u8217's food during the recovery period comes not from the master but from communal charity funds.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 No Feeding Required\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 According to Rav\u8217's position, this is difficult: if the master is entitled to the slave\u8217's labor \u8212- here in the form of {\i shevet} \u8212- he should be obligated to feed the slave. The {\i gemara} draws the inference explicitly: {\i shma mina} \u8212- according to Rabbi Yochanan, {\i yachol ha-rav lomar le-eved asei imi ve-eini zonecha} \u8212- the master may say \u8220"work for me and I will not feed you.\u8221" Rabbi Yochanan and Rav disagree directly: Rav holds that entitlement to the slave\u8217's labor is conditional on feeding him; Rabbi Yochanan holds that the master may collect the benefit of the slave\u8217's labor without any corresponding obligation to provide food.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} attempts to harmonize Rabbi Yochanan\u8217's ruling with the position that a feeding obligation exists. Perhaps {\i nizon min ha-tzedaka} means the master is providing the bare minimum and charity supplements it \u8212- {\i le-hadafah}. But the {\i gemara} rejects this: {\i nizon} specifically denotes the bare minimum subsistence level, whereas {\i mitparnes} would indicate something above the minimum. The word {\i nizon min ha-tzedaka} means that even the bare minimum is coming from charity, not from the master. The inference stands: {\i shma mina} that according to Rabbi Yochanan, the master has no obligation to feed his slave even while collecting the proceeds of the slave\u8217's labor.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The lecturer observes that this may reflect a broader geographic or institutional disagreement \u8212- Rav, the Babylonian authority, insisting on the feeding obligation; Rabbi Yochanan, the Palestinian {\i amora}, permitting its waiver.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Doctor Bills\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} closes with a final question about the {\i refuah} payment. If the tortfeasor must pay {\i refuah} to the master, why? The medical bills are incurred for the slave\u8217's treatment and would seem to belong to the slave so he can pay his doctors. The {\i gemara} explains with a specific case: the doctors assessed that recovery would take five days and payment was made accordingly, but through more aggressive treatment \u8212- harder on the slave, but more effective \u8212- he recovered in three days. Two days of medical expenses were saved. Who keeps those two days of savings?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might think the slave is entitled to them, since it was his suffering \u8212- {\i tza\u8217'ara didei hu} \u8212- that produced the savings. The {\i gemara} rules otherwise: {\i kamashma lan} \u8212- just as the master is entitled to the fruit of the slave\u8217's labor generally, he is entitled to the savings realized through the slave\u8217's endurance of harder treatment. The doctor-bill savings, like the {\i shevet} payments, belong to the master.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 With this, the {\i sugya} of {\i asei imi ve-eini zonecha} concludes: Rav holds that a master may not demand a slave\u8217's labor while refusing to feed him; Rabbi Yochanan holds that there is no legal obligation to feed a slave, and the master may collect {\i shevet} while leaving the slave to charity. In either case \u8212- whether because the master need not feed at all, or because he may release the slave\u8217's labor in lieu of feeding \u8212- the {\i chakhamim}\u8217's position that manumission is an unqualified benefit for the slave stands. The slave is never in a position where his status as owned property guarantees him sustenance, and therefore the act of freeing him involves no element of harm that would require the slave\u8217's own presence or consent.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Gittin 13\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Freedom as Benefit\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shtar Shichrur & Get\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The daf opens in the middle of a debate between Rabbi Meir and the {\i chachamim} concerning a case where someone instructs another to deliver either a {\i shtar shichrur} to his slave or a {\i get} to his wife. The question is whether the recipient of those instructions can be considered a {\i shaliach} acting on behalf of the slave or wife, such that the divorce or manumission takes effect automatically upon that person\u8217's acceptance of the document.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 In the case of the wife, the principle of {\i zachin l\u8217'adam shelo befanav} \u8212- whereby one may act on another\u8217's behalf to confer a benefit, even without the other\u8217's knowledge \u8212- cannot apply, because there is a potential downside to being divorced: the husband is no longer legally obligated to provide for her. The case of the {\i eved} is more complex. The {\i chachamim} hold that freedom is an unambiguous benefit to the slave, and therefore {\i zachin} does apply. Rabbi Meir disagrees, arguing that there are real losses attendant upon manumission.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chachamim Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i chachamim} reason straightforwardly: to be freed from beneath one\u8217's master\u8217's hand and enter into liberty is an absolute good. Since it is purely beneficial, someone may represent the slave and act on his behalf to receive the {\i shtar shichrur}, and the slave is thereby automatically freed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Meir: Terumah Loss\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Meir objects. The {\i baraita} records the exchange directly: the {\i chachamim} said to him, \u8220"Is it not a pure benefit to the slave to go out from under his master\u8217's hand to freedom?\u8221" Rabbi Meir replied, \u8220"{\i chov hu lo} \u8212- it is a detriment to him.\u8221" His first contention was that the master might lose the practical incentive to feed the slave abundantly, since a master who owns a {\i kohen}\u8217's slave can supply him with the inexpensive {\i terumah}, and freeing the slave removes that access. But more fundamentally, Rabbi Meir argues: {\i she-im haya eved kohen pasul min hatruma} \u8212- if he was the slave of a {\i kohen}, manumission causes him to lose the right to eat {\i terumah}. The wife of a {\i kohen} and the slave of a {\i kohen} both eat {\i terumah} \u8212- the wife because of the marital bond, the slave because he is the {\i kohen}\u8217's property. Upon manumission, that privilege disappears. Rabbi Meir therefore holds that at minimum in the case of a {\i kohen}\u8217's slave, there is a genuine loss, which disqualifies the application of {\i zachin}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 No Feeding Obligation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i chachamim} respond to the argument about the master\u8217's feeding obligation: {\i v\u8217'lo mai mafsed she-eino chayav le-zono u-le-farneso rashai} \u8212- what loss is there if the master has no legal obligation to feed the slave in the first place? If the master was never required to provide food, the slave loses nothing in that regard by being freed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} explains that the {\i chachamim} understood Rabbi Meir\u8217's {\i terumah} argument as being about the cost of food \u8212- the {\i kohen} master could cheaply supply {\i terumah} food, making the slave\u8217's material situation better \u8212- and they responded that since there is no legal feeding obligation, the cheapness of {\i terumah} is irrelevant.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Terumah Independent\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Meir, however, is not satisfied. He replies that the {\i terumah} point is entirely independent of any feeding obligation. The {\i baraita} states: {\i u-mah ilu eved kohen she-barach v\u8217'eshet kohen she-marda al ba\u8217'alah halo ochlin b\u8217'terumah} \u8212- even a slave of a {\i kohen} who has run away from his master, or a wife of a {\i kohen} who has rebelled against her husband, can still eat {\i terumah}. In other words, the access to {\i terumah} does not depend on the master or husband actually providing it \u8212- the slave can go independently to the threshing floors, to the grain stores, and acquire {\i terumah} on his own. This benefit belongs to him by virtue of his status as the {\i kohen}\u8217's property, and it is entirely separate from the master\u8217's feeding obligation. Once he is freed \u8212- {\i v\u8217'zeh eino ochel} \u8212- he loses this right entirely.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i baraita} makes the contrast sharp: {\i aval isha chov hu lah she-ken posla min hatruma u-mefasda min hamzonot} \u8212- by a woman, both losses are present upon divorce: she loses {\i terumah} rights and also the husband\u8217's legal obligation to feed her. For the slave, even though the feeding obligation doesn\u8217't apply, the {\i terumah} right is real and its loss is real.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then pauses to reconstruct the exact flow of argument: the {\i chachamim} had responded to the feeding point, and Rabbi Meir acknowledged it but pressed them on the {\i terumah} point which remained unaddressed. One might ask: could not the master simply throw the {\i get} within the slave\u8217's {\i arba amot} and free him against his will, rendering the question of representation moot? Rabbi Meir anticipates this: {\i shavik lei v\u8217'arak v\u8217'azil l\u8217'alma} \u8212- the slave can run away, and without the slave or his representative present, the master cannot remove the slave\u8217's {\i terumah} rights unilaterally. It is precisely because a {\i shaliach} is stepping in to represent the slave that the master gains the power to sever the slave\u8217's connection to {\i terumah}. Therefore, the act of representation is itself the vehicle of the slave\u8217's loss, and {\i zachin} cannot apply.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Master Can Sell\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} asks: so the {\i chachamim}\u8217's response was a good one \u8212- but then what is their rejoinder to Rabbi Meir\u8217's {\i terumah} argument? Rava explains: {\i hainu d\u8217'ka mahadrei lei b\u8217'matnisin mipnei she-hu kinyano} \u8212- the answer lies in the {\i mishnah}\u8217's phrase \u8220"because he is his possession.\u8221" Since the slave is the master\u8217's property, and since the reason the slave eats {\i terumah} in the first place is that he is the {\i kohen}\u8217's possession, the master can eliminate this benefit at any time without the slave\u8217's involvement: {\i d\u8217'i ba\u8217'i shakil arba zuzei mi\u8217'Yisrael u\u8217'pasleh leh le-chol heicha d\u8217'iteih} \u8212- if the master wishes, he simply sells the slave to a non-{\i kohen} for a nominal sum, and wherever the slave is \u8212- even if he has fled to the ends of the earth \u8212- he is immediately barred from {\i terumah}. Since this benefit was always revocable by the master alone, the fact that a {\i shaliach} represents the slave and facilitates manumission does not constitute the source of any new loss. The {\i terumah} right was never secure to begin with.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sexual Freedom Loss\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This disposes of Rabbi Meir\u8217's objection in the case of a {\i kohen}\u8217's slave \u8212- but what about the slave of a {\i Yisrael}, an ordinary Israelite? There is no {\i terumah} to lose there. Where is the downside for such a slave? The {\i gemara} cites Rabbi Shmuel bar Rav Yitzchak: {\i mipnei she-mafsido mi-shifcha kena\u8217'anit} \u8212- because manumission deprives him of the ability to cohabit with a {\i shifcha kena\u8217'anit}, a non-Jewish female slave.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} immediately challenges this: {\i adaraba}, on the contrary, he is now permitted to marry a Jewish woman! Why should the loss of access to a {\i shifcha} be considered a detriment when he gains access to a Jewish wife?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Slave Preference\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara}\u8217's answer is striking: {\i avda b\u8217'hefkera nicha lei} \u8212- a slave prefers a life of {\i hefkera}, of unrestrained, unbounded existence. Upon manumission, the slave enters into the obligations and social constraints of Jewish law, including the norms governing marriage and sexual conduct. The passage elaborates, in Rashi\u8217's formulation: {\i zola hi b\u8217'einav u-mezalzel ba l-chol ta\u8217'avato} \u8212- the female slave is cheap in his eyes, he treats her with contempt and exploits her however he wishes. She is {\i shchicha lei}, perpetually available to him, and {\i pritza lei}, she too has no sexual restraints. The {\i gemara} describes this life of unchecked access as something the slave, reprehensibly, prefers to the constrained dignity of freedom. This entire picture is presented, of course, through the lens of the Talmudic sages imagining the slave\u8217's psychology \u8212- a perspective with its own troubling dimensions worth noting \u8212- but the legal point is clear: since there are scenarios in which a slave might genuinely not benefit from freedom, Rabbi Meir holds that one cannot automatically assume {\i zachin} applies.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Mishnah: After Death\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishnah Cases\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} before us presents a new set of cases: {\i ha-omer tenu get zeh l\u8217'ishti u-shtar shichrur zeh l\u8217'avdi u-meis lo yitnu l\u8217'achar misa} \u8212- if a man says \u8220"give this {\i get} to my wife\u8221" or \u8220"this {\i shtar shichrur} to my slave,\u8221" and he then dies, the document may not be given after death. However, if a man says \u8220"give a hundred dollars to so-and-so\u8221" and then dies \u8212- {\i yitnu l\u8217'achar misa} \u8212- it is given.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 There is a textual question about whether the word {\i zeh} (\u8220"this\u8221") appears in the first clause, suggesting a document already prepared and being handed over. Some read the {\i mishnah} without {\i zeh}, understanding the case as an instruction to write and then deliver \u8212- in which case the slave transfers to the heirs and the woman becomes an {\i almanah} upon the husband\u8217's death, and neither transaction can be completed. Either way, the {\i mishnah}\u8217's logic is that the {\i get} and {\i shtar shichrur} cannot take effect after death. The puzzle is why the money {\i does} transfer.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Yitzchak: Conditions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Yitzchak bar Shmuel bar Marta, citing Rav, states: {\i v\u8217'hu she-tzvurim u-munachim b\u8217'keren zavis} \u8212- the rule that the money transfers applies only when the money is piled together and set aside in a specific location, like a designated stack in the corner of a room. This condition of {\i tzburim u-munachim} \u8212- specific, concretely identifiable funds \u8212- is required for the transfer to take effect.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Healthy vs.\u160 ?Deathbed\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might ask: under what circumstances does this case arise, and why does the specificity of {\i tzburim u-munachim} matter? The {\i gemara} poses the question directly: {\i b\u8217'mai askinan} \u8212- what are the circumstances? If we are dealing with a {\i bari}, a healthy person, then the mere fact that the money is piled in a corner does not help \u8212- {\i hala meshach}, the recipient never took possession. If no acquisition was made and the man later dies, his estate passes to the heirs; why should his instructions transfer the money?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 We must therefore be discussing a {\i shchiv mei\u8217'ra}, a person on his deathbed. The {\i halacha} is that the words of a {\i shchiv mei\u8217'ra} take automatic legal effect \u8212- {\i divrei shchiv mei\u8217'ra k-chsuvim u-chmssurim damu}, the words of a dying person are regarded as if already written and delivered \u8212- because we are concerned that frustrating his final wishes would agitate him and hasten his death. But if that is the case, why does the money need to be in a pile at all? Even a general instruction to give a hundred dollars from his estate should suffice.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shchiv Mei\u8217'ra Effect\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} also considers why the {\i mishnah} does not explain the {\i get} and {\i shtar shichrur} as simply not transferring \u8212- rather than having actually taken effect \u8212- by resort to {\i mitzvah l\u8217'kayeim divrei ha-meis}, the obligation to fulfill the wishes of the deceased. {\i Tosafot} and Rashi discuss this; while the concept exists and is debated, the {\i gemara}\u8217's assumption is that in the case of money, an actual transfer has occurred, not merely a posthumous wish to be honored. The question therefore remains: how and why does the money transfer, and why the requirement of {\i tzburim u-munachim}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Ashi answers: {\i le-olam bari u-kh\u8217'Rav Huna} \u8212- we can in fact be discussing a healthy person, consistent with Rav Huna\u8217's ruling. Rav Huna, citing Rav, had stated: {\i maneh li b\u8217'yadcha tenehu l\u8217'ploni b\u8217'maamad shloshtan kanah} \u8212- if Reuven says to Shimon \u8220"you owe me a hundred dollars; instead of giving it to me, give it to Levi,\u8221" and all three parties are present at the time of this declaration, the transfer takes effect immediately. Shimon now owes Levi. This is the mechanism of {\i maamad shloshtan}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 On this reading, the {\i mishnah} is describing exactly this scenario: the money is piled in Shimon\u8217's possession, the man instructs Shimon to give it to a third party, all three are present, and the transfer takes effect by {\i maamad shloshtan}. This explains the requirement of {\i tzburim u-munachim} \u8212- it must be a concrete, specific object, because according to Rav, {\i maamad shloshtan} works only for a {\i pikadon}, something tangible, not for an abstract debt. Furthermore, as {\i Tosafot} notes, the advantage of {\i maamad shloshtan} over simple {\i zachin} is that {\i maamad shloshtan} can effect the transfer even if Shimon objects and does not wish to comply.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This also explains why the {\i get} and {\i shtar shichrur} cannot transfer: {\i maamad shloshtan} applies to money and property, not to documents whose legal effect depends on personal relationship.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Pappa Alternative\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Pappa offers an alternative reading: {\i le-olam b\u8217'shchiv mei\u8217'ra u-kh\u8217'idach d\u8217'Rav} \u8212- we are indeed discussing a {\i shchiv mei\u8217'ra}, and the relevant ruling of Rav is a different one: {\i shchiv mei\u8217'ra she-amar tenu maneh l\u8217'ploni mi-nechasai}, if a dying person says \u8220"give so-and-so a hundred dollars from my estate\u8221" \u8212- {\i maneh zeh nosnim}, if he specified which hundred dollars, it is given; {\i maneh stam ein nosnim}, if he said \u8220"a hundred dollars\u8221" without specification, it is not given. Why? {\i Chashinan shema maneh kavur ka-amar} \u8212- we are concerned that perhaps he meant a buried cache of money that no one can locate, and there is no identifiable sum to transfer.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 On this reading, {\i tzburim u-munachim} means not the {\i pikadon} aspect for {\i maamad shloshtan}, but the specificity required so that the {\i shchiv mei\u8217'ra}\u8217's instruction can be carried out without ambiguity. The {\i gemara} concludes: {\i v\u8217'hilchasa l\u8217'kavor lo chaishinan} \u8212- as a practical matter of {\i halacha}, we do not maintain the concern about buried money.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Buried Money Issue\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The logic of Rav Pappa\u8217's position, then, is that the dying person\u8217's words automatically take effect \u8212- {\i divrei shchiv mei\u8217'ra k-chsuvim u-chmssurim damu} \u8212- but only when the subject of those words is concretely identifiable. \u8220"A hundred dollars in that pile in the corner\u8221" works; \u8220"a hundred dollars from my estate\u8221" does not, because we cannot identify which funds he intended, and we fear he may have had buried money in mind. The {\i tzburim u-munachim} requirement functions here as a marker of sufficient specificity, not as the mechanism of transfer.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Zevid Objection\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Zevid resists Rav Pappa\u8217's reading. He argues: {\i lo misukma masnisin b\u8217'shchiv mei\u8217'ra} \u8212- the {\i mishnah} does not appear to be dealing with a {\i shchiv mei\u8217'ra} at all, and we should not introduce that reading. His evidence: the {\i mishnah} says that if the man said \u8220"give this {\i get} to my wife,\u8221" then so long as he was alive, one would deliver it; the reason is that he said {\i tenu}, \u8220"give\u8221" \u8212- implying that had he merely said {\i kisvu}, \u8220"write,\u8221" delivery would not follow. But by a {\i shchiv mei\u8217'ra}, the rule is {\i af al gav d\u8217'lo amar tenu nosnim} \u8212- even if he only said {\i kisvu}, \u8220"write,\u8221" we assume he meant write it and deliver it. This principle is established in a {\i mishnah} elsewhere: a man being led out in chains to execution who said {\i kisvu get l\u8217'ishti} is understood to have meant \u8220"write it and give it.\u8221" Similarly, one departing on a sea journey or caravan. Rabbi Shimon Shezuri adds even a person who is critically ill, i.e., a {\i shchiv mei\u8217'ra}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Accordingly, if the {\i mishnah} were discussing a {\i shchiv mei\u8217'ra}, the distinction it draws between {\i tenu} and {\i kisvu} would not hold \u8212- a {\i shchiv mei\u8217'ra} who says {\i kisvu} would also have his instructions delivered. This undermines reading the {\i mishnah} as a {\i shchiv mei\u8217'ra} case.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Write vs.\u160 ?Give\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Ashi challenges Rav Zevid: {\i u-mimai d\u8217'masnisin Rabbi Shimon Shezuri hee dilma rabbanan hee} \u8212- why must we assume the {\i mishnah} follows Rabbi Shimon Shezuri\u8217's view? It may follow the {\i rabbanan}, who did not extend the {\i kisvu}-means-{\i tenu} principle to a {\i shchiv mei\u8217'ra}. Under the {\i rabbanan}\u8217's view, a {\i shchiv mei\u8217'ra} who says only {\i kisvu} has his document written but not necessarily delivered. On that reading, the {\i mishnah}\u8217's implication that {\i tenu} was necessary and {\i kisvu} would be insufficient is perfectly compatible with the case being a {\i shchiv mei\u8217'ra}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Thus the {\i mishnah}\u8217's distinction between {\i tenu} and {\i kisvu} in the context of {\i get} and {\i shtar shichrur} reflects a real legal boundary: \u8220"give\u8221" triggers delivery and therefore the full process of granting freedom or divorce, while \u8220"write\u8221" does not. The money case is categorically different \u8212- whether by {\i maamad shloshtan} for a healthy person or by {\i divrei shchiv mei\u8217'ra} for one on his deathbed \u8212- and therefore transfers even after death.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Maamad Shloshtan\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Huna Transfer\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now sets aside its analysis of the {\i mishnah} and examines the principle of {\i maamad shloshtan} on its own terms. Rav Huna, citing Rav, states: {\i maneh li b\u8217'yadcha tenehu lo l\u8217'ploni b\u8217'maamad shloshtan kanah} \u8212- if Reuven says to Shimon \u8220"you owe me a hundred dollars; give it to Levi,\u8221" and all three of them are present, Levi acquires it. The question before us is: what is the legal mechanism that makes this work, and what are its limits?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Pikadon vs.\u160 ?Debt\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava initially reasons that {\i maamad shloshtan} makes intuitive sense in the case of a {\i pikadon} \u8212- a concrete object held in trust. If Shimon is holding Reuven\u8217's laptop, and Reuven says in the presence of all three \u8220"give that laptop to Levi,\u8221" one can identify a mechanism: Shimon\u8217's possession of the object, combined with his agreement, operates to transfer ownership to Levi. The object is specific; the relationship is tangible. Rava therefore says {\i mistabra milta d\u8217'Rav b\u8217'pikadon} \u8212- Rav\u8217's ruling is most naturally understood as applying to a {\i pikadon}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But by a {\i milveh} \u8212- an abstract debt \u8212- there is no object changing hands. Shimon simply owes Reuven money. How does Reuven\u8217's declaration in the presence of all three suddenly convert that obligation into one running to Levi? Rava was initially inclined to say {\i aval b\u8217'milveh lo} \u8212- it does not work for a debt.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Milveh Application\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 However, the {\i gemara} immediately corrects this: {\i v\u8217'ha lav hachi, amar Rava afilu b\u8217'milveh} \u8212- in fact, Rava himself said it applies even to a {\i milveh}. And this is supported by a parallel tradition: Shmuel, citing Levi, stated explicitly that {\i maneh li b\u8217'yadcha tenehu l\u8217'ploni b\u8217'maamad shloshtan kanah} applies even where what Shimon holds is a loan, not a tangible deposit. The consensus is therefore that {\i maamad shloshtan} works for {\i milveh} as well.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Implicit Agreement\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then asks {\i v\u8217'ta\u8217'ama mai} \u8212- what is the rationale? Amemar explains: {\i na\u8217'aseh k\u8217'omer lo b\u8217'sha\u8217'at matan ma\u8217'ot shibadna lach u-l\u8217'didach u-l\u8217'chol d\u8217'asu machmatchach} \u8212- it is as if, at the moment Shimon received the loan, he implicitly said to Reuven: \u8220"I obligate myself to you, and to you, and to anyone who comes on your account \u8212- any designee you name.\u8221" When one borrows money, one implicitly undertakes to repay it not only to the lender but to whomever the lender might direct. This implicit undertaking is the legal mechanism behind {\i maamad shloshtan} for {\i milveh}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Future Designees\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Ashi raises a sharp difficulty with Amemar\u8217's explanation. The mechanism Amemar describes roots the obligation in what Shimon implicitly agreed to at the time he borrowed the money. But this only works if Levi was already alive at that time \u8212- Shimon could then be said to have implicitly obligated himself ten years ago to pay whoever Reuven might designate, including Levi. But what if Levi was born only after the loan was made?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Ashi makes the point precise: {\i da\u8217'afilu le-Rabbi Meir de-amar adam makneh davar she-lo ba le-olam} \u8212- even according to Rabbi Meir, who holds that one may transfer ownership of something not yet in existence (e.g., selling future produce of a tree), that leniency concerns the {\i object} being transferred, not the {\i recipient}. {\i Aval le-davar she-eino ba\u8217'olam lo} \u8212- one cannot transfer to a person who does not yet exist. Shimon ten years ago could not have obligated himself to Levi who was not yet born, because one cannot incur obligations toward a non-existent person. Amemar\u8217's mechanism therefore breaks down in such cases.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 New Obligation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Ashi therefore reformulates the mechanism: {\i be-hai hana\u8217'ah de-ka mishtanya lei bein milveh yeshanah le-milveh chadashah gamar u-meshabeid nafsheih} \u8212- at the moment of the {\i maamad shloshtan} transfer, Shimon benefits. An old debt is transformed into a new obligation running to Levi. Shimon, who was in arrears on a ten-year-old loan, now has a fresh obligation with Levi, giving him some breathing room. It is this benefit \u8212- received in real time at the moment of the transfer \u8212- that constitutes the consideration for Shimon\u8217's current self-obligation to Levi. This sidesteps the problem of retroactive obligation to someone not yet born, because the operative moment is now, not ten years ago.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Undesirable Parties\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Huna Mar, son of Rav Nechemya, challenges Rav Ashi\u8217's reformulated mechanism. If the logic is that Shimon benefits from the conversion of an old debt into a new one, then consider a case: {\i ke-gon hani de-bei Bar Elyashiv} \u8212- parties like those of the Bar Elyashiv household, {\i de-kaftei ve-shaklei le-altar} \u8212- who bind debtors and collect immediately. If Reuven transfers Shimon\u8217's debt to these people, Shimon is clearly worse off, not better. There is no benefit to him in this conversion whatsoever. Does {\i maamad shloshtan} therefore fail to work in such a case? Would we say {\i de-lo kanu} \u8212- no acquisition took place? And if we do say that, then we have created a mechanism that varies case by case, requiring us to always assess whether the specific transferee is one from whom Shimon genuinely benefits \u8212- {\i natata devarekha le-shiyurin}, you have made the law contingent on subjective assessment.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbinical Decree\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Mar Zutra resolves the impasse by stepping back from the search for mechanism entirely: {\i hani telat milei shavinu rabanan ke-hilkheta belo ta\u8217'ama} \u8212- there are three things the Rabbis established as binding {\i halacha} without a clearly justifiable legal mechanism. {\i Maamad shloshtan} is one of them.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is a striking conclusion. {\i Belo ta\u8217'ama} here does not mean without any motivation \u8212- presumably there was ample practical reason for the Rabbis to want such a transfer mechanism to exist. What it means is that there is no fully coherent underlying legal theory that explains {\i why} it works in all cases. In the case of {\i pikadon}, one can sketch a mechanism \u8212- Shimon\u8217's possession, combined with his consent, operates to transfer the object. But extending {\i maamad shloshtan} to {\i milveh} and to cases where the transferee was not yet alive at the time of the loan, and to cases where Shimon derives no benefit from the conversion \u8212- in all these extensions, the legal mechanism breaks down. The Rabbis nonetheless decreed that it works, and it stands as one of those rabbinic enactments grounded in practical wisdom rather than derivable doctrine. The discussion will continue on the following daf.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Gittin 14\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Shachiv Mera & Maamad\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishna Overview\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} at the opening of our discussion presents a fundamental distinction: if a {\i shachiv mera} \u8212- someone on his deathbed \u8212- commands that money be distributed, that instruction takes effect even after his death. By contrast, a {\i get} or {\i shtar shichrur} cannot be delivered once the sender has died. The {\i gemara} seeks to understand the precise legal basis for this distinction, and in doing so it unpacks two separate explanations for the {\i mishna}\u8217's ruling.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shachiv Mera Explanation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The first explanation invokes the principle of {\i divrei shachiv mera kkesuvon u\u8217'kmesuran damu} \u8212- the words of a person on his deathbed are considered as though they were already written, signed, and delivered. This is a rabbinic construct granting the deathbed bequest immediate legal force. Under this reading, the instruction to distribute the money works after death because {\i Chazal} endowed such expressions with a kind of automatic efficacy from the moment they are uttered.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Maamad Shloshtan\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The second explanation does not require the speaker to be ill at all. Rather, it invokes the principle of {\i maamad shloshtan} \u8212- the three of them being present. The scenario is this: Reuven owes me money, and rather than collecting it myself, I instruct Reuven in Shimon\u8217's presence to pay that money directly to Shimon. All three parties \u8212- the creditor, the debtor, and the intended recipient \u8212- are in the room together at the same time. Under the principle of {\i maamad shloshtan}, this instruction effects a legally binding transfer.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mechanism Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara}, however, presses on the underlying mechanism. When the subject is a concrete object \u8212- a {\i pikadon}, a deposit that Reuven is physically holding \u8212- one can understand how it might be transferred: the object is in Reuven\u8217's possession and can be re-designated for Shimon\u8217's benefit. But {\i maamad shloshtan} applies even to an abstract {\i milve}, a monetary debt. What is the legal mechanism by which a debt owed to one party can be redirected to a third party simply because all three are present in the room? The {\i gemara} proposes several possible mechanisms but ultimately rejects each of them, leaving the question open as it transitions to Mar Zutra\u8217's formulation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Mar Zutra\u8217's Ruling\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mar Zutra Teaching\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} on 14a, approximately six lines in, records the teaching of Mar Zutra: {\i hani tlat mili shavyinhu rabbanan k\u8217'hilchata belo ta\u8217'ama} \u8212- these three matters the Rabbis established as operative {\i halachos} without a stated reason. The phrase {\i belo ta\u8217'ama} \u8212- without a reason \u8212- requires careful unpacking, and it is precisely here that {\i Tosafot} offers its important interpretive intervention.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafot Interpretation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} clarifies that {\i belo ta\u8217'ama} does not mean there is no purpose or benefit in having these institutions. Rather, it means there is no clear legal {\i mechanism} \u8212- no satisfying technical explanation for {\i how} they work. The motivation for enacting each of them is apparent enough. As {\i Tosafot} puts it: {\i aval ta\u8217'am yesh lama tiknu chachamim maamad shloshtan} \u8212- there is indeed a reason why the Rabbis instituted {\i maamad shloshtan}, namely {\i shelo yitzatarech litroach vela\u8217'asot kinyanim} \u8212- so that one would not need to go to the trouble of executing formal {\i kinyanim} in every commercial transfer. It was a practical and efficient mechanism for commerce. What is lacking is not the rationale for the enactment but the internal jurisprudential logic \u8212- the {\i mechanics} \u8212- of how it achieves its result.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Three Enactments\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} enumerates three rabbinic enactments that share this character. The first is {\i maamad shloshtan} itself, as described above. The second is the rule stated by Rav Yehuda in the name of Shmuel: if a man writes all his property over to his wife \u8212- {\i hakosev kol nechasav le\u8217'ishto} \u8212- we do not take the document at face value as a complete transfer of ownership. Instead, {\i lo asah ella apotropa} \u8212- he has effectively designated her as an {\i apotropa}, a kind of executor or power of attorney, not as the outright owner of his estate. The third enactment concerns the case of a father who marries off his son and holds the wedding celebration in a house built or dedicated specifically for the son.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Enactments Details\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} explains the logic underlying each of the latter two enactments, even as it acknowledges they function without a fully transparent mechanism. Regarding the wife as {\i apotropa}: {\i mishum dela shavik livrei ve\u8217'yahiv le\u8217'achrini} \u8212- we assume a man would not wish to disinherit his children entirely by giving everything to his wife, since it is not the usual practice to pass one\u8217's estate to a spouse rather than to one\u8217's descendants. Therefore, however the document reads on its face, we interpret the gesture as one of honor and authority \u8212- {\i lachalek lah kavod velashavya apotropa} \u8212- rather than as a genuine conveyance of title. The intent, while not provable from the document alone, is presumed from the social logic of the situation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The third case \u8212- the {\i chuppah} transaction \u8212- similarly turns on inferred {\i da\u8217'at}. If a father holds the wedding party for his adult son in a house set aside specifically for that purpose, emptying it of his own possessions and dedicating it to the occasion, we presume that he has expressed a genuine intent to transfer ownership of the house to the son. In all three cases, the Rabbis drew on their understanding of what a person in such a situation would plausibly intend and declared those consequences to follow as a matter of {\i halacha}, even without a formally airtight legal mechanism.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Tosafot Questions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Refusal Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Once {\i maamad shloshtan} is understood to operate as a rabbinic enactment without a clear technical mechanism, {\i Tosafot} raises a pair of consequential questions. The first: what happens if one of the three parties refuses? In the standard scenario, Reuven owes me money, and I instruct him \u8212- in Shimon\u8217's presence \u8212- to pay that money to Shimon instead. But what if Reuven refuses? Does {\i maamad shloshtan} still work? {\i Tosafot} suggests it might, because the principle does not depend on Reuven\u8217's cooperation \u8212- {\i Chazal} granted the creditor the authority to redirect the debt regardless of the debtor\u8217's preferences. The point of {\i maamad shloshtan} is precisely that no formal {\i kinyan} or consent mechanism is required.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Non-Jewish Parties\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The second question {\i Tosafot} raises is whether {\i maamad shloshtan} applies when one or more of the three parties is not Jewish. This is less a question of mechanism than of the enactment\u8217's scope \u8212- did the Rabbis institute {\i maamad shloshtan} universally, or only in transactions among Jews? {\i Tosafot} raises the question without resolving it definitively, leaving it as a matter of {\i halachic} speculation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Saffron Transaction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Saffron Transfer\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now introduces a practical illustration of {\i maamad shloshtan} from Rav\u8217's own conduct. Rav said to Rav Acha Bardla: {\i kava de\u8217'morika} \u8212- a {\i kav} of {\i morika}, which the {\i gemara} identifies as saffron, a commodity of extraordinary value, comparable by weight to gold even in antiquity (though {\i Tosafot} on 47a in Gittin notes an alternative identification as safflower, a cheaper substitute used for producing yellow dye). Rav told Rav Acha Bardla: {\i is li gabach} \u8212- you are holding this for me, and I want you to give it to a certain {\i ploni}. He said: {\i be\u8217'apei ka\u8217'amina lach} \u8212- I am telling you this in that person\u8217's presence, {\i delo hadarna bi} \u8212- and I will not retract. The {\i gemara} notes the apparent implication: the phrasing suggests that Rav was invoking the social pressure of saying this in front of the intended recipient as a personal guarantee against backing out, which would imply that {\i legally} he retained the right to retract. Why else emphasize the presence of the third party as a bulwark against reversal?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Repetition Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then asks: {\i ha amar Rav chada zimna} \u8212- but Rav has already taught this principle! The {\i gemara} cites the previously stated ruling: {\i de\u8217'amar Rav Huna amar Rav \u8212- maneh li b\u8217'yadcha tnahu l\u8217'ploni b\u8217'ma\u8217'amad shloshtan kana} \u8212- if you instruct the holder of your money to give it to a third party in the presence of all three, the acquisition is effective. If Rav has already articulated this {\i halacha}, what does the saffron incident add?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Small vs.\u160 ?Large Gifts\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} answers: the earlier ruling might have been understood to apply only to a large gift \u8212- {\i matana meruba}, like a {\i maneh} \u8212- on the theory that the gravitas of a substantial transaction justifies the special efficacy of {\i maamad shloshtan}. One might have thought that for a small gift \u8212- {\i matana mu\u8217'etet} \u8212- the presence of all three parties is not required. Rav\u8217's conduct with the saffron teaches that {\i maamad shloshtan} applies equally to smaller transfers. {\i Rashi} frames the smaller gift as the saffron itself, though as one student pointed out, saffron by weight is comparably priced to gold, making it not obviously a trivial amount. {\i Rashi}\u8217's point is apparently about the relative categorization within the {\i gemara}\u8217's framework, where a {\i maneh} is the paradigmatic large gift. The {\i gemara}\u8217's answer accordingly clarifies that the requirement of all three parties being present \u8212- {\i bifanav} \u8212- holds regardless of the magnitude of the transfer.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Gardeners Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Account Settlement\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now turns to a vivid practical illustration. A group of gardeners \u8212- sharecroppers working land together \u8212- sat down to balance their accounts with one another after the growing season, tallying who had borrowed what and who owed what to whom. At the end of the accounting, five {\i istarei zuzei} \u8212- five units of a lesser coinage \u8212- remained outstanding, owed by one of them to the group collectively. The group decided that rather than collecting those five units among themselves, they would give the amount as a bonus to {\i marei ar\u8217'a}, the owner of the land. They declared this in the landowner\u8217's presence \u8212- {\i be\u8217'apei marei ar\u8217'a} \u8212- and the debtor even performed a {\i kinyan sudar}, a handkerchief transfer, to underscore his commitment. This constituted a classic {\i maamad shloshtan}: the group\u8217's claim against the debtor was redirected to a third party \u8212- the landowner \u8212- in the presence of all three parties.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mathematical Error\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Subsequently, the debtor reviewed the books and discovered that the original accounting had been mistaken. He did not owe the group any money at all. {\i Tosafot} explains that either the books themselves made this self-evident, or the other gardeners acknowledged the error upon examination \u8212- in any event, the mistake was not contested. The question then became: having already performed {\i maamad shloshtan} and having done a {\i kinyan sudar}, was he still obligated to pay the five units to the landowner?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Nachman\u8217's Ruling\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The parties came before Rav Nachman, who ruled: {\i mai avid lachu} \u8212- there is nothing I can do for you, you must pay. His reasoning was twofold: first, the principle of {\i maamad shloshtan} itself \u8212- as stated by Rav Huna in the name of Rav \u8212- effects a binding transfer; second, and independently, the debtor had performed a {\i kinyan}, which creates its own binding obligation. You committed yourself, said Rav Nachman, and you cannot retract.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava\u8217's Objection\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava immediately challenged this ruling. The debtor, he pointed out, was not saying {\i lo yahvina} \u8212- I refuse to pay. He was saying {\i delika gabai} \u8212- I do not actually have those funds. He had agreed to transfer money he {\i believed} he owed; it turned out he owed nothing. This is not a case of refusing to honor {\i maamad shloshtan} or reneging on a {\i kinyan} \u8212- it is a case where the entire transaction was premised on a factual error. Upon hearing this clarification, Rav Nachman reconsidered: {\i im ken, kinyan bete\u8217'ut hu} \u8212- if that is the case, then this is a {\i kinyan} executed on the basis of a mistaken assumption, and {\i kol kinyan bete\u8217'ut chozer} \u8212- any {\i kinyan} rooted in a fundamental mistake is void. The case thus illustrates both the power of {\i maamad shloshtan} and its limits: it can only transfer what actually exists as a valid underlying obligation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Holich vs.\u160 ?Ten\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Holich Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 With the analysis of {\i maamad shloshtan} complete, the {\i gemara} transitions to a distinct but related set of questions about the language used when instructing a messenger to deliver funds. Recall that the {\i gemara} earlier established the principle of {\i ten kezachi}: when I say {\i ten} \u8212- give \u8212- to a messenger, that word functions as an authorization for the messenger to take possession on behalf of the recipient, grounded in the principle of {\i zachin leadam shelo befanav} \u8212- one may acquire something on behalf of another person even in their absence. The question now is whether the word {\i holich} \u8212- deliver, or go bring \u8212- carries the same legal weight. Is {\i holich kezachi}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This question is not merely semantic. Two distinct issues converge. First, does my use of the word {\i holich} indicate that I {\i want} the messenger to take possession on behalf of the recipient, or does it merely instruct conveyance, leaving possession in my hands until delivery? Second, even setting aside my intent, is it actually in the recipient\u8217's interest for the messenger to take full possession on his behalf? If so, {\i zachin leadam shelo befanav} would support it; if not, it would not.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav\u8217's Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav rules: {\i chayav be\u8217'achrayuso} \u8212- the sender remains responsible if the money is lost. The debt has not yet been paid. And yet \u8212- {\i ve\u8217'im ba lachazor eino chozer} \u8212- if the sender wishes to retract, he cannot. This is a striking intermediate position. The messenger holds the money in a way that prevents the sender from demanding it back, but the recipient has not yet acquired it sufficiently for the debt to be discharged. If the money is lost in transit, the debtor still owes the creditor. The messenger has taken a {\i partial} possession \u8212- enough to block retraction, not enough to complete payment.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shmuel Opposition\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Shmuel disagrees. His position is that there is no logical space for such a middle state. {\i Mitoch shechayav be\u8217'achrayuso} \u8212- since the sender remains liable if the money is lost \u8212- it follows that the money is still legally his. And if it is still his, {\i iy ba lachazor chozer} \u8212- he retains the right to retract. You cannot simultaneously say the money is not yet the recipient\u8217's and also that the sender cannot take it back. Either the debt is paid and the sender is off the hook, or it is not paid and he retains full control. There is no halfway position.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Middle Position Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} explores whether the Rav-Shmuel dispute turns on the question of {\i holich kezachi} directly \u8212- that is, whether Rav holds {\i holich kezachi} and Shmuel holds {\i holich lav kezachi} \u8212- or whether the disagreement is at a deeper level. The {\i gemara} concludes that in fact both Rav and Shmuel may agree that {\i holich} functions like {\i zachi} in the sense that the sender is prepared to have the messenger represent the recipient\u8217's interest. The actual dispute, the {\i gemara} suggests, is whether a middle state of partial possession is legally coherent.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav\u8217's position is that such a middle state is possible: the messenger takes possession on the recipient\u8217's behalf in a limited fashion \u8212- sufficient to prevent retraction, insufficient to discharge the debt. This serves the recipient\u8217's interest optimally: he is protected from the sender changing his mind, while also protected from bearing the risk of loss in transit. Shmuel\u8217's counter \u8212- characterized by the {\i gemara} using the term {\i migo}, though not in its usual technical sense of a credibility argument \u8212- is that this intermediate state simply cannot exist as a matter of legal logic. If you have not yet paid the debt, the money remains yours, and you may reclaim it. Shmuel\u8217's {\i migo} here simply means: you cannot have it both ways. Partial possession is not a recognized category.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is worth noting that within this framework, {\i ten} \u8212- \u8220"give\u8221" \u8212- has already been firmly established as {\i kezachi}: if I say {\i give} this money to Ploni, the messenger takes full possession on Ploni\u8217's behalf immediately. The ongoing question throughout this section of the {\i gemara} is specifically whether {\i holich} \u8212- the language of delivery rather than giving \u8212- carries the same force.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Braita for Rav\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} cites a {\i braita} in support of Rav\u8217's position. The {\i braita} states that whether one says {\i holich maneh l\u8217'ploni she\u8217'ani chayav lo} \u8212- deliver this {\i maneh} to Ploni whom I owe \u8212- or {\i ten maneh l\u8217'ploni she\u8217'ani chayav lo} \u8212- give this {\i maneh} to Ploni whom I owe \u8212- and whether the underlying asset is a monetary debt or a {\i pikadon} held on deposit, in all cases {\i chayav be\u8217'achrayut} \u8212- the sender remains responsible if anything is lost \u8212- {\i ve\u8217'im ba lachazor eino chozer} \u8212- and he cannot retract. This {\i braita} supports Rav\u8217's position that {\i holich} and {\i ten} are legally equivalent: both create the binding intermediate state Rav described.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Pikadon Holding\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Pikadon Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i braita}\u8217's extension of this rule to the case of a {\i pikadon} \u8212- a concrete object held on deposit \u8212- raises a sharp question. When the {\i braita} says the sender cannot retract even in the case of a {\i pikadon}, this requires justification. In the monetary-debt scenario, one can argue that the messenger automatically represents the creditor\u8217's interest because it is beneficial to the creditor to have someone holding the money on his behalf. But in the case of a {\i pikadon} \u8212- say, a valuable silver watch that the debtor has been holding and now wishes to return through a messenger \u8212- can we truly say the messenger represents the recipient\u8217's interest? The recipient may well say: I did not appoint this messenger as my {\i shaliach}. I only trust the original holder. I would prefer the watch to remain where it is rather than have it pass through someone else\u8217's hands. In that case, is it not in the recipient\u8217's interest to say {\i ein retzono sheyehei pikdono beyadecha} \u8212- I do not want my deposit in your hands \u8212- and demand that the sender take it back and return it himself?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Zeira Exception\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Zeira provides the answer: this concern is valid in the normal case, but the {\i braita} is speaking of an exceptional circumstance \u8212- {\i keshehuchzak kafran} \u8212- when the holder of the deposit has established himself as one who denies the deposit\u8217's existence. In such a case, the holder had been consistently lying, telling the owner that he had no such watch, denying all knowledge of the deposit whenever asked. Then his conscience impelled him to arrange for its return through a messenger. In this situation, it is manifestly in the owner\u8217's interest for the messenger to hold onto the {\i pikadon} and refuse to return it to the denying party. The concern that the recipient might not want a third party handling his property is overridden by the far greater concern that the {\i pikadon} would disappear again if returned to someone who has already demonstrated the willingness to deny its existence. Thus the {\i braita}\u8217's ruling stands: even in the {\i pikadon} case, there are circumstances where the messenger\u8217's holding is clearly in the recipient\u8217's best interest, and in those circumstances the sender cannot retract.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Sheshet Collection\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Collection Instructions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now presents a series of practical cases that illuminate the dynamics of the {\i shaliach}\u8217's role in debt collection. Rav Sheshet had sold {\i sarbale} \u8212- coats, as {\i Rashi} explains \u8212- in Machuza on credit, and he was seeking to collect from his debtors. Rav Yosef bar Chama happened to be traveling to Machuza, so Rav Sheshet asked him: on your way back, {\i aitinhu nihali} \u8212- please collect the money and bring it to me. This was an explicit appointment of a {\i shaliach} for collection \u8212- no principle of {\i zachin leadam shelo befanav} was even needed, since Rav Yosef was explicitly authorized.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kinyan Refusal\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Yosef went to the debtors, who paid him. But then the debtors asked: {\i nikni minach} \u8212- do a {\i kinyan} with us, a {\i kinyan sudar}, agreeing that we are now fully discharged and that if anything happens to the money you are now responsible. Rav Yosef said he would, but in the end {\i ishtamit lehu} \u8212- he evaded them, and never actually completed the {\i kinyan} that would have considered the debt paid upon handover to him. He thus preserved the optimal position for Rav Sheshet: he held the money, but the debtors remained liable until it was actually delivered.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sheshet\u8217's Praise\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 When Rav Yosef returned to Rav Sheshet, Rav Sheshet praised him: {\i shappir avadt} \u8212- you did well not to agree to assume that liability. The reasoning: {\i delo shaveit nafshach eved loveh le\u8217'ish malveh} \u8212- you should not have made yourself into a debtor to those debtors, you should not have placed yourself in their debt by agreeing to bear responsibility for the money. The {\i pasuk} teaches that a borrower is like a servant to a lender. Rav Sheshet\u8217's instruction was that a {\i shaliach} collecting a debt should not agree to take on the debtor\u8217's liability. The debtors remain in the position of obligation until the creditor himself receives the money. An alternative version of Rav Sheshet\u8217's praise makes the point from the creditor\u8217's side: those people are {\i my} servants until the debt is paid \u8212- why should you relieve them of that obligation?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Goblet Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Goblet Retrieval\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A further illustration comes from the case of Rav Achai berabi Yoshiya, who had a silver goblet \u8212- an {\i ishpaka de-chaspa} \u8212- being held for him in Nahardea. He asked two rabbis, Rav Dostai berabi Yannai and Rav Yosef berabi Keifar, to retrieve it on their way back from Nahardea. They went, and the holders of the goblet gave it to them.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kinyan Demand\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Then the holders had second thoughts and said: {\i nikni minaychu} \u8212- do a {\i kinyan} with us, confirming that we are now discharged and that responsibility for the goblet has passed to you. Rav Yosef berabi Keifar refused, correctly holding to the position we have been tracing: the messenger should not agree to release the holders from responsibility, as this would allow a middle state where he holds the goblet without the debt being considered repaid. The holders then demanded the goblet back. Rav Yosef refused to return it as well, correctly recognizing that he was entitled to hold it in the best interest of the owner without yielding either to the kinyan demand or to the retraction request. Rav Dostai berabi Yannai, however, agreed to return it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Pressure Defense\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Yosef berabi Keifar then came to Rav Dostai and said: {\i chazi mar} \u8212- look what they are doing to me, they are beating me up. Remarkably, Rav Dostai responded: {\i tov} \u8212- good, let them beat you, you should give it back. When they later came before Rebbi Oshaya and Rav Yosef complained \u8212- {\i lo mistaya d\u8217'lo siyan ela amar lehu nami tov ramu lei} \u8212- not only did Rav Dostai fail to help me, he told them to keep beating me \u8212- Rav Dostai explained his reasoning. The people they were dealing with, he said, were men of enormous physical stature, with thunderous voices and terrifying names \u8212- Arda, Arta, and Pili Barish \u8212- and with connections to the government, horses and mules at their command, and the effective power to execute anyone they wished. {\i Iy harguvei l\u8217'Dustai, mi nasan l\u8217'Yannai abba bar kamosi} \u8212- if they had killed me, who would have given my father Yannai a replacement son?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Practical Lesson\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The concluding exchange underscores the lesson. When Rav Dostai was asked whether these men had governmental connections, horses, and mules, and he confirmed that they did, the response was: {\i shappir avadt} \u8212- then you did the right thing, you should have given back the goblet. The principle being illustrated here is not only the legal rights of the {\i shaliach} but the practical wisdom that legal entitlements must be weighed against real-world circumstances. The {\i gemara} presents this with some humor, but the underlying point is serious: a {\i shaliach} operating under genuine physical threat is not bound to enforce his principal\u8217's interests at the cost of his life.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Holich Kizachi\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Recipient Death Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} returns to the foundational question of {\i holich kezachi}. The scenario under consideration is as follows: a sender instructs a messenger \u8212- {\i holich maneh l\u8217'ploni} \u8212- deliver this {\i maneh} to Ploni. The messenger goes to find Ploni but cannot, because Ploni has died. The question is what happens to the money. Two {\i braitot} appear to contradict each other: one says {\i yachzeru lameshale\u8217'ach} \u8212- it goes back to the sender; the other says {\i le\u8217'yorshei mi shenishtalach lo} \u8212- it goes to Ploni\u8217's heirs.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Holich Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The simple reading of this dispute would be that it maps directly onto the question of {\i holich kezachi}: if {\i holich} is {\i kezachi}, then Ploni acquired the money the moment the sender said {\i holich}, and when Ploni died, his heirs inherited it; if {\i holich} is not {\i kezachi}, then Ploni never acquired it, and it returns to the sender. But the {\i gemara} does not accept this simple mapping.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Memel Resolution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abba bar Memel resolves the apparent contradiction by asserting: {\i uvechuli alma holich lav kezachi} \u8212- in fact, {\i everyone} holds that {\i holich} is not {\i kezachi}. This is a striking reversal of what the {\i gemara} had seemed to assume throughout the earlier discussion. If that is so, what is the case where the money goes to Ploni\u8217's heirs? Abba bar Memel explains: {\i velo kashya \u8212- ha b\u8217'bari, ha b\u8217'shachiv mera} \u8212- the two {\i braitot} are not in conflict at all. The case where the money goes to the heirs is a case of {\i shachiv mera} \u8212- where the sender was on his deathbed when he gave the instruction. A deathbed bequest takes effect immediately upon utterance, so Ploni owned the money the moment the {\i shachiv mera} spoke. When Ploni subsequently died, his heirs inherited what was already his. The case where the money returns to the sender is an ordinary, healthy person \u8212- a {\i bari} \u8212- instructing delivery, where {\i holich} is not {\i kezachi} and Ploni never acquired it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Alive vs.\u160 ?Dead\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Zevid offers an alternative resolution that also preserves the principle that {\i holich lav kezachi}. He maintains that both {\i braitot} address cases of {\i shachiv mera}, and that the distinction lies in the timing of Ploni\u8217's death relative to the moment of the gift. If Ploni was alive at the time the {\i shachiv mera} gave the instruction \u8212- {\i de\u8217'isa lemakabel b\u8217'sha\u8217'as matnaso} \u8212- then the deathbed bequest took effect with Ploni as its beneficiary, and when Ploni died, his heirs receive it. If Ploni was already dead at the moment of the instruction \u8212- {\i de\u8217'laisa lemakabel b\u8217'sha\u8217'as matnaso} \u8212- then the bequest never had a valid recipient and returns to the sender\u8217's estate.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Pappa Approach\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Pappa offers a third resolution, one that introduces a distinct legal mechanism. He holds that both {\i braitot} address the case of a {\i bari} \u8212- a healthy sender. Since {\i holich lav kezachi}, Ploni does not acquire the money upon the instruction. But the {\i braita} that sends the money to Ploni\u8217's heirs is explained differently: what happened is that the sender {\i died} before the money was delivered. Once the sender dies, a special obligation arises \u8212- {\i mitzvah lekayeim divrei hameis} \u8212- there is a {\i mitzvah} upon the heirs to fulfill the expressed wishes of the deceased. This obligation impels delivery to Ploni\u8217's heirs because Ploni was still alive when the sender died and the obligation crystallized, even if Ploni subsequently died before delivery. The {\i braita} that returns the money to the sender reflects the scenario where Ploni died {\i before} the sender, meaning that when the sender died and {\i mitzvah lekayeim divrei hameis} came into force, there was no longer a living Ploni whose heirs could claim under it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is an important distinction from {\i shachiv mera}. A deathbed bequest automatically transfers title. {\i Mitzvah lekayeim divrei hameis} does not automatically transfer title; it imposes a moral and {\i halachic} obligation on the heirs to carry out what the deceased wanted. The practical results can be similar, but the mechanism differs. As one student raised: what happens in the {\i shachiv mera} case if Ploni dies before the {\i shachiv mera} himself dies? The {\i gemara}\u8217's formulation \u8212- {\i de\u8217'isa lemakabel b\u8217'sha\u8217'as matnaso} \u8212- indicates that the recipient must be alive at the moment the bequest is made for the transfer to take effect, and {\i Rashi} notes that the {\i shaliach} acquires on the recipient\u8217's behalf immediately, with the heirs then inheriting from that acquisition. This is a matter that merits further examination, and we leave it as an open question.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Holich Not Kizachi\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The cumulative effect of Abba bar Memel, Rav Zevid, and Rav Pappa\u8217's positions is to drain the {\i holich kezachi} formulation of its assumed force. Throughout the earlier discussion, we had been proceeding on the assumption that {\i holich} was {\i kezachi} \u8212- that the word \u8220"deliver\u8221" authorized the messenger to take possession on the recipient\u8217's behalf immediately. Now the {\i gemara} systematically explains every case in which the recipient would receive the money as owing to a special mechanism \u8212- either the automatic efficacy of {\i shachiv mera}, or the {\i mitzvah} to fulfill the wishes of someone who died \u8212- rather than to {\i holich} carrying inherent {\i kezachi} force. The {\i gemara} thus closes the discussion of this section with the position that in an ordinary case \u8212- a healthy person instructing a messenger to deliver money to a healthy recipient \u8212- {\i holich lav kezachi}, and the recipient acquires nothing until the money actually reaches him.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 We will pick up tomorrow with the {\i braita} that directly addresses this question, which will take us through to the end of the {\i perek}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Gittin 15\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Holech Status Review\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Debt vs Gift Distinction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Two important points require clarification from the previous shiur. The first concerns the distinction between the two {\i sugyot} on the {\i daf}. On {\i amud aleph}, the {\i gemara} assumed that {\i holech} is {\i k\u8217'zachi} \u8212- that when a messenger conveys money on behalf of the sender, the recipient effectively acquires it at the moment it is dispatched. The scenario there involved paying back a debt or returning a {\i pikadon}, and the question was whether the concept of {\i zachin} \u8212- acquiring on behalf of another \u8212- operated sufficiently to prevent the sender from retracting, even if not yet fully discharging his obligation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i sugya} now under discussion, however, deals with a case of a one-sided gift, and here the {\i gemara} pushes firmly in the opposite direction: {\i holech} is {\i lav k\u8217'zachi}. Unless the giver is a {\i shchiv mera} \u8212- someone on his deathbed \u8212- the messenger\u8217's conveyance does not immediately transfer ownership to the recipient, and if the recipient is found to have died, the money reverts to the sender.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafos on Debt/Gift\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This apparent tension \u8212- that {\i holech} is {\i k\u8217'zachi} in one {\i sugya} and {\i lav k\u8217'zachi} in the next \u8212- is addressed by {\i Tosafos} several {\i daf} earlier. {\i Tosafos} explains that the distinction tracks the nature of the transaction. When the sender owes money to the recipient, there is a stronger sense of legal and moral commitment to ensuring the money reaches its destination. Whether because psychologically the sender is more deeply invested in delivering what is owed, or because the legal framework treats money that is genuinely owed as already conceptually belonging to the other side, we interpret {\i holech} as {\i k\u8217'zachi} in the debt context. In the case of a pure gift, however, where the other side has no preexisting claim on the money, we interpret {\i holech} with more reservation, as {\i lav k\u8217'zachi}, and the money does not transfer until actually received.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shchiv Mera Conditions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The second point requiring clarification involves {\i shchiv mera} cases. The {\i sugya} assumes that even where the giver is a {\i shchiv mera} \u8212- which would ordinarily cause the gift to take effect \u8212- the recipient must have been alive at the moment the gift was given. The question this raises is pointed: the words of a {\i shchiv mera} take effect only at the moment of his death, not when he speaks them. So even if the recipient was alive when the gift was declared, if he died before the {\i shchiv mera} himself died, the gift should already be nullified, since it had not yet legally taken effect.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafos} on 14b raises this question explicitly. The {\i Ri} answers that what matters is the intention of the {\i shchiv mera} at the moment of giving: {\i dedaas nosen hu d\u8217'keivan sheyeshno l\u8217'mekabel b\u8217'shas masnos} \u8212- since the giver\u8217's intent was conditioned on the recipient\u8217's existence at the time of the gift declaration, the {\i shchiv mera} meant his bequest to reach either the recipient or his heirs: {\i sheyizke mekabel achar misaso o hu o yarshav}. The {\i Rosh}, however, leaves the question as a {\i tzarich iyun}, and the answer remains less than fully satisfying. The point is flagged not because it is resolved, but because it is a genuine difficulty in the {\i sugya} that the {\i rishonim} grapple with and that should not pass without acknowledgment.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Gift-Giving Tannaic Deba\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Baraita Setup\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now asks {\i leima holech k\u8217'zachi tanna\u8217'ei} \u8212- perhaps the question of whether {\i holech} is {\i k\u8217'zachi} is itself a {\i tannaic} debate. This is essentially a {\i chazarah} of the ground covered in the previous discussion, but applied now to the more contested domain of gift-giving.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} introduces a {\i baraita}: if a sender says {\i holech mane l\u8217'ploni} \u8212- convey this hundred dollars to so-and-so as a gift \u8212- and the messenger goes and cannot locate the recipient because he has died, the question becomes: what is done with the money?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Competing Positions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i baraita} presents multiple opinions. {\i Tanna kamma} says {\i yachzeru l\u8217'mishaleach} \u8212- return it to the sender. If the sender himself has since died, {\i Rabbi Natan} and {\i Rabbi Yaakov} say it goes to the heirs of the sender, which is functionally the same ruling: the recipient never acquired it. {\i Yesh omrim}, by contrast, says it goes to the heirs of the recipient \u8212- {\i l\u8217'yorshei mi shenishtalach lo} \u8212- which implies that the recipient had in fact acquired the money when it was dispatched.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Mapping these positions onto the {\i holech} question: {\i tanna kamma}, {\i Rabbi Natan}, and {\i Rabbi Yaakov} all hold {\i holech lav k\u8217'zachi} \u8212- the recipient does not acquire at the moment of dispatch \u8212- and so the money remains in the sender\u8217's domain and reverts accordingly. {\i Yesh omrim}, by saying it goes to the recipient\u8217's heirs, implicitly holds {\i holech k\u8217'zachi} \u8212- the recipient acquired the moment the messenger set off.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Yehuda HaNasi} then adds a further position, citing {\i Rabbi Yaakov} in the name of {\i Rabbi Meir}: {\i mitzvah l\u8217'kayem divrei hamet} \u8212- there is an obligation to fulfill the wishes of the deceased. Importantly, this formulation presupposes {\i holech lav k\u8217'zachi} \u8212- the other side does not automatically own the money \u8212- but once the sender has died, a separate principle creates a moral-legal obligation to honor what he wished. The outcome resembles that of {\i yesh omrim} but differs fundamentally in mechanism: it is not that the recipient owns the money by right, but that the fulfillment of the deceased\u8217's wishes creates an imperative to deliver it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafos} notes that {\i mitzvah l\u8217'kayem divrei hamet} does not apply universally whenever someone expresses a wish before dying. It applies particularly in situations like this, where money was already placed in the hands of an agent for a specific purpose and the person who designated it subsequently died. It would not apply, for instance, to money still sitting in the deceased\u8217's account with no specific instruction attached. Additionally, {\i mitzvah l\u8217'kayem} differs from {\i shchiv mera} in a further respect: {\i shchiv mera} operates automatically and takes legal effect at death; {\i mitzvah l\u8217'kayem} creates an obligation to act, but does not effect a legal transfer by itself.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Chachamim omrim yachloku} \u8212- the {\i chachamim} say to divide the money \u8212- reflecting their uncertainty about which rule governs. A further opinion, attributed to {\i Ben Batera}, says {\i kol mah sheyirtzeh shaliach ya\u8217'aseh} \u8212- let the messenger do as he sees fit.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The question of what {\i shuda} means in this context is itself debated. {\i Rashi} explains that {\i shuda d\u8217'dayni} means the {\i dayan} should exercise his best judgment \u8212- assessing what the sender would have wished, based on his knowledge of the relevant relationships and circumstances. {\i Rabbeinu Tam} in {\i Tosafos} objects that where there is genuinely nothing to go on, such an assessment is impossible. {\i Tosafos} therefore reads the phrase more broadly: {\i ela yehe hadayan l\u8217'mi sheyirtze} \u8212- the {\i dayan} may simply decide as he wishes, perhaps in favor of the needier party, or on some other equitable basis. This opens a genuinely interesting range of possibilities: does decision-making in a legal vacuum revert to the sender\u8217's intent, to policy, or to something essentially arbitrary?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One further structural question arises here. When the {\i shaliach} holds the money and we do not know whose domain it belongs to, there is no obvious {\i chezkat mamon} to fall back on in the usual sense. {\i Tosafos} notes that the general rule \u8212- {\i kol teiku shebaShas}, the {\i Gemara}\u8217's unresolved questions in monetary matters, is resolved by splitting \u8212- applies only where the {\i gemara} explicitly states {\i yachloku}. Otherwise, the default rule of {\i haMotzi mechavero alav hara\u8217'ayah} would apply. Here, since we genuinely cannot determine which party the {\i shaliach} is holding for, splitting is eminently sensible.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Precedent Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Shimon ben Nansi} then reports a practical precedent: {\i oy bi hayah ma\u8217'aseh} \u8212- I had a case just like this, and the ruling came down in favor of the heirs of the sender, consistent with the first position.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} asks whether this precedent case is being cited to indicate that {\i Rabbi Shimon ben Nansi} holds {\i holech lav k\u8217'zachi} \u8212- with the money reverting to the sender \u8212- or whether it simply constitutes a legal story without {\i halachic} weight. This opens a broader question: {\i Rabbi Shimon HaNasi} \u8212- is this figure a {\i nasi}, or is the attribution {\i Rabbi Shimon} who says it in the name of {\i the nasi}? {\i Tosafos} notes that there is no known {\i Rabbi Shimon HaNasi} in the tradition, making the attribution puzzling. The {\i gemara} leaves this unresolved: {\i teiku}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The deeper issue is whether all the positions in this {\i baraita} represent a genuine {\i tannaitic} debate about {\i holech k\u8217'zachi} versus {\i lav k\u8217'zachi}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Healthy Giver Rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} answers: {\i lo} \u8212- this is not a debate about {\i holech k\u8217'zachi}. {\i B\u8217'bari d\u8217'kulei alma lo peligi} \u8212- if the giver was healthy, all opinions agree: {\i holech lav k\u8217'zachi}, and the money reverts to the sender. The {\i gemara} resolves the apparent plurality of positions by relocating the entire debate to a different axis.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shchiv Mera Exception\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i baraita} is speaking throughout of a {\i shchiv mera} case \u8212- the evidence being that in the scenario, the sender dies. {\i Yesh omrim}, which said the recipient\u8217's heirs receive the money, was not asserting {\i holech k\u8217'zachi}; rather, it was applying the rule that the words of a {\i shchiv mera} take automatic legal effect. The positions that deny the recipient his money are not denying {\i holech k\u8217'zachi} either \u8212- they are denying that {\i divrei shchiv mera} are effective at all, essentially holding like {\i Rabbi Eliezer} in the dispute cited from elsewhere: that a {\i shchiv mera} must still perform a formal {\i kinyan} for his gift to take effect, and mere words are insufficient.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This resolution is frankly convoluted, but its purpose is clear: the {\i gemara} is deeply committed to avoiding any acknowledgment that a {\i tanna} holds {\i holech k\u8217'zachi} by a gift. The elaborateness of the resolution reflects how strongly the {\i gemara} resists that conclusion.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tanna kamma}, then, holds like {\i Rabbi Eliezer} \u8212- {\i holech lav k\u8217'zachi} and {\i shchiv mera} is ineffective without a formal {\i kinyan}. {\i Yesh omrim} holds like the {\i rabbanan} \u8212- {\i shchiv mera}\u8217's words are automatically effective. {\i Rabbi Yehuda HaNasi}, citing {\i Rabbi Meir}, holds like {\i Rabbi Eliezer} on the {\i shchiv mera} question, but introduces {\i mitzvah l\u8217'kayem divrei hamet} once the sender has died. {\i Chachamim} are uncertain. The position allowing the {\i shaliach} to decide represents a {\i safek} between splitting and {\i shuda d\u8217'dayni}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The practical bottom line is this: the {\i gemara}\u8217's determined effort to neutralize every reading that would support {\i holech k\u8217'zachi} by a gift strongly implies that the {\i halachic} consensus runs against it. By a {\i matanah}, {\i holech lav k\u8217'zachi}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Amoraic Refinements\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Yosef\u8217's Ruling\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} states: {\i gufa amar Rav Yosef halacha Rabbi Shimon HaNasi} \u8212- the {\i halacha} follows {\i Rabbi Shimon HaNasi}. {\i Rabbi Shimon HaNasi} ruled that the money reverts to the sender, which aligns with {\i holech lav k\u8217'zachi}. This is straightforward as far as a healthy giver is concerned.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Deceased Giver Obligation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The complication arises immediately: {\i v\u8217'ka\u8217'imalan divrei shchiv mera kechisuvin u\u8217'kmsurin damu} \u8212- we also hold that the words of a {\i shchiv mera} are treated as though written and delivered. If that is the case, how can we rule that the money reverts to the sender when the giver was a {\i shchiv mera}? {\i Rav Yosef} therefore interprets the ruling of {\i Rabbi Shimon HaNasi} as applying to a {\i bari} \u8212- a healthy person \u8212- not to a {\i shchiv mera}. This limits the ruling\u8217's scope but preserves its coherence.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Reconciling Principles\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A further tension remains: {\i v\u8217'ka\u8217'imalan mitzvah l\u8217'kayem divrei hamet} \u8212- we also hold that there is an obligation to fulfill the wishes of the deceased. So when {\i Rabbi Shimon HaNasi} rules that the money returns to the sender, does that mean even if the sender subsequently dies, his heirs may keep it and ignore his expressed wish to give the money away?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} resolves this by limiting the ruling: {\i tani yachziru l\u8217'meshaleach} \u8212- when {\i Rabbi Shimon HaNasi} says it returns to the sender, he means to the sender while alive. Once the sender dies, the principle of {\i mitzvah l\u8217'kayem divrei hamet} takes effect, and the money should be delivered to the recipient or the recipient\u8217's heirs. The {\i pesak} thus integrates both principles: {\i holech lav k\u8217'zachi} in the ownership sense, so that the money never automatically passes to the recipient; but {\i mitzvah l\u8217'kayem divrei hamet} applies once the sender dies, creating an imperative \u8212- though not a legal transfer \u8212- to honor his wishes.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This means that as long as the {\i shaliach} holds the money, it remains entirely within the {\i meshaleach}\u8217's legal domain. If the recipient dies, the money returns to the sender, for {\i holech lav k\u8217'zachi} and no ownership ever transferred. If the sender dies while the money is in transit, his heirs inherit it \u8212- but should, by dint of {\i mitzvah l\u8217'kayem divrei hamet}, deliver it to the recipient or the recipient\u8217's heirs. In neither case does ownership vest in the recipient prior to actual receipt. The broader conceptual richness of this {\i sugya} \u8212- touching on {\i zachin l\u8217'adam shelo b\u8217'fanav}, {\i divrei shchiv mera}, {\i mitzvah l\u8217'kayem divrei hamet}, and {\i holech k\u8217'zachi} \u8212- illustrates why {\i Gittin} has long been a tractate beloved in the {\i yeshivos} for precisely these kinds of expansive conceptual discussions.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 B\u8217'Fanai Nichtav/Nechtam\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava\u8217's Framework\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now turns to the {\i mishnah} governing the {\i shaliach} who brings a {\i get} from overseas. The {\i mishnah} requires the {\i shaliach} to declare {\i b\u8217'fanai nichtav u\u8217'v\u8217'fanai nechtam} \u8212- it was written in front of me and it was signed in front of me. This declaration is a {\i takkanah d\u8217'rabbanan} \u8212- a rabbinic enactment \u8212- and it is striking how much of the opening {\i prakim} of {\i Gittin} is devoted to this relatively secondary enactment. Nevertheless, it touches on core issues of {\i chasimat haget} and document validation, and the {\i gemara}\u8217's analysis is accordingly detailed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The foundational framework, following {\i Rava}, is that {\i b\u8217'fanai nichtav} is a special {\i takkanah} that is categorically distinct from normal {\i kiyum shtarot} \u8212- the ordinary process of validating a document by having witnesses recognize the signatures. Normal {\i kiyum shtarot} requires two witnesses. The {\i takkanah} here allows a single {\i shaliach} to validate the document, but it does so through a distinct mechanism: the {\i shaliach} demonstrates that he was personally present at both the writing and the signing, thereby signaling that this is not ordinary {\i kiyum shtarot} but a {\i takkanat chachamim}. The declaration of {\i b\u8217'fanai nichtav} is precisely what marks it as belonging to a different category.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Several variants of the declaration are listed in the {\i mishnah} as invalid. If the {\i shaliach} says {\i b\u8217'fanai nichtav aval lo b\u8217'fanai nechtam} \u8212- it was written in front of me but not signed \u8212- the {\i get} is {\i pasul}. Similarly, {\i b\u8217'fanai nechtam aval lo b\u8217'fanai nichtav} \u8212- signed in front of me but not written \u8212- is {\i pasul}. One might have thought the signing testimony alone should suffice, since it is the signatures that {\i kiyum shtarot} validates, but the {\i takkanah} requires both.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Full Attestation Require\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 More significantly, the {\i mishnah} invalidates partial testimony across the two requirements. Even if the {\i shaliach} says {\i b\u8217'fanai nichtav kulo u\u8217'v\u8217'fanai nechtam chetzyo} \u8212- it was all written in front of me, and I saw both signatures \u8212- the {\i get} is still {\i pasul} if only one signature was witnessed. Conversely, {\i b\u8217'fanai nichtav chetzyo u\u8217'v\u8217'fanai nechtam kulo} \u8212- where the {\i shaliach} witnessed the full signing but only part of the writing \u8212- is equally {\i pasul}. The {\i takkanah} must be executed in its entirety; any deviation renders the {\i get} invalid.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Half Writing Invalid\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The case of partial writing testimony \u8212- {\i b\u8217'fanai nichtav chetzyo} \u8212- might have seemed acceptable given that the purpose of mentioning the writing is simply to mark this as a distinct mode from normal {\i kiyum shtarot}. If half the writing was witnessed, one might argue that purpose is served. The {\i mishnah} rejects this: the {\i takkanah} must be performed completely.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Ashi Clarification\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} presses further: which half of the writing matters? {\i Rav Ashi} clarifies that if the {\i shaliach} witnessed only the final portion of the writing, the {\i get} remains {\i pasul}, since his testimony does not cover the beginning. The implicit rule established by {\i Rabbi Elazar} \u8212- that witnessing even a single line of writing at the outset suffices, since we assume the rest followed suit \u8212- explains why only the {\i first} half of the writing would have any potential validity. Witnessing the {\i last} half cannot retroactively validate what the {\i shaliach} did not see. The {\i mishnah}\u8217's ruling of {\i pasul} for {\i b\u8217'fanai nichtav chetzyo}, then, is understood to refer specifically to witnessing the later portion without the beginning.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} then introduces a case with two {\i shluchim}: one says {\i b\u8217'fanai nichtav} and the other says {\i b\u8217'fanai nechtam}. Despite having testimony covering both the writing and the signing, this too is {\i pasul}. The {\i chiddush} here is that the {\i shaliach} who declares {\i b\u8217'fanai nichtav} must himself also testify to the signing. The whole point of the declaration is that one person can do what normally requires two, precisely because he was present for the entirety of the process. Splitting the testimony between two people defeats the mechanism. {\i Rabbi Yehuda}, however, considers the {\i get} valid in such cases \u8212- and the {\i gemara} indicates that his leniency extends to the earlier cases as well, wherever the combination of testimonies covers writing and signing even if not by the same individual.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Finally, if two witnesses together say {\i b\u8217'fanenu nichtav} and a single separate individual says {\i b\u8217'fanai nechtam}, the {\i get} is {\i pasul}. But if one says {\i b\u8217'fanai nichtav} and two witnesses say {\i b\u8217'fanenu nechtam}, the {\i get} is valid \u8212- for the obvious reason that two witnesses testifying to the signatures constitutes ordinary {\i kiyum shtarot}, which is always available as an independent mode of validation.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Witness Testimony Modes\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Chisda Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rav Chisda} articulates a principle that runs through several cases on this {\i amud}: {\i o kulo b\u8217'kiyum haget o kulo b\u8217'takkanat chachamim} \u8212- one must either rely entirely on the ordinary process of {\i kiyum shtarot} or entirely on the {\i takkanat chachamim} of {\i b\u8217'fanai nichtav}. Mixing the two modes, even when the combination would seem to provide adequate validation, is {\i pasul}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The specific case {\i Rav Chisda} addresses is where the {\i shaliach} says {\i b\u8217'fanai nechtam} for the first signature \u8212- which works through the {\i takkanah} \u8212- and then two other witnesses from the {\i shuk} validate the second signature through normal {\i kiyum shtarot}. Naively, this seems stronger than using the {\i takkanah} alone. Yet {\i Rav Chisda} rules it {\i pasul}: one must choose one mode and apply it throughout. There is no compelling {\i sevara} that explains why mixing modes is categorically prohibited, but {\i Rav Chisda}\u8217's position is that the {\i takkanah} is designed as a self-contained mechanism, and one does not blend it with regular document validation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava\u8217's Three-Quarter Iss\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rava} challenges {\i Rav Chisda}: is there really a rule that having {\i more} witnesses makes a document {\i less} valid? If the {\i shaliach} alone could say {\i b\u8217'fanai nechtam} on both signatures and achieve {\i kashrus}, how can adding two fully qualified witnesses for the second signature create {\i pesul}? {\i Mi ika midi d\u8217'ilu amar chad kasher v\u8217'chi amri terei pasul} \u8212- is there truly a scenario where one witness suffices but two invalidate?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rava} therefore reframes the {\i chiddush}. The problematic case, in his view, is where the {\i shaliach} himself \u8212- together with one other witness from the {\i shuk} \u8212- validates the second signature. Here {\i Rava} provides an actual {\i sevara} for the {\i pesul}: {\i atei l\u8217'achlufei b\u8217'kiyum shtarot d\u8217'alma} \u8212- this arrangement creates a dangerous resemblance to an invalid mode of regular {\i kiyum shtarot}. In ordinary {\i kiyum shtarot}, it is impermissible for one witness to contribute disproportionately to the validation of a document. If the same person validates one full signature on his own and then provides half the testimony for the second signature, three-quarters of the document\u8217's validation rests on a single witness \u8212- {\i v\u8217'kanafik nechi riv\u8217'a d\u8217'mamona apuma d\u8217'chad sahada}. That is structurally defective by the rules of {\i kiyum shtarot}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 To illustrate: if a document bears two signatures and the {\i shaliach} validates the first entirely by saying {\i b\u8217'fanai nechtam}, and then the {\i shaliach} and {\i Reuven} together validate the second through signature recognition, then one witness has validated half the document outright and half of the remaining half \u8212- three-quarters in total. This is exactly the sort of lopsided validation that {\i kiyum shtarot} prohibits. And because this arrangement so closely resembles that prohibited structure, one who witnesses it might mistakenly conclude that such testimony is acceptable in ordinary {\i kiyum shtarot} as well.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rav Ashi} raises a pointed challenge against {\i Rava}: in the case just described, had the {\i shaliach} been present for the signing of the second signature, he could have validated the entire document himself simply by saying {\i b\u8217'fanai nechtam} on both. If one person can validate the whole document through the {\i takkanah}, why should validating three-quarters of it be problematic? {\i Rav Ashi} argues that {\i Rava}\u8217's concern about the appearance of three-quarters validation does not translate cleanly, since the baseline capacity here is full single-person validation. {\i Rava}, however, maintains his position on the grounds of {\i atei l\u8217'achlufei} \u8212- the concern is not theoretical capacity but practical confusion with the rules of {\i kiyum shtarot}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Ashi Solution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rav Ashi} offers his own formulation of the {\i pesul} case. He agrees that the cases {\i Rav Chisda} and {\i Rava} addressed are not {\i pasul}. But a case that {\i is} invalid, according to {\i Rav Ashi}, is where the {\i shaliach} says {\i b\u8217'fanai nechtam} for the first signature \u8212- relying on the {\i takkanah} \u8212- and then testifies to his own signature for the second, relying on the standard rule that a signatory may always authenticate his own signature. The result is that the {\i get} is validated partly through {\i takkanat chachamim} and partly through the ordinary mode of self-authentication \u8212- two different validation mechanisms, each legitimate on its own, but improperly combined. As {\i Rav Ashi} puts it, echoing {\i Rav Chisda}\u8217's principle: {\i o kulo b\u8217'kiyum haget o kulo b\u8217'takkanat chachamim} \u8212- choose one mode and stay in it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The three {\i amoraic} positions thus present a spectrum. {\i Rav Chisda} holds that even completely valid {\i kiyum shtarot} for the second signature cannot be combined with the {\i takkanah} for the first \u8212- pick one box entirely. {\i Rava} holds that two strangers validating the second signature is fine, but the {\i shaliach} himself contributing to validating the second creates a problematic resemblance to defective {\i kiyum shtarot}. {\i Rav Ashi} holds that even the two-strangers case and the {\i shaliach}-plus-one case are both fine, but the {\i shaliach} who mixes {\i b\u8217'fanai nechtam} with self-authentication of his own signature cannot combine the two.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Mishna Hermeneutics\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Implicit vs Explicit\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having laid out the positions of {\i Rav Chisda}, {\i Rava}, and {\i Rav Ashi}, the {\i gemara} raises a methodological challenge. If the {\i mishnah} \u8212- by stating {\i b\u8217'fanai nechtam chetzyo pasul} \u8212- is meant to convey a genuine {\i chiddush} about partial signing testimony being invalid, the case it implies must be one where {\i something} was done to validate the second signature, not merely nothing. After all, if no attestation whatsoever was provided for the second signature, the {\i pesul} would be entirely trivial. Any meaningful reading of the {\i mishnah} requires us to assume that some attempt at validation was made for the second signature \u8212- and that it still failed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But if so, the {\i mishnah} must be alluding to one of the specific cases debated by the {\i amoraim} \u8212- either {\i Rav Chisda}\u8217's case, {\i Rava}\u8217's, or {\i Rav Ashi}\u8217's. The {\i gemara} suggests that the {\i mishnah} is therefore: {\i o k\u8217'Rava o k\u8217'Rav Ashi} \u8212- oriented toward one of those latter two positions. And this implies: {\i ule\u8217'apuki miderav Chisda} \u8212- to exclude {\i Rav Chisda}\u8217's ruling. The argument is that if {\i Rav Chisda}\u8217's case \u8212- where two complete, independent, qualified witnesses validate the second signature \u8212- were the {\i chiddush} of the {\i mishnah}, the {\i mishnah} would have stated it explicitly. Such a dramatic {\i chiddush} \u8212- that more witnesses can make things worse \u8212- would not be left as a mere implication of oblique phrasing.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Chisda Defense\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rav Chisda} responds with a hermeneutical counter-principle. The {\i gemara} itself is employing the assumption that every case in the {\i mishnah} must convey a new {\i chiddush}. But this is not always the case. {\i Rav Chisda} points to the {\i mishnah}\u8217's earlier line \u8212- {\i b\u8217'fanai nichtav aval lo b\u8217'fanai nechtam} \u8212- which states that if only the writing but not the signing was declared, the {\i get} is {\i pasul}. That is obviously {\i pasul}. There is no {\i chiddush} there at all. Yet the {\i mishnah} lists it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} follows the rhetorical pattern of {\i lo zu af zu} \u8212- not only this, but also that. It lists cases in order, not because each individual case is a {\i chiddush}, but to build systematically through a range of scenarios, some obvious and some not. Accordingly, the {\i mishnah}\u8217's mention of partial signing testimony being invalid does not necessarily imply that the second signature was validated by some additional means. It may simply be listing an obvious scenario as part of a larger progressive enumeration. {\i Rav Chisda}\u8217's ruling \u8212- that even full, two-witness {\i kiyum} for the second signature is {\i pasul} when combined with the {\i takkanah} for the first \u8212- is therefore not excluded by the {\i mishnah}\u8217's silence. The {\i mishnah} simply was not making a point of that specific case; it was following the {\i lo zu af zu} structure.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is a characteristically striking exchange. Usually when the {\i gemara} argues that a {\i mishnah} does not make a {\i chiddush}, the purpose is to {\i deny} that something special is being communicated. Here, paradoxically, {\i Rav Chisda} uses the same argument to {\i defend} the largest {\i chiddush} of the three \u8212- by maintaining that the {\i mishnah} need not communicate it at all.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Gidud/Mechitza Combinati\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Chisda Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now applies the {\i o kulo\u8230 ? o kulo} \u8212- all-or-nothing \u8212- principle to an analogous case in the laws of {\i Shabbos}. {\i Rav Chisda} states: {\i gidud chamisha u\u8217'mechitza chamisha ein mitztarfin} \u8212- a ditch five {\i tefachim} deep combined with a fence five {\i tefachim} high does not constitute a {\i reshut hayachid}. To qualify as a {\i reshut hayachid}, an enclosure needs walls or barriers of ten {\i tefachim}. One might think that a five-{\i tefach} ditch and a five-{\i tefach} wall placed on its edge could combine \u8212- after all, from the inside of the ditch, the effective barrier height is ten {\i tefachim}. {\i Rav Chisda} says no: either the full ten {\i tefachim} must come from the ditch alone, or from the wall alone. Combining two different physical mechanisms, even when the arithmetic works out, does not suffice. {\i Ad d\u8217'ika o kulo b\u8217'mechitza o kulo b\u8217'gidud.}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mareimar & Halacha\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Mareimar} disagrees: {\i gidud chamisha u\u8217'mechitza chamisha mitztarfin} \u8212- they do combine. And the {\i halacha} is ruled in accordance with {\i Mareimar}: {\i v\u8217'hilchata mitztarfin}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafos Distinction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafos} raises an interesting point here. {\i Tosafos} considers the case as {\i Rav Chisda} framed it \u8212- ditch plus fence on its edge \u8212- to be obvious: from inside the ditch, the entire surrounding barrier is ten {\i tefachim}, and of course this constitutes a {\i reshut hayachid}. For {\i Tosafos}, the less obvious case, and thus the one actually under discussion, involves a different configuration: a mound that is five {\i tefachim} high, with a five-{\i tefach} wall built atop it. In that scenario, someone standing at ground level outside the mound sees only a five-{\i tefach} wall; someone inside, atop the mound, also faces only a five-{\i tefach} drop on the outside. The combined barrier of ten {\i tefachim} is not experienced uniformly from any single vantage point, which is what makes the case genuinely debatable. Either way, the {\i halacha} is that they combine.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Lashon Bnei Adam\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Lav Yaseira\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} presents a case involving vow language. {\i Konam she\u8217'ani to\u8217'em} \u8212- may what I taste be forbidden like a {\i korban} \u8212- is a straightforward prohibition against tasting. But the {\i mishnah} then addresses {\i konam she\u8217'ani lo to\u8217'em} \u8212- {\i konam} that I do not taste \u8212- and rules that this formulation is {\i equally} a prohibition against tasting. This is surprising, since {\i she\u8217'ani lo to\u8217'em} would grammatically mean \u8220"if I do not taste,\u8221" which should constitute a vow to taste rather than a vow not to.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} explains: {\i lashon bnei adam} \u8212- we follow colloquial speech. People commonly insert an extra {\i lo} in their speech without intending a logical negation. Someone who says {\i she\u8217'ani lo to\u8217'em} colloquially means exactly the same thing as someone who says {\i she\u8217'ani to\u8217'em} \u8212- they are not eating. The additional {\i lo} is a {\i lav yaseira}, a superfluous negative, a speech pattern of ordinary people that does not reverse the meaning. Both formulations therefore express the same prohibition.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Nedarim vs Shavuot\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This raises a natural question: why should {\i nedarim} be treated differently from {\i shavuot}? In {\i shavuot}, precision of language is essential. {\i Shavuah she\u8217'ochal} \u8212- an oath that I will eat \u8212- obligates eating. {\i Shavuah she\u8217'lo ochal} \u8212- an oath that I will not eat \u8212- prohibits eating. The {\i lo} makes all the difference; its logical force is fully operative.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} explains that {\i nedarim} and {\i shavuot} function on different linguistic bases. {\i Nedarim} follow {\i lashon bnei adam} \u8212- the way ordinary people actually speak. Since ordinary people speak loosely and sometimes insert extra negatives without intending to reverse meaning, the laws of {\i nedarim} accommodate that colloquial reality. {\i Shavuot}, by contrast, are governed by strict legal precision of language; the common speech patterns of {\i lashon bnei adam} do not override the logical content of the words used. The same phrase therefore produces opposite results depending on whether it appears in the domain of {\i nedarim} or {\i shavuot}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Gittin 16\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Half-Pure Hands\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Half-Pure Hands Query\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The daf opens with a query posed by Ilfa: {\i yadayim tehorot lachata\u8217'in o ein tehorot lachata\u8217'in} \u8212- can hands be half {\i tahor}, or is that simply not a meaningful category? The question is, on its face, analogous to asking whether someone can be a little bit pregnant. To understand what the question is actually asking, the Gemara walks through a series of candidate cases, systematically eliminating each one before arriving at the genuine {\i sha\u8217'alah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Eliminating Cases\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara begins by asking {\i heichi dami} \u8212- what kind of case could this be? It first proposes that perhaps the question concerns two people washing from a single {\i kli} containing one {\i reviit}. But this is immediately dismissed: we have already learned explicitly ({\i umatnan}) that a {\i reviit} suffices for the {\i netilat yadayim} of one person, or even two. There is nothing unresolved there.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A second possibility is raised: perhaps he washes one hand at a time, sequentially. Again, this is set aside, because we have likewise already learned that if one hand is washed with {\i netila} and the other with {\i shtifa} \u8212- that is, dunking it in a body of water rather than having water poured from a vessel \u8212- both hands are nonetheless {\i tehorot}. Certainly if both hands are washed with proper {\i netila}, sequential washing would be fine. And a third possibility \u8212- washing half the hand at a time, in stages, with full drying in between \u8212- is dismissed by the statement of the school of Rabbi Yannai: {\i yadayim ein tehorot lachata\u8217'in}, hands simply cannot be purified in halves.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Moisture Remaining Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara therefore narrows the question: {\i lo tzericha d\u8217'ika mashkeh tofeach} \u8212- the case requires that there be residual moisture on the first portion of the hand when the second portion is washed. In other words, the person washed the hand in stages but did not fully dry the first section before washing the second. The moistness of the first section remains.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might ask why that would change anything. After all, we have learned that {\i nitzok}, {\i katrafes}, and mere {\i mashkeh tofeach} are not considered {\i chibur} \u8212- connection \u8212- neither for {\i tuma} nor for {\i tahara}. A thin stream of liquid connecting two vessels, for instance, does not allow {\i tuma} to travel from one to the other, and similarly does not confer {\i tahara} upon the {\i tamei} one. So the residual moisture ought to be irrelevant.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But the Gemara refines the case further: the question is specifically where the moisture remaining on the first portion is {\i tofeach lehatfiach} \u8212- wet enough that if placed against another surface, it would render that surface wet as well. This is a higher threshold than mere dampness. And indeed, regarding {\i netilat yadayim}, we do have a tradition that {\i tofeach lehatfiach} constitutes {\i chibur}. So this particular category remains genuinely unresolved.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chibur & Mikvah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then raises another possible dismissal: perhaps the {\i chibur} principle of {\i tofeach lehatfiach} is known only in the context of a {\i mikvah}, not for {\i netilat yadayim}. If that is so, there is no established ruling for the hand-washing case at all, and the question stands. This framing \u8212- that the {\i chibur} idea may belong exclusively to {\i mikvah} discussions \u8212- sets up the next step.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Yehuda Chibur\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mikvah} source for {\i chibur} is introduced from a Mishnah: if a {\i mikvah} contains exactly forty {\i seah} \u8212- precisely the minimum required \u8212- and two people immerse, one after the other, some water will cling to the first person and leave the {\i mikvah} slightly deficient for the second. The {\i tanna kamma} rules that the first is {\i tahor} but the second is {\i tamei}. Rabbi Yehuda, however, rules that if the first person\u8217's feet are still touching the water \u8212- still in contact with the {\i mikvah} as he exits \u8212- then the water on his body is considered connected to the {\i mikvah} through {\i chibur}, and the second person\u8217's immersion is also valid.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The relevance to the {\i yadayim} question is this: the {\i chibur} principle in the hand-washing case may simply be an extension of Rabbi Yehuda\u8217's position regarding {\i mikvah}. If so, it is Rabbi Yehuda\u8217's view, not a universally accepted principle.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Unresolved Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The question is ultimately left unresolved. What we can say is that the answer likely follows party lines: Rabbi Yehuda, consistent with his {\i mikvah} position, would likely say the partially-washed hand is {\i tahor}, while the {\i tanna kamma} would not. As a practical note, the proper custom is to ensure a full {\i reviit} covers each hand completely \u8212- not to divide the water \u8212- precisely to avoid any such ambiguity, though the underlying legal question remains without a definitive answer in the Gemara.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Drawn Water Decrees\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Head in Drawn Water\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yirmeyah introduces two related rulings. First: one who immerses {\i rosho ve-rubo} \u8212- head and majority of the body \u8212- in {\i mayim she\u8217'uvim}, drawn water, is {\i tamei}. Second: a {\i tahor} person upon whom three {\i lugin} of drawn water are poured is likewise {\i tamei}. The question immediately arises: why should this be? In the first case, the person may have already been {\i tahor} \u8212- either they had just immersed in a valid {\i mikvah} or had never become {\i tamei} in the first place. Why would immersing in drawn water undo that?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rashi\u8217's Explanation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rashi explains that such a person is {\i tamei l\u8217'terumah} \u8212- considered {\i tamei} with respect to {\i terumah} \u8212- until he immerses again in a valid {\i mikvah}. Rashi further notes that this is one of the eighteen things decreed during the famous confrontation between the disciples of Beit Shammai and the disciples of Beit Hillel, recorded at the beginning of Masechet Shabbat. These eighteen decrees were exceptional in that, notwithstanding the general principle that one does not issue a {\i gezeirah} upon the public unless the majority can uphold it ({\i ein gozerin gezeirah al ha-tzibur ela im ken rov tzibur yecholin la\u8217'amod bah}), these decrees were imposed because of the extraordinary intensity of feeling behind them at the time.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Filthy Mikvah Caves\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara explains the historical circumstance that gave rise to this {\i gezeirah}: {\i she-hayah lahem mei me\u8217'arot sruchim l\u8217'tevilah} \u8212- they used to immerse in the water of caves, which was foul and turgid. One can imagine the conditions: cave water, depending on what resided in or passed through those caves, could be thoroughly unpleasant. After immersing in such a {\i mikvah}, people would naturally cleanse themselves with drawn water \u8212- in effect, bathing after the immersion.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Bathing Prohibition\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This created a serious perceptual problem. Bystanders seeing this sequence of events might conclude that it was not the {\i mikvah} immersion but the subsequent bathing that effected {\i tahara} \u8212- {\i atu elu meta\u8217'arin elu meta\u8217'arin}, \u8220"is it these that purify, or these?\u8221" The concern was that people would simply skip the {\i mikvah} and rely on the drawn water alone. To prevent this, the Rabbis decreed that immersing in drawn water, or bathing with drawn water after a {\i mikvah}, renders one {\i tamei}, thereby preserving the {\i mikvah}\u8217's exclusive role as the vehicle of {\i tahara}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 As a practical aside, this decree has contemporary relevance primarily in the context of ascending the Temple Mount or for a convert undergoing {\i tevilah} for conversion. For the purpose of marital {\i tahara}, where the {\i mikvah} is used for {\i niddah} and {\i zavah} restrictions rather than classic {\i tumah ve-tahara}, the question of a subsequent shower does not affect the woman\u8217's status vis-\u224 ?-vis her husband. Rashi\u8217's formulation \u8212- {\i tamei l\u8217'terumah} \u8212- reflects that this {\i tuma} has practical significance only for {\i terumah} and {\i kodshim}, neither of which applies today outside of the Temple Mount context.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Three Lugin Extension\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The same {\i gezeirah} was extended to the case where three {\i lugin} of drawn water are poured over the head and majority of a person\u8217's body. The Gemara states: {\i de-ha u-me-ha chada hilcheta hi} \u8212- these two cases are governed by the same legal principle. Whether one enters a pool of drawn water or has it poured upon them, the rabbinic consequence is identical.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Half Immersion Half Pour\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rami bar Abba poses a question about a hybrid case: {\i chatzyo be-shfichah ve-chatzyo be-vi\u8217'ah} \u8212- what if half was from immersion and half was from pouring? Does the {\i gezeirah} apply to a combination of the two modalities, each of which alone constitutes only half the triggering condition? The Gemara concludes {\i teiku} \u8212- the question stands unresolved.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Takanat Ezra\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Nine Kabin Remedy\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Papa then introduces an analogous ruling: {\i ba\u8217'al keri choleh she-natnu alav tish\u8217'ah kabin mayim tahor} \u8212- a sick person who has had a seminal emission upon whom nine {\i kabin} of water are poured is rendered {\i tahor} in the relevant sense. This ruling is ancient and well established.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ezra\u8217's Decree\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Stepping back to the broader context: Ezra enacted a {\i takkanah} requiring a {\i ba\u8217'al keri} to immerse in a {\i mikvah} before engaging in Torah study or prayer, as Rashi notes \u8212- {\i Ezra tiken tevilah le-ba\u8217'alei kerin k\u8217'dei shelo yehoyu k\u8217'tarnegalim}, so that they would not approach Torah with the casual, thoughtless attitude of roosters. The goal was to ensure that Torah study is approached with gravity and intentionality.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sick Exception\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The exception allows a {\i ba\u8217'al keri} who is ill and unable to go to the {\i mikvah} to instead have nine {\i kabin} of water poured over him \u8212- even drawn water \u8212- and thereby fulfill the requirement sufficiently. The practical effect is essentially a shower. This {\i heter} applies only to the sick; a healthy {\i ba\u8217'al keri} would still be required to immerse in a proper {\i mikvah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Modern Application\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i takkanah} of Ezra is generally not observed today. The underlying rationale was to ensure that Torah study is approached with the proper seriousness and composure that follows {\i tevilah}, as opposed to the levity that may have preceded the {\i keri}. The sad commentary embedded in this abandonment is that, since we do not in general approach Torah with the sustained gravity that would make the contrast meaningful, the {\i gezeirah} no longer has its intended effect and has effectively lapsed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ritva Clarification\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Ritva adds an important clarification: the nine {\i kabin} do not actually purify the {\i ba\u8217'al keri} in the full {\i halachic} sense of {\i tahara}. Rather, they remove specifically the prohibition enacted by Ezra against Torah study and prayer. For purposes requiring actual {\i tahara} \u8212- such as going to the {\i Beit Ha-Mikdash} or its modern analog, ascending the Temple Mount \u8212- a full {\i mikvah} immersion remains necessary.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Temple Mount Immersion\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This point has direct practical implications. The only contexts today in which {\i mikvah} immersion for men carries concrete {\i halachic} weight are, first, ascending the Temple Mount, and second, conversion immersion for a convert. The nine {\i kabin} option of Ezra\u8217's {\i takkanah} would not suffice for these purposes, since the Ritva\u8217's distinction between removing a rabbinic prohibition and achieving actual {\i tahara} is operative there. For men who customarily immerse before {\i chag}, there is a view that {\i b\u8217'diavad} a shower might suffice based on this {\i takkanah}, though this remains a matter requiring consultation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Papa Extension\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Papa extends the same logical question that Rami bar Abba asked about drawn water: {\i chetzyo be-tvila ve-chetzyo be-netina mah} \u8212- what if half the required quantity came from immersion and half from pouring, in the context of the nine {\i kabin} for a sick {\i ba\u8217'al keri}? Just as before, the Gemara answers {\i teiku} \u8212- the question is left without resolution.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Get Witnesses\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishnah Setup\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara returns to the {\i Mishnah} governing a {\i get} brought from outside of Israel ({\i medinat ha-yam}). The {\i takkanah} requires the messenger to declare {\i bifanai nictav uv\u8217'fanai nechtam} \u8212- \u8220"it was written in my presence and sealed in my presence\u8221" \u8212- a single person attesting to both the writing and the sealing. The Mishnah presents several cases involving multiple witnesses, each attesting to only one part of this declaration, and asks whether the {\i get} is valid.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Three Cases\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Mishnah presents three configurations. In the first, one witness says {\i bifanai nictav} \u8212- it was written in my presence \u8212- and a different witness says {\i bifanai nechtam} \u8212- it was sealed in my presence. The {\i get} is {\i pasul}, invalid. In the second case, two witnesses attest to the writing and one attests to the sealing. The {\i tanna kamma} rules this {\i pasul}, while Rabbi Yehuda rules it {\i kasher}. In the third case, one attests to the writing and two attest to the sealing, and all opinions agree the {\i get} is {\i kasher}. These three cases form the framework for the ensuing analysis.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Get Under Both Hands\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Shmuel bar Yehuda reports in the name of Rabbi Yochanan: {\i lo shanu ela she-ein ha-get yotzei mitachat yedei shneihem} \u8212- the Mishnah\u8217's ruling of {\i pasul} in the first case applies only where the {\i get} does not exit from under the hands of both witnesses, meaning the two individuals were not both appointed as {\i shlichim}, messengers. One person is the primary carrier; the other merely happened to be present and can attest to only part of the required declaration. Rashi explains: {\i mitachat yadei she-shneihem adukim bo u-shneihem shlichim} \u8212- if both are holding the {\i get} and both are designated as messengers, the situation is different.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Foreign Get Takana\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i takkanah} for a {\i get} brought from outside Israel was specifically designed to require that one person testify to both the writing and the sealing together. The reason, as we shall explore, relates to either the {\i lishma} concern or the {\i edim metzuyim} concern \u8212- the two competing rationales that will be debated. The key structural point here is that the {\i takkanah} was crafted around a single person bearing full testimony, not a composite of partial attestations from multiple individuals.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two Messengers\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If, however, both witnesses are formally appointed as messengers \u8212- {\i shneyhem shlichim}, both designated to carry and deliver the {\i get} \u8212- then the {\i get} is valid even according to the Mishnah\u8217's first case framing. {\i Amar kasavar shnayim she-heviu get mimedinas hayam ein tzrichim she-yomru bifanai nechtav uv\u8217'fanai nechtam} \u8212- when two people bring the {\i get} together as co-messengers, the {\i takkanah} requiring the combined declaration does not apply to them in the same way. The logic of why this should be the case, and how far the principle extends, is the subject of the dispute between Rava and Rabbah.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rava\u8217's Edim Principle\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava-Rabbah Dispute\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara identifies two competing rationales for the {\i takkanah} requiring a messenger to declare {\i bifanai nictav uv\u8217'fanai nechtam}: the position of Rabbah and the position of Rava. This dispute, first introduced at the beginning of the tractate, is now directly applied to the Mishnah\u8217's cases. Rabbah holds that the {\i takkanah} was instituted because people outside of Israel were not expert in the requirement that a {\i get} be written {\i lishma} \u8212- for the specific purpose of the woman\u8217's divorce. Rava holds that the concern was {\i edim metzuyim l\u8217'kaymo} \u8212- the absence of available witnesses to authenticate the document through its signatures, since people in one country are not familiar with the signatures of witnesses from another.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Edim Metzuyim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava\u8217's position \u8212- {\i lefi she-ein edim metzuyim l\u8217'kaymo} \u8212- generates a specific analytical framework. If the underlying concern is the availability of witnesses to establish the {\i get}\u8217's authenticity, then when two people bring the {\i get} together as co-messengers, there are in fact two witnesses present and available. The {\i edim metzuyim} problem is solved. This explains why, under Rava\u8217's reasoning, two co-messengers need not make the declaration: they themselves constitute the available witnesses.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Abaye\u8217's Challenge\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye challenges Rava by directing attention to the second case in the Mishnah: {\i shnayim omrim bifanaynu nictav ve-echad omer bifanai nechtam} \u8212- two say it was written in their presence, one says it was sealed in his presence. Under this configuration, you have three witnesses total, and certainly {\i edim metzuyim} are present. Yet the {\i tanna kamma} still rules {\i pasul}. How does Rava account for this? If {\i edim metzuyim l\u8217'kaymo} is the operative concern and that concern is satisfied here, why is the {\i get} still invalid?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Confusion Concern\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava resolves Abaye\u8217's challenge by clarifying the nature of the {\i takkanah}: the concern is not simply whether you have a sufficient number of witnesses in the abstract, but whether the particular configuration will lead to confusion with the authentication of regular documents ({\i kiyum shetarot d\u8217'alma}). Regular documents require two witnesses who together attest to the document\u8217's authenticity. If we permit this split-testimony arrangement for a {\i get} \u8212- where one group attests to writing and a different individual attests to sealing \u8212- people will begin to confuse that with the ordinary rules for authenticating {\i shetarot}, thinking perhaps that one witness can establish a document in general contexts. The {\i gezeirah} extends to prevent that confusion even where multiple people are present.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Appointed vs.\u160 ?Present\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The critical distinction in Rava\u8217's framework is between witnesses who are formally appointed as {\i shlichim} \u8212- designated messengers \u8212- and witnesses who are merely present and happen to be able to testify to one element or another. When both individuals are appointed as co-messengers, the {\i get} exits from under both their hands; they collectively bear the responsibility and function of the {\i shliach}, and the {\i takkanah} does not require them to make the composite declaration. But where they are not both formally appointed, even if their combined testimony covers all the required elements, the configuration is structurally flawed: no single person is standing fully behind the document, and the {\i gezeirah} against confusion with regular {\i kiyum shetarot} applies.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rabbah Lishma\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Lishma Common Ground\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbah advances a different framework: {\i d\u8217'chulei alma ba\u8217'inan lishma} \u8212- both the {\i tanna kamma} and Rabbi Yehuda agree that the {\i takkanah} was instituted because of the {\i lishma} concern, that people were not expert in the requirement that a {\i get} be written specifically for the sake of this woman\u8217's divorce. This is Rabbah\u8217's operating principle, shared across all positions in the Mishnah.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gezeirah Lest Revert\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Given that shared premise, Rabbah explains the disagreement: {\i v\u8217'hacha b\u8217'mai askinan le\u8217'achar she-lamdu u-gezeirah shema yachzor davar l\u8217'kilkulo} \u8212- the debate concerns a later stage, after knowledge of the {\i lishma} requirement has spread, and specifically whether we issue a {\i gezeirah} lest things revert ({\i davar ha-chozer l\u8217'kilkulo}) to the original state of ignorance. Originally, the {\i takkanah} was enacted because people did not know the {\i get} had to be written {\i lishma}. Eventually, this became widely understood. The question is whether, even after the principle became common knowledge, we maintain the original safeguards to prevent a future regression.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Yehuda Lenient\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yehuda is lenient: since everyone already understands the {\i lishma} requirement, we need not worry that the situation will revert. The knowledge is sufficiently embedded that allowing some flexibility in how the testimony is arranged poses no risk of causing people to forget or disregard {\i lishma}. Rabbi Yehuda therefore permits the second case \u8212- two attesting to the writing and one to the sealing \u8212- because the concern that originally gave rise to the {\i takkanah} is no longer pressing.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tanna Kamma Stringent\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i tanna kamma}, by contrast, is {\i gazrinan} \u8212- we do issue the {\i gezeirah} against reversion. Even though everyone currently knows about {\i lishma}, the {\i tanna kamma} fears that if we relax the requirement, the structure that enforces awareness of {\i lishma} will erode, and future generations will lose sight of it. The {\i tanna kamma} therefore insists that the declaration of {\i bifanai nictav uv\u8217'fanai nechtam} by a single person must be maintained in all configurations, precisely because that unified declaration is the mechanism that keeps the {\i lishma} concern visible and enforced.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Baraita Resolution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Baraita Cases\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Ulla\u8217's teaching is introduced in response to a logical pressure: if Rabbi Yehuda is not worried about {\i davar ha-chozer l\u8217'kilkulo}, he should be lenient not only in the second case but in the first case as well. {\i Ve-liflog nami Rabbi Yehuda b\u8217'reisha} \u8212- why does Rabbi Yehuda not disagree in the first case too? If the {\i lishma} concern is no longer operative, even one person saying {\i bifanai nictav} and another saying {\i bifanai nechtam} should be acceptable under Rabbi Yehuda\u8217's logic.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ulla Interpretation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Ulla resolves this: {\i chaluk hayah Rabbi Yehuda af b\u8217'rishona} \u8212- Rabbi Yehuda in fact does disagree in the first case as well. The Mishnah\u8217's formulation, which seems to show Rabbi Yehuda disagreeing only in the second case, is to be read as recording his leniency in that specific configuration while leaving his first-case position implicit. But this is complicated by a {\i Baraita} that was taught to Ulla by Rav Oshaya: {\i Rabbi Yehuda machshir b\u8217'zu ve-lo b\u8217'acheret} \u8212- Rabbi Yehuda validates {\i this} case and not {\i that} one. This {\i Baraita} explicitly limits Rabbi Yehuda\u8217's leniency, stating that there is one case he permits and another he does not.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 One Sealing Only\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The question then becomes: what is the case that Rabbi Yehuda does {\i not} permit? If he disagrees in the first Mishnah case (one attests to writing, one to sealing) and permits the second (two attest to writing, one to sealing), then what is the \u8220"other\u8221" case the {\i Baraita} refers to that he rejects? The Gemara concludes that the missing case \u8212- not discussed in the Mishnah at all \u8212- is where a single witness says only {\i bifanai nechtam}, attesting solely to the sealing without any attestation to the writing. This configuration: zero witnesses to writing, one to sealing, is universally {\i pasul}. The {\i Baraita} is informing us that this is the case Rabbi Yehuda excludes, not one of the Mishnah\u8217's own cases.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Salka Daatach\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara explains why we might have thought otherwise: {\i salka daatach amina} \u8212- one might have assumed that since Rabbi Yehuda is not worried about {\i davar ha-chozer l\u8217'kilkulo}, he would extend his leniency all the way, validating even a case where only one person attests to only one element. After all, if the {\i lishma} concern is no longer pressing, and if we are not going to confuse this with regular {\i kiyum shetarot} \u8212- why not be maximally lenient?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Yehuda Limits\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The answer is that Rabbi Yehuda, despite not being worried about {\i davar ha-chozer l\u8217'kilkulo}, still holds that there must be {\i some} substantive testimony to both elements of the declaration. He cannot simply accept half a job. The {\i takkanah} may have relaxed in one direction \u8212- Rabbi Yehuda does not require a single person to deliver the entire composite declaration \u8212- but it does not relax to the point of permitting a {\i get} where only one element is attested to at all. Attestation to sealing alone, without any testimony to writing, remains invalid even for Rabbi Yehuda.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Final Resolution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The final resolution is thus clear. Rabbi Yehuda\u8217's leniency is structurally limited: it extends to cases where both persons delivering the testimony are appointed as {\i shlichim} \u8212- co-messengers formally charged with bringing the {\i get}. In that configuration, the {\i get} exits from under both their hands, and the functional requirements of the {\i takkanah} are satisfied through their shared appointment even if their testimonies are formally divided. What Rabbi Yehuda will not permit is a case where only one element is testified to in total, or where the individuals are not designated messengers and are merely bystanders attesting to fragments of what the {\i takkanah} requires as a unified whole.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Gittin 17\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Two Witnesses Abroad\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabba bar bar Chana\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The sugya opens by recalling the unresolved question from the previous amud: when two witnesses bring a {\i get} from {\i medinat hayam}, must they declare {\i bifnei nechtav uvifnei nechtam}, or not? Two versions of the ruling had been presented, and it was unclear which one to follow.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now continues with a narrative set approximately six lines from the bottom of the prior daf. Rabba bar bar Chana fell ill, and Rabbi Yehuda and Rabba came to visit him \u8212- a {\i bikur cholim} call. They posed the question directly: if two witnesses bring a {\i get} from abroad, must they state {\i bifnei nechtav uvifnei nechtam} or not? Rabba bar bar Chana answered them: they need not.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Edei Mesirah Logic\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 His reasoning is a clean logical argument. He said: if these two witnesses were to say simply, \u8220"{\i bifneinu geireshah}\u8221" \u8212- \u8220"in our presence he divorced her\u8221" \u8212- would they not be believed? Of course they would. If they were present at the actual act of delivery, they would function as {\i edei mesirah}, witnesses to the divorce itself, and their testimony would be fully accepted. All the more so, then, their failure to recite the formal declaration of {\i bifnei nechtav uvifnei nechtam} creates no disqualification.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chaveirim Incident\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 At that point the Gemara interpolates an incident that, as the text itself signals, has little to do with the halachic ruling just stated \u8212- yet it is colorful enough to record. The story continues: {\i ad hachi ata hahu chavara} \u8212- while this was going on, a group of {\i chaveirim} arrived. Here {\i chaveirim} does not refer to the learned scholars who earned that honorific in certain circles, but rather to Persian ruffians. They seized the candle from before the rabbis. As Rashi explains, it happened to be one of their festivals, during which only the temples were permitted to have light; the {\i beit midrash} was suddenly plunged into darkness.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabba bar bar Chana then cried out: {\i Rachmana, o vetulach o vetula d\u8217'var Eisav} \u8212- \u8220"Merciful One, either in Your shade or in the shade of the son of Esau.\u8221" In other words: Master of the Universe, either protect us Yourself, or \u8212- implausible as it sounds \u8212- we would rather be living under Roman rule than under these Persians.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara challenges this immediately: are the Romans better than the Persians? Was there not a {\i baraita} in which Rabbi Chiyya expounded the verse, \u8220"{\i Elokim hevin darkah v\u8217'hu yada et mkomah}\u8221" \u8212- God understood its ways and knew its place \u8212- to mean that God, knowing Israel could not survive under the Romans, exiled them to Babylonia? That clearly implies Babylonian exile was preferable to Roman subjugation. Yet here, Rabba bar bar Chana was wishing they were under Rome?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara resolves the tension: {\i lo kashya} \u8212- there is no contradiction. One statement refers to the period before the {\i chaveirim} descended upon Babylonia; the other refers to after. In other words, the original Persian rulers were benign enough that exile there was preferable to Roman domination \u8212- but once this particular faction of Persian ruffians took hold, Rome was the better option. With that, the Gemara closes the historical digression and returns to the halachic analysis.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Get Yotzei Mitachat\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Ami then cites Rabbi Yochanan with a significant qualification: {\i lo shanu ela sheget yotzei mitachat yedei edei chtivah} \u8212- the ruling that one witness saying {\i bifanai nechtav} combined with two witnesses saying {\i bifaneinu nechtam} is valid applies specifically when the {\i get} comes from the hands of the witness to writing \u8212- that is, when the one who declared {\i bifanai nechtav} is the primary bearer of the {\i get}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The background here is the case from the Mishnah at the close of the prior discussion: one says {\i bifanai nechtav} and two say {\i bifaneinu nechtam}, which was declared valid. Rabbi Yochanan now explains why. That first witness, the one who declared {\i bifanai nechtav}, serves as the primary {\i shliach}; because he is the one in whose hands the {\i get} rests, he is elevated \u8212- functioning, as the Gemara puts it, {\i k\u8217'shnayim al zeh} \u8212- equivalent to two witnesses for that element. Combined with the two who declared {\i bifaneinu nechtam}, the result is effectively two-and-two, satisfying the requirement on both counts.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 However, {\i aval mitachat yad edei chatima pasul} \u8212- if the {\i get} comes from the hands only of the signing witnesses, with that single witness merely a secondary figure rather than the primary bearer, it is invalid. In such a case you have only two and one, not two and two, and the requirement is not met.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The implication, as the Gemara draws it, is that two witnesses bringing a {\i get} from {\i medinat hayam} do need to declare {\i bifaneinu nechtav uvifaneinu nechtam}. This appears to contradict the lenient ruling of Rabba bar bar Chana.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Assi\u8217's Challenge\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Assi presses the point. He confronts Rabbi Ami (or the authority behind this ruling) with a challenge from the {\i reisha} of the Mishnah: the case where two say {\i bifaneinu nechtav} and one says {\i bifanai nechtam} \u8212- the {\i machloket} between the {\i Tanna Kamma}, who declares it invalid, and Rabbi Yehuda, who validates it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Assi\u8217's logic runs as follows: if you hold that two witnesses bringing a {\i get} from abroad {\i must} declare the full formula \u8212- and thus the requirement is strict \u8212- then even the case where the {\i get} comes from the hands of both of them would be disqualified by the Rabbanan, since you still only have two and one. But on the other hand, the Gemara elsewhere records an alternate version of the same teaching in which Rabbi Ami ruled more leniently \u8212- that even a {\i get} coming from the hands of the signing witnesses ({\i edei chatima}) is valid. If that is your position, then plainly you hold that two witnesses from abroad need {\i not} state the full formula, and in that case, why would the Rabbanan declare the Mishnah\u8217's case of two-and-one invalid? If two witnesses together are sufficient without any declaration, then even if the {\i get} rests primarily in their hands, the Rabbanan should validate it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Assi thus confronts Rabbi Ami with the inconsistency: you appear to have stated two irreconcilable positions.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Final Resolution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Ami\u8217's reply is crisp: {\i amar leh in} \u8212- yes, that is so \u8212- and then, regarding his earlier stricter formulation: {\i b\u8217'ha zimna lo amarta hachi} \u8212- on that occasion you did not say it this way. He had in effect reversed himself, and now he states clearly: {\i yated hi shelo tamut} \u8212- this is a peg that shall not be moved. The definitive position is the lenient one: two witnesses who bring a {\i get} from abroad need not recite {\i bifaneinu nechtav uvifaneinu nechtam}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 With this resolution in hand, Rav Assi\u8217's challenge dissolves. Why does the Rabbanan\u8217's ruling in the Mishnah case render it invalid? Because in that case, the {\i get} does not come from the hands of both of them \u8212- {\i she\u8217'ein haget yotzei mitachat yedei shneihem} \u8212- the two are not the primary bearers. It is that factor alone, not any failure to recite the formula, that creates the problem. But when two witnesses come together as the primary bearers of a {\i get} from abroad, they need not declare anything at all, because their joint presence would have sufficed even as {\i edei mesirah} \u8212- witnesses to the delivery \u8212- and no formal attestation is required.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Get Mukdam\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Intro: Predating\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 With the foregoing questions settled, the Gemara opens a new Mishnah and a new topic of great practical importance: {\i get mukdam} \u8212- the predated {\i get}. The Mishnah lays out a grid of possibilities based on the timing of writing ({\i ketivah}) and signing ({\i chatimah}):\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i get} written by day and signed by day is valid. Written at night and signed at night is valid. Written at night and signed the following day is likewise valid \u8212- this presents no difficulty because the natural progression of time moves forward from night into the following day, so there is no discrepancy. The problematic case is the fourth combination: written by day and signed at night. Here the Mishnah presents a {\i machloket}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 To appreciate why this fourth case is contentious, one must understand the general problem with any document that bears two different dates \u8212- whether the gap is a day, a month, or a year. In ordinary loan documents ({\i shtarot chov}), the concern is this: a creditor holding a {\i shtar} has the power not only to collect from the debtor\u8217's own assets but also from land he has since sold to third parties, provided the sale took place after the {\i shtar} took effect. This is because the signing of the {\i shtar} creates a lien ({\i shiabud}) from its effective date. If a document purports to have taken effect on a date earlier than it actually did, the creditor can unjustly pursue property that was legitimately sold during the intervening period. The purchaser ({\i Jonathan}, in the lecturer\u8217's running example) bought the land fairly and had no way to know of any prior claim \u8212- yet if the document is predated, he loses his property when he should not have.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The same structural problem arises with a {\i get}, albeit with different stakes. The {\i get} involves the husband\u8217's rights to the produce ({\i peirot}) of his wife\u8217's property. From some point in the process \u8212- the precise moment being subject to dispute \u8212- those rights cease. If the {\i get} is predated, the apparent termination of those rights is pushed back artificially, potentially harming purchasers or creating other legal distortions.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishnah Dispute\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Mishnah presents the following {\i machloket}. The {\i Tanna Kamma} holds that a {\i get} written by day and signed at night ({\i nichtav bayom venichtam balaila}) is invalid \u8212- {\i pasul}. Rabbi Shimon, however, is {\i machshir} \u8212- he validates it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But Rabbi Shimon\u8217's position requires care. The Mishnah states: {\i kol hagittin shenichtevu bayom venichtamu balaila pesulin} \u8212- all {\i gittin} written by day and signed at night are invalid \u8212- and then immediately carves out the exception: {\i chutz migittei nashim}, with the exception of bills of divorce. So in the very breath in which he appears to disqualify such documents generally, Rabbi Shimon explicitly exempts {\i gittin} \u8212- the divorce documents for women \u8212- and validates them. Understanding the logic of this exemption is the central task of the ensuing Talmudic discussion.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yochanan: Bas Achoto\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then introduces a famous {\i machloket} between Rabbi Yochanan and Reish Lakish about the foundational reason the Rabbanan required a date in a {\i get} at all.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yochanan says: the reason is {\i mishum bas achoto} \u8212- on account of his niece. The concern is {\i shema yechapeh al bas achoto} \u8212- lest a husband, married to his own niece (which is permitted by Torah law), seek to cover up her adultery by producing a {\i get} antedated to before the act of infidelity occurred. Without a date, or with a manipulable date, she could claim to have been divorced before she committed adultery and thus escape punishment. To prevent this kind of cover-up, the Rabbanan required a clear and immovable date on every {\i get}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Lakish: Peirot\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Reish Lakish gives an entirely different reason: {\i mishum peirot} \u8212- on account of produce. The husband, during the marriage, retains the right to benefit from his wife\u8217's property \u8212- specifically its yield, the {\i peirot}. Those rights do not persist indefinitely after the {\i get} process begins. The date in the {\i get} establishes precisely when those rights were severed. Without a date, the husband could claim to have retained his rights over the produce all along, or conversely the wife could claim he had none when in fact he did. The {\i zman} in the {\i get} thus functions as the halachic boundary of the husband\u8217's property entitlements.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Lakish Rejects Bas Achoto\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then systematically asks why each of these Amoraim does not adopt the other\u8217's reasoning.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Why does Reish Lakish not hold like Rabbi Yochanan \u8212- that is, why does he reject the {\i bas achoto} rationale? His answer: {\i znus lo shechicha} \u8212- adultery is not common. The Rabbanan would not institute a universal requirement of dating every {\i get} throughout all of Israel for the rare scenario in which a man married his niece, she subsequently committed adultery, and he then sought to cover it up through a predated {\i get}. Such a chain of circumstances is too attenuated to serve as the foundation of a broad {\i takkanah}. Reish Lakish therefore insists that the reason must be something that arises routinely in every divorce: the {\i peirot} question.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yochanan: Until Netina\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Conversely, why does Rabbi Yochanan not hold like Reish Lakish \u8212- why does he not ground the {\i zman} requirement in {\i peirot}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yochanan\u8217's answer: {\i kasavar yesh l\u8217'baal peirot ad sha\u8217'at nesina} \u8212- he holds that the husband retains his rights to the {\i peirot} all the way until the moment of delivery ({\i netinah}), the final stage of the {\i get} process. If the husband\u8217's entitlement runs until the very moment the {\i get} is placed in his wife\u8217's hands, then the date inscribed in the document \u8212- which records only the writing or signing \u8212- is irrelevant to the {\i peirot} question. No matter what date appears in the {\i get}, the husband was legitimately exercising his rights until the last possible moment. There is thus no potential for injustice with respect to {\i peirot}, and no need to institute a {\i zman} for that reason. The only reason that remains, and the one Rabbi Yochanan therefore does invoke, is {\i bas achoto}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shimon via Lakish\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Now the Gemara turns to explaining the {\i machloket} between Rabbi Shimon and the {\i Tanna Kamma} through the lens of each Amora.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 According to Reish Lakish, who holds that the entire institution of {\i zman} in a {\i get} is about {\i peirot}, what is the basis of the dispute between Rabbi Shimon and the {\i Tanna Kamma}? The answer is elegant. They disagree about the precise moment at which the husband\u8217's rights to {\i peirot} are extinguished.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Shimon holds {\i ein lo mizman ketivah} \u8212- the husband has no rights from the moment of writing. The instant the {\i get} begins to be written, the husband loses all entitlement to {\i peirot}. With this understanding, a predated {\i get} is unobjectionable. Suppose the {\i get} was written in the beginning of 2015 and signed and delivered at the end of 2015. The {\i get} is dated from the writing \u8212- the beginning of 2015. From that moment, says Rabbi Shimon, the husband\u8217's rights ceased. So if the wife comes to claim that the husband had no right to sell produce or encumber property after that date, she is correct, and anyone who purchased from him during that intervening period \u8212- even a legitimate third-party buyer \u8212- cannot claim to have acquired it free of her claim. Rabbi Shimon is therefore comfortable with a predated {\i get}: the date reflects the moment of writing, and from that moment all rights were already gone.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Tanna Kamma}, by contrast, holds that the husband retains rights to {\i peirot} until {\i sha\u8217'at chatimah} \u8212- the moment of signing. If the {\i get} is predated to the writing but the husband\u8217's rights actually ran until signing, then the wife can unjustly reach back into the period between writing and signing and dispossess legitimate purchasers. The predated {\i get} thus creates a false impression that the husband had no rights during a period when he actually did. The {\i Tanna Kamma} therefore declares such a {\i get} invalid.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 To crystallize this in concrete terms: the produce that accrues between {\i sha\u8217'at ketivah} and {\i sha\u8217'at chatimah} is what stands between Rabbi Shimon and the {\i Tanna Kamma}. That is precisely what the Gemara states: {\i peirei d\u8217'vishaat ketiva ve\u8217'ad sha\u8217'at chatima ika beineihu} \u8212- the produce of that intervening period is the fulcrum of their dispute.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shimon via Yochanan\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 According to Rabbi Yochanan, however, the entire institution of {\i zman} has nothing to do with {\i peirot} \u8212- it is about {\i bas achoto}. So what, according to Rabbi Yochanan, is the basis of the dispute between Rabbi Shimon and the {\i Tanna Kamma}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is more complex, and the Gemara addresses it directly. Rabbi Yochanan essentially says: {\i aliba d\u8217'Rabbi Shimon lo kamina, kamina d\u8217'Rabbanan} \u8212- I am not speaking according to Rabbi Shimon\u8217's view; I am speaking according to the Rabbanan, i.e., the {\i Tanna Kamma}. In other words, Rabbi Yochanan\u8217's {\i bas achoto} rationale explains why the {\i Tanna Kamma} instituted a {\i zman} in the {\i get}, but Rabbi Shimon himself does not accept this rationale. Rabbi Shimon does not share the concern about covering up adultery \u8212- whether because he considers it too uncommon, or for some other reason \u8212- and therefore he does not require the {\i zman} for that purpose.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But one might press the point: Rabbi Shimon presumably does agree that the husband has {\i peirot} rights that need to be protected by a date. Why then does he validate a predated {\i get} \u8212- does that not expose the buyer to an unjust claim? The answer, consistent with the framework already established, is that Rabbi Shimon holds the husband loses {\i peirot} rights from {\i zman ketivah}. So from his perspective, the predated {\i get} does not create any injustice with respect to {\i peirot}: the husband\u8217's rights ended when the writing began, and the date in the {\i get} accurately reflects that cutoff.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 For the {\i Tanna Kamma}, by contrast, the husband retains {\i peirot} until {\i sha\u8217'at netinah} \u8212- the moment of delivery. Because rights persist until delivery, the {\i zman} in the {\i get} cannot be the writing date, since that would mislead buyers into thinking the husband\u8217's rights ended earlier than they did. But this {\i peirot} concern is not, on Rabbi Yochanan\u8217's account, the reason the {\i Tanna Kamma} requires a date; that reason is {\i bas achoto}. Rather, it is a separate, background consideration that runs through the whole system.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Peirot Timing\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 With both accounts now laid out, the Gemara crystallizes the underlying positions. According to Reish Lakish, the entire {\i machloket} between Rabbi Shimon and the {\i Tanna Kamma} rests on when the husband\u8217's {\i peirot} rights terminate: Rabbi Shimon holds from {\i sha\u8217'at ketivah}, the {\i Tanna Kamma} holds from {\i sha\u8217'at chatimah}. According to Rabbi Yochanan, the {\i Tanna Kamma}\u8217's rationale for requiring a {\i zman} is {\i bas achoto}, while Rabbi Shimon does not share that concern.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The broader framework that emerges is this: there are three temporally distinct moments in the {\i get} process \u8212- {\i ketivah} (writing), {\i chatimah} (signing), and {\i netinah} (delivery). Different authorities anchor the husband\u8217's rights to {\i peirot} at different points along this timeline, and those anchoring decisions determine whether a predated {\i get} is valid or creates a legal fiction that harms third parties.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Peirot Sources\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then presents an alternate version of the dispute \u8212- {\i ve\u8217'ika d\u8217'amri}, there are those who state it differently. In this formulation, the question is not why the Rabbanan required a date in the {\i get}, but rather: from when does the wife begin to collect {\i peirot}? That is, from which moment do the husband\u8217's entitlements cease and the wife\u8217's property rights become fully operative?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yochanan says: {\i mishat ketivah} \u8212- from the moment of writing. Reish Lakish says: {\i mishat netinah} \u8212- from the moment of delivery.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This alternate version inverts the positions relative to what was stated earlier, and the Gemara notes that a {\i baraita} supports the {\i netinah} reading for one of them. The resolution is {\i efoch} \u8212- reverse the attribution. The precise assignment of which Amora holds which position in this alternate version requires the flip, bringing the positions back into alignment with the {\i baraita}\u8217's evidence.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The sugya thus concludes with a rich, multi-layered dispute about the halachic timeline of a {\i get} \u8212- a dispute whose ramifications extend to the rights of buyers, the protection of wives, and the fundamental question of when a divorce takes legal effect in its various dimensions.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Gittin 18\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Zman in Get Review\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yochanan v. Lakish\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The daf opens with a brief review of the central debate that occupied the preceding discussion: the argument between Rabbi Yochanan and Reish Lakish concerning when a {\i get} takes effect, and what precisely is the purpose of requiring a {\i zman} \u8212- a date \u8212- in a {\i get}. The {\i mishna} under examination deals with the permutations that arise when a {\i get} is begun on one day and completed on the following night, and whether such a document is valid given that technically the writing ({\i ketivah}) and the signing ({\i chatima}) occurred on different calendar days.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two Reasons for Zman\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Two major reasons were proposed for why {\i chazal} instituted the requirement of a {\i zman} in a {\i get}. The first is to protect a wife from wrongful execution: if a husband were to predate a {\i get}, his wife could claim she was already divorced prior to any alleged act of adultery, thus escaping punishment she would otherwise deserve. Without a {\i zman}, she could always claim she was divorced earlier than the affair occurred. The second reason concerns the purchaser of {\i nichsei milog} \u8212- the wife\u8217's personal property over which the husband holds usufruct rights during the marriage. The question is until when the husband may legitimately sell the produce ({\i peirot}) of that property. If the {\i get} is predated, a buyer of those {\i peirot} might be disadvantaged, purchasing produce the husband was no longer entitled to sell. The later the husband\u8217's rights terminate, the more exposure the purchaser faces; the earlier the termination, the more circumscribed his ability to transact.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Shimon Get\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 These two foundational reasons allow us to understand the debate between Rabbi Shimon and the {\i Tanna Kamma}. The central question is: when does the husband\u8217's right to the {\i peirot} end? Rabbi Shimon, who is {\i machshir} \u8212- who validates \u8212- what is called a {\i get mukdam}, a predated {\i get}, holds that the husband loses his rights at the earliest possible time. If someone produces a {\i get} bearing an earlier date, Rabbi Shimon is unconcerned, because in his view the husband was in any case already divested of his rights to the {\i peirot} from the time of writing. Whether one follows Rabbi Yochanan or Reish Lakish regarding the precise moment this takes effect \u8212- the {\i ketivah}, the {\i chatima}, or the {\i netinah} \u8212- Rabbi Shimon places that cutoff earlier than either of them, and that is the basis of his leniency.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 By contrast, the {\i Tanna Kamma} holds that the operative moment is later. Accordingly, if a {\i get} bears an earlier date than when it actually took legal effect, there is a problematic gap during which the husband might have sold {\i peirot} he should not have. It is precisely this gap that renders the predated {\i get} unacceptable to the {\i Tanna Kamma}, regardless of whether one follows Rabbi Yochanan or Reish Lakish on the specific timing question.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Takanah Challenges\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Psulim Gets Kelali\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The discussion turns to a pointed challenge against the entire framework of the {\i takanat chazal} requiring a {\i zman}. The {\i Gemara} introduces the teaching cited by Abaye to Rav Yosef: there are three categories of {\i gittin} that are {\i psulim} \u8212- invalidated \u8212- yet if a woman went ahead and remarried using one, the children born of that subsequent marriage are not considered {\i mamzerim}. One of those three defective {\i gittin} is a {\i get} that has witnesses but lacks a {\i zman}. This prompts the larger question: if such a {\i get} can in practice enable remarriage without producing {\i mamzerim}, what exactly did {\i chazal} accomplish with their {\i takanah} requiring a {\i zman}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Circumventing Zman\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} then raises two ways a bad actor might circumvent the {\i takanah}, putting pressure on its effectiveness. First, one might simply instruct the {\i sofer} to write the {\i get} without any {\i zman} at all. The {\i Gemara} dismisses this: {\i l\u8217'chatchila} no one would do this, because the {\i sofer} would refuse. The requirement is well-known enough that a competent scribe would decline to write a {\i get} without a date. This practical deterrent gives the {\i takanah} real force.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But the {\i Gemara} presses further: what if the {\i sofer} writes the {\i get} with a proper {\i zman}, and then the husband physically cuts out the date and delivers the document without it? The {\i Gemara} responds: we do not suspect people for such games \u8212- {\i lo micheshvinan}. The concern does not rise to the level requiring us to legislate against every possible manipulation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A third objection is then raised. What if the {\i zman} is written, but imprecisely \u8212- not the full date, but only the year, or the {\i shemita} cycle, or the month, or the week? Does such vagueness render the {\i takanah} ineffective? The {\i Gemara} rules that even such a document is {\i kasher}. And to the obvious follow-up \u8212- if so, what does the {\i takanah} accomplish if you can write something so general? \u8212- the {\i Gemara} answers: it at least delimits the time to the immediately preceding and immediately following units. If you wrote the {\i shemita} cycle but not the day, you know it was not the prior cycle and not the next one. That is meaningful precision, even if imperfect. And this is no different from the way we always write a {\i get} with the day but not the hour \u8212- we have always tolerated some degree of temporal imprecision. Writing the day tells us it was not yesterday and not tomorrow, and writing the {\i shemita} year tells us it was not the prior one and not the subsequent one. The {\i takanah} therefore always confers something, even when the {\i zman} is approximate.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Takanah Effectiveness\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A further challenge is posed by Ravina to Rava: what if the husband did everything correctly \u8212- wrote a proper {\i get} with a full {\i zman} \u8212- but then simply held onto it, placing it in his pocket in the hope that reconciliation might come? He thought things were rocky, wrote the {\i get} as a precaution, but then underwent a period of deliberation. Now the {\i get} is delivered much later than it was written. What did the {\i takanah} accomplish in this interval? Rava replies: a man does not hasten his own misfortune. If the husband genuinely hopes the marriage will work out, he would not write a {\i get} in the first place. The very act of writing signals resolve; one does not initiate divorce proceedings in anticipation of a trip to marriage counseling.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Then Ravina poses the question to Rav Ashi regarding {\i gittin} that arrive from {\i medinat hayam} \u8212- from overseas. A {\i get} written in Nisan may not be delivered until Tishrei, leaving six months or more between {\i ketivah} and {\i netinah}. How does the {\i takanah} help in such cases? The {\i Gemara} answers: {\i hani kala it lehu} \u8212- matters like this are publicly known. Rashi explains that the interim period raises concerns about both {\i zenut} and {\i peirot}, and in both cases the burden falls on her to bring proof of when the {\i get} was actually received. People will investigate and inquire, and the husband will not be able to transact freely with her property during the gap, because the community is aware of the circumstances. Whether the concern is adultery or the sale of {\i peirot}, public knowledge serves as a check: no one will be misled into thinking the {\i get} was delivered when it was not, because everyone knows it came from abroad and can calculate the timeline.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Three-Month Wait\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two Wives Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now pivots to a distinct question: from what point do we begin counting the three-month waiting period that a divorced woman must observe before remarrying? The question is introduced via a case of two wives living in the same courtyard, both given {\i gittin} by the same husband at the same time, but whose {\i gittin} bear different {\i zmanim} because they were written at different times. This would create an odd disparity \u8212- one wife appears to be divorced a month earlier than the other, despite both receiving their {\i gittin} simultaneously. Abaye resolves this by noting that {\i zmana mochiach aleha} \u8212- the date on each {\i get} speaks for itself. Since the {\i zmanim} are different, people can see that they were written at different times, and there is no confusion.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Netinah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav holds that the three-month waiting period begins at the time of {\i netinah} \u8212- delivery. A {\i baraita} supports this: if an agent was entrusted with a {\i get} to deliver to a man\u8217's wife, and the agent was delayed three months on the road, when the {\i get} finally reaches the wife she must wait an additional three months from the moment of delivery. If the period began from writing, she could remarry immediately upon receiving the {\i get}, since three months have already elapsed. The {\i baraita}\u8217's ruling that she must wait further supports Rav\u8217's position that delivery is the operative moment.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i baraita} adds that in this case we are not concerned about {\i get yashan} \u8212- the problem of an \u8220"old {\i get}\u8221" \u8212- because the husband did not have {\i yichud} with his wife in the interim. The concept of {\i get yashan} refers not necessarily to a {\i get} that is old in terms of calendar time, but to the concern that if the husband wrote a {\i get} and then cohabited with his wife before delivering it, we may infer that he reconsidered the divorce. Even if the {\i get} was written and delivered on the same day, if he slept with her in between, it becomes a {\i get yashan}. In the case of delivery from overseas, however, the parties are clearly not together, so there is no possibility of {\i yichud}, and the concern is entirely absent.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shmuel Ketivah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Shmuel holds that the three-month period begins at the time of {\i ketivah} \u8212- writing. A supporting {\i baraita} presents the case of a man who entrusted a {\i get} to an agent with the instruction not to deliver it for three months. The {\i baraita} rules that once the agent receives the {\i get}, the wife may remarry immediately upon delivery \u8212- because the three months have already been counted from the time of writing. This clearly supports Shmuel\u8217's position. The {\i baraita} likewise adds that we are not concerned about {\i get yashan} because the husband had no {\i yichud} with her in the interim.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Final Ruling Ketivah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Among the later {\i amoraim}, Rav Kahana, Rav Pappa, and Rav Ashi ruled in practice according to the time of {\i ketivah}, while Rav Pappa and Rav Huna the son of Rav Yehoshua ruled according to the time of {\i netinah}. The {\i Gemara} concludes: {\i v\u8217'hilchata mishaat ketivah} \u8212- the final ruling is that the three-month period begins from the time of writing.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Ketubah Shemittah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ketubah Not Cancelled\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now raises a question that might seem surprising at first but proves remarkably relevant: is a {\i ketubah} cancelled by {\i shemita}? The {\i shemita} year cancels debts that are outstanding and collectable. However, not all financial obligations are treated as ordinary debts. Obligations that qualify as {\i tnai beis din} \u8212- conditions established by rabbinic enactment rather than purely private agreement \u8212- are not cancelled, because they are classified differently from personal loans. The {\i ketubah} is paradigmatically such an obligation: {\i chazal} instituted it as a condition of marriage, and a man is halachically forbidden to cohabit with his wife without one. It thus carries the character of a {\i beis din}-type imposition rather than a voluntary transaction.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 In the ordinary case, therefore, a wife\u8217's {\i ketubah} is not cancelled by {\i shemita}. But what about a case in which the couple is no longer married \u8212- she is divorced or he has died \u8212- and the {\i ketubah} has become collectable but has not yet been paid? At that point, does it shift from a {\i tnai beis din} obligation to an ordinary private debt subject to {\i shemita}? That is the question the {\i Gemara} addresses.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Two Conditions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav holds that a {\i ketubah} becomes subject to cancellation by {\i shemita} only when two conditions have both been met. The first is {\i pegimah} \u8212- she has begun to collect, having received a partial payment of her {\i ketubah}. The second is {\i zekifah} \u8212- she has explicitly converted the remaining balance into a loan, declaring that what is owed to her should be treated as a monetary debt to be repaid. Only when both conditions are satisfied does the {\i ketubah} lose its {\i tnai beis din} character and become vulnerable to cancellation by {\i shemita}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The logic is that {\i pegimah} alone \u8212- beginning to collect \u8212- signals that she is actively pursuing payment and the {\i ketubah} is now \u8220"live\u8221" in the sense of being processed. But the remaining unpaid balance is still a {\i tnai beis din} obligation unless she affirmatively converts it into a loan. Conversely, {\i zekifah} alone \u8212- declaring it a loan without yet having received any payment \u8212- also does not fully transform the obligation. Only when both are present has the {\i ketubah} truly migrated from the domain of {\i beis din} enactment into the realm of ordinary personal debt.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shmuel One Condition\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Shmuel disagrees and holds that either condition alone is sufficient. If she has begun collecting ({\i pegamah}), even without declaring the remainder a loan, the {\i ketubah} becomes subject to {\i shemita}. And if she has declared it a loan ({\i zakfah}), even without having yet received any payment, that too is sufficient to render the remaining amount cancellable. Either act of converting the obligation from its {\i tnai beis din} status into something more like a private transaction is enough.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Baraita Support\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} then cites a {\i baraita} that explicitly supports Rav\u8217's position. The {\i baraita} states: when is a {\i ketubah} cancelled by {\i shemita}? When she has both engaged in {\i pegimah} and performed {\i zekifah}. It then specifies: if she performed {\i pegimah} without {\i zekifah}, or {\i zekifah} without {\i pegimah}, the {\i ketubah} is not cancelled \u8212- not until she has done both. This is about as explicit as a {\i baraita} can be.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Women\u8217's Obligations\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i baraita} cited in support of Shmuel enumerates several categories of obligations arising from situations involving women \u8212- {\i ones}, {\i knas}, {\i pitui}, and {\i ketuvat isha} \u8212- and states that these are cancelled by {\i shemita} when the obligation has been converted into a loan ({\i shezakfam b\u8217'malveh}). The process of {\i zekifah} is defined as {\i mishe\u8217'amda badin} \u8212- when the matter has been brought before a court and adjudicated, at which point the obligation is formally established as a monetary debt.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This {\i baraita} mentions only {\i zekifah} and says nothing about {\i pegimah}, which might appear to support Shmuel: perhaps {\i zekifah} alone, without {\i pegimah}, is sufficient to trigger {\i shemita} cancellation. However, the {\i baraita} does not explicitly say that {\i pegimah} is unnecessary \u8212- it simply does not address it. One could argue that the {\i baraita} presupposes that {\i pegimah} has already occurred, or at minimum that the absence of {\i pegimah} from the text does not prove that {\i zekifah} alone is enough. The {\i baraita} thus only partially supports Shmuel \u8212- it shows that {\i zekifah} without an explicit mention of {\i pegimah} can trigger cancellation, but it does not address whether {\i pegimah} alone, without {\i zekifah}, would also suffice. Shmuel claims both directions independently, and the {\i baraita} establishes only one leg of his position. Unlike the other debates discussed earlier in the {\i daf}, the {\i Gemara} here does not issue a final ruling between Rav and Shmuel.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Ketubah Parallel\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shmuel Parallel\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Shmuel articulates an important principle: a {\i ketubah} is comparable to a {\i ma\u8217'aseh beis din} \u8212- an act of court. Just as documents issued by {\i beis din} may be written during the day and signed at night without the intervening time rendering the document invalid, so too a {\i ketubah} may be written during the day and signed at night. This is directly relevant to the question that has run throughout the {\i daf} \u8212- the concern about {\i get mukdam} and the gap between {\i ketivah} and {\i chatima}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chiya Ketubah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A practical illustration is offered: the {\i ketubah} of Rabbi Chiya bar Rav was written on one day and signed on a different day, and Rav was present and raised no objection. One might infer from his silence that he agrees with Shmuel\u8217's position. However, the {\i Gemara} qualifies this inference.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Same Matter Leniency\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The reason Rav did not object in that case was not because he agreed with Shmuel\u8217's principle in the abstract, but because the parties were still {\i oskim b\u8217'oto inyan} \u8212- engaged in the same matter. When those writing and signing a document remain continuously involved in the same transaction or proceeding throughout, the gap between writing and signing does not introduce the kinds of concerns that would otherwise arise. A {\i tanna} from the school of Rabbi Elazar bar Rabbi Yosi supports this: the rule that writing in the day and signing at night is invalid applies only when the parties are no longer engaged in the same matter, but if they remain so engaged throughout, it is {\i kasher}. This leniency does not extend indefinitely \u8212- if the gap grows to ten days or more, or the parties are no longer involved in the same proceeding, the concern reasserts itself.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rabbi Shimon Scope\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Shimon Reasoning\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now takes up the formal analysis of why Rabbi Shimon is {\i machshir} a {\i get mukdam}, a predated {\i get}. Rava explains: Rabbi Shimon holds that {\i keivan shenatan einav l\u8217'garshah, shuv ein lo piyus} \u8212- once the husband has set his mind to divorce, he can no longer be appeased or reconciled. The act of {\i piyus} \u8212- reconciliation \u8212- is no longer available to him. The concrete moment that marks this irreversible decision is the writing of the {\i get}. From that point forward, whatever appears in the {\i get} as the date is treated as though the divorce effectively began at that time, because the husband\u8217's intent was firm and irrevocable. This is why predating is tolerable according to Rabbi Shimon: no practical harm flows from an earlier date, since the husband\u8217's rights over the {\i peirot} were already forfeit from the moment he committed to the divorce through the act of writing.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Lakish Limits Window\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Reish Lakish, however, limits Rabbi Shimon\u8217's leniency sharply. He holds that Rabbi Shimon spoke only about the immediate case: the {\i get} was written that same day and signed that same night \u8212- {\i l\u8217'altar}, immediately. The window between {\i ketivah} and {\i chatima} that Rabbi Shimon permits is only that narrow span of a day and the following night. Extending the gap to ten days is something Reish Lakish does not permit under Rabbi Shimon\u8217's leniency. The concern is {\i shema piyes} \u8212- perhaps during that longer period the husband was reconciled with his wife, in which case the {\i get} would no longer represent a firm and irrevocable decision but a superseded one.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yochanan Expands\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yochanan disagrees and permits a window of up to ten days even within Rabbi Shimon\u8217's framework. His reasoning: {\i ei ita d\u8217'piyes, kala it lehu l\u8217'milta} \u8212- if the parties had truly reconciled, people would know. Reconciliation is not a private matter; it manifests in behavior that the community observes. If a couple that was known to be headed for divorce is suddenly seen together again, going out, resuming their life \u8212- people talk. Since we would hear about a reconciliation, we are not concerned that one occurred silently during the ten-day gap. Rabbi Yochanan therefore permits relying on Rabbi Shimon\u8217's leniency for that extended period.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Ten People Get\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ten People Command\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} introduces a new case: a husband instructs ten people to write a {\i get} for his wife. This requires interpretation. It cannot mean that all ten physically write the document \u8212- that would be neither practical nor halachically coherent. What then does he intend? The answer must lie in the signing. But a {\i get} requires only two witnesses to sign. So what role do the other eight play?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i baraita} clarifies: if a husband says to ten people \u8220"write a {\i get} for my wife\u8221" without specifying {\i kulchem} \u8212- \u8220"all of you\u8221" \u8212- then one person writes and two sign. The other seven need not be involved at all. From this, we see that the act of addressing ten people does not inherently require all ten to participate. Rashi explains that in a case where all ten are indeed required to sign \u8212- without the word {\i kulchem} having been used \u8212- the husband\u8217's intention was to embarrass his wife publicly, using the gathering as a forum for the declaration of divorce. He wanted all those present to know about it. The {\i get} itself bears only the two standard witnesses, but the husband chose to make the matter public.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Given this, the case before us must be one in which the husband explicitly said {\i kulchem} \u8212- all of you \u8212- thereby creating an obligation for all ten to participate in the signing. The debate between Rabbi Yochanan and Reish Lakish turns on what that participation means.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yochanan Two Edim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yochanan holds: two of the ten sign as {\i edim} \u8212- as the actual halachic witnesses whose signatures give the {\i get} its validity \u8212- and all the rest sign because of a {\i tnai}, a condition. The husband, by saying {\i kulchem}, created a condition that all ten must sign, but only two of them function as witnesses in the technical sense. The remaining eight are satisfying a condition, not serving as witnesses.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Lakish All Edim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Reish Lakish holds that all ten are {\i edim} \u8212- all ten function as full witnesses. The {\i get} is not valid until all ten have signed, because all ten were designated as witnesses when the husband invoked {\i kulchem}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Practical Differences\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What practical difference emerges between these two positions? The {\i Gemara} identifies two.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The first: if two of the ten signed on one day and the remaining eight signed up to ten days later, according to Rabbi Yochanan \u8212- who holds the other eight are signing due to a {\i tnai} rather than as witnesses \u8212- this is unproblematic. A {\i tnai} carries no defined time requirement; the condition can be fulfilled whenever the parties get around to it. But according to Reish Lakish, who holds all ten are {\i edim}, all ten witnesses must sign at the same time. A {\i get} cannot have its witnesses sign across multiple days, and so this arrangement would render the {\i get} invalid.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The second difference: if one of the ten is found to be a {\i karov} or {\i pasul} \u8212- a disqualified witness \u8212- the consequences differ. According to Rabbi Yochanan, only the two designated {\i edim} need to be valid; if one of the other eight is disqualified, it is of no consequence, because they are signing due to a {\i tnai} and the {\i tnai} can simply be fulfilled by the remaining valid signatories. According to Reish Lakish, since all ten are {\i edim}, any disqualification among the ten affects the validity of the {\i get}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A further sub-question arises: what if one of the {\i first} two to sign is found to be {\i karov} or {\i pasul}? According to those who say this is still {\i kasher}, the reasoning is that since the husband did not specify {\i which} two are the witnesses, the identity of the witnesses is determined by who among the ten is valid, not by the order in which they signed. The first two to sign are not inherently the designated witnesses; any two valid signatories from among the ten fulfill the requirement. The fact that a {\i pasul} signed first does not corrupt the {\i get}, because we look to the valid signatures as the operative witnesses. The opposing view holds that this creates a problem of {\i ati l\u8217'ichlofu b\u8217'shtar d\u8217'alma} \u8212- people may mistake the {\i get} for an ordinary legal document and validate other documents on the strength of a {\i pasul}\u8217's signature appearing first.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rabbi Shimon Ten Days\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two Days Signing\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now describes an actual case: a man said to ten people to write a {\i get} for his wife, and two of them signed on one day while the remaining signatories signed up to ten days later. The case came before Rabbi Shimon ben Levi.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Shimon Valid\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Shimon ben Levi ruled: {\i k\u8217'dai hu Rabbi Shimon lismoch alav b\u8217'sha\u8217'at had\u8217'chak} \u8212- Rabbi Shimon is sufficiently authoritative to rely upon in a time of pressing need. The validation requires invoking Rabbi Shimon\u8217's leniency that a {\i get} written and signed at different times can be valid.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sha\u8217'at Hadeichak\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But the language of {\i sha\u8217'at had\u8217'chak} \u8212- a time of pressing need or hardship \u8212- is significant and calls for explanation. If Rabbi Shimon\u8217's position is a legitimate {\i l\u8217'chatchila} ruling, why frame reliance upon it as a concession to emergency? Rashi explains: {\i sha\u8217'at had\u8217'chak} here refers to cases such as where the husband has since departed and cannot be located to write a new {\i get}, or where the woman has already remarried on the basis of this {\i get} and has children \u8212- situations where retroactively invalidating the {\i get} would produce severe consequences, including stigmatizing her children and calling her subsequent marriage into question. These are not abstract halachic exercises but real human crises that require a practical resolution.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yochanan Leniency\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} resolves the apparent tension: Rabbi Shimon ben Levi holds like Reish Lakish regarding the scope of Rabbi Shimon\u8217's leniency \u8212- that it applies only to the immediate day and night, not to ten days. But in this case he extends the window to ten days by relying on Rabbi Yochanan\u8217's position, which permits that longer interval under Rabbi Shimon\u8217's framework. This is a compound ruling: he relies on Rabbi Shimon\u8217's leniency for the validity of the {\i get} despite the gap between signings, and on Rabbi Yochanan\u8217's ruling that the window extends to ten days.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Why then is this only a {\i sha\u8217'at had\u8217'chak} ruling rather than a {\i l\u8217'chatchila} one? Because Rabbi Shimon ben Levi himself does not hold all these lenient positions {\i l\u8217'chatchila}. He holds primarily like Reish Lakish \u8212- who would require all the signatures to be contemporaneous and would limit Rabbi Shimon\u8217's window to the immediate day and night. It is only because the case already happened, and because invalidating the {\i get} would cause genuine hardship \u8212- {\i mishum iguna akilu bah rabanan}, the rabbis were lenient for the sake of preventing women from becoming {\i agunot} \u8212- that he is willing to reach across and rely on the more permissive opinions. This is not rabbi-shopping in the pejorative sense, but rather the established halachic principle that where the stakes involve freeing a woman from an uncertain marital status, authorities have greater latitude to adopt lenient readings than they would otherwise exercise {\i l\u8217'chatchila}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Gittin 19\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Perek Overview\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Masechet Framework\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Daf 19 opens at the {\i mishna}, and it is worth pausing to appreciate how the {\i perakim} of {\i Gittin} have been structured and how the transition from the first {\i perek} to the second unfolds. The {\i masechet} begins in a somewhat indirect manner, organizing itself around the {\i takkana} of {\i bifanai nichtav ubifanai nechtam} \u8212- the requirement that the {\i shaliach} who delivers a {\i get} declare that it was written and signed in his presence. That framework dominates the opening {\i perek}, and it resurfaces at the beginning of the second {\i perek} as well, addressing variations on the same theme. But one can also read this structure as a way of approaching the central themes of the {\i masechet} obliquely \u8212- entering through a side door, as it were, before arriving at the heart of the matter.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One important thread that emerged throughout the first {\i perek} was a series of parallels between a {\i get} of an {\i isha} and a {\i shtar shikhrur} of an {\i eved}. The {\i bifanai nichtav} formulation applies to both, and this parallel itself underscores something significant: this is not merely any {\i shtar}. It is a document that changes personal status. That may be precisely why testifying about the {\i nichtav} \u8212- the writing itself \u8212- carries special weight: there are particular criteria governing the act of writing that do not apply to ordinary documents.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ketivat Haget Focus\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having established that broader framework, the second {\i perek} uses {\i bifanai nichtav ubifanai nechtam} as a bridge into the core concerns of {\i ketivat haget}. Where the first {\i perek} assembled a somewhat varied collection of topics \u8212- {\i arkha\u8217'ot}, {\i shtar shikhrur}, {\i shtar matana}, and the like \u8212- the second {\i perek} begins to focus squarely on the {\i get} itself and on what the Torah requires of its writing.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Dating Requirements\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The preceding {\i mishna} addressed the case of {\i nichtav hayom venechtam balaila} \u8212- what if the {\i ketiva} and the {\i chatima} occurred on different days? This was a natural transition from the {\i bifanai nichtav ubifanai nechtam} framework and led the {\i gemara} to explore the requirement to include a date in a {\i get} ({\i lamma hitkinu zeman begittin?}) as well as the particular {\i psul} of a {\i get mukdam} \u8212- a {\i get} bearing an earlier date than its actual execution. That discussion, emerging organically from the {\i nichtav-nechtam} framework, brought us to a central requirement of {\i ketivat haget}. Now, the current {\i mishna} moves even more concretely into the physical dimensions of that writing: what materials may be used, and on what surfaces may a {\i get} be written?\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Writing Materials\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Permitted Inks\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} opens with the principle {\i bekhol kotvin} \u8212- one may write with everything \u8212- and then proceeds to enumerate: {\i bediyo} (ink), {\i besam}, {\i besikra}, {\i uvekomus}, {\i uvekhankantom}, and {\i uvekhol davar shehu kayyama} \u8212- with any substance that endures. These are all permitted writing materials for a {\i get}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Permanence Definition\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 There is an alternative {\i girsa} of the {\i mishna} which reads {\i bedik khol davar shehu roshem} \u8212- anything that leaves a mark \u8212- rather than {\i shehu kayyama}. The distinction is worth pondering. {\i Roshem} suggests an impression or mark, whereas {\i kayyama} emphasizes lasting permanence. One might ask whether carving or etching would be included under {\i roshem}, but the {\i mishna} does not address that case directly, and the {\i gemara} will have more to say about the question of permanence.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Prohibited Liquids\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} then states: {\i ein kotvin lo bemashkin velo bemei peirot velo bekhol davar she\u8217'eino mitkayyem} \u8212- one may not write a {\i get} with liquids, fruit juices, or any substance that does not endure. The contrast with the permitted list is deliberate: {\i shehu kayyama} versus {\i she\u8217'eino mitkayyem}. This raises a natural question: precisely how long must something last? Is there a defined {\i shiur} of permanence? Does it need to resist erasure, or is it enough that it not evaporate immediately? The case of a {\i ketuba} whose ink has faded over decades, or of writing that disappears within moments of application \u8212- these represent very different points on the spectrum. The problem with {\i mashkin} and {\i mei peirot} appears to be that they are essentially ephemeral \u8212- they do not leave a genuinely lasting {\i roshem}. The word {\i roshem} itself may be instructive here: even if a substance does not endure for years, it should at minimum leave a visible, distinguishable mark that does not vanish instantaneously. The question of a pencil is interesting in this regard: a pencil certainly leaves a mark and does not fade on its own, though it can be erased. The {\i mishna} does not mention resistance to erasure as a criterion \u8212- that is a separate concern related to {\i davar sheyachol lehizdayef} \u8212- and so a pencil, or something like it, might not be disqualified on the grounds of {\i she\u8217'eino mitkayyem} alone.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Permitted Surfaces\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Turning to the surface, the {\i mishna} states {\i al hakol kotevin} \u8212- one may write on anything. The examples given are striking: {\i al ha\u8217'aleh shel zayit} \u8212- on an olive leaf \u8212- and {\i al hakeren shel parah} \u8212- on the horn of a cow. In the latter case, the {\i mishna} adds {\i venoten lo et haparah}: one must give her the cow itself along with the {\i get}. We will see in the {\i gemara} why this is so. Similarly, {\i al yad shel eved venoten lo et ha\u8217'eved} \u8212- one may write on the arm of a slave, but then the slave himself must be given to her. The operative rule is {\i vekhatvo venatano} \u8212- the {\i get} must be written and then delivered as a single act; if separating the document from its surface requires an additional act after the writing, complications arise, as the {\i gemara} will clarify.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Yose HaGalili\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Yose HaGelili} disputes part of this ruling: {\i ein kotevin lo al davar she-yesh bo ruach chayim velo al ochlim} \u8212- one should not write a {\i get} on a living creature, and not on food. We will see his reasoning in the {\i gemara}, along with the question of whether his position is grounded in a {\i de\u8217'oraita} concern or a rabbinic one.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is worth noting one structural asymmetry in the {\i mishna}: the requirement of permanence is stated explicitly for the substance used to write {\i with}, but not for the surface written {\i on}. Yet an olive leaf is hardly a permanent object. This tension points back to the question of what {\i mitkayyem} really demands: perhaps the concern is only with substances that are entirely ephemeral \u8212- that evaporate or vanish as soon as they are applied \u8212- rather than with materials that will eventually deteriorate under normal conditions.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Ink Analysis\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Dyo & Sam\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now turns to identify the materials listed in the {\i mishna}. {\i Dyo} is straightforwardly {\i dyuta} \u8212- ink. {\i Sam} is more elusive. Rashi identifies it as orpiment; the Steinsaltz commentary says arsenic; another tradition describes it as ink derived from a grass or root. Whatever the precise referent, it belongs to the category of plant- or mineral-derived colorants used as writing substances.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sikra & Kankantom\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Sikra}, according to {\i Rabbah bar Bar Hana}, is {\i sikrastah} \u8212- a transliteration that essentially concedes the difficulty of rendering the word in another language, illustrating the fundamental challenge of translation when no ready equivalent exists. Rashi understands {\i sikra} as a reddish substance, and that characterization will be important momentarily. {\i Kankantom}, as {\i Rabbah bar Bar Hana} reports in the name of Shmuel, is {\i khartsa d\u8217'ushkhasei} \u8212- the blacking used by shoemakers. The Steinsaltz commentary identifies it as copper sulfate, though it is likely iron sulfate.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kumos\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Kumos} is identified as {\i kuma} \u8212- gum arabic.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Permanent Materials\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having listed these substances, the {\i mishna} concludes with the broader category of {\i bekhol davar shehu mitkayem} \u8212- anything that endures. The {\i gemara} asks: {\i le\u8217'atuyei mai} \u8212- what does this phrase come to include beyond the specific items already enumerated? The answer draws on a teaching of Rabbi Chiyya: if one wrote a {\i get} with {\i mei tarya}, it is {\i kasher}. Rashi explains {\i mei tarya} as water in which a fruit has been soaked \u8212- not fruit juice squeezed directly from the fruit, but rather a liquid in which some dye-bearing plant material has been steeped, producing something closer to an infusion or rudimentary ink. Rashi identifies the relevant substance with {\i afatzim} \u8212- gallnuts \u8212- a material that, as we will see later in the {\i gemara}, plays a significant role in the preparation of {\i kelafim} as well.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A separate {\i braita} cites Rabbi Chiyya teaching that if a {\i get} was written with {\i avar} (lead), with {\i shachor} (which Rashi understands as coals or charcoal), or with this shoemaker\u8217's dye, it is {\i kasher}. These may seem obvious, as all three are clearly permanent substances, but their explicit enumeration serves a purpose that will become clearer as the {\i gemara} proceeds.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Writing Over Writing\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Dyeing on Sikra\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now introduces a conceptually rich discussion about inking over existing writing, using the laws of {\i Shabbat} as a comparative framework. The case is {\i ha-madyo al gabei sikra be-Shabbat}: someone on {\i Shabbat} applies black ink ({\i dyo}) on top of writing that had already been done in {\i sikra} \u8212- the reddish, permanent but less visually distinct substance. Rabbi Yochanan and Reish Lakish both agree: {\i chayav shtayim} \u8212- one incurs two violations.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kotev vs Mochek\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The first violation is {\i mishum kotev} \u8212- writing. Even though the letters were already there in red, covering them with black ink constitutes a new and improved {\i ketiva}: black letters on white parchment are more crisp and legible than red ones, and so something qualitatively new has been produced. The second violation is {\i mishum mochek} \u8212- erasing. This is the striking part. The intuition might be that erasing means physically removing something; covering it up seems different. Yet the {\i gemara} holds that by applying black over red, one has in effect caused the red letters to disappear \u8212- and that counts as {\i mochek}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This may be compared to the well-known discussion about lettering on cake on {\i Shabbat}. The {\i Maharach Or Zarua} discouraged eating it on account of {\i mochek}, though Rabbi Akiva Eiger called this position extreme. One objection is that eating the letters is {\i mitkalkel} \u8212- a destructive, not constructive, act \u8212- and also {\i eino mitkaven}. But one crucial difference is worth noting: eating the letters on a cake is a form of destruction, whereas inking over red letters with black is arguably an act of construction that simultaneously renders the red invisible. The classic {\i mochek} is {\i al menat lichtov} \u8212- erasing in order to write \u8212- because erasing makes the parchment beneath available for new writing. Here, by covering red with black, one is doing something analogous: the space that held red letters now holds black ones, and the surface has been put to productive use. That is the logic of the double liability.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} engages this question directly. In explaining why the {\i sikra-on-dyo} case (discussed below) might constitute {\i mochek al menat lichtov}, {\i Tosafot} argues that applying a red underlay actually improves the subsequent black layer \u8212- {\i ketiva elyona reshuma nikeret tefei mima\u8217'aseh tachtona levada} \u8212- the upper black writing will be even more legible when written over a red underlayer than if applied to bare parchment. This is a creative explanation, though its technical accuracy may be questioned.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Red on Black\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The case of {\i sikra al gabei deyo} \u8212- red applied over black \u8212- is more ambiguous. Here the {\i gemara} records a dispute: {\i amrei lah chayav ve\u8217'amrei lah patur}. Those who say {\i chayav} argue that this constitutes {\i mochek}: the black letters beneath have been rendered invisible. Those who say {\i patur} counter that this is {\i mekalkel} \u8212- a destructive act with no constructive purpose. The logic of {\i mochek} requires not merely that letters be made to disappear, but that their disappearance renders the parchment more usable for subsequent writing. When one covers black with red, the result is worse \u8212- red ink on top of black leaves the surface no more usable than before. The parchment has not become a blank slate ready for new writing; it now has red ink sitting atop black ink. One might respond that if the person {\i prefers} red, then covering black with red could be considered constructive \u8212- and in that case perhaps {\i chayav mishum kotev} would also apply \u8212- but the {\i gemara} leaves the matter as a dispute.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ink on Ink\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The cases of {\i deyo al gabei deyo} and {\i sikra al gabei sikra} are more straightforward: if one adds fresh ink of the same color over existing ink of the same color, one is {\i patur}. Nothing qualitatively new has been produced. The letters were already there, the color is unchanged, and merely refreshing the same ink atop itself does not constitute a new act of {\i ketiva}. (If the underlying ink were fading, the analysis might differ \u8212- in that case one could argue that something genuinely new and improved has resulted \u8212- but the case under discussion assumes clearly legible, intact original writing.)\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Writing Over Writing (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Pre-inked Signatures\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 With this {\i Shabbat} framework in view, the {\i gemara} transitions to a related but distinct question: what happens when witnesses who cannot sign their own names are offered pre-written letters in {\i sikra} over which they then trace in black ink? Does the result count as a valid {\i chatima}? The preceding discussion about {\i kotev} and {\i mochek} by {\i Shabbat} is the backdrop against which this question now unfolds.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Signature Over Writing\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Witnesses Cannot Sign\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Reish Lakish poses the question to Rabbi Yochanan: {\i edim she\u8217'ein yode\u8217'im lachtom} \u8212- witnesses who do not know how to sign \u8212- may one pre-write their names for them in {\i sikra} and have them sign over it with black ink? Is the resulting {\i chatima} valid?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Is Upper Writing New\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yochanan responds: {\i ktav elyon eino ktav} \u8212- the upper layer of writing is not considered writing. The names were already present in {\i sikra}; tracing over them in ink does not constitute a genuine act of signing.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shabbat Precedent\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Reish Lakish presses back: but did our master not teach us that {\i ktav elyon ktav le\u8217'inyan Shabbat} \u8212- that by {\i Shabbat}, writing over existing writing is considered genuine {\i ketiva} and one is {\i chayav}? If it counts as writing for {\i Shabbat}, should it not count as writing for the {\i chatima} of a {\i get} as well?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Analogy Limitation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yochanan\u8217's reply is striking: {\i veki mipnei she\u8217'anu m\u8217'damim na\u8217'aseh ma\u8217'aseh} \u8212- just because we can draw an analogy, does that mean we should act upon it? This is a remarkable methodological statement. The phrase {\i mipnei she\u8217'anu m\u8217'damim na\u8217'aseh ma\u8217'aseh} appears elsewhere in {\i halachic} literature as an expression of reluctance to translate a compelling argument into an actual ruling. Poskim sometimes construct an entire line of reasoning and then pull back with precisely this formulation \u8212- as if to say: the argument may be internally coherent, but I am not willing to cross the threshold from analysis to practical decision on its basis alone. But it is worth distinguishing two different contexts in which this reluctance can operate. In some cases, a {\i posek} builds an argument entirely within a single area of {\i halacha} and then hesitates to follow his own reasoning to its conclusion \u8212- that is a kind of general caution about the limits of one\u8217's own analysis. Here, however, something more specific is being said: we are making an analogy {\i across} two different areas of {\i halacha} \u8212- {\i hilkhot Shabbat} and {\i hilkhot gittin} \u8212- and Rabbi Yochanan is questioning whether the criteria operative in one domain can simply be imported into the other.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Different Halakhic Areas\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What might explain a difference in the definition of {\i ketiva} between {\i Shabbat} and the {\i chatima} of a {\i get}? One possible approach concerns the conceptual structure of {\i hilkhot Shabbat}. The framework of {\i avot} and {\i toldot} in {\i Shabbat} and the concept of {\i melekhet machshevet} together suggest that what matters in many cases is the {\i result} achieved rather than the precise mechanical process by which it was achieved. If the end result is that something of significance has been produced \u8212- if {\i nitkayma machshavto} \u8212- one may be {\i chayav}, regardless of the particular path taken. On this reading, inking over red letters to produce crisp black letters is a meaningful transformation: a new {\i cheftza d\u8217'ksav} has been created, even if the process was not a conventional act of writing from scratch. But for the {\i chatima} of witnesses on a {\i get} \u8212- or on any {\i shtar} \u8212- the question may be whether the witnesses performed a genuine {\i ma\u8217'aseh ketiva}. For a signature to be their signature, perhaps it must emerge from an act of writing something that was not already there, rather than from the act of filling in letters pre-formed by someone else. Writing on top of something that is already written may achieve a new result for the purposes of {\i Shabbat} while still not constituting a valid act of {\i chatima}. The {\i gemara} here leaves this distinction suggestive rather than fully resolved, noting that a more extended discussion of the {\i Shabbat} dimension will follow on the next {\i daf}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Illiterate Witnesses\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Stencil Method\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now presents the practical solutions developed by the Amoraim for witnesses who cannot sign their names. Rav\u8217's approach: {\i mekarim lahem niyar chalak u-memalim et ha-keraim b\u8217'dyo} \u8212- one prepares for them a stencil of blank paper and they fill the cutout letters with ink. {\i Tosafot} understands this straightforwardly as a physical stencil. Rashi offers a different reading: one makes an indentation in the {\i klaf} itself and the witnesses fill the indentation with ink.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Lead Underwriting\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Shmuel\u8217's approach is different: {\i be-avar} \u8212- one pre-writes the letters for them in lead, and they write over it. This is similar in structure to the {\i sikra} case discussed above, but lead poses an even more pronounced difficulty.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Lead Writing Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} immediately challenges this: {\i be-avar salka da\u8217'atach?} A {\i braita} of Rabbi Chiyya already established that writing with {\i avar} is {\i kasher} for a {\i get} \u8212- which means it constitutes valid {\i ksav}. If so, the letters are already written; how can the witnesses write over them and call it their {\i chatima}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Avar vs Mei Avara\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} resolves the difficulty by distinguishing between {\i avara} \u8212- actual lead rubbed directly onto the {\i klaf} \u8212- and {\i maya de-avara} \u8212- a liquid solution in which lead has been dissolved or processed, functioning as an ink. Rashi explains the distinction in terms that are illuminating. Lead rubbed onto parchment, like a pencil on paper, does not penetrate the surface; it remains on top and can be wiped away. A liquid solution, on the other hand, is absorbed into the {\i klaf} and becomes part of it, making it genuinely permanent. Rashi says explicitly: {\i be-avar leshafshef be-chatikha shel avar al ha-klaf umashchirah eino ksav} \u8212- rubbing an actual piece of lead across the parchment does not constitute {\i ksav}. The {\i maya de-avara}, by contrast, is {\i ksav}. This is a significant {\i chiddush}: something that leaves a visible mark \u8212- that is clearly {\i roshem} \u8212- and that does not disappear spontaneously, is nevertheless not considered {\i ksav} because it is not absorbed into the surface and is therefore erasable. This pushes back against the earlier reading of the {\i mishna} that did not seem to require deep permanence, and raises again the question of how precisely {\i mitkayyem} is to be defined.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gallnut Solution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Abba proposes yet another approach: {\i be-mei milin} \u8212- using gallnut juice as the underlayer over which the witnesses sign. Rashi identifies {\i mei milin} with ground gallnuts soaked in water \u8212- an infusion that, in other contexts, functions as genuine ink. This creates an immediate problem: a {\i braita} of Rav Chanina teaches that writing a {\i get} with {\i mei tarya va-afatza} (gallnut water) is {\i kasher}, meaning it counts as valid {\i ksav}. If so, how can the {\i mei milin} underlayer be permissible?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gallnut Worked Klaf\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} resolves this with the distinction {\i ha de-afitz ha de-lo afitz} \u8212- it depends on whether the {\i klaf} was prepared ({\i afitz}) with gallnut juice. {\i Tosafot} explains this well: if the {\i klaf} itself was treated with a gallnut solution during its preparation, and one then writes on it with a gallnut-based ink, the ink will not leave a visible, distinguishable mark \u8212- {\i she\u8217'ein mei milin al gabei mei milin} \u8212- because the same substance has already saturated the surface. In this case, the underlayer effectively does not exist as a discernible {\i ksav}, and the witnesses\u8217' writing over it constitutes a genuine {\i chatima}. On a {\i klaf} that was not treated with gallnuts, the same gallnut solution would leave a permanent and visible mark, and writing over it would face the same problem as the {\i sikra} case.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This resolution also sheds light on the broader structure of the discussion. The {\i gemara} is consistent in assuming that whether a substance constitutes {\i ksav} for purposes of {\i get} writing is also the operative criterion for whether witnesses can write over an underlayer. That is itself a kind of {\i dimui} \u8212- an analogical application \u8212- but one the {\i gemara} accepts without hesitation. What the {\i gemara} refuses to accept is the analogical import of {\i Shabbat} liability into the definition of valid {\i chatima}. The distinction between these two analogical moves is itself instructive: the definition of {\i ksav} for {\i ketivat haget} presumably sets the threshold for the entire document, including the {\i chatima}, whereas {\i Shabbat} operates under a different conceptual architecture and different standards of what constitutes a meaningful act.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Get vs Other Documents\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Saliva Method\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Pappa\u8217's practical solution for illiterate witnesses is the most striking of all: {\i be-rok} \u8212- using saliva. He demonstrated this method to a man called Pappa the ox-driver, showing him how to moisten the surface with spit and have the witnesses sign over it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gets Only\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Immediately the {\i gemara} qualifies: {\i vehanei milei be-gitten} \u8212- all of these accommodations apply only to {\i gitten}, not to other documents. {\i Aval bi-shtaros lo.}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Agunah vs Fraud\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rashi articulates the reason: {\i be-gitten mishum agunah ke-dilekamman} \u8212- for {\i gitten}, these leniencies are justified because of the {\i agunah} problem. The scenario is familiar: a husband is about to depart on a sea voyage, there are witnesses present but they do not know how to sign, and without a {\i get} the woman risks being left an {\i agunah} \u8212- perhaps forever unable to remarry. Even a {\i yibbum} situation is treated as an {\i agunah} case if obtaining the {\i get} would forestall the need to locate an unwilling {\i yavam}. In such circumstances the rabbis were willing to accommodate witnesses who could not sign in the ordinary manner. For monetary documents, however, no such urgency exists \u8212- one simply waits until proper witnesses are available. More importantly, allowing unrecognizable or stenciled signatures in monetary matters would open the door to catastrophic fraud: anyone could produce a {\i shtar} with block-letter signatures and claim it was signed by illiterate witnesses.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Kahana\u8217's Lashes\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The seriousness with which this was treated is illustrated by a vivid episode: a man who applied these methods of assisted signing to non-{\i get} documents was brought before Rav Kahana, who administered {\i malkot} \u8212- lashes. Rav Kahana apparently felt that the integrity of the institution of {\i shtarot} was so fundamental, and the risk of abuse so acute, that even without a specific Torah prohibition, the use of these techniques in monetary documents warranted corporal punishment. This is not a case of {\i akhal cheilev} \u8212- a classic Torah transgression \u8212- but Rav Kahana evidently regarded it as a threat to the entire structure of commercial documentation that required the strongest possible deterrent.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Literacy Requirements\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tanna Kamma\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i braita} consistent with Rav\u8217's position is cited: {\i eidim she\u8217'ein yod\u8217'im likhtom} \u8212- if witnesses do not know how to sign \u8212- {\i mekarim lahem niyar halak u-memalim et ha-keraim dyo} \u8212- one prepares a stencil and they fill it with ink. A further expansion: if the witnesses do not even know how to {\i read} the document, one may read it aloud for them ({\i korin lifneihem ve-khosmim}) and then have them sign with the stencil. The {\i tanna kamma} permits both accommodations \u8212- reading aloud by others and stenciled signatures \u8212- with no stated limitation to {\i gitten} specifically.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabban Shimon Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel disagrees with the scope of this leniency. He agrees that both accommodations \u8212- reading aloud and stenciled signatures \u8212- are permitted, but limits them to {\i gittei nashim}. For {\i shichrurei avadim} (emancipation documents for slaves, which stand at the intersection of {\i mammonot} and personal status) and all other {\i shtarot}, he rules: if the witnesses know how to read and sign, they may sign; if not, they may not sign at all.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Agunah Reason\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Elazar explains Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel\u8217's reasoning: {\i shelo yehu bnot yisrael agunot} \u8212- so that Jewish women should not be left as {\i agunot}. The formulation focuses on what makes {\i gitten} exceptional rather than on what makes other {\i shtarot} consequential, though both considerations could have been stated.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Accepted Ruling\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava rules: {\i halacha ke-Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel} \u8212- the law follows Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel\u8217's distinction. This is the accepted position.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Gamda Rejected\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Gamda reports in Rava\u8217's name a contradictory ruling: {\i ein halacha} \u8212- we do not follow Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel, apparently allowing these leniencies for all {\i shtarot}. But the {\i gemara} rejects this position immediately by invoking Rav Kahana\u8217's episode. If Rav Kahana administered lashes to someone who used these methods with ordinary documents, it is clear that the institution was considered inviolable. It is inconceivable that Rav Gamda intended to permit what Rav Kahana saw fit to punish with such severity.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Reading Verification\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Reading Allowed\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now resolves the apparent contradiction in Rav Gamda\u8217's ruling with the clarification {\i targema akria} \u8212- his statement was addressed only to the {\i kria} dimension, the reading aloud of a document. When Rav Gamda said the leniency applies to all {\i shtarot}, he meant only that one may have a document read aloud before signing it; he was not permitting stenciled or unrecognizable signatures in monetary documents. The {\i chatima} itself must still be the witness\u8217's own handwriting. The question now becomes how far the {\i akria} leniency extends and what safeguards must be in place.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Trusted Readers\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Two stories illustrate how senior Amoraim handled this. Rav Yehuda, apparently as his eyesight declined, would strain and squint to read documents himself before signing \u8212- suggesting he held that even having someone else read a document aloud is not fully reliable, and one should read it independently if at all possible. Ulla challenged this position, pointing out that Rav Elazar \u8212- considered {\i marei de\u8217'eretz yisrael}, the master authority of the Land of Israel, renowned in particular for his expertise in matters of {\i ketamim} and careful visual examination \u8212- would regularly have others read documents aloud before signing them. Similarly, Rav Nachman had the scribes of the {\i dayanim} read documents before him and then signed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Trusted Scribes\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But the {\i gemara} immediately pulls back: {\i vedavka Rav Nachman vesafrey dayanei} \u8212- it was specifically Rav Nachman together with the judges\u8217' own scribes, {\i de\u8217'it lehu eimta} \u8212- because they were afraid of him and would not misrepresent what was written. With other scribes, or with other individuals, even Rav Nachman\u8217's presence would not have been sufficient to guarantee accuracy.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Deception Concern\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This qualification reveals how seriously the {\i gemara} takes the potential for abuse even in the apparently benign case of having a document read aloud. After all, the document is sitting right there \u8212- in theory, one could always take it to someone else to verify. But in practice, witnesses typically do not do that: they hear what is read to them, they trust the reader, and they sign. A dishonest reader can therefore misrepresent the content of a document with remarkable ease. The example is not difficult to imagine: a lender presents a document to the witnesses, reads it as stating that a borrower owes him money, when in fact it says the reverse. The witnesses sign, the document is right there for all to see, and yet they have been deceived. Because of this acute risk, the {\i gemara} restricts the {\i akria} leniency even for {\i shtarot} to circumstances where the reader can be trusted with near-absolute certainty \u8212- effectively limiting it to someone in a formal, accountable relationship with the signatory, like the judges\u8217' own scribes who feared the consequences of deceiving Rav Nachman.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Foreign Languages\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Persian Documents\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now turns to the case of documents in a foreign language. Rav Pappa would sometimes receive Persian {\i shtarot} that had been produced in non-Jewish courts ({\i arka\u8217'ot shel goyim}). Recall that an early {\i mishna} already established that documents executed in non-Jewish courts are valid as evidence that a transaction took place, even if they do not satisfy the full technical requirements of a Jewish {\i shtar}. But how could Rav Pappa know what was written in a document he could not read?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Double Translation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 His solution: {\i makrey lehu lishney kutim zeh shelo bifney zeh} \u8212- he would have two non-Jews each translate the document independently, not in each other\u8217's presence. If their translations matched, and they were {\i bemasiach lefi tumo} \u8212- casual, uninvolved individuals who had no prior knowledge of the case \u8212- there was no plausible explanation for the agreement other than that both were accurately rendering what the document actually said. Two strangers, unaware of the particulars of the dispute, could not coincidentally produce identical mistranslations.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Liens Recognized\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Pappa\u8217's confidence in this method was such that he would collect even from {\i meshabedi} \u8212- property that was subject to a lien \u8212- on the basis of such a translated document. The concept of {\i meshabedi} is significant: a lien on a debtor\u8217's property attaches, and binds even subsequent purchasers of that property, only when there is a {\i shtar} that constitutes a matter of public record. Rav Pappa was willing to treat a properly verified Persian document as satisfying this standard \u8212- a striking endorsement of the method\u8217's reliability.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Jewish Persian Get\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Ashi reports a related ruling in the name of Ameimar, via Rav Huna bar Natan: a Persian {\i shtar} written by Jews and bearing Jewish witnesses is {\i kasher} and supports collection from {\i meshabedi}. This is distinct from the {\i arka\u8217'ot} case \u8212- there we were dealing with documents from non-Jewish courts; here the document is Jewish in origin, executed by Jewish parties with Jewish witnesses, but written in Persian.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Forgery Prevention\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} raises an objection: does this not require {\i ketav she\u8217'eino yakhol lehizdayef} \u8212- writing that cannot be forged or altered? Documents produced outside the formal Jewish legal system might be written with erasable materials, allowing subsequent alteration of amounts or names.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gallnut Protection\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} answers: {\i bi-defitzna} \u8212- we are speaking of a {\i klaf} that was treated with gallnut ({\i afitz}), which prevents erasure. The gallnut preparation bonds with the parchment in such a way that ink written on it cannot be erased without leaving visible evidence of tampering. In such a case, even a document written in Persian by a community less familiar with the full technical requirements of Jewish document law is protected against the specific vulnerability of forgery.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A further objection: a valid {\i shtar} must repeat the essence of its content in the final line ({\i tzarich lachzor me-inyano shel shtar be-shitah achronah}). Does such a Persian document satisfy this requirement? The {\i gemara} answers that in the case under discussion, {\i ahadreih} \u8212- it did repeat the key content in the final line. So the document is entirely congruent with a proper Jewish {\i shtar} in every respect except its language.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Any Language Valid\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This prompts the {\i gemara} to ask: {\i ela mai ka mashma lan} \u8212- what then is the ruling actually teaching us? The answer: {\i de-khol lashon kasher} \u8212- a {\i shtar} may be written in any language. But is that not already established by the {\i mishna} concerning a {\i get} written in Hebrew with Greek witnesses, or in Greek with Hebrew witnesses, which is {\i kasher}? The {\i gemara} responds: from that {\i mishna} alone, one might have concluded {\i hanei milei be-gittin} \u8212- that the latitude for different languages is a feature of {\i gitten} specifically, where the rabbis are more lenient across a range of dimensions, but that {\i shtarot} might require Hebrew or Aramaic. The ruling of Ameimar therefore adds {\i ka mashma lan} \u8212- that in {\i shtarot} as well, language is not a determinative criterion; what matters is that the document meet all the substantive requirements of a valid {\i shtar}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 To summarize the conclusions that emerge from this entire discussion: for {\i gitten}, the rabbis permitted both having the document read aloud to the witnesses and the use of stenciled or assisted signatures. For {\i shtarot}, stenciled signatures are absolutely prohibited \u8212- the risk of fraud is too great, as demonstrated by Rav Kahana\u8217's willingness to administer lashes. Having a document read aloud before signing is permitted in {\i shtarot}, but only in circumstances where the reliability of the reader can be established with near-certainty. And the language in which a document is written \u8212- whether Hebrew, Aramaic, Persian, or Greek \u8212- does not in itself determine the document\u8217's validity, provided all other substantive requirements are met.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Gittin 20\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Blank Paper & Invisible\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shmuel\u8217's Blank Paper\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The daf opens, technically, toward the end of Gittin 19b, roughly fifteen lines before the lines widen on the page. There, {\i Shmuel} presents a striking ruling: if a man gave his wife a blank piece of paper ({\i neyar chalak}), declared \u8220"this is your {\i get},\u8221" she is considered {\i megureshet} \u8212- divorced. The reason? {\i Chashishinan shema be\u8217'mei milin katava} \u8212- we are concerned that he may have written it in {\i mei milin}, water in which gallnuts had been steeped, a substance that produces a mark initially visible but prone to disappearing entirely.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is a genuinely shocking proposition. The document is, by all appearances, blank. Why should there be any concern, and on what basis would such a claim be entertained? The {\i Gemara} does not immediately address those deeper questions; instead it first confronts a textual challenge from a {\i beraita}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Beraita Challenge\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Meitivei} \u8212- the {\i Gemara} raises a difficulty from a {\i beraita}: a man gives his wife a {\i get} saying {\i harei zeh gittech}, and she, in her anger, hurls it into the sea, or into fire, or destroys it in any manner. He then claims it was never a genuine {\i get} at all \u8212- it was a {\i shtar pasim}, a document he carried to project an appearance of wealth, or a {\i shtar amanah}, a pre-signed IOU before actual lending \u8212- in short, some other kind of document, not a bill of divorce. The {\i beraita} rules: {\i eino ne\u8217'eman} \u8212- he is not believed. Once he declared it a {\i get}, he cannot retroactively render her forbidden to remarry.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} draws an inference: {\i ta\u8217'ama de\u8217'ika ktav} \u8212- the reason the {\i beraita} works is that there {\i was} writing on the document, and his claim now contradicts that prior declaration. But the implication is sharp: {\i ha leka ktav} \u8212- had the document been blank, the outcome would presumably be different, and we would not consider the wife divorced. If {\i Shmuel}\u8217's concern were correct \u8212- that even a blank document might constitute a {\i get} \u8212- the {\i beraita} would have taught the case of a blank document, where the {\i chiddush} would be even greater. The fact that it did not suggests that a blank document carries no such concern.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shmuel & Beraita\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} resolves the tension: {\i hacha bamai askinan de\u8217'badkinan leh be\u8217'maya de\u8217'nara} \u8212- the {\i beraita} actually deals with a case where the document was subsequently processed with some other liquid that brought out previously invisible writing. When examined with this reagent, it became clear that something had in fact been written. So the {\i beraita}\u8217's case was never truly blank; it always contained writing, merely invisible, and the processing revealed it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But this resolution immediately softens the radicalism of {\i Shmuel}\u8217's ruling. If the invisible writing can be made visible through processing, then we are no longer dealing with a document that is {\i entirely} blank \u8212- we now have actual evidence that something was written. The {\i Gemara} presses further: {\i ve\u8217'hashta de\u8217'pilit mai havei hashta hu de\u8217'pilit} \u8212- even if the writing emerges now under processing, what does that accomplish? At the moment the {\i get} was handed over, it was blank. We have established throughout the {\i mishna}\u8217's discussion that writing must be visible to function as a valid {\i get}. What does it matter that it becomes visible only now?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} answers: {\i Shmuel nami chashinan ka\u8217'amar} \u8212- {\i Shmuel} too only means that we are {\i concerned}, not that she is definitively divorced. The concern is that the writing may have been visible at the precise moment of transfer and disappeared only afterward. {\i Tosafot} clarifies: had witnesses observed the document to be blank at the very moment of handover, there would be no concern at all, not even a {\i safek megureshet}. The case {\i Shmuel} contemplates is one where the document was examined only later and found to be blank, with no contemporaneous observation at the moment of transfer.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Processing Invisible Ink\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Building on this, the {\i Gemara}\u8217's conclusion is that a completely blank document about which nothing further can be demonstrated raises no concern. The {\i beraita} at most suggested concern when there was writing. But where processing reveals that the document {\i had} been written in a disappearing ink, then {\i Shmuel} holds that there is at least a {\i safek megureshet} \u8212- for perhaps the writing was visible when the {\i get} was handed over. {\i I pali pali, ve\u8217'i lo pali lo}: if the processing reveals the writing, there is a concern; if nothing emerges, there is none.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Witnesses Reading Get\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Reading Before Handing\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now cites {\i Ameimar} transmitting in the name of {\i Mareimar} in the name of {\i Rav Dimi}: {\i hani bei trei de\u8217'yahavi gita kamaihu tzerichinu lemikrei} \u8212- when two witnesses are present at the handing of a {\i get}, they are required to read it beforehand. This makes abundant sense. According to the view that {\i edei mesirah karsi} \u8212- the witnesses to the delivery are the operative legal witnesses \u8212- they must know what it is they are observing, for it is their observation that constitutes the testimony to the {\i get}\u8217's content. And even according to {\i edei chasima karsi}, where the signed witnesses are primary, reading beforehand at least addresses the concerns already raised about invisible ink and disputed claims.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Beraita Without Reading\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Meitivei} \u8212- the {\i Gemara} challenges this from the very same {\i beraita} cited earlier: a man gives a {\i get}, the woman destroys it, he claims it was something else, and the rule is {\i eino ne\u8217'eman}. But if witnesses had read the {\i get} beforehand, how could he possibly make such a claim? {\i Batar de\u8217'karyei mi matzei amar lei hachi} \u8212- once they have read it, his assertion that it was a different type of document is instantly refuted. The fact that the {\i beraita} must teach the ruling at all implies that no such reading had taken place.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Resolution & Pockets\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} resolves this: {\i lo tzericha de\u8217'batar de\u8217'karyei ayeilu le\u8217'vei yadei ve\u8217'afkeih} \u8212- the witnesses did read it, but after reading it, the husband put it back into his jacket pocket and withdrew it again before handing it over. His claim is not merely that the document was something else from the start, but that he performed an actual switch \u8212- he palmed a different document when he reached into his pocket. The {\i beraita} therefore teaches {\i kama mashma lan} that we are not concerned with such a claim, even if technically conceivable. The witnesses read it, they saw it handed over, and his assertion of a switcheroo is not believed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafot Lechatchila Deba\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} raises an important question from this sugya. Why did the {\i Gemara} not simply explain the {\i beraita} as a {\i bedieved} case \u8212- one where the witnesses had failed to read it, the {\i get} is nonetheless valid {\i bedieved}, and the {\i beraita} therefore must address the consequent claim that it was a different document? The fact that the {\i Gemara} did not take that route implies that even {\i bedieved}, if the witnesses did not read the {\i get}, it is invalid. This is a highly practical question: if witnesses failed to read the {\i get} at the time of delivery, must one treat the divorce as ineffective even after the fact?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} works through the back and forth without settling it in a simple formula. What the {\i Gemara} does clearly establish is that the requirement to read is at least {\i lechatchila}, and there are strong grounds to view it as operative even {\i bedieved}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Reading Before & After\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The final line of that {\i Tosafot} is worth highlighting. {\i Tosafot} records that the {\i Ri} had a standard practice: the witnesses would read the {\i get} both before the delivery and after \u8212- before, because the {\i Gemara} makes clear that the primary obligation is prior to handing, so that at the moment of transfer the witnesses know what they are observing; and after, to foreclose any claim that a switch had been made in the interval between reading and delivery, precisely the concern raised by the {\i Gemara}\u8217's resolution.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} adds: it is {\i pshita} \u8212- obvious \u8212- that even if one holds that reading is necessary {\i bedieved}, if the witnesses read the document not before but after handing, and the woman returned it to them to examine, and they confirmed it was a {\i get}, that would suffice. For any evidentiary concern is resolved: there is no possibility of a pocket switch, and they can clearly testify to what was transferred. One might argue it is not so {\i pashut} \u8212- if the legal moment of transfer is the handing itself, and at that instant the witnesses had not yet verified the contents, perhaps their subsequent reading cannot retroactively complete the act. This touches broader questions about combining observations across time, analogous to debates about whether witnesses who observed a ring on a woman\u8217's finger after it was placed, having not directly seen the placement, can combine those perceptions. These remain significant questions in the laws of {\i gittin}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Found Document Cases\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mezuza Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now presents a series of found-document cases that build on the earlier discussion. {\i Hahu gavra de\u8217'zarak leh gita le\u8217'deveiteih le\u8217'veini danei} \u8212- a man threw a {\i get} to his wife, and it landed among stacked barrels of wine in her space. When they went to retrieve it, they found not a {\i get} but a {\i mezuza}. The dimensions of a {\i mezuza} are, apparently, close enough to those of a {\i get} in this period to raise the question. Is this the very document he threw her, meaning he actually handed her a {\i mezuza} while claiming it was a {\i get} \u8212- perhaps withdrawing the real {\i get} from his pocket and substituting the {\i mezuza}, in light of what we just discussed \u8212- or did he genuinely throw a {\i get} that is now buried deeper in the barrel pile, while the {\i mezuza} had been there independently?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Nachman\u8217's Logic\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rav Nachman} rules: {\i mezuzta be\u8217'vei danei lo shechicha} \u8212- it is not common to find a {\i mezuza} among wine barrels. The presence of this {\i mezuza} is therefore suspicious and must be explained. Since we know a document of that size was thrown, and this is the only such object found, the presumption must be that this {\i mezuza} is what he actually gave her \u8212- in which case she is not divorced.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 However, this logic applies only when a single {\i mezuza} is found. {\i Aval bitartei u\u8217'tlat} \u8212- if two or three {\i mezuzot} are discovered among the barrels, then since some of them were clearly there before the incident, the reasonable assumption is that all of them were pre-existing, and that the document actually thrown was a {\i get} now unaccounted for.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Lost Get Reasoning\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 As for the missing {\i get} in the two-or-three {\i mezuzot} case: {\i ve\u8217'gita eimar achbarim shakluho} \u8212- the {\i get} may simply have been dragged off by rats, as documents in such locations often were. The inability to produce the {\i get} is therefore no bar to treating the wife as divorced, especially in light of what was established earlier: once a {\i get} is properly given, declared by the husband, and \u8212- ideally \u8212- read by the witnesses, the absence of the document afterward need not undermine the divorce.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sefer Torah Get\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} then describes a far more dramatic case. {\i Hahu gavra de\u8217'al le\u8217'vei knishta, shakal sefer torah yahav leh le\u8217'deveiteih ve\u8217'amar lah ha gitich} \u8212- a man entered a {\i beit knesset}, took a {\i Sefer Torah}, handed it to his wife, and declared it to be her {\i get}. {\i Rav Yosef} dismissed the concern that this could constitute a valid {\i get}, and then enumerated the reasons. Could we be concerned about {\i mei milin}? Perhaps he wrote a {\i get} in disappearing ink on the parchment of the {\i Sefer Torah}, it was visible at transfer, and now is no longer seen. The {\i Gemara} answers: {\i mei milin agabi mei milin} \u8212- the {\i klaf} of a {\i Sefer Torah} is itself treated with {\i mei milin} during its preparation, and therefore {\i mei milin} written on it would never be visible to begin with. There is consequently no moment at which such writing could have appeared, and no basis for the concern {\i Shmuel} raised.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Could the {\i Sefer Torah} itself serve as a {\i sefer kritut} by virtue of the very {\i parasha} containing {\i ve\u8217'chatav lah sefer kritut}? Perhaps the Torah\u8217's own text constitutes a bill of severance. The answer: {\i habe\u8217'inan ve\u8217'chasav lah lishmah ve\u8217'leka} \u8212- the {\i get} must be written {\i lishmah}, for the sake of this particular woman, and a {\i Sefer Torah} written by a {\i sofer} in the normal course of his work was not written with any such intention. Moreover, even if he had paid the {\i sofer} to write that {\i parasha} with his wife in mind, {\i habe\u8217'inan shino shemo ve\u8217'shemah ve\u8217'shem iro ve\u8217'shem irah ve\u8217'leka} \u8212- a valid {\i get} requires the names of the husband, the wife, and their respective cities, none of which appear in the text of the Torah. {\i Tosafot} notes that while the {\i lishmah} requirement in the context of {\i sota} may be more flexible \u8212- since the scraping itself is an integral part of the ritual, perhaps reducing the demands on the {\i ve\u8217'chasav} \u8212- the {\i lishmah} of a {\i get} is categorically different. It is an expression of the husband\u8217's will and intent, not merely a technical condition of the writing, and therefore cannot be satisfied by a generic text written by a {\i sofer} in the ordinary course.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 So {\i Rav Yosef}\u8217's statement that \u8220"there is nothing to worry about\u8221" is straightforward in all its components. What, then, is his {\i chiddush}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mei Milin on Klaf\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i She\u8217'ein mei milin agabi mei milin} \u8212- the {\i chiddush} is specifically the teaching that a {\i Sefer Torah}\u8217's {\i klaf}, having been treated with {\i mei milin} in preparation, renders any writing in {\i mei milin} permanently invisible from the outset. This is not obvious. One might have thought that {\i mei milin} on {\i mei milin} could still produce some temporary visibility, analogous to {\i Shmuel}\u8217's concern about disappearing ink on ordinary paper. {\i Rav Yosef} teaches that this is not the case, and that concern is therefore entirely inapplicable to a {\i Sefer Torah}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Lishmah & Overwriting\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Lishmah Overwriting\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having discussed the {\i lishmah} requirement in the context of the {\i Sefer Torah} case, the {\i Gemara} now turns to a related and practically significant question. {\i Rav Chisda} states: {\i get she\u8217'kesavo shelo lishmah ve\u8217'he\u8217'evir alav kolmos lishmah, banu lemachlokes Rabbi Yehuda ve\u8217'Rabanan} \u8212- if a {\i get} was written {\i shelo lishmah}, with all the correct names but without the particular intention toward this husband and wife, and the scribe subsequently passed the pen over the writing again with proper {\i lishmah} intention \u8212- writing directly on top of the existing letters \u8212- does that retroactively or effectively confer {\i lishmah}? {\i Rav Chisda} says this question is the subject of a {\i machlokes} between {\i Rabbi Yehuda} and the {\i Rabbanan}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 God\u8217's Name Overwrite\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} cites the relevant {\i beraita}: a {\i sofer} writing a {\i Sefer Torah} arrived at the divine name \u8212- {\i Yud-Kei-Vav-Kei} \u8212- but in his mind he had {\i yehudah} in mind. He wrote the letters {\i Yud-Kei-Vav-Kei} correctly, but his intention was for the word {\i Yehudah}, not for {\i shem Hashem}; he simply forgot to add the {\i dalet}. The name was thus written {\i shelo lishmah}. {\i Rabbi Yehuda} says: {\i ma\u8217'avir alav kolmos u\u8217'mikadsho} \u8212- pass the pen over the name again, this time with proper intention for {\i shem Hashem}, and this sanctifies it. {\i Va\u8217'chachamim omrim eino shem min hamuvchar} \u8212- the Sages say this is not the proper way to write God\u8217's name. The context of the ruling suggests this is a {\i bedieved} dispute, not merely a {\i lechatchila} preference. {\i Rav Chisda} holds that the same dispute governs the {\i lishmah} of a {\i get}: {\i Rabbi Yehuda} would permit overwriting to supply the missing {\i lishmah}, while the {\i Rabbanan} would reject it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Acha Distinction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rav Acha bar Yaakov} pushes back: {\i dilma lo hi} \u8212- perhaps the two cases are not identical. The {\i Rabbanan}\u8217' rejection of the overwritten divine name may rest on a specific principle: {\i ad kan lo kamri rabbanan hasam de\u8217'be\u8217'inan zeh Eli ve\u8217'anveihu} \u8212- the Sages\u8217' stringency in that case derives from the demand for {\i noi mitzvah}, the glorification of God\u8217's name implied by the verse \u8220"this is my God and I will glorify Him.\u8221" Writing the divine name by tracing over an improperly intended prior inscription is simply not dignified enough \u8212- the name ends up as a piggyback on a name that was not created with the proper sanctity, and the whole of {\i shem Hashem} was not formed {\i lishmah} from the outset. That specific demand of dignity and glorification may not apply to the {\i lishmah} of a {\i get}, and therefore {\i aval hacha lo} \u8212- here, perhaps everyone would agree that overwriting with {\i lishmah} intention is valid.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafot Missing Component\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} notes a tension with an earlier discussion in the sugya. Earlier, the {\i Gemara} considered whether witnesses could effectively sign a {\i get} by tracing over letters that already bore their names, written in a light ink, with a darker ink. There, the {\i Gemara} was hesitant to accept ink-over-ink as constituting a valid signature. Yet here, {\i Rav Chisda} treats overwriting as at least the position of {\i Rabbi Yehuda} and perhaps universally valid. How are these consistent?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} distinguishes the two cases as follows. In the case of the {\i get}\u8217's {\i lishmah}, the first writing \u8212- though physically correct \u8212- lacked the essential component of {\i lishmah}. Because {\i lishmah} was absent, the first writing in a certain sense did not fully constitute the act of writing required by the {\i get}. The second writing therefore comes to replace what was deficient with something properly accomplished; it creates something new, even though the letters look physically identical before and after. The earlier case of witness signatures is categorically different: the underlying writing was fully valid in every respect. There was no missing component. The act of writing the signature had already been completed \u8212- nothing was lacking. A second tracing of identical letters over a complete prior writing adds nothing, for nothing was absent to be supplied.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One could alternatively frame the distinction thus: in the signature case, the problem was whether the act of writing had occurred at all, and since writing had already occurred, the second pass added no new act. Here, the problem is not whether writing occurred but whether {\i lishmah} is present \u8212- and perhaps {\i lishmah} can be introduced by an act of rewriting even when the physical substrate already exists, precisely because {\i lishmah} is not the act of writing itself but a quality that can be inserted into an already-written text.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rav Chisda\u8217's Concern\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Invalidate All Gittin\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now records a remarkable theoretical statement. {\i Rav Chisda} says: {\i yachilna lemipsal lechol gittei de\u8217'alma} \u8212- I could construct an argument to invalidate every {\i get} in the world. He presents this as a theoretical observation, not a practical ruling. {\i Rava} asks: what is the argument?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Woman\u8217's Payment Issue\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rav Chisda}\u8217's first suggestion: the Torah says the husband should write the {\i get} \u8212- {\i ve\u8217'chasav} \u8212- but in practice it is the woman who pays the {\i sofer}\u8217's fees. If she is the one commissioning and paying the scribe, one might argue the scribe is functionally her agent, not the husband\u8217's, undermining the requirement that the husband be the effective author. {\i Rava} dismisses this: {\i vedilma aknoiyei aknu lei rabanan} \u8212- the {\i Rabbanan} may have established that even though the wife pays, the fee is legally assigned to the husband, so that the {\i sofer} is treated as an agent of the husband. The practical arrangement of payment does not determine the halakhic attribution of agency.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Material Value & Issurei\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The second possible argument: the Torah says {\i ve\u8217'natan} \u8212- he must {\i give} it to her \u8212- and perhaps this implies that the object given must have monetary value, at least a {\i perutah}. Yet the {\i mishna} permits writing a {\i get} on a leaf or other minimal-value material. Perhaps the {\i get} is therefore not a proper act of giving.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Issurei Hana\u8217'ah Valid\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} rejects this line of reasoning by citing a ruling sent from the West, from {\i Eretz Yisrael}: {\i ketavo al issurei hana\u8217'ah kasher} \u8212- a {\i get} written on an object from which benefit is forbidden is valid. This is a striking ruling. An object of {\i issurei hana\u8217'ah} has, by definition, no halakhic monetary value \u8212- indeed, there is a fundamental debate in the {\i poskim} as to whether such objects can even be considered property in a meaningful legal sense. Yet the {\i get} is valid. This demonstrates that {\i ve\u8217'natan} does not require a transfer of monetary ownership at all. The obligation is the physical act of handing the object, {\i netinat get}, not the conveyance of an item of value. The {\i get} belongs to a different conceptual framework from ordinary {\i kinyanim} \u8212- acquisition is not the operative act; the physical delivery is.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rav Ashi} notes that this principle might already be derivable from the {\i mishna} itself, which permits writing on an olive leaf. The {\i Gemara} responds that an olive leaf is still worth something \u8212- in aggregate, many such leaves have value, and a single leaf is therefore not literally worthless. An object of {\i issurei hana\u8217'ah} is a categorically more extreme case, and the ruling from {\i Eretz Yisrael} is therefore a genuine {\i chiddush}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Levi\u8217's Announcement\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} records a {\i beraita}: {\i Rebbi} said that writing a {\i get} on {\i issurei hana\u8217'ah} is {\i kasher}. {\i Levi} went out to publicize this ruling {\i mishmeih de\u8217'Rebbi}, and the people did not praise it \u8212- presumably because they were uncomfortable accepting it as the position of a single authority. He then taught it {\i mishmei d\u8217'rabbanan} \u8212- as the consensus of the Sages \u8212- and it was praised. {\i Amar hilchata ke\u8217'vassai} \u8212- from this we derive that this is the operative {\i halacha}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rashi} understands that we learn the {\i psak} from {\i Levi}\u8217's own behavior: his insistence on teaching it as consensus opinion, and his discomfort with its being received as a {\i da\u8217'at yachid}, reflects his own conviction that it was the correct {\i psak}, and the {\i Gemara} accepts his determination. {\i Tosafot} disagrees: we learn from the audience\u8217's reaction. When the ruling was presented as a lone opinion they were not opposed to its substance \u8212- they simply disliked it being framed as a {\i da\u8217'at yachid}. When {\i Levi} reframed it as consensus, they accepted it enthusiastically, demonstrating that the community of scholars recognized it as the accepted {\i halacha}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Writing: Ink vs.\u160 ?Carving\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Writing Not Engraving\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now turns to the fundamental requirement of {\i ketivah} \u8212- writing \u8212- in the context of a {\i get}. A {\i beraita} teaches: {\i ve\u8217'chasav ve\u8217'lo chakak} \u8212- the verse says \u8220"he shall write,\u8221" not \u8220"he shall engrave.\u8221" {\i Chikuk} \u8212- engraving or carving \u8212- is not {\i ketivah}. The underlying distinction is clear: writing involves applying a substance to a surface to form letters; engraving involves carving away material, neither adding ink nor producing letters through deposition. The mode of creation is fundamentally different.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tablets Engraving\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} immediately challenges this: {\i eved she\u8217'yatza bi\u8217'ktav she\u8217'al gabei tavla u\u8217'pinkas yatza le\u8217'cheirus} \u8212- a slave who received a {\i shtar shichrur} written on a tablet or {\i pinkas} goes free. These tablets \u8212- typically wood surfaced with wax \u8212- were written on with a stylus, a process that is inherently a form of {\i chakika}, engraving into the wax rather than depositing ink. Yet such a document is valid. Furthermore, the {\i beraita} specifies that writing on a {\i kipa} or {\i anduchtri} \u8212- some form of a garment \u8212- with sewn or attached letters is {\i not} valid. {\i Rashi} understands this as letters that have been cut out separately and affixed to the surface. The problem is not that they are engraved but that they are not integrated into the medium \u8212- the letters are merely incidentally attached and can be removed without disturbing the surface itself. This implies that the problem with the garment case is the lack of integration, not the mode of inscription, and therefore that {\i chakika} per se on a proper surface is acceptable.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chok Tocho & Yerechot\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Ulla} in the name of {\i Rabbi Elazar} resolves the contradiction by distinguishing between two types of carving. {\i Chak tochot} \u8212- carving out the interior space \u8212- means that rather than tracing the shape of the letter with the stylus, one removes the material {\i surrounding} the letter, so that the letter is left standing raised. {\i Chak yerachot} \u8212- carving the legs, i.e., the letter-form itself \u8212- means the stylus traces the actual shape of the letter directly, carving into the surface to form the letter\u8217's contours.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The critical distinction: in {\i chak yerachot}, the act of the hand traces the shape of the letter itself, analogously to the act of writing with ink. The instrument is working directly on the letter. In {\i chak tochot}, the instrument works on the space around the letter; the letter itself is never directly acted upon \u8212- it merely remains as what was not carved away. It follows that {\i chak yerachot} is {\i ketivah}, while {\i chak tochot} is not.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This distinction is also operative in laws of {\i Sefer Torah}. If a drop of ink fell into the interior of a {\i chet}, creating a solid rectangle rather than two legs joined by a roof, and one fixes it by scraping away the ink from the interior \u8212- the {\i chet} has been created by {\i chak tochot}, working on the interior space rather than forming the letter itself, and the letter is therefore invalid.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tzitz Protruding\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now challenges the ruling that {\i chak tochot} is invalid. The {\i tzitz} \u8212- the frontplate worn by the {\i Kohen Gadol} \u8212- bore the inscription {\i Kodesh l\u8217'Hashem}, and the {\i Gemara} states: {\i lo haya ketavo shoke\u8217'a ela bolet ke\u8217'dinrei zahav} \u8212- the letters were not sunken/engraved into the surface; rather they protruded, like the letters on a gold {\i dinar}. But the {\i tzitz} is described with the verb {\i ve\u8217'chatavta alav} \u8212- \u8220"you shall write upon it\u8221" \u8212- so the protruding letters must constitute {\i ketivah}. How? If letters protrude because the material {\i around} them has been pushed down or carved away, that is {\i chak tochot}, which we have said is not {\i ketivah}. And the analogy to {\i dinrei zahav} \u8212- minted gold coins \u8212- is itself problematic: coins are produced by pressing the material around the image down into a mold, so that the image remains raised \u8212- a paradigmatic case of {\i chak tochot}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} answers: {\i ke\u8217'dinrei zahav ve\u8217'lo ke\u8217'dinrei zahav} \u8212- like {\i dinrei zahav} in that the letters protrude, but not like {\i dinrei zahav} in method. For coins, {\i de\u8217'ilu hatam tochot} \u8212- the protruding letters result from pushing down the surrounding material, which is {\i chak tochot}. For the {\i tzitz}, {\i ve\u8217'hacha yerachot} \u8212- the protruding letters were formed by engraving the letter-form itself from the {\i back} of the plate. When one engraves the letter from the reverse side, it causes the corresponding area on the front to protrude. The act of the hand was tracing the shape of the letter \u8212- {\i chak yerachot} \u8212- even though the visible result on the front is a raised letter rather than a sunken one. Working from the back, one is still directly forming the letter-shape, which constitutes {\i ketivah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This does raise a natural question: when engraving from the rear, one is working on the letter in reverse, relating to the mirror image of its form. The {\i Gemara} does not treat this as a disqualification, implicitly holding that working directly on the letter-form \u8212- even in reverse from behind \u8212- is a more genuine act of {\i ketivah} than working on the surrounding space from the front.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Coins Minting\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Ravina} then raises a precise question to {\i Rav Ashi}: {\i rushma michratz charitz o kinufay michnaf} \u8212- when a metal-pressing die is used to mint a coin, what conceptually is occurring? Does the die push {\i down} the material around the image \u8212- carving out the space, which would be {\i chak tochot} \u8212- with the raised areas simply remaining as they were? Or does it push {\i in} the material that will become the raised image \u8212- pressing the metal into the indentations in the mold, so that one is effectively working on the letter-areas themselves? The conceptual answer matters for how we classify the act.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rav Ashi} answers: {\i michratz charitz} \u8212- the die pushes down the surrounding material. The raised letters remain raised not because they were pushed up but because the space around them was pushed down. This is {\i chak tochot} and, for purposes of {\i ketivah}, is not considered writing. This is consistent with everything said until now.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yerechot is Writing\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} then raises the {\i tzitz} passage once more \u8212- the same challenge, the same answer. {\i Ve\u8217'lo ke\u8217'dinrei zahav de\u8217'ilu hatam migavai ve\u8217'hacha mibarai} \u8212- coins are made by working the die from the front, pushing down the surroundings; the {\i tzitz} was worked from the back, tracing the letter-form itself.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The conclusions of the sugya: a {\i get} does not require ink in the conventional sense \u8212- the physical deposit of a marking substance on a surface. Engraving without any liquid substance can constitute {\i ketivah}, provided the act of engraving directly traces the form of the letters themselves ({\i chak yerachot}). If instead the space around the letters is what is worked upon, leaving the letter raised by default ({\i chak tochot}), this is not {\i ketivah}. And sewing separately-cut letters onto a garment is likewise invalid, because the letters are not integrated into their background medium \u8212- they exist independently and are merely attached. The question of whether embroidered letters, sewn into the fabric of a garment by threading through it, would be more analogous to {\i chak yerachot} \u8212- as the needle traces the letter-form directly \u8212- or would suffer from the same lack of surface-integration as affixed cut-out letters, remains a question the {\i Gemara} leaves to further reflection.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Gittin 21\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Value vs.\u160 ?Transfer\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gold Plate Get\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The daf opens with a question posed by Rava to Rav Nachman: what is the {\i halacha} in a case where a husband wrote a {\i get} on a gold plate \u8212- a {\i tass} of gold, some kind of gold foil \u8212- and then said to his wife, {\i hiskabli giteich v\u8217'hiskabli kesuvasech}, \u8220"receive your {\i get} and receive your {\i kesuva}\u8221"? The value of the gold on which the {\i get} is written is substantial, and the husband is proposing that this very value constitutes payment of her {\i kesuva}. What is the {\i halakha}? Is it a valid {\i get}, and does the value of the gold discharge the {\i kesuva} obligation?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The question turns on an issue already in play from the previous daf. The giving of a {\i get} requires a physical transfer of the object. But what exactly is the nature of that transfer \u8212- is it about the object\u8217's monetary value being conveyed, or is it simply the physical handing over of the thing? We established previously that even a leaf of an olive tree, worth less than a {\i perutah}, or even an item that is {\i issurei hana\u8217'ah} \u8212- something from which benefit is forbidden, which may not even be capable of being owned \u8212- nonetheless constitutes a valid {\i get} when physically handed over. That being so, if value is irrelevant to the act of giving a {\i get}, why can\u8217't the value of the gold {\i get} simultaneously serve as payment of the {\i kesuva}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafot on Object\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot addresses this directly. He acknowledges that we write a {\i get} on {\i issurei hana\u8217'ah} and it is valid \u8212- so value is clearly not the defining feature. Why, then, should attributing value to the {\i get} pose any problem? Tosafot answers: {\i hai mishum d\u8217'yehav la miah kol ha-get} \u8212- in the standard case, there is only one thing happening: the object is being given over entirely as a {\i get}. But here, {\i lo yehav la ela tofsa} \u8212- if you have a ten-thousand-dollar {\i get} and the focus is on the monetary value, the act of conveyance begins to look like the transfer of a valuable object rather than the giving of a {\i get}. The concern is that the nature of the act may be redefined by the monetary dimension. Tosafot ultimately concludes, however, that both things can be happening simultaneously \u8212- the physical giving constitutes the {\i get} and the taking of possession of the valuable object constitutes payment of the {\i kesuva}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Margins Teaching\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} then introduces a {\i baraita} that raises a related question. Suppose the husband says to his wife: {\i hiskabli giteich v\u8217'hashar l\u8217'kesuvasech} \u8212- \u8220"receive your {\i get}, and the remainder goes toward your {\i kesuva}.\u8221" That is, the {\i get} was written on a piece of gold, and the margins \u8212- the portions not covered by the writing \u8212- should serve as {\i kesuva} payment. This arrangement is ruled valid: it is a good {\i get} and the margins discharge the {\i kesuva}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} infers from this that {\i taima d\u8217'ika shar} \u8212- the reason it works is precisely because there are margins. This implies that if the area of the {\i get} itself were to pay off the {\i kesuva}, that would not work \u8212- it would redefine the nature of the act. But the {\i Gemara} immediately rejects this inference: {\i hu hadin af al gav d\u8217'leka shar} \u8212- even with no margins at all, even if the entire {\i get} also serves as the {\i kesuva} payment, it is valid. The reason the {\i baraita} mentioned the case with margins is to teach something else entirely: that even when there are margins, the husband cannot unilaterally retain them. If he says nothing, {\i ada\u8217'ata d\u8217'megilta hu} \u8212- the margins are considered part of the scroll as a unified object, and she is entitled to the whole thing. Only if he explicitly specifies, before the giving, that the margins are to be credited toward the {\i kesuva}, does he retain that claim.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Possession Principle\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What emerges from these discussions is a consistent underlying principle: the giving of a {\i get} is not governed by the logic of {\i kinyan} \u8212- of acquiring monetary ownership. The {\i get} can be worth less than a {\i perutah}, can be worth nothing, and by some opinions, an item of {\i issurei hana\u8217'ah} may not even be capable of being owned at all. Nevertheless, the physical transfer of the {\i get} is valid. So complete is the separation between the act of giving the {\i get} and the logic of {\i dinei mamonot} that two entirely distinct acts can occur simultaneously: the physical giving of the {\i get} constitutes the divorce, while the taking of possession of the same object constitutes payment of the {\i kesuva}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Paper Ownership\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Husband Retains Paper\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now turns to a {\i baraita} that directly tests the limits of this principle. If a husband says {\i harei ze giteich v\u8217'haniyar sheli} \u8212- \u8220"here is your {\i get}, but the paper is mine\u8221" \u8212- she is not divorced. This is qualitatively different from the case of {\i issurei hana\u8217'ah}. In the {\i issurei hana\u8217'ah} case, no one can own the paper, and yet it is fully in her possession; he has no claim to it. Here, by asserting ownership, the husband is in effect not fully giving the object over. The retention of ownership means the transfer is incomplete.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Conditional Return\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 By contrast, if he says {\i al menat she-tachziri li es haniyar} \u8212- \u8220"on the condition that you return the paper to me\u8221" \u8212- she is divorced. The giving is complete. She takes full possession of the {\i get}; she simply has a future obligation to return the paper. This is structurally like a {\i matana al menat lehachzir} \u8212- a conditional gift \u8212- in which the act of giving is whole and unimpaired even though the object must eventually be returned. The {\i get} is fully under her control at the moment of giving, and she is therefore divorced.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 White Space Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Pappa then poses a more refined question: what if the husband says he is retaining not the paper on which the letters are written, but only the white space between the words and between the lines \u8212- {\i bein shita l\u8217'shita, bein teiva l\u8217'teiva}? Is she divorced?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 On the one hand, perhaps what he is retaining is not really the {\i get} itself, since she receives full possession of everything the letters are written on. On the other hand, if one were to physically remove the paper he is claiming \u8212- all the white space surrounding the letters \u8212- what would remain would be tiny, disconnected scraps. Can that truly be called a {\i get}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Teiku on Letters\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} leaves this as {\i teiku} \u8212- unresolved.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} considers whether this should be obvious from the rule that a {\i get} must be a single, unified document \u8212- {\i safra echad Rachmana, v\u8217'lo shnayim u\u8217'shlosha sfarim} \u8212- and not multiple pieces. But it answers: {\i lo tzricha dime\u8217'ura} \u8212- the case is one in which the letters are written in an interwoven fashion, such that removing the claimed white space would still leave the letters physically connected. For example, a {\i lamed} from one line might extend into the space below, connecting with the next line, so that even after excision of the \u8220"white space,\u8221" the document would remain one physical unit.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The difficulty with this answer, however, is not resolved neatly. If the document is truly constructed so that it would remain whole even after the husband\u8217's space is removed, it is not clear why it should not simply be valid. And if it cannot remain whole, it is not clear why the {\i Gemara} even entertains the possibility of validity. This is, in truth, a puzzling {\i teiku}, and it is not entirely clear what the {\i Gemara} finds genuinely uncertain.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Interwoven Letters\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rashi explains {\i dime\u8217'ura} as meaning that the letters were intentionally written long so as to reach from line to line at the head of each word \u8212- {\i otiyot arukot hamagi\u8217'ot mishita l\u8217'shita b\u8217'rosh kol teiva v\u8217'teiva} \u8212- thereby creating physical connection throughout the document. On Rashi\u8217's reading, the construction is comprehensive enough to ensure continuity.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Ribam} \u8212- Rabbeinu Yitzchak bar Mordechai \u8212- draws a different conclusion. He argues there is no proof here, because {\i dime\u8217'ura} refers only to cases where letters from one line intrude naturally into the space of the adjacent line \u8212- a {\i regel kaf} of an upper line into the space of the line below, or a {\i lamed} into a {\i heh} or {\i chet} above it \u8212- not to letters that are actually touching one another. Moreover, the {\i Ribam} argues, there is no proof that letters touching is required in a {\i get} at all; that requirement may be specific to a {\i Sefer Torah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot, meanwhile, derives a broader principle from this passage: since the {\i Gemara} discusses the case of {\i dime\u8217'ura} \u8212- interwoven letters \u8212- and treats it as the operative scenario where the question becomes meaningful, it follows that the letters must actually be touching for the concern to be meaningful. The {\i Ribam} disputes this, maintaining that touching letters are not required, and that the concern arises simply from the structural configuration described. The question of whether letters in a {\i get} must have white space around them, or whether touching letters invalidate it, is left as a live debate between these {\i rishonim}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Slave as Medium\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Slave Holding Get\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rami Bar Chama poses the following question: it was known that a certain slave belonged to the husband; a {\i get} was written on the slave\u8217's arm; and now the slave is found in the possession of the wife \u8212- {\i harei hu yotze mitachas yada}. What is the {\i halakha}? Is she divorced?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The question is whether we say {\i akneiye aknei lah} \u8212- that the husband formally transferred the slave to her \u8212- or {\i dilma hu minafshei azal} \u8212- perhaps the slave simply walked over of his own accord, since slaves are capable of independent movement, and her physical possession of the slave proves nothing about whether it was deliberately given over as a {\i get}. This seems, on its face, to be a general question of evidentiary standards in ownership, not a {\i get}-specific question.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tattooing Objection\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava interjects with a prior objection: before we even reach the question of whether the slave was transferred, the {\i get} written in ink on the slave\u8217's arm is susceptible to forgery \u8212- it could be erased and rewritten. That disqualifies it as a valid document, regardless of how it was transferred.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} responds: this is {\i kashia matnitin} \u8212- how does Rava explain the {\i Mishna}, which explicitly permits writing a {\i get} on a slave\u8217's hand? Rava answers: the {\i Mishna} is referring to a case of {\i eidei mesira} \u8212- witnesses present at the moment of transfer, who can testify to what was written. This is consistent with Rabbi Elazar\u8217's position. In such a case, the document\u8217's susceptibility to forgery is irrelevant because the witnesses themselves attest to the content and the transfer.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tattooed Resolution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Returning to Rami Bar Chama\u8217's question: since his case involves no witnesses to the transfer \u8212- the entire question is whether the slave\u8217's presence in her possession constitutes proof \u8212- the problem of forgery is real. The {\i Gemara} resolves this by stipulating that the case involves {\i biksivat ka\u8217'aka} \u8212- a tattoo. A tattooed {\i get} cannot be forged. The document is therefore valid and not subject to the forgery concern.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot notes that tattooing involves a potential {\i issur de\u8217'oraisa} only when performed with certain specific inks, as described in the third chapter of {\i Makkot}, and that even an {\i issur de\u8217'rabbanan} may attach in some configurations. However, Tosafot concludes that even if an {\i issur de\u8217'oraisa} were involved, it would not prevent the tattooed inscription from functioning as a valid {\i get}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Flocks Rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The underlying question \u8212- whether the slave\u8217's presence in her possession is sufficient proof that it was transferred \u8212- is addressed by the principle stated by Resh Lakish: {\i ha-godot ein lahem chazakah} \u8212- flocks have no presumption of ownership based on possession alone. An animal that moves on its own can arrive in your yard without having been transferred. Accordingly, possession of a moving creature does not establish ownership the way possession of a static object does. For flocks \u8212- and similarly for slaves \u8212- one establishes ownership either through three years of uncontested possession or through a bill of sale.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Slave Possession\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot raises the question: if this rule is well established, what was the {\i safek} \u8212- the doubt \u8212- that prompted Rami Bar Chama\u8217's question? The answer Tosafot offers is that perhaps the combination of physical possession {\i and} the presence of a {\i get} written on the slave should together serve as proof that it was deliberately given over. But that argument ultimately fails, since the {\i halacha} of {\i ein lahem chazakah} is decisive.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 There is, however, a more analytically interesting dimension to this question. We have established throughout this daf that the giving of a {\i get} does not require the kind of monetary transfer recognized by {\i dinei mamonot}. It can be given on {\i issurei hana\u8217'ah}, objects that may not be capable of conventional ownership. If that is so, perhaps the {\i standards of proof} for a {\i get} are also distinct from the standards of {\i dinei mamonot}. One might argue that even if physical possession of a slave does not prove ownership in {\i dinei mamonot}, it might nonetheless constitute sufficient evidence that she received the {\i get}, since for {\i get} purposes we are not operating within the framework of {\i Choshen Mishpat} but of {\i Even HaEzer}. The act of transfer might not need to mirror a monetary {\i kinyan}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Conversely, one could argue the reverse: that even if after three years she could establish ownership of the slave under {\i dinei mamonot}, that still would not retroactively prove that the {\i get} was given to her at the outset, since the evidence of {\i get} transfer must be apparent from the moment of giving \u8212- not established through a process that takes years to complete.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara}\u8217's resolution via {\i ein lahem chazakah} seems to cut across both frameworks, essentially holding that the slave was not even genuinely \u8220"in her possession\u8221" in any legally cognizable sense, and the question therefore fails on its own terms.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Sefer Makneh\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tablet Ownership\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rami Bar Chama poses a related question: it was known that a woman owned a particular {\i tavla} \u8212- a tablet \u8212- and a {\i get} was written on it. Now the husband is found holding it. What is the {\i halakha}? Can the {\i get} be valid?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The scenario is that she gave him the tablet specifically so he could write a {\i get} on it and give it back to her. But for a {\i get} to be valid, the husband must own the document \u8212- the principle of {\i sefer makneh} requires that the document be owned by the party transferring it. The question is whether she genuinely transferred ownership to him, or whether, since she expected to receive it back, her intention was never a full and complete transfer of ownership. This is analogous to the classic question of whether a bride can give her groom a family heirloom ring for him to give back to her under the {\i chuppa} \u8212- is the initial giving considered a real transfer if the return is anticipated from the outset?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Woman\u8217's Knowledge\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye frames the uncertainty differently: {\i lo yad\u8217'ah la\u8217'akneye} \u8212- perhaps a person in this situation simply does not know that they are legally required to fully transfer ownership of the object even when expecting it back. The question is not only one of intent but of awareness.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Elder Lender Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye then invokes a precedent: the testimony of Rabbi Yehuda ben Bava regarding a certain small village near Jerusalem, where there was one elder who would lend money to all the village residents. He would write the loan documents in his own handwriting \u8212- {\i bichtav yado} \u8212- and other people would sign as witnesses. This practice was brought before the {\i Chachamim} and validated.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} asks: how does this work? The concept of {\i sefer makneh} requires that the document be owned by the party from whom it passes. In a loan transaction, it is the borrower who must ultimately convey the document to the lender. But here, the lender was preparing all the documents himself. The answer implied by the {\i Gemara} is that the elder would prepare the document, hand it to the borrower, have witnesses sign it, and the borrower would then hand it back. Even though the borrower knew the document was coming back to the lender, the act of taking possession and returning it constitutes a valid transfer. We therefore conclude: {\i akneiye aknei lehu} \u8212- we presume that someone can and does fully transfer an object even knowing it will be returned to them.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafot Distinction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot questions whether {\i sefer makneh} truly applies to a loan document, as opposed to a document effecting a transfer of property. Tosafot\u8217's distinction is that the formal requirement of {\i sefer makneh} \u8212- which derives from a {\i pasuk} \u8212- applies to a {\i shtar} that itself operates as the instrument of transfer or change of status, such as a deed of sale or a {\i get}. A {\i shtar raya} \u8212- a document that merely serves as evidence that a transaction occurred, such as a simple loan record \u8212- may not carry the same formal requirement regarding which party must own the physical document, since it is the testimony of the witnesses, not the object itself, that does the legal work. Tosafot therefore suggests that the elder lender was also handling property transfer documents for his borrowers, not merely loan records, and those documents would indeed be subject to {\i sefer makneh}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The plain reading of the {\i Gemara}, however, seems to apply {\i sefer makneh} across the board \u8212- whether the {\i shtar} effects a change or merely records evidence \u8212- requiring in all cases that the document be owned by the party conveying it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Co-signer Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava challenges whether the precedent of the elder lender proves anything about a woman\u8217's knowledge and intent. Perhaps the elder was different \u8212- {\i zaken shani de-yada l\u8217'akneye} \u8212- he was the professional, the literate man of business, the one who arranged all the transactions in the village. It is reasonable to assume he knew precisely what legal formalities were required. One cannot extrapolate from his conduct to a case involving a woman who may not be versed in these requirements.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Ashi therefore seeks a better precedent: the case of an {\i arev ha-yotze l\u8217'achar chitum shtarot} \u8212- a guarantor who signs a document after it has already been sealed and handed over to the lender. If I borrowed money from someone, signed a document and gave it over, and then a guarantor subsequently comes and signs it while the document is already in the lender\u8217's possession, this is a valid {\i halacha} under which the lender may collect from the guarantor. But how does that work? The guarantor\u8217's signature can only create a {\i shtar} that functions against his property if the document was in his possession and conveyed to the lender \u8212- the logic of {\i sefer makneh} again. The only way this works is if the lender gave the document back to the guarantor so that the guarantor could sign it and formally re-convey it. And we trust that this is what happened, even without witnesses to the intermediate step of conveyance back and forth. The guarantor knew what he needed to do and did it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Woman Writes Get\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava challenges this precedent as well: {\i gavra shani de-yada l\u8217'akneye} \u8212- a man, presumably familiar with business dealings, knows that he must formally transfer and reconvey the document. That tells us nothing about whether a woman understands the same requirement.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Ashi then cites a final and decisive source: {\i isha kotevet et gittah} \u8212- a woman may write her own {\i get}. The {\i Mishna} teaches that a woman can be appointed as a {\i shaliach} to write the {\i get}, and {\i v\u8217'ish kotev et shovro} \u8212- and the husband can write his own receipt acknowledging payment of the {\i kesuva}. These are both valid. The critical point is that {\i she-ein kiyum ha-get ela b\u8217'chotmav} \u8212- the {\i get} only becomes effective once it is signed by witnesses.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot anticipates an objection: perhaps {\i isha kotevet et gittah} simply means she does the writing on whatever material is at hand, and proves nothing about whether she knows to formally transfer ownership of her own parchment. But the {\i Gemara}\u8217's language is {\i stam} \u8212- unqualified \u8212- and therefore must be understood to include even a case where the parchment belongs to her. This means that even when the parchment is hers, we presume she knows to transfer it fully to her husband so that he can then give it back to her as a {\i get}. The {\i chiddush} is not merely that she can hold a quill, but that she is credited with knowing and executing the formal ownership transfer required by {\i sefer makneh}, even without anyone explicitly instructing her to do so.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 All of this bears directly on the practical question of a ring given by a bride to a groom for use under the {\i chuppa}. The basic answer that emerges from the {\i Gemara} is that such a transfer is valid \u8212- the initial giving is recognized as a complete and legally cognizable act of transfer, even when the giver knows the object is coming back.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Indirect Transfer\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Get+Slave+Field\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava now poses a striking case. A husband wrote a {\i get} and placed it in the hand of his slave, and then wrote a {\i shtar matana} \u8212- a deed of gift \u8212- giving the slave to his wife. She takes possession of the {\i shtar matana} on the slave, thereby acquiring the slave. Does this constitute a valid {\i get}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This question probes the fundamental nature of {\i nesinat get} \u8212- the act of giving a {\i get}. Throughout this daf we have established that the giving does not require a {\i kinyan} in the {\i dinei mamonot} sense. But here the scenario is more radical: there was no direct act of giving the {\i get} to the wife at all. The {\i get} was placed in the hand of the slave; the slave was given to the wife; and she receives the {\i get} only indirectly \u8212- through taking possession of the slave who is holding it. One could argue that this is all one unified package \u8212- he is giving her the slave along with everything the slave holds, and the whole gift constitutes the act of {\i nesinat get}. Or one could argue that the act of giving the {\i get} and the act of taking possession are here so completely separated and indirect that it cannot be called a {\i nesinat get} at all.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Moving Chatzer\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} accepts that this constitutes a valid {\i nesinat get} but raises a different concern: {\i ve-amai chatzer mehalechet hi} \u8212- is this not a {\i chatzer mehalechet} \u8212- a moving {\i chatzer}? The principle of {\i kinyan chatzer} holds that just as a woman\u8217's {\i chatzer} \u8212- her field or domain \u8212- can acquire a {\i get} on her behalf when it is placed therein, so too objects within her domain become hers. But a slave, because he moves of his own accord, is considered a {\i chatzer mehalechet} \u8212- a moving {\i chatzer} \u8212- and {\i chatzer mehalechet lo kana}, a moving {\i chatzer} does not effect acquisition. So even if we grant that the act of giving the slave with the {\i get} is a valid {\i nesinat get}, she may not actually acquire the piece of paper with the {\i get} written on it, because it is in the hand of a moving {\i chatzer}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} considers the counterargument that the slave happened to be standing still at that moment \u8212- {\i bein omed}. Rava\u8217's rule, however, forecloses this: {\i kol she\u8217'ilu mahalech lo kana, omed ve\u8217'yoshev lo kana} \u8212- anything that would not effect a {\i kinyan} when moving does not effect a {\i kinyan} when standing still either. The category of {\i chatzer mehalechet} is definitional, not positional. The resolution is: {\i v\u8217'hu she-kafus} \u8212- the slave is physically bound, and a bound slave is in a different category.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Get in Field\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava then formulates the simpler version of the case: he wrote a {\i get}, placed it in his {\i chatzer} \u8212- his field \u8212- and then wrote a {\i shtar matana} giving the field to his wife. She takes possession of the field, and thereby acquires the {\i get} within it. This too, the {\i Gemara} confirms, is valid: {\i k\u8217'natahu u-mitgarshet bo}. The {\i chiddush} here is not about {\i chatzer mehalechet} at all \u8212- a field is entirely stable \u8212- but about something more fundamental: the act of giving the {\i get} is being achieved indirectly, through the giving of the field that contains it. This is one unified package that constitutes an act of {\i nesinat get}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Field vs Slave\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot raises a pointed question: if the field case works, why did Rava need to state the slave case at all? And conversely, if the slave case works, why state the field case? The {\i Gemara} answers that both cases are necessary: {\i utzricha}. Had we only learned from the slave case, we might have thought the reason it works is something specific to a slave, but a {\i chatzer} would be subject to the concern about a {\i chatzer haba\u8217'ah l\u8217'achar mikan} \u8212- a {\i chatzer} that comes into her possession only after the {\i get} was placed there, perhaps through a third-party intermediary \u8212- and we might not allow it. And had we only learned from the {\i chatzer} case, we might have thought the reason it works is the stability of a field, but we would not allow a slave even when bound, lest we come to allow an unbound slave. Each case therefore teaches something the other does not.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rashi explains {\i chatzer haba\u8217'ah l\u8217'achar mikan} as referring to a scenario where the husband places the {\i get} in his yard, sells the yard to a third party, and the third party subsequently sells it to the wife. That obviously cannot constitute a {\i nesinat get}, since there is no act of giving from husband to wife. The concern is that if we allow the basic case, people will confuse it with this impermissible scenario.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Active Participation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The deeper {\i chiddush} of Rava\u8217's cases \u8212- both slave and field \u8212- is now brought into relief. The standard principle of {\i nesinat get} is that it is a unilateral act: the husband gives and it is effective regardless of whether the wife agrees, actively participates, or even knows. A {\i get} thrown into her {\i chatzer} divorces her without her consent. But in Rava\u8217's case, the wife must actively participate: she must agree to receive the gift of the field or the slave. Without her willing acceptance of the {\i matana}, she acquires nothing and there is no {\i get}. This is fundamentally different from the standard unilateral act of giving a {\i get}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shlichut Parallel\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye raises exactly this challenge: {\i chatzer meheicha itrabai} \u8212- from where do we derive that a {\i chatzer} works as a mechanism for {\i get}? It is derived from {\i yadah} \u8212- \u8220"her hand.\u8221" Just as her hand receives the {\i get} regardless of her consent, so too her {\i chatzer} must receive it regardless of consent. {\i Mah yadah de\u8217'isa bein meda\u8217'atah bein be\u8217'al korchah, af chatzeirah de\u8217'isa bein meda\u8217'atah bein be\u8217'al korchah} \u8212- the {\i chatzer} analogy to the hand requires that it function even without her agreement. But in Rava\u8217's case of the gift, {\i meda\u8217'atah isa, be\u8217'al korchah leisa} \u8212- it only works with her consent, not without it. It is therefore of a fundamentally different character.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Ashi responds with the case of {\i shlichut lekabbala} \u8212- a woman\u8217's agent appointed to receive her {\i get} on her behalf. This too requires her active participation: she must appoint the agent, and presumably if she revokes her consent during the process the {\i shlichut} lapses. Yet it is fully valid as an act of receiving a {\i get}. So active participation does not automatically preclude validity.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye replies that {\i shlichut} is derived from a separate {\i parsha} \u8212- {\i ve-shalach ve-shilchah} \u8212- not from the analogy to {\i yad}. The laws of {\i shlichut} operate under their own derivation and framework. A field is not a {\i shaliach}; it is supposed to be an extension of her {\i yad}, and if it requires her active agreement to acquire the field, it cannot function as an extension of her hand receiving the {\i get}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One can also conceptualize the distinction as follows: in {\i shlichut}, the woman sets up the arrangement in advance, and once done, the actual act of giving the {\i get} to the agent is still essentially unilateral. But in Rava\u8217's case, the act of giving cannot reach her at all without her active participation at the very moment of transfer. This is a categorically different kind of involvement.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} then considers a further answer: {\i shlichut lekabbala nami ashkachan be\u8217'al korchah} \u8212- we find {\i shlichut lekabbala} that works even against the woman\u8217's will: a father may accept a {\i get} on behalf of his minor daughter even against her will \u8212- and Tosafot notes that it may even work against the father\u8217's own will. Since the institution of {\i shlichut lekabbala} functions in some cases entirely without consent, it operates validly even in the ordinary case where the woman does appoint her agent willingly.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot argues that this does not rehabilitate Rava\u8217's case, because the case of the {\i chatzer} is different: the woman can always, in theory, divest herself of her {\i chatzer}. There is always a theoretical opening through which she could undo the relationship. By contrast, the relationship of father to minor daughter is absolute and inescapable, which is why the institution of {\i shlichut} is understood to work even against all parties\u8217' will. That absolute form of the institution carries over to the voluntary case. But Rava\u8217's case involves an act where she must affirmatively participate at the moment of {\i netina}, and that remains a genuine {\i chiddush} for which the {\i Gemara} struggles to find a proper precedent or derivation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava\u8217's {\i chiddush} ultimately stands \u8212- the {\i Gemara} does not formally reject it \u8212- and the standard ruling follows Rava. But the challenge of Abaye remains analytically significant: giving a {\i get} is normally a unilateral act, and any mechanism that requires the wife\u8217's active agreement at the moment of transfer is a {\i chiddush} of considerable magnitude.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Materials Limits\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Horn of Cow\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} turns the page and addresses a question that flows naturally from the laws about writing a {\i get} on different surfaces. The {\i Mishna} had discussed writing on a slave\u8217's hand and giving over the slave. The {\i Gemara} asks: granted that one cannot cut off the slave\u8217's hand \u8212- that is prohibited \u8212- but if one wrote a {\i get} on the horn of a cow, why not simply cut off the horn and hand it to the wife? {\i Ve-leisva lah} \u8212- just give it to her!\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The answer is derived from the {\i pasuk}: {\i ve-kasav ve-nasan lah} \u8212- \u8220"and he shall write and give to her.\u8221" The verse teaches: {\i mi she-eino mechusar ela kesiva ve-nesina} \u8212- only something that requires only writing and giving. {\i Yatza zeh she-mechusar kesiva ketzitza ve-nesina} \u8212- excluded is something that requires writing, then cutting, then giving. The requirement is that the transition from the writing stage to the giving stage be direct and unmediated by an act of {\i ketzitza} \u8212- cutting or detachment.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yose vs Rabbanan\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yose HaGalili takes this further and rules that one may not write a {\i get} on a living creature at all \u8212- {\i baalei chayim} \u8212- not only that one may not cut from it. The {\i Gemara} proceeds to derive both his position and that of the {\i Rabbanan}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sefer Limits\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The derivation for Rabbi Yose HaGalili runs as follows. The {\i pasuk} says {\i ve-kasav lah sefer kritut} \u8212- \u8220"and he shall write for her a {\i sefer} of severance.\u8221" {\i Ein li ela sefer} \u8212- I would know only a scroll. {\i Minayin lerabbos kol davar} \u8212- how do I know to include any material at all? {\i Talmud lomar ve-kasav lah} \u8212- the word {\i ve-kasav}, \u8220"and he shall write,\u8221" teaches that writing on any material is valid. {\i Im kein mah talmud lomar sefer} \u8212- if so, what does the word {\i sefer} come to add? It must be teaching something. It teaches: {\i mah sefer davar she-ein bo ruach chayim ve-eino ochel} \u8212- just as a scroll is something that is not a living creature and is not food, so too any material used for a {\i get} must be neither living nor food.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot adds a brief but important methodological observation: the selection of which characteristic of a {\i sefer} to use as the defining criterion \u8212- not that it must be rectangular, or must be made from vegetation, but specifically that it is not alive and not food \u8212- is determined by the sages according to what is contextually most relevant. The sages determine, in each instance, what constitutes the meaningful point of comparison. That is not a mechanical derivation but a rabbinic judgment about contextual relevance.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ketiva Requirement\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 For the {\i Rabbanan}, who do not limit the material in the same way as Rabbi Yose HaGalili, the word {\i ve-kasav} serves a different purpose entirely. The {\i Gemara} asks: {\i mai avid leh} \u8212- what do the {\i Rabbanan} do with {\i ve-kasav}? They use it to teach that {\i bechtiva mitgarshet ve\u8217'eina mitgarshet bekesef} \u8212- she is divorced through writing, not through money. One might have thought \u8212- {\i salka da\u8217'atach amina} \u8212- that since marriage is effected through money, divorce could be effected through money as well, by analogy: {\i mah havaya bekesef, af yetzia nami bekesef}. The word {\i ve-kasav} therefore teaches that only a written document can effectuate a {\i get}, not a monetary conveyance.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Krit/Kritut\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The dispute then extends to the derivation of {\i kritut} \u8212- complete severance. Rabbi Yehuda derives from the word {\i sefer} itself that the physical material must be limited, and derives the requirement of complete severance from the word {\i kritut} as opposed to {\i krit}. {\i Sefer karata ve\u8217'ein davar acher karata} \u8212- only a {\i sefer} creates the divorce, not another thing. The {\i Rabbanan} derive the material limitation differently, using {\i sefer} for one purpose and {\i ve-kasav} for another.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What both agree upon is that the word {\i kritut} \u8212- or the contrast between {\i krit} and {\i kritut} \u8212- teaches the requirement of {\i davar ha-koret beino leiveinah} \u8212- something that creates complete severance between the husband and wife. This is illustrated by the {\i baraita}: if a husband says \u8220"here is your {\i get} on condition that you never drink wine\u8221" or \u8220"on condition that you never visit your father\u8217's house,\u8221" {\i ein ze kritut} \u8212- this is not a {\i get}, because the condition, being permanent, does not create a complete severance. But if the condition is for thirty days, {\i harei ze kritut} \u8212- it is valid, because the restriction is not permanent and the severance is therefore complete.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The question of what constitutes {\i mechusar ketzitza} \u8212- requiring an act of cutting that disqualifies the {\i get} \u8212- is left with some complexity. The {\i Gemara}\u8217's language implies that even a voluntary, unnecessary act of cutting from a major object would disqualify the {\i get}, not only a case where cutting was required. A trivial trimming of edges presents no issue. The serious case is where the {\i get} is cut away from a large piece of material. Tosafot suggests that this concern may be limited to cases where one cuts from something alive and growing \u8212- like a leaf from a tree or a horn from a cow \u8212- because that constitutes a major transformation of the material, from something organic and living to something inert. Cutting paper from paper, even from a very large sheet, may not rise to the same level of concern. Tosafot does not resolve this definitively, however, and leaves it as an open question whether a {\i get} cut from a large inanimate sheet would be valid.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Gittin 22\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Mechubar & Ketzitzah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Previous Materials\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The previous discussion established the range of materials on which a {\i get} may be written, culminating in the case of a {\i get} written on the horn of a cow or the arm of a slave. In the latter case, one cannot cut off the arm \u8212- one must give the entire slave. Yet the analysis raised a subtler point: even for the horn, which could in principle be cut off, the act of cutting that intervenes between the writing and the giving of the {\i get} seems to constitute a problem \u8212- {\i mechusar ketzitzah}, literally \u8220"lacking a cutting,\u8221" meaning that the required act of separation had not yet taken place at the time of writing.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} engaged with the question of what exactly counts as an invalidating act of cutting. Does trimming a margin from a larger sheet of paper constitute {\i ketzitzah}? {\i Tosafot} considers this trivial and not problematic. But when the {\i get} occupies a small piece of a very large sheet and must be cut away, {\i Tosafot} entertains a genuine debate about whether that separation invalidates. The more fundamental conclusion of {\i Tosafot} is that the only act of cutting that truly invalidates is one that is transformative in nature \u8212- separating something that was growing (plucking a leaf from a tree on which the {\i get} was written) or cutting a horn from an animal. These acts are qualitatively different from simple trimming. With this framing in mind, we turn to the {\i mishna} on 22b.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishna: Mechubar\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Ein kotvin bimchubar lekarka} \u8212- one may not write a {\i get} on something that is attached to the ground. If one nevertheless did write it on something {\i mechubar}, and then uprooted it, signed it, and gave it to her \u8212- {\i kasher}, it is valid.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This requires explanation. The {\i gemara} will grapple with the precise logic, but the surface reading of the {\i mishna} already implies a certain structure: {\i lechatchila} one does not write on {\i mechubar}, presumably because one may come to sign it while still attached; {\i bedi\u8217'avad}, if one did write it while attached, as long as one signed it only after it was separated, the {\i get} is valid.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The question of why {\i mechubar} is invalid at all is never explicitly resolved by the {\i gemara}, though it is taken for granted. One might have thought that since we established yesterday that a {\i get} written on the hand of a slave can be given by transferring possession of the slave, the same logic could apply here \u8212- write a {\i get} on the bark of a tree, give the woman the deed to the property, and she simultaneously acquires the {\i get} through her acquisition of the land. Yet that analogy seems to fail. The {\i Rambam} understands the problem in terms of {\i mechusar ketzitzah} \u8212- the {\i get} requires separation \u8212- but that itself begs the question of why one cannot simply transfer the property. The {\i gemara} seems to assume, without spelling it out fully, that a {\i get} must ultimately be something movable \u8212- {\i metaltelin} in character \u8212- and cannot remain permanently {\i mechubar}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Meir vs.\u160 ?R. Yehuda\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} continues: {\i Rabbi Yehuda posel ad sheyehei ketivato vechatimato bitalush} \u8212- Rabbi Yehuda invalidates unless both the writing and the signing occurred after separation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The debate between Rabbi Meir and Rabbi Yehuda maps onto the foundational dispute about what gives a {\i get} its legal force. Rabbi Meir holds {\i eidei chatima kartei} \u8212- it is the signatures that constitute the {\i get}, and the {\i pasuk} of {\i vekatav} is interpreted as pointing to the moment of signing. If so, all the requirements of the {\i get} attach to the signing. As long as the signing occurred when the object was no longer {\i mechubar}, the {\i get} is valid, even if the writing took place while it was still attached. Rabbi Yehuda, by contrast, holds {\i eidei mesira kartei} \u8212- the witnesses present at the moment of handover are what give the {\i get} its legal force, not the signatures. For him, the {\i Torah}\u8217's emphasis on {\i ketivah} falls on the act of writing itself. The writing must therefore occur when the object is {\i talush}, already separated. Signing after separation is insufficient if the writing took place while still attached.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Y. ben Beteira\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} adds a third opinion: {\i Rabbi Yehuda ben Beteira omer ein kotvin lo al niyar machuk velo al diftera} \u8212- one may not write a {\i get} on erased paper or on {\i diftera}, because such surfaces {\i yakhol lehizdayef}, they are susceptible to forgery.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The concern is precise. If a witness signs on a surface that already shows erasure, and the top portion of the document is subsequently altered \u8212- the original content erased and something new written in its place \u8212- the forgery is undetectable. The signature appears on the same kind of surface as the text, so everything looks consistent. Contrast this with a clean document whose top portion has been erased: the erasure below a clean signature would be immediately suspicious. Here, when the signatures themselves appear on an erasable surface, there is no way to know whether what is above them is original.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Vechachamim machshirin} \u8212- and the {\i Chachamim} permit it. This is striking. How can one permit writing a {\i get} on a forgeable surface? The {\i gemara} will explain that the {\i Chachamim} here are those who hold {\i eidei mesira kartei}. If the witnesses who are physically present at the handing over of the {\i get} are what validates it, then their direct observation at the moment of delivery provides sufficient security. Even if the document were subsequently forged, those witnesses saw what was actually there at the time. The {\i gemara} will unpack this further.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tofes vs.\u160 ?Toref\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} opens its analysis with a statement of {\i Rav Yehuda} in the name of {\i Shmuel}: {\i vehu sheshiyar makom haToref} \u8212- the {\i mishna}\u8217's ruling that the {\i get} is valid if signed after separation applies only when the writer left blank the place of the {\i toref}, the essential content.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This introduces the critical distinction between {\i tofes} and {\i toref}. The {\i tofes} is the boilerplate \u8212- the standard formulaic language that appears in every {\i get}. The {\i toref} is the individualized essential content: the names of the parties, the date, the place, and the essential declaration of divorce ({\i harei at muteret lechol adam}). {\i Rashi} defines the {\i toref} as {\i giluyav shel shetar} \u8212- the name of the man, the name of the woman, and the time. A brief look at the text of the {\i get} as preserved in the {\i Rif} (45b of the {\i dapei harif}) illustrates the full scope of what this entails: the date, the place, the full names and alternate names of both parties, the voluntary declaration, the threefold formula of release, the permission to remarry any man, and the closing declaration {\i veharei at muteret lechol adam kedas Moshe veYisrael}. It is these elements \u8212- not the connecting formulaic language \u8212- that constitute the {\i toref}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} thus reinterprets the {\i mishna}: when the {\i mishna} says the {\i get} is valid if signed after separation, it does not simply mean that one wrote the entire {\i get} while it was attached and then merely signed it after separation. Rather, {\i vehu sheshiyar makom haToref} \u8212- one left the essential parts blank while it was attached, and filled them in only after separation. The same statement is then attributed by the {\i gemara} to {\i Rav Elazar} in the name of {\i Rabbi Oshaya} and to {\i Rabbah bar bar Chana} in the name of {\i Rabbi Yochanan}. The {\i gemara} then identifies this position as that of {\i Rabbi Elazar}, who holds {\i eidei mesira kartei}, meaning that since it is the writing \u8212- not the signing \u8212- that is the determinative act, the entire {\i toref} must be written after separation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Yehuda vs.\u160 ?R. Lakish\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Resh Lakish} disputes this reading. He insists: {\i chatamo shaninu} \u8212- the {\i mishna} says \u8220"signed it,\u8221" and one cannot reinterpret the word {\i chatamo} to mean \u8220"filled in the essential parts.\u8221" The {\i mishna} means what it says: it was signed after separation. According to {\i Resh Lakish}, the {\i mishna} follows Rabbi Meir, who holds {\i eidei chatima kartei}. The entire {\i get} \u8212- including the {\i toref} \u8212- could have been written while still attached, because all the requirements cluster around the moment of signing, not writing. The signing is what transforms the document into a {\i get}, and as long as that signing occurs after separation, the {\i get} is valid {\i bedi\u8217'avad}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Thus we have two complete readings of the {\i mishna}. According to the reading attributed to the position of {\i Rabbi Elazar} ({\i eidei mesira kartei}): one may write the {\i tofes} while still attached, but must write the {\i toref} \u8212- including {\i harei at muteret lechol adam} \u8212- only after separation; what the {\i mishna} calls \u8220"signing\u8221" is a reference to filling in the essential content. According to {\i Resh Lakish} and the position of Rabbi Meir ({\i eidei chatima kartei}): one may write even the {\i toref} while still attached, and the {\i mishna} means exactly that signing \u8212- the witnesses\u8217' signatures \u8212- occurred after separation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Why Mechubar Invalid\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} never fully explicates the conceptual basis of the {\i mechubar} prohibition, but one reading that emerges \u8212- symbolically if not legally \u8212- is that a {\i get} must be like {\i metaltelin}, something mobile and transferable, rather than something permanently rooted in the earth. The {\i Rambam} frames this in terms of {\i mechusar ketzitzah}: the {\i get} lacks the necessary separation. Whatever the precise rationale, the {\i gemara} takes it as axiomatic that something {\i mechubar lekarka} cannot serve as a {\i get}, and the dispute between the {\i tanna\u8217'im} concerns only the question of when \u8212- at writing or at signing \u8212- the relevant act of separation must have already occurred.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Atzitz Nakuv\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Pottery vs.\u160 ?Leaf\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now applies these principles to a novel case: {\i katavo al cheres shel atzitz nakuv} \u8212- one wrote the {\i get} on the pottery of a flowerpot that has a hole in the bottom.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The significance of the hole ({\i nakuv}) requires explanation. A flowerpot with a hole in the bottom is considered {\i mechubar lekarka} \u8212- attached to the ground \u8212- even if there is air space between the bottom of the pot and the earth below. The hole creates a halachic connection: we view the dirt in the pot as a continuation of the ground beneath, and therefore the plant and its container are treated as if anchored to the earth, even though they are physically movable. A flowerpot without a hole ({\i atzitz she\u8217'eino nakuv}), by contrast, is treated as {\i metaltelin}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If one wrote the {\i get} on the pottery of such an {\i atzitz nakuv}, the {\i gemara} rules it is {\i kasher} \u8212- because {\i d\u8217'shakil lei veyahiv lei nihala}, one can simply pick up the pot and give it to the woman. The act of giving the pot itself, with the {\i get} written on it, constitutes the delivery.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But if one wrote the {\i get} on a leaf of the plant growing in the {\i atzitz nakuv}, a dispute arises.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Abaye vs.\u160 ?Rava\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Abaye amar kasher} \u8212- Abaye rules it valid, for the same reason: one picks up the pot, leaf and all, and hands it to the woman. {\i Rava amar pasul} \u8212- Rava rules it invalid, {\i gezeirah shema yiktom}, out of concern that one will pluck the leaf off. The fear is that the husband, wishing to give the {\i get}, will detach the leaf from the plant \u8212- thereby severing something {\i mechubar} from its living source \u8212- which constitutes the problematic {\i ketzitzah} that invalidates the {\i get}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The disparity between the leaf and the pottery is noteworthy. We are not similarly afraid that the husband will smash the pottery. This asymmetry is presumably because plucking a leaf is far simpler and more natural an act than breaking a clay pot.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafot Questions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} uses this case as a platform to examine a series of fundamental questions about the nature of {\i ketzitzah} and {\i mechubar} status.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The first question concerns what type of cutting invalidates a {\i get}. One reading of the {\i gemara}\u8217's structure suggests that only an act that transitions something from {\i mechubar} to {\i lo mechubar} \u8212- severing something living from its source \u8212- constitutes the invalidating {\i ketzitzah}. Plucking a leaf does exactly that: it was {\i mechubar} and is now {\i talush}. Breaking off a piece of pottery, by contrast, is analogous to trimming a margin off a larger sheet of paper \u8212- it changes the shape of the {\i metaltelin} but does not sever a {\i mechubar} connection. Under this reading, breaking the pot would not invalidate the {\i get}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The second and more intricate question is this: when the husband delivers the pot to the woman, does it still have to be {\i lo mechubar} at the moment of delivery? After all, the {\i get} is written on a {\i mechubar} object. Can a {\i mechubar} object be given as a {\i get}? {\i Tosafot} assumes that it must be made {\i lo mechubar} at the time of delivery. But here the analysis becomes subtle. If one simply lifts the pot and places it on a table \u8212- thereby eliminating the halachic connection to the ground, since there is no longer open air space between the hole and the dirt \u8212- the pot becomes {\i lo mechubar} without any physical alteration to the {\i get} itself. According to {\i Tosafot}, this is permissible and valid. What is impermissible is plucking the leaf: that act also makes the {\i get} {\i lo mechubar}, but it does so by physically severing a living connection, which constitutes an invalidating {\i ketzitzah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The result is a carefully calibrated distinction. Placing the pot on a table: the {\i get} becomes {\i lo mechubar}, no physical change to the {\i get} itself, valid. Plucking the leaf: the {\i get} becomes {\i lo mechubar}, but through an act of {\i ketzitzah} that intervenes between writing and delivery, invalid. The {\i gemara} itself does not spell out these distinctions explicitly; they are {\i Tosafot}\u8217's analysis of the implications.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kinyan Agav\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now pivots from the {\i gittin} discussion to explore the {\i kinyan} law of the {\i atzitz nakuv}. The case: the pot belongs to one person and the seeds \u8212- and by implication the soil \u8212- belong to another.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If the owner of the seeds wishes to acquire the pot ({\i machar baal atzitz lebaal zeraim}), he simply performs {\i meshichah} \u8212- drawing the pot toward himself \u8212- since the pot is {\i metaltelin} and that is how {\i metaltelin} is acquired. {\i Keivan shemashach kanah} \u8212- once he performs {\i meshichah} on the pot, he acquires it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The reverse case is more complex. If the owner of the pot wishes to acquire the seeds, he cannot use {\i meshichah}, because the seeds in a {\i nakuv} pot are classified as {\i karka} \u8212- real property \u8212- even though they are movable. Real property is acquired through {\i chazakah} (an act of improvement or possession applied to the land), through {\i kesef} (payment, as {\i Rashi} notes), or through a deed ({\i shtar}). The {\i gemara} rules: {\i lo kanah ad sheyachzik bazeraim} \u8212- he does not acquire until he performs {\i chazakah} on the seeds themselves, such as by watering them.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This raises the question of how acquiring the {\i karka} (seeds) also transfers the {\i metaltelin} (pot). The answer is the principle of {\i kinyan agav} \u8212- piggybacking. The {\i mishna} in Kiddushin teaches that {\i nechasim she\u8217'ein lahem achrayut niknim im nechasim sheyesh lahem achrayut} \u8212- chattel can be acquired together with real property through the methods of real property acquisition (payment, deed, or {\i chazakah}). By performing {\i chazakah} on the seeds (which are {\i karka}), one simultaneously acquires the pot ({\i metaltelin}) {\i agav} \u8212- on the back of \u8212- the {\i karka} acquisition.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chazaka on Seeds\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The full rule as articulated by the {\i gemara}: if one person owns both the pot and the seeds and sells them together, the buyer acquires both once he performs {\i chazakah} on the seeds. The {\i chazakah} on the {\i karka} (seeds) drags along the {\i metaltelin} (pot) through {\i kinyan agav}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} raises a further nuance: in the standard {\i kinyan agav} discussed in the first chapter of Kiddushin, when one sells chattel {\i agav} a piece of land, one must explicitly link them \u8212- saying, in effect, \u8220"acquire this chattel {\i agav} this land.\u8221" But in the present case, where the pot and the seeds are physically integrated \u8212- the seeds are literally growing in the pot \u8212- {\i Tosafot} considers whether such an explicit declaration is necessary. The reasoning is that the pot is intrinsically secondary to and dependent on the seeds; the two are seen as essentially a single unit. If so, the {\i chazakah} on the seeds may draw the pot along automatically, without any explicit {\i agav} declaration. {\i Tosafot} leaves this as a {\i tzarich iyun}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The practical paradigm underlying {\i kinyan agav} more broadly may in fact be this very case. The classic image is purchasing a furnished estate: acquiring the field brings the cattle, equipment, and produce with it as a natural extension of the acquisition. The flowerpot case, where the pot literally contains and is integrated with the seeds, may be the foundational model on which the broader rule \u8212- even for unconnected chattel \u8212- is built.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chazaka on Pot Fails\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then states the negative rule: {\i hechzik baatzitz af atzitz lo kanah} \u8212- if a buyer who wishes to acquire both pot and seeds performs {\i chazakah} on the pot rather than on the seeds, she acquires neither the pot nor the seeds.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The logic: {\i chazakah} is a method of acquiring {\i karka}, not {\i metaltelin}. Performing {\i chazakah} on the pot \u8212- say, by painting it \u8212- is performing {\i chazakah} on {\i metaltelin}, which is a legal nullity. It neither acquires the pot (which as {\i metaltelin} requires {\i meshichah}, not {\i chazakah}) nor the seeds (which require {\i chazakah} performed on the {\i karka} itself, not on the container). The {\i chazakah} was directed at the wrong object and accomplishes nothing. {\i Ad sheyachzik bazeraim} \u8212- one must perform the {\i chazakah} on the seeds themselves.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Border Tree Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Nikvu vs.\u160 ?Nofu\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now turns to a case that occupies it at length: {\i nikvah ba\u8217'aretz venofo bechutz la\u8217'aretz} \u8212- a flowerpot whose hole (and thus its halachic {\i mechubar} connection to the ground) is situated in {\i Eretz Yisrael}, but whose foliage extends over the border into {\i chutz la\u8217'aretz}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The practical stakes involve whether the fruit is obligated in {\i terumot uma\u8217'asrot}. {\i Abaye amar batar nikvo azlinan} \u8212- Abaye holds that we follow the location of the hole: since the hole is in {\i Eretz Yisrael}, the pot is halachically rooted there, and the fruit is {\i chayav} in {\i terumot uma\u8217'asrot}. {\i Rava amar batar nofo azlinan} \u8212- Rava holds that we follow where the foliage actually grows: since the foliage is in {\i chutz la\u8217'aretz}, the fruit is exempt.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} clarifies when this dispute applies. {\i Bed\u8217'ashrush kulei alma lo pligi} \u8212- if the roots have actually taken hold in the ground of {\i Eretz Yisrael}, everyone agrees: it is an {\i Eretz Yisrael} plant, defined by where it is rooted. The dispute pertains only to {\i bed\u8217'lo ashrush} \u8212- when the plant has not taken root, meaning it remains genuinely movable. In that case, Rava can argue that since the entire pot could theoretically be moved to the other side of the border, it is the actual location of the foliage and fruit that determines status.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Baraita: Half/Half\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} challenges the proposition that when the roots have genuinely taken hold, everyone agrees the plant follows the soil. It cites a {\i baraita}: {\i ilan miktzato ba\u8217'aretz umiktzato bechutz la\u8217'aretz} \u8212- a tree partly in {\i Eretz Yisrael} and partly outside. {\i Rabbi} says: {\i tevel vechullin meuravim zeh bazeh} \u8212- every fruit is a mixture of {\i tevel} (obligated) and {\i chullin} (exempt), because the tree is being nurtured by both types of soil simultaneously. {\i Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel} says: {\i hagadel bechiyuv chayav, hagadel bepattur pattur} \u8212- what grows in {\i Eretz Yisrael} is {\i chayav}, and what grows in {\i chutz la\u8217'aretz} is exempt.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The implication seems to be that {\i Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel} divides the fruit based on where the actual foliage is located \u8212- and this is a case of a tree that presumably has taken root. If so, even in a rooted tree, one authority holds that we follow the foliage rather than the soil, which would suggest that the debate between Abaye and Rava is not limited to the {\i atzitz nakuv} case.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Foliage Defense\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} asks: is this {\i baraita} indeed a case where the foliage alone determines status, irrespective of root location? {\i Amar leih Abaye} \u8212- Abaye responds: perhaps this challenge fails. The {\i baraita} about the two gardens \u8212- {\i Rabbi Meir omer shel elyon} (the upper gardener\u8217's, defined by whose soil it grows from), {\i Rabbi Yehuda omer shel tachton} (the lower gardener\u8217's, defined by whose airspace it occupies) \u8212- was about a question of ownership, not about {\i Eretz Yisrael} status. The ability to destroy the other party\u8217's claim (by removing the soil or filling in the airspace) determines possession. That is a different legal question entirely from whether fruit is {\i chayav} in {\i ma\u8217'asrot}. So the earlier {\i baraita} does not prove that when a tree is rooted in {\i Eretz Yisrael} we follow the foliage.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then presses further: {\i mai lav miktzat nofah ba\u8217'aretz umiktzat nofah bechutz la\u8217'aretz} \u8212- is the {\i baraita} not a case where half the foliage is on each side, with the roots irrelevant? If so, even in a rooted tree, {\i Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel} divides by foliage, demonstrating that one can follow the foliage even when roots are present.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rock Divides Roots\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} responds: {\i lo \u8212- miktzat shorashin ba\u8217'aretz umiktzat shorashin bechutz la\u8217'aretz} \u8212- in the {\i baraita}, it is not just the foliage that is divided; the roots themselves are divided, running partly into {\i Eretz Yisrael} and partly into {\i chutz la\u8217'aretz}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But this creates a problem: if the roots are mixed, how can {\i Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel} say that one portion of the fruit is purely {\i chayav} and the other purely {\i pattur}? The entire tree is drawing nutrients from both soils \u8212- every fruit should be a mixture, as {\i Rabbi} holds. The {\i gemara} therefore postulates a special case: {\i demafsik tsunma} \u8212- a rock runs along the border underground, physically separating the roots on one side from the roots on the other. The roots in {\i Eretz Yisrael} go only into {\i Eretz Yisrael} soil; the roots in {\i chutz la\u8217'aretz} go only into {\i chutz la\u8217'aretz} soil. Underground, they do not intermingle.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mixed Nutrients\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But even with the rock dividing the underground roots, once the nutrients enter the trunk of the tree, do they not mix? The {\i gemara} identifies this as the precise point of the dispute between {\i Rabbi} and {\i Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel}. {\i Mar savar avira mivalbel} \u8212- {\i Rabbi} holds that above ground, in the trunk and branches, all the nutrients merge; every fruit therefore receives a mixture of {\i Eretz Yisrael} and {\i chutz la\u8217'aretz} nourishment, and every fruit is simultaneously {\i tevel} and {\i chullin}. {\i Umar savar hai lechudei kai vehai lechudei kai} \u8212- {\i Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel} holds that even above ground, the nutrients from each side continue to feed only their respective side of the tree; the separation maintained underground by the rock persists functionally into the branches and fruit. Consequently, the fruit on the {\i Eretz Yisrael} side is nourished purely from {\i Eretz Yisrael} soil, and the fruit on the {\i chutz la\u8217'aretz} side is nourished purely from {\i chutz la\u8217'aretz} soil.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara}\u8217's resolution thus preserves the principle: when roots are genuinely in the ground, the determination of status tracks where the nutrients originate \u8212- i.e., the soil. {\i Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel}\u8217's ability to split the fruit depends not on ignoring the roots but on positing that the rock keeps the root systems fully separate, so that each side of the tree is effectively its own independent nutritional unit. The ability to split the fruit by foliage location is therefore not a case of overriding root-defined status; it is a case where the root-defined status itself is already cleanly divided. The {\i gemara} thereby protects the conclusion that in the {\i atzitz nakuv} case \u8212- where the plant has genuinely taken root in {\i Eretz Yisrael} \u8212- everyone would agree to follow the soil, while when it has not taken root, Rava and Abaye dispute whether we follow the hole or the foliage.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Forgery & Witnesses\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Three Leathers\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now turns to the ruling of {\i Rabbi Yehuda ben Beteira} against writing on forgeable surfaces, and opens with a classification that provides the halachic context for {\i diftera}. {\i Amar Rabbi Chiyya bar Ashi mishmeih de-Ulla: gimmel orot hen} \u8212- there are three categories of leather.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The first is {\i matza}: untreated leather that has not been salted, treated with flour, or processed with gallnut ({\i afitz}). Because it is of minimal utility, its halachic significance ({\i hilchata}) pertains to {\i hotza\u8217'at Shabbat} \u8212- the minimum {\i shiur} of carrying that incurs liability on {\i Shabbat}. As taught by {\i Rabbi Shmuel bar Yehuda}, the relevant {\i shiur} is the amount sufficient to wrap a small weight \u8212- the tiny weights used in commerce, wrapped in leather to prevent wear. Abaye specifies this as approximately the smallest weight used in Pumbedita.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The second is {\i chippa}: leather that has been salted but not treated with flour or gallnut. Its halachic significance also pertains to {\i hotza\u8217'at Shabbat}, with a {\i shiur} corresponding to the amount needed to make a {\i kamia} \u8212- an amulet wrapper \u8212- as per the {\i mishna}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The third is {\i diftera}: leather that has been salted and treated with flour but not with gallnut. It is this partial processing that creates the problematic surface: it can be written on, but subsequent erasure would not be evident, since the gallnut treatment that would \u8220"fix\u8221" the writing permanently has not been applied. For contrast, fully processed {\i klaf} \u8212- which includes treatment with gallnut \u8212- has a {\i shiur} corresponding to the smallest {\i parasha} of {\i tefillin}, reflecting its use for sacred writing.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Y. ben B. Rejects\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Vechachamim machshirin} \u8212- the {\i Chachamim} permit writing a {\i get} on {\i diftera}, but {\i Rabbi Yehuda ben Beteira} forbids it. The concern he raises is precise: the problem is not simply that the text could be altered. The problem is that because the witnesses\u8217' signatures appear on the same erasable surface as the text, any alteration to the text would look entirely consistent with the rest of the document. If the signatures appeared on clean parchment and the text above them had been erased and rewritten, the discrepancy would be immediately apparent. But when signatures and text alike appear on {\i diftera} \u8212- a surface where erasure leaves no visible trace \u8212- there is no way to detect whether the original text has been replaced.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The specific scenario the {\i gemara} envisions: a husband writes a {\i get} on {\i diftera} with a {\i tenai} \u8212- a condition \u8212- in it; the wife then erases the {\i tenai} and replaces it with neutral text, leaving the signatures intact. Alternatively, one could imagine a more extreme forgery: erasing the entire original document above the signatures and replacing it with a {\i get}, when the original was an entirely different document such as a loan instrument. The forgery is undetectable precisely because the signatures, the text, and the surface all present identically.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chachamim Allow\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Why do the {\i Chachamim} permit it nonetheless? {\i Amar Rabbi Elazar} \u8212- the {\i gemara} identifies the {\i Chachamim} as holding {\i eidei mesira kartei}, the position of Rabbi Elazar. If what validates a {\i get} is not the signatures themselves but the testimony of the witnesses physically present at the moment of delivery, then the forgeable nature of the writing surface is not fatal. Those witnesses saw the {\i get} at the time it was handed over; they read it; they know what it said. Even if someone subsequently forged the document, the witnesses\u8217' testimony about what they directly observed remains the operative legal validation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Elazar: Time Limit\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then adds a significant qualification from Rabbi Elazar himself: {\i lo hikhshir Rabbi Elazar ela le-altar} \u8212- Rabbi Elazar permitted such a document only when the witnesses came immediately before {\i beit din} and testified. {\i Aval mikan ve-ad asara yamim lo} \u8212- but if ten days were to pass, he would not permit it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The reason: {\i chashinan dilma havei bei tenai vezayafteh} \u8212- we are concerned that there was a {\i tenai} in the {\i get} and it was forged in the interim. This may seem paradoxical: if Rabbi Elazar holds {\i eidei mesira kartei}, why does the passage of time matter? He is relying on witnesses, not on the document itself. The answer, as {\i Tosafot} works out at length, lies in the imperfection of human memory. When witnesses testify immediately, their recollection of the document\u8217's precise contents is sharp. Combined even with a partially reliable document, their memory fills in any gaps. But with a forgeable document, the witnesses\u8217' memory alone must carry the entire evidentiary weight, because the document cannot corroborate their account. After ten days, memory of specific details \u8212- particularly any {\i tenai} \u8212- may fade, and we can no longer trust it with sufficient confidence.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The contrast with an unforgeable document is instructive. Were the document reliable, witnesses could point to it and say: \u8220"That is the {\i get} we saw; these are the signatures we witnessed.\u8221" Memory and document mutually reinforce each other. With a forgeable document, the witnesses\u8217' memory stands alone. Therefore, Rabbi Elazar insists on immediate testimony.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kinyan vs.\u160 ?Rayah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} draws an important conceptual distinction that illuminates the entire sugya. There are two fundamentally different types of legal documents: a {\i shtar kinyan} and a {\i shtar rayah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i shtar kinyan} is an instrument \u8212- it functions as the operative legal act. A {\i get} is the paradigmatic example: the document itself effects the divorce. Similarly, a deed of sale transfers ownership, and a writ of manumission frees a slave. The question for such documents is whether they function as valid instruments, not whether they can prove a historical fact.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i shtar rayah}, by contrast, is an evidentiary document \u8212- its purpose is to prove what happened. A promissory note proves that a loan occurred; a title deed proves who owns a field. Such documents serve their function only insofar as they reliably testify to past events.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} argues that when Rabbi Elazar permits writing on {\i diftera}, this applies to {\i shtar kinyan} documents. A {\i get} written on {\i diftera}, in the presence of witnesses at delivery, functions as a valid instrument of divorce \u8212- it effects the {\i kinyan} of {\i gerushim}. The question of whether it can subsequently prove the divorce is separate. For {\i shtar rayah} documents, however, even Rabbi Elazar would require a non-forgeable surface, because a document whose testimony cannot be trusted cannot serve the purpose of an evidentiary instrument at all.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This also clarifies the position of Rabbi Meir. For Rabbi Meir, {\i eidei chatima kartei}: the signatures constitute the {\i get}. A {\i get} must testify for itself \u8212- it is {\i mukhach mitocho}. A document that is {\i mezuyyaf mitocho} \u8212- susceptible to internal forgery \u8212- cannot serve as a {\i get} for Rabbi Meir even in principle, not because it fails as {\i rayah} but because it fails as a {\i kinyan} instrument. For Rabbi Meir, a document that cannot self-authenticate is not a {\i get} at all, regardless of what witnesses say.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Yochanan\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Yochanan amar: afilu mikan ve-ad asara yamim} \u8212- Rabbi Yochanan disagrees with Rabbi Elazar\u8217's ten-day limitation and permits reliance on such a document together with witnesses even after ten days have passed. His reasoning: {\i de-ee issa de-havei bei tenai midkar dakhiri} \u8212- if there had been a {\i tenai} in the {\i get}, the witnesses would remember it. Their memory on this point remains sufficiently reliable even after ten days.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then states: {\i amar Rabbi Elazar lo hikhshir Rabbi Elazar ela begitten} \u8212- Rabbi Elazar\u8217's permit was limited to {\i gitten}. Other documents ({\i shear shtarot}) may not be written on {\i diftera}. The {\i gemara} cites the verse {\i unesatem bikhli cheres lema\u8217'an yaamdu yamim rabbim} \u8212- \u8220"place them in clay vessels so that they last many days\u8221" \u8212- as a basis for requiring that other documents be written on durable surfaces.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} clarifies what \u8220"other documents\u8221" means here. It does not mean all non-{\i get} documents indiscriminately. Rather, it refers to {\i shtar rayah} documents \u8212- those whose function is evidentiary rather than instrumental. For other {\i shtar kinyan} documents, such as a writ of manumission or a deed of sale, Rabbi Elazar might well agree that they too can be written on {\i diftera}, since they too function as instruments rather than as proof. The prohibition would apply specifically to {\i shtar rayah} documents, because the entire purpose of such a document is to testify reliably to past events \u8212- a function a forgeable document cannot serve.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yochanan\u8217's position extends further: {\i afilu beshtarot} \u8212- even for {\i shtar rayah} documents, a forgeable surface can be used, provided witnesses testify alongside it. The verse about lasting \u8220"many days\u8221" is, on his view, merely {\i eitzah tovah} \u8212- good practical advice about how to write a reliable document \u8212- but not a formal legal requirement. If witnesses are available to testify, their testimony combined with even a forgeable document may suffice.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 To summarize the positions at the close of this sugya: for Rabbi Meir ({\i eidei chatima kartei}), a forgeable {\i get} is simply not a {\i get} \u8212- it cannot serve as a {\i shtar kinyan} for divorce, because the document must testify for itself, and a forgeable document cannot do so. For Rabbi Elazar ({\i eidei mesira kartei}), a forgeable {\i get} functions perfectly well as a {\i shtar kinyan}; the concern is only about reliability of witness memory for purposes of proof, which is why he limits the permit to immediate testimony. For Rabbi Yochanan, even this temporal limitation is rejected \u8212- witnesses reliably remember whether there was a {\i tenai}, even after ten days \u8212- and indeed the logic may extend even to {\i shtar rayah} documents when witnesses are present. The distinction between {\i shtar kinyan} and {\i shtar rayah} thus provides the organizing framework within which all these positions can be precisely located.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Gittin 23\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Eliezer vs.\u160 ?Meir: Get De\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Get Nature Intro\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The present daf opens with a review of the foundational debate that runs throughout {\i masechet Gittin}: what is the essential nature of the {\i get}, and which component \u8212- the writing or the signatures \u8212- is the operative act of divorce?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chatima vs.\u160 ?Ketiva\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Talmudic dispute between Rabbi Eliezer and Rabbi Meir turns on a single conceptual pivot. Rabbi Meir holds {\i edei chatima karsi} \u8212- the signing witnesses are what \u8220"cut\u8221" the {\i get}, making the {\i get} operative through its signatures. Rabbi Eliezer, by contrast, locates the operative act in the {\i ketiva}, the writing itself, and emphasizes the witnesses present at the moment of {\i mesira}, the physical handover of the {\i get}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mechubar Problem\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This distinction generates practical consequences throughout the tractate. For Rabbi Meir, who focuses on the signatures, the prohibition against writing a {\i get} on something {\i mechubar} \u8212- still attached to the ground \u8212- is a problem located in the signing, not in the writing. For Rabbi Eliezer, who focuses on the writing, the {\i mechubar} problem arises at the moment the text is inscribed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Forgeable Background\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Similarly, the earlier discussion about a {\i get} written on a forgeable background \u8212- parchment that had been erased and could be erased again without detection \u8212- generated different outcomes under the two positions. For Rabbi Meir, the {\i get} is defined partly by its function as written testimony: the signed {\i get} must genuinely testify to what occurred. If the document is written on a forgeable background, the testimony is compromised and it can no longer serve as a {\i get}, even if witnesses were present at the {\i mesira} and even if there is no actual doubt about what transpired. The documentary testimony itself has become meaningless, and that disqualifies it. For Rabbi Eliezer, by contrast, the testimonial function is separable from the instrument of divorce.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Get Definition\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This brings us to a conceptually crucial distinction. For Rabbi Meir, part of the very definition of a {\i get} is that it constitutes written testimony \u8212- a {\i shtar raya} \u8212- and if it fails to genuinely testify, it is not a {\i get} regardless of independent proof of the underlying facts. For Rabbi Eliezer, the testimony aspect is entirely separate from the {\i get}. One can ask the {\i edim} who witnessed the handover what occurred; how one knows the facts is independent of whether the document functions as an instrument of divorce.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shtar Kinyan/Raya\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This distinction maps onto the broader categories of {\i shtar kinyan} \u8212- a document that itself effects a change in status \u8212- and {\i shtar raya} \u8212- a document that serves as evidence of what occurred. For Rabbi Eliezer, these two identities can be fully separated. A {\i get} can be a {\i shtar kinyan} without being a {\i shtar raya}, and therefore may even be written on a forgeable background without invalidation. For Rabbi Meir, the {\i get} as written testimony is bound up in its very definition as an instrument of divorce.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Other Kinyan Cases\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} raises the question of whether Rabbi Eliezer\u8217's permissive position extends to other instruments of {\i kinyan}, such as a {\i shtar shichrur} for a slave or a deed of sale for a field. While the {\i gemara}\u8217's simple reading is that the unique nature of a {\i get} permits separation of the two identities \u8212- {\i shtar kinyan} without {\i shtar raya} \u8212- the question of whether Rabbi Eliezer would allow the same leniency in other {\i shtarei kinyan} is left open. {\i Tosafot} inclines toward the view that only by {\i get} does Rabbi Eliezer permit the separation; for other instruments, even a {\i shtar kinyan} may require simultaneously functioning as a {\i shtar raya}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Fundamental Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The deepest level of the dispute, then, is not merely a technical question of whether {\i vechatav lah} focuses on writing or signing. It is a question about the very character of the {\i get} as a legal instrument: to what degree must a {\i get} be written testimony? For Rabbi Meir, it must be \u8212- and a forgeable document fails categorically. For Rabbi Eliezer, the {\i get} is purely a {\i shtar kinyan} and need not serve as written testimony at all. And this distinction ramifies into discussions of {\i shichrur} and the sale of land, where the degree to which the {\i shtar kinyan} must also serve as {\i shtar raya} remains a live question.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Student Q&A\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Eliezer Challenge\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might ask: what recourse does Rabbi Eliezer offer when a {\i get} is challenged in court? Since Rabbi Eliezer does not rely on the document\u8217's written signatures as operative testimony, the answer is that one must go and locate the {\i edim} who were physically present at the {\i mesira}. The signed document cannot itself serve as proof of the transaction under Rabbi Eliezer\u8217's framework.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 That said, the picture is more complicated than a clean binary. Some {\i rishonim} hold that even for Rabbi Eliezer, where the {\i get} was written on a non-forgeable background, the document can in practice also function as a {\i shtar raya}. If you have it, you may use the written signatures without needing to locate the physical witnesses. Some {\i rishonim} go further, holding that when written signatures are present, Rabbi Eliezer would not require physical witnesses to be present at the handover at all \u8212- those written witnesses can serve both testimonial functions. Yet even according to these views, the key point stands: it need not function that way. It is a {\i get} independently of whether it serves as written testimony.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mesira Role\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The question of {\i mesira} and the role of external witnesses draws out the further ramifications of the debate. For Rabbi Meir, the signed witnesses function as the witnesses to the {\i mesira} itself \u8212- no external witnesses are required at the handover, because the signatures accomplish everything. For Rabbi Eliezer, since the emphasis falls on the {\i mesira}, external witnesses must be physically present. The signed text is not what constitutes the divorce; the witnessed handover is.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Further complexity arises from Rabbeinu Tam, who holds that even for Rabbi Meir \u8212- who demands the signatures \u8212- this requirement pertains only to what turns the document into a {\i get}, but when it comes to the actual giving over, physical witnesses are required externally. This creates a striking inversion: Rabbi Meir\u8217's position ends up, in this view, resembling Rabbi Eliezer\u8217's in practice with respect to {\i mesira}. The most unambiguous point that emerges is that for Rabbi Eliezer, the demands of {\i vechatav} \u8212- whatever the {\i Torah} requires in how the {\i get} is written \u8212- focus on the {\i ketiva}; for Rabbi Meir, they focus on the {\i chatima}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Mishna: Who Can Write\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hakol Kosherim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} at the bottom of 24b states: {\i hakol kesherim lichtov et haget} \u8212- everyone is eligible to write a {\i get} \u8212- {\i afilu cheresh shoteh vekatan} \u8212- even a deaf-mute, a person who is mentally incompetent, and a minor. These three categories \u8212- {\i cheresh}, {\i shoteh}, and {\i katan} \u8212- are the paradigmatic cases of people who cannot be made agents, cannot execute legally binding acts, and are not considered {\i bnei da\u8217'at}. The fact that they can write a {\i get} is, at first glance, astonishing.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishna Simple Sense\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The simplest reading of this {\i mishna} \u8212- and the one confirmed by the {\i gemara}\u8217's eventual resolution \u8212- is that it follows the position of Rabbi Meir. {\i Stam mishna} is Rabbi Meir, and the previous {\i mishna} established the same principle: a {\i get} written {\i mechubar lakarka} is valid so long as it is signed after detachment. The consistent implication of both {\i mishnayot} is that what matters is the signing, not the writing. If the signing is all that counts, one could in principle print a {\i get} and simply have the correct witnesses sign it \u8212- it does not matter who produced the text.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Woman/Man Writing\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} continues: {\i ha\u8217'isha kotevet et gitah veha\u8217'ish kotev et shovro} \u8212- a woman can write her own {\i get}, and a man can write the receipt acknowledging that the {\i ketuba} was paid. The apparent problem \u8212- that one might misrepresent facts by writing one\u8217's own document \u8212- is dissolved by what follows.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kiyum Haget\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} closes: {\i she\u8217'ein kiyum haget ela b\u8217'chotamav} \u8212- the validation of the {\i get} rests exclusively on its signatures. This is the operative principle. Writing without signatures is nothing \u8212- it is equivalent to printing a document without authorization. It becomes a {\i get} only when witnesses sign it, and witnesses will not sign unless the act is genuinely occurring.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Misrepresentation Preven\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is why there is no concern about misrepresentation. A woman cannot falsely claim she received a {\i get} by writing the text herself, because without proper signatures the document has no standing whatsoever. Similarly, a man cannot fabricate a receipt for the {\i ketuba} by writing it himself; the witnesses must sign, and they will only sign if the {\i ketuba} was actually paid. The entire structure of protection against fraud is built into the requirement of witness signatures, not into any restriction on who may write the text.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Gemara: Intent Problem\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Bnei Daas Problem\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} initially approaches the {\i mishna} as though it might represent the position of Rabbi Eliezer \u8212- a surprising assumption, though one generated in part by the fact that the previous discussion about {\i mechubar} had been associated with Rabbi Eliezer. On that reading, the {\i gemara} presses a challenge: {\i vehala lav bnei de\u8217'ah ninhu} \u8212- how can a {\i cheresh}, {\i shoteh}, and {\i katan} write a {\i get} if they are not considered {\i bnei de\u8217'ah}? Their intent cannot constitute {\i lishma}, and they cannot execute legal acts. So how does the {\i mishna} permit this?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gadol Omed Al Gavo\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Huna answers: {\i vehu shehaya gadol omed al gabav} \u8212- the case is where an adult stands over them and supervises the writing.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafot: Instruction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} immediately observes that {\i gadol omed al gabav} can mean different things in different contexts. By {\i shechita}, where the only requirement is technical correctness in the performance of the act, the adult\u8217's presence is primarily one of observation \u8212- ensuring the mechanics were executed properly. Here, where the issue is writing with the correct intent of {\i lishma}, {\i gadol omed al gabav} means something more active: the adult is directly instructing the writer as the writing proceeds. This explains why the {\i gemara} presents {\i gadol omed al gabav} as a solution even for the case of a {\i shoteh}, which would seem to be the hardest case. By standing over and continuously directing the act, the adult creates the context and content of the intent, which is then attributed to or channeled through the person writing.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} also notes a related conceptual framework: since the person writing is acting entirely in accordance with the adult\u8217's instructions, and this is observable from their actions \u8212- write the name {\i Leah bat Rachel}, and they write {\i Leah bat Rachel} \u8212- there is concrete evidence that the writer is being shaped by the correct intent. One might even conceptualize the relationship as the adult writing through the other person as a vehicle, though {\i Tosafot} does not explicitly adopt this framing.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shlichut Not Required\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} raises a further question. If we are operating within Rabbi Eliezer\u8217's framework (as the {\i gemara} initially assumes), doesn\u8217't the writer need to be a {\i shaliach} of the husband? The husband presumably instructs the {\i sofer}: write this {\i get} for my wife. Isn\u8217't this a formal act of {\i shlichut}? {\i Tosafot} answers: no. The proof is in the {\i gemara} itself \u8212- since a {\i cheresh}, {\i shoteh}, and {\i katan} cannot be made {\i shelichim} at all, and yet the {\i gemara} considers them valid writers, it follows that {\i shlichut} is not a requirement for {\i ketiva} of a {\i get}. The reason the husband speaks to the {\i sofer} and instructs him to write it for his wife is not to create a formal {\i shlichut} relationship, but solely to establish the proper context of {\i lishma}. Without hearing from the husband that this {\i get} is for his specific wife, the {\i sofer} cannot genuinely claim to be writing it for the sake of divorcing this particular woman. The instruction creates the mindset of {\i lishma}; it does not create {\i shlichut}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Bnei Krisus Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} raises a third problem. The {\i gemara} states in multiple places that those who write and sign a {\i get} must be {\i bnei krisus} \u8212- persons within the legal domain of the laws of {\i gittin}. Does this not exclude the {\i cheresh}, {\i shoteh}, and {\i katan}? {\i Tosafot} addresses this in two ways. First, one might argue that {\i cheresh}, {\i shoteh}, and {\i katan} are theoretically within the {\i parsha} \u8212- a {\i katan} will eventually become an adult, and a {\i cheresh} or {\i shoteh} might recover. But the stronger argument emerges from the {\i gemara}\u8217's next move: the {\i gemara} considers whether even a {\i nochri} could write the {\i get}. A {\i nochri} is certainly not {\i bnei krisus} within the framework of our laws of {\i gittin vekiddushin}. Yet the {\i gemara} entertains the possibility. This forces the conclusion that the requirement of {\i bnei krisus} is contingent on {\i shlichut}: one must be in the {\i parsha} to be a {\i shaliach}, but if {\i shlichut} is not required for {\i ketiva} of a {\i get}, then even a {\i nochri} could in principle write it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Intent via Instruction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The picture that {\i Tosafot} constructs, then, is coherent and radical simultaneously. For Rabbi Eliezer, who cares very much about the {\i ketiva}: (1) {\i lishma} in writing can be achieved through {\i gadol omed al gabav}, even for a {\i cheresh}, {\i shoteh}, and {\i katan}; (2) {\i shlichut} is not required \u8212- the husband\u8217's instruction creates {\i lishma}, not agency; and (3) even {\i bnei krisus} is not required for the writer, since that requirement is a function of {\i shlichut} which is itself not required for writing.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Intent/Mechanics Q&A\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Right Intent\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A close examination of what \u8220"right intent\u8221" means in the context of {\i lishma} for writing a {\i get} raises genuinely difficult questions. The requirement is that the {\i get} be written for the sake of this particular woman\u8217's divorce \u8212- for this husband, for this wife.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Observable Intent\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The principle at work here is something like {\i kavanatam nikeret mitoch maaseihem} \u8212- the intent of the writer is observable from their actions. The adult instructs: write for the divorce of this man\u8217's wife, her name is {\i Leah bat Rachel}, and write these words. When the person writes those specific words, there is concrete, observable evidence that they are being guided by the instructions. The assumption is that whatever ideas occupy their mind are the ideas placed there by the supervising adult.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gadol Intent Role\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Several questions arise about the precise mechanism of {\i gadol omed al gabav}. If the person\u8217's intent is inferred from their faithful execution of instructions, one might ask whether the {\i gadol}\u8217's presence is strictly necessary in all cases \u8212- could there be scenarios where the correct intent is independently evident? Conversely, one might conceive of the {\i gadol} as the true agent, with the {\i cheresh}, {\i shoteh}, or {\i katan} functioning as the {\i gadol}\u8217's instrument or extended \u8220"pen.\u8221" {\i Tosafot} does not take this direction explicitly, and the two framings \u8212- the writer as having their intent shaped by instruction, versus the writer as the vehicle of the {\i gadol}\u8217's intent \u8212- carry different implications. If the emphasis falls on the writer as having independent, observable intent, then in principle one might imagine cases where {\i gadol omed al gabav} is unnecessary. If the emphasis falls on the {\i gadol} as the true writer, then the {\i gadol}\u8217's active presence is indispensable.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Cheresh Instruction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One further difficulty presents itself in the case of the {\i cheresh} specifically: if the mechanism of {\i gadol omed al gabav} is the communication of instructions, how does one instruct someone who cannot hear? In those times, written placards or other forms of communication were not readily available, and the {\i gemara} does not appear to unpack this question. It remains a genuine difficulty left in the text.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Non-Jew Exclusion\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Non-Jew Challenge\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Nachman poses a sharp challenge to the {\i gadol omed al gabav} framework: {\i oved kochavim veYisrael omed al gabav, hachi nami dekasher} \u8212- if the principle is that an adult supervising creates the proper intent for the writing, why would the same not apply to a non-Jew with an adult Jew standing over him? The logic of {\i gadol omed al gabav} seems to make no distinction between a {\i cheresh} or {\i katan} and an adult non-Jew.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Adaata Denafshei\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} answers: {\i oved kochavim ada\u8217'ata d\u8217'nafsheih avid} \u8212- a non-Jew is always considered to be acting on his own initiative. He is a full adult, and one cannot assume that what occupies his mind is entirely determined by the instructions given to him.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Control Distinction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This answer illuminates the deeper logic of {\i gadol omed al gabav}. The reason it works for a {\i cheresh}, {\i shoteh}, and {\i katan} is that these persons \u8212- by virtue of being not fully {\i bnei de\u8217'ah} \u8212- can be seen as having their mental content entirely shaped by the supervising adult. What they think and intend is what the adult places in their minds. Whether one frames this as the writer being the {\i gadol}\u8217's instrument or as the writer\u8217's observable actions demonstrating that the correct intent was instilled, the underlying assumption is that they are fully malleable in this respect. A non-Jew, by contrast, is a complete adult, acting on his own initiative. However faithfully he may follow instructions and write the correct words, one cannot assume that his intent has been wholly determined by those instructions. He may write {\i Leah bat Rachel} for entirely his own reasons, or with no particular intent at all \u8212- and crucially, one cannot project the correct {\i lishma} onto him.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} presses on the precise nature of the problem with the non-Jew\u8217's intent. It would be strange to say that the non-Jew is positively thinking something contrary to {\i lishma} \u8212- writing {\i l\u8217'hachis}, so to speak. The real issue is the opposite: by a {\i get}, the default assumption is {\i stam lav lishmah} \u8212- an unstated, undirected act of writing does not carry the presumption of {\i lishma}. This is unlike {\i mila}, for example, where {\i stam lishmah kai} \u8212- the default assumption for circumcision is that it is performed with the appropriate intent. For a {\i get}, one needs positive evidence of the correct intent, and by the non-Jew, even with instructions, one cannot assume those ideas were genuinely operative in his mind. The {\i cheresh}, {\i shoteh}, and {\i katan} will have in their minds the ideas put there; the non-Jew may follow the words and have nothing in mind at all.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Nachman Retracts\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having posed the challenge and received the answer, Rav Nachman then retracts his original concern entirely: {\i lav milta hi de\u8217'amri} \u8212- what I said was incorrect.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mesira Exclusion\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 His reasoning: {\i dimichdi kapaslei oved kochavim min hamuva} \u8212- the upcoming {\i mishna} will exclude a non-Jew specifically from the {\i shlichut} of {\i mesira}, from serving as an agent to deliver the {\i get}. {\i Michlal de\u8217'le\u8217'inyan ketiva kasher} \u8212- from the fact that the exclusion is limited to the {\i mesira}, one can infer that a non-Jew is valid for the writing. The structure of the next {\i mishna} thus implies that a non-Jew\u8217's ineligibility is confined to acting as a {\i shaliach}, not to writing the text.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Non-Jew Good\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 How then does one account for the {\i braita} that explicitly says {\i oved kochavim pasul} \u8212- a non-Jew is invalid? Rav Nachman answers: {\i hahi Rabbi Eliezer hi} \u8212- that {\i braita} follows Rabbi Eliezer, who holds that {\i edei mesirah karsei} \u8212- the witnesses at the handover are the operative ones \u8212- and who therefore cares very much about the {\i ketiva} being done properly and {\i lishma}. For Rabbi Eliezer, the non-Jew\u8217's {\i ada\u8217'ata d\u8217'nafsheih} problem is fatal. But our {\i mishna} follows Rabbi Meir, for whom the writing is entirely immaterial.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Final Position: Rabbi Me\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Braiata Problem\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now confronts the {\i braita} that states {\i oved kochavim pasul} \u8212- a non-Jew is disqualified from writing a {\i get} \u8212- against the {\i mishna}\u8217's blanket inclusion of all writers.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Braiata vs.\u160 ?Mishna\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The resolution is clean: {\i hahi Rabbi Eliezer hi} \u8212- the {\i braita} is Rabbi Eliezer; the {\i mishna} is Rabbi Meir. Under Rabbi Eliezer, who focuses on the {\i ketiva} and demands it be written {\i lishma}, the non-Jew\u8217's {\i ada\u8217'ata d\u8217'nafsheih} is disqualifying and even a {\i cheresh}, {\i shoteh}, and {\i katan} would presumably be invalid, even with {\i gadol omed al gabav}. Under Rabbi Meir, neither the non-Jew nor anyone else is an obstacle, because writing is simply immaterial.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Meir: Signing Only\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 With this resolution, the entire elaborate construction of {\i gadol omed al gabav} for Rabbi Eliezer is set aside as the {\i gemara}\u8217's preferred reading of the {\i mishna}. For Rabbi Meir, the {\i vechatav} of the {\i pasuk} \u8212- {\i vechatav lah sefer keritut} \u8212- means {\i vechatam}: signing is the operative act. Writing is nothing until signed, and it is only the signing that matters.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Meir: Garbage Get\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Meir\u8217's position is rendered vivid by an illustration: {\i afilu metza\u8217'o ba\u8217'ashpah} \u8212- even if one finds a {\i get} in the garbage, written originally for a different woman, {\i chasmo venatno lo kasher} \u8212- have it signed with the correct intent and give it over, and it is valid. Before the signature, it is a piece of paper. The signature transforms it into a {\i get}, and it is only the signature that matters.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two Explanations\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} thus preserves two readings of the {\i mishna}. The first: the {\i mishna} follows Rabbi Eliezer, with the mechanism of {\i gadol omed al gabav} creating the necessary {\i lishma} \u8212- a position that generates the remarkable {\i chidush} that for Rabbi Eliezer, writing a {\i get} requires neither {\i shlichut} nor {\i bnei krisus} on the part of the writer. The second, and ultimately stronger: the {\i mishna} follows Rabbi Meir, writing is categorically immaterial, and none of these concerns apply at all.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Challenges: Leshmah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava Challenge\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now subjects Rabbi Meir\u8217's position to a series of challenges from other texts that appear to demand {\i lishma} specifically in the {\i writing} of the {\i get}. Rava opens: the {\i pasuk} states {\i vechatav lah} \u8212- and he should write it for her \u8212- {\i lishma}. Does this not refer to the writing of the {\i get}? How can Rabbi Meir claim that {\i lishma} in writing is irrelevant?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Vechatav=Signing\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Nachman responds: {\i lo, chasimas edim} \u8212- no, \u8220"writing\u8221" in the {\i pasuk} refers to the signing. {\i Vechatav lah lishma} means: have it signed for her sake. The word {\i vechatav} encompasses signing.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava Second\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava presses a second challenge: the {\i mishna} states {\i kol get shenichtav shelo lishum ishah pasul} \u8212- any {\i get} that was written not for the sake of the woman is invalid. The word {\i nichtav} \u8212- written \u8212- seems to refer unambiguously to the text of the {\i get}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Nichtav=Signed\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The answer is parallel: {\i eima shenichtam shelo lishum ishah} \u8212- read {\i nichtav} as meaning \u8220"signed.\u8221" Writing does not mean writing; it means signing. Again, what was \u8220"written\u8221" invalidly means what was \u8220"signed\u8221" without the correct intent.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Abaye Challenge\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye raises the strongest textual challenge. A later {\i mishna} states {\i keshu kotevo lishma} \u8212- when one writes part of it {\i lishma}, it is as though the whole was written {\i lishma}. Does this not explicitly indicate that {\i lishma} applies to the writing \u8212- specifically the key written portion?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Signing=Writing\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The answer: read {\i keshu kotevo} as {\i keshu chasmo} \u8212- when it is signed {\i lishma}, that is as if the whole was written {\i lishma}. Signing is a form of writing in the language of the text, and the entire challenge dissolves on this reading. The objection to this reading is that it departs from the plain sense of the word {\i kotevo}, which most naturally refers to the textual content of the {\i get}. Nevertheless, this is the {\i gemara}\u8217's answer.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Third Answer\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 An alternative resolution is also offered: {\i iy ba\u8217'it eima, hani masnitin mani Rabbi Eliezer hi} \u8212- if you prefer, one may simply say that those {\i mishnayot} requiring {\i lishma} in the writing follow Rabbi Eliezer, not Rabbi Meir. Under Rabbi Eliezer, the writing must be {\i lishma} \u8212- or at least the {\i toref}, the essential particulars, must be written {\i lishma}. Under Rabbi Meir, these requirements simply do not apply to the writing at all. There is no need to reinterpret the word {\i kotevo}; one need only recognize that the texts speaking of {\i lishma} in writing are Rabbi Eliezer\u8217's, and the texts under discussion follow Rabbi Meir.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Strongest Reading\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Abba: Blanket\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Abba draws the decisive conclusion from the structure of the {\i mishna} itself. If the {\i mishna} intended to say that these ineligible writers could write a {\i get} only under specific conditions \u8212- such as leaving the {\i toref} blank for a competent adult to fill in, or requiring {\i gadol omed al gabav} \u8212- it should have said so. The {\i mishna}\u8217's blanket statement that {\i hakol kesherim lichtov}, everyone is eligible to write, with no qualifications, is itself the message. The {\i mishna} is teaching that the writing of a {\i get} is immaterial.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yochanan: Immaterial\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This reading is confirmed by the tradition in the name of Rabbi Yochanan, transmitted by Rav Zeira: the {\i mishna} demonstrates conclusively that the writing of a {\i get} does not matter for Rabbi Meir.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ein Koach Leshmah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Kan hodi\u8217'ach she\u8217'ein koach lishma} \u8212- the {\i mishna} is informing us that there is no force to the {\i lishma} requirement in the writing of a {\i get}, according to Rabbi Meir, {\i de\u8217'amar edei chatimah karsei}. All that matters is who signs, not who writes. The {\i mishna}\u8217's sweeping formulation is its proof: it is stating that the writing is entirely immaterial, and the only reading consistent with that sweep is Rabbi Meir\u8217's.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Next Mishna: Bringing Get\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hakol Kosherim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The next {\i mishna} shifts from writing to delivery: {\i hakol kesherim l\u8217'havi et haget chutz m\u8217'cheresh shoteh vekatan} \u8212- everyone is eligible to bring a {\i get}, meaning to serve as a {\i shaliach} for its delivery, except for a {\i cheresh}, {\i shoteh}, and {\i katan}. Here, unlike the previous {\i mishna}, we are dealing explicitly with {\i shlichut} \u8212- the formal appointment of an agent \u8212- and the exclusions reflect the requirements of agency.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Blind Excluded\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} adds: {\i vesuma} \u8212- and also a blind person is excluded from serving as {\i shaliach}. The reason for this exclusion is not immediately self-evident and will be explored by the {\i gemara}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Non-Jew Excluded\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Ve\u8217'oved kochavim} \u8212- a non-Jew is also excluded from {\i shlichut} for delivering a {\i get}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Status Change Cases\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} then proceeds to address cases of status change during the course of the {\i shlichut}: {\i kiblo katan vehigdil} \u8212- if the {\i get} was received by a minor who subsequently became an adult; {\i cheresh venispa\u8217'ach} \u8212- a deaf-mute who recovered; {\i suma venitpateach} \u8212- a blind person who regained sight; {\i shoteh venishtapeh} \u8212- a {\i shoteh} who regained competence; {\i oved kochavim venisgayyer} \u8212- a non-Jew who converted \u8212- in all these cases, {\i pasul}. The appointment of {\i shlichut} must occur when the person already has the status that qualifies them as a {\i shaliach}. If they were appointed before acquiring that status, the {\i shlichut} is invalid even if they subsequently attain it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 However, the reverse sequence \u8212- beginning in a qualified state, lapsing temporarily, and returning \u8212- is treated differently. {\i Pikeach venitcharesh vechazar venitpakeach} \u8212- sighted, then deaf-mute, then again hearing; {\i patuach venistama vechazar venitpateach} \u8212- sighted, blind, and sighted again; {\i shafui venishtateh vechazar venishtapeh} \u8212- competent, then a {\i shoteh}, then competent again: {\i kasher} in all these cases.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Zeh Haklal\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} concludes with a general principle: {\i zeh haklal, kol shetechilato vesofo beda\u8217'at kasher} \u8212- this is the principle: anyone whose beginning and end are with {\i da\u8217'at} is valid. The {\i mishna} does not require {\i kashrus} at every intermediate stage; what matters is that the {\i shaliach} received the {\i get} from the husband and delivered it to the wife in the requisite state of {\i da\u8217'at} and eligibility.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Shlichut Requirements\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Bnei Daas\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} begins unpacking the exclusions. The exclusion of {\i cheresh}, {\i shoteh}, and {\i katan} is readily explained: {\i d\u8217'lav bnei deiah ninhu} \u8212- they are not {\i bnei da\u8217'at}, and the formal legal relationship of {\i shlichut} requires a person capable of genuine legal cognition and intent.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Bnei Krisus\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The exclusion of the non-Jew is explained by a different principle: {\i lav bar heiteira hu} \u8212- sometimes expressed as {\i lav bnei krisus} \u8212- he is not within the legal framework of {\i gittin vekiddushin}, our laws of divorce and marriage. He cannot represent a Jewish husband in an act governed by a legal framework that does not apply to him.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Blind Exclusion\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The exclusion of the blind person requires more explanation, since a blind person is generally considered a {\i bar da\u8217'at}. Rav Sheshet answers: {\i lefi she\u8217'eino yode\u8217'a miman notlah ulemi notnah lo} \u8212- because the blind person does not know from whom he is taking it and to whom he is giving it. Without visual identification, the precise execution of the {\i shlichut} \u8212- taking the {\i get} from the specific husband and delivering it to the specific wife \u8212- is compromised.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tziuta Dekala\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Yosef challenges Rav Sheshet\u8217's answer: {\i bitzi\u8217'uta dekala} \u8212- one can recognize by voice. He extends the analogy: how can a blind man have relations with his wife if he cannot see whom he is with? How can anyone have relations with a spouse in the dark at night? In all these cases, voice recognition suffices for personal identification. The same should apply to the blind {\i shaliach}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Bifanay Nichtav\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Yosef then offers his own answer: {\i hacha bechutz la\u8217'aretz askinan} \u8212- the case is specifically in {\i chutz la\u8217'aretz}, outside of {\i Eretz Yisrael}, where the {\i shaliach} must declare {\i bifanay nichtav uvifanay nechtam} \u8212- it was written and signed before me. This declaration requires that the {\i shaliach} have actually seen the writing and signing. Voice recognition of the scratching of the pen is insufficient for this purpose; {\i bifanay} requires actual visual witness. A blind person therefore cannot make this declaration and cannot serve as {\i shaliach} in {\i chutz la\u8217'aretz}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Patuach V\u8217'Nistama\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye raises a challenge to Rav Yosef\u8217's reasoning: if the only problem is the inability to say {\i bifanay nichtav}, then a person who was sighted when the {\i get} was written and signed \u8212- and therefore can truthfully make the declaration \u8212- but subsequently became blind before delivering the {\i get}, should be perfectly valid. He saw the writing; he can say {\i bifanay nichtav}. If so, the {\i mishna}\u8217's requirement that sighted-then-blind-then-sighted cases require restoration of sight at the end seems to exclude the case where the person ends blind \u8212- but on Rav Yosef\u8217's reasoning, there should be no problem even if the person remains blind at delivery, as long as he saw the writing. The {\i gemara} resolves this: even if the person ends blind, it is in fact valid. The reason the {\i mishna} formulated the case as {\i patuach venistama vechazar venitpateach} \u8212- sighted, blind, and sighted again \u8212- was for literary parallelism with the other cases involving {\i shoteh}, where the ABA pattern genuinely is required. For the blind case specifically, even the AB pattern would suffice.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Ashi\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Ashi derives the same conclusion from a close reading of the {\i mishna}\u8217's general principle: {\i zeh haklal kol shetechilato vesofo beda\u8217'at kasher} \u8212- the principle specifies {\i beda\u8217'at}, in a state of cognitive competence, at beginning and end. It does not say {\i kol shetechilato vesofo bekashrus} \u8212- in a state of {\i kashrus} or eligibility in every respect at beginning and end. Blindness is not a {\i da\u8217'at} problem; it is a different kind of {\i kashrus} issue. Therefore the {\i zeh haklal} does not require restoration of sight at the end, only {\i da\u8217'at} at beginning and end.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Eved as Shaliach\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Eved Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} poses a question to Rabbi Ami: {\i eved mahu she-ye\u8217'aseh shaliach lekabbel get isha miyad ba\u8217'ala} \u8212- can a slave, not the slave of either party, serve as a {\i shaliach} to receive or bring a {\i get}? A slave owned by the woman would be treated as an extension of her domain \u8212- receipt by her slave would be receipt by her. But what of an independent slave serving as a formal {\i shaliach}? The slave is partially obligated in {\i mitzvot}; is this sufficient?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Eved Inference\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 An initial argument from the structure of the {\i mishna} favors the slave\u8217's eligibility: {\i midkatanei pasul ve\u8217'akum bechada, michlal de\u8217'eved kasher} \u8212- since the {\i mishna} lists only a non-Jew among those excluded, one might infer that a slave is included among the valid {\i shelichim}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Assi Answer\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Assi in the name of Rabbi Yochanan rules against this: {\i ein eved na\u8217'aseh shaliach lekabbel get le\u8217'isha miyad ba\u8217'alah} \u8212- a slave cannot serve as {\i shaliach} to receive a {\i get} on behalf of a woman from her husband. The reason: {\i lefi she\u8217'eino betoras gittin vekiddushin} \u8212- because the laws of {\i gittin} and {\i kiddushin} do not apply to slaves. Marriage, as a legal institution, does not pertain to slaves; and therefore they are not within the {\i parsha} that would qualify them to serve as agents in matters governed by that {\i parsha}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Elazar Counter\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Elazar challenges this conclusion from a different direction. He does not dispute the ruling but questions whether the reasoning is internally consistent. If the criterion for {\i shlichut} is being within the relevant {\i parsha}, then a non-Jew should be able to serve as {\i shaliach} in areas of law where the non-Jew is within the {\i parsha}. And indeed, there are areas of {\i halacha} where non-Jews are within the {\i parsha} \u8212- yet they cannot be {\i shelichim} even there. This suggests that being within the {\i parsha} is not sufficient on its own; there must be an additional criterion.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Non-Jew Teruma\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The case in point is {\i teruma}. A non-Jew who owns land in {\i Eretz Yisrael} and separates {\i teruma} from his own grain \u8212- {\i akum vekuti shetaramu mishelahen terumasam terumah} \u8212- the {\i teruma} has legal status. The non-Jew is within the {\i parsha} of {\i teruma} in this sense. Yet if a non-Jew takes {\i teruma} on behalf of a Jew \u8212- {\i akum shetaram shel Yisrael afilu birshus ein terumasam terumah} \u8212- even if he was formally appointed, it does not work. So the non-Jew is in the {\i parsha} but cannot be a {\i shaliach}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gam Atem\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What is the additional criterion? The {\i gemara} derives it from the {\i pasuk}: {\i kein tarimu gam atem} \u8212- even you should take {\i teruma}. The {\i gam} expands to include the {\i shaliach}; the {\i atem} restricts the {\i shaliach} to be like the principal. {\i Mah atem Yisrael af sheluchachem Yisrael} \u8212- just as you are Jewish, so must your agent be Jewish. This requirement \u8212- that the {\i shaliach} be like the principal in relevant respects \u8212- is the second criterion.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two Criteria\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 There are thus two independent requirements for valid {\i shlichut}: first, the {\i shaliach} must be a {\i ben brit}, within the covenantal framework of obligated persons; second, the {\i shaliach} must be within the {\i parsha} \u8212- the specific domain of law governing the transaction. A non-Jew fails the first criterion and therefore cannot be a {\i shaliach} for anything involving Jewish law, regardless of whether the non-Jew is within the {\i parsha}. A slave satisfies the first criterion but may fail the second, depending on the transaction.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Bnei Brit Key\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The school of Rabbi Yannai \u8212- {\i d\u8217'bei Rabbi Yannai} \u8212- refines the formulation: {\i mah atem bnei brit af sheluchachem bnei brit} \u8212- it is not {\i Yisrael} that is the criterion, but {\i bnei brit}, members of the covenant. This is a somewhat broader category. A slave, male at least, has undergone {\i berit milah} and is bound by {\i mitzvot} in the relevant ways.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Eved Bnei Brit\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The implication is that slaves are {\i bnei brit}: they are circumcised and bound by the covenant of {\i mitzvot}. {\i Rashi} on this passage emphasizes both elements \u8212- {\i mehulin uvo\u8217'ei bivrit mitzvot}, circumcised and having entered into the covenant of {\i mitzvot}. And support is found from the {\i pasuk} in {\i Bamidbar} 18: the {\i meichotev etzecha ad sho\u8217'ev meimecha} \u8212- your hewers of wood and drawers of water \u8212- are your slaves, and the continuation is {\i la\u8217'avarcha bivrit Hashem Elokecha} \u8212- to bring you into the covenant of the Lord your God. The slaves are part of the {\i brit}. This raises, in passing, an interesting question about {\i areivut} and the degree to which slaves are part of the {\i am} in an extended sense.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 With this understanding: a non-Jew can never be a {\i shaliach} because the {\i gam atem} requirement \u8212- {\i bnei brit} \u8212- excludes him. A slave can be a {\i shaliach} for matters within his {\i parsha}, such as {\i terumot} and {\i ma\u8217'asrot}, because he satisfies the {\i bnei brit} requirement and is within that {\i parsha}. But a slave cannot be a {\i shaliach} for {\i gittin vekiddushin}, because the laws of {\i gittin} and {\i kiddushin} do not apply to him \u8212- he lacks {\i kiddushin} entirely \u8212- and he therefore fails the second criterion.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Chiya\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Chiya bar Abba in the name of Rabbi Yochanan states the ruling explicitly: {\i ein eved na\u8217'aseh shaliach lekabbel get isha} \u8212- a slave cannot be a {\i shaliach} to receive a {\i get} for a woman. The reason coheres with the two-criteria framework: while the slave satisfies {\i bnei brit}, he is not {\i betoras gittin vekiddushin} \u8212- not within the domain of the laws of divorce and marriage \u8212- and therefore cannot represent a principal in a transaction governed by those laws.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The daf closes with this ruling in place. The subsequent sugya, to be continued, addresses a further point arising from the slave\u8217's distinct status \u8212- an aspect of the discussion that belongs to the next session.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Gittin 24\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Slave Shlichim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Slave Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Today\u8217's daf is 24, and we resume in the middle of the {\i amud}, just before the {\i mishnah}. The previous discussion concerned the question of whether a slave can serve as a {\i shaliach}. There are two basic requirements for {\i shelichut}: first, the agent must be a member of the Jewish community, and second, the laws governing the relevant transaction must be personally applicable to the agent.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} had examined what can be derived from the {\i pasuk} of {\i gam atem} in the context of {\i terumah} \u8212- specifically, whether the phrase excludes only a non-Jew or extends to exclude a slave on the grounds that a slave is not fully considered {\i Yisrael}. The {\i gemara} concluded that a slave would in fact be included in the category of {\i ben brit}, obligated in {\i mitzvot}. Rashi points to the {\i pasuk} at the end of Devarim \u8212- {\i chotzev etzecha} \u8212- as establishing the slave\u8217's inclusion in the {\i brit}. A slave is therefore not categorically excluded from serving as a {\i shaliach}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Bar Hachi\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Nevertheless, the second requirement remains: in order to serve as a {\i shaliach} for a particular legal domain, the agent must be, in the language of the {\i gemara}, {\i bar hachi} \u8212- the relevant laws must actually apply to him or her.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Terumot/Maasrot\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A slave can serve as a {\i shaliach} for {\i terumot u\u8217'maasrot} because those obligations are relevant to the slave.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gittin/Kiddushin Exclusi\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A slave, however, cannot serve as a {\i shaliach} for {\i gittin ve\u8217'kiddushin}, because there is no binding {\i kiddushin} or {\i gittin} that applies to a slave \u8212- even though a slave is {\i chayav} in {\i mitzvot} more broadly. That is the conceptual exclusion.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Gitov Veyado\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Pregnant Slave Challenge\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 With that established, the {\i gemara} presents a statement of Rabbi Chiya bar Abba in the name of Rabbi Yochanan \u8212- appearing approximately fifteen lines from the top of the {\i amud} \u8212- which reads: {\i ein eved na\u8217'aseh shaliach l\u8217'kabel get isha miyad ba\u8217'alah}, a slave cannot serve as a {\i shaliach} to receive a bill of divorce on behalf of a woman from her husband\u8217's hand, {\i lefi she\u8217'eino b\u8217'torat gittin ve\u8217'kiddushin}, because a slave is not within the legal category of {\i gittin} and {\i kiddushin}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The statement then continues: {\i ve\u8217'af al pi she\u8217'shaninu}, although we have taught that a person can give his female slave a {\i shtar shichrur} and say, in effect: you remain a slave, but your unborn child I am freeing \u8212- and this actually works. {\i Im hayta ubarah zachsa lo}: if she was pregnant, she takes possession of the writ of manumission on behalf of the child and the child is freed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} immediately asks: {\i mai im hayta ubarah zachsa lo}? How is this relevant to the preceding discussion? And more pointedly \u8212- how is she functioning here? Is she serving as a {\i shaliach} for her {\i ubar}, her fetus? If so, there would be no problem on the grounds of {\i gittin ve\u8217'kiddushin}, since a slave is certainly within the category of {\i shichrur} and could in principle receive a {\i shtar shichrur} for herself or for another. So why should this case pose any challenge at all?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} proceeds to develop this question. The real concern is not the {\i bar hachi} problem of {\i gittin ve\u8217'kiddushin}, but something more fundamental: the question of {\i netinah} \u8212- the act of giving. A slave who belongs to a master cannot be a valid separate legal domain for the purposes of receiving a document from that same master. If the master gives a {\i shtar shichrur} to slave A in order for slave A to receive it on behalf of slave B \u8212- when both slaves are owned by the same master \u8212- it is as though the master took it from his right hand and placed it in his left. There is no genuine act of {\i netinah}, because slave A, even after accepting the {\i shtar shichrur}, remains within the master\u8217's domain.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This contrasts with the principle of {\i gitov veyado ba\u8217'im ke\u8217'echad}: when the master frees his own slave, in that very act of giving, the slave simultaneously becomes an independent entity. The giving itself creates a new domain, and so the act of {\i netinah} is valid. But this only works when the slave receiving the document is the very slave being freed by that act. When the slave receiving the document is not the one being freed in that act, no such transformation occurs, and the document cannot be said to have been genuinely transferred.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 So the question becomes sharp: when the master gives a {\i shtar shichrur} to his pregnant slave to free her unborn child, both the slave woman and the fetus are his property. He is not freeing her \u8212- only the fetus. Why then is this not considered giving from his right hand to his left? Where is the {\i ma\u8217'aseh netinah}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two Explanations\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Ki ata Rav Shmuel bar Yehuda amar Rabbi Yochanan tarti amar} \u8212- when Rav Shmuel bar Yehuda came and reported the teaching of Rabbi Yochanan, he clarified that Rabbi Yochanan actually said two things that were not directly connected to each other. The statement about a slave being unable to receive a {\i get} on behalf of a woman was one statement. The subsequent clause about the pregnant slave receiving a {\i shtar shichrur} for her fetus was not a challenge to it; it qualified a different earlier teaching \u8212- namely that a slave {\i can} serve as a {\i shaliach} to receive a {\i shtar shichrur} for a fellow slave from {\i another} master. The pregnant slave case is a separate ruling with its own logic, which then has to be explained.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Emor lo shenei gedolei hador perishu et hadavar}, the {\i gemara} says: two of the great ones of the generation interpreted this matter. And who are they? Rabbi Zeira and Rabbi Shmuel bar Rav Yitzchak. {\i Chad amar} \u8212- one said one thing; {\i vechad amar} \u8212- the other offered a complementary explanation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Freeing Half\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The first explanation: {\i ha mni Rabbi hi} \u8212- this ruling follows the position of Rabbi, who taught {\i hamishacher chetzi avdo kanah}, that one can free half of one\u8217's slave. The principle of {\i gitov veyado ba\u8217'im ke\u8217'echad} applies even when the slave does not become a completely independent entity. One can renounce ownership over half the slave \u8212- not a specific limb necessarily, but a conceptual half \u8212- and because in that act of giving, half of the slave becomes free, there is enough of a transformation to constitute a valid {\i netinah} for that half.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Fetus Separation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The second explanation builds on the first: {\i mai ta\u8217'ama de-Rabbi beha} \u8212- how would Rabbi\u8217's principle apply to the specific case of the pregnant slave? The answer is {\i ubar yerech imo hu}, the fetus is not considered a separate entity but is legally part of the mother. Therefore, when the master frees the fetus, it is as though he is freeing a specific limb or part of the slave woman herself \u8212- {\i denafek minei she\u8217'it lei leachad me\u8217'eivarav}. It is as though he said: your right arm is no longer mine; only your left arm remains in my service. So freeing the fetus is a kind of {\i chetzi eved} \u8212- freeing an identifiable part of the slave woman.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gitov Principle\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This then produces enough of a {\i gitov veyado ba\u8217'im ke\u8217'echad} dynamic: because in this very act of the slave woman receiving the {\i shtar shichrur}, some portion of her becomes her own entity \u8212- not the fetus physically taking hold of the document, but the conceptual reality that this act simultaneously partially frees her \u8212- there is sufficient of a separate domain created to constitute a genuine {\i netinah}. This is admittedly a {\i chiddush}: the separation is entirely conceptual, not physical.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 To summarize the framework: a slave can serve as {\i shaliach} for something relevant to the slave\u8217's own legal status \u8212- yes for {\i shtar shichrur}, yes for {\i terumot u\u8217'maasrot}, but not for {\i gittin ve\u8217'kiddushin}. And a master cannot give a {\i shtar shichrur} through one of his own slaves to free another of his own slaves, because there is no genuine {\i ma\u8217'aseh netinah} \u8212- it is all within his domain. The exception is when he frees that slave directly, or frees half of that slave, or frees the fetus of that slave \u8212- in all of those cases the principle of {\i gitov veyado ba\u8217'im ke\u8217'echad} applies, and the act is valid.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Women Witnesses\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Disqualified Witness\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now turns to the {\i mishnah}, which introduces a new dimension of the question of who may deliver a {\i get}. The {\i mishnah} states: {\i af hanashim she\u8217'einam ne\u8217'emanot lomar met ba\u8217'alah} \u8212- certain women who are not believed to testify that another woman\u8217's husband has died are nevertheless {\i ne\u8217'emanot l\u8217'havi et gittah} \u8212- believed to deliver her {\i get}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is drawn from a broader discussion in Masechet Yevamot, where we accept the testimony of a single witness, including a woman, that a husband has died, allowing the wife to remarry. However, certain women are excluded from this leniency because we suspect they harbor animosity toward the wife and might falsely report the husband\u8217's death in order to bring disaster upon her \u8212- causing her to remarry while her husband is still alive.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} enumerates these women: {\i chamotah}, the mother-in-law; {\i u\u8217'vat chamotah}, the mother-in-law\u8217's daughter (the sister-in-law); {\i v\u8217'tzaratah}, the co-wife \u8212- obviously suspect; {\i v\u8217'yevamtah}, the husband\u8217's brother\u8217's wife \u8212- because if the husband dies without children, the husband\u8217's brother will perform {\i yibum} with this woman, making the existing wife a {\i tzarah} to her; and {\i u\u8217'vat ba\u8217'alah}, the stepdaughter.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 All of these women are potentially suspect of wishing harm to the wife. Yet the {\i mishnah} says they are believed to bring a {\i get}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Self-Proving Doc\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} then asks: {\i mah bein get l\u8217'mitah}? What is the difference between delivering a {\i get} and testifying about death? The answer is {\i she\u8217'haktav mochiach} \u8212- because here there is a signed writ, and we are not relying solely on their word. The document is signed by witnesses, and so we do not suspect the women of forgery. The document speaks for itself.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} further states that {\i ha\u8217'ishah atzmah mevi\u8217'ah gittah}, a woman may bring her own {\i get}, {\i v\u8217'hi shaliach l\u8217'atzmah} \u8212- she functions as a {\i shaliach} to herself \u8212- though the conceptual problem of {\i netinah} in that arrangement will be addressed shortly by the {\i gemara}. In any case, she must say {\i bifnei nichtav u\u8217'bifnei nechtam}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is itself somewhat puzzling: the declaration of {\i bifnei nichtav u\u8217'bifnei nechtam} is only required when bringing a {\i get} from a distant location. If the husband gave it to her directly, why would she need to say this? And if she is bringing it from elsewhere, how does she give it to herself? These questions will be explored by the {\i gemara} in the next section.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i baraita} is then cited that seems to contradict the {\i mishnah}: {\i keshem she\u8217'eina ne\u8217'emanet lomar met ba\u8217'alah kach eina ne\u8217'emanet l\u8217'havi et gittah} \u8212- the very women who are not believed to testify about death are also not believed to deliver a {\i get}. This appears to directly contradict the {\i mishnah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Yosef Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Yosef resolves the apparent contradiction: {\i lo kashya kan b\u8217'Eretz kan b\u8217'Chutz la\u8217'Aretz} \u8212- it is not a difficulty. The two sources refer to different contexts. In {\i Eretz Yisrael}, where the {\i shaliach} does not need to declare {\i bifnei nichtav u\u8217'bifnei nechtam}, the woman is barely saying anything at all; the {\i get} speaks for itself. {\i B\u8217'Eretz d\u8217'lav adibura didah samchinan} \u8212- we are not relying on her testimony; the writ does the work. So the {\i mishnah} is correct that she is believed in {\i Eretz Yisrael}. In {\i Chutz la\u8217'Aretz}, however, {\i dibura didah ka samchinan} \u8212- we are relying on her declaration of {\i bifnei nichtav u\u8217'bifnei nechtam} to validate the {\i get}, and in that case she is not believed, as the {\i baraita} states.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Abaye Counterargument\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye challenged this: {\i ad raba, ipcha mistabra} \u8212- on the contrary, the opposite logic is more compelling. In {\i Eretz Yisrael}, precisely because there is no formal declaration of {\i bifnei nichtav u\u8217'bifnei nechtam}, the wife remains vulnerable. If the husband were ever to contest the {\i get}, she would have difficulty validating it, and her situation could be ruined. {\i D\u8217'ika l\u8217'meimar l\u8217'kula ka michavna} \u8212- we might suspect that this hostile woman engineered the entire situation precisely to set the wife up for catastrophe. Therefore, in {\i Eretz Yisrael}, these women should {\i not} be believed. In {\i Chutz la\u8217'Aretz}, by contrast, once {\i bifnei nichtav u\u8217'bifnei nechtam} has been declared, the husband cannot challenge the {\i get}. The wife is protected. So in {\i Chutz la\u8217'Aretz}, these women {\i are} believed \u8212- the woman is secure, and there is far less to be concerned about.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Protection Via Bifnei\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i baraita} is then cited: {\i tanya k\u8217'vatei d\u8217'Abaye} \u8212- the ruling follows Abaye\u8217's reasoning, establishing that the problem is specifically in {\i Eretz Yisrael}, where the wife is left vulnerable and susceptible to having her life ruined by a hostile {\i shaliach}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Ashi then adds that the {\i mishnah} itself supports Abaye, through close reading. The {\i mishnah} specifies that a woman bringing her own {\i get} must say {\i bifnei nichtav u\u8217'bifnei nechtam}, which means the entire context of the {\i mishnah} \u8212- including the ruling about these potentially hostile women \u8212- is one of delivery from {\i Chutz la\u8217'Aretz}. The {\i mishnah} is therefore discussing the case where these women are {\i believed}, namely when they bring a {\i get} from outside the land and make the declaration that protects the wife. {\i Shma mina}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 As for Rav Yosef \u8212- who held that the {\i mishnah}\u8217's leniency applies only to {\i Eretz Yisrael} \u8212- one might ask how he would handle the fact that the opening case in the {\i mishnah} (about a {\i suma} who cannot bring a {\i get}) and the closing case (a woman bringing her own {\i get}) both clearly concern {\i Chutz la\u8217'Aretz} and {\i bifnei nichtav u\u8217'bifnei nechtam}, while the middle section (these hostile women) would on his reading concern {\i Eretz Yisrael}. His answer: {\i reisha ve-seifa b\u8217'chutz la\u8217'aretz, metzi\u8217'asa ba-aretz} \u8212- yes, the beginning and end discuss {\i Chutz la\u8217'Aretz}, the middle discusses {\i Eretz Yisrael}. This is a possible reading, though the evidence overwhelmingly favors Abaye\u8217's position that the entire {\i mishnah} is set in a {\i bifnei nichtav u\u8217'bifnei nechtam} context, i.e., {\i Chutz la\u8217'Aretz}. The phrase {\i mipnei she\u8217'haktav mochiach} \u8212- that the writ itself testifies \u8212- is what pushed Rav Yosef toward his reading; to him it implies that the woman is saying nothing at all and only the document speaks, pointing to {\i Eretz Yisrael}. But the {\i gemara}\u8217's evidence tips decisively toward Abaye.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shimon Ben Elazar\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Shimon ben Elazar then cites a teaching in the name of Rabbi Akiva: {\i ishah ne\u8217'emenet l\u8217'havi gittah mi\u8217'kal vachomer} \u8212- a woman may bring her own {\i get} by means of a {\i kal vachomer}. The argument runs: women who are not believed to say that this woman\u8217's husband died are nonetheless believed to deliver her {\i get}. Should it not follow by {\i kal vachomer} that a woman who {\i is} believed to testify that her own husband died should certainly be believed to deliver her own {\i get}? This establishes the wife\u8217's credibility as her own {\i shaliach}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But then the {\i gemara} applies the limiting principle {\i mimakom shebata} \u8212- you cannot derive a conclusion that is stronger than its source. Since the {\i kal vachomer} is built on the case where those hostile women bring a {\i get} with the declaration {\i bifnei nichtav u\u8217'bifnei nechtam}, the same requirement must apply when the wife brings her own {\i get}: {\i tzricha shetomar bifnei nichtav u\u8217'bifnei nechtam}. And this, in turn, confirms that we are speaking throughout the {\i mishnah} in the context of delivery from {\i Chutz la\u8217'Aretz} \u8212- supporting Abaye\u8217's position.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Woman Delivers Own Get\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Self-Agency Problem\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now turns to the conceptual puzzle raised by the {\i mishnah}\u8217's ruling that {\i ha\u8217'ishah atzmah mevi\u8217'ah gittah} \u8212- a woman can bring her own {\i get}. The problem is this: {\i isha, mikdei mati gitta l\u8217'yada, igrashah lah} \u8212- the moment the {\i get} reaches her hand, she is divorced. How, then, can she simultaneously function as a {\i shaliach} to deliver something to herself? The instant of delivery and the instant of receiving are the same moment, and more fundamentally, the {\i shaliach} and the recipient are the same person.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Huna Solution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Huna offers an initial answer: {\i be\u8217'omer lo titgarshi bo ela bifnei beit din ploni} \u8212- the husband stipulates that the divorce should not take effect until she presents it before a specific {\i beit din} in another city. So she must travel to, say, Miami, and the divorce takes effect only upon her arrival there. This creates an interval during which she is a {\i shaliach} transporting the {\i get} without yet being divorced.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ground Placement\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But the {\i gemara} presses: even under that formulation, she is still not a conventional {\i shaliach} to herself; the moment she arrives and the divorce takes effect, she is simultaneously {\i shaliach} and recipient. Moreover, why does she need to say {\i bifnei nichtav u\u8217'bifnei nechtam} at all if the husband gave it to her directly?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then considers a different approach attributed to Rav Huna bar Manoach in the name of Rav Acha the son of Rav Ika. He suggests the following formulation: the husband instructs her to travel to a designated place, and when she arrives, to place the {\i get} on the ground \u8212- {\i anachi a\u8217'ara} \u8212- and then pick it up. The idea is that she, in her capacity as {\i shaliach}, puts the {\i get} down; then, in her capacity as the wife, she picks it up. The problem is immediately apparent: this is not an act of {\i netinah}. She gave it to the ground, not to herself. The {\i gemara} invokes the teaching of Rava: {\i tali gittech mei\u8217'al gabei karka lo amar klum} \u8212- if a husband says to his wife, \u8220"Pick up your {\i get} from the ground,\u8221" he has said nothing. That is not a valid act of giving. The same logic applies here.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Status Change\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} therefore arrives at the formulation of Rav Huna bar Manoach: {\i hevei shaliach l\u8217'holacha ad demati hatam, ki mati hatam hevei shaliach l\u8217'kabala v\u8217'kabli at gittech} \u8212- be a {\i shaliach l\u8217'holacha} for transport until you arrive there, and when you arrive, your status changes: you will become a {\i shaliach l\u8217'kablah}, and you will receive your {\i get}. The Rashba notes that the husband need not literally appoint her as {\i shaliach l\u8217'kablah}; she simply becomes herself. But the formulation is stated in parallel terms to clarify the conceptual structure.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The key moment is this: she is holding the {\i get} as the husband\u8217's agent. At the designated place, at the designated moment, her identity shifts \u8212- she transitions from holding the document as {\i shaliach} to holding it as the wife. That transition itself, that change of legal identity while holding the document, constitutes the act of {\i netinah}. It is entirely conceptual and in no way physical, but that is precisely the point: she went from holding it in one capacity to holding it in another, and that is sufficient to constitute giving.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is because of this conceptual act of {\i netinah} that she must say {\i bifnei nichtav u\u8217'bifnei nechtam} \u8212- she is, after all, bringing it from one jurisdiction to another, and the declaration is required.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Agency Survival\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara}, however, objects: {\i veha lo hazra shelichut eitzel ba\u8217'al} \u8212- the {\i shelichut} did not return to the husband. The principle implied here, articulated in the manner Rashi and Tosafot explain it, is that for genuine {\i shelichut} to exist, the agent\u8217's identity must survive beyond the act itself. Theoretically, the agent should be able to return to the principal and report: \u8220"I completed what you instructed me to do.\u8221" In this case, however, the only way she can complete the act is by ceasing to be the husband\u8217's agent. Her capacity as {\i shaliach} is extinguished at the very moment of execution. Since the {\i shelichut} cannot survive the act, it cannot properly be said to have functioned as a valid agency in the first place.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Intermediary Solutions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Given this difficulty, the {\i gemara} explores alternative approaches. One suggestion: when she arrives at the destination, she should appoint someone else as her {\i shaliach l\u8217'kablah} to receive the {\i get} from her. This way she transfers it as the husband\u8217's {\i shaliach}, and a separate person receives it on her behalf as wife \u8212- two distinct parties, two distinct roles, a genuine act of {\i netinah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This, however, immediately raises another issue: {\i hanicha leman de\u8217'amar isha osa shaliach l\u8217'kablah miyad shaliach ba\u8217'al} \u8212- this works only if a woman can appoint a {\i shaliach l\u8217'kablah} to receive a {\i get} from her husband\u8217's {\i shaliach}. And that is itself a matter of debate, which the {\i gemara} will now address in the following section.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Alternatively \u8212- and the {\i gemara} presents this as well \u8212- she is instructed: when you arrive, say {\i bifnei nichtav u\u8217'bifnei nechtam} before a {\i beit din}, and then the {\i beit din} will appoint a second {\i shaliach l\u8217'holacha} to carry it further and ultimately hand it to her. A {\i beit din} functions as an institutional intermediary, which is in fact a practical arrangement used in real cases: when a {\i shaliach} cannot complete delivery, the {\i shaliach} transfers responsibility to a {\i beit din}, which then arranges a replacement. In both solutions, the key move is that a second party becomes involved so that she is not simultaneously the agent delivering and the person receiving.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One may note that the {\i gemara} does not raise the seemingly simpler solution of placing the {\i get} in her property \u8212- which in many contexts constitutes a valid {\i kinyan} and {\i netinah}. This absence is itself worth noting, and the {\i gemara}\u8217's preference for these conceptual solutions over that apparently straightforward one remains an open question.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Layered Agency Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Question Posed\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 As noted, the {\i gemara}\u8217's solution of having the wife appoint her own {\i shaliach l\u8217'kablah} to receive the {\i get} from the husband\u8217's {\i shaliach} raises a disputed question: {\i hanicha leman de\u8217'amar isha osa shaliach l\u8217'kablah miyad shaliach ba\u8217'al} \u8212- this works only for the opinion that a woman {\i may} appoint a {\i shaliach} to receive a {\i get} from the husband\u8217's {\i shaliach}. {\i Ela leman de\u8217'amar ein isha osa shaliach l\u8217'kablah miyad shaliach ba\u8217'al} \u8212- but according to the opinion that she may {\i not} do so, what can be said?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A woman may certainly appoint a {\i shaliach l\u8217'kablah} to receive a {\i get} from the husband directly. The question is whether she may do so when the {\i get} is being delivered by the husband\u8217's own {\i shaliach}. This is disputed, and the {\i gemara} now examines the grounds for the restrictive opinion.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Bizayon Ba\u8217'al\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One explanation for why a woman cannot appoint a {\i shaliach l\u8217'kablah} to receive from the husband\u8217's {\i shaliach} is {\i mishum bizayon deba\u8217'al} \u8212- it is disparaging to the husband. The husband chose to send his {\i get} through a {\i shaliach}; he was not present himself. If the wife then also sends a {\i shaliach} rather than appearing herself, the husband might feel doubly slighted. He is not there in person, and neither is she \u8212- the {\i get} passes between two agents with neither principal present. This is considered an affront to the husband\u8217's dignity.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Now, if that is the concern, then {\i vehacha ba\u8217'al lo kapid} \u8212- in our case, the husband himself has instructed her to arrange things this way. He has no basis to object. If the {\i bizayon} concern is the entire rationale, then when the husband explicitly authorizes this arrangement, it is entirely resolved.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Property Transfer Issue\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A second explanation: the concern is {\i mishum chatzeira haba lachar mikan} \u8212- the arrangement looks too much like a case of transferring property through a series of intermediate transactions, in a way that creates structural ambiguity about whether a genuine {\i netinah} occurred. Consider: if a man places a {\i get} in a courtyard and then the courtyard is transferred to his wife, is she divorced? No \u8212- there was no valid {\i netinah} at the time the {\i get} was placed, and the subsequent transfer of the property does not retroactively create one. This case, while only loosely analogous, resembles the situation where neither the husband nor the wife is the direct party in the {\i get} exchange \u8212- the husband\u8217's {\i shaliach} gives it to the wife\u8217's {\i shaliach} \u8212- and it begins to look structurally similar to those cases of indirect property transfer where no genuine {\i netinah} takes place.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If this is the concern, it is harder to resolve simply by pointing to the husband\u8217's explicit instruction, because the structural problem \u8212- the resemblance to {\i chatzeira} transfer cases \u8212- persists regardless.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Acceptable Pathways\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} therefore proposes an alternative approach for this second opinion. The husband instructs her: be a {\i shaliach l\u8217'holacha} until you arrive at the destination, and when you arrive, appoint a second {\i shaliach l\u8217'holacha} to replace you. You then receive the {\i get} \u8212- as yourself, the wife \u8212- from that second {\i shaliach l\u8217'holacha}. In this formulation, the wife is not appointing a {\i shaliach l\u8217'kablah} to receive from the husband\u8217's {\i shaliach}; rather, she transfers her role as {\i shaliach l\u8217'holacha} to a second party, and then separately receives the {\i get} as the wife from that second party. This avoids the problem regardless of which rationale underlies the restrictive opinion.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} adds: {\i v\u8217'ei ba\u8217'it eima} \u8212- alternatively, she may go before a {\i beit din}, declare {\i bifnei nichtav u\u8217'bifnei nechtam}, and transfer the task to the {\i beit din}, which then appoints a new {\i shaliach l\u8217'holacha} to deliver the {\i get} to her. Tosafot notes that this is essentially a variation on the same structure. But the principle is the same: by bringing in a {\i beit din} as the institutional intermediary, the chain is broken into two clear stages, and a genuine act of {\i netinah} from a distinct party to the wife can take place.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The conceptual elegance of the solution \u8212- that the wife\u8217's mere change of legal status while holding the {\i get} could constitute an act of {\i netinah} \u8212- ultimately does not survive the {\i gemara}\u8217's scrutiny, because of the {\i shelichut} survival problem. In the end, the {\i gemara} requires a second party to be present so that there is an actual giving from one domain to another.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Lishmah Requirement\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Four Invalid Cases\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 With {\i Hadran alach hameivi get} \u8212- the conclusion of the second {\i perek} \u8212- we now enter the third {\i perek}, which addresses the criteria for the writing of a {\i get} itself, centering on the requirement of {\i lishmah}. This is thematically apt: according to Rabba, the very purpose of {\i bifnei nichtav u\u8217'bifnei nechtam} was to ensure {\i lishmah} \u8212- that the {\i get} was written specifically for this man and this woman. The third {\i perek} thus deepens that theme directly.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} states: {\i kol get shenichtav shelo l\u8217'shem ishah pasul} \u8212- any {\i get} written not for the sake of the specific woman is invalid. The {\i mishnah} then presents four cases.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Practice Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The first: {\i haya over bashuk veshamah kol sofrim makrin} \u8212- one was walking in the marketplace and heard the sound of a scribal school, where scribes were being instructed by their teacher reciting aloud: so-and-so divorces so-and-so from such-and-such a place. The student scribes were practicing by writing out this text. Now, a man hears this and recognizes that the names being called out are his name and his wife\u8217's name. May he take this {\i get} and use it to divorce his wife? {\i Pasul} \u8212- it is invalid, for it was not written for him and his wife.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava clarifies: {\i besofrim ha\u8217'asuyin lehitlamed askinan} \u8212- we are dealing with a scribal school practicing. They were not writing this {\i get} for anyone in particular; it was purely practice. Rav Ashi adds a textual proof: the {\i mishnah} says {\i sofrim makrin} \u8212- someone was {\i reciting} to them \u8212- not that the scribes themselves were writing on their own initiative. The teacher dictated; they wrote as practice. {\i Shma mina}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Discarded Get\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The second case: a man wrote a {\i get} with intent to divorce his wife and then changed his mind and discarded it. Another man in the same city happens to share the same name, and his wife shares the same name as the first man\u8217's wife. He finds the discarded {\i get} and proposes using it. {\i Pasul legaresh es ishto} \u8212- invalid. Even though this {\i get} was written for the genuine purpose of {\i gerushin} \u8212- not mere practice \u8212- it was not written for {\i this} man and {\i this} woman.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Wrong Wife\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The third case: a man has two wives, both with the same name. He wrote a {\i get} to divorce his elder wife. {\i Lo yegaresh bo es haketanah} \u8212- he may not use it to divorce the younger. Even though the {\i get} was written for genuine {\i gerushin}, for him, the correct names appear \u8212- still, it was written for wife number one and cannot be used for wife number two.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Unspecified Wife\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The fourth case: the man instructs the {\i lavlar} \u8212- the scribe \u8212- to write a {\i get} saying: I will divorce whichever wife I choose. He leaves it open-ended, intending to decide later which wife to divorce with it. {\i Pasul legaresh bo} \u8212- this too is invalid.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} presents a {\i tana devei Rabbi Yishmael} who articulates the escalating logic of these four cases. Not only is the one written by the scribal school invalid \u8212- for it was not written {\i l\u8217'shem gerushin} at all \u8212- but even one written genuinely for divorce purposes is invalid if it was written for a {\i different} man. And not only that, but even one written for the right man is invalid if written for the {\i wrong wife}. And not only that, but even one written for the right man without specifying which of his wives is also invalid \u8212- even when it is not written for a specific wrong wife but simply left open.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ketav Derivation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Mai ta\u8217'ama}? The {\i gemara} explains. Had the Torah simply said to deliver a {\i sefer keritut}, the only requirement would have been that it function as an instrument of divorce. A {\i get} written for a different person would still qualify, since it was written as a genuine {\i get}. {\i Kasav Rachmana vekasav} \u8212- the Torah\u8217's formulation, \u8220"and he shall write\u8221" ({\i vekasav lah}), teaches that the writing must be {\i for him} \u8212- {\i l\u8217'shem} the specific {\i megaresh}. This excludes a {\i get} written by someone else for another person. And {\i lah} \u8212- \u8220"for her\u8221" \u8212- teaches that it must be written for this specific woman, excluding the case where a man wrote it for his elder wife and now wishes to use it for his younger.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ein Bereirah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 As for the open-ended case \u8212- {\i le\u8217'eizo she\u8217'ertzeh agaresh bo} \u8212- the {\i gemara} explains this as {\i ein bereirah}: one cannot leave a legal act undefined and allow a later determination to retroactively fix what the act was \u8220"for.\u8221" The {\i lah} requirement demands clarity {\i at the time of writing}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot adds an important qualification: this principle applies here even according to opinions that generally accept {\i yesh bereirah} in other legal contexts \u8212- for instance, the retroactive designation of {\i terumot u\u8217'maasrot}. Even those who would normally permit later determination agree that here it is invalid, {\i mishum de\u8217'vekasav lah lishmah mashma sheyehe mevurar besha\u8217'as kesivah} \u8212- because the requirement of {\i lah}, of writing specifically for {\i her}, implies that it must be definitively clear at the moment of writing for whom the {\i get} is being written.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One may note that this entire analysis of {\i lishmah} follows the view of Rabbi Eliezer, who places emphasis on the {\i ketivah} \u8212- the act of writing. According to Rabbi Meir, {\i vekasav} is understood to refer to {\i vechasam}, the signing, and as long as the signing reflects the proper parties, the writing itself is less critical. This explains why the {\i stam mishnah} here, with its focus on {\i lishmah} requirements in writing, follows Rabbi Eliezer \u8212- while an earlier {\i mishnah} permitting a {\i cheresh shoteh ve\u8217'katan} to write a {\i get} reflected Rabbi Meir\u8217's position. This juxtaposition itself prompted the {\i gemara} to explore ways of reconciling those two {\i mishnayot}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Ambiguous Names\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Name Ambiguity\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} states that a man may write a {\i get} for his elder wife and validly use it to divorce her \u8212- {\i hagedolah matzi megaresh bah}. This assumes that even though both wives share the same name, the {\i get} is nonetheless valid for the one it was written for. This immediately raises a broader question: if two people in the same city share the name of the husband, and two people share the name of the wife, is a {\i get} with those names sufficient? Is it valid if the document itself does not unambiguously identify which parties are intended?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This question carries particular weight according to Rabbi Meir, for whom {\i edei chasimah karti} \u8212- the witnesses who sign the {\i get} are the ones who, in effect, validate the divorce \u8212- and the {\i get} must also function as independent {\i edut}, testimony. If the testimony is ambiguous \u8212- if we cannot know from the document itself which parties are referred to \u8212- perhaps the {\i get} is inherently defective.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava\u8217's Parallel\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava draws a parallel to the laws of debt. If two people named Yosef ben Shimon live in the same city, and a creditor holds a {\i shtar chov} \u8212- a debt instrument \u8212- that says Yosef ben Shimon owes a certain sum, the creditor cannot collect from the {\i wrong} Yosef ben Shimon. The instrument alone does not establish which one is meant.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Holder Identity\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Yet the answer in the {\i get} context is that ambiguity is resolved by the identity of the party {\i holding} the document. If the woman is holding the {\i get}, then her name is established by her possession. The name in the {\i get} that identifies the husband is then necessarily the name of her husband \u8212- the man who was married to {\i her}. So even if there are two men with the same name in the city, the fact that she is holding the {\i get} identifies which man it refers to: by definition, it refers to her husband.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Abaye Challenge\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye, however, challenges this resolution with a pointed question. In the second case of the {\i mishnah} \u8212- where a man found a discarded {\i get} whose names matched his own and his wife\u8217's \u8212- we ruled that {\i get} invalid: {\i sheni hu de-lo matzi megaresh bah, ha-rishon matzi megaresh bah}. The first man could use it, the second cannot. But this seems inconsistent with Rava\u8217's principle. If the holder\u8217's identity resolves ambiguity, why can\u8217't the second man use the discarded {\i get}? He is holding it, and his wife\u8217's name matches \u8212- should that not settle which party is meant?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Moreover, Abaye notes: we said that when two people share the same name, one cannot use a {\i shtar chov} to collect from the other \u8212- {\i veha amrinan de-lo acher yakhol lehotzi aleihem shtar chov}. That is, a third party named Michael cannot present a debt instrument saying Yosef ben Shimon owes money and collect from either Yosef ben Shimon. But when {\i Yosef ben Shimon himself} holds it, the identity of the relevant Yosef ben Shimon is clear. In the {\i get} case, when a woman holds a {\i get} and the name of the husband appears in it, the question is which husband is meant. Unlike a {\i shtar chov}, where the holder\u8217's identity resolves the question of the debtor, in a {\i get} it is not the {\i holder} whose identity is in question \u8212- it is the {\i other party}, the husband. Having the wife hold the document does not by itself tell us which husband she is referring to.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye\u8217's challenge is, as the {\i gemara} acknowledges, somewhat striking. After all, if we know who the wife is, we necessarily know who her husband is \u8212- at least in a case where she has only one husband. But Abaye is pressing on the general principle.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Edei Mesirah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} provides the resolution: {\i be\u8217'idei mesirah de-Rabbi Eliezer hi, hacha nami be\u8217'idei mesirah de-Rabbi Eliezer hi} \u8212- this entire framework operates according to the view of Rabbi Eliezer, who holds that what validates a {\i get} is not {\i edei chasimah} \u8212- the witnesses who signed \u8212- but {\i edei mesirah} \u8212- witnesses to the act of delivery itself. For Rabbi Eliezer, the {\i get} does not need to function as self-contained testimony. Its validity rests not on the document\u8217's ability to independently attest to the divorce but on the fact that witnesses observed the act of transfer. Therefore, any ambiguity in the document itself is immaterial \u8212- the witnesses to delivery observed precisely who gave the {\i get} to whom, and {\i that} is what establishes the divorce.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 For Rabbi Meir, by contrast, {\i edei chasimah karti} \u8212- the witnesses who signed are the essential validating mechanism \u8212- and the document must be able to testify on its own. Under Rabbi Meir\u8217's framework, a {\i get} whose names are shared by others in the town would be problematic, since the document cannot independently identify the parties. Rabbi Eliezer has no such requirement; external witnesses to delivery supply what the document alone might lack.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is why the {\i gemara} notes that the observation could appear strange \u8212- once we know who the wife is (from her holding it), the husband\u8217's identity follows necessarily. The {\i gemara}\u8217's answer, that we follow Rabbi Eliezer\u8217's framework of {\i edei mesirah}, clarifies that the question was never really about the document\u8217's text at all. The {\i get}\u8217's validity is grounded in witnessed delivery, and once those witnesses establish that this man gave this document to this woman, no textual ambiguity can undo it. We will pick up from here tomorrow.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Gittin 25\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Lishmah & Same Names\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Review Lishmah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The daf begins on 24b, picking up mid-{\i mishnah} in the discussion of {\i lishmah} \u8212- the requirement that a {\i get} be written with specific intent for a particular woman. The {\i mishnah} had established that a husband cannot instruct a {\i sofer} to write a {\i get} in an open-ended fashion, leaving the determination of which wife it is intended for to be resolved later. The underlying concern is whether such a {\i get}, lacking the concrete {\i lishmah} required at the moment of writing, can be valid at all.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Identity Clarifies\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} had raised a related question: if both wives share the same name, and a {\i get} is written naming that shared name, does the identity of the woman actually holding the {\i get} serve to clarify and establish its validity? On the surface this seems reasonable \u8212- the {\i get} is in her hand, and that physical reality might suffice to resolve any ambiguity.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Eliezer Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} ultimately suggested that this question may need to be understood according to the view of Rabbi Eliezer, who does not regard the {\i get} as functioning primarily as a {\i shtar re\u8217'ayah} \u8212- a document that must itself bear evidentiary testimony as to its subject. Rabbi Eliezer holds that {\i adei mesirah} \u8212- the witnesses to the act of delivery \u8212- are what effect the divorce, not the text of the document as a self-sufficient proof-text. Under that view, the identity of the recipient, confirmed by those witnesses, could supply what the document alone does not.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rei\u8217'ach Haget\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav\u8217's Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now introduces a question of considerable practical consequence: if one of these invalid {\i gittin} \u8212- written {\i shelo lishmah} but bearing the correct names and physically indistinguishable from a valid {\i get} \u8212- was actually delivered to the wife, what is her status? The problem is invisible. Everything observable about the {\i get} and its delivery appears perfectly proper. The {\i pasul} consists entirely in what was \u8212- or was not \u8212- in the mind of the {\i sofer} at the time of writing.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav rules: {\i kulam poslin lakehunah chutz min harishon} \u8212- all of these {\i gittin} render her {\i pasul} for {\i kehunah}, with the sole exception of the first case, namely a {\i get} written purely as scribal practice in a {\i sofer}\u8217's school, never intended to serve as an actual {\i get}. That first case, Rav maintains, is sufficiently well understood in context \u8212- it was never treated with the weight of a real {\i get} \u8212- and therefore creates no impression that a divorce took place.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The severity of this ruling deserves emphasis. If such a {\i get} was delivered, the woman finds herself in the worst of both worlds: she is not actually divorced ({\i lo megureshet}), since the {\i get} is invalid; yet because it looks entirely valid, she is treated {\i miderabbanan} as a {\i gerusha}. If her husband is a {\i kohen}, he must now divorce her \u8212- she can no longer remain with him \u8212- even though the original {\i get} accomplished nothing legally. As Rashi notes: {\i im ba\u8217'alah kohen nifselah alav mishum grusha}. One might even imagine a perverse scenario in which a vindictive husband exploits this mechanism to ensure his wife cannot remarry a {\i kohen} even after his death, without actually effecting a valid divorce.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rei\u8217'ach Concept\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This doctrine is known as {\i rei\u8217'ach haget} \u8212- the {\i get} \u8220"smells\u8221" valid. If a {\i get} walks like a {\i get}, talks like a {\i get}, and smells like a {\i get}, are we to treat the woman as though she were {\i miderabbanan} a {\i gerusha}? The answer, at least for Rav, is yes \u8212- with the one exception noted above.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What makes the cases in this {\i mishnah} particularly apt for this concern is precisely the invisibility of the defect. The problem is entirely internal, residing in the mind of the {\i sofer} during writing. There is nothing in the physical document, in the names, or in the manner of delivery that signals anything is wrong. It is this imperceptibility that generates the {\i rei\u8217'ach haget} problem.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shmuel\u8217's View\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Shmuel disagrees with Rav and goes further: {\i af rishon nami posel} \u8212- even the first case, the {\i get} written in a {\i sofer}\u8217's school, creates the {\i rei\u8217'ach haget} problem. For Shmuel, the criterion is whether the {\i get} looks valid, and since all of these cases appear valid on their face, all of them render her {\i pasul lakehunah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shmuel Terminology\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Shmuel\u8217's position reflects a broader terminological principle. Wherever the Sages use the phrase {\i get pasul}, Shmuel understands that formulation to mean {\i pasul u\u8217'posel} \u8212- invalid, but invalidating her status as well. Similarly, wherever the Sages describe a {\i chalitza} as {\i psula}, they mean {\i psula u\u8217'posaltah} \u8212- it does not constitute a valid {\i chalitza}, yet it disqualifies her from {\i yibum} with the remaining brothers and, like a {\i gerusha}, renders her {\i asura lakehunah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This creates the characteristic irony of the {\i rei\u8217'ach} doctrine: the act was legally insufficient to accomplish its purpose, yet it is sufficiently valid-looking to impose the most serious consequences.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rambam Pasul/Batel\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Rambam, with characteristic precision, systematizes the terminology that the {\i gemara} itself does not always apply consistently. In {\i Hilchos Gittin}, Rambam reserves the term {\i pasul} for that which is rabbinically defective, and {\i batel} for that which is void at the Torah level. This distinction helps clarify what Shmuel may be driving at: when Shmuel says a {\i get pasul} is {\i pasul u\u8217'posel}, the {\i pasul} language signals that while the {\i get} may be {\i batel de\u8217'oraisa} \u8212- not a valid Torah-level divorce \u8212- it looks enough like a valid act that rabbinic law imposes the consequences of one. The {\i gemara}\u8217's own usage is less consistent, but Rambam\u8217's framework provides an analytic grid for understanding the phenomenon.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Geographic Dispute\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Eliezer Chalitzah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 In {\i Marava} \u8212- the Land of Israel \u8212- they transmit in the name of Rabbi Eliezer a parallel ruling regarding {\i chalitza}: if she removed the shoe from his left foot rather than his right, or if the {\i chalitza} was performed at night rather than during the day, these {\i chalitzos} are {\i psulos u\u8217'poslos} \u8212- invalid, but disqualifying. The defect is not obvious enough to eliminate all legal significance; the act resembled a valid {\i chalitza} closely enough that its effects linger in a negative form.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Obvious vs Subtle\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The contrast with cases involving a {\i katan} or an {\i anfilya} \u8212- a minor performing {\i chalitza} or a soft slipper used instead of a proper shoe \u8212- is instructive and rather striking. A minor is obviously disqualified, since the {\i pasuk} requires {\i ish}; a slipper obviously fails the requirement of {\i na\u8217'alo}, his shoe. These defects are self-evident from the text itself. And yet, precisely because they are so obvious, those {\i chalitzos} are {\i psulos ve\u8217'ein poslos} \u8212- invalid, but not disqualifying. They create no impression of validity; no one could mistake them for the real thing.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Here the law produces a counterintuitive result: the more egregious and transparent the defect, the less damage it causes. It is the subtle, nearly-invisible {\i pasul} \u8212- one that mimics the valid act convincingly \u8212- that creates the most serious problems. An act that looked completely proper, even if internally defective, generates the worst-of-both-worlds status. An act that announced its own invalidity produces no consequences at all.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This logic applies equally to {\i gittin}. A {\i get} whose defect is entirely hidden \u8212- the {\i sofer}\u8217's internal state of mind at the time of writing \u8212- looks precisely like a valid {\i get} and thus falls squarely within the {\i rei\u8217'ach haget} concern. The Babylonian {\i amora\u8217'im} (Rav and Shmuel) disagreed about exactly which cases clear the threshold, but both accepted the category.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The sages of the Land of Israel preferred to examine each case individually rather than apply a global rule based on the word {\i pasul}. Their criterion was whether the defect was or was not self-evident from the face of the act itself. Something explicitly disqualified by the {\i pasuk} \u8212- a {\i katan}, an {\i anfilya} \u8212- is too obviously invalid to create an impression. Something less explicit, something that requires looking beneath the surface, is precisely the kind of {\i pasul} that creates a {\i rei\u8217'ach} problem.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Zeiri\u8217's Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Zeiri takes a much narrower position. He limits the {\i rei\u8217'ach haget} category to the final case in the {\i mishnah} alone \u8212- the case where a husband has two wives with the same name and declares that he will decide later which one he wishes to divorce. This is, Zeiri argues, the single case that looks most like it could be a valid {\i get}: the document was not written for someone else, there is no technical scribal error, and nothing on its face is wrong. It was simply left open-ended. Moreover, the scenario of two wives sharing an identical name is itself rare. Only this case, in Zeiri\u8217's view, comes close enough to the appearance of validity to trigger the {\i rei\u8217'ach haget} consequence. All other {\i pasul gittin} are simply {\i pasul} \u8212- invalid \u8212- without imposing any additional {\i gerusha} status.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Assi Agrees\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Assi endorses Zeiri\u8217's position verbatim: {\i kulan ein poslin chutz min ha\u8217'achrona} \u8212- none of these cases create the {\i rei\u8217'ach haget} problem except the last one.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Yochanan Rejects\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yochanan goes further still \u8212- and in a direction that might seem surprising given the general pull of the discussion. He rules: {\i af achrona nami eino posel} \u8212- even the last case does not create the {\i rei\u8217'ach haget} problem. For Rabbi Yochanan, if a {\i get} is invalid, it is simply invalid, full stop. The law does not create a middle category in which an act is simultaneously not a {\i get} and yet treated as one for the purpose of imposing {\i gerusha} status. Either she is divorced or she is not.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Yochanan Consistency\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ein Breira Principle\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} notes that Rabbi Yochanan\u8217's ruling is consistent with his broader jurisprudential position. {\i Ve\u8217'azal Rabbi Yochanan leta\u8217'ameih} \u8212- Rabbi Yochanan goes according to his own reasoning, as transmitted by Rav Assi in the name of Rabbi Yochanan: {\i ha\u8217'achin shechalku lekuchot hen umachzirin zeh lazeh beyovel} \u8212- brothers who divide an inherited estate are considered to have purchased from one another, and they must return their respective portions to one another at {\i yovel}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The connection requires unpacking. The last case in the {\i mishnah} \u8212- the husband who declares he will decide which wife\u8217's {\i get} this is \u8212- is at its core a question of {\i breira}: can a determination made after the fact retroactively define what was in the writer\u8217's mind at an earlier moment? {\i Yesh breira} holds that such retroactive determination is legally operative; {\i ein breira} holds that it is not.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The inheritance case illustrates the same structure. When a man dies and leaves his estate to multiple brothers, no one has designated which portion belongs to whom. When the brothers subsequently divide the estate, two conceptual frameworks are possible. Under {\i yesh breira}, one would say that the later division retroactively reveals what was always destined to be each brother\u8217's portion \u8212- he always \u8220"owned\u8221" the house, his brother always \u8220"owned\u8221" the fields, and the division merely makes explicit what was always the case. Under {\i ein breira}, no such retroactive determination is possible. Instead, the estate was held as a communal entity, and when the brothers divide it they are effectively purchasing one another\u8217's shares \u8212- I give you my half-interest in the house in exchange for your half-interest in the fields. That is a sale. And since it is a sale, at {\i yovel} it must return \u8212- {\i umachzirin zeh lazeh beyovel}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yochanan rules that the brothers are {\i lekuchot} \u8212- purchasers \u8212- and must return their portions at {\i yovel}. This means he holds {\i ein breira}. Since he holds {\i ein breira} as a general principle, the last case of the {\i mishnah} is not merely invalid under some special rule; it is obviously invalid under the same logic Rabbi Yochanan applies universally. There is nothing impressive about it, no near-miss with validity that would generate a {\i rei\u8217'ach haget} concern. For someone who holds {\i yesh breira}, that last case might look almost kosher \u8212- which is precisely why it could be a {\i rei\u8217'ach haget} problem for others. But for Rabbi Yochanan, it never looked close.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yovel Inheritance\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} adds that it was necessary to state Rabbi Yochanan\u8217's position in both contexts \u8212- the {\i mishnah}\u8217's case and the inheritance case \u8212- and explains why neither can be inferred from the other alone. If we only knew his ruling in the {\i mishnah}, one might say: Rabbi Yochanan holds that even the last case is not a {\i rei\u8217'ach haget} problem, but that is only because of the special {\i lishmah} requirement for {\i gittin}. Perhaps even Rabbi Yochanan would agree that in general {\i breira} does not apply to {\i gittin}, given the Torah\u8217's explicit demand that the {\i get} be written concretely {\i lishma} \u8212- for her sake specifically, at the moment of writing \u8212- a requirement that forecloses any retroactive determination regardless of one\u8217's general {\i breira} position. That says nothing about what Rabbi Yochanan holds regarding inheritance and {\i yovel}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Conversely, if we only knew his ruling about inheritance \u8212- {\i aval yerusha umattanah lo} \u8212- one might argue differently. The Torah\u8217's law of {\i yovel} applies specifically to sales: {\i mecher hu de\u8217'amar Rachmana lihadar beyovel}. Inherited portions and gifts are not subject to {\i yovel}. So Rabbi Yochanan\u8217's ruling that the brothers must return at {\i yovel} may not reflect a strong {\i ein breira} principle at all \u8212- it may simply reflect the stringency of treating the division as a sale-like transaction, or the fact that there is an identifiable original estate to restore. One could therefore hold that in general Rabbi Yochanan accepts {\i breira}, and it would still not follow that the {\i mishnah}\u8217's final case fails to create a {\i rei\u8217'ach haget} impression. Both statements are needed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Lishmah vs Breira\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara}\u8217's resolution is precise. The {\i mishnah}\u8217's case operates on two distinct tracks. First, there is the {\i lishmah} requirement: regardless of one\u8217's position on {\i breira}, the Torah\u8217's demand that a {\i get} be written with concrete, specific intent for a particular woman at the moment of writing may be a special requirement that {\i breira} cannot satisfy even according to those who generally accept it. One who holds {\i yesh breira} might still concede that {\i lishmah} cannot be supplied retroactively, because the Torah demands present and concrete intentionality. Second, there is the {\i rei\u8217'ach haget} question: does the last case of the {\i mishnah}, which fails only because of this open-ended framing and not for any visible reason, create enough of an appearance of validity to impose {\i gerusha} status? For Rabbi Yochanan, who holds {\i ein breira} as a general matter, the answer is clearly no \u8212- it was never even close to valid. For those who hold {\i yesh breira}, however, the case may genuinely look like it could have worked, and that appearance is precisely what generates the {\i rei\u8217'ach haget} problem.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This explains the range of opinions. Rav held that all cases in the {\i mishnah} \u8212- except the purely academic scribal exercise \u8212- create a {\i rei\u8217'ach haget} problem because in all of them the defect is invisible. Shmuel went further, applying {\i rei\u8217'ach haget} wherever the Sages use the word {\i pasul}. Zeiri and Rav Assi restricted {\i rei\u8217'ach haget} to the final case alone. And Rabbi Yochanan, who holds {\i ein breira} universally, finds no {\i rei\u8217'ach haget} anywhere.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Breira Sugya Start\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hoshaya Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having established the connection between Rabbi Yochanan\u8217's rejection of {\i rei\u8217'ach haget} and his principle of {\i ein breira}, the {\i gemara} now enters the classic {\i sugya} of {\i breira} in its own right. Rav Hoshaya poses a question to Rabbi Yehuda: if a husband instructs a {\i sofer}, {\i kesov le\u8217'eizo she\u8217'teitzei be\u8217'pesach techila} \u8212- write the {\i get} for whichever wife walks out of the door first \u8212- does that work? This is what the {\i gemara} calls the {\i Yiftach} version of a {\i get}: the husband has made up his mind to divorce one of his wives, but the identity of that wife will be determined by an external event \u8212- who happens to exit first.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Toleh Distinction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} introduces the fundamental distinction the question is probing: the difference between {\i toleh bedaat atzmo} \u8212- leaving the matter to one\u8217's own later decision \u8212- and {\i toleh bedaat acherim} \u8212- leaving the determination to the action of someone or something else. In the {\i mishnah}\u8217's case, the husband says \u8220"I\u8217'll decide which wife I want to divorce\u8221" \u8212- that is {\i toleh bedaat atzmo}. Rav Hoshaya\u8217's question is whether the case of \u8220"whichever wife walks out first\u8221" \u8212- {\i toleh bedaat acherim} \u8212- is legally different and might support {\i breira}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rashi Indecisive\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rashi captures the logic precisely. When a man says \u8220"I\u8217'll decide later which wife I mean,\u8221" he is demonstrating that at the moment of writing he is {\i pose\u8217'ach al shtei hasefim} \u8212- jumping between two doorsteps, unable to commit. The phrase comes from {\i Tanach} and conveys radical indecision. If he could not make up his mind then, we cannot say that later, when he does decide, it retroactively reveals what he had in mind at the moment of writing. What he had in mind then was precisely that he had not made up his mind. There is no hidden intent to be revealed; there is only unresolved ambiguity. {\i Breira} depends on the premise that a later determination uncovers what was always true; it cannot function when the earlier moment was itself defined by the absence of any determination.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Toleh bedaat acherim} is entirely different. When the husband says \u8220"the wife who walks out of the door first is the one I am divorcing,\u8221" he has made a complete decision. He does not know which wife that will be, but his will is fully formed and specific: he wants to divorce the wife who acts first. There is no indecision; there is only an unknown fact. And {\i breira} may be able to work when a later fact retroactively specifies what was always \u8212- from a determined perspective \u8212- the intended object.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Pesach Korban\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Father\u8217's Offer\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 To explore whether {\i toleh bedaat acherim} can indeed support {\i breira}, the {\i gemara} adduces a {\i mishnah}: a father declares that he will slaughter the {\i korban Pesach} for whichever of his sons arrives first in Jerusalem on {\i Erev Pesach}. He then slaughters the {\i Pesach}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Retroactive Inclusion\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} rules: as soon as the first son enters Jerusalem \u8212- {\i rosho verubo}, his head and the majority of his body \u8212- he retroactively becomes included in the {\i korban Pesach} that was already slaughtered. More strikingly, {\i umezakeh et echav imo} \u8212- through him, all of his brothers become included as well. This seems to be a clear case of {\i yesh breira}: the identity of the beneficiary is determined by a later event, yet it retroactively fixes the intent of the slaughter.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Not True Breira\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yochanan responds: {\i vechi pesachim etzel gittin} \u8212- can you compare the {\i korban Pesach} to {\i gittin}? But more fundamentally, he explains that the {\i korban Pesach} case is not genuine {\i breira} at all. The father\u8217's declaration \u8212- \u8220"for the son who arrives first\u8221" \u8212- was not an expression of genuine uncertainty about who should receive the {\i korban}. It was {\i kedei lezarzen bemitzvah} \u8212- a motivational device, intended to spur his sons to move with alacrity. In reality, the father intended the {\i korban} for all of them from the outset.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Daughters Proof\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} finds textual support for this in a {\i baraita}: there was once a case where daughters arrived in Jerusalem before the sons. The {\i baraita} notes this as a demonstration that the daughters showed greater {\i zerizus} \u8212- alacrity \u8212- and the sons were comparatively lax. Crucially, the {\i baraita} does not say the sons were therefore excluded from the {\i korban Pesach}. They were still included. They were simply shamed by their sisters\u8217' swiftness. If the father\u8217's declaration were genuine {\i breira} \u8212- if the identity of the beneficiary truly depended on who arrived first \u8212- then the sons, having arrived later, would have been excluded entirely. The fact that they were not excluded confirms that the father always intended the {\i korban} for all of them, and the \u8220"first to arrive\u8221" language was purely about inspiring {\i zerizus}, not about limiting participation. Accordingly, the {\i korban Pesach} case cannot serve as a proof that {\i breira} works for {\i toleh bedaat acherim}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Abaye\u8217's Critique\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Different Cases\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye objects sharply to how the exchange unfolded. Rav Hoshaya\u8217's question was specifically about {\i toleh bedaat acherim} \u8212- the case where the husband has fully decided and the outcome is determined by external events. The response he received cited the {\i mishnah}\u8217's ruling about {\i toleh bedaat atzmo} \u8212- the case where the husband himself defers his own decision \u8212- as if that settled the matter. But these are clearly distinct categories, and answering a question about one by citing a ruling about the other simply misses the point.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Question Remains\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 In consequence, Abaye notes that the question stands. The {\i korban Pesach} case that Rav Hoshaya had adduced \u8212- before Rabbi Yochanan explained it away as {\i zerizus} rather than true {\i breira} \u8212- was in fact directly relevant to {\i toleh bedaat acherim}. That was the right category of case to examine. The dismissal of that case on grounds of {\i zerizus} may or may not be satisfying, but at least it was engaging with the correct question. The earlier answer, citing the {\i mishnah} about {\i toleh bedaat atzmo}, was simply a non-answer \u8212- it addressed a different category entirely.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava then interjects that perhaps the respondent who cited the {\i mishnah} simply rejected the premise that there is any meaningful legal distinction between the two categories. If one holds {\i breira} at all, one holds it across the board \u8212- {\i lo shna toleh bedaat atzmo velo shna toleh bedaat acherim}; and if one rejects {\i breira}, one rejects it across the board as well. On that view, citing the {\i mishnah} \u8212- which shows {\i breira} fails when the husband says \u8220"I\u8217'll decide\u8221" \u8212- is a valid response to any {\i breira} question, because the distinction Rav Hoshaya drew simply does not exist. But this, of course, is itself the position to be proven, and Rav Mesharshiya sets out to challenge it directly.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Mesharshiya Proof 1\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Wine Separation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Mesharshiya presses Rava: Rabbi Yehuda, he argues, is a tanna who holds {\i ein breira} for {\i toleh bedaat atzmo} but {\i yesh breira} for {\i toleh bedaat acherim} \u8212- demonstrating that the distinction Rava would collapse is in fact legally operative.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The first proof comes from the {\i baraita} about wine purchased from Samaritans on {\i Erev Shabbat}. Since the Samaritans are assumed certainly not to have separated {\i trumot u\u8217'ma\u8217'asrot}, the wine is {\i vadai tevel}. If there is no time to perform a proper {\i hafrasha} before {\i Shabbat}, a person might attempt to declare: the two {\i lugim} I will separate after {\i Shabbat} shall be {\i terumah}; ten {\i lugim} shall be {\i maaser rishon}; nine of the remaining shall be {\i maaser sheni} \u8212- and then begin drinking immediately.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Meir vs Others\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Meir permits this procedure, holding {\i yesh breira}: the designations made now, with the specific quantities identified later, are legally effective. Rabbi Yehuda, Rabbi Yose, and Rabbi Shimon all prohibit it. For them, this is a case of {\i toleh bedaat atzmo} \u8212- the person is deferring his own completion of the act, pushing off to a later moment his own identification of which physical wine corresponds to the designations he has made now. That is {\i ein breira}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Conditional Get\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But Rabbi Yehuda does hold {\i yesh breira} in a different context. A man on his deathbed says to his wife: this shall be your {\i get} if I do not recover from this illness. What is her status {\i mahi ba\u8217'osun yamim} \u8212- during the intervening period, before his fate is known? Rabbi Yehuda rules she is {\i ke\u8217'ishet ish lechol devareha} \u8212- treated as a married woman for all purposes during that period. Yet when he dies, {\i havei gitta} \u8212- it is a valid {\i get}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Time TBD\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might initially think this is simply a standard conditional {\i get} \u8212- a {\i tenai} \u8212- and has nothing to do with {\i breira}. But Tosafot explains why this is, in fact, a {\i breira} case. Rabbi Yehuda reads the husband\u8217's words not as a condition (\u8220"if I die, the {\i get} takes effect from now\u8221") but as a temporal specification: the {\i get} takes effect {\i when} I die \u8212- meaning, a minute before I die. The moment of the {\i get}\u8217's effectiveness is thus undefined at the time of writing; it is a real time, but a time to be determined by events entirely outside the husband\u8217's control. The {\i get} is not contingent \u8212- it will certainly take effect \u8212- but the precise moment is, in the language of {\i breira}, to be specified later by events external to the husband. This is {\i toleh bedaat acherim} (here, in a sense, the determination rests with God \u8212- {\i mi shehachayyim vehamavet teluyim beyado}), and Rabbi Yehuda accepts it. The {\i breira} consists in the retroactive fixing of the moment at which the {\i get} became effective.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Mesharshiya Proof 2\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Father Agreement\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Mesharshiya then offers a second proof, this time from Rabbi Shimon. A man says to a woman: {\i hareini bo\u8217'alaich al menas sheyirtzeh abba} \u8212- I am having relations with you on condition that your father agrees. Marital relations ({\i bi\u8217'ah}) constitute one of the three recognized modes of {\i kiddushin}. The question is whether this act of {\i bi\u8217'ah} creates {\i kiddushin} contingent on the father\u8217's later agreement.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Father Determines\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The first opinion in the {\i baraita} holds she is {\i mekudeshet} even if the father does not agree, on the grounds of {\i ein adam oseh bei\u8217'alato bei\u8217'lat zenut} \u8212- a man does not intend for sexual relations to occur outside the framework of marriage. Accordingly, we presume he truly wants the {\i kiddushin} unconditionally, and the reference to the father\u8217's agreement is not genuinely operative. Rabbi Shimon ben Yehuda, transmitting in the name of Rabbi Shimon, disagrees: if the father agrees, she is {\i mekudeshet}; if not, she is not.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Context Not Condition\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Why is this a case of {\i breira} rather than a simple {\i tenai}? The {\i gemara} explains that {\i kiddushei bi\u8217'ah} operates differently from other forms of {\i kiddushin}. An ordinary {\i tenai} works by imposing a condition on a deliberate act of will: \u8220"I choose to effect {\i kiddushin} on this condition.\u8221" {\i Kiddushei bi\u8217'ah}, by contrast, does not depend on explicit intent to create marriage; rather, it is the marital context of the act itself \u8212- the fact that the relations occur in a framework of husband and wife \u8212- that constitutes the {\i kiddushin}. The act automatically creates marriage when done in a marital context; it does not require a formal declaration of intent.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Given this, a {\i tenai} in the ordinary sense cannot function here \u8212- you cannot say \u8220"I will these relations to be {\i kiddushin} only if X.\u8221" What matters is not your will but the nature of the context. The question, then, is not whether a condition has been met, but what context the act occurred in \u8212- marital or not? And that context, Rabbi Shimon holds, is to be determined retroactively by the father\u8217's agreement. If the father agrees, the act took place in a marital context; if not, it did not. The father\u8217's later decision retroactively defines the nature of the act at the time it occurred \u8212- which is precisely {\i breira}. And since this is a case where the determination rests with another ({\i toleh bedaat acherim}), Rabbi Shimon accepts it, just as he rejects {\i breira} in the wine case where the person himself defers his own decision.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rava\u8217's Counter\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava All Breira\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava responds to both proofs from Rav Mesharshiya with a unified counterargument. He rejects the premise that the two cases \u8212- wine separation and the conditional {\i get} or {\i kiddushei bi\u8217'ah} \u8212- demonstrate a principled distinction between {\i toleh bedaat atzmo} and {\i toleh bedaat acherim}. Rather, Rava maintains, both Rabbi Yehuda and Rabbi Shimon hold {\i yesh breira} across the board \u8212- whether {\i toleh bedaat atzmo} or {\i toleh bedaat acherim}. {\i Bein le\u8217'Rabbi Yehuda bein le\u8217'Rabbi Shimon, lo shna tola bedaat atzmo velo shna tola bedaat acherim \u8212- is lei breira.} The distinction Rav Mesharshiya is pressing, in Rava\u8217's view, does not reflect a difference in the underlying {\i breira} principle but rather a separate practical concern.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Wine Spill Risk\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If that is so, why do Rabbi Yehuda, Rabbi Yose, and Rabbi Shimon prohibit the wine procedure in the {\i baraita}? The {\i gemara} explains: the prohibition is not grounded in {\i ein breira} but in a different concern entirely. They said to Rabbi Meir: {\i shema yibaka hanod} \u8212- perhaps the barrel will burst. {\i Venimtza shoteh tvalim lemafrea} \u8212- and it will turn out retroactively that one was drinking {\i tevel} the whole time. The entire {\i breira} mechanism depends on the wine remaining intact until after {\i Shabbat}, when the actual {\i hafrasha} can be performed. If the barrel breaks and all the wine spills, the designations made before {\i Shabbat} never get actualized \u8212- there is nothing left to separate \u8212- and one will have consumed untithed produce retroactively. It is this risk, not a fundamental rejection of {\i breira}, that motivates their ruling.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Meir Not Concerned\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Meir\u8217's response is almost laconic in its confidence: {\i lichsheyibaka} \u8212- when it breaks, I\u8217'll worry about it then. He is not concerned about the risk in advance. This is, incidentally, somewhat ironic, since Rabbi Meir is generally known for {\i chaish lemiutei} \u8212- taking seriously even minority-probability risks. Here, however, he dismisses the concern entirely.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Practical Safeguard\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava\u8217's resolution is therefore elegant. The other Tannaim \u8212- Rabbi Yehuda, Rabbi Yose, Rabbi Shimon \u8212- accept {\i breira} in principle, just as Rabbi Meir does. Their prohibition of the wine procedure is not a statement about {\i breira} but a practical safeguard against the risk of barrel-breakage leading retroactively to the consumption of {\i tevel}. They would permit the procedure if that risk could be eliminated. The apparent distinction between {\i toleh bedaat atzmo} and {\i toleh bedaat acherim} that Rav Mesharshiya identified dissolves on this reading: there is no principled distinction between the two categories, and the Tannaim hold {\i breira} universally or reject it universally.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The opposing view, of course, remains on the table: perhaps there is indeed a principled difference between the two categories, such that {\i breira} is available when external events supply the determination, but not when the deciding party is the same person who originally left the matter unresolved. The {\i sugya} closes with these two positions in tension, the question of which framework accurately describes the views of the individual Tannaim left for continued analysis.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Gittin 26\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Tofes Forms Overview\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Pre-written Forms\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i daf} opens by situating this {\i mishnah} within the broader discussion of writing a {\i get} {\i lishmah}. The previous {\i mishnah} had clearly reflected the position of {\i Rabbi Elazar} \u8212- emphasizing the {\i lishmah} requirement in writing, focusing on the names of the specific man and woman and the {\i leshem gerushin}. With that framework in place, the {\i mishnah} before us turns to a related but distinct question: the practice of pre-writing {\i tofes} forms.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} opens with the case of {\i hakotev tofsei gittin} \u8212- scribes who wish to prepare the boilerplate text of a {\i get} in advance, ready to be filled in when a couple actually appears for a divorce. The analogy is apt: just as a {\i kesuvah} today is often printed with blank lines for the relevant particulars, so too a scribe might want a standardized {\i get} form at hand.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Leaving Blanks\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} rules: {\i tzarich she\u8217'yaniach mekom ha\u8217'ish umekom ha\u8217'ishah umekom hazman} \u8212- the scribe must leave blank the space for the husband\u8217's name, the wife\u8217's name, and the date. At first glance this seems almost self-evident: of course one leaves those spaces blank, since the details are not yet known. If that were the entire reason, the ruling would add nothing of substance \u8212- filling in incorrect information would obviously be disqualifying, and the {\i mishnah} would hardly need to tell us so.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Lishmah Concern\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This prompts the deeper question: is the requirement to leave blanks an expression of the {\i lishmah} requirement, or is it simply a practical necessity because the information is not yet available? Could a scribe who anticipates a particular divorce \u8212- hearing that a couple has been fighting for months and sensing that a {\i get} is imminent \u8212- go ahead and fill in the names in advance? And is there a {\i lishmah} dimension at all to the boilerplate portion of a {\i get}, as opposed to the essential part? These questions drive the {\i Gemara}\u8217's analysis.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Loan Documents\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} then extends the same framework to other documents. {\i Shtarei milveh} \u8212- promissory notes recording that a borrower owes money to a lender, witnessed and signed \u8212- carry no {\i lishmah} requirement whatsoever. Yet here too the rule applies: {\i tzarich she\u8217'yaniach mekom hamilveh umekom haloveh mekom hamamon umekom hazman} \u8212- the scribe must leave blank the space for the lender, the debtor, the amount of money, and the date on which the loan was made.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sale Documents\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Similarly, {\i shtarei mekach} \u8212- documents recording the sale of a plot of land \u8212- must have blanks left for the names of buyer and seller ({\i mekom halokeach umekom hamocher}), the price ({\i mekom hamamon}), the location of the field ({\i mekom hasadeh}), and the date ({\i mekom hazman}).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Takanah Principle\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} concludes: {\i mifnei hatakanah} \u8212- all of this is on account of a {\i takanah}. The nature of that {\i takanah} remains to be identified. As {\i Rashi} observes, the practical scenario motivating the rule is precisely the one that might seem harmless: a scribe who, upon hearing that a couple is headed for divorce, or that a real estate deal is nearly finalized, decides to prepare the document in advance \u8212- much as newspapers keep pre-written obituaries for public figures, updating them periodically so they are ready when needed. The {\i mishnah} evidently sees a problem with this practice; the {\i Gemara} will clarify exactly what that problem is and which authority\u8217's reasoning underlies it.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Three Positions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tanna Kamma\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} records three opinions. The {\i Tanna Kamma} \u8212- the first, anonymous position \u8212- holds that one may write the boilerplate portion ({\i tofes}) of any document but must leave blank the essential particulars ({\i toref}).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Yehuda Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Yehuda} takes a stringent view: {\i poseil bekullan} \u8212- he invalidates all of them. His position, as the {\i Gemara} will make clear, is that one may not write even the boilerplate portion of any document in advance, presumably out of concern that permitting the {\i tofes} will lead the scribe to inadvertently write the {\i toref} as well.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Eliezer\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Elazar} presents a striking asymmetry: {\i machshir bekullan chutz migittei nashim} \u8212- he validates everything, except for bills of divorce. For {\i gittin}, he invokes the verse {\i bekosav lo lishmah} \u8212- the {\i lishmah} requirement \u8212- as the basis for a stringency that extends even to the boilerplate. But for all other documents, he permits the scribe to proceed freely.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The question this raises is what exactly {\i Rabbi Elazar} means by {\i machshir bekullan}. Is he merely restating what was already implicit \u8212- that the {\i tofes} of non-{\i get} documents may be written? {\i Rashi} reads him this way: {\i Rabbi Elazar} is not adding anything novel about other documents; he permits only the {\i tofes}, not the {\i toref}, exactly as the {\i Tanna Kamma} does. His distinctive position is solely his stringency about the {\i get}: even the boilerplate may not be pre-written there, as a protective measure ({\i gezeira}) against inadvertently writing the essential part without {\i lishmah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafot Reading\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} offers a bolder reading \u8212- one that the {\i Gemara}\u8217's subsequent discussion actually supports. According to {\i Tosafot}, {\i Rabbi Elazar} is simultaneously the most stringent and the most lenient of the three opinions. He is strictest regarding the {\i get}, where the {\i lishmah} requirement is paramount, and therefore prohibits even the boilerplate. But he is most lenient regarding everything else, precisely because {\i lishmah} is irrelevant there. In {\i Tosafot}\u8217's reading, {\i machshir bekullan} means that for all non-{\i get} documents, the scribe may fill in the entire document \u8212- names, amounts, locations, everything \u8212- without any concern at all, provided only that the witnesses do not yet sign. There is no {\i lishmah} issue and no reason to withhold anything. The scribe who knows a land deal is being finalized may write out the entire document in full; the witnesses will sign when the transaction closes.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This produces a clean tripartite structure. The {\i Tanna Kamma} treats {\i get} and other documents symmetrically: {\i tofes} permitted, {\i toref} not, for all document types. {\i Rabbi Yehuda} treats them symmetrically in the opposite direction: nothing may be pre-written for any document, out of a cascading set of protective concerns. {\i Rabbi Elazar} distinguishes sharply: complete leniency for other documents, maximal stringency for a {\i get}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One student raised the obvious practical concern: {\i Rabbi Yehuda}\u8217's position, if followed as {\i halacha}, would invalidate the pre-printed forms that have been in universal use for centuries. The {\i Gemara} will confirm that we do not rule like {\i Rabbi Yehuda}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Gemara Resolution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Harei at Muteret\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} opens with a ruling from {\i Rav Yehuda amar Shmuel}: the {\i mishnah}\u8217's requirement to leave blanks extends beyond the names and the date \u8212- {\i tzarich she\u8217'yaniach af makom harei at muteret le-chol adam}. The scribe must also leave blank the space for the essential phrase of the {\i get}: {\i harei at muteret le-chol adam}, \u8220"you are hereby permitted to any man.\u8221" This is the punchline of the {\i get}, its operative declaration.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Toref Requirement\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This teaching clarifies the {\i lishmah} dimension of the {\i mishnah}. The {\i Tanna Kamma}\u8217's requirement to leave blanks does not obviously signal a {\i lishmah} concern, since it applies equally to {\i shtarei milveh} and {\i shtarei mekach}, where {\i lishmah} is irrelevant. One might have concluded that the entire framework is about something else entirely. But {\i Rav Yehuda amar Shmuel} reads the {\i mishnah} as a {\i lishmah} ruling: the blanks must be left precisely because the essential content of the {\i get} must be written {\i lishmah}. And if {\i lishmah} applies, it applies not merely to the names \u8212- which could be seen as incidental \u8212- but to the {\i toref} proper, the operative declaration that constitutes the divorce.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Indeed, one might argue that the {\i lishmah} requirement, taken at face value from {\i vechatav lah}, should demand that every single word of the {\i get} be written {\i lishmah}. The {\i Gemara} is therefore being innovative in restricting the {\i lishmah} obligation to the {\i toref} alone and not the {\i tofes} \u8212- the boilerplate is explicitly excluded from the {\i lishmah} requirement. But within the {\i toref}, {\i lishmah} applies in full, and that includes {\i harei at muteret le-chol adam}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gemara Attribution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now makes explicit what {\i Rav Yehuda amar Shmuel} implies: the {\i mishnah}, including even the {\i Tanna Kamma}, is to be read according to {\i Rabbi Elazar}\u8217's view \u8212- {\i k\u8217'Rabbi Elazar hi de-amar edei mesirah karti}. Since {\i Rabbi Elazar} holds that the witnesses of delivery ({\i eidei mesirah}) are what legally consummate the {\i get} rather than the witnesses who sign it, the essence of the {\i get} is its writing, not its signature. And since the writing is essential, {\i lishmah} in the writing is essential, which in turn explains why the blank for {\i harei at muteret le-chol adam} must be left unfilled: that phrase must be written {\i lishmah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Three Cases\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This reading of the {\i mishnah} as a {\i Rabbi Elazar} position is part of a broader interpretive project. {\i Rav Yehuda amar Shmuel} has made this attribution in three recent {\i mishnayot}, each presenting a distinct case. The first was the {\i mechubar} case \u8212- the rule that one should not write a {\i get} while the parchment is still attached to the ground. The second was the case of {\i hakol kesheirin lichtov et haget} \u8212- the rule that anyone, even a {\i cheresh}, {\i shoteh}, or {\i katan}, may write a {\i get}, because its validity rests on {\i ein kiyum haget ela bechotmav}. The third is the present {\i mishnah} regarding {\i tofsei gittin}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 In each case, {\i Shmuel} reads the {\i mishnah} as a {\i Rabbi Elazar} position, which requires reinterpreting certain words. In the {\i mechubar} case, the end of the {\i mishnah} uses the word {\i chatmu} \u8212- \u8220"they signed it\u8221" \u8212- but {\i Shmuel} reads this to mean they wrote the {\i toref}, not that they signed it. In the {\i hakol} case, the phrase {\i ein kiyum haget ela bechotmav} sounds entirely like {\i Rabbi Meir}\u8217's position that the signatories are decisive \u8212- yet {\i Shmuel} reinterprets {\i bechotmav} to refer to the witnesses present at the moment of delivery rather than those who sign the document.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Why Three Cases\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} explains why each of the three cases was necessary to state. Had {\i Shmuel} attributed only the {\i mechubar} case to {\i Rabbi Elazar}, one might have accepted it: the emphasis on the writing in that {\i mishnah} \u8212- {\i ein kotvin bemechubar} \u8212- at least makes a {\i Rabbi Elazar} reading plausible, even if the end requires reinterpretation. But one might have insisted that the {\i hakol} case is genuinely a {\i Rabbi Meir} {\i mishnah}, since its explicit rationale \u8212- {\i ein kiyum haget ela bechotmav} \u8212- sounds precisely like {\i Rabbi Meir}\u8217's view that the signatories are determinative. The {\i Gemara} notes the additional difficulty with the present {\i mishnah}: since {\i Rabbi Elazar} himself appears at its end \u8212- {\i chutz migittei nashim} \u8212- the implication is that the beginning is {\i not} his view. The {\i Tanna Kamma} permitting the {\i tofes} seems to contradict {\i Rabbi Elazar}, who at the end of the {\i mishnah} forbids even the {\i tofes} of a {\i get}. To assert that even the beginning of the {\i mishnah} reflects a {\i Rabbi Elazar} framework \u8212- albeit a position within that framework that countenances the {\i tofes} \u8212- required {\i Shmuel} to make the attribution explicit all three times. The {\i Gemara} resolves the apparent contradiction between the {\i Tanna Kamma} and {\i Rabbi Elazar}\u8217's end-of-{\i mishnah} position as a case of {\i trei tanna\u8217'ei aliba d\u8217'Rabbi Elazar}: two {\i tanna\u8217'im} debating the precise implications of {\i Rabbi Elazar}\u8217's view.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Three Takanos\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbeinu Tam Practice\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Before turning to the {\i Gemara}\u8217's three explanations for the {\i takanah}, {\i Tosafot} raises a remarkable historical observation about the phrase {\i harei at muteret le-chol adam} itself. Throughout the {\i Gemara} this phrase is treated as the {\i ikar haget}, the essential declaration of the {\i get} \u8212- indeed, the next chapter will explicitly teach {\i gufo shel get harei at muteret le-chol adam}. Yet {\i Tosafot} notes that {\i Rabbeinu Tam} was the one who instituted the practice of including this language in {\i gittin}. The very fact that he had to institute it implies that {\i gittin} written prior to {\i Rabbeinu Tam} did not contain the phrase.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 How is this possible? {\i Tosafot} explains that the standard boilerplate text of {\i gittin} before {\i Rabbeinu Tam}\u8217's time did not include {\i harei at muteret le-chol adam} \u8212- not because the concept was absent, but because the operative language of divorce was expressed differently, often in Aramaic. The {\i Rif}\u8217's text of a {\i get}, for instance, contains the phrase {\i l\u8217'hitnasva l\u8217'chol gvar di titzviyan} \u8212- \u8220"you may marry any man you wish\u8221" \u8212- which conveys the identical content in Aramaic. {\i Tosafot} then adds the crucial caveat: {\i ein l\u8217'hotzi la\u8217'az al gittin harishonim} \u8212- one must not cast aspersions on earlier {\i gittin}. The adoption of new stringent formulations, however well-grounded, does not retroactively invalidate prior practice, particularly when the earlier texts expressed the same substantive content through parallel Aramaic formulations. {\i Rabbeinu Tam} introduced the Hebrew phrase going forward, but the precedent from an earlier {\i Gemara} \u8212- that adopting new stringencies risks undermining the validity of what came before \u8212- remains relevant, and {\i Tosafot} is careful to address it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Takanat Sofrim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now turns to the underlying rationale for the {\i takanah} mentioned in the {\i mishnah}. {\i Rav Yonatan} offers the first explanation: {\i mipnei takkanat sofer} \u8212- the {\i takanah} was enacted for the benefit of the scribes. The context is {\i Rabbi Elazar}\u8217's position: since {\i Rabbi Elazar} requires {\i lishmah} in the writing, one might have thought that no part of a {\i get} \u8212- not even the boilerplate \u8212- could be prepared in advance. Writing anything before the proper moment might compromise the {\i lishmah} that the {\i get} demands. Yet pre-writing the {\i tofes} is extremely useful for scribes, enabling them to have forms ready for immediate use. The Rabbis therefore enacted a {\i takanah} permitting the {\i tofes} despite the general {\i lishmah} concern.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This framing also explains why the {\i mishnah} extends its requirements to other {\i shtarot}. Within a {\i Rabbi Elazar} framework, the temptation to fill in blanks by other documents might spill over into improper filling of blanks in a {\i get}. The {\i Tanna Kamma} therefore extends the restriction: just as you may not fill in the {\i toref} of a {\i get} \u8212- as that would compromise {\i lishmah} \u8212- so too you may not fill in the blanks of other {\i shtarot}, lest that practice migrate to the {\i get}. {\i Rabbi Yehuda} extends the concern further: since the {\i lishmah} requirement for a {\i get} is so demanding, he forbids even the {\i tofes} of a {\i get}, and once he forbids the {\i tofes} of a {\i get}, he forbids even the boilerplate of other {\i shtarot} by the same logic. {\i Rabbi Elazar} himself, by contrast, has no concern about other {\i shtarot} \u8212- there is no {\i lishmah} issue there \u8212- and is content to permit them entirely, while maintaining his stringency about the {\i get}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Thus the three positions resolve into a coherent picture within the world of {\i Rabbi Elazar}. All three agree that the {\i get} is the locus of concern; they disagree only about how far the protective fences must extend.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Takanat Ketata\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rav Shabtai} cites {\i Chizkiya} with an entirely different reading of the {\i mishnah}: {\i mishum ketata} \u8212- the reason for leaving blanks is to prevent domestic quarrels. On this account, the {\i mishnah} has nothing to do with {\i lishmah} at all. The {\i mishnah} should be read as a {\i Rabbi Meir} position, where the {\i lishmah} requirement does not apply to the writing. Why then may one not fill in the blanks? Because a woman who overhears a scribe writing a {\i get} with her name in it will naturally assume her husband has commissioned the document, and the result will be fights in the household. More broadly, it is simply bad policy to have documents pre-filled with sensitive personal details \u8212- whether it is a loan, a sale, or a {\i get} \u8212- before the underlying transaction has actually occurred. No one wants a fully written document floating around that purports to record something that has not yet happened.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 On this reading, the {\i takanah} is not one that {\i enables} the writing of the {\i tofes} despite {\i lishmah} concerns; it is rather a {\i takanah} telling scribes not to fill in the blanks even though there would be no technical prohibition in doing so. In principle, within a {\i Rabbi Meir} framework, one could pre-fill the entire document right down to the last detail and there would be no legal problem, since validity depends entirely on the signing. The {\i takanah} is a prudential restriction, not a {\i lishmah}-based one.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Takanat Agunot\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rav Chisda} cites {\i Avimi} with a third explanation: {\i mishum takkanat agunot} \u8212- the {\i takanah} is designed to prevent the creation of {\i agunot}. The {\i Gemara} notes that this rationale can be applied within either a {\i Rabbi Meir} or a {\i Rabbi Elazar} framework.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Agunot Application\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 According to the {\i Rabbi Meir} reading, the {\i takanat agunot} rationale works as follows. Since all that matters in a {\i Rabbi Meir} world is the signing, there is in principle nothing wrong with having an entirely pre-written {\i get} ready to go \u8212- including names, date, and the operative declaration. The {\i takanah} says: do not do this. Why? Because having a fully written {\i get} immediately available makes it fatally easy for a husband to act in anger. He has a fight with his wife, runs to the scribe\u8217's shop, picks up the pre-filled document, gets witnesses to sign it on the spot, and throws it at her. What was a resolvable domestic dispute becomes an irreversible divorce. {\i Rav Dov} raised an alternative nuance worth considering: even if the {\i get} is technically invalid because the signing was rushed or incomplete, the act of throwing a {\i get}-like document at one\u8217's wife may shatter the marriage in practice \u8212- the husband storms off and never returns, leaving the woman in a state of abandonment that the {\i Gemara} characterizes as a form of {\i agunah}, even if her formal legal status has not changed. This broader use of the term is noteworthy.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 According to the {\i Rabbi Elazar} reading, the {\i takanat agunot} works in the opposite direction. Since {\i Rabbi Elazar} demands {\i lishmah} in the writing, one might conclude that scribes may not pre-write even the boilerplate of a {\i get}. But this creates its own {\i agunah} problem: a husband who must travel overseas and wishes to leave his wife a conditional {\i get} \u8212- divorce to take effect if he does not return within a specified period \u8212- may be unable to find a scribe to write the entire {\i get} from scratch on short notice. {\i Lo ashkach safra} \u8212- he will not find a scribe, and he will depart without leaving any protection for his wife. If he fails to return, she becomes a genuine {\i agunah}. The {\i takanah} therefore permits the pre-writing of the {\i tofes} precisely to ensure that a {\i get} can be prepared quickly when circumstances demand it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} thus presents two dimensions of {\i takanat agunot}: one aimed at preventing precipitous divorce when a {\i get} is too easily available, the other aimed at enabling divorce when the inability to prepare one quickly would leave a woman permanently bound.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Pre-dating Problems\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Get for Arusa\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} rules without distinction \u8212- {\i lo shna min hanisuin velo shna min ha\u8217'erusin} \u8212- that the requirement to leave the date blank applies equally to a {\i get} given after {\i nisuin} and to a {\i get} given after {\i erusin} alone, before the couple has established a shared household. The {\i Gemara} accepts the {\i nisuin} case without difficulty.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 For the {\i nisuin} case, the prohibition on pre-dating has two well-established rationales. One, associated with the concern about a {\i bat achoso}, is that a husband might wish to protect his wife from a death penalty for adultery by backdating the {\i get} to make it appear she was divorced before the act occurred. The other rationale is about {\i peiros nichsei melog}: the produce of the wife\u8217's property that the husband is entitled to during the marriage. Pre-dating a {\i get} would distort the record of when the husband\u8217's entitlement ended, potentially harming third parties who relied on the husband\u8217's lien over those assets.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i erusin} case is more difficult. The concern about adultery and misrepresentation of the divorce date applies equally whether or not the couple had established a household. But the {\i peiros} concern seems irrelevant: {\i arusa mi is lah peiri} \u8212- an {\i arusa} does not yet have {\i nichsei melog} producing fruit over which the husband has any rights, since that entitlement begins only from {\i nisuin}. If the only reason to prohibit pre-dating were the {\i peiros} issue, there would seemingly be no problem with a pre-dated {\i get} for an {\i arusa}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Takanas Valad\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rav Amram} reports that he heard an explanation from {\i Ulla}, though he initially did not understand its application: {\i d\u8217'amar mishum takanas valad} \u8212- the rule exists for the protection of children. The relevant {\i braita} involves a husband who says: write a {\i get} for the woman I have just betrothed, and after we consummate the marriage I will divorce her. The {\i halacha} rules that such a {\i get} \u8212- written during {\i erusin} but intended for use after {\i nisuin} \u8212- is invalid.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Reputation Concern\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Ulla} explains the reason: {\i gzeira shema yomru gita kodem livno} \u8212- a decree lest people say that her {\i get} predates her children. If the {\i get} is dated from the time of {\i erusin}, and the couple subsequently lives together and has children before the {\i get} is delivered, observers will see a {\i get} bearing a date that appears to precede the birth of the children. Even though the children are not {\i mamzerim} \u8212- their status is not affected \u8212- the appearance that they were born after their mother was divorced is a {\i pegam mishpacha}, a blemish on the family\u8217's reputation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Arusa Protection\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This teaching resolves the {\i Gemara}\u8217's question about the {\i erusin} case. When the {\i get} will be given to the woman while she is still an {\i arusa}, pre-dating is not intrinsically a problem: there are no {\i peiros} at stake, and the date makes no practical difference between January and March if she remains an {\i arusa} throughout. The {\i Gemara} concedes this. But the reason the {\i mishnah} prohibits writing the date before the {\i get} is actually given \u8212- even for an {\i arusa} \u8212- is that the scribe cannot know the {\i get} will actually be delivered during {\i erusin}. The husband may hold onto it. He may not give it until after {\i nisuin}. And if he then gives it after {\i nisuin}, the earlier date will appear to predate any children born from the marriage, creating precisely the {\i pegam mishpacha} that the {\i takanas valad} is designed to prevent.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Halacha Ruling\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Elazar Ruling\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i halacha} is ruled explicitly: {\i amar Rabbi Zeira amar Rav Abba bar Shila amar Rav Hamnuna Saba amar Rav Adda bar Ahava amar Rav halacha k\u8217'Rabbi Elazar} \u8212- the ruling follows {\i Rabbi Elazar}. This is a chain of five transmitters, underscoring the solemnity of the ruling. The practical implications are significant: we require {\i lishmah} in the writing of a {\i get}, we hold {\i eidei mesirah karti} \u8212- the witnesses of delivery are what legally consummate the {\i get} \u8212- and, most stringently, we do not permit even the boilerplate of a {\i get} to be written in advance. {\i Rav} even praises {\i Rabbi Elazar} as {\i tuveina d\u8217'hakimi} \u8212- the best of the sages. A brief clarification: the {\i Rabbi Elazar} at issue here is {\i Rav Elazar}, the student of {\i Rabbi Akiva} and colleague of {\i Rabbi Yehuda} and {\i Rabbi Meir}. He is not to be confused with {\i Rabbi Eliezer}, {\i Rabbi Akiva}\u8217's own teacher, whose stringent position resulted in a {\i cherem} and the designation {\i Shmuti}. Throughout this tractate the relevant figure for the {\i lishmah} discussion is {\i Rav Elazar}, and any prior confusion between the two names should be set aside.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Other Documents\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having ruled like {\i Rabbi Elazar} regarding {\i gittin}, the {\i Gemara} raises the follow-on question: do we also rule like {\i Rabbi Elazar} regarding other documents, {\i l\u8217'kula} \u8212- in the direction of leniency? {\i Rabbi Elazar}\u8217's position on other {\i shtarot} is that the scribe may fill in everything in advance \u8212- names, amounts, dates, all terms \u8212- so long as the witnesses have not yet signed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mechezi k\u8217'Shikra\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rav Pappa} cites {\i Rava} to raise a concern. The case involves a court\u8217's validation of witnesses\u8217' signatures \u8212- a form of notarization. Suppose the judges begin writing their certification before the witnesses have formally testified to their signatures; they intend to sign only after hearing the testimony. {\i Rava} rules that such a pre-written certification is {\i pesulah} \u8212- invalid \u8212- because it constitutes {\i mechezi k\u8217'shikra}: it has the appearance of falsehood. Writing a document that asserts as fact something that has not yet occurred, even if one intends to sign only when it becomes true, creates an inherently misleading document. By analogy, pre-filling any document before the underlying transaction occurs might be {\i mechezi k\u8217'shikra}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Non-Get Leniency\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} resolves this concern by invoking the principle attributed to {\i Rabbi Meir} \u8212- that {\i rav nachman amar haya Rabbi Meir afilu matzo be-ashpah chasmo u-nosno lo kasher}: even a {\i get} found in a garbage heap, if signed and delivered, is valid. We do not rule this way for {\i gittin}, because we require {\i lishmah}. But the underlying principle \u8212- that writing a document does not itself constitute a legal act, and that validity depends on signing \u8212- applies to other {\i shtarot}. {\i Veafilu rabbanan lo pligi alei d\u8217'Rabbi Meir ela b\u8217'gitei nashim d\u8217'ba\u8217'inan ketivah lishmah} \u8212- the Sages dispute {\i Rabbi Meir} only regarding {\i gittin}, where {\i lishmah} in the writing is required. {\i B\u8217'sha\u8217'ar shtaros lo} \u8212- for other documents there is no such dispute, and the full leniency of pre-writing applies.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Pre-writing Leniency\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} thus confirms: for loan documents, bills of sale, and {\i kesuvot}, the scribe may write out the entire document in advance of the transaction, provided the witnesses do not sign until the transaction is complete. This is not {\i mechezi k\u8217'shikra}, because the document has no legal force until signed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ketubah Practice\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} adduces further support from a ruling of {\i Rabbi Yitzchak} citing {\i Rabbi Yochanan}: a promissory note with which one borrowed money and then repaid the loan on the same day may not be reused to borrow again, because {\i kvar nimchal shibudo} \u8212- the lien created by the document was already discharged. Critically, the {\i Gemara} notes: {\i ta\u8217'ama d\u8217'nimchal shibudo, aval mishum shikra lo chaishinan} \u8212- the only reason the document cannot be reused is that its legal effect was already exhausted, but the fact that it was written before being needed is not itself a problem. Concern about {\i shikra} \u8212- falsity in the pre-writing \u8212- does not apply.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The practical upshot is illustrated by the {\i kesuvah}. A {\i kesuvah} is written with the couple\u8217's names, the date, the amount, and all the stipulated terms before the {\i kinyan} has been performed. The text may even include the formula {\i ve-kanina minei} \u8212- \u8220"we acquired from him\u8221" \u8212- even before the {\i kinyan} takes place. Some scribes are careful to leave that particular word incomplete until after the {\i kinyan}, filling it in just before signing, out of an abundance of caution regarding {\i mechezi k\u8217'shikra}. But the {\i Gemara}\u8217's ruling makes clear that this scruple, while understandable, is not required: the pre-writing of the entire document, including all terms and even the formula of acquisition, is permissible, and only the witnesses\u8217' signatures must wait until the transaction is finalized.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Gittin 27\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Lost Get\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishnah Intro\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Mishnah at the top of the page opens with the case of {\i hamavi get ve-avda heimenu}: a {\i shaliach} who is transporting a {\i get} to deliver to the wife loses it along the way. The question is what he may do when he subsequently recovers it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Valid & Invalid\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Mishnah establishes a straightforward principle: {\i matza\u8217'o le-altar}, if he finds the {\i get} immediately \u8212- he dropped it, turned around, saw it, picked it up, and proceeded to deliver it \u8212- then it is {\i kasher}. {\i Ve-im lav pasul}: if he did not find it right away, it is invalid. The concern underlying this ruling is that after any meaningful interval, one cannot be confident that the recovered document is in fact the same {\i get} that was lost. However far-fetched it may seem, it is possible that another couple in the same city shares the husband\u8217's name, the wife\u8217's name, and the place name, and that what the {\i shaliach} has recovered is a different {\i get} altogether \u8212- one belonging to a different couple with identical names. It is on account of this worry that a {\i get} found only after some delay cannot be delivered.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Recognition Markers\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Mishnah provides an exception: {\i matza\u8217'o be-chafisah o be-deluskama} \u8212- if the {\i get} is recovered inside a recognizable sack or purse. {\i Im makiro kasher}: if he recognizes it, it is valid. Rashi explains that this operates in two distinct ways. Either the {\i shaliach} recognizes the container itself \u8212- he lost his wallet, found his wallet, and the {\i get} was inside \u8212- or, even without a recognized container, he is able to identify the {\i get} itself through physical markers. What is required is not merely that he knows the names written within it, but that he can identify the document through some concrete physical characteristic. Recognition of the container is of course implicit in either case.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Bava Metzia Cases\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 No Return Rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now introduces a challenge from a Mishnah elsewhere. The teaching there reads: {\i matza gittei nashim ve-shichrurei avadim, diyateiki matanot ve-shovrin, harei zeh lo yachazir} \u8212- if one finds a woman\u8217's {\i get}, a writ of freedom for a slave, a deathbed bequest, a gift document, or a receipt, one may not return it to either party. The concern is expressed as {\i be-din omer}: one can argue that the document was written but perhaps the underlying transaction was never completed \u8212- the money was never lent, the {\i get} was never delivered. In the case of a loan document, returning it to the party written as the lender could prejudice the borrower if the money was never in fact advanced. Since one cannot determine from which party the document fell, it is held by {\i beis din} and returned to neither. In the case of a {\i get}, returning it to the wife risks allowing her to remarry if it was never actually delivered, while returning it to the husband would deprive her of evidence that she had received it. The document therefore remains with {\i beis din}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Writer\u8217's Approval\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara infers that this prohibition is conditional: {\i ha amar tnu notnim} \u8212- implicitly, if the party who originally caused the document to be written were to say \u8220"give it,\u8221" it would be given. In the case of a {\i get}, if the husband says to deliver it to the wife, one complies. The reasoning is {\i mimah nafshach}: whichever party the document fell from, the husband\u8217's instruction resolves the ambiguity. If it fell from the wife, she is now recovering what is hers; if it fell from the husband, he is now directing that it be given. {\i Ve-afilu zman merubeh} \u8212- the implication is that even after a considerable time has elapsed, once the doubt about delivery is removed, one would proceed. This, however, appears to conflict directly with our Mishnah, which invalidates a {\i get} found after any meaningful delay. The Mishnah in Bava Metzia is apparently unconcerned about the possibility that the recovered document might belong to a different couple entirely; it is concerned only with which party the document fell from. Our Mishnah, by contrast, worries that the recovered document may not be the same {\i get} at all.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Document Identity\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The apparent contradiction thus resolves into a distinction between two different concerns. The Mishnah in Bava Metzia addresses {\i ownership} \u8212- to which party does this document belong, and can we be sure it was delivered? It takes for granted that the document found is the same document that was lost. Our Mishnah raises an entirely different concern: {\i identity} \u8212- is this even the same {\i get}, or might it be a document from an entirely different couple who happen to share the same names? These are two separate problems, and the Gemara now turns to resolving why our Mishnah raises the identity concern while the Mishnah in Bava Metzia does not.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rava\u8217's Resolution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 High Traffic Concern\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava resolves the tension as follows: {\i kan bemakom she-shayarot metsuyot, kan bemakom she-ein shayarot metsuyot} \u8212- the cases differ based on whether or not the location in question is a high-traffic area frequented by caravans. Our Mishnah\u8217's concern that the recovered {\i get} might belong to a different couple is, on its face, a remarkably remote possibility \u8212- that another man in the same city shares the husband\u8217's name, another woman shares the wife\u8217's name, and their {\i get} happened to be lost in the same vicinity. Rava explains that our Mishnah entertains this concern only in a setting with heavy foot traffic and caravan passage. If the {\i shaliach} loses the {\i get} in a bustling crossroads, the worry becomes at least conceivable. The Mishnah in Bava Metzia, which is unconcerned with document identity, addresses a case where there is no such heavy traffic \u8212- the question there is simply which party the document fell from, not whether it came from an entirely different couple.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Duplicate Couples\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara immediately adds a further limitation: {\i ve-afilu bemakom she-shayarot metsuyot, ve-hu she-huchzeku shenei Yosef ben Shimon be-ir achat} \u8212- even in a high-traffic area, the identity concern arises only where it is actually known that two couples bearing the same names exist in the same city. The concern, even in a busy location, must be grounded in a concrete known fact \u8212- that a second couple with identical names is present \u8212- not in mere theoretical possibility.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This two-part requirement emerges not only from Rava\u8217's stated position but is also demonstrated by the case of a {\i get} found in the {\i beis din} of Rav Huna. A {\i shaliach} had come to deliver a {\i get}, lost it, and wished to reclaim it. The {\i get} bore the name of a town, {\i Shiyurei}, situated on the river {\i Rakhis}, and named a man divorcing a woman. Rav Huna asked: ought one to be concerned that there is another city called {\i Shiyurei} \u8212- one we do not know of \u8212- where another couple bears the same names? Rav Hisda directed Rabbah, then a student of Rav Huna, to investigate relevant precedents in time for Rav Huna\u8217's deliberations that evening. Rabbah went and found the teaching in Bava Metzia: {\i kol ma\u8217'aseh beis din harei zeh yachazir} \u8212- any document that has been authenticated by {\i beis din} may be returned, since {\i beis din} authentication occurs only after delivery has been confirmed. From this, Rabbah inferred that one is not concerned about other cities of the same name harboring couples with the same names.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara observes that Rav Huna\u8217's {\i beis din} was itself a high-traffic venue \u8212- all documents passed through there, and it was precisely the sort of place where lost documents might turn up. Yet Rabbah was willing to infer from the Bava Metzia Mishnah that even in such a setting one returns the document, and apparently was not worried about an unknown city of the same name with a similarly named couple. This proves that Rabbah requires both criteria \u8212- high traffic {\i and} known duplicate couples in the same city \u8212- before entertaining the concern that a recovered document might be a different {\i get} entirely. High traffic alone is insufficient.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Flax Marketplace Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This two-pronged analysis is borne out by a practical ruling Rava issued. A {\i get} was found in the place of flax in Pumbedita, and Rava returned it to the {\i shaliach} following his own ruling, dismissing the concern that it might belong to a different couple. Two versions of the case are reported. According to some, {\i beduchta heicha d\u8217'tavu kitna} \u8212- it was a place where flax was processed, an industrial location outside the marketplace. Even if it were known that two couples with identical names both hailed from that town, there are no caravans there; the traffic criterion is not met, and so the concern does not arise. According to others, the case involved a place where flax was {\i sold} \u8212- a marketplace with genuine caravan traffic \u8212- yet it was not known that two couples with the same names existed in that city. Again, one criterion is absent. Either way, Rava returns the {\i get}, demonstrating that his position requires {\i both} high traffic and known duplicate couples before one withholds the document.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rav Zeira\u8217's View\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishnah vs Baraita\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Zeira now undertakes essentially the same exercise as Rava, but frames the contradiction differently. Rather than contrasting our Mishnah with the Mishnah in Bava Metzia, he sets our Mishnah against a {\i baraita} in Bava Metzia. The Mishnah is restated: {\i hameivi get ve-avado heimenu, matza\u8217'o le-altar kasher, ve-im lav pasul} \u8212- found immediately, valid; found after a delay, invalid. This is contrasted with the {\i baraita}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Husband Agreement\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i baraita} states explicitly: {\i matza get isha be-shuk, bi-zman she-ha-ba\u8217'al modeh yachzir le-isha} \u8212- if a woman\u8217's {\i get} is found in the marketplace, and the husband acknowledges it is hers, one returns it to the woman. {\i Ein ha-ba\u8217'al modeh lo yachzir lo le-zeh ve-lo le-zeh} \u8212- if the husband does not acknowledge it, one returns it to neither party. And critically, the {\i baraita} adds: {\i ve-afilu le-zman merubeh} \u8212- even after a long time has elapsed, once the husband concedes the {\i get} is hers, it is returned. The {\i baraita} is thus entirely unconcerned with the possibility that the document might belong to a different couple, even when considerable time has passed. This is the same tension Rava addressed, and Rav Zeira resolves it with the same solution: our Mishnah is the case of high traffic, the {\i baraita} addresses a case without such traffic.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Some report that Rav Zeira requires both criteria, just as Rava does \u8212- high traffic {\i plus} known duplicate couples \u8212- and holds that only when both conditions are met does one not return the {\i get}. Others say Rav Zeira may be stricter than Rava: once the location has heavy traffic, even without confirmed knowledge of a second couple bearing the same names, Rav Zeira may deem the concern sufficient to invalidate the {\i get}. On that reading, high traffic alone would be enough to worry that somewhere a similarly named couple exists, even if one has no concrete knowledge of them. This position, admittedly far-fetched in its scope, would then {\i pliga deRava} \u8212- stand in opposition to Rava\u8217's ruling.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Implicit Language\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara asks a pointed question about methodology: given that Rava accomplished the same reconciliation using two Mishnayot \u8212- which are more authoritative sources \u8212- why did Rav Zeira instead compare our Mishnah with a {\i baraita}? The answer is that Rav Zeira found the Mishnah in Bava Metzia inadequate as a proof text. {\i Mi katani im ha-ba\u8217'al modeh yachzir ve-afilu le-zman merubeh?} \u8212- does the Mishnah there actually state explicitly that when the husband agrees, one returns the {\i get} even after a long time? It does not. That inference is implicit. And since it is only implicit, one might argue that the Mishnah\u8217's permission to return the document when the husband agrees is limited to cases of immediate recovery. The Mishnah may simply mean: yes, under the right conditions \u8212- including immediacy \u8212- you return it if the husband agrees. Only the {\i baraita} states explicitly that one returns the {\i get} when the husband agrees {\i even after considerable time}. When a teaching states a rule explicitly without qualification, it communicates that the rule applies broadly; had immediacy been a required condition, the {\i baraita} would have said so. Therefore, Rav Zeira found the {\i baraita} a sharper proof of the genuine contradiction, even at the cost of using a less authoritative source.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Stricter Approaches\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Witness Testimony\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yirmiya takes a substantially stricter position. Rather than reading our Mishnah as the exceptional case warranting concern about document identity, he inverts the framing: the case in Bava Metzia where the document is returned is itself the exceptional situation, and the rule is one of pervasive concern about document identity. Under Rabbi Yirmiya\u8217's reading, a {\i get} may be returned only when the witnesses signed on it testify: {\i me\u8217'olam lo chasmanu ela al get echad she-Yosef ben Shimon} \u8212- we have never in our lives signed on any other {\i get} bearing this husband\u8217's and wife\u8217's names. Only that affirmative declaration suffices to remove all doubt that the document is the unique {\i get} it purports to be.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Same-Name Witnesses\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara raises an objection: {\i mahu d\u8217'teima leichosh shemma isromi shma ke-shma ve-edim ke-edim} \u8212- ought one not to worry that even the witnesses could by coincidence share their names with witnesses on a different {\i get}? Perhaps another couple bears the same husband\u8217's and wife\u8217's names, their {\i get} is signed by witnesses who happen to share the names of these witnesses, and we have only displaced the problem one level. The Gemara dismisses this: one does not entertain that degree of concern. The witnesses\u8217' testimony that they have never signed another such {\i get} is taken as decisive; the possibility that both the parties {\i and} the witnesses are coincidentally replicated is too remote to take seriously.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Siman Muvhak\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Ashi offers yet another approach. He essentially applies the principle embedded in the end of our Mishnah to the case in Bava Metzia. For Rav Ashi, concern about document identity is the general rule, and one returns a {\i get} only when the {\i shaliach} is able to identify the specific document through a {\i siman muvhak} \u8212- an absolute, unambiguous identifying mark. The example given is: {\i nekev yesh bo be-tzad os ploni} \u8212- there is a hole directly beside a specific named letter. Such a marker is uniquely identifying and admits of no reasonable doubt.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Siman Types\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara draws a sharp distinction between a {\i siman muvhak} and an ordinary {\i siman}. A {\i siman} is an identifying marker that increases the likelihood that an object is the one claimed \u8212- for example, noting that a wallet had a hole in it somewhere near the top. While this certainly makes it more probable that the wallet is the claimant\u8217's, it is not conclusive: other documents may also have holes, and the description is insufficiently precise. A {\i siman muvhak}, by contrast, is an absolute identifier that removes all reasonable doubt \u8212- a hole beside a {\i specific} letter on a {\i specific} line, for instance. In the context of returning lost objects generally, even an ordinary {\i siman} suffices to return an {\i aveidah}. This practice, however, may be only a rabbinic accommodation; at the Torah level, one might require a {\i siman muvhak} to establish ownership conclusively. For a {\i get}, this distinction is critical. Even if {\i simanim} of the ordinary kind are sufficient to return a wallet \u8212- whether because {\i simanim} are {\i d\u8217'oraita} or because rabbinic law permits it for monetary objects \u8212- they may be insufficient when the validity of a divorce hangs in the balance. Rav Ashi\u8217's uncertainty about whether {\i simanim} are {\i d\u8217'oraita} or only {\i d\u8217'rabbanan} leads him to require the more exacting standard: only a {\i siman muvhak} permits returning a {\i get}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Siman vs Recognition\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two Options\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbah Bar Bar Channah lost a {\i get} in the {\i beis midrash}. When the document was found, he declared that he could identify it in either of two ways: {\i i simanei it li bagah} \u8212- he could provide an identifying mark, some specific physical characteristic he could describe and that could be verified \u8212- or, alternatively, {\i i tvi\u8217'at eina it li bagah} \u8212- he could simply look at the document and recognize it through {\i tvi\u8217'at eina}, the impression of the eye. This mode of recognition is familiar from ordinary experience: one may be entirely certain that a garment is one\u8217's own without being able to articulate which specific feature makes it recognizable, because the knowledge arises from a gestalt of subtle accumulated cues. In Bava Metzia, the Gemara establishes that a {\i talmid chacham} may recover a lost object through {\i tvi\u8217'at eina}, because his reliability is trusted; extending this privilege to ordinary people would of course create an open-ended mechanism for fraudulent claims.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Uncertain Grounds\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The document was returned to Rabbah Bar Bar Channah. However, he himself expressed uncertainty: {\i lo yadana im mishum simana hadaruhu} \u8212- he did not know on what basis it had been returned to him. Was it because he offered a {\i siman}, and the {\i beis din} deemed that sufficient \u8212- thus implying a view that {\i simanim d\u8217'oraita} and adequate even for a {\i get}? Or was it {\i mishum tvi\u8217'at eina} \u8212- because he had asserted personal recognition?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Torah Scholars Only\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The uncertainty carries practical {\i halachic} weight. If the {\i get} was returned on the basis of {\i tvi\u8217'at eina}, then this procedure is available exclusively to a {\i talmid chacham} \u8212- {\i ve-davka tzurba me-rabbanan, aval inish de-alma lo} \u8212- but not to an ordinary person. If, however, the {\i get} was returned because the ordinary {\i siman} he offered was deemed sufficient, then an important and broader principle follows: even without a {\i siman muvhak}, a {\i siman} of the standard kind suffices to return a {\i get} \u8212- a ruling that would apply to any {\i shaliach}, not only to a scholar. The inability to determine which of these two grounds motivated the ruling leaves both the scope of {\i tvi\u8217'at eina} and the sufficiency of ordinary {\i simanim} for a {\i get} unresolved.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Immediacy Measures\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Caravan Duration\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having established the framework of immediate versus non-immediate recovery, the Gemara presents a {\i baraita} in which Tannaim debate exactly what constitutes {\i she-lo le-altar} \u8212- a non-immediate interval. Rabbi Natan defines it functionally, within Rava\u8217's framework of caravans: {\i kedei she-tavor shayara ve-tishreh} \u8212- the amount of time it would take for a caravan to pass by and to spend some time encamped in the area. Once that interval has elapsed, the concern that the document might have arrived from an entirely different group is deemed relevant.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Witness Requirement\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Shimon ben Eleazar does not define the interval by a fixed duration at all. Rather, {\i kedei she-yehei adam omed ve-ro\u8217'eh she-lo avar sham adam} \u8212- one must have a person who was standing watch and can testify that no one passed through the area between the moment the {\i get} was dropped and the moment it was recovered. This standard is inherently more demanding, since even a small number of passersby would disqualify the recovery absent such testimony.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tarrying Standard\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Others offer an intermediate position: {\i she-lo shaha adam sham} \u8212- what matters is not whether anyone merely passed by, but whether anyone actually tarried in the area. If people walked through without stopping, the concern is not triggered; once someone stopped and remained, one worries that the document might have fallen from that person.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Writing/Reading Time\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yehudah HaNasi offers a time-based standard using a unit drawn from the subject matter itself: {\i kedei likhtov et ha-get} \u8212- the time required to write a {\i get}. There is no logical nexus between writing time and the risk of document confusion; Rabbi simply chose a time unit familiar to the context. Rabbi Yitzchak shortens this considerably: {\i kedei likroso} \u8212- the time it takes merely to read a {\i get}, a substantially briefer interval. Yet others combine the two: the time to write {\i and} read it.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Simanim Evidence\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Marks Testify\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now turns to the role of identifying marks in recovering a {\i get} after what would otherwise be a disqualifying delay. The principle stated is: {\i va-afilu sha\u8217'ah, ve-yesh bo simanim, me\u8217'idim alav} \u8212- even if considerable time has elapsed, if the {\i get} bears identifying marks, those marks testify on its behalf. This restates and reinforces what the Gemara concluded in the analysis of Rav Ashi\u8217's position: where a sufficient identifier is present, the lapse of time does not render the document invalid.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hole Mark\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The example of a qualifying {\i siman muvhak} is given: {\i nekev yesh bo be-tzad os ploni} \u8212- there is a hole positioned directly beside a specific named letter. The precision of this description \u8212- a particular hole in relation to a particular letter \u8212- is what elevates it to the level of an absolute identifier. It is not merely suggestive; it is conclusive.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Insufficient Marks\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 By contrast, physical characteristics of the document as an object \u8212- {\i simanei ha-guf}, as it were \u8212- are insufficient: {\i ein me\u8217'idim al simanei ha-guf, d\u8217'amri aroch ve-gutz} \u8212- that the parchment is long or short, wide or narrow, dark or light in color. These are generic physical attributes that many documents may share and that cannot serve as identifying marks. They constitute at most an ordinary {\i siman}, not a {\i siman muvhak}, and as established earlier, an ordinary {\i siman} is inadequate to permit delivery of a {\i get}. The same principle that applies to the {\i baraita}\u8217's analysis here parallels precisely the conclusion already reached in the immediately preceding sugya.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Accepted Standard\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tarrying Rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara records two practical rulings on the question of non-immediacy. Rav Yehuda said in the name of Shmuel: {\i halacha she-lo shaha adam sham} \u8212- the accepted standard is that non-immediacy is constituted when someone actually {\i tarried} in the area. Mere passage is insufficient to trigger the concern; it is only lingering that creates the worry that the document might have fallen from another person.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 No Passerby\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbah bar bar Chana said in the name of Rav Yitzchak bar Shmuel: {\i halacha she-lo avar adam sham} \u8212- the accepted ruling is stricter: the interval is non-immediate as soon as even a single person has {\i passed through}, not merely tarried. One might ask why these authorities stated the {\i positions} rather than simply ruling in favor of one of the named Tannaim above. The Gemara explains: {\i mishum d\u8217'aphlei} \u8212- because there were variant traditions about which Tanna held which view, it was cleaner to state the operative rule directly rather than risk confusion by citing a name associated with a disputed attribution.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Wallet Definitions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chafisa\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Mishnah\u8217's mention of a {\i chafisah} and a {\i deluskama} as containers whose presence permits identification of the {\i get} invites a brief lexical clarification. What is a {\i chafisah}? Rabbah bar bar Chana explains: {\i cheima k\u8217'tana} \u8212- a small flask.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Deluskama\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 And what is a {\i deluskama}? {\i Talika d\u8217'savi} \u8212- a purse carried by elderly people. In both cases, the point is the same: if the {\i get} is found within an object that itself can be recognized and identified as belonging to the {\i shaliach}, or if the document itself bears a {\i siman muvhak}, the combination of markers is sufficient to establish that this is indeed the same {\i get} that was lost. Without such markers \u8212- whether the container is unrecognized, the {\i siman} falls short of {\i siman muvhak}, or the claimant cannot offer {\i tvi\u8217'at eina} at the level the law requires \u8212- the document may not be delivered if found after any meaningful delay, given the concern, narrow as it may be, that it represents an entirely different {\i get} from a differently named couple who happen to share every relevant name.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Gittin 28\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Get Delivery & Chazaka\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Opening & Mishnah Intro\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Today\u8217's {\i daf} is dedicated to the learning in memory of Goldie, {\i Goldie bas Golda bas Yitzchak Yehuda}, on her third {\i yahrtzeit}. May it be in her memory and in her {\i zechus}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 We pick up with the {\i mishna} on 28a. The {\i mishna} opens: {\i hamavei get} \u8212- someone is delivering a {\i get}. The entire first {\i masechta} opens with the concept of {\i shelichus}, and that framework allows the Gemara to explore some genuinely interesting scenarios. We already dealt with what happens if the {\i get} is lost by the {\i shaliach}; now we confront a different problem.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chazaka & Status Quo\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} states: {\i hamavei get vehenicho zaken o choleh} \u8212- if the {\i shaliach} left the husband and at that time he was old or he was sick, {\i notno lah bechezkas shehu chayim} \u8212- the {\i get} may be delivered on the presumption that he is still alive. The wife is treated as divorced, with all the relevant consequences. Presumably the most immediate concern is {\i yibum} \u8212- but it is worth noting that even if one later discovers that the husband died, there may be no way to precisely date events, and the matter may simply never be determined. The operative principle is {\i chazaka}: he was alive then, and we presume he remains alive now.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Wife of Kohen Overseas\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} then introduces a second case: {\i bas Yisrael hanesua leKohen vehalach baalah limdinas hayam} \u8212- an Israelite woman married to a {\i Kohen} whose husband travels overseas. {\i Ocheles baterumah bechezkas shehu chayim} \u8212- she continues eating {\i terumah} on the presumption that he is alive. The question naturally arises: for how long? A hundred years? The Gemara will address this to some extent, but the baseline ruling is that she continues indefinitely without being required to stop.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chattat from Overseas\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} adds a third case: {\i hasholeiach chataso mimdinas hayam} \u8212- someone who sends a {\i chatas} from overseas. {\i Makrivim osah bechezkas shehu chayim} \u8212- the offering is brought on the presumption that he is alive. Were he not alive, it would be a {\i chatas shemeisu baaleha} and would have to go {\i limisa}; it could not be brought as a {\i korban}. And yet the {\i chazaka} permits it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Terumah as Chidush\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Of all these cases, {\i terumah} stands out as the most remarkable {\i chidush}. One might say that {\i gittin} is enormously weighty \u8212- the prohibition of {\i eishes ish} is at stake \u8212- though it bears noting that either way the woman is permitted, whether as a {\i gerusha} or an {\i almana}. And {\i terumah} consumed by a {\i zar} carries a penalty of {\i chiyuv misa bidei shamayim}, which is severe. But the deeper reason {\i terumah} is the greatest {\i chidush} is that it is ongoing. In the other cases, the {\i chazaka} is invoked at one moment in time \u8212- a single determination that he was alive when the {\i get} was sent or when the {\i korban} was dispatched. The {\i terumah} case, by contrast, requires an indefinite, open-ended presumption: she eats {\i terumah} continuously for years, without any terminus, on the basis of nothing more than the status quo.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rava on Age & Illness\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava\u8217's Limitations\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara opens with a qualification from Rava: {\i lo shanu ela zaken} \u8212- the {\i mishna}\u8217's ruling applies only to someone who is merely old in the ordinary sense, {\i shelo higia ligvurot}, meaning he has not yet reached the age of eighty. Similarly it applies to one who is merely sick, {\i sherov cholim lachayim} \u8212- because most sick people recover.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Exceptions: Elderly & Go\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava continues: {\i aval zaken shehigia ligvurot} \u8212- if the man has already reached eighty years of age \u8212- {\i v\u8217'goses} \u8212- or if he is a {\i goses}, meaning he is actively in the process of dying \u8212- {\i sherov gosesim lemisa} \u8212- the majority of such people do die, and therefore one cannot invoke {\i chezkat chayim} on their behalf. In such cases the {\i chazaka} may be understood as {\i chazakah asuyah lehishtanot} \u8212- a status quo that is already in the process of changing.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Braita Challenge\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye challenges Rava from a {\i braita}: {\i hamavei get vehenicho zaken afilu ben me\u8217'ah shanah} \u8212- even if the husband is a hundred years old, {\i notno lo bechezkas shehu kayam} \u8212- the {\i get} is delivered on the presumption that he is alive. This appears to directly contradict Rava\u8217's position that an elderly man who has reached {\i gevurot} cannot be presumed alive.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Plateau Effect\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava responds: {\i keivan de\u8217'ifrak ifrak} \u8212- once someone has made it that far, he has demonstrated genuine longevity. The concern with most people after eighty is that the probability of death is increasing; but once a person reaches an extremely advanced age \u8212- Rashi suggests around ninety \u8212- the risk plateaus. He is no longer at escalating risk of death on any given day, and therefore we can once again presume he remains alive.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gosses Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 There is, however, an important asymmetry between the case of old age and the case of a {\i goses}. Old age, even at eighty, does not carry a distinct {\i halachic} status as such \u8212- Rava creates the category of {\i gevurot}, but it is not a formal {\i halachic} classification. A {\i goses}, by contrast, carries a clearly defined {\i halachic} status: there is an explicit {\i halacha} that one may not move or handle a {\i goses}, out of concern that the very act of touching him will hasten his death. Because {\i goses} is a recognized {\i halachic} category in its own right, the argument that a {\i goses} has no {\i chezkat chayim} stands on considerably firmer ground than the argument about an old man.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Get vs.\u160 ?Terumah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 The Contradiction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye now presents Rava with a pointed internal contradiction. The {\i mishna} states that we deliver a {\i get} on the presumption that an old or sick husband is alive. Yet there is another ruling: if a husband says {\i harei zeh gitech sha\u8217'ah achas kodem lemisasi} \u8212- this is your {\i get}, to take effect one hour before I die \u8212- the wife is immediately prohibited from eating {\i terumah}. She cannot eat {\i terumah} because he might die in the next moment. This appears to be a direct conflict: in one case we are unconcerned about the husband\u8217's death, and in the other we are immediately concerned.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava\u8217's Distinction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava responds: {\i terumah agittin karamis?} \u8212- why are you challenging a {\i terumah} ruling from a {\i gittin} ruling? These are not comparable cases. {\i Terumah efshar efshar velo efshar} \u8212- when it comes to {\i terumah}, she has a choice; she can simply refrain from eating {\i terumah} if there is any uncertainty. But the {\i get} \u8212- this woman needs to get on with her life. Just because we invoke the {\i chazaka} for purposes of {\i gittin} does not mean we apply the same standard to {\i terumah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafot Reframes\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot notes, however, that this response does not go far enough, because the very next line of the {\i mishna} itself applies the {\i chazaka} to {\i terumah}. It is therefore better to compare {\i terumah} against {\i terumah}. The {\i mishna} says the wife of a {\i Kohen} who has gone overseas eats {\i terumah} on the presumption he is alive. Yet the ruling about {\i harei zeh gitech sha\u8217'ah achas kodem lemisasi} says she immediately cannot eat {\i terumah}. That is a direct contradiction within {\i terumah} itself, and it requires an answer.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Ada\u8217's Answer\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Ada bar Abba offers a response: {\i shani hasam sheharei asar aleha sha\u8217'ah achas kodem lemisaso} \u8212- the case of one hour before death is different, because in that case the husband has explicitly created a prohibition that will apply to her at some moment during his lifetime. Rashi acknowledges that this is more of a descriptive distinction than an explanatory one \u8212- the Gemara has identified that the two cases differ, but has not yet fully explained why the difference matters. As will become clear, the real distinction lies between a concern about whether he is already dead ({\i shema met}) versus a concern about whether he will die ({\i shema yamut}), but the Gemara at this stage has not yet articulated that distinction with full clarity.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Papa\u8217's Challenge\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Papa challenges: {\i midi de\u8217'ihu meiyas barishah dilma ihi meisa barishah} \u8212- how do you know he will die first? Perhaps she will die before him. The implicit reasoning behind the earlier analysis had been that since there will inevitably be a moment in this woman\u8217's life when she is prohibited from eating {\i terumah} \u8212- namely, that one hour before his death \u8212- we are always concerned that this might be that moment. Rav Papa\u8217's challenge dismantles this: if she dies before him, there will never be such a moment at all. The argument that {\i she must always be on guard because such a moment is coming} fails, because no such moment may ever arrive. This challenge lays the groundwork for the distinction the Gemara will draw between a concern about the present ({\i shema met}) and a concern about the future ({\i shema yamut}).\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Tannaitic Dispute\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Abaye\u8217's Proposal\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye proposes a different resolution: the apparent contradiction may simply reflect a {\i tannaitic} dispute. {\i Ha Rabbi Meir delo chaish lemisa, ha Rabbi Yehuda dechaish lemisa} \u8212- the permissive ruling of the {\i mishna} follows Rabbi Meir, who is not concerned about the possibility of death, while the stringent position about {\i sha\u8217'ah achas kodem lemisasi} follows Rabbi Yehuda and others who are concerned about such a possibility. This is somewhat ironic because, as a rule, Rabbi Meir is known for being {\i chaish lemi\u8217'uta} \u8212- attentive to minority cases \u8212- and yet here he is the lenient position.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Barrel Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This brings Abaye to cite the relevant {\i mishna}: someone who purchases wine from Samaritans just before {\i Shabbat}, when there is no time to properly separate {\i terumah} and {\i maaser}, may say: two {\i lugin} of this wine shall be designated as {\i terumah}, ten of the remaining hundred as {\i maaser rishon}, and nine of the remaining ninety as {\i maaser sheni}. He may then immediately proceed to drink from the wine. This is the position of Rabbi Meir. After {\i Shabbat}, he designates from the remaining wine what corresponds to the designated portions. Rabbi Yehuda, Rabbi Yosi, and Rabbi Shimon all prohibit this arrangement.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Meir Unconcerned\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara elsewhere explains the basis for this dispute. When asked whether he is not afraid that the barrel might break before {\i Shabbat} ends \u8212- leaving no wine to be designated later, which would mean he had drunk {\i tevel} \u8212- Rabbi Meir responds: when it breaks, I will worry about it. He is not concerned the barrel will break. This parallels a concern about death or some other catastrophic change in circumstances: is one concerned that some tragedy will occur and transform the current state of affairs?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Parallel to Death\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara understands the case of the barrel as a close parallel to the concern about death \u8212- whether conceptually or because both reduce to the same question: are we concerned that some accident or unforeseen tragedy will upset the current status? According to this reading, the dispute between Rabbi Meir and Rabbi Yehuda about the barrel maps onto the question of whether we worry that the husband might die.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Shema Met vs.\u160 ?Yamut\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 The Distinction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava, however, offers a sharper analysis that goes beyond attributing the matter to a {\i tannaitic} dispute. Rava says: {\i shema met lo chayshinan, shema yamut chayshinan} \u8212- whether he is already dead, that we do not worry about; whether he will die, that we do worry about. This is a fundamental and practically significant distinction.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chazaka & Future\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i chazaka} speaks to the present status: it asserts that the status quo we knew to obtain continues to obtain now. It does not address the future. If we knew he was alive and have heard nothing to the contrary, we presume he remains alive \u8212- the {\i chazaka} resolves the question of his present status. But the {\i chazaka} cannot resolve a question about what will happen going forward.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Present vs.\u160 ?Future\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 In the case of the wife eating {\i terumah} while her husband is overseas: the question is whether he is alive right now. We knew he was alive when he left; we presume he is still alive. The {\i chazaka} answers this question directly. In the case of {\i harei zeh gitech sha\u8217'ah achas kodem lemisasi}: the husband is sitting right there, alive. The question is not whether he is alive now \u8212- that is not in dispute \u8212- but whether he will die within the next hour. That is a question about the future, and the {\i chazaka} that he is presently alive says nothing about it. Since the {\i chazaka} provides no answer to the future question, one must remain concerned, and she may not eat {\i terumah}. This is {\i shema yamut}, a future concern, and the {\i chazaka} does not address it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Ada Challenges\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Ada bar Matna challenges Rava\u8217's formulation: if {\i shema met} is universally not a concern and {\i shema yamut} is universally a concern, then why does the barrel case appear as a {\i tannaitic} dispute? If both matters are agreed upon by all, neither the leniency nor the stringency should be a point of disagreement. This challenge presses Rava to refine his position further.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rava\u8217's Resolution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Barrel vs.\u160 ?Death\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava begins to respond by noting a factual distinction between barrels and people: not every barrel breaks, and one can be careful to prevent it from breaking. Every person, by contrast, will eventually die \u8212- death is inevitable. This asymmetry might explain why the barrel case is disputed while the inevitability of death is not.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Guardian Needs Guardian\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Mesharshiya pushes back: {\i shomer tzarich shomer} \u8212- your guardian himself needs a guardian. You cannot rely on someone\u8217's carefulness to guarantee that the barrel will never break; even the most careful guardian cannot be fully trusted to prevent all accidents. This undercuts the proposed distinction between barrels and human mortality.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 All Agree: No Shema Met\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava therefore clarifies: {\i shema met lo chayshinan} \u8212- the concern that someone is already dead, that we are not concerned about, according to everyone. The {\i mishna}\u8217's ruling that we deliver the {\i get} and permit the eating of {\i terumah} is agreed upon by all {\i tannaim}. The {\i chazaka} that a person remains in the status we last knew him to be in applies universally.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shema Yamut Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Shema yamut} \u8212- the concern that some accident or tragedy will occur in the near term and cause a change in status \u8212- that is a {\i tannaitic} dispute. Rabbi Meir is not concerned that such events will occur imminently; Rabbi Yehuda and the other {\i tannaim} are. This is the correct framing of where the disagreement lies.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Tosafot on Time\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Meir & Long Term\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot refines this picture significantly. Even Rabbi Meir, who is not concerned that the barrel will break before {\i Shabbat} is over, would still be concerned if asked whether something might go wrong over a span of ten years. Rabbi Meir\u8217's leniency is not absolute: it applies to short-term concerns, but not to the possibility of death or catastrophe over a genuinely long period of time.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Yehuda\u8217's Concern\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Conversely, Rabbi Yehuda, who is concerned that the barrel might break in the short term \u8212- before {\i Shabbat} ends \u8212- is not concerned about {\i shema met le\u8217'altar}, that the husband dropped dead the very instant he walked out the door.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Absurd Consequence\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot demonstrates why this must be so with a reductio ad absurdum: if a wife of a {\i Kohen} were required to worry that her husband died the moment he stepped out of the house, she could never eat {\i terumah} once he left her presence \u8212- not even when he went to the synagogue. She would have to be watching him at all times. This is plainly unworkable and cannot be the {\i halacha}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Time-Qualified Dispute\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot therefore concludes that the real dispute is more precisely framed as follows: the disagreement is not between {\i shema met} in the absolute and {\i shema yamut} in the absolute, but between {\i shema met lezman merubah} \u8212- the concern that after a substantial period of time he may be dead \u8212- and {\i shema yamut lezman mu\u8217'at} \u8212- the concern that he will die imminently or in the near term. The former is universally not a concern, the latter is universally a concern, and the {\i tannaitic} dispute occupies the middle ground: whether we worry about accidents or death occurring over the short-to-medium term. These are critical distinctions in {\i halacha} that find application in a number of areas, including {\i hilchos niddah}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Chattat & Smicha\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 The Challenge\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now turns to a challenge against the {\i mishna}\u8217's third case \u8212- the {\i chatas} sent from overseas. If we presume the sender is alive, should we not require {\i smicha} \u8212- the laying of hands on the {\i korban} \u8212- which demands the owner\u8217's physical presence? The fact that the {\i korban} is brought without {\i smicha} seems inconsistent with truly treating the owner as alive.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Chattat & Smicha (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Yosef\u8217's Answer\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Yosef answers: the case involves a {\i korban nashim} \u8212- an offering brought by a woman. Women are not obligated to perform {\i smicha} on their offerings, and therefore the absence of {\i smicha} raises no difficulty.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Pappa\u8217's Answer\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Pappa offers an alternative: the case involves a {\i chatas ha\u8217'of} \u8212- a bird sacrifice \u8212- and {\i smicha} is simply not performed on bird offerings at all.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Three Cases Explained\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then explains why all three cases in the {\i mishna} \u8212- {\i gittin}, {\i terumah}, and {\i chattat} \u8212- are necessary. Each one teaches something the others do not. The {\i get} case might have been explained by the urgency of the woman\u8217's situation \u8212- she has no alternative but to rely on the {\i chazaka}. If only {\i gittin} had been mentioned, one might have said that {\i terumah} is different because she could simply refrain from eating {\i terumah} and eat ordinary {\i chulin} instead. The {\i terumah} case is therefore needed to teach that even where an alternative technically exists, the {\i chazaka} permits her to eat. And the {\i chattat} case is needed to teach that even where there is no urgency \u8212- she can simply wait until the owner next comes to the {\i Beis HaMikdash} \u8212- we still permit the {\i korban} to be brought on the basis of {\i chezkat chayim}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Three Cases of Life\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Three Hazakas\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara presents three cases introduced by Rabbi Elazar ben Parta, which the Sages affirmed. First, a city surrounded by a siege ({\i karkom}). Second, a ship caught in a storm at sea. Third, someone brought before a court on a capital charge. In all three of these cases, the person is presumed to still be alive \u8212- {\i bechezkat kayamin} \u8212- and one may proceed accordingly with respect to a {\i get}, {\i terumah}, and related matters.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 When Hazaka Fails\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 However, once the situation has been resolved in the worst direction \u8212- the city has actually been conquered, the ship has been lost at sea, the death sentence has been carried out \u8212- then we can no longer simply presume the person is alive. In these cases, we apply {\i chumrei chayim ve\u8217'chumrei meisim} \u8212- the stringencies of both life and death simultaneously \u8212- because there is genuine uncertainty as to whether the person is alive or dead.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Consequences for Terumah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This dual stringency has concrete implications. Consider a {\i bas Yisrael} married to a {\i Kohen}: if her husband\u8217's fate is now uncertain \u8212- his city has been conquered, or he has been sentenced and the execution is imminent \u8212- she cannot eat {\i terumah}, because we must entertain the possibility that he is dead and she is no longer part of the {\i Kohen\u8217's} household. At the same time, she cannot remarry, because we must also entertain the possibility that he remains alive. Similarly, a {\i bas Kohen} married to an Israelite: if he might be dead, she might revert to her father\u8217's household and regain the right to eat {\i terumah}, but since he might be alive, she cannot eat {\i terumah} either. Either way, the uncertainty produces maximum stringency.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Time Limits & Witnesses\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 As for time limits \u8212- whether there is any point at which the uncertainty is resolved \u8212- there is no simple rule. There is an entire chapter in Yevamos dealing with testimony about death, including the application of {\i eid echad} and {\i masiach lefi tumo} (innocent speech by a single witness). Those mechanisms allow for a lower evidentiary threshold in certain circumstances, enabling a determination that the husband has died. Until such testimony is available, however, the full dual stringency applies, and the most severe consequence is of course that she cannot remarry.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Jewish vs.\u160 ?Non-Jewish Co\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Yosef\u8217's Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Yosef qualifies the third case of Rabbi Elazar ben Parta \u8212- the person brought to trial on a capital charge. The uncertainty as to whether such a person has actually been executed applies only when the trial was conducted before a Jewish court ({\i Beis Din shel Yisrael}), which retains the theoretical possibility of retracting even after judgment has been passed. A non-Jewish court, by contrast, once it has passed judgment, will not retract.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Abaye Challenges\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye challenges: even a non-Jewish court can be bribed. How can one be certain they will not reverse the judgment in exchange for payment?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sealed Judgment\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Yosef responds: they are willing to accept bribes only before the judgment has been formally sealed \u8212- before the {\i pursishnamag} (whether this refers to a particular official or the act of affixing a formal seal, Tosafot explores the possibilities). Once the final seal has been affixed, they will not retract even for a bribe, because to do so at that stage would be too obvious and would undermine their credibility entirely.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Baraita on Courts\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i baraita} is cited: if two witnesses come forward in any location and testify that they were present in a particular city, in a particular court, and they witnessed the formal sentencing of a named individual \u8212- that testimony is accepted and the man is executed on its basis. This shows that we are not concerned that a Jewish court\u8217's judgment may have been subsequently overturned.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 On the other side: if one hears from a Jewish court that a certain man died or was executed, {\i yasiu es ishto} \u8212- his wife may remarry. But if the same announcement comes from a non-Jewish court, {\i al yasiu es ishto} \u8212- she may not remarry on that basis.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Met vs.\u160 ?Neherag\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 The Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now asks: when the {\i baraita} uses the words {\i met} (died) and {\i neherag} (was killed), does it mean that the man has literally already died or been executed, or does it mean that he is in the process of being led out to die?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Logical Problem\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If the {\i baraita} refers to a literal announcement of death or completed execution, a difficulty arises: we have an established principle that {\i kol masiach lefi tumo heimnuhu rabanan} \u8212- witnesses who speak in innocent, uncontrived speech are believed by the Rabbis for purposes of allowing a wife to remarry, and this applies even to non-Jewish witnesses. Why, then, would we not accept the announcement of a non-Jewish court that the man has been executed?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Resolution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The resolution suggested is that {\i met} does not mean the man has literally died, but rather {\i yotzei lamus} \u8212- he is being led out to be left to die. Similarly, {\i neherag} means {\i yotzei le\u8217'hareig} \u8212- he is being led out to be executed, not that the execution has already occurred. They are announcing their intention, not a completed fact. Under this reading, the {\i baraita} is consistent with Rav Yosef\u8217's original formulation: when a Jewish court announces that a person is being brought out for execution, we are confident the execution will be carried out; when a non-Jewish court makes the same announcement, we are not equally certain.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Alternative View\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 An alternative interpretation holds that {\i met} and {\i neherag} do mean literally dead and executed. Under this reading, the question becomes: why do we not accept the testimony of the non-Jewish court? The answer would have to be that the principle of {\i masiach lefi tumo} \u8212- believing witnesses who speak without a vested interest \u8212- does not apply here, because the non-Jewish court is not a disinterested party.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Vested Interest\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mesiach Lefi Tumo\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The principle of {\i masiach lefi tumo} \u8212- believing those who speak innocently and without a stake in the matter \u8212- applies precisely because the speaker has no vested interest in the outcome. The reason such testimony is trusted even for so weighty a matter as allowing a woman to remarry is that someone speaking casually, without knowing the import of their words, has no reason to fabricate. Once a party has a vested interest, that reliability evaporates.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Court\u8217's Vested Interest\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The non-Jewish court is in exactly such a position. They announced that they would execute a particular individual. If they did not actually carry it out, they have failed to deliver on their formal judgment \u8212- a significant loss of institutional credibility. To avoid this embarrassment, they will claim they executed the person whether or not they actually did so. They are therefore not a disinterested party, and the principle of {\i masiach lefi tumo} cannot apply to their announcement.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Jewish Court Finality\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Jewish court, by contrast, while technically capable of reopening a case even after {\i gmar din} \u8212- final sentencing \u8212- does so only in rare and exceptional circumstances in practice. Before {\i gmar din}, evidence and merit ({\i zchuta}) are actively considered; after the sentence is sealed, the case is effectively closed. It is this practical finality that allows us to rely on the testimony of witnesses who report that a Jewish court passed judgment, and that permits a wife to remarry on the basis of such testimony.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Gittin 29\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Shaliach Appoints Shalia\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Intro: Sick Shaliach\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i daf} opens with a {\i mishnah} addressing a scenario central to the laws of {\i gittin}: a {\i shaliach} who has been appointed to deliver a {\i get} in {\i Eretz Yisrael} becomes ill and cannot complete his {\i shlichus}. The {\i mishnah} rules that in such a case, {\i harei zeh m\u8217'shalcho b\u8217'yad acher} \u8212- he may appoint another person in his stead. What is notable at the outset is that the {\i mishnah} appears to require no {\i beit din} and apparently no witnesses for this reassignment, which raises an interesting broader question about how the {\i shaliach}\u8217's status is established and verified in the first place.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 There is a general question of {\i eidei kiyum hadavar} \u8212- whether witnesses must testify to the chain of agency in order for the delivery to be valid. If someone presents himself as an agent to deliver a {\i get}, how does one know that this is the right party, the actual {\i shaliach} of the husband? One answer may be that the very act of carrying the {\i get} itself provides sufficient evidence of the bearer\u8217's status. The {\i get} is not merely a document; it is, in a sense, the embodiment of the {\i shaliach}\u8217's authority. This would leave open a related question for the woman\u8217's side: if the wife appoints a {\i shaliach} to receive the {\i get}, there is no comparable physical object to attest to that person\u8217's standing. The degree to which testimony is required to establish a {\i shaliach}\u8217's identity, either on the delivering or receiving side, remains a question the {\i mishnah} does not fully resolve.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The second clause of the {\i mishnah} introduces a qualification. If the husband had instructed the {\i shaliach}, when you deliver the {\i get}, to also retrieve from the wife a particular object she has been holding \u8212- {\i ve\u8217'im amar lo tul li heimena cheifetz ploni} \u8212- then {\i lo yishlachenu b\u8217'yad acher}: he may not hand the {\i shlichus} over to someone else. The reason given is {\i she\u8217'ein retzono sheyehei pikdono b\u8217'yad acher} \u8212- it is not the husband\u8217's desire that his entrusted object be in the hands of another. Just because the husband trusted this particular {\i shaliach} to retrieve his prized possession does not mean he would be comfortable with a stranger doing so.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This immediately raises a structural question: are these two tasks \u8212- delivering the {\i get} and retrieving the object \u8212- one unified {\i shlichus} or two separate ones? The {\i mishnah} appears to treat them as intertwined: it says not to send the {\i get} at all under these conditions, rather than simply instructing the {\i shaliach} to deliver the {\i get} while forgoing the retrieval of the object. Whether the {\i get} itself would be valid after the fact, if the {\i shaliach} gave it without having retrieved the object, is something the {\i mishnah} leaves unresolved.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chala Tenan\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} opens with an observation by Rav Kahana: {\i chala tenan} \u8212- our {\i mishnah} specifically says the {\i shaliach} got sick. The {\i Gemara} immediately asks: {\i peshita} \u8212- that is obvious, it says it right there in the text. Why does Rav Kahana feel the need to underscore this? Because one might have thought that {\i chala} is merely a common illustrative scenario and that the {\i halakha} would apply even if the {\i shaliach} did not fall ill \u8212- that he could appoint a substitute for any reason whatsoever. Rav Kahana is teaching that the {\i mishnah} means what it says: illness is a genuine condition, not merely an example. Absent such a circumstance, the {\i shaliach} is not entitled to appoint someone in his stead.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Holech vs.\u160 ?At Holech\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} then anticipates a tension with an upcoming {\i braisa} and introduces the key distinction between {\i holech} and {\i at holech}. If the husband said simply {\i holech get zeh l\u8217'ishti} \u8212- deliver this {\i get} to my wife \u8212- the {\i tanna kamma} of the {\i braisa} rules that the {\i shaliach} may appoint a substitute even without having fallen ill.\u160 ?If, however, the husband said {\i at holech get zeh l\u8217'ishti} \u8212- you deliver this {\i get} to my wife \u8212- the {\i shaliach} may not appoint another even if he does fall ill, since the use of the word \u8220"you\u8221" implies a personal designation. {\i Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel} goes further, ruling that regardless of which formulation the husband used, {\i ein shaliach oseh shaliach} \u8212- a {\i shaliach} may never appoint a substitute. Neither the {\i tanna kamma} nor Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel invokes illness as a relevant condition, leaving the {\i mishnah} apparently in tension with the {\i braisa}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Three Reconciliations\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} proposes three ways to harmonize the {\i braisa} with the {\i mishnah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The first and most restrictive reading holds that both conditions must be met simultaneously: the husband must have said {\i holech} (not {\i at holech}), and the {\i shaliach} must have fallen ill.\u160 ?Only under both conditions may he appoint a substitute.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The second reading is more permissive: either condition is sufficient. Even if the husband said {\i at holech}, if the {\i shaliach} fell ill, that changes matters \u8212- {\i chala shani}, illness is different. Conversely, if the husband said {\i holech} without the restrictive formulation, the {\i shaliach} may appoint a substitute regardless of illness.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The third reading applies this same logic to the position of Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel. Even according to Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel, who rules that a {\i shaliach} may never appoint a substitute under ordinary circumstances, {\i chala shani} \u8212- illness creates an exception. His ruling stands only for the default case; when the {\i shaliach} is incapacitated, even Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel would permit reassignment.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Husband\u8217's Intent\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Underlying all three reconciliations is a single animating principle: the entire analysis is a question of the husband\u8217's intent and the scope of the authority he granted. Whether the governing factor is the specific word {\i at}, or the absence of illness, these are all tools for reading what the husband implicitly authorized when he empowered his {\i shaliach}. It is not a special dispensation triggered by illness as such \u8212- {\i chala} is not some independent {\i halacha l\u8217'Moshe m\u8217'Sinai}. Rather, when we say {\i chala shani}, we mean that in cases where it becomes genuinely impossible for the original {\i shaliach} to complete the task, we presume the husband \u8212- whose overriding interest is that the {\i get} be delivered \u8212- would have consented to a transfer of the agency. The same logic would presumably apply to any other {\i ones}, any other circumstance that genuinely prevents the {\i shaliach} from completing his mission. Whether this reasoning extends to a {\i shaliach} who simply changes his mind and refuses to complete the delivery, without any objective impediment, is a harder question whose answer is not clearly resolved.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Shaliach Oseh Shaliach D\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two vs.\u160 ?Three People\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now introduces a challenge to the entire idea that a {\i shaliach} may appoint another {\i shaliach}. A {\i mishnah} elsewhere states: if the husband said to two people {\i tenu get l\u8217'ishti} \u8212- give a {\i get} to my wife \u8212- or to three people {\i kitvu get utenu l\u8217'ishti} \u8212- write a {\i get} and give it to my wife \u8212- those individuals themselves must carry out the task. They may not delegate it. {\i Inhu in, aval shaliach lo} \u8212- they yes, but a {\i shaliach} no.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} explains the internal logic of this {\i mishnah}. When the husband addresses three people with the word {\i tenu} \u8212- \u8220"give\u8221" or \u8220"see that it is given\u8221" \u8212- those three constitute a {\i beit din}, and the word {\i tenu} implies supervision rather than personal execution. They may therefore appoint someone to carry out the writing. But if he says {\i kitvu utenu} to those three, he is explicitly directing them to write it themselves, and personal execution is required. When he addresses only two, even if he says merely {\i tenu}, those two are simply agents \u8212- {\i shluchim} \u8212- not a {\i beit din}, and an agent cannot delegate his agency. The principle is stark: {\i ein shaliach oseh shaliach}, a {\i shaliach} cannot make a {\i shaliach}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This apparently contradicts the {\i mishnah} that opened the {\i daf}, which permits the sick {\i shaliach} to appoint a replacement. The {\i Gemara} must explain the difference.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Beit Din Exception\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} draws a fundamental structural distinction. When the husband addresses a {\i beit din} and uses the language of {\i tenu}, he is not appointing them as personal agents; he is conferring upon them institutional authority to oversee the process. As a public institution, a {\i beit din} occupies a different role than an individual {\i shaliach}. They are not transmitting a personal agency they received; they are exercising a public institutional function that allows them to appoint an agent to carry out the delivery. By contrast, two individuals addressed as {\i shluchim}, even if told simply to \u8220"see that it is given,\u8221" remain personal agents and cannot pass on what they received.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This distinction also explains something that might otherwise seem arbitrary. The husband does not need to sign a document or perform a {\i kinyan} appointing the {\i beit din}; a verbal instruction is sufficient. While the ephemeral quality of purely verbal appointment will soon become a central issue, the point here is that the {\i beit din}\u8217's power derives not from a transferred personal agency but from its own institutional standing, once activated by the husband\u8217's directive.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Bizayon D\u8217'Baal\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye offers the first of two answers to explain why a private {\i shaliach} \u8212- as opposed to a {\i beit din} \u8212- cannot delegate the writing of a {\i get}. His reasoning: {\i mishum bizyona d\u8217'baal} \u8212- because of the embarrassment of the husband. In contrast, when it comes to the delivery of the {\i get} across a distance, {\i hacha ba\u8217'al lo kapid} \u8212- there the husband does not mind.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What specifically is embarrassing about the writing stage? Rashi explains that there was a social presumption that a husband would write his own {\i get}. This may seem surprising today, when the involvement of a specialist {\i sofer} is entirely standard, but the underlying concept is that the {\i get} is fundamentally an expression of the husband\u8217's own will, much as a standard deed of sale \u8212- {\i sadi mechura lach} \u8212- is an expression of the seller\u8217's intent. When the husband delegates the writing to someone else, there is something inherently private about that act, and the presumption is that he wants to keep it contained. Anything short of a formal {\i beit din}, explicitly told to oversee the matter, is assumed to reflect the husband\u8217's desire for discretion. By contrast, when the {\i get} is being transported across a distance, the very nature of the situation requires an intermediary; there is no presumption of discretion to protect, and no embarrassment in passing on the task.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is important to note the character of Abaye\u8217's answer: it is contingent and contextual, not intrinsic. There is nothing about the nature of {\i shlichus} itself that prevents delegation; it is a presumed preference of this particular husband under these particular circumstances.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mili Lo Mimsran\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava offers a more fundamental answer: {\i mishum de-mili ninhu} \u8212- because an oral appointment consists of nothing but words, and {\i mili lo mimsran l\u8217'shaliach} \u8212- words cannot be handed over to a {\i shaliach}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This gets to something structurally deeper. When the husband says \u8220"write a {\i get} for my wife,\u8221" there is nothing tangible that the appointed {\i shaliach} receives. No object changes hands, no document of agency is produced. The appointment is entirely abstract, existing only in the verbal instruction. Because there is nothing concrete to pass on, there is no mechanism by which the {\i shaliach} can transfer his status to another. He received a verbal charge directly from the husband, and that charge has no form that can be re-transmitted.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The case of delivering a {\i get} is different precisely because the {\i get} itself exists as a physical object. The {\i shaliach} carries the {\i get}, and the {\i get} embodies his authority. Whoever holds it is manifestly empowered to deliver it, and that tangible embodiment allows the agency to be transferred along with the object.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The implications of Rava\u8217's principle extend further than the {\i get} case. It would also explain why a woman\u8217's {\i shaliach} to receive a {\i get} cannot appoint a substitute \u8212- she gave him no object to carry forward. And it would mean that even if the husband explicitly declared he has no objection to the {\i shaliach} delegating his role in the writing of the {\i get}, this declaration would not help; the inability to delegate is not rooted in the husband\u8217's {\i hakpada} but in the structural impossibility of transmitting a purely verbal appointment. The {\i beit din} remains the sole exception, because the {\i beit din} is not receiving a transmitted agency at all \u8212- it is exercising its own institutional authority once the husband has activated it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Matanah K\u8217'Get\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} asks what the practical difference is between Abaye\u8217's and Rava\u8217's answers. The case that distinguishes them is a {\i shaliach} appointed to execute a gift \u8212- {\i l\u8217'shaliach matanah} \u8212- where the husband instructs one person to write a deed transferring a plot of land and give it to the recipient. There is no {\i bizayon} here; there is nothing private or embarrassing about making a gift. If Abaye\u8217's reasoning is correct, then in this case the {\i shaliach} could delegate the writing to someone else. But if Rava is correct, the structural impossibility of transmitting a verbal appointment applies here just as it does to the {\i get}, and the {\i shaliach} cannot delegate.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This becomes the debate of Rav and Shmuel. Rav holds that {\i matanah einah k\u8217'get} \u8212- a gift is unlike a {\i get}, and the {\i shaliach} for the writing of a gift deed may appoint another. Shmuel holds that {\i matanah harei hi k\u8217'get} \u8212- it is governed by the same principle, because the issue has nothing to do with embarrassment and everything to do with the inherent impossibility of transmitting a purely verbal appointment. In Shmuel\u8217's view, Rava\u8217's principle \u8212- {\i mili lo mimsran l\u8217'shaliach} \u8212- applies to any verbal agency that lacks a physical embodiment, whether the document in question is a {\i get} or a gift deed.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Object Condition\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Borrower Comparison\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} returns to the second clause of the {\i mishnah}: if the husband instructed the {\i shaliach} to retrieve a specific object from the wife, the {\i shaliach} may not appoint a substitute, because the husband does not want his {\i pikadon} in the hands of another person. Reish Lakish observes that the {\i mishnah} is teaching the same principle established elsewhere: {\i ein hasho\u8217'el rashai lehash\u8217'il ve\u8217'ein hasocher rashai lehaskir} \u8212- a borrower may not re-lend, and a renter may not sublet. Just as a person who entrusts an object to you does not thereby consent to having it in someone else\u8217's possession, so too the husband who entrusts the retrieval of his object to this {\i shaliach} does not consent to having another person handle it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chiddush Identified\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yochanan pushes back sharply: {\i zo afilu tinokos shel beis raban yode\u8217'in} \u8212- even young schoolchildren know that principle. It requires no {\i mishnah} to teach us that entrusting something to one person does not authorize that person to hand it to another. That is not the {\i chiddush} of the {\i mishnah} at all.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The true {\i chiddush}, says Rabbi Yochanan, is {\i zimnin de-gitta nami lo havei} \u8212- there are circumstances in which the {\i get} itself may be invalid as a result of this entanglement with the object. The {\i mishnah} is not merely saying that the {\i shaliach} violated a secondary duty by passing the object to someone else; in certain configurations, the very act of giving the {\i get} under the wrong conditions is an invalid giving. The {\i get} and the retrieval of the object can become so intertwined, depending on how the husband framed his instruction, that the {\i kashrut} of the {\i get} is conditional upon the object being handled correctly.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yochanan draws the analogy: it is as if the husband said \u8220"do not divorce her except in a house\u8221" and the {\i shaliach} divorced her upstairs, or \u8220"give it with your right hand\u8221" and he gave it with his left. When the husband frames an instruction as a condition on the manner of delivery, violating that condition invalidates the {\i get}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Woman Greeted\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} proceeds to delineate the cases. Where there is no dispute: if the woman came out to greet the {\i shaliach} and proactively handed him the object, and he then gave her the {\i get} \u8212- {\i kulei alma lo peligi de-gitta gitta ma\u8217'alya havei} \u8212- everyone agrees it is a valid {\i get}. The object was received before the {\i get} was given, the order was preserved, and there is no concern. This scenario is uncontroversial.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Order Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The dispute arises specifically when the husband framed the instruction in a particular order: {\i shekuli minach chefetz ve-hadar hav lah gitta} \u8212- first take the object from her, then give her the {\i get}. In this formulation, the husband is clearly treating the retrieval of the object as a precondition for the delivery of the {\i get}. Now if the {\i shaliach} reversed the order \u8212- {\i d\u8217'azil ihu ve-yahvei lah gita ve-hadar shakil minah chefetz} \u8212- he first gave her the {\i get}, forfeiting his leverage, and only then retrieved the object \u8212- this is where the debate between Rabbi Yochanan and Reish Lakish comes into focus.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Yochanan/Resh Laki\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Reish Lakish holds that the {\i get} and the retrieval of the object are always independent of one another. Even when the husband used the language of a specific order, Reish Lakish does not read this as creating a genuine condition on the {\i kashrut} of the {\i get}. Therefore, the {\i get} is valid whether delivered by the original {\i shaliach} himself in the wrong order, or by a substitute {\i shaliach} \u8212- {\i machshir b\u8217'shlucho ve-chol shekein bo}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yochanan holds that when the husband framed the instruction with a specific sequence \u8212- first the object, then the {\i get} \u8212- the {\i kashrut} of the {\i get} is dependent on the correct order. {\i Posel bo ve-chol shekein b\u8217'shlucho}: the {\i get} is invalid even if the original {\i shaliach} himself delivers it in the wrong order, and certainly if a substitute does it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The logic of Rabbi Yochanan\u8217's position deserves attention. Even if the {\i shaliach} first gave the {\i get} and then successfully retrieved the object two seconds later, this does not remedy the defect. At the moment the {\i get} was given, the {\i shaliach} was acting contrary to the husband\u8217's expressed intent \u8212- he was surrendering his leverage before securing the object. The invalidity attaches to the moment of giving, and what happens afterward cannot retroactively cure it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yochanan\u8217's reading of the {\i mishnah} encompasses two distinct ways in which the {\i get} can be invalidated when the husband expressed this conditional order. First: the original {\i shaliach} himself delivers the {\i get} before retrieving the object. Second: the original {\i shaliach} appoints a substitute, and even if that substitute performs the acts in the correct order, the {\i get} is still invalid \u8212- because the husband never consented to having a stranger handle his object at all.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 As one might observe, in practical life when two parties seek leverage over each other, the standard solution is a trusted neutral intermediary to whom both simultaneously deliver their respective obligations. Such a mechanism would in principle satisfy Rabbi Yochanan\u8217's framework, provided the husband had consented to the terms. But the asymmetry here is that the husband holds unilateral power over the {\i get}, making the dynamic structurally different from a bilateral exchange.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Get from Overseas\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Overseas Scenario\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} now turns to a more complex case. A {\i shaliach} bringing a {\i get} from overseas \u8212- {\i mimedinat hayam} \u8212- becomes ill and cannot complete the delivery. In {\i Eretz Yisrael}, where the {\i shaliach} is not required to declare {\i bifnei nichtav u-vifnei nechtam}, he may simply hand off the {\i get} to a substitute. But outside {\i Eretz Yisrael}, matters are more complicated: the original {\i shaliach} was the one who witnessed the writing and signing of the {\i get} and is therefore the one qualified to make the declaration. A substitute {\i shaliach} was not present at those events and cannot make that declaration in his own name.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Beit Din Testimony\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} provides the solution: the original {\i shaliach} must appear before a {\i beit din} and testify \u8212- {\i omer lifneihem bifnei nichtav u-vifnei nechtam} \u8212- he witnessed the writing and witnessed the signing. The {\i beit din} then appoints a new {\i shaliach} to carry the {\i get} forward. That second {\i shaliach} need not himself declare {\i bifnei nichtav u-vifnei nechtam}, because he was not a witness to those events; instead he declares {\i shaliach beit din ani} \u8212- I am an agent of the court. The court\u8217's appointment, grounded in the testimony of the original {\i shaliach}, supplies the necessary authentication.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This procedure also has an implicit bearing on the question of where and how {\i bifnei nichtav u-vifnei nechtam} must be recited \u8212- the {\i mishnah} presupposes that it is delivered before a {\i beit din}, a point that was subject to debate in the first {\i perek} of the tractate.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shaliach D\u8217'Shaliach\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The rabbis posed a question to Avimi, the son of Rabbi Abbahu, to bring to his father: can the second {\i shaliach} \u8212- the one appointed by the {\i beit din} \u8212- in turn appoint a third {\i shaliach}, and so on down the chain?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Multiple Shlichim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Abbahu dismissed the question as unnecessary. The answer is evident from the {\i mishnah} itself, which refers to {\i shaliach acharon} \u8212- the final {\i shaliach} \u8212- rather than simply the second {\i shaliach}. The use of {\i acharon} implies that there can be any number of {\i shluchim} in the chain. From the {\i michlal} \u8212- from the logical implication \u8212- that it says \u8220"the last {\i shaliach}\u8221" rather than \u8220"the second {\i shaliach},\u8221" it is clear that the delegation may continue indefinitely, {\i shaliach} appointing {\i shaliach} in succession.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Court Requirement\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Abbahu then identified what he considered the genuinely interesting question: when {\i shaliach} number two appoints {\i shaliach} number three, must that appointment also take place before a {\i beit din}, or may it be done informally? The second {\i shaliach} does not himself have a declaration of {\i bifnei nichtav u-vifnei nechtam} to offer \u8212- he can only say he was appointed by the {\i beit din}. The question is whether his ability to extend the chain depends on a court being involved at each successive stage, or whether having once established the chain through a {\i beit din}, subsequent transfers may proceed without one.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah}\u8217's language resolves this as well. Since every {\i shaliach} in the chain declares {\i shaliach beit din ani} \u8212- I am an agent of the court \u8212- this implies that a {\i beit din} must be involved at each stage of appointment. One might further suggest that the entire mechanism works precisely because at no stage is one personal {\i shaliach} transmitting his personal agency to another; rather, the court continuously reasserts its institutional appointment at each step, so that each {\i shaliach} is genuinely a direct appointee of the court.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Eretz Yisrael Chain\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava\u8217's Ruling\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava now addresses the parallel question for {\i Eretz Yisrael}. {\i Shliach b\u8217'Eretz Yisroel oseh kama shluchim} \u8212- a {\i shaliach} in {\i Eretz Yisrael} may make any number of {\i shluchim}, passing the {\i get} down an extended chain of agents.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 No Court Needed\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is not subject to the same requirements as the overseas case. Since there is no obligation to declare {\i bifnei nichtav u-vifnei nechtam} in {\i Eretz Yisrael}, the entire procedure is private rather than court-mediated. The first {\i shaliach} need not say {\i bifnei nichtav u-vifnei nechtam}, and the final {\i shaliach} need not say {\i shaliach beit din ani}. The chain is sustained without any {\i beit din} involvement at any stage. As Rashi spells out: none of those formal requirements apply in {\i Eretz Yisrael}, and yet the chain may extend as far as needed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Death of First\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Ashi raises a striking question about this chain. What if the first {\i shaliach} dies? Does the death of {\i shaliach} number one cause all the subsequent {\i shluchim} to become invalid \u8212- {\i im meis rishon batlu kulan}? This would be the case if each successive {\i shaliach} were understood as deriving his authority from the one who appointed him, making the chain\u8217's validity dependent on each link remaining intact.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Power from Husband\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Bar Rav Ashi swiftly and decisively refutes this position. {\i Ilu meis baal, midi mashasha is behu?} \u8212- if the husband died, would any of the {\i shluchim} retain validity? Of course not. The fact that a husband\u8217's death extinguishes all the {\i shluchim} simultaneously demonstrates that all of them derive their authority from the husband alone, not from any intermediate {\i shaliach}. {\i Kulam mi\u8217'koach d\u8217'baal ka\u8217'asu} \u8212- they all come from the power of the husband. {\i Isei l\u8217'baal isei l\u8217'kulehu} \u8212- as long as the husband lives, all the {\i shluchim} are valid. {\i Leisei l\u8217'baal leisei l\u8217'kulehu} \u8212- if the husband is gone, none of them have any standing regardless of how many intermediate {\i shluchim} remain alive.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This conclusion may seem intuitive, but it is worth noting that an analogous case \u8212- {\i shomer she\u8217'masar l\u8217'shomer}, where one guardian transfers an object to another guardian \u8212- is not quite so clear. There, the question is genuinely open as to whether the second {\i shomer} is watching the object on behalf of the first {\i shomer} or directly on behalf of the original owner. The {\i sugya} in Bava Metzia explores this in considerable depth, including the possibility that one could actually profit from the arrangement by converting a {\i shomer chinam} arrangement into a {\i shomer sachar} one at an intermediate step. But in the case of {\i gittin}, the logic is different: the act of divorcing one\u8217's wife is something that only the husband has standing to perform, and anyone empowered to carry out that act must necessarily be representing the husband directly. There is simply no other party they could be representing.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Abba Bar Minyumi Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Scenario Setup\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now presents a practical case that tests these principles. A man sent a {\i get} to his wife via a {\i shaliach}. The {\i shaliach} said: I do not know who she is \u8212- {\i lo yadana lah} \u8212- I cannot identify the wife. The husband responded: go give it to Abba bar Minyumi, {\i d\u8217'ihu yada lah} \u8212- he knows who my wife is \u8212- and he will then go and give it to her. This was presumably a case involving {\i chutz la\u8217'aretz}, where the formal procedures of {\i bifnei nichtav u-vifnei nechtam} and {\i beit din} appointment would be implicated.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbis\u8217' Solution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i shaliach} arrived at the destination but could not find Abba bar Minyumi. He did, however, encounter Rabbi Abbahu, Rabbi Chanina bar Pappa, and Rabbi Yitzchak Napcha \u8212- and Rav Safra was also present. The three rabbis proposed a solution: testify before us {\i bifnei nichtav u-vifnei nechtam}. Once you have done so, we as a {\i beit din} will appoint Abba bar Minyumi when he is located \u8212- {\i de-chi atei Abba bar Minyumi ve-nispineih lei ve-leizil ve-lisveih nihala} \u8212- and he will go and deliver the {\i get} to the wife. The solution maps cleanly onto the procedure described in the {\i mishnah}: the first {\i shaliach} testifies before the court, the court appoints the next agent, and the chain continues.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Safra\u8217's Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Safra, who had been sitting alongside, raised a pointed objection: {\i ve-ha shaliach she\u8217'lo nitan l\u8217'geirushin hu} \u8212- but this {\i shaliach} is one who was not empowered to deliver the {\i get} himself. He was only empowered to hand the {\i get} to Abba bar Minyumi. If the {\i shaliach} was never fully invested with the authority to deliver the {\i get} directly, how can he appear before a {\i beit din} and transfer to them a responsibility he himself did not have?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Full Empowerment\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Safra\u8217's challenge rests on a substantive principle: in order to confer upon a {\i beit din} the authority to oversee the delivery of a {\i get}, the transferring {\i shaliach} must himself have been fully empowered to deliver that {\i get}. It is a question of what he can give over \u8212- if you are only authorized to hand the {\i get} to a specific intermediary, you do not have the full role of a {\i shaliach l\u8217'gittin}, and therefore you cannot activate the {\i beit din}\u8217's institutional authority in your stead. The three senior rabbis were visibly embarrassed \u8212- {\i ikhsifu} \u8212- by this challenge.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Resolution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava then commented that Rav Safra had managed to embarrass three ordained {\i rabbanim} with his question. Rav Ashi, however, questioned whether the embarrassment was warranted \u8212- {\i bemai kaphinei} \u8212- what kind of a challenge was that really? {\i Mi amar lei Abba bar Minyomi velo at?} The husband never said \u8220"only Abba bar Minyumi and no one else.\u8221" The instruction to find Abba bar Minyumi was practical advice \u8212- Abba bar Minyumi knows who my wife is \u8212- not a restrictive condition excluding the original {\i shaliach} from delivering the {\i get} himself. If the {\i shaliach} had happened to encounter the wife and identified her himself, there would have been no objection to his delivering the {\i get} directly. He was therefore {\i nitan l\u8217'geirushin} \u8212- fully empowered to deliver the {\i get} \u8212- and Rav Safra\u8217's challenge collapses.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 There is an alternative reading in the {\i Rishonim} in which Rav Ashi reads the situation differently, concluding that the husband did indeed mean \u8220"Abba bar Minyumi and not you.\u8221" But the more logical reading of the circumstances, as presented, is that the husband\u8217's specification of Abba bar Minyumi was a means of solving the {\i shaliach}\u8217's practical problem \u8212- the {\i shaliach} did not know the wife \u8212- not an expression of distrust in the {\i shaliach} himself.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What makes this case enduringly interesting, however, is not merely its resolution but the principle it exposes. If a {\i shaliach} were truly appointed only to hand the {\i get} to a designated intermediary, and had no personal authority to deliver it himself, one would have to ask: in what sense is he a {\i shaliach} at all? His role would be indistinguishable from a messenger service. If all he is doing is conveying the {\i get} to the next person in line, one might argue that the husband has really appointed Abba bar Minyumi as the true {\i shaliach}, and the first person is merely a courier. The question then becomes whether one can appoint a {\i shaliach shelo b\u8217'fanav} \u8212- in the absence of the appointee \u8212- or whether that appointment requires a direct, face-to-face charge. If the courier is not actually a {\i shaliach}, what is the mechanism by which Abba bar Minyumi\u8217's appointment takes effect? These questions, which the {\i Gemara} raises implicitly in its closing discussion, remain open.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Gittin 30\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Shliach shelo nitan\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shliach empowerment\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The daf is 30. We pick up at the bottom of 29b, continuing the discussion of a {\i shliach} appointing another {\i shliach}. When the delivery of a {\i get} takes place within Eretz Yisrael but originates from {\i chutz l\u8217'aretz}, the {\i shliach} must make the transfer before a {\i beis din} by declaring {\i befanai nechtav}. The preceding sugya had introduced the concept of a {\i shliach shelo nitan l\u8217'geirushin} \u8212- a messenger who has not yet been activated to deliver the {\i get} himself, and who therefore lacks the full empowerment necessary to transfer that responsibility to another.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Thirty-day delay\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now turns to a new case: {\i hahu gavra dishadar leh gitta l\u8217'vei ittei} \u8212- a man sent a {\i get} to his wife through a {\i shliach}, instructing him, {\i lo titein lah ad t\u8217'latasin yomin} \u8212- do not give it to her until thirty days have passed. Within those thirty days, however, circumstances intervened and the {\i shliach} was compelled to leave before the thirty days elapsed. The {\i get} was not yet valid for delivery \u8212- not because it was literally postdated, but because the husband had placed a temporal hold on its delivery. Unable to wait, the {\i shliach} came before Rava.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava\u8217's ruling\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava reasoned as follows: the Mishnah had already established that a {\i shliach} may appoint a substitute when he falls ill \u8212- {\i chala ta\u8217'ama mai? mishum d\u8217'anus} \u8212- because illness is beyond his control, and we assume the husband would not object to the transfer of the {\i shliach}\u8217's responsibility under such compelled circumstances. So too in this case \u8212- {\i hai nami anus hu} \u8212- this is equally a situation of {\i ones}. Rava therefore instructed the {\i shliach}: {\i mesoru milach kamei didan} \u8212- declare your appointment before us. The {\i beis din} would then, after thirty days, appoint a new {\i shliach} to deliver the {\i get}. The case maps neatly onto the Mishnah: a transfer of agency, declared before a {\i beis din}, under circumstances of {\i ones} \u8212- entirely in keeping with established procedure.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Challenge to Rava\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Rabbis immediately challenged Rava: {\i ve\u8217'ha shliach shelo nitan l\u8217'geirushin hu} \u8212- is this not a {\i shliach} who has not yet been empowered to deliver the {\i get}? His {\i shlichus} is, as it were, on hold until the thirtieth day. He has not yet been activated. And if he is not yet empowered to deliver the {\i get} himself, how can he transfer that authority to the {\i beis din}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Empowerment debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava responded by drawing a distinction between fundamental empowerment and temporal activation. {\i Kevan d\u8217'levatar t\u8217'latasin yomin matzi m\u8217'garesh} \u8212- since at the conclusion of thirty days he will have full authority to deliver the {\i get} \u8212- {\i k\u8217'shliach she\u8217'nitan l\u8217'geirushin hu}: he is fundamentally a {\i shliach} who has been empowered to deliver the {\i get}, merely waiting for the designated moment. The fact that the moment has not yet arrived does not redefine him as someone who lacks empowerment altogether. His agency is real; it is only the timing that is constrained. He may therefore transfer that responsibility to the {\i beis din}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Piyes concern\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now raises a different concern: {\i ve\u8217'neichosh shema piyes} \u8212- should we not worry that in the interim thirty days, the husband may have reconciled with his wife? Why would a husband put a {\i get} on hold for thirty days in the first place? Possibly because he was considering whether to go through with the divorce. If they reconciled, and then the husband and wife were in {\i yichud} together \u8212- or worse, actually cohabited \u8212- the {\i get} becomes disqualified {\i m\u8217'divrei sofrim}, because the {\i get} bears a date prior to that encounter. People seeing the {\i get}\u8217's date will calculate that the child was born out of wedlock. This is the problem of a {\i get yashan}: the {\i get} is dated January first, delivered February first, and if they slept together on January fifteenth, observers will conclude from the {\i get} that she was divorced at the time of that encounter, creating a false impression about the child\u8217's status.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Concretely: the concern is not merely the general delay any {\i shliach} traveling from {\i chutz l\u8217'aretz} to {\i Eretz Yisrael} might experience \u8212- where the husband is distant and the delay is incidental \u8212- but rather a deliberate thirty-day hold explicitly imposed by the husband. The husband is not necessarily far away; the {\i shliach} was nearby, waiting. The deliberate withholding strongly suggests that the husband was allowing himself time to attempt reconciliation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 12-month condition\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara draws a parallel to a case taught elsewhere: {\i me\u8217'achshav im lo basi mikan v\u8217'ad yud beis chodesh} \u8212- if a husband says \u8220"this is your {\i get} effective now if I do not return within twelve months,\u8221" and he then died within that period, {\i harei zeh get} \u8212- it is a valid {\i get}, since he did not return. Yet the Gemara there already raised the same challenge: {\i ve\u8217'neichosh shema piyes} \u8212- perhaps before he died he secretly returned and reconciled? The Gemara answered that the resolution depends on a particular formulation: {\i be\u8217'omer ne\u8217'emenes alai lomar shelo basi} \u8212- the husband had declared that his wife is believed over a hundred witnesses to testify that he had not returned.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This principle of {\i ne\u8217'emenes alai} is a significant one. It has contemporary relevance in the context of halachic pre-nuptial agreements, where one of the provisions that can be included is a statement by the husband that his wife is believed even over his own counterclaims about what transpired or what conditions were set. It directly addresses the problem of disputed facts and counter-claims.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ne\u8217'emenes alai\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The upshot of the parallel is clear: absent a specific declaration of {\i ne\u8217'emenes alai}, we would indeed be concerned that the husband returned and reconciled. In the case of the thirty-day hold, no such declaration was made. How, then, could Rava have proceeded to allow the {\i get} to be delivered?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Arusa distinction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara resolves the difficulty by noting that {\i ichsof} \u8212- Rava was embarrassed by the question. And then it emerged: {\i u\u8217'lesof iglaei milsa de\u8217'arusa havaya} \u8212- it turned out that the woman in question was only an {\i arusa}, not a {\i nesuah}. Since they had never lived together as a married couple, there is considerably less reason to fear either that reconciliation is likely or that {\i yichud} and cohabitation would have occurred in the interim. Rava then stated: {\i im amru be\u8217'nesuah, yomru be\u8217'arusa?} \u8212- if they applied this concern about reconciliation in the case of a {\i nesuah}, would they apply it to an {\i arusa} as well? The concern does not extend to an {\i arusa} in the same way.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This passage raises a significant broader point: whenever a husband has had the opportunity to return and had explicitly put a {\i get} on hold, we face a potential concern \u8212- absent particular circumstances \u8212- that he may have returned and reconciled. This is not a trivial issue and applies across various scenarios.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shliach presence\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now raises a further procedural question: {\i ki mashvei bei dina shliach befanav v\u8217'shelo befanav} \u8212- when the {\i beis din} appoints a new {\i shliach} to replace the original one, must the first {\i shliach} be physically present at the time of that appointment? The Gemara then answers: {\i bein befanav bein shelo befanav} \u8212- whether in his presence or not. This ruling was also transmitted from Eretz Yisrael, confirming the same conclusion. The reasoning is clear: if the first {\i shliach} were himself a {\i shliach} of a {\i shliach}, one might think his presence necessary to formally demonstrate the transfer. But as established earlier, the {\i shliach} appointed by {\i beis din} is fundamentally the {\i shliach} of the husband \u8212- or at minimum the {\i shliach} of the {\i beis din} \u8212- and so there is no requirement for the first {\i shliach} to be present. The agency has been transferred to the {\i beis din}, and the {\i beis din} acts independently.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Ones be-gitin\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Return condition\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having mentioned the case of \u8220"if I do not return within twelve months,\u8221" the Gemara transitions into a self-contained sugya that also appears in Ketubot and elsewhere. The case: {\i hahu de\u8217'amar \u8212- ilu lo ateina taltin yomei, leihavei gita} \u8212- a man declared that his {\i get} should take effect if he does not return within thirty days. He then came back before the thirty days were up \u8212- {\i ata} \u8212- but {\i upaska mabara} \u8212- he found that the river crossing was blocked. The banks had risen and he could find no way to cross. As the deadline ran out, he called out from the other side: {\i chazu de\u8217'atai chazu de\u8217'atai} \u8212- \u8220"see, I\u8217'm here, I\u8217'm coming, I\u8217'm trying to return.\u8221"\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shmuel\u8217's ruling\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Shmuel ruled: {\i lo shmei matya} \u8212- his calling out and trying to return does not count as having returned. The condition was formulated as \u8220"if I don\u8217't return,\u8221" and he did not return. The {\i get} is therefore valid. His intention to return, his effort, his crying out \u8212- none of it satisfies the literal language of the condition.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ones doctrine\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This ruling opens the sugya of {\i yesh ones be-gitin o ein ones be-gitin} \u8212- whether the doctrine of {\i ones} applies in the context of conditional divorces. The argument in favor of {\i ones} applying would run as follows: when a person frames a condition, he presumably means it to cover the range of situations he could foresee. If his intent was \u8220"if I willingly choose not to return, the {\i get} takes effect,\u8221" then a case where he tried desperately to return but was physically prevented was never within the scope of what he meant. We ought to interpret his words in light of his evident intent.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Against this stands Shmuel\u8217's position: halacha must go by what a person says, not by the more nuanced intention one might retroactively attribute to him. If he wanted to exclude cases of {\i ones}, he should have said so explicitly. The condition as stated is unambiguous \u8212- he did not return.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Reconciliation debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A second case sharpens the debate. {\i Hahu gavra d\u8217'amar lehu \u8212- ee lo paisna lah tlasi yomin, leihavei gita} \u8212- a man told witnesses that if he does not reconcile with his wife within thirty days, the {\i get} should take effect. He then went to try to reconcile \u8212- {\i aza pisa velo ipisa} \u8212- but she refused to be reconciled.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Yosef challenges the claim that this is a genuine {\i ones}: {\i mi yahiv lah tikra tarkava de-dinari velo paisa?} \u8212- did he send her a basket full of gold coins and she still refused? Perhaps he simply did not try hard enough. However, some report Rav Yosef\u8217's position in the opposite direction: {\i midi tarkava de-dinari baia u-maihava lah?} \u8212- does he have to give her a basket of gold coins? That would be excessive. He tried, and that should be sufficient.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot notes that even in the version where Rav Yosef questions whether he tried hard enough, the underlying point should really be: even if he had given her a basket of gold coins and she still refused, the condition still was not met. The issue is not the quality of the effort, but whether trying is sufficient at all.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ones conclusion\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara concludes by mapping the two cases onto the two positions: {\i kad keman de\u8217'amar yesh ones be-gitin, ha keman de\u8217'amar ein ones be-gitin} \u8212- one case follows the view that {\i ones} is a valid consideration in {\i gitin}, the other follows the view that it is not. The question at stake is whether, when a person formulates a condition, we interpret that condition as implicitly excluding cases where he tried and was prevented \u8212- or whether we hold strictly to the letter of what was said. The sugya in Ketubot develops this at greater length; here we note the two positions and the cases that illustrate them.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Teruma/Ma\u8217'aser loan\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Loan setup\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now turns to a Mishnah that, at first glance, has nothing to do with {\i gitin}. The connection lies in the theme of acting with a {\i chazakah} \u8212- a presumption \u8212- that someone is still alive, paralleling the earlier discussion of a {\i shliach} who delivers a {\i get} presuming that the husband remains living. This Mishnah introduces a range of similar scenarios and also serves as a bridge toward the next {\i perek}, which opens with a {\i get} case and then introduces the concept of {\i tikkun olam}, leading into two full chapters of material largely unrelated to {\i gitin} but connected through that broader idea.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Mishnah: {\i hamalveh ma\u8217'os et ha-kohen ve\u8217'et ha-levi ve\u8217'et he-ani} \u8212- one who lends money to a {\i kohen}, a {\i levi}, or a poor person, with the following arrangement: the lender is a farmer who, after his crops come in, will separate {\i terumah} or {\i ma\u8217'aser} and designate it toward repayment of the loan, rather than physically delivering it to the {\i kohen} or {\i levi} and having it returned. Instead, he will simply separate it and retain it himself \u8212- since the debt is thereby offset, and there is no need to go through the formality of delivery and return.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i halacha}: {\i mafrish aleihem b\u8217'chezkat she-hen kayamim} \u8212- he may go ahead and separate and retain it, acting on the presumption that the {\i kohen}, {\i levi}, or {\i ani} is still alive. He is not required to worry \u8212- {\i ve\u8217'eino choshesh shema meis ha-kohen o ha-levi o ha-ani o she\u8217'he\u8217'eshir he\u8217'ani} \u8212- that perhaps the {\i kohen} died, or the {\i levi} died, or the poor man died, or that the poor man became wealthy. If the {\i ani} became wealthy, he would no longer be eligible to receive {\i ma\u8217'aser ani}, and the arrangement would be undermined. Nevertheless, the lender may proceed on the presumption that the original circumstances still obtain.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Separation mechanism\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The mechanism works as follows. Rather than physically handing the {\i terumah} or {\i ma\u8217'aser} to the {\i kohen} or {\i levi} and having it handed back as repayment, the lender simply declares the separation mentally and retains the produce. In the case of {\i ma\u8217'aser ani}, the lender can himself eat {\i ma\u8217'aser} \u8212- it is not restricted to {\i kohanim} \u8212- and so the same logic applies. The arrangement was made in advance: when I separate, I keep it, and it counts as repayment of the debt.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Presumption aliyah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Mishnah permits this to proceed even when the {\i kohen}, {\i levi}, or {\i ani} is not present \u8212- operating on the {\i chazakah} that he is still alive, precisely as a {\i shliach} delivering a {\i get} presumes the husband still lives.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Death of creditor\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If, however, it becomes known that the {\i kohen}, {\i levi}, or {\i ani} has actually died, the situation becomes more complex. The question is whether the heirs assume the obligation. In general, heirs do not inherit the personal debts of their father as a personal obligation; rather, there is a {\i mitzvah al ha-yorshim liproa chov avihem} \u8212- a rabbinic obligation for heirs to repay their father\u8217's debts \u8212- but not a legally enforceable personal liability. If, however, there is a lien on inherited property \u8212- established through a {\i shtar chov} \u8212- then the lender retains rights against that property.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 In the present case, since the arrangement was likely made orally rather than through a formal {\i shtar}, there probably would be no lien on the property. Nevertheless, the Mishnah provides a solution: if the heirs \u8212- who are themselves {\i kohanim} or {\i leviyim} \u8212- agree to honor their father\u8217's arrangement, the lender may retain the {\i terumah} or {\i ma\u8217'aser} as repayment. The mechanism is conceptually as though the lender gives the {\i terumah} to the heirs, and they return it as repayment. This requires the heirs\u8217' agreement \u8212- they are not legally obligated \u8212- but if they consent, the arrangement transfers smoothly. In the case of an {\i ani} who died, the same could apply in principle, though complications arise if the heirs are not themselves poor.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If the original loan was made {\i bifnei beis din} \u8212- in the presence of a {\i beis din} \u8212- the Mishnah rules {\i eino tzorich litol reshus min ha-yorshim}: the lender does not need the heirs\u8217' permission. The {\i beis din}, having official standing in matters of {\i dinei mammonos}, can in effect sanction the arrangement and speak on behalf of the heirs \u8212- analogous, in a certain way, to the institution of {\i prozbul}, which we will encounter later in Gittin.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Possession problem\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara raises a sharp challenge: {\i ve\u8217'af al gav de\u8217'lo asu le\u8217'yadei} \u8212- even though the {\i terumah} never came into the {\i kohen}\u8217's possession \u8212- how does this arrangement work at all? There are two distinct problems. First, a straightforward {\i dinei mammonos} question: this {\i terumah} right now could be given to any {\i kohen}. How does this particular {\i kohen} acquire ownership over it such that the lender can then retain it as repayment of the debt? Second, there is a separate {\i mitzvah} of {\i nesina} \u8212- delivery \u8212- which requires actually handing the {\i terumah} to the {\i kohen}. When and how was that {\i mitzvah} ever fulfilled?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rashi articulates both points explicitly: {\i maan zachye lei le-hai kohen} \u8212- who conferred ownership on this particular {\i kohen}? And {\i heicha yatza yedei nesina} \u8212- where did the lender ever fulfill the obligation of giving?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Established families\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav answers: {\i be-makirei kehuna u-leviya} \u8212- this applies to established families, where there is a longstanding and well-known practice of giving {\i terumah} and {\i ma\u8217'aser} to a specific household. In such a case, the very act of separating, given the firmly established pattern, is sufficient to confer ownership on that family automatically, even without a formal act of delivery. This was a known institution \u8212- families who had, across generations, given consistently to a particular {\i kohen} family or {\i levi} family. In that context, separation alone suffices.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Third party\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Shmuel offers a different explanation: {\i be-mezakeh lahem al yedei acheirim} \u8212- the lender calls over a third party, asks him to take possession of the {\i terumah} on behalf of the {\i kohen} or {\i levi}, and then the third party transfers it back to the lender as repayment. This fulfills both the ownership transfer and the act of giving, through the mechanism of {\i zechiyah} \u8212- acquiring on behalf of another. Shmuel\u8217's arrangement is simply more practical in cases where the {\i kohen} or {\i levi} is not nearby.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Asu institution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Ulla offers a third explanation: {\i ha mani Rabbi Yossi hi \u8212- de\u8217'amar asu et she\u8217'eino zacheh ke\u8217'zacheh} \u8212- this Mishnah follows the view of Rabbi Yossi, who held that the Rabbis enacted that a person who has not formally acquired something is treated as if he has, in order to allow this institution to function. Ulla\u8217's explanation is frankly rabbinic: the Rabbis recognized that technically the {\i kohen} or {\i levi} does not own the {\i terumah} or {\i ma\u8217'aser}, but they enacted that it should be treated as though they did, in order to make this system viable.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The institution is in everyone\u8217's interest. The {\i kohen}, {\i levi}, or {\i ani} benefits enormously: they can borrow money and have it repaid with something they would likely have received anyway \u8212- or perhaps even something they would not have received had the lender not agreed to this arrangement. It is, in effect, free money. The lender benefits as well: he has confidence that his loan will be repaid, and the terms \u8212- as we will see \u8212- are quite favorable to him. The Rabbis therefore greased the wheels, as it were, and declared that the mechanics of {\i kinyan} are satisfied even without a formal act of delivery.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara notes that none of the three explanations fully satisfies the commentators\u8217' preference. {\i Ke-Rav lo amri} \u8212- Rav\u8217's explanation of {\i makirei kehuna} is not mentioned in the Mishnah. {\i Ke-Shmuel lo amri} \u8212- the third-party mechanism is also not mentioned. {\i Ke-Ulla nami lo amri} \u8212- why not like Ulla? Because {\i bi\u8217'yechida lo mokminan} \u8212- we prefer not to establish a Mishnah as following a single minority opinion. Nevertheless, one way or another, the institution of ownership transfer must be operative; the debate concerns only the precise mechanism.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i baraita} then repeats essentially the same case, adding the detail of {\i posekin imahen ke-sha\u8217'ar ha-zol} \u8212- the exchange rate to be used when calculating repayment.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Exchange rate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i baraita} states: {\i ufosek imahem ke-sha\u8217'ar ha-zol} \u8212- one stipulates with them according to the lowest market rate. That is, when calculating how much produce the lender retains as repayment, the parties agree that the produce is valued at the lowest prevailing market price. If wheat, for instance, fluctuates between one and two dollars per bushel over the course of the season, the agreement stipulates the one-dollar rate. This maximizes the quantity the lender can retain \u8212- a hundred bushels repays a hundred dollars at one dollar per bushel, which at the higher market price might actually be worth two hundred dollars.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara initially observes {\i peshita} \u8212- this is obvious, since there is already a Mishnah in Bava Metzia permitting such arrangements. The underlying logic treats this less as a loan and more as a pre-purchase or forward transaction: the lender is in some sense pre-acquiring the {\i terumah} or {\i ma\u8217'aser} that will later be separated. The Gemara clarifies that the {\i chidush} is the implicit presumption: even if the parties did not explicitly fix the exchange rate, we assume the lowest market rate applies, since that is what benefits the lender and incentivizes the arrangement.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ribbis exemption\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i baraita} states: {\i ve-ein bo mishum ribbit} \u8212- there is no concern of {\i ribbit} in this arrangement. Even if the lender ends up retaining produce worth considerably more than the original loan \u8212- because the market price rose while the exchange rate was fixed at the low end \u8212- this does not constitute forbidden interest.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot extends this further: not only does the {\i sha\u8217'ar ha-zol} rule not raise a {\i ribbit} issue, but even if the parties agree to an exchange rate lower than anything the market actually reached \u8212- a rate more favorable to the lender than any prevailing price \u8212- there is still no {\i ribbit} violation. The Rabbis permitted this generously in order to make the institution as attractive as possible to both parties.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara explains the exemption: {\i keivan de-ki leis lei lo yahiv lei} \u8212- since if the crop fails entirely and there is nothing to separate, the borrower has no obligation to repay the loan at all. This fundamentally distinguishes the arrangement from a standard loan. In an ordinary loan, if the borrower cannot pay, the debt still exists. Here, if there is no crop, there is no debt. That feature makes the arrangement structurally closer to a forward sale than to a loan, and it is precisely that feature which removes the {\i ribbit} concern. {\i Ki it lei nami, ein bo mishum ribbit} \u8212- when the crop does come in and the lender retains more than he lent, we analyze it as the profit of a sale, not as interest on a loan.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shemita exception\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i baraita} further states: {\i ve-ein shevi\u8217'it meshamtattu} \u8212- the {\i shemita} year does not annul this debt. Normally, {\i shemita} annuls loans by prohibiting the creditor from pressing the borrower for repayment, which ultimately renders the debt uncollectable. Here, however, the borrower is essentially out of the picture \u8212- there is no creditor pressing anyone. The lender will simply separate {\i terumah} and retain it himself. Since the mechanism of {\i shemita}\u8217's annulment \u8212- {\i lo yigos} \u8212- requires active pressure on a debtor, and no such pressure exists in this arrangement, {\i shemita} does not apply.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Retraction rules\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i baraita} then states: {\i ve-im ba lachazor eino chozer} \u8212- if one wants to retract, he cannot. The Gemara, following Rav Pappa, clarifies which party is being addressed. The {\i ba\u8217'al ha-bayis} \u8212- the lender \u8212- cannot retract from the arrangement. But {\i aval kohen be-ba\u8217'al ha-bayis, im ba lachazor chozer} \u8212- the {\i kohen} can retract.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The reasoning draws on the principle that in a normal transaction, if the buyer has paid but has not yet taken possession of the object, he may retract \u8212- the transaction is not complete. The Gemara applies a parallel logic here. From the {\i ba\u8217'al ha-bayis}\u8217's perspective, his side of the transaction is complete: he lent the money, and there is nothing further for him to do. He cannot claim that his act is unfinished and seek to reverse it. From the {\i kohen}\u8217's perspective, however, his side of the transaction \u8212- the act of receiving the {\i terumah} and having it returned as repayment \u8212- is not yet complete. His portion of the transaction remains open, and that incompleteness gives him a degree of flexibility to withdraw.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rashi formulates it this way: the {\i ba\u8217'al ha-bayis} has discharged his obligation on his end; the {\i kohen} has not yet fulfilled his end, and therefore retains the ability to reverse course. A student raised a helpful way of putting it: the lender has lent the money; the {\i kohen} effectively holds an option of repaying through this mechanism, but until the separation and retention actually occur, that side of the transaction is not complete, and the option remains his to exercise or not.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If the {\i kohen} does retract, the lender is entitled to a {\i mi she-para} \u8212- a rabbinic censure \u8212- but no more.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ye\u8217'ush concept\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i baraita} also states: {\i nityashu ha-be\u8217'alim, ein mafrishin aleihem} \u8212- if the lender has despaired of collecting, he may no longer use the {\i terumah} or {\i ma\u8217'aser} toward repayment of the debt. {\i Mipnei she-hu mafrish al ha-avud} \u8212- because he would be separating in relation to something already lost.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is a surprising ruling. Normally, {\i ye\u8217'ush} \u8212- despair \u8212- does not in itself extinguish a debt. If a borrower absconds, the lender\u8217's despair does not cancel what is owed. What makes this case different is the concrete, specific nature of the anticipated payment. The repayment here was not a general monetary obligation but was tied to a particular object \u8212- the {\i terumah} and {\i ma\u8217'aser} from this lender\u8217's crop. When the lender despairs and considers that produce lost, he has effectively relinquished his claim on that specific object as a vehicle of repayment. It is a {\i ye\u8217'ush} directed at the thing itself, not merely at the hope of collection. Once that despair is registered, even if the crop recovers, the lender can no longer use it as repayment.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara further clarifies that this ruling is needed even in a case where the grain was already standing in the straw when it was damaged \u8212- one might have thought that such grain, which often recovers, was never truly lost, and that the lender\u8217's despair was therefore premature and ineffective. The Gemara teaches that even so, if the lender genuinely despaired, that {\i ye\u8217'ush} takes effect and forfeits his right to use that produce as repayment.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Beis din takana\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i baraita} then cites Rabbi Eliezer ben Yaakov: {\i hamalveh ma\u8217'os le-kohen u\u8217'le-levi bifnei beis din u\u8217'meisu, mafrish aleihem b\u8217'chezkas oso ha-shevet} \u8212- if the loan was arranged before a {\i beis din} and the {\i kohen} or {\i levi} subsequently died, the lender may still separate and retain the {\i terumah} or {\i ma\u8217'aser} by virtue of the status of that entire tribe. He does not need the heirs\u8217' permission.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The logic here is institutional. When the original arrangement was made formally and publicly before a {\i beis din}, the {\i beis din} implicitly speaks on behalf of the {\i kohanim} and {\i leviyim} as a class. The broader community of {\i kohanim} and {\i leviyim} has an interest in this institution functioning \u8212- after all, the ability to borrow money and have it repaid through {\i terumah} and {\i ma\u8217'aser} is a tremendous benefit for them. The {\i beis din}, acting in their collective interest, can therefore authorize the lender to retain the {\i terumah} even when the original {\i kohen} has died, without needing the specific heirs to consent.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A version in the Tosefta reads not {\i b\u8217'chezkat oso ha-shevet} but {\i bi-reshus oso ha-shevet} \u8212- by the permission of that tribe \u8212- capturing more explicitly the idea that the {\i beis din} speaks with the implicit authorization of the {\i kohanim} or {\i leviyim} as a group.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 For an {\i ani}: {\i mafrish aleihem b\u8217'chezkat aniyei Yisrael} \u8212- one may presume on behalf of the poor of Israel generally. Rabbi Akiva extends this further: {\i b\u8217'chezkat aniyei olam} \u8212- on behalf of the poor of the world. The practical difference, as the Gemara notes, concerns {\i aniyei Kuti} \u8212- Samaritan poor in the town. If one holds like {\i aniyei Yisrael}, one speaks on behalf of Jewish poor specifically; if there are no Jewish poor in the town and only Samaritan poor, it may not work in certain configurations. Rabbi Akiva\u8217's formulation, reaching to {\i aniyei olam}, ensures that Jewish poor elsewhere can also serve as the relevant class.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Death vs.\u160 ?wealth\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i baraita} adds: {\i he\u8217'eshir he\u8217'ani, ein mafrish alav} \u8212- if the {\i ani} became wealthy, the arrangement no longer applies, even when the loan was made before a {\i beis din}. The {\i beis din} speaks on behalf of the poor as a class in order to sustain this institution \u8212- but only in the case of death. If the {\i ani} merely got rich, the lender cannot retain the {\i ma\u8217'aser ani} and consider the debt repaid.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Why does the rabbinic enactment cover death but not wealth? The Gemara answers: {\i misa shchicha, ashirus lo shchicha} \u8212- death is common; getting rich is not. The Rabbis constructed the institution to address predictable and frequent occurrences. Death is universal and inevitable; the Rabbis therefore made provision for it, allowing the institution to continue functioning when the {\i kohen}, {\i levi}, or {\i ani} dies. But enrichment of a poor man is unusual enough that the Rabbis did not extend the {\i takkanah} to cover it. Rav Pappa encapsulates this with a folk saying: if you hear that your friend died, you can take it as true \u8212- as the {\i chazakah} holds, people do die. But if you hear that your friend got rich, do not be so quick to believe it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Heirs and land\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Mishnah had stated that if the {\i kohen} or {\i levi} dies, the lender must obtain permission from the heirs \u8212- {\i tzorich litol reshus min ha-yorshim}. A {\i baraita} citing Rebbi then stipulates: this applies to heirs who inherited \u8212- {\i yorshim she-yarshu}. The Gemara asks: are there heirs who did not inherit? Rabbi Yochanan clarifies: {\i yorshim she-yarshu karka v\u8217'lo she-yarshu kesafim} \u8212- heirs who inherited land, not merely cash. Only when there is actual landed property is there a lien upon which a debt can be collected; and only then is the permission of the heirs relevant in this framework.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yochanan then rules: {\i hiniah m\u8217'lo machat gove m\u8217'lo machat, m\u8217'lo kardom gove m\u8217'lo kardom} \u8212- if the deceased left an estate worth only a pin, the lender may collect only a pin\u8217's worth; if worth a hammer, he collects a hammer\u8217's worth. One can collect only proportionally to the actual land.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 However, a further ruling \u8212- associated with the story of the small fields of Abaye in Ketubot \u8212- holds that even if the estate is tiny, one can collect the full debt through repeated cycles: take the land, accept the heirs\u8217' cash payment to redeem the land, apply that cash as partial payment of the debt, take the land again, and so on. Since this entire framework is a {\i takkanah d\u8217'rabbanan} aimed at making the institution work, Tosafot reads this position as applying even where the heirs have not formally consented \u8212- where there is a lien, the {\i beis din}\u8217's institution ensures the debt can ultimately be collected even from a small estate.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Gittin 31\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Lending to Kohen/Levi\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Unspecified Ma\u8217'aser\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Today\u8217's daf begins at {\i Gittin} 31b, picking up in the middle of the discussion concerning the institution of lending money to a {\i kohen}, {\i levi}, or {\i ani}, whereby the borrower repays through {\i terumot} and {\i ma\u8217'asrot}. Within that context, the {\i gemara} introduces a related {\i baraita}: if a {\i Yisrael} says to a {\i levi}, \u8220"{\i ma\u8217'aser yesh lecha b\u8217'yadi}\u8221" \u8212- \u8220"you have {\i ma\u8217'aser} in my possession\u8221" \u8212- {\i ein chosheshin l\u8217'trumat ma\u8217'aser shebo}, we need not be concerned that there is {\i trumat ma\u8217'aser} within that {\i ma\u8217'aser}. The {\i halakha} is that there is no such concern. By contrast, if he specified a measure \u8212- \u8220"{\i kor ma\u8217'aser yesh lecha b\u8217'yadi},\u8221" you have a {\i kor} (thirty {\i se\u8217'ah}) of {\i ma\u8217'aser} in my possession \u8212- then {\i chosheshin l\u8217'trumat ma\u8217'aser shebo}, we are concerned about the {\i trumat ma\u8217'aser}. The {\i gemara} immediately protests: {\i mai ka\u8217'amar}? What is this even saying? The {\i baraita} seems impenetrable as stated.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Abaye\u8217's Reading\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye steps in with a clarification: {\i hachi ka\u8217'amar} \u8212- here is what the {\i baraita} actually means. A {\i Yisrael} says to a {\i levi}: \u8220"{\i ma\u8217'aser yesh lecha b\u8217'yadi v\u8217'heilach damav}\u8221" \u8212- \u8220"I have separated {\i ma\u8217'aser} in my home, I have you in mind, it is yours; now here is money, let me buy it back from you.\u8221" This fits squarely within the context of the {\i mishnah}, where a {\i Yisrael} separates {\i ma\u8217'aser} and then repurchases it from the {\i levi} in order to keep the grain in his own possession.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafot on Timing\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Given this reading, {\i Tosafot} explains the concern as follows. The {\i levi} has agreed in principle to sell the {\i ma\u8217'aser} back, but the sale does not become binding until he takes the money \u8212- and in this case, since the grain is already physically in the {\i Yisrael}\u8217's house, taking possession is effectively accomplished the moment he receives the money. Before the money changes hands, however, the {\i levi} still owns the {\i ma\u8217'aser}. {\i Tosafot} therefore asks: are we concerned that in that window of time, before the sale is binding, the {\i levi} might have used the separated {\i ma\u8217'aser} to perform {\i havdalah} \u8212- declaring it to be {\i trumat ma\u8217'aser} on behalf of {\i other ma\u8217'aser} he has at home? We are not concerned, says the {\i baraita} in the unspecified case.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Switcheroo Concern\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The concern that {\i Tosafot} is addressing is the following. Suppose the {\i levi} has a thousand bushels of {\i ma\u8217'aser} at home, and the {\i Yisrael} has just given him one hundred bushels of {\i ma\u8217'aser} (in some halakhically recognized manner, perhaps via a third-party {\i kinyan} on his behalf). The {\i levi} could say: \u8220"I will now designate these hundred bushels as {\i trumat ma\u8217'aser} on my other nine hundred bushels.\u8221" He then takes the money from the sale \u8212- and the {\i Yisrael} discovers that what he bought back is now entirely {\i terumah}: ninety percent cannot be eaten by a non-{\i kohen} at all. In the unspecified case, we are not concerned that this occurred, because the {\i levi} did not know the precise amount of {\i ma\u8217'aser} involved, and one cannot properly designate {\i trumat ma\u8217'aser} without knowing the quantity. If, however, the {\i Yisrael} stated an exact measure \u8212- {\i kor ma\u8217'aser} \u8212- and that is also how the {\i levi} came to know the precise amount, then indeed we must be concerned: he knew exactly how much he had, and could have performed this \u8220"switcheroo,\u8221" converting the full amount to {\i trumat ma\u8217'aser al makom acher}, on other {\i ma\u8217'aser} elsewhere.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Wickedness Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 At this point the {\i gemara} objects sharply: {\i atu b\u8217'rishi\u8217'ei askinan}? Are we dealing with wicked people? {\i Deshaklu d\u8217'mei u\u8217'machlei lehu trumat ma\u8217'aser}? That they take money for the grain and yet turn it all into {\i trumat ma\u8217'aser} behind the buyer\u8217's back? If the {\i levi} did such a thing, the entire sale should be a {\i mekach ta\u8217'ut} \u8212- a transaction made in error \u8212- and void entirely. The {\i gemara} concludes that this reading, while it fits the language of the {\i baraita} elegantly, cannot be its true {\i pshat}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Deceased Levi Scenarios\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Mesharshiya\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} therefore proposes an entirely different scenario, attributed to Rav Mesharshiya son of Rav Idi. The {\i baraita} now reads as follows: a {\i Yisrael} says to the son of a {\i levi}: \u8220"{\i ma\u8217'aser l\u8217'avicha b\u8217'yadi}\u8221" \u8212- \u8220"your father entrusted {\i ma\u8217'aser} to me before he passed away.\u8221" The scenario has shifted: we are no longer dealing with a {\i levi} who might act improperly in the present, but with {\i ma\u8217'aser} that a now-deceased {\i levi} had left in someone else\u8217's possession. The {\i Yisrael} now wishes to purchase this {\i ma\u8217'aser} from the son. The question becomes: must we be concerned about what the {\i father} did to this {\i ma\u8217'aser} before he died?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Trumat Ma\u8217'aser Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The concern is whether the father, before his death, might have designated the entire sum of {\i ma\u8217'aser} as {\i trumat ma\u8217'aser al makom acher} \u8212- as {\i trumat ma\u8217'aser} on other {\i ma\u8217'aser} he possessed elsewhere. The {\i Yisrael} who wishes to buy the grain needs to know whether it is clean {\i ma\u8217'aser} (with only a tenth yet to be given to the {\i kohen}) or whether the whole amount has been converted to {\i trumat ma\u8217'aser}, leaving it entirely {\i terumah}. The {\i baraita} rules: if the amount was unspecified \u8212- \u8220"{\i ma\u8217'aser l\u8217'avicha b\u8217'yadi}\u8221" with no quantity stated \u8212- {\i ein chosheshin}, we need not be concerned. If a specific amount was named \u8212- \u8220"{\i kor ma\u8217'aser l\u8217'avicha b\u8217'yadi}\u8221" \u8212- then {\i chosheshin}, we are concerned.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 There are in principle three possible states in which the father could have left the {\i ma\u8217'aser}: first, he did nothing at all, and it remains raw {\i ma\u8217'aser} awaiting {\i trumat ma\u8217'aser}; second, he removed the {\i trumat ma\u8217'aser} from within it, though we do not know where it ended up; and third, he converted the entire amount to {\i trumat ma\u8217'aser} on other grain elsewhere. The {\i gemara} treats the third scenario as the most extreme and the one animating the concern.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Non-Standard Practice\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then raises a pointed objection: {\i v\u8217'chi nechsh\u8217'du chaveirim litrom shelo min hamukaf}? Are we really to suspect scrupulous people \u8212- {\i chaveirim} \u8212- of separating {\i trumat ma\u8217'aser} in a manner that is not {\i min hamukaf}, not from grain that is all physically together in one place? The requirement that the grain being separated and the grain it serves as {\i terumah} for should be in the same physical space ({\i mukaf}) is the standard, normative procedure. Suspecting this {\i levi}, who was presumably a {\i chaver} scrupulous in his observance of {\i terumot u\u8217'ma\u8217'asrot}, of routinely designating grain in one location as {\i trumat ma\u8217'aser} for grain in an entirely different location seems unwarranted. The {\i gemara} accordingly dismisses this reading as well.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Ashi\u8217's Reading\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Ashi proposes a third interpretation. The {\i baraita} describes yet another scenario: a {\i ben Yisrael} reports to a {\i levi}: \u8220"{\i kach amar li Abba} \u8212- this is what my father told me before he died \u8212- \u8216'{\i ma\u8217'aser l\u8217'avicha b\u8217'yadi},\u8217' that he had {\i ma\u8217'aser} in his possession belonging to your father.\u8221" So here it is not the {\i Yisrael} himself reporting on grain entrusted to him, but the {\i Yisrael}\u8217's son relaying what his deceased father said. Reuven says: my father Yaakov, before he died, separated {\i ma\u8217'aser} from his grain and designated it for you, the {\i levi}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The question now is what Yaakov \u8212- the deceased {\i Yisrael} \u8212- might have done with that {\i ma\u8217'aser} before he died. Specifically, the concern here is not the {\i switcheroo} of {\i al makom acher}, but rather the opposite: did the father do the {\i levi} a favor and proactively separate the {\i trumat ma\u8217'aser} from within the {\i ma\u8217'aser}, so that the {\i levi} receives clean, ready-to-use {\i ma\u8217'aser}? Or does the {\i ma\u8217'aser} still contain its {\i trumat ma\u8217'aser} waiting to be taken?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Articulation Effect\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i baraita} rules as follows: if the father said only \u8220"{\i ma\u8217'aser lecha b\u8217'yadi}\u8221" without specifying the amount, then {\i chosheshin l\u8217'trumat ma\u8217'aser shebo} \u8212- we are concerned that there is still {\i trumat ma\u8217'aser} within it, because since the amount was unspecified ({\i lo kayitz}), the father likely did not know the exact quantity and would not have taken the trouble of properly separating the {\i trumat ma\u8217'aser}. If, however, the father specified \u8220"{\i kor ma\u8217'aser lecha b\u8217'yadi}\u8221" \u8212- a precise measure \u8212- then {\i ein chosheshin l\u8217'trumat ma\u8217'aser shebo}: we need not be concerned, because since the amount was known and articulated, we assume the father fixed it up completely before he died.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Father\u8217's Preparation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This reading, it should be noted, inverts the apparent direction of the original {\i baraita}. In the original text, the unspecified case triggered no concern and the specified case triggered concern; in Rav Ashi\u8217's reading, it is the specified case where we assume the father acted charitably on the {\i levi}\u8217's behalf and there is no concern, while the unspecified case is where we remain concerned. As the {\i gemara} itself acknowledges, this does not map cleanly onto the literal language of the {\i baraita} \u8212- {\i adarabba} \u8212- but at least the underlying scenario is coherent. The operative principle is that just as we generally assume a {\i chaver} does not die and leave his grain in an unfixed state, so too if a {\i Yisrael} knew the exact amount of {\i ma\u8217'aser} he had designated for a particular {\i levi}, we presume he finished the job \u8212- that {\i tikkunei takniya baal habayis}, the householder fixed it up \u8212- separating the {\i trumat ma\u8217'aser} so the {\i levi} would receive clean grain, ready for use, what one might call {\i trumat ma\u8217'aser genummen}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Ba\u8217'al HaBayit Rights\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Question of Authority\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having established that the {\i ba\u8217'al habayit} is presumed to have separated {\i trumat ma\u8217'aser} from the {\i ma\u8217'aser} in his possession, the {\i gemara} pauses to ask a fundamental {\i halachic} question: {\i v\u8217'chi yesh lo reshut l\u8217'baal habayit litrom trumat ma\u8217'aser}? Since when does the {\i ba\u8217'al habayit} have the authority to separate {\i trumat ma\u8217'aser} from {\i ma\u8217'aser} that is not his? Once {\i ma\u8217'aser} has been separated, it belongs to the {\i levi} \u8212- or more broadly to {\i shevet Levi}. The {\i ba\u8217'al habayit} would normally need to be appointed as a {\i shaliach} to act on the {\i levi}\u8217's behalf. The assumption in the previous discussion seemed to be that the {\i ba\u8217'al habayit} has an inherent entitlement: having separated the {\i ma\u8217'aser}, he simply finishes the job by separating the {\i trumat ma\u8217'aser}. But what is the basis for this?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Abba Elazar View\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} answers: this is the position of Abba Elazar ben Gamla. As established in a {\i baraita}, Abba Elazar ben Gamla derives this authority from the {\i pasuk} concerning {\i trumat ma\u8217'aser}: {\i v\u8217'nechshav lachem trumatchem} \u8212- \u8220"and your {\i terumah} shall be considered for you.\u8221"\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Scriptural Parallels\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i pasuk} in full (Bamidbar 18:26\u8211-27) reads: the {\i levi\u8217'im} shall take a {\i ma\u8217'aser min hamaaser} \u8212- a tenth of the tenth \u8212- and this shall be {\i v\u8217'nechshav lachem trumatchem k\u8217'dagan min hagoren uch\u8217'mleiah min hayekev} \u8212- \u8220"your {\i terumah} shall be considered as grain from the threshing floor and fullness from the winepress.\u8221" Rashi explains that the verse is drawing a comparison between the two {\i terumot}: the {\i trumat ma\u8217'aser} that the {\i levi} gives is treated identically to the {\i terumah gedolah} that the {\i Yisrael} takes from his grain. The {\i baraita} makes this explicit: {\i bishtei trumos hakatuv medaber}, the {\i pasuk} is speaking of two {\i terumot} simultaneously and equating them.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Estimation Method\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 From this equation the {\i gemara} derives: {\i keshem sheterumah gedolah nitelet b\u8217'omed uvemachshavah}, just as regular {\i terumah} is separated by estimation and by thought alone, {\i kach trumat ma\u8217'aser nitelet b\u8217'omed uvemachshavah} \u8212- so too {\i trumat ma\u8217'aser} may be separated by estimation and thought. Since {\i terumah} has no fixed Torah-mandated amount (even a single kernel exempts the entire pile, with the Rabbinic standard being approximately two percent), one cannot be expected to give a precise measure and is encouraged to estimate, presumably rounding up rather than coming up short. {\i Tosafot} notes that we specifically encourage estimation for this reason, similar to the practice of a merchant who heaps a measure generously. Even though {\i trumat ma\u8217'aser} does have a fixed amount \u8212- one tenth of the {\i ma\u8217'aser} \u8212- the Torah\u8217's equation to {\i terumah gedolah} teaches that a guesstimated tenth suffices and need not be precisely measured. Furthermore, {\i bemachshavah}: the entire act of separation can be accomplished through thought alone, without any verbal declaration. The source for this is the word {\i v\u8217'nechshav} in the {\i pasuk} \u8212- the root {\i cheshbon}, calculation or thought \u8212- indicating that a purely mental act of designation suffices.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Conclusion\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 From this equation the {\i baraita} proceeds: {\i ukeshem sheyesh reshut l\u8217'baal habayit litrom terumah gedolah}, just as the owner of the grain separates {\i terumah gedolah}, {\i kach yesh reshut l\u8217'baal habayit litrom trumat ma\u8217'aser} \u8212- so too he has the authority to separate {\i trumat ma\u8217'aser} from the {\i ma\u8217'aser} in his possession. Having separated the {\i ma\u8217'aser}, he is entitled to complete the process by separating the {\i trumat ma\u8217'aser} as well. One might ask whether this entitlement also grants him the right to determine which {\i kohen} receives the {\i trumat ma\u8217'aser}, or whether that right remains with the {\i levi} \u8212- but the {\i gemara} does not explore this question, and at minimum the {\i ba\u8217'al habayit}\u8217's authority to perform the separation itself is established.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Setting Aside Produce\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Designated Room\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now transitions to a new {\i mishnah}, which continues the overarching theme of the {\i masechet} \u8212- presuming that an agent remains alive and his actions valid \u8212- applied now to {\i terumot} and {\i ma\u8217'asrot}. The {\i mishnah} states: {\i hameniach peiros lihyos mafrish aleihen terumah uma\u8217'asrot} \u8212- one who sets aside produce in a specific location (say, a room) intending to use that stockpile as a source from which to separate {\i terumah} and {\i ma\u8217'asrot} over time. The practical scenario is this: a {\i Yisrael} fills a room with grain or fruit and maintains a running ledger as he processes batches from his fields. Each time he has new {\i tevel} grain and needs to separate {\i terumah}, he declares: \u8220"two more bushels in that room are {\i terumah}\u8221" \u8212- or {\i ma\u8217'aser} \u8212- until the entire room has been exhausted in designations. {\i Tosafot} notes that this arrangement is technically {\i shelo min hamukaf}, since the grain being designated and the grain it exempts are in different locations, and explains that the Rabbis permitted this specifically in cases of pressing need such as {\i erev Shabbat}, when one needs to prepare food quickly.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chazaka Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Mafrish aleihem bechezkas sheheim kayamim} \u8212- one may continue separating in the ordinary fashion with the presumption that the grain in the room still exists. There is no need to verify the continued existence of the stockpile every time one makes a designation; the {\i chazaka} that it was there permits the assumption that it remains.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 24-Hour Window\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Im avdu, harei zeh choshesh me\u8217'es le\u8217'es} \u8212- if he goes back and discovers the stockpile is gone \u8212- whether rotted, stolen, or lost \u8212- he must be concerned for twenty-four hours. That is, any designations made in the last twenty-four hours are retroactively void, since the grain may have been gone during that window. Designations made before that point, however, stand. The reasoning behind this specific number is not obvious: if no one ever checked on the room, why should we assume the grain must have been present until twenty-four hours before discovery rather than having been lost months earlier? The {\i gemara} will address this tension directly, but the underlying conceptual framework is one of competing {\i chazakot}: the {\i chazaka d\u8217'meikara} \u8212- the original status quo that the grain was there \u8212- and the {\i chazaka d\u8217'hashta} \u8212- the inference from the current state that things may always have been as they are now. At what point does the current status override the original presumption? The {\i mishnah}\u8217's answer is twenty-four hours. {\i Tosafot} notes this is reminiscent of the {\i me\u8217'es le\u8217'es} principle in the laws of {\i nidda}, but stops short of explaining why the twenty-four-hour figure works here \u8212- it simply observes the parallel without resolving the underlying rationale.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Wine Checking Times\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yehuda adds a further rule specifically for wine: {\i bishlosha perakim bodkin et hayayin} \u8212- one must check on wine designated in such a room at three specific times, because wine can turn to vinegar, and one cannot separate {\i terumah} from wine over vinegar. The three times are: when the east wind blows after {\i Sukkot} (specifically in a year where {\i Sukkot} falls after the fall equinox, as Rashi explains); when the grape blossoms ({\i smadar}) emerge; and when water begins to enter the unripe grapes ({\i boser}). These are moments when wine is particularly vulnerable to spoilage, and a {\i ba\u8217'al habayit} relying on a designated room of wine must verify at each of these junctures that his wine has not become vinegar.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Me\u8217'es Le\u8217'es Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Yochanan\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now examines precisely what the {\i mishnah}\u8217's phrase {\i me\u8217'es le\u8217'es} means. Does it look backward from the moment of discovery, or forward from the moment of deposit? Rabbi Yochanan holds: {\i me\u8217'es le\u8217'es she-bdika} \u8212- the twenty-four hours run backward from the time of inspection. You discovered the problem now; you must be concerned that this problematic state began up to twenty-four hours ago. Everything done in the last twenty-four hours is potentially void; anything before that is presumed valid.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Elazar ben Tanchum\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Elazar ben Tanchum, citing Rabbi Elazar son of Rabbi Yanai, takes a far more extreme position: {\i me\u8217'es le\u8217'es shel hanacha} \u8212- the twenty-four hours run forward from the time of deposit. From the moment the stockpile was set aside, you may only assume with confidence that the first twenty-four hours were good. Once that initial window closes, you must treat everything as potentially problematic, up through the present moment of discovery.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Language Test\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} tests both positions against the language of the {\i mishnah}: {\i im avdu harei zeh choshesh me\u8217'es le\u8217'es} \u8212- if lost, one is concerned for {\i me\u8217'es le\u8217'es}. The word {\i choshesh} \u8212- concerned \u8212- clearly refers to the problematic state. Under Rabbi Yochanan\u8217's reading, the phrase fits naturally: you are concerned about the past twenty-four hours. Under Rabbi Elazar ben Tanchum\u8217's reading, however, if the good window is only the first twenty-four hours of deposit, the {\i mishnah} should have said {\i ad me\u8217'es le\u8217'es} \u8212- \u8220"until twenty-four hours\u8221" \u8212- rather than simply {\i choshesh me\u8217'es le\u8217'es}. The {\i gemara} concludes: {\i kashya} \u8212- this is indeed difficult for Rabbi Elazar ben Tanchum\u8217's position. The plain reading of the {\i mishnah} clearly supports Rabbi Yochanan.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mikva Analogy\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} ends with {\i divrei Rabbi Elazar} (the {\i gemara} corrects the reading to Rabbi Elazar rather than Rabbi Eliezer). The {\i gemara} then fills in the implicit counterpoint: {\i chalukin alav chaveirav al Rabbi Elazar} \u8212- his colleagues dispute him. The proof comes from a {\i mishnah} in the laws of {\i mikva}: a {\i mikva} that was measured and found to be deficient \u8212- {\i nimdad v\u8217'nimtza chaser} \u8212- meaning it had been valid at some known past point and is now found lacking. The rule is: {\i kol taharos she-nasu al gabav l\u8217'mifrea}, all purifications performed in it retroactively \u8212- {\i bein b\u8217'reshut hayachid bein b\u8217'reshut harabbim} \u8212- whether in the private or public domain \u8212- are {\i teme\u8217'ot}, presumed invalid.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The logic here is crucial. Normally, in the public domain, a doubt about {\i tumah} is resolved leniently ({\i safek tumah b\u8217'reshut harabbim tahor}). But that rule applies when an object started as {\i tahor} and we are uncertain whether it became {\i tamei}. Here, the object \u8212- a vessel, a person \u8212- started as {\i tamei}. Its {\i chazaka} was {\i tumah}. It was brought to the {\i mikva} to achieve {\i tahara}, but now we discover that the {\i mikva} itself was in doubt. The {\i chazaka} of {\i tumah} has never been definitively broken. Therefore, even in the public domain, it remains {\i tamei}, and this is true retroactively for as long as the {\i mikva}\u8217's validity was in question \u8212- without any twenty-four-hour limitation. The same logic, by extension, applies to the grain: the grain had a {\i chazaka} of being {\i tevel}. The householder\u8217's ongoing designations were meant to change its status. If the stockpile on which those designations depended was gone, the designations never took effect, and the grain retains its original {\i chazaka} of {\i tevel}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chazaka Principle\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} anticipates a possible reconciliation: one might have tried to read the {\i mikva mishnah}\u8217's word {\i l\u8217'mifrea} (\u8220"retroactively\u8221") through the lens of our {\i mishnah} and claim that {\i l\u8217'mifrea} means only the past twenty-four hours \u8212- thus limiting the retroactive reach in the {\i mikva} case as well and harmonizing it with the twenty-four-hour rule. The {\i gemara} rejects this: Rabbi Elazar\u8217's position is that the grain case goes all the way back, not merely twenty-four hours. His colleagues therefore apply the same logic to the {\i mikva}, rendering all past {\i tevilot} invalid without limit. In the {\i mikva} case, everything is {\i tamei} all the way back to the last verified measurement; there is no twenty-four-hour buffer. The competing {\i chazakot} are resolved decisively in favor of the original status: when an object\u8217's {\i chazaka} was {\i tumah}, a cloud of doubt over the act meant to purify it leaves the {\i chazaka} intact, indefinitely.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Eastern Wind Effects\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Wind Characteristics\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Because Rabbi Yehuda\u8217's rule about checking wine invoked the east wind ({\i ruach kadim}) blowing after {\i Sukkot}, the {\i gemara} takes up an extended discussion of the nature and effects of this wind. The point of departure is a {\i pasuk} from the book of Yonah: {\i vayeman Elokim ruach kadim charishit} \u8212- God appointed an east wind, {\i charishit}. What does {\i charishit} mean? Rav Yehuda says: when this east wind blows, {\i osa telamim telamim bayam} \u8212- it carves furrows in the sea, like a plow ({\i choreish}) carving rows in the earth. So powerful is its force that it produces these wave-furrows in the water. Rava objects: if that is what {\i charishit} means, how does it fit the context of the {\i pasuk}, which continues {\i vatach hashemesh al rosho shel Yonah vayitalaf} \u8212- the sun beat upon Yonah\u8217's head and he fainted? The context speaks of heat, not of the raw power of a wind at sea.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Four Winds Balance\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Before returning to resolve this, the {\i gemara} presents a tradition reported by Gneiva \u8212- who happened to pass by Rav Huna and Rav Chisda as they sat together. One of them suggested they rise for him as a great {\i talmid chacham}; the other demurred, noting that Gneiva was known for his contentious nature. While they deliberated, Gneiva approached them and, upon being told they were discussing winds, offered the following teaching in the name of Rav Chanan bar Rava citing Rav: {\i arba ruchot menashvot bechol yom} \u8212- four winds blow every day. {\i Ruach tzonit menashevet im kulam} \u8212- the northern wind blows in conjunction with all of them. {\i She\u8217'imalai kein ein ha\u8217'olam mitkaiyam afilu sha\u8217'ah achat} \u8212- were it not for the northern wind, the world could not endure even a single hour. Rashi explains that the northern wind has a tempering, balancing effect \u8212- neither too hot nor too cold \u8212- and without it, the other winds would be destructive.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Southern Wind Danger\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The southern wind, however, is the most powerful and dangerous of them all: {\i veruach dromit kashah mikulam} \u8212- the southern wind is the harshest. Were it not for an angel called Netz, who stands in its path and holds it back, {\i machrevet kol ha\u8217'olam kulo mipaneha} \u8212- it would destroy the entire world, presumably through an excess of cold. The scriptural basis is {\i hamavinatcha ya\u8217'aver Netz yifros knafav l\u8217'teiman} \u8212- \u8220"from Your understanding does Netz spread its wings to the south\u8221" (Iyov 39:26). This angel spreads its wings southward to restrain the southern wind and protect the world from its destructive force.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Destructive Effects\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Returning to the east wind and the context of Yonah, the {\i gemara} next reports a series of traditions about the damaging effects of the {\i ruach kadim}. Rava citing Rav states: {\i isha mapelet bo} \u8212- the east wind can cause a woman to miscarry. Shmuel says: {\i afilu margaliyot she\u8217'bayam markivot bo} \u8212- even the pearls in the sea rot because of it. And Rabbi Yochanan states: {\i afilu shichvat zera she\u8217'b\u8217'mei isha masrachet bo} \u8212- even semen within a woman\u8217's body spoils as a result of it, preventing conception.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Scriptural Basis\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Nachman bar Yitzchak then notes that all three Amoraim derived their respective positions from the same single {\i pasuk}: {\i ki hu bein achim yafria yavo kadim ruach Hashem, mimidbar ya\u8217'aleh v\u8217'yeivosh m\u8217'koro v\u8217'yecharav mayano v\u8217'hu yishasseh otzar keli chemdah} \u8212- \u8220"For he will cause division between brothers; the east wind of God shall come from the desert; his wellspring shall dry up and his spring shall wither; it shall plunder the treasure house of every precious vessel\u8221" (Hoshea 13:15). Rava then completes the exegesis: {\i adei Sura hu dedaykei kraei} \u8212- these are the scholars of Sura who know how to read {\i pesukim} in precisely this way. {\i V\u8217'yeivosh m\u8217'koro} \u8212- \u8220"his wellspring shall dry up\u8221" \u8212- refers to the womb of the woman, source of life; this is the miscarriage. {\i V\u8217'yecharav mayano} \u8212- \u8220"his spring shall wither\u8221" \u8212- refers to {\i shichvat zera she\u8217'b\u8217'mei isha}, the semen in the woman\u8217's body. {\i V\u8217'hu yishasseh otzar keli chemdah} \u8212- \u8220"it shall plunder the treasure house of precious vessels\u8221" \u8212- refers to {\i margaliyot she\u8217'bayam}, the pearls of the sea.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Interpretations\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} finally returns to the opening of the {\i pasuk}: {\i ki hu bein achim yafria} \u8212- \u8220"for it will cause division between brothers; {\i yafria}.\u8221" Rava interprets: {\i afilu shufsa bekufa demara rafya} \u8212- even the handle set into the head of an axe will be loosened and rotted by this east wind. The term {\i bein achim}, \u8220"between brothers,\u8221" refers to the two sides of the axe-head through which the handle passes; and {\i yafria} is a rearrangement of the letters of {\i rafya} \u8212- weakening. Even that tightly fitted wood will be rotted away. Rav Yosef interprets the same verse differently: {\i afilu sachsa bedafna rafya} \u8212- even a peg driven into a wall will be loosened. And Rav Acha bar Yaakov: {\i afilu kanya bekufsa rafya} \u8212- even wicker woven into a basket will weaken and rot.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 With that, we conclude our survey of the effects of the eastern wind, and complete {\i masechet Gittin}, chapter five \u8212- {\i hadran alach Kol HaGet}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Gittin 32\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Perek Hasholeach Intro\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Perek Overview\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 With Daf 32 we begin the fourth {\i perek} of Masechet Gittin, {\i Hasholeach}. While the {\i perek} opens with a discussion of {\i get}, what follows it moves into a range of topics that at first glance might appear unrelated to the laws of divorce. The unifying theme, however, is {\i tikkun olam} \u8212- the betterment of the world \u8212- a concept that motivates a series of rabbinic enactments throughout this {\i perek} and the ones that follow. Understanding what precisely is meant by {\i tikkun olam} here is itself one of the central tasks ahead of us.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i perek} is also significant because it demonstrates the scope of rabbinic power. We will see how the rabbis could use their authority to shape, and in certain circumstances even to contravene, {\i d\u8217'Oraita} law. This is not a digression; it is the very point.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Core Topics Preview\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Beyond the question of {\i get}, the {\i perek} introduces core conceptual issues in a number of distinct areas. The most famous is {\i pruzbul}, the mechanism by which Hillel addressed the annulment of debts on {\i shemita}. To appreciate {\i pruzbul} properly, one must understand the underlying mechanics of how debts function during the {\i shemita} year \u8212- and this {\i perek} gives us that opportunity. Cases involving slaves and other varied and foundational topics will also arise, making this {\i perek} one of the richest in the masechet.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Annulling Get Mishnah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Get Nullification Concept\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Mishnah} opens at the top of daf 32: {\i Hasholeach get l\u8217'ishto} \u8212- a man sends a {\i get} to his wife. We are still within the framework of {\i shlichut} established in the first {\i perek}, where the mechanics of delivering a {\i get} through an agent were laid out. What is new here, and immediately striking, is the concept of {\i bitul} \u8212- annulling the {\i get}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 When we hear that a husband can annul the {\i get} he sent, this demands some explanation. What does it mean to annul a {\i get}? One could straightforwardly annul the {\i shlichut} \u8212- tell the agent that he is no longer acting on your behalf \u8212- but the {\i Mishnah}\u8217's emphasis seems to be on something more: changing the very status of this document, this piece of paper.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Given all that has been emphasized until this point about {\i lishmah} \u8212- that the {\i sofer} must write with the specific intent and under the direction of the husband, that the {\i get} must be a full and genuine expression of the husband\u8217's will \u8212- a certain logic emerges. The {\i get} is not merely a legal document the way an ordinary {\i shtar} might be; it is an expression of the husband\u8217's will. The text {\i harei at muteret lechol adam} is the verbal crystallization of that will. If the husband genuinely and formally retracts that will before the {\i get} has taken effect, does the document not cease to be what it was? Does it not become merely a piece of paper?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This idea is sometimes expressed in the {\i acharonim} literature in terms of the husband being {\i mevatel} the {\i lishmah} of the {\i get}. That formulation requires a moment\u8217's unpacking. The {\i lishmah} \u8212- the intent \u8212- is a fact; it was written with proper names and proper intent. But what it really means is that the {\i get} is an expression of the husband\u8217's ongoing will. The {\i acharonim} are saying: if that will is genuinely and formally retracted, the document no longer embodies it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Consider the contrast with other {\i shtarot}. A {\i shtar kiddushin} \u8212- {\i harei at mekudeshet li} \u8212- is also an expression of one\u8217's will, and one might ask whether the same logic applies there. But a {\i shtar mecher} \u8212- {\i sadi mechurah lach} or {\i sadi netunah lach} \u8212- does not require {\i lishmah} in the same way. The {\i sofer} could theoretically pre-fill such a document, and the prohibition on pre-filling a {\i get} is by rabbinic {\i gezeirah} specific to {\i Gittin}. It is the {\i get} that demands this heightened sense of {\i lishmah} as an expression of the husband\u8217's will, and it is for that very reason that there seems to be this possibility of retracting it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Mishnah} assumes, at least on its surface, that a verbal annulment suffices. One need not write a formal document of revocation. The verbal withdrawal of will, communicated to the right parties, is enough. The mechanics of how this works, and its limits, will be developed throughout the {\i Gemara}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Four Annulment Scenarios\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Mishnah} describes four distinct scenarios of annulment. In the first, the husband himself catches up with the {\i shaliach} \u8212- {\i v\u8217'higia bashaliach} \u8212- and tells him: {\i get shenatati lecha batel, harei hu batel} \u8212- the {\i get} I gave you is annulled, behold it is annulled. In the second scenario, {\i o sheshalach acharav shaliach} \u8212- he sends a second {\i shaliach} after the first \u8212- and this second agent conveys the message of annulment to the first.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 In the third scenario, {\i kadam etzlo ishto} \u8212- the husband races ahead and gets to his wife before the {\i shaliach} does \u8212- and tells her directly: {\i get sheshalachti lach batel} \u8212- the {\i get} I sent you is annulled, before she has received it. In the fourth, {\i o sheshalach etzlah shaliach} \u8212- he sends a second {\i shaliach} directly to his wife \u8212- and that second agent conveys the annulment to her before the {\i get} arrives.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 All four scenarios are presented as valid forms of {\i bitul}. The {\i Gemara} will examine each in turn to explain why each one needed to be stated explicitly, since each one might have been thought ineffective for a different reason.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Cutoff Point Rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Before turning to the {\i Gemara}\u8217's analysis of those scenarios, the {\i Mishnah} states the obvious but essential limit: {\i im meshehigia get leyadah, shuv eino yachol levatlo} \u8212- once the {\i get} has been placed in the wife\u8217's hand, he can no longer annul it. She is divorced. The document has taken effect and its work is done.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabban Gamliel Takana\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Mishnah} then records that {\i barishonah} \u8212- originally, in the earlier practice \u8212- a husband could convene a {\i beis din} in his own hometown and annul the {\i get} there, without notifying the {\i shaliach} or the wife. The {\i shaliach} would continue on his way thinking the {\i get} was valid; the wife would receive it thinking she was divorced; and no one in the relevant location would have any reason to doubt it \u8212- while back home, the husband and his small {\i beis din} knew otherwise.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The problem this creates is obvious. {\i Hitkin Rabban Gamliel HaZaken shelo yehu osin ken mipnei tikkun olam} \u8212- Rabban Gamliel the Elder enacted a {\i takana} that this practice is forbidden, for the sake of {\i tikkun olam}. A woman who believed herself divorced and remarried on the basis of a {\i get} that had been secretly annulled would be in a catastrophic halachic situation. The {\i takana} therefore requires that any annulment be communicated to the relevant parties \u8212- the {\i shaliach} or the wife \u8212- so that they are aware of it and the {\i get} cannot be acted upon in error.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} will raise the question: if someone defied Rabban Gamliel\u8217's {\i takana} and annulled the {\i get} secretly before a hidden {\i beis din}, is the {\i bitul} nonetheless effective \u8212- {\i batel o eino mevutal} \u8212- or does violating the {\i takana} invalidate the annulment itself?\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Gemara Analysis Early\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Higia Intentionality\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} opens by noting that the {\i Mishnah} does not use the plural {\i higu} \u8212- they reached \u8212- but rather the singular {\i higia} \u8212- he reached. This is a reading about intentionality. {\i Higia} suggests that the husband simply happened to encounter the {\i shaliach}, perhaps coincidentally, without necessarily having set out to catch him. The {\i Gemara} raises the question of whether such an unintentional encounter could support a valid {\i bitul}. One might reason that since the husband was not focused on annulling the {\i get} \u8212- he just happened upon the {\i shaliach} \u8212- perhaps his subsequent words of {\i bitul} were not serious, or perhaps we should be concerned that he was not genuinely retracting his will but rather seizing an opportunity. The {\i Gemara} teaches: {\i afielu mimaila} \u8212- even if the encounter was unintentional \u8212- the {\i bitul} is valid. We do not say that the husband was acting on a whim or merely exploiting the moment to cause his wife difficulty.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Second Shaliach Override\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The second scenario \u8212- sending a second {\i shaliach} \u8212- requires explicit mention because one might have thought that {\i shaliach} number two lacks the standing to override {\i shaliach} number one. {\i Shaliach} number one carries the actual document; he is the embodiment of the husband\u8217's original will, and there is something to the idea that physical possession of the {\i get} invests that original {\i shlichus} with greater weight. Perhaps a mere verbal message carried by a second agent \u8212- himself not in possession of the document \u8212- cannot override the status conferred by the first {\i shaliach}\u8217's role.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Kamashmalan} \u8212- the {\i Mishnah} teaches that it can. The second {\i shaliach} effectively represents the husband\u8217's current will, and that current will takes precedence.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Reaching Wife Directly\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The third scenario \u8212- the husband himself races to his wife \u8212- also requires separate mention. Here, the concern cuts differently. When the husband goes directly to the woman who has been waiting for the {\i get}, and he appears before her and tells her that the {\i get} she was expecting is annulled, we might be deeply suspicious of his motives. {\i Vadai letzura d\u8217'achamchaman} \u8212- certainly we might suspect he is doing this purely to cause her anguish, not as a genuine retraction of his will. When he annuls through a messenger to the first {\i shaliach}, there is a degree of remove; but here he is confronting her directly, and the opportunity for cruelty is patent.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Nevertheless, the {\i Mishnah} teaches that we do not make that assumption. The {\i bitul} is valid regardless of how it was received emotionally.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Second Shaliach to Wife\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The fourth scenario \u8212- sending a second {\i shaliach} to the wife \u8212- requires mention for yet another distinct reason. One might have thought that hiring a {\i shaliach} to deliver the message of {\i bitul} to the wife suggests that the husband is not troubled by the effort and cost of causing her anguish at arm\u8217's length. He sits comfortably at home while someone else carries the distressing message. {\i Vadai letzura d\u8217'achamchaman} \u8212- perhaps in this configuration, where the husband incurs no personal inconvenience, we should certainly suspect his motives. The {\i Mishnah} teaches that we still do not invalidate the {\i bitul} on this basis.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Running After Shaliach\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Following the analysis of all four scenarios, the {\i Gemara} addresses a further case: {\i iglaei milta lemafreia d\u8217'bitulei batlei} \u8212- what if we discover in hindsight that the husband was clearly trying to annul the {\i get}? Specifically, the case imagined is one where the husband was visibly running through the marketplace after the {\i shaliach}, clearly trying to catch him, but the {\i shaliach} delivered the {\i get} to the wife a moment before the husband arrived. Should we reason that since it is now manifest what the husband\u8217's intent was \u8212- he was plainly trying to be {\i mevatel} \u8212- perhaps his running itself should count retroactively as an act of {\i bitul}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} says: {\i ka mashma lan} \u8212- no. This does not work. And the reasons are multiple. First, we ordinarily say {\i devarim shebelev einam devarim} \u8212- unarticulated thoughts carry no halachic weight. The husband said nothing; he was thinking and running, but those inner intentions do not constitute a valid speech act. Second, even if one could somehow treat the running as equivalent to an articulation of will, the husband still did not communicate that will to the {\i shaliach}, the wife, or a {\i beis din}. He was shouting, so to speak, in the empty air of the marketplace. The {\i Mishnah}\u8217's structure throughout is that valid {\i bitul} requires communication to the relevant parties. Without that, there is no {\i bitul}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafot Devarim Shebelev\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} engages this case with particular depth. {\i Tosafot} begins from the question of why {\i devarim shebelev einam devarim} \u8212- why do unarticulated thoughts carry no weight? The conventional answer is systemic: a legal framework cannot function if people can later claim \u8220"this is what I was thinking.\u8221" Halachic acts require articulated, measurable, knowable expression.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But {\i Tosafot} notes a second rationale that appears in certain contexts: the reason unarticulated thoughts don\u8217't count is that if a person really meant them, they would have expressed them. The failure to articulate is itself evidence that the commitment was not firm enough to have legal weight.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} then points out that this second rationale fails to apply to the running-after-the-{\i shaliach} case. No one doubts that the husband was serious. He was running. He was making every effort. The failure to articulate was not a sign of ambivalence but a failure of logistics \u8212- he simply did not reach the {\i shaliach} in time. If the only problem were lack of seriousness, it would not apply here.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} further imagines a scenario in which the {\i shaliach} actually saw the husband running. At the time, the {\i shaliach} may have interpreted it as the husband urging him on \u8212- {\i hurry up and deliver it}. Only in hindsight, once the husband explains himself, does the {\i shaliach} understand that the running was an attempt at {\i bitul}. If {\i devarim shebelev havei devarim} in such a case \u8212- if we can treat the running as a form of articulation \u8212- should we not retroactively interpret what the {\i shaliach} witnessed as a communicated act of {\i bitul}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} toys with this possibility seriously. Yet in the end, {\i Tosafot} concludes that the rabbis treated such a case as equivalent to {\i devarim shebelev} \u8212- even if the running was unambiguously sincere. {\i Shekeivan d\u8217'ktzas dami l\u8217'devarim shebelev lo chashiv akimas dibur min haTorah} \u8212- since it resembles {\i devarim shebelev} closely enough, it is not treated as a fully constituted speech act under Torah law. And {\i Tosafot} is explicit that this amounts to a rabbinic override: the rabbis effectively prevented what might technically have been a valid {\i bitul} from taking effect. As {\i Tosafot} notes \u8212- and this is a theme that will recur throughout the {\i perek} \u8212- the rabbis sometimes use their power to prevent something that would otherwise work under {\i d\u8217'Oraita} law, provided the contravention is not so blatant as to undermine the Torah structure.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The bottom line is that this does not constitute valid {\i bitul}, for a convergence of reasons: {\i devarim shebelev einam devarim}; even if it were treated as articulation, it was not communicated to the right parties at the right time; and even if the {\i shaliach} witnessed the running, a retroactive reinterpretation of that moment \u8212- based on later clarification \u8212- does not convert it into a contemporaneous speech act. Valid {\i bitul} requires explicit, timely communication to the {\i shaliach}, the wife, or a {\i beis din}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Annulment Formulas\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Valid Invalid Formulas\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now turns to the {\i Baraita} that addresses the question of what language suffices for valid {\i bitul}. A student had earlier asked whether there is a required formula. The {\i Baraita} teaches: if he said {\i batel hu} \u8212- it is annulled \u8212- or {\i ee efshi bo} \u8212- I do not desire it \u8212- {\i devarav kayamim}, it is effective. If he said {\i pasul hu} \u8212- it is invalid \u8212- or {\i eino get} \u8212- it is not a {\i get} \u8212- {\i lo amar klum}, he has said nothing.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The distinction is conceptually interesting. {\i Ee efshi bo} \u8212- I no longer desire it \u8212- does not explicitly void the document; it simply withdraws the will behind it. Yet that is enough. {\i Pasul hu}, on the other hand, is a statement of alleged fact \u8212- you are asserting that the {\i get} is halachically invalid. But by what grounds is it invalid? You are not making a declarative act of retraction; you are making a false assertion about its current legal status. That carries no weight.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Batel Status vs Effect\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The more subtle case is {\i batel hu} itself. The {\i Gemara} asks: {\i lemimra de-batel lishna de-livatel mashma} \u8212- does saying \u8220"it is {\i batel}\u8221" mean it shall become {\i batel}, i.e., I am hereby annulling it? Or is it a description of current status \u8212- it already is {\i batel}? Consider the language of {\i kiddushin}: {\i harei at mekudeshet li} \u8212- behold you are betrothed to me. Is that a description of a fact or the performance of an act? Obviously the latter. But the grammar is identical to a description. The question of whether language is constitutive or merely descriptive is not always resolved by the words themselves.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Matana Gift Comparison\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} brings a ruling of Rav Sheshet (also attributed to Rabba bar Abba or Rabba bar Avuh) concerning {\i matana} \u8212- a gift. If someone received a gift and thereafter said {\i matana zo mevutelet} \u8212- this gift that I have received shall be annulled \u8212- or {\i ee efshi bah} \u8212- I do not desire it \u8212- {\i lo amar klum}. Once you have received the gift, the gift is yours. If you wish to divest yourself of it, go give it away; you cannot retroactively annul the receipt. The language of {\i shall be annulled} is simply inapplicable to something already completed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 However, if the recipient said {\i beteila hi} \u8212- it is annulled \u8212- or {\i eina matana} \u8212- it is not a gift \u8212- {\i devarav kayamin}, that is effective. Here, the logic is essentially the reverse of the {\i get} case. By saying \u8220"it is not a gift, it was never a gift,\u8221" you are articulating that you never accepted it as such; you are describing the status from its inception, not trying to effect a change now.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Alma batel me\u8217'ikara mashma} \u8212- so when {\i batel} is used effectively in the {\i matana} context, it means \u8220"it was never valid from the outset.\u8221" The {\i Gemara} thus faces a tension: by the {\i get}, {\i batel hu} seems to mean \u8220"I am hereby annulling it\u8221" \u8212- a future-oriented performative act. By the {\i matana}, {\i beteila hi} seems to mean \u8220"it was never valid\u8221" \u8212- a backward-looking description. Does {\i batel} mean one thing or two different things?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Abaye Context Analysis\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Abaye} resolves this: {\i batel shtei leshonot mashma} \u8212- {\i batel} carries both meanings, and which meaning obtains depends entirely on context. {\i Le-gabei get lishna de-bahani beh ka-amar} \u8212- by a {\i get}, the clear purpose of the statement is to effect an annulment, so we interpret it accordingly; {\i batel hu} means it shall hereby be annulled. {\i De-gabei matana lishna de-bahani beh ka-amar} \u8212- by a {\i matana} already received, the only way the language can function effectively is if it refers to original status; so {\i beteila hi} means it was never a valid gift.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The straightforward explanation is simply that words are often ambiguous, and context disambiguates them. This is entirely parallel to the concept of {\i yadayim mokhichot} \u8212- when context is clear, it determines the meaning of an otherwise ambiguous expression. Under the {\i chuppah}, {\i harei at mekudeshet li} is unmistakably performative. Said in a different context, the same words might describe a fact. Here too: the intent behind the speech act in its context tells us which reading of {\i batel} is operative.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Ravina Formula Questions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Batel Without Hu\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Ravina} found Rav Nachman bar Yitzchak leaning in a doorway and posed him the following question: what if the husband simply said {\i batel} \u8212- without {\i hu}? He did not say {\i harei hu batel} or {\i batel hu}; he just uttered {\i batel}. Is that an effective formula? {\i Rashi} explains that the problem is the absence of {\i hu} \u8212- he did not even form a complete sentence.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} answers: {\i teiku} \u8212- the question stands unresolved. We do not know whether this stripped-down utterance suffices or not.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Sheshet Baraita\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i Baraita} is cited in the name of Rav Sheshet \u8212- some say it was taught in a {\i matnitta}: if he said {\i get zeh lo yoil} \u8212- this {\i get} shall not work \u8212- {\i lo yattir} \u8212- shall not render her permitted \u8212- {\i lo yaaziv} \u8212- shall not send her forth \u8212- {\i lo yishalach}, {\i lo yigoresh} \u8212- shall not divorce her \u8212- or {\i yehe kacheres} / {\i tehe kacheres} \u8212- it shall be like pottery \u8212- {\i devarav kayamim}, all of these are effective formulas.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What these formulations share is that they are forward-looking: they say what the {\i get} shall no longer do. They constitute a speech act of annulment. {\i Eino mo\u8217'il, eino matir, eino ma\u8217'aziv, eino meshaleach, eino megaresh} \u8212- it does not work, does not permit, does not send her forth, does not divorce \u8212- {\i lo amar klum}, these are not effective. These formulations merely describe what the {\i get} currently does not do \u8212- they are statements of alleged fact about its present status rather than acts of voiding it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Harei Hu Cheres\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 An ambiguous case is then raised: what of {\i harei hu cheres} \u8212- behold, it is pottery? Is this a performative declaration \u8212- I hereby declare it to be mere pottery, stripping it of its status as a {\i get} \u8212- or is it a description of current status \u8212- I am asserting that it already is nothing more than pottery? {\i Mahu} \u8212- what is the ruling?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hekdesh Hefker Compare\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One {\i amora} said to another: {\i mai shena me-harei hu hekdesh, harei hu hefker} \u8212- what is the difference from \u8220"behold it is {\i hekdesh}\u8221" or \u8220"behold it is {\i hefker}\u8221"? In those cases, the declaration clearly effects a change in status; {\i harei hu hekdesh} is the paradigmatic performative speech act by which something becomes consecrated. Why should {\i harei hu cheres} not be read the same way?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} notes the difficulty with this comparison. The reason {\i harei hu hekdesh} or {\i harei hu hefker} are unmistakably performative is that {\i hekdesh} and {\i hefker} are statuses that exist exclusively through speech acts. When you say something \u8220"is {\i hekdesh},\u8221" the context makes clear that you mean you are making it {\i hekdesh}, because that is the only way {\i hekdesh} comes into being. {\i Cheres}, however \u8212- pottery \u8212- is a material description. Saying something \u8220"is pottery\u8221" does not inherently invoke a speech act that changes status; it might simply be identifying what the object is made of. Moreover, {\i Tosafot} points out, the parallel does not map cleanly: saying {\i kikar zeh hekdesh} \u8212- this loaf of bread is {\i hekdesh} \u8212- would clearly be effective, whereas {\i be-get zeh cheres} \u8212- this {\i get} is pottery \u8212- might certainly be saying nothing at all. One cannot mechanically substitute one predicate for another and assume the performative force transfers. The specific word matters enormously to the sense of the sentence, and {\i Tosafot} here does not arrive at a definitive resolution.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Reversing Annulment\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chozer Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now raises what is perhaps the most conceptually arresting question of the entire {\i sugiyah}: {\i chozer umegaresh bo o eino chozer umegaresh bo?} \u8212- once the husband has annulled the {\i get}, can he then reverse that annulment and use the {\i get} to divorce his wife, or has he effectively destroyed the {\i get} beyond recovery?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is the equivalent of asking whether stopping a check and then un-stopping it would allow the check to be cashed. Can the {\i bitul} be undone?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Nachman Sheshet Dispute\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Nachman says {\i chozer umegaresh bo} \u8212- he can reuse it; the {\i bitul} can be reversed and the {\i get} used. Rav Sheshet says {\i eino chozer umegaresh bo} \u8212- he cannot; the {\i get} has been rendered permanently void. {\i Vehilchata kevateih de-Rav Nachman} \u8212- the law follows Rav Nachman.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This ruling is striking. We have spent considerable time reading through formulas of {\i bitul} \u8212- {\i batel hu, harei hu cheres, lo yoil, lo yattir} \u8212- all of which are directed emphatically at the efficacy of the {\i get}, at stripping it of its status as an instrument of divorce. And yet the {\i halacha} says it can be un-done?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kiddushin Parallel\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} draws a comparison to a case in {\i Kiddushin}. If a man says to a woman {\i harei at mekudeshet li be-tabaat zu le-achar sheloshim yom} \u8212- you are betrothed to me with this ring in thirty days \u8212- and then during those thirty days one of the parties retracts, {\i Rabbi Yochanan} rules that this retraction is effective. {\i Vekayma lan hilchata kevateih de-Rabbi Yochanan be-chozeret} \u8212- and the established ruling follows Rabbi Yochanan that she can retract. Furthermore, the kiddushin, once reversed in this way, is fully undone. The initial act, which created a pending kiddushin, can be annulled before it takes effect, and the annulment is complete.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} adduces this to support the idea that annulment before an act has fully taken effect is possible and reversible: just as the retraction of {\i kiddushin} before the thirty days expire is clean and complete, so too should a {\i bitul} of a {\i get} before delivery be reversible.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Dibbur Umaseh Analysis\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} immediately rejects this comparison. {\i Hatam dibbur u-dibbur hu} \u8212- there, in the {\i kiddushin} case, it is speech against speech. The {\i kiddushin} was created by a speech act conditioned on time, and it is undone by another speech act before the condition matures. {\i Ati dibbur u-mevatel dibbur} \u8212- one speech act cancels another. Nothing concrete has happened; no status has been effected in the world; the act is entirely in the realm of verbal declaration, and a subsequent verbal declaration can undo it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Hacha}, here, by contrast, the situation is different. {\i Nehi de-bitul li-shlichutei de-shaliach} \u8212- granted, when he said {\i batel hu}, he did annul the {\i shlichus} of the {\i shaliach}, the agency. But {\i gita gufiah mi ka batel?} \u8212- since when did he annul the {\i get} itself? This is the crucial move. The {\i Gemara} is suggesting that what the husband\u8217's words actually accomplished was the cancellation of the agency \u8212- nothing more. The {\i get} as a legal document was not changed. A valid document sitting in an agent\u8217's hand is not rendered legally void by the principal\u8217's subsequent verbal statement of displeasure with it. The words canceled the {\i shlichus}; they did not alter the status of the document.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 And if all that was canceled was the {\i shlichus}, then the {\i shlichus} can simply be re-established. The document remains valid; appoint a new {\i shaliach} or take the document yourself, and deliver it. {\i Chozer umegaresh bo} \u8212- yes, he can reuse it \u8212- because nothing happened to the {\i get} itself.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafot Annul Get\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} presses on this reading carefully. From the language of the {\i Gemara} \u8212- {\i gita gufiah mi ka batel} \u8212- {\i Tosafot} draws the inference that the {\i Gemara} is stating it is simply impossible to annul the {\i get} itself through speech. {\i Mitoch ha-lashon mashma de-afilu bitlo be-perush eino mevutal} \u8212- even if the husband had explicitly stated \u8220"I am not annulling the {\i shlichus}, I am annulling the {\i get} itself,\u8221" it would be ineffective. {\i Ve-dibbur li-shlichuto hu de-batel} \u8212- whatever he said, we will interpret it as a cancellation of agency, not a change in the document\u8217's status.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is significant because it means that all the rich analysis of formulas \u8212- {\i batel hu}, {\i harei hu cheres}, {\i lo yoil} \u8212- was really about the annulment of {\i shlichus}, not about changing the inherent legal status of the {\i get} as a document. The {\i get}, as a validly executed document, is beyond the reach of the husband\u8217's subsequent speech. {\i Ve-ka-amar gita gufiah mi ka batel \u8212- mashma de-ein lo koach levatel} \u8212- the language implies he simply does not have the power to annul it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} also notes, however, that a parallel {\i sugiyah} reads slightly differently: {\i nehi de-batlei li-shlichutei, midei de-gita lo batlei} \u8212- granted he annulled the {\i shlichus}, but from the {\i get} itself he did not annul anything. {\i Tosafot} notes that this formulation only says he {\i did not} annul the {\i get}, not that he {\i could not}. Based on this, {\i Tosafot} argues that in a case where the {\i bitul} was explicit and unambiguous \u8212- where the husband clearly declared the {\i get} itself void \u8212- {\i Rav Nachman} would actually concede that it is {\i batel} and the {\i get} cannot be reused. Furthermore, {\i Tosafot} maintains that one necessarily must say that the {\i get} is not yet an irretractable completed act as long as it has not reached the woman\u8217's hand \u8212- otherwise, various other principles would be in tension. The upshot is that the {\i sugiyah} leaves genuine ambiguity: does Rav Nachman\u8217's ruling that {\i chozer umegaresh bo} rest on the view that annulment of the {\i get} itself is categorically impossible, or merely that in the ordinary case we interpret the husband\u8217's words as canceling {\i shlichus} rather than the {\i get}, while a fully explicit annulment could in principle still work?\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Original Beis Din\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Barishonah Original\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now takes up the second part of the {\i Mishnah} \u8212- the {\i barishonah} clause. Originally, before Rabban Gamliel\u8217's {\i takana}, a husband could annul the {\i get} by convening a {\i beis din} back in his hometown while the {\i shaliach} was en route, with no notification to the {\i shaliach} or the wife. This has now been prohibited. But the {\i Gemara} asks: in that original state, when this was permissible, how many people were required to constitute the {\i beis din} before which the {\i bitul} was made?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two Three Judges\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Nachman says {\i bifnei shnayim} \u8212- before two. Rav Sheshet says {\i bifnei gimel} \u8212- before three, a full {\i beis din}. Rav Sheshet\u8217's position is straightforward: the {\i Mishnah} says {\i beis din}, and a {\i beis din} is three. Rav Nachman counters that two are also called a {\i beis din} in certain contexts \u8212- {\i beis terei nami beis din kari lehu}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} notes that Rav Nachman holds a position in Sanhedrin that {\i shnayim she-danu, dineihem din} \u8212- two who adjudicated, their ruling stands retroactively \u8212- but points out that other {\i amoraim} who agree with Rav Nachman here do not hold that position in Sanhedrin, suggesting that Rav Nachman\u8217's ruling here reflects a broader definitional claim about the term {\i beis din} in certain contexts, not merely a leniency about judicial quorums.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Agency vs Document\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The deeper question behind the dispute between Rav Nachman and Rav Sheshet over two versus three is conceptual, and it connects elegantly to the earlier dispute between them. Why did the original procedure require a {\i beis din} at all? If all the husband is doing is retracting his will \u8212- annulling the {\i shlichus} \u8212- why not simply do it privately? The only reason to require witnesses is to establish proof that it occurred. In that case, two witnesses suffice, just as any other fact requiring proof requires two witnesses, not three judges. This is Rav Nachman\u8217's position: the {\i bitul} is fundamentally a retraction of {\i shlichus}, an act of the husband\u8217's will, which requires only {\i eidim} to authenticate it. The use of the term {\i beis din} is loose; two witnesses are all that is really needed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Sheshet, who holds that the {\i bitul} effects a genuine change in the status of the {\i get} itself \u8212- and who correspondingly holds that once done it cannot be reversed \u8212- sees this as a fundamentally judicial act. Something that changes the legal status of a document cannot be accomplished by a private expression of will, however witnessed. It requires a formal juridical proceeding, a {\i beis din} of three. The two positions \u8212- Rav Nachman and Rav Sheshet \u8212- thus map perfectly: {\i chozer umegaresh bo} / {\i eino chozer umegaresh bo}, and {\i bifnei shnayim} / {\i bifnei gimel}, are two expressions of a single underlying conceptual disagreement about the nature of {\i bitul}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Gittin 33\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Shaliach Cancellation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two vs.\u160 ?Three Judges\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The fourth chapter of {\i Gittin} opens with the {\i mishna} of {\i hasholeach get l\u8217'ishto} \u8212- the case of a husband who appoints a {\i shaliach} to deliver a {\i get} to his wife, and then seeks to cancel that appointment. Since the {\i shaliach} is no longer authorized to deliver the {\i get}, his doing so would render the {\i get} null, leaving the wife to believe she is divorced when she is not. To prevent this, {\i Rabban Gamliel HaZaken} enacted a {\i takana}: {\i hitkin Rabban Gamliel HaZaken shelo yehu osim ken mipnei tikkun ha-olam} \u8212- he ordained that a husband may no longer cancel the {\i shlichus} of the {\i shaliach}, for the sake of {\i tikkun ha-olam}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} records that originally \u8212- {\i barishona} \u8212- the husband could convene a {\i beit din} elsewhere, in a place where the {\i shaliach} was not present, and before that court announce the cancellation of the {\i shlichus}. This would nullify the {\i get} entirely. After {\i Rabban Gamliel}\u8217's {\i takana}, this was no longer permitted.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 There is a deeper conceptual point worth noting here. When a husband appoints a {\i shaliach}, he transfers the power to deliver the {\i get} to that agent. The question is: how independent does the {\i shaliach} then become? Does the initial command carry itself through to completion, or does the husband remain, so to speak, umbilically connected to the {\i shaliach} at every moment, retaining the capacity to withdraw authorization? The husband\u8217's power to cancel the {\i shaliach} mid-process reflects the latter understanding \u8212- that the {\i shlichut} continues to depend on the ongoing will of the husband. There are related {\i sugyot} in {\i Kiddushin}, in the second {\i perek}, that engage the question of how far a {\i shaliach} exercises his own independent judgment within the parameters granted to him.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} at the bottom of 32b now takes up a new question: {\i bifnei kamah hu mevatlo} \u8212- in front of how many judges must the husband announce the cancellation? This refers to the era before {\i Rabban Gamliel}\u8217's {\i takana}, when cancellation was still permissible.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rav Nachman} says {\i bifnei shnayim} \u8212- two judges suffice. {\i Rav Sheshet} says {\i bifnei shlosha} \u8212- three are required.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The conceptual underpinning of {\i Rav Nachman}\u8217's position is that {\i le-bei trei nami beis din kari lehu} \u8212- even two people can be called a {\i beit din}. This is at least plausible, since the standard requirement for a {\i beit din} of three is rooted in the principle of {\i ein beit din shakol} \u8212- we do not allow an evenly divided court. That concern applies when the court is voting on a case and an even split would leave it deadlocked, as would occur with two judges. But here, no judgment is being rendered; the husband is simply making a declaration before a court that oversees it. It could therefore be argued that the concern of {\i ein beit din shakol} simply does not apply.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Pruzbul Proof\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rav Nachman} supports his position with a proof from the {\i mishna} in {\i Shevi\u8217'it} dealing with the institution of the {\i pruzbul}. The {\i pruzbul} is a legal document by which the {\i beit din} acquires the right to collect a loan even after the {\i shemita} year would otherwise cancel it. The text of the {\i pruzbul} as quoted in the {\i mishna} reads: {\i masrani lifneichem Ploni u-Ploni ha-dayanim she-be-makom Ploni} \u8212- \u8220"I hereby transfer [the loan] before you, so-and-so and so-and-so, the judges of such-and-such a place.\u8221" Only two names appear \u8212- {\i Ploni u-Ploni} \u8212- suggesting that for the {\i pruzbul}, a court of two suffices.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rav Nachman} further adduces a proof from the same context: the {\i mishna} states that {\i ha-dayanim chotmin mi-lemata o ha-edim} \u8212- either the judges or the witnesses sign at the bottom of the {\i pruzbul}. He reasons: {\i mai lav dayanim dumya de-edim} \u8212- just as witnesses are two, so too judges in this context are two. The {\i mishna} equates the two tracks, implying that the same numerical threshold applies to both.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sheshet\u8217's Objection\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rav Sheshet} objects to the first proof. He asks rhetorically: {\i atu tanna ki rukhla le-meichshav ve-leizil?} \u8212- Does the {\i tanna} have to go listing names like a peddler in the marketplace calling out his wares? Even if three {\i dayanim} were present, the {\i mishna} would not write {\i Ploni u-Ploni u-Ploni}; such repetition would be superfluous. The {\i mishna} mentions two names not to indicate that only two are required, but simply because that is sufficient to convey the formula. The reader is presumed to know that a {\i beit din} requires three.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 To {\i Rav Nachman}\u8217's second proof \u8212- the equation of judges and witnesses in the {\i pruzbul} \u8212- {\i Rav Sheshet} responds: {\i mi-di irya ha ke-dita ve-ha ke-dita} \u8212- the two tracks are simply independent. There is no implication that the two tracks share the same numerical requirement. Two witnesses are two; three judges are three. The {\i mishna} needed to mention both possibilities because the {\i shtar} can be drafted either in the voice of the witnesses or in the voice of the judges, and in either case the other party may sign. It is not that judges can replace witnesses with a reduced number; rather, the language of the document and the identity of the signatories are flexible, but the counts remain fixed at their respective minimums.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then works out the implications of this last point more carefully. The {\i mishna}\u8217's ruling that {\i lo shena katuv bi-leshon dayanim ve-chatmu edim ve-lo shena katuv bi-leshon edim ve-chatmu dayanim} \u8212- it makes no difference whether the document is written in the voice of the judges and signed by witnesses, or in the voice of the witnesses and signed by judges \u8212- teaches that these two formalities are interchangeable. The judges and witnesses each retain their respective roles and numerical requirements, but the language of the document need not match the identity of its signatories.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Halachic Conclusion\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i halacha} follows {\i Rav Nachman}: cancellation before two judges is sufficient. This is the general rule whenever {\i Rav Nachman} and {\i Rav Sheshet} dispute a matter \u8212- the {\i halacha} follows {\i Rav Nachman}. Both the {\i Rambam} and the {\i Shulchan Aruch} rule accordingly, as does the {\i Rif}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One can observe that the proofs offered on each side are not particularly decisive. {\i Rav Nachman}\u8217's argument from the {\i pruzbul} is answerable, and {\i Rav Sheshet}\u8217's objections, while cogent, are not airtight either. The deeper question \u8212- what role this {\i beit din} plays when it oversees a cancellation of {\i shlichus}, a proceeding that involves no adjudication \u8212- remains somewhat open. Both positions carry internal logic. In practice, since {\i Rabban Gamliel}\u8217's {\i takana} has closed off the entire mechanism of cancellation, the question is largely theoretical. Yet the later {\i amoraim} engage it seriously, and as will become clear, {\i Rabbi Yochanan}\u8217's alignment with {\i Rav Nachman} and {\i Resh Lakish}\u8217's alignment with {\i Rav Sheshet} gives this dispute an earlier, tannaitic resonance.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Takanah Rationale\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mamzerim Concern\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} states that {\i Rabban Gamliel} enacted his {\i takana mipnei tikkun ha-olam} \u8212- for the sake of fixing the world. The {\i gemara} now asks: what precisely is the {\i tikkun ha-olam} at stake?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Yochanan} says: {\i mipnei tikkun mamzerim} \u8212- to prevent {\i mamzerim}. When a husband secretly cancels his {\i shaliach} before a {\i beit din} of two \u8212- according to {\i Rav Nachman}\u8217's position \u8212- the news of that cancellation may never reach the wife. She receives the {\i get} from the {\i shaliach}, believes she is divorced, remarries, and has children. Those children, born of what is in fact an adulterous union, would be {\i mamzerim}. The {\i takana} prevents this by blocking the husband\u8217's ability to cancel the {\i shlichus} altogether.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Agunot Concern\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Resh Lakish} disagrees and says: {\i mipnei takkanat agunot} \u8212- to prevent {\i agunot}. He holds like {\i Rav Sheshet}, that cancellation must take place before three {\i dayanim}, and a formal court of three does generate a {\i kol} \u8212- {\i be-tlata it lehu kala} \u8212- a public voice sufficient to ensure that the wife hears of the cancellation before acting on the {\i get}. Given that, the problem is not that she will remarry and produce {\i mamzerim}; she will hear about it and refrain. The real danger, in {\i Resh Lakish}\u8217's view, is the opposite: the husband, knowing that the court\u8217's announcement will reach her, will use the threat of cancellation to hold her hostage indefinitely. She cannot remarry even though she wishes to, and she remains an {\i aguna}. The {\i takana}, on this reading, is designed to protect women from being permanently chained.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This {\i machloket} recalls the broader tension in {\i Ketubot} between the concern for {\i tznuot} \u8212- women who are scrupulously careful about their marital status and may therefore wait indefinitely \u8212- and {\i prutzot} \u8212- women who may act on an invalid {\i get} and inadvertently commit adultery. {\i Rabbi Yochanan} is concerned with the second scenario; {\i Resh Lakish} with the first.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kol & Public Knowledge\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} points out that the two positions are not simply in tension; they are logically nested. According to {\i Rabbi Yochanan}, even if the {\i takana} had merely changed the requirement from two witnesses to three, there would still be a concern \u8212- because even a court of three may not generate a {\i kol} sufficient to inform the wife in all cases. {\i Rabban Gamliel} therefore needed a two-stage response: first moving from two to three, and then eliminating the option entirely. The {\i takana} was not a minor procedural adjustment but a complete revocation of the husband\u8217's power to cancel.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A second {\i Tosafot}, on the phrase {\i be-tlata it lehu kala}, examines the broader assumption that three people sitting as a court create an effective public announcement. The suggestion is that the formality of a court proceeding \u8212- as distinct from a casual statement before two witnesses \u8212- is what generates the {\i kol}. {\i Tosafot} notes that in other areas of {\i halacha}, two witnesses are generally sufficient to establish public knowledge, and so the precise threshold at which something becomes widely known enough to protect the wife remains a matter of some delicacy.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Bitulo Mevutal\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi\u8217's Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now presents a {\i baraita} containing two separate {\i machlokot} between {\i Rabbi} (Judah the Prince) and {\i Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel}. The first: {\i bitalo mevutal, divrei Rebbe} \u8212- if the husband cancelled the {\i shaliach} [after {\i Rabban Gamliel}\u8217's {\i takana}], the cancellation nonetheless works, according to {\i Rabbi}. Even though the husband is forbidden from cancelling the {\i shlichus} under {\i Rabban Gamliel}\u8217's {\i takana}, {\i Rabbi} holds that if he did so, the cancellation is legally effective. This is an application of the general question \u8212- {\i kol mah de\u8217'asrah rachmana, ee avid mehani ee lo mehani} \u8212- whether an act that is prohibited nonetheless takes legal effect when performed. In some contexts, the act works despite being forbidden; in others, the prohibition also nullifies the act. {\i Rabbi} holds that here the cancellation falls into the first category: forbidden but effective.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabban Shimon\u8217's Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel} disagrees on two points. First, the husband cannot cancel the {\i shaliach} \u8212- and second, he cannot extend a {\i tnai} attached to the {\i get}. If the husband gave a {\i get} conditional upon some event within thirty days and then, on the twenty-ninth day, extended the condition for another month, this would effectively create a perpetual {\i tnai}, circumventing the requirement of {\i kritut} \u8212- the complete severing of the marital bond. While a finite {\i tnai} such as \u8220"on condition that you do not drink wine for thirty days\u8221" is valid \u8212- because at the expiration of the term the husband has no further control \u8212- indefinite extension undermines the entire structure of {\i kritut}. {\i Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel} therefore prohibits this mechanism as well.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 His overarching rationale is the principle of {\i mah koach beit din yafeh} \u8212- what then is the power of the court? If the husband can simply circumvent {\i Rabban Gamliel}\u8217's {\i takana} by going ahead and cancelling anyway, with the cancellation remaining legally effective, then the {\i takana} accomplishes nothing. The {\i beit din} must have the authority to make its enactments effective, not merely advisory.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} presses this position hard: {\i umi ika de-mide\u8217'oraita batel gita, u-mishum mah koach beit din yafeh sharina eshet ish le\u8217'alma?} \u8212- Is there really a case where the {\i get} is invalid at the {\i de\u8217'oraita} level and yet, in the name of {\i mah koach beit din yafeh}, we permit a married woman to remarry? The husband cancelled the {\i shlichus}; {\i de\u8217'oraita}, the {\i get} is void; this woman is still a married woman; and yet the rabbis step in and declare her permitted? The {\i gemara} answers: yes \u8212- and this is the striking principle at the heart of the {\i sugya}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbinic Authority\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara}\u8217's answer is: {\i kol de-mekadesh ada\u8217'ata de-rabbanan mekadesh, ve-afke\u8217'inhu rabbanan le-kiddushin minei} \u8212- whoever gets married, marries on the basis of the rabbis\u8217' authority, and the rabbis therefore have the power to retroactively annul the {\i kiddushin}. This is a foundational principle, and a consequential one. Marriage is not simply a private affair between two individuals; it is a legal status change that takes place within the framework of a society governed by {\i halacha}. The {\i kiddushin} formula itself encodes this: {\i harei at mekudeshet li be-taba\u8217'at zu ke-dat Moshe ve-Yisrael} \u8212- according to Mosaic law and according to {\i dat Yisrael}, which includes the {\i halacha} as it exists at the time of the marriage, including rabbinic enactments. It is thus implicit in every marriage that it is contracted under, and subject to, the authority of {\i chazal}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kiddushin Types\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} records {\i Ravina} posing a sharp question to {\i Rav Ashi}: {\i tenah de-kadesh be-kaspa} \u8212- this principle makes sense when a man betroths a woman with money, i.e., {\i kiddushei kesef}, such as a ring. Why? Because most {\i rishonim} invoke the principle of {\i hefker beit din hefker} \u8212- the court can declare property ownerless \u8212- and therefore the rabbis can retroactively nullify the monetary value used to effect the {\i kiddushin}, thereby undoing the {\i kinyan} at its root. But {\i kadish be-viah mai ika le-meimar} \u8212- what of {\i kiddushei biah}, one of the three forms of {\i kiddushin} recognized in the first {\i mishna} of {\i Kiddushin}, whereby marriage is effected through cohabitation? How can the rabbis retroactively declare that a sexual act did not effect {\i kiddushin}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rav Ashi} responds: {\i shavveiyeh rabbanan le-beilato beilat zenut} \u8212- the rabbis recast that sexual act not as {\i kinyan kiddushin} but as mere {\i beilat zenut}, a licentious act. They strip it of its marital legal weight entirely. The mechanism is the following: there is a {\i chazakah} \u8212- a legal presumption \u8212- that {\i ein adam oseh beilato beilat zenut}, a man does not engage in cohabitation as a mere promiscuous act; he presumably intends it within a marital framework. The rabbis can reverse that {\i chazakah} and declare the opposite \u8212- that it was {\i beilat zenut} after all \u8212- and with that, the {\i kiddushin} falls away.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is worth noting in this context that {\i Rav} famously placed a {\i cherem} on {\i kiddushei biah} because of its impropriety, which raises its own question: to what extent did the rabbis effectively close off this avenue of {\i kiddushin} entirely through their enactments, even though it is one of the three modes listed in the {\i mishna}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rashi\u8217's Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 There is a lengthy and important {\i Rashi} on this page that deserves careful attention. {\i Rashi} cites his rabbis \u8212- the German scholars in whose {\i yeshivot} in Mainz and Worms he studied \u8212- who explain the principle of {\i kol de-mekadesh ada\u8217'ata de-rabbanan mekadesh} as follows: {\i de-kiddushei kesef de-rabbanan} \u8212- marriage by money is only a rabbinic institution to begin with. On this view, {\i kiddushei kesef} is not {\i de\u8217'oraita}, and therefore the rabbis\u8217' power to annul it is straightforward. The reason they give is that {\i kiddushei kesef} is derived through a {\i gezeirah shavah}, and a {\i gezeirah shavah} does not necessarily carry {\i de\u8217'oraita} force.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rashi} disagrees sharply and directly. He writes \u8212- and this is one of his characteristic moves in the commentary, where he explicitly parts ways with his teachers \u8212- that a {\i gezeirah shavah} is always {\i de\u8217'oraita}. He does not know what the basis for their view could be.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is worth noting that the {\i Rambam} in {\i Hilchot Ishut} presents a related gradation. In at least some textual versions, the {\i Rambam} states that {\i kiddushei biah} and {\i kiddushei shtar} are {\i min ha-Torah}, while {\i kiddushei kesef} is {\i mi-divrei soferim}. The {\i Rambam}\u8217's category of {\i mi-divrei soferim} occupies a middle zone in his system \u8212- learned through {\i drash}, but not explicitly stated in the {\i Torah}, and therefore not categorized as {\i de\u8217'oraita} in the strict sense of the {\i Rambam}\u8217's taxonomy. The {\i Kesef Mishneh} has an important comment on this {\i Rambam} as well. This places the {\i Rambam} in a position somewhat related to, though not identical with, that of {\i Rashi}\u8217's rabbis \u8212- and the debate between {\i Rashi} and his teachers over the {\i de\u8217'oraita} status of {\i kiddushei kesef} is directly relevant to interpreting the {\i Rambam}\u8217's ruling.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Multiple Messengers\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ten Messengers\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i baraita} presents the second {\i machloket} between {\i Rabbi} and {\i Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel}. If a husband said to ten people, {\i amar l\u8217'asara kitvu get l\u8217'ishti} \u8212- write a {\i get} for my wife \u8212- in practice this means two of them will serve as witnesses and one will write, with the others largely superfluous to the formal process. Now, can the husband go to some of them \u8212- say, two of them, without the other eight present \u8212- and cancel the {\i shlichus}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi} says yes: {\i yachol le-vatel zeh shelo bifnei zeh} \u8212- he can cancel one without the other knowing. {\i Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel} says no: {\i eino yachol le-vatel ela zeh bifnei zeh} \u8212- he can only cancel in the presence of all of them together.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Witness Grouping\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} asks: {\i be-mai kamipalgi?} \u8212- what is the underlying {\i machloket}? The answer: they dispute the principle of {\i eidut she-batla miktzata batla kula} \u8212- if part of a group of witnesses is cancelled, is the entire group cancelled?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi} holds {\i eidut she-batla miktzata lo batla kula} \u8212- cancelling two of the ten does not cancel the mandate of the other eight. If so, telling two that their {\i shlichus} is revoked does not prevent the other eight from proceeding. And therefore {\i Rabbi} permits it: it is not harmful to the process of giving the {\i get}, so there is no reason to prohibit it. {\i Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel}, conversely, holds {\i eidut she-batla miktzata batla kula} \u8212- if two of the ten are cancelled, all ten are cancelled. Therefore, going to two of them and telling them the {\i shlichus} is revoked effectively voids the mandate of all ten. This is dangerous: the other eight do not know their mandate has been voided, and they will proceed to write and deliver the {\i get}, which is now invalid. Thus {\i Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel} forbids it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Formality Principle\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 An alternative reading is offered: {\i ve-iba\u8217'it eima, de-kulei alma eidut she-batla miktzata lo batla kula} \u8212- perhaps everyone agrees that cancelling part of the group does not cancel the whole. {\i Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel}\u8217's objection, on this reading, stems from a different principle entirely: {\i kasavar milta de-misavda be-apei asara tzricha be-asara le-mishlafa} \u8212- anything that was done in front of ten people requires the full ten to be present in order to undo it. This is a formality requirement: when a mandate is given to a group, it can only be revoked in front of that entire group. This is not about what will happen if the revocation is partial; it is about the inherent legal incapacity of a partial revocation to take effect at all.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} points out a striking consequence of this reading. Even the two to whom the husband privately declared the revocation are not actually revoked \u8212- because the announcement was not made before all ten. They remain bound by their mandate and may still proceed to write the {\i get}. This is a significant {\i chiddush}, and {\i Tosafot} highlights it explicitly.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two Messengers\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then raises the question of the case where the husband said {\i kulchem} \u8212- \u8220"all of you.\u8221" In the earlier case he instructed ten people and left open which of them would actually serve as witnesses and writer. But if he specified {\i kulchem kitvu get le-ishti} \u8212- \u8220"all of you write a {\i get} for my wife\u8221" \u8212- meaning all ten must sign, the analysis shifts.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If {\i Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel}\u8217's reason is {\i eidut she-batla miktzata batla kula}, then in the {\i kulchem} case \u8212- where the entire group must function as a unified body \u8212- it would be even clearer that cancelling two of them cancels the entire enterprise, since the eight remaining cannot constitute the complete group the husband specified. But if {\i Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel}\u8217's reason is the formality principle \u8212- that a group mandate requires a group revocation \u8212- then the {\i kulchem} case and the original case are equally governed by that rule.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i nafka mina} between the two readings becomes apparent: according to the first reading, {\i Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel}\u8217's ruling in the {\i kulchem} case would rest on the same {\i eidut} logic, whereas according to the second reading, the formality principle applies regardless of whether the husband said {\i asara} or {\i kulchem}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A different formulation enters the analysis as well. The {\i gemara} asks: what if the husband appointed only two {\i shluchim} \u8212- not ten \u8212- and said {\i tenu get le-ishti}? Can he cancel one without the other present? {\i Rabbi} says yes; {\i Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel} says no, only {\i zeh bifnei zeh}. The {\i gemara} asks: are two {\i shluchim} comparable to the {\i kulchem} case of ten? They are somewhat analogous, but the analysis diverges \u8212- as {\i Rav Ashi} notes \u8212- depending on whether we are speaking of {\i edei holacha} (witnesses for the delivery of the {\i get}) or {\i edei ketiva} (witnesses involved in writing the {\i get}). Witnesses who sign the {\i get} must function as a group and satisfy the requirement that {\i ein eidutan mitztarefet ad she-yiru shnayim ke-echad} \u8212- their testimony only combines when both witnesses perceive the act together. {\i Shluchim} who merely deliver the {\i get}, on the other hand, need not function as a group in the same way, and cancelling one independently of the other is therefore less problematic.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shmuel\u8217's Uncertainty\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Shmuel bar Yehuda} reports: {\i shmi\u8217'a li minei de-Rabbi Abba tartei chad tartei} \u8212- I heard from {\i Rabbi Abba} [that the {\i halacha} is ruled] two ways, one like {\i Rabbi} and one like {\i Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel} \u8212- {\i chada ke-Rebbi ve-chada ke-Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel}. But {\i ve-lo yadana hai ke-Rebbi ve-hai ke-Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel} \u8212- he does not know which of the two {\i machlokot} is ruled like which {\i amora}. This uncertainty sets up the resolution that follows.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Halachic Resolution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi\u8217's Retraction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rav Yosef} introduces a crucial piece of evidence: when {\i Rav Dimi} came from {\i Eretz Yisrael}, he reported that there had been a case in which {\i Rabbi} retracted his opinion and ruled like {\i chachamim}. The {\i chachamim}\u8217's position, as explained by {\i Rashi}, concerns the case of {\i shum ha-dayanim} \u8212- a court\u8217's assessment of property value for purposes of sale. If the judges over-estimated or under-estimated by more than a sixth \u8212- more than 120% or less than 80% of market value \u8212- the ruling of {\i chachamim} is {\i michran batel}, the sale is cancelled. This implies that the court\u8217's assessment, even when erroneous, is not shielded by the principle of {\i mah koach beit din yafeh} in that context. {\i Rabbi} originally held that {\i mah koach beit din yafeh} should protect the court\u8217's ruling even when mistaken, but he retracted and agreed with {\i chachamim} that in a case of significant error the sale is voided.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Prata}, son of {\i Rabbi Elazar ben Prata}, grandson of {\i Rabbi Prata haGadol}, challenged this retraction: {\i im kein mah koach beit din yafeh} \u8212- if the sale is cancelled even when the court made the error, what power does the court retain? To this, {\i Rabbi} responded by reversing to the position of {\i Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel} \u8212- affirming that {\i mah koach beit din yafeh} is indeed a weighty principle \u8212- {\i de-chazar Rebbi ve-assak ke-Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The implication for our {\i sugya} is this: since {\i Rabbi} ultimately embraced {\i mah koach beit din yafeh} as a guiding principle, he must have held accordingly in the first {\i machloket} \u8212- i.e., that the husband cannot cancel the {\i shaliach} even after the {\i takana}, aligning with {\i Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel}. Consequently, in the second {\i machloket} \u8212- concerning the ten messengers \u8212- {\i Rabbi} must have reverted to his own original position, ruling that {\i yachol le-vatel zeh shelo bifnei zeh}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rav Yosef} also cites {\i Rabbi Oshaya} and {\i Rabbi Yehoshua min Usha} as holding one {\i machloket} like {\i Rabbi} and one like {\i Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel}, though each is uncertain which ruling goes which way.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Forced Get Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then introduces a remarkable incident. Five {\i sages} were sitting before {\i Rabbi Yehoshua min Usha}, and a man was brought before them and compelled to give a {\i get} against his will \u8212- {\i ashkley gita al korchei}. This invokes the {\i halachic} mechanism of {\i kefauhu ad she\u8217'yomar rotzeh ani} \u8212- the court coerces the husband until he declares his consent, a power that {\i batei din} exercised in certain circumstances in that era, though not in ours. Once the husband was compelled to appoint two witnesses to write and deliver the {\i get}, the court instructed them: {\i zilu ve-itmeru ve-kitvu lah} \u8212- go, hide, and write the {\i get} for her. The concern was that the husband, having been coerced, would try to track down the witnesses after the court proceeding and cancel the {\i shlichus} before they delivered the {\i get}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is a telling detail: the court\u8217's instruction to the witnesses to hide makes sense only if the husband retains the power to cancel the {\i shlichus} by approaching the witnesses directly. If the husband had no power whatsoever to cancel \u8212- which would be the case if we held like {\i Rabban Gamliel}\u8217's {\i takana} applies even to the cancellation-before-witnesses scenario \u8212- hiding would be unnecessary. The husband could simply cancel by appearing before a different court. Thus the instruction to hide tells us something about how this court understood the mechanism of cancellation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Case Inference\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} draws the inference: {\i ve-i salkha da\u8217'atach ke-Rabbi, ki mitmarei mai havvei?} \u8212- If we held like {\i Rabbi} that the husband can cancel even after {\i Rabban Gamliel}\u8217's {\i takana}, why would hiding help? The husband could simply convene two other people and cancel the {\i shaliach} before them. The fact that hiding was the prescribed remedy implies that we must be holding like {\i Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel} in this context \u8212- that the husband cannot cancel the {\i shlichus} at all after the {\i takana}. Since the cancellation itself is blocked, the only way the husband could interfere is by physically locating the witnesses and cancelling in their presence; hiding prevents that.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But then, if {\i Rabbi Yehoshua min Usha} held like {\i Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel} in the first {\i machloket}, he must have held like {\i Rabbi} in the second: {\i shema mina chada ke-Rebbi ve-chada ke-Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel}. This confirms the report of {\i Shmuel bar Yehuda} and resolves his uncertainty \u8212- at least as to which position {\i Rabbi Yehoshua min Usha} held.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava\u8217's Ruling\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rava} now delivers the authoritative ruling: {\i halacha ke-Rabbi bi-shteihen} \u8212- the {\i halacha} follows {\i Rabbi} in both cases. First, even after {\i Rabban Gamliel}\u8217's {\i takana}, if the husband cancelled the {\i shlichus}, the cancellation is effective ({\i bitalo mevutal}). Second, if the husband appointed ten messengers and cancelled before two of them without the others present, that cancellation is effective as well.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This ruling directly overrules the analysis attributing one {\i machloket} to {\i Rabbi} and one to {\i Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel}. {\i Rava} also specifies that {\i Rav Nachman} does not hold {\i mah koach beit din yafeh} in this context \u8212- which is why he rules consistently like {\i Rabbi}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Monetary vs.\u160 ?Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But this immediately raises a contradiction: {\i Rav Nachman} himself stated in the name of {\i Shmuel} that in a case of {\i yetomim} who come to divide their father\u8217's estate, the {\i beit din} appoints an {\i apitropus} for each of them and selects optimal portions on their behalf \u8212- {\i borin lahem chelek yafeh} \u8212- and {\i Rav Nachman} himself ruled that once the {\i yetomim} reach adulthood they cannot contest the division: {\i higdilu ein yecholim limchot}. This implies that {\i Rav Nachman} does hold {\i mah koach beit din yafeh} in monetary matters \u8212- because otherwise why would the court\u8217's assessment be binding on the adult orphans?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} resolves: {\i hatam mamona, hacha issura} \u8212- there is a fundamental distinction between monetary cases and cases of {\i issurim}, matters of ritual prohibition and personal status. {\i Rav Nachman} accepts {\i mah koach beit din yafeh} in purely monetary contexts, where it is the court\u8217's role to manage and adjudicate property affairs with finality. But in matters of {\i issurim} \u8212- including marital status, which is governed not merely by convention and mutual agreement but by the direct laws of the {\i Torah} \u8212- {\i mah koach beit din yafeh} does not override the underlying {\i de\u8217'oraita} reality.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rambam\u8217's Ruling\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i sugya} concludes with a notable observation about the {\i Rambam}. The {\i Ran} on the {\i Rif} points out that on the one hand, the {\i Rambam} rules like {\i Rabbi} in both {\i machlokot} \u8212- consistent with {\i Rava}\u8217's conclusion. Yet in the case of {\i shlichus she-batla miktzata batla kula} \u8212- the parallel principle to {\i eidut she-batla miktzata batla kula} but applied to {\i shlichut} rather than testimony \u8212- the {\i Rambam} rules against {\i Rabbi} and in accordance with {\i Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel}. This appears to contradict the {\i gemara}\u8217's explicit statement: {\i halacha ke-Rabbi bi-shteihen}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Ran} resolves this by drawing a principled distinction between {\i eidus} and {\i shlichut}. The {\i gemara}\u8217's ruling \u8212- {\i halacha ke-Rabbi bi-shteihen} \u8212- addresses the {\i machloket} as it applies to testimony: {\i eidut she-batla miktzata la batla kula}. But the same principle does not automatically carry over to {\i shlichut}. {\i Shlichus she-batla miktzata lo batla kula} \u8212- cancelling one {\i shaliach} out of two does not cancel the other \u8212- is a separate proposition, and the {\i Rambam} holds that by {\i shlichut}, the parallel principle does not apply in the same way.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Ran} leaves this as a question without fully resolving the conceptual distinction, but it is possible to suggest one. A {\i shaliach} can operate as a {\i yachid} \u8212- a single individual agent. There is nothing in the nature of {\i shlichut} that requires two agents to function as a unified group. {\i Eidut}, by contrast, is structurally a group enterprise: the {\i halacha} of testimony requires that witnesses act together \u8212- {\i ein eidutan mitztarefet ad she-yiru shnayim ke-echad} \u8212- and the principle {\i eidut she-batla miktzata batla kula} flows naturally from that structural requirement. Since the two witnesses constitute a single composite unit of testimony, breaking part of that unit breaks the whole. {\i Shlichut} carries no equivalent structural requirement of group unity, and therefore the same principle does not follow. The {\i Ran} leaves the question hanging, but this conceptual distinction \u8212- that {\i eidus} demands collective integrity in a way that {\i shlichut} does not \u8212- offers a coherent basis for the {\i Rambam}\u8217's position.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Gittin 34\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Giddul & Gilluy Da\u8217'as\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Giddul\u8217's Regret\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The daf opens six lines from the top of 34a, within the broader context of the {\i mishna}\u8217's discussion of a husband\u8217's ability to be {\i mevatel} a {\i get} before it reaches his wife. Recall that {\i Rabban Gamliel Hazaken} had already reformed the older practice of nullifying a {\i get} without the wife\u8217's knowledge, as part of {\i tikkun olam}. The {\i gemara} now introduces a narrative that will occasion a major {\i machloket} between Abaye and Rava.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Giddul bar I\u8217'lai shadru lei gita l\u8217'devitehu} \u8212- Giddul bar I\u8217'lai sent a {\i get} to his wife. The {\i shliach} arrived and found her sitting and weaving. He said: here is your {\i get}. She responded: go for now, come back tomorrow. The {\i shliach} obliged \u8212- though he was not technically required to, since a husband may divorce his wife against her will \u8212- and returned to Giddul bar I\u8217'lai to report what had happened. At that point, Giddul bar I\u8217'lai broke into the blessing {\i Baruch Hatov Vehametiv}, an expression of gratitude \u8212- transparently indicating his relief that the {\i get} had not yet been delivered and his clear regret at having sent it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Abaye: No Gilluy\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Abaye amar Baruch Hatov Vehametiv v\u8217'lo batil gita} \u8212- Abaye holds that despite this unambiguous expression of remorse, the {\i get} was not nullified. If the {\i shliach} were to proceed and deliver the {\i get}, it would still be fully effective. Abaye\u8217's formulation speaks of {\i bitul} of the {\i get} itself, though Rashi frames the analysis more in terms of {\i bitul} of the {\i shlichut}, the agency. Either way, for Abaye, both the {\i get} and the {\i shlichut} remain operative.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava: Gilluy Works\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rava amar Baruch Hatov Vehametiv u\u8217'batil gita} \u8212- Rava disagrees. Once Giddul bar I\u8217'lai\u8217's expressed delight makes his intent manifest, the {\i get} is {\i batel}. Rashi again emphasizes that it is primarily the {\i shlichut} that is {\i batel}, with the implication for the {\i get} being that if one wished to proceed with a divorce, a new {\i get} would be required.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 The Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Bemai kamiflagi? Begilui da\u8217'ata begita kamiflagi} \u8212- the {\i gemara} identifies the crux: the dispute concerns whether {\i gilui da\u8217'as} \u8212- the public revelation of one\u8217's intent \u8212- is legally operative in the context of a {\i get}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 There is no ambiguity about Giddul bar I\u8217'lai\u8217's {\i da\u8217'as}. The question is whether demonstrating desire, without a formal speech act of revocation, carries legal weight. Abaye holds: {\i gilui da\u8217'as begita lav milta hi} \u8212- it is of no legal consequence. From a strictly formal legal standpoint, this is compelling: one must execute an actual act of {\i bitul} of the {\i get} and revoke the {\i shlichut} through a proper verbal declaration. The mere revelation of intent, however transparent, leaves everything in place.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava counters: {\i gilui da\u8217'as begita milta hi} \u8212- once the {\i shliach} can no longer be said to be acting on the husband\u8217's behalf, because the husband\u8217's contrary wishes are plain, the {\i shlichut} is dissolved.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rav Sheshet Proof\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Forced Divorce\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava now offers a proof from a case involving Rav Sheshet. Rav Sheshet compelled a man to divorce his wife against his will \u8212- a practice recognized as legitimate when carried out through a {\i beis din} with proper grounds, even when the husband\u8217's compliance is extracted under duress. The man submitted under pressure, saying the requisite words of consent and directing witnesses to proceed with the {\i get}. Thereafter, however, he turned to the witnesses and declared: {\i hachi amar lacho Rav Sheshet l\u8217'vatulei gita} \u8212- so said Rav Sheshet to you, that the {\i get} should be nullified. And Rav Sheshet then found himself needing to produce a new {\i get} \u8212- implying that the first had somehow been undermined.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava\u8217's Inference\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava\u8217's argument is that the man\u8217's statement \u8212- though patently false, since Rav Sheshet had said no such thing and lacks the authority to nullify someone else\u8217's {\i get} \u8212- nonetheless served to manifest the man\u8217's lack of genuine desire to divorce. It was a declaration of intent, not a formally valid act of {\i bitul}. And yet it apparently sufficed to create a problem with the {\i get}, requiring a second one. This is Rava\u8217's evidence that {\i gilui da\u8217'as} is operative.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Abaye\u8217's Rebuttal\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Active Bitul\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye responds with a complete reinterpretation of the case. {\i Atu Rav Sheshet mevatel gitta de\u8217'inshei havae?} \u8212- Rav Sheshet is certainly not in a position to nullify someone else\u8217's {\i get}. An ordinary person might not appreciate this \u8212- especially someone facing a {\i beis din} \u8212- but logically the story cannot be what Rava says it is. Abaye\u8217's reading is that the man actually did perform an act of {\i bitul}, a genuine formal revocation of the {\i get} after he had directed the witnesses to proceed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Lashes Defense\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The reason the man invoked Rav Sheshet\u8217's name was simply a cover story. The {\i beis din}\u8217's officers were lashing him for attempting to nullify the {\i get} he had been compelled to give. Under the pain of those lashes, he gave a pretextual excuse \u8212- he falsely claimed that Rav Sheshet had authorized the {\i bitul} \u8212- not because the {\i bitul} consisted of that claim, but because he needed a reason to make the lashing stop. In Abaye\u8217's account, the {\i gilui da\u8217'as} was incidental; the {\i bitul} was an actual formal act. The need for a second {\i get} is therefore no evidence that {\i gilui da\u8217'as} alone sufficed.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Plugged Ears\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Forced Get\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye now marshals his own proof. The case involves Rav Yehuda, who sought to compel his son-in-law \u8212- the son-in-law of Rav Yirmiyah Birah \u8212- to divorce his wife. Each time Rav Yehuda applied sufficient pressure for the man to say he consented and direct the {\i sofer} and witnesses to write the {\i get}, the man would then formally be {\i mevatel} the {\i get} as soon as the pressure lifted. This happened repeatedly: {\i tana ashkaleih u\u8217'batlei, hadar tana v\u8217'ashkaleih al korchei} \u8212- again and again Rav Yehuda forced the declaration, and again and again the man performed {\i bitul}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Finally, Rav Yehuda directed the witnesses: {\i otivu karrei b\u8217'oznaichu} \u8212- put a gourd in your ears. Write the {\i get} and ensure you are not in a position to hear him issue any further revocation directed at you, the persons charged with writing and delivering it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Formal Act\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye\u8217's inference is precise and elegant. The instruction to plug the witnesses\u8217' ears only makes sense if one holds that {\i bitul} or revocation of {\i shlichut} requires a formal speech act directed at those authorized to act. If {\i gilui da\u8217'as} sufficed \u8212- if merely demonstrating one\u8217's intent were enough \u8212- then plugging their ears would be useless. As the {\i gemara} states: {\i ve\u8217'ha chazu leih d\u8217'ka rahi batraihu} \u8212- they can plainly see him chasing after them, making every effort to communicate his desire to retract. His intent is manifest even without words. If {\i gilui da\u8217'as} worked, all of that visible, frantic effort would already constitute {\i bitul}. The only reason the plugged ears accomplish anything is if formal, audible speech is required \u8212- precisely Abaye\u8217's position.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Conditional Gets\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava\u8217's Counter\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava responds to the plugged-ears proof. He argues that one may reinterpret what the man was shouting as he ran after the witnesses. Perhaps he was not attempting {\i bitul} at all \u8212- perhaps he was calling out: {\i ashur havu lah} \u8212- go and give it to her quickly, already, so that I can be done with this ordeal. Once the witnesses\u8217' ears are stopped up, Rava argues, we are free to reinterpret ambiguous behavior. The man is running \u8212- perhaps out of urgency to complete the divorce, not to abort it. In Rava\u8217's view, {\i gilui da\u8217'as} must genuinely manifest the relevant intent, and under these circumstances that manifestation is contestable.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava\u8217's position is thus nuanced: he does not say {\i gilui da\u8217'as} is always sufficient regardless of context. He says it suffices when the intent is genuinely and unambiguously revealed \u8212- but the circumstances may sometimes allow for alternative readings that prevent {\i gilui da\u8217'as} from being established.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ferry Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye is not yet finished pressing his position. A man gave a {\i get} to his wife on a {\i tenai}, a condition: {\i ee lo ateina ad tlatin yomei leihavei gitta} \u8212- if I do not return within thirty days, let it be a {\i get}. This was perhaps motivated by concern that he might perish on a journey with no witnesses to testify, leaving his wife an {\i agunah}. The thirty days elapsed; the man actually did return and was attempting to cross the river back to the city. But the ferry was on the opposite bank, and by the time it could have reached him, the thirty-day limit would have passed. He stood at the water\u8217's edge, clearly within earshot, and called out: {\i chazu de\u8217'asai chazu de\u8217'asai} \u8212- look, I have arrived, I have come back!\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shmuel Ruling\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Lav shemei matya} \u8212- Shmuel rules that this does not count as having returned. The condition was not met; the {\i get} takes effect. This is a striking ruling, because the man\u8217's desire that the {\i get} not take effect could not be more apparent. He is standing at the riverbank, calling out, clearly demonstrating with everything at his disposal that he does not wish to be divorced. And yet the {\i get} is effective.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye draws his inference: even this transparent {\i gilui da\u8217'as} is insufficient to prevent the {\i get} from taking effect. Why, then, does it not constitute {\i bitul} according to Rava?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava\u8217's response, however, is subtle. {\i Atu hasam levitulei gitta baei? Hasam lekiyumei tnaia ka baei} \u8212- he is not attempting {\i bitul} of the {\i get}. He is attempting to argue that he fulfilled his {\i tenai}, that the condition of the {\i get} was satisfied on his end. His {\i gilui da\u8217'as} is not focused on the {\i get} itself; it is focused on the factual question of whether he returned. Moreover \u8212- and this is analytically significant \u8212- there is no {\i shlichut} in play here. He gave the {\i get} thirty days ago with a condition attached. There is no agent whose authority can be revoked. The only question is whether the condition was met, and the only mechanism by which {\i gilui da\u8217'as} could operate would be through an actual {\i bitul} of the {\i get} as an object. But his {\i gilui da\u8217'as} is not directed at the {\i get}; it is directed at the {\i tenai}. And even if one were to say that Rava holds {\i gilui da\u8217'as} suffices, it would have to be {\i gilui da\u8217'as} of an intent to perform {\i bitul} \u8212- which is not what is happening here.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Nissuin Condition\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Another case: a man gave a {\i get} to his betrothed \u8212- there being {\i kiddushin} already in place \u8212- on the condition that if they did not proceed to {\i nissuin} within thirty days, the {\i get} would take effect. When thirty days arrived without {\i nissuin} having been completed, he claimed to be working on it, trying to make the arrangements. Is that enough to stave off the effectiveness of the {\i get}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Debate Applied\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i L\u8217'mai neichush lah} \u8212- what legal theory could help him? {\i Ei mishum ones ein ones b\u8217'gittin} \u8212- if the argument is that he was unable to satisfy the condition through no fault of his own, there is a well-established ruling in a separate {\i sugya} that a claim of {\i ones} does not operate to excuse an unmet {\i tenai} in a {\i get}. So the condition is simply unfulfilled. As for {\i gilui da\u8217'as} \u8212- {\i plugta d\u8217'Abaye v\u8217'Rava} \u8212- that is precisely the dispute between Abaye and Rava. And yet here too, as the {\i gemara} notes, following Rava\u8217's logic in the ferry case, one could argue that even Rava would concede: there is no {\i shlichut} to revoke and there is no {\i gilui da\u8217'as} directed at a {\i bitul} of the {\i get} itself. His {\i gilui da\u8217'as} is about the {\i tenai}, not the {\i get}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Month Confusion\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A third case: a man similarly gave a {\i get} on condition that {\i nissuin} not occur by {\i Rosh Chodesh Adar}. When {\i Rosh Chodesh Adar} arrived without {\i nissuin}, he claimed: I said {\i Nissan}, not {\i Adar}. The witnesses clearly remembered he had said {\i Adar}; he was either lying or misremembering. In any event, it is manifest that he does not want the {\i get} to take effect.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Again the {\i gemara} asks: {\i l\u8217'mai neichush lah}? {\i Ei mishum ones ein ones b\u8217'gittin} \u8212- his inability or failure to satisfy the condition does not excuse him. {\i Ei mishum gilui da\u8217'as \u8212- plugta d\u8217'Abaye v\u8217'Rava} \u8212- and whether his clear manifest desire is legally operative is once again the debate. And as before, even granting Rava\u8217's position, the {\i gilui da\u8217'as} here is not a {\i gilui da\u8217'as} of {\i bitul}; there is neither {\i shlichut} to revoke nor a properly focused {\i gilui da\u8217'as} of the {\i get} itself.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Final Halacha\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ruled Like Abaye\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i V\u8217'hilcheta k\u8217'Nachman} \u8212- the {\i gemara} now states the {\i halacha} on several related matters. It rules like Rav Nachman on the earlier questions concerning whether {\i bitul} before two or three witnesses is required and whether a {\i bedi\u8217'eved} {\i bitul} performed in violation of {\i Rabban Gamliel}\u8217's {\i takanah} is nonetheless effective. And crucially: {\i v\u8217'hilcheta k\u8217'Nachmani} \u8212- the {\i halacha} follows Nachmani, which is a name for Abaye.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 We rule like Abaye: {\i gilui da\u8217'as begita lav milta hi}. Revealing one\u8217's intent is not legally sufficient to effect {\i bitul} of a {\i get} or to revoke a {\i shlichut}. An affirmative speech act of revocation is required. This is one of the exceptional cases in which the {\i halacha} follows Abaye against Rava, enumerated by the mnemonic {\i ya\u8217'al kagam} \u8212- and this is the {\i gimel} of {\i ya\u8217'al kagam}: {\i gilui da\u8217'as begita}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Nachmani Etymology\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Why was Abaye called Nachmani? Rashi, in the second line of his commentary here, explains: {\i nire b\u8217'einai d\u8217'al shem she-Rabbah bar Nachmani gidel Abaye b\u8217'veito v\u8217'limdo Torah} \u8212- it appears to him that Abaye was named after Rabbah bar Nachmani, who is otherwise referred to simply as {\i stam} Rabbah, because Rabbah raised the orphaned Abaye in his home and taught him Torah. Others reverse the relationship and suggest that Nachmani was actually Abaye\u8217's given name, but because it too closely resembled the name of Rabbah bar Nachmani \u8212- or Rabbah\u8217's own father \u8212- he was given the name Abaye instead. The {\i Maharsha} notes that the name {\i Abaye} carries a beautiful allusive quality as a {\i rashei teivos}: {\i asher b\u8217'cha yerucham yasom} \u8212- through You, O God, the orphan finds consolation. Either way, the {\i halacha} follows Abaye \u8212- Nachmani \u8212- that {\i gilui da\u8217'as begita} does not suffice.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Multiple Names\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabban Gamliel\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} transitions to the next {\i mishna}, still within the thematic context of {\i tikkun olam} in the world of {\i gittin}. {\i Berishona haya mishane shemo v\u8217'shma shem iro v\u8217'shem ira} \u8212- originally, a {\i get} might be written using a name or a city name that was not the primary, fully established designation: a secondary name, a name recognized in one locale but not another, a nickname, without full transparency about all the names by which a party was known.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Hitkin Rabban Gamliel Hazaken} \u8212- Rabban Gamliel the Elder enacted that the {\i get} should state: this man and any name that he has, and this woman and any name that she has \u8212- {\i mipnei tikkun olam}. This raises an important question that will recur throughout the {\i gemara}\u8217's analysis: does the phrase {\i v\u8217'chol shum sheyesh lo} mean that one writes those words formulaically \u8212- a standard rubric inserted in all {\i gittin} \u8212- or does it mean that one must actually enumerate and list each additional name explicitly? That question, as the {\i gemara} makes clear, remains open to significant debate among the {\i rishonim}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Regional Names\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Name Problem\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} provides the historical context for Rabban Gamliel\u8217's enactment. {\i Shalchu leih bnei medinas hayam l\u8217'Rabban Gamliel} \u8212- the people of overseas communities sent a question to Rabban Gamliel in {\i Eretz Yisrael}. The problem was this: {\i shemo Yosef v\u8217'korin lo Yochanan, Yochanan v\u8217'korin lo Yosef} \u8212- people emigrating from {\i Eretz Yisrael} were known by one name at home and an entirely different name in their new community. Perhaps they had adopted a local name; perhaps the names were used interchangeably in different social contexts. The question was pointed: {\i heichi megarshin nishoseihem?} \u8212- if such a man wished to send a {\i get} back to his wife in {\i Eretz Yisrael}, which name should be used \u8212- the name by which he is known where the {\i get} is written, or the name by which he is known where it will be received?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This question illuminates precisely what the {\i mishna} meant by its concern about changing or misusing names. The ambiguity was real and consequential.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Amar Rabban Gamliel v\u8217'hitkin} \u8212- Rabban Gamliel responded with his enactment: write the name and every other name, so that there is no ambiguity whatsoever. The question of which name takes priority \u8212- whether the primary name is determined by the location of writing or the location of delivery \u8212- is not resolved explicitly here; the {\i Tosafot} engage that question at length.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Ashi\u8217's Rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Amar Rav Ashi v\u8217'hu de-itchazak bitrei shemei} \u8212- Rav Ashi qualifies: the requirement applies only when it is {\i established} that a person goes by two different names. If only one name is known, the obligation to enumerate additional names does not apply.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might wonder what this qualification adds \u8212- after all, one cannot be expected to list names one does not know. The practical significance, as Rashi explains, lies in the {\i bedi\u8217'eved} question. If a man is known by only one name at the time the {\i get} is written and someone later comes forward to assert that he also goes by another name, the {\i get} already delivered is nonetheless {\i kosher bedi\u8217'eved}. The {\i takanah} \u8212- and the consequence of {\i bitul} \u8212- applies only when both names were already {\i established} at the time of writing.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This in turn raises a significant question that will resonate through the rest of the discussion: is the {\i bitul} a rabbinic consequence that {\i Chazal} imposed through Rabban Gamliel\u8217's enactment \u8212- meaning {\i Chazal} are exercising their authority to invalidate a {\i get} \u8212- or is the invalidity rooted {\i de-Oraita}, in that a {\i get} lacking sufficient identification is inherently defective, and Rabban Gamliel\u8217's enactment was simply designed to prevent that underlying problem? The answer has significant implications for how broadly the {\i bitul} applies.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Amar lei Rabbi Abba le-Rav Ashi} \u8212- Rabbi Abba confirmed to Rav Ashi that Rav Mari and Rav Elazar both hold in accordance with his view. And a {\i braita} is cited that supports Rav Ashi: {\i tanya kevathei de-Rav Ashi}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Two Wives Braita\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two-Region Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Hayu lo shetei nashim} \u8212- a man had two wives, one in Judea and one in Galilee. Furthermore, he had two names: one name by which he was known in Judea and another in Galilee. This is the case that makes the {\i bedi\u8217'eved} ruling explicit.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Both Names Required\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Gireish et ishah she-bi-Yehudah bishmo she-bi-Yehudah ve-et ishah she-ba-Galil bishmo she-ba-Galil \u8212- einah megureshet} \u8212- if he divorced his Judean wife using only his Judean name, or his Galilean wife using only his Galilean name, the {\i get} is invalid. The {\i braita} makes clear that to divorce his Judean wife, he must use his Judean name and include his Galilean name as well \u8212- {\i veshem de-Galil imo} \u8212- and vice versa for his Galilean wife. Failure to include both names produces a {\i batel get bedi\u8217'eved}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This {\i braita} establishes two important points. First, the {\i bitul bedi\u8217'eved} is real and operative when both names were established and only one was included. Second \u8212- and here the {\i Tosafot} comment at length \u8212- the default name, the one written first, appears to be determined by the place where the {\i get} is being delivered: where the wife lives. Divorcing the Judean wife, one begins with the Judean name; divorcing the Galilean wife, one begins with the Galilean name.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Third Place\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Yatza le-makom acher vegireish be-achad mei-hem megureshet} \u8212- now the {\i braita} introduces what appears to be a surprising ruling. If the same man leaves both Judea and Galilee and goes to a third place, and from there divorces one of his wives using only one of his names, the {\i get} is valid. This seems inconsistent. The same man, the same two names \u8212- why should his geographic location affect the validity of the {\i get}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Unknown Names\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Ela shema mina ha de-itchazek ha de-lo itchazek} \u8212- the {\i gemara} draws the inference: the distinction must rest entirely on whether the second name is {\i established} in the place of writing. In Judea, it is known that this man also goes by a Galilean name; in Galilee, his Judean name is known. In either case, writing only one name leaves a known gap. But if he travels to a third place \u8212- say, outside the land \u8212- that community knows him by only one name. His other name is not {\i established} there. Therefore, {\i bedi\u8217'eved}, the {\i get} is {\i kosher}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This reasoning powerfully suggests that the {\i bitul} is rabbinic in nature. If the problem were a genuine {\i de-Oraita} lack of identification, the man\u8217's geographic location would be irrelevant \u8212- the ambiguity would exist regardless of whether the community writing the {\i get} was aware of it. The fact that ignorance of the second name renders the {\i get} valid implies that the requirement is imposed by {\i Chazal}, and {\i Chazal} calibrated the {\i takanah} to circumstances where the omission is blameworthy. Where one cannot be blamed \u8212- because the second name is simply unknown \u8212- the {\i get} stands.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Majority Name\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Miriam vs Sarah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} concludes with a case of a different character: {\i hahi d\u8217'hava karu lah Miriam v\u8217'ukmusa Sarah} \u8212- a woman was known by two names, Miriam and Sarah, and both names were used in the same locale. This differs from the earlier cases involving different names in different geographic regions; here, the two names coexist in a single community.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Amar Nahardai: Miriam v\u8217'chol shum sheish lah v\u8217'lo Sarah v\u8217'chol shum sheish lah} \u8212- the sages of Nehardea rule that one writes Miriam \u8212- the majority name, the name by which she is predominantly known \u8212- as the primary name, and not Sarah. The logic is straightforward: the default name should be the one most widely recognized.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This ruling prompts further questions that the {\i gemara} does not fully resolve. The question of what happens {\i bedi\u8217'eved} if the names are reversed \u8212- if Sarah is written first and Miriam second \u8212- is left open. And importantly, this case extends Rabban Gamliel\u8217's {\i takanah} beyond the geographic scenario to the situation where a single person goes by multiple names in the same place. One must list all established names even absent any regional ambiguity.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Finally, the underlying structural question persists: does {\i v\u8217'chol shum sheish lah} mean that one writes those words as a formula, or that one actually lists each name by name? This question, raised at the outset of the {\i gemara}\u8217's discussion, threads through every case and remains a central point of dispute among the {\i rishonim}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Gittin 35\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Tikkun Olam Overview\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shift to General Tikkun\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The daf opens on Gittin 35b, and we now transition from {\i tikkun olam} rulings that were specifically connected to {\i gittin} to {\i tikkun olam} rulings of a more general character \u8212- improvements to the world that extend beyond the narrow context of divorce. The phrase {\i tikkun olam} has, of course, acquired broad connotations in other cultural contexts \u8212- universal social justice and the like \u8212- but as it has been used throughout this sugya, it carries a far more specific and halachic meaning: protecting people from legal abuse, or from situations where the halacha itself places them in untenable circumstances, particularly to protect women from having their {\i get} challenged improperly or from other related hardships.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Defining Tikkun Olam\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This raises a genuine conceptual question: who exactly is the {\i olam} being improved? In the cases we have seen, the {\i olam} is a fairly local one \u8212- primarily women who find themselves in precarious legal positions. The {\i tikkun} is about managing a specific societal reality and making the halachic system function in a way that doesn\u8217't leave people stranded.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tikkun Olam Examples\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} presents several examples. The first \u8212- which will be the focus of the {\i gemara} \u8212- is the rule that a widow cannot collect from the orphans\u8217' estate except by taking an oath. The second is the already-familiar rule that witnesses sign a {\i get} for the sake of {\i tikkun olam}, enabling the woman to remarry without producing the original witnesses \u8212- the position of Rabbi Eliezer that biblically no signatures were required, but rabbinically they were instituted to protect the woman. The third is Hillel\u8217's institution of the {\i pruzbul} for the sake of {\i tikkun olam}. Notably, the phrase {\i mipnei tikkun olam} does not explicitly appear alongside the widow\u8217's case \u8212- the {\i mishna} simply says {\i hitkin Rabban Gamliel} \u8212- while it appears explicitly by the witnesses and the {\i pruzbul}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Who Benefits from Tikkun\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The question of who constitutes the {\i olam} being addressed is worth pausing over. In one case it is widows; in another it is women being divorced; in the third it is the broader economic reality of lending and borrowing. In the final case, the {\i olam} encompasses the entire Jewish community\u8217's financial life. But in all cases, the {\i olam} remains the Jewish {\i olam} \u8212- it is the internal world of halachic society that is being ordered and improved.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Widow\u8217's Kesuba Collection\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 General Debt Collection\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} immediately challenges the formulation of the {\i mishna}. Why does the {\i mishna} specify that an {\i almana} \u8212- a widow \u8212- cannot collect from the orphans without an oath? This rule ought to apply universally: anyone who comes to collect a debt from orphans after the father has died must take an oath, since the father is no longer alive to verify that the debt was never repaid. Perhaps he trusted the creditor and did not demand a receipt; perhaps he paid and the receipt was lost. The general rule is well established: {\i haba lipara minechasei yesomim lo yipara ela bishvua} \u8212- one who comes to collect from orphan heirs does not collect without an oath. So why single out the widow?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishum China Doctrine\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} answers: {\i almana itztricha lei} \u8212- it was necessary to underscore that even the widow must take an oath, because one might have thought otherwise. The reasoning behind this assumption is the doctrine of {\i mishum china} \u8212- literally, finding favor. The idea is that because the {\i kesuba} was instituted to make marriage attractive and to protect the woman financially, one might have thought the rabbis would be lenient and not require her to take an oath, both to encourage women to marry with confidence that their financial interests would be protected, and to ensure the {\i kesuba} would genuinely function as a security. As Rashi explains: {\i mishum china sheyehei chen hanashim beeinei haanashim linasei lahem} \u8212- so that women would find favor in the eyes of men and be prepared to marry. Therefore, the {\i mishna} teaches us that notwithstanding this consideration, the oath requirement still applied to her \u8212- {\i ka mashma lan} \u8212- this leniency was not granted.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Dinar-Bread Narrative\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But then the situation changed. The {\i gemara} records that they stopped requiring the widow to take an oath \u8212- {\i nimne\u8217'u mil\u8217'hashbia} \u8212- because they became deeply concerned about the consequences of a false oath. The {\i gemara} introduces this concern through a famous story. Rav Kahana \u8212- and some say it was Rav Yehuda in the name of Rav \u8212- reports: there was an incident during years of famine in which a certain man deposited a gold {\i dinar} with a widow for safekeeping. She placed it in a pitcher of flour. She then forgot it was there, kneaded the dough, and baked it into bread. Given that it was a time of famine, she gave the loaf to a pauper. When the owner returned and asked for his coin, she could not produce it \u8212- she genuinely did not know where it had gone \u8212- and so she took what was intended as an oath: {\i yehaneh sam hamaves b\u8217'echad mibaneha shel osa isha im neheness midinaracha klum} \u8212- let poison take effect upon one of my children if I derived any benefit from your coin. A short time later, one of her children died.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 When the sages heard of this, they said: if someone who swore truthfully \u8212- or at least believed she was swearing truthfully \u8212- suffered such consequences, how much more severe would be the punishment of one who swears falsely with intent. This incident crystallized the communal recoil from the oath and led to the reluctance to impose it upon widows.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Benefit Analysis\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then probes: why was she punished at all, if she swore truthfully? The answer is {\i d\u8217'ishtareshi lah makom dinar} \u8212- she did in fact benefit from the coin, since the coin displaced a volume of flour in the bread, meaning she effectively gained that volume\u8217's worth of flour. But the irony compounds: she did not even eat the bread herself \u8212- she gave it to a pauper. What benefit did she derive? The {\i gemara} holds that her voluntary decision to give away the bread does not negate the fact that she used another person\u8217's property without authorization. The benefit was incidental, but it was real. And yet in her own mind, she was swearing entirely truthfully \u8212- she genuinely believed she had derived no benefit. The {\i gemara} addresses this by characterizing her as {\i k\u8217'mi shenishba be\u8217'emes} \u8212- as if she had sworn truthfully \u8212- meaning her subjective intention was entirely honest. And even so, the consequences were severe. Hence the sages\u8217' alarm: if this is what befalls one who swears honestly but is factually mistaken, what will befall one who swears with deliberate falsehood?\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Why Widows Uniquely at R\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Specificity Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now presses the obvious question: if the concern is about the weight of oaths and the danger of swearing falsely \u8212- even innocently \u8212- why is this uniquely a problem for the {\i almana}? Why do we continue to allow {\i gerushos} (divorced women) to take oaths, or indeed any creditor collecting from orphans? The concern about inadvertent false oaths should apply universally.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Widow\u8217's Rationalization\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} answers in the name of Rav Zeira citing Shmuel: {\i almana shani} \u8212- the widow is different. The reason is {\i debei hi na dekatarcha kmei dyasmei} \u8212- because she is there in the house, she exerts effort on behalf of the orphans. She is living in her late husband\u8217's home, cooking and cleaning, performing services for the orphans that no one is compensating her for. This creates the conditions for an almost unconscious rationalization: she may find a sum of money lying around and, having worked so hard without pay, tell herself she is entitled to take it as partial payment of her {\i kesuba}. She does not necessarily think of it as stealing \u8212- she genuinely believes she is owed this amount and that she is entitled to it. It is the kind of rationalization familiar from everyday life: the employee who takes home a notepad from the office because they have been working overtime without recognition.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Divorced vs.\u160 ?Widow\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gerusha}, by contrast, is not living in her former husband\u8217's home. She has no ongoing relationship with the estate, no daily proximity to the orphans, and no accumulating sense of uncompensated labor. There is therefore no analogous scenario in which she would take money and rationalize it as legitimate. Hence the concern is specific to the {\i almana}. The story of the {\i dinar} happened to involve a widow, but the widow\u8217's status was largely incidental to that narrative; the {\i ongoing} concern is structural and arises precisely from the widow\u8217's unique domestic situation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafot on Negligence\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot raises a pointed question based on a {\i gemara} in {\i Shevu\u8217'os} which states that an oath taken in good faith \u8212- believing oneself to be telling the truth \u8212- does not constitute a transgression, since the verse {\i lo yenakeh Hashem es asher yisa shemo lashav} applies only to those who take the name in vain, not to those who genuinely believe what they are saying. If so, why was the widow punished? Tosafot works through this at length and concludes: {\i veyesh lomar dehacha gabei pikadon haya lo lehizaher yoser leshomro heytev rauei lasukei adaitei shetavo lidei shvua} \u8212- here, since she was entrusted with the coin as a {\i pikadon} (a deposit), she bore a heightened responsibility to take adequate care of it. Because her error arose from negligence \u8212- she should have been more careful in tracking its location \u8212- the mistake cannot be considered entirely faultless, and therefore the normal exculpation for an unintentional false oath does not apply. The principle is significant: when one\u8217's false belief is itself the product of negligence, it is not treated with the same indulgence as a genuinely unavoidable error.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Oath Types & Contexts\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shift to Cherem\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot continues with a remark that opens onto a broader practice. He notes that in the present era, the practice has shifted further: {\i ulekach nimneu milehashbia bishvua meruba} \u8212- because the oath is so weighty, they have moved away from it, {\i ela behadara delo chamir kuli hai} \u8212- toward the {\i neder}, which is not as weighty. And then Tosafot adds: {\i vehashata bizman hazeh tiknu lekabel cherem bemakom shvua shelo yehei hamichshol kol kach} \u8212- in our times they have instituted taking a {\i cherem} (a conditional curse) in place of the {\i shvua}, so that the potential for catastrophic consequences is diminished while still maintaining a form of solemn attestation. This observation is important not only for the specific case of the widow but for the entire institution of testimony and sworn statements. The deep-seated recoil from taking a {\i shvua} \u8212- even a {\i shvuas emes}, a truthful oath \u8212- has been a feature of Jewish practice for centuries. The practical question of what a {\i beis din} actually administers today follows from this entire trajectory of concern.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shvua vs.\u160 ?Alah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 There is also a subtle distinction worth noting regarding the widow\u8217's statement in the story itself. A {\i shvua} proper is an invocation of God\u8217's name to attest to the truth of one\u8217's statement, anchored in the verse {\i lo yenakeh Hashem es asher yisa shemo lashav}. An {\i alah}, by contrast, is a conditional curse \u8212- \u8220"let such-and-such happen to me if I am lying\u8221" \u8212- which calls down consequences rather than calling God as witness. The woman\u8217's declaration in the story \u8212- {\i yehaneh sam hamaves b\u8217'echad mibaneha} \u8212- is linguistically an {\i alah}, not a {\i shvua}. It is possible, of course, that a proper {\i shvua} preceded it and is simply not quoted. But the distinction is interesting: the {\i gemara} treats it as a {\i shvua}, even though the precise language cited is that of an {\i alah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Oath Context Distinction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} introduces a further distinction regarding the differential weight of oaths depending on context. Rav Yehuda cites Rav and Shmuel as saying: {\i lo shanu ela b\u8217'beis din} \u8212- the restriction on making the widow take an oath applies only within {\i beis din}; {\i aval chutz leveis din mashbinan lah} \u8212- outside of {\i beis din}, she can be made to take an oath. Rashi explains the difference: a {\i beis din} oath is a Torah-level obligation, administered while holding a {\i sefer Torah} or {\i tefillin}, invoking God\u8217's name or an appellation thereof \u8212- as we find by Avraham who said {\i sim na yadecha tachas yerechi vehishvacha baShem} \u8212- and its consequences are correspondingly severe. Outside {\i beis din}, the oath is rabbinic in character: there is no holy object held, the formulation is more in the nature of an {\i arur} (a curse), and the penalty, while still real, is not as severe.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rav\u8217's Stringent Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav\u8217's Refusal\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now raises a difficulty with this framework. If outside {\i beis din} the widow can be made to take an oath, then why does the {\i gemara} report that Rav refused to collect the {\i kesuba} for a widow \u8212- {\i lo magbi kesuba l\u8217'armalta} \u8212- at all? If there exists a lower-stakes mechanism (the oath outside {\i beis din}) that could enable collection, why did Rav block collection entirely?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} initially treats this as a {\i kashya} \u8212- a genuine difficulty \u8212- against the version in which Rav himself stated that outside {\i beis din} an oath can be administered. It then presents an alternative version from {\i Neharde\u8217'a}: in {\i Sura} the teaching attributed the outside-{\i beis din} leniency to Rav; in {\i Neharde\u8217'a}, it is attributed exclusively to Shmuel. Under the {\i Neharde\u8217'a} version, Rav held a far more stringent position: {\i afilu chutz l\u8217'beis din nami ein mashbi\u8217'in osah} \u8212- even outside {\i beis din}, we do not make her take an oath. And Rav is then internally consistent \u8212- {\i Rav l\u8217'ta\u8217'ameih} \u8212- since he refused to collect the {\i kesuba} for widows altogether.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav\u8217's Concerns\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But then the question shifts: if Rav held that a {\i shvua} \u8212- whether in or out of {\i beis din} \u8212- cannot be administered to the widow, why didn\u8217't he simply follow the {\i mishna}\u8217's {\i takanah} of Rabban Gamliel and have her take a {\i neder} instead? The {\i gemara} answers: {\i bishnei d\u8217'Rav kili nidrei} \u8212- in the time of Rav, people treated {\i nedarim} lightly. A {\i neder} was no longer weighty enough to function as a reliable instrument of truth-telling. The {\i takanah} of Rabban Gamliel had depended on the {\i neder} occupying a middle ground \u8212- weighty enough to keep the woman honest, but not so weighty as to carry the catastrophic consequences of a {\i shvuas sheker}. In Rav\u8217's era, that balance had shifted: {\i nedarim} had lost their gravitas, and Rav was therefore unwilling to use them. This left the widow with no viable instrument for collection, which is precisely what the {\i mishna}\u8217's {\i takanah} was designed to prevent. The irony is not lost: the authority of Rav was now functioning as an obstacle to the very {\i tikkun} that Rabban Gamliel had worked to achieve.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Woman\u8217's Workaround\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Against this background the {\i gemara} relates the story of a woman who came before Rav Huna. He told her he could not help her \u8212- {\i d\u8217'Rav lo magbi kesuba l\u8217'armalta} \u8212- citing Rav\u8217's authority. The woman, without waiting to be asked, declared: {\i chai Hashem Tzevaos im nehenesi miksuvasi klum} \u8212- by the life of the God of Hosts, I did not derive any benefit from my {\i kesuba}. She simply took the oath unilaterally.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav\u8217's Concession\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Huna responded: {\i modeh Rav b\u8217'kofetzes} \u8212- even Rav concedes in the case of one who \u8220"jumps in\u8221" and takes the oath voluntarily. The reluctance to administer the oath arose from rabbinic hesitation to impose it; but once the woman takes it on her own initiative, the oath is valid and she may collect. This creates a significant asymmetry: the court will not make her take the oath, but if she takes it herself, it is binding. Women needed to know this option existed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Widow Trapped\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The second story is more troubling. A woman came before Rabbah bar Rav Huna seeking to collect her {\i kesuba}. He told her: {\i d\u8217'Rav lo magbi kesuba l\u8217'armalta} \u8212- and furthermore, {\i Abba Mari lo magbi kesuba l\u8217'armalta} \u8212- my father and teacher likewise did not collect a {\i kesuba} for a widow. The woman, finding this avenue closed, said: fine, then at minimum support me from my late husband\u8217's estate \u8212- {\i hav li mezoni}. He replied: {\i mezoni nami leis lach} \u8212- you cannot have that either. For Rav Yehuda had said in the name of Shmuel: {\i hatovasa kesuvashah b\u8217'veis din ein lah mezonos} \u8212- once a woman has gone to {\i beis din} to demand her {\i kesuba}, she has effectively declared her intention to sever her connection from her late husband\u8217's household, and she therefore forfeits the ongoing support that was premised on her continued identification with that household.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The woman was now caught between two rulings: she could neither collect her {\i kesuba} nor continue to receive support from the estate. She cursed Rabbah bar Rav Huna \u8212- {\i afchuah lekursai}, let your chair be turned upside down \u8212- and the {\i gemara} relates that they literally inverted his chair in an attempt to symbolically fulfill the curse and thereby neutralize it. Nevertheless, {\i va\u8217'afilu hachi lo ifrik mechulsha} \u8212- even so, he did not escape falling ill as a result of the curse. What the episode illustrates is how the invocation of Rav\u8217's authority, transmitted through generations of disciples without reassessment, had drifted entirely away from the animating concern of {\i tikkun olam} that motivated the original {\i takanah} of Rabban Gamliel.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rav Yehuda\u8217's Challenge\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Neder + Oath\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Yehuda said to Rav Yirmiyah Bira\u8217'a: {\i adra b\u8217'veis din} \u8212- have her take a {\i neder} in {\i beis din}, {\i ve\u8217'ashbe\u8217'uha chutz leveit din} \u8212- and have her take a {\i shvua} outside of {\i beis din}. The proposal is to combine both instruments. Given the concern that neither alone is fully reliable \u8212- the {\i neder} because it had lost weight in Rav\u8217's era, and the {\i shvua} outside {\i beis din} because it may not be sufficiently weighty \u8212- perhaps the combination of both would satisfy the competing concerns. The {\i neder} in the formal setting of {\i beis din} and the {\i shvua} in the more lenient extra-judicial context would together constitute a sufficient basis for collection.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Demand for Action\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Yehuda did not stop at the theoretical proposal. He demanded: {\i velesei kalla velipo be\u8217'udni} \u8212- let the announcement go out and let me hear with my own ears that you have actually acted on this \u8212- {\i debe\u8217'inah keheichi de\u8217'avid bah ma\u8217'aseh} \u8212- I want to know that you have carried this through in practice and that she has in fact collected her {\i kesuba}. The frustration is palpable: this is not a theoretical discussion. There is a woman who is owed money and cannot collect it, and the whole purpose of Rabban Gamliel\u8217's {\i takanah} was to ensure she could. The response of Rav Yehuda reflects a reassertion of the {\i tikkun olam} imperative against the paralysis that had set in through excessive deference to Rav\u8217's stringency.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Widow vs.\u160 ?Divorced\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 At this point the {\i gemara} circles back to reinforce the earlier distinction. The leniency for a {\i gerusha} \u8212- who can still take the standard oath \u8212- rests on the absence of the rationalizing dynamic. Unlike the {\i almana}, the divorced woman has no ongoing entanglement with her former husband\u8217's estate. She is not living there, not working there, not accumulating a sense of uncompensated labor. The scenario in which she would unconsciously take funds and tell herself she was entitled to do so simply does not arise. Hence the concern that drove the abandonment of the oath for widows does not apply to her.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Sarah bat Yaakov Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Case Facts\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then presents a concrete case transmitted from {\i Eretz Yisrael} to {\i Bavel}. A message was sent: how should we handle the case of a certain woman \u8212- Sarah the daughter of Yaakov \u8212- who received her {\i get} from Akha bar Kida, also known as Ayah Mari, and who took a {\i neder} regarding produce \u8212- that all the fruit of the world would be forbidden to her \u8212- attesting that the only things she had received from her {\i kesuba} were certain specified items? The case is therefore a {\i gerusha} who took a {\i neder} rather than a {\i shvua}, and the question sent to {\i Bavel} was: proceed to collect for her the remainder of her {\i kesuba}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kesuba Contents\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The items she admitted to having already received as partial payment of her {\i kesuba} deserve a moment\u8217's attention. They were: a coat, a scroll of {\i Tehillim}, a scroll of {\i Iyov}, and a scroll of {\i Mishlei} \u8212- worn and valued at five {\i maneh}. That a woman\u8217's {\i kesuba} included {\i Tehillim}, {\i Iyov}, and {\i Mishlei} is a striking window into the reality of women\u8217's learning. {\i Tehillim} and {\i Mishlei} might be expected \u8212- {\i Tehillim} for prayer, {\i Mishlei} for its {\i mussar} character. But {\i Iyov} is something else \u8212- a text of profound theological and philosophical inquiry. That these scrolls formed part of a woman\u8217's valued personal property speaks for itself.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Ashi Analysis\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Ashi intervenes: {\i hahu get yavmim hava} \u8212- that {\i get} she received was actually a {\i get yavmim}, a {\i get} given by a brother-in-law in the context of {\i yibum}. What this means is that the woman\u8217's husband died and she became subject to {\i yibum}; the brother-in-law gave her a {\i get} to prevent the {\i yibum} from occurring, but the case is in reality one of an {\i almana}, not a {\i gerusha}, since it is her first husband who died and from whose estate she is collecting. Tosafot points out that {\i chalitza} \u8212- not a {\i get} from the brother-in-law \u8212- is technically what would finalize her status, and raises the question accordingly. Tosafot\u8217's preferred resolution is that the brother-in-law in fact performed {\i yibum} and then divorced her, making her technically divorced from the {\i yavam} (the brother-in-law), but since {\i yibum} is understood as a continuation of the first husband\u8217's bond, she collects from the first husband\u8217's estate. Either way, her status is ultimately that of an {\i almana} with respect to the original estate \u8212- and the case therefore does not provide the proof one might have thought for allowing a {\i gerusha} to take a {\i neder} in lieu of an oath.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Neder Timing Rules\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Huna Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now turns to the question of when, precisely, the widow may take the {\i neder} that enables her to collect. Rav Huna rules: {\i lo shanu ela b\u8217'shelo nises} \u8212- this applies only when she has not yet remarried. The {\i takanah} of Rabban Gamliel permitting her to take a {\i neder} and collect her {\i kesuba} is available only while she remains unmarried.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Husband Annuls Neder\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The reason is straightforward: once she has remarried, her husband acquires the right of {\i hafarah} \u8212- the power to annul her vows. If she takes a {\i neder} while already married to a second husband, he can simply annul it, and the {\i neder} provides no protection whatsoever. It cannot serve as the required instrument of attestation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gemara Challenge\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} challenges: but even if she takes the {\i neder} before remarrying, why does this help? The moment she remarries, her new husband will learn of the {\i neder} and annul it on the day of their marriage. So even a pre-marriage {\i neder} would be vulnerable.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Pre-Existing Vows Safe\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} answers: {\i ein haba\u8217'al meifer bikodmin} \u8212- a husband cannot annul vows that predate the marriage. His power of {\i hafarah} operates only on vows made during the marriage, or at most on the day he first hears of them if they were made just before the marriage. Vows that were established prior to the marriage relationship are beyond his reach. Therefore, if she takes the {\i neder} while still single, it will remain binding even after she remarries, and the instrument works as intended.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Release from Neder\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chacham Annulment Risk\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} raises a further concern: even if remarriage is not a problem \u8212- since the husband cannot annul pre-existing vows \u8212- what prevents her from going to a {\i chacham} and obtaining a release from the {\i neder} through the mechanism of {\i hatarat nedarim}? Unlike a {\i shvua} regarding a past event, which either was true or was false at the moment it was taken and admits of no subsequent \u8220"release,\u8221" a {\i neder} remains technically open to annulment by a {\i chacham} who finds a legitimate {\i petach} (opening) for release. If she can simply obtain an annulment of the {\i neder} after collecting her {\i kesuba}, the entire instrument is undermined.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Detailing Requirement\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara}\u8217's answer: {\i kasavar tzarich lefareit es haneder} \u8212- we hold that one must detail the circumstances of the {\i neder} when seeking release. The requirement of {\i leprat es haneder} means that the woman must explain to the {\i chacham} not merely the formal content of the vow but the conditions and circumstances that gave rise to it. The standard {\i hatarat nedarim} formula recited on {\i Erev Rosh Hashanah} \u8212- where we acknowledge that the {\i halacha} requires detailing but waive that requirement because the vows are too numerous \u8212- reflects awareness of this principle.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chacham\u8217's Logic\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Once the circumstances are detailed, the {\i chacham} will immediately understand that the {\i neder} was taken precisely to enable collection of the {\i kesuba} \u8212- it is the very instrument that secured her right to collect. He cannot release her from it without invalidating the basis on which she collected. The release is therefore unavailable. The reasoning depends, of course, on the woman being truthful with the {\i chacham}. If she lies about the circumstances and the {\i chacham} annuls based on false information, the annulment would presumably not be valid \u8212- a genuinely interesting question, though one the {\i gemara} does not explore here.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rav Nachman Leniency\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Neder B\u8217'Rabbim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Nachman takes a more lenient position: {\i afilu nises} \u8212- even if she has remarried, she can still take the {\i neder} and collect. The concern that her husband will annul the {\i neder} is addressed by ensuring that {\i demadrinan lah berabbim} \u8212- we have her take the {\i neder} publicly. Once taken in public, the {\i neder} can no longer be annulled.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Berabbim Concept\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The concept of {\i berabbim} rests on the principle that once a matter has become public, the private mechanisms of annulment no longer apply. A {\i neder} taken publicly is no longer a private affair susceptible to personal retraction; it belongs, in a sense, to the public record.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Berabbim Types\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} distinguishes this from a related but distinct concept: {\i al da\u8217'as rabbim}. The {\i berabbim} principle holds that once a vow is publicly pronounced, the private mechanisms of annulment are structurally inapplicable \u8212- it is as if the vow has been \u8220"published.\u8221" The {\i al da\u8217'as rabbim} principle is different: it means the vow is made not merely on the basis of the individual\u8217's own intent, but on the basis of what the community would desire for the conditions of the vow. This makes it resistant to annulment because the annulment would require addressing not just the individual\u8217's own circumstances but the communal understanding. These are two different tools, and the {\i gemara} will draw on both.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Baraita Evidence\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} cites {\i baraisa} support for Rav Nachman\u8217's position. One {\i baraisa} states that if she has remarried \u8212- {\i nises govah kesuvashah im nadrah} \u8212- she collects her {\i kesuba} if she made a {\i neder}, which the {\i gemara} initially reads as meaning she made the vow after remarrying, and thus supporting Rav Nachman. A second {\i baraisa} is more explicit: {\i nises noderes v\u8217'govah kesuvashah} \u8212- she remarried, she takes a vow now, and she collects. Both {\i baraisas} clearly support the view that a post-marriage {\i neder} can work.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tannaitic Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} resolves the tension between the two positions \u8212- Rav Huna\u8217's that she must be unmarried and Rav Nachman\u8217's that she can be married \u8212- by declaring {\i tannai hi}: this is a dispute among {\i tannaim}. The dispute hinges on a fundamental question in the laws of {\i nedarim}: does a {\i neder shehuddar berabbim yesh lo hafarah} \u8212- can a vow taken publicly be annulled \u8212- or {\i ein lo hafarah} \u8212- is it definitively beyond annulment? The opinion that she can take a {\i neder} even after remarrying must hold that a public {\i neder} cannot be annulled; the opinion that she cannot must hold that even public vows remain technically open to annulment, and the husband\u8217's power therefore remains a threat. The {\i beis din} context itself suffices to make the vow \u8220"public\u8221" \u8212- Rashi specifies {\i ba\u8217'asarah}, before ten, and in the {\i beis din} setting this is easily achieved.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Detailing Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Nachman: No Detail\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now presents a direct dispute between Rav Nachman and Rav Papa on whether {\i leprat es haneder} \u8212- detailing the circumstances of the vow \u8212- is required when seeking release from a {\i chacham}. Rav Nachman holds: {\i eino tzarich} \u8212- one does not need to detail the circumstances. His reasoning is given in the negative: if you required detailing, {\i zimnin degayiz lei lediburei} \u8212- sometimes a person will abbreviate and not give the full circumstances, {\i vechacham bemai deshama meifer} \u8212- and the {\i chacham} will annul based only on what he heard, which may be incomplete. If the {\i chacham} annuls based on a partial account, the annulment may be technically valid even though the full picture would have led him to refuse. By not requiring detail, you avoid the problem of partial disclosures generating flawed annulments.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 False Release Risk\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But this raises an obvious concern: if no detailing is required, then a woman who took a {\i neder} to enable collection of her {\i kesuba} could subsequently go to a {\i chacham}, give him no specifics, and obtain a release without his knowing the {\i neder} was the instrument of her collection. This is precisely the scenario the {\i gemara} had earlier invoked as a reason to worry about the effectiveness of the {\i neder} mechanism. Under Rav Nachman, the answer to this concern is the {\i berabbim} solution \u8212- the vow is taken publicly and is therefore not open to annulment, regardless of whether details are given.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Papa: Detail Required\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Papa disagrees: {\i tzarich} \u8212- one must detail the circumstances, {\i mishum milta de-issura} \u8212- because without the details, the {\i chacham} may release someone from a vow that was serving as a safeguard against a transgression. The illustration is sharp: if a person took a {\i neder} not to walk down a certain street in order to avoid the temptation of a forbidden establishment, and then goes to a {\i chacham} and simply says \u8220"I have a {\i neder} not to walk down such-and-such street\u8221" without explaining why, the {\i chacham} may well annul it \u8212- thereby inadvertently removing the person\u8217's protection against sin. Without knowing the purpose of the vow, the {\i chacham} cannot properly evaluate whether annulment is appropriate. In the {\i kesuba} context, the same logic applies: without knowing that the {\i neder} was taken to enable collection of the {\i kesuba}, the {\i chacham} cannot know that annulment would allow the woman to retain money she does not deserve.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Kohein\u8217's Neder Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kohein\u8217's Obligation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} adduces a parallel case from the laws of {\i kehuna} to illuminate the same underlying tension. The {\i mishna} states: {\i hanosei nashim ba-aveira pasul} \u8212- a {\i kohen} who has married a woman he is forbidden to marry is disqualified from performing the {\i avoda} in the {\i Beis Hamikdash}. The mechanism to restore his eligibility is: {\i ad sheyidor hana\u8217'ah} \u8212- until he takes a {\i neder} forbidding himself any benefit from his wife, which will effectively compel him to divorce her since the marriage becomes untenable. Once he has taken that {\i neder}, a related {\i baraisa} states: {\i noder ve-oved} \u8212- he takes the vow and performs the {\i avoda}. He does his {\i avoda}, descends from the {\i mizbe\u8217'ach}, and then divorces her.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Parallel Problem\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The same question arises here: if one does not need to detail the circumstances of a vow, what prevents this {\i kohen} from going to a {\i chacham}, obtaining release from his {\i neder} without disclosing that the whole point was to secure his divorce from a forbidden wife, and thereby escaping both the obligation to divorce and the disqualification from {\i avoda}? The mechanism by which the {\i neder} was supposed to guarantee his behavior becomes hollow if it can be quietly annulled.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Same Solution Applied\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} gives the same answer: {\i demadrinan lah berabbim} \u8212- we ensure that the {\i neder} is taken publicly, and a public vow cannot be annulled. The parallel reinforces the point that the two mechanisms \u8212- {\i leprat es haneder} and {\i berabbim} \u8212- are the twin pillars on which the effectiveness of these vows depends.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Neder Protection Methods\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Detail Method\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The two mechanisms that protect a {\i neder} from being circumvented operate in different contexts. The first \u8212- {\i leprat es haneder}, requiring that the circumstances be detailed when seeking release from a {\i chacham} \u8212- ensures that the {\i chacham} has full information and can decline to annul when doing so would enable a transgression or undermine the purpose for which the vow was taken.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Berabbim Method\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The second \u8212- taking the {\i neder berabbim}, in public \u8212- removes the vow from the domain of private arrangements amenable to personal retraction. A vow made in public is no longer susceptible to annulment by a {\i chacham} (according to the view that {\i ein lo hafarah}) and certainly not by a husband.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Context Application\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The two mechanisms are not interchangeable in all contexts. Where the concern is a {\i chacham} annulling the vow, either mechanism can in principle address the problem: requiring detail will prevent a well-intentioned {\i chacham} from annulling without full information, and a {\i berabbim} vow forecloses the option entirely.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Husband Annulment\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But where the concern is the husband exercising his power of {\i hafarah}, the {\i leprat es haneder} mechanism is simply irrelevant. The husband does not need to be informed of the circumstances or reasons for the vow; his right of annulment is a marital prerogative that operates independently of his knowledge of the vow\u8217's purpose. In that context, only the {\i berabbim} solution works: by taking the {\i neder} publicly, the wife removes it from the reach of the husband\u8217's unilateral power of annulment. We will continue to explore what types of vows can or cannot be annulled as we proceed in the sugya.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Gittin 36\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Widow\u8217's Vow vs.\u160 ?Oath\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Vow instead of oath\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 We begin at the very top of 36a, picking up a topic that has been developing across the preceding pages: the substitution of a {\i neder} (vow) for a {\i shvuah} (oath) when a widow comes to collect from her deceased husband\u8217's estate. The reason for the substitution is the severity of a {\i shvuat sheker} \u8212- a false oath \u8212- even an unintentional one carries grave consequences. By switching to a {\i neder}, the Rabbis hoped to give the widow a viable path to collection while protecting her from the dangers of a potentially false oath. We have already seen how that institution experienced a certain pushback at a later stage, and we will continue to see that dynamic as we proceed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Vow annulment problem\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now asks: why should a {\i neder} suffice? Two problems immediately present themselves. First, can she make the vow at all once she is already married, given that her husband has the power to annul it \u8212- in which case the vow would not accomplish anything? Second, even for an unmarried widow, what prevents her from going to a {\i chakham} (sage) and having the vow released, thereby undermining the entire mechanism?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Married woman\u8217's vow\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 On the question of a married woman, the {\i gemara} records a dispute. According to one opinion, a married woman cannot make such a vow for precisely this reason: her husband will simply annul it and it will prove nothing. According to another opinion, it is possible \u8212- but only under certain conditions.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Be-rabbim vs.\u160 ?al da\u8217'at\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Before examining those conditions, a preliminary distinction is essential: the difference between {\i be-rabbim} and {\i al da\u8217'at rabbim}. {\i Be-rabbim} means the vow was made in public \u8212- {\i Rashi} defines the relevant quorum as ten people. {\i Al da\u8217'at rabbim} means the vow was made not merely on the basis of the individual\u8217's own {\i da\u8217'at} (intent or understanding) but in some sense on the basis of what the community would intend \u8212- it is grounded in communal, rather than personal, {\i da\u8217'at}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafot on husband annul\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} observes that when it comes to the husband\u8217's power of annulment ({\i hafarah}), {\i al da\u8217'at rabbim} does not help. The mechanism of {\i hatarah} \u8212- going to a {\i chakham} to have a vow released \u8212- works by identifying an error in the vower\u8217's {\i da\u8217'at}: had I known this or that, I never would have made the vow; my intent was mistaken, and so the vow should be dissolved. If the vow is grounded not in my subjective {\i da\u8217'at} but in the community\u8217's {\i da\u8217'at}, that avenue is closed off, because the subjective circumstances that might have constituted my personal error are no longer operative. That is the power of {\i al da\u8217'at rabbim} in blocking {\i hatarah} by a {\i chakham}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But {\i hafarah} by a husband operates on an entirely different principle. The husband does not dissolve the vow by identifying an error in his wife\u8217's intent. He has a legal power, independent of {\i da\u8217'at} altogether, to declare that this vow cannot stand within the marriage. The wife\u8217's intent \u8212- whether completely certain or completely ambiguous \u8212- is irrelevant to this mechanism. And if the wife\u8217's {\i da\u8217'at} is irrelevant, substituting the community\u8217's {\i da\u8217'at} for hers changes nothing. Accordingly, {\i al da\u8217'at rabbim} provides no shield against a husband\u8217's {\i hafarah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Public vs.\u160 ?communal dist\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Be-rabbim}, however, may work precisely because it operates on a different logic altogether. When a vow is made in public, it is no longer a private matter between husband and wife. The vow has entered the public domain \u8212- it is a matter of public record, so to speak \u8212- and is thereby removed from the private relational sphere in which the husband\u8217's power of annulment operates. According to the opinions that {\i be-rabbim} functions in this way, it would block both {\i hafarah} by a husband and {\i hatarah} by a {\i chakham}, and it would do so without exception, since the mechanism is not about {\i da\u8217'at} at all.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 There are, however, opinions that {\i be-rabbim} does not help: merely pronouncing a vow in front of others does not alter the inherent nature of the vow. According to those opinions, {\i al da\u8217'at rabbim} remains the operative mechanism \u8212- effective against the {\i chakham}, ineffective against the husband, and, as we shall see, subject to its own internal limitations.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Three solutions explained\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Returning to the question of how to block a woman (married or not) from going to a {\i chakham} to dissolve the vow: the {\i gemara} presents multiple approaches. One answer is that when the vow is made for purposes such as these, she must specify the circumstances in which it was made ({\i tzarikh lefareit et haneder}), and once those circumstances are explicit, the {\i chakham} will recognize that this is not a vow he may dissolve \u8212- it is the very instrument by which she collects. A second answer holds that ordinarily one need not detail the conditions of the vow, and therefore we are concerned she may seek release. According to this view, the vow is only protected if made either {\i be-rabbim} or {\i al da\u8217'at rabbim}. These two mechanisms, and the interplay between them, are what the {\i gemara} now proceeds to examine in detail.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Al da\u8217'at rabbim mechanism\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gemara on two concepts\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} opens this discussion with a case that clarifies how {\i be-rabbim} and {\i al da\u8217'at rabbim} function in practice. Three lines from the bottom of 35b, the {\i gemara} frames its question around a case analogous to the widow\u8217's: a {\i kohen} who made a vow not to derive benefit from a woman he is forbidden to marry \u8212- say, a {\i gerusha} \u8212- in order to render himself eligible to perform the {\i avodah}. The concern is identical: perhaps he will simply go to a {\i chakham} and have the vow dissolved.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} at the top of 36a proposes: {\i demadirin lei be-rabbim} \u8212- we have him take the vow in public. That will close off the avenue of {\i hatarah}. But the {\i gemara} immediately complicates this: {\i hani-cha le-man d\u8217'amar neder she-hudur be-rabbim ein lo hafarah} \u8212- this works well for the opinion that a vow made in public cannot be annulled. {\i Ella le-man d\u8217'amar yesh lo hafarah} \u8212- but according to the opinion that it can still be annulled, what solution remains? The {\i gemara} answers: {\i demadirin lei al da\u8217'at rabbim} \u8212- we have him take the vow on the communal {\i da\u8217'at}, not merely in their presence.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ameimar\u8217's position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This leads to a direct ruling. {\i Amar Ameimar}: the {\i halacha} is that even according to the opinion that a vow made {\i be-rabbim} can still be annulled, a vow made {\i al da\u8217'at rabbim} cannot be annulled ({\i al da\u8217'at rabbim ein lo hafarah}).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A clarification is in order here. {\i Tosafot} has already established that {\i al da\u8217'at rabbim} cannot prevent a husband\u8217's {\i hafarah}, since the husband\u8217's power is independent of {\i da\u8217'at} entirely. Therefore, the {\i hafarah} that {\i Ameimar} says is blocked must refer specifically to {\i hatarah} \u8212- release by a {\i chakham} \u8212- not to a husband\u8217's {\i hafarah}. {\i Ameimar}\u8217's ruling is thus: {\i be-rabbim} may or may not block {\i hatarah} (it is disputed); {\i al da\u8217'at rabbim} blocks {\i hatarah} according to everyone. The mechanism, as noted above, is that {\i hatarah} works by identifying subjective error in the vower\u8217's {\i da\u8217'at}. When the vow is grounded not in one\u8217's individual {\i da\u8217'at} but in a communal one, the personal subjective circumstances that might have constituted error are simply no longer relevant.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is worth pausing to note the difference in scope between the two mechanisms. If {\i be-rabbim} does work \u8212- and that remains disputed \u8212- it is arguably broader and more absolute than {\i al da\u8217'at rabbim}. Making a vow in public transforms it into a matter of public record, removing it entirely from the private sphere; this would block {\i hafarah} by a husband as well as {\i hatarah} by a {\i chakham}, and it would permit no exceptions. {\i Al da\u8217'at rabbim}, by contrast, operates through {\i da\u8217'at} \u8212- it replaces subjective individual intent with communal intent \u8212- and therefore it is only relevant where {\i da\u8217'at} is operative. This means it does not help against a husband\u8217's {\i hafarah}, and, as the {\i gemara} now demonstrates, it is not absolute even against a {\i chakham}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Dardeki case study\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} introduces an important limitation: {\i ve-hani milei le-dvar reshut} \u8212- this rule that {\i al da\u8217'at rabbim} prevents {\i hatarah} applies only when the vow pertains to a discretionary matter. {\i Aval le-dvar mitzvah, yesh hafarah} \u8212- but if keeping the vow would interfere with the performance of a {\i mitzvah}, it can be annulled despite having been made {\i al da\u8217'at rabbim}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The illustrative case is the {\i mikri dardeki} \u8212- an elementary school teacher. {\i Rav Acha} imposed a vow on him {\i al da\u8217'at rabbim} \u8212- binding him, under communal {\i da\u8217'at}, to the effect that he would no longer be permitted to teach children. The background is that he had been negligent, apparently meaning he had been striking the children \u8212- a recognized form of discipline in that era, though one that the {\i gemara} treats as grounds for removal. The vow was structured as {\i al da\u8217'at rabbim} precisely to close off any avenue of release. Nevertheless, {\i Ravina} subsequently restored him to his position ({\i ahadrei Ravina}), because {\i lo ishtakach de-dayek ke-votei} \u8212- no other teacher could be found who was as exacting and demanding as he was, and the children\u8217's education was therefore suffering.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Communal intent limits\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The question naturally arises: how could {\i Ravina} dissolve a vow made {\i al da\u8217'at rabbim}? The answer lies in distinguishing between subjective and objective grounds for release. The mechanism of {\i al da\u8217'at rabbim} closes off the avenue of {\i ta\u8217'ut} (error) that is personal and subjective. If the vower says, \u8220"Had I known my own personal circumstances, I would not have made this vow,\u8221" that subjective claim is not available \u8212- the community\u8217's {\i da\u8217'at} does not share those private circumstances. But there is a category of error that is not subjective at all: had we known that this vow would come to block the performance of a {\i mitzvah}, no community would ever have countenanced such a vow. The {\i rabim} never intend their communal {\i da\u8217'at} to be invoked in service of obstructing a {\i mitzvah}. That is an objective error \u8212- one that any community would universally recognize \u8212- and it therefore qualifies as a valid basis for {\i hatarah} even against a vow made {\i al da\u8217'at rabbim}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This refines our understanding of what {\i al da\u8217'at rabbim} actually does and does not accomplish. It removes the vow from the domain of individual, subjective {\i da\u8217'at}, preventing release on the grounds of personal error or personal circumstance. But it does not insulate the vow against objective, universal errors \u8212- specifically, circumstances that no community would ever have wished to support. A {\i mitzvah} being impeded is precisely such a circumstance; the community, by definition, never would have wanted their {\i da\u8217'at} to stand in the way of Torah obligations. So {\i al da\u8217'at rabbim} is more limited than it might initially appear: it works against personal error, but not against objective necessity.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The overall picture, then, is as follows. {\i Al da\u8217'at rabbim} works according to everyone to block {\i hatarah} in ordinary discretionary cases. {\i Be-rabbim}, if it works at all (which is disputed), is a categorically different mechanism \u8212- it transforms the vow into a public matter and is therefore broader, more absolute, and operates even against a husband\u8217's {\i hafarah}. And {\i al da\u8217'at rabbim}, despite its power, has two significant limits: it does not block a husband\u8217's {\i hafarah} (as {\i Tosafot} established), and it does not prevent {\i hatarah} in cases where the vow obstructs a {\i mitzvah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Oath vs.\u160 ?vow nature\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is worth stepping back to recall why this entire discussion is taking place in the register of {\i neder} rather than {\i shvuah}. The window of possibility for a {\i chakham} to release a vow, and for all of these mechanisms of {\i be-rabbim} and {\i al da\u8217'at rabbim} to become relevant, exists precisely because we shifted from {\i shvuah} to {\i neder}. A {\i shvuah} regarding a past event \u8212- \u8220"I never took any money from the estate\u8221" \u8212- is determined at the moment it is uttered. Either the words are true or they constitute a {\i shvuat sheker}. There is nothing for a {\i chakham} to act upon after the fact; no release is possible because the binding obligation exists only in the past. A {\i neder}, by contrast, creates an ongoing obligation that can be modified or dissolved. All the analytical machinery the {\i gemara} deploys in this section is a consequence of that initial substitution.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Witness signatures\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Biblical requirement\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} now turns to a second {\i tikkun olam}: {\i ve-ha-eidim chosmin al ha-get mi-pnei tikkun olam} \u8212- the witnesses sign on the {\i get} for the sake of {\i tikkun olam}. At first glance this is surprising. Surely the requirement that witnesses sign a document is biblical in origin? The verse says {\i ve-chasov be-sefer ve-chasom} \u8212- \u8220"write in a scroll and sign.\u8221" Is this not a {\i de-oraita} requirement?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Elazar view\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rava} explains: this {\i mishnah} is needed specifically according to the position of {\i Rabbi Elazar}, who holds {\i edei mesirah karti} \u8212- that biblically, the critical witnesses are those who are physically present at the moment the {\i get} is handed over, not those who sign the document. According to {\i Rabbi Elazar}, another {\i gemara} teaches, the verse cited is not a mandatory requirement for documents to be valid, but rather {\i eitzah tovah ka-mashma lan} \u8212- sound advice. The Torah recommends that signatures be included, but does not make them a biblical prerequisite for the legal efficacy of a {\i get}. Biblically, what is needed is the physical presence of witnesses at the moment of transmission \u8212- {\i edei mesirah} \u8212- whether understood as required for the {\i davar she-be-ervah} dimension, or as the witnesses whose observation of the handover constitutes the validating act of the {\i shtar} itself. Either way, their names need not appear in writing.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbinic innovation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It was the Rabbis who established {\i edei chasimah} \u8212- that witnesses must actually sign their names \u8212- {\i mi-pnei tikkun ha-olam}. The practical concern is stated plainly: {\i de-zimnin de-maisi sahadei} \u8212- sometimes witnesses die, or they travel far away ({\i azli li-medinas ha-yam}), and the woman is left with a document that divorces her but without any means of proving that she is indeed divorced. The signed {\i get} solves this problem elegantly: the very document she receives to effectuate her divorce simultaneously serves as her evidence of divorce. She need not locate the original witnesses; their names are there in the document itself.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 An interesting structural point follows from this. One might have imagined an alternative arrangement: the {\i get} itself would not bear signatures, but a subsequent rabbinic {\i beit din} document would separately attest that the divorce occurred. The Rabbis\u8217' solution is more elegant: the single document that functions as the instrument of divorce also bears witness to its own execution.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Original practice\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might ask how witness signatures were verified in earlier times. A {\i baraita} records the history: {\i ba-rishonah hayah kosev ani ploni chasamti eid} \u8212- originally, witnesses would write only their first name, something like \u8220"I, Reuven, have signed as a witness.\u8221" This was sufficient {\i im kesav yado yotzei mi-makom acher} \u8212- if the person\u8217's handwriting was recognizable from another source, one could confirm the authenticity of the signature. {\i Ve-im lav, pasul} \u8212- but if the handwriting was not independently recognizable, the signature could not be verified. With a common name like Reuven, there could be any number of individuals who might have signed, and without a means of identifying the particular person, the {\i get} could be rendered invalid.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabban Gamliel takana\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Amar Rabban Gamliel: takana gedola hiskinu} \u8212- they enacted a major {\i takana} that witnesses must spell out their names fully in {\i gittin}: the full name and patronymic, as in Reuven ben Yaakov. With this information, it becomes possible to identify the specific individual, to locate other exemplars of his handwriting, and to verify the signature properly. The {\i baraita} presents this as the content of the {\i tikkun olam} referred to in the {\i mishnah} \u8212- indeed, {\i Tosafot} notes that the conjunction in the {\i mishnah} ({\i ve-ha-eidim chosmin}) might suggest this was part of {\i Rabban Gamliel}\u8217's own {\i takana}, but the {\i gemara} makes clear that witness signatures predated {\i Rabban Gamliel}; his contribution was the standardization of full identifying names.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Symbols as signatures\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then raises an apparent tension. If the concern is identification, what about those sages who signed with pictorial symbols rather than names? {\i Ve-ha Rav tziyer kavra} \u8212- {\i Rav} drew a little fish. {\i Rav Chanina tziyer charusa} \u8212- {\i Rav Chanina} drew a palm tree. {\i Rav Chisda} drew a {\i samech} (presumably for {\i Chisda}). {\i Rav Oshaya} drew an {\i ayin}. {\i Rabba bar Rav Huna tziyer makusa} \u8212- he drew a sail of a ship. These symbols, then, served as valid signatures among the {\i tanna\u8217'im} and {\i amora\u8217'im}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This raises two distinct questions: first, are such symbols distinctive enough to prevent forgery, in the way a handwritten signature is? Second, how can a symbol adequately identify the signatory when, unlike a name with a patronymic, it gives no inherent information about who the person is?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Symbol establishment\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} addresses the second concern: {\i shani rabbanan de-bekiei be-simanaihu} \u8212- the rabbinic class was distinguished by the fact that their symbols were publicly known. These scholars had recognizable, known symbols, such that people could identify the symbol as belonging to a particular sage. Once a symbol is publicly recognized and associated with a known individual, it functions no differently from a fully spelled-out name for purposes of identification and verification.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This prompts a further question: {\i me-ikara be-mai apteinu} \u8212- but how did they begin using their symbols in the first place? There is a circular problem: you cannot use a symbol on a {\i shtar} before people know it, but how do they come to know it before you use it? The {\i gemara} answers: {\i be-diskei} \u8212- through personal correspondence. Before a scholar began signing {\i shtarot} with his symbol, he would use it extensively in private letters and communications, such that the symbol became recognizable. Only once it was publicly known could it then be employed on legal documents.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Prozbul introduction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hillel\u8217's takana\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} in {\i Shevi\u8217'it} is cited: {\i pruzbul eino meshamet} \u8212- a loan protected by a {\i pruzbul} is not annulled in the {\i shemita} year. This is identified as one of the institutions that {\i Hillel ha-Zaken} established. The {\i mishnah} explains the motivation: {\i she-ra\u8217'ah ha-am she-nimnu mi-lhalvot zeh la-zeh} \u8212- Hillel saw that people were refraining from lending money to one another as the {\i shemita} year approached, thereby transgressing what is written in the Torah: {\i hishamer lecha pen yihyeh davar im levavecha beliya\u8217'al} \u8212- \u8220"Beware lest there be a treacherous thought in your heart\u8221" leading you to withhold a loan from the poor because the {\i shemita} year is near. Hillel responded by establishing the {\i pruzbul}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mitzvah framing\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A crucial point must be made about how this institution is framed, because it is commonly misunderstood. The {\i pruzbul} is not presented as a pragmatic concession to economic reality \u8212- as though the Torah\u8217's law of {\i shemita} had become too burdensome and a workaround was needed. Rather, it is framed entirely in terms of competing {\i mitzvot}. The existing situation \u8212- people refusing to lend \u8212- was causing active biblical violations. People were transgressing the commandment to lend to the poor. Hillel\u8217's {\i takana} is thus presented as a resolution to a conflict between two Torah mandates, not as an accommodation of human weakness against a Torah institution.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This framing matters significantly. Hillel does not start by saying this {\i mitzvah} is economically unworkable; he says that the current state of affairs is causing the transgression of biblical commandments. There is still a genuine trade-off: is it better that people transgress the prohibition against refusing to lend but that the {\i shemita} annulment functions properly, or is it better to preserve the lending relationship at the cost of not fulfilling the annulment? Hillel clearly judges that the well-being of the poor \u8212- who are better served by receiving loans that must eventually be repaid than by seeing their creditors refuse to lend at all \u8212- tips the scale toward protecting the lending relationship. But the entire analysis is conducted within the framework of Torah {\i mitzvot}, not against it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Prozbul text\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} records the formula of the {\i pruzbul}: {\i mosrani lachem ploni u-ploni dayanim she-bi-makom ploni} \u8212- \u8220"I am handing over to you, Reuven and Shimon, the judges in such-and-such a place, that any debt owed to me by so-and-so I retain the right to collect at any time I wish.\u8221" The judges or witnesses then sign below.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Debt transfer mechanism\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The underlying legal mechanism, clarified by the {\i mishnah} in {\i Shevi\u8217'it} and by {\i Rashi} here, is {\i moseir shtarotav le-veit din} \u8212- handing the debt over to the {\i beit din}. By making this declaration before the court, the creditor is understood to transfer the authority to collect the debt to the {\i beit din} rather than retaining it as a personal claim against his neighbor. The verse {\i shamot} \u8212- \u8220"you shall release\u8221" \u8212- speaks of a personal claim: you shall not exact it from your neighbor. But once the debt is in the hands of {\i beit din} rather than of the individual creditor, it is the communal institution of the court, not the individual, that holds the right to collect. The {\i shemita} annulment operates against individual creditors; it does not apply to {\i beit din}. {\i Rashi} makes this explicit: {\i mosrani lachem} \u8212- \u8220"I am handing it to you, you will be the ones to demand it, and I will not.\u8221"\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Legal basis for prozbul\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gemara\u8217's challenge\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} presses with a fundamental question: {\i u-mi ika midi de-mi-d\u8217'Oraita meshamta shevi\u8217'it, ve-hiskin Hillel de-lo meshamta} \u8212- is there any case where the Torah biblically annuls a debt, and Hillel can simply establish by {\i takana} that it should not be annulled? How can a rabbinic institution override a biblical law of {\i shemita}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Abaye\u8217's answer\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Amar Abaye}: the entire discussion concerns {\i shemita} of our time, and it follows the position of {\i Rebbi} \u8212- that the annulment of money debts in our era is only rabbinic ({\i derabbanan}), not biblical. Since the Rabbis instituted the debt annulment in the first place, they retain the authority to create exceptions to it or workarounds from it. Hillel\u8217's {\i pruzbul} operates against a rabbinic institution, not a biblical one.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi\u8217's teaching\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The source for this claim is a {\i baraita} in which {\i Rebbi} expounds: {\i ve-zeh dvar ha-shemita shamot} \u8212- \u8220"this is the manner of the {\i shemita}, you shall release.\u8221" The verse speaks of two {\i shemitot} simultaneously \u8212- {\i shemitat karka} (the sabbatical release of the land) and {\i shemitat kesafim} (the annulment of debts): {\i bizman she-ata meshamet karka, ata meshamet kesafim; bizman she-i ata meshamet karka, i ata meshamet kesafim} \u8212- when you observe the release of the land, you observe the release of money; when you do not observe the release of the land, you do not observe the release of money. The two {\i shemitot} are halakhically interdependent.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 No yovel status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Since there is no {\i shemitat karka} in force biblically in our era, there is correspondingly no biblical {\i shemitat kesafim}. But why is there no biblical {\i shemitat karka} nowadays? {\i Rashi} explains that {\i shemitat karka} is itself dependent on {\i yovel} \u8212- the Jubilee year \u8212- and {\i yovel} requires {\i kol yoshveha aleha}, all twelve tribes settled on the land. This condition was not met even during the Second Temple period ({\i Bayit Sheni}), and certainly has not been met since. Consequently, even during the period of {\i Hillel}, there was no biblical {\i yovel}, and without biblical {\i yovel} there was no biblical {\i shemita} of land, and without biblical {\i shemita} of land there was no biblical {\i shemita} of money. The entire framework at issue was already rabbinic before {\i Hillel} instituted his {\i pruzbul}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Prior puzzle\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} is still not satisfied. Even if we accept that the existing debt annulment was only rabbinic, there is a prior puzzle: how did the Rabbis come to institute a rabbinic {\i shemitat kesafim} in the first place? Before they acted, during {\i Bayit Sheni}, there was no biblical {\i shemita} of money \u8212- debts had to be paid. By what authority did the Rabbis institute a new obligation that people\u8217's debts would be annulled, effectively taking away the creditor\u8217's biblical right to collect?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shev ve\u8217'al ta\u8217'aseh\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Amar Abaye}: the institution of rabbinic {\i shemita} was possible because it operates in the mode of {\i shev ve-al ta\u8217'aseh} \u8212- passive non-action rather than active violation. The Rabbis did not actively compel anyone to violate a biblical obligation; they simply instructed creditors not to collect. This is analogous to the Rabbinic practice of setting aside the {\i lulav} when the relevant day falls on {\i Shabbat} \u8212- a passive restraint from action rather than an active override of the Torah.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hefker beit din\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rava} offers a stronger answer: {\i hefker beit din hefker} \u8212- what a {\i beit din} declares ownerless becomes ownerless. The Rabbis have inherent authority to control the disposition of property. When it comes to monetary matters, the court can release people from their debts, reassign ownership, or restructure financial obligations. It was by virtue of this principle that the Rabbis were able to institute a rabbinic {\i shemitat kesafim} \u8212- they exercised their authority over property to declare debts annulled.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Proof texts\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Yitzchak} provides the biblical source for {\i hefker beit din hefker}: the verse in {\i Ezra}: {\i ve-chol asher lo yavo li-shloshet ha-yamim ke-atzat ha-sarim ve-ha-zekenim, yocharam kol rechusho} \u8212- anyone who does not appear within three days, in accordance with the directive of the officers and elders, shall have all his property put under the ban. This demonstrates that the court has the power to divest an individual of his property. {\i Rabbi Elazar} adduces a different verse: {\i eileh ha-nachalot asher nichalu Elazar ha-Kohen vi-Yehoshua bin Nun} \u8212- these are the portions that {\i Elazar ha-Kohen} and {\i Yehoshua bin Nun} apportioned. The text mentions {\i rashei ha-avot} \u8212- heads of the fathers \u8212- rather than heads of the tribes, which the {\i gemara} reads as a comparison: just as a father may bequeath to his children whatever he wishes, so too the leaders of the nation may apportion and redistribute the wealth of the people as they see fit.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The two proof texts point in somewhat different directions. The first ({\i Ezra}) demonstrates the punitive dimension of the court\u8217's power \u8212- property can be seized as a sanction. The second ({\i Yehoshua}) points to something broader: a fundamental, non-punitive authority to restructure ownership, comparable to a father\u8217's power to bequeath. {\i Hefker beit din hefker}, in {\i Rava}\u8217's usage, invokes this broader principle.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Tosafot critique\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mechanism vs.\u160 ?will\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rashi} notes that once {\i hefker beit din hefker} is introduced, it resolves not only the question of how the Rabbis could institute rabbinic {\i shemitat kesafim} in the first place, but also the original challenge of how {\i Hillel} could institute the {\i pruzbul} even against a potentially biblical {\i shemita}. If the court can control ownership, then even if the Torah\u8217's {\i shemita} voided the debt, {\i beit din} could simply reinstate it after {\i shemita}, and Hillel\u8217's institution is thereby justified even on a {\i de-oraita} level.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Propriety concern\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} takes sharp issue with this. Their question is not about mechanism but about propriety. As {\i Tosafot} puts it: even granting that Hillel had the mechanism of {\i hefker beit din hefker} available to him, {\i lo hayah lo le-Hillel la\u8217'akor shevi\u8217'it she-hi de-oraita} \u8212- it was not appropriate for Hillel to uproot a Torah institution of {\i shemita} in such a blatant way. The existence of a mechanism does not, by itself, justify its use. To openly deploy rabbinic power to directly override a foundational biblical institution is a different category of act than working within or around it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Fig leaf approach\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} draws an illuminating parallel to the concept of {\i hafka\u8217'at kiddushin} \u8212- retroactive rabbinic annulment of a marriage. The {\i Rashba} holds that the Rabbis have the technical power to retroactively annul a marriage, but they will not exercise that power openly. To look back at twenty years of recognized marriage and declare it never existed would be a blatant and intolerable show of rabbinic power against a biblical institution. Therefore, the Rabbis will only invoke {\i hafka\u8217'at kiddushin} in the presence of a {\i get} \u8212- even if the {\i get} is technically invalid ({\i pasul}), its existence provides a cover: it looks as though the woman was divorced by the {\i get}, not as though the Rabbis voided the {\i kiddushin} by fiat. This is what might be called the {\i fig leaf} approach: the mechanism used must at least appear to be a non-confrontational resolution, not a naked exercise of power against the Torah.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Prozbul as fig leaf\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The same logic applies to {\i pruzbul}. {\i Tosafot} explains why the {\i gemara} frames the {\i pruzbul} around the mechanism of {\i moseir shtarotav le-beit din} \u8212- handing over the debt to the court \u8212- rather than simply invoking {\i hefker beit din hefker} directly. The transfer-to-{\i beit-din} framework provides the necessary appearance of legitimacy. Rather than openly declaring that the Rabbis are overriding {\i shemita}, the {\i pruzbul} presents itself as a structural change in who holds the debt: it is now the {\i beit din\u8217's} debt, not the individual creditor\u8217's, and {\i shemita} does not apply to {\i beit din}. Even if the underlying {\i takana} is really being driven by {\i hefker beit din hefker}, the {\i pruzbul} gives it a face that avoids the appearance of direct confrontation with a Torah law.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Transfer doesn\u8217't work\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Indeed, {\i Tosafot} goes further and suggests that the transfer to {\i beit din} may not actually be legally operative in the strict sense \u8212- it may be a legal fiction rather than a real transfer of the debt. But that does not undermine its purpose; if anything, it makes the point more sharply. The function of the {\i moseir shtarotav} framework is not primarily mechanical but presentational. We need a structure in which the {\i pruzbul} does not look like a straight override of a Torah institution, even if the underlying justification is ultimately rooted in the court\u8217's broader power over property.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This provides the proper lens through which to understand {\i tikkun olam} in the context of {\i pruzbul}. Several dimensions converge. First, the problem is framed not as economic hardship but as a competition of Torah {\i mitzvot} \u8212- the {\i pruzbul} resolves a clash between the commandment to lend and the institution of {\i shemita} by siding with what better serves the poor. Second, even where the Rabbis have the raw mechanism to act, they do not deploy it brazenly against a Torah institution; they require a framework \u8212- the {\i fig leaf} of the transfer to {\i beit din} \u8212- that presents the resolution as something other than a naked rabbinic override. These are not mere technicalities; they reflect a deeper commitment to operating within the spirit of Torah authority even when exercising the outer limits of rabbinic power.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Gittin 37\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Pruzbul Foundation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hillel\u8217's Authority\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Today\u8217's {\i daf} is 37, and we pick up in the middle of 36b, in the midst of the remarkable {\i sugya} of {\i pruzbul}. The preceding discussion addressed the fundamental question of how Hillel could establish {\i pruzbul} without undermining the {\i d\u8217'oraita} principle that {\i shmitta} annuls debts. The {\i Gemara} resolved this by noting that the annulment of debts nowadays is Rabbinic in nature. But that itself raised a further difficulty: how did the Rabbis create that Rabbinic annulment in the first place? The {\i Gemara}\u8217's answer introduced the principle of {\i hefker bet din hefker} \u8212- the authority of a court to reassign questions of property ownership. For Rashi, this power was sufficient to ground {\i pruzbul} even if {\i shmitta} were biblical, since it gives Hillel the authority to restructure monetary obligations at their root.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot, however, pressed a deeper point. The problem was not merely mechanical \u8212- how {\i pruzbul} could circumvent a {\i d\u8217'oraita} \u8212- but ethical: how could Hillel have done such a thing at all? That is why it was a more satisfying answer that {\i shmitta} nowadays is Rabbinic. And this connects to the way Hillel himself framed the issue. Against the popular conception, Hillel did not frame {\i pruzbul} as a workaround because the law was too burdensome. Rather, he framed it as a weighing of one {\i d\u8217'oraita} against another.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Balancing Obligations\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Hillel recognized a genuine conflict: on one side, the {\i shmitta} annulment of debts; on the other, the prohibition against withholding loans from the poor. People were violating the latter because they feared the former. In order to prevent the ongoing violation of {\i that} commandment, Hillel found a means of addressing the debt annulment. The entire enterprise was framed as a {\i d\u8217'oraita} trade-off \u8212- one Torah obligation being preserved at the cost of circumventing another \u8212- and that framing is obviously critical when one is in the business of constructing such an institution.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hefker Bet Din\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 With that conceptual foundation in place, the {\i Gemara} continues. The principle of {\i hefker bet din hefker} is what gives a {\i beit din} the power to reassign ownership and thereby enable the mechanism of {\i pruzbul}. As we will see, this principle carries significant implications not only for {\i pruzbul} but for broader questions about the authority of rabbinic courts \u8212- including, parenthetically, the concept of {\i hafk\u8217'as kiddushin}, which at least according to Rashi in the case of {\i kesef kiddushin} may partly rest on this foundation, though the {\i Gemara} ultimately grounds {\i hafk\u8217'as kiddushin} in the principle of {\i al da\u8217'ata derabbanan mekadesh}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Temporal Scope\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Temporary vs.\u160 ?Permanent\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now raises a new question, approximately fifteen lines down on 36b, beginning {\i iba\u8217'u lehu}: when Hillel established {\i pruzbul}, did he do so only for his generation, or for all future generations? This question is notable because it was not asked about the other {\i tikkunei olam} instituted earlier in the tractate. Why should it arise specifically here?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One possibility is that {\i pruzbul} was framed as a response to a particular observed reality \u8212- {\i ra\u8217'ah Hillel she-nimnu ha-am mi-lilvot zeh et zeh}, Hillel saw that people were refraining from lending to one another \u8212- and this framing suggests a situational assessment rather than a permanent institutional change. A second possibility is that because {\i pruzbul} involves circumventing something that looks like a {\i d\u8217'oraita}, it was a radical step, and perhaps Hillel did not intend it as a permanent alteration.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The practical stakes of this question are made explicit: {\i l\u8217'mai nafka mina} \u8212- what difference does it make? The {\i Gemara} answers: {\i levatlohei}, the question is whether we can annul it. If Hillel established {\i pruzbul} only for his generation, then perhaps it is currently maintained only by force of habit, not by the binding authority of his enactment, and a later court could dissolve it. If, however, he established it for all generations, then no subsequent {\i beit din} could annul it unless it were greater in wisdom and number \u8212- {\i ein beit din yakhol levatel divrei beit din chavero ela im kein gadol mimenu be-chochma u-ve-minyan}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shmuel\u8217's Restriction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} adduces evidence from a ruling of Shmuel: {\i lo katvinan pruzbul ela ei be-veit dina d\u8217'Sura ei be-veit dina d\u8217'Nahardei} \u8212- a {\i pruzbul} may only be written before the major {\i batei din} of Sura or Nahardea, the central courts of the region. The {\i Gemara} asks: if Hillel established {\i pruzbul} for all generations, why would it be limited to these central institutions? Surely Hillel wanted this tool to be broadly available, in which case he should have empowered all {\i batei din} to execute it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The logic of the limitation seems to point toward a specific conclusion: perhaps the original authority of Hillel\u8217's enactment is no longer operative, and {\i pruzbul} now functions only by virtue of the power of a current central {\i beit din} exercising {\i hefker bet din hefker}. If that is so, only a {\i beit din} with the authority to be {\i motzi mamon} \u8212- to actually extract money \u8212- would have the standing to execute it. This would suggest not merely that Hillel\u8217's {\i takana} can be annulled, but that it has in effect already lapsed, and {\i pruzbul} now requires a fresh act of rabbinic authority each time it is invoked.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Intentional Limitation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} rejects this inference, however. {\i Dilma ki takkin Hillel l\u8217'dorei alma, kegon beit dina didei} \u8212- perhaps when Hillel established {\i pruzbul} for all generations, he himself always intended it to be executed only before a {\i beit din} of sufficient authority to be {\i motzi mamon}, empowered with {\i hefker bet din hefker}, and not before every local court. In other words, the restriction to central {\i batei din} may have been part of Hillel\u8217's original design. Given how radical the institution was \u8212- circumventing what appears to be a {\i d\u8217'oraita} \u8212- perhaps Hillel deliberately chose to confine its use to courts of the highest caliber.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The question accordingly remains unresolved: is {\i pruzbul} still operative on the basis of Hillel\u8217's original enactment, or are we doing it on the independent authority of the current central {\i beit din}? This {\i Gemara} carries important implications beyond {\i pruzbul} \u8212- it suggests that {\i hefker bet din hefker} is limited to {\i batei din} of central and high authority, a point relevant to other applications of that principle.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Shmuel\u8217's Opposition\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Olban Shel Dayanim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now introduces another statement of Shmuel, which is fully consistent with his restrictive posture toward {\i pruzbul}. {\i De-amar Shmuel: hai pruzbul ulbana de-dayyani hu} \u8212- this {\i pruzbul} is an embarrassment to the judges. Shmuel characterizes the institution as a disgrace, presumably because it involves circumventing what presents itself as a Torah law \u8212- even if {\i shmitta} nowadays is Rabbinic, it looks like a Torah law \u8212- and the judiciary is implicated in this circumvention.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shmuel\u8217's Chutzpah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Shmuel goes further still: {\i ve-ei yashar cheili, chaya, avatleinei} \u8212- if I had the strength, I would nullify it entirely. This statement likely motivated the {\i Gemara}\u8217's earlier question about whether {\i pruzbul} can be annulled. Given that Shmuel expressed such displeasure and such a desire, the {\i Gemara} naturally wondered whether the institution was in fact susceptible to annulment.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What drove Shmuel\u8217's opposition? Several possibilities present themselves. He may have felt the weight of the appearance of circumventing Torah law. He may have believed that in his generation, people were willing to absorb the annulment of small debts and would have been prepared to do so, making {\i pruzbul} unnecessary. Or, much as one might observe about the institution of {\i mechirat chametz}, he may have felt that a well-intentioned workaround was being abused and applied far beyond the cases that genuinely warranted it \u8212- thereby undermining the very {\i mitzvah} it was designed to protect.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} at this point introduces an {\i aggadic} passage that may carry an implicit response to Shmuel. The phrase {\i ulbana} \u8212- embarrassment or disgrace \u8212- leads the {\i Gemara} to the {\i braita}: {\i ha-ne\u8217'elavin ve-einam olevim, shom\u8217'in cherpatam ve-einam meshivim, osim me-ahava u-semechim be-yisurin} \u8212- those who are shamed and do not return the shame, who hear themselves being disgraced and do not respond, who serve God out of love and rejoice in affliction \u8212- on them the verse says, {\i ve-ohavav ke-tzet ha-shemesh bi-gvurato}, those who love God shall be as the sun going forth in its strength. One might read this as a subtle pushback against Shmuel: rather than responding to the perceived embarrassment of {\i pruzbul} by seeking to dismantle it, the {\i Gemara} gestures toward the virtue of bearing that discomfort and maintaining the institution.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Cannot Annul Unilaterally\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} then asks explicitly: Shmuel said he would annul {\i pruzbul} \u8212- but how could he? {\i Ve-chi beit din yakhol levatel divrei beit din chavero ela im kein gadol mimenu be-chochma u-ve-minyan?} A {\i beit din} cannot annul the legislation of an earlier one unless it is greater. So what was Shmuel thinking?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} clarifies: {\i im yashar cheili yoter me-Hillel avatleinei} \u8212- Shmuel was not saying that he could annul it. He fully recognized that he could not, because {\i pruzbul} remains grounded in Hillel\u8217's authority, which Shmuel did not surpass. The statement was a wishful expression: if only I had the authority greater than Hillel\u8217's, I would do away with it. The practical reality is that as long as the institution rests on Hillel\u8217's enactment, no later {\i beit din} of lesser standing can dislodge it. Shmuel\u8217's two statements together \u8212- limit it to major courts, and wish it could be abolished \u8212- paint a consistent picture of an authority deeply uncomfortable with the institution and doing everything within his power to curtail it.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rav Nachman\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Automatic Pruzbul\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Against Shmuel\u8217's restrictive posture, Rav Nachman says {\i akimnah} \u8212- I would affirm and establish it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Already Established\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} responds with an obvious question: {\i ha-mekayem kayam} \u8212- what do you mean you would establish it? It\u8217's already established. Who are you to re-establish something already in force? This question, too, touches on whether {\i pruzbul} still rests on Hillel\u8217's original authority or whether it requires fresh affirmation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rashi\u8217's View\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} resolves this by explaining that Rav Nachman\u8217's intent was something more radical. {\i Eima bei milta de-af al gav de-la katav ki-katav dami} \u8212- he would declare that even if one did not write a {\i pruzbul}, it is treated as if it were written. As Rashi explains: {\i oshiv beit din ve-atkin de-stam malvin yihyu ke-mosrin shetaroteihen le-veit din de-af al gav de-la ketiv yehei ke-mi she-katav} \u8212- he would convene a {\i beit din} and establish that all lenders by default be considered as having transferred their documents to the {\i beit din}, such that even without any act of writing, a {\i pruzbul} would be automatically and implicitly in effect for every loan.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The contrast between the positions of Shmuel and Rav Nachman could not be starker. Shmuel wants to eliminate or radically restrict {\i pruzbul}; Rav Nachman wants to make it so universal that no affirmative act is required at all.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This range of opinion has direct {\i halachic} implications. Some {\i Rishonim} and {\i poskim} understand the ease with which certain {\i Amora\u8217'im} applied {\i pruzbul} as evidence that they regarded {\i shemittat kesafim} nowadays \u8212- particularly outside of {\i Eretz Yisrael} \u8212- as no more than {\i minhag chasidus}, a praiseworthy custom but not a binding obligation. On this view, even without a {\i pruzbul}, one may still collect one\u8217's loans. Others maintain that it retains at least Rabbinic force and requires some act, even if a minimal one. The question of how seriously to take {\i shemittat kesafim} today has also been explored by Rav Kook as a lens through which to understand the broader debate about the status of {\i shmitta} in our era, including the disputed mechanism of {\i heter mechira} for {\i shemitat karka}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Etymology\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chisda\u8217's Meaning\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} asks: {\i mai pruzbul} \u8212- what does the word {\i pruzbul} mean? Rav Chisda explains: {\i pruz buli u-buti}. The {\i pruz}, which Rashi understands as an establishment or enactment, is for both the {\i buli} and the {\i buti}. {\i Buli eilu ashirim} \u8212- the {\i buli} are the wealthy, as derived from the verse {\i ve-shavarti et ge\u8217'on uzchem}, and as connected with the {\i bulaot she-biYehuda}, the powerful class of Judea. {\i Buti eilu aniyim} \u8212- the {\i buti} are the poor, derived from {\i havet ta\u8217'avitenu}, you shall surely lend them. So {\i pruzbul} is an establishment that benefits both the wealthy, who can collect their loans, and the poor, who will now be lent to.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Greek Origins\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The actual etymology of the word is close to Rav Chisda\u8217's reading but not identical. The word {\i buli} is a Greek term for a council or assembly. As Klein\u8217's dictionary records, the word {\i pruzbul} is probably shortened from the Greek {\i pros boule bouleuteon} \u8212- meaning, presented before the assembly of counselors. The {\i Gemara} identifies the {\i buli} with the {\i ashirim}, the wealthy class, which reflects the social reality that the council members were typically the powerful elite. But the primary meaning of the full term is one who presents a matter before a court or council. This is why the institution is called {\i pruzbul} \u8212- the debt is, as it were, brought before the court. The lower house of the modern Greek legislature is still known as the {\i boule}, the same term.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One of the {\i Amora\u8217'im} suggests another etymology based on a foreign language: {\i porsa de-milta} \u8212- something that fixes or addresses the matter. Rashi seems uncertain about this derivation, but it is recorded alongside Rav Chisda\u8217's explanation.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Land Requirement\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishna\u8217's Rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now turns to the {\i Mishna} from {\i Shevi\u8217'it}: {\i ein kotvin pruzbul ela al ha-karka} \u8212- a {\i pruzbul} may only be written if the debtor has land. This requirement is the focus of the next extended discussion.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Limiting to Large Loans\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Why does {\i pruzbul} require the debtor to hold land? Rashi and Tosafot both offer the following reasoning: the Rabbis established {\i pruzbul} only for the standard, common case of lending, which typically involves a debtor who owns land \u8212- since land is what allows the creditor to collect. One does not ordinarily lend large sums to someone with no assets at all. On small loans, without land present, people may well be content to have {\i shmitta} annul the debt.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This rationale suggests a secondary purpose as well: the land requirement serves to limit {\i pruzbul} to substantial loans, precisely the cases for which the institution was designed. Small debts, for which people are borrowing without any underlying assets, are arguably meant to be absorbed by {\i shmitta}. By requiring land, the Rabbis ensured that {\i pruzbul} would be used only where there is genuine need.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 There is also a second explanation, which anticipates material the {\i Gemara} will develop later. Among the types of debt not annulled by {\i shmitta} even without a {\i pruzbul} is a loan for which the creditor holds collateral \u8212- because in such a case the creditor is not personally exacting payment, and the debt has, in a sense, already been partially collected. The combination of {\i pruzbul} with {\i karka} may similarly be designed to give the debt a quality of partial collection: real estate cannot be hidden, cannot be moved, and can always be seized by a court. The presence of {\i karka} may thus transform the debt from a pure obligation into something more concretely within the creditor\u8217's reach \u8212- lending the {\i pruzbul} a more substantive foundation.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Land Requirement (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Expanded Land\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having established the {\i karka} requirement, the {\i Gemara} addresses what happens when the debtor does not own land. {\i Im ein lo, mezakehu be-toch sodehu} \u8212- if the debtor has no land, the creditor may transfer even a tiny parcel of his own land to the debtor, thereby enabling the writing of a {\i pruzbul}. The {\i Gemara} establishes that {\i kol shehu}, even the most minimal amount, suffices. Rav Chiya bar Ashi citing Rav says: {\i afilu klach shel kruv} \u8212- even a single head of cabbage, even a square inch\u8217's worth of ground, is sufficient.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Yehuda then extends this further: {\i afilu hishilo makom le-tanur ve-chirayim} \u8212- even if the debtor has merely borrowed space from someone on which to place his oven, that constitutes sufficient connection to land to permit writing a {\i pruzbul}. Borrowed land, not even owned, counts.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Overcoming Requirement\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Transfer Strategy\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The practical implication of the expanded definition of {\i karka} is that the creditor can always manufacture the land requirement by transferring a tiny parcel to the debtor. This transfer can be effected through a third party even without the debtor\u8217's active consent, since acquiring land is a {\i zechus} \u8212- a benefit \u8212- for the recipient, and one may confer a {\i zechus} upon a person in their absence. The {\i Gemara} does note the tension: this is ultimately a {\i zechus} that will be used against the debtor\u8217's interests, since it enables the {\i pruzbul} that will allow collection of the debt. Nevertheless, the act is effective.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Perforated Pot\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i braita} citing Hillel himself specifies that one may write a {\i pruzbul} only if the debtor has an {\i atzis nakuv} \u8212- a perforated flower pot. A flower pot with a hole in the bottom is considered to have a connection to the soil beneath it and thus constitutes {\i karka}. One without a hole, {\i eino nakuv}, does not. The {\i Gemara} finds this striking: why should the unperforated pot not qualify?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Borrowed Land\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} raises a sharp objection: {\i ve-ha\u8217'ika mekomo} \u8212- wherever the debtor places his flower pot, he presumably has some right to that space of ground. If borrowed land suffices for {\i pruzbul}, as Rav Yehuda just ruled, then anyone who has so much as a place to set down any possession should have sufficient connection to land. Why, then, does Hillel\u8217's statement require specifically an {\i atzis nakuv}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} answers: {\i lo tzricha de-manach asichi} \u8212- the case is one where the pot is placed on pegs rather than directly on the ground. In such a case, the debtor has rights to use someone\u8217's table or elevated surface, but has not been granted any rights to the ground itself. Therefore, despite the physical presence of his possession, he has no connection to actual land, and the {\i pruzbul} cannot be written. The distinction is fine but operative: it is specifically the right to {\i the ground} that matters, not merely any right of placement.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What the {\i Gemara} is doing throughout this discussion is working both sides of a tension. On one side is the impulse \u8212- consistent with Shmuel\u8217's view \u8212- to maintain {\i pruzbul} as a meaningful, somewhat restricted institution that is not available to everyone. On the other side is the impulse \u8212- consistent with Rav Nachman\u8217's view \u8212- to make it as widely available as possible. The progressive expansion of what counts as {\i karka} reflects the triumph of the latter tendency: in practice, anyone can always come up with some connection to land.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Palm Tree Trunk\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Consistent with that tendency, Rav Ashi in practice transferred a {\i gidma de-dikla} \u8212- the trunk of a palm tree \u8212- to a debtor through a third party and then wrote a {\i pruzbul} upon it. A trunk of a tree serves as the minimal {\i karka} equivalent sufficient to ground the institution.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Verbal Pruzbul\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Academy Practice\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The progression toward simplification reaches its endpoint in the practice of the scholars of Rav Ashi\u8217's academy. {\i Rabbanan d\u8217'bei Rav Ashi} did not write anything at all. They simply verbally transferred their loans to one another. In practice, one would walk over to three colleagues sitting nearby, say words to the effect of \u8220"you are my {\i beit din} and I am transferring my loans to you,\u8221" and the matter was considered complete. No document, no transfer of land, nothing beyond a few words spoken informally among colleagues.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Verbal Transfer\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yochanan similarly transferred his loans verbally to Rabbi Chiya bar Abba. Having done so, Rabbi Chiya bar Abba asked whether anything further was required. Rabbi Yochanan replied: {\i lo tzrichas} \u8212- nothing further is needed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The trajectory from a formal written document executed before a major {\i beit din}, to a verbal declaration before one\u8217's colleagues, reflects the dominant position that in practice {\i pruzbul} should be maximally accessible. Some {\i Rishonim} explain this extreme simplification by suggesting that for these {\i Amora\u8217'im}, the annulment of debts nowadays was regarded as no more than {\i midas chasidus} \u8212- a praiseworthy stringency \u8212- and therefore any minimal gesture acknowledging the institution was sufficient. Others suggest this leniency was confined to {\i talmidei chachamim} who could be trusted not to abuse the device.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 No Land Cases\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Basic Rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Returning to earlier {\i mishnayot} from {\i Shevi\u8217'it} that address situations where the debtor lacks land, the {\i Gemara} now outlines the basic rule: {\i ein lo karka v\u8217'la-arev yesh lo karka} \u8212- if the debtor himself has no land but the guarantor of the loan does, one may write a {\i pruzbul}, since ultimately the creditor could collect from the guarantor\u8217's landed property. {\i Kotvin alav pruzbul} in such a case.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chain Debts\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} extends this further: if neither the debtor nor the guarantor has land, but a third party who owes money {\i to the debtor} has land, that too suffices. I have lent money to Reuven, who has no land. Shimon, who guaranteed the loan, also has no land. But Levi owes money to Reuven, and Levi has land. Since I could ultimately reach Levi\u8217's property through the chain of obligations, {\i kotvin alav pruzbul} \u8212- a {\i pruzbul} may be written.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Natan\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 How does one jump over an intermediate link in a chain of debts? The {\i Gemara} cites Rav Natan: {\i mi-nayin le-noshe be-chavero maneh ve-chavero be-chavero maneh she-motzi\u8217'in mi-zeh ve-notnin le-zeh} \u8212- how do we know that if A is owed money by B, and B is owed money by C, we may collect directly from C to pay A? The {\i Gemara} derives this from the verse {\i ve-natan la-asher asham lo} \u8212- the money is given to the one to whom the guilt is ultimately due. Since that money is ultimately coming to me, I may go directly to the end of the chain and collect. This ability to bypass intermediate parties is what allows the creditor to have constructive access to Levi\u8217's land, thereby satisfying the {\i karka} requirement for {\i pruzbul}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Shmita\u8217's Scope\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 All Annulled\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now surveys {\i Mishnayot} from the tenth chapter of {\i Shevi\u8217'it} \u8212- the chapter devoted entirely to {\i shemittat kesafim}, since there is no {\i Bavli} on {\i Shevi\u8217'it} itself. The {\i Mishna} states: {\i ha-shevi\u8217'it meshametet et ha-malveh bein be-shtar bein she-lo be-shtar} \u8212- the seventh year annuls loans, whether they were made with a document or without.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav and Shmuel\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav and Shmuel explain this {\i Mishna} together: {\i be-shtar} refers to {\i shtar she-yesh bo achrayut nechasim}, a document that has explicitly written into it liens on the debtor\u8217's property. {\i She-lo be-shtar} refers to {\i she-ein bo achrayut nechasim} \u8212- literally a document that lacks those explicit liens, or indeed a verbal loan with no document at all. On this reading, {\i shmitta} annuls everything \u8212- even a {\i shtar} with explicit liens, and {\i kal va-chomer} a purely verbal debt.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yochanan Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yochanan and Resh Lakish read the {\i Mishna} differently. {\i Be-shtar} refers to {\i shtar she-ein bo achrayut nechasim} \u8212- a document without explicit liens. {\i She-lo be-shtar} means a purely verbal loan with no document whatsoever. The implication of this reading is that there exists a third category: {\i shtar she-yesh bo achrayut nechasim}, a document with explicit liens written in, which is {\i not} annulled by {\i shmitta}, even without a {\i pruzbul}. That case is omitted from the {\i Mishna}\u8217's list of what {\i shmitta} annuls, because according to Rabbi Yochanan and Resh Lakish, {\i shmitta} does not touch it at all.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Liens as Ownership\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Right vs.\u160 ?Ownership\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This position of Rabbi Yochanan and Resh Lakish rests on a fundamental conceptual distinction. How does one understand the nature of a lien? Is it a right that a creditor may exercise upon the debtor\u8217's property when collection becomes necessary, which crystallizes only at the moment of collection? Or is it an actual partial ownership of the property itself \u8212- present and real from the moment the {\i shtar} is written, even before any collection occurs?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Some {\i Rishonim} imply the latter view: when a {\i shtar} is written with {\i achrayut nechasim} explicitly stated, that {\i shtar} functions not only as a {\i shtar re\u8217'ayah} \u8212- a document of proof of the debt \u8212- but also as a {\i shtar mecher}, a document that transfers a form of property rights. The creditor does not merely have a claim against the debtor; he has a form of partial ownership in the debtor\u8217's real estate from the moment of the loan.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Consequence\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The consequence of this understanding is direct: {\i shmitta} annuls debts \u8212- obligations of one person to another \u8212- but it cannot annul what is already in a sense one\u8217's own property. If the written {\i achrayut} has already transferred a partial ownership in the land to the creditor, there is no longer a pure debt to be annulled. The debt has been, as it were, partially absorbed into a property right, and {\i shmitta} cannot reach it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Supporting Braita\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} cites a {\i braita} in support of Rabbi Yochanan and Resh Lakish: {\i shtar chov meshamet} \u8212- a loan document is annulled by {\i shmitta}. {\i Ve-im yesh bo achrayut nechasim eino meshamet} \u8212- but if it has liens written into it, it is not annulled. This {\i braita} is adduced independently of {\i pruzbul}; it reflects a baseline {\i halacha} that a document bearing explicit liens is not annulled by {\i shmitta} as a matter of principle.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A second {\i braita} reinforces this: {\i sicheid lo sadeh achat be-halvahato eino meshamet} \u8212- if the creditor specifically identified a particular field from which he would collect in the event of default, {\i shmitta} does not annul the debt. {\i Ve-lo od} \u8212- and furthermore, even if no specific field was identified but the debtor wrote {\i kol nechasai achra\u8217'in ve-arva\u8217'in lach}, all my property is encumbered and pledged to you \u8212- that too is not annulled by {\i shmitta}. This mirrors the position of Rabbi Yochanan and Resh Lakish: the explicit written {\i achrayut}, whether specific or general, transforms the nature of the debt.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Practical Hesitation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Assi\u8217's Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A practical case now arises. A relative of Rav Assi held a {\i shtar} with {\i achrayut nechasim} explicitly written into it, and sought to know: will {\i shmitta} annul this debt? The person was concerned \u8212- understandably, since if {\i shmitta} would annul it, he needed to act promptly, either by writing a {\i pruzbul} or otherwise protecting his claim.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Initial Opinion\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Assi, consistent with the position of Rabbi Yochanan and Resh Lakish, initially ruled that the debt would not be annulled. The written {\i achrayut} gave the creditor a form of property ownership that {\i shmitta} could not reach. The relative, apparently pleased with this answer but wanting authoritative confirmation \u8212- since the ability to actually collect the debt depends not merely on one opinion but on a reliable {\i halachic} consensus \u8212- went to Rabbi Yochanan himself.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Reversal\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yochanan ruled: {\i meshamet} \u8212- the debt {\i will} be annulled. Rav Assi, having heard this, went to Rabbi Yochanan directly and pressed him: but did you yourself, together with Resh Lakish, not rule that a {\i shtar} with {\i achrayut nechasim} is not annulled? Rabbi Yochanan acknowledged the argument but replied: {\i ve-chi mi-penei she-anu medamim na\u8217'aseh ma\u8217'aseh?} \u8212- just because we can construct an argument, does that mean we should act upon it? Of course, the entire enterprise of {\i Shas} is precisely the construction of legal arguments \u8212- but here Rabbi Yochanan is making a more pointed claim. Where there is no explicit textual grounding for a ruling, where it rests on {\i svara} alone, he is not prepared to issue a practical ruling on that basis. The consequence in this case is significant: without a firm ruling in his favor, the creditor must write a {\i pruzbul} to protect his debt.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Beit Shammai\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} pushes back on Rabbi Yochanan\u8217's reversal: but you had a {\i braita} supporting your position! It was not pure {\i svara}. Rabbi Yochanan responds: {\i dilma hi Beit Shammai hi} \u8212- perhaps that {\i braita} follows the opinion of Beit Shammai. Beit Shammai holds, in another context, that {\i shtar ha-omed li-gvot ki-gavuy dami} \u8212- a document ready for collection is treated as if already collected. If that principle underlies the {\i braita}\u8217's ruling, then the {\i braita} may reflect a minority position we do not follow. According to Beit Hillel, until the money is actually paid, it is not in the creditor\u8217's possession, and no anticipatory ownership can be imputed. Rabbi Yochanan, therefore, could not rely on the {\i braita} to issue a practical ruling, lest it be grounded in Beit Shammai\u8217's rejected position. In the end, the wise counsel was to write a {\i pruzbul} and avoid the entire uncertainty.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Other Exemptions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Collateral and Court\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} returns to the {\i Mishna} from {\i Shevi\u8217'it}: {\i ha-malveh et chavero chov al ha-mashkon, ve-ha-moseir shtarotav le-veit din \u8212- ein meshamtin} \u8212- a loan made against collateral, and a loan whose documents were actually handed over to {\i beit din}, are not annulled by {\i shmitta}. This is independent of {\i pruzbul}; it reflects the baseline {\i halacha} that these two categories of debt are simply not subject to {\i shmitta}\u8217's annulment.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Possession Logic\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Be-shlama moseir shtarotav le-veit din} \u8212- when the documents are genuinely handed over to {\i beit din}, the rationale is clear: it is the {\i beit din} that holds the documents and is empowered to collect, and the creditor is no longer personally exacting payment. The prohibition of {\i lo yigosh} \u8212- the creditor shall not press the debtor \u8212- is therefore not violated, and the debt, having been placed in the court\u8217's hands, is as if already somewhat collected. This is precisely the model on which Hillel constructed {\i pruzbul} as a legal fiction: the {\i pruzbul} works {\i ke-mosir shtarotav le-veit din}, as if one has handed the documents to the court, even though the actual giving over is a formal fiction.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But the {\i mashkon}, the collateral case, requires its own explanation. Why should holding collateral exempt a loan from {\i shmitta}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Living on Property\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye poses a sharp question: {\i ella me\u8217'ata hilvehu ve-dar be-chatzeiro, d\u8217'tafsilei, hachi nami ve-lo meshamet?} \u8212- if you lent someone money and happen to be living on his property, would we also say the debt is not annulled? As Tosafot clarifies, the case is one where you are not merely a tenant but are living there by virtue of a right connected to the loan \u8212- in essence, a physical presence in the debtor\u8217's property linked to the credit relationship. If mere possession is the logic, why does living on the debtor\u8217's property not equally protect the debt from {\i shmitta}?\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Mashkon Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Distinct Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} answers through a distinction. Living on the debtor\u8217's property, even with a legal connection to the loan, does not create ownership in the property. But a {\i mashkon} \u8212- collateral \u8212- is different, because the creditor has a form of actual ownership over the collateral itself.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yitzchak Drasha\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yitzchak derives this from a {\i pasuk}: {\i m\u8217'nayin le-ba\u8217'al chov she-koneh mashkon} \u8212- how do we know that the creditor owns the collateral to some degree? From the verse that describes the mitzvah of returning a pledge: {\i u-lecha tihiyeh tzedakah} \u8212- and it shall be accounted to you as righteousness. {\i Im eino koneh, tzedakah m\u8217'nayin?} \u8212- if you did not own it, where does the {\i tzedaka} come from? {\i Tzedaka} by definition involves giving away something of yours to another. The act of returning the collateral is described as {\i tzedaka} because the collateral belongs, in some sense, to the creditor. By returning it, the creditor is performing an act of generosity with property that is, at that moment, legally his.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Already Collected\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The consequence follows directly. Since the creditor has a form of ownership over the collateral, the debt has been, in a meaningful sense, already partially collected \u8212- it is no longer a pure obligation floating between two parties, but an obligation partially secured in the creditor\u8217's actual possession. Therefore, {\i shmitta} cannot annul it. The same {\i mechaber} of logic that produced the analysis of {\i achrayut nechasim} as partial ownership of land applies here: where a debt has been absorbed into some form of property right or actual possession, {\i shmitta} cannot reach it.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Debt Ownership\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Spectrum\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i sugya} as a whole traces a spectrum of debt types, distinguished by the degree to which the creditor\u8217's claim has been absorbed into something more concrete than a pure interpersonal obligation. At one end sits the purely verbal debt \u8212- {\i milveh al peh} \u8212- a bare obligation with no documentation, no lien, no collateral, and no possession. {\i Shmitta} annuls this most readily. Moving along the spectrum: a {\i shtar} without explicit liens, a {\i shtar} with explicit {\i achrayut nechasim} debated by the {\i Amora\u8217'im}, a loan against which the creditor holds collateral and thereby has genuine ownership, and finally a debt whose documents have actually been transferred to {\i beit din} and are thus effectively in the court\u8217's hands. At the far end, the debt is as close to already collected as a debt can be without actually having been paid. The institution of {\i pruzbul} functions by positioning a debt, through legal fiction or actual transfer, somewhere toward the collected end of this spectrum \u8212- and thus out of {\i shmitta}\u8217's reach.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Gittin 38\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Shmitat Kesafim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Creditor\u8217's Obligation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara opens by citing a {\i mishnah} from {\i Shevi\u8217'it}: {\i hamachzir chov lechaveiro bishmita tzarich she\u8217'yomar lo meshameit ani} \u8212- one who returns a debt to his fellow during {\i shmita} must be told by the creditor, \u8220"I release you.\u8221" If the debtor then says {\i af al pi chen} \u8212- \u8220"nevertheless, I still wish to repay\u8221" \u8212- the creditor is permitted to accept the money, as derived from the verse {\i v\u8217'zeh dvar hashmita}: this is the \u8220"matter\u8221" of {\i shmita}, referring to speech \u8212- the creditor must verbally articulate the release.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This raises an immediate question about the mechanics of {\i shmitat kesafim}. We have generally assumed that {\i shmita} automatically annuls debts \u8212- {\i shamot kol ba\u8217'al masheh yado}. So what precisely is the relationship between the creditor\u8217's verbal declaration of {\i meshameit ani} and the automatic annulment? Is the debt erased by force of law, with the declaration serving merely as a reinforcement? Or is there some other structure at work?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rosh\u8217's Two-Stage View\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Rosh} offers what is perhaps the most compelling resolution. He reads the term {\i bishmita} \u8212- during the {\i shmitta} year \u8212- with precision. According to the {\i Rosh}, during the year of {\i shmita} itself, the underlying debt still legally exists; what the Torah prohibits is the active demanding or exacting of that debt. The obligation of {\i meshameit ani} is thus the creditor\u8217's active participation in that prohibition: you declare \u8220"I release you,\u8221" and your verbal release reflects the commandment not to press for payment. Only at the conclusion of {\i shmita} \u8212- at the onset of the eighth year \u8212- is the debt automatically and permanently annulled.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This reading elegantly explains the {\i mishnah}\u8217's scenario. During {\i shmita}, the debt is real but uncollectable. If the debtor voluntarily appears and wishes to pay, the debt still technically exists, which is precisely why the creditor may accept the payment once the debtor says {\i af al pi chen}. An alternative reading \u8212- that the debt has already been erased and repayment is simply a moral gesture, like reciprocating a Shabbos invitation \u8212- is possible but less legally satisfying. The {\i Rosh}\u8217's framework gives the entire structure a cleaner internal logic.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Pressuring for Repayment\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} pushes further on the concept of {\i af al pi chen} as a vehicle for recovering the debt. Rabba states: {\i talvei lei ad d\u8217'amar hachi} \u8212- one may \u8220"string him up\u8221" until he says {\i af al pi chen}, meaning one may apply pressure to get the debtor to voluntarily declare his intention to repay. The creditor is not demanding payment as a debt; he is urging the debtor to understand his moral responsibility.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Abaye\u8217's Objection\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye challenges this directly. The {\i mishnah} elsewhere specifies that when the debtor does repay after the creditor has said {\i meshameit ani}, he should not say \u8220"I am giving this to you on account of my debt\u8221" \u8212- {\i be-chovi ani noten lach} \u8212- but rather \u8220"this is mine, and I am giving it to you as a gift\u8221" \u8212- {\i sheli hen u-v\u8217'matana ani noten lach}. If the very framing of the repayment must avoid any acknowledgment of debt, how can one pressure the debtor to pay at all? That pressure itself seems to acknowledge that a real debt is being pursued.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava responds: {\i talyuhu v\u8217'yahiv} \u8212- you pressure him until he gives it as a gift. The pressure is not framed as debt collection; it is an appeal to moral conscience. You are not saying \u8220"pay me what you owe me\u8221" but rather nudging him to understand that it is the right thing to do, and then he gives \u8212- voluntarily, framed as a gift. This is admittedly a fine line, and even a somewhat counterintuitive one. As the lecturer notes, it sits in obvious tension with the spirit of {\i shmitat kesafim}, much as the {\i pruzbul} does \u8212- it is the {\i Gemara} trying to balance the ideal of debt release with the practical reality that creditors have legitimate interests and debtors have genuine moral obligations. According to the {\i Rosh}\u8217's reading, however, this is somewhat less strained: if the debt truly still exists during {\i shmita}, then the debtor is being encouraged to honor an obligation that is real, even if uncollectable.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Abba bar Marta Story\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} illustrates this tension through a vivid narrative. Abba bar Marta \u8212- also known as Abba bar Minyumi \u8212- owed Rabba money. During {\i shmita}, he came to repay the loan. Rabba did the right thing and said {\i meshameit ani} \u8212- \u8220"I release you.\u8221" The debtor took Rabba at his word, pocketed the release, and left with his money. Abaye later found Rabba in a state of anguish and asked what was wrong. Rabba explained what had happened: he had done exactly what he was supposed to do, and the debtor had simply left without understanding the script he was meant to follow.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye \u8212- the same Abaye who had challenged the legitimacy of applying pressure \u8212- went to the debtor and, with notable subtlety, walked him through the exchange. He asked: \u8220"Did you bring your money to the Master?\u8221" Yes. \u8220"What did he say?\u8221" {\i Meshameit ani}. \u8220"And did you say to him {\i af al pi chen}?\u8221" No.\u160 ?\u8220"Had you said {\i af al pi chen}, he would have happily accepted it.\u8221" Abaye then instructed him: go back now, bring the money, and say {\i af al pi chen}. The debtor did so, and Rabba accepted it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This story is striking on multiple levels. Rabba himself, after the fact, said that the debtor \u8212- a Torah scholar \u8212- should have known better, since the mechanism of {\i af al pi chen} is explicitly stated in a {\i mishnah} in {\i Shevi\u8217'it}. But more striking is Abaye\u8217's conduct: having challenged the very principle of pressuring debtors, he himself applied exactly such pressure \u8212- albeit gentle and indirect \u8212- in service of his own {\i rebbe}\u8217's financial interests. The {\i Gemara} does not comment on this irony, but it is unmistakable.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Pruzbul\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Nachman\u8217's Leniency\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now pivots to the {\i pruzbul} \u8212- the mechanism instituted by Hillel to circumvent {\i shmitat kesafim} by transferring debts to a court, which is not subject to the {\i shmitta} release. Rav Nachman rules: {\i ne\u8217'eman adam lomar pruzbul hayah be-yadi ve-avad} \u8212- a person is believed to claim that he had a {\i pruzbul} and lost it, without having to produce the document. This is a significant leniency: previously we had seen opinions suggesting that certain {\i talmidei chakhamim} might not even need a formal written {\i pruzbul} or could rely on a verbal declaration; Rav Nachman does not go quite that far, but he does rule that production of the document in court is not required.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rationale: Presumed Dili\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The reasoning is straightforward: {\i keivan de-tikinu rabanan pruzbul} \u8212- since the Rabbis instituted the {\i pruzbul} as the legitimate and permitted path for collecting debts after {\i shmita}, {\i lo shevik hete\u1080 ?\u1088 ?\u1072 ? ve-achil issurah} \u8212- we presume that people would not abandon the permissible path and proceed in a transgressive manner. In other words, anyone who is claiming to collect a debt almost certainly wrote a {\i pruzbul}; we simply presume they did. This is a generalized principle used elsewhere in halakhic reasoning: where a straightforward permissible path exists, we assume people availed themselves of it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava\u8217's Suggestion\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava applied this principle in practice. When a case came before him and the creditor had not produced a {\i pruzbul}, Rava asked him directly: {\i midi pruzbul havah lach ve-avad?} \u8212- \u8220"Is it possible that you had a {\i pruzbul} and you lost it?\u8221" This is the principle of {\i ptach picha le-ilem} \u8212- \u8220"open up your mouth to the mute\u8221" \u8212- the idea that someone who had indeed written a {\i pruzbul} and lost it might not even think to assert that fact. Rava\u8217's prompt invited the creditor to recall and state what was presumably the truth. There is, the lecturer acknowledges, something of a wink and nod in this exchange \u8212- Rava is actively facilitating the creditor\u8217's claim \u8212- but the underlying principle is sound: we presume people acted in the permitted manner.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishnah Contradiction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A difficulty arises from a {\i mishnah} that seems to state the opposite: {\i ha-motzi shtaro ve-ein imo pruzbul harei elu lo yifare\u8217'u} \u8212- a creditor who produces his loan document without an accompanying {\i pruzbul} may not collect. This appears to require production of the {\i pruzbul}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Evolving Standards\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} resolves this as a {\i tannaitic} dispute: a {\i baraita} cites the Sages as saying that the {\i pruzbul} need not be produced alongside the loan document. Beyond this formal resolution, there is a broader historical explanation available: the {\i pruzbul} at the time of the {\i mishnah} was a weightier and more formal institution, and its requirements were accordingly more stringent. Over time, as the institution developed and became more routine, the standards relaxed \u8212- consistent with the general pattern of the {\i pruzbul} becoming progressively easier to utilize, including the loosening of requirements around land ownership and other formal conditions. The principle of {\i lo shevik hete\u1080 ?\u1088 ?\u1072 ? ve-achil issurah} thus becomes ever more operative as the {\i pruzbul} becomes ever more accessible.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Enslaved Captive\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishnah Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} transitions to a {\i mishnah} in an entirely different domain, though it remains within the broader framework of {\i tikkun olam} \u8212- the organizing theme of this chapter. Interestingly, the {\i mishnah} itself does not mention {\i tikkun olam}, and it is not immediately clear from the {\i mishnah\u8217's} language which ruling constitutes the {\i takkanah} and what its social purpose is. That unpacking is left entirely to the {\i Gemara}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} states: {\i eved she-nishba u-fadahu} \u8212- a slave who was taken captive and redeemed. {\i Im le-shum eved} \u8212- if he was redeemed for the purpose of returning to servitude \u8212- {\i yishtabed}, he goes back into servitude. {\i Im le-shum ben chorin} \u8212- if he was redeemed for the purpose of freedom \u8212- {\i lo yishtabed}, he does not return to slavery and goes free.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is intuitively sensible. The slave is someone\u8217's property. Non-Jewish forces have captured him. If his own master or someone acting on the master\u8217's behalf ransoms him back, he returns to his master. But if third parties \u8212- people unconnected to the master \u8212- encounter this captive slave, take pity on him, and redeem him with the intention of setting him free, he goes free.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabban Gamliel\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabban Gamliel dissents: {\i bein kach u-vein kach yishtabed} \u8212- either way, he remains a slave. The rationale behind his position, and the question of which view constitutes the {\i tikkun olam}, will be developed by the {\i Gemara}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ye\u8217'ush Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} asks: {\i bemai askinan?} \u8212- what precisely is the case? The analysis turns on the concept of {\i ye\u8217'ush} \u8212- the master\u8217's renunciation of hope of recovering his slave. Since the slave is legally property, the same principles that govern lost or stolen objects apply. Before {\i ye\u8217'ush}, the master has not relinquished his claim; after {\i ye\u8217'ush}, he has, and the object can pass into the possession of a third party. The question is: in which of these two states does the {\i mishnah}\u8217's case take place?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If it is before {\i ye\u8217'ush}, then when the slave is redeemed {\i le-shum ben chorin} \u8212- for freedom \u8212- why does he go free? He is still fully the master\u8217's property. A third party who purchases stolen property from a thief before the owner\u8217's {\i ye\u8217'ush} cannot claim ownership; similarly, why should these redeemers be able to grant the slave his freedom? The slave is still mine, regardless of what you intended when you paid the captors.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Conversely, if it is after {\i ye\u8217'ush}, then when the slave is redeemed {\i le-shum eved} \u8212- for servitude \u8212- why is he still enslaved? After {\i ye\u8217'ush}, he arguably should already be a free person, since no one owns him. These two questions create the central dilemma that Abaye and Rava each resolve in their own ways.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Abaye: Before Ye\u8217'ush\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye says: {\i le-olam lifnei ye\u8217'ush} \u8212- the case is before {\i ye\u8217'ush}. Before {\i ye\u8217'ush}, {\i le-shum eved ishtabed le-rabo rishon} \u8212- obviously, if redeemed for servitude, he returns to the first master. He is still the master\u8217's property and the redemption simply restores him.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The harder question is {\i le-shum ben chorin}: if he is redeemed for freedom before {\i ye\u8217'ush}, why does he go free? {\i Lo ishtabed lo le-rabo rishon ve-lo le-rabo sheni} \u8212- he is enslaved neither to the first master nor to the second. That the redeemers cannot enslave him is straightforward \u8212- {\i de-ha le-shum ben chorin parkei} \u8212- they redeemed him to be free. But why does he not return to the first master? He was never subject to {\i ye\u8217'ush}; he remains the master\u8217's property. The answer lies in {\i tikkun olam}: {\i dilma mimnei ve-lo parkei} \u8212- we are concerned that if people know that a slave redeemed for freedom will simply be returned to his master, they will hold back and not redeem him at all. We wish to incentivize redemption. People may feel the pull of {\i pidyon shvuyim} \u8212- the obligation to redeem captives \u8212- but only if they perceive the captive as a potential free person do they feel sufficiently motivated to act. Rashi captures this: once a person knows that his redemption will result in the slave\u8217's freedom, he begins to see the slave as a would-be free man and feels the weight of {\i pidyon shvuyim} more acutely.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Therefore, as a {\i tikkun olam}, we rule that if someone redeems the slave {\i le-shum ben chorin} before {\i ye\u8217'ush}, the slave goes free \u8212- even though by strict law he should be returned to the first master.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava: After Ye\u8217'ush\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava takes the opposite position: {\i le-olam le-achar ye\u8217'ush} \u8212- the case is after {\i ye\u8217'ush}. After {\i ye\u8217'ush}, the first master has relinquished his claim. The slave does not automatically become free merely because of {\i ye\u8217'ush}, however; a third party who takes possession of him \u8212- {\i shinui reshut} combined with {\i ye\u8217'ush} \u8212- can acquire rights to him.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Thus, {\i le-shum eved ishtabed}: if redeemed for servitude after {\i ye\u8217'ush}, the redeemer acquires him through the mechanism of {\i ye\u8217'ush ve-shinui reshut}. {\i Le-shum ben chorin, lo yishtabed}: if redeemed for freedom, he goes free \u8212- the redeemer did not intend to acquire him, and the first master has been {\i meya\u8217'esh}. {\i Le-rabo sheni lo} \u8212- the redeemer cannot enslave him because that was not his intent. {\i Le-rabo rishon lama lo \u8212- de-ha le-achar ye\u8217'ush hu} \u8212- and the first master cannot reclaim him because {\i ye\u8217'ush} has already transpired.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 On Rava\u8217's reading, the Sages ({\i chakhamim}) are simply stating the straightforward {\i din}: after {\i ye\u8217'ush}, the outcome depends on the redeemer\u8217's intent. There is no {\i tikkun olam} in the Sages\u8217' position; it is the plain law of {\i ye\u8217'ush ve-shinui reshut}. Rabban Gamliel, by contrast, is the one introducing a {\i tikkun olam}: {\i bein kach u-vein kach yishtabed} \u8212- either way, the slave goes back to the first master. This is not because of any legal logic, but because of the principle articulated by Chizkiyah.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 As Chizkiyah explains: {\i shelo yehei kol echad ve-echad holech u-mapil atzmo le-gayyasot u-mafki\u8217'ah atzmo mi-yad rabo} \u8212- lest every slave deliberately allow himself to be captured by marauding troops as a strategy for escaping his master. If slaves know that capture leads to {\i ye\u8217'ush} and {\i ye\u8217'ush} leads to freedom, they have an incentive to manufacture the situation. The {\i tikkun olam} of Rabban Gamliel is therefore to ensure that whatever happens, the slave returns to his first master \u8212- according to Rashi, {\i le-rabo rishon} specifically, even if the second party intended to enslave him to himself.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is a striking {\i Gemara} precisely because {\i tikkun olam} here is not the expansive, progressive concept it is sometimes romanticized to be. One version of {\i tikkun olam} incentivizes redemption by granting freedom. The other version of {\i tikkun olam} ensures runaway slaves are always returned to their masters, because social order depends on the stability of that institution. Both are {\i tikkun olam} in the technical sense: rabbinic enactments for the sake of societal welfare. They point in opposite directions.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Baraita Challenge\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} cites a {\i baraita} in which Rabban Gamliel himself says to the Sages: {\i keshem she-mitzvah lifdot bnei chorin, kach mitzvah lifdot ha-avadim} \u8212- just as there is a {\i mitzvah} to redeem free persons, so too there is a {\i mitzvah} to redeem slaves. This statement fits naturally with Abaye\u8217's reading: Abaye says the Sages\u8217' position is a {\i tikkun olam} to incentivize redemption, and Rabban Gamliel challenges it by saying people need no special incentive \u8212- there is already an obligation to redeem slaves, just as there is to redeem free persons. On Rava\u8217's reading, however, this statement seems out of place. According to Rava, the Sages are simply stating the {\i din} of {\i ye\u8217'ush}, and Rabban Gamliel is introducing a {\i takanah} to prevent runaways; in that context, his invocation of the {\i mitzvah} to redeem slaves seems entirely beside the point.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava\u8217's Response\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava resolves this by saying: {\i Rabban Gamliel lo hava yada mai kamri rabbanan} \u8212- Rabban Gamliel did not know what exactly the Sages were arguing. He was not responding to a specific position but rather laying out a comprehensive rebuttal to any possible rationale: if you are arguing that the slave goes free before {\i ye\u8217'ush} in order to incentivize redemption, you are wrong \u8212- there is already a {\i mitzvah} to redeem slaves, so no extra incentive is needed. And if you are arguing that after {\i ye\u8217'ush} the slave goes free because that is the plain {\i din}, I also disagree \u8212- we should override that {\i din} because of Chizkiyah\u8217's concern about slaves engineering their own capture. Either way, {\i bein kach u-vein kach yishtabed}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Non-Jewish Slave Ownersh\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Captors\u8217' Rights\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 With the basic framework of the {\i mishnah} established, the {\i Gemara} turns to a foundational question: how do the non-Jewish captors acquire any ownership rights over the slave at all? This matters because, according to Rava, it is only after {\i ye\u8217'ush} that a third party can redeem the slave and enslave him to themselves through {\i ye\u8217'ush ve-shinui reshut}. For this to work, the captors must have some degree of recognized ownership over the slave. If the captors have no rights whatsoever, then the master\u8217's {\i ye\u8217'ush} would leave the slave simply ownerless \u8212- and one might argue he would go free at that very moment, with no mechanism for the second party to acquire him.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} answers: {\i in, kanei lei le-ma\u8217'aseh yadav} \u8212- yes, the captors do acquire him, but only for his labor \u8212- {\i ma\u8217'aseh yadav} \u8212- not as full property. This partial ownership is precisely the concept of {\i kinyan geneivah} or {\i kinyan gezeila}: when a thief or violent aggressor takes possession of someone\u8217's property, they acquire a de facto partial ownership. A well-known consequence of this principle is that a thief who steals an object and is later caught does not owe the owner rental fees for the period of use, because that de facto possession granted him some right of use, however illegitimate his acquisition. The same structure applies here: the captors, by seizing the slave, acquire rights to his labor, even without purchasing him and even without the master\u8217's consent. This partial ownership prevents the slave from being automatically freed by {\i ye\u8217'ush} and enables the subsequent transfer of possession to a Jewish buyer who wishes to enslave him.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Reish Lakish\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This raises a broader question about the structure of slave ownership in halakhic thought. Reish Lakish articulates a general principle: {\i minayin le-oved kochavim she-kanah oved kochavim le-ma\u8217'aseh yadav} \u8212- how do we know that a non-Jew who acquires a non-Jew does so only for his labor ({\i ma\u8217'aseh yadav}) and not as full bodily property ({\i kinyan guf})? This is a striking halakhic distinction. Jews can own non-Jewish slaves as full property \u8212- {\i kinyan guf}. Non-Jews can own other non-Jews, or own Jews, only for their labor \u8212- {\i ma\u8217'aseh yadav}. Only a Jewish master holds full property rights over a non-Jewish slave.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is deeply unsettling on its face. But one suggested rationale, offered in the course of discussion, is that Jewish law does not trust pagan society to exercise the kind of restrained mastery over another human being that full property ownership would entail. The experience of {\i shibud mitzrayim} \u8212- Egyptian bondage \u8212- and the ethical framework that follows from it means that the only context in which full property ownership of a person can be sanctioned is one governed by Torah law. It remains a troubling framework by any measure, but it is the halakhic structure the {\i Gemara} is operating within.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Verse Derivation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The derivation for this distinction comes from the verse in {\i Vayikra}: {\i v\u8217'gam mi-bnei ha-toshavim ha-garim imachem, me-hem tiknu eved va-amah} \u8212- and also from the children of the resident aliens who dwell among you, from them you may purchase slaves. The {\i Gemara} reads this as a limiting verse: {\i atem konim me-hem} \u8212- you may purchase them as full property, {\i ve-lo hem konim mi-kem} \u8212- but they may not purchase you as full property. And {\i ve-lo hem konim zeh mi-zeh l\u8217'gufo} \u8212- nor may they purchase one another as full property. Non-Jews may own non-Jews and may own Jews, but only for {\i ma\u8217'aseh yadav}, not {\i kinyan guf}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The implications of {\i kinyan guf} \u8212- full bodily ownership \u8212- go beyond mere economic rights. As Rashi emphasizes, {\i kinyan guf} is a matter of personal status. If a slave is owned {\i le-gufo}, then to change that status, a formal document of manumission \u8212- a {\i get shichrur} \u8212- is required. Merely renouncing one\u8217's economic claim is insufficient to alter the slave\u8217's halakhic identity. Furthermore, a slave owned {\i le-gufo} cannot simply choose to undergo conversion and thereby redefine his status; his identity is determined by the ownership relationship, and only the master can change it through formal manumission. A slave owned only {\i le-ma\u8217'aseh yadav}, by contrast, has a more fluid status: his labor belongs to another, but his person does not, and if that labor-obligation ends, his status adjusts accordingly.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kal va-Chomer\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i kal va-chomer} is then constructed. If a non-Jew can own a Jew for {\i ma\u8217'aseh yadav} \u8212- as we know from the {\i pesukim} in the Torah describing a Jew who sells himself into servitude to a resident alien \u8212- then certainly a non-Jew can own another non-Jew for {\i ma\u8217'aseh yadav}. The argument is: the {\i chumra} case (a Jew owned by a non-Jew for his labor) establishes the category; the {\i kal} case (a non-Jew owned by a non-Jew for his labor) follows {\i a fortiori}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A complication arises: we know a non-Jew can own a Jew for {\i ma\u8217'aseh yadav} through the mechanism of purchase ({\i be-kaspa}) \u8212- the Jew sells himself. But in our case, the captors did not purchase the slave; they seized him by force. What is the source for acquisition through forceful seizure \u8212- {\i chazakah} \u8212- rather than purchase?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kibbush Milchamah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} introduces the concept of {\i kibbush milchamah} \u8212- forceful conquest as a mode of acquisition. Rav Papa demonstrates this from the episode of Sichon: Sichon captured the lands of Ammon and Moab through military conquest. When the Jewish people subsequently conquered Sichon\u8217's territories, they were permitted to take those lands even though the Torah had commanded {\i al tetzer et Moav} \u8212- do not distress Moab. The resolution was that those territories were no longer Moab\u8217's; Sichon had acquired them through {\i kibbush milchamah}, and they had become Sichon\u8217's lands. When Israel conquered Sichon, they inherited lands that were legitimately Sichon\u8217's. This demonstrates that forceful seizure in war constitutes a recognized form of acquisition. Tosafot identifies this principle explicitly as {\i kibbush milchamah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If this is true of land, the same principle extends to people: a non-Jewish force that captures a slave by force acquires rights to him through {\i kibbush milchamah}, even without any purchase transaction.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Captives Verse\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The final step extends this principle from non-Jews seizing non-Jewish property to non-Jews seizing property belonging to Jews. For the former, the case of {\i Amon u-Moav} and Sichon is sufficient. For the latter \u8212- {\i akum mi-Yisrael minayin?} \u8212- the {\i Gemara} derives it from the verse {\i va-yishbi mimenu shevi} \u8212- and he took captives from them. The fact that the verse uses the noun {\i shevi} (captive) rather than merely saying that a person was taken implies that the captured person acquired the status of {\i shevi} \u8212- captive \u8212- as a result of being seized. The seizure itself changes the person\u8217's status, giving the captors recognized rights to him. This establishes that non-Jewish captors acquire rights \u8212- {\i ma\u8217'aseh yadav} \u8212- over Jewish-owned slaves whom they capture by force, and that those rights persist even after the master\u8217's {\i ye\u8217'ush}, enabling the subsequent transfer of possession.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Escape & Manumission\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Escape Goes Free\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yochanan, cited by Rabbi Shimon bar Abba, teaches: {\i eved she-barach mi-beit ha-asurin yatza le-cheirut} \u8212- a slave who escapes from captivity goes free. Moreover, {\i ko\u1092 ?in rabo ve-kotev lo get shichrur} \u8212- we compel his former master to write him a writ of manumission, to ensure his freedom is formally and publicly established.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Apparent Contradiction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This creates an apparent contradiction with Rabbi Yochanan\u8217's own stated position \u8212- recorded in the name of Rabbah bar bar Chana \u8212- that wherever Rabban Gamliel appears in the {\i mishnah}, the {\i halacha} follows him (with three specified exceptions, none of which is our case). Since our {\i mishnah} features Rabban Gamliel ruling {\i bein kach u-vein kach yishtabed} \u8212- he is always returned to slavery \u8212- and we rule like Rabban Gamliel, how can Rabbi Yochanan simultaneously hold that a slave who escapes goes free?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Abaye Resolves\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 According to Abaye, the resolution is straightforward. Abaye\u8217's reading of the {\i mishnah} is {\i lifnei ye\u8217'ush}: before {\i ye\u8217'ush}, the slave is fully the master\u8217's property and Rabban Gamliel\u8217's ruling \u8212- that he always remains enslaved \u8212- is correct and we rule accordingly. But the case of Rabbi Yochanan \u8212- where the slave escapes his captors \u8212- is {\i le-achar ye\u8217'ush}. After {\i ye\u8217'ush}, the master has no claim, the captors own him only for {\i ma\u8217'aseh yadav}, and when he escapes, no one retains ownership over him. He goes free.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The need for a {\i get shichrur} even after {\i ye\u8217'ush} is not self-evident, since the master\u8217's monetary claim has lapsed. But this is precisely where the distinction between monetary ownership and personal status matters. Even if the master no longer owns the slave in any economic sense \u8212- cannot demand his labor, has no property rights \u8212- the slave\u8217's {\i halakhic status} may still be that of a slave. Personal status is not automatically altered by the lapse of property rights; it may require a formal document \u8212- the {\i shtar shichrur} \u8212- to effect the change.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava: Escape Different\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 For Rava, the resolution is more complex, since on his reading, even {\i le-achar ye\u8217'ush} we apply the {\i takanah} of Chizkiyah \u8212- slaves are always returned to their masters, lest they engineer their own capture. If we rule like Rabban Gamliel based on Chizkiyah\u8217's concern, and Rabbi Yochanan says we always rule like Rabban Gamliel, how can Rabbi Yochanan simultaneously teach that an escaped slave goes free?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava answers: {\i barach shani} \u8212- escape is different. The reason we send slaves back to their masters after {\i ye\u8217'ush} is to disincentivize deliberate self-captivity. But a slave who escapes his captors has demonstrated the opposite: he risked his life \u8212- {\i le-katla masar nafsheih} \u8212- to get out of captivity. Presumably the penalty for escape was death. A person willing to accept mortal risk to flee captivity clearly did not seek out captivity as a means of liberation. The entire concern underlying Chizkiyah\u8217's {\i takanah} simply does not apply to this person. Therefore, even according to Rava, when a slave escapes rather than being redeemed, he goes free \u8212- and Chizkiyah\u8217's {\i takanah} does not operate.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Ownership vs.\u160 ?Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shmuel\u8217's Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now introduces a case involving the maidservant of Shmuel. She was taken captive \u8212- {\i ishtava\u8217'i} \u8212- and a group of people redeemed her {\i le-shum amahuta}, intending to restore her to Shmuel\u8217's service as a gift to him. They sent Shmuel a message: we follow the ruling of Rabban Gamliel ({\i ke-Rabban Gamliel sevira lan}), and even if you hold like the Sages, we redeemed her specifically for servitude \u8212- so please accept her back.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The difficulty is that they had assumed the case was {\i lifnei ye\u8217'ush} \u8212- before Shmuel had given up hope. But in fact, {\i le-achar ye\u8217'ush hava} \u8212- Shmuel had already been {\i meya\u8217'esh} from her. Once this is established, Shmuel had no interest in retaining her in any case, and no one wished to press a claim to her.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shmuel\u8217's Ruling\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} records that Shmuel went further: not only would he not retain her in servitude, but she did not even require a {\i get shichrur} \u8212- a writ of manumission. This reflects Shmuel\u8217's consistent position: {\i ha-mafkir avdo yatza le-cheirut ve-eino tzarich get shichrur} \u8212- one who renounces ownership over his slave, the slave goes free without needing a formal writ. The renunciation of monetary ownership is sufficient to change the slave\u8217's halakhic status entirely.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Verse Interpretation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Shmuel derives this from the verse {\i ve-chol eved ish miknat kasef} \u8212- the slave of a man. The phrase {\i eved ish} \u8212- slave of a man \u8212- implies: {\i eved she-yesh lo reshut rabo alav karui eved} \u8212- a slave is only called a slave when the master retains authority over him. {\i She-ein lo reshut rabo alav, ein karui eved} \u8212- when no such authority exists, he is no longer designated a slave, and his personal status changes accordingly without need for any further document. Monetary status and personal status are thus intertwined; one automatically implies the other.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Abba Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} then brings the parallel case of the maidservant of Rabbi Abba bar Zutra. She was taken captive and redeemed by a non-Jew \u8212- {\i tarmuda\u8217'ah} \u8212- who intended to take her as a wife. Jewish community members sent word to Rabbi Abba: if you have been {\i meya\u8217'esh} from her, send her a {\i get shichrur}. But the {\i Gemara} asks: what would the {\i get shichrur} accomplish? If these community members have the financial means to redeem her, they can simply do so and return her to Rabbi Abba; there is no need for a writ. And if they cannot afford to redeem her, how does sending a writ of manumission help?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Writ Purposes\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} resolves this with two alternative explanations. The first: they do have the means to redeem her, but the point of the {\i get shichrur} is to incentivize broader communal participation. {\i Keivan de-mashdar lah gita de-cheiruta} \u8212- once a writ of manumission is sent and it becomes known that she is a free woman, people will feel a stronger obligation of {\i pidyon shvuyim} \u8212- redeeming a free person is more compelling than redeeming a slave \u8212- and they will pool their resources to buy her back. This parallels Abaye\u8217's {\i tikkun olam} reasoning from the {\i mishnah}: the prospect of freedom is what mobilizes people.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The second approach: they cannot afford to redeem her. But if Rabbi Abba sends a {\i get shichrur}, it becomes publicly known that she had been a slave. This embarrasses the non-Jew who wishes to take her as a wife \u8212- publicly known as the wife of a former slave is not a dignity he wishes \u8212- and he will therefore lower his asking price to a level they can afford. The {\i get shichrur} here is not about changing her status for its own sake, but about creating public information that changes the non-Jew\u8217's leverage. The {\i Gemara} qualifies: non-Jews may be privately happy to take advantage of opportunities involving Jewish-owned property, but {\i be-farhesia} \u8212- publicly \u8212- {\i zila behu milta} \u8212- such things are embarrassing to them. Public exposure thus becomes a tool for reducing the ransom price.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Branded Woman Story\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Wedding Discovery\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} concludes with a remarkable narrative drawn from {\i Bava Kamma} (daf 114). A man in Lod married off his son. Guests came to the celebration, and as the host\u8217's wife poured wine for the assembled guests, her sleeves shifted and her arms were exposed. The guests saw that she had a {\i kivuyah} \u8212- a brand \u8212- on her arm, the kind of mark used to identify slaves as property of their owner. The implication was immediate and devastating: if she was a branded slave who had never been formally freed, she could not legally marry a Jew. Her husband\u8217's marriage was no marriage. Their son, whose wedding was at that very moment being celebrated, was himself a slave. The entire gathering fell under a cloud.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Martyrdom Explanation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The host addressed the whispers directly. He acknowledged the brand but offered an explanation: his wife had been taken captive by non-Jews who sought to compel her to serve their idols. She refused \u8212- categorically, repeatedly. In response, they tortured her; they took a red-hot iron and burned her arms to break her will. She endured the torture and still did not submit to {\i avodah zarah}. The brand on her arms was not a slave\u8217's mark; it was the mark of a {\i tzadeket}, a holy woman who had accepted physical torment rather than violate her faith.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Acceptance\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The guests believed him entirely. And the {\i Gemara} records that they not only accepted the explanation but honored him more after hearing it \u8212- contributing additional money and expressing greater esteem for him than they had before.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gemara\u8217's Lesson\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} draws a careful lesson. Ordinarily, a woman who claims she was captured and mistreated is not simply believed to explain away evidence of slave status. The {\i mishnah} elsewhere makes clear that such claims require substantiation. Why was she believed here? Because there was tangible physical evidence that required explanation. The brand {\i existed}; it was visible to everyone. Given that evidence, the question was not whether to believe a claim out of nowhere, but how to interpret an observable fact. The choice was between two explanations \u8212- slave branding or torture for refusing idolatry \u8212- and given that she was a Jewish woman of apparent standing and reputation, the more favorable explanation was accepted. The underlying principle is that credible, corroborating physical evidence creates a context in which alternative explanations can be credited. Without such evidence \u8212- if a woman simply makes an unsubstantiated claim \u8212- she is not believed. The physical reality of the brand is what opens the door to the charitable reading. Without it, the door remains closed.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Gittin 39\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Freeing Slaves\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Yehuda/Shmuel\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The daf opens at the very bottom of 38a, in the midst of a discussion concerning slaves and {\i tikkun olam} \u8212- though not necessarily in the direction one might have expected. The Gemara presents the following case: there was a female slave in Pumbedita ({\i hahi amata de\u8217'hava be-Pumbedita}) who was being sexually exploited ({\i de\u8217'hava kam\u8217'avdi ba issura}). Abaye declared that were it not for the ruling of Rav Yehuda in the name of Shmuel \u8212- {\i kol hamesachrer avdo over be-asei}, anyone who frees his slave transgresses a positive commandment \u8212- he would have compelled the master to write her a {\i get shichrur}, a writ of manumission, so that she could achieve full free status and thereby be protected from abuse.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The positive commandment in question derives from the verse {\i le\u8217'olam bahem ta\u8217'avodu} \u8212- you shall enslave them forever. The irony of this {\i mitzvat asei} is not lost: given the centrality of {\i Yetziat Mitzrayim} to the Jewish moral imagination, a positive obligation to maintain the institution of slavery is striking. One might distinguish between the Torah tolerating the institution of slavery \u8212- which could be understood as a concession to the realities of the ancient world \u8212- and actively reinforcing it through a prohibitory commandment. The latter is harder to account for. Perhaps the concern was systemic: freeing an individual slave without addressing the underlying economic structure may have been seen as disruptive in ways that were not necessarily in the slave\u8217's best interest. Or perhaps the commandment was understood as applying only when there is no legitimate purpose \u8212- a reading that several {\i Rishonim} favor, as we will see shortly.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Abaye\u8217's Exception\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Ravina qualifies the ruling: {\i ki ha modei Rav Yehuda} \u8212- Rav Yehuda himself would concede that in a case of ongoing transgression ({\i mishum milsa de\u8217'issura}), freeing the slave is permitted. Whether this means the prohibition is overridden or simply inapplicable when there is legitimate cause is a matter of debate among the {\i Rishonim}, and the Gemara returns to this question when it reaches the famous case of Rabbi Eliezer and the {\i minyan}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Half-Slave Woman\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now raises a challenge: there is a teaching ({\i ma\u8217'aseh b\u8217'ishah achas}) about a woman who was half-slave, half-free \u8212- owned jointly by two partners, one of whom freed her and the other did not. Her status was entirely ambiguous: she could not marry a Jewish man, nor could she marry a slave. Rav Nachman bar Yitzchak explains that people were treating her with contempt, exploiting her as if she were ownerless ({\i minhag hefker nahagu bah}). In that case the master was compelled to free her.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Does this case not demonstrate that Abaye should have acted similarly with the slave in Pumbedita? The Gemara distinguishes the two: in the case of the half-free woman, there was no alternative \u8212- {\i lo l\u8217'eved chazya v\u8217'lo l\u8217'ven chorin chazya}, she could neither marry a slave nor a free man. In the Pumbedita case, however, it would have been possible to arrange for her a slave husband who could protect her ({\i efshar d\u8217'meyachad lah avdi v\u8217'minatrah}). Since an alternative existed that did not require overriding the {\i mitzvat asei}, Abaye felt constrained from compelling her manumission.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Eliezer Minyan\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara returns to examine the core ruling of Rav Yehuda in the name of Shmuel. It raises a well-known challenge: {\i ma\u8217'aseh b\u8217'Rabbi Eliezer shenichnas l\u8217'veit ha\u8217'knesses v\u8217'lo matza asarah} \u8212- Rabbi Eliezer entered the synagogue and did not find ten men to form a {\i minyan}. He freed his slave ({\i shichrar avdo}) and thereby completed the quorum ({\i v\u8217'hishlimo l\u8217'asarah}).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara responds: {\i mitzvah shani} \u8212- for a {\i mitzvah}, the case is different. This formulation invites scrutiny. If the prohibition against freeing slaves is a {\i mitzvat asei d\u8217'oraita}, how does a rabbinic obligation to maintain a {\i minyan} override it? The Ramban addresses this directly, citing the Gemara\u8217's teaching that when God arrives at a synagogue and finds fewer than ten people assembled, divine anger is immediate. The theological weight of the {\i minyan} thus transcends the technical question of whether it is {\i d\u8217'oraita} or {\i d\u8217'rabbanan}. Other {\i Rishonim}, however, favor the alternative reading: the prohibition against freeing slaves was never intended to apply when there is a legitimate purpose. On this reading, Rabbi Eliezer did not override the commandment \u8212- he simply did not transgress it, because the commandment is limited to gratuitous manumission.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might further ask: when the slave is freed, does he not undergo a kind of {\i geirus}? In fact, the acts associated with conversion \u8212- {\i milah} and {\i tevilah} \u8212- were already performed when he was made a slave. But those acts were, in a sense, held in suspension by the reality of his enslavement. Upon manumission, that earlier status is released and given full expression. The Gemara does not view the prospect of the slave accumulating additional {\i mitzvot} through freedom as sufficient grounds by itself to mandate manumission; the concern about actively overriding a {\i mitzvat asei} remains the operative consideration.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yishmael vs Akiva\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i baraita} is then cited: {\i Tanu Rabbanan} \u8212- from the verse {\i le\u8217'olam bahem ta\u8217'avodu}, Rabbi Yishmael derives that maintaining a slave is merely permitted ({\i reshut}), not obligatory. Rabbi Akiva, by contrast, rules {\i chova} \u8212- it is an obligation. The Gemara then considers whether Rabbi Eliezer, who freed his slave for the {\i minyan}, might have held like Rabbi Yishmael. This is rejected: a {\i baraita} states explicitly that {\i Rav Elazar omer chova} \u8212- Rav Elazar himself rules it is an obligation, the position of Rabbi Akiva. His freeing of the slave must therefore be explained on other grounds, as discussed above.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Three Ways Lose Wealth\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava offers a related observation: {\i behani telat mili nacheti ba\u8217'alei batim minichseihon} \u8212- there are three ways in which wealthy householders descend from their wealth. First, freeing their slaves. Second, inspecting their property on {\i Shabbos} ({\i desayrei nichseihon beshabta}). Third, establishing their main meal on {\i Shabbos} at the time when the public should be attending the rabbinic lecture in the {\i beit midrash} ({\i dekeva\u8217'ei seudasa beshabta be\u8217'idna debei midrasha}).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The convergence of these three cases suggests something beyond mere economic observation. There is clearly a {\i mida kineged mida} dimension: in each case, the person imagines they are acting generously or properly, but in fact they are misusing what has been entrusted to them \u8212- their slaves, their property, or their table \u8212- and they are punished accordingly. In the case of {\i Shabbos} morning, a lavish {\i kiddush} scheduled to coincide with the public lecture is not {\i oneg Shabbos} \u8212- it is a violation of the communal obligation to learn.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Chiya bar Abba in the name of Rabbi Yochanan adds a historical illustration: two prominent families in Jerusalem were destroyed ({\i ne\u8217'ekru}) because of misuse of their {\i Shabbos} meals \u8212- one made its major feast on {\i Shabbos} day at the wrong time, and one on {\i erev Shabbos}. Rashi offers two explanations for the problem with the {\i erev Shabbos} family. One is that they made their feast on Friday night; Rashi notes that the principle {\i kavod yom kodem le\u8217'kavod laila} \u8212- the honor of the day takes precedence over the night \u8212- means that by making Friday night more lavish than {\i Shabbos} day they inverted the proper hierarchy. When told not to feast during the {\i shiur} on {\i Shabbos} day, they shifted to Friday night \u8212- but introduced a new problem. Rashi\u8217's second explanation is that they feasted Friday afternoon, arriving at {\i Shabbos} already satiated and unable to honor {\i Shabbos} with the proper appetite.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Methods of Freedom\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sanctification Model\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now transitions from the permissibility of freeing a slave to the mechanisms by which a slave may actually become free. Rava cites Rav: {\i hamakdish avdo yatza l\u8217'cheirus} \u8212- if a person sanctifies ({\i makdish}) his slave, the slave goes free. The mechanism is not obvious, and Rashi and Tosafot understand it differently.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara\u8217's first step is to note {\i gufeih lo kadesh} \u8212- you cannot actually make the slave\u8217's body holy in the direct sense. Rashi understands this to mean that you cannot sanctify him as you would a beam designated for the {\i Beis Hamikdash} \u8212- he cannot be treated as a physical object consecrated to Temple use. Tosafot reads the principle differently: the slave cannot become {\i assur behana\u8217'ah}, forbidden in benefit, as consecrated property typically becomes, because he remains a human being. It is impossible for a person to be forbidden in benefit \u8212- he cannot refrain from benefiting himself, nor can others be categorically prohibited from all contact with him. Tosafot therefore understands {\i gufeih lo kadesh} to mean that he cannot acquire the standard legal character of {\i hekdesh} property.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rashi then raises the obvious alternative: perhaps when one says {\i makdish avdo}, one means that the slave is transferred to the ownership of the {\i Beis Hamikdash} to be sold for his monetary value \u8212- {\i lidmei}. Rashi\u8217's answer to this is striking. He invokes the principle of automatic upgrade of holiness ({\i kedushah}): just as an unblemished animal designated to be sold by {\i hekdesh} automatically acquires full sacrificial sanctity rather than the limited sanctity one attempted to impose, so too a slave who is made {\i makdish} cannot have his {\i kedushah} artificially limited. The maximum expression of {\i kedushah} that can apply to a human being is membership in the holy people \u8212- {\i am kadosh}. Therefore, Rashi says, when one sanctifies a slave, the {\i kedushah} manifests itself as freedom: the slave becomes a free Jew.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Renouncing Ownership\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Yosef then cites Rav\u8217's parallel teaching: {\i hamafkir avdo yatza l\u8217'cheirut} \u8212- if one renounces ownership ({\i mafkir}) of his slave, the slave goes free. This is in one sense more intuitive: if the slave is no longer owned by anyone, he is no longer enslaved as property. The Gemara notes the logical relationship between the two cases: whoever accepts the more counterintuitive proposition \u8212- that sanctification ({\i makdish}) results in freedom \u8212- will certainly accept the more straightforward case of renouncing ownership ({\i mafkir}). Conversely, one who begins only from the {\i mafkir} case need not accept the {\i makdish} case, since there the natural reading would be that the slave becomes owned by {\i hekdesh} for his monetary value.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Status vs Property\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This generates the central question of the sugya: {\i tzarich get shichrur o lo?} When an owner verbally renounces property ownership of a slave \u8212- whether by {\i makdish} or {\i mafkir} \u8212- is that declaration alone sufficient to change the slave\u8217's personal status? Or does the slave\u8217's personal status, his identity as an {\i eved}, require a separate {\i get shichrur} even after property ownership has ended?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara here revisits the position of Shmuel, mentioned previously in the sugya: {\i eved ish} \u8212- a slave is a slave only as long as he is owned by someone. On Shmuel\u8217's view, personal status follows property ownership automatically: the moment ownership ends, the personal status of slavery ends with it, and no writ is required. But this position is now placed in tension with other opinions.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Get Required\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara resolves the question with the ruling of Rav Chiyya bar Avin in the name of Rav: {\i echad zeh v\u8217'echad zeh yatza l\u8217'cheirus v\u8217'tzarich get shichrur} \u8212- in both the case of {\i makdish} and the case of {\i mafkir}, the slave is no longer enslaved in the property sense but still requires a {\i get shichrur} to change his personal status. This is an explicit rejection of Shmuel\u8217's position: property ownership and personal status are treated as legally distinct, and the cessation of one does not automatically entail the cessation of the other.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Treasurers Authority\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava then raises a challenge to Rav\u8217's position on {\i makdish}. A {\i baraita} teaches: if someone declares {\i hamakdish kol nechasai} \u8212- all my property is sanctified \u8212- and among that property are slaves, the {\i gizbarin} (the Temple treasurers, administrators of {\i hekdesh}) may not free those slaves. Rather, they may sell them to others, and those others may then free them. This {\i baraita} appears to assume that when a master sanctifies his property including slaves, the slaves become owned by {\i hekdesh} \u8212- they do not automatically become free. This directly contradicts Rav\u8217's position.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rashi Value Model\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara\u8217's first response is that Rav, who was of the transitional generation between {\i tanna\u8217'im} and {\i amora\u8217'im}, is permitted to disagree with a {\i baraita} ({\i Rav tanna u\u8217'falig}). The Gemara then tries to find a reading of a subsequent source \u8212- a verse involving {\i cherem} \u8212- that would be consistent with Rav\u8217's position, and asks whether one could simply say that in all the problematic cases the owner specified {\i ledmai}, that he was sanctifying only for monetary value. If so, Rav would agree that the slave is not automatically freed, since the owner limited the consecration explicitly.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rashi\u8217's answer to this question is distinctive. For Rashi, even an explicit {\i ledmai} \u8212- \u8220"I sanctify him for his monetary value\u8221" \u8212- does not avoid the automatic upgrade problem if the slave himself is being sanctified. The {\i kedushah} imposed on the slave himself will always express itself maximally as freedom. The only way to avoid the slave going free, on Rashi\u8217's reading, is if the owner never sanctified the slave at all \u8212- instead, the owner accepted a personal monetary obligation to pay the slave\u8217's value to the {\i Beis Hamikdash}. In that case, {\i ledmai} means not \u8220"the slave is consecrated for his value\u8221" but \u8220"I personally undertake to pay his value\u8221"; the slave himself is entirely unsanctified and remains enslaved.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This reading allows Rashi to explain why, in the case of the {\i gizbarin}, the {\i baraita} speaks of the treasurers selling the slave and others freeing him: clearly the slave himself has been consecrated and is owned by {\i hekdesh}, since the treasurers are transacting with him directly. That scenario is therefore one that Rav must reject \u8212- it represents a position incompatible with his own, and that is why the Gemara required the answer of {\i Rav tanna u\u8217'falig}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Meir Principle\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then cites a further {\i baraita}: {\i hamakdish avdo oseh v\u8217'ochel shelo hikdish ela damav} \u8212- if one sanctifies his slave, the slave continues to work and consume as before, for the owner only sanctified his monetary value. Here the slave does not go free; the {\i baraita} seems to present a middle position in which the slave is not transferred to {\i hekdesh} ownership and is not freed, but the owner bears an obligation to pay his value.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara identifies this as the position of Rabbi Meir, based on his principle {\i ein adam motzi devarav l\u8217'vatala} \u8212- a person does not issue words for naught. The application here runs as follows: when an owner says {\i makdish avdo}, he cannot literally sanctify the slave\u8217's body (for the reasons discussed), so his words must be given some other valid legal meaning. Rabbi Meir\u8217's principle directs us to reinterpret the declaration as the owner accepting a personal financial obligation \u8212- to pay the slave\u8217's market value to the {\i Beis Hamikdash}. This is similar to Rabbi Meir\u8217's ruling elsewhere that if someone says {\i erko alai} \u8212- his assessed value is upon me \u8212- for a person whose {\i erech} under Torah law is zero (such as an infant under one month old), we do not conclude that nothing is owed; rather, we reinterpret the word {\i erech} to mean the person\u8217's market value.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rashi explains that under this model, the slave himself is entirely unsanctified \u8212- {\i lo hikdish ela damav} means the owner assumed a personal debt, not that the slave acquired any sacred status. Accordingly, the slave continues to work, the master retains his labor, and no question of automatic upgrade arises. For Rav, there are thus two possible models when one is {\i makdish} a slave: either the sanctification applies to the slave himself and automatically results in freedom (the standard {\i makdish} case), or the owner\u8217's declaration is reinterpreted as a personal financial commitment and the slave is not sanctified at all (the Rabbi Meir model). The {\i baraita} of {\i hamakdish avdo oseh v\u8217'ochel} reflects the second model, and the Gemara confirms this by noting that a parallel clause \u8212- {\i v\u8217'chen hu she\u8217'hikdish atzmo}, if the slave sanctifies himself \u8212- makes sense only under Rabbi Meir\u8217's reading, since a slave has no external owner to transfer him to {\i hekdesh} ownership; the only coherent interpretation is that he undertook a personal monetary obligation.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Holiness Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 All Agree Holy\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now introduces a {\i baraita} that shifts the framing entirely: {\i hamakdish avdo ein moalin bo} \u8212- if one sanctifies his slave, there is no transgression of {\i me\u8217'ilah} (misuse of sacred property) with respect to the slave. Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel adds: {\i moalin b\u8217'sa\u8217'aro} \u8212- there is {\i me\u8217'ilah} with respect to his hair.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara asks whether this dispute tracks the fundamental question of whether the slave is sanctified at all. But this reading is rejected: {\i de\u8217'chulei alma kadosh} \u8212- everyone agrees the slave is sanctified. The debate is not about whether {\i kedushah} applies but about the legal category into which the slave falls for purposes of {\i me\u8217'ilah}. One opinion holds that {\i avda k\u8217'mankarkei dami} \u8212- a slave has the legal status of land \u8212- and the laws of {\i me\u8217'ilah} do not apply to land. The other holds {\i k\u8217'mitalteli dami} \u8212- a slave is like chattel \u8212- and {\i me\u8217'ilah} does apply. This entire discussion stands against Rav\u8217's position that the slave goes free upon {\i makdish}: according to this {\i baraita}, the slave is genuinely sanctified and remains enslaved.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hair Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The question about hair \u8212- {\i se\u8217'ar ha\u8217'omed ligzoz} \u8212- arises because hair that is ready to be shorn occupies an ambiguous status. It is physically connected to the slave\u8217's body, but it is also effectively detached in the sense that it serves no further functional purpose while still attached. The debate in the {\i baraita} is whether hair that is ready to be shorn is considered as if it has already been cut \u8212- {\i k\u8217'gazuz dami} \u8212- and therefore treated as chattel (with {\i me\u8217'ilah} applying), or whether it is still considered part of the slave\u8217's body \u8212- {\i lav k\u8217'gazuz dami} \u8212- and therefore categorized like land (with no {\i me\u8217'ilah}).\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Holiness Debate (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Grapes Analogy\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara finds a parallel for this question in a {\i baraita} and {\i mishnah} concerning Rabbi Meir\u8217's ruling that there are things which are like land yet not treated as land for purposes of certain oaths ({\i yesh devarim she\u8217'hein k\u8217'karka v\u8217'einan k\u8217'karka}). The specific case involves ten grapevines laden with grapes: one party claims to have transferred ten laden vines, the other concedes only five. Is the admission regarding the grapes a {\i modeh b\u8217'miktzat} requiring an oath? Rabbi Meir says yes; the Sages say no, since anything attached to the ground is treated as land ({\i ha-mechubar le-karka harei hu ke-karka}).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yose bar Chanina clarifies that the debate does not concern the vines themselves \u8212- all agree the vines are like land \u8212- but the grapes that are {\i omdot livtzor}, ripe and ready for harvest. Rabbi Meir holds {\i k\u8217'vtzurot damyan} \u8212- they are considered as if already harvested; the Sages hold {\i lav k\u8217'vtzurot damyan} \u8212- they are not. The parallel to the hair question is exact: in both cases, the question is whether readiness for detachment causes the item to be treated as if already detached.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara, however, declines to equate the two debates directly. Even if Rabbi Meir considers ripe grapes as effectively harvested, he might not apply the same logic to hair. The reason: grapes that remain on the vine past the optimal harvest time deteriorate ({\i kama de-kaimi machash kachishi}) \u8212- their connection to the ground is actively harmful, and there is strong motivation to sever it. Hair, by contrast, continues to grow and increase in value the longer it remains ({\i kama de-kaimi ashvuchi mashvchi}) \u8212- it remains intrinsically connected to the body and beneficial in that connection. Therefore, even hair that is ready to be shorn might not be treated as if already shorn, and the two {\i tanna\u8217'im} debating the hair may not track Rabbi Meir and the Sages on the grapes.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Deceased Convert\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav\u8217's Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now pivots to the case of {\i mafkir} in a new context. When Rabbi Chiyya bar Yosef traveled to Eretz Yisrael, he transmitted Rav\u8217's teaching \u8212- that renouncing ownership of a slave ({\i mafkir avdo}) causes the slave to go free but still requires a {\i get shichrur} for the change in personal status \u8212- before Rabbi Yochanan.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Yochanan Agrees\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yochanan\u8217's reaction was: {\i amar Rav hachi?} \u8212- did Rav really say this? The Gemara clarifies the ambiguity: one version of the exchange records Rabbi Yochanan as genuinely surprised that Rav held this way, prompting the Gemara to note that Rabbi Yochanan himself said precisely the same thing ({\i amar Ulla amar Rabbi Yochanan ha-mafkir avdo yatza le-cherut ve-tzarich get shichrur}) \u8212- so his question was not a challenge but an expression of pleased agreement: \u8220"Rav says it just like I do?\u8221" An alternate version records that Rabbi Chiyya bar Yosef had not yet completed the teaching before Rabbi Yochanan interjected, resulting in a momentary misunderstanding that was quickly resolved. At the end of the day, both Rav and Rabbi Yochanan agree: renouncing property ownership of a slave does not automatically change his personal status; a {\i get shichrur} is still required.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Slaves Take Themselves\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Abba then challenges Ulla with a {\i baraita} about a {\i ger} \u8212- a convert \u8212- who dies leaving no heirs. Because conversion severs all halakhic familial relationships, a convert who has no children dies with no legal relatives. His property becomes ownerless, and anyone who seizes it acquires it. If among his property there are adult slaves, those slaves \u8212- at the moment of the owner\u8217's death \u8212- find themselves in a state equivalent to {\i hefker}: they are owned by no one. The {\i baraita} rules {\i kanu atzman b\u8217'nei chorin} \u8212- the adult slaves take possession of themselves and become free people.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Abba Shaul Minor\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abba Shaul qualifies this: adult slaves can indeed take possession of themselves, but minor slaves cannot. A halakhic minor lacks the legal capacity to acquire property, including themselves. Therefore, minor slaves in the deceased convert\u8217's estate remain legally ownerless objects available for acquisition by whoever seizes them first.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Writ Problem\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This {\i baraita} appears to challenge Rabbi Yochanan\u8217's position directly: if {\i mafkir} requires a {\i get shichrur} for personal status change, and the convert who died left no heirs and no one alive to write such a writ, how can the adult slaves go free? The challenge is pointed: {\i mi katav get shichrur l\u8217'elu?} \u8212- who writes them their writ of manumission?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Ulla\u8217's reply is reported as dismissive \u8212- he compared the questioner to someone who had never learned the relevant {\i sugya}. But the Gemara presses: {\i ta\u8217'ama mai?} Setting aside the tone, what is the actual answer?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Master-Slave Relation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Nachman explains Ulla\u8217's reasoning: {\i kasavar Ulla d\u8217'ger k\u8217'ishto} \u8212- Ulla holds that the master-slave relationship is analogous to the husband-wife relationship with respect to the {\i get}. Just as a wife who is freed by her husband\u8217's death does not require a {\i get} \u8212- death itself dissolves the personal bond \u8212- so too a slave whose master dies without heirs is freed without a writ. The {\i get shichrur} is necessary only when the master-slave relationship continues in some form \u8212- specifically, when there remains a living person who holds the legal identity of master, even if property ownership has already been renounced.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The key distinction is between two components of the master-slave relationship: the property dimension (ownership of the slave as chattel) and the personal dimension (the relational identity of master and slave). Even after property ownership ends \u8212- whether through {\i mafkir} or through {\i hekdesh} \u8212- the personal relationship persists. The former master retains the legal standing to issue a {\i get shichrur} precisely because, at the level of personal status, the identity of master has not yet dissolved. It is that ongoing personal relationship that makes the {\i get} necessary \u8212- and also makes the former master the uniquely qualified person to write it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 When the master dies and there are heirs, those heirs inherit not only the property dimension but also the relational identity of master. They become the ones in the position to write the writ. When the {\i ger} dies with no heirs, however, there is no one who inherits the identity of master. With no living master, the relational basis for the personal status of slavery dissolves entirely \u8212- just as a wife is freed without a {\i get} when her husband dies. No writ is needed because there is no surviving master to write one, and the absence of a master means the slave\u8217's personal identity as a slave has no anchor.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Master Dies After\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara presses this logic with a new case: what if a master first renounces property ownership ({\i mafkir avdo}) \u8212- so the slave is no longer owned as property but still requires a {\i get shichrur} for personal status \u8212- and then the master dies? By the same reasoning applied to the deceased convert, one would expect the slave to go free automatically: the master is dead, no heir exists to assume the identity of master (since the slave was already {\i mafkir} and therefore is not an inherited asset), and therefore the relational basis for the personal status of slavery should dissolve. Should this case not parallel the {\i ger} who dies?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ameimar Stuck\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara notes that Ameimar rules otherwise: {\i hamafkir avdo u\u8217'met oto eved ein lo takanah} \u8212- if one renounced ownership of his slave and then died, that slave has no remedy. He is stuck: he is not owned as property, so he works for himself and retains his own earnings, but his personal status as slave has not changed. He cannot marry a Jewish woman and is not fully obligated in {\i mitzvot}. He needs a {\i get shichrur} to change his personal status, but there is no living master with standing to write one. The Gemara concedes: {\i de\u8217'Ameimar kashya} \u8212- Ameimar\u8217's ruling is difficult to reconcile with the model derived from the {\i ger}. The two positions stand in tension, and the Gemara leaves Ameimar\u8217's ruling as a difficult point.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Writ Problem\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The broader theoretical point stands: Rav and Rabbi Yochanan hold that property ownership and personal status are legally distinct. Renouncing property ownership \u8212- whether through {\i mafkir} or {\i makdish} \u8212- does not automatically dissolve the personal status of slavery. Only a {\i get shichrur} issued by the person holding the legal identity of master can accomplish that. The cases of the deceased {\i ger} and the {\i mafkir} who subsequently dies represent edge cases where the legal machinery breaks down: the writ is required but the person uniquely qualified to issue it no longer exists.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Abba Shaul Rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Finally, Rav Yaakov bar Idi cites Rabbi Yehoshua ben Levi: {\i halacha k\u8217'Abba Shaul} \u8212- the ruling follows Abba Shaul. In the case of a convert who dies with no heirs, adult slaves acquire themselves and go free, but minor slaves do not and may be seized by anyone. Rav Zeira asks Rav Yaakov bar Idi whether this ruling was received explicitly or inferred from something else, and the Gemara begins to explore what the inferential basis might have been \u8212- though this discussion extends beyond the present daf.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Gittin 40\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Property vs.\u160 ?Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ownership vs.\u160 ?Relationsh\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The sugya on the bottom of 39b opens by probing a fundamental conceptual tension: can one separate the monetary dimension of slave ownership \u8212- the proprietary claim a master holds over his slave as chattel \u8212- from the personal-status dimension of the master-slave relationship? This question, as we shall see, ramifies across an entire series of cases and disputes.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ger\u8217's Slaves\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The opening case involves a {\i ger} \u8212- a convert \u8212- who dies without heirs. Since his biological family has no halakhic standing and he has no children, his estate passes to no one; his property becomes ownerless. What happens to his slaves? The {\i Tanna Kamma} holds that the adult slaves simply go free: they take possession of themselves, and since there is no master remaining who could give them a {\i shtar shichrur}, the entire framework of personal-status obligation dissolves. {\i Abba Shaul}, however, draws a distinction: adult slaves can take possession of themselves as property and thereby achieve freedom, but minor slaves lack the legal capacity to acquire property \u8212- they cannot take possession of themselves \u8212- and therefore remain in a kind of suspended, vulnerable status, susceptible to seizure by others.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Explicit or Inferred?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} at the bottom of 39a records that {\i Rabi Yehoshua ben Levi} ruled: {\i halacha k\u8217'Abba Shaul} \u8212- the law follows {\i Abba Shaul}. {\i Rav Zeira} then asked {\i Rabi Yaakov bar Idi}: did you hear this ruling from {\i Rabi Yehoshua ben Levi} explicitly, {\i b\u8217'feirush}, or did you infer it, {\i mi-klala}? The answer was: it was inferred. The {\i Gemara} then asks \u8212- from what would such an inference have been drawn?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mafkir & Shtar\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} identifies the source of the inference as a separate teaching of {\i Rabi Yehoshua ben Levi}. He reported that a case was brought before {\i Rebbi}: if someone declares {\i nityashti mi-ploni avdi} \u8212- \u8220"I give up hope of ever recovering my slave,\u8221" or more precisely, \u8220"I renounce ownership of my slave\u8221" \u8212- what is the {\i halacha}? {\i Rebbi} responded: {\i omer ani, ein lo takana ela bi-shtar} \u8212- in my view, there is no remedy for this slave except through a {\i shtar shichrur}. The slave can no longer be compelled to work \u8212- no one owns his labor \u8212- and yet his personal status remains unresolved. He cannot marry freely; he is neither fully a slave nor a free man. A formal document of manumission is required to change his personal status.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A debate between {\i Rashi} and {\i Tosafot} underlies this scenario. {\i Tosafot} maintains that in such a case the slave remains fully a slave in terms of personal status, capable of continuing his prior marital relationship with a slave woman, even though no one can claim his labor. {\i Rashi} holds that he exists in a genuine in-between status \u8212- he cannot be married to either a slave or a free woman \u8212- and that this limbo persists until a {\i shtar} is issued.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Woman & Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabi Yochanan} then explains {\i Rebbi}\u8217's reasoning: {\i gamar lah lah me\u8217'isha} \u8212- he derives it through a {\i gezeira shava} linking the word {\i lah} in the context of a woman\u8217's {\i get} and the word {\i lah} in the context of a female slave\u8217's manumission. Just as a woman\u8217's personal status is changed exclusively through a document \u8212- a {\i get} \u8212- so too a slave\u8217's personal status requires a document. The point being driven home is that even once the property dimension of slavery has dissolved, a distinct personal-status dimension remains, and that dimension demands its own formal act: the giving of a {\i shtar}. This is, as the {\i Gemara} notes, beautifully underscored by the analogy to a woman: {\i ma isha bi-shtar, eved nami bi-shtar} \u8212- just as a woman\u8217's status is changed through a {\i get}, a slave\u8217's status is changed through a {\i shtar shichrur}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Inference Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Now, how does this teaching connect to {\i Abba Shaul}\u8217's ruling about the {\i ger}\u8217's slaves? The {\i Gemara} explains that one might have inferred {\i Abba Shaul}\u8217's position from {\i Rabi Yehoshua ben Levi}\u8217's teaching by noting the following parallel: just as a woman\u8217's situation involves only personal status ({\i issur}) and not property ({\i mamon}), so too the case {\i Rabi Yehoshua ben Levi} describes is one in which only personal status remains after the property dimension has been renounced. Applying this to {\i Abba Shaul}\u8217's scenario \u8212- when the {\i ger} dies and his adult slaves take possession of themselves \u8212- the property dimension is resolved through their self-acquisition, and only the personal-status issue lingers. But the minor slaves cannot resolve the property dimension, and therefore the status dimension cannot be addressed either. One might have inferred from {\i Rabi Yehoshua ben Levi}\u8217's teaching that the two dimensions are sequentially dependent: the property issue must first be addressed before the personal-status issue can be resolved.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} immediately pushes back: {\i vi\u8217'im mi-klala, mai?} \u8212- and if it was only an inference, what is the problem? The answer: {\i adarabba dok mina l\u8217'ipkha} \u8212- on the contrary, you could have inferred the opposite. The comparison to a woman teaches that a {\i get} applies whether the woman is an adult or a minor. If we apply that symmetry to the slave scenario under {\i Abba Shaul}, one might equally infer that the status change applies to adult and minor slaves alike. Since the inference is genuinely indeterminate \u8212- it can support either ruling \u8212- we cannot rely on it as the basis for the {\i halacha}. Accordingly, {\i Rabi Yaakov bar Idi} acknowledges: {\i b\u8217'feirush shemia li} \u8212- I heard it stated explicitly. The ruling that we follow {\i Abba Shaul} is not inferred; it was transmitted directly.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two Versions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} then presents a competing tradition. {\i Rabi Chiyya bar Abba} transmitted in the name of {\i Rabi Yochanan}: {\i ein halacha k\u8217'Abba Shaul} \u8212- we do not rule like {\i Abba Shaul}, and both adult and minor slaves of the {\i ger} go free. {\i Rav Zeira} poses the same question to {\i Rabi Chiyya bar Abba}: was this heard explicitly or inferred? And the {\i Gemara} runs through precisely the same structure as before \u8212- the inference from the woman analogy could equally support ruling against {\i Abba Shaul} (since a {\i get} works for a minor girl as well), but it could also support ruling like {\i Abba Shaul} (since the woman analogy highlights the {\i issur}-only dimension, suggesting that status change requires the property dimension to be resolved first). In the end, {\i Rabi Chiyya bar Abba} also declares: {\i b\u8217'feirush shemia li} \u8212- I too heard it explicitly, from {\i Rabi Yochanan}, that we do not rule like {\i Abba Shaul}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What emerges from this entire passage, even if the precise inferential logic is elusive, is a profound conceptual framework. The {\i Gemara} is working with two separate dimensions of the master-slave bond. One dimension is proprietary: the master owns the slave\u8217's body and labor as one owns property. The other is a personal-status dimension: a legally defined relationship that generates its own set of {\i issurim} \u8212- obligations and prohibitions \u8212- independent of ownership. The question that animates the whole discussion is whether these two dimensions are separable, and if so, how the dissolution of one affects the other.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One important framing: a {\i shtar shichrur} might function primarily by changing the slave\u8217's personal status, with the property dimension dissolving as a natural consequence. Alternatively, it might work primarily by extinguishing the property claim, with the personal-status change following. If it works through status, then even where property ownership has already dissolved \u8212- as in the {\i mafkir} case \u8212- the status relationship may persist and still require a formal act. If it works through property, then dissolving the property claim without a formal act of redemption leaves the status unaddressed. Both directions are at play in this {\i sugya}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The analogy to {\i kiddushin} is relevant here as well: the {\i Gemara}\u8217's insistence that a woman is not owned as property \u8212- that the marital bond is a personal-status relationship, not a proprietary one \u8212- is underscored by the fact that {\i gerushin} operates through a document alone. {\i Ma isha bi-shtar} \u8212- only through the {\i get} does her status change. A husband holds no property right in his wife analogous to that which a master holds in his {\i eved Kena\u8217'ani}; what he holds is a personal-status claim. The {\i Gemara} here is careful never to lose sight of the fact that even an {\i eved Kena\u8217'ani}, though owned as property in the fullest sense, remains a human being \u8212- a point the {\i Torah} underscores by making a master liable if he beats his slave to death.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Shtar vs.\u160 ?Kesef\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Self-Redemption\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having established the basic framework, the {\i Gemara} now turns to the question of {\i how} a slave is formally freed. Two mechanisms appear in the sources: {\i shtar} \u8212- a deed of manumission \u8212- and {\i kesef} \u8212- monetary redemption. The case that focuses the discussion is derived from the {\i pasuk} regarding a {\i shifcha kena\u8217'anit}, the non-Jewish female slave: {\i o chufsha lo nitan lah} \u8212- or her freedom has not been given to her. This {\i pasuk} also mentions {\i hafdeih}, redemption, suggesting that being redeemed \u8212- whether by a third party paying the master or by the slave herself paying \u8212- is a recognized mode of manumission.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rebbi}\u8217's ruling cited in an earlier context had been that a slave who was consecrated ({\i makdish}) to the {\i Beis HaMikdash} can pay the {\i gizbarim} for his own redemption and thereby go free. The {\i Gemara} now clarifies this position: the two modes are {\i o be\u8217'kesef o be\u8217'shtar} \u8212- either through monetary redemption or through a deed of manumission. However \u8212- and this is the crucial point \u8212- when an owner has already been {\i mafkir} his slave, renouncing all property ownership, the option of {\i kesef} is no longer available. Redemption only makes sense when there is a property claim to be extinguished; one cannot redeem something from an owner who no longer owns it. The only path remaining for such a slave is a {\i shtar shichrur}. This also illuminates the broader conceptual point: if {\i kesef} works, it focuses on dissolving the property claim, after which the personal status follows. If {\i shtar} works, it directly addresses the personal status, and the property dimension dissolves as a consequence.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Shimon\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Shimon}, citing {\i Rabbi Akiva}, challenges the position that {\i kesef} constitutes an independent mechanism of manumission at all. The {\i Gemara} frames this as a {\i drasha}: the {\i pasuk} says {\i v\u8217'hipadeh lo nifdasa} \u8212- she has not been redeemed \u8212- followed by {\i o chufsha lo nitan lah} \u8212- or her freedom has not been given to her. {\i Rabbi Shimon} reads the entire passage as being drawn back to the conclusion \u8212- {\i chufsha lo nitan lah} \u8212- meaning that even if there is an act of redemption, what ultimately changes the slave\u8217's status is the giving of freedom, a formal act of {\i shichrur} through a {\i shtar}. The {\i kesef} does not independently accomplish the status change; {\i shtar gomer bah v\u8217'ein ha-kesef gomer bah} \u8212- it is the document that completes her manumission, not the money.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Nachman vs.\u160 ?Yosef\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rami bar Chama} said in the name of {\i Rav Nachman}: {\i halacha k\u8217'Rabbi Shimon} \u8212- we rule like {\i Rabbi Shimon}, and only a {\i shtar} frees a slave, not {\i kesef}. But {\i Rabbi Yosef bar Chama} said in the name of {\i Rabbi Yochanan}: {\i ein halacha k\u8217'Rabbi Shimon} \u8212- we do not rule like him, and {\i kesef} can also serve as a mode of manumission. The tension between these two positions is then explored through a series of transmitted rulings.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hat Story\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} presents a poignant narrative to clarify how {\i Rav Nachman}\u8217's ruling came to be understood. There was a slave woman whose master lay dying. She came before him weeping, saying: how long must I remain enslaved? She had served him for seventy years; he was dying \u8212- could he not free her now? The master took his hat, threw it at her, and said: {\i zili kni ha u-kni nafshach} \u8212- go, take this hat, and through it acquire your own freedom. They came before {\i Rav Nachman}, who ruled: {\i lo asa v\u8217'lo klum} \u8212- he accomplished nothing; she is not free. From this ruling, observers concluded that {\i Rav Nachman} held {\i kesef} does not free a slave.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Exchange Direction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} immediately corrects this inference. The ruling had nothing to do with whether {\i kesef} can free a slave. The problem was directional: in the formal act of {\i kinyan kesef}, it is the slave who must pay the master, not the master who gives something to the slave. The logic is analogous to {\i kinyan chalipin} \u8212- the handkerchief must travel from the one seeking to acquire toward the one who holds what is being acquired. Here, the master was transferring an object {\i to} the slave woman; this is {\i keli shel makneih} \u8212- the object belongs to the one doing the giving \u8212- and that is not the correct direction for a redemption transaction. The ruling does not demonstrate that {\i kesef} is categorically invalid; it demonstrates only that this particular transaction was structured incorrectly.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Final Ruling\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Several further traditions are cited: {\i Rav Shmuel bar Achisfei} transmitting through {\i Rav Yitzchak bar Ashin} transmitting through {\i Rav Huna} in the name of {\i Rav Hamnuna} \u8212- an impressively long chain \u8212- that {\i halacha k\u8217'Rabbi Shimon}, we rule like {\i Rabbi Shimon} that only a {\i shtar} frees and not {\i kesef}. The {\i Gemara} then closes: {\i v\u8217'lav hi} \u8212- this is not the {\i halacha}. We do not, in the end, rule like {\i Rabbi Shimon}; {\i kesef} can also accomplish manumission.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Evidence of Freedom\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Marriage Evidence\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rav Zeira} transmitted in the name of {\i Rabbi Chanina} in the name of {\i Rav Assi} in the name of {\i Rav}: if a slave marries a free woman in the presence of his master \u8212- {\i eved she-nasa bat chorin bifnei rabo} \u8212- he goes free, {\i yatza l\u8217'cherut}. The near-universal understanding of this ruling is that it does not describe an act of manumission in itself. Where is the {\i shtar}? Where is the {\i kesef}? Rather, the master\u8217's silence in the face of this marriage is taken as {\i evidence} that the slave had already been freed. A master would never countenance his slave marrying a free woman \u8212- for a slave to do so would be a grave violation \u8212- and therefore the master\u8217's passive presence is proof that he must have already freed the slave.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabi Yochanan} challenges this teaching by pointing to the following {\i braisa}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Dual Purpose Shtar\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If a man writes a {\i shtar eirusin} to his own female slave \u8212- declaring {\i harei at mekudeshet li} \u8212- one might expect this to function as proof that he had freed her, since he certainly would not attempt to marry his own slave without first freeing her, and he is directly participating in the act. Yet this is the subject of a dispute between {\i Rabbi Meir} and {\i Chachamim} \u8212- it is not simply assumed that such an act constitutes proof of prior freedom.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} explains this case on the basis of a teaching of {\i Rav Abba bar Rav Huna Sheila}: the cases where we infer freedom from the master\u8217's conduct are those where the master is {\i directly and actively involved} in performing the act together with the slave \u8212- not merely present. This resolves the apparent tension. In the case of the {\i shtar eirusin}, the context makes clear that the master has not previously freed his slave; rather, he is attempting to make the {\i shtar kiddushin} simultaneously serve as a {\i shtar shichrur}. The formula {\i harei at mekudeshet li} is being offered as a dual-purpose document \u8212- both a deed of marriage and a deed of manumission.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Meir\u8217's View\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Meir} holds that {\i yesh bi-lashon hazeh lashon shichrur} \u8212- the phrase {\i harei at mekudeshet li} contains within it an implicit language of manumission. Since one cannot be married to a master, saying \u8220"you are betrothed to me\u8221" necessarily implies \u8220"and I am freeing you.\u8221" Moreover, since this is written in a {\i shtar}, the document itself carries the force of the act. {\i Rabbi Meir} would allow the {\i shtar kiddushin} to function simultaneously as a {\i shtar shichrur} because the implication of freedom is carried within the language of betrothal, and it is operative {\i because} it is a document. {\i Chachamim} disagree: {\i ein bi-lashon hazeh lashon shichrur} \u8212- the language is not explicit enough to constitute a deed of manumission. The implication is insufficient; a {\i shtar shichrur} requires its own language.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 In either case, this scenario is distinct from the evidentiary question. Evidence of prior freedom requires direct, active involvement by the master \u8212- not merely his presence. When the {\i shtar kiddushin} is offered explicitly as a dual instrument, it is clear from the context that the slave has {\i not} yet been freed; the question is only whether the document can accomplish both acts simultaneously.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Master\u8217's Role\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i braisa} is then adduced: if a slave puts on {\i tefillin} in the presence of his master, this is cited as evidence of freedom. But the {\i braisa} itself lists several acts that one might think would constitute such evidence \u8212- the master lending money from his slave, appointing the slave as {\i apotropos}, the slave receiving an {\i aliyah} in {\i shul} in the presence of the master \u8212- and rules that {\i none} of these suffice: {\i harei ze lo yatza l\u8217'cheirus}. How then can {\i tefillin} constitute proof?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbah bar Rav Sheila} resolves the difficulty: {\i keshe-rabo hiniyach lo tefillin} \u8212- the case where we accept this as proof is specifically where the master himself placed the {\i tefillin} on the slave\u8217's arm. The master\u8217's direct, active involvement in an act socially associated exclusively with free Jewish men \u8212- he himself wrapping the {\i tefillin} \u8212- is the evidence. Mere passive presence is insufficient; what is required is that the master actively participated in doing something that no master would do for a slave, thereby demonstrating that he has already freed him.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The distinction between the cases in the {\i braisa} and the conclusive case parallels the distinction established earlier: it is not enough that the master is present ({\i bifnei rabo}); what matters is whether the master is the active agent in an act categorically inconsistent with the slave\u8217's continued servitude.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafot & Tefillin\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} raises a subtle point regarding the {\i tefillin} case specifically. One might have thought that a master would hesitate to put {\i tefillin} on his slave because slaves are exempt from {\i tefillin}, and if the slave recites a {\i beracha} on them it would constitute a {\i beracha levatalah} \u8212- a blessing recited in vain. {\i Tosafot} dismisses this concern: {\i delo ma\u8217'avid lei issura de-mivareich aleihen de-ika beracha levatalah} \u8212- this is not the reason. Women are similarly exempt from many time-bound positive commandments and yet, according to the {\i Ashkenazic} practice, may recite blessings upon them; the same reasoning would apply to slaves. The real reason that a master would not put {\i tefillin} on his slave is purely societal \u8212- {\i ein avadim regilin b\u8217'tefillin} \u8212- slaves do not customarily wear {\i tefillin}, and therefore when the master directly performs this act, it is conclusive societal evidence that the slave has been freed. It is a matter of public status, not of any specific halakhic prohibition.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Deathbed Directives\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Al Yishtabedu\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 When {\i Rav Dimi} came from {\i Eretz Yisrael}, he reported in the name of {\i Rabi Yochanan}: if a person says at the moment of his death \u8212- {\i ploni shifchasi al yishtabedu bah achar mosi} \u8212- \u8220"My heirs should not enslave this slave woman of mine after my death,\u8221" then {\i kofin es ha-yorshim ve-chosvin lah get shichrur} \u8212- we compel the heirs to write her a {\i get shichrur}. The reasoning: the dying owner\u8217's words appear to constitute a {\i mafkir} declaration, a renunciation of property ownership. Since a {\i shtar shichrur} is still required, and he is no longer alive to provide it, the heirs write it in his stead.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} records a challenge to this ruling: {\i Rabi Ami} and {\i Rabi Assi} asked \u8212- do you not concede that her children would be slaves? If the woman is freed, her future children will be free. But if she is merely told she will not be worked, while remaining a slave in personal status, her future children remain slaves and the heirs retain a financial interest in keeping her enslaved. This challenge suggests that the language {\i al yishtabedu} \u8212- \u8220"do not enslave her\u8221" \u8212- may not be a renunciation of ownership at all, but simply a directive not to compel her labor. Under that reading, she remains a slave and no {\i shtar shichrur} is owed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Koras Ruach\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A different version of {\i Rabi Yochanan}\u8217's ruling was transmitted by {\i Rav Shmuel bar Yehuda}. In this version, the dying master says: {\i ploni shifchasi koras ruach astah li, ya\u8217'asu lah koras ruach} \u8212- \u8220"My slave woman brought me comfort; you should do for her what she wishes.\u8221" The {\i halacha} here is also that {\i kofin es ha-yorshim la\u8217'asos lah koras ruach} \u8212- we compel the heirs to fulfill her wishes. Since her wish is to be freed, the heirs are compelled to free her.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Divrei Hameis\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} explains the principle underlying this second version: {\i mitzvah l\u8217'kayem divrei ha-meis} \u8212- it is a {\i mitzvah} to fulfill the wishes of one who expressed them on his deathbed. This is a distinct category from {\i divrei shechiv mera}, a deathbed bequest, in which ownership transfers automatically at death with the force of a {\i kinyan}. Here, the master is not making a legally operative property transfer; he is issuing a directive \u8212- a request \u8212- that his heirs carry out his final wishes. The question of when {\i beis din} compels compliance with such a directive, and under what halakhic theory the compulsion operates, is left with these interesting parameters.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Amimar\u8217's View\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Amimar} then articulates a stark position: {\i ha-mafkir avdo, oso eved ein lo takana} \u8212- if an owner renounces ownership of his slave while alive, that slave has no remedy. {\i Amimar}\u8217's reasoning: {\i gufei lo kani lei} \u8212- the owner no longer holds a proprietary claim over the slave\u8217's body. What remains is the {\i issura} \u8212- the personal-status dimension. But \u8212- {\i issura lo matzi makni lei} \u8212- the owner is no longer in a position to transfer or resolve that personal-status dimension, because he has relinquished the ownership that grounded his authority to do so. The slave needs a {\i shtar shichrur} but cannot receive one, because the only person who could give it no longer has the standing to do so. This is the worst of all possible outcomes.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Ashi\u8217's Challenge\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rav Ashi} challenges {\i Amimar} from the tradition transmitted by {\i Ulla} in the name of {\i Rabi Yochanan} and {\i Rabbi Chiyya bar Avin} in the name of {\i Rav}: when an owner is {\i mafkir} his slave, the slave goes free but requires a {\i get shichrur} \u8212- implying that the {\i get shichrur} can and should be given. {\i Amimar} responds: you have misread the tradition. {\i Tzarich v\u8217'ein lo takana} \u8212- when they said he \u8220"requires a {\i get shichrur},\u8221" they meant: he is in a state in which his resolution depends on receiving a {\i get shichrur}, and he is never going to get one. The tradition was not offering a path to resolution; it was describing an impasse.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rav Ashi} returns with a second challenge: but the story of {\i Rav Dimi} \u8212- the case of {\i al yishtabedu bah} \u8212- ended with the ruling that the heirs write her a {\i get shichrur}. Does this not demonstrate that even after the owner dies, having in effect renounced his claim over the slave, the heirs can provide the {\i shtar shichrur}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Heirs Can Free\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Amimar} responds: {\i gabei d\u8217'Dimi ta\u8217'us hi} \u8212- that ruling was in error. The entire {\i psak} in the {\i Rav Dimi} version was called into question precisely because {\i Rabi Ami} and {\i Rabi Assi} pointed out that {\i al yishtabedu} was not a renunciation of ownership \u8212- the owner was merely saying \u8220"don\u8217't make her work,\u8221" not \u8220"I renounce all claim to her.\u8221" Since the language was ambiguous, the principle of {\i mafkir avdo u-meis} was never actually tested in that case. Had the owner said explicit language of renunciation \u8212- {\i b\u8217'lashon shichrur} \u8212- everyone would have accepted that he had renounced ownership and died, and the heirs could then provide the {\i shtar shichrur}. {\i Amimar} insists: {\i ana k\u8217'Rav Shmuel bar Yehuda sevira li} \u8212- I hold like the version of {\i Rav Shmuel bar Yehuda}, where the case turned on {\i koras ruach} \u8212- a deathbed directive rooted in {\i mitzva l\u8217'kayem divrei ha-meis} \u8212- not on a {\i mafkir} scenario at all.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Non-Jewish Owners\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} presents a practical case that frames the stakes of this dispute. There was a certain region ({\i diskara}) of slaves who had all been sold to non-Jews \u8212- a sale which, halakhically, obligates the original Jewish owners to redeem them and issue a {\i shtar shichrur}. The non-Jewish owners then died. The original Jewish owners had also died. The slaves came before {\i Ravina}. He ruled: {\i ziloo hadiru a-b\u8217'nei mar bar Rav Ashi kamai} \u8212- go to the sons of the original Jewish masters, and {\i v\u8217'yichtevu lach gita d\u8217'cheruta} \u8212- they will write you a {\i shtar shichrur}. As {\i Ravina} himself recognizes, this is essentially a case of {\i mafkir avdo u-meis}: the moment the original masters sold the slaves to non-Jews, they relinquished property ownership; they then died without having issued a {\i shtar shichrur}; and {\i Ravina} is ruling that the sons \u8212- the heirs \u8212- can now write it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Rabbanan} challenged {\i Ravina}: but {\i Amimar} said that in a case of {\i mafkir avdo u-meis}, the slave has no remedy! {\i Ravina} replied: {\i ana k\u8217'Rav Dimi sevira li} \u8212- I hold like the principle of the {\i Rav Dimi} case, which demonstrates that heirs can free a slave after the owner has died. The {\i Rabbanan} pushed back: but was the {\i Rav Dimi} case not a {\i ta\u8217'ut}? {\i Ravina} responded with the same resolution that emerged earlier: {\i d\u8217'lo amar b\u8217'lashon shichrur} \u8212- the {\i ta\u8217'ut} was only because in that specific case the owner did not use clear language of renunciation. The principle \u8212- that the heirs can write a {\i shtar shichrur} after the owner renounced ownership and died \u8212- was never in dispute. {\i Ha amar b\u8217'lashon shichrur}, had he used such language, no one would have challenged it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ravina\u8217's Ruling\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} closes with the definitive ruling: {\i v\u8217'hilkheta k\u8217'Ravina} \u8212- the {\i halacha} follows {\i Ravina}. In a case of {\i mafkir avdo u-meis}, the slave is not permanently stuck; the heirs retain the capacity to write a {\i shtar shichrur} and free the slave.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Chatzi Eved\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two Owners\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now turns to the well-known problem of the {\i chatzi eved v\u8217'chatzi ben chorin} \u8212- a slave who is half-owned by two masters, one of whom frees his share. As the {\i Mishnah} elsewhere teaches, this person is caught in an impossible situation: he cannot marry a free woman because he is half slave, and he cannot marry a slave woman because he is half free. The {\i tikkun olam} requires that the remaining owner be compelled to free his half as well, so that the slave can live as a complete person.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Minor Son Loophole\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A case arose in which one owner sought to circumvent this compulsion. Knowing that the {\i Rabbanan} would force him to free his half of the slave, he transferred his half-ownership to his minor son. A {\i beis din} will not compel a minor to do anything; the minor cannot be made to write a {\i shtar shichrur}. As long as the son remains a minor, the slave remains trapped in his half-free, half-slave status \u8212- and the father has effectively protected his family\u8217's interest in the slave\u8217's labor and in any children the slave might produce.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The case was sent by {\i Rav Yosef the son of Rava} to {\i Rav Papa} for resolution.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Guardian Solution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rav Papa} responded with a creative solution: {\i ka\u8217'asher asah ken ye\u8217'aseh lo} \u8212- we will counter his maneuver with one of our own. Since children are susceptible to being won over by money \u8212- {\i d\u8217'mikarva datei l\u8217'gabei zuzei} \u8212- we appoint a {\i guardian}, an {\i apotropos}, and arrange for money to be jingled before the child, so that he does not object to the proceedings. A {\i shtar shichrur} is then written on behalf of the minor owner, freeing the slave.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rashi vs.\u160 ?Tosafot\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A debate between {\i Rashi} and {\i Tosafot} concerns precisely what role the {\i apotropos} plays. {\i Rashi} explains that the child himself writes the {\i shtar shichrur} \u8212- that a minor of sufficient age has the capacity to engage in such transactions \u8212- and the {\i apotropos} is appointed to ensure that a fair valuation is placed on the slave, since the slave will owe payment for his freedom and someone must protect the minor\u8217's financial interest in that transaction. {\i Tosafot}, citing {\i Rabbenu Shmuel} (the {\i Rashbam}), disagrees. According to {\i Tosafot}, a minor cannot affect personal status ({\i issur}), even if he has some capacity to engage in property transactions; the {\i apotropos} is therefore the one who actually writes the {\i shtar shichrur}, acting in the minor\u8217's stead. The money jingled before the child serves to prevent him from objecting or interfering. {\i Tosafot} further raises the question whether this authority of the {\i apotropos} to free a slave on behalf of the minor is grounded in the ordinary law of {\i apitropus}, or whether it is a special measure \u8212- {\i hefker beis din hefker} \u8212- ordained by {\i beis din} for this specific circumstance.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Minor & Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The dispute between {\i Rashi} and {\i Tosafot} crystallizes the core conceptual distinction. If manumission is primarily a property transaction, a minor of sufficient maturity might have enough legal capacity to accomplish it. If, however, manumission is fundamentally an act affecting personal status \u8212- an {\i issur} dimension rather than a {\i mamon} dimension \u8212- then a minor, who lacks the legal standing to affect another person\u8217's personal status, cannot write the {\i shtar shichrur} at all. {\i Tosafot}\u8217's position that the {\i apotropos} writes the {\i shtar} reflects the view that manumission operates primarily in the domain of {\i issur}, and therefore requires an adult with full legal standing.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Shtar Language\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Valid Formulas\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i braisa} is cited: {\i asiti ploni avdi ben chorin} \u8212- \u8220"I have made my slave free\u8221" (past tense); {\i asui ben chorin} \u8212- \u8220"he is made free\u8221" (present); {\i harei hu ben chorin} \u8212- \u8220"behold he is free\u8221" \u8212- all of these are valid formulations in a {\i shtar shichrur}. Even past tense, which might seem to describe something already done rather than to accomplish anything new, is read as: \u8220"through this document, I have made him free.\u8221" All are operative.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Future Tense\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The dispute concerns the future tense: {\i a\u8217'asenu ben chorin} \u8212- \u8220"I will make him free.\u8221" {\i Rebbi} holds that this works: the intent is that through this {\i shtar} he becomes free, even if expressed in the future tense. {\i Chachamim} disagree: this is merely a promise of future intent, not a present act of manumission, and therefore does not take effect.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Language Irrelevant\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabi Yochanan} resolves the underlying issue with a sweeping principle: {\i v\u8217'kullan bi-shtar} \u8212- all of these are evaluated as language within a document. The document itself is the operative act; the linguistic tense within it is secondary. In a {\i shtar}, what matters is that the document formally accomplishes the manumission \u8212- not whether the verb is conjugated in the past, present, or future.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Field Transfer\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The identical analysis applies to a {\i shtar} transferring a field as a gift. {\i Nasati sadeh ploni l\u8217'ploni} \u8212- \u8220"I have granted this field\u8221" (past); {\i nesunah l\u8217'ploni} \u8212- \u8220"it is granted\u8221" (present); {\i harei hi shelo} \u8212- \u8220"behold it is his\u8221" \u8212- all are valid. {\i Etenenah l\u8217'ploni} \u8212- \u8220"I will give it\u8221" (future): {\i Rebbi} says it takes effect, {\i Chachamim} say it does not. And {\i Rabi Yochanan}\u8217's principle applies here too: {\i v\u8217'kullan bi-shtar} \u8212- in the context of a document, the language accomplishes what it intends, regardless of tense.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Delivery Disputes\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Secret Freeing\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} turns to disputes over whether manumission actually occurred. If the owner says, \u8220"I have freed you,\u8221" and the slave says, \u8220"You did not free me\u8221" \u8212- {\i chashinan shema zika lo al yedei acher} \u8212- we are concerned that the owner may in fact have freed him through a third party. Since delivering a {\i shtar shichrur} through an intermediary counts as delivery \u8212- receiving freedom is presumed to be a benefit, and a person can accept a benefit for another without the other\u8217's knowledge \u8212- the slave may have been freed without being aware of it. The slave\u8217's protestation cannot therefore be taken at face value.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Direct Delivery\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If the owner specifies: {\i kasavti v\u8217'nasati lo} \u8212- \u8220"I wrote a {\i shtar shichrur} and gave it directly to him\u8221" \u8212- and the slave responds: {\i kasav li v\u8217'lo nasan li} \u8212- \u8220"he wrote it but did not give it to me,\u8221" or {\i lo kasav li v\u8217'lo nasan li} \u8212- \u8220"he neither wrote it nor gave it to me\u8221" \u8212- the {\i Gemara} invokes the principle:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Slave Believed\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Hoda\u8217'as ba\u8217'al din k\u8217'me\u8217'ah edim dami} \u8212- the admission of a litigant counts as the testimony of one hundred witnesses. In this case, the slave is the litigant whose admission controls: he is admitting that he is not free, which hurts his own interest. The question of {\i whose} admission is operative here is subtle \u8212- both parties might seem to be making admissions against their own interest \u8212- but the {\i Gemara}\u8217's ruling is that it is the slave who is believed. {\i Tosafot} explains: one can construct a plausible scenario in which the owner attempted to give the {\i shtar shichrur} but the slave did not truly consent to accept it, rendering the delivery ineffective. There is no comparable scenario in which the owner wrote and delivered the {\i shtar} without genuinely intending to do so. Since the slave\u8217's account is consistent with a plausible set of facts, he is credited, and the slave remains in his current status.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Field Escrow\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The parallel case: if a person says, \u8220"I gave this field to so-and-so,\u8221" and the recipient says, \u8220"You never gave it to me\u8221" \u8212- again {\i chashinan shema zika lo al yedei acher}, we are concerned that perhaps it was delivered through a third party. If the owner then specifies that he gave it directly, and the recipient denies it \u8212- {\i hoda\u8217'as ba\u8217'al din k\u8217'me\u8217'ah edim dami} \u8212- the recipient\u8217's denial is credited and the gift is not binding.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Fruit Rights\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A further question arises in cases of uncertainty: {\i mi ochel peiros} \u8212- who benefits from the fruit of the field in the interim? {\i Rav Chisda} holds that the original owner ({\i nosen}) continues to eat the fruit \u8212- since we have concluded that the gift did not take effect, the field remains his. {\i Rabbah} holds that the fruit is placed in escrow \u8212- {\i meshalshein es ha-peiros} \u8212- held by a third party pending resolution.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Father vs.\u160 ?Son\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} resolves the apparent dispute by distinguishing between cases. {\i Ha b\u8217'abba ha b\u8217'bra} \u8212- the two rulings refer to different scenarios. If it is the {\i father} \u8212- the original owner \u8212- who denies having received or accepted the gift, his denial is fully credited: he is the one most likely to know, and his admission against interest is entirely reliable. The field remains the gifter\u8217's, and the gifter retains the fruit. But if it is the {\i son} \u8212- an heir \u8212- who claims his father never received the gift, the son\u8217's knowledge is not as certain. Perhaps his father did receive it and never told him. In that case we cannot simply credit the son\u8217's denial, and the fruit is therefore held in escrow until the matter is clarified.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Gittin 41\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Slave & Apoteki\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tikkun Olam Intro\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The daf begins at the bottom of 40b, where the {\i mishnah} introduces another case falling under the category of rabbinic enactments made {\i mipnei tikkun olam} \u8212- for the betterment of the world \u8212- with a particular focus on the legal status of slaves. The {\i mishnah} reads: {\i eved she\u8217'asa rabbo apoteki l\u8217'acherim} \u8212- a slave whose master designated him as an {\i apoteki}, a focused lien, for others.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Apoteki Definition\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 An {\i apoteki} is a particular form of lien that grants the lienholder strong and concentrated rights over the specific object designated as collateral. As we will see, this focused character cuts in both directions: it may intensify the lienholder\u8217's claim to the designated property while simultaneously weakening \u8212- or even eliminating \u8212- his ability to collect from the debtor\u8217's other assets.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishna Scenario\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 In the {\i mishnah}\u8217's case, a slave owner \u8212- we will call him Reuven \u8212- owes money to Shimon. To secure the debt, Reuven designates his slave as an {\i apoteki}, granting Shimon the right to collect his debt from the slave. Subsequently, {\i veshichraro} \u8212- he freed him. The simple reading is that Reuven freed the slave. Shimon is now without recourse: his lien was on a piece of property, and a free person is no longer property against which a lien can be enforced. As a strict matter of law, {\i shurat hadin ein ha\u8217'eved chayav klum} \u8212- the slave bears no legal obligation whatsoever. It was Reuven who owed Shimon money; the slave did nothing wrong.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishna Readings\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Yet the {\i mishnah} then prescribes a remedy {\i mipnei tikkun olam}: {\i kofin et rabbo ve\u8217'oseh oto ben chorin} \u8212- we compel the master to make him a free person. This is immediately puzzling, since the slave appears already to have been freed. The {\i mishnah} adds that {\i vechotev shtar al damav} \u8212- the slave writes a promissory note for his own monetary value \u8212- apparently obligating himself to reimburse that value to Shimon. This too is difficult: why should the slave bear any financial burden for what Reuven did? And why must a new writ of freedom be written at all? These difficulties drive two distinct readings of the {\i mishnah} in the {\i gemara}, which we will now examine. Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel offers an alternative: {\i eino kotev ela meshachrer kotev} \u8212- it is not the slave who writes the debt but rather the one who freed him, i.e., Reuven, who must compensate Shimon. At least that much makes intuitive sense, even if the standard reading of the {\i mishnah} resists a clean interpretation.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Raba\u8217's Principle\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Three Categories\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Before presenting the two readings of the {\i mishnah}, the {\i gemara} invokes the principle of Rava: {\i hekdesh chametz veshichrur mafki\u8217'in midei shibud} \u8212- sanctification to the Temple, {\i chametz}, and the freeing of a slave each have the power to destroy any lien that may rest upon the object in question. This principle explains the threshold question: how was Reuven able to free the slave in a way that extinguished Shimon\u8217's lien entirely? The answer lies in the nature of these three categories.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hekdesh Analysis\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The case of {\i hekdesh} \u8212- sanctification \u8212- requires a distinction. If one owns a house or field, designates it as an {\i apoteki} for a creditor, and then transfers it to the {\i Beis Hamikdash}, this is fundamentally a question of ownership: who now owns this object? Because Shimon\u8217's lien gives him property rights that survive a transfer of ownership \u8212- just as he could seize property sold to a third party \u8212- he can do the same even when the property is transferred to the {\i Beis Hamikdash}. The Temple\u8217's ownership is, in legal terms, simply another form of ownership. Shimon\u8217's lien survives this transaction, and he may, through some formal process of redemption to remove the sanctity, still collect his debt.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hekdesh Types\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The analysis changes, however, when the sanctification is of an entirely different character. Consider a cow designated as an {\i apoteki} for Shimon, which the owner then consecrates as an {\i olah}, a burnt offering. Here the transformation is not one of ownership but of {\i identity}. The cow is no longer a piece of transferable property \u8212- it has become a sacrifice, and its very being, {\i kedushat haguf}, is now categorically holy. No concept of property lien can attach to such an object, for the same reason one cannot redeem a sacrifice unless it bears a blemish: it is simply no longer in the legal universe of ownable, transferable things. The lien dissolves entirely.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shichrur & Chametz\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The case of {\i shichrur} \u8212- freeing a slave \u8212- follows the same logic. A slave is {\i halachically} classified as property, and as such, Shimon\u8217's lien on him is fully operative: were Reuven to sell the slave, Shimon could pursue him from the buyer. But the moment Reuven frees the slave, that person becomes a {\i ben chorin} \u8212- a free person \u8212- and the very concept of a lien on a free human being is inconceivable. Freedom is not a transfer of ownership; it is a transformation of identity that removes the person entirely from the category of property.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Chametz} works analogously, though with a different mechanism. Suppose a non-Jew holds an {\i apoteki} on someone\u8217's {\i chametz}, and Passover arrives, rendering that {\i chametz assur behana\u8217'ah} \u8212- forbidden to derive any benefit from. Even if the owner would be happy to have the non-Jew collect his debt by taking the {\i chametz}, he cannot, because deriving benefit is forbidden. But more fundamentally, something that is {\i assur behana\u8217'ah} is no longer a normal object of commerce and ownership. Its forbidden status removes it from the ordinary legal sphere in which liens operate. Thus all three categories \u8212- {\i hekdesh} of the sanctified-in-identity type, {\i shichrur}, and {\i chametz} \u8212- share the common feature of transforming the fundamental nature of the object and expelling it from the domain of liened property.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rav\u8217's Reading\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 First Master Freed\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 With Rava\u8217's principle established, the {\i gemara} presents its first interpretation. {\i Amar Rav rabbo rishon} \u8212- Rav reads the {\i mishnah} as saying that the first master, Reuven, is the one who freed the slave. This is the straightforward reading: Reuven freed him, and by operation of Rava\u8217's principle, Shimon\u8217's lien was thereby extinguished. {\i Shurat hadin ein ha\u8217'eved chayav klum l\u8217'rabbo sheni} \u8212- as a matter of law, the slave owes nothing to what would have been his second master, i.e., Shimon. Shimon simply loses his debt.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 False Reclaim Risk\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But a practical problem remains. {\i Shema yimtza\u8217'enu bashuk veyomar lo avdi atah} \u8212- lest Shimon encounter the slave in the marketplace and declare: \u8220"You are still my slave.\u8221" This is not a merely theoretical concern. Shimon had highly visible and documented rights over this slave, in the form of a strong {\i apoteki} lien. Even after Reuven\u8217's legally valid act of manumission, Shimon has both the motive and the potential leverage to reclaim the slave, arguing that Reuven\u8217's debt remains unpaid and that the slave is rightfully his. As anyone familiar with the brutal history of slavery knows, legal freedom does not always translate into effective protection against those who have more power.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Forced Manumission\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 To protect the freed slave, the rabbis therefore enacted: {\i kofin et rabbo sheni ve\u8217'oseh oto ben chorin} \u8212- we compel Shimon, the one who would purport to be his new master, to write a writ of manumission. Even though Shimon was never truly the slave\u8217's owner, this formal document prevents him from making any future claim. With the {\i shtar} of freedom in hand, the slave is shielded from Shimon\u8217's potential reclamation. But in order to induce Shimon\u8217's cooperation, the rabbis required {\i vechotev eved shtar al damav} \u8212- the slave writes an IOU to Shimon for his own monetary value. This is admittedly unfair to the slave, who did nothing wrong. Reuven is the one who deprived Shimon of his security. Nevertheless, the rabbis recognized this as a worthwhile tradeoff from the slave\u8217's perspective: better to be definitively protected against being seized as a slave by someone with more social power, in exchange for a debt that can be repaid over time. The protection of one\u8217's freedom is worth the financial burden.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gamliel Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabban Gamliel disagrees: {\i eino kotev ela meshachrer kotev} \u8212- it is not the slave who writes the debt but the one who freed him, i.e., Reuven, who owes the money to Shimon. This is the morally intuitive position: Reuven caused the damage to Shimon by freeing the slave; Reuven should bear the financial consequence.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Hezek Shibud Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Damaging Lien\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What is the underlying dispute between Rabban Gamliel and the {\i chachamim}? {\i B\u8217'mazik shibudo shel havero kamifligi} \u8212- they debate the question of whether destroying another person\u8217's lien constitutes a compensable tort. The scenario can be generalized: if Reuven owns a house on which Shimon holds a lien, and I destroy the house, I may be liable to Reuven for destroying his property \u8212- but am I also liable to Shimon, whose security interest I have wiped out? Shimon held a form of property right, and my action eliminated it. Is the destruction of a lien treated as a tort against the lienholder?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gamliel: Liable\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabban Gamliel holds {\i hayyav} \u8212- liable. Reuven, by freeing the slave, destroyed Shimon\u8217's lien. That act constitutes a tortious injury to Shimon\u8217's property right, and therefore Reuven owes Shimon compensation. Because Reuven is legally obligated to pay, the rabbis\u8217' enactment of {\i tikkun olam} rests on Reuven paying \u8212- not the slave.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chachamim: Not Liable\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i chachamim} hold {\i patur} \u8212- exempt. Reuven is not legally obligated for having freed his own slave, even though this cost Shimon his security. Once there is no tort liability on Reuven, and the rabbis need nonetheless to induce Shimon to write a manumission document for the slave\u8217's protection, the one who stands to benefit from this arrangement \u8212- the slave \u8212- becomes the one who bears the financial burden. As the {\i gemara} reinforces: {\i itmar nami, hamazzik shibudo shel havero \u8212- banu l\u8217'mahloket Rabban Gamliel verabanan} \u8212- when one damages another\u8217's lien, that is precisely the core of their dispute.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Ulla\u8217's Reading\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shimon Freed\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Ulla offers a fundamentally different reading of the {\i mishnah}. {\i Rabbo sheni shichraro} \u8212- it is the second master, Shimon, who freed the slave. That is, the {\i mishnah}\u8217's phrase \u8220"and he freed him\u8221" refers not to Reuven\u8217's genuine act of manumission but to Shimon\u8217's attempted \u8212- and legally ineffective \u8212- declaration of freedom. Shimon, the creditor who held the {\i apoteki}, went ahead and wrote a writ purporting to free the slave. Since Shimon was never actually the slave\u8217's owner, this act had no legal force.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 On this reading, {\i shurat hadin ein ha\u8217'eved chayav klum} \u8212- the slave is not obligated \u8212- means not that he owes no money, but that he is not yet obligated in {\i mitzvot} as a full {\i ben chorin}, since he has not actually been freed. This is admittedly a very difficult reading of the {\i mishnah}, which speaks in context about financial obligations, not about {\i mitzvah} obligations. Ulla\u8217's interpretation requires reading that phrase in an unusual register.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Perception & Reality\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The problem that {\i tikkun olam} must address on Ulla\u8217's reading is one of perception rather than legal status. {\i Shema yatza alav shem ben chorin} \u8212- a rumor has spread that this person is a free man. Shimon, a man presumably of means and social standing, went around announcing he was freeing the slave; people now believe the slave is free. This creates serious social harm: the slave will be treated as a {\i ben chorin}, may contract a marriage that turns out to be invalid, and will be assumed to bear obligations in {\i mitzvot} that he does not yet fully bear. The perception does not match the reality.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i chachamim}\u8217's solution is elegant: rather than correct the perception, correct the facts. {\i Kofin et rabbo rishon ve\u8217'oso ben chorin} \u8212- we compel Reuven, the true owner, to actually free the slave, thereby making reality conform to the widely held perception.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Reuven Pays Back\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Once Reuven is compelled to free the slave, the slave receives his freedom without having done anything to earn or pay for it. {\i Vechotev shtar al damav} \u8212- the slave then writes a promissory note to Reuven for his value, which represents a reasonable and even advantageous arrangement for the slave: freedom now in exchange for a debt to be repaid over time.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel again disagrees: not the slave but {\i meshachrer kotev} \u8212- the one who freed him, i.e., Shimon, pays. As Rashi explains, consider the case where the slave\u8217's value exceeds the underlying debt \u8212- say, the debt was eight hundred and the slave is worth a thousand. Had Shimon simply seized the slave to collect his debt, he would have owed Reuven the surplus of two hundred. Now that Reuven is compelled to free the slave \u8212- costing him a thousand \u8212- and the debt of eight hundred is extinguished by Shimon\u8217's action, Shimon must return to Reuven the two hundred difference. The question, then, is whether that remaining obligation falls on the slave or on Shimon.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What underlies this dispute? {\i Behezek she\u8217'eino nikkar kamiflagi} \u8212- they debate the question of {\i hezek she\u8217'eino nikkar}, a non-visible or non-manifest damage. Shimon\u8217's act of purporting to free the slave effectively caused Reuven\u8217's ownership to be dissolved \u8212- the rabbis, responding to the perception Shimon created, compelled Reuven to lose his property. The damage, however, is not straightforwardly visible. {\i Tosafot} notes a legitimate difficulty here: is it truly imperceptible? A person walking around as a free man is visibly different from a slave. {\i Tosafot} further observes that the better characterization may be that the damage was indirect \u8212- Shimon did not himself take the slave; he merely made a declaration or issued a document that, through the rabbinic response to the resulting perception, led Reuven to lose his property. Either way, the dispute is whether this kind of damage \u8212- indirect, or at minimum not fully visible \u8212- constitutes a compensable tort: {\i mar savar shemeh hezek, umar savar lo shemeh hezek}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If Shimon\u8217's action is treated as damage, then Shimon pays Reuven the difference. If it is not treated as damage, then since no tort liability attaches to Shimon, the slave \u8212- who is the beneficiary of the freedom \u8212- bears the financial obligation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Textual Problems\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then asks why Ulla did not simply adopt Rav\u8217's reading, and vice versa. {\i Amar lach seifa rabo kari lei?} \u8212- Ulla objects that on Rav\u8217's reading, the {\i mishnah} refers to Shimon as \u8220"the master\u8221" when describing who writes the writ, but Shimon is not truly the master; calling him such would be misleading. Rav responds in kind: {\i sheini meshacher kari lei} \u8212- on Ulla\u8217's reading, the {\i mishnah} calls Shimon \u8220"the one who freed him\u8221" when it discusses Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel\u8217's position, but Shimon did not really free anyone; it was Reuven who actually executed the manumission. The conclusion is that both readings have textual problems: only Reuven is genuinely both the master and the freer, but the {\i mishnah}\u8217's structure forces one to read Shimon into one of those roles, and neither assignment is entirely natural. Ulla\u8217's reading is particularly strained, since it requires interpreting {\i ein ha\u8217'eved chayav klum} as referring to {\i mitzvah} obligations rather than monetary ones \u8212- a difficult read by any measure.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Apoteki Types\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 River Swamps Field\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having treated the {\i mishnah}\u8217's case at length, the {\i gemara} takes the opportunity to examine the nature of the {\i apoteki} lien more closely. A new case is introduced: {\i ha\u8217'oseh sadeh apotiki l\u8217'acherim veshatfah nahar} \u8212- someone designates his field as an {\i apoteki} for a creditor, and the field is subsequently swamped by a river and rendered worthless. The question is whether the creditor can now collect from the debtor\u8217's other assets.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Exclusive Rights\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Immi Shafir Noeh} \u8212- a sage whose name means \u8220"of pleasant visage\u8221" \u8212- cites Rabbi Yochanan: {\i eino goveh mishar nechasim} \u8212- the creditor cannot collect from the debtor\u8217's other property. The {\i apoteki} is exclusive; by accepting this focused lien, the creditor surrendered his diffuse lien rights over the debtor\u8217's other holdings. If this property is gone, so is his ability to collect. {\i Va\u8217'avuah deShmuel amar goveh mishar nechasim} \u8212- the father of Shmuel disagrees, holding that the {\i apoteki} prioritizes that particular asset but does not eliminate the creditor\u8217's rights to other property; if that asset becomes unavailable, the creditor may look elsewhere.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Nachman bar Yitzchak quips that because Immi is so attractive in person, his {\i halachic} rulings are correspondingly unattractive \u8212- he did not find this position compelling. Why should strengthening one\u8217's lien on a specific asset work to eliminate all other liens?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Three Grades\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} resolves the apparent tension by distinguishing three grades of lien status. At the baseline level, one has ordinary, {\i stam} liens \u8212- diffuse rights over the debtor\u8217's assets generally, with no particular concentration on any one item. A standard {\i apoteki} \u8212- {\i stam apotiki} \u8212- grants the creditor stronger and more immediate rights to the designated property (perhaps he can void any attempted sale of it), but it does not eliminate his residual rights over the debtor\u8217's other assets. If the designated property is unavailable, he may still collect elsewhere. An {\i apoteki meforash}, however, is an explicitly exclusive designation \u8212- the parties agreed that {\i lo yehe pira\u8217'on ela mizu}, collection will come from this and this alone. In such a case, if the property is destroyed or lost, the creditor is simply out of luck and cannot pursue other assets.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This threefold scheme is corroborated by a {\i braitah}: {\i ha\u8217'oseh sadeh apotiki l\u8217'acher veshatfah nahar goveh mishar nechasim} \u8212- a standard {\i apotiki} does not prevent collection from other assets. But {\i ve\u8217'im amar lo lo yehe lach pira\u8217'on ela mizu} \u8212- if he explicitly said there shall be collection from this alone, then {\i eino goveh mishar nechasim} \u8212- no collection from elsewhere is permitted.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Wife vs.\u160 ?Creditor\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A further {\i braitah} extends the analysis to the {\i ketubah}. {\i Ha\u8217'oseh sadehu apotiki l\u8217'baal chov o liktuvas isha goveh mishar nechasim} \u8212- designating a field as an {\i apoteki} for a creditor or for a wife\u8217's {\i ketubah} does not prevent collection from other property. However, Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel distinguishes the two cases: {\i baal chov goveh mishar nechasim} \u8212- a creditor retains his general collection rights even after an {\i apoteki}, but {\i isha einah govah mishar nechasim} \u8212- a wife may not collect from other property. The reason given is {\i she\u8217'ein darchah shel isha l\u8217'chazer achar batei dinin} \u8212- it is not the manner of women to go pursing debts through the court system, tracing paper trails of transferred assets across multiple jurisdictions and proceedings.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might initially think this is a disadvantage \u8212- by restricting her to one asset, you limit her recovery. {\i Tosafot} addresses this directly: it is in fact protective, in that the {\i apoteki meforash} gives her iron-clad rights to that one designated asset and shields it from being sold away from her. She need not trace anything; she has a secured, nearly unchallengeable claim on that specific property. That is Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel\u8217's view of what serves her interests best.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Half-Slave Half-Free\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Half Status Paradox\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now turns to a celebrated {\i mishnah} dealing with the {\i chatzi eved chatzi ben chorin} \u8212- the person who is simultaneously half slave and half free. The simplest way this status arises is when a slave is owned jointly by two partners and one of them frees his half share while the other does not. The legal result is genuinely paradoxical. As we observed regarding the freeing of a slave, the transformation is not merely a question of who owns him \u8212- it is a question of identity. The half of him that has been freed has the legal status of a {\i ben chorin}, with the obligations and privileges that entails: he is obligated in {\i mitzvot} as a free person, and he is permitted to marry a Jewish woman. The half of him that remains a slave has the status of an {\i eved}, with a diminished level of {\i mitzvah} obligation and a prohibition against marrying a free Jewish woman. That {\i halacha} permits this dual status to coexist in a single person is itself remarkable.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Beit Hillel View\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Beit Hillel} addressed this situation pragmatically: {\i oved et rabbo yom echad ve\u8217'et atzmo yom echad} \u8212- he works for his master one day and for himself the next. {\i Beit Hillel} focused on the economic dimension of the dual status. The slave\u8217's master is entitled to half his labor and earnings by virtue of the remaining half-ownership, and the slave himself retains the other half. A rotating daily schedule satisfies this, and {\i Shabbos} provides an even number of workdays that makes the division clean.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Marriage Problem\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Beit Shammai} objected: {\i tikantam et rabbo ve\u8217'et atzmo lo tikantam} \u8212- you have taken care of the master\u8217's interests, but you have not taken care of the slave\u8217's. The financial arrangement addresses only the monetary dimension of the dual status. But there is a deeper problem: the slave cannot marry. {\i Lisha shifcha i efshar shekvar chatzyo ben chorin} \u8212- he cannot marry a female slave because the part of him that is a free man is governed by the prohibition derived from the verse {\i lo yiheyeh kadeshah}, which bars a free Jewish man from such a union. {\i Bas chorin i efshar shekvar chatzyo eved} \u8212- he cannot marry a free woman either, because the part of him that is a slave is subject to the prohibition against a Jewish woman marrying a male slave, derived from the verse {\i lo yekadesh}. The {\i chatzi eved chatzi ben chorin} is entirely shut out from the institution of marriage \u8212- not as a consequence of poverty or circumstance, but as a direct legal result of his status.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Lo Tohu Bera\u8217'ah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Perhaps one might suggest that he simply remain unmarried and forgo procreation. {\i Beit Shammai} rejected this emphatically: {\i lo tohu bera\u8217'ah, lashevet yetzarah} \u8212- as the verse says, God did not create the world to be void and empty; He fashioned it to be inhabited and populated. The world\u8217's very purpose is procreation and settlement, and to leave this man in a state where he is permanently barred from that most fundamental of human activities is intolerable. {\i Ela mipnei tikkun olam} \u8212- for the sake of {\i tikkun olam}, {\i kofin et rabbo umotzi\u8217'o ben chorin} \u8212- we compel the master to free him entirely, and {\i vechotev shtar chatzi damav} \u8212- the slave then writes a note for half his monetary value, reimbursing the master for the cost of the half that was not yet free.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Beit Shammai}\u8217's language here is suggestive: {\i tikantam et rabbo, ve\u8217'et atzmo lo tikantam} \u8212- you have \u8220"fixed\u8221" the master\u8217's situation, but have not \u8220"fixed\u8221" the slave\u8217's. The word {\i tikkun} echoes {\i tikkun olam}, and the connection is explicit: it is not just the {\i mitzvah} of {\i peru urvu} that is being violated, it is something more foundational \u8212- the very purpose of creation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Beit Hillel Reversal\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Vechazru Beis Hillel lehorot kedivrei Beis Shammai} \u8212- ultimately, {\i Beit Hillel} reversed their position and ruled in accordance with {\i Beit Shammai}. This is a striking reversal. We generally expect {\i Beit Hillel} to be the more lenient, more accommodating school \u8212- the ones responsive to the complexities of human circumstance. Here it is {\i Beit Shammai} who argued for the slave\u8217's liberation, and {\i Beit Hillel} who eventually accepted their stringent position.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Why did {\i Beit Shammai} invoke the verse {\i lo tohu bera\u8217'ah} rather than simply citing the {\i mitzvah} of {\i peru urvu}? {\i Tosafot} addresses this directly: the {\i mitzvah} of {\i peru urvu} alone might be insufficient grounds to compel manumission, because one can be in a state of {\i ones} \u8212- involuntary inability to fulfill a {\i mitzvah} \u8212- without this generating any obligation on others to remedy the situation. People sometimes cannot perform {\i mitzvot} through no fault of their own, and God does not hold them accountable. We would not free a slave merely because he cannot sit in a {\i sukkah}. But {\i beit Shammai}\u8217's argument goes beyond the technical question of {\i mitzvah} obligation: this is not just a {\i mitzvah} the slave is unable to fulfill. It touches something so fundamental to the human condition \u8212- the very reason the world was created \u8212- that leaving him in this state is simply unacceptable. A fully owned slave can marry and have children within his permitted sphere; this {\i chatzi eved chatzi ben chorin} cannot marry anyone at all. That is what demands correction.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} develops from this a broader idea, elaborated in later {\i halacha}, that there exists a separate obligation called {\i lashevet} \u8212- derived from {\i lo tohu bera\u8217'ah} \u8212- which applies to women and to slaves and which expresses this profound imperative of population and settlement that underlies and exceeds the technical {\i mitzvah} of {\i peru urvu}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Half-Freedom Creation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Master Frees Half\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now turns to the question of how the status of {\i chatzi eved chatzi ben chorin} can be created by a single owner. The {\i mishnah} assumed the case of two partners, but can one person, who owns a slave entirely, free only half? {\i Tanu rabbanan} \u8212- our rabbis taught: {\i hamishachrer chatzi avdo} \u8212- one who frees half of his slave. {\i Rabbi omer kana} \u8212- Rabbi holds that it takes effect; the slave acquires half of himself and becomes half-free. {\i Vachachamim omrim lo kana} \u8212- the sages hold that it does not take effect at all.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi: Half Works\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Raba explains: {\i machlokes beshtar} \u8212- the debate is specifically when the attempted half-liberation is effected by a {\i shtar}, a written writ of manumission. Rabbi derives his position from the verse dealing with a man who sleeps with a female slave: {\i vehaftay lo niftasah} \u8212- she has not been fully redeemed, {\i o chufshah lo nitan lah} \u8212- or her freedom has not been given to her. The first clause speaks of redemption through money ({\i pidyon}) and uses the formulation \u8220"not fully redeemed,\u8221" already implying the possibility of partial redemption. The second clause speaks of freedom given through a document \u8212- the {\i shtar}. Rabbi reads a {\i hekesh}, a juxtaposition: the {\i shtar} is linked to the money. Just as money can achieve half-liberation \u8212- paying half one\u8217's value and thereby half-owning oneself \u8212- so too a {\i shtar} can achieve half-liberation. {\i Af shtar nami bein kulo bein chatziyo} \u8212- the {\i shtar} works whether for full or for half freedom.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chachamim: All-or-Nothing\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i chachamim} derive from this same verse by way of a {\i gezeirah shavah} \u8212- a verbal analogy. The phrase {\i chufshah lo nitan lah}, \u8220"her freedom given to her,\u8221" employs the word {\i lah}, \u8220"to her,\u8221" which also appears in the laws of divorce: {\i vechatav lah sefer keritut}, \u8220"and he shall write for her a writ of separation.\u8221" This {\i gezeirah shavah} ({\i lah lah}) teaches that the {\i shtar} of manumission is structurally analogous to a {\i get} of divorce. And a {\i get} operates on an all-or-nothing basis \u8212- one cannot half-divorce a wife; a woman is either divorced or she is not. Accordingly, {\i af eved nami chatzio lo} \u8212- a slave cannot be half-freed by a {\i shtar}. The {\i shtar} speaks to the totality of personal identity and cannot be applied in fractional terms.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Raba\u8217's Reading\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Raba\u8217's reading of the debate thus emerges as follows. When it comes to money, all opinions agree that partial payment for one\u8217's freedom can produce a genuine half-free status \u8212- this is textually explicit in the {\i pasuk} ({\i peduya ve\u8217'eina peduya}). The debate is solely about the {\i shtar}: can a writ of manumission be issued for half a slave, or must it be all-or-nothing by analogy to a {\i get}? The conceptual underpinning of this question is significant. Money-based liberation is grounded in ownership: I pay half my value; I now half-own myself. The {\i shtar}, by contrast, speaks to identity rather than ownership \u8212- as discussed earlier in the context of why freeing a slave destroys a lien. A {\i shtar} either transforms a person into a {\i ben chorin} or it does not; it is about who you are, not about who owns what percentage of you. That is the pull toward the {\i get} analogy. Rabbi, however, holds that because money works at half \u8212- and because a slave is fundamentally different from a wife in that money can free a slave but not dissolve a marriage \u8212- the {\i shtar} too can operate at the halfway mark.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Aval bechesef divrei hakol kana} \u8212- by money, everyone agrees it works.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rav Yosef\u8217's View\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shtar All-or-Nothing\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Yosef offers a fundamentally different reading of the same debate. {\i Machloket bechesef} \u8212- the dispute between Rabbi and the {\i chachamim} is specifically about money, not about the {\i shtar}. On Rav Yosef\u8217's reading, there is no dispute about the {\i shtar}: {\i aval beshtar divrei hakol lo kana} \u8212- by a {\i shtar}, everyone agrees that half-liberation does not take effect; a {\i shtar} is all-or-nothing for all opinions. The debate is whether money can produce a half-free status. Rabbi reads {\i vehafpedeh lo nifdatah} as explicitly describing a woman who is half-redeemed \u8212- {\i peduya ve\u8217'eina peduya} \u8212- and therefore money can achieve half-freedom. The {\i chachamim} counter with {\i dibrah Torah kileshon bnei adam} \u8212- the Torah speaks in ordinary human language, and the phrase simply means she has not been redeemed at all; there is no textual evidence for a half-way monetary liberation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 So the two readings are mirror images. Raba holds that money is universally agreed to work halfway and the debate is about the {\i shtar}. Rav Yosef holds that the {\i shtar} is universally agreed to be all-or-nothing and the debate is about money.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Braita Contradiction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Meitivei} \u8212- an objection is raised from a {\i braitah}: {\i hamishachrer chatzi avdo beshtar \u8212- Rebbi omer kana, vachachamim omrim lo kana} \u8212- if someone frees half of his slave with a {\i shtar}, Rabbi says it works and the sages say it does not. This {\i braitah} makes it explicit that Rabbi and the {\i chachamim} debate the case of a {\i shtar}. That directly contradicts Rav Yosef\u8217's claim that everyone agrees a {\i shtar} is all-or-nothing. {\i Tiuvta deRav Yosef tiuvta} \u8212- this is a definitive refutation of Rav Yosef. Moreover, the {\i gemara} observes, the {\i braitah} debates only the {\i shtar} case; by mentioning only that, it seems to imply that money working halfway is undisputed \u8212- which would align with Raba and further undermine Rav Yosef\u8217's position that the money case is the one in dispute. The {\i gemara} asks whether this {\i braitah} constitutes a double refutation of Rav Yosef, on both counts.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Defense of Rav Yosef\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Yosef responds: {\i phaligi beshtar vehu hadin bechesef} \u8212- yes, I accept that they debate the {\i shtar} case; I was wrong about that. But I maintain that they also debate the money case. There is no implication from the {\i braitah}\u8217's silence about money that they agree on it. The {\i braitah} chose to frame the debate in terms of the {\i shtar}, and that does not establish consensus about money. The debate, in Rav Yosef\u8217's revised view, covers both modes: Rabbi holds that both money and {\i shtar} can achieve half-liberation; the {\i chachamim} hold that neither can.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Power of Stringency\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This raises the obvious question: if both money and {\i shtar} are in dispute, why did the {\i braitah} frame the debate specifically about the {\i shtar} and not about money? {\i Lehodiecha kocho deRebbe} \u8212- to demonstrate the extent of Rabbi\u8217's position, its full strength. That Rabbi permits half-liberation via money is, in some ways, less surprising \u8212- money-based liberation tracks ownership, and partial ownership is easily conceptualized. But that Rabbi would extend this to the {\i shtar}, which speaks to identity and which is by analogy all-or-nothing like a {\i get}, represents a far more striking and bold ruling. The {\i braitah} chose to highlight the {\i shtar} case to show how far Rabbi is willing to go.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But then why not, conversely, frame the debate in terms of money, to demonstrate the reach of the {\i chachamim}\u8217's restrictive position \u8212- that not even money works halfway? Here the {\i gemara} invokes the principle {\i koach deheteira adif leh} \u8212- the power of the permitting position is always the more significant, and it is that which the tradition prefers to highlight. It is easy and intuitive to say that something does not work \u8212- that it is not perfect, not complete, and therefore invalid. The {\i chachamim}\u8217's refusal even of half-liberation via money is not surprising; things tend toward either-or. What is remarkable is Rabbi\u8217's readiness to validate the halfway, and most remarkable of all is his willingness to validate it even via {\i shtar}. That is the position worth highlighting, and that is why the {\i braitah} frames the debate as it does.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Gittin 42\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rabbi vs.\u160 ?Rabbanan\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbah\u8217's Framework\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The daf opens by picking up from the bottom of 41b, in the midst of the debate between Rabbi and the Rabbanan over whether a slave can be half-freed. Two approaches had been proposed. Rabbah argued that the dispute concerns the {\i shtar} \u8212- the documentary act of freedom \u8212- which pertains to the slave\u8217's personal status. By contrast, with regard to {\i kessef}, monetary redemption, which concerns the proprietary dimension of the slave, all agree that a partial redemption is effective. The {\i shtar}, which transforms personal status, is precisely where Rabbi and the Rabbanan diverge: Rabbi holds it can work partially; the Rabbanan maintain it cannot.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ulla\u8217's Counter\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Ulla inverts this framework entirely, contending that a {\i shtar} obviously cannot take effect partially \u8212- the dispute, in his view, concerns {\i kessef}. The question of whether monetary redemption can be applied halfway is what divides Rabbi from the Rabbanan.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shifcha Canaanis\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now introduces a {\i baraita} drawn from the {\i parsha} of a {\i shifcha kena\u8217'anis} \u8212- a case in which a man is liable for relations with a non-Jewish slave woman who had been partially designated to another. The {\i pasuk} reads: {\i vehispaday lo nifsasah o chuphshah lo nittan lah} \u8212- \u8220"she has not been redeemed and her freedom has not been given to her.\u8221" Rather than reading this as an emphatic absolute, as one might read {\i nakei lo yenakeh} (\u8220"he shall surely not be cleansed\u8221"), the {\i gemara} reads it as indicating a partial state: {\i vehispaday} \u8212- she has been redeemed; {\i lo nifsasah} \u8212- she has not been redeemed. The resulting status is {\i piduyah ve\u8217'eina piduyah}: she is half-redeemed. Crucially, the {\i baraita} specifies that this partial redemption operates {\i bekessef uveshvei kessef} \u8212- through money or its equivalent. This establishes the principle that at least monetary redemption can function in a partial manner.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Verses Analysis\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 From the {\i baraita}\u8217's opening, we know only that partial freedom can be achieved through money \u8212- {\i ein li ela bekessef}. How do we know that a {\i shtar} can also effect freedom? The {\i baraita} derives this from the continuation of the same {\i pasuk}: {\i o chuphshah lo nittan lah}, \u8220"or her freedom has not been given to her.\u8221" The {\i lah lah} comparison \u8212- a classic {\i gezerah shavah} linking the {\i lah} here to the {\i lah} in the verse {\i vechasav lah sefer kerisus}, which concerns a woman\u8217's bill of divorce \u8212- teaches that just as a woman is divorced by a writ, so too a slave is freed by a writ.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This yields the baseline: a slave can be freed both by money and by {\i shtar}. But the {\i baraita} goes further. From the monetary case we know partial freedom is achievable; but what of partial freedom by {\i shtar}? The verse contains both clauses \u8212- {\i vehispaday lo nifsasah} and {\i o chuphshah lo nittan lah} \u8212- and the {\i baraita} links them through {\i makish shtar lekessef}: the {\i shtar} is analogized to {\i kessef}. Just as monetary redemption can be total or partial, so too documentary freedom can be total or partial. This {\i baraita} is thus thoroughly consistent with Rabbi\u8217's position.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Baraita Attribution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} observes that according to Rav Yosef \u8212- who, after being challenged, conceded that the debate touches upon {\i shtar}, and then argued that it also extends to {\i kessef} \u8212- this {\i baraita} presents no difficulty: it is simply the view of Rabbi, while the Rabbanan would reject both halves of it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The difficulty arises for Rabbah. According to Rabbah, partial redemption through {\i kessef} is a point of universal agreement, while the dispute concerns only {\i shtar}. If so, why does the {\i baraita} first establish the {\i kessef} rule as if it were an innovation, and then derive the {\i shtar} rule from it? Is the opening of the {\i baraita} {\i divrei hakol} while the conclusion is exclusively Rabbi\u8217's position?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava resolves this straightforwardly: yes, precisely that \u8212- {\i reisha divrei hakol, seifa Rebbi}. The first half, that partial monetary redemption is valid, is universally accepted. The second half, that a {\i shtar} can likewise effect partial freedom, represents Rabbi\u8217's contested position. The {\i baraita} naturally opens with the uncontested premise and then uses it as a platform for Rabbi\u8217's novel ruling.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Ashi offers a slightly different formulation: the entire {\i baraita} is Rabbi\u8217's, and Rabbi himself is the one who opens with the agreed-upon {\i kessef} rule before extending it to {\i shtar}. There is nothing unusual about an author building from common ground toward his own distinctive position.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Half-Slave Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishna Problem\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} turns to the {\i mishna}, which addresses a person who is half-slave and half-free \u8212- {\i mi she-chetzyo eved ve-chetzyo ben chorin} \u8212- and the question of whether the master is compelled to free him so that he may marry a woman and have children. According to Rava, the {\i mishna} poses no difficulty: it can be attributed to {\i divrei hakol}, since the half-slave status arose through partial monetary redemption, which all agree is valid.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The difficulty falls on Rav Yosef. Having maintained that the Rabbanan reject not only partial {\i shtar} freedom but also partial monetary redemption, Rav Yosef faces a {\i mishna} premised on the very phenomenon the Rabbanan supposedly deny. Can the {\i mishna} really represent only Rabbi\u8217's minority view and not the Rabbanan?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Joint Ownership\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Ravina resolves the difficulty \u8212- as Rashi had already noted on the earlier {\i mishna} \u8212- by identifying the {\i mishna}\u8217's case as one of joint ownership: the slave belongs to two masters, and one of them frees his portion entirely. Each master divests himself completely of his own half-ownership. Since the act of the freeing master is total with respect to what he owns, even the Rabbanan agree it is effective. The resulting anomaly \u8212- that the slave is half-owned by a second master and half-free \u8212- arises not from a deficient act of liberation but from an external constraint on the outcome.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava\u8217's Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava now offers a more expansive formulation of the same principle: the dispute between Rabbi and the Rabbanan is confined to the case where a single master frees half and {\i retains} half \u8212- {\i shichrer chetzyo ve-hiniach chetzyo}. But if the master frees half and {\i sells} half, or gifts half to another person \u8212- {\i shichrer chetzyo u-machar chetzyo o nasan be-matanah chetzyo} \u8212- then, since he completely divests himself of the slave, {\i keivan de-nafik minei kulei}, even the Rabbanan concede that the act is effective, producing a half-slave half-free individual.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rashi explains the underlying logic: the defect the Rabbanan identify in a partial {\i shtar} is not that it produces an intermediate status but that the act itself is incomplete \u8212- akin to a husband who divorces his wife while retaining some hold over her. The act of {\i shichrur} must mirror the totality of {\i gerushin}: a complete severance. Accordingly, even if the result is that the slave is now half-owned by someone else, what matters is that the master\u8217's own act of liberation was absolute. On this reading, simultaneously selling half and freeing half in a single moment also qualifies, since at the conclusion of the act the master retains nothing.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Estate Division\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye challenges Rava\u8217's position with a {\i baraita}: one who writes over his entire estate to two of his slaves \u8212- {\i ha-kosev nechasav li-shnei avadav} \u8212- produces a situation in which each slave receives half the estate including half of the other slave. Since each is now half-free (by virtue of having received half of himself), each can own property and hold the other slave\u8217's half-ownership, and so {\i hem meshachrerim zeh es zeh} \u8212- they free one another by writing writs of liberation to each other. This {\i baraita} seems to validate Rava\u8217's view that full divestiture in a half-half arrangement works even according to the Rabbanan.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But a second {\i baraita} complicates matters: {\i ha-omer kol nechasai nesunim li-feloni u-feloni avadai} \u8212- one who says \u8220"all my property is given to Reuven and Shimon, my slaves\u8221" \u8212- {\i af atzman lo kanu}, they do not acquire even themselves. This sounds identical to the first case, yet the result is the opposite.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} first proposes attributing the two {\i baraitot} to different authorities: the first to Rabbi, whose view permits partial {\i shichrur}; the second to the Rabbanan, who deny it. But the {\i gemara} then rejects this and argues that both {\i baraitot} can reflect the Rabbanan\u8217's view.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Document Distinction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The resolution turns on two distinct variables. The first concerns the linguistic formula used: {\i kulhu} versus {\i chatzi chatzi}. When the master says to each slave \u8220"I give you {\i all} my property,\u8221" each slave in principle receives the totality \u8212- including the full person of himself. Since, in one valid sequential arrangement, this would work (if you gave everything to one slave, he would own himself and be free), it also works simultaneously. But when the master says \u8220"I give you {\i half}\u8221" to each slave, this can never work sequentially: half-freeing a slave effects nothing in sequence, and therefore, by the same logic, it effects nothing simultaneously either. This distinction \u8212- articulated by Tosafot \u8212- underlies the difference between the two {\i baraitot}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The second resolution ({\i ve-iba\u8217'it eima}) focuses on the number of documents: {\i kan bi-shtar echad kan bi-shnei shtarot}. A single {\i shtar} addressed jointly to two slaves is analogous to a single {\i get} issued to two wives simultaneously \u8212- a technical invalidity. Thus the case of {\i af atzman lo kanu} may involve a single document, which is defective for this reason alone, irrespective of the language used. Two separate {\i shtarot}, by contrast, each addressed to a single slave, would avoid this defect entirely \u8212- and according to Rava, if the formula is {\i kulhu} (all to each), even the Rabbanan would concede that it works.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} ultimately preserves Rava\u8217's principle: the Rabbanan agree that full divestiture in a half-half arrangement is effective, provided the act of liberation meets the technical requirements of a valid {\i shtar shichrur} \u8212- one document per slave \u8212- and the formula used could function validly in sequence.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rav Ashi\u8217's View\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Terminology Issue\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Ashi proposes an entirely different explanation of why the second {\i baraita} \u8212- in which the master says \u8220"all my property is given to Reuven and Shimon, my slaves\u8221" \u8212- fails. The problem, he argues, is not structural or sequential: it is terminological. The master called them {\i avadai} \u8212- \u8220"my slaves\u8221" \u8212- thereby indicating that he conceived of them as remaining his slaves even as he transferred property to them. The word {\i avadai} reveals his intent: he did not mean to free them.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Refram Objects\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rafram challenges this immediately: perhaps {\i avadai} merely describes their prior status \u8212- \u8220"who have been my slaves until now\u8221" \u8212- and carries no implication about their ongoing servitude. To support this, Rafram points to the {\i mishna}: {\i ha-kosev kol nechasav le-avdo} \u8212- one who writes all his property to his slave, the slave {\i yatza le-cheirus}, goes free. The {\i mishna} uses the identical construction {\i avdo} \u8212- \u8220"his slave\u8221" \u8212- and yet that slave undeniably goes free. The formula therefore cannot be read as evidence of intent to retain ownership.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishna Evidence\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} confirms Rafram\u8217's objection. In the {\i mishna}, even when the text reads {\i le-avdo} \u8212- to his slave \u8212- the act is treated as a full {\i shichrur}. The term merely designates the person\u8217's prior status. The {\i gemara} therefore concludes that Rav Ashi\u8217's terminological argument fails: one cannot derive from the word {\i avadai} that the master intended to retain the slaves. The {\i mishna}\u8217's use of {\i avdo} proves that such language does not negate the liberating effect of the transfer.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The consequence is that the terminological explanation is rejected, and the earlier resolutions \u8212- based on document structure or the {\i kulhu} versus {\i chatzi chatzi} distinction \u8212- remain the operative frameworks for understanding the two {\i baraitot}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Goring Damages\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Day-Based Ownership\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now turns from the mechanism of half-freeing to its practical consequences. Before Beis Hillel agreed with Beis Shammai and ruled that the half-slave must be fully freed, the {\i halacha} was that he works half his days for his master and half for himself. The {\i gemara} raises the question: if an ox gores this slave on a day designated for his master, does the master collect the damages? And on a day the slave works for himself, does the slave collect?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The initial premise \u8212- {\i yom shel rabo lerabo, yom shel atzmo le\u8217'atzmo} \u8212- would suggest that his identity, at least in monetary terms, alternates based on the day: on the master\u8217's day he is entirely the master\u8217's, and on his own day he is entirely his own.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Status vs.\u160 ?Labor\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} immediately rejects any extension of this principle to matters of personal status. The suggestion that he might therefore be permitted to marry a free woman on his own days and a slave woman on his master\u8217's days is dismissed out of hand: {\i issura lo ka\u8217'amrinan} \u8212- we make no such ruling regarding his personal status, which remains fixed as half-slave, half-free regardless of the day. The day-based framework applies only to the question of whose earnings he generates, not to who he is.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Calya Karna\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But even within the monetary domain, the day-based principle has limits. The {\i gemara} introduces the concept of {\i kalya karna} \u8212- destruction of the principal. If the slave is gored and killed, the {\i gemara} rules that the payment does not depend on whose day it was but is divided according to his permanent ownership structure: half a {\i knas} to the master and half a {\i kofer} to his heirs. This is because the death of the slave is not a question of whose earnings are lost on a given day \u8212- it is a permanent destruction of the property itself, and the question of who owns him is a permanent, fundamental one that must be assessed at the level of basic ownership. Since he is half-owned by himself and half by his master at all times, the payment is split accordingly.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The day-based rule, then, applies only to situations that are not {\i kalya karna} \u8212- not permanent damage. A temporary injury from which the slave will fully recover does not constitute a destruction of the principal. The slave will eventually return to full capacity, so the owners have not permanently lost anything; they have only lost his labor for the period of recovery. That lost labor is treated as earnings, and earnings go to whoever is entitled to them on the relevant day.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shevet Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Here the {\i gemara} surfaces a dispute between Abaye and Rava with significant implications for this framework.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye holds that even in the case of a non-permanent injury, the payment takes the form of {\i shevet gedola ve-shevet ketana}. {\i Shevet gedola} \u8212- the major work-suspension payment \u8212- treats the temporary incapacity as a form of {\i nezek}: just as permanent injury is assessed by comparing the slave\u8217's market value before and after, so too is a temporary injury, even though he will recover. The comparison is made against the current condition of the slave while injured. {\i Shevet ketana} then covers the additional period of complete incapacitation \u8212- the time during which he cannot perform even minimal tasks. Under Abaye\u8217's view, an ox that gores a slave in a temporary injury would be liable for an assessable {\i nezek}, and this payment, since it concerns a valuation of the slave\u8217's current state rather than a permanent alteration of the principal, would go to whoever is entitled to his proceeds on that day.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava disagrees. In a case of non-permanent injury, there is no {\i nezek} to be assessed via the slave market. The injury produces no permanent diminution of value; once healed, the slave is worth exactly what he was before. The only payment owed is for {\i shevet} \u8212- time out of work \u8212- which is calculated day by day based on what work the slave is unable to perform relative to what he would otherwise do in his injured state. Crucially, {\i shevet} of this kind is a category of payment in cases of personal injury caused by a human being ({\i chavala}); an ox, however, only pays {\i nezek} \u8212- the actual, assessable cost of damage. Since under Rava\u8217's view a temporary injury produces no {\i nezek}, an ox that causes a non-permanent injury to a slave owes nothing at all under the laws of goring.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Damage Split\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This creates a tension in the {\i gemara}\u8217's framework. We had said the day-based rule applies to non-permanent injuries \u8212- implying there is a payment from an ox in such cases that can then be allocated to the appropriate party. But according to Rava, an ox owes nothing for a non-permanent injury, because there is no {\i nezek} and an ox does not pay {\i shevet}. On what basis, then, would the day-based rule ever operate in an ox-goring scenario?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} resolves this in two ways. First, {\i ee-ba\u8217'it eima keshe-hikahu adam}: perhaps the day-based rule applies not to a case of ox-goring at all, but to a case where the injury was caused by a human being. A human injurer does pay {\i shevet}, and if the injury is non-permanent, the {\i shevet} payment \u8212- being a function of lost labor rather than a destruction of the principal \u8212- goes to whoever was entitled to that labor on the relevant day. Second, {\i ee-ba\u8217'it eima memra hu}: the rule as stated is simply an independent teaching ({\i memra}), and Rava himself does not subscribe to it. It is not a {\i baraita} or {\i mishna} that demands his agreement.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The upshot is that permanent damage \u8212- whether death or lasting injury \u8212- is always allocated based on the slave\u8217's fundamental half-half ownership, split between master and slave. Temporary, non-permanent damage, if actionable at all, is allocated based on whose day it was, since it functions as a form of lost earnings rather than a diminution of the underlying property.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Get Shichror Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Payment Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now raises a distinct and unresolved {\i ibba\u8217'ya} concerning the slave who is {\i mechusar get shichrur} \u8212- one who lacks only a {\i get shichrur} to become free. The paradigmatic case is a slave whom the master has declared {\i hefker} (ownerless), or one whom Beis Hillel now compels the master to free: the master no longer holds any monetary claim over the slave, yet the slave\u8217's personal status has not changed, because a formal {\i get shichrur} has not yet been delivered. The master retains, in some sense, the residual status of {\i adon} \u8212- enough standing to write and deliver the liberating document \u8212- but no proprietary claim.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The question: if an ox gores this slave during the interim period, does the master collect the thirty-{\i shekel} {\i knas}? The Torah says {\i kesef shloshim shekalim yiten le-adonav} \u8212- the payment goes to \u8220"his master.\u8221" Is this former master still an {\i adon} in the relevant sense? Or has he forfeited that status entirely along with his monetary ownership? Perhaps the {\i knas}, being a fixed fine rather than a payment for lost property, could be owed to whoever holds formal master-status \u8212- and the residual status needed to write a {\i get shichrur} might suffice. The {\i gemara} leaves the question open.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Half-Slave Proof\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} attempts a proof from the earlier ruling: {\i hamamit mi she-chatzi eved ve-chatzi ben chorin nosei chatzi knas le-rabo ve-chatzi kofer le-yorshav} \u8212- an ox that kills a half-slave, half-free person pays half a {\i knas} to the master and half a {\i kofer} to the heirs. Perhaps this refers to the situation {\i after} Beis Hillel agreed with Beis Shammai \u8212- i.e., the master has effectively been compelled to free the slave and is now only awaiting the delivery of a {\i get shichrur}. If even in that situation the master collects half the {\i knas}, that would prove that the {\i mechusar get shichrur} retains enough master-status to collect {\i knas}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} rejects the proof: {\i lo, be-mishnah rishonah} \u8212- the ruling refers to the original situation ({\i mishnah rishonah}), before Beis Hillel reversed their position, when the half-slave arrangement was stable and the master fully owned his half. This case is not analogous to one in which the master has no monetary ownership at all.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Blinding Proof\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A second attempted proof: if the master knocks out the slave\u8217's tooth and blinds his eye, the slave goes free \u8212- {\i yotzei be-shino}, {\i yotzei be-eino}. This creates a {\i mechusar shtar shichrur} scenario: the master no longer exercises monetary dominion, yet a {\i shtar shichrur} remains to be written. Now, if an ox were to gore the slave during this interval, and if the rule is that the master collects the {\i knas}, then by parity of reasoning, personal injury ({\i chavala}) payments should likewise flow to the master. But the {\i halacha} is that when the master himself injures the slave in this state, the payment goes to the slave. Does this not prove that in the {\i mechusar get shichrur} scenario the master does not collect \u8212- neither {\i knas} nor {\i chavala}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} deflects: {\i eima keivan de-amar eino tzarich} \u8212- perhaps this {\i baraita} follows the opinion that a slave freed by loss of tooth or eye requires no {\i get shichrur} at all. He is automatically free the moment the tooth or eye is lost. In that case, when the master subsequently blinds the other eye, the slave is already a complete free person and collects as such. The scenario is not one of {\i mechusar get shichrur} at all \u8212- there is nothing to be awaited.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Limb Loss\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This leads the {\i gemara} to cite a {\i baraita} cataloguing the dispute among {\i tannaim} on precisely this question: when a master destroys one of the slave\u8217's limbs, does the slave need a {\i get shichrur} to effect his freedom, or does he go free automatically?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Freedom Opinions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yishmael holds that in all such cases the slave goes free but requires a {\i get shichrur} from his master. Rabbi Meir holds he does not require one \u8212- freedom is automatic. Rabbi Eliezer says he does require one; Rabbi Tarfon says he does not; Rabbi Akiva says he does.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i baraita} records that those who weighed the opinions before the sages ({\i ha-machri\u8217'in lifnei chachamim}) \u8212- Rashi notes he is uncertain of the precise referent \u8212- reached a compromise: {\i nir\u8217'im divrei Rabbi Tarfon be-shein va-ayin} \u8212- for tooth and eye, Rabbi Tarfon\u8217's position is compelling: the slave goes free without a {\i get shichrur}, since the Torah explicitly mandates his freedom in those cases. {\i Ve-divrei Rabbi Akiva bi-she\u8217'ar eivarim} \u8212- for other limbs, Rabbi Akiva\u8217's view is correct: a {\i get shichrur} is required, since the slave\u8217's freedom in those cases derives from rabbinic enactment ({\i knas chachamim}) rather than a direct biblical mandate.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} objects: if it is {\i knas chachamim}, why call it a {\i knas}? It is derived from {\i pesukim}! The response: {\i ela eima ho\u8217'il u-midrash chachamim hu} \u8212- since it is known only through rabbinic {\i midrash} and not explicitly stated in the Torah, there is concern that the master might later find the slave in the marketplace and declare \u8220"you are still my slave \u8212- no limb-related freedom applies to you,\u8221" since it is not as universally recognized as the explicit cases of tooth and eye. Tosafot ({\i Rabbeinu Tam}) makes the reasoning explicit: for tooth and eye, where the Torah states it openly, no one can plausibly deny it, and the slave needs no document for evidentiary protection; for other limbs, derived only by inference, the slave may be vulnerable to a master\u8217's bad-faith denial, and a {\i get shichrur} serves as his protective evidence.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Unresolved Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} cannot complete its proof regarding the {\i mechusar get shichrur} because the tooth-and-eye case is disputed: according to several {\i tannaim}, that slave requires no {\i get shichrur} at all and goes free immediately, making the subsequent injury a case of injuring a free person entirely \u8212- not a case of {\i mechusar get shichrur}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The fundamental question \u8212- whether a slave who is no longer the master\u8217's property but is still awaiting a {\i get shichrur} retains enough connection to the master for the master to collect {\i knas}, or {\i chavala}, on his behalf \u8212- remains {\i teku}, unresolved. The {\i gemara} leaves open what formal residue of master-status persists during that intermediate period and which monetary consequences, if any, attach to it. As the {\i gemara} closes the daf, the question of whether it is the {\i knas} (a fixed fine, perhaps more plausibly tied to formal status) or the {\i chavala} payment (a monetary loss that follows actual ownership) that the master might claim is left for further analysis.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Gittin 43\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Half-Slave Monetary Stat\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Half-Slave Intro\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Today\u8217's {\i daf} is 43. We pick up in the middle of the bottom of 42b, dealing with the fascinating phenomenon of an {\i eved} who occupies a middle status: half-free and half-owned by his master. The practical arrangement was that he works for his master half the time and for himself the other half. Yet his personal status creates intractable problems \u8212- he cannot marry a free woman nor a slave woman. Beyond the realm of {\i issur}, monetary questions arise as well: what happens if he is gored?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Permanent vs.\u160 ?Temporary\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} on the previous {\i daf} established a distinction based on the nature of the damage. If the injury is permanent \u8212- his arm is severed, or he is killed \u8212- he is treated as being owned by both parties, and the award is split between himself (or his heirs, a point we will return to) and his master. If the damage is temporary \u8212- his arm swells and he cannot work for a period \u8212- and if that temporary incapacitation is classified as a form of injury rather than merely lost labor, then since the ox that caused it is not liable for lost labor, the payment goes to whichever party \u8220"owns\u8221" him on the day the goring occurred.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Me\u8217'ukav Get Shichrur\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A second scenario concerns a slave awaiting a writ of freedom \u8212- a {\i me\u8217'ukav get shichrur}. Two sub-cases arise. First, the slave who is half-owned: following the ruling of {\i Beis Shammai}, the master is compelled to free him. Second, and perhaps the cleaner case for isolating the conceptual issue: the master renounced ownership \u8212- {\i mafkir} him \u8212- or sold him to {\i Chutz La\u8217'aretz}, thereby incurring an obligation to free him. In these situations, monetary ownership has already dissolved; only the personal status of {\i eved} persists.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} raises whether even in this state the master can still collect {\i knas} \u8212- the fixed penalty paid when a slave is gored and killed. The {\i knas} of thirty {\i shekalim} need not be understood as compensation for the loss of property; it is a fixed punishment imposed on the negligent ox-owner, and conceivably it could still flow to the former master even absent ongoing property ownership. What is striking is that the {\i Gemara} entertains the further possibility that even personal-injury payments might still reach the master, despite the fact that he no longer owns the slave as property. That is genuinely difficult to understand, since any such payment would ordinarily be grounded in a proprietary relationship that has already lapsed.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Terumah Eligibility\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Can He Eat Terumah?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The discussion now shifts from monetary matters to questions of status, beginning approximately twenty lines from the bottom of the {\i amud}, at the line opening with the words {\i u-midrash Chachamim hu}. The {\i Gemara} poses the question: a {\i me\u8217'ukav get shichrur} \u8212- a slave who no longer has any monetary ownership over him but is still awaiting his writ of freedom \u8212- may he eat {\i terumah} if his master is a {\i kohen}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} frames the tension around the verse. The {\i pasuk} identifies the basis for a slave eating {\i terumah} as {\i kinyan kaspo} \u8212- he is the monetary acquisition of his master. Yet this slave is {\i lav kinyan kaspo} \u8212- he is no longer property in any meaningful sense. On the other hand, perhaps {\i terumah} is fundamentally about belonging to the household, a relational rather than strictly proprietary status, and perhaps that relationship persists. Yet the {\i Gemara} does not frame the question in those terms. The emphasis falls squarely on the {\i pasuk\u8217's} insistence on {\i kinyan kaspo}, and the question is whether that language can still apply to someone who is no longer owned.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mixed Babies Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} offers a {\i ta shema} from a ruling of Rav Mesharshiya: a {\i kohenes} whose infant became mixed up with the infant of her {\i shifcha} \u8212- one of the babies is the master\u8217's heir and one is the slave, but no one knows which is which. The ruling is that {\i harei elu ochlin b\u8217'terumah} \u8212- both may eat {\i terumah} \u8212- because {\i terumah} may be eaten either as a {\i kohen} or as the slave of a {\i kohen}, and each child qualifies under one of those categories, even if the identities are uncertain. Furthermore, when they go to collect {\i terumah} at the threshing floor, they receive only one portion together \u8212- and the reason is that a slave may not collect {\i terumah} alone at the threshing floor, lest people mistake him for a {\i kohen}. He must therefore go together with his master. In this confused situation the two children must appear together.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Babies Grow & Free\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 When the mixture grows up \u8212- {\i higdilu hata\u8217'arovet} \u8212- the father has died and one of them now owns the estate, which means one of them owns the other, though no one knows which is which. So they free one another: {\i meshachrerin zeh et zeh}. Each writes the other a {\i shtar shichrur} \u8212- if you are the slave, I am freeing you; if I am the slave, you are freeing me. The {\i Gemara} identifies this as a case of {\i me\u8217'ukav get shichrur} that eats {\i terumah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Status Comparison\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} immediately rejects the comparison, however: {\i hachi ata?} \u8212- is that really a parallel? {\i Hatam}, there, if {\i Eliyahu} would come and identify which child is the slave, we would say {\i kinyan kaspo karinan bei} \u8212- we still call him property in the full sense, because in reality one of them fully is. There is simply an unresolved {\i safek}. The reality beneath the uncertainty is intact. {\i Hacha}, here, by contrast, the {\i me\u8217'ukav get shichrur} is {\i lav kinyan kaspo hu kelal} \u8212- not owned as property at all. There is no underlying reality of ownership; only the procedural barrier of the missing {\i shtar shichrur} remains. The phrase {\i lav kinyan kaspo hu kelal} suggests the logic would point toward this person {\i not} eating {\i terumah} if the {\i pasuk} requires genuine property status. Yet the {\i Gemara} leaves the question unresolved.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Selling for Knas\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Can Sell for Knas?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now poses a fresh {\i iba\u8217'ya lehu}: can a master sell his slave specifically for the purpose of the buyer collecting future {\i knas}? Normally, when a slave is gored and killed, the owner collects the {\i knas}. The question is whether the owner can in effect sell off that future entitlement \u8212- you pay me something now, and if he is ever gored, you collect the {\i knas}, not me. This is analogous to a case in {\i Bava Metzia} regarding an object that is burgled: can the owner sell to a third party the right to collect {\i kefel}, the double payment, if the object is stolen? Or must the collector of {\i kefel} actually be the owner? Here the question is whether one can carve off this particular financial right and transfer it separately.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Meir Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} frames the question as one that may be asked both according to Rabbi Meir and according to the {\i Rabbanan}, with different force in each direction. Even for Rabbi Meir, who holds that a person may sell {\i davar shelo ba le\u8217'olam} \u8212- something that does not yet exist in the world \u8212- one might argue that the {\i knas} cannot be sold. When Rabbi Meir permits selling future items, the paradigm case is buying the future dates of a palm tree: the fruit will naturally come into being, it is a predictable natural outcome. {\i Aval hacha mi yemar demingach} \u8212- but here, who says the slave will even be gored? It is not a natural eventuality arising from the slave himself. And further: {\i mi yemar demishalem} \u8212- even if the slave is gored, who says the tortfeasor will actually pay? If the person who was negligent admits to it, he is exempt from {\i knas} \u8212- {\i modeh umiptar}. The potential liability is therefore doubly speculative: the goring may never happen, and even if it does, admission forecloses payment.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbanan Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Conversely, even for the {\i Rabbanan}, who generally hold that one cannot sell {\i davar shelo ba le\u8217'olam}, there may be grounds to permit this transaction. {\i Ad kan lo ka\u8217'amri Rabbanan hatam ein adam makneh davar shelo ba la\u8217'olam} \u8212- when did they say you cannot sell something that does not yet exist? In a case like {\i peires dekla} \u8212- selling the fruit of the palm tree \u8212- the fruit does not yet physically exist and one is attempting to transfer the fruit itself. {\i Aval hacha, ha kai shor ha kai eved} \u8212- here, however, the slave is present and the ox is present. As {\i Tosafot} explains, this is structurally different: everyone agrees that I can sell you ownership in the tree for the purpose of its fruit. I am giving you something concrete now \u8212- a present ownership \u8212- even though the fruit has not yet grown. So too here: perhaps I am not selling you a future right; I am selling you the slave himself for this purpose, and that constitutes a present transaction in a concrete object. Perhaps that works even for the {\i Rabbanan}. On the other hand, {\i knas} for goring is not a natural outgrowth of owning a slave the way fruit is a natural outgrowth of owning a tree. The {\i knas} arises from a tortious event inflicted by an outside party; it is a peculiar and unnatural basis for an ownership transfer. The question remains unresolved on both sides.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Zero Value Evidence\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Abba then introduces a {\i ta shema} from an exegetical discussion of the {\i pasuk} about {\i terumah}, citing the phrase {\i v\u8217'yelid beiso hem yochlu belachmo}. The {\i Gemara} asks: why does the {\i pasuk} need to mention both {\i kinyan kesef} and {\i yelid bayis} \u8212- a slave purchased with money and one born into the household? If one who is purchased may eat {\i terumah}, surely one born into the house may eat all the more so. The answer is that if we had only the phrase {\i kinyan kesef}, we might have inferred: only a slave who currently has monetary value \u8212- {\i shaveh kesef} \u8212- may eat {\i terumah}, since the {\i pasuk} emphasizes the monetary dimension of the acquisition. The {\i yelid bayis} verse teaches that even a slave born into the household may eat regardless of whether he has present monetary value. But then one might argue the reverse: a {\i yelid bayis} eats {\i terumah} whether or not he has value, but a {\i kinyan kesef} \u8212- a purchased slave \u8212- perhaps requires present monetary value, and if after purchase he becomes worthless due to illness or disability, he may no longer eat {\i terumah}. Therefore the {\i pasuk} places {\i kinyan kaspo} and {\i yelid beiso} side by side to teach: just as one born into the house eats {\i terumah} even with no monetary value, so too one purchased eats {\i terumah} even with no monetary value \u8212- {\i af al pi she\u8217'eino shaveh klum ochel}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The point for our purposes is that there exists a slave with zero monetary value who nonetheless eats {\i terumah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Zero Value Objection\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Now the {\i Gemara} asks: {\i v\u8217'i salka daiteich eved she\u8217'machro rabbo l\u8217'knas machur} \u8212- if one could sell a slave solely for the purpose of {\i knas}, then how could any slave ever have zero monetary value? A slave with no value \u8212- one whose upkeep exceeds his productivity, or one whom no one wishes to take into their household \u8212- could always be sold for at least a {\i perutah}, because someone would be willing to pay a small amount on the chance of eventually collecting thirty {\i shekalim} in {\i knas}. The logic resembles a lottery: the purchaser takes possession not in the ordinary sense but only for the {\i knas}, and pays a {\i perutah} for the possibility of collecting a substantial sum. Since someone would always take that wager, no slave could ever be worth less than a {\i perutah}, and the {\i Gemara\u8217's} premise of a slave with zero monetary value would be impossible.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This generates a productive discussion. One might ask: why would the owner sell for a {\i perutah} if the potential upside is entirely his? The answer is that the owner, doing the expected-value calculation, may conclude the risk is too small to bother holding \u8212- while a poorer party may rationally pay a {\i perutah} for the lottery-like chance at thirty {\i shekalim}. Conversely, if the arrangement allows the buyer to take the {\i knas} risk without actually taking the slave into his household \u8212- leaving all the upkeep obligations with the original owner \u8212- then the buyer has essentially pure upside, and the owner might prefer that arrangement. The {\i Gemara\u8217's} logic therefore stands: if such a sale is valid, the slave would always have at least minimal market value, and the case of {\i eino shaveh klum} would be impossible.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Unfit Slave Exception\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} responds: {\i in, ika avda treifa} \u8212- yes, there is a slave who is a {\i treifa}, one who has received an internal fatal injury. A {\i treifa} is halachically not considered fully {\i chai}, and accordingly, the {\i knas} for killing a {\i treifa} slave would not apply. Such a slave could not be sold for even a {\i perutah} on the basis of {\i knas}, since no {\i knas} would ever be owed. But the {\i Gemara} presses further: even a {\i treifa} slave can stand at your side and serve you \u8212- {\i chazi lemeikom kamei} \u8212- so how can any slave have zero value? The {\i Gemara} concludes with the limiting case: {\i bimnuval umukeh shechin} \u8212- a slave who is both a {\i treifa} and physically repulsive, afflicted with a skin disease so severe that no one wishes to be in his presence. In such a case he truly has zero value, no {\i knas} can ever be collected for him, and the {\i Gemara\u8217's} premise holds.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Kiddushin Half-Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Half-Slave Man Marriage\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} returns to the half-slave, half-free status \u8212- {\i mi shechetzyo eved v\u8217'chetzyo ben chorin} \u8212- presenting what is perhaps the most fascinating cluster of questions. Can such a man validly perform {\i kiddushin} with a free woman? The {\i eved} half of him is legally incapable of marrying a free woman, but is the marriage binding to the extent of his free half?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Half Woman Marriage\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Before resolving the question about the half-slave man, the {\i Gemara} introduces two analogous cases involving free persons. First: {\i ben Yisrael she\u8217'amar l\u8217'vas chorin hitkadshi bechetzi} \u8212- a fully free man who says to a free woman, \u8220"be married to half of me.\u8221" The {\i Gemara} indicates this is {\i mekudeshes} \u8212- it works. The reason is that {\i kiddushin} restricts the woman, not the man. In a halachic framework that permits polygamy, marriage creates exclusivity for the woman but not for the man, and so the man being \u8220"half married\u8221" is coherent: his other half could theoretically be married to another woman. The woman is fully restricted by the relationship; the man speaking in halves is merely describing a social reality, not performing a defective act.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 By contrast, if a man says to a free woman \u8220"half of you is married to me,\u8221" that does not work \u8212- {\i einah mekudeshes} \u8212- because for the woman, marriage is fully exclusive. The half of her that is not married to this man could not be married to another man; therefore the category of \u8220"half a woman married\u8221" is incoherent.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Now the question is pressed in the more complex case. {\i Im timtzei lomar} \u8212- if the rule is that a free man marrying half a woman does not work because {\i shiyyer b\u8217'kinyano} \u8212- he held back, he performed only a half-act \u8212- perhaps the half-slave man who says to a free woman \u8220"I marry you\u8221" presents a different case. He did not hold anything back \u8212- {\i lo shiyyer b\u8217'kinyano}. He performed a full act; it simply could only take half-effect, because half of him is a slave. Does that make a difference?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 On the other hand, one might argue: {\i dechazi lekulei} \u8212- in the case of the free man saying \u8220"half of me,\u8221" the woman is in fact fully married to him, since his whole status is that of a free person who could potentially marry her entirely. The \u8220"half\u8221" is a manner of speaking. {\i Ha lo chazi lekulei} \u8212- here, by contrast, half of this man is fundamentally incapable of being married to her. Perhaps one cannot conceive of her being married to the free portion of him, {\i shebo}, and therefore the {\i kiddushin} is not binding.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Full vs.\u160 ?Half Act\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The distinction the {\i Gemara} is working toward is between two types of partial {\i kiddushin}. In the case where a man says \u8220"half of you is married to me,\u8221" the act itself is defective \u8212- he performed a half-act, he held back. In the case of the half-slave man marrying a free woman, the act is complete and unqualified; the limitation is not in the act but in his status. Similarly, when a man says \u8220"half of me is married to you,\u8221" he may be performing a full act that takes full effect, with the \u8220"half\u8221" being descriptive rather than constitutive of the act. These structural differences motivate the subsequent rulings regarding the half-slave woman.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Half-Woman Evidence\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The discussion of the half-slave woman proceeds as follows. \u1512 ?\u1489 ?\u1488 ? {\i Rava} states: {\i kesheim she-hamkadesh chatzi isha einah mekudeshes \u8212- kach chatzi shifcha v\u8217'chatzi bas chorin she-nitkadesha ein kiddusheha kiddushin}. Just as marrying half a woman produces no valid {\i kiddushin}, so too the {\i kiddushin} of a half-slave woman is not binding. Rabba bar Rav Huna subsequently delivers the same ruling publicly. Rav Chisda challenges him: {\i mi dami?} How can you compare? {\i Hasam shiyyer b\u8217'kinyano} \u8212- there, by marrying half a woman, the man held back and did not do a full act. {\i Hacha lo shiyyer b\u8217'kinyano} \u8212- here, in marrying the half-slave woman, the man held nothing back; he married her fully, as fully as he was able to. Rav Chisda implies this distinction should lead to the {\i kiddushin} being valid.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabba bar Rav Huna thereupon reversed his position entirely. He convened a public lecture and acknowledged the correction, prefacing his revised ruling with the famous dictum: {\i ein adam omed al divrei Torah ela im kein nichshal bahen} \u8212- a person does not truly come to stand on his Torah unless he has first stumbled in it. Having publicly erred and retracted, the lesson is genuinely internalized.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 He then ruled: even though a free man marrying half a woman produces no {\i kiddushin}, by a half-slave half-free woman the {\i kiddushin} does take effect. {\i Hasam shiyyer b\u8217'kinyano, hacha lo shiyyer b\u8217'kinyano} \u8212- there he held back and performed a half-act; here he performed a full act that took half-effect. Since the act was complete and unqualified, it takes effect to whatever degree it can, and the half of her that is free is {\i mekudeshes}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Sheshet, however, did not accept this revision and maintained his original position: just as marrying half a woman produces no {\i kiddushin}, so too the half-slave half-free woman\u8217's {\i kiddushin} is not binding.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Half-Woman Evidence\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 In response to Rav Sheshet\u8217's position, an interlocutor raises the case of {\i shifcha charufa} from the {\i Torah}: the passage describes a woman who is half-slave, half-free, designated to an {\i eved Ivri}, and refers to her as {\i me\u8217'ureset l\u8217'eved Ivri} \u8212- betrothed to a Hebrew slave. Does this not demonstrate that {\i kiddushin} is binding with a half-slave woman?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Yishmael View\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Sheshet replies: go and see what Rabbi Yishmael says. Rabbi Yishmael holds that the {\i Torah\u8217's} case of {\i shifcha charufa} is not about a half-slave woman at all \u8212- it is about a fully enslaved {\i shifcha Kena\u8217'anit}, one hundred percent a slave. And yet the {\i Torah} uses the term {\i me\u8217'ureset l\u8217'eved Ivri}. Now, a fully enslaved Canaanite woman is {\i not bat ishut} \u8212- she is legally incapable of {\i kiddushin} \u8212- so what does {\i me\u8217'ureset} mean in that context? {\i Meyushedet} \u8212- she has been designated to him, she is living with him in a recognized domestic arrangement, but there is no legally binding {\i kiddushin}. The master has assigned her to the {\i eved Ivri} to cohabit and produce slave-children; the word {\i me\u8217'ureset} denotes designation and cohabitation, not formal marriage.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Therefore, Rav Sheshet argues, the {\i Torah\u8217's} use of {\i me\u8217'ureset} proves nothing about whether {\i kiddushin} is valid for a half-slave woman. Even if the {\i Torah\u8217's} case involves a half-slave woman, the term means only {\i meyushedet} \u8212- designated \u8212- not {\i mekudeshes} in the legally binding sense.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Heirs Contradiction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} had earlier cited a ruling about an ox that kills a {\i mi shechetzyo eved v\u8217'chetzyo ben chorin}: the payment is split \u8212- half {\i knas} to the master and half {\i kofer} to {\i yorshav}, his heirs. The difficulty is immediately apparent: {\i v\u8217'i amart kiddushav lav kiddushin \u8212- yorshin mena lei?} If the {\i kiddushin} of a half-slave is invalid, how does he have heirs?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Adda bar Ahava proposes: {\i keshe\u8217'asa\u8217'o treifah} \u8212- the ox did not actually kill him; it merely rendered him a {\i treifah}, delivered a fatal internal injury but did not cause immediate death. Since he is still alive \u8212- Monty Python\u8217's \u8220"I\u8217'm not dead yet\u8221" \u8212- he collects himself; {\i nafshei}, he is his own heir. {\i Rava} objects with two responses: first, the text says {\i yorshav} \u8212- his heirs \u8212- not him; second, {\i kofer} by definition is only paid after death \u8212- {\i kofer ein mishtalem ela le\u8217'acher mitah}, as Reish Lakish rules \u8212- it is an atonement payment for a death, not for a wound, even a fatal one.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rava} therefore offers an alternative: {\i ra\u8217'uy liten v\u8217'ein lo} \u8212- those heirs should receive the {\i kofer} payment, but since in fact there are no heirs, that half of the {\i kofer} goes uncollected. No one receives it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} notes a subtle point: why does the {\i Gemara} frame the question of heirs as dependent on {\i kiddushin} at all? Even illegitimate children \u8212- even a {\i mamzer} \u8212- are their father\u8217's heirs. One does not need {\i kiddushin} to have heirs. {\i Tosafot} explains that the issue is not legitimacy but the recognition of family structure. The principle {\i eved ein lo chiyus} \u8212- a slave has no legally recognized family \u8212- means that a slave\u8217's biological children are not halachically recognized as his children. It is analogous to the secular legal concept of a bastard child: biology alone was insufficient to establish legal parenthood; formal recognition was required. In {\i halacha}, a child born out of wedlock is legally recognized, but a slave\u8217's children are not. This applies even if the slave has children with a free woman \u8212- the {\i halacha} refuses to recognize the family structure of a slave, and the children are legally his master\u8217's slaves, not his heirs.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} therefore argues: the relevance of {\i kiddushin} here is that valid {\i kiddushin} is an affirmation that family structure is recognized for this person. If we say that the half-slave man is capable of {\i kiddushin}, we are simultaneously affirming that his family structure is legally cognizable \u8212- and therefore that children resulting from such a union would be legally recognized as his heirs. The question of heirs is thus not a question of legitimacy but of status-recognition.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Kiddushin After Freedom\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Scenario Setup\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Chisda now introduces the further scenario: {\i chatziya shifcha v\u8217'chatziya bas chorin she-nitkadsha l\u8217'Reuven} \u8212- a half-slave half-free woman who was married to Reuven, and then {\i v\u8217'nishtachrerah} \u8212- the remaining half of her was freed \u8212- and {\i v\u8217'chazra v\u8217'nitkadsha l\u8217'Shimon} \u8212- she then went ahead and performed {\i kiddushin} with Shimon. She is now somehow half-married to Reuven and half-married to Shimon. Neither man could fully consummate the marriage while the other remained. Then both Reuven and Shimon died, and they happened to be brothers. Now Levi, their brother, has an obligation of {\i yibum}. And Rav Chisda says: {\i v\u8217'eini korei bah eishes shnei meisim} \u8212- I do not read about her as \u8220"the wife of two dead men,\u8221" which would have been an obstacle to {\i yibum}. The {\i Gemara} in {\i Yevamos} establishes that a widow who comes to a surviving brother from two dead brothers \u8212- because one did {\i maamar} but not full {\i yibum}, leaving his connection to her overlapping with the first brother\u8217's \u8212- cannot perform {\i yibum} with the third brother. Yet here, even though she was seemingly half-married to two men, Levi can do {\i yibum}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Nullify or Complete?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The reason is that this situation cannot be characterized as her being half-married to both. When she was freed, one of two things necessarily happened to the original {\i kiddushin} with Reuven: either it expanded and completed, making her fully Reuven\u8217's wife, in which case the subsequent {\i kiddushin} with Shimon was entirely ineffective \u8212- or it dissolved entirely, making her fully available for Shimon\u8217's {\i kiddushin}, in which case the connection to Reuven is wholly gone. She is therefore either entirely Reuven\u8217's wife or entirely Shimon\u8217's wife. {\i Iy kiddushei d\u8217'Reuven kiddushei \u8212- kiddushei d\u8217'Shimon lav kiddushei; v\u8217'kiddushei d\u8217'Shimon kiddushei \u8212- kiddushei d\u8217'Reuven lav kiddushei.} She cannot be simultaneously half-married to each. And therefore she does not come to Levi as {\i eishes shnei meisim}. The question of which branch obtained remains unresolved \u8212- but either way, {\i yibum} with Levi is permissible.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kiddushin Bursts\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The debate is now stated explicitly: {\i chatzi shifcha v\u8217'chatzi bas chorin sheniskadsha l\u8217'Reuven v\u8217'nishtachrerah v\u8217'chazra v\u8217'nitkadsha l\u8217'Shimon} \u8212- what is the law? Rav Yosef bar Chama in the name of Rav Nachman: {\i paku kiddushei rishon} \u8212- the original {\i kiddushin} burst. When she was freed, her legal identity changed so fundamentally that the half-{\i kiddushin} she had with Reuven dissolved. She became a new legal person, and Reuven\u8217's {\i kiddushin} evaporated. She is therefore fully available for Shimon\u8217's {\i kiddushin}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kiddushin Completes\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Zeira in the name of Rav Nachman: {\i gamru kiddushei rishon} \u8212- the original {\i kiddushin} was completed. Once the impediment of her partial slavery was removed, there was nothing holding back the {\i kiddushin} from taking full effect. The half-{\i kiddushin} expanded to cover her entirely, and she is fully Reuven\u8217's wife. Shimon\u8217's subsequent {\i kiddushin} was therefore meaningless.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shifcha Charufa Text\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Zeira adds: {\i kavasi didi mistabra} \u8212- it makes sense like my position. The proof is from the {\i pasuk} of {\i shifcha charufa}: {\i lo yumsu ki lo chufasha} \u8212- they will not be put to death because she has not yet been freed. The {\i Gemara} reads this as a case of a half-slave half-free woman who is half-married to a man; another man sleeps with her and the {\i Torah} says it is not a capital crime because it is not fully adultery \u8212- she is only half-married, because she has not yet been freed. {\i Ha chufasha \u8212- yumsu}: had she been freed, they would be put to death, meaning had that other half of her been freed, the original half-{\i kiddushin} would have become full {\i kiddushin}, and adultery with her would have been a capital crime. This supports the view of {\i gamru kiddushei rishon}: freedom causes the original {\i kiddushin} to complete itself.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Abaye Challenge\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye challenges Rav Zeira: {\i u\u8217'letana d\u8217'vei Rabbi Yishmael d\u8217'amar b\u8217'shifcha Kena\u8217'anit hame\u8217'ureset l\u8217'eved Ivri} \u8212- what does this {\i pasuk} say according to Rabbi Yishmael? Rabbi Yishmael holds that the woman in the {\i pasuk} is a fully enslaved {\i shifcha Kena\u8217'anit} with no valid {\i kiddushin} at all, only {\i eirusin} in the sense of designation and cohabitation. According to Rabbi Yishmael, then, how does the {\i pasuk} support {\i gamru kiddushei rishon}? There was no {\i kiddushin} to complete! Rav Zeira\u8217's reading of the {\i pasuk} collapses.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye explains how Rabbi Yishmael reads the {\i pasuk}: {\i she\u8217'chufasha v\u8217'chazra v\u8217'nitkadsha} \u8212- had she been freed and then performed a new {\i kiddushin}, and then someone committed adultery with her, only then would it be a capital crime. The {\i pasuk} does not say that freedom automatically completes an existing {\i kiddushin}; it says that freedom followed by a new {\i kiddushin} would create a fully binding marriage. Therefore the {\i pasuk} cannot serve as evidence for {\i gamru kiddushei rishon} \u8212- it is equally compatible with {\i paku kiddushei rishon}, where freedom dissolves the original {\i kiddushin} and necessitates a fresh one. The question is left unresolved.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Historical Precedent\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Forced Freedom\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Huna bar Chinena in the name of Rav Yitzchak reports: {\i maaseh b\u8217'shifcha she\u8217'chetziyah shifcha v\u8217'chetziyah bas chorin} \u8212- there was an actual case of a half-slave half-free woman, and {\i v\u8217'chafu es rabbo v\u8217'asah bas chorin} \u8212- the rabbinic authorities compelled her master to free her entirely.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Childbearing Motive\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This raises a question. Throughout our {\i sugya}, the case compelling the freeing of a half-slave {\i man} was justified by his inability to fulfill the {\i mitzvah} of {\i p\u8217'ru u\u8217'rvu} while trapped in this status. But does that reasoning apply to a woman? The {\i Gemara} asks: is this ruling in accordance with Rabbi Yochanan ben Broka, {\i d\u8217'amar al shneichem hu omer v\u8217'yevarech osam Elokim vayomer pru u\u8217'rvu} \u8212- who derives from the verse that the obligation of {\i p\u8217'ru u\u8217'rvu} applies to women as well? Perhaps the reason the rabbis forced the master to free this woman was specifically to allow her to fulfill that obligation by marrying and bearing children.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Protection Motive\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Nachman bar Yitzchak, however, offers an entirely different explanation: {\i lo, minhag hefker nahagu bah} \u8212- that was not the reason at all. The actual motivation was that people were taking advantage of her. Because she was neither fully a slave nor fully free, her status was anomalous in both directions: she may not have been fully designated to a slave as a {\i shifcha} might be, and she could not be fully protected through marriage as a free woman could be. She had no man \u8212- whether master or husband \u8212- to protect her, and she was therefore being exploited. The rabbis freed her not to enable {\i p\u8217'ru u\u8217'rvu} but to protect her from that exploitation. The compelled manumission had nothing to do with the positive obligation of bearing children.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Gittin 44\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Slaves: Status & Property\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tikun olam & Slaves\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The daf we take up today continues the {\i Gemara}\u8217's sustained engagement with the laws governing slaves, which has emerged as a central preoccupation of the {\i tikun olam} enactments of {\i Gittin}. What makes this discussion particularly striking is the explicit and recurring comparison between the slave and the woman \u8212- a comparison that is not merely implicit but structurally built into the halacha itself. The {\i get shichrur}, the writ of manumission that frees a slave, is derived through a {\i gzera shava} of {\i la la} from the {\i get} that divorces a woman, and the level of the slave\u8217's obligation in {\i mitzvot} is likewise derived from this same verbal analogy.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 At first glance, this parallel might seem to cast the woman in an unflattering light \u8212- as though she, like the slave, is a form of property being released. But the {\i Gemara} repeatedly uses the comparison to draw a careful contrast, and it is that contrast which animates much of this {\i sugya}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Issura & Mamona\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The key analytical distinction is this: by a woman, there is only {\i issura} \u8212- a dimension of personal status. The laws governing her relationship to her husband are entirely about who she is and to whom she belongs in terms of personal, halachic standing. By the slave, however, there are two dimensions operating simultaneously: {\i issura}, personal status, and {\i mamona}, property ownership. The slave is both a person with a halachic identity and an item of property with a monetary value that can be bought, sold, pledged, and inherited.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A great deal of the {\i sugyot} in this chapter have explored precisely what happens when one of these two dimensions is altered without the other \u8212- when a master, for example, renounces monetary ownership ({\i mafkir}) of the slave without yet executing a formal act of manumission. The {\i Gemara} is consistent: changing the {\i mamona} dimension alone does not change the {\i issura}. The status change requires its own act, its own writ. This dual structure is what makes the slave\u8217's situation legally unique, and it is what makes the {\i tikun olam} concerns here far more complex than anything we encounter in the laws of divorce.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Sale to Non-Jews\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishna: Slave Freed\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} at the opening of our discussion states: {\i ha-mokher avdo le-ovdei kokhavim o le-chutz la-aretz yatza ben chorin} \u8212- if someone sells his slave to a non-Jew or outside the Land of Israel, the slave goes free. The language sounds automatic: {\i yatza ben chorin}, he goes free, as though the sale itself operates the manumission. We will see that the {\i baraita} qualifies this considerably, but the broad principle is established here.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rationale: Mitzvot\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The underlying rationale is that a slave is obligated in {\i mitzvot} to a degree comparable to that of a woman \u8212- again, the {\i la la gzera shava} at work. He is obligated in all negative commandments and in all positive commandments that are not time-bound. This partial but real level of {\i kedusha yisrael}, as it were, means that placing him under the dominion of a non-Jew creates a serious and ongoing violation. The non-Jew will compel him to work on {\i Shabbat} and {\i Yom Tov}, will feed him food that is not {\i kosher}, and will in every practical sense prevent him from observing the {\i mitzvot} to which he is obligated.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The case of selling to {\i chutz la-aretz} is a lesser concern \u8212- the slave does not lose his obligation in all {\i mitzvot}, only in those that apply specifically in {\i Eretz Yisrael} \u8212- but it remains a genuine halachic problem. Notably, the comparison to the woman surfaces again here: there is an extensive halachic discussion about whether a husband can compel his wife to move to {\i chutz la-aretz}, and the answer is that he generally cannot force her to give up life in {\i Eretz Yisrael}. The parallel, though imperfect, reflects a shared underlying concern about the religious consequences of displacement.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Jewish Presence\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 There is an additional dimension to the prohibition that goes beyond the individual slave\u8217's ability to observe {\i mitzvot}. {\i Chazal} were deeply invested in maintaining Jewish presence in the Land of Israel and issued broad {\i takanot} designed to prevent Jews living in Israel from transferring land, slaves, and cattle to non-Jews. All of these \u8212- land, animals, slaves \u8212- were understood as elements of an established, rooted presence in the land, and their transfer to non-Jewish ownership was seen as erosion of that presence. This communal concern does not displace the {\i mitzvot} rationale, which remains primary and most acute in the case of sale to a non-Jew, but it operates alongside it, especially in the case of sale to {\i chutz la-aretz}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Baraita: Writ Needed\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Get Shichrur\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i baraita} immediately clarifies that the {\i mishna}\u8217's language of {\i yatza ben chorin} is not as automatic as it sounds: {\i ha-mokher avdo le-oved kokhavim yatza le-cheirus v-tzarich get shichrur me-rabo rishon} \u8212- he has gone out to freedom, but he requires a writ of manumission from his first master. The first master is the one who must act, since the non-Jew who now holds him cannot effect a change in the slave\u8217's halachic personal status. A non-Jew cannot write a {\i get shichrur} that alters the slave\u8217's {\i issura} dimension.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabban Shimon\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel} adds a nuance: {\i bameh devarim amurim ke-shelo katav alav ono} \u8212- when is this requirement for a formal writ applicable? When no document has already been written. {\i Aval katav alav ono zehu shichruro} \u8212- if a document has been written for him, that document itself constitutes his freedom. The question then becomes: what exactly is this {\i ono}, this document?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Form of Writ\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rav Sheshet} explains its content: the master writes to the slave before the sale the following language \u8212- {\i likshe-tivrach mimeni ein li esek bach} \u8212- if you ever manage to escape from the one to whom I am selling you, I make no claim upon you. Formally, this language sounds more like {\i hefker} \u8212- a renunciation of ownership \u8212- than like a declaration of {\i shichrur}, a change of personal status. And yet, when it is committed to writing and delivered to the slave as a writ, it can function as his {\i shtar shichrur}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} raises the obvious difficulty: the writ cannot take effect at the moment of writing, because if the slave were freed at that moment, the subsequent sale to the non-Jew would be invalid \u8212- you cannot sell a free man. The document must therefore be conditional, taking effect only when the slave escapes. The phrase {\i likshe-tivrach mimeni} is exactly this: the freedom is triggered by the condition of escape.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hefker vs Shichrur\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This brings us to a conceptually important distinction. {\i Hefker} and {\i shichrur} are not the same thing, even though both involve the master relinquishing his hold over the slave. {\i Hefker} addresses only the {\i mamona} dimension \u8212- the master renounces monetary ownership. But the {\i issura} dimension, the slave\u8217's personal status, is not altered by {\i hefker} alone. {\i Shichrur} \u8212- effected through the formal act of delivering a {\i shtar} \u8212- addresses the status dimension directly. What {\i Rav Sheshet} is telling us is that the language of the document need not explicitly say {\i harei atah ben chorin}; it can articulate the {\i mamona} dimension in the form of a renunciation, but because it is being delivered through the ritual act of {\i shtar} delivery, it effects the change of status as well. It is the formal act, the giving of the {\i shtar}, that carries the halachic weight, not merely the content of the declaration.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Tosafot: Labor Only\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Non-Jew Labor Only\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} addresses a deeper structural problem: how can the first master\u8217's writ take effect at all once the slave is under the control of the non-Jew and entirely outside the master\u8217's reach? The answer is grounded in an earlier principle established in the {\i Gemara}: {\i lo hem konin mikem} \u8212- non-Jews cannot acquire slaves from Jews in the full sense of ownership. More precisely, a non-Jew can only acquire {\i maasei yadayim}, the labor and work product of the slave, but cannot acquire the slave\u8217's {\i guf}, his body and person, as property.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Master Owns Body\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The practical consequence of this principle is far-reaching. Because the non-Jew acquires only the slave\u8217's labor and not his person, the original Jewish master retains full ownership of the slave as property \u8212- ownership of his {\i guf}. And since he retains that ownership, he retains the standing and the capacity to free the slave. The sale to the non-Jew transfers only one layer of the slave\u8217's dual status: the {\i mamona} dimension of his labor is transferred, but the {\i mamona} dimension of his body \u8212- and with it the {\i issura} dimension entirely \u8212- remains with the original master. It is precisely this retained ownership that allows the first master to write a {\i get shichrur} that has legal effect even after the sale has been completed.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Loan & Collateral\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Nimuso Definition\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now moves to a related but distinct scenario governed by a new {\i baraita}: {\i laveh alav min ha-oved kokhavim} \u8212- you borrowed money from a non-Jew with your slave as collateral. The keyword here is {\i alav}: you borrowed specifically {\i against} the slave, designating him explicitly as the surety. This is crucially different from simply borrowing money and having the non-Jew later choose to collect against your slave. When you designate the slave as the collateral, you have in effect chosen to put him in harm\u8217's way, and the {\i Gemara} treats that as analogous to a sale.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The question the {\i baraita} poses is: at what point does the non-Jew\u8217's relationship with the slave rise to the level that triggers the obligation to free him? The {\i baraita}\u8217's answer is: {\i keivan she-asah lo oved kokhavim nimuso} \u8212- once the non-Jew does for him his {\i nimuso}. The term {\i nomos}, from which {\i nimuso} derives, means law or custom, but what specific act or event does it describe?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Nimuso = Seal\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rav Huna bar Yehuda} proposes that {\i nimuso} refers to a {\i nashki} \u8212- some kind of seal, mark, or perhaps even a physical brand that the non-Jew places on the slave to indicate ownership. Once the non-Jew has marked the slave as his, the slave is considered effectively in his possession, and the obligation to free him is triggered.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sheshet\u8217's Objection\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rav Sheshet} objects forcefully: the same word {\i nimuso} appears in the context of a field taken as collateral \u8212- {\i oved kokhavim she-mishkan sadeh le-Yisrael} \u8212- where a non-Jew has pledged his field to a Jew as surety for a loan. In that case as well, the {\i baraita} speaks of the {\i nimuso} occurring. But you do not place a seal on a field. A field cannot be branded or marked the way a slave can. Therefore, {\i nimuso} cannot mean a physical seal or mark; it must refer to something else that applies equally to slaves and to fields.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Nimuso = Time\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rav Sheshet} therefore redefines {\i nimuso} as {\i zman} \u8212- the time for repayment has arrived. When the loan comes due and the borrower has not repaid, the non-Jew is now legally positioned to take possession of the collateral. In the slave\u8217's case, this means the non-Jew can now claim the slave, and at that moment the obligation to free the slave is triggered. In the field case, the parallel language in the {\i baraita} refers to the same {\i zman} arriving \u8212- the time for collection \u8212- which is why the same term is used for both.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Body vs Field\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Difficulty Posed\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This resolution, however, generates a new difficulty. If {\i nimuso} means the {\i zman} has arrived in both cases, why does the arrival of the {\i zman} trigger the obligation to free the slave but not create an obligation regarding the field? The {\i Gemara} formulates this as {\i kashya zman a-zman}: the same moment in time is being described, yet the legal consequences differ. The {\i Gemara} proposes that perhaps in the slave case we are speaking of a moment when the {\i zman} has arrived, while in the field case the {\i zman} has not yet arrived \u8212- but this reading forces the same word to describe opposite temporal states in the same {\i baraita}, which is unsatisfying, and the {\i Gemara} rejects it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafot: Slave In House\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} offers the more coherent resolution. In both cases, the {\i zman} has not yet arrived \u8212- meaning the loan is not yet due. But the two situations differ structurally. When a slave is designated as collateral, the non-Jew typically takes physical possession of the slave before the loan comes due. The slave is already in the non-Jew\u8217's house, already under his daily control, already being forced to work on {\i Shabbat} and eat {\i treif} and violate {\i halacha} in every practical dimension of his life. The {\i guf} of the slave \u8212- his body, his person \u8212- has already been taken into the non-Jew\u8217's possession, even though the {\i zman} for formal collection has not yet arrived.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Field: Fruit Only\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 By contrast, a field never moves. When a Jew holds a non-Jew\u8217's field as collateral, the Jew may be present on the field, farming it, enjoying its produce \u8212- its {\i peira} \u8212- but the {\i karka} itself, the land, remains exactly where it is. We say the Jew has possession only of the {\i peira}, the fruit, not of the {\i guf}, the body, of the field. Since the field itself has not been transferred into anyone\u8217's physical possession, there is no parallel to the slave situation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Conclusion\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The operative distinction, then, is whether the {\i guf} has been physically transferred into the non-Jew\u8217's domain. Once the slave is living in the non-Jew\u8217's house and being subjected daily to violations of {\i halacha}, the obligation to free him is triggered \u8212- regardless of whether the formal {\i zman} of the loan has arrived. The ongoing nature of the violation, the daily compelled breach of {\i mitzvot}, is what creates the urgency and triggers the {\i takana}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Seizure vs Forced Sale\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Debt Seizure\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A further {\i baraita} introduces two new scenarios that test the boundaries of when the master\u8217's choice is implicated. The first: {\i gevaho be-chovo} \u8212- if the non-Jew seized the slave as payment of a general debt, without the master having specifically designated the slave as collateral. In this case, {\i lo yatza le-cheirut} \u8212- the slave does not go free, and the master is not required to redeem and free him. The non-Jew chose to collect against the slave on his own initiative; the master did not choose to expose the slave to non-Jewish ownership. The {\i Gemara} reinforces this by comparing it to the case of grain seized from a threshing floor for taxes: if the seizure was in payment of a legitimate debt ({\i bechovo}), the owner has effectively benefited because his debt was discharged, making it akin to a voluntary transaction. But in that case {\i chamishtershi lei} \u8212- the benefit flowed to the owner \u8212- whereas in the slave case, even if the debt was discharged, the master did not specifically choose to submit the slave to non-Jewish possession, so we do not impute that choice to him.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} also notes the case of {\i sikrikon} \u8212- a strongman who forces the sale \u8212- in which the slave likewise does not go free, because the master was coerced without any meaningful choice or opportunity for alternatives.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Forced Sale\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The second scenario: {\i parhang} \u8212- a term understood, following {\i Rashi}, as a kind of strongman or blackmailer, and according to {\i Tosafot} a Persian term for someone who forces a sale but does pay for the slave. If someone forces you to sell your slave to him, the {\i Gemara} rules that {\i yatza le-cheirut} \u8212- the slave does go free. This is at first glance surprising: you were forced, so why are you held responsible?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Responsibility\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} answers: {\i havah lei le-fayusei ve-lo piyes} \u8212- you had the option of appeasing the person, of offering him alternative property, of paying him off or negotiating your way out of the situation. The assumption built into the ruling is that you were not entirely without options, and your failure to pursue those options makes you, at least partially, the agent of the slave\u8217's transfer into non-Jewish hands. Because you could have prevented it and did not, you are treated as though you chose it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 No Liability\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The contrast is clear: where the seizure was entirely beyond the master\u8217's control \u8212- through sheer military force or the like, where no negotiation or substitution was possible \u8212- the master is not held liable, and the obligation to redeem and free the slave does not arise. The threshold is meaningful choice and meaningful opportunity to act otherwise. When that opportunity existed and was not used, the master bears responsibility.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Conditional Scenarios\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Thirty-Day Sale\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Yirmiyah} poses a series of penetrating questions about cases that fall at the edges of these categories. The first: {\i machro li-shloshim yom mahu} \u8212- what if you sold the slave to a non-Jew for only thirty days, with the understanding that the slave returns to you afterward? The slave will be compelled to violate {\i halacha} for thirty days, but it is not a permanent sale. Are you required, when the slave returns at the end of the thirty days, to then free him? The {\i Gemara} attempts to resolve this from the case of the {\i parhang}, reasoning that in that scenario the non-Jew presumably intends eventually to resell the slave. But the {\i Gemara} rejects this inference because the {\i parhang} case involves a sale with no anticipated return ({\i she-eino chozer}), which is not comparable to an explicitly time-limited transaction. The question therefore remains unresolved.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 No Work Clause\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A second question: {\i chutz mi-melachto mahu} \u8212- what if you sell the slave to a non-Jew on the explicit condition that the non-Jew does not put him to work? {\i Rashi} explains the realistic context: the non-Jew might want the slave not for labor but to have him breed with the non-Jew\u8217's own female slave, producing additional slaves. In such a case, the slave might not be compelled to violate {\i Shabbat} through labor. Does the condition change the master\u8217's obligation?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mitzvot Clause\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A third question: {\i chutz min ha-mitzvos mahu} \u8212- what if you sell the slave on the explicit condition that he is permitted to observe his religious obligations? Or more specifically, {\i chutz mi-Shabbasos ve-yamim tovim} \u8212- on the condition that he is given {\i Shabbat} and {\i Yom Tov} off? If all of this is written into the contract, does that now exempt the master from the obligation to redeem and free him?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mixed Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Yirmiyah} then extends the inquiry to questions of the buyer\u8217's identity: {\i le-ger toshav o le-Yisrael mumar mahu} \u8212- what if the sale is to a {\i ger toshav}, a non-Jew living in the Land of Israel who observes the {\i sheva mitzvos b-nei Noach}, or to a {\i Yisrael mumar}, a Jew who is formally Jewish but entirely non-observant? And {\i le-Kuti mahu} \u8212- what about a Samaritan, whose status as Jew or non-Jew is itself debated?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 These questions cut to the heart of a broader jurisprudential tension: do we apply formal categories \u8212- Jew versus non-Jew \u8212- regardless of the contextual reality of whether the slave will actually be able to observe {\i halacha}? Or do we take into account the practical circumstances? A {\i ger toshav} might in practice respect the slave\u8217's religious life far more than a non-observant Jew would. Does that practical reality count?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Status Determinations\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} partially resolves one of these questions: {\i ger toshav harei hu ke-oved kokhavim} \u8212- a {\i ger toshav} is treated like a non-Jew for our purposes. His observance of the Noachide laws does not give him the status of a Jew, and the formal rule applies. As for {\i Kuti ve-Yisrael mumar}, the {\i Gemara} records a dispute: {\i amri lei ke-ovdei kokhavim ve-amri lei ke-Yisrael} \u8212- some say they are treated like non-Jews, some say like Jews. That debate remains unresolved, as do the questions about the conditional sales and the clauses regarding {\i Shabbat} observance. The {\i Gemara} leaves {\i Rabbi Yirmiyah}\u8217's questions as {\i teiku} \u8212- unresolved \u8212- reflecting the genuine difficulty of adjudicating between formal status and contextual reality.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Accepting Compensation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Money Instead\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now takes up a practical scenario of a different kind. A non-Jew has taken your slave without your consent \u8212- through force or coercion \u8212- and while you cannot recover the slave himself, the non-Jew offers you money in compensation. May you accept it? On the surface, accepting money might seem tantamount to ratifying the sale and abandoning your obligation to redeem and free the slave. But if recovery is genuinely impossible, is it permitted to at least salvage the monetary value?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Investigation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Yirmiyah} instructs {\i Rabbi Zerika} to investigate the {\i tannaic} sources \u8212- his {\i michilta}, his collections of teachings \u8212- because {\i Rabbi Ami} has been asked this question and needs the relevant source material to render a ruling.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 House Analogy\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Zerika} finds the following {\i baraita}: if someone sells his house to a non-Jew \u8212- which is itself prohibited, both because of the biblical {\i lo techonem} prohibition against giving non-Jews a foothold in the land and because of the broader rabbinic {\i takanot} about maintaining Jewish presence in {\i Eretz Yisrael} \u8212- the proceeds of such a sale are forbidden as {\i issur hana\u8217'ah}. However, if a non-Jew forcibly seized the house and {\i ein ba-alav yakhol le-hotzio lo be-dinei Yisrael ve-lo be-dinei ovdei kokhavim} \u8212- the owner cannot recover it through any legal means, neither in Jewish courts nor in non-Jewish courts \u8212- then {\i mutar litol dameihah}, the owner may collect monetary compensation. Furthermore, {\i ve-chotev u-ma\u8217'aleh be-arka\u8217'ot shelahem}, he may even register the transaction in non-Jewish civil courts to ensure the money is legally enforceable \u8212- because {\i mipnei she-hu ke-matzil mi-yadam}, this is considered salvaging what remains from their hands, not voluntarily selling to them.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Slippery Slope\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} raises the question of whether this precedent from the house can be applied to the slave. Perhaps they are not comparable. With regard to a house, people do not typically sell their homes freely; the loss of a house is a severe and unusual circumstance, and therefore permitting the monetary collection after the fact will not create a {\i slippery slope} toward people engineering such situations in order to profit. But a slave is more expendable \u8212- {\i sagi lei be-lo eved} \u8212- people can live without a slave, and if we allow the owner to simply collect money and walk away, it might encourage masters to arrange such forced seizures deliberately.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Ami Rules\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Nevertheless, {\i Rabbi Ami} concludes that the house precedent is sufficiently analogous and issues a formal ruling \u8212- framed with the weight of a public proclamation, {\i mini Ami bar Natan Torah yotza le-khol Yisrael} \u8212- that from him the following teaching goes out to all of Israel: a slave who has fallen into the hands of soldiers ({\i geisot}), and whose master cannot recover him through any legal means, whether in Jewish courts or in non-Jewish courts \u8212- {\i mutar litol dameihav ve-chotev u-ma\u8217'aleh be-arka\u8217'ot shel ovdei kokhavim mipnei she-hu ke-matzil mi-yadam} \u8212- the master may collect the compensation and may register the arrangement in non-Jewish civil courts, because this is simply salvaging what can be salvaged. The formal prohibition on selling to non-Jews is not violated when the transaction is already a fait accompli that cannot be undone.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Redemption Penalty\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Price Multiple\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Yehoshua ben Levi} now introduces the mechanism by which the entire rabbinic system discourages the sale of slaves to non-Jews in the first place: {\i ha-mokher avdo le-ovdei kokhavim kohnsin oto ad me\u8217'ah be-dameihav} \u8212- if you sell your slave to a non-Jew, the court compels you to buy him back at up to one hundred times his value. The obligation to free the slave is not merely moral; it is backed by an extraordinary financial penalty designed to make the transgression so costly that no rational master would undertake it. Whether this penalty was ever successfully enforced in practice is a separate question, but as a statement of halachic principle it is remarkable.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Precise or Approximate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} immediately asks whether the figure of one hundred is {\i davka} \u8212- a precise upper limit \u8212- or merely an approximation, an expression of \u8220"a very great multiple.\u8221"\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Animal Comparison\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Reish Lakish} provides a parallel ruling: {\i ha-mokher behemah gasah le-ovdei kokhavim kohnsin oto ad asarah be-dameihah} \u8212- if you sell a large animal to a non-Jew, which is similarly prohibited (the {\i Gemara} in {\i Avodah Zarah} explains this is because animals can be worked on {\i Shabbat}, and there are also considerations of maintaining Jewish presence in the land), you are fined up to ten times the animal\u8217's value. Since the slave {\i baraita} says one hundred and the animal {\i baraita} says ten, these cannot both be approximations meaning the same thing. The numbers must be {\i davka}, and they differ.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Slave vs Animal\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} then wrestles with the direction of the reasoning. One version of {\i Rabbi Yehoshua ben Levi}\u8217's statement has the slave at ten times and the animal at one hundred times \u8212- the mirror image of the other version. The {\i Gemara} considers whether the slave\u8217's penalty might be lower because the slave eventually must be freed rather than returned ({\i de-lo hadar lei}), meaning the master recovers nothing at the end of the redemption. But this reasoning is immediately questioned: if the animal {\i is} returned to the master upon redemption, why must the master pay up to one hundred times its value just to get it back? That seems disproportionate to the single unit of value gained from the return.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} ultimately resolves the matter differently: {\i eved milta de-lo shechicha} \u8212- selling a slave to a non-Jew is a rare transgression. {\i U-milta de-lo shechicha lo gazru bah Rabbanan} \u8212- the rabbis do not need to impose an extremely heavy fine on something that rarely occurs. Hence the slave\u8217's fine is ten times. The animal is one hundred times precisely because animal sales to non-Jews were more common and required a more powerful deterrent.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Heir Liability\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Question Posed\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Yirmiyah} poses the final major question of the {\i sugya} to {\i Rabbi Assi}: {\i machar avdo u-met mahu she-yiknasu et b-no acharav} \u8212- if a master sold his slave to a non-Jew and then died, are his sons obligated to buy the slave back \u8212- at the inflated price \u8212- and free him? Or does the penalty die with the transgressor?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tension\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The question pulls in two directions. On the one hand, the rabbinic fine is a form of monetary punishment directed at the person who violated the prohibition. Fines of this kind are inherently personal \u8212- they target the sinner, not his estate or his heirs. On the other hand, the slave\u8217's situation is not resolved by the master\u8217's death. He continues, every single day, to be compelled to violate {\i halacha} while under non-Jewish ownership. The ongoing violation makes the children\u8217's inaction look like their own failure to remedy an existing wrong.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Bechor Comparison\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} examines analogous cases. One is {\i tzaram ozen bechor u-met} \u8212- a {\i kohen} who deliberately inflicted a blemish on a {\i bechor} animal in order to avoid the obligation of bringing it to Jerusalem as a sacrifice, thereby violating the biblical prohibition of {\i kol mum lo yihiyeh bo}, and who was therefore penalized by being forbidden to eat from that animal. If this {\i kohen} then died, was the fine imposed on his sons as well \u8212- {\i kohnsu b-no acharav}? The consideration is that this was an {\i issura de-oraisa}, a biblical violation. Perhaps the severity of the biblical transgression justifies extending the penalty to the next generation. But that would not necessarily help us with our case, where the prohibition against selling a slave to a non-Jew is {\i issura de-rabbanan} \u8212- of rabbinic origin.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chol HaMoed\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A contrasting case: someone who intentionally delayed performing necessary work until {\i Chol HaMoed}, at which point he could claim it was a {\i davar ha-aved} \u8212- an unavoidable loss \u8212- and thereby permit himself to do labor that he should have completed before the holiday. Having thus circumvented the rabbinic prohibition on labor during {\i Chol HaMoed}, the person is not permitted to benefit from that labor. If he then died, are his sons also prohibited from benefiting? The {\i Gemara} suggests that here, precisely because it is a rabbinic violation and moreover because the person did not commit a direct transgression so much as engineer circumstances to exploit a leniency, we would not impose the fine on the children.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But even this does not map perfectly onto the slave case. The {\i Gemara} notes that the {\i Chol HaMoed} case might be weaker precisely because the violation was indirect \u8212- {\i de-lo avid issura} \u8212- the person did not really commit the prohibited act so much as maneuver around it. The slave case involves a clear and direct rabbinic violation, with the added weight of the slave\u8217's ongoing suffering.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shmita Precedent\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} ultimately resolves the question by citing the following teaching from {\i Rabbi Yose bar Rabbi Chanina}: if someone fertilized a field during {\i Shmita} \u8212- which is itself a rabbinic prohibition \u8212- the field may not be planted after {\i Shmita} as a penalty. But if the person died, {\i hetiva u-met b-no zora} \u8212- if he fertilized it and died, his son may plant it. {\i Amar le-didei knasu Rabbanan le-brei lo knasu Rabbanan} \u8212- the rabbis imposed the fine on the transgressor himself; they did not impose it on his heirs.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} notes that this parallel is not perfect: one might argue that {\i Shmita} violation of this kind is only rabbinic, while the slave case involves both a rabbinic violation and the additional dimension of the slave\u8217's daily ongoing suffering. Nevertheless, the {\i Gemara} treats the {\i Shmita} precedent as sufficiently analogous to establish the principle: rabbinic fines are personal to the transgressor and do not pass to heirs.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Invisible Damage\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Abaye} adds a final case that reinforces the principle. If someone took a {\i sheretz} \u8212- a ritually impure rodent \u8212- and deliberately rendered his neighbor\u8217's {\i teruma} {\i tamei}, this constitutes {\i hezek she-eino nikkar}, damage that is not visible. {\i Tuma} cannot be seen; the {\i teruma} looks exactly the same before and after. Under the strict laws of damages, you would be exempt from liability for non-visible damage, because {\i hezek she-eino nikkar lo shmei hezek} \u8212- non-visible damage does not halachically constitute {\i nezek} in the full legal sense. Nevertheless, the rabbis imposed a fine to deter such behavior. If the person then died, do the children pay the fine? {\i Lo knasu Rabbanan b-no acharav} \u8212- no, the rabbis did not impose this rabbinic fine on the children. {\i Knasa de-rabbanan hi} \u8212- it is a rabbinic penalty, and {\i le-didei knasu Rabbanan, le-brei lo knasu Rabbanan} \u8212- they fined the transgressor; they did not fine his heirs.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The principle is thereby established through multiple cases and articulated cleanly: rabbinic fines are inherently personal. They are a form of monetary punishment imposed on the individual who violated the rabbinic ordinance, and they do not survive him. The obligation to redeem and free the slave \u8212- however ongoing and real the slave\u8217's situation remains \u8212- does not transfer as a penalty to the master\u8217's children.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Gittin 45\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Sale to Chutz La\u8217'aretz\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Baraita: 2nd Master\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i daf} opens with a {\i baraita}: {\i hamocher avdo l\u8217'chutz la\u8217'aretz yatza l\u8217'cherut v\u8217'tzarich get shichrur merabbo sheni} \u8212- one who sells his slave to {\i chutz la\u8217'aretz} to another Jew, that slave goes free and requires a {\i get shichrur} from the second master. The {\i chiddush} of the {\i baraita} is that the obligation to issue the writ of freedom falls on the second master, not the first. The second master is, after all, the Jew currently residing in {\i chutz la\u8217'aretz}, and the {\i baraita} does not suggest he receives his money back. The entire weight of the sanction, it appears, is placed on the buyer.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is worth pausing to acknowledge how striking this concern is from the perspective of the Babylonian {\i Talmud}. For those of us in {\i chutz la\u8217'aretz}, and indeed from the vantage point of the {\i Bavli} itself, the tragedy of being outside the Land of Israel \u8212- deprived only of {\i mitzvot hatluyot ba\u8217'aretz}, of {\i terumot u\u8217'ma\u8217'asrot} \u8212- can produce a certain cognitive dissonance. Yet the {\i mishnah} was composed in {\i Eretz Yisrael}, and the {\i takkanot} of {\i Chazal} were designed to maintain a rooted Jewish presence in the Land, not merely a physical one but one expressed through land, cattle, and slaves. That context makes the severity of the concern more intelligible.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafot\u8217's Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} raises a sharp question: when exactly does the slave go free? The slave cannot be forced to go to {\i chutz la\u8217'aretz} \u8212- if he refuses, the new master cannot compel him. But if he agrees to go willingly, he has exercised his own choice, and one might argue he has forfeited his right to freedom on those grounds. So what is the operative scenario in which we free him?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} resolves this by explaining that {\i beit din} forces the master to free the slave before he even departs, precisely to prevent the master from persuading him to go. The concern is that the master may pressure or convince the slave to agree, and we do not want such consent to vitiate the slave\u8217's right. The {\i get shichrur} is therefore issued preemptively, at the point where the slave still says he does not wish to leave.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabban Shimon Defaults\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel} introduces a more nuanced position: {\i pe\u8217'amim yatza u-fe\u8217'amim lo yatza} \u8212- sometimes the slave goes free and sometimes he does not. The determinative factor is where the buyer actually lives, not merely where he originates. If you say {\i machartiv l\u8217'Ploni Antochi} \u8212- I sold him to a certain Antiochian \u8212- that is ambiguous, and {\i Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel} says the slave does not automatically go free; perhaps this Antiochian is living in Israel. But if it is specified that this is an Antiochian {\i she-b\u8217'Antochia} \u8212- who resides in Antioch \u8212- the slave goes free.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A counter-source complicates the picture: a separate {\i baraita} states that if you merely say {\i machartiv l\u8217'Antochi} without qualification, the slave does go free, on the presumption that he is returning to Antioch. And if you know the buyer has an apartment in {\i Lod}, he does not go free. The {\i gemara} reconciles: {\i lo kashya \u8212- ha d\u8217'is lei bayit b\u8217'Eretz Yisrael, ha d\u8217'is lei ushpiza b\u8217'Eretz Yisrael} \u8212- there is no difficulty; the cases differ based on whether the Antiochian has a {\i house} in Israel versus merely a lodging, a temporary rental.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Buyer\u8217's Intent/Location\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What if you know nothing about the buyer\u8217's plans \u8212- you met him in the marketplace without inquiring? The {\i gemara} does not directly address the case of total uncertainty, implying one would presumably investigate. The underlying principle, however, is clear: the question is entirely whether the slave is actually being transferred to {\i chutz la\u8217'aretz}, and that depends on where the buyer is domiciled. Interestingly, from the {\i gemara}\u8217's formulation, it appears that if the buyer splits his time between {\i Eretz Yisrael} and {\i chutz la\u8217'aretz}, the slave would not go free \u8212- though the case is left somewhat open.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Husband-Wife Slave Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Setup & Dowry\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now introduces a question posed by {\i Rabbi Yirmiyah} that intersects with the laws of marriage and divorce: {\i ben Bavel she-nasa ishah} \u8212- a man from {\i Bavel} who marries a woman in {\i Eretz Yisrael}, and she brings slaves into the marriage as part of her dowry. Crucially, this is not a case of {\i nichsei milog}, where the wife retains title and the husband enjoys only the usufruct. This is {\i nichsei tzon barzel}, where the husband assumes full title to the property, with the obligation to return its assessed value to the wife upon divorce. The husband intends to return to {\i Bavel}. Does his acquisition of these slaves constitute something akin to a sale to {\i chutz la\u8217'aretz}, such that the slaves should go free?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tzon Barzel Rights\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 To assess this, one must first understand the nature of {\i nichsei tzon barzel} more precisely. At the time of marriage, the wife\u8217's property is appraised and its value is written into the {\i kesubah}. The husband assumes ownership. If the slaves die, he owes her their assessed value at divorce. If they are alive and she wants them back at the time of divorce, the question \u8212- a general {\i nichsei tzon barzel} question \u8212- is whether she can demand the slaves themselves or only their monetary value.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The resolution of that underlying question bears on the present case: the degree to which we treat the transfer of the slaves to the husband as a full sale, versus a contingent or partial transfer, will determine whether his taking them to {\i Bavel} constitutes the kind of transfer that triggers the slave\u8217's right to freedom.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ownership Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} presents the question in both directions. Even if you hold that she retains the right to reclaim the slaves themselves at divorce \u8212- {\i hadini mahu} that she gets them back \u8212- one could still argue: {\i keivan d\u8217'mishta\u8217'abdei leh l\u8217'peira k\u8217'dideh dami} \u8212- since the husband has the full right to use them and benefit from them for the entire duration of the marriage, that is sufficient ownership to treat this as a sale to {\i chutz la\u8217'aretz}, and the slaves should go free.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Conversely, even if you hold that the husband can keep the slaves and need only pay her their value at divorce \u8212- {\i hadini mahu} that he is not required to return them \u8212- one could still argue: {\i keivan d\u8217'lo kanei l\u8217'gufeh k\u8217'didah dami} \u8212- since he does not own them {\i for their body}, meaning his ownership is of a different character than ordinary ownership, they are treated as still belonging to her, and the transfer does not qualify as a sale that would free them.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} leaves this unresolved: {\i teiku}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rashi\u8217's Explanation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rashi} addresses the puzzling phrase {\i lo kanei l\u8217'gufeh} \u8212- that the husband does not own the slaves \u8220"for their body.\u8221" {\i Rashi} explains: {\i d\u8217'ei mipattar migaresh lah hadrei lah} \u8212- if he divorces her, they go back to her. But this is precisely the case that is in dispute! We are entertaining the possibility that {\i hadini mahu} \u8212- she is entitled to take them back. So the {\i pshat} in {\i Rashi} here is admittedly somewhat difficult to follow. The best reading may be that even within the scenario where she can theoretically demand the slaves themselves, the fact that his ownership is always shadowed by the possibility of having to return them renders it qualitatively different from ordinary ownership \u8212- something more akin to holding property in trust. It remains a challenging {\i Rashi}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Syria/Willingness Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Syria Ruling\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rav Abba} teaches in the name of {\i Rabbi Yochanan}: an {\i eved} who followed his master to {\i Suriya} \u8212- the region of Syria, which has a quasi-{\i Eretz Yisrael} status having been conquered by {\i Dovid HaMelech} \u8212- and the master then sold him there, the slave goes free. This was already established in the first {\i perek}: selling a slave to {\i Suriya} is treated like selling to {\i chutz la\u8217'aretz}. The {\i chiddush} here is that even though the slave went along willingly, we do not say that he forfeited his right to freedom simply by choosing to accompany his master on the journey.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A counter-{\i baraita} from {\i Rabbi Chiya} states that the slave {\i lost} his right. The apparent contradiction is resolved without difficulty.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Intent Distinction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Lo kashya: kan she-da\u8217'at rabo lachzor, kan she-ein da\u8217'at rabo lachzor} \u8212- there is no contradiction. The two {\i baraitot} address different scenarios based on the master\u8217's intent. If the master was traveling to {\i Suriya} temporarily, intending to return to {\i Eretz Yisrael}, then the slave who accompanied him cannot be said to have voluntarily chosen to settle outside the Land. When the master subsequently sells him there, it is a sale to {\i chutz la\u8217'aretz} against the slave\u8217's wishes, and he goes free. But if the master was relocating permanently to {\i Suriya} and the slave willingly followed, the slave has chosen to settle outside the Land of his own accord. Having made that choice, he cannot now claim that being sold in {\i Suriya} entitles him to freedom.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Proof Problem\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This raises an evidentiary question: how does one determine the master\u8217's intent, particularly in ambiguous cases where the master never explicitly stated his plans? If the slave simply followed his master without asking, and the master never told him whether this was a permanent move or a trip, what is the default assumption? The intuition would be to favor the slave \u8212- we would not say he forfeited his rights unless we can establish that he affirmatively and knowingly chose to resettle outside {\i Eretz Yisrael}. But the {\i gemara} does not spell this out explicitly, and the question remains somewhat open.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might also ask: if no master who sells his slave to {\i chutz la\u8217'aretz} will ever recover his investment, who would ever buy such a slave? The point may be precisely that \u8212- this {\i takana} is designed to make such purchases unattractive, and thereby to prevent the slave from ever being taken out of {\i Eretz Yisrael} in the first place.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Sale Goes Through?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Anan Confused\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rav Anan} reports: {\i shma\u8217'na minei d\u8217'Mar Shmuel tarti} \u8212- I have heard from {\i Shmuel} two rulings, and I am uncertain how they relate. The first concerns the selling of a slave to {\i chutz la\u8217'aretz}; the second concerns the case of {\i hamocher sadeh bishnas hayovel} \u8212- one who sells a field during the {\i yovel} year.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 In the {\i yovel} case, {\i Rav} and {\i Shmuel} dispute the status of such a sale. {\i Rav} holds that the sale {\i does} go through \u8212- it is a valid {\i kinyan} \u8212- and then a moment later it is reversed and the field returns to the original owner. One practical consequence is that the money is retained, since a valid sale occurred. Another {\i nafka mina} involves {\i metaltelin agav karka}: if movable property was acquired {\i agav} the field transaction, that acquisition stands, since the underlying {\i kinyan} was legitimate. {\i Shmuel} disagrees and holds that the sale does not go through at all \u8212- {\i eino mechura kol ikkar}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Anan\u8217's uncertainty is: in the slave case and in the {\i yovel} case, Shmuel holds one way in one and another way in the other. In which case does the sale go through, and in which does the money return to the purchaser?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Abaye\u8217's Proof\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rav Yosef} says: {\i v\u8217'lo yadana} \u8212- I don\u8217't know which is which. {\i Abaye} responds: {\i nechezei anan} \u8212- let us work it out ourselves. He cites the {\i baraita}: {\i hamocher avdo l\u8217'chutz la\u8217'aretz yatza l\u8217'cherut v\u8217'tzarich get shichrur merabbo sheni} \u8212- the second master must free him. {\i Shma minei}: the fact that it is the {\i second} master who must write the {\i get shichrur} proves that the sale {\i went through}. The buyer acquired the slave. {\i V\u8217'lo hadra zvini} \u8212- and the buyer does not get his money back; he loses it, and he must also free the slave. {\i De-chi amar Shmuel hatam eino mechura u-mos chozrin} \u8212- and in the {\i yovel} case, when {\i Shmuel} says the sale did not go through, the money returns to the purchaser.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Why Rav Anan Uncertain\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might ask: is this not obvious from both cases? From the {\i baraita}, the second master is the one who frees the slave \u8212- clearly the sale went through. From {\i Shmuel}\u8217's ruling on the {\i yovel} field \u8212- clearly the sale did not go through. Why was {\i Rav Anan} confused?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The answer is twofold. First, {\i Rav Anan} did not have access to this {\i baraita}. From the {\i mishnah} alone, one might have read the ruling as saying the sale did {\i not} go through \u8212- that the original seller must free the slave, similar to the {\i shemita} case. Second, even in the {\i yovel} case, even if {\i Shmuel} says the sale did not go through, does it necessarily follow that the money returns? Perhaps the money is treated as a {\i matanah} \u8212- a gift \u8212- on the theory that both parties knew the sale was invalid and the money was given anyway. This possibility is illustrated by the case of {\i ha-mekadesh et achoto} \u8212- one who gives money as {\i kiddushin} to his own sister. {\i Rav} holds the money is returned; {\i Shmuel} holds it is a {\i matanah}, since one cannot marry one\u8217's sister and the parties presumably knew this. So too, one might have argued the {\i yovel} money was a gift. The {\i gemara} concludes that in the {\i yovel} case, {\i Shmuel} means the money returns \u8212- it is not a gift \u8212- but {\i Rav Anan} could not have assumed this without the {\i baraita} to clarify the slave case.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shemitta Comparison\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The parallel to {\i shemita} is instructive. In the {\i shemita}/yovel case where {\i Shmuel} says the sale did not go through, the money returns. In the slave case, the sale {\i did} go through, and the money does {\i not} return \u8212- the buyer loses. These are two distinct responses to halakhically problematic transactions: in one, the transaction is voided entirely; in the other, it is allowed to stand but the buyer bears the penalty.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Buyer Loses: Why?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Abaye Challenges\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This asymmetry \u8212- that the buyer is the one who loses his money and must free the slave \u8212- prompts {\i Abaye} to press {\i Rav Yosef}: {\i mai chazit de-kansinan le-loke\u8217'ach?} Why do we fine the purchaser? Why not say the sale did not go through and fine the seller \u8212- {\i nikhansinah le-mocher} \u8212- requiring {\i him} to free the slave while the buyer gets his money back, parallel to the {\i shemita} case?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Weasel/Hole\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rav Yosef} responds with a memorable formulation: {\i lav achbara ganav ela chura ganav} \u8212- it is not the weasel that steals, it is the hole. That is, the seller may be the proximate wrongdoer, but it is the buyer\u8217's domicile in {\i chutz la\u8217'aretz} that makes the whole transaction possible and problematic. Without the hole \u8212- without someone willing to receive the slave in {\i chutz la\u8217'aretz} \u8212- the weasel has nowhere to go.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Abaye} pushes back: {\i ei lav achbara chura mai avid?} \u8212- if there were no weasel, what could the hole do? The hole does nothing on its own; the weasel is the initiator. This is in fact a genuine tension. In a market transaction, it is not always obvious who is the initiator. The buyer may well have approached the seller, sought him out, and pressured him to sell. The seller\u8217's being the {\i mocher} does not inherently make him the active wrongdoer. The analogy to a thief and his fence is imperfect, because there the wrongful initiative is clearly with the thief; here, either party could be the driving force.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Locus of Problem\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The resolution offered is: {\i mistabra heicha de-ika issura hatam kansinan} \u8212- we fine where the {\i issur} is located. The buyer is the one living in {\i chutz la\u8217'aretz}; {\i chutz la\u8217'aretz} is the locus of the problem. It is the buyer\u8217's life situation \u8212- his residence outside the Land \u8212- that creates the problematic condition into which the slave is being introduced. Regardless of who initiated the transaction, the buyer is the one whose circumstances represent the ongoing {\i issur}. This is an interesting form of moral and legal reasoning: not simply who acted first, but where the sustained violation resides.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Runaway Slave Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Facts of Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} presents a new case: {\i hahu avda de-ark mi-chutz la\u8217'aretz le-aretz} \u8212- a slave ran away from {\i chutz la\u8217'aretz} to {\i Eretz Yisrael}. His master, who had come from {\i chutz la\u8217'aretz}, pursued him and brought the matter before {\i Rabbi Ami}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Ami Ruling\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Ami} told the master: {\i nichtov lach shtra admei} \u8212- I will write you a document for his monetary value, so you are not left without compensation. {\i Ve-chtov lei gita de-cheiruta} \u8212- and you will write the slave a {\i get shichrur}. But if you refuse: {\i mafkeina lei minach} \u8212- I will free him from you against your will, and you will receive nothing. The incentive structure is clear: cooperate and be compensated; refuse, and lose everything. This is the inverse of the previous case: there, selling a slave to {\i chutz la\u8217'aretz} triggers a penalty on the buyer; here, a slave who flees {\i from chutz la\u8217'aretz to Eretz Yisrael} is freed, with the master offered compensation as an inducement to comply.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Source Dispute\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The basis for this ruling is {\i de-Rabbi Achi be-Rabbi Yoshiya}, from a {\i baraita}. The {\i baraita} opens by interpreting the verse {\i lo yeshvu be-artzecha pen yachti\u8217'u otcha li} \u8212- they shall not dwell in your land lest they cause you to sin against Me. A preliminary reading asks: might this apply even to a non-Jew who accepted upon himself not to worship idols \u8212- even to someone keeping the {\i sheva mitzvot benei Noach}? The {\i talmud lomar} responds by citing: {\i lo tasgir eved el adonav asher yinatzel eleicha me-im adonav} \u8212- do not hand over a slave who has fled to you from his master.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 That verse is then read \u8212- in a striking and admittedly forced fashion \u8212- as referring not to a slave at all, but to a non-Jew seeking to leave a pagan community and find refuge among a monotheistic people. Even if he has not converted, the verse instructs us not to return him to his {\i avoda zara}. The continuation, {\i imcha yeshev be-kirbecha}, supports this reading.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Yoshiya Reading\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Yoshiya} was dissatisfied with that reading. The phrase {\i me-im adonav} \u8212- from his master \u8212- does not fit a man fleeing from his religious community; it would have said {\i me-im aviv} or {\i me-im elohav}. {\i Ella amar Rabbi Yoshiya}, here is the {\i pshat} of the verse: {\i bemocher avdo l\u8217'chutz la\u8217'aretz hakasuv medaber} \u8212- the verse is speaking of one who sells his slave to {\i chutz la\u8217'aretz}. On this reading, {\i lo tasgir} means do not hand the slave over to the second master who bought him in {\i chutz la\u8217'aretz}; {\i asher yinatzel eleicha} describes the slave seeking to remain in {\i Eretz Yisrael} rather than being transferred outside. This is the {\i pshat} of our {\i mishnah}, according to {\i Rabbi Yoshiya} \u8212- the verse is the direct source for the prohibition of selling a slave to {\i chutz la\u8217'aretz}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Achi Challenge\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Achi b\u8217'rei d\u8217'Rabbi Yoshiya} \u8212- his son \u8212- challenged this reading. The phrase {\i asher yinatzel eleicha} \u8212- who seeks refuge with you \u8212- implies active movement {\i toward} the speaker. If the verse meant to describe a slave being taken away from someone, it should have said {\i asher yinatzel me-imcha}, that he is being wrested {\i from} you. The word {\i eleicha} \u8212- toward you \u8212- strongly implies the slave is running {\i to} someone. {\i Ella amar Rabbi Achi b\u8217'rei d\u8217'Rabbi Yoshiya}: {\i b\u8217'eved shebarach mi-chutz la\u8217'aretz la\u8217'aretz hakasuv medaber} \u8212- the verse speaks of a slave who fled from {\i chutz la\u8217'aretz} to {\i Eretz Yisrael}. This is the straightforward {\i pshat}: a runaway slave. Do not hand him back. {\i Imcha yeshev be-kirbecha b\u8217'echad she\u8217'arecha b\u8217'tov lo} \u8212- he shall dwell with you in the land, in one of your cities, wherever is good for him.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 On this reading, if a slave runs from one place in {\i chutz la\u8217'aretz} to another in {\i chutz la\u8217'aretz}, or from one place in {\i Eretz Yisrael} to another in {\i Eretz Yisrael}, the verse does not apply and you would return him. It is only the specific case of flight from {\i chutz la\u8217'aretz} to {\i Eretz Yisrael} that triggers the protection.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Written Writ Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A separate {\i baraita} is then cited: {\i lo tasgir eved el adonav} \u8212- {\i Rebbi} interprets this as speaking of one who {\i buys a slave on condition that he will free him}: {\i b\u8217'lo\u8217'ke\u8217'ach eved al m\u8217'nas l\u8217'shachreru hakasuv medaber}. If the buyer reneges and tries to keep the slave, {\i beit din} will not hand the slave over to him. {\i Rav Dimi bar Yitzchak} specifies the precise scenario: {\i dechasiv lei hachi \u8212- l\u8217'cheshitkaneh harei atzmecha k\u8217'nuyeh lach me-achshav} \u8212- the buyer had already written a {\i shtar shichrur} to take effect upon purchase. Having issued such a document, he may not now reverse course and enslave the man.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Runaway Slave Case (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Samaritans Episode\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rav Chisda}\u8217's slave ran to the {\i Kutim} \u8212- the Samaritans. {\i Rav Chisda} sent word: {\i haderoni nihalei} \u8212- return him to me. The {\i Kutim} responded by quoting the verse {\i lo tasgir eved el adonav} \u8212- we do not return runaway slaves. {\i Rashi} notes that the {\i Kutim} were in {\i chutz la\u8217'aretz}, though they read the verse as a flat prohibition without any geographical qualification.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rav Chisda} countered: {\i v\u8217'chen ta\u8217'aseh lachamoro v\u8217'chen ta\u8217'aseh l\u8217'simlato v\u8217'chen ta\u8217'aseh l\u8217'chol avedat achicha} \u8212- there is also a verse obligating the return of lost property, and a slave is property. The {\i Kutim} replied that the explicit verse {\i lo tasgir eved el adonav} overrides a general lost-property obligation. {\i Rav Chisda} then invoked the reading of {\i Rabbi Achi b\u8217'rei d\u8217'Rabbi Yoshiya}: {\i hahu b\u8217'eved shebarach mi-chutz la\u8217'aretz la\u8217'aretz} \u8212- that verse applies only when the slave fled {\i from chutz la\u8217'aretz to Eretz Yisrael}, not to an ordinary runaway.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} notes that {\i Rav Chisda} chose to invoke {\i Rabbi Achi b\u8217'rei d\u8217'Rabbi Yoshiya}\u8217's {\i drasha} rather than {\i Rebbi}\u8217's. Why not simply cite {\i Rebbi}, who also limits the verse? Because {\i Rabbi Achi b\u8217'rei d\u8217'Rabbi Yoshiya}\u8217's reading is closer to the {\i pshat} of the verse, and the {\i Kutim} were more likely to be persuaded by an argument grounded in the natural reading of the text than by rabbinic authority alone.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Abaye\u8217's Mule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Abaye} lost a mule that strayed to the {\i Kutim}. He sent word requesting its return. They replied: {\i tzaruch simana} \u8212- give us an identifying mark. He sent back: {\i d\u8217'chivara kerisei} \u8212- it has a white belly. They replied: if you were not {\i Nachmani} \u8212- his given name \u8212- we would not return it to you. {\i Atu kulei chamra lav keriseihu chivranin ninhu?} What kind of {\i siman} is that? Every mule has a white belly. But given who you are, we will send it back. The episode illustrates the looseness of identifying marks when a claimed {\i siman} is common to many animals, while also reflecting the particular dynamics of interactions with the {\i Kutim}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Pidyon Shvuyim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Value Limit\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} states: {\i ein podin et hashvuyim yoter k\u8217'dei d\u8217'meihen} \u8212- one does not redeem captives for more than their monetary value. That value is assessed against the slave market: what would this person fetch in an open slave market? The captors, of course, know that a particular individual is worth far more to his family or community, and will demand accordingly. The {\i mishnah} instructs that one should not pay the inflated ransom. The stated reason: {\i mipnei tikkun ha\u8217'olam} \u8212- for the betterment of the world.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} also states: {\i v\u8217'ein mavrichin et hashvuyim} \u8212- one does not mount an operation to free captives by stealth or rescue. This too is {\i mipnei tikkun ha\u8217'olam}, or, according to {\i Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel}, {\i mipnei tikkun hashvuyim}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabban Shimon Reason\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel} says the reason is {\i mipnei tikkun hashvuyim} \u8212- for the welfare of the captives themselves, not for the world at large. The distinction between {\i tikkun ha\u8217'olam} and {\i tikkun hashvuyim} proves significant, as the {\i gemara} will show.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two Reasons\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Ibaya lehu}: the {\i gemara} asks what {\i tikkun ha\u8217'olam} means in this context. Is the concern {\i mishum duchka de-tzibbura} \u8212- because the financial burden on the community of paying inflated ransom is simply unsustainable? Or is it {\i mishum d\u8217'lo ligrebu v\u8217'layeisu tefei} \u8212- because paying high ransom encourages future kidnapping, as captors learn that Jews will pay whatever is demanded?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i nafka mina} is substantial. If the reason is financial burden, then an individual with sufficient private means may pay whatever he wishes for his own family member, since he is not burdening the community. But if the reason is systemic \u8212- that paying inflated ransoms incentivizes future kidnappings \u8212- then even private individuals should be prohibited from doing so, because their actions affect the broader policy. Similarly for the rescue operation: if the concern is about reprisals against remaining captives ({\i tikkun hashvuyim}), then the prohibition only applies when others will suffer for it. If no one is left behind, a rescue operation would be permitted. If the concern is discouraging future kidnappings ({\i tikkun ha\u8217'olam}), one must weigh that even a successful rescue with no immediate victims perpetuates the broader problem.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Levi Story\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The story of {\i Levi bar Darga} is brought as evidence: {\i parkei lbarteih btlisar alfei dinarei zahav} \u8212- he redeemed his daughter for 13,000 gold dinars, clearly far in excess of her market value. If the reason for the prohibition is communal financial burden, this presents no problem, since he used his own funds. If the reason is systemic discouragement of future kidnapping, it should have been prohibited even here.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Abaye Uncertainty\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Abaye} responds: {\i u-man leima lan de-biratzon chachamim avad} \u8212- who says the sages approved of what he did? {\i Dilma shelo biratzon chachamim avad} \u8212- perhaps he acted without their approval, and the case cannot serve as a proof. The question thus remains unresolved by this story.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This issue resonated through the medieval period. The {\i Maharam miRothenburg} famously refused to allow his community to redeem him at the exorbitant price demanded by his captors, invoking precisely this principle, and died in captivity as a result. The irony was compounded when, after his death, an enormous sum was paid to retrieve his body for burial.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafot Distinctions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} addresses what seems to be an exception: a {\i baraita} in {\i perek Naarah} states that if a man\u8217's wife is taken captive and her captors demand ten times her value, {\i paam rishon podeh} \u8212- the husband is obligated to redeem her the first time, even at this inflated sum. How is this consistent with the prohibition?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} explains: {\i shani ishto de-havya k\u8217'gufo yoter mibito} \u8212- his wife is different because she is like his own body, even more so than his daughter. As for redeeming oneself: {\i deal atzmo lo tiknu shelo yitein kol asher lo bad nafsho} \u8212- the sages never enacted that a person may not give everything he has to save his own life. Whatever the broader societal concern, we would not extend it to the point of preventing a person from paying any price for his own life. And since one may redeem oneself, one may also redeem one\u8217's wife, who is treated as one\u8217's own body. The daughter, however, does not benefit from this exception; {\i Levi bar Darga}\u8217's case, as {\i Abaye} noted, was of uncertain approval.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rav Nachman Story\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Pot Miracle\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i daf} concludes with a striking aggadic narrative. {\i Bantei de-Rav Nachman} \u8212- the daughters of {\i Rav Nachman} \u8212- possessed the ability to stir a boiling pot directly with their bare hands, apparently a miraculous sign of their righteousness.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Illish Puzzle\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rav Illish} found this perplexing. The verse states {\i adam echad me-elef matzati v\u8217'ishah be-chol eleh lo matzati} \u8212- I found one righteous man among a thousand, but among women I found not one. How then could {\i Rav Nachman}\u8217's daughters be recipients of such a miracle? They appeared to be exceptional {\i tzadikim}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Birds Warning\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 While {\i Rav Illish} and {\i Rav Nachman}\u8217's daughters were all in captivity together, {\i Rav Illish} was sitting one day with a certain man who could understand the language of birds. A raven came and called out. {\i Rav Illish} asked: what is it saying? The man told him: {\i Illish berach Illish berach} \u8212- Illish, flee, Illish, flee. {\i Rav Illish} dismissed it: {\i urva shikra hu v\u8217'lo samichna aleh} \u8212- ravens are liars; I do not trust him. Then a dove came and called out the same message: {\i Illish berach Illish berach}. {\i Rav Illish} took this seriously: {\i Knesset Yisrael ke-yonah metila} \u8212- the Jewish people are compared to a dove, as in {\i Shir HaShirim}. {\i Shma mina mitrachish li nisa} \u8212- this tells me that a miracle will indeed happen for me if I flee.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This episode carries a {\i halachic} implication as well: the prohibition against mounting rescue operations for captives does not prevent {\i the captive himself} from attempting to escape. Just as one may redeem oneself, one may save oneself \u8212- paralleling {\i Tosafot}\u8217's point about self-redemption in the {\i pidyon shvuyim} discussion.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Daughters Prefer\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Before fleeing, {\i Rav Illish} resolved to check on {\i Rav Nachman}\u8217's daughters: {\i i kaiman be-heimanutaihu} \u8212- if they had kept their faith and not surrendered themselves sexually to their captors, {\i adrinhu} \u8212- he would take them with him. Women, he knew, share their secrets with one another in private. He went to eavesdrop outside the bathroom. He heard them saying: {\i adei guvrin u-neharda-ei guvrin} \u8212- we used to have husbands back there, and now these are our husbands. {\i Leimru lehu le-shavuyaihu de-lerachkinu me-hacha de-la leitu inshi ve-lishme-u ve-lifre-kunu} \u8212- let us tell our captors to take us further away, so that our former husbands cannot find out and redeem us. They were happy with their situation and wanted to prevent being rescued.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Resolution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Clearly, they had not kept faith. They were not {\i tzadikim}. The miracle of stirring the pot could not have been on their account. {\i Rav Illish} fled \u8212- {\i kam arak} \u8212- without them. He and the bird-interpreter escaped together. For {\i Rav Illish}, a miracle occurred: {\i avar be-mavra} \u8212- he was able to cross a body of water and escape the captors. The bird-interpreter, {\i dahu gavra}, was found by the captors and killed. The miracle was for {\i Rav Illish} alone.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Witchcraft Theory\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 When {\i Rav Illish} returned, he concluded: {\i havei kavshi kaderah be-chashafim} \u8212- they had been stirring the pot with witchcraft, not righteousness. The miracle was never theirs; they had simply been practicing sorcery. The verse, after all, is vindicated: righteous women of that caliber are not to be found, and what appeared to be a miracle of piety turns out to have been an act of {\i kishuf}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Gittin 46\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Sefer Torah & Tikkun Olam\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Pidyon Shvuyim Intro\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The present daf picks up at the Mishnah toward the bottom of 45a, following an extended discussion of the {\i tikkun olam} of {\i pidyon shvuyim} \u8212- a wide-ranging and conceptually rich treatment of what {\i tikkun olam} means in practice: balancing personal needs against community needs, and weighing present circumstances against future consequences. The Mishnah now introduces another application of {\i tikkun olam}, this one concerning the redemption of sacred objects from non-Jews.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Seforim Pricing Rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Mishnah states: {\i ein lokhin sfarim tefillin u\u8217'mezuzot min ovdei kokhavim yoter k\u8217'dei dmeihem} \u8212- we do not purchase {\i sifrei torah}, {\i tefillin}, or {\i mezuzot} from non-Jews for more than their market value. The reasoning is precisely parallel to the rule against over-paying for captives: doing so would only encourage more theft. This is a real and serious concern; synagogues require substantial security measures to protect {\i sifrei torah}, given how valuable they are. One might feel instinctively that a {\i sefer torah} is not merely a matter of dollars and cents \u8212- it is a holy object, surely worth whatever price is demanded. Yet the Mishnah rules otherwise. One does not redeem it for more than its going rate, and this, the Mishnah declares, is {\i mipnei tikkun olam} \u8212- for the sake of {\i tikkun olam}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sefer Torah Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now focuses on a different legal dimension of the same Mishnah. Rav Budya poses a question to Rav Ashi: the Mishnah specifies that one does not pay {\i more} than market value \u8212- implying that at market value one {\i does} purchase it. From this, may we infer that a {\i sefer torah} found in the possession of a non-Jew is presumed to be kosher? After all, who writes {\i sifrei torah} if not Jews? Does the Mishnah\u8217's implicit permission to buy it back indicate that it is fit for use?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Lignoz Principle\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara deflects the inference: {\i dilma lignoz} \u8212- perhaps one buys it back only in order to bury it properly. The Mishnah\u8217's permission to purchase at market value does not necessarily imply that the {\i sefer torah} is fit for use; one might purchase it {\i lignoz}, to give it proper disposal out of respect, while still suspecting that it may be {\i pasul}. This is not merely theoretical \u8212- it is not uncommon to encounter old {\i sifrei kodesh} in second-hand settings where no one will care for them properly, and purchasing them solely in order to place them in {\i sheimos} is a recognizable practice, whether or not there is a formal {\i chiyuv} to do so.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Min & Yisaref\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara proceeds to a related ruling. Rav Nachman declares: {\i naktinan} \u8212- our accepted position is that {\i sefer torah she-ktavo min yisaref}, a {\i sefer torah} written by a {\i min} must be burned. The {\i min} in this context is a Jewish sectarian \u8212- most relevantly, a Jewish Christian \u8212- someone who has broken fundamentally from the faith, and whose understanding of God is categorically different from that of normative Judaism. When such a person writes a {\i sefer torah}, the divine names are written with a conception of God that is, from the rabbinic perspective, idolatrous or at least profoundly distorted. Beyond the technical question of intent, such a text poses a particular danger precisely because it comes from within \u8212- it is the most insidious threat. Hence the severe ruling: not merely {\i yiganeiz}, burial, but {\i yisaref}, destruction by fire. The text does not merely lack {\i kedushat sefer torah}; it is treated as something actively dangerous, analogous to idolatrous literature, and must be destroyed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Three Opinions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What of a {\i sefer torah} written by an ordinary non-Jew ({\i oved kochavim})? The Gemara records three positions, attributed to different {\i tannaim} in different {\i baraitot}: one says it must be burned ({\i yisaref}); one says it must be buried ({\i yiganeiz}); and one says it may actually be read ({\i korin bo}). The most radical of these \u8212- that a {\i sefer torah} written by a non-Jew is fit for use \u8212- raises a striking halakhic question. Does the writer need to be a believing monotheist? Is any {\i kavanah} required at all? The Gemara notes that {\i sifrei torah} written in Greek have, in some opinions, been considered valid; perhaps when a non-Jew simply copies letter by letter, functioning as a scribe without any particular religious intent in either direction, the text retains its validity.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Eliezer View\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara resolves the three-way {\i machloket} not as a genuine dispute but as representing distinct positions: {\i lo kashya} \u8212- there is no real contradiction. The opinion that the {\i sefer torah} must be burned reflects the view of Rabbi Eliezer, who holds that {\i stam machshevet oved kochavim l\u8217'avodat kochavim} \u8212- one presumes by default that the thoughts of a non-Jew are directed toward idolatrous worship. If the {\i sefer torah} was written with idolatrous intent, it is not merely {\i pasul} but is transformed into an idolatrous work requiring destruction \u8212- a striking and severe position.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Disqualified Writers\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The intermediate position \u8212- that a {\i sefer torah} written by a non-Jew requires burial \u8212- reflects the view of a {\i tanna} transmitted in the name of Rav Hamnuna the son of Rav Ami of Pashrunya. The {\i braita} states that {\i sifrei torah}, {\i tefillin}, and {\i mezuzot} written by a {\i min}, an informer ({\i masur}), a non-Jew, a slave, a woman, a minor, a Samaritan ({\i kuti}), or a Jewish apostate ({\i yisrael mumar}) are all {\i pasul}. The disqualification is derived from a {\i drasha} on the verse {\i ukeshartem uchetavtem} \u8212- \u8220"you shall bind and you shall write\u8221": whoever is subject to the obligation of {\i tefillin} is qualified to write sacred texts; whoever is not subject to that obligation is not qualified.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This excludes women, minors, and slaves, who are not obligated in {\i tefillin}. The Samaritan is excluded presumably because, in rejecting the rabbinic understanding of {\i tefillin}, he places himself outside that {\i mitzvah}. The apostate Jew, though technically obligated, has by his rejection of his obligations effectively removed himself from the category. Most striking is the inclusion of the {\i masur}, the informer \u8212- a person who may be fully observant and believing but who, by betraying his community, is classified as outside it, and that classification is sufficient to disqualify him from writing sacred texts.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This Gemara sparks a broader question taken up by Tosafot: is the principle that one must be obligated in a {\i mitzvah} in order to create its object a general rule, or specific to {\i sefer torah}, {\i tefillin}, and {\i mezuzot} by virtue of this {\i drasha}? Tosafot cites the case of {\i tzitzit}, where the Talmud excludes only non-Jews (from the verse {\i bnei Yisrael v\u8217'asu}) while implying that women are qualified \u8212- and similarly the rule that a {\i sukkah} built by a woman or various other categories of people is nonetheless valid. The conclusion is that {\i kol sheyeshno bikshirah yeshno bichitivah} operates as a specific {\i drasha} limited to these three sacred texts, and is not a default rule for {\i mitzvah} objects generally.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabban Gamliel Permit\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The third and most permissive position \u8212- that a {\i sefer torah} written by a non-Jew is {\i korin bo}, fit for use \u8212- is supported by a {\i braita}: {\i lokchim sefarim me\u8217'ovdei kochavim bechol makom u\u8217'vilvad she\u8217'yihyu kesuvim kehilchasam} \u8212- one may purchase {\i sifrei torah} from non-Jews anywhere, provided they are written according to {\i halacha}. The {\i braita} goes further and records a {\i maaseh}: a certain non-Jew in Tzidan was actually employed as a {\i sofer}, writing {\i sifrei torah}, and Rabban Gamliel permitted purchasing from him \u8212- meaning, using them. So the radical position is not merely theoretical but has practical precedent: a non-Jew who writes a {\i sefer torah} correctly, without any demonstrable idolatrous {\i kavanah}, produces a valid text.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Apostate Explanation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara, however, raises an internal contradiction: Rabban Gamliel himself requires that the {\i klaf} \u8212- the parchment \u8212- be prepared {\i lishma}, with the intent to be used for a sacred text. As a {\i braita} records concerning {\i tefillin}: if one covered them with gold or attached a leather patch from a non-kosher animal they are {\i pasul}; a patch from a kosher animal is valid, {\i ve\u8217'af al pi shelo ibdan lishma} \u8212- even if the leather was not processed {\i lishma} \u8212- but Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel says that even leather from a kosher animal is {\i pasul} unless it was processed {\i lishma}. If Rabban Gamliel demands {\i lishma} even for the raw materials, how could he not require {\i lishma} for the writing itself?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara resolves this by reinterpreting the {\i maaseh}: {\i amar Rabbah bar Shmuel be-ger she-chazar le-surro} \u8212- the non-Jew in Tzidan was actually a convert who had reverted to his former ways. He had once been Jewish, knew how to write a {\i sefer torah}, and was now outwardly practicing as a non-Jew. Rav Ashi pushes this further: {\i she-chazar le-surro mishum yirah} \u8212- he reverted out of fear, like a {\i converso}, while inwardly remaining observant. But this interpretation essentially evacuates the {\i pshat} of the story entirely: the man in Tzidan, on this reading, is fundamentally an observant Jew practicing under duress, and the {\i chiddush} is minimal.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafot Alternative\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot offers a different approach. Rather than accepting that the writing need not be {\i lishma}, Tosafot argues that in fact writing {\i does} require {\i lishma} \u8212- but that the {\i lishma} of the writing is supplied automatically when the {\i klaf} has been prepared {\i lishma}. Once the parchment has been designated and prepared for the purpose of a {\i sefer torah}, the context is established, and the writing upon it is understood as occurring within that sacred framework. The writing, in other words, draws its {\i lishma} from the {\i klaf}. Tosafot\u8217's further point is that {\i stam} writing \u8212- writing whose purpose is not otherwise specified \u8212- should by default be understood as writing {\i lishma}, since why else would one be writing a {\i sefer torah}? This contrasts with {\i stam ibud}, tanning leather without specification, which could be for any of countless purposes. The default for tanning is therefore not {\i leshem sefer torah}, whereas the default for writing words of {\i Torah} in the form of a {\i sefer torah} should naturally be understood as {\i lishma}. Tosafot acknowledges the difficulty without fully resolving it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The upshot of this entire sugya is that there does appear to be a genuine {\i tannaic} position that a non-Jew may write a {\i sefer torah} without any requirement of {\i lishma} on his part. Because of the apparent contradiction with Rabban Gamliel\u8217's position on {\i ibud lishma}, the Gemara reinterprets the precedent. But the underlying possibility retains its interest. Notably, the Gemara elsewhere entertains the analogous {\i hava amina} that a non-Jew might perform {\i milah} \u8212- two acts one would naturally assume require the deepest Jewish connection and inner commitment turn out to be, at least as a matter of Talmudic discussion, potentially performable by a non-Jew.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Divorce & Remarriage\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishnah Rules\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Mishnah states two prohibitions: {\i hamotzi et ishto mishum shem ra lo yachazir} \u8212- a man who divorces his wife on account of {\i shem ra}, an ill reputation regarding her conduct, may not subsequently remarry her; {\i mishum neder lo yachazir} \u8212- and similarly, if he divorces her on account of vows she has taken. The Mishnah thus creates a category of divorce that carries a permanent consequence for the husband: he forfeits the ability to remarry this woman.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two Rationales\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Two explanations are given for this prohibition, and they correspond to two fundamentally different understandings of the {\i takkanah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The first and more widely cited rationale concerns protecting the wife\u8217's interests. The concern is that the husband, after divorcing his wife on the stated grounds, might subsequently want to reclaim her \u8212- particularly if the basis for the divorce is later undermined. If the {\i shem ra} turns out to have been false, or if the {\i neder} is annulled by a sage, the husband might assert: {\i ilu hayisi yodeia sheken hu afilu hayu nosnim lo meiah maneh lo hayisi megarshah} \u8212- had I known this, not for any amount of money would I have divorced her. By claiming that the {\i get} was issued under a fundamental misapprehension, he raises questions about the validity of the original divorce \u8212- and if the divorce was invalid, any subsequent marriage she entered into is adulterous, and children born from it are {\i mamzerim}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 According to Rashi, the {\i takkanah} addresses this by ensuring that the husband\u8217's retroactive claim will not be believed: since he was told explicitly at the time that divorcing her under these circumstances would permanently foreclose the option of remarrying her, and he chose to proceed anyway, he clearly had full {\i gemirut da\u8217'at} \u8212- complete and unconditional intention to divorce. He cannot now claim otherwise.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 According to Tosafot, the mechanism is different: the husband has no incentive to raise the claim in the first place, since making it will not restore the marriage. If he cannot remarry her regardless of what he claims, there is no reason to spread rumors or fabricate the claim that the divorce was invalid.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The second rationale is of a different character entirely: the prohibition serves to control women\u8217's behavior. The fear is that women will become {\i perutzot ba\u8217'arayot u\u8217'vi\u8217'nedarim} \u8212- too loose in matters of sexual propriety and in taking vows. Knowing that any {\i shem ra} arising about her conduct could lead to an irreversible divorce, a woman will be more careful. Similarly, excessive vow-taking will carry a permanent personal cost. It should be noted, as Tosafot observes, that the {\i shem ra} rationale sits somewhat awkwardly with the behavioral-control explanation: if someone is simply the victim of rumors, there is little she could have done differently.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Yehuda\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yehuda draws a distinction within the {\i neder} case: {\i kol neder she\u8217'yadu vo rabbim lo yachazir v\u8217'shelo yadu vo rabbim yachazir} \u8212- if the vow was publicly known, the man may not remarry; if it was made privately, he may. This position is consistent with the behavioral-control rationale: the prohibition is aimed at discouraging reckless vow-taking, and it is only the public vow \u8212- which by its nature cannot be retracted, and which demonstrates a particular lack of care \u8212- that triggers the sanction. A private vow, which could be quietly annulled, reflects a lesser degree of irresponsibility and does not attract the prohibition.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yehoshua ben Levi grounds Rabbi Yehuda\u8217's position in the episode of the Gibeonites: the Israelite leaders could not break their oath to spare the Gibeonites because it had been made publicly, as the verse states, {\i ki nishbeu lahem nesi\u8217'ei ha-edah}. The Rabbis, however, respond that the oath to the Gibeonites was not binding at all \u8212- since it was made under false pretenses ({\i be-ta\u8217'us}), it never took effect halachically. The reason the oath was nonetheless honored was {\i mishum kiddush Hashem}: repudiating a public oath, even a technically invalid one, would have constituted a {\i chillul Hashem} in the eyes of the nations. How many people constitute {\i rabbim} for the purposes of Rabbi Yehuda\u8217's ruling? Rav Nachman says three, deriving the number from the word {\i rabbim} in the context of a {\i zavah} ({\i yamim rabbim}: {\i yamim} = two, {\i rabbim} = one more, totaling three). Another opinion holds ten, derived from the word {\i edah}, which elsewhere connotes a quorum of ten representing the community.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Meir\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Meir offers a different criterion: {\i kol neder she\u8217'tzarich hakiras chacham lo yachazir v\u8217'she\u8217'eino tzarich hakiras chacham yachazir} \u8212- if the vow required a sage to permit it, the husband may not remarry; if it did not require a sage \u8212- meaning the husband himself had the power to annul it \u8212- he may. Rabbi Meir\u8217's concern is specifically about the husband\u8217's retroactive claim. If the vow required a sage to annul, he might plausibly say: had I known you could have gone to a {\i beit din} and had it annulled, I never would have divorced you. But if he himself could have annulled the vow, that claim has no credibility: of course he knew. His decision to divorce her despite having the power to annul the vow himself indicates that his objection was not to this particular vow but to her pattern of vow-taking \u8212- and that is a fully deliberate divorce with complete {\i gemirut da\u8217'at}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Elazar\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Elazar reverses Rabbi Meir\u8217's logic. The primary concern, Rabbi Elazar holds, is actually the case where the husband {\i could} have annulled the vow himself \u8212- because in that case his claim ({\i had I known I could have done it, I wouldn\u8217't have divorced her}) has maximum plausibility. In the case where a sage is required, the claim is weaker: a husband would not naturally expect his wife to go seeking out a {\i beit din} to annul her vow, and so he cannot convincingly say that he would have relied on that possibility. Having established the prohibition for the more credible case, Rabbi Elazar extends it to include the less credible case as well \u8212- not because the claim there is truly feared, but to maintain the coherence of the rule.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara articulates the underlying dispute: Rabbi Meir holds that {\i adam rotzeh she\u8217'tisbazeh ishto b\u8217'veit din} \u8212- a man does not mind his wife being subject to the process of appearing before a court for annulment. Rabbi Elazar holds the opposite: a man does {\i not} want his wife making the rounds of rabbinic courts, and therefore the claim that {\i had I known she could have gone to a sage} is simply not believable.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gemara Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara frames a debate about the fundamental rationale of the Mishnah\u8217's prohibition. Is the prohibition rooted in {\i kilkul} \u8212- the concern that the husband will damage his ex-wife\u8217's status by claiming the divorce was invalid \u8212- or is it rooted in the desire to control women\u8217's behavior?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Explicit Statement\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Nachman rules: {\i v\u8217'hu she\u8217'amar lah mishum shem ra ani motzi\u8217'ach} \u8212- the prohibition applies specifically when the husband explicitly states, at the time of the divorce, that this is the reason he is divorcing her. If he never articulates the reason, he cannot later make a credible claim that undermines the {\i get}: without an explicit statement at the time, any subsequent assertion that he would not have divorced her under different circumstances amounts to {\i devarim she\u8217'belev einam devarim} \u8212- an unexpressed intention with no legal standing.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two Versions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara presents two versions of this ruling. The first version holds that the prohibition applies {\i only} when the husband explicitly states the reason at the time of the divorce; if he does not state it, the prohibition does not apply, and he may remarry, because any retroactive claim of his will carry no weight. The second version holds that the prohibition applies regardless of whether the reason was stated, but we nonetheless instruct him to make the declaration explicitly \u8212- not for halachic effect, but for the purpose of social publicity. By announcing before witnesses why he is divorcing her, the divorce becomes a public statement of consequence, serving the goal of controlling behavior: {\i kol Yisrael yishme\u8217'u ve\u8217'yira\u8217'u}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two Versions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i (continued above \u8212- see preceding subsection)}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafot vs Rashi\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 On the question of how the {\i takkanah} operates, Tosafot and Rashi diverge. According to Rashi, the husband\u8217's claim of {\i had I known} is simply not believed \u8212- the reasoning is that since he was informed he could never remarry her under these circumstances and yet proceeded with the divorce, he is presumed to have had complete {\i gemirut da\u8217'at}, unconditional intention. According to Tosafot, the mechanism is prophylactic: the man has no incentive to raise the retroactive claim at all, since raising it will gain him nothing \u8212- he cannot remarry her regardless. The practical difference emerges in a case where the husband makes the claim anyway, not to remarry her but for some other purpose. On Rashi\u8217's view the claim is categorically disbelieved; on Tosafot\u8217's view, the question is more complex.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Public Announcement\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 According to the second version of Rav Nachman\u8217's ruling, the requirement that the husband make a declaration is about achieving public awareness \u8212- the logic of {\i kedei she\u8217'lo yiheyu bnot Yisrael perutzot ba\u8217'arayot u\u8217'vi\u8217'nedarim}. The husband is instructed to say, openly, to his wife: {\i mishum shem ra ani motziech} \u8212- it is because of your reputation that I am divorcing you; or {\i mishum neder ani motziech} \u8212- it is because of your vow. The purpose of this declaration is not to give halachic teeth to the retroactive-claim concern (that applies regardless), but to publicize the cause and thereby deter similar conduct among others. This is akin to the principle of {\i kol Yisrael yishme\u8217'u ve\u8217'yira\u8217'u}: public consequences function as a social deterrent. The question of how one could ever substantiate the husband\u8217's motivation if he does not announce it is implicit in the discussion \u8212- the answer is presumably that by the time of the divorce, he has already communicated his grievance to others, but the formal declaration at the time of the {\i get} is what gives it the required publicity and effect.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i braita} recorded in support of the first position reflects Rabbi Meir\u8217's approach: {\i mipnei mah amru hamotzi et ishto mishum shem ra lo yachazir} \u8212- why did the sages say he cannot remarry? Because he may otherwise go and claim the divorce was made in error, that the underlying premise was false, that {\i ilu hayisi yodeia} he never would have divorced her \u8212- thereby retroactively calling the {\i get} into question and rendering her subsequent children {\i mamzerim}. Therefore, the sages tell him in advance: know that regardless of what you later discover, you will never be able to remarry this woman. This removes the incentive or, on Rashi\u8217's account, ensures that any subsequent claim is disbelieved.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i braita} supporting the second version reflects Rabbi Elazar son of Rabbi Yosi\u8217's approach: the goal is that women not be {\i perutzot ba\u8217'arayot u\u8217'vi\u8217'nedarim}, and therefore the husband is instructed to say {\i to his wife} \u8212- not merely to himself \u8212- why he is divorcing her. The declaration is not received by the husband as a warning but is made by him as a public statement of cause.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might ask whether the {\i takkanah} should ever compel a man to divorce his wife against his will on these grounds. The answer is no \u8212- even from the perspective of {\i shelo yiheyu bnot Yisrael}, the community does not intervene to dissolve a marriage. The husband\u8217's private decision whether to tolerate his wife\u8217's conduct remains his. What the {\i takkanah} does is attach permanent consequences to the exercise of {\i that} decision: if he chooses to divorce her on these grounds, he surrenders the option of returning to her. The marriage is not broken by rabbinic authority; rather, the one who breaks it on these grounds is held to have done so definitively.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 There follows a discussion touching on a concern raised about whether the husband\u8217's claim, if made, could ever actually succeed in retroactively voiding the {\i get}. The answer involves the principle of {\i devarim she\u8217'belev einam devarim}: unstated intentions carry no legal weight, and retroactive claims about what one would or would not have done \u8212- divorced purely in his own mind without any articulated condition \u8212- receive no halachic credence. The Gemara also addresses the question of whether, even if such a claim were theoretically valid, the wife who remarried in good faith on the strength of a {\i get} she had every reason to believe was valid would be considered to have acted {\i be-ones} \u8212- under compulsion \u8212- such that while the children might technically be {\i mamzerim} on his account, her return to him would in principle be possible, since the second marriage was entered into without any real {\i get} having divorced her from the first husband. These scenarios are treated as matters of serious concern warranting the structural protections the Mishnah establishes.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 The Case of the Man\u8217's Vow\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Mishnah concludes this section with a case narrated by Rabbi Yosi ben Rabbi Yehuda: {\i maaseh be-Tzidan be\u8217'echad she\u8217'amar le\u8217'ishto konam im eini megareshech} \u8212- an incident in Tzidan involving a man who declared, \u8220"All fruit of the world be forbidden to me if I do not divorce you,\u8221" and then divorced her to prevent the {\i konam} from taking effect. The sages permitted him to remarry her, {\i mipnei tikkun olam}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara asks what this case has to do with what preceded it, since the earlier {\i mishnah} concerned the wife\u8217's vow, not the husband\u8217's. The Gemara resolves this by explaining that the text is incomplete as transmitted: the full teaching would distinguish between the case where {\i she} makes the vow \u8212- in which the restrictions of the {\i mishnah} apply \u8212- and the case where {\i he} makes the vow \u8212- in which case he may remarry. The asymmetry is not surprising in formal terms: the concern about {\i bnot Yisrael perutzot bi\u8217'nedarim} is causally linked to the wife\u8217's conduct leading to divorce, not the husband\u8217's. When it is the husband who vows in a way that forces his own hand, the same dynamic does not obtain.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara nonetheless wonders whether we should impose a {\i knas} even in the husband\u8217's case, under the principle that vow-making is itself problematic: Rabbi Natan teaches that {\i hanoder ke\u8217'ilu banah bamah} \u8212- one who makes a vow is as though he built an illicit altar, and one who fulfills it rather than seeking annulment is as though he sacrificed upon it. Perhaps, then, since his vow caused his wife to be divorced, he should bear a penalty and not be permitted to remarry. The Gemara rejects this: {\i ka mashma lan} \u8212- the ruling comes to teach us that we do not impose such a {\i knas}. The {\i tikkun olam} of allowing him to remarry is that there was no sufficient countervailing {\i tikkun olam} interest to prevent it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then debates whether the {\i tikkun olam} language of the Mishnah refers to the earlier cases \u8212- in which the prohibition on remarriage was itself the {\i tikkun olam} \u8212- or to this final case, in which the permission to remarry represents the {\i tikkun olam}. Rav Sheshet says the language refers to the opening of the Mishnah, the cases where remarriage is prohibited. Ravina says it refers to the final case: {\i ein bezo mipnei tikkun olam} \u8212- in the case of the husband\u8217's vow, there was no sufficient {\i tikkun olam} concern to prevent remarriage, and so the sages left the default in place.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Aylonit Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishnah Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The next Mishnah introduces a third scenario: {\i hamotzi et ishto mishum aylonit} \u8212- one who divorces his wife because she is an {\i aylonit}. An {\i aylonit} is a woman who does not develop female physical characteristics \u8212- she does not mature sexually in the expected way, presenting what might today be described as a failure to develop secondary sexual characteristics, and possibly some characteristics typically associated with males such as a deep voice. Rabbi Yehuda says: {\i lo yachazir} \u8212- he cannot remarry her. The Sages say: {\i yachazir} \u8212- he may.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 An important preliminary observation from Tosafot: in many contexts in the Talmud it appears that when a woman turns out to be an {\i aylonit} unknown to her husband at the time of the marriage, the marriage itself is void \u8212- a {\i mekach taut}, a fundamental error \u8212- and no {\i get} is required. Rashi argues that after {\i nissu\u8217'in}, once the couple has consummated the marriage, the husband\u8217's reservations are presumed to be set aside, because he would not want the marriage to be retroactively void and his {\i bi\u8217'ah} to be {\i bi\u8217'at znut}. Tosafot rejects this: there are cases where even after consummation the error is so fundamental that the marriage is deemed void. The {\i aylonit} case is one of them. Therefore, Tosafot explains, the Mishnah\u8217's discussion of a {\i get} presupposes that she has not been definitively identified as an {\i aylonit} \u8212- it is a case of suspicion or reasonable doubt that she is one, not a case of certainty.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Wife Remarries\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Mishnah proceeds: {\i niset le\u8217'acher ve\u8217'hayu lo banim heimena} \u8212- she subsequently married another man and had children with him. This sequence of events retroactively demonstrates that she was {\i not} an {\i aylonit} after all \u8212- she bore children, which an {\i aylonit} cannot do. Now she returns to claim her {\i ketubah} from her first husband: since it is now apparent that she was not an {\i aylonit}, the premise of the divorce was false, and she is entitled to her {\i ketubah}, which presumably was not paid when he divorced her on account of a perceived defect on her part.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Silence Strategy\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yehuda counsels: {\i ashtikusaich yafeh midiburaich} \u8212- your silence is better than your speech. He advises the woman not to press her claim too vigorously, because by loudly insisting that she was not an {\i aylonit}, she risks reopening the entire question of the validity of the original divorce. If the divorce is called into question, and she is found to have remarried while the {\i get} was in doubt, serious consequences follow. Better, Rabbi Yehuda advises, to quietly accept the situation and not pursue the {\i ketubah} in court.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Reconciliation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara notes an apparent contradiction. Rabbi Yehuda in the present Mishnah seems to be concerned about {\i kilkul} \u8212- the danger that the husband will raise questions about the divorce \u8212- since that is precisely the concern underlying his advice to the woman to remain silent. But earlier, in the Mishnah about {\i shem ra} and {\i nedarim}, it was the {\i Rabbanan} who prohibited remarriage out of concern for {\i kilkul}, while Rabbi Yehuda was the one who permitted remarriage when the vow was not publicly known \u8212- suggesting that Rabbi Yehuda was {\i not} concerned about {\i kilkul}. Conversely, in the present Mishnah the {\i Chachamim} permit remarriage, suggesting they are {\i not} concerned about {\i kilkul}. The two Mishnahs seem to swap the positions of Rabbi Yehuda and the Sages entirely.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara initially proposes simply reversing the attributions ({\i ipoch}), but this creates further problems, since the end of the present Mishnah \u8212- where Rabbi Yehuda counsels silence \u8212- is clearly attributable to Rabbi Yehuda and clearly reflects a concern for {\i kilkul}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Abyei Analysis\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye resolves the contradiction without reversing any attribution. {\i L\u8217'olam lo tipoch} \u8212- leave the text as it stands. In the earlier Mishnah, Rabbi Yehuda\u8217's position on {\i nedarim} combined elements of two different Tannaic positions: in cases where the vow required a sage to annul ({\i tzarich chacham}), he holds like Rabbi Elazar \u8212- the husband would not be believed to claim {\i had I known you could go to a sage, I wouldn\u8217't have divorced you}, because a husband would not want his wife making the rounds of rabbinic courts. And in cases where the husband himself could have annulled the vow ({\i eino tzarich chacham}), he holds like Rabbi Meir \u8212- who says that of course you knew you could have annulled it, so your claim carries no weight. The practical result is that in the {\i neder} case, Rabbi Yehuda allows remarriage because whatever claim the husband might make will not be believed. But this is not because Rabbi Yehuda is categorically unconcerned about {\i kilkul}; it is because, in those specific cases, the {\i kilkul} concern lacks practical force. Where it does have practical force \u8212- as in the {\i aylonit} case \u8212- Rabbi Yehuda is indeed concerned.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava adds a further dimension: even granting Abaye\u8217's reconciliation of Rabbi Yehuda\u8217's positions, what about the Rabbanan? In the earlier Mishnah they prohibited remarriage out of concern for {\i kilkul}, yet here they permit it by a husband who divorced an {\i aylonit}. Are they not equally concerned that he will later say {\i had I known she wasn\u8217't an aylonit, I wouldn\u8217't have divorced her}? Rava answers: {\i man Chachamim? Rabbi Meir} \u8212- the {\i Chachamim} who permit remarriage in the {\i aylonit} case are identified as Rabbi Meir, who holds that conditions which were not formally articulated carry no legal weight. Whatever was in the husband\u8217's mind at the time of the divorce, unless he articulated a proper {\i tnai}, it is irrelevant to the validity of the {\i get}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tnai Kafful\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Meir requires {\i tnai kafful} \u8212- a double condition \u8212- for any condition to be legally operative. A proper {\i tnai} must be stated in both its positive and negative forms: \u8220"if this is the case, the {\i get} takes effect; if this is not the case, the {\i get} does not take effect.\u8221" Without this formulation, no mental reservation, however sincere, can undermine the legal act. Therefore, even if the husband genuinely would not have divorced her had he known she was not an {\i aylonit}, this is of no halachic consequence: he failed to articulate a {\i tnai kafful}, and the {\i get} is completely valid. Any retroactive claim he makes will be met with the response: whatever your unexpressed intentions were, you did not make the proper conditions, and therefore the {\i get} stands. {\i Devarim she\u8217'belev einam devarim} \u8212- what remains in the heart, unspoken and unformalized, has no legal standing.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is a position of considerable stringency and clarity: the halachic act speaks for itself. Subjective intention that is not properly expressed and formalized in the act itself simply does not alter its legal character. Rabbi Meir\u8217's insistence on {\i tnai kafful} here serves a protective function \u8212- not for the husband who might want to reclaim his wife, but for the wife who acted in good faith on the basis of a {\i get} she received and whose subsequent life and children should not be placed in jeopardy by the husband\u8217's retroactive assertions.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Final Resolution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Chachamim} thus permit remarriage in the {\i aylonit} case for a straightforward reason: since no properly formalized condition was attached to the {\i get} at the time it was given, the husband\u8217's claimed intentions are irrelevant. Even if he goes around asserting that he never would have divorced her had he known, that claim can get no traction. Nobody will question the validity of the {\i get} on that basis, because the recognized rule \u8212- that {\i tnai} without {\i tnai kafful} is ineffective \u8212- forecloses it. There is accordingly no danger of {\i kilkul}, and therefore no basis for the prohibition on remarriage. The {\i get} was valid; she was free to remarry; and the husband is free to take her back.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Gittin 47\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Self-Sale to Non-Jews\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishna Intro\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The daf opens near the bottom of 46b with the {\i mishna}: {\i hamocher atzmo u\u8217'vanav l\u8217'ovdei kochavim} \u8212- one who sells himself and his children to non-Jews. The broader sugya has been preoccupied with slaves, freeing slaves, and various forms of {\i tikkun olam}, and the theme of redeeming captives has run throughout. Now those threads converge: a Jew, presumably driven by severe poverty, sells himself \u8212- and his children \u8212- into some form of servitude to non-Jews. Whether this is outright slavery or a form of indentured servitude, the person cannot simply leave; he must be redeemed by others paying to release him.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Logic of Prohibition\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna}\u8217's ruling is stark: {\i ein podin oto} \u8212- we do not redeem him. This seems harsh, but the logic is coherent. Unlike the case of captives seized by enemies \u8212- where redeeming them risks encouraging future raids \u8212- here there is only one behavior we wish to discourage: voluntarily selling oneself. If a person knows the community will feel compelled to redeem him through {\i pidyon shvuyim}, the decision to sell himself becomes far easier to make. Removing that safety net imposes real consequences on the choice.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might protest that the result is terrible: the person will violate {\i Shabbat}, eat {\i treifot}, and be cut off from Jewish communal life. But {\i tikkun olam} is not always a gentle enterprise. Sometimes it means accepting the immediate suffering of individuals in order to prevent a deeper, systemic deterioration. A comparable logic appears in the {\i gemara} about {\i Shema}: some hold that if one recited {\i Shema} without {\i kavana}, one does not repeat it \u8212- not because repetition is categorically forbidden, but because the permission to repeat would make lack of {\i kavana} far more common. Hard rules sometimes prevent worse outcomes.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might further ask whether the community bears some responsibility for allowing a member to reach such a state of poverty. This is a genuine question, and in cases where the community\u8217's own negligence contributed to someone\u8217's predicament, there may be a stronger immediate obligation to act. The case is not unlike contemporary situations where individuals travel to dangerous places against advice and are subsequently seized \u8212- the degree of personal responsibility affects how strongly the obligation of rescue falls on others. Here, however, the concern is not a one-time lapse of judgment but a broader societal problem: if people know they will be redeemed, they will keep selling themselves, and the community will be perpetually burdened by rescuing people from consequences of their own choices.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Children Redeemed\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} continues: {\i aval podin et habanim le\u8217'achar mitat avihem} \u8212- but we do redeem the children after the father\u8217's death. The children did not choose this situation; they were brought into it. Still, the question arises: why wait until the father dies? One reading in the {\i gemara} is that as long as the father lives, he can at least protect them to some degree. Another reading preserves the deterrent logic: even if we will eventually redeem the children, knowing that it will not happen in your own lifetime makes the decision to sell them that much more consequential and harder to rationalize.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Assi Limit\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} immediately softens the ruling. {\i Amar Rav Assi: v\u8217'hu shemachar v\u8217'shana v\u8217'shilesh} \u8212- this applies only to one who sold himself once, twice, and three times. Only after the third instance, establishing a {\i chazakah} of repeated behavior, does the community withhold redemption. The push-and-pull is striking: the {\i mishna} states the rule in its starkest form, and the {\i gemara} at once pulls it back, confining the harshness to truly habitual offenders.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Debt Seizure Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Michsi Story\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now presents a case. There were people from the city of {\i Michsi} who had borrowed money from non-Jews and could not repay them. The creditors came and seized the borrowers themselves as human collateral \u8212- effectively enslaving them as payment of the debt. These people retained enough freedom of movement to come before Rav Huna and ask the community to redeem them.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Huna Refuses\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Huna responded: {\i mai a\u8217'avid lachem} \u8212- what can I do for you? He cited the {\i mishna}\u8217's rule: {\i hamocher atzmo u\u8217'vanav l\u8217'ovdei kochavim ein podin oto}. The case is not identical to voluntary sale \u8212- being seized as debt collateral involves at least some degree of negligence rather than pure intention \u8212- but Rav Huna treated it as falling within the {\i mishna}\u8217's scope. One might note the distinction: these people were not deliberately selling themselves; they simply borrowed money they could not repay, and if there was culpability it was more negligence than design. Then again, if one borrows from creditors who are known to seize people as debt payment, that negligence carries its own moral weight.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Abba Objects\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Abba challenged Rav Huna directly: {\i limadtenu Rabbeinu} \u8212- but our Master, you yourself taught us {\i v\u8217'hu shemachar v\u8217'shana v\u8217'shilesh}, that the rule applies only to one who has done this repeatedly. Why are you applying the {\i mishna}\u8217's blanket prohibition here?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Habitual Behavior\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Huna replied: {\i hani marga regili d\u8217'avdi hachi} \u8212- these people are accustomed to doing this. They have a pattern. Whether this means they had been seized by creditors two or three times previously, or simply that their pattern of borrowing beyond their means from such creditors had become habitual, is not entirely resolved by the {\i gemara}. If they had been physically seized and redeemed multiple times already, the case is merely an illustration of the {\i Rav Assi} limit. But perhaps the pattern of negligent borrowing itself \u8212- even if it had not previously resulted in seizure \u8212- was sufficient to classify them as {\i regila} in this behavior and thus withold redemption. The {\i gemara} leaves this somewhat open.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Ludian Gladiators\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Who Are Ludians\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Another case. {\i Hahu gavra d\u8217'zavin nafshei l\u8217'Luda\u8217'ei} \u8212- a certain person sold himself to the {\i Luda\u8217'ei}. Who are these {\i Luda\u8217'ei}? Rashi identifies them as {\i umah she\u8217'ochlin benei adam} \u8212- a nation that eats human beings, i.e., cannibals. The Soncino translation, drawing on Krauss and Gretz, identifies them as {\i Ludi} \u8212- those who organize gladiatorial contests, specifically the men hired to kill off enraged beasts in the Roman games, or to deliver the finishing stroke. Jastrow similarly associates the term with those involved in Roman games, and notes an additional identification with a group that practiced human sacrifice in their religion. In any case, it is clear that one\u8217's life is in acute danger in the hands of the {\i Luda\u8217'ei}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Ami Rules\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 People who had sold themselves to the {\i Luda\u8217'ei} came before Rabbi Ami and said: {\i pirkin} \u8212- redeem us. Rabbi Ami responded initially by citing the {\i mishna}: {\i hamocher et atzmo v\u8217'et banav l\u8217'Ludim ein podin oto aval podin et habanim} \u8212- we don\u8217't redeem him, but we do redeem the children. His reasoning for why we redeem the children is telling. Rather than saying simply that the children are not at fault, he frames it in terms of {\i kilkula} \u8212- the risk of moral or personal deterioration, including the possibility of abuse, when the father is no longer alive to protect them. When there is a heightened risk to the person\u8217's safety, we redeem regardless.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 From this he argues {\i kol shekein}: all the more so in the present case, where there is {\i katla} \u8212- the threat of actual killing. Whether we are speaking of gladiatorial combat, human sacrifice, or cannibalism, these people\u8217's lives are in immediate danger. The obligation to redeem is overwhelming.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mumarim Objection\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The rabbis objected to Rabbi Ami: {\i v\u8217'hai Yisrael mumar hu d\u8217'chazi lei d\u8217'kachil neveilot u\u8217'treifot} \u8212- this Jew is a {\i mumar}, an apostate, because we have seen that he eats {\i neveilot} and {\i treifot}. We have no obligation toward such a person. The context raises the obvious question of where and when they were observed eating {\i treifot} \u8212- presumably while in the hands of the {\i Luda\u8217'ei}, where their food options were not their own to determine.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Appetite vs Apostasy\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Ami responded: {\i eima l\u8217'teiavon hu d\u8217'kachil} \u8212- perhaps they eat it out of appetite, out of the pressure of circumstance, not out of genuine rejection of Jewish identity. Being given meat and eating it rather than subsisting on vegetables \u8212- especially in an era when vegetarianism was not a realistic option \u8212- does not make one a {\i mumar}. It reflects {\i l\u8217'teiavon}, eating because the food is there and one is not pious enough to refuse it under such extreme conditions. That is very different from {\i mumar l\u8217'hachis}, actively choosing transgression as a statement of defiance.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gemara Counter\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The rabbis pushed back: {\i v\u8217'zimnin d\u8217'ika heteira v\u8217'issura kamei v\u8217'shavik heteira v\u8217'achil issura} \u8212- there are occasions when both kosher and non-kosher food are placed before them simultaneously, and they abandon the kosher food and eat the non-kosher. This is not appetite-driven eating under constraint; this is a deliberate choice. The scenario the {\i gemara} envisions is not choosing ham over beef for reasons of taste \u8212- it is being presented with two identical cuts of meat, one properly slaughtered and one not, and choosing the {\i treifa} when the kosher option is equally available. That, the {\i gemara} suggests, is {\i mumar l\u8217'hachis} \u8212- or at minimum a category of complete indifference to {\i mitzvot} that places one outside communal obligation. It is not that one actively seeks to spite God; one simply no longer feels bound by {\i kashrut} at all. In the period of the {\i gemara}, that level of indifference \u8212- choosing an {\i aveirah} when the {\i mitzvah} is just as easy \u8212- was sufficient to classify someone as {\i chutz l\u8217'kahal}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Ami Surrenders\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Ami\u8217's response is plaintive: {\i zil lo shavku li d\u8217'afrokinach} \u8212- go, they are not letting me redeem you. The other rabbis have made it halachically impossible for him to act, even though he clearly wishes to. The {\i gemara} does not record him invoking {\i pikuach nefesh} as an override \u8212- perhaps because the categorization of such a person as approaching the status of a {\i min} removes even that obligation in this context, given other {\i gemarot} that treat a {\i min} differently with respect to life-saving. The story ends on a somber note.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Reish Lakish Story\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 RL Context\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now introduces a famous biographical episode. {\i Reish Lakish zavin nafshei l\u8217'Luda\u8217'ei} \u8212- Reish Lakish sold himself to the {\i Luda\u8217'ei}. Tosafot is quick to clarify: {\i kodem shechazar l\u8217'mutav} \u8212- this was before he became a {\i frum} Jew, in his earlier days as a bandit. Once he had joined the rabbinic class, one cannot imagine him selling himself to gladiators. This was the younger, rougher Reish Lakish.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Preparation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Reish Lakish came prepared. {\i Shakal bahadei chiyusa v\u8217'gilgla} \u8212- he took with him a sack and a stone inside the sack. He knew \u8212- {\i gmiri}, it is a known tradition \u8212- that on the final day, {\i yoma batra}, the day appointed for one\u8217's death in the arena, the {\i Ludim} would grant any last request. Why? {\i Ki heichi de\u8217'leichlu adamei} \u8212- so that his blood would not be on their hands, so they could say they had been generous to him at the end, that they bore him no ill will. This custom of a final request was his opportunity.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Final Request\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 On his last day, they asked him: {\i mai nicha lach} \u8212- what would you like? He answered: {\i be\u8217'ina akmitinchu} \u8212- I want to bind you all, {\i v\u8217'osvinchu} \u8212- and have you sit down, {\i v\u8217'chol chad minaikhu amchei chiyusa u\u8217'palga} \u8212- and I want to strike each of you one and a half times with my sack. His captors agreed readily. What harm could it do? This is a man who wants to feel powerful for a moment before he dies \u8212- let him slap us around. They did not reckon with the stone inside the sack.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Execution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Kamtinehu v\u8217'osvinhu} \u8212- he bound them and had them sit down. Then, {\i kad machyei chad chiyusa} \u8212- when he struck each one with the sack, {\i nafak nishmatei} \u8212- either they died outright or were rendered unconscious, stunned or paralyzed. Soncino translates this as fainting; either way, they were incapacitated and could not reverse the situation since they were already bound.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One of them, however, gritted his teeth at Reish Lakish. He demanded: {\i achucha kamachaychat bi} \u8212- are you laughing at me? And he announced: {\i akati pash lach gabai palga dechiyusa} \u8212- I still have half a strike coming to you. And with that, {\i katlinhu kulhu} \u8212- whether he had merely stunned them initially, his rage at what he perceived as mockery led him to finish the job and kill them all.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Aftermath\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Nafak v\u8217'ata yasiv ke\u8217'achel veshasei} \u8212- he escaped, returned home, and sat down to eat and drink. His daughter asked him: {\i lo ba\u8217'yat midi lemizgal lei} \u8212- don\u8217't you need something to recline upon? He replied: {\i bitti, kareisi kari} \u8212- my daughter, my stomach is my pillow. (Other stories in the {\i gemara} similarly describe Reish Lakish as a man of considerable physical bulk.)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Self-Quotation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 When Reish Lakish died, {\i shavak kava demurika} \u8212- all he left behind was a measure of saffron. It should be noted that saffron is extraordinarily valuable, more so by weight than gold even in antiquity. He applied to himself the verse from Tehillim: {\i ki yireh chachamim yamutu yachad kesil uva\u8217'ar yovedu ve\u8217'azvu le\u8217'acherim cheilam} \u8212- for one sees that even the wise die, that the fool and the brute alike perish and leave their wealth to others. The {\i pasuk} is recited at a {\i beit avel} and carries a tone of futility: what good does accumulated wealth do you when you die and others inherit it? In context, Reish Lakish seems to be reflecting on the lesson of his life \u8212- eat, drink, live fully now, and don\u8217't accumulate wealth for the benefit of others. Whether the measure of saffron was all he had to his name \u8212- consistent with the portrait of a man who spent freely and saved nothing \u8212- or whether it was itself a treasure, the {\i pasuk} he chose speaks to the philosophy of a man who never hoarded.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Land to Non-Jews\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishna Land Sale\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now pivots from the sale of persons to the sale of land. The {\i mishna}: {\i hamocher et sadehu l\u8217'ovdei kochavim} \u8212- one who sells his field to non-Jews. We are speaking of a field in {\i Eretz Yisrael}. The concern is twofold: there is the {\i mitzvah} dimension \u8212- selling to a non-Jew threatens to remove the land from its obligations of {\i terumot u\u8217'ma\u8217'asrot} \u8212- and there is the societal dimension, the concern for maintaining a Jewish presence in the land. Masechet Avodah Zarah makes clear that selling land, slaves, and livestock to non-Jews is deeply disfavored precisely because those are the elements that establish the Jewish character of the land.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two Readings\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Lokeiach u\u8217'meivi mimenu bikkurim mipnei tikkun ha\u8217'olam} \u8212- the seller is to purchase and bring {\i bikkurim} from it, for the sake of {\i tikkun ha\u8217'olam}. There are at least two ways to read this {\i mishna}. Rashi reads {\i lokeiach} as part of the {\i takana} itself: we require the seller to go back each year and purchase the first fruits from the non-Jew so that he can bring them as {\i bikkurim}. The repurchase is mandatory. The alternative reading is {\i ha\u8217'lokeiach meivi mimenu bikkurim}: there is no obligation to purchase, but if you did purchase the fruit from the non-Jew, you must still treat it as carrying a {\i bikkurim} obligation. The {\i tikkun ha\u8217'olam} consists in maintaining the land\u8217's {\i kedusha} by ensuring {\i bikkurim} continues to be brought from it, not necessarily in compelling a buyback.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now enters one of the central debates of {\i Shas}. Rava states: {\i af al pi she\u8217'ein kinyan l\u8217'oved kochavim b\u8217'Eretz Yisrael l\u8217'hafki\u8217'a mi\u8217'yedei ma\u8217'aser} \u8212- even though a non-Jew owns land in {\i Eretz Yisrael}, this does not alter the land\u8217's {\i kedusha} status. Grain grown in that field remains obligated in {\i terumot u\u8217'ma\u8217'asrot}, even if the non-Jew will not in practice separate them. The source: {\i ki li ha\u8217'aretz} \u8212- for the land is Mine. God\u8217's {\i kedusha} inheres in the land regardless of who holds legal title. {\i Li kedushat ha\u8217'aretz} \u8212- the sanctity of the land remains His.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Yet at the same time, Rava holds, {\i yesh kinyan l\u8217'oved kochavim b\u8217'Eretz Yisrael lachpor bah borot shichin u\u8217'me\u8217'arot} \u8212- a non-Jew does acquire full property rights in {\i Eretz Yisrael} for purposes of digging pits, cisterns, and caves, i.e., making structural changes to the land. He has absolute {\i dinei mamonot} ownership. The source: {\i hashamayim shamayim l\u8217'Hashem v\u8217'ha\u8217'aretz natan livnei adam} \u8212- the heavens belong to God, but the earth He gave to human beings. Ownership as a legal matter is fully transferable; {\i kedusha}, however, is not.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Eliezer\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Eliezer holds the precise inverse. {\i Af al pi she\u8217'yesh kinyan l\u8217'ovdei kochavim b\u8217'Eretz Yisrael l\u8217'hafki\u8217'a mi\u8217'yedei ma\u8217'aser} \u8212- non-Jewish ownership does affect the land\u8217's status, and grain grown while the land is in a non-Jew\u8217's possession is exempt from {\i terumot u\u8217'ma\u8217'asrot}. The source: {\i degancha v\u8217'lo degan ovdei kochavim} \u8212- your grain, not the grain of non-Jews. But Rabbi Eliezer simultaneously holds that {\i ein kinyan l\u8217'ovdei kochavim b\u8217'Eretz Yisrael lachpor borot shichin u\u8217'me\u8217'arot} \u8212- a non-Jew cannot make structural changes to the land, because the land belongs to God and therefore a non-Jew never acquires full title. His {\i kinyan} is more like a {\i kinyan peirot} \u8212- a right of use \u8212- without the deeper property ownership. The source for this limitation: {\i l\u8217'Hashem ha\u8217'aretz}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Conceptual Framework\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 An important question lurks beneath Rabbi Eliezer\u8217's position. When non-Jewish ownership exempts the grain from {\i terumot u\u8217'ma\u8217'asrot}, does this reflect a fundamental claim that the land loses its {\i kedusha} under non-Jewish ownership? Or is it a more technical point: the {\i pasuk} defines {\i chiyuv} in terms of {\i degancha}, your grain, and if the grain grew while owned by a non-Jew, it fails that condition \u8212- just as grain that grew while the field was {\i hefker} or {\i hekdesh} might similarly be exempt for technical reasons without implying any diminishment of the land\u8217's metaphysical holiness?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The same ambiguity applies by analogy to {\i hefker} and {\i hekdesh}: if grain grew when the field was declared ownerless or consecrated, it may be exempt from {\i terumot u\u8217'ma\u8217'asrot}, but no one claims those categories remove the land\u8217's {\i kedusha}. So the exemption under non-Jewish ownership need not be conceptualized as a loss of {\i kedusha} \u8212- it could simply be a technical matter of who holds ownership at the moment of critical halachic obligation. The {\i gemara} will continue to press this question.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 TuM Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava\u8217's Proof\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} attempts to identify the pivot of the dispute and then to marshal proof. {\i B\u8217'mai kamifligi?} What is at stake between the two positions? One position learns from {\i degancha v\u8217'lo degan ovdei kochavim} that the critical moment is when the grain reaches {\i shlish}, one-third ripening. At that point, if a non-Jew owns the field, the grain is exempt from {\i terumot u\u8217'ma\u8217'asrot} for the entire subsequent growth, regardless of who later owns it. The other position reads {\i degancha} as referring not to the moment of {\i shlish} but to the moment of {\i digun} \u8212- the smoothing and piling of the winnowed grain, the {\i miru\u8217'ach} \u8212- which is the final critical stage, after which the grain is fully {\i tevel} and eating from it without separating {\i terumot u\u8217'ma\u8217'asrot} carries severe consequences. On this reading, what matters is not who owned the field when it became a third ripe, but who performed the final smoothing-out. If a non-Jew did the {\i digun}, the grain is exempt; otherwise, it is not. Under this view, the grain could grow and ripen entirely in a non-Jew\u8217's hands, but if a Jew does the final {\i miru\u8217'ach}, it would still be fully obligated.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 With this framework established, Rava declares: {\i mina amina lah} \u8212- where do I derive my position? From a {\i mishna}: {\i leket shikcha u\u8217'pe\u8217'ah shel oved kochavim chayavim b\u8217'ma\u8217'aser ela im ken hefkir} \u8212- if a non-Jew leaves {\i leket}, {\i shikcha}, and {\i pe\u8217'ah} in his field, the grain is still obligated in {\i ma\u8217'aser}, unless he truly declared it {\i hefker}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two Interpretations\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} immediately asks: {\i heichi dami} \u8212- what exactly is the case? There are two possible readings. The first: the field belongs to a Jew, and the non-Jew is the impoverished collector \u8212- the {\i ani} \u8212- who gathers the {\i leket shikcha u\u8217'pe\u8217'ah} left by the Jewish owner. But the {\i gemara} challenges this reading: the Jew has already been {\i mafkir} the grain by leaving it as {\i pe\u8217'ah}; the grain is already {\i hefker}. Why should the fact that a non-Jew picked it up make it obligated in {\i ma\u8217'aser}? And what would it mean to say the Jew can be {\i mafkir} it again? He already was {\i mafkir} it. This reading collapses.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} therefore concludes: {\i ela lav}, the case must be that the non-Jew owns the field and leaves {\i leket shikcha u\u8217'pe\u8217'ah}, and the Jew comes and collects it. Now the {\i mishna}\u8217's logic becomes clear: the non-Jew declared what he thought was {\i pe\u8217'ah}, but halachically, grain growing in a non-Jew\u8217's field does not have the status of {\i pe\u8217'ah} \u8212- there is no {\i mitzvah} of {\i pe\u8217'ah} incumbent upon a non-Jew. So when the non-Jew left the corners of his field unharvested and invited poor Jews to collect, he was not in fact rendering the grain {\i hefker} through the mechanism of {\i pe\u8217'ah}, because {\i pe\u8217'ah} by a non-Jew carries no such legal effect. The grain remained in his ownership and was transferred to the Jew as something closer to a {\i matana} \u8212- a gift. Since it was never truly {\i hefker}, it retains its obligation of {\i terumot u\u8217'ma\u8217'asrot}. And the {\i mishna} says it is only exempt if the non-Jew explicitly declared full {\i hefker} \u8212- renouncing ownership entirely to anyone, Jew or non-Jew alike. This would support Rava\u8217's position that non-Jewish ownership does not remove the land\u8217's obligation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Intent Reading\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 At this point the {\i gemara} considers a more nuanced reading of the case where the field belongs to a Jew and the non-Jew is the collector. On this reading, when the Jewish farmer left {\i pe\u8217'ah}, he declared it {\i hefker} with a certain intent: {\i adaita d\u8217'Yisrael} \u8212- specifically with the understanding that Jews would come to collect it. The {\i pe\u8217'ah} was meant for Jewish {\i aniyim}. When a non-Jew comes and takes it, the {\i hefker} did not apply to him \u8212- he was not within the class of recipients for whom the {\i hefker} was declared. Therefore, vis-\u224 ?-vis the non-Jew, the grain was never truly {\i hefker}; it is as if he took the Jew\u8217's property, and if he now sells it to a Jew, that Jew must separate {\i terumot u\u8217'ma\u8217'asrot} because the grain was {\i tevel} all along.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna}\u8217's caveat \u8212- {\i ela im ken hefkir}, unless he declared {\i hefker} \u8212- means: unless the Jew explicitly declared unconditional {\i hefker}, open to all comers regardless of identity. In that case, even if a non-Jew takes it, it genuinely was {\i hefker} for him as well, and the grain is exempt. But a {\i pe\u8217'ah} declaration, which by its nature is directed toward the Jewish poor, does not accomplish universal {\i hefker}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is connected to a broader question about the {\i halacha} of {\i pe\u8217'ah} vis-\u224 ?-vis non-Jews. The {\i pasuk} of {\i v\u8217'achlu evyonei amecha} \u8212- the poor of your people shall eat \u8212- and the narrative in Ruth where {\i Boaz} arranges for Ruth to glean despite her being a foreigner ({\i l\u8217'hakireni v\u8217'anochi nochriya}) raise the question of whether a {\i ger toshav} or an outright {\i nochri} is legally entitled to {\i leket shikcha u\u8217'pe\u8217'ah}, or whether the {\i halacha} is that {\i pe\u8217'ah} goes to Jews and non-Jews collect only by way of {\i darkhei shalom}. If the latter, then the non-Jew\u8217's taking of {\i pe\u8217'ah} is not grounded in {\i hefker} regardless of the farmer\u8217's subjective intent \u8212- the {\i halacha} itself defines the class of recipients, and vis-\u224 ?-vis anyone outside that class the grain is not {\i hefker}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The practical consequence: if a non-Jew collected what a Jew left as {\i pe\u8217'ah} and ate it himself, nothing further is required \u8212- it is as if some of the farmer\u8217's grain was lost. But if that non-Jew sells the grain in the marketplace and a Jew purchases it, the Jew must separate {\i terumot u\u8217'ma\u8217'asrot}, because the grain was {\i tevel} when the non-Jew acquired it and remains so.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Suria Limitation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having explored these readings, the {\i gemara} then attempts to use other {\i mishnayot} as proof for Rabbi Eliezer\u8217's position that non-Jewish ownership does exempt from {\i terumot u\u8217'ma\u8217'asrot}. One such source: a Jew who bought a field from a non-Jew before the grain reached {\i shlish}, then sold it back after {\i shlish} \u8212- the grain is obligated because it hit the critical moment of {\i shlish} in Jewish hands. The implication is clear: had it reached {\i shlish} while still in the non-Jew\u8217's possession, it would be exempt.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} deflects: {\i hacha b\u8217'mai askinan b\u8217'Suryia} \u8212- we are not speaking of {\i Eretz Yisrael} proper, but of {\i Suria}, the lands conquered by King David. Those lands carry only {\i kedusha mi\u8217'derabbanan} \u8212- rabbinic-level holiness \u8212- because {\i kibush yachid lo shmei kibush}, the conquest by an individual monarch without the full national participation that characterized the original conquest of Canaan does not confer the same {\i kedusha d\u8217'Oraita} as {\i Eretz Yisrael} proper. In {\i Suria}, therefore, non-Jewish ownership does exempt from {\i terumot u\u8217'ma\u8217'asrot} even under Rava\u8217's framework, because the {\i kedusha} there is rabbinic and susceptible to such qualification.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mixed Ownership\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A further proof is attempted from the case of a Jew and non-Jew who own a field jointly, {\i b\u8217'shutafut}. Rebbi holds: {\i tevel v\u8217'chullin me\u8217'uravim zeh ba\u8217'zeh} \u8212- each kernel of grain is simultaneously half {\i tevel} (obligated in {\i terumot u\u8217'ma\u8217'asrot}) and half {\i chullin} (exempt), since each owner holds a half-share. Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel disagrees: the grain that the Jew takes home is fully {\i chayav}, and the grain the non-Jew takes home is fully exempt \u8212- based on the principle of {\i yeish breirah}, the retroactive clarification that what each party takes at division was always theirs from the moment of growth.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} notes: {\i aval di\u8217'chulei alma yesh kinyan l\u8217'oved kochavim b\u8217'Eretz Yisrael l\u8217'hafki\u8217'a mi\u8217'yad ma\u8217'aser} \u8212- regardless of the dispute about {\i yeish breirah}, it is taken for granted by all parties that non-Jewish ownership exempts from {\i terumot u\u8217'ma\u8217'asrot}. This is strong proof for Rabbi Eliezer\u8217's position. The {\i gemara} again deflects: {\i hacha nami b\u8217'Suryia v\u8217'kasavar kibush yachid lo shmei kibush}. Any case that seems to prove non-Jewish ownership exempts from {\i terumot u\u8217'ma\u8217'asrot} can be limited to {\i Suria}, where such exemption is uncontested. This retreat to {\i Suria} is the {\i gemara}\u8217's consistent escape hatch \u8212- though it should be noted that {\i pshat} strongly favors the exemption as applying in {\i Eretz Yisrael} proper as well.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Takkanot Reversal\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Original Obligation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Chiya bar Avin now invokes our {\i mishna} itself as evidence: {\i hamocher sadeh l\u8217'oved kochavim lokeiach u\u8217'meivi bikkurim mipnei tikkun ha\u8217'olam} \u8212- one who sells his field to a non-Jew purchases and brings {\i bikkurim} from it because of {\i tikkun ha\u8217'olam}. The language {\i mipnei tikkun ha\u8217'olam} implies that {\i me\u8217'ikar ha\u8217'din} \u8212- as a matter of strict {\i d\u8217'Oraita} law \u8212- there would be no obligation to bring {\i bikkurim}. The {\i tikkun ha\u8217'olam} is what creates or restores the obligation. This supports the view that non-Jewish ownership does in principle exempt from such obligations, and the Rabbis are overriding that exemption through {\i tikkun ha\u8217'olam}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} accepts this framing: {\i mid\u8217'Oraita lo} \u8212- biblically it is not obligated; {\i mipnei tikkun ha\u8217'olam in} \u8212- it is the {\i takana} that mandates it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 First Takkana\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But Rav Ashi complicates the picture by introducing a two-stage history: {\i shtei takanos havu} \u8212- there were actually two distinct {\i takkanot}. Originally, the law was in accordance with Rava: the land retained its {\i kedusha} even when sold to a non-Jew, and grain grown on it was {\i d\u8217'Oraita} obligated in {\i terumot u\u8217'ma\u8217'asrot} and {\i bikkurim}. Sellers would buy back the first fruits and bring {\i bikkurim}, and this was entirely {\i d\u8217'Oraita}. But the Rabbis observed that this very continuity of {\i kedusha} \u8212- the fact that one could still bring {\i bikkurim} \u8212- made it too easy to sell the land. People felt the sale was not so consequential: the {\i kedusha} would remain, they could always buy back the fruit and bring {\i bikkurim}, and everything would be fine. So the Rabbis enacted a {\i takana}: do not bring {\i bikkurim} from such land. Remove that comfort. Make the sale feel irreversible in practice.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Stringency Failed\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The problem is that {\i takkanot} intended to deter a behavior sometimes fail to do so. {\i Kivun de\u8217'hazei de\u8217'man de\u8217'lo sagi lei mazvin v\u8217'kamishtakei b\u8217'yad ovdei kochavim} \u8212- when the Rabbis saw that people who had no financial alternative were selling their land anyway, regardless of the consequences, the {\i takana} had not achieved its goal. The land was still passing into non-Jewish hands, but now {\i bikkurim} was not being brought from it either. This is the worst of both worlds: the problem the {\i takana} was meant to solve was not solved, and an additional loss \u8212- the cessation of {\i bikkurim} \u8212- had been created as a collateral consequence.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Reversal\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Hadaru v\u8217'takinu lehu de\u8217'ayisu} \u8212- the Rabbis therefore reversed the first {\i takana} and reinstated the obligation to bring {\i bikkurim}. The result is what the {\i mishna} records as {\i tikkun ha\u8217'olam}: the obligation to purchase the fruit and bring {\i bikkurim} is not a new rabbinic creation but rather the restoration of the {\i d\u8217'Oraita} situation, now enacted as a {\i takana} because the intermediate prohibition had been in force. On this reading, the {\i mishna}\u8217's language of {\i tikkun ha\u8217'olam} is somewhat ironic \u8212- it is {\i tikkun ha\u8217'olam} in the sense of reversing a failed rabbinic intervention and returning to the original state.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tikkun Olam\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This episode illuminates the broader theme of {\i tikkun ha\u8217'olam} that has run throughout this chapter of Gittin. {\i Tikkun olam} is not a one-directional enterprise. It sometimes means imposing harsh consequences \u8212- withholding redemption, prohibiting {\i bikkurim}, refusing to readmit a divorced wife \u8212- in order to address deep systemic problems. But systemic interventions do not always succeed. Economic and social realities can overwhelm even well-designed deterrents. When the Rabbis saw that their stringency was producing only harm without any corresponding benefit, they reversed course. The willingness to reverse a {\i takana} is itself an act of {\i tikkun olam} \u8212- recognizing that a societal remedy that does not remedy must be abandoned, even at the cost of acknowledging that the initial intervention failed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Kinyan Peirot Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Core Dispute\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now transitions to another fundamental debate in {\i Shas}, one that will continue into the next {\i daf}. {\i Itmar: hamocher sadehu l\u8217'peirot} \u8212- if one sells his field {\i l\u8217'peirot}, granting the purchaser {\i kinyan peirot} \u8212- usufruct ownership, the right to benefit from the land\u8217's produce. The buyer owns the land for the purpose of its produce; he cannot make structural changes, and underlying title remains with the seller. The question: can such a buyer bring {\i bikkurim} from that land? Can he recite the declaration of {\i al ha\u8217'adamah asher natata li} \u8212- upon the land which You gave me?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Yochanan\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yochanan says: {\i meivi u\u8217'korei} \u8212- he brings {\i bikkurim} and recites the declaration. {\i Kinyan peirot k\u8217'kinyan haguf dami} \u8212- ownership of the produce is legally equivalent to ownership of the body of the land itself. Since the buyer has the right to all the produce, he has sufficient standing to declare the land as his own for purposes of {\i bikkurim}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Reish Lakish\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Reish Lakish disagrees: {\i meivi v\u8217'eino korei} \u8212- he brings {\i bikkurim} but does not recite the declaration. {\i Kinyan peirot lav k\u8217'kinyan haguf dami} \u8212- usufruct ownership is not legally equivalent to full ownership. The buyer has real rights to the produce, sufficient to create a {\i bikkurim} obligation \u8212- the fruit grew in {\i Eretz Yisrael} under Jewish ownership of some kind \u8212- but he cannot say {\i al ha\u8217'adamah asher natata li}, because he does not hold title to the land itself.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Wife\u8217's Produce\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yochanan challenges Reish Lakish: {\i u\u8217'lveisecha melamed she\u8217'adam meivi bikkurei ishto v\u8217'korei}. The {\i pasuk} regarding {\i bikkurim} reads: {\i v\u8217'samachta b\u8217'chol hatov asher nasan lecha Hashem Elokecha u\u8217'lveisecha} \u8212- you shall rejoice in all the good that God has given you and to your household. The phrase {\i u\u8217'lveisecha}, to your household, is understood to include a man\u8217's wife \u8212- meaning a husband brings {\i bikkurim} from his wife\u8217's fields and recites the declaration. But the husband\u8217's right to his wife\u8217's land is precisely a {\i kinyan peirot}: the wife retains title to her fields, and the husband\u8217's entitlement to their produce is a rabbinic institution \u8212- perhaps reflecting a pre-existing social reality that the {\i Torah} then incorporated \u8212- by which he benefits from those fields without owning them outright. If the husband can say {\i al ha\u8217'adamah asher natata li} over his wife\u8217's land despite holding only {\i kinyan peirot}, this proves that {\i kinyan peirot} is sufficient for the full {\i bikkurim} declaration.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Proof Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Reish Lakish responds: {\i shani hasam d\u8217'chsiv u\u8217'lveisecha} \u8212- that case is different because the {\i Torah} specifically wrote {\i u\u8217'lveisecha}, creating a special rule for the husband. It is not a general principle about {\i kinyan peirot}; it is an exception carved out by the {\i pasuk}. Accordingly, it cannot be used to establish that {\i kinyan peirot} generally suffices for {\i meivi v\u8217'korei}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then records an alternate version of the exchange: {\i ika d\u8217'amri}, some say the proof ran in the opposite direction \u8212- Reish Lakish cited {\i u\u8217'lveisecha} against Rabbi Yochanan, as evidence that the husband can bring his wife\u8217's {\i bikkurim} only because of the special verse, implying that without such a verse {\i kinyan peirot} alone would not suffice. To this Rabbi Yochanan replies: {\i ta\u8217'ama d\u8217'idi nami hacha kamina} \u8212- you think that is an exception? That is my paradigm. The {\i Torah} said {\i u\u8217'lveisecha} and thereby established the rule: one who has {\i kinyan peirot} in a field brings {\i bikkurim} and recites the declaration. The verse does not create an exception; it enunciates the principle. This debate will continue on the following {\i daf}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Gittin 48\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Kinyan Peirot Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kinyan Peirot Intro\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The daf begins nine lines from the bottom of 47b, at the word {\i eitvei}. We are in the midst of an ongoing debate between Rabbi Yochanan and Reish Lakish, which grew out of the Mishnah\u8217's discussion of {\i bikkurim} \u8212- specifically, what happens to the status of fruit for purposes of {\i bikkurim} when a field is sold to a non-Jew. From there the Gemara moved to a broader dispute: if someone sells a field {\i le-peirot} \u8212- meaning the purchaser acquires not actual title to the land but only the right to its produce \u8212- does that fruit-ownership entitle him to be {\i mevi ve-korei}, to bring {\i bikkurim} and recite the declaration {\i ha-adamah asher natata li}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yochanan holds that it does. Since the fruit belongs to the purchaser, he has sufficient ownership and title to the land to declare {\i ha-adamah asher natata li}. Reish Lakish disagrees: although such a person brings the {\i bikkurim} as his own fruit, he cannot recite the declaration, for {\i kinyan peirot lav ke-kinyan haguf dami} \u8212- the ownership of land for the sake of its fruit is not equivalent to actual ownership of the land itself.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Nichsei Milog Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now brings a {\i beraisa} as a potential proof: a man is traveling and carries with him fruit from his wife\u8217's property. This is a case of {\i nichsei milog} \u8212- the wife holds title to the land while the husband holds the right to its produce. The husband thus has exactly what we have been discussing: {\i kinyan peirot} without {\i kinyan haguf}. While he is on the road, he hears that his wife has died. Since he inherits from her, he now acquires full title to the land. The ruling is that he may bring the fruit and recite the declaration.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara draws the obvious inference: {\i meisah in, lo meisah lo} \u8212- the fact that he recites only because his wife died implies that had she not died, he could not have recited it. This supports Reish Lakish: {\i kinyan peirot lav ke-kinyan haguf dami}, and one who holds the field only for its fruit is {\i mevi} but {\i eino korei}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Baraisa Proof\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yochanan rejects this inference. He maintains that even had his wife not died, he could have recited {\i ha-adamah asher natata li}. The reason the {\i beraisa} presents the case where she dies, he explains, is to teach a novel ruling even in that scenario \u8212- namely, that even after the wife\u8217's death, when a status change has occurred, he may still recite the declaration.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might wonder: what is the significance of a status change here, if according to Rabbi Yochanan his relationship to the fruit was unaffected? The answer is that the status change, however subtle, might have been thought to disqualify the recitation because it resembles a case discussed by Rabbi Yose ben Chanina.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shaliach Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yose ben Chanina taught: if the owner harvests the fruit and sends them via a {\i shaliach}, and the {\i shaliach} dies along the way, and the owner then retrieves the fruit and completes the delivery to Jerusalem \u8212- the owner brings the fruit but does not recite the declaration. The reason: {\i ve-lakachta ve-heiveta} \u8212- the Torah requires that the taking and the bringing be performed by one person. When the {\i shaliach} was involved, there were two statuses: the owner performed part of the process, the {\i shaliach} performed part. That interruption, or that divided agency, is enough to preclude the {\i keriah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The cases are not entirely parallel. In the {\i shaliach} case there is a genuinely meaningful change in status, since a {\i shaliach} cannot recite the declaration in any event \u8212- the fruit is not ultimately the {\i shaliach}\u8217's property. In our case, by contrast, according to Rabbi Yochanan the husband could have recited from the outset. Nevertheless, the Gemara explains that Rabbi Yochanan reads the {\i beraisa} as teaching that even the relatively minor status change caused by the wife\u8217's death does not disqualify the recitation \u8212- {\i kamashma lan}. As a straightforward reading of the {\i beraisa}, this is certainly not compelling evidence; it simply shows how Rabbi Yochanan would respond to it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Yovel Extension\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Both Cases Needed\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara notes that this dispute between Rabbi Yochanan and Reish Lakish {\i azlu le-ta\u8217'amaihu} \u8212- follows their respective positions in a parallel debate stated elsewhere. There it was taught: one who sells a field during the period when {\i yovel} is in effect \u8212- and during {\i yovel} any sold field ultimately reverts to its original owner \u8212- does he have sufficient ownership to be {\i mevi ve-korei}? Rabbi Yochanan says yes, {\i kinyan peirot ke-kinyan haguf dami}. Reish Lakish says no, {\i kinyan peirot lav ke-kinyan haguf dami}, and therefore one is {\i mevi} but {\i eino korei}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is worth pausing to note that in the {\i yovel} case the argument for full ownership is considerably stronger than in the {\i peirot} case. When a field is sold during {\i yovel}, the purchaser intends to acquire the land outright; it is only an external legal mechanism \u8212- the institution of {\i yovel} \u8212- that causes eventual reversion. The purchaser has made a genuine land purchase, not merely a contract for fruit rights. One would therefore expect it to be easier to say {\i ha-adamah asher natata li} here.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara accordingly states that both cases are necessary, {\i tzricha}. Had the Gemara only stated the {\i peirot} case, one might have thought Reish Lakish\u8217's ruling is limited to that scenario \u8212- where from the very outset, {\i dachi nachit ada\u8217'ata de-peirei nachit}, the purchaser\u8217's entire relationship to the land was conceived as fruit-rights only. But in the {\i yovel} case, {\i da-ada\u8217'ata de-gufa nachit} \u8212- the purchaser intended to acquire the land itself, and one might have thought even Reish Lakish would agree with Rabbi Yochanan.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Peirot Intent\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Conversely, had only the {\i yovel} case been stated, one might have inferred that Rabbi Yochanan permits the recitation there precisely because the purchaser conceived of himself as acquiring the land. But in the {\i peirot} case, where from the start the acquisition was framed as fruit-rights only, one might have thought even Rabbi Yochanan concedes to Reish Lakish. Hence both cases must be taught.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yovel Intent\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The full scope of the dispute thus emerges: Rabbi Yochanan is willing to call even a purchaser of fruit-rights an owner sufficient to recite {\i ha-adamah asher natata li}, while Reish Lakish maintains a standard so demanding that even a purchaser who intended to acquire the land itself, yet whose ownership is temporally bounded by {\i yovel}, cannot be regarded as a full owner for purposes of {\i keriah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tree Proofs\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara attempts to bring proofs. {\i Ta shema} \u8212- one who purchases a tree along with its underlying ground may bring {\i bikkurim} and recite the declaration. This would seem to show that even a purchaser, when {\i yovel} is practiced, may be {\i mevi ve-korei}. The Gemara deflects: {\i hacha be-mai askinan bi-zman she-ein ha-yovel noheg} \u8212- the case refers to a time when {\i yovel} is no longer in effect, which was true toward the end of the First Temple period when the twelve tribes were no longer all settled on their respective portions of the land.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A further attempt: one who purchases two trees within another\u8217's field is {\i mevi} but {\i eino korei}, since two trees do not convey rights to the land beneath them; but if he purchases three trees \u8212- which, as established elsewhere, carries a presumption that the land between them is included \u8212- he is {\i mevi ve-korei}. Again, the Gemara answers: {\i hacha nami bi-zman she-ein yovel noheg}. Any Mishnaic case in which a purchaser is {\i mevi ve-korei} can simply be referring to a time when {\i yovel} does not apply. It is therefore difficult to bring a counterproof from such sources.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Yovel Rishon\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Pre-Yovel Agreement\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Against this backdrop, Rav Chisda offers a significant teaching: the dispute between Rabbi Yochanan and Reish Lakish applies only from {\i Yovel sheni} \u8212- the second {\i yovel} cycle and beyond. But regarding {\i Yovel rishon} \u8212- the first {\i yovel} cycle ever practiced in the Land of Israel \u8212- {\i divrei ha-kol meivi ve-korei}. Everyone agrees that a purchaser during the first {\i yovel} cycle may bring and recite.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Perception Principle\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Why? Because {\i d-achti lo samcha da\u8217'ataihu} \u8212- people\u8217's minds had not yet settled into confidence that the reversion would actually take place. The institution of {\i yovel} had been proclaimed in the Torah, but it had never been practiced. Neither the buyer nor the seller truly felt certain it would be enforced. In their minds, the sale might well turn out to be permanent. And since their psychological relationship to the land was that of full and final ownership, that perception was sufficient to grant the purchaser the status of {\i kinyan haguf}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is a striking principle. Legally, on paper, the sale was absolutely time-bound and the reversion fully operative. What makes the ownership incomplete in Reish Lakish\u8217's view is not a formal legal deficiency but the lived awareness that the land will not remain permanently in the purchaser\u8217's hands. And when that awareness is absent \u8212- because the institution of {\i yovel} had never yet been tested \u8212- the halakhic assessment shifts accordingly. Perception reshapes the legal relationship to the land.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yovel Divide\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This teaching also resolves the evidentiary problem. Any source that appears to show {\i meivi ve-korei} during {\i yovel} can be explained as referring to {\i Yovel rishon}, where everyone agrees. Any statement that one is {\i mevi} but {\i eino korei} applies from {\i Yovel sheni} onward, and that is the context of Reish Lakish\u8217's ruling. {\i Lo kashya: ha bi-Yovel rishon, ha bi-Yovel sheni.}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Tannaim Parallel\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hekdesh Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now asks whether this debate between Rabbi Yochanan and Reish Lakish parallels a {\i tanna\u8217'im} dispute. Before examining the parallel, it is worth noting that Rabbi Yochanan and Reish Lakish originally spoke only about the narrow question of {\i mevi ve-korei} in {\i bikkurim}. It is the Gemara that has generalized their positions into the broader principle of {\i kinyan peirot ke-kinyan haguf} or {\i lav ke-kinyan haguf}, a principle that could have implications throughout {\i halakha}. Accordingly the Gemara now tries to connect this to other debates.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i beraisa} in question asks: how do we know that if someone purchases a field from his father, sanctifies it, and then his father dies \u8212- all before {\i yovel} arrives \u8212- the field is treated as a {\i sdei achuzah}, an ancestral field? The mechanics here are important. When a person sanctifies a {\i sdei miknah}, a purchased field, to the {\i Beit ha-Mikdash}, that field reverts at {\i yovel} to its original owner, because the sanctifier could not dedicate more than he owned. But when a person sanctifies a {\i sdei achuzah}, his ancestral land, the Torah provides that at {\i yovel} the field goes to the {\i kohanim} and does not revert to the original owner \u8212- the {\i Beit ha-Mikdash} retains it permanently.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 In our case: the son purchased the field from his father during {\i yovel}. As a purchased field, it would have reverted to the father had {\i yovel} come. But the son sanctified it, and before {\i yovel} arrived, the father died. The son now inherits the land outright. When {\i yovel} comes, does it revert \u8212- returning to the \u8220"father,\u8221" who is now dead, and passing from there to the son as heir? Or does it stay with the {\i Beit ha-Mikdash}, since by now the son has full title to it? The {\i beraisa} rules it is treated as a {\i sdei achuzah} and stays with the {\i Beit ha-Mikdash}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The derivation comes from the verse: {\i im sdei miknaso asher lo mi-sdei achuzaso} \u8212- if he sanctifies a field he purchased, which was not a field of his inheritance. The phrase {\i asher lo mi-sdei achuzaso} \u8212- that it was not from his ancestral holdings \u8212- is seen as excluding from the reversion category any field that was either actually ancestral land or had the potential and actualized fitness to become ancestral land.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Timing Issue\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yehuda and Rabbi Shimon interpret the verse to mean: the field reverts only if it was a purchased field lacking even the potential to become ancestral. If it had the potential to become ancestral \u8212- as in our case, where the son\u8217's eventual inheritance of the field was a genuine possibility \u8212- and that potential was actualized by the father\u8217's death, then even if it was sanctified before the father died, it is treated as a {\i sdei achuzah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This raises a structural question: according to Rabbi Yehuda and Rabbi Shimon, why did they need the verse to tell them about the case where the son sanctified first and the father died afterward? Why not begin with the simpler teaching that if the father dies {\i before} the son sanctifies, the sanctification is certainly of a {\i sdei achuzah}? The fact that they go directly to the more complex case \u8212- sanctification first, then the father\u8217's death \u8212- suggests they regarded the simpler case as obvious and requiring no verse.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Meir View\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Meir, by contrast, asks a different question: how do we know that if someone purchases a field from his father, the father then dies, and only afterward does the son sanctify it \u8212- that this constitutes a {\i sdei achuzah}? The verse teaches this explicitly.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But why would Rabbi Meir need a verse for this apparently obvious case? If the father died before the sanctification, the son clearly inherits the land fully; how could one think it is still a {\i sdei miknah}? The Gemara explains: Rabbi Meir holds {\i kinyan peirot ke-kinyan haguf dami}. From the moment the son purchased the field from the father during {\i yovel}, he was already considered the full owner. When the father subsequently died, there was, in a sense, nothing new for the son to inherit with respect to this land \u8212- {\i hu de-lo yaris ve-lo midi}, it is as if he inherited nothing additional. Since he was already treated as the full owner, the father\u8217's death does not produce a change in his legal status. One might therefore have thought that the {\i sdei miknah} status established at the time of purchase persists and is not superseded by the father\u8217's death. The verse is therefore needed to tell us that the {\i sdei achuzah} status does prevail \u8212- that the fact of actual inheritance supersedes the earlier purchase-status, and therefore the sanctification thereafter is of a {\i sdei achuzah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This requirement for a verse demonstrates {\i kinyan peirot ke-kinyan haguf dami} \u8212- Rabbi Meir\u8217's position aligns with Rabbi Yochanan.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Yehuda View\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yehuda and Rabbi Shimon hold {\i kinyan peirot lav ke-kinyan haguf dami}. Therefore, when the son purchased from the father during {\i yovel}, he was not considered the full owner \u8212- only the owner of fruit-rights. When the father died, the son then truly inherited and became the full owner. In that sequence \u8212- purchase, then inheritance before sanctification \u8212- it is entirely obvious that the sanctification is of a {\i sdei achuzah}, and no verse is needed. The verse can therefore be deployed for the more novel case: that even if the son sanctified it while it was still only a {\i sdei miknah}, if the father subsequently dies and the potential for ancestral status is actualized, it is still treated as a {\i sdei achuzah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This positions Rabbi Yehuda and Rabbi Shimon as paralleling Reish Lakish.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Alt. Reading\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Nachman bar Yitzchak proposes an alternative reading. He maintains that Rabbi Yehuda and Rabbi Shimon actually hold {\i kinyan peirot ke-kinyan haguf dami} \u8212- they agree with Rabbi Meir on that underlying principle, and thus align with Rabbi Yochanan. According to this reading, the dispute between Rabbi Meir and Rabbi Yehuda and Rabbi Shimon is not about {\i kinyan peirot} at all.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Pasuk Language\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 How then does Rav Nachman bar Yitzchak explain why Rabbi Yehuda and Rabbi Shimon could skip directly to the more complex case? They found a doubly redundant formulation in the verse. The Torah could have simply said {\i mi-sdei miknaso asher lo achuzaso}. Instead it says {\i asher lo mi-sdei achuzaso} \u8212- the added prefix {\i mi-} makes the phrase {\i mi-sdei achuzaso} even more expansive, as if to say not even {\i derived from} the category of ancestral land. This double extra language allows Rabbi Yehuda and Rabbi Shimon to perform two derivations simultaneously \u8212- one for the case where the son inherits before sanctifying (which, according to this reading, also requires a verse even for them, because of the {\i kinyan peirot ke-kinyan haguf} principle), and one for the case where the potential was present and actualized after the fact.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rabbi Yosef\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Brothers Division\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yosef offers a striking observation: were it not for Rabbi Yochanan\u8217's ruling that {\i kinyan peirot ke-kinyan haguf dami}, one could not even get started in learning, {\i lo matza yadav ve-raglav be-veit ha-medrash}. The reason lies in a further ruling of Rabbi Yochanan, cited by Rabbi Assi: brothers who divide an inherited estate are considered as purchasers of one another\u8217's portions \u8212- {\i lakuchos hein u-machzirin zeh la-zeh ba-yovel}. At {\i yovel}, they must return the property and renegotiate the division.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The conceptual basis of this ruling is that when brothers inherit jointly, each acquires an undivided half-interest in every property. When they subsequently divide \u8212- one taking the northern field, one the southern \u8212- this is legally conceived as a swap: each transfers his half-interest in one field in exchange for the other\u8217's half-interest in another. They have, in effect, purchased from one another.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This produces an almost startling consequence: come {\i yovel}, everything reverts. Not only formal commercial sales but every inheritance that has ever been divided among siblings would need to be reconsolidated and re-divided. One wonders how society could function under such a system, but that is the logical consequence of the ruling.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Bikkurim Problem\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Now the problem for {\i bikkurim} becomes apparent. If {\i kinyan peirot lav ke-kinyan haguf dami}, and if brothers are {\i lakuchos} \u8212- considered purchasers of one another during the {\i yovel} period \u8212- then virtually no landowner could ever declare {\i ha-adamah asher natata li}, since virtually every plot of land has passed through at least one inheritance division somewhere in the family line. {\i Lo meshkachat de-maisi bikkurim ela chad bar chad ad Yehoshua bin Nun} \u8212- one would never find anyone who could bring {\i bikkurim} with the declaration unless he could demonstrate a completely uninterrupted father-to-single-son chain of descent going all the way back to Joshua bin Nun. The absurdity of this conclusion demonstrates why Rabbi Yochanan\u8217's ruling is indispensable.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafos Fix\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafos grapples with this, since the bottom-line {\i psak} is not clearly settled. If we rule like Reish Lakish \u8212- {\i kinyan peirot lav ke-kinyan haguf dami} \u8212- and we also rule {\i ein breira} (which generally prevents retroactive clarification of ownership), we seem to be back to the same problem. Tosafos resolves this by distinguishing between the technical concept of {\i kinyan peirot} in the context of commerce and the practical realities of inheritance. Even if one holds {\i ein breira} in general, that principle does not extend so far as to treat the division of an inheritance as a purchase that triggers the {\i yovel} reversion. The Torah\u8217's reversion mechanism applies to genuine commercial sales; the division of an estate, even if theoretically conceptualized as a swap of partial interests, would not fall within the category of sale subject to reversion. This allows one to hold {\i kinyan peirot lav ke-kinyan haguf dami} without generating the absurd consequence that no one could ever bring {\i bikkurim}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rava Proofs\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yovel Pasuk\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava brings independent support for Reish Lakish from both a verse and a {\i beraisa}. The verse: {\i be-mispar shnei tevuot yimkor lach} \u8212- in the sale of a field during {\i yovel}, the price is calculated based on the number of years of produce remaining until {\i yovel}. More pointedly, the verse concludes {\i ki mispar tevuot hu mocher lach} \u8212- for it is years of produce that he is selling you. The Torah itself frames the transaction as a sale of produce-years rather than a sale of land, which supports Reish Lakish\u8217's view that the purchaser\u8217's relationship to the land is that of a {\i kinyan peirot}, not a {\i kinyan haguf}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Bechor Proof\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i beraisa} proof: a {\i bechor}, a firstborn son, receives a double portion of the estate. The rule is that he receives a double portion of assets that were under the father\u8217's control at the time of death, but not of assets that had passed out of the father\u8217's hands. The {\i beraisa} teaches that a {\i bechor} receives a double portion of a field that had been sold by the father and that will revert to the father\u8217's estate at {\i yovel}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Father Ownership\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is a direct proof for Reish Lakish. The fact that the field counts as part of the father\u8217's estate for purposes of the {\i bechor}\u8217's double portion demonstrates that even after the sale, the father retains a meaningful ownership interest in the land. The purchaser has only {\i kinyan peirot} \u8212- rights to the produce for the interim period \u8212- while the father retains {\i kinyan haguf}. Reish Lakish\u8217's position is thereby supported: the two categories are legally distinct.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Abaye Proof\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Harsha\u8217'ah Need\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye contributes a further proof from an accepted practical ruling: {\i naktinan}, we have received as settled law, that a husband who wishes to litigate on behalf of his wife to protect her ownership of a field requires a {\i harsha\u8217'ah} \u8212- a formal power of attorney \u8212- to represent her interests in court. In a {\i nichsei milog} marriage, the wife holds title to the land ({\i kinyan haguf}) while the husband holds the right to the produce ({\i kinyan peirot}). If {\i kinyan peirot} were equivalent to {\i kinyan haguf}, the husband would himself be considered a full owner and would have standing to litigate without any special authorization. The requirement of {\i harsha\u8217'ah} therefore demonstrates {\i kinyan peirot lav ke-kinyan haguf dami}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Fruit Exception\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye adds a qualification: this applies {\i delo nachit a-peirei} \u8212- only where the litigation concerns title to the land alone. But if the dispute also implicates the fruit-rights \u8212- {\i nachit a-peirei} \u8212- then {\i migo de-mishtei dina a-peirei mishtei dina a-gufa}: since the husband can litigate in his own right to defend his fruit interests, he simultaneously acquires standing to defend his wife\u8217's ownership interests as well. This is a limited exception to the general principle, and the underlying rule remains that {\i kinyan peirot lav ke-kinyan haguf dami}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Tikkun Olam\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Quality Grades\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i perek} of {\i ha-shole\u8217'ach} is now complete, and the Gemara moves into the following {\i perek}, which continues with matters of {\i tikkun olam}. The Mishnah opens: {\i ha-nizakin shamin lahen be-iddit} \u8212- when assessing damages in a tort case, the injured party collects from the highest-quality property of the one who caused the damage. The halakha recognizes three grades of land quality: {\i iddit} (grade A, finest), {\i beinonis} (grade B, intermediate), and {\i ziboris} (grade C, lowest). As Rashi notes, people would far rather receive an acre of prime land than many acres of inferior land; high-quality real estate is far more liquid and valuable per unit, making it significantly more desirable as a form of compensation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Collection Order\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Mishnah\u8217's hierarchy: the tort victim ({\i nizak}) collects from {\i iddit}, the finest land of the tortfeasor. A creditor collecting on a loan collects from {\i beinonis}, intermediate quality \u8212- and this applies equally when collecting from property that has been sold to third parties. The wife collecting her {\i ketubah} collects from {\i ziboris}, the lowest grade. Rabbi Meir holds that even the {\i ketubah} is collected from {\i beinonis}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Encumbered Property\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Another rule: one may not collect from encumbered property \u8212- property over which one holds liens because it was sold by the debtor \u8212- when unencumbered property remains in the debtor\u8217's own possession, even if that unencumbered property is of inferior quality. So if the debtor retains only {\i ziboris} land in his possession but has sold {\i beinonis} land to third parties, the creditor cannot invoke his liens against those third-party purchasers. He must accept the {\i ziboris}. This protects innocent purchasers who acquired property from the debtor before the debt was called in.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Heirs Collect\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Similarly, when one collects from the estate of a deceased debtor \u8212- from the heirs \u8212- one may only collect {\i ziboris}, the lowest grade. Though ordinarily a creditor collects {\i beinonis}, the heirs stand in a position analogous to third-party purchasers who have come into ownership of the property, and the protection of {\i tikkun olam} requires that collection against them be limited to the lowest grade.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Indeterminate Amounts\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Improvements Limit\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Mishnah introduces a further category of {\i tikkun olam}: one may not collect {\i achilas peirot} or {\i shevach karka\u8217'os} from encumbered property that has been sold. {\i Achilas peirot} and {\i shevach karka\u8217'os} refer to the improvement value of land \u8212- the enhancement in value produced by labor and investment. The scenario: one purchases land with a guarantee ({\i achrayus}). Another party then proves that the seller had no valid title, and seizes the land along with all improvements. The buyer is compensated for his labor by the one who seized the land \u8212- retroactively treated as a hired laborer \u8212- but not fully. He then turns to the seller to invoke the insurance ({\i achrayus}).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 For the base purchase price of the land, the buyer may invoke his liens against property the seller sold to third parties, if the seller has no unencumbered assets. But for the value of {\i achilas peirot} and {\i shevach karka\u8217'os} \u8212- the improvements and produce \u8212- he may collect only directly from the seller, not from third parties who purchased the seller\u8217's other lands. The reason: purchasers of the seller\u8217's lands cannot protect themselves against this exposure, because the amount of future improvements on the contested property is inherently indeterminate at the time of their purchase. It would be unjust to subject them to liens of unknown and unpredictable magnitude.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Support Limit\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The same principle applies to a widow\u8217's and daughters\u8217' right of support from the estate. When a husband dies, his widow and daughters are entitled to be sustained from the estate. However, they may not collect this support from property the husband sold during his lifetime \u8212- only from unencumbered property remaining in the estate. Again, the reason is {\i tikkun olam}: the total amount of support over time is indeterminate, and purchasers of the husband\u8217's property could not have calculated and protected themselves against an open-ended lien of this nature.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Found Property\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Finder Exemption\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Mishnah adds a qualitatively different case of {\i tikkun olam}: one who finds a lost object and returns it to its owner is exempt from taking an oath if the owner disputes how much was in the wallet or what the object contained. Ordinarily, a litigant in this type of dispute would be required to swear. But if finders were obligated to take oaths, people would simply decline to return lost objects rather than expose themselves to the burden and risk of oath-taking. The exemption is therefore a {\i tikkun olam} \u8212- an enactment for the welfare of society that encourages the return of lost property.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Tannaim Sources\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Torah Source\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now investigates the Torah source for the rule that tort victims collect from the finest land of the tortfeasor. The verse: {\i meitav sadehu u-meitav karmo yeshaleim} \u8212- he shall pay from the best of his field and the best of his vineyard. The context is the case of an animal that goes out and grazes in another\u8217's field ({\i shen va-regel}), but the principle is extended to all cases of damage. This, says the Gemara, is a biblical obligation. Why then does the Mishnah frame it as {\i tikkun olam}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Yishmael\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye clarifies: the framing as {\i tikkun olam} is necessary only according to Rabbi Yishmael. The {\i beraisa} records: {\i meitav sadehu shel nizak u-meitav karmo shel nizak divrei Rabbi Yishmael} \u8212- the verse refers to the finest field of the {\i nizak}, the injured party, not of the {\i mazik}, the tortfeasor. On this reading, the measure is not the objective quality of the tortfeasor\u8217's land but rather what counts as finest quality for the damagee. If the damagee\u8217's own best land rates, say, 80 on a scale of 100, and the tortfeasor\u8217's land ranges from 90 to 100, the tortfeasor need not give his 90-to-100 quality; he can satisfy his obligation with his 80-quality land, since that is what constitutes {\i meitav} from the damagee\u8217's subjective vantage point.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Akiva disagrees. He reads the verse as establishing the right of the injured party to collect from the finest property of the tortfeasor regardless of the relative quality of the damagee\u8217's own land, {\i lo ba ha-katuv ela ligvot li-nezikin min ha-iddit}. And from this principle of tort law he derives by {\i kal va-chomer} that the same applies \u8212- and even more so \u8212- with respect to {\i hekdesh}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Abaye Doubtful\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i beraisa} initially presents Rabbi Yishmael\u8217's position in a way that seems to mean something different: the tortfeasor must pay as if his animal ate from the finest of the damagee\u8217's fields, even if it actually ate from inferior land. Taken literally, this would mean: if the animal ate from grade C land, the tortfeasor pays as though it ate from grade A. Abaye proposes that the case is one of uncertainty \u8212- the animal ate a row of crops and it is not known whether it consumed from the prize grapes worth ten thousand or the ordinary grapes worth one thousand \u8212- and in that case of doubt, one pays based on the finest.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava Rejects\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava rejects Abaye\u8217's reading. If we knew the animal ate from the lesser quality, it would pay for the lesser quality. How then could the Torah\u8217's verse cause us to pay the higher amount merely because we are uncertain? This would violate the fundamental principle of {\i ha-motzi me-chaveiro alav ha-ra\u8217'ayah} \u8212- the burden of proof lies with the claimant. In a case of doubt, one cannot extract a larger sum than what can be affirmatively proven.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Collection Resolution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara thus arrives at what will be the final resolution, to be developed further: Rabbi Yishmael and Rabbi Akiva agree that the amount owed is determined by the actual damage caused \u8212- not inflated by doubt or by hypothetical enhancement. Their dispute is about {\i which property} of the tortfeasor the damagee is entitled to collect from. Rabbi Yishmael holds that the biblical measure entitles the damagee to collect from tortfeasor\u8217's land that is comparable in quality to the damagee\u8217's own finest land \u8212- even if that is not the absolute finest land the tortfeasor owns. Rabbi Akiva holds the damagee is entitled to collect from the genuinely finest land of the tortfeasor. The Rabbis\u8217' {\i tikkun olam} \u8212- framed as a rabbinic enactment \u8212- is in fact to adopt Rabbi Akiva\u8217's standard and allow collection from the actual {\i iddit} of the tortfeasor, providing a stronger deterrent against negligence.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Gittin 49\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Tikkun Olam Framework\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Three Categories\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Today\u8217's daf, 49, opens a new {\i perek} \u8212- {\i mipnei tikkun olam} \u8212- though it draws us into a realm that feels far closer to {\i Choshen Mishpat} than to the laws of {\i gittin} we have been studying. The governing question is: which types of creditors and claimants may collect from which grade of real estate? The {\i mishnah} establishes a three-way division. A {\i nizak} \u8212- one whose property or person has been damaged \u8212- collects from the {\i idit}, the top-grade, grade A real estate of the {\i mazik}. A creditor who has lent money collects from grade B property. And a woman collecting her {\i ketubbah} collects from grade C. Each of these carries its own {\i tikkun olam} logic, and it is precisely that logic which the {\i Gemara} will now unpack.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The most immediately striking case is the first: why should a {\i nizak} collect from the {\i mazik}\u8217's very best property? The stated rationale is deterrence \u8212- by forcing the {\i mazik} to part with the highest-quality land, we incentivize people to be more careful and less negligent about inflicting harm. But the {\i Gemara} immediately notes that this does not seem to be a rabbinic {\i tikkun olam} at all: the {\i Torah} itself states {\i meitav sadehu u-meitav karmo yeshalem} \u8212- he shall pay from the best of his field and the best of his vineyard. So what exactly are the rabbis adding?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ishmael vs.\u160 ?Akiva\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The answer depends on a foundational dispute between Rabbi Yishmael and Rabbi Akiva. According to Rabbi Yishmael, the {\i Torah} verse, read at face value, means that the {\i nizak} collects from the best of {\i his own} fields \u8212- that is, the highest-quality property belonging to the {\i damagee}, not the {\i damager}. The rabbinic {\i tikkun olam} then adds a further layer: the {\i nizak} is entitled to collect from the best of the {\i mazik}\u8217's property. According to Rabbi Akiva, however, the {\i Torah} already mandates collection from the best of the {\i mazik}\u8217's property \u8212- so there is no {\i tikkun olam} for the case of {\i nezek} at all. The {\i mishnah}\u8217's {\i tikkun olam} language, on Rabbi Akiva\u8217's reading, applies only to the other categories in the {\i mishnah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Property Equivalence\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} then asks: what precisely does it mean that the {\i nizak} collects from \u8220"the best of his fields\u8221"? An initial reading \u8212- that damage to grade C property of the {\i damagee} would be compensated by that same {\i damagee}\u8217's grade A property \u8212- is quickly rejected as absurd. The {\i Gemara} therefore presents the answer of Rav Acha bar Yaakov: {\i kigon she-hayesa idit de-nizak k-ziboris de-mazik} \u8212- the case under discussion is one where the finest property of the {\i damagee} is equivalent in quality to the worst property of the {\i mazik}. In other words, suppose the {\i damagee} is a person whose best holdings barely qualify as grade C, while the {\i mazik} owns grade A, grade B, and grade C property. Which of the {\i mazik}\u8217's lands may the {\i nizak} collect from?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 To make the arithmetic vivid: suppose the damage equals one acre of grade C land, and each ascending grade is worth twice the previous. If the {\i nizak} is limited to collecting based on the value of his own best property \u8212- grade C \u8212- he would receive the equivalent in the {\i mazik}\u8217's grade C land, namely one full acre. But if he may collect from the {\i mazik}\u8217's grade A property, he receives a quarter of an acre of superior land, equivalent in value to his full acre of grade C. This is the heart of the dispute. According to Rabbi Yishmael, we evaluate what the {\i nizak} is entitled to based on his own best property \u8212- so if his best is grade C, he collects the equivalent from the {\i mazik}\u8217's grade C. The {\i tikkun olam} then adds the entitlement to collect from the {\i mazik}\u8217's best. According to Rabbi Akiva, the {\i Torah} already grants the {\i nizak} the right to collect from the {\i mazik}\u8217's finest holdings, and this is the more powerful disincentive: the prospect of losing prime real estate for a moment of negligence.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is worth noting that at the end of the day \u8212- once the rabbinic {\i tikkun olam} is in place \u8212- everyone agrees that the {\i nizak} is entitled to collect from the {\i mazik}\u8217's best property. The dispute is whether that entitlement is {\i de-oraita} or rabbinic. And lest one wonder why the quality of land matters when the total value is the same, the practical answer is well known: a house or field in a desirable location has an official market price but may be far more liquid and valuable in practice than the equivalent nominal value in a less desirable holding. It makes a significant difference what grade of land one is actually receiving.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Ishmael\u8217's View\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gezerah Shavah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now lays out the textual basis for each position. Rabbi Yishmael derives his reading through a {\i gezeirah shavah}: the word {\i sadeh} \u8212- \u8220"field\u8221" \u8212- appears twice in the relevant passage. The first time it describes the field that was consumed \u8212- plainly the {\i nizak}\u8217's field, since it is the one that was grazed upon. The second time, in {\i meitav sadehu yeshalem}, it says \u8220"the best of the field he shall pay.\u8221" Since the {\i sadeh} mentioned below follows the {\i sadeh} mentioned above \u8212- and the upper usage clearly refers to the {\i damagee}\u8217's field \u8212- Rabbi Yishmael reads the lower usage identically: the best of the {\i damagee}\u8217's field. According to this reading, the verse establishes that collection is measured against the {\i nizak}\u8217's highest-quality property, and the rabbinic {\i tikkun olam} upgrades that to the {\i mazik}\u8217's best.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Akiva\u8217's View\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Simple Reading\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Akiva\u8217's position is based on a simple reading of the verse\u8217's syntax. {\i Meitav sadehu yeshalem} \u8212- \u8220"he shall pay the best of his field.\u8221" The subject who is paying is the {\i mazik}, and so the possessive {\i his} refers to the {\i mazik}. The verse is grammatically straightforward: the one paying compensates from the best of what belongs to him, the {\i damager}. Rabbi Akiva does not require a {\i gezeirah shavah} \u8212- the plain sense of the pronouns in the verse already points to the {\i mazik}\u8217's property.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Ishmael\u8217's View (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Grade Mismatch\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 How does Rabbi Yishmael then explain the second half of the verse \u8212- {\i meitav sadehu yeshalem} \u8212- which Rabbi Akiva reads as referring to the {\i mazik}\u8217's field? Rabbi Yishmael assigns different work to the {\i gezeirah shavah} and to the plain sense of the verse respectively: {\i hani gezeirah shavah ve-hani kra}. The {\i gezeirah shavah} establishes the baseline rule \u8212- the {\i nizak} collects based on the grade of his own best property. The plain sense of {\i meitav sadehu} then addresses a distinct scenario: what happens when the {\i mazik}\u8217's holdings do not include the grade of property that the {\i nizak} is entitled to collect?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 For example, suppose the {\i nizak}\u8217's best property is grade B, and the {\i nizak} is therefore entitled to collect grade B from the {\i mazik}. But the {\i mazik} owns only grade A and grade C \u8212- he has no grade B at all. Must the {\i nizak} settle for the {\i mazik}\u8217's grade C? Rabbi Yishmael says no: in that case the {\i nizak} may collect from the next higher grade available, the {\i mazik}\u8217's grade A. We cannot force the {\i nizak} to accept less than what he is entitled to merely because the {\i mazik} happens not to own the precise grade. This is the function of {\i meitav sadehu} \u8212- \u8220"the best of his field\u8221" \u8212- understood as the {\i mazik}\u8217's field: when the {\i mazik} lacks the appropriate grade, the {\i nizak} may upgrade and collect the closest superior quality the {\i mazik} possesses.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 So on Rabbi Yishmael\u8217's account, the framework is: you begin with the {\i nizak}\u8217's best property as the measure of entitlement; but if the {\i mazik} lacks that grade, the {\i nizak} may reach up to the next grade available from the {\i mazik}\u8217's holdings. What does {\i tikkun olam} add? In Rabbi Yishmael\u8217's scheme, the rabbinic layer is precisely the general rule that the {\i nizak} collects from the {\i mazik}\u8217's best \u8212- not merely from whatever grade of the {\i mazik}\u8217's land happens to correspond to the {\i nizak}\u8217's best. Rabbi Akiva, of course, maintains that this entitlement to collect the {\i mazik}\u8217's absolute best is already {\i de-oraita}, and is thus clearly a more powerful disincentive against negligence.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Akiva\u8217's View (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hekdesh Kal Vachomer\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Akiva adds a further element to his position: {\i lo ba ha-katuv ela ligbos la-nizikin min ha-idit, ve-kal va-chomer la-hekdesh} \u8212- the verse comes to establish that victims of damage collect from grade A, and {\i a fortiori} so does {\i hekdesh}. This {\i kal va-chomer} requires careful unpacking, and the {\i Gemara} works through several possibilities.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The initial question is: what does it mean that {\i hekdesh} should collect grade A as a {\i kal va-chomer}? In what capacity is {\i hekdesh} collecting? The first possibility considered is: our ox gored an ox belonging to {\i hekdesh}. But the {\i Gemara} immediately raises the verse {\i ki yigach shor ish et shor re\u8217'ehu} \u8212- the ox-goring liability applies when a normal person\u8217's ox gores {\i his fellow\u8217's} ox. {\i Hekdesh}\u8217's ox is not the ox of \u8220"his fellow\u8221" in the relevant sense: {\i shor re\u8217'ehu amar Rachmana ve-lo shor shel hekdesh}. The verse exempts the case where {\i hekdesh} is either party, so there would be no obligation to pay at all.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The second possibility is a standard monetary obligation to {\i hekdesh} \u8212- for instance, someone who declared {\i harei alai maneh le-vedek ha-bayit}, pledging a hundred {\i maneh} to the Temple treasury. In that case the {\i gizbar} \u8212- the Temple treasurer \u8212- comes to collect, and the {\i Gemara} entertains the idea that he could seize grade A property. But this faces a different objection: {\i lo yehei ella ba\u8217'al chov, u-va\u8217'al chov dino be-veinonit} \u8212- a simple creditor collects grade B, not grade A. Why should {\i hekdesh} as a creditor fare better? A {\i kal va-chomer} does not elevate the conclusion above what has been demonstrated; it establishes that the same rule applies, not a superior one. And we have no independent basis to grant {\i hekdesh} grade A status when collecting a debt.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Moreover, even this {\i kal va-chomer} could be challenged: {\i mah le-va\u8217'al chov she-ken yafeh kocho be-nizikin, tomar be-hekdesh she-ken hora kocho be-nizikin} \u8212- one can refute the analogy by noting that a normal person is in a stronger position regarding damage claims, while {\i hekdesh} is in a {\i weaker} position, since {\i hekdesh}\u8217's ox goring a layperson\u8217's ox generates no liability. Since {\i hekdesh} is disadvantaged in the domain of damages, there is no valid {\i kal va-chomer} to give it superior collection rights.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 At this point the {\i Gemara} returns to the original scenario \u8212- an ox goring {\i hekdesh}\u8217's ox \u8212- and says: {\i Rabbi Akiva savar lah ke-Rabbi Shimon ben Menasya}, Rabbi Akiva holds in accordance with Rabbi Shimon ben Menasya\u8217's position on that very question.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Hekdesh Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hekdesh\u8217's Ox\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i baraita} records: {\i shor shel hekdesh she-nagach le-shor shel hedyot patur} \u8212- if {\i hekdesh}\u8217's ox gores a layperson\u8217's ox, there is no liability. {\i Ve-shel hedyot she-nagach le-shor shel hekdesh, bein tam bein mu\u8217'ad meshalem nezek shalem} \u8212- but if a layperson\u8217's ox gores an ox belonging to {\i hekdesh}, one pays the full amount regardless of whether the ox has previously gored or not, rather than the half-payment that ordinarily applies for a first-time {\i tam}. As one participant aptly noted: the house always wins.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shimon\u8217's View\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Shimon ben Menasya reads the verse {\i ki yigach shor ish et shor re\u8217'ehu} asymmetrically. When {\i hekdesh} takes the role of the {\i mazik}, it pays nothing \u8212- it falls outside the verse\u8217's scope. But when {\i hekdesh} takes the role of the {\i nizak}, the layperson pays in full, because the exemption from full payment that applies during the first three gorings is precisely the exemption for the case where {\i shor re\u8217'ehu} \u8212- a fellow person\u8217's ox \u8212- is the {\i mazik}. If the ox gored {\i hekdesh}\u8217's property, that exemption no longer applies. {\i Hekdesh} benefits in both directions: it pays nothing when it is the damager, and it collects in full when it is the damagee. With this framework in place, Rabbi Akiva\u8217's {\i kal va-chomer} becomes intelligible: since {\i hekdesh} is granted such extraordinary privilege in the law of ox-goring \u8212- collecting full damages even from a first-time {\i tam} \u8212- it stands to reason that when {\i hekdesh} collects, it is entitled to collect from the best property, grade A, just as a normal {\i nizak}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Normal vs.\u160 ?Hekdesh\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} then raises a further question about the structure of the dispute. Given that Rabbi Akiva makes his point about {\i hekdesh} at the end of his position, one might wonder: perhaps Rabbi Akiva and Rabbi Yishmael are not debating the normal case of {\i nizak} and {\i mazik} at all. Perhaps the entire dispute between them concerns only the {\i hekdesh} question \u8212- Rabbi Akiva holding like Rabbi Shimon ben Menasya (that {\i hekdesh} collects in damage cases), and Rabbi Yishmael holding like the {\i rabbanan} (that {\i hekdesh} does not collect in damage cases). On this reading, the two {\i tannaim} would agree about the normal case, and their disagreement would be confined to whether {\i hekdesh} has collection rights.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} rejects this interpretation on two grounds. First, if Rabbi Akiva agreed with Rabbi Yishmael on the normal case, his language \u8212- {\i lo ba ha-katuv ela ligbos la-nizikin min ha-idit} \u8212- would make little sense, since he would not be expanding the {\i nizak}\u8217's rights beyond what Rabbi Yishmael already grants. Second, {\i Rav Ashi} cites an explicit {\i baraita} that settles the matter: {\i meitav sadehu shel nizak u-meitav karmo shel nizak divrei Rabbi Yishmael; Rabbi Akiva omer meitav sadehu shel mazik u-meitav karmo shel mazik}. The debate is explicit and unambiguous \u8212- Rabbi Yishmael and Rabbi Akiva are disagreeing about the normal case of {\i nizak} and {\i mazik}, not merely about {\i hekdesh}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rav Ashi\u8217's Resolution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Torah vs.\u160 ?Rabbinic\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Now that the dispute between Rabbi Yishmael and Rabbi Akiva is clearly established, a question remains about the {\i mishnah} itself. Rav Ashi proposes: {\i le-olam okimna ke-Rabbi Akiva} \u8212- we can read the {\i mishnah} as going according to Rabbi Akiva throughout. But if Rabbi Akiva already holds that the {\i Torah} mandates collection from the {\i mazik}\u8217's best property, why does the {\i mishnah} describe this as a {\i tikkun olam}? Rav Ashi answers: {\i de-Rabbi Shimon hi, de-darash ta\u8217'ama dikra}. What the {\i mishnah} is saying is not that this is a rabbinic enactment layered on top of {\i Torah} law; rather, it is explicating the {\i rationale} behind the {\i Torah}\u8217's own rule. The {\i mishnah}\u8217's invocation of {\i tikkun olam} is functioning as a statement of purpose, not as a marker of rabbinic legislation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ta\u8217'am HaKra\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This invokes the distinct {\i talmudic} principle associated with Rabbi Shimon: {\i doresh ta\u8217'ama dikra} \u8212- he derives legal consequences from his understanding of the reason behind a verse. The key insight from {\i Tosafot} is that Rabbi Shimon\u8217's method is not merely a philosophical exercise in explaining {\i ta\u8217'amei ha-mitzvot}. Rather, he uses the identified rationale to determine the {\i parameters} and {\i applications} of the law. {\i Tosafot} illustrates this with two examples: the prohibition against taking a widow\u8217's garment as collateral ({\i lo tachbol beged almanah}), and the restriction on the king multiplying wives. In the garment case, for instance, Rabbi Shimon arrives at a strikingly unexpected {\i ta\u8217'am}: the concern is not economic oppression of the poor widow but rather the appearance of sexual impropriety, since returning a poor person\u8217's garment every evening would require the creditor to visit her home nightly. However counterintuitive, Rabbi Shimon uses this rationale to define who is and is not covered by the prohibition.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Deterrence Rationale\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Applied to our case: {\i mipnei mah amru ha-nezikin shamin lahem be-idit, mipnei ha-gazlanim u-mipnei ha-chamsanim} \u8212- why did they say that damage cases are assessed with grade A property? Because of thieves and {\i chamsanim}, those who coerce others into selling property against their will. The deterrent logic runs as follows: a person contemplating theft or coercive acquisition should say to himself, {\i lamah ani gozel, lamah ani chomes \u8212- le-machar beit din yordin le-nechasai ve-notlim sadeh na\u8217'ah sheli} \u8212- \u8220"Why should I steal or seize property? Tomorrow the court will come and take my finest field.\u8221"\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This points to a real lacuna in pure compensation logic. If all a {\i gazlan} need do is return what he took, the disincentive is minimal \u8212- he simply gives back the equivalent. The {\i Torah} does impose double payment on a {\i ganav} (one who steals covertly) but only equivalent repayment on a {\i gazlan} (one who seizes openly). Where, then, is the disincentive for open seizure? The answer is that if the {\i gazlan} cannot pay in cash, he will be made to hand over his best land. That is a genuine deterrent.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i baraita} adds: {\i ve-samchu al mah she-katuv ba-Torah meitav sadehu meitav karmo yeshalem} \u8212- and the rabbis relied on what is written in the {\i Torah}. This phrase is itself revealing. The {\i Torah} explicitly invokes {\i meitav} only in the case of {\i shen va-regel} \u8212- an animal grazing in another\u8217's field. It is not stated explicitly for other categories of damage. What motivated {\i Chazal} to extend this rule across all damage cases? The {\i tikkun olam} rationale: once we understand that the {\i Torah}\u8217's purpose in mandating grade A collection is deterrence, we can reason that if the {\i Torah} imposes this penalty even for {\i negligence} \u8212- letting one\u8217's animal wander onto another\u8217's land \u8212- certainly the same principle should apply in cases of willful damage or coercive seizure. The {\i gezeirah shavah} provides the technical mechanism; the {\i tikkun olam} rationale provides the conceptual justification for extending it broadly. This is Rabbi Shimon {\i doresh ta\u8217'ama dikra} in action: using the identified reason to expand the rule\u8217's application beyond its narrow textual context.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Creditor Protection\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Predatory Lending\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having explained the grade A rule for {\i nezikin}, Rabbi Shimon proceeds to the second tier of the {\i mishnah}: {\i mipnei mah amru be-chov be-veinonit} \u8212- why did the rabbis say that a creditor collects grade B? The concern here is predatory lending: {\i kedei she-lo yireh adam le-chavero sadeh na\u8217'ah ve-dirah na\u8217'ah ve-yomar ekpotz ve-alvenu kedei she-agvenu be-chovi} \u8212- a person should not look at his neighbor\u8217's beautiful field and fine house and think, \u8220"Let me lend him money, he will default, and I will acquire his property through debt collection.\u8221" Allowing collection from grade A would invite this sort of predatory calculation. Therefore, the rabbis limited the creditor to grade B.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Lending Incentives\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} immediately asks: if the concern is that lending will be used as a vehicle for seizing the borrower\u8217's best property, why not go further and limit the creditor to grade C? The answer is equally direct: {\i im ken atah no\u8217'el delet bifnei lovin} \u8212- if you do that, you shut the door before borrowers. The system requires that lending remain viable. This is not a trivial concern in the context of {\i Torah} law: the prohibition on interest, the {\i shemitah}-based annulment of loans every seven years, and the general uncertainty of recollection all make lending a poor financial proposition already. We cannot disincentivize it further. The constraint that a creditor may collect grade B but not grade A balances two competing social goods: protecting borrowers from predatory lenders on one side, and protecting the institution of lending on the other. It is worth recalling that the {\i Gemara} earlier in {\i Gittin} devoted an entire extended discussion to {\i pruzbul} \u8212- the mechanism Hillel instituted specifically to prevent the social harm caused when lending dried up in the years before {\i shemitah}. The grade B rule is part of the same ongoing effort to keep credit accessible.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Ketubah Collection\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Marriage Incentive\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The third category in the {\i mishnah} is {\i ketubbat isha bi-ziborit} \u8212- a woman collects her {\i ketubbah} from grade C property. This is the view of Rabbi Yehuda; Rabbi Meir holds she collects grade B. Rabbi Shimon, consistent with his practice of identifying the rationale behind rulings, explains: {\i mipnei mah amru ketubbat isha bi-ziborit} \u8212- why did they say the {\i ketubbah} is collected from grade C? {\i She-yoter mi-mah she-ish rotzeh liso isha rotzah lihinase\u8217'a} \u8212- because a woman\u8217's desire to marry exceeds a man\u8217's. To put it more precisely: men must be incentivized to assume the financial obligations that marriage entails under rabbinic law. The {\i ketubbah} is an enormous innovation protecting the woman \u8212- it provides financial security against divorce and widowhood, and imposes ongoing financial obligations during the marriage. But precisely because it represents a significant liability for the husband, we must make its terms bearable enough that men will be willing to marry. Limiting {\i ketubbah} collection to grade C land achieves this: the husband can say to himself that even in the worst case, the debt will be settled from his least valuable holdings.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Divorce Asymmetry\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i baraita} then offers a second rationale \u8212- {\i davar acher} \u8212- which is considerably more puzzling: {\i isha yotzah li-rtzonah ve-shelo li-rtzonah, ve-ish eino motzi ela li-rtsono} \u8212- a woman can be divorced whether she wills it or not, while a man only divorces willingly. The {\i Gemara} immediately asks {\i mai davar acher} \u8212- how is this relevant to the question of {\i ziborit}? If anything, the fact that a woman can be divorced against her will would seem to argue for {\i greater} protection, not lesser.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} constructs the following {\i hava amina}: since we are limiting the {\i ketubbah} to grade C out of concern for the husband\u8217's interests, one might wonder \u8212- if we are so solicitous of the husband \u8212- why not go further and say that when {\i he} is the one being divorced (or more precisely, when {\i she} is the cause of the divorce), he should receive a {\i ketubbah} from {\i her}? The {\i Gemara}\u8217's response is that this is essentially absurd: {\i efshar de-mashli lah be-gitta} \u8212- it is entirely within his power to withhold a {\i get}. He is always the one who decides whether the marriage ends. He cannot be in the position of the divorce recipient; that entire framework is inapplicable to him. The asymmetry of divorce rights is not a reason to award him a {\i ketubbah} \u8212- it is a reminder that the system as structured places control of the divorce in his hands alone.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What the {\i davar acher} seems to be gesturing toward is the observation that the woman\u8217's financial protection through the {\i ketubbah} \u8212- paid by the husband \u8212- is inherently tied to the reality that {\i he} controls the exit from the marriage. Because she cannot initiate divorce, and because all the financial burden of the {\i ketubbah} falls on him regardless of who is responsible for the breakdown of the marriage, the rabbis chose not to make that burden so heavy as to be crushing. The grade C limitation reflects a deliberate calibration, not a derogation of the woman\u8217's rights. The bottom line, as the {\i Gemara} affirms, is that the woman never becomes the one to incur debt as a result of divorce \u8212- the obligation runs from husband to wife, and the question is only how burdensome to make it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Orphans Limitation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Mar Zutra the son of Rav Nachman then offers a restrictive reading of the entire discussion: {\i lo amru ela mi-yatmei} \u8212- the ruling that {\i ketubbah} is collected from grade C applies only when she is collecting from orphans, meaning after the husband has died and his estate has passed to his children. If she is collecting directly from the husband himself \u8212- in the case of divorce, while he is still alive \u8212- she collects grade B, like any ordinary creditor. This makes intuitive sense: why should a woman have fewer rights than a standard creditor when collecting from a living husband?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Grade B Direct\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} challenges this reading: if the {\i mishnah} is discussing {\i only} collection from orphans, then why does it single out {\i ketubbat isha} as collecting grade C? There is already a general rule \u8212- {\i ein nifra\u8217'in mi-nichsei yetomim ela min ha-ziborit} \u8212- one may only collect from orphans\u8217' property using grade C. That rule would already cover the {\i ketubbah}. Why state it separately? The {\i Gemara} therefore considers the reverse: the grade B versus grade C debate about {\i ketubbah} is specifically about collection from the husband himself, and the {\i mishnah} is telling us that even here, the {\i ketubbah} is grade C (Rabbi Yehuda) or grade B (Rabbi Meir).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} then offers a further possibility: {\i le-olam mi-yatmei} \u8212- the debate really is about orphans \u8212- and the novelty of specifying {\i ketubbat isha} is this: one might have thought that because of {\i mishum china} \u8212- for the sake of the woman\u8217's social dignity \u8212- the rabbis would elevate the {\i ketubbah}\u8217's collection rights even from orphans to grade B. {\i Ka mashma lan} \u8212- the {\i mishnah} therefore teaches us that they did not. Note that {\i Rashi} and {\i Tosafot} differ about the meaning of {\i mishum china}. {\i Rashi} reads it as incentivizing the woman \u8212- making the {\i ketubbah} attractive enough that women will want to marry. {\i Tosafot} offers a more indirect reading: if the court consistently forces women to collect from the worst property, their social status declines, which paradoxically disincentivizes men from wanting to marry women of lower social standing. According to {\i Tosafot}, {\i mishum china} is ultimately about maintaining the woman\u8217's social status high enough that men will want to marry her \u8212- but not so high that men are financially crushed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava and Abaye each bring further proofs aimed at resolving whether the grade levels described in the {\i mishnah} concern collection from the debtor himself or from his orphaned heirs. Abaye\u8217's proof is the most decisive in its structure: the {\i mishnah} lists all three categories \u8212- {\i nezikin} at grade A, creditor at grade B, {\i ketubbah} at grade C \u8212- in a single list. If all three concerned collection from orphans, then there would be no reason to distinguish between them, since the rule of grade C from orphans would flatten the distinction entirely. Clearly the {\i mishnah} is describing collection from a living debtor. And if the creditor and the {\i nizak} are collecting from the living debtor at grade B and grade A respectively, then {\i ketubbat isha} must also be discussing collection from the living husband \u8212- not from orphans.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Guarantor Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Guarantor Setup\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 At this point Rav Acha bar Yaakov proposes a sophisticated resolution that avoids the force of Abaye\u8217's proof. The {\i mishnah}, he suggests, is discussing a case that is neither the debtor himself nor his orphaned children \u8212- it concerns a guarantor: {\i kegon she-na\u8217'asa arev le-nizkei bno u-le-va\u8217'al chov bno u-le-ketubbat kalato} \u8212- someone who agreed to stand as guarantor for the damage obligations of his son, for his son\u8217's debt, and for his son\u8217's {\i ketubbah} to his daughter-in-law. Now all three categories of claimants come to collect not from the son himself but from the guarantor.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 For the {\i nizak} collecting from the guarantor, the rate is grade A \u8212- the same as if he were collecting from the son directly. For the creditor, grade B. But for the {\i ketubbah}, only grade C. The {\i mishnah} thereby establishes distinct collection rights for each category, applicable even to a guarantor, and explains why the {\i ketubbah} specifically drops to grade C even when the husband is in principle alive and a guarantor is being pursued.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Lifetime Collection\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Why are the rules for {\i nezikin} and for debt unchanged when moving from the debtor to the guarantor? The {\i Gemara} explains: {\i nezikin u-va\u8217'al chov de-mechayim gavu} \u8212- damages and debts are normally collected while the debtor is alive. Their collection is, so to speak, a {\i mechayim} transaction. When one then turns to the guarantor, one collects under the same conditions that would have applied against the debtor \u8212- as a lifetime collection, at grade A or grade B respectively.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Post-Mortem Collection\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i ketubbah}, however, is different: {\i ketubbat isha de-le\u8217'achar mita gavya} \u8212- a {\i ketubbah} is normally collected after the husband\u8217's death. (Divorce exists, of course, but death is arguably the more common and more natural trigger for {\i ketubbah} collection, particularly in the context of this discussion.) When one goes to the guarantor and that collection is treated as if it occurs in the mode of a standard {\i ketubbah} collection \u8212- which is a post-mortem collection \u8212- then the applicable standard is the one that governs post-mortem collection: {\i mi-yatmei}, from orphans, at grade C.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rashi} in fact sharpens this considerably by suggesting the {\i Gemara} is discussing a case where the son has already died. In that scenario: for the {\i nizak} and the creditor, they were entitled to collect from the son {\i during his lifetime}, and their entitlement does not diminish merely because he has since died and they are now pursuing the guarantor. The grade A and grade B rights travel with them. But for the {\i ketubbah}, the wife\u8217's entitlement arose precisely because the husband died \u8212- her cause of collection is the death itself. That being so, even when she goes to the guarantor, she is collecting in a {\i le\u8217'achar mita} mode, which means grade C.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The result is elegant: the case is neither the debtor himself nor his orphaned children, but a guarantor \u8212- a genuinely distinct category \u8212- and the three-way division of grade A, grade B, and grade C all apply simultaneously and coherently to a single set of claimants approaching the same guarantor.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Areiv vs.\u160 ?Kablan\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} raises a final technical objection: {\i ve-teipok lei de-areiv de-ketubbah lo mishtabed} \u8212- a standard guarantor ({\i areiv}) does not become obligated for a {\i ketubbah}, because no money changes hands in the formation of the {\i ketubbah} obligation, and the guarantor therefore cannot truly be said to have assumed it. The {\i Gemara} answers: {\i be-kablan} \u8212- the case concerns a {\i kablan}, a higher-order form of guarantor in which money does effectively change hands and a genuine assumption of obligation takes place. The {\i Gemara} then notes that this answer works according to the opinion that a {\i kablan} incurs obligation even where the borrower has no assets of his own \u8212- the {\i kablan} takes on a primary obligation rather than merely a backstop guarantee. The {\i sugya} concludes at this point, with the resolution of the {\i mishnah}\u8217's collection hierarchy through the innovative construction of the guarantor case.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Gittin 50\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Ketubah & Tikkun Olam\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Prior Discussion Review\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i daf} opens in the midst of a broader discussion about {\i tikkun olam} and the question of whether a {\i ketubah} is collected from grade A, grade B, or grade C property. The {\i mishnah} had established that a woman collects her {\i ketubah} from {\i ziboris} \u8212- grade C, the lowest quality land. The reason offered is to incentivize men to marry: since the husband bears the substantial financial obligations of the {\i ketubah}, the Rabbis made the collection somewhat less burdensome by limiting it to the least valuable property. However, Mar Zutra had proposed a much narrower reading of the {\i mishnah}, arguing that the limitation to {\i ziboris} applies only when the woman collects from {\i yesomim} \u8212- orphans \u8212- where grade C collection is the norm in any case. According to Mar Zutra, when the woman collects from the husband himself in a case of divorce, she collects from grade B, {\i beinonis}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} is attempting to resolve this debate. In the course of doing so, one proposed explanation of a problematic {\i braitah} was that the {\i mishnah} was not speaking of the woman collecting from the principal debtor \u8212- the husband or his orphaned heirs \u8212- but rather from an {\i areiv}, a guarantor. This explanation opened a significant digression into the laws of guarantors and their obligations, which the {\i gemara} now pursues at length before returning to the central question.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Areiv & Ketubah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} on the bottom of 49b raises an immediate difficulty with explaining the {\i mishnah} as a case of collecting from a guarantor: {\i v\u8217'teipuk lei d\u8217'areiv d\u8217'ketubah lo mishtabeid} \u8212- a guarantor of a {\i ketubah} does not become legally obligated to pay. The reason goes to the heart of what creates a guarantor\u8217's liability. In an ordinary loan, when a person agrees to serve as guarantor, the lender actually extends money to the borrower in reliance on the guarantor\u8217's word. Because actual funds changed hands based on the guarantor\u8217's commitment, the guarantor acquires a genuine sense of indebtedness \u8212- it is as though the money passed through him. That sense of real obligation, of {\i gemirat da\u8217'at}, is what makes the guarantee legally binding.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 No Money Exchange\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 By contrast, a {\i ketubah} involves no transfer of money at all. The husband simply obligates himself in the abstract; the wife does not hand over funds that the husband promises to return. Since no money is disbursed based on the guarantor\u8217's word, the mechanism that would ordinarily create a binding guarantee is entirely absent. One might argue that the couple agreed to marry on the guarantor\u8217's say-so, and that this ought to suffice \u8212- but the Rabbis hold that even if the marriage arguably would not have taken place without the guarantor\u8217's assurance, the specific financial obligation of the {\i ketubah} is not something that came into being through any concrete monetary act dependent on the guarantor\u8217's word. Accordingly, a simple {\i areiv} of a {\i ketubah} does not become legally indebted.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Areiv vs.\u160 ?Kablan\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kablan on Ketubah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} resolves the difficulty by distinguishing between an {\i areiv} and a {\i kablan}. The word {\i kablan} derives from {\i lekabel}, to receive \u8212- it denotes a guarantor who actually takes the money himself before passing it on to the debtor. In an ordinary loan, this means that the lender gives the funds directly to the {\i kablan}, who in turn transfers them to the borrower. Rashi explains how a {\i kablan} can be created even in the context of a {\i ketubah}, despite the absence of any actual debt being incurred at the time of the marriage.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ritual Pre-Payment\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rashi\u8217's formulation ({\i she\u8217'na\u8217'aseh lah kablan she\u8217'tafsah bo mataltelin le\u8217'chtubatah ve\u8217'hi masartam le\u8217'yado be\u8217'torat kablanut}) describes a deliberate ritual arrangement. The prospective husband gives his bride a sum of money as a pre-payment against her {\i ketubah} obligation. She holds this sum momentarily and then, rather than returning it directly to the groom, transfers it to the intended {\i kablan} \u8212- who receives it on behalf of the loan arrangement and then passes it to the groom. Through this process, the {\i kablan} has physically received money. He is no longer a mere promissory guarantor; he has accepted the funds, and the debt now rests on him. This transforms his status into a {\i kablan} and creates the legal obligation that a simple {\i areiv} would lack. The ritual may seem formal, but it accomplishes something real in {\i halachah}: money has genuinely changed hands through the {\i kablan}\u8217's possession.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kablan in Loans\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 In the context of an ordinary loan, the {\i kablan} arrangement is more straightforward. The lender, rather than trusting the borrower, gives the money directly to the {\i kablan}, who then transfers it to the borrower. The lender\u8217's money moved not on the borrower\u8217's account but on the {\i kablan}\u8217's account; the {\i kablan} is the one who first received the funds and is therefore the primary obligant in a meaningful sense.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Creditor Collection\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Indeed, there are opinions that precisely because the {\i kablan} is the one who directly received the money, the creditor may go straight to the {\i kablan} to collect \u8212- without first being required to attempt collection from the borrower himself. Tosafot engages with this point and works through the implications of the {\i kablan}\u8217's status as the primary recipient.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Kablan Obligation Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 No Property Scenario\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having established the {\i kablan} framework, the {\i gemara} turns to a further complication. It poses the question: {\i hani nicha le\u8217'man de\u8217'amar kablan af al gav de\u8217'leit lei nichsei le\u8217'loveh mishta\u8217'abed shapir} \u8212- this works out nicely according to the opinion that holds a {\i kablan} obligates himself even when the original borrower has no property at the time of the loan. The woman then cannot collect from the orphans because the estate is insufficient, so she turns to the {\i kablan} \u8212- and according to this view he is bound regardless of the borrower\u8217's financial state at the time.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But according to the other opinion \u8212- {\i ei it lei mishta\u8217'abed, ei leit lei lo mishta\u8217'abed} \u8212- a {\i kablan} only obligates himself if the original borrower had property at the time the loan was made. If the borrower had no property, the {\i kablan}\u8217's commitment is treated as a kind of {\i asmachta}, an overreach: since there is no realistic expectation that the borrower will ever repay, the {\i kablan} cannot truly have committed himself with full seriousness \u8212- it is too extreme an obligation for him to have genuinely meant it. Under this view, how can the {\i mishnah}\u8217's scenario hold? If the borrower had property, why is the woman going to the {\i kablan} rather than directly to the orphans?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Property at Loan Time\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} answers simply: {\i eima d\u8217'hava lei v\u8217'ishtadif} \u8212- he had property at the time of the loan but it subsequently deteriorated or was lost, leaving insufficient funds in the estate. This is precisely the normal function of a guarantor: the obligation was undertaken when the principal debtor was solvent, and the guarantee becomes operative when the estate can no longer cover the debt. The woman therefore comes to the {\i kablan} not because there was never any property, but because what was there has since dissipated.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Woman & Guarantor\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 With this in place, the {\i mishnah}\u8217's scenario is coherently reconstructed. The woman is collecting her {\i ketubah} not directly from the orphans \u8212- which would ordinarily be limited to {\i ziboris} \u8212- but from a {\i kablan} who had been arranged at the time of the marriage. The husband had property when the {\i kablan} took on his obligation, that property has since diminished, and the woman now turns to the {\i kablan} for collection.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Choson\u8217's Father\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then offers an alternative explanation: {\i v\u8217'iba\u8217'is eima, kol legabei breih shibudei m\u8217'shabeid nafshieh} \u8212- one may alternatively say that the guarantor in the {\i mishnah}\u8217's scenario is the groom\u8217's father. Even if one holds that a {\i kablan} only obligates himself when the borrower has property, a father is willing to go further for his son\u8217's sake. A father obligates himself with greater seriousness and completeness than an ordinary guarantor would, and therefore his guarantee holds even in situations where an ordinary guarantor might not be bound.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Areiv/Kablan Analysis\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Areiv Ketubah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now presents a comprehensive analytical framework, working through the four possible combinations of the two relevant factors: whether money changed hands ({\i ba\u8217'al chov}) and whether the guarantor is a {\i kablan} (one who directly received the funds) or merely an {\i areiv} (one who did not). A simple {\i areiv} of a {\i ketubah} \u8212- {\i areiv d\u8217'kesubah divrei hakol lo mishta\u8217'abeid} \u8212- does not obligate himself under any view. Both factors that would create a genuine legal indebtedness are absent: no money was actually given out on the guarantor\u8217's word, and the guarantor never received any funds himself. The {\i ketubah} obligation arose entirely in the abstract, through the marriage arrangement, not through any concrete monetary act.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kablan Loan\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 At the opposite extreme, a {\i kablan} of an ordinary loan \u8212- {\i kablan d\u8217'ba\u8217'al chov, divrei hakol mishta\u8217'abeid} \u8212- obligates himself under every view. Here both positive factors converge: the creditor actually disbursed funds based on the guarantor\u8217's involvement, and the {\i kablan} himself was the one who first received those funds before passing them to the borrower. With both factors present, there is no question of {\i asmachta} or insufficient {\i gemirat da\u8217'at}; the obligation is fully formed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Middle Cases\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The two intermediate cases \u8212- an {\i areiv} of an ordinary loan and a {\i kablan} of a {\i ketubah} \u8212- each possess only one of the two factors, and these are where the debate arises. The {\i areiv d\u8217'ba\u8217'al chov} has the positive factor that real money was lent on his word, creating the creditor\u8217's reliance that grounds an obligation. But the {\i areiv} himself never received the funds. The {\i kablan d\u8217'kesubah}, set up through the ritual described by Rashi, has the positive factor that the {\i kablan} directly received money during the arrangement \u8212- but the underlying situation is a {\i ketubah}, meaning that ultimately no party is genuinely out of pocket in any lasting way.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 For these middle cases, there is a debate: some hold that a {\i kablan} or {\i areiv} in such cases obligates himself even when the original debtor has no property, while others hold that the obligation only arises when the debtor had property at the time \u8212- reasoning that without such property the commitment takes on the character of an {\i asmachta}, a not-quite-serious undertaking. The logic may seem counterintuitive \u8212- surely if the debtor has no resources the {\i kablan} should be all the more firmly obligated, since everyone knows collection will fall on him \u8212- but the Rabbis treat a guarantee in such extreme circumstances as lacking the requisite {\i gemar da\u8217'at} to constitute a true legal undertaking.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Halacha on Kablan\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i halachah} resolves this: {\i v\u8217'hilchata b\u8217'chulhu, af al gav d\u8217'leit lei mishta\u8217'abeid} \u8212- in all cases where at least one of the two factors is present, the {\i kablan} or {\i areiv} obligates himself even if the original debtor had no property at the time.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Areiv Ketubah Exception\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The single exception, as already established, is the {\i areiv d\u8217'kesubah}, where neither factor is present. In that case, {\i d\u8217'af al gav d\u8217'ait lei lo mishta\u8217'abeid} \u8212- even if the original debtor does have property, the guarantor does not incur any legal obligation. Without either the concrete monetary reliance of the creditor or the direct receipt of funds by the guarantor, no legally binding debt can be created.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafot & Mitzvah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 At this juncture the {\i gemara} adds an explanation that had not previously appeared in the analysis: {\i mitzvah hu d\u8217'avid v\u8217'lo midi chasrah} \u8212- the guarantor was merely performing a {\i mitzvah}, helping a couple get married, and no one was left financially worse off. This reason is presented as the rationale for why the {\i areiv d\u8217'kesubah} incurs no obligation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot seizes on this and derives an important {\i chiddush}. Until this point the analysis had focused entirely on whether money changed hands and whether the {\i kablan} directly received funds. The introduction of the {\i mitzvah} factor opens a new dimension. Tosafot argues: {\i tartei ba\u8217'inan} \u8212- both conditions must be present to exempt the {\i areiv} from obligation. The absence of financial loss ({\i lo midi chasrah}) alone is not sufficient. If the guarantor makes his promise {\i after the marriage has already taken place} \u8212- {\i she\u8217'na\u8217'aseh areiv achar hanisusin} \u8212- then the {\i mitzvah} motivation is no longer operative. The couple is already married; no one is being encouraged toward the {\i mitzvah} of marriage by this promise. In that case, even though no money changed hands, the guarantor {\i does} obligate himself, because his word in that context cannot be explained away as mere {\i mitzvah}-encouragement. Conversely, the case where both factors are present \u8212- a post-marriage guarantee where money also changed hands \u8212- is obviously an obligation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is striking. Prior to this line in the {\i gemara} the entire framework rested on whether something concrete happened \u8212- money lent, money received \u8212- to create the sense of a real legal debt. According to Tosafot, a bare post-marriage promise, with no money changing hands at all, suffices to create legal obligation. This seems to be a significant {\i chiddush}, and one might find it difficult to derive from the {\i gemara}\u8217's overall reasoning, which consistently required some concrete financial act to ground the obligation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Mishnah Resolution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Incentive for Marriage\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 After the extended digression through the laws of {\i areiv} and {\i kablan}, the {\i gemara} finally returns to the original question of whether Mar Zutra\u8217's position can be sustained. Ravina offers a conclusive proof: {\i ta shma \u8212- me\u8217'ikara de-takantin} \u8212- from the very rationale of the {\i takanah}. The {\i takanah} that a {\i ketubah} is collected from {\i ziboris}, grade C, was explained precisely because {\i yoser mi-mah she-ish rotzeh lisa, ishah rotzah lihinasei} \u8212- women are more eager to marry than men, and we must therefore incentivize men to take on the financial obligations of marriage by making those obligations less burdensome. If that is the established reason for the {\i takanah}, the entire logic presupposes that we are speaking of the man\u8217's obligation during his lifetime \u8212- what a man considers when deciding whether to assume the financial weight of marriage.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 From Husband Direct\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The incentive rationale makes sense only when the {\i ketubah} is collected from the husband himself. It is what the husband anticipates paying that needs to be made manageable. The prospect of one\u8217's orphans someday paying {\i ziboris} rather than {\i beinonis} is not the calculation running through a man\u8217's mind when he contemplates marriage.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mar Zutra Refuted\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Accordingly, {\i v\u8217'i salka da\u8217'atach mi-yasmi, hai mishum de-yasmi hu} \u8212- if the only rationale for {\i ziboris} were the protection of orphans, then the reason given in the {\i gemara} \u8212- incentivizing men to marry \u8212- would be irrelevant. Since the {\i gemara} explicitly grounds the {\i takanah} in the incentive rationale, it follows that the {\i takanah} operates when collecting from the husband himself, not merely from orphans. {\i Tiyuvta d\u8217'Mar Zutra tiyuvta} \u8212- Mar Zutra\u8217's position is definitively refuted.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Balance Protection\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This resolution reflects a broader tension running through the entire topic of {\i kesuvot}. The institution of the {\i ketubah} represents a comprehensive system of protections for the wife \u8212- ongoing obligations during the marriage and a lump-sum payment upon dissolution through divorce or death. Yet the {\i gemara} recognizes that these protections cannot be made so onerous that they deter marriage altogether. The balance being struck is real and consequential: yes, the woman receives significant protection, but it is calibrated so as not to be so burdensome as to discourage men from assuming those obligations.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shidduch Crisis\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This concern may strike modern readers as exaggerated, but there is contemporary evidence for how demographic and financial incentives genuinely affect marriage rates. Research into the so-called {\i shidduch} crisis has identified a clear demographic source: in much of the non-{\i chassidic} Orthodox community, men typically marry women two to three years younger. Combined with a positive birth rate, this means that for every hundred men seeking marriage partners in a given cohort, there are approximately 112 women in the younger cohort they are seeking to marry. The resulting imbalance creates real delays and missed matches. The {\i chassidic} world, where couples marry within their own age cohort, does not experience this phenomenon. The {\i gemara}\u8217's concern about incentivizing men toward marriage is thus not a theoretical nicety \u8212- sometimes incentive structures genuinely matter, and the {\i takanah} limiting {\i ketubah} collection to {\i ziboris} reflects that awareness.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Collection Orphans\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Note Grade A\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now shifts to a new question brought by Mar Zutra son of Rav Nachman in the name of Rav Nachman, dealing specifically with collecting from orphans. The basic rule is that all collection from orphans is limited to {\i ziboris}, grade C \u8212- a protection for the orphans who have inherited their father\u8217's estate. The question raised is what happens when a {\i shtar chov}, a promissory note, explicitly granted the creditor a lien on the debtor\u8217's grade A property, {\i idis}. Does that explicit upgrade carry over to collection from orphans? The {\i halachah} rules clearly: {\i shtar chov ha-yotzeh al yesomim af al pi she-kasuv bo shevach, eino goveh ela min ha-ziboris} \u8212- even if the document specifies the highest-quality land, collection from orphans is limited to grade C regardless.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Abaye Downgrade\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye offers a proof for this rule: {\i d\u8217'ba\u8217'al chov dinei b\u8217'veinonis, u-me\u8217'yasmi b\u8217'ziboris} \u8212- a normal creditor is entitled to grade B from the debtor himself, yet when collecting from orphans he is downgraded to grade C. By analogy, even someone who was explicitly upgraded to grade A in his document loses that upgrade when collecting from orphans and reverts to grade C. If the standard downgrade from B to C is applied to orphans, the same logic should apply to reduce grade A to grade C as well.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava Torah Law\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava challenges this analogy sharply: {\i hachi hashta?} \u8212- is this really a comparable case? To understand Rava\u8217's position, one must first understand why a normal creditor collects from orphans at grade C at all. Rava\u8217's analysis, following Ulla, begins from the Torah\u8217's baseline rule. Ulla establishes: {\i devar Torah ba\u8217'al chov b\u8217'ziboris} \u8212- from a Torah perspective, a creditor is only entitled to grade C even from the debtor himself. The source is the verse ({\i Devarim} 24:11): {\i ve-ha\u8217'ish asher attah nosheh bo yotzi elecha et ha\u8217'avot ha-chutzah} \u8212- the debtor himself brings out the collateral, and naturally, {\i mah darko shel ish l\u8217'hotzi \u8212- pachos she-bakelim} \u8212- he brings out his least valuable items. From this the Rabbis derived that the Torah\u8217's default entitlement for a creditor is the lowest-quality property.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Why then is a creditor normally entitled to grade B from a living debtor? {\i K\u8217'dei she-lo tina\u8217'el deles bifnei lovin} \u8212- to ensure that the door is not locked to borrowers, the Rabbis enacted that creditors collect grade B, creating sufficient incentive to lend money. This is particularly necessary in a {\i halachic} framework where interest is prohibited, loans are subject to {\i shemitah} cancellation, and lenders need additional assurance. But this upgrade from C to B is entirely a rabbinic {\i takanah} enacted for a policy reason.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Explicit Lien Logic\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 With this foundation, Rava constructs his counter-argument. {\i Ve-gabei yasmi ukmi rabanan ad\u8217'oraita} \u8212- when it comes to orphans, the Rabbis simply restored the Torah\u8217's baseline: the rabbinic {\i takanah} that upgraded creditors to grade B was not applied to the case of orphans, and so the creditor falls back to the Torah\u8217's default of grade C. This is not a special protective enactment {\i for} orphans \u8212- it is simply the reversion to the underlying Torah law in the absence of the incentive-based rabbinic upgrade.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But Rava\u8217's critical point follows: {\i ella hacha, keivan d\u8217'dinei m\u8217'deoraisa b\u8217'idis} \u8212- in the case where it is written explicitly in the document that the debtor granted a lien on grade A property, the creditor\u8217's entitlement to grade A is not a rabbinic upgrade but a Torah-level right, because the debtor explicitly created that encumbrance. {\i Af me\u8217'yasmi nami b\u8217'idis} \u8212- therefore, when collecting from orphans, the creditor should be entitled to grade A even from them, since the right to grade A is grounded in the Torah and not in a rabbinic {\i takanah} that was withheld from the orphan context.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The distinction Rava is drawing is precise: when the creditor has grade B by virtue of the rabbinic {\i takanah}, orphans restore him to the Torah default of grade C. But when the creditor has grade A because the document explicitly created a Torah-level lien, there is nothing to revert from \u8212- he is already at the Torah baseline or above it, and that right persists even against orphans.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Damages & Orphans\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Damages Grade C\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} presses Rava further: if his theory is correct that orphans merely restore us to the Torah baseline, then collection of {\i nezikin} \u8212- damages \u8212- from orphans should yield grade A, since the Torah entitles a {\i nizak} to collect {\i meitav sadehu}, the best quality field. Why does the {\i braitah} cited by Avram Choza \u8212- {\i ein nifroin min nechsei yesomim ela min ha-ziboris} \u8212- apply even to damage claims?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Special Case Damage\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} resolves this: {\i hacha be-mai askinan k\u8217'gon d\u8217'havya idis d\u8217'nizak k\u8217'ziboris d\u8217'maziq} \u8212- the {\i braitah} addresses a special case in which the best property of the {\i nizak} (the damaged party) is equivalent in value to the worst property of the {\i maziq} (the damaging party). In this scenario, the Torah entitlement of {\i meitav sadehu} actually translates to grade C of the {\i maziq}\u8217's holdings, not grade A.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yishmael Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This reading follows Rebbi Yishmael, who holds that {\i meitav sadehu} is assessed based on the {\i nizak}\u8217's best property, not the {\i maziq}\u8217's. Under this view, the {\i nizak} cannot collect property of higher quality than his own best field. If the {\i nizak}\u8217's top-quality land is equivalent to the {\i maziq}\u8217's lowest-quality land, then from a Torah perspective the {\i nizak} is entitled only to grade C of the {\i maziq}. The rabbinic {\i tikkun olam} enactment upgraded this to allow collection from grade A of the {\i maziq}, but {\i gabei yasmi okmuha rabanan ad\u8217'oraita} \u8212- for orphans, they reverted to the Torah baseline, which in this special case is grade C. The {\i braitah} thus does not refute Rava\u8217's general theory; it represents a narrow case where the Torah entitlement itself is grade C.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Abaye vs.\u160 ?Rava\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Baraita Evidence\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Despite Rava\u8217's elegant explanations, the {\i gemara} continues to press. Rebbi Eliezer Nivta\u8217'ah\u8217's teaching states: {\i ein nifroin min nechsei yesomim ela min ha-ziboris \u8212- v\u8217'afilu hen idis} \u8212- you collect from orphans only from grade C, even if it is grade A property. The {\i gemara} reads this as apparently meaning: even if the document grants the creditor an explicit lien on grade A property, collection from orphans is still limited to grade C. This would be a direct refutation of Rava\u8217's position.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shafay Idis Redefined\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} deflects the challenge through a reinterpretation: {\i mai idis? Shafay idis} \u8212- when the {\i braitah} says \u8220"even grade A,\u8221" it does not mean explicitly-documented grade A liens. Rather, it refers to {\i shafay idis} \u8212- the marginal edges of an otherwise high-quality field, land situated near a river that is periodically flooded and is therefore actually grade C in value, despite being physically part of a top-grade estate. Tosafot explains: fertile land along a riverbank that is regularly inundated by overflow is essentially grazing land \u8212- it looks like part of a prime field but functions as grade C. This land, though nominally associated with an {\i idis} parcel, is reclassified as {\i ziboris}. The {\i braitah} is therefore saying: even when collecting from orphans, one may not take even the {\i shafay idis} of the estate at above its true grade C value. This interpretation preserves Rava\u8217's thesis intact.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Damages & Orphans (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shafay Idis Teaching\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava has his own distinct teaching about {\i shafay idis} in the context of damage law, which goes beyond the orphan discussion. If someone damaged grade C land ({\i ziboris}), the {\i nizak} ordinarily collects from grade A. But if the damaged land was {\i shafay idis} \u8212- the flooded margins of an {\i idis} field, which is in effect sub-grade-C, a kind of grade D \u8212- then {\i goveh min ha-beinonis}, the {\i nizak} collects only from grade B, not from grade A. The logic appears to be that the degraded nature of the damaged land correspondingly reduces the quality from which compensation is drawn. This ruling of Rava is striking \u8212- ordinarily a {\i nizak} always collects from the best property, even if it was the worst property that was damaged. The {\i shafay idis} case represents an exception whose underlying rationale is not entirely transparent, but Rava\u8217's position is clear.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The conclusion of the orphan discussion, however, remains as stated: all collection from orphans, whatever its Torah-level basis, is limited to {\i ziboris}. Rava\u8217's position that Torah-level entitlements are preserved against orphans is logically coherent, but the weight of the {\i baraisos} consistently pushes back toward a blanket grade C rule for orphans in all cases. Rava manages to reinterpret each challenge, but the plain reading of the {\i baraisos} consistently supports Abaye\u8217's position: regardless of what one is entitled to under Torah law, the Rabbis instituted a uniform {\i ziboris} rule for orphans.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Adult vs.\u160 ?Minor\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Scope Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now turns to a question raised by Rav Acha bar Adda: {\i mi yesomim she\u8217'amru \u8212- ketanim o afilu gedolim?} When the Rabbis said one collects from orphans only {\i ziboris}, were they speaking specifically of minor orphans, or does this apply even to adult orphans?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Minors Only\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One approach: the Rabbis made this enactment to protect orphans, and minors are the paradigmatic case requiring protection. Their interests cannot be adequately safeguarded by themselves, and the {\i takanah} ensures they are not taken advantage of. Adults, by contrast, can manage their own affairs \u8212- once they reach majority they can appear in court, negotiate, and advocate for themselves. Under this reading, the {\i takanah} applies only to minors.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Lack Incentive\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The alternative approach reframes the entire question. Perhaps the grade C rule for orphans is not a proactive protective {\i takanah} at all, but simply the absence of the incentive that normally upgrades creditors to grade B. The reasoning: a creditor\u8217's entitlement to grade B rests on the rabbinic desire to incentivize lending \u8212- {\i lo tina\u8217'el deles bifnei lovin}. But the creditor\u8217's calculation when deciding whether to lend money centers on the normal case of collection from a living debtor. The relatively rare scenario that the debtor will die before repaying and the creditor will have to collect from the estate was not the eventuality that the Rabbis needed to incentivize against. Therefore, for collection from an estate inherited by orphans, no upgrade was given \u8212- not as a protection for orphans per se, but simply because the {\i takanah} upgrading creditors to grade B was never extended to that scenario. Under this view, even adult orphans would be subject to grade C collection, because the question is not about their age or capacity for self-protection, but about whether the incentive-based upgrade applies to this type of collection at all.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Damages Implication\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If the rationale is the absence of a creditor incentive, however, a difficulty arises with the earlier {\i gemara} that applied grade C even to damage claims collected from orphans. Damage collection is not about incentivizing creditors \u8212- there is no {\i ba\u8217'al chov} seeking encouragement to lend money. If the grade C rule for orphans is merely the absence of an incentive enactment, it should not apply to {\i nezikin} at all. Yet the earlier discussion showed that even damage claims are limited to grade C when collected from orphans. This suggests the rule {\i is} a proactive protection of orphans, not merely an incentive absence \u8212- which would in turn support the view that it applies only to minor orphans.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Oath Proof\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} attempts to resolve the question through a {\i tannaitic} teaching: {\i yesomim she\u8217'amru \u8212- gedolim, v\u8217'ein tzarich lomar ketanim} \u8212- the very orphans the Rabbis spoke of are adults, and this is certainly true of minors as well. The {\i gemara} challenges this proof, arguing that perhaps this rule was stated only with respect to the requirement of a {\i shevuah} \u8212- the oath a creditor must swear that the debt was never paid. When collecting from orphans, the creditor must swear that their father never discharged the debt. In that context, {\i degadol bemilei de\u8217'avuh kekatan dami} \u8212- even an adult, with respect to his father\u8217's financial dealings, is like a minor: he has no personal knowledge of whether the debt was paid, and therefore the {\i shevuah} requirement applies regardless of the orphan\u8217's age. But this logic is specific to the {\i shevuah} \u8212- perhaps when it comes to grade C property, the rule applies only to minor orphans.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Halacha All Orphans\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} rules: {\i v\u8217'hilchata \u8212- yesomim she\u8217'amru gedolim, v\u8217'ein tzarich lomar ketanim, bein le-shevuah bein le-ziboris} \u8212- the law applies to both adults and minors, both with respect to the oath and with respect to limiting collection to grade C property.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gemara Tension\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The adoption of this ruling, however, creates a tension with the logic presented earlier in the {\i sugya}. If the grade C rule applies even to adult orphans, the explanation that it is merely the absence of a creditor incentive gains force \u8212- since the protection-of-orphans rationale would not naturally extend to adults who can protect themselves. But the earlier {\i gemara} applied grade C even to {\i nezikin} collected from orphans, which fits the proactive-protection theory, not the incentive-absence theory. These two {\i gemara} passages thus pull in different directions: the ruling that adults are included pushes toward the incentive-absence theory, while the application to damage claims pushes toward the proactive-protection theory. The two positions do not appear fully reconciled.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Encumbered Property\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sold Property Rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} establishes: {\i ein nifra\u8217'in mi-nechasim meshubadim b\u8217'makom she-yesh bnei chorin} \u8212- one may not collect from encumbered property that has been sold to a third party as long as the debtor still has free assets available. Even if the creditor is entitled to grade B property and the debtor sold off all his grade B land, the creditor may not pursue the purchasers while the debtor still holds grade C assets. The creditor must exhaust the debtor\u8217's own holdings first.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gift Application\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The question then raised is whether this rule extends to gifts. The case of a sale is governed by clear policy logic: a creditor cannot pursue a buyer\u8217's land as long as other assets remain. But does the same protection extend to someone who received land as a {\i matanah}, a gift?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Buyer Protection\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One approach holds that the protection was enacted specifically for buyers: {\i takanata hi d\u8217'avdu rabanan mishum psida d\u8217'lekochot} \u8212- the Rabbis were concerned that commerce would be undermined if purchasers could not rely on the security of their acquisitions. People must be willing to buy land, and they will only do so if they know the seller\u8217's creditors cannot come after them except as a last resort. This rationale applies to buyers, but a recipient of a gift has paid nothing and has no comparable reliance interest. Perhaps the Rabbis saw no need to protect gift recipients from having their received property seized by a creditor.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Both Protected\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The alternative holds that gifts too are protected: {\i matanah nami} \u8212- even a gift is an institution the Rabbis wish to preserve.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gift Institution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The reasoning is that gift-giving is not truly random or gratuitous: {\i i lav d\u8217'is lei hana\u8217'ah minei lo yehav lei matanah} \u8212- one does not give a gift without some benefit or relationship that motivated the giving. Whether through friendship, gratitude, or some form of mutual relationship, gifts express something real in the social fabric. The institution of gift-giving, like the institution of commerce, is something the Rabbis want to protect. If recipients of gifts cannot rely on keeping what they received, the willingness to give gifts \u8212- and the social bonds that gifts express \u8212- would be undermined. Therefore, even a gift recipient is protected by the rule that the creditor must collect from the debtor\u8217's remaining assets before seizing encumbered property, whether that property was sold or given away.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Deathbed Gifts\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Proportional Division\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Mar Kashisha son of Rav Chisda raises a {\i braitah} concerning a {\i shchiv mera} \u8212- a person on his deathbed \u8212- who instructs that specific sums be distributed to multiple beneficiaries: 200 zuz to Reuven, 300 to Shimon, 400 to Levi. The estate, however, contains only 700 zuz, insufficient to cover the total of 900. The rule: {\i ein omrim kol ha-kodem ba-shtar zachah} \u8212- one does not apply a first-come-first-served rule simply because one beneficiary was mentioned before another. All three distributions were declared simultaneously, and we cannot treat the order of speaking as creating priority. Accordingly, the 700 zuz is divided proportionally: Rashi explains this as dividing the estate into nine units, with Reuven receiving two-ninths, Shimon three-ninths, and Levi four-ninths.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Debt Effect All\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If a creditor then comes forward with a {\i shtar chov} \u8212- a promissory note establishing that the deceased owed him money \u8212- the debt is deducted from the total estate before distribution. All three beneficiaries lose proportionately, since they share the estate on equal footing from the moment of the declaration.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sequential Language\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The rule changes if the deathbed declaration included the word {\i acharav} \u8212- \u8220"after him.\u8221" If the dying person said: give 200 to Reuven, and after him give 300 to Shimon, and after him give 400 to Levi \u8212- {\i omrim kol ha-kodem ba-shtar zachah} \u8212- here priority does apply. Reuven receives his full 200 zuz first; Shimon receives his 300 from whatever remains; Levi receives his 400 from whatever remains after that.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Debt Last Only\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 When a creditor presents a {\i shtar chov} in this sequential case, the debt is deducted from the end of the line. Only Levi, the last-named beneficiary, loses out: since the sequential structure means we work backward, and since the estate is treated as having been encumbered from the deceased\u8217's lifetime, it is as though Levi\u8217's share was always the most vulnerable. The logic is that the prior beneficiaries had already received their portions sequentially, and it is Levi\u8217's final share that bears the shortfall.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Backup Collection\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If Levi\u8217's portion is entirely exhausted and still does not cover the debt, the creditor then goes back to Shimon \u8212- {\i goveh mishi-lefanav}. If Shimon\u8217's portion is also insufficient, the creditor proceeds back to Reuven. The sequential priority cuts both ways: first priority in receiving, but also last exposure to the creditor \u8212- working through the sequence in reverse when collection is needed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Baraita Evidence\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} cites this {\i braitah} as evidence bearing on the earlier question of whether gift recipients are protected from creditors in the same way purchasers are. Since the {\i shchiv mera} scenario involves gifts \u8212- the dying person is distributing his estate as gifts to the beneficiaries \u8212- and the {\i braitah} discusses how creditors collect from those gifts, the {\i braitah}\u8217's structure reveals assumptions about when and how {\i matanah} recipients can be reached by creditors. The full implications of this {\i braitah} for the gift-protection question will be explored in the continuation of the {\i sugya}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Gittin 51\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Third-Party Protection\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rule: \u1489 ?\u1504 ?\u1497 ? \u1495 ?\u1493 ?\u1512 ?\u1497 ?\u1503 ? First\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The daf opens in the middle of a series of {\i tikkun olam} enactments concerning the collection of debts. One such enactment establishes that a creditor cannot collect from {\i meshubadim} \u8212- property encumbered by a lien that is now held by a third-party purchaser \u8212- so long as the original debtor still possesses available {\i bnei chorin}, that is, unencumbered assets. This principle applies even where the property in the hands of the third party is of a superior grade. If the debtor retains even inferior-grade land, the creditor must exhaust that option first before turning to the purchaser. The rationale is straightforward: purchasers of land must be protected from the prospect of creditors bypassing the original debtor and proceeding directly against them. Without such protection, the entire market for land transactions would be chilled.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The question the Gemara now raises is whether this protection extends beyond purchasers to recipients of {\i matana} \u8212- outright gifts. If someone received land as a gift, can a creditor be forced to first collect from the debtor\u8217's remaining assets before seizing the gifted property?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Braita: Distribution Ord\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 To explore this question, the Gemara cites a {\i braita} concerning a {\i shechiv meira} \u8212- a person on his deathbed \u8212- who distributes his estate to three recipients in amounts of 200, 300, and 400. If he simply declares that each person should receive these amounts without using the word {\i acharav} (\u8220"after him\u8221"), the distribution is understood as simultaneous and equal. Should the estate prove insufficient to cover the full 900, each recipient receives a proportional share. Similarly, if a creditor subsequently appears with a {\i shtar chov}, the debt is collected proportionally from all three recipients.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If, however, the dying man uses the word {\i acharav} \u8212- \u8220"and after him\u8221" \u8212- establishing a sequential order of priority, then in the case of a shortfall, the first recipient receives the full allotted amount before anything is distributed to the second, and the second receives in full before anything reaches the third. When a creditor appears with a lien, the Gemara explains that he collects first from the last recipient \u8212- the one with the weakest claim \u8212- and only if that is insufficient does he proceed to the second, and then the first.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gift Protection Analysis\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Here the Gemara\u8217's analysis becomes sharply focused. Consider a scenario in which the first recipient received grade-B property and the later recipients received grade-C property. The creditor, who held a lien on the grade-B property predating the distribution, should ostensibly be able to proceed directly against the first recipient and demand his grade-B property. Why is he instead directed to collect from the last recipient, who holds only grade-C land?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The implicit answer \u8212- and the proof for our case \u8212- is that once property is given away, whether by sale or by gift, the creditor is confined to collecting from whatever the debtor still held at each successive moment. The fact that the Gemara does not permit the creditor to seize the grade-B property gifted to the first recipient, even though his liens attached to it, demonstrates that even a {\i matana} entitles its recipient to the same protection extended to purchasers. The creditor is relegated to the last person in the distribution chain, working backward only if earlier assets are insufficient.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Debts vs.\u160 ?Gifts\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara declines to accept this as a definitive proof. It offers an alternative reading: perhaps the {\i braita} is not discussing gifts at all. Perhaps the {\i shechiv meira} was not distributing gifts but rather acknowledging debts \u8212- declaring that he owed these amounts to each of the three recipients. Under this reading, {\i tnu} (\u8220"give\u8221") means \u8220"pay off,\u8221" and the protection afforded to the recipients follows naturally from their status as creditors being repaid, not from any extension of the gift-protection principle.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara immediately challenges this reinterpretation. If all three are creditors, priority among them should follow the dates of their respective {\i shtarot}. The earlier-dated document should prevail. So why does the {\i braita} establish priority based on the sequential language of the declaration? The Gemara responds: {\i v\u8217'leika shtar} \u8212- they have no documentary proof of their debts. The dying man is simply admitting that he owes them money, but since there is no written {\i shtar}, the order of the declaration document itself determines priority. The creditor with a formal {\i shtar} meanwhile has priority over all of them because his lien is documented, even though he is directed to collect from the last recipient first.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Declaration Priority\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then addresses the {\i braita}\u8217's phrase {\i kol hakodam bashtar zachah} \u8212- \u8220"whoever precedes in the document acquires.\u8221" On the creditor-repayment reading, this refers to whoever appears first in the deathbed declaration document, not to whoever holds the earlier-dated debt instrument. The phrase {\i bishtar pekadata} \u8212- \u8220"in the declaration document\u8221" \u8212- clarifies that it is the order of names in the dying man\u8217's statement that governs, not the chronology of independent debt records.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara ultimately offers two additional resolutions. First, it suggests that perhaps {\i goveh min ha\u8217'acharon} \u8212- \u8220"he collects from the last\u8221" \u8212- does not mean the creditor physically takes the last recipient\u8217's property. Rather, the creditor takes the grade-B property to which his lien attaches, the estate is then redistributed, and it is the last recipient who bears the loss in the reallocation. The recipient of the gift is not physically dispossessed; the mathematical burden falls on whoever is last in line. Second, the Gemara suggests that perhaps all recipients received property of equal grade, and the question of differential protection simply does not arise on the facts of the {\i braita}. On this reading, the question of whether gift recipients enjoy the same protection as purchasers remains unresolved. As is often the case with {\i tikkun olam} enactments, protecting one party necessarily comes at the expense of another.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Indeterminate Debts\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Daughters & Wives\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara moves to the next cluster of {\i tikkun olam} cases: {\i ein motzin le\u8217'achilat peirot} \u8212- we do not allow collection from {\i meshubadim} for certain categories of ongoing obligations. The common thread is that the amount of the liability is not yet determinable at the time a third party purchases the property.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Two paradigmatic examples illustrate the problem. First, a man\u8217's daughters are entitled to be supported from his estate after his death until they marry. But the number of daughters he will ultimately have, and the duration of their support, cannot be known at the time he sells property to a purchaser. If daughters could collect their ongoing {\i mezonos} from third parties, purchasers would have no way of assessing the risk they were assuming. Second, when a person sells land that subsequently turns out to have been stolen, the purchaser is entitled to compensation \u8212- including for improvements \u8212- from the seller\u8217's remaining assets. But if the purchaser has made substantial capital improvements, the amount owed to him vastly exceeds what could have been anticipated at the time any subsequent purchaser acquired those assets.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Stolen Land Risk\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The second scenario deserves elaboration because it involves a chain of transactions. A purchases land from B, who in turn purchased it from C. It subsequently emerges that C never had valid title. A is dispossessed and turns to B for compensation. B has no money but has sold other properties to third parties. A holds an implicit lien on B\u8217's estate \u8212- the standard guarantee embedded in any land transaction \u8212- but the amount of that lien is unknown. It includes the purchase price and potentially the full value of capital improvements A made. The people who purchased B\u8217's other properties cannot know, at the time of their purchase, how much liability they are implicitly assuming from A\u8217's claim against B. For this reason, they are protected: A may only collect from property B still holds directly, not from what B has sold to others.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ulla vs.\u160 ?Not Written\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara asks: {\i mai ta\u8217'ama} \u8212- what is the underlying rationale? {\i Amar Ulla amar Reish Lakish: lefi she\u8217'ein ketuvim} \u8212- because these obligations are not written. On this view, the problem is not primarily that the amount is uncertain, but that because the debt is unwritten, purchasers are simply unaware of it. They do not know that any such risk exists, and it would be fundamentally unfair to subject them to a liability they had no way of discovering.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rashi notes a difficulty with this formulation: in the case of the stolen land and the right to compensation, the obligation is actually written into the standard {\i shtar} of sale. Yet Ulla and Reish Lakish still say the reason is that it is \u8220"not written.\u8221" Rashi resolves this by explaining that even when it appears in the document, the indeterminate nature of the obligation means that no one pays serious attention to it \u8212- it is effectively unknown.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kesubah Tension\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Abba challenges Ulla directly: the obligation of a husband to support his wife and daughters is not written in any document, yet {\i Chazal} established that it carries the full legal weight of a written {\i shtar}. If the rationale for refusing collection from third parties is simply that the debt is not written, why does the wife\u8217's and daughters\u8217' {\i mezonos} \u8212- which is treated {\i k\u8217'man dichtivei}, as if it were written \u8212- still not entitle them to collect from {\i meshubadim}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Zeira poses the same question to Rabbi Assi, who had transmitted the same principle in the name of Rabbi Yochanan. The answer in both cases is identical: {\i meikara hachi itkin} \u8212- this is precisely how {\i Chazal} structured the obligation from the outset. The wife\u8217's and daughters\u8217' {\i mezonos} is {\i ketuvim etzel bnei chorin} \u8212- it has the force of a written document with respect to the estate itself \u8212- but {\i ein ketuvim etzel meshubadim} \u8212- it does not carry that force against third-party purchasers. The protection of purchasers was built into the very structure of the rabbinic enactment.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Chanina: Not Fixed\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Chanina offers a cleaner and more intuitive rationale: {\i mipnei she\u8217'ein ketzuvim} \u8212- not because the debts are unwritten, but because the amounts are not fixed. When a purchaser acquires property and assumes an indeterminate liability, the unfairness lies not in his ignorance that some risk exists, but in the impossibility of knowing how large that risk might be. An open-ended obligation cannot fairly bind third parties. This explanation, the Gemara implies, is more satisfying than the \u8220"not written\u8221" formulation, and the subsequent discussion will largely revolve around Rabbi Chanina\u8217's position.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Fixed Not Written\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Both or One?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now poses the question directly: {\i ibaya lehu, Rabbi Chanina ketzuvim vektuvim ba\u8217'i?} Does Rabbi Chanina require both conditions \u8212- that the amount be fixed {\i and} that it be written \u8212- for a lien to be enforceable against third parties? Or is a fixed amount alone sufficient, even without a written document?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rashi suggests that the case of a fixed but unwritten debt is a {\i milve al peh} \u8212- an oral loan. Tosafot rejects this, noting that an oral loan has independent reasons for not creating liens against third parties. Tosafot instead understands the question as concerning other categories of debt \u8212- for instance, {\i mazik}, tortious damage \u8212- where the amount might be fixed and determined, but where no formal document was ever drawn up. The question is whether such debts, fixed in amount but lacking documentary form, can give rise to liens on property in the hands of third parties.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two Daughters Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara introduces a {\i tana} case to sharpen the question. A man dies leaving two daughters and a son. The son inherits the estate. Each daughter is entitled, upon marriage, to a dowry \u8212- {\i parnasa} \u8212- equal to one-tenth of the estate, a fixed percentage established by rabbinic enactment. The first daughter marries and collects her one-tenth. Before the second daughter can collect her one-tenth, the son dies without children. The daughters now inherit the estate from the son. The second daughter argues: I have a lien on the estate for my one-tenth, just as my sister had hers. My sister already collected from the top. I want my one-tenth off the top before we split the remainder equally.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is precisely the structure of a debt that is {\i katzuv} \u8212- fixed in amount \u8212- but not written in any document. The {\i parnasa} obligation arises automatically from the father\u8217's death; no {\i shtar} is drawn up memorializing it. Rabbi Yochanan rules that the second daughter loses out \u8212- {\i shniya vitrah}. She cannot collect her {\i parnasa} from the property now held by her sister.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Parnasa Collectible\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Chanina is sharply critical of this ruling. He invokes an even stronger case: {\i motzin l\u8217'parnasa v\u8217'ein motzin l\u8217'mezonos} \u8212- we allow collection of {\i parnasa} from {\i meshubadim}, even though we do not allow collection of {\i mezonos} from {\i meshubadim}. The distinction is that {\i mezonos} is indeterminate in amount, while {\i parnasa} \u8212- the one-tenth dowry \u8212- is a fixed figure. If the father sold property to third parties during his lifetime and the daughter\u8217's {\i parnasa} was not paid before he died, she can seize that property from the third-party purchasers to satisfy her fixed claim.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If that is so, argues Rabbi Chanina, then certainly she can collect from her sister \u8212- who is not even a third-party purchaser but a co-heir sitting on the same estate. He expresses astonishment that Rabbi Yochanan would say the second daughter loses out entirely. This case of {\i parnasa} \u8212- {\i demikat katz, michtav lo ktivan} \u8212- a fixed amount that is not written \u8212- is a direct proof that Rabbi Chanina\u8217's position is correct: a fixed amount, even without a written document, creates an enforceable lien.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Parnasa Well-Known\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara responds on behalf of those who require the debt to be written: {\i shani parnasa, keivan d\u8217'is lei kala, k\u8217'man dichtiva dami} \u8212- {\i parnasa} is different, because it is so universally known and publicized that it is treated as if it were written. Everyone knows that daughters receive a one-tenth dowry from their father\u8217's estate; no one can claim ignorance of this obligation. Therefore, those who purchase land from a father are on constructive notice of the daughters\u8217' {\i parnasa} claim, and the daughters may accordingly collect from {\i meshubadim}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This response, however, threatens to swallow the distinction entirely. If every fixed and well-known obligation is treated as if it were written, then any debt sufficiently established by common knowledge would qualify. The Gemara nonetheless proceeds with this resolution and turns to further cases.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Stepdaughter Kinyan\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Mativ Rav Huna bar Manoach}: A {\i braita} presents a case involving a stepdaughter. A woman remarries, and as a condition of the marriage her new husband commits to supporting her daughter \u8212- his stepdaughter \u8212- for a fixed period of five years. This is a fixed-duration, fixed-amount obligation that is not embodied in any formal {\i shtar}. The husband subsequently divorces the mother, but the stepdaughter\u8217's right to five years of support survives the divorce. The mother then remarries again, and her second husband makes the same commitment. The stepdaughter thus holds two parallel five-year support obligations from two different men.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 When both men die, the {\i braita} draws a stark contrast. Their own daughters \u8212- {\i benoseihem} \u8212- can only collect their {\i mezonos} from {\i bnei chorin}, from the estate property directly held. But the stepdaughter \u8212- {\i vehi nizonos minechasim meshubadim} \u8212- can collect from {\i meshubadim}, from property the stepfather sold to third parties. She is treated {\i k\u8217'ba\u8217'alas chov} \u8212- like a creditor holding a documented lien. This appears to be a direct proof that a fixed but unwritten obligation creates enforceable liens against third parties.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kinyan Distinction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara deflects this proof as well. {\i Hacha b\u8217'mai askinan b\u8217'shekinu miyado} \u8212- the case involves an obligation that was formalized through a {\i kinyan}, a formal act of acquisition such as {\i chalipin}, the exchange of a {\i sudar}. Because the husband performed a {\i kinyan}, the stepdaughter\u8217's claim has the force of a documented obligation even without a written {\i shtar}. This is what entitles her to collect from {\i meshubadim}; it is not proof that a merely verbal fixed-amount obligation would carry the same force.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara pushes back: if the {\i kinyan} is the distinguishing factor, then why not say the same for the husband\u8217's own daughters? If he performed a {\i kinyan} committing to support his stepdaughter, surely one could equally say he performed one for his daughters. The Gemara responds: {\i b\u8217'shekinu l\u8217'zo v\u8217'lo konu l\u8217'zo} \u8212- the {\i kinyan} was performed for the stepdaughter, not for the daughters. The {\i braita} is specifically constructed to contrast these two situations.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara objects again: {\i mai pseeka} \u8212- why is this so definitive? The {\i braita}\u8217's entire point seems to be the contrast between the stepdaughter and the daughters. Reading into it a specific {\i kinyan} for one and not the other seems forced. The Gemara responds: {\i bas ishto dehavya b\u8217'sha\u8217'as kinyan mahni lah kinyan} \u8212- the stepdaughter was already in existence at the time the {\i kinyan} was performed, so the {\i kinyan} was effective for her. {\i Beto d\u8217'lo havya b\u8217'sha\u8217'as kinyan} \u8212- his own daughters, who did not yet exist when he married their mother and undertook his obligations via {\i kinyan}, cannot benefit from that {\i kinyan}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara anticipates an objection: one can construct a case where his daughters {\i were} present at the time of a {\i kinyan} \u8212- for instance, if he divorced his wife and then remarried her, at which point his existing daughters were alive when he renewed his obligations. In that scenario, {\i dehavu tarye b\u8217'sha\u8217'as kinyan}, both the stepdaughter and the daughters were present. The Gemara\u8217's final answer is dispositive: {\i beto keivan d\u8217'bitnai beis din ka\u8217'achlah, lo mahni lah kinyan} \u8212- because his daughter\u8217's right to support derives from a rabbinic obligation, an independent {\i kinyan} is superfluous and adds nothing. It cannot elevate her rights beyond what {\i Chazal} already prescribed, which is collection limited to {\i bnei chorin}. The stepdaughter, by contrast, has no rabbinic entitlement at all; her entire claim rests on the {\i kinyan} the stepfather voluntarily performed, and it is precisely that {\i kinyan} which grants her the status of a {\i ba\u8217'alas chov} entitled to collect from {\i meshubadim}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara raises one final difficulty: {\i v\u8217'chi migra gara} \u8212- surely the daughter, who began with a stronger foundational right, should not end up in a worse position simply because that right derives from {\i beis din} rather than from a voluntary act. The Gemara\u8217's answer is that since the daughter\u8217's obligation is so thoroughly rooted in {\i beis din}, there is a concern that the father may have already prepaid it by setting aside funds in escrow. Because of this possibility, {\i beis din} will not permit her to collect from third parties even if a {\i kinyan} were theoretically relevant. This is an admittedly complex resolution, but the essential upshot is that the stepdaughter case cannot serve as straightforward proof that a fixed, unwritten obligation entitles a claimant to collect from {\i meshubadim}. The {\i kinyan} is doing all the work.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Capital Improvements\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Improvements Before Sale\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara cites one further {\i braita} in pursuit of the question of whether a fixed, unwritten obligation creates enforceable liens. {\i Amar Rabbi Natan: eimasai} \u8212- when is it that the person whose stolen land was improved cannot collect the value of those improvements from third parties? {\i Bizman shekodam mekach sheni lishvacho shel rishon} \u8212- when the third party purchased the property before the improvements were made. In that case, the third-party purchaser did not know and could not have known the extent of the liability he was assuming. He acquired the property without any way to assess the risk.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Aval kodam shvacho shel rishon lemekach sheni} \u8212- but if the improvements were already made before the third party purchased, then when he purchased he knew, or could have known, that there was a specific, determinable claim against the seller\u8217's estate. In that case, {\i goveh minechasim hameshubadim} \u8212- the claimant may collect even from {\i meshubadim}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafot: Amount Fixed\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot reads this {\i braita} as a direct vindication of Rabbi Chanina\u8217's position. Here is a case where the obligation is plainly not written in any document \u8212- it is an implicit warranty embedded in every land transaction \u8212- yet once the amount is fixed and determinable, the claimant may collect from third parties. The critical variable is not documentation but knowability. Once Michael acquired the property after the improvements were already made, the risk was ascertainable, and David\u8217's right to collect is enforceable. This demonstrates that it is the indeterminacy of amount, not the absence of a written instrument, that is the operative concern.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tannaim Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara draws its conclusion: {\i tannai hi} \u8212- this dispute between the \u8220"not written\u8221" rationale and the \u8220"not fixed\u8221" rationale reflects a genuine disagreement between {\i tannaim}. A {\i braita} explicitly states that collection from {\i meshubadim} is prohibited for {\i achilat peirot}, improvement of land, and {\i mezon ha\u8217'ishah vehabanot} \u8212- {\i mipnei tikkun ha\u8217'olam}, for the sake of the public good \u8212- {\i lefi she\u8217'ein ketuvim}, because these obligations are not written. This {\i braita} explicitly frames the issue as one of documentation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yosei pushes back within the {\i braita} itself: {\i vechi mah tikkun ha\u8217'olam yesh bazeh?} \u8212- what kind of {\i tikkun olam} is achieved by pointing to the absence of a written document? The real issue, he argues, is {\i halo ein katzuv} \u8212- that the amount is not fixed. It is the open-ended, indeterminate nature of the liability that makes it impossible for purchasers to assess what they are assuming, and it is that impossibility which justifies the rabbinic protection. By the end of the discussion, the Gemara\u8217's position is that if an obligation is fixed in amount and the purchaser knew of the risk at the time of acquisition, collection from third parties is permitted \u8212- even without a formal {\i shtar}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Finder Dispute\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Finder Exempt\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara turns to another {\i tikkun olam} enactment: {\i ha-motzi metziah} \u8212- the finder of lost property is exempt from taking an oath {\i mipnei tikkun olam}. The scenario is familiar: a person returns a lost object to its owner, who then claims that the original contents were more than what was returned. Under the normal rules of {\i modeh b\u8217'miktzat} \u8212- partial admission \u8212- the finder, by acknowledging the object as the owner\u8217's while contesting the amount, would ordinarily be required to take a {\i shvua}. But if finders were routinely subjected to oaths every time an owner disputed the recovered contents, no one would bother returning lost objects. {\i Tikkun olam} therefore exempts the finder from this obligation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Wallets vs.\u160 ?Oxen\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Yitzchak, however, rules in a manner that appears to entirely disregard this principle. He states: if a person says to another, \u8220"You found two wallets of mine that were tied together,\u8221" and the finder replies, \u8220"I found only one,\u8221" the finder must take a {\i shvua} \u8212- it is a case of {\i modeh b\u8217'miktzat}. But if the claim is \u8220"You found two of my oxen bound together\u8221" and the finder responds \u8220"There was only one,\u8221" the finder need not take a {\i shvua}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The distinction rests on the natural behavior of the objects. {\i Shvarim mitnachu hadadai} \u8212- oxen, even when tied together, will naturally separate. Therefore the claimant\u8217's assertion that both oxen were found together cannot be stated with certainty; it is a {\i ta\u8217'anat shema}, a claim based on conjecture. Without a definitive claim, there is no {\i modeh b\u8217'miktzat}. Wallets, by contrast, {\i kisim lo mitnachu hadadai} \u8212- do not naturally come apart. If you found one, you found both. The claimant\u8217's assertion is therefore a {\i ta\u8217'anat barai}, a certain claim, and the finder\u8217's partial denial constitutes a genuine {\i modeh b\u8217'miktzat} requiring a {\i shvua}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i braita} adds a variation: if the finder says \u8220"I found both oxen and I have already returned one to you,\u8221" and the owner denies receiving any return, the finder must take a {\i shvua} in that case as well.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R.E.b.Y. Opinion\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara notes that Rav Yitzchak\u8217's rulings appear to contradict the {\i mishna}\u8217's {\i tikkun olam} exemption. His answer: {\i hu d\u8217'amar k\u8217'Rabbi Eliezer ben Yaakov} \u8212- Rav Yitzchak follows the opinion of Rabbi Eliezer ben Yaakov, who holds that even a finder of lost property may be required to take a {\i shvua}. This is a {\i machloket tannaim}, and Rav Yitzchak has ruled in accordance with the minority position.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Admission Oath\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now elaborates on Rabbi Eliezer ben Yaakov\u8217's position. {\i Rabbi Eliezer ben Yaakov omer: pe\u8217'amim she\u8217'adam nishba al ta\u8217'anat atzmo} \u8212- there are circumstances in which a person may be required to take a {\i shvua} based on their own admission, even in the absence of a formal legal claim by the opposing party. This captures the very situation of the {\i motzi metziah}: the finder voluntarily came forward and is now subjected to a {\i shvua} not because the owner prosecuted a winning claim, but because the finder\u8217's own admission creates the structure of {\i modeh b\u8217'miktzat}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Half-Debt Admission\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara specifies the case: {\i maneh l\u8217'avicha b\u8217'yadi} \u8212- a person approaches the son of a deceased and says, \u8220"Your father deposited one hundred {\i zuz} with me. I must tell you that I spent half of it, so I owe you fifty.\u8221" He has spontaneously admitted to a debt, declared that half has been satisfied, and thereby placed himself in the position of one who admits to part of a claim. {\i V\u8217'harei zeh nishba} \u8212- he must take a {\i shvua} on the fifty he claims to have paid. This is the paradigm case of what Rabbi Eliezer ben Yaakov calls an oath on one\u8217's own admission.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Migo Analysis\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Migo Principle\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The obvious difficulty with Rabbi Eliezer ben Yaakov\u8217's position is the principle of {\i migo}. {\i Migo d\u8217'i ba\u8217'i shatik} \u8212- I could have simply remained silent. Had this person said nothing, the heir would have had no claim against him at all. The fact that he voluntarily came forward and disclosed the debt should render him credible when he says he repaid half. Why, if I had the option of total silence, should my partial admission subject me to a {\i shvua}? This is a powerful {\i migo} argument, and the Chachamim employ it to exempt the finder: he could have simply not disclosed that he found the object, so his voluntary disclosure entitles him to be believed when he gives an account of the contents.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ignorant Claimant\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav resolves the difficulty: {\i b\u8217'ta\u8217'anat katan} \u8212- the case involves a {\i katan}. The heir confronting the debtor is a child who is pressing him for money. The debtor therefore cannot say \u8220"I could have kept quiet\u8221"; he was, after a fashion, being challenged. The {\i migo} of \u8220"I could have remained silent\u8221" is inapplicable when there is some form of pressure, even if that pressure lacks full {\i halachic} force. The term {\i ta\u8217'anat atzmo} \u8212- \u8220"his own claim\u8221" \u8212- therefore means that while there was technically a claim against him, it was not a legally operative one, and his response is effectively self-generated.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Katan Objection\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara objects: a {\i katan}\u8217's claim has no {\i halachic} standing at all. {\i D\u8217'tnan: ein nishba\u8217'in al ta\u8217'anat cheresh, shoteh, v\u8217'katan} \u8212- one does not take an oath in response to the claim of a deaf-mute, an imbecile, or a minor. If the {\i katan}\u8217's challenge carries no legal weight, why does it neutralize the debtor\u8217's {\i migo}? The {\i migo} should still apply: even facing a {\i katan}, I could simply have denied everything, so my partial admission should be credited.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ignorant = Katan\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara reframes the case: {\i mai katan? gadol} \u8212- when we say {\i katan}, we do not mean a literal minor. We mean an adult who is ignorant of his father\u8217's financial affairs \u8212- one who does not know what debts his father held or was owed. {\i D\u8217'lgabei milei d\u8217'avuh katan hu} \u8212- with respect to his father\u8217's dealings, he is like a minor. This adult heir is pressing the debtor on a matter he does not fully understand. The debtor therefore cannot comfortably say he could have remained completely silent; the heir was making some kind of claim. Yet because the heir is effectively ignorant, his claim lacks the force of a genuine legal {\i ta\u8217'anah}. This is precisely why the Chachamim characterize the debtor\u8217's conduct as {\i meshiv aveida} \u8212- returning lost property \u8212- and exempt him from a {\i shvua}. Rabbi Eliezer ben Yaakov, by contrast, holds that the debtor\u8217's partial admission constitutes {\i modeh b\u8217'miktzat} regardless, and he must swear.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Claim vs.\u160 ?Admission\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara closes with an unresolved aporia. When Rabbi Eliezer ben Yaakov says the person swears {\i al ta\u8217'anat atzmo} \u8212- on his own claim \u8212- the Gemara notes that this description is paradoxical. If there was a genuine claim by the other side, then calling it \u8220"his own claim\u8221" is misleading \u8212- it is the other side\u8217's claim. And if there was no genuine claim by the other side, then {\i modeh b\u8217'miktzat} should not apply, because the {\i migo} of silence was available. But if we say the heir\u8217's claim {\i was} legally meaningful, then describing the admission as self-generated seems wrong. {\i Kullehu ta\u8217'anata nami ta\u8217'anas acherim vehoda\u8217'as atzmo ninhu} \u8212- every case of {\i modeh b\u8217'miktzat} involves a claim by one side and an admission by the other. What is unique about this case?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara leaves this question open. Tomorrow\u8217's session will return to clarify precisely what Rabbi Eliezer ben Yaakov and the Chachamim are debating, and why one side holds the debtor obligated while the other exempts him entirely.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Gittin 52\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Modeh BeMiktzat\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Eliezer vs.\u160 ?Rabanan\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The discussion picks up approximately halfway through 51b, continuing the {\i machlokes} between Rabbi Eliezer ben Yaakov and the {\i rabanan} regarding the circumstances under which one can compel another to take a {\i shvuah} in the cases we have been examining \u8212- and specifically, the question of {\i kemeshiv aveidah}: whether a minor can force someone to take a {\i shvuah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Three Shvuot Categories\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 There is an interesting {\i machlokes rishonim} about which {\i shvuot} this exemption applies to. Three distinct categories of {\i shvuah} are relevant to the discussion. The first is {\i shvuas ed echad}: where a single witness testifies that a debt is owed, the witness cannot extract the debt but his testimony compels the defendant to take a {\i shvuah}. The second is {\i shvuas hapikadon}: where a {\i shomer} \u8212- someone watching over property \u8212- can be compelled to swear that he did not mishandle what was entrusted to him. The third, and the one the {\i gemara} now addresses directly, is {\i shvuas modeh bemiktzas}: where a person admits to part of a claim and is consequently compelled to take a {\i shvuah} regarding the remainder.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} states: {\i ella keRabbah kamipalgei} \u8212- this {\i machlokes} is actually grounded in a principle articulated by Rabbah.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbah\u8217's Chazakah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbah asks: {\i mipnei mah amrah Torah modeh bemiktzas hata\u8217'anah yishava} \u8212- why does the Torah require someone who admits to a partial debt to take a {\i shvuah}? One might wonder why Rabbah needs to inquire into the rationale at all; if it is {\i halacha}, it is {\i halacha}. But the reason Rabbah\u8217's rationale matters here is that it becomes the operative principle for determining how {\i modeh bemiktzas} intersects with the dispute between Rabbi Eliezer ben Yaakov and the {\i rabanan}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbah\u8217's answer: {\i chazakah ain adam meiz panav bifnei ba\u8217'al chovo} \u8212- there is a {\i chazakah}, a presumption, that a person will not brazenly contradict someone to his face with a bald-faced lie. Tosafot formulates this sharply: {\i ain adam meiz kol makom she\u8217'oso kenegdo yode\u8217'a shehu meshaker} \u8212- a person will not be so brazen as to lie to someone who knows with certainty that he is lying. This {\i chazakah} is, admittedly, a claim about human psychology, and one might wonder whether it applies uniformly across all people and eras. Nonetheless, since {\i modeh bemiktzas} is a {\i halacha de\u8217'oraisa}, the operative rule certainly remains in force regardless.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Delay Mechanism\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then traces the psychological logic of the partial admission. Since {\i ain adam meiz panav bifnei ba\u8217'al chovo}, an honest person ought to have admitted to the entire debt. Why, then, does he admit only to part? Because {\i demishta\u173 ?mutei hu dekamishta\u173 ?mit lei} \u8212- he is trying to delay. He knows he owes the full amount, but he lacks the funds to pay it all right now, so he admits to part, pays that immediately, and hopes to buy time until he has the rest: {\i ad dehavu li zuzei ufarna lei}. He intends to pay eventually, but uses the mechanism of {\i modeh bemiktzas} to force a delay \u8212- against the lender\u8217's will.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is this manipulation of the system that the Torah addresses. {\i Ve\u8217'amar Rachmana remei shvuah aleih} \u8212- the Torah imposes a {\i shvuah} on him, as a way of pressuring him to admit the full amount rather than engineer a delay. The {\i shvuah} is designed to make the partial denial untenable.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Eliezer\u8217's Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having established Rabbah\u8217's rationale, the {\i gemara} explains the positions of the {\i tanna\u8217'im}. Rabbi Eliezer ben Yaakov holds {\i lo shna bo velo shna bivno} \u8212- it makes no difference whether one is confronting the original creditor or that creditor\u8217's heir. {\i Eino meiz} \u8212- the debtor will not be brazen even in the presence of the son. The psychological restraint remains operative whether the opposing party is the original lender or his child, and therefore the same {\i shvuah} logic applies.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabanan Counter\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i rabanan} disagree. In their view, {\i bo de\u8217'eino meiz aval livno meiz} \u8212- a person is indeed restrained from lying to the creditor himself, but he will be far more willing to stand up against the creditor\u8217's child, who was not party to the original transaction. Since the psychological impediment is diminished or absent, the very fact that the debtor admits to {\i part} of the claim is no longer surprising \u8212- he could easily have denied everything. His partial admission therefore resembles the conduct of a {\i meshiv aveidah}, someone voluntarily returning a lost object. Because the internal psychological pressure that would normally explain the partial admission is not in play, we give him greater benefit of the doubt, do not compel him to take a {\i shvuah}, and treat him as the honest person he appears to be.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Apotropsin Powers\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Types of Apotropsin\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 With the beginning of the new {\i mishnah} at the top of 52a, the topic shifts entirely to {\i apotropsin} \u8212- appointed guardians who oversee the estates of orphans. The {\i mishnah} and subsequent {\i gemara} distinguish among three types of {\i apotropos}. The first is appointed by the father himself \u8212- anticipating his own death, a father may designate someone to administer the estate on behalf of his minor children. This has clear resonance for any case where a person can foresee his own death in advance; it is an interesting precedent for instruments akin to living wills and advance directives. The second type is self-appointed \u8212- someone who simply steps forward and assumes responsibility for the property. The third is appointed by {\i beit din}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} opens: {\i yetomim shesamkhu etzel ba\u8217'al habayit} \u8212- orphans who have taken up residence with a householder, {\i o sheminah lahen avihen apotropos} \u8212- or whose father designated an {\i apotropos} for them \u8212- {\i apotropos chayav le\u8217'aser peroteihem} \u8212- the {\i apotropos} is obligated to separate {\i ma\u8217'aser} from their produce.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This immediately raises a question. The underlying issue is one of {\i zekhiyyah} and its relationship to ownership: who has the right to separate {\i terumah} and {\i ma\u8217'aser} from produce that belongs to someone else? The {\i gemara} in {\i Kiddushin} in the second {\i perek} distinguishes three different mechanisms by which land was apportioned when the Jewish people entered {\i Eretz Yisrael} with Yehoshua: {\i shelikhut} \u8212- formal agency; {\i zekhiyyah} \u8212- automatically doing something beneficial for another, with a {\i machlokes rishonim} as to whether {\i zekhiyyah} operates through the mechanism of {\i shelikhut}; and {\i apotropos} \u8212- a distinct category unto itself. The {\i apotropos} is not the owner of the produce. A {\i shaliach} can separate {\i terumah} from another\u8217's produce based on a special {\i drashah} from a {\i pasuk}, but the {\i pasuk atem} \u8212- \u8220"you,\u8221" implying only owners \u8212- would seem to exclude an {\i apotropos} from this power.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might ask: why does this matter so urgently, given that {\i katanim} are only obligated in {\i mitzvot} through {\i chinuch} anyway, making any {\i issur} at worst {\i derabanan}? Yet someone must manage their religious and financial lives, and that is precisely what motivates this entire institution of {\i apotropus} \u8212- the {\i yetomim} need someone to oversee their financial dealings and ensure their proper upbringing.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Regarding the underlying mechanism by which {\i apotropsus} functions: Rabbeinu Tam maintains that the {\i drashah} from {\i atem} is only an {\i asmachta}, and the principle excluding {\i apotropsin} from separating {\i terumah} is merely {\i derabanan}. The exception allowing the {\i apotropos} to separate {\i terumah} in the case of actively feeding the {\i yetomim} is itself a {\i derabanan} creation designed to protect them. Other {\i rishonim} propose different approaches. As we will see later, Tosafot Rid invokes {\i hefker beit din hefker} \u8212- the principle that a {\i beit din} can divest and reassign ownership \u8212- as the underlying mechanism that grants the {\i apotropos} his powers. That principle will resurface shortly.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tithes Separation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} raises an apparent contradiction: {\i uvreminhu} \u8212- a {\i beraisa} that seems to exclude {\i apotropsin} from separating {\i terumah}. Rav Chisda resolves the tension with a distinction: {\i kan le-hachil, kan le-haniach} \u8212- one case concerns feeding the {\i yetomim} now, while the other concerns storing produce for the future. When the {\i apotropos} is separating {\i terumah} and {\i ma\u8217'aser} so as to actively feed the {\i yetomim} from the produce at hand, this is permitted. But to separate {\i terumah} from produce that will simply sit in storage for a decade until the orphans mature \u8212- that is not permitted, because they will be fully capable of separating it themselves when they are grown. The {\i gemara} confirms: {\i ve-hatanya apotropsin tormin u-maasrin le-hachil aval lo le-haniach} \u8212- the {\i beraisa} supports Rav Chisda\u8217's distinction, and Rashi notes that {\i ve-hatanya} here is used {\i be-nichuta}, as a supporting citation rather than a challenge.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Braita\u8217's Restrictions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i beraisa} continues with a comprehensive list of what {\i apotropsin} may and may not do. They may sell animals, slaves, houses, fields, and vineyards \u8212- {\i le-hachil}, to actively support the {\i yetomim}, but not {\i le-haniach}, not to build up a long-term reserve. Similarly, they may sell fruits, wine, oil, and flour for immediate support but not for storage. The language {\i u-mochrin lahem} encompasses both selling on behalf of the {\i yetomim} and purchasing for them \u8212- the {\i apotropos} manages active financial support in either direction.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Fixed vs.\u160 ?Indefinite Pri\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i beraisa} proceeds to enumerate {\i mitzvah} objects that may be purchased: the {\i apotropos} may make for the orphans a {\i lulav}, {\i arava} \u8212- notable since {\i arava} on its own is a {\i minhag} rather than a biblical {\i mitzvah}, though the {\i Rif}\u8217's version of the text omits {\i arava} \u8212- a {\i sukkah}, and {\i tzitzit}. The {\i beraisa} then adds a general category: {\i kol davar sheyesh lo kitzvah} \u8212- anything with a fixed and defined cost; the {\i gemara} notes this includes {\i shofar} and, separately, {\i megillah}. Further, the {\i apotropos} may purchase for the orphans a {\i Sefer Torah}, {\i tefillin}, and {\i mezuzot}. Regarding a {\i Sefer Torah} in particular: there is a {\i mitzvah} for every Jewish man to write one, and beyond the ritual dimension, in antiquity a {\i Sefer Torah} was the means of Torah study itself \u8212- effectively, purchasing a {\i Sefer Torah} amounted to providing an education. The Maharam Shiff examines each item in this list individually, treating each as a {\i chiddush} in its own right.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Court & Property Limits\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Corresponding to these permissions, the {\i beraisa} specifies what {\i apotropsin} may not do: {\i ein poskin aleihen tzedakah} \u8212- they may not donate {\i tzedakah} from the orphans\u8217' inheritance; {\i ein podin aleihen et hashvuyim} \u8212- they may not use orphan funds to redeem captives; and {\i lo kol davar she\u8217'ein lo kitzvah} \u8212- nothing in the category of indefinite, open-ended expense. The paradigm case is {\i tanchumei aveilim} \u8212- bringing food to a house of mourning \u8212- since communal and charitable claims of this nature could arise indefinitely and drain the estate without limit. The governing principle is that any expenditure must be capped and predictable; anything unlimited falls outside the {\i apotropos}\u8217's authority.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This typology raises an interesting question: does it apply uniformly to all types of {\i apotropos}, whether father-appointed, self-appointed, or {\i beit din}-appointed? The {\i mishnah} will address that shortly.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i beraisa} further states: {\i ein apotropsin resha\u8217'im ladun lachov velizkos benichsei yetomim} \u8212- {\i apotropsin} may not litigate, whether the outcome would be adverse or favorable, with regard to orphan property. The {\i gemara} asks: if it is an entirely beneficial case, why may they not litigate? The answer: {\i ela lachov al menas lizkos} \u8212- even a case with long-term benefit may involve short-term detriment, and that suffices to prohibit it. But something that is unambiguously and entirely beneficial \u8212- that they may pursue. The practical difficulty, of course, is that no one can predict with certainty how litigation will resolve before the case is decided, which is why the {\i rishonim} struggle to define precisely what situations are contemplated here. The broader principle is clear: {\i katanim} cannot be brought to court, and active litigation of claims against their property must generally await their majority.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Field Sale Restrictions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i beraisa} further prohibits: {\i ein apotropsin resha\u8217'im limkor berachok velikhol bekarov} \u8212- they may not sell distant property and acquire nearby property in its stead, nor sell inferior property to acquire superior property. The rationale given by the {\i gemara}: {\i dilma mishta\u173 ?dfin} \u8212- the newly acquired property might be struck by blight, flooding, or some other calamity. One thought it was good property; it may turn out to be ruinous. This reflects a broader awareness in {\i Chazal} \u8212- visible across several of the recent {\i dapim} \u8212- of the tension between objective assessments of property quality and the reality that property values fluctuate wildly over time.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Slaves for Fields\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i beraisa} adds: {\i ein apotropsin resha\u8217'im limkor sadot velikaach avadim} \u8212- they may not sell fields to acquire slaves. And Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel goes further: {\i af lo limkor avadim velikaach sadot} \u8212- even selling slaves to acquire fields is prohibited. His rationale: {\i dilma lo mashpyan} \u8212- the fields may not produce abundantly. The {\i apotropos} may have thought that converting liquid assets into permanent productive land was a sound investment; Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel warns that the field may yield little, rendering the transaction a net loss \u8212- and the {\i apotropos} is only authorized to act for the {\i yetomim}\u8217's clear benefit.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Freeing Slaves Prohibiti\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i beraisa} states: {\i ein apotropin resha\u8217'im lehotzi avadim lacherut} \u8212- {\i apotropsin} may not free slaves; {\i aval mocherin otan la\u8217'acherim} \u8212- but they may sell them to others, {\i ve\u8217'acherim motzi\u8217'in otan lacherut} \u8212- and those others may then free them. Note that the {\i tanna kamma} permits selling slaves to acquire real estate, while Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel disputes this.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rashi and Tosafot Rid dispute the underlying reason for the prohibition on freeing slaves. Rashi holds that the {\i apotropsin} simply lack the requisite legal power \u8212- {\i ein gufan kanui lahen leshachreran} \u8212- freeing slaves is not within their authority. Tosafot Rid presses the point: if they can sell slaves, why can they not free them, since the two transactions are functionally nearly equivalent? Tosafot Rid\u8217's answer is grounded in the principle of {\i hefker beit din hefker}: the {\i apotropos}\u8217's powers derive from a rabbinic framework in which {\i beit din} effectively takes the orphans\u8217' property and re-vests authority in the {\i apotropos} to act on their behalf \u8212- but only for the specific transactions within that authorization. Freeing a slave, though similar to selling, falls outside the scope of what the rabbinic framework empowers the {\i apotropos} to do. The alternative conceptual framework \u8212- that {\i apotropsus} operates through {\i zekhiyyah} or {\i shelikhut}, perhaps as a superpowered form of {\i zekhiyyah} capable of encompassing even temporary loss for long-term gain \u8212- would have different implications.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi\u8217's Self-Redemption\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi interjects with his own view: {\i omer ani af hu noten demei atzmo veyotze mipnei shehu kemochro lo} \u8212- even the slave himself may pay for his own freedom, since a slave buying his own freedom is legally equivalent to someone else selling the slave to him. This is an interesting extension of the earlier discussions regarding {\i avadim}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Accounting Requirements\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i beraisa} states: {\i vetzarich lechashev imahem ba\u8217'achronah} \u8212- the {\i apotropos} must render a full accounting to the orphans at the conclusion of his tenure, documenting how much was spent on their upkeep, food, and other expenditures, to ensure complete transparency and honesty.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel says: {\i eino tzarich} \u8212- no accounting is required. His reasoning, which the {\i gemara} will develop: if you impose the burden of detailed accounting, no one will be willing to serve as {\i apotropos}. This tension \u8212- between protecting the {\i yetomim} through oversight and ensuring that capable people are willing to take on the responsibility \u8212- runs throughout this entire discussion.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ineligible Apotropsin\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i beraisa} concludes: {\i ein osin apotropsin nashim va\u8217'avadim uketannim} \u8212- women, slaves, and minors are ineligible to serve as {\i apotropsin}. It is important to note that these three categories are disqualified for very different reasons. Rashi explains regarding women: {\i she\u8217'ein darchan latzeis ulavo ulitrach} \u8212- it was not the custom of women in that era to go out into the marketplace, engage in business dealings, and manage court cases. Rashi\u8217's formulation implicitly acknowledges that this rationale is circumstantial rather than inherent: were the relevant social conditions different, the application of this {\i halacha} might well look different.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 However, there is a significant qualification: {\i ve\u8217'im minahu avi yatmei harreshut beyado} \u8212- if the father himself designated such a person as {\i apotropos}, that appointment stands. The father\u8217's authority over his own estate and his knowledge of those he trusts give him power to override what {\i beit din} would otherwise require. It is his estate, and he may exercise his own judgment as to whom he entrusts it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Apotropsin Case Studies\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Meir\u8217's Dream\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now turns to a series of narrative case studies illustrating {\i apotropsin} in practice. {\i Hahu apotropos dehava bishivvusei deRabbi Meir} \u8212- there was an {\i apotropos} in the town of Rabbi Meir who was selling real estate and selling slaves. Rabbi Meir did not intervene. {\i Itlai be-chelma} \u8212- the deceased father appeared to Rabbi Meir in a dream, crying: {\i ani leharos ve\u8217'atah livnot} \u8212- you are letting him destroy everything and I am left having to rebuild? Yet even after this dream, Rabbi Meir still did not act. {\i Amar divrei chalomot lo maalin velo moridin} \u8212- dreams carry no halakhic weight; they neither elevate nor diminish the legal standing of a matter. The {\i gemara} records many conflicting attitudes toward dreams across {\i Shas} \u8212- in {\i Yevamos}, {\i Nedarim}, and {\i Berakhot} dreams are sometimes taken with great seriousness \u8212- but Rabbi Meir here represents a position of firm dismissal.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Satan Possession Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} interjects a famous story about Rabbi Meir. {\i Hanehu betrei de\u8217'igrei behu satana} \u8212- there were two men who were seized by {\i satana}, such that every {\i erev Shabbat} they would erupt in conflict. Rabbi Meir came and stayed with them for three consecutive {\i Shabbat} evenings until he brought peace between them. Upon his departure, he heard Satan cry out in frustration: {\i vai de\u8217'afkei Rabbi Meir lehahu gavra mibeisei} \u8212- woe, for Rabbi Meir has driven that man out of his house. The {\i satana} brings {\i machlokes}; it cannot coexist in a space where {\i shalom} prevails. The story reveals Rabbi Meir as a person who went out of his way to engage with people and intervene in their conflicts \u8212- a picture consistent with other accounts of him elsewhere in {\i Shas}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Oxen Sale Uncontested\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A parallel case: {\i hahu apotropos dehava bishivvusei deRabbi Yehoshua ben Levi dehava kamazvin ar\u8217'a u-zavin tori velo amar lehu lei velo midi} \u8212- an {\i apotropos} in the domain of Rabbi Yehoshua ben Levi was selling land and oxen, and Rabbi Yehoshua ben Levi said nothing to him. The {\i gemara} explains that Rabbi Yehoshua ben Levi followed the opinion of Rabbi Yosi. As the {\i beraisa} records, Rabbi Yosi said that in his life he never called his wife \u8220"my wife\u8221" but rather \u8220"my house,\u8221" for she oversaw the entire household, and he never called his ox \u8220"my ox\u8221" but rather \u8220"my field,\u8221" for the ox was indispensable to working all his fields. In other words, Rabbi Yosi regarded the ox as equivalent in importance to the field itself \u8212- and consequently Rabbi Yehoshua ben Levi did not object to the {\i apotropos} selling the orphans\u8217' oxen.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Widow\u8217's Ox Sale\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Another case: {\i hanehu yasmei dehavu samchei gabei hahu sabta} \u8212- orphans who were under the care of an older woman. She had an ox belonging to them and sold it. The relatives came before Rav Nachman and protested. She invoked the case of {\i yetomim shesamkhu etzel ba\u8217'al habayit} from our {\i mishnah} \u8212- orphans who had taken up residence with a householder \u8212- arguing that she was acting within her authorized capacity to sell the ox for their support.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then notes: {\i veha ikar bireshusa delokeach} \u8212- but the ox had appreciated in value once it was in the buyer\u8217's possession. Moreover, {\i ve\u8217'ada lo nekiti damei} \u8212- the money had not yet been paid over. What could or should be done?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Property as Hekdesh\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Nachman ruled on the basis of a principle transmitted by Rav Chanilai bar Idi in the name of Shmuel: {\i nichsei yetomim harei hen kehekdesh velo makni ela bechespa} \u8212- the property of orphans is like {\i hekdesh}, consecrated property, in that it can be acquired only through the transfer of money. This generates an important practical rule: just as a transaction involving {\i hekdesh} is not finalized until money changes hands, so too a transaction involving orphans\u8217' property.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This contrasts with the general rule for {\i metaltelin}, where there is a {\i machlokes} between Rav Yochanan and Reish Lakish \u8212- {\i ma\u8217'os konos} versus {\i eino nikneh ela bemeshicha} \u8212- whether acquisition of movable property takes effect through payment of money or through the act of {\i meshicha}, physically taking possession. For orphans\u8217' property, the rule is fixed: only cash completes the transaction, akin to {\i hekdesh}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This principle operates in both directions, always to the {\i yetomim}\u8217's benefit: if the price rises after {\i meshicha} but before payment, the {\i yetomim} collect at the higher price. The system is, in effect, rigged in their favor \u8212- whatever arrangement yields them the better outcome is the operative one.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Wine Price Fluctuation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} works through several scenarios. {\i Chamra de-rabna Ukva yatmei mashkhuhu be-arba\u8217'ah arba\u8217'ah} \u8212- wine belonging to the estate of the orphans of Rabna Ukva was drawn ({\i meshicha} was performed) when it was worth four per barrel. The price then rose to six per barrel. Rav Nachman invoked the ruling of Rav Chanilai bar Idi: since {\i nichsei yetomim} are like {\i hekdesh} and are acquired only through money, and money had not yet been paid, the transaction is resolved at the higher price \u8212- a very substantial windfall for the {\i yetomim}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Conversely: if {\i meshicha} was performed on behalf of the {\i yetomim} when they were purchasing produce and the price then dropped \u8212- {\i lo yehei koach hedyot chamur mehekdesh} \u8212- the ordinary mechanism of sale must not work worse for them than {\i hekdesh} does. We give them the benefit of either form of acquisition, whichever is more advantageous.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Merchant Incentives\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This raises a practical concern: if sellers know that the price will always resolve against them when dealing with {\i yetomim}, people will simply refuse to do business with them. Moreover, applying the {\i hekdesh} principle in both directions \u8212- such that {\i yetomim} always pay the lower price when buying and receive the higher price when selling \u8212- creates a system that merchants will circumvent by waiting until payment and delivery coincide exactly. Rav Shisha, son of Rav Idi, identifies the countervailing risk on each side: if {\i yetomim} pay cash first before taking possession, the seller can always invoke {\i nisrefu chitechem ba\u8217'aliya} \u8212- your wheat burned in the attic \u8212- claiming the goods were destroyed before delivery, leaving the {\i yetomim} with no recourse. The {\i gemara} works through these tensions carefully, ultimately leaving the general principle of protecting the {\i yetomim} intact while acknowledging the practical complications.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Ashi adds: he and Rav Kahana signed a document authorizing the sale of a field belonging to orphans to pay a tax, without performing {\i achrazata} \u8212- the public announcement calling for the best possible buyer that is normally required before selling orphan property. The {\i Nehardai} ruled that for three categories of pressing need \u8212- paying taxes, providing food, or funding a burial \u8212- {\i achrazata} is not required. Tosafot notes that the case of burial seems so self-evidently urgent as to be unworthy of explicit mention; the {\i gemara} must therefore be referring not to purchasing a burial plot itself but to repaying a debt incurred for a burial.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Amram Tzeva Accusations\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Amram Tzeva} was serving as {\i apotropos} for orphans. Relatives came before Rav Nachman claiming he was dressing and clothing himself from the orphans\u8217' property. Rav Nachman\u8217's response was measured: perhaps he is eating and drinking from his own means \u8212- and if someone found an unexpectedly valuable lost object ({\i metzia}), one gives him the benefit of the doubt.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Removing Negligent Guard\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then asks: but if he is in fact depleting their estate, that is a serious problem. Rav Nachman replied: bring me witnesses that he is causing loss, and I will remove him. The {\i apotropos} is entitled to a presumption of good faith \u8212- and that presumption serves a practical function. If an {\i apotropos} can be removed on bare accusation, no one will be willing to accept the responsibility.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Huna stated in Rav\u8217's name: {\i apotropos demafsid mesalkinan lei} \u8212- an {\i apotropos} who causes loss to the orphans is removed. This is the subject of a direct {\i machlokes} among {\i amora\u8217'im}: Rav Huna citing Rav rules that he is removed; the school of Rabbi Shila says he is not removed. The {\i halacha} follows Rav Huna: he is removed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Shvuah for Apotropsin\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tanna Kamma Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} rules: {\i apotropos sheminahu avi yetomim yishava} \u8212- an {\i apotropos} appointed by the father must take a {\i shvuah}; {\i minahu beit din lo yishava} \u8212- one appointed by {\i beit din} does not.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Incentive Rationale\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} explains the {\i tanna kamma}\u8217's reasoning. The father-appointed {\i apotropos} is asked to take a {\i shvuah} precisely because {\i i lav de\u8217'is lei hana\u8217'ah minei lo hava lei apotropos} \u8212- if he were not receiving some benefit from the arrangement, he never would have agreed to take it on. The father must have offered him compensation or some special inducement. Accordingly, {\i mishum shvuah lo ati le\u8217'amnuyei} \u8212- the prospect of taking an oath at the conclusion of his tenure will not deter him from having accepted the position in the first place; the benefit still outweighs the burden. Therefore the {\i shvuah} can be imposed without creating a disincentive.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Conversely, the {\i beit din}-appointed {\i apotropos} does not take a {\i shvuah}. His motivation is simply {\i milta be\u8217'alma hu de\u8217'avid le\u8217'bei dina} \u8212- he is doing a favor for the court. {\i Ve\u8217'i ramit lei shvuah ati le\u8217'amnuyei} \u8212- if we burden him with the obligation of an oath at the end, the negatives will outweigh whatever public benefit he derives, and no one will agree to serve. The {\i beit din} does not pay its {\i apotropos}, making the deterrent effect of the {\i shvuah} far more pronounced.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This discussion invites reflection on how courts today think about foster care arrangements \u8212- with relatives versus non-relatives \u8212- and the degree to which financial incentives and public recognition affect people\u8217's willingness to step into caregiving roles for orphans.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Abba Shaul Reversal\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Abba Shaul omer chiluf hadvarim} \u8212- Abba Shaul reverses the ruling entirely.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Public Reputation Logic\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abba Shaul\u8217's rationale for his reversal: {\i minuhu beit din yishava be\u8217'hahi hana\u8217'ah de-nafik lei kala de\u8217'inish meheman hu} \u8212- when {\i beit din} appoints you, your appointment becomes publicly known. You become recognized in the community as a person of trustworthiness whom the court has seen fit to select. {\i Deha samchu lei beit din} \u8212- the court has placed its confidence in you. The public reputation you thereby acquire is itself a form of {\i hana\u8217'ah}, and it is sufficient to keep you motivated. Therefore {\i mishum shvuah lo ati le\u8217'amnuyei minehon} \u8212- the prospect of taking a {\i shvuah} will not deter you from having accepted the appointment.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Regarding the father-appointed {\i apotropos}, Abba Shaul argues the opposite: {\i milta be\u8217'alma hu de\u8217'avid le-hahu gavra} \u8212- he is doing a favor for a man who is now dead. A private appointment by a deceased father does not generate the same public recognition as a {\i beit din} appointment. The reputational reward is minimal, and therefore {\i ve\u8217'i ramit lei shvuah ati le\u8217'amnuyei} \u8212- the burden of a {\i shvuah} will indeed deter him from having agreed to serve.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shmuel\u8217's Ruling\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Chanan bar Ami reports in the name of Shmuel: {\i hilchata ke-Abba Shaul} \u8212- the {\i halacha} follows Abba Shaul. The {\i Rif} indeed rules accordingly.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Elazar\u8217's View\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 However, Tosafot raises a difficulty: a subsequent {\i beraisa} records that Rabbi Elazar ben Yaakov says {\i zeh vezeh yishava} \u8212- both types of {\i apotropos}, whether appointed by the father or by {\i beit din}, must take a {\i shvuah}. The {\i beraisa} itself concludes: {\i halacha kidvarav} \u8212- the {\i halacha} is in accordance with his view. This appears to contradict the ruling of Shmuel following Abba Shaul, and Tosafot grapples with how to resolve the tension.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Paid Guardian Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Tachlifa bar Ma\u8217'arava taught before Rabbi Abbahu: the reason the father-appointed {\i apotropos} takes a {\i shvuah} is {\i mipnei shehu nosei sachar} \u8212- because he is a {\i shomer sachar}, a paid guardian. Rabbi Abbahu challenges the precise formulation: he is not literally a {\i shomer sachar}; rather, he is {\i kenosei sachar} \u8212- like a paid guardian. He derives a certain {\i hana\u8217'ah} from the arrangement that places him in the functional position of someone who benefits from the {\i shmirah}, even if not technically a hired watchman.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 There is a great deal more to be said about this {\i daf}. The {\i Ran} and the {\i Ra\u8217'avad} and {\i Rambam} on the {\i dapei ha-Rif} engage in a rich {\i machlokes} about how the institution of {\i apotropus} interacts with the various {\i halakhot} surveyed here. It is, in all, a remarkably dense and jam-packed {\i daf}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Gittin 53\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Hezek She\u8217'eino Nikar\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Three Cases Defined\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} on 52b introduces a new category of {\i tikkun olam} centered on the concept of {\i hezek she\u8217'eino nikar} \u8212- damage that is not visible. The {\i mishna} states: {\i hametameh vehamedameh vehamensach, beshogeg patur, bemeizid chayav} \u8212- one who contaminates, mixes, or pours a libation: if accidental, exempt; if intentional, liable. Before addressing the conceptual framework underlying these rulings, the {\i gemara} first examines the three specific cases.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Metameh} refers to causing ritual impurity \u8212- for instance, touching {\i terumah} grain with a {\i sheretz} (a dead rodent), rendering it {\i tamei}. The grain, which could previously be eaten by {\i kohanim}, is now fit only to be burned. One may derive benefit from {\i tamei terumah} only as it burns, making it substantially less valuable. Yet nothing about the grain looks any different. {\i Medameh} refers to mixing separated {\i terumah} grain with {\i chullin} grain. Before the mixing, the {\i chullin} might be worth ten dollars a pound, available to anyone, while the {\i terumah} is worth five dollars a pound, restricted to {\i kohanim}. Once mixed, the entire pile is effectively restricted to {\i kohanim} and worth only five dollars a pound \u8212- yet to the eye, it is simply a large pile of grain. {\i Menasech} refers to pouring someone\u8217's wine as a libation to {\i avodah zarah}, rendering it {\i assur behana\u8217'ah} \u8212- prohibited even for any benefit \u8212- though again, nothing visible distinguishes it from ordinary wine. These three cases represent the distinctive phenomenon of a halakhic world where damage can be real, severe, and yet entirely invisible.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Default Rule Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The central question raised by the {\i mishna} is: which direction does the {\i tikkun olam} run? For ordinary damages, one is liable whether the act was {\i beshogeg} or {\i bemeizid}. One might therefore assume that the same is true here, and that the innovation of the {\i mishna} is the exemption for {\i shogeg} \u8212- that {\i chazal} suspended the ordinary obligation as a social measure. Alternatively, one might hold that the baseline rule is exemption \u8212- {\i hezek she\u8217'eino nikar} is simply not considered actionable damage \u8212- and the innovation is that {\i chazal} imposed liability for intentional acts to prevent people from willfully harming others with impunity. As the {\i gemara} will make clear, this question is not merely academic: it determines the very nature of the obligation in the {\i meizid} case, whether it is a form of compensation or a fine.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Menasech Interpretation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav vs.\u160 ?Shmuel\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Before reaching the fundamental conceptual debate, the {\i gemara} first clarifies a dispute between Rav and Shmuel about which act the {\i mishna} designates by the term {\i menasech}. Rav holds that the {\i mishna} refers to {\i menasech mamash} \u8212- literally pouring someone\u8217's wine as a libation to {\i avodah zarah}, thereby rendering it {\i assur behana\u8217'ah}. Shmuel, by contrast, holds that the {\i mishna} refers to mixing {\i yayin shel avodah zarah} into kosher wine \u8212- {\i me\u8217'arev}, an act analogous in structure to {\i medameh}, in that the wine\u8217's status is changed by admixture rather than by direct cultic act.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kam Leih B\u8217'Derabbah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} asks why Shmuel does not simply accept Rav\u8217's reading of {\i menasech} as literal pouring. The answer is {\i kam leih biderabbah mineih}: one who performs {\i nisuch}, pouring wine as a libation to {\i avodah zarah}, incurs capital liability, and this greater punishment displaces any monetary obligation arising from the same act. The principle of {\i kam leih biderabbah mineih} holds that when a single act generates both a capital transgression and a financial obligation, the weightier punishment subsumes the lesser \u8212- the offender is \u8220"established\u8221" with the greater sanction.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This principle operates in two distinct modes. The first is the well-known rule of {\i ein adam lokeh umeshalem} \u8212- one is not both flogged and made to pay \u8212- where two actual punishments cannot be exacted for a single act. The second, and more far-reaching, mode is {\i kam leih biderabbah mineih} proper: even when no punishment is actually being administered \u8212- because there are no witnesses, no {\i hasra\u8217'ah} (forewarning), or no functioning court with capital jurisdiction \u8212- the sheer gravity of the religious transgression renders any concurrent monetary obligation legally inconsequential. The act of {\i avodah zarah} is so weighty that the accompanying financial damage becomes irrelevant in its shadow.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} therefore asks: according to Rav, how is there any monetary liability at all? The answer is drawn from the principle stated by Rabbi Yirmiyah: {\i mishas hagbahah hu dekana} \u8212- the moment of acquisition, and hence the moment of financial liability, is the lifting of the vessel. {\i Mischayav b\u8217'nafsho lo havi ad sha\u8217'as nisuch} \u8212- capital liability for the act of {\i avodah zarah} does not attach until the moment of actual pouring. The financial act and the religious transgression thus occur at different moments, preventing {\i kam leih} from blocking the monetary claim.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafot Resolution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This resolution raises a further difficulty. If the financial liability arises at the moment of lifting \u8212- which is an act of {\i geneivah} or {\i gzeilah} \u8212- then the case in the {\i mishna} is simply one of theft, not of {\i hezek she\u8217'eino nikar}. The {\i mishna}\u8217's entire concern is with invisible damage: an act that alters an object\u8217's halakhic status without any visible change. If the obligation tracks the lifting rather than the pouring, it is ordinary theft, not invisible damage.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} addresses this problem directly. He writes: {\i yesh lomar d\u8217'savar Shmuel dehagbahah tzorech nisuch hu} \u8212- one may say that Shmuel holds that the lifting is a necessary precondition for the {\i nisuch}, since pouring cannot occur without first lifting the vessel. The key insight of {\i Tosafot} is that the moment of liability is defined differently depending on which body of law is being applied. Through the lens of {\i hilchos avodah zarah}, the prohibited act begins at the moment of pouring. But through the lens of {\i hilchos nezek}, the damaging process begins earlier \u8212- from the moment the perpetrator took hold of the wine with the intent to harm it. He has already begun invading another\u8217's property and initiating the chain of damage. Accordingly, there are two overlapping but temporally distinct acts: the {\i nezek} process commences with the lifting, while the {\i avodah zarah} offense crystallizes at the pouring. Because they do not fully coincide, {\i kam leih biderabbah mineih} does not apply, and the financial liability stands as a genuine case of {\i hezek she\u8217'eino nikar} \u8212- not mere theft.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then inverts the question: why, according to Rav, does Shmuel not simply accept the plain reading of {\i menasech} as literal pouring? One answer is that the plain meaning of the word supports Rav\u8217's reading. A deeper answer is that if the {\i mishna} had only intended mixing wine \u8212- which is structurally identical to mixing {\i terumah} with {\i chullin} \u8212- it would be no new {\i chiddush}; we would have derived it directly from {\i medameh}. But the other side responds: {\i knasa hu} \u8212- all of these obligations are {\i kenasos}, rabbinic fines, and one may not draw inferences from one fine to another. The fact that {\i chazal} chose to impose a {\i kenas} in one case does not logically compel the same in a different case.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This response introduces a crucial conceptual point. The assertion that the obligation is a {\i kenas} implicitly affirms that the baseline rule is exemption \u8212- {\i me\u8217'ikar hadin}, one is {\i patur} in all these cases. The question of whether the {\i meizid} obligation is properly characterized as a {\i kenas} (a fine imposed for social control or deterrence) or as a form of rabbinic {\i tashlumim} (compensation instituted to close a gap in the law) carries significant implications. If it is compensation, then wherever the same injustice exists, the same remedy should logically follow. If it is a {\i kenas}, however, each case must be evaluated on its own terms: how prevalent is the problem, how severe is the harm, how pressing is the social need for deterrence? These factors vary, and a {\i kenas} instituted in one context cannot simply be extended to another by analogy.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Why Three Cases?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Baraita Progression\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} presses further: if one accepts that inferences can be drawn from one {\i kenas} to another, why does the {\i mishna} need to specify all three cases? A {\i baraita} attributed to the father of Rabbi Avin records that originally the ruling was stated only for {\i metamei} and {\i menasech}, and only later was it extended to include {\i medameh} as well. This progression is itself revealing: the fact that {\i medameh} was the last and apparently most difficult case to include reinforces the character of these obligations as targeted rabbinic interventions rather than applications of a general principle of compensation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Case Distinctions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} explains why each case is indispensable and cannot be inferred from the others. Had the {\i mishna} stated only {\i metameh} \u8212- specifically, contaminating {\i terumah} \u8212- one might have thought the liability arises only because the loss is nearly total: {\i terumah temei\u8217'ah} can no longer be eaten at all and is useful only as fuel. One would not have extended the ruling to cases of partial loss. Alternatively, had the case been contaminating {\i chullin}, the monetary loss is minimal \u8212- {\i tamei chullin} is barely worth less than {\i tahor chullin} for most people \u8212- but there is the additional factor that causing {\i tumah} to {\i chullin} in {\i Eretz Yisrael} is itself a ritual transgression. One could not have known whether the fine was imposed because of the partial loss or because of this supplementary violation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Had the {\i mishna} stated only {\i medameh}, one might have concluded that the obligation arises only because {\i medameh} is a common occurrence \u8212- people regularly separate {\i terumah} and maintain distinct piles, making accidental or deliberate mixing a practical and frequent hazard. One would not have extended the fine to {\i metameh}, which the {\i gemara} treats as a less common scenario.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 And had the {\i mishna} stated only the first two without {\i menasech}, one might have concluded that these cases are straightforward because they do not implicate {\i kam leih biderabbah mineih}. Upon encountering {\i menasech}, one might have thought the capital dimension of {\i avodah zarah} exempts entirely. The {\i mishna} therefore includes all three to teach, in accordance with Rabbi Yirmiyah\u8217's principle, that the liability precedes the capital transgression, and the fine applies even there.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Each case is thus distinguished by a combination of factors: the degree of financial loss (total, near-total, or partial), the frequency with which the situation arises, and the presence or absence of additional ritual transgressions or competing legal doctrines. That all three parameters must be addressed independently confirms that what is operating here is a targeted series of rabbinic penalties, not a uniform principle of compensation.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Damage Definition\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chizkiyah Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having worked through the cases, the {\i gemara} now turns to the fundamental conceptual dispute. {\i Chizkiyah} holds: {\i devar Torah echad shogeg ve\u8217'echad meizid chayav} \u8212- as a matter of Torah law, whether one acted accidentally or intentionally, one is liable for this type of damage. The reasoning is {\i hezek she\u8217'eino nikar shemo hezek} \u8212- invisible damage is nonetheless called damage. An object whose halakhic status has been destroyed, rendering it worthless or severely diminished in value, has been damaged in the full legal sense, even if nothing visible has changed. The innovation of the {\i mishna}, on this reading, is not the liability for {\i meizid} but the exemption for {\i shogeg} \u8212- that is a departure from the ordinary rule and requires explanation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Disclosure Incentive\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Why would {\i chazal} exempt an accidental damager? {\i Kedei sheyodi\u8217'o} \u8212- so that the person will disclose what happened. The concern is entirely practical: the damage caused by {\i hezek she\u8217'eino nikar} is not apparent to the victim. If the inadvertent damager knows that disclosure will result in financial liability, he faces a strong {\i yetzer hara} not to inform. He can simply stay silent, and the victim will never know \u8212- he will unknowingly consume {\i terumah temei\u8217'ah}, drink {\i yayin nesech}, or eat food that is halakhically forbidden. The financial harm is compounded by the ritual harm that follows from ignorance. To break this incentive structure, {\i chazal} removed the financial consequence for accidental acts, ensuring that the person has no monetary reason to conceal what occurred.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might ask: if disclosure is the concern, should we not exempt even the intentional damager, so that he too will come forward and inform the victim? The {\i gemara} rejects this: when someone acts {\i bemeizid}, his motivation is to cause harm in this world \u8212- to make the victim\u8217's property worthless, to humiliate him, to damage him materially. Such a person will typically make sure the victim knows what has been done, either by informing him directly or by acting openly. The policy rationale for exemption applies only to the {\i shogeg}, who has no such motivation to inform and who faces a genuine financial disincentive to disclose.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yochanan Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yochanan takes the contrary view: {\i devar Torah echad shogeg ve\u8217'echad meizid patur} \u8212- as a matter of Torah law, whether accidental or intentional, one is exempt. The reason is {\i hezek she\u8217'eino nikar lo shemei hezek} \u8212- invisible damage is not legally considered damage at all. On this view, there is no biblical obligation in these cases, and the {\i mishna}\u8217's innovation is not the {\i shogeg} exemption but the {\i meizid} liability. The reason {\i chazal} imposed liability for intentional acts is precisely what was anticipated earlier: {\i shelo yehei kol echad ve\u8217'echad holech umetamei taharotav shel chaveiro ve\u8217'omer patur ani} \u8212- lest every person go and contaminate his neighbor\u8217's {\i taharos} and declare himself exempt. Without this rabbinic measure, anyone with malicious intent could willfully destroy another\u8217's property through invisible means and face no legal consequence whatsoever.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This framing is significant. By anchoring the obligation in the need to deter wrongdoers rather than in the victim\u8217's entitlement to compensation, Rabbi Yochanan\u8217's position strongly colors the liability as a {\i kenas} \u8212- a punitive fine aimed at social control \u8212- rather than a form of {\i tashlumim}. The question of whether the obligation is fundamentally about deterrence or about closing a gap of injustice is not merely theoretical: if it is deterrence, each case must be evaluated on its own social conditions; if it is compensation, any case in which the victim suffers and the damager was at fault would logically call for the same remedy. The {\i gemara}\u8217's framing through Rabbi Yochanan\u8217's language \u8212- focusing on preventing the wrongdoer from acting with impunity \u8212- pushes toward the {\i kenas} characterization.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Knas vs.\u160 ?Tashlumim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This distinction deserves further attention. One might have framed the same rabbinic intervention very differently: it is unjust that a person can willfully destroy another\u8217's property and the victim receives nothing; therefore {\i chazal} created a category of rabbinic compensation to close that gap. This framing would yield a {\i tashlumim} \u8212- an extension of the law\u8217's existing compensatory logic into a domain where Torah law had not yet reached. Under that model, the comparison between cases is natural: wherever someone suffers a loss attributable to another\u8217's willful act, the same logic compels compensation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But the {\i gemara}\u8217's language, and particularly Rabbi Yochanan\u8217's formulation, focuses on preventing the wrongdoer\u8217's impunity rather than on making the victim whole. That focus reflects a {\i kenas} mentality: we are not filling a gap in the law of compensation; we are imposing a deterrent penalty for socially dangerous behavior. And if it is a {\i kenas}, we cannot assume that the same penalty applies across all analogous cases. Different situations carry different degrees of risk, different frequencies of occurrence, and different social pressures \u8212- factors that determine whether a targeted fine is warranted. As {\i Rashi} puts it tersely: wherever they imposed a fine, they imposed it; wherever they did not, they did not. The apparent arbitrariness dissolves once one understands that the fine is a pragmatic instrument calibrated to specific social needs, not a logical extension of a general principle.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Proofs & Parallels\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Pigul Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now brings proofs from related cases to resolve the dispute between {\i Chizkiyah} and Rabbi Yochanan. A {\i mishna} in {\i Zevachim} states: {\i kohanim shepiggelu b\u8217'mikdash meizidim chayavim} \u8212- priests who rendered a sacrifice {\i pigul} (invalid by harboring an improper intention during its offering, such as intending to eat it at the wrong time or place) are liable if they acted intentionally. The Talmud notes: {\i vetanya alah mipnei tikkun ha\u8217'olam} \u8212- and a {\i baraisa} teaches that this obligation exists {\i mipnei tikkun ha\u8217'olam}. This is another case of {\i hezek she\u8217'eino nikar}: the sacrifice looks no different after the {\i kohen}\u8217's improper thought, yet it is now {\i pasul} and the owner has suffered a real loss. The formulation \u8212- {\i meizidim chayavim mipnei tikkun ha\u8217'olam} \u8212- sounds like the innovation is the liability for intentional acts, implying that the baseline is exemption. This supports Rabbi Yochanan.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} notes that if one follows {\i Chizkiyah} \u8212- that the baseline is liability and the innovation is the {\i shogeg} exemption \u8212- then the {\i baraisa} should have stated that {\i shogegim} are exempt {\i mipnei tikkun ha\u8217'olam}. The {\i gemara} deflects this by reading the {\i baraisa} as implicitly teaching the {\i shogeg} exemption through the {\i meizid} liability: since it says the intentional are liable, the accidental are implicitly exempt, and it is the exemption that is the {\i tikkun ha\u8217'olam}. This is a forced reading, and the proof-force leans toward Rabbi Yochanan.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Parah Adumah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A stronger challenge comes from a {\i baraisa} concerning {\i parah adumah} and {\i mei chatat}: one who performs {\i melachah} with the red cow or with the purification waters is {\i patur midinei adam vechayav bidinei shamayim} \u8212- exempt from human courts but liable in the heavenly court. Performing {\i melachah} with the cow while it is still alive, or using the {\i mei chatat} (the waters mixed with the ashes of the {\i parah adumah}) as counterweights in a balance, invalidates them for their sacred purpose \u8212- again a case of {\i hezek she\u8217'eino nikar}. The {\i baraisa} does not distinguish between {\i shogeg} and {\i meizid}; it simply declares the person {\i patur midinei adam}. If {\i Chizkiyah} is correct that the baseline is liability, why is there no {\i meizid} obligation here? This seems to support Rabbi Yochanan: the default is exemption, and in this case {\i chazal} chose not to impose a fine.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rashi} notes that {\i chayav bidinei shamayim} here implies, at minimum, that a person who intentionally damaged another\u8217's property is answerable before God \u8212- and in another context, {\i Rashi} explains that this formulation may indicate a moral obligation to compensate the victim even though the human court cannot compel it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Cow Intention\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} offers a preliminary resolution. The case of the cow becoming invalid through {\i melachah} is distinguishable: the cow\u8217's activity (nursing from its mother, threshing grain on the floor) invalidates the {\i parah adumah} only because the owner {\i intended} for it to thresh. Without that intent, the cow\u8217's nursing would not be considered {\i avodah} (labor). The act of invalidation is thus mediated through the owner\u8217's thought, not through a direct physical act. This is not a case of {\i hezek she\u8217'eino nikar} in the usual sense \u8212- the problem is not merely that the damage is invisible, but that no direct physical action was performed at all; the invalidation occurred through mental intention alone. Accordingly, one cannot derive from this case that invisible damage carries no liability.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Water Displacement\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But the {\i gemara} presses further. What of the case of {\i mei chatat} used for water displacement \u8212- placing objects into the water to measure by displacement? There, the person performs a direct physical act and the water is invalidated. This is invisible damage caused by a real action. Why is the person still {\i patur midinei adam}? Does this not prove that {\i hezek she\u8217'eino nikar lo shemei hezek}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} offers a resolution: {\i lo kashya ha begufan ha bichenegdan} \u8212- it depends on the mode of use. Placing the {\i mei chatat} on one side of a balance with something on the other side ({\i bichenegdan}) is not considered {\i melachah} with the water itself, and therefore does not invalidate it. Placing an object {\i into} the water to measure displacement ({\i begufan}) does constitute a direct use of the water and would invalidate it. If so, the case of water displacement ({\i begufan}) should involve a direct act and yield liability \u8212- which would support {\i Chizkiyah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hesach Hada\u8217'at\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} ultimately resolves the tension by reframing both cases. It concludes that both the cow case and the water case can be explained as instances of {\i hesach hada\u8217'at} \u8212- a lapse in attentiveness \u8212- rather than as cases of direct physical action. With {\i mei chatat}, even the act of using the water as a counterbalance ({\i bichenegdan}) is not what produces invalidation through {\i melachah} per se; rather, the moment one turns one\u8217's attention to weighing grain, one is no longer fully focused on the {\i mei chatat}, and it is this {\i hesach hada\u8217'at} \u8212- the distraction from guarding its sanctity \u8212- that invalidates it. In other words, what makes the {\i mei chatat pasul} in that case is not the physical act but the absence of mindful attention. This is analogous to the cow case: the invalidation occurs through thought or the absence of thought, not through a straightforward physical act of damage.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara}\u8217's conclusion is therefore that neither case succeeds as a proof about the nature of {\i hezek she\u8217'eino nikar}. In both instances, what is actually happening is that the invalidation is caused by a dimension of mental intent or attentional lapse, not by a direct physical act whose only deficiency is invisibility. The question of whether {\i hezek she\u8217'eino nikar shemei hezek} \u8212- whether Rabbi Yochanan or {\i Chizkiyah} has correctly identified the baseline \u8212- remains unresolved by these cases.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Gittin 54\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Invisible Damage\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishna Setup\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The daf opens on 53b, picking up the sustained discussion around the concept of {\i hezek she\u8217'eino nikar} \u8212- damage that is not visible. The {\i mishna} establishes the basic framework: when a person causes damage that is not perceptible to the eye \u8212- damage that exists in a halachic sense, because the object is worth less due to its legal status, yet cannot be seen \u8212- liability turns on intentionality. A {\i meizid}, one who acts willfully, must pay; a {\i shogeg}, one who acts accidentally, is exempt.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Presumption\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara}\u8217's opening posture leans strongly toward treating {\i hezek she\u8217'eino nikar} as not constituting genuine damage at all. The reasoning is that {\i halacha} looks to an act of damage, not merely the causing of a loss; if the harm is invisible, it would not be classified as a true act of {\i mazik}, and one would be fundamentally exempt. The rabbinic intervention, then, is directed specifically at the {\i meizid}: in order to close the loophole that would allow a person to willfully damage another\u8217's property without consequence, the Sages decreed that intentional actors must pay. This is the {\i tikun olam} rationale \u8212- the rabbinic corrective is the imposition of liability in the {\i meizid} case. The competing position, that of Chizkiyah, runs in the opposite direction: fundamentally the actor is liable, and the Sages provided relief in the {\i shogeg} case so that the damaging party would have an incentive to inform the owner. The weight of the evidence accumulated thus far has been pointing toward the first view \u8212- {\i lo shemei hezek}, it does not constitute damage.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Pappa Challenge\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 At the two-dot marker near the top of 53b, Rav Pappa raises a challenge. The {\i gemara} cites three cases: one who steals a coin that subsequently becomes invalid, {\i teruma} that becomes {\i tamei}, and {\i chametz} over which Passover has passed and which is therefore worthless. In each of these cases, the thief may say to the victim {\i harei shelcha lefanecha} \u8212- here is your object before you, I am returning it \u8212- and this constitutes valid restitution despite the object now being essentially without value. The underlying principle is that the primary obligation of a thief is to return the stolen object itself, {\i v\u8217'heshiv et hagezeilah}. Since the object is being returned in the same physical state in which it was stolen, the obligation of return is discharged.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is worth pausing to note that the three cases are not perfectly parallel. The invalid coin retains its precious metal value. {\i Teruma} that is {\i tamei} can still be burned, so it retains some use. And {\i chametz} that has passed over Passover is forbidden only by rabbinic decree. So none of them is truly and completely worthless \u8212- but the {\i gemara} treats them as functionally equivalent for purposes of this discussion.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Harei Shelcha\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The connection to {\i hezek she\u8217'eino nikar} is as follows. The {\i gemara} argues: if you maintain that {\i hezek she\u8217'eino nikar shemei hezek} \u8212- that invisible damage does constitute genuine damage \u8212- then this thief has caused real damage to the object, and {\i harei shelcha lefanecha} should not work. He would be obligated to pay full compensation, since he cannot be said to be returning the object in its original state if he has genuinely damaged it. The {\i gemara} therefore concludes {\i tiyuvta} \u8212- this is a decisive challenge \u8212- implying that the law of {\i harei shelcha lefanecha} proves that we do not treat such a changed object as truly different, and therefore the {\i mazik} would not have to pay.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafot Analysis\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot presses on something genuinely puzzling here. Until this point, every proof brought in the debate about {\i hezek she\u8217'eino nikar} has come from the laws of {\i mazik}. This case, however, comes from the laws of the {\i gazlan} \u8212- the thief \u8212- and whether he can return a now-valueless object. These are distinct areas of {\i halacha}. Even if one were to say that a {\i mazik} who causes invisible damage must pay in the laws of damages, one might still argue that in the laws of theft \u8212- where the fundamental obligation is to return the object \u8212- the thief can say {\i harei shelcha lefanecha} as long as the physical object is unchanged, regardless of its changed halachic value. Why, then, does the {\i gemara} treat these two areas as mutually illuminating?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot answers that the comparison itself reveals something important: the question of {\i hezek she\u8217'eino nikar} is not merely a local question within the laws of {\i mazik}. It is a more fundamental and abstract question: when an object loses value without any physical change, do we consider the object itself to be different? Has it become, in some legally meaningful sense, a different object? If the answer is yes \u8212- if we say the object is substantively changed \u8212- then a {\i mazik} would have to pay and a {\i gazlan} could not say {\i harei shelcha lefanecha}. If the answer is no \u8212- if we say the object is not considered different \u8212- then the {\i mazik} is exempt and the {\i gazlan} may return it with that declaration. The fact that the {\i gemara} draws the proof from the {\i gazlan} case demonstrates that this is the underlying unified question, cutting across both bodies of law. The conclusion the {\i gemara} draws is that we do not consider the object to be changed, and thus the presumption is that a {\i mazik} is not liable.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Tannaim Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishna Positions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having established that the weight of evidence favors {\i lo shemei hezek}, the {\i gemara} now asks whether this might nonetheless be a {\i tannaitic} debate. The {\i gemara} introduces a {\i baraita}: {\i ha-metamei}, {\i ha-medamea}, and {\i ha-menasech} \u8212- one who causes {\i tumah} to {\i teruma}, one who mixes {\i teruma} with {\i chullin}, and one who pours libation wine \u8212- whether done accidentally ({\i shogeg}) or intentionally ({\i meizid}), is liable according to Rabbi Meir. Rabbi Yehuda says: by {\i shogeg} one is exempt, by {\i meizid} one is liable. That is the position reflected in the {\i mishna} itself.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Meir Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The natural reading would be that Rabbi Meir and Rabbi Yehuda are debating the fundamental question of {\i hezek she\u8217'eino nikar} \u8212- Rabbi Meir holding {\i shemei hezek} (genuine damage, hence you pay even {\i beshogeg}) and Rabbi Yehuda holding {\i lo shemei hezek} (not genuine damage, hence you are fundamentally exempt but are fined in the {\i meizid} case).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Nachman Resolve\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Nachman bar Yitzchak, however, rejects this framing. He maintains that both opinions agree: {\i hezek she\u8217'eino nikar lo shemei hezek} \u8212- it does not constitute genuine damage. All the evidence has been pointing that way, and Rav Nachman bar Yitzchak insists the conclusion is universal. The debate between Rabbi Meir and Rabbi Yehuda is therefore not about the fundamental nature of the damage. Rather, it concerns the scope of the rabbinic fine: given that we have already decided to impose a penalty on the {\i meizid}, do we extend that fine to the {\i shogeg} as well?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Yehuda Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Meir says yes: {\i knasu shogeg atu meizid} \u8212- the Sages fined the {\i shogeg} on account of the {\i meizid}. The reasons for extending the fine are practical: it is often impossible to distinguish between a {\i meizid} and a {\i shogeg}, and a willful actor could always claim accident; furthermore, even the inadvertent actor has caused real loss to another and may deserve some deterrent consequence. Rabbi Yehuda, by contrast, says {\i lo knasu shogeg atu meizid} \u8212- the Sages did not extend the fine that broadly; they limited it to the {\i meizid} alone. Both positions treat the underlying liability as a {\i knas} \u8212- a rabbinic penalty \u8212- not a Torah-based obligation. The debate is only about how narrow or how broad that penalty should be.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Reversals & Cases\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shabbat Cooking\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 With this framework established, the {\i gemara} proceeds to demonstrate that when one examines other cases attributed to these same {\i tannaim}, their positions appear to reverse \u8212- sometimes dramatically. The {\i gemara} introduces a {\i baraita} about one who cooks on Shabbat. Rabbi Meir says: {\i beshogeg yokhal, bemeizid lo yokhal} \u8212- if done accidentally, one may eat the food; if done willfully, one may not. So here, Rabbi Meir does {\i not} impose a fine on the {\i shogeg}, only on the {\i meizid}. That appears to be the opposite of his position in the {\i hezek she\u8217'eino nikar} cases.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yehuda says: {\i beshogeg yokhal lemotza\u8217'ei Shabbat} \u8212- if done accidentally, one may eat after Shabbat ends, but not on Shabbat itself; {\i bemeizid lo yokhal le\u8217'olam} \u8212- if done willfully, one may never eat the food. So Rabbi Yehuda here does impose a consequence on the {\i shogeg} \u8212- he may not benefit from it during Shabbat itself \u8212- though not as severe a consequence as in the {\i meizid} case. A third opinion, that of Rabbi Yochanan HaSandlar, holds that {\i bemeizid} neither the cook nor anyone else may ever eat the food, because from a Torah perspective the product of a willful {\i chilul Shabbat} is intrinsically forbidden, not merely subject to rabbinic fine. That is a separate {\i de\u8217'oraita} framework and is noted only in passing.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The chart that emerges is striking: in the {\i hezek she\u8217'eino nikar} case, Rabbi Meir fines {\i shogeg} and Rabbi Yehuda does not. In the Shabbat cooking case, Rabbi Meir does {\i not} fine {\i shogeg} and Rabbi Yehuda does. They have switched sides.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Meir\u8217's Principle\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} resolves Rabbi Meir\u8217's apparent self-contradiction: {\i ki kanis bidrabbanan, bede\u8217'oraita lo kanis} \u8212- Rabbi Meir imposes the fine on a {\i shogeg} only in the context of a rabbinic violation, not a biblical one. The logic runs as follows: when the underlying prohibition is {\i de\u8217'oraita}, people are already vigilant; the weight of the Torah obligation creates its own deterrent, and there is no particular laxity to correct. By contrast, where the prohibition is only rabbinic, people are more likely to be casual, and there is a genuine slippery slope that requires the rabbis to step in and fine even the {\i shogeg} to tighten enforcement.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 In the {\i hezek she\u8217'eino nikar} cases ({\i metamei}, {\i medamea}, {\i menasech}), the underlying acts \u8212- causing others financial loss \u8212- are in the domain of a rabbinic corrective, and so Rabbi Meir fines the {\i shogeg}. In the Shabbat cooking case, one has violated a {\i de\u8217'oraita} prohibition, and Rabbi Meir does not extend the fine.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Avodah Zara Exception\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} immediately presses a difficulty: one of the cases in the {\i mishna} is {\i menasech} \u8212- pouring libations to {\i avodah zarah} \u8212- which is itself a {\i de\u8217'oraita} violation. Why does Rabbi Meir fine there? The {\i gemara} answers: {\i mishum chumra de\u8217'avodah zarah kanis lei} \u8212- because of the unique severity of {\i avodah zarah}, Rabbi Meir treats it as a special case and does fine. The very extremity of {\i avodah zarah} as a transgression overrides the general rule, and the fine is imposed regardless. This is structurally ironic \u8212- the gravity of the offense that would normally counsel against fining is here the very reason for the fine \u8212- but the {\i gemara} accepts this as the explanation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yehuda\u8217's Reverse\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yehuda\u8217's self-contradiction is resolved by applying the mirror-image principle: {\i ki lo kanis bidrabbanan, de\u8217'oraita kanis} \u8212- Rabbi Yehuda does {\i not} fine the {\i shogeg} for a rabbinic violation, but does fine him for a biblical one. Rabbi Yehuda\u8217's reasoning operates in the opposite direction from Rabbi Meir\u8217's: where the violation is biblical and weighty, Rabbi Yehuda feels there is both justification and need for imposing the fine even on the inadvertent actor. Where the violation is merely rabbinic, he limits the fine to the {\i meizid}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Again the {\i gemara} presses: the {\i mishna} case of {\i menasech} is also biblical \u8212- why does Rabbi Yehuda not fine there? The answer is the same logic reversed: {\i mishum chumra de\u8217'avodat kokhavim mivdal badle minei} \u8212- the severity of idolatry is so extreme that people are simply not going to commit it; there is no need for a rabbinic deterrent, because {\i avodah zarah} is self-deterring by virtue of its theological weight.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Planting Cases\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} is not yet satisfied. It introduces a {\i baraita} about planting on Shabbat and planting during {\i shemita} to demonstrate that Rabbi Meir and Rabbi Yehuda appear to reverse themselves even within the category of {\i de\u8217'oraita} violations. The {\i baraita} states: one who plants on Shabbat \u8212- {\i beshogeg yekayem}, if done accidentally the planting may stand; {\i bemeizid ye\u8217'aker}, if done willfully it must be uprooted \u8212- according to Rabbi Meir. On {\i shemita}, however, whether {\i shogeg} or {\i meizid}, the planting must be uprooted \u8212- Rabbi Meir. And Rabbi Yehuda takes the opposite positions: on {\i shemita}, {\i beshogeg yekayem}; on Shabbat, whether {\i shogeg} or {\i meizid}, it must be uprooted.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is more difficult to resolve through the {\i de\u8217'oraita}/{\i derabbanan} distinction because both Shabbat and {\i shemita} are {\i de\u8217'oraita}. The {\i gemara} points out that the problem actually emerges from within the {\i baraita} itself: why do both Rabbi Meir and Rabbi Yehuda treat Shabbat and {\i shemita} differently from each other when both are biblical?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shemita Laxity\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} turns to a statement in the {\i baraita} where Rabbi Meir himself explains his reasoning. He says: why do I rule {\i beshogeg yekayem} for Shabbat but impose uprooting even for {\i shogeg} on {\i shemita}? One reason: {\i mipnei she-yisrael monin lishmita ve-ein monin leShabbasos} \u8212- because the Jewish people count years according to the {\i shemita} cycle but do not count by Shabbat weeks in the same way. The practical consequence is that if someone planted during {\i shemita} and one does the arithmetic from when the fruit becomes edible, it will be evident to everyone that the planting occurred during the {\i shemita} year. That public visibility is a violation of {\i shemita}\u8217's sanctity, and therefore we force uprooting to prevent the impression that such planting was condoned. By contrast, there is no corresponding calculation that reveals whether a planting occurred on a weekday or on Shabbat.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 An additional explanation is given: {\i necheshdu Yisrael al hashevi\u8217'is ve-lo necheshdu al haShabbatos} \u8212- Israel was suspected of laxity regarding {\i shemita} observance, but not regarding Shabbat. This is the more direct parallel to Rabbi Meir\u8217's general principle. Even though {\i shemita} is {\i de\u8217'oraita}, the actual observance level in the community was loose \u8212- there was practical laxity around {\i shemita} \u8212- and so it resembles a {\i derabbanan} situation in terms of behavioral patterns. Since people treat it with insufficient seriousness, Rabbi Meir applies the same logic that would normally attach to a {\i derabbanan} violation: fine the {\i shogeg} to create enforcement. The {\i gemara} offers a brief intermediate discussion about whether someone planting near the end of a year could be detected as having planted on Shabbat, before ultimately settling on the {\i necheshdu} principle as the operative reason.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yehuda\u8217's reverse position on {\i shemita} is explained by the local tradition in Rabbi Yehuda\u8217's locale: {\i be\u8217'atrei de-Rabbi Yehuda chamru lehu shevi\u8217'is} \u8212- in Rabbi Yehuda\u8217's region, {\i shemita} was observed with great stringency, and therefore there was no need for an additional rabbinic fine in the {\i shogeg} case. The {\i gemara} cites an anecdote about someone in that region who boasted to a neighbor that he did not eat {\i shemita} produce as others did, suggesting a culture of heightened {\i shemita} observance. Admittedly the connection is not entirely self-evident \u8212- the anecdote might equally demonstrate that violations were common enough to be noteworthy \u8212- but the {\i gemara} cites it as evidence of local stringency.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Teruma Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tashlumin Halakha\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now introduces the laws of someone who eats a {\i kohen}\u8217's {\i teruma}. The baseline {\i halacha} is that eating {\i teruma} intentionally ({\i meizid}) makes one liable to pay its monetary value. Eating it accidentally ({\i shogeg}), however, triggers a different mechanism: one must pay back in food, and that food, upon transfer to the {\i kohen}, itself becomes {\i teruma}. There is a debate about whether the quantity or the value must be matched, but the basic structure is that the repayment is made in food which takes on {\i teruma} status in the act of being given.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The present case involves someone who ate {\i teruma temeiah} \u8212- ritually impure {\i teruma}, which has substantially diminished value, since a {\i kohen} in a state of {\i tumah} can consume it but its general utility is limited. The question is what happens when this person repays with {\i chullin temeiim} \u8212- impure ordinary food \u8212- rather than {\i chullin tehorim}, which is what he should have given, since the latter would upon transfer become {\i teruma tehorah}, the more valuable form of {\i teruma}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sumchus Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Sumchus, in the name of Rabbi Meir, rules: {\i beshogeg, tashlumin tashlumin} \u8212- if done accidentally, the repayment counts and the obligation is discharged; {\i bemeizid, ein tashlumin tashlumin} \u8212- if done willfully, the repayment does not count. The {\i hakhamim} say: in both cases the payment counts as {\i teruma}, but in both cases the person must pay a second time with {\i chullin tehorim}. So Rabbi Meir fines only the {\i meizid}, whereas the {\i hakhamim} fine even the {\i shogeg}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Challenge to Fine\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Before applying this to the larger debate, the {\i gemara} raises a striking logical question: why should willfully repaying {\i chullin temeiim} not count at all? The {\i kohen} ate {\i teruma temeiah}, which he could consume only in a state of {\i tumah} and which was of diminished value. The person repaying gave back {\i chullin temeiim}, which the {\i kohen} could likewise consume while {\i tamei}. Yes, the food became {\i teruma temeiah} upon transfer, but what exactly is the harm? The {\i kohen} has received back something functionally equivalent to what was taken.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava Reinterprets\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava, and some transmit it anonymously, responds with a radical solution: {\i chasuri michasra vehachi katani} \u8212- the {\i baraita} is incomplete as stated and must be reread. The entire scenario must be reframed. The {\i baraita} is not speaking of a case where one ate {\i teruma temeiah} and repaid with {\i chullin temeiim}. Rather, the case must be that one ate {\i teruma tehorah} \u8212- pure {\i teruma}, of full value \u8212- and then repaid with {\i chullin temeiim}. Now it is obvious why repaying with impure food is inadequate: the {\i kohen} had pure {\i teruma} taken from him, and he is now receiving back something far less valuable (whether measured by value according to Rashi, or by utility and convenience according to Tosafot, who holds the repayment is matched by value but notes that people prefer less {\i teruma tehorah} over more {\i teruma temeiah}). The {\i baraita} thus becomes: {\i achal teruma tehorah, meshalem chullin tehorim} \u8212- you ate pure {\i teruma}, you must repay with pure {\i chullin}. If instead you repaid with {\i chullin temeiim} ({\i shilem chullin temeim}), Sumchus in the name of Rabbi Meir says: {\i beshogeg, tashlumin tashlumin}; {\i bemeizid, ein tashlumin tashlumin}. And the {\i hakhamim} fine in both cases.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Restitution Policy\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Applying this to the Rabbi Meir / Rabbi Yehuda structure: the inadequate repayment is a {\i derabbanan} violation, and Rabbi Meir would normally fine the {\i shogeg} in {\i derabbanan} cases. Why then does Rabbi Meir not fine the {\i shogeg} here? The {\i gemara} says: {\i hatam gavra lishlumei ka michavein, anushei uveli knisa?} \u8212- there, the person is trying to make restitution; are we going to impose a fine on him? The critical distinction Rabbi Meir draws is between an act that is fundamentally bad \u8212- causing damage, acting destructively \u8212- and an act that is fundamentally good or remedial. Rabbi Meir\u8217's policy of fining {\i shogegin} in {\i derabbanan} cases is directed at curbing negligent harmful conduct. When someone is in the process of trying to make things right, even if he does it imperfectly, applying that fine is counterproductive. We do not penalize people who are genuinely trying to remedy a situation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This principle is then applied to two additional cases the {\i gemara} had already addressed but which fit the same analysis. One who takes {\i maaser} on Shabbat ({\i hame\u8217'aser beShabbat}) \u8212- a rabbinic violation of Shabbat \u8212- is told {\i beshogeg yokhal, bemeizid lo yokhal}. Why is Rabbi Meir not fining the {\i shogeg} here, given that this is a {\i derabbanan} violation? {\i Hatam gavra letikuni kamikhaven} \u8212- the person was trying to do a {\i mitzvah}, separating {\i terumot u\u8217'maasrot}. Similarly, one who immerses vessels in a {\i mikvah} on Shabbat ({\i hamatbil kelim beShabbat}), again a rabbinic violation: {\i beshogeg yeshtamesh bahem, bemeizid lo yeshtamesh bahem}. Why no fine for {\i shogeg}? {\i Hatam gavra letahorei kamikhaven} \u8212- he was trying to purify his vessels.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Dam & Meserech\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 There is one additional case that was returned to after it had initially been overlooked: {\i dam she-nitma ve-zarko} \u8212- blood of a {\i korban} that became {\i tamei} and was thrown on the {\i mizbeach}. The {\i halacha} is that even if such blood is thrown {\i bemeizid}, the {\i korban} is still valid because the {\i tzitz} worn by the {\i kohen gadol} has the power to render such service acceptable. Rashi clarifies that throwing {\i tamei} blood is itself only a rabbinic violation, since the presence of the {\i tzitz} makes it {\i de\u8217'oraita} permissible. The {\i baraita} rules: {\i beshogeg hurtzah, bemeizid lo hurtzah} \u8212- if accidental, the service is accepted; if willful, it is not, meaning the {\i kohanim} may not eat the meat. Why does Rabbi Meir not fine the {\i shogeg} here, given the {\i derabbanan} character of the violation? {\i Hatam gavra lechapporei kamikhaven} \u8212- the {\i kohen} is engaged in the {\i avodah}, trying to achieve atonement for the owners. One does not impose fines on people in the midst of performing {\i mitzvot} and the Temple service.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The corresponding case for Rabbi Yehuda\u8217's apparent reversal also involves the {\i derabbanan} category. In the case of {\i nafelu nitpatzu} \u8212- precious nuts, some of which are {\i orlah}, that all get cracked and thereby lose their {\i chashivus} (halachic significance) and become {\i batel} \u8212- Rabbi Meir says whether {\i shogeg} or {\i meizid}, the mixture remains forbidden. This is consistent with Rabbi Meir. But Rabbi Yehuda joins Rabbi Meir \u8212- which is inconsistent with Rabbi Yehuda\u8217's usual position of not fining in {\i derabbanan} cases. Rabbis Yose and Shimon, by contrast, are lenient and only disallow the mixture when it was {\i meizid}. The {\i gemara} explains Rabbi Yehuda\u8217's strictness: {\i mishum de-ati le\u8217'arumei} \u8212- because in this scenario there is a powerful incentive to scheme. If a person\u8217's entire stock of valuable nuts is ruined because of a single {\i orlah} nut, the temptation to deliberately crush them all and then claim it was an accident is enormous. Rabbi Yehuda therefore closes off this avenue by not distinguishing between {\i shogeg} and {\i meizid} at all.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Finally, regarding Rabbi Yose\u8217's reversal in the case of a shoot of {\i orlah} mixed with other plantings ({\i netia shel orlah she-nit\u8217'arba benetiot acherot}): Rabbi Yose there rules that even intentional picking (which creates {\i bitul}) is acceptable \u8212- {\i afilu mitkavein lilkot, ya\u8217'alu be\u8217'echad umatayim}. This seems far more lenient than his position on the nuts. The {\i gemara} resolves this by citing the principle of Rabbah and Rabbi Yochanan: {\i chazakah ein adam oser karmo benetiyah achat} \u8212- it is simply inconceivable that a person would allow an {\i orlah} shoot to be planted among his other vines without having taken precautions. The scenario is so implausible, so unlikely ever to arise in practice, that the rabbis did not impose their stricter rules upon it. The nuts case, by contrast, involves a genuinely common scenario, and there fining is warranted.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Pigul & Testimony\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Pigul Definition\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now pivots from the question of liability for {\i hezek she\u8217'eino nikar} to the question of testimonial credibility regarding invisible acts. The setting is {\i pigul}: a {\i korban} is rendered {\i pigul} \u8212- halachically disqualified \u8212- when the {\i kohen} performing the service harbors the intention at the wrong moment to eat it or offer it beyond its permitted time. This is an invisible act; no one can see what the {\i kohen} is thinking. The {\i mishna} says that {\i kohanim} who intentionally cause {\i pigul} are liable to the owners. The underlying {\i hezek she\u8217'eino nikar} question has already been resolved \u8212- we fine the {\i meizid}. But now the {\i gemara} turns to a different question: when will we believe someone who claims such an invisible act occurred?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Abaye: Immediate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i baraita} is introduced: if one was working together with another person on {\i taharot} (maintaining ritual purity) and declared {\i taharot she\u8217'asiti imcha nitme\u8217'u} \u8212- the pure items we were working on together have become {\i tamei} \u8212- he is believed ({\i ne\u8217'eman}). Similarly, a {\i kohen} who declares during or immediately upon the service that {\i zvachim she\u8217'asiti imcha nitpiglu} \u8212- the sacrifices I performed for you have been rendered {\i pigul} \u8212- is believed. But if the same person comes days later and says {\i taharot she\u8217'asiti imcha beyom ploni nitme\u8217'u} or {\i zvachim she\u8217'asiti imcha beyom ploni nitpiglu} \u8212- referring to events of a specific past date \u8212- he is not believed ({\i eino ne\u8217'eman}).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye explains the distinction through the principle of {\i kol shebeyado ne\u8217'eman} \u8212- whoever has the matter in his control is believed. At the moment of the first declaration, the person is still actively working with the {\i taharot} or the {\i kohen} has shechted the animal but not yet thrown the blood. Because he retains control over the outcome \u8212- he could render them {\i tamei} or {\i pigul} at this very moment if he chose \u8212- this gives his claim a special credibility, somewhat analogous to a {\i migo}. According to Abaye, this principle applies strictly: the person must literally still have the matter in hand.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava: Suspicion\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava disagrees with Abaye\u8217's framework. It is not necessary that the act still be {\i mamash beyado} at the moment of the claim. Rather, the reason for disbelieving the later claim is not absence of control but suspicious delay: {\i keivan de\u8217'ashkechei velo amar lei velo midi} \u8212- since he encountered the person and said nothing at the time, and only later makes the claim, the delay itself is suspicious. Why would someone who witnessed or caused a problem fail to mention it immediately? The belated disclosure raises the inference of ulterior motive. But if someone reports the issue promptly after completing the service \u8212- even if it is technically no longer {\i beyado} \u8212- he would be believed under Rava\u8217's view.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Delayed Claim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The practical upshot is that if someone waited and then came to another person saying, for instance, that three days ago the {\i taharot} became {\i tamei}, his claim is rejected. A case is brought before Rabbi Ami involving precisely this scenario, and Rabbi Ami rules {\i eino ne\u8217'eman} \u8212- he is not believed, because the claim concerns something that happened in the past with no immediate disclosure.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kohen Gadol\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Assi raises a counterposition in the name of Rabbi Yochanan in the name of Rabbi Yossi: {\i ma e\u8217'eseh she-haTorah he\u8217'eminaso} \u8212- what can I do, the Torah has believed him. The {\i gemara} asks: where does the Torah demonstrate this believability? Rabbi Yitzchak bar Bisna answers: the {\i kohen gadol} on Yom Kippur serves as proof. If the {\i kohen gadol} were to emerge from the {\i Kodesh Kodashim} and declare that something had gone wrong \u8212- that the service had been rendered {\i pigul}, for example \u8212- presumably we would believe him, even though the act occurred in a space where no other person was permitted. This shows that even after the fact, the Torah grants believability to such declarations.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} challenges this: perhaps we would only believe the {\i kohen gadol} because we heard his voice from within, indicating something was said at the time of the service. The {\i gemara} responds: that would not suffice either, because the {\i kohen gadol} could have uttered those words after performing the service, intending to deceive. Therefore it must be that the Torah grants inherent credibility to such testimony. The {\i gemara} then raises a further challenge \u8212- perhaps there were peepholes through which observers could have seen the {\i kohen gadol} \u8212- and ultimately concedes {\i kashya}: it is not an airtight proof. The case of the {\i kohen gadol} may be consistent with Rava\u8217's position \u8212- that if the declaration came immediately upon emerging, there was no suspicious delay \u8212- so it does not definitively establish the broader point.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Sefer Torah Cases\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Ami Rules\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A case is brought before Rabbi Ami: a scribe who had written a {\i Sefer Torah} for a client comes forward and admits that when writing the divine names ({\i azkarot}), he did not write them {\i lishmah} \u8212- with the proper intention. The {\i Sefer Torah} has already been transferred to the purchaser; it is no longer {\i beyado}. Rabbi Ami rules: {\i ne\u8217'eman atah lehafsid sekharekha ve-i atah ne\u8217'eman lehafsid Sefer Torah} \u8212- you are believed to the extent that you forfeit your fee, but you are not believed to render the {\i Sefer Torah pasul}. The {\i Sefer Torah} retains its {\i kasher} status in the hands of the purchaser, while the scribe loses his payment for having admitted to performing the work improperly.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This creates an apparent internal tension: if the {\i Sefer Torah} is going to be treated as valid for the purchaser\u8217's use, why should the scribe not be paid for a valid {\i Sefer Torah}? The {\i gemara} does not fully resolve this in this context; Rabbi Ami\u8217's ruling simply bifurcates the two consequences.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yirmiyah Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yirmiyah presses the ruling further: granted that the scribe has forfeited some fee \u8212- but must he lose the entire fee for the {\i Sefer Torah}? He wrote the entire text correctly; only the {\i azkarot} were written without proper intention. Surely he should at minimum be paid for all the writing other than the {\i Shem Hashem} portions?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Worthlessness\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Ami responds: {\i she-kol Sefer Torah she-ein azkaroteha ketuvot lishmah einah shaveh klum} \u8212- a {\i Sefer Torah} whose divine names were not written {\i lishmah} is worth nothing at all. The reason: one cannot simply rewrite the names. Erasing {\i Shem Hashem} is forbidden. There is no remediation available.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Spotted Look\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} presses: why not simply re-ink over the names? Could Rabbi Yehuda\u8217's ruling be invoked here \u8212- that {\i ma\u8217'avir alav kulmus u-mekadesho}, one may re-ink and thereby sanctify the writing? The {\i gemara} answers that Rabbi Yehuda only permits re-inking a single name. To re-ink every instance of {\i Shem Hashem} throughout an entire {\i Sefer Torah} \u8212- {\i mishum de-mikhzei ke-menumar} \u8212- would make the scroll appear spotted, with every divine name visibly different from the surrounding text. A {\i Sefer Torah} that appears this way is not fit for use, whether this is a {\i de\u8217'oraita} or {\i derabbanan} disqualification. Either way, the scribe has produced something worthless, and accordingly forfeits his entire fee.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Abbahu Rules\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A parallel case is brought before Rabbi Abbahu: a scribe comes forward and says that the {\i gevilin} \u8212- the parchment \u8212- of a {\i Sefer Torah} he wrote for a client were not processed {\i lishmah}, with the requisite intention during their preparation. The {\i Sefer Torah} is already in the purchaser\u8217's possession. Rabbi Abbahu rules: {\i mitoch she\u8217'ata ne\u8217'eman lehafsid sekharekha, ata ne\u8217'eman lehafsid Sefer Torah} \u8212- since you are believed to lose your fee, you are also believed to render the {\i Sefer Torah pasul}. This is the opposite of Rabbi Ami\u8217's ruling: Rabbi Abbahu believes the scribe not only about his financial loss but about the invalidity of the scroll.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Reconciliation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} confronts the apparent contradiction between Rabbi Ami and Rabbi Abbahu. It resolves the two cases by focusing on the scribe\u8217's financial exposure. In Rabbi Ami\u8217's case, where only the {\i azkarot} were written without {\i lishmah}, Rabbi Yirmiyah had raised a reasonable argument that the scribe should perhaps only lose the fee for writing the divine names, not the entire fee. One might say the scribe calculated that he was in a relatively favorable position \u8212- he could claim to lose only a small portion of his fee while the {\i Sefer Torah} would be disqualified. There was therefore an incentive to make the false claim: he would lose very little while harming the purchaser\u8217's {\i Sefer Torah}. Since the claim carried only limited financial self-damage, the {\i migo}-like circumstantial argument for truthfulness is not compelling, and we therefore do not extend his believability to {\i poseling} the scroll.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 In Rabbi Abbahu\u8217's case, the {\i gevilin} themselves \u8212- every single piece of parchment \u8212- were not processed {\i lishmah}. There is no question of partial loss: the entire {\i Sefer Torah} is unambiguously worthless, and the scribe will forfeit his entire fee. {\i Kivan de-ka mafsid kula agrei} \u8212- since he knows he will lose everything and comes forward anyway, {\i eimar kushta ka\u8217'amar} \u8212- we must presume he is telling the truth. The magnitude of his financial self-sacrifice eliminates any plausible ulterior motive, and this circumstantial evidence of truthfulness is sufficient to believe him even though the matter is no longer technically {\i beyado}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Gittin 55\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 R. Yochanan ben Gudguda\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Opening & Mishna\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Daf 55 of {\i Gittin} opens by continuing the broader sugya of enactments made for {\i tikkun ha-olam} and similar {\i takkanot}. The {\i mishna} presents the testimonies of Rabbi Yochanan ben Gudguda \u8212- a {\i mishna} that appears as well in {\i Eduyos}, the tractate dedicated to collecting halachic traditions that various Sages transmitted. Rabbi Yochanan ben Gudguda testified to four distinct rulings, each of which, as we shall see, carries its own significance for the theme of rabbinic enactment.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chereshet & Divorce\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The first testimony concerns a {\i chereshet} \u8212- a deaf woman. The term {\i chereshet} technically refers to one who is deaf-mute, though it does not necessarily mean she is entirely incapable of speech; rather, because she was born deaf and had no means of learning intelligible language in the manner of hearing people, {\i halacha} categorizes her as someone without sufficient {\i da\u8217'as} to be obligated in {\i mitzvos} or to execute binding legal transactions. This assumption, it should be noted, is rooted in pre-modern realities: until relatively recent centuries, when schools for the deaf were developed and sign language emerged, it was widely believed that deafness precluded the development of full intellect. {\i Halacha} operated within that understanding, and the reassessment of that assumption is itself a significant discussion of the past few hundred years.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 In the case before us, the girl is both a minor and a {\i chereshet}, meaning she has two reasons why she cannot independently transact. However, a father has the authority to betroth his minor daughter, and that betrothal is binding {\i d\u8217'oraisa}. Now the husband wishes to divorce her, and the question arises: since we assume she lacks full {\i da\u8217'as}, does she have sufficient awareness to receive a {\i get} properly? Rabbi Yochanan ben Gudguda testified that yes, {\i yotzei b\u8217'get} \u8212- the delivery of a {\i get} does work. The reasoning: since a woman can be divorced even against her will, it follows that she can be divorced even without full cognizance of what is occurring. That conclusion is striking \u8212- not being willing and not being fully aware are not necessarily the same thing \u8212- yet that is the {\i halacha} as received.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ketana & Teruma\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The second testimony involves a {\i ketana bat yisrael} married to a {\i kohen}. When a minor girl is married not by her father but through her mother and brothers, that marriage is only {\i d\u8217'rabbanan} \u8212- the Torah does not recognize it as a fully binding betrothal, but the rabbis established it to protect the interests of girls in a society where they could not generally provide for themselves independently. Despite being only a rabbinic marriage, Rabbi Yochanan ben Gudguda testified that {\i ocheles biterumah} \u8212- she may eat {\i teruma}. A {\i kohen}\u8217's wife is permitted to eat {\i teruma}, and even though this marriage is not Torah-binding, it was treated with sufficient weight that she was allowed to eat it. The reasoning behind this, and how it is possible given the rabbinic nature of the marriage, is developed at length in the {\i gemara}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} also states {\i ve\u8217'im mesa ba\u8217'ala yorshah} \u8212- if she dies, her husband inherits her. Even for a {\i d\u8217'rabbanan} marriage, the inheritance follows the same rule as a Torah marriage.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Marish Gazul\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The third testimony concerns a stolen beam \u8212- {\i marish gazul} \u8212- that has been incorporated into a building. In principle, if a stolen object retains its identity and can still be identified, the owner has a right to reclaim it. In some cases, the beam might be embedded deep within a structure and yet technically still be the same beam it always was, with no sufficient {\i shinui} to sever the owner\u8217's claim. The {\i halacha} established was nevertheless that {\i shekinitahu} \u8212- the owner is simply paid the monetary value of the beam and cannot demand its physical return. The reason given is {\i mishum takanas hashavim} \u8212- because we wish to enable and encourage repentance. We do not want the practical consequences of returning stolen property to become so burdensome that they deter people from owning up to their wrongdoing.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chatas Gazula\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The fourth testimony concerns {\i chatat hagzulah shelo nodah larabim} \u8212- a stolen sin-offering that was not publicly known to be stolen. The case in the {\i gemara} is specific: Reuven stole Shimon\u8217's unsanctified animal and then sanctified it himself as his own {\i korban}. The question is whether this {\i korban} \u8212- stolen and then sanctified by the thief \u8212- can function as a valid {\i chatat} for Reuven. The {\i mishna} rules that if the theft was not public knowledge, {\i hi mechaperes} \u8212- it does atone \u8212- {\i mipnei tikkun hamizbe\u8217'ach}, for the sake of the {\i mizbeach}. The precise import of this phrase, and the underlying question of what the {\i d\u8217'oraisa} rule actually is, occupies the {\i gemara} at considerable length.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mnemonic Pattern\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is worth noting that while these four testimonies may appear random, there is a mnemonic logic connecting them. The first case begins with {\i hisia aviha} \u8212- her father married her off \u8212- the {\i chereshet}. The next case flows naturally: {\i niset l\u8217'kohen} \u8212- she married a {\i kohen}, with the husband ultimately inheriting her. Then comes {\i hamarish hagazul}, the stolen beam. And finally {\i chatat hagzulah shelo nodah larabim}. Each testimony flows into the next through shared language or shared conceptual links, allowing one to remember the full sequence.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Chereshet Divorce\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Undisclosed Get Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} opens its analysis with a ruling from Rava. From the testimony of Rabbi Yochanan ben Gudguda, Rava derives an additional {\i halacha}: if a man said to witnesses, \u8220"See this {\i get} \u8212- I am about to give it to my wife,\u8221" and then folded it up and handed it to her while telling her it was a {\i shtar chov} \u8212- a promissory note \u8212- without disclosing it was a {\i get}, and she took possession of it, {\i harei zo megureshet} \u8212- she is in fact divorced. The delivery worked, even though she was entirely unaware that she was receiving a {\i get}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 No Cognizance Needed\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The source for this ruling is the principle derived from Rabbi Yochanan ben Gudguda: {\i lo bainan da\u8217'atah} \u8212- we do not require her full awareness. Just as the {\i chereshet}, who we assume lacks full {\i da\u8217'as}, can nonetheless receive a {\i get}, so too a woman who takes possession of a {\i get} without knowing it is a {\i get} is divorced. The {\i gemara} anticipates the objection that this should be obvious once the {\i chereshet} principle is established, but then raises a genuine concern.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Nullification Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might argue that the two cases are not analogous. In the {\i chereshet} case, the husband simply did not inform her of the meaning of what was happening \u8212- a passive absence of communication. But here, when the husband says \u8220"take this {\i shtar chov},\u8221" he is actively calling it something other than a {\i get}. Perhaps this constitutes a {\i bittul} \u8212- a nullification \u8212- of the {\i get}\u8217's status. Perhaps by reframing the act of giving, he has effectively undone the {\i get}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Witness Declaration\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} responds: {\i im ita d\u8217'vatleih, leadurei hava amar lehu} \u8212- if he truly intended to nullify the {\i get}, he would have gone back and told the witnesses. He had already declared to the witnesses that he was divorcing his wife. The fact that he did not update them demonstrates that the statement \u8220"take this {\i shtar chov}\u8221" was not meant as a {\i bittul}. {\i V\u8217'hai d\u8217'kamar hachi mishum kisufa} \u8212- he called it a {\i shtar chov} out of embarrassment, or perhaps simply to be particularly unkind, but it was not a formal reframing of the act. The {\i get} therefore stands.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Ketana Teruma\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Why Ketana?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} turns to the second testimony \u8212- the {\i ketana} married to a {\i kohen} who eats {\i teruma} \u8212- and immediately asks a pointed question. If the goal of this testimony was to establish that a {\i d\u8217'rabbanan} marriage to a {\i kohen} is sufficient to allow the wife to eat {\i teruma}, why did the {\i mishna} move to the case of a {\i ketana}? We had just been discussing the {\i chereshet}, whose marriage is also {\i d\u8217'rabbanan}. Why not remain with the {\i chereshet} and use her as the example? The implicit answer, which the {\i gemara} explores, is that {\i v\u8217'ilu hereshet lo achla} \u8212- the {\i mishna} is implying that a {\i chereshet} could not eat {\i teruma} in that situation. And the {\i gemara} sets out to explain why.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Cheresh Gezeira\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The concern is a {\i gezeira}: {\i shema ya\u8217'achil cheresh b\u8217'chereshet} \u8212- we are afraid that allowing a {\i chereshet} married to a hearing {\i kohen} to eat {\i teruma} will lead people to allow a {\i cheresh} \u8212- a deaf male {\i kohen} \u8212- married to a hearing, fully obligated woman to similarly feed her {\i teruma}. And why is this such a problem? The {\i gemara} clarifies: a {\i chereshet} married to a hearing {\i kohen} \u8212- the person eating is the {\i chereshet}, who is not halachically obligated, so one might argue the violation is tolerable given the practical need to validate the {\i d\u8217'rabbanan} marriage. But the mirror image \u8212- a {\i cheresh kohen} married to a {\i pikachat}, a hearing and fully obligated woman \u8212- in that case, {\i she} is eating {\i teruma}, and she is fully obligated, and her eating genuinely prohibited {\i teruma} is a real violation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mirror Image Problem\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Here lies the structural problem. The {\i chereshet} case and the {\i cheresh} case are natural mirror images of each other. If we permit the {\i chereshet} to eat, it will be easy to allow the analogous {\i cheresh} case as well \u8212- and in that case, a fully obligated woman would be eating {\i teruma} she is not permitted to eat. Since we cannot permit the {\i cheresh} case, we must also prohibit the {\i chereshet} case, to prevent one from leading to the other. The {\i ketana} case, by contrast, does not have this mirror-image problem. {\i Halacha} recognizes the marriage of an adult male to an underage female, both {\i d\u8217'oraisa} through the father and {\i d\u8217'rabbanan} through the mother and brothers, but it does not recognize the marriage of a minor male to an adult female \u8212- neither {\i d\u8217'oraisa} nor {\i d\u8217'rabbanan}. Since the reverse scenario does not exist, the fear of a problematic mirror image is eliminated, and we can permit the {\i ketana} to eat {\i teruma} without concern that this will lead to any genuine violation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Teruma Allowance\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then asks: even if we concede that the {\i chereshet} case cannot work for {\i teruma d\u8217'oraisa}, why not allow her at least to eat {\i teruma d\u8217'rabbanan} \u8212- rabbinic-level {\i teruma}, applicable, for example, to produce not ordinarily subject to the full Torah prohibition? Then, even if the {\i cheresh} case were to arise in its mirror image, the woman eating would only be violating a rabbinic prohibition. The {\i gemara} responds: {\i gezeira shema ati l\u8217'meichal b\u8217'teruma d\u8217'oraisa} \u8212- we are nonetheless concerned that permitting {\i teruma d\u8217'rabbanan} in the {\i chereshet} case will ultimately lead to eating {\i teruma d\u8217'oraisa} in the {\i cheresh} case. Even though this requires two things to go wrong, sometimes two layers of protection are warranted, and here the rabbis chose to apply that stricter approach.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rashi adds that even in the {\i ketana} case, we are likely dealing with {\i teruma d\u8217'rabbanan}, though the {\i gemara} itself seems comfortable suggesting that even {\i teruma d\u8217'oraisa} would be permitted in the {\i ketana} case, since as a minor she is not obligated, and there is no mirror-image scenario to fear.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Marish Gazul Dispute\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Demolition vs.\u160 ?Payment\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i baraita} presents the dispute between Beis Shammai and Beis Hillel on the question of the stolen beam. If one stole a beam and incorporated it into a building, Beis Shammai rules {\i meka\u8217'ke\u8217'a kol habira kula u\u8217'machazir marish l\u8217'ba\u8217'alav} \u8212- he must demolish the entire building and return the actual beam to its owner. Beis Shammai, characteristically uncompromising in upholding strict legal entitlement, holds that the thief\u8217's practical difficulty is irrelevant. The owner\u8217's right to his property cannot be subordinated to the thief\u8217's interest in keeping his building intact.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Takanas Hashavim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Beis Hillel, by contrast, rules {\i ein lo ela d\u8217'mei marish bilvad} \u8212- the owner is entitled only to the monetary value of the beam. The reason: {\i mishum takanas hashavim} \u8212- to encourage repentance. If the {\i halacha} demanded complete demolition, many thieves would be deterred from ever admitting their wrongdoing or seeking to make amends, calculating that the cost of restitution is too catastrophic. By limiting the obligation to a monetary payment, the rabbis removed that obstacle and made {\i teshuva} a realistic and accessible path.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Chatas Gazula\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ulla\u8217's Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now presents two competing views on the underlying {\i d\u8217'oraisa} status of the stolen {\i korban}. Ulla holds: {\i devar Torah bein noda bein lo noda einah mechaperes} \u8212- by Torah law, whether the theft was publicly known or not, the stolen {\i korban} does not atone. His reasoning is {\i yeiush k\u8217'dei lo kani} \u8212- {\i yeiush} alone, without {\i shinui reshus}, does not transfer ownership to the thief.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 To understand this, one must recall the general debate about {\i yeiush}. When an owner despairs of recovering a lost or stolen object, does that despair itself confer ownership on whoever holds it? In the case of a lost object found by a third party, {\i yeiush} combined with {\i shinui reshus} \u8212- a change of domain \u8212- does effect a transfer. But the {\i gazlan} is different. He was holding the object at the very moment the owner gave up hope. He cannot claim that the owner\u8217's despair freed up the object for him to acquire, since he took it while it was fully under the owner\u8217's control and he has an independent obligation to return it. So {\i yeiush} alone, while the object remains in the {\i gazlan}\u8217's hands, does not work.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If Reuven\u8217's {\i yeiush} did not transfer ownership to him, then when Reuven sanctified the animal, he was sanctifying something that was not his. One could argue that the act of sanctification itself constitutes a {\i shinui reshus} \u8212- a transfer into the domain of {\i hekdesh} \u8212- or a {\i shinui hashem}, a change of identity, such that the animal becomes genuinely sanctified. The {\i gemara} entertains both possibilities. It is possible that the animal does become a valid {\i korban} but remains one that cannot atone for Reuven, since at the moment he sanctified it, it was still stolen \u8212- applying the principle of {\i mitzvah haba\u8217'ah b\u8217'aveirah}. Either way, according to Ulla, the {\i d\u8217'oraisa} baseline is that this {\i korban} does not atone.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kohanim Anxiety\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Given that this is Ulla\u8217's position, the {\i gemara} asks: {\i uma ta\u8217'am amru lo noda mechaperes?} \u8212- why then did the rabbis say that if the theft was not publicly known, it does atone? The answer: {\i shelo yehu kohanim atzeivim} \u8212- so that the {\i kohanim} should not be filled with anguish. The {\i kohanim} eat from the {\i korban}, and if they are always anxious that they might have consumed from a stolen offering that was not truly valid \u8212- and therefore may not have been truly a {\i korban}, leaving them in a state of having eaten what was in effect {\i chullin she-nishchatu ba-azarah} \u8212- they would become paralyzed with worry. Their anxiety would disrupt the entire operation of the {\i mikdash}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} notes that the language of the {\i mishna} says {\i mipnei tikkun hamizbe\u8217'ach}, not {\i mipnei kohanim atzeivim}. Ulla responds: {\i keivan de-kohanim atzeivim nimtza mizbe\u8217'ach batel} \u8212- since if the {\i kohanim} are anguished, the {\i mizbeach} itself will be impaired. The two are inseparable: the functioning of the {\i mizbeach} depends on the {\i kohanim} being able to perform their service with confidence and wholeness. Therefore, when it is not publicly known, we declare the {\i korban} valid \u8212- even though {\i d\u8217'oraisa} it should not be \u8212- so that the {\i kohanim} will not be consumed by worry. However, when the theft is public knowledge, we cannot extend that protection, and the {\i korban} will not be accepted.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Yehuda Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Yehuda takes the opposite {\i d\u8217'oraisa} position: {\i bein noda bein lo noda mechapper} \u8212- by Torah law, it atones regardless of whether the theft was known. His reasoning: {\i yeiush k\u8217'dei kani} \u8212- {\i yeiush} does work to transfer ownership. The moment Shimon despaired of recovering his animal, Reuven became the owner. When Reuven then sanctified it, it was already his, and therefore it is a completely valid {\i korban} that should work in every case.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mizbeach & Theft\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 According to Rav Yehuda, the {\i takana} runs in the other direction. The {\i takana} is not to expand the circumstances in which the {\i korban} works, but to restrict it: {\i shelo yomru mizbe\u8217'ach ochel gezeilos} \u8212- so that people should not say that the {\i mizbe\u8217'ach} consumes stolen goods. If a thief can steal an animal and then bring it publicly as a {\i korban} and have it accepted, it creates a horrific impression: that the {\i mizbeach} is complicit in theft, that one can steal and atone through that very stolen object. The {\i pasuk} states {\i sonei gezel b\u8217'olah} \u8212- Hashem despises theft in a burnt offering \u8212- and this concern extends even to a {\i chatat}, where only the blood and fats go on the {\i mizbeach} while the {\i kohanim} eat the meat. Even in that case, the {\i gemara} concludes, it still models the {\i mizbeach} consuming stolen property, and we must not allow that when the theft is publicly known.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then asks: according to Rav Yehuda, why did the {\i mishna} specifically mention a {\i chatat}? If the concern is that the {\i mizbeach} appears to consume stolen goods, surely an {\i olah} \u8212- which is entirely consumed on the {\i mizbeach} \u8212- would be even more problematic. The {\i gemara} answers {\i lo mibaya ka\u8217'amar}: it goes without saying that a publicly stolen {\i olah} would not be accepted. The {\i mishna} chooses the {\i chatat} precisely because one might have thought it less objectionable \u8212- only the blood and fats go on the {\i mizbeach}, and the meat is eaten by the {\i kohanim} \u8212- and one might argue this does not really look like the {\i mizbeach} consuming stolen goods. The {\i mishna} therefore teaches that even so, the rabbis prohibited it, because it still gives the impression of the {\i mizbeach} endorsing theft.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Slaughter Outside Mikdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kereis Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava now raises a powerful challenge against Ulla\u8217's position from a {\i mishna} elsewhere. The {\i mishna} states: {\i ganav vehikdish v\u8217'achar kach tavach umachar} \u8212- one stole, sanctified, and then slaughtered or sold the animal. He pays double ({\i tashlumei kefel}) for the theft, but does not pay the fourfold or fivefold payment ({\i tashlumei arba va-chamisha}) for slaughtering or selling, because by the time he slaughtered or sold it, it had already passed into {\i hekdesh} and was no longer in Shimon\u8217's ownership \u8212- therefore Shimon no longer has that claim. This much Ulla can accommodate.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But the {\i mishna} continues: {\i vetani alah, bachutz kehai gavna anush kares} \u8212- if he took this sanctified animal and slaughtered it outside the {\i beit hamikdash}, he is liable for {\i kares}, the penalty for {\i shichutei chutz} \u8212- offering a sanctified animal outside the {\i mikdash}. This is deeply problematic for Ulla. One is only liable for {\i kares} for slaughtering outside the {\i mikdash} something that would have been a valid {\i korban} inside the {\i mikdash}. If the animal is valid enough to generate {\i kares} when slaughtered outside, it implies that it would have worked as a valid {\i korban} inside \u8212- which is exactly what Ulla denies.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kereis Midvreihem\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Shizbi attempted to resolve this by suggesting that the {\i kares} here is {\i midivreihem} \u8212- a rabbinic enactment that treats it as if {\i kares} applies, even though it would not apply {\i d\u8217'oraisa}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kereis Cannot Be Rabbinic\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This answer was met with laughter in the {\i beit midrash}, and Rava rebuked that response: {\i gavra rabba amar milta, lo techechu alei} \u8212- a great Sage said something; do not laugh at it. There must be something to it. But the substantive rejection stands: there is no such thing as {\i kares midivrehem}. Rabbinic enactments can prohibit actions, can impose lashes, but {\i kares} is a metaphysical reality \u8212- premature death at the hands of Heaven. The rabbis cannot create that. When the {\i mishna} says {\i kares}, it means {\i kares d\u8217'oraisa}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava therefore reframes what Rav Shizbi must have meant: {\i kares al yedei divreihem ba lo} \u8212- the {\i kares} comes to him through the medium of a rabbinic enactment. It is a genuine {\i d\u8217'oraisa kares}, but it arrives as a consequence of what the rabbis did.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Retroactive Ownership\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Before Sanctification\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafos provides the clearest explanation of what this means. When Ulla said that the {\i takana} is that a stolen {\i korban} will atone if the theft is not publicly known, the {\i takana} did not merely declare that we will {\i treat} it as if it atoned while secretly acknowledging it did not. Rather, the {\i takana} actually conferred ownership. At the moment Reuven sanctified this animal, the rabbis assigned him ownership \u8212- {\i d\u8217'rabbanan}, the rabbis have the authority to assign ownership \u8212- so that at the instant of sanctification, the animal was genuinely his. Now it becomes a fully valid {\i korban}: valid to atone inside the {\i mikdash}, and generating genuine {\i d\u8217'oraisa kares} if slaughtered outside. The {\i kares} is real and {\i d\u8217'oraisa}; it came to exist through the rabbinic enactment of assigning ownership.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Timing Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava now raises a further question: {\i de\u8217'okmuha rabbanan birshusei mishsha geneiva o mishsha hekdesh?} \u8212- when the rabbis assigned him ownership, did they do so from the moment of the theft, or only from the moment of sanctification? The practical difference: {\i ligizozeha ulvladoteha} \u8212- what happens to the wool shorn from the animal and the offspring born to it during the intervening period, while Reuven held the animal between stealing it and sanctifying it? If he owned it from the moment of theft, all of that belongs to him. If he owned it only from the moment of sanctification, then all of that remains Shimon\u8217's, and Reuven must return it when he does {\i teshuva}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 At Sanctification\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava himself resolves this: {\i mistabra mishsha hekdesh} \u8212- it is logical that the ownership was assigned from the moment of sanctification, and not retroactively from the moment of theft. The reason: {\i shelo yehei chotei niskar} \u8212- we will not allow the sinner to benefit. If we need to assign him ownership in order to make the {\i korban} valid, we will do so \u8212- but only for as long as necessary. There is no reason to reach further back and grant him ownership of the wool and offspring from the moment of theft. We assign him ownership only at the moment of sanctification, which is precisely when it is needed.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Sikrikon Law\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sikrikon Definition\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} now introduces the law of the {\i sikrikon}. {\i Sikrikon} were non-Jewish extortionists \u8212- Romans or others \u8212- who threatened Jews with death unless they sold their land. A Jew faced with such a threat would sell under extreme duress, almost certainly at a significant discount, simply to preserve his life. The {\i mishna} establishes that special rules apply to property that passed through the hands of such extortionists. The {\i gemara} notes that during the immediate period of active warfare and mass slaughter \u8212- {\i harugei milchama} \u8212- the law of {\i sikrikon} did not apply at all. Afterwards, once the killing subsided but the extortion continued, the rabbis ruled {\i yesh ba-sikrikon} \u8212- the law of {\i sikrikon} is now operative.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Original Owner Retains\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The original ruling \u8212- the {\i mishna rishona} \u8212- was that the original Jewish owner, call him Reuven, retained true ownership rights even after the forced sale. The {\i sikrikon} could not acquire the land in any meaningful halachic sense through coercion. In practice, though, anyone wishing to actually possess the land would have to deal with both parties: Reuven, the true owner, and the {\i sikrikon}, who physically controls it. The original ruling therefore focused on the order in which a third-party purchaser went about acquiring it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sikrikon Then Owner\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If one first paid the {\i sikrikon} and then went to Reuven to obtain his consent, the purchase is void \u8212- {\i mikcho batel}. The reason: Reuven\u8217's agreement in that scenario is not freely given. The {\i sikrikon} has already been paid; Reuven knows that if he creates difficulties, the {\i sikrikon}, who already received his payment and has a stake in the deal\u8217's completion, will make Reuven\u8217's life miserable. Reuven\u8217's ostensible consent is therefore coerced, and we do not treat it as genuine.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Owner Then Sikrikon\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 By contrast, if one first approached Reuven \u8212- before any dealing with the {\i sikrikon} \u8212- and Reuven freely agreed, calculating that he may never recover the land and that at least he can salvage some value by selling his rights, then Reuven\u8217's consent is genuine and uncoerced. When the purchaser then goes to the {\i sikrikon} to pay him off and gain physical control, {\i mikcho kayam} \u8212- the purchase stands.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Husband-Wife Analogy\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} draws an analogous case from property within a marriage. Where the wife has rights to certain property \u8212- whether brought as a dowry or secured through her {\i ketuba} \u8212- but the husband has practical control over it, the structure mirrors the {\i sikrikon} situation. The wife is like Reuven, the true holder of rights; the husband is like the {\i sikrikon}, with practical control. If a purchaser first bought from the husband and then sought the wife\u8217's consent, the purchase is void \u8212- her apparent agreement is suspect, as she would fear the consequences of opposing her husband. If he first approached the wife, who freely agreed, and then dealt with the husband, the purchase stands.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Later Beit Din Ruling\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sikrikon as Owner\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Zo mishna rishona} \u8212- that was the first ruling. A later {\i beit din} took a more pragmatic approach. Rather than treating Reuven as the true owner and requiring buyers to navigate the order-of-purchase rules, this later court ruled: {\i halokeach misikrikon nosein libaalim revia} \u8212- one who buys from the {\i sikrikon} must give the original owner a quarter of the value. In practice, the {\i sikrikon} is treated as the owner for purposes of the transaction, and the buyer deals only with the {\i sikrikon} \u8212- but must then compensate Reuven with one quarter of the purchase price, corresponding to the discount Reuven presumably suffered when he was forced to sell under duress. The assumption, somewhat remarkable, is that even with a gun to his head, the {\i sikrikon} only extracted a twenty-five percent discount.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The logic of this pragmatic shift is significant. The original ruling, while legally principled, created enormous practical obstacles to returning land to Jewish control. Requiring buyers to navigate competing claims, worry about the order of purchase, and secure Reuven\u8217's genuinely free consent may have been theoretically correct but was functionally paralyzing. The later {\i beit din} chose to treat the facts on the ground as given and devise a workable framework: acknowledge the {\i sikrikon}\u8217's de facto control, compensate the original owner for his loss, and make it feasible for the land to change hands in an orderly way.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Unable to Repurchase\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The new rule applies specifically {\i bizman she\u8217'ein beyadam likach} \u8212- when the original owner does not have the wherewithal to repurchase the property himself.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Right of First Refusal\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If the original owner does have the means to buy it back, {\i heim kodmin lechol adam} \u8212- he retains the right of first refusal before any other purchaser. The later court thus preserved Reuven\u8217's priority where it was realistic, while removing it where it was not.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Twelve-Month Rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 12-Month Cutoff\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi convened a {\i beit din} and they voted on a further refinement: {\i shehasah bifnei sikrikon shenayim asar chodesh, kol hakodem likach zachah} \u8212- if the property remained in the hands of the {\i sikrikon} for twelve months, it is simply first come, first serve. Reuven\u8217's right of first refusal, which the later {\i beit din} had preserved where he had the means to buy, applies only within that twelve-month window. After twelve months, we no longer require any buyer to seek out Reuven and offer him priority.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Quarter Payment\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 However, the obligation to pay Reuven one quarter of the transaction price remains in force even after the twelve months. The financial compensation to the original owner is not contingent on the time elapsed; it is a standing obligation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Practical Rationale\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The rationale behind this further enactment is twofold: practical administration and the broader goal of returning property to Jewish control. Requiring buyers to locate Reuven and offer him priority indefinitely would create an impossible situation. At some point the process must be regularized. Rabbi\u8217's {\i beit din} drew the line at twelve months \u8212- a full year \u8212- and declared that beyond that point, the property may pass freely to whoever comes first, provided the quarter-payment obligation to the original owner is honored. This balance between principled protection of original ownership and practical functionality represents the culminating refinement of the {\i sikrikon} law on this daf.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Gittin 56\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Three Edicts & Sicricon\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sicricon Existence\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The daf begins at the bottom of 55b, where the {\i Gemara} picks up a discussion emerging from the {\i mishnah}\u8217's treatment of the {\i sicricon} \u8212- a type of assassin or violent extorter \u8212- and the question of when the legal framework governing property seized by such figures applies. The {\i Gemara} poses a pointed question: {\i hashta b\u8217'harugei milchama lo haya sicricon, me\u8217'harugei milchama v\u8217'ilech yesh sicricon?} If, during the very height of the killing during the destruction, the law of {\i sicricon} did not apply, how much more so should it not apply afterward? How is it possible that the phenomenon didn\u8217't generate legal consequences during the wars but did afterward?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Yehuda resolves this apparent difficulty: the {\i mishnah} does not mean to say the {\i sicricon} himself didn\u8217't exist during the wars. Of course violent extorters were operating then. Rather, it means that the specific {\i din} \u8212- the legal doctrine \u8212- governing transactions involving {\i sicricon}-seized land did not apply.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Assi\u8217's Edicts\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The reason for this is explained by Rav Assi, who teaches that there were three consecutive edicts \u8212- {\i gimmel gezeiros} \u8212- during the period of the {\i Churban HaBayis}. The first edict, {\i gezeira kamaisa}, was {\i kol d\u8217'lo katil liktlu\u8217'u}: any soldier who refused to kill a Jew would himself be killed. The second edict, {\i metzi\u8217'asa}, was {\i kol d\u8217'katil leisi arba zuzei}: killing a Jew would earn a reward of four {\i zuz}, but there was no longer a death penalty for failing to do so. The third edict, {\i basraisa}, reversed course entirely: {\i kol d\u8217'katil liktlu\u8217'u}, killing a Jew was once again punishable by death, as the Roman authorities sought to restore order following the wars.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sale Doctrine\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The distinction between these three stages has direct {\i halachic} consequences. During the first and second edicts, when a {\i sicricon} threatened a Jew with death and demanded his land, the threat was entirely credible \u8212- the extorter either was obligated or at least rewarded for killing. Consequently, {\i keivan d\u8217'katlei, agav onseh gamur makni}: since the victim knew the threat was real, he genuinely and fully intended to transfer the land in exchange for his life. The sale was a complete {\i kinyan}. A third party who subsequently purchased that land from the {\i sicricon} acquired full ownership and need not return to the original owner to re-validate the transaction.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Third Edict Rights\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The third edict presents an entirely different legal picture. Under this edict, the {\i sicricon} was prohibited from killing the Jew. When the {\i sicricon} nonetheless demanded the land under threat, the owner reasoned: {\i idna lishkol, u\u8217'lemachar tavna lei b\u8217'dina} \u8212- let him take it today, and tomorrow I will pursue him in court. The owner never fully consented to the transfer; he merely yielded temporarily, expecting eventual legal redress. A third party who purchases such land from the {\i sicricon} therefore does not acquire good title. He must also go back and purchase it from the original owner in order for the transaction to be halachically sound.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Narrative Setup\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Notably, the {\i Gemara} does not linger on the further {\i halachic} details of the {\i mishnah} \u8212- the stages of {\i takkanah} governing whether a third party must pay the original owner a quarter of the land\u8217's value, and the proper order of purchase. Instead, it uses this brief legal excursus as a backdrop for the transition into the great {\i aggadata} of the {\i Churban HaBayis}, having established the violent historical context of soldiers killing Jews and the legal frameworks that emerged from it.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Fear & Destruction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Yochanan\u8217's Verse\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} opens its {\i aggadic} account of the destruction with a verse from Rabbi Yochanan: {\i ashrei adam m\u8217'fached tamid, u-maksheh libo yipol b\u8217'ra-ah} \u8212- praised is the person who is constantly in a state of fear, while the one who hardens his heart will fall into evil. This verse serves as the thematic key to everything that follows. The {\i m\u8217'fached tamid} is not mere anxiety; it is the posture of a person who is genuinely concerned about his actions, alive to the larger picture, aware of the possible ramifications of how he conducts himself. The {\i maksheh libo}, the one with the hardened heart, is the person moving through the world with blinders on, unable to see beyond the immediate moment.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Causes of Destruction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Against this backdrop, the {\i Gemara} names three events that brought about three destructions: {\i a-Kamtza u-var Kamtza charuv Yerushalayim} \u8212- Jerusalem was destroyed on account of Kamtza and Bar Kamtza. {\i A-tarnagola v\u8217'tarnagolta charuv Tur Malka} \u8212- the city of Tur Malka was destroyed because of a hen and a rooster. {\i A-shaka d\u8217'rispak charuv Beitar} \u8212- Beitar was destroyed because of the axle of a carriage.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The stories to follow will be primarily concerned with Jerusalem, and the connection to the verse is already implicit. The destruction came not only through the hardness of heart of those who acted \u8212- the host who expelled his guest, the zealots who refused to make peace \u8212- but also through those who lacked the fear, the {\i yir\u8217'ah}, to stand up and intervene. Both forms of failure \u8212- active cruelty and passive acquiescence \u8212- find their indictment in this verse.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is also worth noting the etymology of the name {\i Kamtza} itself. A {\i kometz} refers to a closed fist \u8212- the opposite of open-handed generosity. The very name telegraphs the theme of withholding, of miserly refusal to extend grace.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Kamtza & Bar Kamtza\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Feast & Mistake\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The story opens: {\i d\u8217'hahu gavra d\u8217'rachmei Kamtza u-va\u8217'al devaveih Bar Kamtza} \u8212- there was a man whose close friend was named Kamtza and whose enemy was named Bar Kamtza. He made a great feast and dispatched his attendant: {\i zil aysei li Kamtza} \u8212- go and invite Kamtza for me. The attendant went and by mistake invited Bar Kamtza. The host discovered his enemy sitting at his table.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Bar Kamtza Pleas\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The host confronted him directly: {\i mai ba-yis hacha} \u8212- what are you doing here? Get out. Bar Kamtza, acutely aware of the public shame that expulsion would bring him, pleaded: {\i ho-il v\u8217'asai shavkan} \u8212- since I am already here, simply let me stay. I will pay for whatever I eat and drink. The host refused. Bar Kamtza escalated his offer: I will pay for half the feast. Refused again. He offered to pay for the entire feast. {\i Amar lei lo} \u8212- still no.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Naktye b\u8217'yadeih v\u8217'okmeih v\u8217'afkeih} \u8212- the host took him by the hand, stood him up, and drove him out. No offer of money, not even covering the full cost of the banquet, could overcome this man\u8217's commitment to his hatred. There is something almost parabolic here about the siege of Jerusalem itself \u8212- people so entrenched in their enmity that they cannot shift course even when an obvious path of accommodation presents itself.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Public Expulsion\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Bar Kamtza drew a conclusion that would have devastating consequences: {\i ho-il vehavu yatvi rabbanan velo machu bei, shema mina kanicha lehu} \u8212- since the rabbis were sitting there and did not protest his expulsion, evidently they found it acceptable. This raises one of the central themes of the entire passage. The rabbis bear responsibility not through what they did but through what they failed to do. The principle that evil flourishes when good people stand by in silence is directly implicated here. The rabbis\u8217' passivity is not a minor failing; the {\i Gemara} will return to it with force. The question is not only about the host who was cruel, or the zealots who forced the catastrophe \u8212- it is also about the leaders who could have intervened at multiple junctures and did not.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Caesar & Sacrifice\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Bar Kamtza decided to act: {\i izil eichol behu kurtza bei malka} \u8212- he went and spread a malicious report to Caesar, informing him that the Jews were in rebellion. Caesar was skeptical: {\i mi yeimar} \u8212- who says so? Bar Kamtza proposed a test: send them a sacrifice and see whether they offer it up.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Caesar sent a fine calf \u8212- {\i egla tilta}, a well-fattened animal. On the way to Jerusalem, Bar Kamtza introduced a blemish: {\i be-niv sefatayim} \u8212- in the upper lip, or according to another opinion, {\i be-dukin she-ba-ayin} \u8212- a tiny film over the eye. The halachic asymmetry was precise: {\i duchta de-ledidan havei muma, u-ledidehu lav muma hu} \u8212- by Jewish law this constituted a disqualifying blemish for a Temple offering, but by Roman standards it was not considered a blemish at all. The Romans would have no idea why the Jews refused to sacrifice it, and would take it as confirmation of rebellion.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The detail of the blemish on the lip \u8212- {\i niv sefatayim}, which also connotes speech \u8212- is not incidental. The power of speech, for both harm and good, runs as a thread through these stories.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Sacrifice Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Peace Consideration\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 When the blemished animal arrived, the rabbis found themselves confronted with a fateful choice. {\i Savar rabbanan le-kruvei mishum shelom malchut} \u8212- the rabbis considered offering the blemished animal for the sake of peace with the government. This reflects an important posture: the willingness to bend halachic norms for the sake of a larger national interest. The {\i Gemara} here implicitly endorses this flexibility \u8212- the wiser course, as will become clear, was to offer it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Zechariah\u8217's Refusal\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Zechariah ben Avkulos objected: {\i yomru ba\u8217'alei mumin kreivin legabi mizbeach} \u8212- people will say that it is permissible to bring a blemished animal to the altar. He could not look past the halachic precedent that might be set.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kill Bar Kamtza Plan\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The rabbis then proposed an alternative: since they cannot offer the sacrifice and risk a diplomatic rupture, at least let them kill Bar Kamtza so he cannot carry back a negative report to Rome. Rabbi Zechariah objected to this as well: {\i yomru matil mum be-kodashim yehareg} \u8212- people will conclude that the punishment for blemishing a sacrifice is death.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yochanan Blames Zechariah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yochanan\u8217's verdict is famous and severe: {\i anvetanuto shel Rabbi Zecharia ben Avkulos hechriva et beiteinu ve-sarfa et heichaleinu ve-higlitnu me-artzeinu} \u8212- the humility of Rabbi Zechariah ben Avkulos destroyed our Temple, burned our sanctuary, and exiled us from our land.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The choice of the word {\i anvetanuto} \u8212- humility \u8212- is striking and deliberate. This was not simple obstinacy or stubbornness. Rabbi Zechariah\u8217's position can be framed as a kind of piety: who are we to override the {\i halacha}? Who are we to take such drastic action? His posture was the humble one, the {\i frum} one, the one that deferred to established norms rather than arrogating to himself the authority to deviate from them. And yet this very humility \u8212- this refusal to exercise rabbinic initiative and flexibility \u8212- was catastrophic.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But the criticism cannot be aimed at Rabbi Zechariah alone. The other rabbis were present and did not stand up to him. Their own passivity, their unwillingness to confront his position and override it, is equally implicated. This mirrors the passivity at the feast: again, the rabbis stand by. If Rabbi Zechariah\u8217's {\i anavah} destroyed the Temple, then the rabbis\u8217' reluctance to challenge him was its own form of failure. This forms a pointed contrast with the material on the preceding pages of the {\i masechta}, where the Rabbis exercised sweeping authority \u8212- employing {\i hefker beis din hefker}, overriding what would normally be the rules governing {\i korbanos}, redefining personal status \u8212- all for the sake of {\i tikkun olam}. The question the {\i Gemara} seems to be raising is: where was that boldness when it was needed most?\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Nero\u8217's Omens\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Arrow Divination\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 After the catastrophe was set in motion, {\i shadar alaihu le\u8217'Neron Keisar} \u8212- they dispatched Nero Caesar against Jerusalem. As he drew near, he engaged in a form of divination by shooting arrows. He shot to the east \u8212- {\i asa nefal bi\u8217'Yerushalayim}, the arrow fell toward Jerusalem. To the west \u8212- toward Jerusalem. {\i Le\u8217'arba ruchos ha\u8217'shamayim} \u8212- in all four directions \u8212- the arrow fell toward Jerusalem. The message was unambiguous: the divine will was pointing him to this city.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Child\u8217's Verse\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Nero then asked a child to recite whatever {\i pasuk} he happened to be studying at that moment. The child recited: {\i ve\u8217'nasati es nikmasi be\u8217'Edom, be\u8217'yad ami Yisrael} \u8212- I will take my vengeance against Edom through my people Israel. For the rabbis, Edom was the code for Rome.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Nero Converts\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Nero drew his own conclusion: {\i kudsha Berich Hu ba\u8217'ei le\u8217'charuvi beiseih} \u8212- God wants His Temple destroyed, {\i u-ba\u8217'ei le-kichuyei yadeih be-hu gavra} \u8212- and He intends to wipe His hands on me. God will use me to do His dirty work, and then I will bear the blame. This was not something Nero was willing to accept. {\i Arak va-azil ve-igaier} \u8212- he fled and converted to Judaism.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Meir Lineage\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} adds a remarkable coda: {\i ve-nafik mineih Rabbi Meir} \u8212- Rabbi Meir descended from Nero. Whatever one makes of this tradition in the rabbinic imagination, it is a striking claim \u8212- that one of the great Tannaim of the generation after the destruction traced his lineage to the Roman emperor who had been sent to destroy Jerusalem and refused.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Siege & Wealthy\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Vespasian & Siege\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 With Nero gone, {\i shadreih ilaveih le-Aspasyanus keisar} \u8212- Vespasian was dispatched. He is called \u8220"Caesar\u8221" in the narrative, though at this point he was still a general. {\i Ata tzar alah telat shani} \u8212- he besieged Jerusalem for three years.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Three Wealthy Men\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Within the besieged city, three extraordinarily wealthy men were positioned to sustain the population. {\i Nakdimon Ben Guryon} \u8212- a figure known elsewhere in the Talmud, whose name derives from {\i shenakdah lo chamah ba-avuro}, as if the sun itself broke through the heavens for his sake, a reference to the miracle associated with him. {\i Ben Kalba Savua} \u8212- {\i she-kol ha-nichnas le-beiso keshehu ra\u8217'ev kekelev yetzei keshehu savei\u8217'a} \u8212- anyone who entered his house as hungry as a dog would leave fully satisfied. And {\i Ben Tzitzit HaKeset} \u8212- {\i shehayesah tzitziyaso nigreret al gabei kesasos} \u8212- whose fringes would drag along cushions, because servants would walk beneath his garment holding pillows so the fringes would never touch the ground. Another tradition holds that his seat was literally placed among the great of Rome.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Support Offerings\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Each man stepped forward to guarantee provisions for the entire besieged city. One offered to supply all of Jerusalem\u8217's wheat and barley. A second offered all the wine, salt, and oil. The third offered to supply firewood. The {\i Gemara} notes that the rabbis gave special praise to the one who offered the firewood \u8212- citing a teaching of Rav Chisda: {\i akliba de-chitei ba\u8217'eia shisin aklibei de-detzivi} \u8212- one storage warehouse of wheat requires sixty warehouses of firewood to bake it. The sheer volume and value of what was being pledged in firewood was staggering.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Zealots Burn Food\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Together, the three pledges were sufficient to sustain Jerusalem under siege for twenty-one years. But this immense resource was rendered worthless. {\i Havu behu hanahu biryoni} \u8212- there were zealots in the city. When the rabbis counseled making peace \u8212- {\i nafek vena\u8217'avid shelma bahadeihu} \u8212- the zealots blocked them. When the rabbis said that going to war would not succeed \u8212- {\i lo mistaye\u8217'a milta} \u8212- the zealots responded by going to war anyway. And to force the population\u8217's hand and eliminate any possibility of negotiated surrender, {\i kalin\u8217'hu lehannu ambarei dechitti usha\u8217'arei} \u8212- they burned down the warehouses of wheat and barley. The famine was now inevitable.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Martha & Famine\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Martha\u8217's Flour Quest\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Martha the daughter of Baitos was among the wealthiest women in Jerusalem. With the famine setting in, she dispatched her attendant: {\i zil aeiti li smida} \u8212- go get me fine flour. By the time he arrived at the market, it had sold out. He returned and reported: no fine flour, but white flour is available. She sent him for the white flour. By the time he arrived, that too was gone. He returned: no white flour, but coarser flour is available. She sent him for that. Gone by the time he arrived. Barley flour remained. Gone as well.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Martha\u8217's Death\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Finally, Martha decided to go out herself \u8212- barefoot, it would seem, since the text says {\i havah shalpa masana}, she had removed her shoe. She stepped into dung, and the shock and revulsion of it \u8212- she who had been so utterly refined \u8212- killed her.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Martha\u8217's Verse\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabban Yochanan ben Zakkai applied to her the verse: {\i haraka becha vehanuga asher lo nista chaf ragla} \u8212- the tender and delicate woman who had never set the sole of her foot upon the ground. This was the very curse whose fulfillment she had now experienced.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 An alternative tradition is offered: {\i ika de\u8217'amri grogerot deRabbi Tzadok achala} \u8212- some say she found and ate one of the dried figs that Rabbi Tzadok had discarded. Rabbi Tzadok, as will be described, was so emaciated from his decades of fasting that even illness clung to the scraps he rejected. She consumed one of these and died from the contagion.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Tzadok Fast\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This brings the {\i Gemara} to Rabbi Tzadok himself. {\i Yativ arba\u8217'in shanin betanita} \u8212- he sat for forty years in a state of fasting, {\i dela licherav Yerushalayim} \u8212- so that Jerusalem might not be destroyed. When he ate at all, he was so thin that you could see the food going down from the outside of his body. When he was at his most recovered, they would bring him dried figs, {\i maitz meyayuhu} \u8212- he would suck out the juice, {\i v\u8217'shadileihu} \u8212- and throw them out. The contrast with Martha is pointed: Rabbi Tzadok is the {\i tzaddik} \u8212- in name and reality \u8212- who through private, invisible righteousness has been holding back the catastrophe. But his righteousness, as profound as it is, is not enough to reach others or redirect the course of events.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Martha\u8217's Wealth Cast\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 As she lay dying, Martha did something extraordinary: {\i afikta lechol dahava vechaspa veshadisa beshuka} \u8212- she took out all her gold and silver and threw it into the marketplace. {\i Hai lemai mibaya li} \u8212- what use is any of this to me now? The {\i Gemara} connects this to the verse: {\i kaspam bachutzot yashlichu} \u8212- their silver they shall throw out into the streets.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Yochanan\u8217's Escape\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Family Connection\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Abba Sikara} \u8212- whose name connects back to the {\i sicricon} with which the {\i daf} opened, and who is identified with the Sicarii, the dagger-men \u8212- was {\i reish biryonei diyerushalayim}, the head of the Jerusalem zealots. He was also {\i bar achtei d\u8217'Rabban Yochanan ben Zakkai} \u8212- the nephew of Rabban Yochanan ben Zakkai.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Confrontation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabban Yochanan sent for him privately: {\i ta betzina legabai} \u8212- come to me in secret. When Abba Sikara came, Rabban Yochanan confronted him directly: {\i ad matai avdisu hachi vekatlisu lehu l\u8217'alma dechafna} \u8212- how long will you continue like this, killing everyone through famine?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sikara\u8217's Dilemma\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abba Sikara\u8217's response was not defiance but despair: {\i mai avidei, de\u8217'amina lehu midi, katlu li} \u8212- what can I do? If I tell them to stand down, they will kill me. Even the head of the zealots was trapped by the logic of the movement he led.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Fake Death Plan\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabban Yochanan asked him to help engineer an escape: {\i chazi li takanta dididi, de\u8217'ipuk, efshar dehavi hatzalah purta} \u8212- find me some solution, that I might get out, perhaps there will be some small salvation. The phrase {\i hatzalah purta} \u8212- a little salvation \u8212- is significant and will recur.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abba Sikara devised the plan. Rabban Yochanan was to take to bed feigning illness, let students and community come to pay their respects for many days, then arrange for something decomposing to be placed nearby so that the smell of death would be reported. {\i Denach nafshach} \u8212- it would be said that he had died. His students \u8212- who would be in on the scheme \u8212- would carry him out in a coffin. No one else could be admitted, because {\i dekalil at} \u8212- a live person is lighter than a dead one, and unfamiliar visitors would notice the difference in weight.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gate Escape\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Eliezer and Rabbi Yehoshua \u8212- the two great students who would themselves become teachers of Rabbi Akiva \u8212- carried the coffin out of the city. At the gate, the zealot guards wanted to pierce the body to verify death. Abba Sikara deflected them: {\i yomru rabanan dakru} \u8212- do you want it said that you stabbed your own Rabbi? They then wanted at least to push the coffin around. {\i Yomru rabanan dachfu} \u8212- do you want it said that you shoved your Rabbi around even in death? The gate was opened. Rabban Yochanan ben Zakkai emerged.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Vespasian Meeting\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 When he reached Vespasian\u8217's camp, Rabban Yochanan shed his disguise and greeted the general: {\i shlama aleichah malka, shlama aleichah malka} \u8212- peace to you, O king.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Honey & Serpent\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Identity Challenge\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Vespasian responded sharply: {\i mechayeivat trei katla} \u8212- you deserve two deaths. First, {\i delav malka ana} \u8212- I am not king, and you are mocking me by calling me one. Second, {\i ei malka ana, ad idna amai lo aseit legabai} \u8212- if I were king, why did you wait until now to come and pay your respects?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Scripture Proof\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabban Yochanan responded to both challenges. As for the first: you are indeed king, {\i de ee lav malka at, Yerushalayim lo mitnasyah be-yadach} \u8212- because only a king could receive Jerusalem into his hands. The proof is from scripture: {\i dechtiv vehalevanon beadier yipol} \u8212- the {\i Levanon} will fall to the {\i adier}, the mighty one. {\i Veein adier ela melech} \u8212- {\i adier} means king, as proven from the verse {\i vehaya adiero mimenu, umeshlo mikirbo yeitzei} \u8212- his ruler will come from within him. {\i Veein Levanon ela Beis HaMikdash} \u8212- and {\i Levanon} is the {\i Beis HaMikdash}, as proven from {\i hahar hatov hazeh vehalevanon} \u8212- the good mountain and the {\i Levanon}. Since Jerusalem and the {\i Beis HaMikdash} are being delivered into his hands, Vespasian must be king.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 As for the second challenge: {\i biryonei deis behan lo shavkin} \u8212- the zealots would not permit me to come. They were the reason.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Parable\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Vespasian pressed back: {\i ilu chavis shel devash vedarkon karuch aleha, lo hayu shovrin es hachavis bishvil hadarkon} \u8212- if there is a barrel of honey and a serpent is coiled around it, would you not break the barrel open to get rid of the serpent? Here too: you should have broken the walls of Jerusalem, we would have come in, dealt with the zealots, and ended the whole matter far sooner.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Response Failure\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Ishtik} \u8212- Rabban Yochanan was silent. He had no answer. Rav Yosef, and some say Rabbi Akiva, applied the verse {\i meishiv chachamim achor vedaatam yesakel} \u8212- God sometimes causes the wise to retreat and their wisdom to become folly. The proper response, they said, should have been: {\i shaklinan tzavta veshaklinan lei ledarkon vekatlinan lei vachavita shavkinan lah} \u8212- we would take tongs, remove the serpent, kill it, and preserve the barrel. Here too: the goal was to find a way to deal with the zealots without destroying Jerusalem.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Caesar & Shoe\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Vespasian Crowned\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 While they were still in conversation, {\i asei pristaka miRomi} \u8212- a messenger arrived from Rome. {\i Kum, demeis lei keisar} \u8212- arise, the Caesar has died, {\i veamri hanu chashivei deRomi leoshivach bereisha} \u8212- and the senate of Rome has appointed you as the new Caesar.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Swollen Feet\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 At that moment Vespasian was putting on his shoe. He went to put on the second shoe \u8212- it would not go on. He tried to remove the first \u8212- it would not come off. {\i Amar mai hai} \u8212- what is happening?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabban Yochanan explained: {\i shemuah tovah asya lach, dechtiv shemuah tovah tedashen atzem} \u8212- good news has come to you, and as the verse says, good news fattens the bone. The sudden swelling of pride and joy has caused his feet to expand so that neither shoe fits.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Pride Deflation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The solution: {\i leisi inish dela misava daatach mineih vechalif kamach} \u8212- bring someone whose presence is distressing to you, let him walk before you, {\i dechtiv veruach necheiah tyavesh garem} \u8212- and a downtrodden spirit dries up the bones. He did so, and his feet returned to normal.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Request for Yavne\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Why Not Sooner\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Vespasian then turned to Rabban Yochanan: {\i umiaechar dechachmitu kulei hai, ad idna amai lo aseitu legabai} \u8212- since you are so wise, why did you not come to me sooner? Surely you could have found a way.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Zealot Barrier\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabban Yochanan repeated the answer: the zealots would not allow it. Vespasian\u8217's implied counter \u8212- that Rabban Yochanan should have found a means to neutralize them \u8212- reflects the same argument he had made with the serpent parable.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Three Grants\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Vespasian then told him: {\i meizal azilna}, I must return to Rome, {\i ve-inash achrina meshadrana}, and I will send another commander to complete the campaign. {\i Ela bei minai midi de-aten lach} \u8212- but ask of me something and I will grant it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabban Yochanan\u8217's request: {\i ten li Yavne ve-chachameiha} \u8212- give me Yavne and its sages. And {\i shishulta de-Rabbi Gamliel} \u8212- preserve the chain of Rabban Gamliel\u8217's family, meaning the {\i nasi} line. This is the great pivot of the passage and, in a sense, of Jewish history: the recognition that the {\i Beis HaMikdash} will fall, and that what must be preserved in its place is a Torah center and the continuity of Jewish communal leadership. The phrase {\i hatzalah purta} \u8212- the little salvation \u8212- echoes back. Rabban Yochanan will not save Jerusalem. But he will save something. The shift from a Temple-centered Jerusalem to a rabbinic Judaism centered on Torah study begins here, at Yavne.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Doctors Request\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The third request is of a different register entirely: {\i ve-asvata de-masyan lei le-Rabbi Tzadok} \u8212- provide doctors who will heal Rabbi Tzadok. After the grand national requests \u8212- a Torah center, the continuity of leadership \u8212- Rabban Yochanan asks for the care of a single righteous individual. Rabbi Tzadok, who had fasted for forty years to prevent the destruction, now needs to be healed so he can live into whatever comes next. There is something deeply moving in placing this personal request alongside the national ones \u8212- an insistence that even amid catastrophe, the individual {\i tzaddik} matters and must be carried into the future.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Akiva\u8217's Critique\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Wisdom Diminished\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 At this point the {\i Gemara} returns with a critique. Rav Yosef \u8212- and some say Rabbi Akiva \u8212- applied to Rabban Yochanan the verse {\i meishiv chachamim achor vedaatam yesakel}: God caused the wise to retreat, their wisdom diminished.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Better Answer\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What should Rabban Yochanan have asked for? {\i Ibei lei le-meimar lei shavkinehu hada zimna} \u8212- he should have asked Vespasian simply to leave Jerusalem alone entirely. If you will grant me something, leave. Do not destroy it at all.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yochanan\u8217's Fear\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Why did he not ask this? {\i Dilma kulei hai lo avid, ve-hatzalah porta nami lo havei} \u8212- he was afraid that Vespasian would refuse even that, and then he would have gotten nothing at all. Not even Yavne. Not even the little salvation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The voice of Rabbi Akiva here is characteristic. Rabbi Akiva, who would later support Bar Kochba and envision the full restoration of Jewish sovereignty, could not accept the compromise. He wanted to hold out for everything. Rabban Yochanan, with clearer eyes about what was politically achievable, chose to secure what he could. Neither position is without cost.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Tzadok\u8217's Healing\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gradual Nutrition\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} then describes in clinical detail how the doctors went about healing Rabbi Tzadok. {\i Yoma kama ashkyuha maya defari} \u8212- on the first day they gave him water in which bran had been soaked: mostly water, a trace of nourishment, nothing more than his devastated body could absorb. The next day: {\i maya desipuka} \u8212- water with bran and a bit of flour. The day after: {\i maya de-kimcha} \u8212- water with flour. Each day the diet was slightly more nutritious than the day before.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Stomach Expansion\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Ad deravach me\u8217'eih purta purta} \u8212- until his stomach expanded little by little. The word {\i purta} \u8212- a little, a small amount \u8212- is the same word used earlier for {\i hatzalah purta}, the little salvation. The gradual physical recovery of Rabbi Tzadok mirrors in miniature the gradual spiritual and communal recovery that the founding of Yavne represents. Just as Rabban Yochanan chose to accept a partial salvation rather than lose everything, the doctors choose to introduce nourishment gradually rather than overwhelm the body. The {\i Churban} has happened; the rebuilding must be slow and careful, one small step at a time.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Titus Defilement\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Holy of Holies Act\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 With the transition to what follows, the {\i Gemara} completely skips over the military details of the siege\u8217's end and the burning of Jerusalem, moving directly to the figure of Titus. The verse {\i ei eloheimo tzur chasayu vo} \u8212- where is their God, the rock in whom they take refuge \u8212- is identified as referring to {\i Titus HaRasha}, the wicked Titus, who blasphemed toward Heaven.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tafas zonah beyado ve-nichnas le-veit kodshei kodashim} \u8212- he took a prostitute in his hand and entered the Holy of Holies. He spread out a {\i sefer Torah} and committed his act of desecration upon it. The specific detail of the {\i sefer Torah} is significant: not content with the physical destruction of the Temple, Titus enacts a deliberate symbolic desecration of the Torah itself.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Parochet Pierce\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 He then took a sword \u8212- {\i ve-gider et ha-parochet} \u8212- and pierced the curtain, the {\i parochet} separating the Holy of Holies from the {\i heichal}. A miracle occurred, though not a saving one: {\i ve-hayah dam mivatzbetz ve-yotzei} \u8212- blood spurted from the curtain. According to Tosafot, this was to demonstrate how great and severe the destruction of the {\i Beis HaMikdash} was \u8212- or alternatively, it was intended to mislead Titus.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 God\u8217's Silence\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Kasavar harag et atzmo} \u8212- Titus thought, through the euphemism of {\i atzmo}, that he had killed God Himself. He saw this as confirmation, citing the verse {\i sha\u8217'agu tzorecha bekerev mo\u8217'adecha} \u8212- your enemies have cried out within your sanctuary. Abba Chanan responded: {\i mi kamocha chasin yah, mi kamocha chasin ve-kasheh} \u8212- who is like You in Your strength, who is like You in Your harshness and restraint, {\i she-attah shome\u8217'a ni\u8217'utzo ve-giduffo shel oto rasha ve-shotek} \u8212- that You can hear the blasphemies of that wicked man and be silent?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Rabbis reframe God\u8217's silence as an expression of incomprehensible might \u8212- not weakness, but a restraint so great it staggers comprehension. The school of Rabbi Yishmael taught: {\i mi kamocha ba\u8217'eilim Hashem} \u8212- who is like You among the mighty \u8212- {\i mi kamocha ba\u8217'ilmim} \u8212- who is like You among the silent ones?\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Titus Plunder\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Vessel Plunder\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Ve-asao kemin gargutni} \u8212- Titus took the {\i parochet} and made it into a sack, then filled it with all the vessels of the {\i Beis HaMikdash} \u8212- {\i kol keilim shebamikdash} \u8212- and loaded them onto a ship, intending to return to Rome and display his triumph.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} applies a verse from Kohelet: {\i ubchen raiti reshaim kburim} \u8212- I have seen the wicked buried. The {\i Gemara} re-reads this: {\i al tikrei kburim, ela kbutzim} \u8212- do not read \u8220"buried\u8221" but \u8220"gathered,\u8221" meaning the wicked gathered together. {\i Al tikrei veyishtakchu, ela veyishtabchu} \u8212- do not read \u8220"and they were forgotten\u8221" but \u8220"and they praised themselves\u8221" in their cities, meaning Titus parading the Temple vessels through Rome. An alternative reading accepts {\i kburim} as meaning \u8220"buried\u8221" literally: {\i de\u8217'afilu mili demitamran iglei lehu} \u8212- even the buried treasures were revealed to Titus.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Wave Challenge\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Out at sea, a massive wave rose up to destroy him. Titus interpreted this theologically: {\i kmidumeh ani she\u8217'eloheihem shel elu ein gvurato ela bamayim} \u8212- it seems that the God of the Jews only exercises His power in water. He listed the precedents: Pharaoh was drowned, Sisera\u8217's forces were swept away by the waters of the Kishon \u8212- {\i nachal Kishon grefam} \u8212- so of course God tries to fight him in the sea.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gnat Plague\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Titus then issued his challenge: {\i im gibor hu ya\u8217'ale layabasha veya\u8217'aseh imi milchama} \u8212- if God is truly mighty, let Him come up on dry land and fight me there. {\i Yatzta bat kol} \u8212- a heavenly voice issued forth: {\i lo larasha ben rasha ben bno shel Esav harasha} \u8212- wicked one, son of a wicked one, grandson of the wicked Esau \u8212- the connection to Edom and Rome is explicit. {\i Birya kala yesh li be\u8217'olami veyitush shma} \u8212- I have a tiny creature in My world called a gnat. {\i Ala layabasha veta\u8217'aseh ima milchama} \u8212- go up on land and fight it.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Gnat Torment\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gnat Entry\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 When Titus came ashore, the gnat entered his nose \u8212- {\i venichnas bechotmo} \u8212- and began boring into his brain \u8212- {\i venakar bemocho} \u8212- and this continued for seven years.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Blacksmith Relief\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One day Titus happened to pass the doorway of a blacksmith\u8217's forge. He heard the sound of hammering \u8212- {\i shama kol arzafta} \u8212- and the gnat, startled by the harsh clanging, stopped its drilling. {\i Amar ika takanta} \u8212- here, finally, was some relief.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Daily Visits\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Every day thereafter they would bring a blacksmith and have him bang before Titus. {\i Lenochri yahiv leih arba zuzei} \u8212- if the blacksmith was a non-Jew, Titus would pay him four {\i zuz}. {\i LeYisrael amar lei} \u8212- if the blacksmith was a Jew, Titus would say: {\i mistayach dekachazit besanach} \u8212- it is enough for you that you have seen your enemy in torment. No payment necessary.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Immunity Develops\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 For thirty days this provided relief. Then, {\i keivan dedash dash} \u8212- once the gnat had grown accustomed to the sound, it developed an immunity, and the hammering no longer startled it. The relief ceased.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Titus\u8217's Death\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gnat Growth\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Pinchas ben Aruvah testifies: I was among the prominent people of Rome, and {\i ukshemet patzu et mocho umatzu bo} \u8212- when Titus died and they cracked open his skull, they found that the gnat had grown. According to one tradition it had reached the size of {\i ketzippor dror}, a free bird, {\i mishkalo shnei sela\u8217'im} \u8212- weighing two {\i sela\u8217'im}. A {\i Baraita} describes it as the size of a year-old pigeon, weighing two {\i litrin}. Abaye adds a received tradition: {\i piv shel nechoshet} \u8212- its mouth was of brass, {\i vetzipornav shel barzel} \u8212- and its claws of iron. The creature that had been growing inside Titus for seven years \u8212- fed, in a sense, by the same iron and brass of the blacksmith\u8217's forge that briefly gave him respite \u8212- had become a metallic instrument of divine retribution.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Cremation Order\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 On his deathbed, Titus issued a final command: {\i liklyeh l\u8217'hahu gavra} \u8212- cremate me. {\i U\u8217'nivdrei l\u8217'kitmei ashivah yamei} \u8212- scatter my ashes across the seven seas.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Afterlife Fear\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The reasoning was explicit: {\i d\u8217'lo lishkachayeh eloha d\u8217'yehudai v\u8217'lyokmayeh b\u8217'dina} \u8212- so that the God of the Jews should not find me and bring me to judgment in the afterlife. Titus, who had blasphemed and desecrated with apparent impunity in this world, feared the accounting that awaited him in the next. His entire strategy of cremation and dispersal was an attempt to evade divine justice \u8212- a final act of defiance that itself testifies to his acknowledgment that such justice exists. The {\i Gemara} will continue this account on the following {\i daf}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Gittin 57\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Titus & Onkelos\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Churban Intro\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Today\u8217's {\i daf} is 57, and we continue in the {\i aggadeta} \u8212- some of the most essential {\i aggadeta} about the {\i Churban ha-Bayit}. We pick up at the bottom of 56b, having just dealt with the downfall of Titus and the gnat that found its way into his nostril. One must acknowledge something almost darkly comic in this material: Vespasian\u8217's foot swells up, and Titus is undone by an insect in his nose. Even though Vespasian is something of a relatively sympathetic figure in these narratives, the theme running through all of this is very much the bringing low of the mighty \u8212- a note of mockery directed at the great powers of Rome.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Titus & Gnat\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 By way of background: Titus, having destroyed the {\i Beis ha-Mikdash}, asked that after his death God would not be able to judge him, and requested that his ashes be scattered over the seven seas so that he could not be gathered for judgment.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Onkelos Raises Titus\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Picking up four lines from the bottom of 56b, the {\i Gemara} introduces {\i Onkelos bar Kalonikos bar achatei de-Titus} \u8212- Onkelos, the son of the sister of Titus. He {\i ba\u8217'ei le-giurei}, he wished to convert, and indeed the tradition identifies him as {\i Onkelos ha-Ger}, the famous convert who produced the authoritative Aramaic translation of the Torah.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Azal askei le-Titus be-negida} \u8212- he went and raised Titus from the dead through a form of necromancy. He asked him: {\i man chashiv be-hahu alma} \u8212- who is important in the world to come? Titus replied: {\i Yisrael} \u8212- the Jewish people. Onkelos then asked: {\i ma lei devukei behu} \u8212- should I therefore cleave to them in this world? Titus replied: {\i milaihu nefishan velo matis le-kiyumaihu} \u8212- their commandments are many and you will not be able to fulfill them all. Rather: {\i zil igrei behu be-hahu alma} \u8212- go and attack them in this world, {\i ve-havis reisha} \u8212- and you will be a head, you will be a leader. As the verse says, {\i hayu tzareha le-rosh} \u8212- its enemies became the head. The implication, taken at face value, is almost troubling: afflict Israel and you will become a leader. The commentators struggle to reread this, suggesting it means that only one who is already a great leader will manage to oppress Israel \u8212- but that is admittedly not the plain {\i peshat} of the {\i Gemara} as it stands.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Titus\u8217's Punishment\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Onkelos then asked Titus: {\i dinei de-hahu gavra be-mai} \u8212- what is the judgment of that man, meaning yourself, in the world to come? Titus answered: {\i be-mai de-pasak anafshei} \u8212- whatever I decreed upon myself. {\i Kol yoma machneshi lei le-katmei} \u8212- every day my ashes are gathered from the seven seas, {\i ve-dayni lei} \u8212- I am re-judged, {\i ve-kalu lei} \u8212- I am burned again, {\i ve-mabdru a-shva yamei} \u8212- and my ashes are scattered once more over the seven seas. This cycle repeats daily. His counsel to Onkelos, then, is: you cannot win in the world to come, but you might as well be successful in this world by oppressing Israel.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Onkelos & Balaam\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Balaam Summoned\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Azal askei le-Bilam be-negida} \u8212- Onkelos then went and raised Balaam from the dead through necromancy. He asked the same question: {\i man chashiv be-hahu alma} \u8212- who is important in the world to come? Balaam too answered: {\i Yisrael}. And again the follow-up: {\i ma lei devukei behu} \u8212- should I cleave to them?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Balaam\u8217's Hatred\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Balaam\u8217's answer was the opposite of what one might hope: {\i lo tidrosh shelomam ve-tovatam kol ha-yamim} \u8212- do not seek after their well-being or their good, ever. The counsel is straightforward hatred toward Israel in this world.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Balaam\u8217's Torment\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Onkelos asked Balaam as well: {\i dinei de-hahu gavra be-mai} \u8212- what is your punishment? {\i Be-shichvat zera rotachat} \u8212- in boiling semen. Two explanations are offered for this: one, that Balaam caused {\i Bnei Yisrael} to sin with {\i Benot Moav}, and the other, that it reflects his own personal licentiousness.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Yeshu Raised\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yeshu Summoned\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Azal askei be-negida le-poshei Yisrael} \u8212- the text as we have it reads \u8220"sinners of Israel,\u8221" but the original {\i girsa} is {\i Yeshu ha-Notzri} \u8212- Jesus. Onkelos raised him through necromancy and asked the same question: {\i man chashiv be-hahu alma} \u8212- who is important in the world to come? The answer was again: {\i Yisrael}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yeshu\u8217's Advice\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Here, strikingly, the answer diverges from what both Titus and Balaam had said. Onkelos asked: {\i ma leidvuki behu} \u8212- should I cleave to them? And the reply was: {\i tovatam drosh ra\u8217'atam lo tidrosh} \u8212- seek their well-being and do not seek their harm. This does not necessarily mean one should convert, but the counsel is to be good to them in this world. {\i Kol hanogea bahen kenogea be-vavat eino} \u8212- anyone who harms them is as one who pokes at the pupil of his own eye.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yeshu\u8217's Torment\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Onkelos asked about his punishment as well: {\i dinei de-hahu gavra be-mai} \u8212- what are you judged with? {\i Be-tzoa rotachat} \u8212- in boiling excrement. {\i De-amar mar kol ha-mal\u8217'ig al divrei chachamim nidon be-tzoa rotachat} \u8212- for anyone who mocks the words of the sages is judged in boiling excrement. It is notable that the sin attributed here is specifically mocking the sages \u8212- framing the figure as a Jewish heretic rather than as an external oppressor like Titus or Balaam.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Heretic Contrast\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} then draws the lesson explicitly: {\i ta chazi ma bein poshei Yisrael le-niviei umot ha\u8217'olam} \u8212- come and see the difference between sinners from Israel and the prophets of the nations who are idolaters. Even in the world to come, under harsh divine judgment, the idolaters \u8212- Titus and Balaam \u8212- still advise enmity toward Israel, while the Jewish heretic, even in his torment, says to seek Israel\u8217's well-being.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Power of Shame\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shame\u8217's Power\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tanya}, it was taught in a {\i baraita}: {\i amar Rabbi Elazar, bo u\u8217're\u8217'eh kama gadol kocha shel busha} \u8212- come and see how great is the power of shame. This serves as a concluding reflection on the story of {\i Kamtza} and {\i Bar Kamtza}, which was covered previously, and which the {\i Gemara} now wraps up thematically.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kamtza Story\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i She-harei siya HaKadosh Baruch Hu et Bar Kamtza} \u8212- God assisted {\i Bar Kamtza}, meaning He guided events through this story, {\i ve-hachiv et beito ve-saraf et heichalo} \u8212- and ultimately it led to the destruction of the {\i Beis ha-Mikdash} and the burning of the {\i Heichal}. This is worth pausing over. Earlier in the {\i Gemara}, the destruction was attributed to {\i Rabbi Zecharya ben Avkulas} and his excessive humility: {\i hechriv et bateinu ve-saraf et heichaleinu} \u8212- he destroyed our homes and burned our sanctuary. Here, God Himself is identified as the agent who destroyed His own house. And yet the {\i Gemara} also frames it as coming through {\i Bar Kamtza}\u8217's shame. Who, then, bears the responsibility?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Causes of Destruction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 These layered attributions \u8212- the excessive humility of leadership, the shame of {\i Bar Kamtza}, and ultimately God\u8217's own guidance of events \u8212- all coexist in the {\i Gemara} without contradiction, and each illuminates a different dimension of the {\i churban}. There is also a case to be made that the entire story of {\i Kamtza} and {\i Bar Kamtza} is not merely a tale about the sin of shaming, however real and serious that sin is, nor only about {\i sinat chinam} in a narrow interpersonal sense. It reflects something more politically concrete: the factionalism and internal warfare of the period. The Zealots were spending their energy fighting one another. There was no compelling reason that the Jews had to rebel against Rome when they did, nor that they could not have negotiated a way to preserve the {\i Beis ha-Mikdash}. The destruction was in large measure brought about by internal strife and a failure of leadership \u8212- precisely the {\i Gemara}\u8217's broader point about the rabbis of that generation who saw what was happening and did not act.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Tur Malka Chickens\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Wedding Tree Custom\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now shifts to the story of {\i Tur Malka}, another city that was destroyed. {\i Aturnegoila ve-tarnegoilta chariv Tur Malka} \u8212- on account of a hen and a rooster, the city of {\i Tur Malka} was destroyed. The practice there was: {\i ki havu mapki chatan ve-challa}, when a wedding procession would go out, {\i mapki kameihu tarnegoila ve-tarnegoilta} \u8212- they would lead a rooster and a hen in front of the couple. {\i Kolomar pru u\u8217'revu ka-tarnegolim} \u8212- this symbolized the wish that they be as fruitful as hens and roosters.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Caesar\u8217's Carriage\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Yoma chad hava ka chalif gunda de-Romai} \u8212- one day a troop of Romans was passing through. There happened to be a wedding procession at the time, and the hungry soldiers {\i shaklineihu mineihu} \u8212- grabbed the chickens.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Jews Fight Back\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Naflu alaihu} \u8212- the people attacked the soldiers, demanding their chickens back. {\i Machunu} \u8212- they struck them. {\i Azlu amru lei le-Keisar} \u8212- the Romans went and reported to Caesar: {\i mardu becha Yehuda\u8217'ei} \u8212- the Jews are rebelling against you. {\i Ata alaihu} \u8212- and Caesar came with an army against them.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Bar Droma Hero\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Havah behu hahu Bar Droma} \u8212- there was in this community a man called {\i Bar Droma}, {\i de-havah kafitz mila} \u8212- who could leap a {\i mila}, and {\i ve-katlu behu} \u8212- he would cut down the Romans as he vaulted through them. The imagery is almost of a superhero, and the parallel to the stories that may have inspired the Asterix comic series \u8212- a tiny village resisting Rome through what amounts to superhuman ability \u8212- is not difficult to see. Just as Asterix is a story of a small enclave that held out against Rome, Bar Kochba\u8217's revolt is clearly the historical event that gave rise to that imagination.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Caesar Prays\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Shaklei Keisar le-tageih ve-asfeh a-ara} \u8212- Caesar took his crown and placed it on the ground. He prayed: {\i Ribonei de-alma}, Master of the world, {\i im nicha lach lo timsereih le-hahu gavra le-didei u-malchuteih be-yadei de-chad gavra} \u8212- if it please You, do not hand me and my entire kingdom over to one man. His prayer was heard: {\i achshaleih pumeih le-Bar Droma} \u8212- Bar Droma\u8217's mouth caused him to stumble. Bar Droma said: {\i halo atah Elohim zenachtanu ve-lo tetzei Elohim be-tzivotenu} \u8212- Have You not, O God, abandoned us, and You do not go out with our armies? This is a complaint against God rather than an expression of wonder.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} immediately raises an objection: this is a verse from {\i Tehillim} \u8212- David also said it! The {\i Gemara} responds that {\i David itmuhei ka-matmei} \u8212- David was expressing astonishment, as if to say, \u8220"Could it be that You have abandoned us?\u8221" It was a cry of disbelief. Bar Droma, by contrast, was leveling a charge: God, You abandoned us. And this distinction proved fatal.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Bathroom Death\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Ala be-veit ha-kisei} \u8212- Bar Droma went to the bathroom. {\i Ata darkona} \u8212- a serpent appeared \u8212- and {\i shamteih le-karkashei} \u8212- his innards dropped out, {\i ve-nach nafshei} \u8212- and he died. The Superman of the Jewish resistance died in the bathroom. As with Titus\u8217's gnat and Vespasian\u8217's swollen foot, the manner of death is almost grimly comic.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Caesar Returns\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Ho-il ve-itrachash li nisa} \u8212- Caesar acknowledged that a miracle had occurred for him. He declared: {\i ha zimna ishvakinhu} \u8212- this time I will leave them. {\i Ve-azal izdakar} \u8212- and when Caesar departed, the Jews rose up in celebration, {\i ve-achalu ve-shatu ve-adliku shragei} \u8212- eating, drinking, and lighting candles. So great was the illumination that {\i ad de-itchazi dilyona de-gushpanka be-richuk mila} \u8212- the impression of a signet ring could be made out from a distance of a {\i mila}. Caesar, observing this celebration as he withdrew, interpreted it as mockery: {\i michdi chadu be-Yehuda\u8217'ei} \u8212- the Jews are laughing at me. {\i Hadar ata alei} \u8212- and he turned back and attacked.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tur Malka Slaughter\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Amar Rav Assi: tlat me\u8217'a alfi shlifei saifa ailu le-Tur Malka} \u8212- 300,000 men with drawn swords entered {\i Tur Malka}, {\i ve-katlu bah tlata yoma ve-tlata leilavata} \u8212- and they killed there for three days and three nights.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 City Divided\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i U\u8217've-hach gisa hilulei ve-chingei} \u8212- on one side of the city the slaughter was underway, while on the other side, celebrations and festivities continued. {\i Ve-lo hava yada hanei be-hanei} \u8212- the one half of the city did not know what was happening in the other. This detail is haunting in its own right. It may strain the imagination as to how such a thing is possible logistically, but its truth as a historical phenomenon \u8212- that in times of catastrophe communities in close proximity can remain entirely unaware of one another\u8217's fate \u8212- is well documented. One thinks of Jewish communities in Eastern Europe during the {\i Shoah}, where Jews in one country had no knowledge of the mass murder already underway in neighboring lands.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The deeper point of these stories about {\i Tur Malka} is often overlooked, because on {\i Tisha B\u8217'Av} people tend to stop the reading of this material before reaching it, since it no longer concerns the {\i Churban ha-Bayit} directly. But the {\i Gemara} is making precisely this point: the destruction was not only of the {\i Beis ha-Mikdash}. It was the destruction of a civilization, of cities, of an entire population and way of life. The rooster and hen, the wedding procession \u8212- these are prosaic details of traditional life, precisely the kind that are swept away in catastrophe. The Jews of {\i Tur Malka} could not adapt even slightly to the Roman presence, could not absorb even the minor disruption of the requisitioned chickens. And ultimately everything was destroyed.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Yanai\u8217's Villages\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Jacob\u8217's Beauty\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i U\u8217've-hach gisa hilulei ve-chingei} \u8212- having described the slaughter of {\i Tur Malka}, the {\i Gemara} now quotes the verse: {\i bala Hashem ve-lo chamal et kol ne\u8217'ot Yaakov} \u8212- God swallowed up and had no mercy on all the dwellings of Jacob. This verse from {\i Eicha} frames what follows.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 600K Villages\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Ki ata Ravin amar Rabbi Yochanan: ailu shitin ribo ayarot she-hayu le-Yanai ha-melech be-Har ha-Melech} \u8212- these are the 600,000 villages that the Hasmonean king Yanai had on {\i Har ha-Melech}. {\i Ve-chol achat ve-achat hayu bah ke-yotzei Mitzrayim} \u8212- and each one of them had as many inhabitants as those who went out of Egypt, which is to say 600,000 males of fighting age, and by extension a total population of perhaps two million per village. The arithmetic, at 600,000 villages each with something like two million people, produces numbers that are frankly staggering.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Three Villages\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Chutz mi-shalosh she-hayu bahen kiflayim ke-yotzei Mitzrayim} \u8212- with the exception of three villages that had double that number. These three were {\i Kfar Bish}, {\i Kfar Shichlayim}, and {\i Kfar Dichraya}. Their names are expressive. {\i Kfar Bish} \u8212- \u8220"the bad village\u8221" \u8212- {\i dela yahavi beis ushpiza}, because they did not provide lodging to wayfarers, in the manner of Sodom. {\i Kfar Shichlayim} \u8212- named for {\i shichlayim}, a type of watercress, because the residents made their living from that vegetable. {\i Kfar Dichraya} \u8212- \u8220"the village of males\u8221" \u8212- because, as Rabbi Yochanan explains, {\i she-hayu nesheihem yoldos zacharim techilla ve-yoldos nekeiva ba-achrona ve-poskos} \u8212- the women would first bear sons, then daughters, and then stop. The precise critique embedded in this last name is somewhat unclear.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ulla Challenges\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Amar Ulla: ledidi chazi li hahu atra} \u8212- Ulla said, I myself saw that place you are describing. {\i Ve-afilu shitin ribo kanei lo machzik} \u8212- it cannot even contain 600,000 reeds, let alone 600,000 villages. Ulla is directly calling out the exaggeration. In the {\i Gemara}\u8217's version, a {\i min} \u8212- a heretic, though the parallel to {\i Tzadoki} in some texts suggests the original {\i girsa} was indeed {\i min} \u8212- challenges Rabbi Chanina: {\i shiku meshakritsu} \u8212- you people are lying.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Land Expands\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Chanina responds: {\i Eretz Tzvi kesiv} \u8212- the land of Israel is described in Scripture as the land of the deer. {\i Mah tzvi zeh ein oro machzik et besaro} \u8212- just as a deer\u8217's hide, once removed, cannot be stretched back over the carcass that it once contained, because it shrinks outside the body, {\i af Eretz Yisrael, bi-zman she-yoshveha aleha ravcha} \u8212- so too the land of Israel, when its people dwell upon it, it expands. {\i U-vi-zman she-ein yoshveha aleha gamda} \u8212- and when its people are gone from it, it contracts. The physical smallness of the land today in no way contradicts the tradition that at its height it contained all those villages, because the land itself is of a different nature when inhabited by its people.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Kfar Sekaniah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Three Rabbis Query\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rav Minyumi bar Chilkiya ve-Rav Chilkiya bar Tuvya ve-Rav Huna bar Chiya havu yasvei gabei hadadi} \u8212- three rabbis were sitting together and one of them said: {\i ika de-shamei lei milsa mi-Kfar Sekanya shel Mitzrayim leima} \u8212- if anyone has heard something about this place, {\i Kfar Sekanya} of Egypt, let him speak. A note on the geography: Soncino suggests that the original text may have read {\i Kfar Sekanya shel Notzrim} rather than {\i Mitzrayim} \u8212- that is, Nazareth \u8212- and that this was a place that later became associated with early Jewish-Christian sectarianism. Steinsaltz notes a city in the Galilee called {\i Sekanya}. Whatever the precise location, the tradition being invoked is of a place that had once been a model of piety and righteousness.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Betrothed Couple\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Patach chad mineihu ve-amar: maaseh be-arus ve-arusa} \u8212- one of the three opened and said: there was a story involving a betrothed man and woman \u8212- meaning they had completed {\i kiddushin} but had not yet had {\i chuppah} and were not yet living together as husband and wife. {\i She-nishbu le-vein ha-ovdei kochavim} \u8212- they were taken captive by idolaters. {\i Ve-hisi\u8217'um zeh la-zeh} \u8212- and the captors, apparently not realizing the two were already married, put them together for the purpose of having them produce children as slaves.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might think: they are already married, so what is the problem? The issue is that they had only {\i kiddushin} without {\i nisuin}, and one may not live as husband and wife without a {\i kesuva}. {\i Ve-amra lo: bevakasha mimcha al tiga bi, she-ein li kesuva mimcha} \u8212- she said to him: please do not touch me, for I have no {\i kesuva} from you. {\i Ve-lo naga bah ad yom moso} \u8212- and he did not touch her until the day he died.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 When he died, she said to their captors: {\i sifdu la-zeh she-kipah et yitzro, ve-yoter mi-Yosef} \u8212- eulogize this man who subdued his desires, and he was greater than Joseph. {\i De-havah Yosef lo hava ela chada sha\u8217'ata} \u8212- Joseph resisted for only one moment. {\i Ve-da kol yoma ve-yoma} \u8212- this man, every single day. {\i U\u8217've-Yosef lav be-chada mita havu} \u8212- Joseph and Potiphar\u8217's wife were not sharing a bed. {\i Ve-ha be-chada mita} \u8212- but this couple were sharing a bed. {\i U\u8217've-Yosef lav ishto} \u8212- and Potiphar\u8217's wife was not Joseph\u8217's wife, {\i ve-ha ishto} \u8212- while this woman was his own wife. He resisted every day what Yosef resisted for a single moment, under far more intimate circumstances, and from his own lawful spouse.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Grain Price Story\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Patach idach ve-amar: maaseh ve-amdu arbaim modiyot be-dinar} \u8212- the second rabbi opened and told: there was a time in this town when forty measures of grain could be bought for a dinar \u8212- grain was plentiful and cheap, a sign of prosperity. Then {\i nechsar ha-sha\u8217'ar} \u8212- the rate dropped, so that now only thirty-nine measures could be bought for a dinar. They investigated the cause. {\i U-vadeku u-matzu av u-veno she-vau al naara meurasah be-yom ha-kippurim} \u8212- and they found a father and son who had committed adultery with a betrothed young woman on Yom Kippur. The gravity of this sin is compounded at every level: the forbidden relationship, the sacred day, the violation of a betrothed woman, and the fact that it was father and son together. {\i Ve-heviyu le-veit din ve-sakalu} \u8212- they brought them to {\i beit din} and stoned them. {\i Ve-chazar ha-sha\u8217'ar li-mkomo} \u8212- and the grain price returned to its prior level. So thorough was their communal responsibility that even a single transgression had a measurable effect on the prosperity of the entire town \u8212- and so thorough was their justice that when the sin was addressed, the effect was reversed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 False Accusation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Patach idach ve-amar: maaseh be-adam echad she-natan einav be-ishto le-garesha} \u8212- the third rabbi told: a man wished to divorce his wife. {\i Ve-hayetah ketubatah merubah} \u8212- but her {\i kesuva} was very large, and he sought a way to avoid paying it. He arranged to manufacture evidence against her. {\i Halach ve-ziman et shoshvinav} \u8212- he invited his friends. {\i Ve-he\u8217'echilan ve-hishkan} \u8212- he fed them and plied them with drink until they were drunk. {\i Ve-hishkivan al mita achat} \u8212- he had them all lie together on one bed. {\i Ve-hevi loven beitza ve-hitipah beineihen} \u8212- he brought egg white and placed it among them, making it appear as semen. {\i Ve-he\u8217'emid lahen eidim} \u8212- and he stationed witnesses to observe. He then came before {\i beit din} claiming that these men had committed adultery with his wife. The staging of the scene recalls something almost theatrical in its calculated wickedness.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Ve-haya sham zaken echad mi-talmidei Shammai ha-Zaken, Bava ben Buta shemo} \u8212- but there was present one elder from the disciples of Shammai the Elder, his name Bava ben Buta. {\i Amar lahem: kach mekubalni mi-Shammai ha-Zaken, loven beitza soled min ha-or, ve-shichvat zera docha min ha-or} \u8212- he said: this is the tradition I received from Shammai the Elder: egg white, when exposed to heat, shrivels and contracts, while semen is absorbed into cloth when brought near fire. {\i U-vadeku u-matzu ki-dvarav} \u8212- they tested it and found exactly as he had said: it was egg white, not semen. {\i Ve-heviuhu le-veit din ve-hilkuhu} \u8212- the husband was brought before {\i beit din} and flogged. {\i Ve-higbu ketubatah mimenu} \u8212- and they compelled him to pay his wife\u8217's {\i kesuva}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The progression of the three stories is instructive. The first speaks to the personal righteousness of individuals. The second and third speak to the communal structures of justice: the {\i beit din} acting swiftly and correctly, the town taking full responsibility for the sins committed within its bounds. This stands in sharp contrast to the rabbinic failures described earlier in the tractate, where the leadership of Jerusalem saw what was unfolding and did not act.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Abaye Questions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Amar leih Abaye le-Rav Yosef: ve-achar de-havu tzaddikim kulei hai} \u8212- Abaye asked Rav Yosef: if they were so righteous, why were they punished?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 No Mourning\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Amar leih: mishum de-lo iabalu al Yerushalayim} \u8212- because they did not mourn over the destruction of Jerusalem. As the verse says: {\i simchu et Yerushalayim ve-gilu vah kol ohaveha, sisu itah masos kol ha-mitablim aleha} \u8212- rejoice with Jerusalem and exult in her, all who love her; be glad with her in gladness, all who mourned for her. Only those who share in the mourning will share in the rejoicing.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is a historically grounded observation. The {\i Churban} was felt differently depending on proximity and circumstance. How much did a community in the Galilee, far from Jerusalem, truly feel the destruction of the {\i Beis ha-Mikdash} as having altered their daily lives? One can imagine a town \u8212- and Nazareth is in the Galilee, far from Jerusalem \u8212- that was genuinely righteous in its internal conduct but that did not cultivate the larger national and communal sense of loss. Highly responsible within their own {\i arba amos}, their own village, but without the broader sense that they were part of something larger whose destruction demanded mourning. This is the critique the {\i Gemara} levels at them: personal and communal righteousness alone is not sufficient. One must also feel the loss of {\i Yerushalayim} as one\u8217's own.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Beitar Cedar Trees\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tree Planting\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now moves to the destruction of {\i Beitar}. {\i De-hava nahigi ki avei misyalid yanuka} \u8212- the practice there was: when a baby boy was born, {\i shatlei arza} \u8212- they would plant a cedar tree; {\i yanukta} \u8212- when a baby girl was born, {\i shatlei turnisa} \u8212- they would plant a pine or cypress tree. It is a beautiful {\i minhag}, rooting the lives of the children literally in the land.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Trees for Chuppah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Ve-ki aveu minsevei} \u8212- when the boy and girl grew up and married, {\i kaitzei lo} \u8212- they would cut down their respective trees {\i ve-avdu ganana} \u8212- and construct a {\i chuppah} from them. The marriage canopy was thus built from trees planted at the moment of birth, intertwining the couple\u8217's origins with the culmination of their lives together.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Caesar\u8217's Axle Breaks\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Yoma chad avei kachalfa bartei de-Keisar} \u8212- one day Caesar\u8217's daughter was passing through {\i Beitar} when {\i itbar ashaka de-rispak} \u8212- the axle of her carriage broke. {\i Katzu arza} \u8212- the soldiers cut down a nearby cedar tree to repair the carriage.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Jews Defend Tree\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Asu naflu alaihu} \u8212- the Jews of {\i Beitar} attacked the Romans for cutting down the tree. {\i Machun} \u8212- they beat them. {\i Asu amri leh le-Keisar: mardu bach Yehuda\u8217'ei} \u8212- they went and told Caesar that the Jews were rebelling. {\i Asu alaihu} \u8212- and Caesar came with his army.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Once again, the inability to absorb even the slightest disruption to their traditional practices \u8212- however beautiful those practices were \u8212- proved catastrophic. The very qualities that made the community admirable in peacetime, their rootedness in custom and land, could not accommodate the realities of Roman occupation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Beitar Destroyed\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Gada be-chori af kol keren Yisrael} \u8212- God cut down in fierce anger all the horn of Israel. {\i Amar Rav Zeira amar Rav Abba amar Rabbi Yochanan: eilu shemonim elef karnei milchamah she-nichnasu le-krach Beitar} \u8212- these are eighty thousand horns of war that entered the city of {\i Beitar} when they conquered it, {\i ve-hargu bah anashim ve-nashim ve-taf} \u8212- and they slaughtered men, women, and children.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Blood Flows\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Ad she-halach daman ve-naful le-yama ha-gadol} \u8212- until the blood flowed all the way to the Mediterranean. Lest one think the sea was nearby, the {\i Gemara} notes: {\i rechokah hayetah mil} \u8212- it was a {\i mil} away, and the blood flowed that distance. {\i Tanya, Rabbi Eliezer ha-Gadol omer: shenei nechalim yesh be-Bikat Yadayim} \u8212- there are two wadis in {\i Bikat Yadayim}, near {\i Beitar}, one flowing one direction and one the other, {\i ve-shi\u8217'aru chachamim shenei chalakim mayim ve-echad dam} \u8212- and the sages estimated that at that time these wadis ran two-thirds water and one-third blood. {\i Ve-masnista tana: sheva shanim batzru ovdei kochavim et karmeihem mi-daman shel Yisrael belo zevel} \u8212- and the {\i braita} teaches that for seven years afterward the non-Jews harvested their vineyards fertilized by the blood of Israel without any additional manure. The image is searing, and one cannot help but think of what is known about the soil of Auschwitz.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Zechariah\u8217's Blood\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Elder\u8217's Testimony\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Amar Rabbi Chiya bar Avin amar Rabbi Yehoshua ben Korcha: sach li zaken echad mei-anshei Yerushalayim} \u8212- a certain elder from the men of Jerusalem told him: {\i be-vika zu harag Nevuzaradan Rav Tabachim} \u8212- in this valley Nevuzaradan, the head of the butchers, Nebuchadnezzar\u8217's chief general, slaughtered 211 myriads \u8212- 2.1 million \u8212- and in Jerusalem itself, 940,000. {\i Al even achas, ad she-halach daman ve-naga be-damo shel Zechariah} \u8212- all of them on one stone, until their blood reached and joined the blood of Zechariah. This Zechariah was a {\i kohein} who had been murdered by order of King Yoash because he refused to stop rebuking the people in the name of God \u8212- not the {\i Zechariah ha-Navi} of the {\i Tanach} who returned from Babylonian exile and figures in the second {\i Beis ha-Mikdash} period, but an earlier figure. {\i Ve-damim be-damim nagu} \u8212- and the bloods met one another, fulfilling the verse.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Blood Bubbles\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Ashkechei le-damei de-Zechariah, de-hava kamartach ve-salik} \u8212- Nevuzaradan, upon entering the {\i Beis ha-Mikdash}, found the blood of Zechariah bubbling on the floor. {\i Amar: mai hai} \u8212- he asked, what is this? {\i Amru lei: dam zvachim de-ishtapuch} \u8212- the Jews told him it was sacrificial blood that had been spilled. {\i Ayise damma ve-lo dami} \u8212- he brought animal blood for comparison, and it did not match. He knew they were lying.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Zechariah Confession\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Amar lehu: ei amritu li mutav, ve-ei lav masrikna le-visraichu be-masreki de-farzla} \u8212- tell me the truth, or I will flay your flesh with iron combs. {\i Amru lei: navi haya banu de-hava kamocheach lan be-milei de-shmaya, kaminnu alavei ve-katlinan lei} \u8212- they confessed: there was a prophet among us who rebuked us with words of Heaven, and we rose up against him and killed him. {\i Ve-ha kamma shanin de-lo ka nayach damei} \u8212- and for many years his blood has not stopped bubbling.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sanhedrin Slaughter\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Amar lehu: ana mefaysina lei} \u8212- Nevuzaradan said he would appease the blood. {\i Ayise Sanhedrin Gedolah ve-Sanhedrin Ketana ve-katlinhu alavei} \u8212- he slaughtered the Great Sanhedrin and the Small Sanhedrin over it, {\i ve-lo nach} \u8212- and the blood did not rest. The pattern of leadership bearing responsibility is reinforced: the very institutions of Jewish governance that had failed are the first to be called to account. {\i Bachurim u-vesulos ve-katlinhu alavei} \u8212- he slaughtered young men and young women over it, and still it did not rest. {\i Ayise tinokot shel beis rabban ve-katlinhu alavei} \u8212- he brought the schoolchildren and slaughtered them over the blood, and still it did not stop boiling.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Blood Settles\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Amar lei: Zechariah, Zechariah, tovim she-bahen ibadtum} \u8212- Nevuzaradan said: Zechariah, Zechariah, I have destroyed the best among them. {\i Nicha lach de-avdinhu le-chulhu} \u8212- do you want me to wipe out all of them? {\i K\u8217'd-amar lei hachi, nach} \u8212- when he said this, the blood finally ceased to boil.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i U-ve-hahu sha\u8217'ata hirher teshuvah be-da\u8217'atei} \u8212- at that very moment, Nevuzaradan had a thought of {\i teshuvah} in his mind. He said: {\i u-mah al nefesh achas kach, hahu gavra de-katil kulei hanei nishmasa, al achas kamma ve-chamma} \u8212- if for one soul such vengeance is exacted, then I who have killed all these souls \u8212- how much more greatly will I be punished?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Nevuzaradan Converts\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Arak} \u8212- he fled. {\i Shadar shetar partisa le-veiseh} \u8212- he sent a document home to distribute his property and settle his affairs. {\i Ve-isgayar} \u8212- and he converted.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Enemies Convert\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Naaman & Nev.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tanu rabbanan: Naaman ger toshav haya} \u8212- the {\i Braita} teaches: Naaman, healed of his {\i tzara\u8217'at} by Elisha, became a {\i ger toshav} \u8212- he abandoned {\i avoda zara} but did not become a full Jew, as is evident from the plain meaning of the {\i psukim}. {\i Nevuzaradan ger tzedek havay} \u8212- but Nevuzaradan became a full convert. So the very general who destroyed the {\i Beis ha-Mikdash} in its first iteration ultimately entered the covenant of Israel.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Haman\u8217's Line\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Mi-bnei banav shel Haman lamdu Torah bi-Vnei Brak} \u8212- the grandchildren of Haman studied Torah in {\i Bnei Brak}. The descendants of the man who sought to annihilate the Jewish people are found learning Torah in one of the great centers of Jewish life.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sisra\u8217's Line\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Mi-bnei banav shel Sisra limdu tinokot bi-Yerushalayim} \u8212- the descendants of Sisra taught schoolchildren in Jerusalem.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sancheriv\u8217's Line\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Mi-bnei banav shel Sancheriv limdu Torah ba-rabbim} \u8212- and the descendants of Sancheriv taught Torah in public. {\i U-man inun? Shmaya ve-Avtalyon} \u8212- and who are these? Shmaya and Avtalyon, the great rabbinic leaders who were the teachers of Hillel and Shammai themselves. The enemies of Israel \u8212- Haman a descendant of {\i Amalek} \u8212- ultimately find their descendants not only entering the Jewish people but becoming its greatest teachers. There is a {\i teshuvah} of Rav Oshry, {\i She\u8217'eilot U-Tshuvot Mi-Ma\u8217'amakim}, written in the aftermath of the {\i Shoah}, in which the question of whether a German soldier consumed by guilt might be permitted to convert is addressed. The precedent of Nevuzaradan and the descendants of Haman are part of the broader conversation about whether even one who participated in such evil can find a path to repentance and entry into the Jewish people. The {\i Rambam} takes up related questions regarding whether {\i Amalek}, if they do {\i teshuvah}, can become converts.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Yaakov & Esav\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Zechariah Verse\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Haynu de-ksiv: nasati et damayich al tzhiach sela le-vilti hikasos} \u8212- this is the verse: I have placed your blood on the bare rock so that it cannot be covered over. The blood of Zechariah could not be absorbed, could not be hidden, lying exposed on the floor of the {\i Beis ha-Mikdash}, demanding justice.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hadrian & Vespa.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Ha-kol kol Yaakov ve-ha-yadayim yedei Esav} \u8212- the voice is the voice of Jacob and the hands are the hands of Esau. The {\i Gemara} now interprets this verse in terms of the great destructions. {\i Ha-kol ze Adryanus Keisar she-harag be-Alexandrya shel Mitzrayim shishim ribo al shishim ribo} \u8212- the voice is Hadrian, who killed in Alexandria of Egypt 600,000 times 600,000, {\i kiflayim ke-yotzei Mitzrayim} \u8212- double those who went out of Egypt. Rashi explains: {\i ha-kol tzevacha u-vechi ola mi-Yaakov} \u8212- the voice, meaning the outcry and wailing, rises from Jacob, because of the hands of Esau. {\i Kol Yaakov ze Aspasyanus Keisar} \u8212- in another reading, the voice of Jacob refers to Vespasian, {\i she-harag bi-krach Beitar arba me\u8217'os ribo} \u8212- who killed in {\i Beitar} four million, {\i ve-amri lah arba\u8217'as alafim ribo} \u8212- or according to another version, forty million. {\i Ve-ha-yadayim yedei Esav zu malchus ha-resha\u8217'a she-hachriva et beisenu ve-sarfa et heichaleinu ve-higlisenu me-artzenu} \u8212- the hands of Esau, this is the evil kingdom of Rome, which destroyed our home, burned our sanctuary, and exiled us from our land.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Prayer & War\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Davar acher: kol kol Yaakov, ein lecha tefilla she-mo\u8217'elet she-ein bo mi-zaro shel Yaakov} \u8212- another interpretation: you have no prayer that is effective unless it contains something of the descendants of Jacob. {\i Ve-ha-yadayim yedei Esav, ein lecha milchama she-notzachat she-ein bo mi-zaro shel Esav} \u8212- and you have no war that is won unless there is something from the descendants of Esau involved in it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Heinu de-amar Reb Elazar: be-shot lashon techavei, be-chirchurei lashon techavei} \u8212- and this is what Rabbi Elazar said: in the slander of the tongue it will be hidden, in the contentiousness of the tongue it will be hidden. Rashi connects this back to the story of {\i Kamtza u-Bar Kamtza} \u8212- the in-fighting, the {\i lashon hara}, that ultimately caused the destruction.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two Destructions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Amar Rav Yehuda amar Rav: mai de-chtiv, al naharot Bavel sham yashavnu gam bachinu be-zochreinu et Tziyon} \u8212- what does this verse mean? {\i Melamed she-her\u8217'ah HaKadosh Baruch Hu le-David churban bayit rishon ve-churban bayit sheni} \u8212- God showed David both the destruction of the First Temple and the destruction of the Second. {\i Churban bayit rishon: she-ne\u8217'emar al naharot Bavel} \u8212- the First Temple\u8217's destruction: {\i by the waters of Babylon}. {\i Bayit sheni: de-chtiv zechor Hashem li-vnei Edom et yom Yerushalayim ha-omrim aru aru ad ha-yesod bah} \u8212- the Second Temple: remember, God, against the children of Edom the day of Jerusalem when they said, destroy it, destroy it to its very foundation. The {\i bnei Edom} here is read not as the literal Edomites but as the figurative Edom \u8212- Rome \u8212- that destroyed the Second {\i Beis ha-Mikdash}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Four Hundred\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Children Captive\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Amar Rav Yehuda amar Shmuel, ve-iteima Reb Ami} \u8212- it was taught: {\i maaseh be-arba me\u8217'ot yeladim u-me\u8217'ot yeladot she-nishbu le-kalon} \u8212- four hundred boys and girls were taken captive for degradation \u8212- meaning they were to be used for sexual purposes by their Roman captors. {\i Hirgishu be-atzmam lema hen mitbakshim} \u8212- they sensed why they were being taken.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Eldest Interprets\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 They asked: {\i im anu tov\u8217'im ba-yam anu ba\u8217'im le-chayei ha-olam ha-ba} \u8212- if we were to drown ourselves in the sea, would we attain the world to come? {\i Darash lahen ha-gadol she-bahen} \u8212- the eldest interpreted for them the following verse: {\i Amar Hashem mi-Bashan ashiv, ashiv mi-metzulot yam} \u8212- the Lord said, from the Bashan I will bring back, I will bring back from the depths of the sea. {\i Mi-Bashan ashiv: mi-bein shinei arayot ashiv} \u8212- from the Bashan, meaning from the teeth of the lions. {\i Mi-metzulot yam: elu she-tvu\u8217'im ba-yam} \u8212- from the depths of the sea, these are those who drown themselves in the sea. God declares that He will bring back those who give their lives to the deep rather than surrender themselves to the lions \u8212- Rome.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Children Jump\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Kivan she-shamu yeladot kach, kafzu kulan ve-naflu le-toch ha-yam} \u8212- when the girls heard this interpretation, they all jumped and fell into the sea. {\i Nas\u8217'u yeladim kal va-chomer be-atzmam} \u8212- the boys then made a {\i kal va-chomer} upon themselves: {\i ve-amru: u-ma halalu she-darkan le-kach, kach anu kach} \u8212- if the girls, for whom this form of abuse is in some sense more expected, were willing to do this, {\i anu she-ein darkenu le-kach, al achas kama ve-chama} \u8212- we, who would suffer what is even less natural, how much more so. {\i Af hen kafzu le-toch ha-yam} \u8212- they too jumped into the sea.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sheep Verse\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Ve-aleihem ha-katuv omer: ki alecha horagnu kol ha-yom, nechshavnu ke-tzon tivcha} \u8212- and upon them the verse says: for Your sake we are killed all the day; we are counted as sheep to the slaughter.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Halakhic Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This {\i midrash} is of considerable {\i halachic} significance. {\i Tosafot} and the {\i chachmei Ashkenaz} drew upon it in the context of the Crusader persecutions, when Jews faced the prospect of forced baptism without any direct threat of death. The question is: is one permitted \u8212- or perhaps even obligated \u8212- to take one\u8217's own life in order to avoid being forced to commit {\i aveira}? The doctrine of {\i yehareg ve-al ya\u8217'avor} applies when the choice offered by the oppressor is: convert or die. But what if the oppressor will not kill you, only forcibly sin against you? May one actively choose death in that circumstance?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Haym Soloveitchik argued at length that there is no sound {\i halachic} basis for this, and that {\i Tosafot}\u8217's reliance on {\i midrashim} like this one reflects a post-facto rationalization of what Jews had already done in extremis, rather than a genuine {\i halachic} derivation. His argument begins from the premise that {\i midrashim} are not a legitimate source for {\i psak halacha}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One may question this premise at its root. Even conceding that a {\i midrash} is not a standard {\i halachic} text in the way a {\i sugya} of {\i Gemara} is, it nonetheless expresses the values and attitudes of {\i Chazal}. One cannot read this {\i midrash} without sensing clearly that {\i Chazal} regarded what these children did as not merely understandable but admirable \u8212- perhaps even praiseworthy. And if one knows what {\i Chazal}\u8217's attitude toward a phenomenon is, that cannot be entirely excluded from the way one reads {\i halachic} texts bearing on the same phenomenon, even if the {\i midrash} itself does not constitute direct {\i halachic} evidence.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} on this {\i Gemara} reads: {\i kafzu kulan ve-naflu le-toch ha-yam}. The countertext they must address is the story of Rabbi Chanina ben Teradyon, who when being burned said {\i mutav she-yitla mi she-netana ve-al yachavel be-atzmo} \u8212- better that God should take my soul and I should not injure myself. {\i Tosafot} explains the distinction: {\i hachi yere\u8217'im hayu mi-yisurim} \u8212- these children feared the torture. {\i De-amrinan: ilmale nigdu le-Chananya Mishael ve-Azarya palchu le-tzalma} \u8212- for we are told that had Chananya, Mishael, and Azarya been tortured, they would have worshipped the idol. The fear is not of death but of capitulation under torture. Furthermore, {\i Tosafot} notes: {\i ve-od al korcham hayu me\u8217'anim otam ve-lo hayu horgim otam klal} \u8212- in addition, they could have been raped without being killed. If the oppressor will neither kill them nor offer them the option of honoring {\i yehareg ve-al ya\u8217'avor}, and if the only escape from the sin is death, then {\i Tosafot} holds that what these children did was at least an acceptable course of action.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This remains an important {\i Gemara} in the larger conversation about martyrdom, the limits of {\i yehareg ve-al ya\u8217'avor}, and the circumstances \u8212- if any \u8212- under which {\i halacha} regards self-destruction as other than forbidden.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Gittin 58\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Woman & Seven Children\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Seven Sons Refuse\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara continues from the middle of 57b, following the {\i aggadata} about young children who threw themselves into the sea to avoid a life of moral degradation \u8212- a passage the Tosafot cites as evidence that, in extremis, even taking one\u8217's own life may constitute an act of {\i kiddush Hashem}, departing from the standard halachic framework that ordinarily governs martyrdom. With that context in mind, the Gemara introduces another story in the same spirit.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The verse that frames the entire discussion is from Tehillim: {\i ki alecha horagnu kol hayom, nechshavnu k\u8217'tzon tivcha} \u8212- \u8220"For Your sake we are slaughtered all day long, we are counted as sheep for the slaughter.\u8221" Rabbi Yehuda applies this verse specifically to the story of a woman and her seven children.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 They were brought before Caesar one by one and commanded to worship idols. The first son refused, citing {\i Anochi Hashem Elokecha} \u8212- \u8220"I am the Lord your God.\u8221" He was taken out and executed. The second quoted {\i Lo yihyeh lecha elohim acherim al panai} \u8212- \u8220"You shall have no other gods before Me\u8221" \u8212- and was likewise killed. The third answered with {\i Zoveach la\u8217'elohim yochoram} \u8212- \u8220"Whoever sacrifices to foreign gods shall be destroyed.\u8221" The fourth cited {\i Lo tishtachaveh l\u8217'el acher} \u8212- \u8220"Do not bow to another god.\u8221" The fifth declared {\i Shema Yisrael Hashem Elokeinu Hashem Echad}. The sixth quoted {\i V\u8217'yadata hayom vahasheivota el levavecha ki Hashem hu ha\u8217'elokim bashamayim mima\u8217'al v\u8217'al ha\u8217'aretz mitachat ein od} \u8212- \u8220"You shall know today and take to heart that the Lord is God in the heavens above and on the earth below; there is no other.\u8221" One notices here a progression: the earlier sons quoted the {\i lo ta\u8217'asehs}, the prohibitions against idolatry, while the later sons shifted to positive affirmations of faith.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Seventh Son & Mother\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 When the seventh child was brought before Caesar, the emperor, frustrated by this point, tried a different strategy. He would throw down his imperial coin \u8212- bearing his own image \u8212- and instruct the boy to pick it up, so that onlookers would think he was bowing in submission to the emperor\u8217's authority. This was, of course, the imperial cult: the veneration of Caesar as a god. The child\u8217's response was striking: {\i Chaval alecha keisar, chaval alecha keisar} \u8212- \u8220"Woe to you, Caesar, woe to you.\u8221" If you are so insistent on your own honor, he said, how much more is God insistent upon His honor? How then could I even make it {\i appear} that I am betraying God\u8217's honor?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The child was taken out to be executed. At that moment, his mother asked the soldiers to bring him to her for a moment so she could embrace him. She gathered all seven of her children \u8212- apparently before any of them had been killed \u8212- and spoke to them. She said: Go and tell Abraham your forefather, {\i Atah akadata mizbe\u8217'ach echad, va\u8217'ani akadeti shivah mizbachot} \u8212- \u8220"You bound one altar, while I have bound seven altars.\u8221" Then she too went up to the roof, fell, and died. A {\i bat kol} then declared: {\i Em habanim smeichah} \u8212- \u8220"The mother of children rejoices.\u8221" They had sanctified God\u8217's name.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is worth pausing over the precise wording. The text uses {\i mizbe\u8217'ach} \u8212- altar \u8212- rather than {\i zevach} \u8212- sacrifice \u8212- which seems grammatically awkward. One senses that the language is deliberately softened, shifting away from the stark statement that Isaac was literally offered as a slaughter. This connects to a {\i midrash} in which Abraham did not actually slaughter Isaac \u8212- it says {\i al tishlach yadcha el hana\u8217'ar}, \u8220"do not lay your hand upon the lad\u8221" \u8212- but that the fire on the altar consumed him nonetheless, and a miracle reconstituted him. The {\i midrash} about the {\i eifer shel Yitzchak munach lifnei Hashem} \u8212- the ashes of Isaac resting before God \u8212- plays on exactly this reading.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This imagery proved extraordinarily potent. During the Crusades, Jewish communities facing forced conversion invoked the {\i akeidah} as a model for taking their own lives, and in some cases the lives of their children, rather than allow forcible baptism. The Crusade chronicles record parents using language almost identical to this passage \u8212- \u8220"Abraham offered one sacrifice; I offer all my children.\u8221" It raises profound halachic questions: not merely taking one\u8217's own life, but actively taking a child\u8217's life. And the anguish was compounded when, on at least one occasion, the threatened assault never materialized, and the tragedy had been carried out in vain. Here in the Talmudic story, the mother took her own life but did not herself kill her children \u8212- and the text\u8217's use of {\i mizbe\u8217'ach} rather than {\i zevach} perhaps reflects that same hesitation about stating the thing too directly.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Jeremiah\u8217's Lament\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now applies the verse from Tehillim \u8212- {\i ki alecha horagnu kol hayom} \u8212- to several other contexts, moving through a series of interpretations that carry us from literal martyrdom into the world of {\i talmud Torah}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Martyrdom Interpretations\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Brit Milah & Martyrdom\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yehoshua ben Levi reads {\i ki alecha horagnu kol hayom} as referring to {\i brit milah}, which was {\i nitnah bashmini} \u8212- given on the eighth day. During Roman persecutions, the Romans frequently prohibited circumcision, in part because they considered it a disfigurement of the body, and Jews were repeatedly prepared to risk their lives in order to perform the commandment. Rashi offers a somewhat different reading \u8212- that sometimes the surgical procedure itself could lead to complications and death \u8212- but it seems more compelling to read the verse as referring to the willingness to give one\u8217's life, or risk it, for the sake of {\i milah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Torah Tent Slaughter\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Shimon ben Lakish offers a reading that is, on the face of it, startling. He says {\i ki alecha horagnu kol hayom} refers to {\i talmidei chachamim shemarim hilchot shechitah be\u8217'atzman} \u8212- Torah scholars who demonstrate the laws of {\i shechitah} on themselves. When learning {\i hilchot shechitah} and wanting to illustrate the proper movement of the knife on the animal\u8217's neck, one slips \u8212- and that is {\i ki alecha horagnu kol hayom}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is a jarring juxtaposition. From Roman persecution and the Caesar demanding emperor worship, we have suddenly shifted to the {\i beit midrash} and someone demonstrating slaughter techniques. The Gemara itself adds: {\i d\u8217'amar Rava, kol milei lachzei inish b\u8217'nafshei bar mishechitah} \u8212- one may demonstrate anything on oneself except {\i shechitah}. Do not try this at home. There is also mention of {\i tzara\u8217'at umar\u8217'ot nega\u8217'im} \u8212- one should not demonstrate the appearance of leprous afflictions on one\u8217's own body, presumably because of {\i ayin hara}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Yet the shift itself is theologically significant. Recall the arc of Rabban Yochanan ben Zakkai: the entire camera of Jewish life pivoted from the {\i Beit HaMikdash} and Roman Caesar and martyrdom to {\i ten li Yavneh v\u8217'chachameha} \u8212- the world of Torah replacing that other world. Here too, a verse that once described literal death at Roman hands is now given new meaning in the {\i beit midrash}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Torah Devotion\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Nachman bar Yitzchak completes the transformation. He reads {\i ki alecha horagnu kol hayom} as referring to {\i talmidei chachamim shememitim atzmam al divrei Torah} \u8212- Torah scholars who kill themselves, figuratively, over words of Torah. The literal martyrdom of the {\i churban} era becomes the self-sacrifice of the scholar who devotes himself entirely to learning.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is grounded in the dictum of Reish Lakish: {\i ein divrei Torah mitkaymin ela bemi shememit atzmo aleihem} \u8212- words of Torah endure only in one who is willing to die for them \u8212- as the verse says, {\i zot hatorah adam ki yamut b\u8217'ohel} \u8212- \u8220"This is the Torah: when a person dies in the tent.\u8221" The figurative death in the tent of Torah replaces the literal death {\i ki alecha horagnu kol hayom}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Destruction of Beitar\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tefillin at Beitar\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now turns to the destruction of Beitar. Rabbah bar bar Chana said in the name of Rabbi Yochanan: {\i arbaim se\u8217'ah ktzitzai tefillin nimtze\u8217'u berashei harugei Beitar} \u8212- forty {\i se\u8217'ah} of {\i tefillin batim} were found on the heads of those slaughtered at Beitar. Forty {\i se\u8217'ah} is the measure required for a {\i mikvah}, which one listener noted is perhaps symbolically resonant, though the precise import requires further thought.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yannai b\u8217'Rabbi Yishmael disputes the figure: it was not forty {\i se\u8217'ah}, but {\i shalosh kupot shel arbaim arbaim} \u8212- three containers each of forty {\i se\u8217'ah}, three times the amount. A {\i braita} teaches yet another version: {\i arbaim kupot shel shalosh shalosh} \u8212- forty containers of three {\i se\u8217'ah} each. The Gemara resolves that these two versions do not in fact contradict: {\i had\u8217'reisha had\u8217'dara} \u8212- one refers to {\i tefillin shel yad} and the other to {\i tefillin shel rosh}. Rashi explains that the {\i tefillin shel rosh}, which requires four compartments, cannot be made as small as the {\i shel yad}; anyone who has seen the tiny {\i tefillin} discovered at Masada will understand the difficulty of fitting four {\i batim} into a single very small housing, and so the {\i shel rosh} would necessarily be larger \u8212- roughly three times the size of the {\i shel yad}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The image of all these slaughtered people still wearing their {\i tefillin} is arresting. People in those days wore {\i tefillin} throughout the day. The {\i tefillin} represents both the {\i mitzvah} and the {\i parshiyot} written within \u8212- a symbol of the {\i Torah} world. And so the image of the {\i tefillin} on the heads of the slain intersects the three themes woven throughout this passage: the destruction of the {\i Beit HaMikdash}, the destruction of the people, and the destruction of the world of {\i Torah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Assi adds: {\i arba\u8217'a kabbin mo\u8217'ach nimtze\u8217'u al even achat} \u8212- four {\i kav} of brains were found on a single stone, from the smashing of skulls. Ulla says the figure was nine {\i kabbin}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Kahana (and some say it was Shila bar Mari) asks: what is the {\i pasuk} for this? The verse cited is from Tehillim 137: {\i Bat Bavel hashidudah, ashrei she-yeshalem lach, ashrei she-yo\u8217'chez venipetz et olalayich el hasala} \u8212- \u8220"O devastated Daughter of Babylon, praised is the one who repays you what you have done to us; praised is the one who seizes your infants and dashes them against the rock.\u8221" This is a deeply challenging verse, one that has generated much contemporary discussion given its apparent expression of bloodlust. Here it is cited as evidence of what the Jewish people suffered \u8212- if this is what is considered appropriate repayment, then by inference this is what was done to them.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Children of Zion\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara turns to the verse in {\i Eichah}: {\i B\u8217'nei Tzion ha-yekarim ham\u8217'sula\u8217'im bapaz} \u8212- \u8220"The precious children of Zion, esteemed above gold.\u8221" What does {\i m\u8217'sula\u8217'im bapaz} mean? Does it mean they were literally covered in gold jewelry?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara rejects this: {\i d\u8217'bei Rabbi Shila} reports that only two measures of the finest gold, {\i pizi}, descended into the world \u8212- one in Rome, and one distributed across the entire rest of the world. There was nowhere near enough for every child in Zion to be coated in gold. Rather, {\i m\u8217'sula\u8217'im bapaz} means {\i she-hayu meganim et ha-paz be-yofyam} \u8212- they shamed gold by the superiority of their beauty. Gold jewelry paled compared to how these children looked.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara continues: originally, prestigious Romans would keep an image of a handsome person \u8212- a seal or portrait \u8212- on the wall of their bedchambers during intercourse, believing that what one envisions at the moment of conception influences the appearance of the resulting child. This goes back to some degree to the story of Yaakov and the striped rods. After Israel was taken captive, the Romans would instead {\i maiti benei Yisrael ve-asrei be-charei de-puryeihu} \u8212- bring Israelite captives and tie them at the foot of their beds \u8212- in order to produce beautiful offspring.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One Roman asks his friend: where is this terrible humiliation actually prophesied? The friend responds with the verse {\i gam kol choli ve-chol maka asher lo katuv be-sefer ha-Torah ha-zot} \u8212- \u8220"also every illness and every plague that is not written in this book of Torah\u8221" will befall you. Even the unimaginable, the unprecedented degradations, were encompassed in that catch-all phrase. The first man says: how far was that verse from where I was up to in my learning? His friend answers: about a page and a half. The man replies: had I gotten there myself, I wouldn\u8217't have needed to ask you. A strange little exchange \u8212- but it perhaps lightens the devastating material slightly.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Beitar Synagogues\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Yehuda said in the name of Shmuel in the name of Rabbi Shimon ben Gamliel: what does the verse {\i einai olela le-nafshi mi-kol benot iri} mean \u8212- \u8220"my eye afflicts my soul, more than all the daughters of my city\u8221"? {\i Arba mea batei kneisiyot hayu be-krach Beitar} \u8212- there were four hundred synagogues in the city of Beitar. Each synagogue had four hundred schoolteachers of young children. Each of those teachers had four hundred students. These are clearly hyperbolic numbers, but they evoke a vast and thriving world of Jewish education. When the enemy entered the city, they pierced the children with their staffs. When the enemy ultimately prevailed and captured the people, {\i karchum be-sifreihem ve-hitzitum ba-esh} \u8212- they wrapped the children and teachers in their books and set them ablaze. This is the same fate we associate with Rabbi Chananya ben Teradyon, but here it is depicted as happening to an entire civilization of Torah scholars and students. One cannot read this without thinking of later destructions.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rabbi Yehoshua Rescues\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ransom from Prison\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara presents the story of Rabbi Yehoshua ben Chananya, who traveled to the great city of Rome. He was told: {\i tinok echad yesh be-veit ha-asurim} \u8212- there is one child in the prison, {\i yafeh einayim ve-tov ro\u8217'i ve-kvutzotav sdurot lo taltalim} \u8212- beautiful of eyes, handsome of appearance, his locks arranged in layers. Coming just after the discussion of the beauty of the {\i Benei Tzion}, the echo is unmistakable.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Child\u8217's Knowledge\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yehoshua went and stood at the entrance of the prison. He quoted the first half of a verse from Yeshayahu: {\i Mi natan limshisah Ya\u8217'akov ve\u8217'Yisrael le\u8217'vozezim} \u8212- \u8220"Who gave Jacob over to plunder and Israel to spoilers?\u8221" The child, from inside, responded with the second half of the verse: {\i Halo Hashem zo chatanu lo, velo avu bidrachav haloch, velo shamu betorato} \u8212- \u8220"Is it not the Lord, against Whom we sinned, in Whose ways they would not walk, and Whose Torah they did not heed?\u8221"\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The child knew the end of the verse. Rabbi Yehoshua declared: {\i Muvtachni bo she-moreh hora\u8217'ah be\u8217'Yisrael} \u8212- I am confident that this child will rule on halachic matters in Israel. And he took an oath: {\i she\u8217'eini zaz mikan ad she\u8217'efdenu bechol mamon sheposkin alav} \u8212- I will not leave here until I redeem him, no matter the price demanded.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This raises an immediate question in light of the earlier {\i Gemara} about {\i tikkun olam} \u8212- the rabbinic enactment not to redeem captives for more than their worth, lest it encourage further taking of captives. Tosafot addresses this directly: {\i ki itcha sakanat nefashot podin shvuyim yoter mikdei dameihen} \u8212- when there is a genuine threat to life, one redeems captives for any sum. The earlier limitation applies only where life is not at risk. This is an important qualification of the earlier {\i Gemara} that had not been stated there explicitly.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot offers a second explanation: {\i mishum de\u8217'mufla be\u8217'chochmah hayah} \u8212- because this child was so obviously exceptional in his Torah knowledge, one might make an exception for him. This raises a perennial tension: on one hand, we resist distinguishing between lives based on social worth; on the other hand, {\i Mishnah Horayot} establishes that a {\i talmid chacham} takes precedence over all others.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Yishmael Identity\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara reports: he did not leave until he redeemed him for a great sum of money, and it was not long before that child ruled on halachic matters in Israel. {\i U-manu} \u8212- and who was he? {\i Rabbi Yishmael ben Elisha.}\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rabbi Yishmael\u8217's Children\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Captors\u8217' Plan\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Yehuda said in the name of Rav: there is a story about the son and daughter of Rabbi Yishmael ben Elisha \u8212- himself a {\i kohen} of distinguished lineage \u8212- who were taken captive by two separate masters. We have just been told how beautiful these children were; their beauty made them desirable. {\i Liyamim nizdavgu sheneihem bemakom echad} \u8212- after some time, the two masters happened to meet. One said: I have a male slave of unparalleled beauty. The other said: I have a female slave more beautiful than any in the world. They agreed: let us have them marry one another, and we will divide the children between us. They placed them together in a room, treating them as commodities whose offspring would be split between owners.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Siblings\u8217' Reunion\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Each sat in a separate corner of the room. He said to himself: I am a {\i kohen}, descended from {\i kohanim gedolim} \u8212- shall I marry a slave woman? She said to herself: I am a {\i kohenet}, the daughter of {\i kohanim gedolim} \u8212- shall I marry a slave? They wept through the entire night, presumably unable to see one another in the dark. When dawn broke and they could finally see each other, {\i hikiru zeh et zeh} \u8212- they recognized one another as brother and sister. {\i Ve-nafalu zeh al zeh ve-ga\u8217'u bi-vchiyah} \u8212- they fell upon each other and cried out, {\i ad she-yatzta nishmatan} \u8212- until their souls departed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 There is something worth noting about their initial reasoning. Neither knew the other person was a Jew. They refused on the grounds of their priestly status \u8212- they could not marry a slave, even having themselves become slaves. It is this instinctive clinging to identity and dignity, before any recognition of kinship, that makes the reunion all the more devastating.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 On them, Yirmeyahu composed his lament: {\i al eileh ani bochiya, eini eini yordah mayim} \u8212- \u8220"For these I weep; my eye, my eye runs down with water.\u8221"\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Tzafnat Bat Pnu\u8217'el\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tzafnat\u8217's Assault\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Reish Lakish relates the story of a woman named {\i Tzafnat bat Pnu\u8217'el}. What does {\i Tzafnat} mean? {\i She-hakol tzofim be-yofyah} \u8212- everyone would gaze upon her beauty. {\i Pnu\u8217'el} means {\i bito shel kohen gadol she-shimesh lifnai ve-lifnim} \u8212- the daughter of a {\i kohen gadol} who served in the innermost chambers, the {\i lifnai ve-lifnim}, meaning the Holy of Holies. She was taken captive, and her captor abused her sexually the entire night. In the morning, he dressed her in seven garments and brought her out to sell her.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 An exceedingly ugly man came and demanded to see her beauty before purchasing her. The captor said: if you want to buy her, buy her. The man insisted on seeing her. Six of her garments were stripped off. The seventh she herself tore away, rolled herself in the dust, and cried out to God.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Challenge to God\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Her words to God were: {\i Ribono shel olam, im aleinu lo chasata, al kedushat shimcha ha-gibor, lamah lo tachos} \u8212- \u8220"Master of the Universe, if You have no compassion on us, why will You not have compassion on Your great Name?\u8221" This is a striking moment in the Gemara \u8212- perhaps the first explicit articulation of what will become the central emotional register of {\i kinot}: a direct challenge to God, asking how He could allow this to happen.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 For this, Yirmeyahu composed the lament: {\i biti ami chagri sak ve-hitpalshi be-afar} \u8212- \u8220"Daughter of my people, gird yourself with sackcloth and roll in the ashes; make mourning as for an only son, bitter lamentation.\u8221" The phrase {\i ki pitom yavo ha-shoded aleinu} \u8212- \u8220"for the ravager will suddenly come upon us\u8221" \u8212- is notable: it says {\i aleinu}, \u8220"upon us,\u8221" not {\i alayich}, \u8220"upon you.\u8221" The Gemara observes: {\i ki kavyachol alai ve-alecha} \u8212- as if to say, it is upon Me and upon you. God Himself, as it were, is afflicted by the suffering of Israel. Yet this makes the challenge of the verse all the more acute: if God too is pained, how can God not defend His own name against the {\i shoded}?\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Societal Corruption\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Apprentice\u8217's Scheme\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Yehuda said in the name of Rav: what does the verse {\i ve-ashku gever u-veito ve-ish ve-nachalato} \u8212- \u8220"they oppressed a man and his household, a man and his inheritance\u8221" \u8212- mean? There was a man who lusted after his master\u8217's wife. He was a carpenter\u8217's apprentice. One day his master found himself in need of a loan. The apprentice, who had more available cash, was approached for help. Instead of simply lending the money, the apprentice said: send your wife to me and she will receive the loan. The master sent his wife. She stayed three days.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Then the apprentice came to the master and asked about his wife\u8217's whereabouts. The master said: I sent her to you. The apprentice replied: I gave her the money right away and sent her off. But what I heard, he said, was that {\i ha-tinokot nit\u8217'allu bah ba-derech} \u8212- the young men had sport with her on the way back. The implication is clear: the three-day absence is to be understood not as anything that occurred in the apprentice\u8217's house, but as evidence that she had been faithless on the road. This is, of course, false \u8212- a trap.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Marriage & Servitude\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The master asked: what should I do? The apprentice said: divorce her. The master replied: her {\i ketubah} is too large; I cannot afford it. The apprentice said: I will lend you the money, and you can pay her {\i ketubah} with it. The master divorced his wife. The apprentice then went and married her. When the time came to repay the debt and the master had no means to do so, the apprentice said: come and work off your debt as my servant. And so there sat the apprentice and the former master\u8217's wife, eating and drinking, while the master stood and served them, pouring their wine.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Master\u8217's Tears\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 His tears fell from his eyes into their cups. {\i U-ve-otah sha\u8217'ah nechtam gezar din} \u8212- and at that moment, the decree was sealed. The Gemara understands this not merely as the fate of these individuals but as a symbol of the moral corruption that had consumed the entire society, and it was this moment that triggered the irreversible judgment against them.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Some say the final {\i makkah be-pattish} was {\i shetei petilot be-ner echad} \u8212- two wicks in one candle \u8212- meaning when women began entering into relationships with two men simultaneously. Again, the emphasis falls on sexual corruption and on the exploitation of the vulnerable.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is worth noting that many of these devastating narratives \u8212- the story of the apprentice, the story of the woman in Beitar and the children, and others \u8212- are attributed to the same {\i amora}, Rav Yehuda. He seems to have been deeply engaged in trying to provide some theological reckoning with the catastrophe: alternating between the incomprehensible suffering that defies explanation and the model of {\i schar va-onesh} that seeks to locate the destruction in human failure. The same oscillation runs through {\i Eichah} itself: God, how could You allow this? \u8212- followed by: because we did not listen, because we did not walk in His ways. Neither answer is sufficient on its own, and the Talmud does not pretend otherwise.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Sikrikon Land Law\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav vs.\u160 ?Shmuel\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 With the {\i aggadata} of the {\i churban habayit} concluded, the Gemara returns to the {\i Mishnah} and to halachic analysis of the {\i sikrikon} laws.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The background: during and after the Roman destruction, Romans or their agents would seize Jewish land \u8212- sometimes under threat to the original owner\u8217's life \u8212- and then sell it. The Mishnah developed a {\i takana} governing under what conditions a purchaser from the {\i sikrikon} acquires valid title, which required in its first stage that the buyer also purchase the property from the original Jewish owner. A central question is how to determine whether the original owner\u8217's subsequent sale is genuine or merely performed under duress to avoid trouble with the {\i sikrikon}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav rules: the distinction between valid and invalid turns on the form of the second transaction. If the original owner merely said verbally {\i lech chazek ukeni} \u8212- go take possession of it \u8212- this is insufficient; we presume he said so only to avoid confrontation, not because he genuinely wished to sell. But if he executed a written {\i shtar} of sale, {\i kana} \u8212- the transaction is valid, because the formality of a document demonstrates genuine intent.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Shmuel disagrees: {\i af beshtar nami lo kana ad she-yichtov lo achrayut} \u8212- even a written {\i shtar} is insufficient unless it explicitly includes a clause of {\i achrayut} \u8212- a guarantee of indemnification, whereby the seller undertakes liability if anyone later seizes the property from the buyer. Writing a liability clause demonstrates such a high level of commitment that it can only reflect genuine intent to sell, even following a coerced transaction with the {\i sikrikon}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i braita} is cited in support of Shmuel\u8217's position.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Wife & Husband Order\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Shimon ben Elazar applies the same logic to a parallel case involving a husband and wife. When a woman has a lien on property through her {\i ketubah}, and the buyer purchases from the wife first and then from the husband, {\i mekacho kayam} \u8212- the purchase stands. But if he purchases from the husband first and then from the wife, {\i mekacho batel} \u8212- the purchase is void \u8212- because we presume the wife sold only to please her husband and not of genuine free will. This remains invalid {\i ad she-yichtov lo achrayut} \u8212- unless she writes a guarantee of indemnification in the {\i shtar}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara asks: does this not contradict Rav, who required only a {\i shtar} without an explicit {\i achrayut} clause? Rav responds: {\i mai achrayut nami shtar} \u8212- by {\i achrayut} we mean simply a {\i shtar}; the document itself is what makes it binding. Rashi explains that {\i achrayut} here does not mean a lien on property but simply that the {\i shtar} makes the transaction final. Tosafot, however, suggests that the dispute between Rav and Shmuel is precisely whether {\i achrayut} must be written explicitly in a document or whether it is implied \u8212- with Rav holding that any {\i shtar} carries {\i achrayut} by implication.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Three-Year Rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Three-Year Possession\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara presents a {\i baraita}: if someone purchased land from the {\i sikrikon}, ate its produce for three years in the presence of the original owners, and then sold it to a third party \u8212- {\i ein la-be\u8217'alim al lokeach sheni klum} \u8212- the original owners have no claim against the second purchaser.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This invokes the {\i halacha} of {\i chazakah} from Bava Batra: if one has been in possession of another\u8217's property for three years and the original owner lodges no objection, the possessor may, when challenged, claim that he purchased the property and has simply lost the {\i shtar}. One is not obligated to retain documents beyond three years when the owner watched and said nothing.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara presses: what exactly is the case? If the first buyer \u8212- who purchased from the {\i sikrikon} \u8212- is claiming to the original owner that he bought it from him and the three-year possession corroborates that claim, then even without involving the third party, the first buyer is already protected. And if the first buyer is {\i not} making that claim \u8212- if he says he never bought it from the original owner \u8212- then the three-year possession helps neither him nor any subsequent purchaser.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Secondary Protection\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Sheshet resolves: {\i l\u8217'olam d\u8217'lo ka-ta\u8217'in lei} \u8212- the first buyer is simply not around or not making any claim at all. He has dropped out of the picture. The third party, having purchased the land, is now confronted by the original owner with a demand for return of the property, and does not know how to defend himself in {\i beit din}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara explains that in such a case we assist the third party: we prompt him with the appropriate legal argument. We instruct him to say to the original owner: the man who sold you this land \u8212- from whom I purchased it \u8212- must have bought it from you, and he need not prove it because he was in possession for three years. The original owner saw him there for three years and made no objection; therefore we can presume the first buyer acquired it legitimately, and the third party holds it through him.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Heirs & Claims\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara extends this principle: {\i tainan le-yores ve-tainan le-lokeach} \u8212- we provide this guided claim both for an heir and for a subsequent purchaser. Neither necessarily knows the history of the property or what they would need to say to defend their claim in court. If someone challenges an heir \u8212- saying that the father never legitimately owned the land \u8212- we step in and instruct the heir: your father possessed this for three years without objection; therefore you can claim he bought it from the original owner. The heir, like the third-party purchaser, does not need to have independent knowledge of all the legal arguments required to defend the title.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 However, the Gemara qualifies: {\i ve-idach ei ta\u8217'an in, ei lo, lo} \u8212- the original first buyer, {\i Shimon} in the example, must defend himself if he is the one being challenged. We do not provide {\i him} with a claim he has not himself made. The prompted claim is available only to subsequent parties \u8212- heirs and purchasers \u8212- who come to the property without full knowledge of the surrounding circumstances.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Debt & Theft\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 No Sikrikon\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara presents a {\i baraita}: {\i haba machmat hov u-machmat amparut ein bo mishum sikrikon} \u8212- land seized for debt or taken by simple theft, without any threat to the original owner\u8217's life, does not fall under the {\i sikrikon} laws. The {\i sikrikon} framework \u8212- including the {\i takana} that after twelve months the original owner is presumed to have given up hope and the buyer need only compensate him a quarter of the land\u8217's value \u8212- applies only where the original transfer was coerced under threat to life. A simple creditor seizing land for debt, or a thief stealing it outright, does not trigger these provisions, and the original owner never loses his claim.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Twelve Months\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i baraita} adds: {\i ve-amparut atzmo tzricha she-tisheh shneim asar chodesh} \u8212- and in a case of theft itself, twelve months are required. The Gemara clarifies: this means that the twelve-month waiting period before the second stage of the {\i takana} applies is itself a feature of the {\i sikrikon} law, not of the theft case. In theft, there is no {\i sikrikon} law at all \u8212- the original owner never loses his presumptive right to reclaim the property.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Yosef then states: {\i naktinan, ein amparut be-Bavel} \u8212- we hold that there is no {\i din amparut} \u8212- no law of {\i amparut} \u8212- operative in Babylonia. The Gemara immediately challenges: surely theft of land occurs in Babylonia. Rav Yosef refines: what we mean is that there is no legal {\i din} of {\i amparut} in Babylonia. If someone seizes your land in Babylonia and you do not go to court to assert your claim \u8212- {\i keivan de-ika bei davar ve-lo azil kavel, amachulei achlei} \u8212- since there is a functioning court system and you chose not to file a claim in it, we presume you have waived your rights. The existence of a trustworthy legal framework changes the calculus entirely: a person who could have sought redress and did not is presumed to have relinquished the claim.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is a striking observation about the relationship between legal systems and presumed intention. The Babylonian authorities commanded more trust in this respect than the Roman system \u8212- not necessarily because the Romans were unjust, but because Jews under Roman rule faced a very different relationship to imperial courts.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Gittin 59\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Gido bar Re\u8217'ili\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Land Tax Arrangement\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The daf opens in the middle of 58b with the case of Gido bar Re\u8217'ili, situated within the broader discussion of {\i sikrikon} \u8212- the legal framework governing property seized under threat of life and the various {\i takkanot} enacted to address it. By way of review: the first stage of the {\i takana} required a purchaser to acquire the property from both the {\i sikrikon} and the original owner, and in the correct order, so that the original owner would not feel coerced into a sale. The second stage, enacted later, permitted purchase from the {\i sikrikon} alone after twelve months, on the assumption that the owner had by then relinquished hope of recovering the land.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The case of Gido bar Re\u8217'ili unfolds as follows. A valley\u8217's land had been abandoned by its owners \u8212- perhaps they had left the region \u8212- yet the community as a whole remained liable for the land tax. To resolve this, the community arranged for Gido bar Re\u8217'ili to work the land in the owners\u8217' absence: he would keep the produce and pay the tax on their behalf. It was a reasonable arrangement for all parties.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Prepayment Problem\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The difficulty arose when Gido bar Re\u8217'ili, perhaps overeager, prepaid the land tax for three years in advance, presumably hoping to secure three years of produce. However, after only the first year, the original owners returned and reclaimed their land. Their position was entirely reasonable: you paid the tax and kept the produce for the first year while we were away \u8212- fair enough. But from this point forward, {\i anan yavnan va\u8217'anan achlinan} \u8212- we will pay the tax and we will take the produce. It is, after all, our land.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Pappa\u8217's Solution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The matter came before Rav Pappa, who considered writing Gido bar Re\u8217'ili a {\i turfa} \u8212- a writ of the {\i beis din} authorizing him to collect his prepaid taxes from the people of the valley. The reasoning was that the valley community had, in effect, appointed him to this role, and having paid out money on their behalf, he ought to be able to recoup it from them.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Huna Rejects\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Huna son of Rav Yehoshua objected to Rav Pappa\u8217's proposed remedy: {\i im ken asita sikrikon} \u8212- you have turned this into a case of {\i sikrikon}. The analogy Rashi draws, though admittedly somewhat difficult, is as follows. In the {\i sikrikon} scenario, a purchaser buys land that originally belonged to someone else, and the {\i takana} protects him from losing out simply because the land had previously belonged to the original owner. Here, Rav Pappa would be treating the valley people as though they had some right to grant Gido bar Re\u8217'ili a claim over the land \u8212- as if, now that the original owners have returned, Gido bar Re\u8217'ili is guaranteed not to lose out on his investment, analogous to the protection afforded by the {\i sikrikon takana}. But this analogy fails entirely. There was no seizure of land under threat of life, no {\i sikrikon} in any meaningful sense, and therefore no foundational presumption that this person had a right to buy the land such that the owners\u8217' return cannot be held against him.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sikrikon Analogy\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The response of Rav Huna makes clear that the {\i sikrikon} framework cannot be extended by analogy to situations that lack its defining features. The {\i sikrikon} scenario involves a coercive seizure; the arrangements by the valley community involved no such thing. They simply needed someone to manage the land and pay the tax. There is no underlying structure that would generate a protected expectation of the kind that the {\i sikrikon takana} was designed to address.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Heniah Maotav\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rather, Rav Huna son of Rav Yehoshua rules: {\i heniah maotav al keren hatzvi} \u8212- he placed his money on the horns of a deer. In other words, he effectively threw his money away. Nobody asked him to prepay three years of taxes in advance. He has no fundamental right, simply because the owners abandoned the land, to assume some protected status analogous to a {\i sikrikon} purchaser such that he will not lose out. One might ask: since those taxes were already paid to the government, shouldn\u8217't the returning owners at least reimburse him, since he saved them that expense? Rashi seems to suggest that the government would actually double-collect from the returning owners \u8212- a jarring result, but that appears to be how Rashi reads the case. More broadly, {\i heniah maotav al keren hatzvi} can apply even where there may be some moral responsibility to repay \u8212- the Gemara is addressing the question of legal obligation, and legally, Gido bar Re\u8217'ili is not owed anything. He acted unilaterally and must bear the consequences.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Sikrikon Takana\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 First Takana\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now returns to the {\i sikrikon} framework more directly, reviewing its two stages. The first {\i takana} \u8212- {\i mishna rishona} \u8212- required that a purchaser acquire the property from both the {\i sikrikon} and the original owner, and in the proper sequence, so the original owner would not feel compelled.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Second Takana\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The second {\i takana}, enacted by a later {\i beis din}, held that if the property sat in the hands of the {\i sikrikon} for twelve months and the original owner did not repurchase it, anyone could go ahead and buy it from the {\i sikrikon} \u8212- on the implicit assumption that after twelve months the owner had fully relinquished his expectation of recovery. However, the purchaser must still pay the original owners a {\i revi\u8217'a} \u8212- a quarter \u8212- because he acquired the property at a discount.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav vs.\u160 ?Shmuel\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The question then becomes: how exactly is this {\i revi\u8217'a} calculated? The Gemara presents a dispute between Rav and Shmuel on the matter.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Quarter Calculations\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav holds {\i revi\u8217'a bemaot} \u8212- a quarter of the purchase price. So if the purchaser paid one hundred for the land, which was really worth one hundred and twenty-five, he pays the original owners twenty-five \u8212- that is, a quarter of what he paid, representing the discount he received. {\i Revi\u8217'a bekarka}, in Rav\u8217's formulation, would mean that equivalent amount expressed in land: since the land is worth one hundred and twenty-five and the purchaser paid one hundred, returning a portion of the land worth twenty-five actually means returning a fifth of the land \u8212- hence the apparent paradox where {\i revi\u8217'a bekarka} in practice means a fifth of the land.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Shmuel, by contrast, holds {\i revi\u8217'a bekarka shehen shalish bemaot} \u8212- a quarter of the land, which works out to a third of the purchase price. In Shmuel\u8217's view, the discount was larger: the purchaser paid one hundred for land worth approximately one hundred and thirty-three, so he must pay the owners thirty-three \u8212- a full third of what he paid. In terms of land, thirty-three out of one hundred and thirty-three is one quarter, hence the phrase {\i revi\u8217'a bekarka}. The underlying dispute is simply whether the discount was one-fifth of the actual value ({\i nechi chumsha zavin}) or one-quarter of the actual value ({\i nechi revi\u8217'a zavin}).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i braisa} is then cited: {\i zu mishna rishona} \u8212- after twelve months, anyone may purchase, {\i aval nosein l\u8217'baalim revi\u8217'a bekarka o revi\u8217'a bemaot}, and the owners may choose whether they want their portion in land or in cash. The {\i braisa} concludes with the phrase {\i revi\u8217'a bemaot}, which appears to support Rav\u8217's position \u8212- that a quarter of the purchase price, i.e., twenty-five out of one hundred, is owed to the owners.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Ashi Resolves\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Ashi reconciles the {\i braisa} with Shmuel\u8217's position as follows. Under Shmuel\u8217's formulation, the purchaser pays the {\i sikrikon} one hundred and then pays the owners thirty-three. Out of the total amount expended \u8212- one hundred and thirty-three \u8212- the owners received thirty-three. That is precisely a quarter of the total money spent. So when the {\i braisa} says {\i revi\u8217'a bemaot}, it can be read in Shmuel\u8217's terms as well: the owners receive a quarter of all the money the purchaser spent in total, counting both the payment to the {\i sikrikon} and the payment to the owners. For Rav, the quarter is of the purchase price alone; for Shmuel, the quarter is of the total expenditure. Both positions can be described as {\i revi\u8217'a bemaot}, resolving the apparent tension.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rav in Beit Din\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Voted First\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav now makes a striking personal disclosure: {\i ana hava b\u8217'minyana d\u8217'vei Rebbi} \u8212- I was present in the {\i beis din} that Rebbi convened, and {\i minai didi manu b\u8217'reisha} \u8212- they asked for my vote first. This is itself a remarkable datum, because Rav is the great transitional figure between the {\i tannaim} and {\i amoraim} \u8212- {\i Rav tanna hu u-falig} \u8212- and here he testifies that he participated in Rebbi\u8217's court when the vote on the {\i sikrikon} was taken.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Voting Order Rules\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara notes what appears to be a procedural irregularity. The {\i mishna} teaches: {\i dinei mamonot v\u8217'ha-taharot v\u8217'ha-tumos matchilin min ha-gadol} \u8212- in monetary cases and matters of {\i tumah} and {\i taharah}, the court begins with the most senior member voting first. The rationale is honor and respect for the senior authority. In {\i dinei nefashot} \u8212- capital cases \u8212- the procedure is reversed: {\i matchilin min ha-tzad}, voting begins from the side, from a more junior member. Two reasons are given for this: first, in a capital case where someone\u8217's life is at stake, you want every judge to feel maximally free to voice an independent opinion, without being overawed by the senior authority\u8217's position. Second, Rashi cites a {\i drasha} from the {\i pasuk} {\i lo sa\u8217'aneh al rav} \u8212- one may not simply acquiesce to a senior authority\u8217's ruling in {\i dinei nefashot}. So in capital cases, starting from the junior members protects the integrity of deliberation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Since the vote on {\i sikrikon} was clearly not a capital case, Rebbi himself should have voted first. How, then, did Rav \u8212- presumably not the most senior member \u8212- cast the first vote?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rebbi\u8217's Humility\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara answers, in the name of Rabbah son of Rabbah (or, some say, Rav Hillel son of Rav Vallas): {\i shani minyana d\u8217'vei Rebbi} \u8212- Rebbi\u8217's court was different. {\i D\u8217'kulei maminu lei min hatzad} \u8212- in all matters, they always began from the side, not from Rebbi. Tosafot explains this in light of the famous last {\i mishna} of {\i Sotah}: {\i batul anavah} \u8212- when Rebbi died, humility ceased. Rebbi was so humble that even in cases where he was entitled by right to vote first, he voluntarily stepped back and allowed others to begin deliberations. He was {\i mochel} on his prerogative, and the result \u8212- more open, more honest deliberation \u8212- was in every respect beneficial. This is why Rav could have voted first in a non-capital case: Rebbi chose, as a matter of {\i anavah}, to let others lead.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Torah & Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rebbi Unique\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The implicit reference to Rebbi\u8217's {\i anavah} \u8212- and the account of his convening a {\i beis din} \u8212- leads the Gemara into an extended digression. Rabbah son of Rabbah (or Rav Hillel son of Rav Vallas) states: {\i mi-yemos Moshe v\u8217'ad Rebbi lo matzinu Torah u\u8217'gedulah bemakom echad} \u8212- from the time of Moshe Rabbeinu until Rebbi, we never find Torah greatness and worldly greatness ({\i gedula}, which Rashi explains as wealth, though one might also read it as broader societal authority and stature) combined in a single person. The claim is not that there were no great Torah scholars, nor that there were no figures of great status or wealth \u8212- but that the two were never unified in one individual to the degree they were in Rebbi. One might wonder whether this implies Rebbi was the greatest Torah scholar of his time in an absolute sense, or whether it is more a claim about the extraordinary degree of {\i both} qualities simultaneously residing in one person. The Gemara proceeds to test this claim historically.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Early Examples\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara raises a series of counterexamples. {\i Havei Yehoshua} \u8212- was Yehoshua not both a great Torah scholar (as Moshe\u8217's foremost student) and the political leader of Israel? The response: {\i havei Elazar} \u8212- Elazar was also present, a great Torah authority in his own right as Aharon\u8217's student, and therefore Yehoshua was not unchallenged in his combination of roles. The Gemara then asks: after Yehoshua\u8217's death, was not Elazar unrivaled? {\i Havei Pinchas} \u8212- Pinchas was also there. And when Elazar died and Pinchas remained, was he not uniquely combining these roles? {\i Havah zekenim} \u8212- the elders remained as political and religious authorities alongside him, a point reinforced by the repeated motif in {\i Sefer Shoftim} of {\i ein melech b\u8217'Yisrael} \u8212- suggesting that no single figure held unchallenged authority of both kinds.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A question worth raising in this context: this entire sugya seems to run counter to the principle of {\i yeridas hadoros} \u8212- the decline of generations. The response is that the claim is always relative: in any given generation, the question is whether one person towers above all others in both dimensions simultaneously, not whether they surpass figures of previous ages.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Royal & Sage\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara continues through the monarchic period. {\i V\u8217'ha Shaul} \u8212- Shaul was a king and, according to rabbinic tradition, a great Torah scholar. Response: {\i havah Shmuel}, who was the supreme religious authority of the age. But when Shmuel died while Shaul was still alive, was Shaul not then unrivaled? {\i Kulei shani ka\u8217'amina} \u8212- the claim is about someone who held this status throughout his entire career, not merely for a brief window.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i V\u8217'ha David} \u8212- David was a king and his Torah scholarship is well attested throughout {\i Masechet Brachos} and elsewhere. Response: {\i havah Ira haYairi}, who is, admittedly, a name one would not otherwise encounter, but whom rabbinic tradition regards as a great Torah scholar. This response feels somewhat weaker \u8212- Ira haYairi had no real authority comparable to David\u8217's \u8212- and Rashi limits his description simply to {\i gadol baTorah}, without addressing the {\i gedula} dimension. Again the Gemara responds that he did not hold this dual status throughout his entire career. The same pattern plays out with Shlomo: {\i havah Shimi ben Gera} \u8212- and when Shlomo had him killed, the response is again that Shlomo was not uncontested his entire reign.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i V\u8217'ha Chizkiyahu} \u8212- Chizkiyahu was a king and a great Torah scholar. Response: {\i havah Shevna}, a sage of his time. Shevna was killed, but again: {\i kulei shani ka\u8217'amina}, the whole-career qualification applies. Notably, the Gemara invokes Shevna rather than Yeshayahu as the counterweight to Chizkiyahu \u8212- why Yeshayahu is not cited here is unclear.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i V\u8217'ha Ezra} \u8212- Ezra HaSofer was both political leader and supreme Torah authority of the return from Babylonian exile. Response: {\i havah Nechemya ben Chilkiya}. With this, all the historical challenges have been answered.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Later Period\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then adds the parallel claim for the later period: {\i mi-yemos Rebbi v\u8217'ad Rav Ashi lo matzinu Torah u\u8217'gedula bimkom echad} \u8212- from Rebbi\u8217's time until Rav Ashi, the same combination was not seen, until Rav Ashi, who was evidently a man of both great Torah stature and great wealth or societal standing. The Gemara anticipates a challenge: {\i v\u8217'ha havah Huna bar Nasan}, who also seemed to combine both qualities. The response: {\i d\u8217'mikaf havah kaif lei l\u8217'Rav Ashi} \u8212- Huna bar Nasan actually submitted himself to Rav Ashi\u8217's authority, and therefore he cannot serve as a genuine counterexample to Rav Ashi\u8217's unique status.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Cheresh Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Definition Context\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now returns to the {\i mishnah}\u8217's enumeration of rabbinic {\i takkanot}, moving from the {\i sikrikon} framework toward a broader set of measures addressing individuals with diminished legal capacity. The phrase {\i tikkun olam} has by this point largely receded from the discussion \u8212- we are now in the realm of rabbinic enactments addressing changing social circumstances and specific populations, though the {\i mishnah} we are about to examine is, in scope, as broad as some of the classic {\i tikkun olam} provisions.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i cheresh} \u8212- the deaf-mute \u8212- is, as a matter of Torah law, not considered a {\i bar daas}, a person of full legal competency. As such, he cannot enter into legally binding transactions: buying and selling, {\i kiddushin}, {\i geirushin}, and so forth. He cannot discharge others\u8217' obligations such as blowing the {\i shofar}, and is not personally {\i chayav b\u8217'mitzvot} \u8212- his status is comparable in this respect to that of a minor.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 That said, it is important to note that the Gemara does contain an opinion that regards the {\i cheresh} as having a meaningful degree of {\i daas}, or at least holds that competency ought to be assessed on a case-by-case basis. Even those who classify him as lacking full {\i daas} treat him differently from a {\i shoteh} \u8212- a person categorized as mentally ill in a more fundamental sense, perhaps unable to properly interpret reality. The {\i cheresh} is understood as diminished in {\i daas} but not entirely devoid of it, and this intermediate status is precisely what makes {\i takkanot} on his behalf possible.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 An important historical observation is worth making here. One might wonder how frequently a person would be both deaf and mute. The answer is that in the pre-modern world, virtually every deaf person fell into this category \u8212- because individuals who cannot hear are generally unable to modulate their speech, and without education or training they could not speak intelligibly. The only exceptions were those who had been born hearing and later lost their hearing. As a result, nearly every deaf person was treated halachically as a {\i cheresh}. In recent centuries, as schools for the deaf developed and it became undeniable that deaf individuals can communicate with full intelligence through sign language, many {\i poskim} have revisited this categorization. Some have argued that a person communicating through sign language does not fall into the category of {\i ilemet} (mute) at all, and therefore is not a {\i cheresh} in the halachic sense. These developments lie beyond the scope of the Gemara, but they are essential background for understanding why the Gemara\u8217's enactments were designed as they were: the {\i cheresh} was understood to possess real, if imperfect, {\i daas}, enough to justify accommodating him in the normal functioning of society.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gesture Transactions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} states: {\i cheresh romez v\u8217'nirmas} \u8212- a {\i cheresh} can engage in buying and selling through gestures. If he points to an item he wants and holds out money, the transaction is valid at the rabbinic level, even though {\i d\u8217'Oraita} it would not be binding. This was enacted as a {\i takanna} precisely because of this intermediate status of the {\i cheresh}: he is not like a {\i shoteh}, and it would be untenable to deny him the ability to engage in basic commercial transactions.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ben Beteira\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i U\u8217'ven Beteira omer kofetz v\u8217'nikpatz} \u8212- Ben Beteira holds that even jumping \u8212- an action less precisely expressive than hand gestures \u8212- constitutes a sufficient signal. The {\i chachamim}, by contrast, apparently regarded jumping as too ambiguous to be reliably interpreted as communicating transactional intent.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Moveable Only\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 All of this applies only to {\i mitaltelin} \u8212- moveable property. The {\i takanna} was not extended to the purchase of land ({\i karka}). Two reasons converge: first, there was not the same pressing necessity, since the {\i takanna} for {\i mitaltelin} was grounded in the need for the {\i cheresh} to be able to function in everyday life \u8212- to buy basic provisions. Land transactions are qualitatively different in nature and not part of ordinary daily commerce. Second, land is far too weighty a matter, and the Rabbis were not willing to institute a {\i takanna} extending such capacity to a person who is not {\i d\u8217'Oraita} a full {\i bar daas}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Get Standards\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now addresses the question of the {\i get} \u8212- the divorce document. Rav Nachman explains: the dispute between Ben Beteira and the {\i chachamim} pertains to {\i mitaltelin}, as the {\i mishnah} explicitly states. When it comes to a {\i get}, by contrast, {\i divrei hakol b\u8217'remiza} \u8212- all agree that a proper hand gesture is required; jumping would not suffice.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The case of a {\i get} for a {\i cheresh} applies specifically when the man was already a {\i cheresh} when he entered into {\i kiddushin}. Since the {\i kiddushin} of a {\i cheresh} is only rabbinically valid \u8212- it is a rabbinic enactment parallel to the one permitting him to engage in transactions \u8212- the resulting marriage is dissolved through a corresponding rabbinic {\i get}. The {\i cheresh} husband can signal to the {\i sofer} his intention to write a {\i get} for his wife, and this suffices. But even in this context, which involves only rabbinic {\i kiddushin} and rabbinic divorce, the standard for gesture is higher than for {\i mitaltelin}: everyone requires clear {\i remiza}, proper hand signaling.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might have thought the opposite: since the {\i get} is only undoing a rabbinic institution, perhaps the standards would be {\i lower}, and even jumping would suffice. The Gemara therefore clarifies that this is not the case \u8212- {\i mishnah} addresses {\i mitaltelin}, and for {\i get} the standards are uniformly higher, with everyone requiring {\i remiza}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A second version ({\i ika d\u8217'amri}) of Rav Nachman\u8217's statement holds that the dispute between Ben Beteira and the {\i chachamim} applies equally to {\i mitaltelin} and to {\i gittin}. Under this reading, the {\i mishnah} mentioning {\i mitaltelin} simply addresses that context, but the dispute carries over to the {\i get} case as well, with Ben Beteira maintaining that jumping suffices even there.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Pe\u8217'utot Minors\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Age Range\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} also states that {\i pe\u8217'utot} \u8212- young children \u8212- may buy and sell {\i mitaltelin}, and such transactions are rabbinically valid. The Gemara asks: {\i v\u8217'ad kama} \u8212- until what age, or rather, down to what age? Rav Yehuda indicated to his son Rav Yitzchak: approximately six or seven years old. Rav Kahana says seven or eight. A {\i baraita} teaches nine or ten.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara resolves the apparent disagreement: {\i v\u8217'lo pligi} \u8212- these do not actually dispute one another. {\i Kol chad v\u8217'chad l\u8217'fi chorfei} \u8212- it depends on the sharpness of the individual child. There is no fixed biological age; what matters is whether the child has reached a sufficient level of understanding \u8212- knowing the value of money, understanding what a transaction means, being able to manage change appropriately. The different age ranges reflect the range of children\u8217's development rather than competing fixed thresholds.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rationale\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Why was this {\i takanna} enacted? Rav Abba bar Yaakov said in the name of Rabbi Yochanan: {\i mishum k\u8217'dei chayyav} \u8212- because of his livelihood, so that he can function in society. In earlier periods, children as young as six or seven might be apprenticed or otherwise fending for themselves to a considerable degree. The inability to execute any valid commercial transaction would be practically untenable.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara at this point also brings a passage in which the same {\i baal memra} \u8212- Rav Abba bar Yaakov in the name of Rabbi Yochanan \u8212- interprets the word {\i maltechah} (from 2 Kings 10:22: {\i vayomer la\u8217'asher al hamaltechah hotzei levush l\u8217'chol ovdei hab\u8217'aal}) as {\i davar hanimlal v\u8217'nimtach} \u8212- something spun and stretched by hand, i.e., a fine linen garment. This is connected to an account of Bunaya ben Nunaya sending Rebbi several types of extraordinarily fine fabrics \u8212- {\i sivni v\u8217'chumes} (folded to the size of a nut or half a nut), {\i salsala u\u8217'malmala} (folded to the size of a pistachio or half a pistachio) \u8212- with {\i malmala} receiving the same etymology: {\i davar hanimlal v\u8217'nimtach}, spun and stretched linen. The passage is cited here because of the shared {\i baal memra}, not because of topical relevance to the {\i pe\u8217'utot} laws.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Overpayment Limits\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If a {\i pe\u8217'utat} overpays, does the standard law of {\i ona\u8217'ah} apply? Rabbi Yochanan said in the name of Rabbi Zeira: {\i ad shtus k\u8217'gadol} \u8212- up to a sixth, as with an adult. The transaction stands but the excess must be returned; an overpayment exceeding a sixth would void the transaction, just as it would for a competent adult.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gift Validity\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye raises a further question: {\i matnaso mah} \u8212- what is the status of a gift given by a {\i pe\u8217'utat}? The {\i takanna} was enacted to allow children to participate in normal commercial life; a gift is outside that rationale, and there is greater opportunity for exploitation. Can a minor give a valid gift?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Yeimar holds that it is not a valid gift; Mar bar Rav Ashi holds that it is. When the question was transmitted to Rav Mordechai for his view, the names were accidentally reversed \u8212- it was reported to him that Mar bar Rav Ashi held gifts were invalid and Rav Yeimar held they were valid, precisely the opposite of what each had actually said.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Mordechai responded: go tell the son of my master \u8212- meaning Mar bar Rav Ashi, whose father Rav Ashi was Rav Mordechai\u8217's {\i rebbe} \u8212- don\u8217't you remember the incident? When Rav Ashi was walking up the steps to the {\i beis hamidrash} \u8212- one foot on the ground, one foot on the step \u8212- we asked him: {\i matnaso mah}, what is the law regarding a {\i pe\u8217'utat}\u8217's gift? And he told us: {\i matnaso matnah} \u8212- it is a valid gift. This applies whether it is a gift of a {\i shechiv mera} (given on a deathbed), a {\i matnas bari} (given while healthy), a large gift, or a small gift \u8212- all are valid.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This of course confirms what Mar bar Rav Ashi had actually said \u8212- that gifts are valid \u8212- rather than the reversed version that had been reported to Rav Mordechai. Tosafot notes that the Gemara does not specify whether this ruling applies to {\i mitaltelin} only or also to {\i karka}; given the context \u8212- the entire discussion of {\i pe\u8217'utot} concerns {\i mitaltelin} \u8212- it is reasonable to assume only moveable property is intended, since there was no {\i takanna} extending transactional capacity to land.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Darchei Shalom\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Aliyot Order\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} now shifts to a new organizing principle: {\i mipnei darchei shalom} \u8212- for the sake of peaceful relations and social order. Where {\i tikkun olam} addressed deep systemic inequities, {\i darchei shalom} addresses the more quotidian need to establish conventions that minimize conflict. Having a recognized way of doing things \u8212- an acknowledged order or sequence \u8212- preempts many disputes before they arise. The opening example is the {\i aliyot} order: a {\i kohen} reads first, then a {\i levi}, then a {\i Yisrael}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 There is something worth pausing over in this institution. The authentic {\i halacha} of {\i krias hatorah} is that the most accomplished Torah scholar present should receive the first {\i aliyah}. Torah study is the great equalizer \u8212- as the Gemara elsewhere teaches, a {\i mamzer talmid chacham} takes precedence over a {\i kohen gadol am ha\u8217'aretz}. The institution of {\i krias hatorah} in principle reflects this: {\i hakol olim l\u8217'minyan shivah}, even a woman, even a minor. Torah is accessible to all, and greatness in Torah supersedes hierarchies of birth.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Yet this very egalitarianism creates a practical problem: precisely because greatness in Torah is a matter of achievement rather than birth, there is no predefined, unambiguous hierarchy. Disputes over who is the greater {\i talmid chacham} are endemic. The flip side of equal access is endless contest over rank. By instituting the {\i kohen-levi-Yisrael} sequence {\i mipnei darchei shalom}, the Rabbis introduced a clear, uncontested ordering that resolves at least the initial hierarchy of honors, even at the cost of some of the pure meritocracy that {\i krias hatorah} in principle demands.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Torah Equalizer\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 As noted, while the {\i kohen-levi-Yisrael} order serves {\i darchei shalom}, the Torah itself is a domain of equal access. The priority of status applies in matters of {\i kavod}, but the very existence of Torah as the measure of worth means that a {\i talmid chacham} who is a {\i mamzer} takes precedence in Torah honor over a {\i kohen gadol} who is an {\i am ha\u8217'aretz}. This reflects a broader principle running through rabbinic Judaism: the shift from a {\i beis hamikdash}-centered hierarchy (which is about birth status, tribe, priestly lineage) to a Torah-centered hierarchy (which is about achievement and learning). The institution of {\i krias hatorah} lives in the tension between these two frameworks, and the {\i darchei shalom} ordering of {\i kohen-levi-Yisrael} is one resolution of that tension.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Eiruv & Wells\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Two further applications of {\i darchei shalom} follow. First: {\i eiruvin b\u8217'vayis yashan mipnei darchei shalom} \u8212- when an {\i eiruv chatzeros} has customarily been stored in a particular house, one should continue placing it there rather than shifting it to a different house. This preserves an established expectation and avoids disputes over whose home is used as the locus of the {\i eiruv}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Second: {\i bor she\u8217'haya karov l\u8217'ama, mitmaleh rishon mipnei darchei shalom} \u8212- if a well was situated near an irrigation canal, the owner of the well closest to the water source has the first right to fill his well from the canal. In times of scarcity, when everyone wishes to store water in their wells before the canal level drops, having a recognized principle of priority by proximity prevents conflict.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Traps Ownership\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} states: {\i metzudot chayah v\u8217'ofot v\u8217'dagim yesh bahem mishum gezel mipnei darchei shalom} \u8212- traps set for wild animals, birds, or fish are protected {\i mipnei darchei shalom}, such that if another person comes upon the trap before the trapper returns and takes the animal, it is considered a form of theft. Strictly speaking, the trapper has not yet performed a formal {\i kinyan} \u8212- he has not physically taken possession of the animal, and depending on the circumstances, the trap may not function as a {\i kinyan kli} that transfers ownership automatically. Nevertheless, the Rabbis declared that one who takes from another\u8217's trap commits a form of {\i gezel}. The reason goes beyond mere conflict avoidance: the very institution of trapping as a livelihood depends on trappers being able to rely on ownership of their catch.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yose says: {\i gezel gamur} \u8212- it is real theft. The Gemara will explain that Rabbi Yose means rabbinic theft \u8212- the Rabbis enacted that taking from the trap constitutes genuine {\i gezel} rabbinically \u8212- rather than merely establishing a convention. The difference between the {\i tanna kama} and Rabbi Yose is thus not whether the trapper has a right, but whether the Rabbis articulated that right as a true {\i gezel} or merely as a {\i darchei shalom} convention.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Lost Property\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Metziat cheresh shoteh v\u8217'katan yesh bahem mishum gezel mipnei darchei shalom} \u8212- lost objects found by a {\i cheresh}, {\i shoteh}, or minor are treated as their property for purposes of {\i darchei shalom}, even though these individuals lack the full legal capacity to effect a {\i kinyan} through finding. Taking from them what they have found is treated as {\i gezel}. Rabbi Yose again: {\i gezel gamur} \u8212- real rabbinic theft.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Olive Knocker\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Ani hamenakef berosh hazayit} \u8212- a poor person who has climbed to the top of an olive tree and knocked olives down has not yet formally acquired them, since he has not yet performed {\i hagbahah} or another recognized {\i kinyan}. If someone passes by and takes the olives lying below, {\i ma shetachtav gezel mipnei darchei shalom} \u8212- what is beneath him is considered stolen {\i mipnei darchei shalom}. Rabbi Yose: {\i gezel gamur}. The pattern is consistent throughout this section.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Non-Jewish Poor\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Ein memachin b\u8217'yad aniyei ovdei kochavim beleket shichcha u\u8217'peah mipnei darchei shalom} \u8212- one does not prevent non-Jewish poor from collecting {\i leket}, {\i shichcha}, and {\i peah} from the fields, for the sake of {\i darchei shalom}. It is important to situate this ruling within the context of what {\i darchei shalom} has meant throughout this {\i mishnah}. It is not simply about cultivating good relations with non-Jewish neighbors for self-interested reasons. {\i Darchei shalom} here expresses a value of peaceful, well-ordered society in which people\u8217's rights are respected and the vulnerable are not excluded. This reading is supported by the Rambam\u8217's use of the verse {\i deracheha darchei noam v\u8217'chol netivoteha shalom} in a related context \u8212- all the ways of the Torah are ways of pleasantness and peace.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Kohen Honor\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Biblical Sources\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now asks: {\i mina hani milei} \u8212- what is the biblical source for giving {\i kohanim} the first {\i aliyah}? Three sources are offered. Rav Mattana cites: {\i vayichtov Moshe et hatorah hazot vayitnah el hakohanim bnei Levi} \u8212- Moshe wrote the Torah and gave it to the {\i kohanim bnei Levi}. The Gemara asks the obvious: we already know that {\i kohanim} are {\i bnei Levi} \u8212- why state both? The answer: to teach precedence \u8212- first {\i kohanim}, then {\i levi}. This functions as an {\i asmachta} for the ordering in {\i krias hatorah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yitzchak Napcha cites: {\i venigshu hakohanim bnei Levi} \u8212- and the {\i kohanim bnei Levi} shall draw near. Again, the doubling of the description teaches the order of precedence. Rav Ashi cites: {\i bnei Amram Aharon uMoshe vayibadel Aharon le\u8217'hakdisho kodesh kodashim} \u8212- Aharon was separated and sanctified as {\i kodesh kodashim}, above even Moshe, who was a {\i levi}. This establishes that the {\i kohen} occupies a higher level of {\i kedushah} than the {\i levi}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Honor Rights\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Chiya bar Abba derives the principle from the {\i pasuk} {\i ve\u8217'kidashto} \u8212- you shall sanctify him \u8212- applied to the {\i kohen}, {\i lekhol davar shebikdusha}: in every matter of sanctity or honor. This manifests in three specific honors: {\i liftoach rishon} \u8212- he opens first (gives the first speech or {\i drashah}); {\i ulvarekh rishon} \u8212- he leads {\i birkat hamazon}; and {\i ulitol manah yafah b\u8217'rishon} \u8212- at a meal, he has first choice of the food. These are matters of {\i kavod} in social settings, not financial matters like dividing an estate.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 De\u8217'oraisa Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This sets up a striking exchange between Abaye and Rav Yosef. If {\i ve\u8217'kidashto} establishes a {\i d\u8217'Oraita} obligation to honor {\i kohanim} in these ways, then the {\i kohen}\u8217's precedence in {\i krias hatorah} is not merely a rabbinic convention for {\i darchei shalom} \u8212- it reflects a {\i d\u8217'Oraita} principle. Abaye asks Rav Yosef directly: {\i mipnei darchei shalom, d\u8217'Oraita?}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Yosef responds: {\i d\u8217'Oraita u\u8217'mipnei darchei shalom} \u8212- it is {\i d\u8217'Oraita}, and the Torah itself commands it {\i mipnei darchei shalom}. This is a puzzling formulation: the Torah\u8217's command to honor {\i kohanim} is rooted in their elevated {\i kedushah} and the recognition of their special role, not in {\i darchei shalom} as such.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye pushes back sharply: {\i kol hatorah kulah nami mipnei darchei shalom} \u8212- if you use that reasoning, then every {\i d\u8217'Oraita} law is {\i darchei shalom}, since {\i de\u8217'chtiv deracheha darchei noam v\u8217'chol netivoteha shalom} \u8212- the entire Torah is characterized as ways of pleasantness and peace. This is an extraordinarily powerful statement. If every Torah obligation can be described as serving {\i shalom}, then {\i darchei shalom} becomes a vacuous explanation. Abaye\u8217's point is that one cannot collapse the specific category of {\i darchei shalom} into the general theological claim that Torah promotes peace.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye therefore offers an alternative synthesis, citing what the {\i mar} \u8212- Rabbah bar Nachmani \u8212- taught. A {\i baraita} states: if two people are eating together, one waits for the other before beginning; with three, one need not wait. The one who recited {\i ha-motzi} has first access to the shared dishes, but may yield his priority to his {\i rebbe} or to someone of greater stature if he chooses. And on this Rabbah taught: {\i lo shanu ela b\u8217'seudah} \u8212- the option to waive one\u8217's {\i kavod} applies at a private meal. {\i Aval b\u8217'veit haknesset lo, de\u8217'atu le\u8217'intzuyei} \u8212- but not in {\i shul}, because it leads to fights.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 No Waiving\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The synthesis now becomes clear. The {\i d\u8217'Oraita} obligation of {\i ve\u8217'kidashto} establishes that a {\i kohen} must be honored in settings involving {\i kedushah} \u8212- and {\i krias hatorah} in {\i shul} is such a setting. That honor manifests as receiving the first {\i aliyah}. However, were the {\i kohen} permitted to waive this honor at will \u8212- to say, I am {\i mochel}, let my friend take the first {\i aliyah} \u8212- the result would be precisely the competitive\u20105 ? claiming of honor that the {\i darchei shalom} ordering was designed to prevent. People would lobby to be chosen, others would feel slighted for not being chosen, and communal harmony would break down. The rabbinic layer, then, is not the {\i kohen}\u8217's honor itself \u8212- that is {\i d\u8217'Oraita} \u8212- but the prohibition on the {\i kohen}\u8217's waiving that honor in {\i shul}. This prohibition is the {\i darchei shalom} element: it protects the institution by ensuring it remains stable and non-negotiable.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Day Distinction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Mattana adds a further refinement: the prohibition on the {\i kohen} waiving his {\i kavod} in {\i shul} applies specifically on {\i Shabbat} and {\i Yom Tov} \u8212- {\i d\u8217'shechichi rabim}, when there is a large crowd in {\i shul}. On Mondays and Thursdays, when attendance is smaller, the {\i kohen} may be {\i mochel} on his {\i kavod} without generating the {\i darchei shalom} concerns that would arise in a full public setting.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Here the daf concludes. The discussion of {\i kohanim} and {\i aliyot} and the interplay between {\i d\u8217'Oraita} honor and rabbinic {\i darchei shalom} protections will continue in the following daf.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Gittin 60\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Aliyot Order & Peace\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kohen-Levi-Yisrael\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The daf opens near the end of the {\i perek} dealing with {\i tikkun olam} and {\i darchei shalom}, before the tractate returns to the laws of {\i gittin} proper. The sugya at the bottom of 59b concerns the established order of {\i aliyot} \u8212- {\i kohen}, {\i levi}, {\i yisrael} \u8212- and the principle that this sequence exists as a function of {\i darchei shalom}, so that competition over communal honors not become a source of conflict. The Gemara acknowledges that the obligation to honor a {\i kohen} first derives from {\i de-Oraita} authority \u8212- the concept of {\i vekiddashto} \u8212- and that technically a {\i kohen} may be {\i mochel}, may waive his honor in favor of another. The Rabbis, however, instituted that he should not do so, since permitting such waivers opens the door to disputes.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shabbat vs Weekday\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Mattana introduces a qualification. The prohibition against a {\i kohen} ceding his {\i aliyah} to another applies only on {\i shabbatot} and {\i yamim tovim} \u8212- {\i deshehichi rabbim}, when the congregation is large and the potential for conflict correspondingly greater. On Tuesdays and Thursdays, however, where the minyan is typically small and intimate, the concern is less acute, and a {\i kohen} may be {\i mochel}. An important note is appended by Tosafot in the name of Rabbeinu Chananel: this distinction applied in Talmudic times when weekday attendance was sparse due to people being occupied with their work. Tosafot observes that in their own era, Tuesday and Thursday attendance could rival that of {\i Yom Tov}, and the leniency would not apply in such circumstances.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Huna Exception\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara raises an objection: we know that Rav Huna, who was not a {\i kohen}, received the {\i kohen} {\i aliyah} on {\i shabbatot} and {\i yamim tovim}. This appears to contradict Rav Mattana\u8217's rule. The Gemara resolves the difficulty: Rav Huna was categorically different. Even Rav Ami and Rav Assi \u8212- the most distinguished {\i kohanim} in {\i Eretz Yisrael} \u8212- deferred to him. His preeminence was so universally acknowledged that calling him first was not an act of waiver at all; it was simply the objective recognition of his uncontested authority. The {\i kohen}\u8217's honor was not so much conceded as it was superseded by an undisputed communal consensus.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 No Kohen Present\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Bundle Unravels\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye introduces the following tradition: {\i ein sham kohen, nisperdah hachavilah} \u8212- if no {\i kohen} is present, the bundle unravels. The entire edifice of hierarchical ordering rests on the premise that the {\i kohen} receives the first honor. Without a {\i kohen}, the structure loses its foundation, and the order collapses entirely. One might ask why, in such a case, one would not simply proceed to {\i levi} before {\i yisrael}. The answer lies in the nature of {\i vekiddashto}: the {\i mitzvah} of sanctifying and honoring applies fundamentally to the {\i kohen}. The {\i levi}, though assigned greater communal responsibility and role, does not carry the same {\i kedusha} in this regard. Without the {\i kohen}, there is no longer a basis for the hierarchy, and in principle any {\i aliyah} may be given to anyone.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 No Kohen Present (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rashi vs Rav Yitzchak\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rashi\u8217's first explanation of {\i nisperdah hachavilah} is striking: the {\i levi} loses his {\i aliyah} entirely \u8212- {\i eino korei kelal}. This is the position of {\i Mori ha-Zaken} and Rabbeinu Yitzchak ben Yehudah, and it accords with the {\i Siddur Rav Amram}. Rashi then cites a second view, from Rav Yitzchak ha-Levi, that there is simply no fixed order anymore \u8212- a {\i levi} may receive an {\i aliyah} somewhere in the middle, as may anyone else, but there is no reason to deprive the {\i levi} of an {\i aliyah} altogether.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafot Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot takes a third position: Rav Yitzchak bar Yehudah maintains that the {\i levi} receives the second {\i aliyah}. That is, a {\i yisrael} is given the first {\i aliyah} \u8212- in the slot normally reserved for {\i kohen} \u8212- and the {\i levi} then reads second, in his proper position within the remaining order. The concern is that placing a {\i levi} in the first slot too closely mimics the role of {\i kohen}, creating a blurring of boundaries that might itself carry the character of {\i pegam kehunah}. A {\i yisrael} in the first slot signals more clearly that no status claim is being made. The practical custom \u8212- giving the remaining {\i aliyot} freely to {\i yisraelim} \u8212- reflects the {\i peshat} of the Gemara, though the precise practice when a {\i levi} is present varies, and the issue remains genuinely contested.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Aliyot Order & Peace (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Levi Without Kohen\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 To summarize the three positions: first, the {\i levi} receives no {\i aliyah} at all; second, the {\i levi} may receive any {\i aliyah} in any position, with no fixed order applying to anyone; and third, the {\i levi} receives the second {\i aliyah} specifically. The simple reading of the Gemara supports the second view \u8212- once the bundle has unraveled, {\i aliyot} are given without predetermined order. A related tradition from Abaye: {\i ein sham levi, kari kohen} \u8212- if there is no {\i levi}, the same {\i kohen} who received the first {\i aliyah} reads again in the second position, since a {\i kohen} is, by lineage, also a {\i levi}. Whether this constitutes a demotion in any sense is something the Gemara proceeds to examine.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Kohen/Levi Stigma\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Double Status Rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Yochanan rules: {\i kohen achar kohen lo yikra} \u8212- one may not call up a second {\i kohen} immediately after a first {\i kohen} has already received an {\i aliyah}, because this will lead to {\i pegam} on the first: observers will assume that after the first was called, it was discovered that he was not truly a {\i kohen}, and a second genuine {\i kohen} had to be substituted. Similarly, {\i levi achar levi lo yikra} \u8212- a second {\i levi} may not be called after a first {\i levi} \u8212- but here the concern is {\i mishum pagmam shel sheneihem}, a stigma that falls on both of them, not merely on the first.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Same Person Twice\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This immediately raises the question posed above: if there is no {\i levi}, the Gemara instructs that the same {\i kohen} reads again \u8212- but does this not create the precise problem Rav Yochanan described? The Gemara resolves it: {\i ki ka\u8217'amrinan be\u8217'oto kohen} \u8212- when the same {\i kohen} reads twice, everyone understands the reason. The gabbai announces {\i ya\u8217'amod bimkom levi}, or makes it clear in some equivalent way, and there is no ambiguity. No one imagines that the first {\i aliyah} was invalid; it is simply understood that no {\i levi} is present.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Stories\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Two illustrative anecdotes bear on the practical reality of this rule. Hertzel Hefter, himself a {\i levi}, once studied this passage and was genuinely surprised to learn that the same {\i kohen} is truly called up twice \u8212- having never been in a {\i shul} when no {\i levi} was present, he had not encountered the practice. The other story involves Rabbi Weiss, who on a visit to a {\i Teimani shul} in Israel was given the {\i kohen aliyah} and discovered \u8212- unprepared \u8212- that the congregation expected him to read aloud himself. He managed to get through it. The following visit, he prepared the {\i aliyah} in advance, read it flawlessly, and immediately heard the {\i gabbai} announce: {\i ya\u8217'amod bimkom levi}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Pegam Analysis\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then probes why two {\i leviim} in succession generate a {\i pegam} on both, while two {\i kohanim} generate a {\i pegam} only on the first. The initial answer \u8212- that in both cases observers might question either individual\u8217's status \u8212- does not adequately distinguish the cases. The Gemara refines the analysis with reference to a specific scenario: suppose it is well established that the second {\i kohen}\u8217's father was genuinely a {\i kohen}. Can that not also be stipulated for the second {\i levi}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara\u8217's final answer is structural. If a {\i kohen} turns out to be a {\i chalal} \u8212- his father having married a {\i gerusha} and the son thereby losing his {\i kehunah} \u8212- he would receive a {\i yisrael aliyah}, not a {\i kohen} one. Therefore, when a second person receives the second {\i aliyah} after a {\i kohen}, there is only one available interpretation: the second person is the genuine {\i kohen} and the first was not. No observer can think the second person is a {\i chalal}, because a {\i chalal} would be receiving the {\i yisrael} slot, not the second position.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 With {\i leviim}, by contrast, the second {\i aliyah} is also the slot a {\i yisrael} would receive if the {\i levi} had been demoted or disqualified. A {\i levi} who married a {\i mamzeret} and thereby blemished his descendants\u8217' status would produce offspring who might receive a {\i yisrael aliyah}. So when two people receive what look like consecutive {\i levi} slots, observers can read it either way: the first person may have been a non-{\i kohen} substitute later replaced by the real {\i levi}, or the second person may be a demoted {\i levi} whose true status is really {\i yisrael}. The stigma thus branches in two directions and lands on both.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara further asks: who exactly are these observers whose misinterpretation we fear? Those who remain through the entire {\i kriat haTorah} would presumably see the full sequence of {\i aliyot} and understand that the first one counted. The concern therefore applies specifically to those who leave {\i shul} in the middle, {\i leyotzim}, who see the second {\i kohen aliyah} without having witnessed the complete context.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Higher Hierarchy\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Helbo Query\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The people of the Galilee sent a question to Rabbi Helbo: after {\i kohen} and {\i levi} have received their {\i aliyot}, how is the order determined among the remaining recipients? Rabbi Helbo did not have a ready answer and referred the question to Rabbi Yitzchak Nafcha.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Talmidei Chachamim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yitzchak Nafcha replied that the post-{\i levi} {\i aliyot} go first to {\i talmidei chachamim} who are actually appointed as {\i parnasim al hatzibbur} \u8212- communal religious leaders. The {\i parnas} in this context is a figure of genuine Torah authority: elsewhere the Gemara defines one who is fit to be a {\i parnas} as someone who can answer a halakhic question from any area of Jewish law immediately and without hesitation. This is the model of the city\u8217's rabbinic authority, whether or not he formally holds that position.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Full Hierarchy List\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 After the appointed {\i talmidei chachamim} come those who are {\i ra\u8217'uyim lemanotam parnasim} \u8212- scholars who are genuinely qualified for rabbinic leadership, but for whom the position is already filled. After them come the children of {\i talmidei chachamim} whose fathers are appointed as communal leaders. This introduces a notable intersection of two distinct principles: Torah scholarship, which is in theory a pure meritocracy, and inherited status, which mirrors the structure of {\i kehunah} and {\i leviyah}. The children of the religious leadership receive preferential standing \u8212- which, at a minyan where the same families appeared regularly, would have been a consistent and perhaps uncontested feature of the order.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Status vs Merit\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The hierarchy continues with the {\i rashei knesiyot} \u8212- the heads of the {\i shuls} \u8212- who in the relevant historical period appear to have occupied a civic or administrative role somewhat distinct from the rabbinic authority structure. Archaeological evidence from ancient synagogues in {\i Eretz Yisrael} confirms that synagogue leadership was not coextensive with rabbinic leadership; the {\i rosh knesset} was often a prominent communal figure whose authority derived from a different source. After the {\i rashei knesiyot}, the order is open: {\i vechol adam}, and from that point any reasonable allocation may be made. The interplay throughout is striking \u8212- a system rooted in inherited priestly status transitions, via an intermediate meritocratic hierarchy, back again to something resembling inherited communal prestige.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Chumashim & Haftarot\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chumashim Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i bnei Galila} also sent Rabbi Helbo a second question: {\i mahu likrot be-chumashin be-veit ha-knesset be-tzibbur} \u8212- may individual {\i chumashim} be used for public Torah reading? Here the term {\i chumash} does not refer to a printed codex; rather, as Rashi emphasizes, it refers to a scroll \u8212- but a scroll containing only one of the five books of the Torah rather than all five together. Each such scroll was written with the same technical requirements as a full {\i sefer Torah}, but it was only a fifth of the complete {\i chamisha chumshei Torah}. Rabbi Helbo did not know the answer, nor \u8212- when the question was referred onward \u8212- did Rabbi Yitzchak Nafcha. The question was then brought to the {\i beit midrash} for resolution.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The attempted proof came from a ruling of Rabbi Shimlai bar Nachmani in the name of Rabbi Yochanan: a {\i sefer Torah} from which one {\i yeri\u8217'ah} \u8212- one parchment section \u8212- is missing may not be read from publicly. If something that is ninety-nine percent complete is disqualified, surely a scroll containing only one-fifth of the Torah would be as well. The Gemara, however, rejects this reasoning: {\i hatam mechasar be-mileih} \u8212- in the case of the incomplete {\i sefer Torah}, something is actually missing from its contents. {\i Hacha lo mechasar be-mileih} \u8212- a complete {\i Sefer Bereishit} lacks nothing within itself; it is whole as what it is. The concern with a {\i chasar sefer Torah} is the {\i pegam} \u8212- the perception of brokenness \u8212- which differs from the case of a formally complete but partial scroll.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kavod HaTzibbur\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The matter is ultimately resolved by Rabba and Rav Yosef, who rule jointly: {\i ein korin be-chumashin be-veit ha-knesset, mishum kevod ha-tzibbur} \u8212- one may not read from individual {\i chumashim} in {\i shul} because of the dignity owed to the congregation. The {\i tzibbur} deserves the full {\i sefer Torah}, and reading from a partial scroll, however internally complete, fails to meet that standard.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rambam Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Rambam\u8217's position introduces an important nuance. In {\i Hilchot Sefer Torah} Perek 10, the Rambam lists twenty things that invalidate a {\i sefer Torah}. He concludes: if any one of these defects is present, {\i harei hu ke-chumash min ha-chumashin she-melamdim bo ha-tinokot} \u8212- it has the status of a {\i chumash} used to teach children, it lacks the {\i kedusha} of a {\i sefer Torah}, and one may not read from it publicly. The implication is that a {\i pasul sefer Torah} and a {\i chumash} occupy the same halakhic category: suitable for private study but not for public reading.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Beit Yosef\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Beit Yosef} \u8212- Rav Yosef Karo, writing in the {\i Kessef Mishneh} \u8212- draws out a critical inference from the Rambam. A responsum attributed to the Rambam states that if a {\i sefer Torah pasul} is all that is available, one may read from it and even recite a {\i beracha} over it, and that {\i geonei olam} practiced accordingly. The {\i Rashba} found this startling and suspected a textual error. The {\i Beit Yosef} defends it by arguing that the Rambam\u8217's ruling \u8212- {\i ein korin be-chumashin be-tzibbur} \u8212- applies only {\i lechatchila}, as an ideal standard. Where no alternative is available, the {\i bidieved} situation invokes the framework of {\i lo efshar}, and reading from a {\i pasul sefer Torah} is permitted after the fact.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This distinction carries direct practical consequence. If, in the middle of {\i kriat haTorah}, an error is discovered in the {\i sefer Torah} \u8212- the reader is midway through an {\i aliyah} and a letter is found to be missing \u8212- a second {\i sefer Torah} is brought, and reading resumes from the point of the error. The {\i aliyot} already completed from the {\i pasul sefer Torah} are not repeated. The basis for this ruling, as the {\i Beit Yosef} explains in {\i Shulchan Aruch}, is precisely the Rambam\u8217's position: since those {\i aliyot} were read {\i bidieved} without a valid {\i sefer Torah}, and the prohibition is {\i lechatchila}, what was done stands.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might further ask: if a community\u8217's only available {\i sefer Torah} is {\i pasul} and there is no time to repair it before {\i Shabbat}, may they read from it {\i lechatchila}? According to the {\i Beit Yosef}\u8217's reading of the Rambam, the answer would be yes. How this plays out {\i halacha le-ma\u8217'aseh} given the resistance this approach encounters in practice remains a live question. One might add that all of this discussion still operates within the framework of a {\i chumash} understood as a genuine scroll \u8212- not a printed codex, which differs in parchment, form, script, and composition in so many dimensions that whether even the Rambam\u8217's {\i bidieved} leniency would apply to it is an entirely separate question.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Haftara Books\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Haftara Prohibition\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabba and Rav Yosef further rule: {\i ha-sefer aftarta asur le-mikrei bei be-shabbat} \u8212- it is forbidden to read the {\i haftarah} from a compiled book of {\i haftarot} on {\i Shabbat}. This refers to exactly the kind of volume familiar from contemporary {\i shul} practice: a convenient collection of {\i haftarot} with the relevant {\i berachot}, organized by {\i parasha}, in large and legible print.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Out of Context Problem\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The reason is that such a book {\i lo nitan le-hikatev} \u8212- it should not have been written. The concern is distinct from the {\i sefer Torah} discussion: this is not a question of ritual fitness or {\i kavod ha-tzibbur} in the same sense. The issue is that excerpting a {\i perek} of {\i Yeshayahu} or {\i Yirmiyahu} from its full context and presenting it as a standalone unit is itself problematic. One who wishes to learn {\i Sefer Yeshayahu} should learn it from the {\i sefer} in its entirety, not from an extracted chapter. Since the object was not appropriate to write, it is not appropriate to read from publicly.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Muktzeh Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Ashi extends the ruling: since such a book may not be used for its intended purpose \u8212- reading the {\i haftarah} \u8212- it is also {\i muktzeh} on {\i Shabbat} and may not even be moved. The logic is that an item lacking any permitted use on {\i Shabbat} falls under the category of {\i muktzeh}. This implies a further dimension of concern: not merely that one should not read from it publicly, but that the very existence of the book, as a decontextualized excerpt of {\i Nach}, has a kind of problematic character even for private engagement.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Aggadata Exception\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A counterpoint is introduced immediately. Rabbi Yochanan and Reish Lakish would study from a {\i sifra de-aggadata} \u8212- a written compilation of Rabbinic stories and homilies \u8212- on {\i Shabbat}. This presents a different but parallel challenge: such a book is {\i Torah she\u8217'baal peh} in written form, and {\i Torah she\u8217'baal peh} is in principle not to be written. Yet they used it. It is tempting to ask what it meant for the transmission of aggadic traditions in their era, and whether such material existed in written form from early on or emerged only gradually. One might also note that there were established traditions of {\i Shabbat drashot} of an aggadic character \u8212- works like the {\i She\u8217'iltot} blend {\i halacha} and {\i aggadata} in a homiletical framework, and portions of {\i Bereishit Rabbah} may themselves reflect sermon traditions. Whether Rabbi Yochanan and Reish Lakish were encountering material outside their own area of expertise and therefore required a written text, or whether the oral transmission of {\i aggadata} was simply less rigorous than that of {\i halakha}, is an interesting open question.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Lo Efshar Principle\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara\u8217's resolution invokes a principle: {\i keivan de-lo efshar, eit la\u8217'asot l\u8217'Hashem heferu Toratecha} \u8212- since it is impossible otherwise, the time demands action for God\u8217's sake even at the cost of setting aside one aspect of Torah. This is the same rationale used to justify the writing down of {\i Torah she\u8217'baal peh} more broadly: the imperative to preserve Torah, when transmission by purely oral means becomes impracticable, overrides the formal prohibition against committing the oral tradition to writing.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The same logic applies, says the Gemara, to the {\i sefer haftarot}. Rashi explains: {\i she-ein kol tzibbur ve-tzibbur yachol lischir ve-likhtov nevi\u8217'im shalem} \u8212- not every community can commission and pay for complete scrolls of all the prophetic books from which {\i haftarot} are drawn. Given this practical impossibility, the compiled {\i haftarah} book \u8212- though not ideal \u8212- is permitted by the principle of {\i lo efshar}. This is precisely the rationale behind the contemporary practice. The speaker notes that he personally tries, when receiving an {\i aliyah} for {\i haftarah}, to read from a complete {\i Tanakh} rather than from a {\i haftarah} book, since that at least constitutes a {\i sefer shalem} in the relevant sense \u8212- and notes that publishers have even produced {\i Tanakhim} in which the {\i haftarah} sections are printed in larger type for exactly this purpose. Some {\i shulot}, particularly in Israel, have gone further and commissioned actual scrolls \u8212- individual {\i sefarim} of {\i Yeshayahu}, {\i Yirmiyahu}, and the other prophetic books from which {\i haftarot} are read \u8212- so that the reading is performed not merely from a {\i sefer shalem} but from a proper scroll. Whether the value of {\i shalem} applies equally to a printed book as to a scroll, and how it intersects with the contemporary reality of printed {\i chumashim} in schools, are questions the Gemara\u8217's discussion raises without fully resolving.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Megillot for Learning\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Megillah Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye poses a question to Rabba: {\i mahu le-michtav megillah le-tinok le-hitlamed bah} \u8212- is it permitted to write a {\i megillah}, a subsection of the Torah, for a child to learn from? This is narrower than the {\i chumash} discussion: not a complete book such as {\i Sefer Bereishit}, but a segment of a book \u8212- say, {\i Parashat Bereishit} alone, or an even smaller unit. The context is pedagogical rather than liturgical; the question is not about public reading but about private study. Perhaps, one might think, the considerations of {\i kavod ha-tzibbur} and ritual propriety do not apply here, and a child who will spend the year learning a single {\i parasha} should be able to do so from a self-contained text.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Megillah vs Chatumah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The question, Abaye notes, has different valences depending on how one understands the original transmission of the Torah. This introduces a fundamental debate about how the Torah was given: was it written down section by section as each passage was revealed \u8212- {\i megillah megillah nitnah} \u8212- or was the entire text preserved orally throughout the forty years and written down as a sealed, indivisible unit at the end \u8212- {\i Torah chatumah nitnah}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If one holds that Torah was given {\i megillah megillah}, perhaps individual sections have always had a kind of independent identity, and writing a subsection for a child follows from that premise. Or perhaps even on that view, once the sections were joined together \u8212- {\i keivan de-idbik, idbik} \u8212- the composition became indivisible and may no longer be broken apart. Conversely, if Torah was given {\i chatumah} as a single sealed unit, perhaps the very difficulty of study and the principle of {\i eit la\u8217'asot l\u8217'Hashem} would nonetheless permit a pedagogical excerpt. The question, in other words, is not easily resolved by appeal to either premise \u8212- it is a genuine question either way.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tannaitic Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabba responds simply: {\i ein kotvin} \u8212- you may not write such a section. The rationale flows from the view that once the Torah was joined together it is {\i keivan de-idbik, idbik} \u8212- an indivisible unit. Even where {\i eit la\u8217'asot l\u8217'Hashem} permits certain accommodations, as in the writing of {\i Torah she\u8217'baal peh}, this leniency does not extend to fragmenting {\i Torah she\u8217'bikhtav}, which is treated as sacrosanct and utterly indivisible. The Gemara then brings a {\i Beraita} framing this as a {\i tannaitic} dispute. The first position: {\i ein kotvin megillah le-tinok le-hitlamed bah} \u8212- one may not write a section of Torah for a child to learn from it. The modification: {\i ve-im da\u8217'ato le-hashlim, mutar} \u8212- but if one intends to complete the full text, it is permitted. The interpretation of this condition is that one begins with the opening {\i parasha} and continues adding to the scroll as the child advances, ultimately producing a complete {\i sefer} \u8212- a kind of living scroll that grows along with the student\u8217's learning.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Heleni Tablet\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yehuda\u8217's position is then cited: he permits writing sections of specific lengths \u8212- from {\i Bereishit} through {\i Dor ha-Mabul}, or in {\i Torat Kohanim} through {\i Vayehi Bayom ha-Shemini} \u8212- implying that at least these natural units may stand on their own. Separately, the Gemara raises the case of the golden tablet of Heleni ha-Malka. Queen Helena had a golden tablet made, inscribed with {\i Parashat Sota}, so that the {\i kohen} could consult it when writing the {\i megillat sota} for the {\i sotah} ceremony. The Gemara initially understands this as a case of a Torah section written as an independent text \u8212- but Rabbi Shimon ben Lakish in the name of Rabbi Yannai explains that the text on the tablet was written {\i be-seirugin}: not in full, but in a partial or abbreviated form, with the beginning of each {\i pasuk} written out and the remainder indicated only by {\i rashei teivot}. Rashi understands this to mean the opening words were complete while the endings were abbreviated. The point is that the tablet signaled the text rather than presenting it as a full, self-standing document. Presenting something as the actual, complete, authoritative text \u8212- such that it implicitly declares itself capable of standing alone \u8212- is precisely the problem. A text written in abbreviated or partial form avoids that declaration. This is what made the Heleni tablet acceptable.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Modern Schools\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The contemporary practice of using individual printed {\i chumashim} in schools is worth examining against these standards. The Gemara\u8217's concern was with a scroll \u8212- a {\i megillah} in the technical sense. A printed codex differs so dramatically in form, material, and script that it may constitute a categorically different object. Moreover, since {\i chumash} in the sense of a complete book of the Torah is in principle acceptable \u8212- it was the subject of the earlier discussion about public reading \u8212- a printed {\i Sefer Bereishit} may not qualify as the problematic {\i megillah} at all. There is also the {\i da\u8217'ato le-hashlim} consideration: if the school\u8217's intent is that students will eventually learn all {\i chamisha chumshei Torah}, the individual {\i chumash} may be understood as part of an ongoing, completable process. One might further note that a {\i chumash} is precisely a {\i chumash} \u8212- a fifth of the Torah \u8212- and the earlier discussion did acknowledge {\i chumashim} as a distinct category from a {\i megillah}, which is a sub-section of a {\i sefer}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Torah Transmission\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Yochanan vs Reish Lakish\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The debate about how the Torah was given is now examined directly. Rabbi Yochanan, citing Rabbi Banna\u8217'ah, holds: {\i Torah megillah megillah nitnah} \u8212- the Torah was given section by section. His prooftext is from {\i Tehillim}: {\i Az amarti hinei vati be-megillat sefer katuv alai} \u8212- \u8220"Then I said, behold I come with a scroll-book written about me.\u8221" The text calls it a {\i megillat sefer}, a scroll or sectioned book, and is understood as referring to the Torah \u8212- implying that the Torah is a composition of {\i megillos}, distinct sections. Reish Lakish disagrees: {\i Torah chatumah nitnah} \u8212- the Torah was given as one sealed, indivisible unit. His prooftext is from the end of {\i Devarim}: {\i Lakoch et Sefer ha-Torah ha-zot} \u8212- \u8220"Take this Sefer Torah,\u8221" the singular {\i sefer} pointing to one complete and unified document.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Prooftexts\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara works through the counter-prooftexts in both directions. How does Rabbi Yochanan handle {\i Lakoch et Sefer ha-Torah}? {\i Hahu le-vatar de-idbik} \u8212- that verse refers to after the sections were assembled and joined together; by the end they formed one {\i sefer}, but that does not speak to how the text was originally transmitted. And how does Reish Lakish handle {\i megillat sefer}? {\i Chol ha-Torah kullah ikri megillah} \u8212- the word {\i megillah} can refer to the Torah as a whole, not to any particular section within it. He adduces a verse from {\i Zechariah}: {\i va-yomer elai mah attah ro\u8217'eh va-omar ani ro\u8217'eh megillah afah} \u8212- \u8220"And he said to me, what do you see? And I said, I see a floating scroll.\u8221" There the word {\i megillah} clearly refers to a single whole scroll rather than a section of a larger work.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Eight Parshiyot\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Yet Reish Lakish concedes something to the structure of Rabbi Levi\u8217's tradition: {\i shemonah parshiyot ne\u8217'emru be-yom she-hukam bo ha-Mishkan} \u8212- eight {\i parshiyot} were communicated on the day the {\i Mishkan} was erected. These are: {\i Parshat Kohanim} \u8212- the laws of priestly status ({\i Emor el ha-kohanim}); {\i Parshat Leviyim} \u8212- the role of the {\i Leviyim} in the {\i Mishkan}; {\i Parshat Tme\u8217'im} \u8212- the laws of impurity (Rashi understands this as the narrative of those who were {\i tamei le-nefesh adam} and could not bring the {\i korban Pesach}; Tosafot reads it as the broader section of {\i tumat meit}, {\i tumas sheretz}, and {\i tumas neveilah}); {\i Parshat Shiluach Tme\u8217'im} \u8212- the {\i mitzvah} of sending the impure out of the camp; {\i Parshat Acharei Mot} \u8212- concerning {\i Yom Kippur}, understood by Rashi as connected to the tragedy of {\i b\u8217'nei Aharon} and the commandment limiting entry to the {\i kodesh}; {\i Parshat Shituyei Yayin} \u8212- the prohibition against entering the sanctuary in a state of intoxication, also linked to the death of {\i b\u8217'nei Aharon}; {\i Parshat Neirot} \u8212- the lighting of the {\i Menorah} ({\i Be-ha\u8217'alotecha}); and {\i Parshat Parah} \u8212- the laws of the {\i parah adumah}, necessary because the people needed a means of purification in order to enter the {\i Mishkan}. These eight sections, though dispersed across different locations in the Torah, were communicated together on a single occasion. Reish Lakish may acknowledge that these particular sections had a kind of distinct identity and communicative moment, while still maintaining that the Torah as a written text was given as one sealed unit.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Written vs Oral (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Elazar View\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A further debate concerns the relative proportion of {\i Torah she\u8217'bikhtav} and {\i Torah she\u8217'baal peh}. Rabbi Elazar holds: {\i Torah rov be-chsav u-miut al peh} \u8212- the majority of Torah is written, a minority is oral. His prooftext: {\i Echtov lo rubei Torati} \u8212- \u8220"I will write for him the majority of my Torah\u8221" \u8212- and {\i kemo zar nechshavu}, taken by Rashi to mean \u8220"they have treated it like something foreign,\u8221" i.e., they have not upheld the written Torah. The verse thus asserts that it is the {\i written} dimension that constitutes the majority.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Yochanan View\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yochanan disagrees: {\i rov al peh, u-miut be-chsav} \u8212- the majority of Torah is {\i Torah she\u8217'baal peh}, and only a minority is in written form. His prooftext: {\i ki al pi ha-devarim ha-eileh} \u8212- \u8220"by the mouth of these things,\u8221" meaning the oral tradition is the primary medium of transmission. He handles Rabbi Elazar\u8217's verse by reading it as a rhetorical question with an exclamatory tone: {\i Echtov lo rubei Torati?} \u8212- could I possibly write down the majority of my Torah? {\i Halo kemo zar nechshavu} \u8212- that would be an extraordinary, even absurd, claim. He thus denies that the verse asserts what Rabbi Elazar took from it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rashi Interpretation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The question as it stands seems almost unanswerable in its plain sense: there are roughly seventy volumes of Rabbinic literature against five books of the written Torah. On any raw count, the oral tradition is vastly larger. Rashi\u8217's interpretation resolves the difficulty ingeniously. The debate is not about sheer volume at all. The question is {\i from where} the content of {\i Torah she\u8217'baal peh} is derived. How much of {\i Torah she\u8217'baal peh} has its ultimate source in, and is derivable from, {\i Torah she\u8217'bikhtav} \u8212- through the mechanisms of {\i midrash}, {\i derashah}, textual analysis \u8212- versus how much exists independently of {\i Torah she\u8217'bikhtav}, whether as {\i halacha le-Moshe mi-Sinai} or as tradition not anchored in a {\i pasuk}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Derivation Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This maps onto a question that occupies scholars of {\i Midrashei Halacha}: are the Sages genuinely {\i deriving} their halakhic conclusions from close reading of the text \u8212- that is, they did not know the {\i halacha} and went to the {\i psukim} to determine it \u8212- or did they begin with a received tradition and subsequently find support for it in the {\i pasuk}? If the former, the majority of {\i Torah she\u8217'baal peh} is ultimately rooted in {\i Torah she\u8217'bikhtav}; if the latter, the majority exists independently of what is written. Rabbi Elazar holds that the derivational source of most {\i halacha} is textual; Rabbi Yochanan holds that the tradition is primary and the textual connections secondary. This is a remarkable dispute about the epistemological structure of {\i Torah she\u8217'baal peh} itself.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Written vs Oral (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Separate Genres\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yehuda bar Nachmani, the {\i meturgeman} \u8212- the formal announcer \u8212- of Reish Lakish, expounds the following {\i derasha}. One verse says {\i Ktov lecha et ha-devarim ha-eileh} \u8212- \u8220"Write these words for yourself.\u8221" Another says {\i ki al pi ha-devarim ha-eileh} \u8212- \u8220"by the mouth of these things.\u8221" The juxtaposition generates a principle: {\i devarim she-bi-chsav ee attah rashai le-omran be-al peh} \u8212- that which is written may not be transmitted orally, and {\i devarim she-be\u8217'al peh ee attah rashai le-omran bi-chsav} \u8212- that which is oral may not be committed to writing. Each genre must remain in its proper domain.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The power of this principle is not merely procedural. There is something theologically significant about the {\i Torah she\u8217'bikhtav} being fixed, concrete, and immutable \u8212- written in stone, as it were \u8212- while the {\i Torah she\u8217'baal peh} remains alive in the relationship between {\i rebbe} and {\i talmid}, transmitted by voice and internalized through engagement. Writing {\i Torah she\u8217'bikhtav} anchors it; the orality of {\i Torah she\u8217'baal peh} preserves its capacity for development, application, and response to circumstance. To collapse the boundary \u8212- to turn the written into an oral tradition or to freeze the oral in writing \u8212- would distort the nature of each.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 There is a related tension worth noting: it is sometimes said that the Rabbis may have been pushing back against sectarian groups \u8212- such as those behind the Dead Sea Scrolls \u8212- who produced {\i pesharim}, written interpretive texts on {\i Torah she\u8217'bikhtav}. One might argue that writing down {\i derashot} directly tied to the text gave those interpretations a textual authority they did not deserve. If the {\i derashot} are presented in written form alongside {\i Torah she\u8217'bikhtav}, they may be mistaken for the original text itself \u8212- or at least accorded a similar standing. The school of Rabbi Yishmael\u8217's distinction, discussed next, addresses this very boundary.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Drasha vs Halacha\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The school of Rabbi Yishmael taught: {\i eileh} \u8212- referring to the written {\i devarim} \u8212- {\i attah kotev, ve-ee attah kotev halakhot}. You may write {\i derashot} \u8212- the homiletical and exegetical engagements directly linked to the written text \u8212- but you may not write {\i halakhot}, the bottom-line legal rulings of the oral tradition. The {\i halakhot} as a genre \u8212- the kind of material later crystallized in the {\i Mishnah} \u8212- must remain oral. This represents resistance to codification: not merely to writing, but to the concretization of {\i halacha} into a fixed, authoritative written document. The {\i Mishnah} itself, it is worth recalling, was not published in written form but transmitted orally; its eventual writing down was itself an act of {\i eit la\u8217'asot l\u8217'Hashem}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The distinction the school of Rabbi Yishmael draws may also reflect a different concern. {\i Derashot} are explicitly in conversation with {\i Torah she\u8217'bikhtav} \u8212- they are anchored in and point toward the text. Writing them alongside the text preserves their derivative status. {\i Halakhot}, by contrast, are presented as self-standing rulings without explicit textual reference. Writing them could make them appear equivalent in authority to the written Torah, or could give the false impression that human legal reasoning has the same standing as divine revelation. Keeping them oral preserves their character as {\i Torah she\u8217'baal peh} \u8212- transmitted, received, and applied through an ongoing human relationship with the tradition.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Covenant & Oral\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The sugya concludes with a statement of Rabbi Yochanan that is at once a legal observation and a theological declaration: {\i lo karat ha-Kadosh Baruch Hu berit im Yisrael ella bishvil devarim she-be\u8217'al peh} \u8212- the Holy One, Blessed be He, made His covenant with Israel only by virtue of {\i Torah she\u8217'baal peh}. The prooftext: {\i ki al pi ha-devarim ha-eileh karati ittecha berit ve-et Yisrael} \u8212- \u8220"for by the mouth of these things have I made a covenant with you and with Israel.\u8221" Even if {\i Torah she\u8217'bikhtav} is immutable, fixed, and inviolable \u8212- the bedrock of the divine communication \u8212- the {\i brit}, the living covenantal relationship between God and the Jewish people, is constituted through {\i Torah she\u8217'baal peh}: through the ongoing human engagement with interpretation, derivation, and application. The process of {\i Torah she\u8217'baal peh} \u8212- its development, its transmission, its responsiveness to history \u8212- is not secondary to the covenant but is itself the very medium through which the covenant is enacted and sustained. On this striking note, the {\i daf} concludes.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Gittin 61\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Eruv & Reputation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Eruv Placement\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The daf opens in the middle of Gittin 61b, returning to the halachic portion of the Mishnah and its discussion of {\i darkei shalom} \u8212- the principle of maintaining peaceful relations. The Mishnah states: {\i me\u8217'arvin b\u8217'bayit yashan mipnei darkei shalom} \u8212- one places the {\i eruv} in the established house, the house in which it has been kept until now, because of the ways of peace. The Gemara then asks: {\i mai ta\u8217'ama darkei shalom} \u8212- what precisely is the {\i darkei shalom} rationale here? If we suppose it is a matter of {\i kavod} \u8212- of honor, that the person in whose house the {\i eruv} is stored would feel slighted were it moved \u8212- we can challenge this from a case involving a {\i shippura}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shippura Definition\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara introduces the {\i shippura} that was kept in the house of Rav Yehuda and subsequently moved as the generations changed. Rashi explains that the {\i shippura} was a shofar blown to warn the public of the approaching Shabbat. Tosafot, citing one of the Geonim, offers an alternative reading: the {\i shippura} was a shofar-shaped charity box \u8212- a vessel with a narrow slot at the top and a wide base \u8212- used to collect donations for the students of the yeshiva. The Geonic source indeed reflects the reality of that era, when such collections were essential for the maintenance of the great academies.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shippura Succession\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara traces the movement of the {\i shippura}: first it was kept in the house of Rav Yehuda, then in the house of Rabba, then Rav Yosef, then Abaye, and finally Rava. Rashi explains the succession in terms of the passing of each {\i rosh yeshiva} of Pumbedita: when one died and the next rose to lead the academy, the {\i shippura} was transferred to his household. Tosafot raises a geographic difficulty: Rav Yehuda was in Pumbedita while Rava was in Mechuza \u8212- these are different cities. Tosafot resolves this by suggesting that the {\i shippura} followed whoever held the position of primary {\i rosh mesivta}, wherever he happened to reside, since it was understood communally that the fund traveled with the leading authority and was his to distribute to the students.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Suspicion Rationale\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara concludes that the {\i darkei shalom} rationale for keeping the {\i eruv} in its established location is not {\i kavod} but rather {\i chashdah} \u8212- suspicion. Rashi explains: people are accustomed to seeing the {\i eruv} kept in a particular house. If it is moved and someone enters that house and does not find it there, they may assume the {\i eruv} was never made, and suspect the entire courtyard of carrying without an {\i eruv}. It is not an issue of dishonoring the original host; it is the concern for generating ill repute among the members of the {\i mavoy}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot formulates the suspicion differently. According to Rabbeinu Tam, the concern is that if the {\i eruv} is removed from one house and placed in another, people will suspect that the original host can no longer be trusted \u8212- that he was suspected of stealing from the communal {\i eruv} chest. That is the reputational harm {\i darkei shalom} seeks to prevent. Tosafot then asks: if we are worried about such suspicion, how much more so should we be worried about moving the {\i tzedakah} box, where suspicion of misappropriation would be even more natural? Tosafot answers that everyone understands the charity fund follows the {\i rosh mesivta}, and so its movement from one leading scholar\u8217's home to the next raises no suspicion \u8212- it is a known and accepted practice.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Water Rights Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Well & Canal\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara next takes up the Mishnah\u8217's case of {\i bor she-haya karov le-amah} \u8212- a well that is situated near a water channel \u8212- and the question of who has priority to draw from that channel first. The underlying dispute concerns which landowners along a waterway have preferential rights to its water.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav vs Shmuel\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara introduces a formal dispute: {\i beni nahara} \u8212- the people of the river. Rav says {\i tata\u8217'ei shatu maya be-reisha} \u8212- those at the bottom of the river have the first right to draw water. Shmuel says {\i ila\u8217'ei shatu maya be-reisha} \u8212- those at the top have priority.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara clarifies that when water flows freely and there is no concern of it running out \u8212- or when people are simply drawing from the moving river without damming it \u8212- there is no dispute; everyone may use the water freely. The debate is specifically about {\i be-miskar ve-ashkoyei} \u8212- the act of damming the river in order to irrigate one\u8217's fields from the pooled water. Who has the right to dam it up first?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Shmuel maintains that those upstream have priority, reasoning {\i de-amri anan mekarvinan tfei} \u8212- they are closer to the water\u8217's source and thus have a stronger natural claim. Rav disagrees, ruling in favor of those downstream: {\i nahara ke-fashtei leizil} \u8212- the river must be allowed to run its natural course. The phrase {\i ke-fashtei}, meaning \u8220"as it spreads,\u8221" connects to the well-known expression {\i nahara nahara u-fashtei} \u8212- each river and its tributaries \u8212- used to acknowledge the legitimacy of varying practices. Rav\u8217's position is that you cannot stop the river at its upper reach before it has fed into the tributaries along the way; only after the river has done that may those downstream claim the right to dam it. Rashi elaborates: as the river descends it branches into tributaries, and the people upstream argue they must act first because by the time the water has spread into all those branches, little will remain. The people downstream counter that it is unjust to stop the flow before all those branches have been fed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shmuel\u8217's Reading\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having stated their own positions, the Gemara now invokes the Mishnah: {\i bor ha-karov le-amah mitmalei rishon mipnei darkei shalom} \u8212- the well closest to the channel fills first because of {\i darkei shalom}. This would seem to favor Rav, since proximity to the channel would imply priority for those upstream. But {\i targumah Shmuel aliba de-Rav} \u8212- Shmuel, to his credit, interprets this Mishnah in a way that is consistent with Rav\u8217's position even though it appears to contradict his own. The Mishnah, Shmuel explains, is dealing with {\i amah ha-malechet al pi boro} \u8212- a channel that runs directly along the mouth of the well, such that the well fills naturally from the water\u8217's flow without any damming at all. Rashi: {\i she-mitmalei me-eilav ke-derech hillucho} \u8212- it fills on its own as the water runs its course. In such a case neither Rav nor Shmuel has anything to say; the water simply overflows into the adjacent well with no act of diversion.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara raises an objection: if that is so, what is the novelty of the ruling? What is the {\i chiddush}? The Gemara answers: one might have thought the well\u8217's owner is obligated to seal or cover his well so as to allow more water to flow downstream to those further along. The Mishnah tells us he is not required to do so. If water naturally flows into his well, he is permitted to allow it. This, however, says nothing about the separate question of whether one may actively dam the river \u8212- and it is only in that context that the dispute between Rav and Shmuel applies.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Might Makes Right\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Since neither Rav nor Shmuel\u8217's position was definitively ruled upon, Rav Huna bar Tachalifa states: {\i kol d\u8217'alim gavar} \u8212- whoever is stronger prevails. This is, as the Gemara implicitly acknowledges, the antithesis of {\i darkei shalom}. Rather than establishing orderly norms for communal resource allocation \u8212- perhaps alternating months or some other equitable arrangement \u8212- the outcome is effectively \u8220"go fight it out.\u8221" It is both ironic and troubling that the discussion of {\i darkei shalom} ends at this juncture with a principle that is precisely the opposite of peaceful coexistence.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Shimi\u8217's Story\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then relates the story of Rav Shimi bar Ashi, which illustrates how {\i kol d\u8217'alim gavar} can be exploited. Rav Shimi bar Ashi came before Abaye and asked him to learn with him. Abaye replied that he had only his own study time left and could not spare it for teaching. Rav Shimi then asked about learning at night, and Abaye said he used his nights to water his fields. Rav Shimi offered a deal: he would water Abaye\u8217's fields during the day if Abaye would teach him at night. Abaye agreed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 To fulfill his end of the bargain conveniently, Rav Shimi went to the people upstream and told them the ruling was {\i tata\u8217'ei shatu be-reisha} \u8212- those downstream have priority. He then went to the people downstream and told them the ruling was {\i ila\u8217'ei shatu be-reisha} \u8212- those upstream have priority. Each group, deferring to his halakhic authority, held back from damming the river, whereupon Rav Shimi dammed it first himself and irrigated Abaye\u8217's fields.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 When Rav Shimi returned to Abaye, Abaye confronted him: {\i k\u8217'bei trei avadt li} \u8212- you issued contradictory rulings on my behalf? This attribution to me is entirely inappropriate. As a consequence, Abaye refused to eat the produce of those fields for the entire year. Tosafot, sensing the need to explain how Rav Shimi could have done something so manifestly unjust, suggests he reasoned that since the law was {\i kol d\u8217'alim gavar} \u8212- a total free-for-all \u8212- any stratagem was equally valid. But Abaye clearly did not accept that justification.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Beni Becharmach\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara closes this section with the case of the {\i bnei Becharmach} \u8212- the people of that locale. They dug a canal diverting the river {\i Shanvata} so that it would flow around their fields and then return to the river\u8217's lower course, thereby ensuring their fields received the lion\u8217's share of the water. The people upstream, whose water supply was now cut off, came before Abaye complaining: {\i ka-matzli lana nahara} \u8212- they are destroying our river.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye first suggested that they dig the channel deeper, perhaps allowing the water to flow below and reach them more readily; he acknowledged not fully understanding the mechanics but supposed it might help. They replied that digging deeper would lower the water level relative to their orchards and the fruit would dry out. Abaye then said {\i lizlu ve-lisalku nafshaikhu meihatam} \u8212- go and remove yourselves from there. The question is to whom this was addressed. Rashi reads it as directed at the {\i bnei Becharmach}, telling them they must remove their illegal diversion. But Tosafot reads it as addressed to the complainants \u8212- Abaye was telling them he could not help them, since the rule was {\i kol d\u8217'alim gavar}. This is a deeply unsatisfying conclusion. The entire {\i sugya} has been framed around {\i darkei shalom}, yet it ends with an outcome that encourages precisely the kind of deceptive and unjust behavior \u8212- and the sense of grievance \u8212- that {\i darkei shalom} was meant to prevent.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Property & Possession\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Traps & Possession\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara continues with further applications of {\i darkei shalom} in the domain of property: {\i metzudot chaya ve-ofot ve-dagim yesh bahem mishum gezel mipnei darkei shalom} \u8212- if one\u8217's animal traps catch something, taking what is in them constitutes a form of theft, but only {\i mipnei darkei shalom}, since full legal possession has not yet been acquired. Rabbi Yossi goes further, ruling it is actual theft. The Gemara asks how this can be real theft if the owner of the trap has not legally acquired the animal.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Vessels vs Hooks\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara distinguishes between types of traps. When it comes to {\i ozlei u-oharei} \u8212- nets and wicker baskets used for fishing \u8212- everyone agrees there is no debate about {\i darkei shalom} at all; the catch is actually owned by the trap\u8217's owner through {\i kinyan kli}, since the animal is fully enclosed within a vessel belonging to that person. The debate arises specifically with regard to {\i lechi ve-kukari} \u8212- hooks or spiked barriers against which fish or animals become caught without being contained. In such cases there is no {\i kinyan kli}, and taking what is caught there is prohibited only {\i mipnei darkei shalom}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If Rabbi Yossi holds it is {\i gezel gamur} \u8212- actual theft \u8212- Rav Chisda clarifies that he means {\i gezel gamur mi-divreikhem}, real theft by rabbinic enactment rather than biblical law, since genuine acquisition cannot occur through a trap of this type. The practical difference between the two positions \u8212- {\i darkei shalom} versus {\i gezel midrabanan} \u8212- is whether a beit din can compel the return of what was taken. If the prohibition is merely one of {\i darkei shalom}, it is a behavioral norm without enforceable judicial consequence; if it is {\i gezel midrabanan}, a court may order restitution.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Olive Tree Story\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Similarly, a poor person perched atop an olive tree knocking down olives has not yet taken possession of them. This is prohibited only {\i mipnei darkei shalom}. However, the Gemara cites a {\i baraita}: if he plucked the olives with his hand, that is {\i gezel gamur} \u8212- genuine theft \u8212- because he has physically taken possession. Tosafot points out that this seems obvious and asks what the novelty is. Tosafot answers that the case involves someone who picked the olive off the branch with his hand but did not hold it in his palm \u8212- he simply pulled it from the tree and let it fall. One might have thought that since the olive never rested in his hand, there was no act of {\i kinyan}. The {\i baraita} teaches that even so, the act of {\i tlisha} \u8212- detaching it from the tree \u8212- constitutes sufficient acquisition, and it is {\i gezel gamur} at the biblical level.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Dates Story\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then relates a story involving Rav Kahana. He was traveling and observed a man throwing palm branches up into a date palm, causing dates to fall. According to Rashi, the man was hurling the branches like javelins to knock down the dates. Rav Kahana went over and ate the dates, reasoning the man had not yet taken possession of them. The man then challenged him: {\i chazi mar de-vi-yadash dasin hu} \u8212- look, I knocked those down with my own hands. Whether the man was claiming {\i gezel gamur} or merely {\i darkei shalom} is not fully clear.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Kahana responded: {\i m\u8217'atra de-Rav Yoshiya at} \u8212- you must be from the place where Rav Yoshiya teaches. He quoted the verse: {\i v\u8217'tzaddik yesod olam} \u8212- the righteous is the foundation of the world \u8212- implying that Rav Yoshiya\u8217's teachings had properly shaped this man\u8217's halakhic sensibility.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot offers a different reading of the story. In Tosafot\u8217's version, the man was not hurling branches to knock down dates but was cutting down the palm branches from atop the tree while the dates fell accidentally. Rav Kahana assumed the man was interested only in the branches and not the dates, so that the falling dates were {\i hefker} and he could take them. The man then corrected him: I am also shaking the tree in order to bring down the dates, so they are mine, and the {\i darkei shalom} prohibition applies.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Non-Jewish Poor\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Braita Text\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara turns to another dimension of {\i darkei shalom}: obligations toward non-Jews. The Mishnah states: {\i ein memachin be-yad aniyei nochrim be-leket shikha u-feah mipnei darkei shalom} \u8212- one does not prevent the non-Jewish poor from collecting {\i leket}, {\i shikha}, and {\i peah} because of {\i darkei shalom}. This is a relatively passive form of {\i darkei shalom}: one does not proactively give but simply does not obstruct.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then quotes a {\i baraita}: {\i mefarnesin aniyei nochrim im aniyei Yisrael} \u8212- one sustains the poor of the non-Jews together with the poor of Israel; {\i u-mevakrin cholei nochrim im cholei Yisrael} \u8212- one visits the sick of the non-Jews together with the sick of Israel; {\i ve-kovrin metei nochrim im metei Yisrael} \u8212- one buries the dead of the non-Jews together with the dead of Israel; all of this {\i mipnei darkei shalom}. The word for non-Jews in the {\i baraita} is {\i nochrim} rather than {\i Akum}, signaling that this applies to all non-Jews without distinction.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rashi\u8217's Reading\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A number of questions arise immediately. First, what does the word {\i im} \u8212- \u8220"with\u8221" or \u8220"together\u8221" \u8212- mean? Does it imply burying non-Jews in the same cemetery as Jews? Rashi rules it does not. Rashi explains that {\i im} means in the context of the same communal effort: if one is engaged in an effort of burying the dead and there are both Jews and non-Jews involved, one attends to both. Similarly, if one is feeding the poor, one includes the non-Jewish poor in that same effort. The primary obligation is oriented toward {\i Yisrael}, but one does not exclude the non-Jewish poor encountered in the same circumstances. This reading is careful and perhaps measured \u8212- it does not assert an independent primary obligation but rather a policy of inclusion within efforts already directed toward the Jewish community.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The question of what {\i darkei shalom} means in this context is itself significant. The most self-serving reading would be that we treat non-Jews well so they will treat us well \u8212- a kind of {\i mipnei eiva} logic. But it is worth noting that the {\i darkei shalom} of earlier portions of the chapter was not self-serving in that narrow sense. When it came to establishing priority for {\i aliyot} or setting norms for the {\i eruv}, the point was to build a society and community with less conflict, to establish conventions that allowed people to live together better. One can read the obligation toward the non-Jewish poor in the same light: {\i darkei shalom} as the cultivation of a broader peaceful social order, not merely an instrumental calculation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rambam View\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Maimonides\u8217' approach to this passage is striking. Recall that at the beginning of the broader {\i sugya} on {\i darkei shalom}, the Gemara considered whether honoring the {\i kohen} was biblical or only {\i darkei shalom}, and ultimately stated that the entire Torah is {\i mipnei darkei shalom} \u8212- for it says {\i dracheha darchei noam ve-chol netivoteha shalom}, its ways are ways of pleasantness and all its paths are peace. Rambam, when codifying the obligation to sustain the non-Jewish poor {\i mipnei darkei shalom}, invokes precisely this framework: {\i vaharei ne\u8217'emar tov Hashem la-kol ve-rachamav al kol ma\u8217'asav} \u8212- for it is said that God is good to all and His mercies extend over all His creatures \u8212- {\i ve-ne\u8217'emar dracheha darchei noam ve-chol netivoteha shalom}. For Rambam, {\i mipnei darkei shalom} is not a pragmatic accommodation but an expression of the core moral values of the Torah. Acting in accordance with {\i darkei shalom} means acting in accordance with God\u8217's own attribute of {\i rachamim} over all creation, and living a life shaped by Torah values of care and concern for others.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Reciprocity Theme\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A further dimension of this passage was noted by Suzanne Stone, who observed that the logic of {\i darkei shalom} is often presented as unidirectional: we are generous to non-Jews so they will be generous to us. But she proposed reversing the framing entirely: because of all that we receive from living within a broader society \u8212- all that we benefit from our participation in a larger human community \u8212- what are our reciprocal obligations toward that society? {\i Darkei shalom}, on this reading, is not primarily self-interested but arises from an awareness of what we owe to those alongside whom we live. The emphasis shifts from instrumental self-protection to genuine social responsibility. This is a considerably more demanding and more morally compelling reading, and it is worth appreciating that when the Gemara presents this principle it may have precisely this richer meaning in view.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosefta Version\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Tosefta} on this subject \u8212- in chapter 3, section 18 \u8212- goes considerably further than the Mishnah. It opens with the striking ruling that in a city containing both Jews and non-Jews, the {\i parnasim} \u8212- the communal charity collectors \u8212- collect from both Jews and {\i ovdei kokhavim} because of {\i darkei shalom}. This is not merely a case of not excluding non-Jews from what one is already doing for Jews; it is a statement of shared communal responsibility. The non-Jews are taxed alongside the Jews for communal welfare purposes. The {\i Tosefta} then lists the same obligations \u8212- feeding the poor, eulogizing and burying the dead, comforting mourners \u8212- but notably does not include the phrase {\i im Yisrael} in each case. The formulations stand independently, without the qualifier that limits the obligation to circumstances in which one is already acting on behalf of Jews. The {\i Tosefta} thus projects a more expansive and more egalitarian vision of {\i darkei shalom} than does the Mishnah. It is worth noting that the {\i Tosefta} was composed in Eretz Yisrael, as was the Mishnah \u8212- yet Rabbi Yehuda HaNasi chose in the Mishnah to articulate a considerably more limited version of this principle than the {\i Tosefta} contains.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Lending & Suspicion\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shemitah Lending\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara returns to further applications of {\i darkei shalom}, now examining when one is permitted to lend tools or otherwise assist someone suspected of violating religious law. The Mishnah rules: {\i mash\u8217'elet ishah le-chavertah ha-chashudah al ha-shevi\u8217'it nafah ve-kevar ve-reichayim ve-tannur} \u8212- a woman may lend her neighbor who is suspected of disregarding the laws of {\i shemitah} a sieve, a colander, a millstone, and an oven, despite the fact that such neighbor may be using them to process {\i shemitah} produce she should not have. {\i Aval} \u8212- however \u8212- she may not actively grind or sift together with her, since that would cross the line into {\i mesayea yedei oveir aveirah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot brings into the discussion the parallel {\i sugya} in Avodah Zarah regarding {\i lifnei iver}. The question is how one can lend tools to someone when one knows, or strongly suspects, they will use them for a prohibited purpose. Tosafot notes that if there were genuine doubt about the intended use, no {\i darkei shalom} argument would be needed \u8212- the lending would be permitted in any case since the item might be used legitimately. Therefore, Tosafot concludes, the Mishnah must be dealing with a case where one is fairly certain the neighbor will use the tools for {\i shemitah} produce. This raises the difficulty: if so, why is it not {\i lifnei iver}? Tosafot does not fully resolve this, but one possible answer \u8212- developed by Tosafot in other contexts \u8212- is that since the borrower could obtain the same items from someone else, the lender is not the direct enabler of the violation. This principle became a major basis in the medieval period for permitting Jews to sell certain items to non-Jews: since the non-Jew could acquire the item from another source, the Jewish seller was not the cause of the prohibition. The Mishnah\u8217's ruling, then, permits lending but draws the line at active participation in the processing.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Am Ha\u8217'aretz Wife\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Mishnah continues with a further case: the wife of a {\i chaver} \u8212- someone meticulous about {\i terumos u-ma\u8217'asros} and {\i tumah ve-taharah} \u8212- may lend to the wife of an {\i am ha-aretz} a sieve, a colander, and a millstone, and may actively sort, grind, and sift together with her. Yet once water has been added to the flour \u8212- once dough is being prepared \u8212- she may not continue to work with her, because {\i ein machzikin yedei ovrei aveirah} \u8212- we do not strengthen the hands of those who transgress. {\i Machzik} implies more than merely enabling; it means actively encouraging or affirming the transgression by one\u8217's participation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This {\i halakha} immediately invites a comparison with the previous case. There, you were not permitted to actively grind together with the {\i shemitah} suspect; here you are. What is the distinction?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Abaye\u8217's Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye answers: {\i rov ammei ha-aretz me\u8217'asrin hen} \u8212- the majority of {\i ammei ha-aretz} do in fact tithe their produce. The concern about {\i tevel} is therefore addressed by the principle of {\i rov}: we can assume, probabilistically, that the flour she is working with has been properly tithed. Since the risk of enabling a {\i terumos u-ma\u8217'asros} violation is low enough to be set aside by majority, {\i darkei shalom} can override it. But once water touches the grain and it becomes dough, it becomes {\i chayav be-challah}, and if the {\i am ha-aretz} wife handles it in a state of {\i tumah}, she renders the {\i challah} portion {\i tamei} \u8212- and that is a more sensitive concern that cannot be overridden by {\i darkei shalom}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava\u8217's Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava offers a different explanation. The {\i am ha-aretz} of this Mishnah is not primarily a {\i terumos u-ma\u8217'asros} concern at all. Rava cites a {\i baraita}: Rabbi Meir defines {\i am ha-aretz} as one who does not eat {\i chullin be-tahara}, while the Sages define {\i am ha-aretz} as one who does not tithe his produce. Rava adopts Rabbi Meir\u8217's definition for the Mishnah\u8217's case: we are dealing with an {\i am ha-aretz} with respect to {\i tumah ve-taharah} only, and the problem is entirely about {\i tumah}. Before water touches the grain it cannot become {\i tamei}, so there is no violation to be concerned about, and working with her is permitted. Once water is added, the grain becomes susceptible to {\i tumah}, and if she contaminates it she creates {\i chulin tamei} \u8212- which is merely a rabbinic {\i chaver} concern \u8212- but she also contaminates the {\i challah} that is now embedded in the dough, and {\i challah} is a form of {\i terumah}, whose defilement is a biblical prohibition. At that point the wife of the {\i chaver} must stop.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then challenges Rava\u8217's reading from a {\i baraita} which states that you may not help someone who eats their produce in {\i tumah}, implying this applies even to ordinary {\i chulin}. But according to Rava, helping with {\i chulin be-tumah} should be permitted. Abaye resolves the contradiction: that {\i baraita} is dealing specifically with a {\i kohen} who eats {\i terumah} in a state of {\i tumah}, which is a biblical violation. That is why one cannot assist him \u8212- it is a {\i tumah de-oraita} concern.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Entrust & Swap\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Grain Grinding\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara continues with the question of entrusting grain to those who may not be careful about {\i tumah} or {\i shemitah}. A new text is introduced: {\i tochnin u-mafkidin etzel ochlei shevi\u8217'it ve-etzel ochlei peroteihen be-tumah} \u8212- one may grind grain and entrust it with those who eat {\i shemitah} produce or who eat their produce in {\i tumah}. The rationale for the absence of concern is that we trust people to be careful about other people\u8217's property even if they are not careful about their own. However, you may not grind on behalf of someone who eats {\i shemitah} produce or on behalf of someone who eats in {\i tumah}, because doing so would be {\i mesayea yedei ovrei aveirah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sealed Vessels\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This last clause \u8212- that you cannot help someone who eats their produce in {\i tumah} \u8212- creates a tension with Rava\u8217's position, since Rava permits helping someone with {\i chulin be-tumah}. The Gemara\u8217's earlier resolution, attributed to Abaye, is that the case involves a {\i kohen} eating {\i terumah} in {\i tumah}, which is a biblical violation. The Gemara then asks: if we trust people to handle others\u8217' grain carefully \u8212- even a {\i kohen am ha-aretz} who is known to be comfortable with {\i tumah} \u8212- how can we do so if he might render it {\i tamei} by contact? The Gemara brings another text establishing that one may entrust {\i terumah} with an ordinary {\i Yisrael am ha-aretz} but not with a {\i kohen am ha-aretz}, precisely because the {\i kohen} feels at ease around {\i terumah} and is not careful. Rabbi Ila resolves this by positing the case involves a pottery vessel sealed with a tight cover ({\i kaveiret} with {\i tzamid patil}), so that even if the {\i kohen} handles the vessel the {\i terumah} inside cannot become {\i tamei}. This resolution is admittedly not {\i peshat} of the {\i baraita}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Unsoaked Grain\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara pushes further: even with a sealed vessel, one should be concerned that the {\i kohen}\u8217's wife, when she is a {\i niddah}, might move the vessel, and since {\i tumas ha-guf} can convey {\i tumah} to objects through moving them, the sealed vessel would not protect the contents. Rabbi Yirmiyah therefore offers a different resolution: the case where one need not be concerned about the {\i kohen} touching the produce is specifically a case of grain that has not come in contact with water \u8212- {\i peirot she-lo huchsharu}. Before being soaked, produce cannot become {\i tamei} regardless of contact, so there is nothing to worry about. This again resolves the apparent contradiction but is concededly not the straightforward reading of the {\i baraita}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then introduces yet another text: {\i ha-molikh chittin le-tochein Kuti o le-tochein am ha-aretz harei elu be-chezkatan} \u8212- if one brings wheat to a Samaritan miller or to an {\i am ha-aretz} miller, one may assume upon receiving it back that it has not been swapped, and one need not take {\i terumos u-ma\u8217'asros} again or be concerned about {\i shemitah} produce having been substituted. However, one must be concerned about {\i tumah} \u8212- that the miller may have come into contact with the grain. The Gemara notes this seems to contradict the earlier conclusion that we are not always worried about {\i tumah} by such individuals. But the Gemara responds: it does not contradict it, since the only cases where we said there is no concern are those where the grain had not come in contact with water \u8212- making the matter of {\i tumah} irrelevant in any case. The Gemara then acknowledges something unusual: this text was not introduced to raise a genuine difficulty; it was introduced to lay the groundwork for the next question, which is about the concern of swapping.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mother-in-Law\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i baraita} just cited said we are not concerned that the miller swapped out the grain. Is this really true as a general principle? The Gemara challenges from the Mishnah: {\i ha-noten la-chamoto} \u8212- if one gives flour to one\u8217's mother-in-law to prepare baked goods, one must take {\i terumos u-ma\u8217'asros} from the flour given to her, and again from the finished product received back. The reason: we are concerned she swapped out the flour. Specifically, she is suspected of swapping when something goes wrong: {\i mipnei she-chashudah mechalfet ha-miskalkeil} \u8212- she will swap out flour that has spoiled or baked goods that have fallen. The mother-in-law is not trying to defraud; she is trying to do good for her daughter. If her first attempt at baking fails, she will discard it, bake again from her own flour, and return a beautiful cake \u8212- but that cake is now made from her own un-tithed flour, so both what was given to her and what was received back must be subject to {\i ma\u8217'aser}. The Gemara accepts this as a special case: the concern for swapping does not apply generally but arises specifically because of the mother-in-law\u8217's emotional investment. Rav Yehuda articulates it: {\i rotzah hee be-takkunat bitah u-voshah mi-chasna} \u8212- she wants her daughter\u8217's household to be well-served, and she is embarrassed in front of her son-in-law.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Innkeeper Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then tests the principle further. If one gives flour to a woman innkeeper to bake bread for a traveler, the same {\i halakha} applies: one must take {\i terumos u-ma\u8217'asros} both before and after, because the innkeeper is also suspected of swapping. But here the motive is entirely different. Rav Yehuda explains: {\i bar bei rav lachol chamima, ve-ana achol karira} \u8212- she reasons, \u8220"Should this yeshiva student get fresh bread while I eat my day-old stale bread?\u8221" The innkeeper swaps not out of warmth but out of a sense of resentment at the inequity. In both cases the swap is driven by a subjective emotional response to the transaction, and both are recognized as occasions for concern. But these remain special cases \u8212- not the general rule \u8212- and as a matter of principle one does not assume that every miller or handler has swapped out the grain entrusted to him.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Gittin 62\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Food Entrustment\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Swapping Food Concern\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 We pick up at the bottom of 61b, wrapping up the fifth {\i perek} of {\i HaNizakin}. The broader context of this {\i perek} concerns things done for {\i darchei shalom} \u8212- ways of peace \u8212- and the {\i Gemara} has been exploring the interactions between a {\i chaver} and an {\i am ha\u8217'aretz} in matters of {\i tumah}, {\i taharah}, and {\i terumot u\u8217'ma\u8217'asrot}. The particular question at hand is whether we are genuinely unconcerned, as a general matter, that an {\i am ha\u8217'aretz} will swap out food entrusted to him for his own inferior produce.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} challenges this presumption: {\i v\u8217'hatanya} \u8212- is it not taught in a {\i Tosefta} from {\i Taharot} that seems to imply real concern about the reliability of an {\i am ha\u8217'aretz} and his household?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Wife Grinding Grain\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Tosefta Taharot} teaches: {\i eshet chaver tochenet im eshet am ha\u8217'aretz bizman she\u8217'hi t\u8217'meiah aval lo bizman she\u8217'hi t\u8217'horah} \u8212- the wife of a {\i chaver}, one who is scrupulous in the laws of {\i tumah v\u8217'taharah}, may grind grain together with the wife of an {\i am ha\u8217'aretz} when she herself is {\i t\u8217'meiah}, but not when she is {\i t\u8217'horah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The logic is as follows. When the wife of the {\i chaver} is {\i t\u8217'meiah} \u8212- whether as a {\i niddah} or for any other reason \u8212- she is habituated at home to avoid direct contact with the grain, and she will carry that habit with her here. She will not cause the grain to become {\i tamei}. But when she is {\i t\u8217'horah}, she may absent-mindedly handle the food, or even pop a morsel into her mouth \u8212- and that food will not have had {\i terumot u\u8217'ma\u8217'asrot} taken from it. To prevent that inadvertent consumption, the {\i Tosefta} forbids the arrangement when she is {\i t\u8217'horah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Shimon ben Elazar\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Shimon ben Elazar takes a stricter position: {\i af bizman she\u8217'hi t\u8217'meiah lo titchan} \u8212- even when the wife of the {\i chaver} is {\i t\u8217'meiah}, she should not grind together with the wife of the {\i am ha\u8217'aretz}. His concern is {\i mipnei she\u8217'chavertah notenet lah ochel} \u8212- because the wife of the {\i am ha\u8217'aretz}, appreciating the shared labor, may well offer her friend some of the food as a gesture of collegiality. That social offering, however well-intentioned, could lead the wife of the {\i chaver} to eat produce from which {\i ma\u8217'asrot} have not been separated. So even the passive concern of absent-minded eating is compounded here by the active pressure of a friend\u8217's hospitality.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Concern of Theft\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now draws out a troubling implication of Rabbi Shimon ben Elazar\u8217's reasoning. If the wife of the {\i am ha\u8217'aretz} is prepared to offer food to her friend without her husband\u8217's explicit permission, she is effectively stealing from him \u8212- since the food belongs to her husband, and he has not authorized the gift. As Rashi formulates it: {\i chasheida eshet am ha\u8217'aretz lignov mishel ba\u8217'alah u\u8217'latet l\u8217'chavertah}. And if this woman is capable of such theft, the {\i Gemara} asks: {\i hashta mignav ganva chilufei lo mechalfa} \u8212- if she will steal from her husband to give to her friend, surely she would swap out food entrusted to her if she preferred it to her own. How, then, can the {\i Gemara} maintain the general presumption that we need not worry about swapping?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rationalizing Offer\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Yosef resolves the difficulty: {\i hatam nami moreh v\u8217'amrah} \u8212- even there, in the case of the {\i Tosefta}, the woman is not stealing in any straightforward sense. She is {\i moreh hetter}, rationalizing her action as permitted. Her reasoning is captured in the principle {\i tora m\u8217'dashi k\u8217'achil} \u8212- just as the Torah forbids muzzling an ox while it threshes, so too one who labors together with another ought to be able to benefit from the produce. In her mind, her friend is working alongside her, and it is only natural and proper to share. This rationalization does not transfer to the case of straightforward swapping, where no such logic applies. A person will engage in behavior they can morally reframe; they will not as readily commit what they recognize, without any rationalization available, as simple theft.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Challah & Terumah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Challah Restriction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 We now turn to a new but related topic, beginning three lines from the top of 62a. Rav Yossi ben Meshullam testified in the name of Rav Yochanan \u8212- his brother \u8212- who said in the name of Rabbi Elazar ben Chasma: {\i ein osin challas am ha\u8217'aretz b\u8217'taharah} \u8212- one may not separate {\i challah} from the dough of an {\i am ha\u8217'aretz} while in a state of {\i taharah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tamei Assumption\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The case is that of a professional bread-kneader who is a {\i chaver} and is currently {\i tahor}. An {\i am ha\u8217'aretz} brings him dough and asks him to knead it, bake {\i challot}, and while he is at it, please perform {\i hafrashat challah} \u8212- separate out the requisite portion for the {\i kohen}. This seems like an opportunity for a praiseworthy act: a {\i chaver}, {\i tahor}, taking {\i challah} on behalf of an {\i am ha\u8217'aretz} who may not know how.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The problem, however, is that the flour delivered by the {\i am ha\u8217'aretz} is assumed to be {\i tamei} \u8212- flour in the possession of an {\i am ha\u8217'aretz} carries that presumption. If the {\i chaver} separates {\i challah} while in a state of {\i taharah}, the {\i kohen} who receives it will see that it was separated by this known {\i chaver}, and will naturally assume the {\i challah} is {\i tahora}. But it is not \u8212- it derives from {\i tamei} dough. The {\i kohen} may then eat {\i tamei challah} under the mistaken impression that it is {\i tahora}, which is a serious transgression. This is why the procedure is forbidden when the kneader is {\i tahor}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Dough Alternative\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 There is, however, a permitted path: {\i aval osin issas chulyav b\u8217'taharah} \u8212- one may take the flour from the {\i am ha\u8217'aretz} and make it into dough while in a state of {\i taharah}. The critical distinction is that flour does not yet have the status of food susceptible to {\i tumah}; it is only once water is added and the dough forms that {\i tumah} can take hold. If the {\i am ha\u8217'aretz} delivers the flour \u8212- before any liquid contact \u8212- the kneader who is {\i tahor} can transform it into dough himself, in {\i taharah}, and then separate {\i challah} in {\i taharah}. The resulting {\i challah} genuinely is {\i tahorah}, and the {\i kohen} will not be misled.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Unusual Vessels\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Once the {\i challah} is separated, the task of delivering it to the {\i kohen} falls to the {\i am ha\u8217'aretz}, and a new concern arises: will he touch it and render it {\i tamei}? The solution given is {\i v\u8217'menicha b\u8217'kefishah o b\u8217'anchusah} \u8212- the separated {\i challah} is placed in a {\i kefishah} or an {\i anchusah}. Rashi explains that these are unusual, distinctive vessels \u8212- something like a worn flask or, according to the {\i Geonim}, a flat wooden board. These peculiar containers serve a specific purpose: when the {\i am ha\u8217'aretz} comes to retrieve his bread and the separated {\i challah}, he takes both together in those vessels \u8212- {\i notel es shteihen v\u8217'ein choshesh} \u8212- and we are not concerned that he will touch the {\i challah}, for reasons the {\i Gemara} will shortly explain.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Terumah Olives\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A parallel set of rules applies to the separation of {\i terumah} from olives. {\i V\u8217'ein osin trumas zeisav b\u8217'taharah} \u8212- similarly, one may not press olives that have already been crushed by an {\i am ha\u8217'aretz} and then separate {\i terumah} while {\i tahor}. The olive-pressing industry involved two stages: first, crushing the olives with a large stone wheel to begin releasing the oil, and then pressing them under sustained pressure to extract the remainder. Once the olives have been crushed and the liquid begins to emerge, contact between the liquid and the olive renders the produce susceptible to {\i tumah}, just as water does to flour. Olives delivered after crushing are therefore already {\i tamei}, and the same deception of the {\i kohen} would result if {\i terumah} were separated from them while the presser is {\i tahor}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The alternative: {\i aval osin zeisim chulyav b\u8217'tahara} \u8212- one may take the whole, uncrushed olives from the {\i am ha\u8217'aretz} before any liquid contact has occurred, perform the crushing oneself in {\i taharah}, and thereby produce {\i terumah} that is genuinely {\i tahorah}. {\i V\u8217'notel mimenu k\u8217'dei terumah u\u8217'minicha bikli chaver} \u8212- one takes the portion designated as {\i terumah} and places it in one\u8217's own vessel \u8212- a vessel of the {\i chaver} that is {\i tahor} \u8212- and the {\i am ha\u8217'aretz} takes both portions when he comes to collect, without concern that he will touch the {\i terumah} improperly.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Parnassa Logic\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Livelihood Allowance\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now asks: {\i v\u8217'ta\u8217'ama mai} \u8212- why do we permit these procedures at all? Even in the cases that are technically permitted, we should be concerned that the {\i am ha\u8217'aretz} will touch the separated {\i challah} or {\i terumah} and render them {\i tamei}. What justifies proceeding?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The answer comes from Rabbi Yochanan: {\i mishum k\u8217'dei chayei d\u8217'gaval u\u8217'mishum k\u8217'dei chayei d\u8217'badad} \u8212- for the sake of the livelihood of the bread-dough kneader and the sake of the livelihood of the olive presser. The vast majority of the population were {\i amei ha\u8217'aretz}, not {\i chaveirim}, and a professional kneader or presser who could also perform the required separations would have a significant business advantage. The halachic accommodations were crafted specifically to enable these artisans to make a living \u8212- an allowance analogous in spirit to the {\i darchei shalom} concessions of our {\i Mishnah}, though here the rationale is {\i k\u8217'dei chayei} rather than social harmony.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Both Cases Needed\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i V\u8217'tzricha} \u8212- the {\i Gemara} establishes that both cases, the kneader and the olive presser, must be explicitly taught; neither can be inferred from the other.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Had the {\i Gemara} told us only about the dough-kneader, one might have understood the dispensation specifically because {\i d\u8217'lo nafish agrei} \u8212- his profit margin is very thin. It is entirely understandable that we would extend special accommodations to someone whose livelihood is marginal. But {\i aval badad d\u8217'nafish agrei} \u8212- the owner of the olive press, by contrast, earns a considerably larger profit during the pressing season, and one might have thought we need not be so solicitous of his commercial interests. Hence the olive presser\u8217's case must also be stated.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Conversely, had the {\i Gemara} told us only about the olive presser, one might have justified the allowance on account of {\i d\u8217'lo shechi\u8217'ach lei} \u8212- the pressing season is brief. It is true he earns well during those weeks, but that limited seasonal income must support him the entire year, and so perhaps a special accommodation is warranted. {\i Aval gaval d\u8217'shechi\u8217'ach lei} \u8212- but the bread-kneader works year-round and has ongoing income, and one might have thought he requires no special accommodation. Therefore {\i tzricha} \u8212- both must be stated, to make clear that the dispensation applies in either situation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tevel Warning\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now turns to the mechanism by which the halachic concerns are actually addressed. The statement {\i amar mar} reintroduces the case of {\i challah}: you take the {\i challah} in {\i taharah}, place it in the unusual vessels, and the {\i am ha\u8217'aretz} takes both the baked bread and the {\i challah} without your having to worry.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But the {\i Gemara} probes: {\i v\u8217'leichosh dilma naga bah} \u8212- why should we not be concerned that he will touch it? The answer is {\i d\u8217'amrinan lei} \u8212- we tell him: {\i chazi ei nagat bah hadar l\u8217'tivla} \u8212- if you touch this, everything will revert to being {\i tevel} and forbidden to eat. The {\i am ha\u8217'aretz}, though not punctilious about {\i tumah v\u8217'taharah}, is presumed to care about {\i tevel}. After all, the general principle is {\i rov amei ha\u8217'aretz me\u8217'asrin hen} \u8212- most {\i amei ha\u8217'aretz} do in fact take {\i terumos u\u8217'ma\u8217'asros}. Certainly one who has gone to the trouble of asking someone to separate {\i challah} on his behalf demonstrates that he cares. So we issue this warning \u8212- even if it is not literally accurate \u8212- and the fear of causing his bread to revert to {\i tevel} will deter him from touching the {\i challah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Visual Reminder\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} pushes further: {\i v\u8217'leichosh dilma lo ichpas lei} \u8212- but perhaps this particular {\i am ha\u8217'aretz} does not care? {\i Hashta l\u8217'sikuni k\u8217'michavein michpas lo ichpas lei} \u8212- this is readily answered. The very fact that he came to have {\i challah} taken for him demonstrates he cares. If he cared enough to request the separation, he cares enough to be deterred by the warning that touching it will ruin everything.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} then raises a more pointed difficulty for the case of {\i terumah} from olives. In the {\i challah} case, the separated portion is placed in the unusual vessels \u8212- {\i kefishah} or {\i anchusah} \u8212- which serve as a {\i hikeira}, a visual cue. When the {\i am ha\u8217'aretz} reaches for his produce and sees these distinctive containers, he is reminded of the warning and keeps his hands off. {\i Bishlama hasam is lei hikeira} \u8212- the unusual vessel functions as an ongoing visual reminder. But in the case of {\i terumah}, the text said to place it in {\i kli chaver}, a vessel of the {\i chaver}, which need not be unusual or distinctive at all. {\i Hacha mai hikeira is lei} \u8212- what visual marker will remind him? Without something to jog his memory, he may simply open the vessel and help himself, forgetting the warning entirely.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Vessel Types\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} resolves this by clarifying what is meant by {\i kli chaver}: {\i d\u8217'manach lei b\u8217'chlei glalim b\u8217'chlei avanim b\u8217'chlei adamah} \u8212- one places the {\i terumah} in vessels made of dried dung, stone, or unfinished earthenware. These materials cannot become {\i tamei} under any circumstances \u8212- unlike pottery, they do not receive {\i tumah}. This serves a dual purpose: first, even if he touches the vessel, the {\i terumah} inside will not become {\i tamei} through the vessel\u8217's contact; and second, these are sufficiently unusual containers that they function as a visual reminder, just as the distinctive vessels did in the {\i challah} case.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} then asks: {\i ee hachi mai irya yad d\u8217'chaver} \u8212- if the point is simply that the vessels cannot become {\i tamei}, why must they belong to a {\i chaver}? Even vessels of an {\i am ha\u8217'aretz} made of these same materials would serve equally well. {\i Hachi nami ka\u8217'amar} \u8212- the {\i Gemara} concedes the point entirely. The intention of {\i kli chaver} is not ownership by a {\i chaver} specifically, but vessels {\i she\u8217're\u8217'uyim l\u8217'hishtamesh bahen chaver} \u8212- vessels suitable for use by a {\i chaver}, meaning vessels that cannot become {\i tamei}. Even vessels belonging to an {\i am ha\u8217'aretz} would qualify, provided they are of the materials that cannot receive {\i tumah}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Non-Jews Shemitah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Machzikin Definition\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 We now come to the final portion of the {\i Mishnah} in the fifth {\i perek}, which states: {\i machzikin yedei ovdei kochavim b\u8217'shvi\u8217'is} \u8212- one may {\i machzikin}, strengthen or give encouragement to, non-Jews who are working the land during {\i shemitah}. This is partly a matter of {\i darchei shalom}, but perhaps more essentially a practical necessity: since Jews cannot work the land during {\i shemitah}, the labor of non-Jews may be essential for the community\u8217's sustenance. Encouraging them is therefore not merely a social nicety.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hoeing Prohibition\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} asks what precisely {\i machzikin} entails. The initial assumption might be that it means working alongside them, similar to the {\i Mishnah}\u8217's earlier allowances of lending implements or grinding grain. The {\i Gemara} challenges this: {\i v\u8217'amar Rav Dimi bar Shishna mishmei de\u8217'Rav} \u8212- Rav Dimi said in the name of Rav that {\i ein odrin im akum bishviit} \u8212- one may not hoe together with a non-Jew during {\i shemitah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This ruling raises a larger question \u8212- one that the {\i Gemara} and {\i Tosafot} address with implications for the {\i heter mechira}. The underlying question is {\i yesh kinyan le\u8217'akum le\u8217'hafkiya miyad trumot u\u8217'maasrot b\u8217'Eretz Yisrael} \u8212- does non-Jewish ownership of land in Eretz Yisrael affect its {\i shemitah} status sufficiently to permit working it? Those familiar with the {\i heter mechira} will recognize this as precisely the principle upon which that dispensation rests. And yet this {\i Gemara} indicates that one may not hoe together with a non-Jew even on his own land.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} addresses the related {\i Gemara} in {\i perek zeh borer} concerning a {\i kohen} plowing during {\i shemitah} who claims to be an {\i agiston}. Rashi there explains that the {\i agiston} is a hired hand of a non-Jew, and the non-Jew\u8217's ownership of the land makes it permissible. {\i Tosafot} rejects this reading: our {\i Gemara} explicitly forbids working alongside a non-Jew during {\i shemitah}, and there is no compelling reason to distinguish between paid and volunteer labor.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} offers several alternative explanations. First, the {\i agiston} case may involve {\i sakanat nefashot} \u8212- if the farmers failed to pay the king\u8217's tax, their lives would be at risk, and {\i pikuach nefashot} overrides {\i shemitah}. Second, perhaps that {\i Gemara} holds, in contrast to ours, that non-Jewish ownership does indeed {\i hafkiya}, remove the {\i shemitah} prohibition \u8212- meaning these two {\i Gemarot} represent competing positions, and the {\i posek} who rules like the other {\i Gemara} may permit working land owned by a non-Jew. Third, perhaps even according to our {\i Gemara}, since {\i shemitah} in the present era is {\i derabbanan}, there is room for leniency in specific circumstances \u8212- such as paying a tax \u8212- even though as a general matter the prohibition remains.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 These final two explanations of {\i Tosafot} are directly significant for the {\i heter mechira} debate.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Encouragement Examples\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Returning to the {\i Gemara}\u8217's resolution: {\i lo tzricha le\u8217'meimar lehu achzukei be\u8217'alma} \u8212- what {\i machzikin} means in the {\i Mishnah} is simply saying {\i achzukei}, a word of encouragement, akin to {\i yasher koach}. This is illustrated by the examples that follow. {\i Ki had de\u8217'Rav Yehuda amar lehu achzuka} \u8212- when Rav Yehuda saw people working the land during {\i shemitah} he said {\i yasher koach}. {\i Rav Sheshet amar lehu ashrasa} \u8212- Rav Sheshet offered a similar word of encouragement, meaning something like \u8220"more power to you.\u8221"\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shalom Etiquette\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Mishnah}\u8217's final clause states that one asks after the well-being of non-Jews \u8212- {\i sho\u8217'alin bishlomam mipnei darchei shalom} \u8212- but one does not say {\i shalom aleichem} twice. Rashi understands this as a general statement, not limited to the {\i shemitah} context: when one encounters a non-Jew in ordinary circumstances, one may greet him with {\i shalom aleichem} \u8212- as illustrated by Rav Chisda, who made a point of going out to greet non-Jews \u8212- but one should not be {\i kofel shalom}, doubling the greeting with excessive enthusiasm. Rav Kahana would say {\i shlama lemar}, \u8220"peace to the master,\u8221" as a courteous greeting.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Achzuki Meaning\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Amar Rav Geviba lo nitzrecha ela leyom chagam} \u8212- Rav Geviba clarifies that the {\i Mishnah}\u8217's clause about {\i sho\u8217'alin bishlomam} refers not to {\i shemitah} at all, but to non-Jews\u8217' festival days. As taught in a {\i braita}: do not enter a non-Jew\u8217's home on his festival to offer well-wishes \u8212- that would be too encouraging of his religious celebration. But if you encounter him in the marketplace, you may address him, {\i noten lo besafa rafa uvichoved rosh} \u8212- in a soft tone, with a certain gravity and restraint, without warmth or enthusiasm. The overall framework thus emerges: during {\i shemitah}, give genuine {\i achzuki} encouragement; year-round, a standard courteous greeting is fine; on their festivals, a measured and understated acknowledgment is permitted but must be kept at a minimum.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Gneiva Story\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gneiva Greeting\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now relates a memorable episode. Rav Huna and Rav Chisda were sitting together when Gneiva \u8212- a figure who appeared at the beginning of the tractate in a troubling context, having caused great distress to Mar Ukva and eventually being led away in chains \u8212- passed by. One said to the other: {\i nekum mikamei de\u8217'bar oryan hu} \u8212- let us rise before him, for he is a scholar. The other demurred: {\i lo umikmi palga nekum} \u8212- should we stand for someone who is a divisive troublemaker?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 While they were debating, Gneiva approached them, and he greeted them with {\i shlama lechu malchi, shlama lechu malchi} \u8212- \u8220"Peace to you, O kings; peace to you, O kings.\u8221" This scene is placed here not incidentally: the {\i Gemara} has just been discussing {\i kefilat shalom}, and here Gneiva demonstrates precisely that \u8212- doubling his greeting and addressing them with regal honor.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kings Justification\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 They asked him: {\i mena lach de-rabbanan ikru malchi} \u8212- where do you get the idea that sages are called kings? He cited the {\i pasuk} in Mishlei where the Torah speaks in its own voice: {\i bi melachim yimlochu} \u8212- through me kings have their reign. The implication is that true kingship is derived from Torah; those who bear and transmit Torah are in that sense kings. They then asked: {\i u\u8217'mena lach de-kafliinan shlama le-malchi} \u8212- and where do you know that one doubles the greeting to a king?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Double Shalom Source\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 He replied: {\i de-amar Rav Yehuda amar Rav} \u8212- I have the tradition from Rav Yehuda in the name of Rav, who derived from a {\i pasuk} that one doubles the {\i shalom} to a king. The {\i pasuk} in which the spirit takes hold of Amashai, head of the troops, and he declares to David: {\i lecha David ve-imecha ben Yishai shalom shalom lecha} \u8212- \u8220"Peace, peace to you, David.\u8221" The word {\i shalom} appears twice, establishing the practice of {\i kofel shalom} before a king. This man, it turns out, was not merely a {\i bar oryan} in a superficial sense \u8212- he knew the traditions of Rav Yehuda in the name of Rav and could source them precisely.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Animal Feeding\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The rabbis, apparently impressed, invited him to share a meal with them: {\i litim mar midi} \u8212- would you like to taste something? He declined, citing another ruling of Rav Yehuda in the name of Rav: {\i asur le-adam she-yitim klum ad she-yiten ma\u8217'achal le-vehemto} \u8212- it is forbidden for a person to taste anything before feeding his animals. The source: the {\i pasuk} says first {\i ve-natati esev be-sadecha le-vehemtecha} \u8212- I will give grass in your field for your animals \u8212- and only afterward {\i ve-achalta ve-savata} \u8212- you shall eat and be satisfied. The sequence in the {\i pasuk} is instructive: animals are fed before people eat.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The story is a striking one with which to close the fifth {\i perek}. Here was a figure who, from what we knew of him, seemed to represent social disorder. Yet he turned out to be deeply learned, respectful of the rabbis in the most formal and considered way \u8212- addressing them as kings, doubling his greeting with full awareness of its source \u8212- and possessed of an admirable personal ethic in the treatment of his animals. One might see in this a quiet commentary on the theme of {\i darchei shalom} that has animated the entire {\i perek}: the pursuit of a society in which people are treated with respect and consideration is not only a rabbinic prescription but a human aspiration. It is expressed not just in formal rules about lending implements or greasing the wheels of commerce, but in how one enters a room, addresses those one encounters, and orders one\u8217's own behavior even in private matters like feeding one\u8217's animals before sitting down to eat.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Get Retraction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Receive\u8212-Retractable\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 We now turn the page and begin the sixth {\i perek}, sometimes called {\i Ha-omer} and sometimes {\i Ha-itkabel}, after its second and more distinctive word. We are back to the central subject matter of {\i Gittin}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Mishnah} opens: {\i ha-omer hitkabel zeh get le-ishti} \u8212- if a husband says to someone, \u8220"Receive this {\i get} for my wife,\u8221" or {\i o holech get zeh le-ishti} \u8212- \u8220"Deliver this {\i get} to my wife\u8221" \u8212- then {\i im rotze lachazor yachzor} \u8212- if he wishes to retract, he may do so, up until the moment the {\i get} is actually delivered.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The reason: the wife did not appoint this person as her {\i shaliach}. To be divorced is assumed to be a detriment to the wife, and an act to one\u8217's detriment cannot be performed on one\u8217's behalf by an agent one never appointed. This person can function as the husband\u8217's agent to bring the {\i get}, but the divorce is not final until the wife actually receives it. The word \u8220"receive\u8221" is therefore reread as an instruction to deliver \u8212- {\i hitkabel v\u8217'holech} \u8212- make her receive it by bringing it to her; but in either formulation, the husband retains the ability to retract.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Wife\u8217's Shaliach\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If, however, the wife herself appoints a {\i shaliach} and instructs him: {\i hitkabel li giti} \u8212- receive my {\i get} on my behalf \u8212- then {\i im ratza lachazor lo yachzor} \u8212- once the husband places the {\i get} in this agent\u8217's hands, he cannot retract. The wife\u8217's {\i shaliach le\u8217'kabbalah} has received the {\i get}, and from that moment the divorce is effective. The rationale is the inverse of the first case: divorce, whatever its effect on the wife, is something she has affirmatively sought and for which she has formally appointed an agent.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Take & Deliver\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i U\u8217'l\u8217'fichach} \u8212- therefore, if the wife\u8217's {\i shaliach} presents himself and the husband wishes to preserve a window to retract, he may do so by recasting the arrangement. If the husband says {\i ee efshi shetikabel lah, ela holech v\u8217'ten lah} \u8212- \u8220"I do not want you to receive this on her behalf; rather, take this and deliver it to her\u8221" \u8212- he thereby converts the agent from a {\i shaliach le\u8217'kabbalah} of the wife into a {\i shaliach} of the husband for delivery. The divorce is then not effective until the {\i get} is placed in the wife\u8217's hand, and the husband retains the right to retract until that moment: {\i im ratza lachazor yachzor}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Shimon View\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel takes a broader view: {\i af ha-omeret tol li giti im ratza lachazor lo yachzor} \u8212- even if the wife says to the {\i shaliach} not \u8220"receive\u8221" but \u8220"take my {\i get} for me,\u8221" this too constitutes a valid appointment as {\i shaliach le\u8217'kabbalah}, and the husband cannot retract once the {\i get} is handed over. The {\i chachamim} presumably disagree \u8212- \u8220"take it for me\u8221" is not equivalent to \u8220"receive it for me,\u8221" and the wife\u8217's ability to effect her divorce turns on using the proper formulation \u8212- but Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel holds that {\i tol} is interpreted as {\i kabbalah}, and the husband is bound.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Holech vs.\u160 ?Zachi\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 No Kabbalah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} opens its analysis. Rav Acha brei de-Rav Avya said to Rav Ashi: {\i ta\u8217'ama d\u8217'lo shavitia ihi shaliach l\u8217'kabbalah} \u8212- the reason the husband can retract in the first case of the {\i Mishnah} is because the wife never made this person a {\i shaliach le\u8217'kabbalah}. The {\i Mishnah}\u8217's teaching of the case where the husband says \u8220"receive this {\i get} for my wife\u8221" \u8212- which obviously cannot work immediately, since the recipient is no one\u8217's {\i shaliach} for {\i kabbalah} \u8212- is instructive for what it implies: we are told the wife did not appoint this person as her agent.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Holech Effectiveness\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This raises a question about the second case. {\i Ha shavitia ihi shaliach l\u8217'kabbalah} \u8212- had the wife made this person her {\i shaliach le\u8217'kabbalah}, would the first case clearly be effective with no possibility of retraction? Certainly. But what about the second case \u8212- {\i holech v\u8217'ten lah}, take this and deliver it to her? If the wife had made the agent a {\i shaliach le\u8217'kabbalah}, and the husband then said not \u8220"receive this\u8221" but \u8220"take this to her,\u8221" is the {\i get} immediately effective? Or does the husband\u8217's language of {\i holech} effectively demote the agent from {\i shaliach le\u8217'kabbalah} to {\i shaliach holacha} \u8212- an agent for conveyance rather than for receipt?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Holech Zachi\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The inference proposed is: since the first case in the {\i Mishnah} would obviously work if the wife had made this person a {\i shaliach le\u8217'kabbalah}, the {\i Mishnah} must be stating its rule for both cases in order to teach something about the second case. And if the {\i Gemara} infers that the second case \u8212- {\i holech v\u8217'ten lah} \u8212- would also be immediately effective when the wife had made the agent a {\i shaliach le\u8217'kabbalah}, this tells us something significant: {\i holech kezachi dami} \u8212- saying \u8220"take this\u8221" to someone is equivalent to saying \u8220"take possession of this on behalf of another.\u8221" The word {\i holech} is not merely an instruction for physical conveyance; it transfers ownership. Those familiar with earlier discussions in the tractate will recall the debate over whether {\i zachi} \u8212- an instruction to acquire on behalf of another \u8212- is effective immediately; here, {\i holech} is proposed to carry the same force.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Ashi\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Ashi rejects the inference. {\i Lo amina lach holech lav kezachi dami} \u8212- I do not accept that {\i holech} is like {\i zachi}. And if that is so, then the second case in the {\i Mishnah} would not be immediately effective even if the wife had made the agent a {\i shaliach le\u8217'kabbalah}. But then what is the purpose of the first case \u8212- {\i hitkabel get zeh le-ishti} \u8212- which seems to be stated as a {\i chiddush}? If the answer is simply \u8220"he is not a {\i shaliach le\u8217'kabbalah},\u8221" that is self-evident. Why does the {\i Mishnah} need to tell us this?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Itkabel Chidush\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The resolution: {\i itkabel} is a genuine {\i chiddush} in its own right. {\i D\u8217'salka daitecha amina, ho-ilu ba\u8217'al lav bar shavei shaliach le-kabala hu} \u8212- one might have thought that since the husband has no power to appoint a {\i shaliach le\u8217'kabbalah} on his wife\u8217's behalf, the formula {\i hitkabel get zeh le-ishti} is entirely ineffective. When the {\i get} is ultimately delivered to the wife, one might have thought it doesn\u8217't work at all \u8212- the husband said \u8220"receive,\u8221" but he cannot make this person a {\i shaliach le\u8217'kabbalah}, so perhaps the entire transaction fails. The {\i Mishnah} therefore teaches: {\i de-itkabel ve-holech kama} \u8212- \u8220"receive it\u8221" means \u8220"bring it to her and have her receive it.\u8221" Even though the husband used the language of {\i kabbalah}, we interpret his intent as an instruction for delivery. The {\i chiddush} is not that it fails immediately \u8212- of course it does, there is no {\i shaliach le\u8217'kabbalah} \u8212- the {\i chiddush} is that when the {\i get} reaches the wife, it does work, because {\i hitkabel} is understood as an authorization to deliver.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The question of whether {\i holech} is like {\i zachi} \u8212- and what consequences flow from that for cases where the wife has made the agent a {\i shaliach le\u8217'kabbalah} \u8212- remains open, and will be taken up as the {\i Gemara}\u8217's analysis of this {\i perek} continues.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Gittin 63\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Holech k\u8217'Zachi\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rejecting Shaliach Role\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The sugya opens in the midst of a discussion central to the sixth {\i perek}, {\i Haomer} (also known as {\i Hitkabel}), concerning whether the word {\i holech} \u8212- used by a husband when addressing the wife\u8217's agent \u8212- functions as {\i k\u8217'zachi}, meaning that the {\i get} takes effect immediately upon being received by that agent. The question turns on the fact that the husband is speaking to someone who already stands in the position of a {\i shaliach l\u8217'kabala}, that is, an agent appointed by the wife to receive the {\i get} on her behalf. Given that predisposition, does {\i holech} \u8212- \u8220"go, bring this {\i get}\u8221" \u8212- transform into an act of immediate transfer?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} introduces a {\i ta shma} from the {\i Mishnah} (Gittin 66b): {\i lefichach im amar lo habaal ee efshi shetikabel lah ela holech v\u8217'ten lah} \u8212- if the husband says to the wife\u8217's appointed {\i shaliach l\u8217'kabala}, \u8220"I do not want you to accept the {\i get} on her behalf; rather, go and deliver it to her,\u8221" then {\i im ratza lachzor yachzor} \u8212- he can retract, because the agent has now been repositioned as a mere {\i shaliach l\u8217'holacha}, and the {\i get} has not yet been given. The {\i Gemara} draws a pointed inference: {\i ta\u8217'ama d\u8217'amar ee efshi} \u8212- the reason he can retract is specifically because he explicitly rejected the {\i shaliach l\u8217'kabala} role. But {\i ha lo amar ee efshi}, had he simply said {\i holech} without the rejection, {\i ratza lachzor lo yachzor} \u8212- he could not retract. This implies that {\i holech}, addressed to a {\i shaliach l\u8217'kabala}, functions as {\i zachi} \u8212- immediate acquisition on the wife\u8217's behalf.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might ask: how can the husband unilaterally override the agency that the wife established? The answer is that the husband is not obligated to respect her appointment. He can refuse to give the {\i get} in the capacity she designated. The {\i chidush} of the {\i Mishnah} is precisely that the husband retains control \u8212- he alone decides in what capacity the {\i get} passes hands.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Holech vs.\u160 ?Heilech\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} pushes back on the proof: {\i dilma be\u8217'heilech} \u8212- perhaps the {\i Mishnah}\u8217's operative word is not {\i holech} but {\i heilech}. To modern ears the two sound nearly identical, but they are semantically distinct words. {\i Holech} connotes {\i holacha}, delivery. {\i Heilech} \u8212- {\i hei lach} \u8212- means \u8220"here it is for you,\u8221" a direct act of transfer. If the {\i Mishnah} is speaking of {\i heilech}, then the case where the husband must explicitly say {\i ee efshi} to prevent immediate effect is a case of {\i heilech}, not {\i holech}, and we have learned nothing about whether {\i holech} by itself connotes {\i zachi}. Whether the {\i Gemara} is proposing an alternative {\i girsa} in the {\i Mishnah} or simply raising an interpretive possibility is left somewhat unclear, but the consequence is plain: the question of {\i holech k\u8217'zachi dami} remains open, to be revisited.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Zachi Definition\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The unresolved question \u8212- whether {\i holech}, when addressed to a {\i shaliach l\u8217'kabala}, functions as {\i zachi} \u8212- will continue to drive the sugya throughout the daf. As will become evident, this is not merely a question of linguistics but of fundamental agency law: does the contextual framing supplied by the {\i shaliach} and the wife\u8217's prior appointment color the husband\u8217's words, or does the plain meaning of {\i holech} govern regardless of context?\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Gender & Shaliach\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Default Roles\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} pivots to a related structural question. {\i Pshita} \u8212- it is obvious \u8212- that a man ({\i ish}) can serve as a {\i shaliach} to deliver a {\i get} to the wife ({\i shaliach l\u8217'holacha}), since men are the ones who give {\i gittin} to their wives. Equally obvious is that a woman can represent the wife to receive the {\i get} ({\i shaliach l\u8217'kabala}), since women are the ones who receive {\i gittin}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Reverse Roles\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But what of the reverse? Can a man serve as a {\i shaliach l\u8217'kabala} \u8212- appointed by the wife to receive the {\i get} \u8212- and can a woman serve as a {\i shaliach l\u8217'holacha}, appointed by the husband to deliver it? The {\i Gemara} raises this as a genuine question. The underlying {\i hava amina} is drawn from the principle of {\i shlichus}: an agent must be \u8220"in the {\i parsha}\u8221" of the transaction they are performing. Just as a non-Jew cannot serve as an agent for something from which he is excluded by the Torah, perhaps the role of {\i shaliach l\u8217'kabala} is limited to those whose natural position in the {\i parsha} of {\i gittin} is that of receiving \u8212- i.e., women \u8212- and the role of {\i shaliach l\u8217'holacha} is limited to those who naturally give \u8212- i.e., men. The {\i Gemara} considers whether we require not just general inclusion in the subject matter of {\i gittin} but specific inclusion in the relevant transactional role.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} attempts to resolve this from the {\i Mishnah} ({\i Haomer hitkabel get zeh l\u8217'ishti o holech get zeh l\u8217'ishti}): a husband appoints an agent to receive or convey the {\i get}, and then a woman appoints someone ({\i Hitkabel li giti}) to receive it. The {\i Gemara} wonders whether the {\i Mishnah} speaks of the same individual serving both roles \u8212- in which case we would have proof that whoever is valid for one role is valid for the other. But the {\i Gemara} dismisses this: {\i lo, bishnei sheluchim} \u8212- perhaps the {\i Mishnah} refers to two different agents, a man appointed by the husband and a woman appointed by the wife, respectively.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} then turns to the case in the {\i Mishnah} where the husband explicitly switches the agent from {\i shaliach l\u8217'kabala} to {\i shaliach l\u8217'holacha} ({\i ee efshi shetikabel lah ela holech v\u8217'ten lah}). Here it is clear we are dealing with one and the same person operating in both capacities. From this it follows: {\i kashar l\u8217'kabala kashar l\u8217'holacha} \u8212- whoever is valid to receive is valid to deliver. But the {\i Gemara} again limits the proof: {\i pshot minah} \u8212- we may conclude that one person can serve in both capacities, but perhaps only a man, given the precedent that a father receives a {\i get} on behalf of his minor daughter ({\i av mekabel get l\u8217'vito ktanah}). A man is therefore \u8220"in the {\i parsha}\u8221" of receiving a {\i get}, but the question of whether a woman can deliver remains unresolved.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Women as Deliverers\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Mari resolves this by citing an earlier {\i Mishnah}: {\i af nashim she\u8217'einam ne\u8217'emanot lomar met baalah ne\u8217'emanot lehaviv et gittah} \u8212- women who are not believed to testify that a husband has died (such as the mother-in-law and sister-in-law) are nonetheless believed to deliver a {\i get} and to declare {\i bifnai nichtav uvifnai nechtam}. Here women are explicitly functioning as agents of delivery. Rav Ashi adds that the {\i seifa} of that {\i Mishnah} is even more striking: {\i ha\u8217'ishah atzmah mevia et gitah} \u8212- the woman herself can deliver her own {\i get}, and we established that this refers to a case of {\i holacha}. Even if it is conceptually unusual to speak of a woman serving as her own {\i shaliach l\u8217'holacha}, the legal point is clear: women can perform the act of delivery. With this, the {\i Gemara} establishes black and white that both men and women may serve in either capacity.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Misrepresentation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Agent\u8217's Lie\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now introduces a series of cases in which the {\i shaliach} misrepresents his role to the husband. The woman tells her agent {\i havei li giti} \u8212- go bring me my {\i get}, which is understood as appointing him as a {\i shaliach l\u8217'holacha} on behalf of the husband, or at minimum as someone who will facilitate delivery. The agent then goes to the husband and reports: {\i ishtecha amra hitkabel li giti} \u8212- your wife wants me to receive her {\i get} for her, i.e., to serve as {\i shaliach l\u8217'kabala}. This is a misrepresentation, perhaps not intentional, but a misrepresentation nonetheless.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The husband then responds: {\i heilech kemah she\u8217'amrah} \u8212- \u8220"here it is, as she said.\u8221" The question is what the husband means by \u8220"as she said.\u8221" Does he mean as you are representing what she said \u8212- following the agent\u8217's report \u8212- or what she actually said?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Relying on Agent\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Nachman, citing Rabbah bar Avuha citing Rav, rules: {\i afilu higia get l\u8217'yadah einah megureshet} \u8212- even if the {\i get} ultimately reaches the wife\u8217's hand, she is not divorced. The reasoning: the husband said {\i heilech kemah she\u8217'amrah}, meaning he is relying on the agent\u8217's representation. He believes the agent is a {\i shaliach l\u8217'kabala} and intends the {\i get} to take effect immediately upon the agent receiving it. But in reality the wife never appointed this man as her {\i shaliach l\u8217'kabala}, so no valid {\i kabala} occurs. And since the husband never designated him as his {\i shaliach l\u8217'holacha} either, the subsequent delivery to the wife is also legally empty. {\i Shma mina adibura didei kasamich} \u8212- the husband\u8217's words are pegged to what the agent says, not to what the wife actually instructed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The reverse inference follows: {\i d\u8217'i adibura didah kasamich} \u8212- if instead the husband\u8217's intent tracked what the wife actually said, then since she truly wanted {\i holacha}, he would be implicitly appointing the agent as his {\i shaliach l\u8217'holacha}, and the {\i get} should work when delivered. The fact that Rav holds it does not work proves that the husband operates on the basis of the agent\u8217's report, not the wife\u8217's actual words.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Ashi Objects\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Ashi raises an objection: {\i hachi hashta} \u8212- what kind of comparison is this? He notes that the case under discussion, in which the wife said {\i kabala} but the agent reported {\i holacha}, cannot serve as the basis for the broader rule about {\i adibura didei kasamich}, because the consequences in this case happen to be symmetrical in a way that obscures which principle is operative.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Ashi constructs the mirror-image scenario: suppose the wife had said {\i hitkabel li giti} \u8212- appoint yourself as my {\i shaliach l\u8217'kabala} \u8212- and the agent instead reports to the husband {\i havei li giti}, \u8220"she wants me to deliver the {\i get}.\u8221" The husband says {\i heilech kemah she\u8217'amrah}. Now: if the husband is going by the agent\u8217's words, he is genuinely appointing the agent as his {\i shaliach l\u8217'holacha}, and the {\i get} should work when delivered. If instead he is going by what she actually said, he is in effect ratifying the {\i kabala} appointment, and she would be divorced the moment the agent receives the {\i get}. According to Rav Nachman, who rules that it works right away in this scenario ({\i kivan she\u8217'higia l\u8217'yado megureshet}), we would conclude {\i dibura didah hu d\u8217'kasamich} \u8212- the husband tracks her actual words, not the agent\u8217's representation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But Rav Ashi argues that these two scenarios are not equivalent. In the original case \u8212- where the agent reports {\i kabala} but was actually appointed for {\i holacha} \u8212- the reason the {\i get} fails is not because the husband tracks the agent\u8217's words. The reason is that the agent, by declaring himself a {\i shaliach l\u8217'kabala}, is effectively {\i rejecting} the role he was assigned. He was sent to deliver the {\i get} back to the wife; by announcing himself as a {\i shaliach l\u8217'kabala}, he is declining that mission. The {\i Gemara} articulates this: {\i de\u8217'amar shaliach l\u8217'kabala havina l\u8217'holacha lo havina} \u8212- he has repudiated the {\i holacha} role, and since he is not validly a {\i shaliach l\u8217'kabala} either, nothing works. This has nothing to do with whether the husband follows the agent\u8217's words or the wife\u8217's; the failure is internal to the agent\u8217's own act of rejection.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rejecting Delivery Role\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} thus establishes a principle: when a {\i shaliach} makes his mission easier by claiming a different role than the one he was assigned \u8212- specifically, by claiming {\i kabala} when he was designated for {\i holacha}, thereby ending his obligation once he receives the {\i get} rather than carrying it back \u8212- we interpret this claim as a rejection of the original role. Since he cannot validly occupy either position (not the wife\u8217's {\i shaliach l\u8217'kabala} because she never appointed him thus, not the husband\u8217's {\i shaliach l\u8217'holacha} because he has declined it), the {\i get} has no legal effect.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The consequence is that from Rav Nachman\u8217's ruling we cannot directly derive the broader principle about {\i adibura didei kasamich}. The {\i Gemara} leaves this as an open question, pursued through the cases that follow.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Contextual Interpretation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Reinterpretation Rules\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Huna bar Chiya introduces a new {\i ta shma} from the {\i Mishnah}: {\i ha\u8217'omer hitkabel get zeh l\u8217'ishti o holech get zeh l\u8217'ishti ratza lachzor yachzor} \u8212- if the husband says \u8220"receive this {\i get} for my wife\u8221" or \u8220"convey it to her,\u8221" he can retract. This is the {\i Mishnah}\u8217's basic case. The {\i Gemara} asks an arresting question: {\i ve\u8217'amai habaal lav bar shavei shaliach l\u8217'kabala hu?} \u8212- why does the {\i Mishnah} treat this as a valid appointment at all? The husband says {\i hitkabel}, \u8220"receive it,\u8221" which literally implies a {\i shaliach l\u8217'kabala} appointment \u8212- but the husband cannot appoint a {\i shaliach l\u8217'kabala} for his wife; only she can do that.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The answer is that we do not read his words with rigid literalism. {\i Keivan shenatan einav l\u8217'garsha} \u8212- since it is clear he intends to divorce her, {\i meimar amar tigaraish b\u8217'chol heichi demigaraisha} \u8212- we interpret his words in whatever way will make a valid {\i get} possible. When he says \u8220"receive it for my wife,\u8221" the only legally operative reading is \u8220"deliver it to my wife,\u8221" and so we interpret it accordingly. The husband is presumed to want the {\i get} to work, and we bend his words toward the only legally valid form they can take.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} then asks: {\i hacha nami} \u8212- why not apply the same principle in our case of misrepresentation? Even if the agent is claiming {\i kabala} when he was designated for {\i holacha}, should we not interpret the husband\u8217's words ({\i heilech kemah she\u8217'amrah}) in whatever way will make the {\i get} valid?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Law vs.\u160 ?Fact\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} draws a sharp distinction: {\i hatam ihu yada de\u8217'ein shlichut l\u8217'kabala gabeih venachti l\u8217'shem holacha} \u8212- in the {\i Mishnah}\u8217's basic case, any person who knew the {\i halacha} would have understood that the husband cannot appoint a {\i shaliach l\u8217'kabala}, and so we may say that deep down he intended the only legally valid interpretation: {\i holacha}. His error, if any, was one of legal expression, not substantive intent. {\i Gamar ve-natan l\u8217'shem holacha} \u8212- at the moment he acted, he implicitly conformed to the legal requirement that would make his transaction work.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But {\i hacha ta\u8217'i} \u8212- in the case of misrepresentation, the husband\u8217's error is not about law but about fact. He genuinely believes the agent is the wife\u8217's {\i shaliach l\u8217'kabala}. Given those (mistaken) facts, his stated intention would have been perfectly valid. We therefore cannot say he \u8220"really meant\u8221" something else; he meant exactly what he said, under a mistaken assumption about the facts. Since his intent \u8212- as he understood it \u8212- was coherent and legal under his own perception of reality, we have no basis to reinterpret his words. The distinction, then, is: mistake of law permits reinterpretation toward the legal form, because we assume a person wishes to act in accordance with law; mistake of fact does not, because the person\u8217's stated intent was already internally consistent.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Minor Wife Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava introduces a further {\i ta shma}: {\i ketana she\u8217'amra hitkabel li giti eino get ad she\u8217'yagi\u8217'a get l\u8217'yadah} \u8212- a minor girl cannot appoint a {\i shaliach}, so if she tells someone to receive her {\i get}, it is not a {\i get} until the {\i get} reaches her hand. The {\i Gemara} presses: but when she gets it, is she even then divorced? After all, if the husband gave the {\i get} to this person with the intent that he receive it for her as a {\i shaliach l\u8217'kabala}, and that appointment was void, the husband never designated him as his own {\i shaliach l\u8217'holacha} \u8212- so how does delivery to her work at all?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye answers: {\i kevan de\u8217'natan me\u8217'amen l\u8217'gareshah meimar megaresh k\u8217'heichi demigarsha} \u8212- since it is evident the husband intends to divorce her, we interpret the very act of handing the {\i get} to this person as a {\i shaliach l\u8217'holacha} appointment, even though he never said so explicitly. We reinterpret his act to align with the only legally operative mode by which divorce could occur.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} immediately limits this ruling: {\i hatam ihu yada de\u8217'ein shlichus l\u8217'katan} \u8212- there, the husband should know that a minor cannot appoint a {\i shaliach}, and therefore {\i gamar v\u8217'nasa l\u8217'shum halakha l\u8217'halakhta didei} \u8212- his act of giving the {\i get} is understood as an implicit {\i shaliach l\u8217'holacha} designation, because that is the only legally valid framework for what he was trying to accomplish. {\i Hacha ta\u8217'i} \u8212- but in the misrepresentation case, the error is factual, and the same logic does not apply.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} then continues with a {\i tanu rabbanan} that presents multiple permutations \u8212- a {\i shaliach} reporting {\i kabala} when appointed for {\i holacha}, and vice versa, combined with various responses from the husband ({\i holech v\u8217'ten lah}, {\i zechi lah v\u8217'hitkablah}) \u8212- all converging on the same ruling: she is divorced only {\i mishe\u8217'yagi\u8217'a get l\u8217'yadah}, upon actual receipt. The {\i Gemara} considers whether this {\i baraita} proves that {\i dibura didei kasamich} (the husband tracks the agent\u8217's report) or {\i dibura didah} (the husband tracks her actual words), but in each permutation the {\i Gemara} finds a way to read the ruling as consistent with either interpretation. The back-and-forth concludes without directly disproving Rav Nachman, though the weight of the analysis leans against his categorical formulation.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Tanna Kamma vs.\u160 ?Rabbi Na\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tanna Kamma Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now presents a {\i baraita} that engages the core {\i machlokes} directly. The case: the agent presents himself as the wife\u8217's {\i shaliach l\u8217'kabala}, and the husband says {\i holech v\u8217'ten lah} \u8212- \u8220"go and give it to her.\u8221" The {\i tanna kamma} rules: {\i ratza lachzor lo yachzir} \u8212- he may not retract. The {\i tanna kamma} further holds the same for {\i zechi lah v\u8217'hitkablah} \u8212- \u8220"acquire it for her and receive it.\u8221" In both formulations, the act is immediately binding. This is the {\i tanna kamma}\u8217's position that {\i holech k\u8217'zachi} \u8212- that {\i holech}, addressed to a {\i shaliach l\u8217'kabala}, conveys immediate legal effect.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi, whose position is then stated, agrees with the {\i tanna kamma} on this fundamental point: {\i be-chulan im ratza lachzor lo yachzir} \u8212- in all these formulations he cannot retract. However, Rabbi adds an important qualification: {\i aval im amar lo ee efshi she\u8217'tikabel lah ela holech v\u8217'ten lah} \u8212- if the husband preceded {\i holech} with the explicit rejection {\i ee efshi}, then {\i ratza lachzor yachzir} \u8212- he can retract. This is precisely the case from the opening of our sugya: when {\i holech} is framed by an explicit {\i ee efshi}, it reverts to its plain meaning of delivery, not immediate {\i zachi}. The {\i Gemara} notes: {\i Rabbi hainu tanna kamma} \u8212- Rabbi\u8217's position is essentially the same as the {\i tanna kamma}\u8217's. The {\i Gemara} explains this by saying that Rabbi is either clarifying the force of the {\i ee efshi} formulation, or identifying who the {\i tanna kamma} is.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Natan Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Natan takes a fundamentally different view: {\i holech v\u8217'ten lah ratza lachzor yachzor} \u8212- even when the husband says {\i holech} in the context of a {\i shaliach l\u8217'kabala}, he may retract. {\i Holech}, for Rabbi Natan, never means {\i zachi}, regardless of context. However, {\i zechi lah v\u8217'hitkablah ratza lachzor lo yachzor} \u8212- when the husband uses an explicit {\i zechi} or {\i hitkabel} formulation, it is immediately binding.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This makes the debate precise: the {\i tanna kamma} (and Rabbi) hold that {\i holech k\u8217'zachi} when addressed to a {\i shaliach l\u8217'kabala}; Rabbi Natan holds that even in that context {\i holech} means only delivery. The {\i Gemara} observes that this debate maps directly onto the question raised at the beginning of the {\i perek}: is {\i holech k\u8217'zachi dami}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} then raises a question about Rabbi Natan: does he agree that {\i heilech} is {\i k\u8217'zachi}? Logically it seems obvious \u8212- {\i heilech} is {\i hei lach}, \u8220"here it is for you,\u8221" an unmistakable act of transfer, entirely different from {\i holech}. The only reason one might hesitate is because the words sound superficially similar. But substantively they are entirely distinct.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Explicit Distinction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} introduces a {\i baraita} that makes the distinction explicit: {\i holech get zeh l\u8217'ishti ratza lachzor yachzor} \u8212- if a husband says {\i holech}, he can retract; {\i heilech get zeh l\u8217'ishti ratza lachzor lo yachzor} \u8212- if he says {\i heilech}, he cannot. The {\i Gemara} asks: {\i man sham\u8217'at lei d\u8217'amar b\u8217'holech ratza lachzor yachzor} \u8212- who holds that {\i holech} allows retraction even when the agent is a {\i shaliach l\u8217'kabala}? Rabbi Natan. And this same {\i baraita} says that {\i heilech} does not allow retraction. {\i Shma mina heilech k\u8217'zachi dami shma mina} \u8212- we therefore conclude that even according to Rabbi Natan, {\i heilech} is {\i k\u8217'zachi}, which is sensible, since the words are semantically unrelated despite their acoustic similarity.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The final resolution: the question of {\i holech k\u8217'zachi} is a {\i machlokes tannaim} between the {\i tanna kamma} (and Rabbi) on one side, and Rabbi Natan on the other. The {\i tanna kamma} holds {\i holech k\u8217'zachi} in the context of a {\i shaliach l\u8217'kabala}; Rabbi Natan holds it is not, but {\i heilech k\u8217'zachi} according to all opinions.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Holech Zachi Doubt\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Dual Agency\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having established the {\i machlokes}, the {\i Gemara} returns to a case that generates genuine uncertainty even in practice. The wife\u8217's agent presents himself to the husband as a {\i shaliach l\u8217'kabala}. The husband responds: {\i holech v\u8217'ten lah}. This is exactly the disputed case \u8212- does {\i holech} here mean {\i zachi}, following the {\i tanna kamma}, or mere delivery, following Rabbi Natan?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} asks: {\i l\u8217'meimra d\u8217'misfaka lei l\u8217'Rav ee holech k\u8217'zachi dami o lav k\u8217'zachi dami} \u8212- does this mean that Rav himself is uncertain about the {\i halacha} in this dispute?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Both Agents\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Abba, citing Rav Huna citing Rav, rules: {\i na\u8217'aseh shlucho u\u8217'shlucha} \u8212- the agent becomes simultaneously the husband\u8217's agent and the wife\u8217's agent. This is, as the {\i Gemara} effectively acknowledges, a way of expressing genuine uncertainty. The wife appointed him as her {\i shaliach l\u8217'kabala}; the husband\u8217's {\i holech} may or may not have ratified that role or instead designated him as a {\i shaliach l\u8217'holacha}. We cannot determine which.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Safek Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The practical consequence flows from this ambiguity: {\i vechol sheken} \u8212- and certainly, once she receives the {\i get}, she is divorced {\i mima nafshach}, regardless. The uncertainty pertains to the moment before she receives it. If the husband or the wife dies in the interim, the critical question becomes: is she an {\i almanah} (widow, who may perform {\i yibbum}) or a {\i grushah} (divorcee, who may not)? Rav\u8217's answer is that she must perform {\i chalitzah} and cannot perform {\i yibbum} \u8212- a practical expression of {\i safek}, because if she were certainly an {\i almanah} she could perform {\i yibbum}, and if she were certainly a {\i grushah} she would need neither. The requirement of {\i chalitzah} reflects genuine doubt as to her status.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Money Analogy\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} then observes that Rav himself seems to have ruled in an analogous monetary case: {\i holech maneh l\u8217'ploni she\u8217'ani chayav lo} \u8212- \u8220"go bring a hundred dinars to so-and-so whom I owe\u8221" \u8212- Rav rules {\i chayav b\u8217'achrayuto}, the sender remains responsible for the debt (since the recipient never appointed this person to collect on his behalf), but {\i v\u8217'im ba lachazor eino chozer} \u8212- the sender cannot demand the money back, because the {\i shaliach}\u8217's possession functions to some degree as acquisition for the recipient. This seems to imply that {\i holech} means {\i zachi} even in a non-{\i get} context. The {\i Gemara} resolves the tension: {\i hatam safek mamona l\u8217'kula hacha safek issura l\u8217'chumra} \u8212- there, in a monetary case, a {\i safek} defaults toward leniency (the money remains in the {\i shaliach}\u8217's hands), but here, in a matter of marital status, a {\i safek} requires stringency. Both are cases of {\i safek}; the difference lies only in how {\i safek} is resolved in each domain.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The conclusion of this entire discussion, then, is that the question of {\i holech k\u8217'zachi} is an unresolved {\i machlokes tannaim}, and even at the level of {\i amoraim} Rav leaves it as a {\i safek}, applying domain-appropriate rules to navigate the uncertainty.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Two Agents Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav\u8217's Restriction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now introduces a new ruling from Rav: {\i ein ha\u8217'isha osa shaliach l\u8217'kabel gitah miyad shaliach ba\u8217'alah} \u8212- a woman may not appoint an agent to receive her {\i get} from the husband\u8217's agent. Rabbi Chanina disagrees: {\i isha osa shaliach l\u8217'kabel gitah miyad shaliach ba\u8217'alah} \u8212- she may.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The scenario is one in which neither the husband nor the wife wishes to be present at the moment of delivery. The husband appoints an agent to deliver the {\i get}; the wife wishes to appoint her own agent to receive it from that {\i shaliach}. In principle, if each party may have a {\i shaliach}, why should two agents interposing between the parties be invalid? The question is what concern underlies Rav\u8217's restriction.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Honor Concern\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} offers one possible reason: {\i mishum bizayon d\u8217'baal} \u8212- out of concern for the honor of the husband. The husband, who holds all the legal power in this transaction, may feel affronted if his wife does not personally receive the {\i get} or attend to the matter herself. Even though the husband has appointed his own {\i shaliach} and will not be personally present either, there may be an implicit expectation that the wife \u8212- the recipient \u8212- will receive the {\i get} herself. If the husband learned after the fact that she had sent a {\i shaliach}, he could claim: had I known she intended to send an agent, I never would have authorized my {\i shaliach} to give it to someone other than her personally. In the case where the husband himself hands the {\i get} to the wife\u8217's {\i shaliach}, no such complaint is possible \u8212- he saw the situation and consented. But when it is his {\i shaliach} acting, and he is not present, the concern of {\i bizayon d\u8217'baal} becomes operative.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Indirect Acquisition\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The second possible reason: {\i mishum chatzer haba\u8217'ah me\u8217'achareha} \u8212- a concern rooted in the laws of {\i chatzer}, an enclosed domain that can effect acquisition. The rule is that if I place a {\i get} in a field that at that moment belongs to the wife, she acquires it and is divorced. But if the field was not yet hers at the moment of placement \u8212- if she subsequently purchased the field with the {\i get} already inside \u8212- she is not divorced, because acquisition via {\i chatzer} must be contemporaneous with ownership.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The concern here is analogous. The husband\u8217's {\i shaliach} holds the {\i get} in his hands in the capacity of the husband\u8217's agent. If the wife\u8217's {\i shaliach} then \u8220"receives\u8221" the {\i get} from him without any active transfer occurring \u8212- especially if her {\i shaliach} was appointed after the husband\u8217's {\i shaliach} was already in possession of the {\i get} \u8212- this resembles the case of a field acquired after the {\i get} was placed in it. There was no genuine moment of delivery; the {\i get} simply transitioned from one holding to another without a clear act of {\i netina}. The {\i Gemara} distinguishes: if the wife had appointed her {\i shaliach} before the husband appointed his, the concern of {\i chatzer haba\u8217'ah me\u8217'achareha} is less applicable, since her {\i shaliach}\u8217's status pre-dates the husband\u8217's {\i shaliach}\u8217's possession.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} suggests a more straightforward {\i nafka mina} between the two reasons: if the husband explicitly states that he is content for his {\i shaliach} to deliver to the wife\u8217's {\i shaliach}, the concern of {\i bizayon d\u8217'baal} is obviously inapplicable \u8212- he has waived any such objection. Whether the divorce is valid in that case depends entirely on whether the {\i chatzer haba\u8217'ah me\u8217'achareha} concern survives the husband\u8217's explicit consent, which it might, since that concern is rabbinic in character and not dependent on the husband\u8217's subjective preference. An additional {\i nafka mina} concerns {\i b\u8217'diavad}: if the restriction is grounded in honor, perhaps {\i b\u8217'diavad} the {\i get} is valid; if it is grounded in the acquisition concern, {\i b\u8217'diavad} may be more stringent.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Chanina Permits\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Chanina holds that the wife may appoint her own agent to receive from the husband\u8217's agent. The {\i Gemara} reports that Rav Nachman said: {\i im ita l\u8217'deRabbi Chanina avdinan uvda} \u8212- had Rabbi Chanina\u8217's ruling been before me in the relevant case, I would have ruled accordingly. The case in question: a man sent a {\i get} to his wife; the {\i shaliach} found her kneading dough and therefore her hands were occupied. He said to her {\i heilech giteich} \u8212- \u8220"here is your {\i get}.\u8221" She replied: {\i leihavei b\u8217'yadech} \u8212- \u8220"you hold it for me,\u8221" thereby attempting to turn the husband\u8217's {\i shaliach l\u8217'holacha} into her {\i shaliach l\u8217'kabala}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 One vs.\u160 ?Two Agents\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava immediately objects to Rav Nachman: {\i im ita d\u8217'Rabbi Chanina, avdas bei uvda? Halo hadra shlichus eitzel habaal} \u8212- even according to Rabbi Chanina, would you really have ruled that way? What Rabbi Chanina permits is the scenario of two genuine agents: the husband\u8217's {\i shaliach} gives the {\i get} to the wife\u8217's {\i shaliach}, with both parties maintaining their distinct roles. What the woman in this case attempted was fundamentally different: she tried to convert the husband\u8217's {\i shaliach} himself into her {\i shaliach}, not by receiving from him but by having him change sides.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is the key distinction. When there are two separate agents, there is a genuine act of delivery: the husband\u8217's {\i shaliach} gives, and the wife\u8217's {\i shaliach} receives. Both identities persist through and after the act. But when the wife attempts to transform the husband\u8217's {\i shaliach} into her own \u8212- as in this case, where she says \u8220"you hold it for me\u8221" \u8212- there is no moment of delivery at all. The agent\u8217's identity would have to shift from representing the husband to representing the wife without any intervening act of {\i netina}. The {\i Gemara} phrases this as {\i hadra shlichus eitzel habaal} \u8212- the {\i shlichus} would have to revert, or more precisely, there would be no point at which the {\i shliach} could return to the husband and report: \u8220"I have completed the delivery,\u8221" because his identity changed before the act was accomplished.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This case, then, is not what Rabbi Chanina had in mind. Rabbi Chanina\u8217's leniency applies to two distinct agents occupying their respective roles simultaneously. Transforming a single agent\u8217's identity mid-transaction is a categorically different problem \u8212- one that closely resembles the very concern of {\i chatzer haba\u8217'ah me\u8217'achareha} that underlies Rav\u8217's restriction, inasmuch as there is never a genuine, identifiable moment when the {\i get} passes from one party\u8217's legal domain to another\u8217's. This distinction will be taken up further in the continuation of the sugya.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Gittin 64\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Dual Shlichus Problem\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Wife\u8217's Shaliach Reception\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The daf opens in the middle of 63b, continuing an extended discussion about a fundamental question in the laws of {\i gittin}: can a wife appoint a {\i shaliach} to receive her {\i get} at the very same time that the husband appoints a {\i shaliach} to deliver it? Two concerns animate this question. The first is whether this arrangement resembles {\i chatzer haba mi-kochi} \u8212- that the wife is somehow not actively involved in receiving the {\i get} \u8212- raising doubts about whether her appointment of a {\i shaliach} constitutes genuine reception. The second is whether the husband would fundamentally object to such an arrangement and therefore never truly consent to it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shaliach Role Switch\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara addresses this through a narrative case. A man sent a {\i get} to his wife. The {\i shaliach} went and found her kneading dough \u8212- her hands were occupied, which is why she did what she did next. The {\i shaliach} said to her, {\i heilach gita\u8217'ich}, here is your {\i get}. She responded: {\i yehei b\u8217'yadcha} \u8212- hold onto it. In other words, she appointed this very same man, the husband\u8217's {\i shaliach}, to now serve as her {\i shaliach l\u8217'kabala}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chazar Shlichus Principle\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Nachman said: if we were to rule in accordance with Rabbi Chanina \u8212- that a wife can indeed appoint a {\i shaliach} to receive the {\i get} from the husband\u8217's {\i shaliach} \u8212- then I would have ruled that this case is valid. After all, the husband had appointed the man as his {\i shaliach}, and the wife subsequently appointed him as her {\i shaliach} to receive it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Simultaneous Representation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava objected: even if we rule like Rabbi Chanina, this case would still be problematic \u8212- {\i lo chazar shlichus eitzel ha\u8217'ba\u8217'al}. There is a principle that the {\i shaliach}\u8217's role as the husband\u8217's representative must persist through the completion of his {\i shlichus}, such that he can return and report that he fulfilled his mission. This does not mean literally that he must remain alive afterward, nor that the {\i shlichus} cannot be time-limited. Rather, the problem here is that at the very moment of giving the {\i get}, the {\i shaliach} cannot simultaneously occupy both roles \u8212- representing the husband in the act of giving while also representing the wife in the act of receiving. When he switches to become the wife\u8217's {\i shaliach}, he ceases to represent the husband. There is also a lack of a proper act of {\i nesina}. He has essentially given the {\i get} to himself, which is no giving at all. The {\i shlichus} on the husband\u8217's side therefore fails at the critical moment.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Ami\u8217's Opinion\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The question was sent to Rabbi Ami for his ruling. He responded: {\i lo chazar shlichus eitzel ha\u8217'ba\u8217'al} \u8212- the {\i shlichus} did not return to the husband, meaning this arrangement is invalid.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Safek Megureshes Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Chiya bar Abba declared that the matter requires further investigation \u8212- it is a significant question with major consequences. They sent the question back to him, and after some time, sent it again asking what he had concluded. His response, offered with a certain wry humor given the context of {\i shlichus}, was: just as you have repeated doubts and keep asking me, I too have doubts. I cannot resolve this question.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Since it involves {\i davar she\u8217'be\u8217'erva} \u8212- a matter affecting personal status \u8212- he was compelled to rule with caution: she is a {\i safek megureshes}. Practically, this means that if the husband were to die and there is a {\i yavam}, she cannot perform {\i yibbum} because she might already be a {\i gerusha}, yet she is not free from the {\i yibbum} obligation entirely because she might be an {\i almana}. She is therefore required to perform {\i chalitza}. The case thus closes with a formal {\i safek} as to whether the principle of {\i chazar shlichus eitzel ha\u8217'ba\u8217'al} invalidates this arrangement entirely.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Nafsha Case & Shlichus Completed\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now introduces a case involving a man known as Nafsha. Witnesses were sent to write his {\i get}, but they wrote the name \u8220"Tafsha\u8221" instead. Rabbi Yitzchak bar Shmuel bar Marta ruled in the name of Rav: the witnesses have completed their {\i shlichus}. Since the husband appointed them as his {\i shlichim} to write the {\i get} and they wrote one \u8212- even if incorrectly \u8212- they have discharged their agency. If a new {\i get} is needed, the husband must reappoint them.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rewriting Defective Get\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava challenged this: when the husband said \u8220"write,\u8221" did he say {\i kesuvu v\u8217'chasuvu} \u8212- write a piece of pottery? He did not tell them to write just any document. Writing a {\i get} means writing a {\i kosher get}. Since they did not write a proper {\i get}, they have not yet fulfilled their {\i shlichus}, and they may rewrite it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava then addressed a different scenario: suppose the witnesses wrote a perfectly valid {\i get} but it was subsequently lost before they could deliver it. In that case, {\i asu eidim shlichusam} \u8212- the witnesses have completed their agency of writing. If the husband wants another {\i get} written, he would need to appoint them anew.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Unified Writing & Delivery\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Nachman challenged Rava\u8217's latter position: when the husband said \u8220"write a {\i get},\u8221" did he say {\i kesuvu v\u8217'anchu b\u8217'kisaychu} \u8212- write it and keep it in your pocket? The entire purpose of writing a {\i get} was to write it and deliver it. Writing and delivery are one indivisible package. Until a kosher {\i get} has actually been delivered to the wife, the {\i shlichus} is not complete, and the witnesses may write and rewrite as many times as necessary \u8212- {\i kosvin v\u8217'nosnin afilu mea pe\u8217'amim}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafot: Bad vs.\u160 ?Lost Get\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot raises a careful distinction. Tosafot understands that where a {\i get} was written and is not clearly defective but its validity is uncertain \u8212- one does not know whether it was written properly \u8212- the witnesses have completed their {\i shlichus} and it is considered {\i kasher}. They should not write another {\i get} in that circumstance.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This position seems puzzling at first glance. If we are uncertain whether they completed their {\i shlichus}, why not simply write another {\i get}? If the first was valid they did not need the second, and if it was not, the second would fix the problem. Tosafot\u8217's concern is that one should not give the woman a {\i get} that is only {\i safek kasher}. Since you do not wish to hand her a document of uncertain validity, and yet if it was in fact valid you are no longer authorized to write another, the resulting difficulty is that you cannot hand over a {\i get} of which you are proud. Tosafot therefore concludes: it is best to instruct the husband to say explicitly that he authorizes the witnesses to do whatever is necessary until a fully kosher {\i get} is delivered with certainty. That formulation resolves all the potential difficulties.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Get Given to Shaliach Lost\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A question was then posed to Rava and Rav Nachman: suppose the husband says to witnesses {\i kitvu u-tenu le-shaliach} \u8212- write the {\i get} and give it to the {\i shaliach}. The witnesses do so, but the {\i shaliach} subsequently loses the {\i get} or dies in transit before delivering it. What is the rule? Have the witnesses been {\i siluki} \u8212- effectively dismissed, their role complete \u8212- such that they would need to be reappointed? Or perhaps the husband\u8217's instruction to give the {\i get} to a {\i shaliach} rather than deliver it themselves was merely a concession to spare them the trouble of the journey, while their underlying obligation still encompassed ensuring the delivery. Since the {\i get} was never in fact delivered, they remain empowered to rewrite.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The question turns on how one reads the instruction: did the husband intend the witnesses\u8217' mandate to end the moment they handed the document to the {\i shaliach}, or did he simply mean to make their task easier while still expecting them to see the {\i get} through to completion? The Gemara leaves the matter unresolved \u8212- {\i teiku}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ravina\u8217's Refinement\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Ravina posed a related refinement to Rav Ashi: what if the husband says not merely \u8220"give it to the {\i shaliach}\u8221" but {\i ve-yolich} \u8212- \u8220"give it to the {\i shaliach} and let him deliver it to her\u8221"? In that formulation, the husband explicitly mentions to the witnesses that the {\i shaliach} is to be the one to hand it over. Perhaps this explicit mention suggests that part of the witnesses\u8217' responsibility is to ensure the delivery occurs, and until it does, their {\i shlichus} is incomplete. This too is left as {\i teiku}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kabbalah Language Baraita\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Mishnah} concludes with Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel ruling that even a woman who says {\i ha-motzi li gitti} \u8212- take from me my {\i get} \u8212- has appointed the recipient as a {\i shaliach kabbalah}, not a {\i shaliach holachah}, and the husband therefore cannot retract after handing over the {\i get}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i Baraita} elaborates on the language of reception: {\i toli}, {\i sali}, {\i yehei b\u8217'yadcha} \u8212- \u8220"lift it for me,\u8221" \u8220"carry it for me,\u8221" \u8220"let it be in your hand\u8221" \u8212- all of these are formulations of {\i lashon kabbalah}, not formulations of conveyance. The Gemara makes clear that Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel is not introducing a novel controversy but is rather clarifying a matter on which there is no real dispute. Thus, the determination of whether a {\i shaliach} is a {\i shaliach holachah} or a {\i shaliach l\u8217'kabala} depends both on the language used and on the context in which that language was spoken \u8212- and both of these have significant consequences for whether the husband retains the ability to retract and for what type of {\i shlichus} the parties have created.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Evidence & Proof Requirements\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two Sets of Witnesses\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now turns to a different and very important problem within the law of {\i shlichus} in {\i gittin}: how one proves that a valid {\i get} was in fact delivered through a {\i shaliach}. This question operates on two levels. First, there is the evidentiary question of how the woman demonstrates that she is divorced. Second, and more fundamentally, there is the question of whether {\i eidei kiyum} \u8212- witnesses whose presence makes an act halachically binding, not merely provable after the fact \u8212- are required for the {\i gerushin} itself. This second question implicates the principle that {\i gerushin} is a {\i davar she\u8217'be\u8217'erva} and {\i ein davar she\u8217'be\u8217'erva pachot mi-shnayim}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Mishnah} rules: a woman who said to someone {\i hitkabel li giti} \u8212- receive my {\i get} for me \u8212- requires two sets of witnesses. Two witnesses must testify that she appointed him as her {\i shaliach} in their presence, and two witnesses must testify that he actually received the {\i get} from the husband and tore it up in their presence. The tearing, the Gemara notes, reflects a particular historical circumstance \u8212- a period of Roman persecution during which carrying documents connected to Jewish religious practice was dangerous. The essential testimony, however, is that the {\i get} was received, not that it was torn.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafot\u8217's Challenge\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot raises a serious question. The {\i Mishnah} then adds: {\i afilu hen ha-rishonim ve-hen ha-acharonim} \u8212- even if the same witnesses appear at both the appointment and the reception, that is sufficient. Tosafot asks: what is the force of {\i afilu} here? One might have expected the opposite \u8212- that the same witnesses who were present at both stages would be {\i preferable}, since they can testify as a single body to everything they witnessed without any concern about combining half-testimonies from different groups.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The problem Tosafot is pointing to is the principle of {\i davar v\u8217'lo chatzi davar}: witnesses testify to a matter, not to half a matter. If the witnesses who saw the {\i get} received knew nothing about the prior appointment, how can their testimony about reception alone constitute valid {\i eidut} on the act of {\i gerushin}? The {\i get} was given to some man by the husband, but those witnesses may not have known he was the wife\u8217's {\i shaliach}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might ask why the woman\u8217's own testimony cannot simply resolve the matter \u8212- she appointed him, after all. The answer is that her testimony alone does not constitute objective evidence. The husband may be contesting the validity of the {\i get}, and merely because he trusted the {\i shaliach} at the time of delivery does not mean his wife\u8217's word alone suffices to prove retroactively that the {\i shaliach} was properly authorized. A third party who held himself out as an agent and was believed at the time cannot thereby establish the transaction\u8217's validity if the principal later contests it without independent objective evidence.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot therefore explains the {\i afilu} differently: it tells us that we do not suspect the wife of having coordinated false witnesses. Ordinarily, the very reason one uses a {\i shaliach} is that the wife is in a distant location and cannot receive the {\i get} herself. Given that, it would be quite unlikely that the same witnesses happened to be present both in the location where she appointed him and in the entirely different location where he received the {\i get}. One might therefore suspect collusion. The {\i afilu} dispels this concern: even if the same witnesses appear at both events, they are believed and we do not suspect the wife of bribery.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 As for the underlying question of {\i chatzi davar}, Tosafot notes that there are circumstances under which additional parties \u8212- such as the {\i shaliach} himself \u8212- could be believed to supply the missing information about his appointment, or the husband could be believed in certain respects. When such alternative means exist to fill the gap, it may not constitute a true case of {\i chatzi davar} that disqualifies the testimony.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Same or Different Witnesses\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Mishnah} further states: {\i v\u8217'echad min ha-rishonim v\u8217'echad min ha-acharonim v\u8217'echad mitztaref imahem} \u8212- even if a single witness overlaps between the two groups, that is also valid. Tosafot explains the novelty here as well: one might have suspected that a single individual who appears at both stages \u8212- say, Shimon \u8212- was hired by the wife to stitch together a false narrative, testifying alongside Reuven that the {\i shaliach} was appointed and alongside Levi that he received the {\i get}. The {\i Mishnah} tells us we do not entertain this suspicion. The upshot is that it does not matter whether the witnesses at the appointment and the reception are entirely the same, entirely different, or partially overlapping \u8212- the requirement is simply that there be witnesses at both stages.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shaliach\u8217's Credibility\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now asks: the {\i Mishnah} speaks of a {\i shaliach l\u8217'kabala} who received the {\i get} and tore it up. Since he is no longer holding the {\i get} intact, why are all these witnesses necessary? If he were still holding the {\i get} whole, might his own credibility suffice to establish what occurred?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Huna vs.\u160 ?Rav Chisda\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A dispute is recorded regarding a {\i shalish} \u8212- a third party holding the {\i get} \u8212- where the husband claims he gave it to him only as a {\i pikadon}, for safekeeping, while the {\i shaliach} claims it was given to him as a {\i get} for the wife to be divorced through. Rav Huna rules that the husband is believed. Rav Chisda rules that the {\i shaliach} is believed, on the grounds of {\i d\u8217'haimneih} \u8212- by entrusting the document to him, the husband has implicitly accepted that whatever the {\i shaliach} says about it is reliable.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Same Town Qualifier\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot qualifies the entire dispute: it applies only when all three parties \u8212- the husband, the {\i shaliach}, and the wife \u8212- are present in the same town. Rav Huna\u8217's reasoning for believing the husband is that if he truly intended to divorce his wife, he could and would have handed the {\i get} to her directly without any intermediary. That argument collapses if the wife is in a distant city. When the wife is elsewhere, the husband obviously could not have given it to her directly, and therefore the very fact that he chose to use a {\i shaliach} cannot be taken as evidence against the {\i get} being for divorce. This also explains, Tosafot notes, why a {\i shaliach} delivering a {\i get} from overseas is believed when he says {\i b\u8217'fanai nichtav u-v\u8217'fanai nechtam} \u8212- the husband has no plausible argument that the {\i get} was only a {\i pikadon}, since the wife is plainly not available to receive it herself.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hemnei Principle\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Chisda\u8217's position rests on the principle of {\i hemnei}: when one entrusts an object to a third party, one implicitly agrees to be bound by whatever that third party later asserts about the nature of the transaction. Tosafot presses the point: even if we accept this principle of implicit trust, how does the {\i shaliach}\u8217's say-so create an act of {\i gerushin}? The husband may have trusted him, but if no {\i get} was actually given as a {\i get}, the wife is simply not divorced regardless of what the {\i shaliach} claims.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Money vs.\u160 ?Get Distinction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara addresses this through a {\i Baraita} that records: {\i hoda\u8217'at ba\u8217'al din k\u8217'me\u8217'ah edim dami} \u8212- an admission by a party against his own interest is equivalent to a hundred witnesses. And: {\i v\u8217'shalish ne\u8217'eman mishneihem} \u8212- the third-party custodian is believed more than either principal. The {\i Baraita} illustrates a case where two parties dispute the context in which funds were deposited with a third party, and the third party\u8217's account is definitive.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara draws the parallel: here too, the husband and wife dispute the context of the {\i get} entrusted to the {\i shaliach}, and by analogy the {\i shaliach} should be believed. The Gemara then asks whether this principle extends to {\i gittin}. The response is a critical distinction: {\i shani mamon d\u8217'it-yahiv l\u8217'mechila} \u8212- money is different because one can be {\i mochel} on it. By entrusting money to a third party, you can implicitly say: whatever he decides about this money, I will acquiesce, including simply giving it away. That implicit acquiescence \u8212- {\i hemnei} \u8212- works because money is transferable and the principal can effectively ratify whatever the custodian asserts retroactively, simply by choosing not to contest it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i get}, however, is different. Even if the husband implicitly trusted the {\i shaliach}, if he never actually gave the {\i get} as a {\i get} \u8212- with {\i da\u8217'at} to divorce his wife \u8212- the act of {\i gerushin} never occurred. The implicit agreement to trust the custodian cannot conjure a {\i da\u8217'at} of divorce where none existed. A {\i get} cannot be given away by mere acquiescence the way money can be waived. The Gemara does entertain the possibility that the {\i hemnei} principle could operate here as well \u8212- that when you hand a {\i get} to a {\i shaliach} you are implicitly saying that whatever he later says about its purpose will retroactively define your intent, meaning that if he says you meant to divorce your wife, that becomes your {\i da\u8217'at} at the time of the giving. But the Gemara acknowledges this is a far greater leap than the monetary analogy.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Whole Get Held\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Returning to the {\i Mishnah}\u8217's requirement of witnesses, the Gemara asks: the {\i Mishnah} is discussing a case where the {\i shaliach} tore the {\i get}. But if he were still holding the {\i get} intact, why would witnesses be needed at all? If he is holding a whole, signed {\i get}, that fact itself constitutes circumstantial evidence that the husband gave it to him. Given that he is holding it, he should have the special standing of a custodian who is believed regarding the context of what he holds. The {\i shaliach} could then simply state that he received it as a {\i get}, and the wife would be divorced.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot refines this: why isn\u8217't the {\i shaliach} believed even when the {\i get} is torn, on the basis that we can see he is holding torn pieces? The answer is that torn pieces of paper could have come from anywhere \u8212- there is no way to verify that these particular fragments were once the husband\u8217's {\i get} and were torn only after reception. A whole, signed {\i get} in the {\i shaliach}\u8217's hands is a different matter: it carries with it the implicit credential that the husband entrusted him with it, and that credential supports his believability about the circumstances of the transfer.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Eidei Mesira Kartei\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But even granting that a {\i shaliach} holding a whole {\i get} has enhanced credibility, there is a further requirement. The Gemara says: {\i ha mani? Rabbi Elazar hi d\u8217'amar edei mesirah kartei} \u8212- this reflects the position of Rabbi Elazar, who holds that witnesses to the actual handover are what effects the {\i get}, not the witnesses who signed the document. According to Rabbi Elazar, {\i edei mesira kartei} \u8212- it is specifically the witnesses who witness the act of the {\i get} being handed over that create the binding act of {\i gerushin}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is significant. It is not enough to have circumstantial evidence, however strong, that the {\i get} was received. Even if one could deduce from the {\i shaliach}\u8217's possession of an intact document that he must have received it from the husband, that would not satisfy {\i edei mesira}, because {\i edei mesira} does not mean proving after the fact that a handover occurred. It means that witnesses must directly witness the act of the {\i get} being passed from hand to hand. The {\i gerushin} itself, not merely the evidentiary record of it, requires that act to have been witnessed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It follows that even if a {\i shaliach} is holding a whole {\i get} and would be believed regarding what the husband intended, that would still not replace the need for witnesses to the delivery itself \u8212- at least according to Rabbi Elazar.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A note on what requires witnesses: there is an asymmetry worth observing. The husband\u8217's {\i shaliach} appears to carry his own credential in the form of the {\i get} itself \u8212- the fact that he possesses the document is in itself a form of testimony to his appointment. The wife\u8217's {\i shaliach}, by contrast, derives his status entirely from her words, which are unaccompanied by any physical credential. This may explain why the need for witnesses to the appointment was felt most acutely with respect to the wife\u8217's {\i shaliach}, while the husband\u8217's {\i shaliach} gains some implicit credibility from the document he carries.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Torn Get, No Holdings\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now considers the situation where the {\i get} was torn, the {\i shaliach} is not holding it, and only testimonies are available. The husband says he gave the {\i get} to the {\i shaliach l\u8217'holacha} as his agent to deliver it. The {\i shaliach} says yes, the husband gave it to him as a {\i shaliach l\u8217'holacha}, and he duly delivered it to the woman. The woman says she received it and it has since been lost. The husband does not contest his having given it; no one challenges the basic facts. Yet there is a problem: how do we know the woman is actually divorced?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The husband\u8217's admission that he appointed the {\i shaliach} establishes that part of the chain. But the {\i shaliach}\u8217's testimony that he delivered it is that of a single witness with no document in hand, giving him no special standing. The woman\u8217's testimony that she received it is also that of a single witness without a document. There are no {\i edei mesirah}. The Gemara asks why we cannot simply believe the {\i shaliach}, and the answer already established is that {\i mi ka nafik gita mi-tusei yadei l\u8217'heimneih} \u8212- the {\i shaliach} is believed only when the {\i get} is in his hands. Without it, he has no special credibility.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then asks: let us at least believe the husband. Rav Chiya bar Avin said in the name of Rabbi Yochanan: a husband who says \u8220"I divorced my wife\u8221" is believed, because he could do so now if he wished. By the same logic, the husband\u8217's statement that he appointed this man as his {\i shaliach} should be believed. Indeed, the Gemara accepts this: we believe the husband that he made the man a {\i shaliach l\u8217'holacha}. What we do not know is whether the {\i shaliach} actually delivered it, and for that we cannot rely on the {\i shaliach}\u8217's unaccompanied word.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chazaka Shaliach\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara asks: rather than requiring witnesses, why not simply invoke the {\i chazaka} that a {\i shaliach} performs his mission? Rabbi Yitzchak taught: if one says to his {\i shaliach}, \u8220"Go and betroth a woman for me,\u8221" and the {\i shaliach} dies without reporting back, the husband is forbidden to marry any woman in the world, lest any one of them be his betrothed wife\u8217's relative. Tosafot notes that this seems extreme \u8212- one should also bear in mind that the woman, whoever she is, would herself presumably know she was betrothed. Tosafot therefore suggests this is a {\i knas}, a punitive measure for irresponsible conduct, but it is grounded in the underlying presumption that the {\i shaliach} did carry out his mission. This demonstrates the principle of {\i chazaka shaliach oseh shlichuso}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Why then can that {\i chazaka} not resolve the present case? The Gemara answers: {\i hani mili l\u8217'chumra} \u8212- the {\i chazaka} is applied strictly, to create prohibitions. {\i Aval l\u8217'kula lo} \u8212- we do not invoke it leniently, to permit a woman to remarry. Presuming the {\i shaliach} delivered the {\i get} would mean permitting the woman as a divorced woman, and for that leniency one cannot rely on presumption alone.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Woman\u8217's Chazaka\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then asks: why not believe the woman herself? Rav Hamnuna taught: a woman who says to her husband \u8220"you divorced me\u8221" is believed, on the basis of a {\i chazaka} \u8212- {\i ein isha me\u8217'izah paneiha bifnei ba\u8217'alah}, a woman would not be so brazen as to lie to her husband\u8217's face about something of this magnitude unless it were true. This is a striking ruling: after establishing that a very high standard of proof is required at every stage of this chain, the Gemara now considers whether a single {\i chazaka} about human behavior can replace all of that.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Witness Synthesis\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara resolves the tension. Rav Hamnuna\u8217's {\i chazaka} applies only when there is nothing that supports her claim \u8212- when she stands entirely alone in her assertion, she is presumed to be telling the truth because brazenness without any corroboration is implausible. But here, the {\i shaliach} is backing up her account, and the husband himself does not know whether the delivery actually occurred. Given that backdrop, she is not without support, and therefore it would not take unusual brazenness for her to make a false claim under these circumstances. The {\i chazaka} therefore does not apply.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This brings the discussion back to the {\i Mishnah}: witnesses are essential precisely in cases where neither the {\i shaliach} nor the woman holds the {\i get}, and neither has the special credibility that would otherwise come with possession of the document. The witnesses serve two functions simultaneously: they provide the objective evidence that the chain of {\i shlichus} was properly established and executed, and \u8212- depending on the position one holds regarding {\i edei kiyum} \u8212- they may also constitute the required witnessing of the act of {\i gerushin} itself. When the {\i get} is in someone\u8217's hands, that physical presence may supply some of what the witnesses would otherwise need to cover. When it is not \u8212- when the {\i get} has been torn, lost, or is otherwise unavailable \u8212- witnesses become indispensable to the entire construct.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Minor Girl & Father\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Naara Me\u8217'urasa Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The next {\i Mishnah} addresses a case of pre-assigned {\i shlichus} that arises naturally from a particular legal status. The subject is a {\i naara me\u8217'urasa} \u8212- a girl who is betrothed and who has reached the age of twelve but is not yet twelve and a half, and therefore has not yet achieved the status of {\i bogeres}. During this window, she is legally an adult in certain respects, yet her father retains authority over her. She is simultaneously {\i me\u8217'urasa} \u8212- betrothed \u8212- and still under paternal control. The reason the {\i Mishnah} focuses on this specific category is that it requires all three conditions at once: she must be an adult ({\i naara}), she must be under her father\u8217's authority, and she must be in a state of marriage from which a {\i get} is required. A {\i nesua} \u8212- one who has entered {\i chuppah} \u8212- would be fully out of her father\u8217's control and is therefore outside this particular category.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Get Reception Authority\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Mishnah} states: {\i hi v\u8217'aviha mekablin et gittah} \u8212- she and her father can receive her {\i get}. Both represent her interests. But Rav Yehuda rules: {\i ein shtei yadayim zachot ke\u8217'achat} \u8212- two hands cannot acquire simultaneously. The question then is whose hand prevails. Rav Yehuda rules: {\i aviha mekabel et gittah bilvad} \u8212- the father alone receives her {\i get}. Where the father is present, his authority is dominant, and the {\i get} must be given to him rather than to her.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Mishnah} then adds a general principle: {\i ve-chol she-eina yechola lishmor et gittah eina yechola l\u8217'hitgareish} \u8212- any woman who cannot safeguard her own {\i get} cannot be divorced.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rashi vs.\u160 ?Tosafot: Understanding Divorce\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rashi and Tosafot dispute the meaning of this principle. Rashi understands it to mean that even when the father receives the {\i get}, if the child is so young that she does not understand what {\i gerushin} means \u8212- if she cannot grasp that she is divorced and act accordingly, described in the Gemara with the phrase {\i meshaleches v\u8217'eina chozeres}, that she goes away and does not return the next day thinking nothing has changed \u8212- then the {\i get} cannot effect a divorce even through the father\u8217's reception. Rashi requires a minimal level of comprehension on the girl\u8217's part as a condition of the {\i gerushin} taking effect, even if she is not the one receiving it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot disagrees. Tosafot argues that the issue of understanding pertains only to the act of reception by the girl herself. If the {\i get} is given to the father, it is entirely irrelevant how young or uncomprehending the girl is. After all, the Talmud elsewhere permits a father to betroth his daughter when she is extremely young \u8212- if one can be married off as an infant, one can presumably be divorced under the same conditions. For Tosafot, {\i yoda\u8217'at lishmor gittah} is a condition for when the girl herself receives the {\i get}, not for when the father does so on her behalf.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Torah\u8217's Extra Hand Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara asks: what is the underlying dispute between the {\i Rabbanan} who say {\i hi v\u8217'aviha} and Rabbi Yehuda who says only the father? The {\i Rabbanan} hold {\i yadi yeseira zachei lah Rachmana} \u8212- the Torah granted her an extra hand. That is, the Torah provided her with her own independent capacity to receive the {\i get} in addition to, not instead of, the father\u8217's authority. Both hands are available. Rabbi Yehuda holds: {\i bimkom aviha yadah dilah lav klum hi} \u8212- when the father is present, her hand is nullified entirely. His authority does not merely supplement hers; it displaces it. She has no standing of her own as a recipient when her father is there to act on her behalf.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot raises a further dimension of this dispute: even regarding when the girl can receive the {\i get} on her own \u8212- either when there is no father or in a case where one might give it to her \u8212- when precisely does that right begin? Tosafot\u8217's view is that even as a {\i ketana}, she may receive a {\i get}, because receiving a {\i get} is a passive act requiring no legal capacity to transact. The point about {\i naara} is therefore not that she newly acquires this right upon reaching twelve; rather, it highlights that even once she becomes a {\i naara} and the father\u8217's authority would otherwise persist, the status of {\i naara} does not remove the father from the picture. We will continue with this discussion in the next session.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Gittin 65\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Shlichus & Na\u8217'arah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Father as Shaliach\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The daf begins in the middle of 64b with the phrase {\i v\u8217'chol she\u8217'eina yachola lishmor gita} \u8212- any girl who cannot watch her own {\i get} cannot be divorced. To situate this discussion, we must recall the context: the Gemara is exploring the concept of {\i shlichus}, specifically the automatic or permanent {\i shaliach} that the Torah assigns to a father to represent his minor daughter. The Mishnah addresses the case of a {\i na\u8217'arah me\u8217'orasah} \u8212- a girl between twelve and twelve-and-a-half years old who is {\i me\u8217'orasah} but not yet {\i nisuah}. Since she has not yet begun living with her husband, she has not been emancipated from her father\u8217's authority. In this intermediate stage, she can receive her own {\i get} and represent herself, and simultaneously her father can receive the {\i get} on her behalf, given that he retains legal control over her and is entitled to her benefits during this period.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rashi vs.\u160 ?Tosafot\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 When the girl is a {\i ketanah} \u8212- a minor below the age of twelve \u8212- Rashi holds that only her father may receive the {\i get} on her behalf. Tosafot disagree sharply: even as a {\i ketanah}, the {\i get} may always be given to the girl herself. Since she is a passive recipient in the transaction, no {\i da\u8217'as} on her part is required. She always represents herself in this sense.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The practical debate between Rashi and Tosafot becomes sharpest when we examine the Mishnah\u8217's statement about {\i v\u8217'chol she\u8217'eina yachola lishmor gita}. Rashi reads this as a requirement of status: even if the {\i get} is given to the father, if the girl herself is so young that she would not understand that she has been divorced, the divorce is ineffective. For Rashi, it is not merely about the physical act of giving the {\i get} \u8212- there must be some comprehension on the part of the girl that she has achieved the status of being divorced.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Understanding Get\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot\u8217's reading is more compelling and will guide the analysis going forward. For Tosafot, {\i v\u8217'chol she\u8217'eina yachola lishmor gita} addresses solely the act of giving the {\i get} to the girl herself: if she does not understand what she has received, the act of delivery to her is meaningless. But if the {\i get} is given to the father, even a one-day-old infant can be divorced \u8212- just as a one-day-old infant can be married through her father\u8217's agency. If a girl can acquire the status of marriage at any age through her father, she can equivalently acquire the status of divorce through her father. The only constraint is whether giving the {\i get} to the girl herself constitutes a valid act of reception. Tosafot thus explains the Mishnah as speaking about cases where the {\i get} is delivered directly to the girl.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Under this reading, the phrase emphasizes whether she understands the physical object she has been given and appreciates its significance. It is not about the status of being divorced \u8212- that can happen regardless \u8212- it is about whether giving the {\i get} to her constitutes a legally meaningful delivery.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ketanah Yoda\u8217'at\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara cites a {\i Tannaic baraita}: {\i ketanah yoda\u8217'at lishmor gita} \u8212- a minor who knows how to watch her {\i get} \u8212- {\i mitgareshet}, she can be divorced. {\i V\u8217'she\u8217'eina yoda\u8217'at lishmor gita} \u8212- if she does not know how to watch her {\i get} \u8212- {\i eina mitgareshet}, she cannot be divorced. The {\i baraita} then asks: what constitutes knowing how to watch her {\i get}? The answer given is: {\i kol she\u8217'meshameres gita v\u8217'davar acher} \u8212- whoever watches her {\i get} and something else.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yochanan clarifies this cryptic phrase: {\i kol she\u8217'meshameres davar acher machmat gita} \u8212- she watches something else on account of her {\i get}. Rashi explains that even if she loses her {\i get}, she has held onto another object in its place \u8212- a book, for instance \u8212- because she understood the {\i get} was something important. She grasps that it is a significant item even if she substitutes another object for it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Distinguishing Objects\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Huna bar Manoach rejects Rashi\u8217's reading forcefully: {\i ha shotah b\u8217'alma hi} \u8212- such a girl is essentially an imbecile! If she confuses a book with her {\i get} merely because she knows the {\i get} is important, that is hardly a relevant standard of knowledge. Accordingly, Rav Huna bar Manoach, citing Rav Acha the son of Rav Ika, reverses the reading: {\i kol she\u8217'mavchenes bein gita v\u8217'davar acher} \u8212- the girl who understands the {\i difference} between her {\i get} and something else. This means that if someone says to her, \u8220"Give me the {\i get}, I\u8217'll give you a different piece of paper in exchange,\u8221" she refuses, insisting that her {\i get} is important and she must hold onto it specifically. It is not merely that she can watch the {\i get} as a physical object; she understands its unique importance and will not accept a substitute. This is the level of knowledge required for a valid delivery of the {\i get} to her directly.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Minor Development\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tzror & Egoz\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having established that intellectual development is a determining factor in whether a {\i get} given to a minor constitutes a valid act, the Gemara transitions to a broader discussion of levels of intellectual development in minors as they pertain to transactions and acquisitions generally. The first stage is described by the phrase {\i tzror v\u8217'zorko egoz v\u8217'notlo}: if a child finds a pebble and throws it away, but finds a nut and keeps it, the child has demonstrated an understanding of differential value. The pebble is worthless to him; the nut is something he wants to hold. He recognizes that not all objects are equivalent.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Self vs.\u160 ?Others\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 At this first stage, the Gemara rules: {\i zocheh l\u8217'atzmo v\u8217'ein zocheh l\u8217'acherim} \u8212- the minor can acquire for himself but not on behalf of others. Rabbinically, once a child understands the difference between a pebble and a nut \u8212- a fairly young age \u8212- he can take possession of an object given to him. This is a rabbinic institution; a minor cannot take {\i de\u8217'oraita} possession until adulthood. Nevertheless, the Rabbis recognized this capacity and validated it. The rationale is that when someone gives the child an object, the adult is supplying the necessary {\i da\u8217'as} \u8212- the requisite intent to transfer ownership. The child need not himself be the fully competent party who initiates the transaction. He merely needs to do something that physically constitutes a meaningful act of taking \u8212- holding the object with some sense of what is occurring. The giver is the one effectuating the transfer. By analogy to {\i get}: the husband is the one effecting the divorce, but the recipient still needs to perform something that constitutes a meaningful reception.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 At this stage, however, the minor cannot take possession on behalf of someone else. That requires a substantially higher level of {\i da\u8217'as}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Da\u8217'as Acheret\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The case of {\i da\u8217'as acheret makneh oso} \u8212- another party providing the requisite intent \u8212- is relevant here. When you give the minor an object, you are the source of {\i da\u8217'as}. But for the minor to acquire on behalf of a third party, the minor would need to function as a conduit through which your intent reaches that third party. The minor would need to hold not only his own understanding but to represent another\u8217's interests \u8212- something beyond his current capacity.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Minor Development (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Object Permanence\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara introduces a second and more advanced stage: {\i chefetz u\u8217'machziro l\u8217'achar sha\u8217'ah} \u8212- if you give the minor an object and an hour later ask him to return it, and he can retrieve it for you. This reflects something akin to object permanence: the child retains a mental representation of the object beyond the immediate present moment. He does not forget about it; he can locate it after an interval of time. This suggests a more developed cognitive capacity than the simple immediate recognition of the first stage.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 At this stage, the Gemara rules: {\i zocheh bein l\u8217'atzmo bein l\u8217'acherim} \u8212- the minor can acquire both for himself and on behalf of others. Because the child has demonstrated an understanding that extends beyond the here-and-now, there is enough comprehension to serve as a vehicle through which another person takes possession when that other person is supplying the {\i da\u8217'as}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 De\u8217'oraita Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The question arises whether this capacity operates on a {\i de\u8217'oraita} level. Tosafot raises the issue: since it is the adult providing the {\i da\u8217'as} \u8212- similar to the {\i get} scenario, where the husband is the one effecting the divorce \u8212- perhaps even these rabbinic capacities of the minor could function on a {\i de\u8217'oraita} level. In general, however, the assumption is that all of this operates rabbinically. This is debated among the Rishonim. As will be seen, the Gemara\u8217's subsequent discussion will illuminate what the default assumption is and why.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rava\u8217's Gimmel Midot\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Stage Two\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara records that when this analysis was presented before Shmuel, he responded: {\i da veda achat hi} \u8212- this and that are the same {\i halacha}. Rav Chisda explains: {\i echad zeh ve\u8217'echad zeh zocheh l\u8217'atzmo ve\u8217'ein zocheh l\u8217'acherim} \u8212- in both the first stage and the second stage, the minor can only acquire for himself; neither stage permits acquiring on behalf of others. Even the capacity to return an object after an interval does not elevate the minor to the status where his taking possession on behalf of a third party is meaningful, according to Shmuel as understood by Rav Chisda.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Possession & Shituf\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Chanina Challenge\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Chanina of Vardan challenges this ruling from a {\i baraita} concerning {\i shituf mavoi} \u8212- the joint acquisition used to create an {\i eiruv} for a shared alleyway.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shituf Mavo\u8217'ot\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i baraita} asks: {\i keitzad mishtatfin bemavoi} \u8212- how does one perform {\i shituf} of a {\i mavoi}? The answer is that one takes a barrel of wine (or another food), declares it to be for all the residents of the {\i mavoi}, and transfers possession of it to everyone through an intermediary. Specifically, the {\i baraita} lists the valid intermediaries: through one\u8217's adult son or daughter ({\i bno u\u8217'vito hagedolim}), or through one\u8217's Hebrew male or female slave ({\i avdo v\u8217'shifchato ha\u8217'ivrim}).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara immediately questions the case of the Hebrew female servant: {\i hai shifcha heichi dami} \u8212- what is the case of this maidservant? If she has already developed {\i shtei se\u8217'arot} \u8212- the two pubic hairs that mark puberty, typically around age twelve \u8212- she should already have been freed, since that is when a {\i shifchah ivriyah} goes free. It must therefore be that she does not yet have those signs of maturity \u8212- meaning she is a minor. Yet the {\i baraita} states that she can take possession on behalf of all the residents of the {\i mavoi}. This directly contradicts the position that a minor cannot acquire on behalf of others, even at the more advanced stage of {\i chefetz u\u8217'machziro}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This challenge applies against Shmuel\u8217's understanding that {\i da veda achat hi} \u8212- that both stages only permit acquisition for oneself. Here is a minor girl taking possession for others.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Derabbanan Pattern\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara answers on behalf of Rav Chisda: {\i shani shitufei mavo\u8217'os derabbanan} \u8212- {\i shituf mavoi} is a rabbinic institution, and therefore the rules that apply to it need not mirror the {\i de\u8217'oraita} rules for acquisition. Because it is entirely a rabbinic creation, the Rabbis may permit a minor to serve as the acquiring agent even for others, in a context where the underlying institution itself has no {\i de\u8217'oraita} analog.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Chisda reports that Rav Chanina of Vardan was silenced \u8212- {\i ishtik Vardan} \u8212- by this response. Yet the Gemara wonders what he should have said, implying his original question had force. His response could have been: {\i kol detikkun rabbanan ke\u8217'ein d\u8217'oraisa tikkun} \u8212- everything the Rabbis enacted is patterned after {\i de\u8217'oraita} law. If the Rabbis permitted it in {\i shituf mavoi}, it is because in general minors can acquire for others, and the Rabbis were following that principle.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then articulates the counter-response that would defeat this rejoinder: {\i ke\u8217'ein d\u8217'oraisa tikkun} applies only when the rabbinic institution has a {\i de\u8217'oraita} root \u8212- {\i bemilta de\u8217'is lah ikar min haTorah}. When a rabbinic institution is built from whole cloth \u8212- {\i milta deleis lah ikar min haTorah} \u8212- as {\i shituf mavo\u8217'os} is, we cannot infer from it that the Rabbis were tracking a {\i de\u8217'oraita} principle. The case of, say, chicken and milk being rabbinic is modeled after the {\i de\u8217'oraita} prohibition of meat and milk; one can infer from the rabbinic extension that it tracks the original. But {\i shituf mavo\u8217'os} has no {\i de\u8217'oraita} analog at all, so we cannot use it as proof for {\i de\u8217'oraita} capacity.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Crucially, this entire exchange implies something significant: the premise throughout the Gemara seems to be that when an adult provides the {\i da\u8217'as} and gives an object to a minor, the minor can take possession even on a {\i de\u8217'oraita} level. The dispute is specifically about whether the minor can then serve as an agent for a third party. This point is not as obvious as it might appear, and it is noted as being debated among the Rishonim.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ma\u8217'aser Pidyon\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara introduces a second challenge, this time concerning {\i ma\u8217'aser sheni}. The context involves a practice the Gemara terms {\i ma\u8217'arimin al ma\u8217'aser sheni} \u8212- \u8220"scheming\u8221" with respect to {\i ma\u8217'aser sheni}. When a person redeems his own {\i ma\u8217'aser sheni} onto coins, he is required to add a {\i chomesh} \u8212- the additional fifth (which in practice equals 25%, since the additional quarter becomes a fifth of the total). This obligation to add a {\i chomesh} applies only when the owner himself redeems his own {\i ma\u8217'aser sheni}. If, however, a different person redeems it \u8212- using his own money \u8212- no {\i chomesh} is required.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i baraita} describes the permitted maneuver: one says to his adult son or daughter, or to his Hebrew male or female slave, {\i hei lachem ma\u8217'ot halalu} \u8212- here, I am giving you this money as a gift. It is now yours. You then use it to redeem my {\i ma\u8217'aser sheni}. Since they are now the owners of the money, and they are the ones performing the redemption, no {\i chomesh} is required.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara again asks: {\i hai shifcha heichi dami} \u8212- what is the case of this Hebrew maidservant? If she has already reached adulthood, she is no longer a {\i shifchah}. She must therefore be a minor. And yet here she is performing an act of {\i pidyon ma\u8217'aser sheni} \u8212- redeeming {\i ma\u8217'aser sheni} \u8212- which is not merely an act of taking possession on behalf of others; it is an independent legal act. This is an even stronger challenge.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafot Problem\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot raises a pointed difficulty: in what sense does any analogy to acquisition on behalf of others \u8212- {\i zocheh l\u8217'acherim} \u8212- apply here? When we discussed acquiring for others, the model was that an adult provides the {\i da\u8217'as} and the minor serves as a conduit. But here, the minor herself is the active party. She owns the money (which was given to her) and she herself is performing the {\i pidyon}. There is no {\i da\u8217'as acheret makneh} at work \u8212- no external adult is supplying the intent. She is fully the agent of the transaction. Moreover, Tosafot notes, in general one requires adult status to perform {\i pidyon ma\u8217'aser sheni}. How, then, can a minor do it at all?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hafla\u8217'ah Theory\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot resolves this through the principle of {\i mufla samuch l\u8217'ish} \u8212- the halacha that in the year before a minor reaches {\i bar mitzvah} or {\i bat mitzvah} (a twelve-year-old girl or a thirteen-year-old boy), his or her verbal declarations that change status \u8212- {\i nedarim} and {\i hekdesh} \u8212- take effect. This is a {\i de\u8217'oraita} principle. Now, how do we characterize {\i pidyon ma\u8217'aser sheni}? Is it a transaction \u8212- a {\i kinyan} \u8212- in which the owner of the {\i ma\u8217'aser sheni} acquires the coins in exchange for the food? Or is it more like {\i hafla\u8217'ah} \u8212- a verbal declaration that transfers a status? One might argue that the minor is not \u8220"buying\u8221" anything; she is merely transferring the {\i kedusha} from the fruit to the coin. It is not a {\i kinyan} of an object she did not previously own; it is a transfer of a sanctified status from one substrate to another. If we view {\i pidyon} as analogous to {\i hekdesh}, then just as a minor in the year before {\i bar/bat mitzvah} can consecrate objects through verbal declaration, she might also be able to transfer the sanctity of {\i ma\u8217'aser sheni} from fruit to coin.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The connection to the broader discussion of minors acquiring on behalf of others remains somewhat unclear even according to Tosafot \u8212- but the Gemara plainly treats the case as relevant.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara\u8217's response to the challenge is, once again: {\i hacha bemai askinan b\u8217'ma\u8217'aser b\u8217'zman hazeh d\u8217'rabbanan} \u8212- this is discussing {\i ma\u8217'aser sheni} in our era, which is only rabbinically obligatory. And the institution of the Hebrew maidservant ({\i amah ivriyah}) no longer applies at all in the present day, since {\i ein eved ivri noheg ella b\u8217'zman she\u8217'hayovel noheg} \u8212- the entire institution of Hebrew slaves operates only when {\i Yovel} is observed, and {\i Yovel} requires the Jewish people to be settled in the Land. So neither of these cases establishes that a minor can perform such acts when {\i de\u8217'oraita} stakes are involved. The Gemara thus successfully brackets each challenge as involving only rabbinic law, leaving open the question of the {\i de\u8217'oraita} position.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rava\u8217's Gimmel Midot (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Stage One\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 With the preceding analyses as background, the Gemara now presents Rava\u8217's famous tripartite summary: {\i amar Rava gimmel middos b\u8217'katan} \u8212- Rava said there are three stages in the development of a minor\u8217's legal capacities. The first corresponds to {\i tzror v\u8217'zorko egoz v\u8217'notlo}: once a child recognizes that a pebble is worthless but a nut has value, he can take possession for himself \u8212- {\i zocheh l\u8217'atzmo v\u8217'ein zocheh l\u8217'acherim}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Girl\u8217's Stage One\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava\u8217's formulation then turns to the female parallel: {\i v\u8217'knegedan b\u8217'ktana} \u8212- the corresponding stage for a girl. The male stage involves a child\u8217's recognition of differential value among objects. For a girl, the corresponding legal institution is {\i miskadeshes l\u8217'miyun} \u8212- she can be betrothed for the purpose of {\i miyun}. This requires explanation. By Torah law, only a father may betroth his minor daughter; if the father is deceased, the Torah provides no mechanism. The Rabbis enacted that when there is no father, the mother and brothers may arrange the girl\u8217's betrothal. Because this is a rabbinic institution, the girl may subsequently annul it through {\i miyun} \u8212- an informal verbal rejection, without the need for a {\i get}. At what age can this take effect? The girl must be old enough that if she were given the {\i kesef kiddushin}, she would understand that she received something of value. Rashi notes this is the capacity to preserve {\i kiddushin} the same way she preserves any other object of value \u8212- {\i kol tinokes she\u8217'eina yechola lishmor kiddusheha} \u8212- if she cannot protect the money of her betrothal, the betrothal enacted by the mother and brothers is not operative even rabbinically. Once she can, it is effective, and since it is only rabbinic, she retains the right of {\i miyun} to walk away.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rashi goes further and says that at this stage, not only can the mother and brothers betroth her \u8212- if she is a complete orphan, she herself can accept the {\i kiddushin}. This is a remarkable position, though understandable in light of the desperate social realities that Tosafot elsewhere describes: the frequency of death and tragedy in medieval life was such that children required protection through betrothal arrangements as early as possible.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Pautot Age\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The second stage in Rava\u8217's scheme corresponds to {\i hapautot} \u8212- the age range of roughly six to nine years old \u8212- at which Rabbinic law permits minors to engage in buying and selling of movable goods ({\i mikach u\u8217'mimkar b\u8217'mitaltelin}, not land). The Gemara says this is permitted {\i kedei chayeihem} \u8212- to enable them to manage basic daily life. For a girl, the corresponding legal capacity is {\i miskadeshes b\u8217'kiddushei aviha} \u8212- she is old enough that if her father betroth her, it is a fully valid {\i de\u8217'oraita} betrothal. And crucially, this is the age that is the threshold for {\i yoda\u8217'at lishmor et gita} \u8212- knowing how to watch her {\i get}. The distinction here is illuminating: {\i yoda\u8217'at lishmor et kiddusheha} \u8212- knowing how to watch money of {\i kiddushin} \u8212- requires only that she recognizes physical value. But {\i yoda\u8217'at lishmor et gita} requires that she understand the {\i get} as an abstract symbol of a changed legal status. The {\i get} does not have intrinsic monetary value; its significance is conceptual. Therefore, understanding a {\i get} requires a more developed level of cognition than understanding a coin. The {\i pautot} age is when this higher threshold is reached.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Stage Three\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The third stage is {\i higi\u8217'u l\u8217'onas nedarim} \u8212- when minors reach the year before {\i bar} or {\i bat mitzvah} (a twelve-year-old girl, a thirteen-year-old boy). At this point, {\i nidra neder hekdesha hekdesh} \u8212- their vows take effect and their acts of {\i hekdesh} are binding. For a girl, the corresponding capacity is {\i choletzes} \u8212- she may perform {\i chalitza}. Tosafot notes that there is a Talmudic opinion that a woman performing {\i chalitza} need not be an adult, even if the man must be; this stage follows that view.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Land Sale Limit\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava adds a special rule regarding the sale of land inherited from one\u8217's father: {\i ul\u8217'makor b\u8217'nichsei aviv ad sheyehei ben esrim} \u8212- even after a boy reaches {\i bar mitzvah} at thirteen and is fully an adult, the Rabbis did not recognize the sale of inherited land as valid until he reached twenty. The concern was that a young man of thirteen, even legally an adult, lacked the life experience and judgment necessary to appreciate the full consequences of selling real property. Given that selling land was among the most consequential financial transactions possible, the Rabbis delayed full competence for this specific act until age twenty, regardless of {\i bar mitzvah} status.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Minor Shaliach\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Location Conditions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Mishnah presents the case of someone who says to his {\i shaliach}: {\i ten get l\u8217'ishti b\u8217'makom ploni} \u8212- give this {\i get} to my wife in a specific location \u8212- and the {\i shaliach} delivers it to her in a different location. The {\i get} is invalid, because the husband appointed him a {\i shaliach} specifically for that place, and outside that place the appointment does not hold.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 No Shaliach Power\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Mishnah then states: {\i ketanah she\u8217'amra hitkabel li giti eino get ad sheyagiya l\u8217'yadah} \u8212- a {\i ketanah} who said \u8220"accept my {\i get} for me\u8221" \u8212- this is not a valid {\i get} until it reaches her hand directly. The reason is that a minor cannot appoint a {\i shaliach}. If she attempts to appoint someone to receive her {\i get}, her attempt is legally ineffective. What happens instead is that when the husband gives the {\i get} to the agent, the husband has implicitly made that agent a {\i shaliach} for delivery ({\i shaliach holachah}). The {\i get} will only take effect when the agent delivers it to her hand. Since she cannot appoint a {\i shaliach}, the agent was never her representative \u8212- and the husband can retract his delivery {\i shaliach} at any point before the {\i get} reaches the wife. As the Mishnah states: {\i she\u8217'ein katan oseh shaliach} \u8212- a minor cannot create a {\i shaliach}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Father Appoints\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If, however, the father says to an agent, {\i tzei v\u8217'hitkabel l\u8217'biti gita} \u8212- go and accept the {\i get} for my daughter \u8212- the father\u8217's appointment is valid. The father, who does legally represent his minor daughter, can appoint a {\i shaliach} to receive the {\i get} on her behalf. Once the husband gives the {\i get} to this agent, {\i im ratza lachazor lo yachazor} \u8212- the husband may not retract, because the father has validly appointed the agent as the daughter\u8217's representative for {\i kabbalah} (reception), and the {\i get} takes effect upon delivery to the agent.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Place Conditions (III)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mar\u8217'eh vs.\u160 ?Hakpid\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Mishnah distinguishes between two types of location specifications in the context of {\i shlichus} for a {\i get}. When the husband says to his {\i shaliach}, {\i ten get l\u8217'ishti b\u8217'makom ploni} \u8212- give the {\i get} to my wife in a specific place \u8212- and the agent delivers it in a different place, the {\i get} is invalid. The husband specified the place, and we read that specification as a {\i hakpadah} \u8212- a genuine restriction on the terms of the {\i shlichus}. By contrast, if the husband says {\i harei hi b\u8217'makom ploni} \u8212- \u8220"she can be found at such-and-such a place\u8221" \u8212- and the agent delivers it elsewhere, the {\i get} is valid. Here the husband was merely offering logistical guidance \u8212- pointing the {\i shaliach} toward where to find his wife \u8212- not conditioning the {\i get} on a specific location.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Mishnah then addresses the parallel case where the wife specifies a location: {\i ha\u8217'isha she\u8217'amra hitkabel li giti b\u8217'makom ploni} \u8212- a woman who said \u8220"accept my {\i get} for me in a specific place.\u8221" If the agent receives it elsewhere, according to the {\i Rabbanam} the {\i get} is invalid. Rabbi Eliezer, however, validates the {\i get}. The disagreement concerns how to read the woman\u8217's specification of a place.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rashi\u8217's Logic\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara asks: why does Rabbi Eliezer rule differently when it is the wife who specifies a location, compared to when it is the husband? The answer articulates the fundamental asymmetry: {\i ihu d\u8217'midaiteih megareish, kapid} \u8212- the husband, who is initiating the divorce entirely of his own will, can genuinely restrict the terms under which his {\i shaliach} operates. He can say: I only want to divorce her here. {\i Ihee d\u8217'al karchah migareshet} \u8212- she is being divorced against her will and cannot control the circumstances. Therefore, when she names a location, she is merely being {\i mareh makom} \u8212- pointing the {\i shaliach} toward where he can find her husband \u8212- not imposing a genuine condition.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rashi elaborates the precise logic. Since the wife is appointing a {\i shaliach} and must presumably want the {\i get} to happen (otherwise, why appoint anyone?), she cannot realistically condition her appointment on a specific location, because the husband may simply refuse to cooperate there. The husband is the one with ultimate control over whether and where the {\i get} is given. Once we presume that the wife wants to be divorced \u8212- which her appointment of a {\i shaliach} demonstrates \u8212- we conclude that any location she names is guidance, not restriction. She cannot genuinely restrict it to one location because she cannot compel the husband to comply. Therefore, the {\i Rabbanam} read her specification as {\i hakpadah} while Rabbi Eliezer reads it as {\i mareh makom}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This analysis is worth pausing over. One might argue that this is precisely the moment when a woman has actual agency: she is the one appointing the {\i shaliach}, and she can define the terms of her appointment. If she says \u8220"only accept it here,\u8221" why should we ignore that because she is generally disempowered in {\i gittin}? Rashi\u8217's answer is practical: given that the husband controls whether the {\i get} happens at all, any condition she imposes is hollow \u8212- he can always simply decline to give the {\i get} in that location. Once she has made a {\i shaliach} and shown she wants the divorce, we presume she will accept it wherever it is offered.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might add that in a post-{\i Cherem d\u8217'Rabbeinu Gershom} world, where a wife cannot be divorced against her will, these assumptions might shift \u8212- the fundamental asymmetry that drives Rashi\u8217's logic is no longer as stark.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Hulkach vs.\u160 ?Kabbalah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Husband\u8217's Hulkach\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The distinction between {\i shaliach l\u8217'holachah} and {\i shaliach l\u8217'kabbalah} is clarified through the case introduced in the Mishnah. When the husband says to an agent {\i holech} \u8212- convey this {\i get} to my wife \u8212- he has made the agent a {\i shaliach} for delivery ({\i shaliach holachah}). The {\i get} does not take effect until it reaches the wife\u8217's hand.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Wife\u8217's Hulkach\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 When the wife says to an agent {\i havei li giti} \u8212- bring me my {\i get} \u8212- she is not appointing him as a {\i shaliach} for {\i kabbalah} (reception) on her behalf. Rather, as the Gemara understands it through the principle of {\i holech k\u8217'zachi}, the wife is effectively making the agent a {\i shaliach holachah} for the husband \u8212- asking him to go and serve as the husband\u8217's delivery agent. The {\i get} does not take effect until it reaches her hands.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Terumah Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This distinction has an immediate practical consequence: if the wife is married to a {\i kohen}, she eats {\i terumah} while she is married and must stop once she is divorced. When she says {\i havei li giti}, since the agent is a {\i shaliach holachah} and she is not divorced until the {\i get} reaches her, she continues eating {\i terumah} until the {\i get} arrives \u8212- {\i ochelet b\u8217'terumah ad sheyagiya get l\u8217'yadah}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Place Conditions (III)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Woman\u8217's Place\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 However, when she says {\i hitkabel li giti}, appointing the agent as a {\i shaliach l\u8217'kabbalah}, the moment the husband gives the {\i get} to the agent she is effectively divorced (since reception by her designated agent equals reception by her). Since she does not know when that moment will arrive, she must immediately stop eating {\i terumah} \u8212- {\i assur l\u8217'echol b\u8217'terumah miyad}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Place Conditions (III)\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mixed Places\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Mishnah states that if the wife says {\i hitkabel li giti b\u8217'makom ploni} \u8212- accept my {\i get} for me in a specific place \u8212- she may eat {\i terumah} until the {\i get} arrives at that place ({\i ochelet b\u8217'terumah ad sheyagiya get l\u8217'oto makom}). This phrasing is striking: it says until the {\i get} arrives at that place, not until the {\i shaliach} arrives at that place. The Gemara works to clarify this.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara explains that the Mishnah is describing a compound case: she says to the {\i shaliach}, \u8220"Accept my {\i get} for me in {\i Mata Machasia}, but sometimes you will find my husband in {\i Bavel}.\u8221" In other words, she named a primary location but acknowledged that the husband could sometimes be found elsewhere. In such a case, the Gemara understands her to be saying: wherever you find my husband, take the {\i get} from him \u8212- but I do not want it to take effect until you arrive at the named location. The husband may give the {\i get} to the {\i shaliach} anywhere, but the {\i get}\u8217's effect is suspended until the {\i shaliach} reaches {\i Mata Machasia}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafot Problem\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is a genuinely strange reading, as Tosafot immediately observes. The mechanics are puzzling: the husband hands the {\i get} to the {\i shaliach} in {\i Bavel}, but the {\i get} does not yet take effect. So in what capacity does the {\i shaliach} hold the {\i get} while traveling to {\i Mata Machasia}? He cannot be the wife\u8217's {\i shaliach l\u8217'kabbalah}, since the {\i get} has already been received but not yet taken effect. Is he then the husband\u8217's {\i shaliach l\u8217'holachah}? But the husband gave it to him without making him a {\i shaliach} for delivery. Tosafot tries to resolve this by suggesting that since the wife stipulated this condition, and the husband plays into it, there is some sense in which the husband effectively delays the operation of the {\i get}. But how the wife can impose this delay \u8212- given that the {\i da\u8217'as} for the {\i get}\u8217's effect belongs to the husband \u8212- remains mechanically unclear.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Eliezer\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Eliezer rules {\i oser miyad} \u8212- she must stop eating {\i terumah} immediately \u8212- because in his view, when a woman names a location it is merely {\i mareh makom}: guidance for finding the husband, not a genuine restriction. Since the {\i get} could be given anywhere at any time, she must stop eating {\i terumah} from the moment she appoints the {\i shaliach}. The Gemara adds a refinement: even if the wife told the {\i shaliach} \u8220"go east, you\u8217'll find him in the east,\u8221" and he went west instead, Rabbi Eliezer would still require her to stop eating immediately \u8212- because {\i dilma bahadi deka\u8217'azal u\u8217'mati gabei}, perhaps as he walked out the door he happened to run into the husband and the {\i get} was given. Since there is no {\i halachically} enforceable restriction on where it takes place, there is always a theoretical possibility that it happened immediately.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Eiruv Analogy\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Eiruv Dispute\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now applies the debate between the {\i Rabbanam} and Rabbi Eliezer beyond {\i gittin}, to the domain of {\i eiruv}. The Mishnah-level rule for {\i eiruv} involves designating specific food for the purpose. If a person instructs his {\i shaliach}, \u8220"Make my {\i eiruv} with dates,\u8221" and the {\i shaliach} makes it with figs instead, or vice versa, two {\i beraitot} conflict: one says the {\i eiruv} is valid, the other says it is not. Rabba resolves this: {\i ha rabbanan ha Rabbi Elazar} \u8212- one {\i baraita} follows the {\i Rabbanam}, who read instructions as a {\i hakpadah} (and thus the {\i eiruv} with the wrong food is invalid), and the other follows Rabbi Eliezer, who reads instructions as {\i mareh makom} (and thus the {\i eiruv} is valid regardless).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava\u8217's Synthesis\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This suggests that the dispute between the {\i Rabbanam} and Rabbi Eliezer is not limited to the specific case of a woman appointing a {\i shaliach} for her {\i get}, but reflects a general jurisprudential disagreement: when a person specifies a particular detail in giving instructions to a {\i shaliach}, do we presume that specification is a genuine restriction ({\i hakpadah}) or merely helpful guidance ({\i mareh makom})? The {\i Rabbanam} default to {\i hakpadah}; Rabbi Eliezer defaults to {\i mareh makom} based on contextual reasonableness.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Yosef\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Yosef offers an alternative resolution that avoids invoking the Rabbi Eliezer/{\i Rabbanam} dispute. He says both conflicting {\i beraitot} could represent the {\i Rabbanam}: {\i kan b\u8217'shelo kan b\u8217'shel chaveiro} \u8212- here we are dealing with the {\i shaliach}\u8217's own food, and there we are dealing with the sender\u8217's food. If I tell a {\i shaliach} \u8220"make an {\i eiruv} with dates\u8221" and I am providing the dates, I am {\i makpid} about what is used \u8212- it is my property and I can restrict it. If, however, the {\i shaliach} is providing the food from his own stores, then even the {\i Rabbanam} would say I cannot genuinely be {\i makpid} about what he uses \u8212- I have no right to dictate which of his foods he employs.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Abaye\u8217's Challenge\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye challenges Rav Yosef\u8217's framework from another {\i baraita}: the case where someone instructs his {\i shaliach} to make an {\i eiruv} in a tower ({\i migdal}) and the {\i shaliach} makes it in a pigeon coop ({\i shovach}), or the reverse. Again, one {\i baraita} validates the {\i eiruv} and one does not. If Rav Yosef\u8217's resolution is based on whose food it is, what does that have to do with whether the {\i eiruv} was made in the tower or the coop? The location of the deposit is not about ownership of food.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Food Sources\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Yosef responds: in this case too, the reference to the {\i migdal} or {\i shovach} is not about the physical location of the {\i eiruv} deposit \u8212- it is about the {\i source} of the food. The {\i migdal} and the {\i shovach} are storage locations, and specifying one or the other indicates which stores of food the instruction refers to. \u8220"Make the {\i eiruv} from the tower\u8221" means \u8220"use the food stored in the tower,\u8221" and \u8220"make it from the pigeon coop\u8221" means \u8220"use the food stored there.\u8221" Thus the case is really still about whose food and which specific stores are being designated. If the {\i shaliach} uses the food from the wrong location, the question is whether the {\i ba\u8217'al} was genuinely restricting which of his stores should be used. Once understood this way, Rav Yosef\u8217's framework holds: when it is the sender\u8217's food, specification indicates {\i hakpadah}; when it is the {\i shaliach}\u8217's food, the {\i Rabbanam} themselves concede that no {\i hakpadah} is imposed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye\u8217's own preferred resolution, by contrast, is simpler: the two {\i beraitot} reflect the straightforward disagreement between the {\i Rabbanam} and Rabbi Eliezer as general disputants over the presumption of {\i hakpadah} versus {\i mareh makom}, without any need to distinguish between whose food is involved.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Gittin 66\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Two/Three Person Agency\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Language Assumptions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Today\u8217's study begins at the final line of Gittin 66a, where the {\i mishna} turns to a question that will occupy the bulk of the {\i daf}: when a husband appoints others to write and deliver a {\i get}, does his appointment bind those individuals personally, or may they in turn delegate that responsibility to someone else? The {\i mishna} explores this question through a series of cases distinguished by the number of people addressed and the precise language used.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two People Direct Action\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} opens: {\i amar lishnayim tnu get l\u8217'ishti} \u8212- if a husband says to two people, \u8220"give a {\i get} to my wife,\u8221" and since one cannot give a {\i get} without first writing it, the instruction to give necessarily implies writing as well. Similarly, if he says to three people {\i kitvu get utnu l\u8217'ishti} \u8212- \u8220"write a {\i get} and give it to my wife\u8221" \u8212- then {\i harei elu yichtevu veyitnu}: those people themselves must both write and deliver it. They may not hand that responsibility to others.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Three People/Beit Din\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Here, however, the {\i mishna} introduces an important distinction, and it is precisely this distinction that explains why the {\i mishna} treats the two-person {\i tnu} case separately from the three-person {\i tnu} case. If a husband says to three people {\i tnu get l\u8217'ishti} \u8212- \u8220"give a {\i get} to my wife\u8221" \u8212- without specifying {\i kitvu}, the word {\i tnu} alone can plausibly be understood as \u8220"take responsibility to see that it is given,\u8221" rather than as a directive to perform every step personally. When said to only two people, we would not interpret the instruction that way, because two people do not constitute a body that normally supervises and delegates; they would be expected to act themselves. Three people, however, constitute a {\i beis din}, and a {\i beis din} can be understood as a supervisory body that need not execute matters directly. Therefore, when a husband tells three people {\i tnu} without the concrete specification of {\i kitvu}, the {\i mishna} rules: {\i harei elu yomru l\u8217'acherim veyichtevu} \u8212- they may instruct others to write the {\i get}. This is because {\i she\u8217'asan beis din} \u8212- he has cast them in the role of a {\i beis din} \u8212- and that is the ruling of Rabbi Meir.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} adds that this is also the {\i halacha} brought down by Rabbi Chanina Ish Ono from prison \u8212- a transmission from Rabbi Akiva, who was then incarcerated under the Roman decrees: {\i mekubal ani b\u8217'omer lishlosha tnu get l\u8217'ishti, yomru l\u8217'acherim veyichtevu, mipnei she\u8217'asan beis din} \u8212- I have received the tradition that when one says to three people \u8220"give a {\i get} to my wife,\u8221" they may delegate to others, because he has made them a {\i beis din}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Yose\u8217's View\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yose, however, disputes this emphatically. {\i Nominu l\u8217'shaliach} \u8212- using an archaic term for \u8220"we said to the agent\u8221" \u8212- he declares: I too have a tradition. And his tradition is that {\i afillu amar l\u8217'beis din hagadol shebirushalayim tnu get l\u8217'ishti} \u8212- even if one says to the Great Court in Jerusalem, \u8220"give a {\i get} to my wife,\u8221" since he used the word {\i tnu} and not {\i kitvu}, and those justices are obviously a {\i beis din} and one would never expect them to take out parchment and begin scribing themselves \u8212- nevertheless, if this {\i get} is to be given through this appointment, {\i she\u8217'yilmedu veyichtevu veyitnu}: they themselves must learn how to write a {\i get} and do it. They may always decline the appointment, but they cannot simply supervise and pass the task to others. {\i Harei elu yichtevu veyitnu} \u8212- it is they who must act.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is a striking position. Rabbi Yose seems unconcerned with the husband\u8217's apparent intent, or at least he holds that whatever one\u8217's intent, the words of appointment bind only those who directly received them.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ten People Distribution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Shifting to a different scenario, the {\i mishna} takes up the case where the husband says to ten people {\i kitvu utnu get l\u8217'ishti} \u8212- \u8220"write a {\i get} and give it to my wife.\u8221" Since he specifies {\i kitvu}, it is clear he does not mean mere supervision; he means they should execute it. How does this work in practice when addressing a group? {\i Echad kotev ushnayim chotmin} \u8212- one person writes, two people sign. The phrase \u8220"write and give,\u8221" addressed to many, means simply: from among you, some combination should accomplish this. One person writes, two sign as witnesses, one delivers.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kulhon Requirement\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A further case: {\i kulhon kitvu} \u8212- if he explicitly says \u8220"all of you write a {\i get}.\u8221" Here, {\i echad kotev v\u8217'chulam chotmin} \u8212- one person writes and all of them sign. This prompts a question that will recur in the {\i Gemara}: if all of them must write, how is it that only one person does the actual scribing? The answer the {\i Gemara} will give is that when the husband says {\i kulhon}, the requirement is not that each person physically holds the pen, but that all are present while one writes \u8212- a kind of representative action, analogous to a {\i shaliach tzibur} who prays on behalf of the entire congregation. Everyone is present and constitutes the body; one person is the instrument through which the group acts.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Invalidation by Death\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} closes this section with a consequential ruling: {\i im meit echad meihen harei zeh get batel} \u8212- if one of the ten dies before the {\i get} is written and delivered, the {\i get} is invalid. This flows directly from the husband\u8217's demand that all of them be part of the process. What is notable here \u8212- and worth pausing over \u8212- is how powerfully the husband\u8217's {\i da\u8217'at} and {\i ratzon} shape the very validity of the {\i get}. One might have assumed that a {\i get} has a fixed form: a specific text, signed by two witnesses, properly delivered. But in fact the husband controls far more than that. If he specified that ten people must all be involved and that requirement is not fulfilled, the {\i get} itself is {\i batel}. His will is not merely a preference; it is constitutive of the document\u8217's validity.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Milei/Shaliach Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Scribe vs.\u160 ?Witness\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} opens with a remarkable historical note: Rabbi Yirmiyah bar Abba reports that they sent a question {\i mi\u8217'bei Rav l\u8217'Shmuel} \u8212- from the academy of Rav to Shmuel. The very fact that the students of Rav would send a question to Shmuel is itself striking, given that the two were contemporaries generally regarded as peers.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The question: {\i amar l\u8217'shnayim kitvu utnu get l\u8217'ishti, v\u8217'amru l\u8217'sofer v\u8217'chatav v\u8217'chatmu hem mahu} \u8212- if a husband says to two people \u8220"write and give a {\i get} to my wife,\u8221" and they do not write it themselves but instead have a {\i sofer} write it while they sign it as witnesses \u8212- what is the {\i halacha}? The question, at its core, is: when the husband says {\i kitvu} to these two people, does he mean that they must do the actual scribing, or does {\i kitvu} mean that they must sign? If it means scribing, they may not have the {\i sofer} do it. If it means signing, then having the {\i sofer} write it is merely finding someone to perform a task the husband implicitly left open.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Shmuel\u8217's reply: {\i teitzei v\u8217'hadavar tzarich talmud} \u8212- if she remarried on the basis of this {\i get}, she cannot remain with her second husband because the {\i get} is of questionable validity, but the matter requires further study. It is a {\i safek get}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shmuel: No Delegation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now asks: what exactly requires further investigation? One possibility is that the doubt concerns whether they are permitted to pass on their verbal appointment to someone else \u8212- the principle of {\i mili lo mimsaran l\u8217'shaliach}, that words cannot be delegated to an agent. But this possibility is immediately challenged: {\i veha\u8217'amar Shmuel amar Rav halacha k\u8217'Rabbi Yose de\u8217'amar mili lo mimsaran l\u8217'shaliach} \u8212- Shmuel himself rules that we follow Rabbi Yose, who holds that a verbal appointment cannot be passed on. If that is already settled, why is there any doubt?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kitvu Meaning Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} therefore refocuses: Shmuel\u8217's real question is not about delegation but about the meaning of {\i kitvu} itself. When the husband says to the two people \u8220"write it,\u8221" does he mean {\i kesav yadan} \u8212- that they must sign it \u8212- or does he mean {\i kesav haget} \u8212- that they must actually scribe the document? This is the live question.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ten-Person Proof\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} attempts to resolve this from the {\i mishna} itself: {\i amar l\u8217'shnayim tnu get l\u8217'ishti, o lishlosha kitvu utnu get l\u8217'ishti, harei elu yichtevu veyitnu} \u8212- when the {\i mishna} says they must write it, doesn\u8217't that clearly mean they must scribe it? The {\i Gemara} rejects this, noting that the question about the meaning of {\i kitvu} is precisely what is at issue; the {\i mishna} cannot serve as its own resolution. More useful is the end of the {\i mishna}, where in the ten-person case the language {\i echad kotev ushnayim chotmin} suggests that someone in the group must be the actual scribe \u8212- which would imply that {\i kitvu} does include scribing.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Signing-Only Rejected\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} pushes toward clarification: {\i pishita dekesav haget hu} \u8212- it is obvious that {\i kitvu} means scribe the document. The proof: the {\i mishna} cites Rabbi Yose as saying that even the {\i beis din hagadol} in Jerusalem would have to {\i she\u8217'yilmedu veyichtevu} \u8212- learn how to write a {\i get} and do it themselves. If {\i kitvu} meant only signing, it would be absurd to say that the Supreme Court might not know how to sign their own names. That they would need to learn how to sign makes no sense; that they might need to learn the technical craft of scribing a {\i get} is at least intelligible.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} deflects this: {\i in, ika beis dina chadata} \u8212- perhaps it is a newly constituted court whose members have not yet signed anything. This is a forced answer, acknowledged as such, and the question remains live.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Finding a Scribe\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now restates the issue in its proper form. If {\i kitvu} means sign \u8212- not scribe \u8212- then having the {\i sofer} do the writing is not a case of passing on one\u8217's agency ({\i mili lo mimsaran l\u8217'shaliach}); rather, it is a case where the two people are implicitly authorized to find a {\i sofer}, and their communication to that {\i sofer} is simply informing him that the husband has appointed him. That is the concept of {\i omer imru} \u8212- the husband is telling the two people, in effect, \u8220"tell the {\i sofer} to write it,\u8221" meaning the husband is appointing the {\i sofer} through the intermediary of the two witnesses. The {\i sofer} has not received a sub-delegated appointment from the witnesses; he has received the husband\u8217's appointment, communicated through messengers. The question Shmuel is raising, then, is whether {\i omer imru} is permissible \u8212- and that depends on whether the {\i sofer} can be validly appointed through intermediaries rather than through direct communication from the husband himself.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Milei vs.\u160 ?Omer Imru\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Milei Principle\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The distinction being developed here is fundamental. {\i Mili lo mimsaran l\u8217'shaliach} \u8212- words cannot be delegated to an agent \u8212- addresses the following situation: I appoint Reuven, and Reuven, rather than performing the task himself, sub-delegates it to Shimon. Reuven has taken his own appointment, his {\i shlichus}, and attempted to pass it on. The question is whether he has the authority to do that. By a {\i get}, especially given the centrality of the husband\u8217's {\i da\u8217'at} and the verse {\i v\u8217'chasav lah sefer krisus} \u8212- \u8220"and he shall write for her a bill of divorce\u8221" \u8212- there are strong grounds to say that the appointment must remain with the original appointee. Reuven cannot make Shimon his substitute.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Omer Imru Concept\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Omer imru} is a structurally different situation. Here the husband says to Reuven and Shimon: \u8220"go tell the {\i sofer} to write, and go tell Ploni and Ploni to sign.\u8221" The husband himself is appointing the {\i sofer} and the witnesses; he is simply using Reuven and Shimon as messengers to communicate that appointment. They are not delegating their own agency; they are transmitting the husband\u8217's appointment to third parties. The question is whether this indirect form of appointment \u8212- {\i shlichus shelo befanav}, appointment not made in the presence of the appointee \u8212- is valid for a {\i get}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Chasam Sofer Issue\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sofer V\u8217'Ed Valid\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now introduces a {\i mishna} from elsewhere that will generate significant discussion. Rabbi Yirmiyah reports: {\i chasam sofer shaninu} \u8212- the {\i mishna} speaks of a case where the {\i sofer} himself signs as one of the witnesses, together with one other witness. That configuration \u8212- {\i chasam sofer v\u8217'ed} \u8212- is valid.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Validity Problem\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But this raises an immediate difficulty. Rav Chisda states: {\i matnisin mani Rabbi Yose hi} \u8212- that {\i mishna} follows the position of Rabbi Yose. How so? Because the validity of {\i chasam sofer v\u8217'ed} presupposes that everything was arranged in a particular way, and the question is how that arrangement can be assumed to be legitimate under Rabbi Yose\u8217's stringent requirements.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Embarrassment Scenario\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} works through a troubling scenario. Suppose the husband, through {\i omer imru}, tells two intermediaries: \u8220"tell the {\i sofer} to write it and tell Ploni and Ploni to sign it.\u8221" The intermediaries go to find the appointed witnesses, but the {\i sofer} objects \u8212- he is embarrassed that he is deemed fit to write the {\i get} but not fit to sign as a witness. Moved by concern for the {\i sofer\u8217's} dignity ({\i mishum kisufa d\u8217'sofer}), one of the intermediaries makes an adjustment: he has the {\i sofer} serve as one of the witnesses alongside only one of the originally designated witnesses. {\i U\u8217'va\u8217'al lo amar hachi} \u8212- but the husband said no such thing. The {\i get} is therefore {\i pasul}, yet those who rely on it may not know this and will assume it is {\i kasher}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shouldn\u8217't Do/Valid\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is precisely the concern: if {\i omer imru} is permissible, one can readily imagine this corruption arising. The {\i Gemara} responds: {\i kivan d\u8217'amar mar kasher v\u8217'lo ta\u8217'aseh chen b\u8217'Yisrael} \u8212- since we rule that {\i chasam sofer v\u8217'ed} is valid but one should never do this in Israel, meaning the {\i sofer} himself is required to refuse such an arrangement. Since this practice is prohibited and the {\i sofer} ought not comply, we are not afraid that this scenario will in fact unfold. The prohibition makes the bad outcome sufficiently uncommon that we need not treat it as a standing concern.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Witness Sign Alternative\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But the {\i Gemara} is not satisfied. A second scenario: suppose the husband directly appoints the witnesses themselves and tells them to sign \u8212- he does not use {\i omer imru} for the witnesses at all \u8212- but he simply asks them to find someone to be the {\i sofer}. They are themselves committed to signing; only the scribing is left open. Now one might think this is clearly acceptable. Yet the same problem can arise: the witnesses, concerned about the {\i sofer\u8217's} feelings, have him sign as a witness instead of one of them \u8212- and again the husband never authorized this arrangement.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava\u8217's Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava concludes: {\i ha nami kasher v\u8217'lo ta\u8217'aseh hu} \u8212- this second scenario also falls under the rubric of {\i kasher v\u8217'lo ta\u8217'aseh}. One may not do this {\i lechatchila}, and the {\i sofer} should refuse to participate as a witness in such a circumstance. Since both scenarios are prohibited, neither presents a practical danger that should lead us to invalidate {\i chasam sofer v\u8217'ed} entirely.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Yose Explains It\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 At this point the {\i Gemara} draws a clean conclusion: {\i Rabbi Yose tarti amar} \u8212- Rabbi Yose holds both positions simultaneously. Not only does he hold {\i mili lo mimsaran l\u8217'shaliach} \u8212- that a verbal appointment cannot be sub-delegated \u8212- he also holds that {\i omer imru} is invalid. No one can represent the husband in the writing, signing, or delivery of a {\i get} unless that person heard the appointment directly from the husband himself. This dual position is, upon reflection, entirely coherent. When the {\i mishna} says that even the {\i beis din hagadol} in Jerusalem must do it themselves, the implicit situation is one where the husband almost certainly intended them to delegate \u8212- and yet Rabbi Yose disallows it. This is not only about sub-delegation; it is about the principle that only those who heard directly from the husband can be part of the process. That is {\i omer imru} logic as much as {\i mili} logic.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Milei vs.\u160 ?Omer Imru (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Reconciliation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 With Rabbi Yose\u8217's position now fully articulated, the {\i Gemara} returns to Shmuel\u8217's ruling: {\i halacha k\u8217'Rabbi Yose de\u8217'amar mili lo mimsaran l\u8217'shaliach}. The question is whether Shmuel adopts Rabbi Yose\u8217's position entirely \u8212- both {\i mili} and {\i omer imru} \u8212- or only partially. To understand the answer, it is worth being precise about the conceptual difference between the two principles.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Mili lo mimsaran l\u8217'shaliach} addresses the internal authority of an agent: if I appointed Reuven, does Reuven have the power to take that appointment and transfer it to Shimon? The answer is no \u8212- the appointment stays with Reuven. {\i Omer imru} addresses a different question: can the husband appoint someone who is not in his presence, by having a messenger communicate the appointment? This is the question of {\i shlichus shelo befanav} \u8212- can you create agency in someone who did not directly hear the appointment from the principal?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 These are distinct. In the first case, the appointed agent is fully empowered but lacks the authority to transfer his role. In the second case, the question is whether a valid appointment was ever created in the first place.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Yose Both Rules\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yose holds that both are prohibited, and this is what makes his position internally consistent and explains the full force of the {\i mishna\u8217's} ruling about the {\i beis din hagadol}. Even in that case \u8212- where the implicit intent seems clearly to delegate \u8212- Rabbi Yose says no: the only people who can act are those who directly heard the appointment from the husband. {\i Mili lo mimsaran} prevents sub-delegation; the {\i omer imru} prohibition prevents indirect appointment altogether.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shmuel\u8217's Split\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Shmuel, however, splits the difference. {\i U-Shmuel savar lah kevaseih b\u8217'chada u-palig aleih b\u8217'chada} \u8212- Shmuel agrees with Rabbi Yose on one point and disagrees on the other. Shmuel holds {\i mili lo mimsaran l\u8217'shaliach}: if you are the appointed agent, you cannot pass your appointment on. But Shmuel disagrees regarding {\i omer imru}: the husband may appoint someone through an intermediary. An indirect appointment, where a messenger informs the prospective agent that the husband is appointing him, is valid. The appointee need not hear it directly from the husband himself.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This split is the key that unlocks Shmuel\u8217's original question. When the husband says to two people {\i kitvu utnu get l\u8217'ishti}, and Shmuel asks whether {\i kitvu} means sign or scribe \u8212- here is what depends on the answer. If {\i kitvu} means the actual scribing, then having the {\i sofer} do it would require them to transfer their own appointment to the {\i sofer}, which is {\i mili lo mimsaran}, and Shmuel would rule it invalid. But if {\i kitvu} means sign, then the husband never appointed these two to scribe at all; he implicitly left the scribing open, to be arranged through them as intermediaries. That is {\i omer imru}, which Shmuel permits. They are not sub-delegating their appointment; they are communicating the husband\u8217's appointment of the {\i sofer}. Hence the {\i get} would be valid. Shmuel\u8217's uncertainty about whether the {\i get} is valid \u8212- his response of {\i teitzei v\u8217'hadavar tzarich talmud} \u8212- reflects precisely his uncertainty about what {\i kitvu} means in this context.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Lishnayim Agency\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Indirect Appointment\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now takes up a case directly related to {\i omer imru}. The husband says to two people: {\i imru l\u8217'sofer veyichtov, uleploni ploni veyachtimu} \u8212- tell the {\i sofer} to write it and tell so-and-so and so-and-so to sign it. Here the husband himself is not directly appointing anyone; he is using two intermediaries to communicate all of his appointments. Everything is transmitted indirectly.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Concern: Bribed Witnesses\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Huna says in the name of Rav: {\i keshera v\u8217'lo ta\u8217'aseh chen b\u8217'Yisrael} \u8212- it is valid, but one should not do this in Israel. The {\i Gemara} asks: if it is valid, why should one not do it? The answer: {\i chayishinan shema tiskor edim} \u8212- we are concerned lest the woman have hired witnesses. If the husband does not directly appoint anyone and instead two intermediaries come to the {\i sofer} and the prospective witnesses claiming that the husband instructed them, there is no independent verification. Perhaps the woman bribed these two people to fabricate the husband\u8217's appointment, enabling the {\i get} to be written without the husband\u8217's genuine consent.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Trust in Documents\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} challenges this concern: is it not true that witnesses who sign a bill of sale or a {\i get} are trusted, and the Sages have no concern about forgery? {\i Edim hachatumim al sadeh mekach v\u8217'al get ishah, lo chashu chachamim l\u8217'davar zeh} \u8212- witnesses signed on a deed of sale or on a woman\u8217's {\i get}, the Sages had no such worry.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Words vs.\u160 ?Writing\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} resolves the tension: {\i ma\u8217'aseh lo avdi} \u8212- people will not actually forge a document. We are not afraid they will put their names to a fraudulent {\i get}. But {\i dibura ka\u8217'amri} \u8212- spoken messages are another matter. Finding someone willing to pass on a fabricated verbal message, without personally signing anything, is a realistic concern. The two intermediaries are not personally implicated by any written document; they are merely transmitting words. That is where the potential for corruption lies, and that is why the {\i sofer} and the witnesses should not agree to be appointed through such an indirect chain.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Witnesses Self-Commit\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} then considers a modified scenario: {\i amar lishnayim imru l\u8217'sofer veyichtov v\u8217'atem chatomu} \u8212- the husband tells the two directly that they themselves will sign, and only asks them to communicate to the {\i sofer} that he should write the {\i get}. Here the witnesses are personally committed \u8212- they are signing \u8212- so there is no concern that the woman hired them. The {\i sofer} can have confidence that the witnesses\u8217' word is genuine, since they are putting their own names on the document.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Amoraic Dispute\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Chisda rules: {\i keshera v\u8217'lo ta\u8217'aseh} \u8212- even this is valid but should not be done, as a precautionary measure to prevent sliding into the broader prohibited practice. The {\i Gemara} then records a remarkable range of {\i Amoraic} opinions. Rabbah bar bar Chana says: {\i kasher veta\u8217'aseh} \u8212- it is permitted outright. Rav Nachman: {\i kasher v\u8217'lo ta\u8217'aseh}. Rav Sheshet: {\i kasher veta\u8217'aseh}. Rabbah: {\i kasher v\u8217'lo ta\u8217'aseh}. Rav Yosef: {\i kasher veta\u8217'aseh}. And the {\i Gemara} adds that some reverse the attributions to Rabbah and Rav Yosef. What emerges is that in practice we follow Rav Yosef, but the picture is complicated by Shmuel\u8217's earlier ruling, which acknowledged {\i omer imru} as technically valid while the subsequent {\i Amoraic} discussion introduces pragmatic concerns about reliability that counsel against it even where it would be {\i bedieved} valid.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Counting Names\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kulkhem Distinction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now takes up the {\i braita} elaborating on the ten-person case from the {\i mishna}. {\i Amar l\u8217'asarah kitvu get utnu l\u8217'ishti, echad kotev al yedei kulam} \u8212- if he says to ten people \u8220"write a {\i get} and give it to my wife,\u8221" one writes on behalf of all. By contrast, {\i kulchem kitvu} \u8212- if he says \u8220"all of you write it\u8221" \u8212- then {\i echad kotev bema\u8217'amad kulam}: one writes but all must be present.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 One Writes Distribution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The same logic applies to delivery. {\i Holichu get l\u8217'ishti, echad molich al yedei kulam} \u8212- if he says \u8220"bring the {\i get} to my wife,\u8221" one person delivers on behalf of all. {\i Kulchem holichu} \u8212- if he specifies all of you \u8212- then {\i echad molich bema\u8217'amad kulam}: one delivers, but all must be present. The analogy to the {\i shaliach tzibur} is instructive: just as the prayer leader represents the entire congregation in {\i tefillat hatzibur} \u8212- each member present, one person as the mouthpiece \u8212- so here, one person performs the act while all are present as the constitutive body.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Counting Effect\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This leads to a novel question: {\i mana otan mahu} \u8212- if the husband does not say {\i kulchem} explicitly but instead counts them \u8212- one, two, three, four, five, six, seven, eight, nine, ten \u8212- does that counting carry the same legal weight as saying {\i kulchem}? Does enumerating each person individually signal an intent that all of them must participate, or does it merely mean \u8220"from among you\u8221"?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Huna: No Effect\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Huna rules: {\i mana eino ke\u8217'kulam} \u8212- counting is not equivalent to saying {\i kulchem}. The counting does not impose the requirement that all must be present.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yochanan: Does Mean\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Yochanan, citing Rabbi Elazar from Rome, rules: {\i mana harei hu ke\u8217'kulam} \u8212- counting is indeed equivalent to {\i kulchem}. By identifying each person individually, the husband signals that each of them is specifically included and required.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Papa Resolves\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Papa steps in to resolve the dispute: {\i v\u8217'lo pligi} \u8212- they do not actually disagree. The distinction is whether the husband counted all the people in the room or only some of them. {\i Ha d\u8217'mana kulhu ha d\u8217'mana miktzatayhu} \u8212- one ruling applies when he counted everyone present, and the other applies when he counted only some. Which case yields which ruling is itself debated \u8212- {\i amri lah l\u8217'hai gisa v\u8217'amri lah l\u8217'hai gisa} \u8212- with some holding that counting everyone means {\i kulchem} and counting some means \u8220"from among you,\u8221" while others hold the reverse. The underlying logic in each direction is not entirely transparent, but the operative distinction is whether the counting was exhaustive or selective.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Takanah for Formula\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Fixed Nusach\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Yehuda instituted a {\i takanah} to address the practical hazards created by the {\i kulchem} requirement. He established a fixed {\i nusach} \u8212- a standard formula \u8212- for the husband to use when appointing agents to write and deliver a {\i get}. The formula: {\i ktovu o kulchem o kol chad v\u8217'chad minchon, chatomu shtein o kulchem o kol trei minchon, ovilu o kulchem o kol chad v\u8217'chad minchon} \u8212- write it, whether all of you or any one of you; sign it, whether all of you or any two of you; deliver it, whether all of you or any one of you. By explicitly including the alternative of any individual, the formula ensures that the {\i get} will not be invalidated by the death or absence of one of the appointees.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava\u8217's Caution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava raises a concern with this formula. {\i Lo zimnin degayiz lei l\u8217'diburei, v\u8217'omar kulchem v\u8217'lo amar kol chad v\u8217'chad minchon} \u8212- it sometimes happens that a person will cut off what he is saying and will say {\i kulchem} without completing the rest of the formula. If the husband says \u8220"all of you\u8221" and fails to add \u8220"or any one of you,\u8221" the {\i get} will become {\i pasul}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Better Wording\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava therefore proposes an alternative: do not begin with {\i kulchem} at all. {\i Ktovu kol chad minchon, chatomu kol trei minchon, ovilu kol chad minchon} \u8212- simply establish a text that opens with \u8220"any one of you\u8221" and does not introduce {\i kulchem} in the first place. If the formula never raises the problem of an all-inclusive requirement, there is no risk that the husband will inadvertently impose one. The word {\i kulchem} is the source of the difficulty; the better practice is to teach people a formula that avoids it entirely.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Gittin 66b\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Multiple Messengers\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two People: Write & Deli\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The present daf picks up with the final line of 66a, continuing the {\i mishna}\u8217's discussion of the language a husband uses when appointing a {\i shaliach} and the scope of the word {\i kitvu}. The next {\i mishna} turns to a related but distinct issue: whether the individuals whom the husband has appointed must carry out the task personally, or whether they may delegate that responsibility to others.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} opens with two cases. If the husband says to two people, {\i tnu get l\u8217'ishti} \u8212- give a {\i get} to my wife \u8212- then since {\i tnu} implies giving but necessarily also writing, the two must themselves carry out both actions. Similarly, if he says to three people, {\i kitvu get u\u8217'tnu l\u8217'ishti} \u8212- write a {\i get} and give it to my wife \u8212- then {\i harei elu yichtevu v\u8217'yitnu}: it must be precisely those individuals who do it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Three People: Delivery O\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 There is, however, an important exception. If the husband says to three people, {\i tnu get l\u8217'ishti} \u8212- give a {\i get} to my wife \u8212- without specifying {\i kitvu}, then those three may in turn instruct others to write it: {\i harei elu yomru l\u8217'acherim v\u8217'yichtevu}. The {\i mishna} explains: {\i mipnei she\u8217'asah beis din} \u8212- because he has constituted them as a {\i beis din} \u8212- and this is {\i divrei Rebbi Meir}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The distinction between two and three people rests on a conceptual difference in what a group of that size represents. Two people are not a constituted body; they are simply the individuals appointed, and the expectation is that they themselves perform the task. Three people, by contrast, constitute a {\i beis din}, and a {\i beis din} is understood as a supervisory body \u8212- one that takes responsibility for seeing that something is done, not necessarily one that performs every action directly. Accordingly, when the husband says {\i kitvu u\u8217'tnu} explicitly to a group of three, he is being concrete about the actions and means that they themselves must perform them. But when he says only {\i tnu} to three, the word is understood as conveying oversight and administrative authority, which they may then delegate.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Beit Din Authority\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} adds that this ruling was transmitted by Rebbi Chanina ish Ono from prison \u8212- and Rashi explains that this means in the name of Rebbi Akiva, who was imprisoned at the time due to the Roman decrees. The report states: {\i mekubal ani b\u8217'omer lishlosha tnu get l\u8217'ishti sheyomru l\u8217'acherim v\u8217'yichtevu mipnei she\u8217'asah beis din} \u8212- I have a tradition that when one says to three people \u8220"give a {\i get} to my wife,\u8221" they may transmit that to others, because he has made them a {\i beis din}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Yosi\u8217's Tradition\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rebbi Yosi responds with a counter-tradition. {\i Af anu mekubalim} \u8212- we too have a received tradition \u8212- and his tradition is far more demanding. Even if the husband says {\i tnu get l\u8217'ishti} to the {\i beis din hagadol sheb\u8217'Yerushalayim} \u8212- the Great Court of Jerusalem itself \u8212- and says only {\i tnu} without {\i kitvu}, those distinguished jurists cannot delegate the task. If they wish to give the {\i get}, {\i sheyilmedu v\u8217'yichtevu v\u8217'yitnu} \u8212- they must learn how to write a {\i get} and write it themselves. The appointment, according to Rebbi Yosi, is non-transferable; only the individuals who heard the instruction directly from the husband may execute it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ten People: Role Distrib\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} then turns to a different scenario: the husband says to ten people, {\i kitvu get u\u8217'tnu l\u8217'ishti}. Since he has said {\i kitvu} explicitly, he cannot have meant mere oversight; he means they themselves should act. But how is that interpreted when there are more than the minimum number of required participants? The {\i mishna} rules: {\i echad kotev u\u8217'shnayim chotmim} \u8212- one writes it and two sign it. The word {\i kitvu} addressed to the group does not mean all ten must write and all ten must sign; it means that from among you, the necessary actions must be performed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kulchem: All Present\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If, however, the husband says {\i kulchem kitvu} \u8212- all of you write \u8212- then {\i echad kotev v\u8217'chulam chotmim}: one writes and they all sign. The word {\i kulchem} changes the character of the instruction.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kitvu: Write or Sign\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 At this point the {\i mishna} raises \u8212- or at least invites \u8212- the question that the {\i Gemara} will pursue explicitly: what is the primary meaning of the word {\i kitvu} in this context? Does it refer to the scribing of the {\i get}, or to signing it? It would seem from the {\i mishna} that signing is the more fundamental referent, since in the case of {\i kulchem kitvu} addressed to ten people, only one person actually scribes the document while all of them sign. The {\i mishna} is evidently not troubled by the fact that not all ten physically write the text. Rather, it seems to be the signing that constitutes the operative meaning of {\i kitvu} \u8212- or at least that signing is its core dimension. One might instead argue that even if {\i kitvu} also encompasses the scribing, the {\i mishna} interprets it practically: for the scribing, it suffices that one person writes on behalf of all, whereas for the signing, each must affix his own signature. The {\i Gemara} will analyze this more carefully.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Death Invalidates Get\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Finally, the {\i mishna} states: {\i im mes echad mehem harei zeh get batel} \u8212- if one of the group dies after the husband said {\i kulchem}, the {\i get} is invalid. This is striking, and worth pausing over. One might have thought that a {\i get} has a fixed form and fixed requirements \u8212- a particular text, two witnesses \u8212- and it would not be obvious how the husband\u8217's specifications could render an otherwise valid {\i get} invalid. But what we see here is how central the husband\u8217's {\i da\u8217'at} and {\i ratzon} are to the very nature of the {\i get}. The husband has shaped the conditions of his own {\i get}, and if those conditions are not fulfilled \u8212- if he required everyone\u8217's participation and one of them dies \u8212- then the {\i get} has not been executed according to his will, and it is therefore {\i batel}. The {\i get}\u8217's validity is inseparable from the husband\u8217's intent as to how it is to be produced.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Kitvu Meaning Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav\u8217's Question to Shmuel\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} opens with a remarkable report: {\i shalechu mibei Rav l\u8217'Shmuel} \u8212- they sent from the {\i yeshiva} of Rav to Shmuel. This is itself a striking phenomenon, since Rav and Shmuel were contemporaries of roughly equal stature, and yet the scholars of Rav\u8217's academy directed this question to Shmuel. The question they sent was: {\i amar l\u8217'shnayim kitvu u\u8217'tnu get l\u8217'ishti v\u8217'amru l\u8217'sofer v\u8217'chatav v\u8217'chatmu hem mahu} \u8212- if the husband said to two people \u8220"write and give a {\i get} to my wife,\u8221" and they did not scribe it themselves but instead had a {\i sofer} write it while they themselves signed, what is the {\i halacha}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The question is fundamentally about the meaning of {\i kitvu}. The two individuals were appointed, they signed the document, but the actual scribing was done by someone else. Does the husband\u8217's instruction {\i kitvu} require that they personally scribe the {\i get}, or does it require only that they sign it? If {\i kitvu} means scribing, then having the {\i sofer} do the scribing violates the husband\u8217's instruction. If {\i kitvu} means signing, then the two have fully complied.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shmuel: Needs Investigat\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Shmuel\u8217's response was: {\i tetzei v\u8217'hadavar tzarich talmud} \u8212- if she remarried on the basis of this {\i get}, she cannot remain with her second husband, because the {\i get} is of uncertain validity; but the matter requires further investigation. Shmuel was genuinely uncertain, and the {\i Gemara} now sets out to determine what the core of the investigation must be.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Words & Delegation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} asks: what exactly is the issue Shmuel identified as requiring investigation? One possibility is that the question is whether {\i mili lo mimsaran l\u8217'shaliach} \u8212- whether words, meaning a purely verbal appointment, can be passed on to another agent. This principle, which will be central to much of the remainder of the {\i daf}, holds that when an agency involves no physical object, only a verbal appointment, the agent may not delegate that agency to a further agent. When the husband gives the {\i shaliach} the physical {\i get} to deliver to the wife, the physical object in some sense represents and carries the agency. But the appointment of the {\i sofer} to write a {\i get} \u8212- before the {\i get} even exists \u8212- is purely verbal, and the question is whether that verbal appointment can be passed on.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Verbal vs.\u160 ?Physical\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 To understand this, it is useful to recall the case of a {\i shaliach lekabbalah} \u8212- an agent appointed to receive the {\i get} on behalf of the wife \u8212- who becomes ill.\u160 ?The question of whether such an agent may appoint a substitute agent is precisely this problem: the agent has only a verbal appointment; there is no physical object that he received which he could now transfer. Without the physical object, the authority may not be transferable.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} raises a different formulation of the same issue. The concern of {\i mili lo mimsaran l\u8217'shaliach} is that the originally appointed agents cannot pass on their appointment to the {\i sofer}; they were appointed, and they cannot on their own authority re-appoint someone to take their place. This is the position underlying the entire {\i mishna}: the whole structure of \u8220"you must do it yourselves\u8221" presupposes that the verbal agency cannot be transferred.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But if this is already the logic of the {\i mishna}, and if Shmuel himself rules that {\i halacha k\u8217'Rebbi Yosi d\u8217'amar mili lo mimsaran l\u8217'shaliach} \u8212- the {\i halacha} follows Rebbi Yosi who holds that a verbal agency cannot be passed on \u8212- then the question seems already answered: the {\i sofer} cannot have been used, because the two appointed agents could not delegate to him. Why then does Shmuel say {\i tzarich talmud}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} therefore reframes the question. Shmuel is not uncertain about {\i mili lo mimsaran l\u8217'shaliach} \u8212- that rule he knows and accepts. The question is rather: {\i hai kitvu, iksav yadan iksav haget?} \u8212- when the husband says {\i kitvu}, does he mean \u8220"sign it\u8221" or does he mean \u8220"scribe it\u8221"? These two possibilities lead to fundamentally different analyses.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If {\i kitvu} means \u8220"scribe it,\u8221" then the two appointed agents were supposed to do the scribing themselves, and having the {\i sofer} scribe it is a violation of the appointment \u8212- a case of {\i mili lo mimsaran l\u8217'shaliach}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If {\i kitvu} means \u8220"sign it,\u8221" then the husband never appointed the two to scribe anything. He told them to sign the {\i get} \u8212- and implicitly, to find someone to write it. In that case, they are not delegating their agency at all. They remain the signatories; they are merely communicating to the {\i sofer} that the husband wants him to write the {\i get}. That is a different concept entirely: {\i omer imru} \u8212- informing a third party of the husband\u8217's wish \u8212- rather than transferring one\u8217's own agency. And {\i omer imru}, as we will see, may be permitted even according to positions that prohibit {\i mili lo mimsaran l\u8217'shaliach}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishna Presumption\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} asks: can we not simply resolve this from the {\i mishna} itself? The {\i mishna} states that if the husband says to two people {\i kitvu u\u8217'tnu get l\u8217'ishti}, then {\i harei elu yichtevu v\u8217'yitnu} \u8212- they must write and give it. This suggests that {\i kitvu} means something they must do themselves, which would favor the interpretation that {\i kitvu} refers to scribing. The {\i Gemara} responds that the {\i mishna} itself is the subject of the question \u8212- the question of what {\i yichtevu} in the {\i mishna} means is precisely what we are investigating. The {\i mishna} does not independently resolve the ambiguity; it merely reuses the same term.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Beit Din Learning\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} then argues more forcefully that {\i kitvu} must mean scribing, drawing on Rebbi Yosi\u8217's ruling in the {\i mishna}. Rebbi Yosi says that even the {\i beis din hagadol shebiYerushalayim}, if told {\i tnu get l\u8217'ishti}, must {\i she\u8217'ilmedu v\u8217'yichtevu v\u8217'yachtemu} \u8212- learn how to write a {\i get} and do so themselves. If {\i kitvu} meant only signing, this passage would be absurd: {\i mi ika bei dina d\u8217'lo yadei michtav chasimas yedaihu} \u8212- is there conceivably a {\i beis din} whose members do not know how to sign their own names? The very premise of Rebbi Yosi\u8217's ruling \u8212- that they would need to {\i learn} \u8212- only makes sense if what they must learn is the scribing of a {\i get}, not the writing of their signatures.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Signing Possibility\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} deflects this argument. One could theoretically posit a newly constituted {\i beis din} whose members have not yet established their customary signatures. While the {\i Gemara} acknowledges this is a weak answer, it means the proof from Rebbi Yosi\u8217's ruling is not airtight. The possibility remains, at least formally, that {\i kitvu} refers to signing. The {\i Gemara} then approaches the issue from yet another angle: if {\i kitvu} means signing and therefore implies that the two signatories may find their own {\i sofer} to scribe the {\i get}, is that not itself a form of {\i mili lo mimsaran l\u8217'shaliach}? The {\i Gemara} answers: no. If the husband said {\i kitvu} meaning \u8220"sign,\u8221" then the husband himself implicitly authorized the signatories to procure a {\i sofer}. Whoever they find to write the {\i get} is, in effect, being appointed by the husband through the mode of {\i omer imru} \u8212- the husband is essentially saying, through his agents, \u8220"go tell someone to write it.\u8221" The signatories are not re-delegating their own agency; they are transmitting the husband\u8217's appointment to the {\i sofer}. And {\i omer imru} \u8212- indirect appointment by informing a third party \u8212- may well be permitted.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Chasam Sofer V\u8217'Ed\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Witnesses Inform Sofer\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now raises a new case to probe these distinctions. A husband says to two people: {\i imru l\u8217'sofer v\u8217'yichtov u\u8217'l\u8217'ploni u\u8217'ploni v\u8217'yachtemu} \u8212- tell the {\i sofer} to write it, and tell so-and-so and so-and-so to sign it. Here the husband himself is using {\i omer imru}: he is appointing the two people not to sign or scribe, but merely to convey his wishes to the {\i sofer} and to the designated witnesses. Rav Huna citing Rav rules: {\i keshera v\u8217'lo ta\u8217'aseh zo b\u8217'Yisrael} \u8212- it is technically valid, but this should never be done.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Scenario Requirement\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This scenario is worth noting. It only makes sense to entertain this case if {\i omer imru} is at least conceivably permitted. Were {\i omer imru} entirely invalid \u8212- were the rule that every participant must hear the appointment directly from the husband \u8212- then this entire arrangement would be obviously invalid and the {\i Gemara} would not need to say it is {\i keshera} while adding a caution against doing it. The very structure of the ruling \u8212- {\i keshera b\u8217'lo ta\u8217'aseh} \u8212- implies that {\i omer imru} is halachically operative.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Embarrassment Problem\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} asks: if it is {\i keshera}, why should one not do it? The answer is: {\i mishum kisufa d\u8217'sofer} \u8212- because of the embarrassment of the {\i sofer}. Consider: the two messengers approach the {\i sofer} and tell him to write the {\i get}, and then instruct him that Reuven and Shimon will sign it. The {\i sofer} may well feel affronted: you trust me to write this document but not to sign it? Am I not a fit witness? Out of concern for the {\i sofer}\u8217's dignity, the messenger may quietly adjust the arrangement \u8212- he might tell the {\i sofer} to sign as well, and drop one of the originally designated witnesses. The resulting {\i get} would then have been executed in a manner the husband never sanctioned. The document would appear valid but would in fact be {\i pasul}, since the witnesses who signed it were not those the husband designated.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Permitted, Not Done\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} resolves this concern through the ruling itself: {\i kivan d\u8217'amar mar kasher v\u8217'lo ta\u8217'aseh kein b\u8217'Yisrael} \u8212- since we rule that it is {\i kasher} but should not be done, this means the {\i sofer} and the designated witnesses should refuse to participate in any arrangement where the embarrassment dynamic might lead them to deviate from the husband\u8217's instructions. Since they are expected to refuse such a scenario, the feared outcome is unlikely to materialize \u8212- {\i lo shechiach} \u8212- and the theoretical validity of {\i omer imru} does not, in practice, give rise to the feared abuse.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Witnesses Find Sofer\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Direct Witness Appointme\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A related but distinct case now presents itself. Suppose the husband says directly to two people: {\i imru l\u8217'sofer v\u8217'yichtov v\u8217'atem chatomu} \u8212- tell the {\i sofer} to write it, and you yourselves sign it. Here the witnesses are directly appointed \u8212- they will personally sign the {\i get} \u8212- and they are only asking the {\i sofer} to write the text. The witnesses have a direct appointment, and the {\i sofer}\u8217's involvement is not a re-delegation but a practical arrangement for the scribing.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Permitted Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Whether this arrangement may be followed {\i lechatchila}, or whether it too falls under the prohibition of {\i lo ta\u8217'aseh}, is debated. The {\i Gemara} will return to this at the end of the {\i daf}. The general concern, as will become clear, remains that a woman might hire individuals to falsely report the husband\u8217's instructions, and any arrangement in which the {\i sofer} is not directly appointed by the husband is potentially vulnerable to that abuse.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Mili vs.\u160 ?Omer Imru\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two Concepts\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Before proceeding, it is important to clearly distinguish between two concepts that run through this entire discussion. The first is {\i mili lo mimsaran l\u8217'shaliach}: if I have appointed you as my agent, you may not pass that appointment on to another. Your agency is personal; it came to you directly from me, and the authority it carries cannot be transferred further. The second concept is the question of {\i shlichus shelo befanav} \u8212- can the husband appoint an agent without speaking to that agent directly? Can he tell Michael to inform Jenna that she is to write the {\i get}, so that Jenna becomes the husband\u8217's agent even though the husband never spoke to her personally?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 These are conceptually distinct. {\i Mili lo mimsaran l\u8217'shaliach} addresses what happens after an appointment has been validly made \u8212- the appointee cannot re-delegate. The question of {\i shlichus shelo befanav} addresses whether a valid appointment can be made indirectly in the first place. {\i Omer imru} is precisely the question of {\i shlichus shelo befanav}: does the husband\u8217's instruction, conveyed through a third party, constitute a valid appointment?\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Mili vs.\u160 ?Omer Imru (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Yosi: Both\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rebbi Yosi, as the {\i Gemara} now establishes, holds both restrictions. He prohibits {\i mili lo mimsaran l\u8217'shaliach} \u8212- once you are appointed, you cannot pass the agency on \u8212- and he also prohibits {\i omer imru} \u8212- the husband cannot appoint someone through an intermediary; the appointee must hear the instruction directly. This is precisely why, in Rebbi Yosi\u8217's ruling in the {\i mishna}, even the {\i beis din hagadol shebiYerushalayim} cannot delegate the writing of the {\i get}: the members of the {\i beis din} are the only ones who heard it from the husband, and only they may execute it. Any indirect appointment \u8212- even one where the husband clearly intended to confer authority on others \u8212- is invalid.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shmuel\u8217's Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Shmuel, by contrast, accepts {\i mili lo mimsaran l\u8217'shaliach} \u8212- he rules like Rebbi Yosi that you cannot re-delegate a verbal appointment \u8212- but he permits {\i omer imru}. The husband may communicate his appointment through a third party, and the person thus informed becomes a valid agent. Shmuel therefore occupies a middle position: tighter than the {\i tanna kamma} but not as restrictive as Rebbi Yosi across the board.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Kitvu Meaning Debate (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shmuel\u8217's Split\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This split in Shmuel\u8217's position is the key to understanding his original question. When the husband says {\i kitvu} to two people, if it means \u8220"scribe it,\u8221" then they cannot have the {\i sofer} do it, because they cannot re-delegate their verbal appointment \u8212- {\i mili lo mimsaran l\u8217'shaliach}. But if {\i kitvu} means \u8220"sign it,\u8221" then implicitly the husband authorized the two to find a {\i sofer} through {\i omer imru}, which Shmuel permits. In that case, the {\i sofer} writes entirely validly, and the {\i get} is {\i kasher}. Shmuel\u8217's uncertainty \u8212- {\i tzarich talmud} \u8212- is thus the unresolved question of which meaning {\i kitvu} carries.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Following Rabbi Yosi\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Shimon\u8217's Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} returns to the ruling cited earlier: {\i amar Shmuel amar Rebbi halacha k\u8217'Rebbi Yosi d\u8217'amar mili lo mimsaran l\u8217'shaliach} \u8212- Shmuel said in the name of Rebbi that the {\i halacha} follows Rebbi Yosi. Rebbi Shimon the son of Rebbi is surprised. Given that both Rebbi Meir and Chanina ish Ono \u8212- reporting in the name of Rebbi Akiva \u8212- disagree with Rebbi Yosi and hold that a group of three constituted as a {\i beis din} may delegate the task, what motivated Rebbi to rule like Rebbi Yosi? {\i Mah ra\u8217'ah Rebbi lomar halacha k\u8217'Rebbi Yosi?} \u8212- what did Rebbi see that led him to do so?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Nimuko Imo\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The response is: {\i shtok beni shtok. V\u8217'ei illu lo ra\u8217'isa es Rebbi Yosi} \u8212- be silent, my son. Had you ever merited to see Rebbi Yosi in person, you would understand. {\i Nimuko imo} \u8212- his reasoning is with him. This is the famous principle by which the {\i halacha} generally follows Rebbi Yosi: {\i halacha k\u8217'Rebbi Yosi shenimuko imo}. Whatever Rebbi Yosi ruled was backed by particularly thorough and well-grounded reasoning. Even when his position is outnumbered, even when the logic on the surface seems to favor his opponents, the presumption is that Rebbi Yosi\u8217's position has a depth of reasoning that justifies the ruling in his favor.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Praises of Sages\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} supports this by citing a {\i braisa} from {\i Avos d\u8217'Rebbi Natan}, in which Issi ben Yehuda would enumerate the distinctive qualities of the Sages. {\i Rebbi Meir chacham v\u8217'sofer} \u8212- Rebbi Meir was a sage and a scribe. Notably, he was literally a scribe, which lends an interesting resonance given our {\i sugya}\u8217's concern with the scribing of a {\i get}. {\i Rebbi Yehuda chacham lecheshe\u8217'yirtzeh} \u8212- Rebbi Yehuda was wise when he chose to be; Rashi explains that when he took the time to think something through, he could be very wise. {\i Rebbi Tarfon gal shel egozim} \u8212- Rebbi Tarfon was like a pile of nuts: ask him something and everything comes cascading out, one thing flowing from the next in abundance. {\i Rebbi Yishmael chanut meyuzenet} \u8212- Rebbi Yishmael was like a well-stocked store; whatever you need, he has it, and he knows where to find it. {\i Rebbi Akiva otzar balum} \u8212- Rebbi Akiva was a warehouse organized into compartments. Rashi and Tosafos debate the precise meaning of {\i balum}, but {\i Avos d\u8217'Rebbi Natan} itself clarifies: Rebbi Akiva would receive teachings in a mixed, unsorted form \u8212- {\i midrash}, {\i halacha}, analysis \u8212- and then come home and sort everything into its proper category. This is indeed what his students did in editing the {\i midrashei halacha} \u8212- the {\i Sifra}, the {\i Sifrei} \u8212- systematizing the entire corpus, a feat not unlike what the {\i Rambam} later achieved.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rebbi Yochanan ben Nuri kupa shel rochlim} \u8212- Rebbi Yochanan ben Nuri was like the basket of traveling merchants, who carried unusual and varied wares from town to town. Unlike the stocked store with its staples, the merchant\u8217's basket contains the rare and the unexpected. {\i Rebbi Elazar ben Azarya kupa shel besamim} \u8212- he was like the spice merchant\u8217's basket; Rashi says this is similar in concept, though perhaps smaller in scope. {\i Mishnas Rebbi Eliezer ben Yaakov kav v\u8217'naki} \u8212- the teaching of Rebbi Eliezer ben Yaakov is only a {\i kav} \u8212- a small measure \u8212- but it is a very clean measure: few rulings, but each one carefully sifted and pure, so that what he does say carries correspondingly great weight.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 And then: {\i Rebbi Yosi nimuko imo} \u8212- Rebbi Yosi\u8217's reasoning is with him. This is striking, because the reasoning is typically not spelled out explicitly in the {\i mishna}; on the surface, one might even think the more logical position belongs to Rebbi Meir and Chanina ish Ono, who held that a group constituted as a {\i beis din} may naturally delegate. Yet the received principle is that Rebbi Yosi\u8217's underlying reasoning, even when unstated, is so sound that we rule with him.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} also cites the description of Rebbi Shimon: {\i tochen harbeh u\u8217'motzi kim\u8217'a} \u8212- he grinds a great deal and produces only a little. This sounds like a criticism, but the {\i Gemara} clarifies: {\i mishake\u8217'ach kim\u8217'a} \u8212- he forgets only a little. The grinding is thorough; what he \u8220"produces\u8221" outward, what he actually forgets, is minimal \u8212- and even what he does forget is {\i suvin}, the chaff, the material that should have been discarded anyway. Rebbi Shimon himself told his students: {\i shanu midotai} \u8212- learn my methodology or my teachings \u8212- {\i sheymidotai trumot mitrumot midotav shel Rebbi Akiva} \u8212- for they are the choicest of the choicest of Rebbi Akiva\u8217's methods.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Lo Ta\u8217'aseh Practice\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Huna\u8217's Ruling\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now examines the practical ruling. The case is: {\i amar lishnayim imru l\u8217'sofer v\u8217'yichtov u\u8217'l\u8217'ploni ploni v\u8217'yachtemu} \u8212- the husband says to two people: go tell the {\i sofer} to write the {\i get}, and go tell so-and-so and so-and-so to sign it. Rav Huna citing Rav rules: {\i keshera v\u8217'lo ta\u8217'aseh zo b\u8217'Yisrael} \u8212- it is valid but should not be done this way.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Lo Ta\u8217'aseh Practice (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Woman Hiring Concern\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The question is pressed: if it is {\i keshera}, why the prohibition? The answer is: {\i chaishinan shema tischor eidim} \u8212- we are concerned that the woman may have hired witnesses. If the husband did not personally appoint anyone, and two people come to the {\i sofer} and the witnesses claiming that the husband instructed them to arrange the {\i get}, there is a real concern that the woman herself hired those two people to make this claim. Since the intermediaries bear no direct personal responsibility \u8212- they are not signing the {\i get} themselves, they are merely conveying a message \u8212- they might be persuadable to lie. The {\i sofer} and the witnesses, by relying on these intermediaries, risk participating in the production of a fraudulent {\i get}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Dibbura vs.\u160 ?Ma\u8217'aseh\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} challenges this concern: {\i eidim hachatumim al hasadeh mekach v\u8217'al get ishah lo chashu chachamim l\u8217'davar zeh} \u8212- witnesses who are signed on a bill of sale or on a {\i get} are not suspected of forgery; the Sages had no general concern that witnesses might falsify documents. The {\i Gemara} distinguishes: {\i ma\u8217'aseh lo avdi} \u8212- people will not commit an actual act of forgery, will not sign a false document. But {\i dibbura ka\u8217'amri} \u8212- they may be willing merely to convey a false oral message, to tell the {\i sofer} and the witnesses that the husband gave these instructions when in fact he did not. The act of signing a forged document is a categorical offense that people generally refuse. But repeating a message \u8212- even a false one \u8212- without personally being a signatory to the resulting {\i get}, carries a lower psychological threshold, and the concern that the woman\u8217's hired agents might do so is realistic. The prohibition of {\i lo ta\u8217'aseh ken b\u8217'Yisrael} addresses precisely this vulnerability.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Amoraic Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A subsequent case introduces a further variation: {\i amar lishnayim imru l\u8217'sofer v\u8217'yichtov v\u8217'atem chatomu} \u8212- suppose the husband tells two people: go tell the {\i sofer} to write it, but you yourselves sign it. Here, the witnesses are directly appointed \u8212- they will personally sign the {\i get}, with full personal accountability \u8212- and they are only instructing the {\i sofer} to write. Rav Chisda rules: {\i keshera b\u8217'lo ta\u8217'aseh} \u8212- even this arrangement is valid but should not be done, though here the prohibition is only a {\i gezeira}, a protective extension, lest permitting this lead to permitting the prior case. Rabba bar bar Chana permits it outright: {\i kasher v\u8217'ta\u8217'aseh}. Rav Nachman rules {\i kasher v\u8217'lo ta\u8217'aseh}. Rav Sheshet says {\i kasher v\u8217'ta\u8217'aseh}. Rabba says {\i kasher v\u8217'lo ta\u8217'aseh}. Rav Yosef says {\i kasher v\u8217'ta\u8217'aseh}. The {\i Gemara} notes that some authorities reverse the positions attributed to Rabba and Rav Yosef.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The practical bottom line is nuanced. The {\i halacha} of {\i mili lo mimsaran l\u8217'shaliach} is accepted, ruling like Rebbi Yosi. {\i Omer imru}, however \u8212- the indirect appointment through an intermediary \u8212- is technically valid according to Shmuel\u8217's position, which the {\i Gemara} does not fully abandon. Yet {\i omer imru} in practice should not be relied upon, not because it is halachically invalid in principle, but because of the risk that those conveying the message may not genuinely be representing the husband.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Witnesses Find Sofer (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hired Witnesses Fear\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This connects back to the case where witnesses are appointed directly but find their own {\i sofer}. There too, the real concern underlying the prohibition of {\i lo ta\u8217'aseh} is not the technical question of {\i shlichus shelo befanav} but the fear that the woman has hired witnesses to fabricate the husband\u8217's instructions. {\i Ma\u8217'aseh lo avdi} \u8212- people will not forge a signature on the document \u8212- but {\i dibbura ka\u8217'amri} \u8212- they may be willing to falsely represent a verbal instruction. The responsibility falls on the last link in the chain: the {\i sofer} and the witnesses who will ultimately produce the {\i get} must not rely on intermediaries whose report cannot be independently verified, lest the entire get rest on a fabricated mandate.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Counting Ten People\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Enumeration Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} raises a final question concerning the {\i mishna}\u8217's case of ten people. If the husband did not say {\i kulchem} \u8212- all of you \u8212- but instead counted them one by one: one, two, three, four, five, six, seven, eight, nine, ten, give a {\i get} to my wife \u8212- what is the legal effect of enumeration? Does counting each person mean the same as saying {\i kulchem}, requiring all of them to participate? Or does it mean merely that he selected this group as the pool from which the requisite participants are to be drawn?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Huna on Counting\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Huna rules: {\i mana eino kechulam} \u8212- counting is not equivalent to {\i kulchem}. Enumerating the individuals simply designates the group; it does not require all of them to participate. Any of them may act on behalf of the group.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Yochanan on Counting\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Yochanan, citing Rabbi Elazar from Rome, rules: {\i mana harei hu kechulam} \u8212- counting is equivalent to {\i kulchem}. When the husband counts each person individually, he is singling out each one specifically, and the effect is the same as explicitly requiring all of them.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Papa\u8217's Resolution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Papa offers a resolution: {\i velo pligi} \u8212- they do not actually dispute each other. The disagreement depends on a specific factual distinction: {\i ha demana kulhu, ha demana mikztayehu} \u8212- one ruling applies where the husband counted all the people present, and the other applies where he counted only some of them. The {\i Gemara} notes that there is a further dispute as to which direction the rule runs. Some say: if he counted all of them, it is equivalent to {\i kulchem}, since he specifically accounted for each one; if he counted only some, it merely designates a subgroup from which any may act. Others reverse this: if he counted all of them, it is not equivalent to {\i kulchem}, since that is simply how one refers to a group; but if he singled out a subset, the enumeration of that specific subset implies that each of the counted individuals is required.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Yehuda\u8217's Takanah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Yehuda instituted a {\i takana} to address the ambiguity surrounding {\i kulchem}. He established a recommended text for cases where a husband wishes to involve multiple people, specifying: {\i ktovu o kulchem o kol echad v\u8217'echad michem} \u8212- write, either all of you or any one of you; {\i chatmu tarrei o kulchem o kol tarrei michem} \u8212- sign, either all of you or any two of you; {\i avilu o kulchem o kol echad v\u8217'echad michem} \u8212- deliver, either all of you or any one of you. By explicitly including both possibilities in the husband\u8217's instructions, the ambiguity is dissolved: whether all participate or only some, the {\i get} is valid.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava\u8217's Practical Advice\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava, however, objects to this formulation. {\i Zimnin d\u8217'gazlei le-diburei v\u8217'amar kulchem v\u8217'lo amar kol echad v\u8217'echad michem} \u8212- it sometimes happens that one seizes on the first part of the instruction and says {\i kulchem} without completing the formulation with {\i kol echad v\u8217'echad michem}, thereby inadvertently invoking the more restrictive requirement. The problem is that beginning with {\i kulchem} plants a word that, if the remainder of the formula is omitted or forgotten, will invalidate the {\i get}. Rava therefore advises: eliminate {\i kulchem} from the formula altogether. Simply instruct: {\i ktovu kol echad michem} \u8212- write, any one of you; {\i chatmu kol tarrei michem} \u8212- sign, any two of you; {\i avilu kol echad michem} \u8212- deliver, any one of you. By removing {\i kulchem} entirely from the husband\u8217's instructions, the risk of inadvertently imposing that requirement disappears.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 With this practical guidance, the {\i perek} concludes: {\i hadran alach ha\u8217'omer}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Gittin 67\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Kitvu & Shlichim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two vs.\u160 ?Three People\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i daf} properly begins with the final line of 66a, where the {\i Mishnah} turns to a question of fundamental importance in the laws of {\i get}: when a husband appoints agents to write and deliver a {\i get}, must those specific individuals carry out the task themselves, or may they delegate to others?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Mishnah} draws a critical distinction based on both the number of people addressed and the precise language used. When a husband says to two people, {\i \u8220"tnu get l\u8217'ishti\u8221"} \u8212- give a {\i get} to my wife \u8212- they themselves must execute the task. Since giving a {\i get} necessarily implies writing it, they cannot hand that responsibility to anyone else. Similarly, if he says to three people, {\i \u8220"kitvu get u-tnu l\u8217'ishti\u8221"} \u8212- write a {\i get} and give it to my wife \u8212- the use of {\i kitvu} makes explicit that they must do it directly: {\i harei elu yichtevu v\u8217'yitnu}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The more nuanced case arises when he says to three people simply {\i \u8220"tnu get l\u8217'ishti\u8221"} \u8212- without the word {\i kitvu}. Here the {\i Mishnah} reasons that three people constitute a {\i beis din}, and a {\i beis din} is understood as a supervisory body rather than a group expected to perform each action personally. Therefore, when {\i kitvu} is absent and the group is three, the husband may be understood as delegating oversight rather than direct execution, and those three may instruct others to write the {\i get}. This is {\i divrei Rabbi Meir}. The {\i Mishnah} further records a tradition transmitted by Rabbi Chanina ish Ono from prison \u8212- understood by {\i Rashi} as being in the name of Rabbi Akiva, who was then held under Roman imprisonment \u8212- confirming: {\i mekubalani b\u8217'omer lishloshah tnu get l\u8217'ishti she\u8217'yomru l\u8217'acherim v\u8217'yichtevu, mipnei she\u8217'asan Beis Din} \u8212- when three are told to give a {\i get}, they may delegate, because he constituted them as a {\i beis din}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Yosi\u8217's Rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yosi dissents sharply: {\i af anu mekubalin she-afilu amar l\u8217'Beis Din HaGadol she-bi-Yerushalayim tnu get l\u8217'ishti, harei elu yichtevu v\u8217'yitnu} \u8212- even if one said to the High Court in Jerusalem, \u8220"give a {\i get} to my wife,\u8221" they themselves must write it and give it. Rabbi Yosi\u8217's position strips away any leniency based on the nature of the body addressed. Even the most eminent court in the world, if they received only a verbal instruction to give a {\i get}, must carry it out personally. If they do not know how to write a {\i get}, they must learn. They cannot delegate what was entrusted directly to them.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Mishnah} then shifts to a related but distinct case: when the husband addresses ten people. If he says {\i \u8220"kitvu get u-tnu l\u8217'ishti\u8221"} without the word {\i kulchem} \u8212- all of you \u8212- it means from among you: one writes and two sign. But if he adds {\i kulchem} \u8212- all of you \u8212- then all ten must be involved. The practical resolution, as the {\i Gemara} will clarify, is that one writes while all are present, analogous to the role of a {\i shaliach tzibur}, who acts on behalf of the entire assembled group. Crucially, {\i lifichach im meis echad meihem harei zeh get batel} \u8212- if one of them dies, the {\i get} is void \u8212- because the husband stipulated that everyone participate, and that condition was not met. This underscores a point worth pausing over: the husband\u8217's {\i da\u8217'at} and {\i ratzon} are so central to the nature of the {\i get} that failure to comply with his precise specification invalidates the document entirely.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Signing vs.\u160 ?Scribing\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} opens with a question sent from the academy of Rav to Shmuel \u8212- a remarkable fact in itself, given that Rav and Shmuel were contemporaries of roughly equal stature. The question: {\i amar l\u8217'shnayim kitvu u-tnu get l\u8217'ishti, amru l\u8217'sofer ve\u8217'chasav ve\u8217'chasmu hein mahu?} \u8212- if a husband told two people to write and give a {\i get}, and they instructed a {\i sofer} to scribe it while they themselves signed, what is the {\i halacha}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The underlying question is what {\i kitvu} means when directed at a group. Does it refer to the physical scribing of the document, or does it refer to the signing \u8212- the affixing of witnesses\u8217' signatures? This distinction carries enormous practical weight. If {\i kitvu} means scribing, then having someone else write it violates the husband\u8217's instruction. If {\i kitvu} means signing, then having a {\i sofer} write the document is not a deviation at all \u8212- it was never their task to scribe it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Shmuel\u8217's response was: {\i teitzei vehadavar tzarich talmud} \u8212- if she remarried on the basis of such a {\i get}, she must leave her second husband, because the {\i get} is uncertain; but the matter requires further investigation. The {\i Gemara} then explores what exactly requires investigation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mili Lo Mimsaran\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One possibility is that the question is about {\i mili}: can a verbal appointment be transferred to another agent? The {\i Gemara} immediately notes that this cannot be Shmuel\u8217's question, because Shmuel himself already rules {\i halacha keRabbi Yosi d\u8217'amar mili lo mimsaran l\u8217'shaliach} \u8212- the {\i halacha} follows Rabbi Yosi that a verbal agency cannot be passed on to another agent. If that principle is settled, why is there any uncertainty?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The concept of {\i mili lo mimsaran l\u8217'shaliach} requires some unpacking, as it is the central analytical thread running through this entire {\i daf}. When a husband appoints a {\i shaliach} to deliver a {\i get}, he hands over a physical document. That physical object, in some sense, embodies and concretizes the agent\u8217's authority, and it may be possible in certain configurations to transfer that authority along with the document. But when the appointment consists only of words \u8212- as when the husband tells two people to write a {\i get} before the document exists \u8212- there is nothing physical to convey. The agent holds only a verbal mandate. The question is whether such a verbal mandate is one the agent can pass on to someone else. Rabbi Yosi\u8217's view, accepted by Shmuel, is that it cannot. If I appoint you to perform this task verbally, you must do it yourself; you cannot re-appoint another in your place.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This principle, however, does not answer the question Shmuel actually intends to investigate. That question is narrower and more precise: {\i hai ktovu, ktav yadam hu o ktav ha-get hu?} \u8212- when the husband says {\i kitvu}, does he mean \u8220"sign it\u8221" or \u8220"scribe it\u8221"?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If {\i kitvu} means \u8220"sign it,\u8221" then the husband never directed these two people to do the scribing at all. What he implicitly said was: find someone to write the {\i get} and sign it yourselves. Having a {\i sofer} write it is not delegating their task \u8212- it is executing the husband\u8217's implicit instruction that someone else handle the scribing. This falls under a distinct concept: {\i omer imru}, the husband directing his appointees to communicate to a third party. In that framework, the two witnesses are not passing on their own agency; they are simply informing a {\i sofer} that he has been appointed by the husband. The husband effectively appointed the {\i sofer} through them.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If, on the other hand, {\i kitvu} means \u8220"scribe it,\u8221" then having someone else do the scribing is forbidden \u8212- not as a violation of {\i mili lo mimsaran l\u8217'shaliach} (since that concerns re-delegation of one\u8217's own agency), but simply because the husband told them to scribe it and they did not.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} attempts to resolve whether {\i kitvu} means scribing from the {\i Mishnah} itself: if it meant signing, why would the {\i Mishnah} say that members of the {\i Beis Din HaGadol} would have to {\i learn} to write the {\i get}? Surely they know how to sign their names. The {\i Gemara} deflects \u8212- perhaps it was a newly constituted court \u8212- but acknowledges the deflection is weak. The sugya thus arrives at two separate issues, which the {\i Gemara} now treats explicitly.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Omer Imru Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Omer Imru Basics\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The concept of {\i omer imru} must be distinguished from {\i mili lo mimsaran l\u8217'shaliach}, though both concern verbal agency. {\i Mili lo mimsaran} addresses whether an agent who received a verbal appointment can pass that appointment on to a third party. {\i Omer imru} addresses a different question entirely: can the husband direct his appointed agents to {\i inform} others that they have been appointed directly by the husband?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The distinction is this: in {\i mili lo mimsaran}, I appoint Michael, and Michael tries to hand off his role to Jenna \u8212- something he has no authority to do. In {\i omer imru}, I tell Michael to inform Jenna that I am appointing her \u8212- the appointment comes from me, and Michael is merely the messenger conveying it. If {\i omer imru} is valid, then the husband can appoint a {\i sofer} and witnesses without speaking to them directly, so long as he instructs an intermediary to relay the appointments. If {\i omer imru} is invalid, then every person involved in the {\i get} must hear their appointment directly from the husband himself.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now asks: does Rabbi Yosi actually agree that {\i omer imru} is permissible? And if so, what does that imply for the case of {\i chasam sofer ve\u8217'eid}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chasam Sofer Concern\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} raises the ruling of {\i chasam sofer ve\u8217'eid} \u8212- where the {\i sofer} who wrote the {\i get} is also one of the two signing witnesses. Rabbi Yirmiyah bar Abba clarifies that the {\i Mishnah} here means {\i chasam sofer shaninu}: the {\i sofer} signed as a witness. And Rav Chisda notes that this {\i Mishnah} follows the position of Rabbi Yosi, for whom {\i mili lo mimsaran l\u8217'shaliach}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Now the {\i Gemara} presses: {\i ve\u8217'i salka da\u8217'atach modei Rabbi Yosi b\u8217'omer imru} \u8212- if Rabbi Yosi agrees that {\i omer imru} is valid, {\i nafka minah churbah}, disaster will follow. Here is the scenario: a husband says to two people, {\i \u8220"imru l\u8217'sofer veyichtov u-l\u8217'Reuven v\u8217'Shimon veyachtomu\u8221"} \u8212- go tell the {\i sofer} to write and tell Reuven and Shimon to sign. This is a legitimate use of {\i omer imru} if the concept is valid. But here is the problem: {\i mishum kisufa d\u8217'sofer} \u8212- out of embarrassment for the {\i sofer}. When the messenger approaches the {\i sofer} and says \u8220"write the {\i get}, but Reuven and Shimon are going to sign it,\u8221" the {\i sofer} may feel slighted. \u8220"You trust me to write it but not to be a witness?\u8221" The messenger, anxious to avoid this embarrassment, decides to make the {\i sofer} one of the witnesses instead of Shimon. The result: the {\i get} is signed by witnesses whom the husband did not appoint, and {\i u\u8217'va\u8217'al lo amar hachi} \u8212- the husband never said this. The {\i get} is invalid, yet no one will know.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} attempts to deflect this concern by noting that {\i kasher v\u8217'lo ta\u8217'aseh kein b\u8217'Yisrael} has already been established \u8212- such appointments ought not be done in Israel, and the {\i sofer} should refuse to participate in this kind of arrangement. If no one is supposed to engage in it {\i lechatchila}, then we need not worry about the scenario. The {\i Gemara} then raises a second version of the same concern: even if the husband directly appoints the two witnesses but asks them to find a {\i sofer}, might the witnesses nonetheless rearrange the roles out of embarrassment \u8212- substituting the {\i sofer} for one of them? The same deflection applies: if witnesses who find their own {\i sofer} also fall under {\i lo ta\u8217'aseh kein b\u8217'Yisrael}, we are not concerned.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two Separate Issues\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 At this point the {\i Gemara} introduces a crucial clarification. The two concerns \u8212- {\i mili lo mimsaran l\u8217'shaliach} and {\i omer imru} \u8212- are conceptually separate, even though they may both bear on the validity of a {\i get}. Shmuel holds both principles, but distinguishes between them. {\i Mili lo mimsaran} means that an agent cannot re-delegate a verbal appointment he himself received. {\i Omer imru} is a distinct question about whether the husband can appoint someone {\i shelo befanav} \u8212- not in their presence \u8212- by having a messenger relay the appointment.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Yosi Both Ways\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} concludes that the only way to fully account for the {\i Mishnah}\u8217's treatment of {\i chasam sofer ve\u8217'eid} \u8212- without concern that the {\i sofer} was substituted for a witness out of embarrassment \u8212- is to say that Rabbi Yosi holds {\i both} prohibitions. {\i Rabbi Yosi tartei amar}: Rabbi Yosi says both that {\i mili lo mimsaran l\u8217'shaliach} and that {\i omer imru} is invalid. According to Rabbi Yosi, every participant in the {\i get} process must have heard their role directly from the husband. This resolves the {\i chasam sofer} concern entirely: if every appointment must be direct, there is no intermediary who could have rearranged things out of embarrassment.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This also explains Rabbi Yosi\u8217's position in the {\i Mishnah} far more coherently than it might first appear. When Rabbi Yosi says that even the {\i Beis Din HaGadol} in Jerusalem would have to write the {\i get} themselves if that is what was said, the implicit point is that no one may act as a conduit for an appointment. Even if the husband\u8217's practical intent was for the court to supervise and delegate, the {\i halacha} is that only those who heard the instruction directly from the husband may execute it. The reasoning of {\i mili lo mimsaran} and {\i omer imru} are conceptually distinct \u8212- the former concerns passing on one\u8217's own delegated authority; the latter concerns whether one can receive an appointment indirectly \u8212- but Rabbi Yosi holds that both are disqualifying. Together they constitute a comprehensive requirement that all agency in the writing and delivery of a {\i get} flow directly from the husband\u8217's own words to each individual actor.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Shmuel\u8217's Psak\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shmuel Splits\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now turns to the {\i gufa} of Shmuel\u8217's ruling: {\i ushmu\u8217'el savar lah keva\u8217'tei bechada u\u8217'falig alei bechada} \u8212- Shmuel agrees with Rabbi Yosi in one respect and disagrees in another. Shmuel accepts {\i mili lo mimsaran l\u8217'shaliach} \u8212- a verbal agency cannot be transferred by the agent who holds it. He does not accept that {\i omer imru} is prohibited. According to Shmuel, the husband may appoint a {\i sofer} or a witness by relaying the appointment through an intermediary; {\i omer imru} is valid, and one may make a {\i shaliach shelo befanav}, appointing someone who is not present, so long as the message is conveyed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is precisely what clarifies the original question from Rav\u8217's academy. Shmuel\u8217's uncertainty about the case where two people had a {\i sofer} write the {\i get} while they signed was not a question about {\i mili} \u8212- that is settled. It was a question about {\i kitvu}: if {\i kitvu} means signing, then the two appointees were only ever asked to sign, and by implicitly directing them to find a {\i sofer}, the husband was effectively using the mechanism of {\i omer imru}. Since Shmuel holds {\i omer imru} is valid, such a {\i get} would be valid in principle \u8212- hence the need to determine what {\i kitvu} actually means in the husband\u8217's instruction.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Nimuko Imo\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} then records an exchange that illuminates the general standing of Rabbi Yosi\u8217's rulings. Shmuel transmitted this {\i halacha} in the name of {\i Rebbi} (Rabbi Yehuda HaNasi), prompting Rabbi Shimon, Rebbi\u8217's son, to object: given that Rabbi Meir and Chanina ish Ono \u8212- who brought the ruling from Rabbi Akiva in prison \u8212- both disagree with Rabbi Yosi, {\i mah ra\u8217'ah Rebbi lomar halacha keRabbi Yosi?} What motivated his father to rule like Rabbi Yosi against those other prominent opinions?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rebbi\u8217's response is striking: {\i shtok beni shtok} \u8212- be silent, my son. Had you ever merited to see Rabbi Yosi in person, you would understand, because {\i nimuko imo} \u8212- his reasoning is with him. This is an ancient expression of confidence in Rabbi Yosi\u8217's intellectual authority, one that transcends the mere counting of opinions.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} then cites the famous passage from {\i Avot d\u8217'Rabbi Natan} in which Isi ben Yehudah catalogued the distinctive qualities of the great sages. Rabbi Meir: {\i chacham ve\u8217'sofer} \u8212- wise and a scribe (notably apt given the subject of this {\i daf}). Rabbi Yehudah: {\i chacham l\u8217'chesheyirtzeh} \u8212- wise when he chose to be, meaning when he took the time to reason things through carefully. Rabbi Tarfon: {\i gash shel egozim} \u8212- a heap of walnuts: ask him one thing and everything comes cascading out. Rabbi Yishmael: {\i chanut meyuzenet} \u8212- a well-stocked storehouse, anything one needed was available and organized. Rabbi Akiva: {\i otzar balum} \u8212- a sealed warehouse divided into compartments. The {\i Gemara} clarifies that this refers not to creativity \u8212- which one might naturally associate with Rabbi Akiva \u8212- but to his systematic organization: he would absorb traditions from his teachers in their mixed form (legal analysis, {\i midrash}, and {\i mishnah} all intertwined) and then sort them meticulously into their proper categories, a feat reflected in the work of his students who edited the {\i midrashei halacha}. Rabbi Yochanan ben Nuri: {\i kupat harochlim} \u8212- the basket of a traveling merchant, stocked with a variety of unusual wares. Rabbi Elazar ben Azariah: {\i kupat shel besamim} \u8212- the spice merchant\u8217's basket, similarly varied if somewhat smaller in scope. {\i Mishnat Rabbi Eliezer ben Yaakov}: {\i kav venaki} \u8212- a small measure, but clean: he taught little, but everything he taught was reliable and precise. And Rabbi Yosi: {\i nimuko imo} \u8212- his reasoning is with him.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The phrase is all the more striking because, as the {\i Gemara} itself notes, the reasoning is not always visible in the {\i Mishnah}. If anything, the surface logic might seem to favor Rabbi Meir and the tradition from Rabbi Akiva, who allow delegation when three people are addressed as a {\i beis din}. Yet the rule {\i halacha keRabbi Yosi she-nimuko imo} reflects a presumption that Rabbi Yosi\u8217's positions are grounded in deep, worked-out reasoning, even when that reasoning is not spelled out. The passage concludes with the description of Rabbi Shimon: {\i tochein harbeh u-motzi kima} \u8212- he grinds much and produces little. The {\i Gemara} clarifies that {\i motzi} here means \u8220"expels\u8221" rather than \u8220"produces\u8221": he forgets very little, and what he does forget was only the intellectual chaff.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Two Appoint Sofer\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kasher V\u8217'Lo Taaseh\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now examines the concrete case that had been hovering in the background. {\i Amar l\u8217'shnayim imru l\u8217'sofer veyichtov u-l\u8217'Ploni v\u8217'Ploni veyachtomu} \u8212- a husband says to two people: go tell the {\i sofer} to write, and go tell so-and-so and so-and-so to sign. Here the husband has appointed nobody directly; every appointment is conveyed through the intermediary of these two. Rav Huna citing Rav rules: {\i keshera v\u8217'lo ta\u8217'aseh zot b\u8217'Yisrael} \u8212- it is valid, but one should not do this in Israel.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hired Witnesses Fear\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The reason for {\i lo ta\u8217'aseh}, when asked, is the concern {\i shema tiskor edim} \u8212- perhaps the woman has hired witnesses. In a case where the two appointed intermediaries are telling a {\i sofer} and witnesses what to do, and none of those actors are directly implicated by signing the document themselves, there is a real danger that the woman could find two people willing to falsely claim that the husband gave them this instruction. Unlike the case of forging signatures on a {\i get} \u8212- which the {\i Gemara} elsewhere says people will not do, since {\i ma\u8217'aseh lo avdi}, people will not commit an outright forgery by signing their names to a lie \u8212- here the two intermediaries are not signing anything. They are only transmitting a message. That lower threshold of personal implication makes bribery a credible concern.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Dibura Kamari\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} distinguishes this from the case of forged documents more precisely. By full document forgery, {\i ma\u8217'aseh lo avdi} \u8212- people will not place their own signatures on a fabricated document. But {\i dibura ka\u8217'amri} \u8212- mere speech is another matter. Relaying a false message, without signing one\u8217's name to any document, is exactly the kind of act that bribed witnesses might be willing to perform. This is the specific danger that makes {\i lo ta\u8217'aseh zot b\u8217'Yisrael} necessary even when the arrangement is technically valid.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Witnesses & Sofer\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Chisda Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} then considers a variant: {\i amar l\u8217'shnayim imru l\u8217'sofer veyichtov v\u8217'atem chatomu} \u8212- what if the husband\u8217's two appointees are themselves the witnesses who will sign, and they only ask the {\i sofer} to write? In this case the hired-witness concern evaporates, since the witnesses are personally accountable \u8212- they are putting their own signatures on the {\i get}. Rav Chisda rules: {\i keshera v\u8217'lo ta\u8217'aseh} \u8212- valid, but should not be done. Here the only reason not to do it is prophylactic: if this is permitted, it may lead to the previous scenario being permitted as well.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Amoraim Dispute\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Several {\i Amoraim} then weigh in with differing positions. Rabba bar bar Chana holds the practice is {\i kasher ve-ta\u8217'aseh} \u8212- not only valid but permissible. Rav Nachman rules {\i kasher v\u8217'lo ta\u8217'aseh}. Rav Sheshet rules {\i kasher ve-ta\u8217'aseh}. Rava rules {\i kasher v\u8217'lo ta\u8217'aseh}. Rav Yosef rules {\i kasher ve-ta\u8217'aseh}. The {\i Gemara} adds that some authorities reversed the attributions of Rava and Rav Yosef. The practical upshot is that Shmuel\u8217's position \u8212- {\i halacha keRabbi Yosi} on {\i mili}, while accepting {\i omer imru} in principle \u8212- does not translate straightforwardly into permission {\i lechatchila}, since the {\i lo ta\u8217'aseh} concern about unverifiable messages applies even when {\i omer imru} is conceptually valid.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Ten People Rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Without Kulchem\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} turns to the rules governing a husband who addresses ten people. A {\i baraisa} teaches: {\i amar l\u8217'asara kitvu get u-tnu l\u8217'ishti, echad kotev al yedei kulam} \u8212- if he says to ten people to write a {\i get} and give it to his wife, one of them writes on behalf of all of them. The word {\i kitvu} without {\i kulchem} is interpreted as \u8220"from among you\u8221" \u8212- any one may scribe, any two may sign.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 With Kulchem\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If he adds {\i kulchem} \u8212- {\i kulchem kitvu} \u8212- then {\i echad kotev bemaamad kulam}: one writes while all are present. The analogy to {\i shaliach tzibur} is instructive. Just as the prayer leader in the repetition of the {\i Shemoneh Esrei} represents the entire congregation \u8212- the entire {\i tzibur} prays as a single unit through one mouthpiece \u8212- so too here, when all ten are physically present and one writes on their behalf, it is as though all ten have written it. Similarly, {\i kulchem holichu} \u8212- all of you bring the {\i get} \u8212- means {\i echad molich bemaamad kulam}: one carries the {\i get} while all are present for the conveyance.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Counting Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} then raises a question: {\i mana otan mahu} \u8212- what if the husband counts them individually, naming each one? Does enumeration carry the force of {\i kulchem}, requiring all of them, or does it merely identify the pool from which some may act?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Counting Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Huna holds {\i mana eino kechulchem} \u8212- counting is not equivalent to saying {\i kulchem}; it means any one of them may act. Rav Yochanan in the name of Rabbi Elazar demin Romi holds the opposite: {\i mana harei hu kechulchem} \u8212- counting is equivalent to {\i kulchem}, and all of them are required.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Papa Resolves\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Papa resolves the apparent contradiction: {\i velo peligi} \u8212- they do not actually disagree. The distinction lies in whether the husband counted all of the people present or only some of them. {\i Ha demana kulhu} \u8212- if he counted everyone in the group \u8212- and {\i ha demana miktzathu} \u8212- if he counted only some. The {\i Gemara} reports that there is disagreement about which direction the rule runs: some hold that counting everyone is like {\i kulchem} (since he addressed each individually), while counting a subset means only \u8220"from among those I counted\u8221"; others hold the reverse. Either way, Rav Papa\u8217's point is that the two {\i Amoraim} were addressing different factual scenarios.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Takana & Text\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Yehuda Takana\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} concludes with a practical ordinance. {\i Atkin Rav Yehuda be-gitta de-kulchem} \u8212- Rav Yehuda instituted a standardized formula for a {\i get} involving the {\i kulchem} scenario, designed to avoid the pitfalls just discussed. The formula he proposed: {\i ktovu o kulcham o kol chad minichon} \u8212- write it, whether all of you or any one of you; {\i chasomu shnayim o kulcham o kol trei minichon} \u8212- two of you should sign, whether all or any two; {\i holichu o kulcham o kol chad minichon} \u8212- bring it, whether all or any one of you. By explicitly including both alternatives, the husband\u8217's instruction encompasses all possible configurations and prevents invalidation if not everyone is ultimately present or involved.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava Correction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava objects to this formulation and improves upon it. The problem with Rav Yehuda\u8217's text is structural: {\i zimnin de-gazlei diburei} \u8212- it sometimes happens that the end of the sentence gets cut off. If the husband says {\i \u8220"kulcham\u8221"} first and then fails to complete the phrase {\i \u8220"o kol chad minichon\u8221"}, the instruction defaults to requiring everyone, and the {\i get} becomes vulnerable to invalidity if even one person is absent or dies. Therefore, Rava proposes an alternative formulation: begin with the permissive language rather than the restrictive. Say {\i ktovu kol chad minichon} \u8212- write it, any one of you; {\i chasomu kol trei minichon} \u8212- sign it, any two of you; {\i holichu kol chad minichon} \u8212- bring it, any one of you. By establishing the permissive formula first \u8212- without ever invoking {\i kulchem} \u8212- the text eliminates the risk entirely. If the husband never says {\i kulchem}, there is nothing to satisfy and no way for accidental omission to generate a {\i pasul} {\i get}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Gittin 68\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Kordyakos & Da\u8217'as\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 New Perek Intro\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Daf 68 marks the beginning of a new {\i perek}, one that is distinguished by an extended engagement with {\i aggadah} before returning to {\i halakha}. The daf in fact opens mid-discussion from the final lines of 67b, transitioning seamlessly into its first {\i mishnah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kordyakos Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} introduces the case of {\i mi she\u8217'achazo kordyakos} \u8212- one who is seized with {\i kordyakos}, a condition that renders a person not in his right mind. The Gemara will eventually characterize it as a kind of spirit or demon, though it more likely refers to a specific illness producing mental incapacity. The governing principle is clear: such a person lacks {\i da\u8217'as}, and therefore when he says {\i kisvu get l\u8217'ishti} \u8212- write a {\i get} for my wife \u8212- {\i lo amar klum}, his words are legally meaningless. The appointment of {\i shlichus} requires genuine mental capacity, and that capacity is here absent.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Retraction Valueless\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} then presents a more striking case: {\i amar kisvu get l\u8217'ishti v\u8217'achazo kordyakos v\u8217'chazar v\u8217'amar lo tichtovenu} \u8212- he first said \u8220"write a {\i get} for my wife\u8221" while in his right mind, was then seized with {\i kordyakos}, and in that altered state retracted his instruction. The ruling is that {\i ein devarav ha\u8217'achronim klum} \u8212- his subsequent retraction is legally null. This is a considerable {\i chiddush}. One might have thought that the retraction, even if expressed during an episode of {\i kordyakos}, creates at minimum a {\i safek} sufficient to halt the writing of the {\i get}. Yet the Gemara rules that the wife is {\i vaday megureshes} \u8212- certainly divorced \u8212- and the retraction is treated as entirely without weight. The {\i shlichus} was validly constituted at the outset, and a declaration made while bereft of {\i da\u8217'as} cannot undo it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This pair of cases illuminates a fundamental principle: what {\i halakha} requires is {\i da\u8217'as}, not merely verbal expression. One may speak without {\i da\u8217'as} \u8212- the first case \u8212- or one may lack the ability to speak while possessing full {\i da\u8217'as} \u8212- as the {\i mishnah} goes on to demonstrate. The two scenarios are mirror images of one another.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} then addresses {\i nishtatek} \u8212- one whose speech was silenced, whether through physical ailment or some other cause. If others ask him, \u8220"Shall we write a {\i get} for your wife?\u8221" and he nods, one tests him three times: {\i im amar al lav lav v\u8217'al hen hen} \u8212- if he can consistently signal no for things that are no and yes for things that are yes \u8212- then {\i yichtevu v\u8217'yitnu}, a {\i get} may be written and delivered. This procedure establishes two things simultaneously: that his signals can be interpreted reliably, and that his inability to speak does not reflect any deeper mental impairment.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kordyakos Definition\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then asks {\i mai kordyakos} \u8212- what precisely is this condition? Shmuel answers: {\i d\u8217'nachirei chamra chadta d\u8217'matzarta} \u8212- it is triggered by new wine fresh from the press. This appears to mean that the wine sets off something larger than ordinary intoxication, since the Gemara could simply have described the person as {\i shikor}, a drunk. The Gemara explicitly notes this: {\i v\u8217'lisni mi she\u8217'nashcho yayin chadash} \u8212- why not just say \u8220"one affected by new wine\u8221"? The answer is: {\i ha kamashma lan d\u8217'ha rucha kordyakos shema} \u8212- the text is teaching us that this particular spirit or illness has a name: {\i kordyakos}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Amulets & Names\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i L\u8217'mai nafka mina?} \u8212- why does it matter that it has a name? {\i L\u8217'kameia} \u8212- because if one wishes to write an amulet against this affliction, one must know the name of the {\i shed} being warded off. The name is thus halakhically significant, not merely academically interesting.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Medical Treatments\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kordyakos Remedy\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Immediately following the question of the amulet, the Gemara pivots to medical treatment \u8212- a striking juxtaposition that moves from the metaphysical to the practical. {\i Mai asusa} \u8212- how does one heal {\i kordyakos}? The prescription is {\i bisra sumka agumre} \u8212- lean red meat on coals \u8212- {\i vechamra marka} \u8212- and diluted wine.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sunstroke Treatment\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye then introduces a series of remedies transmitted in the name of his nursemaid, whom he calls {\i em}, mother \u8212- as Abaye was orphaned and reserved this term of endearment for the woman who raised him. For {\i shimsha bas yoma}, a sunstroke or fever of a single day, the remedy is a flask of water. If the condition persists for two days, {\i sikurei} \u8212- bloodletting is required. If it has lasted three days, one returns to the same regimen prescribed for {\i kordyakos}: lean meat on coals and diluted wine.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chronic Fever Cure\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 For {\i shimsha atikta} \u8212- a chronic or long-standing fever \u8212- the treatment is considerably more involved. One takes a black hen, cuts it lengthwise and widthwise, shaves the middle of one\u8217's head, and places the hen on the shaved area, leaving it there {\i ad demisrach} \u8212- until it begins to decompose. One then descends into the water, submerging up to the neck, remaining until faint, then swimming out and sitting down. If this does not suffice, one eats cress and repeats the water immersion in the same manner.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chills Remedy\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The treatment for chills \u8212- {\i talga} \u8212- is characterized by Rashi as a condition of cold. As a kind of counterpart to the fever remedy, one prescribes {\i bisra shamina agumre} \u8212- fat meat on coals \u8212- and {\i vechamra chaya} \u8212- undiluted wine, the reverse of what is recommended for {\i kordyakos}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Amram Story\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Amram Chasida suffered at the hands of the servants of the {\i Reish Galusa}\u8217's household. The {\i Reish Galusa}, the political head of Babylonian Jewry, was not always surrounded by the most upright people, and Rashi explains that Rav Amram drew their hostility precisely because {\i mipnei shehaya chasid uparush umachmir aleihem be\u8217'isurin} \u8212- he was a pious and disciplined man who rebuked the household for its transgressions. In retaliation, they forced him to lie in the snow.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The next day, when they offered to make amends by bringing him whatever he wished, Rav Amram employed reverse psychology. Knowing that {\i hani kol de\u8217'amina lehu mafich apchi} \u8212- whatever he asked for they would give him the opposite \u8212- he requested lean meat on coals and diluted wine. They promptly brought him fat meat on coals and undiluted wine, which was precisely the remedy for chills that he required.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yalta & Bath\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 When Yalta, the learned wife of Rav Nachman and a member of the {\i Reish Galusa}\u8217's family, heard of Rav Amram\u8217's condition, she took matters into her own hands. She brought him to the bathhouse and had him immerse in the waters until they turned red \u8212- the blood and fatty residue of the prescribed meat, Rashi explains, working their way out of his body. His flesh afterward showed markings that Rashi likens to coins, what we might describe as blotches, perhaps consistent with frostbite.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rav Sheshet & Trust\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Decline the Meal\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The narrative shifts to Rav Sheshet, who was blind, and his interactions with the {\i Reish Galusa}\u8217's household. When the {\i Reish Galusa} asked him why he had declined to eat at the household\u8217's table, Rav Sheshet replied without hesitation: {\i delo ma\u8217'alu avdei dechasidi eiver min hachai} \u8212- your servants are not trustworthy; they are suspect of tearing a limb from a living animal rather than producing properly prepared meat. The {\i Reish Galusa} challenged him: {\i mi yeimar} \u8212- who says so? Rav Sheshet replied: {\i hashta machveina lach} \u8212- I will demonstrate it to you now.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Three-Leg Test\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Sheshet instructed his attendant to slip into the kitchen and steal one leg from the animal being prepared. When the servants then brought him a dismembered lamb, he had them place before him a full spread. They came with three legs, having failed to notice that one was missing. Rav Sheshet remarked: this animal had three legs? When they went and tore a leg off of another living animal to make up the count, Rav Sheshet had his attendant place the stolen leg on the table as well. He then observed: so this animal had five legs? The deception was fully exposed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The episode illustrates with painful clarity the necessity of {\i mashgiach temidi} \u8212- constant supervision. Kosher establishments may operate with full integrity under normal conditions, yet the question is what happens when a shipment does not arrive, when a substitution must be made under pressure. The temptation to cut corners in such moments is precisely the vulnerability that requires constant oversight.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mashgiach Role\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Reish Galusa}, rather than accepting Rav Sheshet\u8217's point as definitive, offered an alternative: let the preparations be done in front of Rav Sheshet himself, and he may supervise and eat. Rav Sheshet agreed to this arrangement.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Dangerous Bone\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A plate was set before him, and meat was brought out and placed at his place along with {\i ristana dechancha} \u8212- a particular bone near the thigh region which, Rashi explains, carries a small embedded bone capable of causing choking. The servants, now hostile after being publicly humiliated, were evidently hoping this bone would lodge in Rav Sheshet\u8217's throat. He sensed the danger before placing the meat in his mouth \u8212- apparently suspicious that they might attempt something \u8212- felt the piece carefully, identified it as the dangerous bone, and quietly wrapped it in his handkerchief, saying nothing.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 When the meal ended, the servants accused him of having stolen a silver cup, using the charge as a pretext to search his belongings and expose what was wrapped in his handkerchief. They declared triumphantly: {\i chazi mar d\u8217'lo meichal ka be\u8217'ei ela l\u8217'tzaurin} \u8212- you see, you never intended to eat with us; you only came here to cause trouble.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Disease Detection\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Sheshet responded that he had in fact eaten from the meat, but had detected from its taste that the piece he set aside had the flavor of {\i chivra} \u8212- a diseased, leprous quality. The servants denied this, insisting the animal had no such condition. Rav Sheshet told them: {\i bidku b\u8217'duchtei} \u8212- go examine the specific location on the animal where that piece came from, for Rav Chisda taught that {\i uchma b\u8217'chivra v\u8217'chivra b\u8217'uchma l\u8217'kishuta hu} \u8212- black spots on a white animal or white spots on a black animal indicate a diseased, {\i treif}-like condition. They examined the animal and found that Rav Sheshet was correct. Once again, the servants had been feeding guests from an animal with a problematic condition, and once again Rav Sheshet had caught them.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Pit Trap Escape\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 As Rav Sheshet was departing from the {\i Reish Galusa}\u8217's household, the servants dug a pit in his path and covered it with a mat, hoping he would lie down on it \u8212- as they suggested he do \u8212- and fall in. Rav Chisda, who was walking behind Rav Sheshet and could see, cleared his throat audibly \u8212- {\i nachar lei meachorei} \u8212- as a covert warning, since he apparently could not speak openly without risk.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Sheshet, alerted, turned to a child traveling with him and asked: {\i psuk li psukecha} \u8212- tell me the verse you are studying. The child recited a verse from Shmuel 2: {\i neteh lecha al yeminecha o al semolecha} \u8212- turn to your right or to your left. Rav Sheshet understood the message and asked his attendant what he saw ahead. The attendant said: {\i tsifta deshadya} \u8212- a mat thrown on the ground. {\i Hadar minei} \u8212- go around it, Rav Sheshet instructed, and they did.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Afterward, Rav Chisda asked: how did you know there was danger? Rav Sheshet explained: your throat-clearing alerted me; the verse the child recited fit the situation exactly; and {\i de\u8217'chasidi avdei dela ma\u8217'alu} \u8212- I knew these servants were not to be trusted. Putting all those signs together, the conclusion was obvious.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Demons & Shamir\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Singers vs Demons\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now turns to the world of {\i shedim}, entering through a verse in {\i Kohelet}: {\i asiti li sharim vesharot veta\u8217'anugot benei adam shida veshidot} \u8212- I made for myself male and female singers and all pleasures of humanity, {\i shida veshidot}. The Gemara parses the verse: {\i sharim vesharot} \u8212- male and female singers \u8212- refers to various types of music; {\i ta\u8217'anugot benei adam} \u8212- the pleasures of people \u8212- refers to pools and bathhouses. But what are {\i shida veshidot}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Here the Gemara records a divergence between the Babylonian and Palestinian traditions. In Bavel, {\i hacha targimu shida veshidtin} \u8212- they translated it as male and female demons. In {\i Eretz Yisrael}, {\i amrei shidta} \u8212- the word denotes a kind of palanquin, a carriage. This divergence is not merely lexical; it reflects a broader cultural difference. The world of demons was far more prominent in the Babylonian {\i Gemara} than in the {\i Yerushalmi}, shaped by the surrounding Persian and Zoroastrian culture in which demonic figures played a significant role. The Roman milieu of {\i Eretz Yisrael} posed its own spiritual dangers, but demons were not their primary framework.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Types of Demons\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Yochanan declares: {\i shlosh me\u8217'ot minei shedim hayu be\u8217'shichin} \u8212- there were three hundred different types of {\i shedim} in the place called Shichin. As for {\i shida} itself, Rav Yochanan says he does not fully know what it is \u8212- Rashi offering two readings: either that the nature of the {\i shed} remains uncertain to him, or that {\i shida} is the name of the mother or chief of all the {\i shedim}, and that identity he cannot determine.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Building Challenge\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now explains why {\i Shlomo HaMelech} needed {\i shedim} at all. The verse in connection with the building of the {\i Beit HaMikdash} states that it was constructed with {\i even shlema masa} \u8212- whole, undressed stone \u8212- reflecting the prohibition of {\i lo tanif aleihem barzel} \u8212- you shall not raise iron over them. The rabbis therefore asked: how could the stonework of the Temple be accomplished without metal tools? The answer involves the {\i shamir} \u8212- a worm-like creature possessed of the capacity to split stone, which Moshe had used for the engraving of the names on the stones of the {\i ephod}, where similarly the stone had to remain whole while the letters were inscribed. The {\i shamir} would trace the path along the surface and the stone would crack along that line without being chipped, leaving the stone intact yet marked.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i halakhic} question of whether iron implements were used outside the Temple precincts \u8212- at the quarry \u8212- and prohibited only within them is noted as a discussion among the {\i poskim}, but the Gemara\u8217's {\i aggadic} framework proceeds on the basis that the {\i shamir} was the operative solution.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Finding Shamir\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Amru lei rabbanan} \u8212- the sages advised Shlomo: {\i ika shamira d\u8217'ayti Moshe l\u8217'avnei ephod} \u8212- there is the {\i shamir} that Moshe had for the stones of the {\i ephod}. But where is it now? {\i Heichi ishtakach?} They recommended: take a male and a female {\i shed} \u8212- {\i ayti shida v\u8217'shidtin} \u8212- press them against one another \u8212- {\i kavshinhu ahadadi} \u8212- cause them affliction \u8212- and {\i efshar d\u8217'yadei umaglu lach} \u8212- perhaps they will know and reveal it to you.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Shlomo had ordinary {\i shedim} at his disposal; capturing the king of demons would require a more elaborate stratagem. So he seized male and female {\i shedim} and coerced them. They responded: {\i anan lo yadinan} \u8212- we do not know. But, they said, {\i dilma Asmedai malka deshidi yada} \u8212- perhaps Ashmadai, the king of the {\i shedim}, knows. Shlomo asked: where is he? {\i Isei betur ploni} \u8212- at such and such a mountain. And here is what he does, which you may use to capture him.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ashmadai\u8217's Routine\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Ashmadai digs for himself a pit \u8212- {\i karya lei bera} \u8212- fills it with water \u8212- {\i umali lei maya} \u8212- and covers it with a great rock \u8212- {\i umachsei lei betinra} \u8212- so that no one may tamper with his water supply. He seals it with his signet \u8212- {\i vachasmei begushpanka}. Every day he ascends to the heavenly {\i yeshiva} \u8212- {\i salik l\u8217'rakia v\u8217'gamar mesivta d\u8217'rakia} \u8212- and then descends to study in the earthly {\i yeshiva} as well \u8212- {\i v\u8217'gamar mesivta d\u8217'ara}. This dual engagement reflects the intermediate nature of {\i shedim}: they are partly human and partly divine, as the Gemara in {\i Berachot} notes that they resemble humans in three ways and angels in three. Having studied in both realms, Ashmadai returns, inspects his seal, uncovers the cistern, drinks, reseals, and departs.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Capturing Ashmadai\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Well Scheme\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Shlomo dispatched Benayahu ben Yehoyada, equipping him with a chain bearing the divine name \u8212- {\i shalshelta d\u8217'chakika aleh shem} \u8212- a signet ring also engraved with the divine name, bundles of wool, and skins of wine. Benayahu went to the mountain. He dug a pit below Ashmadai\u8217's cistern \u8212- {\i khara beira mittata\u8217'ei} \u8212- and opened a channel between them so the water from the upper cistern drained into the lower pit. He then stopped up the channel with wool \u8212- {\i vastminu b\u8217'gavvei d\u8217'amra} \u8212- so that the flow would not be visible. He then dug a pit above the cistern \u8212- {\i v\u8217'khara beira m\u8217'eila\u8217'ei} \u8212- and poured wine into it \u8212- {\i v\u8217'shafkhinu l\u8217'chamra} \u8212- so that the wine flowed down into the now-empty cistern. He then covered everything over so that no disturbance would be visible. He climbed a tree and waited.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Wine Temptation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 When Ashmadai returned, he inspected his seal, found it untampered, uncovered the cistern \u8212- and found wine where water had been. He immediately began to recite verses he had learned: {\i leitz hayayin homeh shechar v\u8217'chol shogeh bo lo yechkam} \u8212- wine is a mocker, strong drink is a brawler, and whoever is led astray by it will not be wise. And he cited the verse from Hoshea: {\i znus v\u8217'yayin v\u8217'tirosh yikach lev} \u8212- licentiousness and wine take away the heart. {\i Lo ishtei} \u8212- he would not drink. Yet {\i ki tzachi} \u8212- thirst overcame him, and {\i lo sagya leh} \u8212- he could not withstand it. He drank, became sated, and fell asleep.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is worth noting the irony: just as new wine triggered the condition of {\i kordyakos} in a human being \u8212- diminishing his {\i da\u8217'as} \u8212- here the king of the {\i shedim} succumbs to wine despite his full awareness of its danger. And Ashmadai\u8217's refusal to drink was itself ironic: he was citing verses from {\i Kohelet}, the very book authored by Shlomo, whose name he would soon usurp.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chain Capture\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Benayahu descended from the tree, approached, threw the chain around Ashmadai \u8212- {\i shada beh shalshelta} \u8212- and locked it. When Ashmadai woke and struggled \u8212- {\i hava ka mifarkes} \u8212- Benayahu told him: {\i shma d\u8217'marach alach} \u8212- the name of your Master is upon you. God\u8217's name on the chain held him, and he could not break free.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Journey & Kindness\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Benayahu now led Ashmadai back toward Shlomo. During the journey, as Ashmadai walked alongside or before his captor, he brushed against a palm tree and knocked it down \u8212- demonstrating the enormous physical force he contained even while restrained. He came close to a house and knocked it down as well simply by passing. When he came near the small hut of a widow who came out and implored him, he was moved by her entreaty \u8212- {\i itchanena leh} \u8212- and bent his enormous frame in the opposite direction to avoid harming her dwelling. In doing so he strained and broke a bone. He then cited the verse from {\i Kohelet}: {\i ulashon raka tishbor garem} \u8212- a soft tongue breaks a bone. He had been appeased by her gentle words, and look what it cost him.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Ashmadai\u8217's Nature\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Acts of Kindness\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Benayahu then began to ask Ashmadai about the curious behaviors he had observed along the way. Why, when you saw a blind man going astray on the path, did you set him right? Ashmadai replied: {\i machrezei aleih birki\u8217'a} \u8212- it was announced in heaven that {\i tzaddik gamur hu} \u8212- he was a completely righteous person, and {\i u\u8217'man de\u8217'avid leih nicha nafshia zachi le\u8217'alma de\u8217'ati} \u8212- anyone who did something good for him would merit a share in the world to come. Ashmadai, it seems, was not moved by compassion alone; he was moved by the knowledge, gained through his daily study in the heavenly {\i yeshiva}, of who this person was and what reward awaited those who helped him.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mortality Signs\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Why, then, did you set right the drunk man who was wandering off the path? Here Ashmadai\u8217's answer was the reverse: {\i machrezei aleih birki\u8217'a de\u8217'rasha gamur hu} \u8212- it was announced in heaven that he was a completely wicked person. By doing him a kindness, Ashmadai ensured that he would receive his reward in this world \u8212- {\i ki heichi de\u8217'leikhalu le\u8217'alma} \u8212- so that in the world to come he would face only punishment, with no remaining merit to soften it. This reflects the classical {\i Chazal} understanding of {\i rasha ve\u8217'tov lo} \u8212- the wicked who prospers in this world is receiving his payment here, leaving nothing for the next.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The connection to {\i Kohelet} runs deep here. {\i Kohelet} wrestles with the apparent equivalence of {\i tzaddik} and {\i rasha}, the shared fate of the righteous and the wicked. Ashmadai\u8217's explanations pierce through that surface equivalence: the blind {\i tzaddik} and the wayward {\i rasha} receive the same outward act of assistance, but for entirely opposite reasons, with entirely opposite eschatological consequences.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Why did you weep at the wedding celebration? {\i Ba\u8217'ei mimat gavra be\u8217'go tlatin yomei} \u8212- the bridegroom is going to die within thirty days of the wedding. {\i U\u8217'va\u8217'ya mintar leih yavam katan} \u8212- and the bride will be bound in {\i yibbum} to a young brother-in-law who is still a baby, and she will wait thirteen years until he reaches adulthood to receive {\i chalitza} or {\i yibbum}. This too resonates with {\i Kohelet}\u8217's {\i tov lalechet el beit avel milechet el beit mishteh} \u8212- better to go to the house of mourning than to the house of feasting, for that is the end of all humanity. The wedding celebration conceals a coming tragedy that Ashmadai, with his heavenly knowledge, could already see.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Why did you laugh when the man ordered shoes to last for seven years? {\i Hahu shiva yomei leit leih masanei le\u8217'shava shanin ba\u8217'ei} \u8212- that man will not live seven more days; he is ordering shoes for seven years. Once more the theme: human beings make plans over vast horizons while they stand at the very edge of mortality.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Why did you laugh at the magician performing his divinations? {\i De\u8217'hava yativ avei gaza de\u8217'malka} \u8212- because he was sitting directly above a royal treasure buried in the earth beneath him, and he was attempting through his magic to discover hidden things \u8212- unable to find the treasure that lay immediately below him. {\i Liksum mai de\u8217'ika tuteih} \u8212- he should have tried to divine what was directly underfoot.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 All of Ashmadai\u8217's observations during the journey function collectively as a kind of walking commentary on {\i Kohelet}: the vanity of human plans, the hidden decree of heaven, the tragic arc between celebration and mourning, the irony of the wise man who cannot see what is before him.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Solomon\u8217's Fall\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Testing Power\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Ashmadai was kept in Shlomo\u8217's presence even after the {\i shamir} had been obtained \u8212- perhaps to extract further heavenly knowledge, perhaps simply because Shlomo was reluctant to release him. One day they were sitting together, and Ashmadai posed a challenge. He cited the verse: {\i ke\u8217'to\u8217'afot re\u8217'em lo} \u8212- like the {\i to\u8217'afot} of the {\i re\u8217'em} are to God. The interpretation given is that {\i to\u8217'afot} refers to the ministering angels, and {\i re\u8217'em} refers to the {\i shedim}. So God is praised for his mastery over both angels and demons. Ashmadai then asked: {\i mai ravuteichu minan} \u8212- what makes you so much greater than us? How is it that God is praised for his dominion over you and over us together? You want to see how great I am? Take the chains off me. Give me your signet ring. {\i Ve\u8217'achvei lach ravutai} \u8212- I will show you my greatness.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Casting Away\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Shlomo removed the chains. He handed over the ring. Ashmadai swallowed Shlomo \u8212- or, as some versions read, swallowed the ring \u8212- spread one wing to the heavens and the other to the earth, spanning the full distance between them, and cast Shlomo {\i arba me\u8217'ah parsa} \u8212- four hundred {\i parasangs} away.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kohelet Realized\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 At that moment, Shlomo said the words of {\i Kohelet}: {\i mah yitron l\u8217'adam bechol amalo she\u8217'ya\u8217'amol tachas hashemesh} \u8212- what profit does a person have from all his labor under the sun? He had amassed everything \u8212- music, pleasure, wisdom, dominion over demons and kings \u8212- and a single moment of foolishness had stripped it all away. The verse {\i vezeh hayah chelki mikol amali} \u8212- this was my portion from all my labor \u8212- was interpreted by Rav and Shmuel as referring to what he had left: one opinion says {\i maklo}, his staff alone; the other says {\i gundo}, his cloak. After all his wealth, this was his portion.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Solomon\u8217's Fall (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Beggar King\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Shlomo now wandered from door to door. {\i Kol heicha demati amar} \u8212- wherever he arrived, he would declare: {\i ani Kohelet hayisi melech al Yisrael biYerushalayim} \u8212- I, Kohelet, was once king over Israel in Jerusalem. The word {\i hayisi} \u8212- I used to be \u8212- is itself poignant: his very name had shifted; he was no longer Shlomo but Kohelet, the gatherer, the philosopher, now asking in exile what meaning underlies all human striving. The philosophizing of {\i Kohelet} takes on a new dimension here \u8212- it was born not from detached wisdom but from devastating loss. Only once he had been stripped of everything did the deep questions of the book become truly his own.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Meanwhile, Ashmadai was impersonating him in the palace.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Impersonation Discovered\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sanhedrin Inquiry\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 When Shlomo came before the Sanhedrin, the rabbis took note of something that did not fit the profile of genuine madness. {\i Shoteh bechada milta lo sarich} \u8212- a person who is genuinely deranged does not act sanely in all matters except one. Yet this man was entirely coherent in every regard, and his only apparent aberration was his insistence that he had once been Shlomo HaMelech. The sages recognized this inconsistency as grounds for investigation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Detection Methods\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The rabbis asked Benayahu: {\i kabei lach malka legabei} \u8212- has the king summoned you recently? Benayahu replied that he had not been called to see the king in some time. They then sent word to the queens asking whether the king had visited them. The queens replied that yes, he had come. The rabbis then instructed the queens: {\i bidku bechar\u8217'ei} \u8212- examine his feet. The queens reported back that he keeps his feet covered at all times, wearing slippers in bed. Furthermore, they noted two profoundly disturbing behaviors: he was demanding relations even during {\i niddah}, and he had made advances toward Bat Sheva, his own mother. Something was deeply wrong.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Restoration & Fear\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The real Shlomo was brought back. He was given a signet ring and a chain engraved with the divine name \u8212- the same mechanism that had originally bound Ashmadai. When Shlomo entered, Ashmadai saw him and fled.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Guards & Defense\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Yet even after Ashmadai\u8217's flight, Shlomo was left with an ongoing fear \u8212- {\i havah lei bi\u8217'asusa minei} \u8212- a lasting dread of what Ashmadai could do. This explains the verse in {\i Kohelet}: {\i hinei mitaso shel Shlomo shishim giborim saviv lah migiborei Yisrael kulam achuzei cherev melumadei milchama ish charbo al yerecho mipachad baleilos} \u8212- behold, the bed of Shlomo, sixty warriors surrounding it from among the heroes of Israel, all grasping swords, trained in war, each man with his sword on his thigh because of the fear of the nights. That fear was the lingering shadow of Ashmadai.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Final Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 King or Beggar\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara closes with a dispute between Rav and Shmuel. {\i Chad amar melech vehedyot} \u8212- one holds that Shlomo was first a king and then became a commoner, and never again regained his throne. {\i Vechad amar melech vehedyot umelech} \u8212- the other holds that he was king, then commoner, then king again, meaning the restoration described above represents a genuine return to his former status.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 According to the first opinion, Shlomo spent the rest of his life wandering, declaring to anyone who would listen that he had once been king over Israel \u8212- a {\i Kohelet} indeed, gathering the meaning of everything he had lost, asking {\i mah yitron} with no answer forthcoming. According to the second opinion, the story has a restoration, though one shadowed permanently by fear. Either way, the lens through which the Gemara reads {\i Kohelet} is now fully in view: the book is not merely abstract philosophy, but the utterance of a man who had touched the heights of human achievement and learned from catastrophic personal experience that it could vanish in a moment.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Gittin 69\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Blood/Migraine Remedies\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Blood in Head\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The daf opens in the middle of an extended discussion of various {\i refuot} \u8212- medical treatments and remedies \u8212- that the Gemara presents in connection with a Mishnah dealing with a particular figure. We pick up following a remarkable {\i agadata} concerning Shlomo HaMelech and Ashmedai, and now arrive at the practical medical material beginning near the bottom of 68b.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i L\u8217'dama d\u8217'reisha} \u8212- for blood in the head \u8212- the Gemara prescribes an elaborate herbal preparation. One should bring {\i shorvina}, {\i vina}, fresh myrtle ({\i asa}), olives, poplar, sea willow, and cynodon, and boil them all together. The resulting mixture is then poured in 300 cups over one side of the head and 300 cups over the other side. Should this prove insufficient, one brings a white rose \u8212- specifically one where all the petals grow on a single side, as Rashi explains \u8212- boils it, and pours 60 cups of that mixture over each side of the head.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Migraine Treatment\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 For {\i tzilchata} \u8212- which Rashi describes as a pain in half of the head, and which Koren translates as a migraine \u8212- the treatment is of a different character entirely. One takes a wild rooster and slaughters it with a sharpened silver coin ({\i zuza chivra}) on the side of the head where the pain is located. One must take great care that the blood does not enter the eyes, as Rashi warns it will cause blindness. The carcass is then hung on the doorpost so that one brushes up against it both upon entering and upon leaving the house, and that contact is understood to provide the therapeutic benefit.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Eye Diseases\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Cataracts\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 At the top of 69a, the Gemara turns to eye diseases. For {\i frukita} \u8212- an eye ailment that Rashi identifies and which appears to correspond to something like cataracts \u8212- the treatment involves a scorpion. One takes a seven-colored scorpion, which Rashi understands to mean a very old one, and dries it out in the shade. One then grinds together two portions of {\i kuchla} \u8212- the substance used for eye application \u8212- and one portion of the powdered scorpion. Three applications of this mixture are placed in each eye using an eyebrush. The Gemara adds a clear warning: no more than three applications should be used, because too much will cause the eye to burst.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Night Blindness\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 For {\i shavrire de-leilya} \u8212- night blindness \u8212- the Gemara prescribes an elaborate ritual that combines physical actions with incantation. One brings a rope made of animal hair, ties one end to one\u8217's own leg and the other end to the leg of a dog, and has a child follow behind banging on a pottery shard while reciting that the dog is old and the rooster is foolish. Seven pieces of raw meat are then collected from seven different houses, with the owners of each house placing the meat into the socket of their doorpost. The patient eats these pieces at the refuse heap of the city. Afterward, the rope is untied, and an incantation is recited in which the blindness of so-and-so son of so-and-so is declared to have departed from the patient and to have passed instead into the eyes of the dog. The treatment concludes by blowing into the pupils of the dog\u8217's eyes.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is worth noting that the Gemara here clearly incorporates what it understood as incantation alongside physical remedies \u8212- presumably invoking some force associated with {\i shedim} or related phenomena. The Gemara evidently did not regard all of this as purely medical science in our sense of the term.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Day Blindness\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 For {\i shavrire de-yami} \u8212- day blindness \u8212- one takes seven animal spleens from the innards of animals and roasts them on the pottery vessel used by a bloodletter to collect blood. The patient sits inside the house while another person sits outside. The blind person calls out to the outside person asking for food, and the outside person responds, handing him the meat to eat. After eating, the pottery must be broken, because if it is not, the condition will return.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Nosebleed Remedies\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Nosebleed Treatment\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 For {\i dama de-asi mi-nichiri} \u8212- a nosebleed \u8212- the Gemara offers a graduated series of remedies of increasing complexity. First, a Kohen whose name is Levi should write the word {\i Levi} backwards. If that does not help, any ordinary man should write the phrase {\i ana Papi Shila bar Sumkei} \u8212- \u8220"I am Papi Shila bar Sumkei\u8221" \u8212- backwards. If that too fails, he should write the following words: {\i aton deli bemei chasaf, aton deli bemei pgam} \u8212- formulaic phrases about drawing from a bucket in silver water and in spoiled water.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If none of these inscriptions succeeds, the treatment becomes more physically involved. One takes the root of fodder, a rope from an old bed, a type of paper, saffron, and the red of a palm branch, burns them all together, takes a cluster of wool, spins two threads from it, soaks them in vinegar, rolls them in the ashes of the burned mixture, and places them in the nostrils. This is the first remedy in the sequence that involves actually introducing something into the nose.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Should that also fail, one finds a water conduit flowing from east to west and straddles it \u8212- one foot on each bank. One picks up a piece of clay with the right hand and holds it under the left arm, and simultaneously picks up another piece of clay with the left hand and holds it under the right arm. Two threads of wool are spun, submerged in this clay, and placed in the nostrils.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 And if even that proves ineffective, the patient should sit beneath a drainpipe. Water is poured through the pipe so that it hits him, and he recites: just as this water has stopped flowing, so too may the blood of so-and-so son of so-and-so cease.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mouth Blood Diagnosis\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 For {\i dama de-asi mi-puma} \u8212- blood coming from the mouth \u8212- the Gemara first prescribes a diagnostic test before any treatment. A straw of wheat is introduced into the mouth to examine the blood. If the blood is viscous and sticky ({\i sarich}), it is coming from the {\i reeya} \u8212- the lungs \u8212- and there is a remedy. If it is not sticky and flows freely, it is coming from the {\i kavda} \u8212- the liver \u8212- and there is no remedy.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Treifa Dispute\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This diagnostic conclusion prompts an important exchange. Rav Ami challenges Rav Ashi: you are saying that blood from the lung is more treatable than blood from the liver, but this seems to contradict what we know from the laws of {\i treifa}. Regarding an animal, if the liver is removed entirely and nothing remains, it is a {\i treifa}. But a punctured or partially missing lung also renders the animal a {\i treifa}, and relatively minor damage to the lung is more significant halakhically than partial damage to the liver. So it would seem the lung is the more vulnerable organ \u8212- how can you say that lung bleeding is more treatable?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Ashi responds by distinguishing the cases. The law of {\i treifa} addresses what we find when we open the animal and examine it \u8212- and indeed, if some liver remains, the animal is not a {\i treifa}. But when a person is spitting blood from the mouth, we are concerned that the liver has already dissolved entirely, which is why there is no remedy. So the comparison is not to partial liver damage but to total dissolution. The two cases are not parallel.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Lung Bleeding\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Now the Gemara returns to ask: what is the remedy when the blood is determined to be coming from the lungs? One brings seven handfuls of chopped beet ({\i silka}), seven handfuls of chopped leek ({\i karti}), five handfuls of a grass ({\i frada} according to Rashi), three handfuls of lentils ({\i talpachi}), a handful of cumin ({\i kamuna}), a handful of rope ({\i chavli}), and equal to the entire combined amount, the entrails ({\i kinsha petirta}) of a firstborn animal \u8212- Rashi explains this as the {\i peter rechem}. All of this is cooked together, eaten, and followed by drinking strong beer ({\i shichra charifa}).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Tooth/Gum Care\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Toothache\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 For {\i kucha} \u8212- tooth pain \u8212- Rabba bar bar Chana states: take a single garlic bulb, specifically one in which only a single clove grew, as Rashi explains \u8212- though the practical difficulty of obtaining such a thing is evident. Grind it with olive oil and salt, and apply it to the thumbnail of the side of the body that is experiencing the pain. Then surround the thumbnail with a rim of dough as a barrier. One must be careful that the preparation does not touch the flesh directly, as it can cause boils.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gum Disease\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 For {\i chinchei} \u8212- a problem with the gums \u8212- Rabbi Yochanan prescribes {\i chumsei}, a plant similar to {\i mamru}, and notes that the root of {\i chumsei} is even more effective than {\i mamru} itself. This is placed in the mouth, and it serves to prevent the gum problem from spreading. That is the formula for {\i le-ukmei} \u8212- containing the disease. However, if the goal is {\i le-vshulei} \u8212- to draw the pus together so that it can be drained, as Rashi explains \u8212- one takes bran from the top of a sieve, lentils with the dirt they came from, fenugreek ({\i tavlulita}), and a piece of the hop plant ({\i chumasta de-chashuta}), combines them, and places an amount the size of a nut in the mouth.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gum Blister\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If one then wishes to open the blister that has formed \u8212- {\i liftochi} \u8212- a friend blows a white seed through a straw of wheat, using it as a pea shooter aimed at the blister to cause it to burst. Once it has burst, for the purpose of healing and scabbing over \u8212- {\i le-asokei} \u8212- one takes dirt from the shade beneath an outhouse, kneads it with honey, and eats it, as this is said to be beneficial.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Birsam Disease\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Birsam (Ammonia/Galbanum)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 For {\i birsam} \u8212- an ailment that Rashi describes as coming to the head through the nose \u8212- the primary remedy involves the following preparation: a nut-sized amount of {\i nishtor}, which Jastrow identifies as ammonia and Rashi calls {\i ammonica}; a nut-sized amount of sweet galbanum ({\i chalbinisa dushana}); a spoonful of white honey; and a full measure of a vessel from Mechuza \u8212- which Rashi says held a {\i revi\u8217'is} \u8212- of clear wine. All of these are cooked together. One knows everything is properly cooked when the ammonia is done, since that ingredient takes the longest.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Birsam (Milk Alternative)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If that does not work, one takes a {\i revi\u8217'is} of milk from a white goat and stirs three stalks of cabbage into it, using a twig of {\i maharmin} \u8212- apparently marjoram \u8212- as the stirring implement. Again, the dish is considered fully prepared when the marjoram twig is cooked through.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Birsam (Excrement)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If that alternative also proves ineffective, one takes the excrement of a white dog and mixes it with balm. The Gemara adds a notable warning: as much as possible, one should avoid relying on this remedy, because overuse of it causes a separation of the limbs. The Gemara clarifies that this means the remedy does work and is to be consumed, but one should exhaust every other option first, given the dangerous side effect of excess use.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Internal Ailments\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gira Pain\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 For {\i gira} \u8212- described as a stinging pain \u8212- one takes a {\i gira de-lilasa}, which Rashi explains is a hailstone shaped like an arrow associated with Lilith, some form of demonic being. One turns it so that the point faces downward, pours water over it, and drinks the water. If that does not suffice, one takes water that a dog drank from at night and uses that \u8212- though one must be particularly careful to keep this water covered, since leaving things uncovered is in general problematic, and in this case may be especially dangerous.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Uncovered Water\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If one has already drunk uncovered water \u8212- {\i giluya} \u8212- the remedy is to drink a measure of {\i chamra chaya}, undiluted raw wine. For a blister or pustular sore ({\i mursa}), one drinks a {\i revi\u8217'is} of wine mixed with the plant {\i ahala tulana}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Heart Palpitations\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 For {\i pircha de-liba} \u8212- a fluttering or lightness of the heart \u8212- one takes three barley loaves, soaks them in {\i kimcha}, a yogurt-like substance that is no more than 40 days old, eats them, and then drinks diluted wine.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Heart Remedy Dispute\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Acha of Difti raises an objection to Ravina: surely drinking diluted wine will only increase the fluttering of the heart, since such foods are considered light and would exacerbate rather than ground the condition. Ravina responds that the remedy he had described was actually for {\i yukra de-liba} \u8212- heaviness of the heart, not lightness. For heaviness, one wants precisely these lighter preparations \u8212- the barley loaves, the yogurt, and the diluted wine \u8212- to produce more lightness.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Heart Pain\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 For {\i pircha de-liba} \u8212- the actual lightness \u8212- heavier foods are called for. One takes three wheat loaves rather than barley, soaks them in honey rather than yogurt, eats them, and then drinks {\i chamra chaya}, raw undiluted wine, rather than diluted wine.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Heart Pain\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 For {\i tzircha de-liba} \u8212- pain in the heart \u8212- one takes three egg-worths of mint ({\i ninya}), one egg-worth of cumin, and one egg-worth of sesame, and eats them.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Stomach Ache\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 For {\i kev maya} \u8212- a stomach ache \u8212- one takes 300 {\i pilpali arichta}, long peppers, and each day drinks 100 of them prepared with wine, doing this for three days. The Gemara notes that Ravina of Narsh made this remedy for the daughter of Rav Ashi, using only 150 of the Babylonian variety of pepper \u8212- which were apparently more potent than those of Eretz Yisrael \u8212- and she was healed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Roundworm\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 For {\i kirtza} \u8212- roundworm \u8212- one drinks a {\i revi\u8217'is} of wine in laurel leaves. For {\i kirtza chivra} \u8212- white worm specifically \u8212- one brings {\i bizra galglai}, cress seeds, ties them in a piece of cloth, soaks the bundle in water, and drinks the water. One must take great care not to consume the seed itself, because if one swallows the seed it will puncture the intestine.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Bowel Remedies\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 For {\i maser} \u8212- the need to bind or stop diarrhea \u8212- one eats fresh, wet {\i sisin}, chamomile, in water. For {\i mishra} \u8212- the opposite problem, constipation requiring loosening of the bowels \u8212- one uses dried chamomile soaked in water rather than fresh. The Gemara offers a mnemonic: wet grass is used to dam rivers. Hence, wet chamomile stops flow, and dried chamomile releases it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Spleen Remedies\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Leeches Remedy\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 For {\i techla} \u8212- a disease of the spleen \u8212- the Gemara prescribes a graduated series of treatments, paralleling the structure seen earlier for the nosebleed. One begins by taking seven water leeches ({\i bani de-maya}), drying them out in the shade, and drinking two and a third each day with wine for three days. The Gemara notes in passing that some of the remedies across this entire sugya involve non-kosher materials \u8212- water leeches being one example \u8212- but since these illnesses were understood to be potentially life-threatening, presenting a consideration of {\i sakanas nefashos}, the Gemara records these treatments as valid.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Goat Spleen (Oven)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If that does not work, one takes the spleen ({\i techla}) of a female goat that has never given birth ({\i shakun} as Rashi understands it), places it in the oven to dry out, and stands near it reciting: just as this spleen is drying out, so may the spleen of so-and-so son of so-and-so dry out.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Goat Spleen (Bricks)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If that proves insufficient, one places the goat spleen between the bricks of a new house and recites the same incantation \u8212- the same logic of sympathetic drying applied through a different medium.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Dead Hand Remedy\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If still ineffective, one locates someone who died on Shabbat ({\i shichiv be-Shabbat}), takes that person\u8217's hand ({\i yad shachufa}), places it on the spleen of the sick person, and recites: just as this hand has dried, so may the spleen of so-and-so son of so-and-so dry.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Blacksmith Water\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If that does not work, one takes a fish, roasts it at the house of a blacksmith ({\i beit naphcha}), eats it with the water from the blacksmith\u8217's workshop, and drinks that water as well. The Gemara adduces an empirical proof for the efficacy of blacksmith water: there was a certain goat that regularly drank the runoff water from a blacksmith\u8217's shop, and when it was slaughtered, no spleen was found in it. The blacksmith water had evidently caused the spleen to disappear entirely \u8212- a striking piece of evidence that the Gemara brings to validate this remedy.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Wine Barrel/Health\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If none of the preceding remedies succeeds, one should open a new barrel of wine ({\i chavita dechamra}) for the sake of the sick person and drink liberally from it. Rava bar Rav objects to Rav Ashi that this remedy is impractical: if the patient had an entire barrel of wine available to him, he would not have needed to seek out the other remedies in the first place, since wine was understood as a reliable cure but required a substantial quantity. The Gemara concludes with a more general piece of advice: eating bread in the morning is beneficial for the whole body \u8212- a remark that seems to transcend the specific treatment for the spleen and offers general counsel for maintaining health.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Gittin 70\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Medical Remedies\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hemorrhoids Remedy\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The daf opens with a series of medical remedies, beginning with a treatment for {\i leruchshasa} \u8212- hemorrhoids or a related condition in that area. The prescribed compound involves acacia wood, aloe, mercury (or, as Rashi understands it, raw silver, perhaps quicksilver), a silver dross, a small perfume vial containing an aromatic liquid mixture, and dog excrement. All of these are to be wrapped together in worn-out flax cloths \u8212- in summer using those cloths directly, and in winter or fall using cotton. Presumably the resulting package is applied to the affected area, though the Gemara does not spell this out explicitly. If this treatment proves unavailable or ineffective, the Gemara offers the considerably simpler alternative of drinking diluted beer.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hip Weakness\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 For {\i shiggrona} \u8212- a weakness or illness of the thighs or hips \u8212- the Gemara prescribes a pot full of fish brine. Rashi defines the vessel as one filled with {\i tzir dagim}, fish juice. The treatment involves rubbing it sixty times on one side and sixty times on the other.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Bladder Stones\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 For {\i tzamirta} \u8212- a stone lodged in the bladder \u8212- the remedy calls for three drops of liquefied tar, three drops of leek juice, and three drops of clear wine. For a man, this mixture is applied to his member; for a woman, to the corresponding area. If that does not resolve matters, the next step is to take the ear of a pitcher and suspend it in the appropriate place \u8212- on the man\u8217's member, and for a woman on her breasts. If that still fails, one procures a purple string that was spun by a woman of ill repute, the daughter of a woman of ill repute, and suspends it in the same manner.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Genital Ailments\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If none of the above treatments work, the Gemara prescribes obtaining a louse \u8212- understood to mean one taken from a man and one from a woman, not a male and female louse per se \u8212- and applying it to the genital area. Additionally, when the afflicted person needs to urinate, they should do so onto a dried thorn bush near the doorpost frame, and then observe the stone that passes. The Gemara notes that such a stone, if retrieved, has health benefits for all manner of {\i tzimra}, fever.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 For {\i tzimra barana} \u8212- an external fever, possibly a surface condition \u8212- the treatment involves three sacks of date pits and three sacks of alder leaves, each boiled separately. The person sits between the boiling vessels, then stands over one and bends toward the other, alternating, until sweating begins, at which point one bathes in the liquid. The Gemara adds an important warning: when one drinks from these preparations, drink from the alder-leaf water, not from the date-pit water \u8212- because the date-pit water causes sterility.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 For {\i tzimra gavna}, an internal fever, one takes seven handfuls of beets from seven different garden rows, together with their soil, eats them, and then drinks alder leaves steeped in beer. Alternatively, one may drink or eat grapes from a vine that grew around a palm tree beside water.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Skin Lesions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 For {\i chazizta} \u8212- a skin lesion, something resembling anthrax \u8212- seven kernels of a particularly large type of wheat ({\i arzanita}, according to Rashi) are burned on a new iron hoe, allowing a liquid to emerge from them, which is then rubbed onto the affected area.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Shimi Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara records that Rav Shimi bar Rav Ashi \u8212- the son of Rav Ashi \u8212- applied precisely this wheat-kernel ointment to a non-Jew ({\i nochri}) who suffered from a condition described obliquely as {\i davar acher}, leprosy, and the man was healed. This brief narrative closes out what is evidently a self-contained collection of anonymous remedies, likely originating in the circle of Ravina and Rav Ashi, as is indicated by the Gemara\u8217's own internal evidence: at one point Ravina himself clarifies his own position within the discussion. The style of this collection \u8212- formulaic, anonymous, and structured as \u8220"for this disease, do this\u8221" \u8212- stands in marked contrast to what follows.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Fatal Conditions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Persian Spear Poison\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 With the transition to named {\i amoraim} and a more narrative register, the Gemara shifts to a different category: not cures for recoverable ailments, but palliative care for conditions that are definitively fatal. Shmuel states that a person pierced by a Persian spear \u8212- whose tip was coated with poison \u8212- will not survive. However, in the time remaining, one should give him fatty roasted meat and raw wine; this may extend his life somewhat. The benefit of this extension is explicitly stated: it gives him enough lucidity to instruct his household and put his affairs in order \u8212- to give his last will and testament.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Wasp Ingestion\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Similarly, Ravidi bar Avin teaches that if a person swallows a wasp, he will not live. In the interim, however, he should drink a {\i revi\u8217'it} of {\i shamzag} \u8212- a particularly potent type of vinegar \u8212- which may keep him alive a little longer, long enough to make arrangements for his household. The underlying assumption is that a wasp, which causes severe damage through its sting on the outside of the body, would be catastrophically destructive from within.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Diet & Prevention\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Beef & Moonlight\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now turns from emergency palliative measures to preventative medicine \u8212- identifying behaviors and foods that are dangerous and conditions to be avoided. Rabbi Yehoshua ben Levi teaches: if a person eats beef ({\i basar shor}) together with a turnip ({\i lefet}), and then sleeps outdoors in the moonlight on the fourteenth or fifteenth of the month \u8212- the full moon \u8212- during the summer months ({\i tekufat Tammuz}), he will be seized by {\i achilu}. The Gemara will shortly explain what {\i achilu} is.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Overeating Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara appends a {\i tanna}\u8217's teaching: one who stuffs his stomach with any food whatsoever is likewise subject to {\i achilu}. Rav Pappa adds that this applies even if the overeating is with dates. The Gemara challenges this: is that not obvious, given that the teaching said \u8220"any food\u8221"? The Gemara explains that one might have thought dates are exempt, since the tradition praises them as a superfood \u8212- they cause satiety, they warm the body, they aid digestion, they strengthen, and they do not ruin the palate. One might therefore have supposed that dates, possessing all these remarkable properties, do not carry this risk. Rav Pappa therefore teaches us: even with dates, overstuffing oneself can bring on {\i achilu}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara asks: what is {\i achilu}? Rabbi Elazar explains it as {\i eshet shel atzamot} \u8212- a fire of the bones. Abaye then glosses that with {\i eshet garmi} \u8212- which is simply the Aramaic equivalent, a fire of the bones. The Soncino suggests this may be arthritis, and the logic is not implausible: the heavier one becomes from overeating, the harder the joints must work to support the additional weight, and the worse the arthritic condition becomes.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Achilu Treatment\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara asks: what is the remedy? Abaye reports in the name of his mother \u8212- understood to mean his nursemaid \u8212- a comprehensive principle: all medicinal drinks are typically administered for a set period of three, seven, or twelve days; but the treatment for this {\i achilu} must continue until it is fully healed. Moreover, while all other medicinal drinks are taken on an empty stomach, this one is different.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The treatment is as follows: after eating and drinking a meal, the patient goes to the bathroom, relieves himself, comes out, and washes his hands. He is then given a fistful of lentil powder ({\i shasisa}), some sort of lentil-salt porridge, together with a fistful of aged wine. These are kneaded together and eaten \u8212- on a full stomach, after relieving oneself. The patient then wraps himself in his blanket and lies down to sleep; nobody should wake him until he rises on his own. When he does get up, he must make sure to remove the blanket immediately. If the blanket stays on him even after he wakes, the illness will return.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two-Thirds Full\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara records that Eliyahu advised Rav Nassan: eat a third of your capacity, drink another third, and leave the final third empty \u8212- do not fill yourself completely. The rationale given is that when you become angry, your anger fills your belly; if the stomach is already full, the resulting tension may cause internal damage. The broader principle is one of calorie restriction: it is not merely a question of managing weight, but a more fundamental teaching about longevity. Indeed, the Rambam likewise rules that one should not eat until full. Studies on calorie restriction in animal populations have consistently shown significant extensions of lifespan, and researchers believe the same principle, applied to humans, could dramatically extend the human lifespan.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Stomach Health\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Chiya taught in a {\i baraita}: one who wishes to avoid intestinal illness should make a habit of dipping foods in something \u8212- vinegar, oil, or the like \u8212- both in summer and in winter. At a meal that is particularly pleasurable, one should withdraw one\u8217's hand before eating one\u8217's fill. And one should never delay relieving oneself when the need arises. This last point is also recorded by the Rambam. These teachings from Rabbi Chiya belong to an earlier stratum than the extended anonymous remedies that opened the daf; their character is more that of general health advice than specific cures, and they are correspondingly more transparent and intuitive. The Rambam\u8217's general skepticism about the medical remedies in the Gemara \u8212- he regarded the rabbis as not being physicians, and therefore did not treat their remedies as normative \u8212- makes more sense when applied to the elaborate, often opaque prescriptions from earlier in the daf than to these straightforward principles. Notably, virtually none of this material appears in the {\i Shulchan Aruch}; whatever does survive in normative {\i halacha} was preserved selectively by the Rambam.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Sexual Health\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 White Wine Disease\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Mar Ukva teaches that one who drinks inferior white wine ({\i tilaya chivra}) will be seized by a disease called {\i vitak}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Wine Quality\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Hisda elaborates: there are sixty types of wine in the world. The finest of all is red aromatic wine ({\i sumka richana}); the worst of all is precisely this inferior white wine.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Morning Warmth\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Yehuda adds another cause of {\i vitak}: sitting in the mornings of Nisan beside a fire, rubbing oneself with oil, and then going outside to sit in the sun.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Bloodletting Effect\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i baraita} teaches: one who lets blood and then has intercourse will have children who are {\i vitakin} \u8212- weak, a general sapping of their constitution. The Gemara understands {\i vitak} to describe a condition of pronounced weakness, and sees it as reflecting the state of the parents at the time of conception: after bloodletting, the body\u8217's strength is depleted, and that depletion is somehow transmitted to the child. If both parents had let blood before intercourse, the children will be still weaker, afflicted with {\i ra\u8217'atan}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Eating Mitigation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Pappa qualifies this: the concern applies only when the person did not eat anything after letting blood. If one ate something between the bloodletting and the intercourse, the problem does not arise \u8212- a sensible precaution, corresponding to the contemporary practice of providing food to blood donors immediately after donation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Travel Exhaustion\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava bar Rav Huna adds that if one engages in intercourse immediately upon returning from a long journey \u8212- when the body has been worn down by the physical exertion of travel \u8212- the children will similarly be {\i vitakin}, weakened.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Bathroom Waiting\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i baraita} teaches that one who has just returned from the bathroom should not engage in intercourse until a time equivalent to walking half a {\i mil} has elapsed \u8212- roughly nine minutes or so. The reason given is that the {\i shed} of the bathhouse, the demon associated with that space, accompanies the person for a period of time after leaving. One should not engage in intimacy while that spirit is still attached.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might ask how much of this translates into normative {\i halacha}. The principle {\i chamira sakanta me\u8217'isura} \u8212- that danger is treated more strictly than prohibition \u8212- would suggest these matters deserve serious consideration. However, a glance at the marginal notes confirms that virtually none of this is codified in the {\i Shulchan Aruch}, and the Rambam was highly selective.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Standing Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Another {\i baraita}: one who engages in intercourse while standing will be seized by {\i avit} \u8212- Rashi identifies this as {\i karnafa}, some form of spasms or cramps. If one does so while seated, one will be seized by {\i dulyaria} \u8212- delirium, or possibly the variant reading {\i uleria}. Similarly, if the woman is on top and the man below, the same {\i dulyaria} results. The Gemara notes that there are other discussions elsewhere concerning the effects of different positions on the nature of children conceived; here the concern is specifically for the health of the participants.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Delirium Treatment\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara asks: what is {\i dulyaria}? Rather than defining it, the Gemara provides the remedy: Rabbi Yehoshua ben Levi says the cure for {\i dulyaria} is {\i dardura}. Abaye explains that {\i dardura} is {\i morika dechuchei} \u8212- a type of saffron that grows among thorns. Rav Pappa would chew it and swallow it; Rav Pappi would chew it and spit it out.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sexual Weakness\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye then teaches: one who is not capable ({\i baki bederech eretz}) \u8212- which Rashi understands to mean one who is too weak for intercourse \u8212- should take three {\i kafizim} of {\i kortamei dechuchei}, which Rashi again identifies as a saffron variety, growing from soil that has been fertilized with sheep dung. Tosafot explains that three {\i kafizim} equals three-quarters of a {\i log}. This is ground, boiled in wine, and drunk. Rabbi Yochanan testifies: this is what restored me to the strength of my youth \u8212- and Rashi specifies that this refers to his capacity for sexual intercourse.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Health Principles\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Three Weakeners\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now moves to a series of general health principles structured as numbered lists. Three things weaken a person\u8217's strength: {\i pachad} \u8212- anxiety or fear; {\i derech} \u8212- travel; and {\i avon} \u8212- sin, or, as Tosafot suggests, anxiety over sin. Each is supported by a {\i pasuk}: for anxiety, \u8220"my heart has fluttered, my strength has abandoned me\u8221"; for travel, \u8220"God has sapped my strength on the path\u8221"; for sin, \u8220"my strength has stumbled through my sin.\u8221"\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Three Shattering\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Three things shatter the body: eating while standing, drinking while standing, and engaging in intercourse while standing.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Five Fatal Things\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Five things bring a person closer to death than to life: eating and then immediately standing up, drinking and then immediately standing up, letting blood and then immediately standing up, sleeping and then suddenly getting up, and having intercourse and then immediately standing up. The implication is not necessarily literal death, but that one will feel devastatingly ill.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Six Lethal Acts\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Six acts, performed in sequence, bring about actual death: coming home exhausted from a journey, letting blood, entering a bathhouse, drinking, becoming intoxicated, sleeping on the ground, and then having intercourse. If one performs all six in precisely that order, the outcome is death.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Order Requirement\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yochanan qualifies: it must be in exactly that order. Abaye adds: in that order, one dies; in a different order, one merely becomes seriously weakened. The Gemara then asks whether this is really true and cites a case: a woman named Me\u8217'arat did three of these things to her servant and he died. The Gemara deflects: that servant was already of a weak constitution to begin with.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Eight Moderation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Eight things are harmful in large quantities but beneficial in small amounts: travel; {\i derech eretz} (sexual intercourse); wealth; work; wine; sleep; hot water (both for bathing and drinking, according to Rashi); and bloodletting. One might wonder whether the inclusion of wealth and work is a health statement or a moral-religious one. A little wealth is good \u8212- enough to meet one\u8217's needs and have modest comfort. Too much wealth, however, leads to indulgence and, critically, crowds out {\i Torah} study. Similarly, a certain amount of work is healthy; excessive work is harmful.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Eight Semen\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Eight things diminish semen production: salt, famine, a certain skin disease ({\i netek}), crying, sleeping on the ground, coriander ({\i gadgedaniyot}, which the Gemara identifies with {\i gad ha-sadeh}), and hops ({\i chsus}) consumed out of season. The Gemara adds that bloodletting from the lower part of the body \u8212- below the testicles \u8212- is doubly bad, as bad as any two of the items on this list, while bloodletting from the upper body is doubly beneficial.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i baraita} confirms: just as lower-body bloodletting is doubly harmful, upper-body bloodletting is doubly beneficial. Rav Pappa defines the dividing line as the testicles. Regarding hops specifically: the Gemara teaches that just as hops out of season ({\i shelo bizmanah}) are harmful, hops in their proper season ({\i bizmanah}) are beneficial for the body. Rav Pappa defines the proper season as Tammuz \u8212- summer; the improper season as Teves \u8212- winter; and the months of Nisan and Tishrei \u8212- spring and fall \u8212- as neutral, neither beneficial nor harmful.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Get & Seizure\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishna Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now returns to the {\i mishna}. The case: a husband instructs that a {\i get} be written for his wife, then is seized by {\i kordaikos}, and in that state retracts his instruction, saying not to write it. The {\i mishna} rules: his last words mean nothing. This is a striking ruling. It is one thing to say that the retraction during the seizure carries no weight. It is quite another \u8212- and a significant {\i chiddush} \u8212- to say that we proceed without any concern whatsoever for that retraction, and that the wife will be {\i vadai megureshet}, definitively divorced. The implication is that statements made during a {\i kordaikos} episode are entirely disregarded; the person in that state is not considered a {\i bar daas} whose expressed will has legal significance.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Write Timing\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Reish Lakish holds: the {\i get} is written and delivered immediately \u8212- even while the husband is still in the midst of the {\i kordaikos} episode \u8212- on the basis of his original instruction before the fit. Rabbi Yochanan disagrees: the {\i get} is written only once he recovers. Rabbi Yochanan does not mean that after recovery one must ask him again whether he still wishes to give a {\i get} \u8212- if that were so, the {\i mishna}\u8217's ruling that the retraction counts for nothing would be superfluous. Rather, Rabbi Yochanan holds that the {\i get} can only be executed when the husband is a {\i bar daas}, a person of sound legal mind. His retraction during the episode is null, but the {\i get} itself cannot be written or given while he lacks that status.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The mechanism underlying Rabbi Yochanan\u8217's position is the principle of {\i shelichus}: an agent ({\i shaliach}) acts on behalf of the principal, and the rule is {\i kol milsa de\u8217'ihu lo matzi avid, shaliach lo matzi avid} \u8212- whatever the principal himself could not do, the agent cannot do on his behalf. This refers not to physical incapacity but to {\i halachic} incapacity. The husband, during his {\i kordaikos} episode, lacks the halachic standing to deliver a {\i get} himself; therefore his agent cannot deliver it for him either. Notably, this is different from physical paralysis: one who is physically unable to hand over the {\i get} can still appoint an agent, because physical disability does not affect one\u8217's {\i halachic} capacity. Only a deficit in {\i halachic} capacity \u8212- in {\i da\u8217'at} \u8212- prevents the use of an agent.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sleeping vs Shote\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What is the root of the dispute? Reish Lakish compares the {\i kordaikos} victim to a sleeping person. A person who is asleep is temporarily unconscious and not in control of his {\i da\u8217'at}, but {\i begavra} \u8212- as a legal entity \u8212- he remains a {\i bar daas}. Rabbi Yochanan compares him to a {\i shote}, one who is genuinely of unsound mind and who is not considered a {\i bar daas} as a personal status.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara probes: why does Rabbi Yochanan not simply compare the case to a sleeping person? Because a person who is sleeping is not lacking any further act ({\i lo mechusar ma\u8217'aseh}) \u8212- he will simply wake up naturally, and his current unconsciousness does not change his legal status. The {\i kordaikos} patient, by contrast, is {\i mechusar maysa} \u8212- he lacks a treatment; his condition will not resolve on its own. That dependency on external treatment is what defines his status.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Conversely, why does Reish Lakish not compare the case to a {\i shote}? Because a {\i shote}\u8217's condition is not in our hands to cure ({\i lo sami b\u8217'yadan}). The {\i shote} has no available remedy; his diminished status is effectively permanent or at least beyond our intervention. The {\i kordaikos} patient, however, does have a remedy in our hands ({\i sami b\u8217'yadan}): and here the earlier material pays off. The Gemara has just spent considerable time establishing a treatment for {\i kordaikos} \u8212- fatty roasted meat and diluted wine. The point of all that earlier medical material now becomes clear: we know that this condition is treatable, and therefore it does not define the person\u8217's status as a permanent {\i gavra} who lacks {\i da\u8217'at}. This is a fascinating conceptual question: at what point does a person\u8217's current state of mind define their {\i halachic} status as a legal entity, rather than merely representing a temporary condition that does not affect who they are?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Treatability Test\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then considers a practical implication of Rabbi Yochanan\u8217's position. If the husband gives the instruction to write the {\i get}, is then seized by {\i kordaikos}, and never recovers, the {\i get} can according to Rabbi Yochanan never be delivered \u8212- the wife remains in legal limbo indefinitely. That consequence is precisely correct on Rabbi Yochanan\u8217's view.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara also notes that there is no expiration on the original {\i tzivuy} \u8212- the husband\u8217's instruction. As long as he has not retracted it while he was a {\i bar daas}, the instruction stands. The difficulty arises only at the moment of delivery: if at that moment the husband is not a {\i bar daas} as a status, no delivery can take place on his behalf.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Mortal Wounds\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Slit Throat\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara introduces a {\i baraita} cited by Rav Yehuda in the name of Shmuel: if a person has had both his trachea and esophagus slit \u8212- or the majority of each \u8212- and he indicates (by gesture, since he presumably cannot speak) that a {\i get} should be written for his wife, it may be written and delivered. The clear motivation is to make the wife a {\i grusha} rather than an {\i almanah}, so that she need not worry about {\i yibum}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This prompts a question on Rabbi Yochanan\u8217's position. Just as the {\i kordaikos} patient is {\i mechusar maysa} \u8212- lacking a treatment \u8212- so too is the person with a slit throat: his condition is certainly fatal and will not resolve. Should we not therefore say he lacks the status of a {\i bar daas} and the {\i get} cannot be written? Moreover, a further {\i baraita} teaches that even a person who was witnessed in a dismembered state ({\i meguyod}) or crucified ({\i tzaluv}), and who indicates that a {\i get} be written, may have the {\i get} written and delivered. These appear to be cases where the person is unambiguously dying and \u8212- one might think \u8212- will imminently lose coherent {\i da\u8217'at}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara\u8217's answer is that these cases are distinguishable from {\i kordaikos}. In the case of the dying person \u8212- whether from a slit throat or from crucifixion \u8212- {\i chaziman da\u8217'atei tziluta}: we observe that their mind remains clear. Even if the person is physically incapacitated, wracked by pain, or unable to speak clearly, this is attributed to {\i kechishuta} \u8212- bodily weakness \u8212- rather than to any confusion or loss of mental capacity. The dying person retains {\i da\u8217'at} even as their body fails. The {\i kordaikos} patient is different: {\i da\u8217'atei shagishta} \u8212- his mind is actively confused. He is awake, but his thinking is disordered. It is this confusion of the mind \u8212- not physical weakness \u8212- that removes his status as a {\i bar daas}. Hence the cases of the mortally wounded, the dismembered, and the crucified are all in a different category and do not challenge Rabbi Yochanan\u8217's ruling.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Death Testimony\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This leads to a question on Shmuel\u8217's own position. Rav Yehuda cites Shmuel elsewhere: if a person has had both trachea and esophagus slit and then fled \u8212- presumably on a wagon \u8212- testimony can be given that he is dead and his wife may remarry. Does that not imply that once the throat is slit, the person is {\i halachically} already dead? If so, how can a {\i get} be written on his behalf?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara answers: the person is in the state of {\i chai hu vesofu lamut} \u8212- alive, but certain to die. The testimony about his death does not mean he is dead at this instant; it means that given his condition, witnesses may reliably testify after the fact that he did indeed die, without needing to observe the actual moment of death. As long as he is still visibly alive, he is {\i halachically} alive, and a {\i get} can be written.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 No Exile\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This raises a further question. If the person is definitively going to die, why is the one who accidentally slits his throat exempt from {\i galus} \u8212- exile, the penalty for unintentional killing? If the death is certain, the causal chain from the act to the death seems unbroken, and exile ought to apply.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Contributing Factors\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara cites Rabbi Oshaya: even though death from a slit throat is certain, there may be contributing factors that complicate the causal attribution. Wind entering the wound may have accelerated the death ({\i ruach bilbelato}); or the victim himself, by trembling and convulsing, may have hastened his own end ({\i hu kirev et mitato}). The Gemara asks: what would be a case distinguishing these two possibilities? If the slitting took place in a sealed marble chamber \u8212- where no wind could enter \u8212- then the wind cannot be a contributing factor; only the shaking would be relevant. If it took place outdoors but the victim did not convulse, then the wind might be a factor, but the victim\u8217's own movements cannot.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot raises an important objection: if someone intentionally slits a person\u8217's throat, surely they are liable for murder and execution, regardless of whether the wound got infected or the wind sped up the death. Tosafot resolves this by noting that for {\i galus} \u8212- as opposed to execution \u8212- the halachic framework is significantly narrower. The categories eligible for {\i galus} require a specific configuration: not too close to {\i shogeg} that it borders on accident, not too close to {\i meizid} that it approaches intention. Beyond the specific requirements of proximity to a ladder and similar conditions, the principle can be understood through the concept of {\i misasek}: the classic case of unintentional killing eligible for exile is one where the person did not truly perform a directed {\i ma\u8217'aseh} of killing. He was chopping wood and the ax head flew; that is {\i misasek} \u8212- his act did not make contact with the victim in the directed sense.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 In the case of unintentionally slitting a throat, if we work forward from the perpetrator\u8217's act, then the act itself constitutes a {\i ma\u8217'aseh retzichah} \u8212- a mortal wounding \u8212- and had it been intentional, execution would follow. But {\i galus} is established not from the forward direction but in reverse: we see that someone has died, and we trace responsibility back to this individual, who caused it without intent. Because we are working in reverse, any contributing factor \u8212- even one that logically would have occurred in any case, such as natural convulsing \u8212- suffices to break the direct line of attribution, and the person escapes {\i galus}. This framework elegantly accounts for why unintentional throat-slitting does not result in exile, even though the death is certain: the absence of a clean, unmediated causal chain, once we reason from outcome back to act, means that contributing factors remain relevant.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Gittin 71\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Testing Non-Speakers\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 False Nodding Problem\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The daf opens by continuing a discussion from the preceding amud concerning a husband who has lost the ability to speak \u8212- {\i nishtatek} \u8212- and whether those around him may write a {\i get} on his behalf based on his nodding responses. The {\i mishna} had established that one may pose yes-or-no questions and, if he responds correctly and coherently, his nodded instructions may be followed. But the {\i gemara} immediately raises a difficulty: {\i v\u8217'lechosh d\u8217'lamma shita d\u8217'lav lav naktay} \u8212- perhaps the man has been seized by a fit of head-shaking and is simply nodding or shaking reflexively. If all the questions put to him happen to align with his involuntary movement, his responses prove nothing.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Alternating Questions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Papa\u8217's solution is to ask the questions {\i b\u8217'sirugin} \u8212- alternating between questions that should elicit a \u8220"yes\u8221" and questions that should elicit a \u8220"no.\u8221" Rashi understands this to mean waiting an interval between each question; the approach suggested here, however, is that the questions themselves alternate in the type of answer they require, so that a man simply locked into a repetitive motion would immediately be exposed. But the {\i gemara} presses further: perhaps he is seized by a fit of {\i alternating} \u8212- first nodding, then shaking, then nodding again. The answer is that one should vary the pattern unpredictably: {\i chad lav u\u8217'trei hen, u\u8217'trei lav u\u8217'chad hen} \u8212- one question requiring \u8220"no,\u8221" then two requiring \u8220"yes,\u8221" then two requiring \u8220"no,\u8221" then one requiring \u8220"yes,\u8221" in no fixed sequence, so that no mechanical repetition could accidentally produce the correct results throughout.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Fruit Test Questions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i baraita} from the school of Rabbi Yishmael adds a further method: {\i amrinan leih devarim shel yemos hachama b\u8217'yemos hageshamim v\u8217'shel yemos hageshamim b\u8217'yemos hachama} \u8212- one asks him about things characteristic of summer during the winter, and about things characteristic of winter during the summer. The {\i gemara} questions what precisely this means. If you ask whether he wants a winter coat or a summer coat, that proves nothing, since a person might be cold in summer or warm in winter. Rather, says the {\i gemara}, the test is conducted through fruit \u8212- {\i b\u8217'peiri} \u8212- asking about seasonal produce that is simply unavailable out of season. Tosafot notes that some fruits can be preserved, and therefore suggests the question be posed in terms of going out to the orchards to pick fruit, which clearly cannot be done in the wrong season.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Deaf-Mute Writing\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Writing = Speech\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A statement of Rav Kahana in the name of Rav now introduces one of the most important and far-reaching {\i chiddushim} on the daf: {\i cheresh she\u8217'yachol l\u8217'daber mitoch haksav} \u8212- if a person who is deaf can \u8220"speak\u8221" through writing, then {\i kosvin venosin get le\u8217'ishto} \u8212- a {\i get} may be written and given to his wife.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 To appreciate the full weight of this ruling, one must recall what the term {\i cheresh} means in the {\i gemara}. It refers not simply to a person who cannot hear, but specifically to the deaf-mute \u8212- someone who is both deaf and unable to speak intelligibly. The {\i gemara} operated under the presumption that most people born deaf never acquired intelligible speech, and were therefore classified as lacking {\i bar da\u8217'as} status. This is a categorical {\i halakhic} designation: such a person is considered to lack the intellectual competence required for binding legal transactions at the {\i d\u8217'oraisa} level. He may marry and be married {\i d\u8217'rabbanan}, but a marriage contracted before he became a {\i cheresh} \u8212- when he was a {\i pikeach}, a person of full faculties \u8212- is a full {\i d\u8217'oraisa} marriage, which would ordinarily require a valid {\i get} for dissolution.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Kahana\u8217's ruling addresses precisely this case. If such a person can communicate through writing, he is no longer halakhically a {\i cheresh} at all. One might understand this in one of two ways. The formal reading is that writing constitutes an act of speech \u8212- {\i yachol l\u8217'daber mitoch haksav}, he can \u8220"speak\u8221" through writing \u8212- and therefore he is definitionally not mute. The functional reading is that the designation {\i cheresh} was always a proxy for lacking {\i da\u8217'as}, and that writing demonstrates unambiguously that he possesses {\i da\u8217'as}, rendering the category inapplicable. As will become clear, the distinction between these two framings is not merely theoretical.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Yosef Challenge\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Yosef challenges the novelty of this ruling: {\i tanina} \u8212- we already know this from a {\i braita} concerning a man who became silent ({\i nishtatek}), was asked whether a {\i get} should be written, nodded, was tested three times, and answered yes-and-no questions correctly, whereupon it was ruled that a {\i get} may be written. Is it not obvious that non-verbal communication suffices?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ilem vs.\u160 ?Cheresh\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Zeira responds that the {\i braita} is no precedent at all, because it speaks of an {\i ilem} \u8212- a person who is mute but can hear \u8212- and that case is entirely different. As a {\i braita} establishes: {\i medaber ve\u8217'eino shomea zehu cheresh} \u8212- one who can speak but not hear is a {\i cheresh}; {\i shomea ve\u8217'eino medaber zehu ilem} \u8212- one who can hear but not speak is an {\i ilem}. And {\i ve\u8217'ze ve\u8217'ze harei hen kepikechin lekhol divreihem} \u8212- both the person who is only deaf and the person who is only mute are treated as fully competent, {\i kepikechin}, in all respects. An {\i ilem} is unquestionably a {\i bar da\u8217'as}; the only question regarding him is whether his nodding constitutes a valid instruction. The {\i gemara} notes a scriptural support for the terminology from the verse {\i va\u8217'ani kacheresh lo eshma, ukhe\u8217'ilem lo yiftach piv} \u8212- \u8220"I was like a deaf person who did not hear, and like a mute person who did not open his mouth\u8221" \u8212- showing that the deaf person and the mute are designated by distinct terms in {\i Tanakh} as well.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i chiddush} of Rav Kahana amar Rav, therefore, is not about a {\i bar da\u8217'as} who happens to be unable to speak. It concerns someone the {\i halakha} would categorize as an {\i eino bar da\u8217'as} \u8212- a {\i cheresh} \u8212- who ordinarily cannot engage in binding {\i halakhic} transactions. The ruling is that if this person can write, that recategorizes him entirely. That is a genuinely revolutionary position.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Writing Testimony\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Zeira raises a further question from a different angle. The verse in {\i Vayikra} states {\i im lo yagid venasa avono} \u8212- if a person who was adjured to testify fails to come forward, he bears his sin and must bring a {\i korban shvua}. The verse adds {\i perat le\u8217'ilem she\u8217'eino yachol lehagid} \u8212- excluding a mute person who cannot speak. But according to Rav Kahana, writing is a form of speaking \u8212- {\i yachol lehagid mitoch haksav} \u8212- so why should a mute person who can write be excluded from the laws of testimony and the {\i korban shvua}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye responds: {\i shani eidus derachmana amar mipihem velo mipi ksavam} \u8212- testimony is different, because the Torah specifically requires {\i al pi shenayim eidim}, \u8220"by the mouth of two witnesses,\u8221" and the {\i gemara} derives from this that testimony must come from their mouths and not from their writing. Even if writing constitutes a form of speech for other {\i halakhic} purposes, the specific demand of {\i mipihem} \u8212- from their mouths \u8212- requires actual verbal articulation. One might further note that this principle also underlies the requirement that a {\i beis din} know the seventy languages \u8212- {\i shivim lashon} \u8212- since testimony cannot pass through an interpreter, for {\i mipihem velo mipi hameturgeman}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This exchange actually clarifies the formal nature of Rav Kahana\u8217's position. The {\i gemara} is not merely saying that writing is a reliable indicator of {\i da\u8217'as}; it is making the stronger claim that writing counts as speech {\i halakhically}. This is why the objection from testimony is worth raising at all \u8212- if writing were merely a {\i de facto} test of competence, there would be nothing to ask. But because writing is being declared a formal act of speech, Rav Zeira can legitimately ask whether it should satisfy {\i lehagid}, and Abaye must answer that the particular requirement of {\i mipihem} is more exacting than the general category of speech.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A subsequent {\i braita} cited in the {\i gemara} states: {\i keshem shebodkin oso l\u8217'gittin, kach bodkin oso l\u8217'massa u\u8217'mattanot, ul\u8217'eduyos, ul\u8217'yerushos} \u8212- the same testing procedure used for {\i gittin} applies to business transactions, testimony, and bequests. This seems to reintroduce the problem, since {\i eduyos} is listed. The {\i gemara} resolves: Rav Yosef bar Minyumi in the name of Rav Sheshet explains that the mention of testimony refers to {\i edus isha} \u8212- testimony concerning whether a woman\u8217's husband has died \u8212- where the rabbis were lenient and did not require the full standards of formal testimony. As for bequests ({\i yerushos}), Rabbi Abahu explains that it refers to a case where the person is declaring which of his sons is the firstborn \u8212- again not a case of formal {\i eidus}. And {\i massa u\u8217'mattanot}, business transactions, refers to his own transactions, not to his testifying about someone else\u8217's. So the principle that formal testimony requires verbal speech remains intact.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Braita Contradiction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Braita Rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now introduces a {\i braita} that directly challenges Rav Kahana\u8217's position. The {\i braita} states that when it comes to a {\i cheresh}, the {\i chachamim} did not follow his signaling, his jumping, or his writing \u8212- {\i v\u8217'halchu bo achar remizosav v\u8217'achar k\u8217'fitzosav v\u8217'achar k\u8217'sav yado} \u8212- except for {\i mitaltelin}, transactions involving movable property, where a lower standard of {\i da\u8217'as} has always been accepted. But {\i aval lo l\u8217'gittin} \u8212- not for {\i gittin}. This directly contradicts Rav Kahana\u8217's ruling that a {\i cheresh} who can write may divorce his wife through that writing.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might ask at this point: does this difficulty not arise only regarding a marriage that is {\i d\u8217'oraisa}? After all, the principle {\i keshem shekones bi\u8217'remiza kach motzi bi\u8217'remiza} \u8212- just as he married through signaling, so too he may divorce through signaling \u8212- seems to already allow a {\i cheresh} who married rabbinically to be divorced in the same mode. The {\i gemara} will confirm that this principle applies only to a woman he married as a {\i cheresh}, where the original marriage was {\i d\u8217'rabbanan}. The hard case is a woman he married before becoming a {\i cheresh}, or \u8212- as will emerge \u8212- a {\i yevama} who fell to him from a {\i pikeach} brother, where the relationship carries {\i d\u8217'oraisa} force.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tannaitic Dispute\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} resolves the contradiction: {\i tanna\u8217'ei hi} \u8212- it is a dispute between {\i tannaim}. The {\i braita} that denies writing any power to redefine a {\i cheresh} represents the position of the {\i chachamim}, while Rav Kahana\u8217's ruling follows Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabban Shimon Caveat\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i braita} records: {\i bameh devarim amorim b\u8217'cheresh mei\u8217'ikaro} \u8212- when is it true that writing does not help? When the person was {\i always} a {\i cheresh}. {\i Aval pikeach venischaraish} \u8212- but if he was originally a {\i pikeach} and then became deaf-mute \u8212- {\i hu kosev vehein chosmin} \u8212- he writes and others cosign the {\i get}, and it is valid.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel thus draws a distinction: a person who began life with full faculties and then lost them may be recategorized if he can communicate through writing. A person who was always a {\i cheresh} cannot be recategorized no matter how clearly he writes. The {\i chachamim} disagree and make no such distinction \u8212- for them, writing helps in neither case.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This also raises the question of how a {\i cheresh} who was {\i always} a {\i cheresh} could ever be in a situation where he needs a {\i get} from a {\i d\u8217'oraisa} marriage. If he was always a {\i cheresh}, any marriage he contracted himself was only {\i d\u8217'rabbanan}, and he could dissolve it the same way it was contracted. The answer is {\i yibbum} \u8212- levirate marriage. If his brother, a {\i pikeach}, died, leaving a wife, and this {\i cheresh} then had relations with her, the act of {\i yibbum} itself \u8212- which does not require {\i da\u8217'as} \u8212- would create a {\i d\u8217'oraisa} bond between them. Now he has a {\i d\u8217'oraisa} marriage he cannot dissolve through writing, according to the {\i chachamim}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The implications for the {\i halachic} history of deaf people are significant. This is one of the most consequential {\i sugyot} on the matter, and the {\i gemara} \u8212- through this {\i braita} \u8212- has pushed back hard against Rav Kahana\u8217's liberating position. This deserves to be noted. The concern for {\i agunos} that Rav Kahana\u8217's ruling might have alleviated remains in tension with the outcome of the {\i sugya}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Yibum Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbinic Marriage\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now addresses the question head-on. If a {\i cheresh} was always a {\i cheresh}, then {\i keshem shekone\u8217'sa beremiza kach motzi\u8217'a beremiza} \u8212- the same way he married through signaling, he can divorce through signaling. His marriage was only {\i d\u8217'rabbanan}, so his divorce need only satisfy a {\i d\u8217'rabbanan} standard as well. This is not in dispute.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yibum Obligation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The problematic case is {\i yevamto} \u8212- his {\i yevama}, his sister-in-law who falls to him for {\i yibbum}. If his brother was a {\i pikeach} and was {\i d\u8217'oraisa} married to a woman, and that brother died, then this {\i cheresh} has a {\i d\u8217'oraisa} obligation of {\i yibbum}. The act of {\i yibbum} is effected through sexual relations, and that act does not require {\i da\u8217'as} to be binding at the {\i d\u8217'oraisa} level. Once the act occurs, she is his wife {\i d\u8217'oraisa}, and he cannot divorce her \u8212- not through signaling and not through writing, according to those who reject Rav Kahana.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Wife vs.\u160 ?Sister\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then asks: if his brother was himself a {\i cheresh} and had only a {\i d\u8217'rabbanan} marriage to this woman, then the entire framework remains rabbinic \u8212- her marriage to the brother was {\i d\u8217'rabbanan}, and consequently her status as a {\i yevama} is also only {\i d\u8217'rabbanan}. In that case, {\i keshem shek\u8217'nisata b\u8217'remiza kach yetziata b\u8217'remiza} should apply even to the {\i yibbum} scenario.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} offers an alternative answer: {\i gzeira achiv chereish atu achiv pikeach} \u8212- we make a decree that even the case where the brother was a {\i cheresh} is treated like the case where the brother was a {\i pikeach}, because the two situations \u8212- a {\i yevama} from a {\i cheresh} brother and a {\i yevama} from a {\i pikeach} brother \u8212- might be confused with one another.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But then the {\i gemara} asks: {\i ishto nami} \u8212- if we are concerned about such confusion, why not similarly prohibit divorcing one\u8217's wife when the marriage was {\i d\u8217'rabbanan}, lest it be confused with a {\i d\u8217'oraisa} marriage? The {\i gemara} answers: {\i yevamto b\u8217'yevamto michalfa, ishto b\u8217'yevamto lo michalfa} \u8212- a person can confuse one {\i yibbum} case with another {\i yibbum} case, since both involve the same category of relationship. But no one will confuse a wife with a {\i yevama}, as these are categorically distinct situations in the mind of anyone dealing with them.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two Deaf Siblings\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now challenges the {\i gzeira} from a different direction. A {\i mishna} discusses the case of two brothers who are both {\i charashim}, married to two sisters who may be {\i pikchot}, {\i cherashot}, or one of each. In all these combinations, the marriages are {\i d\u8217'rabbanan}, and consequently the {\i gemara} rules that {\i harei elu peturot min hachalitza umin hayibbum} \u8212- they are entirely exempt from both {\i yibbum} and {\i chalitza}, because we treat the situation as analogous to a case of {\i arayos} in a full {\i d\u8217'oraisa} framework: just as two brothers married to two sisters cannot do {\i yibbum} when everything is {\i d\u8217'oraisa}, so too when everything is operating on the same {\i d\u8217'rabbanan} plane, we apply the same logic. Conversely, {\i v\u8217'im hayu nochriyot yichnesu} \u8212- if the two women are unrelated, {\i yibbum} is performed, and since the {\i yibbum} itself is only {\i d\u8217'rabbanan}, {\i v\u8217'im ratzu lehotzi yotzi\u8217'u} \u8212- the husband may afterward divorce her with a {\i get}, since the marriage is only rabbinic.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What is significant about this {\i mishna} for the present argument is that it treats the {\i cheresh} brother\u8217's case in its own right, without any concern that it will be confused with the {\i pikeach} brother\u8217's case. This directly undermines the {\i gzeira} reasoning. The {\i gemara} acknowledges this: {\i umi gazrinan chereish atu pikeach?} \u8212- are we really going to decree that a {\i cheresh} case be treated like a {\i pikeach} case? The {\i mishna} plainly does not do so.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} therefore concludes: {\i ela michvarta kideshanin me\u8217'ikara} \u8212- the better answer is the original one. The case where the {\i cheresh} who was always a {\i cheresh} cannot divorce his {\i yevama} is specifically the case where his brother was a {\i pikeach}, making the {\i yibbum} a genuine {\i d\u8217'oraisa} bond. Where the brother was also a {\i cheresh}, there is no need for a {\i gzeira} because the two cases are not confused with each other, and divorce remains available.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Recategorization Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rejection Clarified\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Yochanan makes explicit what was already implied: {\i chalukin alav chaveirav d\u8217'Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel} \u8212- the {\i chachamim} disagree with Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel. This is not a case where Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel\u8217's leniency can be taken as a clarification of the majority view. His position stands alone, and the majority reject it entirely.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Can Never Divorce\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye adds that this rejection can be seen from the {\i mishna} itself: {\i nishtatek lo yotzi, nitcharesh\u8230 ? lo yotzi olamit} \u8212- if a man becomes silent he may not divorce his wife, and if he becomes a {\i cheresh} or {\i shoteh} he may {\i never} divorce her. The word {\i olamit} \u8212- forever \u8212- is doing important work here.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Forever = Writing\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Papa explains what he would have said had Rav Yochanan not clarified the matter: he would have read {\i olamit} as meaning that no matter how intelligent the man appears \u8212- {\i af al gav d\u8217'chazina d\u8217'charif} \u8212- as long as he cannot communicate, he can never divorce. But once he can write, he would no longer be classified as a {\i cheresh} and could divorce. In other words, Rav Papa would have read the {\i mishna} as consistent with Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel \u8212- \u8220"{\i never}\u8221" means never {\i as a cheresh}, but writing exits the category. Only Rav Yochanan\u8217's explicit statement that the {\i chachamim} disagree with Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel forecloses this reading. According to the {\i chachamim}, {\i olamit} means that even if he can write, it does not change his status, and he can never divorce her.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shotah Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 An alternative reading of {\i olamit} is offered in the name of Rav Yitzchak: {\i d\u8217'var Torah shota mitgereshes} \u8212- biblically, a {\i shotah} \u8212- a woman who is not of sound mind \u8212- can in principle be divorced. The reasoning is {\i midi d\u8217'hava a\u8217'pikeches ba\u8217'al korcha} \u8212- just as a woman who is fully competent can be divorced against her will, the same applies to a {\i shotah}, since the {\i get} takes effect regardless of her mental state, provided she can retain custody of it ({\i shomeres es gitah}). Why then did the rabbis prohibit divorcing a {\i shotah}? {\i Shelo yinhagu ba minhag hefker} \u8212- so that people should not treat her as ownerless property, exploiting her vulnerability. This concern reflects a sober awareness of the dangers to which cognitively impaired individuals are exposed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Yitzchak\u8217's reading thus proposes that {\i olamit} was said specifically about the {\i man} to emphasize that when {\i he} becomes a {\i cheresh} or {\i shoteh}, the prohibition is {\i d\u8217'oraisa} \u8212- unlike the case where the {\i woman} becomes a {\i shotah}, where the prohibition is only {\i d\u8217'rabbanan}. The word \u8220"forever\u8221" signals the absolute, {\i d\u8217'oraisa} nature of his incapacity to grant a {\i get}, in contrast to her case, where the incapacity is only rabbinic.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Modern Challenge\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Logical Problem\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i sugya} leaves us with a genuine {\i halakhic} challenge that has only grown more acute in modern times. The {\i gemara} \u8212- according to the {\i chachamim} who reject even Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel \u8212- holds that a {\i cheresh} who was born a {\i cheresh} cannot grant a {\i get} even if he can write clearly and intelligently. This is a striking conclusion. We are not dealing with a person whose competence is ambiguous. We are dealing with a person who is demonstrably communicating precisely in writing \u8212- and yet the {\i gemara} still classifies him as a {\i cheresh}, lacking {\i bar da\u8217'as} status at the {\i d\u8217'oraisa} level.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The logical tension is acute. How can a person who writes clearly, reasons clearly, and communicates precisely be described as lacking {\i da\u8217'as}? This is not merely a formal inconvenience \u8212- it has profound human consequences, affecting the person\u8217's ability to give a {\i get}, receive an {\i aliyah}, serve as a {\i shaliach tzibbur}, or perform any {\i mitzvah} at a {\i d\u8217'oraisa} level. The {\i gemara} seems to be applying a rigidly formal category rather than a genuinely cognitive one.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tech Solutions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One interesting technological consideration that arises in this context is text-to-speech software. If a person types text that is then vocalized by a device \u8212- as Stephen Hawking famously did \u8212- one might argue that the result is actual audible speech, not merely writing. This could potentially provide a basis for satisfying even those authorities who require actual {\i mipihem}, verbal articulation, rather than merely writing, at least for purposes other than formal {\i eidus} where the {\i d\u8217'oraisa} requirement of {\i mipihem} applies.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Poskim Redefine\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 In practical {\i halakha}, this {\i sugya} stands at the center of a major debate among contemporary {\i poskim} regarding the status of deaf individuals in the modern era. The development of schools for the deaf \u8212- dating back roughly two and a half centuries \u8212- and above all the emergence of sign language as a fully structured linguistic system with its own grammar, has prompted extensive {\i halakhic} reassessment. Many prominent {\i poskim} have ruled that a {\i cheresh} who can communicate through sign language is no longer classified as a {\i cheresh} for {\i halakhic} purposes. This is both a logically compelling and a practically important ruling.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 At the same time, intellectual honesty requires acknowledging the difficulty this {\i gemara} poses. The {\i gemara} specifically discusses a {\i cheresh} who communicates through {\i writing} \u8212- precise, articulate, unambiguous communication \u8212- and yet still classifies him as a {\i cheresh} at the {\i d\u8217'oraisa} level if he was born that way. Any contemporary ruling in the direction of recategorization must engage with this {\i gemara} directly, whether by distinguishing sign language from writing on formal grounds, or by arguing that changed social circumstances alter the underlying presumptions about cognitive development that originally grounded the category.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Direct Instruction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Direct Instruction Rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} now turns to a different but related problem of {\i gittin}: the requirement that the husband give direct instructions to the {\i sofer} and witnesses. The case presented is: a man is told, \u8220"Should we write a {\i get} for your wife?\u8221" He answers, \u8220"Write it.\u8221" They relay this to a {\i sofer}, who writes it, and to witnesses, who sign it. Even though the {\i get} is written, signed, and delivered to the husband who then gives it to his wife \u8212- {\i harei ha\u8217'get batel} \u8212- the {\i get} is invalid, {\i ad she\u8217'yomar le\u8217'sofer ktov ve\u8217'le\u8217'edim chasomu} \u8212- until he himself directly instructs the {\i sofer} to write and the witnesses to sign.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Invalidity Reason\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The reason, the {\i gemara} states, is {\i taima de\u8217'lo amar tenu} \u8212- his only word was \u8220"write it,\u8221" not \u8220"give it.\u8221" The word {\i ktovu} \u8212- write \u8212- is a more limited instruction; {\i tenu} \u8212- give \u8212- implies broader authorization and somewhat more latitude. The {\i gemara} will explore whether saying {\i tenu} would have made any difference.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Meir Latitude\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} asks who the {\i reisha} \u8212- the beginning of the {\i mishna} \u8212- is consistent with, in light of the implication that the {\i get} might have been valid had a different word been used. The answer: {\i Rebbi Meir, de\u8217'amar mili nimseran le\u8217'shaliach} \u8212- Rabbi Meir, who holds that verbal appointment can be transmitted through an agent. Under Rabbi Meir\u8217's view, if a husband says {\i tenu} to a {\i beis din} \u8212- three people \u8212- those three are empowered to pass on the appointment to others, including instructing a {\i sofer} to write and witnesses to sign. In that case, the {\i get} might be valid even without the husband\u8217's direct instruction to the {\i sofer}. The {\i reisha}\u8217's implication that the defect was specifically the use of {\i ktovu} rather than {\i tenu} resonates with Rabbi Meir\u8217's position.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Yosi Strict\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i seifa} \u8212- the conclusion of the {\i mishna}, which requires that the husband personally instruct the {\i sofer} and witnesses \u8212- sounds like Rabbi Yosi, {\i de\u8217'amar mili lo nimseran le\u8217'shaliach} \u8212- who holds that verbal appointment is categorically non-transferable. According to Rabbi Yosi, there is no formulation \u8212- not {\i tenu}, not {\i beis din}, nothing \u8212- that can substitute for direct verbal instruction from the husband to the {\i sofer} and witnesses.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Verbal Transmission\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Meir Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Meir\u8217's position, {\i mili nimseran le\u8217'shaliach}, holds that words of appointment \u8212- verbal instructions \u8212- can be relayed through an agent. This means that when the husband says {\i tenu} to three people and designates them as a {\i beis din}, those three can themselves appoint a {\i sofer} and instruct witnesses, and the {\i get} produced through that chain is valid. The verbal appointment given to them can be \u8220"carried\u8221" and transmitted.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Yosi Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yosi\u8217's position, {\i mili lo nimseran le\u8217'shaliach}, is the opposite: verbal appointment cannot be relayed. Only the husband himself can instruct the {\i sofer} and witnesses. No intermediary, regardless of the formulation used, can substitute for the husband\u8217's direct word.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Reconciliation Problem\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} struggles to read the {\i mishna} as consistent with a single {\i tanna}. If the {\i reisha} reflects Rabbi Meir\u8217's latitude, it should have said that the {\i get} is invalid specifically because he did not say {\i tenu} \u8212- pointing to the fix. But the {\i seifa} says the {\i get} is invalid until he directly instructs the {\i sofer}, with no mention of {\i tenu} as an alternative. That is pure Rabbi Yosi.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye attempts to read the entire {\i mishna} as Rabbi Meir, arguing that the {\i mishna} is addressing a case where only {\i ktovu} was said, and that {\i tenu} would still allow for indirect instruction. The {\i gemara} objects: if so, the {\i mishna} should have said the fix is to say {\i tenu}, not to directly instruct the {\i sofer}. The {\i gemara} then tries to read the {\i mishna} as Rabbi Meir but in a context of only two people \u8212- since Rabbi Meir\u8217's latitude applies only to a {\i beis din} of three. But the {\i gemara} again objects that in that case the {\i mishna} should have said the fix is to have three people present. The {\i gemara} ultimately prefers reading the {\i seifa} as Rabbi Yosi, leaving the {\i reisha} and {\i seifa} as representing different {\i tannaim}, or attributing the entirety to Rabbi Yosi with a specific reading of the {\i reisha}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Scribe Signature\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Signature Evidence\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i braita} is now cited: {\i ksav sofer ve\u8217'ed kasher} \u8212- if a {\i get} bears the handwriting of the {\i sofer} and one witness, it is valid. Rabbi Yirmiyah adds that the {\i mishna} means specifically {\i chasam sofer} \u8212- the {\i sofer}\u8217's signature, not merely his penmanship throughout the document. The {\i sofer}\u8217's own signature on the {\i get} is sufficient evidence that he was validly appointed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yosi No Fear\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Chisda explains: {\i masnisin mani, Rabbi Yosi hi, de\u8217'amar mili lo mimseran le\u8217'shaliach} \u8212- our {\i mishna} follows Rabbi Yosi, who holds that verbal appointment cannot be transmitted. Under Rabbi Yosi\u8217's framework, one need not fear that the {\i sofer} signed simply because the messengers pressured him or were embarrassed to have hired him and then ask him to sign even though the husband never appointed him. Since Rabbi Yosi requires that the {\i sofer} hear directly from the husband, if the {\i sofer} has signed, we may presume the husband indeed appointed him directly.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Embarrassment Risk\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} raises a concern: {\i mishum kissufa de\u8217'sofer} \u8212- the problem of the scribe\u8217's embarrassment. The scenario is that the husband tells two people: \u8220"Tell the {\i sofer} to write, and tell Reuven and Shimon to sign.\u8221" These people then approach the {\i sofer}, and out of embarrassment \u8212- not wanting to have brought him all this way for nothing, or not wanting to exclude him \u8212- they ask him to sign as well, even though the husband said no such thing. If there is any avenue under Rabbi Yosi for indirect appointment \u8212- even through an explicit {\i omer imru}, telling the agents to tell the {\i sofer} \u8212- this {\i kissufa} risk makes the {\i sofer}\u8217's signature unreliable as evidence of valid appointment.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} therefore argues that the very fact that Rabbi Yirmiyah and Rav Chisda treat the {\i sofer}\u8217's signature as reliable under Rabbi Yosi\u8217's framework demonstrates that Rabbi Yosi recognizes no {\i omer imru} exception at all. There is no pathway under Rabbi Yosi for indirect appointment. The only way the {\i sofer} could legitimately have signed is if he heard directly from the husband, and the {\i kissufa} dynamic simply cannot arise in Rabbi Yosi\u8217's world.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Unresolved\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 At this point, the {\i gemara} has not fully resolved how the {\i reisha} and {\i seifa} of the {\i mishna} can both be read coherently under any single {\i tannaic} position. The {\i reisha}\u8217's implication that {\i ktovu} was the problem \u8212- suggesting {\i tenu} might have worked \u8212- sits uneasily alongside a {\i seifa} that, read according to Rabbi Yosi, leaves no room for any alternative to direct instruction. The question of how to harmonize the two halves of the {\i mishna} is carried forward into the next day\u8217's {\i sugya}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Gittin 72\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Direct Appointment\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishna Overview\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} at the top of 72a states, with particular explicitness, that when a husband instructs witnesses to write a {\i get}, they must carry out that task themselves \u8212- they cannot pass it on to a {\i sofer} and to other witnesses. The {\i mishna} concludes by requiring that the {\i sofer} and the witnesses hear the instruction directly from the husband. He must appoint them personally. This is entirely consistent with the position articulated earlier in the {\i gemara} regarding Rabbi Yosi\u8217's view of {\i mili lo mimseran leshaliach}: verbal agency cannot be transferred. You cannot delegate to a {\i shaliach} the authority to appoint someone else in your place; the appointment must flow directly from the husband.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Three Stages\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is worth distinguishing carefully among the three levels of agency under discussion. The first is the case of {\i mili lo mimseran leshaliach} proper: the husband gives verbal agency to an agent, and that agent takes it upon himself to pass the responsibility to someone else. Here the agent is arrogating authority he was never given. The second case \u8212- {\i omer imru} \u8212- is a middle case: the husband explicitly instructs someone to appoint another party, making that person his {\i shaliach} to perform the appointment itself. The third is direct appointment of the {\i shaliach} in his presence with no intermediary.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Many {\i rishonim} hold that by ordinary {\i shlichus}, one may appoint an agent who is not present \u8212- as, for example, when someone authorizes another to inform a third party of an appointment, without personally appearing before that third party. But by {\i gittin}, an additional and more stringent requirement applies: given that the husband\u8217's {\i da\u8217'as} must be expressed directly in the {\i get}, and given the demand for {\i lishma}, there is a heightened requirement that the appointment be made face-to-face. This applies particularly \u8212- or perhaps exclusively \u8212- to the {\i shlichus} of writing the {\i get}, not merely delivering it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Lo Mibaya\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} addresses an apparent tension within the {\i mishna}: the opening clause seems to leave room for alternative forms of agency that might work (saying {\i tenu} rather than {\i kisvu}, or addressing a {\i beis din}), while the concluding clause states categorically that nothing is valid unless the {\i sofer} and witnesses hear directly from the husband. How can the beginning suggest possibilities while the end forecloses everything?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} offers what it calls {\i ella machvarta} \u8212- the clearest reading \u8212- attributing the {\i reisha} to Rabbi Meir, who allows some flexibility in agency, and the {\i seifa} to Rabbi Yosi. But Rav Ashi goes further, proposing that the entire {\i mishna} reflects Rabbi Yosi\u8217's position throughout. The structure, he explains, is {\i lo mibaya ka\u8217'amar} \u8212- a rhetorical escalation from the obvious case to the extreme. The {\i mishna} begins with a straightforward instance where agency clearly fails: the husband said {\i kisvu} (write) but not {\i tenu} (give), said it to two people rather than three, and did not invoke {\i imru} (the explicit authorization to appoint). That is obviously ineffective. But the {\i mishna} is telling us, through this escalation, that even the less obvious cases also fail: even if he said {\i tenu}, even if he addressed a {\i beis din}, even if he explicitly authorized the agent to appoint another via {\i omer imru} \u8212- none of it suffices. The only valid course is direct appointment of the {\i sofer} and {\i edim} by the husband himself.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This reading by Rav Ashi thus presents a unified {\i mishna} entirely in the voice of Rabbi Yosi.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Rambam} grapples directly with the practical {\i halacha} of {\i omer imru}. He distinguishes between {\i get batel} \u8212- invalid on a {\i d\u8217'oraisa} level \u8212- and {\i get pasul} \u8212- defective on a {\i d\u8217'rabbonan} level. In a case of {\i mili lo mimseran leshaliach}, where two people were told \u8220"write the {\i get}\u8221" and they transferred the task to others, the {\i Rambam} rules it a {\i get batel}. But in the case of {\i omer imru} \u8212- where the husband explicitly instructed them to appoint a {\i sofer} to write and witnesses to sign, and they did so \u8212- the {\i Rambam} rules it a {\i get pasul}, adding that the matter requires much deliberation, as it borders on {\i get batel}. The {\i Ra\u8217'avad} takes a more decisive position: he reads our {\i gemara} in {\i perek Kordaikos} as concluding that the {\i stam mishna} follows Rabbi Yosi, and that {\i omer imru} therefore renders the {\i get batel} outright. The {\i Rambam}\u8217's uncertainty is thus a meaningful point of contention.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yishme\u8217'u Kolo\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} continues with a {\i baraita} reinforcing Rav Ashi\u8217's reading: even if the {\i sofer} wrote {\i lishma} and the witnesses signed {\i lishma}, and the document was transmitted to the husband who gave it to the wife \u8212- it remains a {\i get pasul} \u8212- until {\i yishme\u8217'u kolo}, until they hear his voice directly. The {\i baraita} specifies that he must say to the {\i sofer} \u8220"write\u8221" and to the witnesses \u8220"sign,\u8221" in his own voice, addressed to them personally.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} explains that the inclusion of the word {\i kolo} \u8212- \u8220"his voice\u8221" \u8212- serves to exclude the view of Rav Kahana citing Rav, who held that a {\i cheireish} (deaf-mute) who is capable of writing can be considered to have sufficient communicative capacity to appoint a {\i shaliach} and is not legally classified as a {\i cheireish} for this purpose. The {\i gemara} rejects this: the requirement of {\i kolo} teaches that communicating only through writing does not reassign one\u8217's personal legal status. One who is classified as a {\i cheireish} remains so, and cannot appoint a {\i shaliach} for the {\i get}. This ruling carries significant practical weight and presents a challenge for contemporary approaches that seek to redefine the halakhic status of a {\i cheireish} in light of modern communication capabilities for the deaf.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Non-Verbal Comm\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafos} raises the obvious difficulty: if the {\i baraita} requires {\i kolo} \u8212- literally \u8220"voice\u8221" \u8212- does this not also exclude the case in an earlier {\i mishna} where a mute husband (who is not a {\i cheireish}, simply unable to speak) communicates by nodding his head? The {\i mishna} there validates such signaling. Does this {\i baraita} contradict that ruling?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafos} answers that it is logically evident \u8212- and supported by the authority of the {\i mishna} \u8212- that nodding in that context is equivalent to {\i kol}: it is direct, unambiguous communication. The {\i gemara} does not intend to assign special sanctity to acoustic voice as such. For {\i shlichus} by {\i get}, there is no verse or textual anchor demanding specifically verbal communication the way there is a {\i pasuk} requiring verbal {\i eidus}. Therefore, {\i Tosafos} says, as long as we know with certainty what the husband is communicating and it is being directly expressed rather than inferred or relayed through another party, direct non-verbal communication would suffice for the appointment of the {\i sofer} and witnesses.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The only function of the word {\i kolo} in this context, then, is the narrower exclusion of Rav Kahana\u8217's position: it forecloses the argument that writing by a {\i cheireish} serves to reclassify his personal status. But it does not elevate voice per se above other forms of direct, intentional communication by a {\i bar da\u8217'as}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Conditional Get\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Four Cases\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} now turns to a different dimension of {\i gittin}: the {\i tenai}, or stipulation, in a {\i get} given on the deathbed. The husband, gravely ill, wishes to divorce his wife \u8212- not necessarily because he wishes to end the marriage, but because he fears leaving her in a position where {\i yibbum} becomes obligatory. He wants the {\i get} to take effect only if he dies from this illness. The {\i mishna} presents several formulations, each with distinct legal consequences.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The four cases are: (1) {\i ze gittech im mati} \u8212- \u8220"this is your {\i get} if I die\u8221"; (2) {\i ze gittech mecholi zeh} \u8212- \u8220"this is your {\i get} from this illness\u8221"; (3) {\i ze gittech le\u8217'achar mitah} \u8212- \u8220"this is your {\i get} after I die\u8221"; and (4) {\i mehayom im mati} or {\i me\u8217'achshav im mati} \u8212- \u8220"this is your {\i get} as of today if I die\u8221" or \u8220"as of now if I die.\u8221" The first three, without the word {\i mehayom}, are declared {\i lo amar klum} \u8212- he said nothing of legal effect. The fourth works.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Im/When\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The pivot between valid and invalid turns on the word {\i im} (\u8220"if\u8221") and the effect of {\i mehayom} (\u8220"from today\u8221"). The word {\i im} is ambiguous: it can mean a genuine conditional (\u8220"if I die\u8221") or it can mean \u8220"when I die\u8221" \u8212- that is, a future certainty rather than a condition. Without {\i mehayom}, {\i im mati} is interpreted as \u8220"when I die,\u8221" which renders the {\i get} effective only at the moment of death \u8212- by which point the husband is no longer alive to give it. That is legally incoherent. The addition of {\i mehayom} transforms {\i im} into a genuine conditional: \u8220"as of today, on the condition that I die.\u8221" That is a valid {\i tenai}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mecholi Zeh\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The phrase {\i mecholi zeh} requires separate analysis. The full intended statement is {\i im mati mecholi zeh} \u8212- \u8220"if I die from this illness\u8221" \u8212- the husband qualifying that his death from this particular illness, not death generally at some future time, is the operative condition. The {\i mishna} presents cases where only half that phrase was used, using {\i mecholi zeh} as shorthand for the conditional. Without {\i mehayom}, even {\i mecholi zeh} is read as a temporal designation \u8212- \u8220"after this illness\u8221" \u8212- rather than a true condition.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} further addresses what happens when, after giving the {\i get}, the husband recovers sufficiently to go out in the marketplace, then falls ill again and dies. The {\i gemara} requires an assessment: {\i omdinoso im machmas choli harishon mes} \u8212- we evaluate whether he ultimately died from the original illness. If medical judgment concludes that the relapse was a continuation of the first illness, the {\i get} is valid. If not, it is not.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Abaye\u8217's Answer\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye resolves the apparent contradiction between the opening of the {\i mishna} \u8212- which treats {\i im mati} and {\i le\u8217'achar mitah} as equally ineffective \u8212- and the later clause, which distinguishes between {\i mehayom im mati} (valid) and {\i mehayom ule\u8217'achar mitah} (invalid). If without {\i mehayom} the two phrases mean the same thing, why with {\i mehayom} do they diverge?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye answers that {\i im mati} carries two possible meanings: {\i mashma k\u8217'me\u8217'achshav} (it can be read as \u8220"as of now, conditionally\u8221") and {\i mashma k\u8217'le\u8217'achar mita} (it can be read as \u8220"when I die\u8221"). Without {\i mehayom}, the ambiguity resolves against validity \u8212- we default to the meaning of \u8220"when I die.\u8221" With {\i mehayom}, the phrase is disambiguated and reads as a genuine {\i tenai}. By contrast, {\i le\u8217'achar mitah} is unambiguous \u8212- it explicitly means \u8220"after I die\u8221" \u8212- and therefore even when {\i mehayom} is added, the two halves point in opposite directions, and the {\i get} remains a {\i safek}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Huna\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Huna rules that even in the case where the {\i mishna} says {\i lo amar klum} \u8212- where {\i im mati} without {\i mehayom} is declared entirely without effect \u8212- the wife still requires {\i chalitzah} and may not perform {\i yibbum}. The {\i gemara} challenges this: if the {\i mishna} says {\i lo amar klum}, it is completely invalid, and {\i yibbum} should be permitted. The {\i gemara} responds: {\i lo amar klum d\u8217'asira l\u8217'alma} \u8212- the statement \u8220"said nothing\u8221" means only that the {\i get} is not effective to permit her to remarry; she remains a {\i safek} with respect to {\i yibbum} as well.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} presses further: the {\i mishna}\u8217's later clause explicitly uses {\i choletzes velo mityabemet} to indicate a {\i safek} case. The fact that the {\i reisha} does not use that language implies it is definitively without effect and {\i yibbum} is permitted. Rav Huna therefore cannot be reading the {\i mishna} \u8212- he must be departing from it, ruling instead in accordance with Rabbi Yosi in a different sense, as the {\i gemara} will elaborate.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Date in Document\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Contradiction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This brings the {\i gemara} to a deeper tension. If Rav Huna holds that even {\i im mati} alone generates a {\i safek} rather than being truly worthless, he must be departing from the {\i mishna} and relying on a different halakhic principle. The {\i gemara} identifies this principle as that of Rabbi Yosi \u8212- but a different Rabbi Yosi from the one discussed in the first section: {\i d\u8217'amar zmano shel shtar mochiach alav}, \u8220"the date of a document proves what it intends.\u8221"\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Zmano Shel Shtar\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 In {\i Bava Batra}, the {\i gemara} deals with a parallel case involving a {\i shachiv meira} who gives a gift of property with the formulation {\i le\u8217'achar mitah} \u8212- \u8220"after I die.\u8221" Ordinarily this fails, since a post-mortem gift has no legal mechanism. The Rabbis say the document is void. But Rabbi Yosi says: no \u8212- the document bears a date, and that date is a legally operative statement that the gift takes effect as of today. The document is therefore to be read as {\i mehayom ule\u8217'achar mita} \u8212- the {\i peirot} (fruits) transfer immediately and the {\i guf} (title) after death \u8212- a valid legal structure for deathbed gifts of property. {\i Zmano shel shtar mochiach alav}: the date in the document proves the intent that it take effect as of the date written.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Huna\u8217's position on the {\i get} is that the same logic applies: even if the husband said only {\i im mati} or {\i le\u8217'achar mita} verbally, the date in the {\i get} functions halakhically as a {\i mehayom} statement, generating at minimum a {\i safek} \u8212- perhaps the {\i get} is fully valid.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then recounts the episode of Rabbah bar Avuha\u8217's illness, where Rav Huna asked Rav Nachman to visit him and inquire about the {\i halacha} regarding Rabbi Yosi\u8217's position. Rav Nachman demurred, saying he did not even understand Rabbi Yosi\u8217's reasoning; how could he ask about the {\i halacha}? Rav Huna replied: you ask the {\i halacha}, I will tell you the reason. Rav Nachman went, and Rabbah bar Avuha reported: {\i hachi amar Rav}, Rav himself said \u8212- {\i halacha k\u8217'Rabbi Yosi}. Afterward, Rav Huna explained the reason: {\i d\u8217'kasavar zmano shel shtar mochiach alav} \u8212- the date in the document is itself a halakhic statement of when the document takes effect.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Written vs.\u160 ?Verbal\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If Rav Huna rules like Rabbi Yosi, and Rabbi Yosi holds that the date combined with even a verbal {\i le\u8217'achar mita} is equivalent to {\i mehayom le\u8217'achar mita}, why is Rav Huna\u8217's position expressed as a {\i safek} rather than a definitive ruling?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} identifies the precise point of uncertainty. In the {\i Bava Batra} case, both the date and the conditional clause ({\i im mati} or {\i le\u8217'achar mita}) appear within the written document. They are co-equal textual elements, and their combination yields the reading {\i mehayom ule\u8217'achar mita}. In the case of a {\i get}, the date is in the document but the husband\u8217's verbal statement is external to it. The question is whether Rabbi Yosi\u8217's principle applies even when the {\i tenai} is oral and the {\i mehayom} is derived only from the written date \u8212- or whether the verbal statement operates independently of the written date and is interpreted on its own terms as {\i le\u8217'achar mita}, without modification.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This same question would arise in any {\i shachiv meira} case where the dated document is handed over with a verbal condition: does the date in the written document modify the external verbal statement, or not? This is the doubt Rav Huna could not resolve.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Halacha K\u8217'RY\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} challenges this, arguing that Rav Huna should not have a {\i safek} at all. Rava rules that if one says {\i harei ze gittech im mati} \u8212- \u8220"this is your {\i get} if I die\u8221" \u8212- it is a valid {\i get}; but if one says {\i kishe\u8217'amut} \u8212- \u8220"when I die\u8221" \u8212- or {\i le\u8217'achar mita}, it is not. The {\i gemara} asks: when Rava validates {\i im mati}, what scenario does he mean? If he means {\i mehayom im mati}, that is merely repeating what the {\i mishna} already established. It must therefore mean that Rava validates {\i im mati} even without {\i mehayom} \u8212- ruling like Rabbi Yosi that the written date combines with the verbal {\i im mati} to produce a valid {\i get}. This suggests we rule like Rabbi Yosi even in the case of a verbal condition combined with a written date, which would mean Rav Huna should have no {\i safek}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} deflects this: Rava\u8217's ruling may still be referring to the case where {\i mehayom} was said explicitly \u8212- the {\i mishna} already knew this worked, but Rava was introducing new vocabulary. The {\i mishna} used the phrase {\i im mati}; Rava tells us that {\i she\u8217'ani meis} (\u8220"because I am dying\u8221") is treated like {\i im mati} and works when combined with {\i mehayom}. But {\i kishe\u8217'amut} (\u8220"when I die\u8221") is categorically equivalent to {\i le\u8217'achar mita} and does not work even with {\i mehayom}. Rava\u8217's contribution was halakhic taxonomy \u8212- which phrases count as conditional ({\i im mati}-type) and which count as temporal ({\i le\u8217'achar mita}-type) \u8212- not a ruling on the question of verbal condition versus written date.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Accordingly, the {\i gemara} concludes: for Rava the matter is clear in one direction; for Rav Huna the question of whether we rule like Rabbi Yosi when the {\i tenai} is verbal and the date is only in the document remains genuinely open.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava\u8217's Phrases\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava\u8217's analysis organizes the relevant formulations into two categories. Phrases that function as a genuine conditional \u8212- equivalent to {\i im mati} \u8212- include {\i she\u8217'ani meis} (\u8220"as I am dying,\u8221" reflecting the present precipitous condition). When combined with {\i mehayom}, these produce a valid {\i get}. Phrases that function as a pure temporal designation \u8212- equivalent to {\i le\u8217'achar mita} \u8212- include {\i kishe\u8217'amut} (\u8220"when I die\u8221"), which even when combined with {\i mehayom} does not yield a valid {\i get}, because the two elements point in opposite directions without producing a true {\i tenai}. The {\i gemara} also considers an alternative version of Rav Huna\u8217's position: that his statement about {\i chalitzah} applies not to the {\i reisha} of the {\i mishna} ({\i im mati} alone) but to the {\i seifa} ({\i le\u8217'achar mita} alone). According to Rabbi Yosi, a dated {\i get} with the verbal {\i le\u8217'achar mita} would be equivalent to {\i mehayom ule\u8217'achar mita} \u8212- which is itself a {\i safek} \u8212- and therefore, even on Rabbi Yosi\u8217's view, requires {\i chalitzah}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rabbi vs.\u160 ?RY\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Contradiction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now surfaces what appears to be a further tension. We have established that Rabbi Yosi holds {\i zmano shel shtar mochiach alav} \u8212- the date in the document functions as {\i mehayom}. Rabbi, by contrast, holds that even the explicit formulation {\i mehayom ule\u8217'achar mita} is a valid {\i get}. One might have thought that Rabbi Yosi, given his generous reading of the date in a document, would also embrace Rabbi\u8217's reading of {\i mehayom ule\u8217'achar mita} as a valid {\i tenai}. Why doesn\u8217't he?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two Kulot\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} resolves this by establishing that these are two entirely independent {\i kulot} \u8212- two distinct leniencies \u8212- and each authority holds only his own, not the other\u8217's. Knowing that someone is lenient in one halakhic domain tells us nothing about whether he is lenient in another.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi\u8217's Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi\u8217's position is that the explicit formulation {\i mehayom ule\u8217'achar mita} \u8212- even though it points in two directions and the Rabbis declare it a {\i safek} \u8212- is to be read as a valid {\i tenai}, a fully effective {\i get}. The reasoning is that {\i mehayom} establishes the effective moment and {\i le\u8217'achar mita} specifies the condition. Rabbi chooses to interpret the phrase as a conditional rather than as a contradiction. This is reflected in a {\i baraita}: {\i mehayom ule\u8217'achar mita, get ve\u8217'eino get, divrei chachamim; Rabbi omer k\u8217'zeh get} \u8212- the Sages call it a doubtful {\i get}, but Rabbi says it is a {\i get}. That {\i k\u8217'zeh get} \u8212- \u8220"like this is a {\i get}\u8221" \u8212- is to the exclusion of Rabbi Yosi\u8217's position; Rabbi is articulating his own distinct {\i kula}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kisvu Vetenu\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yosi\u8217's independent {\i kula} operates in a completely different case. The {\i mishna} discusses a husband who says {\i kisvu u\u8217'snu get le\u8217'ishti im lo basi mikan v\u8217'ad yod beis chodesh} \u8212- \u8220"write and give a {\i get} to my wife if I don\u8217't return within twelve months.\u8221" The Rabbis hold that since {\i im} here means \u8220"when,\u8221" and the condition is not met until twelve months have elapsed, the writing too must wait. A {\i get} written during the twelve-month period and given afterward is therefore invalid. Rabbi Yosi disagrees: {\i k\u8217'zeh get} \u8212- he reads the instruction as having an implicit comma. {\i Kisvu} \u8212- write, immediately; {\i u\u8217'snu get le\u8217'ishti im lo basi} \u8212- and give it to my wife when I haven\u8217't returned. The conditional {\i im} applies only to {\i u\u8217'snu} (the giving), not to {\i kisvu} (the writing). Therefore, writing may begin immediately.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} notes that the appearance of {\i k\u8217'zeh get} in both cases \u8212- Rabbi in the {\i mehayom ule\u8217'achar mita} passage and Rabbi Yosi in the {\i kisvu vetenu} passage \u8212- is deliberate: each {\i k\u8217'zeh get} is also a {\i l\u8217'mi\u8217'utei}, an exclusion of the other\u8217's position. Rabbi Yosi says {\i k\u8217'zeh get} to indicate that his {\i kula} applies in the {\i kisvu vetenu} case \u8212- but {\i not} Rabbi\u8217's {\i kula} in the {\i mehayom ule\u8217'achar mita} case. And Rabbi says {\i k\u8217'zeh get} to indicate that his {\i kula} in the {\i mehayom ule\u8217'achar mita} case is valid \u8212- but {\i not} Rabbi Yosi\u8217's principle of {\i zmano shel shtar mochiach alav}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara}\u8217's conclusion is thus nuanced: two independent lenient positions have been developed, one by Rabbi and one by Rabbi Yosi, each operative in its own halakhic domain. Neither authority endorses the other\u8217's {\i kula}, and the two positions are not additive. What emerges from our {\i mishna}, when viewed through this analysis, is a careful map of where each leniency applies and where the standard ruling \u8212- that a {\i get} taking effect only after death is no {\i get} \u8212- continues to govern.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Gittin 73\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rav Huna\u8217's Get\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Explicit Condition\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The daf opens in the middle of a discussion about a husband who gives his wife a {\i get} on the condition that he will die from his current illness. The central question is how this arrangement is framed: if the language implies that the {\i get} takes effect {\i after} death, it is invalid as a {\i get le\u8217'achar mita}; but if it is framed as a true {\i tnai} \u8212- a condition \u8212- it may be perfectly valid. We pick up on 73b, approximately ten lines from the bottom, beginning with the case of {\i zehu gittech mehayom im mati mecholi zeh} \u8212- \u8220"This is your {\i get} from today if I die from this illness.\u8221" Here the husband has formulated a complete and explicit {\i tnai}: the {\i get} takes effect today, contingent upon his dying from the present illness. The question that will occupy the {\i Gemara} is when, precisely, that condition is considered met or definitively not met.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} addresses the case where the husband gets up and walks about in the marketplace \u8212- {\i amad vehalach bashuk} \u8212- but then relapses and dies. The {\i mishna} does not automatically say the condition has been nullified merely because he managed to walk around. Instead, it calls for an {\i omdana}, a medical-style assessment: if it is determined that his death resulted from the original illness and that illness had never truly left him, the {\i get} is valid; if the relapse represents an entirely new illness, it is not.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Get vs.\u160 ?Gift\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 At this point the {\i Gemara} introduces a statement of Rav Huna: {\i gito ke-matnaso} \u8212- the {\i get} of a {\i shechiv mera} (a person on his deathbed) is analogous to the gift of a {\i shechiv mera}, a {\i matnas shchiv mera}. Just as a {\i matnas shchiv mera} \u8212- property distributed verbally on the deathbed \u8212- is automatically revoked if the person recovers ({\i im amad chozer}), so too the {\i get} of a {\i shechiv mera} is revoked if he recovers. Rav Huna then extends the comparison in a second direction: just as the rule that writing a {\i get} ({\i kisvu}) in a context of mortal danger is interpreted to mean {\i kisvu u-tenu} \u8212- \u8220"write and give\u8221" \u8212- so too a verbal gift in that same context, even without a formal {\i kinyan}, is legally binding.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The second part of Rav Huna\u8217's statement \u8212- the {\i matnas shchiv mera} point \u8212- requires clarification. It is well known that a {\i matnas shchiv mera} does not require a {\i kinyan}; that is the entire premise of the institution. What, then, is Rav Huna\u8217's {\i chiddush}? Tosafot explains that Rav Huna is not simply restating the rule of {\i matnas shchiv mera}. Rather, he is extending it: the same contextual circumstances that lead us to interpret {\i kisvu} as {\i kisvu u-tenu} \u8212- whether one is on his deathbed, being led out to execution, or embarking on a distant journey \u8212- those same circumstances should qualify as the context for {\i matnas shchiv mera}. So if someone being taken out to be executed says \u8220"give my property to so-and-so,\u8221" one might object that this person is not technically a {\i shchiv mera}. Rav Huna teaches that the operative principle is not the precise category of {\i shchiv mera} but the set of circumstances in which we read an implicit urgency of death into the person\u8217's words, and those circumstances equally validate a verbal gift as a {\i matnas shchiv mera}. That is the {\i chiddush} of the second part of Rav Huna\u8217's statement, important to clarify even though our primary focus here is the first part.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rashi vs.\u160 ?Tosafot\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The first part of Rav Huna\u8217's statement \u8212- {\i gito ke-matnaso, im amad chozer} \u8212- is the subject of a significant dispute between Rashi and Tosafot, and this dispute will color everything that follows in the {\i Gemara}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rashi\u8217's View\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rashi reads Rav Huna\u8217's statement as introducing a sweeping {\i chiddush}: just as a {\i shchiv mera} who distributes property implicitly means \u8220"in case I die,\u8221" so too a {\i shchiv mera} who gives a {\i get} without articulating any condition at all has nonetheless made an implicit {\i tnai} \u8212- it is as if he said {\i im yamut mecholi zeh}, \u8220"if I die from this illness.\u8221" The explicit conditions laid out in the {\i mishna}, on Rashi\u8217's reading, only come into play when the husband actually bothers to articulate something \u8212- and that is precisely when he can trip himself up by framing it incorrectly. But if he says nothing, the condition is implicit in the situation itself, and the {\i get} is treated as conditional upon his death. This, according to Rashi, is not a rabbinic {\i chiddush} in the technical sense \u8212- it is simply the husband\u8217's implicit {\i da\u8217'as}, assumed from the circumstances.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafot\u8217's View\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot disagrees. There is no implicit {\i tnai}, and an explicit condition is always required. On Tosafot\u8217's reading, all Rav Huna is teaching is what constitutes a failure to satisfy the condition {\i mecholi zeh} once it has been explicitly made. Specifically, Rav Huna is saying: the moment the husband is able to get up, it is no longer {\i mecholi zeh}, regardless of what happens afterward. This, as Tosafot notes, stands in direct tension with the {\i mishna}, which says that even after the husband gets up and walks around, a relapse and death may still be {\i mecholi zeh} depending on an assessment. The {\i Gemara} will press this tension directly.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Recovery & Relapse\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishna Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} states clearly: if the husband got up, went out to the marketplace, fell ill again, and died, we conduct an assessment \u8212- {\i omdin oso} \u8212- and if we determine the death resulted from the original illness ({\i im machmas choli harishon meis harei zeh get}), it is a valid {\i get}; otherwise, it is not.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Huna Challenge\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This {\i mishna} seems to contradict Rav Huna, at least on Tosafot\u8217's reading of his position. According to Rav Huna, once the husband gets up \u8212- {\i amad} \u8212- it is definitively no longer {\i mecholi zeh}. Why, then, does the {\i mishna} entertain the possibility of an {\i omdana}? If {\i amad} constitutes a clean break, there would be nothing to assess.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mecholi L\u8217'Choli\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} resolves this through the answer of Mar son of Rav Yosef in the name of Rava: {\i shenitak mecholi l\u8217'choli} \u8212- the {\i mishna} is speaking of a case where the husband transitioned from one illness to another without achieving a full recovery. He did not walk around in perfect health; he was still contending with some underlying ailment \u8212- perhaps pneumonia gave way to an infection \u8212- and in that state he made his way outside. In that continuous chain of illness, it remains possible to argue that his ultimate death was still {\i machmas choli harishon}, still attributable to the original illness. Rav Huna\u8217's ruling, by contrast, applies when there was a genuine period of complete freedom from illness \u8212- a true {\i amad} \u8212- at which point anything that follows is, by definition, something new.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Amad Definition\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} presses further. The {\i mishna} says he {\i amad vehalach bashuk} \u8212- he stood up and walked in the marketplace \u8212- which sounds like complete recovery. How can we reinterpret {\i amad} to mean he was still sick? The {\i Gemara} responds: {\i halach al mishanto} \u8212- he walked with a cane. He was not in full health; he was ambulatory but still dependent on support.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishna Chiddush\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This raises an obvious question: if the {\i mishna} is describing someone who was plainly still unwell, walking on a cane from one illness to another, what is its {\i chiddush}? One might have assumed that such a person is obviously still in the grip of the original illness, and his subsequent relapse and death would clearly be {\i mecholi zeh} without any need for assessment. The {\i Gemara} answers: {\i de\u8217'halach al mishanto hu de\u8217'ba\u8217'inan omdana} \u8212- precisely because he was walking around on his cane, because there had been some visible improvement, we cannot simply assume the subsequent death was from the original illness; we must assess. The {\i chiddush} is that even a partial, cane-assisted recovery introduces enough ambiguity to require an {\i omdana}. If he had never even reached that level of improvement \u8212- never managed to walk even with support \u8212- no assessment would be needed; it would unambiguously be {\i mecholi zeh}. Conversely, had he achieved full recovery for a period, no {\i omdana} would help: it would definitively not be {\i mecholi zeh} according to Rav Huna.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} then draws an inference from this discussion regarding {\i matnas shchiv mera}: a {\i shchiv mera} who transitioned {\i mecholi l\u8217'choli} without a complete break \u8212- his gifts remain valid, because the entire chain of illness is considered one continuous state. This principle is confirmed by a tradition cited in the name of Rabbi Elazar from Rav: {\i shchiv mera shenitak mecholi l\u8217'choli matnato matana}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Implicit Condition Conce\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava & Rabba\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having worked through the {\i amad} dimension of Rav Huna\u8217's statement, the {\i Gemara} now confronts the second dimension \u8212- Rashi\u8217's reading of the {\i chiddush}, namely that the {\i tnai} can be implicit. Rabba and Rava reject Rav Huna\u8217's position entirely: {\i lo sevira lehu ha de-Rav Huna}. They do not accept that getting up constitutes an automatic break, nor \u8212- more fundamentally on Rashi\u8217's reading \u8212- do they accept that there can be an implicit {\i tnai} in the giving of a {\i get}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Get After Death Fear\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} provides a reason beyond a simple factual disagreement: {\i gezeira shema yomru yesh get le\u8217'achar mita} \u8212- we fear that people will conclude that a {\i get} can take effect after death. This requires unpacking, because it is not immediately obvious how the implicit-{\i tnai} mechanism would produce that misunderstanding.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rashi Explanation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rashi\u8217's explanation is illuminating precisely because his reading of Rav Huna is what makes the concern intelligible. Recall that on Rashi\u8217's reading, Rav Huna held that when a husband gives a {\i get} on his deathbed without stating any condition, it is treated as if he made a condition that it takes effect only if he dies. Now consider what an outside observer sees: in scenario one, the husband gives the {\i get}, dies, and the {\i get} is valid. In scenario two, the husband gives the {\i get}, recovers, and the {\i get} is not valid. Since no explicit {\i tnai} was ever articulated, the observer has no reason to attribute the difference to a conditional structure. What the observer is likely to conclude instead is that the husband\u8217's intention was for the {\i get} to take effect {\i after} his death \u8212- which would make it a {\i get le\u8217'achar mita}, invalid for a different and more fundamental reason. People will say: \u8220"Ah, so a {\i get} given by a {\i shechiv mera} takes effect upon death.\u8221" That is the concern: the implicit {\i tnai} framework, precisely because it is invisible, will be consistently misread as a timing mechanism rather than a conditional one.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Can Rabbis Invalidate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This leads to a striking challenge from the {\i Gemara} itself. If it is true that {\i de\u8217'oraita} there is an implicit {\i tnai} here \u8212- if the husband\u8217's real {\i da\u8217'as} really is \u8220"I mean this only if I die\u8221" \u8212- then how can we simply override that {\i tnai} because of a rabbinic concern about public misunderstanding? The scenario is this: the husband gives a {\i get} with an implicit {\i tnai}; he recovers; {\i de\u8217'oraita}, she is still married to him. But Rabba and Rava, concerned about how it will look, want to say we ignore the implicit {\i tnai} and treat the {\i get} as unconditional. That would mean declaring a woman divorced \u8212- {\i mesharei eshet ish l\u8217'alma} \u8212- when {\i de\u8217'oraita} she is still married. How can a rabbinic {\i gezeira} accomplish that?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kiddushin Principle\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} answers with the standard principle: {\i kol de-mekadesh ada\u8217'ata de-rabbanan mekadesh, ve-afkinu rabbanan le-kiddushin minei} \u8212- whoever betroths a woman does so with the implicit understanding and agreement of the Rabbis, and therefore the Rabbis have the power to annul the marriage retroactively. The reasoning is that since the marriage itself was conducted within the rabbinic framework, the Rabbis retain the authority to declare the marriage nullified when circumstances warrant.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ravina vs.\u160 ?Ashi\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Ravina raises a qualification to Ravina: this solution works if the {\i kiddushin} was accomplished through money \u8212- {\i kiddushei kessef} \u8212- because then the Rabbis can invoke {\i hefker beis din hefker} and retroactively declare the money was never legally his, thereby annulling the transaction. But what if the {\i kiddushin} was accomplished through an act of {\i bi\u8217'ah} \u8212- sexual relations? In that case, how can the Rabbis annul the marriage? Rav Ashi responds: {\i deshavyei le-vi\u8217'ato bi\u8217'at zenut} \u8212- the Rabbis have the power to redefine the character of the act. They declare that his act of {\i bi\u8217'ah} was not a {\i ma\u8217'aseh kiddushin} but simply an act outside of marriage, {\i bi\u8217'at zenut}, and thus no marriage was created in the first place.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Ramban, notably, suggests that this is not meant as a description of what actually happens in practice. The Ramban\u8217's position is that {\i afkinu rabbanan le-kiddushin minei} is really a coercive threat: by declaring that the Rabbis {\i could} retroactively designate the man\u8217's entire marital life as {\i bi\u8217'at zenut}, the implicit pressure is brought to bear on the husband to conform his {\i da\u8217'as} to the result that the Rabbis require. In other words, since no rational person would want his years of marital life reclassified as prostitution, we can effectively say: he really did not want the implicit {\i tnai} to govern here; deep down he accepts the Rabbis\u8217' framework. This is, on the Ramban\u8217's reading, a more principled and less radical use of {\i hafka\u8217'as kiddushin} \u8212- what we are really doing is redefining the husband\u8217's {\i da\u8217'as}, not annihilating his marriage from thin air. Rabba and Rava\u8217's position, then, is coherent: we simply refuse to recognize the implicit {\i tnai}, we treat the {\i get} as an unconditional {\i get}, and because the Rabbis have the theoretical power to annul {\i kiddushin} in cases where a husband\u8217's conduct creates communal harm, that refusal is halachically legitimate.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Ones Contradiction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 House Collapse Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now turns to a {\i braita} that introduces a new dimension of the problem. The case: the husband says {\i zeh gittech mehayom im mati mecholi zeh} \u8212- \u8220"this is your {\i get} from today if I die from this illness\u8221" \u8212- and then a house collapses on him ({\i venafal habayit alav}), or a snake bites him ({\i o hichisho nachash}). The {\i braita} rules: {\i eino get} \u8212- it is not a valid {\i get}, because he stipulated {\i mecholi zeh} and he did not die from the illness; the cause of death was something external.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Recover Condition\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i braita} then presents a second case: the husband says {\i im lo amod mecholi zeh} \u8212- \u8220"if I do not recover from this illness\u8221" \u8212- and again a house collapses on him or a snake bites him. Here the {\i braita} rules: {\i harei zeh get} \u8212- it is a valid {\i get}, because he never recovered from the illness.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Cases Comparison\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} is struck by this and asks: {\i mai shena reisha u-mai shena seifa} \u8212- what is the difference between the two cases? To us, reading the {\i braita} simply, the distinction seems clear: in the first case he said \u8220"if I die from this illness,\u8221" and since he died from something else, the condition was not met; in the second case he said \u8220"if I do not recover,\u8221" and indeed he did not recover, so the condition was met. Simple enough.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 During Illness Reading\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But the {\i Gemara} does not read the {\i braita} that way. Rather than understanding {\i mecholi zeh} as \u8220"as a result of this illness,\u8221" the {\i Gemara} reads both cases as saying \u8220"during this illness\u8221" \u8212- {\i mitoch choli zeh}. Under that reading, in both cases the husband died during the illness; in both cases the condition, so understood, has been technically met. The contradiction then becomes real: in the first case the {\i braita} says it is not a valid {\i get} even though he died during the illness, and in the second case it says it is valid even though the cause of death was external. The {\i Gemara} therefore sees the {\i braita} as internally contradictory, and the contradiction maps onto the question of {\i yesh ones b\u8217'gittin o ein ones b\u8217'gittin} \u8212- does a claim of {\i ones} (circumstances beyond one\u8217's control, acts of God) affect whether a condition in a {\i get} is treated as satisfied?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i ones} framework in this context works as follows: a husband says \u8220"this {\i get} takes effect if I do not return within twelve months,\u8221" and then on the last day he is running to return but a flooded river blocks his path. Technically the condition was met \u8212- he did not return. But {\i ones} is the argument that these were not the circumstances he had in mind; he intended a voluntary failure to return, not an involuntary one. If we accept {\i ones} claims in {\i gittin}, the condition is not met and the {\i get} is invalid despite having been technically satisfied.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 In our {\i braita}, the first case \u8212- where the {\i braita} says {\i eino get} \u8212- implies that we accept the {\i ones} claim: the house collapsed, that was an act of God, and despite the technical satisfaction of the condition we say it was not the kind of death or non-recovery the husband had in mind. The second case \u8212- where the {\i braita} says {\i harei zeh get} \u8212- implies that we reject the {\i ones} claim: he didn\u8217't recover, and it doesn\u8217't matter why he didn\u8217't recover.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Acts of God Resolution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Shalchu mitam} \u8212- they sent from Eretz Yisrael: {\i achlu ari ein lanu} \u8212- \u8220"we do not entertain the case of being eaten by a lion.\u8221" The response from Eretz Yisrael is that extraordinary acts of God \u8212- being devoured by a lion, having a house collapse, being bitten by a snake \u8212- do not satisfy the condition in the husband\u8217's formulation. Even if technically the condition {\i mecholi zeh} (read as \u8220"during this illness\u8221") was met, the husband never intended such cataclysmic, unprecedented circumstances. The {\i braita}\u8217's second case ({\i im lo amod}) is therefore one where the {\i Gemara} of Eretz Yisrael presumably distinguishes between ordinary non-recovery and extraordinary acts of God.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Bavli}, however, does not find this resolution satisfying. The {\i braita} remains a genuine contradiction between {\i reisha} and {\i seifa}. Tosafot notes the difficulty of reconciling this {\i braita} with the earlier {\i Gemara} discussion of {\i ones}, and the {\i Bavli}\u8217's conclusion is essentially that the {\i braita} is unresolved: one clause assumes {\i yesh ones b\u8217'gittin} and the other assumes {\i ein ones b\u8217'gittin}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Field Flooding Story\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Full Responsibility\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now moves to a legal narrative that illuminates the {\i ones} question in the domain of monetary law. Rav Papa and Rav Huna son of Rav Yehoshua purchased a large quantity of sesame seeds \u8212- a commercial transaction, not a household matter \u8212- by the bank of the Nahar Malka. They hired shippers to transport the goods and the shippers accepted full responsibility for any circumstance that might arise: {\i kibbelu aleihu kol onsa de-misyalad}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 River Redirection\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What happened? The Nahar Malka was dammed \u8212- its course was altered \u8212- and as a result the goods could not be delivered. Rav Papa and Rav Huna demanded that the shippers rent donkeys and transport the sesame seeds overland, arguing: you accepted responsibility for {\i kol onsa}, and this is exactly the kind of circumstance where you must make good.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 In an earlier parallel case, someone sold a field and accepted responsibility for any mishap ({\i kol onsa de-misyalid}); subsequently, the course of a river was redirected and the field was flooded. The case came before Ravina, who ruled that the seller must compensate the buyer: {\i ha kableit alach kol onsa de-misyalid} \u8212- you said anything, go pay. The case then came before Rav Acha bar Tachlifa, who challenged Ravina: this is an {\i onsa de-lo shchiach} \u8212- a completely uncommon act of God; surely such an extraordinary event was never contemplated within the scope of \u8220"anything.\u8221"\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Acha Ruling\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Acha bar Tachlifa\u8217's position is that an {\i ones delo shchiach} \u8212- an act of God so unusual and unprecedented that it falls entirely outside normal expectation \u8212- does not fall within the scope of a general acceptance of {\i kol onsa}. Even when a person says \u8220"I accept responsibility for anything,\u8221" there is an implicit boundary: we are talking about the kinds of things that normally go wrong, not unprecedented redirections of major rivers.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The case of the sesame seeds and the shippers came before Rava, who sided with the shippers: {\i onsa de-lo shchiach hu} \u8212- this was an extraordinary act of God, and they are not liable. Rava\u8217's ruling establishes that {\i ones delo shchiach} is categorically different from ordinary {\i ones}. Even a blanket acceptance of {\i kol onsa} does not encompass the truly unforeseeable.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava\u8217's Criticism\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What makes this passage particularly striking is Rava\u8217's language when he rendered his ruling. He said to those who had ruled against the shippers: {\i kakei chivrei, meshatchei glimei de-inshei} \u8212- \u8220"white ducks who rip off people\u8217's shirts.\u8221" As Rashi explains: {\i mafshitim bigdei anashim, klomar porskin aleihem din anas} \u8212- they are stripping people\u8217's garments, meaning they are issuing rulings that line their own pockets (or the pockets of those they favor) under the guise of halacha. Rava\u8217's rebuke is sharp: these rabbis are exploiting their authority to rule against honest businessmen on the basis of a contract clause that no rational person would have understood to include the unprecedented damming of a major river.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The broader principle that emerges is that {\i ones delo shchiach} does not create legal liability, and this holds even in the face of explicit contractual language accepting general responsibility. To be held responsible for an {\i ones delo shchiach}, one would need to say so explicitly \u8212- {\i kol onsa afilu onsa de-lo shchiach} \u8212- and even then the question of whether such extreme language is enforceable is a matter of halachic analysis.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Woman\u8217's Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 No Yichud Rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} now addresses the practical situation of a wife during the period of uncertainty after a conditional {\i get} has been given: {\i lo tityached imo ela bifnei eidim} \u8212- she may not be secluded alone with her husband unless witnesses are present.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 New Kiddushin Risk\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The reason is concern about {\i kiddushin}. The {\i get} was given on condition; it may retroactively take effect from that day if the husband dies. But if the couple continues living together as before, and the husband eventually does die and the {\i get} is retroactively validated, then she will have been living with him after the effective date of the {\i get}. Any intimacy during that period would be viewed as potentially constituting new {\i kiddushin} \u8212- not {\i get yashan}, since the {\i get} had already been given, but a new act of {\i kiddushin} through cohabitation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Witnesses Safeguard\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If witnesses are present, the concern about {\i yichud} is eliminated: witnesses prevent intimacy, and therefore no new {\i kiddushin} can be inferred from their continued presence in the same space. The {\i mishna} further specifies that even a slave, a maidservant, or a non-household member qualifies as a witness sufficient to remove the concern \u8212- {\i afilu al pi eved afilu al pi shifcha} \u8212- with one exception: her own personal maidservant does not suffice, because the woman feels entirely uninhibited in the presence of her own servant and would not restrain herself on that account ({\i mipnei shelibah gas bah bishifchatah}).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Woman\u8217's Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The second question the {\i mishna} addresses is the status of the woman during the intervening period: {\i mah hi be\u8217'otan hayamim} \u8212- what is she during those days? Is she treated as a {\i safek migureshet} (possibly divorced) or as a definite {\i eshet ish}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Yehuda\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yehuda holds that she is treated {\i ke\u8217'eshet ish lachol devareha} \u8212- as a fully married woman for all purposes. This is the more stringent position, treating her as unambiguously married throughout the conditional period.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rashi Reinterpretation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rashi, however, introduces a significant reinterpretation of the case under discussion here. Rashi understands the {\i Gemara} to be speaking not of the standard {\i im mati mecholi zeh} condition established in the earlier {\i mishna}, but of a husband who says the {\i get} should take effect {\i a minute before his death}. This formulation \u8212- analogous to the idea of doing {\i teshuva} the day before one dies, which means one must always do {\i teshuva} since one never knows which day is one\u8217's last \u8212- creates a distinctive analysis. In hindsight, on the actual day of death, the wife was definitively {\i migureshet} for the last minute of that day. But on every other day that preceded it, she was definitively an {\i eshet ish} \u8212- not a {\i safek}, but a {\i vadai eshet ish} \u8212- because the {\i get} had not yet taken effect. The peculiarity is the gap between foresight and hindsight: living through those days, any given day might turn out to be the last day, and therefore prospectively she is a {\i safek migureshet} on each day. But retrospectively, once the husband has died, every day except the last was clearly a period of full {\i ishus}. This has profound consequences for questions such as whether she would bring a {\i chatat} or an {\i asham} if she were to have relations with another man during this period \u8212- a question of whether we assess at the time of the act ({\i be-sha\u8217'at ma\u8217'aseh}) or retrospectively.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yose holds {\i megureshes ve-eina megureshes} \u8212- she is in a state of {\i safek}. Again, on Rashi\u8217's reinterpretation, this refers to the case of \u8220"a minute before death,\u8221" and Rabbi Yose acknowledges the ambiguity of her status throughout the period.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Sexuality & Witnesses\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Braita Conditions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i braita} elaborates on the specific circumstances under which we become concerned about new {\i kiddushin} during this conditional period. The case is: they were seen together in the dark ({\i nistayachada imo ba\u8217'afeila}), or she slept at the foot of his bed ({\i yashna imo tachas margelot ha-mita}).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Zenut Concern\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i braita} states: {\i ein chosheshin shema nis\u8217'asku be-davar acher} \u8212- we are not concerned that they engaged in sexual relations in such circumstances. Yet it also states {\i chosheshin mishum zenut} \u8212- we are concerned about {\i zenut}, about sexual conduct outside the marriage framework. And simultaneously, {\i ein chosheshin mishum kiddushin} \u8212- we are not concerned that any such relations constituted new {\i kiddushin}. This formulation is genuinely puzzling, and the {\i Gemara} works through multiple readings.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Witness Required\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The first reading, proposed by Rav Nachman citing Rabba bar Avuha, unpacks the {\i braita} as follows: {\i ra\u8217'uah she-niv\u8217'ala} \u8212- if witnesses actually observed them engaging in sexual relations, then {\i chosheshin mishum kiddushin}, we are concerned that this may have been a new act of {\i kiddushin}. In other words, direct witnessing of the sexual act is the threshold: below that threshold there is no concern at all.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Money Reframing\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Within that same reading: if after the observed sexual relations the husband gave her money, then {\i chosheshin mishum zenut} \u8212- we are concerned it was {\i zenut} ({\i de-amrinan esnan nasan lah}, we say he paid her as a fee), {\i ve-ein chosheshin mishum kiddushin} \u8212- and we are no longer concerned that it was {\i kiddushin}. The passage of money reframes the act: rather than reading it as a new betrothal, we read the money as a payment, which in turn leads us to read the sexual act as {\i zenut} rather than as {\i kiddushin}. Counterintuitive as it is \u8212- this is his own wife, after all \u8212- the logic is that if money changes hands after intimacy, we interpret the money as {\i esnan zenut}, and therefore the act cannot simultaneously be construed as {\i kiddushin}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Yose\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yose son of Rabbi Yehuda disagrees: {\i af chosheshin mishum kiddushin} \u8212- even when money was given afterward, we are still concerned it may have been {\i kiddushin}. Perhaps the money was a gift; perhaps she needed cash for shopping. The mere presence of money does not suffice to reframe the act as {\i zenut} and thereby dissolve the {\i chashash kiddushin}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Abaye Challenge\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye presses the textual point: {\i midi kaspah katani} \u8212- where does the {\i braita} mention money at all? The reading of Rav Nachman that makes money the pivot of the {\i braita} imports a concept nowhere explicit in the text. Abaye therefore proposes an alternative reading.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Abaye Reading\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 On Abaye\u8217's reading, the {\i braita} is structured differently. The first opinion ({\i tanna kamma}) says: if witnesses saw them actually having sexual relations ({\i ra\u8217'uah she-niv\u8217'ala}), we are concerned about {\i zenut} but not about {\i kiddushin}. That is: we take seriously that something happened, but we interpret it as {\i zenut}, not as a new betrothal. The default interpretive frame, even for witnessed sex, is {\i zenut} rather than {\i kiddushin}. Rabbi Yose son of Rabbi Yehuda disagrees and says: {\i af chosheshin mishum kiddushin} \u8212- we are also concerned that witnessed sex may constitute new {\i kiddushin}. On this reading, the entire debate turns on whether observed sexual relations between the husband and his conditionally-divorced wife should be presumed {\i zenut} or {\i safek kiddushin}, and the money question is simply absent from the {\i braita} entirely.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava Reading\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava offers a third reading, which produces the most expansive range of positions. On Rava\u8217's reading, the {\i tanna kamma} is saying: even if witnesses saw them having sexual relations, we are not concerned about {\i kiddushin} \u8212- it is {\i zenut}, period. And Rabbi Yose son of Rabbi Yehuda, by saying {\i af chosheshin mishum kiddushin}, means: I disagree on two counts. First, if they were seen having sex, that is a {\i safek kiddushin}, not mere {\i zenut}. Second \u8212- and this is the force of {\i af}, \u8220"even\u8221" \u8212- even if they were not observed having sex at all, as long as they were together in {\i yichud}, we are concerned about {\i kiddushin}. On Rava\u8217's reading, Rabbi Yose son of Rabbi Yehuda holds that {\i yichud} alone is sufficient to generate the concern of new {\i kiddushin}, without any direct observation of sexual relations.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Spectrum Positions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} then asks: {\i ke-man azla ha de-amar Rabba bar bar Chana amar Rabbi Yochanan} \u8212- according to which position does the statement of Rabbi Yochanan align? Rabbi Yochanan had ruled elsewhere that the debate between Beit Shammai and Beit Hillel regarding a husband who gives a {\i get} and then spends the night at the same inn as his wife applies only when witnesses observed them having sex; if no sex was observed, {\i divrei hakol ein tzricha heimenu get sheni} \u8212- universally, no second {\i get} is needed. On Abaye\u8217's reading, Rabbi Yochanan\u8217's statement aligns with {\i divrei hakol} \u8212- it fits both {\i tanna kamma} and Rabbi Yose son of Rabbi Yehuda. On Rava\u8217's reading, however, Rabbi Yochanan\u8217's statement fits only the {\i tanna kamma}, not Rabbi Yose son of Rabbi Yehuda, since on Rava\u8217's reading Rabbi Yose son of Rabbi Yehuda holds that {\i yichud} alone \u8212- without any observed sex \u8212- already generates a concern about {\i kiddushin}. The {\i Gemara\u8217's} conclusion is {\i ke-man de-lo ke-chad} \u8212- this statement of Rabbi Yochanan does not fit cleanly with any single position.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What emerges from the full discussion is a spectrum of positions regarding when living together during the conditional {\i get} period creates a {\i chashash kiddushin}. At one extreme is the position that witnessed sex reframes automatically as {\i zenut} and nothing is ever a concern. At the other extreme is the position of Rabbi Yose son of Rabbi Yehuda as read by Rava: {\i yichud} alone, even without sex, creates a concern. Between these poles sits the question of what direct witnessing of sexual relations entails. The {\i Gemara} ends with this range of positions laid out, without a final adjudication \u8212- a rich and unsettled conclusion to the discussions of {\i Gittin} 73.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Gittin 74\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Mehayom Im Mati Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Opening Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The daf opens at the bottom of 73b with the question {\i mai hi be\u8217'otan hayamim} \u8212- what is a woman\u8217's status during the interim period when a {\i get} has been given with the formula {\i mehayom im mati}, \u8220"as of today if I die\u8221"? The previous discussion had already established that {\i yichud} should be avoided during this period, lest it be construed as an act of {\i kiddushin bi\u8217'ah} that would retroactively nullify the {\i get}. The natural assumption is that her status during the interim is that of a {\i safek megureshet}: if he dies she will have been divorced retroactively from the moment the {\i get} was given; if he does not die, she was never divorced at all. That is the straightforward reading \u8212- but the {\i mishnah} reveals a genuine debate.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Yehuda Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Yehuda} says she is {\i ke\u8217'eshet ish lechol devaraha} \u8212- not merely that she must conduct herself as a married woman out of caution, but that she is a {\i vadai eshet ish} in every respect. The {\i gemara} will elaborate: for example, if she had relations with another man during this period {\i bishogeg}, she would bring a {\i chatat} \u8212- even if he ultimately died and the {\i get} took retroactive effect. She is not a {\i safek} at all; she is definitively married. The obvious difficulty is: how can this be? If he dies in the end, she should retroactively have been {\i megureshet} from the moment the {\i get} was given \u8212- in which case she should have been a {\i vadai megureshet}, not a {\i vadai eshet ish}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Yossi Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Yossi} says {\i megureshet ve\u8217'eina megureshet} \u8212- she is divorced and not divorced. On its face this sounds equivalent to {\i safek}, which is roughly what we would have expected. But the {\i gemara} will clarify that {\i Rabbi Yossi}\u8217's position is not quite the same as a resolvable {\i safek}, and a third category \u8212- {\i taluy}, suspension \u8212- will be introduced to sharpen the distinctions.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 To understand {\i Rabbi Yehuda}\u8217's position that she is a {\i vadai eshet ish}, the {\i gemara} first raises the objection: {\i u\u8217'lchi mayis havi gita} \u8212- if you say the {\i get} only takes effect when he dies, that amounts to {\i ein get le\u8217'achar mita}, which is inadmissible. So {\i Rava} says the case must involve a husband who said {\i me\u8217'et she\u8217'ani ba\u8217'olam} \u8212- the {\i get} should take effect at the very last moment he is still alive, a moment before his death.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rashi\u8217's Reading\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is where {\i Rashi} and {\i Tosafot} diverge sharply. {\i Rashi} accepts the {\i gemara}\u8217's text as given and understands that {\i Rava} is not explaining {\i mehayom im mati} but is rather redirecting the {\i mishnah} entirely to a different case. According to {\i Rashi}, the debate between {\i Rabbi Yehuda} and the {\i chachamim} does not concern {\i mehayom im mati} at all; rather, it concerns a case where the husband explicitly said the {\i get} should take effect the moment before he dies. In that scenario, {\i Rabbi Yehuda} holds she was a {\i vadai eshet ish} throughout the entire preceding period \u8212- consistently with the {\i get} taking effect only at the very last instant. The opposing view holds that even in that scenario she retains some status of {\i safek} throughout the interim period, since while she was living through it she experienced it as uncertain. This is an admittedly very difficult reading: the {\i mishnah} had been discussing {\i mehayom im mati} throughout, and suddenly, without transition, {\i Rashi} has the {\i gemara} pivot to an entirely different case. One might ask why any opinion would deny that she was a {\i vadai eshet ish} when the husband had explicitly stipulated the {\i get} takes effect only at the very last instant of his life \u8212- she should have a {\i chezkat eshet ish} and in retrospect should clearly be {\i vadai eshet ish}. The position that even then she retains a {\i safek} retroactively is difficult to defend.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafot\u8217's Girsa\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot}, following {\i Rabbeinu Tam} and {\i Rabbeinu Chananel}, resolve the difficulty by emending just one element of the text. Their reading \u8212- which {\i Tosafot} claim reflects the original {\i girsa} \u8212- has {\i Rava} say not a description of a different case but rather {\i na\u8217'aseh ke\u8217'omer me\u8217'ishtani ba\u8217'olam}: we treat the husband\u8217's formula {\i mehayom im mati} as if he said it should take effect at the last moment he is alive. This is the interpretive principle at stake in the {\i mishnah} itself. When a husband says {\i mehayom im mati}, he is in some sense giving a conditional {\i get} effective \u8220"as of today.\u8221" But what does he really intend? The {\i gemara} reasons that if one were to ask this husband \u8212- knowing he is going to die \u8212- when he truly wants his wife to be divorced, he would say: at the very last minute, of course, but I was told I must say {\i mehayom} because {\i ein get le\u8217'achar mita}. On {\i Tosafot}\u8217's reading, {\i Rabbi Yehuda} holds that we interpret {\i mehayom im mati} to mean the {\i get} takes effect at the very last moment he is alive, and therefore throughout the entire prior period she is a {\i vadai eshet ish}. The opposing position is: no, we read the words as they stand \u8212- \u8220"as of today, if I die\u8221" \u8212- meaning if he dies, it takes effect retroactively from the moment of the giving. During the interim, it is a {\i safek} of all or nothing.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yamim Shebeintayim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i braita} then teaches the particulars of {\i yamim shebeintayim}, the days in between. If one follows {\i Rabbi Yehuda}\u8217's approach \u8212- that she is {\i ke\u8217'eshet ish lechol devaraha} \u8212- then during this period her husband is entitled to her {\i metziah} (found objects), her {\i ma\u8217'asei yadeha} (earnings), he may annul her vows, he inherits her if she dies, if he is a {\i kohen} he becomes {\i tamei} for her, and she eats {\i terumah}. The general rule is {\i harei hi ke\u8217'ishto lechol davar} \u8212- she is as his wife in every respect \u8212- {\i ela she\u8217'eina tzricha heimenu get sheni}, except that if he dies she does not need a second {\i get}, because the {\i get} takes effect at the last moment. That is the position of {\i Rabbi Yehuda}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Taluy vs.\u160 ?Safek\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Meir} says {\i be\u8217'ilata tluyah} \u8212- if she has relations with another man, the act is suspended ({\i taluy}). Depending on whether he dies, it will turn out retroactively either to be adultery or not. This is the straightforward position: the {\i get} is contingent on his death, and if he dies it takes effect from the day it was given, so retroactively she was {\i megureshet} and there was no sin. {\i Rabbi Yossi}, by contrast, says {\i be\u8217'ilata safek} \u8212- her act is in {\i doubt}, not merely in suspension. These sound identical, and the {\i gemara} asks {\i mai beinaihu}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Asham Taluy\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Yochanan} answers: {\i asham taluy}. Assuming he does eventually die, does she bring an {\i asham taluy} for having had relations with another man during the interim period? An {\i asham taluy} is brought when one performs an act that may constitute an {\i aveira} \u8212- one that would normally require a {\i chatat} \u8212- but it is uncertain whether it did. According to {\i Rabbi Meir}, once he dies, we look back retroactively: she was {\i vadai} divorced from the day the {\i get} was given, the act was not adultery, no sin occurred \u8212- she brings nothing. According to {\i Rabbi Yossi}, even after he dies the question remains permanently unresolved. {\i Rabbi Yossi}\u8217's position is that the phrase {\i mehayom im mati} is inherently ambiguous: it could mean the {\i get} takes effect as of the day it was given (the {\i Rabbi Meir} reading) or at the very last moment (the {\i Rabbi Yehuda} reading), and {\i Rabbi Yossi} cannot determine which is correct. Since the statement is inherently indeterminate, no subsequent fact \u8212- not even his death \u8212- ever resolves which reading is right. Therefore even in retrospect she is in an irresolvable {\i safek}, and she brings an {\i asham taluy}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is noted as a {\i chiddush} in two respects. First, an {\i asham taluy} is usually associated with a doubt that is in principle resolvable \u8212- one had two pieces before oneself and did not know which one was eaten. An abstract {\i safek} that can never be verified is a less standard case. Second, there is a debate whether an {\i asham taluy} applies to abstract doubt at all, though there are positions that permit it. {\i Tosafot} note that {\i Rabbi Yossi} is not saying retroactive reassessment is impossible in principle; he is saying the original statement is ambiguous in a way that no future event can adjudicate. She therefore brings an {\i asham taluy} because it is, in effect, a {\i safek} that is permanently sealed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chachamim Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} also records the position of {\i chachamim}, who say {\i megureshet ve\u8217'eina megureshet}. The {\i gemara} notes that {\i chachamim} are essentially aligned with {\i Rabbi Yossi} \u8212- both hold the status is permanently indeterminate. The distinction lies in what {\i Rabbi Zeira} taught in the name of {\i Shmuel}: {\i kol makom she\u8217'amru chachamim megureshet ve\u8217'eina megureshet, ba\u8217'al chayav bimezonoteha} \u8212- wherever the {\i chachamim} use the formula {\i megureshet ve\u8217'eina megureshet}, the husband remains obligated to support his wife. This is a genuine {\i nafka mina} from {\i Rabbi Yossi}\u8217's formulation: {\i Rabbi Yossi} might say the husband is not obligated (perhaps applying the principle of {\i hamotzi mechavero alav hara\u8217'aya}), whereas the {\i chachamim}, by invoking this established formula, affirm that even in an irresolvable state of {\i safek megureshet}, the husband\u8217's support obligation continues.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Al Menat Gitin\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Get Conditional\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The next {\i mishnah} introduces the case of {\i harei zeh gitayich al menat she\u8217'titni li matayim zuz} \u8212- \u8220"here is your {\i get} on the condition that you give me two hundred {\i zuz}.\u8221" It is worth pausing on the figure: two hundred {\i zuz} is precisely the value of a standard {\i ketubbah}. The case is therefore not simply a random monetary condition but something more pointed \u8212- the husband is effectively saying: give up your {\i ketubbah} and I will give you the {\i get}. This is permissible when the wife is seeking the divorce but cannot compel it, and the husband, who has no obligation to divorce her in such circumstances, may set conditions. Were he compelled to divorce her \u8212- in a case of {\i kefiyah} \u8212- this kind of condition would not be allowed. But absent that, he retains the unilateral power to decide whether to divorce at all, and he may condition it accordingly.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The critical feature of the case is that he already hands her the {\i get}. He does not say \u8220"first give me the money and then I\u8217'll give the {\i get}.\u8221" He gives her the {\i get} with the condition embedded in it. The question is entirely about how the {\i al menat} condition operates: does the {\i get} take effect retroactively from the moment it was given once the condition is fulfilled, or does it take effect only at the moment of fulfillment?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} continues: if he says {\i al menat she\u8217'titni li mikhan ad shloshim yom}, on the condition she give it within thirty days \u8212- {\i natnah lo betoch shloshim yom megureshet, ve\u8217'im lav einah megureshet}. If she gives within thirty days she is divorced; if not, she is not. The {\i gemara} observes this seems obvious, and we will see the discussion of whether the thirty-day limit is strictly required or merely advisory.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Huna Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} then uses the phrase {\i ve\u8217'titein} \u8212- \u8220"and she gives\u8221" \u8212- rather than the more expected {\i titein ve\u8217'tehei megureshet}, \u8220"let her give and she will be divorced.\u8221" The {\i gemara} asks {\i mai ve\u8217'titein}: what does this phrasing mean? {\i Rabbi Yehuda} reads it as {\i lechshe\u8217'titein} \u8212- only when she gives the money does the {\i get} take effect. This is a significant position: {\i al menat} means the {\i get} is effective only at the moment the condition is fulfilled, not retroactively from the moment it was given.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rav Huna} takes the opposite view: {\i ve\u8217'hi titein} means she gives the money and that satisfies the condition, but the {\i get} takes effect retroactively from the moment it was given.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Nifka Mina Torn Get\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The practical difference between these two positions is illustrated by a case where the {\i get} was torn or lost in the interim. According to {\i Rav Huna}, since the {\i get} takes effect from the moment it was originally given, the subsequent fate of the document is irrelevant \u8212- {\i ein tzricha heimenu get sheni}, she does not need a second {\i get}. According to {\i Rabbi Yehuda}, since the {\i get} only takes effect at the moment she hands over the money, the document must still be present and valid at that moment \u8212- {\i tzricha heimenu get sheni}, she requires a second {\i get} if the original was lost or destroyed. This flows from the principle that when an act takes effect at a future moment, the instrument or object through which it was created must still be present at that moment. (By contrast, money given as {\i kesef kiddushin} does not need to still be held at the moment the condition is fulfilled, because she remains in a state of having been enriched by it; but a document must physically persist.)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kiddushin Parallel\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 An analogous debate applies in the realm of {\i kiddushin}. If a man says to a woman {\i harei at mekudeshet li al menat she\u8217'etein lach matayim zuz} \u8212- you will be betrothed to me on the condition that I give you two hundred {\i zuz} \u8212- {\i Rav Huna} holds that once he gives the money the {\i kiddushin} takes effect retroactively from when the ring was given. {\i Rabbi Yehuda} holds it takes effect only at the moment the money is given. The {\i nafka mina} here: if in the interim she accepted a ring from another man. According to {\i Rav Huna}, she would be retroactively married from the moment of the first ring, so the second ring has no effect. According to {\i Rabbi Yehuda}, she was not yet married when she accepted the second ring, and she is married to the second man, not the first. A third {\i nafka mina} \u8212- applicable in both {\i gittin} and {\i kiddushin} \u8212- is whether relations with another man during the interim constitute adultery.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Psychology Reasoning\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} explains why both cases need to be stated explicitly, because one might have argued opposite conclusions in each case and therefore wrongly assumed that {\i Rav Huna} and {\i Rabbi Yehuda} would agree in one of the two contexts.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Had the debate only been stated in {\i kiddushin}, one might have reasoned: {\i Rav Huna} says it is retroactive there {\i mishum d\u8217'likrova ka\u8217'ati} \u8212- he is trying to draw her close, he wants to marry her, so naturally we read his words to mean the {\i kiddushin} takes effect as soon as possible. But in {\i gittin}, where {\i d\u8217'lerachoka ka\u8217'ati} \u8212- he is trying to distance himself from her \u8212- perhaps even {\i Rav Huna} would agree with {\i Rabbi Yehuda} that we read the words to mean it takes effect only when the money changes hands, since he is not eager to divorce. On the other hand, one might argue the opposite: in {\i gittin}, where {\i d\u8217'lo kassif lemisbei} \u8212- he has no embarrassment about demanding the money \u8212- he is not threatened by saying it takes effect immediately, because he is confident he can always collect. In {\i kiddushin}, where the woman is in a somewhat more hesitant position and {\i kassif lemisbei} \u8212- she is reluctant to press him for the money \u8212- she might want the {\i kiddushin} to take effect only when the money changes hands, to preserve her leverage.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Similarly, had the debate been stated only in {\i gittin}, one might have argued the reverse logic from {\i Rabbi Yehuda}\u8217's side. The {\i gemara} therefore says {\i tzricha} \u8212- both formulations are necessary. The deeper point is that {\i al menat} should not require any case-by-case psychological analysis at all. {\i Rav Huna} has a consistent rule: {\i al menat} means retroactive, as of the moment the {\i get} was given. {\i Rabbi Yehuda} has a consistent rule: {\i al menat} means the act takes effect only when the condition is fulfilled. The circumstantial psychology can always be argued both ways, which is precisely why {\i halacha} requires a stable rule rather than ad hoc intent-inference.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Proof from Braitot\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then challenges {\i Rabbi Yehuda} from two {\i braitot}. The first teaches: {\i harei zeh gitayich al menat she\u8217'titni li matayim zuz} \u8212- {\i af al pi she\u8217'nikra ha\u8217'get o she\u8217'ne\u8217'evad}, even if the {\i get} was torn or lost in the interim, {\i megureshet} \u8212- she is divorced once she gives the money. This is clearly consistent with {\i Rav Huna}: the {\i get} takes effect retroactively from when it was given, and the subsequent destruction of the document is irrelevant. The {\i braita} adds the practical advice not to remarry until the money is actually given, since if she can never produce it the {\i get} will never take effect.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A second {\i braita} addresses the case where the husband died before the money was given. {\i Tanna kamma} holds that if she gave the money before he died, she is free from the {\i yavam} (she was divorced); if she did not give it before his death, she is not divorced and is {\i zekuka le\u8217'yavam}. {\i Rabban Gamliel} adds that even after his death she may give the money to his father, his brother, or another relative, and that satisfies the condition. The key insight from {\i Rabban Gamliel}\u8217's position is that it presupposes universal agreement that the {\i get} takes effect retroactively \u8212- the only debate is whether giving the money to his heirs satisfies the condition he formulated as \u8220"give it to me.\u8221" Both {\i tannaim} agree that once the money is given, the {\i get} takes effect from the moment it was handed over, not from the moment of payment. This is a clear {\i tiyuvta} against {\i Rabbi Yehuda}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Yehuda Challenged\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Yehuda} responds: {\i ha mani Rabbi} \u8212- these {\i braitot} follow {\i Rabbi}. For {\i Rav Huna} himself stated his rule in the name of {\i Rabbi}: {\i kol ha\u8217'omer al menat ke\u8217'omer me\u8217'achshav dami} \u8212- whoever says \u8220"on the condition that\u8221" is treated as having said \u8220"as of now, on the condition that.\u8221" {\i Rabbi Yehuda} thus concedes that the {\i braitot} go against him, but claims there are other {\i tannaim} who dispute {\i Rabbi}\u8217's principle. The {\i gemara} presses him: who are these {\i tannaim}? At this point {\i Rabbi Yehuda} is in a very weak position \u8212- the {\i braitot} are against him, {\i Rabbi}\u8217's principle is against him, and no {\i tanna} can be produced to support his view.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Eretz Yisrael Consensus\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Zeira} relates that when he was still in {\i Bavel}, the tradition there was that {\i Rav Huna}\u8217's ruling in the name of {\i Rabbi} was disputed by other {\i tannaim}, and that is why {\i Rav Yehuda} felt free to disagree. But {\i ki selik} \u8212- when {\i Rabbi Zeira} made {\i aliyah} to {\i Eretz Yisrael} \u8212- {\i ashkachtei le\u8217'Rabbi Assi} \u8212- he found {\i Rabbi Assi} sitting and declaring in the name of {\i Rabbi Yochanan}: {\i hakol modim be\u8217'omer al menat ke\u8217'omer me\u8217'achshav dami} \u8212- everyone agrees that {\i al menat} is equivalent to saying \u8220"as of now.\u8221" This is not merely the position of {\i Rabbi} \u8212- it is the universal consensus. In {\i Eretz Yisrael}, where knowledge of {\i tannaitic} positions was most authoritative, there was no tradition of any dissent on this point.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Conclusion Distinction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} concludes with a precise formulation: {\i lo nechelku ela be\u8217'hayom u\u8217'le\u8217'achar mita} \u8212- the only genuine {\i tannaitic} debate was not about {\i al menat} at all. {\i Al menat} universally means retroactive, effective as of the day it was given. The debate was about the formula from the previous {\i mishnah} \u8212- {\i mehayom u\u8217'le\u8217'achar mita} \u8212- \u8220"as of today and after my death.\u8221" That phrase sounds contradictory on its face: \u8220"today\u8221" and \u8220"after death\u8221" cannot both be the operative time. The {\i chachamim} read it as an irresolvable internal contradiction that renders it {\i safek megureshet ve\u8217'eina megureshet}. {\i Rabbi} is prepared to read even that formulation as a valid {\i tnai} \u8212- as if the husband is saying the {\i get} should take effect today, on the condition he dies \u8212- and therefore declares {\i kaze get}, it is a valid {\i get} once he dies.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A final observation resolves an apparent structural question in the {\i braita}: why does the debate between {\i Rabbi} and the {\i chachamim} use the {\i mehayom u\u8217'le\u8217'achar mita} case rather than the seemingly easier {\i al menat} case? The {\i gemara} answers {\i kocho de\u8217'heteira adif lei}: the greater {\i chiddush} belongs to the {\i tanna} prepared to read the difficult case permissively. The {\i chachamim} who restrict are only being {\i machmir} \u8212- they are rendering the {\i get} invalid. The remarkable ruling is {\i Rabbi}\u8217's willingness to validate even the strained language of {\i mehayom u\u8217'le\u8217'achar mita}. Stating the debate on that harder case demonstrates the full reach of {\i Rabbi}\u8217's interpretive generosity.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Lost Cloak Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Itzaliti Story\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel} relates a story ({\i ma\u8217'aseh b\u8217'Tzidan}): a man said to his wife {\i harei zeh gitayich al menat she\u8217'titni li itzaliti} \u8212- here is your {\i get} on the condition you give me back my cloak. The cloak was lost ({\i ve\u8217'avda itzalito}). The {\i chachamim} ruled that she may give him the monetary value of the cloak and that counts as fulfilling the condition. This is a striking {\i chiddush}. Even in a case involving a specific personal item \u8212- a garment, something to which the husband might have personal attachment and not merely economic interest \u8212- the {\i chachamim} allow money to substitute for the object once the object is gone.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Missing Teaching\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} asks {\i mai ta\u8217'ama d\u8217'katani ma\u8217'aseh} \u8212- why does the {\i mishnah} simply give the story without first stating the underlying rule? The {\i gemara} answers {\i chasuri mechasra ve\u8217'hachi katni}: words are missing, and the {\i mishnah} should be reconstructed as follows. The implicit rule being illustrated is: if he said {\i al menat she\u8217'titni li itzaliti}, specifying the cloak, and the cloak was lost \u8212- one might have thought {\i itzaliti davka ka\u8217'amar lah}, he meant that specific cloak and nothing else can substitute, so the condition can never be met. {\i Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel} comes to teach {\i lo titten lo et dameha} \u8212- no, she gives him the monetary equivalent and that satisfies the condition.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mechulim Lach\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then introduces a related question posed by {\i Rav Assi} to {\i Rabbi Yochanan}: what if the husband said {\i harei zeh gitayich al menat she\u8217'titni li matayim zuz}, and then later said to her {\i mechulim lach} \u8212- I waive it, you do not have to give me the money? Does that cause the {\i get} to take effect?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} frames this question as applicable across the two positions: one may ask it according to the {\i Rabbanan} (who do not allow money to substitute for the cloak) and according to {\i Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel} (who does allow substitution).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Object vs.\u160 ?Money\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Taking the {\i Rabbanan} first: one might think that even they would allow {\i mechulim lach} to work. After all, the {\i Rabbanan} said money cannot substitute for the cloak \u8212- but that was because the husband was not waiving the condition; he simply declined to accept money as equivalent to the specific object. Perhaps if he explicitly says \u8220"I waive it,\u8221" that is different \u8212- he is not claiming the money equals the cloak but is actively relinquishing his claim. On the other hand, one might argue that even {\i Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel} would not accept {\i mechulim lach}. {\i Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel} permitted money as a substitute precisely because it can purchase the item in the marketplace \u8212- a functional equivalence argument. But if nothing at all is given, there is no basis to say the condition was met. He permitted functional equivalence; he did not permit outright waiver.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 No Waiving Answer\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Yochanan} answers: {\i einah megureshet} \u8212- the {\i get} does not take effect. Saying {\i mechulim lach} does not work, and this applies even according to {\i Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel}. It is one thing to accept money as the functional equivalent of a lost object; it is another thing entirely to simply declare the condition void without anything being given or treated as given.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Neder Al Tnai\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Konam Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Following from {\i Rabbi Yochanan}\u8217's ruling, a challenge is raised from a {\i braita} involving a {\i neder al tnai}. The case: a man says to his fellow {\i konam she\u8217'atah neheneh li im atah noten livni kor echad shel chittin u\u8217'shtei chavyot shel yayin} \u8212- I make a vow that you may derive no benefit from anything of mine, unless you give my son a quantity of wheat and two barrels of wine.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Vow Absolution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Meir} rules {\i asur ad sheyitein} \u8212- the other person remains forbidden from benefiting from this man\u8217's property until the condition is actually fulfilled. {\i Chachamim} say {\i af ze yachol lehatir et nidro shelo al pi chacham} \u8212- the one who made the vow can release it without the formal process of {\i hatarat nedarim} before a {\i chacham}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ke\u8217'ilu Treatment\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 How? By saying {\i hareini ke\u8217'ilu hitkabalti} \u8212- I will treat it as if I have already received what was promised. So by the {\i chachamim}\u8217's ruling, the person who made the vow can declare \u8220"it is as if I received the wheat and wine,\u8221" and the {\i neder} is thereby dissolved. This appears to be precisely the mechanism the husband would need in the {\i get} case \u8212- saying \u8220"it is as if I received the two hundred {\i zuz}.\u8221" If the vow-maker can invoke this formula, why can the husband who gave the {\i get} not do likewise?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Intent Difference\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} asks {\i hachi hashta} \u8212- how can these cases be compared?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tzurah vs.\u160 ?Revacha\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} draws a fundamental distinction: {\i hatam letzi\u8217'ura ka michaven, hacha lerevacha ka michaven ve\u8217'lo letzi\u8217'ura}. In the {\i get} case, when the husband says \u8220"on the condition you give me two hundred {\i zuz},\u8221" we interpret his intent as being primarily about making her suffer \u8212- {\i letzi\u8217'ura}. It is not really about the money as money; it is about the principle, which in the context of an adversarial divorce we read as his desire to impose hardship on her. Therefore, even if he declares \u8220"I consider myself as having received the money,\u8221" the condition is not satisfied, because the condition was never really about his benefit in isolation \u8212- it was about her suffering. Since she has not suffered (no money changed hands, no hardship was imposed), his declaration accomplishes nothing toward fulfilling the {\i tnai}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 In the {\i neder} case, by contrast, the one who made the vow wanted his son provided for \u8212- {\i lerevacha}, for benefit, not to impose suffering on the other party. Since the entire purpose of the condition was his own gain, he has the power to declare \u8220"I consider that gain as received,\u8221" and that declaration genuinely resolves the condition.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Final Conclusion\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What emerges clearly from the {\i gemara} is a precise and important principle: one is not in a position to simply {\i waive} a {\i tnai}. A condition stands as it was articulated; it cannot be unilaterally dissolved. What one {\i may} be able to do \u8212- under specific circumstances \u8212- is to declare that the condition has been treated as fulfilled, {\i ke\u8217'ilu hitkabalti}. But that option is available only when the condition was framed entirely around one\u8217's own benefit ({\i revacha}), so that one may credibly say \u8220"I consider that benefit received.\u8221" When the condition is framed around the other party\u8217's suffering ({\i tzi\u8217'ura}), no such declaration is meaningful \u8212- the condition is not met, and the {\i get} does not take effect.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Gittin 75\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Tna\u8217'im Overview\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Cloak & Conditions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i daf} opens by continuing a rich {\i gemara} about what constitutes satisfaction of {\i tna\u8217'im} \u8212- conditions \u8212- in {\i gittin}. The {\i gemara} has been reading the {\i Mishna} as presenting the following case: a husband says \u8220"give me my cloak\u8221" as the condition of a {\i get}. Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel rules that if the cloak is lost, giving its monetary value suffices. The {\i rabbanan} disagree, holding that the cloak itself must be returned.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Waiving vs.\u160 ?Hana\u8217'ah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then compared this to a separate question: when a condition stipulates that the wife give 200 {\i zuz}, may the husband simply waive the {\i tnai} outright \u8212- {\i achulei machilna lach} \u8212- or alternatively, may he not waive it formally but instead declare that his {\i hana\u8217'ah} has been satisfied, treating the condition as though fulfilled? A parallel case from {\i nedarim} was introduced in which the latter approach does work. In the context of {\i gittin}, however, the {\i gemara} suggests a distinction may be warranted.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Nedarim Parallel\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The critical variable is what the husband had in mind when he formulated the {\i tnai}. If the 200 {\i zuz} were meant as an expression of his {\i hana\u8217'ah} \u8212- his personal benefit \u8212- then declaring that benefit received might well suffice, parallel to the {\i nedarim} case. But if the point of the {\i tnai} was that she must suffer, that she must endure the burden of satisfying his demand, then no declaration of {\i hana\u8217'ah} can substitute for that. The other person still has not suffered. This raises a deeper question about how we interpret any given {\i tnai}: what was the underlying purpose the party had in mind when formulating it?\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Sharecropper Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rain & Irrigation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now presents the case of {\i hahu gavra de-amar lei le-ariseh} \u8212- a man who said to his sharecropper: \u8220"All other sharecroppers irrigate the field three times and receive a quarter of the produce. You irrigate it four times, and I will give you a third.\u8221" The sharecropper agreed. At the end of the season, however, rain fell and rendered the extra irrigation unnecessary \u8212- indeed, counter-productive. Rav Yosef rules: {\i ha lo dala} \u8212- he did not irrigate as agreed, so he is not entitled to the extra share. Rava disagrees: {\i ha lo itztrecha} \u8212- it was not required. The purpose of the extra irrigation was achieved by the rain; the {\i tnai} is accordingly satisfied.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Money vs.\u160 ?Cloak\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} proposes that this dispute maps onto the earlier one: perhaps Rav Yosef follows the {\i rabbanan} \u8212- a {\i tnai} must be fulfilled precisely as stated \u8212- while Rava follows Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel, who permits equivalents. Just as Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel says money may substitute for the cloak, so Rava holds that rain substitutes for irrigation. The {\i gemara} immediately rejects this alignment, noting that we generally rule like Rava, and yet we do {\i not} rule like Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel regarding the cloak. The two cases must therefore rest on different logic.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gittin Specificity\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} resolves the tension as follows: even according to the {\i rabbanan}, who insist that the cloak itself must be returned, Rava\u8217's ruling regarding the irrigation remains valid \u8212- because the two situations are fundamentally different. In the {\i get} context, the {\i gemara} says, {\i delitzira kamichavein} \u8212- the husband\u8217's intent in imposing the {\i tnai} is to make demands of her, to impose hardship. Whether the condition involves money or a garment or anything else, we presuppose he wants no latitude given, no substitutes accepted. His {\i da\u8217'at} is to demand precisely what he stated.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 By contrast, the sharecropper arrangement is a business transaction \u8212- {\i mishum arrucha}, it is all about profit \u8212- and the {\i tnai} is naturally read in terms of its economic purpose. If the rain accomplished exactly what the extra irrigation was meant to accomplish, then {\i ha lo itztrecha} \u8212- it simply was not required, and the {\i tnai} is satisfied. The irrigation was never the point; it was a means to an end. In a {\i get}, however, every {\i tnai} is presumed to be {\i davka}, precisely as stated, because we assume the husband is not in the business of granting the wife any latitude whatsoever.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Hillel Takkana\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hiding Practice\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now introduces a {\i Mishna} from {\i Arachin} about a related phenomenon \u8212- the buyback right in a walled city during the period when {\i yovel} was operative. A seller retained the right to repurchase his house within a year of the sale. If a year passed and the right was not exercised, the sale became permanent. The practice that developed was that the buyer would hide on the last day of the year \u8212- {\i hayah nitman yom shenem asar chodesh kedei sheyehe chalut lo} \u8212- so that the seller could not find him, deposit the money, and thereby exercise the buyback.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Deposit Institution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Hillel instituted a {\i takkana} addressing this abuse: {\i sheyehe cholesh ma\u8217'otav lalishkah} \u8212- the seller could deposit the money in a chamber (apparently at the {\i Beis HaMikdash} or a designated {\i lishkah}), then break down the buyer\u8217's door and reclaim the house. The buyer, wherever he might be hiding, was informed that the money had been deposited and was available for collection whenever he wished.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava\u8217's Inference\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava draws a {\i halachic} inference from Hillel\u8217's {\i takkana}: if a husband writes {\i harei zeh gittech al menas shetitni li masayim zuz} \u8212- \u8220"here is your {\i get} on the condition you give me 200 {\i zuz}\u8221" \u8212- and she gives him the money with his willing acceptance, she is divorced. But what if he refuses to accept it \u8212- {\i al korcho} \u8212- and she throws the money at him, or places it in his home? Rava says she is not divorced. The parallel to Hillel: the fact that Hillel needed to institute a {\i takkana} for the buyer-in-hiding implies that without it, depositing money against someone\u8217's will would not have constituted valid payment. {\i M\u8217'klal d\u8217'be\u8217'alma nethina ba\u8217'al korcho lo havya nethina} \u8212- ordinarily, giving something against a person\u8217's will does not count as giving. Rashi adds that Hillel\u8217's {\i takkana}, whatever its scope, cannot apply to {\i gittin}, because the personal status of a woman as married or divorced cannot be altered by rabbinic enactment the same way monetary matters can.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Pappa Challenge\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Pappa (or, in an alternative formulation, Rav Shimi bar Ashi) challenges Rava\u8217's inference. The premise is that Hillel\u8217's {\i takkana} was necessitated by {\i nesinat ba\u8217'al korcho} \u8212- the unwillingness of the buyer to accept payment. But perhaps, Rav Pappa suggests, Hillel\u8217's {\i takkana} was required solely because the buyer was not physically present \u8212- {\i shelo befanav}. Perhaps, had the buyer been standing there, payment against his will would have been fully effective even without any {\i takkana}. The buyer ran away precisely to avoid the embarrassment of refusing face-to-face, not because refusal would have been legally effective. If so, Hillel addressed only the problem of absence, not of unwillingness.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot explains that the buyer\u8217's motive for hiding was indeed to avoid the social humiliation of standing there and overtly refusing to accept money when the seller had a {\i Torah}-granted right to tender it. The buyer was not hiding because the law empowered him to refuse; he was hiding because the law did not clearly protect him if he refused in person.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ambiguity Remains\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then presents an alternative version of the entire exchange \u8212- {\i v\u8217'ika d\u8217'amri} \u8212- in which Rava himself is the one who began by saying that from Hillel\u8217's {\i takkana} one proves that {\i nesinat ba\u8217'al korcho havya nethina}: giving against someone\u8217's will does work, and Hillel\u8217's {\i takkana} addressed only the problem of physical absence. Rav Pappa then challenges: perhaps even had the buyer been present and refused, it would not have worked, and Hillel\u8217's {\i takkana} covered both scenarios \u8212- presence-and-refusal as well as absence. The {\i gemara} leaves the question unresolved. We do not know definitively whether {\i nesinat ba\u8217'al korcho} \u8212- tendering against someone\u8217's will \u8212- constitutes valid {\i nesinat} in the context of a {\i get} condition. One might argue on principle that in a {\i Torah}-granted right, such as the seller\u8217's right to redeem his house, the right exists regardless of the buyer\u8217's preferences, whereas when a husband creates a {\i tnai} of his own accord, his implicit intent may be that he must willingly accept the money. But the {\i gemara} itself does not resolve the question on those grounds.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Paper & Get\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabban Shimon Rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbah bar bar Chana cites Rabbi Yochanan: {\i kol makom sheshana Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel bemishnasenu halacha kemoso} \u8212- wherever Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel appears in {\i our Mishna} (i.e., the {\i Mishna} of the tractate under study), we rule in accordance with his view \u8212- {\i chutz} from three exceptions: {\i arev} (a {\i Mishna} about guarantors), {\i Tzidan} (our very case, the cloak {\i Mishna}), and {\i raya achrona} (a second debate in {\i Sanhedrin} concerning the deadline for producing evidence). So we do {\i not} rule like Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel in the cloak case of our {\i Mishna}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Paper Condition\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i baraisa} is then cited: {\i harei ze gittech vehaniyar sheli aina megureshes} \u8212- \u8220"here is your {\i get}, but the paper is mine,\u8221" she is not divorced, because she has not received any physical object. She has not taken possession of a {\i get}. However, {\i al menas shetachziri li es haniyar megureshes} \u8212- \u8220"on the condition you return the paper to me,\u8221" she is divorced. The {\i gemara} asks the obvious question: {\i mai shna reisha u\u8217'mai shna seifa} \u8212- what distinguishes the first clause from the second? In the first clause, he keeps the paper outright; in the second, he gives it to her but conditions the divorce on her returning it. If {\i al menas} does not mean {\i me\u8217'achshav} \u8212- from now \u8212- but only takes effect when the condition is fulfilled, then in the second clause, at the moment she returns the paper she no longer holds the {\i get}, and yet it is valid. How?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Chisda Explains\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Chisda offers an explanation: this {\i baraisa} follows Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel, {\i de\u8217'amar titee lo et dameha} \u8212- who holds that the monetary value of the cloak can substitute for the cloak itself. By analogy, even without physically returning the {\i get} parchment, the wife could appease the husband with the monetary value of the paper, and the {\i tnai} is thereby satisfied. Rav Chisda uses the word {\i mifayes} \u8212- appease \u8212- which is notable, as it reintroduces the earlier question of whether payment against one\u8217's will suffices, or whether agreement is required.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Abaye Challenge\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye challenges this explanation sharply: Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel only allowed a substitute when the original object was unavailable \u8212- {\i heicha de\u8217'leisa be\u8217'einei}, where the cloak no longer exists. In such a case, money is a reasonable stand-in. But here, {\i heicha de\u8217'isa be\u8217'einei} \u8212- the paper is still extant. Would Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel really say money substitutes for an object that is right there in hand? The challenge stands, and the {\i gemara} turns to a series of explanations grounded not in the equivalence of substitutes but in the formal rules of {\i tna\u8217'im} \u8212- why the {\i tnai} itself is invalid and therefore does not bind her.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Formal Rules\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Doubling Requirement\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye himself offers a new resolution: {\i Rabbi Meir hi de\u8217'amar beinan tnai kaful} \u8212- the {\i baraisa} follows Rabbi Meir, who requires that any {\i tnai} be stated in doubled form \u8212- {\i v\u8217'hacha lo kafla l\u8217'tna\u8217'ei} \u8212- and here the husband did not double the condition. Rabbi Meir\u8217's position derives from the model of {\i bnei Gad u\u8217'vnei Reuven}: when Moshe laid out their condition for receiving a portion in {\i Ever HaYarden}, the {\i Torah} stated both: if they cross the Jordan, they receive their portion in Canaan, and if they do not cross, they receive their portion there in {\i Ever HaYarden}. Both the positive and negative outcomes were spelled out explicitly. As Dov notes, in propositional logic the statement \u8220"if P then Q\u8221" does not entail \u8220"if not-P then not-Q,\u8221" and Rabbi Meir requires that both possibilities be articulated. Since that doubling was not performed here, the {\i tnai} is invalid \u8212- and an invalid {\i tnai} means the {\i get} takes effect unconditionally.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is a genuinely puzzling moment in the {\i gemara}. The {\i baraisa} is structured to contrast two cases \u8212- the husband who keeps the paper outright versus the husband who conditions return of the paper \u8212- and the implicit contrast suggests they have different {\i halachic} outcomes. Yet Abaye\u8217's resolution essentially collapses that contrast: the reason the second case works is not because the {\i tnai} was satisfied but because the {\i tnai} was never binding to begin with. The {\i chiddush} of the {\i baraisa} turns out to be a separate and entirely independent {\i halacha} about the invalidity of an improperly formulated {\i tnai} \u8212- which is a rather striking way to structure a {\i baraisa}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava Objection\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava challenges Abaye directly: {\i ta\u8217'ama d\u8217'lo kaflia l\u8217'tna\u8217'ei} \u8212- you say the problem is that he did not double the {\i tnai}. But {\i ha kaflia l\u8217'tna\u8217'ei lo havi gitta} \u8212- had he doubled it, the {\i tnai} would have been binding, and the {\i get} would only take effect upon return of the paper. When she returns the paper she no longer holds it, and yet you want the {\i get} to work? Properly doubled, the {\i tnai} creates an insurmountable problem. So the doubling issue alone does not explain the {\i baraisa}\u8217's ruling. Rava therefore turns to a different formal deficiency.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tnai Precedes Act\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Kol tnai me-heicha gamrinan lehu} \u8212- from where do we derive all the rules governing {\i tna\u8217'im}? From the {\i tnai} of {\i bnei Gad u\u8217'vnei Reuven}. {\i Mah hatam tnai kodem l\u8217'maaseh af kol tnai kodem l\u8217'maaseh} \u8212- there, the {\i tnai} preceded the {\i maaseh}: the verse first states \u8220"if they cross and you shall give them their portion,\u8221" and only then the act. So too in every {\i tnai}, the condition must be formulated before the act. Here, however, the husband said {\i harei zeh gittech} \u8212- \u8220"here is your {\i get}\u8221" \u8212- and only afterward added {\i al menas shetachziri li es haniyar} \u8212- \u8220"on the condition you return the paper.\u8221" The {\i maaseh} preceded the {\i tnai}. This renders the {\i tnai} invalid on formal grounds independent of the doubling requirement.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rashi understands {\i tnai kodem l\u8217'maaseh} as a purely formalistic requirement about the order of words \u8212- the condition must be stated first. Some {\i Rishonim} understand it more substantively, but Rashi\u8217's reading is supported by the {\i gemara}\u8217's treatment of the {\i shachiv meira} case below, where the requirement clearly concerns verbal formulation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Different Domains\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Adda bar Ahava presses further: even if we accept that {\i tnai kodem l\u8217'maaseh} explains the invalidity, there is yet another formal deficiency. {\i Mah hatam tnai bidavar echad u\u8217'maaseh bidavar acher} \u8212- in the {\i bnei Gad u\u8217'vnei Reuven} case, the {\i tnai} pertained to one domain (crossing the Jordan to fight) and the {\i maaseh} pertained to a different domain (receiving a portion in {\i Ever HaYarden}). This separateness is required: {\i af kol tnai bidavar echad u\u8217'maaseh bidavar acher}. In our case, however, {\i hatnai ve\u8217'hamaaseh bidavar echad} \u8212- the {\i tnai} and the {\i maaseh} concern the same thing, namely the {\i get} and its paper.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rashi understands this as a prohibition on making a {\i tnai} about the same object as the {\i maaseh} \u8212- one simply cannot condition the {\i get} on something related to the {\i get} itself. Tosafot refines this: the problem is not mere topical overlap but logical self-contradiction. A {\i tnai} whose fulfillment would undermine the very {\i maaseh} it is meant to condition cannot be a valid {\i tnai}, since the entire purpose of a {\i tnai} is to enable the {\i maaseh}. A condition that, if satisfied, destroys what it was meant to activate defeats its own purpose and is therefore not recognized as a {\i tnai} at all.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} thus accumulates multiple independent grounds \u8212- lack of doubling, {\i maaseh} preceding {\i tnai}, and {\i tnai} and {\i maaseh} in the same domain \u8212- each of which independently invalidates the {\i tnai} in the {\i baraisa}. This is also an opportunity for the {\i gemara} to rehearse the full body of {\i mishpatei ha-tna\u8217'im}, the formal rules governing conditions, which derive from the paradigmatic case of {\i bnei Gad u\u8217'vnei Reuven}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Tosafot Categories\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tnai Kaful\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot on {\i daf} 75a takes up the question of how we rule on these formal requirements. Tosafot concludes that the {\i halacha} follows Rabbi Meir and requires {\i tnai kaful} \u8212- the doubled condition \u8212- since Shmuel and Rava in the immediately following {\i sugya} both operate according to Rabbi Meir\u8217's position. As for all the {\i mishnas} in the tractate that deploy {\i al menas} \u8212- the condition that she give 200 {\i zuz}, that she nurse the child, and so on \u8212- Tosafot holds that those cases all involve a {\i tnai kaful}, even though the {\i Mishna} does not spell it out explicitly: {\i af al gav d\u8217'lo peireish}. The {\i gemara} later explains that the {\i tnai} is understood as doubled even when not stated in full. And this applies throughout {\i Shas}: wherever {\i al menas} appears and is assumed to be effective \u8212- for example, the {\i esrog} given {\i al menas l\u8217'hachazir} \u8212- we must assume the {\i tnai} was in fact doubled.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Three Categories\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Yet Tosafot acknowledges that this framework requires careful differentiation. At the end of that {\i Tosafot}, Rabbeinu Yitzchak articulates three distinct categories: {\i gimel inyanim yesh}. First, there are situations that require a fully formal {\i tnai} \u8212- {\i tnai gamur} \u8212- with all the formal rules including {\i tnai kaful} as per Rabbi Meir. Second, there are situations requiring only {\i giluy da\u8217'at} \u8212- disclosure of intent \u8212- without all the formal machinery of a {\i tnai}. Third, there are situations where not even {\i giluy da\u8217'at} is required, because the intent is assumed from context.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Intent & Demands\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This typology resolves what would otherwise be a bewildering proliferation of difficulty: if every {\i al menas} requires {\i tnai kaful} and {\i tnai kodem l\u8217'maaseh} and {\i tnai bidavar acher}, how have so many {\i mishnas} and {\i beraisos} deployed {\i al menas} without apparent concern for these rules? Tosafot\u8217's framework answers: the formal rules apply only when the {\i tnai} is genuinely external to the core {\i da\u8217'at} of the transaction. When a person truly desires to do something \u8212- marry, divorce, buy, sell \u8212- but is in a position to attach external demands, those demands are peripheral to his underlying intent. Because they are peripheral and could theoretically be dispensed with, the {\i Torah} requires that they be formulated with rigorous precision, following all the rules of {\i mishpatei ha-tna\u8217'im}, in order to override what would otherwise be an unconditional {\i maaseh}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 By contrast, when something is so central to a person\u8217's {\i da\u8217'at} that he would not have agreed to the transaction without it, this is not properly a {\i tnai} at all \u8212- it is a fundamental premise of his intent. Such a premise does not require the formal machinery of {\i tna\u8217'im}. It must, however, be articulated; otherwise it falls under {\i d\u8217'varim shebilev einam d\u8217'varim} \u8212- unexpressed inner thoughts have no legal standing.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The third category is {\i d\u8217'varim shebilev kol adam} \u8212- matters so universally understood that no articulation is needed. The classic {\i gemara} example is someone who sold all his possessions {\i adaita lemeisak l\u8217'ara d\u8217'Yisrael} \u8212- with the intention of making {\i aliyah} \u8212- and was subsequently prevented from going. The transactions he undertook in preparation are treated as void, even though he never explicitly conditioned each sale on his {\i aliyah} plans going through. No such articulation was necessary because every bystander understood perfectly well the assumption on which the sales were made.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot\u8217's framework thus creates an organic relationship between legal methodology and the reading of the {\i gemara}\u8217's {\i pshat}. Beginning from the conclusion that the {\i halacha} follows Rabbi Meir on {\i tnai kaful}, Tosafot is impelled to read all the {\i mishnas} as implicitly involving doubled conditions, and then to construct a typology of which cases require those formal conditions at all. The legal methodology and the {\i pshat} reading of the {\i gemara} are not separable enterprises.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Shachiv Meira\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Avoiding Evil Eye\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Shmuel instituted a {\i takkana} for the {\i get} of a {\i shachiv meira} \u8212- a person on his deathbed who wishes to give a {\i get} contingent on his death. The text Shmuel prescribed is: {\i im lo meisi lo yehei get v\u8217'im meisi yehei get} \u8212- \u8220"if I do not die it shall not be a {\i get}, and if I do die it shall be a {\i get}.\u8221" The reason for beginning with the negative \u8212- \u8220"if I do not die\u8221" \u8212- is {\i lo makdmi inish b\u8217'puranusa}: a person does not open by invoking a bad outcome for himself. Beginning with \u8220"if I die\u8221" has the character of {\i ayin hara}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yes Before No\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} challenges: why not simply say {\i lo yehei get im lo meisi} \u8212- \u8220"it shall not be a {\i get} if I do not die\u8221" \u8212- and avoid the need to open with death at all? The answer is {\i be\u8217'ina tnai kodem l\u8217'maaseh} \u8212- the {\i tnai} must precede the {\i maaseh} in formulation. One cannot begin with the {\i maaseh} (\u8220"it shall not be a {\i get}\u8221") and then attach the {\i tnai} afterward.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava then challenges from a different angle: Shmuel\u8217's formulation opens with {\i im lo meisi} \u8212- \u8220"if I do not die\u8221" \u8212- which is the negative case. But the formal requirement derived from {\i bnei Gad u\u8217'vnei Reuven} is {\i hen kodem l\u8217'lav} \u8212- the affirmative must precede the negative. The \u8220"yes\u8221" \u8212- the case where the {\i tnai} is fulfilled \u8212- must come first. Shmuel\u8217's formulation violates this by leading with the negative.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The resolution: Shmuel\u8217's {\i takkana} addresses both requirements simultaneously in a way that avoids {\i ayin hara}. One opens with a statement that is technically the negative ({\i im lo meisi lo yehei get}), which is not the troubling part because it posits the hopeful outcome. Then one proceeds {\i im meisi yehei get v\u8217'im lo meisi lo yehei get} \u8212- stating the affirmative before the negative in the doubled portion, thereby satisfying {\i hen kodem l\u8217'lav}. The opening preliminary statement avoids the need to lead with \u8220"if I die\u8221" while the doubled formulation that follows preserves the formal requirement of affirmative before negative. This {\i sugya} makes clear that {\i tnai kodem l\u8217'maaseh} is indeed a matter of verbal formulation and word order.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Nursing Condition\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Implicit Duration\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Mishna} now turns to conditions regarding service and nursing: {\i harei zeh gittech al menas she-teshamshi es abba} \u8212- \u8220"here is your {\i get} on the condition you attend to my father,\u8221" or {\i al menas she-teniki es beni} \u8212- \u8220"on the condition you nurse my son.\u8221" These conditions are binding. The {\i Mishna} asks: {\i kamah hi menikaso} \u8212- for how long must she nurse? The answer is {\i shtei shanim} \u8212- two years, the conventional nursing duration. Rabbi Yehuda says eighteen months.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Death Exemption\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Mishna} then states that if the child or the father dies \u8212- {\i meis haben o she-meis ha-av} \u8212- the {\i get} remains valid. Rashi understands this to mean that she performed at least one act of nursing or one act of attending to the father, and once the subject of the condition is gone, the {\i tnai} is considered satisfied for its duration. Tosafot rejects this reading as too obvious to require a {\i Mishna}. Tosafot holds that the {\i chiddush} is precisely that she did nothing \u8212- the father or child died before she had an opportunity to attend to them at all \u8212- and yet the {\i get} is valid. This parallels the rain-and-irrigation case discussed earlier: when the purpose of the {\i tnai} has been rendered moot by circumstances, the {\i tnai} is treated as satisfied. That the husband said \u8220"nurse my child\u8221" and she never nursed him, yet the {\i get} holds because there was no longer a child to be nursed \u8212- this is a substantial {\i chiddush}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Explicit Duration\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Mishna} then distinguishes: {\i harei zeh gittech al menas she-teshamshi es abba shtei shanim} \u8212- if he specified a duration of two years \u8212- then even if the father dies or the child dies before the time has elapsed, {\i eino get} \u8212- it is not a valid {\i get}. When a specific duration is stated, that duration is required to be served. The condition is no longer implicitly \u8220"as needed\u8221"; it is a fixed term. Similarly, if the father says {\i ee efshar she-teshamisheni} \u8212- \u8220"I do not wish her to attend to me\u8221" \u8212- without any fault on her part ({\i she-lo b\u8217'kapdah}), the {\i get} is still invalid, because the explicit duration was not satisfied.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabban Shimon Rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel dissents: {\i kol akavah she-einah heimena harei zeh get} \u8212- any failure to fulfill the condition that is not her fault renders the {\i get} valid. The principle is framed around {\i ones} \u8212- external compulsion. If the father refuses her attendance without cause, or if circumstances beyond her control prevent performance, it is as though the condition has been met. This may be understood not merely as a liberal reading of an implicit \u8220"as needed\u8221" provision, but as an application of {\i yesh ones b\u8217'gittin} \u8212- the doctrine that circumstances of compulsion count as fulfillment. Just as Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel was more liberal in the cloak case in accepting substitutes, he is more liberal here in accepting non-performance due to circumstances beyond her control.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Liberal vs.\u160 ?Strict\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 One Day Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i baraisa} is cited that appears to contradict the {\i Mishna}: {\i henikato yom echad shimshato yom echad harei zeh get} \u8212- if she nursed the child for one day or attended to the father for one day, the {\i get} is valid. If no duration was specified, a single day of fulfillment satisfies the {\i tnai}. But the {\i Mishna} said that nursing, even without a stated duration, runs until the child is two years old. The contradiction is direct.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Lenient vs.\u160 ?Strict\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Chisda resolves it: {\i lo kashya ha rabbanan v\u8217'ha Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel} \u8212- the {\i Mishna} and the {\i baraisa} reflect different opinions. The {\i Mishna}, whose opening section does not belong to Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel (since he is only introduced at the {\i Mishna}\u8217's end), follows the {\i rabbanan}. The {\i baraisa} follows Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel, who interprets the satisfaction of {\i tna\u8217'im} more liberally. Since Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel was prepared to accept money in place of a cloak, he is prepared here to accept one day of nursing as satisfying a condition that mentioned no specific duration. The {\i rabbanan}, who demanded the cloak itself, demand the full implied duration.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Interpretation Method\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} refines this further. The issue is not precisely one of leniency versus stringency, but of interpretive approach: do we read a {\i tnai} in a hyper-literal, maximally precise manner, or do we read it in terms of the evident purpose? The {\i rabbanan}, who read {\i tnai} language precisely \u8212- cloak means cloak, no substitute \u8212- also read \u8220"nurse\u8221" to mean \u8220"nurse for the full conventional duration\u8221" even when that duration is unstated. Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel, who accepts functional equivalents, also reads the unstated duration liberally \u8212- one day of nursing fulfills the underlying intent of the condition. The {\i gemara} closes by noting that this framing in terms of hyper-literal versus liberal interpretation is more precise than simply mapping the positions onto leniency and stringency, since those categories shift depending on the case.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Gittin 77\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Im vs.\u160 ?Al Menat\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Opening Cases\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The daf opens by completing the previous perek\u8217's discussion of conditional language in the get, picking up from the mishna on 76b. The mishna presents the case of a husband who says {\i harei zeh gittech im lo bati mikan ad yod bet chodesh} \u8212- \u8220"Here is your get if I do not return from here within twelve months\u8221" \u8212- and then dies within those twelve months. The ruling is {\i eino get}: it is not a valid divorce. The reasoning turns on the word {\i im}. Without the qualifier {\i mehayom} (\u8220"as of now\u8221"), {\i im lo bati} means \u8220"when I do not come\u8221" \u8212- that is, the get is intended to take effect only at the end of twelve months, at which point the husband is already dead. A get that takes effect after death is a {\i get l\u8217'achar mita}, which is invalid.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 By contrast, if the husband said {\i harei zeh gittech mehayom im lo bati mikan ad yod bet chodesh} \u8212- \u8220"as of now, if I do not return\u8221" \u8212- and then died within the twelve months, it is a valid get. Since the condition will certainly be fulfilled and the get was designated as effective from the present moment, the divorce is good.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A third case: the husband instructs others, {\i kitvu utnu get l\u8217'ishti im lo bati mikan ad yod bet chodesh} \u8212- \u8220"Write and give a get to my wife if I do not return within twelve months.\u8221" Here the condition applies not only to the giving of the get but to its very writing. If they wrote it within the twelve months and gave it afterward, the get is invalid \u8212- the instruction was: write it, and give it, when I have not returned, meaning the writing itself was conditioned on the non-return.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The final case in this section is where the husband reversed the order of his statement: {\i kitvu utnu get l\u8217'ishti} first, then {\i im lo bati mikan ad yod bet chodesh}. The first ruling \u8212- that writing it early and giving it late renders it invalid \u8212- is the same. But Rabbi Yossi disagrees in this formulation. He reads the {\i kitvu} as grammatically severed from the conditional clause: \u8220"Write it\u8221" \u8212- then a pause \u8212- \u8220"and give it {\i if} I do not return.\u8221" Because the instruction to write precedes the condition, Rabbi Yossi frees the writing from conditionality. Only the giving is conditional.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The mishna then addresses the case where the husband instructed {\i kitvu v\u8217'chatmu l\u8217'achar yod bet chodesh v\u8217'natnu l\u8217'achar yod bet chodesh} \u8212- write it and give it after twelve months \u8212- and he subsequently died. If the get was given before his death, it is valid; if death preceded the giving, it is not. If the sequence is unknown, she is in a state of {\i safek megureshes} \u8212- doubtfully divorced \u8212- which is, as the mishna acknowledges, the worst of all outcomes.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Yossi & Dating\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara introduces a {\i baraisa}: {\i raboseinu} \u8212- \u8220"our teachers\u8221" \u8212- permitted the woman to remarry even in the case where the husband said simply {\i im lo bati}, without explicitly saying {\i mehayom}. The Gemara asks: who are these {\i raboseinu}? Rav Yehuda citing Shmuel identifies them as the {\i beis din} that permitted the oil of non-Jews \u8212- the court of Rabbi Yehuda Nesiah, known to have been willing to issue lenient rulings and occasionally criticized for it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 How did this {\i beis din} permit her to remarry when the language {\i im lo bati} would seem to mean \u8220"when I do not return,\u8221" yielding a {\i get l\u8217'achar mita}? The Gemara answers: {\i sabri lah k\u8217'Rabbi Yossi} \u8212- they held in accordance with a different Rabbi Yossi, one cited in Bava Basra, who rules {\i zmano shel shtar mochiach alav} \u8212- the date of a document establishes its validity. Rabbi Yossi\u8217's principle is that when a {\i shechiv mera} gives a document that says {\i l\u8217'achar mita}, we read it as {\i mehayom v\u8217'l\u8217'achar mita} because the date written on the document anchors it to the present moment. Applied here: even though the {\i tnai} of {\i im lo bati} was stated verbally and thus might seem to stand on its own as meaning \u8220"when,\u8221" the date inscribed in the get causes us to read it in conjunction with the condition, effectively transforming {\i im} from a temporal \u8220"when\u8221" into a conditional \u8220"if\u8221" effective as of the date.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Abba the son of Rabbi Hiyya bar Abba then quotes Rabbi Yochanan: Rabbi Yehuda Nesiah \u8212- specifically identified as the son of Rabban Gamliel and grandson of Rabbi Yehuda Hanasi \u8212- could not get his colleagues to agree with him on this ruling. Throughout his life, no one from his circle concurred.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Scope of Dating\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abbaye frames the positions of Rabbi Yossi more precisely. Everyone agrees on the two clear cases: if the husband says {\i lich\u8217'she\u8217'tetzei chama min hartika} \u8212- \u8220"when the sun rises\u8221" \u8212- and dies at night, it is unambiguously a {\i get l\u8217'achar mita}, since {\i lich\u8217'she} means \u8220"when.\u8221" If he says {\i al m\u8217'nat she\u8217'tetzei chama min hartika} \u8212- \u8220"on the condition that the sun rises\u8221" \u8212- it is unambiguously a present-moment get with a future condition, because the principle articulated by Rav Huna in the name of Rav is {\i kol ha\u8217'omer al m\u8217'nat k\u8217'omer me\u8217'achshav dami} \u8212- whoever says \u8220"on condition\u8221" speaks as though saying \u8220"as of now.\u8221" The debate of Rabbi Yossi applies exclusively to the word {\i im}. Without {\i zmano shel shtar}, {\i im} means \u8220"when.\u8221" With {\i zmano shel shtar}, Rabbi Yossi holds that {\i im} is read as a conditional \u8220"if,\u8221" and the date supplies the {\i me\u8217'achshav}; his colleagues disagree.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A student raised a sharp point: since {\i zmano shel shtar} \u8212- the date on the get \u8212- records only the day, not the hour, if the get was delivered at night and the husband dies that same night, even according to Rabbi Yossi the date cannot mean \u8220"as of this moment,\u8221" since the date written on the document reflects only the day, which might refer to the daylight hours that follow. This would explain Rashi\u8217's position that when one says {\i lich\u8217'she\u8217'tetzei}, it is categorically a {\i get l\u8217'achar mita} even according to Rabbi Yossi \u8212- because the date, which marks a day, cannot reach back to anchor a nighttime death.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The discussion then turns to a technical ruling: in the formulation {\i kitvu utnu get l\u8217'ishti im lo bati}, Rav Yemar asks Rav Ashi whether Rabbi Yossi might hold that the {\i writing} of a get cannot be made conditional at all \u8212- that {\i ktav get al tnai} is fundamentally invalid. The Gemara rejects this: Rabbi Yossi agrees one can condition the writing. His point is purely grammatical and interpretive. When the husband says {\i kitvu utnu get l\u8217'ishti im lo bati}, Rabbi Yossi reads this as: \u8220"Write it now\u8221" \u8212- and then separately \u8212- \u8220"give it if I do not return.\u8221" The condition attaches only to the proximate verb, the giving. Had he wanted to condition the writing as well, he should have said {\i im lo bati kitvu utnu} \u8212- placing the condition first. The Rabbis disagree and rule that regardless of word order, both the writing and the giving are conditioned on the non-return.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafot: Safek Get\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara raises a question that is left without resolution: once it is known that the husband has died \u8212- and thus that the condition will certainly be fulfilled \u8212- can the wife remarry immediately, or must she wait until the twelve months have elapsed? The question is asked both about the case in the mishna (where {\i mehayom} was stated explicitly) and about the case of Rabbi Yehuda Nesiah\u8217's court (where the date of the document supplies the {\i me\u8217'achshav}). The Gemara notes that the question on the {\i beis din} case was posed to an elder who had actually participated in such a ruling, since a practical ruling would presumably have included thinking through its parameters \u8212- which makes the question more pointed than merely a theoretical one about the mishna. Nevertheless, the Gemara offers no answer.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot addresses this question directly. Tosafot asks: {\i le\u8217'alter mah yesh lachosh} \u8212- if he has died, what is there to worry about? He will not return. Tosafot answers that there is a concern about appearances. People are aware that the condition was \u8220"if he does not return in twelve months,\u8221" and if they observe the woman marrying immediately, they will not know he has died and will conclude that the twelve-month condition need not be waited out in general \u8212- creating a false impression about how conditional divorces work. This is therefore a rabbinic-level concern about {\i mar\u8217'it ayin} or improper inference, not a question about the mechanism of conditional fulfillment.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot also addresses a further point: even if one holds like Rabbi Yossi that {\i zmano shel shtar mochiach alav}, the result in the case where {\i im} is combined with the date of the document is at best a {\i safek get} \u8212- a doubtful divorce \u8212- not a definite one. This is because there remains a question whether the date alone is sufficient to convert {\i im} from a temporal \u8220"when\u8221" to a conditional \u8220"if.\u8221" Moreover, Tosafot notes that the question of whether we rule like Rabbi Yossi in these cases remains genuinely open. Even though it is reported that his colleagues did not agree with him, one cannot prove from that historical fact that the {\i halacha} rejects his position. Rabbi Yochanan, who reports that Rabbi Yehuda Nesiah could not convince his colleagues, may himself have agreed with Rabbi Yossi. And even if there is a basis to reject Rabbi Yossi where the {\i tnai} is external and verbal while the date is in the document, one might still apply his principle where both the date and the condition appear within the document itself.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Timing Language\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 After = Distance\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 With the transition to a new perek, the Gemara turns from questions about the content of conditional language to questions about temporal language \u8212- specifically, what it means to say that a document takes effect \u8220"after\u8221" ({\i l\u8217'achar}) a given unit of time.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i baraisa} establishes the principle through a series of cases, each demonstrating that \u8220"after\u8221" does not mean immediately after but rather fully separated from. If one says a document takes effect {\i l\u8217'achar hashavua} \u8212- after the seven-year shmitta cycle \u8212- it takes effect a full {\i shana}, year, afterward. If one says {\i l\u8217'achar hashana} \u8212- after this year \u8212- it takes effect a {\i chodesh}, a month, into the following year. If one says {\i l\u8217'achar hachodesh} \u8212- after the month \u8212- it takes effect a full {\i Shabbos}, a week, later. And if one says {\i l\u8217'achar Shabbos} \u8212- after this Shabbat \u8212- the question arises: one day later?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Zeira, and some say Rav Assi before Rabbi Yochanan, rules that Sunday, Monday, and Tuesday are still {\i batar Shabbos} \u8212- in the wake of the preceding Shabbat, still connected to it. One can still recite {\i havdala} in those days; one is still in the gravitational field of that Shabbat. Wednesday, Thursday, and Friday are already {\i kamei Shabbos} \u8212- oriented toward the coming Shabbat. The idea throughout is that \u8220"after\u8221" means when one is genuinely distant from the unit in question \u8212- when one is no longer in its shadow or afterglow. Just as someone writing a check in early January might still write the previous year out of habit, the period immediately following a major unit of time is still considered to be \u8220"of\u8221" that unit, not fully beyond it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi: 30 Days\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i baraisa} teaches in the name of Rabbi: {\i l\u8217'achar haregel shloshim yom} \u8212- \u8220"after the festival\u8221" means thirty days after the conclusion of Yom Tov. The festival\u8217's influence is understood to extend across an entire month.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Attribution Issues\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Hiyya went out and taught this ruling in the name of Rabbi, and he was praised for doing so. When he subsequently taught the same ruling as though it represented a majority opinion rather than Rabbi\u8217's individual position, he was not praised \u8212- one does not present a {\i da\u8217'at yachid} as a consensus. The ruling, it seems, is not followed: the festival\u8217's shadow does not actually extend thirty days.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This prompts a brief observation: two instances appear in close proximity in this {\i sugya} where a rabbinic position fails to gain acceptance. Earlier, Rabbi Yehuda Nesiah could not convince his colleagues of Rabbi Yossi\u8217's principle regarding {\i zmano shel shtar}. Here, Rabbi\u8217's ruling on thirty days after the festival is likewise not accepted. In each case, the question of the correct {\i halacha} remains open \u8212- the non-acceptance of a position in a historical sense does not automatically determine the ruling, as Tosafot noted above.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Giving the Get\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishna Cases\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The new chapter opens with a mishna addressing how the get must physically be transferred to the wife. The central case is {\i hazorek get l\u8217'ishto vehi betoch beisah o betoch chatzerah} \u8212- if a man throws a get to his wife while she is in her house or in her yard, she is divorced. The get has landed in her domain, and that suffices. But if he threw it while they are in his house or his yard \u8212- even if she is lying beside him in bed \u8212- she is not divorced. The domain in which the get lands determines validity; his domain does not count as her receiving it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 However, if the get landed {\i betoch cheikah} \u8212- on her body, in her lap or bosom \u8212- or into her {\i kaltah}, her basket or purse, she is divorced even if they are standing in his domain. The get reached her person, not merely her property.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kinyan vs.\u160 ?Transfer\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This mishna raises a fundamental conceptual question: is the act of giving a get an act of {\i kinyan} \u8212- of transferring legal ownership in a halakhically defined sense \u8212- or is it fundamentally a {\i ma\u8217'aseh netinah}, an act of physical giving? The question has practical implications. If it is a {\i kinyan}, then the conditions for valid transfer must mirror those of ordinary acquisition: the object must be capable of being owned, the domain must belong to the recipient, and so on. If it is an act of giving, perhaps more physical or interpersonal factors control validity rather than the formal mechanics of property transfer.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One test case mentioned earlier in the tractate is a get written on {\i issurei hana\u8217'ah} \u8212- objects from which benefit is forbidden. Some hold that such objects cannot be legally possessed at all, existing as it were outside the framework of ownership. If a get on such an object is valid, that would suggest the act is one of giving rather than of kinyan. But since other opinions hold that {\i issurei hana\u8217'ah} can in fact be possessed, the test case is not decisive. The question remains genuinely open, and the {\i sugya} here is best read as probing precisely this tension.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Derasha\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara asks: from where do we derive that the wife\u8217's roof, yard, and {\i karfif} \u8212- a smaller enclosure \u8212- can serve as a valid location for receiving the get? The verse says {\i ve-natan beyadah} \u8212- \u8220"and he shall give it into her hand.\u8221" One might have thought only the hand is valid. The Gemara answers with a {\i derasha}: {\i talmud lomar ve-natan mikol makom} \u8212- the formulation of {\i ve-natan} by itself, separable from {\i beyadah}, extends the act of giving to any of her domains. Rashi explains that the verse could have written {\i beyadah yitnenah}, making the hand the exclusive object; by writing {\i ve-natan beyadah} the Torah allows {\i ve-natan} to be expounded independently.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A parallel {\i derasha} appears in the laws of theft. The verse {\i im himatzei timatzei beyado ha-geneivah} \u8212- \u8220"if the stolen object is found in his hand\u8221" \u8212- might suggest liability only when the thief holds the object directly. But the same {\i midrash} style extends liability to the thief\u8217's roof, yard, and {\i karfif} as well: {\i talmud lomar himatzei timatzei mikol makom}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Get vs.\u160 ?Theft\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then establishes that both {\i derashot} are necessary \u8212- one cannot be inferred from the other. If we had only the case of the get, one might think the wife\u8217's domain works because the husband divorces her against her will ({\i be\u8217'al korcha megarshah}) \u8212- the husband has greater power in the divorce context, so perhaps a broader range of situations counts as \u8220"giving to her.\u8221" But for theft, where there is no such coercive power dynamic, one might have thought property acquisition would require more direct action. Conversely, if we had only the case of the thief, one might think his domain is implicated because the Torah penalized him \u8212- the rabbinic expansion of liability could reflect the punitive context \u8212- while for the get, perhaps the domain rule would not apply.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rashi sharpens the novelty in the theft case. The obvious case \u8212- where the thief actively picked up the object and brought it to his yard \u8212- requires no {\i derasha} at all: the thief performed a direct act of {\i meshichah}, of drawing the object toward him, and is clearly liable. What the {\i derasha} comes to teach is the case where, say, a cow wandered into the thief\u8217's yard on its own, and the thief decided not to return it and locked the gate. He performed no direct act on the object. The {\i chiddush} is that closing the gate \u8212- a passive act relative to the object itself \u8212- nonetheless constitutes an act of {\i geneivah}. Just as if a baseball were thrown and accidentally landed in someone\u8217's hands and he refused to return it \u8212- that constitutes theft under this {\i derasha}. The willingness of the Torah to define such a passive situation as possession for purposes of theft liability is itself a striking {\i chiddush}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Husband Rights\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rights Problem\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now asks the pointed question: the mishna says that throwing the get into the wife\u8217's yard effects a divorce. But {\i mah she\u8217'kaneta isha kanah ba\u8217'alah} \u8212- whatever the wife acquires, her husband acquires. So if the get lands in her yard, the yard\u8217's acquisitions belong to the husband \u8212- which means the get immediately becomes his property, not hers. How then does it constitute giving it to her?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 As noted above, there are two ways to frame this problem. One reading focuses on the mechanics of ownership: when the get lands in her yard, it transfers to the husband. But this framing invites the response that surely he could waive those rights in advance. The other reading focuses on the nature of the yard itself: since the husband has full rights to the {\i peiros} \u8212- the produce and fruits \u8212- of his wife\u8217's property, the yard is not genuinely \u8220"hers\u8221" in the sense required for the verse {\i ve-natan beyadah}. On this reading, the question is not about who takes possession of the get, but about whether the yard can count as an extension of her person when he has such deep claims upon it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The latter framing is more conceptually powerful. If we understand {\i ve-natan beyadah} as requiring that the get be given to {\i her} \u8212- to her as a person, to her domain \u8212- then a yard over which the husband has concurrent rights is not fully her domain. The analogy to the hand makes this vivid: giving something into her hand gives it to her because the hand is unambiguously her person. But a yard in which the husband has {\i kinyan peiros} \u8212- or perhaps even {\i kinyan haguf} on some views \u8212- is not unambiguously hers.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 There is a broader halakhic backdrop here. The question of {\i kinyan peiros k\u8217'kinyan haguf dami} \u8212- whether the right to produce is equivalent to ownership of the body of the property \u8212- bears directly on this. If it is, then in a meaningful sense the husband is the owner of the yard during the marriage, and putting the get in that yard is like putting it in his own yard. If it is not \u8212- if {\i kinyan peiros} is merely a limited usufruct right \u8212- then the wife retains underlying ownership and the yard may still count as hers. Tosafot engages at length with these issues.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Waiving Rights\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Elazar proposes that the case works when the husband has written her a document stating {\i din u\u8217'dvarim ein li benikhsayikh} \u8212- \u8220"I have no claim or dispute against your property.\u8221" By waiving his rights in writing, he removes himself from the yard, leaving it fully hers.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara objects: a {\i baraisa} teaches that if a business partner tells his partner {\i din u\u8217'dvarim ein li al sadeh zu, v\u8217'ein li esek bo, yadi mesuleket heimena} \u8212- \u8220"I have no claim on this field, I have nothing to do with it, my hand is removed from it\u8221" \u8212- {\i lo amar klum}, it is legally meaningless. Rights and ownership are distinct categories. One can disclaim an adversarial claim ({\i din u\u8217'dvarim}) without transferring or relinquishing one\u8217's underlying ownership interest. To give up ownership, one must actually transfer it \u8212- a mere declaration of non-claim is insufficient. The same would apply to the husband\u8217's rights in the wife\u8217's yard.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Timing of Waiver\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The answer proposed by {\i d\u8217'vei Rabbi Yannai} is that the case works when the husband wrote the waiver {\i v\u8217'odah arusah} \u8212- while she was still betrothed, before the {\i nissuin}. The husband\u8217's rights to {\i kinyan peiros} in the wife\u8217's property do not attach until the {\i nissuin}. Therefore, if before the {\i nissuin} he declared in writing that he is waiving any rights he would otherwise receive over the fruits of her property, he never acquires those rights in the first place.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The mechanism is explained through two principles. First, {\i nachlah haba\u8217'ah lo l\u8217'adam mimakom acher} \u8212- an inheritance or benefit coming from an external source, not a biblical default of succession, can be declined in advance. A child cannot preemptively disclaim an inheritance from a parent because the Torah automatically transfers it; but a right that flows from rabbinic institution, arriving from an external source in a contingent way, can be refused before it takes effect. Second, Rava\u8217's principle: {\i ha\u8217'omer ee efshi b\u8217'takkanat chakhamim k\u8217'gon zu shom\u8217'in lo} \u8212- if someone declares that he does not want a rabbinic {\i takkanah} that was enacted for his benefit, we listen to him. Since the right of {\i kinyan peiros} was enacted as a rabbinic benefit for the husband, he may decline it before the marriage creates the entitlement.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Huna in the name of Rav provides the paradigm: a wife may say to her husband {\i eini nizonet v\u8217'eini osah} \u8212- I will not be supported by you and I will not labor for you \u8212- because the arrangement of support in exchange for labor was enacted fundamentally for her benefit. She may therefore decline it. This refusal can even be toggled on and off during the marriage, since the arrangement is ongoing and personal.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The case of {\i kinyan peiros}, however, differs in an important respect. Once the {\i nissuin} occurs and the husband receives his {\i kinyan} in the wife\u8217's property, he cannot undo it merely by saying {\i din u\u8217'dvarim ein li} \u8212- that is the Gemara\u8217's earlier objection. To restore the property rights to her he would need to perform an actual {\i makneh}, a proper act of transferring ownership back. But before the {\i nissuin}, he can simply refuse the {\i takkanat chakhamim}, in which case he never acquires the right at all, and the yard remains unambiguously hers, allowing the get placed therein to count as given to her.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ba\u8217'im K\u8217'Echad\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava, however, proposes a fundamentally different solution \u8212- one that does not require any prior waiver. {\i Atu yadah mi lo kanya leh l\u8217'baal?} \u8212- Is the hand of the wife not also, in a sense, \u8220"owned\u8221" by the husband? His rights to her labor might be understood as a kind of claim on her hand itself. Yet when the get is placed in her hand, it works. Why? Because {\i gito v\u8217'yadah ba\u8217'im k\u8217'echad} \u8212- the get and her hand come into effect simultaneously.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The insight is this: it is true that while the marriage subsists, the husband has rights to her hand through his entitlement to her labor, and rights to her yard through {\i kinyan peiros}. But the get and the divorce take effect at the same moment. At the instant the get is received, the divorce occurs \u8212- and at that same instant, her hand and her yard become entirely hers, free of any claim. The moment of reception and the moment of divorce are not sequential; they are simultaneous. Therefore, at the very moment that matters \u8212- the moment of reception \u8212- her hand and her yard belong to her fully.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Ravina then sharpens a question against Rava\u8217's formulation: granted, there is a meaningful analogy between the yard and the hand regarding simultaneity, but the cases are not truly parallel. The husband does not \u8220"own\u8221" her hand in any proprietary sense \u8212- his right to her labor ({\i ma\u8217'aseh yadeha}) is not the same as ownership of the hand as a physical object. Yet with the yard, the husband has something closer to a property interest. Is Rava\u8217's proof from the hand of the wife really compelling enough to extend to the yard?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava responds that his actual proof was never from the hand of the wife. It was from the hand of an {\i eved Kena\u8217'ani} \u8212- a Canaanite slave. The slave is legally property: {\i yad eved k\u8217'yad rabbo damya}, the slave\u8217's hand is like the master\u8217's hand. Yet the Gemara accepts that one can free a slave by handing him his writ of manumission directly \u8212- {\i le-man de-amar bi-shtar al yadei atzmo}. Why should that work? The slave\u8217's hand is entirely the master\u8217's property. The only possible answer is {\i gito v\u8217'yado ba\u8217'im k\u8217'echad} \u8212- the writ of manumission and the slave\u8217's hand become free simultaneously. At the moment of reception, the slave is freed, and at that same moment the hand is his own. The simultaneity principle is thus established on solid ground, independent of any ambiguity about the wife\u8217's hand.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 From this principle, the application to the wife\u8217's yard follows: {\i hacha nami gito v\u8217'chatzerah ba\u8217'im k\u8217'echad} \u8212- here too, the get and her yard come into effect as one.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Eved Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Freeing Eved\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The case of the {\i eved Kena\u8217'ani} thus serves as the clearest proof of the {\i ba\u8217'im k\u8217'echad} principle. The slave is unambiguously property \u8212- his hand belongs to his master in a much more complete sense than the wife\u8217's hand or yard belong to her husband. There is no rabbinic {\i takkanah} to waive, no prior condition to arrange. The master simply hands the {\i shtar shichrur} to the slave, and it works. The only coherent explanation is that the act of freeing and the conferral of legal personhood upon the slave occur simultaneously \u8212- the hand that receives the document is, at that very instant, the hand of a free person.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Divorce Timing\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The same logic then resolves the problem of the wife\u8217's yard. Just as the slave\u8217's hand becomes legally his at the moment of reception, the wife\u8217's yard becomes legally hers at the moment the get is received. The husband\u8217's {\i kinyan peiros}, whatever its scope, is extinguished at the instant of the divorce \u8212- and since the divorce and the reception are simultaneous, the yard is hers at the relevant moment. {\i Gito v\u8217'chatzerah ba\u8217'im k\u8217'echad}. This is Rava\u8217's answer, and it operates independently of the solution proposed by {\i d\u8217'vei Rabbi Yannai}, which required a specific prior waiver.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Shabbat Deathbed\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Case Setup\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The final {\i sugya} on this daf presents a vivid practical scenario: a {\i shechiv mera} \u8212- a person on his deathbed \u8212- wrote a get for his wife on the eve of Shabbat but did not manage to give it to her before Shabbat came in. On Shabbat, his condition deteriorated severely. He needed to transfer the get urgently, both to spare her the ordeal of {\i yibbum} and because it was unclear how long he would survive.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The immediate problem is identified by the {\i rishonim} in accordance with Rashi\u8217's framing: the get is {\i muktzeh}. Giving a get or performing {\i kiddushin} on Shabbat is normally prohibited as a rabbinic restriction, but the {\i poskim} note that a {\i shechiv mera} is given latitude to perform acts on Shabbat that would ordinarily be impermissible \u8212- since we do everything possible to ease his distress. Therefore the concern is not the act of divorce per se on Shabbat, but the {\i muktzeh} status of the get document itself, which cannot simply be handed over.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kinyan Solution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 They brought the question before Rava. Rava suggested: tell the {\i shechiv mera} to transfer ownership of the small piece of property on which the get is resting to the wife \u8212- {\i le-kanyei nihalah le-hahu duchsah de-yasiv beih gitta}. Then let her go and perform {\i kinyan chazakah} on that spot: {\i tisgor v\u8217'tiftach} \u8212- let her lock and open the enclosure \u8212- since the mishna and {\i baraisa} teach that locking a gate, closing a fence, or breaching one as needed constitutes {\i chazakah} on land \u8212- {\i na\u8217'al, gader, u-faratz, kol she-hu l\u8217'tikkuna, harei zu chazakah}. By acquiring the {\i makom} \u8212- the physical spot \u8212- through {\i chazakah}, she thereby acquires the get that sits upon it, since an object resting in one\u8217's {\i chatzer} is acquired along with the {\i chatzer} itself.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kinyan Problem\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 An immediate objection is raised: this solution seems to trade one rabbinic prohibition for another. If the problem is {\i muktzeh} \u8212- a rabbinic restriction \u8212- how does performing a {\i kinyan} on Shabbat, which is itself rabbinically prohibited, constitute a solution? The Ran addresses this by suggesting that the {\i kinyan chazakah} performed through opening and closing a door is experientially innocuous \u8212- it feels like an ordinary domestic act, not like a formal {\i kinyan} \u8212- whereas {\i muktzeh} involves an overt act of handling the forbidden object. The She\u8217'iltos offers a different explanation: the case is actually one where the get is located across a {\i reshut ha-rabbim} \u8212- a public domain \u8212- in another house, making it physically impossible to bring the get to the dying man on Shabbat. The problem is therefore not {\i muktzeh} per se but inaccessibility, and Rava\u8217's solution of having the wife acquire the spot through {\i kinyan chazakah} in the remote location is the only available mechanism.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Arusa Not Nesua\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava\u8217's solution was met with embarrassment when Rav Ilish pointed out the objection discussed above: {\i mah she-kaneta isha kanah ba\u8217'alah} \u8212- the husband would immediately acquire whatever she acquires, so her {\i kinyan} of the property would revert to him and the get sitting on it would become his. Rava was taken aback \u8212- {\i ikhsif}, he was embarrassed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But then it emerged that the circumstances of the case were different from what had been assumed: the woman was an {\i arusa}, merely betrothed, not yet a {\i nesua}. The rule of {\i mah she-kaneta isha kanah ba\u8217'alah} and the husband\u8217's {\i kinyan peiros} apply only after {\i nissuin}; they do not apply to an {\i arusa}. Therefore, Rava had not actually made an error \u8212- the specific facts of the case meant that his solution was valid after all, since the {\i arusat}\u8217's property was entirely her own.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Final Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Nevertheless, the Gemara records that Rava then retracted even this concession and reaffirmed his position without qualification: {\i lo shena arusa v\u8217'lo shena nesua \u8212- gita v\u8217'chatzerah ba\u8217'im k\u8217'echad}. Whether she is betrothed or married, the principle of simultaneity applies. The get and her domain \u8212- including a domain she acquires in the very act \u8212- come into effect as one.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then notes that it had cited Rava\u8217's ruling of {\i gito v\u8217'chatzerah ba\u8217'im k\u8217'echad} earlier in the {\i sugya}, seemingly before this story was told. It explains: {\i mei-ikara ki amar Rava hai ma\u8217'isa amrah} \u8212- when Rava first formulated the principle of {\i ba\u8217'im k\u8217'echad}, it was this very story that gave rise to it. The deathbed Shabbat case was the original occasion for Rava\u8217's insight. What was presented earlier in the {\i sugya} as Rava\u8217's ruling was, chronologically, a conclusion that emerged from this episode \u8212- the story is the origin, and the earlier citation anticipated the result.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Gittin 78\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Get Delivery Overview\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Daf Identification\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Today\u8217's daf is 78, and we pick up in the middle of 77b \u8212- {\i v\u8217'hi b\u8217'toch beito} \u8212- dealing with the question of the act of giving of the {\i get}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kinyan vs.\u160 ?Act of Giving\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} discusses giving the {\i get} directly to the woman \u8212- either on her body, as in her hand, or in her {\i chatzer}. The question that will continue for some time is: to what degree are we trying to define something that constitutes a classic act of {\i kinyan}, of taking possession of an object? And in what sense might this instead be a different definition entirely \u8212- a physical act of giving, distinct from acquisition? Complicating matters further, a {\i get} can be forced upon the wife, which makes this unlike a standard {\i kinyan} scenario.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two Models of Transfer\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} had raised the question of why giving the {\i get} into her {\i chatzer} should count at all. The principle {\i kanta isha kanta ba\u8217'alah} \u8212- \u8220"what the wife acquires, the husband acquires\u8221" \u8212- can be framed in two ways: either because her taking possession transfers to the husband, which is the {\i kinyan} model, or because the {\i Gemara}\u8217's deeper concern is why the {\i chatzer} is considered part of her in the first place, given that the husband has rights to it \u8212- {\i kinyan peiros} \u8212- and it is therefore not analogous to her hand.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava\u8217's resolution is {\i gita v\u8217'chatzeira ba\u8217'in k\u8217'echad}: we look a moment into the future and recognize that as a result of the {\i get}, the {\i chatzer} will become fully hers. That future reality renders the current delivery valid. This is the {\i chiddush} of {\i v\u8217'natan b\u8217'yadah} \u8212- that giving it into her property, and not only into her hand, constitutes giving it to her. With these two models in view \u8212- the act of giving versus the {\i ma\u8217'aseh kinyan} \u8212- we proceed.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Chatzer & Mishtameret\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishna Emphasis\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} states {\i v\u8217'hi b\u8217'toch beitah} \u8212- she was standing in her house. The {\i mishna} emphasizes her actual physical location within her house or yard. Why?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ulla\u8217's Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Ulla holds: {\i v\u8217'hi she\u8217'omedet b\u8217'tzad beitah, b\u8217'tzad chatzerah} \u8212- she must be physically on the property itself, just as the {\i mishna} implies.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Oshaya\u8217's Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Oshaya disagrees: {\i afilu shehi b\u8217'Tveria v\u8217'chatzerah b\u8217'Tzipori, hi b\u8217'Tzipori v\u8217'chatzerah b\u8217'Tveria} \u8212- even if she is in a different city entirely from her yard, it does not matter. She is {\i megureshet}; it works.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} immediately presses: but the {\i mishna} says {\i v\u8217'hi b\u8217'toch beitah b\u8217'toch chatzerah} \u8212- she is inside her house, inside her yard? The {\i Gemara} answers: {\i hachi ka\u8217'amar} \u8212- what the {\i mishna} means is {\i v\u8217'hi k\u8217'mi sheb\u8217'toch beitah, v\u8217'hi k\u8217'mi sheb\u8217'toch chatzerah}: wherever she is, it is as if she is in her yard. This is a strikingly forced reading of the {\i mishna} \u8212- if her physical presence is fundamentally not required, why would the {\i mishna} need to tell us that it is \u8220"as if\u8221" she were there? It is a very weak read, and the {\i Gemara} itself seems to acknowledge the difficulty.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishtameret Principle\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} then provides the conceptual foundation: {\i d\u8217'keivan d\u8217'chatzer mishtameret l\u8217'da\u8217'atah hi, mitgoreshet} \u8212- since the {\i chatzer} is guarded under her auspices, she is divorced. The requirement is not physical presence but rather that the yard is {\i mishtameret l\u8217'da\u8217'atah} \u8212- watched under her watch, through her, and not through someone else\u8217's control.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What would it mean for a yard to be guarded {\i not} under her auspices? The {\i Gemara} will address this later with the case of her slave. If the {\i get} is placed in the hand of her awake slave, even though the slave is her property, it is the slave who is fundamentally the watcher and controller of the object \u8212- not her. By contrast, if a person hires a watchman for their yard, the yard is still watched {\i through} the owner\u8217's arrangement and considered under their control. But a slave\u8217's body is his own, and so he is seen as the primary one watching and controlling whatever is placed on him. In any case other than this, the property is considered {\i mishtameret l\u8217'da\u8217'atah} and the {\i get} is valid even if she is not physically present.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chatzer Models Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} then raises the broader theoretical question: {\i leima b\u8217'ha kamipalgi} \u8212- perhaps this dispute between Ulla and Oshaya maps onto the general debate in all of {\i Shas} about the nature of {\i kinyan chatzer}. Is {\i chatzer} effective because it is like the owner\u8217's {\i yad} \u8212- an extension of the person \u8212- or is it effective because of {\i shlichus} \u8212- because it represents the owner\u8217's interests?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If {\i chatzer} works because it is like a {\i yad}, then just as a hand is attached to the person, the {\i chatzer} must be in physical proximity to the person, and Ulla\u8217's requirement that she stand on the property follows naturally. If {\i chatzer} works on the {\i shlichus} model, representing the owner\u8217's interests, then physical presence is unnecessary, and Oshaya\u8217's position makes sense.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might further argue that by {\i get} specifically, even if in other contexts {\i chatzer} works via {\i shlichus}, here it must work on the {\i yad} model \u8212- because the {\i chiddush} of {\i natan b\u8217'yadah} seems to require something analogous to her hand, and a {\i get} is not given through a {\i shliach} in the usual sense.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chatzer Resolution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} resolves otherwise. {\i D\u8217'chulei alma chatzer mishum yad itrabai} \u8212- everyone agrees that {\i chatzer} is conceptually subsumed under {\i yad}. The debate is about what it means to be a {\i yad}. One view holds {\i k\u8217'yadah} literally: just as the hand is immediately adjacent to and attached to the body, so the {\i chatzer} must be spatially close, requiring her to stand on the property. The other view pushes back: if you insist on that level of physicality, you would require her to be {\i glued} to the yard \u8212- which is absurd. {\i Ela k\u8217'yadah} must mean something more conceptual: just as something in my hand is something I watch, protect, and control, {\i af chatzer mishtameret l\u8217'da\u8217'atah} \u8212- anything under my auspices and protected through me is considered like my hand. This is not a question of physical extension but of conceptual control. {\i L\u8217'afukei chatzer mishtameret she-lo l\u8217'da\u8217'atah} \u8212- the only exclusion is a {\i chatzer} watched under someone else\u8217's control, like the case of the awake slave.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Hand & Vessel Principle\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Physical Proximity Model\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The debate over what constitutes {\i yad} surfaces in two directions. One view treats {\i yad} as a physical, bodily concept \u8212- something on my person, attached to me, immediately adjacent to my body. On this model, the {\i chatzer} functions as a {\i yad} only when it is physically continuous with the person in some meaningful sense.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yad: Physical vs.\u160 ?Concep\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The two interpretations of {\i yad} map onto this debate directly. {\i Mah yadah b\u8217'sumucha} \u8212- just as her hand is next to her, attached to her, so the {\i chatzer} she must be adjacent to the {\i chatzer} for it to serve as an extension of her. The second view: {\i im mah yadah b\u8217'dvuka af chatzer b\u8217'dvuka} \u8212- if you take the analogy that far, you would require her to be literally connected to the yard, which is untenable. Therefore, we must read {\i yad} conceptually.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Control Model\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The conceptual reading grounds the {\i yad} principle in power and dominion rather than physical proximity. The metaphor of {\i hand} in Tanach itself points in this direction \u8212- the idea of {\i yad} as representing control and sovereign power: {\i af yadi yasdah aretz} \u8212- \u8220"my hand founded the earth.\u8221" Something under her power and control is considered her {\i yad}. This is the principle of {\i mishtameret l\u8217'da\u8217'atah} \u8212- not as a physical proximity test but as a control test.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is the position the {\i Gemara} ultimately endorses: {\i chatzer} as {\i yad} operates on the conceptual model of control and protection. Anything guarded and overseen through her is her {\i yad}; anything overseen by someone else \u8212- like the awake slave who is himself the primary controller \u8212- falls outside that category.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might ask: what is the actual {\i halacha} on whether she must stand in the {\i chatzer}? The Rambam in {\i Hilchot Gerushin} 5 rules that she must be present: {\i im hayta omedet sham b\u8217'tzad chatzerah nitgareshet v\u8217'im lav lo nitgarsha ad she\u8217'ta\u8217'amod b\u8217'tzad chatzerah} \u8212- if she was standing beside her yard she is divorced, and if not, she is not divorced until she stands beside it \u8212- {\i v\u8217'af al pi she-hi chatzer she-mishtameret haget b\u8217'tochah}, even though the {\i chatzer} is guarded and the {\i get} is secured within it. The Rambam frames this not in terms of the {\i yad} requirement but around the inability of {\i chatzer} to function as a {\i shliach} against her interests \u8212- {\i she-chov hu lah}, divorce is a detriment to her, {\i v\u8217'ein chavin l\u8217'adam ela b\u8217'fanav}, and one cannot impose a detriment on a person in their absence. Interestingly, the Rambam rules differently for other {\i halachot} \u8212- in {\i Hilchot Aveidah} 17:10, it appears that {\i chatzer} works for {\i kinyan} without one\u8217's physical presence, presumably because those contexts are to the person\u8217's benefit and can function on the {\i shlichus} model.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This creates an interesting distinction: by {\i isha} \u8212- by the wife receiving a {\i get} \u8212- the {\i Gemara} sounds strongly as though the {\i yad} model is operative, as several {\i gemarot} confirm. By {\i kinyanim} generally, it may operate on the {\i shliach} model. And within the {\i yad} model itself, the debate remains: is the test physical adjacency, or conceptual control?\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Borrowed Space Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Get on Wood Piece\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A certain man threw a {\i get} to his wife. She was standing in a yard, and the {\i get} rolled away and fell on a piece of wood. Rav Yosef ruled: {\i chazina} \u8212- we examine the situation. If the piece of wood is four {\i amos} by four {\i amos}, it constitutes its own domain \u8212- {\i palgei reshuta lenafshah} \u8212- and she is not divorced. If not, it is all one domain and the {\i get} reaches her.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Space Boundaries\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} immediately challenges this: {\i baheichi askinan} \u8212- what exactly is the case? If it is her {\i chatzer}, then what difference does it make if the piece of wood is four {\i amos}? It is in her yard and should work regardless. {\i Ela b\u8217'chatzer dideih} \u8212- the case must be that it is {\i his} yard. But then if the piece of wood is less than four {\i amos}, why does it work at all? It is his yard.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Lending Space Concept\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} answers: {\i lo tzricha de\u8217'ushlamah makom} \u8212- the case is that he lent her the space. He is throwing her a {\i get} and lending her the space to use for taking possession of it. This raises considerable questions. Must she do a {\i kinyan} on the space itself for it to become hers? The {\i Gemara} does not elaborate on what form of acquisition is required. The principle that rented or borrowed space can serve as one\u8217's domain for {\i kinyan} purposes is itself a {\i chiddush}, analogous to discussions about the {\i shechiv mera} who appears to have lent a space to be used. What constitutes sufficient ownership of property to function as {\i yad} for {\i gittin} remains an open question that the {\i Gemara} leaves imprecise.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The logic of the four-{\i amot}-or-not analysis is this: when he lends her a space, he lends her {\i one} space \u8212- the specific area he designated. If the piece of wood is four by four {\i amos}, it constitutes a distinct, separate domain within the larger area. Having lent her {\i one} space, he did not implicitly lend her two spaces. The {\i get} now rests on the second space \u8212- the piece of wood \u8212- which is not hers, and she is not divorced.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Four Amot & Names\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Two further refinements follow. {\i V\u8217'lo amran ela delo gavo\u8217'ah asarah} \u8212- all of this, the rule that a piece of wood less than four by four {\i amos} is treated as part of the same domain, applies only when the piece of wood is less than ten {\i tefachim} off the ground. If it is higher than ten {\i tefachim}, even if it is not four by four {\i amos}, it constitutes its own separate domain \u8212- analogous to the rules about {\i reshuyot} and height thresholds in {\i Shabbat}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Additionally: {\i v\u8217'lo amran ela deleis lei shem levai} \u8212- even when the piece of wood is less than four {\i amos} and within ten {\i tefachim}, it is treated as the same space only if it does not have its own name. {\i Aval is lei shem levai} \u8212- if the piece of wood has its own accompanying name, an identity of its own \u8212- like \u8220"Fred rock\u8221" or \u8220"Pooh corner\u8221" \u8212- then even without meeting the four-by-four or the ten-{\i tefachim} threshold, it is treated as its own domain. Whether an analogous principle applies in {\i Shabbat} contexts is worth exploring, but the fundamental point here is clear: named objects with their own recognized identity may function as separate domains regardless of size or height.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Bed & Vessel Cases\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Husband\u8217's Bed Problem\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} states that if the husband placed the {\i get} on his own bed \u8212- even while the wife is there \u8212- it does not work. This is striking. One might have thought that the husband\u8217's physical presence alongside the wife would, if anything, add to the immediacy of the transfer. Yet the {\i mishna} says {\i afilu} \u8212- even in this case \u8212- it fails.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava\u8217's Distinction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava clarifies: {\i lo shanu ela b\u8217'mita shelo} \u8212- the {\i mishna}\u8217's ruling that it does not work applies only to his bed. {\i Aval mita shela megureshet} \u8212- but if it is her bed, she is divorced. This presumably holds even if he is physically present alongside her, just as dropping a {\i get} into someone\u8217's yard does not fail merely because the husband is also standing in that yard.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i baraita} confirms: {\i Rabbi Eliezer omer b\u8217'mita shelo eina megureshet b\u8217'mita shela megureshet} \u8212- in his bed she is not divorced, in her bed she is divorced.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Vessel in Seller\u8217's Domain\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This raises a fundamental question: why does her bed work at all if it is inside his house? The {\i Gemara} invokes the principle of {\i kelay shel lokeach birshut mocher} \u8212- the vessel of the buyer within the domain of the seller. If you bring your jug to a seller\u8217's premises and he pours wine into your jug, does that constitute a {\i kinyan} for you? Your jug is yours, but it sits within his domain. This is an open question in {\i halacha}, and the {\i Gemara} asks whether the case of her bed working might teach us that {\i kelay shel lokeach birshut mocher} \u8212- the buyer\u8217's vessel in the seller\u8217's domain \u8212- acquires for the buyer.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} deflects: perhaps the case of her bed is special not because of the general {\i kelay shel lokeach} principle but because of the height of the bed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ten Tefachim Height\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i De-gavoah asarah} \u8212- the bed is more than ten {\i tefachim} off the ground. As with the piece of wood in the previous case, an object elevated more than ten {\i tefachim} constitutes its own domain. Though in {\i Shabbat} law everything within a house is treated as {\i reshut hayachid} regardless of height, for these purposes the ten-{\i tefachim} threshold applies: her vessel, elevated above ten {\i tefachim}, is sufficiently its own space that placing the {\i get} in it works even within his domain.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} then asks: but what about the legs of the bed? {\i Ve\u8217'ika makom karay} \u8212- the legs of the bed rest on the floor, which is his domain. Is the bed truly independent? The {\i Gemara} answers: {\i makom karay lo kapid inish} \u8212- a person does not object to his wife\u8217's bed occupying space in the house.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Lo Makpid Principle\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This introduces the principle of {\i lo makpid} \u8212- that the husband does not object to his wife\u8217's presence and use of space in the household. However, the exact interaction between {\i lo makpid} and the ten-{\i tefachim} requirement is somewhat unclear. If {\i lo makpid} alone were sufficient, one would not need the ten-{\i tefachim} threshold at all \u8212- any vessel of hers on the ground should work. If ten {\i tefachim} alone were sufficient, there would be no need to add {\i lo makpid}. It seems that both factors work together: the elevation creates enough of a distinct space, and the husband\u8217's non-objection means his domain does not overwhelm the independence of her vessel within it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot specifies: {\i makom karay lo kapid inish le\u8217'chol hapachot be\u8217'ishto} \u8212- at minimum, a man does not object to his wife\u8217's bed occupying space in his home, and the same applies to the airspace of the bed itself. With others, he might be more particular, but not with his wife.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 We thus see two considerations intersecting: on the one hand, the classic {\i kinyan} framework of {\i kelay shel lokeach birshut mocher}; on the other, special considerations about what constitutes sufficient establishment of a woman\u8217's presence \u8212- whether through verbal permission, {\i lo makpid}, or physical thresholds. The question remains how fully the {\i get} framework tracks normal {\i kinyan} rules versus constituting a distinct model of giving.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Lap & Basket Cases\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Lap Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} turns to the case of throwing the {\i get} into the wife\u8217's {\i cheika} \u8212- her lap or bosom \u8212- or into her basket ({\i kaltah}). The lap seems straightforward: it is on her body. The basket raises the same issues as the bed. Where is the basket?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Lap Explanation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} asks: {\i amai} \u8212- why does the basket work? {\i Kli shel lokeach bireshut mocher hu} \u8212- it is the vessel of the buyer within the domain of the seller. Again, the question of {\i kelay shel lokeach birshut mocher} surfaces. Because if the basket were in her yard, the discussion would be unnecessary.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Suspended Basket\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Yehuda in the name of Shmuel: {\i kegon she-hayta kaltah tluyah bah} \u8212- the case is that her basket is actually suspended on her. It hangs from her, like a chain or a strap around her neck or body, and is therefore truly an extension of her person \u8212- analogous to her hand. Indeed, {\i kaltah} was sometimes a form of head covering involving a basket-like structure worn on the body. If physically attached and suspended, it functions unambiguously as an extension of her person.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tied Basket\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Elazar in the name of Rabbi Oshaya agrees that suspended suffices. Reish Lakish adds: {\i kshurah af al pi she\u8217'einah tluyah} \u8212- if it is tied to her, even if it is not suspended. Even if the basket drags on the ground but is connected to her by a string tied to her leg, it works. Notably, a third possibility would be {\i tluyah} without {\i kshurah} \u8212- suspended but not tied, like holding it \u8212- but the {\i Gemara} will explore this further.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Between Thighs Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Adda bar Ahava: {\i kegon she-hayta kaltah munachat lah bein yarkoteha} \u8212- the basket was placed between her thighs while she was seated. This is apparently distinct from the cases of suspension or tying.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rashi\u8217's Lo Makpid\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rashi explains: {\i bein yarkoteha} \u8212- even if the basket rests on the ground between her thighs, {\i eino makpid al kol makom moshav ishto} \u8212- the husband does not object to wherever his wife sits or places herself. Analogous to the earlier ruling about the bed, his not objecting means his domain does not override the fact that the {\i get} is in her vessel. Rashi even says {\i vead achshav hayta oto makom kanuy lah} \u8212- up to now that space was, in a sense, hers \u8212- though this language of {\i kanuy lah} seems to push further than a mere {\i lo makpid} rationale. The operating principle, however, is that it is in her vessel ({\i keilim}), and his sense of domain does not overpower that because he fundamentally does not object to her being there.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Basket Seller Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Mesharshiya bar Rav Dimi: {\i kegon she-haya ba\u8217'alah mocher kaltot} \u8212- the husband sells baskets for a living. Rashi explains: therefore anywhere a basket appears in the house, the husband is unbothered, since the house is full of baskets in any event. The implication is that in this household, a basket on the ground is the default and not a special indulgence \u8212- making her basket entirely unremarkable in the space.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This raises an important conceptual question. Why must we identify a special scenario \u8212- the husband selling baskets, or the basket being between her thighs \u8212- in order for {\i lo makpid} to apply? Should we not say that in {\i any} scenario where the wife is in the house, the husband implicitly allows her to set things down wherever she likes? Why would we presume that he {\i would} object in an ordinary case? If the principle is that special permission is needed, that implies his ownership is fundamentally established and her use is merely tolerated by exception \u8212- which would not adequately ground her presence as her own domain. But if, as seems more natural, his {\i not} objecting is a default rather than a special grant, then it reflects something like her established right to be in that space \u8212- and that is what enables the {\i get} in her vessel to work.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yochanan: Woman\u8217's Right\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yochanan states this most broadly: {\i makom cheika kanuy lah, makom kaltah kanuy lah} \u8212- just as the space for her lap belongs to her, so too the space wherever her basket is belongs to her. This suggests not a special permission scenario but a general right: wherever the wife places her basket, that space is effectively hers.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava on Lo Makpid\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava explains Rabbi Yochanan\u8217's reasoning: {\i lefi she\u8217'eino makpid lo al makom cheika velo al makom kaltah} \u8212- the husband does not object to where his wife sits or where she places her basket. It is an ongoing allowance, a default state, not a particular permission. As long as it is not a special case of permission that was granted for this moment, but rather a continuous background allowance, his domain does not override the fact that the {\i get} is in her vessel.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Baraita on Baskets\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i baraita} confirms: {\i zarko lah letoch cheika o letoch kaltah o letoch kol davar shehu kekaltah harei zu megureshet} \u8212- anything similar to her basket is valid. The {\i Gemara} asks: {\i kol davar shehu kekaltah lribuyei mai} \u8212- what does \u8220"like her basket\u8221" add? {\i Lribuyei taska de\u8217'achla bah tamrei} \u8212- to include a basket she uses to eat dates from. The {\i tanya nami hachi} here presumably serves to confirm that the category extends to items she habitually uses and has implicit permission to place wherever she wishes \u8212- not just her primary basket but analogous vessels that share the same character of ongoing permitted presence.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Get Identification\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Debt Document Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} now addresses framing. {\i Amar lah kinsi shetarach zeh} \u8212- if the husband said \u8220"take this business document,\u8221" and then she opened it and discovered it was her {\i get}, {\i eino get ad she\u8217'yomar lah hileich gitteich} \u8212- it is not a valid {\i get} until he says to her \u8220"here is your {\i get}.\u8221" The {\i get} must be identified as a {\i get} at the time of giving.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sleeping Wife Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Similarly: {\i natan beyada vehi yeshena} \u8212- if he placed the {\i get} in her hand while she was asleep, and she woke up, read it, and found it to be her {\i get}, {\i eino get ad she\u8217'yomar lah hileich gitteich} \u8212- it is still not valid until he tells her. She must be awake and aware.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This prompts an important question. Michael raises the scenario from the earlier {\i Gemara} where Oshaya ruled that she could be in {\i Tzippori} while her yard is in {\i Tveria} \u8212- physically absent \u8212- yet the {\i get} is valid. If she need not even be present, why is a sleeping wife\u8217's reception invalid? Apparently there is a conceptual distinction: when she is physically elsewhere, there is a sense in which the {\i get} is objectively knowable \u8212- anyone present can see that it is a {\i get} being delivered \u8212- and we combine the pieces into a valid act. But when she is present yet unconscious, the necessary awareness of the act is entirely absent, and no one who is an active party to the exchange is aware of its character.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Why Explicit Naming\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} surprisingly does not press the question of the textual or logical derivation of this requirement \u8212- that the {\i get} must be framed as a {\i get}. Tosafot and the Rambam supply different foundations.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafot Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot holds that the husband who gives a {\i get} must state {\i hileich gitteich v\u8217'harei at miteret lechol adam} \u8212- this is your {\i get} and you are permitted to any man \u8212- both as a verbal declaration and as part of the standard formulation, as is implied in the section of {\i Hamgaresh}. However, Tosafot acknowledges a difficulty from the case in {\i Nezikin} involving Rabbi Yochanan ben Gudgada: a man told witnesses \u8220"see this {\i get} that I am giving my wife,\u8221" and then delivered it to her without her knowledge that it was a {\i get}. The {\i Gemara} there indicates it is valid. Tosafot reconciles: {\i v\u8217'yeish lomar d\u8217'hasam nami achar kach yagidu lah ha\u8217'eidim shehi megureshet} \u8212- even there, the witnesses will subsequently inform her that she is divorced.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The underlying principle for Tosafot is derived from {\i v\u8217'shalachah mibeito v\u8217'halcha v\u8217'haysa l\u8217'ish acher} \u8212- she must leave his home and go to another man \u8212- meaning she must understand the consequences of the divorce. Therefore she must know she is divorced. On this reading, the statement need not accompany the act of giving simultaneously \u8212- it can come after \u8212- as long as she ultimately knows. This also explains why Rabbi\u8217's ruling in the {\i baraita} allows a later declaration to retroactively validate the act.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Witnesses Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Rabbi Yochanan ben Gudgada case illustrates the tension directly. The husband told the witnesses \u8212- not the wife \u8212- that this document is a {\i get}. He then delivered it to her under false pretenses. The witnesses have objective knowledge; she does not. Tosafot says the witnesses will eventually tell her. This is not an entirely comfortable resolution \u8212- combining a misrepresented delivery with a subsequent correction \u8212- but it demonstrates that objective knowability, even without the recipient\u8217's immediate awareness, can be a factor.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rambam Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Rambam, in {\i Hilchot Gerushin} Perek 1, Halachah 10, derives the requirement differently. {\i Uminan she\u8217'eina nitna lah ela b\u8217'torat geirushin} \u8212- from where do we know it must be given in the framework of divorce? {\i Shene\u8217'emar sefer kritut v\u8217'natan b\u8217'yadah} \u8212- the verse says {\i sefer kritut} and {\i natan b\u8217'yadah}: the act of giving must be clearly defined as the giving of a {\i sefer kritut}. {\i Sheyeiteinu otah b\u8217'torat sefer kritut} \u8212- it has to be given in that capacity. {\i Aval im nitan lah b\u8217'torat shtar chov o mezuzah eino get} \u8212- but if given as a debt document or a mezuzah, it is not a {\i get}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Rambam then addresses the {\i eidim} case \u8212- why does that work? {\i Sheharei hodia et ha\u8217'eidim shenitan b\u8217'torat geirushin} \u8212- he informed the witnesses that it is being given as a {\i get}. For the Rambam, the requirement is not that {\i she} knows she is divorced, but that the act itself is objectively defined as an act of {\i get}-delivery. If witnesses know, the act is objectively framed correctly, even if she is ignorant. The distinction from Tosafot is precise: Tosafot requires the witnesses to eventually tell the woman so that she knows; the Rambam does not require her subjective knowledge at all \u8212- only that the act be objectively constituted as a {\i get}-transfer. This also explains why, for the Rambam, a sleeping woman\u8217's case is more difficult: if none of the necessary parties \u8212- not she, not any present witness \u8212- is aware of the act in the moment, one might question whether the act is sufficiently defined.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Active Giving & Release\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Picking Up Issue\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now turns to the case where the wife picked up the {\i get} from behind the husband \u8212- {\i she-shalafto me\u8217'achorav}. The {\i mishna} says this is not valid. The {\i Gemara} asks: surely the problem is not merely that he failed to name it a {\i get} \u8212- the deeper problem is that {\i he} did not give it to her. She plucked it from behind him. {\i Havei lei tli giteich me\u8217'al gabi karka} \u8212- it is equivalent to telling her \u8220"pick your {\i get} up off the ground.\u8221"\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ground Pick-up Ruled Out\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava rules explicitly: {\i tli giteich me\u8217'al gabi karka lo amar klum} \u8212- if he says \u8220"pick your {\i get} up off the ground,\u8221" it is nothing. He must give it to her; she cannot be the one to take it. It must come actively from him to her, not be lifted by her off the ground.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Jutting Out Solution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} therefore reframes the case of picking it from behind him: {\i lo tzricha d\u8217'arkei leh chartzei} \u8212- he jutted out his side, his body, and she then took it from him. He performed an active physical motion, extending himself toward her; she received it from that extension. This counts as an act of giving.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This remains a {\i chiddush}. If he extends his hand to her holding the {\i get} and she plucks it from his hand, one would naturally think she is the primary actor \u8212- she is the one taking, not he. The typical image of giving is that he actively drops it in her hand or places it there. Here, what is required of him seems quite minimal \u8212- jutting out his body. That this constitutes {\i natan b\u8217'yadah} is worth noting as something the {\i poskim} would need to develop and clarify.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Baraita Parallel\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i baraita} states the two scenarios in parallel: if he told her \u8220"take this debt document\u8221" or she took it from behind him \u8212- {\i she-shalafto me\u8217'achorav} \u8212- the {\i baraita} clarifies that she did not pick it up from the ground but took it from his person, and then he said {\i kirtzonach veharei hu gittech} \u8212- as you wish and here is your {\i get}. {\i Eino get ad she\u8217'yomar lah harei zeh gittech} \u8212- it is not valid until he says \u8220"this is your {\i get}.\u8221" {\i Divrei Rabbi.}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi\u8217's Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i chiddush} of Rabbi\u8217's ruling is that the declaration can come {\i after} the act of giving. Once we establish that the act must be framed as a {\i get}, one might think the framing must coincide with the moment of delivery. Rabbi says it need not. If she was conscious throughout and he says \u8220"here is your {\i get}\u8221" afterward, that later statement can retroactively define the whole process. The reasoning: she was conscious and engaged when the document was placed in her hands, capable of being divorced at that moment; when he subsequently says \u8220"here is your {\i get},\u8221" we read the statement backward through the continuous interaction and view it as defining the earlier act.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. S. ben Elazar\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Shimon ben Elazar disagrees: {\i le\u8217'olam eino get ad she-yitlena heimena ve-yachzor ve-yitnena lah ve-yomar lah harei zeh gittech} \u8212- it is never valid until he takes it back from her and gives it again properly, with the correct declaration. A subsequent statement cannot retroactively reframe an earlier act of giving.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Consciousness Factor\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} explains why both cases \u8212- the debt-document misrepresentation and the sleeping wife \u8212- are necessary. Had we only the case of misrepresentation, one might think Rabbi rules as he does because she was conscious throughout and capable of receiving a {\i get} in that moment \u8212- {\i bat gerushin hi} \u8212- such that the later statement can define the earlier act as part of a continuous exchange. When she is asleep, she is {\i lav bat gerushin} \u8212- incapable of receiving a {\i get} at the moment of delivery \u8212- and perhaps even Rabbi would agree with Rabbi Shimon ben Elazar that the {\i get} must be re-given.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Conversely, had we only the sleeping case, one might think Rabbi Shimon ben Elazar requires a re-giving only because of the unconsciousness \u8212- but in the debt-document case, where she was awake, perhaps he would concede to Rabbi. {\i Tzricha} \u8212- both cases are needed to establish the full scope of the dispute.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The underlying question for Rabbi Shimon ben Elazar is coherent: how can a later statement reframe an earlier act? An act was done in one capacity; how does a subsequent declaration transform its identity? For the case of misrepresentation where she was conscious, one can at least argue for a continuous, unified interaction in which the later statement gives meaning to the whole. For the sleeping case, the acts are separated by time and states of consciousness, making the retroactive reframing far harder to sustain.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Slave Intermediary\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sleeping Slave Works\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava rules: if a man wrote a {\i get} and placed it into the hand of his wife\u8217's slave \u8212- {\i yashen u-mesharmato harei zeh get} \u8212- if the slave is asleep and she is watching it, it is a valid {\i get}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Awake Slave Fails\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Ne\u8217'or eino get} \u8212- if the slave is awake, it is not valid. This is counterintuitive. One would think that an awake, alert slave is {\i more} capable of guarding the {\i get}. But the problem is precisely that: {\i de-havei leh chatzer ha-mishtameret she-lo le-da\u8217'atah} \u8212- the slave\u8217's wakefulness means {\i he} is the one in control of the {\i get}, not her. An awake slave is watching the object through his own conscious agency; the {\i chatzer} is therefore not {\i mishtameret l\u8217'da\u8217'atah} \u8212- not guarded under her auspices \u8212- and fails.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Slave as Moving Domain\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Why does even the sleeping slave case not fail on a different ground? {\i Chatzer mehalechet hi} \u8212- it is a moving {\i chatzer}. The principle that {\i chatzer} only functions when stationary rules out placing a {\i get} in an object that moves of its own accord. Vessels ({\i keilim}) can be moved by an external force but do not move on their own \u8212- so placing the {\i get} in her vessel works. But a slave moves himself. How then does the sleeping slave ever work?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava on Movement\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava presses further: {\i kol she\u8217'ilu mehalach lo kanah omed v\u8217'yoshev lo kanah} \u8212- anything that would not acquire when moving would not acquire when standing still or seated. Even seated or standing, a slave who can move of his own volition remains a {\i chatzer mehalechet} in principle, because at any moment he might walk away. The distinction between sleeping and seated is insufficient to resolve the problem.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Bound Slave Solution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i V\u8217'hilchata b\u8217'chafut} \u8212- the practical ruling is that the slave must be bound. When the slave\u8217's legs are bound and he cannot walk even if he wills it, two problems are resolved simultaneously: the {\i chatzer mehalechet} problem disappears because he cannot move, and the {\i mishtameret she-lo l\u8217'da\u8217'atah} problem is addressed because \u8212- being bound and asleep \u8212- he is not the active controller. The {\i chatzer} is then {\i mishtameret l\u8217'da\u8217'atah} and stationary, and the {\i get} is valid. This ties together the earlier discussion of {\i chatzer} with the specific difficulties raised by the use of human agents as intermediaries.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Public Domain Get\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Proximity Rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} states: {\i hayta omedet bireshut harabim} \u8212- if she was standing in the public domain and he threw the {\i get} to her \u8212- {\i karov lah megureshet, karov lo eina megureshet} \u8212- if it lands closer to her she is divorced, if it lands closer to him she is not divorced. {\i Mekhtza al mekhtza megureshet v\u8217'eina megureshet} \u8212- if it lands exactly equidistant, she is doubtfully divorced.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 General Application\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} adds that the same rule applies to {\i kiddushin} \u8212- if a ring was thrown \u8212- and to a debt. These extensions will be significant later.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Debt Parallel\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The debt case is instructive. If a creditor said {\i zerok li chovi} \u8212- throw me the money you owe \u8212- and the borrower threw it: {\i karov l\u8217'malveh zachah malveh} \u8212- if it landed closer to the creditor, the creditor has acquired it and bears the loss if it is subsequently lost. {\i Karov l\u8217'loveh, haloveh chayav} \u8212- if it is closer to the borrower, the debt has not yet been transferred and the borrower remains liable. {\i Mekhtza al mekhtza shneihem yachloku} \u8212- if exactly equidistant and then lost, the borrower must repay half.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kinyan Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This {\i mishna} is striking on its face. Since when is there a {\i kinyan} based on physical proximity in a public domain? Normally, acquiring something requires {\i hagbahah} \u8212- lifting \u8212- or {\i meshichah} \u8212- drawing toward oneself. The mere fact that an object lands closer to me than to you in the middle of a public thoroughfare does not, by ordinary {\i kinyan} law, vest it in me. This would seem to be strong evidence that {\i get} delivery does not operate on the normal {\i kinyan} model \u8212- it is a distinct act of giving, not an acquisition.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 However, the {\i mishna}\u8217's extension to {\i kiddushin} and {\i chov} complicates this. If the proximity rule also governs {\i kiddushin} and debt-payment, it presumably reflects some general {\i kinyan} principle applicable across contexts \u8212- not only a {\i get}-specific rule. Yet proximity in a public domain as a {\i kinyan} is something we have never encountered.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav\u8217's Four Amot\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav resolves this: {\i arba amos shelah zehu karov lah, arba amos shelo zehu karov lo} \u8212- \u8220"closer to her\u8221" means within her four {\i amos}, and \u8220"closer to him\u8221" means within his four {\i amos}. The {\i mishna} is not speaking of literal physical proximity but of the rabbinic {\i kinyan} of {\i arba amos} in a public domain. The Sages created the rule that an ownerless object within someone\u8217's four {\i amos} in the public domain is acquired by that person \u8212- analogous to the concept that carrying an object four {\i amos} in a public domain constitutes a form of displacement. Something within my immediate environs is considered under my control. Rav thus reads the {\i mishna} as invoking this established {\i kinyan} and not introducing anything novel.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Half and Half Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} asks: {\i heichi dami mekhtza l\u8217'mekhtza} \u8212- what is the half-and-half case according to Rav? Rabbi Shmuel bar Avtigon answers: {\i b\u8217'shneihem omdim b\u8217'arba amos} \u8212- the case is where the two are close enough that their respective four-{\i amos} zones overlap. The {\i get} lands in a space that falls within both her four {\i amos} and his four {\i amos} simultaneously \u8212- a Venn diagram of contested space. That is why it is a {\i safek} \u8212- not because we lack factual information about where it landed, but because it objectively falls within both parties\u8217' domains.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} then asks: could we not resolve this by asking who was in the public domain first? The first person there establishes the priority of their four {\i amos}, and whoever arrived later entered into the other\u8217's space. {\i V\u8217'chi teima d\u8217'asu tarvaihu bahadi hadadi v\u8217'ee efshar l\u8217'tzamtzem} \u8212- one might say they arrived at exactly the same time, but perfect simultaneity never occurs in practice, so this should always be determinable in principle. The implication would be that the {\i safek} is merely epistemic \u8212- a lack of facts \u8212- not a genuine indeterminacy. This is less interesting as a legal case.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Conflicting Witnesses\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbah and Rav Yosef resolve differently: {\i hakha b\u8217'shnei kitei edim askinan} \u8212- the {\i mishna} is speaking of a case with two sets of witnesses offering conflicting testimony. One pair testifies the {\i get} landed closer to her; another pair testifies it landed closer to him. The {\i safek} is purely a matter of disputed evidence \u8212- not an objective overlapping of domains. If the instant replay were available, the question would be resolved. Fine as a legal mechanism; it makes the {\i mishna} more a procedural curiosity than a substantive doctrine about contested space.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Yochanan\u8217's Literal Read\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rejects Four Amot\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yochanan takes a more {\i pshat}-oriented reading of the {\i mishna} and explicitly rejects Rav\u8217's four-{\i amos} interpretation. {\i Rabbi Yochanan omer karov lah shaninu} \u8212- \u8220"closer to her\u8221" is what we taught \u8212- literally. {\i Afilu meah amah} \u8212- even a hundred {\i amos} away, as long as it is closer to her than to him. And {\i karov lo shaninu, afilu meah amah} \u8212- \u8220"closer to him,\u8221" even a hundred {\i amos} away.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Protection Model\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} asks: according to Rabbi Yochanan, what then is the half-and-half case? Rav Himnuna bar Abba answers: {\i lididi meparsha li minei d\u8217'Rabbi Yochanan} \u8212- it was explained to me directly from Rabbi Yochanan: the operative question is not rulers and distances but who can protect the object. {\i Hi yechola l\u8217'shomro v\u8217'hu einu yachol l\u8217'shomro} \u8212- if she is able to protect it and he is unable, that is \u8220"closer to her\u8221" and she is divorced. Even though Rabbi Yochanan initially sounds purely literal, his actual principle is conceptual: it is about control, access, and the ability to protect \u8212- not raw physical distance.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Clarification\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 So Rabbi Yochanan\u8217's read is \u8220"literal\u8221" in rejecting the four-{\i amos} framework, but \u8220"conceptual\u8221" in how he defines proximity: proximity means the capacity to control and protect. The categories then emerge clearly.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Protection Categories\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Three categories follow from this: if she can protect it and he cannot \u8212- {\i zehu karov lah}, it is considered near to her, and she is divorced. If he can protect it and she cannot \u8212- {\i zehu karov lo}, it is near to him, and she is not divorced. If both can protect it equally, or neither can \u8212- {\i zehu mekhtza al mekhtza}, that is the half-and-half case of doubt.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This approach departs dramatically from normal {\i kinyan} logic. Under standard acquisition rules, the ability to run over and grab an object does not vest ownership. For Rabbi Yochanan, by contrast, the ability to access and protect \u8212- even from a hundred {\i amos} away \u8212- constitutes the relevant standard for {\i get} delivery. This points strongly toward the view that {\i get} transfer operates on a sui generis model, distinct from ordinary {\i kinyanim}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Praise from Babylon\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 When the scholars in Israel told Rabbi Yochanan that Rabbi Yonatan in Bavel had arrived at the same reading, Rabbi Yochanan responded: {\i yodin chavran b\u8217'vavlayi l\u8217'forushei ki hai ta\u8217'ama!} \u8212- our Babylonian colleagues know how to give good {\i pshat} to a {\i mishna}! This is a telling remark. In general, the scholars of Eretz Yisrael tended toward more literal, text-faithful readings of earlier sources, while the Babylonians were more comfortable with interpretive departures. Here Rabbi Yochanan praises the Babylonian for having arrived independently at what he regards as the true {\i pshat} of the {\i mishna}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Dog Example\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} brings a {\i baraita} in support: Rabbi Eliezer says \u8212- {\i kol shehu karov lah mi lo} \u8212- if it is closer to her than to him, and {\i uba kelev v\u8217'natlo} \u8212- a dog came and took it, {\i einah megureshet} \u8212- she is not divorced. The {\i Gemara} clarifies: surely Rabbi Eliezer does not mean she must guard it forever. The {\i baraita} means: if the object is physically closer to her than to him, but {\i v\u8217'im ba kelev v\u8217'natlo v\u8217'hu yachol l\u8217'shomro v\u8217'hi einah yechola l\u8217'shomro} \u8212- if a dog were to come and he could stop it while she could not, then even though it is physically nearer to her, she is not divorced. Perhaps a river or a large boulder lies between her and the {\i get}, giving her the appearance of proximity but not the actual capacity for control. Physical measurement is never the test; the protection and access test is always determinative.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Practical Stringency\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Bending Down Test\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Shmuel said to Rav Yehuda: {\i shinana} \u8212- sharp one \u8212- {\i kdei she-tishoch v\u8217'titlennu} \u8212- the {\i halacha} in practice is that it must be close enough to her that she can bend down and pick it up. This is more stringent than either Rav\u8217's four-{\i amos} framework or Rabbi Yochanan\u8217's hundred-{\i amos} protection model. Given the gravity of a woman\u8217's marital status, Shmuel insists that in practice we require genuine physical proximity.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Direct Hand Requirement\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 And even that is not the final word: {\i v\u8217'at lo ta\u8217'avid uvda ad d\u8217'mati gita l\u8217'yadah} \u8212- you should not rule in practice that she is divorced until the {\i get} actually reaches directly into her hand. The progression tightens dramatically: from the theoretical possibility of a hundred {\i amos} away, to the requirement of four {\i amos} as per Rav, to the requirement of close enough to bend and pick up, to the requirement that it actually come into her hand directly.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chalitza Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Mar bar Ammi told Rav Ashi: there was an actual case where the {\i get} was thrown close to the wife \u8212- perhaps close enough that she could have bent down to pick it up \u8212- and nonetheless {\i v\u8217'atzrichua chalitza} \u8212- they required her to undergo {\i chalitza}. She was not ruled to be definitely divorced, because the {\i get} had not reached directly into her hand. This shows how stringently the practical ruling was applied.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafot Limit\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot limits this stringency: {\i dehaynu davka bireshut harabim} \u8212- this applies specifically in the public domain. In a public domain, a person has no real, established presence. Without a {\i chatzer}, without a vessel, without any grounded connection to the space, the delivery must be maximally direct. But in a {\i chatzer} \u8212- her yard \u8212- the analysis is different.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yard Exception\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Aval bechatzer ein tzarich sheyagia liyada} \u8212- in the yard, the {\i get} need not reach her person at all. The {\i chatzer} itself is her domain; a {\i get} deposited anywhere within it, provided the {\i chatzer} is {\i mishtameret l\u8217'da\u8217'atah}, reaches her in the legally required sense. Tosafot notes that the {\i Aruch} appears more stringent even in the yard case, requiring the {\i get} to reach her person, but Tosafot rejects this position.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Summary Arc\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The arc traced by this sugya is remarkable. We began with the theoretical possibility \u8212- under Rabbi Yochanan \u8212- that a {\i get} thrown a hundred {\i amos} away, but in a position where she can protect it and he cannot, constitutes a valid delivery. We moved to Rav\u8217's position that the four-{\i amos kinyan} framework applies. We arrived, through Shmuel\u8217's stringency, at the requirement that in practice, for a ruling to be issued, the {\i get} must reach the woman\u8217's hand directly. In the public domain, the practical ruling effectively requires direct personal delivery.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Get Model Distinct\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Non-Kinyan Model\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i sugya} of 78 reveals a radical element in the laws of {\i get} delivery: the transfer of a {\i get} need not conform to the ordinary rules of {\i kinyan}. Under Rabbi Yochanan\u8217's model, proximity is measured by the capacity to protect \u8212- not by the formalities of {\i hagbahah}, {\i meshichah}, or even {\i arba amos}. A {\i get} can be delivered into a space the husband lent for the purpose; it can be delivered into her vessel elevated above ten {\i tefachim} in his house; it can be delivered by a bound, sleeping slave. These are not standard modes of acquisition.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Definition vs.\u160 ?Awareness\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 At the same time, the {\i sugya} reveals a deeper debate about what {\i get} delivery requires beyond the physical transfer. For Tosafot, the wife must ultimately be aware that she is divorced \u8212- {\i v\u8217'shalachah mibeito v\u8217'halcha} \u8212- she must understand the consequence of the act, which is why the witnesses in the Rabbi Yochanan ben Gudgada case must eventually inform her. For the Rambam, the requirement is objective rather than subjective: the act of giving must be constituted and framed as the giving of a {\i sefer kritut} \u8212- and if witnesses know, the act is properly defined, regardless of the wife\u8217's awareness.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Forward Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tomorrow, we will take up the question posed by the end of the {\i mishna}: the same proximity rule is stated to apply to {\i kiddushin} and to the repayment of a {\i chov}. If the proximity model of delivery is a special rule for {\i get} \u8212- not conforming to ordinary {\i kinyanim} \u8212- how can the {\i mishna} apply it equally to {\i kiddushin} and debt? Normal {\i kinyanim} do not work this way. We will see what the {\i Gemara} makes of that extension.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Gittin 79\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Get in Reshus HaRabim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gittin Only\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The daf opens in the middle of the previous discussion on 78b, concerning the scenario in which a husband throws a {\i get} in {\i reshus ha-rabim} and it falls near the woman. The earlier sugya established a debate about what \u8220"near her\u8221" means: one position holds it must land within her {\i arba amot}, the classic mode of rabbinic {\i kinyan}; the other, attributed to Rabbi Yochanan, holds that as long as she is in a position to guard and protect it \u8212- even if it is far from her \u8212- it is considered under her control. Rabbi Yochanan\u8217's reading underscores a fundamental principle: the act of giving a {\i get} need not conform to the same formal requirements as {\i kinyan} of an ordinary object. It can be a {\i netina} without a conventional {\i kinyan}. At the same time, the {\i Gemara} closed with a significant practical stringency \u8212- endorsed by Tosafot \u8212- that in practice the {\i get} must reach her hand directly, or at minimum land in her yard. None of the more lenient constructions of the {\i Mishnah} are relied upon {\i le-halacha}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Against this backdrop, the {\i Gemara} on our daf turns to the final clause of the {\i Mishnah}, which extends the same rule \u8212- that throwing works \u8212- not only to {\i gittin} but also to {\i kiddushin} and repayment of a debt. This raises a sharp question: if Rabbi Yochanan\u8217's position is that the {\i get} works even when it lands far away, provided she can protect it, why would such a non-standard act of transfer function in {\i kiddushin} or debt repayment, which are governed by ordinary {\i kinyan} principles?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} opens with Rav Assi citing Rabbi Yochanan: {\i L\u8217'Gittin amru v\u8217'lo l\u8217'davar acher} \u8212- this principle was stated for {\i gittin} alone and does not apply to anything else. This amounts to reading the {\i Mishnah} as if its additional cases simply do not mean what they appear to say.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kiddushin & Debt\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Abba immediately challenges Rav Assi: the {\i Mishnah} explicitly states {\i v\u8217'chein l\u8217'inyan kiddushin} \u8212- and likewise for {\i kiddushin}! How can Rav Assi, in the name of Rabbi Yochanan, simply bracket the plain text of the {\i Mishnah}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yatza V\u8217'Hayata\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} responds that {\i kiddushin} is not a true counter-example. The verse in Devarim reads {\i v\u8217'yatza v\u8217'hayata} \u8212- she leaves one husband and becomes the wife of another \u8212- which links {\i geirushin} and {\i kiddushin} as conceptually mirrored. If the mechanism works for {\i gittin}, it works for {\i kiddushin} as well, because the Torah itself draws this parallel.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This linkage is less surprising once one recalls that {\i kiddushin} already operates differently from ordinary {\i kinyan}. {\i Kiddushei kesef}, for instance, can be effected through {\i bahahi hana\u8217'ah} \u8212- the abstract benefit a woman derives from some act done on her behalf \u8212- without any concrete object passing into her possession at all. There are even opinions that one can perform {\i kiddushin} with a stolen object, since the woman acquires certain rights in it even absent formal ownership. Given these anomalies, it is not so strange to say that just as {\i gittin} can be effected through a protected-but-not-formally-transferred object, {\i kiddushin} can parallel it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Debt Condition\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The debt case, however, is different and cannot be resolved so easily. The {\i Mishnah} says: {\i Zrok li chovi v\u8217'zarko lo, karov l\u8217'malveh zachah lo} \u8212- if the debtor throws the money and it lands near the creditor, the creditor has acquired it; if it lands nearer to the debtor, the debtor remains liable; and if it falls midway, they split. This appears to be a genuine application of the \u8220"proximity-as-acquisition\u8221" principle in an ordinary property context, which directly contradicts Rabbi Yochanan\u8217's insistence that this mechanism is unique to {\i gittin}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} answers: {\i hacha b\u8217'mai askinan d\u8217'amar lei zrok li chovi v\u8217'tipater} \u8212- the {\i Mishnah} is discussing a case where the creditor specifically stipulated, \u8220"Throw it to me and you will be exempt.\u8221" In other words, it is not that throwing near the creditor is inherently an act of debt repayment; rather, the creditor himself created a special condition under which the throw would discharge the obligation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} immediately objects: if that is all that is happening, {\i mai le-memra} \u8212- what is the {\i chiddush}? Of course if the creditor declared that the throw would suffice, it works. The {\i Gemara} then refines the case further: {\i d\u8217'amar lei zrok li chovi b\u8217'toras gittin} \u8212- the creditor said, \u8220"Throw me my debt in the manner of a {\i get},\u8221" i.e., as if you were giving me a {\i get}. The {\i Gemara} again presses: still, what is the novelty? And the final answer is: {\i mahu d\u8217'teima mechi amar lei meshachek ani bach} \u8212- one might have thought the debtor could later claim he was joking, that he never seriously meant to frame the payment in those unusual terms. The {\i Mishnah} therefore teaches that once he made that declaration, he cannot retract it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is, as the lecturer notes, a strikingly forced reading of the {\i Mishnah}. The plain implication of the {\i Mishnah} is that throwing a debt payment near the creditor functions as a valid act of repayment \u8212- yet Rabbi Yochanan, through Rav Assi, refuses to accept this, driven by the conviction that his radical reading of {\i gittin} (that a {\i get} can work even without a conventional {\i kinyan}) is so exceptional that it cannot bleed into normal monetary law. He is therefore willing to impose a nearly impossible reading on the {\i Mishnah} rather than concede that this mechanism is a general mode of acquisition.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is worth pausing to note that a debt repayment is arguably not identical to an ordinary {\i kinyan}. When one sells a basketball, the object was never the buyer\u8217's, and acquisition requires a formal {\i ma\u8217'aseh kinyan}. But when repaying a debt, the money in some sense already belongs to the creditor \u8212- it is merely being returned. Whether that distinction would affect the formal requirements of repayment is an independent question the {\i Gemara} does not resolve here, but it sharpens why Rabbi Yochanan\u8217's position, however forced as a reading of the {\i Mishnah}, has internal logic.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Direct Handover\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yochanan\u8217's stringency bears revisiting in light of all this. The same Rabbi Yochanan who expands the {\i get\u8217's} validity to cover situations where the woman can merely protect the document \u8212- even a hundred {\i amot} away \u8212- also insists, in practice, that the {\i get} must reach her hand directly. Whatever theoretical latitude the {\i Mishnah} provides, {\i le-ma\u8217'aseh} Rabbi Yochanan does not rely on it, and a woman who received a {\i get} by any of these indirect means would be in a state of doubt. This is a significant practical {\i chumra}, one that transforms what might seem like a liberalization of the {\i Mishnah} into a stringency that renders her status as {\i megoreshet} uncertain unless the {\i get} came directly to her.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Physical Control\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Get & Strings\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Chisda addresses a case of a {\i get} given with strings still attached: {\i get beyada u-meshicha beyado} \u8212- the {\i get} is in her hand, but the husband is holding a string connected to it. The rule is: {\i im yachol lenatko u-lehavyo etzlo einah megureshet} \u8212- if he can yank the string and retrieve the {\i get}, she is not divorced. If the string is effectively slack \u8212- if he cannot use it to reclaim the document \u8212- she is divorced. The reasoning is {\i ba\u8217'inan kerisus} \u8212- the Torah requires a complete severance between husband and wife, and if he retains the ability to reclaim the document, that severance has not been achieved. This is, of course, entirely literal, but it lends itself naturally to the metaphorical expression of a divorce \u8220"with strings attached.\u8221"\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sloped Hands\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Yehuda addresses another scenario: {\i haysah yada asuya ke-tafres} \u8212- her hand was positioned like a slope, angled downward. The husband threw the {\i get} into her sloped hand, and despite physically reaching her hand, it immediately slid off. The ruling is {\i af al pi she-higi\u8217'a le-yada einah megureshet} \u8212- even though it reached her hand, she is not divorced.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Did Not Settle\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} asks: even if we accept that the {\i get} slipping from her hand means she did not acquire it {\i via} her hand, why does it not take effect by virtue of landing within her {\i arba amot} once it hits the ground? The {\i Gemara} answers: {\i delo nach} \u8212- it did not come to rest. According to Rashi, before the {\i get} reached the ground it was blown away, or perhaps burnt, and never actually settled within her {\i arba amot}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A further question arises: even if it did not come to rest, should she not acquire it {\i me-avira de-arba amot} \u8212- from the airspace of her {\i arba amot}? This raises an open question: when the Rabbis instituted {\i kinyan arba amot} in {\i reshus ha-rabim}, did that {\i kinyan} include the airspace above those four {\i amot}, or only the ground level?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} notes that this question \u8212- {\i yesh lahen avir ein lahen avir} \u8212- was itself posed by Rabbi Elazar and left unresolved. One might think the sloped-hands case resolves it: since the {\i get} in the airspace did not effect a divorce, perhaps {\i arba amot} has no airspace rights. But the {\i Gemara} deflects this inference: the case involves a woman standing at the edge of a river, so that when the {\i get} slid off her hands, it immediately passed over the airspace above the river. Since the riverbed is not a surface on which something can come to rest, the airspace above it does not belong to her {\i arba amot} \u8212- {\i mi-ikara le-ibud ka\u8217'ei}, it was headed for destruction from the outset.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Airspace Rights\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The broader principle \u8212- that airspace above a space where objects cannot come to rest does not constitute protected domain \u8212- carries implications for the subsequent discussion of rooftops and enclosed areas. The question of whether {\i arba amot} includes airspace thus remains formally unresolved, but the practical direction of the sugya leans toward excluding it, with the rooftop cases relying instead on property-based airspace rather than the rabbinic {\i kinyan} of {\i arba amot}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rooftop Scenarios\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Woman Above\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The next {\i Mishnah} (79a) opens with two scenarios involving different vertical positions. In the first: {\i haisah omedes al rosh ha-gag} \u8212- the woman is standing on the rooftop and the man is at ground level; he throws the {\i get} up to her. The rule is: {\i keivan she-higi\u8217'a le-avir ha-gag harei zu megureshet} \u8212- once the {\i get} reaches the airspace of the rooftop, she is divorced, even before it lands. This introduces airspace of one\u8217's own property as a mechanism distinct from the {\i arba amot} discussed earlier. One\u8217's property certainly carries airspace rights, and once the {\i get} enters that airspace, if under normal circumstances it would have come to rest, she is already divorced \u8212- even if something intervenes (a wind, a fire) and it never actually lands.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Unprotected Airspace\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The second scenario reverses the positions: {\i hu mi-lema\u8217'ala ve-hi mi-lematah} \u8212- he is on the rooftop and she is below in the yard; he throws it down to her. The {\i Mishnah} states that once the {\i get} leaves the domain of the rooftop \u8212- {\i keivan she-yatza me-reshus ha-gag} \u8212- and enters the airspace of the yard below, even if it is subsequently erased or burnt in mid-air, she is divorced.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} immediately raises the obvious difficulty: {\i lo mintar} \u8212- the rooftop has no fence. It is a flat roof, open to wind and other elements. What makes the airspace above it or the airspace below it \u8220"protected\u8221"? The whole theory of airspace-as-acquisition rests on the concept of {\i chatzer ha-mishtameres} \u8212- a guarded domain that keeps things within it. An open rooftop, or the open air above a yard with no enclosing walls sufficiently high, is not inherently protective in this sense.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Fenced Airspace\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Papa resolves this: {\i begag she-yesh lo ma\u8217'akeh askinan} \u8212- we are dealing with a rooftop that has a fence around it. Once there is a fence, the {\i get} must enter the space {\i within} the fence \u8212- not merely above the level of the fence \u8212- and then it is genuinely contained and protected. The {\i Mishnah}\u8217's statement that she is divorced once it enters the airspace of the rooftop is predicated on the existence of such an enclosure.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The same principle applies to the second case, where the woman is below. The airspace through which the {\i get} travels after leaving the rooftop must be enclosed and protected. The {\i Gemara} cites Rav Yehuda in the name of Shmuel: {\i kegon she-hayu mechitzot ha-tachtonot odofot al ha-elyonot} \u8212- the walls of the lower yard are higher than the walls of the rooftop building. If one imagines a building standing in the middle of a large courtyard whose surrounding walls are taller than the rooftop, then the moment the {\i get} leaves the rooftop it is already within the enclosed, protected airspace of the courtyard below. This ruling is stated in parallel by Rabbi Elazar in the name of Rabbi Oshaya, and by Rabbi Ami in the name of Rabbi Yochanan.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Papa then asks Abaye: {\i ke-man ke-Rabbi?} \u8212- is this position consistent only with the view of {\i Rebbi}, who holds {\i kluta ke-mi she-hunchah damya} \u8212- that an object absorbed into a domain\u8217's airspace is legally equivalent to having landed there? And this would mean that only according to {\i Rebbi} does the {\i get} take effect the moment it enters the protected airspace, even before it comes to rest.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye responds: {\i afilu tima rabbanan} \u8212- even the {\i Rabanan} who dispute {\i Rebbi} on this point do so only for {\i Shabbat} purposes. The {\i Rabanan} hold that for {\i Shabbat}, being in the airspace of {\i reshut ha-yachid} is not equivalent to resting there \u8212- so one who throws an object into the airspace of a private domain but does not land it has not violated {\i Shabbat} according to the {\i Rabanan}. But here the concern is not about {\i Shabbat} at all. The question for {\i gittin} is whether the object is {\i mintar} \u8212- protected \u8212- and once it is in the enclosed protected airspace, {\i ve-ha kamintar}, it is protected. The two frameworks operate by entirely different criteria.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Three Tefachim Rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Ulla bar Manashya, in the name of Avimi, offers a further approach: even without a fence, if the {\i get} is within three {\i tefachim} of the rooftop surface, it works \u8212- {\i d\u8217'chol pachot mi-shlosha samuch le-gag ke-gag dami} \u8212- because within three {\i tefachim} of the ground (or of a surface), halachic law treats the object as if it were already resting on that surface. This principle, familiar from other areas, means that for the first case \u8212- the woman above \u8212- even without an enclosure, if the {\i get} clears the lip of the roof and is within three {\i tefachim} of the roof surface, it is as though it has landed and she is divorced.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Man Above\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 In the second case \u8212- the man above, woman below \u8212- the {\i Gemara} applies the same framework of higher surrounding walls. This is described by Rav Yehuda in the name of Shmuel, and repeated by Rabbi Elazar in the name of Rabbi Oshaya and by Rabbi Ami in the name of Rabbi Yochanan: {\i kegon she-hayu mechitzot ha-tachtonot odofot al ha-elyonot}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava articulates three distinct principles that emerge from comparing {\i gittin} to {\i Shabbat} law in this area. First, {\i Rebbi\u8217's} ruling that {\i kluta ke-mi she-hunchah damya} \u8212- an object is treated as having landed once it enters a domain\u8217's airspace \u8212- is disputed by the {\i Rabanan} for {\i Shabbat} purposes, but applies for {\i gittin} because in {\i gittin} the criterion is protection, not rest. Second, Rav Chisda had earlier ruled that placing an object atop a hundred-{\i amah} pole in {\i reshut ha-yachid} renders one liable for {\i Shabbat} \u8212- {\i reshut ha-yachid olah ad la-rakia}, private domain extends upward to the heavens \u8212- so even at that height, if the object has landed, one is liable. But that ruling is {\i hani milei l\u8217'inyan Shabbat}; by {\i gittin}, {\i mishum neturei hu}, the {\i get} must be in a protected space, and an object balanced on the top of a hundred-{\i amah} pole is not protected from forces that could dislodge it, regardless of its technical presence within {\i reshut ha-yachid}. These two cases are mirror images of one another: the first involves an object that is protected but has not rested; the second involves an object that has rested but is not protected. For {\i Shabbat}, the object in the first case does not create liability (lacking rest), while the object in the second does (having rested within {\i reshut ha-yachid} regardless of protection). For {\i gittin}, precisely the opposite: the first case works (protected space suffices), and the second does not (lack of protection disqualifies it).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Trajectory\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Nachman in the name of Rabbah bar Avuha rules that the case of {\i nimchak} \u8212- the {\i get} being erased in mid-air \u8212- depends critically on the direction of travel at the moment of erasure. {\i Lo shanu ela she-nimchak derech yerida} \u8212- the ruling that she is divorced applies only when the {\i get} was erased on its downward trajectory. {\i Aval nimchak derech aliyah lo} \u8212- if it was erased while still going upward, she is not divorced, {\i me-ikara lo le-minach ka\u8217'ei} \u8212- because at that moment it was not yet headed toward coming to rest in the yard. An object on an upward trajectory, even if gravity will eventually bring it down, is directionally oriented away from the receiving domain and cannot be considered as entering that domain\u8217's space at the moment of erasure. Only when the object is descending is it definitively in the process of landing within the protected space.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Burning Get\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Similarly, the case of {\i nisraf} \u8212- the {\i get} catching fire \u8212- depends on timing relative to when the fire began. If the {\i get} was thrown before the fire broke out, and the fire then destroys it in mid-air, she is divorced. But if there was already a fire burning in the yard when the husband threw the {\i get}, she is not divorced: {\i me-ikara le-sreifah ka-azil} \u8212- from the outset it was thrown into a fire, not into a protected domain. If a wind subsequently blew the {\i get} into a fire that was not in its path when thrown, that would presumably be treated as {\i kadam get le-dleikah} \u8212- the {\i get} preceded the fire \u8212- and she would be divorced, since the throw itself had nothing to do with the conflagration.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Separate Domains\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Different Buildings\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Chisda introduces the principle of {\i reshuyot chalukot be-gittin} \u8212- separate domains in the context of {\i gittin}. The case involves a structure or courtyard above which the rooftop appears as a single continuous surface, but below which different sections are owned by different people. Rav Chisda\u8217's ruling is that the separate ownership below creates separate domains above as well, such that even if on the roof it all looks like one flat space, the airspace over one building\u8217's section is distinct from the airspace over another\u8217's.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Source Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rami bar Chama asks Rava: {\i mina lei le-saba ha?} \u8212- from where does Rav Chisda derive this principle? Rava responds: {\i masnitin hi} \u8212- it comes from the {\i Mishnah} itself.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 One Space\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} works through the logic as follows. The {\i Mishnah} presents two scenarios: (1) she is on the rooftop and he throws from below \u8212- once it reaches the airspace of the roof she is divorced; (2) he is on the rooftop and she is below \u8212- once the {\i get} leaves the domain of the roof she is divorced. In both cases, the {\i Mishnah} simply describes where each party is standing without specifying who owns which space.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Now, who owns what? If she owns both the rooftop and the yard below, there is no {\i chiddush} in the airspace of the rooftop \u8212- the entire space is hers and of course she acquires whatever enters it. Conversely, if he owns both rooftop and yard, neither scenario makes sense, because neither the airspace of the roof nor the airspace of the yard is hers. The {\i Gemara} therefore says: in the first scenario ({\i reisha}), she must own the roof and he owns the yard; in the second ({\i seifa}), he owns the roof and she owns the yard.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But this creates a difficulty: how can the {\i Mishnah} switch the ownership arrangements between the first and second clauses without saying so? Rava\u8217's resolution is: in truth, {\i all} the space is his \u8212- both the roof and the yard. What the {\i Mishnah} is describing is a case of {\i d\u8217'oshla makom} \u8212- he has granted her the use of whichever space she is currently standing in for the purpose of receiving the {\i get}. When she is above, he is effectively lending her the rooftop as her domain for the {\i get}. When she is below, he is lending her the yard.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This reading has a further implication: {\i chad makom moshli inshi, trei mekomot lo moshli inshi} \u8212- people ordinarily lend one space, not two. Therefore, if there are two distinct buildings below, even if the rooftop looks like one unbroken surface above, the husband\u8217's permission to use \u8220"a space\u8221" grants her rights in only one of those spaces. If the {\i get} lands in the airspace over the other building\u8217's section, she has not acquired it, even though from above it appears to be the same continuous roof. This is the derivation of Rav Chisda\u8217's principle of {\i reshuyot chalukot be-gittin}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava\u8217's challenger responds: {\i dilma hachi ve-dilma hachi} \u8212- perhaps the two clauses of the {\i Mishnah} simply describe two different factual scenarios, each with its own ownership configuration, without requiring the harmonized reading. In the {\i reisha} she owns the roof; in the {\i seifa} he owns the roof. The {\i Mishnah} switched cases without announcing it \u8212- a reading that, while less elegant, requires no theory of implicit permission-granting. The {\i Gemara} acknowledges this alternative without definitively rejecting it, leaving Rava\u8217's reading as a compelling but not exclusive interpretation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gittin vs.\u160 ?Shabbat\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The third of Rava\u8217's principles addresses the analogue in {\i Shabbat} law. Rav Yehuda in the name of Shmuel rules: one may not stand on one rooftop and collect rainwater from a neighbor\u8217's rooftop \u8212- {\i she-keshem she-diyurin chalukin mi-lematah, kach diyurin chalukin mi-lema\u8217'alah} \u8212- because just as the inhabitants are separate below, the domains are separate above, even though the two rooftops form one continuous surface. This is a {\i Shabbat} ruling: carrying from one section of the roof to the other constitutes carrying between separate {\i reshuyot} and requires an {\i eiruv}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 However, Rava notes: {\i hani milei l\u8217'inyan Shabbat, aval l\u8217'inyan get, mishum kepeida hu ve-chulei hai lo kapdi inshi} \u8212- this rule applies for {\i Shabbat}, but for {\i gittin} the analysis differs. The rationale in {\i gittin} is about what the husband actually intends when he tells her she may use \u8220"a space.\u8221" When it comes to a rooftop, {\i ein tashmisham kavu\u8217'a} \u8212- people do not regularly use rooftops \u8212- and the two halves of a flat rooftop are not meaningfully distinct in the way a yard and a room are. A husband who says \u8220"you may use this space\u8221" for her {\i get} would not typically distinguish between the two halves of a continuous rooftop. {\i Lo kapdi inshi} \u8212- people are not particular about that. Therefore, the {\i reshuyot chalukot} principle that prohibits carrying across rooftop sections for {\i Shabbat} does not disqualify a {\i get} received on the \u8220"wrong\u8221" half of a flat rooftop. This is a meaningful divergence between the rules for {\i gittin} and the rules for {\i Shabbat}, reflecting the different underlying concerns of each framework.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Connected Roofs\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rashi spells this out explicitly: even if the husband specifically named one rooftop when granting her permission \u8212- {\i hish\u8217'il lah makom be-zeh le-kabel et gittah} \u8212- and she received the {\i get} on the other rooftop, the {\i Gemara} says {\i lo amrinan hacha reshuyot chalukot}. This is because {\i mishum kepeida hu} \u8212- it is about what he cares about, and regarding adjacent rooftops that share one flat surface, {\i ein tashmisham kavu\u8217'a ve-gas}, people do not invest particular significance in which half of the roof is being used. The contrast is with the earlier principle that {\i yesh mash\u8217'il chatzeiro u-makpid al beito} \u8212- one who lends a yard does not thereby grant use of the inner rooms. There, the distinction is functionally obvious and significant. On a flat rooftop, it is not. Therefore, {\i lo kapdi inshi} \u8212- he would not be {\i makpid} about which half of his own connected rooftops she used \u8212- and a {\i get} received on either half is valid.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Yards & Baskets\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Nested Yards\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye presents a case of two concentric yards: {\i pnimit shelah ve-chitzonah shelo} \u8212- the inner yard belongs to her and the outer yard belongs to him. The walls of the outer yard are higher than those of the inner yard, such that the inner yard is fully enclosed within and protected by the outer walls. The husband throws the {\i get} to her. The ruling: {\i keivan she-higi\u8217'a le-avir mechitzot ha-chitzonah harei zu megureshet} \u8212- once the {\i get} enters the airspace of the outer walls, she is divorced. The rationale is {\i pnimit gufah be-mechitzot ha-chitzonah kamintara} \u8212- the inner yard itself is what is protected by the outer walls. Her property (the inner yard) is implicitly shielded by his enclosure. This is a case where she acquires the {\i get} while it is still technically over his property (the outer yard), but because her property is the beneficiary of the protection his walls provide, it counts as sufficiently within her domain.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Nested Baskets\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} then draws a pointed contrast: {\i ma she-ein ken be-kupot} \u8212- this is not true for baskets. If she has the inner basket and he has the outer (larger) basket, and he throws a {\i get} into the nested baskets: {\i afilu higi\u8217'a le-avir pnimit einah megureshet} \u8212- even if the {\i get} reaches the airspace of the inner basket, she is not divorced, because it has not yet landed. She must wait until it actually comes to rest.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Property vs.\u160 ?Vessels\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The reason is fundamental: {\i de-ha lo nach} \u8212- it has not come to rest. The concept of acquiring something through airspace is a feature of real property and enclosed domains, not of movable vessels. A basket has no airspace rights. {\i Kinyan kli} \u8212- acquisition through a vessel \u8212- requires the object to actually land in the vessel; the airspace above a basket conveys nothing. Yards and property have airspace. Baskets do not.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A further complication arises: {\i kli she-lokeach be-reshut mocher hu} \u8212- if her basket is sitting inside his basket, then her vessel is sitting within his domain, which raises a question about whether it counts as her domain at all. The {\i Gemara} addresses this by positing that the outer basket has no bottom \u8212- {\i kuppa she-ein lah shulayim} \u8212- so that it does not constitute a closed domain in which her basket is \u8220"sitting.\u8221" But as the {\i Gemara} makes clear, the operative {\i chiddush} throughout is the first one: even the airspace of her own basket, standing alone, conveys no acquisition. The contrast between {\i chatzer} and {\i kli} in this regard is the defining line: property carries airspace rights; movable vessels do not.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Old Get\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Definition\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The daf concludes with a new {\i Mishnah} addressing the {\i get yashan} \u8212- an old {\i get}: {\i kol she-nityached imah mei-achar she-ketavo lah} \u8212- a {\i get} that was written, not yet given, and during the intervening period the husband was secluded ({\i yichud}) with the wife.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shammai & Hillel\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Beit Shammai} permit the husband to divorce his wife with such a {\i get}: they do not invoke the concern {\i shema yomru gitah kodem livnah} \u8212- that people will say the divorce preceded the child she may have conceived during the {\i yichud}. {\i Beit Hillel} forbid using it \u8212- {\i osrim} \u8212- out of precisely that concern. If the {\i get} is eventually used and she later has a child, people looking at the date on the {\i get} might conclude that she was pregnant after the formal date of the divorce, creating a false impression about the circumstances of the birth.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Forbidden Not Invalid\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Critically, {\i Beit Hillel} said {\i osrim} \u8212- they forbid it \u8212- not {\i pasul} \u8212- they do not invalidate it. This distinction is legally operative: a prohibition against using the {\i get} is a rabbinic {\i gezeirah}, but it does not go so far as to invalidate the {\i get} retroactively if it was used.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Retroactive Validity\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Abba in the name of Shmuel rules accordingly: {\i im nise\u8217'it lo tetze} \u8212- if she used this {\i get} and remarried, she need not leave the second marriage. {\i Be-di\u8217'eved} the {\i get} is entirely valid. A second version of the ruling goes further: {\i im nitgarshah tinasei le-chatchilah} \u8212- once the {\i get} has been given (even though it should not have been), she may remarry {\i le-chatchilah}. The rabbinic concern of {\i Beit Hillel} was never about the validity of the divorce itself, only about the social misperception that might follow. Once the {\i get} has been given and the divorce has taken effect, she is fully and retroactively divorced, with no restrictions on her remarriage.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Gittin 80\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Defects Beyond Giving\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shift in Focus\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Today\u8217's daf is 80, and we pick up with the {\i mishna} at the bottom of 79b. Until now, the extended {\i mishna} \u8212- really at least two {\i mishnayot} joined together \u8212- has been occupied with the act of giving the {\i get}: what constitutes the woman\u8217's domain, the man\u8217's domain, and the precise mechanics of the act of giving itself, with enormous discussion among the {\i rishonim} about whether these definitions parallel those of standard {\i kinyanim} or operate according to independent principles. We have now completely switched gears. The brief {\i mishna} encountered yesterday about an old {\i get} \u8212- a {\i get} written before {\i yichud} \u8212- had nothing to do with the act of giving; it introduced the topic of {\i psulim}, of problems internal to the {\i get} document itself. That transition marks the character of the current {\i mishna}, which addresses additional categories of defects in the {\i get}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Get Yashan Review\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 That earlier case of the {\i get yashan} \u8212- the old {\i get} \u8212- served as the threshold to this new domain. Where previously we asked questions about the giving, we now ask questions about the document. The {\i mishna} before us identifies specific categories of textual problems and their consequences, and it is worth keeping in mind from the outset that these are not all cases of equal severity. The {\i gemara} will later probe whether every category of {\i psul} generates the same grave consequences, or whether different degrees of defect carry different legal weight.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Improper Government\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishna: Wrong Dating\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} states: {\i kasav lishum malchus she\u8217'eino hogenet} \u8212- if the {\i get} was written in the name of an improper government, it is invalid. The {\i gemara} will explain that this refers specifically to dating by the Roman {\i caesar}, and will call Rome a {\i malchus she\u8217'eino hogenet} \u8212- an improper government \u8212- even if the year written is technically accurate.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Historical Problem\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This immediately raises a striking historical difficulty. The {\i mishna} was authored in Roman Palestine. The Jews of that period lived under Roman sovereignty. How, then, can dating by Rome be characterized as naming a {\i malchus she\u8217'eino hogenet}? Rashi appears to frame the {\i gemara}\u8217's reasoning as follows: the problem is that one is dating by a government that is not one\u8217's own \u8212- not one\u8217's recognized local authority \u8212- giving a kind of recognition to a competing sovereign. But this framing seems historically incoherent. If the {\i mishna} was composed under Roman rule, Rome {\i was} the local authority. It is difficult to understand how dating by Rome could constitute using the wrong government\u8217's dating system. This tension cannot be fully resolved, and the {\i gemara}\u8217's explanation \u8212- which we will see centers on {\i shalom malchus}, on giving proper acknowledgment to the reigning power \u8212- presupposes that the relevant authority and the Roman regime are distinct, which historically seems hard to sustain.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Alternative Datings\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} continues: {\i lishum malchus Madai, lishum malchus Yavan} \u8212- if the {\i get} was dated by the Median kingdom or by the Greek kingdom, it is similarly invalid. The {\i gemara} will treat this as a separate and significant {\i chiddush}, since these are dead kingdoms \u8212- the Medians and the Greeks have long since ceased to exist as sovereign powers. One might have thought that invoking a long-defunct monarchy poses no {\i shalom malchus} problem at all, since there is no living competing government whose honor is at stake. The {\i mishna} therefore teaches that even naming a {\i get} after an ancient, dead {\i malchus} is problematic.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Notably, the {\i mishna} includes two further categories: {\i u\u8217'v\u8217'vinyan habayit} and {\i u\u8217'v\u8217'churban habayit} \u8212- dating by the construction of the {\i Beit Hamikdash} or by its destruction. These would seem to be purely Jewish, internally religious datings with no political valence at all, yet they too are invalid. It is worth observing that it was precisely these categories \u8212- {\i minyan shtarot} (the Seleucid dating system, which began with the establishment of the Seleucid monarchy in 311 BCE and is what the {\i mishna} likely means by {\i malchus Yavan}), {\i binyan habayit}, and {\i churban habayit} \u8212- that constituted the historically known and commonly used Jewish dating systems. The {\i mishna} invalidates all of them. The clear implication is that there is no intrinsic prohibition against dating a {\i get} by these systems; the problem is contextual. In the environment the {\i mishna} envisions, these datings were simply not the appropriate ones to use.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Principle: Current Authority\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The consistent principle that emerges from all these cases is that a {\i get} must be dated according to the government and country under which the parties currently live, not by a foreign power, a competing authority, or a dead kingdom. This, as the {\i gemara} will explain, reflects the requirement of {\i shalom malchus} \u8212- giving proper fealty and acknowledgment to the existing ruling authority. The same logic extends even to one\u8217's own Jewish historical datings, since using them instead of the current government\u8217's calendar fails to provide that acknowledgment.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Wrong Location\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishna Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} also addresses a separate set of problems: a {\i get} written in the wrong city \u8212- specifically, where the scribe and witnesses indicated the wrong location for where the {\i get} was being written.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 East-West Problem\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} clarifies the precise scenario. {\i Haya bamizrach v\u8217'chasav bama\u8217'arav} \u8212- the {\i sofer} and {\i edim} were in the east of the city but wrote in the {\i get} that they were in the west, or vice versa. To use a contemporary analogy: the {\i get} begins, as a {\i ketubah} does, by specifying the place \u8212- we are here in New York, New York, on such-and-such a date \u8212- but the {\i sofer} wrote a city where he was not actually sitting when he wrote the document. That constitutes an error of location.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Consequences Apply\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 These location errors generate the same severe legal consequences as the government-dating errors described above. The {\i gemara} will identify this entire set of stringent rulings with the position of Rabbi Meir, and we will see the full range of those consequences in the following section.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rabbi Meir\u8217's Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Paradoxical Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The severity of the consequences in these cases derives from a paradoxical status created for the woman. In cases where the {\i get} is {\i batel miderabbanan} \u8212- invalid by Rabbinic law \u8212- yet valid {\i mide\u8217'oraita} \u8212- by Torah law \u8212- she finds herself in the worst of both worlds. From the Torah\u8217's perspective she remains married to her first husband; from the Rabbinic perspective the {\i get} is treated as so problematic that she is regarded as no longer married to him either. When she then marries a second man, she is simultaneously considered married to two people by two different legal frameworks, and the consequences cascade from that compound status.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mamzer Consequence\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Meir articulates his foundational principle: {\i kol hamishaneh mimatbei\u8217'ah shetavu chachamim begittin havlad mamzer} \u8212- anyone who deviates from the formulation established by the Sages for {\i gittin}, the child is a {\i mamzer}. Rabbi Meir is extraordinarily exacting about adherence to the precise text and form of the {\i get}. Any deviation from that established form \u8212- even if the {\i get} remains valid {\i mide\u8217'oraita} \u8212- triggers these consequences according to his view. It is worth pausing on the phrase {\i mimatbei\u8217'ah shetavu chachamim} \u8212- from the coin-stamp that the Sages minted. This type of language appears in other halachic contexts as well, such as the fixed texts of prayer, and carries the implication that the Sages have established a precise formulation that may not be varied. In {\i gittin}, where the formulation is in some respects variable \u8212- the {\i sofer} cannot know in advance which {\i malchus} will be relevant, or exactly what city he will be in \u8212- there are nonetheless very precise rules about how the text is to be constructed. When those rules are violated, the text has been changed, and Rabbi Meir treats such a change as maximally consequential.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Double Divorce\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The first consequence is {\i tetzei mizeh umizeh} \u8212- she must leave both men and receive a {\i get} from each. She cannot remain with the second husband because, from the Torah-law perspective, she was still married to the first husband when she married him, making the second marriage adulterous. She cannot return to the first husband because, from the Rabbinic perspective, the first {\i get} is being treated as valid, meaning she committed adultery with the second man, which permanently bars her from returning to the first. She therefore needs a {\i get} from both: from the second man because we treat her as having been married to him, and from the first man because we treat his original {\i get} as though it effected a divorce.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Financial Loss\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Beyond this, {\i ve\u8217'ein lah lo ketubah, velo peiros, velo mezonot, velo bl\u8217'ot, lo mizeh v\u8217'lo mizeh} \u8212- she does not collect her {\i ketubah} from either husband, she does not receive {\i peiros} (the produce proceeds that would otherwise be a husband\u8217's entitlement during marriage, which Rashi understands here as what she would receive in exchange), she does not receive {\i mezonot} from either party while awaiting her {\i get}, and she does not receive reimbursement for worn-out clothing from either husband.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Unfair Burden\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What is striking, and deeply troubling, is that the woman bears the overwhelming weight of all these consequences despite having played no active role in the defect. It was the scribe who wrote the wrong date or the wrong location. She passively received the {\i get}. Yet she loses her {\i ketubah} from both husbands, her {\i mezonot}, her {\i bl\u8217'ot}, and more. Rashi, drawing on the {\i gemara} in {\i Yevamot}, attempts to explain this as a kind of penalty, arguing that she should have been more careful. But this explanation is difficult to accept. The more literate party in ancient society was the man; she had no ability to verify the technical details of the {\i get} text she received. Rashi\u8217's explanation makes more sense in the parallel case in {\i Yevamot}, where a woman remarried on the basis of a single witness\u8217's testimony that her husband had died, and the husband subsequently reappeared \u8212- there, one can at least argue she should have been more cautious before relying on a single witness. But in the case of a defective {\i get}, the fault lies entirely elsewhere.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A more honest reading may be that this is not a punishment at all, but simply an unfortunate consequence of her indeterminate legal status. Consider the {\i ketubah}: how could she collect her {\i ketubah} from the second husband if, from one legal lens, she was committing adultery with him and was never validly married to him? How could she collect it from the first husband if, from another legal lens, she was committing adultery against him? The financial deprivations are not the product of moral blame; they are the structural result of a status that is simultaneously married and unmarried. That said, one must acknowledge the profound unfairness of the situation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Furthermore, {\i im nitla mizeh umizeh tachzir} \u8212- if she was actually given any of these payments, she must return them. This is not merely a case of {\i hamotzi mechavero alav hara\u8217'ayah}, where the burden of proof rests on the party seeking to extract money; she must proactively return it even if she is already in possession. This detail does suggest something more than a neutral consequence and more than a mere technical result of her status \u8212- though why she should bear such an active obligation remains difficult to explain satisfactorily.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Impurity & Property\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i V\u8217'lo zeh v\u8217'zeh mitam\u8217'im lah} \u8212- if she dies and either husband is a {\i kohen}, he does not become {\i tamei} to bury her. {\i V\u8217'lo zeh v\u8217'zeh zaka\u8217'im lo bimtziatah v\u8217'lo bima\u8217'aseh yadeha v\u8217'lo b\u8217'hafarat nedareha} \u8212- neither husband is entitled to her found objects, her labor, or the right to annul her vows. Here, at least, the husbands too lose something; they have no rights over her, even as she remains legally entangled with both of them.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Priesthood Disqualification\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Haytah bat Yisrael nifselah min hakehunah} \u8212- if she was a {\i bat Yisrael}, she becomes disqualified from marrying a {\i kohen}, since she is considered to have committed relations with someone who was not her husband while she was still married \u8212- rendering her a {\i zonah} in the technical sense. The point is somewhat complicated by the fact that the first {\i get}, even if Rabbinically defective, is technically valid {\i mide\u8217'oraita}, meaning she is already a {\i gerushah} by Torah law and would in any case be disqualified from {\i kehunah}. But the additional disqualification follows as a consequence of the adulterous union. {\i Bat Levi min hama\u8217'aser} \u8212- a daughter of a Levite loses her right to {\i ma\u8217'aser}, which requires its own analysis in {\i Yevamot}. {\i Bat Kohen min hatrumah} \u8212- a daughter of a {\i kohen} loses her right to eat {\i terumah}, since the verse {\i v\u8217'nitma\u8217'ah} is stated three times in connection with a woman who commits adultery, each instance disqualifying her from a different priestly privilege.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Inheritance & Children\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i V\u8217'ein yorshim shel zeh v\u8217'yorshim shel zeh yorshim ktuvatah} \u8212- neither husband\u8217's heirs inherit her {\i ketubah}. This refers to the institution of {\i ktuvat benin dichrin}: when a woman brings a dowry into a marriage, that property, if she predeceases her husband, passes through him but then descends specifically to her children rather than to all of the husband\u8217's heirs from various wives. In this case, because she is in a status of double marriage, that protection is lost. Each husband does inherit from her while she is alive (which itself raises questions, since she receives nothing from them), but upon their deaths, the property descends equally to all heirs rather than being reserved for her children. This seems manifestly unfair \u8212- she loses the very protection the {\i ketubah} institution was designed to provide \u8212- but it reflects the logical consequence of a status that cannot be resolved cleanly into either marriage.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Regarding the {\i mamzeirim}: an important clarification is necessary. The {\i mamzer} status for children from the first husband applies only to children born after she married the second man \u8212- not retroactively. The {\i gemara} makes this explicit: a woman who commits adultery and returns to her first husband does not produce {\i mamzeirim}; {\i mamzeirim} result from a union where she is married to a second man. It is specifically children born to her by the first husband after she went through the second marriage (without a proper {\i get} from the second man) who are {\i mamzeirim}. Children from the second husband are {\i mamzeirim} because she is considered still validly married to the first husband at the time of those relations. But the first husband\u8217's children are {\i mamzeirim} only if she returned to him while still married to the second \u8212- not simply because she received a defective {\i get}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yibum Issues\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i V\u8217'im meis achiv shel zeh v\u8217'achiv shel zeh choltzin v\u8217'lo meyabmin} \u8212- if either husband dies before giving her a {\i get}, his brother must perform {\i chalitzah} and may not perform {\i yibbum}. Since she is in a {\i safek} status with respect to each husband \u8212- possibly married, possibly not \u8212- {\i yibbum} cannot be performed, for she might not be a true {\i yevamah} to the brother; yet since she might be, {\i chalitzah} is required.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Changing Names\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishna Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} continues: {\i shina shemo u\u8217'shmah, shemo u\u8217'shemah, o shemo v\u8217'shem iro} \u8212- if the {\i get} changed the name of the husband, the wife, or their cities, the same consequences apply and she must leave both men.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Definition of Change\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot clarifies: {\i lo shina mamash} \u8212- this does not mean a complete and obvious substitution of one name for another (writing a completely different name is clearly invalid on its face). Rather, the {\i mishna} speaks of a change that is subtle \u8212- a nickname, a slight variation in spelling, an imprecise rendering of a name. The {\i gemara} at 34b discusses this in the context of {\i ramya aspasta}, where the issue is that all secondary names and nicknames must be included, or that the name given was slightly imprecise. The principle is that it is worse to do it incorrectly than to omit it entirely, and Tosafot engages with that problem in detail.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Same Consequences\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} attributes this ruling \u8212- {\i tetzei mizeh umizeh v\u8217'chol hadrachim ha\u8217'elu bah} \u8212- to Rabbi Meir\u8217's position, consistent with his general principle about any deviation from the established {\i matbei\u8217'a} of {\i gittin}. All the consequences catalogued above apply equally to cases of name changes or location changes in the names of the parties.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Arayot & Ailonit\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Arayah Exemption\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} now turns to a different category of cases that generate the same severe consequences, though these have nothing to do with a defective text. The principle of {\i kol ha\u8217'arayot she\u8217'amru tzaroteihen mutarot} \u8212- that whenever one woman is an {\i ervah} to the surviving brother (and thus forbidden to him for {\i yibbum}), her co-wife is also exempt from both {\i yibbum} and {\i chalitzah} \u8212- is central to the opening {\i mishna} of {\i Yevamot}. The classic example: a man married his brother\u8217's daughter (his niece). Since a niece is an {\i ervah} to the surviving brother (who is her uncle), she is exempt from {\i yibbum}, and her co-wife is consequently also exempt and may freely remarry without {\i chalitzah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ailonit Discovery\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The scenario the {\i mishna} presents: the co-wife, relying on her exemption, went and married another man. Subsequently, the woman who was the {\i ervah} \u8212- say, the niece \u8212- grew up and was discovered to be an {\i ailonit}, a woman who never developed sexually, and who would have been a {\i mekach ta\u8217'ut} \u8212- a mistaken transaction \u8212- had her condition been known at the time of marriage. Since presumably neither party knew, the marriage is retroactively voided. There was no deliberate deception; the facts simply became apparent later. But the legal consequence is that retroactively he was never married to his niece. She was therefore never an {\i ervah} to the surviving brother. When the first husband died, the co-wife was therefore always obligated in {\i yibbum} or {\i chalitzah} \u8212- she simply did not know it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Co-Wife\u8217's Problem\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The co-wife\u8217's situation is that she has been married to her second husband, perhaps for twenty years, in the genuine belief that she was free to do so. Nobody deceived her; she had every legal reason to believe she required neither {\i chalitzah} nor {\i yibbum}. Yet retroactively, because the {\i ailonit} determination voids the original marriage, the co-wife was always {\i zekukah l\u8217'yibbum} \u8212- bound to the surviving brother \u8212- before she remarried. The prohibition she violated is {\i lo tihyeh eishet hamet hachutzah le\u8217'ish zar}, and the {\i gemara} understands the resulting child as a {\i mamzer} according to the position of Rabbi Akiva, who holds that a child born from a union prohibited by a {\i lav} is a {\i mamzer}. This may reflect Rabbi Akiva\u8217's general position on all {\i issur lav} relationships, or it may reflect something more specific to {\i yibbum}, where the status of {\i zekukah leyibbum} creates a quasi-{\i kiddushin} bond between the woman and the surviving brother, making her union with another man more analogous to adultery.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Consequences Apply\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tetzei mizeh umizeh v\u8217'chol hadrachim ha\u8217'elu bah} \u8212- she must leave both men and suffers all of the consequences catalogued by Rabbi Meir. The unfairness here is, if anything, more acute than in the {\i get} cases. At least in those cases one could say the parties should have had expert supervision of the {\i get} text. Here, the co-wife did everything correctly given the information available to her, and she is still subjected to all these consequences. What she was supposed to do to prevent this is unclear: as one might ask, was the court supposed to wait, however many years it takes, to determine whether the {\i ervah} was an {\i ailonit} before permitting the co-wife to remarry? Or should the {\i ervah} have been brought to perform {\i chalitzah} just in case \u8212- which itself would have entailed disqualifying her from {\i kehunah} and attendant consequences? The situation is genuinely without good remedy.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Yibum with Ailonit\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Scenario Setup\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A further variation: a man performed {\i yibbum} with one of two {\i yevamot}. The co-wife, understanding herself to be freed by the {\i yibbum}, went and married another man. Subsequently, the woman with whom {\i yibbum} was performed was discovered to be an {\i ailonit}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Nullification\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The retroactive consequence: the {\i yibbum} never legally occurred, since the {\i ailonit}\u8217's original marriage was a {\i mekach ta\u8217'ut} and she was therefore never truly the {\i yevamah}. The {\i zikah} \u8212- the levirate bond \u8212- was never discharged. The co-wife therefore still required {\i yibbum} or {\i chalitzah} from the original brother-in-law before she could remarry.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Unfair Penalty\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tetzei mizeh umizeh v\u8217'chol hadrachim ha\u8217'elu bah} \u8212- she must separate from her second husband and suffers all the consequences. As in the previous case, she did nothing wrong; the situation arose through no fault of hers. Yet the severity of the consequences follows her.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Mistaken Documents\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mix-up Scenario\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} presents a case of scribal error of a different kind: {\i katav sofer get la\u8217'ish v\u8217'shovar la\u8217'ishah} \u8212- the {\i sofer} prepared the {\i get} for the husband and a {\i shovar} (a receipt acknowledging payment of the {\i ketubah}) for the wife. The design was that the wife, upon receiving payment of her {\i ketubah}, would hand the receipt to the husband; the husband would hand the {\i get} to her. The {\i sofer} inadvertently delivered the documents in reverse: the wife received the receipt and the husband received the {\i get}. {\i V\u8217'natnu zeh lazeh} \u8212- and they exchanged documents, each believing they had the correct one. Perhaps both parties were not fully literate, or perhaps the documents were handled quickly and without close inspection.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Unmarried Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The result is that she is not actually divorced \u8212- she received a receipt, not a {\i get} \u8212- and yet she believes herself to be divorced. If she subsequently married another man, she remained, from the Torah\u8217's perspective, married to her first husband throughout.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Later Discovery\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i V\u8217'le\u8217'achar zman harei haget yotzei miyad ha\u8217'ish v\u8217'hashovar miyad ha\u8217'ishah} \u8212- some time later, someone examines the documents and discovers that the husband is holding the {\i get} and the wife the receipt \u8212- the reverse of what they should have.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Consequences\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tetzei mizeh umizeh v\u8217'chol hadrachim ha\u8217'elu bah} \u8212- she must leave her second husband and suffers all the consequences. She married her second husband genuinely believing she was divorced, and yet all the penalties attach.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Immediate Discovery\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Eliezer offers a partial exception: {\i im l\u8217'altar yatza ein zeh get} \u8212- if the mistake is discovered immediately, when the documents are examined right away at the time of exchange, then plainly no valid {\i get} was given, the error is clear, and the relevant consequences follow straightforwardly.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Delayed Discovery\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 However, {\i v\u8217'im l\u8217'achar zman yatza} \u8212- if the error surfaces only after a significant period has elapsed \u8212- Rabbi Eliezer rules differently: {\i lo kol heimenu min harishon l\u8217'abeid zchuto shel sheni} \u8212- the first husband does not have the power to retroactively destroy the second husband\u8217's rights. If the first husband, a year after giving what purported to be a {\i get}, asks to examine the document and it turns out to be a {\i shovar}, Rabbi Eliezer does not accept the resulting claim that the {\i get} was never given. After the passage of time, who can verify that these are the same documents as were originally exchanged? Perhaps the first husband himself swapped the documents in order to reclaim his wife and undermine the second marriage. The {\i gemara} will discuss how much time must elapse for this rule of Rabbi Eliezer to apply.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Contemporary Dating\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 City of Origin\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot raises an important practical question about the {\i mishna}\u8217's prohibition on giving the wrong city. Rabbeinu Yitzchak ({\i Ri}) observes that the requirement to give the correct city applies specifically to the city where the parties currently reside \u8212- {\i ir she\u8217'darim bah}. However, {\i ir she\u8217'nolad bah} \u8212- the city of birth, the city of origin \u8212- is a different matter: {\i afilu shina kasher}, even if the city of origin is written incorrectly, the {\i get} is valid, {\i keivan she\u8217'ein tzarich le\u8217'kotvo klal} \u8212- because there is no obligation to write the city of origin at all.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Historical Context\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot notes that older practice did include the city of origin: {\i ve\u8217'hakadmonim nahagu le\u8217'kotvo} \u8212- the earlier generations customarily wrote it. The reason was identification: {\i mishum shema yihiyeh arar she\u8217'yesh sham Yosef ben Shimon acher yihiyeh shemo v\u8217'shem iro mekom leida} \u8212- because if there might be a challenge and another man named Yosef ben Shimon existed elsewhere, having the city of birth as well would serve to identify the correct individual. It is analogous to the function of a family name. Indeed, as was noted, these place-names very often became the basis for family names over time.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Since the city of origin was never required, one might have thought that if one writes it and gets it wrong, that is worse than not writing it at all \u8212- a case of doing the optional thing incorrectly. But Tosafot rejects this conclusion on practical grounds.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Practical Evidence\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i De\u8217'chazinan de\u8217'kama pe\u8217'amim kotvim mekom leida pi ha\u8217'ba\u8217'al o ha\u8217'ishah} \u8212- Tosafot observes that in practice, the city of birth was simply written on the basis of what the husband or wife stated, without any independent verification. No proof of origin was demanded. Tosafot argues this demonstrates that the city of origin cannot be {\i pasul be\u8217'di\u8217'avad} if incorrect: if getting it wrong would invalidate the {\i get}, one would not simply accept the husband\u8217's word without some form of objective evidence. In fact, {\i Ri} himself validated a {\i get} where the city of birth turned out to be incorrect. And in cases where the city of birth was simply not known \u8212- the family had moved when the person was young, for instance \u8212- {\i Ri} ruled that the city of origin should be omitted entirely rather than guessed at.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Improper Government (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gemara: Rome\u8217's Definition\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now formally addresses the opening clause of the {\i mishna}. {\i Mai malchus she\u8217'eina hogenet?} \u8212- what is an improper government? {\i Malchus ha\u8217'romim} \u8212- Rome. And why call it {\i malchus she\u8217'eina hogenet}? {\i Mishum de\u8217'ein lahem ktav v\u8217'lo lashon} \u8212- the {\i gemara}\u8217's stated reason is that the Romans have no writing or language of their own. This claim is historically puzzling, to say the least: Latin is distinctly Rome\u8217's own language, and the Latin alphabet, derived from the same part of Italy, was Rome\u8217's own as well. The precise meaning of the {\i gemara}\u8217's statement here is difficult to resolve.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Setting that aside, the deeper historical difficulty remains: if the {\i mishna} was composed in Roman Palestine, and the Jews lived under Roman sovereignty, how can dating by Rome represent dating by an {\i improper} government \u8212- a government that is not one\u8217's own? The {\i gemara}\u8217's framing of the issue as a {\i shalom malchus} problem \u8212- you are supposed to date your {\i get} according to the government under which you live, and using a different dating system fails to show proper fealty \u8212- seems to presuppose that Rome is {\i not} the government under which one lives. If the rule is that one must date by the local authority, and the local authority {\i is} Rome, then one should be writing the Roman date. The problem, as the {\i gemara} understands it, apparently lies somewhere else, but the {\i gemara} does not resolve this historical tension cleanly.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shalom Malchut Rationale\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Amar Ula: mipnei mah tiknu malchus be\u8217'gittin? Mishum shalom malchus} \u8212- why did the Sages require that {\i gittin} be dated by the reigning government? For the sake of peace with the government \u8212- {\i shalom malchus}. Tosafot adds that this requirement seems to apply specifically to {\i gittin} and not to other {\i shtarot}; other documents were historically dated by {\i minyan shtarot} and {\i binyan habayit} without concern. The reason {\i gittin} are singled out is that {\i gittin} are so consequential \u8212- they determine personal status, they separate husband and wife \u8212- that they more than any other document warranted official, government-recognized dating. The government has an interest in such documents being precisely and officially dated; dating them by an unrecognized system fails to provide that.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This rationale then drives the {\i gemara}\u8217's analysis of the other cases in the {\i mishna}. The {\i gemara} frames each case as a question of how much the government would be {\i makpid} \u8212- how much it would object \u8212- to the dating used. If you named the {\i get} after Rome (a currently existing other sovereign): {\i mishum de\u8217'malicha} \u8212- because it is an active, competing {\i malchus}, you are giving recognition to a rival power, and the local government would be distinctly offended. If you named it after Median or Greek kingdoms: {\i mah de\u8217'havah havah} \u8212- those kingdoms are gone, yet one might still argue that invoking them implies their greatness \u8212- {\i mishum de\u8217'malchuta havu} \u8212- they were great kingdoms and you are, in some fashion, honoring them over the current sovereign. If you named it after {\i binyan habayit}: {\i de\u8217'amri ka madcharei shivcheihu} \u8212- you are evoking your own ancient sovereignty and greatness, which can feel like a challenge to the current government. If you named it after {\i churban habayit}: {\i de\u8217'tza\u8217'ara hu} \u8212- that is purely an expression of mourning; the government would not feel threatened by it. Yet even this, the {\i gemara} concludes, is problematic from a {\i shalom malchus} perspective. The {\i gemara} thus frames the entire {\i mishna} as a unified teaching about the government\u8217's {\i hakpada}, and the {\i tzricha} \u8212- the necessity of including each case \u8212- lies in the fact that one might have thought each case less serious than the previous.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Contemporary Dating (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Question Posed\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot now raises the central practical question for post-Talmudic practice: how do we justify dating our contemporary documents, including {\i gittin}, {\i l\u8217'vriat olam} \u8212- according to the count from the creation of the world \u8212- rather than by the reigning government\u8217's calendar? The {\i mishna} and {\i gemara} have just established that failing to date by the current {\i malchus} is a significant problem, and yet our standard practice for centuries has been to date by the Jewish calendar rather than by the regnal year of whatever king or government is currently in power.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Government Indifference\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot cites Rav Yosef\u8217's explanation: {\i de\u8217'achshav ein hamalchus makpedet} \u8212- nowadays the government simply does not care how Jews date their documents. The concern that gave rise to the requirement \u8212- that the government has a strong interest in {\i gittin} being dated by the official calendar \u8212- no longer applies in the historical circumstances Tosafot is addressing. Since the {\i shalom malchus} concern has lapsed, the requirement to date by the regnant government\u8217's calendar has correspondingly lapsed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Practical Ruling\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i V\u8217'kayma lan k\u8217'Chachamim} \u8212- and we rule according to the Sages, not according to Rabbi Meir, in this matter. The {\i mishna}\u8217's stringent position, which the {\i gemara} attributes to Rabbi Meir, is not the operative {\i halacha}. The Sages\u8217' more lenient position is the practical ruling.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 No Malchut Issue\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The conclusion Tosafot draws is two-fold: first, even if one were to rule like Rabbi Meir, dating {\i l\u8217'vriat olam} would not be problematic today because there is no {\i shalom malchus} issue \u8212- the government is indifferent, and therefore the underlying rationale for the requirement does not apply. Second, independent of the {\i shalom malchus} question, we do not rule like Rabbi Meir in this matter. Dating by {\i briat olam}, Tosafot concludes, is entirely permissible on both grounds.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Wrong Location (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Written vs.\u160 ?Given\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} returns to the issue of the wrong location in the {\i get}. The question arises: when the {\i get} specifies a location, what moment does that location refer to \u8212- when the {\i get} was written, or when it was given? The {\i gemara} considers the possibility that the {\i baal} (the husband) is the one referred to in {\i haya bamizrach v\u8217'chasav bama\u8217'arav}, meaning he comes from the east but wrote that he is from the west. But the {\i gemara} dismisses this reading: that would just be a case of the wrong city of residence for the husband, which is already addressed in the later clause of the {\i mishna} ({\i shina shemo u\u8217'shem iro}). The correct reading is that it refers to the {\i sofer} \u8212- specifically, where the {\i sofer} was physically located at the moment of writing. The location written in the {\i get} should reflect where the scribe was when he wrote it. What matters is where the document was created, not where the husband gave the instruction.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A natural question arises: who really cares where the {\i sofer} was? Tosafot engages with this extensively, noting that the purpose of writing a location in a {\i get} \u8212- as in a {\i ketubah} \u8212- is to record where the event took place. The difficulty is that sometimes the writing and the giving are separated: a {\i ketubah} might be written a year before the wedding and in a different city, yet reflect the location of the wedding. Similarly, a {\i get} might be written before it is given. Tosafot grapples with what happens if the {\i sofer} wrote it in one location but it was given in another, and whether the date and place should reflect the writing or the giving.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav\u8217's Rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i V\u8217'hilchta safra k\u8217'de\u8217'amar leh Rav l\u8217'safrei, v\u8217'chein amar leh Rav Huna l\u8217'safrei} \u8212- the practical ruling follows what Rav said to his scribe, and what Rav Huna similarly said to his scribe: {\i ki yasvitu b\u8217'Shili ktuvu b\u8217'Shili} \u8212- when you are sitting in Shili and writing, write that you are in Shili. {\i V\u8217'af al gav d\u8217'mimsaran l\u8217'chu mili b\u8217'Hini} \u8212- even if the husband communicated his instructions to you when you were all in Hini, if you are now writing the document in Shili, write Shili. The location to be recorded is where the {\i sofer} physically sits at the moment of writing \u8212- not where he received the instruction, and not where the husband was standing. The converse applies equally: {\i v\u8217'chi yasvitu b\u8217'Hini ktuvu b\u8217'Hini, v\u8217'af al gav d\u8217'mimsaran l\u8217'chu mili b\u8217'Shili} \u8212- if you are in Hini when you write it, write Hini, even if the instruction was given in Shili.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rabbi Meir vs.\u160 ?Sages\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Meir Strict\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Amar Rav Yehuda amar Shmuel: zu divrei Rabbi Meir} \u8212- the entire set of severe consequences we have been discussing is the position of Rabbi Meir. As the {\i gemara} restates it: {\i kol hamishaneh mimatbei\u8217'ah shetavu chachamim begittin havlad mamzer} \u8212- anyone who changes the established form of {\i gittin}, the child is a {\i mamzer}. This returns the {\i gemara} to the question of which cases are governed by Rabbi Meir\u8217's principle and which are agreed upon by all.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Governor Cases\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Local Official\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now considers a case involving a local official rather than the king: what if the {\i get} was dated in the name of a {\i santar} \u8212- a local city official \u8212- rather than in the name of the reigning monarch? The Sages rule: {\i afilu lo katav ela shem santar shebe\u8217'ir harei zu megureshes} \u8212- even if the {\i get} was written only in the name of a local city official, the divorce is valid. To use a contemporary analogy, dating a {\i get} \u8220"in the third year of the reign of the mayor of the city\u8221" would, according to the Sages, be acceptable.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Royal Appointee\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 There was a particular {\i get} that came before Rava, having been dated in the name of a governor of Bashkar who was an appointee of the king \u8212- not the king himself. {\i Shalchu Rav Nachman v\u8217'Rav Chisda l\u8217'kamei d\u8217'Rava: ki hai gavna mai?} \u8212- is such a dating valid or not? {\i Shalach lehu: be\u8217'ha afilu Rabbi Meir modeh} \u8212- even Rabbi Meir agrees that this is valid. {\i Me\u8217'ota malchus hu} \u8212- he is an official of the same government. You have not given honor to a foreign power; you have simply named the official agent of the legitimate authority.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Official Rank\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The distinction between the governor of Bashkar and the {\i santar} of the city is then established. {\i U\u8217'mai shna mi\u8217'santar shebe\u8217'ir?} \u8212- how is the appointed governor different from the petty city official, given that the Sages accept both and Rabbi Meir accepts the governor? {\i Hatam zila leh milta} \u8212- in the case of the {\i santar}, the matter is considered disrespectful to the {\i malchus}: the {\i santar}, as Rashi describes him, is a minor, perhaps insignificant local functionary, possibly not even a government official in any meaningful sense. Dating a {\i get} after him is almost a mockery of the idea of government dating. {\i Hacha shevicha leh milta} \u8212- in the case of the appointed governor, the matter redounds to the honor of the {\i malchus}, since he is a significant official formally appointed by the king. The distinction, then, is not merely between a local and a national official, but between an important, formally appointed official and a petty functionary of no real standing.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rabbi Meir vs.\u160 ?Sages (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sages Lenient\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Amar Rav Abba amar Rav Huna amar Rav: zu divrei Rabbi Meir. Aval Chachamim omrim ha\u8217'vlad kasher} \u8212- the position that wrong dating results in {\i mamzer} status for the child belongs to Rabbi Meir alone. The Sages hold that even if the dating was done incorrectly \u8212- even if the wrong {\i malchus} was named \u8212- the child is {\i kasher}, not a {\i mamzer}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Areas Agree\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 However, {\i u\u8217'modim Chachamim l\u8217'Rabbi Meir she\u8217'im shina shmo u\u8217'shmah shem iro v\u8217'shem irah she\u8217'havlad mamzer} \u8212- the Sages concede to Rabbi Meir that if the names of the husband or wife, or their cities, were changed, the child is a {\i mamzer}. Changing the name of a party to the {\i get} or the name of their city is far more central to the document than the dating system used \u8212- these names identify the very people between whom the divorce is effected. Even if such a change does not necessarily render the {\i get} invalid {\i mide\u8217'oraita} (since it may be a small variation rather than a wholesale substitution), it is sufficiently central to the document that the Sages join Rabbi Meir in applying the {\i mamzer} consequence.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishna Structure\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Amar Rav Ashi: af anan nami tanina} \u8212- Rav Ashi notes that this agreement between the Sages and Rabbi Meir on the name-and-city issue is evident from the structure of the {\i mishna} itself. The {\i mishna} states the case of wrong dating and its consequences, and then separately states: {\i shina shemo u\u8217'shmah shem iro v\u8217'shem irah tetzei mizeh umizeh v\u8217'chol hadrachim ha\u8217'elu bah} \u8212- if he changed the names or locations, she must leave both and all these consequences apply. {\i Man tana lah?} \u8212- who is the author of this second clause? If we say it is Rabbi Meir, then it should simply have been listed together with the first clause and all the other cases of wrong dating. The fact that the {\i mishna} states the name-and-city clause separately \u8212- as a distinct addition \u8212- indicates {\i shma mina rabbanan}: this reflects the position of the Sages as well, not only Rabbi Meir. The Sages, even in their leniency regarding dating errors, agree that changing the names of the parties or their cities carries the full severity of consequences. This explains the extraordinary care taken in the drafting of a {\i get} with respect to names and locations \u8212- because on this point, the consequences are severe according to all opinions.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Gittin 81\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Co-Wife Status Dilemma\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Invalid Co-Wife Setup\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The opening sugya of today\u8217's {\i daf} continues the {\i Mishnah}\u8217's discussion of cases in which a woman suffers severe consequences \u8212- {\i teitzei mizeh umizeh}, {\i uvalad mamzer}, and all the attendant prohibitions \u8212- despite being entirely innocent. Having treated the case of an incorrectly written {\i get} and the case of mistaken names or locations, the {\i Mishnah} now addresses a situation arising from {\i yibbum} law. A woman was waiting for either {\i yibbum} or {\i chalitzah} from her brother-in-law, but the obligation was in fact void because her co-wife turned out to be an {\i erva} \u8212- a woman forbidden to the brother-in-law \u8212- meaning there was never a genuine bond of co-wife status to begin with. Under that misapprehension, however, the waiting woman went ahead and remarried. When the truth emerged, all of the severe consequences applied.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Hamnuna\u8217's Rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} notes that the {\i Mishnah} specifies {\i nisu\u8217'in} \u8212- actual marriage \u8212- as the triggering act, but says nothing about a case where the woman merely had sexual relations with another man ({\i zinu}). This omission prompts the {\i Gemara} to ask whether this silence constitutes a challenge to Rav Hamnuna, who rules that a {\i shomeret yavam} \u8212- a woman awaiting {\i yibbum} \u8212- who commits a sexual act with another man is thereby forbidden to the {\i yavam}. If sexual relations with another man render her forbidden to the brother-in-law, then that act should carry the same severity as remarriage, and the same catalogue of negative consequences ought to follow.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Consequence Discrepancy\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} thus frames the discrepancy sharply: if sexual relations suffice to create the forbidden status described by Rav Hamnuna, why does the {\i Mishnah} restrict the consequences to the case of {\i nisu\u8217'in}? The implication seems to be that the {\i Mishnah} holds sexual relations short of marriage do not trigger these consequences \u8212- which would undercut Rav Hamnuna\u8217's position.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Clean Language Explanati\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} resolves this by invoking the principle of {\i lishna ma\u8217'alya} \u8212- clean or elevated language. The {\i Mishnah} chose to phrase the case in terms of {\i nisu\u8217'in} out of linguistic propriety. The actual law, however, is identical whether she married another man or merely had sexual relations with him; in either case, {\i vehu hadin lezinus}, the same negative consequences apply. The {\i Mishnah}\u8217's use of the term \u8220"married\u8221" is not {\i davka} \u8212- it is simply the more refined way to express the matter.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Alternate Version\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 An alternative version of this teaching frames the relationship between {\i nisu\u8217'in} and {\i zinus} in reverse: since the {\i Mishnah} says {\i nisu\u8217'in}, and {\i nisu\u8217'in} presumably also implies sexual relations, this version reads the {\i Mishnah} as supporting Rav Hamnuna \u8212- {\i leima mesayeya leih l\u8217'Rav Hamnuna} \u8212- since even sexual relations short of formal marriage would generate these consequences.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} then pushes back on this alternative reading and insists that the {\i Mishnah} should be read {\i davka} \u8212- only {\i nisu\u8217'in} triggers the consequences, not sexual relations alone. The reason is that the paradigm case underlying all these rulings is the scenario of a woman who heard her husband had died, remarried, and then the husband reappeared \u8212- {\i mishum demichalfa b\u8217'eishes ish shehalach ba\u8217'alah l\u8217'medinat hayam}. That model case involves remarriage, not mere sexual relations, and therefore the {\i Mishnah} deliberately frames matters in terms of the woman having taken a second husband.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Invalid Yibum Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yibum with Invalid\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Mishnah} then presents a companion case: a man actually performed {\i yibbum} with one of his late brother\u8217's wives, which led the other wife to conclude she was free from any further obligation and entitled to remarry. It subsequently emerged that the wife with whom {\i yibbum} was performed was not in fact obligated \u8212- she was, for example, an {\i aylonit} \u8212- rendering the {\i yibbum} retroactively invalid. Nevertheless, the other wife who had remarried is subject to all the same severe consequences.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two Cases Necessity\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} explains why both cases \u8212- the {\i erva} co-wife and the invalid {\i yibbum} \u8212- are necessary and cannot be collapsed into one ruling. With respect to the first case alone, one might have reasoned that because no act of {\i yibbum} or {\i chalitzah} was ever performed, the woman bore some residual responsibility to investigate before simply walking away from the situation. In the second case, by contrast, an act of {\i yibbum} was actually carried out \u8212- {\i dikayim mitzvas yibbum} \u8212- and one might have said that since everyone did everything correctly, the other wife should be entirely free of negative consequences. Conversely, had only the second case been stated, one might have concluded that because the situation did carry a genuine obligation of {\i yibbum} from the outset, people who participated in it are held to a higher standard even when they acted in good faith. Hence {\i tzricha} \u8212- both cases are required.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Innocent Yet Punished\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This leaves standing what the {\i Gemara} itself acknowledges is a deeply troubling reality: these women are entirely innocent, yet they bear the full weight of consequences as severe as any deliberate transgression. The {\i Gemara} offers no fully satisfying resolution to this basic moral difficulty, and it is important not to paper over that discomfort.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Scribe\u8217's Document Mix-Up\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mixed Documents Setup\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Mishnah} turns to a scenario of scribal error: a scribe was asked to write a receipt ({\i shover}) for a man and a {\i get} for a woman, and he accidentally reversed the documents, giving the receipt to the woman and the {\i get} to the man. Since the woman received a receipt rather than a {\i get}, she is not divorced \u8212- {\i lo megureshet}. Rabbi Elazar, however, introduces a temporal qualification: the problem with the incorrect documents can be raised only {\i l\u8217'alter} \u8212- immediately \u8212- but not after a period of time has passed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Immediacy Requirement\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Elazar\u8217's ruling generates an obvious question: what constitutes {\i l\u8217'alter} and what constitutes {\i l\u8217'achar zman}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 L\u8217'Alter Definition\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Yehuda in the name of Shmuel defines {\i l\u8217'alter} as the period during which the parties are still sitting and engaged in the same matter \u8212- {\i kol zman sheyoshvin v\u8217'oskin b\u8217'oto inyan}. As long as the parties remain present and the conversation has not concluded, discovering the error falls within {\i l\u8217'alter} and the problem can be raised.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 After Meeting Breaks\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Once the meeting breaks \u8212- {\i amdu} \u8212- any moment thereafter constitutes {\i l\u8217'achar zman}. From that point forward, we cannot know with certainty what transpired between then and the time the document is inspected, and the first husband therefore loses the ability to challenge the divorce. This definition effectively limits the window of challenge to the duration of the transaction itself, a remarkably narrow timeframe.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Alternative: No Remarria\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Adda bar Ahava offers a substantially different definition. {\i L\u8217'alter} extends for as long as the woman has not yet remarried \u8212- {\i lo niset zehu l\u8217'alter}. Under this reading, even ten years later, as long as she has not taken a second husband, the problem can still be raised. Once she marries a second man, {\i niset zehu l\u8217'achar zman}, that constitutes the temporal threshold beyond which the first husband can no longer upend her new marriage.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Right to Remarry\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} notes that the {\i Mishnah} specifically mentions a second husband \u8212- {\i lo kol heimenu min harishon l\u8217'aved zchuto shel sheni} \u8212- the first husband cannot act to destroy the rights of the second. This language fits naturally with Rav Adda bar Ahava\u8217's view, since it is the existence of the second marriage that blocks the challenge. The {\i Gemara} then asks how Shmuel\u8217's view accounts for the {\i Mishnah}\u8217's mention of a second party. Shmuel answers: {\i zchut harauya l\u8217'sheni} \u8212- once she leaves the meeting, she has acquired the right to remarry. The {\i Mishnah}\u8217's reference to the second husband is not about a second marriage already having occurred, but about the right that crystallizes the moment the meeting concludes. Once she walks out the door with the right to remarry, the first husband cannot retroactively vitiate that right on the basis of a document discrepancy.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Get Written Then Retract\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Beit Shammai Stringent\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Mishnah} now presents the case of a man who instructed a scribe to write a {\i get} for his wife and then changed his mind \u8212- {\i v\u8217'nimlach} \u8212- and the divorce never took place. Beit Shammai rules: {\i pasla min hakehuna} \u8212- she is disqualified from marrying a {\i kohen} even though no {\i get} was ever given and the marriage remained intact.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rumor Problem\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The reasoning is striking and worth examining carefully. The moment a man approaches a scribe and commissions a {\i get}, word begins to circulate \u8212- {\i yatza alav kol} \u8212- a rumor spreads that the husband is divorcing his wife. Even if the {\i get} is ultimately never delivered, even if the couple continues living together, the perception that a divorce occurred or was in process has been created. Beit Shammai holds that once this {\i rei\u8217'ach} \u8212- this whiff \u8212- of divorce has been generated, we must take it seriously: if the husband subsequently dies and the woman seeks to remarry a {\i kohen}, people who heard the rumor will assume she is a {\i gerusha} and conclude that the children of that marriage are {\i chalalim}. Because the perception cannot be reliably dispelled, Beit Shammai rules preemptively: do not allow her to marry a {\i kohen} in the first place.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The difficulty with this position is obvious, and the {\i Gemara} acknowledges it. The woman has done nothing wrong. Her husband wrote something and changed his mind. Yet Beit Shammai would impose a lifelong restriction on who she may marry based solely on a rumor generated by her husband\u8217's momentary intention. A student correctly notes that this concern would make much more sense if the {\i get} had been written on the husband\u8217's deathbed, where there would be little time for the couple\u8217's continued cohabitation to publicly dispel the rumor. If they lived together openly for years afterward, it is hard to understand how anyone would seriously believe she had been divorced.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Beit Hillel Lenient\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Beit Hillel takes the opposing position, framed as an even stronger case: {\i af al pi she-nasnu lah tnai v\u8217'lo na\u8217'asa hatnai, lo pasla min hakehuna} \u8212- even if he actually handed her a conditional {\i get} and the condition was not fulfilled, she is not disqualified from marrying a {\i kohen}. If the condition goes unsatisfied and she is therefore not actually divorced, there is no divorce and the law treats her accordingly. Beit Hillel refuses to impose legal consequences on the basis of a perceived status that does not correspond to reality. If she is not divorced, she is not divorced \u8212- and no rumor or perception changes that.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is worth noting the concept of {\i rei\u8217'ach hagget} \u8212- the whiff of a {\i get} \u8212- which does exist in the {\i Gemara} in other contexts. There are cases where a {\i get pasul}, a {\i get} that was invalid but close enough to a valid {\i get}, does render a woman {\i pesulah min hakehunah}. This makes Beit Hillel\u8217's position all the more striking: Beit Hillel\u8217's principle is that if a {\i get} was not actually given and did not actually effectuate a divorce, no consequence follows.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Dispelling Get Rumor\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kohen Get Rumor Query\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A question was sent from Rav Yosef son of Rav Menashya of Dovila to Shmuel: a rumor had spread in town that a certain {\i kohen} had written a {\i get} for his wife \u8212- {\i yatza alav kol ish ploni kohein kasav get l\u8217'ishto} \u8212- and yet the couple was still living together, {\i yosheves tachtav umeshamashto}. What is the law?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The case is more complex than it might appear. If the {\i kohen} did in fact give the {\i get}, he is now living with his divorced wife, which would constitute a serious prohibition for a {\i kohen} \u8212- {\i machzir grushato}. The question concerns how to respond to this rumor in the absence of established facts.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Investigation Needed\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Shmuel\u8217's response is: {\i teitzei v\u8217'hadavar tzarich bdika} \u8212- she should leave the marriage and the matter requires investigation. The {\i Gemara} notes that in other contexts, Shmuel used the phrase {\i hadavar tzarich talmud} \u8212- the matter requires further {\i halachic} analysis. Here, the matter requires {\i bdika} \u8212- factual investigation \u8212- since the question is not what the law is, but what actually occurred.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Dispel Rumor Strategy\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} then addresses what kind of investigation {\i bdika} refers to. Is the question whether, when a rumor exists, we attempt to {\i mevatel} the rumor \u8212- to publicly dispel it \u8212- or whether we accept the rumor and act accordingly?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 There is a genuine policy question embedded here. If we know the rumor is false and we publicly declare so, we might successfully neutralize it. Alternatively, attempting to fight the rumor might backfire: people who believe the rumor strongly will suspect that the {\i beit din} has been co-opted \u8212- that the husband has the rabbis in his pocket \u8212- and the attempt to dispel the rumor will only entrench the perception further. The question is therefore not merely factual but strategic: is it ever effective to fight perception with official declarations?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Naharda\u8217'a Doesn\u8217't Dispel\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} notes that {\i Naharda\u8217'a} \u8212- Shmuel\u8217's own city, the locus of his {\i halachic} authority \u8212- does not attempt to be {\i mevatel} rumors of this kind. If even in Shmuel\u8217's home community the policy is not to dispel such rumors, what does Shmuel have to say to the questioner? The practical import is that if one does not dispel rumors, one must act on them \u8212- which in this case means imposing some consequence on the couple.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Language Ambiguity\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Before reaching that conclusion, the {\i Gemara} interrogates the language of the rumor itself. When people say a man \u8220"wrote a {\i get}\u8221" for his wife, does that necessarily mean he {\i gave} the {\i get}? It is entirely possible that {\i kasav} \u8212- \u8220"wrote\u8221" \u8212- means only that: he composed a document, not that he delivered it. If the rumor is only that he wrote a {\i get} and not that he gave one, there may be no rumor of actual divorce at all, and consequently no allegation that he is living with a {\i gerusha}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Double Uncertainty\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Even granting that in common usage \u8220"wrote a {\i get}\u8221" might sometimes mean \u8220"gave a {\i get}\u8221" \u8212- {\i de-migalya milta de-karu li-nisuei ketiva} \u8212- this only means the rumor {\i might} refer to delivery. The result is a {\i safek} within a {\i safek}: there is a rumor, and the rumor might mean he gave the {\i get}, but it might not. This double uncertainty would seem to substantially weaken any grounds for imposing consequences.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Ashi Principle\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 At this point Rav Ashi\u8217's principle enters the discussion: {\i kol kala devatar nisuin lo chaishinan leih} \u8212- we do not give halachic weight to rumors that arise after a marriage has already taken place. This is a significant and welcome clarification. The concern about rumors \u8212- {\i kol} \u8212- is a {\i lechatchila} concern, a prospective one. We say: do not enter into a marriage if there is a credible rumor of prior divorce. But we do not use a rumor to undo a marriage that has already been contracted. The implications for Beit Shammai\u8217's ruling in the {\i Mishnah} are directly relevant: the perception created when a man commissions a {\i get} might lead us to restrict whom the woman may marry {\i going forward}, but it does not require dissolving any existing relationship.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Second Marriage Bar\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} thus clarifies what {\i teitzei} means in Shmuel\u8217's response: it does not mean she must be divorced from her current husband. Rather, {\i teitzei mi-sheni} \u8212- if the first husband were to die and she sought to remarry, she would not be permitted to marry a second husband, specifically a {\i kohen}. The consequence is prospective and limited, not retroactive and destructive.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Perception Creates Reali\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbis Shape Perception\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} then raises a striking counter-argument. If the consequence is only that she may not marry a second man \u8212- a {\i kohen} \u8212- while remaining with her first husband, there is a paradox: people will observe that the rabbis are permitting her to stay with her first husband while barring her from remarrying after his death. That combination of rulings will itself generate a perception. People will not say \u8220"the rumor was false.\u8221" They will construct a narrative to explain the rabbis\u8217' behavior.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Samuch L\u8217'Misa Logic\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Specifically, the {\i Gemara} says people will say {\i samuch l\u8217'misa gershah} \u8212- he must have divorced her just before he died. People will reason: the rabbis would not bar her from remarrying unless there was something to the rumor; and since she lived with her husband until his death, the divorce must have occurred right at the end. The ruling itself thus generates a new and more damaging perception than the original rumor.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is a genuinely unsettling passage. The {\i Gemara} is acknowledging that the rabbis\u8217' response to perception can itself shape perception \u8212- and yet rather than concluding that we should therefore impose no consequence at all (which would send the clearest message that the rumor was false), the {\i Gemara} proceeds to analyze which consequence is tolerable. The answer is: the limited consequence of barring remarriage to a {\i kohen} is tolerable because people who observe it will assume {\i samuch l\u8217'misa gershah} \u8212- and in that case they will understand that she was with her first husband until the end, which at least does not impugn the children of that marriage as {\i chalalim}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is, as acknowledged, a deeply difficult passage. The {\i Gemara} openly grapples with the fact that rabbinic rulings create as well as respond to social perception, and yet does not reach a fully satisfying resolution of how this interplay should be managed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yeridat Hadorot\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} introduces a teaching attributed to Rabbi Ami bar Chama citing Rabbi Yochanan in the name of Rabbi Yehuda the son of Rabbi Ilai: come and see that the later generations are not like the earlier generations \u8212- {\i bo r\u8217'eh shelo kedoros rishonim doros ha\u8217'achronim}. The contrast drawn is between Beit Shammai as representative of the {\i doros rishonim} and Rabbi Dosa as representative of the {\i doros achronim}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is a {\i yeridat hadorot} type of observation \u8212- a romanticization of the stringency and purity of earlier generations relative to later ones. But what is notable is that the particular example chosen complicates any simple evaluation of who was \u8220"better.\u8221"\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Dosa Example\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i baraita} records that a woman taken captive \u8212- {\i shvuya} \u8212- who was married to a {\i kohen} may, according to Rabbi Dosa, continue eating {\i terumah} upon her return. We do not presume she was raped. Rabbi Dosa\u8217's reasoning is direct and almost startling in its humanity: {\i v\u8217'chi mah asah lah aravi zeh? b\u8217'shvil shemeichah bein dadeiha \u8212- tifsel m\u8217'unakehunah?} What did this Arab do to her? Because he may have fondled her \u8212- should she be disqualified from {\i kehunah}? Rabbi Dosa refuses to presume rape even in conditions where it was entirely possible, in order to protect the woman\u8217's status and her marriage.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The contrast with Beit Shammai is therefore not straightforward. Beit Shammai, the {\i doros rishonim}, imposed restrictions on a completely innocent woman based on the faintest whiff of a rumor. Rabbi Dosa, the {\i doros achronim}, refused to strip a woman of her status based on a presumption of violation she may never have suffered. In this framing, the \u8220"later\u8221" generation actually shows greater sensitivity to human dignity and real consequences.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} cites a second contrast \u8212- that the {\i doros rishonim} would bring their produce through the main gate in order to be {\i chayiv} in {\i maaser}, while the {\i doros achronim} would bring it in through rooftops and courtyards to avoid the obligation. This is a less ambiguous case of decline. But the contrast with the {\i shvuya} case is worth sitting with: sometimes what looks like a relaxation of standards is actually a greater moral clarity. It is not that we think the {\i doros achronim} are doing wrong; it is, as framed here, more of a sense of loss \u8212- are we losing some dimension of {\i kedusha} and {\i tahara} even as we do the right thing by human beings? That tension is what the {\i Gemara} appears to be naming.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Overnight Together Probl\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Overnight Inn Scenario\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Mishnah} now presents the case of a man who divorces his wife and then spends the night with her in an inn \u8212- {\i hamigaresh es ishto v\u8217'lan imah b\u8217'pundaki}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Beit Shammai No Second\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Beit Shammai rules: she does not need a second {\i get} from him \u8212- {\i eina tzricha heimenu get sheni}. We do not presume from the fact that they spent the night together that they had sexual relations and thereby entered into a new marriage.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Beit Hillel Requires Sec\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Beit Hillel rules more stringently here: she does require a second {\i get}, but only {\i bizman shenitgarsha min hanisu\u8217'in} \u8212- when the divorce follows a full marriage ({\i nisu\u8217'in}) in which the couple had actually lived together and been intimate.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Eirusin Agreement\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Both schools agree, however, that if the divorce followed only {\i eirusin} \u8212- betrothal, prior to cohabitation \u8212- no second {\i get} is required: {\i mipnei she\u8217'ein libo gas bah}. He is not sufficiently at ease with her \u8212- having never yet lived with her as a wife \u8212- to make it plausible that after granting her a {\i get} he would have sex with her the same night, let alone with the intent of remarriage.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This formulation makes clear that the {\i Mishnah}\u8217's debate turns on whether we presume sexual relations occurred, since the presence of {\i nisu\u8217'in} versus {\i eirusin} is relevant only to the probability of sex, not to the legal significance of sex if we already know it happened.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is worth noting the {\i Yerushalmi}\u8217's insight cited here: this debate may be connected to the famous {\i Mishnah} at the end of {\i Gittin} ({\i daf} 90), where Beit Shammai rules that a man may not divorce his wife unless she has committed a sexual transgression \u8212- {\i ervas davar} \u8212- while Beit Hillel permits divorce even if she merely burnt his food. If Beit Shammai believes divorce only occurs because of a serious transgression, it follows naturally that Beit Shammai would not assume the divorced couple desired to reunite sexually. Beit Hillel, who permits divorce for minor reasons, must account for the possibility that the divorce was over something trivial and the couple, having spent a night together, may have reconciled.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Edei Yichud vs.\u160 ?Edei Biah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sex Witnessed Version\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbah bar bar Chana citing Rabbi Yochanan offers a different version of the debate. He says the {\i machloket} between Beit Shammai and Beit Hillel arises specifically in a case where witnesses actually observed the couple having sexual relations \u8212- {\i beshero\u8217'uha shenivala}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Beit Shammai Zenut\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 On this reading, Beit Shammai holds: {\i adam oseh beilato beilat zenut} \u8212- a person may engage in a sexual act that is not intended to constitute marriage. The sexual act itself does not imply matrimonial intent, and therefore we do not retroactively treat the couple as having remarried.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Beit Hillel No Zenut\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Beit Hillel holds: {\i ein adam oseh beilato beilat zenut} \u8212- we presume that when a man has sexual relations, particularly with a woman who was formerly his wife, he intends it as an act of marriage, not mere fornication. The couple should therefore be treated as having remarried, and a second {\i get} is required.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rashi Clarification\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rashi clarifies an important limitation: this presumption \u8212- {\i ein adam oseh beilato beilat zenut} \u8212- applies specifically to a woman who was previously his wife, {\i nashim shenitgarsha min hanisu\u8217'in}. In that case, the prior marital relationship provides the context in which we interpret the sexual act as matrimonial. The principle does not mean that any two people observed having sexual relations are thereby presumed married. Without the prior marital context, there is no basis for such a presumption.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Seclusion Witnesses\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} then recasts the {\i Mishnah} itself. If the debate is about a case where witnesses actually saw them have sexual relations, why does the {\i Mishnah} distinguish between {\i nisu\u8217'in} and {\i eirusin}? If we witnessed the act, the only question is its legal meaning \u8212- not whether it occurred. The {\i Mishnah}\u8217's distinction based on whether they had a prior {\i nisu\u8217'in} relationship ({\i libo gas bah}) makes sense only if the question is whether sex occurred at all, not what the sex means. The {\i Gemara} therefore concludes: {\i ella matnitin beshelo ro\u8217'uha shenivala} \u8212- the {\i Mishnah} must be dealing with a case where witnesses did not directly observe sexual relations. Rather, there were {\i edei yichud} \u8212- witnesses to seclusion \u8212- who saw the couple enter a private space together. The debate is whether seclusion witnesses are treated as tantamount to witnesses to sexual relations.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shammai Seclusion Only\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Beit Shammai holds: {\i lo amrinan hein hein edei yichud hein hein edei biah} \u8212- we do not say that the witnesses to seclusion are the same as witnesses to sexual relations. Seclusion witnesses testify only to what they saw: the couple entered a private space. They cannot be treated as having implicitly testified to sex, and therefore no presumption of remarriage arises.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hillel Seclusion = Sex\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Beit Hillel holds: {\i amrinan hein hein edei yichud hein hein edei biah} \u8212- witnesses to seclusion are implicitly witnesses to sex. Since we presume that a man does not intend a sexual act with his former wife as mere fornication, and since seclusion is treated as equivalent to the act itself, the couple is presumed to have remarried and a second {\i get} is required.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Eirusin Exception\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Even according to Beit Hillel, however, when the prior divorce followed only {\i eirusin} and not {\i nisu\u8217'in}, we do not make this presumption \u8212- {\i ein libo gas bah}, he is not sufficiently comfortable with her. Without the intimacy of prior cohabitation, even Beit Hillel does not extend the {\i edei yichud} presumption to cover the act of sexual relations.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} closes this section by noting a tension in Rabbi Yochanan\u8217's positions: elsewhere Rabbi Yochanan is reported to hold that the {\i halacha} follows a {\i stam Mishnah} \u8212- an unattributed {\i Mishnah}. But here Rabbi Yochanan\u8217's formulation aligns with the {\i baraita}\u8217's version of the debate (witnesses saw sex), not the {\i Mishnah}\u8217's version (witnesses saw only seclusion). The {\i Gemara} resolves this by saying these represent two {\i amoraim} debating what Rabbi Yochanan\u8217's view actually was: the {\i amora} who reports Rabbi Yochanan\u8217's opinion in line with the {\i baraita} does not accept the general principle that Rabbi Yochanan always follows the {\i stam Mishnah}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Get Kereiach Folded\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Accordion Structure\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The final {\i Mishnah} of today\u8217's {\i daf} introduces the {\i get kereiach} \u8212- the \u8220"bald\u8221" or \u8220"folded\u8221" {\i get} developed specifically to slow down {\i kohanim} who might impulsively divorce their wives. Since a {\i kohen} may not remarry a divorced woman \u8212- even his own wife \u8212- the rabbis wanted to make the act of writing a {\i get} for a {\i kohen} significantly more laborious than for an ordinary Israelite.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The mechanism they devised was an accordion-style document. A sheet of parchment was written upon for a few lines, then folded over, and the fold was tied shut with a knot. A witness then signed on the back of that folded section. The process was repeated: more text was written, another fold was made, another knot was tied, another signature was affixed. The result was a document with multiple sealed sections, each bearing a witness signature on its exterior. The minimum number of such folds \u8212- and signatures \u8212- required by the rabbis for a {\i get kereiach} was three, as opposed to the two witnesses required for an ordinary {\i get}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If a {\i kohen} chose to add more folds \u8212- say, ten \u8212- then ten sections needed to be signed. A {\i get kereiach} is defined as one in which the number of folds ({\i kesharav}) exceeds the number of signatures ({\i edav}) \u8212- {\i kol shekesharav merubim me\u8217'adav}. A fold left blank \u8212- unsigned \u8212- renders the {\i get} defective and treated as {\i pasul}, with all the attendant consequences.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ben Nanas Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Ben Nanas rules that when there is a blank section \u8212- a fold without a signature \u8212- beyond the minimum three, {\i psulim} can fill in the blank: {\i hakol mashlimin alav}. Even witnesses who are disqualified for normal testimony may sign the surplus sections, and the {\i get} is nevertheless valid. The reasoning is that these extra signatures are not functioning as genuine {\i eidus} \u8212- testimony \u8212- but merely as a formal fulfillment of the requirement that no fold be left unsigned.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Akiva Restrictions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Akiva disagrees. He rules: {\i ein mashlimin alav ela kerovim hara\u8217'uyim leha\u8217'id bemakom acher} \u8212- only those who would be valid witnesses in other contexts may fill in the blank sections, specifically relatives ({\i kerovim}). Relatives are disqualified in cases involving their own family members, but in other contexts they are perfectly valid witnesses. Slaves ({\i avadim}) and thieves ({\i gazlanim}) may not sign, even for surplus sections.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kereiach Definition\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The definition of {\i get kereiach} flows directly from this structure: it is any {\i get} in which the number of folds exceeds the number of signatures. The name {\i kereiach} \u8212- \u8220"bald\u8221" \u8212- evokes the image of a fold that has been left bare, without its requisite signature.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kulchem Fear\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Why is a blank fold such a serious problem that it renders the entire {\i get} invalid and triggers all the negative consequences? The {\i Gemara} answers: {\i gezeira mishum kulchem} \u8212- a rabbinic decree arising from the concern that \u8220"everyone sign.\u8221" The reasoning is as follows: if a man creates a {\i get} with ten folds, the creation of ten fold-sections implies that ten witnesses were originally designated to sign. If only three signatures appear and seven folds are blank, an observer might conclude that seven witnesses who were supposed to sign did not \u8212- and the {\i get} is therefore defective for having failed to gather all its designated witnesses. To eliminate this concern entirely, every fold must bear a signature. Leaving any fold blank invites the misperception that a witness who was supposed to sign failed to do so.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Interestingly, the fact that the {\i Gemara} permits {\i psulim} to sign the surplus folds demonstrates that the concern is not that the {\i get} will be seen as requiring more {\i kosher} witnesses. The worry is purely one of perception \u8212- that blank spaces will look like an incomplete process. Even an invalid signature fills the visual gap and eliminates the {\i gezeira}\u8217's concern.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Slave Freed Misconception\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} then asks why Rabbi Akiva excludes a slave ({\i eved}) from signing even surplus sections. The initial suggestion is that people will say: \u8220"Look, a slave signed \u8212- slaves must be valid for {\i eidus}!\u8221" But this reasoning would equally apply to a relative signing, since people might say: \u8220"A relative signed \u8212- relatives must be valid!\u8221" So that cannot be the full explanation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} therefore reformulates Rabbi Akiva\u8217's reasoning for excluding a slave: {\i dilma asu l\u8217'assukei l\u8217'yuchsin} \u8212- people will not say slaves are generally {\i kosher} for testimony, since everyone knows they are not. Instead, they will say: \u8220"How could a slave sign? He must have been freed!\u8221" If people believe the slave was freed, they will treat him as a free man for purposes of {\i yichus}, potentially allowing him to marry a free woman in contexts where that would be problematic.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Thief Teshuvah Misconcep\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The analogous concern for a {\i gazlan} \u8212- a thief \u8212- is: {\i asu l\u8217'meimar teshuva avad} \u8212- people will assume he did {\i teshuva} and was thereby rehabilitated as a valid witness. This would cause people to trust him and have him sign other documents, leading to a reliance on invalid testimony in consequential halachic matters.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Relatives Safe\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 For a {\i karov} \u8212- a relative \u8212- neither of these concerns applies. {\i Karov kulei alma yadei dekarov hu} \u8212- everyone knows he is a relative, and everyone knows that relatives are disqualified only in matters involving their own families. No one will draw any anomalous conclusions from seeing a relative\u8217's signature. It simply means this is a supplementary fill-in signature from a family member, and the situation is self-explanatory.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two Witnesses Dispute\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} then cites a {\i baraita} that extends the analysis: seven folds with six signatures, six with five, five with four, four with three \u8212- in each case, the debate between Ben Nanas and Rabbi Akiva applies. As long as there are at least three {\i kosher} signatures, the question is merely what categories of people may fill in the surplus blanks. {\i Ad kan machlokes Ben Nanas v\u8217'Rabbi Akiva} \u8212- up to this point is the debate.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 However, if there are three folds and only two witnesses \u8212- {\i shachrech sheloshah v\u8217'eideh shenayim} \u8212- {\i divrei hakol ein mashlimim alav ela karov} \u8212- everyone agrees that only a relative may sign the missing third position. The logic is that the minimum required for a valid {\i get kereiach} is three {\i kosher} signatures; without that minimum, the entire {\i get} is deficient in a more fundamental way, and the standards for filling the gap are consequently higher.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Basic Three Challenge\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This ruling, however, immediately raises a challenge. Rav Zeira poses it directly to Rabbah bar Shilas, from whom he had received this teaching: {\i michdi kol sheloshah bi-mekushar k\u8217'shenayim bi-pashut dami} \u8212- three witnesses on a folded {\i get} are the equivalent of two witnesses on a plain {\i get}, since the minimum threshold of valid witnesses is set at three for this document. {\i Mah hasam karov lo, af hacha nami karov lo} \u8212- just as on a plain {\i get} a relative may not serve as one of the two required witnesses, so too here, when you are down to the basic three required signatures, how can a relative serve as one of them?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbah bar Shilas replied that he had asked the very same question of Rav Hamnuna, who had received the ruling from Rav Adda bar Ahava. Rav Adda\u8217's answer was: {\i hanicha sheloshah bi-mekushar delav de-oraisa} \u8212- indeed, the requirement of three signatures on a {\i get kereiach} is only of rabbinic origin; biblically, two witnesses suffice. But even granting that the three-signature requirement is rabbinic, the question remains: if the rabbis defined three as the minimum for this {\i get}, and one of those three is a relative, has the rabbinic minimum actually been met?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} then cites a confirming {\i baraita} and reads a specific case: if a {\i get kereiach} had a surplus blank filled in by a slave, Ben Nanas says it is {\i kasher} (at least {\i bediavad}), while Rabbi Akiva says {\i ha-vlad mamzer} \u8212- the child is a {\i mamzer} \u8212- the most stringent possible consequence. When the {\i Gemara} reaches the case of three folds with two witnesses, it reports that Rav Yosef reads the text differently: where the tradition says {\i karov}, Rav Yosef rules {\i kasher} \u8212- meaning that when you are down to the basic three, one cannot rely on a relative at all, and if the third signer was a relative, the {\i get} would only be treated as valid ({\i kasher}) if that position is filled by a proper {\i kosher} witness. Rav Pappa endorses this correction: {\i tani kasher} \u8212- the reading should be {\i kasher}, not {\i karov}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The resolution emerging from this discussion is that for surplus blank sections \u8212- beyond the minimum three \u8212- the debate between Ben Nanas and Rabbi Akiva governs what categories of invalid signatories may fill in. For the basic three signatures themselves, however, the standards are categorically higher, and the question of whether a relative may serve as one of those three remains a serious difficulty that the {\i Gemara} is still pressing at the close of the {\i daf}. This discussion will continue on the following {\i daf}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Gittin 82\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Get Kereach\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Structure & Definition\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The daf opens at the bottom of 81b with a teaching of Rabbi Yochanan concerning the {\i get kereach} \u8212- the \u8220"bald get,\u8221" which originated from the {\i get mekushar} developed for {\i kohanim} to slow the process of divorcing their wives. In the {\i get mekushar}, the scribe writes several lines of text and folds the document over a blank space, with witnesses signing on the outer panel. This accordion-like structure is then repeated \u8212- fold upon fold, each sewn shut, each bearing a witness signature on its panel. A minimum of three such folds with three signatures is required for the {\i get} to be valid.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i get kereach} arises when a husband prepared many such folds but failed to obtain signatures on all of them. He may have created ten folds but secured only nine signatures, for example. The {\i braita} treats this as a serious defect \u8212- according to Rabbi Meir, the woman must leave the marriage and the offspring is a {\i mamzer}, the gravest of consequences.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Validity Requirements\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Despite the severity of this defect, the {\i halacha} allows the blank spots to be filled in with {\i psulei edim} \u8212- disqualified witnesses \u8212- perhaps even a {\i karov}, a relative. The underlying rule is that the basic minimum of witnesses that the Rabbis required must themselves be {\i kasher}, but beyond that core, the remaining folds may be completed with otherwise disqualified signatories. The question raised at the close of the previous session was whether, when only two valid signatures exist \u8212- which suffices on a Torah level \u8212- the third must also be {\i kasher}, or whether the third may be filled in by a {\i karov} or other {\i pasul} witness.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Disqualified Witnesses\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} is operating with the principle that even disqualified witnesses may fill the remaining blank spots in a {\i get kereach}, provided the foundational two or three valid signatures are in place. What requires further clarification is the scope of this leniency.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 One Disqualified Maximum\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yochanan states explicitly: {\i lo huchsharu bo ela ed echad karov bilvad} \u8212- only one disqualified witness is permitted. All the other folds must bear the signatures of {\i kasher edim}. The permission to use a {\i pasul} extends to one slot only, not two or more.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Validation Logic\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The reason is elegant and practical. When {\i beis din} comes to validate a {\i get} bearing, say, ten signatures, it will seek out people who can identify the handwriting and confirm the signatures. Any three confirmations will suffice. If more than one {\i pasul} witness had signed, there is a real danger that {\i beis din} might validate the {\i get} on the basis of two {\i pasul} signatures \u8212- leaving only one valid witness among the three. Since we cannot guarantee which three of the ten signatures will be brought forward for validation, allowing two {\i pasulim} to sign creates an unacceptable risk that the woman will be permitted to remarry on the strength of invalid testimony. Restricting the {\i pasul} signature to one ensures that any three signatures that {\i beis din} selects will include at least two {\i kasher} witnesses.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Abaye\u8217's Proof\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye adds a corroboration from a careful reading of the {\i braita} itself: {\i matniten nami dika} \u8212- the {\i braita} can be read to support this. The {\i braita} lists its cases as \u8220"seven folds and six witnesses, six folds and five witnesses, five folds and four witnesses,\u8221" always speaking of a shortfall of exactly one witness. It never envisions filling in more than one missing signature. {\i Shma mina}, Abaye concludes: this confirms that only one {\i pasul} signature is ever contemplated.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might ask whether it matters where in the sequence the {\i pasul} witness signs \u8212- must he be at the beginning, middle, or end? The answer flows from the same {\i braita}. Since it never specifies a location for the {\i pasul} signature, we infer that he may sign in any fold. As the {\i gemara} puts it: {\i midlo kavah lei makom} \u8212- because no particular location was specified, the {\i pasul} may sign anywhere \u8212- at the beginning, middle, or end.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Non-Consecutive Validati\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A second inference follows from the same logic: {\i beis din} need not validate three consecutive signatures. If {\i ratsufin} \u8212- consecutive signatures \u8212- were required, one could simply mandate that the {\i pasul} witness sign only in a position immediately following two {\i kasher} witnesses. In that arrangement, any sequence of three consecutive signatures would necessarily include two valid ones. Since the {\i gemara} instead limits the {\i pasul} to a single occurrence regardless of position, it must be that {\i beis din} may validate any three signatures it chooses, not only consecutive ones. This is the rule of {\i mikol tlasa d\u8217'mekaymin} \u8212- validation may be drawn from any three confirmable signatures in the document.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rashi\u8217's Interpretation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rashi\u8217's reading of this passage is remarkable \u8212- remarkable enough that at the end of his commentary he acknowledges it openly. His explanation is as follows: one might have supposed that a {\i pasul} could sign freely, so long as he was always preceded by two {\i kasher} witnesses. Under such an arrangement \u8212- {\i kasher}, {\i kasher}, {\i pasul}; {\i kasher}, {\i kasher}, {\i pasul} \u8212- the sequence would be repeatedly anchored in valid testimony, and a rule requiring consecutive validation would make the arrangement safe no matter how many {\i psulim} signed. Since the {\i gemara} rejects that approach and insists on only a single {\i pasul} signature, it necessarily follows that validation is not limited to consecutive groupings. Any three signatures may be used, and therefore even one {\i pasul} among ten signatories poses a risk if a second {\i pasul} is also present.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 At the end of his commentary, Rashi writes with characteristic directness: {\i k\u8217'ein zu ikara shel sugya zu v\u8217'lo shimatiha ken} \u8212- \u8220"This, in my view, is the true meaning of this passage; that was not the tradition I received. But the version I previously had, from my teacher\u8217's written commentary, did not seem correct to me \u8212- and so I am presenting what I believe to be the proper explanation.\u8221"\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Ammi Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The practical upshot is illustrated by a case that came before Rabbi Ammi. When presented with a {\i get kereach} bearing a blank spot, he ruled: {\i tzei v\u8217'hashleima lav afilu min hashuk} \u8212- go and complete it, even with someone from the marketplace, meaning even a slave could fill in the last missing signature. This reflects the ruling attributed to Ben Nanos, that one {\i pasul} signature \u8212- even that of a slave \u8212- suffices to complete the document.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might have supposed that allowing a {\i pasul} as the {\i first} signer would set the wrong tone, or that there is something structurally significant about where in the sequence he appears. That intuition is understandable, but the {\i gemara} gives it no footing. What saves the document from the principle of {\i nimtza echad mehem karov o pasul} \u8212- that discovering a {\i pasul} among the signatories invalidates the group \u8212- is the context: it is understood that this particular signature serves a distinct purpose and is not part of the core witnessing group. The {\i Rama} applies a parallel logic to the practice in his time of passing a {\i ketuba} around for relatives and disqualified persons to sign after the two valid witnesses, as an honor to the {\i chatan} and {\i kallah}. Because the context makes clear that these additional signatories are functioning differently, they do not invalidate the {\i ketuba}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Conditional Divorce\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Exclusionary Get\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 With the conclusion of this {\i perek}, the {\i gemara} transitions to a new and altogether different set of questions. The new {\i perek} opens with {\i Hamgaresh et ishto} \u8212- a case in which a man divorces his wife and adds: {\i harei at muteres l\u8217'chol adam ela l\u8217'ploni} \u8212- behold, you are permitted to every man except Frank. He excludes one specific individual from the divorce\u8217's effect. Is this a valid {\i get}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Eliezer vs.\u160 ?Chachamim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Eliezer rules that it is valid ({\i matir}), while the {\i Chachamim} forbid it \u8212- meaning they do not recognize it as a valid {\i get} at all. The core objection of the {\i Chachamim} is that a {\i get} must constitute {\i kritut} \u8212- a complete severance \u8212- and a {\i get} that leaves the woman still bound to even one person in the world fails that standard.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Remedial Procedure\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If the {\i get} as given is invalid, what should the husband do? The {\i mishna} prescribes: {\i yitlena mimena veyachzor veyitnena lah} \u8212- he takes the {\i get} back from her and gives it to her again, this time saying {\i harei at muteret lechol adam} without restriction. The underlying principle is that an invalid {\i act} of delivery does not consume the {\i get} itself as an instrument. The {\i cheftza} \u8212- the physical document \u8212- remains a valid instrument, and can be re-delivered with proper language.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 However, if the exclusionary language was written {\i into the body of the get} \u8212- {\i katvo betocho} \u8212- then even if it is subsequently erased, the {\i get} is {\i pasul}. The witnesses signed a document that was from the outset an invalid {\i krisus}, and that defect cannot be undone. The distinction is between a problem with the {\i act} of delivery, which is remediable, and a problem with the {\i instrument} itself, which is not.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Exclusion vs.\u160 ?Condition\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 At this point the {\i gemara} poses its fundamental analytical question: {\i hai ela \u8212- chutz o al menat?} What is the nature of the exclusion expressed by the word {\i ela} (\u8220"except\u8221")? Does it mean {\i chutz} \u8212- that the {\i get} takes effect for the whole world with the actual exception of Reuven, so that vis-\u224 ?-vis Reuven she remains a married woman \u8212- or does it mean {\i al menat} \u8212- on the condition that she not marry or have relations with Reuven, so that the {\i get} takes full conceptual effect for everyone, but is retroactively annulled if she violates the condition?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The practical consequences are sharply different. Under the {\i chutz} reading: she sleeps with Shimon \u8212- not adultery; she sleeps with Reuven \u8212- adultery, because vis-\u224 ?-vis Reuven she is still {\i eishet ish}. Under the {\i al menat} reading: she sleeps with Shimon and subsequently sleeps with Reuven. Once she has violated the condition, the {\i get} is retroactively void \u8212- which means her prior relations with Shimon were also adulterous.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Practical Consequences\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Beyond the question of adultery, the framing has implications for the {\i kehunah} status of the {\i get} as well. As one interlocutor notes, there is a meaningful difference between excluding a {\i kohen} from the {\i get} and excluding an ordinary Israelite. Ordinarily, a {\i kohen} already faces an {\i issur lav} against marrying a {\i grusha}; but if the husband explicitly says she is forbidden to that {\i kohen}, the act would constitute adultery, not merely an {\i issur kehunah}. Moreover, the question of whether the {\i issur kehunah} applies even to relations outside of marriage \u8212- a matter of dispute between the {\i Rambam} and the {\i Ra\u8217'avad} \u8212- gives the exclusion additional force in the case of a {\i kohen}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might also ask about excluding someone she could never marry regardless \u8212- say, a {\i chayav kares} relative, whose {\i kiddushin} would not take effect at all. In that case, excluding that person from the {\i get} would arguably have no legal consequence. The case of a {\i kohen}, by contrast, clearly does have a profound effect, as it transforms what would have been an {\i issur kehunah} into outright adultery.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Word Ela Meaning\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 So which reading is correct \u8212- {\i chutz} or {\i al menat}? The {\i gemara} now argues that the plain meaning of {\i ela} is {\i chutz}. If the two are equivalent, the debate between Rabbi Eliezer and the {\i Chachamim} is most naturally cast as a dispute over whether {\i chutz} \u8212- actual exclusion \u8212- can produce a valid {\i get}. {\i Chutz} is obviously the problematic case: how can a {\i get} that is not effective vis-\u224 ?-vis one person in the world qualify as {\i kritut}? If the dispute were only about {\i al menat}, it would be far less surprising, since any ordinary conditional transaction works, and Rabbi Eliezer would simply be applying standard rules of {\i tenai}. The {\i chiddush} would be minimal.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 To confirm the meaning of {\i ela}, the {\i gemara} invokes a proof from an entirely different domain.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Negaim Proof\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Ravina brings the following {\i braita}: {\i kol habatim mitamim b\u8217'negaim ela shel ovdei kochavim} \u8212- all houses can become {\i tamei} through {\i nega tzaraas}, except the houses of non-Jews. The word {\i ela} there is structurally identical to the {\i ela} in our {\i mishna}. The question: does {\i ela} here mean \u8220"with the exception of\u8221" ({\i chutz}), or \u8220"on the condition that\u8221" ({\i al menat})? If {\i ela} meant {\i al menat}, the sentence would read: all Jewish houses are susceptible to {\i negaim} only on the condition that the houses of non-Jews are not susceptible. And if the condition were violated \u8212- if non-Jewish houses could become {\i tamei} \u8212- then Jewish houses would not become {\i tamei}. This is obviously absurd. Who is one making this condition with? And further, the condition cannot be violated in any case, since a separate {\i braita} derives from the verse {\i v\u8217'nasati nega tzaraas b\u8217'veit eretz achuzaschem} \u8212- \u8220"I will place a plague of {\i tzaraas} in a house in the land of your inheritance\u8221" \u8212- that only property held as inheritance is susceptible; houses of non-Jews, even within {\i Eretz Yisrael}, are never susceptible to {\i negaim}. The {\i al menat} reading is thus completely incoherent in that context. {\i Ela} must mean {\i chutz} \u8212- \u8220"with the exception of.\u8221" {\i Shma mina}, so too in our {\i mishna}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This produces a substantial {\i chiddush} for Rabbi Eliezer\u8217's position: he holds that a {\i get} can take effect even when it is not effective vis-\u224 ?-vis a particular person. The {\i get} is, in Rabbi Eliezer\u8217's view, valid as a document of divorce even though it leaves an island of continued marriage to one individual.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Yose Braita\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then notes that our {\i mishna}, now established as framing the dispute in terms of {\i chutz}, does not accord with a {\i braita} of Rabbi Yose son of Rabbi Yehudah. That {\i braita} frames the dispute differently: Rabbi Yose states that Rabbi Eliezer and the {\i Chachamim} do not disagree at all about {\i chutz}. If a man says {\i harei at muteret l\u8217'chol adam chutz miploni} \u8212- you are permitted to everyone except so-and-so \u8212- everyone agrees she is not divorced. {\i Al mah nechleku?} \u8212- about what do they disagree? About {\i al menat}: if a man says {\i harei at muteret l\u8217'chol adam al menat she-lo tinassei l\u8217'ploni} \u8212- you are permitted to everyone on the condition that you not marry so-and-so. There, Rabbi Eliezer says the {\i get} is valid, while the {\i Chachamim} forbid it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 On Rabbi Eliezer\u8217's view in this {\i braita}, the {\i get} functions like any standard conditional transaction. The conceptual effect of the {\i get} is total \u8212- she is completely divorced from everyone \u8212- and the condition is simply an external restriction, like any other {\i tenai}. The {\i Chachamim} respond: any ordinary condition ({\i kol tenai d\u8217'alma}) works because the conceptual act is not compromised. But here, even if the {\i get} is conceptually complete, it does not in practice make her {\i muteret l\u8217'chol adam}. A {\i get} must produce actual freedom to marry anyone, and if in practice she is barred from one person, that practical limitation renders it an inadequate {\i kritut} \u8212- {\i hacha shiyyer lah b\u8217'get}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} raises a further nuance: even if the condition is formulated as prohibiting both marriage and intercourse with the excluded person \u8212- making the exclusion as total in practice as the {\i chutz} formulation \u8212- Rabbi Eliezer would still say the {\i get} is valid. The reason: Rabbi Eliezer looks at the conceptual structure. The {\i get} itself is one hundred percent effective; the condition is an external layer. The {\i Chachamim} look at practical reality. If she cannot in practice be with that person, the {\i get} has not produced full {\i kritut}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is a genuine and deep {\i machloket}. The question is whether the effectiveness of a legal instrument is measured by its conceptual structure or by its practical outcome. One analogy: if one sells another the fruits of an olive tree \u8212- clearly the whole purpose of the transaction \u8212- but conditions the sale on the buyer not consuming ten percent of the fruits, has the sale accomplished what a sale of this type is supposed to accomplish? The {\i Chachamim} say no; Rabbi Eliezer says yes.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Yannai Verse\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Returning now to the {\i mishna} as established \u8212- that the dispute concerns {\i chutz} \u8212- the {\i gemara} asks: how does Rabbi Eliezer justify his position? How can a {\i get} that explicitly leaves the woman married to one person constitute {\i kritut}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Yannai, citing an unnamed elder, points to the verse: {\i v\u8217'yatza mibeito v\u8217'halcha v\u8217'hayta l\u8217'ish acher} \u8212- she leaves his house and goes and becomes the wife of another man. Rav Yannai derives: {\i afilu lo hutrah ela l\u8217'ish echad harei zu megureshes} \u8212- even if the {\i get} makes her permissible to marry only one man, this constitutes a valid divorce. The Torah does not say the {\i get} must make her {\i muteret l\u8217'chol adam}; it says she goes and marries {\i ish acher} \u8212- another man. So long as some door opens, the {\i get} has done its work.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is a striking {\i gemara}. It challenges the intuition that a {\i get} must fully sever. And it raises a further difficulty: what is the nature of the remaining marriage? Can a man be married to a woman vis-\u224 ?-vis himself but not vis-\u224 ?-vis the rest of the world? Is marriage a binary status, or can it be partial?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chachamim Response\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Chachamim} respond to the verse: {\i l\u8217'chol ish v\u8217'ish} \u8212- {\i ish acher} does not mean some particular man she now has license to marry. It means any man she chooses. A {\i get} must open the door to the entire world; it must make her {\i muteret l\u8217'chol adam} in the full sense. If it cannot do that, it fails as {\i kritut}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is noted that the language of {\i sefer kritut} has not been invoked explicitly, but it is implicit throughout \u8212- it is clearly what drives the objection, and it is why Rabbi Eliezer must resort to a {\i pasuk} to defend his position.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Yochanan Verse\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yochanan offers a different Scriptural basis for Rabbi Eliezer\u8217's position. The verse in {\i Vayikra} regarding {\i kohanim} reads: {\i v\u8217'isha grusha me\u8217'isha lo yikachu} \u8212- a woman divorced from her husband, a {\i kohen} may not take. Why does the verse say \u8220"divorced from her husband\u8221" rather than simply \u8220"a divorced woman\u8221"? Rabbi Yochanan derives: {\i afilu lo nitgarsha ela me\u8217'isha nifsela min hakehuna} \u8212- even if a woman was only \u8220"divorced from her husband\u8221" \u8212- meaning she was given a {\i get} with the declaration {\i harei at megureshes mimeni v\u8217'i at muteret l\u8217'shum adam}, you are divorced from me but not permitted to anyone else \u8212- she is nonetheless {\i pasul l\u8217'kehunah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is a remarkable {\i drasha}. Normally, {\i reiach haget} \u8212- the \u8220"scent of a {\i get},\u8221" the taint of divorce-like circumstances \u8212- is a rabbinic concept: certain types of {\i gittin} that are close to valid create a {\i derabanan} prohibition for {\i kohanim}. Rabbi Yochanan\u8217's {\i drasha} suggests that {\i reiach haget} can operate {\i mid\u8217'oraita}. Even a {\i get} that divorces her from her husband while permitting her to no one \u8212- which everyone agrees is not a valid {\i get} \u8212- creates a Torah-level disqualification from {\i kehunah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 As {\i Tosafot} and Rashi explain, the {\i nafka mina} in such a case would be, for instance, whether the husband\u8217's brother could perform {\i yibum} if the husband subsequently died. It is {\i pashut} that he could, since the {\i get} was entirely ineffective. But the {\i chiddush} is the {\i kehunah} disqualification. And from this, the {\i gemara} builds the following argument: if even a {\i get} that excludes the entire world \u8212- which is obviously void \u8212- still creates a Torah-level {\i kehunah} problem, then a {\i get} that excludes only one person, while leaving her permissible to everyone else, might actually be a real {\i get}. If it were so hopelessly invalid that no concept of divorce were in play at all, the Torah would not have bothered to create a {\i kehunah} disqualification for it. The Torah\u8217's response implies there is something real here.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Chachamim} counter: {\i issur kehunah shani} \u8212- {\i kehunah} law is different. The {\i kehunah} disqualification reflects only {\i reiach haget} considerations and carries no implication about the {\i get}\u8217's underlying validity. No inference about what constitutes a valid {\i get} can be drawn from it.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Polyandry Scenarios\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Partial Kiddushin Questi\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Abba now poses a question that pushes the sugya into still more remarkable territory: if Rabbi Eliezer allows a {\i get} that is only partially effective \u8212- that divorces the wife vis-\u224 ?-vis everyone except Reuven \u8212- then what is the status of {\i kiddushin} given with a similar exclusion? If one can end up in a reality where a woman is \u8220"a little divorced,\u8221" can one create the reverse reality at the outset \u8212- a \u8220"partial marriage\u8221"?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The question is asked {\i tiba\u8217'ei leRabbi Eliezer tiba\u8217'ei leRabanan} \u8212- both against the view of Rabbi Eliezer and against that of the {\i Rabanan}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kiddushin-Gerushin Link\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} identifies the nexus between {\i kiddushin} and {\i gerushin} in the verse {\i v\u8217'yatza v\u8217'hayta} \u8212- the verse links the process of leaving one marriage to the process of entering another. If by {\i gerushin} the Torah tolerates a partial dissolution, then by {\i kiddushin} as well, one might argue, a partial acquisition is possible.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two Men Scenario\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} raises the possibility \u8212- now fully explicit \u8212- that two men could each perform {\i kiddushin} on the same woman, each excluding the other from his stipulation. Reuven says {\i harei at mekudeshes li chutz miShimon}, and Shimon says {\i harei at mekudeshes li chutz miReuven}. Neither man\u8217's {\i kiddushin} forecloses the other\u8217's. The result, within Rabbi Eliezer\u8217's framework, would be a woman simultaneously married to two men \u8212- a form of polyandry created not through violation but through structured exclusion.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Abaye\u8217's Resolution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye resolves the question: {\i bein leRabbi Eliezer bein leRabanan ba\u8217'inan v\u8217'yatza v\u8217'hayta} \u8212- both Rabbi Eliezer and the {\i Chachamim} agree that {\i kiddushin} is linked to {\i gerushin} through {\i v\u8217'yatza v\u8217'hayta}. The implication cuts in both directions: the same way Rabbi Eliezer tolerates a partial {\i get}, he would also allow partial {\i kiddushin}. And by the same token, for the {\i Chachamim}, if a partial {\i get} is invalid, a partial {\i kiddushin} is equally unavailing. The {\i kiddushin} and {\i gerushin} regimes are structurally symmetric.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i chiddush} for Rabbi Eliezer\u8217's position is now fully stated: one may say to a woman {\i harei at mekudeshes li chutz miReuven}, and thereby create a partial marriage that coexists with Reuven\u8217's access to her.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two Brothers Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now works through the strange case of two brothers. Reuven says {\i harei at mekudeshes li chutz miShimon}, and Shimon responds {\i harei at mekudeshes li chutz miReuven}. Each brother excludes the other from his {\i kiddushin}, allowing both relationships to coexist. In principle \u8212- within this framework \u8212- she would be married to both simultaneously. (It is worth noting that the Torah\u8217's prohibitions address a man married to two sisters, not a woman married to two brothers, so there is no Biblical {\i issur} on that dimension \u8212- though the questions of adultery, {\i onah}, and inheritance that would attend such an arrangement are substantial, and the {\i gemara} does not engage them here.)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yibum Problem\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 To test whether these dual {\i kiddushin} are actually legally effective, the {\i gemara} raises the {\i yibum} scenario. If both Reuven and Shimon die, is their third brother Levi obligated in {\i yibum} through both of them? This would constitute {\i zikas shenei yevamim} \u8212- the {\i zika} of two {\i yevamim} \u8212- which is already a category the {\i gemara} in {\i Yevamos} discusses in its rabbinic form. By way of background: if Reuven dies and Shimon performs {\i ma\u8217'amar} \u8212- a rabbinic-level act resembling {\i kiddushin} for {\i yibum} \u8212- but then dies before completing {\i yibum}, Levi\u8217's obligation runs through both brothers, creating a {\i zikas shenei yevamim} at the rabbinic level. The {\i gemara} here asks whether the same phenomenon can arise on a Torah level, through the dual {\i kiddushin} we have just described.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shimon Ineffective\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} answers that this does not produce {\i zikas shenei yevamim}, because Shimon\u8217's {\i kiddushin} was ineffective. When Reuven married her and said {\i chutz miShimon}, she became forbidden to the entire world except Shimon. When Shimon then came and said {\i harei at mekudeshes li chutz miReuven}, his act made no change to her legal status whatsoever. Before his {\i kiddushin}, she was forbidden to everyone in the world except Shimon. After his {\i kiddushin}, she remained forbidden to everyone in the world except Shimon. His act did not alter whom she was permitted to marry or with whom she was forbidden. A legal act that changes nothing in the world has no legal effect. {\i Kidushei d\u8217'Reuven ahannu, kidushei d\u8217'Shimon lo ahannu} \u8212- Reuven\u8217's {\i kiddushin} was effective; Shimon\u8217's was not.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} thus uses the {\i yibum} question as a test case: not to show that she is married to two men, but to show that in the scenario as described, the second {\i kiddushin} simply does not take hold.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Effective Marriage Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 There is, however, a scenario in which both {\i kiddushin} would be effective. Suppose Reuven said {\i harei at mekudeshes li chutz miShimon v\u8217'Frank} \u8212- you are married to me, except that Shimon and Frank remain accessible to you. She is now forbidden to everyone in the world except Shimon and Frank. Shimon now says {\i harei at mekudeshes li chutz miReuven} \u8212- but makes no exception for Frank. Shimon\u8217's {\i kiddushin} does something: it closes the door to Frank. Before Shimon\u8217's {\i kiddushin}, Frank was accessible; after it, Frank is excluded. Shimon\u8217's act changed her status in a meaningful way. In this case, she is genuinely married to both Reuven and Shimon simultaneously \u8212- each marriage having had a distinct legal effect.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} also frames the {\i eshet shenei meitim} case \u8212- a woman who would truly be classified as the wife of two deceased men for purposes of {\i yibum} \u8212- as follows: Reuven marries her with {\i chutz miShimon}, and then Shimon comes and marries her {\i stam}, without any exclusion. Shimon\u8217's {\i stam kiddushin} has the effect of closing the door to Reuven \u8212- whom Reuven\u8217's own {\i kiddushin} had left accessible. {\i Kidushei d\u8217'Reuven ahannu lemisra achulei alma} \u8212- Reuven\u8217's {\i kiddushin} made her forbidden to everyone except Shimon. {\i Kidushei d\u8217'Shimon ahannu lemisra a\u8217'Reuven} \u8212- Shimon\u8217's {\i kiddushin} then made her forbidden to Reuven as well. The resulting state: she is bound to Reuven as a married woman but cannot have relations with him (Shimon\u8217's {\i kiddushin} made her {\i asura} to him), while she is also bound to Shimon but with Reuven still technically present as her husband. When both die, she is classified as {\i eshet shenei meitim}, the wife of two deceased men.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} raises a pointed question in the name of Rabbeinu Yitzchak: if Shimon\u8217's {\i kiddushin} makes her {\i asura} to Reuven, why doesn\u8217't that act knock Reuven\u8217's {\i kiddushin} out altogether? Should not Shimon\u8217's act, which purports to override Reuven\u8217's rights, dissolve the first marriage? The {\i gemara} apparently holds that both marriages coexist: she is married to Reuven, and Shimon\u8217's {\i kiddushin} forbids her to Reuven \u8212- a configuration that is strange on its face, but that this {\i sugya} apparently accepts as coherent within Rabbi Eliezer\u8217's framework.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is worth noting that this scenario has been cited in discussions of {\i hafka\u8217'at kiddushin} \u8212- retroactive annulment of {\i kiddushin} \u8212- in particular the {\i agunah} context. Whether the model could ever be implemented practically is a separate matter entirely.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Simultaneous Marriage\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye raises a final question in this vein: if the two brothers\u8217' {\i kiddushin} scenarios are being entertained, what exactly is the woman\u8217's legal status when both men are alive and both {\i kiddushin} are effective? The {\i gemara} does not resolve who she can be with \u8212- it treats the {\i yibum} and {\i agunah} implications as the operative test cases, not the question of whom she may actually have relations with. The {\i gemara}\u8217's concern throughout is the formal legal question of whose wife she is \u8220"on paper,\u8221" not an adjudication of permitted relations. Whether any of this is practically relevant is left aside; the {\i gemara} is working through the implications of Rabbi Eliezer\u8217's position to their logical conclusion.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Ambiguous Language\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gap-Filling Scenario\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 With the {\i kiddushin} discussion concluded, the {\i gemara} turns to a series of cases involving a husband who partially fills the gap in an exclusionary {\i get}. The scenario: the husband said {\i harei at muteres l\u8217'chol adam chutz miReuven v\u8217'Shimon} \u8212- you are permitted to everyone except Reuven and Shimon. He has excluded both men. He then added \u8212- before delivering the {\i get} \u8212- the words {\i leReuven v\u8217'Shimon}. He handed her the {\i get} with both statements included. What do those closing words mean?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two Interpretations\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} poses the fundamental ambiguity: {\i mi amrinan mah d\u8217'asar shara?} \u8212- do we say that by saying {\i leReuven v\u8217'Shimon} he reversed his own exclusion, making her now permitted to Reuven and Shimon as well as everyone else, filling in the gap he had created? Or do we say {\i mah d\u8217'asar shara u\u8217'mah d\u8217'shara asar} \u8212- he completely inverted the earlier statement, so that now she is permitted only to Reuven and Shimon and forbidden to everyone else, having reversed the original formulation entirely?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Single Name Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then subdivides the question. If, under the first reading, saying {\i leReuven v\u8217'Shimon} fills the gap and permits her to everyone: what if he said only {\i leReuven}? Does {\i leReuven} mean he was speaking elliptically \u8212- naming Reuven because Reuven came first in his original list, but intending to restore permission to both Reuven and Shimon? {\i Lehu hadin leShimon} \u8212- and the same naturally applies to Shimon. Or does {\i leReuven} mean {\i leReuven davka} \u8212- exclusively Reuven, leaving Shimon still excluded?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shimon Only Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If he said only {\i leShimon} \u8212- the reverse: does {\i leShimon} mean he is restoring permission to both men, choosing Shimon\u8217's name because it was the last word of his original exclusion ({\i mishum desalik mineih} \u8212- it was the word most recently in his mouth), and he intends by implication to fill in all the gaps? Or does {\i leShimon} mean only Shimon is now permitted, while Reuven remains excluded?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Even For Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Finally, Rav Ashi raises an additional layer: suppose the husband said {\i af leShimon} \u8212- \u8220"even Shimon.\u8221" Does {\i af} here refer backward to Reuven \u8212- meaning you are permitted to Reuven and {\i even} Shimon, so both are now permitted? Or does {\i af} refer forward to the rest of the world \u8212- meaning you are permitted to everyone in the world and even to Shimon, but Reuven remains excluded?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Ashi Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Teiku} \u8212- all of these questions are left unresolved. The {\i gemara} does not provide answers to any of the cases Rav Ashi raises. It is worth noting that these questions can be read according to either Rabbi Eliezer or the {\i Chachamim}. According to Rabbi Eliezer, the operative question is whether she remains {\i asura} to one of the excluded men even after the follow-up statement \u8212- which affects whether she can actually marry those individuals. According to the {\i Chachamim}, the follow-up statement\u8217's meaning is critical to whether the {\i get} is valid at all: if it successfully fills all the gaps and makes her {\i muteret l\u8217'chol adam}, the {\i get} becomes {\i kasher}; if any exclusion remains, it is {\i pasul}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i daf} closes on these open questions, but the deeper issue running through the entire {\i perek} remains: what is the nature of marriage and divorce? Is either a binary status, or can one be partially married and partially divorced? Can a legal instrument that is not one hundred percent effective still be a valid {\i get} or a valid {\i kiddushin}? These are the questions Rabbi Eliezer forces us to confront \u8212- and they will continue into the next {\i daf}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Gittin 84\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 R. Eliezer\u8217's Chutz\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Eliezer Intro\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The daf opens in the middle of 83b, continuing with the remarkable and legally strange position of Rabbi Eliezer that a woman can be divorced {\i vis-\u224 ?-vis} everyone except one specific person \u8212- a {\i get} that reads {\i chutz mi-ploni}, \u8220"except for so-and-so.\u8221" A series of {\i Tannaim} responding to this position after Rabbi Eliezer\u8217's death are discussed, and we pick up with {\i Heishiv Rabbi Shimon ben Elazar}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Before turning to that response, however, the {\i gemara} establishes an important internal concession that Rabbi Eliezer himself makes. {\i Modeh Rabbi Eliezer ben Horkenus}: even Rabbi Eliezer agrees that if the husband said \u8220"you are permitted to all men except Reuven,\u8221" and she went ahead and married someone else \u8212- and was then widowed or divorced from that second man \u8212- she is subsequently permitted to marry Reuven. The logic is as follows. When the husband declared {\i chutz mi-Reuven}, this was not an {\i al menat} condition but a {\i chutz} exclusion: she remained in some sense his wife {\i vis-\u224 ?-vis} Reuven. But once she entered into a complete new marriage with another man, that marriage entirely supplants and extinguishes whatever residual marital bond existed toward Reuven from the first husband. With the second marriage, she becomes fully the second man\u8217's wife, with no remnant of the first marriage. When the second man dies, she is therefore fully free \u8212- including {\i vis-\u224 ?-vis} Reuven.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Shimon ben Elazar\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Shimon ben Elazar then offers a formal challenge to Rabbi Eliezer\u8217's entire position. He asks: {\i heich matzinu she-zeh oser ve-zeh matir?} \u8212- how can we find that one person prohibits and a second person permits? The first husband said she is forbidden to Reuven; how can a second husband, by marrying and then releasing her, make her permitted to Reuven?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} immediately tests whether this principle holds universally. Is there truly no case where one party\u8217's action creates a prohibition and a different party\u8217's action dissolves it? Two apparent counterexamples are raised.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 First, the case of a {\i yevamah}: the husband who married her caused her to become forbidden to the rest of the world upon his death, since the {\i mitzvah} of {\i yibbum} creates a {\i zikah} that restricts her. Then the {\i yavam} comes and performs {\i chalitzah}, releasing her to everyone. Is this not a case of one person restricting and another permitting? The {\i gemara} answers: no, because it is actually the {\i yavam}, not the husband, who creates the restriction in the first place. Had there been no brother-in-law, the wife would have been fully free upon the husband\u8217's death. It is the {\i yavam}\u8217's existence that generates the {\i zikah}, and so it is the {\i yavam} himself who both creates and resolves the problem through {\i chalitzah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Second, the case of {\i nedarim}: a person makes a vow, restricting something upon themselves, and a {\i chacham} comes and annuls it. Is that not a second party lifting what the first party imposed? The {\i gemara} invokes Rabbi Yochanan\u8217's principle: {\i ein chacham matir klum ela be-charatah} \u8212- the sage does not annul anything except through {\i charatah}, remorse. The mechanism of {\i hatarat nedarim} requires that the person who made the vow articulate genuine regret for having done so, which retroactively reframes the vow as having been made in error. The sage serves as an instrument, but it is ultimately the original maker of the vow who is undoing it through remorse.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Undoing Restrictions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A third challenge: the case of {\i hafarat ha-ba\u8217'al}, the husband\u8217's power to void his wife\u8217's vows. Here the wife makes a vow and the husband annuls it \u8212- with no requirement of {\i charatah} on her part. The husband simply declares that he does not accept it, and the vow is voided. Is this not a genuine case of one person restricting and another person lifting that restriction?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} responds by invoking the ruling of Rav Pinchas in the name of Rava: {\i kol ha-noderet al da\u8217'at ba\u8217'alah hi noderet} \u8212- any time a woman makes a vow, she makes it implicitly with the understanding that her husband accepts it. The underlying presumption is that a woman does not intend her vow to take effect if her husband objects. Therefore, even in {\i hafarat ha-ba\u8217'al}, it is conceptually the woman herself who is undoing her vow, because she never truly intended it to bind without his acquiescence. The husband\u8217's annulment, on this reading, is simply the activation of a condition already embedded in the way she made the vow.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is, admittedly, a striking way to frame {\i hafarat ha-ba\u8217'al}. The principle of {\i charatah} and the construction that the woman \u8220"makes her vow on her husband\u8217's {\i da\u8217'at}\u8221" are presented here as responses to a dialectical challenge, not necessarily as exhaustive accounts of these institutions. The {\i gemara} is not claiming this is the only way to understand {\i hatarat nedarim} or {\i hafarat ha-ba\u8217'al} \u8212- it is pushing back against the analogy to Rabbi Eliezer\u8217's case, where there is no plausible construction under which the first husband\u8217's restriction dissolves: he explicitly wanted her to remain bound to him {\i vis-\u224 ?-vis} Reuven, and no subsequent act by a third party can be read as an expression of his original intent.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kerisus Argument\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now introduces what it describes as the sharpest and most direct challenge to Rabbi Eliezer\u8217's position. Rabbi Eliezer ben Azariah argued from the word {\i kerisus} \u8212- the Torah\u8217's description of a {\i get} as a {\i sefer kerisus}, a document of severance. {\i Kerisus} means something that cuts completely. {\i Almah de-shein zeh kerisus} \u8212- if the divorce excludes one person, it is not a complete severance. A {\i get chutz mi-Reuven} fails at the most fundamental level: it is definitionally not a {\i get}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbis\u8217' Approach\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If Rabbi Eliezer ben Azariah\u8217's {\i kerisus} argument is the strongest and most decisive, why did all the other {\i Tannaim} bother giving their more elaborate and complex critiques of Rabbi Eliezer? Why didn\u8217't they simply cite this verse?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The answer is that the other rabbis could not freely deploy the {\i kerisus} argument because they needed the word {\i kerisus} to teach something else. The {\i gemara} cites a {\i baraita}: if a husband says {\i harei zeh gittech al menat shelo tishti yayin} \u8212- this is your {\i get} on the condition that you never drink wine \u8212- {\i al menat shelo telchi le-veit avich le-olam} \u8212- on the condition that you never go to your father\u8217's house for all time \u8212- {\i ein zeh kerisus}, that is not a {\i get}, because the condition perpetually hovers over her head and binds her to her first husband for the rest of her life. But if he stipulates a condition for only thirty days, {\i harei zeh kerisus} \u8212- that is a valid {\i get}. This ruling, which delimits what qualifies as a complete severance in the context of temporal conditions, is derived from {\i kerisus}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Thus, for the other rabbis, the word {\i kerisus} is already committed to teaching that a condition hovering over her head for her entire lifetime is not a valid {\i get}. They cannot simultaneously use it to prove that excluding one individual is impermissible. Rabbi Eliezer ben Azariah, by contrast, derives both teachings from the fuller expression {\i karet kerisus} \u8212- the word could have been written simply as {\i karet}, but the term {\i kerisus} teaches two things: that an eternally hovering condition is not a {\i get}, and that excluding a specific person is not a {\i get}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The other rabbis, however, do not accept this double {\i derasha} from {\i karet/kerisus}. They read the textual elaboration as yielding only one teaching. Since they need that teaching for the hovering-condition case, they must argue against Rabbi Eliezer on other grounds.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Temporal Conditions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hovering Conditions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having established that a condition hovering over a woman\u8217's head for her entire life is not {\i kerisus}, the {\i gemara} now explores the precise parameters of this rule with greater care.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Duration Analysis\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava poses the following case: {\i harei zeh gittech al menat shelo tishti yayin kol yemei chayai} \u8212- this is your {\i get} on the condition that you do not drink wine for all the days of my life. This is not {\i kerisus}, because the condition perpetually hangs over her and maintains a residual bond to her first husband. But what if he says {\i kol yemei chayai ploni} \u8212- for the entire duration of Reuven\u8217's life? The {\i gemara} initially suggests that this {\i is} valid {\i kerisus}, because Reuven might die and the condition would then be permanently satisfied. The condition is not necessarily eternal and therefore does not perpetually subjugate her to the first husband\u8217's authority.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} immediately challenges this distinction: {\i mai shna chayei ploni} \u8212- why is Reuven\u8217's lifetime different from the husband\u8217's own lifetime? Perhaps the husband will also die before her, in which case the condition would similarly resolve. If the possibility of Reuven\u8217's death makes the condition temporally limited and thus valid, the same logic should apply when the husband stipulates the duration of his own life, since the husband himself might die before the wife.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} therefore revises the formulation. The operative distinction is: if the husband says {\i kol yemei chayaichi} \u8212- for all the days of {\i your} life \u8212- that is not {\i kerisus}, because then the condition will necessarily last as long as she lives and will never cease to hang over her. If the condition is tied to the husband\u8217's own lifespan or to that of a third party, it is {\i kerisus}, because the death of the husband or the third party \u8212- while they are both still alive \u8212- could satisfy or dissolve the condition.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This distinction, however, is not entirely satisfying. {\i Tosafot} points to a different {\i gemara} which appears to hold that both {\i kol yemei chayai} (the husband\u8217's life) and {\i kol yemei chayaichi} (the wife\u8217's life) are invalid as conditions, while only {\i kol yemei chayei ploni} \u8212- a third party\u8217's lifespan \u8212- is valid {\i kerisus}. The reasoning there is that a condition tied to the husband\u8217's lifespan provides no meaningful divorce at all: if the husband must die for her to be free of the condition, his death would have made her a widow and freed her regardless, rendering the {\i get} superfluous. For a {\i get} to have practical meaning \u8212- to free her from him while they are both still alive \u8212- the condition cannot be dissolved only by his death. That version of the rule makes considerably more intuitive sense than what the {\i gemara} here appears to conclude.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 One-Day Get\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Ami posed a question to Rav Nachman: what is the law in the case of {\i hayom at ishti u-le-machar at ishti} \u8212- today you are my wife, and tomorrow you are my wife again? That is, a husband gives a {\i get} that is valid for one day only, expiring at the end of that day, after which the marriage automatically resumes. Does this constitute a valid {\i get}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The question can be framed in two directions. From Rabbi Eliezer\u8217's perspective: Rabbi Eliezer permits dividing a marriage along certain lines \u8212- she is divorced {\i vis-\u224 ?-vis} everyone except Reuven. But in that case, at least those from whom she is divorced are divorced from her permanently. Here the division is temporal: for all people simultaneously, she is free today and not free tomorrow. Perhaps Rabbi Eliezer would not extend his leniency to this kind of on-and-off arrangement. Conversely, the rabbis reject Rabbi Eliezer\u8217's position because when he says {\i chutz mi-Reuven} she is never completely severed from her husband \u8212- the {\i get} never achieves full {\i kerisus}. But here, for one full day, she is completely and unconditionally divorced. Perhaps that momentary complete divorce is itself sufficient.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Severance Principle\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 After deliberation, the {\i gemara} resolves the question and concludes: {\i keivan de-paska, paska} \u8212- once severed, severed. This applies equally according to Rabbi Eliezer and according to the rabbis. If a complete and unconditional divorce took effect \u8212- even for a single day \u8212- that divorce cannot be undone. The state of full {\i kerisus}, once actualized, is permanent. The husband\u8217's stipulation that the marriage would resume the following day is simply ineffective, because a divorce that has fully taken effect cannot be unilaterally reversed by the husband\u8217's subsequent declaration.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Marriage Conditions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Reuven Marriage Condition\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now presents a {\i baraita} with a sharply different kind of condition: {\i harei zeh gittech al menat she-tinasi le-ploni} \u8212- this is your {\i get} on the condition that you marry so-and-so. The {\i baraita} states: {\i harei zo lo tinasei} \u8212- she should not marry him \u8212- and {\i ve-im nis\u8217'et, lo teitzei} \u8212- but if she did marry him, she does not need to leave. The precise meaning of this ruling is far from obvious, and the {\i gemara} will require two attempts to clarify it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The reason to be concerned about actually fulfilling such a condition relates to appearances: {\i shema yomru nesheihem notnim bematana} \u8212- people will say that husbands are trading wives as gifts. If Reuven divorces his wife on the condition that she marry Shimon, and Shimon divorces his wife on the condition that she marry Reuven, the result is an exchange of wives \u8212- a practice deemed morally and socially unacceptable.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Nachman\u8217's Reading\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Nachman offers a first interpretation of the {\i baraita}. {\i Harei zo lo tinasei lo} \u8212- she should not marry Shimon (the specified party). The concern is the wife-swapping appearance. And {\i ve-im nis\u8217'et lo, lo teitzei} \u8212- if she did in fact marry Shimon, she need not leave. Although the rabbis discourage the marriage because of appearances, if she went ahead and fulfilled the condition, the {\i get} was valid and the new marriage stands. We do not uproot a completed marriage simply because of the societal concern.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But the {\i gemara} challenges this first reading. It reads {\i lo tinasei} as applying only to Shimon \u8212- implying that she {\i may} marry someone else. But how can she marry someone else? If the condition is that she marry Shimon, and the condition has not been met, she may not yet be divorced from her first husband. If she marries another man, she is potentially an {\i eshet ish} committing adultery. One might answer that she could marry the other man temporarily, eventually divorce him, and then go and marry Shimon to satisfy the condition \u8212- but this raises the problem of control: can she really guarantee that this chain of events will unfold? The {\i gemara} draws a comparison to the case of {\i konam einai be-sheina hayom im ishan lemachar} \u8212- my eyes are forbidden from sleep today if I sleep tomorrow \u8212- where Rav Nachman ruled that one may sleep today and trust oneself not to sleep tomorrow.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava\u8217's Challenge\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava objects to this comparison sharply. In the sleeping case, the matter is {\i be-didi ka\u8217'ima} \u8212- within the person\u8217's own control. {\i De\u8217'i ba\u8217'ei mavriz nafshei be-silu\u8217'asa} \u8212- if he really wants to, he can scratch himself with a thorn, stand under cold water, do whatever it takes to remain awake the next day. But here, {\i be-dida ka\u8217'ima l\u8217'garushei?} \u8212- can she get herself divorced from the other man if she wants to? Can she get Shimon to marry her? These things depend on other people\u8217's decisions entirely.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Control Analysis\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} accepts Rava\u8217's point: the sleeping case and this case are fundamentally dissimilar because in the sleeping case the person retains direct control over the triggering condition, whereas here the woman has no meaningful control over whether the {\i tnai} of marrying Shimon will ever be satisfied. She cannot compel Shimon to marry her. Even if Shimon were willing, she cannot compel her interim husband to divorce her. And even if all of that were somehow achieved, Shimon might die before any of it could occur.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Resolution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava therefore offers the only coherent reading of the {\i baraita}. {\i Harei zo lo tinasei \u8212- lo lo ve-lo le-acher} \u8212- she should not marry Shimon, and she should not marry anyone else either. She should not marry Shimon because of the wife-swapping appearance. She should not marry anyone else because she cannot guarantee she will ever be able to satisfy the condition, and until the condition is satisfied she may never have been divorced \u8212- meaning any children from such a union could be {\i mamzerim}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Ve-im nis\u8217'et lo, lo teitzei} \u8212- if she did marry Shimon, she does not leave, because in that case the {\i tnai} was in fact satisfied and the {\i get} was valid. {\i D\u8217'mishum gezeirah lo mafkinan} \u8212- we do not force her out of the marriage simply because of the appearance concern. {\i Aval le-acher teitzei} \u8212- but if she married someone other than Shimon, she must leave him, because {\i de-ba\u8217'ei kiyumei le-tnai} \u8212- the condition has not been met and may never be met, and we cannot allow the marriage to stand given the probability that she was never actually divorced. The {\i gemara} notes that a {\i baraita} confirms Rava\u8217's reading precisely: {\i harei zo lo tinasei lo lo ve-lo le-acher; ve-im nis\u8217'et lo, lo teitzei; le-acher, teitzei}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This resolution raises a broader and troubling observation. The structure of this {\i tnai} effectively makes it impossible for the woman to marry anyone. She cannot marry Shimon because of the prohibition against the appearance of wife-swapping. She cannot marry anyone else because she cannot guarantee fulfillment of the condition. The practical result is that a husband, simply by attaching this kind of condition to a {\i get}, has made it impossible for his wife to remarry \u8212- while she cannot be certain she is even divorced. This is precisely the mechanism by which a {\i get} can simultaneously exist and create an {\i agunah}. Unlike the explicit {\i chutz mi-Reuven} of Rabbi Eliezer, which the rabbis openly reject, here the condition has the same practical effect by a more indirect route: she is, in practice, bound.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} pushes the analysis even further. Given that the condition is not within the woman\u8217's control to satisfy \u8212- she cannot ensure that she will marry Shimon \u8212- {\i Tosafot} argues that she should be considered a fully married woman ({\i eshet ish}) from the outset, since the {\i get} is conditioned on something that may never occur. If she is still considered married to her first husband, then any purported {\i kiddushin} to Shimon would be ineffective. Yet if she is considered {\i eshet ish}, then when she does go and marry Shimon to satisfy the condition, she is doing so as a married woman \u8212- and yet, by satisfying the condition, retroactively the {\i get} takes effect and she was divorced\u8230 ? but then she has left Shimon without a {\i get} and would need one. {\i Tosafot} works through these loops without fully resolving them here, but the question itself is illuminating.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Impossible Condition (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Absurd Conditions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now turns to a {\i baraita} dealing with conditions that are not merely difficult to satisfy but physically impossible. Examples given: {\i al menat she-ta\u8217'ali la-rakiya} \u8212- on the condition that you ascend to heaven; {\i al menat she-terdi li-tehom} \u8212- that you descend to the depths; {\i al menat she-tivle\u8217'i kaneh shel arba amot} \u8212- that you swallow a pole four {\i amot} long; {\i al menat she-tavi\u8217'i li kaneh bein me\u8217'ah amah} \u8212- that you bring a pole a hundred {\i amot} in length; {\i al menat she-ta\u8217'avri et ha-yam ha-gadol be-raglayich} \u8212- that you cross the Mediterranean Sea on foot. The anonymous view of the {\i baraita} holds that this is {\i eino get} \u8212- not a valid {\i get}, because the condition is perpetually unmet, and she remains in suspended uncertainty.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Yehuda ben Teima\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yehuda ben Teima disagrees: {\i kaze get} \u8212- this is a valid {\i get}. His reasoning: {\i kol tnai she-ei efshar lo le-kayemo be-sofo, ve-hitnah alav mi-techilato, eino ela ke-miflig bi-devarim ve-kasher} \u8212- any condition that is impossible ever to fulfill, yet was stipulated at the outset, is merely an exaggerated empty utterance and does not constitute a real {\i tnai}. We therefore discard it, and the {\i get} stands unconditionally. The {\i tnai} is so obviously absurd that no reasonable person could have intended it as a genuine legal condition \u8212- it must be understood as hyperbole or bluster, and the {\i get} is therefore valid from the moment it was given.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Nachman\u8217's Ruling\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Nachman rules in accordance with Rabbi Yehuda ben Teima. Rav Nachman bar Yitzchak adds that the {\i mishnah} in Bava Metzia corroborates this position by implication: it states {\i kol she-efshar lo le-kayemo be-sofo\u8230 ? tnao kayam} \u8212- any condition that {\i can} eventually be fulfilled is binding. The inference \u8212- {\i ha ei efshar, tnao batel} \u8212- if it cannot be fulfilled, the condition is void. This is taken as independent support for Rabbi Yehuda ben Teima\u8217's ruling.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Impossible Condition (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Abaye vs.\u160 ?Rava\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 With the ruling in favor of Rabbi Yehuda ben Teima established, the {\i gemara} now probes the precise scope of what \u8220"impossible to fulfill\u8221" means. The critical question: does the category of conditions that are voided as impossible extend only to physically impossible acts, or does it also include acts that are physically possible but {\i halakhically} forbidden?\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Forbidden Acts\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Abaye\u8217's Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye holds that {\i hi hi} \u8212- the case of a forbidden act is the same as the case of a physically impossible act. A condition such as {\i al menat she-tochli basar chazir} \u8212- on the condition that you eat pig meat \u8212- is thrown out and the {\i get} is valid unconditionally. While the woman could physically eat the {\i chazir}, she is not permitted to do so, and Abaye treats this as functionally equivalent to impossibility. The general principle stated in the {\i baraita} \u8212- \u8220"any condition that cannot be fulfilled\u8221" \u8212- is understood by Abaye to include forbidden acts, since they cannot legitimately be performed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava\u8217's Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava disagrees. {\i Efshar de-achla ve-lakya} \u8212- it is possible that she will eat it and receive {\i malkot}. The act is physically executable. The fact that it carries a penalty does not remove it from the category of possible conditions. A condition that can in principle be met \u8212- even through violation \u8212- is a binding {\i tnai}, and the {\i get} is not valid unless the condition is actually fulfilled.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then maps out the implications of each position for the general rule ({\i klal}) stated by Rabbi Yehuda ben Teima. According to Abaye, the {\i klal} \u8212- \u8220"anything that cannot be fulfilled\u8221" \u8212- includes even a case like {\i basar chazir}, which is physically possible but forbidden. The phrase {\i kaze} (\u8220"like this,\u8221" referring to the paradigmatic absurd cases of going to heaven) is understood to establish that only the most extreme physical impossibilities are the paradigm; the {\i klal} extends further to include forbidden acts. According to Rava, {\i kaze} refers precisely to absurd physical impossibilities, and the {\i klal} does not extend to forbidden acts. Rather, {\i basar chazir} \u8212- physically possible even if forbidden \u8212- remains a binding {\i tnai}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sexual Conditions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} cites a {\i baraita} that addresses sexual conditions: {\i harei zeh gittech al menat she-tiv\u8217'ali le-ploni} \u8212- this is your {\i get} on the condition that you have sexual relations with so-and-so. If the condition is met, the {\i get} is valid. If not, it is not a {\i get}. This {\i baraita} presents the condition as binding, consistent with Rava\u8217's position that a physically possible act \u8212- even one that may entail legal or moral problems \u8212- remains an enforceable {\i tnai}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Others\u8217' Violations\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i baraita} continues with a contrasting case. If the husband stipulates: {\i al menat shelo tiv\u8217'ali le-aba u-le-avich} \u8212- on the condition that you do not have sexual relations with my father or your father \u8212- {\i ein chosheshin shema ni\u8217'valah lahem} \u8212- we are not concerned that she might violate this. The implication is that we treat compliance with this condition as essentially guaranteed, and it is therefore a valid {\i get}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then asks: what if the formulation were reversed \u8212- {\i al menat she-tiv\u8217'ali le-aba u-le-avich} \u8212- on the condition that you {\i do} have relations with my father or your father? Would that be a binding condition? The {\i gemara} implies it would not be, and this seems to support Abaye\u8217's view that forbidden acts cannot be conditions. But Rava responds by distinguishing between this case and the {\i basar chazir} case.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava\u8217's Synthesis\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava\u8217's answer: {\i bishlama basar chazir, efshar de-achla ve-lakya} \u8212- eating {\i chazir} is physically possible; she does it, she gets {\i malkot}. Similarly, {\i al menat she-tiv\u8217'ali le-ploni} \u8212- on the condition that she has relations with some other man \u8212- that too is physically possible; {\i efshar de-mishachda bei be-mamona} \u8212- perhaps she could pay him, persuade him, and the act could occur. But {\i aba ve-avich mi avdi issura?} \u8212- can she ensure that her father-in-law or her own father will commit this sin? Here it is not merely that the act is forbidden; it is forbidden by someone {\i else}, over whom she has no control. The combination of the act being prohibited {\i and} requiring a violation by another person places it in the category of impossibility. One who is merely being asked to violate {\i herself} can still choose to do so \u8212- the condition is physically executable. But a condition requiring another person to commit a serious violation is treated as tantamount to impossible, because one cannot realistically compel another person to transgress. Rava thus draws a line: a violation that the woman herself commits can be a binding {\i tnai}; a violation that depends on someone else committing a transgression is not a realistic condition and is voided.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Torah Violations\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosefta Challenge\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} raises a challenge from a {\i Tosefta} that directly addresses the {\i basar chazir} case. The {\i Tosefta} states: {\i harei zeh gittech al menat she-tochli basar chazir} \u8212- this is your {\i get} on the condition that you eat pig meat. Similarly: if she was a non-{\i kohen} \u8212- {\i al menat she-tochli bi-terumah} \u8212- on the condition that she eat {\i terumah} (which a non-{\i kohen} is forbidden to eat). And if she was a {\i nezirah} \u8212- {\i al menat she-tishti yayin} \u8212- on the condition that she drink wine. In each of these cases, the {\i Tosefta} rules: {\i yitkayem ha-tnai harei zeh get; ve-im lav eino get} \u8212- if the condition is fulfilled, it is a valid {\i get}; if not, it is not. This confirms Rava\u8217's position that a forbidden act, being physically possible, constitutes a binding {\i tnai}. For Rava, the ruling is clear. But for Abaye it is difficult, since Abaye holds that such conditions should be voided.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Abaye\u8217's Response\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye responds: {\i mi savrat divrei ha-kol hi?} \u8212- who says this {\i Tosefta} represents everyone? The {\i Tosefta} reflects only the view of the {\i Rabbanan}, not that of Rabbi Yehuda ben Teima. And recall: even the {\i Rabbanan} hold that an impossible condition is still binding \u8212- meaning the woman is simply stuck, unable to fulfill an impossible {\i tnai} and unable to remarry. According to the {\i Rabbanan}, the {\i tnai} is always enforced, regardless of whether it is possible or forbidden. Therefore the {\i Tosefta}\u8217's ruling that {\i basar chazir} is a binding condition tells us nothing about Rabbi Yehuda ben Teima\u8217's view \u8212- which Abaye applies to forbidden acts as well, treating them as non-binding.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Matne al Mah Shekatuv\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 At this point the {\i gemara} poses a more fundamental objection. Rather than debating whether forbidden acts are binding {\i tnai\u8217'im}, perhaps the entire category should be invalidated on a different basis entirely: the principle of {\i matneh al mah she-katuv ba-Torah} \u8212- stipulating against what is written in the Torah. The rule is {\i kol ha-matneh al mah she-katuv ba-Torah, tnao batel} \u8212- any stipulation that runs contrary to Torah law is void. A condition requiring a violation of {\i halakha} would seem to fall squarely within this principle and should therefore be voided automatically, prior to any debate between Abaye and Rava.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Adda bar Rav Ika addresses this challenge. The principle of {\i matneh al mah she-katuv ba-Torah} does not apply to every condition that touches upon a Torah law. Rather, it applies specifically when someone is attempting to alter or eliminate the fundamental legal structure of a Torah institution. The classic example is {\i she\u8217'erah, kesutah, ve-onatah} \u8212- a husband who says \u8220"you are my wife, but I am exempt from providing you food, clothing, and conjugal rights.\u8221" There, the husband is trying to maintain the institution of marriage while stripping out its Torah-mandated obligations. He is not merely attaching a condition to his marriage; he is attempting to restructure the institution itself, removing components that are intrinsic to it. That is {\i matneh al mah she-katuv ba-Torah}, and it is voided.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But here \u8212- a {\i get} conditioned on eating {\i chazir} \u8212- the husband is not trying to alter the structure of {\i gittin}. He is issuing a fully normal {\i get}; he is simply conditioning its effectiveness on a specific act. The institution of {\i gittin} itself is unchanged. The husband is not saying \u8220"I give you a {\i get} but without some Torah obligation of divorce\u8221"; he is saying \u8220"here is a complete {\i get} that takes effect under a certain condition.\u8221" That the condition happens to require a transgression does not transform it into a {\i matneh al mah she-katuv ba-Torah} in the technical sense.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Adda\u8217's Clarification\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The distinction Rav Adda draws is thus structural: {\i matneh al mah she-katuv ba-Torah} refers to attempts to {\i uproot} a Torah institution \u8212- to have the institution exist but without one of its Torah-defined parameters. It does not refer to conditions that require a violation in order for an institution to take effect. In the case of {\i she\u8217'erah, kesutah, ve-onatah}, the institution of marriage is being invoked while simultaneously eliminating obligations that are constitutive of what Torah marriage means. That is an attempt to create a Torah institution on non-Torah terms. In the case of a conditional {\i get}, no Torah institution is being restructured. The {\i get} is a complete and ordinary {\i get}; it simply has not yet taken effect. Once the condition is met, the {\i get} will function exactly as the Torah envisions.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ravina\u8217's Objection\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Ravina challenges this framework. When the woman eats the {\i chazir} in order to satisfy the condition of the {\i get}, who is causing the violation? It is the husband who stipulated this condition. {\i Shehu sachar deideh} \u8212- his benefit, the divorce, is what drives her to transgress. Can one really say the husband is not implicated in the violation she commits in order to satisfy {\i his} condition? Is this not itself a form of {\i matneh al mah she-katuv ba-Torah} \u8212- using the mechanism of a Torah institution to compel a violation?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Ravina ultimately resolves this by refining the application of the principle. {\i Matneh al mah she-katuv ba-Torah} applies only when the Torah institution {\i certainly} and {\i definitively} uproots a Torah obligation \u8212- as in {\i she\u8217'erah, kesutah, ve-onatah}, where the husband\u8217's declaration of marriage is simultaneously and unconditionally a declaration that those obligations do not apply. In the {\i basar chazir} case, there is no certainty that any violation will occur. {\i Lo teichol ve-lo tisgaresh} \u8212- she need not eat the {\i chazir}, and she simply will not receive the {\i get}. The Torah violation is not embedded in the transaction; it is something she may or may not choose to do. The condition does not force a violation; it offers a choice. Therefore, one cannot say the husband\u8217's condition is itself a {\i matneh al mah she-katuv ba-Torah} \u8212- the Torah is not being overridden, because nothing in this transaction definitively requires that it be violated.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Gittin 85\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Reregiving & Framing\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Reregiving Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The daf opens in the middle of 84b, with the {\i gemara} asking {\i keitzad ya\u8217'aseh} \u8212- what should a man do if he gave his wife a {\i get} with a {\i chutz} clause, declaring her permitted to all men except Reuven? Assuming that such a {\i get} is invalid, as the rabbis who disagree with Rabbi Eliezer hold, what is the remedy? The {\i gemara} answers: {\i yitlena heimena} \u8212- he takes it back from her and gives it again, this time simply declaring {\i harei at muteret l\u8217'chol adam}, without any exclusion.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rebbe vs.\u160 ?R. Shimon\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then asks: who is the {\i tanna} behind this requirement to physically redeliver the {\i get}, rather than simply reframing the original giving with a new verbal declaration? Chizkiya answers that this position belongs to Rabbi Shimon Elazar, as taught in a {\i baraita}: Rabbi Shimon Elazar holds that one must take the {\i get} back and give it anew, declaring {\i ha\u8217'geiteich}. This connects to a prior dispute: if a man gives a document to a woman framing it as a promissory note ({\i shtar chov}), and she opens it to discover it is a {\i get}, the {\i chachamim} hold that he may simply declare {\i ha\u8217'geiteich} while it remains in her hand, whereas Rabbi Shimon Elazar requires that he take it back and redeliver it. The {\i chutz} case, where he must redeliver and declare {\i harei at muteret l\u8217'chol adam} without exclusion, seems to align with Rabbi Shimon Elazar\u8217's more demanding position.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yochanan, however, says: {\i afilu teima Rebbi} \u8212- one can maintain that even Rebbi, who disagrees with Rabbi Shimon Elazar, would require regiving here. How so?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Kahana\u8217's Framework\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yochanan invokes the position of Rav Kahana \u8212- referred to here as \u8220"that one of yours\u8221" ({\i dichon amar}), reflecting Rav Kahana\u8217's origins in Bavel when Rabbi Yochanan addressed his Eretz Yisrael audience \u8212- who offers a principled distinction. In the {\i chutz} case, the woman has already acquired a degree of {\i pesul} for {\i kehunah} even from the invalid giving, on account of {\i rei\u8217'ach haget}. This is the crux of the conceptual framework: was the original act of giving {\i not a giving of a get at all}, or was it {\i a giving of a get, but a bad one}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rei\u8217'ach Haget\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This distinction carries significant analytical weight. If the original giving was not a giving of a {\i get} at all \u8212- as in the {\i shtar chov} case, where the husband explicitly framed the document as something entirely different \u8212- then the act of giving a {\i get} was never performed. In that circumstance, a subsequent declaration of {\i ha\u8217'geiteich} may suffice to frame the holding of the document as an act of giving a {\i get}, without requiring a new physical delivery. But if the original giving {\i was} an act of giving a {\i get}, albeit a defective one \u8212- as in the {\i chutz} case, where the husband said {\i harei at muteret l\u8217'chol adam chutz mi\u8217'Reuven} \u8212- then the act of giving was already performed, and performed badly. To correct it, one cannot merely reframe; the entire act must be redone from the start.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This framework also maps onto the question of {\i rei\u8217'ach haget}: where the original giving was not a giving of a {\i get} at all, there is no {\i rei\u8217'ach haget} and no residual {\i pesul} for {\i kehunah}. Where it was a defective giving of a {\i get}, the {\i rei\u8217'ach haget} takes effect. The two phenomena \u8212- whether a restatement suffices, and whether {\i rei\u8217'ach haget} applies \u8212- are expressions of the same underlying question about the nature of the original act.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might consider that the opposite conclusion would be equally defensible: if it was not a giving of a {\i get} at all, one should have to redo everything from scratch; whereas if it was a bad giving, perhaps one can simply correct it. The {\i gemara} runs the other way: a bad giving, precisely because it was an act of giving a {\i get}, cannot be patched over by words alone \u8212- the act must be re-executed. This is the position of Rabbi Yochanan in the name of Rav Kahana, and it provides a coherent account of why even Rebbi would require redelivery in the {\i chutz} case, while remaining consistent with Rebbi\u8217's more lenient stance in the {\i shtar chov} case.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Erasing Conditions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chutz Cannot Be Erased\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} turns to a related problem: if the {\i chutz} exclusion was written inside the body of the {\i get}, erasing it does not remedy the defect. Even if the text is subsequently corrected and no trace of the {\i chutz} remains, the {\i get} remains invalid. The reason is that the {\i get} was never written as a valid instrument of divorce \u8212- at the moment of its composition, it lacked the {\i da\u8217'as gerushin} necessary to function as a {\i get krisus}. No later correction to the text can retroactively supply what was absent at the time of writing.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafot on Erasures\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} uses this ruling as a springboard to clarify the general rule regarding erasures in a {\i get}. The implication of the {\i gemara} \u8212- {\i davka chutz} \u8212- is that the prohibition on erasure applies specifically to the case of {\i chutz}, because {\i chutz} fundamentally compromises the {\i krisus} of the {\i get} and cannot be undone. For other erasures, however, correction is in principle valid, provided procedural requirements are met. The reason we are not concerned that an ordinary erasure represents a tampering with the document is that such corrections can be properly authenticated.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Before Sharir V\u8217'Kayam\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} specifies the mechanism: the correction must be documented before the words {\i sharir v\u8217'kayam}, which serve as the closing formula of the document. Any alteration to the text \u8212- whether in a {\i get} or a {\i ketubah} \u8212- must be noted within the body of the document, ahead of the final {\i hakol sharir v\u8217'kayam}, so that the attesting witnesses can validate the correction. For example, if a name was written incorrectly and then changed, a notation such as \u8220"in line two, there is an erasure\u8221" would be written before {\i sharir v\u8217'kayam}, and the {\i edim} would sign with awareness of that correction. Alternatively, if the notation appears separately, the {\i edim} must themselves authenticate what was written there.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} acknowledges the practical concern: without such safeguards, there would be no barrier to altering a document after the fact \u8212- changing a sum, a name, or any other material detail. The requirement that corrections precede {\i sharir v\u8217'kayam} or be independently attested by {\i edim} is precisely what prevents such abuse. {\i Tosafot} further notes that while erasure is technically permissible in a {\i get} under these conditions, the practice ({\i nahagu ha\u8217'olam}) is to be stringent, and in practice a {\i get} with an erasure would ordinarily be rewritten. {\i Ketubot}, by contrast, are not treated with the same stringency.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The important caveat, however, remains: none of this applies to {\i chutz}. Where {\i chutz} was written into the {\i get}, erasing it does nothing, because the defect is not in the text but in the fundamental nature of the instrument as written.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might ask: if it is the signatures of the {\i edim} that give the {\i get} its legal force \u8212- whether one holds {\i edei mesirah karsei} or {\i edei chasimah karsei} \u8212- why can we not say that as long as the {\i chutz} is erased before the {\i edim} sign, their signature would then ratify a valid instrument? The {\i gemara}\u8217's focus on the act of writing, and Tosafot\u8217's emphasis on the {\i sofer}\u8217's commission from the {\i ba\u8217'al}, suggests that the writing itself \u8212- and the frame of mind and instruction under which it was composed \u8212- carries independent significance, at least according to the view that {\i edei mesirah karsei}, where even the writing must reflect the direct {\i tzivuy} of the husband. But the question is not entirely without force.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Verbal vs.\u160 ?Written Chutz\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Safra: Written Only\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now addresses a fundamental question about the scope of the ruling. Rav Safra notes pointedly: {\i k\u8217'tavo b\u8217'tocho tnan} \u8212- \u8220"we taught: if it was written inside.\u8221" His emphasis is deliberate: only if the {\i chutz} is physically written within the {\i get} does it compromise the instrument. One might think this is obvious \u8212- after all, that is precisely what the {\i mishna} says \u8212- but Rav Safra is forestalling an expansive reading.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Objection: Before Toref\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} raises the objection: one might have thought that the rule {\i k\u8217'tavo b\u8217'tocho} \u8212- that writing {\i chutz} in the {\i get} invalidates it even after erasure \u8212- applies specifically when the {\i chutz} was written {\i after} the {\i toref}, the key operative line of the {\i get} (i.e., {\i harei at muteret l\u8217'chol adam} and its equivalent). In that case the core line was written without the taint of {\i chutz}, and the invalidation comes from what surrounds it. But if the {\i chutz} instruction was given {\i before} the {\i toref} was written \u8212- even if only verbally, communicated to the {\i sofer} before he composed the central line \u8212- perhaps even that verbal framing would invalidate the {\i get} as an instrument, since the {\i toref} itself was written in the context of a {\i chutz} intent.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is not a frivolous concern. If the husband instructed the {\i sofer} \u8212- \u8220"write {\i harei at muteret l\u8217'chol adam}, but understand that my intention is to divorce her {\i chutz mi\u8217'Reuven}\u8221" \u8212- and the {\i sofer} then wrote the {\i toref} under that directive, there is a reasonable argument that the {\i toref} was never written as an expression of {\i da\u8217'as gerushin}. The key operative line of the {\i get} would have been composed without the proper intent, even if nothing problematic appears in the text.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Safra\u8217's point is: do not say this. A verbal instruction \u8212- even one given before the {\i toref} was written \u8212- does not affect the validity of the instrument. The {\i get} as an object is defined by what is written in it, not by what was said to the {\i sofer} in the course of its composition. If nothing invalid appears in the document, the document is valid.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava: Before Toref\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava disagrees sharply. He holds: {\i lo shanu ela l\u8217'achar hatoref} \u8212- the ruling that writing {\i chutz} inside the {\i get} invalidates it even after erasure applies only when the {\i chutz} was written {\i after} the {\i toref}. But if the {\i chutz} instruction was given {\i before} the {\i toref} was written, even a verbal instruction invalidates the {\i get}. In that case, the {\i toref} itself was composed without proper {\i da\u8217'as gerushin} \u8212- the husband\u8217's framing of the entire act was as a {\i chutz} divorce, and the {\i sofer} wrote the key line under that direction. The instrument is therefore invalid, even though the words {\i chutz mi\u8217'Reuven} never appear in the text.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is a striking position, and at first blush it seems to contradict the plain sense of the {\i mishna}, which focuses on what is written. Rava\u8217's reading is admittedly interpretive, locating the {\i mishna}\u8217's ruling in the context of {\i l\u8217'achar hatoref} rather than treating it as a general statement. This is not the simple {\i pshat} of the {\i mishna}, but the {\i gemara} accepts it as Rava\u8217's position.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Silence the Husband\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava\u8217's practical corollary follows from this: {\i d\u8217'amar lehu Rava l\u8217'hanei d\u8217'chasvei gitei} \u8212- Rava instructed the {\i soferim} who write {\i gittin}: keep the husband silent ({\i shatku shutku l\u8217'ba\u8217'al}) until the {\i toref} of the {\i get} has been written. If the husband is going to say something problematic before the {\i sofer} reaches the key line, he can retroactively compromise the validity of the {\i get} as an instrument. The practical solution is to keep the husband occupied and quiet \u8212- distracted from making any declarations or conditions \u8212- until the {\i toref} has been composed, at which point the core of the instrument is already established and any subsequent statements can be managed separately.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This teaching also opens onto the broader concern that follows: even instructions that are not technically {\i chutz} may be problematic if given before the {\i toref}. The reason Rava counsels general silence, rather than simply warning against {\i chutz} specifically, anticipates the discussion of {\i gezeirah atu chutz} \u8212- a protective decree extending the concern about {\i chutz} to all conditions stated before the {\i toref} is written.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Conditions & Chutz\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rebbe: No Erasure\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i baraita} is cited: {\i kol hatna\u8217'im poslin baget} \u8212- all conditions invalidate a {\i get}. This is the position of Rebbi. The {\i chachamim} respond differently.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chachamim: Chutz Only\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i chachamim} state: {\i kol shepasul al peh pasul bichtav} \u8212- anything that is problematic when stated orally is problematic when written, and anything that is not problematic orally is not problematic when written. The application: {\i chutz}, which invalidates the {\i get} when stated verbally at the moment of delivery, likewise invalidates it when written into the document. An {\i al mnat} condition \u8212- a standard conditional clause \u8212- which does not invalidate the {\i get} when stated verbally, does not invalidate it when written either.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Before the {\i gemara} explains how Rebbi is to be understood, it is worth noting that one might have read Rebbi\u8217's position as a principled claim about the {\i cheftza} of the {\i get} itself: a {\i get} that is written without reservation expresses an absolute {\i krisus} in its very text, whereas a {\i get} that incorporates a condition \u8212- even a standard {\i al mnat} \u8212- is itself a qualified instrument, and the instrument as an object no longer expresses absolute severance. That reading has genuine force as a {\i svara}. However, as {\i Tosafot} explains, this is not how the {\i gemara} actually understands the dispute.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} frames the issue as follows: the practical question is whether a condition {\i written} into a {\i get} and subsequently {\i erased} before delivery can be treated as though it never existed. The {\i chachamim} hold that only {\i chutz} is irredeemably problematic \u8212- {\i chutz} written into the {\i get} compromises the instrument fundamentally, and erasing it does not help. Any other condition, however, if written and then erased, simply goes away; it does not taint the instrument, because the {\i chachamim} hold that the equivalence between verbal and written applies symmetrically, and a standard condition stated verbally is not a problem. Rebbi, by contrast, extends the logic of {\i chutz} to all written conditions: just as erasing a written {\i chutz} does not help, so too erasing any other condition that was written into the {\i get} does not help. This is not because all conditions are intrinsically as severe as {\i chutz}, but because of a {\i gezeirah atu chutz} \u8212- a protective decree that, once anything has been written and erased, we do not permit, lest it lead to doing the same with {\i chutz}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Zeira: Before/After\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Zeira offers a further refinement. He holds that the debate between Rebbi and the {\i chachamim} applies specifically to the case of a condition written {\i before the toref} \u8212- the key operative line \u8212- and subsequently erased. In that zone, Rebbi is concerned: {\i gazminan al mnat atu chutz} \u8212- we fear that permitting the erasure of a standard condition will lead to erasing a {\i chutz} written before the {\i toref}, or will otherwise be too close to scenarios where verbal {\i chutz} before the {\i toref} also causes problems. The {\i chachamim} are not worried and permit the erasure of any non-{\i chutz} condition.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But after the {\i toref} \u8212- {\i divrei hakol kasher} \u8212- everyone agrees that a standard condition written after the key line and then erased is entirely valid. The whole debate, on Rabbi Zeira\u8217's reading, is confined to the pre-{\i toref} zone.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} cites a teaching presented before Rabbi Avin before Rabbi Zeira: if a {\i get} was written conditionally ({\i al tnai}), it is invalid \u8212- {\i divrei hakol}. The {\i gemara} challenges this: surely there is a recorded dispute? After correction, the teaching is reformulated: {\i divrei hakol kasher} \u8212- everyone agrees it is valid. The context must be a condition written and erased {\i after} the {\i toref}, where Rabbi Zeira\u8217's framework places consensus. The {\i gemara} explains that the tradition bearer remembered that the ruling was {\i divrei hakol} on something, but may have confused whether it was {\i pasul} or {\i kasher} \u8212- {\i kasher b\u8217'pasul michelef} \u8212- whereas confusing {\i divrei hakol} with {\i Rebbi alone} is not the kind of mistake one makes.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava on Toref\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava disagrees with Rabbi Zeira\u8217's limitation. Rava holds that Rebbi\u8217's concern applies even {\i after} the {\i toref}: {\i de\u8217'Rebbi savar gazrinan atu lifnei hatoref} \u8212- Rebbi worries that permitting erased conditions after the {\i toref} will lead to doing the same before the {\i toref}, and so Rebbi prohibits erased written conditions throughout the {\i get}. The {\i chachamim} are untroubled by that slippery slope and allow erased conditions after the {\i toref}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But before the {\i toref}, both Rava and Rabbi Zeira agree: {\i divrei hakol pasul}. The difference between them is only in how far back they locate the consensus zone. For Rabbi Zeira, before the {\i toref} is where the debate lies; after the {\i toref} all agree it is valid. For Rava, before the {\i toref} all agree it is invalid; after the {\i toref} is where the debate lies.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gezeirah Concern\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The concluding summary of this entire passage identifies the layered structure of the analysis. At the core is {\i chutz}, which fundamentally compromises the identity of the {\i get} as an instrument of {\i gerushin} \u8212- whether stated verbally at the moment of giving or written into the document. Around that core sits a series of protective extensions. Rava\u8217's instruction to keep the husband silent before the {\i toref} is written reflects the concern that even a verbal {\i chutz} before the {\i toref} would invalidate the instrument. The debate between Rebbi and the {\i chachamim} about erasing conditions reflects the concern that permitting erased conditions will lead people to treat a written {\i chutz} as erasable. And the zone before the {\i toref} is treated with particular severity \u8212- {\i divrei hakol} \u8212- because that is where the {\i chutz} concern is most acute.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The two fundamental positions to keep distinct are those of Rav Safra and Rava. Rav Safra holds that verbal instructions to the {\i sofer}, even if framed in terms of {\i chutz}, do not affect the validity of the {\i get} as an instrument \u8212- only what is written inside matters. Rava holds that verbal instructions given before the {\i toref} was composed do affect the instrument, because the key line was written without proper {\i da\u8217'as gerushin}. Everything else in this section \u8212- the debates about erasing conditions, the {\i gezeirah atu chutz}, the scope of Rebbi\u8217's concern \u8212- is secondary to that foundational disagreement.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Categories of Exclusions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Relatives & Slaves\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A new {\i mishna} opens with a series of cases in which a husband declares {\i harei at muteret l\u8217'chol adam} followed by an {\i ela} \u8212- \u8220"except for\u8221" \u8212- excluding specific categories of men. The first group of exclusions: except for my father, your father, my brother, your brother, a {\i eved}, a non-Jew ({\i ovdei kochavim}), and anyone with whom {\i kiddushin} would not be binding \u8212- all of these exclusions leave the {\i get} valid ({\i kasher}). The reason, which the {\i mishna} will clarify through its concluding general principle, is that none of these men are people she could have entered into a binding marriage with. Excluding them from the scope of the {\i get} therefore does not genuinely limit the practical effect of the {\i gerushin}. The {\i kiddushin} with relatives and with {\i ovdei kochavim} or a {\i eved} would not take effect even without the exclusion \u8212- the {\i get} is not withdrawing anything real.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kohanim & Gerusha\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} then presents the contrasting category: except for a {\i almanah} vis-\u224 ?-vis a {\i Kohen Gadol}, a {\i gerushah} or {\i chalutzah} vis-\u224 ?-vis a {\i Kohen hedyot}, a {\i mamzeret} or {\i netinah} vis-\u224 ?-vis a Jew, or a {\i bas Yisrael} vis-\u224 ?-vis a {\i mamzer} or {\i nasin} \u8212- in all of these cases, the {\i get} is invalid ({\i pasul}).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The key question is why these exclusions are different. In the case of the {\i eved} or non-Jew, there is also a prohibition, so why is excluding the {\i Kohen} worse? The answer is not simply the severity of the prohibition. Even an {\i eved} or non-Jew may involve a {\i lo ta\u8217'aseh}; the distinction does not lie in the weight of the prohibition. The critical difference is that {\i kiddushin} with a {\i Kohen} would be {\i binding} even though prohibited. If a {\i gerushah} \u8212- a divorced woman \u8212- were to marry a {\i Kohen hedyot} in violation of the prohibition, the marriage would nonetheless take effect as a valid {\i kiddushin}, because the prohibition is an {\i issur lav}, not one that negates the binding force of the {\i kiddushin}. By contrast, {\i kiddushin} with a non-Jew or an {\i eved} does not take effect at all.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Accordingly, when the husband says \u8220"this is your {\i get} except with respect to {\i Kohanim},\u8221" he is doing something real: without that exclusion, if she had chosen to marry a {\i Kohen} in violation of the prohibition, the marriage would have been binding. By carving out the {\i Kohen}, he is preventing a binding {\i kiddushin} from taking effect \u8212- that is a genuine limitation on the effect of the {\i gerushin}. In the case of the {\i eved} or non-Jew, there is nothing real to limit; the {\i kiddushin} was never going to be binding in any case.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Binding Kiddushin\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} states its general principle explicitly: {\i kol mi she\u8217'yesh lo alav kiddushin} \u8212- anyone with whom {\i kiddushin} would be binding \u8212- even if that {\i kiddushin} would only be binding through a transgression ({\i afilu ba\u8217'aveirah}) \u8212- excluding such a person from the {\i get} invalidates it. And conversely, anyone with whom {\i kiddushin} is not binding at all, excluding that person leaves the {\i get} valid.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Minor\u8217's Future Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} records a question posed by Rava to Rav Nachman: {\i chutz mi\u8217'kiddushei katan mahu}? If the husband says \u8220"this is your {\i get} except with respect to a minor\u8221" \u8212- what is the ruling? The {\i kiddushin} of a {\i katan} cannot take effect right now; he is simply not in the category of one who can effect {\i kiddushin} at present. So perhaps this exclusion does not limit the {\i get} at the moment of giving. Or perhaps, since he will eventually reach the age at which {\i kiddushin} can take effect \u8212- {\i ati l\u8217'khlal havayah} \u8212- the exclusion is real even now.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Nachman responds: we have already been taught this, from the case of {\i ketanah misgarshet b\u8217'kiddushei aviha}. A minor girl whose father had married her off can be divorced even before she reaches adulthood. One might have questioned this on the grounds that {\i gerushin} should parallel {\i kiddushin} \u8212- just as {\i kiddushin} without a father requires adulthood, so too perhaps {\i gerushin} without a father should require adulthood. But the {\i gemara} answers: {\i ati l\u8217'khlal havayah} \u8212- since she will naturally mature and enter the category of those who can be given in marriage, she is already treated as within that category. The same logic applies to the {\i katan}: since he will naturally mature and enter the category of those who can effect {\i kiddushin}, he is considered within that category now, and excluding him is a real exclusion that invalidates the {\i get}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Unborn Children\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} next asks: {\i chutz min hanoladim mahu}? If the husband says this is your {\i get} except with respect to anyone born from today onward, is that a real exclusion? Right now there are no such people. Or do we say that since people will inevitably be born \u8212- {\i asidei d\u8217'miyalid} \u8212- this is an anticipated future that renders the exclusion real even now?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} attempts to resolve this from the {\i mishna}\u8217's ruling that excluding an {\i eved} or {\i oved kochavim} does not invalidate the {\i get}. If future status were always relevant, one should worry even about the {\i eved} or non-Jew, since either might eventually convert or be freed, after which {\i kiddushin} would be binding. Since the {\i mishna} is not concerned about that future, perhaps future status is never relevant.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} rejects this inference: {\i hanahu lo l\u8217'giyurei kaimei} \u8212- a non-Jew cannot be assumed to be on a path toward conversion; {\i hani l\u8217'ileyudei kaimei} \u8212- but children will be born, that is simply the natural course of events. The future arrival of new people in the world is an anticipated certainty; the conversion of a particular non-Jew is not. Accordingly, the question of {\i chutz min hanoladim} remains unresolved.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A conceptual point follows from this entire discussion. The {\i chachamim} who dispute Rabbi Eliezer generally hold that one cannot be {\i muteret} to one person and {\i asurah} to another \u8212- the {\i gemara} earlier invoked the principle {\i lo matzinu muteret lazeh va\u8217'asurah lazeh}. Yet in these {\i teku} cases, the {\i gemara} is entertaining the possibility that a {\i chutz} clause directed at people who are not currently present may in fact leave the {\i get} valid \u8212- which would mean the woman could be a {\i gerushah} vis-\u224 ?-vis most people and something more like an {\i eishes ish} vis-\u224 ?-vis those specified in the {\i chutz}. This is a more permissive stance toward conditional {\i gerushin} than one might have expected from the {\i chachamim}\u8217's position \u8212- the key criterion being not an absolute bar on partial divorce but whether the exclusion limits the effect of the {\i get} in the here and now.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sister\u8217's Husband\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} poses a further question: {\i chutz miba\u8217'al achosah mahu}? If the husband says this is your {\i get} except with respect to your sister\u8217's husband \u8212- right now she cannot marry her sister\u8217's husband in any case, since her sister is alive. But if her sister were to die, he would become available. Does the possibility of the sister\u8217's death render this a real exclusion? Or does the uncertainty of whether that will occur mean it is not a genuine limitation right now?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} attempts to draw an analogy to the {\i eved} and {\i oved kochavim} \u8212- they too might convert or be freed \u8212- and the {\i mishna} is not concerned about that future. But the {\i gemara} distinguishes: {\i geirus lo shchicha} \u8212- conversion is uncommon and cannot be assumed; {\i misah shchicha} \u8212- death is common. Death is an anticipated event; the question is only who dies first. Accordingly, the possibility that the sister will predecease makes the exclusion of the sister\u8217's husband potentially real, and the question of whether it constitutes an invalidating {\i chutz} is left unresolved.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Marginal Exclusions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sexual Exclusion\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now turns to exclusions that concern not the act of marriage ({\i nisuin}) but something more marginal. The {\i mishna}\u8217's entire framework has focused on whether the husband is excluding someone from the scope of {\i kiddushin} \u8212- from the possibility of a binding marriage. What if the exclusion is directed not at marriage but at something else?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Anal Intercourse\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Chutz miznus\u8217'ayich mahu}? If he says this is your {\i get} except with respect to extramarital sex with a certain man \u8212- is that a real {\i chutz}? The {\i mishna}\u8217's framework seems to focus on marriage: the question has always been whether certain men are excluded from the scope of the {\i get} in terms of their ability to marry her. If no marriage is excluded, perhaps this is not a real limitation on the {\i gerushin}. Or perhaps even limiting certain acts of sex constitutes a holding back on the full effect of the divorce.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then asks the parallel question: {\i chutz mishelo kedarkah mahu}? If the only thing excluded is {\i biah shelo kedarkah} \u8212- anal intercourse \u8212- while {\i biah kedarkah} is not excluded, is that a limiting exclusion? The Torah considers both forms of intercourse to constitute {\i mishkevei ishah}, and therefore perhaps even excluding one form of sex constitutes a limitation on the completeness of the {\i gerushin}. The {\i gemara} envisions \u8212- strikingly \u8212- the possibility that a {\i gerushin} could take partial effect with respect to one form of sexual union and not another.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Annulling Vows\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Chutz mehafarat nedarayich mahu}? If he says this is your {\i get} except that he retains the right to annul her vows \u8212- is that a real {\i chutz}? In terms of marriage, she is entirely free: she may marry anyone. But the Torah states {\i ishah yekimenu v\u8217'ishah yeferenu} \u8212- it is the husband who upholds or annuls her vows. If he is still exercising that prerogative, perhaps the Torah considers him still to be her husband, and the {\i get} is therefore not complete.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Terumah Rights\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Chutz mitrumasayich mahu}? If he says this is your {\i get} except that she may continue to eat his {\i terumah} \u8212- perhaps the {\i get} is valid since she remains free to marry anyone. Or perhaps the right to eat {\i terumah} is rooted in the verse {\i kinyan kaspo} \u8212- she is still considered his {\i kinyan}, his wife, and therefore the {\i get} is not genuine.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Inheritance Rights\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Chutz meyerushasayich mahu}? If he says this is your {\i get} except that when she dies he will still inherit her \u8212- she may marry anyone she chooses, but he retains the right of inheritance. Perhaps this is fine since no marriage is excluded. Or perhaps, since the verse establishes {\i lishero yirash osah} \u8212- his flesh inherits her \u8212- if he inherits her she remains considered his, and the severance is not real.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Document Kiddushin\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Chutz mikidushayich beshtar mahu}? If he says this is your {\i get} except that if someone tries to effect {\i kiddushin} with her through a {\i shtar} \u8212- a document \u8212- it will not take hold, though {\i kiddushin} by money or by {\i biah} remains possible. She can in fact enter a new marriage by other means; perhaps this is sufficient. Or perhaps, since all three modes of {\i kiddushin} are equated \u8212- {\i huksha havayah leyetziah}, the modes of marriage are parallel to the modes of divorce \u8212- she must be fully free in all modes simultaneously, and any limitation on even one mode prevents the {\i get} from being effective.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} leaves all of these questions as {\i teku} \u8212- unresolved. What emerges from this remarkable sequence is that the {\i gemara} is genuinely entertaining the possibility that a {\i get} could be valid while leaving some marginal aspect of the marital relationship intact, as long as the core function of the divorce \u8212- freeing her to enter a new binding marriage with any man \u8212- remains intact. At the same time, the {\i gemara} is uncertain whether even these marginal retentions might, on close reading of the relevant verses, indicate that the marital bond has not been fully severed.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Core Language\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Standard Punchline\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A new {\i mishna} states: {\i gufo shel get} \u8212- the essential text of the {\i get} \u8212- is {\i harei at muteret l\u8217'chol adam}: you are hereby permitted to marry any man. This is the minimal statement required at the core of the instrument of divorce.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Testify About Itself\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yehudah adds a further requirement. He cites the {\i Aramaic} text: {\i v\u8217'dein dei l\u8217'hevei lichi minai sefer teiruchin v\u8217'igert shivuchin v\u8217'get piturin l\u8217'meihach l\u8217'hitnasba l\u8217'chol gevar d\u8217'titzabyan} \u8212- \u8220"and this shall be from me a document of dismissal, a writ of separation, and a letter of release, to go and marry any man you desire.\u8221" The {\i gemara} will explain that Rabbi Yehudah\u8217's primary contribution is the word {\i v\u8217'dein} \u8212- \u8220"this.\u8221" Rabbi Yehudah holds that the {\i get} must testify about itself as the instrument of divorce. If the {\i get} merely declares {\i harei at muteret l\u8217'chol adam}, one might suppose that the divorce was effected through some other means and that this document merely records or proves the fact \u8212- that the {\i get} demonstrates she is divorced, rather than being itself the cause. Rabbi Yehudah requires that the document identify itself \u8212- {\i v\u8217'dein}, this very document \u8212- as the instrument through which the divorce is effected.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} concludes by noting the parallel text for a {\i get shichrur}: {\i harei at bat chorin, harei at l\u8217'atzmech} \u8212- you are a free woman, you belong to yourself \u8212- which constitutes the essential language of a writ of emancipation for a {\i shifchah} or {\i eved}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Gittin 86\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Core Get Language\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishna & R. Yehuda\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i daf} begins in the middle of 85b, where the {\i mishna} establishes that the key line of a {\i get} is {\i harei at muteret le-chol adam} \u8212- \u8220"behold, you are permitted to all men.\u8221" Rabbi Yehuda, however, requires that the {\i get} be more explicit and self-referential, stating {\i v\u8217'dein} \u8212- \u8220"and this\u8221" \u8212- should serve as an instrument of divorce. Not only does his formulation contain additional language, but it explicitly declares that the document itself is functioning as the instrument of divorce.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Leatzimech Analysis\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} then explores which formulations are valid and which are not. If a man says to his wife {\i harei at bat chorin} \u8212- \u8220"you are a free woman\u8221" \u8212- that is meaningless as a {\i get}, because {\i bat chorin} carries the specific meaning of being free as opposed to being a slave, and is not a language of divorce. Conversely, if he says to his female slave {\i harei at muteret le-chol adam}, that too is ineffective, since the language of permissibility to all men is not relevant to the slave\u8217's status of bondage or freedom.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The more interesting case arises when a man says to his wife {\i harei at le-atzmech} \u8212- \u8220"you are under your own control.\u8221" This formulation works for freeing a slave, but is it general enough to function as divorce language as well? The {\i Gemara} frames the question: does {\i le-atzmech} mean {\i legamrei} \u8212- fully and completely \u8212- such that it severs all connection including marriage, or does it mean only that he is releasing her from his rights to her labor, which would be relevant only to a slave?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Ravina raises a proof for Rav Ashi from the {\i mishna}: the essence of a {\i get shichrur} is either {\i harei at bat chorin} or {\i harei at le-atzmech}. The {\i Gemara} reasons: {\i uma avda dekani lei gufei} \u8212- by a slave, over whom the master owns the body entirely, if one says {\i harei at le-atzmech}, the slave acquires ownership of himself or herself. This demonstrates that the phrase means a full withdrawal of control, not merely a waiver of labor rights. {\i Isha d\u8217'lo kani lei gufei} \u8212- by a wife, over whom the husband\u8217's control is less comprehensive than that of a master over a slave \u8212- {\i kal va-chomer} that {\i harei at le-atzmech} constitutes a complete removal of ownership. The {\i Gemara} thus establishes that any formulation lacking the specific semantic content of \u8220"slave versus free person\u8221" \u8212- meaning one that expresses a full severance of ownership and control in general terms \u8212- can function for both manumission and divorce.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ein Li Esek\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Ravina then poses to Rav Ashi a further question: if a man says to his slave {\i ein li esek bach} \u8212- \u8220"I will have no dealings with you\u8221" \u8212- what is the status? This language sounds like a waiver of rights rather than a positive grant of freedom or a fundamental change of status. Rav Chanan, or according to another version Rav Chanin of Mechuza, brings a proof from a {\i baraita}: one who sells his slave to a non-Jew \u8212- the slave goes free, and the first master must still write a {\i get shichrur}. Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel qualifies: when is a second document needed? Only when the master had not already written a document at the time of sale. If he had written one, {\i zehu shichruro} \u8212- that earlier document serves as the writ of manumission. What was this document? Rav Sheshes explains: it was a document stating that should the slave ever escape from the non-Jew, {\i ein li esek bach} \u8212- \u8220"I waive all rights to you.\u8221" This establishes that {\i ein li esek bach} functions as a valid {\i shtar shichrur}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is a genuine {\i chiddush}, because the phrase \u8220"I will have nothing to do with you\u8221" does not on its face convey a positive transfer of self-ownership or a change of status \u8212- it sounds more like a unilateral withdrawal. Yet we find that it works for a slave. The natural follow-up question is whether it similarly works in a bill of divorce, and given the {\i Gemara}\u8217's reasoning that a language effective for freeing a slave \u8212- as long as it does not carry the specific meaning of slave-versus-free \u8212- is sufficient for divorce as well, the presumptive answer is yes.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Explicit vs Implicit\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now addresses the heart of the debate between Rabbi Yehuda and the {\i tanna kamma}. Rabbi Yehuda requires the {\i get} to state {\i v\u8217'dein di yehavi lichi minai sefer teiruchin v\u8217'igeret shivukin} \u8212- \u8220"and this shall be to you from me a book of divorce and a writ of release.\u8221" One might ask why any document should need to declare itself the instrument it constitutes \u8212- a standard {\i shtar mechira} simply says {\i sadi mechura lach} and is handed over; a {\i shtar kiddushin} says {\i harei at mekudeshet li}. Neither describes itself as the instrument of transfer. The {\i Gemara}, however, does not frame the debate this way.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbanan savri yadayim she-einan mochichos haviyan yadayim} \u8212- the Rabbis hold that even an implicit or non-explicit indication ({\i yad}) suffices. The document need not explicitly state that it is the instrument of divorce; it is sufficiently clear from context. {\i Mochiach milta de-be-hai gitta kama-garesh lah} \u8212- the fact that this document is being given as a {\i get} makes the matter apparent. Rabbi Yehuda, by contrast, holds {\i yadayim she-ein mochichos lav havyan yadayim} \u8212- an implicit indication that falls short of being fully evident does not qualify. Without the explicit {\i v\u8217'dein}, one might argue {\i be-dibura girsheha} \u8212- he is divorcing her verbally \u8212- {\i ve-shtara re\u8217'aya be-alma} \u8212- and the document is merely serving as evidence of that verbal act. Rabbi Yehuda thus requires both that the {\i get} make clear it is the instrument of divorce and that it do so explicitly. Both of these are genuine {\i chiddushim}, but the first \u8212- that a {\i get} must identify itself as the instrument at all \u8212- is arguably the more striking one.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Nusach & Letters\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Spelling Rules\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now turns to extreme precision in the drafting of the {\i get}. Abaye enumerates a series of spelling hazards. The word {\i v\u8217'dein} must be written without a {\i yod} \u8212- {\i v\u8217'den} \u8212- lest it be read as {\i v\u8217'din}, suggesting \u8220"it is the law\u8221" that the {\i get} be given, which is both inaccurate and misleading. The word for letter or writ must be written {\i ageret} without a {\i yod}, not {\i igeret} with one, lest it be misread as {\i igra} \u8212- a roof \u8212- yielding the absurd meaning \u8220"this should be to you a roof of divorce.\u8221" The word for \u8220"to go,\u8221" {\i le-meihach}, must be written with a {\i heh}, not {\i le-mihach} with a {\i chet}, lest it be read as {\i le-mihach} \u8212- \u8220"to mock\u8221" \u8212- turning the {\i get} into something of a Purim joke.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rashi Nusach\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rashi provides the complete standard {\i nusach ha-get}, which runs as follows. {\i Petirus ychi lichi} \u8212- I have freed you to yourself \u8212- {\i ant plonit bat ploni}, you, such-and-such daughter of such-and-such, {\i de-haveit inteti min kadmat de-na} \u8212- who were my wife prior to this \u8212- {\i ve-chadu} \u8212- and with this \u8212- {\i petirat u-shvakat ve-taricat ychi} \u8212- I have released and divorced you \u8212- {\i di te-haveyan rasha\u8217'a u-shalta\u8217'a be-nafshichi} \u8212- that you should be permitted and under your own authority \u8212- {\i le-meihach u-le-hitnasba le-chol man di tetzbayan} \u8212- to go and be married to whomever you desire \u8212- {\i ve-inash lo yimchei be-yadaichi} \u8212- and no man shall protest or stand in your way \u8212- {\i min yoma de-nan u-le-alam} \u8212- from this day forward \u8212- {\i ve-den di ye-havei lichi minai} \u8212- and this shall be to you from me \u8212- {\i get piturin ve-sefer teiruchin ve-igeret shevukin} \u8212- a bill of release, a book of divorce, and a writ of abandonment \u8212- {\i ke-dat Moshe ve-Yisrael}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One notable observation: Rashi\u8217's {\i nusach} does not explicitly include {\i harei at muteret le-chol adam}, even though the {\i mishna} identifies this as the core line of the {\i get}. The complete {\i nusach} as cited by both Rashi and the {\i Rif} is clearly the product of a received {\i mesora}, which is why Tosafot references the concept of a {\i tikun gittin} \u8212- an established authoritative text for the formulation of a {\i get}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} continues with a requirement that the {\i get} contain three {\i yudin} in the word {\i de-yihavyan} \u8212- \u8220"that you should be\u8221" \u8212- and three in {\i de-yitzbayan} \u8212- \u8220"that you should desire.\u8221" The reasoning is that with only two {\i yudin}, the phrase could be read impersonally \u8212- that women generally may desire or may be permitted \u8212- whereas three {\i yudin} make the language personal and direct, referring specifically to this woman. Tosafot adds that there must be five {\i yudin} in total: three at the end of {\i de-yihavyan} and one each before and after the {\i taf} of {\i de-yitzbayan}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafot Letters\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot raises several fine points regarding letter formation. The {\i vav} in {\i teiruchin} must be written long enough that it is not mistaken for a {\i yod}; if read as a {\i yod}, the word would become {\i terichin} \u8212- divorced women \u8212- rather than the abstract noun meaning \u8220"of divorce,\u8221" yielding the absurd phrase \u8220"a book of divorcees.\u8221" Similarly, the {\i vav} in {\i chadu} must be sufficiently elongated that it is not confused with a {\i yod}, which would transform {\i chadu} \u8212- \u8220"with this\u8221" \u8212- into {\i chadi}, meaning \u8220"for nothing\u8221" or \u8220"for naught.\u8221" The word {\i le-hitnasba} must be written with a {\i heh} rather than an {\i alef}, lest {\i l\u8217'itnasva} be misread as {\i lo yitnasva} \u8212- \u8220"she shall not marry\u8221" \u8212- precisely the opposite of the intended meaning. The {\i heh} in {\i le-meihach} must be written with its leg sufficiently separated from the roof that it does not look like a {\i chet}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot is careful to clarify what these requirements actually entail. The {\i vav} need not be dramatically elongated relative to other letters \u8212- it simply must not be so short as to resemble a {\i yod}. Similarly, the leg of the {\i heh} need not be made especially prominent; it must simply not be attached to the roof so as to resemble a {\i chet}. As for whether extreme elongation is required or merely preferred, Tosafot concludes that {\i vadai ein ha-get pasul im lo he\u8217'erikh} \u8212- certainly the {\i get} is not invalid if the letter was not given extra emphasis, as long as it does not actually look like the wrong letter.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This last point leads to a practical ruling. Tosafot records an actual incident: a {\i get} was brought before Rabbi Eliyahu in which it was unclear whether a particular letter was a {\i vav} or a {\i yod}. Rabbi Eliyahu employed the {\i tinok} test \u8212- bringing a child who is neither especially bright nor especially slow-witted to read the word, paralleling the procedure described in {\i Menachot} regarding letters in {\i tefillin}. If the child reads it correctly, the {\i get} is {\i kasher}; if not, it is {\i pasul}. This is the same standard applied to {\i sifrei Torah}, and it can raise real questions in the context of {\i gittin}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava Takana\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} then asks whether we rule like Rabbi Yehuda \u8212- whether the {\i v\u8217'dein} line is in fact required. The {\i Gemara} notes that Rava, in formulating his {\i takana} for the standard {\i nusach} of a {\i get}, stated: {\i eich planya bar planya pattar ve-tarich yat plonisa ittati de-havas ittati min kadmat de-na, min yoma den u-le-alam} \u8212- so-and-so releases and divorces so-and-so my wife who has been my wife until now, from this day forward \u8212- but did not include the {\i v\u8217'dein} line. This would suggest he did not rule like Rabbi Yehuda.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} pushes back: {\i u-le-ta\u8217'ameicha kulhu mi ka\u8217'amar?} \u8212- by your reasoning, did Rava say everything? That formulation was only one particular addition Rava was establishing; the absence of {\i v\u8217'dein} from his stated formulation cannot serve as proof that he excluded it, any more than the absence of {\i harei at muteret le-chol adam} means he excluded that. {\i Ela ba\u8217'inan} \u8212- we require the other parts as well even though he did not mention them, and {\i hachi nami ba\u8217'inan} \u8212- similarly, perhaps we also require {\i v\u8217'dein} and he simply did not mention it. The {\i Gemara}\u8217's conclusion, given the extraordinary attention to every letter and every word throughout this discussion, is that the {\i nusach} requires explicit inclusion of {\i v\u8217'dein de-yihavi lichi minai}, ruling in accordance with Rabbi Yehuda. Whether the absence of {\i v\u8217'dein} renders the {\i get} invalid {\i bedi\u8217'avad} is perhaps less clear, but {\i lechatchila} the standard demands full explicitness.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What Rava did explicitly insist upon, however, are two additions. First, {\i miyoma de-nan} \u8212- from this day \u8212- {\i la-apoukei mi-d\u8217'Rabbi Yosei}, to avoid relying on Rabbi Yosei\u8217's position that the date of the document implicitly establishes its effective date, making explicit retroactive conditions unnecessary. Rava required that the effective date be stated unambiguously. Second, {\i u-le-olam} \u8212- and forever \u8212- {\i la-apoukei mi-de-ba\u8217'ei minei Rava me-Rav Nachman}, in order to preempt the kind of question Rava once posed to Rav Nachman about whether one could be divorced only for a single day. Even though the {\i halacha} ultimately holds that such a conditional divorce does not work, Rava wanted the {\i get} to state explicitly that the divorce is permanent, so that no one could raise the claim that she was only divorced for that day.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Three Invalid Gittin\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Handwriting No Edim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} states: {\i shelosha gittin pesulim ve-im nisset ha-vlad kasher} \u8212- there are three invalid {\i gittin} such that nevertheless the children are not {\i mamzerim} if the woman remarried on the basis of them. The {\i Gemara} will discuss whether this {\i mishna} goes against Rabbi Meir \u8212- who holds that any deviation from the rabbinic standard of {\i gittin} renders the child a {\i mamzer} \u8212- or whether it represents Rabbi Meir\u8217's own concession that these particular cases, while invalid, do not produce a {\i mamzer}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is striking, because some of these defects seem far more serious than defects Rabbi Meir was willing to hold produce a {\i mamzer} in earlier contexts. Rabbi Meir had ruled that even dating a {\i get} according to the wrong foreign government or according to the {\i binyan beit ha-mikdash} renders the child a {\i mamzer} \u8212- extraordinarily exacting standards. Yet here, with defects that seem more substantive, the child will not be a {\i mamzer}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The first case: {\i katav bi-chtav yado ve-ein alav eidim} \u8212- it was written in the husband\u8217's own handwriting but has no witnesses. Whose position is this? The {\i mishna} ends by introducing Rabbi Eliezer with his {\i edei mesira kartei} position, which implies that the beginning of the {\i mishna} is not Rabbi Eliezer\u8217's view. Two possibilities are offered. The first \u8212- attributed to Tosafot \u8212- is that this reflects Rabbi Meir\u8217's view that {\i edei chasima kartei}, signatures are required; yet the husband\u8217's own handwriting constitutes a form of {\i eidus}, since {\i hoda\u8217'as ba\u8217'al din ke-me\u8217'ah eidim dami} \u8212- the admission of a litigant is equivalent to a hundred witnesses. One\u8217's own handwriting is one\u8217's own testimony. The second \u8212- attributed to Rashi \u8212- is a more radical suggestion: perhaps no {\i eidus} is required in a {\i get} at all. If written by the husband himself as his own direct expression of divorce, perhaps that suffices without any testimony. Rashi entertains this as a possibility, which is, as noted, quite shocking given that the necessity of {\i eidus} has been among the most stable assumptions of the entire tractate. The more natural reading, as Tosafot argues, is that one\u8217's own handwriting constitutes self-testimony.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 No Date/One Witness\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The second case: {\i yesh alav eidim ve-ein bo zman} \u8212- it has witnesses but no date. Dating was critical, as extensively discussed, to establish precisely when the {\i get} took effect. The third case: {\i yesh bo zman ve-ein bo ela eid echad} \u8212- it has a date but only one witness.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Regarding the third case, the {\i Gemara} presents a debate. Rav holds that {\i ktav yado shaninu} \u8212- what the {\i mishna} is discussing is the husband\u8217's own handwriting combined with one witness, not the scribe\u8217's handwriting with one witness. His reasoning: if a {\i get} is found in the street and a single witness attaches his name to it, one cannot know whether it is genuine. The scribe\u8217's handwriting alone carries no testimonial weight about the validity of the {\i get}. But if the husband himself wrote it in his own hand, that handwriting functions as his own testimony, and adding one witness creates a combination of his testimony and one external witness \u8212- sufficient {\i bedi\u8217'avad}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Shmuel disagrees: {\i afilu ktav sofer ve-eid} \u8212- even the scribe\u8217's handwriting combined with one witness is {\i kasher bedi\u8217'avad}, relying on a later {\i mishna} that validates such a combination. Shmuel understands that later {\i mishna} as dealing with the scribe\u8217's handwriting, and holds that the scribe\u8217's writing counts as one form of testimony plus one signature equals two.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav responds that the later {\i mishna} is not discussing {\i ktav sofer} \u8212- the scribe\u8217's handwriting \u8212- but {\i chatam sofer} \u8212- the scribe\u8217's signature alongside one other witness. The proof: that later {\i mishna} says the woman may remarry {\i lechatchila}, whereas in our {\i mishna} she may only do so {\i bedi\u8217'avad}. Clearly they discuss different cases. Rabbi Yochanan agrees with Rav\u8217's position that {\i ktav yadcha shaninu} \u8212- it is the husband\u8217's handwriting that is at issue in the third case of our {\i mishna}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Shmuel responds that there is no contradiction: {\i ha be-sofer mufhak ha be-sofer delo mufhak} \u8212- when the later {\i mishna} permits remarriage {\i lechatchila}, it refers to an expert scribe, {\i sofer mufhak}, whose writing of the {\i get} carries implicit testimonial weight because an expert scribe would not allow a {\i get} to leave his hands unless it was going to be properly used. A non-expert scribe might write a {\i get} for practice and discard it \u8212- his handwriting carries no such presumption. The scribe\u8217's handwriting combined with one witness therefore suffices {\i lechatchila} only when the scribe is {\i mufhak}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Positions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav\u8217's two positions can be reconciled as follows: {\i zimnin amar Rav tetzei} \u8212- sometimes Rav said that if a woman married on the basis of any of the three {\i gittin} in the {\i mishna}, she must be divorced \u8212- and {\i zimnin amar Rav lo tetzei} \u8212- sometimes he said she does not need to be divorced. The resolution is circumstantial: {\i yesh la banim lo tetzei} \u8212- if she has already had children, she does not need to be divorced, because forcing a divorce would cause people to declare the children {\i mamzerim}. {\i Ein la banim tetzei} \u8212- if she has not yet had children, she must obtain a proper {\i get}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tannaitic Proofs\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i baraita} is cited as a possible challenge to the position that she does not need to be divorced: all cases involving doubtful betrothal or doubtful divorce \u8212- {\i sefek kiddushin} or {\i sefek gerushin} \u8212- require {\i chalitzah} but not {\i yibbum}. The {\i baraita} specifies that the three {\i gittin} of our {\i mishna} constitute {\i sefek gerushin}. If she does not need to be divorced upon remarriage, the {\i Gemara} objects, then people will conclude she is fully divorced and a {\i yavam} may come to perform {\i yibbum} with her co-wife, thinking the marriage to the deceased brother\u8217's daughter was certainly severed. The {\i Gemara} deflects: {\i tityabem bein kach u-vein kach} \u8212- let him do {\i yibbum} either way; since {\i bedi\u8217'avad} the divorce is merely a rabbinic problem, the marriage was effectively severed and {\i yibbum} would be technically permissible. The concern is only a {\i chashasha de-rabbanan}, a rabbinic precaution.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} also examines whether these three {\i gittin} should be listed together with other problematic {\i gittin} such as {\i get yashan} \u8212- an old {\i get}, written before the couple later cohabited and then given afterwards \u8212- or {\i get kerei\u8217'ach} \u8212- the bald {\i get} in which not all of a folded {\i get}\u8217's folds were signed. Neither fits neatly with the three of our {\i mishna}. A {\i get yashan} is not listed because, according to the view that in our {\i mishna} she must be divorced, the {\i get yashan} is less weighty \u8212- once given, she may even remarry {\i lechatchila}. A {\i get kerei\u8217'ach} is not listed because there the child is a {\i mamzer}, whereas here the child is {\i kasher}. However, the {\i Gemara} notes a tension: if our {\i mishna} follows the {\i Rabbanan}, who never declare a child a {\i mamzer}, then the distinction from {\i get kerei\u8217'ach} must rest on whether {\i tetzei} applies. This works only for the opinion that in our {\i mishna} she does not need to be divorced; if she does need to be divorced here just as in {\i get kerei\u8217'ach}, then there is no basis to omit {\i get kerei\u8217'ach} from the list according to the {\i Rabbanan}. The {\i Gemara} is forced to concede: {\i okimta ke-Rabbi Meir} \u8212- one can explain the separate lists only by attributing the {\i mishna} to Rabbi Meir, who maintains two distinct categories, one in which the {\i vlad} is a {\i mamzer} and one in which it is not. By the {\i Rabbanan}, who never declare a {\i mamzer}, the lists should be combined, and no fully satisfying answer is given unless one holds that in our {\i mishna} she does not need to be divorced.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} also closes with Rabbi Eliezer\u8217's position: even a {\i get} that bears no witnesses at all is valid if it was given in the presence of witnesses \u8212- {\i edei mesira kartei} \u8212- and the woman may even collect from encumbered property on the basis of it, since the witnesses at the moment of delivery confer upon it the full legal weight of a witnessed document.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Remarriage & Divorce\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Levi states: {\i l\u8217'olam lo tetzei} \u8212- she never needs to be divorced, regardless of whether she has had children. Rabbi Yochanan agrees: {\i l\u8217'olam lo tetzei}. Rabbi Yochanan conveyed this also to the sons of Rav Chalafta of Minhuna \u8212- \u8220"this is what your father said: {\i l\u8217'olam lo tetzei}.\u8221" Once a woman has remarried on the basis of one of these three {\i gittin}, she does not need to be divorced, with or without children, {\i l\u8217'olam} meaning even absent children.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i daf} concludes with a brief appendix conveyed alongside this ruling \u8212- Rabbi Yochanan also told the sons of Rav Chalafta another ruling in their father\u8217's name: a certain type of locust ({\i kartzi}) that dwells among bundles of wheat does not invalidate the waters of a {\i parah adumah} if it drinks from them. The {\i Gemara} asks what a {\i kartzi} is, and identifies it as a small insect or flying grasshopper found between grain bundles. A challenge is raised from a {\i baraita} stating that all birds invalidate {\i mei chatat} if they drink from them, with the sole exception of the dove, which sucks in and does not spit back. If the {\i kartzi} were always valid, the {\i baraita} should have listed it alongside the dove. The {\i Gemara} resolves: the ruling is not absolute \u8212- {\i gedola lo pasla, ketana pasla} \u8212- a large one does not invalidate the water but a small one does. How small must it be to still pose a problem? Until the size of an olive, according to Rav Yirmiya, or according to another version, Rav Ami. This brief digression aside, the substantive {\i halacha} of the {\i daf} concludes with the ruling of Levi and Rabbi Yochanan: once a woman has remarried on the basis of any of the three {\i gittin} of our {\i mishna}, she does not need to be divorced \u8212- {\i l\u8217'olam lo tetzei}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Gittin 87\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Edei Mesira Krati\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Intro: Edei Mesira\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The daf opens in the middle of 86b, where the {\i mishnah} has been discussing three types of {\i gittin} that are {\i pasul} \u8212- those lacking full {\i eidus}. The cases involved either the husband\u8217's own handwriting, a single {\i eid}, or various combinations thereof. That discussion proceeded according to Rabbi Meir\u8217's position that {\i edei chatima krati} \u8212- the witnesses who sign the document are the essential ones \u8212- and raised questions about what counts as valid signatures. At the close of the {\i mishnah}, however, Rabbi Eliezer articulates a fundamentally different position: a {\i get} requires no signatures at all. As long as it was delivered in front of witnesses, it is valid {\i l\u8217'chatchila}. And Rabbi Eliezer extends this principle beyond {\i gittin} \u8212- even a {\i shtar chov}, a loan document, if given in front of witnesses without signatures, creates liens on encumbered property. His principle is {\i edei mesira krati}: the witnesses who observe the transfer are the essential ones, and their {\i eidus} effectively combines with the physical document to constitute it as a document of testimony.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gittin vs.\u160 ?Shtarot\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might have expected the {\i halacha} to follow Rabbi Eliezer more readily in monetary matters than in {\i gittin}. After all, {\i dinei mamonot} is a more flexible area of law \u8212- {\i hefker beis din hefker} and similar principles give the rabbis wider latitude \u8212- whereas {\i gittin} involves personal status, which typically demands stricter standards. Yet the {\i gemara} will demonstrate the opposite, and for a compelling reason. A {\i get} is an instrument that, once it meets its technical requirements, effects a change in status. A {\i shtar}, by contrast, creates liens partly because it constitutes a matter of public record \u8212- buyers of property need to know that liens may exist against what they are purchasing. There is therefore a pragmatic reason to demand more objectively accessible documentation in monetary matters than in {\i gittin}, where the instrument\u8217's function is more purely technical.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Halacha k\u8217'Rabbi Eliezer\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} opens with Rav Yehuda citing Rav: {\i halacha k\u8217'Rabbi Eliezer begittin} \u8212- we rule like Rabbi Eliezer for {\i gittin}, that witnesses of delivery without signatures suffice. When Rav Yehuda transmitted this ruling before Shmuel, Shmuel extended it: {\i af b\u8217'shtarot} \u8212- even by monetary documents. The {\i gemara} then asks whether Rav himself disagreed with Shmuel on this point, since the {\i mishnah} mentions collection from {\i nechasim meshubadim}, which implies that Rabbi Eliezer\u8217's position creates binding liens even in monetary matters. The {\i gemara} resolves this: Rabbi Eliezer himself said both \u8212- both for {\i gittin} and for {\i shtarot}. Nobody disputes what Rabbi Eliezer held. The question is only how we rule. Rav rules like him for {\i gittin} but not for {\i shtarot}; Shmuel rules like him in both domains.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Yannai\u8217's Challenge\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then cites Rabbi Yaakov bar Idi in the name of Rabbi Yehoshua ben Levi: {\i halacha k\u8217'Rabbi Eliezer begittin}. Rabbi Yannai, however, says: {\i afilu reiach haget ein bo} \u8212- a {\i get} without signatures does not even have the whiff of a {\i get}. A {\i get} lacking any {\i reiach haget} is so completely invalid that it does not even create the usual complications for {\i kehunah} that a questionably valid {\i get} would generate.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} immediately objects: does Rabbi Yannai not hold like Rabbi Eliezer? The answer is that of course he does. What Rabbi Yannai is saying is that {\i for the rabbanan} who disagree with Rabbi Eliezer \u8212- and that {\i rabbanan} is no small figure, being Rabbi Meir \u8212- the position is not merely that such a {\i get} is {\i pasul}, but that it is diametrically and categorically invalid. If you believe that a {\i shtar} is fundamentally a signed document, then a document delivered in front of witnesses but bearing no signatures is not a defective {\i get} \u8212- it is simply not a {\i get} at all. The analogy is apt: if someone handed you an unsigned check and told you two people had watched the transaction, you would rightly say that is not a check. That is Rabbi Meir\u8217's position \u8212- and Rabbi Yannai is articulating it faithfully, while personally ruling like Rabbi Eliezer.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} thus presents an extreme polarity: Rabbi Eliezer validates such a {\i get} entirely, while those who oppose him regard it as having no legal status whatsoever.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Amoraic Consensus\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} proceeds to accumulate a striking series of rulings all affirming {\i halacha k\u8217'Rabbi Eliezer begittin}. Rabbi Yochanan bar Rabbi Yannai cites Reish Lakish: {\i halacha k\u8217'Rabbi Eliezer begittin}. When Rabbi Yochanan\u8217's own name appears immediately after with the statement {\i afilu reiach haget ein bo}, the {\i gemara} again clarifies in the same way: Rabbi Yochanan is articulating the position of the {\i rabbanan} who oppose Rabbi Eliezer, not his own ruling.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then relates that Rabbi Abba bar Zavdi sent a message to Mari bar Mar: ask Rav Huna whether we rule like Rabbi Eliezer for {\i gittin} or not. Before the question could be put to him, Rav Huna died. His son Rava then reported: this is what my father said \u8212- {\i halacha k\u8217'Rabbi Eliezer begittin}. Some transmit this in the name of Rav directly. Additionally, {\i raboseinu habeki\u8217'im b\u8217'dvar halacha} \u8212- the rabbis who are expert in matters of law, identified as Rav Chama bar Guria citing Rav \u8212- rule the same way. And some say that not just those experts but the students of Rav, {\i mishum Rabbeinu}, transmitted the same ruling. Finally, when Ravin came from Eretz Yisrael, he reported that Rabbi Elazar said in the name of Rav: {\i halacha k\u8217'Rabbi Eliezer begittin}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The cumulative effect is remarkable. The {\i gemara} is thoroughly insistent that this is the unanimous ruling of the {\i Amoraim} \u8212- that we rule like Rabbi Eliezer for {\i gittin} \u8212- while simultaneously emphasizing, through the careful limitation {\i begittin}, that this ruling does not extend to monetary {\i shtarot}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Mixed-Up Gittin\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Setup: Two Identical\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} turns to a new {\i mishnah}: {\i shnayim she\u8217'shaltchu shnei gittin shavin} \u8212- two men sent two {\i gittin} that are identical, because by coincidence both husbands and both wives happened to share the same names. This raises a preliminary question not addressed by the {\i mishnah} directly \u8212- can a {\i get} be valid if it is not {\i mochiach mitocho}, not self-evidently identifiable as referring to one particular couple? If there are multiple men in a city named Reuven ben Yaakov married to women named Sarah bat Rachel, can the document serve as valid written testimony? This question bears more acutely on Rabbi Meir\u8217's position, where the document itself must function as written {\i eidus}, but the {\i mishnah} sets this aside and assumes the {\i gittin} are otherwise valid. The scenario is simply that both husbands gave their identical {\i gittin} to a single {\i shaliach}, who was to deliver them to the respective wives \u8212- and the {\i shaliach} mixed them up and no longer knows which belongs to which.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah}\u8217's solution is elegant: {\i notnin shnayhem l\u8217'zu v\u8217'shnayhem l\u8217'zu} \u8212- give both {\i gittin} to one wife and then both to the other. Each wife thereby receives the {\i get} that was written for her, even if neither she nor the {\i shaliach} knows which one it is. However, if one of the {\i gittin} is lost \u8212- {\i avad echad meihem, harei hasheni batel} \u8212- the remaining {\i get} cannot be used. Giving it to each wife individually would render each one a {\i safek gerushah}, a woman of uncertain divorced status, which is the worst possible outcome, and so the remaining document is simply invalidated.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Five Husbands, One G (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 One Date, Multiple Names\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} then introduces a distinct case: {\i chamisha shekatvu klal b\u8217'toch haget} \u8212- five husbands who incorporated all their names within a single {\i get}, listing each man and his wife: {\i ish ploni megaresh plonit, v\u8217'ploni plonit}, and so on, with the witnesses signing below. The ruling is {\i kulam kesherim} \u8212- they are all valid \u8212- and the {\i get} is given to each wife in turn.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 However, if {\i haya chosev tofes l\u8217'chol echad v\u8217'echad} \u8212- if there was a separate text for each individual \u8212- and the witnesses sign below, then only the one most proximate to the witnesses is valid: {\i es she\u8217'ha\u8217'eidim niktamim imo kasher}. The question that will occupy the {\i gemara} is: what exactly distinguishes a single unified statement, for which the witnesses\u8217' signatures validate the whole, from multiple separate statements, for which the signatures apply only to the bottommost?\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Mixed-Up Gittin (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Why Problematic?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} asks: {\i man tana} \u8212- according to which {\i tanna} is the case of the two mixed-up {\i gittin} a problem? Rabbi Yirmeyah answers: {\i d\u8217'lo k\u8217'Rabbi Eliezer} \u8212- it is not according to Rabbi Eliezer. This is initially surprising, because one might have expected the opposite: if the {\i get} is ambiguous as to which person it refers to, that seems more problematic under Rabbi Meir, for whom the document must constitute clear written {\i eidus}. Yet the {\i gemara} argues that it is specifically Rabbi Eliezer\u8217's position that creates the difficulty.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Eliezer holds that {\i edei mesira krati} \u8212- the witnesses who observe the delivery are the essential ones; their testimony combines with the physical document and makes it an instrument of {\i eidus}. For this to work, there is a logical requirement: the witnesses must be able to know which document is doing the divorcing. If two identical {\i gittin} are handed over together and neither the witnesses nor anyone else knows which one was written for this particular woman, how can the witnesses\u8217' testimony be said to combine with {\i this} document? The witnesses observed a delivery, but they cannot attest that {\i this} document is the one that was written for {\i this} woman. Their {\i eidus} would need to attach specifically to a document that they can identify as the operative one.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} articulates this precisely: the witnesses did not know that {\i this} document \u8212- as opposed to the other one \u8212- was the one written for this woman. And since Rabbi Eliezer\u8217's entire mechanism requires that the witnessing of the delivery combine with the specific document that was designated for her, the uncertainty about which document is which is fatal to his framework. For Rabbi Meir, by contrast, the witnesses signed the document at the time of writing, when everything was clear. The signing was done {\i lishmah}, with the correct parties in mind, producing a complete and valid instrument. Whatever happens at the time of delivery \u8212- even if the {\i shaliach} is uncertain about which {\i get} to hand over \u8212- is irrelevant to the document\u8217's validity, because the {\i eidus} is already embedded in it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rashi} approaches this from the verse {\i v\u8217'chatav lah sefer keritut} \u8212- for Rabbi Meir, the word {\i v\u8217'chatav} refers to the signing, which was performed {\i lishmah} and completed the instrument. For Rabbi Eliezer, the {\i v\u8217'natanah b\u8217'yadah} \u8212- the delivery \u8212- also bears {\i halachic} weight, and one might argue it too must be {\i lishmah}. But the deeper logic is structural: if it is the act of witnessed delivery that constitutes the document as testimony, then the witnesses\u8217' awareness of what they are witnessing \u8212- specifically, {\i which} document is being activated \u8212- is an essential component of that act.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Abaye\u8217's Answer\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye rejects this analysis: {\i afilu teima Rabbi Eliezer} \u8212- the {\i mishnah} can be understood even according to Rabbi Eliezer. {\i Ketivah lishmah, netinah lishmah mi bei?} \u8212- writing must be {\i lishmah}, but does delivery require {\i lishmah} as a separate condition? Abaye maintains that the requirement is simply that it be delivered {\i as a get} \u8212- not, say, as a {\i shtar chov} \u8212- and the witnesses\u8217' uncertainty as to which document was written for this particular woman does not undermine their testimony about the delivery itself. The document was written correctly and {\i lishmah} at the time of writing; the fact that the witnesses at delivery cannot distinguish between two identical {\i gittin} does not invalidate either one.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Five Husbands, One G (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yochanan: Zman Echad\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now returns to the five-husbands {\i mishnah} and asks {\i heichi dami klal} \u8212- what constitutes a unified statement for which the witnesses validate all five {\i gittin}? And {\i heichi dami tofes} \u8212- what constitutes separate statements for which the witnesses apply only to the last?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yochanan answers: {\i zman echad l\u8217'kullan zehu klal} \u8212- a single date for all of them makes it one unified statement. {\i Zman l\u8217'chol echad v\u8217'echad zehu tofes} \u8212- a separate date for each makes them individual statements. On Rabbi Yochanan\u8217's view, the key variable is the date. If the entire document bears one date at the outset \u8212- \u8220"on the first of the week, I, Reuven ben Yaakov, divorce my wife\u8230 ? and I, Yaakov ben Yitzchak, divorce my wife\u8230 ?\u8221" \u8212- the shared date pulls the entire document together as one continuous statement, and the witnesses at the bottom validate everything. But if each section bears its own date \u8212- even if all the dates are identical \u8212- the repetition of the date marker makes each section self-contained, and the witnesses apply only to the bottommost statement.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The underlying logic is that a separate date makes a statement {\i self-referential} and therefore independent. Without its own date, a statement must look back to the single date at the top, and that shared reference point unifies the whole document. With its own date, each statement stands alone.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Lakish: Klal Needed\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Reish Lakish goes further: {\i afilu zman echad l\u8217'kulam nami avei tofes} \u8212- even with a single date for all, the result is still multiple separate statements. On Reish Lakish\u8217's view, the mere sequence of individual declarations \u8212- even connected by conjunctions \u8212- does not by itself constitute one unified statement. Each declaration \u8220"I divorce my wife\u8221" is an independent legal act; stringing them together with {\i vav}s does not make them one.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What then constitutes the {\i klal} \u8212- the unified statement \u8212- according to Reish Lakish? {\i D\u8217'kasvu anu ploni u\u8217'ploni gerashnu nishoseinu plonit u\u8217'plonit} \u8212- you would need to begin with a collective declaration: \u8220"We, Reuven and Shimon and Levi and Yehuda, divorce our wives\u8230 ?\u8221" That opening collective statement \u8212- {\i anu} \u8212- is what transforms the document into a single unified act, allowing the witnesses\u8217' signatures at the bottom to validate everything that follows.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But there is an immediate difficulty: if everything is so tightly bound into one statement that the witnesses validate the whole, doesn\u8217't that mean one {\i get} is divorcing multiple women? And the Torah says {\i v\u8217'chatav lah} \u8212- {\i lah v\u8217'lo l\u8217'chavertah} \u8212- a {\i get} must be for {\i her}, not for her and her friend simultaneously. The {\i gemara} resolves this for Reish Lakish: after the collective opening, you then write out individual statements \u8212- {\i ploni geiraish plonit, v\u8217'ploni geiraish plonit} \u8212- spelling out each husband\u8217's divorce separately. The collective opening creates the unity that allows the witnesses to cover everything, while the subsequent individual statements preserve the separateness of each {\i get} for each woman.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Klal vs.\u160 ?Tofes\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Abba challenges Rabbi Yochanan\u8217's position. Rabbi Yochanan holds that the conjunction \u8212- the {\i vav} connecting the individual declarations under a single date \u8212- is sufficient to make everything one statement. But why should that be so? Perhaps the witnesses signed only on the final, most proximate statement. The {\i gemara} supports this concern by citing a {\i baraita}: {\i edim she-chotmin al she\u8217'elat shalom b\u8217'get pasul} \u8212- if witnesses sign at the bottom of a {\i get} that concludes with a social pleasantry (\u8220"and please send my regards to so-and-so\u8221"), the {\i get} is invalid, because we suspect the witnesses may have signed only on that closing pleasantry rather than on the {\i get} itself.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This principle establishes that witnesses\u8217' signatures are presumed to apply only to what is most immediately proximate to them. If an interpolated phrase \u8212- even a formulaic one \u8212- can sever the witnesses from the {\i get}, then surely separate individual declarations should do the same.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Abba then clarifies that he had the position of Rabbi Yochanan explained to him directly. The case of {\i she\u8217'alat shalom} is {\i pasul} because the final line reads \u8220"and ask after the welfare of so-and-so\u8221" \u8212- a separate sentence that the witnesses could plausibly have been signing. But if the closing reads \u8220"and you should ask\u8221" \u8212- {\i v\u8217'sha\u8217'alu} \u8212- then it is {\i kasher}, because the conjunction makes it part of the same sentence as the {\i get} itself. Similarly, for Rabbi Yochanan, individual declarations joined by {\i vav} \u8212- \u8220"and I, Yaakov, divorce my wife\u8230 ? and I, Shimon, divorce my wife\u8230 ?\u8221" \u8212- are one run-on statement. The {\i ands} make it all one sentence, and the witnesses at the bottom validate the whole.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 However, the {\i gemara} raises a further difficulty with Rabbi Yochanan\u8217's position. If the declarations are joined by {\i vav} but each bears its own date \u8212- even the identical date \u8212- does the redundant dating create a problem? The {\i gemara} observes that having a repeated date entry is even more self-contained than simply having a new sentence, because the date marker signals a fresh, independent statement. Moreover, if different dates were used, the earlier sections would be invalid on entirely separate grounds: a {\i get} signed on a different day from the one on which it was dated is {\i pasul}, because it was effectively signed on a day other than its stated date. The {\i gemara} resolves this by noting that in Rabbi Yochanan\u8217's case, all the dates are the same \u8212- the same day is simply repeated for each line. The repetition itself, even of the same date, is what signals that each is a self-contained statement \u8212- which is all the more reason it would function as a {\i tofes} even within Rabbi Yochanan\u8217's framework.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Ravina asks Rav Ashi: according to Reish Lakish, who says that even a single date is insufficient to create a {\i klal} \u8212- and that what is needed is the collective opening statement \u8212- what would a {\i klal} look like in practice? The {\i gemara} answers: {\i anu ploni u\u8217'ploni gerashnu nishoseinu plonit u\u8217'plonit} \u8212- the collective \u8220"we\u8221" followed by all the names and their wives. But this immediately generates the problem already noted: {\i nimtze\u8217'u shtei nashim misgarshos b\u8217'get echad} \u8212- two women are being divorced by one {\i get}, which violates {\i v\u8217'chatav lah v\u8217'lo l\u8217'chavertah}. Hence Reish Lakish\u8217's requirement that the collective opening be followed by individual statements for each husband.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} considers whether this is analogous to the case of a master who gives all his property to two slaves, which would free both of them, since each owns half of the other as part of the property. Might that case demonstrate that one document can effect a legal change for two people? The {\i gemara} dismisses this: {\i v\u8217'okimna bishnei shtarot} \u8212- that case was long since explained as involving two separate documents, one given to each slave. One document for one slave and another for the other; the combined effect is joint ownership and mutual freedom. One {\i shtar} for two people remains impermissible. The conclusion stands: you cannot have one {\i get} for two women, and the collective statement in Reish Lakish\u8217's model must be accompanied by individual declarations.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Baraita Consensus\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} confirms both positions with {\i tannaic} support. In favor of Rabbi Yochanan: if five men write {\i ish ploni megaresh plonit v\u8217'ploni plonit} \u8212- each name connected by {\i vav} with a single date \u8212- and the witnesses sign below, {\i kulam kesherim}, and the {\i get} is given to each wife. If each section has its own date, the witnesses apply only to the final section. This is precisely Rabbi Yochanan\u8217's framework.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 In favor of Reish Lakish: if five men write the collective {\i anu ploni u\u8217'ploni gerashnu nishoseinu}, followed by the individual {\i ploni geiraish plonit}, with a single date and witnesses below \u8212- {\i kulam kesherim}. If each individual section has its own date and its own line-space, the witnesses apply only to the final section. This is precisely Reish Lakish\u8217's framework.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 ben Beteira: Revach\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yehuda ben Beteira then weighs in with a position more lenient than anything encountered so far. He says: {\i im yesh revach beneihem pasul, v\u8217'im lav kasher} \u8212- if there is a blank line-space between the sections, they are {\i pasul} as separate units; if there is no such space, they are {\i kasher} as one unit. Crucially: {\i she\u8217'ein zman mafsikan} \u8212- a separate date is not sufficient to create separation. Only a physical gap in the text \u8212- a blank line \u8212- constitutes a break.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 On Rabbi Yehuda ben Beteira\u8217's view, the natural reading of a continuous text is that it is all one document, and a separate date-entry alone does not sever that continuity. One needs an actual physical interruption \u8212- a {\i revach} \u8212- to signal that what follows is a distinct and independent statement.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then raises a sharp internal question against Reish Lakish\u8217's supporting {\i baraita}: Reish Lakish requires a collective statement to create a unified {\i klal}. But if that collective statement is what makes the whole thing one, why does the {\i baraita} then say that you can break it back into separate units by means of separate dates and line-spaces? If you needed the collective to unify them, the simple absence of the collective should be sufficient to produce separate units \u8212- why the need for dates and {\i revach} as well?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} answers: the two cases are distinct. Where no collective statement was ever made, the individual declarations are simply multiple separate units from the outset, and no further mechanism is needed to establish their separateness. But where a collective statement {\i was} made at the outset \u8212- {\i anu ploni u\u8217'ploni gerashnu} \u8212- that collective creates a presumption of unity. To overcome that presumption and re-establish the separateness of the individual sections, something affirmative is needed: {\i mipalgu zman u-shita} \u8212- a separate date-entry and a line-space for each section. The combined force of these two elements can overcome the unifying effect of the initial collective statement. Without both, the collective statement continues to bind the whole document together.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Parallel Columns\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Setup: Hebrew, Greek\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The final {\i mishnah} of the daf introduces another scenario: {\i shnei gittin shek\u8217'tavam zeh b\u8217'tzad zeh} \u8212- two {\i gittin} written side by side in parallel columns on one piece of paper. Below them are four signatures in total: two Hebrew witnesses and two Greek witnesses. The question is which signatures belong to which {\i get}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Language & Direction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The key to the {\i mishnah}\u8217's ruling lies in the direction in which each script is written. Hebrew runs from right to left, so a Hebrew signature that begins at the right will be aligned with \u8212- and is presumed to belong to \u8212- the right-hand {\i get}. Greek runs from left to right, so a Greek signature that begins at the left will be aligned with and presumed to belong to the left-hand {\i get}. If the two Hebrew signatures appear directly beneath the two {\i gittin} \u8212- spanning both columns \u8212- and the two Greek signatures appear below them, the Greek witnesses are two lines removed from the {\i get} on the right. In that configuration, only the right-hand {\i get} is valid: the Hebrew witnesses are proximate to it, and the Greek witnesses, though displaced, are aligned with and proximate to the right-hand column as well by virtue of where they begin. The {\i mishnah}\u8217's underlying presumption is that signatures must be immediately proximate \u8212- directly beneath \u8212- the {\i get} they are validating.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Alternating Pattern\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} then presents the case where the signatures alternate: one Hebrew witness, one Greek witness, one Hebrew witness, one Greek witness. In this arrangement, {\i sheneihem pesulim} \u8212- both {\i gittin} are invalid. No two signatures for the same {\i get} are proximate to one another, and there is a {\i hefsek} \u8212- an interruption \u8212- between any pairing. The {\i mishnah} thus assumes that valid {\i eidus} on a {\i get} requires the two witnesses\u8217' signatures to be proximate both to the document and to each other.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Proximity Problem\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} asks a pointed question about the case of two Hebrew witnesses followed by two Greek witnesses: why can\u8217't we simply let both sets of witnesses validate both {\i gittin}? After all, we know from a prior ruling that a {\i get} written in one language with witnesses who signed in another is valid \u8212- language itself is not a barrier. If so, why not say the Hebrew witnesses validate the right {\i get} and the Greek witnesses, even though displaced by two lines, validate the left {\i get}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} cites Chizkiyah to address this: {\i mil\u8217'o b\u8217'krovim kasher} \u8212- if the gap between a {\i get} and its witnesses was filled in with additional text, even signatures of relatives (who are {\i pasul} as witnesses but whose names in writing fill the space), the {\i get} is valid. Chizkiyah\u8217's ruling establishes that proximity is about continuity: a blank gap breaks the connection between document and witness; filled space \u8212- even with legally useless text \u8212- maintains it. But if filled space suffices to maintain connection, why can\u8217't the Hebrew signatures in the middle, which are themselves writing between the top {\i get} and the bottom Greek signatures, serve as the filling that keeps the Greek signatures proximate?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chizkiyah: Fill Gaps\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Chizkiyah\u8217's principle is that a gap is problematic because it visually and legally detaches the witnesses from the document they are meant to validate. Once the gap is filled \u8212- by any writing, even non-{\i kasher} signatures \u8212- the document runs continuously from beginning to witness, and the connection is maintained. The implication for our case is that the Hebrew signatures physically separating the {\i gittin} from the Greek signatures might constitute just such a fill \u8212- maintaining continuity rather than severing it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ziri: Both Valid\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Ziri} has a variant text of the {\i mishnah} itself. According to Ziri, in the case of two Hebrew witnesses followed by two Greek witnesses, {\i shnayim kesherim} \u8212- both {\i gittin} are valid. On Ziri\u8217's reading, there is no requirement of strict proximity; each set of witnesses is associated with its respective {\i get} regardless of the gap, and both {\i gittin} are therefore fully valid.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Our Tanna\u8217's View\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Our {\i tanna}, however, maintains that only the proximate {\i get} is valid and not the other. The {\i gemara} explains our {\i tanna}\u8217's concern: {\i dilma gundali chasam} \u8212- perhaps the signatures were written in an unusual orientation. {\i Rashi} explains {\i gundali} as referring to some strange or reversed manner of signing. The possibility is that even the Greek signatures, written in some aberrant fashion, may be oriented toward the right-hand {\i get} rather than the left \u8212- meaning all four signatures could be attaching to the same {\i get}, leaving the other without valid witnesses at all. Because we cannot rule out this possibility, we cannot confidently say which {\i get} has two valid witnesses and which does not, and therefore only the {\i get} that is certainly proximate to identifiable signatures is valid.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The same logic applies to the alternating case. One might suggest that the first Hebrew witness and the first Greek witness together constitute two witnesses for the right {\i get}, and the second Hebrew and second Greek together constitute two witnesses for the left {\i get}. The {\i gemara} notes that Ziri again says {\i shnayim kesherim} \u8212- both are valid. Our {\i tanna}, however, again raises the concern that one of the signatures might be written in an unusual direction \u8212- {\i gundali} \u8212- such that instead of the alternating pattern yielding one witness per document, three signatures might be attaching to one {\i get} and only one to the other. Since we cannot determine which {\i get} has two valid witnesses and which has only one, neither can be definitively validated.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The fundamental principle underlying our {\i tanna}\u8217's position, then, is that a signature must be unambiguously aligned with \u8212- visually, directionally, and proximately \u8212- the {\i get} it is meant to validate. Ambiguity in any of these dimensions, whether caused by physical gaps or uncertain orientation, is sufficient to undermine the validity of the {\i get}. The {\i gemara} will continue with the next {\i mishnah} in the following session.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Gittin 88\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Two-Column Get\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two Columns, Signatures\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} before us \u8212- located on 88b, roughly in the middle of the page \u8212- opens with a case that is in many ways a continuation of the previous day\u8217's discussion, albeit the simpler version of it. The case is: {\i shi\u8217'er miktzat haget uchtavo bedaf hasheni vehayedim milmata kasher} \u8212- he wrote part of the {\i get} in one column and the remainder in a second column, and the witnesses signed at the bottom of the second column. Here {\i daf} carries the sense of {\i yeri\u8217'ah}, a column, as in a column of a Torah scroll. The ruling is {\i kasher}: the witnesses sign at the conclusion of the {\i get} after the entire text has been written across both columns, and that is sufficient.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Signatures Top/Side\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} then presents variations on signature placement. {\i Chatmu ha-eidim berosh ha-daf} \u8212- if the witnesses signed at the top of the second column rather than the bottom \u8212- or {\i min ha-tzad}, at the side, meaning in the margins of the document rather than at its conclusion \u8212- these are problematic. The intuition is straightforward: a contract is signed at its end. Signing in the margins or at the top does not follow the expected structure of a legally executed document.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Signing on Back\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A third case: {\i o me\u8217'achorav beget pashut pasul} \u8212- if they signed on the back of a plain {\i get}. This is distinguished from a {\i get mekushar}, the folded-style {\i get} treated in earlier passages where signing on particular external sections was the correct practice. For a standard {\i get}, signing on the back is {\i pasul}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Heads Touching Invalid\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} then introduces cases requiring illustration, and those following along without a visual aid will need to hold the picture in mind. The first case: {\i hikif rosho shel zeh betzad rosho shel zeh veha-eidim ba-emtza sheneihem pesulin} \u8212- imagine two {\i gittin} placed so that the top ({\i rosh}) of one faces the top of the other, with the witnesses signing in the middle between them. Both {\i gittin} are invalid. The reason, as becomes clear, is that the witnesses appear at the head of one of the documents rather than at its conclusion.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tails Touching Valid\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The next case: {\i sofo shel zeh betzad sofo shel zeh veha-eidim ba-emtza} \u8212- the tail ({\i sof}) of one {\i get} faces the tail of the other, with the witnesses between them. The ruling is {\i et she-nikra\u8217'im imo kasher} \u8212- the one that can be read together with the witnesses is valid. Visually: if {\i get} one is written normally and the witnesses appear at its bottom, and {\i get} two is written upside-down below the witnesses, then {\i get} one\u8217's witnesses are properly placed at its conclusion, and it is valid. The second {\i get}, read from the other direction, has the witnesses at its head \u8212- which is invalid.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Two-Column Get (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Head-Tail Adjacent\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The final configuration: {\i rosho shel zeh betzad sofo shel zeh} \u8212- the head of one {\i get} is adjacent to the tail of another, with a single set of witnesses. Again, the ruling is {\i et she-nikra\u8217'im imo basof kasher} \u8212- the one for which the witnesses appear at the end is valid, and the other is not, for the same reason. In each of these cases, the governing principle is that witnesses must appear at the conclusion of the document they are signing.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Hebrew-Greek Mix\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 One Get, Two Languages\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} continues with a series of cases involving language. {\i Get she-ketavo Ivrit ve-chatamo Yevanit, o Yevanit ve-chatamo Ivrit, ed echad Ivri ve-ed echad Yevanit} \u8212- a single {\i get} written in Hebrew and signed in Greek, or the reverse, or one witness signing in Hebrew and the other in Greek \u8212- all of these are valid. This is the simple version of what was treated in more complex form in the previous day\u8217's material: so long as it is a single {\i get}, the mixing of languages and scripts presents no problem.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Scribe + Witness\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Then: {\i katav sofer ve-ed kasher} \u8212- the scribe wrote and signed, along with one additional witness. As the {\i gemara} clarifies, {\i katav} here really means {\i chatam} \u8212- the scribe himself signed the document in addition to writing it. This is not a case of only one witness; rather, the scribe\u8217's signature counts as one of the two required signatures.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} identifies this ruling as the position of Rabbi Yossi: {\i ve-amar Rav Chisda man Rav Yossi hi}. The underlying logic relates to the rule that a {\i shliach} may not deviate from the husband\u8217's instructions. If a {\i shliach} could alter arrangements, one would worry that the scribe, having written the entire {\i get}, might be permitted to sign out of courtesy \u8212- even though the husband never designated him as a witness \u8212- and that a proper witness might then be displaced. But if a {\i shliach} cannot change what the husband specified, then the only way the scribe could be signing is if the husband himself, speaking directly, authorized it. Under those circumstances, the scribe\u8217's signature is entirely legitimate.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Witness Name Variations\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Name Forms Valid\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} turns to different forms of witness identification. {\i Ish ploni ed kasher} \u8212- if a witness signed simply using his first name without his father\u8217's name, this is valid. {\i Ben ish ploni ed} \u8212- if he signed only as \u8220"son of so-and-so,\u8221" giving his father\u8217's name but not his own first name, this too is valid. {\i Ish ploni ben ish ploni} without writing {\i ed} at all \u8212- simply his full name without the word \u8220"witness\u8221" \u8212- is also {\i kasher}, since it is self-evident why one would sign a {\i get} if not as a witness.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Jerusalem Practice\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i V\u8217'kach hayu nikkei ha-da\u8217'at she-bi-Yerushalayim osim} \u8212- this was the practice of the scrupulous and refined people of Jerusalem. The connection to the next clause, {\i katav chanichato va-chanichato kasher} \u8212- if one wrote a nickname or family surname \u8212- may indicate that what the people of Jerusalem did was to sign using family names, emphasizing the family lineage rather than drawing attention to themselves individually. Either way, the ruling stands: all of these forms of identification are valid.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Get Continuity Issues\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 One or Two Gets?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 With the basic cases of the two-column {\i get} established, the {\i gemara} raises a pointed question. How do we know that two columns constitute a single {\i get} rather than two separate {\i gittin}? The concern is concrete: perhaps someone cut part of one {\i get} and part of another and assembled them together, so that what appears to be a continuous document is actually a patchwork of two different instruments.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava: Space Below\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Amar Rava ke-she-yihye revach milmata} \u8212- Rava answers that there is a visible space at the bottom of the first column, indicating that the text continues and the column simply ran out of room. This space signals continuity \u8212- the document picks up where it left off.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Cut and Paste Risk\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} immediately pushes back: {\i u-dilma zman d\u8217'batra migaz gayiz} \u8212- perhaps someone cut off the date from the second column. The space below is still there, but the authenticity of the connection between the two columns remains in doubt.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Papa: Space Above\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Amar Rav Papa ke-she-yihye revach milmata, hacha nami ke-she-yihye revach mil\u8217'mala} \u8212- Rav Papa adds a parallel answer: just as there is a space below the first column, so too there is a space above the second column, indicating it begins mid-text. Both columns show visible evidence of continuation. For both spaces to be coincidentally present in two fraudulently combined documents would be extremely improbable.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Dating Problem\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Yet another concern is raised: {\i u-dilma imlochi imlach ve-katav} \u8212- perhaps the husband changed his mind mid-composition, and the {\i get} was written across two different days, completing the first column on one day and the second on another. This would create a problem of {\i get yashan} \u8212- an old {\i get} \u8212- where there is a meaningful gap between the date written on the {\i get} and when it was actually completed, with all the attendant difficulties: the wife might be considered divorced from the earlier date, potentially creating issues around her subsequent conduct, or questions about property rights. The {\i gemara} entertains this concern but then asks: if the text ended in the middle of a sentence \u8212- say, the first column ended with {\i harei at} and the second began with {\i muteret le-chol adam} \u8212- is one really to worry that the husband changed his mind mid-sentence and resumed writing months later? The {\i gemara} acknowledges that such a scenario would be far-fetched.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Parchment Proof\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rashi offers a more fundamental answer: {\i de-yada bei mi-tachtah de-megilta} \u8212- one can tell from the physical characteristics of the parchment itself. In antiquity, writing surfaces such as {\i klaf} or vellum were individually prepared, and each piece had its own distinctive texture and grain, much as one can tell from the grain of wood that a plank has not been joined from two separate pieces. If both columns share the same physical characteristics \u8212- the same coloration, the same grain, the same aging \u8212- it is evident that they constitute a single original document and are not a cut-and-paste fabrication. Rashi\u8217's answer thus addresses the foundational concern: the very materiality of the document testifies to its integrity.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Signature Orientation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Top/Side Signing Invalid\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Returning to the case of witnesses signing at the top of the page or to the side: the {\i mishna} ruled this invalid. The {\i gemara} expresses surprise \u8212- {\i eini}? Is that really so?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav\u8217's Practice\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Ve-ha Rav chatam min ha-tzad} \u8212- but Rav himself used to sign from the side! If signing to the side were categorically invalid, how could Rav have done so?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Letter Roof Orientation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} resolves the question: {\i de-hatam bi-shgago kelapei ha-ktav} \u8212- the roof ({\i shigago}, meaning the top edge) of Rav\u8217's letters faced toward the text of the {\i get}. That is, Rav signed sideways, but oriented so that the heads of the letters pointed inward toward the document. The {\i mishna}\u8217's invalidation applies to the opposite orientation \u8212- when the letters face away from the text. There is thus a meaningful distinction between a signature that is laterally placed but directed toward the document versus one that faces outward.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Vertical vs Horizontal\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This resolution, however, raises a further question from a {\i baraita}: the case of {\i hikif rosho shel zeh betzad rosho shel zeh ve-ha-eidim ba-emtza sheneihem pesulin} \u8212- heads facing heads, witnesses in the middle, both invalid. In that case, the roof of the letters of the witnesses\u8217' signatures does face the text of one of the {\i gittin}. If facing inward is sufficient to validate the signature, why are both {\i gittin} invalid? The {\i gemara} answers: {\i hatam d\u8217'dami lei ke-ivura} \u8212- in the case of the {\i mishna}\u8217's invalidation, the problem is not the orientation of the letters but that the signatures were written vertically, running up and down the page rather than across it horizontally. Written that way, the signature is ambiguous \u8212- one cannot determine which {\i get} it belongs to, since it is perpendicular to both. Hence: {\i lo adin nikarim imo} \u8212- neither document can clearly claim the witnesses as its own.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Conclusion: Rav Not Signing Gets\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having exhausted attempts to harmonize Rav\u8217's practice with the cases involving multiple documents, the {\i gemara} concludes: {\i ela Rav be-duchti hava chatim} \u8212- Rav was signing in a different context entirely. He was signing court summonses or judicial orders, not {\i gittin}. His lateral signature was an authorization or approval on a court document, not the witnessing signature on a {\i get}. The {\i mishna}\u8217's rules about signature placement apply to {\i gittin} specifically; Rav\u8217's practice was in an entirely separate domain and poses no challenge. Rashi notes that the term for such documents is something like a summons \u8212- an order confirming that a party may be summoned before the court \u8212- and that Rav\u8217's signature was simply his judicial approval, not a witnessing act governed by the laws of {\i get} signatures.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Language Variants\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Language Variants OK\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} briefly revisits the {\i mishna}\u8217's cases of language variation. A {\i get} written in one language and signed in another \u8212- Hebrew written, Greek signed, or the reverse \u8212- is valid in all its combinations. These rulings require little elaboration, as the principle is clear: for a single {\i get}, the language of composition and the language of signature are independent of one another.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Scribe Signs\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The case of {\i katav sofer ve-ed kasher} \u8212- the scribe who also signed \u8212- is now taken up in the {\i gemara}\u8217's own voice. {\i Amar Rabbi Yirmiyah: chatam sofer u-shnei eidim} \u8212- Rabbi Yirmiyah specifies that this means the scribe signed and there are additionally two other witnesses, totaling two signatures alongside the scribe\u8217's.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Direct Speech Requirement\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Ve-amar Rav Chisda: man Rav Yossi hi} \u8212- Rav Chisda identifies this as the position of Rav Yossi. The scribe is permitted to sign only if the husband spoke directly to him \u8212- directly commissioning the {\i get} and thereby implicitly authorizing the scribe\u8217's participation as a signatory. If the husband\u8217's instruction reached the scribe through intermediaries, the concern returns: perhaps the scribe was added as a signatory by those intermediaries without the husband\u8217's explicit authorization, which is impermissible under Rav Yossi\u8217's view that a {\i shliach} may not unilaterally alter the arrangements specified by the husband.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Ketubah Recognition\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Recognizing One Witness\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A practical case is now introduced. A {\i ketubah} came before Rabbi Abbahu, and those present recognized the handwriting of the scribe who had written it and the signature of one of the witnesses. The question arose: since the scribe\u8217's handwriting and one witness\u8217's signature were both recognizable, could the {\i ketubah} be validated on that basis alone, without verifying the second witness\u8217's signature? Rabbi Abbahu was inclined to validate it \u8212- {\i savar Rabbi Abbahu le-achshurah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two Signatures Required\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Abba objected: {\i lav chatam sofer u-shnei eidim tnan} \u8212- did we not learn in the {\i mishna} that the operative case is the scribe\u8217's signature plus two additional witnesses? The {\i mishna}\u8217's permission applies when the scribe himself is a signatory and there are two recognized witness signatures. You cannot substitute recognition of the scribe\u8217's handwriting as a {\i writer} for recognition of his signature as a {\i witness}. Two proper witness signatures are required; the handwriting of the scribe in his capacity as a scribe is not equivalent.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Family Names Limits\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Nicknames Valid\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Katav chanichato va-chanichato kasher} \u8212- if one recorded a nickname or family surname as the identifier of the parties, this is valid for purposes of a {\i get}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Generations Dispute\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i baraita} is cited on how far back one may reach for ancestral identification: {\i tanu rabbanan chanichat avot be-gittin ad yud dorot} \u8212- one may identify a party by an ancestral family name for up to ten generations. {\i Ve-Rabbi Shimon ben Elazar omer: gimmel dorot kasher} \u8212- Rabbi Shimon ben Elazar says three generations suffice and beyond that it is invalid. After ten or three generations \u8212- depending on which opinion one follows \u8212- the ancestral name may no longer serve as a legal identifier: {\i mi-kan ve-ilech pasul}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The logic of the ten-generation position may be connected to a well-known phenomenon in Tanach: the genealogy of Shaul in Divrei HaYamim spans ten generations, even though the Torah in Parshat Lech Lecha telescopes four generations into what Divrei HaYamim shows are actually ten, skipping six intermediate generations because Shaul\u8217's more distant ancestor was sufficiently famous that intervening generations could be omitted. The ten-generation limit may reflect this principle \u8212- that ancestral identification retains legal currency for up to ten generations.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Three Generations Pasuk\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Ka-man azla ha d\u8217'amar Rav Chanina: chatav chanichat avot be-gittin ad gimmel dorot?} \u8212- whose opinion does Rav Chanina follow when he rules that ancestral family names in {\i gittin} are valid for three generations? {\i Ke-Rabbi Shimon ben Elazar} \u8212- he follows Rabbi Shimon ben Elazar. {\i Amar Rav Huna: mai kra?} \u8212- Rav Huna asks what scriptural basis supports the three-generation position.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i pasuk} cited is from Devarim: {\i ki tolid banim u-vnei vanim ve-noshantem} \u8212- when you have children and grandchildren and you have grown old in the land, and then you will sin and be driven out. The word {\i ve-noshantem} carries the sense of becoming ancient, of staleness \u8212- and Rav Huna reads the sequence as: you, your children, and your grandchildren; after that, the ancestral name has grown too old and too stale to serve as current identification. Beyond three generations it is {\i noshen} \u8212- antiquated \u8212- and may no longer be used. A scriptural proof for the ten-generation position is not offered in the {\i gemara} here, which is conspicuous.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Seven Kingdoms Aggada\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Seven Kingdoms\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 At this point the {\i gemara} takes an {\i aggadic} turn. {\i Amar Rabbi Yehoshua ben Levi: na\u8217'atzei de-aggadata} \u8212- here is a fine {\i aggadic} teaching. {\i Lo chavah Eretz Yisrael ad she-ba\u8217'u vah shiv\u8217'ah batei dinim shel avodah zarah} \u8212- the Land of Israel did not become desolate until seven successive dynasties of {\i avodah zarah} had arisen within it. The seven are: Yeravam ben Nevat, Acha ben Achiyah, Achav ben Omri, Yehu ben Nimshi, Pekach ben Remalyahu, Menachem ben Gadi, and Hoshea ben Elah. {\i Va-ilu hein} \u8212- these are the seven.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The scriptural basis: {\i umlala yoledet ha-shiv\u8217'ah} \u8212- the one who bore seven is desolate. The {\i gemara} reads {\i yoledet ha-shiv\u8217'ah} not as a mother of seven children but as the land that gave rise to seven royal dynasties of idolatry. {\i Nafcha nafshah, ba\u8217'ah shamshah be-od yomam, bosha va-chafrah} \u8212- she breathed her last, her sun set while it was still day, she was covered in shame and disgrace. After seven such dynasties, the Land is left empty of its inhabitants, driven into exile.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Amar Rav Ashi: mai kra?} \u8212- Rav Ashi asks how one derives the number seven from the same {\i pasuk} of {\i ki tolid banim u-vnei vanim} cited earlier. The derivation counts as follows: you yourself are one; {\i banim}, children, adds two; {\i bnei vanim}, grandchildren of grandchildren \u8212- following out the tree \u8212- adds four more. One plus two plus four equals seven. Tosafot notes that this arithmetic, while somewhat loose, follows the branching of a genealogical tree, with each generation doubling.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hoshea ben Elah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Kahana raises a pointed question to Rav Ashi: it is written of Hoshea ben Elah that {\i va-ya\u8217'as ha-ra be-einei Hashem rak lo ke-malchei Yisrael} \u8212- he did evil in the eyes of God, but not like the kings of Israel before him. And yet it is also written that Shalmaneser came up against him and exiled his people. If Hoshea was less wicked than his predecessors, why was he the king under whom the final exile took place? Why did God not wait for a worse king?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The answer: {\i otan prosda\u8217'ot she-hoshiv Yeravam ben Nevat she-lo ya\u8217'alu Yisrael la-regel, ba Hoshea u-vitlan} \u8212- those border checkpoints that Yeravam ben Nevat had established to prevent the people of Israel from going up to Jerusalem for the {\i regalim}, the pilgrimage festivals, were abolished by Hoshea. Hoshea ben Elah actually removed the blockade. In this respect he was genuinely less wicked: he gave the people back the ability to go to the {\i Beit HaMikdash}. So why was exile decreed specifically in his time?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Afilu hachi lo alu Yisrael la-regel} \u8212- even so, the people did not go up. It was no longer the king who was stopping them; they simply no longer wanted to go. The sin had become internalized. God\u8217's response: {\i otan shanim she-lo alu Yisrael la-regel yelchu ba-shvi} \u8212- for all those years in which Israel chose not to ascend for the {\i regel}, they would now go into captivity. They had forfeited their connection to the Land, and so the Land would be taken from them. The blame ultimately traces back to Yeravam ben Nevat \u8212- {\i chata ve-hichti et ha-rabbim} \u8212- who sinned and caused the many to sin, as stated in Pirkei Avot, though the particular detail about the people\u8217's continued non-attendance after Hoshea lifted the restriction goes beyond what Pirkei Avot records.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Divine Haste\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Amar Rav Chisda amar Mar Ukva} \u8212- and some say {\i amar Rav Chisda amar Rav Yirmiyah darash Mareimar}: {\i mai de-chtiv vayishkod Hashem al ha-ra\u8217'ah vayevi\u8217'eha aleinu ki tzaddik Hashem Elokeinu?} \u8212- what does it mean that God was {\i vayishkod}, that He hastened the evil and brought it upon us, and that this demonstrates that God is righteous? The root {\i sh-k-d} is the same as in {\i shekedia}, the almond tree, the first to blossom \u8212- suggesting swiftness. But in what sense is it an act of righteousness to hasten punishment?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Exile Preparation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The answer involves the structure of the exile itself. The exile did not occur all at once. There was first the exile of Yechonyah \u8212- {\i im ha-golah asher hogleitah im Yechonyah melech Yehudah}, as recalled in Megillat Esther \u8212- and then afterward the exile of Tzidkiyahu. {\i She-dikdek HaKadosh Baruch Hu mi-Yisrael she-heglah galut Tzidkiyahu ve-adain galut Yechonyah kayemet} \u8212- God was precise: by the time the exile of Tzidkiyahu arrived, the earlier exiles of Yechonyah were already established in Babylon and had prepared the way.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 As the verse records of the exile of Yechonyah: {\i hacharash ve-ha-masgeir elef} \u8212- a thousand {\i charash} and {\i masgeir}. Homiletically: {\i charash she-be-sha\u8217'ah she-posachin na\u8217'asu ha-kol ke-chereshin} \u8212- the {\i charash} are the {\i chachamim}, the great sages, so called because when they speak, everyone else becomes as if deaf to all else, silenced by the authority of their words. And {\i masgeir: keivan she-sogrin, shuv ein poschin} \u8212- the {\i masgeir} are those whose rulings, once closed, are not reopened. A thousand such figures were already in Babylon by the time the second wave arrived. The scholarly infrastructure \u8212- the {\i batei midrashos}, the {\i chachamim}, the capacity for Torah life \u8212- was already in place. The later exiles arrived not into a spiritual vacuum but into a functioning community. This is the righteousness of God: the hastening of the first exile allowed the second exile to survive with their identity intact.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 850-Year Mercy\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Ulla amar she-hikdim shtei shanim le-ve-noshantem} \u8212- Ulla offers a different reading of God\u8217's righteousness. The {\i gematria} of {\i ve-noshantem} is 852. Yet God brought the exile after only 850 years \u8212- two years early. {\i Vayishkod Hashem al ha-ra\u8217'ah}: God hastened the evil by two years. Why is this righteous? Because the full text of the verse concludes with a warning of total annihilation: {\i ki avod toveidun} \u8212- you will utterly perish. Had God waited the full 852 years, the verse implies there would be nothing left of the Jewish people. By hastening the exile by two years, God cut short the countdown before the point of no return, ensuring that something of the Jewish people would survive. The {\i tzedakah} was in the timing: a slightly earlier, less complete punishment rather than a delayed, total destruction.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Amar Rav Acha bar Yaakov: shema mina meheirah} \u8212- we learn from this what \u8220"quickly\u8221" means from God\u8217's perspective: 850 years. As the verse says elsewhere, {\i elef shanim be-einecha ke-yom etmol} \u8212- a thousand years in God\u8217's eyes are like yesterday. On a divine timescale, 850 years is barely a moment. The compassion embedded in the word {\i maher} \u8212- quickly \u8212- is not that God acted immediately, but that He acted before the situation became irreversible, giving centuries for {\i teshuvah} while still ensuring that when the judgment came, it came before the point of utter destruction.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Coerced Get\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Jewish vs Non-Jewish Cou\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} moves to an entirely new topic: the {\i get me\u8217'useh}, the coerced {\i get}. This subject gained notoriety in recent years when a group of individuals in New Jersey were prosecuted for using coercion to extract {\i gittin} from recalcitrant husbands \u8212- a reminder that these laws carry real contemporary stakes. Rashi\u8217's gloss on {\i me\u8217'useh} is a single word: {\i be-chezakah} \u8212- by force.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} states: {\i get me\u8217'useh be-Yisrael kasher, uv\u8217'ovdei kochavim pasul} \u8212- a coerced {\i get} administered by a Jewish court is valid; one administered by a non-Jewish court is invalid. There is however a nuance: {\i ovdei kochavim chovtin oto ve-omrim lo: aseh mah she-Yisrael omrim lecha \u8212- kasher} \u8212- if the non-Jewish court beats the husband and says to him, \u8220"Do what the Jewish court is telling you to do,\u8221" the resulting {\i get} is valid. The non-Jewish enforcement acts as the arm of Jewish judicial authority, not on independent grounds. Only when the non-Jewish court acts entirely on its own initiative, without reference to the Jewish court\u8217's ruling, is the {\i get} invalid.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Acting on Authority\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This distinction is crucial. A non-Jewish court that serves as an enforcement mechanism for a legitimate Jewish {\i beit din} ruling is effectively an extension of that ruling. The {\i get} produced under such circumstances is as if it were produced under direct Jewish court compulsion. The invalidity arises only when the non-Jewish court operates on its own legal authority without grounding in Jewish law.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 According to Din\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Nachman, citing Shmuel, introduces a further grid of distinctions: {\i get ha-me\u8217'useh be-Yisrael ke-din kasher, she-lo ke-din pasul u-posel}. When a Jewish court coerces a {\i get ke-din} \u8212- according to law \u8212- the {\i get} is fully valid. When the coercion is {\i she-lo ke-din} \u8212- not in accordance with law \u8212- the {\i get} is {\i pasul u-posel}. This phrase requires careful parsing. {\i Pasul} means the {\i get} does not function to dissolve the marriage \u8212- she remains married. {\i Posel}, however, means that despite the {\i get}\u8217's failure to actually divorce her, it renders her ineligible to marry a {\i kohen} \u8212- she is, so to speak, partially affected. The {\i get} has done something, just not the right thing.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 For a non-Jewish court: if it acted {\i ke-din}, the result is {\i pasul u-posel} \u8212- the same partial effect as a Jewish court acting improperly. If it acted {\i she-lo ke-din}, the {\i get} has absolutely no effect: {\i pircha ein bo} \u8212- it accomplishes nothing whatsoever. She remains fully and unambiguously married.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Din Criteria\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What does {\i ke-din} mean in the context of a coerced {\i get} by a Jewish court? Rashi specifies: {\i ke-gon hanach d\u8217'amrinan behu: yotzi ve-yiten ketubah, o she-hayetah asurah lo} \u8212- cases in which the {\i beit din} had already ruled {\i yotzi ve-yiten ketubah}, that he must divorce and pay the {\i ketubah}, or cases where the wife was forbidden to him in the first place, where the marriage itself was halachically impermissible. In other words, the paradigmatic case of a coerced {\i get ke-din} is not the common contemporary scenario of a husband who refuses to give a {\i get} to a wife seeking divorce in an otherwise permissible marriage. It is specifically a case where the marriage should not have continued \u8212- or perhaps should never have occurred \u8212- and the {\i beit din} had formally ruled that the husband was obligated to give a {\i get}, but he refused to comply with that ruling. The court\u8217's coercion in such a case is the enforcement of a pre-existing legal obligation, not the imposition of a new one.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Whether this framework can be extended by analogy to the modern problem of the recalcitrant husband in an otherwise valid marriage is a question the {\i gemara} raises implicitly but does not resolve in this passage, and one must be cautious about abstracting too freely from the specific cases the {\i rishonim} describe to broader contemporary applications.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Gittin 88b\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Get Me\u8217'useh\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishna Get Me\u8217'useh\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The penultimate daf of {\i Gittin} opens with a {\i mishna} whose legal and moral weight belies its brevity. The {\i mishna} states: {\i get me\u8217'useh biYisrael kasher} \u8212- a {\i get} that is compelled by a Jewish {\i beis din} is valid. {\i Uv\u8217'ovdei kochavim pasul} \u8212- if non-Jews are the ones compelling it, the {\i get} is invalid. However, {\i uv\u8217'ovdei kochavim chovtin oto v\u8217'omrim lo asei mah sheYisrael omrim lach} \u8212- non-Jews may beat the husband and tell him to do what the Jews instruct him. The final word {\i ve-kasher} appears in the text in parentheses, reflecting a certain ambiguity. Tosafot raises the possibility that even when non-Jews are doing the physical coercion on behalf of a Jewish {\i beis din}, the {\i get} might still be problematic. Tosafot ultimately rejects that reading, reasoning that the {\i dayanim} themselves need not personally carry out the coercive act \u8212- they may delegate the unpleasant work. The simple reading, and the one Tosafot endorses, is that if the non-Jewish court is merely doing the dirty work under the supervision of a Jewish {\i beis din}, the {\i get} is valid.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Nachman Ruling\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then introduces the ruling of Rav Nachman in the name of Shmuel: {\i ha-get me\u8217'useh biYisrael kadin kasher} \u8212- a {\i get} compelled by a Jewish {\i beis din} where there is a genuine legal mandate, meaning where the law actually obligates the husband to give a {\i get}, is valid. {\i Shelo kadin pasul u\u8217'posel} \u8212- if the court compelled it without a legitimate legal mandate, it is an invalid {\i get}, but it creates a {\i re\u8217'ach haget} problem: if the husband subsequently dies, the wife would be prohibited from marrying a {\i kohen}, as there is enough of a \u8220"whiff of a {\i get}\u8221" to affect her status for {\i kehunah}. Turning to the non-Jewish court scenario \u8212- again, assuming the non-Jewish court is acting on its own authority, not merely as the arm of a Jewish {\i beis din} \u8212- {\i kadin pasul u\u8217'posel}: if there is a legal mandate, the {\i get} is invalid because it was not done under Jewish auspices, but it still creates a {\i re\u8217'ach haget} problem. {\i Shelo kadin} \u8212- if it is doubly removed, both non-Jewish and without legal mandate, {\i afilyu re\u8217'ach haget ein bo}: there is not even the faintest whiff of a {\i get}, and there is no {\i kehunah} consequence whatsoever.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Notably, the {\i gemara} throughout this entire discussion does not raise what might seem like the most obvious question: how can a forced {\i get} be valid at all, given that a {\i get} must be given {\i mirtsono}, of the husband\u8217's own free will, and a coerced transaction is ordinarily void? The {\i gemara} elsewhere does address forced transactions \u8212- {\i talu v\u8217'zavin}, where a person was strung up until he agreed to sell his property, and the ruling is {\i zvinei zvin}, the sale is valid \u8212- because even under compulsion, at some level the person is choosing to act. Similarly, {\i talu v\u8217'kideish kiddushei kiddushin}: a shotgun {\i kiddushin} is valid {\i b\u8217'diavad}. One could simply have placed this case in that same category.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rambam Yetzer Hara\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Rambam, however, offers an additional reason specific to the {\i get}. He explains that deep down, every Jew wishes to do what is right. The husband\u8217's {\i yetzer hara} is simply getting in the way. When the {\i beis din} coerces him, it is not overriding his will \u8212- it is wearing down the {\i yetzer hara} and liberating his true, inner will to do the right thing. In that sense, he is ultimately acting {\i mirtsono}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The question arises: why did the Rambam need this additional reason rather than simply relying on the general rule that coerced transactions are valid? The answer may lie in the Rambam\u8217's own formulation at the beginning of {\i Hilchos Gittin}, where he lists ten criteria for a valid {\i get}, one of which is that it must be {\i mirtsono}. The {\i rishonim} and {\i acharonim} ask: isn\u8217't {\i mirtsono} simply the standard requirement for any valid legal act? The answer seems to be that there is a distinction between {\i da\u8217'as} \u8212- consenting to something \u8212- and actually {\i wanting} it in the deeper sense that the Torah describes when it says {\i ki matza bah ervat davar v\u8217'chatav lah sefer kiritut}: the husband is motivated, not merely consenting. It is not enough that he agreed; he must on some level want to give the {\i get}. It is for this reason that the Rambam required his special explanation: the beating does not merely produce {\i da\u8217'as}, it reveals the husband\u8217's genuine, latent will to fulfill his {\i halachic} obligation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This explanation also illuminates why a {\i get} compelled without a legal mandate is invalid. The entire justification for treating the forced {\i get} as truly voluntary is anchored in the existence of a {\i halachic} mandate. Without that mandate, there is no basis to say that the husband\u8217's \u8220"true will\u8221" is to give a {\i get}, and so the {\i get} is not {\i mirtsono} in any meaningful sense.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Four-Case Matrix\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The structure of valid and invalid {\i get me\u8217'useh} cases can be mapped as a matrix of two variables: the source of compulsion (Jewish {\i beis din} or non-Jewish court) and whether there is a legal mandate ({\i kadin} or {\i shelo kadin}). Only one of the four cells produces a genuinely valid {\i get}: a Jewish {\i beis din} acting with a legal mandate. The two cells that combine one condition with its absence \u8212- a Jewish {\i beis din} without legal mandate, or a non-Jewish court with legal mandate \u8212- each produce a {\i re\u8217'ach haget} problem: close enough to the real thing to affect {\i kehunah} status, but not valid as a {\i get}. The fourth cell \u8212- a non-Jewish court acting without a legal mandate \u8212- is so far removed from any legitimate {\i get} that {\i afilu re\u8217'ach haget ein bo}: it carries no halakhic weight whatsoever.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Mesharshaya Rejected\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} probes the logic of this framework by introducing a position of Rav Mesharshaya, which it will ultimately reject. Rav Mesharshaya proposes: {\i dvar Torah}, as a biblical matter, {\i get me\u8217'useh b\u8217'ovdei kochavim kasher} \u8212- if there is a genuine legal mandate, a {\i get} compelled by a non-Jewish court is in principle valid. The reason {\i chazal} declared it invalid is merely rabbinic: {\i shelo t\u8217'hei kol achas v\u8217'achas holches v\u8217'tolah atzma b\u8217'ovdei kochavim u\u8217'mafka\u8217'as atzma miyad ba\u8217'alah} \u8212- so that women would not hire non-Jewish courts to pressure their husbands into giving a {\i get} without a legitimate {\i halachic} basis, particularly since non-Jewish courts may be more susceptible to being paid off.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} immediately challenges this position: if in principle a {\i get} compelled by a non-Jewish court is biblically valid, then the combination of a non-Jewish court and no legal mandate should still generate a {\i re\u8217'ach haget} problem, since {\i shelo kadin} under a valid Jewish {\i beis din} also creates {\i re\u8217'ach haget}. But the {\i mishna} says in that fourth case there is not even a {\i re\u8217'ach haget}. The {\i gemara} therefore concludes: {\i ella hadar Rav Mesharshaya b\u8217'dusiah hi} \u8212- this position of Rav Mesharshaya is sheer foolishness; it is rejected entirely. A {\i get} compelled by a non-Jewish court acting on its own authority is completely invalid on any level.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then asks: given that a non-Jewish court has no authority, what explains the {\i re\u8217'ach haget} problem even when there is a legal mandate? The answer is {\i k\u8217'din b\u8217'k\u8217'din d\u8217'Yisrael mechalaf}: when there is a legal mandate, the non-Jewish scenario resembles closely enough the one case that genuinely works \u8212- a Jewish {\i beis din} with a legal mandate \u8212- that it creates concern for {\i kehunah} status. {\i Shelo k\u8217'din b\u8217'k\u8217'din d\u8217'Yisrael lo mechalaf}: when there is neither a Jewish {\i beis din} nor a legal mandate, the case is too far removed to create any concern at all.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Semicha & Shlichus\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Abaye Challenge\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} next records that Abaye found Rav Yosef sitting and compelling husbands who were obligated to give {\i gittin}, using various forms of pressure and leverage. Abaye challenged him: {\i vaanen hedyotot anan} \u8212- we are mere commoners. We do not possess the {\i semicha} that traces back to Moshe Rabbeinu.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Tarfon\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 In support of this concern, the {\i gemara} cites a {\i baraita} in the name of Rabbi Tarfon: wherever you find gatherings of non-Jews \u8212- {\i Rashi} understands this as referring to non-Jewish courts \u8212- even if their substantive laws are identical to Jewish law, {\i i atah rashai lehizakek lahem}: you are forbidden to submit to their authority. The verse states {\i ve\u8217'eleh hamishpatim asher tasim lifneihem} \u8212- before {\i them}, the Jewish judges \u8212- {\i velo lifnei ovdei kochavim}. This is not merely a question of substantive law. Even if the law itself is the same, the critical question is who is recognized as the empowered legal authority. It is the Jewish court system that is divinely empowered, not merely correct in its rulings.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i baraita} adds a second clause: {\i davar acher}, before them and not before {\i hedyotot} \u8212- commoners. To exercise coercive authority as a {\i beis din}, one requires {\i semicha}. This seemingly creates a grave problem for Rav Yosef\u8217's practice. One might think that if the requirement of {\i semicha} applies to ordinary legal proceedings, it applies with even greater force to cases of compelling a {\i get}, where the court authorizes actual physical force over a transaction that on its surface appears involuntary.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Common Case Exception\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Yosef responds: {\i anan shelichutaihu ka avdinan} \u8212- we act as agents of the court that does possess {\i semicha}. The {\i gemara} supports this by analogy to ordinary cases of loans and admissions of debt, which courts without {\i semicha} handle regularly, on the grounds that they represent the authority of the courts that did have {\i semicha}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Theft Pushback\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} immediately presses: {\i i hachi gezeilos vachavalos nami} \u8212- if that is so, should courts today not also handle cases of theft and personal injury as agents of the {\i semicha} court? The {\i gemara} answers: {\i ki avdinan shelichutaihu bemilsa deshchicha} \u8212- we only function as agents of the {\i semicha} court in matters that are common occurrences. {\i Bemilsa d\u8217'lo shchicha lo avdinan shelichutaihu} \u8212- in uncommon cases, we do not claim that agency.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This generates an extensive body of {\i poskim} literature about the scope of contemporary {\i beis din} authority. The {\i gemara} here implies that theft and personal injury cases fall outside this agency. This is in tension with sources elsewhere in the {\i gemara} that suggest courts did handle such cases. Tosafot addresses the apparent contradiction and suggests that perhaps the agency applies only when certain conditions are met jointly. Additionally, some note a distinction between {\i knas} \u8212- punitive fines, which represent the coercive power of a court of full authority \u8212- and pure restitution in cases of loans and debts, which is more a matter of compensating actual loss and restoring the status quo. The concept that {\i shlichus} applies only in matters of {\i chisaron kis}, where someone has actually suffered a monetary loss, is raised elsewhere, though it is not made explicit in this {\i gemara}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What is striking, as the {\i gemara} implicitly acknowledges, is that forcing a {\i get} \u8212- which involves actual physical coercion to produce a document that on its face looks involuntary \u8212- would seem to be the most demanding case requiring formal authority. Yet the {\i gemara} concludes that this too falls within the common-case {\i shlichus}. That forcing a {\i get} is classified as {\i milsa deshchicha}, a common occurrence, is itself a painful commentary on the frequency with which husbands refuse to give {\i gittin} \u8212- a reality that has not changed since the time of the {\i gemara}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafot Semicha\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot raises two further questions in this context. The first concerns the acceptance of converts: accepting a {\i ger} requires three expert {\i mumchin} with {\i semicha}, as derived from the word {\i mishpat} written in connection with {\i chalitza} in {\i Hacholetz}. Yet we accept converts today, without any court possessing {\i semicha}. Tosafot answers, again invoking the agency principle, {\i d\u8217'avdinan shlichuseihu}, and adding that acceptance of converts is considered sufficiently common \u8212- {\i d\u8217'chashiv k\u8217'shchicha} \u8212- to fall within the scope of that agency. Tosafot notes that the {\i gemara} in {\i Hacholetz} itself implies that they would accept converts in {\i Bavel} even in the absence of {\i semicha}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The second and more fundamental question is this: to say we are agents of a court with {\i semicha} presupposes there is such a court to represent. In the era of the {\i gemara}, courts with {\i semicha} existed in Eretz Yisrael and the later Babylonian courts could claim to represent them. But today, {\i v\u8217'hashta ein mumchin b\u8217'Eretz Yisrael} \u8212- there are no ordained judges in Eretz Yisrael at all. Of whom are we agents? Tosafot answers: {\i d\u8217'shlichus d\u8217'kama\u8217'i avdinan} \u8212- we act as agents of the earlier courts, the ancient courts that no longer exist. This is a remarkable conceptual claim: contemporary {\i batei din} derive their authority from their status as the continuing institutional expression of an ancient court whose {\i semicha} has long since lapsed, yet whose transmitted authority is still, in some sense, operative. It is on that basis that contemporary {\i batei din} are empowered to address the full range of {\i halachic} cases they handle.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Kol & Rumors\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishna Kol\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Following the discussion of {\i get me\u8217'useh}, the {\i gemara} turns to a new {\i mishna} dealing with a categorically different problem: the halakhic weight of rumors about a woman\u8217's marital status. The {\i mishna} states: {\i yatza shemah ba\u8217'ir mekudeshes harei zo mekudeshes} \u8212- if a rumor has spread in the town that a woman is married, she must be treated as married and cannot marry another man without receiving a {\i get}. {\i Megureshes harei zo megureshes} \u8212- if a rumor spread that she is divorced, she is treated as divorced; meaning, at minimum, if she is married to a {\i kohen}, the rumor that her husband divorced her would preclude her from remaining with him. The {\i mishna} adds the crucial qualification: {\i uvilvad shelo yehei sham amatla} \u8212- all of this applies only if there is no contextualizing explanation that neutralizes the rumor.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} provides two examples of an {\i amatla}. If the rumor was that so-and-so divorced his wife, but it was said that he did so {\i al tenai}, on a condition, that does not constitute a rumor of divorce because one may simply assume the condition was not met. Alternatively, if the rumor was of a {\i kiddushin} in which it was unclear whether the object of value reached her \u8212- {\i safeik karov lo safeik karov lah} \u8212- the ambiguity built into the rumor itself constitutes an {\i amatla} that neutralizes it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What immediately becomes apparent is that the {\i gemara} will work systematically and relentlessly to narrow the scope of this {\i mishna} until the cases to which it actually applies are extraordinarily limited. The stakes are high: how much disruption to a woman\u8217's life can be justified on the basis of rumors alone?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Assi Single\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} begins its narrowing. {\i V\u8217'amar Rav Assi kol kal dibatar nisuin lo chashinan lei} \u8212- any rumor about a woman that arises after she has already been married, we do not concern ourselves with it. The second case in the {\i mishna} \u8212- a woman married to a {\i kohen} and a rumor spreads that she was divorced \u8212- falls entirely under this principle. Rav Assi\u8217's position means we do not force a married woman to leave her husband on account of a rumor that she was divorced. The {\i mishna}, Rav Assi therefore clarifies, applies in full only to the first case: a single woman about whom a rumor spreads that she got married. In that case alone, we treat her as potentially married, and she may not marry another man without a {\i get}. The case of {\i mekudeshes u\u8217'megureshes} \u8212- a rumor that she both married and was divorced \u8212- cancels itself out as {\i kol v\u8217'shovro imo}, a rumor that carries its own refutation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Frivolous Gossip\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} continues further limiting the {\i mishna}. Rava rules: {\i yatza lah shem mezanah ba\u8217'ir ein chosheshin lah} \u8212- if a rumor circulates that a woman is promiscuous or sexually loose, we are not concerned with it. This would be relevant, for example, to a {\i kohen} who might otherwise have to avoid marrying her, as intercourse with a non-Jew would render her prohibited to {\i kehunah}. Rava\u8217's reason: {\i pritzuta b\u8217'alma hu d\u8217'chazu} \u8212- people have simply observed some minor behavioral impropriety and extrapolated wildly from it. {\i Rashi} illustrates the range: the \u8220"pritzuta\u8221" might have been something as minor as joking with men in the marketplace, or at the other end, some form of physical contact. The point is the same \u8212- once people observe behavior that slightly transgresses communal norms, they will embroider and amplify, and such rumors carry no halakhic weight.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tannaim Adultery\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} notes that the question of how much evidentiary weight to assign to behavior-based inferences was debated among the {\i Tannaim}. If a woman was observed eating in the marketplace, walking with an outstretched neck, or nursing her child in public, Rabbi Meir says {\i tetzei} \u8212- she must be divorced, as we infer adultery from such conduct. Rabbi Akiva is more lenient, requiring that the town gossips \u8212- those who knit in the moonlight, {\i mezasos balevana} \u8212- have already begun talking about her before we take any such action. Both, however, give significant halakhic weight to behavioral rumors.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ben Nuri Witnesses\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yochanan ben Nuri objects sharply to both positions, and his is the voice of reason in this discussion. {\i Im kein lo hinachta bas l\u8217'Avinu sheyosheves tachas ba\u8217'alah} \u8212- with this approach, no woman will be allowed to remain married to her husband. The Torah explicitly requires {\i ki matza bah ervat davar}, a concrete matter of nakedness, and invokes the general evidentiary principle that {\i al pi shenayim eidim o pi shlosha eidim yakum davar} \u8212- it takes two or three witnesses to establish a fact. {\i Davar} appears in both: evidence must be clear and concrete, not inferential gossip.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot draws out the implication for our {\i mishna}: if we won\u8217't force a married woman to leave her husband based on behavioral rumors, then {\i a fortiori} we should not impose halakhic consequences on a single woman based on such rumors either \u8212- even though the threshold for consequences to a single woman (e.g., {\i kehunah} eligibility) might seem lower than forcing divorce. The {\i gemara}\u8217's thrust is consistent: it is not the identity of the rumored act but the evidentiary quality of the rumor itself that determines whether it carries weight.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Limiting Kol\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 No Concern List\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i baraita} catalogs a sweeping series of cases in which we are categorically not concerned about a rumor: a rumor of sexual intercourse ({\i be\u8217'ula}), a rumor of marriage ({\i nesu\u8217'ah}), a rumor of {\i kiddushin} only ({\i arusa}), a rumor that identifies no specific person ({\i shelo l\u8217'ploni}), a rumor about an event in another city ({\i d\u8217'ir acheret}), a rumor that she is a {\i mamzeret}, and a rumor that she is a {\i shifcha}. The list extends to property-status rumors as well. In short, standing alone, none of these rumors are halakhically actionable. {\i Rashi} summarizes the operative principle: only a rumor that claims she married a specific, named person on this very day in this very city carries any weight at all. A rumor about the past, about an unidentified party, or about a distant location is simply discarded.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Circumstantial Evidence\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Ulla radically narrows the operative case of the {\i mishna} further still. The {\i mishna} is not concerned with a mere {\i kol havarah} \u8212- a disembodied echo, a rumor of uncertain origin that simply passed from mouth to mouth. Rather, the concern arises only when there is robust circumstantial evidence: candles are lit, couches are spread out, there is a catering van at the hall, and {\i uvnei adam nichnasim v\u8217'yotzim} \u8212- people are streaming in and out. It is in that context, where the entire town has witnessed what looks unmistakably like a wedding, that we take a rumor seriously. Levi\u8217's teaching parallels Ulla\u8217's, adding that women are knitting by candlelight and rejoicing.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Nitkadshah Not Mitkad\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Even with all of that circumstantial evidence, the {\i gemara} further demands precision in what is actually being said. If the townspeople say {\i plonit mitkadeshet hayom} \u8212- so-and-so is getting married today \u8212- {\i v\u8217'dilma lo ikadshah}: maybe it got called off at the last moment, and we have only evidence of a planned wedding, not an actual one. The concern arises only when people say {\i plonit nitkadshah hayom} \u8212- so-and-so {\i got} married today, an established fact and not merely an anticipated event. This distinction between {\i mitkadeshet} (getting married) and {\i nitkadshah} (got married) is determinative. Rav Papa and Bar Rav Papa citing Rabbi Yochanan both make this same point, with slight variations in the accompanying details.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then addresses a cryptic statement: if the people did say {\i nitkadshah}, that is a {\i kol}; if they did not say it, it is an {\i amatla} \u8212- an explanation that neutralizes the {\i kol}. This is puzzling: if they said nothing, there is no {\i kol} to neutralize in the first place. The {\i gemara} resolves that it means when an explanation emerges immediately alongside the rumor, it functions as though the rumor was never established. This stands in contrast to the position of Rav Abba bar Rav Huna, who holds that an {\i amatla} can neutralize an established {\i kol} even if it is offered as much as ten days later.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Witness Tracing\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Papa and Rav Huna bar Minyomi citing Rav Abba citing Rav Huna citing Rav offer a second track for establishing a {\i kol}: even without the full battery of circumstantial evidence described by Ulla and Levi, if one can trace the rumor back through a chain of people \u8212- A heard it from B, B heard it from C \u8212- and at the end of the chain identify specific individuals who are claimed to have witnessed the event directly, that too constitutes a {\i kol} worthy of concern. The final version of this tradition adds the crucial detail: {\i v\u8217'halchu lahem l\u8217'medinat hayam} \u8212- those identified witnesses have gone overseas and cannot be questioned. If the tracing leads to a {\i davar barur}, a clearly established fact through identifiable witnesses who can actually testify, it is no longer a {\i kol} at all but direct testimony. It is specifically the case where the original witnesses have been identified but are unavailable that creates a {\i kol}. Tosafot observes that this and Ulla\u8217's approach represent two distinct tracks: circumstantial evidence from a community-wide wedding scene, or a traceable chain ending in named but absent witnesses.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Bittul Kol\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Abaye Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having established the extremely narrow circumstances in which a {\i kol} is halakhically significant, the {\i gemara} turns to a related but distinct question: once a {\i kol} has been established, should the {\i beis din} proactively work to dispel it? Abaye poses this to Rav Yosef: {\i mevatlinan kola o lo mevatlinan?} One might assume that since {\i kol} is given relatively little weight, the {\i beis din} should have every interest in stamping out harmful rumors. On the other hand, one might argue that attempting to dispel a rumor could be counterproductive \u8212- if people believe the rumor, a {\i beis din} denying it will simply discredit itself.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Witnesses Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The answer turns on a dispute between Rav Chisda and Rav Sheshet regarding the standard for establishing a {\i kol} in the first place. Rav Chisda maintains that a {\i kol} is only significant if it is spread by {\i kesherim} \u8212- valid witnesses, or at least those who heard directly from valid witnesses. Since this is such a stringent standard, the {\i gemara} infers {\i shema mina mevatlinan kala}: because we are concerned about rumors only in very narrow circumstances, we would obviously not hesitate to proactively dispel them. Rav Sheshet disagrees and holds that {\i afilu mipi nashim havei kala} \u8212- even if the rumor is spread by women, who are not valid witnesses for such purposes, it constitutes a {\i kol} of concern, given that the other factual prerequisites are in place. From his broader standard, {\i shema mina lo mevatlinan kala}: since the circumstances creating concern are somewhat more expansive, we are more cautious about the underlying facts and do not rush to dispel rumors, lest there be some fire behind the smoke.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Regional Practice\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Yosef resolves the tension practically: this is a matter of regional custom. {\i Be-Sura mevatli kala} \u8212- in Sura they proactively nullify rumors. {\i Be-Nahardea lo mevatli kala} \u8212- in Nehardea they do not. This reflects the respective positions of Rav (Sura) and Shmuel (Nehardea).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Conditional Marriage\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now presents a series of cases that test when nullification is effective. In the first case, a rumor spread that a woman had been betrothed to a {\i bar bei Rav}, a yeshiva student. Rav Chama brought the woman\u8217's father before him and asked what had happened. The father explained that the {\i kiddushin} had been performed {\i al tenai} \u8212- on the condition that the student would not travel to the region of {\i Bei Chuzai} \u8212- but the student subsequently went there, voiding the condition and thus the {\i kiddushin}. Rav Chama rejected this: {\i keivan de-be-idna de-havei kala lo havei amatla} \u8212- since this explanation was not offered at the time the rumor was established, {\i lav kol kaminach de-mechazket amatla} \u8212- the father cannot retroactively establish it as an {\i amatla} to neutralize the rumor. This directly applies the earlier dispute: can an explanation that comes after the {\i kol} was established neutralize it? Here the answer is no \u8212- once the {\i kol} is established, a later explanation does not suffice.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Dates Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The next case involves a woman about whom a rumor circulated that she had been betrothed at a specific wellspring, using what {\i Rashi} identifies as remnants of unripe dates \u8212- an object presumably worth less than a {\i shaveh perutah}, the minimum value required for valid {\i kiddushin}. The question was sent to Abaye: must we be concerned, or may we nullify this rumor? Abaye rules: {\i afilu le-man de-amar lo mevatlinan kola, be-ha mevatlinan kola} \u8212- even according to the position that in general we do not nullify rumors, here we would. Why? Because {\i meimar amri inhu be-derabbanan be-kidushin ve-lo havei bah shaveh perutah} \u8212- people will reason on their own that the object used was not worth a {\i shaveh perutah}, and so the {\i kiddushin} was never valid. The key principle is effectiveness: a {\i beis din} will only act to nullify a rumor when it can expect to be effective. If the {\i beis din}\u8217's nullification directly contradicts public perception, it will discredit the {\i beis din} and accomplish nothing. But when the rumor itself contains internal elements \u8212- here, the specific detail of the unripe dates \u8212- that people will naturally use to explain away the {\i kiddushin}, then working with that internal logic can be effective, and the {\i beis din} will act.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Unnamed Son\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A parallel case: a rumor spread that a woman had become betrothed to one of the sons of a certain family, without specifying which son. Rava rules: {\i afilu le-man de-amar lo mevatlinan kola, be-ha mevatlinan kola} \u8212- even for those who hold we generally do not nullify, here we do. The reasoning: {\i meimar amri inhu be-rabbanan be-kiddushei u-vi-kiddushei katan havu} \u8212- people will reason that since the rumors don\u8217't specify which son, it was probably the minor son, and a minor cannot effect valid {\i kiddushin}. Again, the internal logic of the rumor provides the explanation that allows for effective dispelling.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mature Minor\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A harder case: a rumor that a woman was betrothed to a minor who appeared physically mature \u8212- tall, husky, looking every bit like an adult. Here there is hesitation to work toward nullification. The difficulty is that public perception directly contradicts the halakhic reality. The {\i beis din} might say he was a minor, but people saw what appeared to be an adult. Rav Mordechai told Rav Ashi, however, that in a case like this, there was a precedent and people said {\i adayin lo higia le-phalgut Reuven} \u8212- he had not yet reached the stage of \u8220"the divisions of Reuven.\u8221" This is a reference to the verse in {\i Shirat Devorah}, {\i li-flogos Reuven gedolim chikrei lev}, describing how mature adults engage in deep deliberation. The point is that even if someone looks physically adult, people can perceive from his behavior and intellectual immaturity that he is a minor. Therefore, if the {\i beis din} points out that the young man in question was clearly behaving in childish, immature ways \u8212- {\i gedolim chikrei lev} being the marker of adult capacity \u8212- people will understand and accept that he was a minor, making the nullification effective. The underlying principle throughout these cases is consistent: we will dispel a rumor when we think we can be effective in doing so; we will not if it will merely entrench it.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Amatla Neutralizing\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ten Days Later\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Returning to the question of when an {\i amatla} can neutralize an established {\i kol}, the {\i gemara} cites Rabba bar Rav Huna who holds the lenient position: {\i amatla she-amru afilu mikan ve-ad asarah yamim} \u8212- an explanation, even if it comes as late as ten days afterward, is sufficient to neutralize the {\i kol}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Possible Amatla\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Zvid goes even further: {\i be-makom amatla chosheshin le-amatla} \u8212- in a place where an {\i amatla} is possible, meaning wherever an explanation is conceivable even hypothetically, that theoretical possibility alone is sufficient to neutralize the {\i kol}. One need not even produce an actual explanation; the mere availability of one neutralizes the rumor. The {\i gemara} at this stage does not present the more stringent view \u8212- that an {\i amatla} must come on the very day of the {\i kol} \u8212- but instead consistently cites the lenient positions, reflecting the overall {\i gemara}\u8217's sustained effort to limit the halakhic consequences of rumors.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Papa challenges Rav Zvid from the {\i mishna} itself, which says {\i uvilvad shelo tehe sham amatla} \u8212- the {\i kol} is only neutralized when there is an {\i amatla} present. This implies an actual {\i amatla} is needed, not merely a theoretical one. Rav Zvid responds: {\i bimkom amatla ka-amar} \u8212- the {\i mishna} means in a place where an {\i amatla} is conceivable. Rav Kahana then presses Rav Papa: surely you agree that a possible explanation suffices?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Conditional Kiddushin\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The question is adjudicated with a case from {\i Yevamos}. A woman remarried on the basis of a single witness\u8217's testimony that her first husband had died; her first husband then reappeared alive. If she had only received {\i kiddushin} from the second man and had not yet cohabited with him, {\i muteres lachzor} \u8212- she may return to her first husband. Why? The {\i gemara} suggests that people will naturally assume the {\i kiddushin} was done {\i al tenai}, on a condition, that proved not to be fulfilled. This reflects the rule that {\i kiddushin} without subsequent {\i nissu\u8217'in} is assumed to be conditional when circumstances call for it, while {\i kiddushin} that has led to {\i nissu\u8217'in} is not so easily unwound. No one explicitly stated the {\i tenai} \u8212- yet we read one into the situation because a plausible explanation exists. This appears to support Rav Zvid\u8217's position that the mere possibility of an {\i amatla} suffices.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Husband Contest\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} deflects: {\i shani hasam de-asi ba\u8217'al ve-ka me\u8217'ar\u8217'er} \u8212- that case is different, because the first husband has appeared and is actively objecting, loudly contesting the claim that he ever died or that she was ever free to remarry. It is the husband\u8217's own presence and insistence that drives the {\i gemara} to accept that the {\i kiddushin} must have been conditional, not merely the theoretical availability of an explanation. The {\i gemara} then asks: if the husband\u8217's objection is the operative factor, why not allow her to return to him even if she had already cohabited with the second man? The answer leads to the final principle.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Nisuin Issur\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Nisseis de-avdi issura knasu rabbanan, nitkadsha de-lo avdi issura lo knasu rabbanan} \u8212- once the woman has cohabited with the second man, she has unwittingly committed a violation, and the Rabbis imposed a consequence to give the initial {\i kiddushin} legal permanence and prevent a situation that would look like {\i machzir gerushaso}, a man taking back a woman he had divorced after she had been with another. Before cohabitation, there is no violation \u8212- she had only {\i kiddushin} \u8212- and therefore no rabbinic penalty applies, and she may return to her first husband. The husband\u8217's contestation provides the warrant for assuming the explanation, but that warrant operates differently depending on whether {\i nissu\u8217'in} has occurred.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The arc of this entire discussion is clear and consistent. The {\i mishna}\u8217's seemingly expansive rule \u8212- that a {\i kol} creates halakhic consequences \u8212- has been narrowed at every stage. The circumstances that establish a valid {\i kol} are exceptional: all the hallmarks of a public wedding, or a traceable chain to named, absent witnesses. Even then, the {\i kol} may be dispelled when the {\i beis din} can do so effectively. And when the {\i kol} has been established, an {\i amatla} \u8212- actual or merely possible \u8212- can neutralize it. The {\i gemara}\u8217's consistent movement is toward protecting women from the halakhic consequences of unverified communal rumor, a theme that will continue as the {\i masechet} concludes.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Gittin 89\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Gett Me\u8217'useh\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishnah: Forced Get\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The final {\i daf} of {\i Gittin} reserves its most consequential material for last. After three chapters devoted to the laws of {\i bifnei nichtav u\u8217'bifnei nechtam}, we arrive at a single {\i amud} addressing the compulsion of a husband to give a {\i get}. The {\i mishnah} opens with a foundational distinction: {\i get me\u8217'useh b\u8217'Yisrael kasher} \u8212- a {\i get} that is coerced by a Jewish {\i beit din} is valid. {\i B\u8217'ovdei kochavim pasul} \u8212- if non-Jews are the ones compelling it, the {\i get} is invalid.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} then adds that non-Jews may nonetheless beat the husband and say to him: \u8220"Do what the Jews tell you.\u8221" The final word {\i v\u8217'kasher} appears in parentheses in many texts and is not universally accepted as part of the {\i mishnah}. {\i Tosafot} raises the possibility that even this arrangement \u8212- non-Jews doing the physical work while the Jewish {\i beit din} supervises \u8212- might be invalid, but ultimately rejects that reading. Since the {\i dayanim} themselves need not personally administer the coercion, what matters is under whose auspices the compulsion occurs. If the Jewish {\i beit din} is directing proceedings, the {\i get} is valid.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Nachman\u8217's Rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} refines the {\i mishnah}\u8217's ruling through a statement of Rav Nachman in the name of Shmuel. {\i Haget me\u8217'useh b\u8217'Yisrael k\u8217'din kasher} \u8212- a {\i get} coerced by a Jewish {\i beit din} in a legally mandated case is valid. {\i Shelo k\u8217'din pasul u\u8217'posel} \u8212- if the court compelled the {\i get} without legal mandate, the {\i get} is invalid, but it generates the {\i re\u8217'ach haget} problem: should the husband subsequently die, the woman would be disqualified from marrying a {\i kohen}. Turning to the non-Jewish case: {\i b\u8217'ovdei kochavim k\u8217'din pasul u\u8217'posel} \u8212- even where the legal obligation to give a {\i get} exists, if the compulsion came from a non-Jewish court acting on its own authority, the {\i get} is invalid, yet still generates a {\i kehunah} disqualification. However, {\i shelo k\u8217'din} \u8212- where there is neither legal mandate nor Jewish auspices \u8212- {\i afilu re\u8217'ach haget ein bo}: there is not even a whiff of a {\i get}, and no {\i kehunah} problem arises at all.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A striking issue that the {\i gemara} does not directly analyze here is the threshold question of how a coerced {\i get} can be valid at all. Ordinarily, a coerced transaction is invalid. The {\i gemara} does address analogous cases elsewhere: {\i talu\u8217'ei v\u8217'zavin} \u8212- if someone was strung up until he agreed to sell property, the sale is valid ({\i zinei zinei}), because even under external compulsion, a person at some level is acting on his own volition; circumstances always compel us in various ways, and at the end of the day the act is performed. The {\i gemara} similarly validates a coerced {\i kiddushin}: {\i talu\u8217'ei v\u8217'kiddesh kiddushei kiddushin}. One could simply place the forced {\i get} in this same category. Yet the {\i Rambam} offers a further rationale specific to {\i gittin}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rambam: Yetzer Hara\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Rambam} explains that deep down, a person wants to do what {\i halachah} requires. It is only the {\i yetzer hara} standing in the way. When the {\i beit din} beats him, it is not overriding his genuine will; it is wearing down the {\i yetzer hara} and liberating his true desire to comply with the law. The compulsion thus frees, rather than supplants, authentic consent.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might ask why the {\i Rambam} needed this additional rationale when the general principle of {\i talu\u8217'ei v\u8217'zavin} would seem sufficient. Some {\i acharonim} answer that there may be a heightened standard specific to {\i gittin}. The {\i Rambam} states at the outset of {\i Hilchos Gittin} that among the ten criteria for a valid {\i get} is the requirement that it be given {\i m\u8217'mirtsono} \u8212- of the husband\u8217's own genuine will \u8212- derived from the verse {\i ki matza bah ervat davar v\u8217'chasav lah sefer krisus}. Whereas ordinary transactions require only {\i da\u8217'as} \u8212- intent to perform the act \u8212- a {\i get} requires something more: the Torah\u8217's formulation implies the husband is {\i motivated} to give it, not merely that he consents. The {\i Rambam}\u8217's explanation therefore does the necessary halachic work: by attributing the coercion to suppressing the {\i yetzer hara} rather than to overriding the husband\u8217's will, it establishes that deep down he genuinely wants to do the right thing, satisfying even this elevated standard.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This analysis also clarifies why a {\i get} that is not legally mandated is invalid: the entire justification for treating the coerced {\i get} as a genuine expression of will depends on the existence of a {\i halachic} mandate. Without that mandate, the theoretical underpinning collapses.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Non-Jewish Court Get\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now examines the intermediate cases \u8212- where compulsion comes from a non-Jewish court \u8212- through the lens of a {\i mah nafshach} argument. Either non-Jews have the capacity to meaningfully compel a {\i get}, in which case a {\i get} forced by them in a legally mandated case should be fully valid; or they do not, in which case there should be no {\i re\u8217'ach haget} whatsoever. What justifies the intermediate position?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Marshisha proposes a bold answer: {\i dvar Torah}, as a matter of biblical law, a {\i get} coerced by a non-Jewish court in a legally mandated case is valid. The rabbinic invalidation was enacted as a protective measure \u8212- {\i shelo tehei kol achat v\u8217'achat holechet v\u8217'tola atzma b\u8217'ovdei kochavim u\u8217'mafka\u8217'as atzma miyad ba\u8217'alah} \u8212- lest every wife hire a non-Jewish court, which might be more susceptible to bribery, to pressure her husband into giving a {\i get} when no legal obligation exists. Since the invalidation is purely rabbinic, a legally mandated {\i get} compelled by non-Jews retains its biblical validity, which is why it generates a {\i re\u8217'ach haget} concern.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} immediately challenges this position: if we accept that the non-Jewish court\u8217's compulsion is in principle valid, then a non-legally-mandated {\i get} forced by non-Jews should still carry a {\i re\u8217'ach haget} problem \u8212- analogous to a legally-mandated {\i get} forced by a Jewish court without authority. {\i Ela hadra Rav Marshisha bidusa hi} \u8212- the {\i gemara} dismisses Rav Marshisha\u8217's position as untenable. The compulsion of a non-Jewish court is intrinsically without force.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Why then does a legally mandated {\i get} forced by non-Jews still generate a {\i re\u8217'ach haget} problem? The {\i gemara} answers: {\i kidin k\u8217'chidin d\u8217'Yisrael michlaf} \u8212- where there is a legal obligation to give a {\i get}, a non-Jewish court\u8217's compulsion is similar enough to the case that actually works \u8212- a legally mandated {\i get} forced by a Jewish court \u8212- that it creates a {\i kehunah} concern. {\i Shelo kidin b\u8217'kidin d\u8217'Yisrael lo michlaf} \u8212- but if a non-Jewish court forces a {\i get} without legal mandate, it is two full degrees removed from the valid case, and not even {\i re\u8217'ach haget} applies.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Four-Case Chart\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The resulting framework can be mapped across two axes: whether the compulsion is legally mandated ({\i k\u8217'din}) or not ({\i shelo k\u8217'din}), and whether the compelling authority is a Jewish {\i beit din} ({\i Yisrael}) or a non-Jewish court ({\i goyim}). The only case that yields a fully valid {\i get} is the combination of legal mandate and Jewish court. Both the case of a Jewish court without legal mandate and the case of a non-Jewish court with legal mandate are one degree removed from the valid case and therefore each generates a {\i re\u8217'ach haget} problem. The case of a non-Jewish court without legal mandate is two degrees removed and generates no halachic concern whatsoever \u8212- {\i afilu re\u8217'ach haget ein bo}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Semicha & Authority\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Abaye\u8217's Challenge\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} records that Abaye found Rav Yosef sitting and compelling husbands who were legally obligated to give {\i gittin}, applying whatever pressure and leverage was available to compel compliance. Abaye challenged him: {\i ve\u8217'anan hedyotot anan} \u8212- are we not mere commoners? We lack the {\i semicha} that traces back to Moshe Rabbeinu. How can we presume to exercise this authority?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tanna: Semicha Needed\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye invokes a {\i tannaitic} source: wherever one finds non-Jewish courts ({\i aguriyot shel ovdei kochavim}), even if the substance of their law is identical to Jewish law, {\i af al pi shedineihem kedinei Yisrael, ee atah reshai lehizakek lahem} \u8212- one may not submit to their authority. The verse {\i ve\u8217'eleh hamishpatim asher tasim lifneihem} specifies {\i lifneihem} \u8212- before Jewish judges, and not before non-Jewish ones. This principle is not merely about the content of the law but about which institution is recognized as legitimately empowered. The text then adds: {\i davar acher lifneihem velo lifnei hedyotot} \u8212- \u8220"before them and not before commoners.\u8221" To impose the law one must possess {\i semicha}; a {\i beit din} composed of those without ordained authority cannot exercise coercive legal power.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This raises obvious contemporary questions about the use of secular courts, which people do in practice even within observant communities \u8212- a subject with an extensive halachic literature of its own.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Beit Din Agency\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Yosef responds: {\i anan shelichusaihu ka\u8217'avdinan} \u8212- we act as agents of the court that does possess {\i semicha}. We are not claiming independent authority; we are representing those who are properly empowered.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Common vs.\u160 ?Rare Matters\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} draws an analogy: just as courts today handle cases involving loans and admissions ({\i hoda\u8217'os v\u8217'halva\u8217'os}) on this agency theory, so too can they handle the forcing of a {\i get}. The {\i gemara} immediately asks: if that is so, why not also handle cases of theft ({\i gezeilos}) and personal injury ({\i chavalos})? The {\i gemara} answers: {\i ki avdinan shelichusaihu bemilsa d\u8217'shchichah} \u8212- we are considered agents of the ordained court only in common matters. {\i Bemilsa d\u8217'lo shchichah lo avdinan shelichusaihu} \u8212- in uncommon matters, we do not exercise that agency.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This generates substantial halachic literature about the precise scope of contemporary {\i beit din} authority. {\i Tosafot} notes that the {\i gemara} does treat courts as handling theft and injury in some passages, and works to reconcile this. There is also a distinction noted between matters of {\i knas} \u8212- punitive fines \u8212- where courts without {\i semicha} would not exercise {\i shelichut}, and matters of pure restitution ({\i mamon}), where they may. A further limiting principle is that {\i shelichut} may apply only where someone has actually suffered a monetary loss ({\i chisaron kis}).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What is particularly striking is that forcing a {\i get} \u8212- which involves physical compulsion and the halachic paradox of treating a coerced act as voluntary \u8212- should seemingly require the clearest possible credentialing. Yet the {\i gemara} not only affirms that contemporary courts were empowered to do this, but describes it as {\i milsa d\u8217'shchicha} \u8212- a common matter. That characterization is itself a sobering commentary: even in the time of the {\i gemara}, husbands who were legally obligated to give a {\i get} and refused were a sufficiently frequent occurrence to be classified as routine. This makes the contemporary failure of the Israeli rabbinical establishment \u8212- which possesses actual coercive power (including imprisonment) yet largely declines to use it \u8212- all the more difficult to understand.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafot: Gerim & Shtlich\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} raises two additional points of interest. The first concerns the acceptance of converts ({\i geirim}). The {\i gemara} in {\i Yevamos} establishes that conversion requires three expert judges with {\i semicha} ({\i mishpat kesiv bei}). {\i Tosafot} answers, in the name of the {\i Ri}, that we perform the conversion as agents of the ordained court ({\i d\u8217'avdinan shlichusaihu}), since the matter is considered sufficiently common ({\i d\u8217'chashiv k\u8217'shchiach}) \u8212- though the {\i Tosafot} acknowledges the irony, given that conversion to Judaism in medieval Christian or Muslim lands was punishable by death and was anything but common. Indeed, various {\i poskim} writing about the laws of {\i geirim} felt compelled to include disclaimers that the entire subject was theoretical, given the legal dangers of the time.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The second point concerns a deeper structural question: {\i shelichut} to an existing court with {\i semicha} makes sense, but how can anyone claim to represent a court that no longer exists? {\i Ve\u8217'im tomar heichi avdinan shlichusaihu? V\u8217'ha achshav ein mumchin b\u8217'Eretz Yisrael} \u8212- if there were a living court in Israel with {\i semicha}, we could be their deputies; but when no such court exists, who is authorizing us? {\i Tosafot} answers: {\i d\u8217'shlichus d\u8217'kama\u8217'ei avdinan} \u8212- we exercise the agency of the early, ancient {\i beit din} that once held {\i semicha}. We are the continuing authorized representatives of an authority that no longer exists as a living institution. It is a striking and far-reaching idea: contemporary halachic courts derive their legitimacy not from any presently constituted body but from their status as the ongoing expression of an ancient empowerment.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rumors About Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishnah: Rumor Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now turns to an entirely different set of concerns, introduced by a second {\i mishnah}: {\i yatza shemah ba\u8217'ir mekudeshet harei zo mekudeshet} \u8212- if a rumor circulates in town that a woman is married, she must be treated as married. She cannot marry another man without receiving a {\i get} from her purported first husband. {\i Megureshes harei zo megureshes} \u8212- if there is a rumor that she is divorced, she is treated as divorced.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The second ruling is troubling in both directions. It does not mean we leniently assume she is free: primarily it means that if a married woman\u8217's husband is a {\i kohen} and a rumor circulates that he divorced her, we would not allow her to continue living with him. {\i Uvilvad shelo yehei sham amatla} \u8212- this applies only when there is no explanation that contextualizes or qualifies the rumor. {\i Eizo hi amatla?} What constitutes such an explanation? If the rumor was that he divorced her on a condition ({\i al tnai}), we assume the condition was not fulfilled, and the {\i get} therefore never took effect. Or if the rumor was that {\i kiddushin} occurred but there was uncertainty whether the object of value ({\i kesef kiddushin}) actually reached her \u8212- {\i safek karov la safek karov lo} \u8212- then the rumor is inherently uncertain and is neutralized.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Ashi: Post-Marriage\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} moves immediately to limit the {\i mishnah}\u8217's scope. {\i Ve\u8217'asrinan la agavra?} \u8212- are we really going to forbid a woman to her husband on the basis of a rumor? Rav Ashi rules: {\i kol kala d\u8217'vatar nisuin lo hashishan leh} \u8212- any rumor that arises after a woman is already married carries no halachic weight. The {\i mishnah}\u8217's ruling that {\i megureshes harei zo megureshes} therefore does not mean we force an already-married woman to separate from her husband. Rather, Rav Ashi reinterprets the {\i mishnah}: the case of a woman reported as married ({\i mekudeshet}) refers to an unmarried woman \u8212- a rumor that she became betrothed means she cannot now marry anyone else without a {\i get} from the first. The case of {\i mekudeshet u\u8217'megoreshet} \u8212- married and divorced \u8212- means the rumor contains both elements simultaneously, in which case {\i kol v\u8217'shovro imo}: the rumor carries its own cancellation within it. These two components obviously neutralize one another, and the result is no halachic concern.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Pritzuta Rumors\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava adds a further restriction: {\i yatza la shem mezana ba\u8217'ir ein chosheshin lah} \u8212- if a rumor circulates that a woman is sexually promiscuous, it carries no halachic weight. One might think that a rumor of promiscuity could, for example, disqualify an unmarried woman from marrying a {\i kohen} (if she were suspected of having relations with someone who would render her a {\i zona}). Rava dismisses this: {\i pritzuta b\u8217'alma hu d\u8217'hazu lah} \u8212- people simply observe behavior that strikes them as somewhat loose, and from that the rumor of serious sexual misconduct grows. {\i Rashi} illustrates the range of behaviors that might trigger such rumors: from {\i shichaka bashuk} \u8212- joking with men in the marketplace \u8212- to {\i michla bein dadei} \u8212- a sexual act of some kind. The spectrum is wide, and the connection between the observed behavior and the rumored conclusion is unreliable. Communal gossip, as always, has a tendency to amplify minor infractions into lurid accusations.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tannaim Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This reflects a dispute among {\i tanna\u8217'im}. If a woman was seen eating in the marketplace ({\i achla bashuk}), or walking with an outstretched neck in a manner designed to attract attention ({\i gargara bashuk}), or nursing her child in a public place ({\i henika bashuk}) \u8212- Rabbi Meir rules that she must be divorced from her husband, the assumption being that such behavior indicates adultery has occurred. Rabbi Akiva sets the threshold much higher: {\i mishe-yishiu ve-yitnu bah mezarot balevanah} \u8212- not until the women who spin wool by moonlight \u8212- the proverbial neighborhood gossips \u8212- have taken up the story. Even by Rabbi Akiva\u8217's account, however, communal yenta-talk carries some weight, which is itself a striking position.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Yochanan: Two Witness\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yochanan ben Nuri finally provides the voice of genuine caution: {\i im ken lo hinachta bat le-Avraham Avinu she-yoshevet tachat ba\u8217'alah} \u8212- if we give this weight, no woman will remain with her husband. He then invokes a {\i gezeirah shavah}: the word {\i davar} appears both in the verse requiring a husband to find an {\i ervat davar} before writing a {\i get} and in the verse {\i al pi shenei edim o al pi sheloshah edim yakum davar} \u8212- establishing matters through two or three witnesses. {\i Mah le-halan davar barur af kan davar barur} \u8212- just as the latter verse requires clear, established evidence, so too does the former. Nothing short of clear evidence suffices.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This position also, {\i Tosafot} notes, implies that even rumors about a single woman prior to her marriage cannot simply be given halachic weight. If a {\i kohen} marries a woman unaware that rumors had circulated about her before the marriage, and those rumors then come to light, giving them weight now would retroactively harm him in his current marriage. Once we see that rumors are insufficient to compel a married woman to divorce, the same logic extends to their weight regarding a single woman\u8217's status. The {\i gemara} is thus progressively narrowing the {\i mishnah} from every direction.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Narrowing Rumor Scope\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Baraita: No Concern\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i baraita} enumerates case after case in which rumors generate no halachic concern: {\i be\u8217'ulah ein chosheshin lah} \u8212- a rumor of sexual relations carries no weight. {\i Nesu\u8217'ah ein chosheshin lah} \u8212- a rumor that she got married carries no weight, directly contradicting the plain reading of the {\i mishnah}. {\i Arusah ein chosheshin lah} \u8212- even a rumor of {\i kiddushin}, which is a more private affair than a full wedding and might seem more believable, carries no weight. {\i Shelo l\u8217'Ploni ein chosheshin lah} \u8212- if the rumor does not name the specific person she allegedly married, it is disregarded. {\i V\u8217'ir acheret ein chosheshin lah} \u8212- if the rumored marriage took place in another city, it is disregarded.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rashi} draws the implication explicitly: the only rumor we are concerned about is one that says she got married {\i today}, {\i in this city}, {\i to this specific named person}. Anything that fails to specify all three elements \u8212- the immediacy, the location, and the identity of the husband \u8212- is insufficient to create a halachic concern.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Baraita Extended\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i baraita} continues: {\i mamzeret ein chosheshin lah} \u8212- a rumor that she is a {\i mamzeret} carries no weight. {\i Shifcha ein chosheshin lah} \u8212- a rumor that she is a {\i shifcha} likewise. Similarly, a rumor that a certain person consecrated his property or renounced ownership of it does not affect the halachic status of that property. The general principle is stark: rumors, across a wide range of categories, carry essentially no halachic force.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ulla: Circumstantial\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 With the {\i baraita} having reduced the {\i mishnah}\u8217's operative scope to almost nothing, the {\i gemara} through Ulla articulates precisely what would render a rumor halachically significant. {\i Lo sheshamu kol havarah} \u8212- the case of the {\i mishnah} is not where people merely heard an echo \u8212- that is, an unrooted report passed from person to person. Rather: {\i ela k\u8217'dei she\u8217'yoru nerot v\u8217'yatziu mitot u\u8217'vnei adam nichnasim v\u8217'yotzim} \u8212- only where the rumor is accompanied by substantial circumstantial evidence. Candles are being lit; bedding is being spread; people are going in and out of the hall. And amidst all of this: {\i v\u8217'omrim Plonit nitkadsha hayom} \u8212- people are saying that so-and-so got married today. Only under these conditions does the rumor rise to the level of halachic concern.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Got Married Today\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Even Ulla\u8217's formulation requires clarification. {\i Mitkadeshet hayom} \u8212- \u8220"she is getting married today\u8221" \u8212- leaves open the possibility that the marriage ultimately did not occur. {\i V\u8217'dilma lo ikadsha} \u8212- perhaps it was called off at the last moment. The {\i gemara} therefore insists: the statement must be {\i nitkadsha} \u8212- \u8220"she got married,\u8221" in the past tense, completed, not merely anticipated. Levi\u8217's parallel teaching adds further texture: women knitting by candlelight ({\i nashim tavot l\u8217'or haner}), everyone rejoicing around the bride \u8212- and still the formulation must be {\i nitkadsha}, past tense. Rav Pappa confirms: the statement must be {\i Plonit nitkadsha hayom}. Anything less \u8212- a statement that she {\i will} be married, or a rumor that lacks any of the required specificity \u8212- does not rise to the level of a halachic {\i kol}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Named Witnesses\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A statement of Rav in a chain transmitted through Rav Huna, Rav Abba, and Rav Shmuel bar Yehuda identifies a second track by which a rumor may be established. It is not enough to have heard a disembodied report: one must be able to trace the rumor back \u8212- {\i Ploni me\u8217'heichan shama mi\u8217'Ploni u\u8217'Ploni mi\u8217'Ploni} \u8212- through an identified chain of sources. Taken to its logical conclusion, this chain would eventually reach the people who directly witnessed the event, in which case it would no longer be a rumor but actual testimony ({\i edut ma\u8217'alyata}).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} therefore refines the ruling: {\i v\u8217'halchu lahem l\u8217'medinat hayam} \u8212- the chain leads back to specifically identified witnesses who are known but not currently available, having traveled overseas. In such a case, where we know who witnessed the event but cannot question them, there is enough substance to the rumor to create concern. {\i Tosafot} understands this as an alternative and independent track from Ulla\u8217's circumstantial-evidence approach. Either path suffices to establish a {\i kol}: either the extensive circumstantial evidence at the time of the event (even without knowing who the specific witnesses were), or the identification of specific witnesses who can be named but not presently questioned. One or the other is required; neither alone suffices without meeting its own threshold.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Nullifying Rumors\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Abaye\u8217's Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye poses a question to Rav Yosef: {\i mevatlinan kala o lo mevatlinan} \u8212- when a rumor is circulating, should the {\i beit din} proactively work to stamp it out, or should it leave the rumor alone? The intuitive answer might be that since rumors can harm people\u8217's status, of course we should dispel them. But there is a practical counterargument: attempting to dispel a rumor may simply draw more attention to it, discredit those trying to dispel it, and ultimately make things worse. The question is therefore whether active nullification is wise policy.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chisda vs.\u160 ?Sheshes\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Two {\i amora\u8217'im} offer divergent perspectives that bear on this question. Rav Chisda holds that a {\i kol} is only established when it is heard testified to by {\i kesherim} \u8212- people of unimpeachable credibility. Since {\i Rashi} understands this to mean credible people testifying to what they heard (not directly witnessed), it follows that the threshold for a halachically significant rumor is relatively high. From this, one might infer {\i shma mina mevatlinan kala}: since we generally do not credit rumors, it follows that we would actively work to dispel them.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Sheshes disagrees: {\i afilu mipi nashim havei kala} \u8212- even a rumor spread by women (who are not qualified {\i edim}) can constitute a halachically significant {\i kol}, provided the other conditions of circumstantial evidence or identified witnesses are present. If we are willing to treat this as a meaningful {\i kol}, then {\i lo mevatlinan kala} \u8212- perhaps we do not proactively attempt to dispel it, since there may be some genuine substance behind it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Regional Practice\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Yosef resolves the exchange by noting that the question is itself regionally contested: {\i b\u8217'Sura mevatli kala} \u8212- in Sura, they proactively nullify rumors. {\i Benhardah lo mevatli kala} \u8212- in Nardah, they do not. This corresponds, broadly speaking, to the respective orientations of Rav (Sura) and Shmuel (Nardah).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Unripe Dates Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then presents a series of cases illustrating when active nullification is and is not appropriate. In the first, a rumor circulated that a certain woman had become betrothed to a {\i yeshiva} student using unripe dates ({\i atzifa d\u8217'tuchla}) at the wellspring of a place called Bei Shifei. Rav Idi bar Avin sent the case to Abaye: given these specific circumstances, should the rumor be nullified?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye rules: {\i afilu man d\u8217'amar lo mevatlinan kala beha mevatlinan kala} \u8212- even according to those who ordinarily do not nullify rumors, here they would. Why? {\i Meimar amri} \u8212- people will naturally say that the Rabbis examined the dates at the time of the {\i kiddushin} and determined they were worth less than a {\i shavei pruta}, meaning the {\i kiddushin} was invalid. The key principle the {\i gemara} is articulating here is that active nullification is warranted not in every case, but specifically when the nullification will be {\i effective} \u8212- when it aligns with the existing rumor\u8217's own internal logic in a way that people will find credible. To directly contradict what people think they saw or heard would be counterproductive and self-defeating; but where the explanation flows naturally from within the rumor\u8217's own details, it can be deployed successfully.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Unknown Son\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A second case: a rumor circulated that a woman had become betrothed to one of the sons of a certain individual ({\i l\u8217'chad mibnei planya}), without specifying which son. Under normal circumstances, a rumor that does not name the husband is not a halachic concern. Here, however, the question is whether even this rumor warrants active nullification. Rava rules that even those who generally do not nullify rumors would do so here: {\i inhu barabbanan b\u8217'kiddushei v\u8217'kiddushei katan havi} \u8212- people will naturally say that when they heard she was betrothed to one of the sons, it was probably the minor among them, and {\i kiddushin} by a minor is invalid. Again, the nullification works because it can be advanced within the terms of what people already believe, without requiring anyone to abandon their perception.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Minor Appears Grown\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The third case draws the limit of this principle. A rumor circulated that a woman was betrothed to a minor who appeared physically like an adult ({\i katan hanireh k\u8217'gadol}). Here, explaining that the groom was a minor would directly contradict people\u8217's visual perception. Reality and perception diverge: the boy is truly a {\i katan}, but everyone sees a {\i gadol}. Would such an explanation be effective?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Mordechai reports to Rav Ashi that a case of this kind was brought and people said the boy had not yet reached {\i palgus Reuven} \u8212- a reference to the verse in {\i Shiras Devorah} about the tribe of Reuven, {\i b\u8217'palgus Reuven gedolim chikrei lev}, which the {\i gemara} reads as referring to a distinct level of adult discernment. The point is that even if a young person is physically imposing, observers can nonetheless detect through his behavior and manner that he lacks adult judgment \u8212- {\i gedolim chikrei lev} describes the kind of mature understanding that makes adulthood recognizable. People would therefore accept an explanation that the groom was a minor, because his comportment would have revealed his age. The rumor can thus be nullified even in this case, because the explanation does not require people to disbelieve what they perceived, only to interpret it correctly.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Amtalot (Explanations)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishnah: Amtala\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} had already established the principle that a rumor is halachically operative only {\i uvilvad shelo yehei sham amatla} \u8212- provided no explanation or qualification is present. The {\i gemara} now addresses the conditions under which an {\i amatla} successfully neutralizes an otherwise established rumor.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Timing of Amtala\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabba bar Rav Huna holds: {\i amatla she\u8217'amru afilu mikan v\u8217'ad asara yamim} \u8212- an explanation can neutralize a rumor even if it is offered as much as ten days later. The {\i amatla} need not be contemporaneous with the establishment of the {\i kol}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Possibility Suffices\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Zeira goes further still: {\i b\u8217'makom amatla chosheshin l\u8217'amatla} \u8212- in a context where an explanation is {\i possible}, even the mere possibility of an explanation is sufficient to neutralize the {\i kol}. One need not actually produce a named explanation or have anyone claim one; the fact that an explanation could theoretically exist is enough. This is a sweeping position: it means that wherever we can imagine a plausible benign interpretation of the events that gave rise to the rumor, the rumor carries no halachic weight.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} at this point has effectively adopted the most permissive readings at each stage. Rather than requiring an explanation on the same day (the position that appeared earlier in the discussion), it now cites only Rabba bar Rav Huna\u8217's position that an explanation can come even ten days later. And beyond that, it entertains Rav Zeira\u8217's view that even a theoretical possibility of an explanation suffices. The consistent direction of the {\i gemara}\u8217's movement throughout this entire discussion has been to limit, restrict, and ultimately minimize the halachic weight of rumors.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tenai Example\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Kahana challenges Rav Pappa to accept Rav Zeira\u8217's position, citing a proof from the {\i mishnah} in {\i Yevamos}. If a woman remarried on the basis of a single witness\u8217's testimony that her first husband died, and then the first husband reappeared alive, but she had only reached {\i kiddushin} with the second man \u8212- not yet {\i nisuin} \u8212- she is {\i muteres lachazor} to her first husband. The reason she cannot return to him after {\i nisuin} is that the situation resembles {\i machzir grushato} \u8212- a man remarrying his divorced wife after she has been with another \u8212- which is {\i asur}. But if she has only had {\i kiddushin} and not {\i nisuin} with the second man, she may return to the first.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Why is no objection raised about the appearance of {\i machzir grushato} even in the {\i kiddushin} case? It must be, argues Rav Kahana, because we assume the {\i kiddushin} with the second man was performed {\i al tnai} \u8212- conditionally \u8212- and since the first husband has returned alive, the condition retroactively failed. But this is merely a {\i possible} explanation, not one that was actually stated at the time of the {\i kiddushin}. This proves that the mere possibility of an explanation \u8212- {\i bemakom amatla} \u8212- is enough to neutralize a concern.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Limits of Possibility\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} rejects this proof: {\i shani hasam de\u8217'asi ba\u8217'al veka m\u8217'arer} \u8212- that case is different because the first husband has actually arrived and is actively protesting, asserting that he was always alive and that she was always his wife. It is the husband\u8217's real-world protest and presence that creates the effective explanation, not simply the abstract theoretical possibility of a {\i tnai}. The proof therefore does not establish the general principle that a theoretical possibility of an explanation is sufficient.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Consequence Doctrine\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then asks: if the husband\u8217's protest is what matters, why not allow her to return to him even after {\i nisuin} with the second man? The answer establishes a doctrinal principle: {\i nises de\u8217'avdi issura kansu rabanan, niskadsha delo avdi issura lo kansu rabanan} \u8212- once she has lived with the second man, she has transgressed (albeit unwittingly), and the Rabbis imposed a consequence. Before {\i nisuin}, when no {\i issur} has actually occurred, there is no penalty and no reason to prevent her return to her first husband. This \u8220"consequence doctrine\u8221" \u8212- that rabbinic sanctions attach to situations where an {\i issur} has actually been committed, but not where the prohibition was avoided \u8212- represents the final limiting principle in this extended discussion.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The overall movement of the {\i gemara} across this entire topic is thus unmistakable. Beginning with a {\i mishnah} that seemed to give meaningful weight to rumors about a woman\u8217's status, the {\i gemara} has progressively narrowed the operative cases to an extraordinary degree: requiring specific circumstantial evidence or named witnesses, limiting concern to past-tense statements naming a specific person in a specific city on that very day, allowing proactive nullification wherever it can be effective, permitting explanations even after significant delay, and approaching the position that even the theoretical possibility of an explanation neutralizes a {\i kol}. Tomorrow\u8217's concluding {\i amud} will bring this discussion \u8212- and the tractate \u8212- to its close.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Gittin 89b\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Kol & Substantiation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kol Requires Beit Din\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 We pick up on 89b in Gittin, approximately twenty lines down from where {\i Tosfos} cites the statement of {\i Amar Rav Huna}, and about five lines above that point, at the line beginning with the word {\i rabbanan}. At this stage in the sugya, the Gemara has already established several principles governing the {\i kol} \u8212- the rumor or public report that a woman has contracted a marriage. The {\i mishna} held that such a rumor requires us to be concerned, preventing the woman from marrying another man without receiving a {\i get} from the one she was rumored to have married. The Gemara\u8217's preceding discussion explored the degree of substantiating circumstances required for a {\i kol} to be treated as actionable, and whether {\i beit din} may proactively negate \u8212- {\i mevatlin} \u8212- a {\i kol} once it is determined to have been unfounded.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Into this discussion steps Rav Ashi with a foundational principle: {\i kol kola d\u8217'lo it\u8217'chazeik b\u8217'vei dina lav kola havei} \u8212- any {\i kol} that {\i beit din} has not examined and established as a matter warranting concern does not attain the status of a {\i kol}. This is consistent with the earlier requirement for substantiating evidentiary circumstances. As Rashi explains: we are not concerned about a rumor unless the local {\i beit din} has investigated \u8212- in accordance with the standards discussed earlier in the Gemara \u8212- and has formally declared this particular {\i kol} to be one we must heed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kol After Marriage\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Ashi adds a second ruling: {\i kol kol d\u8217'basar nissuin lo chashinan lah} \u8212- any {\i kol} that arises after a woman is already married, we do not act upon. That is, a rumor emerging after {\i nissuin} \u8212- full cohabitation \u8212- that would impugn her status (for example, that she had previously been married to another man, or had committed an act rendering her forbidden to a {\i kohen}) is simply not heeded, since acting upon it would have a direct impact on the man to whom she is currently married. The Gemara then infers the converse: {\i ha d\u8217'basar eirusin chashinan lah} \u8212- a {\i kol} that arose after {\i eirusin} but before {\i nissuin} would still be heeded, since at that point only {\i kiddushin} has taken place and they are not yet living together as husband and wife.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Chaviva Ruling\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Chaviva disagrees even with that limited scope: {\i afilu d\u8217'basar eirusin nami lo chashinan lah}. Even a {\i kol} that arose after {\i eirusin} is not heeded, because here too there is already a relationship being directly impacted. The {\i halakha} follows Rav Chaviva: once a woman is in any stage of marriage \u8212- whether {\i eirusin} or {\i nissuin} \u8212- we disregard rumors about her prior status.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Kol & Kiddushin\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Query Setup\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now records that a query was sent from the house of Rav to Shmuel: {\i yelamdenu rabbeinu} \u8212- let our master teach us. The case is as follows: a {\i kol} had gone forth that a woman was married to Reuven \u8212- a {\i kol} of the type and strength that would ordinarily require concern. Then Shimon came along and married her through explicit {\i kiddushei Torah}, in full public view. He did not merely create another {\i kol}; people actually witnessed him perform {\i kiddushin} with her. What is the ruling?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shmuel\u8217's Response\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Shmuel\u8217's response is threefold: {\i taitzei} \u8212- she must leave Shimon; {\i v\u8217'amidu hadavar al buryo} \u8212- investigate and establish the facts; and {\i ve\u8217'odiuoni} \u8212- report the findings back to me.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then unpacks what \u8220"clarifying the matter\u8221" means in practice, proceeding in two stages. The first possibility: the investigation reveals that the first marriage \u8212- the one underlying the {\i kol} \u8212- was in fact invalid. For instance, perhaps there were all the social trappings of a wedding (torches, candles, public celebration) sufficient to generate a strong {\i kol}, but no actual valid {\i kiddushin} \u8212- say, because the ring was not worth a {\i perutah}. In that case, {\i mevatlinan kala} \u8212- we would negate the {\i kol} and permit the woman to remain married to Shimon without requiring a {\i get}. The investigation has shown the {\i kol} to be baseless.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But this first resolution is immediately rejected: {\i ve\u8217'ha Nahardea atra d\u8217'Shmuel hu, v\u8217'lo mevatlinan kala} \u8212- Nahardea was Shmuel\u8217's own city, and Nahardea does not negate a {\i kol}. This is a striking point. Even if {\i beit din} determines through investigation that the {\i kol} was factually false, the {\i beit din} of Nahardea will not publicly annul it, because at a certain point, once a {\i kol} has achieved sufficient traction, it cannot simply be overridden by a contrary ruling without appearing to sanction what the public perceives as a real marriage. As one might put it: the only difference between reality and perception is that it is often easier to change reality than perception. And so even if the facts exonerate the first marriage, the second husband will still be required to give a {\i get} \u8212- because the community cannot be expected to simply disregard what it believes it knows.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The second possibility: the investigation reveals not that the first marriage was void, but that it was definitively valid. That is, we move from the realm of {\i kol} \u8212- substantiated rumor \u8212- to actual {\i edut}, direct testimony that she was truly married to Reuven. In that case, {\i lo tzricha get mishinei} \u8212- she does not need a {\i get} from Shimon at all. She is simply married to Reuven; Shimon\u8217's {\i kiddushin} was an act of no legal consequence, and she returns to Reuven directly.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 To summarize: regardless of what the investigation yields, Shimon cannot remain married to her \u8212- {\i taitzei} applies in all scenarios. Whether she requires a {\i get} from Shimon depends on the outcome: if the investigation produces only a negated {\i kol} (Nahardea: she still needs a {\i get} from Shimon), or if the investigation yields actual {\i edut} establishing the first marriage as real (no {\i get} from Shimon needed).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Huna Dispute\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara notes that this ruling stands in tension with the position of Rav Huna: {\i eishet ish she\u8217'pashat yadah ve\u8217'kiblah kiddushin mei\u8217'acher mekudeshet, mi\u8217'din d\u8217'Rav Hamnuna} \u8212- a married woman who extends her hand and accepts {\i kiddushin} from another man thereby becomes betrothed to him, based on the principle of Rav Hamnuna.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Hamnuna had ruled: {\i ha\u8217'isha she\u8217'amra l\u8217'ba\u8217'alah geirashtani ne\u8217'emenet, chazakah ein isha me\u8217'izah paneha bifnei ba\u8217'alah} \u8212- a woman who says directly to her husband\u8217's face \u8220"you have divorced me\u8221" is believed, because we invoke the principle that a woman would not be so brazen as to make such a declaration in her husband\u8217's presence unless it were true. Rav Huna derives from this that a married woman who accepts {\i kiddushin} from another man is in effect making precisely such a declaration \u8212- she is treating herself as divorced \u8212- and therefore she is considered betrothed to the second man as well. Were we to rule in accordance with Rav Huna\u8217's principle, it would follow that even when we know with certainty that she was married to Reuven, her acceptance of {\i kiddushin} from Shimon carries evidentiary weight, and she would at minimum need a {\i get} from Shimon. But the Gemara above concluded that if we have actual {\i edut} of the first marriage, she needs no {\i get} from Shimon. This is therefore a dispute with Rav Huna.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Hamnuna Presence\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara resolves the apparent tension by clarifying the precise scope of Rav Hamnuna\u8217's teaching: {\i ki m\u8217'deRav Hamnuna bifanav \u8212- shelo bifanav, mizeh umizeh} \u8212- Rav Hamnuna\u8217's principle applies only when the woman accepts {\i kiddushin} from the second man in the presence of her husband, or declares her divorce directly to him face to face. It is specifically in that context \u8212- in the first husband\u8217's presence \u8212- that we say a woman would not be so brazen unless it were true. But in our case, the woman accepted {\i kiddushin} from Shimon without Reuven being present. Therefore Rav Hamnuna\u8217's principle does not apply, and the acceptance of {\i kiddushin} from Shimon carries no evidentiary weight regarding her status vis-\u224 ?-vis Reuven.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosfos} notes that this point \u8212- that the evidentiary force of accepting {\i kiddushin} from another man applies only when done in the first husband\u8217's presence \u8212- was never actually in dispute. The Gemara\u8217's point here, then, is narrower: even granting that argument, one might still have thought that accepting {\i kiddushin} from Shimon outside Reuven\u8217's presence should at least require a {\i get} from Shimon as a matter of caution. The answer is no: if we know as a fact that she was married to Reuven, and Shimon\u8217's {\i kiddushin} was performed outside Reuven\u8217's presence, that act is entirely without legal significance, she needs no {\i get} from Shimon, and she simply returns to being married to Reuven.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rumor Unresolved\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Huna Option\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now turns to the scenario where the investigation yields no clear resolution. There was a strong {\i kol} that she was married to Reuven. Shimon then performed {\i kiddushin} with her. The investigation is conducted, but the facts remain indeterminate \u8212- neither the truth nor the falsity of the first marriage can be established. We are left precisely where we began.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Huna rules: {\i megaresh rishon venosay sheni \u8212- megaresh sheni venosay rishon lo}. Contrary to what the word {\i taitzei} might initially suggest \u8212- that she must leave the first arrangement \u8212- Rav Huna explains that {\i taitzei} means she must leave Reuven\u8217's sphere. Reuven is to be the one who divorces her, and she may then, if she chooses, marry Shimon. The reverse \u8212- Shimon divorcing her so she can return to Reuven \u8212- is not permitted. Why? {\i Asya l\u8217'maimar machzir grushaso min ha\u8217'erusin}: people will reconstruct the narrative as follows: the {\i kol} was true, she was married to Reuven, Reuven then divorced her, she married Shimon, Shimon divorced her, and now Reuven is remarrying his divorc\u233 ?e. This appearance of {\i machzir grushaso} is something Rav Huna wishes to avoid, even at the cost of foreclosing an otherwise valid legal option. This is an uncomfortable concession to perception over reality \u8212- we are closing off a permissible avenue simply to avoid what the community might think.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ameimar Resolution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Ameimar offers a different resolution: {\i aynei rabanan b\u8217'kidushei v\u8217'kidushei ta\u8217'ut havei} \u8212- the Rabbis examined the {\i kiddushin} and determined that it was a mistake from the outset. Rather than framing the situation as a chain of marriages and divorces \u8212- which would produce the appearance of {\i machzir grushaso} \u8212- the Rabbis retroactively characterize Shimon\u8217's {\i kiddushin} as an error, a marriage that was never valid. When the woman returns to Reuven, the community\u8217's understanding will simply be that she was always married to Reuven and that the marriage to Shimon never took legal effect.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 According to {\i Tosfos}, this is the ruling we follow. It is, as noted, still somewhat unfortunate that we must accommodate the perception of reality at all \u8212- but Ameimar\u8217's approach at least avoids artificially restricting the available options by nullifying the second marriage conceptually rather than through a second {\i get}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Competing Rumors\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Papa Initial\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A new scenario: the case of two competing {\i kolot}. On one day, a strong {\i kol} emerged that the woman had married Reuven; on another day, an equally strong {\i kol} arose that she had married Shimon. These are pure rumors \u8212- there was no witnessed act of {\i kiddushin} as there was in the previous case \u8212- and we assume that the investigation has not resolved either rumor\u8217's truth or falsity.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Papa\u8217's initial position, following Rav Huna\u8217's logic, is that even here the same restrictions apply: {\i af zu megaresh rishon venosay sheni} \u8212- even in this case, she is limited to the option of Reuven divorcing her and her then marrying Shimon. She may not have Shimon divorce her and return to Reuven, for the same concern about the appearance of {\i machzir grushaso}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ameimar Two Rumors\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Ameimar disagrees: {\i mutteres lishneihem} \u8212- she is permitted to either of them. With two competing rumors, the concern for {\i machzir grushaso} does not apply. Since both marriages rest entirely on the level of {\i kol}, the community will not construct a definitive narrative of married-divorced-married-divorced. One of the rumors must by necessity be false for any coherent story to emerge, and therefore no perception of {\i machzir grushaso} can credibly take hold.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two Rumors Ruling\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i halakha} follows Ameimar: {\i mutteres lishneihem} \u8212- when there are two competing rumors that remain unresolved, she is permitted to either man. We do not give both rumors such absolute weight that we impose the limitation of {\i machzir grushaso} on her options. She may receive a {\i get} from Shimon and remain with Reuven, or vice versa, as circumstances warrant. All of this, it bears noting, remains unfortunate to whatever degree \u8212- particularly according to the position that does not negate a {\i kol} even when the facts are established \u8212- that we must at all accommodate the perception of reality alongside the reality itself.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Grounds for Divorce\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Beit Shammai\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 We now turn to the last {\i daf} of {\i Gittin}, 90a, and arrive at a {\i mishna} that forms a fitting capstone to the entire {\i maseches}: after an entire tractate devoted to the technicalities and legal mechanics of the {\i get} \u8212- its writing, delivery, agency, and conditions \u8212- we finally reach the question of which circumstances actually warrant a divorce in the first place.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} records the famous three-way dispute. Beit Shammai states: {\i lo yegaresh adam et ishto ela im ken matza bah devar ervah} \u8212- a man should not divorce his wife unless he has found in her a matter of {\i ervah}, sexual sin. The scriptural basis is the verse in Deuteronomy that serves as the Torah\u8217's primary reference to divorce: {\i ki matza bah ervat davar}. The entire Torah\u8217's treatment of {\i get} is embedded within the laws of {\i machzir grushaso} \u8212- the prohibition on a man remarrying his divorced wife after she has wed another. In framing the initial divorce, the Torah describes two conditions: {\i ve-haya im lo timtza chen be-einav} \u8212- if she no longer finds favor in his eyes \u8212- and {\i ki matza bah ervat davar} \u8212- because he has found in her a matter of sexual impropriety. Beit Shammai focuses exclusively on the latter: actual adultery, or the equivalent thereof. Only when the woman has committed an act that renders it halachically forbidden for them to remain together \u8212- when there is real evidentiary basis for sexual transgression \u8212- is divorce truly warranted.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is important to observe that Beit Shammai is not saying that a {\i get} given for lesser reasons is legally void. The entire framing of the {\i mishna} is one of guidelines \u8212- of what is appropriate \u8212- not of legal validity. A {\i get} does not require court approval; it is in essence an act between the two parties. It does not need to be judicially warranted for it to take effect. What Beit Shammai is articulating is a moral and normative standard: {\i lo yegaresh}, a man ought not divorce his wife except under these circumstances.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Beit Hillel Leniency\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Beit Hillel permits divorce for any matter that displeases the husband \u8212- {\i afilu hikdicha tavshilo} \u8212- even if she merely burned his soup or over-spiced his stew. Rashi notes that this can mean either that the food was actually scorched or simply that it was seasoned in a way he did not like. Either way, the most trivial domestic misstep suffices. The scriptural hook, as we will see in the Gemara\u8217's analysis, is the word {\i davar} in {\i ki matza bah ervat davar}: {\i matza bah davar} \u8212- he found something, anything, that he did not like.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might note that the characterization of Beit Hillel as {\i le-kula} here requires some nuance. If the dynamic is one in which the wife wishes to remain married and the husband acts capriciously, Beit Hillel\u8217's ruling is hardly kind to the wife. At the same time, it is {\i le-kula} in the formal sense of operating with fewer restrictions \u8212- and of course the dynamic is not always as described, for it may be the wife who wishes the marriage to end.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Akiva Permissive\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Akiva extends the grounds for divorce even further: {\i afilu matza acheret na\u8217'ah heimena} \u8212- even if he has simply found another woman more beautiful, or more pleasing to him. His scriptural basis is the opening clause of the verse \u8212- {\i ve-haya im lo timtza chen be-einav}, if she no longer finds favor in his eyes \u8212- without requiring that she has done anything wrong at all. The wife may be entirely blameless and a model spouse; if her husband has become more attracted to another woman, that is sufficient grounds.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is a genuinely difficult position to read with equanimity. Some have sought to connect Rabbi Akiva\u8217's view with what we know of his biographical relationship with his own wife, his famous deep and passionate love \u8212- reading him perhaps as a romantic who believes one must always follow one\u8217's heart and remain passionately devoted to one\u8217's spouse, so that a loss of that passion is itself grounds for dissolution. But that is a generous reading, and it is worth acknowledging the difficulty. Rabbi Akiva\u8217's position here also connects to his rulings in other areas \u8212- for instance, regarding a woman\u8217's appearance during periods when physical intimacy is restricted \u8212- which reflect an orientation that the wife bears a significant responsibility to ensure she remains attractive and pleasing to her husband. A position that allows a man to remarry anyone who pleases him more places a correspondingly heavy burden on the wife to remain perpetually desirable. These are matters that invite serious reflection in light of contemporary sensibilities.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Textual Analysis\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Beit Hillel Exegesis\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now records a {\i baraita} that traces the hermeneutical exchange between Beit Shammai and Beit Hillel. Beit Hillel opens: {\i v\u8217'lo kvar neemar davar?} \u8212- does the verse not already say {\i davar}, which denotes any matter? Beit Shammai responds: {\i v\u8217'lo kvar neemar ervat?} \u8212- but does it not also say {\i ervat}, which denotes sexual sin?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Beit Hillel then offers the fuller argument. If the Torah had written only {\i ervat} and not {\i davar}, one might have thought: a man may divorce his wife on account of sexual sin, but not for any ordinary matter. And if the Torah had written only {\i davar} and not {\i ervat}, one might have thought: if he divorces her for some ordinary matter, she may go and remarry another man \u8212- but if he divorces her because she has committed adultery, perhaps she is not entitled to remarry at all. For the continuation of the verse reads {\i ve-halchah ve-haytah le-ish acher} \u8212- and she went and became the wife of another man. One might have supposed that this permission to remarry applies only to a woman divorced for trivial reasons, not for adultery. Therefore, says Beit Hillel, both terms are necessary: {\i ervat} teaches that even a woman divorced for adultery may remarry (any man other than the adulterer), and {\i davar} teaches that even the slightest matter suffices as a ground for divorce.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Beit Shammai Witnesses\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 For Beit Shammai, the challenge is to account for the word {\i davar} without conceding Beit Hillel\u8217's reading. The Gemara asks: {\i Beit Shammai, hai davar mai avid lei?} \u8212- what do Beit Shammai do with the word {\i davar}? Their answer is a {\i gezerah shavah}: the word {\i davar} appears here, and the same word appears elsewhere \u8212- {\i al pi shnayim edim o al pi shlosha edim yakum davar} \u8212- a matter is established by the testimony of two or three witnesses. Just as in that context {\i davar} requires two witnesses, so too here: the {\i davar} of {\i ervat davar} teaches that adultery must be established through the testimony of two witnesses. The word {\i davar} is not a second independent ground for divorce; it is a procedural requirement attached to the sole ground, {\i ervah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 One vs.\u160 ?Two Grounds\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The debate then sharpens into a linguistic question about the phrase {\i ervat davar} itself. Beit Hillel presses: {\i mi ktiv ervah b\u8217'davar?} \u8212- the text does not say {\i ervah} was found \u8220"through\u8221" {\i davar}, through testimony. It says {\i ervat davar} as a construct phrase. Therefore {\i davar} cannot be merely the mechanism for establishing {\i ervah}; it must be a separate item. Beit Shammai replies: {\i mi ktiv o ervah o davar?} \u8212- but the text does not say \u8220"either {\i ervah} or {\i davar}.\u8221" The phrase is {\i ervat davar}, a single unified construct, not two alternatives.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Beit Hillel\u8217's final response: {\i hachi ktiv ervat davar, d\u8217'mashma hachi u\u8217'mashma hachi} \u8212- yes, it is written as a single phrase, but it is precisely this dual resonance that is the point. The phrase simultaneously conveys: {\i ervah} alone (adultery as grounds) and {\i davar} alone (any matter as grounds), with the construct form serving to indicate that even in the case of {\i ervah}, the subsequent permission to remarry applies. The phrase thus carries both meanings at once.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 For Beit Shammai, this linguistic analysis reinforces that {\i davar} is not an independent ground at all: the requirement for {\i edut} \u8212- two witnesses \u8212- to establish adultery is the very content the word {\i davar} imports. Their reading is that {\i ervat davar} is a single concept: an actual, evidenced act of adultery. Only under those circumstances does the Torah envision divorce as truly warranted.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Get Validity Issues\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Unjustified Divorce\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having established that the grounds for divorce are debated \u8212- {\i ervah} alone (Beit Shammai), any {\i davar} (Beit Hillel), or even mere loss of attraction (Rabbi Akiva) \u8212- the Gemara now addresses the threshold question underlying the entire {\i mishna}: what if a man divorces his wife with none of these justifications? There was no {\i ervah}, no {\i davar}, nothing at all by even Beit Hillel\u8217's standards. Does the {\i get} take effect, or is the man required to remarry her? Rav Papa poses this question to Rava.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Get Effective\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava responds by invoking the law of the rapist: the Torah states {\i lo yuchal leshalcha kol yamav} \u8212- the man who raped a woman and was compelled to marry her may never divorce her. If he does give a {\i get}, he is required to remarry her \u8212- {\i kol yamav b\u8217'omed ve-hachzir}. Rava\u8217's reasoning: the Torah had to state explicitly that the rapist cannot divorce his wife. This implies that in any other case, even if the divorce transgresses the guidelines articulated in the {\i mishna}, {\i mai de\u8217'avad avad} \u8212- what he has done is done. The {\i get} is valid. The {\i mishna} deals entirely with normative guidelines; it does not define the conditions under which a {\i get} legally functions.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Secret Divorce Plan\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Mesharshiya then raises a related concern \u8212- not a legal one, but an ethical one. He poses the following to Rava: if a man has decided in his heart that he intends to divorce his wife, but conceals this from her \u8212- while she continues to live in his home, serve him, prepare his meals, share his bed \u8212- what is the moral status of that conduct?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava\u8217's response is a scriptural citation: {\i al tacharosh al re\u8217'acha ra ve-hu yoshev lavetach itach} \u8212- do not plot evil against your friend when he dwells with you in trust. The word {\i re\u8217'im} \u8212- friends \u8212- evokes {\i re\u8217'im ha-ahuvim}, the beloved companions of the marriage bond. The betrayal of plotting divorce while one\u8217's wife tends to the home in full trust constitutes a profound breach of the foundational human relationship. It is not a {\i halakhic} violation \u8212- the {\i get} will still work \u8212- but the moral condemnation is severe.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Food & Wife Analogy\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Analogy Framework\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i baraita} is then cited: {\i haya Rabbi Meir omer, kashem she\u8217'hade\u8217'ot b\u8217'ma\u8217'achal kach de\u8217'ot banashim} \u8212- Rabbi Meir would say: just as people differ in their attitudes toward food, so too they differ in their attitudes toward wives. The comparison of food and marital intimacy as analogous areas of human desire is not uncommon in rabbinic literature, though here the analogy focuses less on desire per se and more on fastidiousness and standards of tolerance. Three types of men are described.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Fastidious Type\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The first is the extremely fastidious man. A fly falls into his cup and he throws out the entire cup \u8212- a minor contamination renders the whole thing unacceptable. The {\i baraita} identifies this with the behavior of Pappos ben Yehuda: when he left his house, he would lock the door and keep his wife confined inside. The analogy to extreme fastidiousness is clear \u8212- he could not bear the thought of his wife having any interaction with the outside world, anyone who might constitute, in his mind, any form of contamination. As Rashi notes at the end of his commentary on this passage: {\i umida she\u8217'eina hogenet hee zo shemitoch kach eiva nichneset beineihem umezana tachtav} \u8212- this is an unseemly trait, for from such conduct hatred and enmity enter between them, and it actually leads to the very outcome he feared most. By imprisoning his wife in jealousy and suspicion, he generates the conditions for what he dreads. And beyond that consequence, the controlling and abusive nature of such a relationship requires no commentary.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Normal & Permissive\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The second type is the ordinary person \u8212- {\i midat kol adam}. The fly falls into his cup; he removes the fly and drinks the cup. In the context of marriage: his wife speaks with acquaintances, neighbors, and relatives, and he is not troubled by it. He does not allow groundless jealousy to poison the relationship. This is presented as the normative standard.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The third type is the morally deficient man. The fly falls into the stew \u8212- the {\i tamchuy} \u8212- and he wrings the fly dry to extract all the juices absorbed from the stew, and then eats the stew. He is wholly indifferent to contamination, even when the contamination is substantial. In the marital context: his wife goes out with her head uncovered, spins in the marketplace in a way that draws attention and exposes her arms, wears clothing slit up the sides, and bathes in places shared with men \u8212- and he is unbothered. The Gemara clarifies: bathing literally together with men? Rather, she bathes in the same river or area where men bathe, overlapping in proximity in a way that is far too intimate. The {\i baraita} concludes: {\i zu mitzvah min hatorah le-garesha} \u8212- in such a case it is a {\i mitzvah} ordained by the Torah to divorce her.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Immodest Conduct\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Improper Behaviors\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The behaviors enumerated in the preceding {\i baraita} \u8212- going out with her head uncovered, spinning in the marketplace, wearing garments slit up the sides, bathing in shared spaces \u8212- constitute the category of {\i ervat davar} in a broad sense. This is the same rubric discussed in {\i Ketubot} under the categories of {\i dat Moshe} and {\i dat Yehudit}: behaviors that violate either Mosaic law or the standards of Jewish communal norms as they govern the conduct expected within marriage. It should be noted that while the {\i mishna} and this {\i baraita} address women\u8217's behavior exclusively, the same logic applies to conduct by husbands that draws inappropriate attention or constitutes disrespect toward the marital bond.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mitzva to Divorce\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 When a woman engages in the behaviors enumerated above, the {\i baraita} states that it is a {\i mitzvah min hatorah} to divorce her \u8212- {\i she-garsha, she-ne\u8217'emar ki matza bah ervat davar}. The verse {\i ki matza bah ervat davar} here is read not necessarily as evidence of actual adultery, but as encompassing the broader category of sexually immodest or inappropriate conduct that undermines the integrity of the marriage.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Second Husband Fate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i baraita} continues with a {\i midrashic} reading of the continuation of the verse: {\i ve-halchah ve-haytah le-ish acher} \u8212- she goes and becomes the wife of another man. {\i Hakatuv kera\u8217'o acher} \u8212- the Torah calls him {\i acher}, different or \u8220"other,\u8221" to indicate that he is not of the same quality as the first husband. The first husband did the right thing \u8212- {\i hotzi rishah mibeito}, he removed this woman whose conduct was shameful from his house. The second man \u8212- the {\i acher} \u8212- {\i hichnis rishah le-toch beito}, brings her into his house. If the second man merits it, he will eventually also send her away \u8212- {\i she-ne\u8217'emar ve-saneiah ha-ish ha-acharon}. And if he does not merit it: {\i k\u8217'vartahu} \u8212- she will bury him, as the verse says {\i o ki yamut ha-ish ha-acharon}, or the last husband dies. {\i Kedai hu be-mitah}, he is deserving of death, for the first man expelled this wicked woman from his home, and this man brought her back in.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Malachi Dispute\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Yehuda\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now turns to a {\i pasuk} in {\i Malachi} \u8212- {\i ki sanei shalach} \u8212- which is read as bearing on the question of when divorce is appropriate. Rabbi Yehuda reads the verse permissively: {\i im saneisah shalach} \u8212- if you hate her, send her away. Rashi understands this as aligned with Rabbi Akiva \u8212- or perhaps Beit Hillel \u8212- that subjective aversion, even without any wrongdoing on the wife\u8217's part, suffices as grounds.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Yochanan\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yochanan reads the same verse in the opposite direction: {\i sanui ha-meshaleiach} \u8212- the one who sends her away is himself to be despised. On this reading, the act of divorce is condemned; a man who divorces his wife is hateful. This aligns with a Beit Shammai ethos: divorce without genuine justification is not merely inadvisable but morally contemptible.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 God\u8217's Hate Standard\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 An alternative textual reading ({\i girsa}) of the verse is: {\i snuah hi lifnei ha-makom shalach} \u8212- only if she is hated before God \u8212- i.e., if she has committed adultery, a sin that makes her hateful in God\u8217's eyes \u8212- should you send her away. This is the most restrictive reading, corresponding most closely to Beit Shammai\u8217's position.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Marriage Distinction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then offers a resolution: {\i v\u8217'lo pligi} \u8212- the {\i tannaim} do not ultimately disagree. Rather, their statements apply to different situations. {\i Ha b\u8217'zug rishon, ha b\u8217'zug sheini} \u8212- one refers to the first marriage, the other to the second. The permissive reading (Rabbi Yehuda) may be applied in the case of a second marriage; the restrictive reading (Rabbi Yochanan), which treats the one who divorces as despicable, is specifically addressed to the first marriage. This resolution flows naturally into the teaching of Rabbi Elazar that follows.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 First Marriage Sanctity\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Altar\u8217's Tears\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Elazar teaches: {\i kol ha-megaresh ishto rishonah afilu mizbe\u8217'ach morid alav d\u8217'maos} \u8212- whoever divorces his first wife, even the altar itself sheds tears over him. The image is striking: the physical altar, the site of sacrifice and divine service, mourns the dissolution of a first marriage.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Malachi Midrash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The scriptural basis is the verse in {\i Malachi} (2): {\i ve-zos sheinis ta\u8217'asu, ksos d\u8217'mah et mizbe\u8217'ach Hashem b\u8217'chi va-anakah, mei-ein od penos el ha-minchah v\u8217'lakchas ratzon mi-yedchem} \u8212- this second thing you do, covering the altar of God with weeping and wailing, so that He no longer turns to the offering or accepts it with favor from your hands. And when the people ask {\i al mah}, on what account, the verse answers: {\i al ki Hashem he\u8217'id beinecha u-vein eishet neurecha asher atah bagadtah bah} \u8212- because God has testified between you and the wife of your youth, against whom you have betrayed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Covenant Rupture\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The theological weight of this verse is profound. The rupture of the marital bond \u8212- specifically the betrayal of the wife of one\u8217's youth \u8212- disrupts one\u8217's relationship with God. The altar weeps; God turns away from the offerings. The verse then identifies the wife with two terms: {\i v\u8217'hi chavertecha v\u8217'eishet britecha} \u8212- she is your friend, your companion, and the woman of your covenant. The marriage to the first wife is cast as a covenant \u8212- {\i brit} \u8212- a foundational relationship of partnership and commitment. Betraying that covenant sends ripples outward, all the way to the altar of God.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The resolution the Gemara offers \u8212- that Rabbi Yehuda speaks of a second marriage and Rabbi Yochanan of a first \u8212- is precisely calibrated to this teaching. When we speak of a second marriage, there is greater latitude; the standards of Beit Hillel or even Rabbi Akiva might apply. But when it is the first marriage \u8212- the {\i zug rishon}, the foundational relationship, the {\i eishet brit} \u8212- the bar is incomparably higher, and to dissolve it for trivial reasons is to be considered despicable, {\i sanui ha-meshaleiach}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Modern Balance\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is a beautiful and powerful teaching, and it is worth sitting with its imagery. At the same time, the principle must be handled with care in its practical application. The concern that divorce not be undertaken trivially must not become a cudgel used against people who have arrived at that painful decision through a long and responsible process. There is a real phenomenon \u8212- reflected in personal testimonies and public discourse, including in the Orthodox world \u8212- of people who have carefully deliberated and sought guidance over extended periods, who finally appear before a {\i beit din}, and are made to feel contemptible for being there at all. Sometimes divorce is the right and even necessary decision. The tradition\u8217's emphasis on the gravity of first-marriage dissolution must be balanced against sensitivity to the human beings who stand before the {\i dayan}, and against the reality that prolonging an irreparably broken marriage serves no one.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Partnership Ideal\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is fitting to close on the phrase with which {\i Malachi} describes the wife: {\i chavertecha v\u8217'eishet britecha} \u8212- your friend and the wife of your covenant. Despite everything in this final chapter \u8212- the unilateral power of the husband to initiate divorce, the analogies to food and property, the male gaze that pervades so much of this discussion \u8212- this phrase articulates an ideal of marriage as equal partnership, as friendship, as covenant. Remarkably, {\i ketubot} discovered in the Cairo {\i Genizah} employ this very language to describe the marital bond: {\i ishti ve\u8217'eishet briti, chaverti ve\u8217'eishet briti} \u8212- my wife and the wife of my covenant, my companion and the wife of my covenant. Even in an era when many of the legal structures of marriage were heavily asymmetric, the covenantal ideal of {\i chevratcha v\u8217'eishet britecha} persisted as the animating vision of what marriage is meant to be. It is a fitting note on which to conclude {\i maseches Gittin}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Gittin 90\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Kol: Substantiation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kol Requires Beit Din\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 We pick up on 89b in Gittin, in the middle of a discussion about the {\i kol} \u8212- the rumor or report \u8212- that a woman has contracted a marriage. The {\i mishna} established that when such a {\i kol} circulates, we must be concerned about it and not permit the woman to marry someone else without a {\i get} from the man she was rumored to have married. The {\i gemara} has been probing exactly when that concern applies: how much substantiation a {\i kol} requires, whether we are {\i mevatlinan kala} \u8212- whether we proactively negate the {\i kol} once we discover it was unfounded \u8212- and what the practical consequences of a {\i kol} are once it has been established.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 We now pick up with the ruling of Rav Ashi. The {\i gemara} states: {\i kol kol d\u8217'lo it-chazek b\u8217'vei dina lav kola hu} \u8212- any rumor that the {\i beis din} has not investigated and formally established as a matter of concern is not a {\i kol} to be reckoned with. This reinforces the earlier principle that substantiating circumstances alone are not sufficient; the {\i beis din} must actively examine the matter and declare it worthy of concern. Rashi explains: {\i lo chashinan lei ela im ken yadu beis din ha-ir she-kach yatza kol ploni me-hekhan shama ve-gazru she-yeichushu le-kol zeh} \u8212- we are not concerned about the {\i kol} unless the {\i beis din} of the city investigated the rumor, determined its source, and formally ruled that it must be attended to.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kol Achar Nisuin\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Ashi adds a second ruling: {\i kol kol d\u8217'vasar nisuin lo chashinan lei} \u8212- any {\i kol} that arises after the woman is already married is not a {\i kol} we attend to. Once she is already living with her husband, a rumor about her prior status \u8212- that she had been married to someone else, or had committed some act that would render her forbidden to a {\i kohen} \u8212- is disregarded, because acting on it would impact the man she is currently married to.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then qualifies: {\i d\u8217'vasar erusin chashinan lei} \u8212- but if the {\i kol} arose after {\i erusin} but before {\i nisuin}, when there is a formal bond but the couple has not yet begun living together, we would still be concerned. Rav Chaviva, however, goes further: {\i afilu d\u8217'vasar erusin nami lo chashinan lei} \u8212- even a {\i kol} arising after {\i erusin} is disregarded, since even at that stage there is already an existing relationship being impacted by the rumor. The {\i halacha} follows Rav Chaviva: once {\i erusin} has taken effect, a {\i kol} that emerges thereafter is not acted upon.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Kol + Kiddushin\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Case Setup\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now introduces a case sent from the academy of Rav to Shmuel: {\i yatza lah kol me-rishon u-va acher ve-kiddeshah kiddushei Torah mahu} \u8212- a {\i kol} went out that this woman had been married to Reuven, and then Shimon came and gave her {\i kiddushin} in a full and public manner \u8212- {\i kiddushei Torah}, witnessed {\i kiddushin} performed openly \u8212- in disregard of the existing {\i kol}. What should be done?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is a case in which the {\i kol} was presumably of the serious, substantiated variety that would ordinarily require concern, and yet Shimon proceeded to marry her anyway. The question is how we now navigate the tangle of her status.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tetzei Response\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Shmuel\u8217's reply is: {\i teitzei} \u8212- she must leave the second man. And then: {\i ve-ha-amidu ha-davar al buryo} \u8212- clarify the underlying facts. But what precisely does this instruction to \u8220"clarify\u8221" entail?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Investigation: Eidus\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A first reading: perhaps it means we investigate and discover that the first marriage was in fact not valid. There was, say, a strong {\i kol} supported by circumstantial evidence \u8212- everyone was carrying candles, everyone celebrated what looked like a wedding \u8212- but no formal {\i eidus}. Upon investigation we find, say, that the ring was not worth a {\i shaveh prutah} and the {\i kiddushin} was defective. In that case, {\i mevatlinan kala}, we would set aside the {\i kol} and allow the situation to resolve itself in Shimon\u8217's favor.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But this reading is rejected: {\i v\u8217'ha Neharde\u8217'a atreih de-Shmuel hu, v\u8217'lo mevatlinan kala} \u8212- Neharde\u8217'a was Shmuel\u8217's own city, and the practice of Neharde\u8217'a is that we are {\i not mevatlinan kala}. This is a striking ruling. Even when {\i beis din} determines that the {\i kol} was factually false, the practice in Neharde\u8217'a is still not to proactively negate it. The reason touches on a fundamental principle: the only difference between reality and perception is that it is far easier to change reality than perception. Even if {\i beis din} knows the truth, they cannot rule in a way that openly contradicts the public\u8217's settled understanding of the situation. Therefore, even in such a case, Shimon would still be required to give a {\i get}, since everyone believes the woman is married to someone else.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The correct reading of \u8220"clarify the matter\u8221" must therefore be the opposite: we investigate and find witnesses who can testify that the first marriage to Reuven was, in fact, genuine and valid. In that case, {\i lo tzricha get mi-sheni} \u8212- she does not need a {\i get} from Shimon at all. If the first marriage is proven by {\i eidus}, Shimon\u8217's {\i kiddushin} was simply void, and she returns to Reuven without any further action.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 To summarize the ruling: the {\i teitzei} is absolute \u8212- she cannot remain with Shimon regardless. The only question is whether she needs a {\i get} from Shimon. If there is only a {\i kol} and no firm evidence of her marriage to Reuven, she needs a {\i get} from Shimon before returning. If the marriage to Reuven is established by testimony, she needs no {\i get} from Shimon whatsoever.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Kol + Kiddushin (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Perception Issue\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This ruling is noted to be in conflict with the position of Rav Huna. Rav Huna holds, based on a principle of Rav Hamnuna, that if a married woman reaches out and accepts {\i kiddushin} from another man, she is considered at least {\i safek mekudeshes} to the second man \u8212- because a woman who does such a thing is, in effect, declaring to her husband that he has divorced her. Rav Hamnuna\u8217's principle is: {\i ha-ishah she-amrah le-ba\u8217'alah geirashtani ne\u8217'emenet, chazakah ein ishah me\u8217'izah paneha bifnei ba\u8217'alah} \u8212- a woman who tells her husband directly to his face \u8220"you have divorced me\u8221" is believed, since we presume no woman would be so brazen if it were untrue. Accepting {\i kiddushin} from Shimon is an act tantamount to making that declaration.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If we were to rule according to Rav Huna, then even in a case where we know for a fact that the woman is married to Reuven, the act of accepting {\i kiddushin} from Shimon would minimally require a {\i get} from Shimon. Yet Shmuel\u8217's ruling is that in such a case she does not need a {\i get} from Shimon at all.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} resolves the tension: Rav Hamnuna\u8217's principle applies only when the act of accepting {\i kiddushin} \u8212- or making the declaration \u8212- occurs in her husband\u8217's presence, {\i be-fanav}. It is specifically because the husband is present and does not protest that we infer the divorce must be real. In our case, the woman accepted {\i kiddushin} from Shimon {\i she-lo be-fanav} \u8212- not in Reuven\u8217's presence \u8212- and therefore the act carries no such inferential weight. If we know as fact she is married to Reuven, Shimon\u8217's {\i kiddushin} is meaningless, no {\i get} is required from Shimon, and she simply returns to being married to Reuven. Tosafot notes that this point \u8212- that the principle of Rav Hamnuna requires the husband\u8217's presence \u8212- was never actually in dispute; the discussion here merely clarifies that even absent that concern, no {\i get} is needed from Shimon when the first marriage is established by evidence.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Huna View\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having addressed the confirmed-marriage scenario, the {\i gemara} turns to the unresolved case: {\i lo matza davar barur ve-ma hu} \u8212- investigation was conducted but no definitive conclusion was reached. The {\i kol} remains as it was, neither confirmed nor refuted, and Shimon has already given her {\i kiddushin}. What now?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Huna rules: {\i megaresh rishon ve-nosei sheni} \u8212- the first man, Reuven, must divorce her, and then she may marry the second man, Shimon. This is how Rav Huna understands the instruction {\i teitzei}: she leaves Reuven (through a {\i get} from him), and that resolves the {\i kol}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Machzir Grushaso\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But Rav Huna also rules: {\i u-megaresh sheni ve-nosei rishon lo} \u8212- we would not permit the reverse arrangement, whereby Shimon divorces her and she returns to marry Reuven. Why not? {\i Asi le-meimar machzir grushaso min ha-erusin} \u8212- people will say that Reuven had married her, then divorced her, then Shimon married her, then Shimon divorced her, and now Reuven is taking her back \u8212- which creates the appearance of {\i machzir grushaso}, a prohibited remarriage of a divorced wife. Even though there is no actual prohibition in this case, the appearance of impropriety forecloses this option according to Rav Huna. It is, to put it plainly, unfortunate that we must so constrain our practical options in order to manage public perception. Yet that is Rav Huna\u8217's position.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Idi Disagrees\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The son of Rav Idi \u8212- identified in the discussion of competing rumors as Ashmeimar \u8212- disagrees: {\i af megaresh sheni ve-nosei rishon} \u8212- we are not concerned about the appearance of {\i machzir grushaso}. His reasoning: {\i meimar amri inei rabbanan be-kiddushei ve-kiddushei ta\u8217'us hava} \u8212- people will say the rabbis looked into the matter and determined that Shimon\u8217's {\i kiddushin} was an error, that it was never valid. Since there was already a {\i kol} that she was married to Reuven, and the investigation could not refute it, the public perception will naturally be that Shimon\u8217's marriage was mistaken from the outset \u8212- not that Reuven remarried a woman he had previously divorced. According to Tosafot, the {\i halacha} follows the son of Rav Idi on this point.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Kol: Substantiation (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Lo Mevatlinan Kala\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This section of the discussion crystallizes the position of Neharde\u8217'a regarding {\i mevatlinan kala}. The Neharde\u8217'a position is that we do not negate a {\i kol} \u8212- {\i lo mevatlinan kala} \u8212- even after {\i beis din} has determined that the underlying facts do not support it. As noted above, this reflects the principle that perception, once formed, cannot simply be undone by legal decree. If the {\i kol} had the substantiating features that made it credible in the community\u8217's eyes, {\i beis din} cannot retroactively announce that nothing happened and expect that announcement to undo the public\u8217's settled understanding. The consequence is that even a woman who is in fact free of any prior marriage must still contend with the {\i kol} and all that it requires \u8212- including, in certain scenarios, a {\i get} from a man she was never truly married to.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Competing Rumors\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two Rumors Setup\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now poses a further case: {\i yatza lah kol mi-zeh u-mi-zeh mahu} \u8212- suppose there were not a confirmed {\i kiddushin} by Shimon but rather two competing {\i kolot}. On Monday, a strong {\i kol} emerged that she had married Reuven; on Tuesday, a strong {\i kol} emerged that she had married Shimon. Both {\i kolot} are of the variety that would ordinarily demand concern. We were unable to clarify either one. What are her options?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Papa View\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Papa holds that even in this case of two competing rumors, she remains constrained in the same manner as the single-{\i kol} case under the approach of Rav Huna: {\i af zu megaresh rishon ve-nosei sheni} \u8212- even here, the {\i kol} tied to Reuven must be addressed by Reuven giving a {\i get}, and she may then remain with Shimon. The concern for {\i machzir grushaso} still limits the options to that one arrangement.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ashmeimar View\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Ashmeimar disagrees: {\i muteres li-shneihem} \u8212- she is permitted in relation to both. When both marriages are based purely on rumors and neither is established by evidence, we cannot treat either {\i kol} as so definitive that we become worried about the appearance of {\i machzir grushaso}. Since it is enough for only one of the rumors to have been false in order for her to remain with Reuven without any taint of a prohibited remarriage, we do not impose that constraint here. Both competing {\i kolot} weaken each other, and the strict perception-management that drove Rav Huna\u8217's concern is simply inapplicable when everything is rumor.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Halacha Ruling\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Ve-hilkheta muteres li-shneihem} \u8212- the {\i halacha} follows Ashmeimar: when there are competing {\i kolot} with no established facts, she retains full flexibility and is not restricted to any particular outcome. We must still attend to the {\i kolot} \u8212- they do not simply vanish \u8212- but we will not give both of them such absolute weight as to generate a concern of {\i machzir grushaso}. The understanding is that the public, seeing her return to Reuven, will naturally assume that the marriage to Shimon was never established. This remains, in any case, a less than ideal situation, particularly for those who rule {\i lo mevatlinan kala} \u8212- that even known falsehood must be accommodated as perception. That we are required to manage the appearance of reality even after knowing the truth is, one must acknowledge, genuinely unfortunate.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Grounds for Divorce\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Beit Shammai View\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having concluded an entire tractate devoted to the technical legal act of divorce and the writing of the {\i get}, we arrive at the final {\i mishna} of Gittin, which addresses the circumstances that actually warrant a divorce. It is worth appreciating the framing: the {\i Torah} touches on divorce almost in passing, within the broader prohibition of {\i machzir grushaso}. The verse that frames the entire institution \u8212- {\i ve-hayah im lo timtza chen be-einav ki matza bah ervat davar}, \u8220"it shall be if she does not find favor in his eyes because he found in her {\i ervat davar}\u8221" \u8212- arises primarily to set the context for why the first husband divorced her, before the {\i Torah} goes on to forbid the first husband from remarrying her.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Beit Shammai reads: {\i lo yegaresh adam et ishto ella im ken matza bah devar ervah, shene\u8217'emar ki matza bah ervat davar} \u8212- a man should not divorce his wife unless he has found actual {\i ervat davar} in her, a matter of sexual sin, as it is written that \u8220"he found in her {\i ervat davar}.\u8221" The language of {\i matza bah} \u8212- \u8220"he found in her\u8221" \u8212- suggests actual discovery, something known to have occurred. Beit Shammai therefore grounds the warrant for divorce in genuine adultery, or at the very least demonstrated sexual impropriety.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is important to note what this discussion is and is not about. The question of whether a {\i get} is {\i valid} \u8212- whether it effects a divorce \u8212- is a separate matter entirely. The {\i get} is not like a court-issued civil divorce that requires fault; it is an act performed between husband and wife, and it does not require the approval or certification of {\i beis din}. The {\i mishna} is addressing what is {\i appropriate}, what is {\i warranted}, what the {\i Torah} is saying a man should do \u8212- not whether the {\i get} will take effect if he acts outside those parameters. The entire discussion is framed in terms of guidelines for proper conduct, not legal preconditions for validity.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Beit Hillel View\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Beit Hillel holds: {\i afilu hikdicha tavshilo} \u8212- even if she burned his soup, that is sufficient grounds for divorce. Rashi understands this broadly \u8212- too many spices, or simply something done to annoy him. The textual support: {\i shene\u8217'emar ki matza bah ervat davar} \u8212- the same verse, but Beit Hillel focuses not on {\i ervah} but on {\i davar}, \u8220"any matter,\u8221" any small thing that offended him. One might note, and the {\i gemara} will address this, that Beit Hillel\u8217's position is lenient in the particular sense of expanding human freedom and reducing legal restrictions \u8212- though whether it is beneficial to the wife is another question entirely, depending on whose interests one has in view.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Akiva View\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Akiva goes furthest: {\i afilu matza acheret na\u8217'ah mimennah, shene\u8217'emar ve-hayah im lo timtza chen be-einav} \u8212- even if he simply found someone more beautiful or more pleasing than she is, that is grounds for divorce. The wife need not have done anything wrong whatsoever; she may be the perfect wife in every respect. Yet if the husband is attracted to another woman, the verse says \u8220"if she does not find favor in his eyes\u8221" \u8212- and that subjective failure of attraction is, according to Rabbi Akiva, sufficient.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is a position that invites real reflection. Rabbi Akiva is, of course, the {\i tanna} whose love for his wife is among the most celebrated in all of rabbinic literature \u8212- the man whose wife Rachel sacrificed everything for him and whom he revered and adored in return. Some have tried to read his position here in that light: perhaps he is a romantic who believes one must always be passionately in love, and therefore if that feeling is gone, the marriage should end. But the position does create real difficulties in terms of protecting the interests of the wife, and it also connects to Rabbi Akiva\u8217's rulings elsewhere \u8212- regarding certain practices during {\i niddah} and on {\i Tisha B\u8217'Av} \u8212- where he emphasizes that a wife must take care not to become displeasing to her husband, {\i she-lo titganeh al ba\u8217'alah}. If a husband is understood to have the right to leave whenever he finds another woman more attractive, that places an enormous and asymmetric burden on the wife to always present herself favorably. This is something to genuinely reckon with in light of contemporary sensibilities.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Gemara Analysis\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Davar vs.\u160 ?Ervah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now records a {\i baraita} in which Beit Hillel and Beit Shammai engage each other directly. Beit Hillel says to Beit Shammai: does it not say {\i davar} \u8212- \u8220"any matter\u8221" \u8212- which proves that even minor things suffice? Beit Shammai replies: does it not say {\i ervat} \u8212- sexual impropriety \u8212- which proves it must be something serious? Beit Hillel then explains why both words are necessary: had the verse said only {\i ervat} and not {\i davar}, I might have thought a woman can be divorced for {\i ervah} but not for some lesser {\i davar}. Had it said only {\i davar} and not {\i ervat}, I might have thought that if she remarries after being divorced for a {\i davar}, she is still permitted to marry anyone \u8212- but that if divorced for adultery, she is forbidden to marry anyone else. Since the verse goes on to say {\i ve-halkhah ve-haytah le-ish acher} \u8212- \u8220"she went and became the wife of another man\u8221" \u8212- we needed {\i ervat} precisely to teach that even in the case of adultery she may remarry. The double language thus serves two purposes: {\i davar} extends the grounds for divorce, and {\i ervat} teaches that even an adulteress may remarry (anyone other than the adulterer, as {\i halacha} specifies).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What then does Beit Shammai do with the word {\i davar}? They apply the {\i gezeirah shavah}: {\i ne\u8217'emar kan davar ve-ne\u8217'emar le-ilan davar al pi shnayim eidim o al pi shloshah eidim yakum davar} \u8212- the word {\i davar} appears here and also in the verse requiring two or three witnesses to establish \u8220"a matter.\u8221" Just as there, a matter is established through testimony, so here, the {\i ervah} must be established through witnesses. Beit Shammai thus uses {\i davar} not to expand the grounds for divorce but to impose an evidentiary standard: adultery must be proven through formal testimony before it constitutes grounds for divorce.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Interpretation of Pasuk\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then asks: what is the underlying dispute? Reish Lakish had established that the word {\i ki} in the {\i Torah} carries four meanings: {\i im} (if), {\i dilma} (lest), {\i ela} (rather), and {\i de-ha} (because). Rashi illustrates these with several examples; notably, both {\i ela} and {\i de-ha} appear in proximity in the exchange between Sarah and Avraham \u8212- Sarah saying {\i ki yareti} (because she was afraid) and Avraham responding {\i lo ki tzachakt} (rather, you did laugh). The dispute between the positions turns on how one reads the word {\i ki} in this verse.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Beit Shammai Reading\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Beit Shammai reads the entire clause as a single, unified statement: {\i ve-hayah im lo timtza chen be-einav} describes the lack of favor, and {\i ki matza bah ervat davar} explains why she does not find favor \u8212- \u8220"because\u8221" ({\i de-ha}) he found {\i ervat davar} in her. On this reading, the {\i Torah} presents one scenario: she has committed adultery, and that is why he no longer finds favor with her. The lack of favor is not an independent ground; it is the consequence of the adultery. There is thus only one condition that justifies divorce.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Akiva Reading\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Akiva reads the verse as containing two entirely separate statements: {\i im lo timtza chen be-einav} \u8212- if she does not find favor \u8212- stands alone as an independent ground for divorce, and {\i ki matza bah ervat davar} \u8212- if he found {\i ervat davar} \u8212- is a second, separate ground. The {\i ki} here means {\i im}, \u8220"if.\u8221" On this reading, the verse presents two distinct cases, either of which independently suffices. Beit Hillel\u8217's reading, it should be noted, is closer to Beit Shammai\u8217's in structure \u8212- they too do not read {\i lo timtza chen} as an independent clause \u8212- and it is only Rabbi Akiva who fully separates the two conditions into two freestanding grounds for divorce.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Get Mechanics\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Get Validity\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Pappa asks Rava: if a man divorces his wife with no justification at all \u8212- no {\i ervah}, no {\i davar} even in Beit Hillel\u8217's broad sense \u8212- does the {\i get} take effect? The question itself confirms what was said earlier: the grounds for divorce discussed in the {\i mishna} are matters of propriety and guideline, not legal preconditions. Rava\u8217's answer, however, engages the question from a different angle.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rapist Cannot Divorce\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava says: {\i degali Rachmana gabei ones} \u8212- the Torah made a specific disclosure regarding the rapist ({\i ones}), stating {\i lo yukhal leshalchah kol yamav} \u8212- \u8220"he may not send her away all his days.\u8221" The {\i gemara} understands this as {\i bekum ve-hachzeir}: if a man who raped a woman and was compelled to marry her then divorces her, he must take her back. That is, the Torah explicitly imposes on the rapist not just the prohibition of divorcing but an obligation to re-marry if he does. The implication: {\i hatam hu de-gali Rachmana} \u8212- it was {\i there}, in the case of the rapist, that the Torah made explicit that the {\i get} does not stand; {\i aval hacha mai de-avad avad} \u8212- here, in the ordinary case of a divorce without grounds, whatever was done was done. The {\i get} takes effect, even if the motivation was improper.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hidden Intent\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Mesharshiya poses a different question to Rava: if a man has inwardly decided to divorce his wife but conceals this intention, allowing her to continue living in his home, serving him, and being intimate with him \u8212- what is the ruling on this? Rava does not answer in legal terms. Instead, he cites a verse: {\i al tacharosh al re\u8217'akha ra\u8217'ah ve-hu yoshev lavetach itakh} \u8212- \u8220"do not plot evil against your friend while he dwells securely beside you.\u8221" The language {\i re\u8217'im ha-ahuvim} \u8212- beloved companions \u8212- captures the essence of the marriage bond, and to scheme a betrayal while one\u8217's spouse lives in trusting intimacy is precisely what this verse forbids. It is an act of profound treachery that the verse characterizes as plotting evil against one who reposes complete trust in you.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Fastidious Man\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Meir Parable\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i baraita} records: Rabbi Meir would say \u8212- {\i keshem she-ha-de\u8217'ot ba-ma\u8217'akhal kach de\u8217'ot ba-nashim} \u8212- just as people differ in their attitudes toward food, so they differ in their attitudes toward wives. The analogy, troubling as it may be in certain respects, is developed through three character types.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Overly Fastidious\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The first type: there is a person who, when a fly falls into his cup of wine, discards the entire cup \u8212- so fastidious that the slightest contamination renders the wine undrinkable. {\i Ve-zu hi middat Pappos ben Yehuda} \u8212- this is the character of Pappos ben Yehuda, who, when leaving his house, would lock his wife inside, keeping her completely secluded from the outside world. This is presented as extreme, and Rashi remarks directly on how counterproductive and harmful this is: {\i u-midah she-einah hogenet hi zo she-mi-tokh kakh eivah nikhneset beineihem u-mezanah tachtav} \u8212- it is an improper character trait, because it breeds terrible enmity between husband and wife and may lead, of all ironies, to the very infidelity he feared. The controlling dynamic of locking one\u8217's wife away does not produce fidelity; it produces hatred.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Average Man\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The second type: the ordinary person, when a fly falls into his cup, removes the fly and drinks the cup. {\i Ve-zo hi midat kol adam} \u8212- that is how most people behave. Correspondingly, most husbands allow their wives to speak with brothers and relatives and other familiar contacts, and do not become suspicious or jealous over normal social interaction.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Least Fastidious\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The third type: there is a person into whose stew a fly falls, who wrings out every drop of flavor from the fly and then eats the stew. {\i Zo hi midat adam ra} \u8212- this is the character of a bad person. Such a man sees his wife go out with her hair uncovered, spinning in the marketplace with her arms exposed, wearing clothing slit up the sides \u8212- and does not react. He tolerates everything.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Women\u8217's Conduct\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Immodest Behavior\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i baraita} now enumerates the categories of women\u8217's public conduct that fall under the rubric of {\i ervat davar} in its broader sense \u8212- not necessarily adultery, but behavior that constitutes {\i dat Yehudit}, the norms of modest Jewish conduct within marriage. These include: going out with her hair uncovered, spinning in the marketplace in a manner that exposes her arms, and wearing clothing that is open at the sides.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Public Bathing\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The final and most striking example: {\i ve-rochetzet im bnei adam} \u8212- and bathes with men. The {\i gemara} objects immediately: literally with men? {\i Im bnei adam salka datcha} \u8212- can that really be meant? The clarification: {\i ela bemakom she-bnei adam rochtzin} \u8212- in a location where men bathe, with sufficient proximity to constitute impropriety even if not literal simultaneous bathing.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 In such a case: {\i zo mitzva min ha-Torah le-garshah} \u8212- it is a {\i mitzvah} to divorce her. The {\i gemara} invokes {\i ki matza bah ervat davar} not in the narrow sense of proven adultery but in the broader sense of conduct that is sexually inappropriate and transgresses the norms of modest conduct in marriage. The discussion in {\i Ketubot} on {\i dat Moshe} and {\i dat Yehudit} addresses this at length. The {\i gemara} is careful to note that the verse then says {\i ve-halkhah ve-haytah le-ish acher} \u8212- she went and became the wife of another man \u8212- and the second man who takes such a woman, {\i hakatuv kera\u8217'o acher} \u8212- the verse calls him \u8220"different\u8221" \u8212- suggesting he is not of the same character as the first. The first man, {\i hotzi rasha mi-beito}, rid his house of a wicked woman. The second, {\i hichnis rasha le-tokh beito}, brought her into his. If the second man merits it, {\i shelakh} \u8212- he too will divorce her. If not, {\i kevertahu} \u8212- she will bury him. {\i Kedai hu ba-mitah} \u8212- death is fitting for him who should have known better.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Ki Sane Shalakh\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Yehuda\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i parasha} contains the phrase {\i ki sane shalakh} \u8212- which can be read as \u8220"if he hates, send away.\u8221" Rabbi Yehuda understands this as a directive: {\i im sane\u8217'tah shalakh} \u8212- if you hate her, send her away. Rashi reads this as consonant with the position of Rabbi Akiva, though one might equally associate it with Beit Hillel\u8217's broader reading. The common thread is that subjective disaffection \u8212- the loss of positive feeling \u8212- constitutes grounds for ending the marriage.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Yochanan\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yochanan reads it entirely differently: {\i sanui ha-mishalei\u8217'ach} \u8212- the one who sends her away is the one who is to be despised. On this reading, the verse is not a permission but a condemnation: the man who divorces his wife is hateful. This aligns with a stricter, Beit Shammai\u8211-like sensibility that divorce without serious cause is an act of which one ought to be ashamed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Bezug Rishon/Sheni\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} says: {\i ve-lo peligi} \u8212- in fact they do not disagree. {\i Ha be-zug rishon ha be-zug sheni} \u8212- one statement applies to a first marriage, the other to a second. The context determines which is meant, as we will see in what follows.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Altar Cries\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 First Wife Tragedy\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Elazar states: {\i kol ha-megaresh ishto rishonah, afilu mizbe\u8217'ach morid alav dema\u8217'ot} \u8212- anyone who divorces his first wife \u8212- the {\i mizbe\u8217'ach}, the altar, sheds tears on his account. The verse is brought from the prophet: {\i ve-zot shenit ta\u8217'asu, kessut dim\u8217'ah et mizbe\u8217'ach Hashem be-bekhi ve-anakah} \u8212- \u8220"you cover the altar of God with tears and weeping and sighing.\u8221" God no longer turns to the {\i minchah} and no longer wishes to receive the offering from your hands. And when you ask why \u8212- {\i al ki Hashem he\u8217'id beinekha u-vein eshet ne\u8217'urekha} \u8212- because God has testified between you and the wife of your youth, {\i asher atah bagadta vah} \u8212- that you have betrayed her.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chavratcha\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The verse concludes with words of singular beauty: {\i ve-hi chavratekha ve-eshet beritecha} \u8212- she is your companion and the woman of your covenant. The word {\i chavratekha} \u8212- your friend, your partner \u8212- conjures an image of the marriage relationship that is strikingly egalitarian, especially in a context where so much of the tractate has dealt with the husband\u8217's unilateral power in matters of divorce. The word {\i berit} \u8212- covenant \u8212- suggests a sacred, binding relationship entered into by two parties with equal standing.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Balance Issues\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This brings us back to the tension between Rabbi Yehuda and Rabbi Yochanan, resolved by the distinction between a first and second marriage. It is the divorce of the first wife \u8212- the wife of one\u8217's youth, the foundational relationship \u8212- that causes the altar to cry. Applying a Beit Shammai\u8211-like stringency to that relationship, insisting that divorce not be undertaken lightly and certainly not for trivial reasons, captures something profound and important. At the same time, it is worth acknowledging that this framework, applied rigidly and without sensitivity, can cause enormous harm. A woman who has carefully and thoughtfully determined that a marriage must end, after long deliberation and many steps in the process, should not be made to feel that she is doing something monstrous simply by appearing before a {\i beis din}. Sometimes divorce is the right decision. The challenge is to honor the genuine tragedy of the dissolution of a first marriage without weaponizing that recognition to obstruct, shame, or manipulate people who are trying to move forward with their lives.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Closing: Partnership\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is perhaps fitting to close the tractate on the note of {\i chavratekha ve-eshet beritecha}. Amid all the discussion of the mechanics of {\i gittin}, the rules of agency, the conditions of the document, and the grounds for divorce, these words from the prophets remind us what marriage is at its deepest level: a partnership between two people bound by covenant. It is striking that in {\i ketubot} discovered in the Cairo {\i Geniza}, this very language is used to describe the marriage relationship \u8212- {\i chaverti ve-eshet briti}, my companion and the woman of my covenant. That the language of friendship and covenantal partnership found its way into those documents of daily life speaks to how central this vision of marriage was \u8212- and remains. On that note of {\i chavratekha ve-eshet beritecha}, we conclude {\i Masekhta Gittin}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Kiddushin 2\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Intro to Kiddushin\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Masechet Overview\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 We begin the final {\i masechet} of {\i Nashim}: {\i Masechet Kiddushin}. Like {\i Gittin}, this is a tractate studied intensively in {\i yeshivas}, with a rich tradition of {\i rishonim} and subsequent literature, owing in part to its practical {\i halachic} relevance to questions of marriage and {\i kiddushin}. Yet it is equally important to appreciate the remarkably diverse range of topics it encompasses \u8212- a diversity comparable to what we encountered in {\i Gittin}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A few introductory observations are in order. {\i Gittin} contained eight or nine {\i perakim} and extended to ninety {\i dapim}. {\i Kiddushin}, by contrast, has only four {\i perakim} and runs to eighty {\i dapim} \u8212- nearly the same length with roughly half the {\i perakim}. Alongside the laws of {\i kiddushin} proper, one finds discussions of parental and filial obligations, {\i kibbud av va\u8217'em}, and other subjects a reader might not expect to encounter in {\i Masechet Kiddushin}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 First Perek Structure\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The first {\i perek} is, by any measure, the largest {\i perek} in {\i Shas} \u8212- forty {\i dapim}, half the entire {\i masechet} \u8212- and its scope extends well beyond {\i kiddushin}. The opening {\i mishna} reads {\i ha-isha nikneit b\u8217'shalosh drachim}, a woman is acquired in three ways, and by the end of that same {\i mishna} the topic shifts to {\i ha-yevamah nikneit}, how a {\i yevamah} is acquired. From there, the {\i mishnayot} of the first {\i perek} proceed systematically: how an {\i eved ivri} is acquired, how an {\i eved kena\u8217'ani} is acquired, how one purchases real estate, how one acquires chattel \u8212- all organized around the theme of {\i kinyanim}, of transactions and acquisitions. The operative word throughout is {\i nikneit} \u8212- acquired. So while the {\i masechet} is named {\i Kiddushin} and is named for the institution of betrothal, the first {\i perek} is devoted not solely, and perhaps not even primarily, to {\i kiddushin} itself, but to the broad question of how various things and persons are acquired.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The second {\i perek} returns to a direct focus on {\i kiddushin} with the formulation {\i ha-ish mekadesh}, the man betroths \u8212- and immediately after that, {\i bo u\u8217'vshlucho}, through himself or through his agent. Where {\i Gittin} took the institution of {\i shlichus} somewhat for granted (while raising particular questions about who qualifies as an agent), {\i Kiddushin} gives {\i shlichus} a sustained and direct treatment in the second {\i perek}. The third {\i perek} engages with questions of {\i tna\u8217'im}, conditions, which we also encountered extensively in {\i Gittin}. And the fourth {\i perek} \u8212- {\i asara yuchasin} \u8212- addresses lineage: questions of {\i mamzerut}, uncertain parentage, and related issues of status. A wide and diverse {\i masechet}, then, and hence even four {\i perakim} amounts to eighty {\i dapim}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Ha-Isha Nikneit Mishna\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishna Text\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The first {\i mishna} reads: {\i ha-isha nikneit b\u8217'shalosh drachim}, a woman is acquired in three ways; {\i v\u8217'konah et atzmah b\u8217'shtei drachim}, and she acquires herself \u8212- her own freedom, if you will \u8212- in two ways. Then: {\i nikneit b\u8217'kesef b\u8217'shtar u\u8217'v\u8217'viah}, she is acquired through money, through a document, and through sexual relations.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rashi\u8217's Explanation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rashi explains the phrase {\i v\u8217'konah et atzmah} \u8212- \u8220"acquires herself\u8221" \u8212- as meaning {\i lihyot birshutah}, to be under her own control, and specifically {\i lihinasay l\u8217'acher}, to be free to marry someone else. There are, of course, other dimensions to the change in her status \u8212- for instance, a husband may annul his wife\u8217's vows, and her earnings belong to him. But Rashi focuses on the most fundamental consequence of {\i kiddushin}: that she is now bound to her husband in the sense that she may not marry or have relations with another man. That is the core change effected by {\i kiddushin}, and accordingly that is what \u8220"she acquires herself\u8221" primarily refers to: the dissolution of that bond, restoring her freedom to remarry. The precision of Rashi\u8217's formulation will become clearer as we proceed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Three Methods\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The three modes of {\i kiddushin} are {\i kesef}, {\i shtar}, and {\i biah} \u8212- money, document, and sexual relations.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kesef Method\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Kesef} involves giving an object of value accompanied by the declaration {\i harei at mekudeshet li} \u8212- behold, you are betrothed to me. Also essential, though not stated explicitly in the {\i mishna}, is the requirement of {\i eidim}, witnesses \u8212- not merely to prove that the act occurred, but to constitute it as a legally binding act, just as we saw by {\i gittin}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ring Practice\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The traditional practice of using a ring is not mentioned anywhere in the {\i Gemara}. Its first recorded appearance is in a work called {\i Sefer ha-Chilukim}, a Gaonic-era compilation that documents observed differences in practice between the Jews of {\i Mizrach} \u8212- Babylonia \u8212- and the Jews of {\i Eretz Yisrael}. There it states that the Jews of the East did not use a ring in {\i kiddushin}, whereas the Jews of {\i Eretz Yisrael} did. This appears to reflect a regional divergence. In any event, neither the Bavli nor the Yerushalmi mentions a ring; {\i kesef} simply means the giving of some object of value.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shtar Method\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Shtar} is a written document \u8212- at its simplest, a piece of paper on which is written {\i harei at mekudeshet li} \u8212- given to the woman in the presence of {\i eidim}. It is worth noting that while {\i shtar kiddushin} is fully treated as a valid {\i halachic} mechanism, there is virtually no evidence that it was ever actually practiced. The {\i Gemara} contains many accounts of {\i kesef kiddushin} \u8212- a man in a marketplace, for instance, handing a woman a basket of fruit and declaring {\i harei at mekudeshet li} \u8212- but no stories involving a {\i shtar kiddushin}. It appears never to have been used in practice.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might be tempted to identify the {\i ketubah} as the {\i shtar} referred to here, or the {\i yichud} room as the {\i biah}. This is a common misunderstanding, but a complete {\i halachic} error: the {\i ketubah} and {\i yichud} pertain to {\i nisu\u8217'in}, not to {\i kiddushin}. That said, the ancient {\i ketubot} from the Cairo Genizah are instructive. Scholars such as Mordechai Friedman have noted that some of those documents include language in which the groom declares {\i harei at mekudeshet li}, and there is reason to think that at least some communities conceptualized the {\i ketubah} as bearing some relation to a {\i shtar kiddushin} \u8212- even though {\i halachically} it is not one, and even though the {\i ketubah} is fundamentally a document recording mutual obligations associated with {\i nisu\u8217'in}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Biah Method\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Biah} \u8212- sexual relations \u8212- constitutes the third mode of {\i kiddushin}. The witnesses presumably need not be in the room itself. Crucially, it must be an act of sexual relations performed specifically with the intention of creating {\i kiddushin}, of establishing the couple as betrothed. Not any act of intimacy \u8212- the intent must be directed toward this legal and personal transformation.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Kiddushin vs.\u160 ?Nisu\u8217'in\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kiddushin Defined\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The mention of {\i biah} as a mode of {\i kiddushin} is an apt occasion to clarify \u8212- as it bears repeating \u8212- the fundamental distinction between {\i kiddushin} and {\i nisu\u8217'in}. {\i Kiddushin} is the act that renders a woman legally a married woman. This means that if she were to accept {\i kiddushin} from another man, it would have no effect; and if she were to be intimate with another man, it would constitute adultery. However, {\i kiddushin} does not yet mean that the husband and wife are living together, establishing a household, or having an ongoing sexual relationship. All of that belongs to {\i nisu\u8217'in}, to the moment of {\i chuppah}, when they actually begin life together.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Nisu\u8217'in Defined\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Nisu\u8217'in} is treated in {\i Masechet Ketubot}, which addresses what constitutes {\i chuppah}, the obligations it creates \u8212- the husband\u8217's obligations of {\i shear, kesut, v\u8217'onah} (sustenance, clothing, and conjugal rights) as specified in the {\i Torah}, as well as the reciprocal obligations running in the other direction, including the wife\u8217's earnings going to the husband \u8212- and all the legal dimensions of actually establishing a shared household.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Historical Context\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The existence of {\i kiddushin} and {\i nisu\u8217'in} as two distinct stages reflects a social reality. In the {\i alte heim}, marriages were often arranged by parents when children were very young. What prevented one side from backing out? In later practice, {\i tna\u8217'im} \u8212- formal betrothal agreements with severe financial penalties for withdrawal \u8212- served that purpose. But originally, the binding mechanism was {\i kiddushin} itself. The groom, who had to be of age, would give an object of value, and the legal status of marriage was thereby created \u8212- even if the couple would not begin living together for years. The girl could have been betrothed by her father even as a minor. The actual commencement of married life, the {\i nisu\u8217'in}, might follow a decade later.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is also worth noting that {\i Masechet Kiddushin} is ordered after {\i Ketubot} only because it is shorter \u8212- four {\i perakim} versus {\i Ketubot}\u8217's many more \u8212- but conceptually {\i kiddushin} precedes {\i nisu\u8217'in}, and the ordering reflects size rather than logical sequence. {\i Sotah}, similarly, follows {\i Gittin} despite a different conceptual sequence.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rashi\u8217's Precision\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This historical context illuminates the precision of Rashi\u8217's earlier comment. When Rashi says {\i v\u8217'konah et atzmah} refers specifically to the ability to marry another man, he is deliberately not mentioning vow annulment or the right to her earnings \u8212- because those consequences arise only from {\i nisu\u8217'in}, not from {\i kiddushin}. The only thing that {\i kiddushin}, in the absence of {\i nisu\u8217'in}, actually accomplishes is the prohibition on marrying or having relations with another man. All other ways in which a wife is legally under her husband\u8217's authority are products of {\i nisu\u8217'in}. Rashi is, as always, precise.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Biah Distinction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The same precision applies to the {\i biah} of {\i kiddushin}. This is not the ongoing sexual relationship that characterizes {\i nisu\u8217'in} and married life. It is a single, discrete act performed with the specific intent of creating the status of {\i kiddushin} \u8212- after which the couple might not begin living together for years. The act of {\i biah} that constitutes {\i kiddushin} and the sexual relationship of {\i nisu\u8217'in} are entirely different in kind: the former is a one-time legal act, the latter is the ongoing reality of a shared life.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Kesef Dispute\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Beit Shammai vs.\u160 ?Hillel\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} continues: {\i nikneit b\u8217'kesef} \u8212- acquired through {\i kesef}. What is meant by {\i kesef}? {\i Beit Shammai} say: a {\i dinar} or its equivalent. {\i Beit Hillel} say: a {\i perutah} or its equivalent. The {\i dinar} was the silver coin of the Roman Empire \u8212- and this usage of {\i kesef} was, literally, silver. The additional {\i chiddush} of the phrase \u8220"or its equivalent\u8221" is that the object need not actually be a coin or even metal; it need only be of equivalent value.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Perutah Definition\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Beit Hillel}\u8217's position \u8212- a {\i perutah} or its equivalent \u8212- involves a copper coin, or its equivalent. This of course already demonstrates that {\i kesef} need not mean silver at all. The {\i mishna} then provides a gloss on {\i Beit Hillel}\u8217's view: {\i kamah hi perutah? Echad m\u8217'shmoneh b\u8217'isar ha-italki} \u8212- one-eighth of an Italian {\i isar}. The sources indicate that the Italian {\i isar} was itself a copper coin \u8212- {\i nechoshet} \u8212- a point worth noting. If {\i Beit Hillel} were trying to anchor the {\i perutah} in the language of silver ({\i kesef}), one would expect the reference coin to be silver. The fact that it is copper reinforces that according to {\i Beit Hillel}, {\i kesef} in this context simply means something of value, however minimal.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This also illuminates the structure of the {\i mishna} itself. The earlier, core formulation \u8212- {\i nikneit b\u8217'kesef, b\u8217'shtar, u\u8217'v\u8217'biah} \u8212- reads as a tight, apodictic statement. The discussion of {\i Beit Shammai} and {\i Beit Hillel} is a gloss: a later layer of {\i tannaitic} commentary asking what that word {\i kesef} means. And the definition of the {\i perutah} as one-eighth of an Italian {\i isar} is, in turn, a gloss upon the gloss. Recognizing this layering is important not only for understanding the {\i mishna}\u8217's structure but also for appreciating what {\i kesef} might have meant before {\i Beit Shammai} and {\i Beit Hillel} introduced their debate.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kesef Evolution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Without the {\i Beit Shammai} and {\i Beit Hillel} gloss, one reads simply: {\i ha-isha nikneit b\u8217'kesef} \u8212- a woman is acquired with money. How much money? One might naturally assume something like the {\i chamishim kesef} of the {\i Torah} \u8212- the standard {\i mohar ha-betulot}, the fifty silver pieces that constituted the standard bride-price paid, typically to the father, when a virgin was married. That would be a significant, meaningful sum \u8212- one that would make the transaction feel genuinely transactional, genuinely like a {\i kinyan}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What happened to that fifty {\i kesef}, which became two hundred {\i zuz} in rabbinic reckoning? It migrated to the {\i ketubah}. It became not a sum paid upfront but a sum recorded as a debt, payable upon death or divorce \u8212- designed to protect the woman\u8217's financial interests and, according to {\i Chazal}, to slow a husband from divorcing precipitously by ensuring there would be real financial consequences. By moving this amount to the back end, the upfront act of {\i kiddushin} became symbolic rather than substantial. That is precisely what prompts {\i Beit Shammai} and {\i Beit Hillel}: if we are no longer giving the {\i mohar} upfront, what does {\i kesef} in {\i kiddushin} actually mean? A {\i dinar}? A {\i perutah}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Once that shift occurs \u8212- once the act of {\i kiddushin} involves only something symbolic rather than a significant purchase price \u8212- the institution stops being referred to as {\i kinyan} and is called exclusively {\i kiddushin}. The linguistic change tracks an evolution in how the institution itself is conceptualized.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kesef in Context\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The contrast with other applications of {\i kesef} as a mode of acquisition sharpens this point. When the {\i mishna} later teaches that {\i nechasim she-yesh lahem achrayut} \u8212- real estate \u8212- is acquired through {\i kesef, shtar, u\u8217'chazakah}, what does {\i kesef} mean there? Market value. Whatever is being asked for. Nobody imagines that a {\i perutah} suffices to purchase a house. The {\i kesef} of real estate acquisition is the actual going rate. So if the opening {\i mishna} had said simply {\i ha-isha nikneit b\u8217'kesef} without the {\i Beit Shammai} and {\i Beit Hillel} gloss, the natural reading would be: whatever the agreed-upon price is \u8212- presumably something like the {\i mohar} of the {\i Torah}. The introduction of a {\i dinar} or a {\i perutah} as the operative amount fundamentally transforms how we understand the nature of this transaction.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Intro to Kiddushin (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kinyan vs.\u160 ?Kiddushin\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This brings us to a foundational conceptual tension that will run throughout the entire first {\i perek}: the {\i kinyan} model versus the {\i kiddushin} model of understanding marriage.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Masechet Kiddushin} is named {\i Kiddushin}. And in every {\i mishna} throughout the {\i masechet} \u8212- with a single exception \u8212- the institution of betrothal is referred to using the language of {\i kiddushin}: {\i mekadesh}, {\i mekudeshet}, {\i kiddushin}. The one exception is this very first {\i mishna}, which uses the language of {\i kinyan}: {\i nikneit}, acquired.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One model \u8212- the {\i kinyan} model \u8212- understands marriage as a form of acquisition. Not the acquisition of a slave in the full sense of an {\i eved kena\u8217'ani}, but an acquisition nonetheless: the man acquires the woman in certain defined ways. What would he be acquiring? Not her labor \u8212- that right is rabbinic, not biblical. But perhaps sexual rights: not only are other men prohibited from her, but he has a claim to her. The {\i Gemara} discusses the husband\u8217's {\i chiyuv} of {\i onah}, his obligation to have sex with his wife \u8212- but notably never derives from a verse that the wife is obligated to have sex with her husband. The obligation is simply assumed, perhaps because it is built into the very concept of what {\i kinyan} in marriage means. Similarly, children born of the marriage are considered his in a fundamental way \u8212- reflected in the {\i Torah}\u8217's ruling that one who strikes a pregnant woman causing miscarriage pays the husband, not the mother.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i kiddushin} model, by contrast, is not primarily about possession at all. It is about personal status \u8212- the transformation of a woman from single to married, the creation of an exclusive relationship that renders her {\i asurah} to other men. It is about a status that emerges through a relationship, not about ownership of a person.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The first {\i perek} of {\i Kiddushin} will be the arena in which this tension is worked through. The early, tight collections of {\i mishnayot} \u8212- {\i ha-isha nikneit}, {\i ha-yevamah nikneit}, {\i eved ivri}, {\i eved kena\u8217'ani}, {\i nechasim she-yesh lahem achrayut} \u8212- all structured around \u8220"here is the object, here are the modes of acquisition,\u8221" reflect an early stratum in which the {\i kinyan} model predominated. The later development \u8212- moving the substantial monetary payment to the {\i ketubah}, reducing the upfront amount to something purely symbolic, and renaming the institution {\i kiddushin} \u8212- reflects a fundamental reconceptualization. A ring that is worth a {\i perutah} does not feel like a purchase price. It feels like a symbol. And once it becomes a symbol, the institution is no longer called {\i kinyan}. It is called {\i kiddushin}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Nikneit vs.\u160 ?Mekadesh\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Terminology Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} opens with a direct question: {\i mai shna hacha detani ha-isha nikneit u\u8217'mai shna hatam detani ha-ish mekadesh?} What is the difference between here, where the {\i mishna} says {\i ha-isha nikneit} \u8212- the woman is acquired \u8212- and there, the second {\i perek}, where the language is {\i ha-ish mekadesh} \u8212- the man betroths? Why does the language of {\i kinyan} appear only here?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kinyan from Efron\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} answers: {\i mishum dekaba\u8217'i lemisnei kesef} \u8212- because the {\i mishna} is about to discuss {\i kesef}, and the concept of {\i kesef} as a mode of acquisition is derived from the language of {\i kinyan} used in connection with Avraham\u8217's purchase of the field of Efron. That is why we open with the vocabulary of acquisition.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gezerah Shavah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The derivation is a {\i gezerah shavah} of {\i kicha kicha} \u8212- the word \u8220"taking\u8221" appearing in two contexts. {\i Ketiv hacha: ki yikach ish isha} \u8212- when a man takes a woman. {\i U\u8217'ketiv hatam: natatti kesef ha-sadeh kach mimeni} \u8212- I have given the money for the field, take it from me. The same root, {\i kicha}, appears in both the acquisition of a wife and the acquisition of Efron\u8217's field. This creates the hermeneutical link between {\i kiddushin} and the language of {\i kinyan}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Field Acquisition\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 To complete the {\i gezerah shavah}: not only is the same word {\i kicha} used in both contexts, but the field of Efron is explicitly referred to as a {\i kinyan} \u8212- {\i ha-sadeh asher kanah Avraham}, the field that Avraham purchased. And a further verse confirms that {\i kesef} is what effects the acquisition of fields: {\i sadot b\u8217'kesef yiknu} \u8212- fields are purchased with money. Tosafot raises the possibility that one might argue the money was merely the price, while the actual act of {\i kinyan} was accomplished through something else \u8212- {\i chazakah}, perhaps. The verse {\i sadot b\u8217'kesef yiknu} forecloses that: the giving of money is itself the act that acquires the field. So when we learn {\i kiddushin} from this parallel, we know both that {\i kesef} is a valid mode of acquisition for a wife and that the giving of {\i kesef} is itself the operative act.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Biblical vs.\u160 ?Rabbinic\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} then asks: why not be consistent and use {\i kinyan} language throughout the entire {\i masechet}? The answer: {\i me-ikara tani lishna d\u8217'Oraita} \u8212- at the outset, the {\i mishna} uses biblical language. {\i Kinyan}, as we have seen, is the biblical conceptualization, rooted in the {\i gezerah shavah} from Efron\u8217's field. {\i U\u8217'levasof tani lishna d\u8217'Rabbanan} \u8212- but subsequently, the {\i mishna} uses rabbinic language. The rabbinic vocabulary is no longer {\i kinyan} but {\i kiddushin}. Words are not arbitrary. The shift from {\i kinyan} to {\i kiddushin} across the {\i masechet} reflects a genuine conceptual evolution in how the institution was understood.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Kiddushin Meaning\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hekdesh Model\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What does the rabbinic term {\i kiddushin} mean? The {\i Gemara} explains: {\i d\u8217'asar lah a\u8217'kuly alma k\u8217'hekdesh} \u8212- he renders her forbidden to all other men, as {\i hekdesh} renders an object forbidden. Just as sanctifying an animal or object to the Temple removes it from the domain of all ordinary use, so too a man who is {\i mekadesh} a woman renders her off-limits to all other men. This is the core of the {\i kiddushin} model: not ownership, not possession, but a status of exclusivity \u8212- she is removed from the general domain of eligibility.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafot Relationship\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This formulation, while legally precise, is not entirely satisfying as a description of marriage. As one observes immediately: {\i hekdesh} is off-limits even to the person who sanctified it. If I sanctify an animal, it becomes {\i asur} to me as well. Furthermore, I can only sanctify something {\i to} the Temple if I already own it \u8212- the act of {\i hekdesh} flows from prior ownership. But a man does not own the woman before {\i kiddushin}. So what gives him the right to make her {\i hekdesh}? And she certainly does not become {\i asurah} to him.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot addresses this directly and adds the crucial missing piece. Tosafot asks: in the formula {\i harei at mekudeshet li}, what does the word {\i li} \u8212- to me \u8212- contribute? Tosafot answers: {\i li yehei li} \u8212- she becomes {\i mekudeshet} to the world {\i bishvili}, because of her connection to me. Just as one might say {\i harei hem mekudashim l\u8217'Shamayim} \u8212- these are sanctified to Heaven \u8212- meaning they belong to Heaven and therefore become forbidden to all else, so too {\i mekudeshet li} means: because you are mine, because you are in relationship with me, you are therefore forbidden to everyone else. Tosafot thus adds the relational dimension: the exclusivity that {\i kiddushin} creates is grounded not in a transfer of ownership but in a relationship that, once established, forecloses all other relationships.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kinyan to Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What emerges is a fundamental transition: from a world of {\i kinyan} \u8212- possession, control, acquisition of objects \u8212- to a world of {\i kiddushin} \u8212- personal status arising through a relationship. The man is still the initiating party; {\i kiddushin} is still something the man does. But the framework shifts from \u8220"I am acquiring her\u8221" to \u8220"I am entering into an exclusive relationship with her, and the consequence is that she is removed from the domain of all other men.\u8221" The {\i li} \u8212- the relational dimension \u8212- is what drives the prohibition, not an act of possession.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is reinforced by the comparison to {\i hekdesh} itself. One might understand {\i hekdesh} as a transfer of ownership to God. But one can also sanctify an animal as a {\i korban} \u8212- a sacrifice \u8212- in a sense that is not simply about God owning the animal in the way a person owns property. The sanctification establishes a relationship of dedication. So too with {\i kiddushin}: the model of {\i kiddushin as hekdesh} can be read not as a transfer of ownership but as a dedication \u8212- an act of exclusive consecration that establishes a relationship and, as its consequence, shuts out all others.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Woman\u8217's Agency\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This leads directly to a further point the {\i Gemara} will make explicit. The {\i mishna} says {\i ha-isha nikneit} \u8212- the woman is acquired \u8212- using the passive voice and making the woman the grammatical subject. The {\i Gemara} observes that this is not accidental. While the entire structure of the first {\i perek}\u8217's {\i mishnayot} focuses on the object being acquired (the woman, the {\i yevamah}, the {\i eved ivri}, the {\i eved kena\u8217'ani}, the real estate), the choice to say {\i ha-isha nikneit} rather than {\i ha-ish koneh} \u8212- the man acquires \u8212- has a deliberate legal implication: {\i kiddushin} requires the woman\u8217's {\i da\u8217'at}, her consent and desire. {\i Mida\u8217'atah in, she-lo mida\u8217'atah lo} \u8212- with her knowledge, yes; without it, no. The {\i mishna} makes her a subject, not merely an object.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is a striking interpretive move. The same {\i mishna} that uses the language of {\i kinyan}, and whose derivation is from the purchase of a field, is read by the {\i Gemara} as emphasizing the woman\u8217's subjectivity and agency. The {\i Gemara} is aware, of course, that in the {\i Torah} the father could marry off his daughter entirely without her consent. {\i Chazal} interpreted that power as applying only to a {\i k\u8217'tanah}, a minor \u8212- once she reached maturity, she could not be married off without her own participation. And the {\i Gemara} reads the very language of {\i ha-isha nikneit} as encoding this requirement of consent, even within a {\i mishna} that speaks the language of acquisition.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Tosafot Questions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafot Q1\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot raises two pointed questions on the {\i gezerah shavah} of {\i kicha kicha} from Efron\u8217's field. The first: why is the elaborate invocation of the {\i Efron} account necessary? If we already know that {\i kesef} is the mechanism \u8212- as stated explicitly in {\i ki yikach ish isha} and in the verse {\i sadot b\u8217'kesef yiknu} \u8212- why do we need the entire parallel to the field of Efron to establish that {\i kiddushin} is a {\i kinyan}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafot Answer\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot\u8217's answer is subtle. {\i Kesef} by itself does not necessarily imply {\i kinyan}. Even if we know that a monetary payment is involved in {\i kiddushin}, we would not automatically conclude that this payment constitutes an act of {\i kinyan} in the full technical sense \u8212- the same kind of {\i kinyan} used to acquire a field. Perhaps {\i kesef} in {\i kiddushin} operates through some other model entirely, some mechanism that involves money but does not carry the full conceptual weight of {\i kinyan}. It is precisely the {\i gezerah shavah} from Efron\u8217's field that establishes the equation: the {\i kesef} of {\i kiddushin} is called {\i kinyan} in exactly the same sense that the purchase of a field is called {\i kinyan}. Without that parallel, the word {\i kesef} alone would not carry that implication. One could have {\i kesef} without {\i kinyan}; one needs the {\i gezerah shavah} to prove that here, {\i kesef} constitutes {\i kinyan}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafot Q2\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot\u8217's second question is a logical problem with the parallel itself. The {\i gezerah shavah} links {\i ki yikach ish isha} \u8212- the taking of a woman \u8212- with {\i kach mimenni} \u8212- take from me \u8212- from Efron\u8217's account. But in the {\i Efron} verse, what Avraham is taking is the {\i money} of the field \u8212- Efron is saying, take my money for the field. The {\i kicha} in Efron\u8217's account refers to the taking of the silver, not the taking of the field. Whereas in {\i ki yikach ish isha}, the {\i kicha} refers to taking the woman herself, not to any money. The parallel is therefore imperfect: in one case the {\i kicha} is of the price, in the other case the {\i kicha} is of the object acquired. This is simply a sharp observation on the limits of the {\i gezerah shavah} as deployed here, and Tosafot flags it as such.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Kicha in Torah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Biblical Kicha\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A further and perhaps more fundamental difficulty with the {\i gezerah shavah} from {\i kicha} deserves attention. In the {\i Torah} itself, what does the word {\i kicha} actually mean when used in the context of a woman? The answer, on close reading, is that {\i kicha} in the {\i Torah} refers to {\i nisu\u8217'in} \u8212- to full marriage, cohabitation, and sexual relations \u8212- not to {\i kiddushin} or {\i erusin}. As Tosafot notes, {\i v\u8217'ish asher yikach et achoto} \u8212- a man who takes his sister \u8212- refers to sexual relations, not to betrothal. And {\i ki yikach ish isha u\u8217'va aleha} \u8212- when a man takes a woman and comes to her \u8212- the {\i kicha} and the {\i bi\u8217'ah} are not two sequential stages (first betrothal, then sex). Rather, {\i kicha} is the full taking into the household; {\i u\u8217'va aleha} is simply the natural consequence. The {\i kicha} of the {\i Torah} is the totality of {\i nisu\u8217'in}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Erusin\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The word in the {\i Torah} that actually corresponds to what {\i Chazal} call {\i kiddushin} \u8212- {\i erusin}, betrothal \u8212- is not {\i kicha} at all. It is the root {\i eiras}: {\i asher eiras isha}, the man who has betrothed a woman. {\i Mi ha-ish asher eiras isha v\u8217'lo lekachah} \u8212- who is the man who has betrothed a woman but has not yet taken her \u8212- demonstrating clearly that in the {\i Torah}\u8217's own usage, {\i eiras} comes first and {\i kicha} comes afterward. {\i Kicha} is {\i nisu\u8217'in}; {\i eiras} is {\i erusin}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Methodological Paradox\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This creates a striking methodological paradox. {\i Chazal} derive the laws of {\i kiddushin} \u8212- betrothal \u8212- from the {\i pesukim} that use the language of {\i kicha}. But those very {\i pesukim} about {\i kicha}, in their plain biblical sense, refer to {\i nisu\u8217'in}, not to {\i kiddushin}. The {\i pesukim} that actually correspond to the institution {\i Chazal} are deriving \u8212- betrothal as distinct from full marriage \u8212- are the {\i pesukim} using the root {\i eiras}. Yet the {\i limudim} in this {\i masechet} will overwhelmingly draw on the {\i kicha} verses. This is not a flaw in the derivation but it is worth noting as a structural curiosity: the biblical language most naturally associated with {\i kiddushin} is {\i erusin/eiras}, while the language {\i Chazal} use as their primary hermeneutical tool \u8212- {\i kicha} \u8212- is, in the {\i Torah\u8217's} own usage, the language of {\i nisu\u8217'in}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Woman\u8217's Consent\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Voice Answer\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} returns to the question of why the {\i mishna} says {\i ha-isha nikneit} \u8212- the woman is acquired, passive \u8212- rather than {\i ha-ish koneh} \u8212- the man acquires, active. The more substantive answer is structural: the entire sequence of {\i mishnayot} in the first {\i perek} is organized around the object being acquired. {\i Ha-isha nikneit, ha-yevamah nikneit, eved ivri, eved kena\u8217'ani, nechasim she-yesh lahem achrayut} \u8212- each {\i mishna} names the object and lists the modes of acquisition. Focusing on the object, not the acquirer, is the organizing principle of the entire {\i perek}. Moreover, the {\i mishna} must say {\i v\u8217'konah et atzmah} \u8212- she acquires herself \u8212- and so it is natural that it should also say {\i ha-isha nikneit}, making her the subject throughout.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Voice Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But the {\i Gemara} presses further: why not say {\i ha-ish koneh u\u8217'makneh} \u8212- the man acquires and then releases her \u8212- retaining the active, man-centered formulation throughout while still accommodating both halves of the {\i mishna}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Medatah Requirement\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Because, the {\i Gemara} responds, when the husband dies, it is not he who releases her \u8212- {\i lav ihu ka-maknei lah, min shamayim hu d\u8217'ka-maknei lah} \u8212- it is Heaven that releases her. His death is not an act of {\i makneh} on his part. So the structure cannot be {\i ha-ish koneh u\u8217'makneh}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But beyond this technical point, the {\i Gemara} adds a deeper reason: {\i iba\u8217'it eima} \u8212- if you prefer, the language {\i ha-isha nikneit} is used to make a substantive legal point. Had the {\i mishna} said {\i ha-ish koneh}, one might have thought that a man could perform {\i kiddushin} even without the woman\u8217's knowledge or against her will \u8212- {\i afilu be\u8217'al korchah}. The {\i mishna} says {\i ha-isha nikneit} precisely to emphasize that {\i kiddushin} is only valid {\i mida\u8217'atah} \u8212- with her desire and consent \u8212- and not {\i she-lo mida\u8217'atah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Father\u8217's Rights\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is a striking move by the {\i stam}. In the {\i Torah}, {\i kiddushin} \u8212- or at least the {\i erusin} of the {\i Torah} \u8212- was not {\i mida\u8217'atah} in any general sense. The father married off his daughter; she was not asked. Only in the account of Rivkah\u8217's family ({\i nikra la-na\u8217'ara v\u8217'nish\u8217'ala et piha}) does the {\i Torah} depict the bride being consulted, and that itself is presented as exceptional. Ordinarily, in the {\i Torah}, the woman was entirely the object of a transaction between men \u8212- father and husband.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Chazal} limited the father\u8217's authority to marry off his daughter to her minority alone. Once she became an adult, she could only be married with her own participation and consent. And what the {\i Gemara} does here is remarkable: it reads the language of {\i ha-isha nikneit} \u8212- a {\i mishna} that uses the most objectifying possible vocabulary, the {\i kinyan} of acquisition \u8212- as encoding the requirement of her consent. The same {\i mishna} that says \u8220"the woman is acquired\u8221" is read as insisting that she is a subject, not merely an object, that she must desire and agree to what is happening to her. The linguistic vehicle of {\i kinyan} is being turned, at the moment of its very first deployment, into an affirmation of her agency.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Shalosh Grammar\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Grammar Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now turns to a grammatical question that will occupy the balance of the {\i daf}: why does the {\i mishna} say {\i bi-shalosh drachim} rather than {\i bi-shlosha drachim}? The number three takes different forms depending on the grammatical gender of the noun it modifies \u8212- {\i shalosh} for feminine nouns, {\i shlosha} for masculine. If the {\i mishna} says {\i shalosh}, it is treating {\i derech} as feminine. But is {\i derech} feminine?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Derech Feminine\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Yes: {\i derech} is a feminine noun. The proof: {\i v\u8217'hoda\u8217'ta lahem et ha-derech yelchu vah} \u8212- you shall make known to them the path that they shall walk {\i bah}, using the feminine pronoun {\i bah}. The feminine agreement of the pronoun confirms that {\i derech} is feminine, which is why {\i shalosh} is correct.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Derech Contradiction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But then the {\i Gemara} notes an apparent contradiction. Another verse uses {\i derech} with masculine agreement: {\i be-derech echad yetz\u8217'u eilecha u\u8217've-shiva derachim yanusu lefanecha} \u8212- they shall come out against you by one way and flee before you by seven ways. Here {\i echad} (masculine) is used, not {\i achat} (feminine), suggesting {\i derech} is masculine. And there is a corresponding inconsistency in the {\i mishnayot}: our {\i mishna} says {\i shalosh drachim} (feminine), while another {\i mishna} \u8212- about checking a {\i zav} \u8212- says {\i shiva derachim} (masculine, using {\i shiva} rather than {\i sheva}).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 So the {\i pesukim} seem to contradict one another, and the {\i mishnayot} seem to contradict one another.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Context Resolution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} resolves: {\i kra\u8217'ei ahadadi lo kashya} \u8212- the verses do not actually contradict. The grammatical gender of {\i derech} is context-dependent. The verse {\i v\u8217'hoda\u8217'ta lahem et ha-derech yelchu vah} is speaking about the way of the {\i Torah}. And {\i Torah} itself is a feminine noun \u8212- {\i Torat Hashem temima meshivat nafesh}. A {\i derech} that represents {\i Torah} takes the feminine form; hence {\i bah}, and hence {\i shalosh}. The verse {\i be-derech echad yetz\u8217'u eilecha\u8230 ? be-shiva derachim yanusu} is about war \u8212- and war is the domain of men ({\i darko shel ish la\u8217'asot milchama, v\u8217'ein darchah shel isha la\u8217'asot milchama}). A {\i derech} that represents the masculine domain of warfare takes the masculine form, hence {\i echad} and {\i shiva}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Mishnah Application\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ha-Isha Gender\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The same principle resolves the inconsistency between the {\i mishnayot}. Our {\i mishna} \u8212- {\i ha-isha nikneit bi-shalosh drachim} \u8212- is about a woman. Since the subject is female, {\i derech} takes its feminine form, and the number three appears as {\i shalosh}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Zav Gender\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} about the {\i zav} \u8212- {\i bi-shiva derachim bodkim et ha-zav} \u8212- uses the masculine form {\i shiva} because the checking procedure applies exclusively to men.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Zav Rationale\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Why are only men subject to this examination? Because the investigation of whether a discharge constitutes {\i zivah} of the {\i Torah} \u8212- contingent on its not having been triggered by sleep, food, accident, or some other external cause \u8212- applies only to men. If a woman has a bleeding, she is automatically rendered {\i tamei}, whether it was caused by an outside force or not. The source of the bleeding does not determine her status. Therefore {\i d\u8217'darko shel ish le-hivadek, v\u8217'ein darchah shel isha le-hivadek} \u8212- the checking pertains to the male domain \u8212- and so {\i derech} takes masculine agreement: {\i shiva derachim}, not {\i sheva derachim}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Kiddushin 3\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Kinyan vs.\u160 ?Bi\u8217'ah Models\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Nikneit vs.\u160 ?Mekadesh\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Today we take up {\i Kiddushin} daf 3, picking up towards the bottom of amud 2. The first {\i daf} of the {\i masechet} laid critical groundwork, and the {\i Gemara} continues here with what is, on its surface, a technical grammatical introduction \u8212- yet embedded within it are some of the most fundamental conceptual questions of the entire {\i masechet}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The opening discussion centered on the verb {\i nikneit} \u8212- {\i ha\u8217'isha nikneit} \u8212- rather than what we find later in the {\i masechet}, where the language shifts to {\i mekadesh} or {\i mekudeshet}. This seemingly minor terminological difference opens onto a major conceptual fault line: is {\i kiddushin} to be understood as a type of {\i kinyan}, a property acquisition, in which the woman is essentially the object being purchased? The {\i Gemara} identified this as {\i lishna d\u8217'kra}, the Torah\u8217's own framing of the institution \u8212- rooted, as we saw, in the {\i gezeira shava} from the purchase of Ephron\u8217's field.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Lishna D\u8217'Kra vs.\u160 ?Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 On the other side stands the rabbinic understanding encoded in the term {\i mekudeshet}. As {\i Tosafot} explains it, {\i mekudeshet} is not merely a passive status of being forbidden to others \u8212- it is {\i mekudeshet li}, forbidden to others specifically as a result of the relationship between this particular man and this particular woman. This is a status model rather than a property model, and the distinction could not be more consequential. These two frameworks \u8212- {\i kinyan} and {\i kiddushin} as status \u8212- will compete with one another throughout the first half of the first {\i perek}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Object vs.\u160 ?Subject\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What makes the {\i Gemara}\u8217's introduction even more striking is the way it reads the {\i Mishnah}\u8217's language against itself. The {\i Mishnah} does indeed treat the woman as a kind of grammatical object \u8212- {\i ha\u8217'isha nikneit} sits alongside {\i yivama nikneit}, {\i eved ivri}, {\i eved kna\u8217'ani}, {\i nechasim she\u8217'yesh lahem achrayut}, the list moving between persons and things being acquired. And yet the {\i stama d\u8217'Gemara} insists that the choice of {\i nikneit} rather than {\i ha\u8217'ish koneh} is precisely to foreground her role as a subject \u8212- to emphasize that {\i kiddushin} cannot be done against her will, that she must be a consenting party. This is notably not the Torah model; in the Torah, the woman as an adult acting for herself is nowhere explicit. The {\i Gemara} here is registering a shift: from the Torah\u8217's model, in which the father gives her as a minor, to the world of {\i Chazal}, in which she is an adult participant.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Drachim vs.\u160 ?Devarim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gender Agreement\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Continuing along the bottom of the {\i daf}, the {\i Gemara} raises the question of gender agreement in the phrase {\i shlosha drachim}. Since {\i derech} can be either masculine or feminine in biblical Hebrew, and in fact appears both ways in different {\i mishnayot}, the {\i Gemara} asks {\i mai ta\u8217'ama tani shalosh} \u8212- why the feminine form {\i shalosh} rather than {\i shlosha}? The answer offered is that {\i derech} takes on a feminine character here because the woman, who is the subject of the {\i Mishnah}, inflects the gender of the word. This is, admittedly, a point that is difficult to get especially animated about. But behind it lies a more interesting question, to which the {\i Gemara} immediately pivots.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Derech and Bi\u8217'ah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The deeper question is: why say {\i drachim} at all? Why not simply say {\i b\u8217'shlosha devarim} \u8212- in three things, or three matters? In English the difference between \u8220"a woman is acquired in three ways\u8221" and \u8220"a woman is acquired by three things\u8221" is not trivial, and the {\i Gemara} takes the distinction seriously. The answer: {\i mishum d\u8217'ka ba\u8217'ei l\u8217'mitni bi\u8217'ah} \u8212- because the {\i Mishnah} is going to list {\i bi\u8217'ah}, sexual union, as one of the three modes, and {\i bi\u8217'ah} is linguistically and conceptually associated with the word {\i derech}. As the {\i pasuk} says, {\i v\u8217'derech gever b\u8217'alma} \u8212- the way of a man with a young woman. And similarly, {\i ken derech isha menafet} \u8212- the way of an adulterous woman. The word {\i derech} is bound up with the concept of {\i bi\u8217'ah} in the biblical imagination.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Derech Natural Way\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But then the {\i Gemara} poses the obvious challenge: very well, {\i derech} may be the right word for {\i bi\u8217'ah}, but what about {\i kesef} and {\i shtar}? Why should those also be called {\i drachim}? The answer: {\i hanahu nami tzorech bi\u8217'ah ninhu} \u8212- those two also ultimately exist in service of {\i bi\u8217'ah}. All of {\i kiddushin}, on this reading, is oriented toward and framed by the eventual union of the couple. The word {\i derech}, applied to all three modes, reflects the fact that the entire institution is pointing toward {\i bi\u8217'ah} as its telos. This is a striking claim, and it parallels the earlier move with {\i nikneit}: just as the term {\i nikneit} frames the whole institution even though it is strictly appropriate only to {\i kesef}, so {\i derech} frames all three modes even though it is strictly appropriate only to {\i bi\u8217'ah}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Derech Framing\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Man Seeks Woman\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is where the {\i Gemara} turns from grammatical observation to something considerably more profound. The {\i stama d\u8217'Gemara} offers a second explanation for the word {\i drachim}, introduced with {\i ve\u8217'iba\u8217'it eima ha mani Rabbi Shimon hi} \u8212- an alternative reading attributed to Rabbi Shimon. The {\i Gemara} cites a {\i baraita}: {\i Rabbi Shimon omer, mipnei mah amrah Torah ki yikach ish isha v\u8217'lo k\u8217'tiv ki tilakach isha le\u8217'ish} \u8212- why does the Torah say \u8220"when a man takes a woman\u8221" and not \u8220"when a woman is taken by a man\u8221"? Why is the man framed as the active party in {\i kiddushin}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The answer: {\i mipnei shedarko shel ish lachazor achar haishah v\u8217'ein darkah shel ishah lachazor achar haish} \u8212- because it is the way of a man to seek out a woman, and it is not the way of a woman to seek out a man. Even in today\u8217's more egalitarian social landscape, this dynamic has not entirely disappeared \u8212- the expectation that the man is more often the initiator in courtship remains, however contested, a recognizable pattern. And this dynamic also has a direct {\i halachic} implication: the man is the active party in the act of {\i kiddushin} itself.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Aveidah Metaphor\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What follows is a beautiful and theologically rich {\i mashal}: {\i mashal l\u8217'adam she\u8217'avdah lo aveidah} \u8212- it is like a person who has lost an object. Why does he go looking for it? Because the object was lost from {\i him}. So too, {\i mi chozer al mi? Ba\u8217'al aveidah mechazer al aveidato} \u8212- the one who suffered the loss is the one who goes searching. The man seeks the woman because she is his lost half \u8212- the part of him taken away in {\i maaseh bereishit}, when woman was formed from man. The reference is to {\i al ken ya\u8217'azov ish et aviv ve\u8217'et imo v\u8217'davak b\u8217'ishto v\u8217'hayu l\u8217'vasar echad} \u8212- the two halves rejoining as one flesh.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This metaphor deserves careful attention, because it is doing something quite different from the {\i kinyan} model. Yes, the {\i aveidah} metaphor still contains a possessive element \u8212- it is something of his that he is reclaiming. But the emotional and conceptual valence is entirely different from the acquisition of an object. One\u8217's {\i aveidah} is not a commodity; it is something one is incomplete without. The resonance is with {\i bashert} \u8212- with the idea that the union is a restoration of something originally whole, not the purchase of something external.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Beyond Kinyan\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} is therefore introducing, right at the outset of the {\i masechet}, several competing models of what is actually happening in {\i kiddushin}. On one side sits the {\i kinyan} framework, with its emphasis on acquisition, property transfer, and the woman as object. On the other side sits the {\i bi\u8217'ah} framework, with its emphasis on natural union, biological and existential complementarity, and the man not as purchaser but as the one searching for his lost half. It is worth pausing to appreciate how powerful it is that both of these framings appear simultaneously in the {\i Gemara}\u8217's introduction. Saying that all of {\i kiddushin} is ultimately about the {\i kinyan} model, or all about the {\i bi\u8217'ah} model, would be too simple. The {\i Gemara} is presenting both, letting them sit in tension.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 There is a related and important observation about the man\u8217's role as active party. One might have said: of course the man is the active one, because she is merely the object being purchased. But Rabbi Shimon\u8217's answer reframes this entirely. The man is the active one not because she is a passive object, but because he is the one who has lost something and must go searching. This moves the dynamic from domination to longing.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Natural Operation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} then broadens its inquiry into the word {\i drachim} by testing it against other {\i mishnayot} that use the same term. It asks: {\i vehasnan, be-zayin drachim bodkim et hazav} \u8212- we examine a {\i zav} in seven {\i drachim} to determine whether his flow results from natural causes. Why say {\i drachim} rather than {\i devarim}? The answer: {\i hasam kamashma lan de\u8217'orcha demichla yasira la\u8217'asuyei lidei ziva} \u8212- the word {\i derech} conveys that excessive eating naturally leads to a discharge; this is the way things work. Similarly with drink: {\i ve\u8217'orcha demishtaya yasira la\u8217'asuyei lidei ziva}. The word {\i derech} signals not merely a list of items but a natural causal relationship \u8212- these are the ways in which the body operates.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} then turns to the {\i mishnah}: {\i etrog shaveh le\u8217'ilan be-gimmel drachim} \u8212- the {\i etrog} resembles a tree in three {\i drachim}. Why not say {\i devarim}? The answer: {\i mishum de\u8217'ba\u8217'inan l\u8217'mitni seifa, v\u8217'leyarak be-derech echad} \u8212- because the {\i Mishnah} also needs to say that it resembles a vegetable in one {\i derech}. But this just shifts the question: why say {\i derech} there either? And the {\i Gemara}\u8217's answer in both places is {\i de\u8217'orcha de\u8217'etrog keyarak} \u8212- the {\i way} of the {\i etrog} is like the {\i way} of a vegetable, in that it grows on all waters and goes by the time of its picking for {\i maaser} purposes.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Here it is worth stepping back to explain the underlying {\i halacha}. By trees, the year that determines which {\i maaser} applies is the year of blossoming. By vegetables, it is the year of picking. An {\i etrog} behaves like a tree for {\i shemita} purposes \u8212- we go by when it blossoms. But for {\i maaser} purposes, it behaves like a vegetable, because like a vegetable it grows continuously throughout the year and can be picked at any time; it is not fixed to one season. {\i Rashi} explains that the {\i etrog}\u8217's similarity to a tree includes the {\i halachot} of {\i orlah} and {\i neta revai}, while its similarity to a vegetable includes that its {\i maaser} obligation follows the time of picking. The use of the word {\i derech} in both cases signals that these are not arbitrary classifications but reflect something about how each organism naturally operates \u8212- how it grows, how it fruits, what it requires.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This carries back to {\i kiddushin}. The insight that {\i drachim} is used when natural operation or natural causation is being invoked is significant. {\i Bi\u8217'ah} as a mode of {\i kiddushin} is not arbitrary \u8212- there is something deeply natural about physical union as the expression and actualization of marriage. There is a compelling argument, indeed, that among the three modes, {\i bi\u8217'ah} represents the most primordially natural one. The {\i Rambam} notes, based on the {\i Gemara}, that for {\i Bnei Noach} before the Torah was given, a man and woman who wished to marry would come together in sexual union and thereby be married. This is the {\i derech} in its most natural sense \u8212- not a societal formalism, not a written document, but the physical coming together that {\i Bereishit} itself describes in the words {\i v\u8217'davak b\u8217'ishto}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This observation opens onto a further question raised in the course of the discussion: if {\i bi\u8217'ah} is the most natural and perhaps the most primordially valid mode of {\i kiddushin}, does that affect its {\i halachic} status relative to the others? {\i Rashi} already cites, in the name of his {\i rabotai}, a {\i shita} \u8212- associated also with the {\i Geonim} and reflected in a {\i sugya} at the beginning of {\i Ketubot} regarding {\i hafka\u8217'at kiddushin} \u8212- that {\i kiddushei kesef} is {\i d\u8217'rabbanan}. The {\i Rambam} himself says in several places that {\i kiddushei kesef} is {\i midivrei sofrim}. The most explicitly {\i d\u8217'oraissa} mode of {\i kiddushin} in the Torah may well be {\i bi\u8217'ah}, given the phrase {\i ki yikach ish isha u-ve\u8217'alah} \u8212- though we noted yesterday that {\i ki yikach ish isha} in the plain reading of the Torah refers to {\i nissuin} rather than {\i kiddushin}, and that {\i kiddushin} in the Torah is rooted in {\i asher eirastiv}. Still, the {\i Rambam}\u8217's position invites the reading that {\i bi\u8217'ah} is the primary mode and {\i kesef} is in some sense derived or secondary. If so, then the {\i Gemara}\u8217's framing \u8212- that {\i kesef} and {\i shtar} are themselves in service of {\i bi\u8217'ah}, that the whole institution is shadowed by {\i bi\u8217'ah} \u8212- is not merely a {\i drasha} about word choice but a substantive claim about the architecture of {\i kiddushin}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One participant in the shiur raised a sharp point: the {\i Gemara}\u8217's framing may not be merely that marriage is ultimately oriented toward {\i bi\u8217'ah} in the sense of physical intimacy within the marriage, but that {\i bi\u8217'ah} is the primary act of {\i kiddushin} itself \u8212- and the other two modes are in service of that primary act, not merely in service of the eventual marital union. This reading has real {\i halachic} weight given the positions of {\i Rashi}\u8217's {\i rabotai} and the {\i Rambam}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 There is something else worth noting about how {\i derech} functions across these cases. As one sharp observation during the shiur put it: the {\i Gemara} opens by making strong claims about natural order \u8212- the natural way of a man is to seek a woman, the natural way of {\i bi\u8217'ah} is framed by the word {\i derech} \u8212- and then proceeds to bring cases of in-between things, like the {\i etrog} that is partly like a tree and partly like a vegetable. It is almost as if the {\i Gemara} is subverting its own invocations of natural order by immediately showing that nature is full of hybrids and edge cases. The {\i koi} \u8212- that creature the {\i Gemara} elsewhere identifies as a hybrid of uncertain classification \u8212- is {\i shaveh l\u8217'chaya} in some ways and {\i shaveh l\u8217'behema} in others, and in some ways like neither. {\i Derech} as natural order is the framework, and then immediately the {\i Gemara} illustrates how complex natural order really is.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Drachim vs.\u160 ?Devarim (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Drachim Alternatives\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 After surveying all these cases, the {\i Gemara} arrives at a clean resolution of the {\i drachim} versus {\i devarim} question. The rule is: {\i kol haycha d\u8217'ika plugta tani drachim} \u8212- wherever there are distinctions being drawn, wherever the point is that certain ways are specified as against alternatives, the {\i Mishnah} uses {\i drachim}. When one is listing matters without implying that alternative possibilities are being excluded, the word used is {\i devarim}. The proof: the {\i seifa} of the {\i mishnah} about {\i etrog} says {\i Rabbi Eliezer omer eser shaveh le\u8217'ilan l\u8217'chol davar} \u8212- similar to a tree in all matters \u8212- not {\i l\u8217'chol derech}. When the point is not contrast but comprehensive similarity, {\i devarim} is the right word. When the point is here are the paths as opposed to other possible paths, {\i drachim} is right.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This maps cleanly onto the usage in our {\i Mishnah}: a woman is acquired in three {\i drachim} \u8212- these three, and not others. The very phrasing signals that alternatives are being excluded. And this, of course, sets up the {\i Gemara}\u8217's next move: what are those alternatives being excluded?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is worth pausing on what these grammatical {\i sugyot} at the opening of a {\i masechet} actually accomplish. {\i Rav Sherira Gaon}, in his famous letter, notes that these opening passages are often characterized by an accumulation of Tannaitic material organized around seemingly trivial questions of grammar or terminology. The tendency in {\i yeshiva} study is to hyperfocus on every fine distinction; the opposite tendency, in survey learning, is to gloss over these passages as introductory filler. Neither extreme serves well. What the {\i Gemara} is doing, through these grammatical questions, is introducing the key conceptual vocabulary of the {\i masechet} \u8212- and, more than that, placing several competing models in tension with one another before the {\i halachic} analysis proper begins. The same move occurs at the opening of {\i Bava Kama}, where the question of whether {\i toladot} are equivalent to {\i avot} for purposes of liability serves as the frame for introducing every major category of damage in the first half of the tractate.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Excluded Modes\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Minyana Resha\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having established that the counting in the {\i Mishnah} is designed to exclude alternatives, the {\i Gemara} now asks directly: {\i minyana derisha lema\u8217'utei mai} \u8212- what does the number three in the opening clause exclude? And correspondingly, what does the number two in the closing clause exclude?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chuppah Exclusion\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} answers the first question: {\i minyana derisha lema\u8217'utei chuppah} \u8212- the three modes of acquisition exclude {\i chuppah} as a means of {\i kiddushin}. The case imagined is one where the father hands over his daughter, or perhaps where a man and woman proceed directly to {\i chuppah} without any prior {\i kiddushin}, and the question is whether standing under {\i chuppah} alone could effect {\i kiddushin}. {\i Rashi} here introduces the father specifically, perhaps because the act of {\i chuppah} alone \u8212- simply standing beneath it \u8212- is difficult to conceptualize as a significant legal act. What precisely is being done? {\i Rashi} may find it easier to frame the case if the father actively hands over his daughter, making the {\i chuppah} feel like something more concrete is occurring. Still, the question stands: might {\i chuppah} alone, as a symbol of marriage, suffice to create {\i kiddushin}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} immediately notes: {\i u-lerav Huna de\u8217'amar chuppah konah} \u8212- Rav Huna in fact holds that {\i chuppah} does effect {\i kiddushin}, deriving it through a {\i kal vachomer}. We will encounter his position in the coming days. For Rav Huna, then, the question becomes: what does the number three exclude? To which the {\i Gemara} responds \u8212-\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chalipin Excluded\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \u8212- {\i lema\u8217'utei chalipin}. It would exclude {\i chalipin}, exchange or barter, as a mode of {\i kiddushin}. The {\i hava amina} is serious: {\i salka deitach amina, ho\u8217'il u\u8217'gamar kikha kikha misdei Ephron} \u8212- since we derive {\i kiddushin} through a {\i gezeira shava} of {\i kicha kicha} from the acquisition of Ephron\u8217's field, we might have thought that all modes of acquisition that work for a field should work for a woman. Since {\i chalipin} works for acquiring a field, perhaps it should work for {\i kiddushin} as well. {\i Kamashma lan} \u8212- the {\i Mishnah} tells us no.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chalipin Perutah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But the {\i Gemara} does not stop there. It asks {\i ve\u8217'eima hachi nami} \u8212- why not indeed? What is actually wrong with the {\i hava amina}? The {\i Gemara} answers: {\i chalipin itnehu b\u8217'fachot mishaveh perutah} \u8212- {\i chalipin} can be effected with an object worth less than a {\i perutah}. A {\i keli} of any value, even a toothpick, suffices. {\i Ve\u8217'ishah b\u8217'fachot mishaveh perutah lo mikanya nafshah} \u8212- a woman will not agree to give herself over for less than a {\i perutah}. {\i Rashi} adds: {\i degnai hu lah} \u8212- it is demeaning to her to be party to such a transaction.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Two distinct types of {\i chalipin} must be held in mind here. The first is classic barter \u8212- I give you my donkey, you give me your cow; objects are exchanged on the assumption of rough equivalence. This is in some ways more ancient and more {\i natural} than {\i kesef}, since commerce likely began as barter before abstract monetary value was established. The second type of {\i chalipin} \u8212- often called {\i kinyan} \u8212- is a formal legal act: one party takes an object (classically a handkerchief) as a symbolic gesture that expresses {\i gemar da\u8217'at}, the finalization of a commitment. This second type is more like a handshake than an exchange; in the context of a wedding, for example, the groom takes the rabbi\u8217's watch or handkerchief to obligate himself to the terms of the {\i ketubah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Exchange Model Fails\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 For the exchange model, the inappropriateness for {\i kiddushin} is readily felt. To frame {\i kiddushin} as a swap \u8212- my pen for your daughter \u8212- thoroughly commodifies the woman. {\i Rashi}\u8217's language of {\i degnai hu lah} maps cleanly onto this: it is demeaning because it turns {\i kiddushin} into barter. And this demeanor quality inheres in the transaction type itself, not merely in the value of the object. Consider: if a man were to say {\i harei at mekudeshet li} while handing a woman an iPhone worth several hundred dollars, that works perfectly well as {\i kiddushei kesef}. But if he were to say {\i be-chalifei iPhone zeh harei at mekudeshet li}, framing the same object as {\i chalipin}, it does not work. The object is identical; what changes is the framing. When the transaction is framed as {\i chalipin}, it is framed as exchange \u8212- we are swapping \u8212- and that framing is what makes it inappropriate. The {\i kesef} model, by contrast, allows the object to function symbolically: you give something of value, and that effectuates the institution, without the connotation of a commercial transaction.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemar da\u8217'at} model of {\i chalipin} presents a harder case. Why should a formal act of commitment-expression be inappropriate for {\i kiddushin}? In some ways, {\i kiddushin} as {\i gemar da\u8217'at} seems conceptually apt \u8212- it expresses finality, mutual commitment, the completion of a decision. Yet even the {\i gemar da\u8217'at} form of {\i chalipin} ultimately hearkens back to the root concept of exchange and barter. Even if the handkerchief is purely symbolic, the institutional logic of {\i chalipin} remains that of swap. And it is that root logic \u8212- exchange, commodity, barter \u8212- that is incompatible with {\i kiddushin}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafot Chalipin\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} offers a narrower and more technically precise reading. According to {\i Tosafot}, the question was never whether all modes of field acquisition should work for a woman; it was specifically whether {\i chalipin} might be subsumed under {\i kesef} \u8212- that is, whether {\i chalipin} is simply a form of {\i kesef}. After all, in both cases you give something and the transaction is effected. Why not call {\i chalipin} a species of {\i kesef}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara}\u8217's answer, on {\i Tosafot}\u8217's reading, is that {\i chalipin} operates on fundamentally different parameters. {\i Kesef} works because of the {\i value} of what is given; it requires {\i shaveh perutah}, monetary worth. {\i Chalipin} works because of the {\i keli} \u8212- the object itself, regardless of value. Fruit, for example, would not work for {\i chalipin}. These are categorically distinct mechanisms. Once {\i chalipin} is shown to work {\i b\u8217'fachot mishaveh perutah} \u8212- with objects worth less than the minimum monetary unit \u8212- it is proven that {\i chalipin} is not a form of {\i kesef}. And since only {\i kesef}, {\i shtar}, and {\i bi\u8217'ah} effectuate {\i kiddushin}, {\i chalipin}, having been shown to be none of these, does not work.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is a cleaner and more narrowly {\i halachic} argument than {\i Rashi}\u8217's. For {\i Tosafot}, the issue is not primarily about dignity or demeanor but about categorical classification: {\i chalipin} and {\i kesef} are different legal instruments with different operative parameters, and {\i kiddushin} requires {\i kesef}, not merely any transaction that resembles it.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Woman\u8217's Freedom\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Minyana Sifa\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} turns from the first clause to the second: {\i minyana de-seifa lema\u8217'utei mai} \u8212- what does the number two exclude in the clause that says a woman acquires her freedom in two ways?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chalitza Exclusion\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The answer: it excludes {\i chalitza}. The {\i hava amina} is constructed as a {\i kal vachomer}: {\i mah yevama she-eina yotza b\u8217'get yotza b\u8217'chalitza} \u8212- a {\i yevama}, a widow awaiting {\i yibum}, cannot be released from her obligation to the {\i yavam} by means of a {\i get} prior to {\i yibum}, and yet {\i chalitza} does release her. {\i Zu she-yotza b\u8217'get eino din she-yotza b\u8217'chalitza} \u8212- a regular married woman, whom a {\i get} does free, should {\i kal vachomer} be freeable by {\i chalitza} as well. The {\i Gemara} answers: {\i amar kra, sefer keritut} \u8212- the verse says a {\i sefer keritut}, a written bill of severance. Only a {\i get} dissolves a regular marriage.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This {\i kal vachomer}, it must be said, is one of a series of formally constructed but intuitively strained arguments that appear throughout the opening of {\i Kiddushin}. The whole force of {\i chalitza} in the context of {\i yibum} has everything to do with the specific bond created by the {\i yibbum} obligation \u8212- it is {\i kacha ye\u8217'aseh la\u8217'ish asher lo yivneh et beit achiv}, a specific ceremony for a specific relationship. To suggest via {\i kal vachomer} that {\i chalitza} might therefore dissolve a regular marriage is, as a practical matter, almost absurd. And yet the {\i Gemara} follows the formal logic of the argument to its conclusion, requiring a {\i pasuk} to rule it out. This is characteristic of how the {\i Gemara} operates in this section: it applies the structure of {\i kal vachomer} with full rigor even when the conclusion seems intuitively obvious. The need for {\i sefer keritut} \u8212- a verse that explicitly requires a written document \u8212- closes the argument definitively.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} continues the analysis by exploring further {\i kal vachomers} that might have been constructed and were not, asking why certain arguments were or were not raised. This mode of analysis reaches its apex in the {\i acharon} known as the {\i Atzmos Yosef}, who effectively performs {\i Tosafot} to the second power, raising hypotheticals upon hypotheticals. The point is not to mock the exercise but to recognize that the formal structure of {\i kal vachomer} as applied here is being pushed to its limits, and the limiting factor is always either a {\i pasuk} or an intrinsic incompatibility in the comparison.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Kesef Source\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Menalan Kesef\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having established what {\i kiddushin} does not include, the {\i Gemara} now asks the foundational question: {\i menalan} \u8212- how do we know that {\i kesef} works for {\i kiddushin} at all? This might seem surprising given how much has already been built on this assumption, but the {\i Gemara} now turns to ground it properly.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Father\u8217's Rights\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} approaches this question through the lens of the father\u8217's rights, citing a {\i mishnah} in {\i Ketubot}: {\i zakai b\u8217'vito b\u8217'kiddusheha b\u8217'kesef b\u8217'shtar u-v\u8217'viah} \u8212- the father has rights over his daughter\u8217's {\i kiddushin} and can marry her off through {\i kesef}, {\i shtar}, or {\i bi\u8217'ah}. This is significant because you might have thought that only {\i kesef} would work when it is the father acting \u8212- since the transaction model, with money passing from the groom to the father, fits most naturally with {\i kesef}. The {\i mishnah} establishes that all three modes apply equally when the father acts on his daughter\u8217's behalf.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The emphasis on the father also carries a deeper conceptual weight. As noted above, the Torah\u8217's presentation of {\i kiddushin} is fundamentally the father\u8217's act \u8212- the woman in the Torah is a minor whose marriage is arranged by her father. The father model is where the {\i kinyan} framework is most at home: she is the object, he is the seller, the money comes to him. By beginning the derivation of {\i kesef} from the father\u8217's rights, the {\i Gemara} is in some sense returning to the foundational framework.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Adon Zeh Adon\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The actual derivation of {\i kesef} comes from a {\i pasuk} in the context of a Jewish slave-girl, the {\i amah haivrit}: {\i veyatza chinam ein kesef} \u8212- she goes free without money. The {\i Gemara} reads this as follows: {\i ein kesef le\u8217'adon zeh, aval yesh kesef le\u8217'adon acher} \u8212- there is no money going to {\i this} master when she leaves his control, but there is money going to {\i another} master. The verse cannot be referring merely to the explicit case of the slave-girl\u8217's sale \u8212- that is already stated explicitly. There must be another case in which a woman leaves the father\u8217's control and money is paid to him. That case must be {\i kiddushin}. Hence: when a man marries the daughter, money goes to the father.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Father Active Agent\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But how do we know the father is the one who receives the money, rather than the daughter herself? The {\i Gemara} cites: {\i et biti natati l\u8217'ish hazeh} \u8212- \u8220"I have given my daughter to this man.\u8221" This verse, from the case of {\i motzi shem ra}, establishes that the father is the active agent who gives over his daughter in {\i kiddushin}. He performs the act; he hands her over. {\i Rashi} and {\i Tosafot} both note that the verse technically speaks of a {\i na\u8217'arah} rather than a {\i ketanah}, but the {\i Gemara} reads it as applying to a case where he married her off when she was a minor. In any event, if the father is the one performing the act of {\i kiddushin} \u8212- {\i et biti natati} \u8212- then obviously the money placed in his hand is his to keep.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ketanah Na\u8217'arah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} then introduces a complication. One might have argued: all of the above \u8212- that the father acts, that the money goes to him \u8212- applies clearly when she is a {\i ketanah}, a minor, because as a minor she has no legal personality of her own and no capacity to receive {\i kiddushin} herself. But what about when she is a {\i na\u8217'arah} \u8212- between twelve and twelve and a half, the stage at which she has attained some degree of her own personhood in {\i halacha}? At that stage she does have some capacity to accept {\i kiddushin} on her own. Perhaps, then, when she is a {\i na\u8217'arah}, she could accept the {\i kesef} herself and retain it: {\i tikadesh hi nafshah v\u8217'tishkol kasba} \u8212- she could betroth herself and pocket the money.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This question \u8212- how do we know the father retains his rights and his claim to the money when she is a {\i na\u8217'arah} \u8212- is where the {\i Gemara} leaves us. The status of the {\i na\u8217'arah} is the middle ground: not the {\i ketanah} over whom the father has total control, and not the {\i bogeret}, the adult woman twelve and a half and older who is entirely beyond the father\u8217's control. In the {\i na\u8217'arah} stage, {\i halacha} recognizes her as possessing {\i yad} \u8212- a hand, an agency \u8212- even while the father also retains certain rights. How those rights are defined, and how we know the father is entitled to the {\i kiddushin} money even when she has some independent standing, is the question that will open tomorrow\u8217's discussion.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Kiddushin 4\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Father Sells Daughter\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Intro: Kesef & Father\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i daf} opens in the middle of an ongoing discussion about how we derive the rules of {\i kiddushei kesef} \u8212- betrothal effected through money. Although the {\i stam} of the {\i gemara} earlier introduced this topic through the famous {\i gezerah shavah} of {\i kicha kicha} from the field of Ephron, the {\i gemara} here focuses primarily on the {\i pasuk} dealing with a father selling his daughter into slavery. The thematic proximity of that sale to a father marrying off his daughter makes it a natural anchor for the derivation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} picks up approximately in the middle of 3b. The {\i gemara} establishes that from the {\i pasuk} in the context of {\i motzi shem ra} \u8212- {\i es biti natati la\u8217'ish hazeh}, \u8220"I gave my daughter to this man\u8221" \u8212- we see that the father has the authority to marry off his daughter. And from the {\i pasuk} of {\i ein kesef} in the context of the sale of a daughter as a slave, we learn that {\i kiddushin} is effected through money. These two sources together establish: {\i kiddushei kesef} exists, the father performs it, and the father keeps the proceeds.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ktana: Father Controls\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But this raises an immediate question: from {\i es biti natati la\u8217'ish hazeh}, all we can demonstrate is the father\u8217's authority when she is a {\i ktana} \u8212- a minor. The {\i mishna} in {\i Ketubot} teaches that these rights apply even when she is a {\i na\u8217'arah}, a girl between twelve and twelve and a half years old, who already has some legal standing. How do we extend the father\u8217's rights to that later stage?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} quotes the text: {\i v\u8217'eima hani mili ktana d\u8217'leit lah yad} \u8212- perhaps these rules apply only to a minor who has no independent legal hand. According to our {\i girsa}, the phrase {\i likabel kiddushin} is included: she has no hand to receive her own {\i kiddushin}. Rashi, however, appears not to have had that word, and understood the question more broadly \u8212- a {\i ktana} has no legal standing whatsoever.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Na\u8217'arah Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then presses the question: {\i aval na\u8217'arah d\u8217'it lah yad likabel kiddushin} \u8212- a {\i na\u8217'arah} who does have the capacity to receive her own {\i kiddushin}, perhaps {\i eima takdish ihi nafshah v\u8217'tishkol kaspah} \u8212- she should marry herself off and pocket the money herself? The scope of the question is somewhat ambiguous: is the {\i gemara} asking whether the father loses his right entirely when she is a {\i na\u8217'arah}, or merely whether she can act alongside him? In any case, the question is sharp: what is the source for the father\u8217's authority and his right to the money at this stage?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Slave Sale Source\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} answers from the {\i pasuk}: {\i b\u8217'neureiha beit aviha} \u8212- \u8220"in her youth, in her father\u8217's house.\u8221" This phrase appears in the context of the father\u8217's power to annul her vows. From it the {\i gemara} derives a broader principle.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Neurim to Father\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Kol shevach neurim l\u8217'aviha} \u8212- all the benefit of her youth goes to her father. Therefore, any money she would receive through an act of {\i kiddushin} that she herself performs would flow to him. The {\i gemara} then cites a related teaching: Rav Huna said in the name of Rav \u8212- how do we know that a daughter\u8217's earnings go to her father? From the {\i pasuk} {\i v\u8217'chi yimkor ish et bito l\u8217'amah}: just as a female slave\u8217's labor belongs to her master, so a daughter\u8217's labor belongs to her father \u8212- {\i mah amah ma\u8217'aseh yadeiha l\u8217'rabbah af bat nami ma\u8217'aseh yadeiha l\u8217'aviha}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 As Rashi notes, when she is a {\i ktana}, one doesn\u8217't even need a {\i pasuk} for this \u8212- it follows by {\i kal va\u8217'chomer}: if he can sell her labor to someone else as a slave, obviously he himself is entitled to it. The novelty of the {\i pasuk} is that it extends this right even to the stage of {\i na\u8217'arah}, when he can no longer sell her as a slave.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Three Categories\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chart Three Rights\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 At this point it becomes useful to lay out the full picture. The father has three distinct categories of rights over his daughter. First, as a {\i ktana}, he can sell her as a slave \u8212- {\i v\u8217'chi yimkor ish et bito l\u8217'amah} \u8212- and this obviously entails his right to her {\i ma\u8217'aseh yadeiha}. Second, he can perform {\i kiddushin} on her behalf, derived from {\i es biti natati la\u8217'ish hazeh}. Third, he has the power of {\i hifrat nedarim}, annulment of her vows, from {\i b\u8217'neureiha beit aviha}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Now the {\i gemara} asks: for the stage of {\i na\u8217'arah}, we know {\i hifrat nedarim} applies because the {\i pasuk} itself says {\i b\u8217'neureiha}. We know {\i ma\u8217'aseh yadeiha} applies from the {\i drasha} on {\i v\u8217'chi yimkor}. But we still need to establish that the father keeps the {\i kiddushei kesef} even when she marries herself as a {\i na\u8217'arah}. The {\i gemara} attempts several routes and rejects each.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Why Not Nedarim?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Perhaps we could learn {\i kiddushei kesef} from the principle of {\i hifrat nedarim} \u8212- if he has all the rights of her youth generally, surely he gets her money. The {\i gemara} rejects this: {\i mamoni me\u8217'issuri lo yalfinan} \u8212- monetary law cannot be derived from laws of {\i issur v\u8217'heter}. The right to annul vows is a religious prerogative that tells us nothing about financial entitlements.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Knas Distinction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What about the fact that in cases of {\i ones u\u8217'mifateh} \u8212- rape and seduction \u8212- and {\i motzi shem ra}, the Torah explicitly gives the {\i knas} to the father, {\i v\u8217'natnu l\u8217'avi ha-na\u8217'arah}? Surely that establishes that money flows to the father? The {\i gemara} again rejects this: {\i mamona miknasa lo yalfinan} \u8212- one cannot derive ordinary monetary law from a {\i knas}. A {\i knas} is a penal fine rooted in punishment, not compensation, and its rules carry no implications for a straightforward monetary transaction like {\i kiddushei kesef}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Boshet & Pegam\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might then suggest: what about {\i boshet} and {\i pegam} \u8212- the genuine compensation payments in a case of rape? These are not punitive fines but actual damages for the shame and physical injury suffered by the victim. If these real compensation payments go to the father, that should prove that {\i kiddushei kesef} goes to him as well.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Three Categories (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Father\u8217's Rights\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} responds: {\i d\u8217'avua shaich b\u8217'gufa} \u8212- the father has a stake in her body, meaning in his rights over her, that justifies his receiving {\i boshet} and {\i pegam}. But does this help us prove {\i kiddushei kesef}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Boshet/Pegam Logic\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rashi: Lost Profit\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rashi explains the underlying logic in stark terms. The father has a direct financial interest in her body in the sense that he could have arranged her marriage and received money for doing so. By being raped or harmed, she is now worth less on the marriage market. The {\i boshet} and {\i pegam} compensate for that lost opportunity. This is a disturbing framing, but it is the {\i gemara}\u8217's own logic as Rashi describes it. Notably, if the father\u8217's right to {\i boshet} and {\i pegam} is rooted in his ability to marry her off and profit from doing so, this very logic would seem to imply that {\i kiddushei kesef} should go to him as well.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Why Not Boshet?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Yet the {\i gemara} struggles with this very point. If {\i boshet} and {\i pegam} go to the father because of his authority to marry her off, why can\u8217't we use that to prove he gets {\i kiddushei kesef}? The argument becomes murky here, and the {\i gemara} has difficulty cleanly distinguishing the cases.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafot Resolves\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot resolves this by reframing the scenario. The argument is specifically about the case where she marries herself as a {\i na\u8217'arah}. Even in that case, the father retains the ability to marry her off himself \u8212- he has not lost that right. That is why he is entitled to {\i boshet} and {\i pegam}. But one might still argue: in the specific sub-case where she herself performs the act of {\i kiddushin}, perhaps in that moment she should get the money \u8212- not because he lacks rights generally, but because in this particular transaction she is the active party.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Misavra: Ein Kesef\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} ultimately resolves the question not through a logical proof but through {\i misavra} \u8212- the weight of contextual reasoning. The {\i pasuk} of {\i ein kesef} establishes that when a slave goes free from her master, the master receives no money. The {\i gemara} notes that the implicit parallel is: when she leaves the domain of another authority figure, money does change hands. And when the {\i gemara} seeks who that authority figure is whose analogue receives the money, it is not her but the father. Whether he arranged the {\i kiddushin} or she did, the recipient on the father\u8217's side of the transaction is the father himself.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Departure Parallel\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Still in Father House\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} immediately challenges this parallel. The model of {\i yetzi\u8217'ah} \u8212- departure from the master \u8212- does not translate cleanly to {\i kiddushin}, because after {\i kiddushin} a {\i na\u8217'arah} does not actually leave her father\u8217's house. {\i Hasha\u8217'm nafkah me\u8217'reshus adon legamrei} \u8212- in the case of the slave, she departs fully from her master\u8217's domain. {\i V\u8217'hacha achat michsra mesirah lechupah} \u8212- in the case of {\i kiddushin}, until she actually enters the husband\u8217's home at {\i nisuin}, she remains entirely under her father\u8217's control and within his household.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shared Vow Control\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} responds: {\i leafroshai nedarim mi\u8217'ha nafkah me\u8217'reshusei} \u8212- there is at least a partial departure in the domain of vow annulment. The {\i mishna} teaches that once a {\i na\u8217'arah} is betrothed, {\i aviha u\u8217'va\u8217'alah meferin nedareha} \u8212- both her father and her husband must jointly annul her vows. His authority in this area is no longer exclusive.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Limited Status Change\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is, admittedly, a limited change of status. He still retains vow annulment power; he simply now shares it. It is therefore somewhat puzzling that the {\i gemara} treats this partial shift as sufficient to sustain the comparison to a slave\u8217's departure from her master. The point seems to be not a genuine parallel in terms of lived experience, but a formal change of status that the {\i halacha} recognizes. And once we see the {\i gemara} is working with a formal model rather than a realistic one, the question can be turned on its head: if she doesn\u8217't truly leave his domain, that is all the more reason why \u8212- once we accept {\i kiddushei kesef} in principle \u8212- the money should go to him rather than to her.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Transactional Model\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What emerges from this entire discussion is that the dominant model in the {\i gemara}\u8217's analysis of {\i kiddushei kesef} is the sale of a daughter as a slave. The {\i gemara} repeatedly returns to that framework. The father has control over her, she is sold or transferred to another domain, and money changes hands. The model is transactional in a deep structural sense. One cannot read through this {\i sugya} \u8212- even if some of the arguments are difficult to follow \u8212- without absorbing the message that {\i kiddushei kesef} is being derived from and explained through the template of the father selling his daughter into slavery.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Economics & Symbolism\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 50 Coins & Burden\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is worth pausing to acknowledge the economic reality underlying all of this. In the world of the Torah, the payment involved was fifty silver {\i shekalim} \u8212- equivalent to two hundred {\i zuz} \u8212- a substantial sum. In that context, one might understand the financial logic that daughters, who in the ancient world generally did not go out and earn wages for the household, were perceived as financial burdens, and the father\u8217's primary obligation was to marry them off to someone who would provide for them.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Dowry Shift\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The deep irony is that once {\i kiddushei kesef} became purely symbolic \u8212- reduced to a ring worth a {\i perutah} \u8212- the financial burden shifted entirely to the father. It is now the father who must provide a substantial dowry if he wishes to arrange a favorable match. The one who once received money now pays.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ketubah Balance\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i ketubah} reflects this more complex economic reality. On one side, the groom undertakes financial obligations payable in the event of divorce. On the other, the bride\u8217's family brings property into the marriage. The system is not a simple sale; it involves financial flows in both directions, and the {\i ketubah} functions as a kind of balance between these competing obligations.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Ein Kesef Derasha\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Extra Yud Method\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now turns to a textual puzzle. The {\i pasuk} regarding the release of a Hebrew slave-girl says both {\i yatz\u8217'ah chinam} \u8212- she goes free without payment \u8212- and {\i ein kesef} \u8212- there is no money. If {\i chinam} already tells me she goes free at no cost, what does {\i ein kesef} add? The {\i braita} teaches: {\i yatz\u8217'ah chinam elu yemei bagrut, ein kesef elu yemei na\u8217'arut} \u8212- the first phrase refers to her going free as a {\i bogeret}, the second to her going free already as a {\i na\u8217'arah}. But if that is what {\i ein kesef} teaches, how can the {\i gemara} also use {\i ein kesef} to derive {\i kiddushei kesef}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabina\u8217's Drasha\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Ravina answers: had the Torah meant only to convey the law of {\i na\u8217'arut}, it could have written {\i an kesef} \u8212- without a {\i yud}. The fact that it writes {\i ein} with a {\i yud} allows us to derive two teachings simultaneously. The phrase itself \u8212- {\i ein kesef} \u8212- teaches that she goes free as a {\i na\u8217'arah}, and the extra {\i yud} teaches {\i ein kesef la\u8217'adon zeh v\u8217'yesh kesef la\u8217'adon acher u\u8217'manihu av} \u8212- there is no money to this master, but there is money to another master, namely the father. Thus from a single letter, two separate {\i halachot} are derived.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Zera Ein Lah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} validates this method of interpretation by analogy to the {\i pasuk} of {\i v\u8217'zera ein lah} \u8212- the {\i bat kohen} who married a {\i yisrael} and is now widowed without children returns to her father\u8217's house and may eat {\i terumah} again. The {\i braita} asks: I know only that her own children constitute {\i zera}; how do I know that grandchildren \u8212- {\i zera zarah} \u8212- would also bar her return? {\i Talmud lomar v\u8217'zera ein lah} \u8212- the {\i gemara} reads {\i ein} as {\i ayin lah}, \u8220"look into her\u8221" \u8212- investigate all connections. Furthermore, one might think only {\i zera kasher} \u8212- legitimate offspring \u8212- counts; how do I know that a {\i mamzer} would also constitute {\i zera}? Again from {\i zera ein lah}, {\i ayin lah}. The {\i gemara} then asks: how can you use {\i ein lah} for both grandchildren and {\i zera pasul}? For grandchildren you did not even need the {\i pasuk} \u8212- {\i benei vanim harei hem kevanim}, grandchildren are like children by a general principle. Therefore the {\i pasuk} is needed specifically for the case of {\i zera pasul}, and that is what the {\i ayin lah} comes to teach. The methodology of deriving multiple teachings from an extra {\i yud} is thus established.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Both Verses Needed\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} confirms that both {\i pesukim} are in fact necessary \u8212- both the {\i ein kesef} teaching (that the father receives money) and the teaching that she goes free as a {\i na\u8217'arah}. Neither can be dispensed with.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Why Both Verses\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 If Only Kesef\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If the Torah had only written {\i kiddusheiha l\u8217'aviha} \u8212- that the {\i kiddushei kesef} goes to her father \u8212- one might have thought: this is because she didn\u8217't exert any effort to earn it, like {\i kesef metziah}, found money. Therefore it goes to him rather than to her. But from this I would not know that her ongoing earnings also go to him.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 If Only Yatzah\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Conversely, if the Torah had only taught {\i ma\u8217'aseh yadeiha l\u8217'aviha} \u8212- her earnings go to her father \u8212- the logic is clear: she lives in his house, he feeds her, and in exchange she works and he takes her earnings. This is an implicit reciprocal arrangement, the very model that {\i Chazal} later built into the marital relationship between husband and wife. But by that logic, {\i kiddushei kesef} would not obviously belong to him \u8212- it is not something she earns through ongoing labor. It falls, as the {\i gemara} puts it, {\i me\u8217'alma ka\u8217'ati}, out of the sky, unexpectedly.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Reciprocity Model\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The two {\i pesukim} together thus paint a complete picture: the father supports her, she provides her labor, and the reciprocal arrangement extends even to {\i kiddushei kesef}. Each {\i pasuk} provides what the other cannot.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Unexpected Kesef\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Precisely because {\i kiddushei kesef} is unexpected and not part of the regular rhythm of her labor and his support, it cannot be derived by inference alone \u8212- it requires explicit scriptural teaching. This is why both verses are indispensable.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Na\u8217'arah Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Father Can Override\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} has been clear that the father can marry off his {\i na\u8217'arah} even against her will. His authority is not conditioned on her consent.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Self-Marriage Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A closer question is whether a {\i na\u8217'arah} can marry herself off. The {\i gemara} appears to suggest that she can, given the entire discussion about what happens when she performs the act of {\i kiddushin} herself. But this requires examination in light of the {\i rishonim}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rambam: Prior OK\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Rambam rules {\i lehalacha} that a {\i na\u8217'arah} cannot independently perform her own {\i kiddushin}. If she does, the validity is entirely contingent on her father. And crucially, the Rambam holds that even the father\u8217's subsequent approval \u8212- after the fact \u8212- does not validate the act. The only way her participation in {\i kiddushin} works is if the father gave his approval beforehand. In that framing, she is essentially acting as his {\i shaliach}; he grants her permission in advance, and she executes his decision.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Retroactive Approval\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Others disagree, holding that if she marries herself and the father subsequently ratifies the act, it may be valid. On this view, his approval after the fact is sufficient to confirm what she initiated.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Double Language\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Redundancy Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now addresses a related textual puzzle. The {\i pasuk} about the release of the Hebrew slave-girl mentions both {\i na\u8217'arut} and {\i bagrut} \u8212- the stages of {\i na\u8217'arah} and {\i bogeret}. Since a girl can only be sold as a slave when she is a {\i ktana}, she will necessarily go free either as a {\i na\u8217'arah} or as a {\i bogeret}, never later. So why does the verse need to mention {\i bagrut} at all? If we are told she goes free as a {\i na\u8217'arah}, she will obviously be free before reaching {\i bagrut}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabba: Both Stages\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabba answers: {\i bazzeh u\u8217'limad al zeh} \u8212- one phrase teaches through the other. Without the double language, a reader would naturally apply the single phrase to the later, more extreme stage \u8212- {\i bagrut}. The presence of the second phrase is what allows us to extend the rule to the earlier stage of {\i na\u8217'arut} as well. Had there been only one phrase, we would not have derived {\i na\u8217'arut} independently.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Bazzeh U\u8217'Limad\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} illustrates this principle through the case of {\i toshav v\u8217'sachir} in the laws of {\i terumah}. The Torah states that a {\i kohen}\u8217's {\i toshav} and {\i sachir} may not eat his {\i terumah}. The {\i braita} establishes: {\i toshav} refers to an {\i eved ivri} acquired permanently \u8212- the one who had his ear pierced and serves until {\i yovel}; {\i sachir} refers to the {\i eved ivri} who serves for six years. One might ask: just say {\i toshav} \u8212- the more permanent one \u8212- and I would derive by {\i kal va\u8217'chomer} that the {\i sachir} certainly doesn\u8217't eat {\i terumah}. But had the Torah written only one term, one would have applied it to the more limited case \u8212- the {\i sachir} \u8212- and I would have concluded that the permanent {\i eved ivri}, the {\i toshav}, actually does eat {\i terumah}. The second term is necessary precisely to cover the case that would otherwise seem like the more extreme and more doubtful instance.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abbaye, however, challenges this analogy. In the {\i toshav v\u8217'sachir} case, we are dealing with two different people \u8212- a {\i toshav} and a {\i sachir} are distinct individuals. Even if one were obvious, the Torah can reiterate it because the two statements are independent. But in the case of the slave-girl, it is one person. She cannot go free as both a {\i na\u8217'arah} and a {\i bogeret} \u8212- if she goes free at {\i na\u8217'arut}, she never reaches {\i bagrut} as a slave. So the two phrases cannot both refer to the same girl going free at two different stages. There must be a different explanation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Aylonit Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Aylonit Definition\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abbaye therefore proposes a new resolution: {\i ela amar Abbaye lo nitzrecha ela l\u8217'vagar de\u8217'aylonit} \u8212- the verse is needed for the case of the {\i aylonit}, a woman who never develops the normal signs of puberty and therefore never passes through the halachic stage of {\i na\u8217'arut}. The {\i aylonit} transitions from {\i ketana} directly to {\i bogeret} at age twenty, when she is legally recognized as an adult by default, having failed to develop the physical markers that would have defined her as a {\i na\u8217'arah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Skips Freedom?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The concern is straightforward: since the {\i aylonit} skips {\i na\u8217'arut} entirely, one might have thought she also skips the liberation that occurs at {\i na\u8217'arut}. Perhaps she remains enslaved indefinitely, since the trigger for her freedom \u8212- reaching {\i na\u8217'arut} \u8212- never arrives.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Aylonit Freed\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The verse therefore teaches {\i ka mashma lan}: she does go free when she reaches {\i bagrut}, even though she never passed through {\i na\u8217'arut}. The stage of {\i bagrut} \u8212- whenever it arrives \u8212- is sufficient to effect her release.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kal Va\u8217'Chomer?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Mar bar Rav Ashi challenges this: {\i v\u8217'lav kal va\u8217'chomer hu}? Is this not simply a {\i kal va\u8217'chomer}? When a girl develops the signs of puberty \u8212- {\i simanim} \u8212- this does not free her from her father\u8217's domain; she remains a {\i na\u8217'arah} under his authority. Nevertheless, those same signs do free her from her master. Now, {\i bagrut} \u8212- which does free her from her father\u8217's domain entirely \u8212- surely frees her from her master as well. This seems so obvious that no {\i pasuk} should be needed to teach it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chidush: Sell Her\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Mar bar Rav Ashi himself resolves the difficulty: {\i lo nitzrecha ela l\u8217'ikar zevina de\u8217'aylonit} \u8212- the novel teaching of the {\i pasuk} is not about her liberation but about the permissibility of selling her in the first place. The {\i hava amina} would have been: only a girl who will become a {\i na\u8217'arah} can be sold; a girl who will never become a {\i na\u8217'arah} \u8212- like this {\i aylonit} \u8212- perhaps cannot be sold at all. {\i Ka mashma lan} that even an {\i aylonit} may be sold as a Hebrew slave. This is the genuine {\i chidush}, and since we have no need for the {\i pasuk} to tell us she goes free at {\i bagrut} \u8212- that follows from a {\i kal va\u8217'chomer} \u8212- we apply the {\i pasuk} to this more surprising teaching.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot raises the question of how we could know at the time of sale \u8212- before age twelve \u8212- that this girl is an {\i aylonit}. Tosafot notes that there is a discussion about whether {\i simanei aylonit} can be detected even at a young age. The {\i gemara} appears to assume that she would remain enslaved until age twenty. It is also worth noting the striking halachic point embedded here: this woman is nineteen years old, fully an adult by any intuitive measure, yet is legally classified as a {\i ketana} \u8212- because the {\i halacha} defines majority through specific physical markers rather than age alone. A twelve-year-old with minimal signs of puberty is a legal adult; a nineteen-year-old without them is not.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is also worth stepping back to appreciate the broader significance of this discussion. The repeated emphasis on the father\u8217's sale of his daughter as a slave throughout this {\i sugya} is somewhat mitigated when one reflects on the actual purpose of that sale in the Torah\u8217's framework. The {\i gemara} itself implies that the {\i amah ivriyah} arrangement is ultimately oriented toward marriage \u8212- the master or his son is expected to marry her, and if neither does, she goes free. The labor of a young {\i ketana} is minimal; the real point of the transaction is the eventual marriage decision. Understood this way, the model is less about slavery and more about a structured arrangement for securing her marital future.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Why Ein Kesef?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Braita: Ki Yikach\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now transitions to the {\i braita} that introduces the derivation of {\i kiddushei kesef} from the {\i pasuk} of {\i ki yikach ish isha u\u8217'va\u8217'alah} \u8212- \u8220"when a man takes a woman and has relations with her.\u8221" The {\i braita} states: {\i ein kicha ela b\u8217'kesef} \u8212- the word {\i kicha} (taking) refers to acquisition through money, as evidenced by {\i natati kesef hasadeh kach mimeni} \u8212- Ephron\u8217's statement to Abraham that he should take the money for the field.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Slave to Woman\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But the {\i braita} immediately asks: why do I need a {\i pasuk} at all? Is this not derivable by {\i kal va\u8217'chomer}? {\i U\u8217'mah amah ivriyah she\u8217'eina niknes b\u8217'biah niknes b\u8217'kesef} \u8212- a Hebrew slave-girl, who is not acquired through an act of {\i biah}, is nevertheless acquired through money. {\i Zu she\u8217'niknes b\u8217'biah} \u8212- this woman, who is acquired through {\i biah} (as the continuation of the {\i pasuk} states, {\i u\u8217'va\u8217'alah}), {\i eino din she\u8217'tikne b\u8217'kesef}? Should it not follow all the more so that she can be acquired through money?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yevamah Contrast\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} immediately raises a counterexample: {\i yevamah tochiah} \u8212- the {\i yevamah} disproves this. A {\i yevamah} is acquired through {\i biah} \u8212- the act of {\i yibum} \u8212- yet she is not acquired through money. So the {\i kal va\u8217'chomer} breaks down: acquiring through {\i biah} does not guarantee acquiring through {\i kesef}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yevamah Limited\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i braita} responds by distinguishing the {\i yevamah}: {\i mah l\u8217'yevamah she\u8217'ken eina niknes b\u8217'shtar} \u8212- the {\i yevamah} is a unique case in which only {\i biah} works, not even a {\i shtar}. The methods of {\i kiddushin} for a {\i yevamah} are radically limited. In contrast, marriage ({\i kiddushin}) works through both {\i biah} and {\i shtar} \u8212- as we are about to derive. If the methods of acquisition are broader for ordinary {\i kiddushin}, then money should certainly also be available as an avenue, and we can restore the {\i kal va\u8217'chomer} from {\i amah ivriyah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Derive from Marriage\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Since {\i shtar} works for {\i kiddushin} but not for {\i yibum}, the {\i yevamah} is not a valid parallel. And since the {\i kal va\u8217'chomer} from {\i amah ivriyah} can be restored, the {\i pasuk} of {\i ki yikach} \u8212- together with the {\i kicha-kesef} analogy from Ephron \u8212- serves to anchor {\i kiddushei kesef} and {\i kiddushei shtar} firmly in the realm of ordinary marriage, distinct from the narrow rules of {\i yibum}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Both Verses Needed\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Woman Keeps?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having established that {\i ki yikach} is needed, the {\i gemara} asks why we need both {\i ki yikach} and {\i yatz\u8217'ah chinam ein kesef}. If the Torah had written only {\i ki yikach} \u8212- derived through the {\i kicha-kesef} analogy from Ephron \u8212- the {\i hava amina} would have been: {\i kiddushin d\u8217'yahiv ba\u8217'al dedidah havu} \u8212- the money of {\i kiddushin} belongs to her. This follows naturally from the {\i ki yikach} context, where there are only two parties and the woman herself appears to be the relevant figure in the transaction.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two-Party Model\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Ephron-Abraham model is significant here. In that narrative, there are only two parties: Abraham and Ephron. There is no third-party authority selling the woman as a father sells a daughter. The story therefore naturally suggests a model in which the woman herself is the central figure \u8212- a two-party transaction rather than a three-party arrangement with a father as intermediary. If {\i kiddushei kesef} were derived from this model alone, one might have assumed that the money goes to her.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ein Kesef Ambiguous\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Had the Torah written only {\i ein kesef} \u8212- without {\i ki yikach} \u8212- the ambiguity would run in a different direction. {\i Ein kesef} tells us that money changes hands, but it does not specify who gives and who receives. The {\i pasuk} merely implies that in the analogous case of {\i kiddushin}, money is involved. But in which direction does it flow?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rashi: She Gives\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rashi identifies the {\i hava amina} explicitly: {\i heicha d\u8217'yahava ihi ledidei kesef v\u8217'kidashto d\u8217'amra lei hiskadesh li} \u8212- perhaps she gives the money to him and says \u8220"marry me.\u8221" The {\i pasuk} of {\i ein kesef} alone would not rule this out; it simply asserts that money is part of the equation without specifying the direction.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ki Yikach: He Takes\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Ki yikach} resolves this: {\i ki yikach} \u8212- he takes, he is the active party. The grammatical and legal subject of the act is the man. {\i Ki tikach} \u8212- she takes \u8212- is not what the Torah wrote. The husband must be the one who effects the acquisition, not the woman. Thus the two {\i pesukim} are mutually necessary: {\i ein kesef} teaches that the father receives the money even when she is a {\i na\u8217'arah}, and {\i ki yikach} teaches that he \u8212- the man \u8212- is the active party who gives the money.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Status Asymmetry\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 She Becomes M\u8217'k\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot raises a pointed observation about Rashi\u8217's {\i hava amina}. Rashi suggested that without {\i ki yikach}, one might have thought she gives the money and says {\i harei ata mekudash li} \u8212- \u8220"you are hereby betrothed to me.\u8221" Tosafot challenges this: the language of {\i kiddushin} does not grammatically apply to the man, because {\i kiddushin} does not change his status. He is not rendered prohibited to other women by virtue of being married \u8212- polygamy was technically permitted. The status of {\i mekudeshet} \u8212- betrothed \u8212- is a status that applies to her, not to him. When he marries her, her relationship to all other men is transformed. His relationship to other women is not equivalently transformed. Therefore, saying {\i harei ata mekudash li} is not a coherent formulation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 She Declares\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot therefore reformulates: perhaps the {\i hava amina} was that she says {\i harei ani mekudeshet lach} \u8212- \u8220"I am hereby betrothed to you.\u8221" She is still the speaker who articulates her own status change, even if the language is now correctly formulated.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafot Full\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot takes this one step further. Tosafot argues that {\i d\u8217'yahava ihi ledidei} \u8212- \u8220"she gives the money to him\u8221" \u8212- was never meant literally. We have just established at length that the father receives the {\i kiddushei kesef}. How could the {\i hava amina} be that she gives money to the groom? Rather, Tosafot proposes, the {\i hava amina} was {\i natan hu v\u8217'amrah hi} \u8212- he gives the money and she speaks the declaration. The question was about who recites the formula of {\i kiddushin}, not about who physically transfers the money.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Not Pshat\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is not, however, the plain reading of the {\i gemara}, as Tosafot acknowledges. Rashi\u8217's reading \u8212- that the {\i hava amina} was literally that she hands over the money \u8212- is the straightforward {\i pshat} of the text. Tosafot is reframing the entire discussion to make it consistent with the conclusion that the father receives the money. In doing so, Tosafot surfaces a deeper tension: the {\i gemara} has two very different models in play simultaneously \u8212- the father-centered model derived from {\i ein kesef} and the {\i amah ivriyah}, and the two-party transactional model derived from {\i ki yikach} and Abraham\u8217's purchase of the field. At the very last moment, the {\i gemara} suggests that even the {\i ki yikach} model, taken alone, could have generated an unexpected conclusion \u8212- not that she receives the money, but that she gives it. These two contrasting models, and the tension between them, run through the entire {\i sugya} and animate the {\i gemara}\u8217's sustained need to derive {\i kiddushei kesef} from two independent {\i pesukim} rather than one.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Kiddushin 5\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Three Methods Overview\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Opening\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 We begin toward the bottom of 4b, carrying into 5a. The daf finds us already in the midst of a larger discussion: what are the {\i limudim} \u8212- the derivations \u8212- for each of the three different ways of effecting {\i kiddushin}? Those three ways are {\i kesef}, {\i shtar}, and {\i biah}. The {\i gemara} has already worked through most of the proofs for {\i kiddushei kesef} and has begun its treatment of {\i kiddushei biah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishnah vs.\u160 ?Gemara Order\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One immediately notices a structural peculiarity: the order of derivations in the {\i gemara} differs from the order presented in the {\i mishnah}. The {\i mishnah} proceeds {\i kesef}, {\i shtar}, {\i biah}; the {\i gemara} treats {\i shtar} last. We will encounter this reordering directly as we work through the {\i sugyot} today.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Kiddushei Biah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Uv\u8217'alah Drasha\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} cites the {\i pasuk}: {\i ki yikach ish isha uv\u8217'alah} \u8212- \u8220"when a man takes a wife and {\i ba\u8217'alah},\u8221" where {\i uv\u8217'alah} functions as an active verb, meaning he becomes her {\i ba\u8217'al}, or in a more graphic sense, he consummates the relationship. From this word the {\i gemara} derives: {\i uv\u8217'alah melamed sheniknet b\u8217'biah} \u8212- the word {\i uv\u8217'alah} teaches that the {\i kinyan} of {\i kiddushin} can be effected through a sexual act.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kiddushin vs.\u160 ?Nissuin\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is worth noting at this juncture that several {\i dappim} later the {\i gemara} will raise a crucial question: {\i biah kiddushin oseh o biah nissuin oseh?} \u8212- does that initial act of {\i biah} effect both stages of marriage simultaneously, jumping across both {\i kiddushin} and {\i nissuin}, or does it effect only {\i kiddushin}, leaving {\i nissuin} to be completed through a separate step? We register this question here, as it bears on everything that follows.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kal Vachomer: Yevamah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But the {\i gemara} immediately asks: is the word {\i uv\u8217'alah} even necessary? Could we not derive {\i kiddushei biah} through {\i kal vachomer}? The {\i gemara} proposes: {\i v\u8217'halo din hu} \u8212- is this not a logical inference? A {\i yevamah} cannot be acquired through {\i kesef} and yet she is acquired through {\i biah} (the act of {\i yibbum} is effected exclusively through {\i biah}). Surely then, in a normal marriage, where we already know {\i kesef} works, {\i biah} should work {\i kal vachomer} \u8212- money being more broadly applicable demonstrates that the avenue is even wider, and {\i biah} should follow.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Three Marriage Cases\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} throughout this {\i sugya} continually compares and contrasts three distinct cases: {\i yibbum}, {\i amah ivriyah}, and normal marriage. {\i Yibbum} is the situation where a man\u8217's brother has died childless; the surviving brother and the widow are already in a particular legal relationship \u8212- they must either consummate the {\i yibbum} or dissolve it through {\i chalitzah}. The {\i amah ivriyah} \u8212- a minor girl indentured by her father \u8212- occupies an intermediate position: it is primarily a labor arrangement, though as we shall see, the {\i gemara} insists it is not a marital relationship, while {\i tosafot} will complicate that picture considerably. These three cases interact with the three modes of {\i kiddushin} and with the comparison between {\i kiddushin} and {\i gerushin} \u8212- the {\i hekesh havayah l\u8217'yetziah} \u8212- and sorting out which comparisons are primary and which secondary goes to the core question of what the institution of {\i kiddushin} actually is and how it is effected.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Amah Ivriyah Exception\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i kal vachomer} from {\i yevamah} is challenged: {\i amah ivriyah tochiach} \u8212- the case of the indentured servant refutes the inference. The {\i amah ivriyah} can be acquired through {\i kesef} and yet cannot be acquired through {\i biah}: the transfer of her labor rights cannot be effected through a sexual act. The {\i kal vachomer} thus collapses.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} anticipates a reply: {\i mah l\u8217'amah ha\u8217'ivriyah she\u8217'ein kinyanah leshum ishut} \u8212- the {\i amah ivriyah} is not a marital relationship; perhaps we should limit her counter-example to non-marital contexts and allow the inference to proceed for relationships that are {\i leshum ishut}. The {\i gemara} responds that this is precisely why we need the {\i pasuk}: {\i talmud lomar uv\u8217'alah} \u8212- the word {\i uv\u8217'alah} is required to teach that a normal marriage can be effected through {\i biah}, because the {\i kal vachomer} cannot carry us there on its own.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Ashi Resolution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But why can the {\i kal vachomer} not carry us there? {\i Rav Ashi} explains: the difficulty lies not only with the {\i amah ivriyah} counterexample but with the {\i yevamah} premise itself. {\i Mah li\u8217'yevamah sheken zekukah ve\u8217'omedet} \u8212- what makes {\i yibbum} distinctive is the {\i zikah}, the pre-existing bond that forms between the {\i yavam} and the {\i yevamah} from the moment of the brother\u8217's death. The {\i yevamah} is already in a state of {\i zekukah ve\u8217'omedet} \u8212- she stands in a legal relationship with the {\i yavam} before any act of {\i biah} occurs. That antecedent {\i zikah} sets the stage and enables {\i biah} to consummate what is already, in some sense, a developing relationship. In normal marriage there is no such pre-existing {\i zikah}; the two parties are strangers in the legal sense. Therefore the {\i kal vachomer} fails from both ends: the {\i yevamah} premise is unique in its {\i zikah}, and the {\i amah ivriyah} counterexample undermines any broader application. All three cases \u8212- {\i yibbum}, {\i amah ivriyah}, and normal marriage \u8212- are legally distinct from one another. Hence: {\i talmud lomar uv\u8217'alah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is worth noting the background {\i machloket tanna\u8217'im} of {\i yesh zikah o ein zikah}, and the question of how substantial that {\i zikah} really is \u8212- whether according to some {\i tanna\u8217'im}, including Rabbi Akiva, the {\i zikah} is comparable in force to {\i kiddushin} itself, such that {\i yibbum} brings the relationship from a quasi-{\i mekudeshet} state to completion of {\i nissuin}. That discussion lies in the background of {\i Rav Ashi}\u8217's resolution.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafot Ye\u8217'ud\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot}, commenting toward the bottom of 4b, revisits the {\i amah ivriyah} and complicates the {\i gemara}\u8217's categorical dismissal of her relationship as non-marital. One of the reserved rights of the master over an {\i amah ivriyah} is {\i ye\u8217'ud}: he may designate her to marry himself or to marry his son. In this arrangement, the {\i kesef kiddushin} is retroactively absorbed into the original purchase price \u8212- the money paid at the time of the sale serves double duty. This {\i ye\u8217'ud} is unmistakably a marital dimension of the {\i amah ivriyah}\u8217's status. {\i Tosafot} therefore notes that while the {\i gemara} has categorically rejected the notion of {\i amah ivriyah she\u8217'einah leshum ishut}, the institution of {\i ye\u8217'ud} introduces genuine marital elements into the picture, and there are several additional points {\i tosafot} develops on this question.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Kiddushei Shtar\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shtar Derivation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Moving to 5a, the {\i gemara} turns to {\i kiddushei shtar}, introducing the question with the word {\i af} \u8212- \u8220"also\u8221": {\i u\u8217'minayin she\u8217'af b\u8217'shtar?} \u8212- from where do we know that {\i kiddushin} can be effected by giving a {\i shtar}, a document? The use of {\i af} signals that {\i shtar} is perhaps the least intuitive of the three methods, and we will see why throughout this {\i sugya}. The {\i gemara} follows the same structural template we have observed throughout: first propose a {\i kal vachomer}, then reject it, then introduce an independent {\i limud}. Only once all three independent {\i limudim} have been established will the {\i gemara} attempt to derive each method from the combination of the other two \u8212- the {\i tzad hashaveh} argument \u8212- and that discussion will unfold on {\i amud bet}. The underlying operating principle of the entire {\i sugya} is that it would be preferable to learn through {\i kal vachomer}, which requires no expenditure of {\i pesukim}, and resort to {\i drashot} only when necessary.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kal Vachomer: Kesef\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The proposed {\i kal vachomer}: {\i mah kesef she\u8217'ein motzi machnis} \u8212- money, which cannot be used to effect divorce, can nonetheless be used to enter into marriage. A {\i get} can only be a document; one cannot give {\i kesef} for divorce. {\i Shtar}, by contrast, can end a marriage \u8212- it is the very instrument of divorce. If money, which lacks the power to dissolve, can create the marital bond, surely {\i shtar}, which has the power to dissolve, should {\i kal vachomer} be able to create it as well?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is a striking argument, and worth pausing over. The {\i Rashba} frames it in terms of {\i koach deheteira adif}: the power required to create a {\i heter} where there was none is far greater than the power to create a prohibition. One can generate {\i issurim} relatively easily \u8212- through {\i nedarim}, through the principle of {\i shavei anafshei kachaticha de\u8217'issura}, and so forth. But to permit what is forbidden is impossible absent a genuine legal mechanism. The power of a {\i get} to accomplish exactly that \u8212- to permit a woman who would otherwise be a married woman to remarry \u8212- is extraordinary. {\i Shatarot} generally carry enormous legal weight in {\i halacha}: {\i Rabbi Yochanan} holds that witnesses who sign a {\i shtar} are treated as if their testimony had already been examined by a {\i beit din}, without requiring the usual {\i drisha ve\u8217'chakira}. So the suggestion that {\i shtar\u8217's} power to dissolve should yield {\i kal vachomer} a power to create is a serious argument, even if it will ultimately be rejected.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kesef Uniqueness\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i kal vachomer} fails because {\i kesef} has a unique quality that {\i shtar} lacks: {\i mah l\u8217'kesef sheken podim bo hekdesh u\u8217'ma\u8217'aser sheni} \u8212- money can be used for {\i pidyon}, to transfer the sanctified status of {\i ma\u8217'aser sheni} or {\i hekdesh} onto the coins themselves. {\i Ma\u8217'aser sheni}, which normally must be brought to Jerusalem and consumed there, can be redeemed onto a silver coin \u8212- {\i v\u8217'natan hakesef v\u8217'kam lo} \u8212- allowing the money to be brought instead and used to purchase food in Jerusalem. {\i Hekdesh} similarly can be redeemed. No {\i shtar} can accomplish this. {\i Rashi} raises the {\i hava amina}: what kind of {\i shtar} would one have imagined using for {\i ma\u8217'aser sheni}? Perhaps a {\i shtar chov} \u8212- a lien instrument, where one writes \u8220"I am obligated to bring five silver coins to Jerusalem for {\i ma\u8217'aser sheni}.\u8221" The {\i Rashba}, however, objects strongly: a {\i shtar kiddushin} and a {\i get} are not {\i shtarot chov}. They are {\i shtarot kinyan} \u8212- documents that describe and effect the transaction directly, as when one writes {\i harei perot elu peduyim b\u8217'shtar zo}. The {\i Rashba}\u8217's point is that the {\i hava amina} must be understood in terms of the correct category of {\i shtar}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i pidyon} comparison is also interesting in light of the earlier equation between {\i kiddushin} and {\i hekdesh} noted on 2b: {\i de\u8217'assar lah achulei alma kehekdesh} \u8212- the very word {\i kiddushin} draws on the parallel between marital sanctity and consecrated property, and the transfer of status that {\i kiddushin} effects is in some ways analogous to the transfer accomplished through {\i pidyon}. But the parallel remains imperfect, which is why the {\i kal vachomer} from {\i kesef} ultimately fails.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hekesh Havaya\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having rejected the {\i kal vachomer}, the {\i gemara} turns to an independent {\i limud}. The {\i pasuk} in the context of divorce reads: {\i v\u8217'yatza v\u8217'hayta l\u8217'ish acher} \u8212- she leaves and she can become the wife of another man. The {\i gemara} derives: {\i makish havayah l\u8217'yetziah} \u8212- comparing entry into marriage ({\i havayah}) to exit from marriage ({\i yetziah}). {\i Mah yetziah b\u8217'shtar af havayah nami b\u8217'shtar} \u8212- just as a woman is divorced through a {\i get}, so too she can be married through a {\i shtar kiddushin}. This is the {\i hekesh havayah l\u8217'yetziah}, and unlike the {\i kal vachomer}, it is a genuine {\i hekesh} drawn from the language of {\i mikra}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sanigor Katigor\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A natural question follows: could we not apply the {\i hekesh} in the reverse direction \u8212- {\i v\u8217'ikish nami yetziah l\u8217'havayah} \u8212- and derive from {\i kiddushei kesef} that divorce could also be accomplished through {\i kesef}? {\i Mah havayah b\u8217'kesef af yetziah b\u8217'kesef} \u8212- just as one enters marriage with money, perhaps one could also exit marriage with money?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Abaye} rejects this: {\i yomru kesef machnis kesef motzi} \u8212- people would say the same mechanism that creates the marriage dissolves it. This is {\i sanigor ya\u8217'aseh katigor} \u8212- the defense attorney has become the prosecuting attorney. The instrument by which a woman is bound has become the instrument by which she is released. It is worth noting that {\i kiddushei kesef} is the only method of {\i kiddushin} actually practiced in the time of {\i Chazal}, which is precisely why this concern arises: people would readily associate the two acts if both involved giving money.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Distinct Shtar Documents\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then raises an analogous concern about {\i shtar}: does not the {\i hekesh} cut both ways? If we derive {\i kiddushei shtar} from the fact that {\i yetziah} is accomplished through {\i shtar}, have we not simultaneously derived that {\i yetziah} could also be accomplished through the {\i shtar} used in {\i kiddushin} \u8212- {\i katigor ya\u8217'aseh sanigor}? The {\i gemara} dismisses this concern: {\i milei d\u8217'hai shtar l\u8217'chud u\u8217'milei d\u8217'hai shtar l\u8217'chud} \u8212- these are two entirely different {\i shtarot}. The words written in a {\i get} are unmistakably those of a {\i get}; the words written in a {\i shtar kiddushin} are unmistakably those of {\i kiddushin}. No one could confuse them. Each document is a unique and explicitly identifiable object. The {\i hekesh} therefore derives {\i kiddushei shtar} without creating any confusion about the instruments of divorce and marriage.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Fungibility Distinction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This resolution raises a further question: why can we not apply the same logic to {\i kesef} and say that {\i kiddushei kesef} coins are distinct from divorce coins, just as the two {\i shtarot} are distinct? The {\i gemara} answers: {\i tiba michad hu} \u8212- money has a single nature. Coins are fungible. A five-dollar bill is indistinguishable from any other five-dollar bill; if one is lost, any equivalent sum suffices as replacement. The very essence of {\i kesef} is its interchangeability. A {\i shtar}, by contrast, is a unique, specific object. One could never mistake a {\i get} for a {\i shtar kiddushin} because each is a particular document with particular language. But money given for {\i kiddushin} and money given for divorce would look and feel identical, and the principle of {\i ein kategor na\u8217'aseh sanegor} \u8212- which we apply, for instance, to prohibiting a cow\u8217's horn for a {\i shofar} on {\i Rosh Hashanah}, and to prohibiting the {\i Kohen Gadol} from wearing gold into the {\i Kodesh Kodashim} on {\i Yom Kippur} \u8212- compels us not to use the same medium for such antithetical purposes.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Yose HaGalili\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A loose end requires attention. {\i Rava} offers an alternative derivation that divorce cannot be accomplished through {\i kesef}: {\i amar kra v\u8217'chatav lah} \u8212- the {\i pasuk} explicitly says \u8220"and he shall write for her,\u8221" meaning {\i b\u8217'chtivah mitgareshet ve\u8217'einah mitgareshet b\u8217'kesef} \u8212- she is divorced only through writing, not through money. We spent three months in Masechet Gittin on this principle.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But then: {\i ve\u8217'eima b\u8217'chtivah mitgareshet ve\u8217'einah mitkaddeshet b\u8217'chtivah?} \u8212- why don\u8217't we use the same {\i pasuk} to exclude {\i shtar} from {\i kiddushin} rather than from divorce? The {\i gemara} answers that the {\i pasuk} of {\i v\u8217'yatza v\u8217'hayta} \u8212- the {\i hekesh havayah l\u8217'yetziah} \u8212- connects {\i kiddushin} to {\i gerushin}, and the question of which direction the {\i mi\u8217'ut} runs, and from which domain to which, is precisely the substance of the back-and-forth we have traced.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This raises the question of {\i Rabbi Yose HaGalili}, whose {\i drasha} in Gittin 21a uses the verse {\i v\u8217'chatav lah} for an entirely different {\i derasha}, deriving that she cannot be divorced with money. Since he deploys that verse elsewhere, where does {\i Rabbi Yose HaGalili} learn the prohibition on {\i get b\u8217'kesef}? {\i Amar kra sefer keritut} \u8212- the {\i pasuk} says {\i sefer keritut}, a \u8220"bill of severance,\u8221" implying {\i sefer kartah v\u8217'ein davar acher kartah} \u8212- only a document effects divorce, nothing else. {\i V\u8217'rabanan hai keritut mai avdi lei} \u8212- how do the Rabbis then use the word {\i keritut} rather than simply {\i karat}? {\i Mibaei lei l\u8217'davar hakoret beino l\u8217'beina} \u8212- they require the extra form to teach that the {\i get} must constitute a complete severance between the husband and wife at the time of divorce. This is the {\i baraita} we encountered recently: {\i harei zeh gitech al m\u8217'nat shelo tishti yayin al m\u8217'nat shelo telchi l\u8217'veit avich l\u8217'olam ein zeh keritut} \u8212- a divorce conditioned on permanent constraints is not a valid {\i keritut} because the husband retains residual power over her. {\i Kol shloshim yom harei zeh keritut} \u8212- if the condition is limited to thirty days, it is a valid severance. The {\i Rashba} in a {\i teshuvah} addresses the boundary cases \u8212- what about fifty years? What about conditions that end with the death of one of the parties? {\i Tosafot} here likewise engages with those questions. For {\i Rabbi Yose HaGalili}, by contrast, the doubled form {\i keritut} over {\i karat} yields both {\i drashot} simultaneously. {\i V\u8217'rabanan karat keritut lo mashma lehu} \u8212- the Rabbis do not read the derivation from singular to plural. {\i Chada mechada lo atya} \u8212- one cannot be derived from the other.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Learned One from Two\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Deriving Shtar\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having established that each of the three methods cannot be derived from either of the other two individually \u8212- {\i chada mechada lo atya} \u8212- the {\i gemara} asks whether one might be derivable from the combination of the other two: {\i teiti chada mitarti}. Could {\i shtar} be derived from {\i biah} and {\i kesef} together?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hana\u8217'ah Distinction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 No.\u160 ?{\i Biah} and {\i kesef} share a common feature that {\i shtar} lacks: {\i hana\u8217'atan merubah} \u8212- they both involve {\i hana\u8217'ah}, benefit or pleasure. Money has intrinsic value; there is tangible benefit in receiving it. {\i Biah} involves physical pleasure. A {\i shtar}, by contrast, is a purely legal object. It can be worth less than a {\i pruta}. It carries no intrinsic {\i hana\u8217'ah} whatsoever. Because {\i shtar} lacks this shared quality of {\i biah} and {\i kesef}, we cannot derive it from their combination.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This raises a subtle question addressed by the {\i Sha\u8217'ar HaMelech} in a different context: if {\i biah} works as a mode of {\i kiddushin} because it involves {\i hana\u8217'ah}, what would be the status of {\i kiddushin} effected through anal intercourse, which {\i Chazal} understand to involve diminished {\i hana\u8217'ah}? He suggests the resolution may be that {\i biah} does not actually work through a transfer of {\i hana\u8217'ah} at all \u8212- rather, it is the {\i ma\u8217'aseh kinyan} itself, the legal act, that matters. The {\i gemara}\u8217's statement that {\i biah} is characterized by {\i hana\u8217'atan merubah} would then describe a general feature of the category, not a mechanism on which the {\i kinyan} depends. This is an important conceptual question about how much the {\i gemara}\u8217's formulation reflects an intrinsic requirement versus a descriptive characterization.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Deriving Kesef\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Could {\i kesef} be derived from {\i biah} and {\i shtar} combined?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Biah Not Normal Kinyan\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 No.\u160 ?{\i Biah} and {\i shtar} share a quality that {\i kesef} lacks: {\i kinyanan merubah} \u8212- {\i kesef} and {\i shtar} are general-purpose instruments of {\i kinyan}, applicable across many domains of acquisition including the purchase of land and the acquisition of slaves (topics the rest of the {\i perek} will address). {\i Biah}, by contrast, is not a normal mode of {\i kinyan}. One does not acquire a piece of land by {\i biah}. It is an act specific to particular marital and quasi-marital contexts. And since {\i biah} lacks the broader {\i kinyan} applicability shared by {\i kesef} and {\i shtar}, the combination cannot yield {\i kesef}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Biah Chazakah Analogy\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} on 3a entertains \u8212- and rejects \u8212- the suggestion that {\i biah} might be compared to {\i chazakah} in the acquisition of land, whereby one demonstrates ownership by plowing, fencing, or traversing the property. Like {\i biah}, {\i chazakah} is a physical act that demonstrates the reality of a relationship between the acquirer and what is being acquired. The parallel is genuinely interesting, and it touches on sensitive questions about how the woman\u8217's role in {\i kiddushei biah} relates to the role of the field in {\i kinyan chazakah}. {\i Tosafot} rejects the analogy, noting that these are fundamentally different legal acts \u8212- though the comparison is serious enough to warrant extended treatment.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Deriving Biah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Could {\i biah} be derived from {\i kesef} and {\i shtar} combined?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Be\u8217'al Korchah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 No.\u160 ?{\i Kesef} and {\i shtar} share a quality that {\i biah} lacks in the marital context: {\i yeshnan b\u8217'al korchah} \u8212- they can operate {\i b\u8217'al korchah}, without the woman\u8217's consent. A husband can give his wife a {\i get} against her will (prior to {\i Rabbeinu Gershom}\u8217's {\i takkanah}). {\i Biah} as a mode of {\i kiddushin} in a normal marriage cannot occur {\i b\u8217'al korchah} \u8212- valid marital {\i biah} requires the woman\u8217's {\i da\u8217'at}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} notes that {\i yibbum} seems to complicate this: the {\i biah} of {\i yibbum} can be {\i b\u8217'al korchah} in some sense, because the {\i yevamah\u8217's} agreement to the legal entry into a marital relationship with this particular man is not required at the moment of {\i yibbum}, given that the {\i zikah} already creates a pre-existing bond. But that is specifically {\i yibbum}, not a normal marital {\i biah}, and the distinction holds.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Regarding {\i kesef b\u8217'al korchah}: {\i tosafot} raises the case of {\i amah ivriyah}, where a father sells his minor daughter into indenture \u8212- seemingly imposing the arrangement upon her. But {\i tosafot} resolves this by noting that the father in this case is the minor\u8217's legal agent; because she is not yet a {\i bar da\u8217'at}, there is no {\i ratzon} of her own that is being overridden. The father\u8217's {\i da\u8217'at} substitutes for hers, and it is therefore not classified as {\i b\u8217'al korchah} in the technical sense.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rav Huna Chuppah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chuppah Kal Vachomer\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 We have now concluded the discussion of the three established modes of {\i kiddushin}. Each requires its own independent {\i limud}; none can be derived from any other singly; and each possesses a unique quality \u8212- {\i shtar} has no {\i hana\u8217'ah}, {\i biah} is not a general-purpose {\i kinyan}, and {\i kesef} does not exist {\i b\u8217'al korchah} in a marital context \u8212- that defeats any attempt at {\i tzad hashaveh} derivation from the combination of the other two. It is striking to consider that three distinct processes all yield the same legal outcome and yet have such fundamentally different character.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rav Huna} now proposes something new: {\i chuppah koneh m\u8217'kal vachomer} \u8212- {\i chuppah} should be able to effect {\i kiddushin} through {\i kal vachomer}. {\i Chuppah} is the rabbinic term for {\i nissuin}, the second stage of marriage, and the {\i rishonim} have a wide-ranging {\i machloket} \u8212- something like seven positions \u8212- regarding what {\i chuppah} actually refers to: wrapping the couple in a shared garment (as in some {\i Sephardi} practice), spreading a canopy over them, veiling the bride, {\i yichud}, the act of {\i biah}, or entering the shared home. Each identification carries significant legal implications.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kesef Terumah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rav Huna}\u8217's argument: {\i chuppah koneh m\u8217'kal vachomer umah kesef she\u8217'eino ma\u8217'achil b\u8217'terumah koneh, chuppah she\u8217'ma\u8217'achelet b\u8217'terumah eino din she\u8217'tikneh} \u8212- money, which does not grant the woman the right to eat {\i terumah}, nonetheless effects {\i kiddushin}. {\i Chuppah}, which does grant that right (when a woman enters {\i nissuin} with a {\i kohen}), should certainly effect {\i kiddushin kal vachomer}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Woman Eating Terumah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} challenges the premise: does {\i kesef} truly not allow the woman to eat {\i terumah}? {\i V\u8217'ha amar Ulla devar Torah arusa bat Yisrael ochelet b\u8217'terumah} \u8212- {\i Ulla} holds that {\i mid\u8217'oraita}, a woman who is only {\i mekudeshet} \u8212- {\i arusah} \u8212- may eat {\i terumah}, because the {\i pasuk} states {\i v\u8217'kohen ki yikneh nefesh kinyan kaspo} \u8212- a {\i kohen\u8217's} wife is his {\i kinyan kaspo}, and since marriage is effected as {\i kinyan kaspo}, she is entitled to eat {\i terumah} from the point of {\i kiddushin}. She is {\i kinyan kaspo} already from that stage.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbinic Gezeirah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The reason {\i miderabbanan} an {\i arusah} cannot eat {\i terumah} is a {\i gezeirah}: {\i shema yimzegu lah kos b\u8217'veit aviha v\u8217'tashkeihu l\u8217'acheiha v\u8217'l\u8217'achayoteiha} \u8212- perhaps her family will pour her a cup of {\i terumah} wine in her father\u8217's house and she will share it with her siblings, who are not married to {\i kohanim} and may not eat {\i terumah}. The rabbinic prohibition on {\i terumah} for an {\i arusah} is thus a prophylactic measure, not a reflection of her actual status {\i mid\u8217'oraita}. So {\i kal vachomer}, the {\i kesef} also allows eating {\i terumah} \u8212- Rav Huna\u8217's premise is undermined.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Biah Counter\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then reformulates the {\i kal vachomer} correctly: {\i umah kesef she\u8217'eino gomer koneh, chuppah she\u8217'gomeret eino din she\u8217'tikneh} \u8212- money, which does not complete the marriage (it only effects {\i kiddushin}, not {\i nissuin}), nonetheless creates the {\i kinyan} of {\i kiddushin}. {\i Chuppah}, which completes the marriage by effecting {\i nissuin}, should {\i kal vachomer} be able to effect {\i kiddushin} as well. The counter from {\i biah} is raised: {\i biah tochiach} \u8212- {\i biah} shows that a mode of {\i kinyan} can work for {\i kiddushin}. And {\i mah l\u8217'biah sheken konah b\u8217'yevamah} \u8212- {\i biah} is distinctive in that it effects a {\i kinyan} in the context of {\i yibbum}, which has no parallel for {\i chuppah}. {\i Kesef yochiach} \u8212- and around the cycle we go, each counterexample being refuted by the other. {\i Lo rai zeh k\u8217'rai zeh v\u8217'lo rai zeh k\u8217'rai zeh} \u8212- each has its defects but they are not identical defects.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kesef Yochiach\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} works through the {\i tzad hashaveh}: {\i hatzad hashaveh shebahen shekonin b\u8217'alma v\u8217'koneh kan} \u8212- the common denominator of {\i kesef} and {\i biah} is that they are both instruments of {\i kinyan} in the general sense and they effect {\i kiddushin}. {\i Af ani avi chuppah shekonin b\u8217'alma v\u8217'koneh kan} \u8212- I will bring {\i chuppah} also as an instrument of {\i kinyan} in the general sense, and it too should effect {\i kiddushin}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tzad HaShaveh\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i tzad hashaveh} argument is pressed further. {\i Mah l\u8217'tzad hashaveh shebahen sheken hana\u8217'ah merubah} \u8212- but the common denominator of {\i biah} and {\i kesef} is that they involve {\i hana\u8217'ah}, which {\i chuppah} does not. {\i Shtar yochiach} \u8212- let {\i shtar} serve as the refutation: {\i shtar} has no {\i hana\u8217'ah} and still effects {\i kiddushin}. {\i Mah l\u8217'shtar sheken motzi} \u8212- {\i shtar} is unique in that it can dissolve a marriage through {\i get}, which {\i chuppah} cannot do.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hana\u8217'ah Objection\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Kesef u\u8217'biah yochiach} \u8212- {\i kesef} and {\i biah} will show that the capacity to dissolve is not required. {\i V\u8217'hazar hadin} \u8212- the argument cycles again. {\i Lo rai zeh k\u8217'rai zeh v\u8217'lo rai zeh k\u8217'rai zeh} \u8212- each method, again, has a disqualifying factor the others lack.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Unique Factors\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Mah l\u8217'tzad hashaveh shebahen sheken yeshnan b\u8217'al korchah} \u8212- the common denominator of {\i shtar} and {\i biah} is that both can exist in a context of {\i b\u8217'al korchah}, which {\i kesef} \u8212- and likewise {\i chuppah} \u8212- cannot. {\i V\u8217'Rav Huna kesef mi ha b\u8217'isha lo ashkechan b\u8217'al korchah} \u8212- and {\i Rav Huna} has no answer to the fact that {\i kesef} does not exist in a marital context that is {\i b\u8217'al korchah}. The {\i kal vachomer} cannot be completed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava Objection One\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Amar Rava shtei teshuvot b\u8217'davar} \u8212- {\i Rava} raises two independent objections to {\i Rav Huna}. First: {\i chada d\u8217'shalosh tnan v\u8217'lo arba} \u8212- the {\i mishnah} explicitly states {\i ha\u8217'isha niknet b\u8217'shalosh drachim}, a woman can be married in three ways, not four. {\i Chuppah} is not among the three.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava Objection Two\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Second, and more fundamentally: {\i v\u8217'od klum chuppah gomeret ela al yedei kiddushin} \u8212- {\i chuppah} only effects {\i nissuin} in the wake of prior {\i kiddushin}. It presupposes {\i kiddushin}; it does not operate independently. How then can {\i Rav Huna} propose a {\i kal vachomer} that {\i chuppah} \u8212- which can complete marriage \u8212- should be able to do what the other methods do? {\i Chuppah} completing marriage is a function that operates only once {\i kiddushin} has already occurred. It cannot be bootstrapped into being a method of {\i kiddushin} itself.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Abaye Defense\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Abaye} defends {\i Rav Huna} against {\i Rava}\u8217's first objection: {\i tana milta d\u8217'chtiva b\u8217'hedya katanei, milta delo chtiva b\u8217'hedya lo katanei} \u8212- the {\i mishnah} lists only what is explicitly derivable from {\i pesukim}. {\i Chuppah}, being derived through {\i kal vachomer} rather than from explicit scriptural language, was not listed \u8212- but this is {\i tana veshayer}, the {\i tanna} listed only a subset. The {\i mishnah}\u8217's enumeration of three therefore does not refute the possibility of a fourth.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is perhaps not the most compelling answer, and {\i Rava}\u8217's second objection remains formidable.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Huna Response\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Abaye} then addresses the second objection on {\i Rav Huna}\u8217's behalf: {\i Rav Huna} can actually incorporate {\i Rava}\u8217's challenge into the {\i kal vachomer} and make it even stronger. {\i Mah kesef she\u8217'eino gomer achar kesef koneh} \u8212- money cannot effect {\i nissuin} even after {\i kiddushei kesef} has been performed; giving a second ring does not constitute {\i nissuin}. {\i Chuppah shegomeret achar kesef eino din shetikneh} \u8212- since {\i chuppah} does work after {\i kesef} (it effects {\i nissuin} following {\i kiddushei kesef}), should it not {\i kal vachomer} be able to effect {\i kiddushin} as well?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafot HaRosh\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This leads into the {\i Tosafot HaRosh}\u8217's question: when {\i Rav Huna} says {\i chuppah koneh m\u8217'kal vachomer}, does he mean {\i chuppah} effecting {\i kiddushin} and {\i nissuin} together in a single act, or does he mean {\i chuppah} effecting {\i kiddushin} alone \u8212- requiring some separate act for {\i nissuin} afterward? This is a fundamental question about the shape of {\i Rav Huna}\u8217's position and it remains unresolved within the {\i sugya}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 We note that while {\i Rava}\u8217's objections are serious and we generally rule like {\i Rava} over {\i Abaye}, the {\i Rif} may rule like {\i Rav Huna} \u8212- a notable exception. The practical {\i nafka minah} is rare, but one can imagine the question arising in a tragic case where, for example, a {\i kallah} collapsed and died immediately after {\i bede\u8217'ken} and before a conventional {\i kiddushin} \u8212- and the question becomes whether any form of {\i chuppah} was completed, and whether the {\i kohen} husband is permitted to be {\i mitamei} for her as a full wife. Such cases do not arise often, but the theoretical weight of {\i Rav Huna}\u8217's position is not nothing.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Kiddushei Kesef Mechanics\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Man\u8217's Action Required\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now pivots to the practical mechanics of {\i kiddushei kesef}. A {\i baraita} is cited: {\i natan lah kesef o shaveh kesef v\u8217'amar lah harei at mekudeshet li, harei at me\u8217'ureset li, harei at li le\u8217'intu} \u8212- he gives her money or its equivalent and says one of several designated formulas: \u8220"You are {\i mekudeshet} to me,\u8221" \u8220"You are betrothed to me,\u8221" \u8220"You are my wife.\u8221" {\i Harei zu mekudeshet} \u8212- she is thereby married. The {\i kiddushin} requires both the act of giving and an accompanying verbal declaration, an {\i amira}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Woman\u8217's Declaration Und\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i baraita} continues: {\i aval hi she\u8217'natnah lo v\u8217'amrah hi} \u8212- but if she gives the ring to him and she is the one who speaks \u8212- {\i lo havei kiddushin} \u8212- they are not married. Two variables have changed: she gave rather than he, and she spoke rather than he. This reflects the {\i pasuk} of {\i ki yikach ish isha} \u8212- the man takes the wife, giving him the more active legal role in the process.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Pappa Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rav Pappa} is troubled by the {\i baraita}: {\i ta\u8217'ama d\u8217'natan hu v\u8217'amar hu} \u8212- the reason the first case works is that he gave and he said. From the {\i reisha} one might infer: if he gave but she said \u8212- that would not work. From the {\i seifa} one might infer: the reason it fails is that she gave and she said \u8212- but if he gave and she said, it would work. The two halves of the {\i baraita} appear to contradict each other on this intermediate case, and {\i Rav Pappa} wants to know the answer.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Reisha Davka\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} resolves: {\i reisha davka} \u8212- the first clause is precise. For valid {\i kiddushin}, the man must both give and declare. The {\i seifa} simply came along for parallel formulation and does not yield independent inferences.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Woman\u8217's Amira Effect\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But the {\i gemara} elaborates more carefully: if the man gives the money and the woman is the one who speaks, this is treated {\i ke\u8217'mi she\u8217'natnah hi v\u8217'amrah hi} \u8212- as if she had given it and said it. Her act of {\i amira} when he gives effectively makes the act hers, such that it is not {\i kiddushin}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Safek Kiddushin\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 An alternative reading: {\i natan hu v\u8217'amrah hi safeka hi v\u8217'chaishinan miderabbanan} \u8212- if he gives and she speaks, this may be {\i safek kiddushin}. That is, there is genuine doubt whether her declaration plays the role of the required {\i amira}. Under this view, were she to go and marry another man, she would require a {\i get} from both. The first view would say it is simply not {\i kiddushin} at all; the second view treats it as {\i safek}. This unresolved question about the {\i amira}\u8217's role \u8212- and whether the woman\u8217's participation in the declaration can either substitute for or undermine the man\u8217's declaration \u8212- speaks to the broader question of what precisely the verbal component of {\i kiddushin} accomplishes.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Shmuel Amira Rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Required Formulations\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Shmuel} addresses the {\i amira} requirements directly. If the man gives {\i kesef} or its equivalent and says {\i harei at mekudeshet} \u8212- \u8220"you are {\i mekudeshet}\u8221" \u8212- or {\i harei at me\u8217'ureset}, or {\i harei at le\u8217'intu} \u8212- \u8220"you are my wife\u8221" \u8212- she is married. These formulations share a common feature: they describe a change in the {\i woman\u8217's} status. She becomes {\i mekudeshet}, she becomes {\i me\u8217'ureset}, she becomes {\i le\u8217'intu}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Man Status Doesn\u8217't Change\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But if the man says {\i hareini isheich} \u8212- \u8220"I am your husband\u8221" \u8212- or {\i hareini ba\u8217'aleich} \u8212- \u8220"I am your {\i ba\u8217'al}\u8221" \u8212- or {\i hareini aruseich} \u8212- \u8220"I am betrothed to you\u8221" \u8212- {\i ein kan beit michush} \u8212- there is not even cause for concern. This is simply not {\i kiddushin}. The man\u8217's status does not change through the act of {\i kiddushin}. He can go and marry another wife; nothing in his legal standing is transformed. It is the woman\u8217's status that changes: she becomes forbidden to all other men, she requires a {\i get} to remarry, and an entire structure of obligations and prohibitions attaches to her. When the man declares a change in his own status rather than hers, the declaration is legally inert.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Status Change Principle\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The same principle applies in {\i gerushin}: {\i v\u8217'chatav lah v\u8217'natan b\u8217'yadah} \u8212- the {\i pasuk} requires him to write {\i for her} and give it {\i into her hand}. If a man says {\i harei at meshulachat} \u8212- \u8220"you are sent away\u8221" \u8212- or {\i harei at megureshet} \u8212- \u8220"you are divorced\u8221" \u8212- or {\i harei at muteret l\u8217'chol adam} \u8212- \u8220"you are permitted to every man\u8221" \u8212- {\i harei zu megureshet}, she is divorced. These formulas change the woman\u8217's status. But if he says {\i eini ishech, eini ba\u8217'alech, eini aruseich} \u8212- \u8220"I am no longer your husband, I am no longer your {\i ba\u8217'al}, I am no longer betrothed to you\u8221" \u8212- this is again {\i ein kan beit michush}. He has described a change in his own status, not hers, and a divorce requires changing hers.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yadayim Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rav Pappa} asks {\i Abaye}: does this ruling of {\i Shmuel} imply that {\i Shmuel} holds {\i yadayim she\u8217'einan mochiyot havyan yadayim} \u8212- that an indirect expression, even one that does not explicitly denote the intended legal act, constitutes a valid declaration? After all, {\i Shmuel} rules that saying {\i harei at mekudeshet} without specifying {\i li} \u8212- \u8220"to me\u8221" \u8212- is sufficient. Is this not accepting an incomplete expression?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Nazir Yadayim Example\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rav Pappa} raises the counterexample from {\i Nazir}: if a person sees a {\i nazir} passing by and says {\i aha} \u8212- pointing at him \u8212- is he himself a {\i nazir}? {\i Shmuel} rules: {\i ve\u8217'hu she\u8217'hayah nazir over lefanav} \u8212- only if the {\i nazir} was actually passing by at that moment. {\i Ta\u8217'ama d\u8217'nazir over lefanav hu, lav hachi lo} \u8212- the reason it works is that the {\i nazir} was physically present, making the reference unambiguous; without that context, the exclamation would not suffice. This suggests {\i Shmuel} holds {\i yadayim she\u8217'einan mochiyot einan yadayim} \u8212- imprecise expressions are not valid declarations.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Status Change Resolution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} resolves: {\i Shmuel}\u8217's ruling about {\i kiddushin} teaches not that imprecise expressions are valid, but rather something about whose status must be described. The {\i chiddush} is that the man must declare a change in the {\i woman\u8217's} status \u8212- not his own. {\i Hacha k\u8217'siv ki yikach v\u8217'lo she\u8217'yikach et atzmo} \u8212- the {\i pasuk} says {\i ki yikach ish isha}, \u8220"a man shall take a wife\u8221" \u8212- the act of taking is directed toward her, not toward himself. He cannot \u8220"take himself\u8221" to her. And {\i v\u8217'hacha k\u8217'siv v\u8217'shilchah v\u8217'lo she\u8217'yishlach et atzmo} \u8212- the {\i pasuk} of divorce says {\i v\u8217'shilchah}, \u8220"and he shall send her away\u8221" \u8212- he cannot divorce himself from her. In both marriage and divorce, it is the woman\u8217's legal status that is the object of the act, and the verbal declaration must reflect that.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Kiddushin 6\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Kiddushin Formulatio (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Six Formulations\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} on Kiddushin 6a resumes mid-discussion with a {\i tanna rabbanan}, continuing the examination of which linguistic formulations ({\i lashonot}) constitute a valid {\i ma\u8217'amar} of {\i kiddushin}. The {\i beraita} presents six formulations, grouped in two sets of three. In the first set: {\i harei at ishti} (\u8220"you are my wife\u8221"), {\i harei at arusati} (\u8220"you are my {\i arusa}\u8221"), and {\i harei at knuya li} (\u8220"you are acquired to me\u8221") \u8212- in any of these cases, she is {\i mekudeshet}. In the second set: {\i harei at sheli} (\u8220"you are mine\u8221"), {\i harei at birshuti} (\u8220"you are in my domain\u8221"), and {\i harei at zekuka li} (\u8220"you are bound to me\u8221") \u8212- these, too, effectuate {\i kiddushin}. The term {\i zekuka} carries connotations similar to {\i zika} in the context of {\i yibbum}, conveying a sense of being halachically bound or attached.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} asks why these six formulations are presented as two separate groupings rather than a single unified list. The answer is simply that this is how the tradition was received: {\i tana telat shmi\u8217'ineihu ve-garsineihu} \u8212- he heard them in sets of three and transmitted them that way. Grouping teachings in threes is a familiar mnemonic convention, even when the internal logic might not demand the division.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ambiguous Formulations\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then turns to a series of more ambiguous formulations and poses the question {\i mahu} \u8212- is this effective or not? The list includes: {\i meiyuchedet li} (\u8220"singled out to me\u8221"), {\i meyu\u8217'edet li} (\u8220"designated to me,\u8221" drawing on the language of {\i ya\u8217'adah} from the passage about the {\i bat la\u8217'amah}), {\i ezrati} (\u8220"my helpmate,\u8221" from {\i ezer kinegdo} in {\i Bereishit}), {\i negdati} (\u8220"alongside me\u8221"), {\i atzurati} (from {\i atzeret}, suggesting being gathered together with him at home), {\i tzalati} (from {\i vayikach achat mitzal\u8217'otav}, God\u8217's taking of the {\i tzela} from {\i Adam}), {\i segurati} (from {\i vayisgor basar tachtena}, the enclosing of flesh), {\i tachtai} (\u8220"beneath me,\u8221" from the same verse), and {\i tefusati} (from the language of {\i nitpasha} in the context of a {\i na\u8217'ara me\u8217'orasa} \u8212- carrying connotations of being seized, which, rightly or wrongly, implies the married relationship).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One of these ambiguous cases admits of a ready resolution. The formulation {\i lekuchati} \u8212- \u8220"my taken one\u8221" \u8212- is validated by a {\i beraita}: {\i omer lekuchati harei zu mekudeshet}, because the {\i Torah} itself uses the root {\i kicha} to describe betrothal: {\i ki yikach ish isha}. The term {\i likuchin} is the {\i Torah\u8217's} own word for this act, and so this formulation clearly works.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Charufah Usage\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then raises the case of {\i charufati} \u8212- a term derived from the passage {\i shifcha nechereset l\u8217'ish}, which describes a slave-woman who is {\i nechereset}, or designated, to a man. Does using this term constitute a valid {\i ma\u8217'amar} of {\i kiddushin}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i beraita} is cited: {\i omer charufati mekudeshet} \u8212- yes, it works. The {\i gemara} initially explains this on the basis that {\i bi-Yehudah korin l\u8217'arusa charufa} \u8212- in Judea, {\i arusa} is colloquially called {\i charufa}. The {\i gemara} challenges this: if we already have a {\i pasuk} supporting the term, why do we additionally need to appeal to regional Judean usage? And conversely, if the {\i pasuk} alone is sufficient, why does the regional usage matter?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 After two rounds of revision, the {\i gemara} settles on the following reading: {\i omer charufa bi-Yehudah mekudeshet} \u8212- if one uses this formulation specifically {\i in Judea}, it is effective, because there the word is understood to mean {\i arusa}, likely by association with the {\i pasuk}. But if one uses this term outside of Judea, where the word does not carry that meaning, it would presumably not work.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Context Requirement\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This leads into a broader structural question: all these ambiguous formulations \u8212- {\i tzalati}, {\i segurati}, {\i atzurati}, and the rest \u8212- in what kind of case are we evaluating them? If the parties were not already engaged in conversation about {\i kiddushin} and related matters ({\i iskei gitta ve-kiddusheha}), how would she even know what he means? A bizarre formulation like {\i harei at segurati} or {\i harei at atzurati} would be unintelligible to her without context. And since, unlike a {\i get}, which can be given against the woman\u8217's will, {\i kiddushin} requires her knowing consent \u8212- if she has no idea what he is saying, she cannot meaningfully agree.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But if they {\i are} already talking about marriage-related matters, then \u8212- the {\i gemara} points out \u8212- even if he said nothing at all when giving her the money, it would be valid. This is the ruling of the Mishnah: if a man was speaking with a woman about the matters of her {\i get} and {\i kiddushin} and gave her the document or money without making any verbal declaration ({\i velo peireish}), Rabbi Yose says {\i dayo} \u8212- it is sufficient. Rabbi Yehudah disagrees and holds that one must explicitly state what the act is for.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} cites Rav Huna in the name of Shmuel: {\i halachah ke-Rabbi Yose}. And since even silence is sufficient according to Rabbi Yose, the question about these ambiguous formulations becomes sharper \u8212- if you don\u8217't need to say anything, what does it add to say something ambiguous?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} resolves this by identifying a middle case: the ambiguous formulation is actually more problematic than silence. When parties are engaged in conversation about marriage and one simply hands over the ring without speaking, an outside observer understands perfectly what is happening. But when one uses a strange, oblique phrase \u8212- {\i harei at segurati} or {\i harei at izmargadi} \u8212- one might wonder whether the speaker is trying to redirect the conversation. Perhaps he is now talking about hiring her for work. The ambiguous statement introduces doubt where silence would not.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Silent Kiddushin\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yossi vs.\u160 ?Yehudah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 To review the Mishnah\u8217's dispute more carefully: {\i hayah medaber im ha\u8217'ishah al iskei gitta ve-kiddusheha ve-natan lah gitta ve-kiddusheha velo peireish} \u8212- he was speaking with the woman about matters of {\i get} and {\i kiddushin}, then gave her the document or the money, without specifying what it was for. Rabbi Yose says this is sufficient \u8212- {\i dayo}. Rabbi Yehudah says he must explicitly declare his intent \u8212- {\i tzarich le-fareish}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Halachah: Context Suffic\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Huna reports in the name of Shmuel that {\i halachah ke-Rabbi Yose}. A further qualification is added by Rav Yehudah in the name of Shmuel: even Rabbi Yose concedes that this only applies {\i ve-hu she-asukin be-oto inyan} \u8212- provided they are still actively engaged in that same conversation when the act is performed. If they had moved on to other topics before he handed her the money, the contextual understanding that makes silent {\i kiddushin} effective is no longer present. Rav Elazar in the name of Rabbi Oshaya concurs. A {\i beraita} also records Rabbi [Yehudah ha-Nasi] agreeing with this requirement.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Elazar be-Rabbi Shimon, however, holds that even if they are no longer discussing the same matter, the {\i kiddushin} can still be valid. The {\i gemara} asks: if they have already moved on to a different topic, how does she know what he means? Abaye explains: {\i mitoch inyan le-inyan} \u8212- not a completely unrelated topic, but a related one. So for example, if they had been discussing the {\i kiddushin} itself and then shifted to discussing the financial arrangements of the marriage \u8212- the dowry and assets \u8212- that peripheral conversation is close enough that the subsequent act of giving money is still understood in context, per Rashi.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i halachah} follows Rabbi Yose: {\i amar Rav Huna amar Shmuel halachah ke-Rabbi Yose}. Rav Ami and Rav Assi then apply this in connection with another statement of Shmuel \u8212- that anyone who does not have thorough command of the laws of {\i gittin} and {\i kiddushin} should not involve himself in adjudicating such cases. They suggest that even ignorance of this specific {\i halachah} of Rabbi Yose would qualify one as insufficiently expert to sit on such a {\i beit din}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Kiddushin Formulatio (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Witnesses & Intent\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara\u8217's} analysis of ambiguous formulations prompts a deep examination in {\i Tosafot ha-Rosh ha-Zaken}. The issue is not merely what the two parties understood between themselves, but what an objective third party \u8212- specifically, the {\i edim}, the witnesses \u8212- would have understood at that moment.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot ha-Rosh ha-Zaken} makes the following point: even if both parties afterward declare that they understood the exchange to constitute {\i kiddushin} \u8212- even if the man says he intended it for {\i kiddushin} and the woman says she understood it as such and consented \u8212- this is still insufficient. The reason is {\i harei edim lo nitbarer lahem amitat ha-lashon be-et she-shamu} \u8212- at the moment the witnesses heard the words, the true meaning of the formulation was not clear to them. The validity of {\i kiddushin} cannot rest purely on the subjective agreement of the two parties; it requires that the act be objectively comprehensible to outside observers at the time it occurs.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is not because the witnesses\u8217' understanding supersedes that of the principals, but because {\i kiddushin} is a legally constituted act that requires objectively intelligible expression. If a stranger were dropped into the middle of the scene at that moment, would they understand what was taking place? That is the standard.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot ha-Rosh ha-Zaken} then offers a path by which even an ambiguous formulation could work: {\i im techila amru la-edim} \u8212- if, before the act, he informed the witnesses in advance, explaining \u8220"I am going to give her {\i kiddushin} by means of this particular formulation,\u8221" and she subsequently confirmed that she understood it as {\i kiddushin}, then {\i vadai havu kiddushin} \u8212- it is certainly valid {\i kiddushin}. Why? Because at the moment the witnesses heard the formulation, {\i nitbarer lahem she-hem kiddushin} \u8212- it had already been made clear to them that this is what they would be witnessing. The context was established in advance, and the act is therefore objectively understood.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The practical upshot is that there is an objective but flexible standard. It cannot be entirely subjective \u8212- the two parties\u8217' private mutual understanding is not enough on its own. But the objective understanding can be established through advance disclosure to the witnesses. What cannot work is an ambiguous phrase taken in isolation, with nothing establishing its meaning for outside observers at the time of the act.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This explains why the {\i gemara} found silence to be less problematic than an ambiguous statement. When the parties are {\i asukim be-oto inyan} and the man silently gives money or a ring, any witness present understands perfectly what is occurring \u8212- the entire conversation has provided the context. But an odd phrase inserted into the flow of that conversation could, paradoxically, disrupt the clarity of the situation: it might suggest a shift of subject, introducing ambiguity where the silent act would have left none.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Get Formulations\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Divorce Language\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Parallel to the {\i kiddushin} formulations, the {\i gemara} now surveys valid {\i lashonot} for a {\i get}. If a man gives his wife a {\i get} and says {\i harei at meshulachet} (\u8220"you are sent away\u8221"), {\i harei at megureshet} (\u8220"you are divorced\u8221"), or {\i harei at muteret le-chol adam} (\u8220"you are permitted to any man\u8221"), she is divorced. These are straightforward and clearly effective.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Reversed Language\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then considers switched formulations. If a man gives his wife a {\i get} and says {\i harei at bat chorin} (\u8220"you are free\u8221") \u8212- a phrase appropriate for freeing a slave \u8212- he has said nothing effective. Similarly, if he says to his {\i shifchah} {\i harei at muteret le-chol adam} \u8212- a phrase from the {\i get} context \u8212- that too is ineffective. Each formula was misapplied: you used the slave\u8217's formula for your wife, and the wife\u8217's formula for your slave. Neither effects the intended legal change.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Le\u8217'Atzmaych Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then raises an ambiguous case in the context of {\i gerushin}: if a husband says to his wife {\i harei at le-atzmekh} (\u8220"you are unto yourself\u8221"), what does this accomplish? Is he divorcing her \u8212- meaning \u8220"you are now your own person, detached from me\u8221" \u8212- or is he merely relinquishing his rights to {\i ma\u8217'aseh yadeha}, the fruits of her labor, while remaining married? The latter would be a permissible arrangement, under which he agrees not to support her in exchange for not claiming her earnings.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Ravina raises a proof from a {\i beraita} before Rav Ashi: {\i gufo shel get shichrur} \u8212- the body of a {\i get shichrur} for a slave includes the formulation {\i harei ata ben chorin harei ata le-atzmecha} \u8212- \u8220"you are free, you are unto yourself.\u8221" From here, a {\i kal va-chomer} is constructed: with a {\i eved Kena\u8217'ani}, whom the master owns bodily ({\i kani lei le-gufeih}), when he says {\i harei ata le-atzmecha}, this clearly means complete freedom \u8212- not merely that the slave can keep his own earnings. How much more so when one says this to a wife, over whose body he has no ownership \u8212- it must certainly mean complete release. Therefore, {\i harei at le-atzmekh} said to a wife constitutes a valid {\i get}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Slave Manumission\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ein Li Esek Bakh\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} continues with a question about freeing a slave: if a master says to his slave {\i ein li esek bakh} \u8212- \u8220"I have nothing more to do with you\u8221" \u8212- does this constitute manumission? The question is whether the phrase means he is entirely releasing the slave (full {\i shichrur}, which changes the slave\u8217's status entirely, making him, in effect, a Jew in the full sense) or whether it merely means he is relinquishing control over the slave\u8217's labor while the slave remains legally enslaved. For an {\i eved Kena\u8217'ani}, the distinction is not merely practical but one of fundamental personal status.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ono Document\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Nachman \u8212- and some report it as Rav Chanin from Chuza \u8212- raises this before Rav Ashi, citing a proof. The {\i mishnah} records that if a master sells his slave to a non-Jew, the slave goes free, but he still requires a {\i get shichrur} from his original master. Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel qualifies: this applies only if the master did not write an {\i ono} at the time of sale. But if he wrote an {\i ono}, that document itself constitutes the {\i get shichrur}, and no separate document is needed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Sheshet explains: what is an {\i ono}? It is a document in which the master writes {\i kshe-tivrach mimeni, ein li esek bakh} \u8212- \u8220"when you flee from me, I have nothing to do with you.\u8221" Since such a document is recognized as a {\i shtar shichrur} \u8212- proof that the slave goes free upon flight \u8212- it follows that the phrase {\i ein li esek bakh} does constitute full release. The question is thereby resolved.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Kiddushin with Loan\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Loan Disqualified\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Returning to {\i kiddushin}, Abaye presents a ruling that opens a substantial discussion: {\i ha-mekadesh be-malveh einah mekudeshet} \u8212- if a man attempts to be {\i mekadesh} a woman using a pre-existing loan (money he has already lent her), the {\i kiddushin} is not valid. The operative principle, derived from the model of {\i Sedei Ephron} \u8212- where Avraham gave Ephron money at the moment of the transaction \u8212- is that {\i kiddushin} must be effected by giving something at the time of the act. A loan is different: {\i hotza\u8217'ah nitnah, kevar hein shela} \u8212- once a loan is disbursed, the money is already hers. He no longer controls it; she merely owes him a debt. He cannot use money that is no longer his to effect {\i kiddushin}, for he is not giving her anything new. As Rashi explains the case: {\i de-amar hitkadshi li be-malveh she-yesh li be-yadeich} \u8212- he says \u8220"be betrothed to me through the loan I have in your hands.\u8221" Since the money is already legally hers, he is not transferring anything, and the {\i kiddushin} fails.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Extending Loan Term\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 However, Abaye continues: {\i be-hana\u8217'at malveh mekudeshet} \u8212- if he is {\i mekadesh} her with the {\i hana\u8217'ah} (benefit) derived from extending the loan term, the {\i kiddushin} is valid. The {\i gemara} clarifies what {\i hana\u8217'at malveh} means. It is not {\i arba be-chamesh} \u8212- loaning four to be repaid as five \u8212- because that would simply be the loan itself (and would constitute actual {\i ribit}). Rather, {\i lo tzricha de-arvach lah zimna} \u8212- it means he extended the repayment deadline. She was obligated to pay him back by a certain date; he grants her additional time. The {\i hana\u8217'ah} she derives from this extension \u8212- she would theoretically have been willing to pay someone a {\i perutah} to intercede and secure that extension \u8212- constitutes the monetary benefit with which he is {\i mekadesh} her. Rashi articulates this explicitly: {\i amar lah hitkadshi li be-hana\u8217'ah zu she-at hayit notenet perutah le-adam she-yefayeseni alayich} \u8212- the extension is worth a {\i perutah} to her, and with that value he betroths her.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ribit Problem\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 While this {\i kiddushin} is valid, Abaye cautions: {\i aval asur la\u8217'asot kein mipnei ha\u8217'aramat ribit} \u8212- one may not do this, because it has the appearance of {\i ribit}. The technical distinction is that it is not {\i ribit gamur} \u8212- he is not receiving money from her; he receives a wife. He takes nothing directly from the loan transaction. Nevertheless, he derives benefit from the loan \u8212- the {\i hana\u8217'ah} of the extension is transformed into {\i kiddushin} \u8212- and this creates the appearance of a lender profiting from a loan, which the Torah prohibits. The principle is that a Jewish loan must be neutral: you lend a hundred and receive a hundred back. Here, the lender is getting something more \u8212- a wife \u8212- out of the loan relationship, even if it is not cash payment.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Rosh} agrees that {\i hana\u8217'at malveh} in the sense of extending the time constitutes real monetary value that can serve as the basis for {\i kiddushin}. And as noted, the extension need not be the exclusive path \u8212- forgiving the loan entirely also qualifies as {\i hana\u8217'at malveh} according to this view. The {\i Rashba} adds a further nuance: the effectiveness of the extension may depend on its timing. When the loan is {\i kol ha-omed ligbot ke-gavuy dami} \u8212- effectively ready for collection \u8212- then the creditor truly has the power to extend or demand, and the extension is a tangible new benefit. But if the loan is far from due, the extension is still too abstract to function as real {\i hana\u8217'ah}, and may not support {\i kiddushin}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Loan Forgiveness\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye then adds: {\i ve-kol sheken im machala kol ha-malveh} \u8212- certainly if he forgives the entire loan, this too functions as {\i hana\u8217'at malveh} and would constitute valid {\i kiddushin} (though the same {\i ribit} concern would apply). The reason is that {\i mechilah} creates a genuinely new benefit: where before she owed him a hundred dollars, now she owes nothing. That release of obligation \u8212- {\i hana\u8217'at mechilah} \u8212- is a fresh and real economic benefit conferred upon her at the moment of {\i kiddushin}, unlike the original loan disbursement, which is already spent. As Rashi frames it: {\i hashta hu de-kaya ve-lav afshata de-at malveh} \u8212- it is something that exists now, not merely the residue of the original loan.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Ran}, however, disputes whether forgiving the loan truly works. His position is that {\i mechilah} of the loan is not a genuinely {\i new} benefit \u8212- it is simply the erasure of a prior debt. The woman\u8217's mind is on the hundred dollars that was given, not on some additional, abstract gain. To be valid, the {\i kiddushin} must be effected with something above and beyond the original loan, something new introduced at the moment of betrothal. On this view, only the {\i hana\u8217'ah} of the extended repayment term \u8212- something genuinely incremental and new \u8212- would work, while outright forgiveness of the principal would not. This represents a substantive disagreement between Rashi and the {\i Ran} about the nature of what constitutes a valid new benefit in this context.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Conditional Gifts\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Conditional in Other Cas\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava raises the case of {\i matanah al menat lehachzir} \u8212- a conditional gift given on the understanding that it will be returned. The paradigmatic instance is the {\i arba minim} on {\i Sukkot}: if one person says to another {\i heilach etrog zeh al menat she-tachzirehu li} \u8212- here is my {\i etrog}, but give it back \u8212- and the recipient takes it and uses it and then returns it, he has fulfilled his obligation. Rava himself ruled this way: {\i netalo ve-hachziro yatza}; if he did not return it, he did not fulfill the obligation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava then applies this principle across several domains. In a sale ({\i mecher}), if a buyer says \u8220"here is payment on condition you return it,\u8221" no acquisition takes place. For {\i kiddushin}, if he gives her money on condition she returns it, she is not {\i mekudeshet}. For {\i pidyon haben}, if he gives the {\i kohen} the five {\i sela\u8217'im} on condition they be returned, the son is not redeemed. But for {\i terumah}, if he gives {\i terumah} to the {\i kohen} on condition it be returned \u8212- {\i yatza yedei netinah} \u8212- the obligation to give {\i terumah} is fulfilled, though the practice is forbidden because it creates the appearance that the {\i kohen} has made a deal or purchased the {\i terumah}, compromising the dignity of the gift and smacking of {\i armah} \u8212- evasion of the spirit of the law.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Why It Fails for Kiddush\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} finds this account internally inconsistent. Rava himself holds {\i matanah al menat lehachzir shemah matanah} \u8212- a conditional gift counts as a gift. If so, all these cases should work. And if it does not count as a gift, none of them should \u8212- including {\i terumah}, which the {\i beraita} says {\i does} work. The formulation in which some cases work and others do not seems untenable.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Girsa Dispute\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Ashi resolves this by correcting the {\i girsa} of the {\i beraita}. The proper reading is not that {\i kiddushin}, {\i mecher}, and {\i pidyon haben} all fail while {\i terumah} alone works. Rather: {\i be-chulehu kani} \u8212- all of them work \u8212- {\i levar me-ishah} \u8212- except for the woman. {\i Mecher} works, {\i pidyon haben} works, {\i terumah} works (though one should not do it in practice for the reasons of {\i armah}), but {\i kiddushin} does not work. Why does {\i kiddushin} alone fail?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i She-ein ishah nikneit be-chalippin} \u8212- a woman cannot be betrothed through the mechanism of {\i chalippin}. The {\i kicha} of {\i kiddushin} was derived from the model of {\i Sedei Ephron}, in which Avraham gave Ephron a definitive, unconditional transfer of money. {\i Chalippin} \u8212- the mode of acquisition by exchange or barter, typified by the {\i sudar} (handkerchief) \u8212- operates on a different and incompatible logic. The {\i sudar} in a {\i kinyan chalippin} is taken and immediately returned; the exchange is instantaneous and the physical object passes back at once, much as the {\i etrog} in the {\i matanah al menat lehachzir} case is returned. Saying to a woman \u8220"here is money, give it back\u8221" is structurally identical to {\i chalippin}, and {\i ein ishah nikneit be-chalippin}. Avraham\u8217's gift to Ephron was a clean, permanent, one-directional transfer, and {\i kiddushin} must replicate that model.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Huna bar Rav Nechemia of Rav Ashi confirms: this was indeed the tradition he had in the name of Rava \u8212- the corrected {\i girsa} is the right one. All the cases except {\i kiddushin} work; only {\i kiddushin} fails due to the inherent incompatibility with {\i chalippin}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Kiddushin 7\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rava\u8217's Three Cases\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Problem Setup\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The sugya opens at the very bottom of daf 6b, introducing three remarkable cases articulated by Rava involving unusual mechanisms for effecting {\i kiddushei kesef}. In a standard case of {\i kiddushin}, the man gives something of value to the woman \u8212- that object or sum of money constitutes the {\i kesef kiddushin} that effects the formal transactional change of status we call {\i kiddushin}. It is not a purchase in the simple sense, but there is nonetheless a formalized process that introduces both the prohibitive dimensions of marriage and its attendant obligations. The {\i sugya} here is concerned specifically with {\i kiddushei kesef} \u8212- and the cases Rava presents are, to put it plainly, strange.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 To follow the cases clearly, three parties must be kept in mind: the {\i isha} (the woman), the {\i ba\u8217'al} (the prospective husband), and {\i Ploni} (a third party).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Din Arev\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The first case: {\i ten maneh l\u8217'Ploni ve-ekadesh lach, mekudeshet mi-din arev}. The man says to the woman \u8212- or, grammatically more precisely, she says to the man \u8212- \u8220"Give a {\i maneh} to Ploni, and I will be betrothed to you.\u8221" The direction of the money is striking: it flows from the husband to a third party. The woman receives nothing; the husband does not give anything to his prospective wife. Yet Rava rules she is {\i mekudeshet}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara explains via the analogy of an {\i arev} \u8212- a guarantor or co-signer on a loan. When a borrower seeks funds and the lender requires added security, the {\i arev} steps in to obligate himself to repay in the event of default, despite receiving no benefit whatsoever from the transaction. The Gemara applies this logic: {\i arev, af al gav d\u8217'lo mati hana\u8217'ah l\u8217'yadeih, kamshabd nafsheih} \u8212- the guarantor, even though no benefit reaches him, nonetheless obligates himself. So too the woman: even though no money reaches her, she obligates herself and effects the transfer of her person into the marital relationship.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Arev Analysis\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 There is a major {\i machloket rishonim} about precisely how this case works, and indeed the underlying question touches on the most fundamental issues of what {\i kiddushei kesef} requires.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rashi appears to take the position that each of these archetypical transaction-types \u8212- {\i arev}, {\i eved Kena\u8217'ani}, and the combined case \u8212- functions as a recognized transactional form, and because it works in those contexts, it can work for {\i kiddushin} as well. The later {\i rishonim} find this difficult.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Rosh takes the view that what makes this good {\i kiddushei kesef} is simply the fact that money was transferred \u8212- regardless of direction or party. A formal act of monetary transfer occurred, and that transfer is what {\i kiddushin} attaches to. On this reading, {\i kiddushin} is not about the man purchasing the woman, but about a formal transactional event that gives legal content to the marital intention.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Rambam\u8217's approach, which is perhaps the most conceptually coherent, is that the woman receives a psychological {\i hana\u8217'ah} \u8212- a benefit. Her agreement to do what the husband requests in a financial matter, or more precisely, the benefit she derives from the fact that he acted on her financial direction, constitutes {\i hana\u8217'ah} worth at least a {\i perutah}. This principle \u8212- that intangible psychological value can constitute {\i kesef kiddushin} \u8212- recurs later in the {\i masechta} in cases such as \u8220"do a dance for me and you will be {\i mekudeshet}.\u8221" The difficulty with the Rambam\u8217's approach, as noted, is that none of this is articulated in the Gemara\u8217's formulation of the case itself.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rashi and several other {\i rishonim} respond that for this mechanism to work, it must be articulated explicitly \u8212- otherwise, the woman handing money to a stranger is simply disconnected from {\i kiddushin} entirely. There is no inherent link unless something is stated to make the connection.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Ritva stakes out a middle position: there is a monetary dimension in the sense that money was transferred, but some part of the mechanism is still anchored in the psychological value as well. The cases are genuinely difficult, and that difficulty is precisely the point \u8212- these are strange cases that push at the edges of what {\i kiddushei kesef} can mean.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One further clarification that emerges from discussion: in the first case, who actually gives the money? The text is ambiguous, and whether it is her money or his money being given to Ploni is itself part of the {\i machloket rishonim}. The textual formulation may be read either way. What is clear is that the woman is the one making the request \u8212- she says, in effect, give money to Ploni and I will be your wife \u8212- and the theoretical underpinning is the {\i arev} principle: a person can obligate themselves in a transaction from which they derive no direct benefit.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Din Eved Kena\u8217'ani\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The second case: {\i heilach maneh vehitkadshi l\u8217'Ploni, mekudeshet mi-din eved Kena\u8217'ani}. Here the directions are reversed. A third party gives the woman money, and the man \u8212- the {\i Ploni} here \u8212- marries her as a result, even though he has not spent a single coin. The Gemara grounds this in the law of the Canaanite slave.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 When a slave seeks to purchase his own freedom, the {\i halacha} presents a structural problem: everything a slave owns belongs in principle to his master. Any money the slave accumulates is already the master\u8217's property. Therefore, the slave cannot technically purchase his own freedom using money in his possession. Instead, a third party must provide the purchase price, paying the master directly. Yet the transaction is the slave\u8217's \u8212- it is his freedom that is acquired, his status that changes, and the conceptual framework remains one of him acquiring himself.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Eved Kena\u8217'ani yochiach}: the slave proves that a person can effect a change in his own status through a transaction in which he himself loses nothing \u8212- because someone else bears the financial cost. Applied to {\i kiddushin}: the husband does not lose money, someone else provides the funds to the woman, and yet the marriage is effected. It is a status change, not a purchase, and the status change attaches to the husband even without any financial outlay on his part.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Din Shneihem\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The third case combines both problems simultaneously: {\i ten maneh l\u8217'Ploni vehitkadshi ani lo, mekudeshet mi-din sheneihem}. The woman tells the husband, \u8220"Give money to Ploni, and I will be married to him.\u8221" The husband gives money to a third party, and the woman becomes betrothed to that third party. Neither problem in isolation is sufficient to invalidate \u8212- the woman gets no money, and the prospective husband Ploni loses nothing \u8212- and Rava therefore invokes both principles together to validate the transaction.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara walks through the circular logic explicitly: {\i arev} addresses the woman\u8217's situation (she obligates herself despite receiving no {\i hana\u8217'ah}); {\i eved Kena\u8217'ani} addresses Ploni\u8217's situation (he acquires a wife without losing money). Each principle on its own would face the objection that the other half doesn\u8217't match \u8212- but together, the two principles cover all the anomalies of the transaction and the {\i kiddushin} is valid.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Fourth Case & Agav\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Woman Gives to Husband\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava then poses a question \u8212- {\i ba\u8217'ei Rava} \u8212- which is perhaps the most striking of all: {\i heilach maneh ve-ekadesh ani lach, mahu?} What is the rule when the woman gives money directly to the man and says, \u8220"I will be betrothed to you\u8221"? This is the case that Abaye had in mind when questioning the formulation of {\i hikadesh otach} \u8212- the language of marriage being initiated by the woman rather than the man. The Gemara takes it up as a genuine question rather than disposing of it categorically.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Mar Zutra, citing Rav Pappa, rules: {\i mekudeshet} \u8212- the marriage is valid.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Agav Problem\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Ashi immediately objects. If the woman\u8217's giving of money to the man can effect {\i kiddushin}, that implies the woman is, conceptually, transferring herself to the man {\i agav} the money \u8212- as an incidental acquisition bundled with the primary transfer. But the {\i mishnah} teaches the rule of {\i kinyan agav} as follows: movable property ({\i nechasim she-ein lahem achrayut}) can be acquired along with real estate ({\i nechasim she-yesh lahem achrayut}), not the reverse. Land is the primary vehicle; chattels ride along for free. Here, money is movable property \u8212- and the woman would be the {\i karka}, real estate \u8212- so the transfer is going in the wrong direction. It would be as if one were purchasing a flock of sheep and claiming the field comes along with them, which is precisely the inverse of how {\i kinyan agav} works.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Adam Chashuv\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Mar Zutra deflects the objection entirely: {\i mi savart de-amari agav? hacha be-Adam Chashuv askinan} \u8212- who said I am invoking {\i kinyan agav} at all? We are speaking of a case involving an {\i adam chashuv}, a person of considerable social standing and reputation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rashi explains: an {\i adam chashuv} is someone who, on principle, does not accept gifts, based on the {\i pasuk} in Mishlei, {\i soneh matanot yichyeh} \u8212- the one who hates gifts will live. Refusing gifts is treated by {\i Chazal} as a mark of honor and is regularly invoked as a normative standard for distinguished individuals. If such a person accepts a gift, the psychological benefit to the giver is measurable and real: {\i de-be-hahi hana\u8217'ah de-ka mekaibel hana\u8217'ah minah, gamra u-maknya lei nafshah} \u8212- from the benefit she receives from the fact that this {\i adam chashuv}, who does not ordinarily accept gifts, has accepted hers, she resolves to betroth herself to him completely.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The analogy is apt: if one were to pay for dinner in the company of a distinguished personage who normally declines others\u8217' hospitality, the very fact of that acceptance confers a palpable benefit on the one who treated. That benefit is worth at least a {\i perutah}, and it is this psychological {\i hana\u8217'ah} \u8212- not any principle of {\i kinyan agav} \u8212- that provides the mechanism for the {\i kiddushin}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This parallels the Rambam\u8217's reading of the first case, where the woman\u8217's benefit from being heeded in a financial matter constitutes the {\i hana\u8217'ah} that makes the {\i kiddushei kesef} functional. The difference is that in the {\i adam chashuv} case, the Gemara makes this explicit \u8212- unlike the first case, where the Rambam reads it in by inference.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 In practice, the {\i adam chashuv} principle is extremely narrow. It applies only where the recipient is genuinely known to refuse gifts, and where the psychological benefit to the giver is therefore clear and recognized. The difficulty of assessing such benefit in any given case is considerable, which is part of why the reverse ring ceremony, while theoretically available in this framework, is not practically employed.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Application to Mamona\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kiddushin vs Mamona\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 After teaching the three cases of {\i arev}, {\i eved Kena\u8217'ani}, and {\i din sheneihem}, Rava adds: {\i ve-chen l\u8217'inyan mamona} \u8212- and the same principles apply in monetary transactions. That is, if one wishes to use any of these unusual mechanisms to effect a {\i kinyan kesef} in a standard financial transaction \u8212- purchasing a field, for example, which requires only a {\i perutah} of {\i kesef} \u8212- those mechanisms work there too.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might wonder why this needs to be stated. The Gemara explains: {\i tzricha}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mamona Same Rules\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Had Rava taught only the {\i kiddushin} cases, one might have assumed the permissibility was limited to {\i kiddushin} because the threshold for {\i kesef kiddushin} is extremely low \u8212- a single {\i perutah} \u8212- and moreover because of the well-known presumption of Reish Lakish: {\i tav le-meitav tadu mi-le-meitav armelu} \u8212- it is better to dwell in partnership than to dwell alone, meaning women generally prefer a state of marriage. Given that preference, one might assume the {\i kiddushin} threshold is especially easy to meet on account of the woman\u8217's own inclination. Hence we might have thought these mechanisms work only for {\i kiddushin} and not for ordinary monetary transactions. Rava therefore teaches: no, they work for monetary transactions as well.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 No Mechila in Kiddushin\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Conversely, had Rava taught only the monetary cases, one might have assumed the permissibility there flows from the fact that in monetary contexts, there is room for {\i mechila} \u8212- waiver. A creditor can simply forgive a debt: {\i arba me\u8217'ot shekel kesef beini u-veinecha mah hi} \u8212- four hundred shekels of silver between you and me, consider it a gift. In financial matters, if the parties are satisfied, the formal adequacy of the payment mechanism matters less.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But {\i kiddushin} has no room for {\i mechila}. The formal transactional event is constitutive of the {\i kiddushin} itself; without it, there is no marriage regardless of mutual consent. A man and a woman who simply agree to be married, without any {\i kinyan}, are not {\i mekudeshet}. Therefore, one might have thought these unusual mechanisms \u8212- good enough for {\i mamona} where {\i mechila} smooths over gaps \u8212- would not work for the more demanding formal requirements of {\i kiddushin}. Rava therefore teaches both: the mechanisms are valid for both {\i kiddushin} and {\i mamona}, and each teaching is necessary because neither can be derived from the other.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Half-Marriage Cases\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Half Marriage\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava then turns to a different class of problems: {\i hitkadshi li l\u8217'chetzi} \u8212- \u8220"be betrothed to half of me.\u8221" Can a man offer to marry a woman only partially? Rava rules: {\i mekudeshet} \u8212- she is betrothed. The marriage is valid.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Why Half Fails\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye challenges: if it is valid for a man to offer half of himself in marriage, why should the inverse case \u8212- {\i chetziyech mekudeshet li}, \u8220"half of you be betrothed to me\u8221" \u8212- be invalid? The verse says {\i ki yikach ish isha}, implying a whole woman must be taken. But it also says {\i ish} \u8212- implying, perhaps, a whole man must be the one taking. The same logic that invalidates half-{\i kiddushin} of the woman should apply to the man.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava responds: do not invoke {\i pesukim} at me; I am speaking of {\i svarot} \u8212- substantive logical reasoning. {\i Ititah le-vei terei lo chazya} \u8212- a woman cannot be married to two men simultaneously. Therefore, when a man says \u8220"half of you be betrothed to me,\u8221" the implicit consequence is that the other half remains available for a second man to marry \u8212- which is forbidden. The statement is thus structurally self-defeating: it presupposes an arrangement that cannot legally exist.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But a man in a marriage? {\i Gavra mi lo chazi le-vei terei?} \u8212- can a man not be married to two women? In the period of the Gemara, polygamy, while not normative, was technically permitted. When a man says \u8220"half of me be betrothed to you,\u8221" what he is expressing \u8212- using unconventional language \u8212- is that he reserves the right within this marriage to take a second wife. That is halachically permissible, and therefore the statement does not self-destruct in the same way. The {\i kiddushin} is valid.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is worth pausing to note the interesting implication: the man here is entering marriage on the explicit understanding that he may take a second wife. One might consider the extent to which, in entering a polygamous arrangement, a man must stipulate this at the outset or whether it is a reserved right independent of the {\i kiddushin} formulation. The Gemara does not pursue that question, but the structure of the case raises it naturally.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hekdesh Analogy\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Mar Zutra bar Rav Mari then poses a challenge to Ravina: even if we accept that {\i chetziyech mekudeshet li} fails, why should the {\i kiddushin} not spread through her entire person? This is an importation from the laws of {\i hekdesh}: if a person consecrates part of an animal for the altar, the sanctity can spread ({\i lifshot}) through the entire animal. Does the {\i kiddushin} not spread similarly?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara invokes a specific teaching on the matter: {\i ha-omer regla shel zo olah, tehei kulah olah} \u8212- if one says \u8220"the leg of this animal is an {\i olah},\u8221" the entire animal becomes an {\i olah}. Even according to the opinion in Masechet Temurah that such spreading does not always work, that restrictive view applies only where one consecrated a part from which the animal could survive \u8212- a leg, for instance. An animal can live without a leg. But if one consecrates something that the animal\u8217's life depends on \u8212- its heart, its brain \u8212- then the entire life of the animal is encompassed, and the {\i hekdesh} spreads throughout. By analogy, if one says \u8220"half of you\u8221" in a way that divides the woman down the middle \u8212- a division incompatible with survival \u8212- should not the {\i kiddushin} spread through all of her?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Objects vs Persons\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara\u8217's answer marks a fundamental distinction: {\i mi dami? hasam behemah, hacha da\u8217'at acheret} \u8212- can these cases be compared? There, it is an animal; here, there is {\i da\u8217'at acheret} \u8212- another consciousness, another will.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 When an animal is consecrated for the altar, it is designated \u8212- it does not consent. An animal has no {\i da\u8217'at} to resist or limit the effect of {\i hekdesh}. The sanctity spreads because the animal is a passive object of the transaction.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Marriage is different in the most fundamental way: the woman\u8217's {\i da\u8217'at} is constitutive of the {\i kiddushin}. We have seen already in the {\i masechta} that at the moment of {\i kiddushin}, the husband articulates a verbal declaration of intent, and the woman\u8217's acceptance is her consent to that specific declaration. If the man says {\i chetziyech mekudeshet li} \u8212- \u8220"half of you be betrothed to me\u8221" \u8212- her acceptance is an acceptance of precisely that formulation. She may be agreeing only to what was said; her {\i da\u8217'at} does not expand beyond the terms she was offered. Unlike an animal, which has no consciousness to resist or delimit the spread of holiness, the woman\u8217's active {\i da\u8217'at} means the {\i kiddushin} takes effect only to the extent she has consented \u8212- and \u8220"half of you\u8221" is what she agreed to.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The emphasis here on the woman\u8217's {\i da\u8217'at} as a limiting and constitutive factor in {\i kiddushin} is striking. Even in a case where she seems broadly willing, her agreement is agreement to a particular articulation, and {\i kiddushin} cannot spread beyond what that articulation encompasses.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Before leaving this section, one important dispute between Rashi and Tosafot should be noted. The {\i hava amina} earlier \u8212- that the woman giving money to the man might operate via {\i kinyan agav} \u8212- raised the question of whether a human being can be analogized to land for transactional purposes. Rashi holds that human beings are in a certain general sense comparable to {\i karka} in transactional contexts. Tosafot strongly disagrees: the comparison to real estate applies to Canaanite slaves, who are explicitly equated to land by a {\i pasuk}, but does not extend to free persons. The {\i Shach} in Shulchan Aruch marshals extensive proofs against Tosafot\u8217's position \u8212- as is characteristic of his method when he is firmly convinced of one view. The underlying question \u8212- whether the {\i hekesh} between slaves and land extends conceptually to free persons \u8212- is a significant one and worth careful consideration.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then draws additional lessons from a case involving an animal owned by two partners, one of whom consecrates his half, then purchases the other half and consecrates that as well. Such an animal is {\i kadosh} but cannot be brought as a sacrifice \u8212- {\i eina kreiva} \u8212- because it is, in conceptual terms, two half-animals joined together rather than one whole animal. Yet it has sufficient {\i kedushah} to effect {\i temurah}, the exchange of sanctity to another animal \u8212- which requires {\i kdushat haguf}. And the {\i temurah} itself is likewise {\i eina kreiva}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 From this case the Gemara derives three lessons \u8212- {\i shma mina tlat}, as is the Talmudic convention: that living animals can be subjected to {\i dichui} (disqualification that persists even after the disqualifying condition resolves); that {\i dichui me-ikara havei dichui} \u8212- even initial-state disqualification counts; and that {\i yesh dichui be-damim} \u8212- {\i dichui} applies even in cases of {\i kdushat damim}, value-based sanctification, not only {\i kdushat haguf}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Teiku Cases\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Half-Perutah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava then poses a series of progressively more strained cases, all left as {\i teiku} \u8212- unresolved. The general principle is that most {\i rishonim}, including the Rambam, rule {\i lechumra} in cases of {\i teiku} in {\i kiddushin}, though of course the precise application depends on how a given Gemara is read.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The first case: {\i chetzyeich be-chatzi perutah ve-chetzyeich be-chatzi perutah} \u8212- \u8220"half of you with half a {\i perutah} and half of you with another half a {\i perutah}.\u8221" Can two statements of {\i chatzi pruta} combine to constitute the minimum value? {\i Keivan de-amar lah chatzi perutah, chatzi paska} \u8212- by saying \u8220"half a {\i perutah},\u8221" he has articulated an incomplete transaction, and the two halves may simply be two separate invalid acts. Or perhaps he is {\i moneh ve-holeich} \u8212- building up his count incrementally, in which case the transaction adds up. {\i Teiku}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If one admits that {\i moneh ve-holeich} applies there, a more acute case follows: {\i chetzyeich be-pruta ve-chetzyeich be-pruta} \u8212- \u8220"half of you with one {\i perutah} and the other half with another {\i perutah}.\u8221" Now each half is stated with a full {\i perutah} \u8212- a significant and discrete amount. Does stating a full {\i perutah} indicate a self-contained transaction, thus splitting the two statements into two independently invalid acts? Or does the same {\i moneh ve-holeich} principle apply \u8212- he is simply counting out the {\i kiddushin} in pieces? {\i Teiku}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 And if that case admits of combination: {\i chetzyeich be-pruta hayom ve-chetzyeich be-pruta le-machar} \u8212- \u8220"half of you today with a {\i perutah} and half tomorrow with a {\i perutah}.\u8221" At this point the absurdity is evident; it seems nearly certain that this cannot work. {\i Keivan de-amar lah le-machar, paska} \u8212- the word \u8220"tomorrow\u8221" already severs the transaction entirely. Or perhaps, the Gemara entertains, what he is expressing is that the {\i kiddushin} begins now but is only completed tomorrow \u8212- conceptually drawing out what should be an instantaneous transactional moment across two days. It is worth noting that the time-scale here is days rather than minutes and hours, which themselves are known concepts \u8212- the coarse granularity is itself interesting to observe. {\i Teiku}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Finally: {\i shenei chetzyeich be-pruta} \u8212- \u8220"your two halves be betrothed to me with this one {\i perutah}.\u8221" This is a single statement, made at one moment, referring to the complete person by saying \u8220"both halves.\u8221" Here the Gemara asks whether this single unified statement can save the transaction \u8212- {\i hacha vadai be-chad zimna ka\u8217'amar lah}, he said it at one time. Or perhaps there is a more fundamental principle: {\i ein ishah mitkadeshet le-chatzayin klal} \u8212- a woman simply cannot be married by halves in any form. Framing the {\i kiddushin} in terms of halves, even adding up to a whole in the same breath, reflects a flawed conception of what marriage is about, or signals to the woman an offer she is not able to meaningfully accept. {\i Teiku}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two-to-Two Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Pappa then raises a separate case: {\i shenei benotayich li-shenei banai be-perutah} \u8212- a father, acting as agent for his two sons (having been properly appointed as {\i shaliach}, since a father cannot unilaterally marry off a son without the son\u8217's authorization), approaches another father and says: \u8220"Your two daughters be betrothed to my two sons with a single {\i perutah}.\u8221" Does the single {\i perutah} suffice for both marriages?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The question is whether we follow the people actually conducting the transaction \u8212- the two fathers \u8212- making it in some sense a single unified exchange, with the money satisfying the minimum requirement. Or do we follow the men and women who are themselves being married, meaning each pair needs a separate {\i perutah}, and one {\i perutah} divided between two marriages is insufficient? {\i Teiku}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Daughter & Cow\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Ashi\u8217's case pushes further into the complexity of combining different categories of transaction: {\i bittacha u-faratcha be-perutah} \u8212- \u8220"your daughter and your cow with a single {\i perutah}.\u8221" Are these being split into half a {\i perutah} each, making the {\i kiddushin} invalid for lack of minimum value? Or are they fundamentally different transactions \u8212- the {\i kiddushin} effected by a full {\i perutah} and the cow acquired by {\i meshicha} (the appropriate {\i kinyan} for movable property), the two simply uttered together but legally discrete? {\i Teiku}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Ashi then poses the closest variant: {\i bittacha be-karkacha be-perutah} \u8212- \u8220"your daughter and your field with a single {\i perutah}.\u8221" Human beings and land are, in a certain conceptual sense, closer to each other in the transactional framework than a woman and a cow are. The {\i hava amina} that free persons might be analogized to real estate (whatever one concludes about the Rashi-Tosafot debate) gives this case a special texture. Is the {\i perutah} split half and half, or does the full {\i perutah} go to the {\i kiddushin} while the field is acquired by {\i chazakah}? {\i Teiku}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Silk & Valuation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Silk Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The final section of the {\i daf} is introduced by a real case: {\i hahu gavra de-akadish be-shirai} \u8212- a man who attempted to effect {\i kiddushin} using a piece of silk. Silk in this period was extraordinarily valuable \u8212- the product of silkworms from China, reaching the Near East only through long and costly trade routes. The historical note is apt: Rava and Rav Yosef are late-fourth-generation Amoraim, and silk was making its appearance in the markets of Babylonia precisely in this period, making it exactly the kind of novel, expensive, and imprecisely-valued commodity whose halachic implications needed to be worked out in real time.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Raba vs Rav Yosef\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Raba says: {\i lo tzricha shuma} \u8212- no assessment of value is required. We know silk is certainly worth more than a {\i perutah}; that is sufficient for {\i kiddushei kesef}, and the woman\u8217's consent stands on that basis.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Yosef says: {\i tzricha shuma} \u8212- precise assessment is required. The woman is consenting to the marriage conditional on the object being worth what she believes it is worth. If she has no way of independently verifying the value, her {\i da\u8217'at} \u8212- her real consent \u8212- is not fully secured.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This dispute has direct practical ramifications and underlies the requirement, well-established in later {\i posekim}, to use plain metal rings of determinable value for {\i kiddushin} \u8212- avoiding gemstones or elaborately worked pieces whose market value fluctuates widely and which a non-expert cannot independently evaluate.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Consensus\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara clarifies that there are two cases in which everyone agrees. First: if the man explicitly says {\i be-kulo} \u8212- \u8220"be married to me for whatever minimal value exists in this silk,\u8221" indicating that only a single {\i perutah}\u8217's worth is being designated as the {\i kesef kiddushin}. Since it is beyond question that silk is worth at least a {\i perutah}, everyone agrees {\i lo tzricha shuma} \u8212- no assessment is needed. Second: if the man explicitly stated the silk was worth 50 and it was actually not worth 50 \u8212- {\i ha lo shavu} \u8212- everyone agrees that the {\i kiddushin} is invalid. She was misled about the value, her consent was based on a false representation, and the marriage does not stand.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Core Dispute\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The actual {\i machloket} between Raba and Rav Yosef concerns a case where the man said the silk was worth 50 and it actually was worth 50 \u8212- {\i d\u8217'amar chamishin ve-shavu chamishin} \u8212- but the woman did not know this independently and had no way to verify it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Raba rules: {\i lo tzricha shuma} \u8212- no assessment is required. He said it was worth 50, it was worth 50; the statement was accurate, she agreed to it, the marriage is valid.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Yosef rules: {\i tzricha shuma} \u8212- assessment is required. {\i Keivan de-ittata lo bekia\u8217'ah be-shuma, lo samcha da\u8217'atah} \u8212- since a woman would not normally know how to assess the value of silk, her mind is not settled. She did not really consent in the full sense because she had no way to evaluate the claim being made. Even if it turned out to be true, the {\i da\u8217'at} necessary for {\i kiddushin} was lacking at the moment of the transaction.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Alternative View\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 An alternative reading of the {\i sugya} \u8212- introduced as {\i ika de-amrei} \u8212- extends the dispute further. On this view, Raba and Rav Yosef disagree even in the case of {\i be-kulo}, where the man designated only a minimal {\i perutah}\u8217's worth. Rav Yosef holds: {\i shaveh perutah harei hu ke-kesef} \u8212- an object worth a {\i perutah} is treated as money for purposes of {\i kiddushei kesef}, and therefore it must conform to the qualities of money.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shaveh Kesef Rules\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What is the essential quality of money? {\i Mah kesef de-kayatz} \u8212- money is precisely and fixedly valued. A coin has a determinate worth; there is no ambiguity about how much a {\i perutah} is worth. Rav Yosef therefore holds: {\i af shaveh kesef nami de-kayatz} \u8212- when {\i shaveh kesef} (property used in place of money) is employed for {\i kiddushin}, it too must be precisely measurable in value. Silk, whose price varies and which requires expert evaluation, does not meet that standard unless it is formally assessed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The source for this principle is a {\i baraita}: {\i mi-kesef miknato} \u8212- in the context of the {\i eved Ivri}, the Hebrew bondsman who wishes to buy out the remaining years of his indentured servitude, the {\i pasuk} establishes that the redemption must be {\i be-kesef} \u8212- with money. The {\i baraita} derives that this excludes {\i tvu\u8217'ah} and {\i keilim} \u8212- produce and implements. Not because they cannot be used in transactions generally \u8212- the {\i pasuk} continues, {\i yashiv et geulato}, which the Gemara reads as extending {\i shaveh kesef ke-kesef}, equating property with money for transactional purposes \u8212- but because {\i tvu\u8217'ah} and {\i keilim}, even when they clearly exceed a {\i perutah} in value, lack the property of {\i kayitz}: their value is not fixed, not precisely defined, subject to fluctuation and market variation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The analogy to {\i kiddushin} is thus direct: just as the redemption of the {\i eved Ivri} requires {\i kesef de-kayatz} or {\i shaveh kesef de-kayatz} \u8212- either actual money or property of precisely determinable value \u8212- so too {\i kiddushei kesef} requires that what is given, whether money or {\i shaveh kesef}, have a known and fixed value at the moment of the transaction. Where value is indeterminate, the woman\u8217's {\i da\u8217'at} is unsettled, and the {\i kiddushin} cannot stand.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A final note of historical interest connects the laws of {\i kiddushin} to the approaching holiday: the Rama in {\i siman} 695 rules, strangely, that a man should not send {\i mishloach manot} to a widow, owing to concern about {\i safek kiddushin}. The {\i acharonim} are puzzled \u8212- for {\i kiddushin} to result, both the man\u8217's intent and the woman\u8217's consent are obviously required. Yet the Rama states the concern. Looking closely at this ruling in light of today\u8217's {\i sugya} \u8212- which has established just how expansive the mechanisms of {\i kiddushei kesef} can be, including psychological {\i hana\u8217'ah} and gifts accepted by {\i adam chashuv} \u8212- provides the proper context for understanding what halachic concern the Rama may have in mind.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Kiddushin 8\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rabba vs.\u160 ?Rav Yosef\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two Versions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} on this daf centers on a dispute between Rabba and Rav Yosef concerning a man who attempted {\i kiddushin} by giving a woman silk ({\i shiraim}), and whether the silk required a formal appraisal \u8212- {\i tzrichi shuma} \u8212- before the {\i kiddushin} could be valid. Rabba held that it did not; Rav Yosef held that it did. Crucially, the {\i gemara} presents two distinct versions of this debate, and they differ not only in outcome but in the underlying conceptual question each version raises.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 In the first version, the man claimed a specific value for the silk \u8212- say, it was worth fifty \u8212- and it turned out to be exactly that amount, but no expert had verified this before the exchange. Rabba held that since the silk was in fact worth what he said, she is married. Rav Yosef countered that silk is not something a layperson can readily assess, and therefore even if it happened to be worth what he claimed, the woman could not have been genuinely confident ({\i samcha da\u8217'atah}) of its value at the time of acceptance. That absence of confident reliance is a deficiency in the {\i da\u8217'as} required for {\i kiddushin} to take effect.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The second version is conceptually richer. Here, the man makes no value claim at all \u8212- he simply says he is marrying her with whatever the silk is worth, which is certainly at least a {\i perutah}, but no specific amount is asserted. One can readily understand why Rabba would not require an assessment in this case, since no representation about value was made. Yet Rav Yosef holds that even here, an assessment is required. Understanding why brings us to the heart of the debate.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Yosef on Kayatz\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Yosef\u8217's position rests on a fundamental principle about how {\i shaveh kessef} \u8212- things of monetary value \u8212- function in halachic transactions. The principle that {\i shaveh kessef harei hu k\u8217'kessef}, that items of value may serve as money, is well established and applies across many contexts. We know a woman may be betrothed with {\i kessef} either from the verse concerning the sale of a daughter as an {\i amah ivriyah}, or from the purchase of Ephron\u8217's field by Avraham, where the text specifies payment that functioned as currency.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But Rav Yosef insists that the equation of {\i shaveh kessef} with {\i kessef} is not a blanket substitution \u8212- it means {\i shaveh kessef} must {\i function like} money. And the defining feature of money is that it has a fixed, determined value. The principle he articulates at the opening of daf 8a is: {\i ma kessef d\u8217'kaitz, af shaveh kessef nami d\u8217'kaitz} \u8212- just as money has a fixed value, so too {\i shaveh kessef} must have a fixed value. Silk whose value has not been established \u8212- which might be worth anywhere from ten to a hundred \u8212- cannot function as money, because money by definition is determinate. Therefore, without prior assessment that fixes the value, the silk cannot serve as {\i kessef kiddushin}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Eved Ivri Source\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Yosef then offers a {\i talmudic} source for this position from a {\i braita} dealing with the redemption of a {\i eved ivri}. The verse states {\i mikessef miknaso} \u8212- that a Hebrew slave is purchased and redeemed by means of money \u8212- and the {\i braita} derives from this that redemption works only with {\i kessef} and not with {\i tevuah} (grain) or {\i keilim} (vessels).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then interrogates this {\i braita}: in what circumstances does it exclude grain and vessels? If we say they simply cannot effect the transaction at all, that seems to contradict the well-established principle that {\i shaveh kessef} functions as {\i kessef}, learned from the word {\i geulato} \u8212- \u8220"his redemption\u8221" \u8212- which is general and inclusive. If on the other hand we say they are excluded only when they lack the value of a {\i perutah}, then the exclusion has nothing to do with being grain or vessels specifically \u8212- the same would apply to money itself. So why mention {\i tevuah} and {\i keilim} at all?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} concludes: we must be dealing with grain and vessels that {\i do} have the value of a {\i perutah}, and yet they still fail \u8212- {\i kivan delo kaitz lehu} \u8212- because their values are indeterminate. This is Rav Yosef\u8217's proof: the {\i braita} is teaching that even objects of value fail when their value is not fixed. The principle of {\i kaitz} is therefore derived from the {\i eved ivri} context and applied to {\i kiddushin} as well.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabba\u8217's Response\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabba responds with a fundamentally different reading of the {\i braita}. In his view, the exclusion of {\i tevuah} and {\i keilim} has nothing to do with indeterminacy of value. Rather, the {\i braita} is teaching that the transaction must be structured {\i betorat kessef} \u8212- as an act of monetary payment \u8212- and not {\i betorat chaliphin}, not as an act of symbolic exchange. {\i Chaliphin}, in the relevant sense here, refers to the procedure of kinyan sudra, the handkerchief-type acquisition: one takes an object of significance \u8212- not for its monetary value but as a symbolic act of finalization \u8212- and that effects the transaction. The {\i braita}, says Rabba, is simply excluding framing the acquisition of an {\i eved ivri} as an act of {\i chaliphin} rather than as a monetary purchase.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} pushes back: this reading works for {\i keilim}, which are paradigmatically used for {\i chaliphin}, but what about {\i tevuah} \u8212- grain? Rav Nachman rules that food items ({\i peirot}) cannot function as {\i chaliphin} at all. So the {\i braita\u8217's} exclusion of {\i tevuah} cannot be read as excluding {\i chaliphin} performed with grain, since that isn\u8217't a recognized form of {\i chaliphin} in the first place.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Cornered, Rabba retreats to the position that indeed the case is where {\i tevuah} and {\i keilim} lack the value of a {\i perutah}. But then, as the {\i gemara} objects, why specify those categories? Rabba answers with a {\i lo mibaya} construction: it goes without saying that actual {\i kessef} less than a {\i perutah} does not effect a transaction. The {\i chiddush} of the {\i braita} is that even {\i tevuah} and {\i keilim} \u8212- which one might think could function differently, since their value is direct and experiential (one eats the grain, one uses the vessel) \u8212- even those fail when they fall below a {\i perutah} in monetary value. {\i Kamashma lan} that in the end, value must be monetary value, not merely functional value.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This illuminates the deeper structure of the disagreement. For Rav Yosef, {\i shaveh kessef} functions as {\i kessef} precisely because it is quasi-money \u8212- it stands in for money, and therefore it must exhibit money\u8217's defining characteristic of determinacy. For Rabba, {\i shaveh kessef} functions not as a substitute for money but as an object of value in its own right. The underlying logic of the transaction is that something of value changes hands, not that something money-like changes hands. That is why the requirement of {\i kaitz} \u8212- a fixed, determined value \u8212- does not apply.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Pidyon Ha-Ben\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Yosef then brings a second source from a {\i braita} concerning {\i pidyon ha-ben}. The {\i braita} states: if a man says to a {\i kohen}, \u8220"I am giving you this calf for my son\u8217's {\i pidyon},\u8221" or \u8220"this {\i tallis} for my son\u8217's {\i pidyon}\u8221" \u8212- without specifying a value \u8212- {\i lo amar klum}, it accomplishes nothing. But if he says, \u8220"I am giving you this calf valued at five {\i sela\u8217'im} for my son\u8217's {\i pidyon},\u8221" or \u8220"this {\i tallis} valued at five {\i sela\u8217'im},\u8221" then {\i bno paduy} \u8212- the son is redeemed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} asks: in what case are we dealing? If the object is actually not worth five {\i sela\u8217'im} in either scenario, then in the second case, merely saying the value does not make it so \u8212- {\i kol k\u8217'minei}, he has no power to magically assign worth beyond the item\u8217's true value. So it must be that in both cases the item actually is worth five {\i sela\u8217'im}. And yet when he fails to state a value, it does not work. {\i Ve-keivan de-lo kaitz} \u8212- since no fixed value was declared, the {\i pidyon} is invalid. This, says Rav Yosef, confirms the {\i kaitz} requirement.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabba responds with a different reading: the two cases differ not in what the item is actually worth, but in whether the {\i kohen} is willing to accept it at that valuation. {\i Lo le-olam de-lo shavi} \u8212- in fact, the item is not intrinsically worth five {\i sela\u8217'im}. {\i Ve-kheivan de-kibel lei kohen iluyei} \u8212- but the {\i kohen} was willing to evaluate it at five {\i sela\u8217'im} for himself. This is Rabba\u8217's {\i chiddush}: the question is not about standardized market value or official assessment. It is about whether this recipient \u8212- the {\i kohen} \u8212- subjectively values it at the required amount, and whether that subjective valuation is credible.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is illustrated by the case of Rav Kahana, who \u8212- at the time of performing a {\i pidyon ha-ben} \u8212- took a {\i sudra} (handkerchief) and said that to him, personally, it was worth five {\i sela\u8217'im}. Tosafot notes, interestingly, that Rav Kahana may not himself have been a {\i kohen} but was perhaps married to the daughter of a {\i kohen}, implying that a {\i kohen\u8217's} daughter may also receive the {\i pidyon} payment.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Mar bar Rav Ashi adds a critical qualification: this principle \u8212- that a person may declare a subjective valuation \u8212- applies only to someone like Rav Kahana, who was a {\i gavra raba}, a man of stature, for whom it was genuinely undignified to go about without a head covering. One can understand how he would truly be willing to pay five {\i sela\u8217'im} for a {\i sudra} under those circumstances. But if a normal person, with no particular pressing need, simply asserts that a trivial object is worth five {\i sela\u8217'im}, the law applies the principle of {\i batla da\u8217'ato etzel kol adam} \u8212- his claimed valuation is simply discounted, because no reasonable person under those circumstances would pay that amount. A further illustration is the case of Mar bar Rav Ashi himself, who paid thirteen for a {\i sudra} worth ten, because it was of a quality appropriate to his standing and genuinely difficult to find.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The contrast between Rabba and Rav Yosef thus runs deeper than the question of formal assessment. Rav Yosef, who requires {\i shaveh kessef} to function as money, demands that the value be {\i kaitz} \u8212- fixed \u8212- in a way that reflects the objective determinacy of currency. Rabba, who holds that {\i shaveh kessef} functions as an object of value on its own terms, is comfortable with subjective valuation, provided it is a genuinely credible one. For Rabba, it need not fit the mold of money; it need only represent authentic value.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafot on Shiraim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} concludes with the ruling: {\i vehilchesa shiraim lo tzrichi shuma} \u8212- silk does not require appraisal, and we rule like Rabba. Tosafot raises the well-known question: given that we generally rule like Rabba over Rav Yosef, why does the {\i gemara} need to state the ruling explicitly? This question also touches on which version of the debate the ruling addresses \u8212- the first version, concerning {\i smichat da\u8217'at} when a value is claimed, or the second version, concerning whether any {\i kaitz} is required at all.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbeinu Tam offers a subtle and important distinction. The ruling that {\i shiraim} do not require {\i shuma} is specifically calibrated to the nature of silk: its value is roughly knowable \u8212- {\i shumasan yadu\u8217'a ketzat} \u8212- and the variance in how people assess it is not so wide as to undermine the woman\u8217's confidence. Silk is not the kind of item about which one can be dramatically mistaken.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But other items, such as precious stones and pearls, are a different matter entirely. With diamonds, the variance in valuation can be enormous \u8212- one must know not only whether the stone is genuine but also its cut, clarity, color, and carat weight, factors that can shift the price by orders of magnitude. In that case, {\i tzarich shuma mishum de-lo samcha da\u8217'atah} \u8212- an appraisal is required because the woman may be relying on a grossly inflated estimate of the stone\u8217's worth, and her {\i smichat da\u8217'at} is therefore compromised. Rabbeinu Tam\u8217's point thus operates on the terrain of the first version of the debate, focusing on the woman\u8217's subjective confidence rather than the abstract requirement of {\i kaitz}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This distinction has direct practical implications. Tosafot states: {\i lefikach nahagu olam lekadesh betaba\u8217'at she-ein bah even} \u8212- and this is why the universal practice developed to perform {\i kiddushin} with a plain ring that contains no precious stone. A plain gold ring is valued by weight, and gold\u8217's weight-based valuation admits of no significant variance. The woman may not know the precise sum, but there is no concern about dramatic misjudgment, and therefore {\i smichat da\u8217'at} is not at issue and no assessment is required.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This also explains the practice at the {\i chuppah} whereby the rabbi confirms with the witnesses that the ring is worth at least a {\i perutah}, and asks the {\i chassan} accordingly. This is in part a way of framing the {\i kiddushin} so that the ring is not represented as worth any specific substantial amount \u8212- thereby removing the concern about {\i smichat da\u8217'at} entirely. As for common variations in ring shape or design \u8212- a square rather than a round band, an artistic cut \u8212- these do not generate meaningful indeterminacy in value, since anyone can verify the price through ordinary channels. A ring made by Picasso would present an entirely different problem.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Maneh & Dinar\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Maneh Stam\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Elazar rules: if a man says to a woman, \u8220"Marry me with a {\i maneh}\u8221" \u8212- a hundred \u8212- and then hands her a single {\i dinar}, she is nonetheless betrothed, and he must eventually complete the payment. The reasoning, as the {\i gemara} explains, is that when he said {\i maneh} and immediately handed her a {\i dinar}, the context clarifies his intent: he was not proposing that a hundred coins constitute the {\i kessef kiddushin}. Rather, he was framing the full hundred as a {\i tenai} \u8212- a condition \u8212- while the {\i dinar} in her hand is the actual {\i kessef} of the {\i kiddushin}. The {\i gemara} invokes Rav Huna\u8217's ruling in the name of Rav: {\i kol ha-omer al menat, k\u8217'omer me-achshav dami} \u8212- one who says \u8220"on the condition\u8221" is regarded as having said \u8220"from now,\u8221" meaning the {\i kiddushin} takes effect immediately with the {\i dinar}, subject to the condition of eventual full payment.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Maneh Zu\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then raises a challenge from a {\i braita}: if a man says \u8220"Marry me with a {\i maneh}\u8221" and begins counting out the coins one by one, and either party wishes to retract \u8212- even when only the last coin remains \u8212- the right to retract is in hand, and the {\i kiddushin} has not yet taken effect. This implies that when a man says {\i maneh}, the full hundred is the {\i kessef kiddushin}, not merely a {\i tenai}, and the transaction is incomplete until the entire sum is delivered.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} resolves this by proposing that the {\i braita} deals with the case of {\i maneh zu} \u8212- \u8220"this hundred,\u8221" where the man pointed to a specific stack of coins. The {\i seifa} of the {\i braita} reinforces this: if he said {\i hitkadshi li be-maneh zu} \u8212- \u8220"marry me with this particular hundred\u8221" \u8212- and it turned out that one coin was missing, or one was copper ({\i dinar shel nechoshet}) rather than silver, the {\i kiddushin} is invalid. If, however, one coin was merely {\i dinar ra} \u8212- legitimate currency but in poor condition, one that storekeepers might decline but which would still be accepted at a bank \u8212- the {\i kiddushin} is valid, though he must replace the damaged coin.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Clarification by Action\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} ultimately confirms that both the {\i reisha} and the {\i seifa} of the {\i braita} concern {\i maneh zu} \u8212- a specific, identified sum. The {\i seifa} clarifies and extends the {\i reisha} rather than introducing a new case. This reading preserves Rabbi Elazar\u8217's ruling intact: when a man says {\i maneh stam} \u8212- an unspecified hundred \u8212- and immediately hands over a smaller amount, the action itself clarifies that the hundred is a {\i tenai} and the smaller sum is the {\i kessef kiddushin}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} does briefly consider the possibility that the {\i reisha} was {\i maneh stam} and the {\i seifa} was introduced to clarify it, but settles on the simpler reading: the {\i seifa} is not merely clarificatory \u8212- it introduces a genuine {\i chiddush}. Even within the framework of {\i maneh stam}, there is a meaningful difference between the {\i reisha} and {\i seifa}. In the {\i reisha} (read as {\i maneh stam} where the full hundred is indeed the {\i kessef kiddushin}), if one coin is defective, it can be replaced with another \u8212- since he committed to {\i a} hundred, not {\i this} hundred. But in the {\i seifa}, where he identified a specific stack as the {\i kessef kiddushin}, the entire identified amount must be as represented; replacing a defective coin after the fact does not remedy the failed {\i kiddushin}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Dinar Ra\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara\u8217's} distinction between {\i dinar ra} and {\i dinar shel nechoshet} raises a further question. A {\i dinar shel nechoshet} \u8212- a counterfeit or base-metal coin representing itself as silver \u8212- is simply not what was promised, and the {\i kiddushin} fails. But a {\i dinar ra} is genuine currency, even if worn or damaged; it has not lost its identity as legal tender. Rav Papa clarifies: the case of {\i dinar ra} that still validates the {\i kiddushin} is one where the coin {\i nafik al yedei hadach} \u8212- it circulates, albeit with some difficulty. It is not refused categorically; it may require some insistence, but it is still recognized. The woman received what was promised in substance, even if not in ideal condition, and therefore the {\i kiddushin} holds \u8212- with the obligation to exchange the coin for a better one.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Meshkon Issues\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Meshkon Not Kessef\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Nachman rules: if a man says to a woman \u8220"Marry me with a {\i maneh}\u8221" and places his watch before her as {\i mashkon} \u8212- collateral \u8212- she is not married. The principle he states is {\i maneh ein kan, mashkon ein kan}: the hundred is not here, and the {\i mashkon} is not here as {\i kessef kiddushin}. The {\i mashkon} was not tendered as the {\i kessef} of the transaction but as a pledge of future payment, much as the collateral in the story of Yehuda and Tamar functioned as an assurance of a promised gift. An object given as collateral, however valuable, is not the {\i kessef kiddushin} \u8212- and since the {\i maneh} itself was never given, the {\i kiddushin} did not take effect.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kedisha B\u8217'Meshkon\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava challenges Rav Nachman from a {\i braita} that states: {\i kiddeshah be-mashkon mekudeshet} \u8212- if a man betroths a woman with {\i mashkon}, she is betrothed. The {\i gemara} immediately distinguishes: that {\i braita} refers to {\i mashkon de-acherim} \u8212- collateral belonging to someone else \u8212- and its validity derives from the principle of Rav Yitzchak.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Creditor Owns\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Yitzchak teaches: {\i minayin le-va\u8217'al chov she-koneh mashkon} \u8212- from where do we know that a creditor acquires ownership of collateral he holds? From the verse {\i ul\u8217'cha tihiyeh tzedakah} \u8212- \u8220"and it shall be righteousness unto you\u8221" \u8212- said in the context of returning a garment taken as {\i mashkon} each day or night. If the creditor did not own the collateral, asks Rav Yitzchak, in what sense is returning it an act of righteousness? One can always perform an act of kindness in returning something one lawfully holds without owning it. The inference of {\i tzedakah} implies that he is giving back something {\i his own} \u8212- which means the creditor holds genuine ownership in the {\i mashkon}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot raises a technical objection: even without ownership, returning the garment so the debtor can sleep in it is an act of righteousness on its face. Tosafot then introduces an alternative explanation for the derivation, noting that the verse continues {\i ve-shachav be-salmato u-verachecha} \u8212- \u8220"he will sleep in his garment and bless you.\u8221" If the creditor does not own the {\i mashkon}, then the blessing the poor man gives in return for use of his own garment would constitute {\i ribit devarim} \u8212- verbal usury \u8212- a form of benefit accruing to the lender from the loan. If, however, the creditor owns the {\i mashkon} and is genuinely returning something of his own, the blessing is a response to a gift, not to the loan, and there is no {\i ribit}. Tosafot adds a further interpretation: {\i tzedakah} technically requires that one give from one\u8217's own \u8212- {\i ha-noten mishelo} \u8212- and the verse\u8217's use of that term implies ownership.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The practical upshot of Rav Yitzchak\u8217's principle in the present context is that when Reuven holds Shimon\u8217's watch as collateral for a loan, Reuven has genuine ownership \u8212- or at least a form of ownership \u8212- in that watch. If Reuven then gives Shimon\u8217's watch to a woman as {\i kessef kiddushin}, he is giving her something he owns. Tosafot notes a potential distinction between collateral taken at the moment of the loan versus collateral taken subsequently, but in either case, from the principle in one situation, ownership can be inferred in the other as well.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The question of what the woman actually receives in such a transaction is worth pausing over. She now holds something Reuven owned. But practically speaking, can she use the watch? She cannot \u8212- it remains Shimon\u8217's watch in most practical senses. Does Reuven transfer to her the right to collect the debt if unpaid? That is unclear. The {\i gemara} does not resolve precisely what the nature of her acquisition is; perhaps it is sufficient that something of value, which he owned, was transferred to her possession, even without her accruing direct use-benefit from it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Priti Without Payment\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then recounts the case of the sons of Rav Huna bar Avin, who were purchasing a maidservant with {\i priti} \u8212- cash \u8212- but did not have the money on hand at the moment of the transaction. They placed a piece of silver ({\i naska}) before the seller as a placeholder. Later the maidservant\u8217's value rose, and the seller sought to undo the sale, arguing it had never been finalized. The case came before Rav Ami, who ruled: {\i priti ein kan, naska ein kan} \u8212- the money is not here, and the silver piece is not here as purchase money. Parallel to {\i kiddushin}, what was placed before the seller was collateral, not the purchase price. Since the purchase money was never actually paid, the sale was not final.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot notes an interesting wrinkle: unlike a case of debt, where {\i mashkon} makes halachic sense as security for a sum already owed, here the sale had not yet been concluded and no debt yet existed. What legal function did the {\i naska} serve? Tosafot explores whether the {\i mashkon} can operate in a pre-debt context \u8212- and whether the seller could force the buyers to complete the purchase or forfeit the collateral \u8212- but leaves the question partially open. The {\i gemara\u8217's} ruling stands regardless: the {\i naska} was not the {\i kessef} of the transaction and therefore the acquisition was incomplete.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Acceptance Issues\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Destruction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i braita} teaches: if a man says \u8220"Marry me with this hundred\u8221" and hands her the money, and she takes it and throws it into the ocean or into a fire or otherwise destroys it \u8212- {\i einah mekudeshet}, she is not married. The {\i gemara} initially considers whether simply throwing it back in front of him would suffice as a rejection \u8212- she is clearly saying {\i lo be\u8217'ina}, I do not want it \u8212- and therefore the {\i braita\u8217's} need to specify destruction seems odd.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} resolves this with a {\i lo mibaya} formulation: it goes without saying that throwing it back in front of him rejects the {\i kiddushin}. The {\i chiddush} of the {\i braita} concerns the case of destruction, where one might have reasoned differently. When she destroys the money, she is destroying what could be {\i his} money \u8212- since if she rejected the {\i kiddushin}, it was never hers. That means she will owe him reimbursement. Faced with that liability, we might have supposed that she in fact accepted the {\i kiddushin} \u8212- she took it as her own and then destroyed her own money as a test of his temperament ({\i savra ebdakia le-hai gavra i ritcha hu o lo}). {\i Kamashma lan} \u8212- the {\i braita} teaches that despite this reasoning, we read her act as rejection, and she is not betrothed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sheliach Cases\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A subsequent {\i braita} presents the case of a man who offers a woman {\i kiddushin} money and she responds: \u8220"Give it to my father\u8221" or \u8220"give it to your father.\u8221" In that case \u8212- {\i einah mekudeshet} \u8212- she is not married. The {\i gemara} explains this as mocking rejection: she is saying, in effect, you want to waste your money, go give it to someone else. Even though her father would presumably hold the money for her, or might return it to her, we do not read her response as acceptance of the {\i kiddushin}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 However, if she says {\i al menat she-yekablum li} \u8212- on the condition that they accept it for me \u8212- she {\i is} betrothed, because in that formulation she is appointing them as her {\i shaliach} (agent) and the acceptance on her behalf effects the {\i kiddushin}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i braita} specifies both \u8220"give it to my father\u8221" and \u8220"give it to your father\u8221" to teach distinct lessons. The case of \u8220"my father\u8221" reveals the strength of the {\i reisha}: even though her father is a natural recipient who might hold the money for her, we still read the unqualified directive as rejection. The case of \u8220"your father\u8221" reveals the strength of the {\i seifa}: even directing the money to {\i his} father, if framed with the explicit condition that he accept it on her behalf, constitutes valid agency and the {\i kiddushin} takes effect.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ploni Distinction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i braita} then presents a parallel case with {\i ploni} \u8212- a random third party. If she says \u8220"give it to {\i ploni}\u8221" without qualification, she is not married. If she adds {\i al menat she-yekablum li}, she is. The {\i gemara} explains why both cases are necessary, since each teaches something the other does not.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Had the {\i braita} only stated the case of father: we would have understood that the {\i al menat} formula works with family members \u8212- {\i samcha da\u8217'atah aliyei}, she trusts them to act on her behalf \u8212- but might have doubted whether it works with a stranger, since there is less reason to trust that {\i ploni} will faithfully execute her agency. And had the {\i braita} only stated the case of {\i ploni}: we would have understood that the unqualified directive to a stranger is a rejection \u8212- {\i de-lo mekarva da\u8217'atah legabei}, she has no close relationship with him \u8212- but might have misread a directive to her own father as an acceptance, reasoning that she feels close enough to her father to be directing a gift through him as an expression of her agreeing to the {\i kiddushin}. The {\i braita} therefore teaches both: the unqualified directive to father is still rejection; the qualified directive to {\i ploni} is still valid agency.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rashi makes explicit what underlies this: {\i einah mekudeshet ve-einah doma le-ten maneh le-ploni ve-ikadesh lach} \u8212- this is unlike the case where {\i she} initiates by saying \u8220"give a hundred to so-and-so and I will be betrothed to you,\u8221" because in that earlier case {\i hi ka\u8217'amra lei me-ikara} \u8212- she was the initiator. Here, {\i meshatei hi bo} \u8212- she is mocking him. The principle of {\i areiv} from the earlier {\i gemara} \u8212- where {\i kiddushin} can be effected by a payment directed to a third party \u8212- operates on the condition that the structure of the transaction was set up at her initiative, not his.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sela on Rock\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Another {\i braita}: the man says \u8220"Marry me with this hundred\u8221" and she responds, \u8220"Put it on that rock.\u8221" If the rock does not belong to her \u8212- {\i einah mekudeshet}. If the rock {\i does} belong to her \u8212- {\i mekudeshet}. Rashi explains that when she owns the rock, she acquires the money through {\i kinyan chatzer} \u8212- the halachic principle that one\u8217's property effects acquisition on one\u8217's behalf.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Bibi raises the question: what if the rock belongs to {\i both} of them jointly? {\i Teiku} \u8212- the question is left unresolved.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot takes issue with Rashi\u8217's explanation. If the mechanism were simply {\i kinyan chatzer}, the question of a jointly-owned rock would be simple: partners cannot effect {\i kinyan chatzer} for one another in jointly-held property, so the answer should be obvious. Tosafot therefore proposes a different framework: the determinative factor is {\i smichat da\u8217'at} \u8212- whether she has confident reliance that she will be able to retrieve the money. When the rock is hers, she has that confidence. When the rock is not hers, she does not. And when the rock is jointly owned, it is genuinely unclear whether her {\i smichat da\u8217'at} is sufficient \u8212- hence the unresolved {\i teiku}. This is admittedly a striking approach, since the woman never actually takes possession of the money. The {\i gemara} throughout this section seems less concerned with perfecting a formal act of {\i kinyan} than with identifying what constitutes sufficient acceptance in the context of {\i kiddushin} \u8212- a question that remains to be clarified through the accumulation of these cases.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Dog Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A final {\i braita}: the man says \u8220"Marry me with this loaf of bread\u8221" and she responds \u8220"Give it to the dog.\u8221" If the dog is not hers \u8212- {\i einah mekudeshet}. If the dog belongs to her \u8212- {\i mekudeshet}. The {\i gemara} leaves open whether the mechanism here is that she acquires the loaf through her dog (an extension of {\i kinyan chatzer}), or whether the {\i kessef kiddushin} is not the loaf itself but the benefit she receives from having her dog fed at his expense. This ambiguity is meaningful: across all these cases, the {\i gemara} does not fully resolve what precisely constitutes the act of {\i kiddushin} \u8212- formal acquisition, receipt of benefit, or some combination. As with {\i Gittin}, the question of whether what is required is a genuine act of giving and receiving, or merely placing something in a domain associated with the recipient, remains to be worked out through the full range of cases.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Kiddushin 9\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rejection via Condition\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Dog Distraction Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The sugya begins at the bottom of 8b with a case left unresolved \u8212- a {\i teiku} \u8212- by Rav Mari: a man offers a woman bread for {\i kiddushin}, and she responds by telling him to give the bread to a dog in order to distract it from biting her. That case was left open. The Gemara now moves to a related scenario.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Poor Person Rejection\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Hitkadshi li bekikar tenahu le\u8217'ani} \u8212- the man says, \u8220"Be married to me with this loaf of bread,\u8221" and the woman replies, \u8220"Give it to that poor person.\u8221" The ruling is {\i eina mekudeshes} \u8212- she is not betrothed. This applies even in the case of {\i afilu ani hasamuch aleha}, even if the poor person is one who regularly relies on {\i tzedakah} that she provides.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tzedakah Obligation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The reason, {\i mai ta\u8217'ama}, is articulated as follows: {\i amra leh ki heichi demichayavat beih ana hachi michayavat beih at} \u8212- she is saying to him, \u8220"Just as I am obligated to support him, so too are you.\u8221" In other words, we each bear the same obligation to give {\i tzedakah}. Her instruction to him to give the bread to the poor person is therefore a rejection of the {\i kiddushin} \u8212- she is not accepting the bread on her own behalf at all. It is worth noting that {\i halakha} does recognize a certain precedence in giving {\i tzedakah} to those who regularly depend on a particular individual, to the extent that it takes on the character of a near-obligation. And yet even that special relationship is insufficient to transform her redirection of the bread into an act of acceptance.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Double Language\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hava Mehava (Buttons)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara introduces a series of cases involving {\i lashon kaful} \u8212- double language. {\i Hahu gavra dehavah kamizban chumrei pitchita} \u8212- there was a man selling buttons, or some kind of trinkets. Rashi identifies these as tin buttons ({\i butnin she\u8217'osin shel bedil}), and this is the first Rashi on 9a. A woman approached him and said, {\i havli chad shuka} \u8212- give me one button or necklace. He replied: {\i ee havinah lach mekudeshet li} \u8212- if I give it to you, will you be married to me? Her response: {\i hava me-hava} \u8212- give it, give it \u8212- a doubled formulation. Rav Chama rules: {\i kol hava me-hava lav klum hee} \u8212- when the response is phrased with this double {\i lashon}, it does not constitute acceptance of {\i kiddushin}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The interpretive question is whether {\i hava me-hava} expresses consent or rejection. The double formulation signals that she is simply brushing him off and demanding the item without accepting his condition. Tosafot engages this discussion as well, and the point is that a doubled imperative of this kind reflects insistence on receiving the object rather than agreement to the proposed {\i kiddushin}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ashkey Ashkayan (Wine)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A parallel case: {\i hahu gavra dehavay shatey chamra be-chanuta} \u8212- a man was drinking wine in the market. A woman came and asked, {\i havli chad kasa} \u8212- give me a cup of wine. He responded: {\i ee havinah lach mekudeshet li} \u8212- if I give it to you, will you be married to me? She replied: {\i ashkey ashkayan} \u8212- let me drink, let me drink \u8212- again a double {\i lashon}. Rav Chama rules identically: {\i kol ashkey ashkayan lav klum hee} \u8212- this double formulation does not count as acceptance.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shadi Mishadey (Dates)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A third case: {\i hahu gavra dehavay kashadi tamrey midikla} \u8212- a man was picking dates from a palm tree and throwing them down, the method by which dates were harvested. A woman came and asked, {\i shadi lee tartey} \u8212- throw me down two dates. One might note that she asks for two rather than one, as in the other cases; it has been suggested this is because a single date might not reach the value of a {\i perutah}, or simply that one date would not suffice. He responds: {\i ee shadina lach mekudeshet lee} \u8212- if I throw them down to you, will you be married to me? She replies: {\i shadi mishadey} \u8212- throw it down, throw it down. Rav Zvid rules: {\i kol shadi mishadey lav klum hee} \u8212- again, the double {\i lashon} does not count.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Single Language Dispute\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This naturally raises the question: what if she had responded with only a single {\i lashon} \u8212- simply {\i hav}, or {\i ashkey}, or {\i shadi}? {\i Iba\u8217'ya lehu hav ashkey veshadey mahu?} Ravina rules {\i mekudeshes} \u8212- she would be betrothed. Rav Sama bar Rakta disagrees and says {\i eina mekudeshes}, swearing by {\i taga de-malka} \u8212- the crown of the king \u8212- a striking oath not typically encountered in the Gemara, where one more commonly finds {\i chai Elokim}. This is a {\i machloket Amoraim}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbeinu Chananel, quoted in the Rashba, explains {\i taga de-malka} to mean: even if the man had given her the crown of the king, an object of extraordinary value, she still would not be considered betrothed in this context \u8212- because the manner of her response demonstrates that she was not genuinely agreeing to the marriage at all. The logic in each of these cases, whether single or double {\i lashon}, is that when a woman asks for something and a man offers it conditionally on marriage, and she responds in a way that merely demands the item without clearly accepting his condition, that is a rejection. The double formulation makes it unambiguous; the single formulation is where the {\i machloket} lies.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Hilchata Rulings\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Summary Hilchata\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara concludes the preceding {\i sugyot} with a series of {\i hilchata} rulings. {\i V\u8217'hilchasa eina mekudeshes} \u8212- the ruling in the cases just discussed is that she is not betrothed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Silk, Rings, Diamonds\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i V\u8217'hilchasa shirai lo tzrichi shuma} \u8212- silk does not require assessment of its value for purposes of {\i kiddushin}. This resolves the {\i machloket} between Rabba and Rav Yosef: Rav Yosef had maintained that a piece of silk used for {\i kiddushin} would require a formal appraisal of its worth, while Rabba disagreed. The ruling follows Rabba.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara also rules {\i v\u8217'hilchasa k\u8217'Rabbi Eliezer} \u8212- in the case discussed previously where a man says {\i itkadshi li b\u8217'maneh v\u8217'nasan lah dinar}, meaning he promised 100 {\i dinar} and gave only one, she is {\i mekudeshes} but he owes the remainder. And {\i v\u8217'hilchasa k\u8217'Rava amar Rav Nachman} \u8212- in the case of the {\i mashkon}, where a man promises 100 {\i dinar} but gives her only collateral and no actual {\i kesef kiddushin}, the ruling is {\i maneh ein kan mashkon ein kan} \u8212- neither the collateral nor the promised money constitutes valid {\i kiddushin}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Precious Stones Issue\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might ask: if the ruling is that silk requires no assessment, why do some authorities raise concerns about using a diamond ring under the {\i chuppah}? This is precisely the concern raised by the Ri in Tosafot. The Ri distinguishes between silk, whose value is relatively familiar and not subject to gross error ({\i v\u8217'ein regilin lito\u8217'os bo kol kach}), and other items such as precious stones and gems ({\i avanim tovos u-margaliyos}), whose value may be entirely opaque. A stone that appears magnificent may in truth be nearly worthless \u8212- the example of a polished cubic zirconia is apt \u8212- while giving a woman what appears to be a diamond ring, she naturally assumes it carries significant value.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbeinu Tam responds that the ruling regarding silk teaches specifically that silk does not require assessment {\i because} its value is sufficiently known. Other items, especially precious stones, may well require more care. The concern, then, is not one of {\i shaveh prutah} \u8212- a properly cut diamond is certainly worth more than a {\i perutah} \u8212- but rather of the woman\u8217's subjective understanding of the value of what she is receiving. If she would later discover that the ring is worth dramatically less than she understood, she might argue that she only accepted on the assumption of a certain value. This Tosafot is the source for the common practice of using a plain band under the {\i chuppah} rather than a stone-set ring.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Fundamentally, if a man gives his bride an object of clear and unambiguous value, worth more than a {\i perutah}, with witnesses present and full awareness on all sides, there is no intrinsic halachic difficulty. The practice of using a plain ring has developed from an abundance of caution born of precisely this Tosafot, combined with the symbolic resonances \u8212- wholeness, the chain of tradition reaching back to {\i Rivka Imeinu} \u8212- that have attached themselves to the ring in {\i Ashkenazi} communities.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Shtar Kiddushin\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Writing the Shtar\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara turns to the second mode of {\i kiddushin}: acquisition through a written document. {\i Tanu rabbanan bishtar keitzad} \u8212- how does one effect {\i kiddushin} through a {\i shtar}? The {\i braita} states: {\i katav lo al haniyar o al hacheres af al pi she\u8217'ein bo shaveh prutah} \u8212- one writes on paper or on an ostracon, a shard of pottery used for writing in the ancient Near East, and the document effects {\i kiddushin} even though the physical medium is worth less than a {\i perutah}. The {\i shtar kiddushin}, in other words, derives its efficacy entirely from its content, not from the intrinsic value of the material.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is notable that the first {\i braita} quoted frames the document as written between the husband and the father \u8212- {\i bitecha mekudeshet li}, {\i bitecha me\u8217'ureset li}, {\i bitecha li le\u8217'intu} \u8212- \u8220"your daughter is married to me,\u8221" in three variant formulations, and the ruling is {\i harei zo mekudeshes}. This contrasts with the {\i braita} on {\i kesef kiddushin}, which focused on the interaction between the man and the adult woman directly. Here the {\i braita} envisions a younger woman, a minor daughter, for whom the father is the relevant party.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 As Rabbi Linzer noted at the outset of this {\i masechta}, {\i kiddushei shtar} was likely never widely practiced, and consequently many of its {\i halachot} are underdeveloped. The {\i sugya} is comparatively brief.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A related question noted by the Rashba concerns what minimal content a {\i shtar kiddushin} must contain. The Rashba holds that a {\i shtar} must include at minimum the names of the parties and the date, as with any proper {\i shtar}. The Ritva and many other {\i Rishonim} disagree, holding that the {\i shtar kiddushin} is a more abstract instrument: a piece of material bearing the words {\i harei at mekudeshet li} is sufficient, and nothing further is required. Whether one even needs to pronounce {\i harei at mekudeshet li} verbally when handing over the {\i shtar} is itself debated \u8212- since the words are already written in the document, perhaps the {\i shtar} itself embodies the {\i amirah}, and no separate declaration is required.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One further observation: the directionality of {\i kiddushei shtar} is the reverse of what one might expect from a commercial analogy. In a sale of land, the seller writes {\i sadi mechura lach} \u8212- \u8220"my field is sold to you\u8221" \u8212- and hands the document to the buyer, who thereby acquires the field. In {\i kiddushin}, however, it is the husband who writes and delivers the {\i shtar}, not the woman or her father. The woman, in a certain sense analogous to the seller of the field, is nonetheless not the one writing the document. This reversal is the challenge pressed by Rabbi Zeira.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Zeira\u8217's Challenge\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Matkif lah Rabbi Zeira} \u8212- Rabbi Zeira challenges: {\i ha lo dami hai shtar leh shtar zvini} \u8212- this {\i shtar kiddushin} is not analogous to a deed of sale. In a sale, {\i hatam mocher kotev lo sadi mechura lach} \u8212- the seller writes \u8220"my field is sold to you.\u8221" But here, {\i hacha baal kotev bitcha mekudeshet li} \u8212- it is the husband who writes \u8220"your daughter is married to me.\u8221" The directionality is inverted relative to what the analogy to commercial transactions would suggest. How, then, does it work?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava\u8217's Resolution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava resolves this by appeal to scriptural derivation. {\i Amar Rava hatam me-inyana de-kra ve-hacha me-inyana de-kra} \u8212- each case follows its own scriptural source. In the case of a field sale, the Torah writes {\i u-machar me-achuzato} \u8212- \u8220"and he shall sell of his ancestral land\u8221" \u8212- and {\i be-mocher tala rachmana}, the Torah places legal emphasis on the seller. Accordingly, the seller writes the deed. In {\i kiddushin}, the Torah writes {\i ki yikach} \u8212- \u8220"when a man takes\u8221" \u8212- and {\i be-baal tala rachmana}, the Torah places emphasis on the husband\u8217's act of taking. Therefore it is the husband who writes the {\i shtar}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara attempts to push back. In the context of land sales, one may also read {\i sados be-kesef yiknu} \u8212- \u8220"fields are bought with money\u8221" \u8212- with different vowels ({\i kari bei yaknu}), implying the buyer also has legal standing in the transaction. Similarly in {\i kiddushin}, perhaps the relevant {\i pasuk} should be read {\i ki yukach} \u8212- \u8220"when she is taken\u8221" \u8212- emphasizing the woman\u8217's role. But this is rejected from the {\i pasuk} {\i es biti nasati la-ish ha-zeh} \u8212- \u8220"I gave my daughter to this man\u8221" \u8212- in the context of {\i motzi shem ra}, where it is the father who acts as giver, not the husband who takes.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Biblical Sources Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara\u8217's resolution is ultimately that the scriptural derivations are not fully adequate, and Rava concludes: {\i ela amar Rava hilchasa ninhu} \u8212- these are {\i halacha le-Moshe mi-Sinai}, transmitted traditions from Sinai, {\i ve-asmachinhu rabbanan a-kra} \u8212- and the Rabbis simply attached them to {\i psukim} as mnemonics, but the underlying law is a received tradition.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot raises an objection: it cannot be that both cases are {\i halacha le-Moshe mi-Sinai}, because the commercial case of a seller writing the deed of sale works perfectly from the {\i psukim} without needing recourse to tradition. Tosafot therefore limits the claim: only the directionality of {\i shtar kiddushin} \u8212- that it is the husband who writes the document \u8212- is {\i halacha le-Moshe mi-Sinai}. The Rambam\u8217's framework is relevant here: {\i halacha le-Moshe mi-Sinai} refers specifically to those laws that are entirely arbitrary from the perspective of the text, laws one could never derive through reading and deduction \u8212- such as the minimum size of a {\i kezayit} or the forty {\i se\u8217'ah} of a {\i mikveh} \u8212- as opposed to {\i perushim ha-mekubalim mi-pi Moshe Rabbeinu}, received interpretations that could in principle be recovered if lost.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A further alternative resolution is offered: {\i ve-iba\u8217'it eima hatam nami ketiv va\u8217'ekach et sefer ha-miknah} \u8212- perhaps after all one may say that the buyer\u8217's writing of the deed of sale is also supported scripturally, from the verse in Yirmiyahu where Yirmiyahu himself took the deed of purchase when buying his uncle Hanam\u8217'el\u8217's field in Anatot. In that episode, Yirmiyahu\u8217's act of purchasing ancestral land during the Babylonian crisis was a prophetic gesture of hope \u8212- a public declaration that the exile would end and the people would return. This {\i pasuk} comes from {\i Ketuvim}-adjacent material in Nevi\u8217'im, however, which raises its own difficulty: as a general principle, {\i halakha} is not derived from Nevi\u8217'im and Ketuvim in the same way as from the Torah, and many {\i Rishonim} hold that any derivation from Nevi\u8217'im and Ketuvim is automatically an {\i asmachta}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Shtar Requirements\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Minor Daughter Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara presents further {\i halachot} regarding the writing and transmission of {\i shtar kiddushin}. Rav Amram in the name of Rav Nachman rules: {\i katav lah al haniyar o al hacheres af al pi she\u8217'ein bo shaveh prutah} \u8212- if one writes on paper or pottery, even without the medium reaching the value of a {\i perutah}, and writes {\i bitecha mekudeshet li}, {\i bitecha me\u8217'ureset li}, or {\i bitecha li le\u8217'intu}, the {\i kiddushin} is effective {\i bein al yedei aviha bein al yedei atzmah mida\u8217'ato} \u8212- whether the {\i shtar} is delivered to the father or directly to the daughter herself, provided it is done with the father\u8217's {\i da\u8217'at}. This applies {\i vehu shelo bagra} \u8212- so long as the daughter has not yet reached {\i bagrus} (12\u189 ? years of age). When a father marries off his minor daughter, the operative legal standing is the father\u8217's own, not his daughter\u8217's; he is not merely a proxy for her but rather the primary actor in a distinct mechanism of {\i kiddushin}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Adult Woman Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 When she has reached majority, the {\i shtar} must be written {\i harei at mekudeshet li}, {\i harei at li le\u8217'intu}, or {\i harei at me\u8217'ureset li}, and it may be delivered {\i bein al yedei aviha bein al yedei atzmah mida\u8217'atah} \u8212- either to her or to her father, with her consent, {\i vehu shebagra} \u8212- in the case where she has come of age, meaning she is older than twelve and a half.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This text is somewhat problematic: it appears to say that the father could accept {\i kiddushin} on behalf of a {\i bogeret}, which is inconsistent with other sources in this Gemara that make clear that once a woman has reached majority, her father no longer has independent authority over her {\i kiddushin}. All the {\i Rishonim} therefore explain that {\i mida\u8217'atah} here must mean that she has specifically appointed her father as a {\i shaliach kabbalah} on her behalf. Without such an appointment, delivery to the father of a {\i bogeret} would not constitute valid {\i kiddushin}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Leshem Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Reish Lakish poses the central question of the section: {\i shtar eirusin she-katavo shelo lishma, mahu?} \u8212- what is the law if a {\i shtar kiddushin} was written {\i shelo lishma}, that is, not written with the intent of betrothing this particular woman? We know that a {\i get} must be written {\i lishma} \u8212- it must be written for the sake of this specific woman for the purpose of this specific divorce. Does the same apply to {\i shtar kiddushin}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The question is framed as an alternative: {\i havayah l\u8217'yetziah makshinan?} \u8212- do we apply the principle of {\i hekesh havayah l\u8217'yetziah}, connecting marriage to divorce? If so, just as {\i get} requires {\i lishma}, so too {\i shtar kiddushin} would require {\i lishma}. Or perhaps: {\i havayah l\u8217'hadadi makshinan} \u8212- do we instead compare different modes of marriage to one another? If so, just as {\i kesef kiddushin} requires no {\i lishma} (how would one even make money {\i lishma}? One cannot mint coins {\i leshem kiddushin}), so too {\i shtar kiddushin} would require no {\i lishma}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 After raising the question, Reish Lakish resolves it himself: {\i havayah l\u8217'yetziah makshinan}, and we derive this from the {\i pasuk} {\i v\u8217'yatz\u8217'a v\u8217'haysa}. Reish Lakish is consistently the champion of {\i hekesh havayah l\u8217'yetziah} throughout the {\i masechta}, holding that {\i kiddushin} and {\i gerushin} are deeply and structurally connected such that many {\i halachot} of one may be derived from the other. Rabbi Yochanan will take a more limited view when the dispute is revisited at length on 44a. The Gemara on 44a recounts colorfully that when Rabbi Yochanan and the {\i chachamim} ruled against {\i hekesh havayah l\u8217'yetziah}, Reish Lakish rose and proclaimed {\i tzavech k\u8217'kruchiya} \u8212- squawking like a goose \u8212- {\i havayah l\u8217'yetziah, havayah l\u8217'yetziah, havayah l\u8217'yetziah}, so committed was he to this principle.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The broader stakes of this {\i machloket} are significant. {\i Gerushin} is not simply an undoing of {\i kiddushin} \u8212- a woman who has been divorced does not revert to her pre-marital status in all respects. She acquires a new {\i halachic} identity as a {\i gerushah}, with distinct consequences for {\i terumah} and for the prohibition of marrying a {\i kohen}. Whether {\i kiddushin} and {\i gerushin} are mirror images of one another or rather two distinct processes with their own logic is therefore a question with deep structural implications.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Since {\i shtar kiddushin} was never practically employed, many of these {\i halachot} were never fully {\i paskened}, and a number of foundational questions \u8212- including whether {\i shtar kiddushin} must include names and a date like a {\i get}, as the Rashba holds, or not, as the Ritva holds \u8212- remain contested without definitive ruling.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Writing Knowledge Dispute\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Ismar: kesavo lishma v\u8217'shelo mada\u8217'atah} \u8212- what is the law if the {\i shtar kiddushin} was written {\i lishma} but without the woman\u8217's knowledge? Rava and Ravina rule {\i mekudeshes} \u8212- the {\i kiddushin} is valid. Rav Papa and Rav Sharvya rule {\i eina mekudeshes} \u8212- it is not valid.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Papa then explains both positions from the same source. {\i Eima ta\u8217'ama didhu} \u8212- the reasoning of Rava and Ravina: from {\i v\u8217'yatz\u8217'a v\u8217'haysa}, applying {\i hekesh havayah l\u8217'yetziah}, we see that a {\i get} may be written {\i lishma v\u8217'shelo mida\u8217'ata} \u8212- a woman need not consent to, or even know about, her own divorce. By parallel, a {\i shtar kiddushin} written {\i lishma} is effective even without her knowledge. {\i Eima ta\u8217'ama didi} \u8212- Rav Papa\u8217's own reasoning against this: the same {\i hekesh} cuts the other way. In {\i gerushin}, we require {\i da\u8217'as makneh} \u8212- the consent of the one who is transferring. In divorce, that is the husband, and his consent is indeed required. By parallel, in {\i kiddushin}, the {\i da\u8217'as makneh} belongs to the woman or her father \u8212- they are the ones effecting the transfer \u8212- and therefore {\i their} consent is required, including at the stage of writing the {\i shtar}. Note that this argument does not apply to {\i kesef kiddushin}, which has little connection to the structure of {\i gerushin} in the first place.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tannaim Clarification\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then cites a {\i braita} in apparent support of Rav Papa: {\i ein kotvim shtarei eirusin u-nisuin ela mi-da\u8217'at sheneihem} \u8212- one does not write {\i shtarei eirusin} or {\i nisuin} except with the knowledge and consent of both parties. The Gemara asks: does this not directly support the position that a {\i shtar kiddushin} requires the woman\u8217's prior knowledge?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The response is that the {\i braita} is not discussing {\i shtar kiddushin} itself. {\i Mai lav shtarei eirusin u-nisuin mamash lo \u8212- shtarei pasikta.} The {\i braita} refers to {\i shtarei pasikta} \u8212- the engagement agreement, what we call {\i tannaim}, recording the financial commitments of both families, how much each side will contribute, and related arrangements. Rashi explains: {\i shtarei pasikta ha-tannaim she-beineihem u-mammon she-porskin zeh al zeh} \u8212- the conditions set between them and the financial obligations each side undertakes. These are monetary contracts in the ordinary sense, and of course monetary contracts require the {\i da\u8217'at} of both parties. The {\i braita} therefore says nothing about {\i shtar kiddushin} proper.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is supported by the dictum of Rav Gidel in the name of Rav: if the families verbally agree \u8212- how much will you give your son, how much will you give your daughter \u8212- and the couple then proceeds to perform {\i kiddushin}, the {\i chalot} of the {\i kiddushin} act cements the verbal agreements. {\i Hen ve-hen ha-devarim ha-niknim be-amirah} \u8212- this is one of the rare instances in {\i halakha} where a verbal commitment alone creates binding legal obligation. It is worth noting that in Talmudic times, {\i kiddushin} served a function much closer to what we now call an engagement \u8212- it was performed months before the wedding celebration \u8212- yet because of its full {\i halachic} force, breaking it off required a {\i get}. By the time of Rav, there was already a practice of having some form of prior agreement ({\i shidduchei}) before the {\i kiddushin} itself.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Bi\u8217'ah Kiddushin\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Be\u8217'ulat Ba\u8217'al Source\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now turns to the third mode of {\i kiddushin}: {\i bi\u8217'ah}, acquisition through sexual intercourse. {\i Menalan?} \u8212- from where is this derived? Rabbi Abbahu in the name of Rabbi Yochanan cites the {\i pasuk} {\i be\u8217'ulat ba\u8217'al} from the context of {\i na\u8217'arah ha-me\u8217'orasah} \u8212- the case of a betrothed girl who is raped. The {\i pasuk} uses the phrase {\i be\u8217'ulat ba\u8217'al}, teaching that a man becomes her {\i ba\u8217'al} through {\i be\u8217'ilah}: {\i melamed she-na\u8217'asait lo ba\u8217'al al yedei be\u8217'ilah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Zeira\u8217's Challenge\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Zeira challenges Rabbi Abbahu: why derive this from the {\i na\u8217'arah ha-me\u8217'orasah} context at all? The {\i pasuk} in the {\i parashah} of {\i kiddushin} itself says {\i ki yikach ish isha u\u8217've\u8217'alah} \u8212- this is the verse cited by Rabbi Yehudah HaNasi ({\i Rebbi}) to teach that {\i kiddushin} may be effected through {\i bi\u8217'ah}. Why do we need the additional {\i pasuk} of {\i be\u8217'ulat ba\u8217'al}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The response: {\i ee meihatam hava amina ad demikadesh v\u8217'hadar be\u8217'il} \u8212- if we learned exclusively from {\i ki yikach ish isha u\u8217've\u8217'alah}, we might have supposed that {\i kiddushin} requires both the {\i kinyan} \u8212- money or {\i shtar} \u8212- {\i and} a sexual act, that the two together constitute {\i kiddushin}. The {\i pasuk} of {\i be\u8217'ulat ba\u8217'al} teaches that {\i bi\u8217'ah} alone, without prior {\i kesef} or {\i shtar}, suffices as the {\i kinyan} of {\i kiddushin}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Na\u8217'arah Ha-Me\u8217'orasah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Abba bar Memel then presses a challenge from the other direction. If it were true \u8212- as the rejected {\i hava amina} supposed \u8212- that one must first perform {\i kiddushin} and then have intercourse, how would there ever be a case of {\i na\u8217'arah ha-me\u8217'orasah} of the kind the Torah subjects to {\i skilah} rather than {\i chenek}? The Torah distinguishes this case as a special category: a betrothed virgin who is raped receives a different punishment than an ordinary married woman.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But within the {\i hava amina}, there is a dilemma. {\i Ei d\u8217'akdesh v\u8217'hadar be\u8217'il} \u8212- if he did {\i kiddushin} and then had (vaginal) intercourse, {\i be\u8217'ulah hi} \u8212- she is already {\i be\u8217'ulah}, no longer a virgin, and the rapist would be liable for {\i chenek}, not {\i skilah}. {\i Ei d\u8217'akdesh v\u8217'lo be\u8217'il} \u8212- and if he did {\i kiddushin} but had not yet had intercourse, {\i lav klum hu} \u8212- that is also nothing, since within the {\i hava amina}, {\i kiddushin} without subsequent intercourse would not be complete. How then would the case of {\i na\u8217'arah ha-me\u8217'orasah} with its special {\i skilah} penalty ever arise?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Anal Sex Resolution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i rabbanan} before Abaye propose a resolution: {\i mishkachat lah k\u8217'gon she\u8217'ba aleha arus shelo k\u8217'darkah} \u8212- the case could arise if the betrothed man had engaged in anal intercourse with her. She would remain physically a virgin in the relevant physiological sense, and the subsequent rapist would still face the {\i na\u8217'arah ha-me\u8217'orasah} case.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye rejects this: {\i ad kan lo pligi Rebbi v\u8217'rabanan ela b\u8217'acher} \u8212- the {\i machloket} between {\i Rebbi} and the {\i Rabbanan} (in the {\i braita} from Sanhedrin about ten men raping a woman one after another, with the first punishable by {\i skilah} and all subsequent ones by {\i chenek} according to {\i Rebbi}) concerns only the case of {\i acher}, another man who is not the husband. But as for the husband himself: {\i aval ba\u8217'al divrei hakol im ba aleha shelo k\u8217'darkah as\u8217'ah be\u8217'ulah} \u8212- everyone agrees that if the husband himself has anal intercourse with his wife, she is rendered {\i be\u8217'ulah}, regardless of the absence of hymenal change. A marital act of sex \u8212- whether vaginal or anal \u8212- renders her a {\i be\u8217'ulah} in law.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shtar Alternative\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Nachman bar Yitzchak offers a different resolution to the dilemma of {\i na\u8217'arah ha-me\u8217'orasah}: {\i mishkachat lah k\u8217'gon she\u8217'kideish b\u8217'shtar} \u8212- the case may arise if the {\i kiddushin} was effected through a {\i shtar} alone. A {\i shtar kiddushin} is powerful enough to complete the entire {\i kinyan} of {\i kiddushin} without any {\i bi\u8217'ah} at all. Thus, within the {\i hava amina} that {\i kesef} plus {\i bi\u8217'ah} were required together, a {\i shtar kiddushin} would stand independently, and a woman betrothed solely through a {\i shtar} would be {\i me\u8217'ureset} \u8212- and if subsequently raped, the case of {\i na\u8217'arah ha-me\u8217'orasah} would apply perfectly, without any complication about whether she remains a virgin.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Wife vs.\u160 ?Shifcha\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Finally, Rabbi Yochanan\u8217's use of the {\i pasuk} {\i u\u8217've\u8217'alah} is addressed. Once the Gemara establishes that the derivation comes from {\i be\u8217'ulat ba\u8217'al}, the question is: {\i mai avid lei} Rabbi Yochanan with the {\i pasuk} of {\i u\u8217've\u8217'alah} from {\i ki yikach ish isha u\u8217've\u8217'alah}? The answer: {\i hahu miba\u8217'ei lei} \u8212- he uses it to derive that a wife may be acquired through {\i bi\u8217'ah}, but an {\i amah ha-ivriyah}, a Hebrew maidservant given over by her father as an indentured servant, is not acquired through {\i bi\u8217'ah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might have thought to derive the contrary by {\i kal va-chomer} from {\i yevamah}: a {\i yevamah} cannot be acquired through money, yet she is acquired through {\i bi\u8217'ah}; surely a woman who {\i can} be acquired through money \u8212- {\i kesef kiddushin} \u8212- should also be acquirable through {\i bi\u8217'ah}, and by extension so too an {\i amah ha-ivriyah}. The rejection: {\i mah leyevamah sheken zekukah ve\u8217'omedes} \u8212- a {\i yevamah} is already in a distinct state of {\i zikah} to the {\i yavam}, which makes her case unique and unsuitable for generalization.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Furthermore, one might have argued from the {\i pasuk} {\i im acheres yikach lo} in the laws of {\i amah ha-ivriyah} \u8212- the Torah there connects her to another wife, suggesting that the {\i amah ha-ivriyah} is in some sense analogous to a wife, and just as a wife may be acquired through {\i bi\u8217'ah}, so too an {\i amah ha-ivriyah}. {\i Ka mashma lan} \u8212- it is to exclude precisely this inference that Rabbi Yochanan requires the {\i pasuk} of {\i u\u8217've\u8217'alah}: marriage to a wife is effected through {\i bi\u8217'ah}, but the {\i kinyanim} of {\i amah ha-ivriyah} are not.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Kiddushin 10\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Na\u8217'arah Me\u8217'orasah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Skilah vs.\u160 ?Chenek\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The discussion picks up toward the bottom of 9b, where the Gemara had been analyzing the circumstances under which a man who rapes a {\i na\u8217'arah me\u8217'orasah} \u8212- a betrothed virgin, meaning a woman over whom {\i kiddushin} has taken effect but who remains physically a virgin \u8212- is punished by {\i skilah}, as opposed to the punishment for adultery generally, which is {\i chenek}. Both are capital punishments, and one ends up dead either way; nonetheless, {\i chazal} understood {\i skilah} to be the more severe of the two. That evaluative distinction is itself rooted in a broader discussion in Sanhedrin, where the principle of {\i ve\u8217'ahavta l\u8217're\u8217'acha kamocha} \u8212- in its formulation of {\i bror lo misah yafah}, selecting a fitting or pleasant form of death even for one being executed \u8212- shapes the entire architecture of the different death penalties and how damaging each is to the human body.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ten Rapists Braita\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i braita} under discussion had stated: {\i ba\u8217'u aleha asarah anashim ve\u8217'adayin hi besulah} \u8212- ten men could rape the same woman and she would still retain the status of {\i besulah}. This is because the {\i braita} is discussing anal intercourse, which does not have the same consequences as vaginal intercourse; specifically, she remains a {\i besulah} afterwards, and therefore all ten rapists would be subject to {\i skilah} rather than {\i chenek}. This is a matter of dispute between {\i Rebbi} and the {\i rabbanan}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then asks: {\i ve\u8217'Rebbi hai svara minaleh} \u8212- from where does {\i Rebbi} derive his position? The answer is that the {\i pasuk} referring to marriage uses the word {\i u\u8217've\u8217'alah}. Had the {\i Torah} wished to convey only one idea, it could have simply written {\i u\u8217'vaal}. The additional {\i heh} allows {\i Rebbi} to derive two separate {\i derashos} from a single word.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then introduces {\i Rava}, who uses that same {\i pasuk} for a different {\i drasha} entirely: {\i kiddushin hamesurin l\u8217'viah havei kiddushin} \u8212- {\i kiddushin} for which a sexual relationship would subsequently be permitted constitutes valid {\i kiddushin}. But {\i kiddushin she\u8217'ein mesurin l\u8217'viah} \u8212- {\i kiddushin} effected at a moment when a sexual relationship between the parties would be prohibited \u8212- does not constitute valid {\i kiddushin}. {\i Rava} derives this principle from {\i ki yikach ish ishah u\u8217've\u8217'alah}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Kiddushin Mesurin\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava\u8217's Principle\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rava}\u8217's principle, as stated in the Gemara, is: {\i kiddushin hamesurin l\u8217'viah havei kiddushin, kiddushin she\u8217'einam mesurin l\u8217'viah lo havei kiddushin}. The {\i pasuk} could have said {\i o va\u8217'alah} \u8212- either take {\i kiddushin} or have a sexual relationship \u8212- but because it says {\i u\u8217've\u8217'alah}, using the conjunction \u8220"and both,\u8221" we derive that the two are necessarily linked. This is the {\i shema mina kulu hu}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rashi vs.\u160 ?Tosafot\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Turning ahead to 51a, there is a significant {\i machlokes} between {\i Rashi} and {\i Tosafot} regarding how to understand the category of {\i kiddushin she\u8217'ein mesurin l\u8217'viah}. {\i Rashi}\u8217's position is that the principle refers to any and all of the {\i arayos} \u8212- the prohibited sexual relationships. If one were to attempt {\i kiddushin} with a sister, grandmother, or any other {\i ervah}, such {\i kiddushin} would be entirely ineffective. This connects to the broader principle that for certain categories of {\i arayos}, the relationship is governed by {\i ein kiddushin tofsin} \u8212- the {\i kiddushin} does not take hold at all. Indeed, according to at least some {\i tannaim}, this is precisely the criterion that defines {\i mamzerut}: the {\i ervah} must be so severe that {\i kiddushin} would not function between the parties.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafot\u8217's Reading\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot}, however, disagrees fundamentally. {\i Tosafot} holds that {\i kiddushin she\u8217'ein mesurin l\u8217'viah} does not refer to {\i arayos} at all. {\i Tosafot} understands the principle as addressing the distinction between two categories of prohibited relationships within {\i halacha}. There are {\i chayvei lavin} \u8212- for example, a {\i kohen} marrying a {\i zonah}, or a {\i yisrael} marrying a {\i mamzeret} \u8212- where the prohibition itself is not affected by marital status in the sense that the same prohibition existed independently before any {\i kiddushin} took place.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot}\u8217's own formulation makes this explicit: {\i hatkadshi li b\u8217'kiddushin\u8230 ? she\u8217'issur habiah al yedei kiddushin} \u8212- the case {\i Tosafot} has in mind is one where the {\i issur biah} comes into being {\i because of} the {\i kiddushin} itself. For instance, if a man attempts to marry one of two sisters without specifying which one, the act of {\i kiddushin} with respect to one sister immediately creates a prohibition regarding the other, leaving him in a situation where neither is permitted to him due to {\i safek}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yom Kippur Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Ran} on the first {\i daf} of the {\i Rif} in {\i Kesuvos} raises a penetrating difficulty from the opening {\i mishnah} of {\i Maseches Yoma}, which states that a spare wife is prepared for the {\i Kohen Gadol} in the event that his wife dies on {\i Yom Kippur}, since the {\i Kohen Gadol} is required to be married on that day. The difficulty: one cannot perform {\i kiddushin} on {\i Yom Kippur}, because sexual relations are forbidden on {\i Yom Kippur}, and therefore any such {\i kiddushin} would constitute {\i kiddushin she\u8217'einam mesurin l\u8217'viah} \u8212- and hence be invalid. The {\i Ran} is compelled to address this problem. Part of the answer must involve the fact that {\i kiddushin} need only be {\i reuyim l\u8217'viah} \u8212- suited to a sexual relationship \u8212- in a prospective sense; {\i motzoei Yom Kippur}, the 11th of {\i Tishrei}, sexual relations would immediately become permitted. But the question remains at least an interesting one, and the {\i Ran} must work through it carefully.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Na\u8217'arah Me\u8217'orasah (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rebbi\u8217's Distinction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Returning to the {\i sugya} of {\i na\u8217'arah me\u8217'orasah}: the {\i braita} quoted the {\i tanna kamma} as holding that all ten rapists would be subject to {\i skilah}, since she remains a {\i besulah} throughout. {\i Rebbi} then adds his own position: {\i omer ani harishon b\u8217'skilah ve\u8217'kulum b\u8217'chenek} \u8212- it is only the first rapist who receives {\i skilah}; all the rest receive {\i chenek}. {\i Rebbi}\u8217's reasoning is that the first rapist is the one who would have effected {\i kiddushei biah} with her, as it were \u8212- he is the one who acts in the position of the husband \u8212- and the remaining rapists are not. It is worth noting that the phrase {\i omer ani} \u8212- \u8220"I say\u8221" \u8212- appears in rabbinic literature to be used exclusively by {\i Rebbi}, and several {\i acharonim} have observed that this locution reflects a kind of personal humility: even as the author of the {\i Mishnah}, {\i Rebbi} presents his own positions as personal opinions rather than authoritative rulings, a characteristic notably paralleled in the accounts of his humility at the end of {\i Maseches Sotah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then works through how {\i Rebbi} and the {\i rabbanan} each handle the available {\i pesukim}. The {\i pasuk} by {\i na\u8217'arah hame\u8217'orasah} uses the word {\i be\u8217'ulas ba\u8217'al}. {\i Rebbi} learns from this: {\i ba\u8217'al oseh ota be\u8217'ulah shelo k\u8217'darkah, ve\u8217'ein acher oseh ota be\u8217'ulah shelo k\u8217'darkah} \u8212- a woman\u8217's husband renders her a {\i be\u8217'ulah} through anal intercourse, but if another man anally rapes her, that does not render her a {\i be\u8217'ulah}. She therefore retains her {\i besulah} status, but the punishment for the subsequent rapists is {\i chenek} rather than {\i skilah}, since by {\i Rebbi}\u8217's reading only the first rapist stands in the position of the one who would have married her by {\i kiddushei biah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Zeira\u8217's Reconciliation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Zeira} then clarifies the scope of the {\i machlokes}: {\i modeh Rebbi l\u8217'inyan knas d\u8217'kulu meshalmi} \u8212- {\i Rebbi} agrees with the {\i rabbanan} that all ten rapists pay the fifty-shekel {\i knas}. The {\i machlokes} is exclusively about the form of the capital punishment. What is the basis for distinguishing the {\i knas} from the death penalty? The {\i pasuk} states {\i u\u8217'meis ha\u8217'ish asher shachav imah l\u8217'vado} \u8212- the man who slept with her, he alone will die. {\i Rebbi} uses {\i l\u8217'vado} to exclude the remaining nine rapists from {\i skilah}, limiting it to the first. As for the {\i rabbanan}, they use {\i l\u8217'vado} differently \u8212- for the {\i drasha} taught in a {\i braita}: {\i u\u8217'meisu gam shneihem}, requiring that both parties be equivalent, which is the position of {\i Rabbi Yoshiyah}; while {\i Rabbi Yonasan} derives his position from {\i u\u8217'meis ha\u8217'ish asher shachav imah l\u8217'vado} in a different way. Nonetheless, {\i Rebbi} is able to accommodate all the relevant {\i derashos} within his reading, since, as the Gemara notes, the {\i pasuk} could have written {\i be\u8217'ulas ish}; the fact that it writes {\i be\u8217'ulas ba\u8217'al} allows {\i Rebbi} to derive two distinct {\i halachos} \u8212- {\i shema mina tartei}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Techilat vs.\u160 ?Sof Biah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Opening Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara on 10a opens with a new {\i ibaya}: {\i techilat biah konah o sof biah konah?} \u8212- does the initial penetration of a sexual act effect {\i kinyan kiddushin} in the context of {\i kiddushei biah}, or does the completion of the act do so?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Practical Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The practical {\i nafka mina} is as follows: suppose a man initiated intercourse with a woman but did not complete the act, and they then separated, whereupon she accepted {\i kiddushin} from a different man. Which {\i kiddushin} takes effect? The answer depends entirely on which stage of the sexual act creates the {\i kinyan kiddushin}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafot\u8217's Approaches\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} here confronts the need to reconcile this Gemara\u8217's question with a Gemara in {\i Yevamos} where it appears obvious that initial penetration \u8212- {\i ha\u8217'ara\u8217'ah} \u8212- is what counts as a sexual act throughout {\i halacha}. {\i Tosafot} cites four distinct approaches.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Rashba} (of Shantz) holds that when we say {\i ha\u8217'ara\u8217'ah} effects a {\i kinyan}, that is specifically in a case where only {\i ha\u8217'ara\u8217'ah} occurred and nothing more. If the act was completed, then the operative {\i da\u8217'at} is directed toward the completion. The {\i Rivam} takes a different approach, holding that the husband\u8217's {\i da\u8217'at} is determinative: he may direct his intent toward whichever stage of the act he chooses, and that stage is what creates the {\i kinyan}. {\i Rabbeinu Tam} argues that once the Torah has included penetration within the halachic category of a sexual act, the term {\i bi\u8217'ah} wherever it appears actually refers to {\i ha\u8217'ara\u8217'ah}. Accordingly, the Gemara\u8217's question about {\i techilat biah} versus {\i sof biah} is not asking about the beginning versus the end of the full sexual act, but rather about the beginning versus the end of {\i ha\u8217'ara\u8217'ah} itself \u8212- the beginning of the beginning, as it were, or the end of the beginning. {\i Rabbeinu Nissim Gaon} \u8212- who was from North Africa and was not technically a {\i Gaon} in the institutional sense \u8212- distinguishes according to context: in {\i Yevamos}, the discussion concerns {\i yibbum}, where the {\i yevamah} is already {\i zekukah ve\u8217'omedet} \u8212- already legally bound \u8212- and therefore a lower threshold of sexual activity suffices to complete the legal transaction, much as {\i chuppah} would. In {\i kiddushei biah}, however, where the legal act being accomplished carries greater weight, the {\i da\u8217'at} is properly directed only toward {\i gmar biah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kohen Gadol Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A further {\i nafka mina} concerns the {\i Kohen Gadol}, who has a {\i mitzvas aseh} to marry a {\i besulah}. If the initial penetration renders a woman a {\i be\u8217'ulah} \u8212- as the Gemara in {\i Yevamos} indicates \u8212- and yet the {\i kinyan kiddushin} is only created at the completion of the act, it would follow that it is impossible for a {\i Kohen Gadol} to ever effect {\i kiddushei biah} with his wife. The act would have transformed her status before the {\i kinyan} took hold. It should be noted that already in the {\i amoraic} period, {\i kiddushei biah} was prohibited as a matter of practice; but the theoretical question remains significant for understanding the {\i Kohen Gadol}\u8217's obligation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Amemar\u8217's Resolution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Amemar} resolves the {\i ibaya} by transmitting a ruling in the name of {\i Rava}: {\i kol habo\u8217'el da\u8217'ato al gmar biah} \u8212- whenever a man engages in sexual intercourse, his intent is always directed toward the completion of the act. This is an established {\i chazakah}. The {\i kinyan kiddushin} is therefore created only at the end of the act, not at the initial penetration. This is the point that requires reconciliation with the Gemara in {\i Yevamos} \u8212- the task that the four approaches in {\i Tosafot} each undertake in their own way.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Biah Nisuin Osa\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Central Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A further {\i ibaya} follows: {\i biah nisuin oseh o eirusin oseh?} \u8212- does {\i kiddushei biah} accomplish both stages of marriage in a single act, or does it effect only the first stage, {\i kiddushin} (equivalent to {\i eirusin})? With {\i kiddushei kessef} and {\i kiddushei shtar}, the {\i kessef} or {\i shtar} effects the first stage, and a separate process of {\i chuppah} is required to accomplish {\i nisuin}. The question here is whether {\i kiddushei biah} might bypass this two-stage structure entirely, reaching {\i nisuin} directly.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Three Consequences\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i nafka mina} is spelled out in three practical areas. First, {\i yerushah}: a husband inherits his wife\u8217's property only once they have reached {\i nisuin}. Second, {\i tumah}: if the husband is a {\i kohen}, he may become {\i tamei} to participate in her burial only if they have reached {\i nisuin}. Third, {\i hafaras nedarim}: the husband\u8217's unilateral power to annul his wife\u8217's vows belongs to him alone only at the stage of {\i nisuin}; during {\i eirusin}, it is a combined power shared by both the father and the husband. As the Gemara states: {\i im amarta nisuin oseh, yorshah u\u8217'mitamei lah u\u8217'meifer nedareiah}; and {\i im amarta eirusin oseh, eino yorshah ve\u8217'eino mitamei lah ve\u8217'eino meifer nedareiah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Abaye\u8217's Braita\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Abaye} brings a {\i braita} in support: {\i ha\u8217'av zakai bevito bekiddusheha bechessef bishtar u\u8217'veviah} \u8212- the father has rights to his daughter\u8217's {\i kiddushin} effected by {\i kessef}, {\i shtar}, or {\i biah} \u8212- and then lists his various entitlements: rights to objects she finds, to the work of her hands, to the annulment of her vows, to the receipt of her {\i get}, and the fact that he does not receive the produce of her independently owned property during her lifetime. The {\i braita} then continues: {\i nisseit, yeter alav habaal she\u8217'ochel peirot bechayyeha} \u8212- once she marries, the husband has greater rights than the father, specifically including the right to the produce of the wife\u8217's property during her lifetime. When a woman brings real estate into a marriage, the husband holds certain rights to the produce of that property during the marriage.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two Stages Problem\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i braita} thus mentions {\i biah} as one stage and then refers separately to {\i nisseit} as a subsequent stage \u8212- presenting {\i biah} and {\i nisuin} as two distinct moments. This appears to constitute a direct challenge to the position that {\i biah nisuin oseh}, since if {\i biah} already accomplishes {\i nisuin}, there would be no need for a distinct {\i nisseit}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Defense of Biah Nisuin\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara deflects this challenge by arguing: even according to the position that {\i biah nisuin oseh}, one could read the {\i braita}\u8217's reference to {\i nisseit} as applying only to cases of {\i kiddushei kessef} or {\i kiddushei shtar}, where {\i chuppah} is still required to reach {\i nisuin}. For {\i kiddushei biah}, {\i nisuin} would be accomplished immediately and automatically. The {\i braita} thus fits either position, and is not a decisive proof against {\i biah nisuin oseh}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Age of Three Braita\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Age Threshold\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rava} now brings another {\i braita}: {\i bat shalosh shanim veyom echad mitkadeshet beviah} \u8212- from the age of three years and one day, a girl\u8217's sexual act carries halachic consequence. As {\i Rashi} notes, below this age the act is treated as {\i kenoten etzba ba\u8217'ayin} \u8212- like putting a finger in the eye \u8212- meaning it does not register as a sexual act at all in halachic terms. The age of three is thus an arbitrary but fixed halachic threshold. It should be acknowledged that this topic intersects with serious questions regarding child abuse and how {\i halacha} addresses such violations from multiple angles \u8212- a point that is not uniquely a modern concern, as {\i Tosafot} itself engages with it here.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yibum Application\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i braita} continues: {\i ve\u8217'im ba aleha yavam kna\u8217'ah} \u8212- if her husband has died and she falls before the {\i yavam} (brother-in-law), the {\i yavam} can effect {\i yibbum} through sexual intercourse with her. Furthermore, {\i vachayavim aleha mishum eshet ish} \u8212- anyone who thereafter has relations with her violates the prohibition of {\i eshet ish}, adultery.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Bo\u8217'el Niddah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i braita} then states: {\i u\u8217'metama bo\u8217'alah letame mishkav tachton ke\u8217'elyon} \u8212- whoever has relations with her during a state of {\i niddah} is subject to the {\i tumah} of {\i bo\u8217'el niddah}. One might ask how a girl of three could be in a state of {\i niddah}; various hormonal conditions can produce vaginal bleeding even in very young girls, and the age of puberty has varied considerably across historical periods.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i halacha} of {\i tumah} transfer in this context deserves attention. The {\i Torah} prescribes not only a prohibition on having relations during {\i niddah}, but also a transfer of {\i tumah} from the {\i niddah} to the {\i bo\u8217'el}. A {\i niddah} who sits upon couches \u8212- even a great many in succession, as in the image of the Princess and the Pea \u8212- renders all of them {\i mishkav hazav} or {\i mishkav haniddah}, transferring {\i tumah} that can itself defile people and vessels, {\i letame adam vechelim}. The {\i bo\u8217'el niddah}, however, becomes an {\i av hatumah} through a different mechanism: if he sits on something, the item does not become {\i mishkav elyon} in the full expansive sense, but rather only {\i mishkav tachton} \u8212- transferred by contact rather than through the extended notion of sitting atop multiple layers. This is discussed at length throughout {\i Seder Taharot}. The key distinction is that {\i tumah} normally descends in level: an {\i av hatumah} touching something renders it {\i rishon letumah}, and so on downward. Here, however, there is something closer to a horizontal transfer: the {\i niddah} and the {\i bo\u8217'el niddah} both stand as {\i av hatumah}, but the scope of what each can subsequently defile differs \u8212- she can render things {\i av hatumah} through {\i mishkav}, while he can only produce {\i vlad hatumah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kohen Marriage\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i braita} continues: {\i ve\u8217'im nisset le\u8217'kohen tochal biterumah} \u8212- if she reaches {\i nisuin} with a {\i kohen}, she may eat {\i terumah}. And if a man who is an {\i ervah} to her has relations with her, {\i mumatim al yadah} \u8212- he is executed on her account \u8212- while {\i vehi petura}, she being a minor is exempt from any punishment. Furthermore, {\i im ba\u8217'ala echad min hapesulim, pesulah min hakehunah} \u8212- if she has relations with someone who disqualifies her from marrying a {\i kohen}, that disqualification takes effect; after the age of three, the act counts as a sexual act, so the status of {\i nivelah lapasul} applies and bars her from future marriage to a {\i kohen}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava\u8217's Two Stages\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Just as in {\i Abaye}\u8217's {\i braita}, {\i Rava}\u8217's {\i braita} here also appears to distinguish between the stage of {\i biah} and the subsequent stage of {\i nisseit} as two separate moments \u8212- {\i katani biah vekatani nisset}. This appears again to challenge the position that {\i biah nisuin oseh}. The Gemara deflects once more, however: the clause {\i nisseit le\u8217'kohen} need not be read as a distinct second stage separate from {\i biah}; it can be read as saying that if the {\i nisuin} accomplished through {\i biah} was to a {\i kohen}, then she may eat {\i terumah}. In other words, the {\i braita} can be harmonized with {\i biah nisuin oseh} by reading {\i nisseit} not as a temporally subsequent act but as a description of the character of the {\i nisuin} already effected. Both {\i Abaye} and {\i Rava} have brought {\i beraisos} that seem to distinguish between {\i biah} and {\i niset}, and the Gemara finds a way to deflect each challenge \u8212- evidently working to sustain the position of {\i biah nisuin oseh}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Bag-Bag Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Opening Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now introduces a {\i braita} of a different character: {\i uchvar shalach Yochanan ben Bag-Bag etzel Yehuda ben Beteira le-Nitzivin} \u8212- {\i Rabbi Yochanan ben Bag-Bag} sent a message to {\i Rabbi Yehuda ben Beteira} in {\i Nitzivin}. {\i Ben Bag-Bag} had heard that {\i Rabbi Yehuda ben Beteira} issued a ruling that {\i arusa bat Yisrael ochelet biterumah} \u8212- a {\i Yisrael} woman who has undergone {\i kiddushin} with a {\i kohen} may eat {\i terumah} already at the stage of {\i eirusin}. {\i Ben Bag-Bag} responds: I did not think you held that way. He then adds: {\i muchzakani becha she\u8217'ata baki bechadrei Torah lidrosh kal vachomer} \u8212- I considered you deeply learned in the inner chambers of Torah and capable of constructing a {\i kal vachomer}. This comment raises an interesting point: a {\i kal vachomer} is an argument of logic, yet {\i ben Bag-Bag} frames it as requiring fluency in {\i halacha}. The reason appears to be that one must know the full range of sources and parameters of the system to know where and how a {\i kal vachomer} may properly be applied.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kal Vachomer\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Yehuda ben Beteira}\u8217's {\i kal vachomer} runs as follows. A {\i shifcha kna\u8217'anit} \u8212- a Canaanite female slave \u8212- whose sexual intercourse with her master (which is itself prohibited) does not allow her to eat {\i terumah}, nevertheless may eat {\i terumah} by virtue of the monetary purchase ({\i kessef}) that acquired her. A married woman, whose {\i biah} does effect her entitlement to eat {\i terumah} \u8212- is it not logically compelling that her {\i kessef kiddushin} should also entitle her to eat {\i terumah}? The reasoning is: if {\i kessef} works for a {\i shifcha} even where {\i biah} does not, then certainly {\i kessef} should work for a wife where {\i biah} does. This is the {\i kal vachomer}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbinic Decree\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Yehuda ben Beteira} then anticipates the objection: {\i aval ma\u8217'ase, she\u8217'harei amru chachamim ein arusa bat Yisrael ochelet biterumah ad she\u8217'tikanes lechuppah} \u8212- but in practice, the rabbis ruled that a {\i bat Yisrael} who is {\i me\u8217'orasah} may not eat {\i terumah} until after {\i chuppah}. This is a {\i gezerah derabbanan}. The reason for this rabbinic decree, established a few {\i dafim} earlier, is the concern that during {\i eirusin}, when she still lives in her parents\u8217' home, she may share the {\i terumah} with her {\i Yisrael} family members, who would then come to eat {\i terumah} impermissibly. A question arises regarding a {\i bat kohen} marrying into a {\i kohen} family, where {\i terumah} is permitted regardless \u8212- whether the {\i gezerah} applies in such a case is worth consideration, though it is not resolved here.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Refutation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Yochanan ben Bag-Bag} then challenges the {\i kal vachomer} itself. He asks: {\i heichi dami} \u8212- what exactly is the case? If you are talking about {\i biah} accompanied by {\i chuppah} compared to {\i kessef} accompanied by {\i chuppah}, then of course she eats {\i terumah} in both cases because she is already {\i nesuah}. If you are talking about {\i biah} with {\i chuppah} versus {\i kessef} without {\i chuppah}, then you are comparing two stages of marriage against one \u8212- {\i hacha tarti vehacha chada} \u8212- and it is not surprising that they produce different outcomes. The {\i kal vachomer} lacks logical force because the two cases are not genuinely comparable.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Reformulated Kal\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara therefore reformulates {\i Rabbi Yehuda ben Beteira}\u8217's {\i kal vachomer}: the argument should be understood as comparing {\i biah} without {\i chuppah} to {\i kessef} without {\i chuppah}. This formulation is significant, because it represents a support for the position of {\i biah nisuin oseh}: if {\i biah} without {\i chuppah} is taken as clearly sufficient to allow {\i terumah} consumption \u8212- to the extent that it forms the stronger side of the {\i kal vachomer} \u8212- this only makes sense if {\i biah} accomplishes {\i nisuin} directly, rendering it simply stronger than {\i kessef}. But if {\i biah} only accomplishes {\i eirusin}, then both {\i kessef} and {\i biah} would be equivalent in effect, and one could not constitute a self-evident premise from which to derive the other.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rav Nachman bar Yitzchak} then offers an alternative reading: we may after all be speaking of {\i biah} accompanied by {\i chuppah} versus {\i kessef} without {\i chuppah}. The objection of {\i hacha tarti vehacha chada} can be answered by restructuring the {\i kal vachomer} as follows. A {\i shifcha kna\u8217'anit}, for whom {\i biah} \u8212- even with {\i chuppah} \u8212- does not allow her to eat {\i terumah}, nonetheless may eat {\i terumah} by virtue of {\i kessef} alone, without {\i chuppah}. A wife, for whom {\i biah} together with {\i chuppah} does work to allow her to eat {\i terumah} \u8212- should not {\i kessef} alone, without {\i chuppah}, also work? The asymmetry between {\i shifcha} and {\i wife} now drives the argument.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This reformulated {\i kal vachomer} is noted to rest on the assumption that the role of {\i kessef} in purchasing a slave and the role of {\i kessef kiddushin} in marriage are analogous. The Gemara implicitly highlights the limits of that analogy: the {\i kinyan} of acquiring a slave is a one-stage process, while marriage is a two-stage process, and therefore {\i kessef} in the two contexts plays fundamentally different roles.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The ultimate conclusion is that {\i Rabbi Yehuda ben Beteira}\u8217's {\i kal vachomer}, however reformulated, is met by the answer that the rabbis instituted a {\i gezerah} preventing a {\i me\u8217'orasah bat Yisrael} from eating {\i terumah} until {\i chuppah} \u8212- and the entire discussion ultimately serves to establish that there is no need to read {\i biah nisuin oseh} into the sources. The {\i kal vachomer} of {\i Rabbi Yehuda ben Beteira}, as understood through {\i Ravina}\u8217's reading, concerned not a {\i de\u8217'oraisa} question but a {\i derabbanan} one: {\i me\u8217'd\u8217'oraisa}, {\i mifshet peshita lei d\u8217'achla} \u8212- on the level of {\i de\u8217'oraisa}, it was obvious to {\i Ravina} that a {\i me\u8217'orasah} could eat {\i terumah}. The entire exchange between {\i ben Bag-Bag} and {\i Rabbi Yehuda ben Beteira} was therefore about the {\i gezerah} of the rabbis, specifically whether a {\i me\u8217'orasah} need not worry about {\i simpon}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Simpon Concern\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two Concerns\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now clarifies what {\i Ravina} understood to be the real content of {\i ben Bag-Bag}\u8217's message to {\i Rabbi Yehuda ben Beteira}. What {\i ben Bag-Bag} had actually heard was that {\i Rabbi Yehuda ben Beteira} rules that {\i arusa bat Yisrael ochelet biterumah velo chayshinan lesimpon} \u8212- that she may eat {\i terumah} and we do not worry about {\i simpon}. The question is accordingly not about the {\i de\u8217'oraisa} entitlement to eat {\i terumah}, which is uncontested, but about whether either of the two rabbinic concerns \u8212- sharing with family members, or {\i simpon} \u8212- should prevent her from eating {\i terumah} already at the {\i eirusin} stage.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Simpon}, as {\i Rashi} defines it \u8212- {\i kol davar hamevatel davar karui simpon} \u8212- refers to anything that would retroactively undermine a prior act. In context: {\i mishum simpon, shema yimatzei bah mum venimtzu kiddushei taut} \u8212- there is a concern that after the woman begins eating {\i terumah}, the husband may discover a {\i mum} (a physical defect) that he was unaware of, making the entire {\i kiddushin} a {\i kiddushei taut}, a mistaken betrothal. If the {\i kiddushin} is retroactively annulled, she will have been a {\i bat Yisrael} consuming {\i terumah} illegitimately. The {\i simpon} concern is therefore about the risk of retroactive undoing. It should be noted that the question of {\i kiddushei taut} based on discovered defects \u8212- particularly psychological and behavioral ones \u8212- sits at the very forefront of contemporary {\i agunah} discussions.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Yehuda ben Beteira}\u8217's reformulated {\i kal vachomer} \u8212- transmitted through {\i ben Bag-Bag}\u8217's message \u8212- now addresses both concerns simultaneously: by the {\i shifcha kna\u8217'anit}, {\i kessef} allows her to eat {\i terumah} and we do not worry about {\i simpon}. A fortiori, for a wife, where {\i biah} with {\i chuppah} works, should not {\i kessef kiddushin} also work without concern for {\i simpon}? And {\i Rabbi Yehuda ben Beteira} himself then concedes: despite the {\i kal vachomer}, in practice the rabbis ruled that she may not eat {\i terumah} until {\i chuppah} \u8212- {\i mishum de\u8217'Ulla u\u8217'ben Bag-Bag gabei shifcha kna\u8217'anit}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Slave Defects\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now addresses why the {\i simpon} concern does not apply to a slave transaction. The reason involves the nature of slave acquisition and the kinds of {\i mumim} that might be discovered. If the defect is visible \u8212- {\i mumim shebagaluy} \u8212- the master already saw it when he made the purchase: {\i haka chazi lei}. If it is a hidden defect on a covered part of the body, that too is not a problem in the context of acquiring a slave \u8212- {\i mai nafka lei minei, limalacha kaba\u8217'ei lei velo ichpat lei} \u8212- because the owner purchased the slave for labor, whether in the home or in the field, and a physical defect on a hidden part of the body is irrelevant to the slave\u8217's capacity to work.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Slave vs.\u160 ?Wife\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A further distinction: for a wife, the concern of {\i simpon} exists because marriage is a two-stage process in which the husband has not yet taken full possession of the relationship; the possibility of retroactive annulment is live. For a slave, by contrast, the acquisition is complete in one stage and there is no analogous opening for retroactive undoing based on hidden defects of a physical nature.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ganav Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara does introduce one category of slave defect that remains a concern: {\i nimtza ganav o kubyustos hegi\u8217'o} \u8212- if the slave turns out to be a thief or a {\i kubyustos} (a kidnapper or violent criminal), the purchase stands and the buyer must accept it. Why? {\i Eino din, listim mezuyan} \u8212- if he is an armed robber, {\i nichtav lamalchut, kol it leihu} \u8212- if he has been prosecuted by the government, his criminal record is already publicly known. This means the buyer had access to that information and cannot claim it was hidden.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This raises the question, however, about {\i petty} theft \u8212- a {\i ganav} in the simple sense of a kleptomaniac or ordinary thief. A behavioral or psychological defect of this kind is not obviously well-known, and the {\i braita}\u8217's formulation of {\i nimtza ganav} might encompass it. Indeed, {\i Rashi} in his explanation \u8212- {\i nimtza ganav eino batel bekach, deha kayma lan hamocher eved lachaveiro venimtza ganav o kubyustos higi\u8217'o} \u8212- ties this to the {\i braita}\u8217's ruling. The implication is that for a {\i ganav} in the more ordinary sense, there may still be an opening to treat such a behavioral defect as a {\i mum} that would warrant concern \u8212- one would need to thread the needle carefully with the {\i baraisa}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rambam-Ra\u8217'avad\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rambam Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Turning back to 10a, there is an important {\i machlokes} between the {\i Rambam} and the {\i Ra\u8217'avad} regarding the role of anal intercourse in the law of rape and the payment of {\i knas}. The {\i Tosafot Rid HaZaken} \u8212- which in {\i Maseches Kiddushin} is most likely the work of {\i Rav Avraham Min HaHar}, whose primary project in this tractate was defending the {\i Rambam} against his critics, a project characteristic of the Proven\u231 ?al {\i chachamim} \u8212- addresses this directly. In the narrow-lined commentary, it states: {\i dehaRambam katav bena\u8217'arah bivtulah perek alef she\u8217'eino meshalem knas ela kedarkah} \u8212- the {\i Rambam} rules in {\i Hilchos Na\u8217'arah Besulah} (chapter 1) that the {\i knas} of fifty {\i shekel} applies only to vaginal rape, not to anal rape.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ra\u8217'avad Objection\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Ra\u8217'avad} objects strenuously, arguing that this Gemara appears to fully equate anal and vaginal intercourse in the context of rape. The {\i Tosafot Rid HaZaken} attempts to explain the {\i Rambam}\u8217's position and reconcile it with the other {\i Gemaras} in {\i Ketubot}, but the difficulty is genuine. Underlying the {\i Rambam}\u8217's position is a conceptual question about what the {\i Torah} construes as the crime of rape \u8212- and in particular, the role that the woman\u8217's status as a {\i besulah} plays in that crime. Since only the husband makes her a {\i be\u8217'ulah} through anal intercourse, while any other man\u8217's anal act does not alter her {\i besulah} status at all, the {\i Rambam} may be reading the crime as essentially tied to the vaginal violation that transforms her {\i besulah} status. The {\i Tosafot Rid HaZaken} works through these considerations in the course of defending the {\i Rambam}\u8217's {\i shita} against the {\i Ra\u8217'avad}\u8217's challenge.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Kiddushin 11\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Terumah: Erusin/Kiddushi\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kohen\u8217's Wife Terumah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} on daf 11 opens in the midst of a discussion about whether the wife of a {\i kohen} eats {\i terumah} from the time of {\i erusin} or only from the time of {\i nisuin}. This question arose organically from the {\i gemara}\u8217's broader examination of the modes by which {\i kiddushin} is effected \u8212- {\i kesef}, {\i shtar}, and {\i bia}. The specific issue with {\i bia} is whether it creates {\i kiddushin} alone or whether, since the second stage of {\i nisuin} is when the couple actualizes their marriage and begins living together, an act of {\i bia} in the context of {\i kiddushin} automatically advances directly to {\i nisuin}. As a way of probing that question, the {\i gemara} turned to the case of a {\i kohen}\u8217's wife and the point at which she becomes entitled to eat {\i terumah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kinyan Kaspo vs.\u160 ?Beveitc\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Two distinct models emerge from the discussion. The first, evoked by the {\i braita} that the {\i gemara} cites in connection with Yochanan ben Bag Bag, frames {\i kiddushin} as analogous to the acquisition of a slave: just as a slave eats {\i terumah} by virtue of {\i kinyan kaspo} \u8212- the master\u8217's act of purchase \u8212- so too the {\i arusah} should eat {\i terumah} from the moment of {\i kiddushin}, since she has been, in a sense, acquired. The second model is grounded in the verse {\i kol tahor beveitcha yochal kodesh} \u8212- every pure member of your household may partake of holy things. Under this reading, the woman enters the man\u8217's household only at {\i nisuin}, when she leaves her father\u8217's house and moves into his. She would therefore have no biblical entitlement to {\i terumah} until {\i nisuin}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Bavli vs.\u160 ?Yerushalmi\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What appears on its surface to be a dispute among {\i Tannaim} about the biblical rule is reframed by the {\i Bavli}. The {\i Bavli} concludes that according to everyone, she is biblically entitled to {\i terumah} from the time of {\i kiddushin}. The only debate is about the {\i rabbinic} reason for deferring that entitlement until {\i nisuin}. The {\i Yerushalmi}, by contrast, genuinely embraces the position that even on a biblical level she does not eat {\i terumah} until {\i nisuin}, grounded precisely in the principle of {\i kol tahor beveitcha}. This is a striking divergence, and it reflects a deeper disagreement about the nature of {\i kiddushin} itself \u8212- the degree to which it functions as a {\i kinyan} and the degree to which {\i nisuin}, the entry into the household, is the true legal moment of marriage.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Simpon (Defect) Concern\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ben Bag Bag\u8217's Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Picking up at the bottom of 10b, the {\i gemara} frames its analysis through the exchange between Yochanan ben Bag Bag and Rabbi Yehuda ben Beteira. Ravina had already established that the biblical rule is settled \u8212- {\i mi-de-oraisa mipsha peshita lei de-achla}, everyone agrees she may eat {\i terumah} biblically from the time of {\i kiddushin} \u8212- and that the message Yochanan ben Bag Bag sent to Rabbi Yehuda ben Beteira concerned only the rabbinic dimension. The message reads: {\i shamati alecha she-atah omer arusah bat Yisrael lo achlah biterumah velo chayashta lesimpon} \u8212- I have heard that you say a betrothed Israelite woman does not eat {\i terumah}, and that you are not concerned about {\i simpon}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Simpon} refers to a defect discovered after the couple has moved to {\i nisuin}. The concern is that if at {\i nisuin}, once they become intimate, the husband discovers some physical characteristic he considers a substantive defect, he may void the {\i kiddushin} retroactively, rendering her act of eating {\i terumah} during {\i erusin} a violation. That is the rationale for waiting: we do not want her to eat {\i terumah} while there remains the possibility that the {\i kiddushin} will be found void. Yochanan ben Bag Bag reports that he has heard Rabbi Yehuda ben Beteira does not share this concern \u8212- that he permits her to eat from the time of {\i erusin}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kal Vachomer: Slave\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yehuda ben Beteira responds to Yochanan ben Bag Bag: {\i muchzani bach she-atah baki bechadrei torah lidrosh be-kal vachomer} \u8212- I have a tradition that you are an expert in the inner chambers of Torah and can reason through {\i kal vachomer}. Does the following not apply? By a Canaanite slave woman, {\i bia} with her does not enable her to eat {\i terumah} \u8212- she eats {\i terumah} solely on account of being purchased, {\i kinyan kaspo}. And yet we are not concerned that the master will discover a defect after purchasing her and retroactively invalidate the transaction. If so \u8212- {\i zu she-bi\u8217'atah makhilasah biterumah eino din she-kaspah makhilasah biterumah ve-lo nekhush le-simpon?} \u8212- should we not apply the same logic to the wife, who has even more modes of acquisition available to her?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i kal vachomer} is admittedly unusual in that the underlying issue is a rabbinic concern, which makes the logical leverage of a {\i kal vachomer} from a biblical case somewhat awkward. Nevertheless, the basic thrust is: if we are not worried about {\i simpon} in the slave case, why are we worried about it in the wife case?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Beteira\u8217's Response\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yehuda ben Beteira\u8217's answer is that he agrees in practice \u8212- an {\i arusah} does not eat {\i terumah} \u8212- but not for the reason of {\i simpon}. Rather: {\i aval mah e\u8217'eseh she-harei amru chakhamim arusah bat Yisrael einah ochelet biterumah ad she-tikanes lachuppah} \u8212- what can I do? The Sages ruled that a betrothed Israelite woman does not eat {\i terumah} until {\i chuppah}. And the reason, he explains, is not {\i simpon} but the reasoning of Ula.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ula\u8217's Reason\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Ula\u8217's concern is entirely different. If the {\i kohen} husband sends {\i terumah} to his {\i arusah} while she is still living in her father\u8217's house, she will share it with her father and brothers \u8212- non-{\i kohanim} who are forbidden to eat {\i terumah}. This, not the concern over a later-discovered defect, is the rabbinic reason for prohibiting her from eating {\i terumah} during {\i erusin}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} thus reframes the entire debate. Rather than a dispute over the biblical rule, we have a dispute between two {\i amoraim} \u8212- or, in the {\i gemara}\u8217's reconstruction, two rationales within a rabbinic framework \u8212- over why the rabbis required the woman to wait until {\i nisuin}: one opinion cites the fear of {\i simpon}, the other cites Ula\u8217's concern about her family members partaking of {\i terumah} in her father\u8217's house.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Wife vs.\u160 ?Slave Defect\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having established the two competing rabbinic rationales, the {\i gemara} asks why {\i simpon} should concern us more with regard to the wife than it does with regard to the slave, given that we clearly do not defer the slave\u8217's {\i terumah} eating on account of {\i simpon}. The answer unfolds in two steps.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Visible vs.\u160 ?Hidden Defec\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 First: {\i i mi-mah she-bi-galui ha kakhazi lei} \u8212- anything visible about the slave, the buyer could have seen at the time of purchase. What remains to discover? The slave market is buyer beware. Whatever was evident, the buyer either noticed and accepted or had the responsibility to notice. As for a hidden physical defect \u8212- {\i mum she-bi-seser} \u8212- the {\i gemara} asks: {\i mai nafka mina?} What difference does a hidden physical flaw make in a slave? {\i Bemlacha kabaie lei} \u8212- you buy a slave for labor, not for appearance. A hidden physical mark has no bearing on the purpose of the purchase and cannot be claimed as a substantive defect. By a wife, the calculus is different: once they are intimate, a physical characteristic that the husband finds objectionable could, within this framework, become the basis for a claim that the {\i kiddushin} was a {\i mekach ta\u8217'os}. The asymmetry is troubling, and it reflects a commodification model that has serious implications.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gannav, Kubyustos (Gambl\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What about character defects that could not have been observed at the slave market? The {\i gemara} raises the case of {\i nimtza gannav o kubyustos} \u8212- if the slave turned out to be a thief or a {\i kubyustos}. The {\i gemara} answers: {\i hige\u8217'o} \u8212- you got what you bargained for. Such qualities are to be expected among slaves, and the buyer cannot claim surprise.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then asks: what if he turns out to be a {\i listis mezuyan}, an armed robber, or one who has been condemned by the government \u8212- {\i nichtav lemalchus}? The answer is {\i kala isu lehu} \u8212- those facts would have been widely known. Rashi understands this to mean that if such were the case, the buyer knew and implicitly accepted; Tosafot reads it differently, arguing that we do not worry about {\i simpon} in the slave case as a general matter precisely because such extreme scenarios would always be publicly known and would never arise in a normal sale. If such a thing did somehow occur in secret, it might in principle invalidate the transaction, but we do not withhold {\i terumah} on account of such a remote possibility.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This discussion has a sobering practical dimension. The {\i gemara} here appears far more willing to entertain {\i kiddushei ta\u8217'us} \u8212- a fundamentally flawed betrothal \u8212- in cases involving the woman than in cases involving the man. If he turns out to be a pathological liar or to have concealed something monstrous about himself, most contemporary {\i poskim} resist applying the {\i kiddushei ta\u8217'us} framework to void the marriage. The {\i gemara}, by contrast, invokes defect discovery as a natural concern when it comes to the wife. This asymmetry flows directly from the model in which one party is the purchaser and the other is, in a sense, the object being acquired \u8212- and it has devastating consequences in cases of {\i agunah}, where the {\i bitul kiddushin} of {\i kiddushei ta\u8217'us} is invoked far more readily against women than men.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot\u8217's discussion of the word {\i kubyustos} is worth pausing on. Rashi explains it as a kidnapper \u8212- {\i gonev nefashot}. Tosafot objects, citing a {\i gemara} in the first chapter of {\i Bechorot} where someone asked Rabbi Yochanan ben Zakkai about a discrepancy in the numbers in the Torah concerning the collection of the {\i beka} per head. The non-Jew asked: Was Moshe Rabbenu a thief \u8212- {\i ganav haya} \u8212- pocketing the difference? Or was he a {\i kubyostos}? Or was he simply bad at arithmetic? Clearly {\i kubyostos} cannot mean a kidnapper in that context, since kidnapping has nothing to do with a financial shortfall. Rabbeinu Chananel therefore explains {\i kubyostos} as a gambler \u8212- {\i mesacheik b\u8217'kubia} \u8212- one who has accumulated debts and cannot account for the missing funds. This also illuminates the {\i midrash} in {\i Bereishit Rabbah} where Yaakov says to the angel: are you a thief, or a {\i kubyostos}, that you must flee before dawn? Under Rashi\u8217's reading he fears detection; under Rabbeinu Chananel\u8217's reading he is hiding from creditors.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Kiddushei Bia Nature\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Bia: Statement or Intent\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A question arises about the formal requirements of {\i kiddushei bia}. Does the man need to make a formal declaration \u8212- {\i harei at mekudeshet li b\u8217'bia zu} \u8212- or is it sufficient that the act be performed in a context of intent toward {\i kiddushin}, without a specific verbal formula? And does {\i kiddushei bia} require witnesses in the same way as {\i kesef} or {\i shtar}?\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Difference: Kibeil/Masar\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Practical Difference\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having established that all authorities agree the {\i arusah} does not eat {\i terumah} during {\i erusin}, the {\i gemara} asks: {\i mai beinaihu} \u8212- what practical difference does it make whether the reason is {\i simpon} or Ula\u8217's concern about her family? Either way she does not eat.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kibeil: Waiver\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The first case of {\i nafka mina} is {\i kibeil} \u8212- where the husband, from the outset, declares that he accepts her unconditionally regardless of any defect he might later discover. By explicitly waiving any future defect claims, he eliminates the concern of {\i simpon} entirely. In such a case, if the reason for the rabbinic prohibition is {\i simpon}, she could now eat {\i terumah} immediately upon {\i erusin}. Ula\u8217's concern about her family, however, would remain entirely in force.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Masar V\u8217'Halach\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The second case is {\i masar v\u8217'halach} \u8212- where the father has handed the girl over to agents of the husband, but she has not yet arrived at the husband\u8217's home. She is traveling, no longer in her father\u8217's house and no longer surrounded by her non-{\i kohen} family members. In this case, Ula\u8217's concern about her brothers and father sharing the {\i terumah} does not apply. But the concern of {\i simpon} remains, since the husband has not yet seen her and could still discover a defect upon {\i nisuin}. This is the practical divide between the two rationales.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Kiddushei Bia Nature (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosefta: L\u8217'Shem Kiddushin\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Returning to the question about the formal requirements of {\i kiddushei bia}, the {\i Tosefta} provides important clarification. For {\i kesef}, the {\i Tosefta} states that the man gives money or an object of monetary value and says {\i harei at mekudeshet li} or {\i harei at me\u8217'oreset li}. For {\i shtar}, similarly, he writes a document. For {\i bia}, however, the {\i Tosefta} does not prescribe a verbal formula. Instead it says: {\i kol bia she-hi leshem kiddushin mekudeshet, she-einah leshem kiddushin einah mekudeshet} \u8212- any act of {\i bia} performed in the context of {\i kiddushin} creates {\i kiddushin}; one not performed in that context does not.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Tosefta} is deliberately distinguishing between {\i harei at mekudeshet}, a formal performative declaration, and {\i leshem kiddushin}, a contextual intention. This suggests that {\i bia} operates differently from the other two modes. It is not a formal legal act in the same sense; rather, it is a {\i metziyus} \u8212- a lived reality. When a man and woman have relations in the context of a marital relationship, with the intent that those relations constitute marriage, the reality of marriage is thereby created. This is closer to what might be called a {\i de facto} or common-law marriage: the reality of living as husband and wife is what effects the union, not a discrete performative act.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Common Law Marriage\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This analysis carries enormous halachic consequences. If {\i kiddushei bia} is fundamentally about the reality of a couple living together and having relations in a marital context, then the question arises whether couples who live together without a formal {\i kiddushin} ceremony might nonetheless be halachically married. If the lived reality of cohabitation and intimate relations as husband and wife constitutes {\i kiddushin}, then such couples may require a {\i get} to separate. Furthermore, one might argue that the general awareness of their living arrangement functions as {\i anan sahedi} \u8212- the community effectively serves as witnesses to the ongoing reality of their relationship.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Henkin vs.\u160 ?Feinstein\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This question generated a major dispute between two towering {\i poskim}. Rav Yosef Eliyahu Henkin \u8212- the preeminent halachic authority in America before Rav Moshe Feinstein \u8212- essentially ruled that couples living together as husband and wife are bound by {\i kiddushei bia} and require a {\i get} to dissolve the relationship.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Moshe Feinstein strongly disagreed. His position is that {\i kiddushei bia}, like {\i kesef} and {\i shtar}, requires {\i kavannah} \u8212- the specific intent that this particular act of {\i bia} should effect {\i kiddushin}. Without that specific intent directed at the act itself, there is no {\i kiddushin}, regardless of how the couple is living. The {\i metziyus} of cohabitation, without the focused intent that a specific act of {\i bia} should create the marital bond, is insufficient. This position, whatever its merits as {\i lomdus}, has the practical advantage \u8212- and perhaps the practical motivation \u8212- of allowing Rav Moshe to address countless cases of problematic {\i kiddushin}, including many involving Conservative ceremonies with {\i pasul edim} or other technical deficiencies, without requiring a {\i get}. When there was no act specifically intended as a halachic {\i ma\u8217'aseh kiddushin}, there was no marriage, and the woman was free without a {\i get}. The human implications of Henkin\u8217's position, by contrast, would have been severe \u8212- binding many women who had never had any intention of contracting a halachic marriage.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Kesef Amount: Din\u1072 ?\u1088 ? vs.\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shammai vs.\u160 ?Hillel\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 With the discussion of {\i bia} and {\i terumah} concluded, the {\i gemara} returns to the topic of {\i kesef} and the opening disagreement of the {\i mishnah}: Beit Shammai requires a {\i dinar} for {\i kiddushin} by money, while Beit Hillel requires only a {\i perutah}. The immediate question is why Beit Shammai insists on a {\i dinar} specifically. If {\i kesef} for {\i kiddushin} is essentially symbolic \u8212- it is the act of transfer that matters, not the monetary value \u8212- why does the amount matter at all, and why a {\i dinar} in particular?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Coinage Chart\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 To appreciate the stakes of this debate, it is worth mapping the relative values of the coins in question. The smallest unit is the {\i perutah}, a copper coin. Above it are the {\i issar}, the {\i pundion}, the {\i ma\u8217'ah} (also called {\i gerah}), and the {\i istira} (sometimes called {\i tarapic}). The {\i dinar}, which is the same as a {\i zuz}, is worth approximately 192 {\i perutot}. Above the {\i dinar} are the {\i shekel} and the {\i sela}, which is also called the {\i shekel hakodesh} in the Torah \u8212- worth four {\i zuzim}, or 768 {\i perutot}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This last equivalence is crucial for understanding several other halachic amounts. The fifty {\i shekel} that the Torah prescribes as the {\i mohar habesulot} \u8212- the bride price for virgins, in the cases of rape and seduction \u8212- equals two hundred {\i zuzim}, which is precisely the amount of a {\i ketubah} for a virgin. This correspondence is not incidental. In any case, the gap between Beit Shammai and Beit Hillel is dramatic: a {\i dinar} is approximately two hundred times the value of a {\i perutah}. If a {\i perutah} is a penny, a {\i dinar} is roughly two dollars in symbolic terms \u8212- still modest in absolute value, but representing an entirely different order of magnitude.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Makpedet Al Atzma\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} cites Rabbi Zeira\u8217's explanation of Beit Shammai\u8217's position: {\i sheken isha makpedes al atzma ve-ein miskadeshes b\u8217'pachos midinar} \u8212- a woman has self-respect and will not consent to be betrothed for less than a {\i dinar}. The rationale is that Beit Shammai\u8217's minimum is grounded in a woman\u8217's dignity: even if the {\i kesef} of {\i kiddushin} is in some sense symbolic, there is a minimum below which the amount becomes offensive and demeaning.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might try to reconcile this with the view that {\i kiddushin} is purely symbolic by saying: even symbolic acts require a minimum threshold of seriousness. A gift that is too trivial signals contempt rather than commitment, and even if the legal act would technically be complete, the framework requires that the symbolic gesture carry some minimum weight. However, the difficulty with Rabbi Zeira\u8217's rationale is that it is inherently subjective. Dignity means different things to different people, and a minimum rooted in personal {\i hakpada} \u8212- individual insistence and sensitivity \u8212- does not yield an objective legal standard.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Subjective vs.\u160 ?Objective\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Alas Ma\u8217'ata\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This objection is exactly what the {\i gemara} raises. If the rule is based on a woman\u8217's sense of dignity and self-respect, then the daughters of Rabbi Yannai \u8212- a wealthy family \u8212- would be {\i makpid} on far more than a {\i dinar} and would not consent to {\i kiddushin} for anything less than a basketful of gold coins. Would we say that a {\i dinar} is insufficient for them? And conversely, if a woman actively extends her hand and accepts a {\i zuz} \u8212- {\i pashta yada ve-kibla chad zuza me-acher} \u8212- surely the {\i kiddushin} takes effect even for a wealthy woman who normally would demand more. The {\i gemara} answers: {\i pashta yada ve-kibla lo kamina} \u8212- that case, where she consciously and willingly accepted, is not the one under discussion.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Daughters of Rabbi Yannai\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} clarifies the scope of Beit Shammai\u8217's ruling: it applies specifically when she does not know what is being given \u8212- {\i de-kadsha beleilya}, if the betrothal took place at night and she could not count the money, or {\i de-shavsha shaliach}, if she had appointed an agent to receive the {\i kiddushin} on her behalf. In those cases, since she cannot assess what she is receiving, we invoke a presumed universal minimum standard of {\i hakpada}. If she consciously accepts anything \u8212- even a {\i perutah} \u8212- then even Beit Shammai concedes the {\i kiddushin} takes effect.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This resolution works, but it is intellectually unsatisfying. The rule is ultimately grounded in a psychological presumption rather than an objective legal definition. The {\i perutah} of Beit Hillel is a clean, objective floor \u8212- the minimum that the halachic system recognizes as {\i money}. Beit Shammai\u8217's {\i dinar} is a social norm, a presumed minimum of female self-respect, which varies by individual and requires qualification the moment the woman acts contrary to it. The {\i gemara} is aware of this tension and will seek a more principled explanation for Beit Shammai\u8217's position.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Kesef Tzuri vs.\u160 ?Medina\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two Coinage Systems\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Yosef introduces a different explanation, citing a rule in the name of Rav Yehuda citing Rav Assi: {\i kol kesef ha-amur ba-Torah kesef Tzuri, u-veshel divreihem kesef medinah} \u8212- whenever the Torah mentions {\i kesef}, it refers to Tyrian silver coinage ({\i kesef Tzuri}); when the Rabbis fix a monetary obligation, they use the local coinage ({\i kesef Medina}).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The significance of this distinction can hardly be overstated. The two coinage systems share the same names \u8212- the coins are called by the same terms \u8212- but {\i kesef Medina} is worth only one-eighth of {\i kesef Tzuri}. This is not a minor calibration; it is an eightfold difference in value. Two coinage systems run in parallel, and a legal obligation expressed in terms of a {\i sela} or {\i zuz} means something entirely different depending on whether the source of that obligation is biblical or rabbinic.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 1:8 Ratio\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The practical impact is immediate. The {\i gemara} on {\i kiddushin} uses the {\i gezeirah shavah} of {\i kicha-kicha} \u8212- the word \u8220"taking\u8221" in {\i ki yikach ish ishah} is compared to \u8220"taking\u8221" in the purchase of {\i Sdeh Ephron} \u8212- to establish that {\i kesef} is required. But if the Torah mandates {\i kesef} and means {\i kesef Tzuri}, then the smallest biblical silver coin becomes the relevant threshold, not a copper {\i perutah}. This would explain Beit Shammai.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ketubah Application\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The most consequential application of this principle is to the {\i ketubah}. The {\i ketubah} of a virgin is set at two hundred {\i zuz}. But are we talking about two hundred {\i zuz} of {\i kesef Tzuri} or two hundred {\i zuz} of {\i kesef Medina}? This depends entirely on whether the {\i ketubah} obligation is {\i de-oraisa} or {\i de-rabbanan} \u8212- a dispute with profound financial implications, since the two amounts differ by a factor of eight. Those who rule that {\i ketubah} is rabbinic in origin hold that the two hundred {\i zuz} refers to {\i zuz medinah}, worth only an eighth of {\i zuz Tzuri}, making the actual obligation only twenty-five of the larger {\i zuz}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Kesef Katzuv vs.\u160 ?Generic\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Assi\u8217's Chiddush\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 After this extended analysis, the {\i gemara} steps back to reconsider the full scope of Rav Assi\u8217's rule. The initial presentation \u8212- that any {\i kesef} mentioned in the Torah means {\i kesef Tzuri} \u8212- leads immediately to a contradiction: {\i kiddushin} is effected by {\i kesef}, which is a Torah concept, yet Beit Hillel allows a {\i perutah}, which is copper and not silver. And the redemption of {\i hekdesh} and {\i ma\u8217'aser sheni}, both of which invoke {\i kesef}, are permitted even with a {\i perutah} under certain circumstances.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} therefore reformulates the rule. Rav Assi did not say that all {\i kesef} in the Torah means silver. Rather: {\i kol kesef katzuv ha-amur ba-Torah kesef Tzuri} \u8212- whenever the Torah specifies a fixed, precise amount, that amount is denominated in {\i kesef Tzuri}. When the Torah speaks about money generically \u8212- without specifying a particular sum \u8212- any money suffices, including copper. The distinction is between {\i kesef katzuv}, a prescribed number, and generic {\i kesef}, an unquantified obligation. Where the Torah gives you a number, the coin being referenced is the high-value silver {\i Tzuri} coinage; where it simply says \u8220"money,\u8221" a {\i perutah} will do.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbinic Fixed Amounts\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The second part of Rav Assi\u8217's rule \u8212- that rabbinic fixed amounts use {\i kesef Medina} \u8212- was his genuine {\i chiddush}, since the first part is already taught in the {\i Mishnah}. A {\i mishnah} in the same context already lists the cases: the five {\i sela} for {\i pidyon haben}, the thirty {\i shekel} for a gored slave, the fifty for rape and seduction, the hundred for the slanderer of a virgin \u8212- {\i kulam be-shekel hakodesh be-maneh Tzuri}, all of them are in {\i shekel hakodesh}, which is {\i Tzuri} coinage. That much is explicit and not Rav Assi\u8217's innovation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What Rav Assi adds is that {\i rabbinic} fixed amounts \u8212- even when expressed in the same coinage terminology \u8212- refer to {\i kesef Medina}, and {\i kesef Medina} is worth only one-eighth of {\i kesef Tzuri}. The {\i baraita} illustrates: {\i ha-toke\u8217'a le-chaveiro notein lo sela} \u8212- one who strikes his fellow in a certain manner pays a {\i sela}. One might assume this is a {\i sela} of {\i kesef Tzuri}, worth four {\i zuzim}. Rav Assi teaches that since this is a rabbinic penalty, the {\i sela} here is {\i kesef Medina}, and the {\i sela medinah} is worth only an {\i istira} \u8212- half a {\i zuz}, or one-eighth of the {\i Tzuri sela}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sela Medina: Istira\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} confirms this: {\i ve-lo teima mai sela arba\u8217'ah zuzay} \u8212- do not suppose the {\i sela} here is worth four {\i zuzim}. Rather, {\i ela mai sela palga de-zuzay} \u8212- the {\i sela} here is worth half a {\i zuz}. {\i De-avdi inshei tarti le-palga de-zuzay istira} \u8212- people call a half-{\i zuz} an {\i istira}, and the name {\i istira} is linguistically connected to {\i sela}. So the {\i sela medinah} is the coin called {\i istira}, which is one-eighth the value of the {\i sela Tzuri}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The application to {\i ketubah} follows directly. Those who rule that {\i ketubah} is {\i de-rabbanan} hold that the two hundred {\i zuz} of the {\i ketubah} refers to {\i zuz medinah}, each worth one-eighth of a {\i zuz Tzuri}, bringing the total real value down to twenty-five {\i zuz Tzuri}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Slave Model: Din\u1072 ?\u1088 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Slave Girl Model\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having shown that Rav Assi\u8217's rule \u8212- as now reformulated \u8212- applies only to {\i kesef katzuv}, fixed amounts, and that {\i kiddushin kesef} is mentioned generically in the Torah without a fixed sum, the {\i gemara} can no longer use Rav Assi to explain Beit Shammai. A generic mention of {\i kesef} in the Torah is satisfied by a {\i perutah}, and Beit Shammai therefore needs a different rationale. Rebbi Shimon ben Lakish provides one, grounding it in the parallel between {\i kiddushin} and the sale of an {\i amah ivrit} \u8212- a Hebrew maidservant.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Pidyon Logic\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Torah states regarding the {\i amah ivrit}: {\i ve-hofdah} \u8212- and she shall be redeemed. From this the rabbis derive that she can reduce her purchase price proportionally based on the years of service completed \u8212- {\i migra\u8217'at u-mazla}, the price is discounted downward. She works off her debt year by year, and what remains of the original purchase price is what she owes for her redemption.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The critical implication is this: if she was sold for only a {\i perutah}, {\i mi-perutah mi-migra\u8217'at} \u8212- you cannot reduce a {\i perutah} further. There would be nothing to discount; the redemption mechanism would be meaningless. The fact that the Torah prescribes a redemption process that involves progressive reduction presupposes that the original sale price was substantial enough to permit such reduction. The price must therefore have been more than a {\i perutah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Presumed Amount\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The argument then proceeds: since we know that an {\i amah ivrit} is sold for a substantial amount \u8212- certainly more than a {\i perutah} \u8212- we presume the standard sale price is at least a {\i dinar}, a meaningful sum. And since {\i kiddushin} is modeled after the acquisition of an {\i amah ivrit} \u8212- the very analogy that structures much of this sugya \u8212- Beit Shammai requires that the same minimum apply. Once the {\i amah ivrit} model establishes that the acquisition must be for a significant amount, the amount presumed for {\i kiddushin} is likewise a {\i dinar}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This argument still has a gap: demonstrating that the price must exceed a {\i perutah} does not by itself establish that the minimum is a {\i dinar} rather than, say, two {\i perutot} or a {\i ma\u8217'ah}. The full resolution of this point \u8212- how we move from \u8220"more than a {\i perutah}\u8221" to \u8220"a {\i dinar}\u8221" specifically \u8212- will be taken up in the following session.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Kiddushin 12\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Amah Ivriyah & Proratio\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Reish Lakish\u8217's Model\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The daf opens by returning to the question that has occupied the preceding discussion: what is the basis for Beis Shammai\u8217's requirement that {\i kiddushin} be effected with no less than a {\i dinar}? The Gemara has already considered and set aside two earlier explanations \u8212- one grounded in the presumption ({\i ada\u8217'ata}) that women would not consent to {\i kiddushin} for less than a {\i dinar}, and another rooted in the association of {\i kesef} in the Torah with {\i kesef tzuri}, standard silver coinage. That second explanation was shown to be insufficient: when the Torah uses {\i kesef} generically, without specifying an amount, it does not mandate a particular denomination and could in principle encompass even a {\i perutah}. The rule that {\i kesef} implies {\i kesef tzuri} \u8212- a specific, weighty coinage \u8212- applies only where the Torah specifies a precise sum, such as thirty or fifty shekels. With those explanations behind us, the Gemara turns to a third and more analytically compelling account.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Reish Lakish cites a teaching in the name of Chizkiyah. The verse {\i ve-hefdah} \u8212- \u8220"and she shall be redeemed\u8221" \u8212- written in the context of the {\i amah ivriyah}, the Hebrew maidservant sold by her father, teaches that the maidservant may redeem herself through a pro-rated reduction of her purchase price: she pays back only the portion corresponding to the years of service that remain, having already worked off the rest. The Gemara has elsewhere used the {\i amah ivriyah} as the model from which the laws of the father\u8217's right to receive {\i kiddushin} money on behalf of his daughter are derived, so the two institutions are linked by analogy.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Proratio Challenge\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The implication of this teaching is immediate and consequential. If the redemption of an {\i amah ivriyah} requires the ability to pro-rate the original purchase price, then that purchase price must have been large enough to permit pro-rating in the first place. If the father sold her for a {\i dinar} \u8212- close to 192 {\i perutot} \u8212- then one can indeed reduce that sum incrementally as she works off portions of her term. But if the father had sold her for only a single {\i perutah}, there is nothing to reduce: {\i miprutah mee megara} \u8212- you cannot subtract from a {\i perutah}. Therefore the original sale price must have been more than a {\i perutah}, and by analogy, so must the {\i kesef kiddushin}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara raises an objection. Perhaps the Torah\u8217's instruction regarding pro-rating is simply conditional: where a {\i dinar} was paid, pro-rating is available and may proceed down to a {\i perutah} minimum; but where only a {\i perutah} was paid, no pro-rating applies and the sale remains valid as a simple transaction with no reduction option. On this reading, the institution of the {\i amah ivriyah} itself would not require that a minimum above a {\i perutah} be paid \u8212- it would only mean that in cases where more was paid, the pro-rating mechanism is available.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yi\u8217'ud Analogy\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara responds by invoking a parallel principle drawn from the laws of {\i yi\u8217'ud} \u8212- the right of the master or his son to designate the {\i amah ivriyah} as a wife. This designation is not mandatory; the master may choose not to exercise it. One might therefore argue that if {\i yi\u8217'ud} is merely optional, a sale should be valid even in cases where {\i yi\u8217'ud} is structurally impossible \u8212- for instance, where the buyer is related to the girl in a degree that would prohibit marriage, or where he has already married her sister. Yet the law is otherwise: {\i kol heicha de-la matzi miyad, lav zvini zvini} \u8212- wherever {\i yi\u8217'ud} cannot take place at all, the sale itself is invalid. The entire institution of selling a daughter as an {\i amah ivriyah} was permitted by the Torah only because the hope and expectation of {\i yi\u8217'ud} is inherent to it. If {\i yi\u8217'ud} is off the table entirely, the sale is void. Here there is a meaningful difference, one worth noting: the argument for {\i yi\u8217'ud} rests on the idea that the entire rationale for permitting the sale was the prospect of marriage, so without that prospect the sale lacks its foundational justification. The argument for {\i megara} \u8212- pro-rating \u8212- is less obviously essential to the institution. Why should the absence of an ability to reduce the price retroactively invalidate a sale? The Gemara is nonetheless drawing the comparison, asserting {\i hacha nami}: just as {\i yi\u8217'ud} must be possible for the sale to be valid, so too the pro-rating option must be structurally available. Since a {\i perutah} cannot be reduced, a sale for a {\i perutah} cannot support the pro-rating mechanism, and therefore is not a valid sale.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Perutah to Dinar\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 From this the Gemara concludes that the purchase price of an {\i amah ivriyah} must exceed a {\i perutah}, and by analogy, so must the {\i kesef kiddushin} for a woman. And by the same analogy, Beis Hillel\u8217's position that a {\i perutah} suffices for {\i kiddushin} is derived by its own logic, while Beis Shammai, learning from the {\i amah ivriyah}, requires an amount capable of pro-rating.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Why Dinar Not Perutah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Intermediate Coins\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara immediately presses a further question. Even granting that the minimum must exceed a {\i perutah}, why does Beis Shammai land on a {\i dinar} specifically? Between a {\i perutah} and a {\i dinar} there is an enormous range of values \u8212- why not half a {\i dinar}, or two {\i perutot}, or any of the many intermediate denominations?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Next Level Coinage\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara\u8217's answer is terse but conceptually significant: {\i keivan de-afiktaih mi-perutah, okmah a-dinar} \u8212- once we have established that a {\i perutah} is insufficient, we move directly to the next serious level of coinage, which is the {\i dinar}. The analogy offered is an informal one: if you rule out a penny, you do not land on a nickel or a dime \u8212- you go to a dollar. There is a qualitative leap from the smallest denomination to the next major coinage level. A {\i ma\u8217'ah} or a half-{\i dinar} might seem like natural stopping points, yet the Gemara considers the {\i dinar} to be the next genuinely substantial coin once the {\i perutah} is ruled out. This may be unsatisfying in its precision, but that is the Gemara\u8217's position.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava: Women\u8217's Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava offers an entirely independent rationale for Beis Shammai\u8217's {\i dinar} requirement. {\i She-lo yehu benot Yisrael ke-hefker} \u8212- so that Jewish women should not be treated as ownerless property, as something of negligible worth. If a woman could be acquired in marriage for a {\i perutah} \u8212- a coin of minimal value \u8212- it sends a societal message of degradation. The institution of marriage carries with it a statement about the dignity of the person entering it. A {\i perutah} is too small a sum to convey that dignity; it symbolizes something cheap. A {\i dinar}, by contrast, carries weight as a serious monetary unit.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 There is an irony worth pointing out. The Gemara elsewhere grounds the entire institution of {\i kiddushin} by analogy in the sale of a daughter as a maidservant \u8212- a framework that treats women in explicitly transactional, almost commercial terms. Rava\u8217's reasoning here pulls in precisely the opposite direction, insisting on a minimum that protects women\u8217's social standing and dignity. Both are operative; they simply reflect different layers of the legal and moral reasoning at work.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rashi: Rabbinic Enactment\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rashi\u8217's commentary makes an important clarification about the status of Rava\u8217's reasoning. Rashi, in explaining the phrase {\i ke-hefker}, states explicitly that this is a {\i derabbanan} enactment: {\i min haTorah} a {\i perutah} should technically suffice, but the Rabbis enacted that anything less than a {\i dinar} would not take effect \u8212- {\i afkinu rabbanan lekiddushin minei} \u8212- they uprooted the {\i kiddushin} retroactively. This is a significant ruling. Rava\u8217's reasoning, in Rashi\u8217's reading, is not about deriving a {\i d\u8217'oraita} minimum from a biblical source; rather, it is a rabbinic protection layered over the Torah\u8217's framework, motivated by the concern for women\u8217's dignity in society.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Beis Hillel: Perutah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Beis Hillel Intro\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now turns to Beis Hillel, who holds that {\i kiddushin} is effected with a {\i perutah} or the equivalent value of a {\i perutah} in goods. This raises the question of what exactly a {\i perutah} is and whether its value is fixed or variable.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Fixed vs.\u160 ?Relative Value\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Yosef suggests that when we say a {\i perutah}, we mean {\i kol shehu} \u8212- whatever constitutes the minimal coinage in a given society at a given time. The value of a {\i perutah}, on this view, is not fixed in absolute terms but is relative to the monetary norms of the era and place.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye challenges this immediately. The Mishnah goes out of its way to define a {\i perutah} precisely as one eighth of an {\i issar ha\u8217'italki} \u8212- one eighth of an Italian {\i issar}. If the {\i perutah} were simply whatever counts as minimum coinage in any given context, why would the Mishnah specify this concrete ratio? One might respond that the Mishnah was simply describing the situation as it obtained in its own time \u8212- in the days of Moshe the minimum coinage may have been something else entirely, and in each generation the minimum coinage of that generation would apply. On this reading the Mishnah is not locking in a fixed definition; it is simply illustrating the concept for its era.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara, however, rejects this flexibility. When Rav Dimi came from Eretz Yisrael, he reported that Rabbi Simai in his generation had assessed the value of a {\i perutah} using precisely the standard of the Mishnah \u8212- one eighth of an Italian {\i issar}. Rashi explains the context: a man had betrothed a woman with a {\i perutah}, and Rabbi Simai needed to determine whether the {\i kiddushin} was valid. He did so by checking whether the {\i perutah} in question was still worth one eighth of an Italian {\i issar} by the standards of his time. The Mishnah\u8217's definition was being used as an objective and continuing benchmark, not merely as a historical description.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 When Ravin came from Eretz Yisrael, he reported a related assessment by Rabbi Dostai, Rabbi Yannai, and Rabbi Oshaya: they assessed a {\i perutah} as one sixth of an Italian {\i issar}. This seems to contradict the Mishnah\u8217's figure of one eighth. But the critical point for this discussion is that these {\i Amoraim} too were using an objective, externally fixed standard \u8212- not a shifting social convention. Whether one eighth or one sixth is the correct figure, the {\i perutah} has a determinate value that does not simply track whatever happens to be the smallest coin in circulation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sifra Calculation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Yosef now presses back. If the {\i perutah} has a stable and fixed value, how does one account for a passage in the {\i Sifra}? The {\i Sifra} discusses the case of one who benefits from {\i hekdesh} \u8212- consecrated property \u8212- and thereby incurs the obligation of {\i me\u8217'ilah} and must bring an {\i asham}. The Torah requires that the {\i asham} be worth two {\i shekalim} \u8212- that is, two {\i sela\u8217'im}. The {\i Sifra} offers a striking moral observation: one who derived even a {\i perutah}\u8217's worth of benefit from {\i hekdesh} must bring a {\i korban} worth over 2,000 {\i perutot} \u8212- underscoring the severity of the transgression and the disproportionate weight of atonement required. The {\i Sifra} says: {\i tse vachashov kama prutos beshnei sla\u8217'im} \u8212- go and calculate how many {\i perutot} are in two {\i sela\u8217'im}. And the {\i Sifra} states that this number exceeds 2,000.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The difficulty is arithmetical. A {\i sela} is a {\i shekel}, a {\i shekel} is two {\i zuz} (i.e., two {\i dinarim}), a {\i dinar} is 192 {\i perutot}, and therefore two {\i sela\u8217'im} equals four {\i dinarim} equals 768 {\i perutot} \u8212- wait, let us be more careful. A {\i dinar} is 192 {\i perutot}; two {\i sela\u8217'im} is four {\i dinarim}, giving 768 {\i perutot} \u8212- no, that is also not right. Working from the breakdown given in the Gemara itself: a {\i dinar} is six {\i ma\u8217'ot}, a {\i ma\u8217'ah} is two {\i pundyon}, a {\i pundyon} is two {\i issarin}, an {\i issar} is two {\i musmasin}, a {\i musmas} is two {\i kuntrakin}, a {\i kuntrak} is two {\i perutot} \u8212- giving 192 {\i perutot} per {\i dinar}. Two {\i sela\u8217'im} is four {\i dinarim}, which is 768 {\i perutot} \u8212- yet this is even further from 2,000. Even taking two {\i sela\u8217'im} as two {\i dinarim} (if {\i shekel} here means {\i dinar}), one gets 384, still far short. Let us take the numbers the Gemara actually works with: two {\i shekalim} at the rate of 768 {\i perutot} per {\i shekel} gives approximately 1,536. The {\i Sifra} calls this \u8220"more than 2,000,\u8221" which Rav Yosef argues implies that a {\i perutah} in the {\i Sifra}\u8217's reckoning was worth less than the one-eighth-of-an-{\i issar} standard would dictate. If {\i perutot} were smaller units than the Mishnah assumes, the count would rise, and could exceed 2,000.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara reports that a certain elder ({\i hahu saba}) had a different text of the {\i Braisa}: not {\i yoter me-alpayim} \u8212- \u8220"more than 2,000\u8221" \u8212- but {\i karov le-alpayim} \u8212- \u8220"close to 2,000.\u8221" The Gemara asks, even so, how is 1,536 close to 2,000? The answer: {\i keivan de-nafku mi-palga} \u8212- since 1,536 has already passed 1,500, it is rounded to the nearest thousand, making it \u8220"close to 2,000.\u8221" That is, of the two thousands that bracket it \u8212- 1,000 and 2,000 \u8212- it is closer to 2,000. With the correct {\i girsa} of the {\i Braisa} in hand, the apparent challenge dissolves. The {\i Sifra} does not require a {\i perutah} of lesser value than the Mishnah\u8217's definition. The value of the {\i perutah} is indeed stable and consistent.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Perutah-Dinar Ratio\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having established that the {\i perutah} has a fixed value, the Gemara returns to the apparent contradiction between Rav Dimi\u8217's report (one eighth of an {\i issar}) and Ravin\u8217's report (one sixth of an {\i issar}). Abaye asks Rav Dimi whether their dispute mirrors the {\i Tannaitic} debate between the {\i Tanna Kamma} \u8212- who defines a {\i perutah} as one eighth of an Italian {\i issar}, deriving this from the chain of subdivisions in the Mishnah: six {\i ma\u8217'ot} per {\i dinar}, two {\i pundyon} per {\i ma\u8217'ah}, two {\i issarin} per {\i pundyon}, yielding 24 {\i issarin} per {\i dinar} and therefore 192 {\i perutot} per {\i dinar} and 8 {\i perutot} per {\i issar} \u8212- and Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel, who uses a different set of denominations. Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel\u8217's coinage system yields 144 {\i perutot} per {\i dinar}: six {\i ma\u8217'ot} per {\i dinar}, three {\i hadrasin} per {\i ma\u8217'ah} giving 18, two {\i hanatzim} per {\i hadras} giving 36, two {\i shemanim} per {\i hanatz} giving 72, and two {\i perutot} per {\i shaman} giving 144. If one assumes the same 24-{\i issar}-per-{\i dinar} ratio, 144 divided by 24 gives 6 {\i perutot} per {\i issar} \u8212- i.e., one sixth.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Dimi responds that in fact both he and Ravin agree with the {\i Tanna Kamma}: the ratio of {\i perutah} to {\i dinar} is fixed at 192 to 1. What fluctuates is not the {\i perutah}-to-{\i dinar} ratio but the {\i issar}-to-{\i dinar} ratio, because the value of the {\i issar} was not stable and changed over time. When the {\i issar} was of higher value \u8212- {\i de-ikur issarei} \u8212- there were 24 {\i issarin} to a {\i dinar}, producing a {\i perutah}-to-{\i issar} ratio of 1 to 8. When the {\i issar} was devalued \u8212- {\i de-zul issarei} \u8212- there were 32 {\i issarin} to a {\i dinar}, producing a {\i perutah}-to-{\i issar} ratio of 1 to 6. The {\i perutah} itself never changed in value relative to the {\i dinar}; only the {\i issar} fluctuated.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The practical upshot is significant. The {\i perutah} and the {\i dinar} are the stable anchors of the system. The {\i issar} is an intermediate unit whose purchasing power was historically variable \u8212- the {\i issar} was indeed known to fluctuate radically in value across the Roman period. When someone in one generation describes a {\i perutah} as one eighth of an {\i issar} and someone in another generation describes it as one sixth, they are not disagreeing about the value of a {\i perutah}; they are reflecting different economic realities of the {\i issar} in their respective eras. Both are measuring the {\i perutah} against a {\i dinar}, and both arrive at the same underlying value.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This has broad application. Whenever the {\i halacha} invokes a {\i perutah} \u8212- whether for {\i kiddushin}, for {\i me\u8217'ilah}, for {\i pidyon ha-ben}, or for anything else \u8212- we are not speaking of whatever happens to be the smallest coin in circulation at a given time and place. We mean an objective, stable monetary unit defined as 1/192nd of a {\i dinar}. In contemporary terms, with a {\i dinar} (i.e., a {\i zuz}) valued at approximately $3 based on its precious metal weight in silver, a {\i perutah} works out to roughly two cents. This is the precious-metal-based valuation, which is the standard the {\i halacha} employs \u8212- not a purchasing-power equivalence, which would be far harder to calculate consistently across time.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A brief but illuminating digression: the {\i ketubah} is denominated in 200 {\i zuz}, which once represented approximately a year or two of living expenses. By the Middle Ages, devaluation had rendered 200 {\i zuz} nearly worthless in terms of actual purchasing power, leaving women without meaningful financial protection upon divorce. The {\i Takanah} was therefore enacted to denominate the {\i ketubah} in {\i zekukim} \u8212- 100 {\i zekukim} for the {\i mohar} and 100 {\i zekukim} for the {\i tosefet} \u8212- a {\i zekuk} being a far larger weight of silver than a {\i zuz}. Today 200 {\i zekukim} corresponds to approximately $27,000 in silver value. The {\i zuz}, meanwhile, is now worth approximately $3 in silver, and 200 {\i zuz} correspondingly about $600. The point is that the system is anchored in precious metal weight, not in buying power, and the historical trajectory of coinage devaluation is precisely why the {\i ketubah} denomination had to be reformulated.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Objects of Uncertain Val\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shmuel: Madai Concern\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now introduces a related but distinct problem: what is the {\i halacha} when {\i kiddushin} is effected not with money but with an object of uncertain value? Shmuel states: {\i kiddasha bitmara} \u8212- if a man betrothed a woman with a date \u8212- {\i afilu omid kor tmarin bedinar} \u8212- even if an entire {\i kor} of dates is currently selling for a {\i dinar}, which would imply that a single date is worth far less than a {\i perutah} \u8212- {\i chashinan shema shaveh prutah bemadai} \u8212- we are nonetheless concerned, lest this particular date be worth a {\i perutah} somewhere far away, in a place like {\i Madai}. Shmuel\u8217's position is striking: even an object whose local market value is clearly negligible creates a {\i safek kiddushin} \u8212- an uncertainty of marriage \u8212- because one cannot rule out that it might fetch a {\i perutah} somewhere on the globe.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The implication for the Mishnah\u8217's formulation is profound. Beis Hillel says {\i kiddushin} is valid with a {\i perutah} or {\i shaveh perutah} \u8212- the equivalent of a {\i perutah}. One might have assumed that if an object is worth less than a {\i perutah} locally, {\i kiddushin} is definitively invalid. Shmuel is saying no \u8212- there is no case where one can be entirely certain that an object is not worth a {\i perutah} somewhere in the world.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Definite vs.\u160 ?Safek\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara reconciles this with Beis Hillel\u8217's Mishnah as follows: {\i lo kashya, ha bekiddushei vadai, ha bekiddushei safek} \u8212- there is no contradiction. When Beis Hillel says {\i shaveh perutah}, he means that if the object is worth a {\i perutah} in this locale, the {\i kiddushin} is {\i vadai} \u8212- definitively \u8212- valid. If it is not worth a {\i perutah} here, the {\i kiddushin} is {\i safek} \u8212- uncertain. Beis Hillel was not saying that a sub-{\i perutah} object creates no {\i kiddushin} at all; he was establishing the threshold for {\i vadai kiddushin}. This is a significant {\i chumra} embedded in Shmuel\u8217's ruling: virtually every case of non-monetary {\i kiddushin} with an object of minimal value becomes at least a {\i safek kiddushin}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mother\u8217's Testimony\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara illustrates this with a series of cases. A man betrothed a woman with a bundle of rags \u8212- essentially a bundle of {\i shmattes}. Rav Simi bar Chiya sat before Rav and examined the case: {\i ei beih shaveh prutah, in; ei lo, lo} \u8212- if worth a {\i perutah}, she is married; if not, not. The Gemara objects: even if not worth a {\i perutah}, according to Shmuel there would still be a {\i safek kiddushin}. The Gemara responds: {\i lo kashya, ha bekiddushei vadai, ha bekiddushei safek} \u8212- the same resolution applies.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Then comes the case of the colored stone \u8212- a man betrothed a woman with what appears to be a decorative stone of the kind sold inexpensively by the handful. Rav Chisda sat and assessed: {\i ei it bah shaveh prutah, in; ei lo, lo}. Again the Gemara asks: even if not worth a {\i perutah}, shouldn\u8217't Shmuel\u8217's principle apply? Here, however, the Gemara gives a different answer: {\i Rav Chisda lo savar lah de-Shmuel} \u8212- Rav Chisda simply does not hold of Shmuel\u8217's position.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The narrative continues. The mother of one of the parties \u8212- the text is ambiguous \u8212- testified that on the day of the betrothal, the stone was worth a {\i perutah}: {\i dehahu yoma dekadshah havah bei shaveh prutah}. Rav Chisda dismisses her testimony: {\i lav kol kaminach de\u8217'asrat lah abatra} \u8212- you have no standing to render her forbidden to the second man. The dismissal rests on the principle that {\i ein davar shebe\u8217'erva pachos mishnayim} \u8212- matters of personal status ({\i davar shebe\u8217'erva}) require two valid witnesses. A single woman\u8217's testimony, even if believed in ordinary matters of {\i issurin}, is insufficient to establish or challenge a marriage bond, especially because the ruling has direct implications for third parties \u8212- the second man\u8217's status and rights are at stake, not merely an abstract prohibition.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Chisda draws a parallel to the case of Yehudis, the wife of Rabbi Chiya. Yehudis, suffering through the anguish of yet another difficult labor, said to her husband that her mother had once told her that her father had betrothed her to another man when she was a minor. Was she then forbidden to Rabbi Chiya? Rabbi Chiya responded: {\i lav kol keminach de\u8217'imach, de\u8217'asrat lich alavai} \u8212- your mother has no halakhic standing to make you forbidden to me. The testimony of one woman, carrying no corroboration and implicating a marriage whose consequences fall on others, is entirely dismissed. The ruling in the colored stone case follows the same logic: the stone is assessed on the evidence available, the mother\u8217's testimony is dismissed, and the conclusion is that the stone was not worth a {\i perutah}, so the first {\i kiddushin} was not valid. She is definitively married to the second man and requires no {\i get} from the first.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Witnesses Elsewhere\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The rabbinic colleagues then challenge Rav Chisda: {\i amai, hei ika sahadi be-Idis deyad\u8217'ei dehahu yoma hava bei shaveh prutah} \u8212- why rule so definitively? There are witnesses in a distant place \u8212- somewhere called Idis \u8212- who know that on the day of the betrothal the stone was in fact worth a {\i perutah}. Surely their knowledge creates at least a {\i safek}? Rav Chisda replies: {\i hashata miha lo leisnu kaman} \u8212- at this moment they are not before us. They are unavailable. One cannot indefinitely suspend a ruling because of witnesses who are elsewhere and have not come forward.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 He invokes the principle stated by Rabbi Chanina: {\i eidei b\u8217'tzad istin ve-tinaser} \u8212- there are witnesses over there, but she may still remarry. Rabbi Chanina\u8217's ruling concerned the daughters of Shmuel who were taken captive. The {\i halacha} permits them to remarry on their own testimony that they were not violated, because there were no witnesses who directly observed their captivity. Even if one knew abstractly that witnesses existed somewhere, that knowledge does not override the absence of actual testimony before the court.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye and Rava, however, disagree with Rav Chisda\u8217's application of this principle. They hold {\i lo savari leh l\u8217'ha deRav Chisda}. The analogy to the captive woman, they argue, is imperfect. In the case of the {\i shvuyah}, leniency was granted because the concern about her violation is rabbinic in nature, not a {\i d\u8217'oraita} prohibition in the fullest sense \u8212- and even there, only because there were no witnesses present. But here, where there are actual witnesses who positively know that the stone was worth a {\i perutah}, and whose existence is not merely hypothetical, the {\i d\u8217'oraita} concern of {\i eishet ish} cannot be dismissed so casually.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot steps in to clarify the parameters of the case. He objects that two extremes are each implausible. If the witnesses\u8217' existence is purely conjectural \u8212- a simple {\i shema yesh eidim} with no concrete basis \u8212- it is obvious one cannot require a {\i get} for such a remote possibility, and Rav Chisda would clearly be lenient. On the other hand, if someone had actually heard those witnesses say that the stone was worth a {\i perutah}, it would be equally obvious that Rav Chisda could not dismiss this so leniently, and even he would require a {\i get}. Tosafot therefore concludes that the case must be a middle scenario: there was a {\i kol} \u8212- a rumor, a report \u8212- that witnesses in a distant location knew the stone\u8217's value, but nothing more concrete than that. Rav Chisda was willing to dismiss an unverified rumor of witnesses elsewhere; Abaye and Rava were not. The Gemara adds that in Sura, descendants from the family of that case remained \u8212- some married into the general community following Rav Chisda\u8217's ruling, but the rabbis themselves were more stringent and avoided marrying into that family. The Gemara clarifies: their stringency was not because they held of Shmuel\u8217's principle (that any object might be worth a {\i perutah} in {\i Madai}), but because they followed the position of Abaye and Rava \u8212- that the rumored existence of witnesses elsewhere creates a genuine {\i safek} that cannot be set aside.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The practical conclusion that emerges is this: if an object is clearly and definitively worth less than a {\i perutah} locally, and there is no concrete knowledge of witnesses who know otherwise, we rule that the {\i kiddushin} is not valid. But where there is some genuine \u8212- even if uncertain \u8212- basis for believing witnesses exist who know the object was worth a {\i perutah}, we cannot simply dismiss the possibility of {\i kiddushin}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Myrtle Stick Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The final case in this sequence: a man betrothed a woman in the marketplace with a stick of myrtle \u8212- {\i shatisa de-asa} \u8212- clearly plucked from the ground and worth nothing. Rav Acha bar Huna sent the question to Rav Yosef. The response: {\i nagdi ke-Rav, ve-itzterich get ke-Shmuel} \u8212- he is to receive lashes in the manner of Rav, and she requires a {\i get} in accordance with Shmuel. The lashes follow from Rav\u8217's enactment against {\i kiddushin} conducted in a marketplace, as we shall see. The {\i get} follows from Shmuel\u8217's principle: however obvious it may be that a myrtle stick is worthless locally, Shmuel requires that we treat it as a {\i safek kiddushin} on the possibility that it might be worth a {\i perutah} somewhere far away.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rav\u8217's Lashes\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Marketplace Kiddushin\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav enacted that a man who effects {\i kiddushin} in a marketplace \u8212- spontaneously, in an unplanned manner \u8212- receives rabbinic lashes. The reason is that marriage is a serious institution requiring deliberate forethought and preparation. Even if the {\i kiddushin} is fully valid {\i mid\u8217'oraita} \u8212- witnesses were present, the woman accepted \u8212- the manner in which it was done reflects an attitude toward marriage as something casual and unplanned, like a market transaction. Rav sought to prevent this.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Without Shiduchim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Similarly, Rav enacted lashes for one who effects {\i kiddushin} without any prior {\i shiduchim} \u8212- without an engagement process preceding the betrothal. This is closely related to the marketplace {\i kiddushin} concern: in both cases, the problem is the absence of the deliberateness and gravity that ought to characterize entry into the institution of marriage.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kiddushin BeViah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav also enacted lashes for one who effects {\i kiddushin be-viah} \u8212- {\i kiddushin} through intercourse. The deeper concern here is that the structure of Jewish marriage, with its two distinct stages of {\i kiddushin} and {\i nisuin}, is designed to communicate that marriage is not reducible to sexuality. The binding commitment \u8212- the {\i kiddushin} \u8212- precedes the physical intimacy. When a man makes the act of intercourse itself the vehicle of {\i kiddushin}, he inverts this structure and reduces the institution to its physical dimension. This sends the wrong message about what marriage is: a commitment that endures independently of the couple\u8217's physical life together, a legal and covenantal bond that does not dissolve when circumstances become difficult. Beginning with the physical collapses this distinction. Technically the {\i kiddushin} may be valid, but Rav required lashes because it reflects a misunderstanding \u8212- or a deliberate rejection \u8212- of the institution\u8217's moral architecture.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Other Lash Cases\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav\u8217's enactments extend beyond these three cases. One who {\i mevatel} a {\i get} \u8212- invalidates a bill of divorce that is already in transit \u8212- also receives lashes, since the woman may be left in a state of uncertainty about her status, potentially leading to serious harm. One who deposits a {\i moda\u8217'a} against a {\i get} \u8212- who testifies before witnesses that he is being compelled to give the {\i get} against his will, thereby preemptively undermining it \u8212- is also subject to lashes, since this is a strategy to circumvent the court\u8217's authority and leave the woman as a {\i mesurevet get}. One who abuses a rabbinic emissary sent to summon him to court receives lashes for rejecting rabbinic authority. One who allows a {\i niduy} to remain upon him for thirty days without any effort toward reconciliation shows contempt for rabbinic censure and is escalated to lashes. Finally, a son-in-law who lives in his mother-in-law\u8217's home \u8212- where there is no father-in-law present \u8212- is subject to Rav\u8217's enactment, because of the suspicion such an arrangement may generate.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara clarifies that this last enactment applies only when the son-in-law is actually dwelling there ({\i de-dayer}), not when he merely visits. However, the case of a son-in-law who regularly passed through the door of his mother-in-law\u8217's home and was given lashes by Rav Sheshet is reconciled: in that particular case, {\i hahu midam havei daima chamsei minei} \u8212- there were concrete suspicions circulating about him, not merely the structural concern created by residence.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Nahardai Limitation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The scholars of Nehardea ({\i Nahardai}) limit Rav\u8217's enactment of lashes to a single category: {\i kiddushin be-viah belo shiduchim} \u8212- {\i kiddushin} through intercourse without any prior engagement. On this reading, only the combination of these two factors \u8212- making intercourse the mode of {\i kiddushin} and doing so without a prior engagement process \u8212- triggers Rav\u8217's sanction. The other cases in the list, on the {\i Nahardai} view, do not.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A further opinion in the Gemara holds that even {\i kiddushin be-viah with} prior {\i shiduchim} is subject to lashes \u8212- {\i mishum pritzuta} \u8212- on account of immodesty. Tosafot discusses what exactly the {\i pritzuta} is. One opinion (cited in the name of Rabbeinu Tam) is that it consists in requiring witnesses to the act of intercourse itself, which is inherently immodest. Tosafot\u8217's own preferred reading, attributed to the Ri, is simpler and more direct: the {\i pritzuta} lies in making the inception of the marriage bond \u8212- the foundational moment of {\i kinyan} \u8212- consist entirely in an act of intercourse. Even if witnesses to the act are somehow avoided, the message conveyed is one in which marriage is grounded in and defined by sexuality rather than by commitment. This, the Ri argues, is the real {\i pritzuta}, and it is what Rav sought to uproot through his enactment of lashes.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Kiddushin 13\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Hahu Gavra - Mat Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mat Marriage Story\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara opens with the case of {\i hahu gavra} \u8212- a man who betrothed a woman using a mat woven from reeds of {\i hadas}, myrtle. The incident is located approximately ten lines from the bottom of 12b, where the Gemara has been presenting a series of real-life cases centered primarily on whether the object used for {\i kiddushin} was worth a {\i shaveh prutah}. (The broader context is a series of such stories, following a separate point about a {\i kiddushin} performed in the marketplace, for which Rav would administer lashes on account of its lack of appropriate seriousness.)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 In this case, bystanders objected to the man that the mat was not worth a {\i shaveh prutah} \u8212- a reminder that while a {\i prutah} today may be worth roughly two cents, in a pre-industrial economy where everything was handmade, even a small coin carried real purchasing power, and a simple mat might genuinely have fallen beneath that threshold.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hidden Zuzim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The man responded to the objection by saying: {\i tikadesh b\u8217'arba zuzei d\u8217'ita bah} \u8212- let her be betrothed with the four {\i zuzei} hidden inside the mat. A {\i zuz} is a {\i dinar}, worth nearly two hundred times a {\i prutah}, so four {\i zuzei} represent a sum far in excess of the minimum. The complication, however, is that she had already taken possession of the mat before he made this announcement. She therefore took the mat without knowing that it contained his money. When he declared that the four {\i zuzei} should serve as {\i kesef kiddushin}, she simply said nothing \u8212- {\i shekaltah v\u8217'ishtika}, she held onto the mat and was silent. The question is whether that silence, following his declaration, constitutes an act of acceptance sufficient to effect {\i kiddushin}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava - Silence After\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava rules: {\i havi shetikah d\u8217'le\u8217'achar matan maot}, this is a case of silence after the money has already been transferred, {\i v\u8217'chol shetikah d\u8217'le\u8217'achar matan maot lav klum hu} \u8212- and any such silence is of no legal consequence. Rava draws a sharp distinction between two types of conduct: actively reaching out and accepting an object when someone says {\i harei at mekudeshet li b\u8217'davar zeh} is a positive act of acceptance; merely failing to extract the coins from the mat and return them is an absence of rejection, not an affirmative acceptance. The two are categorically different. If the money is already in one\u8217's possession without any conscious act of receiving it, the failure to throw it back does not rise to the level of a {\i kinyan}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This point has a striking contemporary resonance in discussions of consent \u8212- the question of whether silence should be read as yes or only the absence of no \u8212- but the Gemara establishes clearly that for {\i kiddushin} to take effect, an affirmative act is required.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava then provides the source for this ruling from a {\i braita}. The case in the {\i braita} is: a man says to a woman, {\i kinsi sela zu b\u8217'pikadon} \u8212- take this {\i sela} (worth approximately 800 {\i prutot}) as a {\i pikadon}, to watch on my behalf. She accepts and holds it as a {\i shomer}. He then turns to her and says, {\i hitkadshi li bo} \u8212- be betrothed to me with it. The {\i braita} rules: if he said this {\i b\u8217'sha\u8217'at matan maot}, at the very moment he handed over the {\i sela}, she is {\i mekudeshet} if she agrees. {\i Le\u8217'achar matan maot}, if he said it after the money had already been handed over, {\i ratzta mekudeshet, lo ratzta eina mekudeshet} \u8212- if she agrees, she is betrothed; if she does not agree, she is not.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then presses: {\i mai ratzta, mai lo ratzta} \u8212- what precisely do {\i ratzta} and {\i lo ratzta} mean here? If {\i ratzta} means she explicitly said yes, and {\i lo ratzta} means she explicitly said no, then we would be left with an excluded middle: what about silence? But this cannot be the correct reading, because if explicit refusal were the only disqualifying scenario, then even in the first clause \u8212- where he speaks {\i b\u8217'sha\u8217'at matan maot} \u8212- an explicit refusal should also prevent {\i kiddushin}, and the {\i braita} would be teaching nothing new for the {\i le\u8217'achar matan maot} case. The contrast between the two clauses only makes sense if what works in one case and fails in the other is precisely silence. Therefore: {\i ratzta} must mean she actively said yes, and {\i lo ratzta} \u8212- the case where the {\i kiddushin} fails \u8212- must mean she was silent. This in turn demonstrates that {\i shetikah d\u8217'le\u8217'achar matan maot} is not a valid form of acceptance.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A practical application of this principle arises even under the {\i chuppah}: if the groom places the ring on the bride\u8217's finger and only then recites {\i harei at mekudeshet li b\u8217'davar zeh}, one could raise the question of whether this constitutes {\i shetikah d\u8217'le\u8217'achar matan maot}. The case under the {\i chuppah} is arguably different, since the entire proceeding is framed as {\i kiddushin} from the outset, but the question illustrates the real-world stakes of the principle.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Pikadon vs.\u160 ?Kiddushin\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Huna Challenge\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara records that in the academy of Pumnara, a challenge to Rava\u8217's ruling was raised in the name of Rav Huna the son of Rav Yehoshua: {\i mi dami} \u8212- is this truly comparable? The {\i pikadon} case and the mat case are structurally different. In the {\i pikadon} case, {\i hatam betorat pikadon yahvinhu nihala} \u8212- the money was originally given to her in the capacity of a deposit; she became a {\i shomer} over it. When the man later proposes to use it as {\i kesef kiddushin}, her silence cannot be interpreted as acceptance, because she is already under an obligation to hold the object. The Gemara elaborates: {\i savra} \u8212- she reasons to herself that if she throws it back and it breaks or is lost, {\i mechayavna behu}, she will be liable. A {\i shomer} cannot simply discard what she is guarding. Her continued possession of the object therefore reflects her duty as a {\i shomer}, not any acceptance of {\i kiddushin}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gemara Response\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara distinguishes: {\i hacha betorat kiddushin yahvinhu nihala} \u8212- in the mat case, the original transfer was framed entirely within the context of {\i kiddushin}. He gave her the mat saying {\i harei at mekudeshet li}, and she agreed to receive it. The only deficiency was that the mat itself lacked a {\i shaveh prutah} \u8212- but there were four {\i zuzim} hidden inside it. In this context, silence is quite reasonably interpreted as accepting those {\i zuzim} as {\i kesef kiddushin}, because {\i ve\u8217'ei sevira d\u8217'la nicha lah} \u8212- if she truly did not wish to be betrothed with the four {\i zuzim}, {\i lishdayinhu} \u8212- she should throw them back. She is not a {\i shomer} here; she has no obligation to retain the object. If she keeps it in silence, that silence reasonably signals acceptance.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Acha Objection\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Acha then objects: {\i atu kuli neshei dina g\u8217'miri} \u8212- do you assume that all women know {\i halacha}? Even in the {\i kiddushin} context, one could argue that she retains the mat not because she is accepting the {\i kiddushin} but because she fears liability: {\i ei shadina lehu umitvri, mechaivna be\u8217'achrayusa} \u8212- if I throw it back and it breaks, I will have to pay. This fear \u8212- that returning something might create liability \u8212- is not a uniquely legal concern; it is the kind of intuitive worry anyone might have. Accordingly, silence here too cannot unambiguously be read as acceptance of {\i kesef kiddushin}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ravina Resolution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Acha bar Rav sent the question to Ravina: in such a case, what is the {\i halacha}? Ravina replied: {\i ana lo shmiah lan ha de\u8217'Rav Huna breih de\u8217'Rav Yehoshua} \u8212- we have not received the tradition of Rav Huna the son of Rav Yehoshua that draws this distinction between the {\i pikadon} case and the {\i kiddushin} case. Those who have not received this tradition presumably treat both cases identically, and would rule that silence is not acceptance. {\i Atun de\u8217'shmiah lechu chushu lah} \u8212- but you, who have received this tradition and who draw the distinction, must be concerned, and must treat the silence in the {\i kiddushin}-framed case as a possible {\i safek kiddushin}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The question of why this ruling should vary by locale is interesting: Rav Huna bar Rav Yehoshua did not issue a binding {\i psak halacha}; he raised a cogent logical basis for distinguishing between the cases. And yet, evidently, having a {\i tradition} that a distinction is well-founded is not quite the same as having independently heard the argument \u8212- it represents something between a received {\i psak} and a purely personal analysis. The result is that in the locale that received this tradition, a {\i safek kiddushin} is the operative ruling.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Varshachi Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Man Grabs Bundle\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara presents a new case: {\i hahu itata de\u8217'hava ka\u8217'mezabna varshachi} \u8212- there was a woman who was selling {\i varshachi}. Rashi describes this as a bundle of linen, though other commentators offer gems or a type of head covering; the precise identity of the merchandise is secondary. A man came, {\i chataf varshacha minah} \u8212- grabbed one of the bundles from her. She said to him: {\i hav nihali} \u8212- give it back to me, it is mine. He replied: {\i ei yahivna lach mekudeshet li} \u8212- if I give it back to you, will you be betrothed to me with it? {\i Shakaltia ve\u8217'ishtaka} \u8212- she took it back and was silent. He handed it to her upon this conditional declaration, and she accepted it without a word.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Nachman Ruling\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Nachman rules that she can say {\i in shakli ve\u8217'didi shakli} \u8212- yes, I took it, but I took back what was mine. Even though he said {\i harei at mekudeshet li} and physically handed it to her, and even though she accepted it without objecting, that acceptance does not constitute {\i kiddushin} \u8212- she was simply retrieving her own property.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava Challenge\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava challenges Rav Nachman from a {\i braita}: {\i kidsah be\u8217'gezel u\u8217'be\u8217'chamas u\u8217'be\u8217'gneivah o she\u8217'chataf sela mi\u8217'yadah ve\u8217'kidsha mekudeshet} \u8212- if he betrothed a woman with money he stole, or with {\i chamas} (taking an object by force of payment that the owner never agreed to accept, a form of {\i gezel}), or with {\i gneivah}, or if he grabbed a {\i sela} from her hand and betrothed her with it \u8212- she is {\i mekudeshet}. This creates an apparent difficulty: how can a man betroth a woman using money he does not own? And of the two scenarios \u8212- stolen from someone else, or grabbed from her \u8212- the latter seems more obviously problematic.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara navigates the first scenario through the principle of {\i yeush koneh}: once the original owner despairs of recovering stolen property, the thief acquires legal title, though he remains obligated to reimburse the owner. However, when the object was stolen directly from the woman standing right in front of him, she has obviously not despaired \u8212- {\i mi ya\u8217'eshet}? The Gemara ultimately inclines toward the view that {\i yeush} is not {\i koneh}, so {\i gezel} from third parties would not work, and the entire {\i braita} is understood as referring exclusively to the case where the object was stolen from her. The mechanism by which this works is that her silence \u8212- when he says {\i harei at mekudeshet li} and returns the object \u8212- constitutes an implicit waiver of her ownership, allowing him to hold it for a moment as his own in order to be {\i mekadesh} her with it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is directly in tension with Rav Nachman\u8217's ruling that she can claim {\i in shakli ve\u8217'didi shakli}. How can the {\i braita} say she is {\i mekudeshet} if she could always claim she was merely taking back her own?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sheduch Distinction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara resolves the contradiction: {\i hatam be\u8217'deshadich} \u8212- in the {\i braita} there was a {\i sheduch}, an engagement period, prior to the incident. When the parties have been formally betrothed in the {\i shidduchin} sense and have been planning to marry, the contextual backdrop leads us to read her silence as genuine acceptance. She is implicitly permitting him to hold the object so that he can use it to be {\i mekadesh} her with it. By contrast, if a stranger in the marketplace suddenly grabs a woman\u8217's merchandise and attempts this maneuver, there is no such contextual basis for reading her silence as anything other than retrieving her own property.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara also asks: {\i u\u8217'mena teimara de\u8217'shani lan bein shadich le\u8217'lo shadich} \u8212- from where do we know this distinction matters? The answer comes from a {\i braita} concerning debt repayment, which will be analyzed in the next section.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Debt Money Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Debt Payment Braita\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i braita} states: a man tells a woman, {\i kinsi sela zu she\u8217'ani chayav lichi} \u8212- take this {\i sela} that I owe you, here is repayment of my debt. He then immediately says, {\i hitkadshi bo} \u8212- now be betrothed to me with it. The {\i braita} rules: {\i b\u8217'sha\u8217'at matan maot}, if he said this at the moment of handing over the money, {\i ratzta mekudeshet, lo ratzta eina mekudeshet} \u8212- if she agrees, she is betrothed; if not, she is not. {\i Le\u8217'achar matan maot}, if he said it after the money was already in her hand, {\i afilu ratzta eina mekudeshet} \u8212- even if she says yes, it does not work.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Timing Analysis\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The second ruling \u8212- that even explicit agreement fails once the money has passed \u8212- requires explanation. The reason is that the moment the debt is paid, the money becomes fully hers. He is therefore asking her to be betrothed using her own money. One cannot effect {\i kiddushin} with the recipient\u8217's own property.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ratzta Definition\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara again asks: {\i mai ratzta, mai lo ratzta} \u8212- what do these terms mean in the first clause, where the timing is {\i b\u8217'sha\u8217'at matan maot}? Following the same logical structure applied earlier in the {\i pikadon braita}: if {\i ratzta} means an explicit yes and {\i lo ratzta} means an explicit no, then silence would remain ambiguous, and the {\i braita} should simply have said {\i mekudeshet} without qualification. The necessity of specifying {\i ratzta} and {\i lo ratzta} as alternatives implies that {\i lo ratzta} covers the silent case \u8212- she is not betrothed if she says nothing \u8212- while {\i ratzta} means she actively and explicitly said yes. This confirms the earlier ruling: when money is handed over in a non-{\i kiddushin} context, an explicit acceptance is required for {\i kiddushin} to take effect.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Silence Failure\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Why does silence fail here even in the {\i b\u8217'sha\u8217'at matan maot} case? Because she is receiving money that is owed to her in any event. Unlike a gratuitous gift where the only plausible reason for accepting would be {\i kiddushin}, here she has an independent reason to take the money \u8212- it is a debt repayment. And unlike the case of stealing, where the item at least is handed back to her in a transactional moment framed as {\i kiddushin}, a debt creates an even more natural ground for taking the money without any acceptance of the {\i kiddushin} condition: {\i yechola le\u8217'meimar in shakli ve\u8217'didi shakli} \u8212- I simply took what was owed to me.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Indeed, the debt case is in certain respects an even stronger instance of this principle than the {\i gezel} case, because a debt need not be repaid at this specific moment; he could pay it later. When he chooses this particular moment to pay and simultaneously declares {\i harei at mekudeshet li}, there is no necessary connection between the two acts from her perspective.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gezel Contradiction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This creates a sharp difficulty: if silence fails even in the relatively ambiguous debt case \u8212- where one might have thought the context could reasonably suggest she accepted the {\i kiddushin} framing \u8212- then all the more so should silence fail in the {\i gezel} case. Yet the {\i braita} rules that in the {\i gezel} case she {\i is} betrothed! The Gemara resolves this, as noted above, through the {\i sheduch} distinction: {\i ha de\u8217'shadich, ha de\u8217'lo shadich} \u8212- the {\i braita} where {\i kiddushin} takes effect from stolen property is the case where there was a prior engagement period. In that setting, silence is read as acceptance. The debt {\i braita}, by contrast, and the marketplace grabbing case of Rav Nachman, involve no such prior context, and silence therefore does not constitute agreement.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The upshot of the entire discussion is two distinct principles about silence. The first \u8212- {\i shetikah d\u8217'le\u8217'achar matan maot} \u8212- addresses the case where money was already in the woman\u8217's possession before the declaration of {\i kiddushin}, and rules that her failure to return it is not an act of acceptance. The second principle addresses the case where money is handed over simultaneously with or just before a {\i kiddushin} declaration, but the money is being given for an independent reason (debt repayment, restoration of stolen goods): here too silence is not acceptance, because she may simply be taking what is already hers. The exception in both cases is a prior {\i sheduch} context, which provides the background for reading her conduct as consent.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rav Assi Teachings\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Assi Deathbed\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara transitions with the notice: {\i ki nach nafsheih de\u8217'Rav Assi, ailu rabanan le\u8217'liktinhu le\u8217'shmateih} \u8212- when Rav Assi died, the rabbis gathered to collect his teachings, his {\i shmateih}, lest they be forgotten. One of the rabbis \u8212- Rav Yaakov by name \u8212- offered a tradition in the name of Rav Assi citing Rav Mani. What follows is a cluster of teachings, some original to Rav Assi and some transmitted by him in the names of earlier authorities.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Woman & Land Value\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Assi said in the name of Rav Mani: {\i keshem she\u8217'ein isha nikneit be\u8217'pachot mi\u8217'shaveh pruta, kach ein karka nikneit be\u8217'pachot mi\u8217'shaveh pruta} \u8212- just as a woman cannot be acquired in {\i kiddushin} for less than a {\i shaveh prutah}, so too land cannot be acquired by {\i kesef} for less than a {\i shaveh prutah}. Tosafot notes the irony that this comparison learns {\i karka} from {\i isha}, whereas the usual direction of inference in the Talmud runs the other way. The teaching may be intended as a parallel rather than a formal legal derivation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i braita} challenges: {\i af al pi she\u8217'ein isha nikneit be\u8217'pachot mi\u8217'shaveh pruta, karka nikneit be\u8217'pachot mi\u8217'shaveh pruta} \u8212- even though a woman requires a {\i shaveh prutah}, land can be acquired for less. The response: {\i ki tanya hahi be\u8217'chalipin} \u8212- that {\i braita} is discussing acquisition through {\i chalipin}, such as a {\i kinyan sudar}, not through {\i kesef}. The {\i braita} itself supports this: {\i konin be\u8217'kli ve\u8217'af al pi she\u8217'ein bo shaveh pruta} \u8212- one effects {\i chalipin} with an object even if it is not worth a {\i prutah}. The rule is therefore: {\i kesef} for {\i karka} requires a minimum of a {\i shaveh prutah}, just as {\i kesef kiddushin} does; {\i chalipin} for {\i karka} does not. An interesting asymmetry remains, however: {\i chalipin} works for {\i karka} but not for {\i isha} \u8212- a point addressed in an earlier {\i sugya}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Moral Teaching\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The gathering of rabbis then produced another tradition: {\i chad amar Rav Yehuda amar Shmuel} \u8212- one of the rabbis cited the well-known dictum of Rav Yehuda in the name of Shmuel that {\i kol she\u8217'eino yode\u8217'a betiv gittin ve\u8217'kiddushin lo yehei esek imahem} \u8212- whoever does not understand the intricacies of {\i gittin} and {\i kiddushin} should have nothing to do with them. On this, Rav Assi said in the name of Rabbi Yochanan: {\i dekashim le\u8217'olam yoter mi\u8217'dor ha\u8217'mabul} \u8212- those who blunder in this area cause harm to the world worse than the generation of the flood.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The verse from Hoshea is cited: {\i alo ve\u8217'chachesh ve\u8217'ratzoach ve\u8217'ganov, ve\u8217'na\u8217'of, paretzu ve\u8217'damim be\u8217'damim naga\u8217'u} \u8212- swearing, denying, murder, burglary, and adultery; they burst forth and blood touches blood. Rav Yosef interprets the critical phrase {\i paretzu}: {\i molidin benin minshei chavreihon} \u8212- they father children from their neighbors\u8217' wives, {\i ve\u8217'chovin al chovin mosifin} \u8212- and add iniquity upon iniquity. The connection to incorrect {\i psak} in {\i kiddushin} is that such errors lead to situations of unknowing adultery, which in turn produces illegitimate lineages, which compounds the transgression.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The verse continues: {\i al ken te\u8217'evel ha\u8217'aretz ve\u8217'umlal kol yoshev bah be\u8217'chayat ha\u8217'sadeh u\u8217've\u8217'of ha\u8217'shamayim ve\u8217'gam degei ha\u8217'yam ye\u8217'asefu} \u8212- even the fish of the sea will be gathered in destruction. The Gemara notes: {\i ve\u8217'ilu be\u8217'dor ha\u8217'mabul lo nigzar gezeira al dagim she\u8217'ba\u8217'yam} \u8212- but in the flood, the fish were spared, as the verse says {\i mikol asher be\u8217'charava metu} \u8212- only those on dry land perished, not the fish. Here, even the fish are destroyed \u8212- so this is worse than the flood.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara anticipates two objections. First: perhaps all the sins in the list must be present together before this consequence follows. This is dismissed: {\i dichtiv ki mipnei ala avela ha\u8217'aretz} \u8212- a separate verse states that the land mourns because of {\i swearing alone}, implying any single one of these sins suffices. Second: perhaps {\i alo} (swearing) is sufficient by itself, but the rest of the list requires the full combination. This too is dismissed: {\i mi ketiv u\u8217'paretzu, paretzu ketiv} \u8212- the text does not say \u8220"and they burst forth\u8221" with a conjunctive {\i vav}; it says {\i paretzu} without a {\i vav}, making it a standalone consequence arising from the single sin of adultery and its downstream effects. The moral is clear: issuing incorrect rulings in matters of {\i gittin} and {\i kiddushin}, which brings about unknowing adultery and illegitimate children, is a catastrophic wrong.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Korban Obligation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A third teaching from the gathering: {\i ha\u8217'ishah she\u8217'heiviah chatatah u\u8217'meisah, yavi\u8217'u yorshim olasah} \u8212- a woman who was obligated to bring a {\i chatas} and an {\i olah} (as with a woman after childbirth), brought her {\i chatas} and then died before bringing her {\i olah}; the heirs may bring her {\i olah}. This is possible because while a {\i chatas} cannot be offered on behalf of a deceased owner, an {\i olah} can.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Yehuda said in the name of Shmuel: {\i vehu she\u8217'hifrisha michayim} \u8212- this is only the case if she had already sanctified and designated the animal for the {\i olah} during her lifetime. {\i Aval lo hifrisha michayim, lo} \u8212- if she died before designating the animal, the heirs have no obligation. The Gemara notes the implication: {\i alma kasavar shibuda lav d\u8217'Oraisa} \u8212- Rav Yehuda in the name of Shmuel holds that liens arising from obligations are not biblically mandated. If they were, the Torah-imposed obligation to bring an {\i olah} would automatically create a lien on her estate, obligating the heirs to discharge the debt to the {\i Beis HaMikdash} even without prior designation. The fact that he limits the obligation to a case where she already designated the animal shows that without a specific act creating a concrete obligation, no lien exists.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shibuda Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Assi then cites Rabbi Yochanan in direct disagreement: {\i af al pi shelo hifrisha michayim}, even if she did not designate the animal during her lifetime, the heirs must bring the {\i olah}. {\i Alma kasavar shibuda havei d\u8217'Oraisa} \u8212- Rabbi Yochanan holds that obligations do automatically create liens on property from Torah law.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might wonder whether this dispute is limited to {\i korbanot}, where the {\i Beis HaMikdash} has a uniquely strong monetary claim. But the Gemara notes that this debate is already implicit in a broader dispute between Rav and Shmuel on one side and Rabbi Yochanan and Resh Lakish on the other. {\i De\u8217'Rav u\u8217'Shmuel amri tarvaihu: malveh al peh eino goveh min ha\u8217'yorshim ve\u8217'lo min ha\u8217'lokchot} \u8212- Rav and Shmuel both hold that an oral loan, unrecorded in a {\i shtar}, cannot be collected from heirs or from purchasers of the debtor\u8217's property. {\i Rabbi Yochanan ve\u8217'Reish Lakish amri tarvaihu: malveh al peh goveh min ha\u8217'yorshim u\u8217'min ha\u8217'lokchot} \u8212- Rabbi Yochanan and Resh Lakish hold that an oral loan can be collected from both heirs and purchasers.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This maps directly onto the {\i shibuda} question: does any obligation, by its very nature, create automatic liens on property, or is the lien only created through the formal act of executing a {\i shtar}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then explains why both formulations of the debate \u8212- the {\i korban} case and the {\i malveh al peh} case \u8212- are necessary and cannot be collapsed into one. If we only had the {\i malveh al peh} case and Shmuel ruled there are no liens, one could argue that a voluntary loan does not carry Torah-mandated liens precisely because the obligation is self-assumed rather than divinely imposed. {\i Aval beha} \u8212- but in the {\i korban} case, where the Torah itself imposes the obligation, perhaps Shmuel would concede that such obligations do create liens, and perhaps he would agree with Rabbi Yochanan and Resh Lakish. Conversely, if we only had the {\i korban} case and Rabbi Yochanan ruled there are liens, one might argue that a Torah-imposed obligation is uniquely powerful \u8212- as the Gemara puts it, {\i milve ha\u8217'ketuvah ba\u8217'Torah ki\u8217'ketuvah be\u8217'shtar damya}, a debt inscribed in the Torah is comparable to one inscribed in a {\i shtar} \u8212- and that even Rabbi Yochanan would concede that a mere oral loan lacks such a basis. {\i Tzricha} \u8212- both formulations are necessary to establish the full scope of each position.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The practical question of {\i how} we arrive at the ruling that an oral loan is collectible from heirs but not from purchasers also requires clarification. One theoretical framework holds that {\i shibuda d\u8217'Oraisa} is in force for all obligations, but the protection of {\i lokchot} \u8212- innocent purchasers who had no way of knowing of the debt \u8212- overrides it as a rabbinic enactment. A separate framework holds that fundamentally no debt carries automatic liens, but a {\i shtar} creates an implicit granting of lien rights to the creditor. These two frameworks have different implications: on the first view, the {\i shtar}-less debt does carry Torah-level liens that are rabinically suspended for {\i lokchot} but not for {\i yorshim}; on the second, the protection of {\i yorshim} is less obvious.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Pappa Ruling\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Pappa rules: {\i hilcheta malveh al peh goveh min ha\u8217'yorshim ve\u8217'eino goveh min ha\u8217'lokchot} \u8212- an oral loan can be collected from heirs but not from purchasers. The reasoning follows: {\i goveh min ha\u8217'yorshim} \u8212- {\i shibuda d\u8217'Oraisa} is in force, confirming that obligations create automatic Torah-level liens. {\i Ve\u8217'eino goveh min ha\u8217'lokchot} \u8212- but against purchasers it cannot be collected, {\i de\u8217'leis lei kala}, because since the loan was not recorded and publicly known, we protect the innocent purchasers who had no notice. The {\i yorshim}, by contrast, are not third-party purchasers who paid money in reliance on the debtor\u8217's unencumbered estate; they simply receive what their ancestor had, subject to his existing obligations.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Woman\u8217's Release\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Get & Death Q\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now transitions to the next {\i mishnah}: {\i ve\u8217'konah et atzmah be\u8217'get u\u8217'be\u8217'misat ha\u8217'baal} \u8212- a woman acquires her own freedom through a {\i get} and through the death of her husband. The discussion of {\i kiddushin} naturally gives way to the question of how {\i kiddushin} is dissolved.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Bishlama get} \u8212- that a {\i get} releases her is clear, as the Torah explicitly commands {\i ve\u8217'kasav lah sefer keritut}, a bill of severance. But the husband\u8217's death \u8212- {\i ela misat ha\u8217'baal, menalan} \u8212- where does the Torah ever explicitly state that she is released through his death?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Arayot Problem\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara first suggests a {\i sevara}, a logical inference: {\i hu asrah ve\u8217'hu sharya} \u8212- he made her forbidden (by marrying her), so when he dies he makes her permissible. The Gemara rejects this immediately: {\i de\u8217'arayot de\u8217'asru lehu ve\u8217'lo shari lehu} \u8212- the logic does not hold, because by many of the {\i arayot}, a woman becomes forbidden to a specific person through a relationship that no longer exists, yet the prohibition persists even after the death of the party who created the forbidden connection. The clearest example is a father\u8217's wife: she becomes forbidden to the son because of her marriage to the father. Yet even after the father dies, the prohibition does not lift. So death does not automatically dissolve forbidden relationships, and we cannot simply assume that a wife is released upon her husband\u8217's death.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yevamah Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara attempts a derivation from the laws of {\i yibbum}: the Torah states that a {\i yevamah she\u8217'ein lah banim asurah} \u8212- a childless widow is forbidden to all other men because of her obligation to the {\i yavam}, the deceased husband\u8217's brother. This implies {\i ha yesh lah banim mutteret} \u8212- implicitly, if she has children and is exempt from {\i yibbum}, she is permissible. The Gemara pushes back: {\i vedilma} \u8212- perhaps even if she has children, she remains forbidden to the world at large; the only thing the childless case tells us is that she is also {\i forbidden} to non-{\i yavam} men, while the case with children merely removes the {\i yavam}\u8217's claim, leaving the general question of her status unresolved. The inference therefore fails.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Widow & Kohen\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara tries yet another approach: the Torah forbids a {\i kohen gadol} from marrying a widow. The implication is {\i ha le\u8217'kohen hedyot sharya} \u8212- to an ordinary {\i kohen} and to the rest of the world she is permissible. Again the Gemara rejects the inference: {\i de\u8217'dilma l\u8217'kohen gadol be\u8217'lav} \u8212- perhaps the {\i kohen gadol} violates a specific negative commandment, {\i ve\u8217'le\u8217'chulei alma be\u8217'aseh} \u8212- while for everybody else there is merely an {\i issur aseh} prohibiting her from remarrying. The discussion of whether and how we can definitively derive the permissibility of a widow after her husband\u8217's death will continue in the next session.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Kiddushin 14\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Opening & Context\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Opening Remarks\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Dan Margulis is filling in for Rabbi Linzer. By way of introduction, he notes that it is {\i shoshon purim}, the fifteenth of Adar, which is, by the well-known principle, {\i shloshim yom kodem hachag} \u8212- thirty days before Pesach \u8212- the time when we begin {\i sho\u8217'alin vedorshin b\u8217'hilchot hapesach}. Though the tractate under study is {\i Kiddushin}, the spirit of Pesach is present: the essential act of Pesach is asking questions, and that spirit animates the entire enterprise of Talmud study. The sugya before us today is on 13b.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Eternal Marriage Polemic\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The sugya at hand addresses how we know that a husband\u8217's death permits his widow to remarry. This is not merely a technical halakhic question; it carries polemical weight against theological traditions \u8212- certain of them still present today \u8212- that maintain the concept of an eternal marriage bond persisting even after one spouse\u8217's death. The Gemara\u8217's sustained attention to deriving this permission from scriptural sources reflects a serious halakhic and philosophical commitment to establishing that death fully dissolves the marital bond.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Widow Remarriage\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kohen Gadol Source\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara proposes that we may derive the widow\u8217's permission to remarry from the special prohibition applying to the {\i kohen gadol}: {\i ela mide\u8217'amar rachmana almanah le\u8217'kohen gadol asura ha le\u8217'kohen hedyot sharya} \u8212- the Torah specifically prohibits the {\i kohen gadol} from marrying an {\i almanah}, which implies that a regular {\i kohen} may do so, and therefore an {\i almanah} is in general permitted to remarry. The only one absolutely prohibited from marrying a widow would be the {\i kohen gadol}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mitzvat Asei Proposal\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara immediately challenges this derivation. Perhaps the prohibition for the {\i kohen gadol} is a {\i lav}, while for all others \u8212- {\i Yisraelim}, regular {\i kohanim}, and everyone else \u8212- there remains an {\i issur asei} derived from the verse {\i vedavak be\u8217'ishto}, the idea that one is bound by a positive commandment to be joined with one\u8217's wife. The {\i Ri} in {\i Tosafot} raises precisely this possibility: perhaps even after the husband\u8217's death, some residual {\i mitzvat asei} prohibits another man from marrying the widow. This is framed as the Gemara\u8217's {\i hava amina} \u8212- a position that will ultimately be rejected, but whose very consideration underscores how counterintuitive it would be to require explicit derivation for something as seemingly obvious as a widow\u8217's right to remarry. As the Gemara notes, the entire {\i shakla vetarya} here is designed to push against that intuition and demonstrate that the permission is in fact well-grounded in {\i psukim}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Lav vs Asei\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara probes what this hypothetical {\i mitzvat asei} would actually accomplish. {\i Hai asei mai avid lei} \u8212- what does this {\i asei} do? Either {\i mitat habaal} completely releases her ({\i tishari legamri}), or it does not release her at all and she remains in her prior status ({\i tukma be\u8217'milta kamaisa}). The problem is that a {\i lav} and an {\i asei} operating together cannot produce a clean intermediate result. {\i Rashi} explains that if such an {\i issur asei} existed for everyone, there would be no reason for the {\i kohen gadol}\u8217's prohibition to be expressed as the more severe {\i lav}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then entertains a further refinement: perhaps {\i mitat habaal} removes her from the category of capital crime ({\i chiyuv mitat beit din}) but leaves intact an {\i issur asei}. The analogy offered is {\i psulei hamukdashim} \u8212- animals consecrated for sacrifice that subsequently became disqualified by a permanent blemish. Before the blemish, there is a prohibition of {\i me\u8217'ilah} (using consecrated property) as well as prohibitions against shearing or using the animal for labor. Once an act of {\i me\u8217'ilah} occurs under certain circumstances, the {\i kedushah} is removed and {\i me\u8217'ilah} no longer applies \u8212- yet the prohibitions of shearing and labor ({\i giza va\u8217'avoda}) remain. Similarly, one might think that the husband\u8217's death removes the {\i issur} punishable by {\i chenek}, but an {\i issur asei} persists.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This analogy is ultimately rejected, and the Gemara arrives at a real {\i pasuk}: {\i pen yamut bamilchama ve\u8217'ish acher yikachena} \u8212- from the section of those exempt from military service, the Torah anticipates that a soldier recently betrothed might die in battle, and another man will marry her. The word {\i yikach} is recognized as language of marriage ({\i ki yikach ish isha}), proving incidentally that another man may marry her after the first husband\u8217's death. This {\i drasha}, however, applies only to {\i erusin}, not {\i nisuin}, which will require separate derivation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Sheshet\u8217's Objection\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Matkif lah Rav Sheshet breih de\u8217'Rav Idi}: perhaps the verse does not prove that she may remarry any man she chooses; perhaps the only man permitted to marry her would be the {\i yavam} \u8212- her deceased husband\u8217's brother \u8212- to whom she has an existing obligation. This is a pointed challenge and one that proves highly relevant to deeper questions about the nature of {\i yibum}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yavam Not Acher\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Amar Rav Ashi: shtei teshuvot badavar} \u8212- there are two answers. First, {\i yavam lo ikrei acher} \u8212- the {\i yavam} is not called \u8220"another\u8221" man. This is a significant statement about the nature of {\i yibum}: the {\i yavam} is, in some sense, a continuation of or stand-in for his deceased brother, not a new and unrelated third party. The {\i Rambam}, notably, highlights in the opening {\i halachot} of {\i Hilchot Yibum VeChalitzah} that the {\i ketubah} in {\i yibum} is paid {\i al nikhsei ba\u8217'ala harishon} \u8212- from the estate of the first husband \u8212- underscoring the continuity of the original relationship. Whether {\i yibum} constitutes a new relationship or the continuation of the prior one is a rich question in the {\i rishonim}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Death Like Divorce\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The second answer: {\i ve\u8217'od kativ ve\u8217'sane\u8217'ah ha\u8217'ish ha\u8217'acharon} \u8212- the verse discussing {\i machzir grushato} (the prohibition against a man remarrying his divorced wife after she has been married to and divorced or widowed from another man) refers to the second husband as {\i ha\u8217'ish ha\u8217'acharon}. The verse contemplates that the second husband either divorces her or dies. If he dies, she is then permitted to remarry a third man \u8212- proof that death, like divorce, severs the marital bond entirely and permits remarriage. {\i Ve\u8217'hikish mita l\u8217'gerushin} \u8212- the Torah\u8217's juxtaposition of death and divorce in this verse establishes that just as {\i gerushin} completely severs the relationship and allows her to remarry ({\i ma gerushin sharya ve\u8217'gomar, af mita sharya ve\u8217'gomar}), so too death accomplishes the same full severance.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 An aside of contemporary halakhic interest: the {\i rishonim} discuss the case of {\i Eliyahu HaNavi}, who ascended to heaven in a chariot and did not die in the ordinary sense. What is the status of {\i Eliyahu}\u8217's wife? This serves as a theoretical construct for exploring the case of someone who has neither died nor is still present among the living. Related questions arise regarding persons who died and returned to life \u8212- whether through miraculous revival as recorded in {\i Tanakh}, or through modern resuscitation such as CPR. The {\i Pitchei Teshuva} addresses this, and the {\i Ben Ish Chai} has a {\i teshuva} distinguishing between {\i techiyat hameitim al yedei nes} \u8212- miraculous revival that might characterize modern medicine \u8212- and {\i techiyat hameitim} in the messianic era, which is categorically different. Whether cardiac death followed by resuscitation would in principle dissolve a marriage is thus at least a {\i hava amina} in the {\i poskim}, even if practical ruling consistently forecloses that conclusion.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Yibbum Mechanism\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yibbum Not Kesef\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} taught: {\i hayevamah nikneit be\u8217'viah} \u8212- a {\i yevamah} is acquired through {\i biah} alone, and she acquires her freedom through {\i chalitzah} or the {\i yavam}\u8217's death. The Gemara now asks: {\i be\u8217'viah minalan?} The question, as the {\i rishonim} clarify, is not merely how we know {\i biah} works, but specifically how we know that {\i kesef} and {\i shtar} do not work. The only effective mechanism for {\i yibum} is {\i biah}, and this requires derivation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yavo Aleha Source\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Amar kra: yevamah yavo aleha ulekachah lo le\u8217'ishah} \u8212- the verse in {\i Ki Tetze} states that her brother-in-law will come upon her and take her as his wife. The expression {\i yavo aleha} is the Torah\u8217's euphemism for a sexual encounter. One might have thought that since the verse concludes {\i ulekachah lo le\u8217'ishah}, comparing her to any other wife, all the mechanisms of standard {\i kiddushin} \u8212- {\i kesef}, {\i shtar}, and {\i biah} \u8212- would apply. {\i Rashi} makes this explicit: {\i dilma ke\u8217'ishah, mah ishah kesef ushtar ke\u8217'viah, af yevamah kesef ushtar ke\u8217'viah} \u8212- just as a wife can be acquired through {\i kesef} and {\i shtar} in addition to {\i biah}, perhaps the same is true of the {\i yevamah} precisely because the verse says {\i le\u8217'ishah}. This is why an explicit exclusionary {\i drasha} is necessary.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Veyibmah Redundancy\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i braita} is cited: one might have thought that {\i kesef} and {\i shtar} complete the acquisition of the {\i yevamah} just as {\i biah} does. {\i Talmud lomar: veyibmah} \u8212- the verse contains an apparently redundant word: after stating {\i yavo aleha ulekachah lo le\u8217'ishah}, it adds {\i veyibmah}, \u8220"and perform {\i yibum} with her.\u8221" This redundancy teaches: {\i biah gomeret bah ve\u8217'ein kesef ushtar gomerin bah} \u8212- only {\i biah} completes the acquisition; {\i kesef} and {\i shtar} do not.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This reflects a fundamental difference between {\i yibum} and ordinary {\i kiddushin}. The mechanisms differ, and more profoundly, the {\i chalos} \u8212- the legal status created \u8212- differs. {\i Yibum} is predicated on a pre-existing relationship; it is a very different kind of bond from {\i kiddushin}, which brings together two people with no prior legal connection.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Implicit Consent\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then asks: perhaps the word {\i veyibmah} is needed to teach not the exclusion of {\i kesef} and {\i shtar}, but rather that {\i yibum} may be performed {\i baal karkhah} \u8212- without the explicit present consent of the woman? The Gemara resolves this by noting that the verse could have said {\i veyabem} (masculine verb); the fact that it says {\i veyibmah} (with the feminine suffix, creating an additional textual redundancy) allows us to derive both principles simultaneously: {\i shema mina tartei} \u8212- we learn two things from the single word. {\i Biah} is the exclusive mechanism, and {\i yibum} may proceed {\i baal karkhah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The halakhic understanding of {\i baal karkhah} here is nuanced. The woman\u8217's {\i da\u8217'at} \u8212- her consent \u8212- at the time of her initial marriage is understood to carry with it an implicit legal acceptance of {\i yibum} should her husband predecease her without children. She entered marriage knowing the {\i halacha}; her consent to the marriage therefore encompasses implicit consent to the potential {\i yibum} obligation. This raises profound questions, discussed at length in the {\i rishonim} and {\i acharonim}, about how much voice the woman genuinely has in choosing between {\i yibum} and {\i chalitzah}, particularly given the question \u8212- debated in the first {\i perek} of {\i Yevamot} and in {\i Bechorot} \u8212- of whether we practice {\i yibum} today at all.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Chalitzah Release\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chalitzah Permits Remarr\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} also taught that the {\i yevamah} acquires her freedom through {\i chalitzah}. The Gemara asks: {\i minalan d\u8217'chalutzah muteret lehinnase?} \u8212- how do we know that a woman who has undergone {\i chalitzah} is permitted to remarry? One might think that after {\i chalitzah} she remains unable to remarry anyone: she has been rejected by the {\i yavam} (or has rejected him), but that alone does not necessarily permit her to marry a stranger. The death of her first husband left strings attached; her only options were {\i yibum} or {\i chalitzah}, and it is not self-evident that performing {\i chalitzah} entirely dissolves the bond and opens the door to any other marriage.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shoe Removal Source\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara derives the permission from the verse: {\i ve\u8217'nikra shemo be\u8217'Yisrael beit chalutz hana\u8217'al} \u8212- the {\i yavam} who submitted to {\i chalitzah} will be known in Israel as \u8220"the house of him whose shoe was removed.\u8221" From the phrase {\i shemo be\u8217'Yisrael}, the Gemara infers: {\i keivan shechala\u7827 ? bah na\u8217'al, hutrah le\u8217'chol Yisrael} \u8212- once she removes his shoe, she is permitted to marry any member of {\i Klal Yisrael}. This does not, of course, include a {\i kohen}, since a {\i chalutzah} is prohibited to a {\i kohen} \u8212- though the {\i rishonim} largely hold that the prohibition of {\i chalutzah le\u8217'kohen} is only {\i d\u8217'rabbanan}, since {\i chalitzah} resembles but is not identical to {\i gerushin}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Beth Din Jurisdiction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara immediately challenges this derivation: is the word {\i Yisrael} in this verse really needed to teach her permission to remarry, or does {\i Rav Shmuel bar Yehudah} need it to teach something else? {\i Rav Shmuel bar Yehudah} holds that the word {\i Yisrael} teaches that {\i chalitzah} must be performed before a {\i beit din shel Yisrael} \u8212- a Jewish court \u8212- and not before a non-Jewish court.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The alternative text discussed, following the {\i Hagahot HaBach}, reads {\i beit din shel geirim} rather than {\i beit din shel ovdei kochavim}. The question of whether converts may serve as {\i dayanim} for {\i chalitzah} is significant: it touches on the nature of {\i chalitzah} as a proceeding \u8212- is it a formal legal act requiring a fully qualified {\i beit din}, or is the {\i beit din} present primarily as witnesses to a ceremony? The {\i hava amina} that {\i chalitzah} might be valid before a non-Jewish court is striking and points to a genuine ambiguity about what kind of authority the {\i dayanim} present at {\i chalitzah} are exercising.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This line of reasoning connects to broader questions about civil law in {\i agunah} cases. Rav Kook, in a {\i teshuva} dealing with an {\i agunah} case, made the radical suggestion that at the {\i d\u8217'Oraisa} level, a civil divorce might qualify as a {\i get pasul}, employing this as one {\i snif le\u8217'hakeil} in a broader {\i diyun}. Similarly, questions arise about how much weight to give civil marriage or common-law marriage in Jewish legal contexts.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Multiple Yisrael Uses\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The resolution: {\i trei be\u8217'Yisrael kesivi} \u8212- the word {\i Yisrael} appears twice in the {\i parsha} of {\i chalitzah}, and therefore we may derive multiple {\i halachot} from the multiple occurrences.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Tarfon Procedure\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i braita} quoted by {\i Rabbi Yehuda} teaches that he and others once sat before {\i Rabbi Tarfon} when a {\i yevamah} came to perform {\i chalitzah}. {\i Rabbi Tarfon} instructed all those present to call out {\i chalutz hana\u8217'al, chalutz hana\u8217'al} \u8212- \u8220"the one whose shoe was removed\u8221" \u8212- immediately upon the completion of the ceremony. This raises the question of how literally to understand the verse {\i ve\u8217'nikra shemo be\u8217'Yisrael beit chalutz hana\u8217'al}: is the formal public calling of this name a required act, to be performed right then by those present, or is it merely a figurative description of how he will be known going forward?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Calling Him by Name\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara resolves this: {\i hahu mi\u8217've\u8217'nikra shemo nafka} \u8212- that particular {\i halacha} (the public calling of his name) is derived from the phrase {\i ve\u8217'nikra shemo}, not from the word {\i Yisrael}. Therefore, all three {\i halachot} can be derived independently: (1) the {\i beit din} must be a Jewish court; (2) the {\i chalutzah} is permitted to remarry any {\i Yisrael}; and (3) upon completion of the ceremony, those present must call out {\i chalutz hana\u8217'al}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Yavam\u8217's Death\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kal Vachomer Logic\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} further states that the {\i yevamah} acquires her freedom through the death of the {\i yavam}. The Gemara asks: {\i minalan?} And proposes a {\i kal vachomer} from {\i eishet ish}: if an {\i eishet ish} \u8212- a fully married woman whose violation carries the penalty of {\i chenek} \u8212- is released by the death of her husband, then a {\i yevamah} whose prohibition carries only the lesser penalty of a {\i lav} (punishable by lashes) should certainly be released by the death of her {\i yavam}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Get vs Chalitzah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i kal vachomer} is challenged: {\i mah le\u8217'eishet ish she\u8217'ken yotzah be\u8217'get} \u8212- the {\i eishet ish} is different in that she can be released through a {\i get}, which is not available to the {\i yevamah}, who can only be released through {\i chalitzah}. This asymmetry may undermine the comparison. The response is: {\i ha nami yotza be\u8217'chalitzah} \u8212- the {\i yevamah} too has a release mechanism, namely {\i chalitzah}, and perhaps {\i chalitzah} and {\i get} are sufficiently analogous to preserve the {\i kal vachomer}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yavam\u8217's Dual Role\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A deeper challenge: {\i mah le\u8217'eishet ish she\u8217'ken oserah matirah} \u8212- in the case of {\i eishet ish}, the one who created the prohibition (the husband) is the same one who releases her (through death or {\i get}). Can this be compared to the {\i yevamah}, where at first glance it seems the deceased husband created the prohibition while the {\i yavam} releases her through {\i chalitzah}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Amar Rav Ashi: ha nami osrah u\u8217'matirah} \u8212- Rav Ashi resolves this by pointing out that in the case of {\i yibum} as well, the same party both creates and dissolves the prohibition. The {\i yavam} himself is the one who creates the {\i issur}: if the {\i yavam} did not exist \u8212- if there were no brothers \u8212- the woman would be permitted to remarry immediately upon her husband\u8217's death. It is the very existence of the {\i yavam} that creates the prohibition of {\i yevamah lashuk}. Therefore, it is the {\i yavam} who both creates the {\i issur} and, through {\i chalitzah} or {\i yibum}, dissolves it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Reverse Kal Vachomer\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now explores the mirror image: can we run the {\i kal vachomer} in reverse? {\i Vetehei eishet ish yotza be\u8217'chalitzah mikal vachomer} \u8212- perhaps an {\i eishet ish} could also be released through {\i chalitzah}? The reasoning would be: if a {\i yevamah}, who cannot be released by a {\i get}, can be released by {\i chalitzah}, then surely an {\i eishet ish}, who can be released by a {\i get}, should also be releasable by {\i chalitzah}. {\i Amar kra: sefer kortah ve\u8217'ein davar acher kortah} \u8212- the Torah specified that only a written document severs the marriage of an {\i eishet ish}; nothing else can serve that function.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Similarly, one might argue in the other direction: {\i vetehei yevamah yotza be\u8217'get mikal vachomer} \u8212- if an {\i eishet ish}, who cannot be released by {\i chalitzah}, can be released by a {\i get}, surely a {\i yevamah} who can be released by {\i chalitzah} should also be releasable by a {\i get}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kacha & La\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara invokes two separate scriptural limitations. By the laws of {\i chalitzah}, the Torah used the word {\i kacha} \u8212- {\i kacha ye\u8217'aseh la\u8217'ish asher lo yivneh et beit achiv} \u8212- and {\i kacha ikuva}: the word {\i kacha} creates a restriction, limiting the procedure to precisely this form and no other. This would seem to preclude learning from a {\i kal vachomer} that a {\i get} could work for a {\i yevamah}. But can {\i kacha} truly block a {\i kal vachomer}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yom Kippur Example\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara addresses whether a term of restriction ({\i chukkah} or {\i kacha}) can entirely block a {\i kal vachomer} inference, and brings the example of {\i Yom Kippur} and the two goats. The Torah states that only the {\i goral} \u8212- the lottery \u8212- designates which goat becomes the {\i korban chatat} and which is sent to {\i Azazel}; {\i kriyat shem} (verbal declaration) does not work for this designation. Yet a {\i kal vachomer} argument is conceivable: in other cases where a {\i goral} does not work, {\i kriyat shem} does work; so where {\i goral} does work (on {\i Yom Kippur}), surely {\i kriyat shem} should work as well. The Torah needed a specific verse \u8212- {\i ve\u8217'asahu chatat, hagoral oseh chatat ve\u8217'ein hashem oseh chatat} \u8212- to exclude this. This proves that even when the Torah employs a limiting term like {\i chukkah}, the {\i hava amina} of a {\i kal vachomer} still arises and must be defeated by an explicit verse. Therefore, {\i kacha} alone is not sufficient to block the {\i kal vachomer} that a {\i get} might work for a {\i yevamah}; an explicit verse is needed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 That verse is {\i la} \u8212- {\i ve\u8217'chatav lah}, \u8220"and he shall write it for her.\u8221" {\i La v\u8217'lo le\u8217'yevamah}: the Torah\u8217's use of {\i lah} specifies that the {\i get} is written for his wife, not for a {\i yevamah}. This is the explicit scriptural exclusion that prevents a {\i get} from working for a {\i yevamah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A student raises the question: is not the word {\i lah} needed for the {\i drasha} that a {\i get} must be written {\i lishmah} \u8212- for the specific woman for whom it is intended? {\i Trei lah ketivi} \u8212- the word appears twice, and therefore both {\i drashot} can be sustained. A further question: does the second {\i lah} teach {\i la v\u8217'lo le\u8217'yevamah} or {\i la v\u8217'lo lah ve\u8217'lachavertah} \u8212- that a husband cannot divorce two wives with a single {\i get}? The Gemara resolves this by turning to the {\i parsha} of {\i chalitzah}: {\i naal, naal \u8212- naal ein midi achrina lo} \u8212- just as there is a {\i drasha} that only a written document severs an ordinary marriage ({\i sefer kortah ve\u8217'ein davar acher kortah}), so too in {\i chalitzah} the Torah specifies the shoe, teaching that only the shoe effects {\i chalitzah} and nothing else can substitute for it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The underlying theme of this entire {\i sugya}, as the discussion makes clear, is the {\i chachamim}\u8217's insistence on the primacy of {\i psukim} over {\i kal vachomer}. {\i Kal vachomer} is a powerful tool for deriving {\i halacha}, yet the Gemara works strenuously to ground each rule in explicit scriptural derivation rather than purely logical inference. The reason is that {\i yibum}, {\i gerushin}, {\i eishet ish}, and {\i amah ivriyah} are not simply variations on a single theme; each is a unique and distinct legal institution, and the Torah\u8217's precise language in each context reflects those distinctions. Logical analogy alone cannot safely be used to import rules from one domain into another, because the apparently similar categories are in fact genuinely different.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Chalitzah Shoe\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Whose Shoe Works\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The word {\i naal} in the {\i chalitzah} verse implies the {\i yavam}\u8217's own shoe \u8212- {\i na\u8217'alo}. But the {\i drasha} of {\i naal} (unmodified) teaches expansively: {\i naal shel kol adam} \u8212- any person\u8217's shoe may be used. The {\i yavam} need not own the shoe; it may be borrowed or provided by the {\i beit din}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shoe Exclusions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i braita} provides three exclusions. {\i Prat le\u8217'gadol she\u8217'ein yachol lehalech bo} \u8212- a shoe so large that the {\i yavam} cannot walk in it (the image of clown shoes makes the point) is invalid. {\i Prat le\u8217'katan she\u8217'eino chofeh rov raglo} \u8212- a shoe so small that it does not cover the majority of his foot is likewise invalid. The legal principle of {\i rubo ke\u8217'kulo} (the majority counts as the whole) is operative, though practically speaking a shoe that covers only the majority of the foot would still be uncomfortable; the criterion is a legal one. {\i Prat le\u8217'mesulyah she\u8217'ein lo akeiv} \u8212- certain sandal types lacking a proper heel cup (effectively, flip-flops) are also excluded.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Proper Fit\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The general principle is {\i na\u8217'alo hara\u8217'uy lo} \u8212- the shoe must be appropriately fit for him. A flip-flop, lacking the structural cup to hold the heel, is not considered a proper shoe in the relevant halakhic sense.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Veyibmah Teaches All\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Im kein nechtov kra naal \u8212- mai hanaal? Shema mina tartei}: had the Torah written only {\i naal}, the word {\i hanaal} (with the definite article, creating apparent redundancy) would be unnecessary. From this redundancy we derive two things: first, that {\i chalitzah} can only be effected through the shoe and no other mechanism; and second, all the specifications regarding the shoe\u8217's fitness and physical requirements are also derived. The detailed laws governing the {\i chalitzah} shoe are treated at length in {\i Masechet Yevamot}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Eved Ivri Intro\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Three Sources\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} now shifts to a new subject: {\i eved ivri}, the Jewish indentured servant. The laws of {\i eved ivri} appear three times in the Torah: in {\i Parshat Mishpatim}, in {\i Parshat Behar}, and in {\i Parshat Re\u8217'eh} in {\i Sefer Devarim}. Each passage has a different emphasis and focus. Biblical critics have argued that these three sections reflect different literary sources. Professor Aaron Koller of Yeshiva University has argued persuasively, drawing on both traditional sources and Ancient Near Eastern parallels, that the three sections are entirely compatible, mutually complementary parts of a unified legal system. A recurring failure of modern biblical scholarship, on this view, is to identify apparent tensions and attribute them to source pluralism without adequately examining analogous legal structures in the ancient world, which demonstrate that such complexity is entirely expected within a coherent legal code.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two Paths\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 There are two paths by which a Jew could become an {\i eved ivri}. The first is {\i mocher atzmo} \u8212- a man in financial distress voluntarily sells himself into indentured servitude. The second is {\i mecharu beit din} \u8212- a {\i beit din} sells a person as a servant when he has stolen and is too poor to repay the principal ({\i keren}) of what he stole. The thief can be sold to cover the {\i keren} but not the {\i keifel} (the double payment), since the {\i keifel} is a punitive fine rather than a restitutionary obligation, and one cannot be sold into servitude to pay a penal surcharge.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishnah Overview\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} states: {\i eved ivri nikne be\u8217'chesef uve\u8217'shtar} \u8212- an {\i eved ivri} is acquired through money or a document \u8212- {\i ve\u8217'koneh atzmo be\u8217'shanim} \u8212- and acquires his freedom through the passage of years (specifically six years, as the verse states {\i ve\u8217'avadcha sheish shanim}), {\i uve\u8217'yovel} \u8212- or through the arrival of the Jubilee year, which releases him even mid-term \u8212- {\i uve\u8217'giraon kesef} \u8212- or through a proportional monetary buyout of the remaining term.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Amah Ivriyah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} notes that the {\i amah ha\u8217'ivriyah} has a special status. She is a minor girl indentured by her father. (An adult woman apparently has no mechanism to indenture herself; similarly, minor boys have no such track. Only adult men and minor girls have defined paths into {\i eved ivri} status.) The {\i amah ha\u8217'ivriyah} acquires her freedom also upon the appearance of {\i simanim} \u8212- physical signs of puberty. She has an additional path not available to the adult male: the institution of {\i yi\u8217'ud}, whereby the {\i adon} or his son may marry her, transforming the indentured arrangement into a marital one. {\i Tosafot} return to this point repeatedly in comparing the acquisition of an {\i amah ha\u8217'ivriyah} to the mechanisms of marriage, and the comparison proves illuminating throughout the earlier part of the {\i perek}. From the father\u8217's perspective, the arrangement provides an opportunity for his daughter to marry into a prosperous family \u8212- a context that must be understood against the backdrop of its time, even as it engages obvious tensions with contemporary sensibilities regarding child labor and child marriage.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Eved Nirtza\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} further mentions the {\i eved nirtza}, the pierced-ear servant. After six years of service, if the servant declares that he loves his master and does not wish to go free, the {\i adon} brings him to the doorpost and pierces his ear with an awl. The servant then acquires the status of {\i eved nirtza}, which is distinct from ordinary {\i eved ivri} in that it does not expire after six years; instead, the {\i eved nirtza} is released only at the {\i yovel} year or upon the death of the {\i adon}. He cannot buy his freedom out early, and this status can only be acquired by a {\i mecharu beit din}, not a {\i mocher atzmo} \u8212- a distinction that will be explored further. The {\i midrash} notes that Rabbi Hertz, in his {\i Chumash} commentary, observes that the final law in Hammurabi\u8217's Code addresses what to do when a slave runs away, while the first law in {\i Parshat Mishpatim} addresses what to do when a slave wishes to remain \u8212- a juxtaposition that reflects the Torah\u8217's aspiration that the master\u8217's household be so humane and generous that the servant might genuinely prefer to stay.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Eved Ivri Kinyan\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Non-Jew Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara asks: {\i eved ivri nikna be\u8217'chesef \u8212- minalan?} How do we know that an {\i eved ivri} can be acquired with money? The derivation begins, perhaps surprisingly, with the case of a Jew sold to a non-Jew. The {\i pasuk} in {\i Parshat Behar} discusses a situation where a Jew has been purchased by a non-Jewish resident ({\i ger toshav}) and his relatives wish to redeem him: {\i mikesef miknato} \u8212- from the money of his purchase price, implying he was acquired through money in the first place. For a non-Jew, this is not remarkable, since all acquisitions by non-Jews work through {\i kesef}; the non-Jewish system of {\i kinyanim} operates primarily on a financial-monetary basis. But this still leaves the primary case \u8212- a Jew indentured to a fellow Jew \u8212- underived.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Amah Extension\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara turns to {\i amah ha\u8217'ivriyah}: {\i ve\u8217'hifdah} \u8212- a verse in the {\i parsha} of {\i amah ha\u8217'ivriyah} speaks of redeeming her freedom proportionally ({\i migra\u8217'at pidyonah}), implying she was originally acquired for money. The connection to {\i kesef} is thus established for the {\i amah ha\u8217'ivriyah}. This is bolstered by the analogy to marriage: just as she may be acquired ({\i mekadshah be\u8217'chesef}) through money for the purpose of {\i kiddushin}, so too she is acquired ({\i maknya be\u8217'chesef}) through money for the purpose of indenture. But this still does not directly establish the rule for the adult male {\i eved ivri}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Primary Source\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 For the primary case of an adult male {\i eved ivri} sold to a fellow Jew, the Gemara turns to {\i Devarim}: {\i ki yimacher lecha achicha ha\u8217'Ivri o ha\u8217'Ivriyah} \u8212- when your Jewish brother or sister is sold to you. The verse then says {\i ve\u8217'avadcha sheish shanim} \u8212- he shall serve you six years. {\i Makish eved Ivri le\u8217'Ivriyah} \u8212- the adult male is explicitly connected in this verse to the {\i amah ha\u8217'ivriyah}, and the rules derived for her (including acquisition by {\i kesef}) are extended to him. This establishes the {\i mecharu beit din} case.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mocher Atzmo\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mocher atzmo} case requires separate derivation. The Gemara invokes a {\i gezeirah shavah}: {\i yalif sachir sachir} \u8212- the word {\i sachir} (hired worker) appears in the {\i eved ivri} context both in {\i Vayikra} (where {\i Parshat Behar} describes {\i mocher atzmo}) and in {\i Devarim} (where the {\i mecharu beit din} case is described). This verbal analogy equates the two cases, extending the rules of acquisition by {\i kesef} to {\i mocher atzmo} as well.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 For the view that does not employ the {\i gezeirah shavah} of {\i sachir sachir}, the Gemara offers an alternative: {\i amar kra ve\u8217'chi tassig} \u8212- a verse in {\i Parshat Behar} beginning with \u8220"if his hand reaches\u8221" adds an additional gloss to the prior discussion. {\i Mosif al inyan rishon, veyilmad elyon mitachton} \u8212- the upper passage (the case of a Jew sold to a non-Jew) and the lower passage (the case of a Jew sold to a fellow Jew) appear in the same broader literary unit of {\i Parshat Behar}, and through contextual proximity \u8212- a principle the Gemara describes as {\i zeh ke\u8217'ein semichut parshiyot} \u8212- one learns from the other. This is a literary rather than strictly logical principle: two adjacent paragraphs about the same subject illuminate each other. It is looser than a true {\i heikesh} but functions similarly, and reflects a recognition that the Torah\u8217's arrangement of material is itself an interpretive tool.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Two Types Compared\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tanna Kamma View\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara presents a {\i braita} that enumerates the distinctions between {\i mocher atzmo} and {\i mecharu beit din} according to the {\i tanna kamma}. These distinctions are largely rooted in a careful reading of the {\i pshat} of the relevant {\i psukim}, and reflect a view that the two categories of {\i eved ivri} are genuinely different legal institutions, not merely two paths into the same status.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Six Year Limit\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Hamocher atzmo nimkar le\u8217'shesh ve\u8217'yater al shesh} \u8212- a person who sells himself into indentured servitude may agree to a term longer than six years. Since the arrangement is essentially a voluntary contractual business deal between two parties, the Torah\u8217's six-year limit does not apply to him; he may agree to whatever terms he chooses. {\i Mecharu beit din eino nimkar ela le\u8217'shesh} \u8212- but a person sold by the {\i beit din} against his will for theft can only be sold for a maximum of six years. This is a Torah-imposed limit on an involuntary punitive transaction; the Torah specifically circumscribes it with {\i ve\u8217'avadcha sheish shanim}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Nirtza Only\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Hamocher atzmo eino nirtza} \u8212- a person who voluntarily sold himself cannot have his ear pierced to become an {\i eved nirtza}. The institution of {\i eved nirtza} \u8212- and the entire emotional and social scenario of the slave who has genuinely found a home in his master\u8217's household \u8212- applies only to the {\i mecharu beit din} case, where the servant was thrust into the arrangement against his will and may, over six years, come genuinely to prefer it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Severance Gifts\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Mocher atzmo ein ma\u8217'anikim lo} \u8212- upon release from self-imposed servitude, no severance gifts are provided. {\i Mecharu beit din ma\u8217'anikim lo} \u8212- upon the completion of a {\i beit din}-imposed term, the {\i adon} is obligated to provide the freed servant with provisions: produce, food, and money ({\i ha\u8217'anek ta\u8217'anik lo}). The rationale is clear: the {\i mecharu beit din} servant was someone who was so poor that he could not even repay stolen goods. At the end of his term, his debt is discharged but he has nothing. Without this endowment, he faces an immediate return to destitution with no resources to restart his life \u8212- and perhaps temptation to steal again. The Torah\u8217's mandatory severance gift addresses this directly.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shfachah Rights\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Mocher atzmo \u8212- ein rabo moser lo shifcha kna\u8217'anit} \u8212- if a man sold himself voluntarily, his {\i adon} cannot provide him with a {\i shifcha kna\u8217'anit} to marry and produce children who would themselves become {\i avadim}. {\i Mecharu beit din \u8212- rabo moser lo shifcha kna\u8217'anit} \u8212- in the {\i beit din}-imposed case, the {\i adon} may provide him with a {\i shifcha kna\u8217'anit}, and the children born of that union would be {\i eved kna\u8217'ani}. This is the institution of {\i yelid bayit} \u8212- slaves born in the household \u8212- which the Torah explicitly addresses.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The distinction reflects the different legal characters of the two arrangements: {\i mecharu beit din}, being closer to a form of court-imposed servitude for criminal restitution, carries the fuller range of the Torah\u8217's slave laws, including the ability of the {\i adon} to arrange the servant\u8217's domestic life. {\i Mocher atzmo}, being closer to an employment agreement entered voluntarily, does not carry those features.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Elazar View\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Elazar} disagrees with all of these distinctions: {\i zeh ve\u8217'zeh eino nimkar ela le\u8217'shesh, zeh ve\u8217'zeh nirtza, ve\u8217'zeh ve\u8217'zeh ma\u8217'anikim lo, ve\u8217'zeh ve\u8217'zeh rabo moser lo shifcha kna\u8217'anit} \u8212- both categories are subject to a maximum six-year term, both can become {\i eved nirtza}, both receive severance gifts, and both may be provided with a {\i shifcha kna\u8217'anit}. {\i Rabbi Elazar} collapses the distinction between {\i mocher atzmo} and {\i mecharu beit din}, treating {\i eved ivri} as a single unified institution.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Amoraim Resolution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Amoraim} initially assumed that the {\i machloket} between the {\i tanna kamma} and {\i Rabbi Elazar} reflects a disagreement about whether to apply the {\i gezeirah shavah} of {\i sachir sachir}. On this reading, the {\i tanna kamma} does not apply the {\i gezeirah shavah} and therefore sees the two {\i psukim} \u8212- in {\i Vayikra} and {\i Devarim} \u8212- as addressing genuinely different cases ({\i mocher atzmo} and {\i mecharu beit din} respectively), each with its own distinct rules. {\i Rabbi Elazar}, who does apply the {\i gezeirah shavah}, equates the two and sees a unified system.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Amar Rav Tavyumi mishmeih de\u8217'Abaye}: the {\i Amoraim} reject this framework. In fact, both {\i tannaim} agree that the {\i gezeirah shavah} of {\i sachir sachir} is operative. Their dispute lies elsewhere \u8212- in the interpretation of specific verses. According to the {\i tanna kamma}, the verse {\i ve\u8217'avadcha sheish shanim} limits only {\i mecharu beit din}, not {\i mocher atzmo} ({\i zeh ve\u8217'lo mocher atzmo}). {\i Rabbi Elazar} reads {\i ve\u8217'avadcha lecha ve\u8217'lo le\u8217'yoresh} \u8212- the verse teaches that the {\i eved ivri}\u8217's obligation runs to the specific {\i adon} and does not transfer to his heirs \u8212- rather than imposing the six-year limit exclusively on {\i mecharu beit din}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Additional distinctions in the {\i braita} \u8212- such as {\i eved ivri} service not being inherited by the {\i adon}\u8217's son \u8212- will continue to be worked through in the following session, with the Gemara tracing how each {\i tanna} derives or does not derive each particular rule.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Kiddushin 15\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Three Torah Sources\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Daf Overview\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The present daf picks up at the bottom of Kiddushin 14b, at the line {\i mai lav beha kamipalgi}. We have moved on from the {\i kinyan} of a woman and a {\i yevama} \u8212- topics bound up with marriage, though addressed in the context of {\i kinyanim} \u8212- and we now turn to the {\i kinyanim} of other persons. We are not yet dealing with the acquisition of a non-Jewish slave ({\i eved kna\u8217'ani}), where the person is treated more fully as property while retaining human status; that discussion lies ahead. Rather, we are concerned with the {\i eved ivri} and the {\i amah ivriyah} \u8212- the Hebrew slave \u8212- where ownership is not full ownership of the person as such, but ownership {\i lema\u8217'aseh yadav}, of the person\u8217's labor.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} established that the way an {\i eved ivri} is acquired is through {\i kesef}. The {\i gemara} demonstrated that this applies both to the case of someone sold by the court and to someone who sells himself. Specifically: one can observe the {\i kesef} mechanism in the case of a daughter sold by her father (itself related to the case of {\i makhruhu beit din}, sold by the court, because the man stolen and sold to repay his theft), and one can transfer this to the case of {\i mokhher atzmo}, self-sale, through a {\i gezeirah shavah}. That background was established on the previous daf.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Three Contexts\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Torah discusses the sale of Jewish slaves in essentially three locations. The opening of {\i Mishpatim} addresses the {\i amah ivriyah} \u8212- the minor girl sold by her father \u8212- and {\i hanimkar bignivato} \u8212- the man sold because he burgled and cannot repay. {\i Devarim} continues the discussion of the male slave who goes free after six years, and the various laws attending his release, including the ladening with gifts. Finally, in {\i Behar} at the end of {\i Vayikra}, within the broader framework of {\i shmita} and the jubilee year, the Torah discusses a person who sells himself \u8212- either to a fellow Jew or to a non-Jew.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Categories & Link\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 These sources give rise to two principal categories: {\i makhruhu beit din}, the case in {\i Mishpatim} and {\i Devarim}, and {\i mokhher atzmo}, the case in {\i Behar}. The {\i gemara} asked: how do we know that the rules derived from {\i makhruhu beit din} \u8212- such as acquisition through {\i kesef} \u8212- carry over to the case of {\i mokhher atzmo}? The answer introduced was the {\i gezeirah shavah} of {\i sakhir sakhir}: both cases describe the slave as being like a hired hand, and this terminological link allows us to equate the two categories and transfer {\i halachot} between them. However, the {\i gemara} immediately complicated this by noting that if {\i sakhir sakhir} is disputed, we might need a different source. The {\i gemara} explored an alternative derivation based on the case of sale to a non-Jew. This is the background. The daf now focuses on the central question: do we in fact make this {\i sakhir sakhir} link between {\i mokhher atzmo} and {\i makhruhu beit din}?\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Tanna Kamma Distinc (III)tions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gezeirah Shavah Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} frames the discussion as a potential dispute over the {\i gezeirah shavah} of {\i sakhir sakhir}. The {\i tanna kamma} of a {\i baraita} cited at the end of the previous daf enumerated numerous differences between {\i makhruhu beit din} and {\i mokhher atzmo} as they appear in the {\i pesukim}. The {\i gemara} now asks: is this dispute really about whether to make the {\i gezeirah shavah} at all \u8212- that is, does the {\i tanna kamma} reject the equation of the two categories root and branch, while Rabbi Eliezer accepts it?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Baraita Overview\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i baraita} sets out the position of the {\i tanna kamma} alongside that of Rabbi Eliezer. The {\i tanna kamma} holds that a series of {\i halachot} mentioned in the Torah in connection with {\i makhruhu beit din} do not apply to {\i mokhher atzmo}: the six-year limit on servitude, the rite of ear-piercing when the slave declares {\i ahavti et adoni}, the obligation to laden the slave with gifts upon his release, and the master\u8217's permission to give the slave a non-Jewish maidservant with whom to cohabit and produce children (the case associated with {\i ahavti et adoni et ishti ve\u8217'et banai}). According to the {\i tanna kamma}, none of these apply to {\i mokhher atzmo}. Rabbi Eliezer holds that they do apply, and the {\i gemara} initially reasons that Rabbi Eliezer must be relying on the {\i sakhir sakhir} equation to transfer these {\i halachot} from one category to the other, while the {\i tanna kamma} does not accept that equation.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Sakhir Sakhir Debat (III)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tavyumi on Abaye\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now resists this framing. Rav Tavyumi cites Abaye: in fact, everyone may fundamentally hold of the {\i gezeirah shavah} of {\i sakhir sakhir}. The {\i sakhir sakhir} link may well be universally accepted \u8212- indeed, it was already used to establish that acquisition by {\i kesef} applies to {\i mokhher atzmo}, and perhaps other {\i halachot} are derived from it as well. The dispute between the {\i tanna kamma} and Rabbi Eliezer need not be about whether to make the equation at all. Rather, it may be about how to read specific limiting words within each of the relevant {\i pesukim}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Tanna Kamma Distinc (III)tio\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Six Year Limit\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} proceeds to work through each of the four differences in turn. It begins: what is the {\i tanna kamma}\u8217's reason for holding that {\i mokhher atzmo} is not sold for six years \u8212- that is, that the six-year limit on servitude applies only to {\i makhruhu beit din}? The answer: the Torah says regarding {\i makhruhu beit din}, {\i ve-avadcha sheish shanim} \u8212- \u8220"and he shall serve {\i you} six years.\u8221" The word {\i avadcha} \u8212- \u8220"your slave,\u8221" with the possessive suffix {\i cha} \u8212- functions as a limiting word ({\i miut}), restricting the law to this case and not extending it to {\i mokhher atzmo}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Sakhir Sakhir Debat (III)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Reframing Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This brings the {\i gemara} to its reframing of the entire dispute. The disagreement between {\i tanna kamma} and Rabbi Eliezer is not about whether the {\i sakhir sakhir gezeirah shavah} exists. Both may accept the fundamental equation. The question is instead how to interpret the limiting words embedded in the relevant {\i pesukim}. The {\i tanna kamma} reads each limiting word as excluding {\i mokhher atzmo}; Rabbi Eliezer reads each of those same words as conveying a different legal point while leaving {\i mokhher atzmo} included.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Tanna Kamma Distinc (III)tio\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Eliezer on Avadcha\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What does Rabbi Eliezer do with {\i avadcha}? He reads it not as excluding {\i mokhher atzmo} but as addressing the question of inheritance: {\i avadcha} \u8212- he serves {\i you}, not your heir. The {\i halacha} is that a slave does serve the master\u8217's son, since that is understood as a continuation of the father\u8217's ownership; but the slave would not be transferred to a brother, a father, or any other type of heir. This, Rabbi Eliezer argues, is the more natural reading of the word {\i avadcha} \u8212- it is restricting transmission to collateral heirs, not excluding the category of {\i mokhher atzmo}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then addresses the fact that the word {\i avadcha} appears more than once in the relevant passage. The phrase {\i mishneh sechar sakhir avadcha sheish shanim} \u8212- \u8220"a double wage of a hired hand, he served you six years\u8221" \u8212- is invoked. The {\i tanna kamma} might argue this second {\i avadcha} is an additional limiting phrase. Rabbi Eliezer responds that this second {\i avadcha} is not generating a new {\i halacha} at all; rather, it is the Torah addressing the master, reminding him how much work the slave performed on his behalf so that the master should not begrudge giving the parting gifts. It is a moral exhortation to the master, not a legal restriction.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ear-Piercing Limit\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} turns to the second difference: ear-piercing ({\i retzi\u8217'ah}). Why does the {\i tanna kamma} hold that {\i mokhher atzmo} cannot undergo the ear-piercing ceremony? The Torah says, in the context of {\i makhruhu beit din}: {\i ve-ratza adonav et ozno ba-martzei\u8217'a} \u8212- \u8220"his master shall pierce his ear with an awl.\u8221" The word {\i ozno} \u8212- \u8220"his ear,\u8221" with the possessive suffix \u8212- is the limiting word: {\i ozno shelo velo ozno shel mokhher atzmo}. It applies to this case and not to the case of self-sale. Thus the {\i tanna kamma} holds that {\i retzi\u8217'ah} is restricted to {\i makhruhu beit din}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ozno Analysis\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Eliezer, however, reads {\i ozno} not as a limiting word excluding {\i mokhher atzmo} but as the source for a {\i gezeirah shavah} \u8212- specifically, the link between the ear mentioned here and the ear mentioned in the context of the {\i metzora}, where the ritual is performed on the right ear. Rabbi Eliezer taught in a {\i baraita}: how do we know that the ear-piercing of the slave is performed on the right ear? Because it says here {\i ozen} and it says there (by the {\i metzora}) {\i ozen}, and just as there it is the right ear, so too here it is the right ear. This is why the Torah wrote {\i ozen} \u8212- to generate the {\i gezeirah shavah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This raises an obvious objection: if the {\i gezeirah shavah} is derived from the word {\i ozen} (without the suffix), then the limiting function was supposed to be carried by {\i ozno} (with the suffix {\i vav}). The {\i gemara} addresses this: if that were so, the Torah could simply have written {\i ozen} \u8212- why write {\i ozno}? The additional letter tells us something, and the {\i tanna kamma} says it excludes {\i mokhher atzmo}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Eliezer would reply that {\i ozno} is needed for a different exclusion: {\i ozno velo oznah} \u8212- his ear and not her ear, meaning the rite applies to the male {\i eved ivri} sold by {\i beit din} and not to the {\i amah ivriyah} sold by her father. The {\i tanna kamma} can extract the exclusion of the {\i amah} from elsewhere \u8212- from {\i v\u8217'im yomar ha\u8217'eved}, where the word {\i eved} (masculine) already excludes the female \u8212- and so the {\i vav} of {\i ozno} is free to exclude {\i mokhher atzmo}. Alternatively, the {\i tanna kamma} derives \u8220"while he is still a slave\u8221" from {\i eved}, and derives \u8220"not the {\i amah}\u8221" from {\i ha\u8217'eved} (the definite article), leaving {\i ozno} to exclude {\i mokhher atzmo}. The back-and-forth turns on precisely how many exclusions each word can carry and which readings of the text are considered operative.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gifting Limited\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} arrives at the third difference: the obligation to laden the slave with gifts ({\i ha\u8217'aneik ta\u8217'anik lo}) upon his release. What is the {\i tanna kamma}\u8217's reason for holding that this does not apply to {\i mokhher atzmo}? The Torah says by {\i makhruhu beit din}: {\i ha\u8217'aneik ta\u8217'anik lo} \u8212- \u8220"you shall surely laden him.\u8221" The word {\i lo} \u8212- \u8220"him\u8221" \u8212- is a limiting word: {\i lo velo le-mokhher atzmo}. It applies to this one and not to the person who sold himself.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is a striking observation: the man sold by {\i beit din} was a {\i ganav}, a thief; yet he receives the parting gifts, while the person who chose to sell himself does not. The conceptual distinction behind this collection of differences is worth pausing on, though the {\i gemara} does not spell it out here.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Wages Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then raises a complication with a different reading of {\i lo}. One might suggest that {\i lo} comes not to exclude {\i mokhher atzmo} but to exclude the heirs: {\i lo velo leyorshav} \u8212- \u8220"to him and not to his heirs.\u8221" If the master dies before giving the gifts, his estate has no obligation; the gifts are personal to the slave. But the {\i gemara} challenges this: the Torah itself describes the slave as a {\i sakhir}, a hired hand \u8212- and wages owed to a hired hand do pass to his heirs. The {\i gemara} reasons: just as a worker\u8217's wages go to his heirs, so too here, {\i mah sakhir ploni leyorshav af hai ploni leyorshav}. The {\i gemara} is thus unwilling to read {\i lo} as excluding the heirs, since the Torah\u8217's own framing of the slave\u8217's work as hired labor would require the gifts to be treated as wages and to devolve to the heirs.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Interestingly, the Gra excises this entire passage. Rashi has it, Tosafot has it, but the Gra evidently holds that the gifts are not wages the slave has earned and to which he is entitled; they are a special Torah-mandated award. Therefore, they would not pass to the heirs. But our {\i gemara} insists: the Torah says the slave worked for you like a hired hand, he worked double, and therefore these gifts are treated as wages that would pass to the heirs.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Lo Creditor Reading\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} therefore proposes a different understanding of {\i lo}: {\i lo velo le-ba\u8217'al chovo} \u8212- \u8220"to him and not to his creditor.\u8221" Unsurprisingly, a man in this situation likely has other creditors. One might have thought \u8212- given that we generally hold like Rabbi Natan \u8212- that a creditor of the slave could approach the master directly and demand that the gifts be transferred straight to him, bypassing the slave. As the {\i braita} of Rabbi Natan teaches: if Reuven owes Shimon a hundred, and Shimon owes Levi a hundred, Levi can go directly to Reuven and demand payment \u8212- and the verse {\i venatan la\u8217'asher asham lo} (\u8220"and he shall give it to the one to whom he is liable\u8221") supports this. Tosafot has an extended discussion about whether we {\i pasken} that way.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} says {\i lo} comes to tell us: in this case, the creditor cannot make that demand. The gifts must pass through the slave\u8217's own hands. There is a significance to the act of giving and receiving these gifts \u8212- {\i lo}, specifically to him \u8212- and the creditor cannot short-circuit that by seizing them from the master directly.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Eliezer, by contrast, uses {\i lo} to address this creditor problem rather than to exclude {\i mokhher atzmo}. He thereby applies the parting gift obligation to {\i mokhher atzmo} as well. The {\i tanna kamma}, who uses {\i lo} to exclude {\i mokhher atzmo}, must explain differently why the creditor cannot intercept: he simply does not hold of Rabbi Natan\u8217's principle altogether \u8212- {\i be\u8217'alma nami lo sevira lan ke-Rabbi Natan} \u8212- and therefore needs no exclusion.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Maidservant Limit\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The fourth and final difference: the master\u8217's ability to give the slave a non-Jewish maidservant ({\i shifcha kena\u8217'anit}) with whom to cohabit and produce children. The Torah says by {\i makhruhu beit din}: {\i im adonav yiten lo isha} \u8212- \u8220"if his master gives {\i him} a wife.\u8221" Again the word {\i lo} is the limiting word: {\i lo velo le-mokhher atzmo}. The master can do this only in the case of {\i makhruhu beit din}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Double Wage & Force\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Eliezer reads {\i lo} here differently: {\i lo be\u8217'al korcho} \u8212- even against his will. That is, {\i lo} is not excluding {\i mokhher atzmo}; it is telling us that the master can compel the slave to cohabit with the maidservant even if he is unwilling. This is obviously morally troubling from any contemporary vantage point, but the {\i gemara} proceeds with the analysis.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 How does Rabbi Eliezer know the master can compel this? From a different {\i pasuk}, as a {\i braita} teaches: {\i ki mishneh sechar sakhir avadcha sheish shanim} \u8212- \u8220"for a double wage of a hired hand, he served you six years.\u8221" What does \u8220"double wage\u8221" mean? A regular hired hand works only during the day; an {\i eved ivri} works both day and night, thus performing a double wage\u8217's worth of labor. Does this literally mean the slave works around the clock? The {\i gemara} itself rejects this: the Torah says {\i ki tov lo imach} \u8212- that it should be good for him in your household, including equal provisions and housing. Rabbi Yitzchak therefore explains: working \u8220"at night\u8221" means the slave works for the master\u8217's benefit at night through cohabiting with the maidservant to produce slave children for the master\u8217's household. That is the \u8220"night work\u8221" \u8212- and it is this {\i pasuk} that tells Rabbi Eliezer you may compel him ({\i be\u8217'al korcho}).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i tanna kamma} responds: if that were the only source, I would have read {\i mishneh sechar sakhir} as establishing only that the cohabitation can occur \u8212- but perhaps only if the slave is willing. The word {\i lo} therefore comes to tell you that it can be done even against his will. And since {\i lo} carries that legal force, it cannot simultaneously serve to exclude {\i mokhher atzmo}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot raises the question: if {\i lo} tells us it can be done against his will, what is {\i mishneh sechar sakhir} teaching? Tosafot answers \u8212- in parallel to what was said earlier about {\i hartza\u8217'at adon} \u8212- that it is not generating a new {\i halacha} but rather reminding the master of the slave\u8217's substantial work, so that the master does not begrudge giving the parting gifts. According to the {\i tanna kamma}, by contrast, {\i mishneh sechar sakhir} does establish the permissibility of compelled cohabitation, and {\i lo} restricts the entire provision to {\i makhruhu beit din}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Sakhir Sakhir Debat (III)e\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Fundamental Link\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having worked through all four differences, the {\i gemara} now states its conclusion explicitly: perhaps everyone accepts the {\i sakhir sakhir gezeirah shavah} as a matter of fundamental principle. The differences between the {\i tanna kamma} and Rabbi Eliezer are not about whether the two categories are linked, but about how to read the specific limiting words in each relevant {\i pasuk}. Each difference reflects a disagreement over whether a given word ({\i avadcha}, {\i ozno}, {\i lo}) is excluding {\i mokhher atzmo} or conveying some other legal or homiletical point.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If that is so, then the earlier framing of the dispute \u8212- that the {\i tanna kamma} does not make {\i sakhir sakhir} and Rabbi Eliezer does \u8212- is not sustained. The {\i gemara} therefore asks: who, then, is the {\i tanna} who fundamentally does not hold of the {\i sakhir sakhir gezeirah shavah}?\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Elazar ben Yaakov\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 V\u8217'shav Argument\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} answers: it is the {\i tanna} of the following {\i braita}. The verse {\i veshav el mishpachto ve\u8217'el achuzat avotav yashuv} \u8212- \u8220"and he shall return to his family and to his ancestral inheritance he shall return\u8221" \u8212- appears in {\i Vayikra} in the section dealing with {\i mokhher atzmo} and speaks of his going free at the {\i yovel}. Rabbi Elazar ben Yaakov asks: to which case does this verse refer? If it refers to {\i mokhher atzmo}, that is already the subject of the entire {\i parsha} \u8212- no further verse is needed. If it refers to the {\i nirtza}, the slave who had his ear pierced and chose permanent servitude, that too is already covered elsewhere (as the {\i gemara} will explain). The verse must therefore be speaking of {\i makhruhu beit din} \u8212- a person sold by {\i beit din} who has served two or three years (not exactly two or three, but prior to completing his term) before the {\i yovel} arrives and frees him.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 From this derivation \u8212- the fact that a separate verse is needed to include {\i makhruhu beit din} in the {\i yovel} freedom \u8212- the {\i gemara} infers that Rabbi Elazar ben Yaakov does not hold of the {\i sakhir sakhir gezeirah shavah}. If he did, the fundamental equation of the two categories would have already implied that {\i makhruhu beit din} is freed at {\i yovel}, since {\i mokhher atzmo} is. The very need for an independent verse to achieve that inclusion shows that for Rabbi Elazar ben Yaakov, the categories are not fundamentally linked.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yovel Exception\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} once again pushes back. Rav Nachman bar Yitzchak says: one can accept {\i sakhir sakhir} and still require an additional verse for the {\i yovel} case. Even if we equate the categories in principle, one might have thought that {\i makhruhu beit din} \u8212- sold because he committed an {\i aveira}, because he was a {\i ganav} \u8212- should not benefit from the {\i yovel} release. The {\i yovel} provides a kind of extraordinary freedom that breaks the normal schedule of servitude; perhaps a person who landed in servitude through criminal behavior should be penalized and not receive this benefit. {\i Hu de-avid issura, eima nikansei.} Therefore, even with a {\i sakhir sakhir} link, an additional verse ({\i veshavtem ish el achuzato}) is needed to tell us that {\i makhruhu beit din} does indeed go free at {\i yovel}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two V\u8217'shavtem\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then traces the source for the {\i nirtza}\u8217's inclusion in {\i yovel} freedom. A {\i baraita} is cited: {\i veshavtem ish el achuzato ve-ish el mishpachto tashuvu} \u8212- \u8220"and you shall return, each man to his inheritance, and each man to his family you shall return.\u8221" This is a second {\i veshavtem} verse, distinct from the first. Applying the same logic as before: if it speaks of {\i mokhher atzmo}, that is already clear from the entire {\i parsha}. If it speaks of {\i makhruhu beit din}, that was already covered by the first {\i veshavtem} verse. Therefore this verse must be addressing the {\i nirtza} \u8212- the slave who chose to remain after his six years were up and had his ear pierced \u8212- specifying that even he goes free at the {\i yovel}, if the {\i yovel} arrives two or three years (loosely speaking) into his extended servitude.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The word {\i ish} in this second verse is what signals the {\i nirtza}: {\i eizeh davar she-noheg be-ish ve-eino noheg be-isha} \u8212- what is a {\i halacha} that applies to a man and not to a woman in the context of slaves? It is {\i retzi\u8217'ah}, ear-piercing. We already established that {\i retzi\u8217'ah} applies only to the male. The verse therefore is addressing the {\i nirtza}, including him in {\i yovel} freedom.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Necessity Argument\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} establishes that both verses are necessary. It was necessary to write the inclusion of {\i makhruhu beit din} because one might have said: a person sold by {\i beit din} did not choose this situation and is presumably still seeking his freedom; he may not have had the opportunity to go free, and the {\i yovel} ought to release him. But perhaps the {\i nirtza} is different \u8212- he chose to remain, he declared {\i ahavti et adoni}, and perhaps he should not be freed at {\i yovel} as a kind of penalty for forgoing his earlier freedom. Therefore we needed a verse to include the {\i nirtza}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Conversely, it was necessary to write the inclusion of the {\i nirtza} because one might have said: a person sold by {\i beit din} who has not yet completed even the basic six-year term has not \u8220"served out\u8221" his time. Perhaps it would be unfair to the master for {\i yovel} to free him before he has provided the full labor for which the master acquired him. Therefore we needed a verse to include {\i makhruhu beit din} as well. {\i Tzricha.}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Reconciliation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The two {\i veshavtem} verses thus divide their labor: one includes {\i makhruhu beit din}, one includes {\i nirtza}. Each is necessary precisely because there is an independent reason to have excluded the case it covers.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 L\u8217'olam Resolution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} closes this section by addressing the apparent contradiction between {\i va-avado le\u8217'olam} \u8212- \u8220"he shall serve him forever\u8221" \u8212- and the {\i veshavtem} verse that grants the {\i nirtza} freedom at {\i yovel}. If we had only {\i le\u8217'olam}, we would understand it literally: permanent servitude, no {\i yovel} release. But {\i veshavtem} overrides this. Conversely, if we had only {\i veshavtem} telling us the {\i nirtza} goes free, without {\i le\u8217'olam}, we would not know the duration of his servitude between the piercing and the {\i yovel}. We might have thought: just as the initial servitude was six years, so too the post-piercing servitude would be six years, and at the end of that additional six years he would go free regardless of {\i yovel}. {\i Le\u8217'olam} therefore tells us: absent {\i yovel}, he works indefinitely \u8212- {\i olamo shel yovel}, \u8220"forever\u8221" in the {\i yovel} context means until the next {\i yovel}. Both verses are thus required: {\i le\u8217'olam} to tell us there is no fixed endpoint short of {\i yovel}, and {\i veshavtem} to tell us that {\i yovel} does in fact release him.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rabbi Non-Jew Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi\u8217's Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 At the very end of 15a, the {\i gemara} finally identifies a {\i tanna} who clearly does not hold of the {\i sakhir sakhir} equation: it is Rabbi (Judah the Prince). A {\i braita} at the top of 15b cites the verse {\i ve-im lo yiga\u8217'el ba-eleh} \u8212- \u8220"and if he is not redeemed through these\u8221" \u8212- in the context of someone who has sold himself to a non-Jew. Rabbi states: {\i ba-eleh hu niga\u8217'el ve-eino niga\u8217'el be-sheish} \u8212- through these means (i.e., redemption by relatives) he may be redeemed, but his servitude to the non-Jew does not terminate automatically at the end of six years. This is surprising: one would think that a non-Jew should have {\i less} of a claim over a Hebrew slave than a Jewish master, not more. Yet Rabbi reads the verse as teaching that the six-year release that applies to self-sale to a Jew does not apply to self-sale to a non-Jew.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kal Vachomer\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi supports this with a {\i kal va-chomer}. When a Jew sells himself to another Jew, there is no obligation on relatives to redeem him ({\i ge\u8217'ulat kerovim} does not apply), and yet his servitude ends after six years. By a {\i kal va-chomer}, when a Jew sells himself to a non-Jew \u8212- where {\i ge\u8217'ulat kerovim} does apply (the relatives have a {\i mitzvah} to redeem him and, provided the non-Jew is subject to Jewish authority, can compel the transaction), and where in addition the slave has the right {\i megara pidyono ve-yotzei}, to reduce his outstanding term and go free \u8212- should he not certainly be freed after six years? The argument is compelling: greater protections exist in the case of sale to a non-Jew, so surely the six-year rule should apply there too. Nevertheless, Rabbi says: {\i talmud lomar ba-eleh, ba-eleh hu niga\u8217'el ve-eino niga\u8217'el be-sheish} \u8212- the verse teaches that his only avenue for release is redemption through relatives, not the passage of six years.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Nachman Counter\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} again applies the now-familiar move: Rav Nachman bar Yitzchak says that even Rabbi could in principle hold of the {\i sakhir sakhir gezeirah shavah} between {\i mokhher atzmo} and {\i makhruhu beit din}. The reason Rabbi does not make the {\i kal va-chomer} to extend the six-year release to sale-to-a-non-Jew is not that he rejects the fundamental equation of the two main categories \u8212- it is that a verse explicitly creates an exception for this specific case.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yigalenu Limit\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} identifies that verse: {\i yigalenu}, \u8220"one shall redeem him.\u8221" This word restricts the applicability of {\i ge\u8217'ulat kerovim} to the case of sale to a non-Jew and excludes it from the case of self-sale to a Jew. If {\i sakhir sakhir} fundamentally equates self-sale to a Jew and sale by {\i beit din}, and if {\i ge\u8217'ulat kerovim} applied to self-sale to a Jew just as it applies to sale to a non-Jew, then why did Rabbi argue in his {\i kal va-chomer} that there is no {\i ge\u8217'ulat kerovim} by sale to a Jew? Because {\i yigalenu} restricts the redemption-by-relatives provision specifically to the non-Jew case and excludes self-sale to a Jew. Rabbi thus uses {\i ba-eleh} to exclude the six-year release in the non-Jew case, and {\i yigalenu} to exclude {\i ge\u8217'ulat kerovim} from the Jew case \u8212- and he can hold {\i sakhir sakhir} throughout.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Yossi & Akiva\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Braita Intro\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having examined Rabbi\u8217's position, the {\i gemara} asks: who is the {\i tanna} who disagrees with Rabbi \u8212- that is, who holds that a person sold to a non-Jew {\i does} go free after six years? The answer: Rabbi Yossi HaGlili and Rabbi Akiva. A {\i braita} is cited presenting their dispute.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yossi View\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yossi HaGlili holds: {\i ba-eleh l\u8217'shichrur} \u8212- when it is the relatives who redeem the slave from the non-Jew, the slave goes free. {\i Beshar kol adam l\u8217'shibbud} \u8212- but when some other person (not a relative) redeems him, the slave does not go free. The stranger can force the non-Jewish master to sell the slave (compel the transaction, since the non-Jew is under Jewish authority), but the redeemed slave then must work for this stranger-redeemer \u8212- he has effectively been sold to a new Jewish master \u8212- until the six-year term or {\i yovel}. The value of this is still real: the slave is out of the hands of a non-Jew. But he does not immediately regain his freedom.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Akiva View\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Akiva holds the mirror image: {\i ba-eleh l\u8217'shibbud} \u8212- when it is the relatives who redeem him, the slave must work for them. {\i Beshar kol adam l\u8217'shichrur} \u8212- but when a stranger redeems him, he goes free immediately. This is counterintuitive: relatives, who have a {\i mitzvah} to redeem and can compel the non-Jew, nevertheless end up holding the slave in servitude, while a stranger who redeems him out of pure goodwill grants him freedom? The logic will emerge from the {\i gemara}\u8217's discussion of {\i svara}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Three Geulahs\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} traces how the dispute arises from the text. The relevant passage contains three distinct categories of redemption: {\i o dodo o ven dodo yigalenu} \u8212- the uncle or cousin redeems him; this is {\i ge\u8217'ulat kerovim}. {\i O hisiga yado} \u8212- or his own hand reaches the means; this is {\i ge\u8217'ulat atzmo}, self-redemption. {\i Ve-nig\u8217'al} \u8212- and he shall be redeemed; this functions as a third category, {\i ge\u8217'ulat acherim}, redemption by others. The fact that this third grammatical form appears in the verse establishes that a stranger may compel the redemption and the non-Jew must accept it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yossi Reasoning\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 With {\i ge\u8217'ulat atzmo} \u8212- self-redemption \u8212- positioned in the middle between the two, the question becomes: to which of the flanking categories is {\i ge\u8217'ulat atzmo} connected, and which connection determines who goes free? Rabbi Yossi HaGlili holds {\i mikra nidrash lefanav} \u8212- the verse is interpreted by reference to what precedes it. The earlier category, {\i ge\u8217'ulat kerovim}, is linked to {\i ge\u8217'ulat atzmo}. Just as when one redeems oneself one goes free, so too when relatives redeem: {\i mah ge\u8217'ulat atzmo le-shichrur af ge\u8217'ulat kerovim le-shichrur}. Others ({\i ge\u8217'ulat acherim}), not linked to {\i ge\u8217'ulat atzmo}, result in servitude.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Akiva Reasoning\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Akiva holds {\i mikra nidrash le-acharav} \u8212- the verse is interpreted by reference to what follows it. The later category, {\i ge\u8217'ulat acherim}, is linked to {\i ge\u8217'ulat atzmo}. Just as self-redemption leads to freedom, so too redemption by others: {\i mah ge\u8217'ulat atzmo le-shichrur af ge\u8217'ulat acherim le-shichrur}. Relatives ({\i ge\u8217'ulat kerovim}), not linked to {\i ge\u8217'ulat atzmo}, result in servitude.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ba\u8217'eleh Limiting\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} asks: if the entire debate is about which flanking category is linked to {\i ge\u8217'ulat atzmo}, why do they invoke the word {\i ba-eleh} at all? The {\i gemara} explains: without {\i ba-eleh}, one might say the verse is read in both directions \u8212- both the earlier and later categories are linked to {\i ge\u8217'ulat atzmo}. Then {\i all} forms of redemption would result in freedom. {\i Ba-eleh} tells us this is not so \u8212- only some of the categories enjoy the same status as {\i ge\u8217'ulat atzmo}. The question then is which ones, and the answer is not derivable from {\i ba-eleh} itself.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Svara Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This leads to the {\i gemara}\u8217's conclusion: the debate between Rabbi Yossi HaGlili and Rabbi Akiva is ultimately grounded in {\i svara} \u8212- in reasoning and logic \u8212- rather than in a purely textual disagreement about the direction of interpretation. {\i Ba-eleh} establishes that not all categories are equal, and the reading direction ({\i lefanav} or {\i le-acharav}) may reflect the underlying {\i svara} rather than generating it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yossi Svara\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yossi HaGlili\u8217's {\i svara}: it makes sense that redemption by others results in servitude. {\i De-i amrat le-shichrur havay memna ve-lo parki} \u8212- if you say that a stranger\u8217's redemption leads to freedom, then strangers will simply not bother redeeming the slave. Strangers have no {\i mitzvah} to redeem and no self-interest in doing so; offering them the \u8220"reward\u8221" of enslaving the redeemed person to themselves gives them an incentive to act and at least gets the slave out of non-Jewish hands. The relatives, who have an obligation, will redeem \u8212- and the slave goes free. Thus the system works: relatives are incentivized by obligation, others by self-interest, and the slave ultimately benefits.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Akiva Svara\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Akiva\u8217's {\i svara}: it makes sense that redemption by relatives results in servitude. {\i De-i amrat le-shichrur kol yoma ve-yoma azil u-mezabin nafsheh} \u8212- if relatives\u8217' redemption leads to freedom, and relatives are obligated to redeem, then a penniless person with wealthy relatives would repeatedly sell himself to a non-Jew and pocket the sale price, knowing his relatives are obligated to free him at their expense. This is precisely the kind of abuse of system that must be prevented: like the famous example of offering bounties for pests and thereby incentivizing people to breed the very pests one seeks to eliminate. Servitude to relatives therefore deters the abuse. Strangers, by contrast, have no obligation \u8212- their redemption is wholly voluntary and unexpected \u8212- so if one chooses to act out of pure generosity and the slave goes free, there is no systemic incentive for abuse.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rabbi & Chachamim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Chiya Quote\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Chiya bar Abba cites Rabbi Yochanan: the dispute described above \u8212- between one view that some redeemers free the slave and others subject him to servitude \u8212- is the position of Rabbi Yossi HaGlili and Rabbi Akiva. But there is a third view.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chachamim View\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Chachamim hold {\i hakol le-shichrur} \u8212- in all cases, whether relatives or strangers redeem the slave, the result is freedom. There is no distinction between categories of redeemer; every redemption from the non-Jew results in the slave going free.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Chachamim in this context is Rabbi himself \u8212- not the same position he held in the earlier {\i braita} regarding the six-year release, but specifically on this question of who is {\i Chachamim} here. Rabbi does not use {\i ba-eleh} as a limiting word in the context of {\i ge\u8217'ulat kerovim} versus {\i ge\u8217'ulat acherim}. He uses {\i ba-eleh} entirely for his earlier teaching \u8212- that servitude to a non-Jew does not end at six years. Therefore the verse about all three categories ({\i ge\u8217'ulat kerovim}, {\i ge\u8217'ulat atzmo}, {\i ge\u8217'ulat acherim}) is read in both directions, both forward and backward, meaning {\i all} forms of redemption are equated with self-redemption \u8212- and all result in freedom. {\i Umikra nidrash bein lefanav bein le-acharav.}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 V\u8217'yatza Yovel\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What does Rabbi then do with the verse {\i ve-yatza bi-shnat ha-yovel} \u8212- \u8220"he shall go free in the year of the jubilee\u8221"? This verse, which speaks of {\i yovel} freedom for the slave sold to a non-Jew, is applied by Rabbi to a specific circumstance: a non-Jew who is under Jewish control ({\i she-yeshno tachat yadecha}). Because such a non-Jew is subject to our authority, one might think we would exercise that authority to free the slave even before {\i yovel} \u8212- perhaps immediately. The verse therefore tells us: even in that case, we act within the law. We do not abuse our position of power; we wait for {\i yovel}. Alternatively, one might ask whether the {\i yovel} release applies even to a non-Jew {\i not} under our control \u8212- but that question answers itself: we cannot impose our laws on someone beyond our reach. The verse therefore addresses only the case where the non-Jew is within our authority, and its teaching is that we nonetheless act with restraint and do not deploy that authority to override the legal framework.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Kiddushin 16\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Kinyanim Overview\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Progression Overview\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Today\u8217's daf is 16 and we have moved on from the discussion of how a woman is acquired or acquires herself, and how a {\i yevama} represents a similar type of scenario. We now turn to other acquisitions, and this {\i perek} is really about {\i kinyanim} broadly. The progression moves in stages: we begin with the {\i kinyan} of a woman through marriage, which, as we have discussed, may have evolved from something more purely {\i kinyan}-based to something more centered on personal status. From there we turn to the {\i kinyan} related to a Jewish slave, the {\i eved Ivri}, and then we will proceed to the {\i eved Kena\u8217'ani}, and eventually to animals and inanimate objects.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Eved Ivri Concept\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i kinyan} of a woman through marriage is followed by the {\i kinyan} of the {\i eved Ivri}, which is emphatically not an ownership of the person as property, but rather an ownership of the rights to the person\u8217's labor \u8212- {\i ma\u8217'asei yedeihem}. The {\i eved Kena\u8217'ani}, by contrast, does involve ownership of the person as property, though even there the Torah\u8217's innovation is that the slave retains recognition as a human being \u8212- a point not to be taken for granted in the ancient world. The {\i eved Ivri} thus occupies a middle position: the master acquires the right to compel labor, but does not acquire the person as an object of property.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Shtar Kinyan Source\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishna Teaching\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Mishna} already established {\i kesef} as a mode of acquiring an {\i eved Ivri}, and the Gemara derived the source for that. We now turn to the second mode: {\i shtar}. The Gemara on 16a, five lines from the top, asks: {\i uv\u8217'shtar \u8212- minalan?} Where do we derive that an {\i eved Ivri} or {\i amah Ivriyah} can be acquired through a document?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Amar Ulla amar kra: im acheres yikach lo} \u8212- the verse, speaking of the {\i amah Ivriyah} whose master takes an additional wife, says that the master may not diminish the first wife\u8217's {\i she\u8217'era kesuta ve\u8217'onata}, the conjugal and material obligations due to her. The Gemara uses this verse to make a comparison: {\i hekisha hakatuv le\u8217'acheres} \u8212- the Torah links the original acquisition of the girl as a slave to the subsequent taking of a wife. Just as a wife can be acquired through a {\i shtar} \u8212- {\i mah acheres maknya b\u8217'shtar} \u8212- so too an {\i amah Ivriyah} is acquired through a {\i shtar} \u8212- {\i af amah Ivriyah maknya b\u8217'shtar}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Marriage vs Slave\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This analogy immediately raises a question. The comparison is between acquiring an {\i amah Ivriyah} and acquiring a wife through {\i kiddushin}. But who writes the {\i shtar}? This is not a trivial matter. Consider the scenario: I am purchasing a young girl from her father. Do I write a {\i shtar} saying {\i bitcha knuyah li} \u8212- your daughter is acquired by me \u8212- and hand it to the father? Or does the father write {\i biti mechura lach} \u8212- my daughter is sold to you \u8212- and give it to me?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 By {\i kinyan karka}, the seller writes the {\i shtar}. The logic is clear: a {\i shtar} can serve as proof of a transaction, and the purchaser is the one who needs that proof. So when land is sold, the seller writes {\i sadi mechura lach}. By contrast, in {\i kiddushin}, it is the husband who writes the document \u8212- {\i harei at mekudeshet li}, or in the context of the father\u8217's daughter, {\i harei bitcha mekudeshet li}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two Shtar Models\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 There are thus two distinct models. The first is the {\i shtar mechira} model, where the seller writes the document, as by the sale of land. The second is the {\i shtar kiddushin} model, where the acquirer writes it. These are not merely formal differences \u8212- they reflect two fundamentally different conceptions of what the transaction is. If the {\i amah Ivriyah} acquisition is modeled on {\i kiddushei ishah}, it should be the master writing the {\i shtar}. If it is modeled on the sale of property, it should be the father writing it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The relevance of this distinction to the derivation is direct: the verse {\i im acheres yikach lo} compares the sale of the daughter to the taking of a wife through {\i kiddushin}. This comparison is compelling only if the same party writes the {\i shtar} in both cases \u8212- namely, the acquirer. If the father writes the {\i shtar}, the analogy to {\i kiddushin} collapses, and the basis for deriving {\i kinyan shtar} from {\i im acheres yikach lo} disappears entirely.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Huna vs Chisda\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara records a dispute on precisely this point. {\i Shtar amah Ivriyah \u8212- mi kotvo?} Rav Huna says: {\i adon kotvo}, the master writes it. Rav Chisda says: {\i av kotvo}, the father writes it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 According to Rav Huna, who holds that the master writes the {\i shtar}, the comparison to {\i kiddushei ishah} is apt and the derivation works cleanly. According to Rav Chisda, who holds that the father writes it \u8212- reflecting the property-sale model \u8212- the derivation from {\i im acheres yikach lo} cannot stand. A different source must be found. It is to supply that alternative source that Rav Acha bar Yaakov introduces the verse {\i lo tetzei k\u8217'tzeit ha\u8217'avadim}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Lo Tetzei Derivation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Lo Tetzei Meaning\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Lo tetzei k\u8217'tzeit ha\u8217'avadim} \u8212- she shall not go free like slaves go free. The plain sense of this verse, as Chazal understand it, is that a Jewish slave \u8212- whether male or female \u8212- does not go free when the master knocks out an eye or destroys a limb, as an {\i eved Kena\u8217'ani} does. For an {\i eved Kena\u8217'ani}, loss of an eye or a tooth triggers freedom. For an {\i eved Ivri}, it does not.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This may seem unjust, but there is an important halakhic consideration. If the master knocks out the eye of a Jewish slave, the slave does not go free, but the master does owe substantial monetary compensation for the injury. The slave may then use that compensation to purchase their own freedom. The Torah\u8217's arrangement may therefore actually benefit the slave: rather than losing a few remaining years of service, they receive significant compensation for the injury \u8212- and can use part of it to redeem themselves.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might ask: how do we know the verse is speaking about {\i eved Kena\u8217'ani} when it says \u8220"like slaves go free\u8221"? This requires some discussion, but the Gemara proceeds on this assumption.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Drasha Logic\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i drasha} built on this verse is elegant in structure: {\i lo tetzei k\u8217'tzeit ha\u8217'avadim \u8212- aval nikneit hi k\u8217'kinyan avadim}. She does not go {\i out} like slaves go out, but she is {\i acquired} like slaves are acquired. Since the verse is specifically about going free, the phrase \u8220"like slaves\u8221" must be doing additional work \u8212- pointing us toward the manner of acquisition. And the manner of acquiring an {\i eved Kena\u8217'ani} is, as will be detailed, through {\i kesef}, {\i shtar}, and {\i chazakah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Three Kinyan Methods\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 An {\i eved Kena\u8217'ani}, treated as property, is acquired by the same three modes as land: {\i kesef}, {\i shtar}, and {\i chazakah}. A woman is acquired by {\i kesef}, {\i shtar}, and {\i biah}. An {\i eved Ivri} is acquired by \u8212- as we will see \u8212- {\i kesef} and {\i shtar} only. Notably, there is no third mode parallel to {\i chazakah}. The {\i drasha} from {\i lo tetzei k\u8217'tzeit ha\u8217'avadim} is that the {\i amah Ivriyah} is acquired {\i like} an {\i eved Kena\u8217'ani}, which includes {\i shtar}. But {\i kesef} we already know from other sources, so this verse comes to teach us {\i shtar}. The Gemara then raises an objection: perhaps the verse is teaching {\i chazakah}, not {\i shtar}? After all, those are both modes of acquiring an {\i eved Kena\u8217'ani} and we already have {\i kesef}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What is {\i chazakah} in the context of a slave? It is demonstrating mastery through some form of service rendered to the acquirer. If the slave ties my shoe or helps me on with my coat, that act of service constitutes {\i chazakah}. Interestingly, there is a slight asymmetry between {\i chazakah} over a slave and {\i chazakah} over land: with land, the classic form of {\i chazakah} is improving the property (e.g., repairing a breach in a fence), rather than extracting benefit from it, whereas with a slave it is precisely rendering service \u8212- a form of benefit \u8212- that establishes mastery. One could attempt to construct a parallel to {\i biah} as a form of {\i chazakah}, but that discussion leads into complex territory.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 No Chazakah Principle\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara\u8217's answer for why the verse teaches {\i shtar} and not {\i chazakah} emerges from a different verse. For the {\i eved Kena\u8217'ani}, the Torah says {\i v\u8217'hisnachaltem osam livneichem achareichem} \u8212- you shall bequeath them to your children after you. The Gemara reads from this: {\i osam b\u8217'chazakah v\u8217'lo acher b\u8217'chazakah} \u8212- it is they, the {\i eved Kena\u8217'ani}, who are acquired by {\i chazakah}, and not another. The {\i chazakah} mode of acquisition is reserved for {\i eved Kena\u8217'ani} alone, because {\i chazakah} expresses full possessory ownership \u8212- something that applies only where the person is treated entirely as property.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara anticipates a counter-reading: perhaps the verse is telling us that the {\i eved Kena\u8217'ani} is acquired with {\i shtar} and not another \u8212- i.e., that {\i shtar} is for {\i eved Kena\u8217'ani} exclusively, and the {\i eved Ivri} gets {\i chazakah} instead. But the Gemara responds: {\i haktiv lo tetzei k\u8217'tzeit ha\u8217'avadim} \u8212- the verse establishes that there is {\i some} mode of acquisition shared between {\i eved Ivri} and {\i eved Kena\u8217'ani}. The question is only which one.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rashi on Chazakah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rashi makes this point sharply. The full verse reads {\i v\u8217'hisnachaltem osam livneichem achareichem lareshes achuzah} \u8212- to inherit as a possession. Rashi explains: {\i b\u8217'eved Kena\u8217'ani achsiv osam k\u8217'achuzah} \u8212- the Torah treats the {\i eved Kena\u8217'ani} like a real estate holding, like {\i achuzah}. It is precisely this property-possession framing \u8212- the {\i achuzah} dimension \u8212- that makes {\i chazakah} appropriate for {\i eved Kena\u8217'ani} and limits it to that category. An {\i eved Kena\u8217'ani} is like a parcel of land: fully possessed, fully owned, transmissible. That is the conceptual home of {\i chazakah}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Ownership Concept\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Michael\u8217's Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A closely related question arises regarding {\i shtar} and the {\i amah Ivriyah}. If the master wishes to perform {\i yi\u8217'ud} \u8212- to marry the girl \u8212- we established earlier that the {\i kesef mechira}, the money of the sale, transforms into {\i kesef kiddushin}. But what happens if the {\i amah Ivriyah} was acquired through a {\i shtar} rather than money? If no money changed hands at the moment of acquisition \u8212- or if money was not yet paid \u8212- is there any {\i kesef} available for {\i yi\u8217'ud}? And would a new {\i shtar} be required for {\i kiddushin}, given that a {\i shtar mechira} is too specific an instrument to double as a {\i shtar kiddushin}? Unlike money, which has no intrinsic identity \u8212- {\i tiv\u8217'a chad hu} \u8212- a {\i shtar} is a specific document with specific content, and it cannot simply change its legal character. This question remains to be investigated further.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Chazakah vs Shtar\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shtar Preference\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Returning to the central question: of the two remaining candidates \u8212- {\i shtar} and {\i chazakah} \u8212- which one applies to {\i eved Ivri}? The Gemara offers a principled reason to prefer {\i shtar}: {\i shtar havei lei leribuyei, sheken motzi bebat Yisrael} \u8212- we should derive that {\i shtar} is the operative {\i kinyan} because {\i shtar} is found to work in the context of transactions involving a Jewish person who is not being treated as property. Specifically, {\i shtar} works in the domain of marriage \u8212- {\i kiddushin} and {\i geirushin}. Tosafot notes a slight oddity: the Gemara says {\i motzi bebat Yisrael}, emphasizing the {\i get}, rather than {\i machnisa bebat Yisrael}, the {\i shtar kiddushin}, which would be the more direct parallel. Tosafot explains that the {\i shtar} of divorce is more explicit in the Torah and therefore is the preferred reference. But the underlying point is the same: {\i shtar} operates in contexts of personal status, not only property.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chazakah Power\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara considers the counter-argument: perhaps {\i chazakah} should be preferred, because {\i chazakah} is the more powerful {\i kinyan} in at least one critical respect. It works even where there is no seller \u8212- specifically, by {\i nichsei ha-ger}, the property of a convert who dies without heirs. Since a convert who dies without children has no relatives, his estate is {\i hefker}, ownerless. One cannot write a {\i shtar} and one cannot pay {\i kesef} to a seller, since there is none. But one can acquire the property through {\i chazakah} alone. This makes {\i chazakah} the stronger {\i kinyan} in certain respects. Perhaps then it should be the one that extends to {\i eved Ivri}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shtar Works Persons\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara\u8217's decisive response: {\i be\u8217'ishah miha lo ashkechan} \u8212- we have never found {\i chazakah} working in a context of marriage, of personal status involving a human being who is not treated as property. The force of this response is not merely formal. The deeper point is that {\i chazakah} is conceptually rooted in the idea of taking physical possession of an object \u8212- of property. It works wherever ownership is of that possessory, property-holding character. {\i Shtar}, by contrast, has demonstrated applicability to human beings in non-property contexts: {\i kiddushin} and {\i geirushin}. Since an {\i eved Ivri} is not owned as property but rather under a form of personal-status obligation, the {\i kinyan} that resonates with personal status \u8212- {\i shtar} \u8212- is the appropriate one.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Combined Approach\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara ultimately offers a combined approach: {\i lehachi ahani im acheres}. The verse {\i im acheres yikach lo} pointed toward {\i shtar}, but was not conclusive on its own \u8212- because it depended on the contested question of who writes the {\i shtar}, and if it is the father who writes it, the comparison to {\i kiddushin} does not hold. But taken together with the {\i lo tetzei} derivation, which independently tells us that one of the {\i kinyanim} of {\i eved Kena\u8217'ani} extends to {\i eved Ivri}, the two sources work in concert: {\i im acheres} establishes the {\i shtar} direction, and {\i lo tetzei}, by excluding {\i chazakah}, confirms that among the available {\i kinyanim}, it is {\i shtar} that applies to {\i eved Ivri}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The overarching conclusion is significant: an {\i eved Ivri} has {\i kesef} and {\i shtar} \u8212- but not {\i chazakah}. That absence is not accidental. {\i Chazakah} is the {\i kinyan} most expressive of full possessory ownership, of treating something as property. Its exclusion from {\i eved Ivri} is the Gemara\u8217's way of encoding, at the level of {\i kinyan}, that the {\i eved Ivri} is not property. He retains his fundamental human status even while his labor has been acquired.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Ownership Concept (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Braita Sources\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Mishna} teaches that an {\i eved Ivri} gains his freedom through {\i shanim} (six years of service), {\i yovel}, and {\i gir\u8217'on kesef} (pro-rated redemption). A {\i braita} supplements the {\i Mishna} by adding that he can also acquire his own freedom \u8212- {\i v\u8217'koneh atzmo b\u8217'kessef u\u8217'v\u8217'shaveh kessef u\u8217'v\u8217'shtar}. Not only money but the equivalent of money, and additionally a {\i shtar}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara traces the sources for each. {\i Kesef} derives from {\i mikessef miknato} in Vayikra, which refers to the scenario of a Jew who sells himself into servitude \u8212- the redemption price can be paid back from the money of his acquisition. {\i Shaveh kesef} \u8212- the equivalent of money, not cash \u8212- derives from {\i yashiv geulato} in that same {\i parsha} in Behar, where the general formulation \u8220"shall give back his redemption\u8221" without specifying {\i kesef} indicates that any form of equivalent value suffices.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shtar Admei IOU\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i braita}\u8217's addition of {\i shtar} raises immediate difficulties. {\i Ela hai shtar heichi dami?} What is the scenario of this {\i shtar}? If the {\i eved Ivri} wrote an IOU \u8212- a {\i shtar admei} \u8212- to the master, and the master accepted it as payment for the slave\u8217's freedom, is that not simply {\i kesef}? The Gemara says: {\i hainu kessef} \u8212- that is money; it is already subsumed under the {\i kesef} category.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This assumption deserves pause. Normally, halakha does not treat a {\i shtar chov}, a promissory note, as {\i shaveh kesef}. A check or an IOU has no intrinsic value \u8212- no object of value changes hands; there is merely a statement of debt. The Gemara\u8217's casual equation of a {\i shtar admei} with {\i kesef} is in fact quite surprising. One could rationalize it by saying the master is effectively agreeing to treat the debt as if already paid \u8212- as though he has lent the slave the money and been immediately repaid \u8212- but this is far from self-evident, and the Gemara does not press the point. This is also why the scenario is genuinely anomalous in comparison to {\i kesef kiddushin}, where a check for ten thousand dollars would not work but a coin worth a few cents would \u8212- because {\i kiddushin} requires intrinsic value, not mere obligation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If the {\i shtar} is not an IOU, then it must refer to a {\i shtar shichrur} \u8212- a document written by the master declaring the slave free, {\i harei ata ben chorin}. And here the Gemara raises a deeper question: {\i shtar lamma li?} Why would you need a {\i shtar} for that? Why could the master not simply say in front of two witnesses \u8212- {\i zil}, go, you are free \u8212- or declare it before a {\i beis din}? If releasing the slave is simply a matter of waiving a legal claim, a verbal waiver should suffice.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava\u8217's Principle\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This question leads to one of the most conceptually significant statements in the {\i sugya}. {\i Amar Rava: zos omeres eved Ivri gufo kanui} \u8212- this teaches that even an {\i eved Ivri}\u8217's body is considered owned, acquired by the master. The proof: {\i rav she-machal al gir\u8217'ono, ein gir\u8217'ono machul} \u8212- a master who simply waives his financial claim against the slave does not thereby free the slave. The waiver does not work.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This requires careful unpacking. One might have understood the {\i eved Ivri} relationship as purely contractual: the slave owes a debt, has agreed to pay it off through labor, and the master has a contractual right to demand that labor. If so, the master could simply waive his contractual rights and the slave would be free. But Rava is saying that this is wrong. The relationship is not merely contractual. The master genuinely {\i owns} the {\i eved Ivri} \u8212- not as property in the full sense, but with a real form of ownership. And ownership cannot be extinguished by mere waiver of rights. You cannot free yourself from ownership of a car by simply saying \u8220"I waive my rights to it.\u8221" You must either sell it or perform a positive act of renunciation \u8212- {\i hafka\u8217'ah}, making it ownerless.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Waiving vs Renouncing\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot draws the crucial distinction. While verbal waiver of rights does not work, a verbal renunciation of {\i ownership} \u8212- being {\i mafkir} \u8212- can work. The master could declare: \u8220"I renounce my ownership of you,\u8221" and that would be effective, because it is not a mere waiver but a positive relinquishment of the ownership itself. The difference is between saying \u8220"I no longer want to enforce my claim\u8221" (which changes nothing about the underlying ownership relationship) and \u8220"I declare this person ownerless\u8221" (which actively dissolves the ownership). The former fails; the latter would succeed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i shtar shichrur} that says {\i harei ata ben chorin} works precisely because it is read not as a waiver of rights but as a declaration that dissolves the status \u8212- an act of renouncing ownership, not merely forgoing its exercise. This is why the {\i shtar} is conceptually effective where the verbal claim of {\i mochel} is not: the {\i shtar} effects a status change, which can also be understood as the equivalent of declaring the slave ownerless.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The conceptual parallel here is to a husband\u8217's relationship to his wife\u8217's property. A husband does not merely have contractual rights to the {\i peiros} of his wife\u8217's property; he has a form of ownership \u8212- {\i guf l\u8217'peiros} \u8212- over it. Similarly, the master\u8217's entitlement to the {\i eved Ivri}\u8217's labor is not merely a contractual claim but a form of genuine, if limited, ownership. That is why {\i mechilah} alone cannot dissolve it.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Amah Freedom Sources\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishnah Teaching\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Mishna} teaches that an {\i amah Ivriyah} is in one way greater \u8212- {\i yeseira} \u8212- than a male {\i eved Ivri}: she gains her freedom through {\i simanim}, the physical signs of physical maturity. When she develops the signs of puberty, she is released from her master\u8217's control even if the six years are not complete.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Reish Lakish\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Reish Lakish adds a mode of freedom not listed in the {\i Mishna}: {\i amah Ivriyah konah atzma b\u8217'mitat ha\u8217'av meirshat adon m\u8217'kal vachomer} \u8212- an {\i amah Ivriyah} acquires her freedom from the master\u8217's domain through the death of her father, derived by {\i kal vachomer}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ironic Comparison\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i kal vachomer} runs as follows: {\i simanim}, signs of puberty, do not remove a {\i na\u8217'ara} from her father\u8217's control \u8212- a girl only leaves her father\u8217's authority when she becomes a {\i bogeret}, and {\i simanim} alone do not accomplish that. Yet {\i simanim} do free her from the master\u8217's control. If so, the death of the father \u8212- which does remove her from the father\u8217's control entirely \u8212- should certainly free her from the master\u8217's control as well. The irony is built into the argument: the event that removes her from one domain (her father\u8217's) is precisely the one Reish Lakish claims also removes her from the other.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Defense Reish Lakish\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hoshaya Challenge\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Hoshaya raises an immediate challenge to Reish Lakish. The {\i Mishna} lists {\i simanim} as the way in which an {\i amah Ivriyah} is {\i yeseira} over the male slave. If the death of the father also frees her, why doesn\u8217't the {\i Mishna} mention that as well? The omission appears to refute Reish Lakish\u8217's claim.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Abbreviated List\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara\u8217's first answer is {\i tana v\u8217'shayer} \u8212- it is an abbreviated list; the {\i Mishna} left things out. This answer, however, is only acceptable if something {\i else} was also omitted. Otherwise the charge that the {\i Mishna} left off exactly one item, and that item happens to be Reish Lakish\u8217's novel addition, is damning. The Gemara therefore proposes that the {\i Mishna} also omitted the case of {\i mitat ha\u8217'adon} \u8212- the death of the master. By a normal {\i eved Ivri}, if the master dies, the slave continues working for the sons. For an {\i amah Ivriyah}, the master\u8217's death frees her. That is an additional omission, and so the {\i tana v\u8217'shayer} response has at least two items to point to.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 However, the Gemara immediately questions this. In the case of a {\i nirtza} \u8212- a slave whose ear has been pierced and who continues working until {\i yovel} \u8212- the master\u8217's death also frees him; he does not continue to work for the sons. This is itself a notable irony: the {\i nirtza}, who is bound more permanently, is in this respect treated {\i less} like property than a regular {\i eved Ivri}, who does continue to work for the sons. The logic, as the Gemara implies, is that the regular six-year slave\u8217's situation is more contractual \u8212- the obligation to render six years of service survives the master and is owed to the heirs \u8212- while the {\i nirtza}, who is more like property, is specifically {\i not} passed on to the sons, because the Torah is making the point that even a {\i nirtza} is not property. And property does pass to the sons. Since {\i mitat ha\u8217'adon} also applies to the male {\i nirtza}, it is not exclusive to the woman, and thus does not qualify as an additional omission that distinguishes the {\i amah Ivriyah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Fixed vs Unfixed\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara therefore pivots to a different answer. {\i Tana davar she\u8217'yesh lo kitzva kateni, davar she\u8217'ein lo kitzva lo kateni} \u8212- the {\i Mishna} taught things that have a fixed or predictable time of occurrence, and did not teach things that have no such fixed time. Six years, {\i yovel}, and {\i gir\u8217'on kesef} are all knowable in advance or within a defined framework. The death of the father, by contrast, has no predictable timing. It may happen before the girl becomes an adult; it may never happen during her period of servitude. The {\i Mishna}\u8217's omission is therefore principled, not substantive \u8212- it is not denying that {\i mitat ha\u8217'av} frees her; it is simply that this event falls outside the {\i Mishna}\u8217's organizing criterion.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Safra Refinement\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This answer raises a challenge: {\i simanim} also have no fixed time. You cannot know when a girl will develop the signs of puberty. The Gemara therefore invokes Rav Safra\u8217's refinement: {\i ein lahem kitzva l\u8217'ma\u8217'ala} \u8212- there is no fixed upper limit; you cannot know when {\i simanim} will appear. {\i Aval yesh lahem kitzva l\u8217'mata} \u8212- but there is a fixed lower limit. The signs cannot appear before a certain minimum age, before the age of 12 for a girl. A {\i braita} confirms this in the context of a boy: hairs that grow before age 9 are treated as a {\i shuma}, a mere blemish, of no legal significance. Between 9 and the onset of the thirteenth year the question is disputed, but all agree that hairs appearing after the boy has entered his thirteenth year are unambiguous {\i simanim}. The point for our purposes is that there is a floor \u8212- a defined minimum age \u8212- giving {\i simanim} a form of temporal anchoring even without an upper limit. The death of the father has no such floor. It is entirely unpredictable in both directions.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Four Hanakah Scenarios\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Shimon\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Shimon teaches that there are four scenarios in which the parting gift of {\i hanakah} applies \u8212- {\i ha\u8217'anek ta\u8217'anik lo} \u8212- and three of those four apply to a male slave, and three to a female slave, with neither having all four.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sheshet Challenge\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Sheshet uses this {\i braita} to challenge Reish Lakish. If the death of the father frees the {\i amah Ivriyah}, it ought to appear among the enumerated scenarios. And one cannot simply invoke {\i tana v\u8217'shayer} \u8212- the abbreviated list defense \u8212- when the {\i braita} gives an explicit count of four. When a fixed number is stated, the enumeration is meant to be exhaustive.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Nor can one fall back on the {\i kitzva} principle \u8212- that only events with fixed timing are enumerated \u8212- because {\i simanim} already appears in the list despite lacking a fully fixed time. And Rav Safra\u8217's refinement about a lower limit is already in play.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Exclusion Rationale\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara therefore presses harder on what is and is not included. The initial assumption was that one of the four scenarios for the male slave was {\i mitat ha\u8217'adon} \u8212- the death of the master, as found in the case of the {\i nirtza}. If so, one might ask why {\i mitat ha\u8217'av} for the woman is not listed alongside it. But the Gemara now clarifies: {\i mitat ha\u8217'adon} is also {\i not} in this list. The enumeration does not include events with no predictable timing, and {\i mitat ha\u8217'adon}, like {\i mitat ha\u8217'av}, has no {\i kitzva}. Both are therefore excluded on the same principled basis.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Four Scenarios\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What then are the four scenarios? {\i Shanim v\u8217'yovel v\u8217'yovel shel retziah v\u8217'amah ha\u8217'Ivriyah b\u8217'simanim}. Two are shared between man and woman: {\i shanim} (the six years) and {\i yovel} (the Jubilee year during the six-year period). The third \u8212- {\i yovel shel retziah} \u8212- is the Jubilee year that frees the {\i nirtza}. This is not {\i mitat ha\u8217'adon} but rather the {\i yovel} itself catching up with the {\i nirtza}. The fourth is {\i simanim}, exclusive to the {\i amah Ivriyah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This identification is confirmed by the closing statement of the {\i braita}: {\i i attah yakhol lomar arba\u8217'ah b\u8217'echad mehem} \u8212- you cannot attribute all four to any single individual, because {\i simanim} does not apply to a man, and {\i retziah} does not apply to a woman. Had one of the four been {\i mitat ha\u8217'adon}, that would apply to the {\i amah Ivriyah} through the {\i nirtza} case as well, and then the woman would have all four \u8212- which the {\i braita} explicitly denies. This confirms that {\i mitat ha\u8217'adon} is not among the four, and provides the parallel that justifies the absence of {\i mitat ha\u8217'av} as well: both are excluded because neither has a {\i kitzva}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Reish Lakish Defended\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The conclusion is that Reish Lakish\u8217's position is defended. The {\i Mishna}\u8217's failure to mention {\i mitat ha\u8217'av} as a mode of freedom for the {\i amah Ivriyah} does not refute his {\i kal vachomer}. There is a principled reason for the omission: the {\i Mishna} and the {\i braita} confine themselves to modes of freedom that have at least a lower temporal bound. {\i Mitat ha\u8217'av}, which has no such bound, is excluded on those grounds \u8212- not because it does not free her, but because it does not meet the organizing criterion of the enumeration.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Kiddushin 17\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Reish Lakish Kal Vachomer\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Reish Lakish\u8217's Claim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i daf} picks up in the middle of the ongoing discussion of Reish Lakish\u8217's claim that an {\i amah ivriyah} goes free when her father dies. The argument, framed as a {\i kal vachomer}, runs as follows: she goes free when her father dies while she is still under her father\u8217's control; therefore, by logical inference, she should similarly go free when her father dies even after she has been sold and is under her master\u8217's control. The {\i Gemara} has been lining up proofs against this position, but Reish Lakish has managed to deflect each one. The present passage introduces yet another challenge.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Amram\u8217's Challenge\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Amram raises a challenge from a {\i braisa}. The {\i braisa} states that the following are the categories of slaves who receive the {\i hanaka} \u8212- the gifts with which the master must load the slave upon release: one who goes free after six years ({\i shanim}), one freed by the arrival of {\i yovel} during those six years, one freed through {\i mitat adon} (death of the master, in the case of a {\i nirtza}), and an {\i amah ivriyah} who goes free upon the emergence of {\i simanim} (signs of puberty). If Reish Lakish\u8217's position is correct that the {\i amah ivriyah} also goes free when her father dies, the {\i braisa} should have mentioned {\i mitat ha\u8217'av} (death of the father) as an additional case.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Fixed vs.\u160 ?Uncertain\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might respond that the {\i braisa} is merely an abbreviated list and does not purport to be exhaustive. To this, the {\i Gemara} has previously offered the counter-argument that the {\i braisa} uses the formulation \u8220"and these,\u8221" implying a complete enumeration. An alternative reconciliation was proposed: the {\i braisa} lists only those cases of liberation that have a fixed or predictable character, and the death of the father \u8212- which may occur at any point \u8212- lacks that fixed quality. The {\i Gemara} then pushes back: {\i simanim} also lack a fixed time, yet the {\i braisa} includes them. The prior answer to that objection \u8212- associated with Rav Safra \u8212- was that {\i simanim} have at least a minimum age threshold, lending them some degree of temporal predictability.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Reish Lakish Rejected\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Now, however, the {\i braisa} explicitly mentions {\i mitat adon} \u8212- the death of the master \u8212- which is likewise entirely unpredictable and has no fixed time. Yet the {\i braisa} does not mention {\i mitat ha\u8217'av}. This closes off any remaining line of defense. The {\i Gemara} declares: {\i tyuvta d\u8217'Reish Lakish tyuvta} \u8212- a complete and unqualified rejection of Reish Lakish\u8217's position.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Simanim vs.\u160 ?Father\u8217's Death\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} then addresses the underlying {\i kal vachomer} itself. Even setting aside the {\i braisa}, the {\i Gemara} argues that the {\i kal vachomer} was inherently flawed from the outset. The challenge ({\i pircha}) to the inference is this: the case of {\i simanim} carries with it an intrinsic change \u8212- {\i nishtana ha-guf}, the body itself has been transformed. The {\i amah ivriyah} has moved from childhood into adulthood, and it is precisely this independent transformation of her identity that is sufficient to sever the relationship with her master, even though the same {\i simanim} may not suffice to free her from her father\u8217's domain (where she must reach {\i bogeres}, not merely {\i na\u8217'arah}). The death of the father, by contrast, changes nothing about her \u8212- neither physically, nor in her relationship to the master. The master\u8217's authority over her is entirely unaffected by what happens to her father. The death of the father is relevant to her connection to her father, not to her connection to the master. Accordingly, the {\i kal vachomer} fails, and there is no basis to conclude that {\i mitat ha\u8217'av} grants freedom.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Hanaka Distribution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Contradictory Baraitot\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having dispensed with the {\i kal vachomer}, the {\i Gemara} turns to revisit the question of the {\i hanaka}. A tension emerges between two {\i baraitot}. One {\i braisa} states that when the {\i eved ivri} receives the {\i hanaka}, he keeps it for himself \u8212- and likewise the {\i amah ivriyah} keeps her {\i hanaka} for herself. A second {\i braisa}, however, states that the {\i hanaka} of the {\i amah ivriyah}, as well as her found objects ({\i metzi\u8217'ot}), go to her father; the master receives only {\i sechar battalah} \u8212- compensation for the time she took away from her service in order to retrieve those found objects, since while the master is entitled to her {\i ma\u8217'aseh yadayim} (her productive earnings), the category of {\i metzi\u8217'ah} is treated differently under {\i halacha} and remains associated with the father\u8217's rights. In fact, one can see a certain internal logic here: if while she is still in service, her found objects go to the father, then when the master releases her and gives the {\i hanaka}, one might equally say it flows to the father \u8212- except that one could also argue that the entire purpose of the gift is to benefit the person who actually did the labor.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The contradiction between the two {\i baraitot} must be resolved.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Father Alive Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} initially proposes a straightforward reading: perhaps one {\i braisa} refers to the case where she goes free by {\i simanim} (so she is now a {\i na\u8217'arah} and her property belongs to her father), while the other refers to the case where she goes free because the father died (in which case there is no father to collect it, and she keeps it herself). This reading, however, would seem to provide support for Reish Lakish\u8217's rejected position \u8212- namely, that {\i mitat ha\u8217'av} is itself a mechanism of liberation. The {\i Gemara} therefore rejects this reading. In truth, both {\i baraitot} speak of the same case: she goes free by virtue of {\i simanim}. The question is not the mode of liberation but the status of the father. In one {\i braisa}, the father is alive, and therefore the {\i hanaka} \u8212- like the earnings of a {\i na\u8217'arah} \u8212- goes to him. In the other, the father has already died, and therefore she retains it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Father Dead Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This resolution, though technically satisfying, does raise a question of what the {\i chiddush} is. If the father is dead, it is self-evident that there is no one for the money to go to \u8212- why does a {\i braisa} need to tell us she keeps it? The {\i Gemara} clarifies: the {\i chiddush} is specifically to exclude the brothers. One might have thought that just as a father has rights in his daughter\u8217's earnings and found objects during her minority, those rights pass to his sons after his death. The {\i braisa} teaches that this is not so. A person does not transmit the rights he holds in his daughter to his sons. The source for this is the verse regarding {\i avadim kena\u8217'ani} \u8212- \u8220"you shall bequeath them to your children after you\u8221" \u8212- from which the {\i Gemara} derives that one bequeaths real property, an {\i eved kena\u8217'ani}, to one\u8217's children, but not one\u8217's rights in a daughter. Those rights belong exclusively to the father and do not pass to the brothers. Hence, if the father has died, the {\i hanaka} belongs to the {\i amah ivriyah} herself, and the brothers have no claim.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hebrew Slave\u8217's Hanaka\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} then turns to the apparently obvious ruling in the {\i braisa} that the {\i eved ivri} keeps his own {\i hanaka}. This seems self-evident \u8212- who else would receive it? Rav Yosef uses the idiomatic expression that Rashi explains as meaning one has made a mountain out of a molehill, or a large city out of a tiny {\i yud}. Nevertheless, Abaye cites Rav Sheshet, who quotes a {\i tanna} named Tuta\u8217'i to the effect that the {\i hanaka} goes to the {\i eved ivri} himself and not to his creditor. This is the genuine {\i chiddush}: even if the {\i eved ivri} has outstanding debts, the {\i hanaka} cannot be seized by his creditors. It belongs to him, and he alone decides when and how to use it to satisfy his obligations.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Four Cases of Hanaka\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Four Cases Listed\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now returns to the {\i braisa} itself: the four categories of those who receive the {\i hanaka} \u8212- one who goes free through {\i shanim} (the six-year term), through {\i yovel}, through {\i mitat adon}, and the {\i amah ivriyah} through {\i simanim}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mitat Adon Logic\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The case of {\i mitat adon} deserves attention. For a regular {\i eved ivri} serving out a six-year contract, death of the master does not terminate service \u8212- the slave continues working for the master\u8217's son, as he has simply sold a block of labor. But for a {\i nirtza} \u8212- one who had his ear pierced and committed to serve indefinitely \u8212- {\i mitat adon} does terminate service and brings the {\i hanaka}. This is precisely because the {\i nirtza}\u8217's status is closer to genuine subjugation, like real property. And the {\i chiddush} is that {\i yovel} overrides even this extended commitment, and death of the master likewise ends it. The point is to underscore that even the {\i nirtza} is not an {\i eved kena\u8217'ani} \u8212- he is not heritable property. Similarly, the {\i amah ivriyah} goes free upon {\i mitat adon} in every case, without any scenario of {\i nirtza} applying to women.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Exclusions: Who Doesn\u8217't\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Two categories are explicitly excluded from the {\i hanaka}: the {\i boreach} (runaway slave) and the {\i yotzei begiraon kesef} (one who redeems himself by paying back the remaining value of his service). The {\i Gemara} derives the inclusive cases from the phrase {\i \u8220"lo teshalchennu reikam\u8221"} \u8212- \u8220"you shall not send him away empty-handed\u8221" \u8212- read in conjunction with the doubled language {\i \u8220"hanek ta\u8217'anik\u8221"}, which implies the obligation applies whenever there is a sending-away, under all such circumstances. The exclusions are derived from the phrase {\i \u8220"vechi tishalchennu chafshi me\u8217'imach\u8221"} \u8212- \u8220"when you send him free from you.\u8221" This phrase highlights the requirement that the release be a sending \u8220"from you\u8221" \u8212- meaning, initiated or effectuated from the master\u8217's side. The {\i boreach} ran away on his own; the {\i yotzei begiraon kesef} is redeeming himself.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Self-Redemption Logic\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The exclusion of the {\i yotzei begiraon kesef} is at first glance surprising. If anything, self-redemption seems like the most empowering and dignified form of liberation \u8212- the slave has managed to gather funds and buy back his freedom. Why should he be the one to forfeit the {\i hanaka}? The {\i Gemara}\u8217's answer turns on the phrase {\i \u8220"me\u8217'imach\u8221"} \u8212- \u8220"from you.\u8221" Whose act is this release? When the six years expire, or when {\i yovel} arrives, the Torah compels the master to release the slave \u8212- it is a release \u8220"from you.\u8221" When the slave redeems himself, by contrast, the initiative comes from the slave; it is he who is effectively freeing himself. As Rashi explains in this context, even though the master is halachically obligated to accept the payment, the process is fundamentally one in which the slave is the active party. This is the same conceptual framework as was encountered earlier in the discussion of {\i ishah konah et atzmah} and {\i gevoah ka-mazkei leih} \u8212- where Heaven itself is said to effect the transfer, making it not a release \u8220"from you\u8221" but something external to the master-slave relationship. What drives the distinction is not whether the master acts willingly, but whether the freedom is externally imposed upon the relationship or whether the slave himself is the one dissolving it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Boreach and Yovel\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i boreach} case is more intuitively understandable: one who ran away should not be rewarded with a {\i hanaka} for service that was never rendered. The precise formulation of the exclusion is {\i \u8220"mi sheshilucho me\u8217'imach\u8221"} \u8212- \u8220"one whose sending is from you\u8221" \u8212- which does not apply to someone who departed without authorization. However, the {\i Gemara} clarifies the exact scenario in which the {\i boreach} is excluded.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Boreach Repayment\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 When a slave runs away and is later recaptured, the principle of {\i hashlama} applies \u8212- he must make up the time he was absent. This is derived from the verse {\i \u8220"shesh shanim ya\u8217'avod\u8221"} \u8212- \u8220"he shall work six years\u8221" \u8212- meaning a full six years of actual service, not merely six calendar years from the date of purchase. If the slave runs away but then returns and completes his full six years before {\i yovel}, he would have fulfilled his obligation and would presumably be entitled to the {\i hanaka}. The specific case of exclusion, as Rav Sheshet explains, is where the slave ran away and {\i yovel} intervened before he could make up the lost time. In such a case, he genuinely stole service from his master \u8212- the {\i yovel} arrived during what should have been makeup time, cutting short his obligation to repay. One might have thought that since ultimately it was {\i yovel} that freed him \u8212- and {\i yovel} is a release \u8220"from you\u8221" in the broad sense \u8212- he should receive the {\i hanaka}. The {\i Gemara} rejects this: the fact remains that he failed to provide the service he owed, and therefore he does not receive the {\i hanaka}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sick Leave Rules\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A related question arises: does illness count against the slave the same way that running away does? The {\i Gemara} derives from the verse {\i \u8220"uvashvi\u8217'it yeitze\u8221"} \u8212- \u8220"in the seventh year he shall go free\u8221" \u8212- that involuntary absence does not carry the same consequence as willful flight. Two {\i baraitot} appear to contradict one another on this point. One states that if the slave was ill for three years and worked three, he does not have to make up the time. The other states that only if he was ill for all six years must he make up the time. Rav Sheshet reconciles them: the operative rule is that if the slave was ill for a majority of the period \u8212- more than three years \u8212- he must make up the excess. If he was ill for no more than half the time, the illness is absorbed as sick leave and he need not make up anything. However, if during the period of illness he was still performing some form of service \u8212- the {\i Gemara} invokes the image of needlework performed while bedridden \u8212- then even a majority of illness may be effectively reduced, since he was not entirely idle. The {\i Gemara} resolves the apparent internal contradiction of the {\i braisa} as follows: the threshold of three years and a day into illness is treated as if it were illness for the entire six, and requires {\i hashlama}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafos: Slaves vs.\u160 ?Teac\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafos raises a practically significant question: can one learn from these rules about the {\i eved ivri} to determine the sick leave entitlement of hired workers, specifically {\i melamdei tinokos} \u8212- schoolteachers contracted for a year? Some authorities had argued that just as the {\i eved ivri} is granted sick leave up to half his term without obligation to compensate, so too a hired teacher ill for up to half the contracted period need not make up the time. Tosafos rejects this comparison. The reason the {\i eved ivri} receives generous sick leave is rooted in {\i gufo kanui la-adono} \u8212- there is an element of bodily acquisition in the relationship. The master, in effect, has purchased the slave, paid a purchase price upfront, and as long as the slave is not willfully withholding service, the sale remains in force even if no labor is rendered. The situation resembles a {\i kinyan} model rather than a payment-for-services model. A teacher, by contrast, has merely hired out his services for a defined period. If services are not rendered, there is nothing to pay for \u8212- just as one does not pay a plumber who sends a bill without performing the repair. Therefore, a teacher who is ill may collect only for the time he actually taught, with no sick leave credit.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This distinction \u8212- {\i gufo kanui} versus {\i sechirus} \u8212- sits at a conceptually intermediate point between full property ownership (as with an {\i eved kena\u8217'ani}) and an ordinary employment arrangement. It is precisely because the {\i eved ivri} occupies this unusual middle ground that the sick-leave rules applicable to him cannot be generalized to other hired workers.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Hanaka Amount Dispute\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Meir: Fifteen\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now takes up the question of how much the {\i hanaka} must be. {\i Tanu rabanan}: five {\i sela\u8217'im} of each category mentioned in the verse \u8212- grain, flock, and wine \u8212- for a total of fifteen {\i sela\u8217'im}. This is the position of Rabbi Meir. A {\i sela} is equivalent to four {\i zuz}, so fifteen {\i sela\u8217'im} equals sixty {\i zuz} \u8212- roughly a tenth of the value of a {\i kesuvah}, a not inconsiderable sum.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Meir\u8217's Gezeira Shava\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Meir derives his figure through a {\i gezeira shava} on the word {\i reikam} (\u8220"empty-handed\u8221"). The verse here says {\i \u8220"lo teshalchennu reikam\u8221"} \u8212- you shall not send him away empty-handed. The word {\i reikam} also appears in the context of {\i pidyon ha-ben}, where it is written {\i \u8220"velo yera\u8217'u fanai reikam\u8221"} \u8212- they shall not appear before Me empty-handed. By the {\i pidyon ha-ben}, the fixed amount is five {\i shekel}. Rabbi Meir therefore learns: just as there it is five, so here it is five.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Five of Each Category\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might ask: why five of each category rather than five total, distributed among all three? The {\i Gemara} answers that this depends on the placement of the word {\i reikam} in the verse. Since the verse places {\i \u8220"lo teshalchennu reikam\u8221"} before enumerating the three categories \u8212- grain, flock, and wine \u8212- each category independently carries the force of \u8220"not empty-handed.\u8221" Accordingly, the slave must receive the minimum from each category individually, such that the total must be at least fifteen {\i sela\u8217'im}. The {\i Gemara} clarifies that Rabbi Meir\u8217's figure of five per category is not rigid in its composition \u8212- one may give less from one category and more from another \u8212- but the aggregate must reach fifteen.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} then asks why we do not make the {\i gezeira shava} instead from the {\i olat re\u8217'iyah}, where {\i reikam} also appears but the minimum implied is only two {\i shekel}. The answer: the verse here says {\i \u8220"asher berachecha Hashem Elokecha\u8221"} \u8212- \u8220"as God has blessed you\u8221" \u8212- indicating generosity. When there is a choice between two possible {\i gezeira shava} links, the more generous reading, supported by the language of blessing, is preferred.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Yehudah: Thirty\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yehudah says the {\i hanaka} is thirty {\i shekel}, corresponding to the thirty {\i shekel} paid when an {\i eved kena\u8217'ani} is gored by an ox.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yehudah\u8217's Source\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yehudah derives this through a {\i gezeira shava} on the word {\i netinah} (\u8220"giving\u8221"). The verse here uses {\i \u8220"titein lo\u8221"} \u8212- \u8220"you shall give him\u8221" \u8212- and the passage regarding a gored {\i eved kena\u8217'ani} also uses the language of giving: {\i \u8220"kesef shloshim shekalim yitein la-adonav\u8221"} \u8212- thirty {\i shekel} of silver shall be given to his master. Although one involves an {\i eved kena\u8217'ani} and the other an {\i eved ivri}, Rabbi Yehudah learns one from the other: just as the giving there is thirty, so too here.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} challenges: why not instead make the {\i gezeira shava} of {\i netinah} from {\i erchin}, where {\i netinah} also appears and the amount can reach fifty {\i shekel}? Two responses are offered. First, {\i tafasta meruba lo tafasta} \u8212- if you reach too high you may not hold on to it; when there is uncertainty, the safer and more modest claim is preferred. Second, it is more apt to learn slave from slave: the {\i eved kena\u8217'ani} and the {\i eved ivri} share the common context of {\i avdus}, making that {\i gezeira shava} the more natural one.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Shimon: Fifty\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Shimon says the {\i hanaka} is fifty {\i shekel}, corresponding to the highest {\i erech} value in {\i erchin} \u8212- the fixed valuations the Torah prescribes for a person by age and sex, the maximum of which is fifty {\i shekel}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shimon\u8217's Gezeira Shava\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Shimon likewise uses a {\i gezeira shava} on {\i netinah}, but links it to {\i erchin} rather than to the gored slave. The {\i Gemara} asks: why not simply say fifty is too much and apply {\i tafasta meruba lo tafasta}? Furthermore, and why not learn from the slave (thirty) rather than from {\i erchin} (fifty)? The {\i Gemara} answers that Rabbi Shimon\u8217's {\i gezeira shava} is not on the word {\i netinah} at all. Rather, he employs a different {\i gezeira shava} \u8212- on the word {\i mach} (\u8220"poor\u8221"): by the {\i eved ivri} it says {\i \u8220"vechi yamuch achicha\u8221"} \u8212- \u8220"if your brother becomes impoverished [and sells himself],\u8221" and by {\i erchin} it says {\i \u8220"ve\u8217'im mach hu\u8221"} \u8212- \u8220"if he is poor.\u8221" Since the shared word is {\i mach}, and it links specifically to {\i erchin}, there is only one possible {\i gezeira shava} to make, and one cannot redirect it elsewhere. That is how Rabbi Shimon arrives at fifty.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gezeira Shava Tradition\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This discussion opens onto a broader principle regarding how {\i gezeira shava} functions, articulated here by Rashi. The rule is {\i ein adam dan gezeira shava me-atzmo} \u8212- one cannot construct a {\i gezeira shava} on one\u8217's own initiative; it must rest on a tradition transmitted from one\u8217's teachers, ultimately going back to Sinai ({\i mesurah hi lahem me-raboteihem u-mi-Sinai}). Yet the {\i Gemara}\u8217's back-and-forth \u8212- asking why Rabbi Yehudah doesn\u8217't use the {\i gezeira shava} of {\i erchin}, or why Rabbi Shimon doesn\u8217't use that of the slave \u8212- seems to presuppose that the Tanna has some latitude to choose which {\i gezeira shava} to apply.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rashi resolves this by clarifying what exactly is transmitted as tradition. The tradition is not a fully specified pairing \u8212- \u8220"this word links to that word and produces this {\i halacha}.\u8221" Rather, the tradition conveys only that a particular {\i halacha} is derivable through {\i gezeira shava} involving a particular word. The specific link \u8212- which other passage that word connects to \u8212- is left for the scholar to reason through. Thus, for example, Rabbi Yehudah knew that the {\i hanaka} amount was to be derived through a {\i gezeira shava} on {\i netinah}; but whether to link it to the {\i netinah} of {\i erchin} or the {\i netinah} of the gored slave was a question he had to work through using his own analysis, guided by principles such as {\i tafasta meruba} or {\i eved me-eved}. Similarly, Rabbi Shimon\u8217's tradition was built on the word {\i mach} rather than {\i netinah}, which is why the challenge of redirecting it to the slave\u8217's thirty does not apply to him \u8212- his link is unique.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This represents, as Rashi emphasizes \u8212- and the Ramban makes the same point in his {\i Sefer Ha-Mitzvot} \u8212- a nuanced middle ground between pure {\i halacha le-Moshe mi-Sinai} and free-form rabbinic derivation. The anchor in tradition is the word that opens the {\i gezeira shava}, but the application of that anchor to derive a specific ruling retains a genuine human analytical dimension.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Blessing Requirement\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Things That Increase\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now asks: given that Rabbi Yehudah and Rabbi Shimon hold that the {\i hanaka} is a single fixed sum (thirty or fifty {\i shekel}) and not broken down by category, why does the Torah bother to mention specifically grain ({\i goren}), flock ({\i tzon}), and wine ({\i yekev})? For Rabbi Meir the enumeration is easily understood \u8212- five from each. But what work do these categories do for the other opinions?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} answers from a {\i braisa}: one might have thought that the {\i hanaka} must come specifically from these three. The verse then says {\i \u8220"asher berachecha Hashem Elokecha\u8221"} \u8212- indicating that the master should give from whatever God has blessed him with, not limited to these three. But if the enumeration of grain, flock, and wine is not meant to restrict the categories, why mention them? To teach a limiting principle going in the other direction: just as these three share the characteristic that they are {\i bechlal bracha} \u8212- things that experience natural increase \u8212- so too, whatever is included in the {\i hanaka} must be something of that nature. Grain, sheep, and wine all increase naturally: flocks reproduce, crops grow abundantly, grapes yield wine in quantity. It is this capacity for blessing-driven increase that qualifies them as the paradigm.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Money & Mules Excluded\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 From this principle of {\i bechlal bracha} the {\i Gemara} derives two exclusions. Rabbi Shimon holds that money is excluded \u8212- coins lying in a vault do not naturally grow; they have no intrinsic increase. Mules are excluded by Rabbi Eliezer ben Yaakov \u8212- mules, being sterile hybrids, do not reproduce and therefore do not experience the blessing of increase. Each of these Tannaim, however, offers a response on behalf of the other category: Rabbi Shimon would allow mules, arguing that they do become stronger and more valuable through use and feeding \u8212- a form of increase even without reproduction. Rabbi Eliezer ben Yaakov would allow money, noting that money productively invested does yield increase. Thus the two opinions disagree about which of the two potential exclusions is the correct one.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A further question arises from this discussion that the {\i Gemara} leaves somewhat open: is the requirement that the {\i hanaka} come from {\i bechlal bracha} items a restriction on what the master may use to fulfill his obligation, or is it a {\i lechatchila} directive that he give from each such category in his possession? The {\i Rambam}\u8217's formulation on this point was not entirely transparent, and whether the master must give representatively from every qualifying category or simply from any one of them remains somewhat ambiguous.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} then establishes the logical necessity of the threefold enumeration. Had the Torah mentioned only {\i tzon}, one might have thought the {\i hanaka} must come from living creatures. Had it mentioned only {\i goren} (grain), one might have restricted it to products of the earth. The inclusion of both expands the category to all things that experience natural increase. The specific mention of {\i yekev} (wine) then adds the further limiting function: to underscore through these paradigmatic examples that the category is one of {\i bechlal bracha}, thereby excluding money and mules.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Discretionary Approach\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i braisa} then presents a different reading of the verse altogether. Rabbi Elazar ben Azariah reads {\i \u8220"asher berachecha Hashem Elokecha\u8221"} at face value: if the master\u8217's household was genuinely blessed through the slave\u8217's service \u8212- if the master\u8217's fortunes grew as a result of the slave\u8217's work \u8212- then he must give the {\i hanaka}. If the master was not so blessed, there is no obligation. One can anticipate the practical problem this creates: who determines whether the blessing was \u8220"because of\u8221" the slave? The potential for abuse is obvious. Nevertheless, Rabbi Elazar ben Azariah maintains that this is the plain meaning of the verse.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chazal\u8217's Quantification\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Against this, the first opinion holds that the doubled language {\i \u8220"hanek ta\u8217'anik\u8221"} \u8212- \u8220"you shall surely grant him gifts\u8221" \u8212- indicates the obligation is unconditional, regardless of whether the master prospered. The verse\u8217's reference to {\i \u8220"asher berachecha\u8221"} then becomes not a condition but a calibration: you are obligated to give in any event, but how much you give should reflect how much you were blessed. If the slave brought great prosperity to the household, the master should be commensurately generous beyond the minimum.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What this discussion lays bare is the gap between the verse\u8217's apparently open-ended, discretionary formulation and the fixed quantities established by the Tannaim. The simple reading of the verse does not suggest a specific amount \u8212- it evokes generous giving proportional to divine blessing. It is {\i Chazal} who are uncomfortable leaving the obligation undefined and who therefore quantify it into a halachically actionable minimum. In this sense the structure parallels that of {\i terumah}, where the Torah likewise leaves the amount open and the rabbis define a recommended quantity. Rabbi Elazar ben Azariah, one may infer, would reject the fixed amounts of fifteen, thirty, or fifty and treat the entire matter as proportional and discretionary \u8212- though even he would presumably acknowledge that the rabbinic figures represent a reasonable concrete floor.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Service to Heir\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Son vs.\u160 ?Daughter\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The final section of the {\i daf} discusses the question of whether an {\i eved ivri} continues his service after the master\u8217's death, and for whom. The rule established in the {\i braisa} is: an {\i eved ivri} works for the son of a deceased master but not for the daughter. An {\i amah ivriyah}, by contrast, goes free upon the master\u8217's death in any case \u8212- she does not continue in service for either a son or a daughter. Similarly, an {\i eved ivri} sold to a non-Jew would not continue working for the non-Jew\u8217's heirs upon his death.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Textual Source\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The derivation proceeds from two verses. One verse says {\i \u8220"va-avadcha shesh shanim\u8221"} \u8212- \u8220"and he shall work for you six years\u8221" \u8212- which implies working {\i for you}, not for your heir. The other verse says {\i \u8220"shesh shanim ya\u8217'avod\u8221"} \u8212- \u8220"he shall work six years\u8221" \u8212- without specifying for whom, implying the six years must be completed regardless. The {\i Gemara} reconciles these: the second verse, which speaks unconditionally of six years, applies to the case of the son. The first verse, which specifies \u8220"for you,\u8221" excludes other heirs.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Son as Continuation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This raises the question: on what basis is the son singled out as the one heir for whom the slave continues to work, rather than some other heir? The {\i Gemara} answers: {\i \u8220"sheken kam tachas aviv\u8221"} \u8212- the son stands in the place of his father; he is the primary continuation of the father\u8217's identity. Two proofs are cited for the principle that the son serves as the father\u8217's continuation. First, {\i yi\u8217'ud}: the Torah permits the master\u8217's son to perform {\i yi\u8217'ud} \u8212- betrothing the {\i amah ivriyah} \u8212- if the father himself did not do so. This demonstrates that the son steps directly into the father\u8217's place in the relevant legal relationships. Second, {\i sdeh achuzah}: if a man\u8217's ancestral field is sold and his son purchases it, the field does not go to the {\i kohanim} at {\i yovel} but reverts to the original owner. This rule, derived through {\i drashot} on the relevant verses, is grounded in the son\u8217's unique status as extension of the father.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Brother Excluded\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Could one argue that the brother, not the son, is a stronger continuation of the deceased \u8212- on the grounds that the brother performs {\i yibum}, which is explicitly described as \u8220"standing in the place of his brother\u8221"? The {\i Gemara} rejects this: {\i yibum} applies only when there is no son. The very fact that {\i yibum} is required only in the son\u8217's absence demonstrates that the son is the primary and natural continuation of the father, and the brother\u8217's role through {\i yibum} is merely a secondary substitute when the primary continuation is absent. Therefore it is the son, not the brother, who is considered to \u8220"stand in the father\u8217's place\u8221" for the purposes of continued service.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Brother Excluded\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} adds one further clarification. One might argue that even if the son is a stronger candidate than the brother, the son still has {\i two} connections to his father that establish continuation \u8212- {\i yi\u8217'ud} and {\i sdeh achuzah} \u8212- while the brother has only one ({\i yibum}). So why does the {\i Gemara} rest its entire argument on the logical priority demonstrated by {\i yibum} yielding to the son\u8217's existence, rather than simply saying the son wins on the basis of two factors to one? The {\i Gemara} explains that the {\i sdeh achuzah} precedent is itself only knowable through the same logical inference: the {\i pesukim} governing {\i sdeh achuzah} do not explicitly specify which heir causes the field to revert to the father. The assignment of that role to the son (and not the brother) is derived precisely because we already know the son is the primary continuation \u8212- which we know from the principle that {\i yibum} exists only where there is no son. Both proofs, in other words, ultimately converge on this single foundational point: the son\u8217's primacy as continuation of the father is a given, from which all else flows. And it is on this basis that the {\i eved ivri} continues to work for the son, but for no other heir.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Kiddushin 18\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Hebrew Slaves Framework\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Amah vs.\u160 ?Eved Overview\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The eighteenth daf of Kiddushin picks up at the bottom of 17b, in the middle of a discussion concerning the {\i amah ivriyah} \u8212- the Hebrew maidservant \u8212- and specifically how a Hebrew slave goes free, as well as the differences between a male and a female slave. The {\i mitzvah} of {\i hanakah}, the obligation to laden a departing slave with gifts at the time of release, has already been treated; now the Gemara turns to a new clause.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 No Service to Non-Heirs\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Mishnah states: {\i amah ivriyah einah ovedet lo et haben velo et habat} \u8212- a Hebrew maidservant does not serve either the son or the daughter of the master. To situate this properly, it helps to draw out the full comparative framework that emerges from the prior discussion. An {\i eved ivri} \u8212- a male Hebrew slave \u8212- who has not undergone the ear-piercing of {\i nirtza} continues to serve out his six-year term for the master\u8217's son, since the son is considered a direct continuation of the father. The six-year arrangement is understood as something like a contract, and a son is its natural continuation. However, the {\i eved ivri} does not serve other heirs \u8212- a daughter or more distant relatives \u8212- because a contract is between persons, not a heritable commodity; you finish a contract for the son as a direct continuation of the father, but not for some other heir who stands in a more attenuated relationship.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i nirtza}, by contrast \u8212- the slave who has chosen to have his ear pierced and to remain in perpetual service \u8212- serves neither the son nor any other heir. His service, which is framed around a bond of love to his master\u8217's household rather than a commercial transaction, terminates at the master\u8217's death. And an {\i amah ivriyah}, the Mishnah now tells us, is likewise governed by this rule: she serves neither the son nor the daughter.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The case of the daughter is worth pausing over. A daughter can, in certain circumstances, inherit the estate when there is no son. And yet she is not considered a \u8220"continuation\u8221" of the father in the relevant legal sense. Inheritance and legal continuation are distinct categories here: the law of inheritance does not translate automatically into the relational standing required for the slave\u8217's contractual obligation to transfer.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Deriving from Nirtza\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara asks: {\i mena hanei milei} \u8212- what is the textual source for the rule that an {\i amah ivriyah} serves neither the son nor the daughter? Rav Pappa citing Rav Adda bar Ahavah points to the verse {\i ve\u8217'af la\u8217'amatcha ta\u8217'aseh ken} \u8212- \u8220"and so shall you do for your maidservant as well.\u8221" This phrase appears in the passage in Devarim dealing with the release of Hebrew slaves and the obligations of {\i hanakah}, and it is immediately adjacent to \u8212- indeed embedded within \u8212- the context of {\i nirtza}, the ear-piercing ceremony.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara establishes the derivation through a {\i hatkasha}, a juxtaposition: {\i hekisho hakatuv lenirtza} \u8212- the Torah has linked the {\i amah ivriyah} to the laws of {\i nirtza}. Just as a {\i nirtza} does not serve the son or the daughter, so too an {\i amah ivriyah} does not serve the son or the daughter: {\i af amah ivriyah einah ovedet lo et haben velo et habat}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But the Gemara immediately presses: is this really what {\i ve\u8217'af la\u8217'amatcha ta\u8217'aseh ken} is coming to teach? There is a {\i baraita} which reads this same phrase as the source for the obligation of {\i hanakah} \u8212- the gift-loading at release \u8212- as it applies to an {\i amah ivriyah}. Could it truly be teaching both things at once?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara works through this carefully. According to the plain sense of the verses ({\i pshat}), one might have thought that {\i ve\u8217'af la\u8217'amatcha ta\u8217'aseh ken} means she too undergoes {\i retzia}, the ear-piercing. But a verse in Shemot that introduces the ear-piercing ritual uses the expression {\i ha\u8217'eved} \u8212- \u8220"the slave\u8221" \u8212- with a specific masculine article, from which the rabbis derive {\i v\u8217'lo amah ivriyah}: a female Hebrew slave does not undergo {\i retzia}. Once the possibility of {\i retzia} is excluded by the verse in Shemot, the phrase in Devarim must be serving a different purpose. The Gemara states: {\i hamani mikayem v\u8217'af la\u8217'amatcha ta\u8217'aseh ken \u8212- l\u8217'haanik} \u8212- I read this as the source for the obligation of {\i hanakah} for an {\i amah ivriyah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Double Derivation Issue\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This raises what the Gemara treats as a problem of double derivation. If {\i ve\u8217'af la\u8217'amatcha ta\u8217'aseh ken} is already employed to teach {\i hanakah}, how can it simultaneously be used as the {\i hatkasha} to {\i nirtza} to teach the rule about not serving the son or the daughter?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara\u8217's resolution is elegant: {\i im kein, nichtov kra l\u8217'amascha kein} \u8212- if it only needed to convey one lesson, the verse could simply have said \u8220"and for your maidservant thus.\u8221" The presence of the word {\i ta\u8217'aseh} \u8212- \u8220"you shall do\u8221" \u8212- is textually superfluous and therefore carries independent meaning. From this extra word, {\i shma mina tarti} \u8212- you may infer two separate legal conclusions: the obligation of {\i hanakah}, and the equation of the {\i amah ivriyah} with the {\i nirtza} regarding service to heirs.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is also worth noting that even without this derivation via the extra word, the proximity of the phrase to the {\i nirtza} passage could itself serve as the basis for the {\i hatkasha}, as adjacency in the text ({\i smuchot}) is itself sometimes sufficient to support a derivation. But the Gemara here takes the path of finding the dual teaching in the single word.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Nirtza & Non-Jewish Sale\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Nirtza Non-Service\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 With the rule for the {\i amah ivriyah} established through the {\i hatkasha} to {\i nirtza}, the Gemara now must prove the underlying premise: that a {\i nirtza} himself does not serve the son or the daughter. This had been assumed in the derivation above, and now the Gemara supplies the textual support.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The source is the verse {\i v\u8217'ratza adonav et ozno bamartze\u8217'a v\u8217'avado l\u8217'olam} \u8212- \u8220"his master shall pierce his ear with an awl, and he shall serve him forever.\u8221" The word {\i l\u8217'olam} appears to create unlimited service, but the Gemara limits it in two ways: first by {\i yovel}, the Jubilee year, which terminates even {\i nirtza} service, and second by reading {\i l\u8217'olam} as bound to the master personally \u8212- not to the master\u8217's heirs. The term {\i avado}, \u8220"his serving,\u8221" refers to the master himself. Because the verse driving perpetual service is specific to the person of the master, there is no basis for extending it to a son or daughter. The reason that the regular {\i eved ivri} does serve the son is the verse {\i sheish shanim ya\u8217'avod} \u8212- \u8220"he shall serve six years\u8221" \u8212- which speaks without qualification and therefore extends through the son as a continuation of the father. That verse does not govern the {\i nirtza}, and so the {\i nirtza}\u8217's service is tethered solely to the living master.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Non-Jew Sale Rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara next addresses a related case: a Hebrew slave sold to a non-Jew. The rule is stated: {\i hanirtza v\u8217'hanimkar l\u8217'oved kochavim eino oved lo et haben velo et habat} \u8212- neither the {\i nirtza} nor one sold to a non-Jew serves the son or the daughter. The source for the non-Jew case is derived by Chizkiya from the verse {\i v\u8217'chishav im konehu} \u8212- \u8220"he shall reckon with his purchaser\u8221" regarding the right and obligation to redeem such a slave. Crucially, the verse says {\i im konehu} \u8212- with his purchaser \u8212- and not with his purchaser\u8217's heirs. From this exclusion, Chizkiya derives that once the non-Jewish master dies, the slave goes free; there is no ongoing service owed to heirs.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava\u8217's Inference\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava draws a broader legal principle from this exclusion. The very fact that the Torah had to specify that the slave does not serve the heirs of a non-Jewish master implies that, as a baseline, non-Jews do have laws of inheritance \u8212- because otherwise there would have been nothing to exclude. If non-Jews had no heritable estate, the slave\u8217's status with heirs would never have been a question in the first place. As Rava states: {\i devar torah oved kochavim yoresh et aviv} \u8212- it is a biblical principle that a non-Jew inherits from his non-Jewish father. The necessity of the exclusion proves the existence of the rule from which the exclusion departs.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Non-Jew Inheritance Laws\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Non-Jew Inherits Father\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 From Rava\u8217's inference, the Gemara pivots into a broader discussion of inheritance law as it applies to non-Jews and converts. Rava\u8217's conclusion \u8212- {\i oved kochavim yoresh et aviv} \u8212- is presented as a biblical proposition. The textual proof he adduces is indirect, derived from the logical structure of the exclusion in the verse about the purchased slave. But the Gemara will also present a more direct scriptural basis.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Convert Rabbinical Inher\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A convert presents a distinct case. The halakhic principle is {\i kekatan shenolad dami} \u8212- a convert is legally like a newborn child. This means that all prior legal family relationships are severed. When a man converts, his non-Jewish father is no longer legally his father; consequently, when the non-Jewish father dies, the convert does not inherit him as a matter of biblical law.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 However, the rabbis enacted that a convert does inherit his non-Jewish father, and this is categorized as {\i midevrei sofrim} \u8212- rabbinic legislation rather than biblical law. The Mishnah provides the case: a convert and his brother who did not convert are jointly inheriting from their non-Jewish father. The convert can say to his non-Jewish brother: you take the idols ({\i avodah zarah}) from the estate and I will take the money \u8212- you take the wine used for idolatrous libation ({\i yayin nesech}, which is {\i assur b\u8217'hana\u8217'ah}, forbidden even to benefit from) and I will take the fruit.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Forbidden Items Division\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This division is permitted because it is understood as a carving out of one\u8217's share rather than a transaction involving forbidden items. However \u8212- and this is a critical distinction \u8212- this is only permitted {\i before} they take physical possession of the estate. Once the convert has taken possession of his share, he can no longer arrange a swap in which he gives up his portion of forbidden items in exchange for permitted items, because at that point he would effectively be selling {\i avodah zarah} for money, which is itself forbidden.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Biblical vs.\u160 ?Rabbinical\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara draws a striking inference from this halakhic distinction between before and after physical possession. One might ask: if the convert inherits his non-Jewish father as a matter of biblical law, then the moment the father dies, the inheritance vests immediately and automatically. There would be no meaningful legal gap between the moment of death and the moment of possession \u8212- the convert would be considered to own his share of the estate the instant his father dies. In that case, any arrangement dividing the forbidden items from the permitted ones would already constitute a transaction in {\i avodah zarah}, and the distinction between \u8220"before possession\u8221" and \u8220"after possession\u8221" would be legally vacuous.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The fact that the Gemara does treat this as a meaningful distinction \u8212- the fact that there is legal space between death and possession \u8212- proves that the convert\u8217's inheritance is {\i only} rabbinic. Rabbinically, the sages could structure the inheritance in a way that gives the convert the latitude to ensure he does not end up taking possession of forbidden items. They could, in effect, hold the vesting of the estate in abeyance until physical possession, so that the convert\u8217's reckoning of his share happens at that moment rather than at the moment of the father\u8217's death. This is only possible at a rabbinic level; a biblical inheritance vests automatically at death and admits of no such flexibility.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafot on Breira\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot raises an important question here. Why doesn\u8217't the arrangement work even after physical possession, on the principle of {\i yesh breira} \u8212- the retroactive clarification doctrine according to which a later determination can retrospectively define what was always the case? If {\i yesh breira} applied, one could say that when the two brothers divide the estate, it becomes retroactively clarified that each person\u8217's portion was always his \u8212- so the convert never \u8220"owned\u8221" the idols even briefly.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot rejects this reasoning with precision: {\i d\u8217'im ken afilu bau l\u8217'reshuso nami eima mehai ta\u8217'ama} \u8212- if {\i breira} were the operative principle, it should work even {\i after} physical possession, since the retrospective clarification would apply regardless of when the division occurs. The fact that the Gemara distinguishes between before and after possession proves that {\i breira} is not the mechanism at work. What matters is specifically the gap between death and possession, and that gap only exists in a rabbinic framework.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Non-Jew Inherits Convert\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then surveys several related permutations. First: can a non-Jew inherit from a convert father? This scenario involves a father who converts while his child does not. When the convert father subsequently dies, does his non-Jewish child inherit? The answer is no, because after the father\u8217's conversion, he is legally like a newborn, severing his prior paternal relationship. The non-Jewish child has no legal claim on the estate.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Convert Inherits Convert\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Second: can a convert inherit from a convert father \u8212- a case where both father and son converted together? Even here, because both underwent conversion and became legally as newborns at that moment, their prior familial relationship is extinguished. Neither biblical nor rabbinic inheritance applies, because there is no {\i gezeirah d\u8217'avdu rabanan} \u8212- no rabbinic enactment \u8212- motivated by any practical concern requiring the creation of inheritance in this case. As the Gemara notes, the rabbis generally do not create inheritance; the one exception was the case of the convert inheriting his non-Jewish father, and that was motivated by a specific concern: {\i shema yachzor lesuro} \u8212- lest the convert, upon learning he will receive no share of his father\u8217's estate, be tempted to abandon his conversion and return to his prior religion. No comparable concern drives the creation of inheritance between two converts.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Moreover, creating inheritance between legally unrelated individuals \u8212- even those who are biologically related \u8212- would muddy the legal waters in ways that could generate serious errors. Biological relationship and legal relationship must be kept clearly distinct, because confusing them could produce errors in areas such as {\i yibbum} (levirate marriage) or other domains where precise legal parentage is critical.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Debt Repayment Ethics\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Mishnah illustrates this with a practical case: if one borrowed money from a convert who then converts together with his sons \u8212- and the convert subsequently dies \u8212- the borrower is not obligated to repay the sons, since they are not the deceased\u8217's legal heirs. The Mishnah adds: {\i ve\u8217'im hechzir ein ruach chachamim nocha heimenu} \u8212- if one did repay them, the sages are not pleased with him.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rashi\u8217's reading of this phrase is that it merely withholds approval rather than leveling a prohibition \u8212- no {\i yasher koach} is forthcoming, but it is not necessarily forbidden. However, the plain language suggests active disapproval. The reason for the disapproval, as the Gemara makes clear, is not that the money was wasted but that such conduct muddies the legal relationship. Making voluntary payment to someone in the position of an heir reinforces \u8212- incorrectly \u8212- the impression that a legal inheritance relationship exists.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i baraita} appears to contradict this, stating that {\i ruach chachamim nocha heimenu} \u8212- the sages {\i are} pleased. The Gemara resolves the contradiction: the two sources are speaking of different cases.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Conversion & Parentage\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The resolution depends on a fundamental distinction in the laws of parentage following conversion: {\i horato velidato shelo bikhdusha} versus {\i horato shelo bikhdusha velidato bikhdusha}. In the first case, both conception and birth occurred before anyone was Jewish \u8212- the father and son converted after the son was already born. Here the severance of legal relationship is total and unambiguous. In the second case, a man and his pregnant wife convert together, and the child is subsequently born Jewish. The child\u8217's conception was outside of holiness, but the birth occurred within it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The halakhic consequence of the second scenario is nuanced. After the mother immerses in the {\i mikvah}, the child continues to gestate within her womb. This ongoing physical relationship with the mother, which continues post-conversion, is sufficient to establish a legal maternal bond. The child is therefore legally his mother\u8217's child. However, the father\u8217's biological connection to the child was completed at the moment of conception \u8212- a moment that preceded the conversion. When the parents converted and became as newborns, the father\u8217's legal relationship to the child was not renewed, because no new biological act of fathering occurred post-conversion. The child is thus legally related to his Jewish mother but not to his Jewish father.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This case \u8212- {\i horato shelo bikhdusha velidato bikhdusha} \u8212- looks much more like an ordinary Jewish family. The child was born Jewish, the mother is the legal mother, and a casual observer might assume the father is also the legal father. For precisely this reason, the question of whether to repay the debt to the child carries different weight in each scenario.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot cites an early version of Rashi\u8217's commentary \u8212- no longer extant \u8212- according to which the case where repayment is praised ({\i ruach chachamim nocha heimenu}) is the case of {\i horato shelo bikhdusha velidato bikhdusha}, since the child appears closer to a natural Jewish heir. Tosafot disagrees, arguing the opposite: {\i adrabba}, that is precisely the case where repayment should {\i not} be praised, because it most easily causes confusion. Since that scenario looks indistinguishable from a normal Jewish father-son relationship, voluntary payment to the child in the role of heir would most powerfully reinforce the mistaken impression of a legal inheritance. The case where the child was also born before conversion \u8212- {\i horato velidato shelo bikhdusha} \u8212- is so clearly not a legal inheritance relationship that no confusion results from an act of generosity, and there the sages can look favorably upon it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Chiya\u8217's Sources\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Chiya bar Avin citing Rabbi Yochanan takes up the question of the biblical basis for non-Jewish inheritance, which Rava had derived indirectly from the exclusion in the verse about the slave. Rav Chiya bar Avin prefers to derive it from explicit scriptural testimony: {\i ki yerushah l\u8217'Eisav nasati et Har Seir} \u8212- \u8220"for I have given Mount Seir as an inheritance to Esav.\u8221" The term {\i yerushah} \u8212- inheritance \u8212- appears explicitly in connection with Esav, who is a non-Jew.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara challenges this: perhaps Esav is a special case, since he is a {\i Yisrael mumar} of sorts, being the son of Yitzchak and thus descended from the patriarchal line, even though he personally did not maintain that covenant identity. Perhaps the rules of inheritance were extended to him out of that particular ancestral dignity rather than as a general principle applicable to all non-Jews.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 To avoid this objection, the Gemara cites a second verse: {\i ki livnei Lot nasati et Ar yerushah} \u8212- \u8220"for I have given Ar as an inheritance to the children of Lot.\u8221" Lot was not a descendant of Yitzchak or Yaakov, and there is no comparable ancestral dignity to invoke. The explicit use of {\i yerushah} here establishes that non-Jewish inheritance is a general biblical principle.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then examines why each Amora did not adopt the other\u8217's approach. Rav Chiya bar Avin did not employ Rava\u8217's inference from the slave verse because, he argues, the exclusion of {\i yorshei konahu} (heirs of the purchaser) is not explicit in the verse \u8212- it requires inference \u8212- and he would rather anchor the principle of non-Jewish inheritance in an explicit {\i pasuk} using the term {\i yerushah}. Rava, for his part, did not rely on the verses about Esav and Lot because he considers them inconclusive: in the case of Esav, {\i mishum kevodo d\u8217'Avraham shani} \u8212- one might argue that it was out of honor for Avraham that his grandson received this bequest, and that does not prove a universal principle. Ultimately both authorities arrive at the same substantive conclusion, each finding it supported by their preferred textual strategy.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Eved-Amah Comparison\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Freedom Methods Listed\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now presents a {\i baraita} that compares the laws of the {\i eved ivri} and the {\i amah ivriyah} systematically: {\i yesh b\u8217'Ivri she\u8217'ein b\u8217'Ivriyah v\u8217'yesh b\u8217'Ivriyah she\u8217'ein b\u8217'Ivri} \u8212- there are rules that apply to the male Hebrew slave that do not apply to the female, and vice versa.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i baraita} states: {\i yesh b\u8217'Ivri shehu yotzei b\u8217'shanim u\u8217'v\u8217'yovel u\u8217'v\u8217'misas ha\u8217'adon, ma she\u8217'ein kein b\u8217'Ivriyah} \u8212- the male slave goes free through the completion of six years ({\i shanim}), through the Jubilee ({\i yovel}), and through the death of the master ({\i misas ha\u8217'adon}), none of which applies to the {\i amah ivriyah}. Conversely, the {\i amah ivriyah} goes free through the appearance of physical signs of maturity ({\i simanim}), which the male slave does not. Furthermore, the {\i amah ivriyah} cannot be resold ({\i einah nimkeres l\u8217'shneis}), and she can be redeemed against the will of a relevant party ({\i mifdin otah al karcho}).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This {\i baraita} is striking on multiple counts. Everything it says seems to conflict with what has been assumed throughout the preceding discussion. We have been operating on the assumption that {\i shanim}, {\i yovel}, and {\i misas ha\u8217'adon} apply equally to both the {\i eved ivri} and the {\i amah ivriyah}, and the verse {\i ve\u8217'af la\u8217'amatcha ta\u8217'aseh ken} was the textual basis for that equation. Yet this {\i baraita} appears to deny it entirely. And the claim that an {\i amah ivriyah} can be redeemed against someone\u8217's will seems puzzling \u8212- the entire concept of {\i giron kesef} (proportional buyback) already permits redemption of a male slave against his master\u8217's wishes.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yiudah Interpretation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara challenges the {\i baraita} immediately: does the {\i amah ivriyah} really not go free through {\i shanim} and {\i yovel}? Does our Mishnah not teach {\i yeseirah alav amah ivriyah} \u8212- that the {\i amah ivriyah} has {\i more} paths to freedom than the {\i eved ivri}, not fewer \u8212- since she has all the methods available to him {\i plus} the additional path of {\i simanim}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Sheshet resolves the tension by positing that the {\i baraita} is discussing a case where the master has already performed {\i yiud} \u8212- formal designation of the {\i amah ivriyah} as a wife. Once {\i yiud} has occurred, she transitions from the status of a slave to the status of a married woman. As a married woman, the ordinary mechanisms of slave release \u8212- {\i shanim}, {\i yovel}, {\i misas ha\u8217'adon} \u8212- no longer apply. She can only be released through a {\i get}, a bill of divorce.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara presses: is this not obvious? If she has become an {\i eshet ish} through {\i yiud}, of course she requires a {\i get} rather than the slave-release mechanisms. The Gemara answers that the {\i chidush} is not as self-evident as it seems. One might have thought that {\i yiud} creates a form of marriage that remains embedded within the framework of servitude \u8212- analogous, perhaps, to the relationship between an {\i eved ivri} and the {\i shifcha kena\u8217'anit} given to him by his master, which is a union that exists within and by virtue of the context of slavery. If {\i yiud} were such a relationship \u8212- one that belongs to the category of servitude rather than constituting a full-fledged {\i kiddushin} \u8212- then the mechanisms of slave release might still apply, since what would need to be dissolved is, in essence, a slave status rather than a marriage. The Torah\u8217's ruling that {\i she\u8217'era k\u8217'suta v\u8217'onata lo yigra} \u8212- that a wife acquired through {\i yiud} retains all the rights of a full wife \u8212- is precisely what refutes this {\i hava amina} and establishes that {\i yiud} creates genuine marriage.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The implication then follows: {\i im lo yi\u8217'ada yotza af b\u8217'simanim} \u8212- if the master did not perform {\i yiud}, then all the ordinary paths of release apply, including the additional path of {\i simanim}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It must be acknowledged that this resolution is a forced reading of the {\i baraita}. The {\i baraita} explicitly contrasts {\i shanim} and {\i yovel} with {\i simanim} \u8212- it says she does not go free through the former but does go free through the latter. Reconciling this by saying the {\i baraita} is only discussing the post-{\i yiud} case, in which she goes free with {\i simanim} but not with {\i shanim} and {\i yovel}, strains the plain reading considerably. More naturally, the {\i baraita} reflects a distinct {\i tannaic} tradition that conceived of the {\i amah ivriyah} as standing in a fundamentally different legal category from the {\i eved ivri}, one with minimal overlap in their respective paths to freedom. Given that the {\i pesukim} in Shemot dealing with the {\i amah ivriyah} never explicitly mention {\i shanim} or {\i yovel}, and that the apparent equation comes only through the verse {\i ve\u8217'af la\u8217'amatcha ta\u8217'aseh ken} applied within the context of {\i hanakah}, there is something textually coherent and even attractive about this {\i baraita}\u8217's approach. If one thinks about the actual circumstance of an {\i amah ivriyah} \u8212- a girl sold into servitude before the age of twelve \u8212- the fewer the overlapping laws of slavery between her situation and that of an adult male slave, the more her legal status reflects something categorically distinct from classic {\i avdut}. The Gemara, however, reconciles the {\i baraita} with the predominant position, even at some cost to the plain reading.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 No Resale of Amah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i baraita} also states that the {\i amah ivriyah} cannot be resold \u8212- {\i einah nimkeres l\u8217'shneis} \u8212- while an {\i eved ivri} can be resold. This is a new {\i halakhah} that has not yet been addressed. The Gemara signals that the broader treatment of this rule is coming, and the subsequent analysis will spell out the details.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Eved Resale Limits\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Before arriving at the full treatment of the {\i amah ivriyah}\u8217's resale prohibition, the Gemara addresses an apparent contradiction: does it not also teach that even an {\i eved ivri} faces limits on resale? A {\i baraita} states: {\i begneivato v\u8217'lo b\u8217'khiflo} \u8212- he is sold for his theft, but not for the fine. If a thief stole something worth a thousand and has sufficient assets to pay back the principal ({\i keren}) but not the double payment ({\i kefel}), he is not sold into slavery to cover the outstanding penalty. Further: {\i begneivato v\u8217'lo b\u8217'zimamo} \u8212- he is sold for his own theft, but not if he is an {\i ed zomem}, a conspiring witness who testified that someone else owed a penalty and was shown to have lied. Even though the {\i ed zomem} must pay {\i ka\u8217'asher zamam la\u8217'asot l\u8217'achiv} \u8212- whatever he plotted to do to his fellow \u8212- being sold into slavery does not apply to him. And crucially: {\i keivan shenimkar pa\u8217'am achat shuv i ata rashai l\u8217'mokhro} \u8212- once he has been sold one time for a particular theft, he cannot be sold again. The singular {\i begneivato} implies a single act of being sold.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 So the {\i eved ivri} is also subject to a form of non-resale restriction, at least in the context of {\i genevah}. How does this square with the {\i baraita}\u8217's implication that an {\i eved ivri} can be resold while an {\i amah ivriyah} cannot?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava resolves this: {\i lo kashya, kan b\u8217'gneivah achat, kan b\u8217'shtei gneivot} \u8212- there is no contradiction; the two cases are different. The distinction concerns whether we are speaking of one {\i gneivah} or two. Here Tosafot and Rashi read the resolution in opposite directions, and the difference tracks a textual variant in the Gemara.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 According to Tosafot\u8217's reading: for a {\i single} act of theft ({\i gneivah achat}), if the value of the thief\u8217's service does not cover the full amount owed, he can be sold multiple times \u8212- sold, completes his term, is sold again \u8212- until the debt is paid off. This is consistent with the singular {\i begneivato}: it is one {\i gneivah}, and he keeps being sold for that one act. However, for a {\i second} act of theft \u8212- a separate {\i gneivah} \u8212- he cannot be sold. He has already entered the condition of {\i eved ivri} once, and that experience and its lessons are held to be sufficient; a new theft does not reset his eligibility for a second enslavement.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rashi reads it in precisely the reverse: for one {\i gneivah}, even a large one, he can only be sold once \u8212- one sale per act of theft. But for each separate {\i gneivah} from a separate party, he can be sold once per offense.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye responds to Rava\u8217's formulation by noting that the word {\i begneivato} in the verse actually suggests multiple possible readings and that the plain language implies a broad scope. Abaye therefore reformulates the resolution differently: {\i kan b\u8217'adam echad, kan b\u8217'shnei bnei adam} \u8212- the distinction is not between one {\i gneivah} and two, but between whether the theft was from one person or from two different people. According to Tosafot\u8217's reading of Abaye\u8217's position, if someone steals once or multiple times from a single person, it is all treated as a single category of {\i gneivah} from that person, and the person may be sold \u8212- and resold \u8212- for that entire category. But if he has stolen from a second, different person, he cannot be sold for that second offense. Rashi reads Abaye\u8217's resolution in the opposite direction.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A further {\i baraita} reinforces the analysis with concrete cases. If one stole something worth a thousand and is himself worth five hundred, he can be sold and sold again ({\i nimkar vechozer venimkar}) until the debt is covered \u8212- since for one {\i gneivah} multiple sales are permitted. If one stole something worth five hundred and is himself worth a thousand, {\i eino nimkar klal} \u8212- he is not sold at all. It would be unjust to compel someone to serve six years of slavery when the market value of that service exceeds the debt; there is no mechanism for partial terms of servitude. One is either sold for the full six years or not sold at all.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gra\u8217's variant text ({\i girsa}) reads {\i nimkar v\u8217'eino chozer venimkar} \u8212- sold but not resold \u8212- which is the foundation for Rashi\u8217's contrary reading. The analysis presented here follows the standard text as Tosafot reads it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Eliezer Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Eliezer takes an even more restrictive position, one that Rava acknowledges as logically compelling even if it leads to a near-total elimination of the practice. Rabbi Eliezer says: {\i im haya gneivo keneged mimkaro nimkar} \u8212- the thief is only sold into slavery if the value of his six-year service is {\i exactly} equal to the value of what he stole. If the {\i gneivah} is worth more than his service, he is not sold (everyone agrees on this). But if his service is worth more than the {\i gneivah}, Rabbi Eliezer also says he is not sold.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The logical basis for Rabbi Eliezer\u8217's position, as Rava articulates it, is entirely parallel to the reasoning underlying the universally agreed-upon case. Everyone agrees that if the thief is worth a thousand and the theft was worth five hundred, he is not sold \u8212- because the verse says {\i v\u8217'nimkar bignevato}, \u8220"he is sold {\i for} his theft,\u8221" and the Gemara reads this as requiring that the entirety of the sale value goes toward paying off the theft. If only half his sale value goes to the theft, that is not selling him {\i for} his theft in the required sense. By precisely the same logic, Rabbi Eliezer argues, if the theft is worth a thousand but his service is only worth five hundred, he is not sold \u8212- because again, he would be sold for only half his theft. Selling him would not pay off the {\i gneivah} in full, and the verse requires that being sold suffice to discharge the debt. Rava comments: {\i beha zachinhu Rabbi Eliezer lerabbanan} \u8212- Rabbi Eliezer\u8217's logic outshines the rabbis here, in the sense that the textual reasoning is fully consistent and compelling, even though in practice it means that an {\i eved ivri} sold for {\i genevah} becomes an almost theoretical category.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Forced Redemption\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Returning to the {\i baraita}\u8217's final clause \u8212- {\i umifdin otah al karcho}, that the {\i amah ivriyah} is redeemed against someone\u8217's will \u8212- Rava initially understands this as meaning against the will of the {\i master}: one can compel the master to accept the return of the purchase price and release her. But Abaye rejects this reading. Forcing back a redemption against the master\u8217's will \u8212- returning his money and recovering the slave \u8212- is already applicable to an {\i eved ivri} through the mechanism of {\i giron kesef}. There is nothing distinctive about the {\i amah ivriyah} in that respect. Furthermore, Abaye notes, if it means forcing the master to accept an IOU rather than actual payment, that too is untenable: {\i nakit marganita beyadei, nehavi lei chaspa?} \u8212- the master is holding a pearl in his hand; are we going to force him to accept a pottery shard? The father who sold his daughter presumably had no money to begin with; any IOU he could offer would be essentially worthless.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye therefore reinterprets: {\i be\u8217'al korcho de\u8217'av} \u8212- the forced redemption is against the will of the {\i father}. If the father has available funds, he can be compelled to use them to redeem his daughter from slavery. The motivation is {\i mishum pgam mishpacha} \u8212- the concern for family honor. Having a daughter in servitude reflects badly on the entire family, and the relatives \u8212- or the father himself, per Rashi \u8212- can be legally coerced to undo it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then raises an objection: if this is the reasoning, why not similarly coerce the family to redeem a male {\i eved ivri} who sold himself? The answer: {\i hadar azil umazvin nafshieh} \u8212- he would simply turn around and sell himself again. Forcing a buyback accomplishes nothing if the person can immediately repeat the act.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The same objection seems to apply to the {\i amah ivriyah}: if the father is forced to redeem her, could he not immediately resell her? This is precisely where the earlier ruling becomes operative. The Gemara answers: {\i katani einah nimkeres ushenit} \u8212- we have already established that an {\i amah ivriyah} cannot be resold. Therefore, compelling the father to redeem his daughter is a meaningful and lasting act: once he buys her back, he cannot sell her again, and the family honor is permanently restored. By contrast, there is no corresponding restriction preventing an {\i eved ivri} from reselling himself, so forcing a redemption of a male slave would accomplish nothing permanent.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot sharpens this by disagreeing with Rashi about {\i who} is being compelled. Tosafot understands the compulsion as being directed not at the father but at the other family members \u8212- along the lines of the biblical provision that a kinsman may redeem a sold relative ({\i ben dodo o dodo yigalenu}). The family members are compelled to act for the sake of communal honor. The concern about the father simply pocketing the proceeds and reselling his daughter does not arise, because the resale is prohibited.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This position, the Gemara notes, is the view of Rabbi Shimon \u8212- which will be elaborated in the discussion of who may be resold and under what conditions.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Selling Daughter Rules\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Father\u8217's Resale Rights\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i baraita} now addresses the father\u8217's authority to sell his daughter and the limits on that authority. The baseline rule is: {\i mocher adam et bito le\u8217'ishut veshoneh} \u8212- a man may betroth his daughter (sell her for purposes of {\i ishut}, marriage) and repeat the act. How is this possible? As long as the daughter remains a minor ({\i ketanah}) and has not yet entered {\i nisuin} (full marriage), she remains under her father\u8217's authority. If she was betrothed ({\i kiddushin} was performed) but the betrothal was dissolved through divorce or death of the betrothed before {\i nisuin}, she returns entirely to her father\u8217's control and he may betroth her again. Thus multiple betrothals are possible while she remains a minor.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i baraita} further states: {\i leshifchuto veshoneh} \u8212- he may also sell her as a slave and repeat this. If she was sold and then went free \u8212- for example, through the completion of six years, by {\i yovel}, or through {\i misas ha\u8217'adon} \u8212- but she is still a minor ({\i ketanah}), the father may sell her again. Similarly: {\i le\u8217'ishut achar shifchut} \u8212- he may also arrange her marriage after she has been a slave. All of these are permitted.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The one explicit prohibition in this {\i baraita} is: {\i lo leshifchut achar ishut} \u8212- he may not sell her as a slave after she has already been in a state of marriage ({\i ishut}). Going from the status of a wife to the status of a slave represents a profound diminution of standing, and the Torah prohibits this path.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 First Dispute Source\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is the position of the {\i tanna kamma}. The Gemara will derive it from the verse {\i lo yimshol l\u8217'mochrah b\u8217'vigdo bah} \u8212- \u8220"he shall not have authority to sell her in her betrayal\u8221" (or \u8220"in his garment over her\u8221") \u8212- a phrase whose precise reading lies at the heart of the tannaic dispute.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 According to the {\i tanna kamma} (identified as Rabbi Akiva in the subsequent analysis), the word {\i bigdo} is read as {\i beged} \u8212- garment \u8212- which, in the idiomatic language of marriage (as in the passage in Megillat Rut where spreading one\u8217's cloak symbolizes marriage), refers to the act of marriage itself. The verse is thus saying: once he has spread his cloak over her \u8212- once she has been in a state of {\i ishut} \u8212- he may no longer sell her. This produces the prohibition of {\i shifchut achar ishut}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Shimon Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Shimon extends the prohibition: {\i keshem she\u8217'ein adam mocher et bito l\u8217'shifchah achar ishus, kach ein mocher et bito l\u8217'shifchah achar shifchah} \u8212- just as one may not sell a daughter as a slave after she has been in a state of marriage, so too one may not sell her as a slave after she has already been in a prior state of slavery. For Rabbi Shimon, once the daughter has undergone either {\i ishut} or {\i shifchut}, a subsequent sale into slavery is forbidden. The only path that remains available is selling her into {\i ishut} again, since the prohibition is specifically against entering {\i shifchut} a second time after any prior \u8220"sale\u8221" \u8212- whether that prior sale was {\i ishut} or {\i shifchut}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is the position underlying the earlier ruling that the father cannot resell the {\i amah ivriyah} after she has been redeemed, and it explains why forcing the family to redeem her is an effective and permanent remedy.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Bigdo Textual Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The disagreement among the {\i tannaim} turns on how to read the word in the verse. The text as written (the {\i masorah}, or {\i kesiv}) is {\i bagdo}, without a {\i yud}, which in rabbinic reading yields {\i bagad} \u8212- betrayal, treachery. This textual reading ({\i yesh em l\u8217'masores}) produces the prohibition of {\i shifchut achar shifchut}: once the father has betrayed his daughter by selling her into slavery once, he may not do so again. The verse is warning against a second betrayal following the first.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The word as read aloud (the {\i mikra}, or {\i keri}) is {\i bigdo}, with a {\i yud}, which, as Rashi explains, corresponds to {\i beged} \u8212- garment \u8212- the image of marriage. This oral reading ({\i yesh em l\u8217'mikra}) yields the prohibition of {\i shifchut achar ishut}: once he has wrapped her in the garment of marriage, she may not be sold into slavery.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Three Positions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The three positions thus map onto distinct approaches to the verse. Rabbi Akiva rules {\i yesh em l\u8217'mikra} and derives only the prohibition of {\i shifchut achar ishut}. Rabbi Eliezer rules {\i yesh em l\u8217'masores} and derives only the prohibition of {\i shifchut achar shifchut}. Rabbi Shimon holds {\i yesh em l\u8217'mikra v\u8217'gam l\u8217'masores} \u8212- both readings are legally operative simultaneously \u8212- and therefore derives both prohibitions: neither {\i shifchut achar ishut} nor {\i shifchut achar shifchut} is permitted. For Rabbi Shimon, the daughter who has once experienced either marriage or servitude may never again be placed into slavery by her father.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This synthesis accounts for the position attributed to Rabbi Shimon throughout the preceding discussion, including the ruling that an {\i amah ivriyah} cannot be resold and that it is therefore effective \u8212- and appropriate \u8212- to compel the family to redeem her.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Kiddushin 19\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Yi\u8217'ud: Erusin or Nisu\u8217'in?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava\u8217's Core Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The daf opens in the middle of 18b, in the context of a broader discussion of the {\i ama ivriya} \u8212- the Hebrew maidservant sold by her father while still a minor. A central feature of that institution is {\i yi\u8217'ud}: the possibility that the master, or his son, may designate her as a wife, effected either by the words {\i im livno yiyadena} or {\i asher lo ye\u8217'ada}, using the original purchase money as {\i kesef kiddushin}. The remainder of today\u8217's material is devoted entirely to working through the legal ramifications of {\i yi\u8217'ud}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava bar Avuh opens with a pointed question: {\i yi\u8217'ud nisuin osa o erusin osa} \u8212- does {\i yi\u8217'ud} create full marriage ({\i nisuin}) or merely betrothal ({\i erusin})? The question is structurally parallel to an earlier discussion about {\i kiddushei bi\u8217'ah}, where the same issue arose: since that form of kiddushin involves immediate physical intimacy, does it bypass the {\i erusin} stage and go straight to {\i nisuin}? Here the concern is analogous. The girl is already living in the master\u8217's home as a slave. When he performs {\i yi\u8217'ud}, designating her as his wife, does that act produce full {\i nisuin} \u8212- given that she is already present in his household \u8212- or is it only {\i erusin}, leaving {\i nisuin} as a separate subsequent stage?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The practical difference \u8212- the {\i nafka mina} \u8212- is considerable: does he inherit her if she dies? If he is a {\i kohen}, may he become {\i tamei} for her? May he annul her vows? All of these are consequences of {\i nisuin}, not of {\i erusin} alone. Does the obligation of {\i she\u8217'era kesuta ve\u8217'onata} \u8212- her sustenance, clothing, and conjugal rights \u8212- take effect immediately upon {\i yi\u8217'ud}, or only after a further step? All of these questions hinge on the answer.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shifcha Achar Ishut\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} offers a first attempt at resolution: {\i ta shema}, come and hear. The Torah prohibits what we discussed on the previous daf \u8212- either {\i shifcha achar shifcha} or {\i shifcha achar ishut}, depending on how one reads the verse. One reading of {\i be\u8217'vigdo vah} \u8212- interpreting the word as {\i beged}, garment \u8212- is that once the master has \u8220"spread his garment over her,\u8221" he may no longer sell her. This prohibits selling her as a slave after {\i yi\u8217'ud} has already created a marital bond.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Now, if the master performs {\i yi\u8217'ud} and subsequently dies, the {\i Gemara} notes that he cannot then sell her again \u8212- but that he {\i could} perform {\i yi\u8217'ud} again, meaning he could be {\i mekadesh} her. This raises the critical difficulty: if {\i yi\u8217'ud} created {\i nisuin}, then once {\i nisuin} has occurred, the father has no further authority over her whatsoever \u8212- {\i kivan de\u8217'niset shuv ein le\u8217'aviha reshut bah}. Why would the Torah need a special verse to prevent the father from reselling her? If she is fully married, she is already entirely out of his domain. The very need for a verse to forbid this act seems to imply that she remains under some residual paternal control \u8212- which is only possible if {\i yi\u8217'ud} created only {\i erusin}, not {\i nisuin}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This appears to be a compelling proof. However, Rav Nachman bar Yitzchak deflects it. He argues that {\i be\u8217'vigdo vah} in this context is not discussing {\i yi\u8217'ud} at all \u8212- it is discussing a completely separate scenario. The {\i braita} is teaching that if a father first gave his daughter in ordinary {\i kiddushin} to a man, and that man subsequently died, the father may no longer sell her as a slave. It is {\i not} addressing the case where she was initially sold as a {\i shifcha} and then received {\i yi\u8217'ud}. This is admittedly a strained reading of the {\i braita}, but it neutralizes the proof. The discussion of her being returned to her father\u8217's authority is not evidence about the nature of {\i yi\u8217'ud} \u8212- it concerns a case of ordinary {\i kiddushin} where the father loses the right to sell her thereafter.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Braita Reinterpreted\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now presents a {\i braita} that initially appears unrelated to the {\i erusin}-versus-{\i nisuin} question but is in fact being mined for a proof. The {\i braita} records a dispute: one opinion holds that the father may not sell his daughter to close relatives (where {\i yi\u8217'ud} would be impossible, since {\i kiddushin} with a relative does not take effect), while Rabbi Eliezer permits this. All agree, however, that the father {\i may} sell her to a man who is prohibited from marrying her by a {\i lav} but for whom {\i kiddushin} would nonetheless be valid if performed \u8212- such as a {\i kohen gadol} marrying an {\i almana}, or a {\i kohen} marrying a {\i gerushah}. Even though such a marriage is forbidden, {\i kiddushin} in those cases is {\i tofes}, legally effective, and therefore {\i yi\u8217'ud} remains a theoretical possibility.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} asks: this is still trying to resolve the original question of whether {\i yi\u8217'ud} creates {\i erusin} or {\i nisuin}, yes \u8212- and keeps working through the implications.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Almanah/Gerushah Proof\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now asks about the {\i almana} case more closely. How does this girl come to be an {\i almana} in the first place? If she married herself while a minor, the {\i kiddushin} would not have been valid \u8212- a minor cannot contract her own marriage. It must therefore be that her father previously gave her in {\i kiddushin} to some man, that man died, and now she is an {\i almana}. But in that case, how can the father now sell her as a {\i shifcha} at all? That would be {\i shifcha achar ishut} \u8212- selling her into servitude after she has already been in a marital bond \u8212- which is prohibited according to the {\i tanna} we have been following!\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Nachman bar Yitzchak resolves this by proposing that the scenario works according to Rabbi Yosi b\u8217'Rabbi Yehuda, who holds {\i ma\u8217'ot harishonot lav le\u8217'kiddushin nitnu} \u8212- that the original purchase money was not designated as {\i kesef kiddushin}. Under this view, when the father initially sold his daughter, that act was purely a sale into servitude. The {\i kiddushin} occurred only later, through the act of {\i yi\u8217'ud} performed by the master \u8212- an independent transaction between the master and the girl in which the father played no role. Accordingly, the father never sold her {\i le\u8217'ishut}. The {\i ishut} happened separately, on its own. When the master dies, the father is therefore not in the position of someone who sold his daughter into marriage. He sold her into servitude, period. The subsequent marital bond was created independently. And that is why it is now permissible to discuss him selling her again \u8212- to a {\i kohen} or {\i kohen gadol} \u8212- because it would be {\i shifcha achar shifcha}, not {\i shifcha achar ishut}. The father was never the one who created the {\i ishut}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ma\u8217'os Rishonos Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The deeper issue now emerges. Was the original purchase money \u8212- the {\i ma\u8217'ot rishonot} \u8212- the {\i kesef kiddushin}, or was it the remainder of the service still owed? The question matters enormously for whether the father was ever a party to the {\i kiddushin}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The suggestion had been that the entire discussion of selling an {\i almana} or {\i gerushah} to a {\i kohen} works only according to Rabbi Yosi b\u8217'Rabbi Yehuda \u8212- who holds that the original money was {\i not kesef kiddushin}, and therefore the father was not the one who married her off. But the {\i Gemara} notes that there is a view \u8212- that of Rav Nachman bar Yitzchak \u8212- that even Rabbi Yosi b\u8217'Rabbi Yehuda may agree that it is the original money that serves as {\i kesef kiddushin}. If so, the father effectively did marry her off, and the problem of {\i shifcha achar ishut} returns. How then can the {\i braita} discuss him selling her as an {\i almana} or {\i gerushah} to a {\i kohen}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara}\u8217's answer is that the scenario follows Rabbi Eliezer, who holds the opposite of what we have been assuming: according to Rabbi Eliezer, {\i shifcha achar ishut} is actually permitted \u8212- it is {\i shifcha achar shifcha} that is prohibited. This resolves the difficulty neatly: if {\i shifcha achar ishut} poses no barrier, the father can sell her regardless of whether the original money was {\i kesef kiddushin}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yi\u8217'ud = Erusin\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having worked through these preliminary difficulties, the {\i Gemara} now moves toward a definitive resolution. The {\i Rambam} rules that {\i yi\u8217'ud} creates only {\i erusin} \u8212- which is indeed where the dominant weight of the {\i Gemara}\u8217's proofs has pointed throughout this discussion. The critical argument is this: the very fact that we are discussing scenarios where, after {\i yi\u8217'ud}, the father retains sufficient control to sell his daughter again or to contract a new marriage for her \u8212- all of this presupposes that after {\i yi\u8217'ud} she remains only an {\i arusa}, not a full {\i nisuah}. If {\i yi\u8217'ud} had created {\i nisuin}, she would have been entirely removed from her father\u8217's domain and none of these subsequent discussions would have any traction.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} does attempt one further rejoinder: perhaps one can distinguish between {\i nisuin} effected by the father and {\i nisuin} effected by the girl herself \u8212- {\i nisuin didah} versus {\i nisuin de\u8217'aviha}. But the {\i Gemara} rejects this distinction immediately. The reason a father loses authority over his daughter after {\i nisuin} is not because he performed a particular act that now prevents a subsequent act. It is the simple reality that she has become part of another man\u8217's household. Whether the {\i nisuin} was arranged by her father or arose independently through {\i yi\u8217'ud}, the result \u8212- her integration into another household \u8212- would be the same. Therefore the distinction cannot be sustained, and the proof stands: {\i yi\u8217'ud} creates only {\i erusin}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Conditions for Yi\u8217'ud\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Minor Son Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Reish Lakish now raises a new question: {\i mahu she-ye\u8217'ayeid adam le\u8217'veno katan} \u8212- can a father perform {\i yi\u8217'ud} designating the {\i ama ivriya} for a son who is himself a minor? The Torah says {\i im livno yiyadena} \u8212- he may designate her for his son. Does \u8220"his son\u8221" mean any son, however young? Or does the son have to be an adult?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This question has a structural uniqueness. In ordinary {\i kiddushin}, a minor boy can never be the subject of a {\i kiddushin} arrangement. A girl can be married off while a minor, because her father acts on her behalf. But a minor boy cannot enter {\i kiddushin}: he cannot perform the act himself (lacking legal capacity), and there is no {\i shlichut le\u8217'katan} \u8212- an agent cannot act on behalf of a minor. Therefore, in ordinary law, {\i kiddushei katan} \u8212- a minor boy being betrothed \u8212- simply does not exist as a Torah institution. The question here is whether the unique Torah institution of {\i yi\u8217'ud}, which explicitly places the father in the active role, changes this calculus. The father himself is performing the {\i yi\u8217'ud} \u8212- perhaps the Torah permits it even for a minor son, since the father is the actor.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Two positions are suggested. One reads {\i beno} expansively \u8212- \u8220"his son, any son,\u8221" even a {\i katan}. The other argues {\i beno dumya dideyh}: the son must parallel the father \u8212- just as the father is an adult, so too must the son be an adult. The logic is that while the father performs the act, what is fundamentally occurring is the establishment of a marital bond for the son, and a minor cannot be a party to such a bond.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Minor Marriage Proof\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Zeira proposes a proof from a verse in the context of adultery: {\i ish asher yinaf et eshet re\u8217'ehu} \u8212- a man who commits adultery with his neighbor\u8217's wife. The verse specifies {\i ish} \u8212- a man \u8212- to exclude a minor: a minor who commits an act of adultery is not subject to the capital penalty. Similarly, the phrase {\i asher yinaf et eshet re\u8217'ehu} \u8212- adultery with a man\u8217's wife \u8212- excludes {\i eshet katan}, the wife of a minor: one who commits adultery with the wife of a minor is not liable.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But this immediately raises a question: where does one ever encounter the concept of \u8220"the wife of a minor\u8221"? For the Torah to need to exclude it, the institution must exist in some form. And if {\i yi\u8217'ud} can create a marital bond even for a minor son, that would be precisely such a case. The {\i Gemara} infers: if the Torah is excluding {\i eshet katan} from the laws of adultery, it implies that such a status exists \u8212- and the source of that status would be {\i yi\u8217'ud} performed by the father for his minor son. This would prove that {\i yi\u8217'ud} can indeed be performed for a {\i katan}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yibum Alternative\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Ashi counters with an alternative source for {\i eshet katan}: {\i yibum}. If a man dies and his surviving brother is a boy between nine and thirteen years old, the physical act of {\i bi\u8217'ah} creates {\i yibum} \u8212- levirate marriage \u8212- even without intent, since {\i yibum} does not require conscious decision-making in the way that ordinary {\i kiddushin} does. If the mere physical act of intercourse, performed even by a minor over the age of nine, creates a valid marital bond through {\i yibum}, then the Torah\u8217's exclusion of {\i eshet katan} from the laws of adultery addresses precisely this scenario. It need not be addressing {\i yi\u8217'ud} at all. The {\i Gemara} therefore concludes that the verse does not prove anything about {\i yi\u8217'ud} for a minor son \u8212- the {\i yibum} scenario sufficiently explains the Torah\u8217's need for that exclusion.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rashi/Tosafot Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A fundamental dispute between Rashi and Tosafot emerges here, concerning what the verse is actually teaching \u8212- the {\i kamashmalan} \u8212- in the {\i yibum}-of-a-minor case.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rashi holds that if a boy of nine performs the act of {\i yibum} with his {\i yevamah}, the {\i yibum} is fully valid: she is his wife in every legal sense \u8212- he inherits her, he may become {\i tamei} for her, and so forth. {\i De\u8217'mid\u8217'oraita chazei lei} \u8212- she is biblically bound to him through the {\i zika}, and his act of {\i bi\u8217'ah} completes the acquisition. The {\i kamashmalan} of the verse is therefore not that the marriage is invalid, but rather that despite the validity of the marriage, there is no liability for adultery in such a case. The reasoning appears to be that since the husband is not yet a full adult, the marriage \u8212- while legally real \u8212- has not yet manifested as a complete marital reality, and therefore the capital punishment for adultery does not apply.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot disagrees sharply. Tosafot reads the verse as teaching that the marriage itself is not a Torah-level marriage at all. What might have appeared to be a valid {\i yibum} \u8212- because there is a {\i zika}, there is the physical act, there is the {\i yevamah} \u8212- is in fact only rabbinic in force. {\i Kamashmalan d\u8217'eino koneh ela mi\u8217'derabbanan} \u8212- the acquisition is only rabbinic. The verse teaches that on the Torah level, such a {\i yibum} does not create a marriage. Under this view, the practical consequences diverge dramatically from Rashi\u8217's: according to Tosafot, if another man subsequently has relations with this woman, she does not need a {\i get} \u8212- since there was no Torah-level marriage \u8212- whereas according to Rashi she is fully married and would require a {\i get} or {\i chalitzah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Aivo\u8217's Rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Returning to the substance of {\i yi\u8217'ud} itself, the {\i Gemara} cites a statement of Rabbi Aivo in the name of Rabbi Yannai: {\i ein yi\u8217'ud ela be\u8217'gadol, ein yi\u8217'ud ela mi\u8217'da\u8217'as} \u8212- {\i yi\u8217'ud} can only be performed for an adult son, and it can only be performed {\i mi\u8217'da\u8217'as}. The {\i Gemara} asks: why state both conditions? Is this not redundant?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} answers that Rabbi Aivo is giving a reason rather than two separate rules: the reason {\i yi\u8217'ud} requires a {\i gadol} is precisely {\i because} it requires {\i da\u8217'as} \u8212- conscious awareness or consent. Since {\i da\u8217'as} is required, and a {\i katan} cannot supply the requisite {\i da\u8217'as}, it follows that the son must be an adult. The two clauses function together as principle and rationale.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This raises a further question about the nature of {\i da\u8217'as} in this context. Does it mean merely that the son must be informed, or does it mean that the son is the true principal in the {\i kiddushin} \u8212- that the father acts only as his authorized representative? The Rambam rules that the son must grant his father explicit permission to perform the {\i yi\u8217'ud} on his behalf. Under that reading, the father is genuinely acting as the son\u8217's agent, and the son\u8217's {\i da\u8217'as} is the operative one. This would explain why the son must be an adult: only an adult can authorize an agent, and only an adult can be the party entering into marriage.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Girl\u8217's Da\u8217'as Required\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having established the requirement of the son\u8217's {\i da\u8217'as}, the {\i Gemara} now turns to a separate question: does the girl herself need to be part of the process in some meaningful way? Must {\i yi\u8217'ud} be performed with her knowledge?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Abaye\u8217's Source\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye son of Rav Avuha derives this from the verse itself: {\i asher lo ye\u8217'adah} \u8212- \u8220"that he did not designate her\u8221" \u8212- which he reads as a positive requirement: {\i melamed shetzarich le\u8217'ya\u8217'adah}, the verse teaches that he must designate her, meaning he must make the {\i yi\u8217'ud} known to her. Rashi explains the term as {\i lashon dei\u8217'ah} \u8212- an expression of knowledge or awareness \u8212- indicating that she must be informed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 When Kiddushin Occurs\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} then explains the {\i yi\u8217'ud} requirement by connecting it to Abaye\u8217's teaching: {\i be\u8217'kiddushei yi\u8217'ud ve\u8217'aliba de\u8217'Rabbi Yosi b\u8217'Rabbi Yehuda de\u8217'amar ma\u8217'os rishonot lav le\u8217'kiddushin nitnu}. Why does this requirement of her {\i da\u8217'as} specifically arise according to Rabbi Yosi b\u8217'Rabbi Yehuda? Because if we follow the position that the original purchase money is the {\i kesef kiddushin} \u8212- that the {\i kiddushin} was effected at the time of the original sale by the father \u8212- then the girl need not be involved. The father is the active party, and in ordinary {\i kiddushin} effected by the father, the daughter need not be informed. But according to Rabbi Yosi b\u8217'Rabbi Yehuda, the original money was {\i not kesef kiddushin}, and the {\i kiddushin} occurs later through the remaining service owed to the master \u8212- a transaction that takes place between him and her directly, without the father. Since the father is out of the picture and the {\i kiddushin} is happening between the two of them, she must be made part of the process, which requires at minimum that she be informed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Nachman\u8217's View\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Nachman bar Yitzchak, however, argues that even if one holds that the original money {\i is} the {\i kesef kiddushin} \u8212- even according to the majority view \u8212- there remains a special scriptural requirement that the girl be informed. {\i Shani hacha de\u8217'amar Rachmana ye\u8217'adah} \u8212- here the Torah itself says \u8220"he designated her,\u8221" imposing a unique condition on {\i yi\u8217'ud} that does not apply in ordinary {\i kiddushin}. Even though technically the {\i kiddushin} flows from the money given to the father, the Torah nonetheless demands that when {\i yi\u8217'ud} is actually performed, the girl must be made aware. It is a {\i gezeirat hakatuv} \u8212- a scriptural decree unique to this institution.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Rambam rules simply that a man cannot perform {\i yi\u8217'ud} on the {\i ama ivriya} \u8212- either for himself or for his son \u8212- except {\i mi\u8217'da\u8217'atah}: with her awareness. Whether {\i da\u8217'atah} means she must actively acquiesce, or merely that she must be informed, remains somewhat open. The Rambam does not state that she can refuse and invalidate the {\i yi\u8217'ud}, which suggests that {\i da\u8217'atah} means notification rather than consent. The analogy to {\i mi\u8217'un} \u8212- a rabbinic mechanism by which a minor girl can reject a marriage \u8212- is instructive: since {\i mi\u8217'un} is a rabbinic institution, one cannot import it into this context to argue that she must agree on a Torah level.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Ma\u8217'os Rishonos Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Yosi\u8217's Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having woven the {\i ma\u8217'ot rishonot} debate through multiple earlier discussions, the {\i Gemara} now sets out Rabbi Yosi b\u8217'Rabbi Yehuda\u8217's position explicitly. It is cited from a {\i braita}: the Torah juxtaposes {\i ye\u8217'adah} (designation) and {\i hepdah} (redemption) \u8212- {\i asher lo ye\u8217'adah}. From this juxtaposition Rabbi Yosi b\u8217'Rabbi Yehuda derives that {\i yi\u8217'ud} can only be performed when there is still enough time remaining in the girl\u8217's service for her to redeem herself.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Prutah Requirement\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tzarich she\u8217'yish\u8217'heh bayom kedei fidyah} \u8212- there must be enough remaining on the day of {\i yi\u8217'ud} to constitute a {\i prutah}\u8217's worth of service. Just as the girl can redeem herself by buying back her remaining service with a {\i prutah}, the master can perform {\i yi\u8217'ud} using that same {\i prutah} of remaining service as the {\i kesef kiddushin}. But if there is no service left \u8212- if the six years are at their final instant and there is not a {\i prutah}\u8217's worth of labor remaining \u8212- {\i yi\u8217'ud} cannot be performed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Mikan amar Rabbi Yosi b\u8217'Rabbi Yehuda:} if there is enough time left in the day for her to perform a {\i prutah}\u8217's worth of service, she is {\i mekudeshet}; if not, she is not. And the {\i Gemara} draws the explicit conclusion: {\i alma kasvar ma\u8217'ot rishonot lav le\u8217'kiddushin nitnu} \u8212- this demonstrates that in Rabbi Yosi\u8217's view, the original purchase money was not designated as {\i kesef kiddushin}. The {\i kiddushin} is being effected only through the remaining service, and that is why a {\i prutah}\u8217's worth of remaining service is indispensable.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Nachman Counters\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Nachman bar Yitzchak again presents the counter-position: one need not conclude that Rabbi Yosi b\u8217'Rabbi Yehuda rejects the {\i ma\u8217'ot rishonot} as {\i kesef kiddushin}. One can maintain that even he holds the original money becomes {\i kesef kiddushin}, and nonetheless require a {\i prutah} of remaining service. {\i Shani hacha de\u8217'amar Rachmana ve\u8217'hepdah} \u8212- it is a {\i gezeirat hakatuv} arising from the juxtaposition of {\i ye\u8217'adah} and {\i hepdah}. The Torah is imposing an independent condition: {\i yi\u8217'ud} may only be performed when redemption remains a live option \u8212- i.e., when at least a {\i prutah} of service remains. But the {\i kesef kiddushin} itself is still the original money; the {\i prutah} of remaining service is not itself the {\i kiddushin} but rather a threshold condition mandated by the verse.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Yi\u8217'ud Extends Normal Law\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Minor Daughter Kiddushin\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now uses the framework established by {\i yi\u8217'ud} \u8212- particularly the mechanism through which {\i kiddushin} can occur without the father being the active party \u8212- to derive a broader ruling about ordinary {\i kiddushin}. The question under consideration is whether an underage daughter can herself accept {\i kesef kiddushin}, given that her father has signaled his approval. This touches directly on the {\i yi\u8217'ud} case: according to Rabbi Yosi b\u8217'Rabbi Yehuda, the {\i kiddushin} occurs between the master and the girl directly, without the father. She is a {\i ketanah}, yet the {\i kiddushin} is valid. How is that possible?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Father\u8217's Authorization\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Amram cites Rav Nachman: {\i amar adam le\u8217'vito ketana, tzei ve\u8217'kabeli kiddushayich} \u8212- a father can instruct his underage daughter to go and accept {\i kesef kiddushin} on his behalf. The daughter goes, receives the money from the prospective husband, and the {\i kiddushin} is valid. This ruling is derived from the logic of {\i kiddushei yi\u8217'ud}: since we must explain how {\i yi\u8217'ud} creates valid {\i kiddushin} even when the father is not the active party and the girl is a {\i ketanah}, the answer must be that the father\u8217's intent combined with her physical act of receiving is sufficient. And once we establish that principle, we can apply it to ordinary {\i kiddushin} as well.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafot Problem\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot raises a significant difficulty: how can a minor girl effect a {\i kinyan} \u8212- an acquisition \u8212- of the ring or money? The problem is not merely one of {\i shlichut} (agency); even setting aside the inability of a minor to be an agent, the fundamental issue is that a {\i katan} or {\i ketanah} has no capacity for {\i kinyan}. Acquisition requires legal capacity, which a minor lacks. How then can the girl\u8217's receiving of the {\i kesef} constitute a valid {\i kinyan}, even if the father has authorized it?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two Da\u8217'asos Model\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot resolves this through the concept of {\i da\u8217'as acheres makneh oso} \u8212- a transfer effected by the {\i da\u8217'as} of another party. Two distinct mental acts combine through the medium of the girl\u8217's hand. The prospective husband provides the {\i da\u8217'as} to transfer the object to her \u8212- his intent to convey the ring makes the transfer effective even without her independent capacity. Her hand serves as the physical vehicle through which the ring passes. At the same time, the father provides the {\i da\u8217'as} that she should be married \u8212- his intent constitutes the operative will behind the {\i kiddushin} itself. She does the act of receiving, the husband provides the {\i da\u8217'as} for the transfer of the object, and the father provides the {\i da\u8217'as} for the {\i kiddushin}. These two {\i da\u8217'asos} work through her hand, and together they constitute a valid {\i kiddushin}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This model is precisely what explains {\i kiddushei yi\u8217'ud} according to Rabbi Yosi b\u8217'Rabbi Yehuda: when the father sold his daughter into servitude with the implicit expectation of {\i yi\u8217'ud}, he provided \u8212- at the time of the original sale \u8212- the {\i da\u8217'as} that if the master wished to marry her, he consented to it. When the master later performs {\i yi\u8217'ud}, the girl receives back the service still owed to her (which serves as the {\i kesef kiddushin}), and the combination of the father\u8217's earlier {\i da\u8217'as} and her act of receiving creates the valid {\i kiddushin}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Loan as Kesef\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava cites Rav Nachman with a further application of this framework: {\i ha\u8217'mekadesh be\u8217'milveh ve\u8217'yesh lo mashkon mekudeshes} \u8212- if a man had lent money to a woman and held a {\i mashkon} (collateral) from her, and he subsequently was {\i mochel} (waived) the debt in the context of {\i kiddushin}, she is {\i mekudeshes}. The {\i hana\u8217'ah} \u8212- the benefit she receives from the waiver of her debt \u8212- serves as the {\i kesef kiddushin}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mashkal Analogy\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} explains the application of this case through the {\i mashkon}. Tosafot reads it as follows: one might have thought that when the creditor still holds the {\i mashkon}, there is insufficient {\i gemirut da\u8217'as} \u8212- finalization of intent \u8212- to constitute a proper {\i kiddushin}. After all, even though he declared the debt waived, he has not yet returned her collateral. Perhaps the {\i mochel} is not complete, and therefore the {\i hana\u8217'ah} is insufficient to serve as {\i kesef kiddushin}. The ruling teaches that even in such a case, {\i mekudeshes} \u8212- the {\i kiddushin} is valid.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The source for this ruling is again the framework of Rabbi Yosi b\u8217'Rabbi Yehuda. {\i Lav amar Rabbi Yosi b\u8217'Rabbi Yehuda ma\u8217'ot rishonot lav le\u8217'kiddushin nitnu} \u8212- the original money is not the {\i kesef kiddushin}; it is the remaining service that serves as {\i kesef kiddushin}. That remaining service is effectively a debt owed to the master: she owes him labor, and {\i hi gufa mashkon hi} \u8212- she herself is the collateral. Her presence in his household, her ongoing service, constitutes the security for the \u8220"debt\u8221" of labor. When he performs {\i yi\u8217'ud} and is {\i mochel} the remaining service, he is using that waiver \u8212- with her as the {\i mashkon} \u8212- as the {\i kesef kiddushin}. {\i Ve\u8217'ki meshayar bah shaveh prutah miyad havei kiddushin} \u8212- and from this case we can learn that the parallel scenario of a loan with a {\i mashkon} also works. The {\i Gemara} acknowledges that the analogy is not perfectly tight \u8212- in the {\i mashkon} case the creditor holds the woman\u8217's object as collateral, while in the {\i yi\u8217'ud} case the woman herself is the collateral \u8212- but the principle is being established.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Yi\u8217'ud Procedure\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yi\u8217'ud Declaration\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now sets out the mechanics of how {\i yi\u8217'ud} is performed: {\i omer lah bifnei shnayim harei at mekudeshet li, harei at me\u8217'oreset li} \u8212- he declares before two witnesses that she is betrothed to him. This is the standard {\i erusin} declaration, made in the presence of two valid witnesses, as required for any {\i kiddushin}. The {\i Gemara} adds that this can be performed even at the very end of the six years \u8212- {\i afillu b\u8217'sof shesh}, even {\i samuch le\u8217'shkiat hachama}, within moments of sunset on the last day. From that point on, {\i noheg bah minhag ishut ve\u8217'eino noheg bah minhag shifcha} \u8212- he treats her as a wife and no longer as a slave.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Yosi Condition\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yosi b\u8217'Rabbi Yehuda reiterates his condition: {\i im yesh she\u8217'hut bayom kedei la\u8217'asot imo shaveh prutah, mekudeshes; ve\u8217'im lav, einah mekudeshes} \u8212- she is {\i mekudeshes} only if, at the time of {\i yi\u8217'ud}, there remains enough time in the day for her to perform a {\i prutah}\u8217's worth of service; if not, the {\i yi\u8217'ud} does not take effect.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mashkal Delayed Effect\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} then introduces a {\i mashal} \u8212- an analogy: {\i le\u8217'omer le\u8217'ishah hitkadshi li me\u8217'achshav ve\u8217'le\u8217'achar shloshim yom} \u8212- \u8220"be married to me from now, and it shall take effect in thirty days.\u8221" If another man attempts to betroth her within those thirty days, she is nonetheless {\i mekudeshes la\u8217'rishon} \u8212- betrothed to the first man. Since the {\i kiddushin} was stated to begin {\i me\u8217'achshav}, from now, it has already commenced, and a subsequent betrothal cannot override it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Acha b\u8217'rei d\u8217'Rava explains that this {\i mashal} is addressed specifically to the Rabbis \u8212- the position of {\i chachamim} who hold that the original money is the {\i kesef kiddushin}. For the Rabbis, {\i kiddushei yi\u8217'ud} works precisely like {\i me\u8217'achshav ve\u8217'le\u8217'achar zman}: the money changes hands at the time of the initial sale \u8212- {\i me\u8217'achshav} \u8212- and the {\i kiddushin} is fully realized only years later at the time of {\i yi\u8217'ud} \u8212- {\i le\u8217'achar zman}. The two events are bound together: the first provides the {\i kesef}, and the second completes the {\i kiddushin}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Why Mashkal Matters\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} asks {\i peshita} \u8212- isn\u8217't this obvious? If it began with the original money, of course a subsequent act cannot override it. The {\i Gemara} answers: {\i mah d\u8217'teima hala lo amar lah me\u8217'achshav} \u8212- one might have thought that since the master never explicitly said {\i me\u8217'achshav} at the time of the original sale, and since he had not yet even decided at that moment that he wished to perform {\i yi\u8217'ud}, perhaps the whole {\i kiddushin} takes effect only at the moment of {\i yi\u8217'ud} and is entirely prospective. Under that reading, anything occurring before the {\i yi\u8217'ud} declaration would not be subject to the earlier money at all. The ruling therefore teaches: {\i kamashmalan} \u8212- when he ultimately performs {\i yi\u8217'ud}, the {\i kiddushin} is treated retroactively as having begun when the money changed hands, analogous to {\i me\u8217'achshav}, and the entire sequence is bound together.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 No Interference Rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The practical consequence follows directly: if during the period between the initial sale and the {\i yi\u8217'ud} declaration, a third party attempts to betroth this woman, the subsequent {\i kiddushin} does not take effect. Just as in the {\i me\u8217'achshav ve\u8217'le\u8217'achar shloshim yom} case the earlier {\i kiddushin} prevails, so too in {\i yi\u8217'ud} \u8212- once the master ultimately declares {\i yi\u8217'ud}, the {\i kiddushin} is treated as having begun when the money was first paid, and any intervening attempt by another man to create {\i kiddushin} with her is void.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Kiddushin 20\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Original Money vs.\u160 ?Servi\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yi\u8217'ud Timing\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Daf 20 continues the discussion of {\i eved ivri}, picking up in the middle of 19b. The central question concerns which money constitutes the {\i kesef kiddushin} for {\i yi\u8217'ud}: is it the original purchase price, or is it whatever value of service remains at the time the master decides to perform {\i yi\u8217'ud}? One practical difference is whether {\i yi\u8217'ud} can be performed even when less than a {\i perutah}\u8217's worth of service remains. The {\i gemara} addresses this through a series of {\i braitot} that examine the related question of when, precisely, the {\i kiddushin} process begins.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If the original money is the {\i kesef kiddushin} \u8212- even though that characterization is only confirmed retroactively when the master chooses to perform {\i yi\u8217'ud} \u8212- the question arises: what happens if another man attempts to betroth the girl during the interval between purchase and {\i yi\u8217'ud}? The {\i braita} rules that the second man\u8217's {\i kiddushin} would not take effect if the master ultimately proceeds with {\i yi\u8217'ud}. The transaction is treated as though the master had declared at the moment of purchase, {\i \u8220"at mekudeshet li me\u8217'achshav\u8221"} \u8212- you are betrothed to me from now \u8212- with completion deferred to the moment of {\i yi\u8217'ud}. Even though no such declaration was made, and even though the master had not yet made up his mind, the structure functions like a {\i me\u8217'achshav u-le\u8217'achar zman}, locking out any intervening {\i kiddushin}. This is a significant {\i chiddush} in understanding how the {\i kesef kiddushin} operates when it is identified with the original purchase money.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Father\u8217's Marriage\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A second {\i braita} addresses a related scenario. A man sells his daughter as an {\i amah ivriyah} and then, before the master has had the opportunity to perform {\i yi\u8217'ud}, the father goes and betroths her to another man \u8212- to Shimon. The {\i braita} states: {\i sichek ba-adon u-mekudeshet lashani} \u8212- he laughs in the face of the master, and she is betrothed to Shimon. This ruling, that Shimon\u8217's {\i kiddushin} does take effect, is attributed to Rabbi Yose b\u8217'Rabbi Yehuda.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kesef Kiddushin Dispute\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The reasoning follows directly from Rabbi Yose b\u8217'Rabbi Yehuda\u8217's position on the nature of the {\i kesef kiddushin}. In his view, the {\i kesef kiddushin} for {\i yi\u8217'ud} is not the original purchase money but rather the remainder of the service \u8212- the value of the labor still owed. Since no {\i kiddushin} process has yet commenced under this view, the father retains the ability to betroth his daughter to another man, and that betrothal is fully binding.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i chachamim}, by contrast, rule that the master may still perform {\i yi\u8217'ud} \u8212- {\i im ratza leyayeid, meyayeid} \u8212- because they hold that the original money serves as {\i kesef kiddushin}, and retroactively, if {\i yi\u8217'ud} is performed, that original money renders the earlier {\i kiddushin} as though it had been made {\i me\u8217'achshav}. Shimon\u8217's {\i kiddushin} therefore does not take hold. If the master does not perform {\i yi\u8217'ud}, she would be betrothed to Shimon. The entire question is thus {\i tluim ve-omdim} \u8212- suspended and contingent on the master\u8217's eventual decision.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mashal Comparison\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then introduces a {\i mashal}: the situation is comparable to a man who says to a woman, {\i harei at mekudeshet li le\u8217'achar shloshim yom} \u8212- you are betrothed to me after thirty days \u8212- without the addition of {\i me\u8217'achshav}. If another man betroths her during those thirty days, she is betrothed to the second man, because the first man\u8217's declaration contains no operative {\i me\u8217'achshav} to lock out intervening {\i kiddushin}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} asks: {\i mashal le-man} \u8212- whose position does this {\i mashal} illustrate? It cannot be the {\i rabbanan}, since they allow the master to still perform {\i yi\u8217'ud} and thereby override Shimon\u8217's {\i kiddushin}. Rav Acha the son of Rava therefore concludes that the {\i mashal} illustrates the position of Rabbi Yose b\u8217'Rabbi Yehuda.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then clarifies the added value of this {\i mashal}. One might have thought: even according to Rabbi Yose b\u8217'Rabbi Yehuda, since the master has the power to perform {\i yi\u8217'ud} at any moment he chooses \u8212- unlike the thirty-day case where a fixed interval makes it clear that nothing is operative \u8212- perhaps the ever-present possibility of {\i yi\u8217'ud} should be sufficient to lock out Shimon even without a {\i me\u8217'achshav}. The {\i mashal} comes to teach that we do not say this. According to Rabbi Yose b\u8217'Rabbi Yehuda, since the {\i kesef kiddushin} is only the remainder of the service and no {\i kiddushin} has yet taken effect, the father may betroth her to Shimon and it is binding \u8212- notwithstanding the fact that {\i yi\u8217'ud} could have been performed at any moment.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The two positions thus produce symmetric but opposite results: according to the {\i chachamim}, using the original money as {\i kesef kiddushin} is sufficient to function as {\i me\u8217'achshav} even without an explicit declaration, blocking Shimon\u8217's {\i kiddushin}. According to Rabbi Yose b\u8217'Rabbi Yehuda, even the open-ended and ever-present possibility of {\i yi\u8217'ud} does not block Shimon, because until {\i yi\u8217'ud} is actually performed, nothing has begun.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Stipulation vs.\u160 ?Yi\u8217'ud\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 No Yi\u8217'ud Stipulation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A third {\i braita} raises yet another configuration. A man sells his daughter as an {\i amah ivriyah} and stipulates with the master at the time of sale: {\i al menat shelo leyayeid} \u8212- on the condition that there will be no {\i yi\u8217'ud}. She is sold for service only; the master is not to marry her. The question is whether such a stipulation can validly exclude {\i yi\u8217'ud} from the institution of {\i amah ivriyah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Meir vs.\u160 ?Rabbanan\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Meir rules that the stipulation is upheld \u8212- {\i niskayem ha-tnai}. The {\i rabbanan} disagree: if the master subsequently wishes to perform {\i yi\u8217'ud}, he may do so \u8212- {\i im ratza leyayeid, meyayeid} \u8212- because the stipulation has no force.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Void Stipulation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i rabbanan}\u8217's reasoning is grounded in the principle of {\i matneh al mah she-katuv ba-Torah}: one who stipulates against the way the Torah has defined an institution \u8212- {\i kol ha-matneh al mah she-katuv ba-Torah, tna\u8217'o batel} \u8212- the stipulation is void.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Marriage Obligations\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then raises a challenge to Rabbi Meir\u8217's position from a {\i braita} dealing with marriage. If a man betroths a woman on condition that he has no obligations of {\i she\u8217'er, kesut, ve\u8217'onah} \u8212- sustenance, clothing, and marital relations \u8212- the ruling is that the {\i kiddushin} takes effect but the stipulation is void: {\i harei zo mekudeshet ve-tna\u8217'o batel}. This too is the position of Rabbi Meir. If a man fundamentally intends to perform {\i kiddushin}, the {\i kiddushin} takes effect. But he cannot alter the Torah-defined parameters of the institution. It is a package; if you enter it, you accept the whole of it. How then can Rabbi Meir permit the stipulation against {\i yi\u8217'ud}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yehuda\u8217's Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Before resolving the tension regarding Rabbi Meir, the {\i gemara} notes the intermediate position of Rabbi Yehuda: {\i be-davar she-be-mamon, tna\u8217'o kayam} \u8212- a stipulation against a monetary obligation is valid. Rabbi Yehuda distinguishes between {\i onah}, which is a personal obligation and cannot be stipulated away, and the obligations of {\i she\u8217'er} and {\i kesut}, which are financial in nature. Since financial obligations are the sort of thing a person can waive \u8212- they are akin to a debt \u8212- the Torah leaves them subject to the parties\u8217' agreement.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is analogous to the law of a {\i shomer}: the Torah sets default liability rules for a {\i shomer chinam} and a {\i shomer sachar}, but a person may stipulate to expand or contract those defaults, precisely because they are monetary. One way to understand this is that the Torah\u8217's rules for a {\i shomer} represent presumed contractual defaults \u8212- they reflect what most people intend absent explicit agreement \u8212- and explicit contrary terms simply override those defaults. Applied to marriage, Rabbi Yehuda reasons that while the personal obligation of {\i onah} cannot be contracted away, the financial obligations of maintenance and clothing can be.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Meir takes the stricter view: even monetary stipulations against Torah-defined institutional obligations are void. The challenge, then, is how Rabbi Meir can simultaneously hold that a stipulation against {\i yi\u8217'ud} is valid in the {\i amah ivriyah} case. The answer lies in the position of Chizkiyah, addressed in the following section.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Le\u8217'Amah Derasha\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chizkiyah\u8217's Derasha\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} introduces Chizkiyah\u8217's teaching to resolve the apparent inconsistency in Rabbi Meir\u8217's position. The Torah uses the phrase {\i ki yimkor ish et bito le\u8217'amah} \u8212- when a man sells his daughter as a maidservant. The word {\i le\u8217'amah} is superfluous, and Chizkiyah derives from it: {\i pe\u8217'amim she\u8217'eino mochrah ela le\u8217'amah bilvad} \u8212- there are circumstances under which the sale is purely for service, without any accompanying right of {\i yi\u8217'ud}. The Torah thus itself contemplates a version of the institution in which {\i yi\u8217'ud} is absent. This is not, in Chizkiyah\u8217's reading, a stipulation that overrides the Torah; it is a distinct institutional form that the Torah itself authorizes.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This resolves the tension in Rabbi Meir\u8217's position. Rabbi Meir does not allow parties to modify an institution by stipulating against the Torah\u8217's definition. But Chizkiyah teaches that the Torah itself provides for two forms of the {\i amah ivriyah} arrangement: one that includes the potential for {\i yi\u8217'ud}, and one \u8212- {\i le\u8217'amah bilvad} \u8212- that does not. Rabbi Meir therefore holds that the stipulation works not because the parties have overridden the Torah, but because the Torah itself validates this alternative form.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Pasuk Allocation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i rabbanan} do not accept Chizkiyah\u8217's reading. They understand the institution as a single package \u8212- {\i amah ivriyah} always comes with the right of {\i yi\u8217'ud} \u8212- and therefore a stipulation against {\i yi\u8217'ud} is void. What, then, do the {\i rabbanan} do with the word {\i le\u8217'amah}? The {\i gemara} explains that they require it for a different teaching: {\i melamed she-mochrah li-pesulim} \u8212- it teaches that a father may sell his daughter to someone who is {\i pasul} for marriage, meaning someone whom the Torah prohibits her from marrying at the level of a {\i lav}, such as a {\i kohen} who would be marrying a {\i gerushah}, or a {\i mamzer}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is a significant ruling: even though the sale into {\i amah ivriyah} is predicated on the possibility of {\i yi\u8217'ud}, and {\i yi\u8217'ud} with a {\i pasul} would be forbidden, the Torah nonetheless permits the sale. The institution is not dissolved by the fact that {\i yi\u8217'ud} is practically barred. The prohibition gets in the way of exercising the right, but it does not change the institutional structure. This is precisely the distinction the {\i rabbanan} draw: you cannot stipulate to change the institution, but circumstances can prevent the exercise of a right without voiding the institution itself.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i rabbanan} understand the two cases as follows. When a father sells his daughter to a {\i pasul} with no stipulation, the institution is intact; {\i yi\u8217'ud} simply cannot be exercised. When a father stipulates against {\i yi\u8217'ud} with someone who is not a {\i pasul}, he is attempting to alter the institution \u8212- and that the {\i rabbanan} reject. For Rabbi Meir, the calculus runs in the opposite direction, as will become clear.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Eliezer\u8217's Reading\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Eliezer offers a different allocation of the {\i pesukim}. He argues that there is no need for the word {\i le\u8217'amah} to teach the permissibility of selling to a {\i pasul}, because that can already be derived from another verse: {\i im ra\u8217'ah be\u8217'einei adoneha asher lo ye\u8217'adah} \u8212- if it is evil in the eyes of the master such that he does not perform {\i yi\u8217'ud}. Rabbi Eliezer reads {\i im ra\u8217'ah} as referring to a halachically problematic marriage \u8212- {\i she-ra\u8217'ah be-nisu\u8217'eha}, that there is something wrong with the marriage. The verse is thus already describing the case of a {\i pasul}, where the master cannot perform {\i yi\u8217'ud} because the marriage would be forbidden, and yet the sale has taken effect. From this verse alone one learns that a sale to a {\i pasul} is valid. The word {\i le\u8217'amah} is therefore available for a larger {\i chiddush}: {\i melamed she-mochrah li-krovim} \u8212- even a sale to a relative, where {\i yi\u8217'ud} would not merely be forbidden but would be halachically ineffective ({\i kiddushin} not being {\i tofes} at all among {\i arayot}), the sale is nonetheless valid.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kal VaChomer\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} asks: why is a {\i pasuk} needed to permit the sale to {\i pesulim}? A {\i kal va-chomer} would seem to suffice: if {\i kiddushin} itself takes effect {\i be-di\u8217'avad} even when it involves an {\i issur lav} \u8212- as in the case of a {\i kohen} who marries a {\i gerushah} \u8212- then certainly a sale, which does not yet constitute {\i kiddushin}, should be valid. The {\i gemara} responds that the {\i kal va-chomer} fails because it is not symmetric. The father\u8217's power in the context of marriage is broader than his power in the context of sale: a father can betroth his daughter even when she is a {\i na\u8217'arah}, whereas he can only sell her when she is a {\i ketanah}. Since his power in selling is more limited, one might have thought he cannot sell her to a {\i pasul}. The verse {\i le\u8217'amah} therefore explicitly teaches that he can.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Institution vs.\u160 ?Fact\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The final point in this section concerns the question of {\i krovim} and the mirror-image positions of Rabbi Meir and the {\i rabbanan}. The {\i rabbanan} hold that {\i krovim} \u8212- relatives for whom {\i kiddushin} would be entirely ineffective \u8212- are excluded, and a sale to them is not valid. But a sale to a {\i pasul} \u8212- where {\i kiddushin} would be {\i tofes be-di\u8217'avad} \u8212- is permitted, because the institution remains intact; only the exercise of {\i yi\u8217'ud} is blocked by circumstance. Rabbi Eliezer, by contrast, permits even a sale to {\i krovim}, reading {\i im ra\u8217'ah be\u8217'einei adoneha} to cover that case as well.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 For Rabbi Meir, the picture is striking and, as the {\i gemara} acknowledges, quite strange. Rabbi Meir uses {\i le\u8217'amah} to teach that a father may stipulate against {\i yi\u8217'ud}, producing an institutional form of {\i amah ivriyah} that is purely for service. He uses {\i im ra\u8217'ah be\u8217'einei adoneha} to teach the permissibility of selling to a {\i pasul}, mirroring where Rabbi Eliezer learns {\i krovim}. But Rabbi Meir holds like the {\i rabbanan} that a sale to {\i krovim} is invalid.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The asymmetry is genuinely puzzling. Rabbi Meir permits the parties to define the institution without {\i yi\u8217'ud} through stipulation, yet holds that the institution cannot validly exist when {\i yi\u8217'ud} is simply impossible due to the {\i karov} relationship. The {\i rabbanan} are internally consistent in the opposite direction: they prohibit the stipulation because the institution is indivisible, yet permit the sale to {\i krovim} because the institution is intact and only the circumstances prevent {\i yi\u8217'ud}. Rabbi Meir\u8217's position \u8212- that deliberate exclusion works but factual impossibility does not \u8212- is, as the {\i gemara} itself notes, quite difficult to understand intuitively.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Father vs.\u160 ?Son\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Father/Son Rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now addresses a specific {\i braita} that states: one may sell his daughter to her grandfather (the father of the seller) but may not sell her to her brother (the son of the seller). A second {\i braita} states that one may not sell to either. The {\i gemara} resolves the contradiction: the second {\i braita}, which prohibits both, follows the {\i rabbanan} who hold that {\i krovim} are excluded categorically. The first {\i braita} \u8212- which permits the sale to the grandfather but not to the brother \u8212- requires an explanation of whose view it represents.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yi\u8217'ud Possibility\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} concludes: {\i le\u8217'olam ke-Rabbanan} \u8212- the first {\i braita} also follows the {\i rabbanan}. But the {\i rabbanan} concede in the case of the grandfather \u8212- {\i modu Rabbanan heicha de-ika tzad yi\u8217'ud} \u8212- because there, while the master himself (the grandfather) cannot perform {\i yi\u8217'ud} with his own granddaughter, his son can. If the seller has a brother \u8212- that is, another son of the grandfather who is not a {\i karov} to the girl \u8212- {\i yi\u8217'ud} remains structurally possible. The institution is therefore intact, and the sale is valid.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This reasoning prompts further questions. What if the grandfather has no other son, or no son old enough to perform {\i yi\u8217'ud} within the six-year term? Does the structural availability of {\i yi\u8217'ud} in the abstract suffice, or must {\i yi\u8217'ud} be practically possible in the specific circumstances? The {\i gemara} does not resolve this, and it remains an open question.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Yovel Context\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rambam Yovel\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 At this point, a fundamental contextual question arises: when did the laws of {\i eved ivri} actually apply? The Rambam ({\i Hilchot Shemitah ve-Yovel}, chapter 10, {\i halacha} 9) rules explicitly: {\i be-zman she-ha-yovel noheig, noheig din eved ivri} \u8212- the institution of {\i eved ivri} is operative only when the {\i yovel} is operative. When the {\i yovel} does not function, none of these laws apply. The {\i yovel} ceased to be operative already before the end of the First Temple period, from the time of the Assyrian exile under Sancheriv, when the northern tribes were displaced and the requirement of having all the tribes settled in the land \u8212- a precondition for {\i yovel} \u8212- was no longer met. Accordingly, by the latter part of the First Temple era, the laws of {\i eved ivri} were no longer practically applicable. The extended discussion of the {\i gemara} is thus theoretical in its halachic application, though not diminished in its moral and conceptual weight.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Treatment Rights\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Begapo Yavo\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now turns to a new {\i braita} examining the personal rights of the {\i eved ivri}. The verse states: {\i im be-gapo yavo, be-gapo yeitzei} \u8212- if he comes in by his body alone, he leaves by his body alone. The {\i gemara} will explore what this means in practice.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Eliezer ben Yaakov\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Eliezer ben Yaakov reformulates the principle as: {\i yechidi nichnas, yechidi yeitzei} \u8212- he enters alone, he exits alone. The {\i gemara} asks what distinguishes this formulation from the first.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava\u8217's Reading\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava explains {\i be-gapo yavo, be-gapo yeitzei}: if he enters whole in body, he leaves whole in body. The verse teaches {\i she-eino yeitzei be-roshei evarim} \u8212- that he does not go free on account of loss of limb, as a {\i eved kena\u8217'ani} would. If a master knocks out the eye or tooth of a {\i eved kena\u8217'ani}, the slave goes free. The {\i eved ivri} does not obtain freedom on this basis. This might appear to disadvantage him, but in fact it works in his favor: the master must pay the full range of injury compensation applicable to a free person \u8212- {\i tza\u8217'ar, sheves, ripuy, boshet} \u8212- and that payment may well be sufficient for the {\i eved ivri} to purchase his own freedom outright, leaving him with money to spare. The verse clarifies that bodily injury is compensated but does not of itself terminate the servitude.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Abaye\u8217's Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye challenges Rava: is this teaching not already derived from the verse {\i lo yeitzei ke-tzeit avadim} \u8212- he shall not go free as slaves go free \u8212- which was studied earlier? Why does this verse need to add anything?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Injury Compensation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} answers: if we had only the earlier verse, one might have thought that the rule works differently for the {\i eved ivri} \u8212- that he both goes free and receives compensation for the injury. The verse {\i be-gapo yavo be-gapo yeitzei} therefore teaches definitively that bodily injury does not serve as a basis for freedom at all. He is compensated, but he does not go free on that account.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Wife & Children\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shifcha Provision\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Nachman bar Yitzchak explains the teaching of Rabbi Eliezer ben Yaakov \u8212- {\i yechidi nichnas, yechidi yeitzei}, he enters alone and exits alone. If the {\i eved ivri} enters with a wife and children, the master provides him with a {\i shifcha kena\u8217'anis} to produce slave children from the union. If he enters without a wife and children, the master does not provide a {\i shifcha kena\u8217'anis}. The provision of a {\i shifcha} is thus conditioned on the {\i eved} already being a married man when he enters service.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Viewed from the perspective of the wife, this arrangement is deeply uncomfortable: a husband who has sold himself into servitude due to poverty is now, as part of that servitude, given another woman. The {\i gemara} does not address this perspective directly. There is also the implicit suggestion that this arrangement carries within it a quality of {\i chotei niskar} \u8212- the wrongdoer benefiting from his transgression \u8212- in that the very poverty that forced the man into servitude now enables him a form of license he did not previously have. This too the {\i gemara} leaves unresolved.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Redemption Price\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Value Increased\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A new {\i braita} takes up the question of the redemption price when an {\i eved ivri} seeks to buy back his freedom before the end of his term. {\i Nimkar be-maneh ve-hishbi\u8217'ach ve-amad ma\u8217'asayim} \u8212- he sold himself for a hundred and his value rose to two hundred. How do we calculate the price of redemption? {\i Minayin she-mechashvinan lo ela maneh} \u8212- how do we know we use the original purchase price? The verse states {\i mi-kesef miknato} \u8212- from the money of his purchase. The redemption is calculated against the original price, prorated for the remaining years.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Value Decreased\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The converse case: {\i nimkar be-ma\u8217'asayim ve-hichsif ve-amad maneh} \u8212- he was sold for two hundred but his value declined to a hundred. {\i Minayin she-ein mechashvinan lo ela maneh} \u8212- how do we know we use the current, lower price? The verse states {\i ke-fi shanav yashiv et ge\u8217'ulato} \u8212- according to his years he shall return his redemption. This verse directs us to calculate based on what the remaining years are currently worth.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Lower Price\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The principle that emerges is that we always apply whichever of the two verses yields the lower redemption price \u8212- {\i lekula}. If the original purchase price is lower (because his value rose), we use the original. If the current market value is lower (because his value fell), we use the current. The question of why we consistently apply {\i lekula} rather than {\i lechumra} \u8212- always taking the higher price \u8212- is addressed in the following section.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Lekula Principle\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Abaye\u8217's Boast\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The question of why we read the {\i pesukim} toward leniency is prompted by a famous exchange. Abaye, in a moment of scholarly confidence, declared: {\i hareini ke-ven Azzai be-shukei Tiveria} \u8212- I am like Ben Azzai in the marketplaces of Tiberias. Ben Azzai was renowned as a towering figure of learning, and Abaye was effectively issuing an open challenge: ask me anything. One of the {\i rabbanan} immediately responded with the question: the two {\i pesukim} can be read either {\i lekula} \u8212- always using the lower price \u8212- or {\i lechumra} \u8212- always using the higher. What determines that we read them {\i lekula}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Torah\u8217's Concern\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} answers: {\i mide de-hava Rachmana legabei} \u8212- we know that the Torah expresses concern for the welfare of the {\i eved ivri}. Given this orientation, we assume the Torah intends the reading that is more favorable to him.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ki Tov Lo Imach\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The source for the Torah\u8217's concern is a {\i braita} that many will know from earlier learning: {\i ki tov lo imach} \u8212- it should be good for him with you. The verse mandates that the {\i eved ivri} be treated as an equal. {\i Imach be-ma\u8217'achal} \u8212- equal in food; {\i imach be-mishteh} \u8212- equal in drink. The master may not eat fine white bread while the slave eats bran bread; may not drink aged wine while the slave drinks new wine; may not sleep on soft cushions while the slave sleeps on straw. The accommodations must be equivalent. From this the {\i gemara} derives: {\i kol ha-koneh eved ivri ke-koneh adon le-atzmo} \u8212- whoever purchases a {\i eved ivri} is as if he has purchased a master for himself.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Buying Master\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This teaching carries within it a subtle note of censure directed at the buyer: be aware of what you are taking on. The person you are purchasing is not someone you may treat with contempt or deprive of dignity. He must have what you have.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafot Pillow\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} raises a challenge: the formulation says {\i adon} \u8212- master, not peer. How can he be merely equivalent? {\i Tosafot} resolves this with a passage from the Yerushalmi. Suppose the master has only one pillow. If he sleeps on it himself, he is not fulfilling {\i ki tov lo imach} \u8212- he is treating himself better than the {\i eved}. But if he neither sleeps on it himself nor gives it to the {\i eved}, that is {\i midat Sedom} \u8212- the attribute of Sodom: causing harm to another without any benefit to oneself. Since he is not using the pillow, there is no justification for depriving the {\i eved} of it. Therefore, he must give it to the {\i eved}. The result is that the {\i eved} ends up with better treatment \u8212- and that is why the {\i gemara} calls it {\i ke-koneh adon le-atzmo}: in the final reckoning, the {\i eved} may actually come out ahead.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Treatment vs.\u160 ?Price\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Strict Redemption\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} entertains a counterargument. Perhaps the Torah\u8217's concern for the {\i eved ivri}\u8217's welfare, expressed through {\i ki tov lo imach}, applies only to his treatment while in servitude \u8212- {\i ki heichi de-lo litzta\u8217'er lei}, so as not to cause him suffering. But when it comes to the redemption price, perhaps we should be strict \u8212- {\i nachmir alav} \u8212- and require him to pay the higher amount.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sin Leads to Slavery\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What would motivate applying stringency at the redemption stage? The {\i gemara} invokes a teaching of Rabbi Yose b\u8217'Rabbi Chanina: {\i bo u-re\u8217'eh kama kasha avka shel shevi\u8217'it} \u8212- come and see how grave is even the dust of {\i shevi\u8217'it} transgression. A person who engages in commerce with {\i shevi\u8217'it} produce \u8212- not even the primary prohibition, merely a lighter {\i issur aseh} \u8212- sets himself on a path of decline. Because he pursued profit by disregarding {\i halacha}, the consequence unfolds measure for measure.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Downward Spiral\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The teaching traces the descent through the {\i parshiyot} of {\i Behar}: he sells his movable property, as the verse states {\i bi-shnat ha-yovel tashuvu ish la-achuzato}, juxtaposed to {\i ve-chi timkeru mimkar la-amitecha}. Then he sells his fields \u8212- {\i ki yamuch achicha u-machar me-achuzato}. Then his home \u8212- {\i ve-chi yimkor beit moshav ir choma}. The {\i gemara} then introduces, from {\i Mishpatim}, the sale of his daughter \u8212- {\i ve-chi yimkor ish et bito le-amah} \u8212- even though that {\i parshah} is not found in {\i Behar}. The {\i gemara} explains that a person in this position would sooner sell his daughter than borrow with interest, because the two remaining options are selling his daughter or taking an interest-bearing loan. Finally, having sold everything, he sells himself \u8212- {\i ve-chi yamuch achicha ve-nimkar lach} \u8212- and if he still has not repented, he may wind up sold to a non-Jew, and ultimately to the service of a pagan temple, {\i nimkar la-avodat kochavim atzmah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Awareness Stages\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} notes a shift in language between the stages. After the sale of movable property, it says {\i lo hirgish} \u8212- he was not sensitive, he did not perceive that his poverty resulted from his {\i aveirah}. After the subsequent stages, the language shifts to {\i lo ba l\u8217'yado} \u8212- the awareness did not come to him. Why the difference?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Repeated Sin\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} cites Rav Huna: {\i kivan she-avar adam aveirah ve-shanah bah, hutrah lo} \u8212- once a person has committed a transgression and repeated it, it becomes permitted to him. The {\i gemara} immediately clarifies: not literally permitted, but {\i na\u8217'asit lo ke-heter} \u8212- it becomes psychologically as if permitted. The first time, there was a reasonable expectation that the person would have been {\i murgash} \u8212- sensitive, aware \u8212- and would have recognized the causal connection between his transgression and his punishment. He was not. By the time he has descended further, the expectation of awareness is far lower; it is no longer surprising that awareness did not come to him. The phrasing of {\i lo ba l\u8217'yado} reflects this diminished expectation \u8212- of course it did not come to him; he is already so far gone.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Daughter vs.\u160 ?Interest\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Economic Logic\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} explains why a man in this spiral would choose to sell his daughter before taking a loan with {\i ribbit}. When a daughter is sold, the debt she represents decreases over time \u8212- {\i barta migre\u8217'ah ve-nafkah} \u8212- as she works off her term of service, her redemption price diminishes. The trajectory is toward resolution. A loan with interest, by contrast, only compounds \u8212- {\i ha mosifa ve-azla} \u8212- the debt grows continuously. The practical calculation therefore favors selling the daughter over borrowing with interest, even though morally neither reflects well on the person\u8217's situation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is not a moral endorsement. Rather, it is an economic observation embedded within a trajectory of failure and sin. The inclusion of the daughter\u8217's sale in this sequence \u8212- drawn from {\i Mishpatim} into the context of {\i Behar} \u8212- itself conveys the gravity of the situation. That a man should be reduced to selling his daughter is presented not neutrally but as a marker of how far one can fall through the progressive neglect of {\i mitzvot}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Redemption Mandate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Stone After Fall\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Returning to the question of whether we should be strict on the redemption price because of the {\i chet} that brought the {\i eved} to this position: perhaps, since this person has descended all the way to the service of a pagan temple, we should simply seal his fate \u8212- {\i eima lidchi even achar ha-nofel}, let us throw a stone after the one who has already fallen. This idiom evokes the image of {\i sekilah} \u8212- pushing someone off a cliff and then throwing a stone on top of them \u8212- and is the Talmud\u8217's way of articulating the impulse to give up on someone who has fallen so far.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Acharei Nimkar\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} rejects this: {\i talmud lomar, acharei nimkar ge\u8217'ulah tihyeh lo, echad me-echav yig\u8217'alenu} \u8212- after he is sold, redemption shall be his; one of his kinsmen shall redeem him. Even at this lowest point, the Torah mandates our responsibility to redeem him. The obligation does not expire with his degradation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mandate Conclusion\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Given this strong redemptive mandate, the {\i gemara} concludes, it follows that the Torah intends to make redemption as accessible as possible \u8212- and therefore the lower of the two prices applies. A Torah that commands us to redeem him must intend that the price of redemption not be set at the maximum. The principle of {\i lekula} is thus not merely a formal reading of competing {\i pesukim}; it flows from the Torah\u8217's fundamental orientation toward enabling the {\i eved ivri}\u8217's return to freedom.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Two Psukim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Value Increased\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Nachman bar Yitzchak offers a direct derivation from the {\i pesukim} themselves: {\i im od rabot ba-shanim, le-fihem yashiv ge\u8217'ulato mi-kesef miknato} \u8212- if there are many years, he shall return his redemption according to them from the money of his purchase. The word {\i rabot} \u8212- many or increased \u8212- refers not to the number of years remaining but to the increase in his value {\i over the years}. If his value has increased during the years of his service \u8212- {\i nitraba kaspo ba-shanim} \u8212- we use {\i mi-kesef miknato}, the original purchase price, which is the lower amount.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Value Decreased\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Conversely, {\i ve-im me\u8217'at nish\u8217'ar ba-shanim} \u8212- if his value has decreased over the years \u8212- {\i ke-fi shanav yashiv et ge\u8217'ulato}, the redemption follows his current value, which is the lower amount. In both cases, the operative {\i pasuk} directs us to the figure that is more favorable to the {\i eved}. The two {\i pesukim} thus work in tandem: one covers the case of rising value, the other covers falling value, and in each case the result is the lower price.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Pshat Rejected\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} considers an alternative reading: perhaps the {\i pesukim} simply refer to how many years remain in the six-year term. If many years remain, use the purchase price; if few years remain, use the current price. The {\i gemara} rejects this as the correct reading. If the Torah meant only {\i shanim} \u8212- years \u8212- it would have written simply {\i shanim}. The use of the phrase {\i ba-shanim} \u8212- in the years, over the course of the years \u8212- indicates a change in value {\i over time}, not merely the quantity of remaining years. {\i Nitraba kaspa ba-shanim} \u8212- his value increased over the years \u8212- or {\i nitmeit kaspa ba-shanim} \u8212- his value decreased over the years \u8212- is what is being indicated by the distinctive phrasing.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 K\u8217'Sinai Derasha\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Yosef closes the discussion with a remarkable accolade: {\i darshinhu Rav Nachman le-hani kra\u8217'ei ke-Sinai} \u8212- Rav Nachman interpreted these {\i pesukim} as if the interpretation came directly from Sinai. It is a fitting conclusion to a discussion that began with Abaye\u8217's boast of being like Ben Azzai in the marketplaces of Tiberias. In the event, it is Rav Nachman \u8212- not Abaye \u8212- who produces the decisive reading of the {\i pesukim}, and not a word of Abaye\u8217's brilliant answer appears in the {\i gemara}\u8217's resolution.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Moral Synthesis\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Moral Tensions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} on this {\i daf} presents the institution of {\i eved ivri} within a carefully constructed moral frame that holds two orientations in tension simultaneously. On one hand, there is censure. The teaching of Rabbi Yose b\u8217'Rabbi Chanina traces a downward spiral from the violation of {\i shemitah} law \u8212- not even the primary prohibition, but its secondary dust \u8212- through the progressive loss of property, dignity, and autonomy. Each stage is marked by the failure to perceive, or later even to be capable of perceiving, that sin is the cause of the descent. Selling one\u8217's daughter appears in this sequence as one of the lowest points before the final degradation of selling oneself, and the moral weight of that inclusion is unmistakable.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 On the other hand, the Torah\u8217's response to this situation is not abandonment or additional punishment. The mandate {\i acharei nimkar ge\u8217'ulah tihyeh lo} \u8212- after he is sold, redemption shall be his \u8212- asserts that even at the bottom of the pit, the obligation of kinship and community toward the fallen person does not expire. And the principle of {\i ki tov lo imach} \u8212- that the {\i eved ivri} must be treated as an equal, fed the same food, given the same drink, afforded the same shelter, and in the edge case described by {\i Tosafot}, actually given priority \u8212- transforms the institution from one of domination into something closer to its opposite. {\i Kol ha-koneh eved ivri ke-koneh adon le-atzmo}: the buyer takes on a master, not a servant.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 These two strands \u8212- the moral censure embedded in the path that leads to servitude, and the radical demands of dignity and ease of redemption placed upon the master \u8212- do not resolve into a single clean principle. The {\i gemara} holds them together. The {\i eved ivri} has, in the fullest sense, brought this upon himself through repeated, unrepentant transgression. And yet the Torah\u8217's response is not to make his bondage harsher or his redemption harder, but to surround him with obligations of equal treatment and to mandate his redemption at the lowest possible price. The institution of {\i eved ivri} thus encodes within its very laws a kind of redemptive compassion \u8212- a social and halachic structure that acknowledges moral failure while refusing to use that failure as license for cruelty or indifference.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Kiddushin 21\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Partial Redemption\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Huna\u8217's Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The daf opens on 21b, picking up mid-discussion of the {\i eved Ivri}. The most recent topic had established that when redeeming a Hebrew slave sold to a non-Jew, the redemption amount is calculated based on years of service remaining, priced at the lesser of the original purchase price or the current market value. The Gemara had also articulated, in terms that are partly {\i aggadata} and partly {\i halacha}, that one who purchases an {\i eved Ivri} must treat him at least as well as himself \u8212- a pointed disincentive. The {\i parsha} of Behar makes clear that selling oneself into slavery is a consequence of the most desperate circumstances, and yet even when a person has fallen so low as to sell himself to {\i ovdei avodah zarah} \u8212- to a household of idolatry \u8212- the obligation to redeem him remains.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is worth pausing here to note the precise scope of the Torah\u8217's explicit provisions. The Torah specifies that relatives bear an obligation to redeem in only two cases: where a person sells a {\i sdeh achuza} (an ancestral field), and where a person sells himself into slavery to a non-Jew. The cases of a house sold in a walled city and of a Hebrew slave sold to a fellow Jew are not addressed explicitly. These gaps will drive much of the Gemara\u8217's subsequent inquiry.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The daf begins with a question posed by Rav Huna bar Hinena to Rav Sheshet: {\i eved Ivri hanimkar le-ovdei kochavim} \u8212- a Hebrew slave sold to a non-Jew \u8212- can he be redeemed {\i lachatza\u8217'in}, partially, or not? The question is grounded in a potential {\i gezerah shavah}: the word {\i ge\u8217'ulato} appears both in the context of the {\i sdeh achuza} and in the context of the {\i eved Ivri} sold to a non-Jew. By the {\i sdeh achuza}, the verse says {\i u-matza k\u8217'dei ge\u8217'ulato} \u8212- he must find the sufficient funds for full redemption \u8212- implying that only the complete amount suffices, and partial redemption is ineffective. Should we apply this rule via {\i gezerah shavah} to the {\i eved Ivri} as well, or does the {\i gezerah shavah} function only to import leniencies and not stringencies?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rashi\u8217's View\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Before the Gemara develops its own scenario, it is worth noting that Rashi understands {\i nigal lachatza\u8217'in} in a rather striking way. Rashi explains that the question is whether, if a slave has managed to accumulate funds sufficient to buy back only half his remaining value, he can pay that amount and thereby reduce his remaining term of service by half. In other words, if four years remain and he can pay the equivalent of two years, can he simply buy back two years and go free after two years rather than four? That is Rashi\u8217's reading \u8212- and it is quite radical. As we will see, the Gemara\u8217's actual scenario operates differently.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Price Fluctuation Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The scenario the Gemara itself develops is as follows. Suppose a slave has four years of service remaining, currently valued at 100. He pays 50 \u8212- half the current value. Then the market rises and he is now worth 200. How much must he pay to complete his redemption? If the initial payment of 50 constituted a genuine partial buyback \u8212- if {\i nigal lachatza\u8217'in} applies \u8212- then he has already purchased back half of himself. The remaining half is now worth 100 (half of 200), and that is all he must pay. His total outlay is 150. If, however, partial redemption is invalid and that initial payment of 50 had no legal effect, he must now pay the full 200 to redeem himself \u8212- less the 50 already advanced, meaning an additional 150, for a total of 200.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Abaye\u8217's Analysis\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye then develops both the lenient and stringent dimensions of {\i nigal lachatza\u8217'in}. Consider the lenient case: the slave was purchased for 100. He pays 50 (half the value), and then the market rises so that he is now worth 200. If the initial payment counted as a partial buyback, he only owes an additional 100 to complete his redemption. If it did not, he must pay an additional 150.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara, however, immediately raises a difficulty with this scenario: we have already established that one always calculates the redemption price at the {\i lesser} of the original purchase price and the current market value. So even without the partial buyback concept, if the market has risen to 200 but the purchase price was only 100, you go by the purchase price of 100 \u8212- the partial payment question would be irrelevant.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Abaye\u8217's Analysis (continued)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara therefore refines the scenario to make the question meaningful: the slave was originally purchased at 200 (the market was high), then the market dropped and he paid 50 when his value had fallen to 100, and now the market has recovered to 200 again. In this situation, both the original purchase price and the current market value are 200 \u8212- so the lesser-of-two-values rule provides no relief. Now the question of partial redemption becomes decisive. If his earlier payment of 50 constituted a valid partial buyback, he has already paid for a portion of himself at the favorable lower price, and the remaining amount owed is correspondingly reduced. If that payment had no effect, he faces the full 200 (less what he deposited). This is the scenario \u8212- analogized to a mortgage \u8212- where the partial redemption question carries real halachic weight.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Lesser Price Rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This explains the Gemara\u8217's point that the lesser-of-two-values rule, while important, does not resolve the {\i nigal lachatza\u8217'in} question in every case. Where the market has fluctuated and returned to the original sale price, both benchmarks converge and the slave must look to whether his installment payment constituted a meaningful partial buyback.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Complex Scenario\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The structure also explains what the Gemara means by the {\i stringent} side of {\i nigal lachatza\u8217'in}. Suppose the slave was purchased for 200 and pays 100 immediately (half his value at the time). Then the market falls and he is worth only 100. If the initial payment of 100 counted as a partial buyback, he has bought back exactly half of himself \u8212- but at the then-current price. The remaining half is now worth only 50, so he pays another 50 and goes free, for a total outlay of 150. If, however, the partial payment had no legal effect, the 100 he gave was simply held in escrow, and since his full value has now dropped to 100, that 100 is sufficient to redeem him entirely. He ends up paying exactly what he gave \u8212- 100 total \u8212- which is {\i less} than the 150 he would pay under the partial buyback model. So here, {\i nigal lachatza\u8217'in} would actually work to his detriment.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Market Timing\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara\u8217's point is that if partial redemption is valid, a slave can engage in strategic market timing: accumulate funds, monitor his own market value, and purchase back portions of himself when prices are favorable. The Gemara does not, however, resolve the underlying question \u8212- whether {\i nigal lachatza\u8217'in} is valid for an {\i eved Ivri} sold to a non-Jew. What it establishes is that the question is real and consequential, and that Rashi\u8217's understanding (that partial payment reduces one\u8217's remaining years of service) differs from the Gemara\u8217's own scenario (which frames the question as one of financial accounting for the redemption sum).\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Walled Cities Halakha\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Extending Paradigm\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The same question \u8212- {\i nigal lachatza\u8217'in} or not \u8212- is then posed with respect to a house sold in a walled city: {\i ha-mocher bayit be-vatei arei chomah}, can it be redeemed partially? Again the {\i gezerah shavah} of {\i ge\u8217'ulato ge\u8217'ulato} from the {\i sdeh achuza} is invoked, arguing that just as the ancestral field cannot be redeemed halfway, so too the walled-city house cannot be. Or perhaps the {\i gezerah shavah} imports the rule only in one direction?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Sheshet answers that the matter can be resolved from the {\i drasha} of Rabbi Shimon, who holds that one may borrow money to redeem ({\i loveh ve-goel}) and that one may redeem halfway ({\i goel lachatza\u8217'in}) \u8212- two distinct rules \u8212- in the case of {\i makdish sadeh}, a field one has consecrated to the Temple treasury.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara cites the {\i baraita}: from the verse {\i im gaol yigal ish} (Vayikra 27) \u8212- the doubled language \u8212- we learn {\i melamed she-loveh ve-goel ve-goel lachatza\u8217'in}: one may borrow money and redeem, and one may redeem in installments. This stands in contrast to the rule for {\i sdeh achuza}, where {\i u-matza k\u8217'dei ge\u8217'ulato} teaches that you may not borrow (the money must be one\u8217's own, newly acquired) and you may not redeem partially.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Shimon Logic\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Shimon then provides the underlying rationale. By the {\i sdeh achuza}, the seller is in a position of strength: {\i im higia yovel velo nig\u8217'ala chozeret le-ba\u8217'alah ba-yovel} \u8212- if the field is not redeemed by the {\i Yovel}, it reverts to the owner anyway. Because the seller will recover the property regardless, the Torah does not need to be lenient with him beforehand \u8212- it can afford to make redemption harder (only full amount, only own money). By contrast, by {\i makdish sadeh}, if the field is not redeemed before {\i Yovel}, it goes to the {\i Kohanim} permanently. The seller\u8217's position is weak. And it is precisely because his position is weak \u8212- {\i hura kocho} \u8212- that the Torah compensates by making redemption easier: {\i she-loveh ve-goel ve-goel lachatza\u8217'in}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Strength Comparison\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then applies this logic to {\i batei arei chomah}: a house in a walled city that is not redeemed within the first year is permanently forfeited \u8212- {\i nechlat} \u8212- to the purchaser. The seller\u8217's position is extremely weak. Therefore, following Rabbi Shimon\u8217's logic of counterbalancing: since his position is so weak, the Torah should grant him the rights of partial redemption and borrowing. This is the power of {\i darshinan ta\u8217'ama de-kra} \u8212- reasoning from the underlying principle of a verse to apply it to cases not explicitly mentioned.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The comparison across categories is illuminating. By {\i sdeh achuza}, the buyback window runs from year 3 until {\i Yovel} (the first two years being closed to buyback). By {\i batei arei chomah}, the buyback window is only year 1. By {\i makdish sadeh}, the buyback window runs from the moment of consecration all the way through {\i Yovel} \u8212- the largest possible window. Rabbi Shimon\u8217's logic runs: a larger buyback window means a stronger position, hence more stringent conditions; a smaller window means a weaker position, hence more generous conditions.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Baraita\u8217's Logic\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i baraita}, however, presents the opposite logic. By {\i sdeh achuza}, the seller has strength \u8212- the field returns at {\i Yovel} \u8212- and yet the halacha is stringent (full amount, own money). By {\i makdish sadeh}, the seller\u8217's position is weak \u8212- he loses it at {\i Yovel} \u8212- so one might have argued by {\i kal va-chomer} that the rules should be even stricter. But the doubled language {\i gaol yigal} teaches that in fact the rules are more lenient. This {\i baraita}\u8217's logic is the mirror image of Rabbi Shimon\u8217's: it reasons that where the category is already strong, the accompanying rules are strict; where it is weak, one might expect still stricter rules \u8212- but the verse intervenes to grant leniency.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i baraita} also invokes {\i batei arei chomah} as part of its argument structure: even though one can redeem a house in a walled city within the first year (which sounds like strength), nevertheless the halacha is stringent there (no borrowing, no partial redemption). This shows that the ability to buy back does not automatically generate the favorable rules \u8212- and therefore even the stronger case of {\i makdish sadeh} would not logically derive those rules without a verse.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbanan vs.\u160 ?Rabbi Shimon\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara resolves the apparent contradiction simply: {\i lo kashya} \u8212- there is no difficulty. One {\i baraita} represents the position of the Rabbis, the other reflects Rabbi Shimon. The Rabbis hold that strength goes with strength and weakness goes with weakness \u8212- a category that is already weak in one respect will also be weak in terms of redemption rights. Rabbi Shimon holds a counterbalancing logic: a category that is weak in one respect deserves to be given {\i more} redemption rights, to offset the disadvantage. Accordingly, under Rabbi Shimon, {\i batei arei chomah} \u8212- whose one-year window is a severe limitation \u8212- is precisely the category where borrowing and partial redemption are permitted. Under the Rabbis, that same weakness means those rights are withheld. Two {\i beraitot}, two opinions.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Four Categories Analysis\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Acha Challenge\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Acha son of Rava challenges the {\i baraita}\u8217's argument to Rav Ashi. The {\i baraita} had used {\i batei arei chomah} as a proof-case showing that even a category with buyback rights (the one-year window) lacks the special privileges of borrowing and partial redemption. Rav Acha\u8217's challenge: {\i mah le-mocher bayit be-vatei arei chomah she-ken hura kocho le-goel le-olam} \u8212- the walled-city house is categorically weak because after year 1, the owner can {\i never} buy it back. That is a uniquely severe weakness that does not characterize {\i makdish sadeh} at all. By {\i makdish sadeh}, the owner can redeem at any point up to {\i Yovel} \u8212- {\i yafeh kocho le-goel le-olam} \u8212- so its redemption window is the strongest of all three categories. One should not need a verse to tell me that the strongest category has the most generous rules; it should follow logically.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The implication: the {\i baraita}\u8217's use of {\i batei arei chomah} as a baseline for weakness is flawed, because {\i batei arei chomah} is uniquely weak in a way that {\i makdish sadeh} is not. And {\i sdeh achuza} has its own distinct weakness \u8212- it cannot be redeemed in the first two years. Neither weakness of the other two applies to {\i makdish sadeh}, which is strong across all dimensions.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Weakness Elimination\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Acha the Elder responds to Rav Ashi: {\i nihadar dina ve-teiti mi-ma\u8217'atzad} \u8212- cycle through all the cases. {\i Sdeh achuza} proves that even where the category is strong (returns at {\i Yovel}), the rules are strict. Ah, but perhaps the {\i sdeh achuza}\u8217's rule derives from its own weakness \u8212- that it cannot be redeemed immediately. And {\i batei arei chomah} proves that even when there is an immediate buyback right, the strict rule applies. But perhaps that case\u8217's strictness derives from the weakness of permanent forfeiture after year 1. Each case has its own distinct weakness, and neither weakness applies to the other case. {\i Lo re\u8217'i zeh ke-re\u8217'i zeh} \u8212- the unique characteristics of each case differ from the other.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Generic Halakha\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara therefore proposes a {\i tzad ha-shaveh}: the common denominator between {\i sdeh achuza} and {\i batei arei chomah} is that both appear in the Torah\u8217's {\i parsha} of forced redemptions ({\i she-nig\u8217'alin}), and both require the full amount from one\u8217's own funds. Perhaps this is simply a generic rule \u8212- whenever something is subject to a forced redemption framework, it cannot be done with borrowed money and cannot be done partially. From this common denominator, one might have derived the same rule for {\i makdish sadeh} without a verse. The verse {\i im gaol yigal} is therefore needed to override this inference and teach that {\i makdish sadeh} is the exception.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Year Two Factor\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Mar Zutra son of Rav Mari presses further against the {\i tzad ha-shaveh}: {\i mah le-hatzad ha-shaveh she-bahen she-ken ein kocho le-goel be-shana shniya} \u8212- what is common to both of those cases is that neither can be redeemed in the second year. The {\i sdeh achuza} cannot be redeemed in years 1 and 2; {\i batei arei chomah} can only be redeemed in year 1, so year 2 is also closed. {\i Makdish sadeh}, however, can be redeemed in year 2 \u8212- in fact, in every year. That is its strength. And it is that strength \u8212- the openness of year 2 \u8212- that is the common weakness of the other two, and which would logically entitle {\i makdish sadeh} to the lenient rules. So once again, one should not need a verse.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ravina\u8217's Argument\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Ravina responds: {\i Eved Ivri yokhiach} \u8212- the Hebrew slave sold to a non-Jew is the counterexample that collapses this argument. An {\i eved Ivri} sold to a non-Jew can be redeemed throughout the full term of his service, including in year 2 \u8212- and yet, the halacha (as established earlier in the Gemara via {\i gezerah shavah} or via the language of {\i v\u8217'nigal kulo}) is that he cannot be redeemed partially and cannot be redeemed with borrowed money. So here is a fourth category \u8212- one that {\i can} be redeemed in year 2, whose window is as broad as {\i makdish sadeh}\u8217's \u8212- and yet it carries the stringent rule. The proposed common denominator (inability to redeem in year 2) therefore cannot be the rationale for the strict rule, because this category lacks that characteristic and yet is equally strict. The strict rule is thus a general feature of all forced redemption frameworks, {\i makdish sadeh} is the lone exception, and the verse {\i im gaol yigal} is genuinely necessary to establish that exception.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Relatives\u8217' Redemptio (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Relatives Redeem Houses?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Huna bar Hinena poses yet another question to Rav Sheshet: {\i ha-mocher bayit be-vatei arei chomah} \u8212- a house in a walled city \u8212- {\i nigal le-krovim o ein nigal le-krovim}? Can it be redeemed by relatives, or not?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 To appreciate the question, one must recall what {\i ge\u8217'ulas krovim} means in practice. The Torah\u8217's provision that relatives may redeem is not merely about the family\u8217's right to offer money; it is about the legal compulsion placed on the purchaser to accept that money and release the property. A stranger\u8217's offer can be declined, but a relative\u8217's redemption is binding. The question is: does this power of {\i ge\u8217'ulas krovim} extend beyond the two cases where the Torah mentions it explicitly \u8212- {\i sdeh achuza} and {\i eved Ivri} sold to a non-Jew \u8212- to include {\i batei arei chomah}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Again, the {\i gezerah shavah} of {\i ge\u8217'ulato ge\u8217'ulato} from {\i sdeh achuza} is invoked: just as an ancestral field is not redeemed halfway but {\i is} redeemed by relatives, so too might we say that the walled-city house is not redeemed halfway and {\i is} redeemed by relatives. Alternatively, perhaps the {\i gezerah shavah} was only meant to carry over the {\i lachatza\u8217'in} rule, not the {\i krovim} rule.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Sheshet answers: the house in a walled city is {\i not} redeemed by relatives. Without an explicit verse establishing {\i ge\u8217'ulas krovim} for this category, it does not apply.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gezerah Shavah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara immediately challenges this ruling from the verse {\i u-vekhol ge\u8217'ulah titnu la-aretz} \u8212- \u8220"give redemption in everything.\u8221" What new law does this teach? The proposal: {\i le-rabot battim ve-eved Ivri} \u8212- to include houses and the {\i eved Ivri} (sold to a Jew) within the framework of {\i ge\u8217'ulas krovim}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Inclusive Pasuk\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara entertains this reading: does {\i u-vekhol ge\u8217'ulah titnu} extend the scope of {\i ge\u8217'ulas krovim} to {\i batei arei chomah} and to an {\i eved Ivri} sold to a Jew? The Gemara initially entertains this and asks {\i mai lav} \u8212- isn\u8217't that exactly what the verse is doing?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Scope vs.\u160 ?Obligation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara deflects: {\i lo} \u8212- the verse is not extending the scope to new categories. {\i Batei chatzer} \u8212- houses in unwalled cities \u8212- are already explicitly comparable to fields by the verse {\i al sdei ha-aretz yeichashev}, so they do not need a new derivation. And the verse {\i u-vekhol ge\u8217'ulah titnu} is not extending {\i ge\u8217'ulas krovim} to {\i batei arei chomah} at all; rather, it is deepening the {\i obligation}. It establishes not merely that relatives {\i may} redeem (a {\i reshut}), but that they {\i must} redeem (a {\i chovah}). The scope remains limited to {\i sdeh achuza} and {\i eved Ivri} sold to a non-Jew; the verse adds obligatory force within those categories.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then notes the grammatical difficulty with this reading: the word {\i be-chol} in {\i u-vekhol ge\u8217'ulah} seems naturally to indicate extension of scope (in {\i all} cases), not deepening of obligation within existing ones. This tension is flagged as a {\i kashya} \u8212- a difficulty that is left standing.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Obligation & Hebrew (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Right vs.\u160 ?Obligation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i baraita} directly addresses whether relatives\u8217' redemption is a {\i reshut} (a right, discretionary) or a {\i chovah} (an obligation). The verse {\i ve-ga\u8217'al et mimchar achiv} \u8212- \u8220"and he shall redeem his brother\u8217's sale\u8221" \u8212- might indicate an obligation. Or perhaps it confers only a right?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Yehoshua\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yehoshua derives from the verse {\i ve-ish ki lo yihiyeh lo go\u8217'el} \u8212- \u8220"a man who has no redeemer\u8221" \u8212- that relatives may choose not to redeem. This verse contemplates a scenario where a man has relatives but none of them step forward. Since the Torah acknowledges this possibility and addresses what happens in that case (the slave redeems himself when he accumulates sufficient funds), it implicitly confirms that relatives are not obligated \u8212- they have a {\i reshut}, a right to redeem but not an obligation. {\i She-hareshut be-yado} \u8212- the choice is theirs.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Eliezer\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Eliezer reads {\i ve-ga\u8217'al et mimchar achiv} as establishing a {\i chovah} \u8212- an actual obligation. Then what does {\i ve-ish ki lo yihiyeh lo go\u8217'el} mean? The Gemara resolves: it does not mean he literally has no relatives, since virtually everyone has relatives ({\i ve-chi yesh adam be-Yisrael she-ein lo go\u8217'alim?}). Rather, it describes a situation where no relative is willing to step up \u8212- {\i zeh she-yesh lo ve-eino rotzeh likach} \u8212- i.e., one whose relatives are shirking their obligation. And the verse then tells us that even in such a case, the slave himself can eventually redeem himself. Rabbi Eliezer uses {\i u-vekhol ge\u8217'ulah titnu} not to extend scope but to establish that the obligation of redemption is universal across the enumerated cases: {\i ha-katuv kva\u8217'o chovah}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Relatives\u8217' Redemptio (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yigalenu Repetitions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara turns to another potential source for extending the scope of {\i ge\u8217'ulas krovim}. In the {\i parsha} dealing with a Jew who sells himself to a non-Jew, the word {\i yigalenu} appears three times: {\i o dodo o ven dodo yigalenu}, {\i o mi-she\u8217'ar besaro yigalenu}, {\i o hisiga yado ve-nigal}. Why three repetitions? The {\i baraita} proposes: {\i le-rabot kol ha-ge\u8217'ulot she-nig\u8217'alu ke-seder ha-zeh} \u8212- to include all redemptions that follow this order, i.e., to extend the framework of {\i ge\u8217'ulas krovim} to other categories as well.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara asks: {\i mai lav} \u8212- doesn\u8217't this include {\i batei arei chomah} and {\i eved Ivri} sold to a Jew? The Gemara deflects: no, the repetitions are not extending the scope. {\i Batei chatzerim} and {\i sdeh achuza} are already explicitly covered. Then why the three repetitions of {\i yigalenu}? As Rav Nachman bar Yitzchak explains: {\i karov karov kodem} \u8212- to establish the order of priority among relatives. The Torah is teaching that closer relatives take precedence in the obligation to redeem. The verse {\i ha-karov elav yigalenu} in the {\i sdeh achuza} section already indicates that a closer relative is preferred, but it does not specify the detailed order. The threefold repetition of {\i yigalenu} by the case of the {\i eved Ivri} sold to a non-Jew \u8212- {\i o dodo o ven dodo yigalenu, o mi-she\u8217'ar besaro yigalenu} \u8212- establishes the specific hierarchical order of which relatives redeem first.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 So both potential scope-extensions \u8212- {\i u-vekhol ge\u8217'ulah titnu} and the triple {\i yigalenu} \u8212- are reinterpreted as doing other work: one establishes the degree of obligation, the other establishes the order of relatives. Neither actually extends {\i ge\u8217'ulas krovim} to the two unaddressed categories.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Obligation & Hebrew (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi\u8217's Teaching\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The question of {\i ge\u8217'ulas krovim} for an {\i eved Ivri} sold to a Jew is taken up next. According to Rabbi (Rebbi), the answer is clear and explicit. Rebbi had taught: {\i mi she-eino nigal be-eleh nigal be-shesh} \u8212- only one who is not redeemed by relatives is released in six years. The converse is equally true: an {\i eved Ivri} sold to a Jew, who does go out after six years, does {\i not} go out via {\i ge\u8217'ulas krovim}. Rebbi thus explicitly excludes the Hebrew slave sold to a Jew from the framework of {\i ge\u8217'ulas krovim}. The ruling is: {\i lo miphrik} \u8212- he is not redeemed by relatives.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Slaves to Jews\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The question of {\i ge\u8217'ulas krovim} for an {\i eved Ivri} sold to a Jew is, however, a live dispute among the Rabbis who have not articulated Rebbi\u8217's position. The Gemara asks: do the Rabbis apply a {\i gezerah shavah} of {\i sachir sachir} \u8212- linking the {\i eved Ivri} sold to a Jew to the one sold to a non-Jew \u8212- and thereby extend {\i ge\u8217'ulas krovim} to the former? Or does {\i yigalenu} (stated regarding the slave sold to a non-Jew) function exclusively: {\i le-zeh ve-lo le-acher} \u8212- only for this category and not for the other?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara once again cycles through {\i u-vekhol ge\u8217'ulah titnu} and the triple {\i yigalenu} as potential sources for extending the scope. And once again the Gemara pushes back: {\i batei chatzerim ve-sdeh achuza be-hedya ksiv} \u8212- these are already explicit, and the proposed scope-extensions are doing other work.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rashi\u8217's Explanation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rashi clarifies the final move. The verse {\i u-vekhol ge\u8217'ulah titnu} establishes \u8212- according to the Gemara\u8217's conclusion \u8212- that by {\i eved Ivri} sold to a non-Jew, the relatives\u8217' redemption is a {\i chovah}, not merely a {\i reshut}, and this ruling holds {\i even according to Rabbi Yehoshua}. That is a notable shift: Rabbi Yehoshua holds that {\i ge\u8217'ulas krovim} is generally a {\i reshut} (as in the case of {\i sdeh achuza}), yet even he agrees that by an {\i eved Ivri} sold to {\i ovdei kochavim} it rises to the level of {\i chovah}. As Rashi explains: {\i de-lo yitma be-ovdei kochavim} \u8212- so that he not become assimilated among the gentiles. The urgency of that concern generates an obligation that the less urgent cases do not.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Final Conclusion\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara\u8217's final resolution: the two potential scope-extending derivations \u8212- {\i u-vekhol ge\u8217'ulah titnu} and {\i yigalenu yigalenu yigalenu} \u8212- are both reinterpreted as doing internal work within the two categories that the Torah already mentions. The {\i bechol} verse deepens the obligation in those categories; the triple {\i yigalenu} establishes the hierarchical order of {\i karov karov kodem} \u8212- the closer relative redeems first. Neither verse extends {\i ge\u8217'ulas krovim} to {\i batei arei chomah} or to an {\i eved Ivri} sold to a Jew. According to the conclusion of the Gemara, those two categories remain outside the scope of {\i ge\u8217'ulas krovim}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Kiddushin 22\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Nirtza Overview\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Nirtza Freedom\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} on Kiddushin 22 turns to the {\i nirtza} \u8212- the {\i eved ivri} whose ear is pierced and who thereby commits to extended service beyond the initial six-year term. The {\i mishnah} states: {\i ve\u8217'hanirtza nikneh birtziah} \u8212- the one who has his ear pierced is acquired through that act of piercing, and he then works until the {\i yovel}. The {\i gemara} cites the verse: {\i veratza adonav et ozno bamarzea} \u8212- his master shall pierce his ear with an awl.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} further teaches that the {\i nirtza} acquires his freedom in two ways: through the {\i yovel} and through the death of the master. The source for freedom at {\i yovel} is the explicit verse in {\i Behar}. The source for freedom upon the master\u8217's death is the phrase {\i va\u8217'avado} \u8212- serve {\i him} \u8212- followed by {\i velo es haben velo es habas}, meaning not the son and not the daughter. The Rambam notes that {\i le\u8217'olam} does not independently teach that service continues until the {\i yovel}; rather, it is forced into that reading by the explicit {\i pasuk} in {\i Behar}. The {\i nirtza} relationship, unlike the six-year term which carries the character of {\i schirus} (hired work), involves a more total form of ownership \u8212- and correspondingly, the death of the master brings it to an end in a way that differs from the ordinary six-year arrangement, where service can in certain circumstances continue for the master\u8217's son.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Piercing Tools\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} cites a {\i baraita}: {\i ein li ela marzea} \u8212- I would only know that an awl is valid. The {\i tanna} then asks: how do we know to include {\i hasol} (which Rashi identifies as a piece of wood), {\i hasira} (a thorn), {\i hamachat} (a needle), {\i hamakdeach} (some other type of piercing implement), and {\i hamichtav} (a stylus)? The answer is {\i talmud lomar velakachta} \u8212- the word \u8220"and you shall take\u8221" comes to include anything that can be taken by hand. This is the position of Rabbi Yosi bar Rabbi Yehuda.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi, however, takes a different view: {\i mah marzea meyuchad shel matechet af kol shel matechet} \u8212- just as the awl is specifically of metal, so too anything valid for this purpose must be of metal, excluding the wooden implements mentioned above. The {\i baraita} adds a further point: {\i davar acher} \u8212- {\i hamartzea lehavi hamarzea hagadol} \u8212- the definite article {\i ha-} in {\i hamartzea} comes to include even a large awl, though the precise significance of this will emerge below.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Piercing Location\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Elazar Yudan Beribbi \u8212- Rashi explains that {\i beribbi} denotes a man of prestige \u8212- taught that when the piercing is performed, it is done only in the {\i milsa}, the soft hanging lobe of the ear, not in the cartilage or main body of the ear itself.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i chachamim} disagree and adduce a compelling proof: {\i ein eved ivri kohen nirtza mipnei shena\u8217'aseh ba\u8217'al mum} \u8212- a {\i kohen} who is an {\i eved ivri} does not undergo the {\i retziah} because it would render him a {\i ba\u8217'al mum}. Now, if the piercing were done in the lobe \u8212- as one would do for an earring \u8212- it would not disqualify a {\i kohen} from Temple service. The fact that the {\i halacha} excludes a {\i kohen} because of {\i ba\u8217'al mum} is therefore proof that the piercing is done in the ear itself, in the place where a permanent hole does indeed constitute a disqualifying blemish. The principle is stated directly: {\i ein retziah ela begova shel ozen} \u8212- the piercing is only in the height, that is, the upper body, of the ear.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Ramban connects this discussion to the matter of the large {\i marzea}: once the possibility of using a large awl is introduced, the question of {\i ba\u8217'al mum} for a {\i kohen} becomes acute. Rabbi Elazar Yudan Beribbi\u8217's response was that the {\i retziah} must therefore be in the lobe. The {\i chachamim} counter that the {\i halacha} excluding a {\i kohen} from {\i retziah} entirely proves the location is precisely where a {\i ba\u8217'al mum} can result.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Metal vs.\u160 ?Other\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now addresses the underlying hermeneutic dispute between Rabbi and Rabbi Yosi bar Rabbi Yehuda. What exactly divides them?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rebbi doresh klal uprat} \u8212- Rabbi employs the hermeneutic of {\i klal u\u8217'prat u\u8217'klal}, general-specific-general. In the verse, {\i velakachta} is a {\i klal} (general statement); {\i marzea} is the {\i prat} (specific); and {\i be\u8217'ozno uvadelet} \u8212- the ear and the door \u8212- constitutes a return to the general. The operative rule is: {\i klal u\u8217'prat u\u8217'klal iy ata dan ela ke\u8217'ein haprat} \u8212- one may include only what resembles the specific case. Since the {\i marzea} is of metal, anything of metal qualifies, and nothing else does.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The mechanics of this principle are worth pausing on. When a {\i klal} is followed by a {\i prat} alone \u8212- general then specific \u8212- the result is that we follow the specific. Similarly, a {\i prat} followed by a {\i klal} \u8212- specific then general \u8212- leaves us with the general. It is only when the pattern is general-specific-general that the principle of {\i ke\u8217'ein haprat} applies: one generalizes, but only to what is similar in kind to the specific item named. By identifying the {\i marzea} as the controlling {\i prat}, and metal as its defining characteristic, Rabbi concludes that any metal implement is valid.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yosi bar Rabbi Yehuda, by contrast, {\i doresh ribui u\u8217'miut} \u8212- employs the hermeneutic of expansion and contraction. He reads the same words but frames them not as {\i klal} and {\i prat} but as {\i ribui} (expansive language) and {\i miut} (contracting language). {\i Velakachta} expands; {\i marzea} contracts; {\i be\u8217'ozno uvadelet} expands again. When the structure is {\i ribui-miut-ribui}, the result is {\i riba hakol} \u8212- nearly everything is included. What is excluded? {\i Miet sam} \u8212- something that achieves the piercing not through a pointed implement but through an entirely different mechanism, such as burning with acid. The contracting term does not define a category of similar items; it merely excludes what is most dissimilar. Because the middle term functions as a contraction rather than a specification, it does not require that other valid implements resemble the {\i marzea} in material. Wood is therefore not excluded under this reading.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The conceptual difference is subtle but real. Under {\i klal u\u8217'prat u\u8217'klal}, the specific term anchors the interpretation \u8212- everything valid must be {\i like} it. Under {\i ribui u\u8217'miut u\u8217'ribui}, the middle term only contracts the outer expansiveness; it does not define the genus. This places the {\i ribui u\u8217'miut} method one step further toward inclusion than {\i klal u\u8217'prat u\u8217'klal}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Big Martzea\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} inquires: {\i mai mashma} \u8212- how does one derive from the word {\i hamartzea} (with the definite article) that it includes a large awl? The answer is by analogy to Rava\u8217's principle regarding {\i gid hanasheh}: just as {\i asher kaf hayarech} \u8212- \u8220"the thigh\u8221" \u8212- singles out the prominent one, the right thigh, so too {\i hamartzea} singles out the prominent {\i marzea}, the significant or large one. The {\i gemara} then returns to the positions of Rabbi Elazar Yudan Beribbi and the {\i chachamim} as already analyzed above, affirming that the {\i halacha} excludes a {\i kohen} from {\i retziah} because of {\i ba\u8217'al mum}, which in turn proves the piercing is performed in the upper portion of the ear.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Kohen Restrictions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kohen Nirtza\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having established that a {\i kohen eved ivri} cannot become {\i nirtza} because it would render him a {\i ba\u8217'al mum}, the {\i gemara} asks where the prohibition against making him a {\i ba\u8217'al mum} comes from in the first place. There is indeed a prohibition against creating a {\i mum b\u8217'kodashim} \u8212- a blemish in sacrificial animals \u8212- but what is the source for the prohibition against causing a {\i kohen} to become a {\i ba\u8217'al mum}, even though such a blemish would disqualify him from {\i ha\u8217'avoda}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabba bar Rav Shila answers from the verse: {\i veshav el mishpachto} \u8212- he shall return to his family. This refers to the {\i eved ivri} going free at {\i yovel}, and the {\i gemara} understands it as meaning {\i lemuchzak sheb\u8217'mishpachto} \u8212- he must return to the status he held within his family before his servitude. Since a {\i kohen eved ivri} who becomes {\i nirtza} would be pierced in the upper ear and rendered a {\i ba\u8217'al mum}, foreclosing his return to priestly service, the verse\u8217's requirement that he be restored to his prior status prohibits this outcome.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kohen & Shifcha\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now poses a fresh question: {\i eved ivri kohen mahu sheyimsor lo rabo shifcha kna\u8217'anit} \u8212- may a master assign a non-Jewish female slave to a {\i kohen} who is an {\i eved ivri} for the purpose of fathering children? The Torah permits a master to do this for an ordinary {\i eved ivri} (as stated: {\i im adonav yiten lo isha}), but the question is whether this permission extends to a {\i kohen}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The case is a {\i chiddush} to begin with: ordinarily there is a prohibition against a man having sexual relations with a non-Jewish slave, rooted in the verse {\i lo yikadesh velo yikadesha}. The Torah\u8217's permit for the {\i eved ivri} overrides this {\i issur}. Does this override apply equally to a {\i kohen}, or do the {\i kohen}\u8217's additional {\i mitzvos} \u8212- particularly the restrictions on the women he may marry or engage with \u8212- place him outside the scope of this permission? The Torah\u8217's focus in those laws is on marriage, and while this arrangement is not formal {\i kiddushin}, it approximates a quasi-marital relationship; {\i chazal} might well understand this woman to fall within the category of {\i zona} as they apply it to a {\i kohen}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav says {\i mutar} \u8212- it is permitted. Shmuel says {\i assur} \u8212- it is forbidden.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Challenge to Shmuel\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Nachman challenged the report of Shmuel\u8217's ruling: {\i ki havisos b\u8217'bei Mar Shmuel b\u8217'askamderi itatlisos} \u8212- when you were in Shmuel\u8217's house you must have been distracted (Rashi says {\i askamderi} refers to cards; the {\i Aruch} reads it as playing with puppies). You weren\u8217't paying attention, because had you been, you would have immediately challenged Shmuel. The challenge is straightforward: {\i chachamim omrim ein eved ivri kohen nirtza mipnei shena\u8217'aseh ba\u8217'al mum} \u8212- the {\i chachamim} teach that a {\i kohen eved ivri} cannot become {\i nirtza} because it would make him a {\i ba\u8217'al mum}. But why is that the {\i only} reason he cannot become {\i nirtza}? If Shmuel were right that a {\i kohen} cannot be given a {\i shifcha kna\u8217'anit}, then the {\i kohen eved ivri} could never satisfy the condition of {\i ahavti et adoni et ishti ve\u8217'et banai} \u8212- which, as we will see, requires that he actually have been given a slave wife and fathered children \u8212- and so the inability to become {\i nirtza} would have an entirely different and more basic cause. The fact that the {\i chachamim} locate the sole disqualification in the {\i ba\u8217'al mum} concern proves that the other preconditions {\i can} be met for a {\i kohen} \u8212- and that means the {\i shifcha kna\u8217'anit} is permitted to him. {\i Ve\u8217'su lo midi} \u8212- there is nothing more to say: Shmuel\u8217's position is untenable.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yefat To\u8217'ar\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Still in the vein of a {\i kohen}\u8217's sexual restrictions, the {\i gemara} asks: {\i kohen mah b\u8217'yefas to\u8217'ar} \u8212- may a {\i kohen} take a {\i yefat to\u8217'ar}, a captive woman from the battlefield whom he desires? The Torah describes this as a {\i chiddush} \u8212- a special permission extended to soldiers \u8212- and the same structural question applies: does this {\i chiddush} apply equally to a {\i kohen} and to a {\i yisrael}, or do the {\i kohen}\u8217's additional {\i mitzvos} (particularly regarding forbidden women) exclude him?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav again says {\i mutar}; Shmuel again says {\i assur}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then introduces a crucial distinction. As to {\i biah rishonah} \u8212- the first act of sexual relations \u8212- {\i de\u8217'chulei alma lo peligi deshari}: everyone agrees it is permitted. The reason: {\i lo dibrah Torah ela keneged yetzer hara} \u8212- the Torah speaks only in concession to the evil inclination. The lust of the moment, the heat of battle, the desire expressed in the verse {\i vachashakta bah} \u8212- all of this demands some concession, and the Torah provides it equally to {\i kohen} and {\i yisrael} alike.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The dispute between Rav and Shmuel concerns {\i biah shniyah} \u8212- the second act of sexual relations. Rav says {\i mutar}: once the Torah permitted this woman, the permission holds; {\i ishtarei ishtarei} \u8212- what was permitted was permitted. Shmuel says {\i assur}: by the time of the second act, the woman has gone through the process described in the {\i pesukim} \u8212- she cried for her parents, mourned her prior life, and converted \u8212- {\i de\u8217'hava lah giyores}, she has now become a {\i giyores}. And a {\i giyores} is precisely one of the women forbidden to a {\i kohen}, since {\i chazal} understood {\i zona} (one of the categories the {\i kohen} may not marry) to include a {\i giyores}. Whatever the Torah permitted was the {\i yefat to\u8217'ar} as a non-Jewish captive; it did not and could not permit the {\i kohen} to marry a {\i giyores}, which is a distinct and ongoing {\i issur}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 An alternative formulation of the dispute reverses the terms: according to {\i ika d\u8217'amri}, everyone agrees the {\i biah shniyah} is forbidden to a {\i kohen}, since by then she is a {\i giyores}; the dispute is whether the {\i biah rishonah} is permitted. Rav holds it is, since the Torah\u8217's concession to {\i yetzer hara} applies to {\i kohen} no less than to {\i yisrael}. Shmuel holds it is not: {\i kol heikha delo karinan beih ve\u8217'aveisa el toch beisecha lo karinan beih vera\u8217'isa bashivyah} \u8212- wherever the complete process (taking her home, completing the marriage) is not available to a man, the initial permission of {\i vera\u8217'ita bashivyah} never applied to him at all. For a {\i kohen}, since the {\i biah shniyah} and formal marriage are foreclosed, the Torah never extended the first concession either.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 When Sex Occurs\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This discussion raises a deeper question about exactly when in the {\i pasuk}\u8217's sequence the two acts of {\i biah} occur. The {\i pesukim} in {\i Devarim} read: {\i vera\u8217'ita bashivyah eshet yefat to\u8217'ar vachashakta bah velakachta lecha le\u8217'ishah veheveisa el toch beisecha} \u8212- and then only {\i ve\u8217'achar ken tavo eleha uvealtah} \u8212- and afterwards you may come to her and possess her. On a straightforward reading, all sexual relations occur after the entire process: bringing her home, the mourning period, and implicit conversion. The phrase {\i velakachta lecha le\u8217'ishah} means \u8220"take her to be your wife,\u8221" initiating the process, not consummating a sexual act.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Yet {\i chazal}\u8217's framework of {\i biah rishonah} before the conversion process requires locating a first act of sex somewhere prior to {\i veheveisa el toch beisecha}. The {\i gemara} understands this from {\i lo dibrah Torah ela keneged yetzer hara} combined with {\i vachashakta bah} \u8212- the lust of the moment demands an outlet that the Torah acknowledges. The exact verse from which {\i biah rishonah} is derived at the battlefield remains somewhat oblique, but the conceptual structure is that the Torah could not have withheld all concession from the man at the height of his desire.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i baraita} addresses this through the phrase {\i besha\u8217'at shivyah} \u8212- at the moment of captivity \u8212- which anchors the first act to the battlefield. The word {\i melamed shelo yichtzenah bamilchamah} \u8212- he should not press/force her {\i bamilchamah} \u8212- is then difficult to interpret if one holds that the first act of sex does occur there. Three explanations emerge from the {\i rishonim}. The Rambam understands it to mean he should not do it there in the open battlefield but should take her somewhere more removed, though still before the formal process begins. A second approach attributed to the Re\u8217'em holds that {\i lo yichtzenah bamilchamah} means the Torah\u8217's permission for the first act of sex is conditional on her consent \u8212- she must agree at that moment, making it not an act of violent rape even if the context is inherently coercive. A third opinion understands it as limiting the first act to a single occurrence.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yetzer Hara Concession\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rashi\u8217's position, however, stands apart from all of these. Rashi reads {\i melamed shelo yichtzenah bamilchamah} straightforwardly: do not have sexual relations with her in battle at all. Wait until she has been brought home and completed the full process. On this reading there is no {\i biah rishonah} in the battlefield; all sexual relations occur after the {\i giyurus} is complete. The Torah\u8217's concession to {\i yetzer hara} lies not in permitting immediate sex on the battlefield but in establishing a formal process that channels the desire toward a legitimate outcome rather than leaving the woman as a sexual slave. The distinction between {\i biah rishonah} and {\i biah shniyah} for the {\i kohen} would then not be a chronological before-and-after conversion, but rather a matter of whether the Torah intended to permit even a single act (given the {\i kohen}\u8217's prohibitions) or only ongoing marital relations \u8212- with the answer perhaps being that once is thinkable but the ongoing marriage is not.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Yerushalmi} actually records both of these as distinct opinions among the {\i tana\u8217'im}, which maps onto the debate between Rashi and the other {\i rishonim} in the {\i bavli}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Lesser of Two Evils\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 On 22a, the {\i gemara} articulates the underlying logic of the {\i yefat to\u8217'ar} concession through a vivid analogy: {\i mutav sheyochlu Yisrael basar temutos shechutos ve\u8217'al yochlu basar temutos neveilos} \u8212- better that Jews should eat meat from an animal that was going to die and was slaughtered while still alive, than that they should eat the meat of an animal that died without {\i shechita} and became a {\i neveilah}. The image is striking: a {\i treifa} on the verge of death, slaughtered at the last moment \u8212- the meat is technically {\i mutar}, but no one is pleased with the situation. The alternative, allowing it to die and become {\i neveilah}, is worse. So too here: the Torah fashions a barely-permissible path \u8212- forced {\i giyurus}, a quasi-consensual union \u8212- because the alternative, a man simply taking a non-Jewish woman as a permanent sexual slave or intermarrying with her outside of any conversion process, would be worse.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This line has been invoked by {\i poskim} in later generations when confronted with situations where the only available option is undesirable but the alternative is even more prohibited.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Conversion Prevention\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Ramban develops a more structured account of what {\i issur} the Torah is protecting against. The verse says {\i vachashakta bah velakachta lecha le\u8217'ishah} \u8212- the man actually wants to {\i marry} this woman, not merely have a momentary encounter with her. The real danger is that without the Torah\u8217's intervention, he would keep her and marry her as a non-Jewish woman, violating {\i lo titchaten bam} \u8212- the prohibition of intermarriage. The Torah therefore channels the desire through a process of {\i giyurus}, even if effectively compelled, that places the union on a halakhically legitimate footing. The {\i biah rishonah}, too, is part of this framework: it is not purely an independent concession to {\i yetzer hara} but the beginning of a process that leads to a permissible marriage rather than an ongoing prohibited one. The Ramban further notes the element of {\i eishes ish}: the {\i baraita} states {\i va\u8217'afilu eishes ish} \u8212- even a woman married to a non-Jewish man is included in the Torah\u8217's permission. While the halakhic status of non-Jewish marriage vis-\u224 ?-vis Jews is complex \u8212- the institution is recognized as part of {\i sheva mitzvos bnei Noach} but does not create a Jewish-law {\i issur} of {\i eishes ish} for a Jewish man \u8212- the Ramban explains that this is not a technical halakhic point. Rather, the Torah is emphasizing how far the concession extends: even in a case that is more {\i mechuar}, more morally degraded \u8212- tearing a woman from her husband \u8212- the Torah permits the process because the alternative (permanent forbidden cohabitation) is worse.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Declaration & Conditions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Timing of Declaration\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} cites a {\i baraita}: the verse says {\i amar yomar ha\u8217'eved} \u8212- the slave shall say, shall say \u8212- teaching that he must make his declaration twice. Moreover, if he declared at the {\i beginning} of the six years and not at the end, {\i eino nirtza} \u8212- he does not undergo {\i retziah}. The source is {\i lo etzei chofshi} \u8212- he must say \u8220"I do not wish to go free\u8221" at the {\i moment of going free}, meaning at the conclusion of the six years.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Conversely, if he declared only at the end and not at the beginning, he also cannot become {\i nirtza}. The reason given is {\i shene\u8217'emar ha\u8217'eved} \u8212- it must be said {\i ke\u8217'shehu eved}, while he is still a slave. This initially seems strange, since at the end of six years he is still technically a slave. The {\i gemara} then poses the obvious objection: {\i atu sof shesh lav eved hu} \u8212- at the end of six years is he not a slave?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This leads directly to Rava\u8217's explanation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Last Penny Standard\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava resolves the apparent contradiction: {\i bitchilas pruta achrona bsof pruta achrona} \u8212- the terms \u8220"beginning\u8221" and \u8220"end\u8221" refer not to the beginning and end of the six-year period, but to the beginning and end of the {\i last penny\u8217's worth} of service. When enough time remains that the master still has a monetary claim worth at least a {\i pruta} against the slave, the slave is fully an {\i eved} \u8212- but he is not yet at the {\i sha\u8217'at yetziah}, the moment of imminent departure. Once the slave has entered the last unit of time \u8212- the final hour or fraction of time worth less than a {\i pruta} \u8212- he is at the cusp of freedom and satisfies {\i lo etzei chofshi}, but he is no longer fully {\i ha\u8217'eved} in the complete proprietary sense.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 To satisfy {\i both} requirements simultaneously, the declaration must be made at the precise juncture: before the last {\i pruta}\u8217's worth expires (so that he is still fully an {\i eved}) and after all prior conditions \u8212- wife and children \u8212- are already in place. The precision required here is striking, and one senses that the halakha is not designed to make the path to {\i nirtza} an easy one.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Wife & Children\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i baraita} sets out additional conditions. If the slave has a wife and children (the {\i shifcha kna\u8217'anit} and the children born from that union) but his master does not, {\i eino nirtza} \u8212- the process cannot proceed. The source is {\i ki ahevcha ve\u8217'et beisecha} \u8212- \u8220"because he loves you and your household\u8221" \u8212- implying the master must also have a household that parallels his own.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Conversely, if the master has a wife and children but the slave does not, {\i eino nirtza} \u8212- he cannot say {\i ahavti et adoni et ishti ve\u8217'et banai}. As noted above, this is precisely why the {\i gemara} proved that even a {\i kohen eved ivri} must be permitted a {\i shifcha kna\u8217'anit}: the {\i only} reason the {\i chachamim} gave for excluding a {\i kohen} from {\i nirtza} was {\i ba\u8217'al mum}, proving that the preliminary stage of wife and children is available to him.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Love & Health\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The conditions extend to the relational and physical. If the slave loves his master but his master does not love him, {\i eino nirtza} \u8212- the verse {\i ki ahevcha} (\u8220"because he loves you\u8221") requires the master\u8217's affection to be genuine. If the master loves the slave but the slave does not love his master, {\i eino nirtza} \u8212- the verse {\i ki tov lo imach} (\u8220"for it is good for him with you\u8221") must be true, and it cannot be if the slave is not genuinely content. The slave may have pragmatic reasons for wishing to remain \u8212- he loves his wife and children, he has no prospects outside \u8212- but if he cannot sincerely say he loves his master, the {\i retziah} is unavailable.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Similarly, if the slave is ill and his master is not, {\i eino nirtza} \u8212- {\i ki tov lo imach} cannot be said when he is suffering. If the master is ill and the slave is not, {\i eino nirtza} \u8212- {\i imach} requires good conditions on the master\u8217's side as well. The case of both being ill simultaneously is posed as a {\i ba\u8217'aya} by Rav Bibi bar Abaye: is {\i imach} a requirement of equality, such that if both are equally ill the condition is met, or does {\i ki tov lo} mean that the situation must be genuinely good \u8212- not merely ill together? The {\i gemara} leaves this as {\i teiku}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Equal Treatment\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i baraita} then unpacks the phrase {\i ki tov lo imach} in its material dimensions, and this passage is one of the most celebrated in this entire sugya. {\i Imach bema\u8217'achal} \u8212- with you in food; {\i imach bemishte} \u8212- with you in drink; {\i imach belo\u8217'in} \u8212- with you in lodging. The slave must receive exactly what the master provides for himself:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Shelo tehei atah ochel pas nekiyah vehu ochel pas kivar} \u8212- you may not eat fine white bread while he eats coarse bran bread. {\i Atah shoseh yayin yashan vehu shoseh yayin chadash} \u8212- you may not drink aged wine while he drinks new wine. {\i Atah yashen al gabei mochin vehu yashen al gabei teven} \u8212- you may not sleep on a featherbed while he sleeps on straw.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 From here the {\i gemara} derives the famous principle: {\i mikan amru kol hakoneh eved Ivri kekoneh adon le\u8217'atzmo} \u8212- whoever acquires an {\i eved Ivri} is as if he has acquired a master for himself. The Ramban sharpens this: if there is only one pillow in the house, giving it to oneself would create an inequality, violating {\i imach}; throwing it away so neither has it would be {\i middat Sedom}. The slave therefore takes precedence \u8212- he receives the single pillow. In that sense the {\i eved Ivri} is not merely the master\u8217's equal but, where resources are limited, his superior.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Support Obligations\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A further {\i baraita} addresses the master\u8217's obligations toward the slave\u8217's family. The verse {\i veyatza imach hu uvanav imo} \u8212- he shall leave you, he and his sons with him \u8212- prompts Rabbi Shimon to ask: if he was sold into slavery, who sold his sons? They were not sold. Yet the verse says they leave with him at {\i yovel}, implying they were present throughout the servitude. {\i Mikan sherabo chayav bemezonos banav} \u8212- from here we learn that the master was obligated to provide for the slave\u8217's children throughout the period of service.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 An identical structure applies to the wife: {\i im ba\u8217'al ishah hu veyatza ishto imo} \u8212- if he was married, his wife leaves with him. Rabbi Shimon again asks: who sold his wife? The answer: {\i mikan sherabo chayav bemezonos ishto} \u8212- the master must support the wife as well.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} explains why both rulings are necessary. Had the Torah stated only the obligation regarding children, one might have said the wife can find employment and feed herself. Had it stated only the wife, one might have said that since it is not customary for a woman {\i lehadori} \u8212- to go door to door begging \u8212- the master need not provide for children, who can beg. Each case is therefore independently required.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Ear-Piercing Mechanics\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Through Ear & Door\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i baraita} analyzes the precise procedure of the {\i retziah}. Had the verse said only {\i be\u8217'ozno uvadelet} \u8212- in his ear and in the door \u8212- one might have thought: place his ear against the door and then pierce into the door only, the ear being merely positioned nearby. The word {\i be\u8217'ozno} forces us to require that the ear itself be pierced. But then, had the verse said only {\i be\u8217'ozno}, I might have thought: pierce the ear first, then separately make a hole in the door and align them. {\i Talmud lomar be\u8217'ozno uvadelet} \u8212- both together \u8212- teaches: {\i dokeir veholech ad shemmagi\u8217'a etzel dalet} \u8212- one pierces through the ear and continues until reaching the door. The ear must be pressed against the door, and a single act of piercing passes through both.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Standing Door\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might have thought the door need not be standing and attached; perhaps even a detached door panel would suffice. {\i Talmud lomar mezuza} \u8212- the verse\u8217's juxtaposition with {\i mezuza} teaches: {\i mah mezuza me\u8217'umad af dalet nami me\u8217'umad} \u8212- just as a {\i mezuza} is only called by that name when the doorframe stands in its permanent upright position, so too the door for the {\i retziah} must be standing, properly installed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ear at Sinai\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yochanan ben Zakkai offered a famous {\i mashal} \u8212- Rashi understands {\i kemin chomer} as \u8220"like a fragrant mixture,\u8221" meaning a beautifully composed explanation; Tosafot understand it as \u8220"like a story\u8221" \u8212- to illuminate why the ear specifically is the organ chosen for piercing.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Mah nishtanna ozen mikkol ha\u8217'evarim shebaguf} \u8212- why was the ear singled out from all the organs of the body? The Holy One, blessed be He, declared: {\i ozen sheshame\u8217'ah koli al har Sinai besha\u8217'ah she\u8217'amarti ki li benei yisrael avadim velo avadim la\u8217'avadim} \u8212- the ear that heard My voice at Sinai when I said \u8220"the children of Israel are My servants and not servants to servants\u8221" \u8212- {\i vehalach zeh veqana adon le\u8217'atzmo} \u8212- and yet this person went and acquired a master for himself. That ear must be pierced.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The irony is layered: the very foundation of {\i mishpatim}, which begins with the laws of slavery, is prefaced by {\i anochi Hashem Elohecha asher hotzetecha me\u8217'eretz mitzrayim mibbeit avadim} \u8212- I freed you from a house of slaves. The first substantial legislation after {\i maamad har Sinai} concerns how one frees one\u8217's slave after six years. Yet this slave chooses to perpetuate his bondage \u8212- and his ear, which heard the proclamation of freedom, is marked accordingly.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Door as Witness\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Shimon b\u8217'Rebbi offered a parallel {\i mashal} focusing on the door and doorpost rather than the ear. {\i Mah nishtanna dalet umezuza mikkol kelim shebabayit} \u8212- why were the door and doorpost chosen from all the vessels of the house? The Holy One, blessed be He, declared: {\i dalet umezuza shehayu edim bemitzrayim besha\u8217'ah shepasachti al hamashkof ve\u8217'al shetei hamezuzot} \u8212- the door and the doorpost were witnesses in Egypt when I passed over the lintel and the two doorposts. {\i Ve\u8217'amarti ki li benei Yisrael avadim velo avadim la\u8217'avadim vehotzetim me\u8217'avdut lecherut} \u8212- I declared they are My servants and freed them from slavery to freedom. {\i Vehalach zeh veqana adon le\u8217'atzmo} \u8212- and this one went and acquired a master for himself. {\i Yeratza bifneihem} \u8212- let him be pierced before them, before these witnesses to his people\u8217's redemption, witnesses to the fact that he should not be enslaving himself again.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mashal} of Rabbi Shimon prompts reflection on the distinction between the initial six-year period and the {\i retziah}. For the first six years, the slave was a {\i gannav} who could not pay his debt \u8212- he did not choose slavery freely, or he was driven to {\i moker atzmo} by economic desperation. One can understand, if not excuse, that first entry into servitude. But to then choose {\i ve\u8217'avado le\u8217'olam} \u8212- to serve forever \u8212- is something else entirely, something that demands a symbolic act before these ancient witnesses to say: this choice runs against everything your history and your God intended for you.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Kiddushin 23\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Eved Knaani: Kesef, Shta\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Eved Knaani Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i daf} opens by situating the {\i eved Knaani} within a broader taxonomy of {\i kinyanim}. The {\i mishnah} has just concluded its treatment of {\i kiddushei isha}, {\i yibum}, and {\i eved Ivri} \u8212- forms of acquisition or obligation that pertain to persons with varying degrees of legal personhood. The {\i eved Knaani} represents an altogether different category. On one hand, he is treated as fully fungible property: if an object is placed upon an {\i eved Knaani}, it is as though it were placed on the master\u8217's chattel, and the master acquires it through {\i kinyan chatzer}. On the other hand, the {\i Torah} insists on a revolutionary principle \u8212- this slave remains a human being. He goes free if the master destroys his eye or tooth; in certain circumstances the master may even be {\i chayav misa}. This dual status, simultaneously property and person, is the defining tension of the entire {\i sugya}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Three Kinyanim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} states: {\i eved Knaani nikneh be-kesef u-ve-shtar u-ve-chazakah} \u8212- a {\i Canaanite slave} is acquired through money, a legal document, and {\i chazakah}, an act of taking possession. The {\i mishnah} further establishes the mechanisms by which the slave may acquire his own freedom. {\i Ve-koneh et atzmo be-kesef al yedei acheirim} \u8212- he may be redeemed through money provided by others, provided the master agrees to accept it. The dynamic here is analogous to {\i kesef kiddushin}: the amount is entirely at the master\u8217's discretion, but once the agreed-upon sum changes hands, the slave goes free.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Regarding the {\i shtar}: {\i u-ve-shtar al yedei atzmo, divrei Rabbi Meir} \u8212- a writ of manumission ({\i shtar shichrur}), in which the master writes {\i harei ata ben chorin}, is delivered directly to the slave. Whether this precludes delivery to others is a question to be resolved in the {\i gemara}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Ve-chachamim omrim: be-kesef al yedei atzmo} \u8212- the Sages hold that the slave may redeem himself even with his own money, {\i u-ve-shtar al yedei acheirim} \u8212- and the writ may be delivered through others. The {\i mishnah} adds: {\i u-vilvad she-yihyeh ha-kesef mi-shel acheirim} \u8212- even when the slave is himself handing money to the master, that money must have originated with others. This is not self-contradictory: {\i al yedei atzmo} means he himself is the one delivering the payment, but since any money he earns automatically belongs to his master, the only money he can legitimately call his own is money given to him by outsiders \u8212- for example, as a gift explicitly designated for him rather than for his master.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Comparison to Karka\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} asks {\i minalan} \u8212- what is the biblical source for acquiring an {\i eved Knaani} through {\i kesef}, {\i shtar}, and {\i chazakah}? The derivation comes from the verse {\i ve-hisnachaltem otam li-vneichem achareichem la-reshet achuzah} \u8212- you shall bequeath them to your children after you as hereditary property ({\i Vayikra} 25:46). The {\i katuv} equates the slave to an {\i sdeh achuzah}, an ancestral field: just as land is acquired through money, document, and {\i chazakah}, so too is an {\i eved Knaani}. The comparison to {\i karka} is deliberate and carries broad halakhic consequences, as throughout {\i Shas} there is a recurring discussion of whether an {\i eved Knaani} shares the legal status of {\i karka} in various contexts.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might raise the objection: if the {\i eved Knaani} is like a field, perhaps he should also return to his original owner in the {\i Yovel}, just as an {\i sdeh achuzah} does. The {\i gemara} responds with the verse {\i le-olam bahem ta\u8217'avodu} \u8212- you shall work them forever. Unlike {\i eved Ivri}, for whom the word {\i le-olam} implied release at {\i Yovel} given an additional {\i pasuk}, here there is no such indication, and so the slave remains a permanent acquisition. Crucially, however, he is acquired in the same manner as land \u8212- by {\i kesef}, {\i shtar}, and {\i chazakah}. It is also worth noting that {\i chazakah} did not appear among the methods of acquiring an {\i eved Ivri}: that {\i chazakah} \u8212- the most demonstrative act of asserting property-like control \u8212- is specific to {\i eved Knaani} underscores just how fully he is treated as property.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chalipin Method\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i baraita} adds: {\i af be-chalipin} \u8212- an {\i eved Knaani} may also be acquired through {\i chalipin}, whether through an actual exchange of objects or through the symbolic handkerchief transaction in which the acquiring party takes the handkerchief and thereby effects the transfer of ownership. Why did the {\i tanna} of the {\i mishnah} omit this? {\i Milta de-leita be-metaltelin katani, milta de-ita be-metaltelin lo katani} \u8212- the {\i mishnah} wished to list only those modes of acquisition that are distinctive to {\i karka} and not to {\i metaltelin} (movable goods). {\i Chalipin} works for both {\i karka} and {\i metaltelin}, and even works for {\i karka} \u8212- though notably not for {\i ishah} \u8212- so it does not belong to the set of {\i karka}-specific {\i kinyanim} that the {\i mishnah} seeks to highlight. The outer {\i tanna} of the {\i baraita}, who does include {\i chalipin}, was willing to broaden the list to anything that works for {\i karka}, even if it also works for {\i metaltelin}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Meshicha & Chazakah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shmuel on Meshicha\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Amar Shmuel: eved Knaani nikneh bi-mshichah} \u8212- Shmuel holds that an {\i eved Knaani} may be acquired by {\i meshicha}, that is, by physically dragging him toward the buyer. This is unmistakably a {\i kinyan} of {\i metaltelin}, not of {\i karka}. The {\i gemara} defines how it works: {\i takfo u-va etzlo kna-o} \u8212- if you grabbed hold of him and pulled him, you acquire him; {\i kara-o u-va atzlo lo kna-o} \u8212- if you merely called him and he came on his own, you did not acquire him. This distinction will require explanation shortly.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Why No Meshicha?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} observes that Shmuel\u8217's ruling coheres with the {\i mishnah}\u8217's silence on {\i meshicha}: since {\i meshicha} is a {\i kinyan} of {\i metaltelin}, the {\i mishnah} \u8212- which limited itself to {\i kinyanim} distinctive to {\i karka} \u8212- naturally omitted it. By the same logic, the outer {\i tanna} of the {\i baraita}, who included {\i chalipin}, still restricted his list to methods that operate for {\i karka} as well; {\i meshicha} does not apply to {\i karka}, so even that {\i tanna} would not have included it. The omission is thus explained across all opinions. Everyone concedes, it seems, that {\i meshicha} works \u8212- but the {\i mishnah} and {\i baraita} were organized around a different taxonomic concern.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Dehumanization Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is, however, a striking {\i chiddush} of Shmuel\u8217's, and it is worth pausing over its conceptual significance. The {\i mishnah} grounds the acquisition of an {\i eved Knaani} in the {\i pasuk} comparing him to {\i karka}. Why is {\i karka} the operative comparison? One might suggest that {\i karka} was chosen precisely because of the theological resonance of land-ownership: {\i la-Hashem ha-aretz}, the earth belongs to God; no one truly owns land absolutely, it reverts in {\i Yovel}. There remains an irreducible gap between the landowner and his land. Movable property is different \u8212- once it is in your pocket, in your house, under your full control, the ownership is total and the gap disappears. To treat an {\i eved Knaani} as {\i karka} would thus mean that he is never fully objectified; the master\u8217's ownership, like ownership of land, retains an element of incompleteness. By contrast, {\i kinyanim} of {\i metaltelin} \u8212- {\i meshicha}, {\i hagbahah} \u8212- assert a kind of absolute, intimate dominion that more completely reduces the slave to an object. Shmuel\u8217's move to permit {\i meshicha} therefore deepens the dehumanizing logic rather than ameliorating it, even if Shmuel\u8217's intent was purely technical.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Animals vs.\u160 ?Slaves\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This conceptual tension is sharpened by a {\i baraita} that the {\i gemara} cites in the context of {\i meshicha} by animals. How does {\i meshicha} work for an animal? Grabbing it by the hoof, the hair, the saddle, the saddlebag, the bit, or the bell around its neck constitutes {\i mesira}. As for {\i meshicha} itself: calling the animal and having it come, or striking it with a stick so that it runs \u8212- once it lifts a leg and takes a step, the acquisition is complete. According to some {\i tannaim}, it must travel a distance equal to its full height. The critical point is that for animals, calling and having the animal come {\i does} work as {\i meshicha}. Why does the same not apply to a slave?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Will & Personhood\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Behema da\u8217'atah de-marah azlah} \u8212- an animal has no independent will; when it comes in response to a call, it is simply responding instinctively to the controller\u8217's directive, and so the controller\u8217's {\i da\u8217'at} is driving the movement. {\i Eved da\u8217'atah de-nafsheih azlah} \u8212- the slave, however, decides to listen. Even in a condition of servitude, even where the choice is severely constrained, the slave exhibits something that looks like will, like personhood. Calling him and having him come does not demonstrate that you have taken him as an object under your control, because he is still the one choosing to move toward you. To objectify him \u8212- to make him, for the purposes of {\i kinyan}, like a piece of movable goods \u8212- you must physically seize him and drag him. And yet that very act of seizing and dragging is, as observed above, far more dehumanizing. The rule that {\i meshicha} requires physical dragging thus rests on an acknowledgment of the slave\u8217's subjectivity, even as the {\i kinyan} itself treats him as an object.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Minor Slave Exception\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Amar Rav Ashi: eved katan ke-vehema dami} \u8212- if the slave is a minor, we do not assume he is exercising genuine will; he is more like an animal in this regard. Accordingly, if you call a minor slave and he comes, that would constitute a valid {\i meshicha} for purposes of acquisition.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Chazakah Examples\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Service Acts\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tanu rabbanan: keitzad be-chazakah} \u8212- the Rabbis teach how {\i chazakah} functions for an {\i eved Knaani}. For {\i karka}, {\i chazakah} is performed by improving or working the land, such as plowing or repairing a fence. For a slave, the demonstrative act goes in one direction only: the slave performs an act of personal service for the master. The {\i baraita} enumerates examples: removing the master\u8217's shoe ({\i hin\u8217'ilo}), carrying the master\u8217's bathing implements to the bathhouse ({\i holich keilav acharav le-veit ha-merchatz}), undressing the master ({\i hifshito}), bathing him ({\i hirchitzo}), anointing him with oil ({\i sacho}), scraping him ({\i gerado}), dressing him again ({\i hilbisho}), and putting his shoe back on. The preponderance of bathhouse scenarios likely reflects the deeply personal, servile nature of the service required \u8212- these are acts that only one who stands in a relationship of submission would perform for another.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Whether this list is exhaustive or merely illustrative \u8212- whether, for instance, having the slave mow a lawn would also suffice \u8212- is not explicitly resolved, though the pattern suggests that any act of personal service would qualify.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Higbiho & Gevira\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i baraita} continues: {\i higbiho} \u8212- if he lifted him up \u8212- {\i kna-o}, he acquires him. The {\i gemara} initially finds this ambiguous. {\i Rabbi Shimon} objects: {\i lo tehei chazakah gedolah me-hagbahah} \u8212- {\i chazakah} should not be superior to {\i hagbahah}, which after all effects acquisition in all contexts ({\i hagbahah konah be-chol makom}). {\i Rav Ashi} clarifies: the {\i higbiho} in the list means {\i higbiho hu le-rabbo kna-o} \u8212- the slave lifted the master, which is consistent with the pattern of the slave performing a service. But if the master lifted the slave, {\i lo kna-o} \u8212- that would not constitute {\i chazakah}. Rabbi Shimon\u8217's objection, then, is precisely that if the master lifts the slave, that should work as {\i hagbahah} \u8212- the same {\i kinyan} that operates for all movable goods. This is, in miniature, the same debate as Shmuel\u8217's broader claim: {\i Rabbi Shimon} among the {\i tannaim} was already arguing for the applicability of {\i kinyanei metaltelin} to an {\i eved Knaani}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Bia & Chazakah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having established that the slave lifting the master constitutes a valid {\i chazakah}, the {\i gemara} raises a provocative {\i hava amina}: {\i ela me\u8217'atah shifchah Knaanit tikneh be-viah} \u8212- might sexual intercourse with a female slave constitute a {\i chazakah} for her acquisition? The reasoning, formally at least, follows from the prior case: in the act, the slave\u8217's body supports and effectively elevates the master\u8217's body, which could be characterized as a form of {\i hagbahah}. Moreover, she is performing a kind of service.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rejection of Bia\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} rejects this on the basis that the standard logic of {\i chazakah} through lifting requires that the act be one-sided: {\i zeh neheneh ve-zeh mitzta\u8217'er} \u8212- one party benefits while the other is burdened. Ordinary {\i hagbahah} is uncomfortable for the one being made to lift; it demonstrates the master\u8217's power by imposing effort on the slave. In the case of {\i bi\u8217'ah}, the {\i gemara} says {\i zeh neheneh ve-zeh neheneh} \u8212- both parties experience pleasure, which disqualifies it from the structure of {\i chazakah}. The {\i gemara} then considers whether {\i shelo ke-darka} (anal intercourse) might change the analysis, since the {\i gemara} assumes the woman does not derive pleasure from that act. {\i Rabbi Chiya bar Abba de-min Archei} challenges even this assumption, asking how we know that both parties do not in fact experience pleasure in that act. The {\i gemara} responds: since the verse uses the formulation {\i mishkevei ishah}, which is the source for equating {\i shelo ke-darka} with {\i ke-darka} across {\i kol ha-Torah kulloh}, if {\i ke-darka} does not work for this purpose, {\i shelo ke-darka} does not either. The entire {\i hava amina} is, as one must acknowledge, deeply disturbing \u8212- not least because it proceeds without directly confronting the reality of rape \u8212- but the {\i gemara}\u8217's technical rejection is firm.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Hindua Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ger Death Scenario\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} presents a famous case study. {\i Rav Yehuda Hindua} \u8212- {\i Rashi} explains that {\i Hindua} renders {\i Kush}, translated variously as Ethiopia or India, meaning he was a convert from a distant land \u8212- was a {\i ger} who had no heirs, having converted without subsequently having children. He became gravely ill and was clearly near death. {\i Mar Zutra} came to pay a {\i bikkur cholim} visit and observed that the man was {\i takif leih alma} \u8212- the world had seized him, meaning he was at the very threshold of death.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The halakhic significance of the moment is immediately apparent. When a {\i ger} dies without heirs, his property becomes ownerless \u8212- {\i hefker}. His slaves, who had been owned by him, are at that instant no longer owned by anyone, and can therefore acquire themselves as {\i benei chorin}. To prevent this, a potential acquirer must already be in the middle of a valid {\i chazakah} at the precise moment of death, so that there is no intervening moment in which the slave becomes free.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Mar Zutra} therefore said to the slave of {\i Rav Yehuda Hindua}: take off my shoe ({\i shalof li masanai}) and bring it to my house ({\i ve-amtinhu le-veitei}). The journey to the house would take some twenty minutes. If the {\i ger} died while the slave was in the middle of this act of service, the slave would have had no moment of freedom \u8212- he passes directly from performing {\i chazakah} for {\i Mar Zutra} without ever becoming ownerless.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Adult vs.\u160 ?Minor\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Ika d\u8217'amri}: some say the slave was an adult ({\i gadol}). In that reading, {\i Rashi} explains, the moment the master dies, the adult slave transitions immediately to the control of the living \u8212- he is transferred over. {\i Ve-ika d\u8217'amri}: others say the slave was a minor ({\i katan}). The {\i Rashi} text reads {\i u-de-lo ke-Abba Shaul} \u8212- not in accordance with {\i Abba Shaul} \u8212- though most other {\i rishonim} have the {\i girsa ke-Abba Shaul}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i baraita} is cited: if a {\i ger} died and Jews subsequently seized his property, and among that property were slaves \u8212- whether adult or minor \u8212- they acquire themselves as {\i benei chorin}, because there was a gap in time between the death and the seizure. Even a minor slave can acquire himself in that gap. This is the position of the {\i tanna kamma}. {\i Abba Shaul} dissents: only adult ({\i gedolim}) slaves acquire themselves, because an adult has a {\i yad} \u8212- a capacity for legal acquisition of himself. Minors lack that {\i yad}, and so if there is a gap, their status remains unresolved and anyone who takes possession of them at that point acquires them.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafot Logic\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} and many other {\i rishonim} read the {\i girsa} as {\i ke-Abba Shaul}, and offer a different explanation for why {\i Mar Zutra} had to be in mid-{\i chazakah} at the moment of death. Even according to {\i Abba Shaul}, where a minor cannot acquire himself in the gap, {\i Mar Zutra} still needed to be in possession at that precise instant \u8212- not to prevent the slave from acquiring himself, but to prevent another person from beating him to the acquisition. The story thus reflects a race against other potential claimants, not only against the slave\u8217's own potential freedom.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gadol vs.\u160 ?Katan\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} goes further, however, and argues that the case {\i must} have involved a {\i katan}, not a {\i gadol} \u8212- and that this is precisely the point of the reading {\i ke-Abba Shaul}. Even if {\i Mar Zutra} had been performing {\i chazakah} at the exact moment of death, a {\i gadol} would nonetheless go free. At a deep halakhic and conceptual level, an adult\u8217's ownership of himself takes precedence over any {\i chazakah} being performed upon him at that very moment. The slave\u8217's fundamental self-possession reasserts itself the instant the master dies, overriding even a concurrent act of external acquisition. Only in the case of a {\i katan}, who according to {\i Abba Shaul} cannot acquire himself, does the {\i chazakah} of another party prevail. This reading gives the case sharp halakhic logic: the story works only if the slave is a minor, and only according to {\i Abba Shaul} who holds that minors cannot acquire themselves.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rabbi Meir Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Meir Rules\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} now turns to the mechanisms of self-redemption, and here {\i Rabbi Meir} and the {\i Chachamim} fundamentally disagree. According to {\i Rabbi Meir}: {\i be-kesef al yedei acheirim} \u8212- redemption by money requires that the money come from others, not the slave himself; {\i u-ve-shtar al yedei atzmo} \u8212- and the writ of manumission must be delivered directly to the slave. The {\i gemara} must establish what case is contemplated here and why these particular rules follow.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 No Slave Money\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} works through {\i Rabbi Meir}\u8217's position. Is the case one where the third party pays the money without the slave\u8217's knowledge or consent ({\i shelo mi-da\u8217'ato})? A difficulty arises immediately: {\i Rabbi Meir} holds {\i chov hu le-eved she-yetzei mi-yad rabo le-cheirus} \u8212- that liberation may actually be a {\i chov}, a detriment, from the slave\u8217's perspective. A freed slave takes on additional obligations, loses certain privileges, and may be worse off in various ways; therefore one cannot say {\i zachin} (operate on someone\u8217's behalf without their consent) when it is not clear that freedom is a benefit. A third party cannot receive a {\i shtar shichrur} on behalf of the slave unless the slave has agreed. So the redemption through third-party money must in fact be {\i mi-da\u8217'ato} \u8212- with the slave\u8217's knowledge and consent.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What, then, is the {\i chiddush} of the {\i mishnah}? {\i Al yedei acheirim in, adidech atzmo lo} \u8212- the news is that only others\u8217' money can redeem him; his own money cannot. {\i Alma ein kinyan le-eved belo rabbo} \u8212- according to {\i Rabbi Meir}, a slave has no proprietary capacity independent of his master. Any money he earns belongs to the master automatically; he cannot possess money in his own right.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gito ve-Yado\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This raises a sharp question. If the slave cannot own anything \u8212- since his hand is, as it were, like his master\u8217's hand \u8212- how can the master give him a {\i shtar shichrur} directly? Anything placed in the slave\u8217's hand is as though it were placed in the master\u8217's own hand. The answer is {\i gito ve-yado ba\u8217'im ke\u8217'echad}: in the very moment that the {\i shtar shichrur} reaches the slave\u8217's hand, he simultaneously acquires it and acquires himself. The manumission and the acquisition happen as a single instantaneous event, so there is no logical problem of the slave\u8217's hand being an extension of the master\u8217's at the moment of receipt. This is the {\i chiddush} of the {\i mishnah}\u8217's ruling that {\i shtar} is {\i al yedei atzmo} \u8212- even though he cannot normally own anything, he can receive his own {\i shtar shichrur} by virtue of this principle.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But the {\i mishnah} then seems to be read as excluding third-party delivery of the {\i shtar}: {\i al yedei atzmo} but not {\i al yedei acheirim}. That is puzzling: since the slave has agreed to be freed ({\i mi-da\u8217'ato}), why can a third party not receive the {\i shtar} on his behalf? The {\i gemara} brings a {\i baraita}: {\i be-shtar al yedei atzmo ve-lo al yedei acheirim, divrei Rabbi Meir} \u8212- only {\i al yedei atzmo}, explicitly excluding others. The reason emerges from the subsequent analysis.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Abbaye: Kesef Shelo M\u8217'Da\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Amar Abbaye le-olam shelo mi-da\u8217'ato} \u8212- Abbaye proposes a fundamental reorientation: in fact, the third-party money works even {\i without} the slave\u8217's consent. {\i Ho\u8217'il ve-kana-o be\u8217'al korcho, makni-o be\u8217'al korcho} \u8212- since the slave was originally sold against his will, he can be freed against his will. The act of purchasing a slave is done without requiring the slave\u8217's agreement, and therefore the parallel act of purchasing his freedom is likewise valid without his agreement.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sale-Freedom Parallel\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The conceptual architecture of Abbaye\u8217's argument is that the {\i kesef} that acquires and the {\i kesef} that frees are the same kind of instrument doing the same kind of work; the slave\u8217's will is simply not a variable in money transactions involving him. It is a striking and somewhat counterintuitive move \u8212- freedom is normally something one would want \u8212- but Abbaye\u8217's point is structural: the {\i kinyan} logic of {\i kesef} operates identically in both directions.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} challenges: if so, why not free him with a {\i shtar} delivered by a third party {\i shelo mi-da\u8217'ato} as well, since he was acquired by a {\i shtar} against his will? {\i Ha-i shtar la-chud ve-ha-i shtar la-chud} \u8212- no, a {\i shtar} of sale is fundamentally different in character from a {\i shtar} of manumission. They cannot be equated across the two contexts. By contrast, money is money: {\i tiv\u8217'a mi-chad hu} \u8212- it is all the same coin. So the symmetry argument works for {\i kesef} but not for {\i shtar}. The conclusion under Abbaye: the {\i mishnah} is describing a case {\i shelo mi-da\u8217'ato}; third-party {\i kesef} works even without the slave\u8217's consent; third-party {\i shtar} does not.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava: Master Accepts\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rava} offers an alternative explanation for why third-party {\i kesef} works without requiring {\i zachin}. {\i Kesef kabbalat rabbo garma lo} \u8212- in a {\i kesef} redemption, what actually effects the liberation is the master\u8217's acceptance of the money. The third party who provides the funds is not, in the halakhic sense, {\i representing the slave} \u8212- he is simply providing the instrument. The master\u8217's act of taking the money is the operative moment of freedom. Therefore the principle of {\i zachin} \u8212- which governs representation of a person\u8217's interests \u8212- is never implicated. No one needs to act as the slave\u8217's agent, because the slave is not the actor in this transaction.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Shtar kabbalat acheirim garma lo} \u8212- a {\i shtar}, however, effects liberation through the act of receipt. The natural recipient is the slave himself; if a third party receives it instead, that third party is necessarily acting as the slave\u8217's representative, which requires {\i zachin} and therefore the slave\u8217's prior consent. Under Rava\u8217's formulation, then, {\i shtar al yedei acheirim} would work {\i mi-da\u8217'ato} \u8212- if the slave agrees, a third party can certainly receive the {\i shtar} on his behalf. But it will not work {\i shelo mi-da\u8217'ato}, because representation requires consent.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Sages Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Slave\u8217's Own Money\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Ve-chachamim omrim: be-kesef al yedei atzmo} \u8212- the Sages hold that a slave may redeem himself with his own money. This immediately implies a foundational disagreement with {\i Rabbi Meir}: the {\i Chachamim} maintain that a slave can indeed own money, at least in certain circumstances, independently of his master.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kesef Both Ways\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} asks whether {\i al yedei atzmo} is exclusive \u8212- only his own money, not others\u8217'. This seems unlikely: the {\i Chachamim} hold that freedom is a {\i zachut} and that {\i zachin le-adam shelo be-fanav} \u8212- one may act on another\u8217's behalf when it is clearly to his benefit, even without his knowledge. Surely others could also provide the money. The {\i gemara} resolves: {\i mai al yedei atzmo, af al yedei atzmo} \u8212- the {\i mishnah}\u8217's formulation means \u8220"even through his own money,\u8221" not \u8220"exclusively through his own money.\u8221" The {\i chiddush} of the {\i Chachamim} is the ruling that {\i yesh kinyan le-eved be-lo rabbo} \u8212- a slave can own property in his own right, making self-redemption through his own funds possible. Third-party money certainly also works under the {\i Chachamim}\u8217's view.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shtar by Others\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 For {\i shtar}, the {\i Chachamim} say {\i al yedei acheirim} \u8212- the writ is delivered to others. The {\i gemara} interprets this as meaning others can receive it on his behalf \u8212- which is straightforwardly explicable by {\i zachin}: since freedom is a {\i zachut}, third parties can receive the {\i shtar} for the slave\u8217's benefit without his even being present.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gito ve-Yado Q\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But this raises a sharp question. The {\i Chachamim} hold that a slave {\i can} own money. If so, they presumably also accept {\i gito ve-yado ba\u8217'im ke\u8217'echad}, since that principle is needed to explain how the slave can receive his own {\i shtar}. Why then does the {\i mishnah} under the {\i Chachamim}\u8217's position say {\i al yedei acheirim} rather than allow both? If {\i al yedei acheirim} means \u8220"even {\i al yedei acheirim}\u8221" \u8212- i.e., additionally to {\i al yedei atzmo} \u8212- then the {\i mishnah} should have formulated it to cover both together. But if {\i al yedei acheirim} means \u8220"only {\i al yidei acheirim},\u8221" then why can the slave not receive the {\i shtar} himself, given that the {\i Chachamim} recognize both his capacity for ownership and the principle of {\i gito ve-yado}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Shimon\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} answers: {\i ve-Rabbi Shimon ben Elazar hi} \u8212- this reflects the position of {\i Rabbi Shimon ben Elazar}, as taught in a {\i baraita}: {\i Rabbi Shimon ben Elazar omer: af be-shtar al yedei acheirim ve-lo al yedei atzmo} \u8212- the {\i shtar} may only be delivered to others, not directly to the slave. This is a distinct position that requires its own explanation, which {\i Rava} will provide.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Three Core Issues\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Ve-shalosh machlokos ba-davar} \u8212- there are in fact three independent debates embedded in this {\i mishnah}, though the {\i rishonim} differ on how exactly to align them. The first is the question of {\i zachin} versus {\i chov}: are the {\i Chachamim} correct that freedom is a {\i zachut} (permitting third-party action without consent), or does {\i Rabbi Meir} have the better of the argument that it is a {\i chov} (requiring the slave\u8217's consent)? The main practical consequence of this debate is whether {\i shtar al yidei acheirim} can work without the slave\u8217's consent. The second debate concerns whether a slave can own money at all: {\i Rabbi Meir} says {\i ein kinyan le-eved be-lo rabbo}, the {\i Chachamim} say {\i yesh kinyan}. This determines whether {\i kesef al yidei atzmo} is possible. The third debate is whether {\i gito ve-yado ba\u8217'im ke\u8217'echad} applies \u8212- whether the slave can receive a {\i shtar} directly and thereby acquire himself simultaneously. This governs {\i shtar al yidei atzmo}. {\i Rashi} reads the {\i Chachamim} as actually endorsing both {\i al yidei atzmo} and {\i al yidei acheirim} for {\i shtar}, with the {\i mishnah}\u8217's phrasing {\i al yidei acheirim} merely alluding to the minority position of {\i Rabbi Shimon ben Elazar} rather than endorsing it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Get Analogy\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Amar Rava: mai ta\u8217'ama de-Rabbi Shimon ben Elazar?} \u8212- why does {\i Rabbi Shimon ben Elazar} hold that you cannot give the {\i shtar} directly to the slave? {\i Gamar lah la-mah me-ishah} \u8212- he derives it by analogy to the {\i get} given to a woman. {\i Mai ishah \u8212- ad she-yotzi get li-reshut she-eino shelo} \u8212- when a husband gives a {\i get}, he gives it to a domain that is not his own. The woman is a separate legal entity, outside the husband\u8217's {\i reshut}. {\i Af eved nami \u8212- ad she-yotzi get li-reshut she-eino shelo} \u8212- so too the {\i shtar shichrur} must be given to a domain outside the master\u8217's own. The slave himself, being entirely within the master\u8217's legal domain, cannot be that recipient; the {\i shtar} must be given to a third party who genuinely stands outside the master\u8217's ownership. Although there is the principle of {\i gito ve-yado ba\u8217'im ke\u8217'echad}, {\i Rabbi Shimon ben Elazar} \u8212- and by extension {\i Rava}\u8217's explanation \u8212- holds that the slave\u8217's hand does not attain true independence of domain in the relevant sense: the woman in the {\i get} is not the property of the husband to the same degree that the slave is the property of the master, and this distinction makes the analogy work in a specific direction. This point is noted as requiring further development.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Kiddushin 24\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Gito v\u8217'Yado Principle\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishna Dispute\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The present sugya picks up in the middle of the discussion on 23a concerning {\i eved kenaani} \u8212- how a Canaanite slave is acquired and, more precisely, how a slave acquires his own freedom. The {\i mishnah} records a {\i machloket} between Rabbi Meir and the {\i chachamim} on this question. Rabbi Meir holds that a slave may acquire his freedom {\i bekesef al yedei acherim} \u8212- through money paid by others on his behalf \u8212- or {\i uvishtar al yedei atzmo} \u8212- by personally receiving a {\i shtar shichrur}, a document of freedom, from his master. The {\i chachamim} appear to disagree, holding {\i bekesef al yedei atzmo} \u8212- that the slave himself may hand the money over \u8212- but {\i uvishtar al yedei acherim} \u8212- that the {\i shtar shichrur} must be received through another person.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i shakla ve-tarya} that preceded this point on 23a suggested that there may in fact be a third opinion embedded in the {\i mishnah} \u8212- that of Rabbi Shimon ben Elazar. On this reading, the {\i chachamim} actually permit the slave to acquire his freedom through either money or a {\i shtar} on his own, while Rabbi Shimon ben Elazar is the one who specifically requires that the {\i shtar shichrur} be received {\i al yedei acherim}, through someone other than the slave himself.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava\u8217's Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is against this backdrop that the {\i gemara} opens with the last two words of 23a: {\i bayei Rava le-Rabbi Shimon ben Elazar} \u8212- Rava poses a question directed at the position of Rabbi Shimon ben Elazar. The question is: {\i eved kenaani ma hu sheyaseh shaliach lekabel gito miyad rabo} \u8212- can a Canaanite slave appoint a {\i shaliach} to receive his {\i get} of freedom from his master?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The framing of the question turns on a {\i gezeirah shavah}. The law of slave manumission is derived from the law of divorce through a verbal analogy on the word {\i la} \u8212- appearing in both contexts \u8212- linking the freeing of a slave to the {\i get} given to a woman. Since a woman may appoint a {\i shaliach} to receive her {\i get} on her behalf, one might think a slave should likewise be able to do so. But Rava presses further: {\i o dilma ishah de\u8217'ihi matzi mekabel giteh shaliach nami matzi mashvei} \u8212- perhaps a woman can appoint a {\i shaliach} precisely because she herself has the capacity to receive her own {\i get}. {\i Eved de\u8217'ihu lo mekabel giteh} \u8212- but a slave, according to Rabbi Shimon ben Elazar, who does not have the capacity to receive his own {\i get shichrur} directly \u8212- {\i shaliach nami lo matzi mashvei} \u8212- would likewise be unable to appoint a {\i shaliach} to receive it for him.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The underlying assumption seems to be that this conclusion would be absurd, that surely a slave should be able to appoint an agent. After posing the question, however, Rava resolves it himself: {\i la-la me-ishah} \u8212- the {\i gezeirah shavah} connecting the slave\u8217's freedom to a woman\u8217's divorce does in fact extend to this, and therefore the slave is compared to a woman in the relevant respects.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is worth pausing to appreciate how a principle worked out in Masechet Gittin resurfaces here. The rule of {\i gito v\u8217'yado ba\u8217'im k\u8217'echad} \u8212- that the slave\u8217's {\i get} and his acquiring hand come simultaneously \u8212- is central to understanding why Rabbi Shimon ben Elazar holds that the slave cannot receive his own {\i shtar shichrur} directly. This is precisely why Rabbi Shimon ben Elazar\u8217's opinion is of particular interest, and why tracking opinions across different {\i masekhtot} is essential to a full understanding of any given {\i sugya}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shaliach Limitation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This brings into view a more general principle \u8212- that a {\i shaliach} cannot be empowered to perform an act that the person appointing him could not perform himself. This rule is derived from Masechet Nazir (daf 12) and applies broadly to the laws of {\i shlichut}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kohanim Shlichut\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} connects this to a famous question raised by Rav Huna the son of Rav Yehoshua, which appears in Masechet Nedarim: {\i hani kohanei shluchei de-rachmana ninhu} \u8212- are the {\i kohanim} agents of God, or are they agents of {\i Bnei Yisrael} when they perform the {\i avodah}? The question is framed precisely in terms of this principle of {\i shlichut}: {\i mi ika midi de\u8217'anan lo matzinan avdinan ve\u8217'ihu matzi avid} \u8212- is there any act a {\i shaliach} can perform that the person appointing him could not perform himself? Since a {\i Yisrael} cannot perform the {\i avodah}, and the {\i kohanim} do perform it, the {\i kohanim} cannot be agents of {\i Bnei Yisrael}. They must therefore be {\i shluchei de-rachmana} \u8212- agents of God. The practical {\i nafka mina} raised in Masechet Nedarim concerns whether a {\i kohen} may perform the {\i avodah} for someone who is {\i mudar hana\u8217'ah} from him \u8212- that is, if the {\i kohen} is acting as his agent, the prohibition would apply, whereas if the {\i kohen} is God\u8217's agent, it would not.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Slave vs.\u160 ?Woman\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} draws a distinction between the {\i sugya} here and the {\i sugya} in Nedarim. A {\i Yisrael} has no connection to {\i korbanot} whatsoever \u8212- {\i Yisrael lo shaychei b\u8217'torat korbanot klal} \u8212- and therefore there is no basis for any principle of {\i shlichut} that could apply to him with respect to the {\i avodah}. By contrast, {\i eved shayach begittin} \u8212- a slave is fully connected to the notion of his bill of freedom. The slave is not categorically excluded from the legal framework in the same way a {\i Yisrael} is excluded from performing the {\i avodah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Friend\u8217's Get Shichrur\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i baraita} is cited to illustrate this distinction: {\i nirin hadevarim she\u8217'ha\u8217'eved mekabel gito shel chaveiro miyad rabo shel chaveiro} \u8212- it appears from the logic of the matter that a slave may receive his fellow slave\u8217's {\i get shichrur} from that fellow slave\u8217's master, acting as an agent for his friend. {\i Aval lo miyad rabo shelo} \u8212- but he cannot receive his own {\i shtar shichrur} from his own master. The reason is not that a slave is categorically incapable of receiving a {\i get shichrur}, but rather the specific problem of {\i kol ma shekana eved kana rabo} \u8212- whatever a slave acquires, his master acquires. Therefore, if the slave were to receive his own {\i get shichrur} into his own hand, it would be as if the master were giving it back to himself, and the slave would never actually receive it. This is the very logic of {\i gito v\u8217'yado ba\u8217'in k\u8217'echad} \u8212- the slave\u8217's {\i get} and his acquiring hand come as one \u8212- which Rabbi Shimon ben Elazar does not accept, and which is therefore the crux of his distinctive position.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Conditional Gifts\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kol Mah Shekana\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now shifts from the {\i sugya} of Rabbi Shimon ben Elazar and returns to the original {\i machloket} in the {\i mishnah} between Rabbi Meir and the {\i chachamim}. The {\i chachamim} had said that while the slave may pay his own freedom through money, {\i uvlvad sheyehei hakesef mishel acheirim} \u8212- the money must nonetheless belong to others. This requires explanation: if the slave can hand the money over himself, why must the money belong to someone else?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} raises the possibility that the {\i machloket} turns on the underlying question of {\i kol ma shekana eved kana rabo} \u8212- whatever a slave acquires, his master acquires. Does this mean a slave is entirely incapable of acquiring anything for himself, or might there be some qualified form of independent acquisition?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Meir Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} initially proposes that Rabbi Meir holds {\i ein kinyan l\u8217'eved belo rabo} \u8212- a slave cannot acquire anything without it immediately transferring to the master. Similarly, {\i ein kinyan l\u8217'isha belo ba\u8217'ala} \u8212- whatever a woman acquires transfers to her husband. On this reading, Rabbi Meir maintains there is simply no independent avenue of acquisition available to the slave.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chachamim Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i chachamim}, on this initial reading, would hold {\i yesh kinyan l\u8217'eved belo rabo} \u8212- a slave can have independent acquisition, and similarly {\i yesh kinyan l\u8217'isha belo ba\u8217'ala} \u8212- a woman can acquire independently of her husband. This is why the {\i mishnah} needed to specify that the money must belong to others: since the slave can in principle have his own property, one might have thought he could use his own funds, so the {\i mishnah} tells us that even according to the {\i chachamim} the money cannot be the slave\u8217's own.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Elazar\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabba bar Rav Sheshet in the name of Rav Sheshet, however, rejects this framing entirely. He maintains that in truth everyone \u8212- both Rabbi Meir and the {\i chachamim} \u8212- agrees that {\i ein kinyan l\u8217'eved belo rabo}, a slave cannot acquire anything without it transferring to the master, and {\i ein kinyan l\u8217'isha belo ba\u8217'ala} as well. The {\i machloket} must therefore concern something else.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The case under discussion, Rav Sheshet argues, must involve a third party who goes to the slave and says: {\i al menat she\u8217'ein lerabcha reshut bo} \u8212- here is money on the condition that your master has no rights to it. The donor is attempting to circumvent {\i kol ma shekana eved kana rabo} by building in a condition that the master should not acquire it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Elazar then refines the case still further. It cannot simply be that someone gives the slave money hoping the slave will use it to buy his freedom through a subsequent transaction, because everyone would agree that the slave\u8217's momentary acquisition of the money would immediately vest in the master. Rather, the operative case according to Rabbi Elazar is one where someone hands money to the slave while simultaneously declaring {\i al menat sheteitzei bo l\u8217'cheirut} \u8212- on the condition that you go free through this. The third party is not giving the slave money to hold; he is attempting to purchase the slave\u8217's freedom by transferring funds directly to the slave, with the freedom as the condition.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ownership Dispute\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Given this framing, the {\i machloket} between Rabbi Meir and the {\i chachamim} becomes clear \u8212- and, striking, almost counterintuitive. Both agree that whatever the slave acquires goes to the master. The question is whether that very mechanism can serve as the vehicle for the slave\u8217's freedom.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Meir holds: {\i ki amar lei kani kani eved v\u8217'kani rabo} \u8212- when the donor says \u8220"acquire it,\u8221" the slave acquires and therefore the master acquires, {\i v\u8217'chi amar lei al menat lo klum ka\u8217'amar lei} \u8212- and the condition {\i al menat} is legally void. One cannot impose a condition that subverts the fundamental nature of the slave relationship. The condition therefore has no legal force, and the money simply passes to the master.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i rabbanan} hold that {\i keivan d\u8217'amar lei al menat ahanei lei t\u8217'nai} \u8212- once the donor formulates the condition, the condition does have operative legal force, and through this mechanism the slave may go free. The {\i rabbanan} understand that the slave acquires the money as an extension of the master, and that this is precisely what makes the freedom possible \u8212- he acquires it for the master, and simultaneously goes free. As one might observe, it is almost the reverse of what one expects: the slave acquires his freedom {\i precisely because} he has no independent legal standing. His acquisition being the master\u8217's acquisition is what allows the third party\u8217's directed gift to function as a purchase of freedom.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It should be noted that the analogy to a married woman, while related, is not identical. A married woman has various layers of ownership \u8212- reserved assets, {\i shibudim}, {\i peirot} \u8212- that distinguish her situation from that of an {\i eved kenaani} in important respects. An {\i eved kenaani} has no independent legal standing in any domain analogous to a free person; the principle of {\i kol ma shekana eved kana rabo} is far more comprehensive than anything that applies to a married woman. Similarly, none of this applies to an {\i eved Ivri} \u8212- a Hebrew slave \u8212- who retains full personhood and legal standing. In Masechet Bava Metzia, in the discussion of {\i eilu metzi\u8217'os}, the {\i eved Ivri} is listed among those who retain ownership over found objects, which illustrates the fundamental halakhic distinction between the two categories of slavery.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Ma\u8217'aser Sheni\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Woman & Chomesh\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now introduces a {\i baraita} that appears to contradict the positions of both Rabbi Meir and the {\i chachamim} as they have been understood: {\i ein ishah podeh Ma\u8217'aser Sheni belo chomesh} \u8212- a woman may not redeem {\i Ma\u8217'aser Sheni} without paying the {\i chomesh}. By way of background: normally, {\i Ma\u8217'aser Sheni} produce is to be brought to Yerushalayim and eaten there. If the distance is too great or the quantity too large, the Torah permits redeeming the produce onto money \u8212- but when one redeems one\u8217's own {\i Ma\u8217'aser Sheni}, a {\i chomesh} \u8212- a surcharge of one-fifth of the final amount, which works out to 25% of the original value \u8212- must be added. The {\i chomesh} functions as a kind of penalty on using redemption rather than physically transporting the produce. The statement that a woman {\i must} pay the {\i chomesh} implies that she is acting as an agent of her husband when redeeming {\i his} produce, and therefore the exemption that might apply to her does not.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Meir Woman\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Against this, Rabbi Shimon ben Elazar transmits an opinion in the name of Rabbi Meir: {\i ishah podeh Ma\u8217'aser Sheni belo chomesh} \u8212- a woman may redeem {\i Ma\u8217'aser Sheni} without the {\i chomesh}, treating her as sufficiently independent of her husband in this context that she is not considered his agent.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Case Analysis\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} immediately interrogates what the actual case is. If we are dealing with the husband\u8217's money and the husband\u8217's {\i Ma\u8217'aser Sheni} produce, then she is plainly performing her husband\u8217's {\i shlichut}, and the first position \u8212- that she must pay the {\i chomesh} \u8212- clearly applies. If we are dealing with her own money and her own {\i Ma\u8217'aser Sheni}, then there is a {\i drasha} in the Torah \u8212- {\i ish amar Rachmana velo ishah}, the Torah says \u8220"a man\u8221" to exclude a woman \u8212- which might exempt her from the {\i chomesh} entirely. Rashi understands this exclusion to mean {\i ish mi-maasero amar Rachmana velo ishah mi-maaser ba\u8217'alah} \u8212- a man pays the {\i chomesh} when redeeming his own {\i Ma\u8217'aser Sheni}, but a woman is considered like an outsider with respect to her husband\u8217's {\i Ma\u8217'aser Sheni}, and therefore is not subject to the {\i chomesh}. Tosafot, by contrast, connects the exemption to the broader question of {\i talmud Torah} and the verse {\i ve-lamadeta} that appears in the context of {\i Ma\u8217'aser Sheni}, understanding the obligation of {\i chomesh} to be bound up with the {\i mitzvah} dimension of eating {\i Ma\u8217'aser Sheni} \u8212- a {\i mitzvah} from which women may be exempt. The question of how intrinsic the {\i chomesh} is to the {\i pidyon} of {\i Ma\u8217'aser Sheni} generally \u8212- whether it is simply part of the {\i mitzvah} or a separable surcharge \u8212- underlies this dispute between Rashi and Tosafot.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 In any event, neither of those simple cases generates the interesting {\i machloket}. The {\i gemara} therefore looks for a case that would produce the apparent contradiction to the positions on {\i eved kenaani}. It arrives at the following: someone else comes to the woman and gives her money, saying {\i al menat sheteitzei bo la-cheirut} \u8212- that is, on the condition that she use it specifically to redeem the {\i Ma\u8217'aser Sheni}. This is the precise parallel to the case of the slave. And yet here, Rabbi Meir appears to hold that the condition {\i does} work \u8212- that the woman can use this designated money to redeem the {\i Ma\u8217'aser Sheni} without {\i chomesh} \u8212- while the {\i chachamim} appear to hold that it does not work and she must pay {\i chomesh}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is directly contradicted by what we saw in the context of the slave, where Rabbi Meir said conditions do not work and the {\i chachamim} said they do. Hence: {\i rami de-Rabbi Meir a-de-Rabbi Meir, rami de-Rabbanan a-de-Rabbanan} \u8212- there is an internal contradiction in Rabbi Meir\u8217's position and an internal contradiction in the {\i Rabbanan}\u8217's position.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One proposed resolution is {\i ipcha shamina lehu} \u8212- we simply heard the two sources with their attributions reversed, and by flipping one of them the contradiction disappears. Abaye endorses this: {\i ipoch} \u8212- reverse them. But Rava objects: {\i l\u8217'olam lo sipoach} \u8212- one should not simply reverse {\i mishnayot} and {\i baraisos} to make them fit. There must be an organic distinction that resolves the apparent tension.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava\u8217's Resolution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava proposes that the two cases are not actually parallel. In the {\i Ma\u8217'aser Sheni} case, he specifies: {\i dhacha b\u8217'ma\u8217'aser d\u8217'asya mibei nasha askinan} \u8212- we are dealing with {\i Ma\u8217'aser Sheni} that the woman has inherited from her father\u8217's estate, where she has no brothers and is the sole heir. In this case, the {\i Ma\u8217'aser Sheni} produce belongs entirely to her, not to her husband. It is not, therefore, a case of someone giving her designated funds to redeem her husband\u8217's {\i Ma\u8217'aser Sheni}; rather, she is redeeming her own {\i Ma\u8217'aser Sheni} with her own inherited property.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ma\u8217'aser Ownership\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But Rava goes further, pointing to what he regards as the more fundamental {\i machloket}: {\i Rabbi Meir l\u8217'taimei d\u8217'amar Ma\u8217'aser mamon hekdesh hu} \u8212- Rabbi Meir holds that {\i Ma\u8217'aser Sheni} belongs to {\i hekdesh}, that is, it is property of God. Consequently, {\i velo kanei lei ba\u8217'al} \u8212- the husband does not acquire the woman\u8217's {\i Ma\u8217'aser Sheni} when she inherits it, because it was never truly hers as ordinary property to begin with. Therefore, when she redeems it, she is acting on her own account, and Rabbi Meir\u8217's exemption from {\i chomesh} makes sense.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i rabbanan}, by contrast, hold {\i mamon hedyot hu} \u8212- {\i Ma\u8217'aser Sheni} is ordinary personal property, {\i v\u8217'kanei lei ba\u8217'al} \u8212- and therefore the husband acquires it when she inherits it. When she redeems it, she is acting as her husband\u8217's agent \u8212- {\i shlichusa d\u8217'ba\u8217'al ka\u8217'avda} \u8212- and the {\i chomesh} therefore applies to her.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is a remarkable {\i machloket} with broad implications. {\i Ma\u8217'aser Sheni} occupies a unique place among the various categories of {\i hekdesh} and {\i kodshim}. Unlike {\i terumah}, where the produce belongs to the owner and must be transferred to the {\i kohen}, {\i Ma\u8217'aser Sheni} \u8212- on Rabbi Meir\u8217's view \u8212- belongs to God, and the owner must go to Yerushalayim to eat it there, in a sanctified space, because that is where it belongs. Yet {\i Ma\u8217'aser Sheni} is distinct from {\i korbanos} and other {\i kodshim} in that it may be eaten by {\i Yisraelim} and is subject to redemption and transfer in ways that most {\i hekdesh} is not \u8212- it is a more fluid form of {\i kedushah}, yet retains at its core an obligation that originates from God. It is similar in some ways to {\i bechor} and {\i ma\u8217'aser behemah}, while remaining distinct.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Injury Mechanisms\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tooth & Eye\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i baraita} is cited: {\i yotzei beshein ve\u8217'ayin u-roshei evarim she-einan chozrin} \u8212- a slave goes free when his master knocks out his tooth or his eye, and also through injury to the tips of limbs that do not grow back. There are twenty-four such tips, which also carry significance in the laws of {\i nega\u8217'im}, where the tips of limbs cannot be examined for {\i tzara\u8217'at} from a single vantage point. The explicit biblical source covers only {\i shen} and {\i ayin} \u8212- tooth and eye. The question is therefore raised: from where do we derive that injury to the tips of other limbs also triggers the slave\u8217's freedom?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} answers: {\i dumya de-shein ve\u8217'ayin} \u8212- by analogy to tooth and eye. {\i Ma shein ve\u8217'ayin mum she-bagalui ve-einan chozrin} \u8212- just as tooth and eye are visible blemishes that do not grow back, {\i af kol mum she-bagalui ve-einan chozrin} \u8212- so too any visible blemish that does not grow back.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Klal U\u8217'Prat\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Before accepting this, the {\i gemara} considers a series of hermeneutical alternatives. One might argue that since both {\i shen} and {\i ayin} are explicitly mentioned in the Torah, they function as {\i shnei ketuvim ha-ba\u8217'im ke-echad} \u8212- two verses that arrive together with the same teaching \u8212- and the rule is {\i ein melamdin}, one cannot derive a generalization from two explicit cases that cover the same ground. The {\i gemara} responds that both cases are independently necessary. Had the Torah written only {\i shein}, one might have thought even a milk tooth \u8212- {\i shein she-yanak bah}, a tooth that grew in while the child was still nursing \u8212- would suffice. The inclusion of {\i ayin} tells us that the paradigm is something that is {\i nivra imo}, created with the person from birth. Conversely, had the Torah written only {\i ayin}, one might have read the paradigm as limited to limbs present from birth, excluding teeth that grow in later. {\i Tzricha} \u8212- both are necessary, and therefore the two cases do not function as redundant examples that prevent generalization.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Another hermeneutical path is proposed and rejected: perhaps the verse should be read as {\i klal u-prat} \u8212- general and specific \u8212- such that {\i ki yakkeh} is the general and {\i shein ve\u8217'ayin} is the specific, which would {\i limit} the application to tooth and eye only. The {\i gemara} notes, however, that the verse concludes with {\i le-chofshi yishalchenu} \u8212- \u8220"he shall send him free\u8221" \u8212- which adds a second general term. The structure therefore becomes {\i klal u-prat u-klal}, and by the rules of this hermeneutic, the concluding general term expands the application beyond the specific examples in the middle. The expansion is still constrained, however: {\i ei ata dan ela ke-ein ha-prat} \u8212- the generalization must conform to the nature of the specific examples.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Specific Examples\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What defines the category shaped by the {\i prat}? {\i Mum she-bagalui ve-einan chozrin} \u8212- a visible blemish that does not grow back \u8212- is one dimension. But the {\i gemara} adds a further qualification: {\i batel m\u8217'melachto} \u8212- the injury must also render the affected limb unable to perform its normal function. A tooth that has been knocked out cannot chew; an eye that has been blinded cannot see. Any injury to the body that is visible, permanent, and disabling in this way falls within the category.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Two concrete examples are provided by a {\i baraita}: if the master pulled the slave\u8217's beard and thereby dislocated the slave\u8217's jaw \u8212- {\i talash b\u8217'zikano v\u8217'dildel bo etzem} \u8212- the slave goes free, because a dislocated jaw is visible, does not heal on its own, and prevents normal eating. Similarly, if the master struck the slave\u8217's arm and it withered \u8212- {\i hikahu al yado v\u8217'tzamtah} \u8212- but eventually the arm healed, the slave does {\i not} go free, because the injury was not permanent. The {\i baraita} makes this explicit: {\i ein eved yotzei bo l\u8217'cheirut}. This concrete ruling serves to limit the scope of {\i klal u-prat u-klal}, anchoring the requirement of {\i eino chozer} \u8212- that the injury must be one that does not heal \u8212- as an essential qualification.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tannaim Dispute\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 With the scope of liberating injuries established, the {\i gemara} turns to a deeper dispute: does the injury itself effect the slave\u8217's freedom automatically, or does it merely create an obligation on the master to issue a {\i get shichrur}? A {\i baraita} records the full range of {\i tanna\u8217'itic} opinion. Rabbi Shimon holds that for all such injuries \u8212- including tooth and eye \u8212- the slave goes free but {\i requires} a {\i get shichrur}: the injury creates an obligation, it does not itself accomplish the manumission. Rabbi Meir holds the opposite: {\i eino tzarich} \u8212- no {\i get shichrur} is required; the injury itself liberates the slave. Rabbi Eliezer agrees with Rabbi Shimon, and Rabbi Tarfon agrees with Rabbi Meir.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} also records the view of the {\i machri\u8217'im lifnei chachamim} \u8212- those who adjudicated before the {\i chachamim} \u8212- who adopt a split ruling.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chachamim Compromise\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Nir\u8217'in divrei Rabbi Tarfon b\u8217'shein ve\u8217'ayin} \u8212- the reasoning of Rabbi Tarfon appears correct with respect to tooth and eye, {\i shehatorah zachta lo} \u8212- because the Torah itself explicitly granted the slave his freedom through these specific injuries, and therefore the injury itself is the operative mechanism. {\i Ve-divrei Rabbi Akiva b\u8217'sha\u8217'ar eivarim} \u8212- but with respect to other limbs, whose inclusion is derived only through the {\i midrash} of the {\i chachamim} rather than being explicit in the Torah, Rabbi Akiva\u8217's position appears correct: a {\i get shichrur} is still required.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} notes that to call the derivation for other limbs {\i midrash chachamim} \u8212- as opposed to explicit Torah \u8212- is not to call it merely rabbinic. The {\i drasha} from {\i klal u-prat u-klal} does carry {\i de\u8217'oraita} force. Nevertheless, what is learned through {\i drashot} has a somewhat lesser degree of textual explicitness than what is stated plainly in the {\i pesukim}, and the {\i Rambam} is particularly attentive to this distinction \u8212- though calling it purely rabbinic would be an overstatement. This reflects a broader {\i machloket} between Rabbi Yochanan and Resh Lakish about whether the majority of Torah is {\i Torah she-bichsav} or {\i Torah she-ba\u8217'al peh}, and whether {\i drashot} are uncovering something latent in the written text or transmitting an oral tradition that was subsequently anchored to verses.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 As for the substance of the {\i machloket} between Rabbi Meir and Rabbi Shimon: Rabbi Shimon derives from a {\i gezeirah shavah} of {\i shiluach-shiluach} \u8212- the freeing of a slave compared to the divorce of a woman \u8212- that just as a woman requires a {\i shtar}, so too a slave requires a {\i shtar}. Rabbi Meir, by contrast, reads the phrase {\i le-chofshi yishalchenu} as indicating that at the moment of the injury the slave is {\i already} free \u8212- {\i hava lei chofshi me\u8217'ikara} \u8212- and no subsequent document is needed to accomplish what the injury itself has already accomplished. On this view, the injury does not merely obligate the master to free the slave; rather, by injuring the slave in this way, the master has forfeited his position as slave master entirely, and the slave goes free as a direct consequence.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Direct vs.\u160 ?Indirect\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Direct Contact\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A new {\i baraita} is introduced that addresses the question of what type of injury triggers a slave\u8217's freedom. {\i Hikeihu al eino v\u8217'sima\u8217'ah} \u8212- if the master struck the slave\u8217's eye and blinded it, {\i al ozno v\u8217'chershah} \u8212- or struck his ear and deafened it \u8212- {\i eved yotzei bahen l\u8217'cheirus} \u8212- the slave goes free. The injury here is a direct physical contact to the affected organ, even though the resulting damage is to the function of the organ rather than to its physical structure. Striking the eye and causing blindness, or striking the ear and causing deafness, constitutes the kind of visible, permanent, functional loss that brings the slave his freedom.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Non-Contact Injury\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 By contrast, {\i keneged eino v\u8217'eino ro\u8217'eh} \u8212- if the master did something in front of the slave\u8217's eye such that the slave lost his sight without any physical contact to the eye \u8212- {\i keneged ozno v\u8217'eino shomei\u8217'a} \u8212- or caused the slave to go deaf through some non-contact means \u8212- {\i ein eved yotzei bahen l\u8217'cheirus} \u8212- the slave does not go free. The {\i baraita} thus distinguishes between a direct physical blow to the organ and a non-contact mechanism that produces the same functional result.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sound Damage\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This distinction prompts an immediate challenge from Rav Shemen to Rav Ashi: does this mean that {\i kol} \u8212- sound \u8212- has no physical force in halacha? Is non-contact acoustic injury simply a legal nullity? Surely not, since a {\i baraita} recorded by Rami bar Yechezkel in the context of Bava Kamma addresses precisely this: a chicken that inserts its head into a glass vessel and crows inside it, shattering the glass without any contact \u8212- according to Sumchos, the owner pays full {\i nezek}. And Rav Yosef transmits from {\i bei Rav} that if a horse whinnies or a donkey brays and the sound breaks vessels inside the house, the owner pays {\i chatzi nezek}. Whether one follows Sumchos (full payment) or the {\i rabbanan} (half payment), the point stands that sound-induced property damage does create liability for {\i nezikin}. Why, then, should non-contact acoustic injury to a slave not suffice to free him?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Person vs.\u160 ?Object\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} resolves the difficulty with a crucial distinction: {\i shani adam dekivan debar daat hu ihu miv\u8217'it nafsheih} \u8212- a person is different, because being a {\i bar daat}, a person with awareness, the victim is in some sense involved in the act of being injured. The precise nature of this involvement is elusive. It is not that the slave is actively complicit in his own hearing loss, but somehow the victim\u8217's status as a {\i bar daat} \u8212- as a person with legal consciousness and awareness \u8212- distinguishes the case from that of a {\i kli}, a glass vessel, which has no such standing. The {\i gemara} does not spell out the mechanism in full, but the distinction operates to mean that non-contact injury to a person has a different halakhic character than non-contact damage to an object.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Blowing Baraita\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A final {\i baraita} is cited to crystallize the distinction: {\i hamav\u8217'it chavero patur midinei adam ve-chayav b\u8217'dinei shamayim} \u8212- one who startles another person and causes injury is exempt from human courts but liable in the heavenly court. Then the specific case: {\i taka be\u8217'ozno ve\u8217'chershah patur} \u8212- if one blows into someone\u8217's ear and deafens him, without holding the person, he is exempt. {\i Achazo ve-taka be\u8217'ozno ve\u8217'chershah chayav} \u8212- but if he held the person and then blew into his ear and deafened him, he is liable. The holding of the victim converts what would otherwise be a non-contact acoustic injury into one with a sufficient element of physical contact to create liability. This {\i baraita} neatly encapsulates the principle that non-contact injury to a {\i bar daat} occupies a different legal category \u8212- and, by extension, that only direct physical injury to the relevant organ suffices to free a slave through the mechanism the Torah established for {\i shen} and {\i ayin}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Kiddushin 25\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Eye/Tooth Damage\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Usability Standard\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The daf opens on 24b, several lines before the text widens, with a {\i baraita} addressing the {\i eved kena\u8217'ani} and the question of when damage to an eye or tooth entitles him to freedom. The {\i tana} teaches: if the master struck the slave\u8217's eye and it became dim ({\i hikah lo eino}), or struck his tooth and it became loose ({\i shino venadeda})\u8212-without totally knocking it out or gouging it entirely from the body\u8212-the measure is functional usability. {\i Im yachol lehishtamesh bahen achshav}, if it is still usable, he does not go free; {\i ve-im lav}, if it is no longer usable, he does go free. Precisely what threshold of residual vision or stability constitutes \u8220"usability\u8221" is left open, but the operative principle is clear: functionality, not structural completeness, governs.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Pre-existing Conditions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A second {\i baraita} addresses the mirror image: what if the slave\u8217's eye was already dim before the master\u8217's act, and the master then brought it from dim to totally blind? Or the tooth was already loose, and the master then knocked it out entirely? Again the measure is usability. {\i Im yachol lehishtamesh bahen kevar}\u8212-if the eye or tooth was still usable beforehand, then by taking it from usable to unusable the master has crossed the threshold and the slave goes free. {\i Ve-im lav}\u8212-if it was already unusable beforehand, he does not.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Necessity of Both Cases\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} asks why both directions need to be taught, since both reduce to the same principle. The answer is that each case is genuinely surprising without the other. Had we only heard the first case\u8212-going from full sight to functionally blind\u8212-one might reason that this applied only because the master caused a radical deterioration: {\i mishum d\u8217'me-ikara nehora bari ve-hashta nehora kakhisha}. But where the eye was already barely usable to begin with, perhaps even bringing it to total blindness would not suffice to free the slave. Conversely, had we only heard the second case\u8212-bringing an already-dim eye all the way to total blindness\u8212-one might think that applied only because he reduced the slave to absolute zero ({\i samya l\u8217'gamre}). But where the master merely brought a functioning eye down to a non-functional but not totally sightless state, one might think the slave does not go free. {\i Tzericha}: both cases are necessary.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Intent & Act Nature\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Accidental vs.\u160 ?Intention\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} next presents a {\i baraita}: what if the master himself was a doctor, and the slave asked him to apply an ointment to his eye ({\i lechkhol lo eino}), or to treat a tooth ({\i lachator lo shino}), and in the course of this therapeutic intervention the master accidentally blinded the eye or knocked out the tooth? The {\i baraita} rules: {\i sicheik b\u8217'adon ve-yatzah le-cheirut}\u8212-the slave laughs in his master\u8217's face and goes free. The unintended result governs; the therapeutic purpose does not shield the master.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Intent Requirement\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel dissents. He reads the word {\i ve-shichatah}\u8212-\u8220"and he destroys it\u8221"\u8212-in the relevant verse as describing not merely the result but the nature of the act: {\i ad she-yitkavein le-shachtah}, only when the master intended to destroy. This would exclude a therapeutic act that accidentally causes injury. Critically, an intentional act of violence\u8212-say, striking the slave in the face and thereby knocking out a tooth\u8212-would still qualify, even if the master was not specifically aiming at the tooth, because the act is fundamentally a destructive one directed at the slave\u8217's body. What is excluded is an act that is therapeutic in character and accidentally produces a harmful result.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Rabbanan} who do not require destructive intent must account for the word {\i ve-shichatah}. They respond: {\i mibaei lei le-khidatanya}\u8212-the word is needed to teach the ruling of Rabbi Elazar.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Fetus Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Elazar teaches: if a man inserted his hand into the womb of his maidservant during childbirth, attempting to assist in delivering the baby, and in doing so accidentally blinded the eye of the fetus, he is {\i patur}\u8212-the child does not go free. The reason: {\i amar kra ve-shichatah, ad she-yitkaven le-shachtah}. Here the man was not relating to the eye at all\u8212-he was trying to extract the fetus and was not even aware of, let alone directing his actions toward, the eye of the unborn child.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This raises a subtle distinction. If a master punches a slave in the face and knocks out a tooth, he did not intend the tooth specifically\u8212-he intended the face. Yet this would presumably qualify because hitting the face in that region is, in the words of the {\i gemara}\u8217's framework, something like a {\i psik reisha}: the tooth is within the scope of the violent act. By contrast, reaching into the womb to assist in a birth is not in any way directed at the eye of the fetus. One is not even aware of being near that area. That categorical difference is what the {\i Rabbanan} derive from {\i ve-shichatah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Veshichatah Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} clarifies the underlying dispute. The {\i Rabbanan} read {\i ve-shichatah} as establishing that even they require some connection between the act and the injured limb\u8212-the fetus case is exempt because there is no connection whatsoever. Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel reads the same word as requiring positive destructive intent. {\i Ve-idach}: one position derives from the doubled form {\i shachat ve-shichatah} an additional teaching; the other holds that no such {\i derasha} from the doubled form is operative ({\i shachat ve-shichatah lo darish}). Rashi\u8217's treatment of the case where someone hit nearby and the slave was startled or frightened ({\i mav\u8217'it}) fills in the bracketing question of what it means to strike \u8220"in the vicinity\u8221" rather than directly.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Visible Limbs\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Complete Removal\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Sheshet introduces a further ruling: {\i harei she-hayeta eino smuya ve-chitatah}\u8212-if the slave\u8217's eye was already functionally blind and the master then gouged it out entirely ({\i chitatah}, removing it from the socket), the slave goes free. This is surprising, since there has been no functional change; the slave could not see before and cannot see now. Nevertheless, {\i mechusar eivar hu}\u8212-he is now missing a limb, and that structural change to the body is itself sufficient. The principle is that the loss of an external limb, even one that was already non-functional, effects the slave\u8217's freedom.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 24 Limb Heads\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} supports this from a {\i baraita} that itself draws on the laws of {\i tzara\u8217'at}: {\i tamim ve-zachrut be-vehemah ve-ein tamim ve-zachrut be-ofot}\u8212-the requirements of being unblemished and male apply to large animal sacrifices but not to bird sacrifices. One might therefore think that a bird with a withered wing, a severed leg, or a gouged-out eye would still be valid for a sacrifice. The verse says {\i min ha-of}\u8212-from the birds, implying not all birds\u8212-excluding a bird that is {\i mechusar eivar}, lacking a limb. Rashi emphasizes that this applies {\i af al gav de-sumya me-ikara}, even if the bird was already blind beforehand, since bird sacrifices may be blemished\u8212-yet the final act of removing the eye is still considered a substantive change that renders it {\i mechusar eivar}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then introduces the twenty-four {\i rashei evarim}, the twenty-four limb heads of the human body that do not contract {\i tum\u8217'at michya} (the sign of {\i tzara\u8217'at} at a healed spot) because of their sloped or protruding nature, which prevents the entire spot from being visible to the {\i kohen} simultaneously. These are: the ten fingertips, the ten toetips (totaling twenty), the tips of the ears (twenty-two), the nose (twenty-three), and the top of the penis (twenty-four). By a woman, the nipples replace or supplement the last entry. Rabbi Yehuda adds the male nipples; the {\i chachamim} do not consider them sufficiently protruding.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i baraita} then teaches: {\i u-vichulam eved yotzei bahen la-cheirut}\u8212-by all of these, a slave goes free if the limb is cut off or destroyed. These twenty-four, plus the eye and the tooth already taught in the {\i mishnah}, constitute the catalog of external limbs. The connection to the {\i eved} is not the {\i tzara\u8217'at} logic of sloped visibility, but the identification of what counts as an external, visible part of the body.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Hiyya bar Ashi says in the name of Rav: if the slave had an extra finger and the master cut it off, the slave goes free. Rav Huna qualifies: {\i ve-hu she-nisperet al gav ha-yad}\u8212-only if the extra finger is counted in continuity with the other fingers of the hand, so that one would count \u8220"one, two, three, four, five, six\u8221" running continuously. If the extra digit protrudes in a way that clearly places it outside the normal row of fingers, it is not considered a finger of the hand, and cutting it off would not free the slave.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Testicles Query\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This leads to the famous exchange. The elders of Nazunya stopped attending Rav Chisda\u8217's {\i shiur}. Rav Chisda sent Rav Hamnuna to inquire why, and rather than simply imposing a sanction, Rav Hamnuna asked what was troubling them. They said: we asked him a question and he did not resolve it\u8212-if he cannot answer our questions, why should we attend? Rav Hamnuna invited them to ask him their question.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 They asked: {\i eved she-seiraso rabo be-beitzim}\u8212-if the master castrated the slave by crushing the testicles, does this qualify as {\i ke-mum she-ba-galuye}, a blemish on a visible limb? The question rests on the following tension: on one hand, the principle is that the master must have injured an external, visible limb. On the other hand, while the testicles are located externally in the sense of not being inside the body cavity, they are not fully visible in the way that a finger or an eye is\u8212-they are, as Rashi puts it, {\i tluyim be-kis ve-nikarim mi-bachutz}, suspended within the scrotal sac and discernible from outside, but not exposed in the way that would make the injury itself plainly visible. Rav Hamnuna did not know the answer.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hamnuna\u8217's Mission\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Hamnuna returned to Rav Chisda with the elders\u8217' question unanswered. The irony was palpable: Rav Chisda had sent him saying \u8220"have you ever asked me something I didn\u8217't answer?\u8221"\u8212-and here was exactly such a question. Rav Chisda told him: {\i matnisa ba\u8217'u minach}\u8212-what they asked you is effectively answered by a {\i mishnah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Castration & Tongue\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} cited lists the twenty-four {\i rashei evarim} and then adds: {\i Rabbi omer af ha-sirus}\u8212-even castration causes the slave to go free. {\i Ben Azzai omer af ha-lashon}\u8212-and cutting off the tongue.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} asks: what castration does Rabbi mean? If it refers to the penis ({\i gid}), that was already included in the twenty-four {\i rashei evarim} as the {\i geviyah}. It must therefore be {\i sirus de-beitzim}\u8212-castration by the testicles. This is the answer to the elders\u8217' question: the {\i mishnah} itself treats the testicles as a visible limb, at least according to Rabbi.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then turns to the tongue. Rabbi said {\i af ha-sirus} and did not mention the tongue; Ben Azzai said {\i af ha-lashon}. Does this mean Rabbi would not free a slave whose tongue was cut off? The {\i gemara} answers: {\i Rabbi omer sirus ve-lo mibaye lashon}\u8212-when Rabbi says castration, cutting the tongue goes without saying, since the tongue is at least as visible as the testicles if not more so; you can actually see the tongue, whereas you never fully see the testicles themselves. Ben Azzai, by saying {\i af ha-lashon}, is disagreeing not with Rabbi but with the {\i tana kamma}\u8217's list of twenty-four\u8212-he is adding the tongue to that list. The testicles, in Ben Azzai\u8217's view, do not qualify.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This produces an initially puzzling reading of the text, since Ben Azzai\u8217's statement comes after Rabbi\u8217's and uses the word {\i af}, \u8220"even.\u8221" One might expect that Ben Azzai is adding to Rabbi\u8217's position, making him more inclusive. But the {\i gemara} reads it the opposite way: Ben Azzai is more restrictive than Rabbi (excluding the testicles), and {\i af ha-lashon} adds the tongue not to Rabbi\u8217's enumeration but to the {\i tana kamma}\u8217's original twenty-four. The reason Ben Azzai was appended to the end rather than placed before Rabbi in the sequence is a principle about oral texts: {\i tana samakh le-de-Rebbe ve-kav\u8217'ah, ve-tani mishna lo zaz mi-mkomo}\u8212-once the text was learned and memorized in a certain form, it was not rearranged even when a later opinion needed to be integrated. Ben Azzai\u8217's view was tagged onto the end even though the resulting {\i af} is somewhat confusing.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Tongue Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tumah Context\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having established that the tongue is treated as an external limb for purposes of freeing the slave\u8212-at least according to Rabbi\u8212-the {\i gemara} now examines whether the tongue is consistently classified as external or whether its classification shifts depending on the legal context. Ulla states: {\i hakol modim bi-lashon le-inyan tum\u8217'ah de-galuye hu etzel sheretz}\u8212-everyone agrees that for purposes of contracting {\i tum\u8217'ah} through contact with a {\i tamei} object, the tongue is considered external. The relevant principle is that {\i tum\u8217'at maga}, becoming {\i tamei} through contact, requires touching with an external part of the body. If one were to hold a piece of {\i neveilah} entirely inside one\u8217's mouth without any external surface touching it, one would not become {\i tamei}. But if the tongue touches the {\i tamei} object, that counts. The reason: {\i asher yiga bo amra rachmana}\u8212-the verse speaks of touching ({\i nega\u8217'}), and the tongue is a natural instrument of touching. From the perspective of {\i nega\u8217'}, the tongue is external\u8212-it is how one touches things.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tvilah Context\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 By contrast, Ulla continues, for purposes of {\i tvilah} in the {\i mikvah}, everyone agrees that the tongue is considered internal ({\i ke-tamun dami}) and does not require direct contact with {\i mikvah} water. The reason: {\i ve-rachatz besaro ba-mayim amra rachmana}\u8212-the verse says one should wash one\u8217's {\i basar}, one\u8217's flesh, with water. Just as one\u8217's flesh is external ({\i mibar}), so everything that requires water contact must be external. The tongue, kept inside the mouth and not exposed the way the skin is, does not fall into that category.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The conceptual distinction emerges clearly: when the question is one of {\i nega\u8217'}\u8212-an act of touching\u8212-the tongue qualifies as external, because touching is something done with a tongue. When the question is one of the body\u8217's surface being contacted by water, analogous to skin, the tongue does not qualify. Rashi adds an additional practical dimension: one cannot expose the entire surface of one\u8217's tongue to water even if one tried, whereas any act of touching can be performed with any part of the tongue. This further explains why the two contexts yield opposite conclusions.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Maidservant Story\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A challenge arises: does Rabbi truly hold that the tongue is internal for purposes of {\i tvilah}? After all, the {\i gemara} recounts: {\i ma\u8217'aseh be-shifchato shel beis Rebbe}\u8212-there was an incident with a maidservant in Rabbi\u8217's household who immersed in the {\i mikvah}, and when she emerged there was a bone ({\i etzem}) between her teeth. Rabbi required her to immerse again. This seems to imply that the interior of the mouth must be free of interposition\u8212-that the {\i mikvah} water must in some sense be capable of reaching inside the mouth.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Natural Cavities\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} resolves this: {\i ne-hi de-vi\u8217'at mayim lo be\u8217'inan, makom ha-rauy la-vo bo mayim be\u8217'inan}\u8212-we do not require that {\i mikvah} water actually enter the oral cavity, but the cavity must be fit for water to enter ({\i rauy la-vo}), free of any {\i chatzitzah} that would impede it. This is the principle of {\i beit ha-starim}: concealed orifices and natural cavities\u8212-the inside of the ear, beneath the eyelid, the oral cavity, and similarly {\i beit ha-kamatin}, the natural folds of the body\u8212-do not require the water to actually make contact, but they must not be blocked. The bone between the maidservant\u8217's teeth was a {\i chatzitzah} preventing the cavity from being {\i rauy} for water contact, which is why Rabbi required a second immersion.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Potential vs.\u160 ?Actual\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This leads to the broader question: when does {\i halakha} require that an act actually occur, and when is it sufficient that the act could occur? The parallel principle is that of Rabbi Zeira: {\i kol ha-rauy le-vilah ein vilah me\u8217'akevet bo}\u8212-anything capable of being mixed with oil (in the context of a {\i mincha} offering) does not require that the mixing actually occur; but anything incapable of being mixed requires division into separate offerings. The {\i gemara} applies this logic to {\i tvilah}: the {\i beit ha-starim} need not have water contact it, but it must be capable of being contacted.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot raises the pointed question: how do we know that \u8220"fit for\u8221" is sufficient rather than requiring the act itself? By analogy, one might argue that a {\i lulav} only needs to be \u8220"fit for\u8221" being held, without actually holding it\u8212-which is absurd. Tosafot answers that the distinction lies in how the Torah frames the obligation. Where the Torah says {\i you shall do} an act, the act must occur. Where the Torah describes the {\i nature} of an object\u8212-as when {\i mincha} is described as {\i balul ba-shemen}, mixed with oil, rather than saying \u8220"you must mix it\u8221"\u8212-it is sufficient that the object be of that character in potentia. Regarding {\i beit ha-starim} in {\i tvilah}, the tension between {\i kol besaro} (which seems to include even hidden areas) and the comparison to the hand ({\i ma yadav mi-bora\u8217'ei, af kol mi-bora\u8217'ei}, requiring external surfaces) is resolved by the intermediate category: the {\i beit ha-starim} must be {\i rauy} for water but need not actually be contacted.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Some {\i rishonim} find Tosafot\u8217's explanation insufficient, noting that the obligation of {\i tvilah} is phrased as an act ({\i ve-rachatz}), not a description of character. They therefore hold that the requirement of {\i beit ha-starim} being {\i rauy} is only {\i de-rabbanan}. This has significant practical implications: Rav Ovadia Yosef\u8217's leniencies in {\i sheilos} involving blocked orifices often rest on this position, allowing greater latitude when combined with other {\i shitot}, even though as a general rule a {\i de-rabbanan} concern is not automatically waived even {\i be-di\u8217'eved}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Testicles Visibility\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Blemish Verse\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now returns to the question of whether crushing the testicles constitutes a visible {\i mum}, framing it as a dispute among {\i tanna\u8217'im}. The verse in {\i Vayikra} enumerates blemishes that disqualify an animal: {\i ma\u8217'uach, ve-katus, ve-nasuk, ve-karus}\u8212-crushed, bruised, severed, or cut. According to Rabbi Yehuda, all of these refer to injuries of the testicles ({\i kulam be-veitzim}).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Testicle Blemish\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Eliezer ben Yaakov disagrees: {\i kulam be-gid}\u8212-all of these terms refer to the penis, not the testicles. For Rabbi Eliezer ben Yaakov, an injury to the testicles does not constitute a visible blemish because the testicles themselves are not a visible limb in the relevant sense. While the scrotum is external, the testicles inside it are not exposed in the way that the penis is, and therefore injuries to them\u8212-not involving removal of the scrotum itself\u8212-do not register as blemishes on a visible limb.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yosi\u8217's Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yosi takes a middle position: {\i ma\u8217'uach ve-katus af be-veitzim}\u8212-crushing and bruising apply even to the testicles, but {\i nasuk ve-karus be-gid, be-veitzim lo}\u8212-severance and cutting apply only to the penis, not to the testicles. At first glance this seems backward: severance ({\i nasuk}) and cutting ({\i karus}) seem more dramatic and visible than mere crushing. The resolution, as Rashi explains ({\i de-minkar tefei}), is that crushing produces a visible change in the shape and appearance of the scrotal area, while internal severance of the testicles within the scrotum may not produce any external sign at all. Rabbi Yosi is thus distinguishing not by severity of the injury but by whether the injury is externally discernible. The crushing is visible in its effect on the outside; the detachment is internal and therefore not a blemish on a visible limb.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This raises an important conceptual point about the two legal contexts. For the {\i eved}, the question is whether the {\i limb} is external and visible\u8212-if the testicles qualify as a visible limb, then any significant injury to them frees the slave. For the animal {\i mum}, the question may instead be whether the {\i injury} is externally visible. These are not the same question, and one must be careful about directly equating the {\i tanna\u8217'im}\u8217's positions in one context with the other.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Animals Kinyan\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishna Modes\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having concluded the {\i sugyot} relating to human beings as objects of acquisition\u8212-whether as wives through {\i ishut} or as {\i eved kena\u8217'ani} who, though fully property in some respects, retain a human identity\u8212-the {\i gemara} transitions to the acquisition of animals. The {\i mishnah} teaches: {\i behema gasa nikneit be-mesira}, a large animal such as a cow or horse is acquired by {\i mesira}, the handing over of the reins or lead; {\i ve-ha-daka be-hagbaha}, a small animal such as a sheep or goat is acquired by lifting ({\i hagbaha}). This is the position of Rabbi Meir.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hagbaha Dispute\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Eliezer and the {\i chachamim} disagree: {\i behema daka nikneit be-meshicha}\u8212-a small animal is acquired by {\i meshicha}, pulling or leading, not by {\i hagbaha}. This sets up a central analytical question about the hierarchy of {\i kinyanim}. {\i Hagbaha} is considered the most powerful form of acquisition: lifting an object demonstrates total physical mastery and control. {\i Meshicha}\u8212-causing the animal to move\u8212-is a lesser act. If {\i hagbaha} is possible with a small animal (one can lift a sheep), why does the {\i mishnah} according to Rabbi Meir specify {\i hagbaha}, and why do the {\i chachamim} prefer {\i meshicha}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The key principle is one of hierarchy. If {\i hagbaha} is specified, it means {\i hagbaha} and not the weaker {\i meshicha}. If {\i meshicha} is specified, it means even {\i meshicha} suffices. The {\i chachamim}\u8217's position that {\i meshicha} works for small animals implies that {\i meshicha} is sufficient even though {\i hagbaha} would certainly also work. The debate is whether we demand the superior {\i kinyan} when it is available, or whether a lesser one is adequate.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rava\u8217's Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shmuel Challenge\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} reports that Rava gave a public {\i drasha} in the town of Kimchunia stating that a large animal is acquired by {\i meshicha}. Shmuel encountered Rav\u8217's students and challenged this: did Rav really say {\i meshicha} for a large animal? The {\i mishnah} says {\i mesira}, and Rav himself was known to rule {\i mesira}. What is Rava doing?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Alignment Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} answers that Rava had retracted his earlier position. He now held {\i meshicha}, aligning himself with the {\i chachamim} as quoted in a {\i baraita}: {\i ve-chachamim omrim zo va-zo nikneit be-meshicha}\u8212-the {\i chachamim} rule that both large and small animals are acquired by {\i meshicha}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shimon Opinion\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Shimon, however, takes the most demanding position: {\i zo va-zo be-hagbaha}\u8212-both large and small animals require {\i hagbaha}. Since {\i hagbaha} is at the top of the hierarchy, those who specify it mean {\i only} hagbaha. The practical difficulty this creates\u8212-particularly for animals that cannot be lifted\u8212-will be addressed shortly.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Animals Kinyan (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rashi\u8217's Hierarchy\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rashi\u8217's analysis of the {\i mishnah}\u8217's term {\i mesira} illuminates the broader debate about hierarchy. Rashi explains that {\i mesira} means specifically the handing over of reins or a saddle, and that {\i meshicha} would {\i not} constitute a valid {\i kinyan} for a large animal in this view: {\i aval meshicha lo maknya de-ein darkah be-chach lilech lefanav}. The reason Rashi gives is striking: large animals do not walk in front of you\u8212-they are led, with the owner handing control forward. {\i Meshicha}, in Rashi\u8217's understanding, means driving the animal from behind, which is not how large animals normally move. Accordingly, {\i mesira} is not merely a weaker form of {\i meshicha}; it is the uniquely appropriate act for large animals, while {\i meshicha}\u8212-pushing from behind\u8212-is the natural mode for small animals like sheep and goats.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot disagrees with this hierarchy. For Tosafot, {\i meshicha} is clearly the stronger act\u8212-you do more than merely hand over a lead, you actually cause the animal to move. {\i Mesira} without movement is a lesser demonstration of control. The debate between Rashi and Tosafot thus goes to the heart of how we define the hierarchy: Rashi holds {\i mesira} is higher for large animals because it is the appropriate and meaningful act; Tosafot holds {\i meshicha} is always higher because movement demonstrates greater mastery. On Tosafot\u8217's reading, when the {\i mishnah} says {\i mesira} for large animals, it means even {\i mesira} suffices\u8212-{\i meshicha} would certainly also work. On Rashi\u8217's reading, {\i mesira} means {\i mesira} and not {\i meshicha}, because the latter is simply not an appropriate act for a large animal.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is worth noting that these positions need not entail a strict universal hierarchy. It is possible to hold that {\i mesira} is appropriate exclusively for large animals and {\i meshicha} exclusively for small ones, with neither strictly including the other\u8212-mutual exclusivity based on contextual appropriateness rather than rank. But the {\i gemara}\u8217's general framework tends toward hierarchy, and the debate among {\i rishonim} largely proceeds on that assumption.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Elephant Hagbaha\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Yosef Query\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The difficulty with Rabbi Shimon\u8217's position\u8212-requiring {\i hagbaha} for all animals\u8212-becomes acute when Rav Yosef challenges it: {\i ela me\u8217'ata, pil le-Rabbi Shimon be-meh yikneh?}\u8212-according to Rabbi Shimon, how would one acquire an elephant? No human being can lift an elephant, so {\i hagbaha} seems practically impossible.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Abaye Solutions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye offers two answers. First, {\i be-chalifin}\u8212-one could use {\i kinyan sudar}, the symbolic exchange of a kerchief, to effect the acquisition. Second, {\i i nami be-schirut mekomo}\u8212-one could rent the space the elephant occupies, a mechanism that somehow effects acquisition of the elephant itself. The second answer envisions something like placing stools or bundles beneath the elephant so that it stands upon objects belonging to the purchaser.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Vessels & Bundles\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} explores whether the elephant standing on the buyer\u8217's objects constitutes a valid {\i kinyan}. This would require the buyer\u8217's vessels ({\i kelim}) to effect acquisition even while located in the seller\u8217's domain. The {\i gemara} answers that the case is one where the elephant is standing in a {\i simta}, a public alleyway, where a buyer\u8217's {\i kelim} are recognized as capable of effecting acquisition. Alternatively, the answer involves {\i chavilei zemorot}\u8212-bundles of vines placed for the elephant to step on.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rise vs.\u160 ?Lift\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rashi and Tosafot dispute what the bundle answer accomplishes. For Rashi, placing bundles under the elephant so that it steps on them and rises three {\i tefachim} off the ground constitutes a form of {\i hagbaha}: the buyer caused the animal to rise, even without physically lifting it. Rashi apparently holds that {\i hagbaha} does not require the buyer to personally hoist the animal, only that the animal is elevated by the buyer\u8217's act. Tosafot prefers a different reading: the bundles are elephant food placed high up, causing the elephant to rear up on its hind legs to reach the food. This too is a form of induced rising. However, Tosafot\u8217's answer raises its own difficulties: whether an elephant can meaningfully \u8220"jump,\u8221" and more fundamentally, whether causing an animal to raise part of its body is equivalent to the {\i kinyan} of {\i hagbaha}. The conceptual problem is the same in both accounts\u8212-did the buyer perform {\i hagbaha}, or merely cause the animal to move in a way that resembles it?\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Kinyan Hierarchy\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 One vs.\u160 ?Multiple\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The concluding discussion raises a question that cuts across the entire preceding analysis: when multiple {\i kinyanim} are valid for a given transaction, does the existence of a superior {\i kinyan} preclude using an inferior one? Or do multiple valid modes coexist, any one of which may be used at will?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kinyan Mitzvot\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Rashbam} articulates the challenge with characteristic sharpness: {\i ve-chi kinyanim mitzvot hen she-ein la\u8217'asot ela min ha-muvchar?}\u8212-are {\i kinyanim} like {\i mitzvot}, such that one is obligated to perform only the most distinguished option? The question is deliberately provocative. When we speak of performing a {\i mitzva} {\i min ha-muvchar}, from the finest available, we are talking about a religious obligation where the quality of the act reflects on one\u8217's service of God. But a {\i kinyan} is a legal mechanism for transferring ownership\u8212-a tool, not a religious duty in that sense. To say one must use the superior {\i kinyan} when available seems to misunderstand the nature of the institution. This striking formulation of the Rashbam is left for further discussion.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Kiddushin 26\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Kinyanim Hierarchy\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Jumping Elephants\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Two matters from the previous session warrant brief revisitation before proceeding to the {\i mishna}. The first concerns {\i Tosafos}\u8217' reading of the {\i Gemara}\u8217's discussion of bundles of twigs. According to {\i Rashi}, the case involves walking on top of the twigs, such that one gets the elephant to move off the ground \u8212- a form of {\i hagbaha} that, notably, does not require actually lifting the object clear of the surface. {\i Tosafos}, by contrast, understands the case differently: one holds a twig with a leaf aloft and the elephant leaps for it. The difficulty, of course, is that elephants do not jump \u8212- a point confirmed by those who follow the writings of Natan Slifkin on the natural world. Whether {\i Tosafos} had ever observed an actual elephant is itself an open question. One proposed defense of {\i Tosafos} \u8212- that the discussion concerns an elephant on a trampoline \u8212- may be taken for what it is worth.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hierarchy Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The second point concerns the hierarchy of {\i kinyanim}. When the {\i Gemara} states that a particular {\i kinyan} works for a given type of object, the implication is that lower-ranked {\i kinyanim} do not. So when one says {\i hagbaha} works for {\i behemah dakah}, the point is that {\i meshichah} and {\i mesirah} do not. Understanding which {\i kinyan} is considered stronger is therefore crucial. When the {\i Gemara} states that {\i mesirah} works for {\i behemah gassah}, is that an exclusive statement \u8212- {\i mesirah} and not {\i meshichah} \u8212- as {\i Rashi} reads it? Or does it mean that even {\i mesirah} suffices, and certainly {\i meshichah}? The direction of the hierarchy matters. Once {\i halacha} accepts that multiple {\i kinyanim} work in a given case, one may in principle use whichever is available.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Tam\u8217's Objection\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A position attributed to the {\i Rashbam}, however, goes further: in certain cases where both {\i mesirah} and {\i meshichah} are available, one is obligated to perform {\i meshichah} \u8212- the stronger {\i kinyan} \u8212- and doing the weaker one would be insufficient. {\i Tosafos} records {\i Rabbeinu Tam}\u8217's sharply worded objection to this. The relevant passage in {\i Tosafos} begins approximately ten lines before the end of that {\i Tosafos}, at the word {\i l\u8217'miknayei}. The {\i Tosafos} reads: since {\i meshichah}, which is the more distinguished {\i kinyan}, is available in a place such as a {\i simta}, it would be inappropriate \u8212- {\i lav orach d\u8217'ara} \u8212- to effect the acquisition through {\i mesirah}, which is the inferior {\i kinyan}. Then {\i Rabbeinu Tam} poses his famous challenge: {\i v\u8217'chi mitzvos hein} \u8212- are {\i meshichah} and {\i mesirah} commandments, such that performing one is {\i mevatlo} the other, and one is required to perform the {\i mitzvah min hamuvchar}, the most distinguished option? The point is incisive: {\i kinyanim} are not ritual acts whose relative merit carries normative weight. If both are ruled valid in a given case, why should one be compelled to choose the stronger one? The act either transfers ownership or it does not.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ownership Demonstration\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The conceptual logic underlying the {\i Rashbam}\u8217's position, however, is not without merit. The underlying purpose of a {\i kinyan} is to demonstrate {\i ba\u8217'alus} \u8212- ownership. If a stronger demonstration of ownership is available, then performing the weaker one in its presence may not constitute a meaningful act of ownership at all. {\i Mesirah}, according to {\i Tosafos}, is indeed a relatively weak form of demonstration: merely holding the reins is not the same as drawing an object toward oneself or lifting it. When the stronger forms of demonstrating ownership are available, {\i mesirah} in that context may be insufficiently meaningful \u8212- not because of any ritual hierarchy, but because relative to what could have been done, it does not signify genuine control. In isolation, {\i mesirah} can work; when better options are present, the act may not rise to the level of a meaningful expression of ownership.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This gives rise to a broader conceptual point. One might think of ownership as binary \u8212- either the transfer occurred or it did not \u8212- like a switch being flipped. But the {\i Rashbam}\u8217's view suggests a more graduated picture, where the meaningfulness of the act of acquisition is itself context-dependent. Whether the {\i halacha} follows this graduated view or the binary one is part of what the debate between {\i Rabbeinu Tam} and the {\i Rashbam} is really about.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Land with Money\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Achrayut Defined\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now moves from {\i metaltelin} \u8212- from animals in particular \u8212- to land. The {\i mishna} at the top of 26b reads: {\i nechasim sheyesh lahem achrayut niknim b\u8217'kesef b\u8217'shtar uv\u8217'chazakah} \u8212- property that bears liens is acquired through money, through a document, and through {\i chazakah}. The phrase {\i nechasim sheyesh lahem achrayut} is the older, more antiquated formulation for real estate. In later usage this becomes simply {\i karka}, but this {\i mishna} employs the earlier idiom, a detail that reflects the age and tightness of this collection of {\i mishnayot}. As noted when beginning {\i Masechet Kiddushin}, this series of {\i mishnayot} \u8212- organized around a single theme of degrees of {\i kinyanim}, moving through women, slaves, Hebrew slaves, {\i eved kena\u8217'ani}, and various categories of property \u8212- represents a very early and tightly organized collection.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Nechasim sheyesh lahem achrayut} means land and real estate because real property serves as collateral: when a loan is made, a lien attaches to the lender\u8217's land. The {\i mishna} states that such property is acquired by {\i kesef}, {\i shtar}, and {\i chazakah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Three Methods\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 There is, incidentally, a significant debate regarding what {\i kesef} means in this context. Normally one would assume it refers to payment of the full purchase price. But the parallel to {\i kiddushei isha} \u8212- where {\i kesef} is fulfilled symbolically by a single {\i perutah} \u8212- raises the question of whether paying the first {\i perutah} toward the purchase of land itself constitutes {\i kinyan kesef}. Two conceptual frameworks present themselves. One could say the full payment is technically required, but as soon as a single dollar is paid, we treat it as though the remainder has been paid and the seller has lent the buyer the balance \u8212- so the outstanding amount is a loan, not an unpaid purchase price. Alternatively, one could say that any partial payment effects the {\i kinyan} immediately. The analogous discussion in {\i Bava Kamma} regarding a merchant who has not yet received full payment \u8212- whether such an outstanding debt is a {\i halva\u8217'ah} subject to {\i shemitah} annulment or an unpaid commercial obligation \u8212- is equally relevant. The fundamental question here is whether the model of {\i kiddushei isha}, where the first {\i perutah} is sufficient, teaches that the same symbolic sufficiency applies to land, or whether land is fundamentally different and requires the full purchase price.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} continues: {\i u\u8217'v\u8217'shtar} \u8212- with a document, which as the {\i Gemara} will explain means the seller writes {\i sadi mechurah lach} and hands over the document. And {\i uv\u8217'chazakah} \u8212- through taking possession, which will be discussed in its own right. The reason {\i karka} requires these distinct forms of {\i kinyan} rather than the intuitive forms used for {\i metaltelin} is conceptually clear. {\i Metaltelin} is movable: one can draw it toward oneself, lift it, carry it into one\u8217's home. Ownership of chattels is demonstrated by physical control \u8212- the object is on one\u8217's person, in one\u8217's house, in one\u8217's domain. The principle {\i kol mah shetachat yad adam harei hu shelo} \u8212- whatever is under a person\u8217's control is presumed to be his \u8212- explains why {\i meshichah}, {\i hagbaha}, {\i yad}, and {\i chatzer} all work for movables: they all express meaningful physical control.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Land, by contrast, cannot be lifted or drawn toward oneself. Simply standing on someone else\u8217's land does not demonstrate ownership. The {\i kinyanim} for land therefore focus on the act of exchange itself \u8212- {\i kesef} is transactional, {\i shtar} is transactional, and {\i chazakah}, while touching on something more like control, is still a momentary act rather than an ongoing state of possession. This is why the law of land requires these particular modes.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} goes on: {\i nechasim she\u8217'ein lahem achrayut ein niknin ela b\u8217'meshichah} \u8212- chattel that bears no liens is acquired only through {\i meshichah}. Though {\i hagbaha} would obviously also work, and {\i mesirah} was applicable in certain cases involving animals, the general rule for inanimate movables is {\i meshichah}. Then: {\i nechasim she\u8217'ein lahem achrayut niknin im nechasim sheyesh lahem achrayut b\u8217'kesef b\u8217'shtar uv\u8217'chazakah} \u8212- chattel can be acquired together with land through the same modes that acquire land. The {\i Gemara} will address whether the chattel must be physically present on the land for this to work.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} then adds a further dimension: {\i v\u8217'zokakin et hanechasim sheyesh lahem achrayut l\u8217'hishava aleihem} \u8212- the chattel can drag the land into the orbit of an oath. Normally, no oath is taken regarding land; oaths are taken only regarding chattel \u8212- as in {\i modeh b\u8217'miktzat} or disputes about objects entrusted for safekeeping. But through the mechanism of {\i gilgul shvuah}, once someone is obligated to take an oath regarding chattel, that oath can roll forward and encompass land as well. So the relationship between land and chattel is bidirectional: land can pull chattel along in the act of acquisition, and chattel can pull land along into the domain of oaths.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Money Source\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} asks: {\i b\u8217'kesef m\u8217'nalan} \u8212- what is the source that land is acquired with money? {\i Chizkiyah} answers: {\i amar kra sadot b\u8217'kesef yiknu} \u8212- the verse says fields shall be purchased with money, a passage in {\i Yirmiyah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} immediately challenges: perhaps the verse requires both money {\i and} a document, since the verse continues with instructions to write a document, seal it, and have witnesses sign it. The {\i Gemara} responds with a grammatical argument: if the verb {\i yiknu} appeared at the end of the verse, one might read money and document together as a joint condition. But since {\i yiknu} appears immediately after {\i b\u8217'kesef} \u8212- {\i sadot b\u8217'kesef yiknu} \u8212- the acquisition is effected by the money alone. The subsequent instructions to write a document are, in the context of that verse, purely evidentiary: {\i shtar re\u8217'ayah b\u8217'alma hu} \u8212- the document is merely for proof. The document can, of course, also serve as the operative mode of acquisition in other circumstances, but in this verse it is not what effects the transaction.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A natural question arises: why not derive the rule from the {\i pesukim} regarding {\i Avraham}\u8217's purchase of the cave of {\i Machpeilah}, where money is also paid for land? {\i Tosafos} explains that the {\i Gemara} prefers not to draw from that episode because it involves a transaction with a non-Jew, and the rules of {\i kinyan} for non-Jews may differ.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav\u8217's Condition\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rav} now introduces an important qualification: {\i lo shanu ela b\u8217'makom she\u8217'ein kotvin shtar} \u8212- the rule that money alone suffices applies only where it is not the local practice to write a document. In a place where writing a document invariably accompanies every land transaction \u8212- {\i makom shekotvin shtar} \u8212- money alone does not effect the {\i kinyan}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rashi} explains this as a {\i gemar da\u8217'at} issue. If the universal practice in a given locale is that a document of sale always accompanies the transaction, then even if technically money could suffice, the parties\u8217' own expectations define when ownership is finalized. Since the seller does not consider the transaction complete until the document is transferred, there is no {\i gemar da\u8217'at} on the seller\u8217's part until that occurs \u8212- {\i lo samcha da\u8217'atei d\u8217'lokach ad d\u8217'nakat shtra}. One interpretation of this principle focuses on cases where the practice recognizes that the transaction is simply not concluded without a document, making it a formal condition. Another reading, suggested here, goes further: even if the document functions technically as {\i re\u8217'ayah}, if registering the transfer is so universally expected that no one considers ownership to have moved until that step is completed, then the {\i gemar da\u8217'at} is correspondingly delayed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} adds: {\i v\u8217'ei parish parish} \u8212- but if the parties explicitly state that the money alone will effect the {\i kinyan}, it works. Money is fundamentally valid; the default expectation in a document-writing locale merely shifts the assumption, and that assumption can be overridden by explicit agreement.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Idi Bar Avin\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This leads to the practice of {\i Rav Idi bar Avin}: whenever he purchased land, he would declare \u8212- {\i amar ei baina b\u8217'chaspa ikni, ei baina b\u8217'shtar ikni} \u8212- \u8220"if I choose, the money shall effect the acquisition; if I choose, the document shall effect the acquisition.\u8221" His reasoning was straightforwardly strategic. If the money changed hands first (as is typically more convenient than drafting a document), he could retroactively declare that the {\i kinyan} took effect at the moment of payment \u8212- thereby locking in the transaction and preventing the seller from retracting. Alternatively, if he wished to preserve his own right to withdraw after the money had been paid but before the document was drafted, he could declare that the document would be the operative {\i kinyan}, leaving him free to retract in the interim.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This practice reflects a broader principle about {\i kinyanim}: they are not rigid ritual acts but instruments of {\i gemar da\u8217'at}, and the parties\u8217' articulated intentions can shape which act carries operative weight. This is consistent with the concept of {\i kinyan situmta} \u8212- where the accepted commercial practice of a given community can itself constitute a valid {\i kinyan}, provided genuine {\i gemirus da\u8217'at} is present. In the diamond trade, for instance, the declaration {\i mazel u\u8217'bracha} is understood as the operative signal that the deal is concluded, enforceable before a {\i beit din}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What is perhaps the larger {\i chidush} of {\i Rav Idi bar Avin}\u8217's practice is the idea that one party can reserve the right to determine retroactively which act constituted the {\i maaseh kinyan}. This is a more unusual claim than simply preserving a right of retraction, since it concerns the characterization of which act, among acts that have already occurred, carries the legal weight of the transfer.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Land with Document\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Document Source\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} turns to the second mode: {\i u\u8217'v\u8217'shtar m\u8217'nalan} \u8212- what is the source for acquisition of land by document? One might initially suggest deriving it from the instruction in the same verse of {\i Yirmiyah} to write a document and seal it \u8212- but the {\i Gemara} already established that in that context the document is merely evidentiary. The operative source is instead a different verse in the same passage: {\i va\u8217'ekach et sefer ha\u8217'miknah} \u8212- \u8220"and I took the book of purchase.\u8221" The document is called the {\i sefer ha\u8217'miknah} \u8212- the instrument of acquisition \u8212- indicating that the document itself effects the transaction.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shmuel\u8217's Limit\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Shmuel} now introduces a parallel limitation to {\i Rav}\u8217's ruling about money: {\i lo shanu ela b\u8217'shtar matanah} \u8212- the rule that a document alone suffices applies only to a gift. {\i Aval b\u8217'mecher lo kanah ad sheyiten damim} \u8212- but in a sale, one does not acquire until money actually changes hands.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The reasoning again tracks {\i gemar da\u8217'at}. A gift, by definition, involves no expectation of payment; the document alone fully expresses the donor\u8217's finalized intention. In a sale, however, the seller expects payment, and without receiving it, has not achieved the settled state of mind \u8212- the {\i gemar da\u8217'at} \u8212- that the transaction is concluded. The document alone, in the context of a sale, does not suffice.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hamnuna Challenge\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rav Hamnuna} challenges {\i Shmuel} from a {\i braita}: {\i b\u8217'shtar keitzad} \u8212- how is the acquisition by document accomplished? One writes on paper or even on a shard of pottery \u8212- {\i al haniyar o al hacheres} \u8212- the phrase {\i sadi mechurah lach} or {\i sadi netunah lach}, \u8220"my field is sold to you\u8221" or \u8220"my field is given to you,\u8221" and {\i harei zu mechurah u\u8217'netunah} \u8212- it is thereby sold or given. Crucially, the {\i braita} treats {\i mechira} and {\i matanah} together, implying that a document alone works even in a sale \u8212- directly contradicting {\i Shmuel}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Desperate Seller\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rav Hamnuna} himself resolves the challenge he raised: {\i b\u8217'mocher sadehu mipnei ra\u8217'atah} \u8212- the {\i braita} addresses the special case of someone who is desperate to sell his field because it is worthless or has been on the market for a very long time. In such a case, the seller has {\i gemar da\u8217'at} even without receiving payment \u8212- he is sufficiently eager to conclude the transaction that the document alone suffices. In the standard case, where the seller\u8217's field is desirable and commands a fair price, the seller is not reconciled to the transaction until he actually receives the money, and so the document alone is insufficient.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Ashi Reinterp.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rav Ashi} offers a different reading of the {\i braita} entirely. He reinterprets the two phrases \u8212- {\i sadi netunah lach} and {\i sadi mechurah lach} \u8212- not as two alternative scenarios but as a single transaction. The seller is giving a gift, but writes the language of a sale in the document. Why would a donor describe his gift as a sale? Because if a creditor later seizes the field, and it was documented as a gift, the recipient has no claim against the donor \u8212- easy come, easy go. But if the document describes it as a sale, then the recipient acquires the rights of a purchaser: should a creditor collect the field, the recipient can demand compensation from the donor. So the donor writes {\i sadi netunah lach sadi mechurah lach} \u8212- this is a gift, but I am granting you the standing of a purchaser so that you may collect from me if the field is seized. Under this reading, the {\i braita} is discussing a gift throughout, and is consistent with {\i Shmuel}\u8217's ruling that a document alone works for gifts but not for ordinary sales.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The upshot of this extended discussion is that in most practical cases, both {\i kesef} and {\i shtar} are required: {\i kesef} alone is insufficient where the practice is to write a document, and a {\i shtar} alone is insufficient in most sales where payment is expected. Each acts as the operative {\i kinyan}, but {\i gemar da\u8217'at} requires the other to accompany it in the typical case.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Document Concept\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A further conceptual point about the nature of a {\i shtar} deserves attention. One natural way to understand why a document effects a {\i kinyan} is that it creates {\i re\u8217'ayah} \u8212- written proof of ownership, and the ability to prove ownership effects the transfer. But the {\i braita} notes that the document can be written on a shard of pottery, which can be scraped and altered, and the {\i braita} does not require witnesses. A document lacking witnesses and written on an erasable surface is not reliable proof of anything. This points toward an alternative conceptual understanding: the {\i shtar} is not primarily a {\i re\u8217'ayah} but rather a written expression of {\i da\u8217'as} \u8212- a concretization of the seller\u8217's intent, the word made real. By writing {\i sadi mechurah lach} and handing over the written expression, the seller externalizes and crystallizes his {\i da\u8217'at} in a tangible act. It is not that the recipient can now prove ownership; it is that the act of writing and delivery itself constitutes the expression of finalized intent. This distinction \u8212- {\i shtar} as {\i re\u8217'ayah} versus {\i shtar} as a written expression of {\i da\u8217'as} \u8212- has significant implications for how one understands the entire institution of documentary {\i kinyanim} in {\i Gittin}, {\i Kiddushin}, and the transfer of property.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Possession (Chazaka)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Dwelling Source\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} asks: {\i ub\u8217'chazakah m\u8217'nalan} \u8212- what is the source for acquisition of land through {\i chazakah}? {\i Chizkiyah} cites two verses. The first: {\i ushvu b\u8217'areichem asher tafastem bah} \u8212- dwell in the cities that you have seized. The question is posed: {\i bameh tafastem} \u8212- in what manner did you seize them? And the answer is {\i b\u8217'yeshivah} \u8212- through dwelling in them. The second source, from the school of {\i Rabbi Yishmael}, reads: {\i virishtem osah v\u8217'yashavtem bah} \u8212- you shall inherit it and dwell in it. {\i Bameh yerishtem} \u8212- in what manner do you take possession? {\i B\u8217'yeshivah} \u8212- by dwelling.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kedusha Rishona\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This source carries historical significance beyond its legal function. The {\i Rambam} distinguishes between the first and second {\i kedushah} of {\i Eretz Yisrael}. {\i Kedushah rishona} was established {\i al yedei kibbush milchamah} \u8212- through military conquest, a forcible seizure in war. {\i Kedushah shniyah}, by contrast, was established when the returnees from Babylonian exile were granted permission by the Persian king to return; they did not conquer but simply settled in the cities. The {\i Rambam} characterizes the {\i kinyan} of {\i Eretz Yisrael} the second time as a {\i kinyan chazakah} \u8212- effected through the settling and dwelling in the land. This is precisely the mode derived from the verse cited by {\i Chizkiyah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The conceptual relationship between the verse\u8217's {\i yeshivah} \u8212- dwelling \u8212- and the specific acts of {\i chazakah} recognized by halacha (such as plowing, planting, or fencing) deserves further exploration, as dwelling over time suggests something different from the momentary act that {\i chazakah} typically denotes. That question will become clearer as the {\i Gemara} continues its discussion of what precisely constitutes an act of {\i chazakah} in the context of land acquisition.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Chattels Kinyan\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Meshicha Requirement\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} states: {\i she\u8217'nechasim she\u8217'ein lahem achrayut ein niknin ela b\u8217'meshichah} \u8212- chattels without liens are acquired only through {\i meshichah}. As noted, {\i hagbaha} always works as well; the statement of {\i meshichah} does not exclude {\i hagbaha}, which is universally valid. The focus here is on inanimate movables generally, as distinct from the earlier discussion of animals where {\i mesirah} was potentially relevant.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hand-to-Hand\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} asks: {\i m\u8217'nalan} \u8212- what is the source? The verse cited is: {\i v\u8217'chi timkeru mimkar l\u8217'amitecha o kano miyad amitecha} \u8212- when you sell a sale to your friend or purchase from the hand of your friend. Although the larger context of this verse in {\i Parashat Behar} concerns land \u8212- it is the {\i issur} of {\i ona\u8217'ah}, overcharging \u8212- {\i Chazal} understand it to refer to chattel, since the {\i issur} of {\i ona\u8217'ah} does not apply to land, only to movables. The verse describes the transfer as {\i miyad amitecha} \u8212- from the hand of your friend: {\i davar hanikna miyad l\u8217'yad} \u8212- something that transfers from hand to hand. This is the {\i meshichah}, the drawing of the object into one\u8217's possession, which constitutes the {\i kinyan} for chattels.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Yochanan\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} then notes the position of {\i Rabbi Yochanan} in {\i Bava Metzia}: {\i dvar Torah ma\u8217'os konos} \u8212- biblically, for chattels, it is money that effects acquisition, not physical taking of possession. On {\i Rabbi Yochanan}\u8217's view, {\i meshichah} for chattels is only rabbinic. The verse cited therefore cannot prove a biblical basis for {\i meshichah} on his account. The {\i Gemara} resolves this simply: {\i fein, tana takanta d\u8217'Rabbanan katani} \u8212- fine, then the {\i mishna} is teaching the rabbinic rule, and no biblical verse is needed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Risk and Control\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The conceptual reason underlying {\i Rabbi Yochanan}\u8217's ruling \u8212- and more broadly, why the law of {\i meshichah} developed for chattels at all \u8212- illuminates the fundamental difference between land and movables. Consider a case where one party pays money for a shipment of wheat, but before taking physical possession, the wheat is destroyed. Even if the seller is not at fault and even if some form of {\i shemirah} obligation exists, the buyer has no interest in the object remaining under the seller\u8217's control. The seller does not want to be holding on to the buyer\u8217's property, and the buyer does not want his property exposed to risk under someone else\u8217's watch. Therefore, for {\i Rabbi Yochanan}, the Rabbis instituted that until {\i meshichah} is performed, the transaction is not complete \u8212- precisely to prevent the situation where money has changed hands but the object remains at risk in the seller\u8217's domain.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Land is categorically different. It cannot burn down and run away. It is always there to be taken possession of. As soon as money changes hands for land, there is no concern that the seller is holding the buyer\u8217's property at risk, because the land is simply there. This is why land does not require {\i meshichah} and why the {\i kinyanim} for land focus on acts of exchange \u8212- {\i kesef}, {\i shtar} \u8212- rather than acts of physical control. {\i Chazakah} is the one mode for land that partakes somewhat of the idea of control, and it will be discussed further. But the central contrast is: for movables, being in physical control is the clearest demonstration of ownership, and being in someone else\u8217's physical control is positively problematic; for land, physical control in that sense is simply not available or applicable.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Partial Meshicha\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A practical question follows from this analysis: does one need to perform {\i meshichah} on every individual item of chattel being acquired, or does partial {\i meshichah} suffice? If ten bushels of wheat are being purchased, must the buyer physically draw each bushel? If all ten bushels are in a single container and the buyer performs {\i meshichah} on the container, he may well have acquired the whole. But if they are separate and the buyer performs {\i meshichah} on half of them, the situation is less clear. If the remaining half is subsequently destroyed before {\i meshichah} is performed on it, can the buyer claim it is not yet his \u8212- that he bears no responsibility for the loss? The answer, it seems, is yes: if genuine {\i meshichah} was performed on only part of the chattel, and before {\i meshichah} is performed on the remainder a loss occurs, the buyer may argue that the unpossessed portion remains at the seller\u8217's risk. A box or container that is moved as a unit likely transfers everything within it, but distinct items require their own act of {\i meshichah}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Kinyan Agav\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chattels w/ Land\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Walled Cities\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} states that chattel can be acquired {\i agav} \u8212- incidentally together with \u8212- land, using the same modes of {\i kesef}, {\i shtar}, and {\i chazakah} that acquire the land. The Gemara sources this from the verse: {\i v\u8217'yiten lahem avihem matanos} \u8212- and their father gave them gifts \u8212- {\i im arei metzuros b\u8217'Yehudah} \u8212- together with walled cities in Yehudah. The gifts came along with the cities: by acquiring the walled cities, the recipients acquired the gifts together with them.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Piled Requirement\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A major question is then raised: {\i be\u8217'inan tzuburin o lo} \u8212- must the chattel be physically piled on the land for {\i kinyan agav} to work? The conceptual stakes are clear. If the land and chattel are understood as a single package \u8212- like a furnished house \u8212- then taking possession of the land naturally extends to whatever is on it, since they form one bundle. If the chattel need not be on the land, then the {\i kinyan} of land is being treated as so weighty, so powerful an act of {\i gemar da\u8217'at}, that it can extend its reach to objects entirely elsewhere \u8212- which is conceptually more difficult to justify.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Agav vs.\u160 ?Chatzer\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Before addressing whether {\i tzivurin} is required, a prior question must be answered: if the chattel must be on the land anyway, why is {\i kinyan agav} needed at all? Why does the law not simply apply {\i kinyan chatzer} \u8212- the principle that taking possession of one\u8217's property acquires whatever is within it? The answer given by the {\i rishonim} is that {\i kinyan chatzer} requires a {\i chatzer mishtameres} \u8212- a protected, enclosed domain. A walled house or courtyard satisfies this; an open field of wheat does not. {\i Kinyan agav} therefore provides a mode of transferring chattel that is physically on unprotected land, a situation {\i kinyan chatzer} would not reach.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Yosef Source\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rav Yosef} introduces a {\i braita} of {\i Rabbi Akiva} to resolve whether {\i tzivurin} is required: {\i karka kol shehu chayevet b\u8217'peah} \u8212- even the smallest amount of land is subject to the obligation of {\i peah}. The {\i Yerushalmi} notes the practical minimum involves at least two stalks \u8212- one harvested and one left as {\i peah} \u8212- though even a single stalk with multiple ears might satisfy this if one ear is harvested and the rest left. The {\i braita} continues: even the smallest amount of land obligates {\i bikurim}, since the verse says {\i ha\u8217'adamah asher nasata li}. And then, crucially: {\i v\u8217'lichtov aleha pruzbul} \u8212- even the smallest amount of land owned by the borrower allows a {\i pruzbul} to be written.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i pruzbul}, {\i Hillel}\u8217's enactment, allows a creditor to collect a loan after {\i shemitah} by formally transferring the debt to the court. It is effective only when the borrower owns real property. One reason is that standard loans, which {\i Hillel} was trying to encourage, were typically secured by liens on land; {\i Hillel} therefore built his enactment around cases that mirror a standard secured loan. A second conceptual reason is that {\i shemitah}\u8217's annulment of debts operates on money that is \u8220"out there\u8221" and uncollected \u8212- {\i asher yihiye lecha es achicha tishmet yadecha}. If the borrower owns land on which the creditor holds a lien, the money is not quite so \u8220"out there\u8221" \u8212- it is, in a sense, already accessible, already to some degree within the creditor\u8217's reach. This places a land-secured debt on a conceptual continuum closer to already-collected funds, which {\i shemitah} cannot annul. It does not fully get there on its own \u8212- hence the need for the {\i pruzbul} \u8212- but the presence of land moves it in that direction. An oral, unsecured debt is furthest from this; a debt secured by a lien on land is closer. The {\i pruzbul} bridges the remaining gap.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i braita} then states: {\i v\u8217'liknos imah nechasim she\u8217'einam achrayus} \u8212- and with even the smallest amount of land one can transfer chattel {\i agav}. If {\i tzivurin} is required, this raises an immediate difficulty.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Needle Example\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The difficulty is pointed: {\i ve\u8217'amarta be\u8217'inan tzivurin, kolshehu l\u8217'mai chazi} \u8212- if chattel must be piled on the land, what can possibly be placed on a plot of land so small as to be {\i kolshehu}, the minimum? {\i Rav Shmuel bar Bisna} answered before {\i Rav Yosef}: {\i kegon she\u8217'na\u8217'atz bah machat} \u8212- for instance, a needle stuck vertically into the ground. Transfer a needle on a tiny plot of land. {\i Rav Yosef} responded sharply: {\i kavastan} \u8212- you are causing distress. Would the {\i Tanna} go to the trouble of establishing this rule merely to teach how to transfer needles? If the entire purpose of {\i kolshehu} land is for a needle, the legal principle being taught seems trivial.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Sheshet\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rav Sheshet} defended the position: {\i man leima lan d\u8217'lo tala bah marganita d\u8217'shavya alfa zuzei} \u8212- who says one did not hang a pearl worth a thousand {\i zuz} from that needle? The point is not the needle\u8217's intrinsic value but the legal principle: there is no minimum land requirement for {\i kinyan agav} beyond the minimal existence of {\i karka}; whatever the transferor wishes to pile on the land \u8212- even something dangling from a needle \u8212- may be transferred. The legal rule is significant regardless of whether the common case involves inexpensive objects.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Sick Man Story\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Man\u8217's Situation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Beit Sela\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Elazar} recounts a case: there was a man from Madon who was in Yerushalayim with a great deal of chattel and wished to transfer it all as a gift. The problem posed to him was that effecting {\i kinyanim} on large quantities of diverse chattel is practically daunting \u8212- performing {\i meshichah} on a hundred sheep and a hundred barrels individually would be extraordinarily burdensome. The solution proposed was {\i kinyan agav karka}: transfer the chattel through land. What did he do? {\i Halach v\u8217'lakach beit sela} \u8212- he went and acquired a {\i beit sela}. {\i Rashi} offers two explanations of the term: either a plot of land the size of a coin \u8212- that is, minimal \u8212- or land associated with the word {\i sela} meaning a rock, implying a pointed or irregular surface with negligible usable area. Near Yerushalayim, he declared that the northern portion of this plot would go to a particular recipient, and with it, a hundred sheep and a hundred barrels of wine. He then died, and the {\i chachamim} validated the transfer.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Beit Rova\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rav Yehudah} in the name of {\i Rav} relates a similar case. A man who was ill in Yerushalayim \u8212- the {\i Gemara} will note that this may mean he was a {\i shechiv mera}, in which case one might wonder why a formal {\i kinyan} was necessary at all, since the words of a {\i shechiv mera} themselves effect a transfer. The {\i Gemara} resolves this: either the case follows {\i Rabbi Eliezer}\u8217's view that even a {\i shechiv mera} requires a formal {\i kinyan}, or the man was not actually on his deathbed and was simply ill, such that the {\i halacha} of {\i shechiv mera} did not apply at all. Either way, a proper {\i kinyan} was needed. He too had much chattel and wished to give it as a gift. Told there was no straightforward remedy, he went and acquired a {\i beit rova} \u8212- a quarter-{\i kav} plot, measuring 104 and 6 square {\i amot}, perhaps approximately 200 by 200 feet. He then declared that a {\i tefach} by {\i tefach} of that land would go to a specific recipient, and with it, a hundred sheep and a hundred barrels of wine. He died, and the {\i chachamim} affirmed the transfer.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Piling Problem\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Both stories now create a difficulty for the {\i tzivurin} position. If chattel must be physically piled on the land, how can one pile a hundred sheep and a hundred barrels of wine on a {\i tefach} by {\i tefach}? The {\i Gemara} presses this: {\i v\u8217'ee amarta be\u8217'inan tzivurin, tefach al tefach l\u8217'mai chazi} \u8212- if piling is required, what is the point of a {\i tefach} by {\i tefach}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Money Resolution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} proposes: {\i hacha b\u8217'mai askinan b\u8217'damai} \u8212- the case must be dealing with money. He did not literally transfer a hundred sheep and a hundred barrels; rather, he piled the monetary equivalent of those assets on the small plot of land, and declared that the sum representing the value of the sheep and the wine would transfer to the recipient. This is admittedly a strained reading of the narrative, which explicitly mentions sheep and barrels. But the {\i Gemara} accepts it tentatively, noting it makes the {\i tzivurin} requirement coherent with the small plot described.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Why Land Necessary\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Alt. Methods\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} immediately presses further. If the narrative is really about money being piled on land, this raises the question of why {\i karka} was necessary at all. Were there not other ways to transfer the assets?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 No Chatlipin\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} asks: {\i nikninayhu lei b\u8217'chalipin} \u8212- why not use {\i chalipin}, a {\i kinyan sudar}? A {\i kinyan sudar} operates universally and is a quick and flexible mode of acquisition. The answer is that money cannot be acquired through {\i chalipin}. {\i Chalipin} involves the exchange of objects that have intrinsic value as objects; money, as a medium of exchange rather than an object of value in itself, is not subject to acquisition by {\i chalipin}. Women are similarly excluded. So if the underlying assets were money, {\i chalipin} was not available.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 No Meshicha\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} then asks: {\i nikninayhu lei b\u8217'meshichah} \u8212- why not have someone take the money through {\i meshichah} on behalf of the recipient? The answer is: {\i lo samcha da\u8217'atei} \u8212- the transferor did not feel confident doing so. Specifically: {\i savar shamit v\u8217'achil lehu} \u8212- he feared the third party who would physically take the money would simply keep it. If the recipient is not present to take physical possession himself, entrusting cash to a third party carries the risk that the intermediary will misappropriate it. So {\i meshichah} via a third party was not a realistic option.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 No Participation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} continues: if the issue is that the recipient is not present, why not have a third party stand in and do {\i chalipin} on the recipient\u8217's behalf \u8212- {\i v\u8217'nikninayhu lei agav acher}? The answer is {\i lo samcha da\u8217'atei}: the third parties were not willing to participate. In {\i chalipin}, the facilitating party must hand over an object of their own to initiate the exchange; no one volunteered to do so. Perhaps they were concerned about this dying man\u8217's estate and did not want to be drawn into the transaction; or perhaps they simply had no confidence in the process. Whatever the reason, no one was willing to lend their object to effectuate the {\i chalipin}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Final Answer\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} thus establishes that the fundamental reason {\i karka} was necessary is that the recipient was not present \u8212- {\i d\u8217'leisa l\u8217'mikabel matanah}. When the recipient is absent, {\i meshichah} cannot be performed directly, {\i chalipin} through a third party fails because no one will volunteer their object, and handing cash to an intermediary is too risky. {\i Karka}, however, can be transferred in ways that do not require the recipient\u8217's physical presence and do not depend on third-party cooperation in the same way. A {\i shtar}, for instance, can be written declaring the land given to the absent party and handed to another without the risk of the intermediary pocketing the document.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Zachin Principle\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The final resolution invokes the principle of {\i zachin l\u8217'adam shelo b\u8217'fanav} \u8212- one may acquire merit on behalf of another person even in their absence. Since receiving a gift is an unambiguous benefit \u8212- a {\i zechut} \u8212- for the recipient, a third party may accept it on his behalf without the recipient\u8217's explicit authorization. This principle underlies the viability of the {\i karka} transfer in these stories: even though the recipient was not present, the {\i chachamim} affirmed the transfer because the acquisition was a {\i zechut} for him and could therefore be effected without his presence. Whether this principle fully resolves all the questions raised \u8212- including the unresolved question of whether {\i tzivurin} is required \u8212- remains to be seen in the continuation of the {\i sugya}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Kiddushin 27\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Kinyan Agav Mechanics\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Agav Basics\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i daf} opens in the middle of a discussion carried over from 26b concerning {\i kinyan agav} \u8212- the mechanism by which one who acquires land simultaneously acquires movable property ({\i metaltelin}) through that act of acquisition. The animating question is whether the {\i metaltelin} must be physically present on the land itself ({\i tziburin betocha}) or whether the conceptual bundling of the two objects suffices even when they are elsewhere.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The logic behind requiring physical proximity is that the land serves as the {\i ikar} \u8212- the primary, weightier element \u8212- and the {\i metaltelin} are subordinate to it. Acquiring the land represents acquiring the whole package. The idea that {\i metaltelin} located off the property can be drawn along requires more explanation, since the physical unity that might seem to ground the mechanism is absent.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might ask: if the {\i metaltelin} are physically on the land, why is {\i kinyan agav} even needed? The answer is that {\i kinyan chatzer} \u8212- acquiring objects by virtue of their presence within one\u8217's property \u8212- does not operate in all circumstances. In particular, a courtyard that is not {\i mishtameres} (guarded or protected) cannot effect {\i kinyan chatzer}. The {\i kinyan agav} mechanism thus provides an alternative route. With that background established, the {\i Gemara} proceeds to test the {\i tziburin betocha} requirement through a series of cases.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabban Gamliel Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} (bottom of 26b) introduces a famous episode involving Rabban Gamliel and the elders who were traveling together by ship. Rabban Gamliel said to the elders: the {\i maaser} which I will eventually separate from my grain \u8212- currently in my granary \u8212- is hereby given to Yehoshua (Rabbi Yehoshua, who was a {\i Levi}), and its place is rented to him ({\i umkomo muskar lo}). In this way, by renting the space to Yehoshua, Rabban Gamliel transferred ownership of the {\i maaser} through a rental \u8212- that is, through {\i sechirut} \u8212- of the physical location where the grain sat.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The second clause of the episode is equally striking: another {\i maaser} that I will eventually separate \u8212- this one from {\i maaser ani}, the poor tithe distributed in the third year \u8212- is given to Rabbi Akiva, so that he ({\i kedei sheyizkeh bo l\u8217'aniyim}) may take possession of it on behalf of the poor, for Rabbi Akiva served as a {\i gabbai l\u8217'aniyim}, a communal charity trustee. And again: {\i umkomo muskar lo}, its place is rented to him.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} presupposes that both transfers operate through {\i kinyan agav} rather than {\i kinyan chatzer}, even though the {\i Gemara} does not spell out why {\i kinyan chatzer} is excluded. The significant word in both clauses is {\i mekomo} \u8212- \u8220"its place\u8221" \u8212- which seems to imply that the {\i metaltelin} must be precisely on the rented property for the {\i kinyan} to take effect. The {\i Gemara} therefore raises: {\i shema mina deba\u8217'inan tziburin bah} \u8212- this appears to prove that physical presence on the land is required.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} deflects this inference: {\i shani hatam ki heichi delo letarchinhu} \u8212- the reason Rabban Gamliel specified the very location where the grain already sat was not technical necessity but practical consideration. By renting them that specific space, the recipients would have the right to leave the grain there indefinitely and would feel no pressure to remove it. Had he rented them a different parcel, they might feel compelled to relocate the grain. So specifying {\i mekomo} was a courtesy, not a legal requirement, and the case does not constitute proof that {\i tziburin betocha} is required.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A striking feature of this passage, however, is what the {\i Gemara\u8217's} answer implicitly concedes: that {\i kinyan agav} functions even through {\i sechirut} \u8212- through rental. This is itself a major {\i chiddush}. One might have argued that {\i kinyan agav} requires full ownership of the land, since it is through the acquisition of land that the {\i metaltelin} are swept along. A rental is not full ownership. The {\i Rambam} describes {\i sechirut} in one place as {\i kinyan peiros l\u8217'guf l\u8217'zman} \u8212- I own the property for its use for a limited period \u8212- but this is a qualified form of ownership at best. To say that such a limited acquisition of land is sufficient to effect full {\i kinyan agav} on other property, on {\i metaltelin}, is conceptually bold. The more natural answer would have been: this case involves {\i kinyan chatzer}, not {\i kinyan agav}, and {\i kinyan chatzer} naturally requires the objects to be on the rented premises. The {\i Gemara} declines that path entirely \u8212- and by doing so, commits to the remarkable position that {\i sechirut} suffices for {\i kinyan agav}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafot Analysis\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rashi} explains the background of the episode: Rabban Gamliel was a {\i chaver}, and {\i chaverim} were scrupulous about not leaving grain in an untithed ({\i tevel}) state, lest family members eat from it. The pressure to act, therefore, arose from this concern.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} raises several sharp objections to this reading. First: how did Rabban Gamliel find himself in this situation to begin with? A {\i chaver}, by definition, does not leave his produce untithed. If the grain was {\i tevel}, he should not have departed without first separating {\i terumot uma\u8217'asrot}. As the saying goes, a wise man avoids situations from which a smart man must extricate himself.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Second: Rabban Gamliel never says he is separating {\i terumot uma\u8217'asrot} at this moment. He says, \u8220"the {\i maaser} that I will eventually take ({\i she\u8217'ani atid lamod}).\u8221" The act of {\i hafrashat terumot uma\u8217'asrot} requires a declaration designating a specific portion \u8212- ten percent \u8212- as {\i maaser}, and must identify its location within the granary. None of that is present here. No tithing has actually occurred.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Third: if he had already separated the {\i terumot uma\u8217'asrot}, what urgency is there in transferring it to Yehoshua or Akiva? He could simply give it to them upon his return.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} therefore reconstructs the case differently: Rabban Gamliel had informed his household that the grain was {\i tevel} and they should not touch it. There was accordingly no concern about accidental consumption. The reason for the urgency was not {\i terumot uma\u8217'asrot} per se but {\i biur} \u8212- the obligation that obtains at the end of the third year of the {\i shemittah} cycle requiring one to remove and distribute {\i ma\u8217'asrot} that have not yet been transferred to their recipients. The point of renting the space and transferring it was therefore to fulfill his {\i biur} obligation by getting the {\i maaser ani} out of his possession and into the domain of the appropriate parties.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} acknowledges that this explanation is not entirely without difficulty \u8212- for how can there be an obligation of {\i biur} with respect to grain that is still technically {\i tevel}? And there are further questions about {\i terumah} and whether {\i terumah} is subject to {\i biur} at all. Nevertheless, {\i Tosafot}\u8217's central insight is that the episode is about satisfying {\i biur}, not about the {\i hafrashah} itself.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shetar Categories\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} pivots to introduce a teaching of Rav Abba bar Yitzchak citing Rav: there are two categories of documents ({\i shenei shtarot hen}) associated with land transactions, distinguished by whether the {\i shtar} is integral to effecting the sale or subordinate to it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The first scenario: I instruct witnesses \u8212- {\i zekhu bisedeh zo l\u8217'Ploni vekhitvu lo et hashetar} \u8212- take possession of this field for Michael and write him out a bill of sale. Here two instructions are given: effect the {\i kinyan} (presumably through {\i kinyan sudar}) and then write the document. Since the {\i kinyan} is immediate and the writing of the {\i shtar} takes time, I may retract my authorization to write the {\i shtar} even after the {\i kinyan sudar} has been executed \u8212- {\i chozer bashetar ve\u8217'eino chozer bisedeh} \u8212- the sale itself stands but I may revoke the document. {\i Tosafot} asks what legitimate reason one might have for revoking the {\i shtar} after the fact. The answer offered is that publicizing the sale of one\u8217's property signals financial distress and can invite exploitation from creditors and business partners. This is a plausible consideration, even if the underlying motive is obvious.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The second scenario: I instruct them {\i al menat shetikhtevu lo et hashetar} \u8212- on the condition that you write the {\i shtar}. In this case, revoking the authorization for the {\i shtar} unravels the {\i kinyan} itself \u8212- {\i chozer bein bashetar bein bisedeh} \u8212- because the {\i kinyan sudar} was conditioned from the outset on the writing of the document.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Huna Ruling\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Huna extends the taxonomy to three categories ({\i shalosh shtarot hen}). The third case: the seller goes to witnesses in advance and says, write me a bill of sale for my field \u8212- {\i kotvin shtar lamocher af al pi she\u8217'ein loke\u8217'ach imo} \u8212- the witnesses may write such a document even without the buyer present, since the seller alone authorizes it. This pre-written {\i shtar} is then held by the seller, who will hand it to the buyer upon payment.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The operative ruling: {\i keivan shehechzik imo bekarka niknah shtar bechol makom shehu} \u8212- once the buyer performs {\i chazakah} on the land, he simultaneously acquires the pre-written {\i shtar} wherever that document may physically be located, even if it is nowhere near the property.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} notes: {\i shema mina lo ba\u8217'inan tziburin bah} \u8212- surely this proves that {\i tziburin betocha} is not required, since the {\i shtar} is acquired through {\i agav} even when not physically present on the land. The {\i Gemara} responds: {\i shani shtar de\u8217'asitra de\u8217'ara hu} \u8212- a bill of sale is categorically different because it is, conceptually, a handle ({\i asitra}) of the land itself. The deed of sale is so inherently bound up with the property it conveys that it constitutes an extension of that land. It is as if it is always \u8220"on\u8221" the land in a conceptual sense, and so this case does not require physical presence because the {\i shtar} is never truly separate from its land.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Moreover, when a {\i braita} quotes this ruling as an illustration of the general principle \u8212- {\i nechasim she\u8217'ein lahem achrayut niknim im nechasim sheyesh lahem achrayut} \u8212- it becomes clear that this is being treated not as a sui generis case but as a standard instance of {\i kinyan agav}. From this the {\i Gemara} draws its final conclusion: {\i shema mina lo ba\u8217'inan tziburin bah} \u8212- you do not need the objects to be piled upon the land for {\i kinyan agav} to operate.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Agav Word Requirement\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Bill of Sale Proof\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The preceding ruling about the pre-written {\i shtar} was identified by Rav Chiya bar Avin as an application of {\i kinyan agav}. Once the {\i Gemara} establishes that the {\i braita} treating it as {\i agav} is what Rav Chiya bar Avin himself invoked, this becomes the source of the {\i shema mina} conclusion \u8212- that {\i agav} does not require {\i tziburin betocha}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Agav Terminology\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 With the physical proximity question resolved, the {\i Gemara} turns to a new {\i iba\u8217'ya}: is the word {\i agav} itself technically required? When effecting a {\i kinyan agav}, must the seller explicitly say \u8220"you are acquiring the {\i metaltelin agav} \u8212- by means of / on the back of \u8212- this land,\u8221" or is some other formulation sufficient?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The etymological aside is worth noting: {\i agav} derives from {\i gav}, meaning \u8220"back.\u8221" The word thus conjures the image of the {\i metaltelin} riding on the back of the land \u8212- a conceptual picture of them being virtually present on the property even when physically absent. The question then is whether this very word, which performs that bundling function, must be used, or whether an equivalent expression will do.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Immo vs Agav\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} brings a proof: the cases discussed earlier \u8212- in which a {\i tefach al tefach} of land is transferred together with a hundred sheep \u8212- used the formulation {\i ve-imo me\u8217'ah tzon}, \u8220"and with it, a hundred sheep,\u8221" rather than the word {\i agav}. Does this not prove that the word {\i agav} is unnecessary?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} deflects: {\i ve-li-te\u8217'amecha kni mi katani} \u8212- by the same reasoning, those formulations also do not use the word {\i kni} (take possession), yet obviously some verb of acquisition is required. The absence of {\i kni} in the example does not mean {\i kni} is unnecessary; it means the example is paraphrasing rather than reproducing technical language. The same applies to {\i agav}: the formulation used in the example was a paraphrase, and both {\i agav} and {\i kni} are in fact technically required.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Technical Language\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i halakha} is therefore stated: {\i tziburin lo ba\u8217'inan, agav u\u8217'kni ba\u8217'inan} \u8212- physical presence of the {\i metaltelin} on the land is not required, but the technical terms {\i agav} and {\i kni} are required.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Looking at {\i Rashi}, he illustrates the {\i kni} requirement with the formulation {\i zecheh b\u8217'tefach al tefach l\u8217'Ploni} \u8212- \u8220"take possession of this {\i tefach} for Ploni.\u8221" Note that {\i Rashi} uses the verb {\i zecheh} rather than the word {\i kni} itself, which suggests that what is required is a verb of acquisition, not specifically the word {\i kni}. The defect in the examples was the absence of any verb \u8212- {\i l\u8217'Ploni} (\u8220"this is for Ploni\u8221") lacks the required authorization. Any verb of taking possession, whether {\i kni} or {\i zecheh}, presumably satisfies the requirement.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The status of the word {\i agav} is more rigidly technical. The {\i Gemara} explicitly contrasts {\i agav} with {\i immo} and treats them as non-equivalent. The word {\i immo} (\u8220"with it\u8221") does not perform the bundling function that {\i agav} does. Whether {\i agav} is required even when the {\i metaltelin} are physically present on the land \u8212- where the physical connection itself might substitute for the verbal bundling \u8212- is a {\i machloket Rishonim}, though conceptually the requirement is most pressing when the objects are not on the property and the word alone must bridge the gap.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Separate Acquisitions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mixed Transfers\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} raises a further {\i iba\u8217'ya}: {\i sadeh b\u8217'mecher u\u8217'mitaltelin b\u8217'matanah} \u8212- may one sell the land and simultaneously give the {\i metaltelin} as a gift, effecting both in a single act? This question touches on how to conceptualize the mechanism of {\i agav}. If {\i agav} fuses the two objects into a single unit \u8212- land and {\i metaltelin} as one package \u8212- then it would be incoherent to treat them differently: how can one part of a single object be sold and another part given as a gift? But if {\i agav} is understood as a single {\i kinyan} that operates separately on two distinct objects, then it becomes entirely plausible that the two objects can be transferred through different legal modalities simultaneously.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Maaser Proof\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} brings a {\i ta shma} from the Rabban Gamliel case. In that episode, Rabban Gamliel rented the space \u8212- a form of {\i mecher}, a limited sale \u8212- while giving the {\i ma\u8217'aser} itself as something closer to a {\i matanah}. Since the {\i Gemara} has already established that the episode operates through {\i agav}, this constitutes proof that the land-acquisition ({\i sechirut} as a form of {\i mecher}) and the {\i metaltelin} transfer (the {\i ma\u8217'aser} as {\i matanah}) can be of different legal characters within a single {\i agav} transaction. This is consistent with conceptualizing {\i agav} as one {\i kinyan} effecting two discrete transfers, rather than transforming the two objects into one fused unit.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Third-Party Transfer\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A sharper question follows: {\i sadeh l\u8217'echad u\u8217'mitaltelin l\u8217'achar mahu} \u8212- may one effect a {\i kinyan agav} in which the land goes to one person and the {\i metaltelin} go to a different person entirely? This is conceptually more vexing than the mixed-transfer case. Even if {\i agav} involves two distinct transfers, they ordinarily both run in the same direction \u8212- from seller to buyer. Here, the {\i kinyan} on the land runs to person A, yet the transfer of the {\i metaltelin} is meant to benefit person B. Can a single act of {\i kinyan} simultaneously take something into my possession and take something else into someone else\u8217's possession?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Akiva\u8217's Agency\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} brings another {\i ta shma} from the Rabban Gamliel episode: Rabbi Akiva rented the space (for himself, as the party effecting the {\i kinyan}) while the {\i ma\u8217'aser ani} was given to the {\i aniyim} \u8212- {\i kedei sheyizkeh bo l\u8217'aniyim}. Does this not show that the {\i kinyan} of the land ran to Akiva while the {\i metaltelin} ran to the poor?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} responds with two answers. First: {\i mushkar l\u8217'ma\u8217'aser} \u8212- the rental itself was entirely for the sake of the {\i maaser}, meaning that Akiva was taking possession of the space on behalf of the {\i aniyim} as well, so both the space and the {\i metaltelin} were being acquired for the same beneficiaries. Second: {\i shani Rabbi Akiva d\u8217'yad aniyim hava} \u8212- Rabbi Akiva was effectively the \u8220"hand\u8221" of the poor, their authorized representative, so anything he acquired was intrinsically for them.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Both answers converge on the same point: this is not a true case of splitting the {\i agav} between two different parties. In one formulation, both elements were being transferred to the same beneficiary (the {\i aniyim}); in the other, Akiva and the {\i aniyim} were functionally one and the same agent-and-beneficiary. The underlying question \u8212- whether one can truly bifurcate a {\i kinyan agav} between two distinct parties \u8212- is left unresolved by the {\i Gemara}, along with the deeper theoretical question of what {\i zechiyah} is: does taking possession on behalf of another operate by the acquirer momentarily taking it in and then transferring it outward, or does it operate by the acquirer directly representing the beneficiary from the outset? The latter model makes it much harder to understand how one act could run to two different parties.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Payment for Agav\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava\u8217's Payment Rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava states: {\i lo shanu ela shenatan d\u8217'mei kulhan} \u8212- the rule that {\i agav} transfers {\i metaltelin} applies only if the buyer has already paid for all of the {\i metaltelin} in question. If he has paid for only some of them, {\i lo kanah ella kineged ma\u8217'otav} \u8212- he acquires only those he has paid for.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This ruling may initially seem self-evident: of course one only owns what one has paid for. But in fact it is not trivial. {\i Metaltelin} can normally be acquired through {\i meshichah} \u8212- physically drawing the object toward oneself \u8212- without any prior payment. If {\i agav} is simply a valid mode of {\i kinyan} on {\i metaltelin}, why should payment be a prerequisite here but not elsewhere?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The answer lies in understanding {\i kinyan agav} as an inherently weak mechanism. When one acquires {\i metaltelin}, normally one performs a {\i kinyan} directly on the {\i metaltelin} themselves \u8212- one touches them, pulls them, or takes them into one\u8217's domain. {\i Agav} performs no direct act whatsoever on the {\i metaltelin}. Everything is done to the land, and the {\i metaltelin} are swept along conceptually. This conceptual sweep is potent enough to complete a transaction only when nothing remains outstanding. If the money has already been paid, the transfer is fully prepared and only needs this final act of formalization; the {\i agav} can supply that finalization. But if the money has not been paid, there is an unresolved gap in the transaction, and the weak mechanism of {\i agav} cannot bridge it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kinyan Strengths\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i braita} is cited in support of Rava: {\i yafeh koach kesef mikoach shtar vekoach shtar mikoach kesef} \u8212- {\i kesef} is stronger than {\i shtar} in certain respects, and {\i shtar} is stronger than {\i kesef} in others.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Kesef} is stronger than {\i shtar}: money can be used to redeem {\i hekdeshot} and {\i maaser sheni}, transferring their {\i kedushah} \u8212- objects dedicated to the {\i Beis HaMikdash} or {\i ma\u8217'aser sheni} can have their sanctity transferred onto coins. A {\i shtar} cannot do this, because a {\i shtar} lacks {\i gufo mamon} \u8212- intrinsic monetary value.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Shtar} is stronger than {\i kesef}: a {\i get} \u8212- a bill of divorce \u8212- can sever the marital bond, {\i she\u8217'shtar motzi b\u8217'vanot Yisrael}. Money cannot serve as a medium of divorce.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Both {\i kesef} and {\i shtar} are stronger than {\i chazakah}: {\i shenihem konim b\u8217'eved Ivri} \u8212- money and a document can effect the acquisition of a {\i eved Ivri}, a Hebrew bondsman. {\i Chazakah} cannot, because {\i chazakah} most fully constitutes the {\i kinyan} that makes something \u8220"one\u8217's own\u8221" in the physical and existential sense \u8212- and this character sits uneasily with the acquisition of a person.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Chazakah} is stronger than {\i kesef} and {\i shtar} in the respect now relevant to our discussion, addressed in the next subsection.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Scope of Payment\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i braita} concludes: {\i she\u8217'hachazakah machar lo eser sados b\u8217'eser medinos, keivan she\u8217'hechezik b\u8217'achat mehen kanah kulam} \u8212- if one sells ten fields in ten different countries, once the buyer performs {\i chazakah} on one of them, he acquires all of them.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i braita} then adds: {\i bameh devarim amurim? She\u8217'nosan lo d\u8217'mei kulan} \u8212- when does this hold? When he has paid for all of them. {\i Aval lo nosan lo d\u8217'mei kulan, lo kanah ella kineged ma\u8217'otav} \u8212- if not, he acquires only those for which he has paid. This confirms Rava\u8217's ruling and the underlying logic: {\i kinyan agav} \u8212- or in this case, a parallel mechanism by which one {\i chazakah} effects {\i kinyan} on multiple parcels \u8212- functions only where payment has already been completed, because the mechanism is insufficiently direct to overcome outstanding obligations.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Land to Land Agav\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shmuel\u8217's Ruling\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i braita} itself supports a statement of Shmuel: {\i machar lo eser sados b\u8217'eser medinos, keivan she\u8217'hechezik b\u8217'achat mehen kanah kulam} \u8212- if one sells ten fields in ten different countries, a single act of {\i chazakah} on one acquires all of them. This is itself a major {\i chiddush}, distinct from anything previously established about {\i agav}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The principle of {\i kinyan agav} as discussed until now involved land as the {\i ikar} and {\i metaltelin} as the secondary element swept along. The mechanism was grounded in the primacy of land: {\i metaltelin} are subordinate and can be conceptually placed \u8220"on\u8221" the land even when physically elsewhere. But the case of ten fields in ten countries involves equal-weight objects \u8212- land and land. There is no {\i ikar} and {\i tafel} relationship. How can a {\i chazakah} on one plot of land draw distant plots of land along with it?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Braita Support\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i braita} cited in support of Rava confirms Shmuel\u8217's ruling while appending the payment requirement. That the {\i braita} frames this as the source of the payment condition also implies it treats the ten-field case as an established rule. The {\i chiddush} is therefore: not only can {\i agav} (or an analogous mechanism) transfer {\i metaltelin} along with land, but one can acquire multiple distant parcels of land through a single act of {\i chazakah} on one of them.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Acha Analogy\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Acha the son of Rav Ika attempts to provide an independent proof that such a mechanism should work. He reasons: if I have ten animals tied on a single rope and I perform {\i meshichah} on one \u8212- taking it in my direction \u8212- do I not acquire all of them? Surely yes. So too, by analogy, performing {\i chazakah} on one plot should draw the others along.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Connection Analysis\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} immediately rejects this analogy in its initial formulation: {\i hatam aguda beyado} \u8212- in the animal case, the rope is in one\u8217's hand. One is physically holding a cord that is directly and continuously connected to all ten animals. Taking possession of the rope is in some meaningful sense taking possession of all that is attached to it. By contrast, ten fields in ten countries are not connected by any rope. There is no physical chain running from one to the other. {\i Hacha ein aguda beyado} \u8212- here, there is no rope in one\u8217's hand.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The analogy is then inverted \u8212- {\i ika de-amrei}, a second version of the exchange is reported, in which Rav Acha uses the same animal case as a proof against Shmuel. If I have ten animals on one rope but specify only the last animal \u8212- {\i zu kni} \u8212- do you acquire the others? Of course not; you specified only one. So too, specifying {\i chazakah} on one field should not confer the others. The {\i Gemara} responds to this version as well.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Land as Sadna\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara\u8217's} response to the second version distinguishes land from animals in the opposite direction: {\i hatam gufim mechulakin} \u8212- animals are separate physical bodies, and specifying one excludes the others. {\i Hacha sadna de-ara chad hu} \u8212- here, the \u8220"anvil\u8221" of the land is one. {\i Rashi} identifies {\i sadna} as the anvil on which a blacksmith strikes \u8212- the broad, flat, unified surface. Land, whatever its legal subdivisions, is physically continuous. All land is connected through the expanse of the earth itself. Therefore, doing {\i chazakah} on one plot reaches through the physical continuity of the earth to the other plots as well. The seller has designated all of them for transfer; the physical unity of land provides the conceptual chain that the rope provided for the animals.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might ask: if all land on earth is physically connected, why would only the seller\u8217's other fields be included and not everyone else\u8217's fields? The answer, implicit in the discussion, is that the {\i kinyan} reaches only those parcels that have been designated \u8212- but the mechanism by which a single {\i chazakah} can reach distant designated parcels is the physical unity of the earth\u8217's surface.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chiddushim Summary\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The discussion of {\i kinyan agav} yields a remarkable collection of {\i chiddushim}, each one deserving attention in its own right.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The first {\i chiddush} is that {\i sechirut} \u8212- rental \u8212- suffices for {\i kinyan agav}. Acquiring a space through rental, a limited and qualified form of ownership, is sufficient to draw other property along as {\i agav}. The {\i Gemara} did not take the simpler path of classifying Rabban Gamliel\u8217's case as {\i kinyan chatzer} and instead endorsed the broader principle that even a {\i kinyan} through rental can be the vehicle for {\i agav}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The second {\i chiddush} is that {\i tziburin betocha} is not required \u8212- the {\i metaltelin} need not be physically on the land \u8212- but the technical formulation of {\i agav} and {\i kni} must be used. Whether this verbal requirement applies only when the objects are not on the land or even when they are is debated among the {\i Rishonim}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The third {\i chiddush} is that the two components of an {\i agav} transaction may be of different legal character: one may sell the land and give the {\i metaltelin} as a gift, demonstrating that the two objects are not fused into one unit but remain conceptually distinct, even as a single {\i kinyan} operates on both.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The fourth {\i chiddush} is Rava\u8217's ruling that {\i agav} requires prior payment for the {\i metaltelin} in question, reflecting the inherent weakness of a {\i kinyan} that touches the transferred object only indirectly.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The fifth and perhaps most conceptually surprising {\i chiddush} is Shmuel\u8217's ruling that a single {\i chazakah} on one parcel can transfer multiple parcels of land, grounded in the principle that {\i sadna de-ara chad hu} \u8212- all land is one unified expanse, and {\i chazakah} on one point of that expanse reaches the whole.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Gilgul Shevua\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gilgul Principle\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having concluded the extended analysis of {\i kinyan agav} \u8212- by which land draws {\i metaltelin} along with it in an acquisition \u8212- the {\i Gemara} introduces the converse phenomenon: {\i metaltelin} drawing along a {\i shevuah} obligation on {\i karka}. The formal rule is that one does not make a {\i shevuah} on {\i karka} \u8212- land is categorically excluded from the {\i shevuah} mechanism in monetary disputes. Yet if a {\i shevuah} is properly triggered regarding {\i metaltelin}, it can be \u8220"rolled\u8221" to include {\i karka} as well. This is {\i gilgul shevuah} \u8212- the rolling of an oath. The word {\i gilgul} (cognate with the word for reincarnation, in which a soul rolls and settles into a new vessel) here means the extension of an oath originally anchored on one matter to encompass additional matters that could not independently have triggered a {\i shevuah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sotah Source\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Ulla asks: {\i minayin le-gilgul shevuah min ha-Torah} \u8212- where is the Scriptural basis for {\i gilgul shevuah}? His answer: from the {\i sotah} passage. The woman accused of {\i sotah} responds {\i Amein Amein} \u8212- a double affirmation. The {\i Gemara} (citing the Mishnah) unpacks the double {\i amein}: {\i Amein al ha-alah, Amein al ha-shevuah} \u8212- one {\i amein} accepts the curse ({\i alah}) and one accepts the oath ({\i shevuah}). This double acceptance is itself already an expansion: she accepts consequences on multiple levels from a single proceeding.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Oath Expansion\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But the expansion goes further. The double {\i amein} also addresses {\i amein im me-ish zeh ve-Amein im me-ish acher} \u8212- one oath regarding the specific man about whom the warning ({\i kinuy}) and seclusion ({\i stiiah}) occurred, and another regarding any other man. The {\i sotah} procedure is triggered by a particular warning and particular seclusion; the oath naturally covers that specific case. The fact that it can be extended to cover other men she may have been suspected with is an application of {\i gilgul shevuah}: once the {\i shevuah} is in motion, it rolls to include additional matters.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} continues: {\i amein she-lo satisi arusah u-nesuah ve-shomeres yavam u-kenuah} \u8212- the oath can also be extended to cover conduct during different stages of the woman\u8217's marital history: as an {\i arusah} (betrothed but not yet fully married), as a {\i nesuah} (fully married), as a {\i shomeres yavam} (a widow awaiting {\i yibum}), and as a {\i kenuah} (after {\i yibum} has been performed).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Arusah Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} homes in on the {\i arusah} case as the critical proof of {\i gilgul shevuah\u8217's} reach. The question is posed: {\i hai arusah heichi dami} \u8212- what exactly is the scenario in which the woman must swear about what she did as an {\i arusah}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If the {\i kinuy} (warning) occurred while she was an {\i arusah} and the {\i stirah} (seclusion) occurred while she was an {\i arusah}, this is a case entirely during {\i erusin}. But the Mishnah rules explicitly \u8212- {\i arusah ve-shomeret yavam lo shotot ve-lo notlot kesubah} \u8212- that an {\i arusah} and a {\i shomeret yavam} do not drink the {\i sotah} waters. They are categorically excluded.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tachad Ishech\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The reason for this exclusion is derived from the verse: {\i tachas ishech} \u8212- \u8220"under your husband.\u8221" The {\i sotah} laws apply only to a woman who has fully strayed from under her husband \u8212- that is, a woman in full {\i nisu\u8217'in}. An {\i arusah}, though betrothed, has not yet entered the husband\u8217's home in the full sense of {\i tachas ish}, and therefore the {\i sotah} procedure cannot apply to her conduct as an {\i arusah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} then considers a modified scenario: the {\i kinuy} occurred while she was an {\i arusah}, the seclusion ({\i stirah}) occurred while she was an {\i arusah}, but the husband proceeds with the marriage anyway \u8212- and now, as a {\i nesuah}, wishes to administer the {\i sotah} waters retroactively for the conduct that occurred during {\i erusin}. Now {\i tachas ishech} is satisfied in the sense that she is currently a {\i nesuah}. Can the {\i sotah} process proceed?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} rejects this as well, citing the verse {\i ve-nikah ha-ish me-avon} \u8212- the husband must be clean of sin. If she was suspected during {\i erusin} and he nonetheless slept with her prior to the drinking \u8212- which he would have done by proceeding through {\i chuppah} and {\i nisu\u8217'in} \u8212- then he has himself acted improperly (since a man suspecting his wife is not supposed to cohabit with her), and the waters cannot test her. {\i Bizman she-ish menukeh me-avon mayim bodkin es ishto, ein ish menukeh me-avon ein mayim bodkin es ishto.} There is therefore no configuration in which the {\i arusah} scenario can directly trigger the {\i sotah} process.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gilgul Despite Exclusion\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The conclusion: {\i ella al yedei gilgul} \u8212- the only way she can be required to swear about her conduct as an {\i arusah} is through {\i gilgul shevuah}. Because she is already taking a {\i shevuah} about a qualifying case \u8212- one that directly meets all the requirements \u8212- the oath can be rolled to include the {\i arusah} scenario as well, even though that scenario is categorically excluded from independently generating a {\i sotah} obligation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is precisely the point the {\i Gemara} is making: {\i gilgul shevuah} is not merely an extension to adjacent or similar cases. It can reach matters that are categorically excluded \u8212- that could never, under any circumstances, have been the direct subject of the {\i shevuah}. In the monetary context, {\i karka} is categorically excluded from {\i shevuah}, just as the {\i arusah} scenario is categorically excluded from {\i sotah}. The {\i sotah} passage proves that such categorical exclusions do not block {\i gilgul}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Money Extension\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} asks: {\i ashkan sotah de-isura} \u8212- we have established {\i gilgul shevuah} from {\i sotah}, a case of {\i issur} (religious prohibition). {\i Mamona minalan} \u8212- how do we know {\i gilgul} applies to monetary disputes? One might argue that {\i issur} is more stringent and therefore entitled to a more expansive {\i shevuah} regime. Or conversely, that the two domains operate by different rules entirely.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The school of Rabbi Yishmael ({\i tana devei Rabbi Yishmael}) provides a {\i kal vachomer}: {\i uma sotah shelo nitenah lehitava be-ed echad megalgelin} \u8212- by {\i sotah}, the {\i gilgul} is operative even though the {\i sotah} proceeding itself cannot be triggered by a single witness; it requires two witnesses to the seclusion. Yet even with this high threshold, {\i gilgul} functions. {\i Mamon shenitnah lehitava be-ed echad} \u8212- in monetary disputes, a single witness is sufficient to trigger a {\i shevuah}. The threshold is lower, and if {\i gilgul} functions despite the more demanding requirement in {\i sotah}, all the more so does {\i gilgul} function in monetary disputes where the triggering threshold is easier to meet. Accordingly, one who is obligated to take a {\i shevuah} in a monetary dispute can be required, through {\i gilgul shevuah}, to include {\i karka} in that oath \u8212- even though {\i karka} is categorically excluded from independently generating a {\i shevuah} obligation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} continues with {\i gilgul shevuah} on the following {\i daf}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Kiddushin 28\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Gilgul Shvu\u8217'ah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Intro & Sotah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The daf opens by wrapping up the conclusion of a {\i mishnah} that addressed {\i kinyan al karka} \u8212- the acquisition of land \u8212- and the principle of {\i kinyan agav}, whereby a {\i kinyan} performed on {\i karka} can simultaneously effect acquisition of {\i metaltelin}, whether the chattel is physically on the land or not. The sugya now turns to the reverse connection: the principle of {\i gilgul shvu\u8217'ah}. If one party is compelled to take an oath regarding {\i metaltelin}, one can also compel that party to take an oath regarding {\i karka}, even though {\i karka} is categorically excluded from the normal scope of monetary oaths.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The source for this principle is {\i Sotah}. By the laws of {\i Sotah}, when a woman is compelled by {\i parshat Sotah} to swear that she did not commit adultery with a particular man, the oath can be extended even to a case that could never have constituted a {\i Sotah} situation in the first place \u8212- such as a case involving an {\i arusa}. This establishes the general principle of {\i gilgul shvu\u8217'ah}: an oath, once legitimately initiated, can be rolled to encompass even categorically excluded claims. A {\i kal vachomer} then extends the principle from {\i issur} \u8212- an oath relating to a prohibition such as adultery \u8212- to {\i mamon}, monetary matters. If {\i gilgul shvu\u8217'ah} operates in the domain of {\i issur}, it certainly operates in the domain of {\i mamon}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kal Vachomer\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i kal vachomer} itself is straightforward: we have established that {\i gilgul shvu\u8217'ah} applies from one prohibited matter to another, even categorically excluded ones. The {\i gemara} reasons that if this principle applies in the realm of {\i issur}, it applies no less \u8212- and in fact more readily \u8212- in the realm of {\i mamon}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Vadai vs.\u160 ?Safek\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Picking up at the top of 28a, the {\i gemara} raises a further question. We have established {\i gilgul shvu\u8217'ah} in cases of {\i vadai} \u8212- definite claims. But what of {\i safek}? The distinction here requires some elaboration. The typical case compelling an oath is one of {\i ta\u8217'anat bari}: I claim you borrowed a hundred dollars, you deny it, and there is supporting evidence \u8212- a {\i modeh be-miktzat} or a {\i ed echad} \u8212- that creates a genuine, definite claim. The question is whether {\i gilgul shvu\u8217'ah} can extend to a {\i safek} \u8212- that is, a situation where I merely suspect you of wrongdoing without knowing for certain, and I want to compel an oath on the basis of that suspicion alone.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rashi explains the import of the question: can the rolling oath cover cases where my second claim is not a {\i ta\u8217'anat bari} at all, but merely a suspicion? This would be analogous to what a husband does in the {\i Sotah} context, where the claim is by definition one of suspicion rather than certainty.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Shimon bar Yochai\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} answers by citing a {\i braita} in the name of Rabbi Shimon bar Yochai, who employs a {\i gezerah shavah} between the {\i shvu\u8217'ah} sworn \u8220"outside\u8221" \u8212- in {\i beit din} regarding monetary matters \u8212- and the {\i shvu\u8217'ah} sworn \u8220"inside\u8221" \u8212- in the {\i Beit HaMikdash} in the {\i Sotah} procedure. {\i Ma shvu\u8217'ah bifnim asa bo safek ke-vadai} \u8212- just as the oath taken inside treats a {\i safek} as a {\i vadai} (the husband\u8217's suspicion suffices to initiate the {\i Sotah} procedure), so too {\i af shvu\u8217'ah ba-chutz asa bo safek ke-vadai} \u8212- the monetary oath in {\i beit din} can treat a {\i safek} as a {\i vadai}. In other words, the {\i gilgul shvu\u8217'ah} can be invoked even where the second claim rests only on suspicion, not certainty.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The practical implication: if I have obtained a {\i shvu\u8217'ah} from you on one matter, I can now compel you to swear regarding other things I suspect you of \u8212- not merely things I know definitively \u8212- through the mechanism of {\i gilgul shvu\u8217'ah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mammon & Suspicion\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then affirms that this extension applies fully to monetary suspicions. Once the conditions for {\i gilgul shvu\u8217'ah} are met \u8212- a legitimate initial oath in the domain of {\i mamon} \u8212- the sweep of the rolling oath is broad: it can encompass {\i karka} (categorically excluded from ordinary monetary oaths), and it can encompass {\i safek} claims, where the claimant\u8217's basis is suspicion rather than certainty.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Limits of Gilgul\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Extent of Gilgul\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having established the breadth of {\i gilgul shvu\u8217'ah}, the {\i gemara} asks: {\i ad heichan gilgul shvu\u8217'ah} \u8212- how far does this principle actually extend? The question, read in context, appears to be probing the outer limits of the type of claim that can be appended through {\i gilgul shvu\u8217'ah}. Can even the most far-fetched suspicion be made the subject of an oath?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Eved Ivri Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} cites Rav Yehuda quoting Rav: {\i de-amar leh hishava li she-ein avdi ata} \u8212- swear to me that you are not my slave. At first blush, this reads as an exploration of the most extreme edge of {\i gilgul shvu\u8217'ah}: even a seemingly absurd claim \u8212- that this person is my slave \u8212- can be made the subject of a compelled oath through {\i gilgul shvu\u8217'ah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Name-Calling Laws\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Before the {\i gemara} can address this case on its merits, it immediately flags a threshold problem: {\i hahu de-shamiteih mishamntinan lei} \u8212- the very act of claiming that someone might be your slave is itself grounds for placing the claimant in {\i nidduy}, rabbinic excommunication. A {\i braita} is cited: {\i hakorei le-chaveiro eved yehei be-nidduy} \u8212- one who calls his friend a slave is placed in {\i nidduy}. The {\i braita} continues with a graduated set of penalties: calling someone a {\i mamzer} incurs forty lashes; calling someone a {\i rasha} results in the caller being subjected to unfettered economic competition in ways that would otherwise be prohibited.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot offers a striking explanation of this gradation based on the principle of {\i midah ke-neged midah}. Calling someone a slave invokes the curse of {\i arur Kena\u8217'an} \u8212- slaves are associated with {\i aru}r \u8212- and so the response is {\i nidduy}, which is itself a form of curse and censure. Calling someone a {\i mamzer} implies that his parents violated a {\i lav}, and so the caller receives {\i lashes}, as one who is transgresses a {\i lav}. Calling someone a {\i rasha} generally carries connotations of financial wrongdoing, and so the consequence operates in the financial domain \u8212- the offended party is permitted to compete aggressively with the caller in business in ways otherwise impermissible.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava\u8217's Distinction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 At this point Rava resolves the apparent contradiction: the case is not {\i hishava li she-ein avdi ata} \u8212- \u8220"swear that you are not my slave\u8221" \u8212- which would imply claiming that a Jew is an {\i eved Kena\u8217'ani}, a claim that is wholly intolerable. Rather, Rava reframes it as {\i hishava li she-lo nimkarta li be-eved Ivri} \u8212- swear to me that you were not sold to me as an {\i eved Ivri}. This is a fundamentally different claim: it is a monetary {\i ta\u8217'anah} regarding an {\i eved Ivri}, not an accusation stripping the person of his Jewish status.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is also worth noting \u8212- though one might be reading too much into the precise phrasing \u8212- that the reformulation shifts from attaching a label to the person (\u8220"you are my slave\u8221") to describing a transaction (\u8220"you were sold to me in the capacity of an {\i eved Ivri}\u8221"). The earlier language categorizes the individual; the later language describes a past legal relationship.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Now the {\i gemara} asks: but why is this a {\i chiddush}? If someone owes me money and has been sold to me as an {\i eved Ivri} to pay that debt, that is simply a standard monetary claim \u8212- {\i ta\u8217'anat mamon} \u8212- and {\i gilgul shvu\u8217'ah} on monetary matters is already established! The answer is that Rava follows his own position elsewhere: {\i eved Ivri gufo kanuy} \u8212- the body of an {\i eved Ivri} has a form of {\i kinyan} attached to it. Because Rava holds that the {\i kinyan} in an {\i eved Ivri} is akin to ownership of his person, the oath would technically be an oath about ownership of a person, which \u8212- like {\i karka} \u8212- is categorically excluded from the ordinary framework of monetary oaths. The {\i chiddush} is that {\i gilgul shvu\u8217'ah} extends even here, to an oath touching on the {\i kinyan} of a person.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then presses: but if that is the {\i chiddush}, is it not simply equivalent to the already-established point that {\i gilgul shvu\u8217'ah} extends to {\i karka}? After all, {\i avadim} are legally comparable to {\i karka}. One can hardly imagine anything more in the domain of {\i metaltelin} than a human being \u8212- and yet the halakha is that the {\i kinyan} of an {\i eved} is not fully analogous to ordinary {\i metaltelin} precisely because human beings, like land, are not entirely subject to the same kind of ordinary dominion.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara}\u8217's answer identifies the specific {\i chiddush}: one might have thought this claim too implausible to be admissible at all, since an {\i eved Ivri} relationship is uncommon and, where it does exist, it would typically be publicly known \u8212- {\i kola it lei}, there would be a public noise about it. Therefore one might argue that this claim is simply too far-fetched to compel an oath. The {\i ka mashma lan} is that we do accept it, and the compelled oath stands.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Thus the {\i gemara}\u8217's inquiry into {\i ad heichan gilgul shvu\u8217'ah} ends up not exploring the farthest fringe of speculative claims in general, but rather the specific {\i chiddush} that an oath regarding {\i eved Ivri} can be compelled through {\i gilgul shvu\u8217'ah} \u8212- even though the claim seems implausible for the reasons above. In summary: {\i gilgul shvu\u8217'ah} can extend to categorically excluded matters ({\i karka} and {\i avadim}), to {\i safek} claims, and even to claims that appear far-fetched.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafot Limits Gilgul\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Precisely because the principle of {\i gilgul shvu\u8217'ah} seems so sweeping \u8212- {\i safek}, {\i karka}, far-fetched claims \u8212- Tosafot moves to impose some limit. Without any constraint, {\i gilgul shvu\u8217'ah} could theoretically allow one to compel a person to swear about virtually anything under the sun: \u8220"swear that you never stole anything from me in your life,\u8221" \u8220"swear that you never borrowed anything from me,\u8221" across every conceivable domain, on the basis of pure speculation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot, citing {\i rebbi}, states explicitly: {\i shelo kol safek megalgelin} \u8212- not every speculative claim can be the subject of {\i gilgul}. If you are owed an oath by someone, you cannot simply roll it onto every suspicion you have ever entertained. Rather, {\i davka be-safek she-yesh lanu lachush k-tzat} \u8212- the claim appended through {\i gilgul} must have at least some minimal basis for concern. Tosafot offers the example: if we were partners \u8212- {\i shutaf o aris} \u8212- and we handled each other\u8217's money, then upon dissolution of the partnership there is a reasonable basis to suspect possible misappropriation, and {\i gilgul shvu\u8217'ah} can properly extend to that suspicion.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might ask how this squares with the {\i gemara}\u8217's own case of the {\i eved Ivri}, where the claim seemed quite far-fetched with no particular basis. The tension is real, and Tosafot is consciously pulling back from the most expansive reading of the {\i gemara}. The {\i ka mashma lan} of the {\i sugya} is that we do compel the oath in that case \u8212- but Tosafot argues that to take the principle to its fullest logical extreme, permitting any baseless speculation to trigger {\i gilgul shvu\u8217'ah}, would be absurd. There must be at least some minimal grounding.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Chalifin Overview\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Archaic Language\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now moves to the next {\i mishnah}, which opens a new topic: {\i kinyan chalifin}, the acquisition of property through exchange. The {\i mishnah} employs notably archaic and unusual phrasing, and \u8212- as will become clear \u8212- the {\i gemara} itself struggles to parse the precise meaning of its opening clause. The {\i mishnah} reads: {\i kol hana\u8217'aseh damim be-acher, keivan she-zachah zeh, nitchayav zeh be-chalifav} \u8212- anything that becomes money in relation to something else, once this one takes possession, the other is obligated in its exchange.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The language of the {\i mishnah} is sufficiently elliptical that it is worth pausing on what the basic structure means before even attempting to identify the {\i chiddush}. It appears that the {\i mishnah} is dealing with a situation in which two parties have agreed to exchange one object for another, and the operative question is what happens once one party effects {\i kinyan} on his side of the transaction.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Zachah & Nitchayev\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} then provides an illustration: {\i keitzad? hechlif shor be-farah o chamor be-shor} \u8212- for example, an ox exchanged for a cow, or a donkey for an ox. {\i Keivan she-zachah zeh, nitchayev zeh be-chalifav} \u8212- once this one takes possession, the other is obligated in its exchange.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 There is an interpretive question about this second clause. Tosafot records a variant {\i girsa} that lacks the second {\i zeh}, reading simply {\i keivan she-zachah zeh, nitchayev be-chalifav} \u8212- once you have taken possession, you are obligated to give the exchange. On that reading, the clause means straightforwardly: once party A takes the donkey, he is obligated to give the cow.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The received {\i girsa} \u8212- with the second {\i zeh} \u8212- yields a different meaning. The introduction of a second {\i zeh} suggests we are pointing to the {\i other} party. The meaning becomes: once Shimon takes the donkey, Reuven \u8212- who has not yet retrieved the cow still sitting in Shimon\u8217's backyard \u8212- is now the one who bears responsibility for any loss that befalls the cow. The cow belongs to Reuven from the moment Shimon effects {\i kinyan} on his side of the transaction, even if Reuven has not yet come to collect it. {\i Nitchayev zeh be-chalifav} means not \u8220"he must deliver the exchange\u8221" but rather \u8220"he has become the owner of the exchange object and bears all associated liability for it.\u8221"\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Concretely: Reuven and Shimon agree to exchange a donkey for a cow. Shimon takes Reuven\u8217's donkey. The cow remains in Shimon\u8217's backyard. Reuven is now liable for any mishap that occurs to the cow \u8212- if it injures itself before Reuven comes to collect it, that is Reuven\u8217's loss and not Shimon\u8217's.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Barter vs.\u160 ?Sudar\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Before turning to the {\i gemara}\u8217's analysis of the opening clause of the {\i mishnah}, it is important to appreciate that the topic of {\i kinyan chalifin} actually encompasses two conceptually distinct phenomena that the {\i gemara} tends to treat under unified rules, even though they are in fact quite different in character.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The first is pure barter: I give you one object, you give me another, and each party\u8217's {\i kinyan} on the received object is completed through the exchange itself. This is what the end of the {\i mishnah} \u8212- the case of the ox and the donkey \u8212- is clearly describing.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The second is {\i kinyan sudar}, the handkerchief acquisition, which is modeled on the {\i pasuk} in Rut: {\i va-shalaf ish na\u8217'alo ve-natan le-rei\u8217'eihu, ve-zot ha-te\u8217'udah be-Yisrael} \u8212- and a man drew off his shoe and gave it to his neighbor, and this was the manner of attestation in Israel. In {\i kinyan sudar}, one party hands a garment or object to the other, and this act of transfer constitutes a formal expression of {\i gemirat da\u8217'at} \u8212- complete and binding resolve \u8212- that can effect the transfer of entirely separate property, finalize contractual obligations, and so on. The handkerchief is not being exchanged {\i for} the property; it is rather a symbolic act that signifies commitment.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The difference between these two forms of {\i chalifin} is sharp. In {\i kinyan sudar}, the {\i gemara} debates whether the object ({\i kli}) must belong to the {\i koneh} (buyer) or the {\i makneh} (seller). If it must belong to the {\i makneh}, the logic \u8212- from the perspective of barter \u8212- becomes nearly unintelligible: in what sense is the seller giving the buyer his own object and receiving the sold property in return? As a symbolic signifier of {\i gemirat da\u8217'at}, this is coherent: I hand you my handkerchief as an expression of my decision to transfer my car to you. As barter, it makes no sense at all.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot takes this tension to its logical conclusion. If the rules of {\i kinyan sudar} \u8212- including the possibility that the {\i kli} belongs to the {\i makneh} \u8212- are applied mechanically to the barter case of the cow and the donkey, one arrives at the strange result that the {\i mishnah} would be describing not a swap (I give you my cow and receive your donkey) but a case where one party gives both objects. That is plainly not {\i chalifin} in any recognizable sense.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Yet the {\i gemara} does proceed to apply the categories of {\i kinyan sudar} \u8212- including the debate about {\i peirot} and {\i matbei\u8217'a} \u8212- to the barter case of the {\i mishnah}. One should bear this conceptual tension in mind as the discussion unfolds.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Damim Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} opens its analysis of the {\i mishnah} by asking: {\i chalifin mai nihu?} \u8212- in the opening clause of the {\i mishnah}, what type of {\i chalifin} is being described? The phrase {\i kol hana\u8217'aseh damim be-acher} \u8212- \u8220"anything that becomes money in relation to something else\u8221" \u8212- is opaque, and the {\i gemara} must determine its referent before the {\i mishnah} can be understood.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Three Categories\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Money as Object\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rashi\u8217's reading of the {\i mishnah}\u8217's opening clause proceeds as follows. The phrase {\i kol hana\u8217'aseh damim be-acher} describes a case where something that would ordinarily function as {\i kesef} \u8212- money, used as currency to pay the price of an object \u8212- is being treated instead as a physical object in its own right, to be exchanged for another object in a {\i kinyan chalifin}. On this reading, the {\i mishnah} is introducing the possibility that money ({\i matbei\u8217'a}) can serve as the object of {\i chalifin}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 To appreciate the {\i chiddush}, recall that {\i kinyan kesef} \u8212- acquisition through payment of money \u8212- does not work for {\i metaltelin} under rabbinic ordinance. If Reuven wishes to purchase Shimon\u8217's donkey, paying cash does not complete the sale; {\i meshicha} \u8212- taking physical possession \u8212- is required. Ordinarily, then, money functions as abstract currency, not as an object, and as such it cannot serve as the basis for {\i kinyan chalifin}. But if we reconceptualize the coins not as a payment for the donkey\u8217's price but as {\i an object being swapped for the donkey} \u8212- as if the coins were simply another physical item in a barter arrangement \u8212- then, at least theoretically, they might work.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A useful analogy: imagine a world in which currency had not yet been fully abstracted from physical objects, and all transactions were pure barter. In that world, a pile of coins would simply be another pile of items, no different in principle from a pile of grain or a collection of vessels. The coins would be appraised for their metallic value, just as the donkey is appraised for its utility, and the two parties would swap. In that frame, coins can serve as {\i chalifin}. The {\i chiddush} is that even within a monetized economy, one can conceptually reframe coins as objects rather than as currency and thereby perform {\i kinyan chalifin} with them.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The difficulty \u8212- and the reason this is ultimately rejected \u8212- is that coins are by nature abstract value. Treating them as mere physical objects is somewhat fictive. Indeed, the {\i gemara} eventually rules that {\i matbei\u8217'a lo na\u8217'aseh chalifin} \u8212- a coin cannot serve as the basis for {\i chalifin}. But the {\i hava amina} is that it could.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Valuation Principle\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then revisits the opening clause of the {\i mishnah} and proposes a second, opposite reading. Rav Yehuda interprets {\i kol hana\u8217'aseh damim be-acher} \u8212- or, as the {\i gemara} here reads it, {\i kol ha-nishom damim be-acher} \u8212- not as \u8220"anything that functions as money\u8221" but rather as \u8220"anything that must be assessed for its monetary value.\u8221" In other words: anything that is {\i not itself money} \u8212- anything whose value must be determined by appraisal rather than being intrinsically denominated \u8212- can serve as the object of {\i kinyan chalifin}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 On this reading, the clause is making the opposite point from the first interpretation: cash cannot be {\i chalifin}, but any non-cash object can. Cash is money; it does not need to be \u8220"converted\u8221" or assessed in monetary terms because it {\i is} the monetary term. Everything else \u8212- animals, produce, vessels \u8212- has a value that must be determined relative to money. It is precisely those things that are eligible for {\i chalifin}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The grammar bears this out: {\i nishom} is a {\i nifal} of {\i shuma} \u8212- appraisal, assessment. Anything that must be appraised in monetary terms (because it is not itself monetary) is eligible for {\i chalifin}. {\i Deika nami}, the {\i gemara} notes \u8212- this reading is also supported by the {\i mishnah}\u8217's own illustration: {\i keitzad? hechlif shor be-farah o chamor be-shor}, an ox for a cow and a donkey for an ox. The {\i keitzad} functions as an illustration, and an illustration involving livestock and animals confirms that the {\i mishnah} is talking about non-cash objects \u8212- precisely the category that Rav Yehuda identifies.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Matbea Excluded\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} at this point raises an apparent implication from the first reading of the {\i mishnah}: {\i shema mina matbei\u8217'a na\u8217'aseh chalifin} \u8212- should we infer from the first reading that a coin can indeed serve as {\i chalifin}? The {\i gemara} answers that, to the contrary, the second reading is correct and we reject the first. Under the second reading, it is the very {\i absence} of monetary character \u8212- the need for appraisal \u8212- that qualifies an object for {\i chalifin}. Cash itself is disqualified.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This conclusion reflects the fundamental distinction between {\i kinyan kesef} and {\i kinyan chalifin}. {\i Kinyan kesef} uses money in its role as currency to pay for an object\u8217's price; the Sages annulled {\i kinyan kesef} for {\i metaltelin} out of concern for consumer protection \u8212- if the buyer pays and the seller has not yet delivered, the seller has no incentive to protect the goods. The Rabbis therefore replaced {\i kinyan kesef} with {\i kinyan meshicha}. {\i Kinyan chalifin}, by contrast, is a conceptually distinct mode of acquisition premised on the exchange of objects, not the payment of value. Money, as pure value rather than object, does not fit within {\i kinyan chalifin}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Peiros Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 With the conclusion established that {\i matbei\u8217'a lo na\u8217'aseh chalifin}, the {\i gemara} turns to the intermediate category: {\i peirot}. Between a coin ({\i matbei\u8217'a}) at one end and a formal vessel or instrument ({\i kli}) at the other, there is the broad category of produce and ordinary physical goods \u8212- consumables, animals, and other non-fungible objects that are neither currency nor manufactured instruments.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} cites a dispute: Rav Sheshet holds {\i peirot avdei chalifin} \u8212- produce and ordinary goods can serve as {\i chalifin}; Rav Nachman holds {\i peirot lo avdei chalifin} \u8212- they cannot, and only a {\i kli} qualifies.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah}\u8217's illustration \u8212- an ox for a cow \u8212- is the crux of the matter. Whether a {\i shor} or {\i chamor} belongs in the {\i peirot} category or the {\i kli} category is itself a question. One could argue that animals, as functional creatures that serve practical purposes, are analogous to instruments ({\i kli}); on the other hand, they are not manufactured objects, and their \u8220"functionality\u8221" is not the same as that of a vessel. Rashi and Tosafot both assume, for the purposes of reading this {\i gemara}, that the {\i shor} and {\i chamor} of the {\i mishnah} fall within the {\i peirot} category.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If that is so, the {\i mishnah}\u8217's illustration only works according to Rav Sheshet, who permits {\i peirot} for {\i chalifin}. According to Rav Nachman, the {\i mishnah}\u8217's case of the ox and cow cannot be an example of {\i chalifin} at all.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Money via Debt\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Nachman\u8217's position creates a serious exegetical problem: if {\i peirot} cannot serve as {\i chalifin} and the only eligible object is a {\i kli}, there is no {\i kli} anywhere in the {\i mishnah}. How then does Rav Nachman read the {\i mishnah}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} proposes that Rav Nachman interprets the {\i mishnah} entirely differently. On his reading, the {\i mishnah} is not discussing {\i kinyan chalifin} at all. Rather: {\i yesh damim she-hen ke-chalifin} \u8212- there are cases where money functions analogously to {\i chalifin} in effecting acquisition. The illustration would then be: if you exchange the {\i money owed for} an ox with an actual cow \u8212- that is, if the outstanding debt from one transaction is offset against a new one \u8212- this can work to effect acquisition.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Concretely: Reuven sells his cow to Shimon, who now owes Reuven a hundred dollars. Before Shimon pays, Reuven tells Shimon: I will take your donkey in exchange for the hundred dollars you owe me. You keep the hundred, I get the donkey. On this reading, the \u8220"exchange\u8221" is not of physical objects but of a pre-existing monetary obligation. {\i Dmei shor be-farah} \u8212- the price-value of an ox against a cow \u8212- is being exchanged not in a physical barter but via the reallocation of a debt.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The underlying legal mechanism, the {\i gemara} explains, is that Rav Nachman holds like Rabbi Yochanan, who maintained that {\i mi-de-oraita ma\u8217'ot konot} \u8212- biblically speaking, {\i kinyan kesef} works for {\i metaltelin}. The reason the Sages replaced {\i kinyan kesef} with {\i kinyan meshicha} was a practical concern: if the buyer pays and the seller has no incentive to safeguard the goods, and then claims they were lost or destroyed, the buyer is left unprotected. But the Sages enacted this protective measure only for common scenarios ({\i milta de-shchicha}); for uncommon scenarios ({\i milta de-lo shchicha}), they did not extend the enactment, and the biblical rule of {\i kinyan kesef} remains operative.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is a striking result. The offset of a prior debt \u8212- where nothing changes hands at all and the entire transaction is abstract \u8212- is identified as the uncommon scenario in which the Sages\u8217' enactment does not apply, so that {\i kinyan kesef} works there by biblical right. The {\i gemara} is effectively saying that the scenario where the most tenuous possible \u8220"payment\u8221" occurs is the one in which {\i kinyan kessef} is most firmly operative.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rashi already notes that logically the {\i shor} and {\i chamor} of the {\i mishnah} could just as well be placed in the {\i kli} category rather than {\i peirot}, which would dissolve the entire problem for Rav Nachman. But the deeper issue is conceptual: the {\i gemara} is applying to the plain barter case of the {\i mishnah} a set of categorical distinctions \u8212- {\i matbei\u8217'a}, {\i peirot}, {\i kli} \u8212- that properly belong to the {\i sugya} of {\i kinyan sudar}, where the {\i kinyan} is not a simple exchange of goods but a formal act of symbolic commitment. The conflation of barter and {\i kinyan sudar} under a single framework produces the interpretive contortions the {\i gemara} undertakes here.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} concludes the passage regarding Reish Lakish, who holds that {\i meshicha meforeshet min ha-Torah} \u8212- the requirement of {\i meshicha} for {\i metaltelin} is explicitly derived from the Torah, and he does not share Rabbi Yochanan\u8217's view that {\i kinyan kesef} retains biblical force. For Reish Lakish, the Rav Nachman reading of the {\i mishnah} is simply impossible \u8212- there is no {\i kinyan kesef} to invoke. Reish Lakish must therefore read the {\i mishnah} as a case of {\i chalifin} with {\i peirot}, and is therefore compelled to hold like Rav Sheshet that {\i peirot} do indeed work for {\i chalifin}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Reshus Gavoa vs.\u160 ?Hedyot\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kesef vs.\u160 ?Chazaka\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now turns to a new {\i mishnah}, again in archaic language: {\i reshus ha-gavoah be-kesef u-reshus ha-hedyot be-chazakah}. The {\i mishnah} uses the older terminology of {\i reshus gavoah} \u8212- the domain of the Most High \u8212- for what is elsewhere called {\i hekdesh}, and {\i reshus hedyot} \u8212- the domain of the ordinary person \u8212- for what is called {\i chulin}, non-consecrated property. {\i Hekdesh} acquires through money ({\i kesef}); a layperson acquires through {\i chazakah} \u8212- meaning physical possession, which in practice means {\i meshicha} for {\i metaltelin} and {\i chazakah} for {\i karka}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Amirah Principle\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} adds a further statement: {\i amiraso le-gavoah ki-mesiraso le-hedyot} \u8212- one\u8217's verbal declaration to the Most High is equivalent to physical delivery to an ordinary person. If I declare an ox to be a {\i korban olah}, or declare that my house is {\i hekdesh}, those words immediately transfer the status and ownership of the object to the {\i Beit HaMikdash}, without any physical act of {\i kinyan} being required. Contrast this with a transfer to another person, which requires either {\i meshicha} (for {\i metaltelin}) or {\i chazakah} (for {\i karka}).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gizbar Example\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Tanna\u8217'im} expand on the principle of {\i reshus gavoah be-kesef}. A {\i braita} teaches: {\i gizbar she-nasan ma\u8217'ot be-behemah} \u8212- a Temple treasurer who paid money for an animal acquires that animal for {\i hekdesh} immediately upon payment, {\i afilu behemah be-sof ha-olam kanah} \u8212- even if the animal is at the other end of the world. By contrast, {\i u-ve-hedyot lo kanah ad she-yimshoch} \u8212- an ordinary person does not acquire until he performs {\i meshicha}. The application of {\i kinyan kesef} to {\i hekdesh} is thus unlimited by the physical proximity of the object.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kedusha & Ownership\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might initially ask whether this rule serves {\i hekdesh}\u8217's interests. After all, we noted earlier that the reason the Sages replaced {\i kinyan kesef} with {\i kinyan meshicha} for ordinary transactions was to protect the buyer: if the buyer has already paid but not yet taken possession, and the seller has no incentive to safeguard the goods, the buyer bears the loss when something goes wrong. Does the same concern not apply to {\i hekdesh}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This question sets up the discussion that follows. The {\i mishnah}\u8217's statement that {\i amiraso le-gavoah ki-mesiraso le-hedyot} reflects a broader point about the nature of {\i hekdesh} property. When an animal or house is designated as {\i hekdesh}, two distinct legal dimensions are implicated: the {\i kedusha} \u8212- the sacred status \u8212- and the ownership. By a {\i korban}, such as an animal declared an {\i olah}, the primary dimension is the sacred status: it is not fundamentally a transaction of ownership that drives the designation, but the imposition of a new intrinsic status on the object. One who misuses {\i hekdesh} property violates the prohibition of {\i meilah} \u8212- misappropriation of sacred property \u8212- which is about the violation of the sacred status, not merely a property offense.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But when someone declares his house {\i hekdesh}, the primary intent is typically to make a donation to the {\i Beit HaMikdash}. There the ownership dimension is more central. Yet even there, the Torah derives from the verse {\i ish ki yakdish et beito kodesh la-Shem} \u8212- a man who declares his house holy to God \u8212- that the verbal designation alone effects both the sacred status and the transfer of ownership simultaneously. This is the power of {\i amirah le-gavoah}: words that create {\i kedusha} are also words that transfer ownership, and no separate physical act is required.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Price Changes\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mashchu Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now explores a set of cases involving fluctuating market prices in the context of {\i hekdesh} transactions, and these cases reveal the precise way in which {\i hekdesh}\u8217's acquisition rules are structured in its favor.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The first case: {\i mashchu be-maneh, ve-lo hispik lifdoto ad she-amad be-masayim} \u8212- a buyer did {\i meshicha} on an item priced at a hundred from {\i hekdesh}, but before he paid the money ({\i pidyon} \u8212- the redemption payment that removes the {\i kedusha} from the object), the market price rose to two hundred. Who owns the object?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Since {\i hekdesh} acquires through {\i kesef}, and no money has yet been paid, {\i hekdesh} still owns the object despite the buyer having performed {\i meshicha}. The {\i meshicha} accomplishes nothing: it does not finalize the transfer from {\i hekdesh} because {\i hekdesh} does not go by {\i meshicha}. Consequently, {\i hekdesh} is entitled to demand {\i masayim} \u8212- two hundred \u8212- before releasing the object. The buyer cannot claim the object at the original agreed price. The source is {\i ve-nasan ha-kesef ve-kam lo} \u8212- the object is acquired when the money is given.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Padahu Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The second case is the inverse: {\i mashchu be-masayim, ve-lo hispik lifdoto ad she-amad be-maneh} \u8212- the buyer did {\i meshicha} when the item was worth two hundred, and before paying, the price dropped to a hundred. Now the buyer might reason: the sale was never final (no {\i kesef} has been paid), so I will only pay a hundred. The {\i halacha} is that he must pay two hundred. {\i Mah ta\u8217'ama? Lo yehei koach hedyot chamur me-hekdesh} \u8212- a layperson should not have greater legal force than {\i hekdesh}. If in an ordinary transaction the buyer could insist on a lower price after price fluctuation when the sale was not final, that would give the ordinary counterparty rights that {\i hekdesh} lacks. The rules are structured so that {\i hekdesh} is never placed at a disadvantage relative to a layperson.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The principle that emerges from the first two cases is that the {\i kinyanim} for {\i hekdesh} are stacked in {\i hekdesh}\u8217's favor. {\i Hekdesh} uses whichever mode of acquisition \u8212- {\i kesef} or {\i meshicha} \u8212- works to its benefit in the specific situation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mihu She-Para\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The third case reverses the starting point: {\i padahu be-masayim, ve-lo hispik limshacho ad she-amad be-maneh} \u8212- the buyer paid two hundred in cash but had not yet performed {\i meshicha} when the price dropped to a hundred. The obvious implication of {\i hekdesh}\u8217's ownership rules: {\i kinyan kesef} was effected by payment, and so the buyer owns the object and cannot demand a refund. He paid two hundred, the sale is complete, and the lower price is irrelevant.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But the next case is surprising. {\i Padahu be-maneh, ve-lo hispik limshacho ad she-amad be-masayim} \u8212- the buyer paid a hundred in cash, and before performing {\i meshicha}, the price rose to two hundred. One might expect {\i hekdesh} to invoke {\i meshicha}: since {\i meshicha} was never performed, and {\i hekdesh} can use whichever {\i kinyan} is most advantageous, perhaps {\i hekdesh} can now declare the sale incomplete on the basis that no {\i meshicha} was performed, and demand two hundred. The {\i gemara} rejects this.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Limits on Hekdesh\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Mah she-pada paduy} \u8212- what has been redeemed is redeemed. The payment of a hundred was effective, and {\i hekdesh} cannot retroactively demand more. The {\i gemara}\u8217's reasoning: {\i atu hedyot lav be-mi she-para kai?} \u8212- even in a transaction between ordinary people, if the buyer has paid money and the seller now wishes to back out before {\i meshicha} is performed, the seller does not escape consequence-free. The seller who reneges after receiving cash is subject to the {\i mi she-para} curse: may the One who exacted punishment upon the generation of the Flood and upon all those who violated their commitments exact punishment from one who does not stand by his word.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Hekdesh}, like an ordinary person, is bound by the moral commitment implicit in having received cash. Even though {\i hekdesh} retains the technical right to insist on {\i meshicha} as the completing act, it cannot exercise that right in circumstances where doing so would be seen as reneging on a commitment already evidenced by the exchange of money. As it was put: {\i yado shel hekdesh al ha-elyona} \u8212- the advantage is always with {\i hekdesh} \u8212- but that principle cannot be used to permit the {\i Beit HaMikdash} to act as a scoundrel. {\i Hekdesh} must honor its commitments, and once cash has been accepted, backing out on the basis of subsequent price increases falls within the conduct that even ordinary commercial morality forbids.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Kiddushin 29\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Mitzvot haben al av\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Overview & context\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Daf 29 of Kiddushin marks a significant transition in the sugya. We have been moving through an extended discussion of {\i kinyanim} \u8212- the various modes of acquisition \u8212- organized with the systematic, categorical precision that characterizes these early {\i mishnayot}. Having covered {\i kol hana\u8217'aseh damim be\u8217'acher} and {\i reshut gavoah}, the Mishnah now pivots entirely, bringing the same organizational rigor to bear on a different domain: {\i mitzvot} and the categories of obligation that define who must perform them.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishnah categories\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Mishnah on 29a opens with a characteristically compressed formulation \u8212- the sort of strange, almost elliptical locution one finds in particularly early {\i mishnayot}. It presents four categories, each defined by the intersection of the type of obligation and the gender of the obligated party.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The first category: {\i kol mitzvot haben al ha\u8217'av, anashim chayavin venashim peturot} \u8212- all the {\i mitzvot} of the child upon the father, men are obligated and women are exempt. The second: {\i vechol mitzvot ha\u8217'av al haben, echad anashim ve\u8217'echad nashim chayavin} \u8212- all the {\i mitzvot} of the father upon the child, whether men or women are equally obligated. The third: {\i vechol mitzvot aseh shehazman grama, anashim chayavin venashim peturot} \u8212- all positive {\i mitzvot} caused by time, men are obligated and women are exempt. The Rambam already notes that this rule has approximately as many exceptions as it has clear instances; whether it is a genuinely descriptive principle derived from the particulars or a prescriptive rule that dictates the particulars is itself a major question. The fourth category: {\i vechol mitzvot aseh shelo hazman grama, echad anashim ve\u8217'echad nashim chayavin} \u8212- non-time-bound positive {\i mitzvot} obligate men and women equally. And finally: {\i vechol mitzvot lo ta\u8217'aseh, bein shehazman grama bein shelo hazman grama, echad anashim ve\u8217'echad nashim chayavin} \u8212- all negative commandments, whether time-bound like {\i Shabbat} or not time-bound like the prohibition of {\i chazir}, obligate men and women equally, with three exceptions: {\i mibal takif}, {\i u\u8217'val tashchit}, and {\i u\u8217'val titama lametim}, the last of which applies specifically to male {\i kohanim}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gemara interprets\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara immediately asks: what does {\i kol mitzvot haben al ha\u8217'av} mean? If it means all the {\i mitzvot} that a child is obligated to perform toward a parent \u8212- that is, obligations running from child to parent \u8212- then why would women be exempt? That cannot be right, because a {\i braita} explicitly teaches otherwise. The verse states {\i ish immo ve\u8217'aviv tirau} \u8212- each man shall fear his mother and father \u8212- and the {\i Midrash Tannaim} derives: I know only that a man is obligated; how do I know a woman is as well? Because {\i tirau} is in the plural, implying two \u8212- both men and women are equally obligated in fearing and honoring their parents.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Yehuda therefore reframes the Mishnah: {\i kol mitzvot haben hamutallot alav} \u8212- the {\i mitzvot} of the child that are laid upon the parent, meaning parental obligations directed {\i toward} children. Those, says the Mishnah, are the obligations of fathers toward sons: {\i anashim chayavim venashim peturot}, men are obligated and women are exempt.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kibbud av va\u8217'em\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 With this clarification in place, the structure becomes clear. {\i Kibbud av va\u8217'em} \u8212- the child\u8217's obligation toward parents \u8212- falls under the second category: {\i echad anashim ve\u8217'echad nashim chayavin}. Both men and women, both boys and girls, adult children of all kinds, are equally obligated to honor both father and mother. The asymmetry in the Mishnah applies solely to the parental side: it is fathers, not mothers, who bear the specific duties toward sons that the Gemara is about to enumerate.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Braita: paternal duties\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Five obligations\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now turns to a {\i braita} that spells out concretely what these paternal obligations consist of. {\i Ha\u8217'av chayav bivno} \u8212- the father is obligated with respect to his son \u8212- in five specific ways: {\i lemolo}, to circumcise him; {\i ulfidoso}, to redeem him ({\i pidyon haben}); {\i ulilamedo Torah}, to teach him Torah; {\i ulehashio ishah}, to arrange his marriage; and {\i ulilamedo ummanus}, to teach him a trade. Some add a sixth: {\i af lehashito bamayim}, to teach him how to swim \u8212- a basic survival skill, the sort of competency that in earlier eras was genuinely life-saving.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is worth noting that the {\i braita} does not mention the general obligation of {\i chinuch} \u8212- training a child to perform {\i mitzvot}, to recite {\i brachot}, to shake a {\i lulav}, to light candles, to observe negative commandments. Yet other sources make clear that {\i chinuch} is also primarily the father\u8217's obligation, though there is some discussion as to whether the mother shares in it as well.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Yehuda on trade\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yehuda sharpens the obligation of teaching a trade with a striking formulation: {\i kol she\u8217'eino melamed es beno ummanus melamdo listus} \u8212- anyone who does not teach his son a trade is as if he teaches him banditry. The Gemara immediately clarifies: surely no one actually teaches their child banditry. Rather, {\i ke\u8217'ilu melamdo listus} \u8212- it is {\i as if} he did, because by failing to provide his son with a legitimate livelihood, he leaves him with no honest means of sustaining himself. The hyperbole carries a moral charge \u8212- apparently the obligation to teach one\u8217's son a trade was not being taken seriously enough, and Rabbi Yehuda found it necessary to dramatize the stakes.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Brit Milah analysis\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Father\u8217's obligation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now unpacks each obligation in turn. {\i Lemolo} \u8212- where do we know that circumcision is the father\u8217's obligation? From the verse {\i vayamol Avraham es Yitzchak beno} \u8212- Abraham circumcised Isaac his son. Abraham\u8217's act establishes the paradigm. As for how we know this applies to future generations, the school of Rabbi Ishmael will provide the answer shortly.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Fallback obligations\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then establishes a hierarchy of fallback obligations. {\i Veheicha d\u8217'la malye avuha mechayavei bei dina lemimalye} \u8212- if the father has not circumcised his son, the {\i beis din} becomes obligated, because the verse says {\i himmol lachem kol zachar} \u8212- you shall have circumcised for you all males \u8212- addressing the community collectively. The community bears responsibility to ensure that all Jewish males are circumcised; if the father fails, that obligation reverts to the public.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If {\i beis din} likewise fails to act and the child reaches adulthood uncircumcised, {\i mechayev ihu lemimhal nafshei} \u8212- he is now obligated to circumcise himself, derived from {\i ve\u8217'arel zachar asher lo yimmol besar orlaso venichresa} \u8212- an uncircumcised male who does not circumcise his foreskin shall be cut off. The very fact that he bears punishment for failure to circumcise demonstrates that he carries a personal obligation to do so.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A notable feature of this hierarchy is that it moves from father to community to self \u8212- the mother is not named as an intermediate or backup obligant. This stands in contrast to the story of Tzipporah, who performed the {\i bris milah} herself. It is important to distinguish between two separate questions: who is capable of executing the act (which the Tzipporah story addresses and which the Gemara in Avodah Zarah discusses), and who bears the halakhic obligation to ensure that the act is performed (which is our question here). On the latter question, the mother appears to be written out of the hierarchy entirely. There is, however, a Rashi elsewhere and a {\i Mareh Or Zarua} suggesting that the mother does bear an obligation after the father \u8212- which raises interesting practical questions, such as whether a single mother performing the {\i milah} for her son may recite the {\i bracha} of {\i lehachniso bivriso shel Avraham Avinu} in her own right as mother, or perhaps only as a representative of the community standing in place of {\i beis din}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Women excluded\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 How do we know that the mother bears no obligation? {\i D\u8217'ksiv kaasher tziva oso Elohim} \u8212- because the verse says as God commanded {\i him}, Abraham \u8212- {\i oso v\u8217'lo osa}: him and not her, not Sarah.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tanaic proof\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But this is the verse about Abraham and Sarah in their own generation. How do we know it serves as the binding model for future generations? The school of Rabbi Ishmael taught: {\i kol makom shenemar tzav eino ela zeirus miyad ul\u8217'doros} \u8212- whenever the Torah uses the root {\i tzav} (command), it signals both urgency \u8212- encouraging alacrity in performance \u8212- and applicability to all future generations. This is demonstrated by two verses: {\i v\u8217'tzav es Yehoshua v\u8217'chazkeihu v\u8217'amtzeihu} (command Joshua, strengthen and encourage him) illustrates the urgency component, and {\i min hayom asher tziva Hashem v\u8217'halah l\u8217'doroseichem} (from the day God commanded and onward, for your generations) establishes the multigenerational dimension. Since the verse about Abraham\u8217's circumcision of Isaac uses {\i tziva}, the exclusion of the mother encoded there \u8212- {\i oso v\u8217'lo osa} \u8212- carries forward to all generations.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Pidyon HaBen\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hierarchy of duty\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara turns to {\i pidyon haben}. The source is {\i kol bechor banecha tifdeh} \u8212- all the firstborn of your sons you shall redeem. As with {\i bris milah}, a hierarchy applies: the father redeems the son, and if the father does not, {\i michayiv ihu l\u8217'mifreikeih l\u8217'nafsheih} \u8212- the son must redeem himself as an adult, derived from {\i padoh tifdeh} \u8212- you shall surely redeem, implying a personal obligation if the parental one goes unfulfilled.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One significant difference from the {\i bris milah} hierarchy is worth noting: unlike circumcision, there is no communal obligation that all firstborn sons be redeemed. {\i Pidyon haben} is an obligation running between father and son; if the father fails, the obligation passes directly to the son himself, without an intermediate stage of communal responsibility.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Bidirectional logic\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 How do we know that a woman is not obligated to redeem her son? The Gemara reads {\i tifdeh tipadeh} \u8212- you shall redeem, you shall be redeemed \u8212- as establishing a bidirectional principle: {\i kol shemitzveh lifdos es atzmo mitzveh lifdos es acherim} \u8212- whoever is obligated to redeem themselves is obligated to redeem others; {\i v\u8217'chol she\u8217'eino mitzveh lifdos es atzmo eino mitzveh lifdos acherim} \u8212- whoever is not obligated to redeem themselves is not obligated to redeem others. Since a woman is not subject to redemption herself, she is therefore not obligated to redeem her son.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Logical deduction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But this raises a prior question: how do we know a woman is not obligated to redeem herself? The Gemara applies the same principle in the other direction: {\i kol she\u8217'acherim mtzuvim lifdoso mitzveh lifdos es atzmo} \u8212- if others are obligated to redeem you, you are obligated to redeem yourself. Conversely, {\i v\u8217'chol she\u8217'ein acherim mtzuvim lifdoso eino mitzveh lifdos es atzmo} \u8212- if no one else is obligated to redeem you, you are not obligated to redeem yourself. The child\u8217's obligation to self-redeem exists precisely because the parent\u8217's obligation went unfulfilled; where no parental obligation existed in the first place, no self-redemption obligation can arise.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Daughters excluded\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This brings us to the foundation of the entire chain: how do we know that a daughter\u8217's parents are not obligated to redeem her? From the verse itself \u8212- {\i kol bechor banecha tifdeh} \u8212- {\i banecha v\u8217'lo bnosecha}: your sons and not your daughters. Daughters are not subjects of {\i pidyon haben}; therefore no one is obligated to redeem them; therefore they are not obligated to redeem themselves; and therefore they are not obligated to redeem others, including their own sons.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The logic works neatly from the end backward. {\i Banecha v\u8217'lo bnosecha} establishes that daughters are not redeemed. {\i Tifdeh tipadeh} then establishes, in one direction, that those not obligated to be redeemed are not obligated to redeem themselves, and in the other direction, that those not obligated to redeem themselves are not obligated to redeem others. The entire structure of exclusion rests on the initial textual exclusion of daughters from the category of those who must be redeemed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Self vs.\u160 ?son debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Self first\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now presents a new {\i braita}: {\i hu lifdot u\u8217'vno lifdot, hu kodem livno} \u8212- if a man must redeem himself (because his father did not do so) and must also redeem his son (as a father), and he lacks the resources to do both, he redeems himself first. This position rests on the principle that one\u8217's own personal obligation takes precedence: {\i mitzva degufei adifa} \u8212- an obligation relating to one\u8217's own body or status comes first. There is also a chronological logic: presumably the obligation to redeem oneself predates the birth of one\u8217's son.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Yehuda: son first\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yehuda disagrees: {\i beno kodmo} \u8212- his son comes first. The conceptual argument is elegant. The father\u8217's personal obligation to be redeemed was {\i originally} on his father; his father failed to fulfill it. The obligation that falls {\i natively} on this man \u8212- the one that is properly his own \u8212- is his obligation to his son. Thinking in generational terms: Abraham\u8217's obligation was to Isaac; Isaac\u8217's obligation was to Jacob. If we are speaking of Isaac, his primary obligation as a father is to Jacob, and his obligation to himself exists only as a derivative of his own father\u8217's failure.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Five sela\u8217'im case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Yirmiyahu then reframes the entire dispute, revealing that it is more limited in scope than it initially appears. He says: {\i hakol modim} \u8212- everyone agrees \u8212- that if there are only five {\i sela\u8217'im} available (enough for only one {\i pidyon haben}), {\i hu kodem livno} \u8212- he redeems himself first. {\i Mitzva degufei adifa} \u8212- one\u8217's personal obligation takes precedence. This is not what the Gemara seemed to be saying before, but that is the resolution: the dispute between the first opinion and Rabbi Yehuda does not concern the case where there is only enough for one payment.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ten sela\u8217'im lien\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The dispute, Rav Yirmiyahu explains, concerns a case where there are ten {\i sela\u8217'im} \u8212- five in cash and five in encumbered property (property upon which creditors hold a lien). The question turns on a fundamental halakhic principle: {\i milveh haktuva baTorah kaktuva bishtar damya} \u8212- does a Torah obligation create a lien on property, just as a written loan document ({\i shtar}) does?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yehuda holds that it does. On this view, from the moment this man became obligated to redeem himself (say, from Bar Mitzvah), the {\i kohanim} collectively acquired a lien on his property for five {\i sela\u8217'im}. He subsequently borrowed money from another creditor, Reuven, who also acquired a lien on the same property \u8212- but later in time. Rabbi Yehuda therefore says: use the five {\i sela\u8217'im} in cash to redeem your son (fulfilling that obligation first), and the {\i kohen} will then collect the five {\i sela\u8217'im} from the encumbered property, displacing Reuven\u8217's later lien, thus satisfying both obligations. As Rashi explains, the father is entitled to direct his liquid assets to his son\u8217's redemption because his son\u8217's obligation is primary; it then works out that the {\i kohanim} can enforce their earlier lien against the encumbered property, leaving Reuven, unfortunately, unable to collect. Rashi\u8217's reading, as Tosafot notes, creates some tension \u8212- the rationale invoked to justify directing cash to the son (son\u8217's obligation is primary) seems to cut against the very premise that self comes first \u8212- and Tosafot offers an alternative reading of the Gemara accordingly.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Rabbis hold that {\i milveh haktuva baTorah lav kikesuvah bishtar damya} \u8212- a Torah obligation does not create a property lien. Therefore the {\i kohanim} have no prior lien, the five encumbered {\i sela\u8217'im} are of no help to them, and the man must simply choose between redeeming himself and redeeming his son with whatever cash he has. In that situation, {\i mitzvas degufei adif} \u8212- his own obligation takes precedence.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Pidyon vs.\u160 ?Regel\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Son redeemed first\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara presents another case of competing obligations: {\i lifdos es beno v\u8217'la\u8217'alos laregel} \u8212- if one must choose between redeeming his son and making the pilgrimage ({\i aliyah laregel}) to the Temple. The majority view: {\i podeh es beno v\u8217'achar kach oleh laregel} \u8212- redeem your son first, then make the pilgrimage.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Yehuda rationale\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yehuda, in what at first looks like a reversal of his earlier position (where he prioritized the son), now says the opposite: {\i oleh laregel v\u8217'achar kach podeh es beno} \u8212- go on pilgrimage first, then redeem your son. But here there is no contradiction with his earlier ruling, because this is not a question of self versus son but of two distinct obligations. His reasoning: {\i shezu mitzvah overes v\u8217'zu mitzvah einah overes} \u8212- {\i aliyah laregel} is a time-bound obligation that will pass; if you miss the {\i regel}, you cannot make it up. {\i Pidyon haben} can be performed later when you acquire the means.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Verse order logic\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara is satisfied with Rabbi Yehuda\u8217's rationale. But what is the Rabbis\u8217' rationale for prioritizing {\i pidyon haben}? {\i De\u8217'amar kra kol bechor banecha tifdeh, v\u8217'hadar v\u8217'lo yera\u8217'u fanai reikam} \u8212- the Torah places the verse of redemption before the verse commanding the pilgrimage offering. The sequential order of the {\i pesukim} itself dictates the priority: first take care of your son\u8217's redemption, then address the pilgrimage.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Multiple firstborns\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Plural sons proof\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A new {\i braita} asks: how do we know that if a man has five firstborn sons from five different wives, he is obligated to redeem all of them? From {\i kol bechor banecha tifdeh} \u8212- the word {\i kol} (all) indicates that the obligation extends to multiple firstborn sons.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Peter rechem logic\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might think this is obvious \u8212- surely if you have five sons each of whom is a {\i bechor}, you redeem all five. But there is a genuine question here. {\i Peter rechem tala Rachmana} \u8212- the Torah defines {\i bechor} status by reference to the opening of the womb, making it a status belonging to the mother\u8217's womb, not the father\u8217's lineage. So each of these five sons is indeed a {\i bechor} to his respective mother. The question is whether one also applies the principle of {\i reishis ono} \u8212- the first of his strength \u8212- drawn from the laws of inheritance, where {\i bechor} status is determined by the father. If both conditions were required, a man who already has a son from one wife could never have another {\i bechor} from a subsequent wife. The Gemara teaches: {\i ka mashma lan} \u8212- no, {\i bechor} for purposes of {\i pidyon haben} is determined solely by the mother. Hence a man may have multiple sons who each independently carries {\i bechor} status, and he is obligated to redeem each of them.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Talmud Torah obligation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Father teaches son\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now turns to the obligation of {\i Talmud Torah}. The father is obligated to teach his son Torah, derived from {\i v\u8217'limadtem osam es beneichem} \u8212- you shall teach your sons. If the father does not teach his son, {\i mechayav ihu lemigmar nafsheih} \u8212- the son, upon becoming an adult, is obligated to learn on his own. This is derived from {\i ulimadtem} \u8212- you shall learn \u8212- read not as {\i v\u8217'limadtem} (you shall teach) but as an obligation to learn, implying that if no one taught you, you must teach yourself.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sons not daughters\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 How do we know the obligation is specifically toward sons and not daughters? From {\i v\u8217'limadetem osam es beneichem} \u8212- {\i banecha v\u8217'lo bnosecha}: your sons and not your daughters.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Learn-teach logic\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But this only establishes that there is no obligation to teach daughters. How do we derive the mother\u8217's exemption from the obligation to teach Torah altogether? The Gemara constructs the same bidirectional logic used for {\i pidyon haben} and {\i bris milah}. {\i Kol shemetzuveh lilmod metzuveh lelamed} \u8212- whoever is obligated to learn is obligated to teach. {\i V\u8217'chol she\u8217'eino metzuveh lilmod eino metzuveh lelamed} \u8212- whoever is not obligated to learn is not obligated to teach.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Women\u8217's exemption\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 And how do we know a woman is not obligated to learn Torah herself? The same structure applies in the other direction: {\i kol she\u8217'heim metzuvim lelamdo az metzuveh lelamed es atzmo} \u8212- if others are obligated to teach you, you are obligated to teach yourself. {\i Kol she\u8217'ein heim metzuvim lelamdo ein metzuveh lelamed es atzmo} \u8212- if others are not obligated to teach you, you are not obligated to teach yourself. And since {\i v\u8217'limadetem osam es beneichem} \u8212- {\i banecha v\u8217'lo bnosecha} \u8212- others are not obligated to teach daughters Torah, daughters are therefore not obligated to teach themselves, and therefore not obligated to teach others.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Modern discourse\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is, of course, the foundational {\i Gemara} for one of the most consequential halakhic debates of the modern era. Beginning in the 1950s \u8212- with Rav Soloveitchik on one side and the Lubavitcher Rebbe among others on another \u8212- and continuing through decades of responsa and scholarly discussion, the question of women\u8217's exemption from {\i Talmud Torah} has generated an enormous body of literature. Many approaches have been proposed: drawing distinctions between {\i Torah she\u8217'bichsav} and {\i Torah she\u8217'be\u8217'al peh}; arguing that even if women are exempt from the pure {\i mitzvah} of {\i limud Torah} for its own sake ({\i Torah lishmah}), they are obligated to learn all the Torah necessary for the observance of the many {\i mitzvot} they do perform \u8212- which amounts to a great deal of Torah; and arguing further that learning Torah is an integral part of one\u8217's basic relationship with {\i Hakadosh Baruch Hu} and religious life generally, leaving women\u8217's de facto learning obligation not so different in scope from men\u8217's. One area where the tension becomes particularly acute is when both spouses wish to devote themselves entirely to full-time Torah study \u8212- at which point the technical contours of the exemption become practically significant.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sinai context\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is worth noting the broader {\i midrashic} and {\i peshat} context that complicates any simple application of this technical exemption. The Torah in {\i Devarim} repeatedly emphasizes that at {\i Ma\u8217'amad Har Sinai} everyone was present: men, women, and children. {\i Chazal} themselves underscore this: {\i koh tomar l\u8217'veis Yaakov v\u8217'sageid livnei Yisrael} \u8212- the {\i Midrash} reads {\i l\u8217'veis Yaakov} as referring specifically to the women, who were addressed at Sinai first. Similarly, the requirement of {\i Hakhel} explicitly gathers {\i anashim nashim v\u8217'hataf} \u8212- men, women, and children \u8212- to hear the Torah read. These sources create a picture of women\u8217's deep and primary engagement with Torah that sits in genuine tension with the formal technical exemption, and it is this tension that animates the ongoing debate. The statement in {\i Sotah} about a father who teaches his daughter Torah \u8212- {\i k\u8217'ilu melamdah tiflus} \u8212- has also been subject to extensive analysis, with many authorities drawing careful distinctions about what precisely that statement prohibits and what it permits or even encourages.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Self vs.\u160 ?son Torah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Self first\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now applies the same self-versus-son framework to {\i Talmud Torah}. If a man has not been fully educated in Torah and must choose between studying himself and providing for his son\u8217's Torah education, {\i hu kodem livno} \u8212- he comes before his son.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Yehuda condition\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yehuda refines this: {\i im beno zariz ummulach u\u8217'tamudo mitkayem beyado, beno kodmo} \u8212- if his son is sharp, subtle, and retains what he learns, then his son\u8217's learning takes precedence. The underlying principle here is not simply a question of whose obligation is primary but of which investment will yield the greater result. When father and son are equivalently capable, the father\u8217's own obligation to himself comes first. But when the son clearly has superior capacity \u8212- when his learning will be more productive and enduring \u8212- then directing resources toward the son\u8217's education takes priority.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Yaakov story\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara illustrates this with the story of Rav Yaakov, the son of Rav Acha bar Yaakov. Rav Acha sent his son Rav Yaakov to learn from Abaye. When Rav Acha subsequently came to visit, {\i chazi d\u8217'lo havi mechadidun shmatei} \u8212- he saw that his son\u8217's learning was not sharp; the material was not crisp and precise in his grasp. Rav Acha said to him: {\i ana adif minach, tuv at d\u8217'eizal ana} \u8212- I can learn better than you; you stay, I will go learn. The story illustrates that the default assumption had been to send the son \u8212- but once the father assessed that his own learning would be more productive, he reasserted his own priority. This is somewhat different from the pure formulation of Rabbi Yehuda\u8217's position: rather than saying \u8220"I will send my son unless I find I am better,\u8221" Rav Acha defaulted to sending his son and reversed course only upon discovering his son\u8217's deficiency.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Demon episode\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then appends a narrative connected to the same setting. {\i Shama Abaye d\u8217'ka havi ati} \u8212- Abaye heard that Rav Acha the father was coming. There was a {\i mazik} \u8212- a malevolent spirit \u8212- in Abaye\u8217's {\i beit midrash}, {\i d\u8217'chi havi ailei bitrei afilu biyemama havi mitzkei} \u8212- such that even when two people entered together, even during the daytime (when such spirits are normally constrained), they were harmed. Abaye instructed that no one should provide Rav Acha with lodging, {\i efshar d\u8217'mitracheish nisa} \u8212- perhaps a miracle would occur. Rav Acha was accordingly forced to sleep in the {\i beit midrash}. The {\i mazik} appeared to him in the form of a seven-headed serpent. With each prostration that Rav Acha made in prayer, one of the heads fell off \u8212- {\i kol kria d\u8217'kara, nassar chad reishei} \u8212- until the spirit was vanquished. In the morning he told the students: {\i ilo lo itrachish nisan sakintan} \u8212- had a miracle not occurred, you would have endangered me by forcing me to sleep there.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Summary\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Duties recap\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The sugya of 29a covers, with remarkable density, the core obligations a father bears toward his son: {\i bris milah}, {\i pidyon haben}, {\i Talmud Torah}, arranging marriage, and teaching a trade. In each case, the obligation is directed specifically from father to son \u8212- not from mother to son, not from father to daughter, and not from mother to daughter.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gender patterns\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The textual grounds for excluding women and daughters follow a consistent pattern throughout the {\i sugya}. For {\i bris milah}, the exclusion derives from {\i kaasher tziva oso} \u8212- commanding him alone \u8212- extended to all generations by the principle that {\i tzav} implies future applicability. For {\i pidyon haben}, the chain runs through {\i banecha v\u8217'lo bnosecha} to {\i tifdeh tipadeh} and back, locking women out on both sides of the redemption relationship. For {\i Talmud Torah}, {\i banecha v\u8217'lo bnosecha} generates an analogous chain excluding women from the obligation to be taught, to teach themselves, and to teach their sons. There exist minority opinions \u8212- a Rashi and the {\i Mareh Or Zarua} \u8212- suggesting that the mother may carry at least a backup or secondary obligation in {\i bris milah}, which opens interesting questions for edge cases; but the dominant framework throughout this {\i sugya} is one of consistent textual exclusion.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Balancing rules\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Running through all of these obligations is a second layer of analysis: how to balance competing duties when resources are limited. The tension between one\u8217's personal obligation and one\u8217's filial obligation is resolved differently in different cases. For {\i pidyon haben}, the baseline rule is that one\u8217's personal obligation ({\i mitzva degufei adifa}) comes first \u8212- though Rabbi Yehuda\u8217's position introduces the generational framing that complicates this, and Rav Yirmiyahu\u8217's reconciliation confines the dispute to the case of ten {\i sela\u8217'im} with encumbered property. For {\i Talmud Torah}, personal study generally comes first, but Rabbi Yehuda introduces the aptitude condition \u8212- if the son\u8217's capacity is superior, the son\u8217's learning takes precedence \u8212- illustrated concretely by the story of Rav Acha and his son. And when {\i pidyon haben} competes not with personal obligation but with {\i aliyah laregel}, the majority prioritizes the non-repeatable obligation of redemption, while Rabbi Yehuda prioritizes the time-sensitive {\i mitzvah overes} of the {\i regel}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Kiddushin 30\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Torah vs.\u160 ?Marriage\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tanu Rabbanan\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The daf under discussion is daf 30, and we continue in the middle of a rich and multifaceted {\i mishnah} dealing with a father\u8217's obligations to his son. The preceding material covered {\i bris milah}, {\i pidyon haben}, and {\i talmud Torah}, and the shiur now turns to continue exploring {\i talmud Torah} and related themes.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} on 29b opens with a {\i beraita} introduced by {\i tanu rabbanan}: between learning {\i Torah} and getting married \u8212- {\i lilmod Torah v\u8217'lisa isha} \u8212- which takes priority? This question is no longer about the parental obligation per se, but about competing priorities a young man faces, analogous to earlier questions such as whether one redeems oneself or one\u8217's son first, or whether one teaches oneself or one\u8217's son first.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Torah First\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i beraita}\u8217's answer is unambiguous: {\i yilmod Torah v\u8217'achar kach yisa isha} \u8212- first learn {\i Torah}, and only afterward get married. The reasoning is implicit but clear: once married, a man takes on a range of obligations and responsibilities that will inevitably compromise the time and energy available for {\i Torah} study. Marriage is a weighty undertaking, and one should therefore front-load one\u8217's {\i Torah} learning before assuming those burdens.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It should be noted that implicit in this statement is an assumption of a lifelong, ongoing responsibility to learn. The {\i gemara} had previously described the father\u8217's obligation to educate his son as a kind of foundational primary education \u8212- and indeed noted that if the father failed to teach, the son must teach himself. Yet the broader {\i ethos} of the rabbis is captured in {\i vehagita bo yomam valailah} \u8212- constant, perpetual engagement with {\i Torah}. The verse {\i v\u8217'shinnantam l\u8217'vanecha v\u8217'dibarta bam b\u8217'shivtecha b\u8217'vetecha u\u8217'v\u8217'lechtecha vaderech} gestures toward this unceasing obligation, even if its precise {\i halachic} parameters have not yet been fully articulated. The priority given to {\i Torah} over marriage reflects this assumption: one should be learning as much as possible, growing as much as possible, before taking on responsibilities that will curtail that growth.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Exception\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i beraita} acknowledges a qualification: {\i v\u8217'im iy efshar lo b\u8217'lo isha} \u8212- if it is truly impossible for him to manage without a wife, whether for emotional reasons or, as the {\i gemara} will make explicit shortly, because of the pressure of sexual thoughts ({\i hirhurei aveira}), then {\i yisa isha v\u8217'achar yilmod Torah} \u8212- he may marry first and learn afterward. The hierarchy is not absolute. If a man genuinely needs to marry in order to function, that need takes precedence, but the ideal sequence remains {\i Torah} first. It is worth noting that the obligation to keep learning is ongoing regardless of when one marries.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Shmuel vs.\u160 ?Yochanan\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Marriage First\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then records a striking ruling: {\i Amar Rav Yehuda amar Shmuel} \u8212- the {\i halacha} is {\i nosei isha v\u8217'achar yilmod Torah}, marry first, then learn {\i Torah}. This is the direct opposite of the {\i beraita} just cited. The question immediately arises: how can Shmuel issue a ruling against an explicit {\i beraita}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Millstone\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yochanan, by contrast, sides with the {\i beraita}, and expresses his position with a memorable image: {\i reichaim b\u8217'tzavaro v\u8217'ya\u8217'asok baTorah?} \u8212- you have a millstone around your neck and you think you can learn {\i Torah}? Once a man shoulders the {\i ol parnassah}, the yoke of family responsibilities and the need to earn a living, when will he ever find time to learn? Therefore, {\i Torah} must come first; marriage, with all its weight, must wait.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 V\u8217'lo Pligi\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} resolves the apparent contradiction: {\i v\u8217'lo pligi} \u8212- they do not actually disagree. {\i Ha lan v\u8217'ha lahu} \u8212- this ruling applies to us, and that ruling applies to them. Shmuel\u8217's ruling is directed at the Jews of Babylonia, while Rabbi Yochanan\u8217's ruling is for the inhabitants of {\i Eretz Yisrael}. This resolution requires explanation, and {\i Rashi} provides it.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Bavel vs.\u160 ?EY\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rashi\u8217's Explanation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rashi}\u8217's explanation is unquestionably the {\i pshat}. In Babylonia, scholars would leave home and travel to {\i Eretz Yisrael} to study {\i Torah}, and in doing so they would effectively abandon their wives and children for extended periods. The {\i gemara} at the beginning of {\i Gittin} records this phenomenon vividly: Rabbi Chiya bar Abba, writing from {\i Eretz Yisrael} to those in Babylonia whose husbands had come to learn and left their families behind, invokes the verse {\i vayitnu et hayeled b\u8217'zonah v\u8217'et hayaldah machru bayayin} \u8212- they have handed their children over to prostitution, as the abandoned families were left without support or supervision. The picture is stark: the pursuit of {\i Torah} in {\i Eretz Yisrael} sometimes came at an enormous cost to family life back in Babylonia.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Babylonian Model\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Beyond the geographic dynamic, there is a deeper conceptual difference in how the two communities understood the value of marriage itself. Many Babylonian {\i gemaras} describe the value of marriage in instrumental terms: marriage protects the husband from sin, provides an outlet for desire, frees him from domestic distraction so he can learn {\i Torah}, and supplies a kind of domestic infrastructure supporting his learning. In this framework, marriage serves the man\u8217's {\i Torah} study. It is thus consistent that in Babylonia the ruling would be to marry first: get married, satisfy your needs, and then go and learn {\i Torah} \u8212- even if that means eventually leaving your wife and household for extended periods.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Monogamy/Polygamy\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This difference in the conceptualization of marriage is also bound up with a broader sociological distinction. Babylonia, influenced by the surrounding Sassanid culture and still reflecting the biblical model, maintained polygamy, at least among the wealthy. {\i Eretz Yisrael}, shaped by Roman cultural norms, had moved toward monogamy. This historical difference has long-term consequences: Babylonian Jewish culture eventually flowed into {\i Sefarad}, where Jews lived among Muslims, and polygamy persisted in those communities. {\i Eretz Yisrael}\u8217's culture flowed into {\i Ashkenaz}, embedded in Roman-Christian lands, where monogamy was the norm. This divergence is reflected in the {\i Yerushalmi}, which essentially assumes monogamy, while the {\i Bavli} assumes polygamy.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 When marriage is understood as exclusively between one man and one woman \u8212- a genuine partnership \u8212- one thinks of it in terms of mutual obligation and intrinsic value. When the surrounding culture permits multiple wives, the perception of marriage shifts: a wife is more a member of the household than a full partner, and marriage is more easily framed in utilitarian terms.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 EY Model\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 In {\i Eretz Yisrael}, by contrast, the {\i gemara}s describe marriage in far more intrinsic terms. A man who is not married lacks {\i simcha}, lacks {\i shalom}, is in some fundamental sense incomplete. Marriage is a primary responsibility in itself, not merely a means to some other end. It follows that once a man in {\i Eretz Yisrael} marries, those obligations become paramount \u8212- {\i reichaim b\u8217'tzavaro}, as Rabbi Yochanan puts it. You cannot then simply leave your wife and go learn {\i Torah}. Therefore, the prescription for {\i Eretz Yisrael} is: learn your {\i Torah} first, because once you are married, that is your primary responsibility and it cannot be subordinated to learning. In Babylonia, by contrast, the attitude was more permissive: marry first, but one may still leave and devote long stretches of time to {\i Torah} study, since family obligations do not hold the same absolute priority.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rashi} reads the {\i gemara} this way straightforwardly. {\i Tosafot}, scandalized by what seems to be an endorsement of abandoning one\u8217's wife and children, attempts a complicated re-reading of the {\i gemara} \u8212- but {\i Rashi}\u8217's reading is clearly the {\i pshat}. The tension this generates \u8212- between the all-consuming obligation of {\i talmud Torah} and a man\u8217's obligations to his family \u8212- remains unresolved in the {\i gemara} and is, of course, entirely alive today in communities where husbands learn in {\i kollel} while wives work and fathers are substantially absent from domestic life. The {\i Rambam} wrote sharply in his {\i Peirush HaMishnayot} on {\i Pirkei Avot} against making {\i Torah} into a {\i kardom lachpor bo} \u8212- a shovel to dig with \u8212- and against communal financial support for scholars. Yet the trade-off between {\i talmud Torah} and family responsibility is precisely what this {\i gemara} identifies as the defining difference between Babylonia and {\i Eretz Yisrael}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Marriage Age\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Huna Story\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} continues within this general context of {\i talmud Torah} and marriage. The following story: {\i Rav Chisda} was in conversation with {\i Rav Huna}, singing the praises of {\i Rav Hamnuna} \u8212- {\i adam gadol hu}, a tremendous {\i talmid chacham}. {\i Rav Huna} replied: the next time he is with you, bring him to me. When {\i Rav Hamnuna} was presented to {\i Rav Huna}, {\i Rav Huna} noticed that he was not wearing a {\i sudra} \u8212- the kind of turban or head-covering worn by married men. He asked: {\i mai ta\u8217'ama lo parist sudra?} Why are you not wearing this? {\i Rav Hamnuna} replied: {\i de-lo nessivna} \u8212- because I am not yet married. At that, {\i Rav Huna} turned his face away and refused to look at him: {\i ad nessivta} \u8212- I will not look at you until you are married. The gesture was dramatic, even harsh.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ben Esrim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} explains {\i Rav Huna}\u8217's severity: {\i Rav Huna l\u8217'ta\u8217'ameih} \u8212- this was consistent with his known position. He had stated that {\i ben esrim shana ve-lo nasa isha, kol yamav ba-aveira} \u8212- a man who reaches twenty without having married has spent all his days in sin. The {\i gemara} immediately qualifies this: literally in sin? No \u8212- {\i ella eima kol yamav b\u8217'hirhur aveira} \u8212- he has spent all his days besieged by sexual thoughts.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 God\u8217's Wait\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rava} adds: {\i v\u8217'chen tana d\u8217'vei Rabbi Yishmael} \u8212- and the school of Rabbi Yishmael taught similarly. Until the age of twenty, God sits and waits \u8212- {\i nu}, when will this person get married? Once a person reaches twenty and has still not married, {\i amar tippach atzmotav} \u8212- let his bones be blasted! The parental quality of the image is striking: a kind of divine exasperation at a son who ought to have already acted.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Chisda\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rav Chisda} adds his own personal testimony: {\i hai de\u8217'adifna mikhavray, denasivna beshis\u8217'ar} \u8212- the reason I surpassed my colleagues in religious achievement is that I married at sixteen. Had I married at fourteen, {\i ha\u8217'amina lesatan girei be\u8217'einecha} \u8212- I could have said to the {\i satan}, arrows in your eyes! Marrying young eliminated the {\i yetzer hara} as a factor entirely.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This entire set of statements reflects a very much instrumental, utilitarian view of marriage: its function here is to provide an outlet for sexual desire, freeing the man to focus on {\i Torah} and {\i avodat Hashem}. There is no talk here of partnership, companionship, or intrinsic fulfillment \u8212- this is fully consistent with the Babylonian framework described earlier.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is worth noting that the {\i Rambam} in {\i Hilchos Kiddushin} picks up on this {\i gemara} and gives it a striking {\i halachic} formulation. He states that from the age of seventeen onward, a man becomes obligated in the {\i mitzvah} of {\i peru u\u8217'revu}, and once he reaches twenty without having married, {\i harei ze over u\u8217'mevattel mitzvas asei} \u8212- he has actively negated a positive commandment. This is a remarkable claim. One would expect that as long as the man eventually has children, he has only postponed the {\i mitzvah} rather than negated it \u8212- {\i bittul} implies a definitive failure, not a delay. Yet the {\i Rambam} frames it in terms of {\i bittul}, hinging on the age of twenty established in our {\i gemara}. He does, however, create an exemption: if one is deeply engaged in {\i Torah} study and fears that marriage will derail his learning by imposing the necessity of earning a livelihood, then {\i osek bemitzvah patur min hamitzvah} \u8212- being engaged in one {\i mitzvah} exempts one from another.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Rambam}\u8217's use of the language {\i bittul mitzvas asei} in this context is puzzling in two respects. First, the {\i gemara} itself frames the concern in terms of {\i hirhurim}, not {\i bittul mitzvah}. Second, the standard model of {\i bittul} applies when one has definitively failed to perform something, not when one is merely pushing it off. That said, the {\i Rambam}\u8217's position does carry practical implications: any community that does not actively push its young men to marry by age twenty is either not ruling in accordance with the {\i Rambam} or maintaining that the concept of {\i bittul mitzvah} is contingent on it being the {\i right time} \u8212- and that the appropriate time is itself part of the framing of when the {\i mitzvah} applies.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Parental Guidance\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Age Range\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then records a teaching of {\i Rava} addressed to {\i Rav Natan bar Ami}: {\i al tzavarei devareich} \u8212- literally \u8220"on the necks of your sons\u8221" \u8212- meaning, keep the pressure on, maintain the weight of encouragement on your son, {\i mi-shitz\u8217'ar ve-ad esrim ve-tartin} \u8212- from sixteen to twenty-two. The image of the weight on the neck deliberately echoes {\i reichaim b\u8217'tzavaro} from earlier \u8212- but here it is a productive pressure, parental effort to guide a son toward marriage.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two Views\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A second opinion is recorded: {\i ve-amre lei mi-tamne-isrei ad esrim ve-arba} \u8212- from eighteen to twenty-four. The pressure apparently should not start before sixteen or eighteen because the son would be too young, and should not continue beyond twenty-two or twenty-four because past a certain point it becomes counterproductive \u8212- the son stops listening and the relationship deteriorates. This is framed as a {\i machloket} between Rabbi Yehuda and Rabbi Nechemiah, tied to the verse {\i chanoch la-na\u8217'ar al pi darko} \u8212- train the child according to his path. {\i Rashi} notes that while {\i chanoch} suggests a general sense of {\i chinuch} and upbringing, the ages given make clear that marriage is the primary subject. And indeed the next topic in the {\i mishnah} is a father\u8217's obligation to marry off his son.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Father\u8217's Obligation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 How Far Teach\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Returning now to {\i talmud Torah} proper, the {\i gemara} asks: {\i ad hachan chayav adam le-lammed et beno Torah} \u8212- until what point is a person obligated to teach his son {\i Torah}? The question concerns not only the content of the education but also its generational extent.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Zevulun Example\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rav Yehuda} says in the name of {\i Shmuel}: like {\i Zevulun ben Dan} \u8212- a person known at that time \u8212- whose grandfather ({\i avi aviv}) had taught him {\i mikra u-mishnah} \u8212- {\i Tanakh} and {\i mishnayot} \u8212- {\i ve-Talmud, halachos ve-aggados}, the entire range of learning. Everything. So on first reading, the obligation would seem to include teaching one\u8217's son the full breadth of {\i Torah}: the text of the written Torah, the received traditions, the analytical {\i shakla ve-tarya} of the {\i Talmud}, the {\i halachic} rulings, and the {\i aggadic} material.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mikra Only\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} immediately raises an objection from a {\i beraita}: {\i limmdo mikra ein melammdo mishnah} \u8212- once you have taught him {\i Tanakh}, you have no obligation to teach him even {\i Mishnah}. This is a significant {\i chiddush}. Your entire parental educational obligation is fulfilled with {\i mikra} alone. You are not even required to provide basic rabbinic literacy. The verse {\i v\u8217'shinnantam l\u8217'vanecha v\u8217'dibarta bam} refers, after all, to {\i mikra} \u8212- that is what existed when the verse was given.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava\u8217's View\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rava} then sharpens this further: {\i mikra} here means only the {\i Torah} \u8212- the Five Books \u8212- not even {\i Nevi\u8217'im u-Ketuvim}. That is the outer limit of the parental obligation. The {\i gemara} then reconciles the {\i Zevulun ben Dan} example: the point of invoking {\i Zevulun ben Dan} was not to define the {\i content} of education, but to establish its {\i generational} scope \u8212- specifically, that the obligation extends even to grandchildren, as we will see. The reading is thus {\i ke-Zevulun ben Dan ve-lo ke-Zevulun ben Dan}: like {\i Zevulun ben Dan} in the sense that the obligation extends to grandchildren, but not like him in that the content is not everything \u8212- only {\i mikra}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This limitation to {\i mikra} is genuinely surprising. The natural response is that what is being defined here is the parental obligation of primary education, which is distinct from and far narrower than a person\u8217's independent lifelong obligation of {\i talmud Torah}. When the {\i gemara} earlier said that if the father didn\u8217't teach, the son must teach himself, it appears to be referring to this foundational primary education. A person\u8217's own ongoing obligation to learn is clearly understood as far more expansive \u8212- {\i vehagita bo yomam valailah}, after all \u8212- even if the precise contours of that obligation remain to be worked out fully. It is also worth noting, as one participant observed, that {\i mikra} may have been the only content that was realistically accessible in written form, making it the only component of education every father could provide regardless of his own level of learning.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Grandsons\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now addresses whether the obligation to teach extends to grandchildren. A {\i beraita} argues that it does not: {\i ve-limadetem osam et beneichem} \u8212- you shall teach {\i your sons}, {\i ve-lo benei beneichem} \u8212- not your grandsons. On this reading, the obligation is one generation only.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Generations\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But then the {\i gemara} asks: how do we account for the verse {\i ve-hodastem l\u8217'vanecha u-le-venei vanecha} \u8212- you shall inform your sons and your sons\u8217' sons? One answer given is that the second verse is not a separate obligation but an expression of consequence: if you teach your son {\i Torah}, it is as though you have taught all future generations, because the chain of transmission continues. {\i Kol ha-melamed et beno Torah, ma\u8217'aleh alav ha-kasuv ke-ilu limdo lo u-le-veno u-le-ven beno ad sof kol ha-doros} \u8212- teaching your son effectively plants {\i Torah} in an unbroken chain unto all generations. This is a powerful idea: education has a compounding effect through time.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then resolves the tension by appealing to another {\i tanna} who reads the two verses differently: the verse {\i ve-limadetem} \u8212- teaching your sons \u8212- tells us your sons are obligated; the verse {\i ve-hodastem} \u8212- informing sons and sons\u8217' sons \u8212- extends the obligation to grandsons as well. The exclusion of daughters is derived separately: {\i beneichem ve-lo benoteichem}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Grandson = Sinai\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Yehoshua ben Levi} then adds a remarkable statement: {\i kol ha-melamed et ben beno Torah, ma\u8217'aleh alav ha-kasuv ke-ilu kiblah mi-Har Sinai} \u8212- whoever teaches his grandson {\i Torah}, the verse treats him as though he received it directly from {\i Har Sinai}. The prooftext: {\i ve-hodastem l\u8217'vanecha u-le-venei vanecha} is placed immediately adjacent to {\i yom asher amadta lifnei Hashem Elokecha be-Chorev} \u8212- the day you stood before God at {\i Sinai}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The grammatical referent of \u8220"he received it from {\i Har Sinai}\u8221" is ambiguous \u8212- it could refer to the grandson, who by receiving {\i Torah} from his grandfather is connected to that unbroken chain stretching back to {\i Sinai}, or it could refer to the grandfather, who by teaching his grandchildren demonstrates that he understands Torah transmission as a multigenerational obligation rooted in {\i Sinai} itself. Teaching a grandchild does something that teaching a child does not: it shows that the passing down of {\i Torah} is not merely an individual parental act but a chain of responsibility that reaches back through history. The grandfather who teaches his grandchild places himself within that chain in a uniquely vivid way.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Chiya Story\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Handkerchief\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} relates the following story. {\i Rabbi Chiya bar Abba} came upon {\i Rabbi Yehoshua ben Levi} \u8212- a sage of {\i Eretz Yisrael} \u8212- who had thrown a simple cloth ({\i disna}) over his head, not bothering to wear a proper {\i sudra}, and was hurrying to bring his young child to {\i beis knesses} \u8212- the school-house. People asked him: {\i mai kulei hai?} What is all this? Why did you rush out without properly dressing?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ve-Hodastem\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 He replied: {\i mi zutar mai deksiv \u8212- ve-hodastem l\u8217'vanecha}, immediately followed by {\i yom asher amadta lifnei Hashem Elokecha be-Chorev?} Is it a small thing what the Torah says \u8212- that teaching your children is juxtaposed with the day you stood at {\i Sinai}? In other words: taking a child to school is not a trivial domestic errand. It is an act that participates in the grand tradition of {\i Torah} received at {\i Sinai} and transmitted through the generations. To delay it, or to depute it to someone else, or to pause to dress properly first \u8212- none of that is appropriate when the obligation is this weighty.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 There is perhaps a subtle irony here as well. Recall that the {\i sudra} was the garment that identified a man as both a {\i talmid chacham} and a married man \u8212- its absence in the story of {\i Rav Hamnuna} was the marker of his unmarried state. Here, {\i Rabbi Yehoshua ben Levi} \u8212- himself clearly learned and presumably married \u8212- prioritizes the physical act of getting his child to school over the formality of proper dress. The contrast may be intentional: the outward markers of status are subordinated to the actual performance of parental educational obligation, which is itself the mark of genuine {\i talmud Torah} in {\i Eretz Yisrael}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Personal Practice\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Before Eating\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rav Safra} reports in the name of {\i Rabbi Yehoshua ben Chananya}: from that point on, {\i Rabbi Chiya bar Abba} would not taste even a bit of food in the morning until he had reviewed with a child the {\i pesukim} they had learned the previous day and added a new {\i pasuk} \u8212- {\i umosefei}. That is, his practice was to first fulfill the obligation of teaching a child {\i Torah} before attending to his own eating. Whether this refers specifically to his own child or to any child under his care is not entirely precise from the context, but the thrust is clear: the obligation of {\i talmud Torah} with children takes priority over one\u8217's personal needs and forms the first act of the day.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Beis Midrash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Similarly, {\i Rabba bar Rav Huna} would not eat anything in the morning until he had brought the child to the {\i beis medrash}. The personal practice of these sages illustrates how seriously they understood the parental obligation of {\i talmud Torah} \u8212- not as a background duty but as the day\u8217's first priority.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Shinnantam\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Three Parts\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rav Safra} also transmits a teaching in the name of {\i Rabbi Yehoshua ben Chananya} based on the verse {\i v\u8217'shinnantam l\u8217'vanecha}: {\i al tikrei v\u8217'shinnantam ela v\u8217'shillashtam} \u8212- do not read it as {\i shinnantam} but as {\i shillashtam} \u8212- divide into three. The double {\i nun} in {\i shinnantam} signals not merely repetition but a tripling. From this: {\i le\u8217'olam yeshalesh adam shenotav} \u8212- a person should divide his years, or as the {\i gemara} clarifies, his days, into three: {\i shlish be-mikra, shlish be-mishnah, u-shlish be-Talmud} \u8212- one third to {\i Tanakh}, one third to {\i Mishnah}, and one third to {\i gemara}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Daily Division\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} objects: how can one divide one\u8217's {\i years} into thirds when one does not know how long one will live? The answer: {\i lo tzricha leyomei} \u8212- one should divide one\u8217's {\i days}, not one\u8217's lifetime. {\i Rashi} understands this as dividing the days of the week: two days to {\i mikra}, two days to {\i Mishnah}, two days to {\i gemara}, with {\i Shabbos} perhaps for {\i chazara} or rest.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} challenges {\i Rashi}\u8217's reading: if dividing by weeks, you still face the same problem \u8212- what if you die in the middle of a week, before having completed all three sections? {\i Tosafot} therefore concludes that the division must be within each day itself: every single day, one third to each. This is the basis for the practice, codified by Rav Amram Gaon and preserved in some communities today, of reciting a passage of {\i mikra}, {\i Mishnah}, and {\i gemara} each morning before {\i pesukei dezimra} \u8212- a brief but daily {\i kiyum} of this tripartite obligation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} then raises a further difficulty in the name of {\i Rabbeinu Tam}: in practice, the overwhelming focus of Jewish learning at every stage has been {\i gemara}. How is it possible to justify spending a full third of one\u8217's learning time on {\i mikra} and another third on {\i Mishnah}? {\i Rabbeinu Tam} answers by appeal to a principle stated in {\i Sanhedrin}: the Babylonian {\i Talmud} is called {\i belulah} \u8212- mixed \u8212- because it is interwoven with {\i Tanakh}, {\i Mishnah}, and {\i gemara} together. When you learn {\i Bavli}, you encounter {\i mishnayot}, {\i beraitot}, and occasional citations of {\i pesukim}. This admixture is what makes the {\i Bavli} a vehicle for satisfying all three components simultaneously.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rambam\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Rambam} in {\i Hilchos Talmud Torah}, chapter 1, {\i halacha} 11, takes the {\i gemara}\u8217's ruling very literally. He rules that one is obligated to divide one\u8217's study time into thirds \u8212- {\i shlish baTorah shebikhtav}, {\i shlish baTorah shebe\u8217'al peh}, and a third for independent analysis: {\i yavin veyaskil acharit davar mereshito} \u8212- understanding the conclusions from their premises, drawing inferences, applying the principles through which {\i Torah} is expounded. This last third is what is called {\i gemara}. The {\i Rambam} illustrates: if a person works at a trade three hours a day, he has nine hours available for learning. Of those nine, three go to {\i Torah shebikhtav}, three to {\i Torah sheba\u8217'al peh}, and three to analysis.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But the {\i Rambam} then substantially modifies this ruling: {\i bameh devarim amurim betechilat talmudo shel adam} \u8212- this applies at the beginning of a person\u8217's learning, when he is still building his foundational knowledge and skills. Once he has grown in {\i chochmah} and has mastered the written {\i Torah} and the received traditions, {\i yikra be\u8217'ittim mezumanim Torah shebikhtav vedivrei hashemuah kedei shelo yishkach} \u8212- he should review {\i Torah shebikhtav} and {\i Torah sheba\u8217'al peh} occasionally so as not to forget them \u8212- {\i v\u8217'yifneh kol yamav le-Gemara bilvad} \u8212- but all of his real learning should be devoted to {\i gemara}. This ultimately brings the {\i Rambam} very close to {\i Tosafot}\u8217's conclusion: the mature scholar\u8217's obligation is primarily the study of {\i gemara}, with the tripartite division applying to the formative years of education. The irony, noted in the shiur, is that the {\i Rambam} himself famously described his own exhausting daily schedule as a court physician in the letter he wrote later in life \u8212- a schedule that left him almost no time for Torah study beyond an hour on {\i Shabbos}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Sofrim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Why Sofrim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} transitions with a seemingly abrupt {\i lefikach} \u8212- therefore \u8212- which {\i Rashi} acknowledges is a new independent topic. The early sages were called {\i sofrim} \u8212- {\i shehayu sofrim kol otiyot shebaTorah} \u8212- because they would count all of the letters in the {\i Torah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Halfway Points\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then lists several calculations attributed to these {\i sofrim}. The large {\i vav} in the word {\i gachon} \u8212- in the verse about creatures that go on their belly in {\i Vayikra} \u8212- is said to be the midpoint of the letters of the {\i sefer Torah}. The phrase {\i darosh darash} \u8212- which we just recently encountered in {\i Shemini} \u8212- is said to be the midpoint of the words of the {\i Torah}. The word {\i vehitgalach} is said to mark the midpoint of the {\i pesukim}. Turning to {\i Tehillim}: the {\i ayin} of {\i ya\u8217'ar} in the verse {\i yecharsmena chazir miyaar} is said to be the midpoint of the letters of {\i Tehillim}; the verse {\i vehu rachum yechapeir avon} marks the midpoint of its {\i pesukim}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Precision\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rav Yosef} raises a question about the {\i vav} of {\i gachon}: is it on this side or that side of the midpoint \u8212- meaning, with an odd total number of letters, is the {\i vav} itself the exact middle, or does it fall just before or just after? His interlocutor suggests: let us bring a {\i sefer Torah} and count. {\i Rav Yosef} responds by citing another case where this was actually done \u8212- a case mentioned by {\i Rabbah bar bar Chanah} \u8212- suggesting it is at least possible to undertake such a count.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Limited Knowledge\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But then the {\i gemara} deflates both the historical claims and the proposal: {\i inhu bki\u8217'ei b\u8217'chaseros v\u8217'yesiros, anan lo bki\u8217'inan} \u8212- the earlier generations had precise knowledge of whether words were written {\i malei} or {\i chaser} \u8212- fully spelled or defective \u8212- and could therefore count letters exactly. We, by the time of the {\i Amoraim}, no longer possess that precision. Our {\i sifrei Torah} may not be exactly correct in every {\i chaser} and {\i malei}, and therefore we cannot validate or replicate these counts. This admission by the {\i gemara} is significant: it explicitly concedes that even in the {\i Amoraic} period, the exact orthography of the {\i Torah} text was not perfectly known. The {\i baalei hamesorah}, the Masoretes \u8212- most famously the {\i Ben Asher} family \u8212- only completed the systematic cataloging of every letter, vowel, and cantillation mark around the fifth or sixth century, well after this period, in order to fix the text precisely. This uncertainty also dispenses with any naive confidence in Bible codes or letter-count-based exegesis: the {\i gemara} itself acknowledges that the precise letter count was not reliably known.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Counting Pesukim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Numbers\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} similarly questions the count of {\i pesukim} in the {\i sefer Torah}. {\i Rav Yosef} asks about {\i vehitgalach} \u8212- the alleged midpoint of {\i pesukim} \u8212- whether it falls on this or that side of the divide. {\i Abaye} responds that for {\i pesukim}, unlike letters, we ought to be able to count: let us just do it. The {\i gemara}\u8217's response is: {\i bpsukei nami lo bki\u8217'inan} \u8212- we are also not expert in counting {\i pesukim}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i beraita} records: the {\i sefer Torah} contains 5,888 {\i pesukim} \u8212- whereas our current count comes to approximately 5,845, a discrepancy of about forty. {\i Tehillim} has eight more {\i pesukim} than the {\i Torah}, and {\i Divrei HaYamim} has eight fewer.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Regional Variations\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The reason for uncertainty about {\i pesukim} is then made clear: {\i Rav Acha bar Adda} reported that in {\i Eretz Yisrael}, what we read as a single {\i pasuk} was divided into three separate {\i pesukim}. The example given is the verse {\i vayomer Hashem el Moshe hinei anochi ba eilecha be\u8217'av he\u8217'anan} \u8212- which could be divided at {\i he\u8217'anan}, then at {\i ba\u8217'avur yishma ha\u8217'am b\u8217'dibri imcha v\u8217'gam becha ya\u8217'aminu l\u8217'olam}, and finally at {\i vayageid Moshe et divrei ha\u8217'am el Hashem}. Different regional traditions of where {\i pasuk} divisions fall mean that even the count of {\i pesukim} cannot be determined with certainty. The divergence between Babylonian and {\i Eretz Yisrael} manuscripts was apparently significant enough to make such counts unreliable.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Warrior Imagery\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sharp Words\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} returns to the verse {\i v\u8217'shinnantam l\u8217'vanecha} to derive a different teaching. {\i Tanu rabbanan}: {\i v\u8217'shinnantam l\u8217'vanecha} \u8212- the word {\i shinnantam} connotes sharpness, from {\i shinnun}, sharp. The teaching: {\i she-yihyu divrei Torah mechuddadim b\u8217'ficha} \u8212- the words of {\i Torah} should be sharp in your mouth. If someone asks you something, you should not hesitate or stumble \u8212- {\i al tegagaym ve-tomar lo}, do not mumble \u8212- but answer immediately, with fluency and confidence.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Like Sister\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} cites the verse {\i emor la-chochmah achoti at} \u8212- say to wisdom: you are my sister. Your knowledge of {\i Torah} should be as intimate and immediate as your knowledge of your own sister \u8212- or, as an alternative {\i drasha}, as certain as the prohibition of your sister is to you. Either way, the image is of knowledge so internalized that it admits no hesitation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Arrows\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A series of verses is then brought to expand on the theme of sharpness. {\i Koshrem al etzbe\u8217'otecha katvem al lu\u8217'ach libbecha} \u8212- bind them to your fingers, inscribe them on the chambers of your heart. {\i Torah} should be fully internalized, in the blood. Then: {\i ke-chitzim be-yad gibbor ken bnei ha-ne\u8217'urim} \u8212- like arrows in the hand of a warrior, so are the children of one\u8217's youth. {\i Rashi} interprets this as referring to one\u8217's learning \u8212- the {\i Torah} one acquires is one\u8217's \u8220"children,\u8221" and what one learns while young is particularly sharp and durable. {\i Ve-omer chitzei gibbor shenunim} \u8212- the arrows of the warrior are sharp. {\i Ve-omer chitzeycha shenunim ammim tachteycha yippolu} \u8212- your arrows are sharp, nations fall beneath you. {\i Ve-omer ashrei ha-gever asher millei et aspato me-hem} \u8212- praised is the man who fills his quiver with these arrows, who fills himself with {\i Torah} knowledge that is sharp and ready.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Enemies\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Lo yevoshu ki yedabberu et oyvim ba-sha\u8217'ar} \u8212- they will not be embarrassed when they speak with enemies in the gate. The {\i gemara} asks: {\i mai et oyvim ba-sha\u8217'ar?} What does it mean to speak with enemies in the gate? {\i Rabbi Chiyya bar Abba} answers: {\i afillu ha-av u-vno, ha-rav ve-talmido} \u8212- even father and son, even a master and his student, when they are {\i oskim ba-Torah be-sha\u8217'ar echad} \u8212- engaged in {\i Torah} in the same space \u8212- {\i na\u8217'asim oyvim zeh la-zeh}. They become enemies to one another: they argue, they contest, they push back. Anyone who has sat in a {\i beis medrash} during a heated {\i chavrusa} session recognizes this dynamic. The formality of deference to a father or teacher does not apply within the walls of {\i Torah} debate; there, one argues for the truth without deference.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Love\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 And yet \u8212- {\i v\u8217'aynam zazim misham ad shenasim ohavim zeh la-zeh} \u8212- they do not leave that encounter until they have become lovers of one another. The argument is entirely {\i lishma} \u8212- for the sake of truth \u8212- and so when it is over, there is no residual bitterness, only affection. The prooftext: {\i et vahev b\u8217'sufah} \u8212- from the {\i sefer milchamot Hashem}, the Book of the Wars of God. {\i Al tikrei b\u8217'sufah ela b\u8217'sofah} \u8212- do not read it as \u8220"in a whirlwind\u8221" but as \u8220"at its end\u8221" \u8212- {\i vahev} is related to {\i ahavah}, love. At the end of the wars of {\i Torah}, love follows.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Several dimensions of this passage are worth remarking upon. First, there is the martial, warrior imagery attached throughout to {\i talmud Torah} \u8212- arrows, quivers, warriors, enemies. As scholars of rabbinic literature have observed, this seems to be a deliberate refashioning of an activity that might otherwise be coded as passive or non-masculine into something heroic and vigorous. The rabbis mobilize an entire armory of imagery to invest {\i Torah} study with aggression, strength, and martial valor. Second, notice how far we have traveled from the initial framing: the father\u8217's obligation to provide his son a {\i mikra} education, which was presented as a relatively limited parental duty, gives way, in these {\i aggadic} passages, to a far more totalizing vision \u8212- knowledge that must be perfectly sharp, arguments that are like arrows, devotion so complete that one becomes a warrior for {\i Torah}. The {\i gemara} does not fully reconcile these different framings, and how the limited parental obligation relates to the vast personal obligation remains an open question.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Torah Remedy\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mashal\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} closes with an {\i aggadic} passage of striking beauty and psychological acuity. The verse says {\i v\u8217'samtem et divrei eileh asher anochi m\u8217'tzavcha hayom} \u8212- the word {\i v\u8217'samtem} is read as {\i sam tam}, a complete remedy, a perfect antidote. {\i Nimshla Torah k\u8217'sam chayim} \u8212- the {\i Torah} is compared to a potion of life. A {\i mashal}: a man struck his son a great blow and then placed a bandage ({\i rtiya}) on the wound. He said to him: my son, as long as this bandage is on your wound, you may eat and drink whatever you wish, bathe in hot or cold water, and you need not fear any ill effect \u8212- this bandage will protect you against all of it. But if you remove the bandage, {\i harei hi ma\u8217'aleh numi} \u8212- the wound will fester and become infected.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tavlin\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 God speaks to Israel in the same terms: {\i bni, barati yetzer hara} \u8212- I created the {\i yetzer hara}, which is the blow. {\i U-varati lo Torah tavlin} \u8212- but I also created the {\i Torah} as its {\i tavlin}, its antidote, its spice and remedy.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Protection\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Ve-im atem oskim ba-Torah einchem nimsarim beyado} \u8212- if you are engaged in {\i Torah}, you will not be delivered into its power. The verse cited: {\i halo im teitiv se\u8217'eit} \u8212- if you do good, meaning if you learn {\i Torah}, you will carry yourself above it, you will conquer the {\i yetzer hara}. {\i Rashi} understands {\i se\u8217'eit} as implying elevation \u8212- you will be raised above it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Without Torah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But if one does not engage in {\i Torah}: {\i atem nimsarim beyado} \u8212- you are delivered into the hands of the {\i yetzer hara}. {\i Lapetach chatat rovetz} \u8212- it crouches at the door, waiting. {\i V\u8217'lo od ela shekol masa u-matano bach} \u8212- and furthermore, its entire attention, its whole enterprise, is directed at you: scheming, working, seeking entry. {\i Ve\u8217'eilecha teshukatoi} \u8212- its desire is toward you. And yet \u8212- {\i ve-im ata rotze ata moshel bo, shene\u8217'emar ve\u8217'ata timshol bo} \u8212- if you so choose, you can be master over it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} will continue from this point, taking up the challenge of the {\i yetzer hara} and {\i Torah} as its antidote.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Kiddushin 31\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Yetzer Hara & Torah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yetzer Hara Nature\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i daf} opens five lines down from where we left off, with a {\i tanu rabanan} that addresses the {\i yetzer hara} with striking directness. {\i Kashe yetzer hara she\u8217'afilu yotzro kara ra} \u8212- the {\i yetzer hara} is so formidable that even its Creator called it evil, as it is written: {\i ki yetzer lev ha\u8217'adam ra mine\u8217'urav}. This is worth pausing on. The entire opening of {\i Bereishit} is structured around {\i ki tov} \u8212- God surveys creation and pronounces it good. The one thing that receives the designation {\i ra} is what human beings carry within them. That inversion is deliberate and sobering.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yitzchak adds: {\i yitzro shel adam mitchadesh alav bechol yom} \u8212- the {\i yetzer hara} renews itself against a person every single day, as the verse says {\i rak ra kol hayom}, it is evil the whole day. And Rabbi Shimon ben Levi goes further still: {\i yitzro shel adam mitgaber alav bechol yom u\u8217'mevakesh hamito} \u8212- it grows stronger each day and seeks to destroy you, as it is written, {\i tzofeh rasha latzadik u\u8217'mevakesh lehamito}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What emerges from these statements is not merely a description of natural sinful impulse but something more vivid and almost personified \u8212- a force with creativity, strategy, and malicious intent. This is not simply the {\i pshat} of a human being\u8217's natural lustful drives; the Gemara is drawing on imagery closer to that of a figure who, like a little devil on one\u8217's shoulder, is actively scheming to bring a person to ruin. The {\i yetzer hara}, in this framing, is relentlessly innovating against you, which means the response must be equally dynamic.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Divine Help & Torah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then states: {\i ilmalai Hakadosh Baruch Hu ozro, ein yachollo} \u8212- were God not there to assist, a person could not withstand the {\i yetzer hara}, as the verse says, {\i Elokim lo ya\u8217'azvenu veyado}, God will not abandon him to its hand. This is a striking and somewhat uncomfortable claim. It implies that on one\u8217's own, the deck is already stacked against you. There is no simple resort to free will as a sufficient counterbalance \u8212- without divine assistance, one is simply outmatched.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This raises a genuine conceptual tension. The Jewish conception typically holds that we possess both a {\i yetzer hara} and a {\i yetzer hatov}, and that the capacity for moral choice is genuinely ours. This is not original sin; one does not start out in a state of depravity requiring external salvation. Yet the Gemara insists that at some fundamental level, unaided human striving is insufficient. One way to read this less troublingly is through the principle the Gemara elsewhere articulates: {\i haba letaher mesay\u8217'in oto} \u8212- one who comes to purify himself is aided from Above. God does not do it for you; rather, once you exert your own free will and take the first steps on the right path, divine assistance arrives to {\i chazek}, to strengthen and sustain that effort. On this reading, the Gemara may be offering {\i chizuk} to someone who feels they are constantly failing \u8212- reminding them that the effort to do right, however imperfect, will be met and supported. The Torah itself, given just before this passage, is presented as the divine instrument: {\i sam tam}, a perfect elixir, the tool provided by God to combat the {\i yetzer hara}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is also worth noting, as one participant in the discussion observed, that there is a tendency in contemporary Orthodox discourse to focus almost entirely on human choice and responsibility, with relatively little explicit acknowledgment of the idea that God is present to assist and strengthen us. The prayers carry this message, but ordinary religious discourse often does not. The Gemara here offers a corrective, however challenging its metaphysics may be.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Beit Midrash Solution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tana debei Rabbi Yishmael} offers an arrestingly direct prescription. {\i Beni, im pagah becha menuval zeh} \u8212- my son, if this abominable thing takes hold of you \u8212- {\i moshchehu leveit hamidrash}, drag it to the {\i beit midrash}. Make the {\i yetzer hara} your {\i chavruta}, as it were. {\i Im even hu nimoach} \u8212- if it is stone, it will dissolve; {\i veim barzel hu mitpotzetz} \u8212- if it is iron, it will shatter. The verse cited is {\i halo ko devarai ka\u8217'esh neum Hashem} \u8212- are not My words like fire \u8212- {\i uchefatish yifotzetz sela}, and like a hammer that shatters rock.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} notes a textual difficulty: the verse speaks of the hammer shattering, but the Gemara applies it to the metal being destroyed. {\i Tosafot} proposes reading it as {\i al yedei sela}, by means of the rock \u8212- or alternatively, separates the two images: the fire melts the metal, and the hammer shatters the stone. It is admittedly a difficult reading of the {\i pasuk}, but the homiletical point stands. A complementary verse is then cited \u8212- {\i hoy kol tzame lechu lamayim}, come all who are thirsty to water \u8212- and {\i avanim shachaku mayim}, stones are worn away by water, suggesting that sustained immersion in Torah will erode even the hardest resistance.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The circle thus closes: {\i talmud Torah} is not merely a religious obligation or a form of intellectual cultivation; it is the essential weapon in a daily existential battle. The {\i chidush} of the {\i yetzer hara} \u8212- its capacity for constant innovation and renewal \u8212- demands a corresponding {\i chidush} in Torah learning as its antidote.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Father\u8217's Obligations\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Marry Off Son\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara turns from the {\i aggadeta} of {\i yetzer hara} and {\i Torah} to the father\u8217's specific obligations toward his son \u8212- including, notably, the obligation to marry him off: {\i le\u8217'hashio isha}. The source ({\i manalan}) is the verse {\i kechu nashim veholidu banim uvanot, ukechu livneichem nashim, ve\u8217'et benoteichem tenu la\u8217'anashim} \u8212- take wives, have sons and daughters, take wives for your sons, and give your daughters to men.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What is immediately striking is that this {\i pasuk} appears to obligate the father toward both sons and daughters in the matter of marriage. The Gemara notes {\i bishlama beno beyado} \u8212- I can understand regarding the son, because in theory the father acts as the initiating party on his son\u8217's behalf. But then it says {\i ella bito beyado hi} \u8212- and his daughter is in his hand \u8212- which must rank as one of the more ironic statements in this entire {\i sugya}, given that we learned earlier that a father may actually marry off his daughter against her will, while he cannot do the same for his son. The {\i Gemara} answers: {\i leiten lah midai velilbasheih nechasaya, ki heichi dekaftzu alei inshi} \u8212- give her gifts, clothe her in ornaments, so that men will be attracted to her and wish to marry her. The father\u8217's obligation regarding his daughter, then, is to provide a suitable dowry and presentation, making her a desirable match.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The irony here is layered. Throughout this {\i masechta}, the father\u8217's obligations have been defined as applying to sons, not daughters. Yet here, with barely a pause, the Gemara reads the {\i pasuk} as extending to daughters as well, without flagging the tension. The deeper irony is that while the father legally has the power to contract his daughter\u8217's {\i kiddushin}, he cannot similarly contract his son\u8217's marriage \u8212- yet the framework of initiative and representation is mapped onto the son\u8217's case as if the father were the active party. As the Gemara elsewhere established, {\i ish mechazir achar aveidato} \u8212- it is the male who pursues; so the father, representing his son, can initiate inquiries and pursue a match, whereas representing his daughter, he can only make her attractive enough to receive such pursuit.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Teaching Trade\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then addresses the obligation to teach a trade: {\i lelamdo omanut}. The source is {\i re\u8217'eh chayim im ishah asher ahavta} \u8212- see life with the woman that you love. The Gemara reads this on two levels: if {\i ishah} means a literal wife, then just as one is obligated to marry off one\u8217's son, so too must one teach him a livelihood. If {\i ishah} is a metaphor for Torah \u8212- Torah as the {\i ishah}, the other woman with whom a man lives his life, a motif Boyarin has explored in his analysis of both the militaristic and romantic metaphors for {\i talmud Torah} \u8212- then just as one must teach Torah, one must also teach a trade.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yehuda sharpens this: {\i kol she\u8217'eino melamdo omanut melamdo listut} \u8212- whoever does not teach his son a trade teaches him banditry, or as the Gemara immediately clarifies, {\i ke\u8217'ilu melamdo listut} \u8212- it is as if he teaches him banditry, because without a livelihood, what will the child do? The {\i Gemara} asks what Rabbi Yehuda adds beyond the {\i Tanna Kamma}, and answers: {\i de\u8217'agmarei iska} \u8212- the {\i Tanna Kamma} might have been satisfied with teaching the son commerce, buying and selling. Rabbi Yehuda insists on an actual {\i omanut}, a manual trade, because commerce depends on market conditions and may not always sustain a person, whereas a skilled trade \u8212- plumbing, tailoring, carpentry \u8212- will always find need.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 That the value of a specific manual trade is not merely theoretical was brought into sharp relief in the discussion that followed. One member of the group noted that her relative survived the Shoah because his work as a tailor gave him skills the Nazi regime could use; another shared that his knowledge of plumbing was what kept him alive in the camps. {\i Kol she\u8217'eino melamdo omanut melamdo listut} takes on a dimension of literal life and death that no abstract analysis can match.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 As for the formal status of these obligations \u8212- to marry off one\u8217's son, to teach a trade \u8212- the sources being cited are from {\i Navi}, not from explicit Torah commands, and one might question whether these even rise to the level of {\i mitzvah derabbanan} in a strict sense. They feel more like broad parental responsibilities subsumed under the general obligation to raise a child properly, akin in spirit to {\i chinuch} \u8212- training one\u8217's children in the {\i mitzvot} \u8212- but oriented toward life in the world rather than life as a Torah Jew.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Swimming & Safety\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Some authorities add a further obligation: {\i af lehashito banahar} \u8212- to teach one\u8217's son to swim in the river. {\i Mai ta\u8217'ama? Chayutei hee} \u8212- because it is a matter of preserving life. The ability to swim is presented as a basic life-saving skill a parent is obligated to transmit. Indeed, as was noted in passing, some universities to this day will not grant a degree to a student who cannot swim \u8212- an echo of this ancient recognition that swimming is not a luxury but a survival competency.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Filial Obligations\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sons & Daughters Equal\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now pivots decisively to the second major subject of the {\i amud}: the filial obligations of children toward parents \u8212- {\i kibbud av va\u8217'em}. The {\i mishnah} states {\i kol mitzvat ha-av al ha-ben} \u8212- all obligations of the father upon the son \u8212- and the Gemara initially asks what this means. If it refers to the obligations a father has toward his son, does it follow that women are equally obligated in those parental duties? That cannot be right, since we already established that a mother, unlike a father, is not obligated in {\i brit milah} or {\i pidyon haben}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Yehuda resolves this: the {\i mishnah} must be read as {\i kol mitzvat ha-av ha-mutelet al ha-ben la\u8217'asot le-aviv} \u8212- all obligations that a child owes to a parent \u8212- and these fall equally on {\i echad anashim ve-echad nashim}, both sons and daughters. The {\i baraita} confirms this: {\i ish ish imo ve-aviv tira\u8217'u} \u8212- the plural {\i tira\u8217'u} implies that two people are addressed, both the man and the woman.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Married Women Exemption\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i baraita} then asks: if the plural {\i tira\u8217'u} includes both men and women, why does the verse emphasize {\i ish}, man? The answer: {\i ish she-yesh be-yado la\u8217'asot, isha she-ein be-yada la\u8217'asot} \u8212- a man generally has more capacity to act on behalf of his parents, whereas a married woman\u8217's primary obligations run to her husband\u8217's household. {\i Mipnei she-yesh reshut acherim aleha} \u8212- because others have authority over her. This does not exempt her from the obligation; it acknowledges that her opportunity to fulfill it is often constrained.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} makes a point worth emphasizing here: legally, a married woman\u8217's domestic duties to her husband are only {\i derabbanan}, not {\i de-oraita}. The {\i de-oraita} obligation itself does not subordinate a wife to household service. Yet even {\i Tosafot} acknowledges that as a practical matter, {\i einah metzuya etzel avia ela etzel ba\u8217'alah} \u8212- a married woman\u8217's home is her husband\u8217's home, not her father\u8217's, and her time and energy flow accordingly.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 There is an irony here that was pointed out: while the Gemara frames men as having more {\i opportunity} to honor parents, lived experience frequently reverses this. It is typically the daughter \u8212- or the daughter-in-law \u8212- who bears the primary burden of caregiving for aging parents, whether her own or her husband\u8217's, even in contemporary society with its more egalitarian ideals. The Gemara\u8217's theoretical framework and social reality have long been in productive tension on this point.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Idi bar Avin in the name of Rav clarifies that this asymmetry applies only to a married woman. If a woman is divorced \u8212- or never married \u8212- {\i shneihem shavin}, her obligation is equal to her brother\u8217's. The baseline, then, is genuine equality.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Parents = God\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Honor Parallel\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now presents a series of scriptural parallels that equate the obligations owed to parents with those owed to God. {\i Ne\u8217'emar kabeid et avicha ve\u8217'et imecha} \u8212- honor your father and mother \u8212- and {\i ne\u8217'emar kabeid et Hashem mehonecha} \u8212- honor God from your wealth. {\i Hishvah hakatuv kevod av va\u8217'eim likvod hamakom} \u8212- the Torah has equated honoring parents with honoring God.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Reverence Parallel\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Similarly: {\i ne\u8217'emar ish imo ve\u8217'aviv tira\u8217'u} \u8212- revere your mother and father \u8212- alongside {\i ve\u8217'et Hashem elokecha tira ve\u8217'oto ta\u8217'avod} \u8212- you shall revere God and serve Him. {\i Hishvah hakatuv morat av va\u8217'eim lemorat hamakom} \u8212- the reverence due to parents is equated with the reverence due to God.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Cursing Parallel\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 And: {\i ne\u8217'emar mekaleil aviv ve\u8217'imo mot yumat} \u8212- one who curses his father or mother is put to death \u8212- alongside {\i ish ish ki yekaleil elohav venasa chet\u8217'o}, cursing God being treated as a capital offense. {\i Hishvah hakatuv birchat av va\u8217'eim lebirchat hamakom} \u8212- the Gemara uses the euphemism {\i birchat} (blessing) for cursing in both cases, equating them. {\i Aval behaka\u8217'ah} \u8212- as for one who strikes a parent being put to death \u8212- there is no parallel, since striking God is simply impossible. {\i Vechein badin} \u8212- and it makes sense that parents are thus equated with God.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Three Partners\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Why does this equation hold? {\i She-shelosha shutafin hein ba\u8217'adam} \u8212- because three partners participate in the creation of a human being: {\i Hakadosh Baruch Hu ve\u8217'aviv ve\u8217'imo}, God, father, and mother. And the Gemara adds: {\i bizman she\u8217'adam mechabeid et aviv ve\u8217'et imo, amar Hakadosh Baruch Hu, ma\u8217'ala ani aleichem ke\u8217'ilu darti beineichem vechibduni} \u8212- when a person honors his father and mother, God says: I regard it as if I dwelt among them and they honored Me.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Motivation Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gratitude Model\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The equation of honoring parents with honoring God is weighty, and it invites the question: what is the underlying rationale for this comparison? The intuitive reading is one of gratitude. Parents brought you into the world; they gave you everything. You owe them honor, and recognizing that debt models for you the even greater honor owed to God, who created the entire universe, created your parents, and is the ultimate source of everything you have received. On this reading, the connection moves from the immediate and human \u8212- the visceral sense of owing something to those who nurtured you \u8212- to the theological: a {\i kal vachomer} that your gratitude toward parents should extend all the more to God.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Awe Model\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Gerald Blidstein, in his careful study {\i Honor Thy Father and Mother}, argues for a different reading. He suggests that the comparison is not primarily about the ethics of gratitude \u8212- that model being rooted in human experience and then applied upward to the divine. Rather, Blidstein sees the connection as running along a distinctly religious axis: what governs the relationship to parents is {\i awe}. The same quality of {\i yirah} \u8212- the sense of utter otherness, of one who is at a higher level, of standing before a creator whose act exceeds anything you could fully comprehend \u8212- that characterizes a properly religious relationship with God is what should characterize the relationship with parents. It is not about giving back what you owe; it is about the {\i rimon} of standing before those who brought you into being, which mirrors the awe of standing before the Creator of all being.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 On this alternative reading, the motivational structure is reversed. It is not that we learn from the parent-child relationship how to relate to God; rather, the religious sensibility of awe and reverence that we cultivate toward God is the very sensibility that should shape how we stand before our parents. Parents, as creators, partake of something of the divine creative act, and our response to them should draw on the same deep well. As one participant put it: the obligation to parents is not ethically driven out of a sense of debt, but motivated by a deep sense of awe and reverence \u8212- the same disposition that enables genuine {\i davening} and authentic engagement with {\i Hashem}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Practical Principles\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Action Over Feeling\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Raising the equation of parents with God immediately prompts a question about cases where the equation feels strained \u8212- where parents were absent, neglectful, unkind, or worse. The Torah\u8217's response, as we will see worked out in the Gemara, is not to address internal states at all. The {\i Torah} does not command you how to feel about your parents. It commands how to act. Whether you feel respect or not, you behave in ways that demonstrate honor. Whether you regard your parents as deserving of it or not, you perform the actions that constitute {\i kavod} and {\i yirah}. This is a fundamental principle, and it stands in interesting contrast to many other relational obligations in Jewish law: here, the action itself is not sufficient either (as we will see at the end of the {\i amud}), but the baseline is action, not sentiment.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Self-Care Priority\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What about genuinely abusive parents \u8212- those who have caused real psychological or emotional harm? Here a critical principle applies, drawn from the broader category of {\i mitzvot bein adam l\u8217'chaveiro}: {\i chayecha v\u8217'chayei chavercha chayecha kodmin} \u8212- your life and your friend\u8217's life, your life comes first. The first person you are obligated to is yourself. Where maintaining a relationship with one\u8217's parents causes genuine harm to one\u8217's psychological well-being, the obligation to self-protection is prior. A person in such a situation is not merely {\i permitted} to maintain distance; they may be {\i required} to do so, because {\i kibbud av va\u8217'em} cannot override the primary obligation to protect oneself.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is not a license for avoidance whenever relationships are difficult. {\i Kibbud av va\u8217'em} does demand real personal and financial extension. But where the threshold of genuine harm is crossed, self-care takes precedence \u8212- and that is a halachic principle, not merely a pastoral accommodation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 No Love Command\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One further observation deserves notice. The Torah commands honoring parents. It commands fearing and revering them. It prohibits cursing or striking them. What it conspicuously does not command is {\i love}. There is no {\i ve\u8217'ahavta} directed toward parents. Whether this reflects a recognition that love cannot be commanded, or that the relational framework here is one of awe and duty rather than affection, is itself a rich question \u8212- but the absence is notable and worth sitting with.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Natural vs.\u160 ?Commanded\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Natural Tendencies\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rebbi} teaches: {\i galui veyadua lifnei mi she\u8217'amar vehayah ha\u8217'olam she\u8217'ben mechabeid et imo yoseir mei\u8217'aviv} \u8212- it is revealed and known before the One who created the world that a son shows more honor to his mother than his father. Why? {\i Mipnei she\u8217'meshadelto bidvarim} \u8212- because she encourages him, nurtures him, draws him close with words. {\i Rashi} renders this with a sense of gentle persuasion, even seduction.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The underlying dynamic is clear: the mother is classically the nurturer, the father the authority figure. Toward the nurturer, the natural human response is warmth, care, and the desire to do well by her \u8212- that is {\i kavod}. Toward the authority figure, the natural response is the stepping back, the sense of distance, the slight apprehension \u8212- that is {\i yirah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Torah\u8217's Reversal\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Precisely because these tendencies are natural, the Torah intervenes to counterbalance them. {\i Ulefikach hikdim Hakadosh Baruch Hu kibbud av le\u8217'kibbud em} \u8212- therefore God placed {\i kavod av}, the honor of the father, before {\i kavod em} in the verse {\i kabeid et avicha ve\u8217'et imecha}. The command is directing you to extend to your father the honor that comes less naturally. Similarly: {\i galui veyadua lifnei mi she\u8217'amar vehayah ha\u8217'olam haben mityarei me\u8217'aviv yoter me\u8217'imo} \u8212- a son more naturally has reverence for his father, in part because {\i biglal shemelamdo Torah}, the father embodies the authority of Torah instruction. Therefore the verse {\i ish imo ve\u8217'aviv tira\u8217'u} places the mother first \u8212- to direct you to give your mother her due {\i yirah}, which comes less naturally.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is a general principle that the {\i Sifrei} draws out explicitly: sometimes the command of {\i kibbud av va\u8217'em} requires you to do precisely what does not come naturally. That is, in part, why it requires a command at all. And it may also illuminate why the Torah does not command love \u8212- not because love is unimportant, but because in some relationships, love comes naturally and does not need legislation, while the more formal postures of honor and reverence may not.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Causing Anguish\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 God Withdraws\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara presents an inversion of the earlier promise. If honoring parents leads God to say {\i ke\u8217'ilu darti beineichem vechibduni} \u8212- it is as if I dwelt among them and they honored Me \u8212- then causing parents anguish produces the opposite. {\i Bizman she\u8217'adam mita\u8217'er et aviv ve\u8217'et imo, amar Hakadosh Baruch Hu: yafeh asiti shelo darti beineihem} \u8212- when a person causes anguish to his father and mother, God says: it is good that I did not dwell among them. {\i She\u8217'ilmalei darti beineihem, tzi\u8217'aruni} \u8212- for had I dwelt among them, they would have caused Me anguish. The implication cuts two ways: such behavior amounts to a rejection of the divine presence, and also signals that if this is how they treat their parents \u8212- those who stand in relation to God \u8212- then they would have treated God no differently.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This also raises a question about the precise contours of {\i kibbud av va\u8217'em}. The Gemara earlier cited the case of {\i Dama ben Netina} who would not disturb his sleeping father \u8212- clearly a form of not causing {\i tza\u8217'ar}. But what of adult children who make life choices their parents disapprove of? Is causing parental unhappiness through one\u8217's own legitimate choices a violation of {\i kibbud av va\u8217'em}? There is a meaningful distinction between directly doing something offensive or disrespectful to a parent and simply living one\u8217's life in ways the parent does not prefer. The former may implicate {\i tza\u8217'ar}; the latter generally does not.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Secret Sins\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara digresses \u8212- though the connection to divine presence makes it feel organic. {\i Kol ha\u8217'over aveirah beseter, ke\u8217'ilu dochek raglei Shechinah} \u8212- whoever commits a sin in private is as if he pushes God\u8217's feet out of the world. The verse: {\i hashamayim kis\u8217'i ve\u8217'ha\u8217'aretz hadom raglai} \u8212- the heavens are My throne and the earth My footstool. God\u8217's feet, as it were, rest on this earth; to sin in secret is to implicitly deny that God\u8217's presence extends here. One who sins publicly at least demonstrates, however perversely, that he knows he is being observed but does not care. One who sins in secret suggests that he believes God cannot see \u8212- that God has no presence in this private space \u8212- and thereby pushes {\i Shechinah} out of the world.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yehoshua ben Levi adds: {\i assur le\u8217'adam sheyelech arba amot bekoma zekufa} \u8212- a person may not walk four {\i amot} with a fully upright posture, {\i melo kol ha\u8217'aretz kevodo} \u8212- for the entire earth is filled with God\u8217's glory. To stride with exaggerated erectness is to assert one\u8217's own primacy, to crowd out the awareness of divine presence.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Head Covering\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 From this emerges the practice of Rav Huna the son of Rav Yehoshua: {\i lo masgei arba amot begillui harosh} \u8212- he would not walk four {\i amot} with an uncovered head. {\i Amar: Shechinah lemalah meroshi} \u8212- he said, the {\i Shechinah} is above my head. This is the textual basis for the {\i kippah} \u8212- not a formal prohibition, but a {\i minhag} rooted in the continuous awareness of divine presence overhead.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Returning to the broader context: the Gemara has now constructed a world in which the presence of God is mediated, in part, through parents. The {\i Shechinah} dwells with those who honor their parents; it withdraws when they cause anguish. And the same sensibility that makes a person aware of {\i Shechinah} above their head \u8212- that constant consciousness of being in the presence of something greater \u8212- is precisely what {\i kibbud av va\u8217'em} is meant to cultivate toward one\u8217's parents.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Competing Demands\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Father Precedence\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now enters a halachic case study. A certain son of a widow approached Rabbi Eliezer: {\i aba amar hashkeini mayim, ve-ima omeret hashkeini mayim} \u8212- both my father and my mother are asking me to bring them water. {\i Eizeh mehem kodem} \u8212- which takes priority? Rabbi Eliezer answered: {\i anach kvod imcha va-asei kvod avicha} \u8212- set aside your mother\u8217's honor for the moment and attend to your father\u8217's honor. {\i She-atah ve-imcha chayavim bi-chvod avicha} \u8212- for both you and your mother are obligated in your father\u8217's honor.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This ruling reflects a principle that has been developing throughout the Gemara. While sons and daughters are fundamentally equally obligated in {\i kibbud av va\u8217'em}, and while {\i kavod} and {\i yirah} are equally owed to both parents, the case of competing simultaneous demands must be resolved when the parents are in a marital relationship. In a patriarchal household, the father is the head; the mother herself owes {\i kavod} to the father in certain respects. Just as a married daughter\u8217's obligations to her parents may yield to her obligations to her husband, so the mother\u8217's claim on the child\u8217's immediate service may yield to the father\u8217's, not because the mother is less important, but because the household hierarchy places the father\u8217's demands first when they directly conflict.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Divorced Mother\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The same questioner then approached Rabbi Yehoshua and posed the same scenario. After receiving the same answer, he pressed: {\i Rebbi, nitgarsha mahu} \u8212- what if my mother is divorced? In that case, she is no longer in a position of owing {\i kavod} to the father; the marital hierarchy that made the father\u8217's claim prior no longer operates. What then takes precedence?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Harsh Response\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yehoshua\u8217's response is arresting: {\i mi-bein risei eynecha nikar she-ben almana atah} \u8212- from between your eyelashes it is clear that you are the son of a widow. {\i Rashi} explains that his eyelashes had fallen out from crying \u8212- he was an orphan. Rabbi Yehoshua was rebuking him for asking a question framed in personal terms \u8212- {\i aba amar, ima omeret}, my father says, my mother says \u8212- when he had no living father. He was asking as if it were a real dilemma when it was not.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yehoshua then told him: {\i Hattel lahen mayim besefel vekakei lahen ketarnagolim} \u8212- pour water in a bowl and cluck at them like chickens, let them come drink from it. The image is, to say the least, jarring. Treating this as a mocking dismissal seems pastorally inexplicable when addressed to an orphaned child. Whatever Rabbi Yehoshua\u8217's precise intent, the halachic upshot is clear: where parents are divorced, there is genuinely no principled resolution to competing demands. There is no hierarchy that straightforwardly applies, and the child must exercise judgment \u8212- or, in a sense, the {\i halacha} simply does not give a clear answer.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This absence of resolution is itself significant. The area of {\i bein adam l\u8217'chaveiro} \u8212- and {\i kibbud av va\u8217'em} is paradigmatically such an area \u8212- does not lend itself to the kind of precise, context-independent ruling that governs {\i lulav} or {\i tefillin}. Human relationships are too complex, too contextual, too varied. The Gemara is, in part, teaching that moral judgment must fill the gaps that halachic specificity cannot reach. And this is one reason the Gemara here moves so readily between {\i aggadah} and {\i halacha} \u8212- because neither alone is sufficient to capture the full demand being made.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Two Drashot\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ulla\u8217's Drasha\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Ulla the Elder delivered a {\i drasha} at the gates of the house of the {\i Nasi}. The verse: {\i yoducha Hashem kol malchei aretz ki sham\u8217'u imrei picha} \u8212- all the kings of the earth will acknowledge You, God, because they heard the words of Your mouth. The plural {\i imrei picha}, words of Your mouth, rather than the singular {\i ma\u8217'amar}, is noted. When God declared {\i anochi} and {\i lo yihiyeh lecha} \u8212- I am the Lord your God and you shall have no other gods \u8212- {\i amru umot ha\u8217'olam: lechvod atzmo doresh}, the nations said: He demands this for His own honor; there is no universal moral principle at work, just self-aggrandizement. {\i Kevan she\u8217'amar kabeid et avicha ve\u8217'et imecha} \u8212- but when God then commanded honoring one\u8217's father and mother, {\i chazru v\u8217'hodu lema\u8217'amarot harishonot} \u8212- they came back and acknowledged the validity of the first commandments. For now they recognized that there is a genuine and universal moral principle underlying the obligation to give honor and recognition to those who bring you into the world and do for you. The divine demand for honor is of a piece with that same principle; it is not self-serving but deeply grounded.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava\u8217's Drasha\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava offers a parallel {\i drasha} from a different verse: {\i rosh devarcha emet} \u8212- the first of Your words is true. But is only the first true and not the last? Rather, {\i misof devarcha nikar she\u8217'rosh devarcha emet} \u8212- from the end of Your words it becomes clear that the first was true. When the {\i aseret hadibrot} conclude with honoring one\u8217's father and mother, it retroactively validates the opening claim {\i anochi Hashem} \u8212- for the same divine voice that demands honor of God also demands honor of parents, confirming that the framework is coherent and principled throughout.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Dama ben Netina\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Lost Fortune\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 In response to the question of how far {\i kibbud av va\u8217'em} extends, Ulla directs: {\i tze\u8217'u ur\u8217'u mah asah oved kochavim echad be-Ashkelon, ve-Dama ben Netina shemo} \u8212- go see what a certain non-Jew in Ashkelon did. His name was {\i Dama ben Netina}, and his story is one of the most famous in this entire {\i sugya}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Sages sought merchandise \u8212- precious stones for the {\i ephod} of the {\i Kohen Gadol} \u8212- for which they were prepared to pay six hundred thousand {\i dinarin} in profit to {\i Dama}. (Rav Kahana\u8217's version raises the figure to eight hundred thousand.) The key to his storeroom lay under the pillow beneath his sleeping father\u8217's head. {\i Ve-lo tzi\u8217'aro} \u8212- and he would not disturb him. He forfeited a fortune rather than interrupt his father\u8217's sleep.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This story made an impression far beyond its immediate lesson about {\i kibbud av va\u8217'em}. There is embedded within it a profound statement about the sanctity of another person\u8217's sleep \u8212- {\i gezelat sheina}, the theft of someone\u8217's sleep \u8212- as something close to inviolable. The Kabbalistic and {\i mussar} literature picks up on this dimension, and this story is in many ways its source.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The question of where the prohibition falls halachically is also interesting. Is refusing to disturb a sleeping parent a dimension of {\i kavod} \u8212- providing for his needs, including the need for undisturbed rest? Is it an expression of {\i mora} \u8212- reverence, which involves not treating the parent as an object to be instrumentalized for one\u8217's own purposes? Both framings are plausible, and the story does not resolve the question, though it makes the ideal vivid and unforgettable.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might also wonder: what if the father had woken up and said, \u8220"You didn\u8217't rouse me for {\i six hundred thousand dinarin}?\u8221" The Gemara does not raise this, presumably because {\i Dama ben Netina} was not Jewish and his father was not obligated in {\i kibbud av va\u8217'em} toward {\i Dama}. But it is a reasonable question about the limits of deference.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The non-Jewish exemplar is itself significant. The message is at once universal and {\i kal vachomer}: if a Gentile without the obligation of {\i kibbud av va\u8217'em} as a commanded {\i mitzvah} can reach such heights, how much more is demanded of one who is obligated? Beyond the {\i kal vachomer}, there is a recognition that this value \u8212- the reverence and care owed to those who brought you into the world \u8212- is part of the moral fabric of humanity, accessible and recognizable across religious boundaries. And the Gemara\u8217's use of a non-Jew as the model for this Jewish practice carries its own implicit message about the universality of the underlying moral truth.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is also worth noting what {\i Dama ben Netina} is providing: the precious stones for the {\i ephod}, the garment of the {\i Kohen Gadol}, central to the religious life of all {\i Klal Yisrael}. The non-Jew who models {\i kibbud av} is also the one who enables the religious service of the entire Jewish people. The convergence is striking.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Divine Reward\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The story continues: {\i le-shanah acheret natan Hakadosh Baruch Hu secharo} \u8212- the following year, God provided his reward. {\i Shenoldah lo parah adumah be-edro} \u8212- a {\i parah adumah} was born in his flock. When the Sages of Israel came to him, {\i Dama} said: I know you would pay me any price in the world for this animal. {\i Ela} \u8212- but I ask only this: {\i oto mammon she-hifsadeti bishvil kibbud abba} \u8212- the same sum I lost in the honor of my father. Nothing more. His reward matched exactly what he had relinquished.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i parah adumah} is itself charged with symbolism: it is the paradigmatic {\i chok}, the statute whose rationale is withheld. That the reward for an act of pure moral devotion \u8212- forfeiting enormous profit out of reverence for a sleeping father \u8212- comes through the most inscrutable of all {\i mitzvot} is perhaps not accidental.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Commanded Principle\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now cites Rabbi Chanina\u8217's principle, which contextualizes the entire story: {\i gadol metzuvah ve\u8217'oseh mishe\u8217'eino metzuvah ve\u8217'oseh} \u8212- one who is commanded and does is greater than one who is not commanded and does. This is the framework within which the exemplary behavior of {\i Dama ben Netina}, a non-Jew, must be understood. As remarkable as his conduct was, those who are commanded in {\i kibbud av va\u8217'em} and fulfill it are at a higher level \u8212- not because they are personally superior, but because the commanded act is more deeply desired and demanded by God.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rashi} explains that being commanded actually creates greater resistance \u8212- the external obligation generates a kind of counter-pressure that makes compliance harder \u8212- and therefore greater reward. A more general formulation: if the act is {\i commanded}, it reflects that God desires it more intensely, and therefore the person who performs it under obligation achieves something higher.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This principle opens onto a broader question that the story of {\i Dama ben Netina} uniquely illuminates: what happens to the moral vitality of an obligation once it is formalized as a religious command? The non-Jew, unburdened by the command, could reach those heights perhaps precisely because his motivation remained purely moral. For the Jew, the challenge is to maintain that moral force and inner drive even after the act has been overlaid with the structure of religious obligation. The story of {\i Dama ben Netina} keeps that moral ideal vivid and present, which may be precisely why it is told in this context.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rav Yosef Blind\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Initial Hope\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara presents a remarkable personal disclosure from Rav Yosef, who was blind. He said: {\i meireisha hava amina} \u8212- at first I used to think \u8212- {\i man de-hava amar li halacha ke-Rabbi Yehuda, de-amar suma patur min hamitzvot} \u8212- if someone were to tell me that we rule like Rabbi Yehuda, who holds that a blind person is exempt from {\i mitzvot}, {\i avidna yoma tava lerabbanan} \u8212- I would make a {\i Yom Tov} for the Torah scholars. Why? {\i Dela mipakedna, ve\u8217'avidna} \u8212- because I am not commanded, and yet I perform the {\i mitzvot}; I thought that being an unconstrained volunteer would bring me {\i greater} reward than being an obligated performer.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Reversal\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Ha de-shames leha de-amar Rabbi Chanina} \u8212- but now that I have heard Rabbi Chanina\u8217's ruling, that {\i gadol metzuvah ve\u8217'oseh yoter mishe\u8217'eino metzuvah ve\u8217'oseh}, the one who is commanded is greater \u8212- {\i vehashta adaraba} \u8212- it is now the opposite. Now Rav Yosef says: if someone were to tell me that {\i we do not rule like Rabbi Yehuda} \u8212- that I am indeed obligated \u8212- that is when I would make the {\i Yom Tov}, because being obligated means my reward would be greater. Rav Yosef\u8217's reversal captures with great poignancy how deeply the principle of {\i metzuvah ve\u8217'oseh} reorders one\u8217's understanding of religious merit.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Mother\u8217's Assault\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Public Assault\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Ki asa Rav Dimi amar} \u8212- when Rav Dimi came from {\i Eretz Yisrael} he reported: once {\i Dama ben Netina} was wearing a golden embroidered {\i sirkon} \u8212- some kind of fine toga or garment \u8212- and was seated among the great men of Rome. {\i U-va\u8217'at immo} \u8212- his mother came. {\i Vekara\u8217'aso mimenu} \u8212- she tore it off him. {\i Vetapcha lo al rosho} \u8212- she struck him on the head. {\i Veyarka lo befanav} \u8212- and she spat in his face. {\i Velo hichlima} \u8212- and he did not embarrass her.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The contrast with the earlier story is instructive. In the case of the sleeping father, {\i Dama} demonstrated {\i kavod} \u8212- honor \u8212- by not disturbing him, by treating his father\u8217's repose as paramount even at enormous personal cost. Here, with his mother, he demonstrates {\i mora} \u8212- reverence and self-restraint \u8212- by absorbing a public humiliation without retaliation or shaming her. The Gemara noted earlier that a son more naturally shows {\i kavod} to his nurturing mother and {\i mora} to his authoritative father. In both these stories, {\i Dama ben Netina} performs the {\i less} natural act: honor toward the father, restraint and reverence toward the mother.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Attitude Principle\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Feeding Example\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i amud} concludes with a teaching that synthesizes everything the Gemara has been building toward. {\i Tani Avi berei de-Rav Avo: yesh ma\u8217'achil le\u8217'aviv pasyoni vetordo min ha\u8217'olam} \u8212- there is one who feeds his father fattened pigeons \u8212- a genuine delicacy \u8212- and through doing so drives him from the world, from any share in the {\i olam ha-ba}. {\i Veyesh mat\u8217'chino bereichayim umevi\u8217'o lechayei ha\u8217'olam haba} \u8212- and there is one who causes his father to grind at a millstone and through this brings him to the life of the {\i olam haba}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Yerushalmi} supplies the context. In the first case: a son feeds his father beautiful dishes day after day, and one day the father asks where such fine food comes from. The son replies: \u8220"What do you care, old man? Just chew and eat.\u8221" In the second: the father is called to perform some compulsory service for the king \u8212- dangerous, degrading \u8212- and the son says: \u8220"You stay here and work the millstone; I\u8217'll go do the service for the king,\u8221" thereby protecting his father at personal risk and cost.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Respect Demonstration\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The principle is clear and far-reaching. We have discussed, and will discuss further, the specific actions that constitute {\i kavod} and {\i yirah} \u8212- providing food and drink, not occupying a parent\u8217's seat, not contradicting them publicly, accompanying and serving them. But the Gemara here insists that performing those very actions is not sufficient to fulfill {\i kibbud av va\u8217'em}. It depends entirely on how they are done \u8212- what they communicate, what attitude they express.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 You can provide the finest meals with complete contempt and disdain. That is not {\i kibbud av va\u8217'em}; it may in fact be a desecration of it. You can perform none of the prescribed acts and yet do the single greatest {\i kiyum} of {\i kibbud av va\u8217'em} through an act of self-sacrifice that places your parent\u8217's welfare above your own.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is where {\i kibbud av va\u8217'em} diverges fundamentally from most areas of {\i halacha}. When we put on {\i tefillin}, the attitude with which we do so does not affect whether we have fulfilled the obligation. The precise physical act, performed correctly, is {\i yotzei}. Here, the same physical act can be fulfilled or violated depending entirely on what it expresses. {\i Kavod} is not a checklist; it is a posture, a communication, a demonstration. And that is why the constant movement in this {\i sugya} between {\i halacha} and {\i aggadah}, between the specific rulings and the moral stories, is not incidental. The aggadic dimension \u8212- the stories of {\i Dama ben Netina}, the Gemara\u8217's images of God withdrawing when parents are caused anguish, the equation of parents with God \u8212- is not ornamental. It is the very substance of what {\i kibbud av va\u8217'em} requires.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Kiddushin 32\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Kibbud Stories\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Avimi\u8217's Deference\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The daf opens at the last line of 31a with a {\i tanna} attributed to Avimi the son of Rabbi Abbahu: {\i yesh ma\u8217'achil le\u8217'aviv pisyoni v\u8217'tardo min ha\u8217'olam, v\u8217'yesh mat\u8217'chino b\u8217'reichayim u\u8217'mevi\u8217'o l\u8217'chayei ha\u8217'olam ha\u8217'ba} \u8212- there are those who feed their parents pheasants and thereby drive them from the world, and there are those who make their parents grind behind a millstone and thereby bring them to {\i olam ha-ba}. As Rabbi Linzer discussed the previous day, this opening statement captures the deep subjectivity \u8212- or rather, the essential need for context \u8212- in understanding the obligations of {\i kibbud av va\u8217'em} and {\i mora em va\u8217'av} in a way that other {\i mitzvot} perhaps do not require. The same act can honor or humiliate depending on the relationship and the circumstances.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Immediately following, Rabbi Abbahu himself offers a living example: {\i ke-gon Avimi brei} \u8212- my son Avimi exemplifies the fulfillment of the {\i mitzvah} of {\i kibbud}. The {\i gemara} then details precisely how. Avimi had five {\i benei samchei} \u8212- the term is somewhat ambiguous; it may mean sons who had received {\i semicha}, or more plausibly sons who were already grown and dependent on his table \u8212- in his father\u8217's lifetime. Yet whenever Rabbi Abbahu would come to visit, Avimi himself would run to the door and open it, calling out \u8220"Yes, yes, I\u8217'm coming\u8221" until he reached the threshold. He did not delegate this act of {\i kibbud} to his own grown children but insisted on performing it himself.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This narrative touches on something worth noting: the overwhelming majority of {\i gemara} stories about {\i kibbud av va\u8217'em} concern adult children navigating the care of aging, and in some cases declining, parents \u8212- not teenagers relating to middle-aged parents. Avimi had a household of his own to manage. And according to {\i Tosafot}, Rabbi Abbahu lived in {\i Eretz Yisrael} while Avimi lived in {\i Bavel}, meaning these visits were infrequent, which makes Avimi\u8217's scrupulous deference all the more striking.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Vigil & Merit\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} continues with a second episode. One day, Rabbi Abbahu asked Avimi for a glass of water. By the time Avimi returned, his father had already dozed off. Rather than waking him or setting the water down and leaving, Avimi stood vigil beside him until he woke on his own. The {\i gemara} records that as a result of this act, {\i ista\u8217'yei miltei} \u8212- he merited to expound the {\i mizmor} of {\i Mizmor l\u8217'Asaph, Elohim nitzav b\u8217'adat el} \u8212- which is, as it happens, the {\i shir shel yom} of that very day. The connection drawn between {\i kibbud av va\u8217'em} and the relationship between human beings and God runs as a quiet current beneath this entire sugya.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} raises a separate question about which {\i Mizmor l\u8217'Asaph} is intended here \u8212- {\i Mizmor l\u8217'Asaph, Elohim bau goyim b\u8217'nachalatecha} presents an alternative \u8212- and the {\i rishonim} engage with the textual question at length, though that discussion is set aside here.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mother vs Father\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The next narrative introduces Rabbi Yaakov bar Abuha \u8212- a name which itself prompts a brief observation, since {\i bar Abuha} means \u8220"son of his father,\u8221" and one always wonders in the {\i gemara} whether {\i Abba} was a proper name or a term of address substituted for a parent whose name one could not directly invoke. Rabbi Yaakov bar Abuha posed his question to Abaye: {\i ke-gon ana} \u8212- let me offer my own example. When he would return from the {\i beis midrash}, his father would pour him a glass of wine, and his mother would mix it with water. What should he do? His parents were being deferential to him, and both had prepared something for him.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye\u8217's answer is instructive: accept the drink from your mother, but do not accept it from your father, {\i de-keivan de-bar Torah hu chalsha datei} \u8212- because your father is himself a Torah scholar, and he will be more pained if his son accepts the drink from him. The role reversal here is striking on multiple levels. These are parents serving their son the way the son is supposed to be serving them. And the complicating factor is precisely his status as a {\i talmid chacham} \u8212- his father\u8217's honor for Torah learning is entangled with the familial dynamic in a way that cannot be easily separated. Each of these episodes could sustain extended psychological and {\i halachic} analysis; the {\i gemara} presses forward.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tarfon\u8217's Footstool\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then turns to the well-known story of Rabbi Tarfon, who would lie down on the floor each time his mother needed to get into or out of bed, serving as a living footstool. {\i De-chol eimat de-hava baei lemisik le-purya gachin ve-salik lei} \u8212- whenever she needed to ascend to her bed, he would crouch down and she would step up on him; and whenever she needed to descend, she would step down upon him. Whether his mother was infirm or simply unable to manage the height of the bed on her own, the image is vivid and deliberate.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 When Rabbi Tarfon came to the {\i beis midrash} and spoke proudly of this practice, the response was immediate and sobering: {\i adayin lo higata le-chatzi kibbud} \u8212- you have not yet reached half of what {\i kibbud} requires. The standard set by the {\i gemara} is this: {\i klum zarka arneki be-fanecha le-yam ve-lo hichlamta} \u8212- have you reached the point where if she threw your wallet into the ocean in front of you, you would not be shamed or angered?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Wallet Test\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This wallet question becomes a {\i sugya} in its own right for the {\i poskim}: how much financial loss and how much personal humiliation is a child obligated to absorb without reacting? These are actually two separate questions. {\i Rashi} reads the test primarily as a question of public embarrassment \u8212- the shame of allowing a parent to treat one this way before others. But the financial dimension is also real: a parent destroying your property, in public or in private, tests the inner composure that {\i kibbud} demands. Both threads will return later in the {\i daf}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Yosef Rising\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A brief but vivid vignette follows. Rav Yosef, upon hearing the sound of his mother\u8217's footsteps approaching, would say: {\i ekum mikamei Shechina de-asya} \u8212- I am rising before the {\i Shechina} that is coming. The analogy between honoring one\u8217's mother and receiving the divine presence appears here not as a homily but as the stated reason for Rav Yosef\u8217's own practice.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Orphanhood Blessing\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This leads into one of the more striking statements in the {\i gemara}. Rabbi Yochanan said: {\i ashrei mi she-lo chamman} \u8212- fortunate is one who never saw them. {\i Rashi} explains the difficult word but the meaning is arresting: better perhaps never to have had parents than to have had them and failed so consistently at the {\i mitzvah} of {\i kibbud}. The {\i gemara} then explains this in terms of Rabbi Yochanan\u8217's own biography: {\i ki ivarta imeh meis aviv, ki yeladtih misa imeih} \u8212- when his mother was pregnant with him, his father died; when she gave birth, his mother died. He was orphaned from the outset. In a deeply ironic sense, he never had the opportunity to fail at {\i kibbud av va\u8217'em} \u8212- and this is offered, somewhat ruefully, as a kind of grace. One can read this either as a psychological justification that Rabbi Yochanan constructed for himself, or as a genuine {\i gemara} observation about the extraordinary difficulty of the {\i mitzvah} and the near-impossibility of fulfilling it perfectly.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The same was true of Abaye, the {\i gemara} continues \u8212- {\i ve-khen Abaye}. A trope emerges: the orphaning of great {\i amora\u8217'im} is not merely biographical detail but seems to suggest that greatness and deprivation were intertwined, and that these sages were spared the burden of potential failure at {\i kibbud av va\u8217'em} precisely through their loss. But then the {\i gemara} immediately challenges this: {\i eyni ve-ha-amar Abaye amra li em} \u8212- doesn\u8217't Abaye constantly quote his {\i em}? The resolution: {\i hahi amar bnitei havei} \u8212- that was his nurse, his adoptive mother who raised him. The question of how {\i halacha} regards adoptive parents is genuinely significant, and the {\i poskim} take it seriously. The principle of {\i melamdo Torah k\u8217'megadlo} \u8212- that one who teaches another Torah is considered as though he raised them \u8212- points in the same direction: those who form us and nurture us carry a kind of {\i kibbud} weight that approximates, and in some cases equals, that owed to biological parents.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Asi\u8217's Flight\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The concluding narrative in this section is among the most humanly complex in the {\i daf}. Rav Asi had an elderly mother. She asked him for jewelry; he provided it. She asked for a husband; he said he would look. She asked for a husband as handsome as him \u8212- and at that point he fled to {\i Eretz Yisrael}. He subsequently heard that she had followed him. He came before Rabbi Yochanan and asked: {\i mahu l\u8217'tzeit me-Eretz la-chutz la-Aretz} \u8212- may I leave {\i Eretz Yisrael} and go back to {\i chutz la-Aretz}? Rabbi Yochanan answered: {\i asur} \u8212- forbidden. He then asked: what if I am going to greet my mother? Rabbi Yochanan said: {\i eyni yodeia} \u8212- I do not know. The {\i gemara}\u8217's implication is that Rabbi Yochanan sensed something complicated in the story; the question about going to visit his mother was not quite candid.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Somewhat later, Rav Asi returned and reported that Rabbi Yochanan had blessed him: {\i Ha-Makom yachzirekha l\u8217'shalom} \u8212- God should return you safely. He then went to Rabbi Elazar and related what had happened. Rabbi Elazar responded: {\i chas v\u8217'shalom mirtach rattach} \u8212- {\i Rashi} explains this as Rabbi Elazar\u8217's worry that Rabbi Yochanan would be angry at him for effectively ruling against him. But the ambiguity is genuine: who exactly would be angry at whom? Rabbi Elazar nonetheless concluded that if Rabbi Yochanan had offered a {\i berakha}, he clearly was not planning to get angry: {\i v\u8217'im ita de-rattach lo hamvarkh lakh} \u8212- if he were truly angry, he would not have blessed you.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Then, in mid-deliberation, Rav Asi heard that his mother\u8217's coffin was being brought. {\i Amar: i yadai lo nafki} \u8212- if I had known, I would not have left. She had died in {\i Bavel} and was being transported for burial, and someone else \u8212- presumably other family members \u8212- had already attended to everything. The weight of what he had failed to do, failed to witness, failed to participate in, settles over the story without commentary. She died alone, in some sense, while he was negotiating the {\i halacha} of whether to go back to her.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Halakhic Framework\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kibbud & Mora\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now transitions from narrative to systematic {\i halachic} formulation. A {\i beraita} is cited: {\i tanu rabbanan mechabdo be-chayav u-mechabdo be-moto} \u8212- there is an obligation of {\i kavod} toward one\u8217's parents both during their lifetime and after their death. The {\i gemara} asks: how does one fulfill {\i kavod} during the parent\u8217's lifetime? The answer: if you are about to go do something in your parent\u8217's service, you may not say \u8220"send me along for my own sake\u8221" or \u8220"speed me up for myself\u8221" \u8212- {\i ela kulhu bi-shvil abba} \u8212- everything is framed as being done for the parent.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 After death, {\i kavod} takes the form of how you transmit teachings received from them. You may not say simply \u8220"this is what my father said\u8221" \u8212- you must say {\i kach amar abba mori ha-reini kaparat mishkavo} \u8212- \u8220"thus said my father, my teacher, for whose resting place I am an atonement.\u8221" The {\i gemara} qualifies: {\i ve-hanei milei toch shneim asar chodesh} \u8212- this formulation applies only within the first twelve months. After that, one says {\i zichrono l\u8217'vracha l\u8217'chayei ha\u8217'olam ha\u8217'ba}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The phrase {\i ha-reini kaparat mishkavo} carries genuine ambiguity. It has become associated with the practice of saying {\i kaddish} and attending {\i minyan} regularly after a parent\u8217's passing \u8212- a way of acting on their behalf in the post-mortem realm, whether understood as easing their passage through {\i Gehinnom} or as adding merit toward {\i olam ha-ba}. {\i Rashi} reads the phrase in its more severe sense \u8212- may all evil due to them befall me instead \u8212- while a more positive reading frames it as {\i le-ilui nishmat}, that the child\u8217's good deeds are beneficial to the deceased parent\u8217's soul. Many of the mourning practices that are normative in {\i Ashkenazi halacha} flow from {\i Chasidei Ashkenaz}, and both readings are consistent with that tradition.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Name Change\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The next {\i beraita} addresses the {\i chacham} teaching publicly: {\i chacham meshaneh shem aviv ve-shem rabo} \u8212- when quoting one\u8217's parent or teacher, a scholar must alter how he refers to them. He cannot simply say \u8220"Rabbi so-and-so said this\u8221" but must say \u8220"my {\i rebbe} said such and such.\u8221" The {\i turgaman} \u8212- the one who sits beside the lecturer and repeats the teaching aloud, more slowly and with elaboration \u8212- is exempt from this requirement.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But whose father and whose teacher are meant? If it is the {\i turgaman}\u8217's own father, the {\i gemara} objects: {\i atu turgaman lav bar chiyuva hu} \u8212- is a {\i turgaman} somehow exempt from showing {\i kavod} to his parents? Rather, Rava clarifies: it is the {\i chacham}\u8217's father and the {\i chacham}\u8217's teacher whose names must be modified \u8212- not the {\i turgaman}\u8217's. The {\i chacham} has that specific relationship of honor to express; for the {\i turgaman}, whose function is clarity of transmission, the obligation to modify names does not apply in the same way. The practical illustration involves Mar bar Rav Ashi: when he taught, he would refer to his father simply as {\i Abba Mari}, while the {\i turgaman} would repeat the teaching by name \u8212- {\i kach amar Rav Ashi}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mora vs Kibbud\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now formally distinguishes between the two separate {\i mitzvot}: {\i mora} and {\i kibbud}. What constitutes {\i mora}? {\i Lo omed bimkomo, velo yoshev bimkomo, velo soter devarav, velo machrio} \u8212- do not stand in the parent\u8217's place, do not sit in the parent\u8217's place, do not contradict or overturn their words. The formulation {\i soter devarav} \u8212- literally \u8220"demolish his words\u8221" \u8212- is noteworthy. What constitutes {\i kibbud}? {\i Ma\u8217'achil u-mashkeh malbish u-mechaseh machnis u-motzi} \u8212- providing food, providing drink, clothing ({\i malbish} referring to daytime clothing, {\i mechaseh} to nighttime coverings), and transportation, bringing the parent to where they need to go and returning them.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This formal framework raises a broader {\i halachic} question: how literally must these categories be applied? If a parent is not particular about which chair he sits in, but is deeply sensitive about something else entirely, does {\i mora} require technical adherence to these specific categories, or does it mean cultivating the actual relational state of reverence \u8212- adapting its expression to what truly affects this particular parent in this particular context? Major {\i halachic} works on {\i kibbud av va\u8217'em} have been written taking both more formalist and more contextual approaches, and this tension is genuine.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Money Source\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then raises the question of {\i mishel mi} \u8212- whose money must pay for all of the obligations of {\i kibbud}? The children bear the responsibility to ensure their parents have food, clothing, shelter, and transportation; but must the children pay for this from their own resources?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Yehuda says: {\i mishel ben} \u8212- from the child\u8217's own property. Rav Natan bar Oshaya says: {\i mishel av} \u8212- from the parent\u8217's property. On the latter view, parents are expected to have prepared financially for old age; once they are no longer able to manage their own affairs, the children administer the parental resources appropriately. The {\i rabanan} told Rabbi Yirmiyah \u8212- or some say, his son \u8212- that the {\i halacha} follows {\i mishel av}: it is the parent\u8217's money that funds the obligations of {\i kibbud}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Lost Money\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But a {\i meitivei} is raised: the {\i pasuk} says {\i kabbed et avicha ve\u8217'et imecha}, and in {\i Mishlei} it says {\i kabbed et Adonai mehoncha} \u8212- honor God from your wealth. Just as honoring God requires {\i chisron kis}, actual financial expenditure and loss, so too {\i kibbud} of parents must involve {\i chisron kis}. If the obligation is {\i mishel av}, what does the child actually lose? The {\i gemara} answers: {\i le-bittul melacha} \u8212- the financial loss is in lost wages, the time taken away from work in order to care for the parent. This is a genuinely different conception of {\i chisron kis}: not the direct expenditure of funds, but the opportunity cost of time. One might compare it to other contexts in {\i halacha} where refraining from productive work \u8212- {\i bittul melacha} \u8212- is treated as a form of financial sacrifice.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ma\u8217'aser Ani\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i beraita} is then brought: two brothers, two business partners, a father and son, a teacher and student \u8212- {\i podin ze la\u8217'ze ma\u8217'aser sheni u-machilin ze la\u8217'ze ma\u8217'aser ani} \u8212- they may redeem each other\u8217's {\i ma\u8217'aser sheni} (avoiding the additional {\i chomesh} that applies when one redeems one\u8217's own) and they may feed each other {\i ma\u8217'aser ani}. The case of {\i ma\u8217'aser ani} is where the {\i gemara}\u8217's argument turns sharp.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If we were to say the obligation of {\i kibbud} is {\i mishel ben} \u8212- from the child\u8217's own resources \u8212- then feeding one\u8217's parents with {\i ma\u8217'aser ani} would constitute {\i poreia chovo mishel aniyim}: paying off your own financial obligation to your parents using money that belongs to the poor. {\i Kibbud av va\u8217'em} is not merely a pious aspiration; it is a {\i chov}, a genuine legal obligation, and satisfying that obligation with charity funds designated for others is improper. The {\i gemara} responds: perhaps the {\i beraita} addresses surplus beyond the actual {\i ma\u8217'aser} \u8212- extra produce that was set aside but not technically {\i ma\u8217'aser ani}. And even so, Rabbi Yehuda in the {\i beraita} is pointed: {\i tavo me\u8217'eira l\u8217'mi she-ma\u8217'achil et aviv ma\u8217'aser ani} \u8212- a curse on one who feeds his father {\i ma\u8217'aser ani}. The final note: even if technically permitted, {\i zeila bei milta} \u8212- it is simply not appropriate. The spirit of {\i kibbud} is not satisfied by finding technical workarounds.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Extremes & Tests\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Eliezer\u8217's Standard\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} returns directly to the wallet question introduced in the Tarfon narrative. A {\i beraita} asks: how far must {\i kibbud av va\u8217'em} extend? Rabbi Eliezer answers: {\i k\u8217'dei she-yitol arnaki v\u8217'yizrakenu l\u8217'yam b\u8217'fanav v\u8217'eino machlimo} \u8212- to the extent that if your parent took your wallet and threw it into the sea in front of you, you would not become upset. This is the measure of {\i kibbud}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might ask: does this preclude later legal recourse? Can the child not sue for damages? The {\i gemara} does not address that directly here, but the emotional and behavioral standard is clear: in the moment, there can be no anger and no shaming of the parent. If the obligation is {\i mishel ben}, the money belongs to the child, and the parent is simply destroying the child\u8217's property. The test is whether the child\u8217's composure holds even then.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This connects back to the {\i gemara} from twenty {\i dafim} earlier, in the {\i sugya} of {\i harei at mekudeshet li} \u8212- where a woman takes the {\i kiddushin} money and throws it into the sea. Does that act represent acceptance and exercise of ownership, or rejection? Who would be entitled to hire divers to retrieve it? The emotional and legal parsing of who owns a thrown object, and what that act means, surfaces across multiple {\i sugyot} in this tractate.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then raises: {\i ve-eima be-mishel av} \u8212- perhaps we can explain the wallet test from the perspective of {\i mishel av}. If the money is the parent\u8217's money to begin with, why would the child get angry? The {\i nafka mina} would be in the case of {\i ra\u8217'uy le-yorsho} \u8212- property that the child stands to inherit. Even if the parent is spending their own money, a child who knows they will one day inherit that estate might resent seeing it wasted. The wallet test thus applies even in the {\i mishel av} framework: the child must not become angry even when witnessing the squandering of what they might eventually inherit.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Huna\u8217's Test\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This leads to one of the most unsettling stories on the {\i daf}: {\i ve-chi ha de-Rabbah bar Rav Huna de-Rav Huna kara shirai de-anpai Rabbah berei} \u8212- Rav Huna tore an expensive silk garment in front of his son Rabbah. {\i Ve-amar eizil achezi i ratach i lo ratach} \u8212- and Rav Huna said: I am going to go and see whether he gets angry or not. He was testing his son\u8217's {\i kibbud av va\u8217'em}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is a striking story, and the {\i gemara} immediately recognizes its {\i halachic} problems. First: perhaps Rabbah will get angry \u8212- and then {\i ka avar al lifnei iver} \u8212- Rav Huna has set up a situation in which his own son is caused to sin. You do not set traps designed to make people transgress. Second: even if we say Rav Huna was confident Rabbah would not get angry and had pre-emptively been {\i mochel} on being bothered, there remains {\i bal tashchit} \u8212- the prohibition against wantonly destroying objects of value. Third: if Rabbah knew his father was deliberately testing him, perhaps he would not get angry precisely because he understood it was a test \u8212- in which case the test proves nothing.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} resolves each objection in turn, though the resolutions have a somewhat forced quality. On the {\i lifnei iver} problem: {\i de-machil lei} \u8212- Rav Huna must have already signaled some kind of forgiveness, perhaps indicating to Rabbah in advance that this was a test and that anger would not constitute a genuine transgression in context. On {\i bal tashchit}: {\i de-avad lei be-pumbeioni} \u8212- he tore it in the hem, in a place where a tailor could repair it and the value would not be diminished. It looked dramatic but caused no actual material loss. On the problem of Rabbah knowing it was a test: {\i de-avad lei be-sha\u8217'at retichei} \u8212- Rav Huna did it at a moment when Rabbah was already emotionally agitated, already primed to react, making the self-restraint genuinely tested.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One reasonable reading of the story is that Rav Huna was simply not thinking clearly \u8212- an elderly man behaving erratically \u8212- and the {\i gemara} is working to retroactively justify actions that were in the moment instinctive rather than calculated. This would itself be a kind of commentary on the earlier observation about elderly parents whose behavior may reflect cognitive or emotional decline.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 There is an extended {\i sugya} within this section about a case from {\i Sanhedrin}: Rabbi Yechezkel asked his son Rami about what happens when those condemned to {\i sreifah} become mixed among those condemned to {\i sekilah}. Rabbi Shimon holds they should all receive {\i sekilah}, on the grounds that {\i sreifah} is more severe \u8212- the lesser punishment is applied. Rav Yehudah, the son, responded: {\i Abba, lo titnaya hachi} \u8212- Father, don\u8217't teach it this way. He proposed an alternative rationale: since the majority of those present are {\i niskalin}, the rule of {\i rov} governs, and there is no need to appeal to relative severity of punishment at all. His father pushed back from the {\i seifa} of the {\i mishna}, where the {\i chachamim} seem to require the severity-based rationale. Rav Yehudah held that the {\i seifa} represents the {\i chachamim} responding to Rabbi Shimon, pressing him on his own terms \u8212- and so {\i rov} remains the primary principle while the severity-comparison belongs to the internal {\i machloket} between Rabbi Shimon and the {\i chachamim}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The entire discussion is brought here specifically because of the mode of speech: Rav Yehudah said to his father, {\i lo titnaya hachi} \u8212- don\u8217't teach it like this. That directness is what occasions Shmuel\u8217's rebuke in the following subsection.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shmuel Rebuke\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Shmuel addressed Rav Yehudah \u8212- calling him by the nickname {\i Shinena}, which may refer to his sharpness in learning or to his broad smile \u8212- and said: {\i lo teima lei le-avuch hachi} \u8212- do not speak to your father this way. A {\i beraita} is cited: if you see your father violating a matter of Torah, do not say {\i Abba avarta al divrei Torah} \u8212- Father, you have transgressed the Torah. Rather say: {\i Abba, kach katuv ba-Torah} \u8212- Father, it is written thus in the Torah. The deferential formulation presents the teaching without accusation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But even that formulation, the {\i gemara} pushes further, might cause the parent {\i tza\u8217'ar} \u8212- distress \u8212- to be told that the Torah says something that contradicts what they just did. Therefore the most refined approach: {\i ela omer lo Abba mikra katuv ba-Torah kach} \u8212- say, Father, there is a {\i pasuk} that reads such-and-such. Do not even frame it as \u8220"this is written in the Torah\u8221" with its implicit weight of accusation; simply cite the verse. Whether a given practice is {\i d\u8217'oraita} in the explicit sense or derived through {\i drashot} is itself a layered question \u8212- the {\i Rambam} in {\i Sefer Ha-Mitzvot} and {\i Peirush Ha-Mishna} is careful to distinguish between what is literally written in the Torah and what is derived through rabbinic interpretation, even when the latter carries {\i de\u8217'oraita} weight. But the {\i halachic} principle here is clear: deferential speech toward a parent who is erring requires the softest possible framing.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Prioritization\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Father vs Mitzvah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Elazar ben Masya raises a case: his father asks him for a glass of water, and at that same moment he has a {\i mitzvah} to perform. Should he set aside {\i kibbud av} and go do the {\i mitzvah}? His reasoning: {\i she-ani ve-abba chayavim be-mitzvah} \u8212- both my father and I are equally obligated in this {\i mitzvah}. Since the father is no less obligated than the son, the son need not subordinate himself to serve his father when an equal obligation beckons. The force of the question depends on the nature of the {\i mitzvah} as well \u8212- a {\i mitzvah overes}, one that will expire if not done immediately, poses the case more acutely than one that can wait.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Delegation Rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Issi ben Yehuda offers a more nuanced principle: {\i im efshar le-mitzvah le-asos al yedei acherim, te\u8217'aseh al yedei acherim, vayeilechu be-kibbud av} \u8212- if the {\i mitzvah} can be performed by others, let others do it, and you go fulfill {\i kibbud av}. The category of {\i mitzvah efshar la\u8217'asot al yedei acherim} \u8212- a {\i mitzvah} that others can discharge \u8212- versus a {\i mitzvah} that only you personally can fulfill appears across many {\i halachic} contexts. A {\i mitzvah shebegufo} or a {\i mitzvah} for which you bear a specific personal obligation cannot be handed off; nobody else can hear the {\i megillah} on your behalf. But communal obligations, or tasks that any qualified person can do, can in principle be delegated \u8212- and in such cases, {\i kibbud av va\u8217'em} takes precedence.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This principle has been applied in serious contemporary {\i halachic} discussions, including in {\i chardal} communities regarding whether a {\i yeshiva} student may serve as an EMT or volunteer medic during his {\i yeshiva} years. If there are other qualified individuals who can serve, it may be argued that ambulance work is a {\i mitzvah efshar la\u8217'asot al yedei acherim}, and the student\u8217's primary obligation is to remain in learning. Other communities reason differently, and the {\i gemara} explicitly records {\i beraitot} about {\i yeshiva} students joining military campaigns when necessary. The tension between different categories of obligation, and the finite hours in a day, is precisely what the {\i gemara} is navigating throughout this material.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Matna\u8217's Ruling\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Matna provides the {\i pesak}: {\i halacha k\u8217'Isi ben Yehuda} \u8212- the {\i halacha} follows Issi ben Yehuda. When a {\i mitzvah} can be performed by others, {\i kibbud av va\u8217'em} takes precedence and the child must attend to the parent\u8217's needs. When the {\i mitzvah} is one that only the child can perform \u8212- {\i kibbud av va\u8217'em} yields.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Teacher\u8217's Honor\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Forgiveness Limits\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Chisda (as reported through a chain of transmission: Rav Yitzchak bar Shila citing Rav Matna citing Rav Chisda) establishes a fundamental asymmetry: {\i ha-av she-machal al kevodo, kevodo machul} \u8212- if a parent forgives their own honor, waives the {\i kibbud} owed to them, that honor is indeed forgiven. The {\i mitzvah} is the parent\u8217's to waive. But {\i ha-rav she-machal al kevodo, ein kevodo machul} \u8212- if a teacher forgives their honor, that forgiveness is not effective. The honor owed to a teacher cannot be extinguished simply because the teacher consents.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The reason is embedded in the nature of the obligation. The {\i beraita} already established that {\i ein kavod ela Torah} \u8212- there is no honor except for Torah. The honor you show your teacher is not ultimately about the person; it is about the Torah they represent and transmit. And because it is {\i kavod ha-Torah}, not merely {\i kavod ha-rav}, the teacher cannot personally waive something that is not wholly theirs to waive.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Yosef dissents: {\i afilu ha-rav she-machal al kevodo, kevodo machul} \u8212- even a teacher who forgives their honor has forgiven it. His proof: {\i va-Adonai holech lifneihem yomam} \u8212- God led the entire camp of Israel through the desert, and by doing so, God was in a sense {\i mochel} on God\u8217's own {\i kavod}, descending to lead and to serve. If God could be {\i mochel} on divine honor, a teacher can certainly waive their own.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Torah Ownership\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava responds sharply: {\i heichi hashta? Hatam Hakadosh Baruch Hu alma dilei, ve-Torah dilei, machil likra. Hacha Torah dilei hi?} \u8212- How can you compare? There, God\u8217's world and God\u8217's Torah \u8212- so God can waive the honor. But here, is the Torah really the teacher\u8217's Torah?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This challenge is then answered by Rava himself, who resolves it with a counterintuitive move. He cites the verse {\i u\u8217've-Torato yehgeh yomam va-lailah} \u8212- and in {\i his} Torah he meditates day and night. The possessive {\i Torato} is interpreted as meaning that a person who truly engages with Torah acquires it as their own \u8212- it becomes {\i his} Torah. And therefore the honor a student shows a teacher {\i is} directed at the teacher\u8217's own Torah, not merely at something borrowed from God. On this reading, Rava affirms \u8212- rather than overturns \u8212- the position that the teacher\u8217's honor is genuine and personal, and therefore the teacher {\i does} have the right to waive it. The {\i sugya} thereby comes to rest on a view close to Rav Yosef\u8217's, though the reasoning is refined: the teacher\u8217's Torah is genuinely theirs because they have acquired it through total immersion, and they may therefore be {\i mochel} on the {\i kavod} that flows from it.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Standing & Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Wine & Standing\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} closes with a pair of narratives about standing as a form of honor. Rava was serving wine at his son\u8217's wedding. Rav Papa and Rav Huna the son of Rabbi Yehoshua were present, and they stood up before Rav Mari and Rav Pinchas the son of Rav Chisda \u8212- but they did not stand before Rava. Those present said: {\i hani rabbanan rabbanan, ve-hani rabbanan lav rabbanan} \u8212- those are real rabbis, and those are not real rabbis. Rava was offended. The story illustrates both the social stakes of the standing-up obligation and the judgment being made in real time about who merits such recognition.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hidur Rising\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Similarly, Rav Papa was serving wine at the wedding of his son Abba Mar \u8212- whose name was likely Papa bar Papa, giving rise to the title {\i Abba Mar brei} \u8212- and he poured wine for Yitzchak his son and for Rav Yehudah, but did not stand up for them. Those present were offended. Yet the {\i gemara} notes: {\i afilu hachi hidar mavdei} \u8212- even so, they performed a {\i hidur}. A {\i hidur} is a partial rising, a gesture of deference that falls short of full standing \u8212- one begins to rise without fully completing the motion. This qualified rising is recognized as a meaningful intermediate form of honor, distinct from both full standing and complete indifference.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Here the {\i daf} concludes.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Kiddushin 33\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rav Mochel on Kevodo\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Mochel - Intro\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i daf} opens on 33b, picking up where the previous session left off \u8212- namely, the question of whether a {\i rav} can be {\i mochel} on his {\i kavod}. The Gemara had already established that a father can waive honor owed to him, but the case of a {\i rav} is more complex. A {\i rebbe} does not merely represent himself; he embodies {\i kevod hatorah}. One might therefore argue that even if he personally does not object to being treated without ceremony, the {\i Torah} he represents demands that others show him respect regardless. Nevertheless, the Gemara concludes with a powerful statement about the nature of {\i Torah sheba\u8217'al peh} and the personal investment it entails. Because {\i Torah} becomes genuinely one\u8217's own through the process of learning and transmission \u8212- one has, in a real sense, an ownership stake in it \u8212- a {\i rav} can indeed be {\i mochel} on his {\i kavod}. The very intimacy of that ownership is what grants him the standing to waive the honor.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Even After Mechila\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Yet the Gemara\u8217's conclusion is not that the obligation disappears entirely. The line that opens this session reads: {\i afilu hachi hiddur me\u8217'avad leh ba\u8217'ei} \u8212- even so, some form of {\i hadarta} must still be shown. The {\i Rambam} leaves this deliberately open-ended: even if the {\i rav} has been {\i mochel}, his students remain in a curious state of being simultaneously exempt and obligated. They are exempt from the full requirement, yet still bound to demonstrate some gesture of respect. {\i Rashi} is characteristically precise: {\i linua me\u8217'at ke\u8217'ilu rotze la\u8217'amod mipanav} \u8212- one should shift slightly, as if intending to rise. Anyone familiar with {\i yeshiva} culture will recognize what is being described \u8212- the partial rising from one\u8217's seat, the so-called tush-move, which is not mere social awkwardness but an expression of this very {\i halakhic} balance. The {\i rebbe} has waived the full honor; his standing as a representative of {\i Torah} cannot, however, be entirely ignored. The partial gesture embodies that equilibrium precisely.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Nasi Cannot Mechel\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 With that established, Rav Ashi introduces a further distinction. Even according to the opinion that a {\i rav} may be {\i mochel} on his {\i kavod}, a {\i nasi} cannot do so: {\i nasi shemachal al kevodo ein kevodo machul}. The {\i nasi} in this context refers to the role of the exilarch in {\i Eretz Yisrael} \u8212- a uniquely combined office of political and religious leadership, embodied most famously by {\i Rabbi Yehuda HaNasi} and his descendants. This convergence of authority is distinctive to {\i Eretz Yisrael}; in {\i Bavel} the political and rabbinic leaderships were separate institutions.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara\u8217's reasoning seems to be that certain institutions of authority are so fundamental to the structure of society that no individual can simply choose to dissolve them. The framework of social order \u8212- the subjection of persons to governance and law \u8212- is not a personal privilege that the leader may surrender at will. Just as the Gemara in {\i Pirkei Avot} teaches that one should pray for the welfare of the government because without it people would consume one another, the authority of the {\i nasi} represents something too structurally necessary to be quietly waived.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabban Gamliel Story\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara raises an objection from a {\i baraita}: {\i Rabbi Eliezer, Rabbi Yehoshua}, and {\i Rabbi Tzadok} were reclining at a {\i beis hamishteh} celebrating the wedding of {\i Rabban Gamliel\u8217's} son. {\i Rabban Gamliel} \u8212- who was himself the {\i nasi} \u8212- was moving among the guests and pouring their drinks. The host serving drinks was a known practice, but the {\i nasi} doing so represents at minimum a degree of {\i mechila} on his {\i kavod}; one who insists on the dignity of his position does not take on a serving role toward others.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Eliezer vs.\u160 ?Yehoshua\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabban Gamliel} offered the cup to {\i Rabbi Eliezer}, who refused to accept it. He would not allow the {\i nasi} to occupy a role of service toward him, even at the {\i nasi\u8217's} own initiative. {\i Rabban Gamliel} then offered it to {\i Rabbi Yehoshua}, who accepted. {\i Rabbi Eliezer} challenged him: {\i mah zeh Yehoshua? Anu yoshvim v\u8217'Rabban Gamliel omeid umashkeh aleinu?} \u8212- How can we remain seated while the {\i nasi} stands and serves us?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Yehoshua} responded by invoking a greater precedent: {\i matzinu gadol mimenu sheshimash} \u8212- we have found someone even greater than {\i Rabban Gamliel} who served others. {\i Avraham} was the {\i gadol hador}, the greatest of his generation, and yet {\i v\u8217'hu omeid aleihem} \u8212- he stood over his guests and served them. Nor can one deflect this by saying that {\i Avraham} knew they were angels and therefore this was somehow exceptional \u8212- {\i k\u8217'arviyim nidmu lo}, they appeared to him as ordinary Arabs. Yet he served them. All the more so, {\i Rabbi Yehoshua} argued, we should be willing to allow {\i Rabban Gamliel} to attend to us.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might note that a distinction exists: {\i Avraham} was a {\i gadol hador} but not technically a {\i nasi} in the institutional sense, and the question of whether {\i mechilas kavod} applies specifically to political authority is somewhat separate from the question of how great a person is. Moreover, while the guests {\i Avraham} served were not members of his own clan, the analogy is not exact. Still, it is the argument {\i Rabbi Yehoshua} deploys.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tzadok\u8217's Critique\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Tzadok} then offered a striking counter-critique that reframes the entire conversation. {\i Ad masai atem manichim kevodo shel Makom v\u8217'atem oskim bichvod habriyos?} \u8212- For how long will you set aside the honor of God and occupy yourselves with the honor of human beings? If one wants to invoke a precedent for serving those beneath oneself, the proper model is not {\i Avraham} but {\i Hakadosh Baruch Hu} Himself. God causes the winds to blow, the clouds to rise, the rain to descend, the earth to bring forth produce \u8212- He effectively sets the table for every creature. If God, who is the ultimate authority, provides for every human being in this direct and personal fashion, then surely {\i Rabban Gamliel\u8217's} attending to his guests involves no compromise of his position.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This argument carries interesting implications. On one reading, {\i Rabbi Tzadok} is saying that {\i Rabban Gamliel} is not actually being {\i mochel al kevodo} at all \u8212- acting in a manner that provides for and serves others is not a diminishment of one\u8217's authority; it is a {\i Godly} act, an expression of {\i vehalchata bidrachav}. On another reading, even if such service could in theory be considered {\i mechila}, it is entirely legitimate because God Himself models it. Either way, the Gemara concludes from this episode that a {\i nasi} apparently can be {\i mochel} \u8212- and Rav Ashi\u8217's statement is therefore reformulated: even according to the opinion that a {\i nasi} who waives his honor has indeed waived it, a {\i melech} cannot do so.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 King Cannot Mechel\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The verse invoked for the {\i melech} is {\i som tasim alecha melech} \u8212- you shall place upon yourself a king, with the word {\i alecha} (\u8220"upon you\u8221") signifying that the king\u8217's authority must always remain elevated over the people. His {\i aima}, his commanding presence, must be constant and may not be dissolved. The Gemara draws an analogy to practical governance: just as certain roles in civil society carry enhanced legal protections precisely because the stability they represent cannot be treated lightly, so too the authority of the {\i melech} cannot be compromised through personal {\i mechila}. This is ultimately about what is necessary for society to function \u8212- {\i u-mitpallel lishloma shel malkhut} \u8212- and no individual, not even the king himself, may unilaterally dissolve that.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Scriptural Basis\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 The Pasuk\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having concluded the discussion of {\i mechilas kavod}, the Gemara now turns to the biblical foundation of the entire subject. {\i Tanu rabbanan} \u8212- the Sages taught in a {\i baraita}: {\i mipnei seiva takum ve-hadarta pnei zaken ve-yareta me-elokecha ani Hashem} \u8212- before the hoary head you shall rise, and you shall honor the elderly, and you shall fear your God, I am the Lord. At first glance, the verse sounds like a command to respect the elderly broadly. The {\i baraita} will now analyze precisely what it requires and from whom.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tanna Kamma View\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i tanna kamma} begins with a potential misreading: {\i yakhol afilu mipnei zaken ashmai} \u8212- perhaps the verse obligates one to rise even before an elder who is wicked, or more neutrally in the view of {\i Tosafot}, one who is simply unlearned. The {\i talmud lomar} is the word {\i zaken} itself. {\i Seiva} emphasizes white hair and age, but {\i zaken} carries a different meaning: {\i ve-ein zaken ela chakham} \u8212- {\i zaken} denotes a sage. The prooftext is {\i asfa li shivim ish miziknei Yisrael} \u8212- gather for me seventy men from the elders of Israel \u8212- with the phrase {\i asher yadata ki hem ziknei ha\u8217'am} suggesting that what God {\i knows} about them, the quality that identifies them as {\i ziknei ha\u8217'am}, is not merely their age but their recognized wisdom and authority. Age alone is therefore insufficient; {\i zaken} in the {\i pasuk} means a Torah sage, and {\i seiva} is understood in light of that.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Yosei HaGlili\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Yosei HaGlili} agrees that {\i zaken} means a sage, but arrives there differently: {\i ein zaken ela mi shekanah chochmah} \u8212- {\i zaken} refers to one who has {\i acquired} wisdom. {\i Rashi} notes the play on words: {\i ze kanah} \u8212- this one acquired. The prooftext is {\i Hashem kanani reishit darko}, the emphasis falling on the root {\i kinyan}, acquisition.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i baraita} then asks: {\i yakhol ya\u8217'amod mi-panav mi-makom rachok} \u8212- perhaps one must rise even from a great distance? The {\i talmud lomar} is {\i takum ve-hadarta} read together \u8212- the rising and the honoring are linked. Several inferences flow from this pairing. First, {\i takum} is qualified by {\i ve-hadarta}: a rising that constitutes genuine honor ({\i hiddur}) is one performed in proximity, not from across the room. Second, the question arises whether {\i ve-hadarta} refers to something entirely independent of {\i takum} \u8212- perhaps lavishing the sage with gifts. The {\i talmud lomar} answers: {\i ma kima she-ein ba chisron kis af hiddur she-ein bo chisron kis} \u8212- just as rising costs nothing, so too the {\i hiddur} required involves no monetary expenditure. Honor is expressed through gesture and manner, not through financial outlay.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Third, might one be obligated to rise inside a bathhouse or while using a toilet? Again {\i takum ve-hadarta} \u8212- {\i lo amarti kima ela be-makom she-yesh hiddur}, rising is only required in a context where {\i hiddur} is socially operative. In a bathhouse, particularly the inner section where all are unclothed, the social distinctions that give {\i kavod} its meaning are suspended. Everyone is reduced to the same fundamental human condition; the context does not support the kind of status-based respect the verse demands. Indeed, the {\i halakha} holds that one does not even yield one\u8217's place in a bathhouse entrance to show deference \u8212- in that setting, social standing simply does not assert itself the same way.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Finally, {\i yakhol yaatzim einav mi-panav she-lo yirehu} \u8212- perhaps one may close one\u8217's eyes so as not to see the sage and thereby avoid the obligation. The {\i talmud lomar} invokes the conclusion of the {\i pasuk}: {\i ve-yareta me-elokecha} \u8212- you shall fear your God. This phrase always signals a {\i davar ha-masur la-lev}, something known only to the heart and hidden from others. Closing one\u8217's eyes to avoid seeing the sage is precisely such an act \u8212- it is visible to God alone, and therefore the obligation to fear God applies directly to it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Issi ben Yehuda\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Issi ben Yehuda} reads the verse differently: {\i mipnei seiva takum}, and {\i afilu kol seiva be-mashma} \u8212- the word {\i seiva} includes any person of white hair, any elder. The obligation to rise is not limited to Torah sages; it extends to any elderly person on the basis of age alone.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 One or Two Categories\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 With three positions before it \u8212- the {\i tanna kamma}, {\i Rabbi Yosei HaGlili}, and {\i Issi ben Yehuda} \u8212- the Gemara asks whether the first two are in fact distinct. {\i Rabbi Yosei HaGlili hainu Tanna Kamma} \u8212- aren\u8217't they saying the same thing? Both hold that {\i zaken} means a sage. The Gemara answers: {\i ika beinaihu yanik ve-chakkim} \u8212- the difference is a young Torah sage. For the {\i tanna kamma}, both dimensions are necessary: {\i seiva} and {\i zaken} combine, meaning an older person who is also a sage. A young prodigy, however brilliant, does not qualify. For {\i Rabbi Yosei HaGlili}, wisdom acquired ({\i zeh kanah chochmah}) is sufficient on its own, independent of age.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tanna Kamma vs.\u160 ?R. Yosei\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara probes further: what is {\i Rabbi Yosei HaGlili\u8217's} reason for separating {\i seiva} and {\i zaken} into distinct terms if in the end only {\i zaken} \u8212- the sage \u8212- matters? {\i Ei salka da\u8217'atakh k\u8217'de-amar tanna kamma} \u8212- if you thought like the {\i tanna kamma}, the Torah could have simply written {\i mipnei seiva zaken takum ve-hadarta}, combining them cleanly. {\i Mai shna d\u8217'palginhu rachmana} \u8212- why did the Torah separate them? {\i Le-meimra d\u8217'hai lav hai v\u8217'hai lav hai} \u8212- to indicate they are distinct categories. If so, one might reasonably conclude that each is independently sufficient: either a sage or an elder. But both {\i Rashi} and {\i Tosafot} resist this reading and explain that once {\i zaken} is established as independently sufficient, it effectively absorbs {\i seiva} \u8212- the result is a single category (the sage), and the mention of {\i seiva} serves another purpose.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} adds a nuanced intermediate reading of {\i Rabbi Yosei HaGlili}\u8217's position: {\i seiva} is not meaningless but operates as a supplementary factor. A modest Torah scholar who is also elderly falls into the {\i seiva} category; a young Torah giant falls into the {\i zaken} category. An average scholar who is young, or an ordinary elderly person with no Torah learning at all, would not qualify under either heading. The minimum threshold, in any case, is some degree of Torah knowledge \u8212- but age can compensate for a lower degree of scholarship, while exceptional scholarship can compensate for youth. This is how {\i Tosafot} explains why the verse mentions both terms even according to {\i Rabbi Yosei HaGlili}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i tanna kamma\u8217's} rationale for keeping the terms combined is then explained as well: the separation of {\i zaken} from {\i seiva} was necessary not to signal two independent categories, but to allow the word {\i zaken} to appear separately in the phrase {\i zaken ve-yareta} \u8212- the basis for the teaching that a {\i zaken} should not burden others by placing himself in situations that compel people to show him honor. Had the Torah written {\i seiva zaken} as a compound at the outset, that {\i derashah} would not have been available.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This point connects naturally to the broader question of what motivates the requirement in the first place. One might ask: is the respect demanded here purely about Torah knowledge quantitatively measured, or does it capture something about life experience and wisdom accumulated through years of living? The {\i tanna kamma\u8217's} insistence on combining age and scholarship suggests the latter \u8212- not raw learning alone, but the convergence of accumulated life experience with Torah authority. The question of whether life experience alone, without Torah, suffices is precisely what {\i Issi ben Yehuda} addresses, and what {\i Rabbi Yochanan\u8217's} ruling in his favor will eventually establish.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Kiuma and Hiddur\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Does Hiddur Cost Money\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara returns to re-examine the {\i gezeira shava} between {\i takum} and {\i ve-hadarta}. The question was posed: {\i yakhol yehadreno be-mamon} \u8212- perhaps {\i hiddur} means to honor the sage with gifts and money? The {\i talmud lomar} was {\i takum ve-hadarta}: just as {\i kima} (rising) involves no expenditure, so too {\i hiddur} involves no expenditure.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But the Gemara challenges this premise: {\i ve-kima leit beih chisron kis?} Who says rising up costs nothing? Consider the case of a craftsman drilling holes in pearls \u8212- work that commands an exceptionally high hourly wage. If he must stop mid-task to rise before a sage, reorient himself, and then resume, he may lose twenty minutes of very expensive labor. Rising, in that case, is far from free.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gezeira Shava\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara\u8217's resolution is to run the {\i hekesh} (analogy) in the opposite direction: {\i hekesh kima le-hiddur} \u8212- we compare {\i kima} to {\i hiddur} first. {\i Mah hiddur she-ein bo bitul af kima she-ein bah bitul} \u8212- just as {\i hiddur} does not require interrupting one\u8217's work, so too {\i kima} does not require interrupting one\u8217's work. The specific physical act of rising, from the perspective of {\i halakha}, is one that need not suspend ongoing labor.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Then the analogy reverses: {\i ve-akishana hiddur le-kima} \u8212- having established that {\i kima} involves no work-stoppage, and therefore no financial cost, we bring {\i hiddur} into alignment: {\i af hiddur she-ein bah chisron kis} \u8212- the {\i hiddur} required of a person is not something that costs money. It may take the form of verbal expression, attentive manner, or other non-monetary gestures; it does not extend to lavishing gifts.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Craftsworkers Exception\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 From this {\i gezeira shava} the Gemara draws a practical ruling: {\i ein ba\u8217'alei ummaniyot rasha\u8217'in la\u8217'amod mipnei talmidei chachamim be-sha\u8217'ah she-oskim be-melachtan} \u8212- craftsmen are not required (and {\i Tosafot} clarifies that {\i rasha\u8217'in} here means {\i required}, not literally {\i permitted}) to stand before Torah sages when they are engaged in their work.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} raises the obvious question: perhaps the exemption is specifically because they are working for an employer and rising would constitute stealing the employer\u8217's time \u8212- a concern that would be irrelevant if they were working independently, and in any case a different rationale than the one the Gemara was developing. {\i Tosafot} therefore offers an alternative: the better reading is simply that {\i rasha\u8217'in} means {\i obligated}, and the exemption applies broadly. The deeper logic, as emerges from the Gemara, seems to be less about financial cost and more about context: when a craftsman is visibly occupied with his work, failing to rise does not register as disrespect. Everyone understands why he remains seated \u8212- he is underneath the sink, he is mid-cut on the workbench. The social expectation of standing is simply not operative in that context.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Bikkurim Story\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara presses back with a {\i mishna}: when those bringing {\i bikkurim} entered {\i Yerushalayim}, all the craftspeople in the city would rise for them and inquire after their welfare \u8212- {\i acheinu anshei makom ploni, boachem le-shalom} \u8212- welcoming them warmly. This seems to show that craftsmen do interrupt their work to show honor. Why should it be different for a Torah sage?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Yochanan} answers directly: {\i mipneihem omdim mipnei talmidei chachamim ein omdim} \u8212- for those bearing {\i bikkurim} they rise; for Torah sages they do not (when working). {\i Rabbi Yosei bar Avin} explains the distinction: {\i bo re\u8217'eh kamma chaviva mitzvah be-sha\u8217'atah} \u8212- the lesson is how precious a {\i mitzvah} performed at its proper moment is. The {\i bikkurim} bearers are performing a {\i mitzvah} in its irreplaceable window of opportunity, and that particular form of devotion commands a heightened degree of public respect \u8212- even more than the standing honor owed to a Torah sage. The verse and the institution of {\i bikkurim} together generate a special demand on all who witness it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara counters: perhaps the case of {\i bikkurim} is distinctive for a more practical reason. {\i De-im ken ata machshilan le-atid lavo} \u8212- if no one greets the {\i bikkurim} bearers warmly, those who made the long journey from distant towns will feel unwelcomed and will be discouraged from making the trip in future years. The warmth shown them is as much about sustaining the institution of {\i bikkurim} for coming generations as it is about the honor of the present {\i mitzvah}. Either way, it is a special case, and the general rule remains: a craftsman engaged in his work need not rise for a Torah sage.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Turning back to an earlier point in the {\i baraita} \u8212- {\i yakhol ya\u8217'amod mi-panav mi-beit ha-kise u-mi-beit ha-merchat} \u8212- the Gemara addresses the bathhouse exception in more detail. {\i Rabbi Chiya} was sitting in a bathhouse when {\i Rabbi Shimon berabi} \u8212- the son of {\i Rebbe}, himself a figure of {\i nasi}-level standing \u8212- passed through, and {\i Rabbi Chiya} did not rise. {\i Rabbi Shimon} was offended and went to complain to his father {\i Rebbe}, saying: I taught him two-fifths of {\i Sefer Tehillim} and he did not rise before me.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is a striking Gemara on its own. The complaint is not that {\i Rabbi Shimon} is objectively a Torah giant before whom all must stand. The claim is personal: {\i I taught him}, making {\i Rabbi Chiya} his {\i talmid} in a {\i rebbe-talmid} relationship. The principle in {\i Pirkei Avot} that one who teaches even a single matter to another is called that person\u8217's {\i rebbe} and receives the forms of respect associated with that relationship is clearly operative here. The detail that the teaching was two-fifths of {\i Tehillim} is notable \u8212- this may not have been instruction in the most advanced levels of scholarship. Yet the {\i rebbe-talmid} bond it created was considered sufficient to generate an obligation to stand.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} elaborates on the structure of {\i Sefer Tehillim} in this context. Just as the Torah has five {\i chumashim}, so too {\i Sefer Tehillim} is divided into five books. {\i Rashi} understands {\i shnei chumashim} as two-fifths of {\i Sefer Tehillim}, using {\i chumash} in the sense of one-fifth. {\i Tosafot} adds that the five books of {\i Tehillim} can be identified by where the word {\i Amen} appears as a conclusion \u8212- each of the first four sections ends with {\i Amen}, marking its close, while the fifth (ending with {\i Hallelujah}) has no closing {\i Amen} since it is the end of the entire book. The five books begin respectively at Psalm 1, Psalm 42, Psalm 73, Psalm 90, and Psalm 107.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A second incident is brought: {\i Bar Kappara} (and some say {\i Rabbi Shmuel bar Rabbi Yossi}) was sitting in a bathhouse when {\i Rabbi Shimon berabi} passed by, and again no one rose. {\i Rabbi Shimon} reported this to {\i Rebbe}, saying he had taught them two-thirds of a third \u8212- that is, two-ninths \u8212- of {\i Torat Kohanim}, the {\i Sifra}. {\i Rebbe} responded: {\i shema bahen yoshev u-meharher} \u8212- perhaps they were sitting and reviewing internally what you had taught them, so absorbed in your Torah that they did not notice your passage. The defense is offered gently, even affectionately \u8212- {\i because} of your Torah they were too distracted to stand for you. The Gemara notes that this defense implies they would have been wrong not to stand, had they simply failed to notice. And since they were reviewing Torah, the question arises whether they were in the inner room of the bathhouse \u8212- where Torah thought is generally inappropriate \u8212- or the outer room. The Gemara resolves: they must have been in the outer room, since {\i Rabbah bar bar Chana} in the name of {\i Rabbi Yochanan} taught that one may engage in Torah thought in all places except a bathhouse and a privy. Alternatively, the Gemara suggests, perhaps they were in the inner room but the Torah was so embedded in their minds that they could not stop the thoughts even there \u8212- an involuntary {\i ones} of the intellect.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The bathhouse discussion thus bifurcates cleanly: the inner room, where everyone is unclothed, is a context in which social distinction is suspended and the obligation to rise does not apply. The outer room, where people remain partially clothed and social context is still operative, retains the full obligation. The exception for the inner room is not a license for disrespect but a recognition that {\i hiddur} is inherently contextual \u8212- it operates where the social conditions that give it meaning are present.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Standing Requirements\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Four Amot Rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i baraita} continues: {\i tanna eizehu kima she-yesh bah hiddur \u8212- havei omer ze arba amot} \u8212- what constitutes a rising that contains genuine {\i hiddur}? The answer is: within four {\i amot}. When the sage enters one\u8217's immediate zone of proximity, the rising is unambiguously directed toward him and personally felt by him. From across a room, standing is less clearly connected to the sage\u8217's presence and does not carry the same directness of honor.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Abaye\u8217's Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Abaye} introduces a qualification: {\i lo amran ella be-rabbo she-eino muvhak} \u8212- the four-{\i amot} rule applies only to a teacher who is not one\u8217's primary {\i rebbe}. For {\i rabbo muvhak}, one\u8217's principal teacher, the standard is {\i melo einav} \u8212- as long as the teacher is within eyesight, one must rise.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is genuinely striking. The Gemara had established that the {\i hiddur} of rising is most meaningful within four {\i amot} \u8212- in the sage\u8217's immediate space. Why, then, is the requirement {\i expanded} for one\u8217's primary teacher? The resolution lies in a different logic entirely. For one\u8217's {\i rabbo muvhak}, the act of rising is not primarily about demonstrating to others the respect due a great man. It is about the impossibility of remaining comfortably seated when the person from whom one has received the most Torah \u8212- who has shaped one\u8217's mind and soul \u8212- enters one\u8217's field of vision. The question is not how much the gesture communicates to onlookers; it is what remaining seated would mean for the {\i talmid} himself. One might also note, as raised in discussion, that the {\i rebbe} knows the {\i talmid} and understands why the student rises \u8212- the gesture is received personally in a way it cannot be when rising for an objective stranger across the room. There is also an element of cultivating the inner disposition of gratitude and reverence, not merely performing an outward act of honor.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Donkey Ear Story\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Abaye\u8217's} practice exemplified this. {\i Abaye miki hava chazi lei le-udna de-chamra de-Rav Yosef de-ati} \u8212- when {\i Abaye} would catch sight of the ear of {\i Rav Yosef\u8217's} donkey as it came into view \u8212- he would already rise. So attuned was he to his teacher\u8217's presence that a glimpse of the donkey\u8217's ear crossing into his field of vision was sufficient.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Attention Requirement\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then recounts an incident involving {\i Abaye} himself. He was riding his donkey along the riverbank when {\i Rav Mesharshiya} and other rabbis were sitting on the opposite bank. They were within his eyesight, and he within theirs, yet they did not rise. {\i Abaye} challenged them: {\i v\u8217'lav rabbo muvhak ana} \u8212- am I not your primary teacher? Why did you not stand? They replied: {\i lav ada\u8217'tin} \u8212- we were not paying attention.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Their answer illuminates a genuine difficulty with the {\i melo einav} standard. When a teacher is across a river rather than directly approaching, one\u8217's awareness of his presence may not reach the threshold that generates the felt obligation \u8212- not because the sight is physically absent but because it has not registered. The story implies that part of the {\i kavod} owed to a {\i rabbo muvhak} is precisely this attentiveness \u8212- remaining sufficiently present to one\u8217's surroundings that one notices the teacher even at a distance, and responds accordingly.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Zaken\u8217's Obligation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Don\u8217't Burden Others\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Shimon ben Elazar} asks: {\i minayin le-zaken she-lo yatriach} \u8212- how do we know that a {\i zaken} has a responsibility not to burden others with the obligation to show him honor? The source is the phrase {\i zaken ve-yareta} \u8212- the {\i zaken} himself must fear God, must be conscious of the weight he places on those around him. The {\i zaken} is not merely the passive recipient of honor; he has his own responsibility to minimize situations that compel others to demonstrate respect they might find burdensome.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is distinct from being {\i mochel al kevodo}. The {\i zaken} who avoids entering a crowded room does not thereby waive his {\i kavod} \u8212- he simply chooses not to invoke it unnecessarily.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Abaye Takes Detours\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Abaye} is invoked again: {\i naktinan de-makif chaye} \u8212- we have a tradition that one who takes a detour to avoid situations requiring others to rise for him will live a long life. {\i Abaye} practiced this himself: he would go around places where he knew people would be compelled to stand for him.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This makes for an interesting contrast with the preceding story. {\i Abaye} was {\i makpid} when {\i Rav Mesharshiya\u8217's} students failed to rise across the river \u8212- he insisted on the {\i kavod} when the situation called for it. Yet he simultaneously went out of his way to avoid creating those situations in the first place. The two practices are not contradictory. Seeking honor is not the same as insisting on it when the circumstance already demands it. The {\i zaken} does not circulate hunting for occasions where people must stand for him; but when those occasions arise, the obligation is real and cannot simply be waved away.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mata Mechasya Story\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then narrates an incident in which {\i Ravina} was sitting before {\i Rav Yirmeya of Diphti} when a certain man passed before them with his head uncovered. Ravina remarked on the person\u8217's {\i chutzpah} \u8212- how brazen to walk before rabbis without a head covering. {\i Rav Yirmeya} responded: {\i dilma mi-Mata Mechasya nihu de-gissei be-rabbanan} \u8212- perhaps he is from Mata Mechasya, where people are on such familiar terms with their rabbis that they have ceased to cover their heads in their presence.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The relevance of this story is not immediately obvious, but it speaks directly to the tension at the heart of this entire section. {\i Hiddur} is not only rising; it encompasses other forms of social respect, including, apparently, covering one\u8217's head in the presence of a Torah scholar. The comment about Mata Mechasya carries what reads as a gentle but pointed critique: when rabbis make themselves so accessible, so thoroughly {\i mochel al kevodo}, that ordinary marks of deference fall away, those coming from that community may carry habits that are offensive elsewhere and that reflect an erosion of appropriate {\i kavod hatorah}. The distinction between {\i Abaye\u8217's} practice \u8212- avoiding situations that force others to show honor, while insisting on it when the situation arises \u8212- and the culture implied by the Mata Mechasya remark is meaningful. {\i Abaye} did not allow his {\i kavod} to be simply abandoned; he tried not to impose it unnecessarily. That is very different from a community in which the rabbis have become so casual that the whole framework of {\i kevod hatorah} has weakened.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Respect for Elders\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Halakha ke-Issi\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now turns to {\i Issi ben Yehuda\u8217's} position that {\i mipnei seiva takum} includes any elderly person, and {\i Rabbi Yochanan} rules: {\i halakha ke-Issi ben Yehuda}. The obligation to rise extends beyond Torah sages to elderly people generally.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Yochanan and Elders\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Yochanan} himself would rise before elderly non-Jews ({\i sabei de-armai}). His reasoning: {\i kamma harpatkei adu alaiahu de-hanei} \u8212- how many life events, trials, and experiences have passed over this person. The sheer accumulation of what a long life entails \u8212- the navigating of loss, hardship, uncertainty, and the weight of years \u8212- commands a form of respect in its own right. This is a powerful statement about the intrinsic dignity of a life richly and fully lived, entirely independent of Torah learning.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava\u8217's Approach\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rava}, however, did not rise before elderly non-Jews, though he was not dismissive of the principle. {\i Mikam lo kai, chizur avid leh} \u8212- he would not fully rise, but he would perform a {\i chizur}, a slight lift from his chair. {\i Rava\u8217's} approach represents a calibrated balance: life experience commands genuine acknowledgment, but a Torah sage does not rise fully before someone whose distinction is not Torah. The honor of {\i Torah} cannot be entirely subordinated to the honor of mere age.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Personal vs.\u160 ?Servant Help\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Abaye yahiv yada le-saba} \u8212- {\i Abaye} would offer his hand to elderly people, helping them walk and providing personal assistance. He gave of himself directly, this great Torah sage extending personal service. {\i Rava}, again consistent with his more tempered approach, {\i meshader shluchei} \u8212- would send his attendants to assist, rather than doing so himself. {\i Rav Nachman} similarly sent attendants, {\i meshader guzzei}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Nachman\u8217's Logic\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rav Nachman} articulated the logic behind his restraint explicitly: {\i amar ilav Torah, kamma Nachman bar Abba ika ba-shuka} \u8212- if not for Torah, how many {\i Nachmans bar Abba} are there in the market? What distinguishes me? Torah alone. To offer personal service to an elderly person would imply a kind of equivalence \u8212- that life experience and Torah stand on the same level \u8212- and that, {\i Rav Nachman} felt, compromises the unique and surpassing claim of {\i Torah}. It is not that age deserves no respect; it is that the representative of Torah cannot act in a way that implicitly diminishes what Torah represents. Hence the mediated response: assistance through an attendant acknowledges the elderly person\u8217's dignity without the Torah sage himself performing an act of personal service that would blur this distinction.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Frequency Limit\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Twice Daily Limit\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rav Yannai} teaches: {\i ein talmid chakham rasha\u8217'i la\u8217'amod mipnei rabbo ella shacharit ve-arvit kedei she-lo yehei kevodo merubbeh mi-kevod shamayim} \u8212- a Torah student is not permitted (or, as {\i Tosafot} notes, not {\i required}) to rise before his {\i rebbe} more than twice a day. The reason: one\u8217's honor of a {\i rebbe} should not exceed one\u8217's honor of Heaven. God receives acknowledgment through {\i Keriat Shema} in the morning and evening; the {\i rebbe} should not receive more formal rising than that.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rashi} formulates this in terms of {\i shepenei yotzro eino mekabbel ella shacharit ve-arvit} \u8212- the Creator Himself receives formal acknowledgment only morning and evening. Whether one accounts for {\i Mincha} by combining it with {\i Ma\u8217'ariv}, or whether the reference is specifically to {\i Keriat Shema} rather than {\i Tefilla}, the principle is clear: there is a ceiling on frequency.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The practical context matters. If a {\i rebbe} is present with his students throughout the day \u8212- in the same {\i yeshiva}, never leaving and returning \u8212- then just as one does not recite a new {\i berakha} on each lightning strike during a continuous storm, one does not rise each time the {\i rebbe} passes through. But if the {\i rebbe} departs and returns, each arrival may renew the obligation. The twice-daily limit likely speaks to the case of continuous presence, where morning and evening framings of the relationship suffice to express the ongoing honor, without requiring a fresh rising every time the teacher moves through the room.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Kiddushin 34\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Kibud HaRav\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Standing Req\u8217't\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The sugya opens on 33b with a ruling attributed to Rav Aivo in the name of Rav Yannai: {\i ain talmid chacham rashai la\u8217'amod mipnei rabbo ella shacharis v\u8217'arvis} \u8212- a {\i talmid chacham} is not required, or as Tosafot clarifies, not permitted, to stand before his {\i rebbe} more than twice a day, morning and evening. The rationale is {\i k\u8217'dai shelo yihei kvodo merubeh mi\u8217'kvod shamayim} \u8212- so that the honor shown to the {\i rebbe} should not exceed the honor shown to heaven, which is likewise marked by fixed, discrete moments of recognition rather than a continuous posture of deference.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i meitivei} challenges this: Rabbi Shimon ben Elazar teaches that a sage should not burden his students unnecessarily, as derived from {\i zaken v\u8217'yareita} \u8212- the elder too must be mindful of God and not overreach the honor due him. The question pressed against our ruling is: if students are only obligated morning and evening, then in any given moment the {\i rebbe} could know whether the obligation has already been discharged. There would be no burden at all! The force of the challenge is that it implies students must actually stand any time they see their {\i rebbe} throughout the day \u8212- {\i kulei yoma} \u8212- and it is in that context that the {\i rebbe} is told not to force the issue.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} rejects the challenge: the rule remains morning and evening only, and even for those two moments, the {\i rebbe} should try to avoid placing even that minimal burden on his students if it is at all possible.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafot\u8217's Limits\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot raises a pointed difficulty. Earlier in the {\i gemara}, Rava became visibly upset when he passed students on the other side of a river and they failed to stand for him. But if the obligation is only morning and evening, perhaps they had already stood for him in the morning, and perhaps he simply was not aware of it \u8212- why was he so aggrieved?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot resolves this by drawing a critical distinction: the rule limiting standing to morning and evening applies exclusively to students who are {\i hadarin b\u8217'veis harav} \u8212- those living in the {\i rebbe}\u8217's household or, by analogy, students in a {\i yeshiva} setting who are in continuous proximity to their teacher throughout the day. In such a setting, everyone can be presumed to have stood in the morning, and there is therefore no concern of disrespect. But students who encounter their {\i rebbe} only intermittently are obligated to stand even a hundred times a day, {\i afilu meiah p\u8217'amim bayom}, because the concern of {\i chashad} \u8212- suspicion in the eyes of onlookers \u8212- is very real. A bystander who sees a {\i rebbe} enter a room and no one rise will draw adverse conclusions about the students\u8217' conduct.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot adds a further nuance: even among resident {\i yeshiva} students, if visitors arrive, everyone must stand \u8212- because the entire exemption rests on communal awareness that the obligation has been fulfilled earlier. When outsiders are present, that shared awareness no longer obtains, and the concern for {\i chashad} is reactivated.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This notion of {\i chashad} is deeply bound up with {\i kavod} itself. Honor, after all, operates in a public register \u8212- it is not merely a private sentiment between two people but a social reality, and the {\i gemara} elsewhere acknowledges that the parameters of {\i kavod} shift depending on social context.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Elazar Warning\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Elazar sharpens the stakes considerably: {\i kol talmid chacham she\u8217'eino omed mipnei rabbo nikra rasha} \u8212- any {\i talmid chacham} who fails to stand before his {\i rebbe} is called wicked. Moreover, {\i eino maarich yamim} \u8212- he will not have long days \u8212- and {\i talmudo mishtakeiach} \u8212- his Torah will be forgotten. The proof-text cited is {\i v\u8217'tov lo yihiyeh lara\u8217'sha} \u8212- there will be no good for the wicked, where \u8220"good\u8221" is understood as Torah, the highest good for a {\i talmid chacham} \u8212- {\i v\u8217'lo yaarich yamim k\u8217'tzel asher einennu yarei milifnei Elokim} \u8212- and he will not lengthen his days like a shadow, because he has no fear before God.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yirah Link\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This raises the question: what precisely is this {\i mora}, this fear? The {\i gemara} answers by citing the continuation of the {\i pasuk} in {\i vahadata pnei zaken} \u8212- {\i v\u8217'yareita mei\u8217'Elokecha} \u8212- and you shall fear your God. The {\i gemara} identifies this reverence as referring to the act of standing up. As Tosafot notes, the {\i pasuk} of {\i v\u8217'hadarta pnei zaken} concludes with {\i v\u8217'yareita mei\u8217'Elokecha}, thus linking the act of standing before an elder directly to the fear of God.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} immediately asks: but this phrase {\i v\u8217'yareita mei\u8217'Elokecha} appears in other contexts as well \u8212- by {\i ribit}, where one is forbidden to take interest, and by the laws of weights and measures. How do we know it refers specifically to standing here? Rabbi Elazar resolves this through a {\i gezeira shava} of {\i pnei-pnei} \u8212- the word {\i pnei} appears both in {\i milifnei ha\u8217'Elokim} and in {\i v\u8217'hadarta pnei zaken}, linking the fear clause specifically to the commandment of honoring elders. Tosafot adds that by the weights and measures passage, {\i Amalek} is juxtaposed in Devarim \u8212- and {\i Amalek} is characterized as {\i v\u8217'lo yarei Elokim} \u8212- further anchoring the fear-of-God language to the context of {\i kibbud} rather than commercial law. Tosafot also notes a textual variant reading {\i michshalot} rather than {\i mishkalot}, which would yield a different derivation: {\i lo titein michshal v\u8217'yareita mei\u8217'Elokecha}, the prohibition against placing a stumbling block, as an alternative pairing.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Underlying all of this is the broader principle that {\i moras rabcha k\u8217'mora shamayim} \u8212- the awe of one\u8217's teacher mirrors the awe of heaven \u8212- and this is not merely a function of the textual juxtaposition of {\i v\u8217'yareita mei\u8217'Elokecha}, but reflects something deep about the nature of honor shown to Torah and to those who represent a kind of {\i kedushah} in the world.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Talmid Chacham Q.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A natural question arises: why does the warning specify a {\i talmid chacham} who fails to stand before his {\i rebbe}? If the prohibition is serious, it should apply to anyone who neglects this duty. Several answers suggest themselves. One possibility is that a {\i talmid chacham} might harbor a subtle {\i yetzer hara} against standing \u8212- reasoning that he himself has attained a level of scholarship that makes deference to another seem unnecessary. The prohibition targets precisely this form of pride. Another possibility is that the violation is uniquely severe when committed by a {\i talmid chacham} because it represents a {\i pegiya b\u8217'kavod ha-Torah} \u8212- a blow to the dignity of Torah \u8212- when someone who genuinely understands what Torah represents refuses to honor it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 There is yet a third reading, perhaps the most compelling: the statement may be emphasizing specifically the {\i rebbe-talmid} relationship, not merely the general obligation to stand before any sage. The failure to stand is qualitatively different when it occurs within a formal relationship of transmission, where the bond between teacher and student carries an obligation that transcends general communal deference. And as a student notes, {\i v\u8217'talmudo mishtakeiach} \u8212- the forgetting of one\u8217's Torah \u8212- is by definition a consequence that can only befall a {\i talmid chacham}, which may itself be the reason the ruling is framed around that figure.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Son-Father Conflict\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Precedence Logic\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now turns to the tension between {\i kibbud rav} and {\i kibbud av va\u8217'em}. The background to this discussion is the well-known ruling that if a person must choose between returning a lost object to his {\i rebbe} or to his father, the {\i rebbe} takes precedence \u8212- because the father brings one into this world, but the {\i rebbe} brings one into the world-to-come. This priority of {\i kibbud rav} over {\i kibbud av} in cases of conflict seems to suggest that wherever the two obligations cannot both be fulfilled simultaneously, the {\i rebbe\u8217's} claim wins.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Son Stand Q.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But the case now posed is far more complicated. The {\i ibaya} \u8212- the question raised \u8212- is: {\i beno vehu rabbo} \u8212- what happens when the son is the father\u8217's {\i rebbe}? This is not a simple matter of choosing between two competing obligations running in the same direction. It creates an inversion. If the father must stand before the son as his {\i rebbe}, the entire structure of {\i kibbud av va\u8217'em} \u8212- in which the child defers to the parent \u8212- is turned on its head. The question therefore branches in two directions: does the son still stand before the father even if he is the father\u8217's {\i rebbe}? And does the father stand before the son precisely because the son is his {\i rebbe}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shmuel Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 To address the first question, the {\i gemara} cites a well-known exchange: Shmuel said to Rav Yehuda \u8212- {\i Shinana} \u8212- {\i kum mikamei avuch}, \u8220"stand before your father.\u8221" Those who remember the earlier episode will recall that Rav Yehuda had corrected his father Rav Yechezkel who was teaching a {\i halacha} imprecisely, and Shmuel rebuked him for this breach of respect. Rav Yehuda\u8217's superior Torah knowledge was not in question \u8212- in that sense, he functioned as the {\i rebbe} in the exchange \u8212- and yet Shmuel insisted that he stand before his father and treat him with deference. This seems to prove that even when the son is the {\i rebbe}, the son retains his obligation of {\i kibbud av}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara}, however, pushes back: {\i sha\u8217'ani Rav Yechezkel deba\u8217'al ma\u8217'asim hava}. The case was different because Rav Yechezkel was not an ordinary father \u8212- he was a man of extraordinary righteous deeds, a {\i ba\u8217'al ma\u8217'asim}, whose personal piety demanded a form of reverence that existed alongside and independently of his role as father. The {\i gemara} reinforces this by noting that even Shmuel himself would stand before Rav Yechezkel \u8212- not because Rav Yechezkel was Rav Yehuda\u8217's father, but because Rav Yechezkel himself was worthy of that honor in his own right.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This reinterpretation entirely changes the nature of Shmuel\u8217's instruction. Rather than a ruling about {\i kibbud av} in the context of a {\i rebbe-talmid} relationship, the {\i gemara} rereads it as Shmuel telling Rav Yehuda: sometimes your father will be coming behind me; even if you worry that standing for him in my presence would be disrespectful to my honor as your {\i rebbe}, {\i velo teichosh liyikra didi} \u8212- do not worry about that, stand for him anyway. The {\i chiddush} is about showing honor to a {\i tzaddik} even in the presence of one\u8217's {\i rebbe}, not about the {\i rebbe-talmid} inversion at all. The attempt to derive a ruling about the son standing for his father therefore fails.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Y.B.L. Ruling\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then takes up the inverse question: {\i mahu she\u8217'ya\u8217'amod aviv mipanav} \u8212- does the father stand before the son when the son is his {\i rebbe}? The {\i gemara} cites Rabbi Yehoshua ben Levi: {\i ani eini kedai la\u8217'amod mifnei beni ella mishum kavod beit haNasi}. He declares that he himself would not stand before his son on account of the son\u8217's Torah standing \u8212- the son simply was not great enough in Torah \u8212- but he does stand before him because of the son\u8217's connection to the house of the {\i Nasi}, presumably through political or social ties rather than genealogical ones.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} draws out the implication: {\i ta\u8217'ima de\u8217'ana Rebbi} \u8212- the reason is that Rabbi Yehoshua ben Levi is the {\i rebbe} \u8212- {\i ha ihu rabbai, kamina mikamei} \u8212- but if the son were actually his {\i rebbe}, he would stand before him. This apparently proves that a father must stand before a son who is his {\i rebbe}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} again deflects: {\i hachi ka\u8217'amar} \u8212- what Rabbi Yehoshua ben Levi actually means is: {\i ani eini kedai la\u8217'amod mifnei beni v\u8217'afilu hu rabbai de\u8217'ana avuha} \u8212- I should not have to stand before my son even if he were my {\i rebbe}, because I am his father, and a father should never be placed in a posture of subordination to a son. The only reason he stands is the honor due to the house of the {\i Nasi}. The attempt to prove that the father would stand for the son if the son were the {\i rebbe} is thus also successfully deflected \u8212- leaving the {\i ibaya} entirely unresolved.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rambam Rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 With both proofs bracketed, the {\i gemara} offers no definitive resolution. The Rosh and, following him, the Shulchan Aruch rule that both father and son are obligated to stand for one another \u8212- each acknowledging the other\u8217's status simultaneously. This has an elegant internal logic: if only the father stood for the son, the father\u8217's dignity as a parent would be undermined; if only the son stood for the father, the son\u8217's status as {\i rebbe} and bearer of Torah would be diminished. By requiring both, both statuses are preserved and mutually acknowledged.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Rambam, however, rules differently and asymmetrically: {\i v\u8217'af al pi shehaya talmid beno, ein ha\u8217'av omed mifnei haben, aval haben omed mifnei aviv af al pi shehu talmido} \u8212- even if the son is the father\u8217's student, the father does not stand before the son; but the son stands before the father even though the father is his student. The Rambam thus maintains the integrity of the parent-child relationship as categorically inviolable. Whatever the {\i halacha} says about prioritizing the {\i rebbe} over the father in a case of a lost object, it does not extend so far as to place the father in a position of visible subordination to his child.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Torah Honor Logic\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One way to understand the Rambam\u8217's position is that it reflects something intrinsic to the nature of Torah itself. The full honor owed to a {\i talmid chacham} who represents Torah must itself include the {\i mitzvah} of {\i kibbud av}. A {\i rebbe} who allows his father to stand before him \u8212- or who does nothing to prevent it \u8212- has revealed something hollow about the Torah he carries. The learning, in other words, must translate into action, and one of the central actions that Torah demands is honor to one\u8217's father. By contrast, {\i kibbud av} seems to operate on a more primal, universal register \u8212- it does not depend on Torah for its legitimacy, but exists, in some sense, independently of it. When these two obligations come into direct tension in terms of visible posture, it is the father\u8217's status that is not to be publicly subordinated.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Horseback Q\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Riding Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} poses a new {\i ibaya}: {\i rochev kimahalich dami o lo} \u8212- is riding on a donkey halachically equivalent to walking? The question arises in the context of standing before a {\i rebbe}: if the {\i rebbe} passes by on his animal, does that trigger the obligation of {\i kima} the same way as if he were walking? At first glance, this is puzzling. The verse says {\i mipnei seiva takum v\u8217'hadarta pnei zaken} \u8212- it does not say anything about whether the {\i zaken} is ambulatory; the student is the one who must stand, and the {\i zaken} has simply appeared in one\u8217's presence. What should it matter whether the {\i rebbe} walks or rides?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One line of reasoning might be that standing is only meaningfully reciprocal when the {\i rebbe} himself is in a standing position, or that the act of \u8220"entering one\u8217's presence\u8221" is tied to active movement \u8212- but these remain difficult to articulate cleanly.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} brings a proof from the laws of {\i tumah}: {\i tamei yoshev tachat ha\u8217'ilan v\u8217'tahor omed \u8212- tamei}. The rule is that a {\i metzora} conveys {\i tumah} to others sharing the same overhanging space \u8212- a tree, a roof \u8212- but only when the {\i metzora} is stationary. If the {\i metzora} is moving, no {\i tumah} is transmitted. {\i Tamei omed tachat ha\u8217'ilan v\u8217'tahor yoshev \u8212- tahor}. And if the {\i metzora} then sits down, the {\i tahor} person becomes {\i tamei}. The same rule applies to a stone afflicted with {\i tzara\u8217'at} that is being carried under a tree: if the one carrying it is standing, the people beneath are {\i tahor}; if the carrier sits, they become {\i tamei}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 From this, Rav Nachman bar Kohen derives: {\i zot omeret, rochev kimahalich dami} \u8212- riding is like moving. The reasoning is that the stone itself has no independent mode of transport; its status tracks the one conveying it. If the carrier is sitting, the stone is treated as sitting; if moving, as moving. By analogy, the rider\u8217's state is what defines the operative category. The precise application to the laws of standing remains somewhat difficult to fully articulate \u8212- the {\i gemara}\u8217's reasoning here is admittedly not transparent \u8212- but the technical conclusion is that riding is treated as equivalent to walking.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Sefer Torah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kal Vachomer\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A new {\i ibaya}: {\i mahu la\u8217'amod mipnei sefer Torah} \u8212- does one stand in the presence of a {\i sefer Torah}? Rav Hilkiya, Rav Simon, and Rav Elazar argue from a {\i kal vachomer}: {\i mipnei lomdeha omdim, mipaneha lo kol shekein} \u8212- if one stands before those who learn Torah, certainly one stands before the Torah itself. The underlying logic points toward the primacy of the written text as the source of all that the {\i talmidei chachamim} transmit.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R.S.B.A. Critique\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A striking counter-episode is then introduced. Rabbi Ilai and Rabbi Yaakov bar Zvidi were sitting and learning when Rabbi Shimon bar Abba passed by; they rose before him. He rebuked them on two grounds. First: {\i atun chakimei v\u8217'ana chaver} \u8212- you are scholars and I am merely a {\i chaver}, a communally recognized figure of standing but not a {\i talmid chacham} \u8212- why are you standing for me? Second, and more penetrating: {\i klum Torah omedet mipnei lomdeha} \u8212- does Torah stand before those who learn it? His argument, as Rashi explains it, is that these students were engaged in active Torah study, and by interrupting their learning to stand, they in effect caused the Torah \u8212- the living {\i cheftza} of Torah being articulated at that moment \u8212- to defer to him, a mere learner of Torah. The Torah does not stand before its learners.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} identifies this position as following Rabbi Elazar\u8217's view: {\i ein talmid chacham rashai la\u8217'amod mipnei rabbo b\u8217'sha\u8217'ah she\u8217'osek ba-Torah} \u8212- a {\i talmid chacham} need not rise before his {\i rebbe} when he is in the midst of Torah study. In Rabbi Shimon bar Abba\u8217's reading, the learning itself constitutes the presence of Torah; to interrupt it is to have Torah subordinate itself to a human being.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Abaye Rejection\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye, however, {\i laite lah} \u8212- cursed this practice. He rejected the position of Rabbi Elazar entirely. Even while engaged in Torah study, one must rise before a {\i talmid chacham}. The implicit logic is powerful: if one is learning Torah and cannot bring oneself to honor a Torah sage, what does that reveal about the Torah being learned? The very substance of Torah includes {\i kibbud} toward those who bear it. To exempt oneself from {\i kima} by invoking one\u8217's own learning is to misunderstand what learning is for.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Moshe Example\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} closes this section with a discussion of the verse {\i v\u8217'hibitu acharei Moshe b\u8217'ohel olo} \u8212- they would gaze after Moshe as he entered his tent. Rabbi Ammi and Rabbi Yitzchak Nafcha dispute whether this behavior is described {\i l\u8217'gnai} \u8212- derogatorily \u8212- or {\i l\u8217'shvach} \u8212- approvingly. The one who reads it derogatorily invokes the {\i midrashic} tradition that the Israelites made disparaging comments about Moshe as he walked \u8212- accusing him of enriching himself at their expense \u8212- a behavior scandalous in any context involving a {\i rebbe}, and all the more so with Moshe.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The one who reads it approvingly proceeds to derive from this verse the distance protocols for standing before sages of different stature \u8212- transitioning the discussion toward the practical {\i halachot} of {\i kima} and {\i arba amot}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Horseback Q (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Distance Zones\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now derives, from the verse {\i v\u8217'hibitu acharei Moshe}, the graduated rules for standing before sages of different rank. When an ordinary {\i chacham} passes by \u8212- {\i chacham over} \u8212- one stands when he enters within {\i arba amot} and may sit once he has passed beyond that radius. When the {\i Av Beit Din} passes \u8212- {\i melo einav} \u8212- one stands as soon as one sees him, and may sit once he has passed more than {\i arba amot} away. When the {\i Nasi} passes, the obligation is even more expansive: {\i omed milfanav melo einav v\u8217'eino yoshev ad she\u8217'yeshev bimkomo} \u8212- one stands as soon as he comes into sight and does not sit until the {\i Nasi} himself has taken his seat. The verse {\i v\u8217'hibitu acharei Moshe} anchors this final rule, since Moshe functions as the paradigmatic {\i Nasi} \u8212- standing before him until he entered his tent and was seated.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 There is an apparent irony in the graduated structure: one might have expected that greater honor demands standing at a greater distance, yet the rule is that for a {\i chacham} one stands only at {\i arba amot}, while for a {\i Nasi} one stands from as far as the eye can see. One way of understanding this is that the {\i arba amot} requirement is about the individual\u8217's personal sphere of honor \u8212- marking the space of the {\i chacham}\u8217's immediate presence \u8212- while the more expansive obligation for the {\i Nasi} reflects a public, institutional dimension that demands recognition across the full visual field.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The question of standing before one\u8217's father was briefly revisited in this context. The {\i gemara} does not explicitly formulate the obligation of {\i kima} before a parent in the discussion of {\i kibbud av va\u8217'em}, and the Shulchan Aruch\u8217's ruling that one is {\i chayav la\u8217'amod mifnei aviv} seems to be derived implicitly from the present {\i gemara} rather than from an explicit statement elsewhere. The famous story of a sage who said \u8220"the {\i Shechinah} is coming\u8221" upon hearing his mother approach suggests some tradition of standing for a parent, but the strict formal requirement, as one student noted, appears to flow primarily from the {\i rebbe-talmid} context and is applied to parents by derivation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Women Exemption\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Core Rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now pivots sharply to a new topic: the exemption of women from {\i mitzvot asei she-hazeman grama} \u8212- positive commandments whose obligation is triggered by or limited to specific times. The {\i mishnah}\u8217's rule is unambiguous: {\i kol mitzvas asei she-hazeman grama, anashim chayavim v\u8217'nashim pturot} \u8212- all time-bound positive commandments obligate men and exempt women.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Time-Bound Ex\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} illustrates: {\i sukkah, lulav, shofar, tzitzit}, and {\i tefillin}. These are the paradigmatic cases. {\i Tzitzit} does not apply to nighttime garments; {\i tefillin} does not apply on {\i Shabbat} and, according to many opinions, not at night.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Zman Grama Def\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot raises a significant definitional challenge. Strictly speaking, {\i tzitzit} does apply at night \u8212- it simply does not apply to nighttime {\i garments}, not to daytime garments worn at night. If a person wears a four-cornered daytime garment at night, the obligation of {\i tzitzit} applies. So why is {\i tzitzit} categorized as {\i zeman grama}? Tosafot concludes that {\i zeman grama} does not mean that the {\i mitzvah} itself only applies at certain times; rather, it means that time is a {\i factor} that shapes the scope of the {\i mitzvah}. Because the type of garment obligated in {\i tzitzit} is itself time-sensitive, time plays a determinative role, and that is enough to classify it as {\i zeman grama}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Similarly with {\i tefillin}: Tosafot argues that if the {\i halacha} follows the position that nighttime is actually {\i zeman tefillin} \u8212- one simply refrains from wearing {\i tefillin} at night out of concern that one may fall asleep in them \u8212- then the only time {\i tefillin} are genuinely not worn is {\i Shabbat}. Would one otherwise call this {\i zeman grama}? Probably not \u8212- fundamentally, the obligation is constant, with narrow exceptions. Yet because it is not uninterrupted, it falls under the {\i zeman grama} rubric. The definition of {\i zeman grama} is thus being stretched and refined by the very examples chosen to illustrate it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Non-Bound Ex\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then gives examples of {\i mitzvot asei she-lo hazeman grama} \u8212- non-time-bound commandments \u8212- in which women are obligated: {\i mezuzah, ma\u8217'akeh} (a fence on one\u8217's roof), {\i aveidah} (returning a lost object), and {\i shiluach ha-ken} (sending away the mother bird before taking the eggs).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafot Q\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot finds this list troubling, and the difficulty is substantial. With the exception of {\i mezuzah}, every one of these {\i mitzvot} is directly bound up with a {\i lo ta\u8217'aseh} \u8212- a negative prohibition. By {\i aveidah}, the Torah says {\i lo tuchal le-his\u8217'alem} \u8212- you may not ignore it. By {\i shiluach ha-ken}, the Torah says {\i lo tikach ha-em al ha-banim}. By {\i ma\u8217'akeh}, it says {\i lo tasim damim b\u8217'veisecha}. Women are universally obligated in negative commandments. So even if one were to argue that women are exempt from the positive {\i aseh} in isolation, they would still be bound to act because of the {\i lo ta\u8217'aseh}. Why list these as examples of non-time-bound {\i mitzvot asei} in which women are obligated, when the obligation may actually be flowing from the negative prohibition rather than from the positive commandment?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot tries to construct scenarios where the {\i aseh} and the {\i lo ta\u8217'aseh} can be decoupled. For {\i aveidah}: if a person picked up a lost object with the intention of returning it and then changed his mind, there would be no violation of {\i lo tuchal le-his\u8217'alem} \u8212- since initially he had not ignored it \u8212- but the positive {\i mitzvah} of {\i hasheiv tishivem} would still apply. For {\i ma\u8217'akeh}: if a person built a fence and it subsequently collapsed, {\i lo tasim damim b\u8217'veisecha} would not be violated \u8212- since the person had initially acted \u8212- but {\i va\u8217'asita ma\u8217'akeh} would still require him to rebuild it. These constructions isolate the pure positive {\i mitzvah}, but they are evidently somewhat artificial, and the underlying question remains: these {\i mitzvot} are fundamentally of a different character than a standalone {\i aseh} with no accompanying prohibition, and the list is curious for including them.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rule Unreliable\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This brings the {\i gemara} to its central methodological conclusion on the reliability of the rule itself. The {\i gemara} asks {\i v\u8217'klala hu} \u8212- is this {\i klal} actually dependable as a rule? The answer is clearly no in both directions: there are time-bound {\i mitzvot} in which women are obligated ({\i matzah, simcha, hakhel}), and there are non-time-bound {\i mitzvot} from which women are exempt ({\i talmud Torah, pirya v\u8217'rivya, pidyon ha-ben}).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yochanan therefore states: {\i ein l\u8217'meidim min ha-klalos v\u8217'afilu b\u8217'makom she-ne\u8217'emar bo chutz} \u8212- one cannot derive law from general rules, even when the rule explicitly lists its exceptions. The expectation would be that a rule accompanied by stated exceptions is exhaustive \u8212- all other cases follow the rule. Rabbi Yochanan rejects this. The proof comes from elsewhere in {\i Shas}: the {\i mishnah} states {\i b\u8217'chol me\u8217'arvin u\u8217'mishtatfin \u8212- chutz min ha-mayim u\u8217'melach} \u8212- one may make an {\i eiruv} with anything except salt and water. Yet fungi and mushrooms are also excluded, and the {\i mishnah} does not list them. The stated exceptions are not exhaustive, and the rule cannot be taken as absolute.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What remains at stake is the deeper question of how this rule functions. Does it serve as an operative principle \u8212- such that when the {\i halacha} of a particular {\i mitzvah} is uncertain, one can invoke {\i zeman grama} to predict the outcome? Or is it merely a descriptive pattern, cataloguing cases already known independently from {\i masorah}, with no independent predictive power? The Rambam in his {\i Peirush Hamishnayos} takes the latter view: everything we know, we know from {\i masorah}, not from this rule. But the {\i stam gemara} throughout this sugya operates as if the rule does carry predictive weight \u8212- if a {\i mitzvah} is {\i zeman grama}, the working presumption is that women are exempt unless we have reason to think otherwise.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Tefillin Source\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 K.V. Derivation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now asks for the fundamental source of the entire exemption: {\i minalan} \u8212- from where do we know this? {\i Gemar mi-tefillin} \u8212- the paradigm case is {\i tefillin}. Women are exempt from {\i tefillin}, and from {\i tefillin} we generalize: {\i af kol mitzvas asei she-hazeman grama nashim pturot} \u8212- all time-bound positive commandments exempt women, on the model of {\i tefillin}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But this immediately raises the question of how we know that women are exempt from {\i tefillin} in the first place. The {\i gemara} answers: {\i V\u8217'tefillin gemar mi-talmud Torah} \u8212- we derive it from {\i talmud Torah}. Just as women are exempt from {\i talmud Torah} \u8212- because the verse says {\i v\u8217'shinantam l\u8217'vaneichem}, \u8220"you shall teach your sons,\u8221" which is read as {\i beneichem v\u8217'lo benoteichem}, your sons and not your daughters \u8212- so too women are exempt from {\i tefillin}. The derivation works because {\i tefillin} is textually juxtaposed to {\i talmud Torah} in the passage of {\i krias shema}: {\i v\u8217'dibarta bam\u8230 ? u\u8217'kshartam l\u8217'os al yadecha}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The irony is striking and worth noting. {\i Talmud Torah} is itself not {\i zeman grama} \u8212- it is an obligation that applies constantly, at all times and in all places. Yet {\i talmud Torah} becomes the {\i source} from which we learn the exemption for {\i tefillin}, which then becomes the paradigm from which we generalize to all {\i zeman grama} cases. The chain of derivation thus runs from a non-time-bound {\i mitzvah} to a time-bound {\i mitzvah} to a general rule about time-bound {\i mitzvot}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tefillin Choice\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then asks: why do we learn women\u8217's exemption from {\i tefillin} rather than deriving it the other way \u8212- that women should be obligated in {\i tefillin} as they are in {\i mezuzah}? Both {\i tefillin} and {\i mezuzah} appear in the {\i parshiyot} of {\i krias shema}; why not link {\i tefillin} to {\i mezuzah} rather than to {\i talmud Torah}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The answer is that {\i tefillin} is juxtaposed to {\i talmud Torah} in both the first and second {\i parshiyot} of {\i krias shema}. In the first {\i parsha}: {\i v\u8217'shinantam\u8230 ? u\u8217'kshartam\u8230 ? u\u8217'chsavtam} \u8212- {\i talmud Torah}, then {\i tefillin}, then {\i mezuzah}. In the second {\i parsha}: {\i u\u8217'kshartam\u8230 ? v\u8217'limadetem\u8230 ? u\u8217'chsavtam} \u8212- {\i tefillin}, then {\i talmud Torah}, then {\i mezuzah}. In both passages, {\i tefillin} is directly adjacent to {\i talmud Torah}. By contrast, in the second {\i parsha}, {\i tefillin} is not directly adjacent to {\i mezuzah} \u8212- {\i talmud Torah} intervenes between them. Since {\i tefillin} is consistently and doubly linked to {\i talmud Torah}, and not consistently linked to {\i mezuzah}, we derive the status of {\i tefillin} from {\i talmud Torah} rather than from {\i mezuzah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mezuzah Obligation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having established that {\i tefillin} is linked to {\i talmud Torah}, one might ask the further question: could {\i mezuzah} itself then be linked to {\i talmud Torah} and thereby exempt women from {\i mezuzah} as well? The {\i gemara} says {\i lo salka daitach} \u8212- that cannot be entertained. The verse by {\i mezuzah} says {\i lemaan yirbu yemeichem} \u8212- so that your days shall be multiplied. To say that women are exempt from {\i mezuzah} would imply that women do not need long life \u8212- {\i gavrei bei chayei nashei lo bei chayei}? \u8212- an obviously untenable conclusion. Therefore women are unambiguously obligated in {\i mezuzah}, and no {\i hekkesh} to {\i talmud Torah} can override that explicit textual anchor.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot raises a parallel objection: {\i talmud Torah} itself also says {\i lemaan yirbu yemeichem} \u8212- so should women be obligated there as well? The answer is that the explicit {\i derasha} of {\i beneichem v\u8217'lo benoteichem} tips the scales specifically in that context. When there is no such explicit exclusion, {\i lemaan yirbu yemeichem} anchors women\u8217's obligation; when there is, the exclusion prevails.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rule Testing\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sukkah Needed\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now tests the general rule against specific cases where an explicit {\i pasuk} was needed to either exempt or obligate women, asking whether the need for such explicit statement reveals something about the baseline presumption. If women are presumptively exempt from {\i zeman grama mitzvot} by virtue of the general rule, why did the Torah need to exempt them explicitly from {\i sukkah}? The verse by {\i sukkah} says {\i kol ha\u8217'ezrach b\u8217'Yisrael} \u8212- all citizens \u8212- and the {\i derasha} derives from {\i ha\u8217'ezrach} a specific exclusion of women. If the rule already exempted them, what was this {\i pasuk} doing?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Abaye Pos\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye answers: the {\i pasuk} was necessary despite the general rule \u8212- {\i itzterich}. {\i Salka daitach amina} \u8212- I might have thought that the word {\i teshvu} in {\i basukkot teshvu shivat yamim} \u8212- you shall dwell in {\i sukkot} \u8212- implies {\i teshvu ke\u8217'ein taduru}, you shall dwell as you dwell in your homes. Since dwelling in a home is done jointly by husband and wife, I might have concluded that {\i teshvu} demands women\u8217's participation in {\i sukkah} equally. The {\i pasuk} of {\i ha\u8217'ezrach} was therefore needed to clarify that despite {\i teshvu}, the general rule of exemption holds. The need for the {\i pasuk} thus tells us nothing about the baseline presumption \u8212- it was needed to override a specific countervailing consideration, not to establish the rule itself.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava Pos\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava offers a different reason why the {\i pasuk} by {\i sukkah} was necessary. {\i Neilef chamisha asar chamisha asar mechag ha-matzot} \u8212- one might construct a {\i gezeira shava} between the fifteenth of Tishrei, the date of {\i sukkot}, and the fifteenth of Nisan, the date of {\i matzah}. Since women are obligated in {\i matzah} \u8212- because they too were prohibited from eating {\i chametz} and therefore share the obligation to eat {\i matzah} \u8212- I might have derived from this {\i gezeira shava} that women are equally obligated in {\i sukkah}. The explicit exclusion of {\i ha\u8217'ezrach} was therefore needed to block that derivation. Again, the need for the {\i pasuk} reveals a specific reason it had to be stated, not a general presumption of women\u8217's obligation in {\i zeman grama}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 In this context, Rava\u8217's {\i gezeira shava} also illuminates an interesting {\i machlokes Rishonim} about {\i sukkah} on the first night. Some {\i Rishonim} hold that on the first night of {\i sukkot} one must eat in the {\i sukkah} even if it is raining \u8212- because the linkage between {\i sukkah} and {\i matzah} implies an absolute dimension to the first night\u8217's obligation, parallel to the absolute obligation to eat {\i matzah} on the {\i seder} night. This connection between {\i sukkah} and {\i matzah} is thus not merely an Amoraic construct; it has real practical implications.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Olu Reyiyah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} raises the same question about {\i olat re\u8217'iya} \u8212- the {\i korban} brought on the three {\i regalim}. This is as quintessentially {\i zeman grama} as any {\i mitzvah} can be \u8212- mandated three times per year on a fixed calendar schedule. The Torah explicitly restricts it to males: {\i yera\u8217'eh kol zechurcha} \u8212- all your males shall appear. If the general rule already exempts women, why did the Torah need to say {\i zechurcha}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} again answers: {\i itzterich}. The reason is that women are explicitly obligated in {\i hakhel} \u8212- the seven-year gathering \u8212- where the Torah uses the same language of {\i appearing}, {\i yera\u8217'eh} or a cognate form. One might have constructed a {\i gezeira shava} from this shared language: just as women must appear at {\i hakhel}, perhaps they must appear at the {\i regalim} as well. The explicit exclusion of {\i zechurcha} was needed to block this derivation. Again, no inference about the general rule\u8217's presumptive direction can be drawn from the need to state the exemption here.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Paradigm Resolution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Simcha Not Personal\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having established the general rule and worked through the cases that seem to challenge it from both sides, the {\i gemara} returns to the original observation that the rule breaks down in both directions \u8212- there are time-bound {\i mitzvot} where women are obligated, and non-time-bound {\i mitzvot} where they are exempt. The question is which category should serve as the paradigm.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The three cases of women\u8217's obligation in time-bound {\i mitzvot} were {\i matzah}, {\i simcha}, and {\i hakhel}. Abaye disposes of {\i simcha} first: {\i isha ba\u8217'alah mesamchah} \u8212- a woman\u8217's {\i simcha} obligation is not a personal one. The verse {\i v\u8217'samachta b\u8217'chagecha ata u\u8217'veitecha} places the obligation on the man to make his household rejoice. In Temple times this was accomplished through {\i shalmei simcha} \u8212- the husband brought the peace-offerings and shared the meat with the members of his household, including his wife. The wife participates in {\i simcha}, but as a recipient of the husband\u8217's obligation, not as an independent bearer of it. Accordingly, {\i simcha} is not a genuine exception to the rule.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} adds that even a widow who lives alone is included in the {\i mitzvah} \u8212- derived from {\i v\u8217'ha-ger v\u8217'ha-yatom v\u8217'ha-almanah asher b\u8217'kirbecha} \u8212- but again, as one who must be included in others\u8217' {\i simcha} obligation, not as an independent obligant.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two Verses Rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 With {\i simcha} removed, two genuine exceptions remain on the obligation side: {\i matzah} and {\i hakhel}. The {\i gemara} invokes the principle of {\i shnei ketuvim ha-ba\u8217'im ke-echad ein melamdin} \u8212- when the Torah explicitly states the same rule in two separate contexts, those two instances are treated as exceptions rather than the rule, and neither can be used as the paradigm for a general derivation. {\i Matzah} and {\i hakhel} neutralize each other.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Both Necessary\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} anticipates an objection: on the exemption side, we now have {\i tefillin} and {\i olat re\u8217'iya} \u8212- two explicit statements of women\u8217's exemption. By the same logic of {\i shnei ketuvim}, should they not also neutralize each other, leaving us without a paradigm in either direction?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} answers that {\i tefillin} and {\i olat re\u8217'iya} are not superfluous. Each was necessary for its own reason. If the Torah had stated the exemption only for {\i tefillin} and not for {\i olat re\u8217'iya}, one would have derived women\u8217's obligation in {\i olat re\u8217'iya} from the {\i gezeira shava} of {\i re\u8217'iyah-re\u8217'iyah} from {\i hakhel}. Therefore the explicit exemption for {\i olat re\u8217'iya} was needed. Conversely, if the Torah had stated the exemption only for {\i olat re\u8217'iya} and not for {\i tefillin}, one might have linked {\i tefillin} to {\i mezuzah} rather than to {\i talmud Torah}, and thereby derived women\u8217's obligation in {\i tefillin}. Therefore the explicit statement by {\i tefillin} was also needed. Since each was independently required, they cannot be read as exceptions \u8212- each is doing necessary work. The {\i shnei ketuvim} principle does not apply when both statements were indispensable.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hakhel Superfluous\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 By contrast, on the obligation side, {\i hakhel} was superfluous. Had the Torah stated only that women are obligated in {\i matzah} and said nothing about {\i hakhel}, one could have derived women\u8217's obligation in {\i hakhel} independently: the Torah states {\i ha-anashim v\u8217'ha-nashim v\u8217'ha-taf} \u8212- the men, the women, and the children must appear. Since infants are obligated in {\i hakhel}, and children have a lesser obligation than women in all other contexts, {\i kol she-kein} women should certainly be obligated. There was therefore no need for the Torah to say explicitly that women are obligated in {\i hakhel} \u8212- it was derivable on its own. The fact that the Torah states it anyway marks it as an exception, not a rule. And since one of the two remaining time-bound cases of women\u8217's obligation is superfluous, the {\i shnei ketuvim} principle does not come into play \u8212- only {\i matzah} is a genuine obligation case, and a single case cannot become the paradigm.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Final Conclusion\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The resolution of the entire discussion is therefore as follows. The exemption category \u8212- {\i tefillin} and {\i olat re\u8217'iya} \u8212- comprises two cases each of which was necessary to state, and neither of which is superfluous. They therefore cannot be read as exceptions. By contrast, the obligation category \u8212- {\i matzah} and {\i hakhel} \u8212- includes {\i hakhel}, which was superfluous and therefore marks that whole category as the exceptional one. The general rule thus stands: women are exempt from {\i mitzvot asei she-hazeman grama}, and the cases where they are obligated are exceptions to that rule, not indicators of its reversal.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The deep irony of the entire sugya is that the chain of derivation begins with {\i talmud Torah} \u8212- itself not {\i zeman grama} \u8212- which is used to exempt women from {\i tefillin}, which is then elevated as the paradigm for all {\i zeman grama} exemptions. Had {\i talmud Torah} been made the paradigm directly, women would have been exempt from all positive commandments entirely, time-bound or not. But by moving through {\i tefillin}, and by then arguing that the exemption category cannot be characterized as exceptional while the obligation category can, the {\i gemara} arrives at a precisely calibrated conclusion: women are exempt from time-bound positive commandments, obligated in non-time-bound ones, and the specific cases that deviate from this pattern are identified as exceptions derivable from {\i masorah} and {\i drasha}, not as challenges to the rule itself.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Kiddushin 35\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Exemption from Mitzvot\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rule & Exceptions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The daf begins where we left off at the bottom of 34b, dealing with the source for how we know that women are exempt from {\i mitzvot asei shehazman grama} (time-bound positive commandments) and obligated in {\i mitzvot asei shelo hazman grama} (non-time-bound positive commandments). The difficulty with this entire sugya is immediately apparent: the derivation is convoluted, there are as many exceptions as there are examples that fall under the rule, and the central question \u8212- why we treat certain cases as the paradigm and others as the exception rather than the reverse \u8212- is precisely what the {\i Gemara} itself struggles to answer. And when the {\i Gemara} does answer, the answer is not exactly overwhelming in its persuasiveness.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rambam\u8217's Masora\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This observation leads naturally to what the Rambam explicitly states. Against the straightforward reading of the {\i Gemara}, which seems to treat the rule of {\i mitzvot asei shehazman grama} as genuinely operative \u8212- that it will tell us about cases where we don\u8217't know the status, absent something to teach us an exception \u8212- the Rambam says something fundamentally different. He maintains that none of this is actually known through derivation from a rule; everything is known through {\i masorah}, tradition. We know which {\i mitzvot} women are obligated in and which they are exempt from not because a rule tells us so, but because that is what has been transmitted. The link back to {\i pesukim} is secondary.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This reflects a broader question that {\i rishonim} confront throughout the Talmud: did {\i Chazal} have a tradition about certain matters and then went back to find how it was rooted in the {\i pesukim}, or did they genuinely begin without knowing and then derive the answer? The more convoluted the link to {\i pesukim} is, and the less clear it is why a particular path of derivation was chosen over an equally plausible alternative, the more likely {\i rishonim} are to say that what we are seeing is the second stage: the tradition came first, and the {\i pesukim} were then found to support it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tefillin & Reaiyah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 To recall where we stand: the cases in the {\i Gemara} where women are exempt \u8212- and which we derive from explicit {\i pesukim} \u8212- are {\i tefillin}, {\i re\u8217'iyah} ({\i olat re\u8217'iyah}, the burnt-offering brought on pilgrimage), and {\i sukkah}. Notably, {\i tefillin} serves as the paradigm for the rule of {\i mitzvat asei shehazman grama}, even though its derivation comes by way of its link to {\i talmud Torah}, and {\i talmud Torah} itself is not in the same category. The cases where women are obligated \u8212- derived explicitly \u8212- are {\i hakhel}, {\i matzah}, and {\i simchah}. The irony, of course, is that {\i tefillin} serves as the rule-setting paradigm despite the awkwardness of its derivation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rule vs Exception\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The fundamental question is: which column is the rule and which is the exception? The {\i Gemara} argued that cases explicitly stated twice or more are the exception \u8212- the principle of {\i shnei ketuvim habaim ke\u8217'echad} \u8212- since if something were the rule, the {\i Torah} would only need to say it once. If it is stated more than once, the {\i Torah} is pointing out exceptions, not articulating a rule.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} initially argued that in the column of exemption ({\i patur}), the cases that are explicitly stated are superfluous, and therefore they constitute the {\i shnei ketuvim habaim ke\u8217'echad} \u8212- the exceptions \u8212- establishing the rule to be the other side. The {\i Gemara} explained why each of the obligation ({\i chiyuv}) cases needed to be explicitly stated: by {\i sukkah}, one might have thought {\i teishvu ke\u8217'ein taduru} (you shall dwell as you normally dwell) is a reason to obligate women; by one case, a {\i gezeirah shavah} to {\i hakhel} would have led to obligation; by {\i tefillin}, there was a different reason one might have thought obligation applies. Since each of those cases needed to be said explicitly, they are not {\i shnei ketuvim habaim ke\u8217'echad}; they are necessary specifications. By contrast, {\i hakhel} in the obligation column is superfluous \u8212- one could have known it from {\i matzah} \u8212- so the two obligation cases together are the {\i shnei ketuvim habaim ke\u8217'echad}, marking them as exceptions and the exemption side as the rule.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The corresponding question on the {\i shelo hazman grama} side: how do we know that women are obligated in non-time-bound {\i mitzvot}? The answer given is {\i mora} \u8212- from the verse concerning {\i yirat av va\u8217'em}, where the plural {\i tira\u8217'u} explicitly includes both men and women. And the challenge: why not derive from {\i talmud Torah} instead, which is also {\i shelo hazman grama} yet women are exempt? The answer is that {\i talmud Torah} and {\i piryah ve-rivyah} together constitute {\i shnei ketuvim habaim ke\u8217'echad} on the exemption side of {\i shelo hazman grama}, making them the exceptions, while {\i mora} is the rule.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Peru Verev\u250 ? Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 At this point the {\i Gemara} introduces Rabbi Yochanan ben Beroka, who holds that the verse {\i va-yivarech osam Elohim va-yomer lahem Elohim piru u-revu} (\u8220"and God blessed them and said to them: be fruitful and multiply\u8221") obligates women equally in {\i piryah ve-rivyah}. If so, {\i piryah ve-rivyah} moves to the obligation column, and there is now only one case \u8212- {\i talmud Torah} \u8212- on the exemption side of {\i shelo hazman grama}. One case cannot be {\i shnei ketuvim habaim ke\u8217'echad}; it could simply be the rule. Let {\i talmud Torah} be the rule, and let women be generally exempt from {\i shelo hazman grama}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} answers that even according to Rabbi Yochanan ben Beroka, you now have {\i piryah ve-rivyah} and {\i Pidyon HaBen} in the obligation column. {\i Pidyon HaBen} \u8212- the redemption of the firstborn son \u8212- is an obligation that, once the child reaches thirty days, continues without cessation, has no time-bound interruption, and is therefore {\i shelo hazman grama}. Women are nonetheless exempt. And we learn this from a {\i pasuk}. So again, two cases of {\i shelo hazman grama} where women are obligated \u8212- {\i piryah ve-rivyah} (according to Rabbi Yochanan ben Beroka) and {\i mora} \u8212- and two cases where they are exempt \u8212- {\i talmud Torah} and {\i Pidyon HaBen}. The same structure holds. The cases that needed to be explicitly stated because they are necessary \u8212- and not superfluous \u8212- are the rule. The cases that were superfluous are the exception.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Necessity Standard\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But what makes a case necessary versus superfluous? The {\i Gemara} works this out carefully for the position of Rabbi Yochanan ben Beroka. The {\i Torah} had to explicitly state that {\i piryah ve-rivyah} applies to women because without that, the phrase {\i ve-chivshuha} \u8212- \u8220"and you shall subdue it\u8221" \u8212- in the context of the commandment would have suggested this was addressed only to men, since conquest and dominion are characteristically male activities ({\i ish darcho lichbosh ve-ein ishah darkah lichbosh}). Without an explicit statement, one would have restricted {\i piryah ve-rivyah} to men. Conversely, the {\i Torah} had to explicitly state that women are obligated in {\i yiras av va-em} because without that, one might have said that since a woman does not always have the latitude to fulfill the obligation \u8212- her primary responsibilities at home to husband and children may prevent her \u8212- she might be entirely exempt. Each case was therefore necessary, and the necessary case is the rule.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Unresolved Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 After all of this careful analysis, the {\i Gemara} returns to the earlier unresolved difficulty: all of the above works only according to those who maintain {\i shnei ketuvim habaim ke\u8217'echad ein melamdin} \u8212- that two cases explicitly stated make something an exception rather than a rule. But there is an opinion that {\i shnei ketuvim habaim ke\u8217'echad melamdin} \u8212- even with two cases explicitly stated, it can still be the rule. If so, the entire argument collapses. What makes the obligation cases exceptions rather than the rule? The same problem applies equally on the {\i zman grama} side and the {\i lo zman grama} side. How do we know which column is the rule and which is the exception?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tefillin Paradigm\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava answers this in the name of the people of Papuna \u8212- a place whose location is noted by Rashi \u8212- whose tradition was transmitted by Rav Achai bar Yaakov. The answer, he says, is found in the verse: {\i vehayah lecha le\u8217'ot al yadecha ulezikaron bein einecha lema\u8217'an tihye Torat Hashem befecha} \u8212- \u8220"and it shall be for you a sign on your arm and a reminder between your eyes, so that the {\i Torah} of God shall be in your mouth.\u8221" The conclusion of the verse \u8212- {\i lema\u8217'an tihye Torat Hashem befecha} \u8212- equates the entire {\i Torah} to {\i tefillin}: {\i hukshah kol ha-Torah kulah le-tefillin}. The entire {\i Torah} is measured against {\i tefillin} as its paradigm. Since {\i tefillin} is a {\i mitzvat asei shehazman grama} and women are exempt, the {\i pasuk} is identifying {\i tefillin} as the paradigm for all of {\i Torah}. And since the {\i Torah} chose a time-bound {\i mitzvah} as the model with respect to women\u8217's exemption, the implication is clear: it is specifically the time-bound characteristic that generates the exemption. Anything that is not time-bound, women would be obligated. The converse follows directly: {\i mi-klal de-mitzvat asei shehazman grama nashim pturot, michlal de-mitzvat asei shelo hazman grama nashim chayavot}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This approach bypasses the entire complicated argument about which column has the right number of cases to be the exception. It is not about counting how many cases are in each column or whether they are superfluous. The {\i Torah} itself labels {\i tefillin} as the template for all of {\i Torah}, and that is where we get the rule \u8212- end of story.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tefillin Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} immediately challenges this: it works well according to those who hold that {\i tefillin} is indeed a {\i mitzvat asei shehazman grama} \u8212- that one is exempt on {\i Shabbat} and at night. But what about the opinion that {\i tefillin} is {\i mitzvat asei shelo hazman grama} \u8212- that it applies at all times, including nights and {\i Shabbat}? If {\i tefillin} is not time-bound, it cannot serve as the paradigm for the category of time-bound exemption. The {\i Gemara} identifies this position as that of Rabbi Meir.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The answer is that Rabbi Meir also holds {\i shnei ketuvim habaim ke\u8217'echad ein melamdin} \u8212- that two explicitly stated cases constitute an exception rather than a rule. Accordingly, Rabbi Meir does not need {\i tefillin} as the paradigm; he relies on the structure of multiple explicitly stated cases to identify which side is the exception. The two approaches are thus complementary alternatives: either {\i tefillin} is the paradigm (which works for those who say {\i tefillin} is time-bound), or the logic of {\i shnei ketuvim habaim ke\u8217'echad} identifies the exceptions (which is the approach of Rabbi Meir, who holds {\i tefillin} is not time-bound).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Three Cases Rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} then addresses Rabbi Yehuda, who presents the most demanding case: he holds both that {\i shnei ketuvim habaim ke\u8217'echad melamdin} (two cases can still be a rule) and that {\i tefillin} is {\i shelo hazman grama} (not time-bound). With neither path available, how does he establish which column is the exception?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The answer is {\i shlosheh ketuvim habaim ke\u8217'echad ein melamdin} \u8212- three explicitly stated cases do constitute an exception, even for Rabbi Yehuda. And here the {\i Gemara} reinstates {\i simchah} in the obligation column. Earlier, {\i simchah} had been somewhat set aside because the Talmud suggested the obligation of {\i simchah} on {\i Yom Tov} for a woman is fulfilled through her husband ({\i ba\u8217'alah mesamchah}). But according to Rabbi Yehuda, {\i simchah} is restored, giving three cases \u8212- {\i matzah}, {\i simchah}, and {\i hakhel} \u8212- on the obligation side, which is enough to label it as the exception.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This yields essentially two and a half distinct paths to the same conclusion: {\i tefillin} as the paradigm (for those who hold it is time-bound); or, if {\i tefillin} is not the paradigm, then the sheer number of explicitly stated exceptions \u8212- three against the rule on both sides \u8212- labels those explicit cases as exceptions and establishes the opposite column as the rule. The very fact that the {\i Torah} needed to state so many cases going against what would otherwise be the rule is itself the evidence that the rule runs the other way.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Negative Mitzvot\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 All Lav Mitzvot\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having established the framework for positive commandments, the {\i Gemara} turns to the {\i mishnah}\u8217's next topic: {\i mitzvot lo ta\u8217'aseh}, negative commandments. The rule is that women are obligated in all negative commandments \u8212- {\i nashim chayavot} \u8212- with only a small number of exceptions, to be discussed in turn.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ish ve-Isha\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Where does this rule come from? Rav Yehuda in the name of Rav, and likewise the school of Rabbi Yishmael, derive it from the verse: {\i ish o ishah ki ya\u8217'asu mikol chatat ha\u8217'adam} \u8212- \u8220"a man or a woman who commits any of the sins of a person\u8221" (Bamidbar 5:6). The phrase {\i mikol chatat ha\u8217'adam} is generic and sweeping. The verse frames transgression as something that applies equally to {\i ish o ishah}. From this the {\i Gemara} derives: {\i hishvah ha-katuv ishah le-ish le-chol onashin sheba-Torah} \u8212- Scripture equates a woman to a man for all punishments in the {\i Torah}. Any transgression that makes one liable to punishment applies equally to men and women.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Three Teachings\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} then presents a broader package of three separate {\i limudim} that appear across {\i Shas}, each establishing women\u8217's equal legal standing in a different domain. First, from {\i ish o ishah ki ya\u8217'asu mikol chatat ha\u8217'adam}: equal obligation in transgressions and their associated punishments ({\i chatat ha\u8217'adam}). Second, from the school of Rabbi Eliezer, who derives from the verse {\i asher tasim lifneihem} \u8212- \u8220"which you shall place before them,\u8221" the phrase introducing {\i Mishpatim} \u8212- that {\i hishvah ha-katuv ishah le-ish le-chol dinim sheba-Torah}: women are treated equally in all civil law across the {\i Torah}, all matters of financial litigation, damages, debts, and property. Third, from the school of Chizkiyah, who derives from the verse in {\i Mishpatim} \u8212- {\i ve-hemit ish o ishah} \u8212- concerning an ox that gores and kills a man or a woman and the owner must pay {\i kofer} (atonement payment) \u8212- that {\i hishvah ha-katuv ishah le-ish le-chol mitot sheba-Torah}: when a woman is the victim of a capital crime or a death caused by a negligent animal, the severity of the offense and the consequence are equal to those when the victim is a man.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Why All Three\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} establishes that all three cases were necessary, as any two without the third would have left a gap. Had only the transgression case been stated \u8212- women are punished for their violations \u8212- one might have concluded that this is solely to provide {\i kaparah} (atonement) for women who transgress, not that they have equal standing in civil or capital contexts. Had only the civil law case been stated, one might have said this is necessary to allow women to function in civil society \u8212- without it, someone could steal from a woman with impunity, or she could steal and face no consequence, rendering her a pariah \u8212- but one could not have inferred equal treatment in the context of capital crimes. Had only the {\i kofer} case been stated, one might have attributed this simply to the weight of {\i ibud neshamah} \u8212- the loss of life is so severe that the {\i Torah} showed {\i rachamanut} and required the {\i kofer} payment regardless \u8212- but could not have inferred equal treatment in lesser civil matters.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Status Concern\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The third case is particularly striking and worth pausing over. The {\i Gemara} suggests the {\i hava amina} \u8212- the rejected initial assumption \u8212- that one might have said: an ox must pay {\i kofer} when it kills a man because a man is {\i bar mitzvot} \u8212- obligated in commandments \u8212- but a woman, who has fewer {\i mitzvah} obligations, might not generate the same {\i kofer} liability. Tosafot completes this by adding {\i de-lav bat mitzvot}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is a deeply uncomfortable {\i Gemara}. The notion that a woman\u8217's life might be worth less in terms of the {\i kofer} obligation because she is obligated in fewer {\i mitzvot} is precisely the kind of reading that, misunderstood, would suggest the {\i Torah} regards women as having reduced fundamental status. As Rav Moshe Feinstein addressed in a responsum written decades ago in response to concerns raised during the early period of the feminist movement \u8212- that women in some communities felt the {\i Torah} viewed them as second-class \u8212- he rejected categorically any suggestion that exemption from certain {\i mitzvot} reflects diminished {\i kedushah} or status. The {\i hava amina} here, rejected by the {\i Gemara}, is precisely the kind of reasoning that the {\i Gemara} recognizes as a possible misreading and then refutes.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Equal Citizens\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The practical irony is real: women bear tremendous {\i mitzvah} responsibility in sustaining the Jewish household \u8212- {\i kashrut}, {\i Shabbat}, raising children. And yet the formal structure of {\i mitzvah} obligation as enumerated tends to be weighted toward men, which can create a misleading impression of standing. The {\i Gemara}\u8217's conclusion, however, is unequivocal.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Three Exceptions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 With the general rule of women\u8217's equal obligation in negative commandments established, the {\i mishnah} and {\i Gemara} turn to the exceptions: {\i tumat kohanim}, {\i bal takif}, and {\i bal tashchit} of the beard. Women are exempt from all three.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Tumat Kohanim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Benei Aharon\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The exemption of women from {\i tumat kohanim} \u8212- the prohibition against a {\i kohen} becoming {\i tamei le-meitim} (ritually impure through contact with the dead) \u8212- is the least difficult of the three exceptions. The {\i Gemara} states simply: the {\i Torah} says {\i emor el ha-kohanim bnei Aharon} \u8212- \u8220"speak to the {\i kohanim}, the sons of Aharon.\u8221" {\i Bnei Aharon} \u8212- the sons of Aharon, not the daughters. The daughters of a {\i kohen} are called {\i bat kohen}, and there is even some discussion in {\i Gemarot} elsewhere about whether the term {\i kohenet} applies and whether a {\i bat kohen} receives {\i matnot kehunah} such as {\i zeroa, lechayayim ve-keivah}. But fundamentally, only male offspring carry the status of {\i kohen}. The prohibition against {\i tumat meitim} therefore applies only to male {\i kohanim}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Bal Takif & Tashchit\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Zekanecha Language\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The exemptions of women from {\i bal takif} (cutting the sidelocks of the head) and {\i bal tashchit} (destroying the corners of the beard) require more analysis. The source verse is: {\i lo takifu pe\u8217'at roshchem ve-lo tashchit et pe\u8217'at zekanecha}. The {\i Gemara} establishes the rule: {\i kol she-yeshno be-hashchatah yeshno be-hakafah} \u8212- whoever is subject to the prohibition of destroying the beard is also subject to the prohibition of cutting the sidelocks. Since women are not subject to {\i hashchatah} (the beard prohibition), they are not subject to {\i hakafah} (the sidelocks prohibition) either.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But this raises the immediate question: how do we know women are not subject to {\i hashchatah} in the first place? Two answers are offered. The first is {\i sevara} \u8212- simple logic. The {\i Torah} is evidently addressing men, since the beard is a characteristic of men. Even if there are rare exceptions, the {\i Torah}\u8217's presumed audience is those who naturally have beards. The second is textual: the verse says {\i pe\u8217'at zekanecha} \u8212- in the singular \u8212- rather than {\i pe\u8217'at zekhanchem} in the plural. The shift from the plural {\i roshchem} to the singular {\i zekanecha} is deliberate. {\i Zekanecha} \u8212- your beard, singular \u8212- means your beard and not your wife\u8217's beard ({\i ve-lo zikan ishtecha}). Since the verse itself limits the beard prohibition to a single male addressee, the entire prohibition is addressed to men alone.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sevel Logic\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The principle linking {\i hashchatah} and {\i hakafah} is derived from the juxtaposition within the verse itself: both the sidelocks and the beard appear together, and the {\i Gemara} reads this as establishing that the scope of one prohibition tracks the scope of the other. Whoever is subject to destroying the beard is subject to cutting the sidelocks; whoever is not subject to the former is not subject to the latter. Since women are excluded from {\i hashchatah}, they are likewise excluded from {\i hakafah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Braita Challenge\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} then introduces a challenge from a {\i braita}: {\i zikan ha-ishah ve-ha-saris she-he\u8217'elu sei\u8217'ar harei hen ke-zakan le-chol divreihem} \u8212- a beard grown by a woman or a eunuch (who would not normally grow facial hair) is treated as a beard for all their purposes. If so, this seems to contradict our {\i mishnah}, since the {\i braita} implies that the woman\u8217's beard carries full halachic status \u8212- presumably including the prohibition against shaving it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Abaye\u8217's Resolution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye resolves this firmly: the {\i braita} cannot be referring to the prohibition of {\i hashchatah}. That women are exempt from {\i hashchatah} \u8212- from the beard-shaving prohibition \u8212- is beyond question. Abaye invokes an additional basis beyond the {\i sevara} and {\i zekanecha} analysis: a {\i gezeirah shavah} linking {\i pe\u8217'at} in the {\i Yisrael} verse to {\i pe\u8217'at} in the {\i kohanim} verse. By the {\i kohanim}, the {\i Torah} says {\i bnei Aharon} \u8212- only the sons, not the daughters \u8212- and the verse concerning the beard of the {\i kohanim} uses the same word {\i pe\u8217'at} as the verse concerning {\i Yisrael}. By the {\i gezeirah shavah}, what applies to {\i kohanim} \u8212- i.e., that the prohibition is limited to men \u8212- applies equally to {\i Yisrael}. Since the {\i kohanim} are unambiguously only men, and the beard prohibition for {\i Yisrael} is linked to it by {\i gezeirah shavah}, the {\i Yisrael} prohibition is likewise limited to men.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kal Vachomer Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} interrupts Abaye\u8217's reasoning with a sharp question: if we accept that {\i bnei Aharon} applies throughout the entire section addressing {\i kohanim} \u8212- covering {\i tumah}, {\i korcha}, and {\i pe\u8217'at zekanam lo yigaleichu} \u8212- then why do we need the {\i gezeirah shavah} at all? We could simply derive by {\i kal va-chomer}: if {\i kohanim}, who are subject to greater {\i mitzvah} obligations and more stringent demands than an ordinary {\i Yisrael}, are limited to men ({\i bnei Aharon ve-lo bnot Aharon}) for the beard prohibition \u8212- then for {\i Yisrael}, who are subject to fewer obligations, certainly it should be limited to men as well.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gezerah Shavah Scope\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye\u8217's response is that without the {\i gezeirah shavah}, he would have said {\i hifsik ha-inyan} \u8212- the context was interrupted. That is, {\i bnei Aharon} at the beginning of the section applies only to the {\i tumah} prohibition immediately following; by the time the {\i Torah} reaches the beard prohibition later in the section, the limiting phrase no longer applies, and the beard prohibition would apply equally to men and women. If so, the {\i kal va-chomer} would also fail, since there would be nothing on the {\i kohanim} side limiting it to men with respect to {\i pe\u8217'at ha-zakan}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is where the {\i gezeirah shavah} becomes essential. The very existence of the {\i gezeirah shavah} \u8212- the shared word {\i pe\u8217'at} linking the {\i Yisrael} and {\i kohanim} verses \u8212- signals that there is something to learn from one to the other. If the {\i kohanim} verse were also men and women (because {\i bnei Aharon} didn\u8217't extend that far), there would be nothing to learn. The fact that the {\i Torah} invites a {\i gezeirah shavah} forces us to conclude that there is a meaningful difference between the two cases, and the only meaningful difference is the restriction to men. Therefore, the {\i gezeirah shavah} itself tells us that {\i bnei Aharon} does extend through the entire section, including the beard prohibition.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} then asks: even granting that {\i hifsik ha-inyan} is a real concern, could the {\i gezeirah shavah} not be teaching something else entirely \u8212- not the restriction to men \u8212- while the beard prohibition by {\i kohanim} would still apply to women? And it answers: yes, because the {\i gezeirah shavah} is indeed needed for something else as well.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tashchit Definition\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gezeirah shavah} between {\i pe\u8217'at zekanam lo yigaleichu} (by {\i kohanim}) and {\i lo tashchit et pe\u8217'at zekanecha} (by {\i Yisrael}) is also needed to clarify the definition of what type of shaving is prohibited. {\i Tashchit} \u8212- destruction \u8212- implies getting to the root, cutting destructively at the surface; but it does not specify the instrument. {\i Yigaleichu} \u8212- shaving, cutting with a blade \u8212- implies a blade instrument but does not specify that it must destroy at the root. Each verse alone is incomplete: {\i tashchit} alone might include plucking with tweezers ({\i malket u-rahitani}), since that destroys the hair at the root. {\i Yigaleichu} alone might include scissors ({\i misparayim}), since scissors cut with a blade-like action.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} resolves this through the combination: {\i ha-keitzad, giluach she-yesh bo hashchatah} \u8212- what is the prohibited form? A shaving that both involves a blade and destroys at the root. {\i Havei omer zeh ta\u8217'ar} \u8212- that is a razor blade ({\i ta\u8217'ar}). Scissors do not destroy at the root; tweezers do not involve a blade-style shaving. Only a razor blade satisfies both conditions simultaneously. This is the halachic basis for the opinion that an electric shaver, which operates by a scissors-type action rather than a blade-against-skin action, might not violate {\i bal tashchit}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 So the {\i gezeirah shavah} serves a double purpose: it limits the prohibition to men (via the {\i bnei Aharon} extension), and it defines the nature of the prohibited act as razor-blade shaving only.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Braita Purpose\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Returning now to the {\i braita} that stated a woman\u8217's beard is treated as a beard for all purposes: if it does not refer to {\i hashchatah}, what {\i halacha} does it convey? Mar Zutra initially suggests it refers to {\i tumat nega\u8217'im} \u8212- the impurity of {\i tzara\u8217'at} on the beard. The {\i Gemara} responds that {\i tumat nega\u8217'im} is explicit in a {\i pasuk}: {\i ve-ish o ishah ki yihyeh vo nega be-rosh o ve-zakan} \u8212- a man or a woman who has a spot on the head or beard. No {\i braita} is needed to teach something already in the verse.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Mar Zutra then suggests the {\i braita} addresses {\i taharat nega\u8217'im} \u8212- the purification process. The {\i Gemara} dismisses this as well: once she is subject to {\i tumah} just like a man, she is obviously subject to the corresponding {\i taharah} process.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} then settles on the correct reading: the {\i braita} is needed to clarify how to read the verse {\i ish o ishah ki yihyeh vo nega be-rosh o ve-zakan}. One might naturally read it as follows: {\i ish o ishah} \u8212- a man or a woman \u8212- {\i ki yihyeh vo nega be-rosh}, if there is a spot on the head; {\i o ve-zakan}, or \u8212- in the case of a man \u8212- on the beard. Under this reading, the reference to {\i zakan} reverts to {\i ish} only, and {\i isha} is mentioned only in connection with the head, not the beard. The {\i braita} comes to teach that this reading is incorrect: a woman\u8217's beard, if she has one, has the full status of a beard for purposes of {\i tzara\u8217'at} impurity \u8212- {\i harei hen ke-zakan le-chol divreihem} means for this purpose. The {\i braita} thus does not conflict with the {\i mishnah}\u8217's ruling that women are exempt from {\i bal tashchit}; it addresses an entirely separate question regarding {\i nega\u8217'im}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Bal Yikrechu\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Isi\u8217's Teaching\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now presents a teaching of Isi: {\i af bal yikrechu nashim pturot} \u8212- women are also exempt from the prohibition of {\i bal yikrechu korcha} \u8212- making a bald spot on the head as a sign of mourning ({\i lo tasimu korcha bein eineichem la-met}). This is a separate prohibition from the beard shaving, derived from a separate verse, and Isi teaches that it too does not apply to women.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Banim Atem\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The exegetical basis is the verse in Devarim: {\i banim atem la-Hashem Elokeichem, lo titgodedu ve-lo tasimu korcha bein eineichem la-met} \u8212- \u8220"you are children of the Lord your God; do not cut yourselves and do not make a bald spot between your eyes for the dead.\u8221" Isi reads {\i banim} \u8212- sons \u8212- as limiting: {\i banim ve-lo banot le-korcha}. Sons, not daughters, are the subject of the {\i korcha} prohibition. This is a deliberate narrowing of the prohibition to men, derived from the masculine noun {\i banim}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Korcha vs Gedida\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 However, the {\i Gemara} immediately asks whether this limitation also applies to {\i gedida} \u8212- lacerations in the flesh, which is also mentioned in the same verse ({\i lo titgodedu}). Perhaps {\i banim ve-lo banot} limits women\u8217's exemption to that prohibition as well?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} answers no, because of the continuation of the verse: {\i ki am kadosh atah la-Hashem Elokecha} \u8212- \u8220"for you are a holy people to the Lord your God.\u8221" This phrase, {\i am kadosh}, encompasses everyone \u8212- {\i am} is the entire people, men and women alike \u8212- and it provides the basis for the prohibition of {\i gedida}. Therefore, the prohibition of lacerations in the flesh applies to women equally.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The question then becomes: why do we include women in {\i gedida} from {\i am kadosh} but exclude them from {\i korcha} based on {\i banim}? The answer is the scope of each prohibition. {\i Gedida} \u8212- lacerations in the flesh \u8212- applies to any part of the body, whether in an area of hair or not ({\i she-yeshnah be-makom se\u8217'ar u-velo be-makom se\u8217'ar}). It is a broader prohibition. {\i Korcha} \u8212- a bald spot \u8212- applies only in an area of hair ({\i she-einah ela be-makom se\u8217'ar}). It is a more limited prohibition. Therefore, from {\i banim ve-lo banot} we exclude women from the narrower prohibition, and from {\i ki am kadosh} we include women in the broader one.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 We will continue on {\i Shabbat} with the full analysis of Isi\u8217's position.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Kiddushin 36\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Korcha & Women\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Issi\u8217's View\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} picks up at the very bottom of 35b, continuing the discussion of the scope of {\i mitzvot} as they apply to women and men. The established principle is that all negative prohibitions apply equally to both, with the exception of the shaving and cutting of the forelocks, the shaving of the beard, and the {\i mitzvah} of {\i tuma} for {\i Kohanim}. We are now also dealing with the position of {\i Issi}, who holds that the prohibition of making a bald spot on one\u8217's head \u8212- {\i korcha} \u8212- likewise does not apply to women.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Banim v\u8217'lo Banot\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} raises an initial challenge to {\i Issi}\u8217's position. The verse introducing the prohibition of {\i korcha} opens with the phrase {\i banim atem la-Shem Elokeichem} \u8212- \u8220"you are children of the Lord your God\u8221" \u8212- immediately before prohibiting the making of a bald spot or a laceration. {\i Issi} reads {\i banim} and not {\i banot} as excluding women from {\i korcha}. But the {\i gemara} asks: {\i ve\u8217'eima banim ve-lo banot bein le\u8217'korcha bein le\u8217'gedida} \u8212- why not say that the exclusion of women applies equally to both the bald spot and the laceration? After all, both appear in close proximity to the same phrase.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} answers: {\i ve\u8217'hachi ksiv Am Kadosh atah la-Shem Elokecha bis\u8217'rita hu d\u8217'chsiv} \u8212- the end of the verse, from which {\i Issi} derives the inclusion of women in the prohibition of laceration, speaks specifically of {\i s\u8217'rita}. {\i Rashi} explains that {\i s\u8217'rita} refers to a laceration made with the hand rather than with an implement such as a knife, as distinct from {\i gedida}. One might then ask: since {\i s\u8217'rita} and {\i gedida} appear in different verses, perhaps women should be excluded from both based on {\i banim} and not {\i banot}? The {\i gemara} replies: {\i ksavar Issi s\u8217'rita u-gedida achas hi} \u8212- {\i Issi} holds that {\i s\u8217'rita} and {\i gedida} are in substance the same prohibition. Therefore the verse employing the phrase {\i Am Kadosh} comes to include women in that single prohibition of laceration, and women are correspondingly excluded only from {\i korcha}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Abaye\u8217's Gezeira\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 At the top of 36a, the {\i gemara} presents {\i Abaye}\u8217's derivation of {\i Issi}\u8217's position: {\i Abaye amar hainu ta\u8217'ama d\u8217'Issi, de-gamar korcha korcha mi-Bnei Aharon} \u8212- he learns it through a {\i gezeira shava} of {\i korcha-korcha} from the passage dealing with {\i Bnei Aharon}. The Torah states separately, regarding the {\i Kohanim}: {\i lo yikrechu korcha be-rosham u-fe\u8217'at zekanam lo yegalechu} \u8212- they shall not make a bald spot on their head nor shall they shave the corners of their beards. This parallel prohibition is linked by the shared term {\i korcha} to the prohibition incumbent upon all of Israel.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Because the verse concerning {\i Bnei Aharon} says {\i banim} \u8212- sons \u8212- this naturally implies the exclusion of daughters. {\i Abaye} argues: {\i mi-Bnei Aharon mah le-halan nashim peturot af kan nashim peturot} \u8212- just as the prohibition for {\i Kohanim} is limited to the males and not the females, so too the parallel prohibition for all Israel is limited to men and not women.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gemara Challenge\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} immediately challenges this: if {\i Issi} is already relying on the formulation {\i Bnei Aharon ve-lo bnot Aharon}, he has no need for a {\i gezeira shava} at all. Once we know that female {\i Kohanim} are exempt from {\i korcha}, we could simply apply a {\i kal va-chomer}: if women are excluded even among {\i Kohanim} \u8212- who bear additional {\i mitzvot} \u8212- then certainly women among ordinary Israelites should be excluded. {\i Mah Kohanim she-ribba bahem ha-katuv mitzvot yetirot Bnei Aharon ve-lo bnot Aharon, Yisrael lo kol she-ken} \u8212- all the more so for non-{\i Kohanim}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} responds: {\i ilmalei gezeira shava hava amina hifsik ha-inyan} \u8212- had there been no {\i gezeira shava}, one might have thought that the language {\i Bnei Aharon} does not apply to {\i korcha} in that passage because there is a textual interruption between the introduction of {\i Bnei Aharon} and the eventual verse prohibiting the bald spot. The {\i gezeira shava}, by linking the two passages, tells us there is something to be learned from the connection, and what is learned is the limitation: {\i banim} and not {\i banot}. Without the {\i gezeira shava}, the interruption in the text would have prevented that inference.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then pushes further: but now that there is a {\i gezeira shava}, let us say the interruption nonetheless applies, and do not derive the exclusion of women from it. The {\i gezeira shava} must simply be telling us something else \u8212- namely, what we learn in the {\i baraita}. The question is therefore: what is the {\i gezeira shava} of {\i korcha-korcha} actually accomplishing?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gezeira Details\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} explains the full content of the {\i gezeira shava} as presented in the {\i baraita}. The verse by {\i Kohanim} reads {\i lo yikrechu korcha be-rosham} \u8212- they shall not make a bald spot on their head. One might have thought that making multiple bald spots constitutes only a single prohibition. {\i Talmud lomar korcha} \u8212- the singular form {\i korcha} teaches that one is liable for each individual bald spot, {\i le-chayev al kol achat ve-achat}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Why then does the verse add the word {\i be-rosham}, \u8220"on their head\u8221"? Because the verse for Israel reads {\i lo titgodedu ve-lo tasimu korcha bein eineichem la-met} \u8212- do not make a laceration and do not place a bald spot between your eyes for the dead. One might have thought that the prohibition of {\i korcha} is limited specifically to the area between the eyes. {\i Talmud lomar be-rosham} \u8212- \u8220"on their head\u8221" teaches that the prohibition extends across the entire head, {\i le-chayev al kol ha-rosh ke-vein einav}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 These two details \u8212- liability for each separate bald spot, and application to the whole head \u8212- might have been thought to apply only to {\i Kohanim}, who bear additional {\i mitzvot}. So how do we know they apply to Israel as well? {\i Ne\u8217'emar kan korcha ve-ne\u8217'emar le-halan korcha} \u8212- the same term appears in both contexts, and through the {\i gezeira shava} we derive that just as by {\i Kohanim} one is liable for each bald spot and across the entire head, so too by Israel. And in the reverse direction, just as by Israel the prohibition is specifically in the context of mourning a dead person, {\i al ha-met}, so too by {\i Kohanim} the prohibition is limited to the context of mourning a dead person.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This {\i gezeira shava} of {\i korcha-korcha} is thus carrying a great deal of substantive content. The {\i gemara} therefore argues that it is already fully occupied with these teachings, and one cannot assume it is additionally teaching the exclusion of women. The question becomes: how does {\i Abaye} then derive the exclusion of women from the {\i gezeira shava}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Double Karcha\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} resolves this in favor of {\i Abaye}: {\i im ken nichtov kra kerach} \u8212- if the Torah merely wanted to convey those teachings, it could have written {\i kerach} rather than {\i korcha}. The fact that both the verse for {\i Kohanim} and the verse for Israel use the form {\i korcha} \u8212- with the additional {\i heh} \u8212- signals that there is yet another {\i gezeira shava} embedded in the text, a secondary derivation. And that secondary {\i gezeira shava} is what teaches us the limitation of {\i banim} and not {\i banot}: the exclusion of women from the prohibition of {\i korcha} as derived from {\i Bnei Aharon ve-lo bnot Aharon}. This is {\i Issi}\u8217's reason, as reconstructed by {\i Abaye}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava\u8217's Tefillin\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rava} offers an entirely different derivation of {\i Issi}\u8217's position: {\i de-yalif bein eineichem mi-tefillin} \u8212- he derives the exemption of women from {\i korcha} through an analogy to {\i tefillin}. The verse for Israel says {\i lo tasimu korcha bein eineichem la-met} \u8212- do not place a bald spot between your eyes. The same phrase {\i bein eineichem} also appears in the context of {\i tefillin}. Since women are exempt from {\i tefillin}, we derive by analogy \u8212- {\i mah le-halan nashim peturot af kan nashim peturot} \u8212- that women are also exempt from the prohibition of {\i korcha}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Abaye v. Rava\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then inquires into each {\i amora}\u8217's reasons for rejecting the other\u8217's approach. {\i Rava mai ta\u8217'ama lo amar ke-Abaye} \u8212- why did {\i Rava} not accept {\i Abaye}\u8217's derivation? {\i Kerach karcha lo mashma lei} \u8212- he does not accept that the difference in spelling between {\i kerach} and {\i korcha} generates a second {\i gezeira shava}. He remains troubled by the distance in the text between the introduction of {\i Bnei Aharon} and the eventual discussion of {\i korcha}, and therefore finds {\i Abaye}\u8217's approach unconvincing.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Conversely, {\i v\u8217'Abaye mai ta\u8217'ama lo amar ke-Rava} \u8212- why did {\i Abaye} not accept {\i Rava}\u8217's derivation from {\i tefillin}? {\i Tefillin gufayhu mei-hacha gamir lah} \u8212- because the very rule that {\i tefillin} are placed on the upper part of the head, corresponding to where hair grows above the forehead and opposite the space between the eyes, is itself learned from the passage about {\i korcha}. The {\i gezeira shava} of {\i bein eineichem} was originally employed to teach something about {\i tefillin}: {\i mah le-halan makom she-osim korcha be-govah shel rosh, af kan makom hanacha be-govah ha-rosh} \u8212- just as the bald spot between the eyes is understood to refer to the top of the head where hair grows, so too the placement of {\i tefillin} is on the top of the head. The {\i rishonim} raise the question of whether one {\i gezeira shava} can simultaneously teach two separate laws, and the conclusion is that once a {\i gezeira shava} is focused on clarifying the meaning of a word \u8212- in this case, what {\i bein einav} means \u8212- it cannot simultaneously pivot to teach an entirely separate {\i halacha} such as the exemption of women.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then asks: since both {\i Abaye} and {\i Rava} derive the exclusion of women from {\i korcha} through means other than the opening phrase {\i banim atem la-Shem Elokeichem}, what do they do with that phrase? The answer is that {\i Abaye} and {\i Rava} both require that phrase for an altogether different teaching, as presented in the {\i baraita} that follows.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Banim Atem\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Children Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} transitions to a broader discussion of the phrase {\i banim atem la-Shem Elokeichem}. According to {\i Abaye} and {\i Rava}, this phrase cannot be used to derive the exclusion of women from {\i korcha} since they each have alternative derivations for that. The phrase is instead required for the teaching of the {\i baraita}: {\i banim atem la-Shem Elokeichem, bi-zman she-atem nohagim minhag banim atem keruim banim} \u8212- when you conduct yourselves as children, you are called children of God.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Yehuda\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is the position of {\i Rabbi Yehuda}: {\i ein atem nohagim minhag banim ein atem keruim banim, divrei Rabbi Yehuda} \u8212- when you do not conduct yourselves as children, you are not called children. The implication is that the title is conditional on appropriate behavior, and that engaging in idolatrous practices such as the very rituals of laceration and bald spots \u8212- which the {\i Torah} is here prohibiting \u8212- would forfeit the relationship.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Meir\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Meir} takes a strikingly more compassionate view: {\i bein kach u-vein kach atem keruim banim} \u8212- either way, you are called children of God. This reading seems especially fitting for a period of reflection and repentance, as {\i Rabbi Meir} insists that the parent-child relationship between God and Israel is never truly severed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Scriptural Proofs\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} presents multiple scriptural proofs in support of {\i Rabbi Meir}\u8217's position. The verse {\i banim sechalim hem} \u8212- \u8220"they are foolish children\u8221" \u8212- demonstrates that even foolish children retain the designation of children. The verse {\i banim lo emun bam} \u8212- \u8220"children without trustworthiness\u8221" \u8212- shows that even when Israel acts without faithfulness they are still called children. The verse {\i zera me-re\u8217'im banim mash\u8217'chitim} \u8212- \u8220"an evil seed, children who deal corruptly\u8221" \u8212- shows that even when Israel engages in corruption, which the {\i gemara} reads as {\i avoda zara} in line with the usage of {\i shichet amcha}, they are still called children. And finally, {\i v\u8217'haya bi-mkom asher ye\u8217'amer lahem lo ami atem ye\u8217'amer lahem bnei Kel chai} \u8212- \u8220"in the place where it is said to them \u8216'you are not my nation,\u8217' it will be said to them \u8216'you are the children of the living God\u8217'\u8221" \u8212- showing that ultimately, even when the most severe divine rejection seems to apply, Israel will still be recognized as God\u8217's children, and indeed as children without any negative qualifier, fully restored to their ideal relationship.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Korban Service\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishna Overview\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now turns to a new {\i mishnah} dealing with acts associated with {\i korbanot} from which women are excluded. The {\i mishnah} enumerates: {\i ha-smichot ve-ha-tnufot ve-ha-hagashot} \u8212- placing the hand on the offering, waving it, and bringing it to the altar \u8212- along with {\i kmitzot}, {\i haktarot}, {\i melikot}, {\i kabalot}, and {\i hazayot} \u8212- taking the handful, burning it, slaughtering the bird with the fingernail, receiving the blood, and sprinkling the blood. All of these, the {\i mishnah} states, {\i nohagim ba-anashim ve-lo ba-nashim} \u8212- apply to men and not to women. The first two items \u8212- {\i smicha} and {\i tnufa} \u8212- pertain to the owner of the {\i korban}, while the remaining acts are those performed by the {\i Kohanim}, from whom female descendants of {\i Kohanim} are similarly excluded.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Smicha\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} derives the exclusion of women from {\i smicha} from the verse {\i Daber el Bnei Yisrael ve-samach} \u8212- speak to the children of Israel, and he shall place his hand. {\i Bnei Yisrael somchim ve-ein bnot Yisrael somchot} \u8212- only the sons of Israel, not the daughters. The {\i gemara} notes that {\i Tosafot} raises the broader question of whether women may nonetheless perform {\i smicha} on a voluntary basis \u8212- a significant debate with implications extending beyond this particular act to the general question of women\u8217's participation in {\i mitzvot} from which they are technically exempt. {\i Ramban} similarly raises the question of whether every instance of the phrase {\i Bnei Yisrael} in the Torah is meant to exclude women, noting that the phrase appears in many contexts \u8212- but for its purposes, {\i Chazal} understand it as limiting this act to men.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tnufa\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 For {\i tnufa}, the waving of the {\i korban}, the verse reads {\i Daber el Bnei Yisrael ve-henif} \u8212- and they shall wave. The derivation is parallel: {\i Bnei Yisrael menifim ve-ein bnot Yisrael menifot} \u8212- only the sons of Israel wave, not the daughters.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hagasha & Kmitza\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 For {\i hagasha} \u8212- the bringing of the {\i mincha} to the corner of the altar \u8212- the verse reads {\i Ve-zot torat ha-mincha, hakrev otah bnei Aharon} \u8212- this is the law of the {\i mincha}, the sons of Aaron shall bring it. {\i Bnei Aharon ve-lo bnot Aharon} \u8212- the sons and not the daughters. For {\i kmitza} \u8212- the taking of the handful \u8212- the verse reads {\i ve-heviah el bnei Aharon ve-kamatz} \u8212- and he shall bring it to the sons of Aaron and they shall take the {\i kmitza}. Again, {\i bnei Aharon ve-lo bnot Aharon}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Haktara & Melika\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 For {\i haktara} \u8212- burning the offering on the altar \u8212- the verse reads {\i ve-hiktiru oto bnei Aharon} \u8212- the sons of Aaron shall burn it. For {\i melika} \u8212- the slaughtering of the bird with the fingernail \u8212- the verse reads {\i u-malak ve-hiktir} \u8212- he shall cut it and burn it on the altar. The {\i gemara} notes that {\i melika} is juxtaposed to {\i haktara} \u8212- {\i ve-hukash melika le-haktara} \u8212- and since {\i haktara} requires {\i bnei Aharon} and not {\i bnot Aharon}, so too {\i melika} is limited to male {\i Kohanim}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hazayot\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 For {\i hazayot} \u8212- sprinkling \u8212- the {\i gemara} is careful to identify which act of sprinkling is meant. If it refers to the sprinkling of the ashes of the {\i Para Aduma}, the verse explicitly specifies {\i Elazar}, a named individual, and even though we rule that a {\i Kohen Gadol} is not strictly required for this act, the explicit naming of a {\i Kohen Gadol} makes the exclusion of women obvious. Similarly, if it refers to the inner sprinklings on the {\i Mizbe\u8217'ach ha-zahav} \u8212- the golden altar \u8212- the verse speaks of {\i ha-Kohen ha-Mashiach}, the anointed {\i Kohen}, i.e., the {\i Kohen Gadol}, which again makes the exclusion self-evident. The {\i mishnah} must therefore be referring to something less obvious: the sprinkling of the blood of a bird offering, where the {\i Torah} uses the language of {\i hazayah} rather than {\i zerikah} because the quantity of blood is too small for the standard act of throwing.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This case is derived through a {\i kal va-chomer} from an ordinary animal offering. {\i U-mah ben tzon she-lo kava lo Kohen li-shechitato} \u8212- for a sheep or cattle, the slaughter itself does not require a {\i Kohen} and can be performed even by a woman, yet {\i kava lo Kohen le-hazayato} \u8212- the throwing of the blood still requires a {\i Kohen}, explicitly derived from {\i bnei Aharon ve-lo bnot Aharon}. {\i Ben of she-kava lo Kohen le-melichato} \u8212- a bird offering, where even the slaughter requires a {\i Kohen} to perform it personally, {\i eino din she-kava lo Kohen le-hazzatah} \u8212- it is certainly a logical inference that the sprinkling of its blood likewise requires a {\i Kohen}, and therefore also excludes women.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Sota & Nezirah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Elazar Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} concludes with the exceptions: {\i chutz mi-minchat Sotah ve-nezirah she-hen menifot} \u8212- excluding the {\i mincha} of the {\i Sotah} and the {\i nezirah}, which they wave themselves. {\i Rav Elazar} said to {\i Rabbi Yoshiya} of his generation: do not sit down until you explain to me this {\i shemata}. {\i Minayin li-minchat Sotah she-te\u8217'unah tenufah} \u8212- from where do we derive that the {\i mincha} of the {\i Sotah} requires waving, and moreover that the {\i Sotah} herself waves it?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sota Yad-Yad\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Yoshiya} responds: {\i be-gufeih ketiv ve-henif et ha-mincha} \u8212- it is written explicitly in the passage of the {\i Sotah} that the {\i mincha} is waved. But {\i Rav Elazar} clarifies his question: he already knows the {\i Kohen} waves it. What he wants to know is {\i tenufah bi-ve\u8217'alim me-nayin} \u8212- from where do we know that the owner herself, the woman, waves it? The answer: {\i atya yad yad mi-shelamim} \u8212- this is derived through a {\i gezeira shava} of {\i yad-yad} from {\i shelamim}. In the passage of the {\i Sotah}, the verse reads {\i ve-lakach ha-Kohen mi-yad ha-ishah} \u8212- the {\i Kohen} shall take from the hand of the woman, and then he waves it. By {\i shelamim}, the verse reads {\i yadav tevinah} \u8212- his hands shall bring it, which means the owner waves it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sota Shelamim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 From this {\i gezeira shava}, both passages illuminate each other in a mutual derivation. By the {\i Sotah}, it is explicit that the {\i Kohen} waves it \u8212- he takes it from her hand and waves it. This teaches us that by {\i shelamim} as well, not only does the owner wave it, but the {\i Kohen} also waves it alongside the owner. In the reverse direction, by {\i shelamim} it is explicit that the owner waves it \u8212- {\i yadav tevinah}. This teaches us that by the {\i Sotah} as well, the owner, that is, the woman herself, waves it \u8212- {\i mah le-halan be\u8217'alim af kan be\u8217'alim}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Holding Together\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then asks {\i ha keitzad} \u8212- how exactly is this accomplished in practice, given that there is one physical item being waved? {\i Kohen meniach yado tachat yad be\u8217'alim u-menif} \u8212- the {\i Kohen} places his hand beneath the hands of the owner and they wave together. {\i Tosafot} raises the question of how precisely this is understood: does \u8220"beneath\u8221" mean the hands are in direct contact with one another, in which case there is no concern about {\i negiah} but there might be a concern about whether the {\i Kohen}\u8217's hand constitutes a {\i hefsek} \u8212- an interposition \u8212- between the owner\u8217's hand and the vessel? Or does \u8220"beneath\u8221" mean that the owner holds the vessel near its rim while the {\i Kohen} holds it near its base? {\i Tosafot} presents both possibilities.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Nezirah Kaf-Kaf\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having established the {\i Sotah}\u8217's obligation and the manner in which it is fulfilled, the {\i gemara} notes: {\i ash\u8217'kechan Sotah \u8212- nezirah me-nayin} \u8212- we have found a source for the {\i Sotah}, but from where do we derive the same for a {\i nezirah}? The answer: {\i atya kaf kaf mi-Sotah} \u8212- through a {\i gezeira shava} of {\i kaf-kaf}, the hand, linking the passage of the {\i nezirah} to that of the {\i Sotah}. Just as by the {\i Sotah} both the {\i Kohen} and the owner wave it together, so too by the {\i nezirah} both the {\i Kohen} and the owner wave it together, and the {\i nezirah} herself participates in the waving of her own offering.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Kiddushin 37\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Intro & Mitzvot Categori\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Perek Theme\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i daf} before us is 37, and we pick up with a {\i mishna} appearing at the bottom of 36b. The broader theme of this {\i perek} is the construction of categories: first, the categorization of the various modes of acquisition ({\i kinyanim}), and now a categorization of {\i mitzvot} according to who and where they obligate. The {\i perek} calls to mind the {\i ain bein} passages in {\i Megilla}, which sweep across a wide scope of material. There may also be a linguistic thread \u8212- the {\i kol} construction, as in {\i kol mitzvas haben al ha\u8217'av} \u8212- but the dominant organizing principle seems to be simply the {\i perek}\u8217's ambition to sort broad classes of things into coherent categories. It is, notably, the longest {\i perek} in {\i Shas}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gender to Geography\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having covered the division of {\i mitzvot} between men and women, the {\i mishna} now transitions to a second axis of categorization: which {\i mitzvot} are geographically restricted to {\i Eretz Yisrael}, and which apply wherever one happens to be.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Mitzvot Teluyot Ba\u8217'aretz\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishna Text\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} states: {\i kol mitzva she\u8217'hi teluya ba\u8217'aretz eina noheget ela ba\u8217'aretz} \u8212- any {\i mitzva} that is dependent on the land applies only in the land of Israel. {\i Ve\u8217'she\u8217'eina teluya ba\u8217'aretz noheget bein ba\u8217'aretz uvein bechutz la\u8217'aretz} \u8212- and what is not dependent on the land applies whether inside or outside of the land of Israel.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Orlah & Kilayim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} immediately carves out two exceptions: {\i chutz min ha\u8217'orlah vehakilayim} \u8212- {\i orlah} and {\i kilayim} apply even outside the land, despite being land-based {\i mitzvot}. {\i Orlah} refers to the prohibition on fruit during the first three years of a tree\u8217's planting; {\i kilayim} refers to the prohibition on crossbreeding seeds.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What is striking here is the relative legal weight of these two exceptions. {\i Orlah} outside {\i Eretz Yisrael} is a {\i halacha le-Moshe mi-Sinai}, while {\i kilayim} outside {\i Eretz Yisrael} is only {\i derabbanan}. Intuitively, one might have expected {\i kilayim} to carry the heavier extra-territorial force, since the prohibition on mixing species is not obviously tethered to the sanctity of the land \u8212- it parallels other forms of {\i kilayim} such as crossbreeding animals or mixing wool and linen. {\i Orlah}, by contrast, is closely linked in the {\i Torah} to {\i neta reva\u8217'i}, the fourth-year fruits brought to the {\i Beit Hamikdash}, and would seem on its face to belong to the category of {\i kedushat ha\u8217'aretz}. Yet the {\i halacha} inverts one\u8217's expectations: {\i orlah} outside the land carries the weight of {\i halacha le-Moshe mi-Sinai}, while {\i kilayim} is merely {\i derabbanan}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The further implication of the {\i mishna\u8217's} phrasing is important. By explicitly listing {\i orlah} and {\i kilayim} \u8212- one of which is only {\i derabbanan} \u8212- as the exceptions that apply in {\i chutz la\u8217'aretz}, the {\i mishna} implies that everything else does not apply outside the land even at the {\i derabbanan} level. This is a significant point that will bear on later discussion.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Eliezer on Chadash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Eliezer} adds: {\i af chadash} \u8212- even {\i chadash}, the prohibition on new grain before the {\i omer} is brought on the sixteenth of {\i Nissan}, applies outside the land of Israel. This position sets up the central dispute of the {\i sugya}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Teluyah Definition\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Peshat Proposal\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} opens by asking: {\i mai teluya u\u8217'mai she\u8217'eina teluya} \u8212- what does it mean for a {\i mitzva} to be \u8220"dependent\u8221" on the land, and what does it mean to be \u8220"not dependent\u8221"? The initial proposal is that {\i teluya} means that the relevant verse explicitly conditions the {\i mitzva} on entering the land \u8212- {\i kol mitzva sheketiv bah bi\u8217'ah} \u8212- while a {\i mitzva} that does not say \u8220"when you come into the land\u8221" is not so restricted.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tefillin Objection\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} immediately challenges this reading: {\i va\u8217'harei tefillin u\u8217'peter chamor dekesiv bahen bi\u8217'ah} \u8212- both {\i tefillin} and {\i peter chamor} (the redemption of the firstborn donkey) are explicitly conditioned in the verse on entering the land, yet both apply equally inside and outside of {\i Eretz Yisrael}. The verse in {\i Shemos} 13 \u8212- {\i vehayah ki yeviacha Hashem el eretz hakenani} \u8212- introduces both the {\i mitzva} of the firstborn and, ultimately, {\i tefillin}, within the frame of the entry into the land. If the proposed criterion were correct, these {\i mitzvot} should not apply in {\i chutz la\u8217'aretz} \u8212- or at the very least, one might argue they apply only {\i derabbanan} outside the land. But the {\i mishna} forecloses even that escape: since {\i kilayim} is only {\i derabbanan} outside the land and yet is listed explicitly as an exception, the implication is that everything not listed does not apply even {\i derabbanan}. We know from the {\i gemara} itself that the Sages of {\i Bavel} wore {\i tefillin}, so {\i tefillin} plainly applies in {\i chutz la\u8217'aretz} at the full {\i d\u8217'oraita} level.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rashi\u8217's Answer\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 There is a famous {\i Rashi} on the {\i Torah} \u8212- picked up by the {\i Ramban} \u8212- which is relevant here. On the {\i pasuk} in the second {\i parasha} of {\i Shema} \u8212- {\i vesamtem es devarai eileh al levavchem ve\u8217'al nafshechem} \u8212- {\i Rashi} explains: {\i af acharei shetiglu heyu metzuyanim bemitzvot} \u8212- even after you go into exile, distinguish yourselves with {\i mitzvot}: put on {\i tefillin}, affix {\i mezuzot} \u8212- {\i kedei shelo yihyu lachem chadashim keshetachzoru} \u8212- so that these practices will not seem foreign to you when you return. {\i Rashi} then cites the verse {\i hatzivi lach tziyunim}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Ramban} is struck by this: can one really say that {\i tefillin} and {\i mezuzot} are merely \u8220"practice\u8221" for the real performance back in the land? For the {\i Ramban}, this {\i Rashi} opens onto his broader theological position that even {\i mitzvot} that are {\i chovat haguf} \u8212- personal obligations \u8212- find their true and ideal fulfillment in {\i Eretz Yisrael}. The {\i Torah} does not envision Judaism as an abstract religion that happens to include, as an adjunct, a homeland; the {\i Torah} of {\i Devarim} imagines a nation living as a people in their land. {\i Galus} is called exile for a reason \u8212- it is not the natural condition. So even though it is true that {\i mitzvot ha-teluyot ba-guf} apply in {\i chutz la\u8217'aretz}, that is not the ideal mode of their performance.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Some have been so unsettled by {\i Rashi\u8217's} formulation \u8212- that {\i tefillin} and {\i mezuzot} are essentially preparatory exercises for return \u8212- that they have proposed emending the text of {\i Rashi} to read {\i terumos u\u8217'maasros} instead of {\i tefillin u\u8217'mezuzot}. But that emendation is not authentic; {\i Rashi} is drawing on the plain sense of the {\i pesukim}, which speak about going into exile and then say {\i vesamtem es devarai eileh\u8230 ?uktavtem al mezuzos beiseicha} \u8212- with {\i tefillin} and {\i mezuzah} explicitly in view, followed by {\i lema\u8217'an yirbu yemeichem al ha\u8217'adamah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What makes this {\i gemara} striking is that it presents as a mere {\i havah amina} what {\i Rashi} treats as the {\i peshat} of the {\i pesukim} \u8212- that {\i tefillin} might be restricted to {\i Eretz Yisrael}. The {\i gemara} ultimately rejects that reading, but the underlying intuition that all {\i mitzvot} find their home in {\i Eretz Yisrael} is very much present.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Chovat Haguf & Karka\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Yehuda\u8217's Principle\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rav Yehuda} resolves the question by reframing it entirely. The distinction, he says, is not about how the verse is phrased, but about the nature of the {\i mitzva} itself. {\i Kol mitzvah she\u8217'hi chovat haguf noheget bein ba\u8217'aretz bein bechutz la\u8217'aretz} \u8212- any {\i mitzva} that is a personal obligation applies everywhere. {\i Chovat karka} \u8212- a land-related obligation \u8212- applies only in {\i Eretz Yisrael}, with the exceptions of {\i orlah} and {\i kilayim}. One need not search the {\i pesukim} for contextual cues; one simply looks at the reality of what the {\i mitzva} demands. Does it engage you as a person? Then it applies everywhere. Does it engage the earth and its produce? Then it is restricted to the land of Israel.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Midrash Source\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} asks {\i minah hani milei} \u8212- what is the source? The {\i tanna\u8217'im} teach: {\i eileh hachukim vehamishpatim asher tishmerun la\u8217'asot ba\u8217'aretz} \u8212- these are the laws you shall observe to perform in the land. The {\i midrash} unpacks this: {\i eileh hamidrashot} \u8212- these are the {\i midrash}-derived teachings; {\i vehamishpatim eileh hadinim} \u8212- these are the civil laws; {\i asher tishmerun} \u8212- that you shall guard \u8212- {\i zo mishnah}, that refers to the learning and transmission of the laws; {\i va\u8217'asot} \u8212- to perform \u8212- {\i zu ma\u8217'aseh}, that is the actual practice. Then {\i ba\u8217'aretz} \u8212- in the land.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might think from this that all {\i mitzvot} apply only in the land \u8212- {\i yakhol kol hamitzvot kulan lo yehu nohagot ela ba\u8217'aretz}. The {\i gemara} therefore brings the continuation of the verse: {\i kol hayamim asher atem chayim al ha\u8217'adamah} \u8212- all the days that you live on the land. There is a certain irony in the interpretive move here: the phrase {\i al ha\u8217'adamah} ties the text to the land, yet {\i Chazal} read {\i kol hayamim} \u8212- all times \u8212- to indicate that the obligation is universal. After the text has spoken broadly and narrowly, one must determine what falls into each category.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Avodah Zarah Paradigm\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The operative teaching that follows is: {\i tzei u\u8217'lemad} \u8212- go and look at the opening subject of that passage, which is {\i abed tave\u8217'dun et kol hamekomot asher avdu sham hagoyim et eloheihem} \u8212- you shall destroy all the places where the nations worshipped their idols. {\i Mah avodat kokhavim meyuchedet she\u8217'hi chovat haguf} \u8212- just as destroying {\i avodah zarah} is a personal obligation that {\i noheget bein ba\u8217'aretz bein bechutz la\u8217'aretz} \u8212- applies in all places \u8212- so too everything that is a {\i chovat haguf} applies everywhere.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The choice of {\i avodah zarah} as the paradigm is itself ironic on multiple levels. First, the very phrase {\i kol hayamim asher atem chayim al ha\u8217'adamah} that anchors the teaching in the land is immediately followed by a {\i mitzva} that is at once personal and, in its fullest expression, fundamentally territorial. The obligation to destroy {\i avodah zarah} in {\i Eretz Yisrael} is heightened \u8212- {\i chayav attah lishrosh achareiha}, one must uproot it completely, pursuing it even beneath the surface. The {\i pesukim} speak clearly about clearing the land so that it becomes one\u8217's own national home. The intuition that if one is in India and encounters an idol one is obligated to smash it does not emerge naturally from reading those {\i pesukim} in context.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Second, read {\i Masechet Avodah Zarah} in its entirety, and one would never arrive at the conclusion that there is a positive {\i mitzva} to destroy {\i avodah zarah} \u8212- because the historical realities of Jewish life under Roman rule required the wholesale practical suspension of that obligation. Scholars and academics have commented extensively on this. And yet the {\i gemara} here uses {\i avodah zarah} destruction as the quintessential model of a {\i chovat haguf} that applies everywhere. The double irony \u8212- of a text anchored in {\i al ha\u8217'adamah} teaching universal personal obligation, instantiated through a {\i mitzva} whose real scope is territorial and whose practical observance was historically bracketed \u8212- is difficult to miss.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Chadash Dispute\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tanna Kamma Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i tanna kamma} holds that {\i chadash} applies only in {\i Eretz Yisrael}. To understand this position, one must look at the relevant {\i pasuk}: {\i lechem vekali vekarmel lo tochlu ad etzem hayom hazeh\u8230 ?chukat olam ledoroteichem bechol moshvoteichem} \u8212- you shall not eat bread or parched grain or fresh grain until that very day\u8230 ?it is an eternal statute for your generations in all your dwellings. The phrase {\i bechol moshvoteichem} \u8212- in all your dwellings \u8212- might seem to extend the prohibition everywhere. The {\i tanna kamma} reads it otherwise: {\i moshav le\u8217'achar yerusha veyeshiva mashma} \u8212- {\i moshav} means after you have already inherited and settled the land. Thus {\i bechol moshvoteichem} means once you are settled in the land, not wherever you happen to dwell. On this reading, the prohibition was not even operative during the first fourteen years after entry \u8212- the years of conquest and division \u8212- because the land was not yet {\i moshav}, not yet truly settled.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What makes this practically significant is the agricultural reality of {\i chutz la\u8217'aretz}. In {\i Eretz Yisrael} there is essentially one wheat season, and the harvest comes naturally around the time the {\i omer} is brought; one would have to make a special effort to create a problem of {\i chadash}. But in the diaspora \u8212- in France, Germany, and elsewhere \u8212- grain was planted in spring, took root after the {\i omer}, and came to market around {\i Rosh Hashana}. For those who hold that {\i chadash} applies in {\i chutz la\u8217'aretz}, that grain would be forbidden for the following six months, until the next {\i omer}. This creates an acute practical challenge that the {\i rishonim} had to confront head-on.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Eliezer Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Eliezer} reads {\i bechol moshvoteichem} in its plain sense: {\i bechol makom she\u8217'atem yoshvim} \u8212- wherever you dwell. On this reading, {\i chadash} applies outside {\i Eretz Yisrael} just as it does within it. The {\i gemara} establishes that {\i Rabbi Eliezer} is being {\i machmir} \u8212- his position is the stricter one \u8212- and that the {\i tanna kamma} is the more lenient position, restricting {\i chadash} to the land.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Chadash Dispute (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Nesachim & Central Altar\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Abaye} identifies the {\i tanna kamma} as {\i Rabbi Yishmael}, and frames the entire dispute around a broader hermeneutical principle. {\i Rabbi Yishmael} holds that whenever a verse combines both the language of {\i bi\u8217'ah} \u8212- entering the land \u8212- and {\i moshav} \u8212- dwelling \u8212- the combination restricts the obligation to {\i le\u8217'achar yerusha veyeshiva}, after the land has been inherited and settled. He derives this principle from the laws of {\i nesachim} \u8212- the wine, flour, and oil libations that accompany sacrifices. The relevant {\i pasuk} in {\i Shelach Lecha} reads {\i ki tavo\u8217'u el eretz moshvoteichem va\u8217'asitem isha l\u8217'Hashem} \u8212- when you come to the land of your dwellings and bring a fire-offering to God. {\i Rabbi Yishmael} holds that individuals did not bring {\i nesachim} on a {\i bamat yachid} in the {\i midbar}; only the communal {\i bamah gedolah} had {\i nesachim}. Since the verse says {\i lachem} \u8212- in the plural, indicating communal offering at the {\i bamah gedolah} \u8212- and that was already operative in the {\i midbar}, the {\i chiddush} of the {\i pasuk} must be that individual {\i nesachim} will now be brought \u8212- but only once the land is settled. Given this, {\i moshvoteichem} cannot mean \u8220"wherever you dwell\u8221" in the sense of individual locations throughout the land; it must mean \u8220"after your settlement is complete.\u8221" And this principle, says {\i Rabbi Yishmael}, generalizes: wherever the {\i Torah} combines {\i bi\u8217'ah} and {\i moshav}, it means only after the first fourteen years of conquest and settlement.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 R. Yishmael vs.\u160 ?Akiva\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Yishmael\u8217's Rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Yishmael\u8217's} general hermeneutical principle is thus: {\i kol makom shene\u8217'emar bo moshav eino ela le\u8217'achar yerusha veyeshiva} \u8212- wherever the {\i Torah} uses the language of dwelling in combination with entering the land, it means only after the land has been inherited and settled. When the {\i pasuk} on {\i chadash} says both {\i ki tavo\u8217'u} \u8212- when you come \u8212- and {\i bechol moshvoteichem}, the combination limits the prohibition to {\i Eretz Yisrael} after settlement, not to wherever Jews may dwell.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shabbat Objection\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Akiva} objects: {\i harei Shabbat shene\u8217'emar bah moshvot} \u8212- but {\i Shabbat} also uses the language of {\i moshvot}, and {\i Shabbat} applies everywhere, both inside and outside {\i Eretz Yisrael}. The {\i pasuk} reads {\i Shabbat hi l\u8217'Hashem bechol moshvoteichem} \u8212- it is a {\i Shabbat} to God in all your dwellings. Clearly, {\i moshvoteichem} there means wherever you dwell, not only in the land after settlement.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Response on Shabbat\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Yishmael} responds on two levels. First: {\i ana bi\u8217'ah u\u8217'moshav ka\u8217'amina} \u8212- my principle applies only when the verse contains both the language of entering and the language of dwelling. {\i Shabbat} mentions only {\i moshvot}, without {\i bi\u8217'ah}, and therefore does not fall under my rule. Second \u8212- and in any case \u8212- {\i Shabbat kal vachomer hi}: even if one were to argue from the word {\i moshvot} alone, we know {\i Shabbat} applies in {\i chutz la\u8217'aretz} from a {\i kal vachomer}: if {\i mitzvot} that are lighter apply outside the land, how much more so the weightier {\i mitzva} of {\i Shabbat}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} notes that {\i Rabbi Yishmael\u8217's} response is actually a compound reply \u8212- {\i chada ve\u8217'od ka\u8217'amar lei} \u8212- he is saying one thing and additionally another: first, that {\i Shabbat} is categorically irrelevant to his rule because it lacks the {\i bi\u8217'ah} language; second, that even if it were relevant, the {\i kal vachomer} would resolve it independently.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Chadash Halakha Today\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishna Conflict\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The question of how to rule practically on {\i chadash} outside {\i Eretz Yisrael} is complicated by a conflict between the {\i mishna} in our {\i masechta} and a {\i mishna} elsewhere. The {\i mishna} in {\i Kiddushin} presents the restriction of {\i chadash} to the land as a dispute between {\i Rabbi Eliezer} and the {\i tanna kamma}, suggesting we might simply rule like the {\i tanna kamma} \u8212- and therefore like {\i Rabbi Yishmael} \u8212- that {\i chadash} does not apply in {\i chutz la\u8217'aretz} even {\i derabbanan}. That would be the most straightforward and lenient resolution.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The problem is the last {\i mishna} in {\i Orlah}, which states unambiguously: {\i ha-chadash asur min ha-Torah bechol makom} \u8212- new grain is forbidden by {\i Torah} law everywhere. No dispute, no qualification. A number of {\i rishonim} attempt to rule like the {\i tanna kamma} in {\i Kiddushin} on the grounds that the position of {\i Rabbi Eliezer} there is a {\i da\u8217'as yachid}. Others argue that the {\i stam mishna} in {\i Orlah} \u8212- situated in the primary discussion of these issues \u8212- must be dispositive and cannot be overridden by a {\i da\u8217'as yachid} in {\i Kiddushin}, especially given the principle of {\i stam ve\u8217'achar kach machlokes}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafot on Chadash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot}, at the bottom of 36b, does not resolve the dispute between the two {\i mishnayot} but states plainly: {\i venira de-chadash noheig bizman hazeh} \u8212- it appears that the prohibition of {\i chadash} applies in our times, meaning in the diaspora. He does not elaborate on whether this follows {\i Rabbi Eliezer} or is derived from the {\i mishna} in {\i Orlah}. Instead, he proceeds directly to practical guidance.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rov & Safek Sefeika\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} writes: {\i ul\u8217'kach yesh lehizaher she\u8217'im adam yode\u8217'a bevadai she-se\u8217'orim nizre\u8217'u achar zman hakravas ha-omer} \u8212- one should be careful that if one knows with certainty that grain was planted after the {\i omer} was brought, i.e., after the sixteenth of {\i Nissan}, one should not eat from it. However, {\i u\u8217'missafek ein le\u8217'asur kol ha-se\u8217'orim} \u8212- in cases of doubt, one should not prohibit all grain, {\i keivan d\u8217'rov se\u8217'orim nizre\u8217'u kodem tet-zayin b\u8217'Nisan} \u8212- since the majority of grain was planted before the sixteenth of {\i Nissan}, and one may therefore follow the {\i rov}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} mentions {\i se\u8217'orim} \u8212- barley \u8212- specifically, likely because the most pressing practical challenge involved beer, which was pervasive in the diaspora communities and from which people could not easily abstain. The {\i issur} of {\i chadash} does not apply to benefit ({\i hana\u8217'ah}), but one may not eat it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 There is a certain irony in the structure of this {\i hetter}. {\i Chadash} is an {\i issur d\u8217'oraita}, and normally a {\i safek} in a {\i d\u8217'oraita} prohibition would not suffice to permit; here it is an assertion of {\i rov} that carries the weight. One must ask whether that assertion is truly accurate and whether the {\i rov} is {\i mevurrar} \u8212- established \u8212- or relies on an approximation. In practice, it seems that many {\i rishonim} began with the reality that communities could not avoid eating the grain, and worked backward to find justification.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Beyond {\i rov}, some {\i rishonim} developed a {\i safek sefeika}: perhaps the grain came from last year\u8217's supply, and even if from this year, perhaps it took root before the {\i omer}. Though one might argue that these two doubts collapse into a single question \u8212- was it {\i yashan} or {\i chadash} \u8212- the {\i rishonim} maintained this framework as a further basis for leniency in combination with other factors.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Additional arguments proposed by various {\i rishonim} include: ruling entirely like the {\i tanna kamma} that {\i chadash} does not apply in {\i chutz la\u8217'aretz}; arguing that {\i chadash} grown by non-Jews is exempt; and the position of the {\i Aruch Hashulchan} that grain of a second wheat season \u8212- which could never be fit for the {\i omer} \u8212- should never have been subject to the {\i chadash} prohibition in the first place, limiting the concern to the primary wheat season only. Against the exemption for non-Jewish-owned grain, the {\i Yerushalmi} itself closes the door: it explains why {\i challah} is not listed alongside {\i chadash} in the {\i mishna} \u8212- because {\i challah} does not apply to grain owned by non-Jews \u8212- implying that {\i chadash} as listed does apply to grain owned by non-Jews.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Modern Context\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The practical landscape has changed significantly in the modern era. For centuries, communities maintained the permissive practice because there was simply no way to know with certainty the origin of grain \u8212- whether it had taken root before or after the {\i omer}. The arguments from {\i rov} and {\i safek sefeika} were, at least in part, post-hoc rationalizations of a practice driven by practical necessity.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Today, however, detailed information is available about grain lots and their planting schedules. It is possible to determine, based on barcodes or lot information, precisely when a given batch of grain took root and whether it qualifies as {\i yashan} or {\i chadash}. The availability of this information raises serious {\i halachic} questions about whether one may continue to rely on {\i rov} or {\i safek sefeika} when the facts are in principle ascertainable. The most straightforward {\i halachic} resolution \u8212- and the one that avoids all of these complexities \u8212- would be to rule definitively like the {\i tanna kamma} against {\i Rabbi Eliezer}, holding that {\i chadash} does not apply in {\i chutz la\u8217'aretz} even {\i derabbanan}. Whether the {\i stam mishna} in {\i Orlah} forecloses that option remains the underlying difficulty.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Orlah & Wine\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Orlah Problem\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} raises an equally pressing question about {\i orlah} in the diaspora. Since {\i orlah} applies in {\i chutz la\u8217'aretz} as a {\i halacha le-Moshe mi-Sinai}, and since virtually all vineyards propagate new vines by attaching shoots to existing root systems \u8212- a process that, if it constitutes a new planting, would make the new growth subject to the three-year {\i orlah} prohibition \u8212- how has it been historically permissible to drink wine from diaspora vineyards? {\i Tosafot} notes that one cannot rely on {\i rov} here as one might in other cases, because the majority of vines are indeed propagated this way.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mavrich Solution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} cites {\i Rabbeinu Shimshon} \u8212- the {\i Rash mi-Shantz} \u8212- who investigated the matter by actually speaking with farmers and viticulturists. What he found was that the process being used was not {\i markiv} \u8212- true grafting, where a shoot is severed and joined to a different root \u8212- but rather {\i mavrich} \u8212- layering, where a shoot is bent underground and trained to emerge elsewhere, while still remaining physically attached and connected to the original vine. Crucially, {\i she\u8217'einam chotchim ha-zmorah min ha-sadan} \u8212- they never actually sever the shoot from the trunk. It remains drawing nourishment from the old vine throughout.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The legal implication is decisive: {\i im kein hu yonek od min ha-zkeinah} \u8212- since it is still drawing from the old vine, it retains the legal status of the old plant. A shoot that is not severed is not a new planting; {\i orlah} does not commence.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yerushalmi Source\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Yerushalmi} provides the source for this distinction: {\i ha-mavrich bizman she-yonek min ha-zkeinah patur min ha-orlah} \u8212- when the layered shoot still draws from the old vine, it is exempt from {\i orlah}. Once it is severed and no longer drawing, it becomes subject to the full {\i orlah} counting.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Signs of Attachment\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Yerushalmi} also provides a physical sign by which one may determine whether the layered vine remains attached. {\i Keshe-ha-zmorah yoneket min ha-zkeinah harei hem notim letzad acher} \u8212- when the shoot is still drawing from the old vine, the leaves turn away from it; {\i u-cheshe-posek harei hem notim letzad ha-zkeinah} \u8212- once it is severed and stops drawing, the leaves turn back toward the old vine. The mnemonic offered is charming: {\i de-hai man de-achil me-chavrei ba\u8217'ei le-istakulei bei} \u8212- one who eats from another person\u8217's table is a little embarrassed to look directly at them. So too, the shoot that is still feeding off the old vine averts its leaves. Whether this botanical observation fully maps onto modern viticulture is another question, but that is the tradition as {\i Tosafot} records it.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Nesachim Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Yishmael View\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now clarifies the foundational disagreement between {\i Rabbi Yishmael} and {\i Rabbi Akiva} that drives the {\i chadash} dispute. {\i Rabbi Yishmael savar lo keruvu nesachim ba-midbar} \u8212- {\i Rabbi Yishmael} holds that individuals did not bring {\i nesachim} on a {\i bamat yachid} in the {\i midbar}. Only the communal {\i bamah gedolah} received {\i nesachim} during the wilderness period.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yishmael\u8217's Consequence\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Given this starting point, the {\i chiddush} of the {\i pasuk} in {\i Shelach Lecha} \u8212- {\i ki tavo\u8217'u el eretz moshvoteichem} \u8212- is that upon entering the land, individuals will for the first time bring {\i nesachim} on their individual {\i bamot}. Since the {\i pasuk} is introducing this novel permission or obligation, {\i moshvoteichem} cannot mean \u8220"wherever you are currently located,\u8221" because individuals were not bringing {\i nesachim} anywhere; the {\i pasuk} must mean after you have settled and conquered the land \u8212- {\i le\u8217'achar yerusha veyishiva}. The verse does not broaden the geographic scope of {\i nesachim}; it defers their onset until after settlement is complete.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Akiva View\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Akiva savar keruvu nesachim ba-midbar} \u8212- {\i Rabbi Akiva} holds the opposite: individuals already brought {\i nesachim} on a {\i bamat yachid} in the {\i midbar}. The communal obligation was already operative in the wilderness.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Akiva\u8217's Consequence\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If {\i nesachim} were already being brought individually in the {\i midbar}, the verse {\i ki tavo\u8217'u el eretz moshvoteichem} cannot be introducing something new \u8212- individuals were already bringing {\i nesachim}. The {\i chiddush} must therefore be something else: you will continue to bring {\i nesachim} even on an individual {\i bamat yachid} after you enter the land. And since it is telling you that individual {\i bamot} remain valid in the land, {\i moshvoteichem} must mean {\i bechol makom she\u8217'atem yoshvim} \u8212- wherever you dwell. For {\i Rabbi Akiva}, the word {\i moshvoteichem} naturally carries the broader geographic meaning, and it is from this foundation that he argues against {\i Rabbi Yishmael\u8217's} restrictive reading \u8212- including, by extension, on the question of {\i chadash}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Kiddushin 38\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Chadash Overview\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Winter vs.\u160 ?Spring Wheat\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Today\u8217's {\i daf} is 38, continuing the discussion of {\i mitzvot hatluyot ba\u8217'aretz} and picking up where we left off on the topic of {\i chadash}. By way of introduction, it is worth clarifying the practical application of {\i chadash} to the wheat situation in the United States. Winter wheat is planted in the fall, grows through the winter, and begins to be harvested around spring. Because it takes root over the winter \u8212- before the {\i omer} is brought \u8212- it never presents a {\i chadash} problem: by the time it is ready to come to market, the {\i omer} has already been brought, and the {\i omer} is effective from the moment the grain has taken root. The problem arises specifically with spring wheat, which is planted in the spring after the {\i omer} is brought, takes root after the {\i omer} is brought, and comes to market around {\i Rosh Hashanah}. That spring wheat remains {\i asur} until the following {\i pesach}, until the next {\i omer} is brought.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 As for the practical proportion: approximately 70 to 80 percent of wheat grown in the United States is winter wheat, so the argument that the {\i rov} is {\i yashan} is correct. However, different wheat varieties have different qualities \u8212- gluten content and rising characteristics differ \u8212- and so it is not always possible to assume that a given flour is entirely from one type. Some all-purpose flours may be a blend. Pizza dough, by contrast, is almost invariably winter wheat, chosen specifically for its rising qualities, which is why pizza establishments can readily rely on {\i yashan}. The winter wheat is what gets one out of the {\i chadash} problem.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Contradiction Issue\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might ask why no one seems to address the apparent contradiction between all these {\i sugyot} and the verse in {\i Kohelet}, which states {\i ein chadash} \u8212- there is nothing new. The response offered is simply: {\i ain chadash} \u8212- there you go.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mixing Chadash/Yashan\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A practical question arises about mixing: if flour that is {\i chadash} is blended with {\i yashan} flour by a producer, can one rely on {\i bitul}? The answer is that one cannot simply rely on {\i bitul} here, because {\i chadash} is a {\i davar sheyesh lo matirin} \u8212- something that will eventually become permitted on its own, namely after the next {\i omer} is brought. A {\i ta\u8217'arovet} of {\i chadash} with {\i yashan} therefore does not permit the mixture. One might try to argue along the lines of {\i kol deparish meruba parish} \u8212- treating whatever comes to hand as presumptively from the majority \u8212- but the matter is complicated.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rabbi Yishmael & Binyan\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tanna Contradiction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} picks up on 37b. By {\i chadash} the {\i pasuk} states: {\i lechem vekali vekarmel lo tochlu ad etzem hayom hazeh ad havi\u8217'achem es korban elokeichem, chukas olam ledoroseichem bechol moshavoseichem} \u8212- bread, parched grain, and fresh ears you shall not eat until this very day, until you bring the offering of your God, an eternal statute throughout your generations in all your dwelling places. The question is what {\i bechol moshavoseichem} means. Does it mean wherever you dwell \u8212- even in {\i chutz la\u8217'aretz} \u8212- as Rabbi Eliezer holds? Or does it mean after you have settled the land, as Rabbi Yishmael holds regarding {\i nesachim}, linking {\i moshavoseichem} to the concept of having taken possession and settled?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Abaye} notes that a {\i tanna} from the school of Rabbi Yishmael contradicts another {\i tanna} from that same school. The teaching he introduces runs as follows: {\i ho\u8217'il vene\u8217'emru biyot batorah stam} \u8212- since the {\i Torah} frequently speaks of {\i ki savo el ha\u8217'aretz} without further specification \u8212- {\i ufaret lecha hakatuv be\u8217'achat mehen le\u8217'achar yerusha veyeshiva} \u8212- and in one place it specifies that the obligation applies only after taking possession and settling \u8212- {\i af kol le\u8217'achar yerusha veyeshiva} \u8212- we derive that all instances of {\i ki savo}, even without the added phrase {\i veyarashta veyashavta}, mean after you have settled the land. In other words, in one place the {\i Torah} explicitly links entry to settlement, and that serves as a paradigm for every other usage of {\i ki savo} throughout the {\i Torah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Binyan Av Hermeneutic\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The hermeneutical mechanism here is {\i binyan av}: one clear case in the {\i Torah} \u8212- where entry is explicitly conditioned on {\i yerusha veyeshiva}, possession and settlement \u8212- serves as a model for all other cases, establishing that {\i ki savo} never means the mere act of crossing the border but always implies the completed process of conquest and settlement after the fourteen-year period. The specific examples are the {\i pasuk} by the king \u8212- {\i ki savo el ha\u8217'aretz veyarashta veyashavta bah} \u8212- and the {\i pasuk} by {\i bikkurim} \u8212- similarly, {\i ki savo el ha\u8217'aretz veyarashta veyashavta}, followed by {\i velakachta mereishit kol pri ha\u8217'adamah}. Both specify that the relevant obligation applies only after taking possession and settling.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two Verses Together\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The opposing {\i tanna} from the school of Rabbi Yishmael disagrees. His argument is that you cannot use this as a {\i binyan av} because the condition {\i yerusha veyeshiva} is spelled out explicitly in two separate {\i pesukim} \u8212- by the king and by {\i bikkurim} \u8212- and the rule is that {\i shnei ketuvim haba\u8217'im ke\u8217'echad ein melamdin}: when the {\i Torah} makes two cases explicit, those two cases are to be understood as exceptions, not as paradigms from which to generalize. According to this view, the default meaning of {\i ki savo} is immediate entry, and both the king and {\i bikkurim} are spelled out precisely because they would otherwise have been understood as applying immediately.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The first {\i tanna} responds with {\i tzrichi} \u8212- both {\i pesukim} are necessary and neither is superfluous. He argues that had the {\i Torah} written only the case of the king without {\i bikkurim}, one might have concluded that while the general rule requires settlement, {\i bikkurim} is an exception \u8212- since the fruits of the land are immediately available upon entry, one might have thought {\i bikkurim} should apply at once. Therefore the {\i Torah} had to clarify that {\i bikkurim} too follows the general rule and applies only after settlement. And had the {\i Torah} written only {\i bikkurim} without the king, one might have assumed that a king is needed from the outset \u8212- since waging the wars of conquest requires a king \u8212- and therefore the obligation to appoint a king applies immediately. Therefore the {\i Torah} had to clarify that even appointing a king is only after settlement. Each case is necessary, but they are not exceptions; they are confirmations that the rule of settlement applies even in contexts where one might have thought otherwise.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Challah Comparison\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i tanna} who holds that the two explicit {\i pesukim} create exceptions then pushes back further. He says: the {\i Torah} could have written only the case of the king, and I could have inferred {\i bikkurim} on my own \u8212- {\i umah Melech d\u8217'lichbosh acharei yerusha veyeshiva, Bikkurim lo kol shekein} \u8212- if even appointing a king, who is normally needed to wage war, is only after settlement, how much more so {\i bikkurim}, which is simply bringing the first fruits. The fact that {\i bikkurim} was spelled out again signals that it is an exception, confirming that the general rule of {\i ki savo} means immediately upon entry.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The first {\i tanna} replies that this inference fails because of {\i challah}. By {\i challah}, the {\i Torah} uses the word {\i bevo\u8217'achem} \u8212- when you come \u8212- and the {\i Gemara} elsewhere derives that {\i challah} applies immediately upon entry into the land, without waiting for settlement. Since {\i bikkurim} and {\i challah} are closely analogous \u8212- both are considered a form of {\i terumah}, both are {\i matanot} given to the {\i kohen}, and both are called {\i reishit} \u8212- I would have naturally equated them and concluded that {\i bikkurim}, like {\i challah}, applies immediately. Therefore the {\i Torah} had to specify separately that {\i bikkurim} too requires prior settlement, and this specification is not to mark {\i bikkurim} as an exception but to prevent me from placing it in the category of {\i challah}. The general rule remains that {\i ki savo} means settlement, and {\i bikkurim} is clarified as part of that rule, not as a departure from it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What remains relevant to us regarding {\i chadash} is that in both approaches, {\i moshavoseichem} means after you have settled \u8212- as opposed to the entirely different reading that {\i moshavoseichem} means wherever you dwell, which would entail that {\i chadash} applies immediately in {\i chutz la\u8217'aretz}. Regardless of which hermeneutical approach one takes within the debate above, the question of whether {\i chadash} applies outside the land depends on which meaning of {\i moshavoseichem} one adopts.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Moshvoteichem Meaning\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shabbat Application\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now transitions: {\i hashta de\u8217'amarta chovat haguf noheget bein ba\u8217'aretz bein bechutz la\u8217'aretz} \u8212- now that we have established that personal obligations apply in {\i chutz la\u8217'aretz} \u8212- {\i moshvoteichem dichtav rachmana gabei Shabbat lama li?} Why does the {\i Torah} say {\i bechol moshvoteichem} by {\i Shabbat}? The {\i pasuk} reads {\i Shabbat hi l\u8217'Hashem bechol moshvoseichem}. If {\i moshvoseichem} normally signifies after you\u8217've settled the land, then writing it by {\i Shabbat} is completely inappropriate \u8212- {\i Shabbat} obviously cannot be a condition on settlement. And if it means wherever you dwell, that too is obvious regarding {\i Shabbat}, since everyone knows it applies everywhere.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} answers: {\i itzterich} \u8212- it is necessary. {\i Salka da\u8217'atach amina} \u8212- one might have thought, since {\i Shabbat} is written in {\i Parshat Emor} in the same passage as the other {\i mo\u8217'adim}, that perhaps it would require a process of sanctification parallel to the other festivals, which depend on {\i kiddush hachodesh} \u8212- the declaration of the new month \u8212- to determine when they occur. Perhaps {\i Shabbat} would similarly require some form of declaration to make it holy. {\i Ka mashma lan} \u8212- the {\i Torah} therefore teaches, through the phrase {\i bechol moshvoseichem}, that {\i Shabbat} has no dependence on what takes place in the land of Israel. It applies automatically, without any aspect of sanctification \u8212- emphasizing that it is entirely independent of any centralized process. This is why even though it is obvious that {\i Shabbat} applies everywhere, the {\i Torah} still had to write {\i bechol moshvoseichem}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chelev Vedam\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} continues to other {\i mitzvot} where the {\i Torah} uses the phrase {\i bechol moshvoseichem}, examining in each case why that phrase is necessary. By {\i chelev vedam} \u8212- fat and blood \u8212- the {\i Torah} says {\i bechol moshvoseichem}. Why is this necessary? The prohibition on {\i chelev vedam} is clearly not land-based; of course it applies everywhere. The {\i Gemara} answers: {\i salka da\u8217'atach amina} \u8212- since the prohibition of {\i chelev vedam} appears in the context of {\i korbanot}, one might have thought that {\i chelev} and {\i dam} are only forbidden because they belong on the {\i mizbe\u8217'ach}, and that therefore the prohibition would apply only when there are {\i korbanot}. And as for the {\i chelev} of a {\i chaya} \u8212- a non-domesticated animal \u8212- which cannot be sacrificed on the {\i mizbe\u8217'ach}, one might have thought it is entirely permitted. {\i Ka mashma lan} \u8212- the {\i Torah} therefore teaches through {\i bechol moshvoseichem} that the prohibition applies in all places and at all times, entirely independent of the context of the {\i Mikdash}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Matzah Umaror\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Similarly, by {\i matzah umaror} the {\i Torah} states {\i bechol moshvoseichem tochelu matzot}. Why is this necessary? {\i Salka da\u8217'atach amina} \u8212- the {\i pasuk} itself establishes {\i matzah} and {\i maror} in the context of the {\i korban pesach} \u8212- {\i al matzot umerorim yochelohu} \u8212- and one might have concluded that {\i matzah} and {\i maror} are obligatory only when the {\i Pesach} offering is brought, and not otherwise. {\i Ka mashma lan} \u8212- the phrase {\i bechol moshvoseichem} teaches that {\i matzah} applies even in exile, even after the {\i churban Beis HaMikdash}, even when one is not in the land of Israel and even when there is no {\i korban pesach}. This is of course directly relevant to our own practice. The irony is not lost that {\i maror} in fact does not apply today independently of the {\i korban pesach}, and the {\i Gemara} elsewhere derives the continuing obligation of {\i matzah} from a separate {\i pasuk}. But the principle established by {\i bechol moshvoseichem} is that {\i matzah} is not contingent on the context of the {\i Mikdash}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tefillin & Peter Chamor\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 By {\i tefillin} and {\i peter chamor}, the {\i Torah} uses the phrase {\i ki savo el ha\u8217'aretz} \u8212- when you come to the land \u8212- followed by the instruction regarding the firstborn donkey and the commandment of {\i tefillin}. These {\i mitzvot} are plainly not limited to coming into the land. So why does the {\i Torah} phrase them in terms of entry? The {\i Gemara} answers that we need the phrase for what the school of Rabbi Yishmael teaches: {\i asei mitzvah zo she\u8217'bishvilah tikanes la\u8217'aretz} \u8212- perform this {\i mitzvah}, for in its merit you will enter the land. The reference to entry is not to say that {\i tefillin} and {\i peter chamor} apply only after entry; rather, it is a reversal of the usual dependency. These {\i mitzvot} are the merit by virtue of which one merits entry into the land.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Chadash in Israel\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Omer Necessity\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now returns to {\i chadash} directly. {\i Bishlama lema\u8217'an de\u8217'amar moshav kol makom she\u8217'atem yoshvim mashma} \u8212- according to the view that {\i moshav} means wherever you dwell, and {\i chadash} therefore applies in {\i chutz la\u8217'aretz} like Rabbi Eliezer holds, one can understand the {\i pasuk} in {\i Yehoshua}: {\i vayochlu me\u8217'avur ha\u8217'aretz mimocharat haPesach} \u8212- they ate from the produce of the land from the day following {\i Pesach}. On the reading that {\i chadash} applies everywhere, they could not eat the new grain of the land until the {\i omer} was brought \u8212- {\i mei\u8217'ikara lo achol} \u8212- and only after that could they eat. {\i Alma akrivu omer ve\u8217'hadar achol} \u8212- this implies the {\i omer} was in fact brought and the eating followed. According to {\i Chazal}, something happened on the sixteenth of {\i Nissan} \u8212- evidently the {\i omer} was brought in the traveling {\i Mishkan} \u8212- and that permitted eating from the new grain. This reading coheres with the position that {\i chadash} applies in {\i chutz la\u8217'aretz} and applies in Israel immediately upon entry, requiring the {\i omer} before any new grain may be consumed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Settlement Timing\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But consider the alternative: {\i ela lema\u8217'an de\u8217'amar le\u8217'achar yerusha veyeshiva} \u8212- according to the view that {\i bechol moshvoseichem} by {\i chadash} means after you\u8217've settled the land. In that case, when {\i Yehoshua} and the Jewish people had just entered the land, the {\i issur chadash} had not yet taken effect. So {\i neichlu le\u8217'altar} \u8212- why did they not simply eat the new grain immediately upon crossing the Jordan? The {\i Gemara} answers: {\i lav mitzterichi} \u8212- they simply did not need it. It was coincidence that they waited until the sixteenth of {\i Nissan}, and this had nothing to do with the {\i omer}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Why did they not need the grain? Because {\i dichtiv uvnei Yisrael achlu es haman arbaim shana ad bo\u8217'am el eretz noshevet} \u8212- they ate the {\i man} for forty years until they came to the settled land. But a problem arises: one {\i pasuk} says they ate the {\i man} until they came {\i el eretz noshevet} \u8212- implying they ate it until after they entered \u8212- while another says they ate it {\i ad bo\u8217'am el ketzei eretz Canaan} \u8212- until they reached the edge of the land of {\i Canaan}, suggesting they stopped before entering. {\i Ha keitzad} \u8212- how do we reconcile these?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} resolves: {\i beshiva be\u8217'Adar meis Moshe} \u8212- Moshe died on the seventh of {\i Adar}. The {\i man} stopped falling when Moshe died, when the people were at the cusp of entering. But they continued eating from that last supply of {\i man} \u8212- one day\u8217's portion, which sustained them \u8212- for the following thirty-seven days until they actually entered the land. Both {\i pesukim} are thus true: the {\i man} stopped falling at the edge of {\i Canaan}, and yet they were still eating it when they came {\i el eretz noshevet}. This is the resolution to the apparent contradiction.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The relevance for our question is as follows: if {\i chadash} applies {\i bechol moshvoseichem} everywhere and immediately \u8212- including in the land of Israel from the moment of entry \u8212- then one must explain why they did not eat the new grain immediately upon crossing. The answer is that they still had {\i man} and did not need it. The {\i omer} was then brought on the sixteenth, and only after that did they eat from the new grain. But if {\i moshavoseichem} means only after settlement, they were already permitted to eat the new grain upon entry, and they simply happened not to need it until the sixteenth because they were still sustained by the {\i man}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mahn Narrative\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A separate {\i braisa} addresses the chronology of the {\i man} itself. {\i Uvnei Yisrael achlu es haman arbaim shana} \u8212- they ate the {\i man} for forty years. Did they really eat it for a full forty years? In fact it was forty years minus thirty days, since the {\i man} did not begin to fall on the day they left Egypt but only a month later. How then can the {\i Torah} say forty years? {\i Ela lomar lach} \u8212- the {\i Torah} is telling us that the cakes they had taken out of Egypt \u8212- {\i ugah shehotziu miMitzrayim ta\u8217'amu bahem ta\u8217'am man} \u8212- tasted like the {\i man}. So it was as if they had been eating the {\i man} for forty years, because even in the first month, before the {\i man} fell, the cakes they were eating had the taste of {\i man}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chronology Timeline\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Another {\i braisa} establishes the precise chronology. {\i Beshiva be\u8217'Adar meis Moshe, beshiva be\u8217'Adar nolad} \u8212- Moshe was born and died on the seventh of {\i Adar}. How do we know he died on the seventh of {\i Adar}? From the {\i pasuk} stating that Moshe died in the plains of {\i Moav}, combined with the {\i pasuk} that the people mourned him for thirty days in the plains of {\i Moav}. Then the book of {\i Yehoshua} opens with God commanding Yehoshua following Moshe\u8217's death: {\i moshe avdi meis ve\u8217'atah kum avor} \u8212- Moshe my servant has died, now arise and cross. And soon after: {\i ivru bekerev hamachane vetzivu es ha\u8217'am \u8212- hachinu lachem tzeidah ki be\u8217'od shlosha yamim ta\u8217'avru es hayardein} \u8212- prepare yourselves provisions, for in three days you will cross the Jordan. And then: {\i veha\u8217'am alu min hayardein be\u8217'asor lachodesh harishon} \u8212- the people came up from the Jordan on the tenth of the first month. Working backwards from the tenth of {\i Nissan}: subtract thirty days of mourning and three days of preparation, and one arrives at the seventh of {\i Adar}. That is how we know Moshe died on the seventh of {\i Adar}. And how do we know he was born on that date? Because Moshe said {\i ben me\u8217'ah ve\u8217'esrim shana anochi hayom} \u8212- I am one hundred and twenty years old today \u8212- and the principle is that the righteous live out the full measure of their years, filling out the year entirely, as the {\i pasuk} states {\i et mispar yamecha amaleh} \u8212- the number of your days I will fill. Therefore Moshe died on the same day he was born.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Three Mitzvot Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Shimon View\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tanya Rabbi Shimon ben Yochai omer} \u8212- Rabbi Shimon bar Yochai teaches: {\i shalosh mitzvot nitztavu Yisrael bichnisatan la\u8217'aretz venoheget bein ba\u8217'aretz bein b\u8217'chutz la\u8217'aretz} \u8212- three {\i mitzvot} were commanded to Israel upon their entry into the land, and yet they apply both in the land and outside the land. Those three are {\i chadash}, {\i orlah}, and {\i kilayim}. They began with entry into the land, but once they began to apply, they apply everywhere \u8212- including {\i chutz la\u8217'aretz}. He thus agrees with Rabbi Eliezer that {\i chadash} applies in {\i chutz la\u8217'aretz}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 He then reasons as follows: {\i umah chadash she\u8217'ein issuro issur olam ve\u8217'ein issuro issur hana\u8217'ah veyesh heter l\u8217'issuro noheg bein ba\u8217'aretz bein b\u8217'chutz la\u8217'aretz} \u8212- {\i chadash} is the weakest of these three prohibitions. Why? Three reasons. First, {\i ein issuro issur olam} \u8212- the prohibition is not permanent; the grain that begins as {\i chadash} becomes {\i mutar} once the {\i omer} is brought. Second, {\i ein issuro issur hana\u8217'ah} \u8212- {\i chadash} is not forbidden in {\i hana\u8217'ah}; you may not eat it but you may derive benefit from it. Third, {\i veyesh heter l\u8217'issuro} \u8212- it has a mechanism of permission: not only does the {\i issur} end with time, but if the {\i omer} is brought on the sixteenth it becomes {\i mutar} even on the sixteenth itself \u8212- in effect, half a day early. Rashi understands {\i yesh heter l\u8217'issuro} as essentially a stronger formulation of the first point \u8212- that the {\i omer} creates a {\i heter} even within the {\i issur} period. {\i Tosafot} offers several alternative explanations. In any case, even with all these leniencies, {\i chadash} applies in {\i chutz la\u8217'aretz}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Kilayim she\u8217'issuran issur olam} \u8212- {\i kilayim}, by contrast, is always forbidden; it does not become permitted with time. {\i Ve\u8217'issuran issur hana\u8217'ah} \u8212- it is forbidden even in {\i hana\u8217'ah}. {\i Ve\u8217'ein heter l\u8217'issuran} \u8212- there is no mechanism of permission. {\i Eino din she\u8217'yanogu bein ba\u8217'aretz bein b\u8217'chutz la\u8217'aretz} \u8212- how much more so should {\i kilayim} apply in {\i chutz la\u8217'aretz}. And {\i orlah} has two of these three stringencies \u8212- it is not {\i issur olam} in the same sense, since while the fruit of those first three years remains permanently forbidden, the tree itself eventually yields permitted fruit \u8212- and it too therefore applies in {\i chutz la\u8217'aretz}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Importantly, Rabbi Shimon bar Yochai appears to be saying not merely that {\i chadash}, {\i orlah}, and {\i kilayim} apply rabbinically in {\i chutz la\u8217'aretz}, but that they apply {\i de\u8217'oraita}. This is a significant point and stands in contrast to the positions that will emerge from the {\i Mishnah} later.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} raises a pointed objection from this line of reasoning. If {\i chadash} applies {\i de\u8217'oraita} in {\i chutz la\u8217'aretz} and is demonstrably the weakest of these three prohibitions, then by a straightforward {\i kal vachomer} all the other {\i mitzvot hatluyot ba\u8217'aretz} \u8212- {\i tevel}, {\i terumah}, {\i ma\u8217'aser}, and so on \u8212- should also apply {\i de\u8217'oraita} outside the land. {\i Tosafot} answers that the {\i kal vachomer} can be challenged: {\i chadash} is unique in that it cannot be annulled through a {\i she\u8217'eilah} \u8212- a {\i halakhic} inquiry dissolving the status \u8212- whereas {\i terumah} and {\i ma\u8217'aser} can in principle be reversed through such inquiry, since they acquire their status through a human act of designation. But the question is in some ways more striking than any particular answer: if one takes Rabbi Shimon bar Yochai\u8217's position fully seriously, it opens the door to an expansive view of which agricultural {\i mitzvot} apply everywhere.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Elazar View\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Elazar the son of Rabbi Shimon takes the opposing position: {\i kol mitzvah shenitztavu Yisrael kodem knisatam la\u8217'aretz noheget bein ba\u8217'aretz bein b\u8217'chutz la\u8217'aretz} \u8212- any {\i mitzvah} that applied before they entered the land is not land-dependent and therefore applies outside the land as well. But {\i le\u8217'achar knisatam la\u8217'aretz} \u8212- anything that applied only upon entering the land, such as {\i chadash}, {\i orlah}, and {\i kilayim} \u8212- {\i einah noheget ela ba\u8217'aretz} \u8212- applies only in the land of Israel. He thus disagrees with his father and takes the position that these three are categorically limited to the land of Israel, since they are precisely the type of obligations that came into effect only with entry and are fundamentally land-dependent.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Exceptions: Loans & Slav\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Elazar does, however, identify two exceptions: {\i chutz min hashmattat kesafim veshiluach avadim} \u8212- the annulment of loans in the {\i shemittah} year and the freeing of slaves in the {\i yovel} \u8212- {\i she\u8217'af al pi shenitztavu le\u8217'achar knisatam la\u8217'aretz, noheget bein ba\u8217'aretz bein b\u8217'chutz la\u8217'aretz} \u8212- even though these were commanded with reference to entry into the land, they apply both inside and outside. The question, which the {\i Gemara} will now address, is: why are these two specifically carved out as exceptions? If they are personal obligations with no inherent connection to the soil, why would it even occur to anyone to limit them to the land?\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Shemittah & Release\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Loans Dependency\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} asks: {\i hashmattat kesafim chovat haguf hi} \u8212- annulling loans is a personal obligation, so of course it applies outside the land. Why does Rabbi Elazar need to spell this out as an exception? The {\i Gemara} answers: {\i lo nitzrecha ela l\u8217'chidetanya} \u8212- the ruling is necessary because of what we learn in a {\i braisa}. {\i Rabbi omer}: {\i v\u8217'zeh devar hashemittah shamot} \u8212- this is the matter of {\i shemittah}, release \u8212- {\i bishtei shemittot hakatuv medaber} \u8212- the {\i Torah} here is speaking of two types of {\i shemittah}. {\i Achat shemitat karka ve\u8217'achat shemitat kesafim} \u8212- one is the release of the land from agricultural labor, and the other is the cancellation of loans. The principle established is: {\i bizman she\u8217'atah meshamet karka, atah meshamet kesafim} \u8212- when {\i shemitat karka} applies, {\i shemitat kesafim} also applies. {\i Bizman she\u8217'ei atah meshamet karka, ein atah meshamet kesafim} \u8212- when {\i shemitat karka} does not apply, neither does {\i shemitat kesafim}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 From this dependency, one might have reasoned: {\i bemakom she\u8217'atah meshamet karka atah meshamet kesafim uvemakom she\u8217'ein atah meshamet karka ein atah meshamet kesafim} \u8212- perhaps the annulment of loans applies only where {\i shemitat karka} is geographically practiced, namely in the land of Israel. Therefore it would be {\i mutar} to maintain loans in {\i chutz la\u8217'aretz} even during a {\i shemittah} year, since {\i shemitat karka} does not operate there. This is not an unreasonable reading; indeed, a number of {\i Rishonim} held that {\i shemitat kesafim} outside the land is not even {\i de\u8217'rabbanan} in a full sense but merely a {\i minhag chasidut}, and historically people in the Rishonic period sometimes barely bothered with writing a {\i pruzbul}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Against this, the {\i Gemara} answers: {\i talmud lomar ki kara shemittah l\u8217'Hashem mikol makom} \u8212- the {\i Torah} teaches that {\i shemittah} is declared for God from every place. The word {\i mikol makom} signifies that the cancellation of loans operates regardless of geographic location \u8212- even in {\i chutz la\u8217'aretz} where {\i shemitat karka} does not apply. What {\i shemitat kesafim} does require is that {\i shemitat karka} is being practiced in Israel; the dependency is temporal, not geographic. When {\i shemittah} is operative in Israel, the annulment of loans applies everywhere. When {\i shemittah} is not operative in Israel \u8212- for instance, because {\i yovel} is not practiced, which in turn requires that {\i kol yoshveiha aleha} \u8212- all inhabitants dwell upon it \u8212- the annulment of loans does not apply even in Israel, let alone outside it. This condition has not been met since {\i Sancheriv} exiled the ten tribes, toward the end of the era of the first {\i Beis HaMikdash}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Slaves Release\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Similarly, {\i shiluach avadim chovat haguf hi} \u8212- freeing slaves is a personal obligation, so of course it applies in {\i chutz la\u8217'aretz}. But the {\i Gemara} notes the {\i salka da\u8217'atach amina}: the {\i Torah} states {\i ukratem dror ba\u8217'aretz lechol yoshveiha} \u8212- you shall proclaim liberty throughout the land to all its inhabitants thereof. {\i Ba\u8217'aretz in, bechutz la\u8217'aretz lo} \u8212- one might have read this as limiting the freeing of slaves to the land of Israel. {\i Talmud lomar yovel hi mikol makom} \u8212- the {\i Torah} teaches that it is the {\i yovel}, regardless of location. {\i Im ken mah talmud lomar ba\u8217'aretz?} What then is the function of the word {\i ba\u8217'aretz}? {\i Bizman shehadrur noheg ba\u8217'aretz, noheg bechutz la\u8217'aretz} \u8212- when freedom is proclaimed in Israel, it applies outside Israel as well. {\i Bizman she\u8217'ein dror noheg ba\u8217'aretz, eino noheg bechutz la\u8217'aretz} \u8212- if {\i yovel} is not operative in Israel for whatever reason, it does not apply outside of Israel either. The structure is identical to {\i shemitat kesafim}: the obligation is not geographically confined to Israel, but it is triggered by and dependent on the practice in Israel.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Theological Framework\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This pattern invites a broader theological reflection. The {\i Gemara} is operating throughout with a lens that divides {\i mitzvot} into soil-based obligations, on one hand, and personal obligations, on the other \u8212- assuming that the former are naturally limited to Israel while the latter apply everywhere. But this framing is itself worth questioning. As Rabbi Elazar b\u8217'Rabbi Shimon\u8217's formulation makes clear \u8212- and as the cases of {\i shemitat kesafim} and {\i shiluach avadim} illustrate \u8212- there is a category of {\i mitzvot} that are not literally soil-based but are deeply national in character, tied to an experience of Israel that generates obligations even in {\i chutz la\u8217'aretz}, yet only when that experience is actively taking place in Israel.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This points toward what the {\i Ramban} articulates: the {\i Torah} does not fundamentally conceive of Judaism as a universal religion whose {\i mitzvot} are practiced wherever one happens to live. Rather, the {\i Torah} and its {\i mitzvot} are the laws of the Israelite people living in the land of Israel. Any other state of being \u8212- exile \u8212- is not a natural or normative condition but an aberration. The {\i chiddush} in this framework is not that some {\i mitzvot} are limited to the land; it is that any {\i mitzvot} apply in {\i chutz la\u8217'aretz} at all. The question of which {\i mitzvot} are soil-based and therefore limited to Israel is, in a sense, the wrong starting point. The prior question is why anything should apply outside Israel.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Status Categories\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishna Statuses\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now turns to the {\i Mishnah} in {\i Orlah}. {\i Hachadash assur min hatorah bechol makom} \u8212- {\i chadash} is forbidden by Torah law everywhere. This {\i stam Mishnah} reflects the position of Rabbi Eliezer, and it is the operative {\i halakhic} question whether we rule in accordance with this {\i stam Mishnah} and apply {\i chadash de\u8217'oraita} in {\i chutz la\u8217'aretz}. {\i Orlah halakhah} \u8212- {\i orlah} is a {\i halakhah}, the precise meaning of which the {\i Gemara} will now debate. {\i Vekilayim midivrei soferim} \u8212- {\i kilayim} is rabbinic.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Halachot Medinah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Amar Rav Yehuda amar Shmuel} \u8212- Rav Yehuda citing Shmuel says that the word {\i halakhah} by {\i orlah} means {\i hilchot medinah} \u8212- a very interesting formulation that Rashi interprets as meaning something like an accepted practice, something the people took upon themselves in {\i chutz la\u8217'aretz} rather than a legislated rabbinic enactment from above. The word {\i noheg} captures this \u8212- it is the practice, the custom, rather than a formal decree. This would make {\i orlah} in {\i chutz la\u8217'aretz} weaker even than a standard {\i de\u8217'rabbanan}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Halacha L\u8217'Moshe\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Ulla amar Rabbi Yochanan} \u8212- Ulla citing Rabbi Yochanan disagrees: {\i halakhah} here means {\i halacha l\u8217'Moshe miSinai} \u8212- a tradition handed down orally from Sinai. This is weightier than a rabbinic enactment; it carries the authority of an ancient tradition even though it is not written explicitly in the {\i Torah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Safek Distinctions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The practical difference between these two positions is illustrated by comparing the laws of {\i safek orlah} and {\i safek kilayim} in {\i chutz la\u8217'aretz}. {\i Safek orlah} in the land of Israel is forbidden \u8212- {\i safek de\u8217'oraita lechumra}. In {\i Surya}, which has a certain intermediate status due to the conquests of {\i David HaMelech}, {\i safek orlah} is {\i mutar}. And in {\i chutz la\u8217'aretz}, not only is {\i safek} permitted but one may even go down and purchase from the orchard owner even knowing that some of the trees are {\i orlah} and some are not \u8212- {\i yored velokeach} \u8212- as long as one does not directly witness the seller picking specifically from the {\i orlah} tree. This represents a significant leniency, almost resembling a form of willful ignorance.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Regarding {\i kilayim} in {\i chutz la\u8217'aretz}, the {\i Mishnah} in {\i Kilayim} describes a case of a vineyard planted with vegetables, and a vegetable stand selling from what appears to come from inside the vineyard, right at the edge of the public road. In the land of Israel this is forbidden. In {\i Surya} it is {\i mutar}. In {\i chutz la\u8217'aretz}, one may even observe the seller picking from the mixed planting directly, as long as one does not pick it oneself \u8212- {\i yored veloket ubilvad shelo yilkot beyad}. This is a striking leniency: even witnessing the act of picking directly from what is almost certainly {\i kilayim} does not prevent purchase, as long as there remains some degree of personal remove.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Ulla\u8217's argument is: {\i bishlama l\u8217'didi de\u8217'amina halacha l\u8217'Moshe miSinai} \u8212- if {\i orlah} is {\i halacha l\u8217'Moshe miSinai} and {\i kilayim} is {\i de\u8217'rabbanan}, it makes sense that there would be different thresholds for leniency in cases of {\i safek}. A {\i halacha l\u8217'Moshe miSinai} is weightier than a {\i de\u8217'rabbanan}, and the levels of stringency in the {\i safek} cases reflect this difference. {\i Ela l\u8217'didach} \u8212- but according to Rav Yehuda, who says {\i orlah} is {\i hilchot medinah} \u8212- a practice even weaker than a {\i de\u8217'rabbanan} \u8212- why would there be any different standards at all? Both should be at the same level of leniency. The fact that there is a discernible difference in {\i halachic} stringency between {\i safek orlah} and {\i safek kilayim} in {\i chutz la\u8217'aretz} thus supports Ulla\u8217's position that {\i orlah} is {\i halacha l\u8217'Moshe miSinai} and not merely an accepted regional practice. This point will be developed further in the next {\i daf}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Kiddushin 39\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Orlah vs.\u160 ?Kilayim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Intro: Orlah/Kilayim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 We begin at the bottom of 38b, picking up the thread of which prohibitions apply in {\i eretz yisrael} and which apply in {\i chutz la\u8217'aretz}. The {\i mishna} in {\i Orlah} teaches that {\i chadash} is {\i assur min hatorah} everywhere \u8212- that is the position of Rabbi Eliezer, and we have already discussed how we {\i pasken} that question for {\i chadash} in {\i chutz la\u8217'aretz}. The {\i mishna} then states that {\i orlah} is {\i halacha} and {\i kilayim} is {\i divrei soferim} \u8212- that is, {\i d\u8217'rabbanan}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The question is: what does {\i halacha} mean in this context? One opinion, that of Rav Yehuda in the name of Shmuel, understands it as {\i hilchos hamedina} \u8212- adopted local practice, or at most {\i d\u8217'rabbanan}. The competing position understands {\i halacha} to mean {\i halacha l\u8217'Moshe mi-Sinai}, which would give {\i orlah} considerably more weight than a mere {\i d\u8217'rabbanan}. The {\i sugya} now proceeds to demonstrate that {\i orlah} is indeed weightier than {\i kilayim}, by examining how each handles the question of {\i safek} \u8212- doubtful cases \u8212- in {\i chutz la\u8217'aretz}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ulla\u8217's Challenge\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Ulla raises the question directly to Rav Yehuda: {\i bishlama ledidi} \u8212- according to my position, that {\i orlah} is {\i halacha l\u8217'Moshe mi-Sinai}, I can explain why the {\i mishna} distinguishes between a doubtful case of {\i orlah} and a doubtful case of {\i kilayim}, treating the latter more leniently than the former. {\i Orlah} is {\i halacha l\u8217'Moshe mi-Sinai} while {\i kilayim} is {\i d\u8217'rabbanan}, and thus their respective treatments of {\i safek} differ accordingly.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But according to your view, Rav Yehuda, that both are {\i d\u8217'rabbanan}, why should there be any difference between them in cases of {\i safek}? The {\i mishna} should either make them equally strict or equally lenient \u8212- but not treat them differently.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Leniencies Compared\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 To appreciate the force of Ulla\u8217's challenge, we must look carefully at what the {\i mishna} actually teaches. In a case of {\i safek orlah} in {\i eretz yisrael}, it is {\i assur} \u8212- the land itself is {\i d\u8217'oraita}. In {\i Surya} \u8212- the lands adjacent to Israel conquered by David \u8212- it is {\i mutar}, because the prohibition there is only {\i d\u8217'rabbanan}. In {\i chutz la\u8217'aretz} proper, it is even more lenient: {\i yored velokei\u8217'ach} \u8212- one may go down into the orchard and purchase from the seller there, provided only that one does not actually witness the seller picking directly from the {\i orlah} tree itself. One does not even need to ask. The level of {\i safek} required is remarkably minimal \u8212- as long as it is not blatantly apparent that the fruit came from that tree.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 When we turn to {\i kilayim}, the {\i mishna} presents a parallel structure but with a notably greater leniency in {\i chutz la\u8217'aretz}. The case discussed is a vineyard in which vegetables have been planted \u8212- rendering those vegetables {\i kilayim} \u8212- with a small produce stand right outside selling the very same vegetables. In {\i eretz yisrael} this is {\i assur}; in {\i Surya} it is {\i mutar}. But in {\i chutz la\u8217'aretz}, the leniency goes even further: {\i yored veloket} \u8212- one may actually observe the seller picking the vegetables directly from the vineyard and still purchase and eat them. As Rashi explains at the top of 39a: {\i delo assru be-chutz la\u8217'aretz ela es hazoreya o es haloket beyadayim} \u8212- the only prohibition in {\i chutz la\u8217'aretz} falls on the one who planted the {\i kilayim} or the one who personally picks it. One who merely purchases \u8212- even having watched the picking occur \u8212- is permitted.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 So we have two extreme {\i kulos}, but {\i kilayim} is more lenient still: by {\i orlah} one cannot witness the picking; by {\i kilayim} one can. Ulla\u8217's argument is that if both are {\i d\u8217'rabbanan}, they should be treated identically. Why is one permitted to witness the picking for {\i kilayim} but not for {\i orlah}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shmuel Amends Mishna\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Shmuel, who is precisely the one who taught that {\i orlah} in {\i chutz la\u8217'aretz} is {\i hilchos hamedina} \u8212- effectively {\i d\u8217'rabbanan} \u8212- concedes Ulla\u8217's point entirely. He instructed the {\i rabanana} to amend the {\i girsa} of the {\i mishna} so that both {\i orlah} and {\i kilayim} carry the same ruling. Either both prohibit witnessing the picking \u8212- i.e., the stricter version \u8212- or both permit witnessing the picking as long as one does not personally do the picking. Since Shmuel holds them to be at the same level, the {\i mishna} must reflect uniform treatment, and he accordingly emends the text to achieve that consistency.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mar Brei D\u8217'Ravina\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Mar brei d\u8217'Ravina takes the more lenient of the two positions. He teaches: {\i zeh vezeh yored velokei\u8217'ach} \u8212- in both cases one may even watch the picking occur directly, {\i ubilvad shelo yilkat beyado}, as long as one does not pick it oneself. This presumably reflects his agreement with Shmuel\u8217's view that both {\i orlah} and {\i kilayim} stand at the same level, and if they are both {\i d\u8217'rabbanan}, one might as well adopt the more lenient ruling for both.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shivi\u8217's Proposal\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Shivi said to Shmuel \u8212- calling him {\i aryoch}, apparently a term meaning something like \u8220"the great one\u8221" or \u8220"the lion,\u8221" which may derive from the same root as {\i aryoch melech Elasar} \u8212- {\i sfeik li v\u8217'ana eichol}: make it a {\i safek} for me and I will eat it. Rashi explains: {\i sfeik li likot shelo bifnai sheyisafek etzli v\u8217'ana eichol uv\u8217'Orlah ka\u8217'i} \u8212- go ahead and pick when I am not present, so that there will be a genuine {\i safek} as far as I am concerned, and I will eat it. This is actually the stricter of the two positions: it does not permit witnessing the picking for {\i orlah}, but it does show a practical workaround for a Jew who has, say, an apple orchard in its third year. The seller picks when the buyer is not present, thereby maintaining the technical requirement that one not witness the picking directly.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Exchange of Picking\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Avya and Rabba bar Bar Chana went one step further and would exchange the picking of doubtful produce between one another \u8212- each one picking for the other, so that neither was in the presence of his own orchard\u8217's picking. This is a practical mutual arrangement that achieves the same result as Shivi\u8217's proposal but between two parties acting in concert.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Pumbedita\u8217's Leniency\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i charifei d\u8217'Pumbedita} \u8212- the sharp ones of Pumbedita, whom Rashi identifies as Ha\u8217'eifa u\u8217'Ramibi, the sons of Rechava \u8212- took a far more sweeping position: {\i ein orlah b\u8217'chutz la\u8217'aretz}, there is simply no {\i orlah} in {\i chutz la\u8217'aretz} whatsoever. This was the predictable endpoint of the slippery slope: once the leniencies are extended far enough, someone will ultimately make the blanket declaration that the prohibition does not apply at all.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Yochanan\u8217's Response\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 RavYehuda Reports\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Yehuda sent word to Rabbi Yochanan, who was in {\i eretz yisrael}, reporting that the scholars of Pumbedita were declaring {\i ein orlah b\u8217'chutz la\u8217'aretz} \u8212- that {\i orlah} does not apply at all outside the Land. Rabbi Yochanan\u8217's response was forceful and multidimensional. First, {\i setom sefeika}: silence the {\i safek}. Now that people are sliding into the position that {\i orlah} does not apply at all, one must stop publicizing the various leniencies in cases of {\i safek}, lest the teaching of those leniencies serve as the gateway to the blanket denial of the prohibition. Second, {\i v\u8217'abeid vadah}: actively destroy any produce that is a {\i vaday} case of {\i orlah}. Third, {\i v\u8217'hachreiz al peiroteihen she-te\u8217'unin genizah}: announce publicly that the fruit of those who engaged in these arrangements \u8212- the exchange of picking and similar tactics \u8212- requires {\i genizah}. And finally, regarding anyone who says {\i ein orlah b\u8217'chutz la\u8217'aretz} outright: {\i lo yiheyeh lo nin v\u8217'neched} \u8212- he should have no descendant. This is strikingly severe rhetoric, and reflects Rabbi Yochanan\u8217's alarm at how far the leniency had traveled.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then asks: upon which authority were the scholars of Pumbedita relying? If they were making the blanket statement that {\i orlah} does not apply at all in {\i chutz la\u8217'aretz}, they presumably had a tannaitic source for that position.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Eliezer Tradition\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Indeed, there is such a tradition: {\i Rabbi Elazar b\u8217'Rabbi Yossi omer mishum Rabbi Yossi ben Durmaska she-amar mishum Rabbi Yossi ha-Gelili she-amar mishum Rabbi Yochanan ben Nuri she-amar mishum Rabbi Eliezer ha-Gadol} \u8212- an unbroken chain of authorities going back to Rabbi Eliezer the Great: {\i ein orlah b\u8217'chutz la\u8217'aretz}. It is worth pausing to note that this itself is a remarkable feature of the {\i sugya}: if one holds that anything which is a {\i halacha l\u8217'Moshe mi-Sinai} is never subject to debate \u8212- as the Rambam maintains \u8212- then the very fact that Rabbi Eliezer is denying that {\i orlah b\u8217'chutz la\u8217'aretz} exists at all presents a difficulty, whether one frames it as a debate about whether it is a {\i halacha l\u8217'Moshe mi-Sinai} or as a flat rejection of that category\u8217's application here.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Contradiction Resolved\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} immediately notes an apparent contradiction: we have a {\i mishna} in which Rabbi Eliezer says {\i af he-chadash} \u8212- even {\i chadash} applies outside the Land, in the context of a list that includes {\i orlah} and {\i kilayim}. If Rabbi Eliezer holds {\i ein orlah b\u8217'chutz la\u8217'aretz}, why does he seem to be adding {\i chadash} to a list that includes {\i orlah}? The resolution offered is to emend the {\i mishna}: remove the word {\i af} and read it as saying that Rabbi Eliezer holds that only {\i chadash} applies in {\i chutz la\u8217'aretz} \u8212- {\i orlah} and {\i kilayim} do not apply \u8212- whereas the {\i stam} view of the {\i mishna} holds that all three apply. This way, the two attributed statements of Rabbi Eliezer are brought into alignment.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Halacha L\u8217'Moshe Claim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Assi teaches in the name of Rabbi Yochanan: {\i orlah b\u8217'chutz la\u8217'aretz halacha l\u8217'Moshe mi-Sinai}. This is a reiteration of Rabbi Yochanan\u8217's position \u8212- a strong stance that treats the application of {\i orlah} outside the Land as a law transmitted directly from Sinai, not merely a rabbinic practice or local custom.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Zeira\u8217's Objection\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Zeira raises an objection to Rav Assi: how can you claim this is {\i halacha l\u8217'Moshe mi-Sinai}? But we have the {\i mishna} that teaches {\i safek orlah ba\u8217'aretz assur, be-Surya mutar, u-vechutz la\u8217'aretz yored velokei\u8217'ach} \u8212- all these extensive leniencies in cases of {\i safek}! If {\i orlah b\u8217'chutz la\u8217'aretz} is a {\i halacha l\u8217'Moshe mi-Sinai}, how can there be such extraordinary leniency even in what amounts to a near-{\i vaday} case? Rav Assi was momentarily speechless \u8212- {\i ishtomem kesha\u8217'a chada} \u8212- he was dumbfounded.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Safek Mutar Solution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Then Rav Assi recovered: {\i eima kach ne\u8217'emar} \u8212- perhaps this is precisely how the {\i halacha l\u8217'Moshe mi-Sinai} was formulated: {\i sveika mutar, vadai assur} \u8212- in a case of {\i safek} it is permitted, in a case of {\i vaday} it is forbidden. The {\i halacha l\u8217'Moshe mi-Sinai} itself incorporated this leniency for {\i safek} as an intrinsic part of its content.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 There are two significant observations to make here. First, note that Rav Assi does not say he has a clear transmitted tradition that the {\i halacha l\u8217'Moshe mi-Sinai} included this {\i safek} provision; rather, he says {\i eima kach ne\u8217'emar} \u8212- \u8220"let us say it was stated this way.\u8221" He is reconstructing the content of the {\i halacha l\u8217'Moshe mi-Sinai} from what the {\i mishna} teaches, rather than recalling an unbroken explicit tradition about it. This itself raises questions about what it means to invoke {\i halacha l\u8217'Moshe mi-Sinai} \u8212- whether it always reflects a clearly remembered transmission or whether it can be a category that is inferred and in some measure reconstructed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Second, Rav Zeira\u8217's question implicitly assumes that {\i safek d\u8217'oraisa l\u8217'chumra} should apply to a {\i halacha l\u8217'Moshe mi-Sinai} \u8212- that if this has Sinaitic authority, one should be strict in cases of doubt. And indeed, the Rambam holds that the principle of {\i safek d\u8217'oraisa l\u8217'chumra} is itself only {\i d\u8217'rabbanan} \u8212- that as far as the {\i torah} is concerned, {\i safek d\u8217'oraisa l\u8217'kula}. Yet this {\i gemara} seems to proceed on the assumption that Sinaitic authority calls for stringency in {\i safek}, requiring a special built-in provision to permit leniency. One possible way of resolving this tension is to note that what may have been troubling the {\i gemara} is not the abstract principle of {\i safek l\u8217'kula} but rather the extraordinary practical extremity of the leniency \u8212- a situation so attenuated that it barely qualifies as a {\i safek} in any meaningful sense, such as buying from a seller when one knows the orchard contains {\i orlah} trees but simply did not witness the picking. That kind of manipulated or fictional {\i safek} may be what prompted the objection, quite apart from any general principle about {\i safek d\u8217'oraisa}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This question \u8212- whether {\i safek} in a {\i halacha l\u8217'Moshe mi-Sinai} is {\i l\u8217'chumra} or {\i l\u8217'kula} \u8212- is discussed among the {\i rishonim}; if memory serves, the Ran or Rabbeinu Yona addresses it somewhere. It connects to the broader question of the weight of {\i halacha l\u8217'Moshe mi-Sinai} relative to {\i d\u8217'oraisa} prohibitions in general.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A further note is worth adding about the category of {\i halacha l\u8217'Moshe mi-Sinai} itself. The Rambam held that whatever is truly {\i halacha l\u8217'Moshe mi-Sinai} was never debated \u8212- and he enumerated approximately thirty such items in the {\i gemara}. He used this framework in part as a response to the Karaite challenge: how can you claim an unbroken oral tradition when everything is debated? The Rambam\u8217's answer was that virtually all of {\i torah she-ba\u8217'al peh} was never directly transmitted as bottom-line {\i halachos} but rather as hermeneutic principles and an empowerment to interpret; that interpretation, applied by different minds in different circumstances, naturally generates debate. The one domain in which actual content was transmitted intact is {\i halacha l\u8217'Moshe mi-Sinai}, where there should be no {\i machlokes}. The problem, as this {\i sugya} illustrates, is that there plainly {\i is} debate even over some of those traditions. In addition, there are places in the {\i gemara} where the phrase {\i halacha l\u8217'Moshe mi-Sinai} appears to be used rhetorically \u8212- the {\i Mishnah Yadayim} is one example \u8212- for matters that cannot literally be Sinaitic laws. The Rosh makes this observation explicitly. The Netziv, in his introduction to the {\i She\u8217'iltos}, discusses how certain {\i rishonim} understand the term {\i hilchasa} when it appears after a {\i gemara} dispute: Rashi reads it as {\i halacha l\u8217'Moshe mi-Sinai}, while others understand it as an ancient {\i d\u8217'rabbanan} whose reason was forgotten, without any claim that it goes back to Moshe. These questions of when the phrase is literal and when it is rhetorical are treated in modern scholarship as well.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Kilayim Distinctions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Lashes for Kilayim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 We now turn to {\i kilayim} specifically. Abba teaches in the name of Rabbi Yochanan: {\i lokin al kilayim de-var Torah} \u8212- one receives lashes for {\i kilayim} as a biblical prohibition. The immediate response from Rabbi Elazar bar Rabbi Yossi is a challenge: but we have an explicit {\i mishna} \u8212- {\i ha-kilayim mi-divrei sofrim}! {\i Kilayim} is rabbinic! The entire thrust of our {\i sugya} up to now has been that {\i kilayim} is {\i d\u8217'rabbanan}. How can Rabbi Yochanan say one gets lashes, which is a punishment reserved for biblical violations?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Harkavah vs.\u160 ?Seeds\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The resolution: {\i lo kashya, kan b\u8217'kilai kerem kan b\u8217'harkava} \u8212- there is no contradiction because we are dealing with two entirely different categories. The {\i mishna}\u8217's statement that {\i kilayim} is {\i d\u8217'rabbanan} refers to {\i kilayim} of seeds \u8212- mixing different seeds in a field. But there is another form of {\i kilayim}: {\i harkavat ilan} \u8212- grafting one tree onto another. This form is more analogous to the crossbreeding of animals ({\i harva\u8217'at behema}), since it involves direct physical manipulation of living organisms rather than simply planting seeds in proximity. It is this category \u8212- {\i harkavat ilan} \u8212- for which one receives lashes even in {\i chutz la\u8217'aretz}, based on a teaching of Shmuel.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Shmuel derives this from the verse {\i et chukotai tishmoru} \u8212- you shall observe My statutes. The unusual word order ({\i et chukotai}, placing the object first) implies that these {\i chukim} are ancient, pre-existing the moment of their formal biblical statement. The next verse reads: {\i behemtecha lo tarbia kilayim, sadecha lo tizra kilayim} \u8212- you shall not crossbreed your animals with {\i kilayim}, and you shall not sow your field with {\i kilayim}. The derivation proceeds as follows: just as {\i behemtecha} refers to crossbreeding animals, so too {\i sadecha} here is read not at the level of {\i peshat} (planting seeds) but through {\i derash}, as referring to something analogous \u8212- grafting trees. And just as crossbreeding animals applies equally in {\i eretz yisrael} and in {\i chutz la\u8217'aretz}, so too grafting trees ({\i harkavat ilan}) applies {\i bein ba\u8217'aretz bein be-chutz la\u8217'aretz} \u8212- wherever one is.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 There is a further dimension to this derivation: the antiquity implied by {\i et chukotai} suggests that these prohibitions predate the giving of the {\i Torah} at Sinai \u8212- they go back to the {\i benei Noach}. According to this position, even non-Jews are prohibited in crossbreeding animals and grafting trees, as these are counted among the {\i sheva mitzvos benei Noach}. The Rambam rules accordingly: {\i hamarkiv ilan be\u8217'ilan\u8230 ? harei zeh lokeh min haTorah bechol makom bein ba\u8217'aretz bein bechutz la\u8217'aretz} \u8212- one who grafts tree onto tree receives lashes from the {\i Torah} in every location, whether in {\i eretz yisrael} or in {\i chutz la\u8217'aretz}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 As for the word {\i sadecha} \u8212- \u8220"your field,\u8221" which sounds possessive and might suggest the prohibition is limited to one\u8217's own land in {\i eretz yisrael} \u8212- the {\i gemara} resolves this by saying {\i hahu le-ma\u8217'uti zera\u8217'im she-ba-chutz}: {\i sadecha} is used specifically to exclude {\i kilayim} of seeds in {\i chutz la\u8217'aretz}, which is indeed not prohibited there at the biblical level. So the verse operates on two levels simultaneously: the {\i peshat} level ({\i lo tizra}, seeds, {\i sadecha}, limited to {\i eretz yisrael}) and the {\i derash} level (grafting, analogous to crossbreeding, which applies universally). Rashi\u8217's comment here is striking: {\i chutza la\u8217'aretz lo chasmu lachem min hashomayim} \u8212- outside the Land, heaven has not sealed off for you [the fruit of grafted trees]. The fruit itself remains permitted even where the act is forbidden.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Biblical Sources\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The two verses that govern these prohibitions are {\i behemtecha lo tarbia kilayim} \u8212- your animals you shall not crossbreed \u8212- and {\i sadecha lo tizra kilayim} \u8212- your field you shall not sow with mixed species. The hermeneutic reading of {\i sadecha} as parallel to {\i behemtecha} allows the prohibition to travel from the planting of seeds (its {\i peshat} meaning) to the grafting of trees (its {\i derash} meaning), and simultaneously the comparison to animal crossbreeding establishes that the prohibition is geographically unlimited. The {\i peshat} reading of seeds, by contrast, is confined by {\i sadecha} \u8212- \u8220"your field,\u8221" meaning {\i eretz yisrael} \u8212- to the Land.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Chanan/Anan\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Chanan and Rav Anan were walking along a road when they observed a man planting seeds together. Rav Anan suggested: {\i neisei mar nishmasei} \u8212- let us go and put him in {\i cherem}, for he is transgressing the prohibition of {\i kilayim} in {\i chutz la\u8217'aretz}. His companion replied: {\i lo chavir su} \u8212- it is not clear to you what the {\i halacha} is. The exchange was not finished, however. They then saw another man planting wheat and barley inside a vineyard. Again Rav Anan suggested putting him in {\i cherem}. Again: {\i lo tzahiri tu} \u8212- it is not clear to you. The {\i gemara} explains that we do not rule like Rabbi Yoshiya, whose stringent definition of the act of {\i kilayim} would apply here.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Yoshiya\u8217's Rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yoshiya holds: {\i ad she\u8217'yizra chittah u\u8217'se\u8217'orah ve\u8217'chartzan be\u8217'mapoles yad} \u8212- a person does not transgress the prohibition of planting {\i kilayim} unless he plants wheat, barley, and a grape seed all simultaneously, in a single action. Planting wheat and barley alone does not constitute the prohibition of {\i kilayim} of the vineyard according to his view, because a vineyard requires the grape to be present as part of the act. Given this ruling \u8212- especially in the lenient context of {\i chutz la\u8217'aretz} \u8212- there is no basis for putting the man in {\i cherem}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafot Analysis\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot works through the relationship between the two types of {\i kilayim} with precision. First: Rabbi Yoshiya\u8217's requirement of all three seeds simultaneously \u8212- wheat, barley, and a grape seed \u8212- applies specifically to {\i kilayim hakerem}, {\i kilayim} of the vineyard. By contrast, {\i kilayim zeraim} \u8212- the prohibition of mixed seeds in a field, without a grapevine \u8212- requires only two different species and is violated by planting any two species together.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This raises an obvious question: even if the man planting wheat and barley in the vineyard has not violated {\i kilayim hakerem} (since he did not include a grape seed simultaneously), why is he not liable for {\i kilayim zeraim}? After all, he has planted two different seeds together. Tosafot addresses this with a clever reading of the verse. {\i Karmecha lo tizra kilayim} \u8212- your vineyard you shall not sow with {\i kilayim} \u8212- implies that in order to constitute {\i kilayim hakerem}, the {\i kilayim} must already exist before it comes into the vineyard; that is, one must bring the two grains together with the grapevine simultaneously, as a combined act. By contrast, {\i sadecha lo tizra kilayim} \u8212- your field you shall not sow with {\i kilayim} \u8212- does not carry the same implication, because a {\i sadeh} is simply a field, requiring no additional element to define it. Planting two seeds in a field is sufficient to create {\i kilayim zeraim}. But something that is already a vineyard is only a {\i kerem} by virtue of the grapes, not a plain {\i sadeh}. Therefore, when wheat and barley are planted inside an existing vineyard, it may not qualify as {\i kilayim zeraim} since the location is a {\i kerem}, not a plain {\i sadeh}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i halacha} follows Rabbi Oshaya, as Tosafot concludes: one may plant under a vine as long as all three seeds were not planted simultaneously, and {\i kilayim zeraim} does not apply in {\i chutz la\u8217'aretz} at all.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Yosef Practice\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Yosef would mix seeds together and plant them. Rashi adds: {\i v\u8217'lo b\u8217'kerem} \u8212- not in a vineyard. This reflects his position that {\i kilayim zeraim} does not apply in {\i chutz la\u8217'aretz} and therefore planting two different seeds together in a field outside {\i eretz yisrael} is not prohibited.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Abye Distinguishes\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye challenges: but the {\i mishna} says {\i ha-kilayim mi-divrei sofrim} \u8212- {\i kilayim} is rabbinic! Even if we allow {\i kilayim zeraim} in {\i chutz la\u8217'aretz}, should it not at least be prohibited rabbinically? The response: {\i lo kashya, kan b\u8217'kilai kerem, kan b\u8217'kilai zrayim} \u8212- there is no difficulty, because we must distinguish sharply between the two. {\i Kilayim hakerem} \u8212- the vineyard mixture \u8212- is of such severity that even in {\i eretz yisrael} the produce is {\i assur behana\u8217'ah}, and therefore the rabbis extended its prohibition to {\i chutz la\u8217'aretz} as well. {\i Kilayim zeraim} \u8212- the mixing of seeds \u8212- is never {\i assur behana\u8217'ah} even in {\i eretz yisrael}; accordingly, the rabbis made no such extension, and it is not prohibited in {\i chutz la\u8217'aretz} at all.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Summary of Rules\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Let us now draw together the full picture that emerges from this {\i sugya}. In {\i eretz yisrael}: {\i kilayim hakerem} is biblically prohibited, both the act and the produce, which is {\i assur behana\u8217'ah}. {\i Kilayim zeraim} is prohibited as an act but the produce is not {\i assur behana\u8217'ah} \u8212- one may eat the fruit or vegetables that grew from mixed seeds. Outside {\i eretz yisrael}: crossbreeding animals is biblically prohibited \u8212- a {\i d\u8217'oraita} prohibition with no consequence for the produce (one may use a mule). Grafting trees ({\i harkavat ilan}) is similarly a {\i d\u8217'oraita} prohibition \u8212- but again, with no consequence for the produce: the fruit of a grafted tree is entirely permitted. {\i Kilayim hakerem} in {\i chutz la\u8217'aretz} is rabbinically prohibited, but only in the narrow definition of Rabbi Yoshiya \u8212- planting all three seeds (wheat, barley, and grape) simultaneously. {\i Kilayim zeraim} in {\i chutz la\u8217'aretz} is not prohibited at all, neither biblically nor rabbinically.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The only prohibition that renders produce {\i assur behana\u8217'ah}, whether in {\i eretz yisrael} or {\i chutz la\u8217'aretz}, is {\i kilayim hakerem} \u8212- and this is why the {\i mishna} discussing doubtful {\i kilayim} specifically chose the case of vegetables growing near a vineyard. The Rambam in {\i Ma\u8217'achalos Assuros}, chapter 13, reflects this: among the prohibitions that are of the {\i Torah} with respect to the produce of the earth, he lists {\i chadash}, {\i kilayim hakerem}, {\i tevel}, and {\i orlah} \u8212- not {\i kilayim zeraim}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hybrid Fruits OK\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This last point has important practical implications. Hybrid fruits \u8212- nectarines, tangelos, and similar products of botanical grafting or selective cultivation \u8212- are not {\i assur behana\u8217'ah} and are entirely permitted to eat. The reason is that {\i kilayim zeraim} never renders produce forbidden even where the act is prohibited, and {\i harkavat ilan}, while prohibited as an act even in {\i chutz la\u8217'aretz}, similarly carries no consequence for the fruit. One may make a {\i bracha} and eat the fruit of a grafted tree without any {\i halachic} concern. The only category of hybrid that would raise a concern regarding the produce is {\i kilayim hakerem}, which requires the specific combination of grain and grapevine \u8212- a scenario that does not describe the commercially available hybrid fruits of our time.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One detail does remain somewhat puzzling: that one should have a {\i d\u8217'oraita issur} such as {\i harkavat ilan} with no {\i gezeirah} extending the prohibition to the produce. Compare, for example, {\i basar b\u8217'chalav}, where some {\i rishonim} explain that the {\i Torah} prohibited eating the mixture precisely because there had to be some tangible consequence to the act of cooking them together. Here, by contrast, the act is gravely prohibited and yet the fruit emerges entirely permitted. Perhaps the explanation is that the concern of {\i kilayim} is the act of disrupting the divine order of creation through unnatural combination, rather than the product of that combination \u8212- and therefore the prohibition attaches to the act, not to its fruit.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Reward/Punishment\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishna: One Mitzvah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 We now turn to what is a welcome {\i aggadic} interlude \u8212- though it opens into some of the deepest theology in the tractate. The {\i mishna} states: {\i kol ha\u8217'oseh mitzvah achas metivin lo u\u8217'ma\u8217'arichin lo yamav v\u8217'nochel et ha\u8217'aretz} \u8212- whoever performs even one {\i mitzvah}, good is extended to him, his days are lengthened, and he inherits the land. {\i Nochel et ha\u8217'aretz} is linguistically picking up the theme of {\i eretz yisrael} that has dominated the preceding discussion, whether it is meant literally, metaphorically, or simply as a literary echo. And the passage concludes with the negative: whoever does not perform even one {\i mitzvah} receives none of these benefits. The literary structure also continues the {\i kol} formulation we have seen throughout \u8212- {\i kol mitzvos ha\u8217'av al ha\u8217'ben}, {\i kol mitzvos aseh she\u8217'hazman gerama}, and now {\i kol ha\u8217'oseh mitzvah} \u8212- now turning from the categorization of {\i mitzvos} to the power of performing a single one.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishna Peah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This {\i mishna} appears to be in tension with the first {\i mishna} of {\i Peah}, which specifies a particular list of {\i mitzvos} for which one eats the fruit of the reward in this world: {\i kibbud av v\u8217'em, gemilus chasadim, hachnasat orchim, havaa\u8217't shalom bein adam l\u8217'chavero}, and {\i talmud Torah keneged kulam}. (The inclusion of {\i hachnasat orchim} in some versions is itself subject to textual question.) If any single {\i mitzvah} yields reward in this world, as our {\i mishna} in Kiddushin implies, what is distinctive about the list in {\i Peah}? Conversely, if the {\i Peah} list is exhaustive, then our {\i mishna} seems to overclaim.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is worth pausing to note that the {\i mishna} in {\i Peah} may not be making a purely theological claim about divine reward. It is entirely possible to read {\i ochel peiroteihem ba\u8217'olam hazeh} as describing the natural and pragmatic fruits of those specific {\i mitzvos} \u8212- the person who honors parents, does acts of kindness, brings peace between people, and engages in {\i Torah} study will experience the concrete benefits of those behaviors in this world, quite apart from any supernatural reward. Research on human happiness consistently finds that acts of generosity toward others yield greater personal satisfaction than self-directed spending. The intrinsic payoff of these particular {\i mitzvos} may be precisely what the {\i mishna} is pointing to. The {\i gemara}, however, reads the conflict textually \u8212- the language of reward and its this-worldly versus other-worldly character \u8212- and proceeds to resolve it on those terms.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Yehuda Balance\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Yehuda resolves the tension by reframing the {\i mishna} entirely: {\i kol oseh mitzvah achas yeseira al zechuyosav meitivin lo} \u8212- the {\i mishna} is not saying that the reward for performing a {\i mitzvah} is delivered in this world. Rather, it is describing what happens when a person is at exact equilibrium \u8212- fifty percent merits, fifty percent demerits \u8212- and this one additional {\i mitzvah} tips the scale in his favor. In that case, {\i meitivin lo}, and it is as though he fulfilled the entire {\i Torah}. And correspondingly, the person who fails to perform one {\i mitzvah} when he is at fifty-fifty tips his scale in the negative direction. This reading confines the {\i mishna}\u8217's applicability to the specific and relatively rare scenario of a precisely balanced accounting \u8212- which is a much narrower and less dramatically satisfying claim than a straightforward reading would suggest.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The question then becomes: what distinguishes the {\i mitzvos} listed in {\i Peah}, where this-worldly reward is promised even without the fifty-fifty precondition? The {\i gemara} answers in the name of Rav Shmaya: if one of those {\i mitzvos} from {\i Peah} is already among your fifty percent of merits, then it functions as a slight additional weight \u8212- you do not need a separate extra {\i mitzvah} to tip the scale, because that particular {\i mitzvah} itself carries additional weight. Our {\i mishna} then describes the case where none of the {\i Peah} mitzvos are part of your fifty percent, and you need an additional act to tip the balance.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Baraita Opposites\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then raises a further challenge from a {\i baraita} that teaches the precise opposite of what our {\i mishna} predicts. The {\i baraita} states: {\i kol she-zechuyosav merubin me-avonosav me-arin lo} \u8212- whoever has more merits than sins, it is done badly for him, {\i ve-domeh ke-mi she-saraf kol ha-Torah kullah} \u8212- and he will appear as though he burned the entire {\i Torah}; his suffering will be so severe that it will look as though he committed the greatest sins imaginable. Conversely, {\i kol she-avonosav merubin mi-zechuyosav meitivin lo} \u8212- one whose sins outweigh his merits will have it well in this world \u8212- {\i ve-domeh ke-mi she-kiyyem kol ha-Torah kullah} \u8212- and it will look as though he fulfilled the entire {\i Torah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This, of course, is the classic theodicy invoked to explain {\i tzaddik ve-ra lo}: God cleans the slate of the righteous through suffering in this world, and rewards the wicked in this world so as to deny them any credit in the world to come. But it directly contradicts our {\i mishna}, which promises that doing a {\i mitzvah} leads to good outcomes.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Abye Days Theory\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye\u8217's resolution \u8212- and Tosafot\u8217's reading of Abaye is cleaner than Rashi\u8217's here \u8212- is that these opposing outcomes operate on the level of individual days rather than one\u8217's entire life. The {\i mishna} in Kiddushin describes the overall trajectory of one\u8217's life: a {\i tzaddik} will ultimately have it good and a {\i rasha} will ultimately have it bad. But the {\i baraita} describes what happens on any given day: there will be specific days on which the {\i tzaddik} suffers greatly \u8212- as though he had done the worst things imaginable \u8212- and specific days on which the {\i rasha} prospers remarkably. The two sources are thus not in conflict; they describe different timeframes.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Yaakov\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava offers a different resolution entirely: the {\i baraita} that teaches these counterintuitive outcomes reflects the view of Rabbi Yaakov, because {\i de-ammar sechar mitzvah be-hai alma lekka} \u8212- Rabbi Yaakov maintains that there is no reward for {\i mitzvos} in this world whatsoever. All reward is reserved exclusively for the world to come. According to Rabbi Yaakov, the {\i tzaddik} suffering in this world is not a theodicy requiring explanation \u8212- it is simply the expected state of affairs. This world is not where the divine accounting is settled. Our {\i mishna} in Kiddushin, which speaks of this-worldly reward, reflects the majority view; the {\i baraita} reflects the position of Rabbi Yaakov.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Problem: Tzaddik Vra Lo\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yaakov\u8217's position is articulated in a {\i baraita} that explores it through concrete cases. Rabbi Yaakov says: there is not a single {\i mitzvah} written in the {\i Torah} alongside its stated reward where the real fulfillment of that reward does not depend on {\i techiyas hamessim} \u8212- the resurrection of the dead \u8212- rather than this-worldly realization. Consider {\i kibbud av v\u8217'em}, which promises {\i lema\u8217'an ya\u8217'arichun yamecha u-le\u8217'ma\u8217'an yitav lach}. Consider {\i shilluach haken}, which likewise promises {\i lema\u8217'an yitav lach ve-ha\u8217'arachta yamim}. Now consider the following case: a father tells his son to go up to a tower and bring down some chicks. The son goes up, performs {\i shilluach haken} by sending away the mother bird, takes the chicks, and on the way down falls and dies. {\i Heichen tovas yamav shel zeh? Ve-heichen arichas yamav shel zeh?} Where is the good that was promised? Where is the length of days? This is the problem of {\i tzaddik ve-ra lo} in its starkest and most personal form.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Solution: Olam Haba\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yaakov\u8217's answer is sweeping and unambiguous: {\i lema\u8217'an yitav lach} \u8212- to the world that is entirely good; {\i lema\u8217'an ya\u8217'arichun yamecha} \u8212- to the world that is entirely long. Every promise of reward in the {\i Torah} refers to {\i olam haba}, a world that is {\i kullo tov} and {\i kullo aroch}. There is no commitment in the {\i Torah} to reward in this world. This resolution eliminates the problem entirely by relocating its terms: the promises are real, but they belong to a different register of existence altogether.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hypothetical Challenge\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} immediately probes this answer. Perhaps, it suggests, the case Rabbi Yaakov describes \u8212- where someone performs two {\i mitzvos} and immediately suffers a fatal accident \u8212- is purely hypothetical, and in reality it never happens that way. Perhaps everyone who has ever honored their parents and performed {\i shilluach haken} has in fact lived long. If so, Rabbi Yaakov has built his theology on an imagined scenario that has no real-world instantiation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The response is that {\i Rabbi Yaakov maiseh chaza} \u8212- Rabbi Yaakov actually witnessed such an event, and therefore he knows it is not hypothetical. The {\i gemara} continues probing: perhaps the young man died because he was harboring sinful thoughts at the time, and it was those thoughts, not the failure of the promise, that caused his death. To this the {\i gemara} responds: {\i machashavah ra\u8217'ah ein ha-kadosh baruch hu metzarfah le-ma\u8217'aseh} \u8212- God does not combine a sinful thought with an act and punish a person for it. Mere thought does not constitute transgression.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Perhaps, then, he was harboring thoughts of {\i avodah zarah} \u8212- for which there is a biblical source ({\i lema\u8217'an tpos es beis yisrael be-libbam}) suggesting that even the contemplation of idolatry can be held against a person? To this the {\i gemara} effectively says: if anything, that actually reinforces Rabbi Yaakov\u8217's point. {\i I-sal-ka da\u8217'atach sechar mitzvah be-hai alma} \u8212- if you truly believed that {\i mitzvos} are rewarded in this world \u8212- {\i ammai lo aggen mitzvah aleh ke-heichi de-lo leyessi lidei hirhur} \u8212- then the {\i mitzvah} he was in the midst of performing should have protected him from having that idolatrous thought in the first place. The very fact that the {\i mitzvah} failed to protect him is itself evidence that {\i mitzvos} carry no this-worldly protective guarantee. The real world does not work the way a naive reading of the {\i Torah}\u8217's promises would suggest, and therefore those promises must be referring to something beyond this world entirely. We will pick up from here in our next {\i shiur}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Kiddushin 40\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Reward Theology\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishna Rewards\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The daf opens at the bottom of 39b, following a sequence of {\i mishnayot} categorizing {\i mitzvot}. The {\i mishna} there promises that whoever performs a {\i mitzvah} is {\i nochal et ha\u8217'aretz} \u8212- he inherits a portion of the World to Come \u8212- and, further, that he reaps the rewards of his deeds in this world as well. This claim stands in some tension with the {\i mishna} in {\i Pe\u8217'ah}, which identifies only a specific set of {\i mitzvot} for which one enjoys the fruits of his labor in this world. The {\i Gemara} had attempted various harmonizations, including the suggestion that God evens out the accounts over the course of a lifetime \u8212- some days of blessing, some of tragedy \u8212- so that, broadly speaking, one\u8217's life reflects one\u8217's deeds. That is how {\i Tosafot} reads the passage. But the {\i Gemara} then raises the deeper question: is there not a fundamental theological debate lurking here?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Yaakov Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} introduces the famous position of Rabbi Yaakov, encapsulated in the phrase {\i schar mitzvah be-hai alma leka} \u8212- reward for a {\i mitzvah} in this world simply does not exist. All the Torah\u8217's promises of reward are promises for the {\i olam ha-ba}. This principle represents a fundamental resolution of the challenge of {\i tzaddik ve\u8217'ra lo} \u8212- the suffering of the righteous \u8212- by relocating all recompense to the next world. The paradigmatic case that compelled Rabbi Yaakov to articulate this position is the child who obeys his father, performs {\i shiluach ha-ken}, and then falls from the ladder and dies. He fulfilled two {\i mitzvot} for which the Torah explicitly promises long life, and yet he perished. Even if one insists this particular case never occurred, the scenario is realistically conceivable \u8212- and our honest reckoning with the world demands that we acknowledge it. The Torah\u8217's promises, then, must be understood as directed toward {\i olam ha-ba}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Maharsha Solution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Maharsha} raises a pointed difficulty against Rabbi Yaakov\u8217's position. One can plausibly reinterpret broad promises like {\i ve-ha\u8217'arachta yamim le-ma\u8217'an yitav lach} as referring to {\i olam ha-ba}, but what does one do with the very concrete and specific {\i blessings} and {\i curses} enumerated in the Torah \u8212- things like {\i yakecha Hashem bashechin uvakadachas}? These cannot be casually redirected to the next world. The {\i Maharsha} therefore proposes a crucial distinction: these detailed promises operate at the level of the {\i tzibur} \u8212- the community as a whole. When an entire people is meritorious or sinful, those national promises are fulfilled in this world. But the challenge of {\i schar ve-onesh} \u8212- particularly {\i tzaddik ve\u8217'ra lo} \u8212- is acutely felt at the individual level, not the communal one. The {\i Maharsha} is careful to situate the terms of the {\i Gemara}\u8217's discussion without resolving the broader and more searing questions that communal tragedies \u8212- including those most recent to us \u8212- thrust upon this framework.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Shlichei Mitzvah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Not Injured\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now introduces a new apparent difficulty. Rabbi Elazar teaches: {\i shluchei mitzvah einam nizokin} \u8212- those who go out to perform a {\i mitzvah} are not injured along the way. This statement seems to operate in a very practical, this-worldly register: those engaged in {\i mitzvot} enjoy divine protection from harm. But this immediately creates a tension: if we have just concluded, following Rabbi Yaakov, that all the Torah\u8217's promises of benefit are for {\i olam ha-ba}, how do we account for Rabbi Elazar\u8217's teaching, which plainly implies protection in this world? And more concretely \u8212- what happened to the child who fell from the ladder? He was engaged in a {\i mitzvah}, and yet he was harmed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Fixed Danger\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} offers an initial resolution: {\i hatam be\u8217'halichatan shani} \u8212- the case of the child was different, because he was on his way {\i back} from the {\i mitzvah}, not in the midst of performing it. But the {\i Gemara} immediately rejects this, since Rabbi Elazar explicitly extends the protection to both going and returning \u8212- {\i lo be\u8217'halichatan velo be\u8217'chazirasom}. The {\i Gemara} therefore offers a different answer: {\i sula ra\u8217'ua hava} \u8212- the ladder was rickety. The principle is {\i dekevia hezeika} \u8212- where there is a fixed and established danger, one cannot rely on a miracle for protection. {\i Vechol heicha dekevia hezeika lo samchinan anisa} \u8212- wherever there is a fixed and predictable risk, we do not depend on a {\i nes} to save us. Rabbi Elazar\u8217's guarantee of protection applies to unexpected, unforeseeable harm \u8212- not to situations where the person has voluntarily entered into objectively dangerous circumstances. This principle also carries a significant implication for the original puzzle: the Torah\u8217's promise of long life cannot be invoked by someone who acts negligently and exposes himself to unwarranted risk. One cannot cite divine protection as a license for recklessness.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Samuel Example\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} offers a scriptural illustration of this principle from the story of Samuel. When God commanded Samuel to anoint David as king, Samuel cried out: {\i eich elech ve-shama Shaul ve-harogani} \u8212- how can I go, when Saul will hear and kill me? Samuel was engaged in a {\i mitzvah} of the highest order \u8212- anointing a king at God\u8217's direct command. Why did he not simply rely on the principle that {\i shluchei mitzvah einam nizokin}? The answer is {\i kevia hezeika shani} \u8212- the danger was real and fixed. Saul\u8217's murderous jealousy was not an unlikely or unforeseeable hazard; it was a concrete and predictable threat. Rabbi Elazar\u8217's protection is from the unexpected; it does not generate miracles in the face of genuine, established danger. One might ask whether Samuel\u8217's very fear represents a failing, given the principle in another context that {\i mora lo ya\u8217'aleh al rosho}. A distinction can be drawn: being afraid and taking necessary precautions is not the same as being paralyzed by fear. But the question is not easily dismissed.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Acher Apostasy\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Yosef\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now introduces a dramatic statement from Rav Yosef: {\i ilmalei darsha Acher lehai kra ke-Rabbi Yaakov bar barteh lo chata} \u8212- had Acher, Elisha ben Abuya, known how to interpret the relevant verse the way Rabbi Yaakov, the son of his daughter, interpreted it, he would not have sinned. Several things are striking here. First, this passage attributes Acher\u8217's apostasy to a theological failure \u8212- his inability to reconcile what he witnessed in the real world with the Torah\u8217's apparent promises of reward. This sits alongside the famous account in {\i Chagigah} that attributes his heresy to his experiences in {\i pardes}, to what he beheld in the supernal realm. One may suggest that both are true \u8212- that his mystical experience planted seeds of doubt, which were then watered by his encounter with manifest injustice in the world; or conversely, that his shattering experience of injustice made him vulnerable to a misreading of what he had seen on high. But on the reading presented here, what ultimately drove Acher to apostasy was his inability to interpret the Torah\u8217's promises in the way Rabbi Yaakov had \u8212- as directed toward {\i olam ha-ba} rather than this world. Second, a student noted \u8212- and this is confirmed by {\i Tosafot} \u8212- that Rabbi Yaakov was in fact Acher\u8217's grandson, the son of his daughter. The poignancy of this is unmistakable: the very theological resolution that might have saved the grandfather was articulated by the grandson, who wrestled with the same agonizing question and remained within the fold.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Acher Stories\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} then asks: {\i ve-Acher mai hu} \u8212- what precisely did Acher witness that broke him? Two accounts are offered. Some say he witnessed the very case we discussed \u8212- a child being sent by his father to perform {\i shiluach ha-ken}, who then died. Others say it was something altogether different and more visceral.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chutzpit\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The second account describes what Acher saw regarding the tongue of Chutzpit the Meturgeman, one of the martyrs of the Hadrianic persecution. {\i De-hava garer lei davar acher} \u8212- a pig was dragging it through the refuse heaps. Acher exclaimed: {\i amar peh she-hefik margaliot} \u8212- the mouth from which pearls once emerged, the mouth of the greatest teacher, {\i yelachech afar} \u8212- shall now be licking the dust? For Acher, this was the final rupture. Where were God\u8217's promises? Where was justice? His faith shattered, {\i nafak chata} \u8212- he went out and sinned. The {\i Gemara} presents this as the alternative to the first account, though, as noted, both experiences may have contributed cumulatively to his collapse. What is clear is that his crisis was genuinely theological \u8212- the apparent refutation of divine justice in the most brutal possible terms \u8212- and that his tragedy lies in not having possessed the interpretive key that his own grandson would later articulate.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Active vs.\u160 ?Passive\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rami Contradiction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rami bar Tuvia bar Rav Kisna posed a contradiction to Rava. The {\i mishna} states that the four categories of people who possess all the relevant merits \u8212- one who does a single {\i mitzvah} \u8212- are benefited thereby. The formulation {\i assah in, lo assah lo} implies that only active performance generates merit; passive non-performance does not. Yet a {\i baraita} states: {\i yashav ve-lo avar aveirah} \u8212- if a person simply sits and passively refrains from transgression, {\i notnin lo sechar ke-oseh mitzvah} \u8212- he is rewarded as though he performed an active {\i mitzvah}. This seems to imply that one can accumulate {\i mitzvot} simply by staying home and doing nothing objectionable \u8212- a conclusion that is both intuitively strange and in direct contradiction with the {\i mishna}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Temptation Resistance\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} resolves the contradiction with a crucial distinction. {\i Hatam kegon she-ba\u8217'at davar aveirah leyado ve-nitzal himena} \u8212- the {\i baraita} does not describe a person who merely happened not to sin because the opportunity never arose. It describes a person to whom the temptation came, who encountered the pull of transgression directly, and who found the strength to resist. This is not simply the absence of a bad act \u8212- it is an act of moral fortitude, and it therefore earns a reward equivalent to performing a positive {\i mitzvah}. The analogy is instructive: a {\i mitzvat lo ta\u8217'aseh} normally means one simply refrained from doing something wrong, which is quite different from actively doing something good, much like a {\i mitzvat aseh}. But when genuine temptation presents itself and one overcomes it, that resistance itself constitutes an act \u8212- an act of the will and character that carries the weight of positive performance.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 To illustrate this, the {\i Gemara} cites the case of Rav Chanina bar Pappa. A certain {\i matronita} \u8212- a Roman woman of rank and power \u8212- propositioned him. The power differential made direct refusal dangerous. He uttered a certain mystical word, causing his body to be covered with boils and sores, rendering himself repugnant so that she would leave him alone. She, however, also possessed knowledge of mystical arts, and she reversed his condition, healing him. Now without recourse, Rav Chanina fled and hid in a certain {\i bei banei} \u8212- a bathhouse that was known to be so haunted by {\i mazikin} that even two people entering it in daytime would be harmed. He preferred the company of demons to the risk of succumbing to her. The next morning, the rabbis asked him who had protected him through the night. He replied that two of Caesar\u8217's soldiers had guarded him. The rabbis immediately drew the inference: {\i shema davar ervah ba le-yadcha ve-nitzalta himeno} \u8212- perhaps you were confronted with sexual temptation and were delivered from it? For the tradition teaches: {\i kol ha-ba davar ervah le-yado ve-nitzal himeno, osin lo nes} \u8212- anyone to whom sexual temptation comes and who is saved from it, a miracle is performed on his behalf. And of course, that is precisely what had occurred.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Temptation Stories\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Tzadok\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} introduces a verse: {\i gibbore koach osei devaro lishmoa be-kol devaro} \u8212- mighty ones who do God\u8217's word, who hearken to the voice of His word. This is read as a reference to those who display heroic valor in resisting temptation \u8212- {\i kegon Rabbi Tzadok ve-chaverav}, like Rabbi Tzadok and his colleagues.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Tzadok was similarly propositioned by a {\i matronita}. Recognizing that direct refusal was dangerous \u8212- she was a powerful woman, and the word {\i matronita} alone signals her social power \u8212- he played for time. He said: {\i chalash li libbai}, my heart is weak, {\i lo matzinna}, I do not have the strength for such relations; {\i ika midi le-meichal}, do you have something to eat? She replied that there was forbidden food in the house \u8212- pork. His response was striking: {\i mai nafka mina de\u8217'avid ha achul ha} \u8212- what difference does it make? One who does this will eat this. If I am to be brought so low as to violate one prohibition, I might as well be brought low enough to violate another as well. This can be read on multiple levels: as a lament about the gravity of what she was asking, or as an attempt to communicate to her the weight of what she was demanding \u8212- that for him, both acts are equivalent sins against God. She apparently misread him, taking him at face value and going to heat up the pork. {\i Salik ve-yativ be-gavah} \u8212- he rose and sat inside the oven itself. He was prepared to die rather than transgress. She asked him, {\i mai hai} \u8212- what is this? He replied: {\i de\u8217'avid ha nafil be-ha} \u8212- one who commits such a sin falls into such a fire. He would face {\i gehinnom}. Her response was remarkable: {\i ei yadei kulei hai lo tza\u8217'artich} \u8212- had I known it was so weighty to you, I would not have put you through this.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Kahana\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Kahana was a wicker-basket seller. A {\i matronita} propositioned him while he was in a compromised position \u8212- unable to simply refuse. He told her: {\i izil ikashit nafshai} \u8212- let me go make myself presentable. He went up to the roof, and from the roof he fell \u8212- or more precisely, he jumped \u8212- to the ground below. The {\i Gemara} records that he fell, but given the context and the parallel with Rabbi Tzadok\u8217's act, it appears that these rabbis were prepared to take their own lives rather than transgress. This raises significant {\i halachic} and theological questions: the principle of {\i yehareg ve-al ya\u8217'avor} \u8212- be killed rather than transgress \u8212- applies when an external agent threatens death unless you violate a prohibition, but taking one\u8217's own life proactively is a different matter. The {\i Ashkenazi} {\i rishonim} grappled with precisely this question in the context of the Crusades. Whether the prohibited act here constitutes {\i giluy arayot} \u8212- which would create a {\i yehareg ve-al ya\u8217'avor} obligation \u8212- is itself debated among the {\i rishonim}. But in any case, the {\i Gemara}\u8217's framing presents this as a heroic act beyond what might normally be expected.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Elijah appeared and caught Rav Kahana in mid-air. Elijah remarked with a kind of celestial complaint: {\i atrachtan arba mea parsa} \u8212- you made me travel four hundred {\i parsa} to catch you. Rav Kahana replied: {\i mi garim li? lav aniyuta?} \u8212- what caused this to me? Was it not my poverty? I have to sell wicker baskets for a living, and that exposes me to this kind of encounter. The response came: Elijah gave him a basket full of gold coins. The rescue and reward are connected \u8212- his heroic resistance was not only acknowledged but compensated.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Heroic Resistance\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might ask what connects these narratives to the {\i pasuk} cited: {\i gibbore koach osei devaro lishmoa be-kol devaro}. The word order is curious \u8212- it says they {\i do} God\u8217's word and then {\i hearken} to it. Doing before listening? The {\i Maharsha} reads this as a precise {\i diyuk}: {\i lishmoa be-kol devaro} refers not to the person doing but to God \u8212- if you are {\i gibbore koach} who do God\u8217's work, then God {\i listens} to your voice, God hearkens to your cry. That is why these rabbis who displayed such fortitude were answered with miraculous intervention. There is also a reading in which {\i lishmoa be-kol devaro} describes a depth of internalization \u8212- not merely performing the deed but hearing and feeling the moral and religious imperative so profoundly that it provides the inner strength for these extraordinary acts of resistance when demanded.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is worth noting that these stories invert a familiar cultural script: it is the women who are the aggressors and the men who resist, an echo of the Yosef narrative. Whether this represents a conscious literary parallel or a subversion of assumed gender roles in the world of temptation, the {\i Gemara} makes clear that the capacity for moral heroism in the face of powerful temptation is what it means to be among the {\i gibbore koach} of whom the {\i pasuk} speaks.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Specific Rewards\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Bird Sending\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} returns to the theme of reaping rewards in this world. Rava posed a contradiction to Rav Nachman from the {\i mishna} in {\i Pe\u8217'ah}: {\i eilu devarim she-adam oseh otam ve-okhel peiroteihem ba-olam ha-zeh ve-ha-keren kayemet lo la-olam ha-ba} \u8212- these are the things a person does and eats their fruits in this world while the principal remains for the world to come. The list comprises honoring one\u8217's father and mother, acts of lovingkindness, bringing peace between people, and Torah study \u8212- {\i keneged kulam}, which outweighs them all. For each, a {\i pasuk} is cited demonstrating this-worldly reward: {\i kibbud av va-em} carries {\i lema\u8217'an ya\u8217'arichun yamecha u-lema\u8217'an yitav lach}; {\i gemilut chasadim} carries {\i rodef tzedakah va-chesed yimtza chayyim tzedakah ve-khavod}; making peace is connected through a {\i gezeirah shavah} of {\i redifah} linking {\i bakesh shalom ve-rodfehu} with {\i rodef tzedakah va-chesed}; and {\i talmud Torah} carries {\i ki hi chayyecha ve-orech yamecha}. But then \u8212- why is {\i shiluach ha-ken} not included? The Torah explicitly promises {\i lema\u8217'an yitav lach ve-ha\u8217'arachta yamim} for sending away the mother bird! The {\i Gemara}\u8217's answer that {\i tana ve-shayer} \u8212- the list is simply incomplete \u8212- is immediately challenged: the {\i mishna} opens with {\i eilu devarim}, \u8220"these are the things,\u8221" implying an exhaustive enumeration.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Five Mitzvot\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava then cites Rav Idi\u8217's explanation, which provides the underlying principle governing the list\u8217's selection. The {\i pasuk} in question is {\i imru tzaddik ki tov ki peri ma\u8217'alaleihem yokhelu} \u8212- say to the righteous who is good, that they will eat the fruit of their deeds. The operative phrase is {\i tzaddik ki tov}, and the {\i Gemara} now unpacks its significance: {\i vechi yesh tzaddik tov ve-yesh tzaddik she-eino tov} \u8212- is there indeed a righteous person who is good and one who is not? Yes: {\i tov la-shamayim ve-labriyot zehu tzaddik tov} \u8212- one who is good to God and to other human beings is a {\i tzaddik} who is good; {\i tov la-shamayim ve-ra la-briyot} \u8212- one who is scrupulous in his religious obligations but who treats his fellow human beings poorly \u8212- {\i zehu tzaddik she-eino tov}, that is a {\i tzaddik} who is not good.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is a striking {\i Gemara} with profound religious implications. It gives a name to a phenomenon many observe: the person who is {\i frum} in the ritual domain but morally deficient in the interpersonal one. The {\i Gemara} does not deny his status as {\i tzaddik} \u8212- it acknowledges the religious dimension \u8212- but it identifies the absence of the {\i tov}. And the theological point follows directly: the {\i mitzvot} that yield fruit in this world are precisely those that make one a {\i tzaddik ki tov} \u8212- acts that are not merely between the person and God, but that generate genuine goodness in the world. {\i Kibbud av va-em}, {\i gemilut chasadim}, {\i havaat shalom} \u8212- these are acts that concretely improve the lives of real people. {\i Shiluach ha-ken} is a legitimate {\i mitzvah} for which one is indeed rewarded, but it is a {\i mitzvah} between person and God; it does not generate goodness in the human world in the same way, and therefore it was not included in this particular list. The list is not exhaustive of all {\i mitzvot} rewarded in this world \u8212- it is a curated list of {\i mitzvot} whose reward flows naturally from the goodness they create.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Tzaddik Ki Tov\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Good vs.\u160 ?Righteous\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The distinction between {\i tzaddik tov} and {\i tzaddik she-eino tov} merits further reflection. The categories represent, in one framing, the distinction between vertical piety \u8212- the relationship between person and God \u8212- and horizontal piety \u8212- the relationship between person and fellow. A person may be exemplary in the former and deficient in the latter. The {\i Gemara} does not collapse the categories, but it insists that truly reaping the fruits of one\u8217's deeds in this world is connected specifically to the {\i tzaddik ki tov} \u8212- the person whose righteous acts are also good, that is, acts that create genuine benefit in the world.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 There is another way to read the {\i tov} here: not merely morally good, but {\i creating goodness in the world}. If one\u8217's {\i tzidkut} is a {\i tzidkut} of goodness in this world \u8212- acts that concretely help people, that improve reality, that ripple outward \u8212- then the reward in this world is not simply God\u8217's direct recompense but the natural consequence of having created a better world. The people one helped are real; the kindness one showed has effects; the peace one fostered endures. The reward is, in a sense, built into the very nature of the deed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rasha Ra\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The parallel formulation concludes the {\i derashah}: {\i oi la-rasha ra} \u8212- woe to the wicked evil one, {\i ki gemul yadav ye\u8217'aseh lo} \u8212- for the deeds of his hands shall be done to him. The {\i Gemara} asks: {\i vechi yesh rasha ra ve-yesh she-eino ra} \u8212- is there a wicked person who is evil and one who is not evil? Yes: {\i ra la-shamayim ve-labriyot hu rasha ra} \u8212- one who sins against God and against other human beings is a {\i rasha ra}; {\i ra la-shamayim ve-eino ra la-briyot} \u8212- one who transgresses against God but is good to people \u8212- {\i zeh rasha she-eino ra}, that is a transgressor who is not evil. The {\i Gemara} notes that the Hebrew word {\i rasha} already carries a moral connotation \u8212- it implies wickedness beyond mere transgression \u8212- so one might speak of a good transgressor, a person who violates religious norms but who is genuinely kind and beneficent toward others.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Merit Dividends\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Merit Structure\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i Tosefta} in {\i Pe\u8217'ah} now elaborates the underlying structure of reward and punishment, developing a contrast between the handling of good deeds and bad deeds. {\i Ha-zechut yesh la keren ve-yesh la peirot} \u8212- merit, the reward for a good deed, has both principal and dividends. The principal is deposited, as it were, for {\i olam ha-ba}, while the dividends \u8212- the fruits \u8212- are enjoyed in this world. The {\i pasuk} is again {\i imru tzaddik ki tov ki peri ma\u8217'alaleihem yokhelu} \u8212- they will eat the fruit of their deeds, implying reward beyond the mere act itself, the dividends of the principal investment.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sin Structure\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Sin, by contrast, is treated differently. {\i Aveirah yesh la keren ve-ein la peirot} \u8212- a sin has only principal, not dividends. You are paid back for the sin itself, but punishment does not ramify and extend indefinitely beyond it. The {\i pasuk} is {\i oi la-rasha ra ki gemul yadav ye\u8217'aseh lo} \u8212- {\i lo}, to him specifically, the pay-back of his own hands. The word {\i lo} signals a payment calibrated to the act, not extended beyond it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Bearing Fruit\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 However, the {\i Tosefta} confronts a contradicting verse: {\i ve-yokhelu mi-peri darkam u-mi-mo\u8217'atzoteihem yisbe\u8217'u} \u8212- they will eat from the fruits of their paths and be sated from their scheming. This speaks of {\i fruit} in the context of the wicked \u8212- implying that the wicked too can suffer the extended consequences of their sins. The resolution is the same principle encountered earlier in the discussion of {\i tzaddik ki tov}: {\i aveirah she-osah peirot yesh la peirot} \u8212- a sin that bears fruit, that has a real-world impact beyond the individual act, {\i yesh la peirot} \u8212- there the punishment extends accordingly. {\i Ve-she-einah osah peirot ein la peirot} \u8212- if the sin is contained and does not ramify into harm for others, punishment is limited to the sin itself. This is a precise parallel to the structure of reward: just as good deeds that generate goodness in the world earn dividends, bad deeds that generate harm in the world incur extended punishment. {\i Rashi} explains this to include cases where one\u8217's sin leads others to sin as well \u8212- the public transgressor who gives others license to follow his example. The underlying principle is consistent: real-world impact, in either direction, changes the magnitude of one\u8217's account.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Thought Ethics\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Good Thoughts\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now turns to a related distinction \u8212- between good thoughts and bad thoughts. {\i Machshava tova mitzarfah l\u8217'ma\u8217'aseh} \u8212- God joins a good thought to a deed, crediting it as if one had acted. The source is {\i az nidbru yirei Hashem ish el re\u8217'ehu vayakshev Hashem vayishma vayikasev sefer zikaron lifanav liyirei Hashem ulchoshvei shmo} \u8212- those who feared God spoke one to another, and God listened and heard, and a book of remembrance was written before Him for those who fear God and {\i choshvei shemo} \u8212- those who think about His name. Rav Assi derives: {\i afilu chashav adam la\u8217'asot mitzvah ve-ne\u8217'enas ve-lo asah} \u8212- even one who intended to perform a {\i mitzvah} but was prevented by circumstances beyond his control, {\i ma\u8217'aleh alav ha-katuv ke-ilu asah} \u8212- Scripture accounts it to him as though he had done it. The critical qualifier is {\i ne\u8217'enas} \u8212- he was compelled by circumstance, not by a change of heart. A person who genuinely intended to perform a {\i mitzvah} and truly tried, but was thwarted by external forces, receives full credit. The formulation does not apply to one who thought about performing a {\i mitzvah} and then thought better of it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Bad Thoughts\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The treatment of bad thoughts is markedly asymmetrical. {\i Machshava ra\u8217'ah ein Hakadosh Baruch Hu mitzarfah l\u8217'ma\u8217'aseh} \u8212- God does not join a bad thought to a deed. One is not punished merely for having entertained sinful thoughts. The {\i pasuk} is {\i avon im ra\u8217'iti be-libi lo yishma Hashem} \u8212- if I see iniquity in my heart, God does not listen \u8212- meaning God does not punish for mere thought. However, another verse says: {\i hineni mevi el ha\u8217'am hazeh ra\u8217'ah peri machshavotam} \u8212- I will bring evil to this people, the fruit of their thoughts. This appears to contradict the first: here people {\i are} punished for their thoughts. The resolution follows the same logical structure used earlier: {\i machshava she-osah pri} \u8212- a thought that bears fruit, that becomes an action, {\i Hakadosh Baruch Hu mitzarfah l\u8217'ma\u8217'aseh} \u8212- then God does join it to the deed and punishment is extended to include the thought. {\i Machshava she-ein bah pri} \u8212- a thought that does not produce action \u8212- {\i ein Hakadosh Baruch Hu mitzarfah l\u8217'ma\u8217'aseh} \u8212- is not joined to the deed and incurs no punishment.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Idolatry\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A further verse complicates this picture: {\i le-ma\u8217'an tfos et beit Yisrael be-libam} \u8212- in order to seize the house of Israel by their hearts. This appears to describe punishment for thought itself, independent of action. Rav Acha bar Yaakov explains: {\i hu be-avoda zara hu de-chtiv} \u8212- this verse refers specifically to {\i avoda zara}, idolatry, which is an exceptional case. {\i Chamura avoda zara ke-neged kol ha-mitzvot} \u8212- idolatry is as weighty as all the {\i mitzvot} combined; {\i kol ha-kofer bah ke-modeh be-chol ha-Torah kulah} \u8212- whoever repudiates idolatry is as though he has affirmed the entire Torah; and correspondingly, whoever embraces it, even in thought, has in some fundamental sense rejected everything. One may ask whether the exceptional treatment of {\i avoda zara} reflects the sheer gravity of the act, making even the thought a form of the transgression, or whether it reflects the {\i Rambam}\u8217's view that {\i avoda zara} is fundamentally about belief rather than act \u8212- so that the thought itself, the very considering of other deities, already constitutes a departure from God. On that reading, there is no clean separation between the thought of {\i avoda zara} and {\i avoda zara} itself.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Habitual Sin\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now introduces a related principle from Rav Huna. {\i Keivan she-adam avar aveirah ve-shanah bah, hutrah lo} \u8212- once a person has committed a sin and repeated it, it becomes permitted to him. The {\i Gemara} immediately asks: does it literally become permitted? {\i Ela na\u8217'aseit lo ke-heter} \u8212- no, it becomes in his own mind and experience as though it were permitted. Once a person has transgressed repeatedly, the sense of prohibition has eroded; the act no longer feels off-limits or taboo. The consequence for the laws of thought is direct: for a habitual sinner, the plan to sin is itself punishable, because at this stage the gap between planning and doing has effectively closed. If a person who has violated a prohibition twice is now planning to do so again, his restraint at any given moment is most likely circumstantial \u8212- not a genuine act of resistance, but merely a missed opportunity. {\i Rashi} states this explicitly: at this stage, if someone did not commit the sin, it was probably only because the opportunity was not available. Therefore God counts the plan as the act. This contrasts with the general rule \u8212- for the ordinary person, who has not habituated himself to a given sin, planning to transgress does not incur punishment if the action is not carried out.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Sin Privacy\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Private Sin\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Abbahu in the name of Rabbi Chanina: {\i noach lo le-adam she-ya\u8217'avor aveirah be-seter ve-al yechallel shem Shamayim be-farhesya} \u8212- it is better for a person to commit a transgression in private than to desecrate God\u8217's name in public. The principle is rooted in the danger of {\i chillul Hashem} \u8212- of the public disgrace and its consequences. When a visibly observant person sins openly, the transgression is not merely a violation between himself and God; it becomes a {\i davar she-osah peirot}, a sin that bears fruit, that impacts how others see God and Torah, and potentially licenses others to transgress as well. Better to commit one {\i aveirah} than two. The {\i pasuk} offered is from {\i Yechezkel}: {\i ish gilulav lechu avdu ve-achar im einchem shome\u8217'im elai ve-et shem kodshi lo techallalu} \u8212- go worship your idols if you will not listen to Me, but do not desecrate My holy Name.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Anonymity\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Ilai HaZaken elaborates with practical instruction: {\i im ro\u8217'eh adam she-yitzro mitgaber alav}, if a person sees that his {\i yetzer hara} is overpowering him, {\i yeilech le-makom she-ein makirim oto} \u8212- let him go to a place where he is not recognized, {\i ve-yilbash shechorim ve-yitkaseh shechorim} \u8212- dress in dark clothes and cover himself in dark clothing, {\i ve-ya\u8217'aseh mah she-libbo chafetz} \u8212- and do as his heart desires, {\i ve-al yechallel shem Shamayim be-farhesya} \u8212- but he shall not desecrate God\u8217's name publicly. {\i Rashi} notes that dressing in black and covering oneself signals mourning or social marginality, prompting others to keep their distance and thus affording anonymity. Some {\i rishonim}, citing {\i Rabbeinu Chananel}, suggest that the process itself \u8212- donning dark clothing, going to an unfamiliar place \u8212- will diminish the desire; but the plain meaning of {\i ya\u8217'aseh mah she-libbo chafetz} does not support reading this as mere misdirection toward the intended act. No license is being granted; the person is still sinning. What is being addressed is the {\i manner} of sinning, given that the sin will occur in any case.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This teaching raises practical questions that echo through later {\i halachic} discussion. If the availability of a private sinning option removes the only social barrier that might have prevented the sin entirely, then the advice is counterproductive. The case of the {\i yarmulke} in the movie theater \u8212- a question put to Rav Moshe Feinstein \u8212- captures exactly this tension: should one remove the {\i kippah} to avoid {\i chillul Hashem}, or keep it on in the hope that it will deter further transgression? Both considerations are real, and the resolution is not obvious.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Theological Problem\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now raises the deeper objection. {\i Ein makifin be-chillul Hashem}, we are told elsewhere. But more fundamentally: is sinning in private really a solution? {\i Ve-hatanya: kol shelo chas al kavod kono ra\u8217'uy lo shelo ba la-olam} \u8212- anyone who does not show concern for God\u8217's honor is unworthy of having come into the world. Rav Yosef identifies the person described here as {\i zeh ha-over aveirah be-seter} \u8212- one who sins in private. The theological problem is acute: sinning in private, under the assumption that only other people can observe you, demonstrates either a belief that God cannot see you in private or a greater concern for human perception than for divine perception. It is a form of {\i chillul Hashem} in itself \u8212- a practical denial of God\u8217's omniscience or a statement about one\u8217's hierarchy of concerns.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ability Distinction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} resolves the apparent contradiction: {\i lo kashya \u8212- ha de-matsi kayif lei le-yitzrei, ve-ha de-la matsi kayif lei le-yitzrei} \u8212- it is not difficult: the two teachings refer to two different situations. If a person {\i has the ability to overcome his }yetzer hara** and restrain himself, then choosing to sin in private is itself a theological transgression. The privacy is what gives him license; without it, he would not sin. In that case, sin in private is {\i chillul Hashem}, because it demonstrates that one\u8217's real deterrent is social rather than divine. If, however, a person genuinely {\i cannot} overcome his {\i yetzer hara} and will commit the sin in any case, then at least doing it privately limits the damage \u8212- the sin does not become a {\i davar she-osah peirot}, does not spread, does not license others. In that case, the private sin, while still a transgression, spares the public the {\i chillul Hashem}. The difficulty, practically speaking, is that we rarely know which category we are in \u8212- and the very act of choosing the private option often shapes which category we occupy.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Chillul Hashem\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Unbalanceable\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} turns to a teaching from a {\i mishna} cited from elsewhere: {\i ein makifin be-chillul Hashem, echad shogeg ve-echad mezid} \u8212- we are not {\i makif} regarding {\i chillul Hashem}, whether the transgressor acted unintentionally or intentionally. Two interpretations of {\i makifin} are offered. Mar Zutra says: {\i she-ein osin ke-chanvani} \u8212- God does not extend credit; {\i hakafah} means putting on account, as a storekeeper might do for a trusted customer. {\i Chillul Hashem} is not deferred \u8212- it is collected immediately. Mar berei de-Ravina interprets {\i makifin} differently: {\i she-im hayta shekula machra\u8217'at} \u8212- even if a person\u8217's merits and demerits are perfectly balanced, {\i chillul Hashem} tips the scales. It is not balanced out by other merits. A person who is otherwise fifty-fifty but has a {\i chillul Hashem} on his account does not remain fifty-fifty \u8212- the scale tilts toward liability. {\i Chillul Hashem} is, in this sense, unbalanceable: it cannot be offset by accumulated merit on the other side.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Half-Balance\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Fifty-Fifty\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now presents a teaching from the Rabbis that has direct thematic connection to the preceding discussion: {\i le-olam yir\u8217'eh adam atzmo ke-ilu chetzyo chayav ve-chetzyo zakai} \u8212- a person should always see himself as though he is half liable and half meritorious. This is a remarkable psychological and theological prescription. The stakes of every single act are thereby made vivid: no one may coast on accumulated credit, and no one may despair at accumulated debt.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 One Act\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The teaching continues: {\i asa mitzvah achat}, if he performs one {\i mitzvah}, {\i ashrav} \u8212- praised is he, {\i she-hichria atzmo le-chaf zechut} \u8212- for he has tipped the scales in the direction of merit. {\i Avar aveirah achat oy lo}, if he commits one sin, {\i she-hichria atzmo le-chaf chova} \u8212- he has tipped the scales toward liability. The verse offered is {\i ve-chotei echad ye\u8217'aved tova harbe} \u8212- one sinner, or one sin, destroys great good. {\i Bishvil cheit yechidi she-chata, oved mimenu tovot harbe} \u8212- through a single sin, one loses great good. The weight of a single act \u8212- in either direction \u8212- is not diminished by the lifetime of acts that precede it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Majority Rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Elazar the son of Rabbi Shimon adds the communal dimension: {\i lefi she-ha-olam nidon achar rubo, ve-ha-yachid nidon achar rubo} \u8212- the world is judged according to its majority, and the individual is judged according to his majority. The question is always: overall, is this person \u8212- is this world \u8212- a good one? And because both the individual and the world should be seen as standing precisely at fifty-fifty, a single act by a single person carries maximal weight. {\i Asa mitzvah achat, she-hichria et atzmo ve-et kol ha-olam le-chaf zechut} \u8212- one {\i mitzvah} tips both the individual and the entire world toward merit. {\i Bishvil cheit yechidi she-asah zeh, ibeid mimenu u-mi-kol ha-olam tovah harbe} \u8212- through a single sin, a person can bring himself and the entire world into deficit. The analogy to electoral politics is apt: a single vote in a single precinct that shifts a state and determines an election is only decisive because the underlying distribution was already so close. The teaching assumes that closeness as the baseline.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Final Day\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Righteous Rebellion\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} \u8212- or rather Rabbi Shimon ben Yochai\u8217's teaching in this context \u8212- introduces a startling claim: {\i afilu tzaddik gamur kol yamav u-marad b\u8217'acharonah, ibeid et ha-rishonot} \u8212- even a person who was entirely righteous all his days, if he rebels at the end, he loses all the earlier good. The verse is {\i tzidkat ha-tzaddik lo tatzilenu be-yom pish\u8217'o} \u8212- the righteousness of the righteous person will not save him on the day of his transgression.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Wicked Repentance\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The symmetrical case: {\i afilu rasha gamur kol yamav ve-asah teshuvah be-acharonah, ein mazkirin lo shuv rish\u8217'o} \u8212- even a person who was entirely wicked all his days, if he repents at the end, his wickedness is no longer recalled. The verse is {\i ve-rish\u8217'at ha-rasha lo yikashel bah be-yom shuvo me-rish\u8217'o} \u8212- the wickedness of the wicked person will not cause him to stumble on the day he returns from his wickedness.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gemara Challenge\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The case of {\i teshuvah} at the end of life is, in one sense, consonant with the tradition\u8217's understanding of {\i teshuvah}\u8217's power. One may still ask how it accounts for real harm caused to others over a lifetime \u8212- but at least between the individual and God, the tradition insists that {\i teshuvah} is always efficacious. The case of last-day rebellion is far more troubling. The {\i Gemara} presses the obvious difficulty: {\i ve-nehevei ke-mechtza avonot u-mechtza zachuyot} \u8212- let the person at least be considered fifty-fifty! He has ninety years of righteous deeds and one day of rebellion. Why should that single day of rebellion render him entirely wicked and destroy all prior merit?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Reish Lakish\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Reish Lakish answers: {\i b\u8217'toheh al ha-rishonot} \u8212- we are speaking of a case where, on that final day, the person {\i regrets} his earlier righteous life. He does not merely rebel; he retroactively repudiates the good he had done. This is the inverse of {\i teshuvah}. {\i Teshuvah} is effective because genuine regret retroactively undoes the past \u8212- the sinner says, had I known better, I would not have done those things, and this sincere retroactive rejection changes the status of the earlier acts. In the same structural logic, a righteous person who ends his life saying, what was the point of all my years of righteousness? I should have lived for pleasure \u8212- has retroactively rejected his earlier deeds. By his own declaration, those deeds no longer represent who he truly was. The deeds are, in a sense, disowned.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The question remains, however, and it presses equally on the {\i teshuvah} side of the ledger: what of the real-world effects of those years? The Torah one taught, the kindnesses one performed, the peace one brought \u8212- those affected real people and had real consequences. Can a change of heart at the end retroactively undo what was concretely accomplished? This is one of the deep unresolved tensions in the theology of {\i teshuvah} and its inverse, and the {\i Gemara} does not offer a clean resolution.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Three-Fold Cord\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Non-Sinners\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The final {\i mishna} of the first {\i perek} opens with the phrase {\i kol} \u8212- anyone \u8212- and describes those who are engaged in {\i mikra}, {\i mishna}, and {\i derech eretz}: {\i lo bimehera hu chote} \u8212- such a person does not sin quickly. The three elements are worth distinguishing. {\i Mishna} represents {\i halacha} \u8212- the practical, apodictic teaching of how to live; it is the normative structure of Jewish life. {\i Mikra} represents the religious connection \u8212- engagement with {\i Tanach} as a mode of connecting to God, to the narrative of the covenant, to the prophetic voice. And {\i derech eretz} \u8212- productive engagement with the world, a profession, real involvement in the workings of human life \u8212- keeps one connected to reality and, in its way, also keeps one from sin. The verse cited is {\i ve-ha-chut ha-meshulash lo bimehera yinatek} \u8212- the three-fold cord is not quickly broken.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Outsiders\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The inverse: {\i ve-khol she-eino lo be-mikra ve-lo be-mishna ve-lo be-derech eretz, eino min ha-yishuv} \u8212- whoever lacks all three is not part of settled society. Without a connection to God through {\i mikra}, without the normative structure of {\i halacha} through {\i mishna}, and without genuine engagement in the productive world, a person is on the margins \u8212- not really integrated into the fabric of the community.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Tree Parable\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tzaddik Tree\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Elazar bar Tzadok concludes the {\i perek} with a parable. {\i Lamma tzaddikim nimshalim ba-olam ha-zeh le-ilan} \u8212- why are righteous people in this world compared to a tree? The tree is rooted in a place of {\i tahara} \u8212- purity \u8212- but one branch extends into a place of {\i tuma}. {\i Niktzatz nofo} \u8212- cut that branch back, {\i ve-kulo omed be-makom tahara} \u8212- and the whole tree stands in purity.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Suffering Purpose\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Kacha Hakadosh Baruch Hu mevi yissurin al tzaddikim ba-olam ha-zeh kede sheyirshu ha-olam ha-ba} \u8212- so too, God brings suffering upon righteous people in this world so that they may inherit the World to Come. The {\i tzaddik} is essentially rooted in {\i olam ha-ba} \u8212- that is where he truly belongs, that is his real soil. The suffering he experiences in this world trims away whatever has extended into the wrong place. It is not injustice but restoration \u8212- returning the {\i tzaddik} to the fullness of what he truly is, so that when he arrives at {\i olam ha-ba} he is fully himself. The verse: {\i reshitcha mitzar va-achritcha yisgeh me\u8217'od} \u8212- your beginning will be constrained, but your end will flourish greatly.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rasha Tree\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The inverse parable: {\i ve-lamma resha\u8217'im domim ba-olam ha-zeh le-ilan} \u8212- why are the wicked in this world compared to a tree? Their tree is rooted in a place of {\i tuma}, but a single branch extends into a place of {\i tahara}. {\i Niktzatz nofo} \u8212- cut that branch back, {\i ve-kulo omed be-makom tuma} \u8212- and the entire tree stands in impurity. {\i Kacha Hakadosh Baruch Hu mashpia lahem tova la-resha\u8217'im ba-olam ha-zeh kede le-hordam le-darga tachtona} \u8212- God showers the wicked with goodness in this world in order to bring them to the lowest levels of {\i gehinnom}. The small branch of goodness \u8212- whatever genuine good they had \u8212- is trimmed away, and what remains is the root: the core of what they truly were. The verse: {\i yesh derech yashar lifnei ish ve-achritah darkhei mavet} \u8212- there is a path that seems straight before a man, but its end is the ways of death.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 With this {\i Gemara}, the first {\i perek} of {\i Kiddushin} comes to a close \u8212- a chapter dense with theology: the nature of reward and punishment, the challenge of righteous suffering, the weight of individual acts on the cosmic scale, and the relationship between who we are and what we do, in this world and in the next.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Kiddushin 41\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Merit & Mitzvot\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Intro & Prior Mishnah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Daf 41 opens at the very bottom of 40b, in the last of the narrow lines, continuing a discussion about the weight of {\i mitzvot} and their consequences for the individual. The previous {\i mishnah} had addressed anyone who performs a single {\i mitzvah}, enumerating the great good that follows, and conversely, the grave consequences of failing to perform one. The {\i Gemara} there interpreted this somewhat narrowly \u8212- the case of someone exactly balanced at fifty-fifty, for whom a single {\i mitzvah} tips the scales. That may seem like a narrow reading, since the {\i mishnah} appears to assign enormous weight to any single {\i mitzvah} regardless of context. But the {\i Gemara}\u8217's framing is itself profound: if one is trained to regard oneself as always standing at perfect equilibrium \u8212- exactly half merit, half demerit \u8212- then every {\i mitzvah} one is about to perform carries enormous consequence, as does every {\i mitzvah} one neglects.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Learning & Character\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} that follows shifts from {\i mitzvot} in the narrow sense to a broader triad: {\i mikra}, {\i mishnah}, and {\i derech eretz}. One who is engaged with all three, the {\i mishnah} teaches, will not quickly sin \u8212- {\i lo bimhera hu chotei}. The three components map onto distinct dimensions of religious life. {\i Mikra} \u8212- Tanakh \u8212- orients a person toward God; it is a framework of religious consciousness. {\i Mishnah} corresponds to {\i halakhah}, the legal-observational dimension. {\i Derech eretz} admits of two interpretations. One reading, associated with the {\i Rambam} and developed extensively in his writings, is that {\i derech eretz} means having a livelihood, being embedded in the real world of labor and production \u8212- a corrective to the insularity that purely theoretical study can produce. The {\i Rambam} speaks at length about the dangers of being supported by the community without engaging in productive work. A second reading, which the {\i Rambam} himself gives in his {\i Perush HaMishnayot}, understands {\i derech eretz} as proper conduct between people \u8212- good character traits and refined interpersonal behavior. On this reading, the triad encompasses intellectual-religious formation ({\i mikra}), legal observance ({\i mishnah}), and character ({\i derech eretz}).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} is not primarily about reward in this world or the next; it is about what protects a person and keeps him on the right path. Without these three elements, the {\i mishnah} concludes, one is {\i eino min hayishuv} \u8212- not part of civilized society. With that framing in place, the {\i Gemara}\u8217's next movement is natural: having discussed {\i mitzvot}, {\i mikra}, {\i mishnah}, and {\i derech eretz}, it turns to the question of which among them is most fundamental.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Talmud vs.\u160 ?Maaseh\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Question in Lud\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} at the close of 40b records the following: Rabbi Tarfon and the elders were reclining in the upper chamber of the house of Nitzah in Lud \u8212- an almost Haggadah-like setting, evoking the famous scene in Bnei Brak. The question posed before them was: {\i talmud gadol o ma\u8217'aseh gadol} \u8212- which is greater, learning or doing?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tarfon vs.\u160 ?Akiva\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Tarfon answered that {\i ma\u8217'aseh} \u8212- action, performance of {\i mitzvot} \u8212- is greater. Rabbi Akiva answered that {\i talmud} \u8212- learning \u8212- is greater.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Consensus Reached\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The assembly then reached a consensus: {\i talmud gadol sheh-hatalmud meivi lidei ma\u8217'aseh} \u8212- learning is greater, because learning brings one to action. The formulation is striking. The reasoning offered in support of {\i talmud}\u8217's greatness is itself an argument that {\i ma\u8217'aseh} is the ultimate goal. Learning is declared superior not because it has independent, intrinsic primacy, but because it is the instrument that generates action. At the axiomatic level, then, it almost sounds as though {\i ma\u8217'aseh} is what matters most \u8212- {\i talmud} is a supremely important {\i hechsher mitzvah}, a preparatory act that makes the real thing possible.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafot\u8217's Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This tension is precisely what {\i Tosafot} identifies. The {\i Tosafot} opens with {\i teimah} \u8212- a difficulty \u8212- drawn from a {\i Gemara} at the end of the first chapter of Bava Kamma. There, Rabbi Yochanan washed his hands after using the bathroom, put on {\i tefillin}, and taught the following: at a {\i hesped}, a eulogy, we say {\i kiyyem} \u8212- this person fulfilled a life of {\i mitzvos} \u8212- but we do not say {\i limeid}, that he taught Torah. The {\i Gemara} there asks: but since we say {\i talmud gadol she-hatalmud meivi lidei ma\u8217'aseh}, shouldn\u8217't it be an even greater compliment to say that someone taught? Why would {\i limeid} not be said?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rashi explains the {\i Gemara}\u8217's question there as follows: {\i mi nitlah b\u8217'mi? kattan nitlah b\u8217'gadol} \u8212- the lesser depends on the greater. Since the {\i Gemara} says {\i talmud meivi lidei ma\u8217'aseh}, that actually betrays the fact that {\i ma\u8217'aseh} is the fundamentally superior category. If so, and we are willing to praise someone as a {\i ba\u8217'al ma\u8217'asim}, a person of {\i mitzvos}, should we not be all the more willing to praise him as someone who learned Torah \u8212- since {\i kiyyem} is already the greater compliment? The implication, then, is that {\i ma\u8217'aseh} is genuinely {\i gadol}, and the {\i Gemara} in Bava Kamma takes our {\i Gemara} as evidence of exactly that. {\i Tosafot} presses this: from that {\i Gemara}, it appears that declaring {\i talmud meivi lidei ma\u8217'aseh} actually concedes the supremacy of {\i ma\u8217'aseh} \u8212- so which is it?\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Hierarchy Analysis\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Personal vs.\u160 ?Teaching\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} resolves the tension by distinguishing between different contexts. The key line is: {\i ha l\u8217'migmar ha l\u8217'agmurei} \u8212- this applies to one\u8217's own learning, that applies to teaching others. {\i Klomar}: if the comparison is between your own private learning and your own performance of {\i mitzvot}, then {\i ma\u8217'aseh} is indeed preferable. {\i L\u8217'migmar l\u8217'didei vadai ma\u8217'aseh} \u8212- when it comes to your own learning for yourself, doing is certainly more important. But when the comparison is between {\i ma\u8217'aseh} and teaching others \u8212- {\i l\u8217'agmurei achrinei vadai adif tfei mi-ma\u8217'aseh} \u8212- teaching others is at a higher level still, and that is why we do not routinely say {\i limeid} at a {\i hesped}, since not everyone reaches that level. The result is a hierarchy: one\u8217's own private learning is the lowest tier; performing {\i mitzvot} is the next; and teaching others is the highest religious achievement of all. If you must choose between going to perform a {\i mitzvah} and going to open a {\i Gemara} for your own study, you perform the {\i mitzvah}. But if you must choose between a {\i mitzvah} and delivering a {\i shiur}, you deliver the {\i shiur}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Pedagogy Approach\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A second interpretive approach within {\i Tosafot} reframes the {\i Gemara}\u8217's question entirely. Perhaps the debate was not about axiomatic religious priority at all, but about pedagogy \u8212- what is the right course of religious education for someone just beginning? Should one first focus on learning, or on doing more {\i mitzvos}? The answer, under this reading, is that one should learn first \u8212- {\i ein am ha-aretz chasid}, an unlearned person cannot be truly pious \u8212- spend time learning the {\i Mishnah Berurah}, understand what is required, and then translate that into action. But for someone who has already learned, {\i ha-ma\u8217'aseh tov yoter mi-limmud} \u8212- doing is better than further private study. This approach maintains the hierarchy: ultimately it is about doing; learning is the vehicle that brings it about.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Talmud Gadol\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 On this reading, to say {\i talmud gadol} means only that learning is the practically superior starting point, not that it is axiomatically supreme. {\i Ma\u8217'aseh} remains the end; {\i talmud} is the preferred means, because {\i talmud} brings to {\i ma\u8217'aseh} while {\i ma\u8217'aseh} does not reciprocally bring to {\i talmud}. This is a practical preference, not a statement of fundamental ontological superiority.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Talmud Torah Theology\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Talmud Torah Keneged\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This question does not rest here. For those who invest their entire lives in {\i yeshiva} and {\i kollel}, in uninterrupted learning and teaching, the formulation {\i talmud meivi lidei ma\u8217'aseh} raises a serious challenge. It is worth noting that {\i talmud Torah keneged kulam} \u8212- as it appeared in yesterday\u8217's {\i Gemara} \u8212- did not mean that the most important {\i mitzvah} to perform is {\i talmud Torah}. The context of that list was {\i ochel peiroteihen ba-olam hazeh} \u8212- things whose benefits one enjoys in this world. {\i Talmud Torah keneged kulam} in that context may simply mean that among all the things whose reward one reaps in this world, learning Torah produces the most such reward \u8212- precisely {\i because talmud meivi lidei ma\u8217'aseh}, because that learning translates into doing, shapes character, and transforms one\u8217's relationship to other people. It is not necessarily a declaration that learning is intrinsically the most important {\i mitzvah}; it may mean that learning is the most generative.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kodashim Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbeinu Yonah raises a pointed challenge: if it is all about {\i ma\u8217'aseh}, if the whole purpose of learning is to translate into action, then why do we learn {\i Masechet Kodashim} \u8212- the entire {\i Seder Kodashim}? Why do we learn all these {\i masechtos} dealing with laws that are simply not actionable today? One answer offered is that even abstract {\i halacha} translates into {\i yirat shamayim} and shapes character traits. But Rabbeinu Yonah\u8217's answer is notably not that {\i talmud Torah} is valuable even without {\i ma\u8217'aseh} \u8212- his answer remains firmly within the framework that learning\u8217's purpose is ultimately to shape a religious life.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This position finds expression in the {\i mishnah} in {\i Pirkei Avot}: {\i lo ha-midrash hu ha-ikar ela ha-ma\u8217'aseh}. Interestingly, Rabbeinu Yonah himself \u8212- who elsewhere argues that learning must translate into doing \u8212- interprets that line from {\i Pirkei Avot} quite differently. He reads it as: you do not primarily influence and teach people through what you say. You influence them through what you do. The injunction is not about the hierarchy of learning versus action in the abstract, but about the mechanism of religious influence: modeling through behavior is more powerful than instruction through speech.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two Readings\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 There is a second, fundamentally different way of reading our {\i Gemara} \u8212- one that does not subordinate learning to action. On this reading, both {\i talmud} and {\i ma\u8217'aseh} are {\i mitzvos}, each intrinsically important. The debate is simply: which {\i mitzvah} is greater \u8212- shaking a {\i lulav} or learning {\i Gemara}? And the answer is that learning {\i Gemara} is greater because it is, as it were, a {\i twofer}: you fulfill the {\i mitzvah} of {\i talmud Torah} and you also generate other {\i mitzvos}. Shaking a {\i lulav} does not typically lead to additional {\i mitzvos}; learning does. On this reading, {\i talmud} does not begin at a lower level than {\i ma\u8217'aseh} and earn superiority only through its instrumental power. Rather, it starts at an equal level and then surpasses {\i ma\u8217'aseh} because it also carries the capacity to bring about additional doing. These are two genuinely distinct theological positions regarding the relationship between {\i talmud Torah} and {\i mitzvos} \u8212- and this {\i Gemara} sits at the heart of that debate.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Yosi\u8217's Chronology\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Timeline of Mitzvot\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} continues with a {\i baraita}: Rabbi Yosi says {\i gadol talmud she-kodam la-challah arbaim shanah} \u8212- learning is great in that it preceded the {\i mitzvah} of {\i challah} by forty years. The Torah commanded {\i challah}, but the actual performance of that {\i mitzvah} did not begin until the Jewish people entered the land. Between the giving of the Torah and the first {\i challah} taken was forty years. For {\i terumot u-ma\u8217'asrot}, 54 years, since those {\i mitzvos} began only after fourteen years of conquest and settlement following entry into the land. For {\i shemitah}, 61 years \u8212- fourteen years of entering and settling the land, then beginning to count the seven-year {\i shemitah} cycle. For {\i yovel}, 103 years \u8212- since {\i yovel} takes effect at the beginning of its fiftieth year, {\i kasavar yovel mi-techilaso hu mashmit}, meaning the effect of {\i yovel} kicks in at the start of year fifty, which falls at the end of 49 years of counting after the 54-year baseline, yielding 103.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Learning Precedence\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The point is that just as learning preceded the doing \u8212- the Torah was taught and studied long before these {\i mitzvos} could be performed \u8212- so too, judgment and reward for learning come first. This connects to a principle of Rav Hamnuna: {\i ein techilat dino shel adam ela al divrei Torah} \u8212- the first thing a person is judged for is his learning of Torah, as derived from the verse {\i peter mayim reshit madon}. And correspondingly, just as one is judged first for learning, one is rewarded first for learning: {\i va-yiten lahem artzos goyim va-amal le\u8217'umim yirashu} \u8212- God gave them the lands of the nations and the labor of the peoples \u8212- {\i ba-avur yishmeru chukav ve-torasav yintzoru} \u8212- because they observed His commandments and guarded His Torah.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The verse links guarding Torah with observing the commandments, and the order matters. The predominant sense throughout this section is not that learning and doing are two entirely unrelated categories ranked independently, with learning simply declared more important. The sense, rather, is that they are tightly related \u8212- one leads to the other \u8212- and the one that serves as the gateway and generative force is the one for which judgment and reward come first. As noted, that does not preclude the alternative reading, in which learning has equal intrinsic value {\i plus} additional instrumental value, making it overall superior. But the texture of this {\i Gemara} leans toward the view that the connection between learning and doing is constitutive, not incidental.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Social Exclusion\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Unfit Witness\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now turns to the social and legal consequences of failing to engage with {\i mikra}, {\i mishnah}, and {\i derech eretz}. Rabbi Yochanan rules: {\i kol she-eino lo be-mikra ve-lo be-mishnah ve-lo be-derech eretz \u8212- pasul le-eidus} \u8212- one who is not engaged in learning in any form, not connected to {\i halakhah}, and lacking decent character traits or a productive livelihood, is disqualified from testimony. The disqualification is {\i mi-d\u8217'rabanan}, but it reflects the view that such a person stands so far outside the structures of normative society that his reliability cannot be assumed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Marketplace Eater\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i baraita} adds: one who eats in the marketplace is like a dog \u8212- {\i ha-ochel ba-shuk harey ze domeh le-kelev} \u8212- since dogs eat publicly without social inhibition. Some say this disqualifies one from testimony. Rabbi Idi bar Avin rules that the {\i halacha} follows that view. The {\i Maharsha} is skeptical that this literally means eating in public, and suggests it refers to grabbing food from a stall \u8212- though as the {\i Maharsha} himself notes, that would simply make the person a {\i gazlan}, which raises its own questions.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Character & Fruit\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Bar Kappara taught: {\i ragzan} \u8212- an angry person \u8212- {\i lo alta b\u8217'yado ela ragzanuto} \u8212- gains nothing from his anger but the anger itself. It produces no positive fruit. This connects back to the earlier discussion about {\i oseh pri} \u8212- what does a {\i mitzvah} or {\i aveirah} produce in the world? The {\i ragzan}\u8217's anger produces nothing of value; whatever {\i pri} it yields is, so to speak, an ulcer. By contrast, a good person \u8212- {\i adam tov} \u8212- {\i mati\u8217'imin oto mi-pri ma\u8217'asav}, is rewarded with the fruit of his actions. The discussion here is no longer primarily about {\i mitzvos} and their formal reward but about character itself \u8212- what a person\u8217's way of being generates in the world.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Complete Avoidance\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Finally, {\i v\u8217'chol she-eino lo be-mikra ve-lo be-mishnah ve-lo be-derech eretz} \u8212- one who has no learning, no observance, and neither good character nor a productive livelihood \u8212- {\i dur hana\u8217'ah mimenu}: one should make oneself {\i mudar hana\u8217'ah} from such a person, as if by {\i neder}, keeping maximum distance. The prooftext is {\i u-v\u8217'moshav leitzim lo yashev} \u8212- wherever such a person will be found, it will be a gathering of mockers, a place of wasted time and trivial pursuit. This brings the first section of the daf to a close with a rich portrait: beginning from the enormous weight of any single {\i mitzvah}, moving through the relationship between learning and doing, and arriving at what these values produce \u8212- or fail to produce \u8212- in a person\u8217's character and in their place within society.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Kiddushin Intro\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishnah Recitation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 With {\i Hadran alach ha-ishah nikneis}, the massive first chapter \u8212- the longest in all of {\i Shas} \u8212- concludes, and the new {\i perek} begins. The opening {\i mishnah} announces: {\i ha-ish mekadesh bo u-v\u8217'shlucho} \u8212- a man betroths a woman directly or through his agent. {\i Ha-ishah mitkadeshet bah u-v\u8217'shluchah} \u8212- a woman may be betrothed directly, by receiving the money herself, or through her agent. The language shifts notably from the previous chapter\u8217's {\i kinyan} terminology: the {\i Gemara} later observes that {\i kinyan} was {\i lishnah d\u8217'kra} \u8212- the biblical vocabulary \u8212- whereas {\i kiddushin} is the rabbinic conceptualization of the institution.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Father\u8217's Right\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} adds: {\i ha-ish mekadesh et bito k\u8217'she-hi na\u8217'arah bo u-v\u8217'shlucho} \u8212- a father has the right to marry off his daughter when she is a {\i na\u8217'arah}, whether he does so directly or through an agent. This is a significant {\i chiddush}, since one might have thought that a father who is acting not as principal but as representative of his daughter\u8217's interest could not further delegate that authority to an agent. The {\i mishnah} teaches that he can.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i na\u8217'arah} stage \u8212- a legally defined period of roughly six months following the onset of physical maturity at age twelve \u8212- might seem like an oddly narrow category to specify. But it is extensively developed in {\i halacha} because the Torah repeatedly invokes the status of {\i b\u8217'neureha beis aviha}, treating this transitional period as legally significant across many contexts. The {\i pshat} of the {\i pesukim} may not have conceived of it as a six-month window; {\i Chazal} derived that formal limitation. But regardless of the precise derivation, it is the critical period of marriageability and transition, dense with legal consequence.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Personal Action\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Why Mention Both\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} asks the obvious question: {\i hashta bi-shlucho mekadesh, bo miba\u8217'ya} \u8212- if a man can betroth through an agent, does it even need to be stated that he can do it himself? Of course he can do it personally. So why does the {\i mishnah} mention both? Rav Yosef answers: {\i mitzvah bo yoter mibishlucho} \u8212- to teach that it is preferable to perform the act personally. The mention of both modes is not redundant; it is instructive. The {\i mishnah} includes the {\i bo} \u8212- the personal performance \u8212- precisely to convey that while the {\i shlucho} is entirely valid, the {\i bo} is the preferred mode.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Body Engagement\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This principle extends well beyond {\i kiddushin}. Rav Safra would char the head of the fish or animal himself in preparation for {\i Shabbos}; Rava would salt the fish himself \u8212- despite these being great {\i Amoraim} whose time was extraordinarily valuable. They engaged their bodies personally in {\i kvod Shabbos} because {\i mitzvah bo yoter mi-bishlucho} is a general principle. Rashi explains: {\i dechi asi gufa be-mitzvot, mekabel schar tvei} \u8212- when one engages one\u8217's own body in a {\i mitzvah}, one receives greater reward. But this is not simply an accounting of additional brownie points for greater effort. The deeper point, connected to the earlier discussion of {\i ochel peiroteihem}, is that personal engagement produces a different kind of connection. When you personally do something, it impacts you differently \u8212- it enters your {\i kishkes}, as it were. The {\i mitzvah} becomes more real, more embodied, more yours. That is what it means to engage one\u8217's very person in the act.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Man\u8217's Prohibition\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Some say \u8212- {\i ika de-amri} \u8212- that for the man, failing to perform personally is not merely suboptimal but rises to the level of an actual {\i issur}. The source for this is a ruling of Rav Yehudah in the name of Rav, which the {\i Gemara} will now introduce: {\i assur le-adam she-yekadesh isha ad she-yirena} \u8212- a man is forbidden to betroth a woman until he has seen her first. This generates an {\i issur} dimension to the use of an agent when it would result in betrothing a woman one has never seen.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Seeing the Woman\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav\u8217's Statement\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Yehudah says in the name of Rav: it is forbidden for a man to betroth a woman until he has seen her. This was entirely realistic as a concern in a society where betrothal was often arranged through intermediaries, sometimes without any prior meeting between the parties. Sending an agent to perform the act of {\i kiddushin} could easily mean that the man has never set eyes on his future wife.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Lest Repulsion\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The reason given is: {\i shema yireh bah davar maguneh ve-titganeh alav} \u8212- lest after the marriage he find something repulsive about her and she become repulsive to him. This would violate the obligation of {\i ve-ahavta le-re\u8217'acha kamocha} \u8212- love your neighbor as yourself \u8212- which extends to one\u8217's wife as a fellow human being.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Marriage Context\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might initially find this rationale somewhat flat \u8212- is the whole basis for spousal affection merely the generic commandment to love one\u8217's neighbor? But the {\i Gemara} must be read against its social context. This is not a society in which marriage is primarily an emotional or romantic institution. The scenario being addressed is one where the first time a man might see his wife is at the moment of betrothal, when he hands her the ring. In such a world, where romantic love plays little role in the formation of marriages and people often remain married under deeply imperfect conditions \u8212- because divorce, while technically available, involves a {\i ketubah} payment and carries social weight \u8212- it remains genuinely important to ensure that the couple can at minimum regard each other without visceral repulsion. The {\i ahavah} invoked here is not the {\i ahavah} of romance; it is the baseline of human decency one owes to any fellow person. And yet there is something profound in insisting on even that baseline. Even in a society where marriage is fundamentally a practical institution, where couples may live together for decades without deep emotional connection, the rabbis are concerned that something as elemental as mutual contempt should not be the starting point.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Men vs.\u160 ?Women\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Women Allowed\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} clarifies a textual point about the {\i mishnah}: when it states {\i mitzvah bo yoter mi-bishlucho}, this was said specifically about the {\i seifa} \u8212- the latter clause \u8212- regarding the woman. {\i Ha-ishah mitkadeshet bah u-v\u8217'shluchah} \u8212- for her, it is a {\i mitzvah} to participate personally, but if she chooses to use an agent, {\i issur leit bah} \u8212- there is no prohibition. For the man, {\i ika de-amri} that there is an {\i issur} dimension; for the woman, there is no such {\i issur}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Reish Lakish\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Why is there no concern on the woman\u8217's side about {\i ve-ahavta le-re\u8217'acha kamocha} \u8212- that she might find her husband repulsive? Because of the principle of Reish Lakish: {\i tav lemeitav tan du mi-lemeitav armelu} \u8212- better to sit in tandem than to live as a widow, better to be married, even imperfectly, than to live alone. The presumption is that women are willing to settle to a greater degree than men. Even if a woman does not find her husband particularly appealing, she will not experience the same degree of visceral antagonism that would result on the man\u8217's side. Therefore the concern that generates the {\i issur} for the man \u8212- that he will find her repulsive and this will corrode the marriage \u8212- does not apply with equal force to the woman.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} acknowledges that this principle carries implications beyond the sociological. The presumption that women are more tolerant of imperfect situations has, in other contexts, fed into a certain passivity around issues of {\i agunah} \u8212- a motivation to not be proactive about resolving situations where a woman is unable to receive a {\i get}, on the assumption that she can tolerate the circumstance. That is a significant and troubling extrapolation, and the {\i Gemara}\u8217's observation here about differential marital dynamics should not be read as an endorsement of those downstream applications.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Marriage as Mitzvah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Language Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The use of the term {\i mitzvah} \u8212- {\i mitzvah bo yoter mi-bishlucho} \u8212- raises a fundamental question that the {\i Gemara} has now prompted. The previous chapter analyzed {\i kiddushin} through the lens of {\i kinyanim}, commercial-legal transactions. But now the text speaks of {\i mitzvah}. Why should getting married be a {\i mitzvah}? If the earlier analysis framed it as an economic institution \u8212- the acquisition of a wife \u8212- then the language of {\i mitzvah} seems incongruous, just as one would not invoke {\i mitzvah bo yoter mi-bishlucho} when purchasing a boat.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rishonim Dispute\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This prompts a genuine debate among the {\i Rishonim}. Many {\i Rishonim} hold that marriage is not a {\i mitzvah} per se. The majority of those who do consider it a {\i mitzvah} classify it as a {\i hechsher mitzvah} \u8212- a preparatory act that enables the fulfillment of {\i pru urvu}, the {\i mitzvah} to be fruitful and multiply. This is a natural fit: just as {\i kvod Shabbos} is not itself a Torah-level {\i mitzvah} but is a {\i hechsher} for {\i Shabbos}, so too {\i kiddushin} may be a {\i hechsher} for {\i pru urvu}. The counterargument, raised by the {\i Rosh}, is that one fulfills {\i pru urvu} even through children born outside of marriage \u8212- even through a {\i mamzer} \u8212- so that {\i kiddushin} cannot technically be a {\i hechsher} for the {\i mitzvah} if the {\i mitzvah} can be fulfilled without it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rambam\u8217's View\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Rambam}\u8217's position is notably complex. When the {\i Rambam} discusses the {\i mitzvah} of {\i pru urvu}, he frames it as a two-part {\i mitzvah}: first, to marry a woman, and second, to have children with one\u8217's wife. He thus embeds marriage within the {\i mitzvah} of {\i pru urvu} itself \u8212- at least as the primary mode of fulfillment. Whether the {\i Rambam} would say one fails to fulfill {\i pru urvu} entirely through children born out of wedlock is not fully resolved in his writings. The {\i Rambam} also emphasizes an {\i issur} dimension: relations outside of marriage are forbidden, and thus one must marry before having children. On that reading, the {\i mitzvah} to marry is really a necessary condition for avoiding an {\i issur}, which makes the language of {\i mitzvah bo yoter mi-bishlucho} somewhat difficult to apply.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two Stages\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A third dimension of the {\i Rambam}\u8217's position draws attention to a structural {\i chiddush} of the Torah: marriage is a two-stage process. Before the Torah, one would proceed directly to living together. The Torah introduces the requirement that {\i kiddushin} precede {\i nisuin} \u8212- commitment must precede cohabitation. The {\i Rambam} makes a great deal of this distinction. One way to understand its significance is as follows: if marriage were defined entirely by the lived reality of the relationship \u8212- by the daily experience of life together \u8212- then any period of difficulty, conflict, or estrangement would undermine the marriage at its foundations. But if there is a prior commitment, a formal contractual bond that precedes and is independent of the lived reality, then the marriage has an anchor that can survive difficult periods. The couple committed before they began living together, and that commitment remains even when the current experience of the marriage is challenging. The {\i mitzvah} on the {\i Rambam}\u8217's third reading would then be: when you wish to marry, do so in the manner that the Torah established \u8212- go through {\i kiddushin} first, then {\i nisuin}, honoring the structure the Torah created.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Practically speaking, it is the first reading \u8212- {\i kiddushin} as {\i hechsher} for {\i pru urvu} \u8212- that best supports the language of {\i mitzvah bo yoter mi-bishlucho}, with its parallel to {\i kvod Shabbos}. The other readings are harder to fit into that mold. It is also worth noting that the formulation {\i mitzvah bah yoter mi-bishlucha} is applied to the woman as well, even though {\i pru urvu} is formally the {\i mitzvah} of the man and not the woman according to standard {\i halachic} ruling \u8212- suggesting the term {\i mitzvah} may be used here in a looser, less technically precise sense.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} in {\i Yevamos} discusses whether a man who has already fulfilled {\i pru urvu} should nonetheless remain married after his wife\u8217's death. One answer given there is {\i lo tov heiyot ha-adam levado} \u8212- it is not good for a person to be alone, a value rooted in {\i Bereishit}. That fundamental human dimension of marriage \u8212- the value of shared life \u8212- is not captured by the formal {\i halachic} language of {\i mitzvah} and {\i hechsher mitzvah}, but it is real and acknowledged, even if it does not generate formal {\i halachic} terminology in the present context.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Minor Daughter\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Minor Prohibition\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} states that a father betroths his daughter when she is a {\i na\u8217'arah}. The {\i Gemara} reads this as implying: when she is a {\i na\u8217'arah}, yes \u8212- but when she is a {\i ketanah}, a minor, he should not do so. This supports the ruling of Rav Yehudah in the name of Rav (with some attributing it to Rabbi Elazar): {\i assur le-adam she-yekadesh et bito ke-she-hi ketanah} \u8212- it is forbidden for a father to betroth his minor daughter.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Consent Required\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The reason is: {\i ad she-tigdal ve-tomar bi-ploni ani rotzah} \u8212- she must grow up and say, \u8220"I want to marry this specific person.\u8221" Marriage requires her mature, personal assent to this particular man, and that is simply impossible when she is a minor.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Adult vs.\u160 ?Minor\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} anticipates an obvious question: if we earlier said that a woman can betroth through an agent without having seen the man, relying on the principle that women are willing to settle \u8212- {\i tav lemeitav tan du mi-lemeitav armelu} \u8212- why is it {\i assur} for a father to betroth his minor daughter? {\i Tosafot} draws a critical distinction. When an adult woman says, \u8220"Go find me a husband,\u8221" she is making a conscious, autonomous decision to accept whoever her agent selects. She has effectively consented in advance to the arrangement and to whatever outcome it produces, and we trust that she will make her peace with it. But a minor never made any such declaration. She never agreed to be betrothed to an unknown party through her father\u8217's choice. In the adult woman\u8217's case, her willingness to settle was expressed through her own decision to delegate; in the minor\u8217's case, no such expression of consent ever occurred. Therefore the concern \u8212- that she will find her husband repulsive and the marriage will be deeply troubled \u8212- applies on her end as well, and it is {\i assur}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafot Modern\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} then adds a poignant comment: {\i ve-achshav she-anu nogahin le-kadesh benoteinu afilu ketanot} \u8212- in our time, we do betroth our daughters even when they are minors. {\i Haynu mishum she-be-chol yom ve-yom ha-galut mitgaber aleinu} \u8212- because each day the exile presses harder upon us. With the Crusades and the mounting persecutions, if a father has the means {\i now} to provide his daughter with a dowry \u8212- {\i nadan} \u8212- and secure her future, {\i shema le-achar zeman lo yiheyeh sipek be-yado ve-teshev bito agunah le-olam} \u8212- lest a time come when he lacks the means and his daughter remains an {\i agunah} forever, without a husband to protect and provide for her. {\i Tosafot} thus acknowledges the practice as a deviation from the ideal, justified only by the desperate circumstances of exile.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This passage also raises a broader point. The Torah is replete with cases of fathers marrying off daughters \u8212- {\i natati et biti la-ish hazeh} and similar formulations appear throughout. Yet {\i Chazal} look at all of this and say it is {\i assur le-chatchila}. This reflects an important methodological principle: not everything that appears in the Torah, even repeatedly and without any critical framing, should be read as a {\i mitzvah} or a {\i le-chatchila} ideal. {\i Chazal} were capable of recognizing that the Torah sometimes accommodates social realities without endorsing them as the preferred norm.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Agency Sources\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Divorce Source\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having discussed {\i shlichut} in the context of the {\i mishnah}, the {\i Gemara} now asks: {\i shlichut minalan} \u8212- what is the source that one can appoint an agent at all? The {\i baraita} derives it from the verse about divorce: {\i ve-shilcha mi-beito} \u8212- he shall send her from his house. From the word {\i ve-shalach} \u8212- the active sending \u8212- we learn {\i melamed she-oseh shaliach}, that a man can appoint an agent to give the {\i get}. This establishes {\i shlichut} in the context of {\i girushin}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Grammar Rules\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 From the same verse, the {\i Gemara} reads {\i ve-shalcha} \u8212- taking the final {\i heh} without its {\i mappik}, converting the verb to feminine \u8212- to teach {\i melamed she-ossa shaliach}, that the woman can appoint an agent to receive the {\i get} on her behalf. And from yet another reading of {\i ve-shalach ve-shalcha}, the {\i Gemara} derives {\i melamed she-shaliach oseh shaliach} \u8212- that an agent can in turn appoint a sub-agent. (The full complexity of whether {\i milei mimsran le-shaliach} limits this in the context of {\i gittin} is not the {\i Gemara}\u8217's focus here.)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 That establishes {\i shlichut} from {\i girushin}. But how do we know {\i shlichut} works for {\i kiddushin}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Terumah Source\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might suggest simply learning {\i kiddushin} from {\i girushin}, but the {\i Gemara} rejects this: {\i mah girushin she-ken yeshnan be-al korchah} \u8212- {\i girushin} can be effectuated against the woman\u8217's will. A one-sided transaction in which one party holds all authority is structurally simpler: when you are fully in control, it is more obvious that you can delegate. {\i Kiddushin}, by contrast, requires a meeting of minds between two parties; neither side holds unilateral power. The capacity to delegate in such a context is less obvious and cannot simply be inferred from {\i girushin}. The {\i Gemara} therefore derives {\i shlichut} for {\i kiddushin} from a different verse: {\i ve-yatz\u8217'ah ve-hayta} \u8212- she shall go out and become [the wife of another man]. The {\i Gemara} reads {\i havaya} (becoming a wife) as parallel to {\i yetziah} (leaving through divorce): {\i mah yetziah mashvei shaliach, af havaya nami mashvei shaliach} \u8212- just as you can appoint an agent for divorce, so too for betrothal.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 For {\i terumah}, the {\i Gemara} derives {\i shlichut} from the verse {\i gam atem} \u8212- \u8220"also you\u8221" \u8212- in the context of separating {\i trumat ma\u8217'aser}. The word {\i gam} \u8212- \u8220"also\u8221" \u8212- is read to include one\u8217's representative: {\i le-rabot et ha-shaliach}, even your agent counts as \u8220"you\u8221" in this context.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Why Different\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Why not simply learn {\i shlichut} for {\i kiddushin} from {\i girushin}, and for {\i terumah} from {\i girushin} as well? Because each of these domains has distinctive characteristics that make a simple derivation impossible. {\i Girushin} involves a fundamentally one-sided, passive transaction; {\i kiddushin} is bilateral and active. {\i Terumah} involves {\i kedushah} \u8212- a sanctity \u8212- that {\i girushin}, a secular matter, lacks. These differences mean that each domain requires its own source for {\i shlichut}, which the {\i Gemara} will now elaborate systematically.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Korban Pesach\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Pesach Scenario\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} introduces a third domain of {\i shlichut} through a {\i mishnah}: a group \u8212- {\i chabura} \u8212- that has pooled to bring a {\i korban Pesach} together finds that their lamb has run away. They send one member: {\i tze u-vakesh u-shchot aleinu} \u8212- go find it and slaughter it for us. He goes, finds the lamb, and {\i shechts} it at the {\i Beit Hamikdash}. Meanwhile, unsure whether he will succeed, they go and purchase another lamb and {\i shecht} it as well. The {\i mishnah} works out the rules for which {\i korban} is valid depending on the order of events \u8212- but for the {\i Gemara}\u8217's purposes, the {\i case} establishes that the members of the {\i chabura} can appoint one of their number as {\i shaliach} to {\i shecht} the {\i korban Pesach} on behalf of all.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But why is this even notable? In practice, only one person {\i shechts} for each group \u8212- no one imagines ten people holding the knife together. The distinction lies in the proof: the verse says {\i ve-shachatu oso kol kehal adat Yisrael bein ha-arbayim} \u8212- all the congregation of Israel shall {\i shecht} it. The {\i Gemara} asks: does the entire congregation literally {\i shecht}? Of course not \u8212- {\i eino shocheit ela echad} \u8212- only one person {\i shechts}. {\i Ela mikan she-shlucho shel adam ke-moto} \u8212- this teaches that an agent is like the person himself.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kodashim Logic\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i chiddush} of Rabbi Yehoshua ben Korcha\u8217's derivation is this: if the {\i pasuk} had simply said one person {\i shechts} for the household, one might read that as the basic {\i structure} of the {\i mitzvah} \u8212- the {\i korban Pesach} just works that way, one person {\i shechts} on behalf of the group, not as agency but as the fundamental mode. But the {\i pasuk} says {\i kol ha-kehal} \u8212- the entire congregation \u8212- each individual \u8212- must {\i shecht}. That is an individual obligation. And yet individual {\i shechita} is clearly not what happens; one person {\i shechts}. The only explanation is that this one person is discharging each individual\u8217's personal obligation through {\i shlichut}. That is the proof.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Set Theory Logic\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kodashim Traits\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having established {\i shlichut} in three domains \u8212- {\i girushin}, {\i terumah}, and {\i korban Pesach} ({\i kodashim}) \u8212- the {\i Gemara} asks why each cannot simply be derived from the others, making the separate sources unnecessary. The answer lies in the distinctive characteristics of each domain, and the {\i Gemara} works through the logic systematically.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Kodashim} (as represented by the {\i korban Pesach}) has three notable features: it involves {\i kedushah} (holiness), it is governed by {\i machshava} (thought \u8212- one can sanctify a {\i korban} and affect its legal status through intention alone), and {\i ma\u8217'aseihem al yedei shaliach} \u8212- actions in the {\i Beit Hamikdash} are routinely performed through representatives, the {\i kohanim} acting on behalf of the owners. This third feature means {\i kodashim} is an environment where the use of agents is the norm, not the exception, which makes it less surprising that {\i shlichut} would apply there.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Terumah Traits\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Terumah} shares two of these features: {\i kedushah} (it is a sanctified portion given to the {\i kohanim}) and {\i machshava} (one can designate a portion as {\i terumah} through thought or speech alone, without a formal physical act). However, {\i terumah} does not have the feature of {\i ma\u8217'aseihem al yedei shaliach} \u8212- the routine delegation of all actions to representatives \u8212- in the same way {\i kodashim} does. The owner\u8217's own act of separation is the primary mode.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Girushin Traits\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Girushin}, by contrast, is essentially {\i chol} \u8212- secular. It involves no {\i kedushah} whatsoever. The {\i get} is not a sanctified object; the divorce is not a religious sanctification in the sense of {\i terumah} or {\i kodashim}. {\i Girushin} also has no element of {\i machshava} affecting the legal status of the transaction \u8212- it is a formal legal act, not one governed by intention in the way that sanctification is.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Universal Application\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} thus applies set theory. One cannot learn {\i shlichut} universally from any two domains that share a distinctive characteristic, because one might argue the shared characteristic is what enables {\i shlichut} in those domains. {\i Kodashim} and {\i terumah} both share {\i kedushah} and {\i machshava} \u8212- so learning from them together does not prove {\i shlichut} applies where neither is present. {\i Terumah} and {\i girushin} together share a characteristic on the other end: they are both at a level of holiness {\i less than} that of {\i kodashim} \u8212- and one might argue that {\i shlichut} requires a lower threshold of holiness than full {\i kodashim}. That shared feature prevents learning the rule universally from this pair.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But {\i girushin} and {\i kodashim} share {\i no} common characteristic. {\i Girushin} is {\i chol}; {\i kodashim} is the highest level of {\i kedushah}. {\i Girushin} has no {\i machshava}; {\i kodashim} does. {\i Girushin} has no routine delegation through agents; {\i kodashim} does routinely involve agents. Since these two domains share absolutely nothing, and yet {\i shlichut} applies to both, the principle must be universal \u8212- it applies regardless of the presence or absence of any of these features. The {\i Gemara} concludes accordingly: {\i shlichut} is established universally from the combination of {\i girushin} and {\i kodashim}. The {\i Gemara} then notes that tomorrow\u8217's discussion will address why, given this, the independent verse for {\i terumah} was nonetheless necessary.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Kiddushin 42\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Sources of Shlichut\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Three Sources\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The present daf deals with the institution of {\i shlichut} \u8212- agency \u8212- and three distinct biblical sources from which it is derived. The first is the law of {\i terumah}, where the verse reads {\i gam atem}, \u8220"even you,\u8221" which is understood to include a {\i shaliach}. The second is {\i gerushin} (divorce), where the verse {\i veshilach veshilchah mibeito} \u8212- literally, \u8220"he shall send her out of his house\u8221" \u8212- is read as authorizing the appointment of an agent. The third is {\i korban Pesach}, where the verse states {\i veshachatu oto kol kehal adat Yisrael bein ha\u8217'arbayim}, \u8220"and the entire congregation of Israel shall slaughter it at dusk.\u8221" Since every individual is obligated and yet every individual plainly did not perform the {\i shechita}, the obvious inference is that one person slaughtered on behalf of each household \u8212- a paradigmatic act of agency.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} had already observed, on the previous daf, that two of these three sources \u8212- {\i korban Pesach} and {\i gerushin}/{\i kiddushin} \u8212- together suffice to establish {\i shlichut} across the full range of {\i halacha}. From {\i kodshim} alone one could not learn {\i gerushin}, since {\i gerushin} operates through thought (intention), whereas {\i kodshim} requires a physical act. And from {\i gerushin} alone one could not learn {\i kodshim}, for the reverse reason. By anchoring the principle in both extremes, everything between them is included. The question that follows, then, is why the Torah also needs the verse in {\i terumah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Why Terumah Needed\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} picks up on 41b with the question: {\i ve\u8217'ella atem gam atem lama li} \u8212- why is the phrase {\i atem gam atem} in the {\i terumah} context needed at all, if {\i shlichut} can already be derived from {\i korban Pesach} and {\i gerushin}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gam Atem - Like You\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} answers by invoking the teaching of Rabbi Yannai: {\i gam atem} does not come merely to affirm that a {\i shaliach} can separate {\i terumah}. That much might be derived elsewhere. Rather, it comes to define the parameters of a valid {\i shaliach} \u8212- specifically, to require that a {\i shaliach} be {\i like you}. As the {\i gemara} formulates it: {\i mah atem bnei berit af shluchaichon bnei berit} \u8212- just as you are members of the covenant, so too must your agent be a member of the covenant. A non-Jew cannot serve as one\u8217's {\i shaliach}. The verse teaches not the existence of {\i shlichut} but its qualifications.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Learning From Logic\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then pushes further: why is a verse even necessary to exclude a non-Jew? Cannot this be derived through logic, from the principle stated by Rabbi Chiya bar Abba in the name of Rabbi Yochanan? Rabbi Yochanan taught that a slave cannot serve as a {\i shaliach} to receive a {\i get} on behalf of a woman from her husband, {\i lefi she\u8217'eino betorat gittin vekiddushin} \u8212- because the laws of marriage and divorce simply do not apply to him. The principle is that one cannot serve as an agent in a legal domain that is categorically inapplicable to oneself.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This principle readily explains the exclusion of a slave from {\i gittin} and {\i kiddushin}, since those laws do not govern a slave at all. And yet a slave is nonetheless {\i bnei berit} \u8212- he is within the covenant, partially obligated in {\i mitzvot}, and could in principle serve as an agent in other contexts. He can, for instance, separate {\i terumot u\u8217'maasrot} on behalf of someone or conduct a commercial transaction as a {\i shaliach}. The category-exclusion rule of Rabbi Yochanan thus accounts for why a slave cannot represent a woman in the receipt of a {\i get}, without requiring a special verse.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Non-Jew Terumah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Given this logical principle, why would a verse be required to exclude a non-Jew from serving as a {\i shaliach} for {\i terumah}? A non-Jew is not subject to the laws of {\i terumah}, so surely the same logic applies: since {\i terumah} does not apply to him, he cannot be an agent for it. The {\i gemara} responds that the situation is actually more nuanced. Unlike the slave by {\i gittin}, a non-Jew is not categorically outside the orbit of {\i terumah}. The {\i mishnah} teaches: {\i ha\u8217'oved kochavim veha\u8217'kuti she\u8217'tarmu terumatan terumah} \u8212- if a non-Jew or a Samaritan separated {\i terumah}, that separation is halachically valid. Rashi notes that this assumes the non-Jew\u8217's ownership and processing of the grain do not exempt it from the obligation; but granted those assumptions, a non-Jew can in fact validly separate {\i terumah}. Since a non-Jew is thus \u8220"in the category\u8221" of {\i terumah} in some sense, one might conclude that he can also serve as a {\i shaliach} for {\i terumah} \u8212- {\i eima shaliach nami avid}. The verse therefore comes to exclude him.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Resolution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara}\u8217's resolution points toward a dual requirement. There are in fact two distinct conditions for valid {\i shlichut}: first, the {\i shaliach} must be Jewish \u8212- he must be {\i bnei berit} \u8212- even if the relevant domain of law applies to him in some form (as with the non-Jew and {\i terumah}, or with commercial transactions generally). Second, even if the {\i shaliach} is {\i bnei berit} and fully within the covenant, he cannot serve as agent in a legal domain categorically inapplicable to him \u8212- as with the slave and {\i gittin}. The verse {\i gam atem} establishes the first condition; the logical principle of Rabbi Yochanan establishes the second.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Shimon\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} notes that according to Rabbi Shimon, this entire construction collapses. Rabbi Shimon holds that when a non-Jew separates {\i terumah}, the separation has no legal status \u8212- it is not {\i terumah}. The {\i mishnah} records the dispute: the {\i tanna kamma} holds that the {\i terumah} of a non-Jew causes {\i dima\u8217'a} (if mixed into other produce, it confers upon it the restricted status of {\i terumah}) and one who eats it inadvertently must pay {\i chomesh}; Rabbi Shimon exempts. According to Rabbi Shimon, a non-Jew is simply outside the category of {\i terumah}, and there would be no basis for thinking he could serve as a {\i shaliach} for it. So why is {\i gam atem} needed?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} explains that even according to Rabbi Shimon, the verse serves a necessary function \u8212- albeit a different one \u8212- which leads into the next discussion.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Word \u8216'Atem\u8217' Exclusions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Atem Excludes\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Before the word {\i gam} appears in the verse \u8212- which the {\i gemara} uses to include a {\i shaliach} \u8212- there is the word {\i atem}, \u8220"you,\u8221" which functions as a limiting term. The {\i gemara} records: {\i atem ve\u8217'lo arisim, atem ve\u8217'lo shutafin, atem ve\u8217'lo apitropus} \u8212- \u8220"you\u8221" and not sharecroppers, \u8220"you\u8221" and not partners, \u8220"you\u8221" and not an overseer. None of these individuals can separate {\i terumah} on behalf of the owner. A sharecropper can separate on his own portion, not on the owner\u8217's. A partner can separate on his own share, not his partner\u8217's. An {\i apitropus} \u8212- a guardian or overseer, including one appointed over orphans \u8212- lacks the authority to separate {\i terumah} on behalf of the principal. The word {\i atem} similarly excludes {\i hatoreim terumah chaveiro} \u8212- someone who simply separates another person\u8217's {\i terumah} without authorization.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 All of these are individuals who have some relationship or connection to the owner or the property, yet are not the owner himself and not his appointed agent.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Question on Shaliach\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This raises a natural difficulty. If the word {\i atem} is so restrictive \u8212- excluding sharecroppers, partners, overseers, and others who have genuine connections to the property \u8212- one might conclude that a {\i shaliach} too is excluded. After all, a {\i shaliach} is not the owner himself; {\i atem} would seem to indicate that only the owner may act.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gam Includes Shaliach\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The word {\i gam} \u8212- \u8220"even\u8221" \u8212- resolves this. {\i Gam atem}, \u8220"even you,\u8221" extends the category to include a {\i shaliach}. The verse does two things simultaneously: through {\i atem} it restricts to the principal and excludes various connected but unauthorized parties; through {\i gam} it expands to include the one person who is authorized \u8212- the {\i shaliach}. This is the second explanation of why {\i gam atem} is needed even given the other biblical sources for {\i shlichut}: without it, one might have excluded the {\i shaliach} from {\i terumah} precisely because the word {\i atem} emphasizes the personal nature of the act.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Korban Pesach Shlich (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Yonatan\u8217's Use\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now takes up a difficulty that arises from a different direction. It was assumed above that the {\i korban Pesach} verse \u8212- {\i veshachatu oto kol kehal adat Yisrael} \u8212- teaches the principle of {\i shlichut} in the domain of {\i kodshim} (sacred things). But this works only according to Rabbi Yehoshua ben Korcha, who reads that verse as establishing agency for the {\i shechita}. According to Rabbi Yonatan, that same verse is expended on an entirely different law, and cannot simultaneously teach {\i shlichut}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i baraisa} records: Rabbi Yonatan asks, how do we know that in principle all of Israel can fulfill their obligation through a single {\i korban Pesach}? He answers from the same verse \u8212- {\i veshachatu oto kol kehal adat Yisrael} \u8212- noting that the verse speaks of all of Israel slaughtering \u8220"it\u8221" (singular), implying that one sacrifice could serve the entire nation. The implication is that not every individual needs to eat a {\i kezayis} in order to be {\i yotzei}, since if all of Israel shared one small lamb, most would receive far less than a {\i kezayis}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is not the standard view, which holds that the entire purpose of {\i korban Pesach} \u8212- unlike other sacrifices, whose primary end is the {\i mizbeach} \u8212- is the eating. Accordingly, not eating a {\i kezayis} would normally render one not {\i yotzei}. (Indeed, the unique status of {\i korban Pesach} as an eating-focused sacrifice is evident in the law that if it is offered in a state of {\i tumah}, it is also eaten in {\i tumah}, an exception made precisely because eating is its essential purpose.) Rabbi Yonatan nonetheless derives from the verse that even a {\i kezayis} is not strictly required. The {\i gemara} opens 42a with his formulation: {\i minalan shekol Yisrael kulan yotzin b\u8217'Pesach echad}, \u8220"how do we know that all of Israel can fulfill their obligation through one {\i Pesach}?\u8221" \u8212- and answers from this verse.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 No Shlichut From Pesach\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If that is what the verse teaches \u8212- that all of Israel can share one {\i korban Pesach} and one need not eat a {\i kezayis} \u8212- then the verse has been \u8220"used\u8221" by Rabbi Yonatan for that {\i derashah}, and cannot simultaneously serve as the basis for {\i shlichut} by {\i kodshim}. The {\i gemara} therefore asks: {\i shaliach dikdash minalei} \u8212- from where, according to Rabbi Yonatan, do we derive that {\i shlichut} applies to sacred matters? We know it from {\i gerushin}, we know it from {\i terumah}, but those do not establish the principle for {\i kodshim}. {\i Kodshim} is the most stringent domain; if we lack a source there, the principle of agency may not apply to it at all.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Scene Shows Shlichut\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} answers: {\i minei} \u8212- you learn it from the case itself. Even if the stated {\i point} of the verse is to teach that one need not eat a {\i kezayis}, the verse nonetheless {\i describes} a scenario in which all of Israel own a single lamb and one person performs the {\i shechita} on behalf of everyone. That is a real instance of {\i shlichut} depicted in the text, even if it is not the {\i derasha}\u8217's primary teaching. The {\i scenario} itself demonstrates the concept of agency in {\i kodshim}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Korban Pesach Shlich (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Co-Ownership Problem\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} rejects this solution: {\i vidilma shani hatam de\u8217'isai shutfus begavaihu} \u8212- perhaps there it is different because everyone has a partnership, a shared ownership, in the {\i korban Pesach}. When all members of a household jointly own the same lamb, the fact that one person performs the {\i shechita} on behalf of all the others does not prove anything about general {\i shlichut}. The co-owners are already united in a single enterprise; one of them representing the group follows naturally from that shared ownership. This is fundamentally different from a situation in which a wholly unrelated individual is appointed as one\u8217's agent \u8212- where there is no pre-existing connection or joint stake \u8212- and then acts on the principal\u8217's behalf.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} raises an obvious question: why did the {\i gemara} not ask this objection earlier, on the previous daf, when it used the {\i korban Pesach} as a source for {\i shlichut} without qualification? {\i Tosafot} answers that the discussion on the previous daf was not positing that all of Israel owned one {\i korban}. Rather, it was describing the standard practice: each family or group brings their own {\i korban}, and a single designated {\i shochet} performs the {\i shechita} for every group \u8212- you bring me your {\i korban} and I will slaughter it, and you bring me yours and I will slaughter it. In that scenario, the {\i shochet} does not share ownership with any of the groups for whom he slaughters. That is genuine {\i shlichut} even without a co-ownership connection. It is only here, where the {\i gemara} is speaking about all of Israel sharing one lamb, that the objection of {\i shutfus} \u8212- joint ownership as a potentially sufficient explanation \u8212- becomes relevant.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This discussion touches on a deeper conceptual distinction about the nature of agency. The kind of representation that flows from co-ownership or joint enterprise creates what might be called a corporate identity: the group is a single unit, and one member acts as the mouthpiece or representative of that unit. This is categorically different from the classical model of {\i shlichut}, in which an individual appoints an unrelated third party as his personal agent and the agent\u8217's act is legally attributed to the principal.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two Psukim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then considers whether a different verse from the {\i korban Pesach} narrative can provide the required source. The purchase of the {\i korban Pesach} is described as well: {\i veyikchu lahem ish seh l\u8217'veit avot} \u8212- one person takes a lamb on behalf of the household. Here, before the purchase is made, no one yet has joint ownership; the joint ownership only comes into being {\i through} the act of acquisition. So this person is acquiring on behalf of everyone before anyone has become a co-owner, which looks more like genuine {\i shlichut}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But the {\i gemara} presses the same objection: {\i vedilma hatam nami de\u8217'islei shutfei begavaihu} \u8212- perhaps even there, since these are all members of the same household and are all ultimately going to become co-owners together in this enterprise, their shared connection is sufficient to explain the representation without requiring a general principle of {\i shlichut}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} responds with a different argument: {\i im ken trei kraei lama li} \u8212- if both verses (the one about {\i shechita} and the one about purchase) describe only cases of pre-existing connection and shared enterprise, why are two verses needed? If neither teaches anything beyond what the other already teaches by virtue of the {\i shutfus} rationale, one would be superfluous. The extra verse must therefore be doing additional work \u8212- teaching that {\i shlichut} applies even in the absence of any special connection or co-ownership. However, the {\i gemara} immediately deflects this argument as well: {\i ki dehachi}, the purchase verse is not superfluous, because it is needed for the teaching of Rabbi Yitzchak.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Shlichut Models\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Co-Owner Model\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The co-ownership problem discussed above reflects a broader conceptual distinction between two models of representation. When a group of people share ownership of an object and one member of the group acts on behalf of all \u8212- performing the {\i shechita}, conducting the negotiation, making the decision \u8212- that individual is not necessarily functioning as a personal agent of each member individually. Rather, the group constitutes a single unit, and the person is the operative arm of that unit. The force of the act derives from the group\u8217's collective ownership and shared stake. This explains why one cannot derive general {\i shlichut} from the {\i korban Pesach} scenario: the case may merely illustrate how a joint enterprise operates, not how an individual appoints a stranger to act on his behalf.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shliach Tzibbur Model\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This same conceptual distinction arises in a striking context discussed here. The only place in all of the {\i mishnah} where the principle {\i shlucho shel adam k\u8217'moso} \u8212- a person\u8217's agent is like himself \u8212- is stated explicitly is the {\i mishnah} in {\i Brachot} concerning the {\i shliach tzibbur}. And yet the {\i shliach tzibbur} presents an apparent paradox: the {\i gemara} elsewhere holds that one must pray for oneself and cannot discharge one\u8217's obligation through another\u8217's prayer. If a person simply stands and listens while the {\i shliach tzibbur} recites {\i Shemoneh Esrei}, intending to be {\i yotzei} through him as one would be {\i yotzei} through a personal agent, many authorities hold that this does not work. How, then, did the institution of {\i shliach tzibbur} function historically \u8212- particularly for those who were illiterate and could not pray independently?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The resolution is that the {\i shliach tzibbur} does not function as a personal agent of each individual congregant. He is not discharging each person\u8217's individual obligation as though he were that person\u8217's {\i shaliach}. Rather, the congregation as a whole constitutes a single corporate entity, and the {\i shliach tzibbur} is the voice of that entity \u8212- the mouthpiece through which the community as a single unit addresses God. He represents not each individual but the {\i tzibbur} itself.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Silent vs.\u160 ?Repeated\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This distinction corresponds to the difference between the silent {\i Shemoneh Esrei} and its repetition. The silent {\i Shemoneh Esrei} is {\i tefilla b\u8217'tzibbur} \u8212- an individual praying in the presence of a congregation. Each person is fulfilling a personal obligation as an individual, albeit within a communal setting. The repetition of {\i Shemoneh Esrei}, by contrast, is {\i tefilat ha\u8217'tzibbur} \u8212- the prayer {\i of} the congregation. The individual is no longer acting as an individual at that point; the community as a collective unit is praying through its representative. This is precisely the model that illuminates why the {\i korban Pesach} scenario may not prove general {\i shlichut}: {\i isley shutfei begavaihu}, they all share ownership of the sheep, and the one who performs the {\i shechita} represents the entire household as a single unit. {\i Sha l\u8217'veit avot} \u8212- a lamb for a household \u8212- means everyone has joint ownership, and one person is the operative arm of that household as a unit. This says nothing about how one individual unconnected to another may appoint that other as his personal agent.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Iyakchu Lahem Ish\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Purchase on Behalf\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having exhausted the {\i shechita} verse, the {\i gemara} turns to the purchase verse: {\i veyikchu lahem ish seh l\u8217'veit avot} \u8212- one person takes a lamb on behalf of the household. The argument is that before the purchase, there is no joint ownership; the person is acquiring on behalf of others, which looks like genuine {\i shlichut}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Connection Objection\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} counters, as noted above, that even this case may be explainable through the lens of shared enterprise and pre-existing connection: perhaps they all gave him the money, perhaps they are already bound together as a household unit, and the representation flows from that shared context rather than from a general principle of {\i shlichut}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Why Two Psukim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If both verses \u8212- the {\i shechita} verse and the purchase verse \u8212- are limited to cases with pre-existing connection, neither would prove general {\i shlichut}. And yet two verses have been written. One of them, on this reading, would be entirely superfluous. That superfluity should be redirected to teach the general principle of {\i shlichut} even without connection. But the {\i gemara} closes off this move.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Superfluous Pasuk\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} explains: {\i hahu me\u8217'ish lefi ochlo nafka} \u8212- the exclusion of minors from representing others in the acquisition of the {\i korban Pesach} is derived from the phrase {\i ish lefi ochlo} (\u8220"each man according to his eating\u8221"), where {\i ish} \u8212- man \u8212- already implies that a {\i katan} cannot take possession for others. If the exclusion of minors is derived from that {\i ish}, then the {\i ish} in {\i veyikchu lahem ish seh l\u8217'veit avot} is not needed for that purpose and is superfluous \u8212- and that superfluity is redirected to teach general {\i shlichut}. Without a second source for the {\i katan} exclusion, however, there would be no superfluity in the purchase verse, and no basis for deriving {\i shlichut} from it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Yitzchak\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yitzchak had stated the principle: {\i ish zocheh ve\u8217'lo katan zocheh} \u8212- a man can acquire possession, but a minor cannot. The {\i gemara} invokes the teaching of Rabbi Yitzchak in connection with the purchase verse. {\i Tosafot} raises the question: why does one need a verse specifically here, by {\i korban Pesach}, to teach that a minor cannot acquire? This is a general principle \u8212- {\i ein yad le\u8217'katan}, a minor has no legal capacity for {\i kinyan}. {\i Tosafot} answers: by {\i korban Pesach} one might have thought it was different. A minor can eat from the {\i korban Pesach} \u8212- {\i salka da\u8217'atach ho\u8217'il de\u8217'shayach ba\u8217'achilat pesachim} \u8212- and can register as part of a group ({\i leihimanot alav}), participating in the offering even without owning it himself. One might therefore have thought that since a minor has a genuine connection to the {\i korban Pesach} through eating, perhaps he can also acquire it for others. The verse therefore specifies {\i ish} to teach that only an adult can effect an acquisition on behalf of others; a minor, though he can be part of the group through enrollment, cannot serve as the acquiring agent for others.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This interplay is of particular interest on the eve of Pesach. Minors \u8212- {\i ketanim} \u8212- stand at the center of the {\i Seder} experience, and indeed they can genuinely participate in the {\i korban Pesach} by joining a group. Yet the legal capacity to acquire the sacrifice on behalf of others belongs only to an adult.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Given that {\i ish lefi ochlo} already teaches that a minor cannot acquire for others, the word {\i ish} in {\i veyikchu lahem ish seh l\u8217'veit avot} is superfluous for that purpose. That superfluous {\i ish} is then the source for general {\i shlichut} in {\i kodshim} \u8212- teaching that even where there is no pre-existing co-ownership or connection, one person may act as the agent of another.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Other Shlichut Contexts\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Nasi Land Division\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now introduces a teaching of Rav Gidel in the name of Rav: {\i minayin she\u8217'shlucho shel adam k\u8217'moso} \u8212- from where do we derive that a person\u8217's agent is like himself? The verse cited is {\i v\u8217'nasi echad nasi echad}, from the account of the division of the Land of Israel, where one prince from each tribe participated in the allocation on behalf of his entire tribe. To the degree that any representation of individual tribal members was required, it was performed by the {\i nasi}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then asks why this verse should be considered the source for {\i shlichut} at all, given the other sources already discussed. But more fundamentally, it questions the premise.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Minors Represented\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Ve\u8217'tisabra} \u8212- does this actually make sense? Is the division of the land through tribal princes really a case of {\i shlichut}? {\i V\u8217'ha ketanim lav bnei shlichus ninhu} \u8212- minors cannot appoint a {\i shaliach}. Yet minors were certainly included in the tribal allocation, receiving their portions through the {\i nasi}. If this were {\i shlichut}, it could not have applied to them \u8212- a minor lacks the legal capacity to appoint an agent.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Zchiyah Not Shlichut\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} therefore reframes the teaching. What the verse by the division of the land teaches is not {\i shlichut} but rather {\i zechiyah} \u8212- the principle that one may take possession of something on behalf of another, even not in the other\u8217's presence, and even when the other is a minor. The teaching is reformulated: {\i minayin she\u8217'zachin le\u8217'adam she\u8217'lo bifanav} \u8212- from where do we know that one may acquire on behalf of another not in his presence?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The distinction between {\i zechiyah} and {\i shlichut} is conceptually significant. {\i Shlichut} requires appointment by the principal and depends upon the principal\u8217's legal capacity to appoint. {\i Zechiyah} operates differently: when an act is unambiguously to someone\u8217's benefit, a third party may act on that person\u8217's behalf without formal appointment, and without the other\u8217's capacity to appoint being a prerequisite. In this sense {\i zechiyah} is simultaneously more limited \u8212- it applies only when the benefit is clear and unambiguous \u8212- and more expansive \u8212- it does not require the formalities of {\i shlichut} and can operate even for a minor.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then presses this framing as well. {\i Zechus hu lo} \u8212- is it really an unambiguous benefit? {\i Hachova nami ika} \u8212- there is also a potential detriment. The {\i nasi} did not merely hand each person a gift; he determined which portion of the land each person received. {\i De\u8217'ika de\u8217'nicha lei behar ve\u8217'lo nicha lei bevika} \u8212- one person might have preferred the mountain, another might have preferred the valley. Determining which specific portion someone receives is not a pure benefit; it involves judgment calls and tradeoffs. This therefore cannot be the paradigmatic case of {\i zechiyah} either.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Orphans & Trustees\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Apotropus Appointed\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} finally settles on a more precise characterization of what the verse teaches. It is not general {\i shlichut} and not general {\i zechiyah}; rather it establishes a specific principle: {\i minayin le\u8217'yisomim she\u8217'bau lachlok b\u8217'nichsei avihen she\u8217'bezdin mamidin lahem apitropus l\u8217'chov u\u8217'lizkos} \u8212- from where do we know that when orphans (minors) come to divide their father\u8217's estate, the {\i beit din} can appoint a guardian ({\i apitropus}) to act on their behalf, with the authority to make tradeoffs that may involve both benefit and detriment? This authority is derived from the {\i nasi}\u8217's role in dividing the land of Israel.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The parallel is instructive. The division of {\i Eretz Yisrael} was not purely beneficial for every individual in every respect; it involved allocations that some would have preferred differently. Yet it was conducted by the {\i nasi} on behalf of the entire tribe, including those who could not represent themselves. The authority to make such consequential, judgment-laden decisions on behalf of others \u8212- including those who lack legal capacity \u8212- derives from the fact that the representative was appointed by a higher authority. In the case of {\i Eretz Yisrael}, that authority was divine. In the case of orphans dividing an estate, the parallel authority is {\i beit din}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Nachman bar Shmuel states the practical rule: when minors come to divide their father\u8217's estate, {\i beit din} appoints an {\i apitropus} and chooses a good portion for them \u8212- meaning the trustee attempts to achieve a fair and beneficial distribution for everyone under his charge.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 No Protest After\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Once the orphans reach majority, Rav Nachman rules in his own name: {\i im higdilu einam yecholim limchos} \u8212- they cannot protest the division that was made on their behalf. The principle invoked is {\i mah koach beit din yafeh}: if the court-appointed division could be undone the moment the orphans grew up and decided they were unhappy, the authority of the court would be rendered meaningless. A division backed by {\i beit din} is binding even against the future objection of those who were represented.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Beis Din Authority\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This contrasts with the ordinary principle applicable to {\i zechiyah she\u8217'lo bifanav}: when someone takes possession of an object for an absent party and that party is subsequently informed, the party can refuse the acquisition \u8212- {\i macha\u8217'ah} \u8212- if they did not want it. The presumption that the acquisition was beneficial can be overridden once the party expresses otherwise. But a court-appointed trustee\u8217's decision operates differently; it carries the authority of {\i beit din}, and the orphans\u8217' later preference cannot undo what was done under that authority.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Error Exception\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A difficulty arises from a {\i mishnah} elsewhere. The {\i mishnah} teaches that when judges assess property values in order to authorize a creditor\u8217's seizure, and their assessment was off by a sixth or more \u8212- {\i shepichasu shtus o hosiru shtus} \u8212- the resulting transaction is void. Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel disagrees: the transaction stands, because voiding a court\u8217's assessment would undermine the authority of {\i beit din} \u8212- {\i mah koach beit din yafeh}. Rav Huna bar Chinena reported that Rav Nachman himself ruled in accordance with the {\i chachamim} (i.e., that the transaction is void). But Rav Nachman also said, in the context of the orphans, that one cannot protest a court-appointed division \u8212- on the same grounds of {\i mah koach beit din yafeh}. The {\i gemara} asks: does Rav Nachman really hold consistently by the principle of {\i mah koach beit din yafeh}, or does he apply it selectively?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} resolves the apparent contradiction: {\i lo kashya \u8212- ha detau, ha delo tau} \u8212- it is not difficult. Where the court made a significant error in assessment \u8212- where the valuation was objectively wrong by a substantial margin \u8212- the transaction is void. The principle of {\i mah koach beit din yafeh} does not immunize genuine mistakes. Where there was no error, however \u8212- where the court exercised sound judgment and made reasonable tradeoffs \u8212- the result stands even against later objection.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Preference vs.\u160 ?Error\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then refines further. If the orphan is simply unhappy with the portion he received \u8212- not because the court made a mistake, but because he had a different preference \u8212- he cannot protest. This is the case to which {\i mah koach beit din yafeh} directly applies. The orphan might say: I would have preferred the north field rather than the east field, and here are my reasons. That is a preference, not a correction of an error. The court was authorized to exercise judgment and make tradeoffs. Its decision on matters of preference is final.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The three-tier structure thus emerges: (1) no error at all \u8212- the division stands absolutely; (2) mere preference, no objective error \u8212- the division still stands, under {\i mah koach beit din yafeh}; (3) a genuine and significant error in assessment \u8212- the division may be voided.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Estate Division Rules\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Brothers Like Sales\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Nachman now shifts to a case without court involvement. When brothers divide their father\u8217's estate among themselves, {\i harei hein kelakuchos} \u8212- they are treated like buyers and sellers. The ordinary laws of commercial transactions govern the division.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ona\u8217'ah Thresholds\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Those laws produce three outcomes based on the discrepancy between what each person received: if the disparity was less than a sixth ({\i pachos mishtus}), the division is valid and stands as-is \u8212- {\i nikna mekach}, the transaction holds. If the disparity exceeded a sixth ({\i yeser al shtus}), the division is void \u8212- {\i batel mekach} \u8212- and the brothers must divide again. If the disparity was exactly a sixth ({\i shtus}), the division is valid, but the one who received more must return the difference \u8212- {\i kana u\u8217'machzir ona\u8217'ah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 These three outcomes mirror precisely the {\i halacha} of {\i ona\u8217'ah} in standard purchase-and-sale transactions, applied now to the estate division context.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shaliach Division\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava introduces an important qualification: the above rules \u8212- particularly the one holding that a discrepancy of less than a sixth does not affect the validity of the division \u8212- apply only where the brothers negotiated the division themselves. If one brother appointed a {\i shaliach} to represent him in the division, and the {\i shaliach} obtained a portion that was worth even slightly less than the other portions, the outcome is different. The principal can say: {\i letikuni shedartich ve\u8217'lo le\u8217'avusi} \u8212- I sent you to act for my benefit, not to my detriment. Since the agent\u8217's mandate is defined by the principal\u8217's interest, any outcome unfavorable to the principal \u8212- even below the {\i ona\u8217'ah} threshold \u8212- may be contestable. The practical concern this raises is obvious: anyone entering an estate division could simply appoint an agent and then, after the fact, use that agency relationship to void any outcome they disliked.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 If They Agree to B.D.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A further qualification applies to the {\i shtus} case specifically. The rule that a division with exactly a one-sixth discrepancy is valid but requires repayment of the difference holds only where the brothers made no special agreement about the basis of the division. If, however, they declared that the division should be conducted on the model of a {\i beit din} assessment \u8212- {\i niflog b\u8217'shuma de\u8217'veit din} \u8212- then even a one-sixth discrepancy requires no repayment. The reasoning follows from the ruling of Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel: a court assessment stands even when off by a sixth, because {\i mah koach beit din yafeh}. If the parties voluntarily adopted the {\i beit din} model, the same robustness applies to their private division.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 No Ona\u8217'ah on Land\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Another qualification applies to the medium of the division. The rule that a one-sixth discrepancy requires repayment \u8212- {\i kana u\u8217'machzir ona\u8217'ah} \u8212- applies only to {\i metalteli}, movable property. For {\i karka} \u8212- land \u8212- there is no {\i ona\u8217'ah}: {\i ein ona\u8217'ah le\u8217'karka\u8217'ot}. An overcharge or undercharge in a land transaction, even by more than a sixth in terms of assessed value, does not affect the validity of the transaction. Since most estate divisions involve land, this significantly limits the practical scope of the {\i ona\u8217'ah} rules in this context.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} raises the question of how far the {\i karka} exemption extends. If the {\i mishnah}\u8217's statement that {\i ein ona\u8217'ah le\u8217'karka\u8217'ot} means that land transactions are simply exempt from {\i ona\u8217'ah} in all cases \u8212- including where the discrepancy exceeds a sixth \u8212- then the entire framework of {\i shitus batel mekach} may not apply to land at all. Rashi\u8217's reading, however, appears to locate the {\i karka} rule specifically in the one-sixth case; whether a discrepancy greater than a sixth might void even a land transaction is a question Rashi\u8217's formulation leaves open.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Measurement Errors\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A final distinction must be drawn between two types of errors in a land division. The principle {\i ein ona\u8217'ah le\u8217'karka\u8217'ot} applies where the error was in the price assessment \u8212- that is, where the parties agreed on a certain acreage but differed in their valuation of that acreage, and the assessment of value turned out to be off. In such a case, the {\i karka} exemption applies and the transaction stands.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But where the error was in the measurement itself \u8212- the parties agreed that each would receive thirty acres, and a mistake in drawing the boundary lines left one person with twenty-eight and the other with thirty-two \u8212- that is a different category of error entirely. This is not a case of {\i ona\u8217'ah} (overcharge) but of {\i mekach ta\u8217'ut} (erroneous transaction): one party simply did not receive what was agreed upon. Rava rules accordingly: {\i kol davar she\u8217'bemiddah u\u8217'veshikul u\u8217'veminyan} \u8212- anything sold or divided by measure, weight, or count \u8212- if the quantity is wrong, the party who received less may demand correction {\i even if the discrepancy is less than the ona\u8217'ah threshold}. A numerical or measurement shortfall is not absorbed by the tolerance built into {\i ona\u8217'ah} law; it is a straightforward error that must be rectified regardless of its magnitude.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Kiddushin 43\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Shelichus Sources\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Daf Overview\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Today\u8217's {\i daf} is 43, picking up on 42b at the word {\i hasholeach}. At this point the {\i Gemara} has wrapped up its discussion of the basic sources for the principle of {\i shelichus} \u8212- derived from {\i terumah}, from {\i kiddushin} and {\i gittin}, and from {\i korbanos}. The {\i daf} now turns to a set of exceptions to the general rule of {\i shelichus}, centered on the concept of {\i ein shaliach lidvar aveira}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Fire via Irresponsible\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hasholeach Beyad Cheresh\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} on 42b begins with the {\i Mishnah}\u8217's case: {\i hasholeach es habeeirah beyad cheresh shoteh vekatan} \u8212- if one sends a fire through the hands of a {\i cheresh} (a deaf-mute), a {\i shoteh} (someone with a psychiatric illness who does not process reality clearly), or a {\i katan} (a minor). All three of these individuals are not considered legally responsible for their actions. The ruling is {\i patur midinei adam vechayav midinei shamayim}: the one who sent the fire is exempt from human legal liability but bears responsibility before Heaven, since he clearly caused a conflagration and destruction of property.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Liability Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 However, if the fire was transmitted through the hand of a {\i pikeach} \u8212- a mentally competent adult \u8212- then {\i pikeach chayav}: the adult who performed the act of burning is liable, and the one who sent him is exempt. This resembles the principle underlying organized criminal liability: the person who orchestrates and manipulates the scheme is, from a {\i halachic} standpoint, not directly responsible \u8212- only the person who actually lit the flame bears liability.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} then asks why the {\i meshaleach} is not liable through the principle of {\i shelichus}: {\i vamai, neima sheliach shel adam kemoso} \u8212- why not say that a person\u8217's {\i shaliach} is legally equivalent to himself? One might have thought that {\i shelichus} is simply irrelevant here because agency is about the delegation of legal authority, whereas the physical act of lighting a fire is not the kind of authority that can be transferred. Nevertheless, the {\i Gemara} engages the question directly.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ein Shaliach Lidvar\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara}\u8217's answer is: {\i shani hasam de\u8217'ein shaliach lidvar aveira} \u8212- it is different there because there is no {\i shaliach} for a matter of sin. The reason is stated as {\i divrei harav vedivrei hatalmid divrei mi shome\u8217'in}: between the words of the Master and the words of the student, whose words does one listen to? The point is that when you instruct a {\i shaliach} to commit a transgression, you cannot be held responsible for his action \u8212- he should have listened to God rather than to you. Ultimately, a person is responsible for his own choices, and the sin and the liability belong to the one who performed the act. This is an important principle that operates across the {\i halacha} of {\i shelichus}: {\i divrei harav vedivrei hatalmid divrei mi shome\u8217'in}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shaliach Awareness\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 An interesting question arises from this reasoning: does the principle of {\i ein shaliach lidvar aveira} apply even when the {\i shaliach} was unaware that the act was a sin? If, for example, the {\i meshaleach} asked someone to take what he believed to be his own car to be destroyed, and the {\i shaliach} had no reason to suspect otherwise, perhaps in that case the {\i meshaleach} would in fact bear liability \u8212- since the {\i shaliach} had no grounds to refuse. The {\i Gemara} does not pursue this question directly at this point, but it is worth flagging as a genuine conceptual tension.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Me\u8217'ilah & Shelichus\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Me\u8217'ilah Liability\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now cites a {\i beraita} regarding {\i me\u8217'ilah} \u8212- the inadvertent misappropriation of {\i hekdesh} funds. The ruling is: {\i shaliach shelo asah shelichuso shaliach ma\u8217'al, asah shelichuso ba\u8217'al habayis ma\u8217'al} \u8212- if the {\i shaliach} failed to carry out his mission (for instance, if sent to buy vegetables with {\i hekdesh} money he instead bought something else), then the {\i shaliach} himself committed {\i me\u8217'ilah}, since he was acting on his own rather than on behalf of the {\i meshaleach}. But if the {\i shaliach} carried out the mission as instructed, then the {\i ba\u8217'al habayis} committed {\i me\u8217'ilah} \u8212- the sin reverts to the one who sent him.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Challenge to Principle\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This creates an immediate difficulty: {\i vamai, neima ein shaliach lidvar aveira} \u8212- we should say that there is no {\i shaliach} for a matter of sin. How can the {\i ba\u8217'al habayis} be liable for {\i me\u8217'ilah} if the act was performed by the {\i shaliach}, and {\i ein shaliach lidvar aveira} should prevent attributing that act back to him? {\i Tosafos} raises a related point here: since {\i me\u8217'ilah} applies only in cases of {\i shogeg} (inadvertence), perhaps the principle of {\i ein shaliach lidvar aveira} as expressed through {\i divrei harav vedivrei hatalmid} does not apply in cases where the {\i shaliach} had no reason to refrain. Whether {\i ein shaliach lidvar aveira} is grounded in the principle that the {\i shaliach} should have known better, or whether it is a more fundamental rule that sin can only be attributed to the one who personally performs it, remains an open question \u8212- but the {\i Gemara} does not resolve it at this point.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gezeirah Shavah Answer\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} answers: {\i shani me\u8217'ilah diyalaf cheit cheit miTrumah} \u8212- {\i me\u8217'ilah} is different because the word {\i cheit} appears both in the laws of {\i terumah} and in the laws of {\i me\u8217'ilah}, creating a {\i gezeirah shavah}. Since {\i terumah} is one of the foundational sources for the institution of {\i shelichus} itself \u8212- {\i mah Trumah mishvach shliach af me\u8217'ilah mishvach shliach} \u8212- the same way {\i shelichus} applies for {\i terumah}, so too it applies for {\i me\u8217'ilah}, causing the liability to revert to the {\i meshaleach}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} then asks: {\i veneilaf minah} \u8212- why not use {\i me\u8217'ilah} as a paradigm and extend {\i shaliach lidvar aveira} across all of {\i halacha}? The answer is: {\i mishum dehavei me\u8217'ilah veshlichus yad shnei kesuvin habayim ke\u8217'echad} \u8212- because {\i me\u8217'ilah} and {\i shelichus yad} together constitute {\i shnei kesuvim habaim ke\u8217'echad}, two scriptural instances coming as one, {\i ve\u8217'ein melamdin} \u8212- and two scriptural sources that appear together as exceptions do not generate a general rule.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Shelichus Yad Exception\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shelichus Yad Concept\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} introduces the second exception: {\i shelichus yad}. This refers to the case of a {\i shomer} \u8212- a watchman \u8212- who uses another person\u8217's property without permission. If one is watching someone\u8217's object and instead uses it for himself (the classic case being a {\i shomer} who drives someone\u8217's car rather than merely guarding it), that unauthorized use makes the {\i shomer} liable for any damage that subsequently occurs to the item. Now, what if the {\i shomer} did not use it himself but sent someone else to use it? That too creates liability \u8212- making this another instance where {\i shaliach lidvar aveira} applies, despite it being a prohibited act.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is notable that in both {\i me\u8217'ilah} and {\i shelichus yad}, the operative concern is not only about religious transgression but also about civil liability \u8212- and it is precisely in those categories where the {\i Torah} carves out exceptions to {\i ein shaliach lidvar aveira}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two Exceptions Principle\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Since these two cases \u8212- {\i me\u8217'ilah} and {\i shelichus yad} \u8212- each appear independently in the {\i Torah} to establish {\i shaliach lidvar aveira}, and there are thereby two scriptural sources for the principle, they function as exceptions and not as a general rule. The logic is: had {\i shaliach lidvar aveira} been the rule, the {\i Torah} would have needed only one source. The fact that it appears twice signals that these are exceptional cases, and the governing principle across the rest of the {\i Torah} remains {\i ein shaliach lidvar aveira}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shammai vs.\u160 ?Hillel\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i beraita} elaborates on the {\i shelichus yad} case through a debate between {\i Beis Shammai} and {\i Beis Hillel}. The {\i pasuk} says {\i al kol devar pesha} \u8212- on any matter of sin \u8212- and also {\i asher lo shalach yado bimleches reiehu} \u8212- that one did not cast his hand against his fellow\u8217's property. {\i Beis Shammai} holds that the word {\i devar} (which can mean speech or intention) implies that even expressing a desire to use the property \u8212- a {\i dibbur}, a thought or declaration of intent \u8212- is sufficient to create {\i shelichus yad} liability: {\i lechayav al hamachshavah kema\u8217'aseh}, thought is treated like action. {\i Beis Hillel} holds that one is not liable until {\i sheyishlach bo yad} \u8212- actual physical use \u8212- citing the language of the {\i pasuk} itself.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Beis Shammai} challenges {\i Beis Hillel}: if literal {\i shlach yad} is required, what does {\i al kol devar pesha} add? {\i Beis Hillel} responds: {\i al kol devar pesha} addresses the case where you instructed your slave or your agent to use the property \u8212- {\i amar leavdo ushlucho} \u8212- so that even a {\i dibbur} in the form of directing another person creates liability. According to {\i Beis Hillel}, then, {\i al kol devar pesha} establishes the {\i shelichus yad} exception to {\i ein shaliach lidvar aveira} \u8212- specifically the case of instructing an agent.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} then notes: this all works well for {\i Beis Hillel}, who has two exceptions \u8212- {\i me\u8217'ilah} and {\i shelichus yad} \u8212- giving two scriptural sources. But for {\i Beis Shammai}, who holds that {\i al kol devar pesha} refers to thought rather than agency, only {\i me\u8217'ilah} remains as a scriptural source for {\i shaliach lidvar aveira}. Perhaps, then, {\i me\u8217'ilah} should be the rule rather than the exception. The {\i Gemara} resolves this by introducing a third exception.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Tevicha Umechira Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Stolen Livestock\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} identifies a third category: {\i tevicha umechira}. If a {\i gannav} steals an ox or a sheep and then slaughters or sells it, he is liable to pay four or five times the value. The {\i Gemara} now establishes that this four- or fivefold liability applies even if the {\i gannav} had a {\i shaliach} perform the slaughter or the sale on his behalf. This is yet another case where {\i shaliach lidvar aveira} obtains despite the underlying act being forbidden.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Deriving Shaliach\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Two {\i tannaim} derive this inclusion of the {\i shaliach} from different {\i pesukim}. {\i Devei Rabbi Yishmael} derives it from the word {\i o} (\u8220"or\u8221") in the phrase {\i o utvacho o mechoro} \u8212- \u8220"or slaughter it or sell it.\u8221" Just as selling inherently involves another party (the buyer), so too slaughtering can involve another party (a {\i shaliach}). {\i Devei Chizkiyah} derives it from the word {\i tachas} \u8212- the fourfold and fivefold payment is described as {\i tachas}, and this word is taken to include liability even when the {\i shaliach} performs the act.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Exceptions Not Rules\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 With three exceptions now identified \u8212- {\i me\u8217'ilah}, {\i shelichus yad}, and {\i tevicha umechira} \u8212- the {\i Gemara} is satisfied that these are exceptions to the rule, not paradigm cases. For {\i Beis Hillel}, two exceptions ({\i me\u8217'ilah} and {\i shelichus yad}) suffice to establish them as non-normative by the principle of {\i shnei kesuvim habaim ke\u8217'echad ein melamdin}. For {\i Beis Shammai}, who requires more examples to classify sources as exceptions, the third case of {\i tevicha umechira} provides the necessary additional instance.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 General Rule Confirmed\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shchutei Chutz Rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} then asks: according to those who hold {\i shnei kesuvim habaim ke\u8217'echad melamdin} \u8212- that two scriptural instances do generate a general rule rather than marking exceptions \u8212- how do we know that {\i ein shaliach lidvar aveira} is the baseline? The answer lies in the case of {\i shchutei chutz}, slaughtering a {\i korban} outside the {\i Beis Hamikdash}. The {\i Torah} states {\i dam yeichashev la\u8217'ish hahu dam shafach} \u8212- the blood shall be reckoned to {\i that man}, the one who spilled it. The word {\i hahu} \u8212- \u8220"that man\u8221" \u8212- specifies that it is the doer himself who is liable, not one who instructed another to do it. This verse, cited only once, functions as a categorical statement of the principle: only the doer is liable.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Double Hahu Analysis\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} notes further that the {\i pasuk} actually uses {\i ha\u8217'ish hahu} twice \u8212- once in {\i dam yeichashev la\u8217'ish hahu} and once in {\i venichras ha\u8217'ish hahu} (that man is {\i kares}). The {\i Gemara} asks: why doesn\u8217't the {\i Gemara} instead learn from the exceptions ({\i tevicha umechira} and {\i me\u8217'ilah}) and treat those as the rule? The answer is that the doubled {\i hahu} is the decisive factor. Since the first {\i hahu} already establishes that only the doer of {\i shchutei chutz} is liable, the second {\i hahu} is superfluous to that specific ruling \u8212- {\i im einu inyan legufo, tanehu inyan lechol hatorah kullah} \u8212- and is therefore applied to the entire {\i Torah} as a general rule: only the doer himself is liable, and the cases of {\i me\u8217'ilah}, {\i shelichus yad}, and {\i tevicha umechira} are the exceptions.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Alternative Drashim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 For those who hold {\i shnei kesuvim habaim ke\u8217'echad ein melamdin} \u8212- for whom the two or three exceptions already suffice to establish the rule \u8212- the two instances of {\i hahu} in the {\i shchutei chutz} passage must be put to other uses. The {\i Gemara} explains: one {\i hahu} teaches {\i lema\u8217'utei shnayim she\u8217'ochazin besakin veshochetin} \u8212- to exclude liability when two people hold the knife together and perform the act jointly, in which case neither is liable for {\i kares}. The other {\i hahu} teaches {\i hu velo anus, hu velo shogeg, hu velo mut\u8217'eh} \u8212- only one who acts willfully is liable for {\i kares}, not one who is coerced, acts inadvertently, or is mistaken. (In the case of {\i shogeg} one would still bring a {\i korban}, but there is no {\i kares}.) The {\i Gemara} notes that the other opinion does not independently derive these principles from the double {\i hahu} but arrives at them through other means.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Shammai on Murder\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Murder Instruction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} cites a {\i beraita}: {\i ha\u8217'omer lishlucho tze veharog es hanefesh} \u8212- if one tells his {\i shaliach} to go and commit murder \u8212- {\i hu chayav umshalcho patur}: the murderer is liable and the one who sent him is exempt. This reflects the general principle of {\i ein shaliach lidvar aveira}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shammai\u8217's Ruling\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 However, the {\i beraita} continues: in the name of {\i Chaggai HaNavi}, {\i sholcho chayav} \u8212- even the one who sent the {\i shaliach} is liable. The proof text is {\i oso haragta b\u8217'cherev b\u8217'nei Amon} \u8212- when the {\i Navi} holds {\i David} accountable for the death of {\i Uriyah HaChiti}, the language used is \u8220"you killed him by the sword of {\i b\u8217'nei Amon}.\u8221" Yet {\i David} did not personally kill {\i Uriyah}; he sent him to the front. Nevertheless, {\i David} is treated as the one who killed him. {\i Shammai HaZaken} therefore rules that the {\i meshaleach} is {\i chayav} even in a case of murder.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shammai\u8217's Logic\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What is the reasoning of {\i Shammai HaZaken}? {\i Kasavar shnei kesuvim habaim ke\u8217'echad melamdim} \u8212- he holds that when two scriptural instances establish a principle, that principle is extended as a general rule rather than treated as an exception. For {\i Shammai HaZaken}, who aligns with the position of {\i Beis Shammai}, there are only two exceptions: {\i me\u8217'ilah} and {\i tevicha umechira} \u8212- and those two do function as a general rule, establishing {\i yesh shaliach lidvar aveira} across the {\i Torah}. He does not derive the limiting principle from the double {\i hahu} of {\i shchutei chutz}, and therefore holds that the {\i meshaleach} can bear liability.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 B\u8217'Dinei Shamayim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} then offers an alternative interpretation of {\i Shammai HaZaken}\u8217's position. Perhaps {\i Shammai HaZaken} does in fact agree that there is no {\i shaliach lidvar aveira} in human courts \u8212- and when he says {\i sholcho chayav}, he means {\i chayav b\u8217'dinei shamayim}: liable before Heaven, treated as a murderer in the eyes of God, just as the {\i Navi} addresses {\i David} as one who killed {\i Uriyah}. On this reading, the dispute is not about whether the principle of {\i ein shaliach lidvar aveira} applies in {\i dinei adam}, but rather about the degree of heavenly accountability. The {\i tanna kamma} who says the {\i meshaleach} is {\i patur} would then be understood to be referring to human court liability only \u8212- surely everyone would agree that one who arranges a murder bears some degree of heavenly responsibility. The debate is therefore one of {\i dinei rabbah u\u8217'dinei zuta}: whether the {\i meshaleach} is treated in Heaven as an outright murderer or merely as one who incited murder.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Murder Exception\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} offers yet a further interpretation: {\i shani hasam d\u8217'gali rachmana oso haragta b\u8217'cherev b\u8217'nei Amon} \u8212- perhaps the {\i pasuk} concerning {\i David} and {\i Uriyah} teaches a specific {\i halacha} unique to murder. One might have thought that of all prohibitions, murder would be the one most governed by {\i ein shaliach lidvar aveira} \u8212- since {\i divrei harav vedivrei hatalmid} is at its most compelling when the act is taking a human life. Yet precisely because of the immense gravity of murder, the {\i Torah} singles it out to hold the {\i meshaleach} liable as well, in addition to the one who performed the act.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Those who disagree with {\i Shammai HaZaken} read the {\i pasuk} differently: {\i harei l\u8217'cha k\u8217'cherev b\u8217'nei Amon} \u8212- the comparison to the sword of {\i b\u8217'nei Amon} is meant to say the opposite. Just as one is not held liable when {\i b\u8217'nei Amon} kill people in the ordinary course of war, so too {\i David} should not be held liable for the death of {\i Uriyah}. The {\i pasuk} that {\i Shammai} uses to establish liability is turned on its head by his opponents to establish exemption. Moreover, those who hold {\i David} exempt argue that there was justification for {\i Uriyah}\u8217's death: {\i mored b\u8217'malchus havah} \u8212- he was in a state of rebellion against the king. The evidence is {\i Uriyah}\u8217's own words, in which he referred to {\i Yoav} as his master in the presence of {\i David}, thereby according authority to someone other than the king, or alternatively, by defying {\i David}\u8217's explicit order to return home to his wife.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Raba\u8217's Clarification\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sexual & Food Cases\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rava} adds an important clarification: even if one follows {\i Shammai HaZaken} and holds {\i yesh shaliach lidvar aveira} as a general principle, there remains a category of cases where {\i ein shaliach lidvar aveira} applies even according to {\i Shammai HaZaken}. That category is: if one instructs a {\i shaliach} to engage in sexual intercourse with a forbidden woman, or to eat {\i treif} food \u8212- {\i she\u8217'hu chayav v\u8217'sholcho patur} \u8212- the doer is liable and the sender is entirely exempt.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Pleasure Theory\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The reason is stated as a general principle: {\i she\u8217'lo matzinu b\u8217'chol haTorah kula ze neheneh v\u8217'ze mitchayev} \u8212- we have never found anywhere in the entire {\i Torah} that one person receives the {\i hana\u8217'ah} (the pleasure or benefit) while another person bears the liability. In these categories of sin, the transgression is not constituted merely by the physical act but by the {\i hana\u8217'ah} experienced in performing that act. An act can be attributed to a principal; pleasure cannot. The experience of {\i hana\u8217'ah} is irreducibly subjective and personal \u8212- it belongs only to the one who undergoes it, and there is no mechanism by which it can be transferred or attributed to another. This connects as well to the principle of {\i mita\u8217'sek b\u8217'chalavim v\u8217'arayos}: one cannot divorce the act from oneself when it is the {\i hana\u8217'ah} inherent in the doing that constitutes the prohibition.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Shaliach as Witness\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Initial Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now turns to an additional question within the laws of {\i shelichus}: {\i shaliach na\u8217'aseh eid} \u8212- can a {\i shaliach} serve as a witness to the very mission he was sent to perform? If, for example, two people are sent as {\i shluchim} to betroth a woman or to repay a debt, can those same individuals serve as {\i eidim} to attest that the act occurred? Or is the {\i shaliach} somehow considered equivalent to the principal \u8212- and just as the principal himself cannot be a witness to his own act, neither can the {\i shaliach}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rav} holds {\i shaliach na\u8217'aseh eid}: a {\i shaliach} can indeed serve as a witness. {\i Beis Rabbi Shila} holds {\i ein shaliach na\u8217'aseh eid}: he cannot. The reasoning of {\i Beis Rabbi Shila} is grounded in the principle {\i shlucho shel adam kemoso} \u8212- the {\i shaliach} is considered equivalent to the principal himself. Just as a party to a transaction cannot serve as a witness to it, so too the {\i shaliach}, who stands in the principal\u8217's stead, cannot serve as an independent witness. He becomes, in legal terms, {\i k\u8217'gufei} \u8212- like the principal\u8217's own self.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might initially suggest that the problem is simply that the {\i meshaleach} never explicitly designated the {\i shaliach} as a witness, only as an agent. But this cannot be the issue: if someone betroths a woman in the presence of two witnesses without explicitly saying \u8220"you are my witnesses,\u8221" the {\i kiddushin} is nonetheless valid. Witnesses do not need formal designation when they are observing the act itself \u8212- the only context requiring explicit designation is an admission of debt, where otherwise one might claim he was speaking in jest. So the absence of formal appointment as {\i eid} cannot be the objection.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Baraita Challenge\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} challenges {\i Rav}\u8217's position from a {\i beraita}: {\i amar l\u8217'shlosha tze\u8217'u v\u8217'kadshu li isha} \u8212- if one says to three people \u8220"go and betroth this woman for me,\u8221" {\i Beis Shammai} holds that one of them serves as the {\i shaliach} and the other two as independent {\i eidim} \u8212- you are one or the other, not both. {\i Beis Hillel} holds {\i kulam shluchim hen} \u8212- all three are considered {\i shluchim}, and since they are all {\i shluchim}, {\i v\u8217'ein shaliach na\u8217'aseh eid} \u8212- none of them can simultaneously serve as {\i eidim}. The result under {\i Beis Hillel} is that you need to find other witnesses entirely. Both schools of thought, with {\i Beis Hillel} being the stricter, appear to hold {\i ein shaliach na\u8217'aseh eid}. How can {\i Rav} maintain the contrary?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Position Reversal\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} answers that {\i Rav} rules like a different {\i tanna}: a {\i beraita} in the name of {\i Rabbi Natan} presents {\i Beis Shammai} as holding {\i shaliach v\u8217'ed echad} \u8212- a {\i shaliach} plus one additional witness suffice (meaning the {\i shaliach} himself can serve as one of the two required {\i eidim}), while {\i Beis Hillel} holds {\i shaliach u\u8217'shnei eidim} \u8212- the {\i shaliach} cannot be counted, and two additional witnesses are required. The {\i Gemara} then notes: but would {\i Rav} rule like {\i Beis Shammai}? The resolution is {\i ipech} \u8212- reverse the positions in that {\i beraita}: it is {\i Beis Hillel} who holds {\i shaliach na\u8217'aseh eid}, and {\i Beis Shammai} who holds {\i ein shaliach na\u8217'aseh eid}. On this reversal, {\i Rav} is in agreement with {\i Beis Hillel}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Final Halacha\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rav Acha bar Rava} teaches the debate between {\i Rav} and {\i Beis Rabbi Shila} in the reverse of the original formulation: {\i Rav} holds {\i ein shaliach na\u8217'aseh eid}, while {\i Beis Rabbi Shila} holds {\i shaliach na\u8217'aseh eid}. Regardless of which formulation one follows, the {\i Gemara} concludes: {\i v\u8217'hilcheta shaliach na\u8217'aseh eid} \u8212- the {\i halacha} is that a {\i shaliach} can serve as a witness, though the theoretical grounding for this ruling requires considerable effort given the weight of the opposing sources.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Shaliach Eid Applications\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two Shluchim Rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rava} in the name of {\i Rav Nachman} now states the operative {\i halacha} flowing from this principle: {\i amar l\u8217'shnayim tze\u8217'u v\u8217'kadshu li isha, hen shluchav v\u8217'hen eidav} \u8212- if one says to two people \u8220"go betroth this woman for me,\u8221" they are his {\i shluchim} and they can also serve as his {\i eidim} to the act of {\i kiddushin}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gerushin Concern\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The same rule applies {\i b\u8217'gerushin} \u8212- in the case of divorce. If two people are sent to deliver a {\i get}, they can serve as witnesses to its delivery, with one caveat: there is a concern that one of them might have set his eye on the woman \u8212- that is, he may want to marry her himself \u8212- and this personal interest could compromise his reliability as a witness. Nevertheless, the concern is addressed, as will be explained.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mamonot Concern\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The same rule applies {\i b\u8217'dinei mamonot} \u8212- if two people are sent to repay a debt, they too can serve as witnesses to the repayment. Here the concern is different: perhaps they will pocket the money and then falsely testify that they paid the creditor. Again, however, the concern is addressed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Why Concerns Addressed\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} explains why all three cases remain valid despite these concerns, and why it was necessary to state all three. Had the ruling been stated only for {\i kiddushin}, one might have thought it applies specifically because the woman\u8217's becoming betrothed makes her forbidden to the {\i shaliach} himself \u8212- {\i l\u8217'issurei kasi} \u8212- thereby eliminating any personal interest he might have, and thus any concern of {\i nogea b\u8217'davar}. One could not have inferred from {\i kiddushin} that the same applies to {\i gerushin}, since in {\i gerushin} the {\i shaliach} may wish to marry the woman after her divorce and thus has a stake in the outcome. However, in {\i gerushin} there are two {\i shluchim} \u8212- and since neither can marry both parties, nor can both equally benefit, the concern is neutralized: {\i d\u8217'ita l\u8217'bei trai lo chazi} \u8212- she cannot go to both of them, so you cannot say both are equally {\i nogea b\u8217'davar}. And the case of {\i dinei mamonot} cannot be inferred from {\i gerushin}, since with money there is a more tangible concern that both witnesses will conspire to keep the funds for themselves. Nevertheless, the {\i Gemara} concludes {\i tzrichi} \u8212- all three must be stated, and in all three the {\i shluchim} are valid {\i eidim}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} then deepens the analysis of the monetary case, asking: is there in fact a concern of {\i nogea b\u8217'eidus} \u8212- a disqualifying personal interest in the outcome? The issue turns on a separate {\i halachic} principle: {\i hamalveh et chaveiro b\u8217'eidim tzarich l\u8217'faro b\u8217'eidim} \u8212- if one lends money in the presence of witnesses, must he repay it in the presence of witnesses? If the answer is yes, then the {\i shluchim}, if they fail to deliver the money to the creditor, would have to repay it to the {\i meshaleach} \u8212- and they would not be believed to say they had already returned it. This means that by testifying that they paid the creditor, they are freeing themselves from liability to the {\i meshaleach}, making them {\i nogea b\u8217'eidus}. The {\i Gemara} resolves: {\i le\u8217'olam kasavar ein tzarich l\u8217'faro b\u8217'eidim} \u8212- the {\i halacha} is that one need not repay a debt-by-witnesses in the presence of witnesses. Therefore, if the {\i shluchim} claimed they had returned the money to the {\i meshaleach}, they would simply be believed. Since they could equally have said \u8220"we returned it to you,\u8221" they gain nothing by testifying that they paid the creditor instead. They are thus not {\i nogea b\u8217'eidus}, and their testimony stands.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Shevuas Hesess Impact\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Pre-Hesess Rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Before the rabbinic enactment of {\i shevuas hesess}, the analysis above held cleanly. The {\i shluchim} could serve as {\i eidim} in monetary matters because they had nothing to gain from their testimony: {\i malveh et chaveiro b\u8217'eidim ein tzarich l\u8217'faro b\u8217'eidim} \u8212- and they were equally believed whether they said they paid the creditor or returned the funds to the {\i meshaleach}. There was therefore no interest on their part, no disqualifying {\i nogea b\u8217'eidus}, and their testimony was valid.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Post-Hesess Problem\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 After {\i shevuas hesess} was introduced by the {\i Rabbanan} \u8212- under which virtually any monetary claim carries with it a requirement that the defendant take an oath \u8212- the calculus changed. Now, if the {\i shluchim} were to admit that they had not paid the creditor, the {\i meshaleach} could demand repayment, and they would be required to take a {\i shevuah} to establish that they had returned the funds to him. By testifying instead that they paid the creditor, they free themselves from that {\i shevuah} obligation. They thus have a tangible gain from their testimony \u8212- they are now {\i nogea b\u8217'eidus}, disqualified as witnesses.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 New Outcome\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The practical result under the post-{\i hesess} regime is as follows: since the {\i shluchim} can no longer serve as {\i eidim}, their testimony is not accepted. Instead, the {\i shluchim} take an oath that they did in fact pay the creditor. The creditor in turn takes an oath that he never received payment. The consequence is that the {\i loveh} \u8212- the original debtor who sent the {\i shluchim} \u8212- must pay the {\i malveh} again. The irony is considerable: it is specifically their {\i shevuah} obligation to the {\i loveh} that disqualifies the {\i shluchim} from serving as witnesses \u8212- and it is precisely that disqualification that leaves the {\i loveh} without recourse and compelled to pay twice. That is the unfortunate outcome when {\i shevuas hesess} intersects with the institution of {\i shaliach na\u8217'aseh eid}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Kiddushin 44\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Father vs.\u160 ?Father+Daught\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishna Intro & Reish Lak\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The daf opens in the middle of 43b, approximately fifteen lines from the bottom, at the break in the text. The {\i mishna} states: {\i ha-ish mekadesh et bito} \u8212- a man may betroth off his daughter when she is a {\i na\u8217'arah}, meaning between the ages of twelve and twelve-and-a-half. Even though she is halachically an adult at this stage, her father retains power over her with respect to marriage. The {\i gemara} will explore the precise degree to which the {\i na\u8217'arah} herself also possesses some form of independent agency, or whether authority resides exclusively with her father.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} opens by citing a {\i mishna} from Gittin: {\i na\u8217'arah me\u8217'orasa, hi ve-aviha mekablin et gitah} \u8212- a betrothed {\i na\u8217'arah} or her father may receive her {\i get}. Thus, following {\i kiddushin} without {\i nissu\u8217'in}, a {\i get} may be delivered either to the daughter or to the father.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Yehuda\u8217's Principle\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yehuda dissents from this ruling: {\i ein shtei yadayim zachot ke-echas} \u8212- two hands cannot act as one. There must be a single controlling entity. Accordingly, Rabbi Yehuda holds that only the father may receive the {\i get}, not the daughter. The dispute between Rabbi Yehuda and the {\i tanna kamma} turns on whether the father and daughter constitute two equally empowered recipients, or whether one is implicitly acting as agent for the other \u8212- or whether, more fundamentally, control resides wholly in the father.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Watching & Protecting Get\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A separate {\i halakha} is introduced: anyone who cannot protect and watch a {\i get} cannot be divorced. This raises the question of whether the requirement is about the act of receiving the {\i get} \u8212- such that if the recipient cannot guard it, the delivery is ineffective \u8212- or whether it is a question about the woman\u8217's status, specifically whether she must understand that the divorce means she will no longer be considered married. This has implications for cases involving a {\i shota} or a {\i ketanah}, and it is debated among the {\i Rishonim}, though the {\i gemara} here does not directly explore it. The focus of the sugya is the earlier question of whether the {\i na\u8217'arah} and her father share equally in the power to receive the {\i get}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Reish Lakish Parallel\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Reish Lakish rules: {\i ke-machlokes le-gerushin kach machlokes le-kiddushin} \u8212- the very same dispute between Rabbi Yehuda and the {\i tanna kamma} regarding {\i gerushin} applies equally to {\i kiddushin}. His reasoning is that once a girl reaches {\i na\u8217'arah} and is technically an adult, she may have equal standing alongside her father in both contexts \u8212- the father may act on her behalf and she may act on her own behalf, much as two parties who both hold power of attorney while also retaining the right to sign in their own name.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Yochanan\u8217's View\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yochanan disagrees sharply: {\i machlokes le-gerushin aval kiddushin divrei hakol aviha velo hi} \u8212- the dispute between Rabbi Yehuda and the {\i tanna kamma} applies only to {\i gittin}, but regarding {\i kiddushin}, everyone agrees that authority belongs exclusively to the father. Rabbi Yochanan will offer a principled reason for this distinction, to which the {\i gemara} now turns.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Will vs.\u160 ?Delivery Logic\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Consent Distinction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yose b\u8217'Rabbi Chanina explains the reasoning behind Rabbi Yochanan\u8217's position. By {\i kiddushin}, where the process requires {\i da\u8217'at} \u8212- agreement, will, and decision \u8212- that authority is vested in the father alone: {\i kiddushin demida\u8217'atah aviha velo hi}. By {\i gerushin}, however, a woman can be divorced {\i be\u8217'al korchah}, against her will; the husband delivers the {\i get} and the divorce takes effect regardless of her consent. Because {\i gerushin} does not require any active exercise of will on the receiving side, the question of who receives the {\i get} is a question not of power but of delivery \u8212- who serves as the correct address.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gerushin Returns to Fath\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yose b\u8217'Rabbi Chanina articulates the distinction as follows: {\i gerushin de-machneset atzmah li-reshut aviha, bein hi uvein aviha} \u8212- by {\i gerushin}, the effect of the divorce is that the woman re-enters her father\u8217's authority. She is still a {\i na\u8217'arah}, the {\i kiddushin} was without {\i nissu\u8217'in}, and the {\i get} returns her to her father\u8217's domain. Because her return benefits the father\u8217's sphere of control, the {\i rabanan} regard her as an extension of the father in the receiving act. Whether she or he receives the {\i get}, the outcome flows back to him.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kiddushin Leaves Father\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The contrast is stark: {\i kiddushin de-mafkaat atzmah mi-reshut aviha} \u8212- {\i kiddushin} removes her from her father\u8217's domain. It is a transaction that diminishes his authority over her. Therefore {\i aviha velo hi}: only the father can initiate or consent to that process. The {\i na\u8217'arah} cannot be seen as an extension of her father when the very transaction at hand is one that pulls her away from him. This, according to Rabbi Yochanan, is why the {\i rabanan} agree with Rabbi Yehuda in the case of {\i kiddushin}, even though they dispute him in the case of {\i gittin}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Ma\u8217'amar Exception\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ma\u8217'amar Counterexample\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} immediately challenges Rabbi Yochanan\u8217's framework. {\i Ve-harei ma\u8217'amar} \u8212- what about {\i ma\u8217'amar}? When a married woman\u8217's husband dies without children, the surviving brothers are obligated in {\i yibbum}. Biblically, the only operative act is {\i yibbum} itself; however, the Rabbis instituted a form of rabbinic {\i kiddushin} called {\i ma\u8217'amar}, by which one of the brothers can, as it were, claim her, thereby preventing the other brothers from performing {\i yibbum}. This {\i ma\u8217'amar} functions as a {\i kiddushin} that takes her further away from her father\u8217's domain \u8212- {\i de-mafkaat atzmah mi-reshut aviha}, precisely the condition Rabbi Yochanan identified as limiting authority to the father alone.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ketanah vs.\u160 ?Na\u8217'arah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Yet the {\i mishna} regarding {\i ma\u8217'amar} distinguishes between a {\i ketanah} and a {\i na\u8217'arah}: {\i ketanah min ha-erusin, ein osin bah ma\u8217'amar ela mida\u8217'at aviha} \u8212- if she was a minor who had been betrothed through {\i erusin} and her husband died, {\i ma\u8217'amar} may only be performed with the father\u8217's consent. However, {\i ve-hana\u8217'arah, bein mida\u8217'at atzmah bein mida\u8217'at aviha} \u8212- if she is a {\i na\u8217'arah}, the {\i ma\u8217'amar} is valid whether done with her agreement or with her father\u8217's agreement. This appears to directly challenge Rabbi Yochanan: {\i ma\u8217'amar} removes her from her father\u8217's domain just as {\i kiddushin} does, and yet the {\i mishna} permits it to be effected through the {\i na\u8217'arah} herself, not only through the father.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} accordingly suspends the original framing: given this challenge from {\i ma\u8217'amar}, the in-domain / out-of-domain principle cannot serve as the fundamental explanation. {\i Ela i itamar hachi itamar} \u8212- if this tradition was stated, here is the correct version. Rabbi Yose b\u8217'Rabbi Chanina\u8217's explanation of Rabbi Yochanan\u8217's position must be restated:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Kiddushin de-mida\u8217'atah, aviha velo hi; gerushin de-va\u8217'al korchah, bein hi uvein aviha.} The real distinction is not about whether she is moving into or out of the father\u8217's domain, but about whether the transaction requires {\i da\u8217'at} \u8212- active will and consent. {\i Kiddushin} requires consent; therefore only the father, who holds the authority, can exercise that consent. {\i Gerushin} operates {\i be\u8217'al korchah}, against the woman\u8217's will if necessary; therefore neither she nor the father is exercising power \u8212- they are simply the delivery address, and it makes no difference which of them receives the {\i get}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ba\u8217'al Korcha Resolution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 With this reframing, {\i ma\u8217'amar} can be explained. The {\i gemara} asks: does {\i ma\u8217'amar} not require {\i da\u8217'at} as a form of rabbinic {\i kiddushin}? The answer: {\i hatam be-ma\u8217'amar de-va\u8217'al korchah} \u8212- {\i ma\u8217'amar} can be performed against her will, just as {\i yibbum} itself can be effectuated against her will. The {\i zikah}, the quasi-{\i kiddushin} that transfers from the deceased husband to the surviving brothers, means that the relationship has already been partially established; the subsequent acts of {\i yibbum} or {\i ma\u8217'amar} are merely actualizations of a pre-existing bond. Just as {\i yibbum} is effective even without consent \u8212- though it is not permitted in such circumstances \u8212- {\i ma\u8217'amar} similarly operates {\i be\u8217'al korchah}, rendering the receiving party once again simply the delivery address rather than an active agent of will.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi vs.\u160 ?Rabbanan\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} notes that this explanation follows the position of Rabbi rather than the {\i rabanan}. A {\i baraita} records: {\i ha-oseh ma\u8217'amar bi-yevamto shelo mida\u8217'atah} \u8212- one who performs {\i ma\u8217'amar} with his {\i yevamah} without her consent: {\i Rabbi omer kanah}, Rabbi holds that it takes effect, while {\i va-chachamim omrim lo kanah}, the Sages say it does not. Rabbi\u8217's reasoning is {\i gemar mi-bi\u8217'ah di-yevamah}: just as {\i bi\u8217'ah} with the {\i yevamah} takes effect even against her will, so too {\i ma\u8217'amar} takes effect without consent. The {\i rabanan}, however, hold that {\i ma\u8217'amar} is derived from {\i kiddushin} \u8212- {\i mah kiddushin mida\u8217'atah af hacha nami mida\u8217'atah} \u8212- and therefore requires consent just as {\i kiddushin} does. Though the {\i halakha} follows the {\i rabanan}, the {\i baraita} allows the {\i gemara} to explain the {\i mishna} that permits {\i ma\u8217'amar} through the {\i na\u8217'arah} as following Rabbi\u8217's view.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Mishna Reconciliatio (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishna Conclusion\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now notes that this entire framework actually works well, based on the closing phrase of that {\i mishna}: {\i midekatani seifa ma she-ein ken be-kiddushin} \u8212- the {\i mishna} itself concludes by contrasting {\i ma\u8217'amar} with {\i kiddushin}, noting that what applies to {\i ma\u8217'amar} does not apply to {\i kiddushin}. This closing phrase powerfully corroborates Rabbi Yochanan\u8217's position: {\i kiddushin} is different, and only the father controls it. {\i Ma\u8217'amar} is the exception, and the {\i mishna} itself flags it as such.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This raises a sharp challenge to Reish Lakish. If {\i kiddushin} belongs only to the father and {\i ma\u8217'amar} is handled differently, that structure implies that the {\i rabanan} \u8212- who in Reish Lakish\u8217's reading would say both she and the father have equal say in {\i kiddushin} \u8212- should agree by {\i kiddushin} that it can be effected through the daughter. Yet the {\i mishna} implies otherwise. The {\i gemara} asks: {\i neima tehvei tiyufta de-Reish Lakish} \u8212- should we declare this a refutation of Reish Lakish?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Reish Lakish responds: {\i ha mani Rabbi Yehuda hi} \u8212- this {\i mishna} follows Rabbi Yehuda. Rabbi Yehuda is the one who says {\i ein shtei yadayim zachot ke-achat}; it is always only the father, whether for {\i gittin} or for {\i kiddushin}. Under Rabbi Yehuda\u8217's view, even {\i gittin} goes only to the father, so {\i kiddushin} naturally does as well. The {\i mishna}\u8217's contrast between {\i ma\u8217'amar} and {\i kiddushin} is therefore a contrast within Rabbi Yehuda\u8217's framework where both {\i gittin} and {\i kiddushin} are exclusively the father\u8217's domain, with {\i ma\u8217'amar} as the lone exception.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This, however, generates a new question. If Reish Lakish attributes the {\i mishna} to Rabbi Yehuda, then under Rabbi Yehuda\u8217's view, both {\i kiddushin} and {\i gittin} go only to the father. Why then does {\i ma\u8217'amar} permit the daughter? The {\i gemara} asks further: if it is Rabbi Yehuda\u8217's position, the {\i mishna} should have contrasted {\i ma\u8217'amar} not only with {\i kiddushin} but also with {\i gerushin} \u8212- {\i ma she-ein ken be-kiddushin, ma she-ein ken be-gerushin miba\u8217'ei lei}. The {\i gemara} responds: {\i in hachi nami} \u8212- indeed that is so. The reason the {\i mishna} phrases it as a contrast to {\i kiddushin} specifically is because {\i ma\u8217'amar} resembles {\i kiddushin}: {\i aidi de-tana ma\u8217'amar de-dami le-kiddushin, tani nami ma she-ein ken be-kiddushin} \u8212- since {\i ma\u8217'amar} is a form of {\i kiddushin}, the {\i mishna} contrasts it with {\i kiddushin}. But the real implication is that {\i ma\u8217'amar} is the exception to the rule that applies to both {\i kiddushin} and {\i gittin}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Zekuka V\u8217'Omedet\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This leads to the question: why does {\i ma\u8217'amar} allow the {\i na\u8217'arah} herself to be the recipient even according to Rabbi Yehuda? The {\i gemara} answers: {\i ho\u8217'il ve-zekuka ve-omedet} \u8212- because she is already bound and standing. The {\i zikah}, the quasi-{\i kiddushin} that attached to her upon her husband\u8217's death, means that the subsequent step of {\i ma\u8217'amar} is simply an actualization of a pre-existing bond. It is uniquely easy for this relationship to take effect, and therefore a transaction directed at the {\i na\u8217'arah} herself is sufficient to actualize it. Under Rabbi Yehuda\u8217's system, this makes {\i ma\u8217'amar} a total exception: both {\i gittin} and {\i kiddushin} go exclusively to the father, but {\i ma\u8217'amar} \u8212- because of {\i zekuka ve-omedet} \u8212- can go to the daughter herself.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Hashta Framework\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Yochanan Confirmed\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Ha-shta de-asit le-hachi} \u8212- now that we have established that {\i ma\u8217'amar} is exceptional because of {\i zekuka ve-omedet}, the challenge that {\i ma\u8217'amar} posed to Rabbi Yochanan dissolves as well. The {\i gemara} states: {\i Rabbi Yochanan nami lo tikshei lach me-ikara} \u8212- you need not have troubled Rabbi Yochanan from the outset with the {\i ma\u8217'amar} objection. Whether one employs the original explanation of into-the-domain versus out-of-the-domain, or the revised explanation of {\i be\u8217'al korchah} versus {\i mida\u8217'atah}, {\i ma\u8217'amar} stands apart from both frameworks by virtue of {\i zekuka ve-omedet}: {\i shani ma\u8217'amar ho\u8217'il ve-zekuka ve-omedet}. The upshot is that Rabbi Yochanan\u8217's core position \u8212- that by {\i kiddushin} all the {\i rabanan} agree authority is the father\u8217's alone, with {\i gittin} forming an exception because it operates {\i be\u8217'al korchah} \u8212- remains intact.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 To summarize the two positions as they now stand: Reish Lakish holds that for the {\i rabanan}, the {\i na\u8217'arah} and her father are equally empowered in all these contexts \u8212- both can receive a {\i get}, both can effect {\i kiddushin}, both can receive a {\i ma\u8217'amar}. The {\i mishna} limiting power to the father alone reflects only Rabbi Yehuda\u8217's stricter view. Rabbi Yochanan holds that for the {\i rabanan}, the father is the primary and essentially exclusive locus of authority; {\i gittin} forms an exception because delivery of a {\i get} operates {\i be\u8217'al korchah}, rendering the recipient merely a mailbox; and {\i ma\u8217'amar} is further explained through {\i zekuka ve-omedet}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Mishna Reconciliatio (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tanna Identification\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 With Rabbi Yochanan\u8217's position now confirmed, the {\i gemara} turns to the attribution of the later {\i mishna} in the {\i perek} \u8212- the one concerning a man who says to a woman: {\i hitkadshi li be-tamra zu, hitkadshi li be-zo} \u8212- \u8220"be betrothed to me with this date, be betrothed to me with this one.\u8221" When the word \u8220"be betrothed\u8221" is repeated each time, each date constitutes a separate act of {\i kiddushin}, and validity depends on whether any individual date is worth a {\i perutah}. If, however, he says \u8220"be betrothed to me with this and this and this,\u8221" omitting the repeated verb, the acts are treated as one combined transaction, and even if none individually is worth a {\i perutah}, their combined value suffices.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} asks: {\i man tana hitkadshi hitkadshi} \u8212- whose view is this, that the {\i mishna} hinges on whether the verb is repeated? Rava answers: {\i Rabbi Shimon hi} \u8212- it is Rabbi Shimon. Rabbi Shimon holds by {\i shevuot} that a person is only liable separately for each one if he says \u8220"I swear\u8221" for each one individually \u8212- {\i ad she-yomar shvuah le-chol echad ve-echad}. Our {\i mishna} similarly requires the repeated verb to create separate acts. This is not the view of Rabbi Yehuda, who holds by {\i shevuot} that even without repeating the word {\i shevuah}, each oath target constitutes a separate liability \u8212- {\i shvuah lo lecha, lo lecha, lo lecha, chayav al kol achat ve-achat}. If Rabbi Yehuda were the {\i tanna} of this {\i mishna}, then even without repeating the verb \u8220"be betrothed,\u8221" each date would be a separate act of {\i kiddushin}, which contradicts our {\i mishna}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i baraita} confirms this: {\i zeh ha-klal, klal eino chayav ela achat; perat, chayav al kol achat ve-achat} \u8212- this is Rabbi Meir\u8217's formulation. Rabbi Yehuda explicitly says that even a general construction yields separate liabilities for each. Rabbi Shimon requires the word {\i shvuah} to be repeated for each one. The {\i gemara} concludes: the later {\i mishna} is Rabbi Shimon\u8217's. This means Reish Lakish cannot attribute our first {\i mishna} \u8212- which limits {\i kiddushin} authority to the father \u8212- to Rabbi Yehuda, since the second {\i mishna} in the same {\i perek} is demonstrably not Rabbi Yehuda. The {\i gemara} therefore resolves: {\i kula Rabbi Shimon hi, u-vi-shlichus savar lah ke-Rabbi Yehuda} \u8212- the entire {\i perek} follows Rabbi Shimon, but on the specific question of who controls {\i kiddushin} and {\i gittin}, Rabbi Shimon happens to agree with Rabbi Yehuda\u8217's position. The two debates \u8212- about repeated verbs in betrothal language and about who controls the {\i na\u8217'arah}\u8217's {\i kiddushin} \u8212- are entirely independent, and the fact that Rabbi Shimon agrees with Rabbi Yehuda on the latter does not require them to share any deeper framework.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 First Mishna Test\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Father Alone Principle\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now subjects the competing positions to a test from the very first {\i mishna} of the {\i perek}: {\i ish mekadesh et bito keshe-hi na\u8217'arah, bo u-vishucho} \u8212- a man can betroth off his daughter when she is a {\i na\u8217'arah}, whether he does so himself or through his {\i shaliach}. The {\i mishna} states {\i bo u-vishucho in}, meaning yes to him and his {\i shaliach}, implying {\i bo u-vishucho lo} \u8212- not her and not her {\i shaliach}. This strongly implies that only the father has authority in {\i kiddushin}, not the {\i na\u8217'arah} herself. This aligns perfectly with Rabbi Yochanan\u8217's position.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Reish Lakish Defense\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} asks: {\i ve-tiyuvta de-Reish Lakish} \u8212- is this a refutation of Reish Lakish? Reish Lakish responds once again: {\i ha nami Rabbi Yehuda hi} \u8212- this too follows Rabbi Yehuda. Under the {\i rabanan}, whom Reish Lakish reads as granting equal power to both father and daughter in both {\i kiddushin} and {\i gittin}, the {\i na\u8217'arah} could indeed make {\i kiddushin} on her own. The {\i mishna} that limits this to the father alone reflects only Rabbi Yehuda\u8217's stricter position. The {\i gemara} then challenges this attribution, pointing out that {\i u-mi matzit mokmat la ke-Rabbi Yehuda} \u8212- can you really attribute our {\i mishna} to Rabbi Yehuda? The later {\i mishna} in the same {\i perek} about betrothing with repeated dates is demonstrably Rabbi Shimon, not Rabbi Yehuda, as established above. If the {\i stam} voice of the {\i mishna} is Rabbi Shimon elsewhere in the {\i perek}, one cannot attribute the opening {\i mishna} to Rabbi Yehuda. The {\i gemara} concludes as described: {\i kula Rabbi Shimon hi}, but Rabbi Shimon holds like Rabbi Yehuda on the specific question of the {\i na\u8217'arah}\u8217's authority.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Beit Midrash Consensus\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Avin\u8217's Report\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} narrates a brief episode that encapsulates how the {\i beit midrash} community resolved this debate. One day Rav Assi did not attend the {\i beit midrash}. He encountered Rabbi Zeira and asked him for the substance of that day\u8217's discussion. Rabbi Zeira replied that he himself had also not attended, but that Rabbi Avin had gone, and Rabbi Avin reported: {\i chavrusah kulah ke-Rav Yochanan} \u8212- the entire group came together in agreement with Rabbi Yochanan. By {\i gittin}, perhaps the {\i na\u8217'arah} can receive the {\i get} \u8212- because she is {\i be\u8217'al korchah}, merely a mailbox. But by {\i kiddushin}, only the father; the {\i na\u8217'arah} has no authority.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Screaming Crane\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Ve-tzovach Reish Lakish ke-kruchiya} \u8212- and Reish Lakish shrieked like a crane in protest. He cited the {\i pasuk} that equates divorce and remarriage \u8212- {\i ve-yatza ve-hayeta} \u8212- and argued that if the {\i na\u8217'arah} can receive the {\i get} in divorce, she should equally be able to effect {\i kiddushin} in marriage. {\i Ve-leika de-ashgach bei} \u8212- and no one paid attention to him. The consensus held with Rabbi Yochanan.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is worth noting that this phrase \u8212- {\i tzovach ke-kruchiya ve-leit de-ashgach bei} \u8212- became a recurring expression among the {\i Rishonim} and later {\i poskim} when describing a situation in which a scholar protests a prevailing practice and is simply ignored.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Avin Identity\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Nachman bar Yitzchak adds a caveat: {\i ana lo Rav Avin bar Rebbe Chiya ve-lo Rav Avin bar Kahana, ella Rav Avin stam} \u8212- he does not know whether the reporting Rav Avin was Rav Avin the son of Rabbi Chiya or Rav Avin the son of Kahana; he only knows it was a Rav Avin. The {\i gemara} asks: {\i le-mai nafka mina} \u8212- what practical difference does the identity make? {\i Le-mirmi didei adidei} \u8212- it matters for purposes of identifying contradictions between his statements. If one wishes to challenge his testimony by citing something he said elsewhere, the precise identity of the speaker must be known.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Shaliach Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hand vs.\u160 ?Courtyard\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now poses a new question, which Rava directs to Rav Nachman: {\i na\u8217'arah mahu she-ta\u8217'aseh shaliach lekabbel gitah miyad ba\u8217'alah} \u8212- may a {\i na\u8217'arah} appoint a {\i shaliach} to receive her {\i get} from her husband\u8217's hand? We have established that according to Rabbi Yochanan, a {\i na\u8217'arah} can at least receive her own {\i get} \u8212- the {\i rabanan} permit delivery to her even if only because she functions as a valid address. But can she go further and delegate that receptive function to a {\i shaliach} of her own choosing?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava frames the question through a conceptual dichotomy: {\i keyad aviha damya o ke-chatzer aviha damya} \u8212- is she like her father\u8217's hand, or like her father\u8217's {\i chatzer}? A {\i chatzer} \u8212- a courtyard \u8212- is property: things placed within it belong to its owner through the passive mechanics of {\i kinyan chatzer}, but it exercises no independent function. A hand, in contrast, is an extension of a person\u8217's active agency. If she is like her father\u8217's hand, then \u8212- {\i mah yado mashvei shaliach, af hi nami mashveia shaliach} \u8212- just as the father\u8217's hand represents his power and he can therefore appoint a {\i shaliach}, so too she could appoint a {\i shaliach}. If she is merely like his {\i chatzer}, she has no active function at all and could not appoint a {\i shaliach}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Until Reaches Hand\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} notes that the very premise of {\i chatzer} as a complete analogy already strains credulity. After all, the {\i gemara} states: {\i ve-ad de-mata gita le-yadah lo migarshana} \u8212- until the {\i get} reaches her hand, she is not divorced. This implies that her hand is already the operative instrument for receiving the {\i get}; calling her merely a passive {\i chatzer} seems incompatible with this.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Slave Watchman Cases\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava himself addresses the question by analogy from a case involving a slave. If someone wrote a {\i get} and placed it in the hand of his wife\u8217's slave: if the slave is sleeping and she is watching over him, {\i harei zeh get} \u8212- it is a valid {\i get}, because the slave functions like a {\i chatzer mishta\u8217'meret}, a guarded courtyard. When property is secured within a guarded space, {\i kinyan} is effected through the owner of the space. But {\i na\u8217'ur, eino get} \u8212- if the slave is awake, it is not a valid {\i get}. An awake slave can walk away, and is therefore not truly under the woman\u8217's control; he cannot serve as her {\i chatzer mishta\u8217'meret}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} draws the implication: if we were to treat the {\i na\u8217'arah} as literally her father\u8217's {\i chatzer}, then when the {\i get} is delivered to her hand, that should also fail \u8212- {\i dahavei lei chatzer hamishtameret shelo le-da\u8217'at aviha} \u8212- because she is independently alert and in control of what is on her person, not passively guarded by the father. A {\i chatzer} whose occupant acts autonomously is not a valid {\i chatzer mishta\u8217'meret}. Therefore, the very fact that delivery to her hand is concededly valid proves she is not merely her father\u8217's {\i chatzer}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hand Resolution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} concludes: {\i ela peshita de-keyad aviha damya} \u8212- it is clear that she is like her father\u8217's hand, an extension of his person. But the question remains whether that extension is powerful enough that she can make a {\i shaliach}. Rav Nachman rules: {\i eina osa shaliach} \u8212- she cannot appoint a {\i shaliach}. She is her father\u8217's hand \u8212- she can receive \u8212- but she does not possess independent power sufficient to delegate further.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Baraita Challenge\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} challenges this ruling from a {\i baraita} from Gittin: {\i ketanah she-amrah hitkabel li giti eino get ad she-yagi\u8217'a get le-yadah} \u8212- if a minor says \u8220"receive my {\i get} for me,\u8221" it does not work until the {\i get} actually reaches her own hand, because she cannot appoint a {\i shaliach}. However, {\i ha-na\u8217'arah harei zeh get} \u8212- if she is a {\i na\u8217'arah}, it implies that she can appoint a {\i shaliach} and the {\i get} is valid when the {\i shaliach} receives it. This appears to directly contradict Rav Nachman\u8217's ruling.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Final Shaliach Rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} resolves the challenge: {\i hacha be-mai askinan, be-she-ein lah av} \u8212- this {\i baraita} is discussing a case where the {\i na\u8217'arah} has no father. When there is no father, the {\i na\u8217'arah} is her own agent and has full authority over herself; naturally she may appoint a {\i shaliach}. But this cannot conclude the matter, because the {\i baraita}\u8217's continuation describes a father who says \u8220"go receive a {\i get} for my daughter,\u8221" suggesting that a father is present.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} therefore invokes {\i chisorei mechasra ve-hachi katani} \u8212- the {\i baraita} is incomplete as stated and must be reconstructed as follows: {\i ketanah she-amra hitkabel li giti eino get ad she-yagi\u8217'a get le-yadah} \u8212- the {\i ketanah} who says \u8220"receive my {\i get}\u8221" cannot make a {\i shaliach}, and it is not effective until the {\i get} reaches her own hand. {\i Ha-na\u8217'arah harei zeh get} \u8212- the {\i na\u8217'arah} can do so; {\i bameh devarim amurim, ke-she-ein lah av} \u8212- this applies when there is no father. {\i Aval yesh lah av, ve-amar aviha tzei ve-kabel get le-vitti, im ratza ba\u8217'al la-chazor bo, lo yachzor} \u8212- but if there is a father and the father appoints a {\i shaliach} to receive the {\i get} for his daughter, that {\i shaliach} is effective and the husband cannot retract. The implied corollary is that if there is a father, only he can make a {\i shaliach}, not the {\i na\u8217'arah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} thus closes with a coherent ruling that aligns with Rabbi Yochanan\u8217's position throughout: fundamentally, all authority resides with the father. By {\i gittin}, the {\i rabanan} permit delivery of the {\i get} to the {\i na\u8217'arah} herself because she functions as {\i yad aviha}, an extension of her father\u8217's person \u8212- and it is the valid address for delivery. But she cannot appoint a {\i shaliach}, because that would require independent power she does not possess. And since the entire basis for her receiving the {\i get} is not her own authority but her status as her father\u8217's extended person operating {\i be\u8217'al korchah}, this also suggests \u8212- as the {\i gemara} notes in passing \u8212- that delivery of a {\i get} to a {\i ketanah} even when a father is present might similarly be valid, a question left open by the earlier discussion.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Kiddushin 45\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Ketanah Marriage Setup\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Na\u8217'arah Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The daf opens in the context of a preceding dispute concerning the {\i na\u8217'arah} \u8212- the girl who has entered adolescence but has not yet reached majority. The {\i Chachamim} hold that she may receive a {\i get}, while Rabbi Yehuda maintains she cannot even do that. Rabbi Yochanan\u8217's position is that receiving a {\i get} is precisely all she may do: since it is passive \u8212- {\i b\u8217'al karchah}, against her will in a sense \u8212- it lies within her capacity. Any transaction requiring her affirmative decision, such as entering into marriage, remains entirely within the father\u8217's authority. Even though the {\i na\u8217'arah} is legally an adult in certain respects, all real decision-making power over consequential transactions rests with the father.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ketanah & History\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 With that context established, the discussion shifts to the {\i ketanah} \u8212- the underage girl, clearly not an adult, for whom any independent legal act would seem entirely without effect. Yet the subject of today\u8217's daf, extending into tomorrow\u8217's as well, concerns a scenario in which this {\i ketanah} goes ahead and marries herself off. One might assume that if such an act carries no weight even for a {\i na\u8217'arah}, it would certainly be meaningless for a {\i ketanah}. Nevertheless, we will see that the {\i Gemara} entertains the possibility that her act carries some legal weight, and we will need to understand why.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Core Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is not merely a speculative exercise. The {\i Rishonim} engage in a vigorous debate about how to rule ({\i pasken}) because cases of this kind apparently did arise in practice. Likely the more common scenario was not a ten-year-old acting entirely on her own initiative, but rather a situation where the father was overseas \u8212- {\i medinat hayam} \u8212- for an extended period, and the mother proceeded to arrange the girl\u8217's marriage. As Tosafot notes at the beginning of the {\i perek}, despite the {\i Gemara}\u8217's stated preference that a man not marry off his daughter until she is old enough to express her own wishes, economic and historical realities created a practical necessity to arrange marriages for girls even before they reached {\i bat mitzvah}. This would have been especially acute when the father, though alive, was simply not present.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Shmuel\u8217's Teaching\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shmuel Statement\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} opens with the word {\i itmar} \u8212- indicating a dispute among {\i Amoraim}: {\i ketanah sheniskadshah shelo l\u8217'da\u8217'at aviha} \u8212- a {\i ketanah} who betrothed herself without her father\u8217's knowledge or consent. Shmuel rules: {\i tzrichah get v\u8217'tzrichah mi\u8217'un} \u8212- she requires both a {\i get} and a {\i mi\u8217'un}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is immediately striking. {\i Mi\u8217'un} \u8212- the formal repudiation by a minor \u8212- ordinarily applies only when the mother and brothers have arranged the marriage in the father\u8217's absence. What is {\i mi\u8217'un} doing here at all? And why would both be required? Normally, {\i mi\u8217'un} serves in place of a {\i get}, functioning as the appropriate dissolution of what is recognized as {\i kiddushin d\u8217'rabbanan}. If the marriage is treated as {\i kiddushin d\u8217'rabbanan}, a {\i mi\u8217'un} alone should suffice. If it is treated as {\i kiddushin d\u8217'oraita}, a {\i get} is required \u8212- but then why {\i mi\u8217'un} as well?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Karna Challenge\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This internal contradiction is precisely what {\i Karna}, a named {\i Chacham}, raises. He states: {\i devarim b\u8217'go} \u8212- there are problems inside here, things do not fit together. His challenge is direct: {\i im get lama mi\u8217'un, im mi\u8217'un lama get} \u8212- if a {\i get} is required, why {\i mi\u8217'un}? And if {\i mi\u8217'un} suffices, why a {\i get}? A {\i get} belongs to the world of {\i d\u8217'oraita} marriage; {\i mi\u8217'un} belongs to the world of {\i d\u8217'rabbanan} marriage. Combining them seems internally contradictory.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i batei midrash} respond that if Karna has a problem with Shmuel\u8217's ruling, he should take it to Rav Ukva and his {\i beit din} in the nearby town of Kafri. Apparently they then did so, and the {\i beit din} referred the matter further.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Blind Review\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What happens next is a striking procedural maneuver: {\i afchuha shadroha lekamei d\u8217'Rav} \u8212- they reversed the positions and sent the question to Rav. That is, they presented Shmuel\u8217's position as Karna\u8217's \u8212- that both {\i get} and {\i mi\u8217'un} are required \u8212- and Karna\u8217's objection as Shmuel\u8217's. This was a form of blind review, designed to prevent the result from being influenced by collegial loyalty. Had Rav known the ruling came from Shmuel, Karna might have dismissed Rav\u8217's endorsement as mere solidarity between colleagues. By reversing the attribution, they ensured Rav would respond on the merits alone \u8212- and moreover, if Rav sided with the position that both are required even when he thought Shmuel had opposed it, that would be a powerful and unbiased confirmation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Confirms\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The result is emphatic. Rav declares: {\i Hai Elohim, tzricha get v\u8217'tzrichah mi\u8217'un} \u8212- in God\u8217's name, she requires both a {\i get} and a {\i mi\u8217'un}. He adds that it would be unthinkable \u8212- {\i vechas lei lezarei d\u8217'Abba bar Abba} \u8212- that the son of Abba bar Abba, meaning Shmuel himself, could have held otherwise. So Rav not only affirmed the ruling but expressed something approaching indignation at the suggestion Shmuel would doubt it, all while in fact believing, based on the reversed presentation, that Shmuel was the one raising the objection. Rav held firm. Both Rav and Shmuel thus stand in strong agreement: this {\i ketanah} requires both a {\i get} and a {\i mi\u8217'un}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Get & Mi\u8217'un Logic\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Get Rationale\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now asks: {\i v\u8217'ta\u8217'ama mai?} \u8212- what is the actual reason? The answer is provided by Rav Acha the son of Rav Ika. He explains each requirement independently. She needs a {\i get} \u8212- {\i shema nitratza ha\u8217'av} \u8212- because perhaps the father, upon learning of what happened, will ultimately accept the marriage. If the father does come to accept it, then the marriage could be treated as {\i kiddushin d\u8217'oraita}, and a proper {\i get} would be required to dissolve it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mi\u8217'un Rationale\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 She needs a {\i mi\u8217'un} \u8212- {\i shema lo nitratza ha\u8217'av bekiddushin} \u8212- because perhaps the father did not accept the marriage. In that case, there was never any genuine {\i kiddushin}, and the {\i get} would have been given for nothing. But if all that people observed was a {\i get}, they would conclude that there had been a real {\i kiddushin d\u8217'oraita}, making her a {\i grusha d\u8217'oraita}, a definitively divorced woman. The {\i mi\u8217'un} is therefore given as a signal: its presence alongside the {\i get} communicates that even the {\i get} may have been only a stringency, a {\i chumra}, and not truly required \u8212- because if the marriage were certainly {\i d\u8217'oraita}, no {\i mi\u8217'un} would be added. The combination signals ambiguity and uncertainty.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sister Concern\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The concrete harm the {\i Gemara} seeks to prevent is the following: if people believe she is a {\i grusha d\u8217'oraita}, they will conclude that the man who gave the {\i get} was once truly married to her. And since a man cannot marry his ex-wife\u8217's sister while the ex-wife is still alive, people will think that if this man were to betroth her sister, the {\i kiddushin} would not take effect \u8212- {\i ein kiddushin tofsin b\u8217'achota}. But in truth, since the original {\i kiddushin} may never have been valid, the sister might well be fully available \u8212- {\i kiddushin} to her could be {\i chal}. The {\i mi\u8217'un} prevents this false inference by signaling that the {\i get} was of uncertain necessity.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 At the same time, this explanation raises deep difficulties that the {\i Rishonim} grapple with at length. Even granting that the father might retrospectively accept the marriage, the mechanics are difficult to reconstruct. A {\i ketanah} cannot serve as a {\i shaliach}, so how could money given directly to her function as {\i kesef kiddushin}? The Ritva raises this question and resolves it by analogy to the rule at the beginning of the tractate, where a woman may instruct a man to place the betrothal money on a rock or throw it into the sea, and it nonetheless counts as {\i kesef kiddushin} \u8212- because she derives {\i hana\u8217'ah} from his compliance with her wishes, or through related mechanisms.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 More fundamentally, most {\i Rishonim} reconstruct the scenario as follows: the girl comes home and informs the father of what occurred. The father, at least initially, says nothing. His silence is ambiguous \u8212- it might indicate acquiescence or merely a delayed reaction. If he later objects, the question becomes whether that objection reflects his original position or represents a change from an initial acceptance. The {\i Ra\u8217'avad} suggests the father may have been predisposed to accept even from the outset, but most {\i Rishonim} find this difficult to sustain across the typical cases. And even if the father\u8217's acquiescence can be established, the question of how that retroactive agreement transforms money already in her pocket into operative {\i kesef kiddushin} remains deeply problematic. The entire edifice appears highly rabbinic in character \u8212- a {\i chashash}, a concern \u8212- and this makes it all the more striking that Rav reacted with such vehemence at the thought that Shmuel could dismiss it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rav Nachman Limit\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shidduchin Gate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Nachman attempts to limit the applicability of this entire framework: {\i v\u8217'hu sheshidduchu} \u8212- all of the above applies only if {\i shidduchin} preceded the event. That is, there had to have been prior conversation and negotiation between the families \u8212- the prospective groom and his family had discussed the possibility of marrying the daughter with the father present and involved. Only then, when {\i kiddushin} money was given outside the father\u8217's direct presence, is there a basis for the concern that the father implicitly agreed. This limitation substantially clarifies the {\i nitratza ha\u8217'av} concern, even if it does not resolve the mechanical difficulties: it at least supplies a plausible basis for presuming that the father had the marriage in mind.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Ulla Rejects All\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ulla\u8217's Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Ulla takes the opposite extreme: {\i afilu mi\u8217'un einah tzrichah} \u8212- she does not even require a {\i mi\u8217'un}. Nothing here means anything. She is a {\i ketanah}; her act of betrothing herself is entirely without legal effect, whether {\i d\u8217'oraita} or {\i d\u8217'rabbanan}. There is no {\i get}, no {\i mi\u8217'un}, nothing.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shidduchin Ambiguity\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} then raises the question: does Ulla maintain this position even when {\i shidduchin} preceded the event? The {\i Gemara} answers by distinguishing between two formulations of Ulla\u8217's statement. According to one version ({\i man demasni ha lo masni ha}), whoever transmits the teaching that this law applies specifically in a case of {\i shidduchin} does not transmit Ulla\u8217's counter-ruling \u8212- implying that even Ulla would concede some concern when {\i shidduchin} occurred. But the second formulation ({\i ika d\u8217'amri}) presents Ulla\u8217's ruling as an independent absolute statement: even in a case of {\i shidduchin}, she requires nothing at all.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This distinction matters greatly for practical ruling. As we will see, most {\i Rishonim} rule against Rav and Shmuel, following the line of Ulla and Ravina. It is therefore essential to establish whether Ulla\u8217's rejection extends even to cases of {\i shidduchin}, so that the final ruling can be applied without reservation across all circumstances.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Yibum Scenario\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Emancipated Minor\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now pivots to a different context that illuminates the scope of {\i mi\u8217'un} for a {\i ketanah}. The {\i Mishnah} in Yevamot teaches ({\i v\u8217'chulan im metu o mianu o nitgarshu o shenimtzu ailonit tzaroteihen mutarot}): if any of the women who are {\i ervah} to the surviving brother \u8212- and who would therefore exempt all co-wives from {\i yibum} \u8212- were to die, perform {\i mi\u8217'un}, be divorced, or be found to be an {\i ailonit}, then the co-wives become permitted for {\i yibum} or {\i chalitza}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The case of {\i mi\u8217'un} here is the one that requires explanation. Consider: Shimon is married to Rachel, his daughter. Reuven, Rachel\u8217's uncle, dies. Because Rachel is an {\i ervah} to Shimon, no {\i yibum} occurs with any of Reuven\u8217's other wives. But if Rachel performed {\i mi\u8217'un} \u8212- indicating her marriage to Reuven had been only {\i kiddushin d\u8217'rabbanan} \u8212- then the {\i zika} bond to Shimon is dissolved, and the co-wives can enter {\i yibum}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The question is: how did Rachel, a {\i ketanah}, come to be married to Reuven with a marriage of {\i kiddushin d\u8217'rabbanan} while her father Shimon is still alive? If Shimon had married her off himself, the marriage would be {\i d\u8217'oraita} and {\i mi\u8217'un} would be inapplicable \u8212- she would need a full {\i get}. The {\i Gemara} therefore asks: who married her? If it was the father, {\i mi\u8217'un} is not the appropriate mechanism; she would need a real {\i get}. The answer must be: {\i ella lav d\u8217'kadshah inafshah} \u8212- she married herself off. And the {\i Gemara} notes this as proof that such self-marriage by a {\i ketanah} is treated as {\i kiddushin d\u8217'rabbanan}, requiring {\i mi\u8217'un}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This functions as a challenge to Ulla\u8217's position as well, since the {\i Mishnah} implies that {\i mi\u8217'un} is relevant in such a scenario. The {\i Gemara} itself both raises and resolves this question, answering that the relevant case is {\i k\u8217'gon she-na\u8217'asah lah ma\u8217'aseh yetomah b\u8217'chayei aviha} \u8212- she was treated legally as an orphan during her father\u8217's lifetime. This occurs when she had been previously married by her father, that marriage included {\i nisuin}, the husband died or divorced her, and through the completion of that full marital process she left her father\u8217's domain entirely. Having been fully emancipated in this manner while still a minor, she then married herself off a second time. This second self-marriage, occurring outside any paternal authority, is treated like the case of a girl married off by her mother and brothers \u8212- {\i kiddushin d\u8217'rabbanan} requiring {\i mi\u8217'un}. This scenario is structurally analogous to, but distinct from, the case where a father is present and the {\i ketanah} marries herself off without his involvement.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Hamnuna Query\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Hamnuna raises a further challenge from a {\i baraita} concerning the sale of a daughter as a Hebrew maidservant. The rule is stated: {\i ein mocherah l\u8217'krovim} \u8212- one may not sell her to relatives with whom {\i kiddushin} is impossible, because the entire purpose of the sale is to lead to {\i yi\u8217'ud} (the transformation of servitude into marriage), and a sale to such relatives forecloses that possibility. Rabbi Eliezer dissents, permitting such a sale without any prospect of {\i yi\u8217'ud}. But both opinions agree ({\i v\u8217'shavin she-mocher almana l\u8217'kohen gadol}) that a man may sell his daughter who is an {\i almana} (widow) to a {\i kohen gadol}, or one who is a {\i grusha} or {\i chalutzah} to an ordinary {\i kohen} \u8212- because even though such a marriage would be forbidden, the {\i kiddushin} would nonetheless take effect ({\i chal}), and therefore {\i yi\u8217'ud} is not categorically off the table.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The question the {\i Gemara} poses is: {\i hai almana heichi dami?} \u8212- how did this daughter come to be a {\i widow} while her father is still alive and still selling her? If the father had previously betrothed her ({\i eirusin}), and the {\i chatan} died, she would be an {\i almana} \u8212- but could the father then sell her again? The {\i Gemara} invokes the rule that once a father has married off his daughter ({\i ishus}), he may not then sell her as a maidservant. So if the {\i almana} status arose through the father\u8217's own {\i kiddushin}, the subsequent sale would be prohibited.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Self-Marriage Proof\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It follows that the {\i almana} in the {\i baraita} must have acquired that status through a marriage the father himself did not initiate. The only way this is possible \u8212- given that the father is alive and present \u8212- is if she married herself off: {\i ella lav d\u8217'kadshah hi nafshah}. The {\i Gemara} observes that she is being called an {\i almana} despite having married herself, which implies that even a self-initiated marriage by a {\i ketanah} can produce a recognized status of {\i almana} or {\i grusha}. This again suggests some degree of legal weight attaches to such a marriage.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yiud Mechanism\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But the {\i Gemara} does not accept this as the plain reading of the {\i baraita}. Rav Amram in the name of Rav Yitzchak explains: {\i hacha bekiddushei yi\u8217'ud va\u8217'aliba deRabbi Yose b\u8217'Rabbi Yehuda, d\u8217'amar ma\u8217'ot harishonot lav lekiddushin nitnu} \u8212- the case here involves {\i kiddushei yi\u8217'ud} according to Rabbi Yose son of Rabbi Yehuda, who holds that the original purchase money was not retroactively converted into {\i kesef kiddushin} when {\i yi\u8217'ud} occurred.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The mechanics are as follows. The father sold his daughter to husband number one as a maidservant \u8212- {\i shifcha}. Husband number one then performed {\i yi\u8217'ud}, transforming the remaining service into a form of {\i kiddushin}. According to Rabbi Yose son of Rabbi Yehuda, this {\i yi\u8217'ud} does not retroactively treat the original sale money as {\i kesef kiddushin} \u8212- rather, what becomes the operative act of {\i kiddushin} is the service she still owes him: he accepts that future labor as {\i kiddushin}, so to speak. This is significant because it means the father\u8217's original transaction was purely a {\i sale} \u8212- not a marriage \u8212- and the marriage that emerged from {\i yi\u8217'ud} was the doing of the buyer, not the father.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 After husband number one dies or divorces her, she returns to the father\u8217's domain. Since her previous entry into marriage came through {\i shifchus} (servitude), not through direct {\i kiddushin} by the father, the subsequent transaction is classified as {\i shifcha achar shifcha} \u8212- a sale following a prior sale \u8212- not {\i shifcha achar ishus} \u8212- a sale following a marriage. The prohibition against re-selling applies only to the latter. The father may therefore sell her again to a second man \u8212- for instance, to a {\i kohen gadol} \u8212- while she carries the status of {\i almana} or {\i grusha} from her first marriage. The case in the {\i baraita} thus does not require positing that she married herself; it involves the distinct mechanism of {\i kiddushei yi\u8217'ud} under the specific opinion of Rabbi Yose son of Rabbi Yehuda.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What this discussion establishes for the broader {\i sugya} is the question of how far the concept of {\i mi\u8217'un} \u8212- and {\i kiddushin d\u8217'rabbanan} for a {\i ketanah} \u8212- can extend beyond its classic context of a mother and brothers marrying off a fatherless minor. We have now seen two candidate scenarios: the emancipated minor who marries herself (accepted), and the {\i ketanah} who marries herself while her father is present (contested). The {\i Gemara} will now proceed to apply these principles in the context of {\i yibum}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Yibum Details\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Huna Rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now presents the case directly. The {\i ketanah} married herself off to Reuven \u8212- without her father\u8217's involvement. Reuven then dies, and the case falls before Reuven\u8217's brother Shimon for {\i yibum}. According to Rav and Shmuel, this self-marriage was at least a matter of {\i chashash}, and both a {\i get} and a {\i mi\u8217'un} would have been required to exit it. Now what is Shimon required to do?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Huna in the name of Rav rules: {\i mema\u8217'enes l\u8217'ma\u8217'amaro v\u8217'einah mema\u8217'enes l\u8217'zikatoh} \u8212- she performs {\i mi\u8217'un} in response to Shimon\u8217's {\i ma\u8217'amar}, but not merely in response to the {\i zika} bond alone. {\i Ma\u8217'amar} is the act by which a {\i yavam} \u8212- the surviving brother \u8212- performs a form of {\i kiddushin} with the {\i yevamah} while she awaits {\i yibum} or {\i chalitza}. If Shimon does {\i ma\u8217'amar}, that introduces an additional dimension; if he does not, the situation is different.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Triple Remedy\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If Shimon performed {\i ma\u8217'amar}, the {\i Gemara} specifies: {\i tzricha get v\u8217'tzricha chalitza v\u8217'tzricha mi\u8217'un} \u8212- she requires all three from Shimon. Each is explained separately.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 She needs a {\i get} \u8212- {\i shema nitratza ha\u8217'av bekiddushei sheini} \u8212- because perhaps the father did not accept the first marriage to Reuven, but he did accept the {\i ma\u8217'amar} of Shimon. If the father accepted Shimon\u8217's {\i ma\u8217'amar} as valid {\i kiddushin d\u8217'oraita}, then Shimon has truly married her and must give her a {\i get} to release her.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 She needs {\i chalitza} \u8212- {\i shema nitratza bekiddushei rishon} \u8212- because perhaps the father accepted the original marriage to Reuven. In that case, there is a genuine {\i zika} between her and Shimon as the surviving brother, and Shimon must perform {\i chalitza} to release her from that bond.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 She needs {\i mi\u8217'un} \u8212- {\i shema lo nitratza lo bekiddushei rishon velo bekiddushei sheini} \u8212- because perhaps the father accepted neither marriage, in which case she was never truly married to anyone. The {\i get} and the {\i chalitza} would then both be legally meaningless \u8212- performed only as {\i chumrot}. The {\i mi\u8217'un} signals this uncertainty and prevents people from drawing the erroneous inference that the {\i chalitza} confirms a real {\i d\u8217'oraita} marriage and that if Shimon were to betroth her sister, the {\i kiddushin} would not take effect \u8212- {\i ein kiddushin tofsin b\u8217'achota}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chalitza Alone\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If Shimon did {\i not} perform {\i ma\u8217'amar}, the ruling is simpler: {\i einah tzricha ela chalitza bilvad} \u8212- she needs only {\i chalitza}. The logic is that there are only two relevant scenarios: either the father accepted the marriage to Reuven, in which case there is a real {\i zika} and Shimon needs {\i chalitza}; or the father did not accept it, in which case Shimon has no connection to her at all. Since Shimon did no {\i ma\u8217'amar}, there is no third scenario involving Shimon\u8217's own {\i kiddushin} \u8212- and therefore no need for a {\i get}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might ask: should she not also need a {\i mi\u8217'un} even in this case, to signal that the {\i chalitza} was of uncertain necessity and prevent people from concluding that Shimon marrying her sister would not be {\i chal}? The {\i Gemara} answers: {\i hakol yodim achot chaluza d\u8217'rabbanan} \u8212- everyone knows that the sister of a {\i chalutzah} is forbidden only {\i d\u8217'rabbanan}, not {\i d\u8217'oraita}. Reish Lakish establishes this in Yevamot: the sister of a {\i grusha} (divorced woman) is forbidden {\i d\u8217'oraita}, but the sister of a {\i chalutzah} is forbidden only {\i midivrei sofrim}. Accordingly, even if observers see a {\i chalitza} here and conclude that there was a genuine {\i zika}, they will also know that any subsequent {\i kiddushin} to her sister would take effect {\i d\u8217'oraita} \u8212- there is no risk of a false inference that would permit a prohibited union or negate a valid one. The {\i mi\u8217'un} is therefore unnecessary when there was no {\i ma\u8217'amar}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Ravina Dispute\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Father & Son\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now introduces a case that frames Ravina\u8217's broader rejection of the {\i nitratza ha\u8217'av} concern. Two men were drinking wine together under an awning in Bavel. One took a cup of wine, gave it to the other, and said: {\i mekadsha li bartech livri} \u8212- let your daughter be betrothed to my son. Here the man who accepted the wine had the authority to marry off his daughter; there is no question about that side of the transaction. The issue is that the man giving the wine was acting on behalf of his son without having been appointed as the son\u8217's {\i shaliach}. Could we say \u8212- by parallel to the {\i nitratza ha\u8217'av} reasoning \u8212- that perhaps when the son finds out he will be pleased with the marriage, and that retroactive acceptance will make the father\u8217's action valid?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Ravina says no: {\i shema nitratzei haben lo amrinan} \u8212- we do not invoke the concern that the son may come to accept it. The {\i Gemara} presents the reason on behalf of the rabbis who explained Ravina\u8217's position: {\i de\u8217'av bekiddushei bito bechol dehu nicha lei} \u8212- a father has a low threshold for accepting whatever arrangement his daughter has entered into, because, as the {\i Gemara} characterizes it, {\i detav lemeitav tan du} \u8212- it is preferable for a woman to be partnered than to be alone, and fathers tend to embrace opportunities to see their daughters settled. But {\i aval gavra dayek venasiv} \u8212- a man is exacting and discerning about who he marries.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gender Presumption\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 There is therefore an asymmetry between the two cases. For a daughter: the father\u8217's presumed willingness to accept after the fact provides at least some basis \u8212- however thin \u8212- for the {\i nitratza ha\u8217'av} concern. For a son: no comparable presumption exists. The son is expected to care deeply about his choice of spouse and cannot be presumed to accept whatever was arranged without his input.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Furthermore \u8212- and this is a second, independent reason \u8212- even if the son were inclined to accept what the father did, that acceptance alone would be insufficient. To marry on someone\u8217's behalf, one must be a {\i shaliach}; retroactive willingness does not substitute for the proper appointment of agency. This applies regardless of one\u8217's views on the {\i nitratza ha\u8217'av} concern: {\i ubein eino birshut aviv lekadesh lo isha shelo bishlucho} \u8212- the father does not have the standing to marry for his son without having been formally designated as a {\i shaliach}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ravina Rejects\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} makes explicit what this case implies: {\i befirush amar mar d\u8217'lo savar lah l\u8217'ha d\u8217'Rav u\u8217'Shmuel} \u8212- Ravina explicitly stated that he does not accept the ruling of Rav and Shmuel. And since the discussion in the {\i Gemara} has been framing the son\u8217's case as parallel \u8212- in some ways \u8212- to a {\i shidduchin} scenario (the son had been expressing interest in the girl, which created a context of presumed desirability, analogous to prior {\i shidduchin} with the daughter), Ravina\u8217's rejection extends even to cases where {\i shidduchin} preceded the event. We do not invoke {\i nitratza ha\u8217'av} even then.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Bizayon Exception\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Vegetables Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Even from the perspective of Rav and Shmuel \u8212- who do invoke the {\i nitratza ha\u8217'av} concern \u8212- not every case of self-{\i kiddushin} by a {\i ketanah} triggers it. Ravina articulates an important limitation. A man betrothed a woman with a bundle of vegetables in the marketplace. Ravina rules: {\i afilu man d\u8217'amar chaishinan shema nitratzei ha\u8217'av, hani mili derech kavod} \u8212- even according to those who hold that we are concerned the father may have been {\i meratze} after the fact, that concern applies only when the original {\i kiddushin} was performed in a respectful manner. {\i Aval hacha derech bizayon} \u8212- when the betrothal was done in a degrading manner, we have no concern at all that the father would ever come to accept it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Bizayon Definition\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Acha of Difti asks Ravina: {\i bizayon d\u8217'mai} \u8212- what constituted the degradation here? Was it the object \u8212- a mere bundle of vegetables \u8212- or was it the setting \u8212- the public marketplace? The distinction matters practically: {\i nafka mina d\u8217'kadesh b\u8217'chaspa beshuka} \u8212- if he betrothed her with money but in the marketplace, or with a bundle of vegetables but in a private home, which of these constitutes {\i derech bizayon}? Ravina answers: {\i idei v\u8217'idei derech bizayon hu} \u8212- both constitute degradation. Either the object or the setting, taken alone, is sufficient to render the betrothal a {\i derech bizayon}, removing it from the scope of the {\i nitratza ha\u8217'av} concern.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Public Commitment\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Wife Persuasion\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} presents a case reminiscent of one discussed in tractate Nedarim. A father wanted to marry his daughter to his own relative. His wife disagreed and insisted that the daughter be married to her brother \u8212- the father\u8217's brother-in-law. The wife prevailed: {\i kafstei} \u8212- she pressed him until he conceded and agreed. They then celebrated with a party, announcing the engagement to the wife\u8217's brother. And then, while everyone was eating and drinking, the husband\u8217's own relative came up to the second floor and gave the daughter {\i kesef kiddushin} \u8212- betrothing her himself, exactly as the father had originally wanted. The question is whether we invoke {\i nitratza ha\u8217'av}: the father clearly had preferred this match all along and only reluctantly agreed to the other arrangement.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Abaye Finality\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye rules that we do not invoke {\i nitratza ha\u8217'av} here. He invokes the verse {\i she\u8217'erit Yisrael lo ya\u8217'asu avlah velo yedabru kazav} \u8212- the remnant of Israel will not act perversely or speak falsehood. Once the father gave his word and committed to the other match \u8212- and especially once he made that commitment at a public gathering, announcing the engagement \u8212- we do not fear that he will reverse himself. The public commitment constitutes a moral and social bond that forecloses the {\i nitratza} concern.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava Expense\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava adds a further condition: {\i chazakah ein adam torach beseuda umafsidah} \u8212- there is a presumption that once a man has gone to the trouble and expense of making a party, he will not allow that effort to be wasted. It is not merely the announcement that removes the concern; it is the concrete investment in a celebration. The practical difference between Abaye and Rava is a case where the announcement was made publicly but no party with actual expense took place \u8212- {\i delo tarach}. Abaye would say that even the announcement alone suffices to preclude the reversal; Rava requires that actual expense have been incurred.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Final Pesak\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafot Rules\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i sugya} concludes with the question of how to rule. Do we follow Rav and Shmuel \u8212- who require both {\i get} and {\i mi\u8217'un}, limited perhaps by Rav Nachman to cases of {\i shidduchin} and further qualified by the exceptions for {\i bizayon} and public commitment \u8212- or do we follow the line of Ulla and Ravina, who reject the {\i nitratza ha\u8217'av} concern entirely? Tosafot rules explicitly \u8212- {\i befirush amar mar lo sevira lei deShmuel} \u8212- and cites Rav Alfas as ruling in the {\i She\u8217'iltot} of Rav Achai that the {\i halachah} follows Ravina. The rationale is that Ravina was a later authority \u8212- {\i batra} \u8212- representing the {\i sof hora\u8217'ah} at the close of the Talmudic period. Rav Ashi, too, concurred. Accordingly: {\i ein tzarich lo get velo mi\u8217'un} \u8212- she requires neither a {\i get} nor a {\i mi\u8217'un} \u8212- {\i va\u8217'afilu shidduchu} \u8212- even if {\i shidduchin} had preceded the event. The {\i nitratza ha\u8217'av} concern is entirely set aside.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Father Absent\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A separate question remains: what if the father is not present at all \u8212- not because he is dead, but because he has traveled abroad for an extended period? In that case, the {\i She\u8217'iltot} holds that the {\i ketanah} can enter into a {\i kiddushin d\u8217'rabbanan} \u8212- not {\i d\u8217'oraita}, which is never available without the father, but a rabbinic marriage with the status of {\i mi\u8217'un} as the mode of exit. The reasoning parallels the case of a true orphan: {\i kedin yetomah de\u8217'ein lah av detaknu rabbanan kiddushin mishum hefker} \u8212- the rabbis instituted {\i kiddushin} in such cases to prevent the girl from being treated without dignity or taken advantage of. When the father is overseas and effectively unavailable, she is in a comparable position: {\i v\u8217'zu she-halchah l\u8217'medinat hayam, harei hu k\u8217'yetomah mishum hefker} \u8212- this girl, with her father overseas, is like an orphan in terms of the concern that she will be treated as {\i hefker}. A {\i kiddushin d\u8217'rabbanan} is therefore permitted under such circumstances.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Remote Marriage\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Menachem Me\u8217'yuni dissents. He does not accept that a {\i kiddushin d\u8217'rabbanan} can be effected in the father\u8217's absence when the father is alive. He cites a specific case: a man traveled to a distant land and left behind a young daughter; she was married off; and Rav Menachem ruled that the marriage could not be maintained. His reason goes beyond a mere disagreement about whether {\i kiddushin d\u8217'rabbanan} applies \u8212- he raises a new concern: {\i chaishinan shema y\u8217'kadshana aviha l\u8217'ish acher bimkom shehu} \u8212- we must fear that the father, wherever he is, has in the meantime married her off to some other man. How then can we permit this marriage to stand?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbeinu Tam responds sharply: if one takes that concern seriously, then even after the father dies the girl could never marry, because perhaps the father had betrothed her to someone while abroad, unknown to anyone here. The {\i Gemara} does not treat such unknowable possibilities as legally operative concerns. We are not afraid of what an absent father may have done without our knowledge.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The upshot is a layered picture: when the father is present and the {\i ketanah} marries herself off, the final ruling \u8212- following Ravina, Rav Ashi, Rav Alfas, and Tosafot \u8212- is that nothing is required, not a {\i get} and not a {\i mi\u8217'un}, even with prior {\i shidduchin}. When the father is absent and alive, there is a serious dispute about whether a {\i kiddushin d\u8217'rabbanan} can exist at all, with an additional concern, raised by Rav Menachem, about what the father may have arranged independently. The {\i sugya} thus ties together the speculative legal question with which it began and a practical reality that the {\i Rishonim} clearly recognized as pressing in their own communities.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Kiddushin 45b\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Ketana, Father Away\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Setup\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} picks up in the middle of 45b with a case involving a {\i ketana} \u8212- a minor girl \u8212- whose {\i kiddushin} was performed with her father\u8217's knowledge and approval. Whether the father himself arranged the match or she acted on her own initiative in a way that made his assent clear, the binding character of the {\i kiddushin} is not in question; the father was demonstrably on board. Subsequently, however, {\i ve-halach aviha le-medinat ha-yam} \u8212- the father traveled to a distant land \u8212- and while he was away, {\i ve-amda ve-niset}, she proceeded to {\i nisuin}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Nisuin & Da\u8217'at\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The resulting question is whether {\i nisuin} performed {\i she-lo le-da\u8217'at aviha} \u8212- without the father\u8217's knowledge or consent \u8212- is legally effective. This raises two analytically distinct considerations. First, one might argue that {\i nisuin} is categorically different from {\i kiddushin} as a form of {\i kinyan}: {\i kiddushin} creates the marriage as a matter of law, whereas {\i nisuin} translates it into lived reality, and might therefore require less of the formal {\i da\u8217'at} ordinarily demanded of a {\i kinyan}. On the other hand, {\i nisuin} is precisely the moment when the minor daughter passes out of her father\u8217's legal control and into her husband\u8217's domain. Even if an adult woman\u8217's {\i nisuin} requires less formal {\i da\u8217'at} than an initial {\i kinyan}, one would expect that a {\i ketana} cannot make that transition \u8212- from the father\u8217's authority to the husband\u8217's \u8212- without the father\u8217's explicit consent. The {\i nafka mina} is multiple and concrete: whether she may eat {\i terumah} if her husband is a {\i kohen}, and whether if she dies her husband inherits her estate, along with the full range of reciprocal financial obligations that attach to full {\i nisuin}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav\u8217's Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Amar Rav: ochelet be-terumah ad she-yavo aviha ve-yimcheh.} Rav holds that she is presumed to be a {\i nesuah} and may eat {\i terumah} until her father returns and objects. The presumption runs in favor of the {\i nisuin} being valid; the father, knowing that {\i kiddushin} had taken place and having approved it, can be assumed to acquiesce in the natural continuation to {\i nisuin}. Should he return and protest \u8212- presumably while she is still a {\i ketana} or {\i na\u8217'arah} and within the window of paternal authority \u8212- he can retroactively undo the status of {\i nesuah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Asi\u8217's Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rav Asi amar: eino ochelet.} Rav Asi disagrees. His concern is {\i shema yavo aviv ve-yimcheh} \u8212- the father might protest upon returning \u8212- and therefore one cannot presume the {\i nisuin} valid. The consequence is stark: {\i ve-nimtza zara ochelet be-terumah le-mafreia} \u8212- were she eating {\i terumah} and the father then protested, she would retroactively have been a non-{\i kohen}\u8217's wife consuming {\i terumah} improperly.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav\u8217's Stringency\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Although Rav\u8217's theoretical ruling permits her to eat {\i terumah}, the {\i gemara} records that {\i havei uvda ve-chash leh Rav le-hadei Rav Asi} \u8212- in a practical case that arose, Rav was stringent in accordance with Rav Asi\u8217's position and did not permit her to eat. Some {\i rishonim} understand this to mean that Rav did not abandon his ruling but simply exercised {\i chumra} in practice. Rav Shmuel bar Rav Yitzchak clarifies that Rav himself is the one who held, as a matter of law, that she could eat {\i terumah}; it is merely that when an actual case presented itself, he deferred to the stricter view of Rav Asi.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Inheritance & Safek\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Amar Rav Shmuel bar Rav Yitzchak u-modeh Rav: she-im metah einah yorshah.} Even Rav concedes that if she were to die, her husband would not inherit her estate. Despite holding that she has sufficient status as a {\i nesuah} to eat {\i terumah}, Rav acknowledges that the {\i nisuin} is not certain enough to transfer property: {\i uki mamona be-chezkas mara} \u8212- the money remains in the presumptive possession of its original owner, and one does not transfer an estate on the basis of a doubtful {\i nisuin}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This creates an apparent tension: if Rav is prepared to concede a {\i safek} as to whether there is {\i nisuin} \u8212- sufficient doubt that he will not allow property to transfer \u8212- how can he simultaneously permit her to eat {\i terumah}? Here Rashi and Tosafot both point to an important underlying principle. According to the {\i Bavli}, the right of a {\i kohen}\u8217's wife to eat {\i terumah} derives {\i mid\u8217'oraisa} already from {\i kiddushin} alone, without waiting for {\i nisuin}; the requirement that she first undergo {\i nisuin} before eating {\i terumah} is only {\i mid\u8217'rabanan}. Therefore, Rav and Rav Asi may agree that her status as a {\i nesuah} is a {\i safek} \u8212- and indeed both accept that framework \u8212- but when it comes to the {\i d\u8217'rabanan} restriction, Rav is willing to be lenient. Tosafot further clarifies that even according to Rav Asi, the practical reasons for the {\i gezera} do not apply in the same way here, since she is already living with the man; Rav Asi\u8217's position is therefore a form of {\i lo plug} rather than a strict application of the underlying rationale.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Elopement Cases\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kidushin Good, Nisuin Not\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now turns to a different configuration: {\i niskadsha le-da\u8217'at ve-niset she-lo le-da\u8217'at}, with {\i avia kan} \u8212- the father present. The {\i kiddushin} was performed with the father\u8217's knowledge, but the {\i nisuin} was not. The distinction from the prior case is that there the father was simply absent and could not be consulted; here the father is available and his response \u8212- or rather his lack of explicit response \u8212- is the crux of the matter. She has, it seems, acted behind his back, and when he found out, said nothing. The question is how to interpret that silence.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Huna: Silence = No\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rav Huna amar: eino ochelet.} She may not eat {\i terumah}. Even though Rav permitted eating when the father was away in a distant land, {\i hasam hu de-leika le-av} \u8212- there the father was simply unreachable, and we could presume he would be accepting when he eventually learned of it. But {\i aval hacha de-ika le-av} \u8212- here the father is present and presumably has now learned what occurred. His silence cannot be read as acquiescence. {\i Hai de-ishtik mirtach ratach} \u8212- one who falls silent in this situation may well be simply too angry to speak.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yirmiah: Silence = Yes\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rav Yirmiah bar Abba amar: ochelet.} She may eat. Even Rav Asi, who would not permit eating when the father was far away on grounds that {\i shema yavo aviv ve-yimcheh}, said so only because {\i hatam hu de-shema yavo aviv ve-yimcheh} \u8212- when the father is absent, the concern is that upon returning he will raise an objection. But {\i aval hacha mi-de-shatik, isnochi asnach lei} \u8212- here, where the father is present and has heard what happened and said nothing, that silence is itself an indication of acceptance. The general principle of {\i shtika ke-hoda\u8217'ah} \u8212- silence functions as acknowledgment \u8212- supports the inference that a person who genuinely objects would say so; one who remains silent has made his peace, however reluctantly.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ulla\u8217's Analysis\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 As the {\i gemara} itself notes, there is a structural asymmetry in these two positions. The stance of Rav Yirmiah bar Abba is coherent: ordinarily we fear the father will protest when he hears the news, but actual silence in the face of known facts changes that presumption. By contrast, Rav Huna\u8217's position seems to move in the opposite direction \u8212- starting from a presumption that the father would approve when absent, yet interpreting his silence once present as anger and opposition. Ulla observes that the position of Rav Yirmiah, who was Rav Huna\u8217's student, appears considerably more consistent.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Total Elopement (III)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ulla Rejects\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Moving to the most extreme scenario \u8212- {\i niskadsha she-lo le-da\u8217'at avia ve-niset she-lo le-da\u8217'at avia ve-avia kan} \u8212- both the {\i kiddushin} and the {\i nisuin} were performed entirely without the father\u8217's knowledge, and he is present. Rav Huna holds {\i ochelet}, she may eat; Rav Yirmiah bar Abba holds {\i eina ochelet}, she may not. Ulla characterizes Rav Huna\u8217's position here as {\i ke-chometz la-shinayim u-ke-ashan la-einayim} \u8212- intolerable, like vinegar on the teeth or smoke in the eyes.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Logical Inference\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} articulates why. In the immediately preceding case \u8212- where {\i kiddushin} was done with the father\u8217's knowledge and only the {\i nisuin} was without \u8212- Rav Huna himself said she could not eat, reading the father\u8217's silence as opposition. How then, in the more extreme case where even the {\i kiddushin} was done without his knowledge, can one say she may eat? {\i Hashta u-mah hatam\u8230 ? amrat lo ochlah, hacha lo kol she-ken} \u8212- if even when the {\i kiddushin} was clearly valid and the father was silent about the {\i nisuin} alone, Rav Huna said no, surely when neither act was done with his knowledge, the answer must be no all the more. The {\i gemara} therefore concludes: {\i nirin divrei talmid} \u8212- the position of the student (Rav Yirmiah bar Abba) appears more logically compelling.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Total Elopement (III)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava Reinterprets\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava, however, defends Rav Huna\u8217's position by offering a more nuanced reading of the underlying logic. {\i Mai ta\u8217'ama de-Rav Huna: ho\u8217'il ve-na\u8217'asah be-ma\u8217'aseh yatom be-chayei av} \u8212- the reason is that this girl has conducted herself as though she were an orphan in her father\u8217's lifetime. Two major life events \u8212- {\i kiddushin} and {\i nisuin} \u8212- have occurred without any paternal involvement or reaction. The absence of any assertion of authority across both events is itself a form of relinquishment. The father, by allowing the pattern to continue unchallenged, has in effect yielded his position and left her to make her own decisions. His silence is not opposition in this case; it is acquiescence born of having ceded control entirely.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Setup\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The contrast with the prior case now becomes clear: when the {\i kiddushin} was performed under his auspices and with his approval, there was never a moment when he had stepped back from his role. His silence about the {\i nisuin} alone would then read against a backdrop of active, continuing paternal authority \u8212- and that silence is harder to interpret as acceptance of something done behind his back. But when both acts proceed without him and he says nothing, the cumulative picture is that he has let her be.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Elopement Cases (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava Defends Huna\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is the key to Rava\u8217's defense of Rav Huna. When the {\i kiddushin} was done {\i le-da\u8217'at av} and only the {\i nisuin} was not, the father has never forfeited his standing. His silence regarding {\i nisuin} is therefore ambiguous \u8212- and Rav Huna reads it as tacit objection, in keeping with the view that {\i mirtach ratach}. But when neither act was within his purview, the father has effectively allowed his daughter to function as though she had no father, and the consistent pattern of non-response amounts to a relinquishment of control. Rava thus reconciles Rav Huna\u8217's two rulings by identifying the relevant variable not as whether there was one unapproved act or two, but rather whether there was any prior basis of paternal authority that was then bypassed.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Conclusion on Ketana\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Both Can Object\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Amar Rav: bein hi u-vein aviha yecholim limchot.} Rav states that in the case of a {\i ketana} who was betrothed without her father\u8217's knowledge, both the father and the daughter herself are capable of objecting and thereby invalidating the {\i kiddushin}. This statement should be read in context: Rav had previously held that such {\i kiddushin} creates a concern ({\i chashash}) of valid betrothal \u8212- {\i shema yitratze ha-av} \u8212- meaning that if the father acquiesces, the {\i kiddushin} might take effect. What Rav now clarifies is the other side of that picture: if the father explicitly objects, that {\i chashash} evaporates entirely. And not only the father \u8212- the daughter, too, can object.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Total Elopement (III)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Huna: Yes\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 In the case of full elopement \u8212- {\i kiddushin} and {\i nisuin} both {\i she-lo le-da\u8217'at ha-av}, with the father present \u8212- Rav Huna holds that she may eat {\i terumah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yirmiah: No\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Yirmiah bar Abba holds that she may not eat {\i terumah} in this case.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Conclusion on Ketana (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rashi\u8217's Limitation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rashi understands the daughter\u8217's right to object as temporally limited: she can only object prior to the father finding out about the {\i kiddushin}. Once the {\i kiddushin} is known to the father, the question of its validity passes into his domain, and her prior acceptance of the {\i kiddusin} money can no longer be undone by her protest. The reasoning is that before the father learns of it and before his reaction can ratify the {\i kiddushin}, her ongoing willingness remains a live factor; once she retracts before that window closes, the acceptance is undermined and the {\i kiddushin} cannot take effect.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafot Clarification\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot clarifies the scope of the entire preceding discussion. When Rav said earlier that we are {\i choshesh shema yitratze ha-av}, that concern applies specifically {\i be-stama she-shama ve-shatak} \u8212- when the father heard and was initially silent. The {\i sugyot} about interpreting the father\u8217's silence are thus unified: the entire discussion addresses the ambiguous case of an unexplained non-reaction. {\i Aval heicha de-michah gila be-da\u8217'ato} \u8212- if the father explicitly objected, his intent is manifest and there is no {\i kiddushin}; if he explicitly accepted, the {\i kiddushin} is valid. The interpretive work only begins when the father\u8217's response is silence.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Explicit Intent\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 When the father makes his position clear \u8212- whether by explicit approval or explicit objection \u8212- his stated intent governs the validity of the {\i kiddushin}. The debates among Rav, Rav Asi, Rav Huna, and Rav Yirmiah bar Abba all operate within the space of ambiguity created by silence alone.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Torah Case Analysis\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Torah Source\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Chiya bar Rav challenges Rav Asi by grounding the case in a {\i pasuk} from the Torah. The verse in question \u8212- {\i im ma\u8217'en yema\u8217'en aviha} \u8212- concerns the case of the seducer, where the father refuses to give his daughter in marriage to the man who seduced her, and the man must therefore pay fifty {\i kesef}. Chiya bar Rav reads this case as the precise scenario the {\i gemara} has been discussing: a {\i ketana} or {\i na\u8217'arah} who underwent what amounted to {\i kiddushin} without her father\u8217's prior knowledge, with the father now reacting.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Deriving Daughter\u8217's Right\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 From the {\i pasuk} alone, only the father\u8217's right to refuse is explicit \u8212- {\i ein li ela aviha, hi atzma minayin?} How do we know the daughter herself may refuse? The {\i midrash} derives this from the doubled language of {\i im ma\u8217'en yema\u8217'en}, reading the repetition as an inclusion \u8212- {\i mi-kol makom}, from whatever source, a refusal is valid. This would suggest that her objection also carries legal force in the case of {\i kiddushin she-lo le-da\u8217'at aviha}, and would support Rav\u8217's position that {\i bein hi u-vein aviha yecholim limchot}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav\u8217's Interpretation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav, however, deflects this argument: {\i lo tizul batar ipcha \u8212- yacholni le-shannoye lach k\u8217'gon she-pita le-shem ishus}. The {\i pasuk}, Rav insists, need not be understood as a case of {\i kiddushin} at all. The more straightforward reading is that the seduction occurred {\i she-lo le-shem ishus} \u8212- not in the context of marriage \u8212- and the sexual act was simply that, with no accompanying {\i kinyan kiddushin}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 No Kiddushin Here\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If that is the case, why does the Torah need to tell us that the father can refuse? Of course the father can refuse to allow his daughter to marry this man \u8212- the father\u8217's authority over whom his daughter may marry is a fundamental premise that requires no {\i pasuk}. The {\i gemara} therefore explains that the {\i pasuk}\u8217's point is not to establish the father\u8217's authority to veto the marriage but rather to specify the financial consequence: even when the father refuses and the marriage does not take place, the seducer must still pay the fifty {\i kesef \u8212- amar Rav Nachman bar Yitzchak: lomar she-meshalem knas ke-meputah}. The {\i pasuk} teaches the law of the {\i knas}, not a ruling about {\i kiddushin}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Seduction L\u8217'Shem Ishus\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Asi then responds: {\i ee hachi} \u8212- if that is indeed the reading \u8212- {\i hainu de-tanina etzlo: mahor yimharena lo le-ishah she-tzricha himenu kiddushin}. The language {\i mahor yimharena lo le-ishah} implies that a formal act of {\i kiddushin} now needs to take place, which confirms that no {\i kiddushin} had yet occurred \u8212- consistent with Rav\u8217's reading that the seduction was not {\i le-shem ishus}. Abaye, however, addresses the reading in which the seduction {\i was} for the sake of marriage \u8212- and on that reading, {\i mahor yimharena lo le-ishah} would mean that a new {\i kiddushin} must now be performed with the father\u8217's explicit approval: {\i tzricha kiddushin le-da\u8217'at aviha}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafot\u8217's Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This resolution raises a question that Tosafot presses. If the daughter can refuse, what does her refusal accomplish in practical terms? Consider the scenario as Rav reads it: the seduction was not {\i le-shem ishus}, the father has the right to refuse the marriage, and if the father refuses, the seducer pays fifty {\i kesef}. Now suppose the daughter objects. Does her objection prevent the marriage from going forward? Normally a {\i ketana} or {\i na\u8217'arah} has no independent right to veto a match her father has sanctioned. Is it really possible that simply because she was seduced she now acquires that right?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafot\u8217's Solutions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot offers two answers. The first, which Tosafot itself finds problematic, is that her refusal does not actually prevent the marriage but does obligate the payment of the {\i knas} \u8212- the fifty {\i kesef} \u8212- as though the father had refused, even if the wedding ultimately proceeds. Tosafot acknowledges the strangeness of this: presumably the father and husband could simply coordinate so that the daughter refuses, the {\i knas} is paid to the father, and the marriage goes forward regardless. The second and preferable answer is more straightforward: {\i nafka mina heicha de-ba aleha ve-achar kach nityatmah} \u8212- the daughter\u8217's refusal is operative in a case where the father has since died. Once there is no father to exercise paternal authority, {\i mahani miun didah le-akeiv she-lo tehei ishto} \u8212- her own objection can now function to block the marriage entirely. The broader point, which the {\i gemara} never fully resolves, is that once {\i chazal} recognized the daughter\u8217's right of refusal, the precise circumstances in which it is operative \u8212- when the father is present, when he has approved, and what weight her voice carries \u8212- remain somewhat underdeveloped.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Multiple Dates Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Separate Statements\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now turns from the dynamics of {\i kiddushin} of a {\i ketana} to a {\i mishna} dealing with the mechanics of {\i kesef kiddushin} in the case of an adult woman. The {\i mishna} records: {\i ha-omer le-ishah hiskadshi li be-tamarah zo hiskadshi li be-zo} \u8212- a man who says \u8220"marry me with this date, marry me with this date,\u8221" separately, offering each date with its own declaration. {\i Im yesh be-achas mehen shaveh perutah mekudeshet} \u8212- if any single one of them is worth a {\i perutah}, she is betrothed; {\i ve-im lav einah mekudeshet} \u8212- and if not, she is not, even if their combined value reaches a {\i perutah}. Since each statement of {\i hiskadshi} constitutes a separate act, the dates do not combine.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Combined Statement\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If instead he said {\i b\u8217'zo u-v\u8217'zo u-v\u8217'zo} \u8212- \u8220"with this and this and this\u8221" \u8212- combining everything into one declaration, then {\i im yesh shaveh perutah be-chulan mekudeshet}, if together they are worth a {\i perutah}, she is betrothed; {\i ve-im lav einah mekudeshet}, and if not, she is not. Here the single composite statement ties all the dates into one act of {\i kiddushin}, and their values aggregate accordingly.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Eating While Given\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} then adds: {\i haysah ochelet rishonah rishonah} \u8212- if she was eating each date as he placed it before her \u8212- {\i ve-einah mekudeshet ela im ken yesh be-achas mehen shaveh perutah} \u8212- the combined-statement rule does not apply, and one of the dates by itself must be worth a {\i perutah}. Even in the composite-statement case, the fact that she is consuming each date as it is offered changes the legal analysis.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Why Eating Matters\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} asks: on which clause of the {\i mishna} does this final condition apply? And why does the act of eating matter at all?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Textual Ambiguity\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Ilei\u8217'ma areisha} \u8212- if the clause about eating applies to the first case, where he says {\i hiskadshi} separately before each date, then the condition seems redundant: even without the eating, each date must individually be worth a {\i perutah}, since the statements were never combined. Why would eating change or add anything? The {\i gemara} therefore proposes {\i ela aseifa} \u8212- the clause applies to the final case of the combined statement. But even then, the question presses: if she was eating the dates as he gave them, why can she not be betrothed on the basis of their combined value?\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Milveh Concept\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabba & Shmuel\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabba and Shmuel both hold {\i le\u8217'olam areisha} \u8212- the clause about eating applies to the first case, where each date carries a separate {\i hiskadshi} declaration. The apparent redundancy is resolved as a {\i lo miba\u8217'ya} construction.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Objective Value\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} explains: it goes without saying that when she leaves the dates in front of her \u8212- {\i manacha} \u8212- and one of them is worth a {\i perutah}, she is betrothed, and if not, she is not. But one might have thought that when she is eating each date eagerly as it is placed before her \u8212- {\i ho\u8217'il u-mekarva hana\u8217'asa}, since she derives immediate pleasure from it \u8212- perhaps even a date worth less than a {\i perutah} suffices, on the theory that she has demonstrated by her conduct that she values it at {\i perutah} or more. {\i Ka mashma lan} \u8212- the {\i mishna} teaches that this is not so; the requirement remains an objective valuation of {\i shaveh perutah}. Tosafot notes that this is a rather weak {\i chidush}, since an earlier passage in the {\i gemara} had already established clearly that subjective satisfaction cannot substitute for the minimum objective value, but the answer stands.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Ami\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Ami offers a different reading: {\i le\u8217'olam seifa} \u8212- the eating clause does apply to the last case, the combined statement. And his explanation resolves the structural difficulty by reinterpreting the phrase {\i im yesh be-achas mehen shaveh perutah}: it does not mean that any one of the dates needs to be worth a {\i perutah}, as the plain reading would suggest. Rather, it means {\i ad she-yavo be-achrona shaveh perutah} \u8212- the last date given must itself be worth a {\i perutah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Act Completion\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The logic is as follows. When he says \u8220"this and this and this,\u8221" the act of {\i kiddushin} is not complete until the final word of the declaration. At the moment the declaration is complete, all the dates he gave beforehand have already been consumed. Those earlier dates no longer exist in any concrete form; they are, at best, outstanding obligations \u8212- money she would owe him if the {\i kiddushin} were to fail. Something given as {\i kesef kiddushin} that would have to be returned if the {\i kiddushin} does not take effect functions as a loan, a {\i milveh}, not as a present object. And one cannot effect {\i kiddushin} with a {\i milveh}. The only item that is concretely present and not yet consumed at the moment the declaration is finished is the last date. Therefore, only if that final date is worth a {\i perutah} can the {\i kiddushin} take effect.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Milveh Invalid\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Amar Rava: shema mina mi-de-Rabbi Ami tlat.} From Rabbi Ami\u8217's reading of the {\i mishna}, Rava draws three inferences. The first: {\i shema mina ha-mekadesh be-milveh eina mekudeshet} \u8212- since the {\i mishna}\u8217's analysis treats the eaten dates as {\i milveh} and therefore ineffective for {\i kiddushin}, we learn that marrying with a {\i milveh} does not work. This will be the subject of extended subsequent {\i gemara}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Milveh + Perutah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The second inference: {\i shema mina ha-mekadesh be-milveh u-perutah da\u8217'ata a-perutah}. Suppose a man says to a woman: \u8220"I marry you with the hundred dollars you owe me \u8212- keep that debt \u8212- and with this penny.\u8221" The {\i milveh} component cannot serve as {\i kesef kiddushin}; the question is whether the penny alone can carry the act. One might argue that since he invoked the {\i milveh} as part of the package and that part is invalid, the entire declaration is compromised. Alternatively, the {\i milveh} drops out as irrelevant, and the concrete {\i perutah} before her does the work \u8212- provided that both parties are focused on it. The {\i gemara} phrases this as {\i da\u8217'ata a-perutah} (and in parallel passages {\i da\u8217'ateih}), indicating that the mental focus of both parties on the concrete {\i perutah} is what allows the {\i kiddushin} to function despite the invalid {\i milveh} component.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Milveh vs Matana\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The third inference concerns the status of the money in the first place. {\i Maot be-alma chozrin} \u8212- when {\i kesef kiddushin} is given and the {\i kiddushin} fails to take effect, the money must ordinarily be returned. This is the premise of calling the early consumed dates a {\i milveh}: they would need to be returned if the {\i kiddushin} did not go through. Tosafot raises the question of whether this premise is itself self-evident. What if we were to say instead that {\i kesef kiddushin} is a {\i matana} \u8212- a gift \u8212- regardless of whether the {\i kiddushin} takes effect? If the eaten dates were hers to keep in any event, we would not frame them as a debt; we might rather say she owns them outright and the subsequent dates are simply part of one ongoing {\i kinyan}. Tosafot argues that if the money were indeed a {\i matana}, the {\i mishna} itself would be inexplicable: there would then be no reason why the last date specifically must be worth a {\i perutah}, since the aggregate of everything she received \u8212- all hers in any event \u8212- could simply combine to meet the threshold. The fact that the {\i mishna} requires the last date to carry a {\i perutah} only makes sense if the earlier dates are a {\i milveh} and thus disqualified from serving as {\i kesef kiddushin}. Rava\u8217's third inference is therefore that the premise of {\i maot chozrin} \u8212- money given for {\i kiddushin} must be returned if the {\i kiddushin} fails \u8212- is the correct one.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Sister Marriage Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Setup\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now presents a vivid illustration of these principles. {\i Ha-mekadesh achoto} \u8212- a man who attempts to betroth his own sister, saying {\i harei at mekudeshet li be-taba\u8217'at zo}. The {\i kiddushin} is of course void. The question is what happens to the ring.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava: Return It\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rava amar: maot chozrin} \u8212- she must return the ring; it is a {\i milveh}, and he retains legal title. {\i Ve-gamar ve-nasan le-shum pikadon} \u8212- and the legal framing of what occurred is that he gave it to her as a deposit, a {\i pikadon}, held on his behalf.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Why Pikadon\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But if that is what he intended, why not simply say outright: \u8220"hold this for me\u8221"? {\i Savar lo mekablah} \u8212- she would refuse. By framing it as {\i kiddushin}, even a transparently void {\i kiddushin}, the ring passes into her hand. The practical purpose is to get the object into her possession while preserving his legal ownership, rather than seeking her cooperation in an ordinary bailment.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shmuel: Gift\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i U-Shmuel savar: maot matana.} Shmuel holds the ring is a gift. {\i Adam yodeya she-ein kiddushin tofsin be-achoto} \u8212- he obviously knew that {\i kiddushin} cannot take effect with one\u8217's own sister, {\i ve-gamar ve-nasan le-shum matana} \u8212- and so his giving of the ring, whatever form it took, was in substance a gift. Again the {\i gemara} asks: why not simply say \u8220"this is a gift\u8221"? {\i Savar kesifa lei milsa} \u8212- it would be embarrassing for her to receive it framed as a gift, so the form of {\i kiddushin} provides a face-saving vehicle for the transfer.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ravina\u8217's Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Ravina challenges the underlying logic of this entire dispute. If all parties understand that the {\i kiddushin} is void \u8212- and indeed everyone knows that {\i kiddushin} with one\u8217's sister is invalid \u8212- then the debate between {\i milveh} and {\i matana} might seem academic. Both Rava and Shmuel agree that no valid {\i kiddushin} has occurred; the only question is the legal characterization of the transfer itself.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Hallah Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Flour Not Hallah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} introduces a parallel case. {\i Ha-mafrish chalato kemach} \u8212- one who separates {\i challah} while the grain product is still flour, before it has become dough. {\i Challah} can only be designated from dough, not from flour; the separation is therefore legally ineffective, {\i eino challah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kohen Keeping\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Ve-gezel be-yad kohen} \u8212- if the flour was nonetheless given to a {\i kohen}, and the {\i kohen} retained it, that would constitute theft. This is the position of the {\i gemara}. The challenge to Shmuel is immediate: {\i naima adam yodeya she-ein mafrishin challah kemach} \u8212- according to Shmuel\u8217's principle, since everyone knows that {\i challah} cannot be taken from flour, the transaction should be read as a {\i matana} and the {\i kohen} should be entitled to keep the flour.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Confusion Risk\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} responds: {\i shani hasam de-nafik churba mineih} \u8212- that case is different because permitting the {\i kohen} to keep the flour would cause serious harm. If the {\i kohen} retains it, he will conclude that the separation was in fact valid {\i challah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Partial Knowledge\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But this concern seems to contradict itself: if everyone knows you cannot take {\i challah} from flour, why would the {\i kohen} draw a false conclusion? The {\i gemara} resolves this by distinguishing between knowing a rule and understanding its reason. {\i Yada de-ein mafrishin challah kemach ve-eino yodea tama mai} \u8212- people know that one is not supposed to separate {\i challah} from flour, but they do not necessarily know {\i why}. They may think the reason is only {\i tircha de-kohen} \u8212- an inconvenience to the {\i kohen}, who would have to convert the flour to dough \u8212- and that perhaps the separation is valid {\i be-di\u8217'eved}. If the {\i kohen} is then permitted to keep the flour, they will infer that the separation did take effect retroactively.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Force Return\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 To prevent this confusion, the flour must be returned. Allowing the {\i kohen} to retain it would implicitly signal that the {\i challah} designation was valid, leading people to draw incorrect halachic conclusions.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hydroponics Parallel\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then asks why the same concern does not prevent a comparable ruling in the case of {\i terumah min ha-nakuv al she-eino nakuv} \u8212- terumah taken from produce grown in a perforated pot on behalf of produce grown in an unperforated pot. Produce grown without a perforation in the pot is not biblically obligated in {\i terumah}, so the designation is technically invalid. Nevertheless, the {\i halacha} there is that the {\i kohen} may keep the produce, though he may not eat it until {\i terumah} has been properly separated from another source: {\i de-tehavei terumah ve-lo tochal ad she-yotzi aleha terumah u-ma\u8217'aser mi-makom acher}. Why does this not create the same confusion?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 One vs Two Vessels\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Bitrei mane tsaytei, be-chad mana lo tsaytei} \u8212- with two vessels, people will listen and understand; with one vessel, they will not. In the hydroponics case, there are two distinct containers involved \u8212- the perforated and unperforated pots \u8212- and the distinction between them is tangible enough that people can comprehend why the {\i terumah} taken from one does not cover the other. Knowing that two vessels were involved, they will accept the teaching that the {\i kohen} must separate again and will understand that the original designation was somehow incomplete. By contrast, in the flour case there is only one vessel and one substance throughout; telling the {\i kohen} that he may keep the flour while insisting that it is not really {\i challah} will not register. People will simply take the {\i kohen}\u8217's possession as confirmation that the separation was valid. Therefore, in the flour case, the only way to prevent the false inference is to demand return of the flour entirely.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Kiddushin 46\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Ketana Shelo L\u8217'Da\u8217'as Avi\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Father Travels Abroad\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} on 45b deals with the case of a {\i ketana} whose {\i kiddushin} was performed with her father\u8217's knowledge \u8212- whether the father himself arranged the marriage or whether she entered into it herself with his clear approval. The central question concerns what happens next: {\i vehalach aviha limedinas hayam} \u8212- the father subsequently travels to a distant country \u8212- and then {\i ve\u8217'amda venises} \u8212- she proceeds directly to {\i chuppah} without the father\u8217's knowledge or consent. Do we presume that since the {\i kiddushin} was {\i leda\u8217'as aviha}, he would naturally be pleased that the {\i nisuin} took place whenever it did? Or do we say that the {\i chuppah} without his explicit approval is problematic?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This raises a deeper conceptual question about the nature of {\i nisuin} as a {\i kinyan}. On one hand, one might argue that {\i chuppah} is less a new act of {\i kinyan} and more the translation of an already-established legal status into a factual living arrangement, and therefore requires less {\i da\u8217'as} than an entirely new {\i kinyan}. On the other hand, {\i nisuin} is precisely the moment when a {\i ketana} or {\i na\u8217'arah} leaves her father\u8217's {\i reshus} and enters her husband\u8217's {\i reshus}. Even if in the case of an adult woman {\i nisuin} requires somewhat less formal {\i da\u8217'as}, here one cannot imagine that she can transition out of the father\u8217's control without his approval. Several {\i nafka minas} attend this question: can she eat {\i terumah} if her husband is a {\i kohen} \u8212- since a wife of a {\i kohen} may eat {\i terumah} only once she is a {\i nesu\u8217'ah} \u8212- and does her husband inherit her estate if she dies?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav vs.\u160 ?Rav Assi\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} records the following dispute. {\i Amar Rav ocheles biterumah} \u8212- Rav holds that she is presumed to be a {\i nesu\u8217'ah} and may eat {\i terumah} if her husband is a {\i kohen}, and this presumption holds {\i ad sheyavo aviha veyimcheh} \u8212- unless and until the father returns and objects. If the father protests, retroactively she will not have been a {\i nesu\u8217'ah}, at least for so long as she remains a {\i ketana} or {\i na\u8217'arah} under his authority.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Assi disagrees: {\i eina ocheles}, she may not eat {\i terumah}, {\i shema yavo aviha veyimcheh} \u8212- because we are concerned the father may object when he returns, and if he does, it will turn out retroactively that she was a non-{\i kohen}\u8217's wife eating {\i terumah}, a {\i zar} eating {\i terumah l\u8217'mafre\u8217'a}. The question is whether we begin from a presumption that the father will likely approve, or whether we begin from a posture of concern.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} records that there was an actual incident \u8212- {\i hava uvda v\u8217'chash lah Rav l\u8217'ha d\u8217'Rav Assi} \u8212- and Rav was {\i machmir} in practice like the position of Rav Assi. Some {\i Rishonim} understand that Rav did not actually rule like Rav Assi; he simply adopted the stringent position as a practical matter, while maintaining that {\i min hadin} \u8212- strictly speaking \u8212- she is permitted.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Okki Mamona B\u8217'Chezkas\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Shmuel bar Rav Yitzchak reports a concession that qualifies Rav\u8217's position significantly: {\i u\u8217'modeh Rav she\u8217'im mesa eina yorshah} \u8212- Rav concedes that if she were to die, her husband does not inherit her. Even though Rav considers her {\i nesu\u8217'ah} enough to permit the eating of {\i terumah}, the {\i nisuin} is not sufficiently established to transfer her estate to the husband upon her death. The reason is {\i oki mamona b\u8217'chezkas mareih} \u8212- money, or property, remains in the possession of its original owner under conditions of doubt. Since it is uncertain whether there is {\i nisuin}, we do not remove property from her estate and transfer it to him.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This creates an apparent tension: if Rav concedes enough doubt about the {\i nisuin} that he will not allow the property to transfer, how does he permit her to eat {\i terumah} on the basis that she is a {\i nesu\u8217'ah}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Terumah vs.\u160 ?Yerusha\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The resolution offered by Rashi and Tosafot is grounded in the distinction between {\i mide\u8217'oraisa} and {\i miderabbanan}. According to the Bavli, from the moment of {\i kiddushin} the wife of a {\i kohen} may eat {\i terumah} by Torah law \u8212- she does not need to be a {\i nesu\u8217'ah} for this. The requirement that specifically a {\i nesu\u8217'ah} may eat {\i terumah} \u8212- to the exclusion of an {\i arusin} \u8212- is a {\i miderabbanan} enactment. Accordingly, both Rav and Rav Assi agree that her status as a {\i nesu\u8217'ah} is a {\i safek}. When it comes to the Rabbinic question of {\i terumah}, Rav is willing to be lenient. When it comes to the Biblical matter of {\i yerusha}, where property can only be transferred with certainty, he is not.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot adds a further point: one might ask why Rav Assi is concerned about this {\i miderabbanan} restriction at all, given that the practical rationale behind the Rabbinic enactment \u8212- namely that an {\i arusin} who lives separately might bring {\i terumah} back to her father\u8217's non-{\i kohen} household \u8212- does not apply here, since she is living with her husband. Tosafot answers that the Rabbis made the enactment without drawing internal distinctions \u8212- {\i lo plug} \u8212- and therefore even in this case the formal rule applies.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Father Present, Silent\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kiddushin Yes, Nisuin No\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now turns to a variation: the {\i kiddushin} was {\i leda\u8217'as aviha} \u8212- her father knew of and approved the {\i kiddushin} \u8212- but the {\i nisuin} took place {\i shelo leda\u8217'as aviha}, and the father is present, {\i v\u8217'aviha kan}. Presumably the father found out after the fact and said nothing. What is the legal status of his silence?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Silence: Approval or Obj\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Huna says: {\i eina ocheles} \u8212- she may not eat {\i terumah}. Even accepting Rav\u8217's position that when the father is in a distant country we presume he would approve, {\i hasam hu d\u8217'lo isei l\u8217'av} \u8212- that is only because the father is not present, and in his absence we may assume he will be satisfied when he learns of it. But here, {\i d\u8217'isei l\u8217'av} \u8212- the father is present. He heard about the {\i nisuin} and said nothing. {\i Hai de\u8217'ishtik mirtzach ratach} \u8212- his silence reflects smoldering anger; a man too upset to speak should not be understood as having acquiesced.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Yirmeya bar Abba disagrees: {\i ocheles}. Even granting Rav Assi\u8217's position that when the father is far away we are concerned he will object \u8212- {\i hasam hu d\u8217'shema yavo aviha veyimcheh} \u8212- that is because there is an open question whether he will protest. But here, {\i de\u8217'ishtik, isnuche ishnach leh} \u8212- he heard and said nothing, which we interpret as acceptance. The principle of {\i shtika k\u8217'hoda\u8217'ah} \u8212- that silence in the face of a known fact constitutes a form of assent \u8212- supports this reading. Normally, we would expect an opposed party to say something.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This leads to a striking analytical observation. The position that makes intuitive sense is Rav Yirmeya bar Abba\u8217's: if one begins from a presumption of concern (Rav Assi\u8217's approach), silence that follows upon hearing changes the calculus, since a person who genuinely objects would speak up. What is harder to understand is the reverse switch in Rav Huna\u8217's reasoning: if one begins from a presumption that the father will be fine with it (Rav\u8217's approach), how does the fact that he heard and said nothing make things {\i worse} than when he was simply absent? The idea that silence signals rage rather than approval seems strained when the baseline presumption is benign.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Both Acts Unknown\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now advances to a more extreme scenario: {\i niskadsha shelo leda\u8217'as aviha venisas shelo leda\u8217'as aviha v\u8217'aviha kan} \u8212- both the {\i kiddushin} and the {\i nisuin} were performed without the father\u8217's knowledge, and the father is present. Again, the assumption is that once he found out, he was silent. Here Rav Huna says {\i ocheles} \u8212- she may eat \u8212- while Rav Yirmeya bar Abba says {\i eina ocheles}. The positions are now reversed from the previous case.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Ulla is sharply critical of Rav Huna\u8217's ruling here. He invokes the verse: {\i k\u8217'chometz l\u8217'shinayim u\u8217'k\u8217'ashan l\u8217'einayim} \u8212- like vinegar to the teeth and smoke to the eyes \u8212- to convey how intolerable this position is. The argument is stark: in the {\i immediately preceding case}, where the {\i kiddushin} was definitely valid and performed with his knowledge, and only the {\i nisuin} was done without him \u8212- yet Rav Huna there said the father\u8217's silence signals objection and she {\i cannot} eat \u8212- how can it be that here, where the father was not even present for the {\i kiddushin}, his silence now somehow signals approval?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Nachas B\u8217'Ma\u8217'aseh\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava defends Rav Huna with a different rationale. {\i Mai ta\u8217'ama d\u8217'Rav Huna? Ho\u8217'v\u8217'na\u8217'aseh b\u8217'ma\u8217'aseh yatom b\u8217'chayei av} \u8212- this girl has been conducting herself as though she has no father. Two major events in her life \u8212- {\i kiddushin} and {\i nisuin} \u8212- have occurred without his knowledge or involvement, and he has reacted to neither. In such a pattern of behavior, where the daughter consistently acts autonomously and the father consistently fails to assert his authority, we interpret the cumulative passivity as an implicit relinquishment of control. He has essentially made his peace and decided to let her make her own decisions.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The contrast with the previous case is now coherent: when the {\i kiddushin} was firmly established under his auspices and authority, and only the {\i nisuin} was done without him, he has never done anything to signal that he was stepping back from his paternal role. His silence regarding the {\i nisuin} alone is therefore reasonably read as resistance. But when he allowed {\i both} acts to occur without reaction, the pattern itself speaks \u8212- and we conclude he has, in effect, yielded.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Father\u8217's Direct Response\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Bein Hi U\u8217'Vein Aviha\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now returns to the original case: {\i ketana sheniskadsha shelo leda\u8217'as aviha} \u8212- a {\i ketana} who entered into {\i kiddushin} without her father\u8217's knowledge. This was the primary focus of the previous {\i sugya}, and the conclusion of Rav and Shmuel was that there is a {\i chashash kiddushin} \u8212- one is concerned that {\i kiddushin} may have taken effect \u8212- on the possibility that the father will retroactively approve, {\i shema yisratzeh ha\u8217'av}. What was never clarified there, however, is: what happens when the father actually responds?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav now addresses this directly: {\i bein hi uvein aviha yecholim limchos} \u8212- both the daughter and the father can prevent the {\i kiddushin} from taking effect, or conversely can ratify it. This is, on the surface, somewhat surprising. We already know that if the father objects, there is no concern \u8212- that is obvious. The new information is that {\i she} can also object. But how? She was the one who accepted the {\i kiddushin}. She is a minor, so what does her objection mean?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rashi explains that her objection is only effective if it occurs {\i before} the father finds out. She accepted the money; the father will hear of it tomorrow; but if before he does she retracts, she has cut off the possibility of ratification before it could crystallize. The act of acceptance is undermined by her subsequent retraction before the father\u8217's knowledge can validate it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot offers a broader reading: she may be able to object even {\i after} the father approves, on the theory that the father\u8217's approval is premised on her also being agreeable. However, Tosafot is vague on the outer limits of this \u8212- does she have an indefinite window to object? \u8212- and this reading is admittedly difficult, since she was the one who originally accepted the {\i kiddushin}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shtika vs.\u160 ?Micha\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot clarifies the relationship between this ruling and everything that came before. When Rav earlier said there is a {\i chashash} that the father will approve \u8212- {\i chaishinan shema yisratzeh ha\u8217'av} \u8212- that ruling was specifically in a case of {\i stam}, where the father heard and was silent, {\i sheshama v\u8217'shatak}. The entire preceding {\i sugya} was about how to interpret paternal silence. But where the father explicitly objects \u8212- {\i micha, gila b\u8217'da\u8217'ato} \u8212- he has disclosed his position and the {\i kiddushin} is certainly void. Similarly, if he explicitly approves, it is certainly valid. The debate about silence is thus the operative center of the preceding discussion, which now becomes clear in retrospect.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Daughter\u8217's Miun\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The right of the daughter to object constitutes a form of {\i miun} \u8212- refusal \u8212- in the context of a {\i ketana}. It is a different mechanism than ordinary {\i miun}, since according to Rav there is at least a {\i chashash kiddushin d\u8217'oraisa}. According to Rav Assi, however, only the father can object \u8212- {\i aviha v\u8217'lo hi} \u8212- which is the more standard position. The father\u8217's authority over his minor daughter\u8217's marriage is the default; the daughter\u8217's power of objection is the exceptional ruling that requires derivation.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Seduction & Torah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Midrash on Seduction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Huna (or, in another version, Chiyya bar Rav) presents a challenge to Rav Assi from the Biblical passage dealing with {\i mefuta} \u8212- the case of a man who seduces a {\i na\u8217'arah}. The verse states {\i im ma\u8217'ein yema\u8217'ein aviha lesitta lo} \u8212- if the father absolutely refuses to give her to him, the seducer must pay fifty {\i kesef}. The {\i gemara} asks: from where do we know that {\i she} can also refuse? The answer derived by {\i midrash} is from the doubled language of {\i ma\u8217'ein yema\u8217'ein} \u8212- a doubling that implies both the father\u8217's refusal and hers. The underlying assumption of the question is that the Torah case involves a {\i kiddushin} of sorts \u8212- the seduction was {\i leshum ishus}, for the purpose of marriage, and the act of intimacy constituted {\i kiddushei bi\u8217'ah}, with the father\u8217's knowledge now a condition of validity. This is precisely analogous to the cases we have been discussing.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Pita Shelo L\u8217'Shum\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav deflects the challenge: {\i lo teizil basar ipcha} \u8212- do not seek complications. {\i Yacholni lshinuyei lach lchula} \u8212- I can answer you in a way that resolves everything. Specifically: {\i kegon shepita shelo lshum ishus} \u8212- the seduction in the Biblical case was not for the purpose of marriage. The man simply seduced her for the sake of intimacy, not as an act of {\i kiddushin}. Accordingly, what the Torah is addressing when it says the father can refuse is not refusal of {\i kiddushin}, but refusal of the proposed marriage after the fact \u8212- since of course the father controls whom his daughter may marry, the case tells us the legal consequence of that refusal, namely that the seducer pays fifty {\i kesef}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Maher Yimarena\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Ashi then interjects: {\i ei hachi hainu dtanyana, maher yimarena lo l\u8217'isha shetricha heimenu kiddushin} \u8212- this reading actually makes good sense of the verse that follows, which says {\i maher yimarena lo l\u8217'isha}, meaning he must now marry her. The implication is that he still needs to perform {\i kiddushin}, which confirms that no {\i kiddushin} had yet taken place \u8212- the seduction was indeed {\i shelo lshum ishus}. Some {\i Rishonim} read {\i ei hachi hainu dtanyana} not as a sharp question but as an expression of approval \u8212- \u8220"ah, now it fits perfectly\u8221" \u8212- though others understand it as a genuine challenge.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tzricha Kiddushin L\u8217'Da\u8217'as\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then raises the alternative reading: {\i ve\u8217'ei pita lshum ishus kiddushin lama li} \u8212- what if the seduction {\i was} for the purpose of marriage? In that case, the act of intimacy constituted {\i kiddushei bi\u8217'ah}. What then does {\i maher yimarena lo l\u8217'isha} mean? Abaye answers: {\i tzricha kiddushin leda\u8217'as aviha} \u8212- the verse teaches that even if the father chooses not to object and allows the marriage to proceed, a new act of {\i kiddushin} must be performed, this time with the father\u8217's knowledge and proper approval from the outset. This is admittedly strange \u8212- if the act of seduction already constituted {\i kiddushin} and the father ratifies it, why is another {\i kiddushin} required? The answer seems to be that the Torah is insisting on a fully proper, formal process even where the prior act has a degree of legal standing.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Miun & Knas\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The case of the daughter\u8217's refusal now requires closer attention. On the reading that the seduction was {\i shelo lshum ishus}, if the father objects and she does not marry the seducer, he pays fifty {\i kesef}. But the verse also implies she can refuse. What does her refusal accomplish, given that she is a {\i ketana} or {\i na\u8217'arah} still under her father\u8217's authority?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot addresses this in two ways. The first answer is that her {\i miun} \u8212- her refusal \u8212- does not prevent the marriage from taking place if the father wants it to proceed. {\i D\u8217'keivan d\u8217'avia rotzeh she\u8217'tehei ishto, eino yachola l\u8217'akeiv} \u8212- if the father wishes to marry her off, she cannot block it. Rather, what her refusal accomplishes is to trigger the payment of {\i knas}: just as the father\u8217's refusal causes the seducer to pay, so too her refusal, even if the marriage ultimately proceeds, causes the payment. This leads to what seems like an odd outcome: could the father and daughter coordinate \u8212- the father consents to the marriage, the daughter refuses, they collect the {\i knas} and she marries the man anyway?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot\u8217's second and better answer is that her refusal is operative specifically in the case where the father has died before the matter is resolved. {\i D\u8217'hashta mehani miun didah l\u8217'akeiv she\u8217'lo tehei ishto} \u8212- in the absence of a father, her refusal is what prevents the marriage from taking effect. This is more coherent: once there is no father, she is the one in whose hands the decision rests, and her {\i miun} then has full legal force. The general question that is never fully resolved, however, is what it means to speak of the daughter\u8217's right of refusal when a father is present and she is still a {\i ketana} or {\i na\u8217'arah} \u8212- under normal circumstances, she simply has no such right.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Multiple Objects\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Separate Statements\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} turns to a {\i Mishnah} dealing with the case of a normal adult woman entering into {\i kiddushin}. If a man says to a woman {\i hiskadshi li b\u8217'tmara zo, hiskadshi li b\u8217'zo} \u8212- \u8220"be betrothed to me with this date, be betrothed to me with this date\u8221" \u8212- giving her separate objects with a separate declaration for each, the rule is: {\i im yesh b\u8217'achas meihen shaveh prutah mekudeshes} \u8212- if any one of them was individually worth a {\i perutah}, she is {\i mekudeshes}; if not, she is not, even if all of them combined would equal a {\i perutah}. Since each declaration constitutes a separate act of {\i kiddushin}, the objects cannot be combined.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Combined Statements\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If, however, he said {\i b\u8217'zo u\u8217'v\u8217'zo u\u8217'v\u8217'zo} \u8212- \u8220"with this and this and this\u8221" \u8212- without repeating the declaration between each object, then {\i im yesh shaveh prutah b\u8217'kulam mekudeshes} \u8212- as long as they collectively amount to a {\i perutah}, she is {\i mekudeshes}. Since the single declaration covers all the objects together, they combine into one act.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Eating While Giving\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A third case: {\i haysa ocheles rishona rishona} \u8212- if she was eating each date as he placed it in front of her, {\i einah mekudeshes ad she\u8217'tehei b\u8217'achas meihen shaveh prutah} \u8212- she is not {\i mekudeshes} unless one of the objects is individually worth a {\i perutah}. The reason for this rule is the focus of the subsequent {\i gemara}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} first asks who the {\i tana} is who holds that saying {\i hiskadshi, hiskadshi} between each object is what breaks the statements into discrete acts. Rabbah answers that it is Rabbi Shimon, who holds the same principle in the context of {\i shvuos}: if one says \u8220"a {\i shvuah} I do not owe you, and a {\i shvuah} I do not owe you,\u8221" those are separate oaths with separate {\i korbanos} for false swearing. But if one says \u8220"a {\i shvuah} I do not owe you or you or you,\u8221" that is one oath. The same logic applies here.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Areisha L\u8217'Olam\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then asks on which clause of the {\i Mishnah} the eating case applies. If we say it is the first clause \u8212- the case of separate {\i hiskadshi} declarations \u8212- the point of mentioning eating is unclear. In that case even without eating, if one of the dates was worth a {\i perutah}, she would be {\i mekudeshes}, and if not, not. Why add the detail of eating? Rav and Shmuel both say {\i le\u8217'olam areisha} \u8212- it goes on the first clause \u8212- and the {\i Mishnah} employs the rhetorical device of {\i lo mibaya}: it goes without saying that if the dates are simply lying before her, the rule is that each needs to be worth a {\i perutah}; the {\i chidush} is that even when she is eating them eagerly, demonstrating immediate enjoyment \u8212- {\i ho\u8217'il u\u8217'mekarva hana\u8217'atah} \u8212- one might have thought that her enthusiasm itself signals acceptance even for less than a {\i perutah}. The {\i Mishnah} teaches that this is not so: a {\i perutah} of objective value is still required. Tosafot notes that this is a relatively weak {\i chidush}, since the earlier {\i gemara} made clear that {\i kiddushin} requires an objectively recognized {\i perutah}, not a subjective sense of value.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Aseifa L\u8217'Olam\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Ami offers the more natural reading: {\i le\u8217'olam aseifa} \u8212- it goes on the last clause, the combined-statement case. The {\i Mishnah}\u8217's rule that \u8220"one of them must be worth a {\i perutah}\u8221" means specifically the {\i last} one. Here is the logic: when a man says \u8220"with this and this and this,\u8221" the act of {\i kiddushin} is not complete until the final object is given. By that time, she has already eaten the preceding dates. Those earlier dates no longer exist as concrete objects; they have been consumed. Consequently, the man cannot \u8220"marry her\u8221" with those dates in their current non-existent form \u8212- to do so would be {\i mekadesh b\u8217'milveh}, marrying with a debt, which is invalid. However, the last date, which has not yet been eaten at the moment the statement concludes and the act is completed, is present before her. If that last date is worth a {\i perutah}, the {\i kiddushin} takes effect on the basis of that concrete object.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shma Mina Tlat\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava then draws three inferences from Rabbi Ami\u8217's explanation. {\i Shma mina hamekadesh b\u8217'milveh einah mekudeshes} \u8212- first, it is confirmed that one who performs {\i kiddushin} using a debt (a {\i milveh}) accomplishes nothing, since Rabbi Ami does not challenge the premise of the question but rather redirects which date is operative.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i U\u8217'shma mina hamekadesh b\u8217'milveh u\u8217'prutah da\u8217'atah a\u8217'prutah} \u8212- second, if a man says \u8220"marry me with the hundred dollars you owe me and with this additional penny,\u8221" does it work? One might argue that since he framed it as both the debt and the coin, and the debt portion is invalid for {\i kiddushin}, perhaps the coin does not stand alone either. Rava infers from Rabbi Ami\u8217's ruling that in such a case, both he and she are understood to be focusing on the concrete {\i perutah} before them \u8212- {\i da\u8217'atah a\u8217'prutah} \u8212- and the {\i milveh} component drops away as legally inoperative, leaving the {\i perutah} as the effective {\i kesef kiddushin}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Shma mina ma\u8217'os be\u8217'alma chozrin} \u8212- third, the very fact that we describe the earlier consumed dates as a {\i milveh} implies that money given as {\i kesef kiddushin} that does not result in valid {\i kiddushin} must be returned. If the money were simply a {\i matanah} \u8212- a gift \u8212- there would be no reason to call it a {\i milveh} at all.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mkvadesh B\u8217'Milveh\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot raises a question on this third inference: is Rava not simply reading his assumption back out of his conclusion? The question {\i u\u8217'milveh hi} was posed as a challenge, and now we are deriving from Rabbi Ami\u8217's non-response that yes, it is a {\i milveh}. But what if the money was treated as a {\i matanah}? Tosafot argues that the distinction is legally significant: if the money given during the sequential date-giving is understood as a {\i matanah} regardless of whether {\i kiddushin} ultimately takes effect, then she owns each date the moment she receives it. In that case, there is no conceptual gap between the early dates and the later ones \u8212- she already owns them, and there is no \u8220"debt\u8221" relationship to speak of. Accordingly, the entire combined statement could be viewed as one integrated act, and the collective value might suffice for {\i kiddushin}. The only way to explain why the {\i Mishnah} requires one specific date to be worth a {\i perutah} by itself is through the {\i milveh} framework: since those earlier dates might need to be returned if {\i kiddushin} fails, they are conceptually debt rather than concrete present property, and {\i kiddushin} cannot rest upon them.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Invalid Kiddushin Cases\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sister Marriage\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now presents a clearer case: {\i hamekadesh achoto} \u8212- a man who \u8220"betroths\u8221" his sister, declaring {\i harei at mekudeshes li b\u8217'tabaat zu}. This obviously cannot take effect. The question is: does she return the ring?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Money Returns\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav says {\i ma\u8217'os chozrin} \u8212- the money must be returned. The reasoning is {\i adam yodea she\u8217'ein kiddushin topsin b\u8217'achoso, v\u8217'gamar v\u8217'nasan lshum pikadon} \u8212- he knows perfectly well that {\i kiddushin} cannot take effect with his sister, so his act of giving the ring is to be understood as a deposit, as placing the object in her hands for safekeeping. The {\i gemara} asks: if that is what he intended, why not simply say so explicitly? The answer is {\i savar lo mikabla} \u8212- he was concerned she would refuse to accept it if framed that way, so the {\i kiddushin} language was a pretext to get the ring into her hands. Since the underlying intention was a deposit, it must be returned.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Money as Gift\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Shmuel disagrees: {\i ma\u8217'os matanah} \u8212- the money is a gift. {\i Adam yodea she\u8217'ein kiddushin topsin b\u8217'achoso, v\u8217'gamar v\u8217'nasan lshum matanah} \u8212- knowing that no {\i kiddushin} can take effect, his act of giving must be interpreted as an outright gift. The {\i gemara} similarly asks: if so, why not say explicitly it is a gift? {\i Savar ksifa lei milsa} \u8212- it would be embarrassing. Framing it as a gift is socially awkward, whereas the {\i kiddushin} framing allows the ring to pass into her possession without the discomfort of one party explicitly giving the other a gift in this context. The practical effect is that she keeps it.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Challah & Terumah Cases\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Challah Flour Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Ravina challenges Shmuel\u8217's position with a parallel case: {\i hamfrish chalato kemach} \u8212- one who separates {\i challah} while the grain is still flour rather than dough. This is invalid; {\i challah} cannot be separated until the dough stage. {\i Eino challah} \u8212- it does not take effect as {\i challah}. And yet, if the flour was given to a {\i kohen}, we say {\i v\u8217'gezel b\u8217'yad kohen} \u8212- it is theft in the {\i kohen}\u8217's possession; the {\i kohen} must return it. But why, according to Shmuel?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Confusion Risk\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} applies Shmuel\u8217's logic: everyone knows that {\i challah} cannot be separated from flour, so why not say the person gave it as a {\i matanah}, and let the {\i kohen} keep it? The {\i gemara} answers: {\i shani hasam d\u8217'nafi churbah minei} \u8212- this case is different, because allowing the {\i kohen} to keep the flour would lead to a serious problem. {\i Zimnin d\u8217'is lei l\u8217'kohen pachos m\u8217'chamesh rivayim kemach} \u8212- there may come a time when the {\i kohen} has slightly less than the minimum quantity of flour obligated in {\i challah}. He then takes the flour you gave him \u8212- which he believes is already {\i challah} \u8212- and mixes it with his own supply. Now the combined quantity exceeds the threshold and is genuinely obligated in {\i challah}, but he thinks no {\i challah} need be separated because he believes a portion of the mixture already is {\i challah}. {\i V\u8217'asi l\u8217'meichal b\u8217'tevel} \u8212- he will eat the bread when it is still {\i tevel}, since true {\i challah} was never separated.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then challenges this reasoning: you just said that everyone knows you do not separate {\i challah} from flour \u8212- so why would the {\i kohen} be confused? The answer distinguishes between knowing the rule and understanding its legal consequence. People know that one is {\i not supposed to} separate {\i challah} from flour, and they assume the rationale is {\i mishum tircha dekohen} \u8212- so as not to burden the {\i kohen} with having to convert flour to dough himself. But they do not know with certainty what happens {\i bedi\u8217'avad} if it was done anyway. They may think it worked after the fact, especially since the {\i kohen} is {\i mochel} on his own {\i tircha}. Therefore, if we allow him to keep the flour without requiring return, people will conclude it really did work, leading to the error described.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hydroponic Terumah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} proposes an alternative solution: rather than requiring the {\i kohen} to return the flour, perhaps declare it simultaneously {\i challah} and not-{\i challah} \u8212- allow him to keep it but require him to separate {\i challah} again from another source before eating. A precedent exists for this kind of dual status. The {\i gemara} cites a case: {\i min hanakuv al she\u8217'eino nakuv} \u8212- one takes {\i terumah} from produce grown in a pot with a hole (which is biblically obligated) on behalf of produce grown in a pot without a hole (hydroponic produce, which is not fully biblically obligated). The {\i terumah} separated in this way has an ambiguous status. The ruling is: {\i terumah} \u8212- it is treated with the stringency of {\i terumah} \u8212- {\i velo tochal ad sheyotzi alei terumah uma\u8217'aser mimmakom acher} \u8212- but it cannot be eaten until additional {\i terumah} and {\i ma\u8217'aser} are separated from another source. The {\i kohen} keeps the produce but must separate again. Why not do the same with the flour?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Bitrei Mani vs.\u160 ?Echad\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} answers with a distinction of considerable practical subtlety. {\i Bitrei mani tzyat, b\u8217'chad mana lo tzyat} \u8212- with two vessels they will understand; with one vessel they will not. The hydroponics case involves produce from two conceptually distinct categories \u8212- pots with holes and pots without holes are recognizably different situations. When told that the {\i kohen} can keep the produce but must separate again, the recipient intuitively grasps that there is something unusual about the provenance of this particular produce, and the dual treatment makes sense to him.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Return vs.\u160 ?Re-Separate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But in the flour case, everything is {\i chad mana} \u8212- one vessel, one undifferentiated mass. If we tell the {\i kohen} he can keep the flour but must separate {\i challah} again, he will not understand why. As far as he can see, the flour he holds is {\i challah}, full stop \u8212- and the instruction to separate again will strike him as incomprehensible. He will not internalize the message that the original separation was invalid; he will simply think the requirement to re-separate is an inexplicable imposition. Under these conditions, the confusion is not adequately addressed by requiring re-separation. The only solution that genuinely communicates the invalidity of the original act is insisting on return. Therefore, in the flour case, the {\i kohen} must return the flour; in the hydroponic case, he may keep the produce but must separate again.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Kiddushin 47\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Multiple Dates\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Intro: Dates & Shaveh\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The discussion opens in the context of the {\i mishnah} dealing with a man who counts off individual dates \u8212- or similar objects \u8212- one by one, saying \u8220"marry me with this, and with this, and with this,\u8221" and the question whether each date constitutes a separate act of {\i kiddushin} or whether they are combined into one act, such that collectively they might satisfy the requirement of {\i shaveh perutah}. We pick up on the bottom of 46b, approximately twenty lines from the bottom, at the line beginning with the word {\i bechamna}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Money: Gift or Loan?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 At this point the {\i gemara} temporarily moves away from the question about counting off the dates and raises a different question: if a man performs {\i kiddushin} and for some reason the {\i kiddushin} does not take effect, must the woman return the money? Is the money to be treated as a gift \u8212- a {\i matanah} \u8212- or as a {\i pikadon}, an entrusted object held in trust? Obviously, if he stated his intention explicitly, there is no question. The issue is how to interpret the transaction legally when nothing further was said.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav vs.\u160 ?Shmuel\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} frames the question as a debate between Rav and Shmuel, using the case of a man who betroths his sister \u8212- a {\i kiddushin} that everyone understands is entirely invalid. Does she return the money or may she keep it? Rav holds she must return it, which seems to be the straightforward position: why should we read into the transaction a new gift? Shmuel, however, holds that she may keep it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Challah Analogy\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} challenges Shmuel\u8217's position from a case involving one who separates {\i challah} from flour \u8212- that is, before it has become dough \u8212- and gives it to a {\i kohen}. This is similarly a transaction that everyone should understand is ineffective. The {\i halakhah} is that the {\i kohen} must return the flour; he does not get to keep it. This appears to support Rav\u8217's position.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shmuel\u8217's Reasoning\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} explains that according to Shmuel, the reason the {\i kohen} must return the flour is not because of any general principle about invalid transactions, but rather to prevent confusion about the law. If the {\i kohen} keeps the flour, the {\i ba\u8217'al habayit} or the {\i kohen} himself might come to think the {\i challah} separation was valid, leading to further violations. The flour is therefore returned as a way of reinforcing that it was not effective {\i challah}. Shmuel\u8217's broader position \u8212- that recipients may generally keep what was given in such contexts \u8212- remains intact; it is only the specific concern about legal confusion that mandates return here.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Alternative: 2nd Separat\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then asks: within the logic of Shmuel\u8217's reasoning, why not find an intermediate solution? Let the {\i kohen} keep the flour, but instruct him to separate {\i challah} again from it \u8212- or, equivalently, once it becomes dough. That would signal to everyone that the original separation was invalid, and the {\i kohen} would still benefit from keeping the flour. The {\i gemara} supports this suggestion by noting that the {\i kohen} would be willing to listen ({\i metzayet}) to such an instruction.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Why Alt. Fails\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The problem, says the {\i gemara}, is the {\i ba\u8217'al habayit}. Even if the {\i kohen} would listen to us and separate {\i challah} a second time, the {\i ba\u8217'al habayit} will not see that second separation. From his perspective, he gave flour, the {\i kohen} kept it, and the transaction is done. He will now believe his own obligation has been discharged and will proceed to eat his remaining dough while it is still {\i tevel} \u8212- untithed produce. This creates a serious violation. Therefore, returning the flour to the {\i ba\u8217'al habayit} is the only way to prevent the confusion.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Owner\u8217's Knowledge\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then raises what seems like an obvious objection: surely everyone knows that one does not separate {\i challah} from flour before it becomes dough \u8212- the {\i ba\u8217'al habayit} knows this! The {\i gemara} responds with a subtle but important distinction: {\i yodeia v\u8217'eino yodeia} \u8212- he sort of knows, but not fully. He knows the norm is not to do it. However, he does not know the underlying reason. He assumes the prohibition is merely a matter of not burdening the {\i kohen} with the extra work of turning flour into dough, and since the {\i kohen} here was willing to accept the flour ({\i tircha d\u8217'kohen kablah}), he may conclude that {\i bedi\u8217'avad} \u8212- after the fact \u8212- the separation might have been valid. Because his understanding is incomplete and his error is therefore plausible, we must return the flour to correct any possible misconception.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hydroponic Plants\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} continues exploring alternative solutions. It asks: why not tell the {\i ba\u8217'al habayit} that the {\i kohen} may keep the flour, but that the {\i ba\u8217'al habayit} must separate {\i challah} a second time? This is modeled on a known case: one who takes {\i terumah} from hydroponic plants intending it to apply to produce grown in the soil. In that case, the {\i kohen} keeps the hydroponic produce, and the owner is instructed to separate a second time. So too here, perhaps the {\i ba\u8217'al habayit} can be instructed to separate again. The {\i gemara} rejects this parallel: in the hydroponic case, the two items are {\i trei minei} \u8212- fundamentally different categories \u8212- and for that reason the owner accepts that the first separation did not cover the second type and willingly separates again. But in the case of flour and dough, it is essentially the same substance. There is no reason apparent to the owner why he should have to separate a second time if the {\i kohen} is keeping the flour. He will not listen, and the desired effect will not be achieved.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Challenge: Same Object\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} presses further: is it really true that a person will not listen to a second separation when the same type of object is involved? Is there no precedent for that?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Watermelon Precedent\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} answers its own challenge from a {\i mishnah}: one who separated a gourd ({\i kishut}) as {\i terumah} and it turned out to be bitter, or a watermelon and it turned out to be sour ({\i avatiach ve-nimtzet saruach}) \u8212- the {\i kohen} keeps what was given, and the owner must separate again. Here it is all the same category \u8212- all watermelons \u8212- yet we say the separation was effective and the owner must repeat it. This would seem to show that even from a single type, people do listen to a second separation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Distinction: Biblical Va\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} distinguishes this case. Even in the watermelon situation, it is not clear that people will actually comply with the instruction to separate again. But it does not matter, because even if they do not listen, the first {\i terumah} is {\i min ha-Torah} valid \u8212- it is biblically effective {\i terumah de-Oraita}. Since the sour watermelon is still, at least nominally, somewhat edible, the separation takes effect {\i bedi\u8217'avad}. If they fail to separate again, we are not left with a complete violation, because the first separation was already biblically sound. The flour case is entirely different \u8212- there the first separation was not valid at all, and if the owner does not separate again, the dough will be eaten as {\i tevel}. Therefore, the watermelon precedent does not apply.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Leviticus Source\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 How do we know that separating from the inferior on the superior \u8212- {\i min ha-ra al ha-yafeh} \u8212- takes effect {\i bedi\u8217'avad}? Rabbi Ila derives this from the verse: \u8220"You shall not bear sin when you take the {\i chelbo} \u8212- the best of it \u8212- as {\i terumah}\u8221" (Leviticus). The implication is that if you do not take from the best \u8212- that is, if you take from the inferior on the superior \u8212- you will have sinned. As the {\i gemara} reasons, the concept of bearing sin ({\i nesi\u8217'at cheit}) only makes sense if the act was legally effective. If the bad-on-good separation had no legal force whatsoever, there would be nothing to bear sin over: one tried to do something wrong but achieved nothing. The fact that the {\i pasuk} speaks of {\i cheit} demonstrates that the act did take hold \u8212- it was a valid but sinful {\i terumah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is worth noting that {\i Tosafot} raises a significant question here, since there is an entire discussion in {\i Temurah} about the general principle of {\i kol milta de-amar Rachmana la ta\u8217'avid i avid mahani o lo mahani} \u8212- whether an act performed in violation of a Torah prohibition takes legal effect. The cases vary: some prohibited acts do achieve their intended legal effect ({\i arayot}, for instance, are {\i chal}), while others do not ({\i issurei lav} generally are not {\i chal}). {\i Tosafot} asks how the {\i gemara} here can draw the inference it does without accounting for that general debate. He explains that even according to those who hold that a prohibited act may be punishable without taking effect, in this specific case the {\i gemara} understands from the structure of the {\i pasuk} itself that the only way the actor incurred a {\i cheit} is if the {\i terumah} was in fact {\i chal}. The generalization is fair, but the broader question is independently debated.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 In summary: according to Shmuel, if someone betroths his sister, she gets to keep the money, because in principle parties may retain what was given in an invalid transaction. The {\i kohen} must return the flour only because specific concerns about legal confusion \u8212- on the part of either the {\i kohen} or the {\i ba\u8217'al habayit} \u8212- require it, and none of the proposed intermediate solutions adequately addresses those concerns.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Back to Mishnah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Bezo Vezo Vezo Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava now returns to the {\i mishnah} at the top of 47a. The question is: when does the rule apply that if she eats individual dates as they are handed to her, those eaten dates cannot be counted toward the {\i shaveh perutah}? Rava specifies: this is only where the man said {\i bezu uvezu uvezu} \u8212- \u8220"with this, and with this, and with this\u8221" \u8212- designating each date separately as he gives it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Eating: Last Date Only\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 In the {\i bezu uvezu uvezu} case, if she eats each date as he hands it to her, the problem is that the act of {\i kiddushin} is not complete until the man finishes his statement. Each date eaten before that final moment has already been consumed \u8212- it is no longer present when the act of {\i kiddushin} is definitively concluded. Those dates are therefore analogous to a {\i milveh}: they were given, but they are no longer around at the legally significant moment. The only date that counts is the last one \u8212- the one still in existence when his statement concludes \u8212- and that final date must itself be worth a {\i perutah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Collective: All Combined\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The case is different, says Rava, when the man says {\i ba\u8217'elu} \u8212- \u8220"marry me with these\u8221" \u8212- using a collective formulation while indicating the entire group of dates in his hand. He then hands them to her one by one and she eats each as it is given. Even so, she is {\i mekudeshes}. Why? Because the moment he completed the statement {\i ba\u8217'elu}, the entire group was already designated as {\i kesef kiddushin}. The act of {\i kiddushin} was definitionally complete at that moment. Everything she subsequently receives, she receives as her own property \u8212- {\i ki ka\u8217'achla midida ka\u8217'achla}, when she eats it, she eats what is already hers. There is no {\i milveh} problem, because there is no longer any ambiguity about whether these dates are {\i kesef kiddushin}. They were so designated before she received the first one.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is the precise significance of the {\i chiddush}: in the {\i bezu uvezu uvezu} scenario, where each date is described individually and the act of {\i kiddushin} is not complete until the end, the earlier dates are consumed before the transaction is finalized and cannot be counted collectively. In the {\i ba\u8217'elu} scenario, all dates are declared as a unit at the outset, and even if each one is worth only a fifth of a {\i perutah}, they combine to satisfy the requirement \u8212- because they were all labeled as {\i kesef kiddushin} before any was given.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Baraita: Ba\u8217'elu\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} notes that a {\i baraita} supports Rava\u8217's position. The {\i baraita} reads: if a man says to a woman, \u8220"marry me with an acorn, a pomegranate, and a nut\u8221" ({\i hitkadshi li be\u8217'alon be\u8217'rimon u\u8217've\u8217'egoz}) \u8212- or if he says {\i ba\u8217'elu}, \u8220"with all of these\u8221" \u8212- and collectively they are worth a {\i perutah}, she is {\i mekudeshes}; if not, she is not. Then the {\i baraita} continues to the case of {\i bezo uvezu uvezu} with eating: if she took each and ate it as it was given, she is {\i mekudeshes} only if one of them is worth a {\i perutah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Question: Ba\u8217'elu?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} asks: in the first case of the {\i baraita} \u8212- the acorn, pomegranate, and nut \u8212- what exactly is the case? If the man said \u8220"either with the acorn, or with the pomegranate, or with the nut\u8221" ({\i o be\u8217'alon o be\u8217'rimon o be\u8217'egoz}), how could it suffice for them to be collectively worth a {\i perutah}? He spoke in the disjunctive \u8212- he named one at a time. But if we read it as simply listing them together ({\i be\u8217'alon be\u8217'rimon u\u8217've\u8217'egoz}), then that is identical to {\i bezu bezu bezu} \u8212- individual labeling \u8212- not a {\i ba\u8217'elu} formulation. So in what sense is the opening case of the {\i baraita} a {\i ba\u8217'elu} case?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 First Case: No Distincti\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} resolves this: the first clause of the {\i baraita} ({\i be\u8217'alon be\u8217'rimon u\u8217've\u8217'egoz}) is simply an example of how {\i ba\u8217'elu} works \u8212- it is explaining the {\i ba\u8217'elu} case by specifying what kind of items might be included. The formulation is {\i perusha kampfaresh} \u8212- an explanatory gloss, not a distinct case. Understood this way, the {\i baraita}\u8217's structure becomes clear: first it presents the {\i ba\u8217'elu} scenario and rules that collective worth suffices; then it presents the {\i bezu} scenario with eating and rules that one individual date must be worth a {\i perutah}. The eating clause appears only in connection with the {\i bezu bezu bezu} case \u8212- not the {\i ba\u8217'elu} case \u8212- which is precisely what Rava argued.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Ami vs.\u160 ?Rav\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might ask: why was it necessary to attribute this {\i baraita} to Rava\u8217's position at all, given that the same point was made yesterday by Rav Ami? To understand this, one must recall the prior discussion. The {\i mishnah} presented two main structures: first, where each {\i kiddushin} was a separate statement \u8212- {\i hitkadshi li bezo, hitkadshi li bezo, hitkadshi li bezo} \u8212- and second, where the man said {\i bezu uvezu uvezu}, treating them as a group. The {\i mishnah} then added the case of {\i ochelet} \u8212- eating \u8212- and the question was: to which of the two structures does the eating clause belong?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Ami said that {\i ochelet} modifies the {\i bezu uvezu uvezu} case. When she eats each date as it is named individually in one continuing statement, those eaten dates become {\i milveh}-like, and only the last one counts. This is what the {\i baraita} makes explicit: {\i bezo natla ve\u8217'achaltu, bezo natla ve\u8217'achaltu} \u8212- she took and ate, she took and ate \u8212- and the conclusion is that one of them must be a {\i shaveh perutah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav and Shmuel, by contrast, held that {\i ochelet} modifies the first structure \u8212- the case of separate {\i hitkadshi li} statements. On their reading, each {\i hitkadshi li bezo} is already a complete and independent act of {\i kiddushin}, and the eating clause teaches only the {\i chiddush} that even if she derives immediate benefit by eating the date, the {\i kiddushin} still requires a {\i shaveh perutah}. But this is no real {\i chiddush}: of course if any one of the separate acts is worth a {\i perutah} it works. And indeed the {\i baraita}\u8217's language \u8212- explicitly describing eating date by date within a single continuing sequence \u8212- clearly tracks Rav Ami\u8217's reading, not Rav and Shmuel\u8217's.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Baraita: Rabbi View\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} resolves the difficulty by attributing the {\i baraita} to the position of Rebbi. Rebbi holds \u8212- in a parallel {\i sugya} concerning {\i pigul} intentions ({\i kol ke-zayit ke-zayit u-ke-zayit u-k-zayit trata ha-vei}) \u8212- that even when the conjunctive {\i vav} is used to link items, each is still treated as a separate act. It does not matter whether one says {\i ke-zayit ke-zayit} or {\i ke-zayit u-ke-zayit}; each remains independent. Analogously, according to Rebbi, even {\i bezu uvezu uvezu}, despite the connecting {\i vav}, is treated not as a collective but as a series of separate acts of {\i kiddushin}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This means that the eating clause in the {\i baraita}, though textually attached to the {\i bezu uvezu uvezu} sequence, is functioning according to Rebbi\u8217's framework where each {\i bezo} is a separate act \u8212- like the {\i hitkadshi li bezo, hitkadshi li bezo} case of Rav and Shmuel. The {\i chiddush} is then: not that one must wait for the last date, but that as long as any one of them was worth a {\i perutah} when accepted, that act of {\i kiddushin} is valid. The {\i baraita} accordingly does not require specifically the {\i last} date to be a {\i perutah}, but {\i one of them} \u8212- because each acceptance was its own independent transaction. In this way, the {\i baraita} can be reconciled with Rav and Shmuel\u8217's reading after all, provided we understand it as authored according to Rebbi\u8217's view.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Mekadesh B\u8217'Milveh\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Basic Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now turns to a sustained discussion of a fundamental question: {\i ha-mekadesh be-milveh} \u8212- can a man betroth a woman using a debt she owes him? She owes him a hundred dollars; he says, keep the money, you are {\i mekudeshes} to me. He has not given her anything new \u8212- he has merely remitted an existing obligation. One might argue that the benefit she receives from the forgiveness of the debt ({\i mechilat ha-chov}) has a real monetary value, and that value could function as {\i kesef kiddushin}. But that would be a different act entirely \u8212- he would need to say explicitly, \u8220"marry me with the value of my waiving this debt\u8221" ({\i be-hana\u8217'at mechilat milveh}). What he has actually said is, \u8220"keep the money you owe me, and with that money you are {\i mekudeshes} to me.\u8221" The question is whether that formulation is effective.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is worth noting a contrast that clarifies the issue: if instead of lending money, he had lent her an object \u8212- say, a phone \u8212- and then said \u8220"keep the phone and be {\i mekudeshes} to me with it,\u8221" that would work, because the object remains legally his throughout the loan. Money is entirely different, and that difference is the crux of the upcoming analysis.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav\u8217's Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav rules: {\i ha-mekadesh be-milveh einah mekudeshes} \u8212- one who betroths a woman with a loan does not effect valid {\i kiddushin}. The reason is the principle {\i milveh le-hotza\u8217'ah nitnah} \u8212- a loan is given to be spent. As soon as the money leaves the lender\u8217's hand, it is no longer his. The borrower owns that specific currency outright; all that remains in the lender\u8217's domain is an abstract right to repayment.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Money vs.\u160 ?Object\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i chiddush} here is the sharp distinction between money and other objects. When a person lends money \u8212- even if the borrower has not yet spent it, even if the bills are still sitting in the borrower\u8217's hand \u8212- those bills are no longer the lender\u8217's property. He cannot demand them back. He cannot give them to someone else. He has nothing concrete left: only an abstract debt owed to him. This is {\i milveh le-hotza\u8217'ah nitnah}: the money is given with the understanding that it will be spent, and legally it transitions to the borrower\u8217's ownership immediately upon receipt. By contrast, a borrowed object ({\i shoel}) remains the lender\u8217's property throughout the period of the loan \u8212- the lender can demand its return, and the object itself is still his to dispose of. If he says to the woman, \u8220"keep the object I lent you and be {\i mekudeshes} to me,\u8221" he is genuinely giving her something that was his. With money, he is not.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mechirah Contradiction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} introduces an apparent contradiction. A {\i mishnah} dealing with land purchase ({\i mechirah}) seems to indicate that {\i kiddushin be-milveh} can work. If Rav\u8217's principle is absolute \u8212- the loan money is never the lender\u8217's to give \u8212- then it should fail equally in any transactional context, whether {\i kiddushin} or sale of land. Why, then, does the {\i mishnah} in {\i mechirah} suggest otherwise?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Special Case: Short Paym\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Nachman resolves this by introducing a completely different scenario. The {\i mishnah} in question is not addressing the classic {\i mekadesh be-milveh} case that Rav ruled on. Rather, says Rav Nachman citing Rav Huna, it deals with a case of {\i maneh chaser} \u8212- a short payment. A man said to a woman, \u8220"marry me with a hundred dollars ({\i maneh}),\u8221" she took the money and counted it, and it came up five short \u8212- only ninety-five. The missing five is in the nature of a {\i milveh}: he owes her the remainder. Does the {\i kiddushin} work?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Debate: Embarrassment\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is the subject of the {\i tanna\u8217'im}\u8217's debate. One opinion holds that the {\i kiddushin} is invalid because the woman is {\i kesifa lah milta le-mitba\u8217'ei} \u8212- she is embarrassed to demand the remaining five dollars from her betrothed. Because of this social pressure, even if she said yes, she did not fully and genuinely accept the arrangement. Her consent was tainted by the fact that she felt unable to press her legitimate claim.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gmar Da\u8217'at Principle\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The other opinion holds that she is not embarrassed to ask, and therefore her acceptance reflects genuine {\i gmar da\u8217'at} \u8212- a complete and decisive resolve. The underlying principle is that {\i kiddushin} requires that the woman truly and fully accept what is being offered. If she is being placed in a position where she cannot freely assert her rights, her apparent consent lacks the necessary decisiveness. If, on the other hand, she genuinely trusts she will receive what is owed and freely agrees, the {\i kiddushin} is valid despite the shortfall.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This also explains why the case of land purchase ({\i karka}) works according to both opinions. In a commercial transaction between two parties who are not entering a marriage, neither would hesitate to sue for the outstanding amount. There is no {\i kesifa} in a business deal; the buyer will unambiguously demand what is owed. Therefore, in that context, {\i gmar da\u8217'at} is unquestionable and the transaction succeeds.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Threshold Cases\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then explores the thresholds of this principle. If the shortfall is enormous \u8212- say, ninety-nine dollars short out of a hundred \u8212- she will obviously demand the rest, since the discrepancy is so glaring that embarrassment is out of the question. In that case, {\i gmar da\u8217'at} is present and the {\i kiddushin} may work. If the shortfall is only five dollars out of a hundred, the embarrassment argument applies. These threshold cases help define when {\i kesifa} operates and when it does not.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Elazar Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now raises the case stated by Rav Elazar: a man says \u8220"marry me with a hundred dollars\u8221" ({\i hitkadshi li be-maneh}) and gives her only one dinar \u8212- a small down payment \u8212- saying he will complete the payment later. Does this work?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Elazar rules: {\i harei zu mekudeshes ve-yashlim} \u8212- she is {\i mekudeshes}, and he should complete the payment. The {\i gemara} asks: does this accord with only one side of the {\i tanna\u8217'im} debate? If giving ninety-five and owing five might not work because of {\i kesifa}, does giving only one dinar and owing the rest pose the same problem?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} answers no: the two situations are inverse in the relevant dimension. When he gave ninety-five and owed five, the shortfall is small and the woman is embarrassed to ask for it \u8212- {\i kesifa} is operative. When he gave only one dinar and owes ninety-nine, the gap is so enormous that there is absolutely no expectation that she would absorb the loss. She will obviously press for the remainder \u8212- {\i lo kesifa lah milta} \u8212- and therefore her {\i gmar da\u8217'at} in accepting the {\i kiddushin} is unimpaired. Rav Elazar\u8217's case thus sidesteps the {\i tanna\u8217'im} debate entirely and is acceptable to both opinions.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Pikadon Baraita\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now cites a {\i baraita} about {\i pikadon}: if a man says to a woman, \u8220"marry me with the entrusted object ({\i pikadon}) that I have in your possession\u8221" \u8212- say, a bag of grain she is watching for him \u8212- and she agrees, and then goes to retrieve it and discovers it was stolen or lost. If something worth a {\i perutah} remains, she is {\i mekudeshes}; if nothing remains, she is not. By contrast, continues the {\i baraita}, in the case of a {\i milveh} \u8212- a loan \u8212- even if nothing of the original money remains, she is {\i mekudeshes}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This {\i baraita} is striking, because it directly contradicts Rav\u8217's position. According to Rav, even with the full amount of the loan still in her possession, {\i kiddushin be-milveh} does not work \u8212- {\i milveh le-hotza\u8217'ah nitnah}. The {\i baraita}, however, seems to say the {\i milveh} case works even better than the {\i pikadon} case.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Baraita Positions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i baraita} thus presents two views. The first (the {\i tanna kamma}) says: {\i pikadon} requires a {\i perutah} to remain, but {\i milveh} works even with nothing left. This is the opposite of Rav\u8217's position. The second view, that of Rabbi Shimon ben Elazar in the name of Rabbi Meir, is that {\i milveh} is like {\i pikadon}: something of the original money must remain for the {\i kiddushin} to work.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Both views are, to varying degrees, in tension with Rav. One says {\i milveh} works completely (even better than {\i pikadon}); the other says it works if some money remains \u8212- still more permissive than Rav, who says it fails even when the entire amount is still in her possession.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Text Corruption\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava intervenes with a striking move: {\i hamishabeshta hi} \u8212- the text of this {\i baraita} is corrupt. He argues that the {\i baraita} as received cannot be internally coherent. His argument proceeds as follows: what is the {\i pikadon} case? If she accepted liability for the object ({\i kibla aleha achrayus}), then if it is lost or stolen she owes the man the money \u8212- making her, in effect, a {\i milveh} debtor. In that case, the {\i pikadon} scenario where nothing is left should work just as well as the {\i milveh} case, and the {\i baraita}\u8217's distinction collapses. If she did not accept liability ({\i lo kibla aleha achrayus}), then she owes him nothing when the object is lost, and indeed the {\i kiddushin} can only work if something physical remains. But in that case, why does the {\i baraita} draw the contrast to {\i milveh} at all? It should simply distinguish within the {\i pikadon} case itself: if she accepted liability, it works even without a remainder; if not, it requires a remainder. The fact that the {\i baraita} instead contrasts {\i pikadon} with {\i milveh} in the {\i sefa} reveals an internal incoherence, demonstrating that the text has been corrupted.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Corrected Baraita\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava therefore proposes a corrected reading of the {\i baraita}: {\i ela tani hachi} \u8212- read it this way instead. In the case of {\i milveh}, even if something of the original amount remains ({\i af al pi she-nishtayer heimena shaveh perutah}), she is {\i einah mekudeshes}. This is the exact reversal of the {\i baraita}\u8217's original formulation, and it aligns precisely with Rav\u8217's ruling: {\i milveh le-hotza\u8217'ah nitnah} \u8212- the loan money is entirely the borrower\u8217's from the moment it is given, and even if the bills have not yet been spent and are still physically present, the lender has no ownership claim over them and cannot use them to effect {\i kiddushin}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shimon b. Elazar\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Shimon ben Elazar, citing Rabbi Meir, maintains his counter-position: {\i milveh harei hu ke-pikadon} \u8212- a loan is like an entrusted object. If some of the original money remains, it can function as {\i kesef kiddushin}. This is a principled disagreement with the {\i tanna kamma}\u8217's position as corrected by Rava, and by extension with Rav. Rabbi Shimon ben Elazar holds that the loan money does not instantly and completely transfer to the borrower; while some of it persists in its original physical form, it retains some connection to the lender\u8217's ownership and can therefore serve as a vehicle for {\i kiddushin}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Loan Ownership Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two Views\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now examines the underlying conceptual dispute that drives the {\i mekadesh be-milveh} debate. There are two views on when exactly a loan transitions out of the lender\u8217's ownership. According to the first view \u8212- which is Rav\u8217's \u8212- the transfer is immediate: the moment the money enters the borrower\u8217's hand, it belongs to the borrower fully. {\i Milveh le-hotza\u8217'ah nitnah} means not merely that the money is destined to be spent, but that it is already no longer the lender\u8217's at the instant of transfer. According to the second view \u8212- associated with Shmuel\u8217's formulation \u8212- the money only becomes the borrower\u8217's when he actually begins to spend it ({\i hotza\u8217'ah}). Until that moment, the bills remain, in some meaningful sense, the lender\u8217's property.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Application\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If Shmuel\u8217's view is correct \u8212- that the lender retains ownership until spending begins \u8212- then {\i mekadesh be-milveh} could potentially work as long as none of the money has yet been spent. The lender would be giving the woman something that is genuinely still his: those specific bills, which have not yet crossed into the borrower\u8217's ownership by virtue of being spent. This is what Rabbi Shimon ben Elazar\u8217's position ({\i milveh ke-pikadon}) is premised on: since the money may still be the lender\u8217's property, it can serve as {\i kesef kiddushin} just as an object in a {\i pikadon} relationship can.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Retraction Rights\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava reports that he found the Rabbis of Rav\u8217's academy ({\i rabbanan de-vei Rav}) sitting and discussing this very point. They offered a different way to frame the debate: {\i milveh bi-reshut be\u8217'alim le-chazarah} \u8212- the question is whether the loan, while still in the borrower\u8217's possession unspent, remains within the lender\u8217's domain for purposes of {\i retraction} ({\i chazarah}). Can the lender say, \u8220"I changed my mind, give me back my twenty dollars\u8221"? One opinion holds that he can \u8212- the loan is still {\i bi-reshut be\u8217'alim} before being spent, and just as a lender can recall an unspent loan, he can also use those funds as {\i kesef kiddushin}. The other opinion holds that the transfer is immediate and irrevocable: from the moment of lending, the money is out of the lender\u8217's domain entirely, and he has no right of retraction.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sheol Analogy\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava challenges this framing by raising the case of {\i shoel} \u8212- one who borrows an object (not money). A {\i shoel} is fully liable ({\i chayav be-onassin}) from the moment he takes possession of the object, even though the object itself remains the lender\u8217's property throughout the loan. The {\i gemara} notes: {\i lo gara mi-shoel} \u8212- the money-borrower should certainly not be less liable than the object-borrower. Just as the {\i shoel} accepts full liability for loss and {\i oness} (force majeure) at the moment of taking possession \u8212- even though the lender could in principle demand the item\u8217's return \u8212- so too the money-borrower should be fully liable from the moment he receives the funds, regardless of whether ownership has technically transferred.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Refined Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava therefore corrects the Rabbis\u8217' framing. The debate cannot be about liability for {\i onessin} \u8212- everyone agrees the borrower is liable for any loss from the moment he receives the money. A {\i shoel} accepts liability as soon as he takes possession, even before he begins using the item, and the money-borrower is certainly no less liable. The debate, Rava clarifies, must therefore be exclusively about {\i chazarah} \u8212- retraction rights \u8212- and its implication for {\i kiddushin}: can the lender reclaim the specific bills before they are spent, thereby still owning them in a sense that allows him to give them as {\i kesef kiddushin}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Abstract vs.\u160 ?Physical\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This brings the discussion to its sharpest formulation. At what moment does the concrete, physical ownership of the specific currency dissolve into an abstract debt obligation? Everyone agrees that this transition occurs \u8212- certainly before the last coin is spent, and certainly before the obligation is discharged. The question is whether it occurs immediately upon transfer of the bills ({\i milveh le-hotza\u8217'ah nitnah}, Rav\u8217's view), or only when the borrower actually begins spending the money (Shmuel\u8217's view, and the basis for Rabbi Shimon ben Elazar\u8217's leniency). Once the money is fully abstracted into a debt \u8212- regardless of how much currency remains physically in the borrower\u8217's possession \u8212- the lender can no longer marry the woman with it, because he has nothing concrete left to give. The entire {\i sugya} of {\i mekadesh be-milveh} thus turns on this precise question of when abstract debt replaces physical ownership.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Kiddushin 48\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Milveh Le\u8217'hotza\u8217'ah Nitnah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Marrying with Milveh\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The daf opens in the middle of an extended discussion that began on 47b: can a man betroth a woman using a {\i milveh} \u8212- not by waiving a loan she owes him, but by in some sense \u8220"returning\u8221" the money she borrowed? The complication is that the money may no longer be considered his at all. This is the principle of {\i milveh le\u8217'hotza\u8217'ah nitnah}: once a lender hands over cash, that cash belongs to the borrower, who merely owes an abstract debt in return. If so, when the man says \u8220"be betrothed to me with this money,\u8221" he is attempting to betroth her with something that is already hers \u8212- and that cannot constitute {\i kesef kiddushin}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is Rav\u8217's position: {\i mekadesh bimilveh} does not work. The Gemara has been defending Rav by arguing that the principle of {\i milveh le\u8217'hotza\u8217'ah nitnah} is actually undisputed, and that the only open question is precisely {\i when} the money ceases to be the lender\u8217's \u8212- whether immediately upon transfer or only once the borrower begins to spend it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Money Becomes Hers\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara\u8217's resolution is that everyone agrees the money becomes the borrower\u8217's property once it is handed over. What the {\i tannaim} may disagree about is a narrower question: at what point is the transaction irreversible from the lender\u8217's perspective, and at what point can the money be said to have fully passed out of his domain? The practical consequence of {\i milveh le\u8217'hotza\u8217'ah nitnah} is that by the time the man attempts to use the loan as {\i kesef kiddushin}, the money is already hers. He cannot marry her with what is already in her possession.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Nafka Minas\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Beyond the question of {\i kiddushin} itself, the Gemara identifies two {\i nafka minas} \u8212- practical differences \u8212- between the position that the money passes immediately upon transfer and the position that it passes only when she begins to spend it. The first {\i nafka mina} is liability for losses: if a wind blows the money out of her hand before she has spent any of it, whose loss is it? The second {\i nafka mina} is {\i chazarah} \u8212- whether the lender can retract and demand his money back before she begins spending it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Liability vs.\u160 ?Ownership\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara argues that on the question of liability for losses, both positions would actually agree that she becomes liable immediately upon receiving the money. The reasoning is drawn from the laws of {\i she\u8217'elah}: when one borrows an object, one does not become its owner \u8212- the object remains the lender\u8217's \u8212- yet the moment it passes into the borrower\u8217's physical control, the borrower becomes a {\i sho\u8217'el} and is fully liable for any damage or loss. The same logic applies here: even if the money does not yet fully belong to her, once she has taken physical possession she bears the status of a {\i sho\u8217'el} and absorbs any losses. The genuine point of disagreement, then, is only the second {\i nafka mina}: whether the lender retains the right to retract before she begins spending the money.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 She\u8217'elah - Borrowed Hatc\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Huna\u8217's View\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This analysis leads directly to a statement of Rav Huna: {\i hasho\u8217'el kardom me\u8217'chavero \u8212- bika bo kena\u8217'o; lo bika bo \u8212- lo kena\u8217'o}. If one borrows an axe from a friend, once one begins chopping with it, one \u8220"takes possession\u8221"; if one has not yet begun chopping, one has not taken possession. The Gemara here uses the word {\i kana} \u8212- acquisition \u8212- but it is worth clarifying what this means, since the axe obviously remains the lender\u8217's property throughout. What Rav Huna means by \u8220"taking possession\u8221" is, at minimum, the point from which the owner can no longer retract \u8212- and possibly also the point of full liability, though as we will see, liability begins earlier.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The question the Gemara raises is whether Rav Huna\u8217's position represents only one side of a tannaitic debate. After all, we just established that in the context of loans, there are two views on when the lender loses the right to retract \u8212- either immediately upon handing over the money, or only once the borrower begins spending. Is Rav Huna, who says the borrower of the axe does not \u8220"take possession\u8221" until he begins using it, simply adopting one side of that debate?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Loans vs.\u160 ?Objects\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara rejects this framing: {\i ad kan lo peligi ela bemilveh} \u8212- the tannaitic debate applies only to a monetary loan, not to a borrowed object. The reason is rooted in the fundamental nature of each transaction. A monetary loan is structured precisely so that the borrower will spend the specific coins and return different ones; from the moment the lender hands over the cash, he has in some sense relinquished his hold on those particular coins. One could therefore argue that his proprietary claim dissolves immediately. A borrowed object, by contrast, must be returned in its original form \u8212- {\i dehadra be\u8217'eina}, it comes back as itself. The lender\u8217's sense of ownership over that specific object remains intact throughout the loan period. Consequently, {\i divrei hakol} \u8212- by universal agreement \u8212- the borrower of a physical object does not extinguish the lender\u8217's right to retract until the borrower actually begins using it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This also helps explain why the Gemara ruled the way it did on the monetary loan: the position that the lender loses his claim {\i immediately} upon handing over cash is grounded in the uniqueness of money, not in a general principle applicable to all forms of lending.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Immediate Liability\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot raises a complementary point. The Gemara earlier justified immediate liability for losses in the context of a monetary loan by analogy to borrowed objects \u8212- as soon as the object passes into the borrower\u8217's control, the {\i sho\u8217'el} is liable. Tosafot notes that the same logic must apply in the case of the borrowed axe: the moment it passes into the borrower\u8217's physical control, he is liable for any damages, even before he begins using it. This is in fact the source of the principle in the loan case. So in both the monetary loan and the borrowed object, liability for {\i onsin} \u8212- accidents and losses \u8212- attaches immediately upon transfer, even though the question of {\i chazarah} and full proprietary transfer may differ. The Gemara does not spell this out explicitly in this context, but Tosafot makes clear that the cases are parallel in this respect.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Tannaitic Debate?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shtar Chov Cases\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having settled the internal logic of {\i milveh le\u8217'hotza\u8217'ah nitnah} and its comparison to {\i she\u8217'elah}, the Gemara pivots to the central project of this sugya: can the ruling that {\i mekadesh bimilveh} is ineffective be shown to reflect a tannaitic debate, or is it universally agreed upon? The Gemara now attempts \u8212- in several stages \u8212- to locate a {\i machloket tannaim} that maps onto this question.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The first attempt introduces a {\i baraita} with two cases. In the first, a man betroths a woman by handing her a {\i shtar chov} \u8212- a document of debt. In the second, others owe him money and he authorizes her to collect on his behalf, thereby transferring the debt to her. Rabbi Meir holds she is betrothed ({\i mekudeshet}); the Sages hold she is not ({\i einah mekudeshet}). At first glance, particularly in the second case \u8212- where a concrete right of collection is being conferred \u8212- this looks like a genuine debate about whether a debt can function as {\i kesef kiddushin}, and thus a debate about {\i mekadesh bimilveh}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Whose Debt?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara zeroes in on the first case: what exactly is the {\i shtar chov} being handed over? If it is a document recording a debt that others owe the man \u8212- {\i shtar chov de\u8217'acheirim} \u8212- then that is simply a restatement of the second case, and the two cases collapse into one. The Gemara therefore entertains the possibility that the first case involves a {\i shtar chov deidah} \u8212- a document recording a debt that {\i she} owes {\i him}. In that case, by handing her the IOU, the man is effectively telling her to keep the money she owes him. That {\i would} be {\i mekadesh bimilveh}, and the tannaitic debate would map directly onto the question of whether such betrothal works.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara ultimately rejects this reading \u8212- it is unlikely that the {\i baraita} would use a single list to debate two logically distinct categories (her debt to him versus third parties\u8217' debt to him) without distinguishing between them. Instead, the Gemara concludes that both cases involve debts owed to the man by {\i others}, and the debate concerns entirely different sub-questions about how such a debt is transferred. The debate, in other words, is not really about {\i kiddushin} at all.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Document Transfer\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 With the {\i baraita} now read as involving only third-party debts, the Gemara asks: what exactly do Rabbi Meir and the Sages disagree about? When there is a written {\i shtar chov}, the mechanism of transfer is {\i otiyyot niknot bimesirah} \u8212- the rights embedded in a debt document are acquired by physically handing over the document. Rabbi says this is sufficient; the Sages ({\i Rabbanan}) say that mere transfer of the document is not enough \u8212- one must {\i both} write a supporting document (formally recording the new creditor) {\i and} hand over the {\i shtar}: {\i ad she\u8217'yichtov ve\u8217'yimsor}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 On this reading, the case involves a man who handed her the {\i shtar} but did not write supporting documentation. Rabbi Meir, following the opinion of {\i Rabbi} (Rebbi Yehuda HaNasi) that handing over the document suffices, holds she is {\i mekudeshet}. The Sages, who require both acts, hold she is not. This is a debate about the mechanics of {\i kinyan} in document transfers \u8212- not about {\i kiddushin}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Pappa\u8217's Line\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A second resolution is offered: perhaps everyone rejects Rabbi\u8217's position and agrees that both handing over and writing are required. On this reading, the man performed {\i both} acts \u8212- he handed her the document {\i and} wrote supporting documentation \u8212- but the question is whether the supporting documentation contained the precise formula required by Rav Pappa. For Rav Pappa rules: {\i hai man de\u8217'zavin shtar le\u8217'chavrei tzarich le\u8217'michtav lei \u8212- knei lach ihu ve\u8217'chol shibudei} \u8212- when transferring a debt document, the supporting text must explicitly state \u8220"take possession of it and all its liens.\u8221" One side of the debate holds that this precise formulation is indispensable; the other holds it is not. Once again, the dispute has nothing to do with the law of {\i kiddushin}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shmuel\u8217's Mechilah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A third resolution involves an entirely different dimension of the problem. Assume everyone agrees with Rav Pappa that the required formula was written, and that the transfer was fully executed. The Gemara now invokes Shmuel: {\i ha\u8217'mocher shtar chov le\u8217'chaveiro ve\u8217'chazar u\u8217'machalo \u8212- machul}. If one sells a debt document to another person, and the original creditor subsequently forgives the debt \u8212- {\i mochel} it \u8212- the forgiveness is legally effective, and the purchaser can no longer collect. The reasoning, at its core, is that a debt is fundamentally a personal obligation between debtor and original creditor. What passes to the buyer is not the debt itself in some fully abstracted sense, but rather the right to step into the creditor\u8217's position \u8212- perhaps as a kind of agent or substitute. Since the original creditor\u8217's claim never fully dissolves, he retains the power to be {\i mochel}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot offers a related but differently framed explanation: {\i kinyan shtarot lav mi\u8217'de\u8217'orayta} \u8212- the transfer of debt documents has no biblical basis, only rabbinic. Therefore the rabbinic mechanism of transfer is not strong enough to fully uproot the original creditor\u8217's power ({\i lo alim le\u8217'afkuei koach harishon}). The conceptual formulation offered in the shiur \u8212- that the debtor fundamentally still owes the original creditor, and the buyer merely acquires some right to collect through or in place of that creditor \u8212- captures the same insight.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 How is Shmuel\u8217's rule relevant to {\i kiddushin}? Even if the woman receives a fully transferred {\i shtar chov} of real market value, the original creditor retains the power to forgive the debt and leave her with nothing. This affects the {\i quality} of what she receives as {\i kesef kiddushin}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Samchat Da\u8217'ata\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This leads to the concept of {\i samchat da\u8217'ata} \u8212- settled confidence in the object of betrothal. The Gemara raises the possibility that even though the transferred debt has genuine market value (discounted, of course, for the risk of {\i mechilah}), the woman\u8217's lack of confidence in her ability to collect may itself be a disqualifying factor for {\i kesef kiddushin}. This is a striking argument: that something with real monetary worth, which could be sold on the open market for a price reflecting its discounted value, might nevertheless fail to operate as {\i kesef kiddushin} because of an anxiety about its ultimate collectibility.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara presents two levels of debate. The first is simply whether one holds like Shmuel at all \u8212- whether the original creditor genuinely retains the power to forgive. If not, the transferred debt is fully hers and {\i samchat da\u8217'ata} is unproblematic. The second level, introduced as an alternative framing, accepts that everyone holds like Shmuel, and asks instead whether the specific context of betrothal is different. Here the Gemara distinguishes between selling a field with a transferred debt \u8212- where no one, presumably, would feel confident, since the seller might turn around and forgive the debt to benefit the debtor at her expense \u8212- and the case of betrothal. The argument for {\i samchat da\u8217'ata} in betrothal is romantic but legally grounded: {\i meimar amra \u8212- lo shavik leididi u\u8217'machil le\u8217'achrinei} \u8212- she reasons that he would not deprive her, his new wife, by doing a favor for a third party. One opinion holds that this confidence is real and legally operative; the other holds that even in betrothal, the woman cannot be assumed to feel truly secure, and therefore the anxiety remains. Either way, the underlying debate is not about {\i kiddushin} per se but about the transferability of debt instruments and the reliability of {\i mechilah} \u8212- and the tannaitic {\i machloket} has not yet been established as a debate about {\i mekadesh bimilveh}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Oral Loans Transfer\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ma\u8217'amad Shloshtan\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara turns to the second case in the {\i baraita}: the oral loan ({\i milveh al peh}) \u8212- a debt owed by a third party that is not recorded in any document. Here even the mechanism of {\i otiyyot bimesirah} is unavailable. The Gemara invokes Rav Huna in the name of Rav: {\i maneh li be\u8217'yadcha \u8212- tenahu li\u8217'ploni bema\u8217'amad shloshtan} \u8212- if all three parties are present \u8212- the original creditor, the debtor, and the intended recipient \u8212- and the creditor says \u8220"that hundred you owe me, give it to so-and-so,\u8221" the recipient ({\i koneh}) acquires the debt. This is a special form of {\i kinyan} \u8212- {\i ma\u8217'amad shloshtan} \u8212- enacted by the Sages for precisely this situation. The Gemara further notes, drawing on Tosafot\u8217's analysis, that this mechanism has an important advantage over the written transfer: Shmuel\u8217's rule of {\i mechilah} may not apply. When all three stand together and the creditor explicitly steps out of the picture, the new recipient is not merely a collector on behalf of the original creditor \u8212- the original creditor has, in some conceptual sense, fully substituted the recipient into his own position. He is out; the new party is in. Accordingly, the original creditor may no longer be {\i mochel} the debt.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Oral vs.\u160 ?Physical\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If {\i ma\u8217'amad shloshtan} is a fully effective transfer, why would it not work as {\i kesef kiddushin}? The debate, the Gemara says, is whether one accepts Rav Huna\u8217's ruling at all \u8212- and, if one does, whether it applies to oral debts as well as to objects in safekeeping ({\i pikadon}). One view holds that {\i ma\u8217'amad shloshtan} works only for a {\i pikadon} \u8212- a physical object being held \u8212- because it is easier to conceptualize the transfer of a physical thing when all parties are present and in agreement. A monetary debt, being an abstract obligation, cannot be transferred by this mechanism. The other view holds that there is no distinction: {\i lo shna milveh velo shna pikadon} \u8212- whether it is a debt or a physical deposit, {\i ma\u8217'amad shloshtan} works, and therefore she can be betrothed with it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Once again, what appears to be a debate about betrothal is in reality a debate about the laws of transfer. The Gemara has now exhausted the {\i baraita}\u8217's two cases \u8212- the {\i shtar chov} and the {\i milveh al peh} \u8212- without finding a genuine tannaitic {\i machloket} about {\i mekadesh bimilveh} itself.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Shtar Kiddushin\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Document Witnesses\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara regroups and tries a different {\i baraita}. The case now involves {\i shtar kiddushin} \u8212- betrothal by document \u8212- specifically the scenario of {\i hiskadshi li beshtar}: a man says to a woman, \u8220"be betrothed to me with this document.\u8221" The document is not a {\i shtar chov} at all; rather, it is the classic {\i shtar kiddushin}, the written declaration {\i harei at mekudeshet li}. The {\i baraita} records a three-way dispute. Rabbi Meir says she is not betrothed; Rabbi Eliezer says she is betrothed; the Sages evaluate the monetary worth of the paper itself \u8212- if the paper is worth a {\i perutah}, she is {\i mekudeshet}; if not, she is not.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara asks: what exactly is the {\i shtar} in this case? If it is a {\i shtar chov} of money others owe him, there is an immediate contradiction: in the earlier {\i baraita}, Rabbi Meir said she {\i is} betrothed; here Rabbi Meir says she is {\i not}. The positions would be reversed. Therefore, the Gemara concludes, the case here cannot be a third-party debt document. It must be something else \u8212- and that opens the door to Rav Nachman bar Yitzchak\u8217's reading.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Witness Requirements\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Nachman bar Yitzchak proposes: {\i keivan shekidsha beshtar she\u8217'ein lo eidim} \u8212- the case is where the man betrothed her with a {\i shtar kiddushin} that has no signed witnesses. This is a classic debate between Rabbi Meir and Rabbi Eliezer \u8212- not about loans, but about the law of {\i shtarot}. Rabbi Meir holds {\i edei chasimah karsi} \u8212- the signed witnesses on a document are what give it legal force; without their signatures, it is not a valid {\i shtar}. Rabbi Eliezer holds {\i edei mesirah karsi} \u8212- it is sufficient that witnesses observed the handing over of the document; signatures are not required.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 On this reading, Rabbi Meir says she is not betrothed because the unsigned {\i shtar} has no legal standing. Rabbi Eliezer says she is betrothed because the witnesses were present at the moment of delivery. The debate is the identical {\i machloket} these two {\i tannaim} conduct throughout the laws of {\i gittin} and {\i shtarot} \u8212- it has been transplanted here into {\i kiddushin}, but it is not a debate unique to betrothal.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Paper\u8217's Value\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Sages\u8217' position \u8212- evaluate the paper \u8212- requires explanation. Rashi notes a difficulty: if the Sages are uncertain whether to follow Rabbi Meir or Rabbi Eliezer, then without a {\i perutah}\u8217's worth of paper, she should be {\i safek mekudeshet} (doubtfully betrothed), not definitively {\i einah mekudeshet}. Rashi therefore emends the conclusion: with a {\i perutah}-worthy paper she is {\i vadai mekudeshet}; without it, the matter is unresolved.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A further question arises: why would the Sages evaluate the paper at all, given that the man was not attempting to betroth her with the value of the paper \u8212- he was attempting {\i kiddushei shtar}? The answer appears to be that because there is a real possibility that the {\i shtar} worked as a genuine {\i shtar kiddushin} (following Rabbi Eliezer), and because the paper itself has monetary value, the Sages treat it as potentially doubling as {\i kiddushei kesef}. If the {\i shtar kiddushin} route was possibly effective, then the paper\u8217's monetary value provides an additional basis for betrothal. But if the {\i shtar} was definitively invalid and the paper was worth less than a {\i perutah}, there would be no betrothal on either ground.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shelo Lishmah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara offers an alternative framework for the same dispute. Setting aside the witness question, the debate may instead concern {\i lishmah} \u8212- whether a {\i shtar kiddushin} must be written specifically with {\i this} woman in mind. The scenario: a man writes out a {\i harei at mekudeshet li} document in advance, before identifying his intended bride, and later produces it on a date. Is such a document valid?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This question was raised by Reish Lakish.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Lishmah Required\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Reish Lakish formulated the question as a {\i beia}: does the principle of {\i havayah liyetzi\u8217'ah makishna} \u8212- comparing betrothal to divorce \u8212- require that a {\i shtar kiddushin} be written {\i lishmah}, just as a {\i get} must be written {\i lishmah} (derived from {\i ve\u8217'chasav lah}, \u8220"and he shall write for her\u8221")? Or does one instead compare one form of {\i kiddushin} to another \u8212- {\i havayah le\u8217'hadadi makishna} \u8212- in which case, since {\i kiddushei kesef} (monetary betrothal) requires no {\i lishmah} (money is not minted with a specific woman in mind), {\i kiddushei shtar} similarly requires none?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara reports that after posing the question, Reish Lakish himself resolved it: {\i veyatze\u8217'ah ve\u8217'hayetah \u8212- makish havayah liyetzi\u8217'ah} \u8212- the comparison to divorce is the operative one, and {\i lishmah} is required for a {\i shtar kiddushin}. The tannaitic debate in the {\i baraita} would then be between one who accepts Reish Lakish\u8217's conclusion and one who does not. Rabbi Meir, requiring {\i lishmah}, rules she is not betrothed when the document was written {\i shelo lishmah}. The other opinion, presumably Rabbi Eliezer, does not require {\i lishmah} and holds she is betrothed. This reading has an additional advantage: it preserves the exact same Rabbi Meir / Rabbi Eliezer pairing as the previous explanation, lending it structural coherence.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Lishmah Lo Mida\u8217'atah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara pushes further. What if everyone agrees with Reish Lakish that {\i lishmah} is required, and the document {\i was} written with this specific woman in mind \u8212- but without her knowledge or awareness? This is the case of {\i lishmah ve\u8217'shelo mida\u8217'atah}: he writes {\i harei at mekudeshet li} while thinking of her specifically, perhaps months before proposing, without her having any idea.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Would this satisfy the {\i lishmah} requirement? The question has conceptual depth. A {\i get} need not be written with the wife\u8217's knowledge \u8212- divorce is a unilateral act and she is entirely passive. But {\i kiddushin} is different: she must consent, and she is, in some sense, a party to the transaction. Moreover, there is a subtle structural point: in a standard sale, the {\i shtar} is written by the {\i seller}. In betrothal, the man writes {\i harei at mekudeshet li} \u8212- yet it is the woman who is in the position of the seller (she is \u8220"selling\u8221" herself into the marital bond). Since she is the functional {\i ba\u8217'al hashtar}, one could argue that the document must emerge with her awareness, if not by her hand.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Amoraim Dispute\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This question is the subject of a dispute among {\i Amoraim}: Rabbah and Ravina hold she is {\i mekudeshet} \u8212- the document is valid even without her awareness. Rav Papa and Rav Sharvya hold she is {\i einah mekudeshet} \u8212- the lack of her awareness is disqualifying. The tannaitic {\i baraita} may reflect this very debate, with Rabbi Meir and the Chachamim divided on whether the woman\u8217's awareness at the time of writing is a precondition for a valid {\i shtar kiddushin}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 In any case, the Gemara has now demonstrated that the tannaitic debate in the {\i baraita} is about the internal requirements of {\i kiddushei shtar} \u8212- witness signatures, {\i lishmah}, or the woman\u8217's awareness \u8212- and not about {\i mekadesh bimilveh}. The attempt to locate {\i mekadesh bimilveh} in a tannaitic dispute has again come up empty.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Labor as Kiddushin\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Jewelry Service\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara makes one final attempt to root {\i mekadesh bimilveh} in a tannaitic debate, this time using a case involving labor. A woman approaches a man who is a jeweler and says: fashion for me earrings, rings, and bracelets from my gold and silver \u8212- {\i ve\u8217'ekadesh ani lach} \u8212- and in exchange for your service, I will be betrothed to you. He performs the work and hands the finished jewelry back to her. Has he given her anything? The answer requires careful analysis. Her gold is still her gold \u8212- it was never his. The increase in value that his workmanship produced belongs, in the ordinary case, to the owner of the raw material. All that he has rendered is his labor. The question is how to characterize the legal relationship between that labor and the proposed betrothal.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 When Debt Incurs\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A three-way structure emerges in the {\i baraita}. Rabbi Meir says: {\i keivan she\u8217'asah \u8212- mekudeshet} \u8212- once he completes the work and presumably hands over the jewelry, she is betrothed. The Sages say she is not betrothed {\i until money reaches her hands} ({\i ad sheyagia mamon leyadah}). The Gemara immediately interrogates what \u8220"money\u8221" refers to. If it means the jewelry itself \u8212- the physical objects now completed and returned \u8212- then does Rabbi Meir\u8217's position imply she is betrothed even {\i before} the jewelry is handed back? That would be startling: what exactly has she received at that earlier moment that could serve as {\i kesef kiddushin}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Schirus Timing\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara\u8217's resolution introduces a crucial conceptual distinction: {\i yeshnah li\u8217'skhirus mitechilah ve\u8217'ad sof} versus {\i einah li\u8217'skhirus ella besof}. Does the wage debt accrue continuously from the very beginning of the work \u8212- so that by the time the job is done, what exists is a loan (the accumulated debt for services already rendered)? Or does no debt come into being at all until the work is completed and handed over \u8212- in which case, at the moment of completion, the craftsman is giving her the {\i service itself} rather than forgiving a debt?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is the pivot on which the entire case turns. If the debt was accruing all along ({\i yeshnah mitechilah ve\u8217'ad sof}), then at the moment of completion, the only thing the man can do is forgive the debt she owes him \u8212- which is {\i mekadesh bimilveh}, and which everyone agrees does not work. If the debt arises only at the end ({\i einah ella besof}), then at the moment of completion the man is giving her his service as a gift \u8212- not forgiving a pre-existing debt but providing something new and concrete \u8212- and {\i that} could work as {\i kesef kiddushin}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 On this reading, Rabbi Meir holds {\i einah li\u8217'skhirus ella besof}: the service is given at the end and is a genuine gift of value. The Sages hold {\i yeshnah mitechilah ve\u8217'ad sof}: by the time he is done, it has always been a debt, and {\i mekadesh bimilveh einah mekudeshet}. The tannaitic debate, then, is not about {\i mekadesh bimilveh} per se \u8212- everyone may agree that does not work \u8212- but about when the wage obligation crystallizes.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Artisan\u8217's Share\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara offers yet another reading. Assume everyone holds {\i yeshnah li\u8217'skhirus mitechilah ve\u8217'ad sof} \u8212- the debt accrues from the beginning \u8212- and assume everyone agrees that {\i mekadesh bimilveh} is invalid. The remaining debate concerns {\i uman koneh be\u8217'shvach keli}: does the craftsman acquire a proprietary share in the enhanced object by virtue of his labor?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This principle holds that when an artisan improves an object and is being paid for the job rather than by the hour, he does not merely become a creditor of the owner. Instead, he acquires a partial ownership stake in the improved object proportional to the enhancement his work produced. He must then, as it were, \u8220"sell\u8221" that stake back to the owner upon completion. If {\i uman koneh be\u8217'shvach keli} applies, then what the jeweler transfers to the woman at the end of the job is not simply a forgiven debt but a tangible proprietary interest \u8212- his partial ownership in the finished ring \u8212- and {\i that} is a real and concrete object of value that can serve as {\i kesef kiddushin}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Meir holds {\i uman koneh be\u8217'shvach keli}: the artisan owns a share in the ring, and what he gives her is that ownership stake \u8212- genuine {\i kesef kiddushin}. The Sages hold {\i eino uman koneh be\u8217'shvach keli}: he owns nothing in the ring, all he has is money owed to him, and giving it up is {\i mekadesh bimilveh}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Milveh U\u8217'Perutah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara adds one further layer. Assume everyone agrees {\i uman koneh be\u8217'shvach keli} does not apply, and {\i yeshnah li\u8217'skhirus mitechilah ve\u8217'ad sof} does apply, so the artisan has only a monetary debt and cannot betroth her with it. Nevertheless: what if, out of affection, he added a small precious stone of his own \u8212- something not contracted for, not part of the original agreement? Can he betroth her with that addition?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is the case of {\i milveh u\u8217'perutah}: a large loan being forgiven (which cannot work) combined with a small concrete addition of actual value (which potentially could). The debate is whether {\i da\u8217'atei aperutah} \u8212- his intention, and halachic characterization, is focused on the concrete {\i perutah}-sized addition \u8212- or whether {\i da\u8217'atei amilveh} \u8212- the dominant element is the loan he is forgiving, and the small addition is legally trivial, swept up in and overshadowed by the invalid larger transaction. This parallels, though does not perfectly replicate, the earlier question of whether the paper in a {\i shtar kiddushin} can function as {\i kesef kiddushin} when the man\u8217's primary intent was betrothal by document.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Past/Future Labor\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The tannaitic parallel is now made explicit. A {\i baraita} records: {\i bischar she\u8217'asisi imach \u8212- einah mekudeshet}: \u8220"with the wages you owe me for labor I have already performed\u8221" \u8212- she is not betrothed. This is straightforwardly {\i mekadesh bimilveh}. {\i Bischar she\u8217'e\u8217'eseh imach \u8212- mekudeshet}: \u8220"with the wages you will owe me for labor I will perform\u8221" \u8212- she is betrothed, according to the {\i Tanna Kamma}. This corresponds to the view that {\i einah li\u8217'skhirus ella besof} \u8212- no debt exists yet, and the service to be rendered is a genuine future gift.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Natan disagrees: even {\i bischar she\u8217'e\u8217'eseh imach} does not work \u8212- once performed, it will always have been a debt. Rabbi Yehuda Nasi goes further and rules that {\i both} formulations fail, whether past or future labor. However, Rabbi Yehuda Nasi adds: {\i ve\u8217'im hosif lah nofach mishelo \u8212- mekudeshet} \u8212- if he added something of his own, she is betrothed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara maps out the internal structure. Between the {\i Tanna Kamma} and Rabbi Natan, the debate is {\i yeshnah li\u8217'skhirus mitechilah ve\u8217'ad sof} versus {\i einah ella besof}. Between Rabbi Natan and Rabbi Yehuda Nasi, the debate is {\i milveh u\u8217'perutah}: Rabbi Natan holds {\i da\u8217'atei amilveh} even when a {\i perutah} is added; Rabbi Yehuda Nasi holds {\i da\u8217'atei aperutah} and the small addition rescues the transaction.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Final Ruling\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 In each stage of the {\i baraita}, the Gemara works to show that {\i nobody} is actually debating {\i mekadesh bimilveh} on its own terms. The {\i Tanna Kamma} who permits future-labor betrothal is not saying {\i mekadesh bimilveh} works \u8212- he is saying no debt existed yet, so this is not {\i milveh} at all. Rabbi Yehuda Nasi who permits when a {\i nofach} is added is not saying {\i mekadesh bimilveh} works \u8212- he is saying the small concrete addition is what effects the {\i kiddushin}. The Gemara thus arrives at its conclusion: {\i mekadesh bimilveh einah mekudeshet} \u8212- a man cannot betroth a woman with an existing monetary debt she owes him. This ruling is undisputed. All of the tannaitic debates that appeared to bear on the question turn out, upon careful analysis, to concern something else entirely \u8212- and Rav\u8217's position stands as universally accepted {\i halacha}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Kiddushin 49\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Object Misrepresentation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishnah Overview\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The daf begins in the middle of 48b, continuing the discussion of topics relating to the act of {\i kiddushin}. The {\i Mishnah} presents a series of cases in which the object or condition of {\i kiddushin} turns out to be different from what was specified. The {\i Mishnah} says: {\i hiskadshi li be-cos ze shel yayin ve-nimtza shel devash} \u8212- if a man says to a woman, \u8220"Marry me with this goblet of wine,\u8221" and it turns out to be filled with honey; or conversely, he said honey and it turned out to be wine. Similarly, {\i bedinar ze shel kesef ve-nimtza shel zahav} \u8212- with this silver {\i dinar} and it is found to be gold; or he said gold and it was found to be silver. And finally, {\i al menat she-ani ashir ve-nimtza ani} \u8212- on the condition that I am wealthy and he turns out to be poor; or he said poor and turns out to be wealthy. In all of these cases, the {\i Mishnah} rules {\i eina mekudeshet} \u8212- the {\i kiddushin} is not valid.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This applies even in the cases where, by any objective measure, the woman received something better than what was promised \u8212- gold instead of silver, a wealthy husband instead of a poor one. The reasoning is that the condition was not met, regardless of whether the deviation was for the better or for the worse.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Conditions vs.\u160 ?Objects\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Mishnah} presents two analytically distinct categories of misrepresentation. The first involves the object given to effect the {\i kiddushin} \u8212- the goblet or the {\i dinar} \u8212- where the specific item turned out to be different from what was named. The second involves the {\i al menat} formulation, an explicit conditional stipulation about the man\u8217's personal status or circumstances. When a man says {\i al menat she-ani ashir}, \u8220"on the condition that I am wealthy,\u8221" and it emerges that he is not, that is more obviously problematic: he stated a condition, and the condition was not satisfied. Yet the {\i Mishnah} treats the object cases similarly. Even when the woman received something of greater value than what was specified, the {\i kiddushin} is invalid because it was different from what was stated.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Shimon: Leshevach\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Shimon disputes this ruling in cases where the deviation is {\i leshevach} \u8212- an improvement. {\i Rabbi Shimon omer: im hita\u8217'a leshevach, mekudeshet} \u8212- if she was misled and it turned out to be better, the {\i kiddushin} is valid. This applies both to the object of {\i kiddushin} (the coin or goblet) and to the personal condition ({\i al menat}). The {\i Gemara} will now examine each category in turn.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Goblet Definition\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Three Baraitot\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Before addressing the substantive debate, the {\i Gemara} first establishes what is included when one says \u8220"with this goblet.\u8221" Three {\i baraitot} are cited, and they appear to contradict one another. The first holds {\i bo u-ma she-besocho} \u8212- the goblet and what is inside it. The second holds {\i bo ve-lo ma she-besocho} \u8212- the goblet alone, not its contents. The third holds {\i be-ma she-besocho ve-lo bo} \u8212- the contents alone, not the goblet itself. The {\i Gemara} resolves this by saying the three {\i baraitot} are not in dispute; they address three different circumstances: {\i ha be-maya, ha be-chamra, ha be-tziara} \u8212- one refers to water, one to wine, and one to fish brine or a similar preserved substance.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rashi\u8217's Cases\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rashi explains the three cases as follows. If someone says \u8220"this goblet of water,\u8221" the water is entirely inconsequential \u8212- no one gives a gift of water. The focus must therefore be on the goblet itself. If someone says \u8220"this goblet of wine,\u8221" the wine is meaningful, but Rashi notes that in common usage people say \u8220"here is a glass of wine,\u8221" and the recipient drinks the wine and returns the glass. Just as one who offers a guest a glass of wine does not intend to give away the vessel, so too here \u8212- the gift is the wine, not the goblet. If, however, someone says \u8220"this jar of fish brine\u8221" ({\i tziara}), the analogy shifts: one who gives a jar of preserves does not expect the recipient to empty it and return the container. The item comes together with what it is stored in, and therefore in that case the reference is to both.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Wine/Vinegar Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gemara\u8217's Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now focuses on Rabbi Shimon\u8217's position regarding the object of {\i kiddushin} and raises a pointed question. {\i Ve-lait lei le-Rabbi Shimon yayin ve-nimtza chametz, chametz ve-nimtza yayin, shneihem yecholim lachzor bahem?} \u8212- Does Rabbi Shimon not hold that if someone goes to a storekeeper and asks to purchase wine and receives vinegar, or asks for vinegar and receives wine, either party may retract? This is a standard principle: even if one receives something of greater value than what was requested \u8212- wine instead of vinegar, which is cheaper \u8212- either party can undo the transaction, because the buyer\u8217's desire was for a specific item, not simply for maximum value. Someone might need vinegar for a recipe and have no use for wine. The item\u8217's identity matters independently of its monetary worth.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If that is true, asks the {\i Gemara}, then even according to Rabbi Shimon, if a man says \u8220"marry me with this silver {\i dinar}\u8221" and it turns out to be gold, she should not be {\i mekudeshet} \u8212- because perhaps she wanted silver specifically. The {\i Gemara} thus presses: {\i ika de-nicha lei be-chaspa ve-lo nicha lei be-dahava} \u8212- there are those who prefer silver and not gold, just as some prefer vinegar and not wine. How then can Rabbi Shimon validate the {\i kiddushin} when the object is different from what was stated, even if the substitute is of greater worth?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafot Distinction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} offers a distinction. When it comes to objects, there is a genuine basis for preferring one type over another regardless of value \u8212- a craftsman making jewelry might specifically want silver, not gold. The case of money as a pure monetary instrument \u8212- where the only consideration is value \u8212- is different. If, for instance, a man said \u8220"marry me with a check for one hundred {\i dinars}\u8221" and gave a check for two hundred, that would obviously be valid even according to the {\i Rabbanan}, since there is no reason to prefer less money over more. The debate in the {\i Mishnah} arises precisely because the specific material of the coin \u8212- silver versus gold \u8212- may carry independent significance beyond mere monetary value, just as wine and vinegar do.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} nevertheless presses this question conceptually, observing that even Rabbi Shimon should be troubled by the parallel to the storekeeper case. There is something slightly counterintuitive in the {\i Gemara\u8217's} assumption that a woman accepting {\i kiddushin} is analogous to a buyer at a shop \u8212- she is not acquiring the object for its own sake in the way a customer purchases vinegar for a recipe. Yet the {\i Gemara} frames the two cases as structurally parallel, and that framing drives the subsequent analysis.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Abaye\u8217's Shaliach\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Woman\u8217's Shaliach\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} cites Rav Shimi bar Ashi, who reports that he found Abaye explaining this {\i Mishnah} and Rabbi Shimon\u8217's position to his son. Abaye\u8217's resolution is as follows. The case in the {\i Mishnah} is not one in which the man handed an object directly to the woman and it turned out to be different from what he stated. In that straightforward scenario, all would agree that the {\i kiddushin} is invalid \u8212- even Rabbi Shimon \u8212- because there is no basis to assume she would accept something other than what was specified. Rather, the {\i Mishnah} is dealing with a case in which the man appointed a {\i shaliach}: {\i amar lei le-shlucho, haveini dinar shel kesef, ve-lech ve-kadesh li et isha plonit} \u8212- \u8220"bring me a silver {\i dinar} and go betroth this woman on my behalf.\u8221" The {\i shaliach} then went and used a gold {\i dinar} instead. In that scenario, the woman received exactly what she received \u8212- she had full {\i da\u8217'at}. The question is whether the man\u8217's original instruction to the {\i shaliach} is understood as a precise specification ({\i kefida}) or merely as a general direction ({\i mareh makom}).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Makpid vs.\u160 ?Mareh Makom\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The debate between the {\i Rabbanan} and Rabbi Shimon is framed as follows: {\i mar savar kefida, u-mar savar mareh makom hu lo} \u8212- one holds that when the man specified silver, he was {\i makpid}, exacting, and therefore the {\i shaliach} exceeded his authority by substituting gold; the other holds that specifying silver was merely {\i mareh makom} \u8212- showing the {\i shaliach} the direction, indicating that silver is sufficient, while not excluding the possibility of giving something more. According to the view of {\i mareh makom}, since the {\i shaliach} gave more than the minimum required, the {\i kiddushin} stands.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Language Objection\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} objects that this reading cannot be correct. If the scenario involves the man\u8217's {\i shaliach}, the {\i Mishnah} should have used the language {\i hiskadshi lo} \u8212- \u8220"be married to him\u8221" \u8212- referring to the man who sent the {\i shaliach}. The {\i Mishnah} instead uses {\i hiskadshi li} \u8212- \u8220"marry me\u8221" \u8212- which is the language of the man speaking directly. Moreover, if it says \u8220"it turned out to be gold\u8221" ({\i nimtza shel zahav}), this implies it was always gold and never misrepresented \u8212- the {\i shaliach} simply chose to use gold. These linguistic difficulties show that Abaye\u8217's reading cannot be the correct interpretation of the {\i Mishnah\u8217's} language.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rava\u8217's Reading\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Woman Appoints\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava, together with Rabbi Hiyya bar Abbin \u8212- whom the {\i Gemara} identifies as the {\i arya she-bachevraya}, the lion of the group \u8212- offers an alternative reading. {\i Hacha be-mai askinan:} the scenario involves a {\i shaliach} appointed not by the man but by the woman. The man had previously approached the woman and said, \u8220"Marry me with a silver {\i dinar}.\u8221" She told him she needed to think about it. She then appointed a {\i shaliach} and said to him: {\i tzei ve-kabel li kiddushin mi-ploni she-amar li hiskadshi li be-dinar shel kesef} \u8212- \u8220"Go and accept {\i kiddushin} on my behalf from that man who told me to be betrothed to him with a silver {\i dinar}.\u8221" The {\i shaliach} went, and the man gave him a gold {\i dinar} instead. The {\i nimtza} \u8212- \u8220"it turned out to be\u8221" \u8212- refers to the wrapping ({\i d\u8217'katzir be-vavlisa}): the coin was wrapped up, and when it was unwrapped it was discovered to be gold.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Identification Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This reading preserves the {\i Mishnah\u8217's} language \u8212- the woman appointed the {\i shaliach}, and the language {\i hiskadshi li} reflects the man\u8217's original words that she is quoting to the {\i shaliach}. The debate between the {\i Rabbanan} and Rabbi Shimon now concerns the woman\u8217's intent when she gave instructions to her {\i shaliach}. Did she mean that the {\i shaliach} must accept specifically a silver {\i dinar} ({\i kefida})? Or was she simply identifying which man she was referring to \u8212- \u8220"the man who offered me a silver {\i dinar}\u8221" being her way of pointing out the specific individual ({\i mareh makom})? If the latter, then the silver was merely her way of directing the {\i shaliach} to the right man, and if that man chose to give gold, there is no reason to think she would object. Just as one might say \u8220"the man in the purple suit said he would betroth me\u8221" \u8212- and if the {\i shaliach} finds him in a different suit, that is obviously immaterial \u8212- perhaps mentioning silver was similarly just a way of identifying who was being discussed. Rabbi Shimon holds this reading; the {\i Rabbanan} hold that she was {\i makpid} on the silver itself.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might ask why her {\i da\u8217'at} should be more flexible simply because a {\i shaliach} was involved. The {\i Gemara} does not fully resolve this, but the implication seems to be that when a person empowers a {\i shaliach} to act in a matter, there is a degree of latitude granted that would not exist in a direct transaction. The specific designation given to the {\i shaliach} may function more as identification than as a binding specification.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Three Tannaim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Abaye Reports\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye reports that three {\i Tanna\u8217'im} share the principle of {\i mareh makom} \u8212- that when one gives instructions to a {\i shaliach}, the specification may be understood as pointing a general direction rather than imposing a strict requirement. These three are Rabban Shimon, Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel, and Rabbi Elazar. The concept of {\i mareh makom} is not invented for our {\i Mishnah}; rather, the {\i Gemara} borrows it from a recognized dispute about the latitude of a {\i shaliach}. The introduction of this language into the analysis of our {\i Mishnah} signals that the {\i Gemara} understands it to be an application of this broader principle.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Get Analogy\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel\u8217's position emerges from a {\i Mishnah} dealing with {\i gittin}. There are two types of {\i get}: a {\i get pashut}, which is a flat document with the witnesses signing at the bottom, and a {\i get mukushar}, which is a folded and sewn document, traditionally associated with the special concern for a {\i kohen} who cannot remarry a divorc\u233 ?e and therefore should not divorce hastily. To slow down the process, the {\i get mukushar} required writing on every other line, folding the blank line over the written line and sewing it shut, repeating this for each line \u8212- a laborious process \u8212- with the witnesses signing on the back of those folds. The {\i Mishnah} in {\i Gittin} establishes that a {\i pashut get} has witnesses signing inside (at the bottom) and a {\i mukushar get} has witnesses signing outside (on the back). If these are switched \u8212- a {\i pashut} with witnesses on the back, or a {\i mukushar} with witnesses inside \u8212- both are invalid according to Rabbi Hanina. Rabbi Hanina ben Gamliel says a {\i mukushar} with witnesses inside is valid because it can simply not be sewn, and then it would function as a {\i pashut}. Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel says {\i hakol ke-minhag ha-medina} \u8212- everything follows the practice of the region.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Get Decision\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} asks: does this principle not say anything new? We know the proper form for each type. Rav Ashi clarifies: the statement {\i hakol ke-minhag ha-medina} applies in a specific case \u8212- where a man instructed a {\i shaliach} to write one type of {\i get}, and the {\i shaliach} wrote the other. The debate between {\i kefida} and {\i mareh makom} applies only when both types of {\i get} are in common use in that locale. If the region uses only one type and the {\i shaliach} wrote the other, all agree this is {\i kefida} and the {\i shaliach} exceeded his authority. {\i Ki pligi} \u8212- the dispute arises when both {\i pashut} and {\i mukushar} are accepted in that place \u8212- {\i be-atra d\u8217'nahigi bein be-pashut bein be-mukushar} \u8212- and the man said to write a {\i pashut}, and the {\i shaliach} wrote a {\i mukushar}. One opinion ({\i kefida}) holds the {\i shaliach} deviated from the instruction; Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel\u8217's view ({\i mareh makom}) holds that since both are locally acceptable, the man was merely pointing to the simpler option, and if the {\i shaliach} chose the more involved form, he acted within the scope of the mandate.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Shaliach Conclusion\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Limits of Doctrine\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} establishes that the principle of {\i mareh makom} applies specifically when the {\i shaliach} does {\i more} than what was asked \u8212- he chose the more elaborate form, the more valuable coin, the more difficult option. If the {\i shaliach} does {\i less} than was asked, or switches laterally to something of equal or different character, all agree the principal is {\i makpid}. The only space for the debate between {\i kefida} and {\i mareh makom} is when the {\i shaliach} exceeds the instruction in a way that could plausibly be understood as fulfilling the original intent more amply. This sets the parameters within which Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel\u8217's ruling and Rabbi Shimon\u8217's ruling in our {\i Mishnah} operate.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Three Tannaim (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Elazar Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The third of the three {\i Tanna\u8217'im} cited by Abaye is Rabbi Elazar, whose position derives from a {\i Mishnah} dealing with {\i gittin} as well. The {\i Mishnah} states: {\i ha-ishah she-amrah, hitkabel li giti mi-makom ploni} \u8212- a woman said to a {\i shaliach}, \u8220"Go and accept my {\i get} on my behalf from such-and-such a location\u8221" \u8212- that is, from a specific place where her husband agreed to meet. {\i Ve-kiblah et gitah mi-makom acher} \u8212- and the {\i shaliach} went and accepted the {\i get} at a different location. The {\i tanna kamma} holds this is {\i pasul} \u8212- invalid \u8212- because her instruction specified a particular place, and accepting it elsewhere is a deviation. {\i Ve-Rabbi Elazar machshir} \u8212- Rabbi Elazar validates it. {\i Alma kasavar mareh makom hu lo} \u8212- Rabbi Elazar holds that by naming the location, the woman was merely pointing the {\i shaliach} toward where her husband would be, not requiring that the exchange happen at precisely that street corner. It is worth noting that this is likely the case where the phrase {\i mareh makom} originated in its literal sense \u8212- showing the place \u8212- before being applied metaphorically to other cases of general direction-giving. Unlike the {\i get} and {\i kiddushin} cases above, where one option was more valuable than the other, here the question is simply a different location \u8212- neither easier nor harder, just different. Yet Rabbi Elazar\u8217's principle of {\i mareh makom} encompasses even this: the instruction was directional, not constitutive. By analogy, when the woman in our {\i Mishnah} says \u8220"the man who offered me a silver {\i dinar},\u8221" Rabbi Shimon applies the same logic \u8212- she was identifying the man, not mandating the metal.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Conditions Analysis\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ulla\u8217's Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now turns to the second part of the {\i Mishnah} \u8212- the {\i al menat} cases of rich and poor \u8212- without formal announcement. Ulla states: {\i machloket be-shvach mamon} \u8212- the dispute between Rabbi Shimon and the {\i Rabbanan} is limited to the case where the man\u8217's financial situation turns out to be better than stated, i.e., he is rich rather than poor. Here the {\i Gemara} does not invoke the wine-and-vinegar argument against Rabbi Shimon. The logic is more intuitive: on the assumption that a woman would generally prefer a wealthy husband, Rabbi Shimon holds that if the {\i al menat} condition fails because the man is {\i more} than he represented, the {\i kiddushin} is valid. The {\i tanna kamma\u8217's} objection in this case may simply be formalistic \u8212- the condition was stated and not met \u8212- rather than based on any genuine concern that she preferred poverty.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yichus Objection\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 However, Ulla continues: {\i aval be-shvach yichusin, divrei hakol eina mekudeshet} \u8212- but if it turns out that the man has higher {\i yichus} \u8212- better lineage or personal status \u8212- than he represented, all agree she is not {\i mekudeshet}. Why? {\i Masana de-rav mi-kara\u8217'i lo ba\u8217'ina} \u8212- \u8220"a shoe bigger than my foot, I do not want.\u8221" The woman could say: I am not capable of filling that role. A man of higher status \u8212- a {\i kohen} when she expected a {\i Yisrael}, or a {\i Yisrael} when she expected a {\i mamzer} \u8212- imposes expectations and social obligations she never agreed to. She may genuinely not want to be the wife of someone whose status she cannot match or whose position creates discomfort or tension within the marriage. Better {\i yichus} is not straightforwardly {\i leshevach} from the woman\u8217's perspective, and therefore even Rabbi Shimon agrees the {\i kiddushin} does not stand.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Baraita Agreement\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i baraita} confirms this: {\i modeh Rabbi Shimon she-im hita\u8217'ah le-shvach yichusin she-eina mekudeshet} \u8212- Rabbi Shimon himself concedes that if the misrepresentation was an improvement in personal status, she is not {\i mekudeshet}. This is not a case of Rabbi Shimon being overruled; it is his own stated position. The distinction he draws is between objective material improvement \u8212- greater wealth \u8212- and the more complex category of status, where individual preference and social dynamics render the calculus uncertain.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rav Ashi Inference\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Silence Proof\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Ashi argues that this conclusion \u8212- that Rabbi Shimon\u8217's disagreement is limited to the case of wealth and does not extend to {\i yichus} \u8212- can be inferred from the language of the {\i Mishnah} itself. The {\i Mishnah} that immediately follows our {\i Mishnah} deals with cases such as {\i al menat she-ani kohen ve-nimtza levi, levi ve-nimtza kohen, natin ve-nimtza mamzer, mamzer ve-nimtza natin} \u8212- conditions about tribal and legal status \u8212- and rules {\i eina mekudeshet}. Notably, Rabbi Shimon does not voice any disagreement in that {\i Mishnah}. Rav Ashi argues: {\i ve-lo paligi Rabbi Shimon} \u8212- Rabbi Shimon\u8217's silence indicates that he agrees with the ruling in those cases. Since he expressly disagreed in the case of wealth earlier, his failure to speak up when the {\i Mishnah} discusses {\i kohen}, {\i levi}, and {\i mamzer} demonstrates that he concedes those cases entirely.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Objection\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Mar bar Rav Ashi challenges this inference. He notes that the later {\i Mishnah} also includes a case of {\i al menat she-yesh li bat o shifcha megudelet} \u8212- on the condition that I have an older daughter or maidservant capable of helping in the household \u8212- and again Rabbi Shimon does not disagree. But this is surely a case of financial benefit, not {\i yichus}. If Rabbi Shimon only fails to disagree regarding {\i yichus}, we would have expected him to reassert his position regarding the daughter or maidservant. His silence there undermines the inference from his silence in the {\i kohen}/{\i levi} cases. One could argue, says Mar bar Rav Ashi, that Rabbi Shimon simply didn\u8217't repeat his position because {\i he already stated it} regarding wealth, and {\i hu ha-din le-seifa} \u8212- the same ruling applies to all the cases of wealth, including the daughter and maidservant. If so, his silence in the {\i kohen}/{\i levi} cases is equally uninformative.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gemara Response\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} responds: {\i hatam idi ve-idi shvach mamon} \u8212- both the case of wealth and the case of having a daughter or maidservant belong to the same category of material benefit. It is entirely natural for Rabbi Shimon to remain silent in the later {\i Mishnah} regarding those cases, since he already stated his position \u8212- having a helpful household member falls under the same principle as financial wealth, and the ruling is identical. But the case of {\i kohen} and {\i levi} is {\i shvach yichusin} \u8212- a fundamentally different category. Were Rabbi Shimon to disagree there as well, he would have needed to say so explicitly. {\i Ei ita de-palig litni} \u8212- if he disagreed, he should have said it. His silence in that qualitatively distinct context is therefore meaningful, and Rav Ashi\u8217's inference stands.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Megudelet Definition\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} then offers an alternative answer ({\i iba\u8217'it eima}) that approaches the matter differently. One might reconsider what {\i megudelet} means. The simple reading takes it to mean a grown ({\i gedolah}) daughter or maidservant \u8212- an adult who can be useful around the house, which is indeed a material benefit analogous to wealth. But the {\i Gemara} asks: {\i mai megudelet?} What if {\i megudelet} does not mean simply an adult? {\i Tosafot} explains \u8212- and this reading is the more compelling one \u8212- that {\i megudelet} means a hairdresser. And why would having a hairdresser in the house be a problem rather than a benefit? Because {\i lo nicha li de-shakeila mili minai ve-azla ve-ganya kammai shivvutei} \u8212- the wife can say: I do not want her to take my personal matters and go spread them among the neighbors. A hairdresser, by the nature of her work, talks while she works and shares what she hears. Rashi\u8217's version of this concern is that an important woman ({\i gedolah} in the sense of prominent) would feel comfortable circulating socially and carrying information. Either way, having such a person in the household is not straightforwardly a benefit for the wife \u8212- it introduces the risk of her private affairs becoming public. In this reading, the {\i megudelet} case is analogous to {\i yichus}, not to wealth: it is something that may appear to be an improvement but which the woman may legitimately not want. This explains why Rabbi Shimon does not disagree there either.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Personal Traits\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Reader Condition\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having addressed the {\i al menat} cases involving social status and wealth, the {\i Gemara} now turns to conditions involving personal traits and scholarly achievement. A {\i baraita} discusses each in turn. {\i Al menat she-ani kraina} \u8212- on the condition that I am a reader. The minimum that satisfies this condition is {\i keivan she-kara shelosha pesukim be-veit ha-knesset} \u8212- if he has received an {\i aliyah} and read three {\i pesukim} in the synagogue, the condition is met and she is {\i mekudeshet}. Reading three verses publicly demonstrates basic literacy; a high level of scholarship is not implied by the word {\i kraina} used without further qualification.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yehudah, however, says {\i ad she-yikra ve-yitargem} \u8212- he must be able to read and also do {\i Targum}, the Aramaic translation. The {\i Gemara} asks: does this mean he can translate freely in his own words? Surely not \u8212- the {\i Gemara} recalls that Rabbi Yehudah himself ruled that one who translates a verse {\i ke-tsurato}, in an overly literal fashion, is a liar ({\i badai}), and one who adds his own embellishments to the accepted translation is a blasphemer. The authorized {\i Targum} is the traditional {\i Masorah}-based translation; to deviate from it in either direction is a distortion of the Torah\u8217's meaning. The {\i Gemara} resolves this: {\i ela mai targum \u8212- targum didan}, the {\i Targum} referred to is the accepted traditional one, understood to be {\i Targum Onkelos}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i baraita} further distinguishes: {\i va-hanei milei de-amar lei kraina} \u8212- these standards apply when a man says {\i kraina}, \u8220"I read\u8221" \u8212- an unqualified claim to basic reading ability. But if he says {\i kara ana} \u8212- I am a reader in the full sense, a {\i ba\u8217'al korei} \u8212- then the condition is only satisfied {\i ad de-kari Oraita, Nevi\u8217'ei u-Chtuvei be-didkuka} \u8212- if he can read the entire {\i Tanakh} with precision and grammatical accuracy.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Learner Condition\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Al menat she-ani shoneh} \u8212- on the condition that I am a learner. Here, {\i shoneh} has the connotation of {\i Mishnah}, the core oral tradition. Hezekiah says the condition is met if one knows {\i halachot} \u8212- basic legal rulings. Rabbi Yochanan says {\i Torah}, meaning {\i Midrash Torah}, i.e., the {\i midreshei halacha} \u8212- the halachic midrashim ({\i Sifra}, {\i Sifrei}, {\i Mechilta}) that derive law exegetically from Scripture. A {\i baraita} confirms this, with Rabbi Meir specifying {\i halachot} and Rabbi Yehudah specifying {\i Midrash}. The {\i Gemara} reconciles Rabbi Yochanan\u8217's position with Rabbi Yehudah\u8217's \u8212- {\i mai Torah, Midrash Torah} \u8212- what he meant by \u8220"Torah\u8221" is the {\i midrashic} exposition of the Torah\u8217's laws.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Here again the {\i baraita} distinguishes based on how the man phrases his claim. {\i Va-ani midi de-amar lah tanina} \u8212- this applies when he says {\i tanina}, \u8220"I learn,\u8221" in a general sense. But if he says {\i tana ana}, \u8220"I am a {\i tana}\u8221" \u8212- implying the role of those whose entire function was systematic memorization and transmission of the oral law \u8212- then {\i ad de-tani hilchata, Sifra, Sifrei, ve-Tosefta}: he must have mastered all the {\i halachot}, all the {\i midrashei halacha}, and all the {\i Tosefta}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Student Condition\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Al menat she-ani talmid} \u8212- on the condition that I am a {\i talmid}. Here {\i talmid} refers not to memorization but to {\i Talmud} \u8212- analytical engagement with the material. The minimum for this condition is not the standard of a Shimon ben Azzai or Shimon ben Zoma, who bore the title of {\i talmidim} despite being the greatest scholars of their generation (having never received {\i semikhah}). Rather, the standard is: {\i kol she-sho\u8217'alin oto be-chol makom davar echad be-limudo ve-omer} \u8212- one whom you can ask about anything from a field he has studied, and he is able to answer and explain it. Rashi reads this generously: if in even one area of learning he can answer questions competently, the condition is satisfied. {\i Va-afilu be-Masechet Kallah} \u8212- even in the relatively accessible tractate of {\i Kallah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sage Condition\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Al menat she-ani chacham} \u8212- on the condition that I am a {\i chacham}. Again, the standard is not the level of the sages of Yavneh \u8212- Rabbi Akiva and his colleagues. Rather, the condition is met by {\i kol she-sho\u8217'alin oto davar chochmah be-chol makom ve-omer} \u8212- anyone who can be asked a matter of analytical wisdom in any field and is able to respond. Rashi notes, significantly, that this need not even be a matter of Torah learning specifically: if a man claims to be a {\i chacham} in physics, or mathematics, or any field of knowledge, and he can demonstrate analytical competence in that field when asked, the condition is satisfied. {\i Chochmah} is not restricted to Torah scholarship.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Moral Traits\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Valiant\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Al menat she-ani gibbor} \u8212- on the condition that I am valorous. The standard is not the level of Avner ben Ner or Yoav ben Tzeruyah, the great military commanders. Rather, {\i kol she-chaverav mis-yar\u8217'im heimenu mipnei gevurato} \u8212- anyone whose friends are afraid of him because of his physical strength and formidability. Even if it later turns out that in absolute terms he is not a strong man, if his social circle regards him as someone not to be crossed, the condition is considered met.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rich\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Al menat she-ani ashir} \u8212- on the condition that I am wealthy. Again, not the standard of Rabbi Elazar ben Charsom or Rabbi Elazar ben Azaryah, men of legendary wealth. Rather, {\i kol she-bnei iro mechabdim oto mipnei oshro} \u8212- anyone whom the people of his town honor and respect on account of his wealth. Relative standing in one\u8217's social context defines {\i ashir} for purposes of this condition.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Righteous\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Al menat she-ani tzaddik} \u8212- on the condition that I am righteous. Here the ruling is striking: {\i afilu rasha gamur, mekudeshet} \u8212- even if the man was known to be a complete {\i rasha}, she is {\i mekudeshet}. Why? {\i Shema hirhir teshuvah be-da\u8217'ato} \u8212- perhaps at that very moment, he had a thought of repentance. This is the first case in this series that introduces the word {\i shema} \u8212- \u8220"perhaps.\u8221" The implication of the {\i poskim} is therefore that she is {\i safek mekudeshet} \u8212- doubtfully betrothed \u8212- not definitively {\i mekudeshet} as in the other cases, where a minimum threshold was simply defined. Here the status rests on a possibility.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This passage is the basis for Rav Soloveitchik\u8217's discussion in {\i Al Ha-Teshuvah}. He notes that full {\i teshuvah} \u8212- which absolves the sin \u8212- requires {\i viduy peh}, verbal confession; {\i charatah}, genuine remorse; {\i kabbalah le-atid}, commitment to the future; and {\i meratze chaveiro}, reconciliation with the wronged party. But the {\i Gemara} here is addressing something different: not the mechanics of atonement but the transformation of personal identity. A person can undergo a sudden, profound inner transformation \u8212- going from {\i rasha} to {\i tzaddik} in an instant \u8212- even without any external process. The man did not say \u8220"on the condition that I have done {\i teshuvah}\u8221"; he said \u8220"on the condition that I am a {\i tzaddik}.\u8221" Perhaps, at the moment of his betrothal \u8212- finding the right woman, sensing a turning point in his life \u8212- he has become, at that moment, who he claimed to be.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Wicked\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Al menat she-ani rasha} \u8212- on the condition that I am wicked. {\i Afilu tzaddik gamur, mekudeshet} \u8212- even if the man was known to be a complete {\i tzaddik} his entire life, she is {\i mekudeshet}. {\i Shema hirhir devar avodah zarah be-da\u8217'ato} \u8212- perhaps at that moment, he had a thought of idol worship. A person who has been wholly righteous throughout his life is not immune to a sudden internal turn. As the principle goes, {\i al ta\u8217'amin be-atzmecha ad yom mosecha} \u8212- do not trust yourself until the day of your death. The soul\u8217's moral condition at any given instant cannot be assumed from its history. This case, like the previous one, yields only {\i safek mekudeshet}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Agadic Homily\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Distribution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having traversed the territory of rich and poor, wise and foolish, righteous and wicked, the {\i Gemara} closes this sugya with an {\i aggadic} passage distributing the qualities discussed throughout the world. Ten measures of {\i chochmah} \u8212- wisdom \u8212- came down to the world: nine were taken by {\i Eretz Yisrael} and one by the entire rest of the world. Ten measures of {\i yofi} \u8212- beauty \u8212- came down: nine were taken by {\i Yerushalayim} and one by the rest of the world. Ten measures of {\i ashirut} \u8212- wealth \u8212- came down: nine were taken by the early Romans ({\i Roma\u8217'im kadmonim}) and one by the rest of the world. Ten measures of {\i aniyut} \u8212- poverty \u8212- came down: nine were taken by {\i Bavel}, where the {\i talmidei chachamim} lived in great poverty, and one by the rest of the world. Ten measures of {\i gasut} \u8212- haughtiness \u8212- came down: nine were taken by {\i Elam} and one by the rest of the world.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Elam\u8217's Haughtiness\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} objects: was haughtiness not a trait of {\i Bavel}? The verse in Zechariah describes two winged women carrying an {\i eifah} \u8212- a large measure \u8212- between heaven and earth, and the angel explains they are bringing it to build a house in the land of {\i Shinar}. Rabbi Yochanan explains: {\i zu chanufah ve-gasut ha-ruach she-yardu le-Bavel} \u8212- this represents flattery and haughtiness, which descended to {\i Bavel}. The {\i Gemara} reconciles: yes, it came down to {\i Bavel}, but then {\i ishtarvovei hu de-ishtarvov le-hasam} \u8212- it rolled and made its way to {\i Elam}. The house was never built in {\i Bavel}; it was carried onward. The language {\i livnot lah bayit} \u8212- \u8220"to build her a house\u8221" \u8212- without the house ever having been completed confirms the inference.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But this raises a further question: does haughtiness not belong to {\i Bavel} after all? The principle {\i siman le-gasut aniyut} \u8212- poverty is a sign of haughtiness \u8212- would locate haughtiness in {\i Bavel}, which we just said took nine measures of poverty. The {\i Gemara} resolves: the poverty meant here is {\i aniyut de-Torah} \u8212- poverty in Torah. When a person is haughty, he is self-satisfied with his learning and never genuinely applies himself; he does not give himself over to teaching others or to the self-sacrifice that real {\i Torah} mastery demands. This is the {\i aniyut} associated with {\i gasut}. The proof is the verse applied by Rabbi Yochanan to {\i Elam}: {\i achot lanu ketanah ve-shadayim ein lah} \u8212- \u8220"we have a young sister and she has no breasts.\u8221" This refers to Elam: {\i she-zacheta lilmod ve-lo zacheta lelamed} \u8212- those who merited to study but never merited to teach, because their haughtiness precluded them from attaining the depth and humility that genuine transmission of Torah requires.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Other Traits\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} continues the catalogue. Ten measures of strength ({\i gevurah}) came down: nine were taken by the Persians. Ten measures of lice ({\i kinim}) came down: nine were taken by the Medes. Ten measures of {\i nega\u8217'im} \u8212- leprous afflictions \u8212- came down: nine were taken by pigs. Ten measures of licentiousness ({\i znut}) came down: nine were taken by Arabia. Ten measures of brazenness ({\i azut}) came down: nine were taken by Meshan. Ten measures of idle chatter ({\i sicha}) came down: nine were taken by women. Ten measures of drunkenness ({\i shikhrut}) came down: nine were taken by the Ethiopians. Ten measures of sleep ({\i shena}) came down: nine were taken by slaves, and one by the rest of the world \u8212- and here the {\i Gemara} concludes the discussion.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Kiddushin 50\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Status Conditions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kohen/Levi Cases\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Daf 50 opens at the very bottom of 49b with a new {\i mishnah}. The {\i mishnah} presents a series of cases in which a man betroths a woman by stating a condition regarding his personal status \u8212- {\i al menat she\u8217'ani Kohen venimtsa Levi}, on the condition that I am a {\i kohen} and he is discovered to be a {\i levi} \u8212- or the reverse, on the condition that I am a {\i levi} and he turns out to be a {\i kohen}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Natin/Mamzer Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} continues: {\i natin venimtsa mamzer}, on the condition that I am a {\i natin} \u8212- a Gibeonite, a lower rank of personal status \u8212- and it turns out he is a {\i mamzer}; or {\i mamzer venimtsa natin}, he declared himself a {\i mamzer} and turned out to be a {\i natin}. In each pair, the first-mentioned status is the more preferred or higher-ranking one. The pattern holds throughout: whether the condition stated was better than the reality, or worse, the {\i kiddushin} does not take effect.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Town vs.\u160 ?City\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} goes on: {\i ben ir venimtsa ben krach}, he said he lived in a town and it turned out he lived in a city, or {\i ben krach venimtsa ben ir}, a city-dweller who turns out to live in a town. Since the town is listed first \u8212- as the preferable condition \u8212- the {\i mishnah} is implicitly expressing that it is better to live in a town than in a city. Rashi explains: {\i yeshivat krachim kasha} \u8212- city living is difficult. The city is a place of markets, noise, high rents, and crowds. Of course, precisely those features lead many people to prefer city life, which makes this an interesting reflection of the {\i mishnah}\u8217's social assumptions.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Bathhouse Proximity\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} continues with conditions relating to amenities: {\i al menat sheyesh li bayit karov lemerchats venimtsa rachok}, on the condition that my house is close to the bathhouse, and it turns out to be distant \u8212- or the reverse, distant and it turns out to be close. Either way, the condition was not fulfilled as stated.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Household Help\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Similarly: {\i al menat sheyesh li bat o shifcha megudelet}, on the condition that I have a daughter or a maidservant who is an adult \u8212- reading the straightforward {\i pshat}, someone who can assist with the household (though the {\i gemara} earlier had offered a different reading of this phrase) \u8212- and he does not, or he says he does not have such help and it turns out he does.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sons Condition\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} further addresses: {\i she\u8217'ein li banim}, on the condition that I have no sons. This is a preferred status from the wife\u8217's perspective, since it means there will be no other heirs \u8212- specifically sons \u8212- competing with her future children for the inheritance. Again, whether he stated he has sons and does not, or stated he has none and does, the condition is unfulfilled and the {\i kiddushin} does not take effect.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Strikingly, the {\i mishnah} does not explicitly conclude with {\i eina mekudeshet} \u8212- she is not betrothed. That ruling is apparently presumed from the previous {\i mishnah}. It is also worth noting that Rabbi Shimon, who argued in the previous {\i mishnah} that fulfillment of a condition in the better-than-promised direction should be valid, does not dispute this {\i mishnah} \u8212- because as the {\i gemara} explained, in matters of personal status, \u8220"better\u8221" is not necessarily better. Perhaps one does not want a spouse of a higher status than anticipated. That is how the {\i gemara} resolved the matter there.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Internal Intent Invalid\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} then adds a critical new principle: {\i af al pi she\u8217'amra belibi haya lehitkadesh lo af al pi chen eina mekudeshet} \u8212- even if the woman says, \u8220"In my heart I had already decided to marry him regardless; I didn\u8217't care whether he was a {\i kohen} or a {\i levi}, I love him and the condition was irrelevant to me\u8221" \u8212- she is still not married. Once a condition is articulated, it becomes the legally binding framework of the {\i kiddushin}. The {\i mishnah} adds that {\i vechen hi shehit\u8217'athu} \u8212- the same applies in reverse, if she is the one who misrepresented a stated condition.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is worth noting that the {\i mishnah} frames the issue in terms of deception \u8212- {\i leta\u8217'u} \u8212- but the {\i gemara} consistently does not focus on the wrongfulness of deception as such. The operative point is not the moral dimension of misleading the other party; it is that something was stated as a condition, and that condition was not met. The primary innovation here is that even an explicit disclaimer of internal indifference \u8212- \u8220"I didn\u8217't care about that condition\u8221" \u8212- has no legal effect. What is legally binding is what was said. This leads directly into one of the most consequential principles in the entire {\i Shas}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Devarim Shebalev\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Aliyah Sale Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} introduces the foundational discussion of the principle of {\i devarim shebelev einam devarim} \u8212- that unarticulated intentions have no halakhic weight. The case that opens the discussion: {\i hahu gavra dezavin lenichsei ada\u8217'ata lemisak le\u8217'Ara deYisrael} \u8212- a man sold his property with the intent of making {\i aliyah}. Rashi emphasizes that the property in question was his home. The man had a plan \u8212- to sell everything and move to Eretz Yisrael \u8212- but critically, {\i u-ve-idna de-zavin lo amar velo midi}, when he actually sold the property he said nothing to anyone about this plan. It did not work out and he did not make {\i aliyah}, and he is now effectively homeless. Can he reverse the sale?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava\u8217's Principle\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Amar Rava: havei devarim she-ba-lev}. Rava rules that the unarticulated intent \u8212- the assumption that the sale was contingent on his {\i aliyah} plans \u8212- constitutes {\i devarim she-ba-lev}, and {\i devarim she-ba-lev einam devarim}: things in the heart, things that are never articulated, carry no halakhic weight. The sale stands.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then asks: {\i minalei le-Rava ha?} \u8212- where does Rava derive this principle? The natural candidate is our own {\i mishnah}, which prompted the discussion, but the {\i gemara} works through several possible sources to determine which most cleanly establishes the principle.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sacrifice Forced\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} proposes a source from the top of 50a. In the beginning of {\i Vayikra}, the {\i pasuk} states {\i yakriv oto} \u8212- he shall bring it \u8212- which is read as a superfluous phrase alongside {\i zachar tamim yakrivenu}. The {\i mishnah} derives from this: {\i melamed she-kofin oto} \u8212- you may compel a person to bring a {\i korban} if he made a {\i neder}. But the {\i pasuk} also states {\i lirtzono} \u8212- it must be of his own will. How can one be both compelled and willing? {\i Kofin oto ad she-yomar rotzeh ani} \u8212- you compel him until he says, \u8220"I consent.\u8221" He says the words verbally, even as it is evident he did not wish to do this. The {\i gemara} notes the apparent difficulty: {\i libeih lo nicha lei} \u8212- in his heart he clearly does not want to. Yet we accept his verbal declaration. Does this not prove {\i devarim she-ba-lev einam devarim}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Challenge to Principle\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} pushes back: {\i ve-dilma shani hatam de-anan sahadei de-nicha lei be-khaparah} \u8212- perhaps there it is different, because we can say that in truth he wants the atonement. Even if it took some arm-twisting, deep down he actually desires what he is ultimately being led to do. He wants to be reconciled with his obligations, and in that sense his verbal statement does align with his real desires, once we factor in what a person truly wants.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This points to a deeper issue. The principle of {\i devarim she-ba-lev einam devarim} is not merely an evidentiary claim \u8212- that we cannot verify what someone was thinking. It is a substantive legal principle: even if we somehow know with certainty what the person was thinking, it has no halakhic effect. But the {\i gemara} here is suggesting that in the sacrifice case, perhaps what the person says actually does reflect what he really wants \u8212- so it is not a pure case of discounting the inner state.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Get & Slave Freedom\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then turns to the continuation of that {\i mishnah}: {\i ve-khen ata motzei be-gitei nashim u-ve-shichrurei avadim \u8212- kofin oto ad she-yomar rotzeh ani}. The same principle applies to a {\i get} and to the freeing of a slave: when {\i beis din} determines that a {\i get} must be given or a slave freed, the man is compelled until he says {\i rotzeh ani}. This is directly relevant to the {\i agunah} problem \u8212- if {\i beis din} were to apply compulsion and obtain a verbal declaration of consent, it would be valid, something that cannot practically occur in certain jurisdictions but is theoretically operative in Israel. The principle is that once he says {\i rotzeh ani}, his internal reluctance is legally irrelevant.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yetzer Hatov Defense\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Yet the {\i gemara} deflects this proof as well: {\i ve-dilma shani hasam mishum de-mitzvah lishmo\u8217'a divrei chachamim} \u8212- perhaps there, too, it is different. Since {\i beis din} is compelling him, we can say that deep down he wants to do the right thing; his {\i yetzer hatov} wants to comply with the ruling of {\i chachamim}, and it is only his {\i yetzer hara} that resists. The Rambam develops this idea at length \u8212- that every person fundamentally desires to fulfill his religious obligations, and {\i beis din}\u8217's compulsion is simply helping the {\i yetzer hatov} overcome the {\i yetzer hara}. Under this reading, what the person ultimately says truly reflects his genuine inner will, and the case thus does not cleanly prove {\i devarim she-ba-lev einam devarim}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Woman Assumed Kohen\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Yosef then proposes a different source: {\i ha-mekadesh es ha-isha ve-amar kasavur hayisi she-hi kohenes ve-harei hi levaya} \u8212- a man betroths a woman and says after the fact, \u8220"I thought she was the daughter of a {\i kohen}\u8221" \u8212- the {\i mishnah} calls her a {\i kohenes} rather than {\i bat kohen} \u8212- \u8220"and it turns out she is the daughter of a {\i levi}.\u8221" Or the reverse: he thought she was a {\i levaya} and she was a {\i kohenes}, or poor and she is wealthy, or wealthy and she is poor. In all these cases, {\i mekudeshes mipnei she-lo hit\u8217'ahu} \u8212- the {\i kiddushin} is valid because she did not deceive him. Again, the {\i mishnah} emphasizes deception, but the {\i gemara}\u8217's consistent focus is on the distinction between what was articulated and what was merely assumed. He thought she was a {\i kohenes} \u8212- but he said nothing. His assumption is entirely internal and legally irrelevant. {\i Kasavur hayisi} \u8212- \u8220"I thought\u8221" \u8212- does not constitute a condition. This seems to be a strong proof for {\i devarim she-ba-lev einam devarim}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Stringency Objection\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye, however, objects: {\i dilma shani hasam de-lechumra} \u8212- perhaps there it is different, because there we are discounting the inner intent in the direction of {\i chumra}, of stringency. We refuse to treat her as unmarried based on his claim of what he was thinking \u8212- because we want to play it safe, not because inner intentions are always irrelevant. To truly prove {\i devarim she-ba-lev einam devarim}, one would need a case where inner intent is discounted even {\i le-kula} \u8212- even in the direction of leniency. Discounting intent in the stringent direction could simply be a matter of caution, not a statement about the irrelevance of inner states.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Woman\u8217's Indifference\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye therefore offers his own proof \u8212- from the {\i mishnah} itself. {\i Bakulam af al pi she\u8217'amra belibi hayah le-hiskadesh lo af al pi chen einah mekudeshes} \u8212- in all these cases, even if the woman says she didn\u8217't care about the condition, she is not married. Here, she is saying \u8220"I would have married him regardless\u8221" \u8212- and we discount that to rule {\i lekula}, that she is not married. We are ignoring her stated inner indifference and declaring the {\i kiddushin} void. This is discounting {\i devarim she-ba-lev} in the lenient direction. Surely this proves the principle?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Stated Negates Internal\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} responds: {\i ve-dilma shani hasam de-kivan de-asni lav kol kaminei de-akei lei letna\u8217'ei} \u8212- perhaps there it is different, because since a condition was explicitly stated, she cannot use her inner thoughts to negate it. {\i Kivan de-asni} \u8212- since he articulated a condition, she cannot come and say \u8220"in my heart I didn\u8217't care about that.\u8221" Inner intent cannot override an explicit verbal stipulation. This is a narrower principle than what Rava wants to establish: not that {\i devarim she-ba-lev} are never legally relevant, but that {\i devarim she-ba-lev} cannot negate something that was explicitly stated.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The question, then, is how to prove the broader principle \u8212- that even in cases where nothing was said at all (as in the man who sold his property planning {\i aliyah} without articulating that intent), the unarticulated intent is legally void.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shaliach & Windowsill\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} reports that Rav Chiya bar Avin brought this question before Rav Chisda, who brought it before Rav Huna, and they found support from the following {\i me\u8217'ilah} case. If a person says to his {\i shaliach}, \u8220"Bring me the money from the windowsill\u8221" \u8212- {\i havi li min hachalon} \u8212- and from a certain box \u8212- {\i umin hadeluskema} \u8212- and the {\i shaliach} does so and spends the money, and it turns out the money was {\i hekdesh}, the {\i ba\u8217'al habayis} has committed {\i me\u8217'ilah}. The principle of {\i shlichus} applies in {\i me\u8217'ilah} \u8212- the {\i shaliach} acted on his behalf, so the {\i ba\u8217'al habayis} bears the liability. Now, the {\i ba\u8217'al habayis} claims: {\i lo haya li belibi ella al ze} \u8212- \u8220"I only meant the money on this side of the windowsill, not the other side.\u8221" The {\i shaliach} took from the other side. Does this matter?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shaliach Proof\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Keivan shehevi lo mize ba\u8217'al habayis ma\u8217'al} \u8212- it does not matter. Even though the {\i ba\u8217'al habayis} is articulating what he says he was thinking, the {\i shlichus} was fulfilled and he has committed {\i me\u8217'ilah}. This is an ideal proof for {\i devarim she-ba-lev einam devarim} for several reasons. First, there is nothing here that contradicts what was said \u8212- there was no explicit statement about which side of the windowsill; the inner thought is not being deployed to negate anything articulated. Second, the question of {\i kula} versus {\i chumra} is not straightforwardly applicable here \u8212- either the {\i ba\u8217'al habayis} brings the {\i korban} or the {\i shaliach} does. Third, we are completely discounting what the {\i ba\u8217'al habayis} says he was thinking, even if we believe him entirely. {\i Ella lav mishum de-amrinan devarim she-ba-lev einam devarim.}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Korban Escape Doubt\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} raises one more challenge: {\i ve-dilma shani hatam delimeiftar nafshia mikorban ka\u8217'ati} \u8212- perhaps we discount his claim not because {\i devarim she-ba-lev} are legally irrelevant, but because we suspect he is lying. He wants to escape liability for the {\i korban} and is fabricating a story about which side of the windowsill he meant. If so, we are discounting him because we do not believe him \u8212- not because his inner state would be irrelevant even if sincere.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Easier Escape Routes\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} answers: {\i hava lei lemeimar mezid hava} \u8212- if he only wanted to escape the {\i korban}, he had easier things to say. He could have claimed to have acted intentionally \u8212- {\i mezid} \u8212- since a deliberate act of {\i me\u8217'ilah} does not carry the {\i korban} obligation. But {\i la avid inish demashvi nafshia rashi\u8217'a} \u8212- a person will not declare himself a {\i rasha}, will not portray himself as someone who deliberately misappropriated {\i hekdesh} funds, just to avoid a {\i korban}. Alternatively, {\i hava lei lemeimar nizkarti} \u8212- he could have said, \u8220"I remembered it was {\i hekdesh} while the {\i shaliach} was on the way, but by then I had no way to recall him.\u8221" The {\i braita} establishes: {\i nizkar ba\u8217'al habayis velo nizkar shaliach \u8212- shaliach ma\u8217'al} \u8212- if the {\i ba\u8217'al habayis} remembered and the {\i shaliach} did not, the {\i shaliach} bears the liability. So the {\i ba\u8217'al habayis} had a clean, costless way to escape \u8212- and he did not use it. His claim about the windowsill therefore cannot be dismissed as motivated by self-interest. We believe him \u8212- and we still discount it. {\i Devarim she-ba-lev einam devarim}, even when sincere.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Exceptions Overview\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having established the principle, it is important to note that there are significant exceptions \u8212- cases where {\i devarim she-ba-lev} do carry halakhic weight.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Self-Evident Exception\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One major exception is called {\i devarim she-ba-lev kol adam} \u8212- intent that is self-evident from the circumstances to any reasonable person. Even if something is not explicitly articulated, if the context makes it entirely clear what a person must have intended, that intent may be given legal weight. This opens a broad area of discussion, but the basic idea is that when there is no need to access someone\u8217's private thoughts \u8212- when the circumstances themselves make the intent obvious \u8212- we are not truly in the domain of unarticulated inner states. Related to this are cases where a person makes an elliptical statement \u8212- as at the beginning of {\i Nedarim}, where someone says \u8220"I will be\u8221" as a {\i nazir} walks by, and we understand from context what was meant. There the full sentence is being completed from external evidence, not from speculation about private thought. Situations involving clear external compulsion also raise questions about how much weight to give the verbal statement versus the evident reluctance, though as we saw, the {\i gemara} earlier seemed to indicate that compulsion does not restore legal weight to inner states.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 One vs.\u160 ?Two-Sided\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A further and very important distinction emerges from the Rambam, and the {\i Tosafot Rid} as well: the principle of {\i devarim she-ba-lev einam devarim} applies with particular force in two-sided transactions \u8212- a sale, a betrothal, an appointment of a {\i shaliach} \u8212- where there is another party whose interests and reasonable expectations must be protected. It would be deeply unfair to that other party if halakhic outcomes could be affected by the unarticulated private thoughts of one side. However, in more one-directional acts \u8212- giving a gift, delivering a {\i get}, where the act is essentially emanating from one side \u8212- there may be greater readiness to consider {\i devarim she-ba-lev}. Others among the {\i rishonim} frame the distinction not in terms of one-sided versus two-sided transactions, but in terms of how self-evident the intent is from the circumstances. In any case, the core principle \u8212- that purely private, unarticulated intentions that no one had access to cannot be given halakhic meaning \u8212- is firmly established by this {\i gemara}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Aliyah Condition Met?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Went, Didn\u8217't Settle\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now turns to a related case, this time assuming that the man did articulate his intent when selling his property. {\i Hahu gavra dezavin le-nikhsei ada\u8217'ata le-misak le-ara de-Yisrael} \u8212- he sold his property because he was planning to go up to Eretz Yisrael and said so. Unlike the previous case, the assumption here is that something was stated. He went \u8212- {\i salik} \u8212- attempted to make {\i aliyah}, but {\i ve-lo istadar lei} \u8212- he could not find a place to live, could not get settled, and returned. The condition was stated; was it met?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava: Settling Intent\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Amar Rava kol de-salik ada\u8217'ata le-meidar hu} \u8212- Rava rules that when a person says he is selling in order to go up to Eretz Yisrael, he means to go up and settle there. {\i Ve-ha lo istadar} \u8212- and since he did not manage to settle, the intent was not fulfilled and the sale is reversed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Alternative: Going Only\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Ika de-amri} \u8212- some say the opposite ruling in Rava\u8217's name: {\i ada\u8217'ata le-misak ve-ha salik} \u8212- he said he was going to go, and he went. The condition of going was satisfied; the fact that he did not ultimately settle is beside the point. These are two competing interpretations of what his stated intent meant.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Never Went Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A further case: {\i hahu gavra d\u8217'zavin l\u8217'nichsei ada\u8217'ata l\u8217'misak l\u8217'Eretz Yisrael} \u8212- another man sold his property planning to go up to Eretz Yisrael. {\i Lesof lo salik} \u8212- in the end he changed his mind and never went. Does he have the right to reverse the sale?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Option to Go\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Amar Rav Ashi: ibai salik} \u8212- Rav Ashi responds: who is stopping him? He can still go. The sale cannot be reversed simply because he has decided not to exercise the option. He retains the ability to fulfill the intent; he is simply choosing not to. {\i Ika de-amri: ibai lo salik} \u8212- some formulate it as: well, if he wants to, nothing prevents him from going. Both formulations say essentially the same thing: the option remains available.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Impossible Travel\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} asks: {\i mai beinaihu} \u8212- what is the practical difference between the two formulations? {\i Ika beinaihu d\u8217'ishlei unsa be\u8217'orcha} \u8212- the difference emerges when travel is genuinely impossible, such as when road conditions make the journey truly impracticable. According to the formulation \u8220"who is stopping him,\u8221" there is now an answer \u8212- something genuinely is stopping him \u8212- and the sale may be nullified. But according to the formulation \u8220"if he wants to, he can go,\u8221" the implication is that so long as it is at all possible, the option exists and the sale stands. A legitimate external impediment, however, might nullify it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 External vs.\u160 ?Central\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This case raises a striking question that is addressed by the {\i rishonim}. Since when is a person compelled to fulfill such a condition? Normally, if I sell you a plot of land on the condition that I walk around the block, I may freely choose whether to walk or not \u8212- and my choice determines whether the sale stands. Why then does the {\i gemara} respond \u8220"who is stopping you? Go!\u8221" \u8212- implying he must go? The answer, developed by {\i Tosafot}, Ramban, and others, turns on a fundamental distinction between two types of conditions.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Formal vs.\u160 ?Fundamental\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 When a condition is {\i external} to the basic intent of the transaction \u8212- such as \u8220"this sale will take effect if I walk around the block\u8221" \u8212- it is a technical device layered on top of the transaction. The person may fulfill it or not at will, but if he wants to use the condition to undo the transaction, he must have satisfied all the formal requirements of {\i mishpetei tna\u8217'im} \u8212- the formal rules of conditions modeled after the stipulation of {\i Bnei Gad u-Vnei Reuven}: the condition must be doubled, the positive stated before the negative, and so forth.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 When, however, the reason for the transaction is itself central to the person\u8217's fundamental {\i da\u8217'at} \u8212- as when someone sells his home {\i because} he intends to make {\i aliyah}, and that intent is what motivates the entire transaction \u8212- we are not dealing with an external conditional device. We are speaking about the core of what the person wanted to do. In that case, there is no need to satisfy the formal {\i mishpetei tna\u8217'im}, because this is not a formal condition at all \u8212- it is an expression of what the transaction fundamentally was about. But by the same token, as long as the underlying intent remains achievable \u8212- as long as he could still make {\i aliyah} \u8212- the transaction reflects a genuine, intact {\i da\u8217'at}, and simply choosing not to act on it does not unwind what was done. The formal question of whether to fulfill the condition is replaced by the substantive question of whether the fundamental premise of the transaction has genuinely failed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 These discussions yield two major and interconnected principles. First, {\i devarim she-ba-lev einam devarim}: purely unarticulated intentions \u8212- even if sincerely held and even if we somehow know them \u8212- have no halakhic significance in transactions, particularly two-sided ones. Second, even when things are articulated, the legal analysis of what was said depends on whether it constitutes a formal external condition subject to technical {\i tna\u8217'im} requirements, or whether it expresses the fundamental {\i da\u8217'at} of the transaction. These are among the most consequential principles in the entire {\i Shas}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Shaliach Location\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Different Location\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} turns to a new {\i mishnah}: {\i ha\u8217'omer lishluch\u1086 ? tze vekadesh li isha plonis bemakom ploni vehalach vekideshah bemakom acher} \u8212- a man says to his {\i shaliach}, \u8220"Go betroth this woman for me in such-and-such place,\u8221" and the {\i shaliach} goes and finds her in a different place and betroths her there. The {\i mishnah} rules: {\i eina mekudeshet} \u8212- she is not betrothed. The {\i shlichus} was limited to that particular place.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mareh Makom\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This ruling reflects the principle of {\i makpid} \u8212- that when a person specifies a location as part of a {\i shlichus}, we treat him as genuinely insisting on that location, not merely as indicating where the woman can be found. This is in contrast to the principle of {\i mareh makom} \u8212- merely indicating the woman\u8217's whereabouts as a point of reference, which would not be a binding limitation on the {\i shlichus}. The {\i mishnah} establishes: if the location was articulated as part of the act of {\i shlichus}, it is a substantive requirement, not a mere {\i mareh makom}. A dissenting {\i tanna} does appear in the parallel discussion regarding {\i get}, though not explicitly here in the {\i kiddushin} context.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Get Location\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} also teaches the parallel rule regarding {\i get}: {\i ha\u8217'omer tnu get l\u8217'ishti bemakom ploni unesanu lah bemakom acher \u8212- pasul} \u8212- if a man says to give his wife a {\i get} in a specific place and it is given in a different place, it is invalid. But if he says \u8220"she is in such-and-such place\u8221" and the {\i get} is given elsewhere, {\i kasher} \u8212- it is valid. There the reference to a location was purely {\i mareh makom}, indicating where the woman could be found, not a substantive condition of the {\i shlichus}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Marriage Reputation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Why does specifying the location matter in the context of {\i kiddushin}? The {\i gemara} explains: {\i dekureva ka\u8217'asi} \u8212- the man is trying to bring her close, to marry her. When he specifies a place, it is because {\i behai asra rachmu li velo mamlu mili aluy} \u8212- in that neighborhood or community, people know him favorably and will speak well of him. He does not want the {\i shaliach} to approach her in a place where people might speak poorly of him and cause her to refuse. He is genuinely insisting on the location, not merely indicating where she might be found.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Divorce Embarrassment\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Why must the parallel rule also be taught by {\i get}? The {\i gemara} explains that one might have thought that when it comes to {\i get}, where {\i delerichuka ka\u8217'asi} \u8212- the man is trying to push her away \u8212- perhaps {\i lo ikphas leh} \u8212- he does not particularly care where it happens, since she has no say in accepting a {\i get} and no one can obstruct it. So perhaps even an explicit mention of location is only {\i mareh makom} in the context of {\i get}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Conversely, one might have thought that a man is more {\i makpid} about the location of a {\i get} than of {\i kiddushin}. Rashi explains: {\i delivzi shemegareshes ishto g\u8217'nai hu lo} \u8212- the act of divorcing one\u8217's wife carries a certain disgrace for the man, an embarrassing reflection on the failure of the marriage. He may have strong feelings about where this humiliating event occurs \u8212- {\i behai asra nicha leh denivzi} \u8212- this is a place where he can tolerate the embarrassment, whereas elsewhere he would be much more sensitive. So one might have thought he cares more acutely about the location of the {\i get} than of the {\i kiddushin}. Since the reasoning cuts in different directions, the {\i mishnah} must teach the rule in both contexts. The underlying principle remains: when the location is articulated as part of the {\i shlichus}, it is binding; when it is merely a {\i mareh makom}, it is not.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Vows Condition\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Pre-Marriage Vows\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} presents another category of conditions: {\i hamekadesh es ha\u8217'isha al menas she\u8217'ein aleha nedarim venimtzu aleha nedarim} \u8212- if a man betroths a woman on the condition that she has no outstanding {\i nedarim}, and {\i nedarim} are found, {\i eina mekudeshet} \u8212- the {\i kiddushin} is void.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Void Pre-Nisuin\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If the {\i nedarim} are discovered before {\i nisuin} \u8212- before the full marriage is consummated \u8212- then the condition was explicitly unfulfilled and the {\i kiddushin} simply does not take effect, consistent with everything we have learned.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 No Ketubah Post-Nisuin\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If, however, the man made this condition, they proceeded to {\i nisuin}, began living together, and only then discovered the {\i nedarim}, the situation is different. {\i Kensah stam venimtzu aleha nedarim \u8212- teitzei belo kesuvah} \u8212- if he married her without having explicitly made the condition and {\i nedarim} are found, or if the condition was made but discovered only post-{\i nisuin}, she must leave \u8212- but without a {\i kesuvah}. She receives no {\i kesuvah} because the marriage violated what was stipulated; however, she still requires a {\i get}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Nisuin Nullifies\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Why does she need a {\i get} even though the condition was not met? This is based on the principle of {\i ein adam oseh beilaso beilas zenus} \u8212- we assume that a man does not wish for his act of marital intimacy to be treated as promiscuous. Once they proceeded to {\i nisuin} and consummated the marriage, there is a reasonable assumption \u8212- or at least a real possibility \u8212- that by proceeding he effectively waived the condition. Given that possibility, we treat her as genuinely married and requiring a {\i get}, though she loses her {\i kesuvah} since the condition was violated.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Agunah Solution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This {\i mishnah} raises the broader question of {\i tna\u8217'i bekiddushin} as a proposed solution to the {\i agunah} problem. One suggested approach is to build a condition into the {\i kiddushin} itself \u8212- stipulating that if the couple separates and no {\i get} is given under certain circumstances, the {\i kiddushin} will be retroactively void. The question that this {\i mishnah} directly implicates is: once the couple begins living together, does the {\i nisuin} and subsequent cohabitation automatically constitute a {\i bittul} of the {\i tna\u8217'i}? Or can the condition be explicitly formulated in a way that survives the {\i nisuin}? This is one of the central practical complications in evaluating that proposed solution.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Blemishes Condition\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} then states a parallel case: {\i al menas she\u8217'ein lah mumim venimtzu bah mumim} \u8212- on the condition that she has no physical blemishes, and blemishes are found. If discovered before {\i nisuin}, {\i eina mekudeshet} \u8212- the {\i kiddushin} is void. {\i Kinasa stam venimtzu bah mumim \u8212- teitzei belo kesuvah} \u8212- if discovered after {\i nisuin}, she leaves without a {\i kesuvah} but still requires a {\i get}, for the same reasons discussed above. The {\i mishnah} adds: {\i shekol hamumim haposlim bakohanim poslim banashim} \u8212- all the blemishes that disqualify {\i kohanim} from service similarly disqualify women under this type of condition \u8212- a striking and far-reaching statement that the {\i gemara} does not elaborate upon here.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Savlonos Gifts\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Insufficient Money\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The final {\i mishnah} of the daf addresses: {\i hamekadesh shtei nashim beshaveh perutah} \u8212- a man who betroths two women with a single {\i perutah} between them, each receiving only half a {\i perutah}, which is insufficient for valid {\i kiddushin} \u8212- or {\i isha achas bepachas mishaveh prutah}, one woman with less than a {\i perutah}. In these cases the {\i kiddushin} obviously does not take effect.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Savlonos as Kiddushin\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} then introduces the central question: {\i af al pi sheshalach savlonos achar kach einam mekudashos} \u8212- even if he subsequently sent {\i savlonos}, the traditional gifts sent by the groom to the bride\u8217's family after {\i kiddushin}, she is still not betrothed. Two possible concerns motivate the question. One \u8212- emphasized by Rashi \u8212- is whether the {\i savlonos} themselves might constitute new {\i kesef kiddushin}, functioning as a fresh act of betrothal. The other, which fits other parts of the {\i gemara} more naturally, is whether the sending of {\i savlonos} should lead us to assume that some valid act of {\i kiddushin} occurred that we do not know about, since {\i savlonos} typically follow {\i kiddushin}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Based on Initial\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} resolves both concerns: {\i she\u8217'machmat kiddushin harishonim shelach} \u8212- the {\i savlonos} were sent on account of the initial {\i kiddushin}, invalid as it was. The man thought his {\i kiddushin} had taken effect, and that is why he sent the {\i savlonos}. They are therefore not a new act of {\i kiddushin}, and they do not indicate that some other valid {\i kiddushin} occurred \u8212- they are simply the natural continuation of what he (mistakenly) believed had already happened.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The same principle applies to {\i vechen katan shekiddesh} \u8212- a minor who performed {\i kiddushin} and then, after reaching majority, sent {\i savlonos}. She remains unbetrothed for the same reason: the {\i savlonos} are explained by the prior invalid act.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Why All Cases\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} asks: {\i u\u8217'tzricha} \u8212- why must the {\i mishnah} teach all three cases \u8212- two women with one {\i perutah}, one woman with less than a {\i perutah}, and the case of the {\i katan}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If only the case of two women with one {\i perutah} were taught, one might think: since a full {\i perutah} was paid, the man assumed it was valid, so obviously the {\i savlonos} are not a new {\i kiddushin}. But in the case of less than a {\i perutah} \u8212- {\i aval pachas mishaveh prutah} \u8212- perhaps he knew very well that an amount below a {\i perutah} is invalid, and when he sent {\i savlonos} he intended them as a new act of {\i kiddushin}. The {\i mishnah} teaches us that even there, we do not assume this.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Conversely, if only those two cases were taught, one might think that in cases involving money, men generally do not know or pay attention to the precise threshold \u8212- but when it comes to a {\i katan}, {\i hakol yodim she\u8217'ein kiddushei katan klum} \u8212- everyone knows that the {\i kiddushin} of a minor is entirely invalid. So when, after reaching adulthood, he sends {\i savlonos}, perhaps we should assume he knew his earlier act was void and now intends the {\i savlonos} as a new {\i kiddushin}. The {\i mishnah} teaches that even in that case we do not assume this. Without evidence of a new act of {\i kiddushin}, we attribute the {\i savlonos} to the earlier invalid act, and no new betrothal is established.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Concerned About Savlonos\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now presents an {\i amoraic} debate that stands in some tension with the {\i mishnah}. {\i Rav Huna amar chosheshin lesavlanos} \u8212- Rav Huna rules that when {\i savlonos} are sent, we are concerned about the possibility of a prior {\i kiddushin}. {\i Vechen amar Rabbah chosheshin lesavlanos} \u8212- Rabbah agrees. There is a major debate among the {\i rishonim} about what exactly this means. Rashi understands it to mean that we are concerned the {\i savlonos} themselves were intended as {\i kesef kiddushin}. Many {\i rishonim} resist this reading, pointing out that valid {\i kiddushin} requires explicit articulation of {\i harei at mekudeshet li} and the presence of witnesses. They prefer to read the concern as follows: the sending of {\i savlonos} \u8212- which are traditionally sent after {\i kiddushin} \u8212- raises the possibility that some valid {\i kiddushin} occurred beforehand that we simply do not know about.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Resolves Contradiction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbah then anticipates the apparent contradiction with our {\i mishnah}: if we are concerned about {\i savlonos}, how does the {\i mishnah} say {\i einam mekudashos}? Abaye answers: {\i hatam kideiktani ta\u8217'ama} \u8212- there, the {\i mishnah} itself explains why there is no concern: {\i she\u8217'machmat kiddushin rishonim shelach} \u8212- the {\i savlonos} are accounted for by the prior invalid {\i kiddushin}. The man sent them because he thought the first {\i kiddushin} had worked. That eliminates the evidentiary weight of the {\i savlonos}. But Rav Huna and Rabbah are addressing a different scenario: where {\i savlonos} are sent with no prior known {\i kiddushin} at all. In that case, we are concerned that a valid {\i kiddushin} took place beforehand.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Savlonos Indicate Kiddus\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Ika de\u8217'amrei} \u8212- there is an alternative version of the exchange, in which Rabbah does not present the {\i mishnah} as a problem at all. Rather, he reads the {\i mishnah} itself as the source of his ruling. The {\i mishnah} says the {\i savlonos} are not a concern {\i because} the prior {\i kiddushin} explains them \u8212- {\i hacha hu detai}, in the {\i mishnah}\u8217's case he is mistaken in thinking the prior act was valid. {\i Ha be-alma havei kiddushin} \u8212- but normally, when {\i savlonos} are sent without any preceding invalid act to account for them, we would assume that they reflect a prior valid {\i kiddushin}. Abaye, in this version, pushes back and says the {\i mishnah}\u8217's point was that we need no concern whatsoever \u8212- not even where {\i savlonos} appear without any context \u8212- since the idea that {\i savlonos} indicate prior {\i kiddushin} is not self-evidently true, given that in some communities {\i savlonos} are sent before {\i kiddushin}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Practice Dependent\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Amar Rav Pappa} \u8212- the conclusion depends on local practice. {\i Be-asra demikadshi vehadar mesavli} \u8212- in a place where {\i kiddushin} comes first and {\i savlonos} follow, if we see {\i savlonos} being sent, {\i chassinan} \u8212- we are concerned that a {\i kiddushin} has taken place. {\i Mesavli vehadar mikadshi} \u8212- if the local practice is to send {\i savlonos} before {\i kiddushin}, {\i lo chassinan} \u8212- there is nothing to be concerned about.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kiddushin First Usually\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} notes that the ruling for the case where {\i kiddushin} typically precedes {\i savlonos} might seem self-evident \u8212- of course we are concerned. The ruling is therefore necessary for a more nuanced situation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Savlonos First Usually\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If the practice is to send {\i savlonos} first, there is obviously no concern, since seeing {\i savlonos} tells us nothing about whether {\i kiddushin} has occurred.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Majority Practice\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The novelty of the ruling applies where the {\i majority} practice in a community is for {\i kiddushin} to precede {\i savlonos}, but a minority practice the reverse. One might have thought: this person sent {\i savlonos} and we know no {\i kiddushin} occurred \u8212- perhaps he is from the minority who do things in reverse order. The {\i gemara} rules that because of the weight of {\i eshes ish} \u8212- the gravity of a woman being considered a married woman \u8212- we do not attribute the {\i savlonos} to the minority practice. We follow the majority and are concerned that {\i kiddushin} already took place.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ketubah Writing\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A parallel question is then asked: {\i hukhzak shtar kesuvah bashuk mahu} \u8212- if it becomes known that a {\i kesuvah} was written for a woman, does that indicate that {\i kiddushin} has already taken place? Rav Acha bar Rav Huna poses this question to Rava. The questioner wonders whether the writing of a {\i kesuvah} should be treated like the sending of {\i savlonos} \u8212- as evidence of a prior {\i kiddushin}. Rava responds with some skepticism: why should we simply assume, because a {\i kesuvah} was written, that {\i kiddushin} has occurred? Perhaps the {\i kesuvah} was written in preparation, and perhaps other explanations exist.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ketubah Practice\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Amar Rav Ashi} \u8212- again the answer depends on local practice. {\i Be-asra demikadshi vehadar kisvi kesuvah} \u8212- in a place where the standard practice is for {\i kiddushin} to occur before the {\i kesuvah} is written, and we hear that a {\i kesuvah} was written, {\i chassinan} \u8212- we are concerned that {\i kiddushin} has taken place. {\i Kasvi vehadar mikadshi} \u8212- in a place where the {\i kesuvah} is typically written before {\i kiddushin}, {\i lo chassinan}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sofer Availability\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} notes that the ruling for the case where {\i kiddushin} typically precedes the writing of the {\i kesuvah} might again seem obvious. The ruling is necessary for a case where {\i delo shakhiakh safra} \u8212- a {\i sofer} is not readily available. One can imagine a perfectly reasonable scenario: a man intends to marry, has not yet performed {\i kiddushin}, but a {\i sofer} happens to be available now and may not be available for months. So he has the {\i kesuvah} written in advance. Surely, one might think, we should assume this is what happened rather than concluding that {\i kiddushin} has already occurred.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ashes Ish Concern\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} rules that this reasoning does not override the standard practice. Since the norm in that community is to write the {\i kesuvah} only after {\i kiddushin}, even the plausible scenario of an early {\i kesuvah} written for the sake of {\i sofer} availability does not remove the concern. Just as with {\i savlonos} \u8212- where we follow the majority practice and do not assume this person belongs to the minority that sends {\i savlonos} first \u8212- here too we follow the standard practice, and the concern that {\i kiddushin} has already taken place, with all the gravity of {\i eshes ish}, leads us to treat the situation accordingly until we can investigate further.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Kiddushin 51\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Savlanot & Kiddushin\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Huna Intro\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The sugya opens on 51b, approximately fifteen lines from the top, with a teaching introduced by the formula {\i itmar}: Rav Huna said, {\i choshishin lesavlanot} \u8212- we are concerned that gifts sent after an engagement ({\i savlanot}) may constitute or indicate an act of {\i kiddushin}. This teaching must be understood against the backdrop of the preceding {\i mishnayot}, which dealt extensively with conditional {\i kiddushin} and the principle of {\i devarim shebalev einam devarim} \u8212- that unexpressed mental reservations do not legally alter what has transpired. The immediately preceding {\i mishnah} had established that when an original act of {\i kiddushin} was defective \u8212- worth less than a {\i perutah}, for instance \u8212- we do not reinterpret subsequent gifts as a new and valid {\i kiddushin}. Now, Rav Huna addresses a different scenario.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rashi\u8217's formulation is precise: the case requires that there first be some engagement context \u8212- {\i mi sheshidach be\u8217'isha venitratzes}, a man courted a woman and she agreed \u8212- and then, {\i vekadam veshilach savlanot beyadayim}, he went ahead and sent these gifts. In this situation, where no prior defective {\i kiddushin} preceded the gifts, Rav Huna says we are concerned \u8212- {\i choshishin} \u8212- that the {\i savlanot} themselves may constitute or indicate {\i kiddushin}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rashi v. Tosafot\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 There is a fundamental interpretive dispute between Rashi and Tosafot regarding the nature of this concern. According to Rashi, the question is whether the {\i savlanot} themselves function as an act of {\i kiddushin} \u8212- that is, the man may have intended them to serve as the formal act. According to Tosafot, that is not the question at all; rather, the concern is evidentiary: since it was customary to send such gifts only after {\i kiddushin} had taken place, the presence of {\i savlanot} may serve as evidence that a prior, valid {\i kiddushin} occurred, even if no one witnessed it. These two readings produce meaningfully different legal frameworks, and various passages in the {\i gemara} appear to support one or the other position.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava & Abaye\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava concurs with Rav Huna: {\i vechen amar Rava, choshishin lesavlanot}. But Rava immediately anticipates the obvious challenge from the {\i mishnah}, which states, {\i af al pi sheshilach savlanot acher mikan eina mekudeshet} \u8212- even if he sent {\i savlanot} after the fact, she is not married. Rava himself raises this and says we must ask about it: {\i amar Rava umosivinan ashmatin}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye resolves the tension: {\i hatam kedetani ta\u8217'ama, shemachmat kiddushin rishonim shilach} \u8212- in the {\i mishnah}\u8217's case, the reason the {\i savlanot} are not {\i kiddushin} is that they came in the wake of a prior defective {\i kiddushin} which framed their meaning. The sender was relying on the original (flawed) act; the {\i savlanot} are understood as consequent to that first attempt. In our case, by contrast, no defective {\i kiddushin} preceded the gifts, and so either the gifts may signal a valid {\i kiddushin} took place or may themselves constitute one.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 There is a second formulation in the {\i gemara} \u8212- {\i ika de\u8217'amri}, some say \u8212- in which Rava himself argues from the {\i mishnah}\u8217's reasoning to support his own position. The {\i mishnah} specifies the rationale: {\i shemachmat kiddushin rishonim shilach}, the gifts were sent on account of the prior {\i kiddushin}. This implies that the only reason the {\i savlanot} are harmless in the {\i mishnah}\u8217's case is because of that framing. But {\i ha b\u8217'almam hava kiddushin} \u8212- in a normal case without that framing, they would indeed constitute {\i kiddushin}. This version reads more congenially with Rashi\u8217's position, since it suggests the {\i savlanot} themselves become an act of {\i kiddushin} rather than merely serving as evidence of one.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 In any event, the operative word throughout is {\i choshishin} \u8212- we are {\i concerned}, not {\i certain}. This is a {\i chashash}, not a definitive ruling. The practical implication is that such a woman would require a {\i get} as a precaution, but one would not, for example, treat intimacy with another man as definite adultery subject to the severest penalties.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Cultural Context\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then records: {\i mai havah alah} \u8212- what was the conclusion? Rav Pappa answers that the concern depends on local custom. {\i B\u8217'atra demikadshi vehadar mesabli} \u8212- in a place where the practice is to perform {\i kiddushin} first and only then to send gifts, {\i chashinan}, we are concerned. If the {\i savlanot} were sent and there was no known {\i kiddushin}, the cultural context tells us that such gifts are sent only after {\i kiddushin}, so their presence creates concern that a {\i kiddushin} occurred. By contrast, {\i misavli vehadar mikadshi} \u8212- in a place where gifts are sent before the {\i kiddushin} as the local custom \u8212- obviously the gifts create no concern; they are simply the customary pre-betrothal gifts.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} adds a nuanced case: {\i lo tzericha deruba mikadshi vehadar misavli u\u8217'miuta misavli vehadar mikadshi} \u8212- what if most people in the community first perform {\i kiddushin} and then send gifts, but a minority does it in reverse? One might think to be lenient by ascribing this particular case to the minority practice. The {\i gemara} rejects this: since ninety percent of the community first does {\i kiddushin} and then sends gifts, when these gifts appear without a documented {\i kiddushin}, we follow the majority and are concerned.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This principle is worth pausing over. The {\i chashash} applies specifically because the {\i savlanot} belong to a category of gift that is culturally understood as given after {\i kiddushin}. This is what Rav Pappa is contributing. The point is not merely that he sent a gift in a general engagement context; it is that he sent a particular kind of gift that in this community is exclusively or predominantly associated with the post-{\i kiddushin} stage. Only in that specific cultural configuration does the concern arise.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might wonder whether this applies to the modern engagement ring. As it happens, the engagement ring is paradigmatically {\i misavli vehadar mikadshi} \u8212- it is given before the {\i kiddushin}, not after it \u8212- so it would not raise this concern. The relevant scenario would have to involve a gift that in a given community is the customary token sent after an act of {\i kiddushin} has already occurred.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Ketubah Evidence\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ketubah Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now records a query from Rav Acha bar Rav Huna to Rava: {\i huchzak shtar ketubah bashuk} \u8212- it became known in the marketplace that a {\i ketubah} had been written for a certain woman. {\i Mahu} \u8212- does this constitute evidence that {\i kiddushin} must have occurred?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rashi explains the case as one where people saw the woman\u8217's {\i ketubah} document. The question is whether the existence of a known {\i ketubah} is sufficient grounds to treat the woman as {\i eshet ish}. Tosafot views this as the strongest proof for their reading of the entire {\i savlanot} discussion \u8212- this case is clearly about evidentiary inference rather than about the {\i ketubah} itself functioning as an act of {\i kiddushin}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava responds sharply: {\i vechi mipnei shemachzik shtar ketubah bashuk nachzik ba k\u8217'eshet ish} \u8212- just because a {\i ketubah} is circulating in the marketplace, we are going to treat her as a married woman? The implication is that without further context, this alone would be insufficient. But the {\i gemara} then proceeds to explore what the actual conclusion was.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Ashi Answer\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Ashi provides the resolution, and the parallel to the {\i savlanot} discussion is unmistakable: {\i b\u8217'atar d\u8217'mikadshi vehadar katvi} \u8212- in a place where the custom is to perform {\i kiddushin} and only then to write the {\i ketubah}, {\i chashinan}. The presence of a written {\i ketubah} creates a concern that she is {\i mekudeshet}. {\i Katvi vehadar mikadshi} \u8212- where writing the {\i ketubah} precedes {\i kiddushin}, as is our practice today, {\i lo chashinan}. There is no concern.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} presses: if it is obvious that in a place where {\i kiddushin} precedes {\i ketubah} writing we would be concerned, what is the {\i chiddush}? The answer: {\i lo tzericha d\u8217'lo shchiach safra} \u8212- the case concerns a place where scribes are not readily available. One might say: perhaps this man intended to marry in three months but happened to get the {\i ketubah} written now while the scribe was in town. {\i Mah d\u8217'teima safra hu d\u8217'isrami} \u8212- perhaps the scribe just happened to be available and so the {\i ketubah} was written early. The {\i gemara} rejects this reasoning: since the norm is that writing follows {\i kiddushin}, the presence of a written {\i ketubah} creates sufficient concern to treat the woman as a {\i safek mekudeshet}, even if there is a possible innocent explanation.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Two Sisters Issue\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishna Rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} transitions to a new {\i mishnah}. {\i Ha-mekadesh isha u-vitah} \u8212- if a man attempts to betroth a woman and her daughter, {\i o isha ve-achotah} \u8212- or two sisters, {\i ke-achas} \u8212- simultaneously, {\i einan mekudashot} \u8212- neither is married. The scenario is that he gave the money to effect {\i kiddushin} on behalf of two women at once \u8212- perhaps through an agent who accepted on behalf of both \u8212- without having identified one as primary.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} then records: {\i ma\u8217'aseh be-chamesh nashim, u-va-hen shtei achayot} \u8212- there was a case involving five women, among them two sisters. A man picked a basket of figs, {\i ve-she-la-hen haytah} \u8212- which belonged to them, {\i ve-shel shevi\u8217'it haytah} \u8212- and it was {\i shemitah} produce (a detail we will revisit), {\i ve-amar harei kulchen mekudashot li be-kalkalah zo} \u8212- and he declared, \u8220"Behold, all of you are betrothed to me with this basket.\u8221" One woman accepted the basket on behalf of all of them. {\i Ve-amru chachamim ein ha-achayot mekudashot} \u8212- the Sages ruled that the sisters are not married.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Torah Source\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Menani mili} \u8212- from where is this rule derived? Rami bar Chama cites the verse: {\i isha el achotah lo tikach litzror legalot ervatah aleha be-chayeha} \u8212- \u8220"A woman to her sister you shall not take, to be a rival, to uncover her nakedness in her lifetime.\u8221" Rami bar Chama reads this as establishing that at the very moment when they would become {\i tzarot} \u8212- rivals \u8212- the {\i kiddushin} cannot take effect even for one of them: {\i be-sha\u8217'at she-na\u8217'asu tzarot zo le-zo lo yiheye lecha likuchin afilu be-achas mehen}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Karet Problem\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava challenges this derivation. The verse continues: {\i ve-nichretu ha-nefashot ha-osot mi-kerev amam} \u8212- those who do this are subject to {\i karet}. But {\i karet} for violating the prohibition of a sister\u8217's wife applies only if one is actually married to her sister first. If the {\i kiddushin} never takes effect for either sister, then there is no violation carrying {\i karet} \u8212- you have not married anyone. {\i Ei kiddushin lo tafsi bah, karet mi mechayav?} The verse must therefore be speaking of the sequential case: you married one sister, do not have relations with the other \u8212- and that would incur {\i karet}. The verse is not addressing simultaneous {\i kiddushin} at all.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabba\u8217's Principle\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava therefore concludes: {\i kra bezu achar zo} \u8212- the verse addresses the sequential case. So what is the source for the {\i mishnah}\u8217's rule that simultaneous {\i kiddushin} of two sisters fails? {\i Umatnisin keRabbah} \u8212- the {\i mishnah} follows Rabbah\u8217's famous principle: {\i kol she\u8217'eino bezu achar zo, afilu bevat achas eino} \u8212- anything that cannot work sequentially cannot work simultaneously either.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The logic is elegant. If Reuven is married to Rachel and then attempts to betroth Leah (her sister), it would not take effect \u8212- the second {\i kiddushin} is prohibited because he is already married to the first sister. Therefore, when he attempts to do both at once, since there is no sequential ordering \u8212- no way to determine which is \u8220"first\u8221" and which is \u8220"second\u8221" \u8212- and since for whichever one is treated as \u8220"second\u8221" the {\i kiddushin} categorically cannot take effect, the result is that it takes effect for neither.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Ma\u8217'aser Parallels\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ma\u8217'aser Grain\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye challenges Rabbah\u8217's principle with a case from the laws of {\i ma\u8217'aser}. The {\i mishnah} teaches: {\i hamarbeh bema\u8217'aser, peirotsav metukkanim uma\u8217'asrotav mekulkalim} \u8212- if one separates more than the required tenth as {\i ma\u8217'aser}, his produce is rectified ({\i metukkan}) but his {\i ma\u8217'aser} is disqualified ({\i mekulkal}). The case: you have ten units of grain and you separate two units as {\i ma\u8217'aser}, when only one unit was required. One of those two units properly functions as {\i ma\u8217'aser} and fixes the nine remaining units; the other is merely grain from the {\i tevel} pile, and now the two units of designated {\i ma\u8217'aser} are a mixture of true {\i ma\u8217'aser} and {\i tevel}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye\u8217's challenge: just as with two sisters, you cannot take {\i ma\u8217'aser} sequentially from the same pile after you have already taken it \u8212- the second designation would not work. So when you take both units simultaneously, since one of them cannot function as {\i ma\u8217'aser}, why does the {\i mishnah} not say that neither does? Why not apply Rabbah\u8217's principle and say both units remain {\i tevel}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ma\u8217'aser Solution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} answers: {\i shani ma\u8217'aser d\u8217'ita l\u8217'chatzayim} \u8212- {\i ma\u8217'aser} is different because it can take effect on half of each grain. {\i D\u8217'ei amar tikadesh palga d\u8217'chitata, kadsha} \u8212- if one were to say that half of each grain kernel should be sanctified, that would work. Therefore, in the case of excess {\i ma\u8217'aser}, there is no need to conceptualize the two units as competing: one can instead understand all two hundred kernels (across both units) as having their {\i ma\u8217'aser} sanctity distributed across the whole \u8212- half of every single kernel, across the entire combined pile, is {\i ma\u8217'aser}. Since the sanctity can apply fractionally and uniformly to the whole, there is no question of \u8220"which unit is first and which is second.\u8221" The entire pile is addressed simultaneously as a unified whole.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is precisely what distinguishes it from the two sisters: you cannot say half of each sister is married to you. The {\i kiddushin} must attach to one specific person, and since you have not identified which one, and the sanctity cannot distribute fractionally, the paralysis of undetermined ordering means it takes effect for neither.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Behema & Toda\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then presses with {\i ma\u8217'aser behema} \u8212- the animal tithe. Unlike grain {\i ma\u8217'aser}, {\i ma\u8217'aser behema} cannot take effect on half an animal, {\i d\u8217'leita l\u8217'chatzayim}. And it also cannot work sequentially: once the tenth animal has been designated {\i ma\u8217'aser}, if an eleventh walks out, it is not {\i ma\u8217'aser}. So if two animals walk out of the gate simultaneously when the tenth was expected, and you call both of them \u8220"ten,\u8221" why does the {\i gemara} say {\i asiri ve-achad asar me\u8217'uravin zeh be-zeh} \u8212- ten and eleven are mixed, meaning one is {\i ma\u8217'aser} and one is not, and we simply do not know which \u8212- rather than saying, by Rabbah\u8217's principle, neither is {\i ma\u8217'aser}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} answers: {\i shani ma\u8217'aser behema d\u8217'ita b\u8217'ta\u8217'ut} \u8212- {\i ma\u8217'aser behema} is different because it can take effect even through error. If you miscounted \u8212- calling the ninth animal \u8220"ten,\u8221" or the tenth \u8220"nine,\u8221" the eleventh \u8220"ten\u8221" \u8212- the {\i mishnah} teaches that all three nonetheless acquire {\i kedushah}. This is because to some degree {\i ma\u8217'aser behema} is defined by the objective fact of being the tenth animal, not entirely by the owner\u8217's conscious designation. Since the sanctity arises somewhat automatically, the fact that you erroneously called two animals \u8220"ten\u8221" does not eliminate it from both; one of them objectively is the tenth, and therefore {\i ma\u8217'aser behema} attaches to one of them \u8212- we simply cannot determine which.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This, too, distinguishes it from the two sisters. There, the {\i kiddushin} depends entirely on the person\u8217's intentional act and his identification of a specific woman; there is no objective fact of \u8220"being the tenth\u8221" that would cause {\i kiddushin} to take effect automatically on one of them despite the ambiguity.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Toda Dispute\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} next considers the {\i korban toda} \u8212- the thanksgiving offering \u8212- accompanied by forty loaves. {\i D\u8217'leita b\u8217'ta\u8217'ut} \u8212- the {\i kedushah} of the loaves does not take effect through error; and {\i v\u8217'leisa b\u8217'zeh v\u8217'zeh} \u8212- once you have slaughtered the {\i toda} and sanctified forty loaves, additional loaves cannot become {\i kadosh}. So if someone slaughtered a {\i toda} with eighty loaves rather than forty, this should present the exact same challenge to Rabbah\u8217's principle: since the second forty cannot be sanctified once the first forty are done, and since both sets are presented simultaneously with no specified ordering, why should any forty be sanctified?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 On this very question, {\i Chizkiyah} and Rabbi Yochanan dispute. Chizkiyah says: {\i kadshei lei arbaim mitoch shmonim} \u8212- forty of the eighty are sanctified; we simply do not know which forty. Rabbi Yochanan says: {\i lo kadshei lei arbaim mitoch shmonim} \u8212- none of them are sanctified. Rabbi Yochanan is consistent with Rabbah\u8217's principle; but Chizkiyah appears to contradict it. How do we explain Chizkiyah?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Intent Resolution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yehoshua ben Levi resolves the apparent contradiction by distinguishing cases based on the person\u8217's stated or implied intent. {\i Hakol modim} \u8212- everyone agrees that if the person explicitly said \u8220"forty of the eighty should be sanctified,\u8221" {\i kadshi} \u8212- it works. He is not trying to sanctify all eighty; he specified forty, and forty are sanctified \u8212- we simply do not know which ones. Similarly, {\i lo yikdeshu arbaim ela im ken kadshi shmonim} \u8212- if he said \u8220"I will not sanctify forty unless it works for all eighty,\u8221" everyone agrees it does not work, since it categorically cannot work for all eighty, and he conditioned the partial sanctification on the whole.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Lo nechelku ela bistam} \u8212- the dispute between Chizkiyah and Rabbi Yochanan concerns only the ambiguous case, where the person said nothing explicit. {\i Mar savar l\u8217'achrayus ka mechaven} \u8212- Chizkiyah holds that we assume the person\u8217's underlying intent was to sanctify forty and have the other forty as backup, so forty are sanctified. {\i U\u8217'mar savar l\u8217'korban gadol ka mechaven} \u8212- Rabbi Yochanan holds we assume the person wanted to make an unusually large offering of all eighty, and since that cannot work, none are sanctified.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The upshot for our discussion is clear: the {\i toda} case does not refute Rabbah\u8217's principle in any straightforward way. Where intent is explicit and directed only at forty, everyone agrees forty are sanctified; where intent is directed at all eighty, everyone agrees none are. Only in the ambiguous case \u8212- {\i bistam} \u8212- do Chizkiyah and Rabbi Yochanan part ways, and that debate concerns what presumption to apply when the person\u8217's mind is simply unknown. And crucially, the assumption of the two-sisters {\i mishnah} is that he intended both \u8212- so the case maps cleanly onto the position that neither is sanctified.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Ayin Mesurin L\u8217'Biah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava\u8217's Rationale\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having established Rabbah\u8217's principle as the basis for the {\i mishnah}\u8217's ruling that simultaneous {\i kiddushin} of two sisters is invalid, the {\i gemara} now asks: {\i veRava lama lei leshnuyei keRabbah} \u8212- why did Rava need to explain the {\i mishnah} on the basis of Rabbah\u8217's principle at all? Rava himself holds a broader and more fundamental position \u8212- {\i kiddushin she\u8217'einam mesurin l\u8217'biah} \u8212- that a {\i kiddushin} which cannot ultimately be consummated does not take effect at all. If that is Rava\u8217's general principle, then the {\i mishnah}\u8217's case \u8212- where a man does not know which sister he has married and therefore can never permissibly have relations with either \u8212- should be invalid not because of the simultaneous nature of the attempt, but simply because any {\i kiddushin} that can never lead to {\i bi\u8217'ah} is not {\i kiddushin}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Lidvarav deRami bar Chama ka\u8217'amar} \u8212- the {\i gemara} answers that Rava was responding within the framework of Rami bar Chama\u8217's derivation. Rami bar Chama had attempted to derive the {\i mishnah}\u8217's rule from a verse, and Rava showed that Rami\u8217's verse doesn\u8217't support the simultaneous case. Rava then supplied Rabbah\u8217's principle as the textual basis for the {\i mishnah} even according to those who might not accept his own principle of {\i kiddushin she\u8217'einam mesurin l\u8217'biah}. In other words, Rava was arguing conditionally: even if you reject my general principle, you can still derive the {\i mishnah}\u8217's ruling from Rabbah\u8217's principle. My own principle provides an additional and independent basis.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Abaye v. Rava\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now states the dispute directly. {\i Itmar: kiddushin she\u8217'einam mesurin l\u8217'biah} \u8212- a {\i kiddushin} that cannot be consummated. {\i Abaye amar: havi kiddushin} \u8212- Abaye holds it still takes legal effect. {\i Rava amar: lo havi kiddushin} \u8212- Rava holds it does not.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The paradigm case is the man who attempted {\i kiddushin} with one of two sisters without specifying which. Since he can never determine which one he married, and since having relations with either one risks violating the prohibition of {\i issurei arayot} \u8212- she might be his wife\u8217's sister, or he might be violating the prohibition by having relations with the non-married sister \u8212- his {\i kiddushin} can never permissibly translate into {\i bi\u8217'ah}. Rava says: this means no {\i kiddushin} took effect at all. Abaye disagrees: the {\i kiddushin} is in some sense real; we simply do not know to whom it applies, and both women require a {\i get}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Scriptural Basis\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava grounds his position in a verse. {\i Rava bar Hina asbra li} \u8212- Bar Hina explained the derivation to me: {\i ki yikach ish isha u\u8217'be\u8217'alah} \u8212- \u8220"When a man takes a woman and has relations with her.\u8221" The verse juxtaposes {\i yikach} (taking/betrothal) with {\i u\u8217'be\u8217'alah} (consummation), implying that a valid {\i kiddushin} must be one capable of leading to {\i bi\u8217'ah}: {\i kiddushin hamesurin l\u8217'biah havu kiddushin} \u8212- only a {\i kiddushin} that is \u8220"given over\u8221" to {\i bi\u8217'ah} constitutes {\i kiddushin}. {\i Kiddushin she\u8217'einam mesurin l\u8217'biah, lo havu kiddushin}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafot Nuance\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot raises an obvious difficulty with Rava\u8217's principle: according to Rava, should {\i kiddushin} in cases of {\i issurei lavin} \u8212- prohibitions carrying lashes rather than {\i karet}, such as a {\i kohen} marrying a {\i grushah} or a {\i kohen gadol} marrying a {\i widow} \u8212- not also fail, since these unions cannot be permissibly consummated? Yet the {\i gemara} elsewhere is clear that such {\i kiddushin} takes effect.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 To resolve this, Tosafot refines the principle. The operative category of {\i kiddushin she\u8217'einam mesurin l\u8217'biah} is not simply any {\i kiddushin} where {\i bi\u8217'ah} happens to be prohibited. Rather, it is specifically a case where it is the act of {\i kiddushin itself} that creates the {\i issur bi\u8217'ah}. In the case of two sisters: before any {\i kiddushin} occurs, there is no prohibition on having relations with either woman. It is only because he performed (or attempted) {\i kiddushin} with one of them that the other becomes prohibited as his wife\u8217's sister. The {\i kiddushin} generates the very {\i issur} that makes consummation impossible.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 By contrast, in the case of a {\i kohen} and a {\i grushah}, the {\i issur} preexists and is independent of the {\i kiddushin}. The woman\u8217's status as a {\i grushah} was established before he ever betrothed her; his act of {\i kiddushin} did not create the prohibition. In such cases, the {\i kiddushin} takes effect despite the prohibition.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This also clarifies a distinct scenario: suppose a woman might be a man\u8217's sister but it cannot be determined \u8212- perhaps because her mother\u8217's relationship history is unknown. If he were to perform {\i kiddushin} with her, would that be a {\i safek kiddushin} or null {\i kiddushin she\u8217'einam mesurin l\u8217'biah}? According to Rashi\u8217's broader reading, it might be categorized as {\i einam mesurin l\u8217'biah} since he can never permissibly have relations with her. According to Tosafot\u8217's refinement, it would be a {\i safek kiddushin} \u8212- because the prohibition on his potential sister preexists and is wholly independent of his act of {\i kiddushin}. His betrothal did not create the {\i issur}; it was always there. Therefore Tosafot\u8217's principle does not apply, and she would be {\i safek mekudeshet}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Five Women Story\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Case Setup\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now tests Rava\u8217's principle against the {\i mishnah} itself. {\i Tnan: hamkadesh isha u\u8217'vitah o isha v\u8217'achotah k\u8217'achat, einan mekudashot} \u8212- both are not married. The {\i mishnah} implies: {\i ha achat} \u8212- if he married only one, without specifying which, she would be {\i mekudeshet}. But this seems to contradict Rava directly: such a {\i kiddushin} cannot lead to {\i bi\u8217'ah} and should therefore be null. {\i Tiufta d\u8217'Rava} \u8212- this is a decisive refutation of Rava\u8217's position.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah}\u8217's concluding case involves five women including two sisters, a basket of figs, {\i shemitah} produce, and the declaration {\i harei kulchen mekudashot li be-kalkalah zo} \u8212- \u8220"All of you are betrothed to me with this basket,\u8221" accepted by one woman on behalf of all. The Sages ruled that the sisters are not married \u8212- implying the other three are.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava\u8217's Reading\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava responds: {\i v\u8217'letameich eima seifa} \u8212- let us examine the end of the {\i mishnah} carefully. The case in which the three non-sisters are {\i safek mekudashot} is the {\i mishnah}\u8217's conclusion. But if we take the declaration at face value \u8212- {\i harei kulchen}, all five of you \u8212- then this is the case of {\i at v\u8217'chamor lo kana}: if one attempts to transfer ownership jointly to a valid recipient and an invalid one (classically, a person and a fetus not yet born), the entire transaction fails for the valid recipient as well, since the package cannot be divided. Similarly here, if he tried to marry all five and it failed for two of them, why would it work for the other three?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Therefore, Rava argues, the {\i mishnah}\u8217's end-case must be read as {\i d\u8217'amar lehu achat mikem} \u8212- he said \u8220"one of you,\u8221" not \u8220"all five of you.\u8221" He was attempting to betroth one unspecified woman from the group of five. In that case, three of the five could be {\i safek mekudashot} \u8212- any one of the three non-sisters might be the intended bride \u8212- while neither sister qualifies.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Position Logic\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This reading creates an asymmetry between the beginning and end of the {\i mishnah}. {\i L\u8217'Rava kashya reisha} \u8212- for Rava, the beginning of the {\i mishnah} is difficult: it says {\i ke-achas}, at the same time, implying the problem is the simultaneity. But according to Rava, even attempting {\i kiddushin} with one unspecified sister \u8212- without simultaneity \u8212- should also fail, since it would be {\i kiddushin she\u8217'einam mesurin l\u8217'biah}. The beginning of the {\i mishnah} seems to identify the {\i ke-achas} as the operative defect.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i V\u8217'l\u8217'Abaye kashya seifa} \u8212- for Abaye, the end of the {\i mishnah} is difficult. Reading the end as \u8220"one of you,\u8221" Abaye must explain why neither sister would be married even in that case, since according to Abaye, {\i kiddushin she\u8217'einam mesurin l\u8217'biah} does take effect \u8212- the sisters should at least be {\i safek mekudashot}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Abaye\u8217's Reading\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Abaye metaretz l\u8217'taameih, Rava metaretz l\u8217'taameih} \u8212- each resolves the other\u8217's difficulty according to his own position. Abaye explains the {\i mishnah} as follows: {\i hamkadesh isha u\u8217'vitah o isha v\u8217'achotah k\u8217'achat, einan mekudashot} \u8212- only if he attempted to marry both at once do neither become married. {\i Achat me\u8217'isha u\u8217'vitah o me\u8217'isha v\u8217'achotah, mekudeshet} \u8212- if he attempted to marry only one, without specifying which, she is in principle {\i mekudeshet} (as a {\i safek}). However, {\i ve\u8217'im amar hara\u8217'uya l\u8217'vi\u8217'ah titkadesh li} \u8212- if he explicitly stipulated that the one who is permissible to him should be betrothed, then {\i eina mekudeshet}, since there is no way to know who qualifies, and this {\i kiddushin she\u8217'eino masur l\u8217'vi\u8217'ah, d\u8217'hava lei kiddushin she\u8217'eino masur l\u8217'vi\u8217'ah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 For the five women case, Abaye reads the man\u8217's statement as implicitly including {\i hara\u8217'uya li mikem titkadesh li} \u8212- one of you who is fit for me should be betrothed to me. Since the sisters are not \u8220"fit\u8221" for him in the sense that there is no {\i bi\u8217'ah} possible, they are excluded, and the ruling that they are not married is explained. This reading is something Abaye inserts into the text because Rava\u8217's reading of the {\i mishnah} forced the case into the \u8220"one of you\u8221" framework \u8212- Abaye simply adds the further specification.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava, in turn, explains the beginning of the {\i mishnah}: {\i hamkadesh achat me\u8217'isha u\u8217'vitah o achat me\u8217'isha v\u8217'achotah, na\u8217'aseh k\u8217'mi shekidesh isha u\u8217'vitah v\u8217'isha v\u8217'achotah k\u8217'achat} \u8212- attempting to betroth one unspecified member of a forbidden pair is legally equivalent to attempting to betroth both simultaneously. Even though in reality he only tried to betroth one, the indeterminacy makes it as if he tried to betroth both at once, and therefore neither is married.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Unspecified Daughter\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Baraita Rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} brings a further challenge to Rava from a {\i baraita}: {\i hamkadesh et bito stam} \u8212- if a man betroths off \u8220"my daughter\u8221" without specifying which daughter. {\i Eina bogeres bichlal} \u8212- his adult daughters are obviously not included, since a father has no legal authority to betroth an adult daughter on her behalf. {\i Haketanot bichlal} \u8212- the minor daughters, however, are included; the {\i baraita} implies that one of the minor daughters is {\i safek mekudeshet}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is a direct challenge to Rava. If there are multiple minor daughters and the father did not specify which one he was marrying off, this is structurally identical to the two-sisters case: {\i kiddushin she\u8217'ein mesurin l\u8217'vi\u8217'ah ninhu} \u8212- the groom can never know which daughter he married, and therefore can never permissibly have relations with any of them. {\i U\u8217'visyufta deRava} \u8212- this appears to be a decisive refutation of Rava.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava\u8217's Limit\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava responds: {\i hacha bemai askinan keshein sham ela gedola u\u8217'ktana} \u8212- the {\i baraita} addresses a case where there is only one adult daughter and one minor daughter. In that scenario, it is obvious that the {\i kiddushin} was accepted for the minor daughter, since the father has no authority over the adult. There is no ambiguity whatsoever; the groom knows exactly whom he married. This is entirely unlike the two-sisters case.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} challenges: the {\i baraita} uses the plural {\i bogeros} and {\i ketanot}, implying multiple daughters of each type. Rava deflects: {\i mai bogeros bogeros de\u8217'alma} \u8212- the plural \u8220"adult daughters\u8221" is a general formulation referring to the category of adult daughters that any person might have, not to this particular father having many adult daughters. In this case, we read it as: his one adult daughter is excluded, and his one minor daughter is the one who is {\i safek mekudeshet}. The {\i gemara} then asks, {\i ei hachi mai lemimra} \u8212- if there is only one minor daughter, what is the {\i chiddush}? It is entirely obvious.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shaliach Scenario\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} suggests a more nuanced reading: {\i hacha bemai askinan deshavisa shaliach} \u8212- the {\i baraita} addresses a case where the adult daughter had appointed her father as her {\i shaliach}, her agent, to arrange her {\i kiddushin}. In that case, one might think the father would betroth off the adult daughter first: after all, he is acting as her {\i shaliach}, and perhaps his acceptance of the {\i kiddushin} was on her behalf. The ambiguity is real \u8212- was this the minor daughter (his primary charge) or the adult daughter (whose {\i shaliach} he is)? {\i Mahu d\u8217'teima ki mekabel kiddushin adaita dideihu ka mekabel} \u8212- one might think he accepted the {\i kiddushin} with the adult daughter in mind, since she is older and the pressure to see her settled is perhaps greater.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Priority Rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Ka mashma lan dela shavik inish midi de\u8217'is lei hana\u8217'ah minei} \u8212- the {\i baraita} teaches us that a man does not set aside something from which he derives benefit in order to do something else first. The father benefits financially from the {\i kiddushin} of the minor daughter \u8212- the {\i kesef kiddushin} goes to him in that case. He would therefore not prioritize the adult daughter\u8217's {\i kiddushin}, from which he receives no such benefit, over the minor daughter\u8217's.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then considers: what if the adult daughter said, \u8220"Father, you may keep the {\i kiddushin} money for yourself\u8221"? {\i Mahu d\u8217'teima ki mekabel kiddushin adaita dideihu ka mekabel} \u8212- perhaps even then he might betroth the adult daughter first. The {\i gemara} rejects this: {\i afilu hachi lo shavik inish mitzvah deramya alei ve\u8217'avid mitzvah dela ramya alei} \u8212- even so, a person does not set aside a {\i mitzvah} that is incumbent upon him and perform instead one that is not. The father bears a primary obligatory {\i mitzvah} to marry off his minor daughters; for his adult daughter, any help he provides is supererogatory. He would therefore always marry off the minor daughter first. The result is that the {\i baraita} does not challenge Rava \u8212- and the debate between Abaye and Rava over {\i kiddushin she\u8217'einam mesurin l\u8217'biah} will continue into the next session.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Kiddushin 52\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rava\u8217's Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava\u8217's Principle\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The sugya continues from where we left off on 51b, in the middle of Rava\u8217's position regarding {\i kiddushin she\u8217'eina mesura l\u8217'via} \u8212- a betrothal that cannot ultimately lead to consummation. Although {\i kiddushin} ordinarily precedes {\i nisuin} by a considerable period, Rava holds that if the {\i kiddushin} can never translate into a permissible marital relationship, it is not a valid {\i kiddushin} to begin with.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A clarification is necessary at the outset. As Tosafos already notes, there are prohibited relationships \u8212- {\i isurei lavin}, such as a {\i kohen} marrying a {\i gerusha} \u8212- where the couple is forbidden from having relations, yet the {\i kiddushin} takes effect regardless. Rava\u8217's principle is confined to {\i isurei kareis}: if the relationship would carry the penalty of {\i kareis}, everyone agrees the {\i kiddushin} does not take effect at all. The contested terrain is the case where the {\i kiddushin} itself would be valid but uncertainty about the identity of the woman makes consummation perpetually impossible.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two Sisters Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The paradigmatic example is a man who betroths one of two sisters without knowing which one he has married. Rava\u8217's contention is that in such a case, neither woman requires a {\i get mi\u8217'safek}. Since he can never have relations with either \u8212- doing so might mean sleeping with his wife\u8217's sister \u8212- the {\i kiddushin} is fundamentally defective. The doubt itself prevents the {\i kiddushin she\u8217'eina mesura l\u8217'via} from ever taking effect.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Abaye\u8217's View\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye disagrees with Rava\u8217's position. Thus far, the evidence presented in the sugya has consistently cut against Rava, though Rava has managed to respond to each textual challenge. The Gemara now continues pressing the point.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Mi Sheyeish Lo\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Scenario Setup\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara introduces a {\i braisa}: {\i mi sheyeish lo shtei kitei banot mi\u8217'shtei nashim} \u8212- a man who has two sets of daughters from two wives, each wife having borne him multiple daughters. The man declares that he married his older daughter \u8212- {\i kidashti et biti hagdola} \u8212- to a particular man, but he cannot remember which daughter he meant. Several possibilities present themselves: he may have meant the oldest among the children from his first wife, or the older one from his second wife\u8217's children, or even a younger daughter from the first wife who is nonetheless older than the oldest daughter of the second wife.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Get Requirements\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The ruling \u8212- this is the position of Rabbi Meir \u8212- is that {\i kulan asurot}: all of these daughters are potentially married to this man and require a {\i get}, with the single exception of the youngest daughter of the second wife, {\i katanat sheba-ktanot}, whom the father could not possibly have meant when he said \u8220"my older daughter.\u8221"\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This case seems to pose a strong challenge to Rava. Here is a situation precisely analogous to marrying one of two sisters \u8212- the {\i choson} cannot have relations with any of the daughters because whichever one is truly his wife, the others are potentially her sisters. Yet the {\i kiddushin} is treated as taking effect {\i mi\u8217'safek}. If Rava were correct, none of them should require a {\i get} at all.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Initial Knowledge\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara deflects the proof: {\i hacha b\u8217'mai askinan \u8212- keshehukru u\u8217'levasof nisarvu}. The case is not one where the father never knew which daughter he intended. Rather, at the moment of {\i kiddushin} he knew precisely which daughter he had in mind, and only afterward did he forget. The {\i kiddushin} therefore took effect validly at the moment it was performed \u8212- when it was {\i mesura l\u8217'via} \u8212- and the subsequent confusion does not retroactively undo it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Linguistic Proof\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara adds a textual confirmation: {\i deika nami d\u8217'katani eini yodea} \u8212- the {\i braisa} uses the first-person \u8220"I do not know,\u8221" meaning the father currently does not know, rather than the impersonal {\i eino yaduia}, \u8220"it is not known\u8221" \u8212- which would indicate it was never known. This linguistic precision supports the interpretation that knowledge existed initially and was subsequently lost.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Yose Contrast\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But if so, the Gemara asks, what is the {\i chiddush} of the {\i braisa}? If the father once knew and merely forgot, it seems obvious that all the daughters would require a {\i get}. The Gemara answers: {\i le\u8217'apokei mid\u8217'bei Rabbi Yose}. The {\i chiddush} is that Rabbi Meir disagrees with Rabbi Yose\u8217's position.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Allowing Doubt\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yose would reason that if a person says \u8220"my older daughter\u8221" and that is the only detail he can recall, we assume he meant it in the most unambiguous sense \u8212- the oldest of all his daughters. The reasoning is {\i lo machis inish nafshei li\u8217'sefeika}: a person does not allow himself to enter into such a state of doubt. If \u8220"older daughter\u8221" were ambiguous in your own mind, you would have been more precise in your formulation so you could remember. The very fact that you remember only \u8220"older daughter\u8221" implies you meant it to refer to one clearly identifiable person.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Meir rejects this assumption. {\i Ka mashma lan demachis inish nafshei li\u8217'sefeika} \u8212- people do in fact allow themselves to use loose formulations and wind up in precisely this kind of doubt. Therefore, all the daughters remain candidates, and all require a {\i get} save the youngest of the youngest. That is the {\i chiddush}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 One of Two Sisters\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Marriage Scenario\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara presents another challenge: {\i mi shekidesh achas mi\u8217'shtei achayot v\u8217'eino yodeia eizeh kidesh} \u8212- a man married one of two sisters but does not know which one he married. Again, we are invited to read this as a case where he never knew, which would make it a direct refutation of Rava.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Get Solution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The ruling is {\i nosein get lazo v\u8217'get lazo} \u8212- he must give a {\i get} to each sister, since each one is in a state of {\i safek} betrothal to him. This appears once more to demonstrate that {\i kiddushin she\u8217'eina mesura l\u8217'via} takes effect.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Knew Then Forgot\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara again deflects: {\i hacha b\u8217'mai askinan \u8212- keshehukru levasof v\u8217'nisrefu}. Once more, the case involves a situation where the identity was known at the outset and subsequently forgotten. The linguistic indicator is the same: {\i eini yodea} rather than {\i eino yaduia}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then asks again: if that is so, what is the {\i chiddush}? The answer is: {\i seifa itzterichah lei} \u8212- the {\i braisa} is needed for what follows, namely the complex laws of {\i chalitza} and {\i yibum} that arise if the husband dies without the confusion having been resolved.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Yibum/Chalitza\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Death Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i braisa} continues: if this man \u8212- who was married {\i mi\u8217'safek} to one of two sisters \u8212- dies, and he has one surviving brother, that brother must perform {\i chalitza} with both women, since one of them is his brother\u8217's widow and requires either {\i yibum} or {\i chalitza}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Multiple Brothers\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If there were two brothers, the ruling is {\i echad choleitz v\u8217'echad miyabeim} \u8212- one brother performs {\i chalitza} and the other may perform {\i yibum}. Furthermore, if they both already went ahead and married both sisters \u8212- {\i im kadmu v\u8217'kanesu} \u8212- we do not remove them from the marriage.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Achot Zekukato\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Why can a single brother not simply perform {\i yibum} with one of the sisters directly, without first performing {\i chalitza} for the other? Because before {\i chalitza} is performed, both brothers maintain a {\i zikah} \u8212- a levirate bond \u8212- to both women. If a brother were to perform {\i yibum} with one sister while that {\i zikah} remains intact, he might be marrying {\i achot zekukato} \u8212- the sister of a woman to whom he is already quasi-bonded. That is prohibited.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The mechanics of two brothers work as follows. Suppose brother A performs {\i chalitza} for sister 1. This removes the {\i zikah} that both brothers held toward her. Brother B, who did not himself perform the {\i chalitza}, has no {\i achot chalutzato} issue. He is therefore free to perform {\i yibum} with sister 2. Since it was brother A who did the {\i chalitza}, sister 1 is {\i achot chalutzato} only with respect to brother A \u8212- not to brother B. Brother B\u8217's {\i yibum} with sister 2 is therefore unproblematic.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If, however, the brothers had not first done {\i chalitza} and both simply proceeded to marry the two sisters \u8212- {\i kinsu} \u8212- the fact that {\i achot zekukato} is only a {\i d\u8217'rabbanan} prohibition, combined with the fact that the {\i zikah} dissipates once the marriages are performed, means we allow the marriages to stand after the fact.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Already Married\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The principle established is: once they are already married, {\i ein motzi\u8217'in osam mi\u8217'yadam} \u8212- we do not remove them from their marriages.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Proper Sequence\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The proper sequence is therefore explicit: {\i davka michlatz v\u8217'hadar yabumi} \u8212- specifically, {\i chalitza} must precede {\i yibum}. If one brother does {\i yibum} first and only then the other does {\i chalitza}, it does not work, {\i d\u8217'ka paga b\u8217'achot zekukato} \u8212- because the brother doing {\i yibum} before the {\i chalitza} was performed would be engaging with the sister of a woman to whom he still has a {\i zikah}. This is the genuine {\i chiddush} of the passage \u8212- a {\i halacha} in the laws of {\i yibum}, not a ruling about {\i kiddushin she\u8217'eina mesura l\u8217'via}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Two Men Two Sisters\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mixed Marriage\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara moves to yet another challenge: {\i shnayim shekidshu shtei achayot v\u8217'zeh eino yodeia eizo kiddesh v\u8217'zeh eino yodeia eizo kiddesh} \u8212- two men, who are not brothers, each married one of two sisters. Each man married one sister and each does not know which sister he married. Once again, the assumption is that this means the men never knew.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Get Distribution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The ruling is that each man must give a {\i get} to both women. Man A might be married to Rachel or to Leah, so he must give a {\i get} to each. Similarly for Man B. Rachel and Leah cannot remarry until they have received a {\i get} from each man, since either one might be married to either man. Each man thus gives two {\i gittin}, one to each of the two women.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Once more the Gemara answers that this too is a case of {\i keshehukru levasof v\u8217'nisarvu} \u8212- initially known, subsequently forgotten. The {\i seifa itzterichah lei} \u8212- and the real novelty of the ruling emerges in the more complex scenarios that follow.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Unequal Brothers\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If both men die, and each leaves a surviving brother, each brother must perform {\i chalitza} for both women. But the {\i braisa} then introduces an asymmetric case: one man left one brother, and the other left two brothers. The single brother \u8212- let us call him B\u8217' \u8212- performs {\i chalitza} for both women and thereby removes himself entirely from the scene. Only once B\u8217' has stepped off entirely are the two brothers of the other man \u8212- A\u8217' and A\u8217'\u8217' \u8212- in a position analogous to the earlier case of two brothers with two sisters: one performs {\i chalitza} and one performs {\i yibum}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sequence Req.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The sequence is critical. B\u8217' must first perform {\i chalitza} for both women before A\u8217' and A\u8217'\u8217' can proceed with their {\i chalitza} and {\i yibum}. If A\u8217' and A\u8217'\u8217' were to act before B\u8217' performed his {\i chalitza}, one of the two women would be a {\i yevamah} \u8212- a levirate widow obligated to either B\u8217' \u8212- who has not yet released her. Marrying her before that {\i chalitza} is performed constitutes the prohibition of {\i yevamah la\u8217'shuk} \u8212- a levirate widow going out into the marketplace. Only after B\u8217' has performed {\i chalitza} for both women can the remaining two brothers proceed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Once again, the Gemara notes: {\i aval yabumei v\u8217'hadar michlatz lo} \u8212- if the two brothers of the first man were to perform {\i yibum} and {\i chalitza} before B\u8217' removes himself, it would not work, {\i d\u8217'ka paga b\u8217'yevamah la\u8217'shuk} \u8212- because one of these women is still awaiting her {\i chalitza} from B\u8217'. The {\i halacha} being taught here is the required sequence in a complex {\i yibum} case \u8212- not a ruling about {\i kiddushin she\u8217'eina mesura l\u8217'via}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Five/Five Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Non-Specific Marriage\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now arrives at what it treats as decisive. It cites a teaching of {\i Tiviumi}: one man has five sons, another has five daughters. The father of the sons declares: {\i achas mi\u8217'bnotecha mekudeshet l\u8217'echad mi\u8217'banai} \u8212- one of your daughters is married to one of my sons.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Get Requirements\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The ruling: {\i kol achas v\u8217'achas tzrichah chamishah gittin} \u8212- each of the five daughters requires five {\i gittin}, one from each of the five sons. Since no one knows which daughter was married to which son, every daughter is in a state of {\i safek} betrothal to every son.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Death Adjustment\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If one of the sons dies, each daughter now requires four {\i gittin} from the surviving sons and {\i chalitza} from one of them \u8212- since she might have been betrothed to the deceased son, triggering levirate obligation. Crucially, she does not need {\i chalitza} from all four surviving brothers \u8212- any one brother performing {\i chalitza} suffices. One brother thus both gives a {\i get} (as he might be her husband) and performs {\i chalitza} (as he might be her deceased brother-in-law\u8217's {\i yavam}).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Non-Specific Language\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What makes this case decisive is the precise language of the declaration: {\i achas mi\u8217'bnotecha l\u8217'echad mi\u8217'banai} \u8212- one of your daughters to one of my sons. The statement is non-specific from the outset. There is no question of \u8220"initially knew and then forgot.\u8221" The marriage was constituted in entirely unspecified terms \u8212- and it takes effect. This directly refutes Rava: even {\i kiddushin she\u8217'eina mesura l\u8217'via}, formed when the identity of the parties was never specified and consummation could never take place with any particular woman, is nonetheless valid.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava Refuted\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara declares: {\i tiyuvta d\u8217'Rava tiyuvta} \u8212- Rava is definitively refuted. As a general rule in {\i Shas}, the formulation {\i tiyuvta d\u8217'peloni tiyuvta} signals a complete rejection of that position and a ruling against it. Only on rare occasions does the Gemara say {\i tiyuvta v\u8217'hilchasa} \u8212- a refutation followed nonetheless by a ruling in keeping with the refuted position \u8212- and that itself prompts the Gemara to ask how such a thing can be. Here, the rejection is unambiguous.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 YAL KGAM Acronym\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then states: {\i v\u8217'hilchata k\u8217'vosei d\u8217'Abaye b\u8217'Ya\u8217'al Kegam} \u8212- although we generally rule like Rava in disputes with Abaye, there are six cases where we rule like Abaye, and they are encoded in the acronym {\i Ya\u8217'al Kegam}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i yud} stands for {\i yeiush shelo mi\u8217'da\u8217'as} \u8212- the question treated in the second chapter of Bava Metzia of whether giving up hope of recovering a lost object, had the owner known it was lost, constitutes valid {\i yeiush} even if he did not consciously despair. The {\i ayin} stands for {\i eid zomem} \u8212- whether a witness who is rendered a {\i zomem} is disqualified retroactively from the time he gave his testimony, not merely from the time the counter-witnesses testified against him. The {\i lamed}, though there is some debate about this position, stands for {\i lechi ha\u8217'omed mei\u8217'eilav}. The {\i kuf} \u8212- the case before us \u8212- stands for {\i kiddushin she\u8217'eina mesura l\u8217'via}: we follow Abaye that such a {\i kiddushin} takes effect. The {\i gimmel} stands for {\i gilluy da\u8217'as b\u8217'get}, the question of whether implicit indications of intent are operative even without explicit statement \u8212- a matter discussed in the context of {\i gittin} and the principle of {\i devarim she\u8217'belev}. The {\i mem} stands for {\i mumar ochel neveilot l\u8217'tei\u8217'avon} \u8212- whether someone who transgresses a particular prohibition due to {\i yetzer hara} rather than as an ideological rejection of {\i halacha} is disqualified as a witness. That is Rashi\u8217's account of the six; Tosafos offers a slightly different list.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is worth noting, as Tosafos observes at the conclusion of his discussion of {\i Ya\u8217'al Kegam}, that there are other cases where the ruling follows Abaye against Rava \u8212- such as {\i techilaso bi\u8217'fshia v\u8217'sofo b\u8217'ones} \u8212- that are not included in the acronym. Tosafos resolves this by citing the {\i Halachos Gedolos}, who suggests that the {\i hilchata} ruling in that case was not part of the original text of the Gemara but was appended by the {\i Rabbanan Savorai} or the {\i Geonim} after the {\i chatimas ha\u8217'Shas}. This is a broader phenomenon: certain {\i hilchata} rulings that appear embedded in the Aramaic of the Gemara were actually added by post-{\i siyum} editors whose glosses were subsequently absorbed into the received text. The fact that some rulings following Abaye are not in {\i Ya\u8217'al Kegam} is thus explained as those rulings having been formulated after the original editing of the Talmud.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One final structural observation: the father of the five daughters could marry off his daughters to one of the five sons because he has the legal right to betroth his daughters. But the father of the five sons \u8212- what authorizes him to participate? One must say that each son appointed his father as his {\i shliach}: whichever woman you choose for me, I accept.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Five Women/Figs\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Marriage Scenario\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now returns to the {\i ma\u8217'aseh} \u8212- the incident involving five women and the basket of figs that opens the mishnah. A man took figs and used them to attempt to betroth all five women simultaneously, with two sisters among the group. Rav declares: {\i shema mina mimatnisin arba} \u8212- our mishnah teaches four {\i halachos}. However, he adds: {\i v\u8217'nakad Rav b\u8217'yadei tlas} \u8212- he only has clear rulings for three of them, because the mishnah\u8217's teaching regarding the fourth remains ambiguous to him.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shemita Works\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The first {\i halacha}: {\i hamekadesh b\u8217'peiros shevi\u8217'is mekudeshes} \u8212- betrothal effected with the fruit of {\i shemita} is valid. The {\i chiddush}, as Rashi explains, lies in the prohibition against conducting business with {\i shemita} produce \u8212- {\i v\u8217'hayesa shabbat ha\u8217'aretz lachem le\u8217'ochla}, the Torah specifies the fruit is for eating. One might have thought that using {\i shemita} fruit as the medium of a transaction renders the {\i kiddushin} invalid, or that it constitutes prohibited commerce. The mishnah teaches that even so, the transaction is effective, and this use does not carry the characteristics of prohibited {\i s\u8217'chorah} with {\i shemita} fruit.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 No Stolen Goods\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The second {\i halacha}: {\i kidsha b\u8217'gezel eina mekudeshes} \u8212- betrothal with stolen property does not take effect. Since the man picked the women\u8217's figs and returned them as the medium of {\i kiddushin}, the only reason it worked was that it was {\i shemita} \u8212- when the fruit was {\i hefker} and he was legally entitled to take it. Had it not been {\i shemita}, returning their own figs to them would not have constituted valid {\i kesef kiddushin}, even though he stole the goods from the very women he wished to marry.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Woman as Agent\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The third {\i halacha}: {\i ishah na\u8217'asis shliach la\u8217'chaverta} \u8212- a woman can serve as an agent for another woman to receive {\i kesef kiddushin} on her behalf, {\i afilu b\u8217'makom shena\u8217'asis lah tzara} \u8212- even in a case where the agency results in the woman becoming her co-wife. One might have thought that implicit consent to serve as {\i shliach} could not be assumed in such a circumstance, particularly since the woman accepted the {\i kiddushin} for herself at the same time without explicitly declaring herself an agent for the others. The mishnah teaches that she does serve as their agent nonetheless.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ambiguous Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The fourth matter: the mishnah presumably has something to say about {\i kiddushin she\u8217'eino masur l\u8217'via}, since two of the women were sisters. However, Rav cannot determine what the mishnah is teaching on this point because the case admits two readings. If the man was attempting to marry both sisters simultaneously, then the mishnah\u8217's ruling that they are not married reflects the straightforward prohibition against marrying two sisters \u8212- not any principle about {\i kiddushin she\u8217'eino masur l\u8217'via}. In that reading, the mishnah would imply that ordinary {\i kiddushin she\u8217'eino masur l\u8217'via} \u8212- where only one sister was intended \u8212- would in fact work, consistent with Abaye. Alternatively, if the man was attempting to marry each sister separately without knowing which was which, then the sisters being unmarried would support Rava\u8217's position. Because the mishnah is susceptible to both readings, Rav cannot extract a clear ruling from it about {\i kiddushin she\u8217'eino masur l\u8217'via}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav\u8217's Inference\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What Rav does extract \u8212- and what he presents as the mishnah\u8217's clear teaching \u8212- are the three {\i halachos} above. The fourth point remains, as far as Rav is concerned, unresolved.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Gezel in Kiddushin\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Yochanan\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 When Rabbi Zeira ascended to {\i Eretz Yisrael} and related this discussion to Rabbi Yochanan \u8212- the inference that Rav drew from the mishnah about {\i gezel} \u8212- Rabbi Yochanan responded with evident interest: {\i mi amar Rav haki}? Did Rav really say this? The Gemara initially reads this as an expression of surprise that Rabbi Yochanan seemingly agrees. Rabbi Yochanan\u8217's own position is: {\i gazel v\u8217'lo ni\u8217'yashu ha\u8217'be\u8217'alim \u8212- shneihem einam yecholim l\u8217'hakdish} \u8212- if an object was stolen and the owners have not yet despaired of its recovery, neither the thief nor the owner can sanctify it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The thief cannot sanctify it because it is not his. The owner cannot sanctify it \u8212- and here is the genuine {\i chiddush} of Rabbi Yochanan \u8212- because even though the owner retains legal title, the object is no longer under his control, and one cannot perform acts of sanctification over an object that is not in one\u8217's domain. By analogy, one cannot use an object to betroth a woman if one does not own it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Braita Proof\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But the Gemara raises a challenge from a {\i braisa}: {\i kidsha b\u8217'gazel, b\u8217'chamas u\u8217'vigniva, o shechataph sela mi\u8217'yadah v\u8217'kidshah \u8212- mekudeshes} \u8212- if one betrothed a woman with stolen goods, with extorted money, or seized a coin from her hand and used it to betroth her, the betrothal is valid. This seems to directly contradict the principle that {\i kiddushin} with stolen property does not work.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Stolen from Her\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara resolves: {\i hasam b\u8217'gezel deidah} \u8212- that case involves property stolen from the woman herself. The reasoning is that when she accepts the object back from the thief in the context of a declaration of {\i kiddushin}, we interpret her acceptance as a waiver \u8212- she is {\i mochelet} the object to him, allowing him to use it as {\i kesef kiddushin}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Implicit Willingness\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then examines this resolution more carefully. From the {\i braisa} itself, it appears that the opening clause \u8212- {\i kidsha b\u8217'gazel, b\u8217'chamas u\u8217'vigniva} \u8212- is stated in general terms, without specifying that the stolen goods belonged to the woman. Does this not indicate that even generically stolen goods can effect {\i kiddushin}? The Gemara answers: {\i perushei ka mefareish} \u8212- the end of the {\i braisa} is not adding a new case; it is explaining the opening clause. The meaning is: what kind of stolen goods are we speaking of? The case where he seized a coin from her own hand. Generic stolen goods from a third party do not work.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Intent Distinction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now notes a remaining difficulty: our mishnah with the figs, as Rav interpreted it, establishes that even returning stolen goods from the woman herself does not effect {\i kiddushin} \u8212- the figs worked only because it was {\i shemita} and they were {\i hefker}. How then can the {\i braisa} say that seizing her coin and using it for {\i kiddushin} works? The answer: {\i ha d\u8217'shaddikh v\u8217'ha d\u8217'la shaddikh} \u8212- the distinction depends on whether {\i shidduchim}, a prior engagement process, had taken place. If the {\i kiddushin} is being performed in the context of an established betrothal arrangement, the woman\u8217's acceptance of her own goods back can be construed as an implicit waiver \u8212- she intends to let him have them so he can use them for the {\i kiddushin}. Without that prior context, the most natural reading of her taking back the coin is that she merely wants her property returned; there is no basis for inferring that she is extending a waiver. The mishnah\u8217's case of the figs took place without any such prior arrangement, which is why it would not have worked absent {\i shemita}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Ownership Disputes\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Woman Bathing\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now presents a series of practical cases testing the boundaries of {\i kiddushin} effected with another\u8217's property. In the first: a woman was washing her feet in a basin of water. A man came, seized a coin from someone else\u8217's hand, and threw it to her, declaring {\i mekudeshes li} \u8212- be married to me. The case came before Rava.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava ruled: {\i leis d\u8217'chash lah l\u8217'ha d\u8217'Rabbi Shimon} \u8212- we do not rule in accordance with Rabbi Shimon\u8217's position that ordinary {\i gezeila} \u8212- brazen robbery committed openly \u8212- creates a presumption of {\i yeiush ba\u8217'alim}, that the owners have despaired of recovering the stolen property. Rabbi Shimon\u8217's reasoning would be that the bold, public nature of the theft signals to the victim that recovery is hopeless, and thus {\i yeiush} is presumed. If {\i yeiush} had occurred, and the coin then passed to the woman \u8212- constituting {\i shinui reshuyot}, a change of domain \u8212- the combination of {\i yeiush} and {\i shinui reshuyot} might in principle allow the transaction to take effect, since the woman would acquire the coin cleanly and could not be required to return it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The logic deserves elaboration: if there is {\i yeiush} followed by {\i shinui reshuyot}, the original owner\u8217's claim against the woman lapses, even though the thief still owes him compensation. In that case, the woman would be holding a coin she lawfully possesses, and the {\i kiddushin} might be valid. But since we do not follow Rabbi Shimon\u8217's presumption of {\i yeiush} in cases of {\i gezeila}, we cannot assume {\i yeiush} occurred, the coin remains fully stolen property, and the {\i kiddushin} does not take effect.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sharecropper\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 In the second case: a sharecropper betrothed a woman with a bundle of onions taken from the owner\u8217's field before the crop had been formally divided. He came before Rava. Rava said to him: {\i man achlah lach} \u8212- who was {\i mochel} to you, who permitted you to do this? The onions were not yet the sharecropper\u8217's to give. Although he would eventually be entitled to a percentage of the crop, that entitlement only crystallizes at the time of division.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Bundled Crops\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 However, the Gemara qualifies this ruling: {\i v\u8217'hanei milei b\u8217'muza} \u8212- this applies only when the sharecropper took a random handful. If the produce had already been made into bundles \u8212- {\i kisha} \u8212- then one can say: {\i ana shakilna kisha, shakul at kisha} \u8212- I\u8217'll take one bundle, you take one bundle. Where produce has been gathered into units that lend themselves naturally to division, the sharecropper may perform an impromptu mini-division on the spot, taking one bundle as his share. That is regarded as a legally operative preliminary division rather than unauthorized taking. But to grab a handful before any such bundling has occurred is simply stealing from the owner\u8217's undivided crop.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Beer Vessel\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The third case: a beer-maker \u8212- someone who received dates from an owner, fermented them into beer, and was entitled to a portion of the proceeds \u8212- betrothed a woman with a vessel belonging to the owner, which had been used for storing the beer and had absorbed its flavoring. The owner of the beer came, found him, and said: why did you give from that vessel? You should have given from a better one \u8212- apparently the more saturated vessels were more prized. The question was whether the owner\u8217's comment could be construed as retroactive consent to the act.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava ruled: {\i lo amru k\u8217'lach etzel yafot ela bi\u8217'truma bilvad} \u8212- the principle that saying \u8220"you should have taken the better ones\u8221" signals consent applies only in the context of {\i truma}, not here.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Terumah Parallel\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The operative {\i braisa}: if someone entered another person\u8217's field without permission and separated {\i truma} from the produce \u8212- {\i taram shelo bi\u8217'reshut} \u8212- when does the {\i truma} take effect? If the owner is distressed by it, treating it as theft, the {\i truma} is invalid. If not, it is valid. How do we determine his attitude? If the owner arrives, finds the situation, and says {\i k\u8217'lach etzel yafot} \u8212- you should have taken from the better produce \u8212- and there genuinely is better produce available, we take the statement at face value: he is indicating that he would have wanted better {\i truma} taken on his behalf, and this signals acceptance. If there is no better produce, the comment is read as biting sarcasm \u8212- his way of expressing displeasure at having his best grain consecrated without his consent \u8212- and the {\i truma} is invalid. And if the owner himself joins in the harvesting alongside the agent, the {\i truma} is valid regardless.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Terumah vs.\u160 ?Kiddushin\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Why does the \u8220"you should have taken the better ones\u8221" principle apply to {\i truma} but not to {\i kiddushin} with the owner\u8217's vessel? Because in the case of {\i truma}, the owner has an independent obligation to separate {\i truma} from his grain. When he speaks approvingly of what was done \u8212- provided the words can be read as genuine \u8212- we interpret them as indicating he ratifies the act, since it discharges an obligation he was in any event required to fulfill. By contrast, in the {\i kiddushin} case, no one obligated the beer-maker to use the owner\u8217's vessel to betroth a woman. Whatever the owner says in response to finding what happened, we cannot read it as endorsement. {\i Mishum kisufa de\u8217'avad} \u8212- the owner says what he says out of embarrassment for the beer-maker, not wishing to shame him publicly. It would be unreasonable to interpret social courtesy as legal consent.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 No Permission\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The ruling: {\i v\u8217'eina mekudeshes} \u8212- the betrothal is invalid. The beer-maker used property that was not his and for which he had no authorization. Whatever the owner subsequently said cannot transform unauthorized use into permitted use, and therefore the {\i kiddushin} does not take effect.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Kiddushin 53\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Kodshei Kodashim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Opening\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 We pick up today with the {\i Mishnah} on 52b, a remarkably rich one that touches on several distinct categories of sanctified property and the question of whether any of them can serve as the object of {\i kiddushin}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hamikadesh Bechelko\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Mishnah} opens: {\i hamikadesh bechelko bein kodshei kodashim bein kodashim kalim} \u8212- if someone betroths a woman using his portion in either {\i Kodshei Kodashim} or {\i Kodashim Kalim}, she is not betrothed. The broader context of this {\i Mishnah} concerns not merely whether the object used for {\i kiddushin} has a {\i shaveh perutah} in the simple market-value sense, but deeper questions about the nature of ownership and the halachic status of the object being transferred.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 To situate the discussion: a {\i kohen} who performs a {\i kiddushin} using his portion in a {\i Kodshei Kodashim} offering \u8212- for instance, his share of the meat of a {\i chatat}, which may only be eaten by male {\i kohanim} and only within the {\i Azarah} \u8212- is attempting to use that meat as a transactional object. Similarly, {\i Kodashim Kalim}, such as the meat of a {\i shlamim}, which may be eaten throughout Jerusalem by both {\i kohanim} and {\i Yisraelim} of either gender, is likewise the subject of the {\i Mishnah}\u8217's ruling. In both cases: {\i lo kadesh}, the {\i kiddushin} does not take effect.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Temple Layout\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 To appreciate why, it is worth mapping the relevant areas of the {\i Beit HaMikdash}. At the innermost sanctum stood the {\i Kodesh Kodashim} and the {\i Heichal}, fronted by the {\i Ulam}. Proceeding outward, one entered the {\i Azarah}, which comprised the {\i Ezras Kohanim} and the {\i Ezras Yisrael}. Beyond that lay the {\i Ezras Nashim}. The {\i Kodshei Kodashim} may never leave the {\i Azarah} for any purpose; the {\i Kodashim Kalim} may be eaten throughout Jerusalem. These distinctions will bear heavily on later discussions about the practicability of the {\i Mishnah}\u8217's case.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishulchan Gavoa\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara}\u8217's short formulation for why neither category of {\i korban} portions can effect {\i kiddushin} is: {\i mishulchan gavoa ka zachu} \u8212- he is eating off of God\u8217's table. Even though the {\i kohen} has every right to eat that meat, it is not his meat. He has a right to eat {\i from} the meat that came, figuratively, from the {\i Mizbe-ach}; it is not his in any proprietary sense. Accordingly, he cannot use it to betroth a woman.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Maaser Sheni/Hekdesh\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Mishnah} then turns to two additional cases. First, {\i Maaser Sheni}: whether he betrothed the woman with {\i Maaser Sheni} fruit or money knowingly or unknowingly \u8212- {\i bein beshogeg bein bemezid} \u8212- the {\i kiddushin} does not take effect, according to Rabbi Meir. Rabbi Yehuda disagrees: if it was unintentional ({\i shogeg}), it does not work; if it was intentional ({\i mezid}), it does. Second, {\i hekdesh}: if the object used for {\i kiddushin} was sanctified property \u8212- say, an object he had consecrated to the {\i Beit HaMikdash} and subsequently forgotten about \u8212- Rabbi Meir rules that if it was done intentionally ({\i mezid}), the {\i kiddushin} works; if unintentionally ({\i shogeg}), it does not. Rabbi Yehuda rules precisely the reverse: {\i shogeg} works, {\i mezid} does not.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is a striking arrangement. By {\i Maaser Sheni}, Rabbi Yehuda says {\i mezid} is the operative case for valid {\i kiddushin}. By {\i hekdesh}, Rabbi Meir says {\i mezid} is operative. The two are exact mirror images of each other, and a great deal will need to be unpacked about the role of {\i shogeg} and {\i mezid} in each context. But the {\i Gemara} begins with {\i Kodshei Kodashim} and {\i Kodashim Kalim}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One student raises a question about whether the {\i Mishnah} might be discussing {\i Maaser Sheni} money ({\i maaser kesef}) rather than produce \u8212- an interesting point, but not the subject of the current discussion. Another notices a shift in language: the {\i reisha} of the {\i Mishnah} uses {\i lo kadesh} rather than {\i eina mekudeshet}. This linguistic asymmetry is worth noting and will recur.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} raises an initial challenge: perhaps the {\i Mishnah} does not accord with the position of Rabbi Yosi HaGalili. A {\i Baraita} teaches that the verse {\i u-ma\u8217'ala ma\u8217'al b\u8217'Hashem} \u8212- which concerns one who swears falsely to deny possession of his neighbor\u8217's property, thereby using God\u8217's name to perpetrate a theft \u8212- is extended by Rabbi Yosi HaGalili to include {\i Kodashim Kalim}, because {\i Kodashim Kalim} is still considered the property of its owner ({\i mamono}). The argument: if Michael consecrates his sheep as a {\i shlamim}, entrusts it to someone for safekeeping, and that person falsely swears he never received it, is that person liable under this {\i parasha} of false oaths? Rabbi Yosi HaGalili says yes \u8212- because the verse\u8217's inclusion of {\i b\u8217'Hashem} extends the prohibition even to sanctified property, and {\i Kodashim Kalim} of all sanctified categories is the one most retaining its character as the owner\u8217's property ({\i Kodashim Kalim mamon ba-alim hem}).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 According to Rabbi Yosi HaGalili, then, if someone consecrated a sheep as a {\i shlamim} and damages it, he would be liable to pay. If it is still his property, why should the {\i Mishnah}\u8217's ruling that one cannot use it for {\i kiddushin} be in tension with Rabbi Yosi HaGalili\u8217's view?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} answers: {\i ki ka-amar Rabbi Yosi HaGalili mechayev} \u8212- Rabbi Yosi HaGalili only holds the animal belongs to you while it is still alive, prior to being offered. {\i Aval le-achar shechita lo} \u8212- once it has been slaughtered and offered, even according to Rabbi Yosi HaGalili, the principle of {\i mishulchan gavoa ka zachu} applies. One might think that after the slaughter, when one is actually eating and benefiting from the meat, one\u8217's proprietary connection would be stronger rather than weaker. But the {\i Gemara} is saying the opposite: at the moment the animal is offered on the {\i Mizbe-ach}, the owner has given it to God. Whatever portion returns to him in the form of meat is now received from God\u8217's table \u8212- it is God\u8217's bounty extended to the {\i kohen} or the owner, not the owner\u8217's own property reverting to him.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This does leave a conceptual puzzle \u8212- {\i v\u8217'tzarich iyun} \u8212- since intuitively, one\u8217's rights and practical control over the animal seem {\i less} clear before the offering than after, when one is actively eating it. Yet the principle stands: the living animal is still yours, the offered meat is not.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Mishnah} itself supports this reading: {\i deika nami diktani hamekadesh bechelko} \u8212- the {\i Mishnah} says \u8220"his {\i portion},\u8221" implying a share in the already-divided meat, which presupposes that the offering has already taken place. Thus {\i shma mina}, we can confirm the {\i Mishnah} is speaking post-offering.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One further linguistic puzzle: why does the {\i Mishnah} here say {\i lo kidesh} rather than {\i eina mekudeshet}? This question is noted and left for further examination of the different textual readings ({\i girsaot}).\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rabbi Meir vs Yehuda\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sumchos Story\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now presents a striking narrative. The {\i Mishnah} as we have it is anonymous ({\i stam}), and {\i stam Mishnah} is Rabbi Meir. But behind the surface of the {\i Mishnah} lies a live dispute. {\i Tanu rabanan}: after Rabbi Meir\u8217's death, Rabbi Yehuda said to his students, \u8220"Do not allow Rabbi Meir\u8217's students to enter here.\u8221" His reasoning: {\i mipnei shekantarnin hen} \u8212- they are troublemakers. {\i V\u8217'lo lilmod Torah hen ba-im} \u8212- they are not coming sincerely to learn. {\i Ela lekofachni behalachot hen ba-im} \u8212- they come to challenge me, to show off their dialectical virtuosity, not out of genuine pursuit of {\i halacha}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Despite this prohibition, {\i Sumchos} \u8212- one of Rabbi Meir\u8217's most distinguished students \u8212- forced his way in and declared: \u8220"Here is what Rabbi Meir taught me: {\i hamekadesh bechelko bein kodshei kodashim bein kodashim kalim lo kadesh}.\u8221" Once again, notably, {\i lo kadesh} rather than {\i eina mekudeshet}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yehuda\u8217's Response\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yehuda became angry at his own students for having allowed Sumchos in, and he questioned why this case was worth discussing at all. His challenge: {\i Kodshei Kodashim} cannot leave the {\i Azarah}. Women, Rabbi Yehuda presumes, have no business being in the {\i Azarah}. Therefore this entire case \u8212- a woman receiving {\i kiddushin} with {\i Kodshei Kodashim} \u8212- is a pure impossibility, a theoretical construct with no {\i halacha le-ma\u8217'aseh} application whatsoever. Far from reflecting genuine Torah inquiry, raising such a case proves, in Rabbi Yehuda\u8217's view, that Rabbi Meir\u8217's students are simply engaging in intellectual gamesmanship.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yosi Defends Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yosi would not let this stand. He reasoned that if he stayed silent, it would effectively dishonor Rabbi Meir\u8217's entire approach to learning. {\i Tifei Torah mah tehei aleiha} \u8212- what will become of the honor of Torah? He therefore rose to defend the case on three grounds. First: {\i v\u8217'chi ein adam masur lekabel kiddushin l\u8217'vito b\u8217'Azarah?} \u8212- perhaps a man is betrothing off his minor daughter, and {\i he} is the one in the {\i Azarah}, receiving the {\i kiddushin} on her behalf \u8212- no woman need be present at all. Second: {\i v\u8217'ein isha asuya la-asos lah shaliach lekabel kiddusheha b\u8217'Azarah?} \u8212- can a woman not appoint a male agent ({\i shaliach}) to receive her {\i kiddushin} within the {\i Azarah} on her behalf? Third: {\i v\u8217'od dachaka v\u8217'nichnesa mah hi?} \u8212- and if she forced her way in, what would the {\i halacha} be? \u8212- a pointed echo of Sumchos himself having forced his way into the {\i beit midrash}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Women in Azara\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This brief exchange opens a window onto a broader and theologically weighty question: what was the status of women in the {\i Azarah}? The {\i Gemara} seemingly assumes women were not normally present in the {\i Azarah} proper \u8212- that it was essentially male space. {\i Rashi} states explicitly, citing the first chapter of {\i Keilim}, that women cannot enter the {\i Azarah}. {\i Tosafos}, however, sharply disagrees and says {\i Rashi} was not careful here ({\i v\u8217'lo dak}): nowhere is it stated that women are forbidden from entering the {\i Azarah}. {\i Tosafos} marshals several proofs: the {\i Mishnah} in {\i perek Kol HaPesulim} states that a woman may perform {\i shechita} of {\i kodashim} \u8212- which requires being in the {\i Azarah}; and both the {\i Sotah} and the {\i Nezirah} must enter the {\i Azarah} to perform {\i tenufah} with their {\i korbanot}, as they cannot send a representative for that rite.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafos} therefore reformulates the question: the issue is not that a woman cannot enter the {\i Azarah}, but rather that a woman entering the {\i Azarah} for the purpose of getting married there would be unusual and inappropriate \u8212- {\i milsa d\u8217'lo sh\u8217'chicha}, an uncommon and undignified circumstance \u8212- and it is the combination of a woman\u8217's presence and that particular purpose that strikes Rabbi Yehuda as absurd. All the {\i rishonim} and {\i poskim} accept {\i Tosafos}\u8217' position that women do enter the {\i Azarah} when bringing {\i korbanot} and similar occasions; the {\i Ezras Nashim} exists not as an exclusionary barrier but as a designated area. What remains is a question of degree: does a woman require a specific reason \u8212- like bringing a {\i korban} \u8212- to enter the {\i Azarah}, or is only a completely trivial or inappropriate purpose insufficient justification?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This discussion carries real relevance for the question of {\i mechitzah} in synagogues. Some authorities ground the requirement for a {\i mechitzah} in the model of the {\i Beit HaMikdash}, where the {\i Ezras Nashim} was separate from the {\i Ezras Yisrael}. The {\i Gemara} here has been read as suggesting the {\i Azarah} proper was exclusively male space. But even according to {\i Tosafos}\u8217' reading \u8212- which is the accepted view \u8212- there remains a presumption that the {\i Azarah} was predominantly male space during worship, with women entering only for specific ritual needs. Even Rav Moshe Feinstein, in addressing the question of whether a woman may enter an all-male {\i shul} without a {\i mechitzah} to say {\i Kaddish}, permitted it in principle while noting that in {\i Lita} women would do precisely that, standing in the back without incident.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The resolution of the case is in any event clear: Rabbi Yosi\u8217's three answers demonstrate that a woman receiving {\i kiddushin} in the {\i Azarah} is neither impossible nor purely theoretical. A father, an agent, or even an intrepid woman herself could place the scenario squarely within the realm of {\i halacha le-ma\u8217'aseh}, and the discussion is eminently worthwhile.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Biblical Sources\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Baraita Dispute\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i Baraita} at the bottom of 52b records the dispute directly: Rabbi Yehuda says {\i mekudeshet} \u8212- the {\i kiddushin} takes effect; Rabbi Yosi says {\i eina mekudeshet} \u8212- it does not. Rabbi Yehuda thus consistently fights against Rabbi Meir\u8217's position: not only should we be discussing the case, but Rabbi Meir is wrong \u8212- she {\i is} betrothed. Rabbi Yosi sides with Rabbi Meir: {\i eina mekudeshet}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two Interpretations\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yochanan observes: {\i shneihem mikra echad darshu} \u8212- both Rabbi Yehuda and Rabbi Yosi derive their positions from the very same verse: {\i vezeh yiheyeh lecha mikodesh hakodashim min ha-esh} \u8212- \u8220"and this shall be yours from the holy of holies, from the fire\u8221" \u8212- referring to the {\i kohen}\u8217's portion in the {\i chatat} meat. Rabbi Yehuda reads the word {\i lecha} \u8212- \u8220"to you\u8221" \u8212- as granting broad proprietary rights: this is yours, to use as you will, within whatever constraints govern its consumption. If you can find a legitimate use for it beyond eating it yourself, you may do so. Rabbi Yosi, by contrast, reads {\i ke-esh} \u8212- \u8220"like the fire.\u8221" Just as fire is there to consume, this portion too is solely for consumption. You have no ownership over it in any sense beyond eating it; it is {\i mishulchan gavoa}, God\u8217's table.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Nimmnu v\u8217'Gamru\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yochanan then reports, at the top of 53a: {\i nimnu ve-gamru} \u8212- they took a vote and reached a binding conclusion. {\i Hamekadesh bechelko bein kodshei kodashim bein kodashim kalim lo kadesh} \u8212- whether the betrothal was made with a portion in {\i Kodshei Kodashim} or even {\i Kodashim Kalim}, it does not take effect. The principle of {\i mishulchan gavoa} applies across the board: none of it is yours.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav\u8217's Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav disagrees: {\i adayin hi machlokes} \u8212- no vote was taken and no conclusion was reached; Rabbi Meir and Rabbi Yehuda remain in dispute, and there persists a position that one may indeed have a valid proprietary interest in the {\i korban} portions sufficient to effect {\i kiddushin}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye, however, aligns himself with Rabbi Yochanan: {\i ke-vasei de-Rabbi Yochanan mistabra} \u8212- it is more reasonable that Rabbi Yehuda ultimately reversed his position. The evidence for this comes from a {\i Baraita} drawn from the {\i Sifra} \u8212- {\i Toras Kohanim}, the {\i Tannaitic} commentary on {\i Vayikra} \u8212- whose anonymous author ({\i stam Sifra}) is assumed to be Rabbi Yehuda himself.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Sifra Evidence\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Menachot Division\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Baraita} asks: {\i minayin she-ein cholkin mincha kineged zvachim} \u8212- how do we know that when dividing {\i korbanos} among the {\i kohanim} at the end of the day, one may not swap a {\i mincha} offering against animal sacrifices? One might imagine that a {\i kohen} who prefers vegetarian food could say to his colleague: give me your share of the {\i mincha} and take my share of the meat in exchange. The answer derives from the verse: {\i v\u8217'chol mincha asher te\u8217'afeh ba-tanur l\u8217'chol bnei Aharon tihyeh ish ke-achiv} \u8212- every {\i mincha} shall belong to all the sons of Aaron, each equal to his brother. This teaches that each {\i korban} must be divided equally among the {\i kohanim}, and no one may substitute his portion in one type of offering for another\u8217's portion in a different type.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 No Swapping Rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might argue that the prohibition on swapping a {\i mincha} for animal sacrifices is specifically because a {\i mincha} can never fully substitute for an animal sacrifice in most contexts ({\i shelo bau tachteihem be-dalus}). But perhaps one could swap a {\i mincha} for bird sacrifices, since a {\i mincha} does substitute for a bird on the sliding-scale {\i korban} for the destitute. The {\i Sifra} counters this with a separate verse: {\i v\u8217'chol na-aseh ba-markheshet ul\u8217'chol benei Aharon tihyeh} \u8212- another instance of the same formulaic phrase applied to a different type of {\i mincha}, teaching that this type of swapping is also forbidden. The repetition is instructive: each additional invocation of \u8220"to all the sons of Aaron\u8221" comes to exclude a different proposed form of exchange.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 No Cross-Type Division\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Sifra} continues to elaborate. Perhaps one cannot swap a {\i mincha} for animal sacrifices because one involves flour and the other blood ({\i hallu minei damim ve-hallu minei kemachim}), but perhaps bird sacrifices and animal sacrifices could be swapped since both involve blood. Another verse forecloses this. Perhaps {\i menachos} of different types could be swapped among themselves \u8212- surely one baked {\i mincha} could be exchanged for another baked {\i mincha}, since they are in the same category. Yet another verse prohibits this. Perhaps a {\i machavat mincha} \u8212- a hard, fried wafer \u8212- could be exchanged for another {\i machavat mincha}, since they are identical in type. The {\i Sifra} rules even this out, deriving from yet another verse that even within the same sub-genre, you cannot combine all offerings of that type and distribute them globally. Each individual {\i korban} must be divided among the {\i kohanim} equally \u8212- every offering brought that day must be apportioned separately.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 No Ownership Proof\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The logical consequence of this {\i Sifra} passage is decisive. If a {\i kohen} cannot even swap his portion in one {\i mincha} for his colleague\u8217's portion in a different {\i mincha} of exactly the same type, he plainly has no real proprietary rights over his portion. If it were truly {\i his} property, he could do with it as he wishes \u8212- trade it, sell it, use it. The fact that no such latitude exists demonstrates that the {\i korban} portions do not belong to the {\i kohanim} in any meaningful sense of ownership. And if they have no ownership, they cannot use those portions to effect {\i kiddushin}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Since the {\i stam Sifra} is Rabbi Yehuda, this passage demonstrates that Rabbi Yehuda ultimately conceded the point and agreed with Rabbi Meir: {\i chazar bo}. This is what the {\i Gemara} earlier meant by {\i nimnu ve-gamru}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Lechem Hapanim Story\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava challenges this conclusion. He cites a {\i Baraita} that describes the period following the death of Shimon HaTzaddik, when the divine blessing that had rested upon the {\i Lechem HaPanim} \u8212- which had sufficed to satisfy all the {\i kohanim} \u8212- departed. The showbread now produced no miraculous satiety, and each {\i kohen} received only a tiny portion. The {\i Baraita} says the {\i tzenu-im} \u8212- the modest, self-effacing {\i kohanim} \u8212- would withdraw their hands rather than scramble for a slightly larger crumb. {\i Ve-hagargarnim} \u8212- the ravenous ones \u8212- {\i cholkin}, would divide it. Rava initially reads {\i cholkin} as meaning they would subdivide and trade among themselves, implying that the {\i kohanim} did exercise proprietary rights over the {\i Lechem HaPanim} and could arrange exchanges \u8212- which would seem to support Rav\u8217's position that there remains a valid sense of ownership.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chotfim Clarified\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} resolves this by reinterpreting the word {\i cholkin}. {\i Mai cholkin? Chotfin} \u8212- \u8220"dividing\u8221" here does not mean trading or swapping portions across different {\i korbanos}. It means grabbing. The {\i Baraita}\u8217's continuation confirms this: {\i ma\u8217'aseh be-echad she-chataf chelko ve-chelek chaveiro} \u8212- there was an incident where someone grabbed not only his own portion but his colleague\u8217's as well, and he was called {\i ben hamtzan} \u8212- \u8220"the grabber\u8221" \u8212- for the rest of his life. That is what {\i cholkin} describes: jostling for a larger share within the same {\i korban}, not exchanging portions across different offerings.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafos} adds a further nuance: even according to the view that you may not swap portions across different {\i korbanos}, it remains permissible to reassign your portion {\i within} a single {\i korban} \u8212- if you received your share of one {\i mincha} and you choose to give it to a colleague, that is a permissible act of generosity, not a prohibited trade. What is forbidden is substituting your portion in one {\i korban} for your colleague\u8217's portion in a different one. The {\i Lechem HaPanim} story describes no such cross-{\i korban} exchange; it describes pure grabbing within the same offering. Accordingly, it provides no support for the notion that {\i kohanim} have proprietary ownership over {\i korban} portions sufficient to use them for {\i kiddushin}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The conclusion stands: {\i stam Sifra} Rabbi Yehuda, and he says in the clearest possible terms that there is no right of division or exchange with regard to {\i korban} portions \u8212- proving that even Rabbi Yehuda in the end agrees one cannot betroth a woman with them, whether the offering is {\i Kodshei Kodashim} or {\i Kodashim Kalim}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Maaser Sheni Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Meir on Maaser\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Mishnah} states: {\i Maaser Sheni bein beshogeg bein bemezid lo kadesh divrei Rabbi Meir} \u8212- whether knowingly or unknowingly, {\i Maaser Sheni} does not effect {\i kiddushin}, according to Rabbi Meir.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yehuda Disagrees\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yehuda says: if it was done unknowingly ({\i shogeg}), it does not work; if it was done knowingly ({\i mezid}), it does.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kodesh La-Hashem\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Where does Rabbi Meir derive his position? The {\i Gemara} cites Rav Acha son of Rava, speaking in the name of the {\i Gemara} \u8212- meaning in the name of accepted tradition in the {\i beit midrash}: the verse {\i v\u8217'chol maaser ha-aretz mi-zera ha-aretz mi-pri ha-etz la-Hashem hu} \u8212- \u8220"and all the {\i maaser} of the land, from the seed of the land and from the fruit of the tree, belongs to God\u8221" \u8212- together with the characterization {\i kodesh la-Hashem}, \u8220"holy to God.\u8221" The combination means {\i Maaser Sheni} is not yours; you are eating God\u8217's produce. Just as with {\i korban} portions the operative concept was {\i mishulchan gavoa ka zachu}, here the parallel formulation is {\i mamon gavoa hu} \u8212- it is entirely God\u8217's property \u8212- and you cannot use it to betroth a woman.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kodesh Distinctions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} tests this logic by considering several other items that carry similar designations. {\i Trumat ma\u8217'aser} is called {\i trumas Hashem} \u8212- yet the {\i Mishnah} implies that one can use {\i terumah} to effect {\i kiddushin}. The {\i Gemara} distinguishes: {\i trumas Hashem} means it is God\u8217's {\i terumah} in the sense that God commanded it be given, not that it belongs to God. It is given to the {\i kohen} and remains his property. {\i Challah} carries the language {\i titnu la-Hashem} \u8212- \u8220"give it to God\u8221" \u8212- but it lacks the word {\i kodesh}, which would indicate that it is fully God\u8217's. {\i Shevi-it} produce carries the phrase {\i kodesh tihyeh lachem} \u8212- \u8220"it shall be holy to you\u8221" \u8212- but lacks the phrase {\i la-Hashem}. And while one {\i pasuk} equates the Jewish people to {\i terumah} and calls Israel {\i kodesh la-Hashem}, that is metaphorical; the {\i Gemara} notes this usage pertains to the Jewish people, not to actual {\i terumah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might object that the metaphor implicitly confirms that {\i terumah} itself is {\i kodesh la-Hashem}. The {\i Gemara} accepts this logical inference but adds: even granting that {\i terumah} carries the designation {\i kodesh la-Hashem}, the verse by {\i Maaser Sheni} adds the crucial word {\i hu} \u8212- \u8220"it is\u8221" \u8212- specifically: {\i la-Hashem hu}. {\i Ravin Sava} explains in front of Rav that {\i hu} signifies permanence of status \u8212- it remains God\u8217's, with no movement away from that state. The combination of {\i kodesh}, {\i la-Hashem}, and {\i hu} in the {\i Maaser Sheni} verse places it in a uniquely inviolable category of divine ownership. That is Rabbi Meir\u8217's source.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mamon Gavoa\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Thus, according to Rabbi Meir, {\i Maaser Sheni} is {\i mamon gavoa hu} \u8212- completely God\u8217's property, not yours. You may eat it, but eating it does not make it yours in a proprietary sense; you are partaking of God\u8217's {\i maaser}, and you have no right to transfer it to another.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Intent Analysis\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Woman\u8217's Consent\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having established why Rabbi Meir holds {\i Maaser Sheni} never effects {\i kiddushin}, the {\i Gemara} turns to why Rabbi Yehuda draws a distinction between {\i shogeg} and {\i mezid}. Rabbi Yehuda fundamentally disagrees with Rabbi Meir\u8217's characterization of {\i Maaser Sheni} as {\i mamon gavoa}; in Rabbi Yehuda\u8217's view, {\i Maaser Sheni} belongs to the owner. If so, why should the distinction between knowing and unknowing matter at all?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The key insight is that the {\i shogeg/mezid} distinction here is not about the man\u8217's state of knowledge but about the woman\u8217's. If a man betroths a woman with what appears to be an ordinary apple and it turns out to be {\i Maaser Sheni} fruit, she faces a significant burden she never accepted: that apple cannot be eaten at home; it must be brought to Jerusalem. Had she known this, she would not have agreed to receive it as her {\i kiddushin}. We therefore presume \u8212- as a matter of {\i umdena}, an assessment of intent \u8212- that she would never have consented had she understood what she was receiving. Accordingly, when she is in a state of {\i shogeg}, the {\i kiddushin} fails. But if she knows it is {\i Maaser Sheni} and agrees nonetheless, then she has knowingly accepted the conditions \u8212- she is willing to take the apple to Jerusalem \u8212- and the {\i kiddushin} takes effect.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might ask, as a {\i nafka mina} from knowing whose consent is the operative factor: if in practice the woman later says she would not have minded, does that change the outcome? {\i Rashi} notes that this is indeed the practical implication.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Yeimar\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Yeimar offers a clean resolution to the puzzle Rabbi Yochanan raised \u8212- which case involves only one unwilling party, and which involves both? By {\i Maaser Sheni}, according to Rabbi Yehuda, {\i the man has no problem} using his {\i Maaser Sheni} fruit for {\i kiddushin}. On the contrary, he benefits from it: {\i de-mitparka minei} \u8212- he is thereby relieved of the obligation to schlep the fruit to Jerusalem himself. The woman, however, would not have wanted it had she known. Thus {\i shogeg} does not work because of the woman\u8217's presumed non-consent, while {\i mezid} works because she knew and agreed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Me\u8217'ilah Concern\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 By {\i hekdesh}, the analysis is different. Here, {\i traveihu lo nicha lehu} \u8212- neither party would have consented had they known. Why not? {\i Lo nicha lehu de-nafik hekdesh le-chulin al yadeihu} \u8212- neither would have wanted to be the instrument by which consecrated property was transferred to non-sacred use, thereby transgressing the {\i issur me\u8217'ilah}. When the transaction is done unknowingly ({\i shogeg}), we presume retroactively that neither party would have wanted to participate in an act of {\i me\u8217'ilah} \u8212- and so the {\i kiddushin} is void. It is only when done intentionally ({\i mezid}) \u8212- when both parties knew it was {\i hekdesh} and proceeded anyway \u8212- that Rabbi Meir considers the {\i kiddushin} valid, since at that point both parties accepted the transaction with full knowledge of its nature. We will continue tomorrow with further analysis of why {\i shogeg} fails in these two cases and other approaches to understanding the interplay between intent and the underlying halachic status of {\i Maaser Sheni} and {\i hekdesh}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Kiddushin 54\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Meir vs.\u160 ?Yehuda\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Intro: Two positions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The discussion in our {\i daf} actually begins toward the end of 53b, where the {\i Gemara} is working through a {\i Mishnah} that records two distinct disputes \u8212- one concerning {\i ma\u8217'aser sheni} and one concerning {\i hekdesh} \u8212- and the divergent positions of Rabbi Meir and Rabbi Yehuda. By {\i ma\u8217'aser sheni}, Rabbi Meir holds that one can never use it to betroth a woman, because it is {\i mammon gavoh} \u8212- it belongs to God, not to the individual owner. Rabbi Yehuda disagrees in principle and allows it, but rules that the betrothal is invalid in a case of {\i shogeg}, when the parties acted unwittingly. Thus, according to Rabbi Yehuda, the unwitting case is worse. By {\i hekdesh}, the positions are reversed: Rabbi Meir holds that if one betroths a woman with {\i hekdesh} funds knowingly ({\i mezid}), it is effective, but unwittingly ({\i shogeg}) it is not. Rabbi Yehuda again takes the opposite view.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The challenge, then, is to explain what principle underlies each position. The {\i Gemara} frames the question in terms of who would not have wanted the transaction had they known the true nature of the funds. The key distinction is whether it was the woman alone who would have objected, or whether both parties would have objected \u8212- since the presence of a {\i ta\u8217'us}, an error that undermines the consent of both parties, voids the transaction entirely.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yirmiyah\u8217's answer\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yirmiyah\u8217's initial resolution is that for {\i ma\u8217'aser sheni}, only the woman would have objected had she known, because accepting {\i ma\u8217'aser sheni} as her {\i kiddushin} money would obligate her to schlep the funds to Jerusalem before she could benefit from them. The man, however, would not have minded \u8212- if she was willing to accept {\i ma\u8217'aser sheni} and make the journey to Jerusalem, he would be perfectly content to have married her on those terms. Accordingly, the transaction is voided by {\i shogeg} because her consent was compromised.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 By {\i hekdesh}, Rabbi Yirmiyah explains, neither party would have wanted the transaction had they known the funds were {\i hekdesh}. Both parties would have realized they were committing {\i me\u8217'ilah} \u8212- misappropriating consecrated property \u8212- and neither would have wished to be party to that transgression. The woman would not want to be implicated in the violation of {\i hekdesh}, and the man would not want to spend {\i hekdesh} funds in order to contract a betrothal. Since both parties are in {\i ta\u8217'us}, neither one\u8217's consent was genuine, and the transaction is entirely void.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rashi notes a further implication: the relevant question of whose {\i ta\u8217'us} undermines the transaction depends on whom you would ask \u8212- meaning, in principle, one might think these presumptions about unwillingness are case-sensitive and could be rebutted by the actual parties. Nevertheless, the basic framework stands.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yaakov reverses\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now picks up approximately twelve to fifteen lines into 53b, with the position of Rabbi Yaakov, who says {\i ipcha mistabra} \u8212- the opposite makes more sense. Rabbi Yaakov argues that by {\i ma\u8217'aser sheni} as well, both parties would have objected had they known. The woman, as before, would not have wanted the schlep to Jerusalem. But Rabbi Yaakov adds: the man also would not have wanted to give her {\i ma\u8217'aser sheni} had he known, {\i mishum onsa de\u8217'orcha} \u8212- on account of the burden of the journey. Rashi\u8217's initial reading is that perhaps the woman would not be fully betrothed until the {\i ma\u8217'aser sheni} actually arrived in Jerusalem intact and could be consumed, leaving open the possibility that if the funds were lost along the way she would not be {\i mekudeshet}. Tosafot rejects this reading entirely and insists that the betrothal takes effect immediately regardless of what happens subsequently. Rashi himself offers the more persuasive explanation: even if the man bears no formal legal liability if something goes wrong along the way, he would not have wanted to give her something that she could only benefit from after a difficult journey to Jerusalem \u8212- because she would come back to him with legitimate grievances if anything went wrong. She had a {\i schlep}, the funds rotted or were lost, and now she is unhappy with him. The man, therefore, would also have preferred not to initiate such an arrangement.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 So Rabbi Yaakov concludes that by {\i ma\u8217'aser sheni} both parties would have objected, and thus the {\i ta\u8217'us} is bilateral. This leaves him with a more difficult question about {\i hekdesh}: we understand why the woman would not have wanted to be party to a violation of {\i hekdesh} \u8212- she would not want the consecrated property to be profaned {\i al yadah}, through her. But why would the man have objected? Rabbi Yaakov raises the rather striking point that, from the man\u8217's perspective, if it turned out he accidentally used {\i hekdesh} funds and in the process managed to betroth a woman without spending his own money \u8212- well, perhaps he would not have minded all that much. He committed {\i me\u8217'ilah b\u8217'shogeg}, which is unfortunate, but he got a betrothal at no personal cost. The {\i Gemara} notes this candidly: {\i d\u8217'nafka itatta mimeila} \u8212- the woman came out of the {\i me\u8217'ilah}, as it were, at no expense to him.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} itself acknowledges that Rabbi Yirmiyah\u8217's original answer seems the more compelling one \u8212- that by {\i ma\u8217'aser sheni} the problem is fundamentally hers, and by {\i hekdesh} it is a problem for both \u8212- but Rabbi Yaakov\u8217's position is recorded as the reverse. In any case, the underlying logic of both answers is that the transaction fails in {\i shogeg} when we can say that the relevant parties, had they known, would not have wanted to go forward \u8212- because without that genuine {\i da\u8217'as}, no transaction takes effect.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Ta\u8217'us & Kiddushin\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava\u8217's question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 At this point the {\i Gemara} records a question posed by Rava to Rav Chisda. We have established, operating within Rabbi Yirmiyah\u8217's explanation, that the woman is not {\i mekudeshet} when {\i hekdesh} is used {\i b\u8217'shogeg} \u8212- because had the man known, he would not have wanted to spend {\i hekdesh} money, and thus the transaction was a {\i ta\u8217'us} from the start. Since the act was done in error, and had either of them known they would not have proceeded, the entire transaction is treated as if it never took place.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava\u8217's question is: if the woman is not betrothed because the transaction was void on account of {\i ta\u8217'us} \u8212- if no legal act of acquisition occurred \u8212- then how could the money possibly have become {\i chullin}? The man physically handed the woman some funds, but if we say the transaction never legally took effect, then there was no genuine transfer of possession. If there was no legal transfer, then there was no act of {\i me\u8217'ilah}. And if there was no {\i me\u8217'ilah}, the money did not exit the domain of {\i hekdesh} into {\i chullin}. This is Rava\u8217's sharply posed question: {\i ma\u8217'os heichi yeitz\u8217'u l\u8217'chullin} \u8212- how did the money ever become {\i chullin}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Void transaction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The answer given is direct: if the woman is not betrothed, then the transaction never took place. The money changed hands physically, but it was never legally taken into the woman\u8217's possession. Since no valid act of acquisition occurred, no act of {\i me\u8217'ilah} occurred either, and the money does not become {\i chullin}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This response, however, immediately generates a further problem. If one never commits {\i me\u8217'ilah} when spending {\i hekdesh} funds {\i b\u8217'shogeg} \u8212- because in every such case we can say the person would not have wanted to spend the funds had they known, and therefore the transaction is always void \u8212- then it seems to follow that there can {\i never} be {\i me\u8217'ilah} involving the misuse of {\i hekdesh} funds through a spending transaction when the act was unintentional. That is precisely the opposite of the standard presumption about {\i me\u8217'ilah}, which assumes that the classic case of {\i me\u8217'ilah} is {\i b\u8217'shogeg}, the unwitting misuse of consecrated property. How can Rav Chisda\u8217's answer be correct?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Chisda replies: indeed, the same logic applies to a sale. Rav Chiya bar Avin posed this directly: {\i b\u8217'mecher mai} \u8212- what if one used {\i hekdesh} funds to make a purchase without knowing they were {\i hekdesh}? Rav Chisda responds: {\i af b\u8217'mecher lo kanah} \u8212- even in a case of purchase, nothing is acquired. Because the buyer, had he known, would not have wanted to make the transaction, it is treated as void, there was no genuine acquisition, and therefore no {\i me\u8217'ilah} occurs.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is a remarkable position. It implies that {\i me\u8217'ilah} through the spending or transferring of funds can never occur {\i b\u8217'shogeg}, since any such transaction will be retroactively void on the grounds that the party would not have agreed had they known the truth. One might then ask: is there ever {\i me\u8217'ilah b\u8217'shogeg} at all? The answer is yes \u8212- but only in cases where {\i me\u8217'ilah} does not depend on a transaction. If someone ate {\i hekdesh} food without knowing, or anointed themselves with {\i hekdesh} oil, or used {\i hekdesh} oil to light their lamp \u8212- these are cases of directly benefiting from {\i hekdesh} without any transactional element. There, {\i me\u8217'ilah} occurs {\i b\u8217'shogeg}, and the object may exit {\i hekdesh} into {\i chullin} to whatever extent it can. But where the {\i me\u8217'ilah} would consist of transferring ownership of funds through a transaction, {\i shogeg} renders it void and no {\i me\u8217'ilah} takes place.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Storekeeper case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now raises a challenge from a {\i braita} that distinguishes between a {\i chanvani} (storekeeper) and a {\i shulchani} (money changer) \u8212- a {\i braita} presented explicitly as the position of Rabbi Meir. The case runs as follows: I hold {\i hekdesh} funds, having forgotten they are {\i hekdesh}, and I give them to someone to watch for safekeeping. If the recipient is an ordinary {\i baal habayit}, a homeowner, he has no implicit authorization to use the funds regardless of whether I hand them to him loosely or in a sealed bundle. If he uses them on his own initiative, the {\i me\u8217'ilah} is his. However, if I give the funds to a money changer, and I hand them to him loosely \u8212- loose coins given to a money changer signal, by common practice, that he may use them in his business and simply repay me equivalent funds \u8212- then I am considered to have implicitly authorized his use of those funds, and I am the {\i mo\u8217'el}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The debate between Rabbi Meir and Rabbi Yehuda in this {\i braita} concerns the intermediate case: a storekeeper. Storekeepers also typically have loose change available for making change with customers. If I give a storekeeper loose funds and he uses them, is he acting with my implicit authorization \u8212- making me the {\i mo\u8217'el} \u8212- or is he acting on his own, making himself the {\i mo\u8217'el}? Rabbi Meir holds the storekeeper is like the ordinary homeowner ({\i chanvani k\u8217'baal habayit}) and the storekeeper is liable. Rabbi Yehuda holds the storekeeper is like the money changer ({\i chanvani k\u8217'shulchani}) and I am liable.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This {\i braita} poses a direct challenge. The {\i braita} is framed entirely within a scenario of {\i shogeg} \u8212- neither the depositor nor the recipient knows the funds are {\i hekdesh} \u8212- and yet {\i me\u8217'ilah} is treated as occurring. Both Rabbi Meir and Rabbi Yehuda agree that {\i me\u8217'ilah} takes place; they only debate {\i who} committed it. This seems to flatly contradict the position we have been developing in the name of Rabbi Meir, that there can be no {\i me\u8217'ilah b\u8217'shogeg} through a transaction.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} offers a resolution: Rabbi Meir is not speaking in his own voice in this portion of the {\i braita}. He is responding {\i l\u8217'divrav d\u8217'Rabbi Yehuda} \u8212- within the framework of Rabbi Yehuda\u8217's position. As if to say: I, Rabbi Meir, fundamentally hold that no {\i me\u8217'ilah} occurs through a {\i shogeg} transaction because any such transaction is void. But {\i you}, Rabbi Yehuda, who believe that {\i me\u8217'ilah} does occur through spending {\i b\u8217'shogeg} \u8212- at least concede to me that in this case the storekeeper acted on his own authority, like a homeowner. To which Rabbi Yehuda replies: no, even on my view, the storekeeper is like the money changer, and the depositor is responsible. The entire debate about the storekeeper is therefore internal to Rabbi Yehuda\u8217's framework, with Rabbi Meir only arguing within that framework while maintaining his own fundamental position.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} acknowledges this is a strained reading, since the plain meaning of the {\i braita} strongly suggests both opinions accept that {\i me\u8217'ilah} occurs in such a case. Nevertheless, the {\i Gemara} will continue developing this position of Rabbi Meir.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav rejects position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav himself pulls back from this framework. {\i Chazinu al kol tzedadim shel Rabbi Meir} \u8212- we have examined every position of Rabbi Meir from every angle. And we have never found Rabbi Meir articulating the extreme position that {\i hekdesh b\u8217'shogeg ein mitchalel, b\u8217'meizid mitchalel} \u8212- that {\i hekdesh} is only desanctified when the misuse is intentional and never when it is unintentional. This would be the exact inverse of what we understand about {\i me\u8217'ilah}. The classic, fundamental case of {\i me\u8217'ilah} is {\i b\u8217'shogeg}; when you bring the {\i korban me\u8217'ilah}, the entire institution is predicated on unwitting misuse. To say that unintentional misuse of {\i hekdesh} funds through a transaction never constitutes {\i me\u8217'ilah} is, to Rav, an astonishing and unattested position.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is worth noting that Rabbi Meir does have a {\i chiddush} regarding {\i me\u8217'ilah b\u8217'meizid}. As Tosafot observes, Rabbi Meir derives from the {\i parasha} of {\i Sotah} \u8212- {\i ish ki tisteh ishto u-ma\u8217'alah vo ma\u8217'al} \u8212- that {\i me\u8217'ilah} can apply even to intentional violations. The standard ruling is that {\i b\u8217'meizid} the funds do not exit {\i hekdesh} into {\i chullin}; Rabbi Meir says they do. So Rabbi Meir\u8217's position, properly understood, is an expansion of {\i me\u8217'ilah} to include the {\i mezid} case \u8212- not an elimination of {\i me\u8217'ilah} in the {\i shogeg} case. The claim that Rabbi Meir abolishes {\i me\u8217'ilah b\u8217'shogeg} for transactional misuse is, according to Rav, nowhere to be found and would be an incoherent reading.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 How, then, does Rav explain our {\i Mishnah}, in which Rabbi Meir rules that a woman is not betrothed when {\i hekdesh} is used {\i b\u8217'shogeg}? The {\i Gemara} proposes that the {\i Mishnah} is discussing a very specific case: {\i bigdei kehunah she-lo balu} \u8212- the garments of the priesthood that have not yet worn out. If someone attempted to betroth a woman with a priestly garment \u8212- perhaps he was a {\i kohen} who possessed such a cloak \u8212- and did so unwittingly, then Rabbi Meir rules there is no {\i me\u8217'ilah} and consequently no {\i kiddushin}. But this is due to a special exemption specific to {\i bigdei kehunah}, not a general principle about transactional {\i me\u8217'ilah b\u8217'shogeg}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Priestly garments\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Unworn garments\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Why is there a special exemption for {\i bigdei kehunah} from {\i me\u8217'ilah b\u8217'shogeg}? The {\i Gemara} explains: {\i ho\u8217'il v\u8217'nitnu lihanis bahem} \u8212- since {\i kohanim} are given permission to derive benefit from these garments. The garments of the {\i kehunah} are {\i hekdesh}, but {\i kohanim} are authorized to wear them and benefit from them in the course of the {\i avodah}. Because there is a standing allowance for benefit, there is a corresponding latitude that extends to their incidental use.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Heavenly angels\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} then invokes one of the most celebrated principles in all of {\i halacha}: {\i she-lo nitna Torah l\u8217'malachei ha-shareit} \u8212- the Torah was not given to the ministering angels. A {\i kohen} cannot be held to the standard that the instant the {\i avodah} concludes, he must strip off his priestly garments immediately. If he lingered two minutes after the close of the day, still wearing his {\i bigdei kehunah} while chatting with a colleague, it would be absurd to say he committed {\i me\u8217'ilah} for those two minutes of inadvertent benefit. The Torah is given to human beings operating within human norms. Therefore, a general exemption from {\i me\u8217'ilah} applies to {\i bigdei kehunah} when the use is unintentional \u8212- the latitude given for their ordinary and necessary use extends to cover incidental overlap.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This principle \u8212- {\i she-lo nitna Torah l\u8217'malachei ha-shareit} \u8212- appears in a number of halachic contexts, including discussions of microscopic organisms that are halachically invisible. It is always an expression of the same idea: the Torah calibrates its demands to the realities of human life and human perception.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Worn-out distinction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} then investigates this principle by looking at a {\i braita} concerning worn-out priestly garments: {\i kotonos kehunah shebalu, mo\u8217'alin bahem, divrei Rabbi Meir} \u8212- if the priestly cloaks have worn out and are no longer in use, Rabbi Meir says {\i me\u8217'ilah} applies to them. The {\i Gemara} initially wonders whether to read this broadly \u8212- as if there would be {\i me\u8217'ilah} regardless of whether they were worn out or not. The answer is {\i lo, balu davka} \u8212- no, the ruling is specifically about worn-out garments.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The distinction is essential: the entire basis for the exemption from {\i me\u8217'ilah} was that these garments are objects in active use by {\i kohanim}, and the Torah grants a certain latitude in the context of that active use. Once the garments have worn out and are no longer being worn, they are simply property of {\i hekdesh}, removed from active priestly service. Perhaps the {\i Mikdash} will repurpose the fabric, sell the raw material, or use it for wicks. Whatever the case, the kohen\u8217's personal authorization to wear them has lapsed, and the special exemption no longer applies. They revert to the status of standard {\i hekdesh} property, and ordinary rules of {\i me\u8217'ilah} apply.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What, then, does Rabbi Yehuda say about {\i bigdei kehunah} that are still in use? Rashi explains that Rabbi Yehuda would hold that even though there is a standing allowance for {\i kohanim} to benefit from these garments, that allowance was specifically circumscribed: it covers the {\i kohen} wearing his garments too long after the {\i avodah}, but it does not extend to misusing the garment in some entirely different way \u8212- such as using it to betroth a woman. For Rabbi Yehuda, the limited allowance does not create a blanket immunity from {\i me\u8217'ilah} in every other conceivable context of misuse. This, then, is how Rabbi Yehuda and Rabbi Meir diverge on the specific case of {\i bigdei kehunah} within our {\i Mishnah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 New vs.\u160 ?old coins\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now examines an analogous case involving Temple funds: {\i mo\u8217'alin b\u8217'chadatin v\u8217'ein mo\u8217'alin b\u8217'atikin} \u8212- one is liable for {\i me\u8217'ilah} with new {\i shekalim} but not with old {\i shekalim}. The background is as follows. Beginning in {\i Adar}, people would donate their annual half-{\i shekel} to the {\i Beit HaMikdash} for the purchase of communal sacrifices in the coming year. These new funds \u8212- the {\i chadatin} \u8212- would be drawn upon in three collections throughout the year to buy {\i korbanos}. If someone failed to donate in time and contributed a {\i shekel} from a prior year, those older funds \u8212- the {\i atikin} \u8212- were designated for a different purpose: municipal and Temple infrastructure needs, such as building and repairing the walls of Jerusalem and its towers, and other city needs. Rashi describes this as {\i kol tzorchei tikkun ha\u8217'ir} \u8212- all manner of maintenance of the city.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Now, funds used to build the walls of Jerusalem are something from which ordinary people will naturally benefit \u8212- shade, shelter, protection. This is analogous in some respect to {\i bigdei kehunah}, in that there is an allowance for benefit built into the purpose of these funds. The question the {\i Gemara} poses is: does that allowance create a blanket exemption from {\i me\u8217'ilah} in the same way we are arguing it does for {\i bigdei kehunah}? If I accidentally took those old {\i shekalim} and spent them on something entirely unrelated to the walls of Jerusalem, would there be {\i me\u8217'ilah}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Ta shema} \u8212- the {\i braita} states: {\i mo\u8217'alin b\u8217'chadatin} \u8212- the new {\i shekalim} are subject to {\i me\u8217'ilah}, {\i v\u8217'ein mo\u8217'alin b\u8217'atikin} \u8212- but not the old {\i shekalim}. Then Rabbi Meir says: {\i mo\u8217'alin af b\u8217'atikin} \u8212- even the old {\i shekalim} are subject to {\i me\u8217'ilah}. Rabbi Meir further invokes the rule of {\i shiyarei halishka}: leftover funds from the collection designated for purchasing {\i korbanos}, which were not drawn upon in that year, were allocated to these same communal infrastructure needs. Rabbi Meir holds that even those {\i shiyarei halishka} funds remain subject to {\i me\u8217'ilah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This poses a significant difficulty. If Rabbi Meir is the one insisting on {\i me\u8217'ilah} even when funds are earmarked for communal use from which the general public will benefit \u8212- which is an even more permissive context than {\i bigdei kehunah} \u8212- how can we maintain the reading that it is specifically Rabbi Meir in our {\i Mishnah} who exempts from {\i me\u8217'ilah} due to the allowance given to {\i bigdei kehunah}? The logic should run the other way: if even the old {\i shekalim} and {\i shiyarei halishka} are subject to {\i me\u8217'ilah} despite their broad allowances for public benefit, then certainly there should be {\i me\u8217'ilah b\u8217'shogeg} with {\i bigdei kehunah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Fallen wall stones\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} then brings a further {\i braita}: {\i avnei Yerushalayim shenashru, mo\u8217'alin bahen, divrei Rabbi Meir} \u8212- if stones fall from the wall of Jerusalem, Rabbi Meir says there is still {\i me\u8217'ilah}. This is precisely the question that arose regarding the old {\i shekalim}: once there is a broad allowance for people to benefit from the walls, can we say that when stones tumble off and someone uses them to build their own house \u8212- not realizing the stones belong to the {\i Beit HaMikdash} \u8212- there is {\i me\u8217'ilah}? Rabbi Meir says yes, there still is.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This again contradicts the attempt to explain our {\i Mishnah} as a case where Rabbi Meir grants blanket exemption from {\i me\u8217'ilah} because a limited allowance was given. The {\i Gemara\u8217's} response in both cases is the same: {\i lo teima Rabbi Meir, ella eima Rabbi Yehuda} \u8212- remove Rabbi Meir\u8217's name wherever it appears in these {\i braitot} and replace it with Rabbi Yehuda. It is Rabbi Yehuda who maintains {\i me\u8217'ilah} in these cases, and Rabbi Yehuda who takes the stricter position that a limited, purpose-specific allowance does not create broad immunity.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This answer is, as the {\i Gemara} itself implicitly acknowledges, a significant stretch. The approach of reading {\i bigdei kehunah} as a hyper-specific exception and then reversing all the attributed names to maintain internal consistency becomes increasingly strained \u8212- particularly because the general thrust of the other sources suggests that the more lenient position on the walls of Jerusalem and {\i shiyarei halishka} is precisely the one we should be attributing to Rabbi Yehuda, not Rabbi Meir.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Further complicating matters: the {\i Gemara} observes that Rabbi Yehuda\u8217's approach would require saying that the walls of Jerusalem retain {\i kedushah}, and that stones fallen from those walls remain {\i hekdesh} subject to {\i me\u8217'ilah}. But we know from a {\i Mishnah} in Nedarim that Rabbi Yehuda holds the opposite. If one says a loaf of bread shall be like a lamb, or like the wood of the altar, or {\i k\u8217'Yerushalayim} \u8212- like Jerusalem \u8212- Rabbi Yehuda rules it is not a valid {\i neder}, because Jerusalem does not have the type of personal-property {\i kedushah} that can be invoked in a {\i neder}. Even if one says the correct formula \u8212- {\i k\u8217'Yerushalayim}, with the comparative {\i kaf} \u8212- Rabbi Yehuda rules it is ineffective, because Jerusalem\u8217's {\i kedushah} is not of the operative kind. This strongly implies that Rabbi Yehuda does not treat the walls of Jerusalem as possessing the same {\i hekdesh kedushah} as Temple property, which would mean that assigning him the position that fallen wall-stones are subject to {\i me\u8217'ilah} is inconsistent.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} resolves this final tension with {\i trei tanna\u8217'im aliba d\u8217'Rabbi Yehuda} \u8212- there is a dispute among {\i tanna\u8217'im} about what Rabbi Yehuda\u8217's position actually entails. Some hold Rabbi Yehuda denies {\i kedushah} to the walls of Jerusalem entirely; others attribute to him a view that the walls do retain {\i kedushah} and {\i me\u8217'ilah} applies when their use is taken outside the permitted parameters. The {\i Gemara} is ultimately threading a very fine needle to maintain the interpretation that our {\i Mishnah} addresses only the narrow case of {\i bigdei kehunah}, rather than accepting the broader reading that Rabbi Meir fundamentally rejects transactional {\i me\u8217'ilah b\u8217'shogeg} altogether. And yet, as the {\i Gemara} will make clear in the next section, it is precisely that broader reading \u8212- however extreme \u8212- that is in fact Rabbi Meir\u8217's genuine position.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Halachic ruling\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Follow Meir: Me\u8217'aser\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 With the analysis of the competing frameworks now complete, the {\i Gemara} turns to how we rule in practice. Rav Adda bar Ahava states: {\i halacha k\u8217'Rabbi Meir b\u8217'ma\u8217'aser} \u8212- the {\i halacha} follows Rabbi Meir regarding {\i ma\u8217'aser sheni}. This means that {\i ma\u8217'aser sheni} is {\i mammon gavoh} \u8212- it belongs entirely to God \u8212- and one can never use it to betroth a woman, under any circumstances.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kerem revai proof\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The basis for ruling like Rabbi Meir on {\i ma\u8217'aser sheni} is that a {\i stam Mishnah} aligns with his position. The {\i Gemara} identifies this {\i stam} in the laws of {\i kerem revai} \u8212- the fruit of a vineyard in its fourth year.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shammai vs.\u160 ?Hillel\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Mishnah} records a debate between Beit Shammai and Beit Hillel on the status of {\i kerem revai}. Beit Shammai holds that {\i kerem revai} does not require the addition of a {\i chomesh} \u8212- the twenty percent surcharge added when one redeems {\i ma\u8217'aser sheni} \u8212- and does not require {\i bi\u8217'ur}, the removal of the produce from one\u8217's home in the third and sixth years of the {\i shemita} cycle. Beit Hillel holds that it does require both {\i chomesh} and {\i bi\u8217'ur}, treating it consistently like {\i ma\u8217'aser sheni}. In a further point of dispute regarding the gifts to the poor: Beit Shammai holds that the normal gifts to the poor apply to {\i kerem revai} \u8212- specifically {\i peret} (individual grapes dropped during harvest) and {\i olelot} (unripe or inferior clusters) \u8212- while Beit Hillel says {\i kulo l\u8217'gat}, it goes entirely to the winepress, with no portion left for the poor.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} explains the reasoning. Beit Hillel derives the similarity to {\i ma\u8217'aser sheni} from a {\i gezeirah shavah}: {\i kodesh-kodesh}. The verse by {\i kerem revai} says {\i kodesh hilulim l\u8217'Hashem}, and by {\i ma\u8217'aser sheni} the word {\i kodesh l\u8217'Hashem} also appears. Beit Shammai does not accept this {\i gezeirah shavah}. Beit Hillel\u8217's ruling that all the fruit goes to the winepress, leaving nothing for the poor, follows directly from their view that {\i kerem revai} is like {\i ma\u8217'aser sheni} \u8212- and since {\i ma\u8217'aser sheni} is {\i mammon gavoh}, it is not the owner\u8217's money in a meaningful sense, which means the owner does not bear the personal obligation to leave gifts to the poor from it. If it were his money \u8212- as Rabbi Yehuda maintains \u8212- then it would remain his property subject to those obligations, and he would need to leave the gifts to the poor. Beit Hillel\u8217's ruling that no gifts to the poor apply thus presupposes that {\i ma\u8217'aser sheni} is {\i mammon gavoh} \u8212- exactly the position of Rabbi Meir. Since Beit Hillel constitutes the {\i stam} ruling and the {\i halacha} follows them, this establishes that we rule like Rabbi Meir on {\i ma\u8217'aser sheni}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Follow Yehuda: Hekdesh\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Conversely, Rav Adda bar Ahava states: {\i v\u8217'halacha k\u8217'Rabbi Yehuda b\u8217'hekdesh} \u8212- we follow Rabbi Yehuda regarding {\i hekdesh}. This is the classic, standard position on {\i me\u8217'ilah}: {\i b\u8217'shogeg} the money exits {\i hekdesh} and becomes {\i chullin}, and the woman is {\i mekudeshet}; {\i b\u8217'meizid} the money does not exit {\i hekdesh} and she is not {\i mekudeshet}. Here too the ruling is supported by a {\i stam Mishnah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Mishnah} in question: one had {\i hekdesh} funds and, without realizing it, sent a competent adult \u8212- not a minor, not someone of diminished capacity \u8212- as a {\i shaliach} to spend those funds. The {\i shaliach} was sent to a storekeeper to make a purchase. Before the {\i shaliach} reached the storekeeper, he remembered that the funds were {\i hekdesh} \u8212- meaning both the sender and the {\i shaliach} were now {\i b\u8217'meizid}. In that case, {\i chanvani mo\u8217'el l\u8217'ch\u8217'sheyotzi} \u8212- the storekeeper commits the {\i me\u8217'ilah} when he receives and uses the money, because the storekeeper is {\i b\u8217'shogeg}. The {\i Gemara} clarifies that the case is one in which both the sender and the {\i shaliach} recalled, so both are {\i b\u8217'meizid}, the money did not exit {\i hekdesh} through either of them, and when the unsuspecting storekeeper uses the funds, his {\i shogeg} act is what causes the money to become {\i chullin} and constitutes the {\i me\u8217'ilah}. This perfectly illustrates the principle of Rabbi Yehuda: {\i shogeg} causes the money to exit {\i hekdesh}, {\i meizid} does not. And since this {\i Mishnah} operates on that assumption without attribution, it functions as the {\i stam} that establishes the {\i halacha} like Rabbi Yehuda for {\i hekdesh}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Contradiction: chullin s\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Pappa\u8217's question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 With the ruling now established \u8212- we follow Rabbi Meir that {\i ma\u8217'aser sheni} is {\i mammon gavoh}, and we follow Rabbi Yehuda that {\i me\u8217'ilah} with {\i hekdesh} funds operates in the standard manner, {\i b\u8217'shogeg} desanctifying the money and {\i b\u8217'meizid} not \u8212- Rav Pappa addresses a difficulty to Abaye. We have ruled according to Rabbi Yehuda\u8217's framework on {\i hekdesh}: if one uses {\i hekdesh} money to betroth a woman {\i b\u8217'shogeg}, the {\i me\u8217'ilah} is effective, the money becomes {\i chullin}, and the {\i kiddushin} works. If done {\i b\u8217'meizid}, the money does not become {\i chullin} and the {\i kiddushin} fails.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But compare this to a {\i Mishnah} in Tractate {\i Me\u8217'ilah} involving a {\i balan}, a bathhouse keeper. If someone pays a bathhouse keeper for entry using {\i hekdesh} funds without realizing it ({\i b\u8217'shogeg}), the {\i me\u8217'ilah} takes effect, the donor is liable, and the money becomes {\i chullin}. If he did it {\i b\u8217'meizid} \u8212- knowingly \u8212- the money does not become {\i chullin}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 So far this is consistent with Rabbi Yehuda and our ruling. But Rav Pappa identifies a genuine tension: by {\i kiddushin}, we say that if the man intentionally ({\i b\u8217'meizid}) gives {\i hekdesh} money to betroth a woman, the money becomes {\i chullin} and the betrothal is effective \u8212- that is Rabbi Meir\u8217's position which governs the {\i kiddushin} case in the {\i Mishnah}. Yet by the bathhouse keeper, {\i b\u8217'meizid} it does not become {\i chullin}. Why should the intentional use of {\i hekdesh} funds produce different outcomes in these two cases \u8212- effective desanctification leading to valid {\i kiddushin} in one instance, and failure to desanctify in the other?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is the question with which the {\i daf} concludes, and which will be taken up in the subsequent session.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Kiddushin 55\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Maaser Sheni & Hekdesh\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Maaser Sheni Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 We resume in the middle of 54b, having just established the ruling that we follow {\i Rabbi Meir} on the question of {\i maaser sheni}. The position is that {\i maaser sheni} is {\i mamon gavoah} \u8212- God\u8217's money \u8212- and not the private property of the individual. The basis for this ruling is a {\i stam mishna} reflecting the position of {\i Beit Hillel}, which holds that one does not distribute the gifts of the poor \u8212- {\i peret u\u8217'olelot} \u8212- from {\i neta reva\u8217'i}, since {\i neta reva\u8217'i} shares the laws of {\i maaser sheni}. The logic is that if it is truly God\u8217's money, one cannot use it to betroth a woman, because one cannot effect {\i kiddushin} with property that does not genuinely belong to oneself.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Me\u8217'ilah Distinctions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 When it comes to {\i hekdesh} and {\i me\u8217'ilah}, however, the ruling follows {\i Rabbi Yehuda}. The many {\i mishnayot} in Masechet Me\u8217'ilah demonstrate that when one uses {\i hekdesh} funds, one transgresses {\i me\u8217'ilah b\u8217'shogeg} \u8212- unwittingly \u8212- and does not transgress {\i me\u8217'ilah b\u8217'mezid} \u8212- intentionally. {\i Rabbi Meir} takes precisely the reverse position: {\i me\u8217'ilah} can occur {\i b\u8217'mezid}, and moreover, when it comes to monetary transactions involving {\i hekdesh} funds, {\i me\u8217'ilah} does not occur {\i b\u8217'shogeg}, because had one known one was transacting with sacred funds, one would not have wanted the transaction at all, and the transaction is therefore void. The {\i stam mishna} in Me\u8217'ilah goes against {\i Rabbi Meir} and follows {\i Rabbi Yehuda}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Stam Mishna Logic\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Thus the general picture is: by {\i maaser sheni}, the {\i stam mishna} reflects {\i Rabbi Meir}; by {\i me\u8217'ilah}, the {\i stam mishna} reflects {\i Rabbi Yehuda}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Three Mishnayot Challenge\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Redeeming Maaser Sheni\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now raises a series of challenges. Having said we rule like {\i Rabbi Meir} that {\i maaser sheni} is {\i mamon gavoah}, the {\i gemara} cites the following {\i mishna} with the words {\i v\u8217'ha tnan} \u8212- but do we not teach? The {\i mishna} states: {\i HaPodeh Maaser Sheni shelo} \u8212- one who redeems his own {\i maaser sheni} \u8212- adds a fifth to its value. This applies {\i bein mishelo bein shenitan lo b\u8217'matanah} \u8212- whether the produce is his own or whether it was given to him as a gift, and he now wishes to redeem it rather than carry the fruit to Jerusalem.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gift-Giving Problem\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The difficulty is immediately apparent: if someone can give their {\i maaser sheni} to another person, and that other person genuinely owns it to the point of being required to add a fifth upon redemption, it sounds very much as though {\i maaser sheni} belongs to its owner as private property, transferable at will. The {\i gemara} asks: {\i mani} \u8212- who is the author of this {\i mishna}? If it is {\i Rabbi Meir}, how can one give it as a gift? {\i V\u8217'amar maaser mamon gavoah hu} \u8212- he holds it is God\u8217's money. One may have the right to bring it to Jerusalem and eat it there, but it is not one\u8217's property to transfer to someone else.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tevel Resolution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} answers: {\i la, l\u8217'olam Rabbi Meir} \u8212- no, the {\i mishna} can still reflect {\i Rabbi Meir}\u8217's view. {\i V\u8217'hacha b\u8217'mai askinan k\u8217'de\u8217'yahiv lei b\u8217'tevel} \u8212- what are we dealing with? A case in which the original owner gave the produce to the recipient while it was still {\i tevel} \u8212- before {\i trumot u\u8217'maasrot} had been separated. The giver said, in effect, here is some of my crop; take it, and you handle the separation of tithes. The {\i maaser sheni} thus never belonged to the first person in its final sanctified form; it was given as {\i tevel}, and it was the recipient who rendered it {\i maaser sheni} through his own act of separation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might press the point: even at the stage of {\i tevel}, the future {\i maaser sheni} already exists within the produce in potential \u8212- why does that not prevent transfer? The {\i gemara} responds that this {\i tanna} holds the position of {\i matnas shelo hurmu k\u8217'mi shehurmu d\u8217'mi\u8217'an} \u8212- that gifts not yet separated are treated as though they already have been. On this view, before separation, the grain is simply {\i tevel}, an undifferentiated whole; the future {\i maaser sheni} does not have independent status yet, and therefore the entire {\i tevel} belongs to the owner to transfer. Once transferred, the recipient separates the tithe, and it is his own {\i maaser sheni} that emerges from his own produce. The {\i mishna} is therefore entirely consistent with {\i Rabbi Meir}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 On the related question of why {\i me\u8217'ilah} does not apply to {\i maaser sheni}: {\i me\u8217'ilah} applies only to things sanctified to the {\i beit hamikdash} \u8212- a {\i korban}, or something dedicated to {\i bedek habayis}. {\i Maaser sheni} has {\i kedushah}, but it is {\i kedushah} of a different type \u8212- the obligation to eat the produce in Jerusalem. It is a categorically distinct form of sanctity.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Neta Reva\u8217'i Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then attempts again: {\i ta shma} \u8212- come hear. Another {\i mishna} teaches: {\i hapodeh neta reva\u8217'i shelo} \u8212- one who redeems his own {\i neta reva\u8217'i}, his fourth-year fruit \u8212- adds a fifth. This applies {\i bein mishelo bein shenitan lo b\u8217'matanah}, whether the fruit is his own or was given to him as a gift. The implication is that ownership of {\i neta reva\u8217'i} can genuinely be transferred between individuals.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Critically, the {\i tevel} answer does not apply here. Fourth-year fruit does not exist as {\i tevel} and then become {\i neta reva\u8217'i} through an act of separation. Everything that grows in the fourth year of the vine or tree immediately has the status of {\i neta reva\u8217'i} \u8212- or {\i kerem reva\u8217'i} \u8212- which {\i Beit Hillel} treats as equivalent to {\i maaser sheni}. There is no prior stage at which the fruit is merely {\i tevel} that can be transferred before acquiring its sacred status. Yet the {\i mishna} states that it can be given as a gift and genuinely acquired by the recipient. This appears to be an unambiguous {\i stam mishna} like {\i Rabbi Yehuda}, who holds {\i maaser sheni} is {\i mamon hedyot}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Smadar Answer\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} again insists: {\i la, l\u8217'olam Rabbi Meir} \u8212- the {\i mishna} can be explained as reflecting {\i Rabbi Meir}\u8217's position. {\i V\u8217'hacha b\u8217'mai askinan kegon d\u8217'yahiv lei k\u8217'shehu smadar} \u8212- we are dealing with a case in which the owner transferred the vines to the recipient while the grapes were still in the unripe {\i smadar} stage, before they had developed into actual fruit. Since they had not yet become a {\i pri}, they had not yet acquired the status of {\i neta reva\u8217'i}. While they remained {\i smadar}, they were still ordinary property, and the owner could transfer them freely. The recipient then came to own the vines at the point the grapes ripened into {\i neta reva\u8217'i}, and so it is his own {\i neta reva\u8217'i} that he redeems. This reading diverges from the position of {\i Rabbi Yosi}, who holds that even at the {\i smadar} stage the fruit is already considered a {\i pri} and therefore already {\i orlah}-relevant \u8212- the present explanation cannot proceed according to {\i Rabbi Yosi}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The irony is not lost: the natural reading of both {\i mishnayot} supports {\i Rabbi Yehuda}. These explanations are genuine {\i kvetches}. Nevertheless, because a {\i stam mishna} like {\i Rabbi Meir} exists, the {\i gemara} prefers to reinterpret those {\i mishnayot} as compatible with {\i Rabbi Meir} rather than acknowledge two {\i stamim} in favor of {\i Rabbi Yehuda}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Meshichah Sale\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} tries a third time. Another {\i mishna} in {\i maaser sheni} states: {\i mashach heimenu maaser b\u8217'selah} \u8212- someone pulled {\i maaser sheni} into his possession at the agreed price of one {\i selah}, performing {\i meshichah}, the formal act of acquisition. But {\i velo hispik lifstoso ad she-amad bish\u8217'tayim} \u8212- before he got around to completing the redemption by handing over the money, the market value of the produce doubled. The {\i mishna} rules {\i noten selah umistaker besela} \u8212- he pays the original agreed price and profits the difference. {\i U\u8217'maaser sheni shelo} \u8212- and the {\i maaser sheni} is his.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The underlying mechanics require some analysis. The act that determines the purchase price is the {\i meshichah}: once he performed {\i meshichah}, the transaction was legally finalized at the price agreed upon at that moment. Even though the money has not yet changed hands \u8212- and handing over the money would constitute the act of {\i pidyon}, redemption \u8212- the sale price was set at {\i meshichah}, and any subsequent change in market value does not alter his obligation. He pays the original {\i selah}, and the {\i kedushah} of the {\i maaser sheni} transfers onto that money.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Meshichah Logic\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Now the {\i gemara} identifies the problem. {\i Mani} \u8212- who is this {\i mishna} going like? If it is {\i Rabbi Meir}, there is a fundamental difficulty. {\i Rabbi Meir} holds {\i maaser sheni} is {\i mamon gavoah}. If so, there can be no {\i mechira}, no sale. The transaction cannot be treated as a commercial exchange finalized by {\i meshichah}, because {\i meshichah} effects acquisition only when ownership can genuinely be transferred. If it is God\u8217's money and not the seller\u8217's private property, {\i meshichah} is meaningless. What can happen is only {\i pidyon} \u8212- redemption \u8212- and {\i pidyon} takes effect at the moment the money is handed over, by the verse {\i v\u8217'nasan es hakesef vekam lo}, one gives the money and it is established. If that is the operative moment, then when the money is handed over and the value has risen to twenty, the redemption must be calculated at the current value of twenty. The fact that {\i meshichah} finalizes the transaction and locks in the lower price demonstrates that this is a genuine sale, treating {\i maaser sheni} as {\i mamon hedyot}. This is, apparently, an unambiguous {\i stam mishna} like {\i Rabbi Yehuda}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} concedes: {\i ella l\u8217'olam Rabbi Yehuda} \u8212- fine, this {\i mishna} is {\i Rabbi Yehuda}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Eduyos Authority\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two vs One\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 At this point the {\i gemara} recalibrates. {\i Ve\u8217'hacha chad stama ve\u8217'hacha trei stami} \u8212- here we have one anonymous {\i mishna} like {\i Rabbi Yehuda}, and elsewhere we have two {\i stam mishnayot} like {\i Rabbi Meir}. The first two {\i mishnayot} \u8212- the {\i tevel} case and the {\i smadar} case \u8212- were reinterpreted through strained but technically possible readings; the {\i meshichah} case is conceded to {\i Rabbi Yehuda}. But the {\i mishna} about {\i Beit Hillel} and {\i peret u\u8217'olelot} from {\i neta reva\u8217'i} appears both in Masechet Ma\u8217'aser Sheni and again in Masechet Eduyot, giving us two {\i stamim} aligned with {\i Rabbi Meir} against one with {\i Rabbi Yehuda}. We rule by the majority.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Eduyos Weight\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then raises a sharper objection: {\i mai li chad stama mai li trei stamei} \u8212- what difference does it make whether a {\i stam} appears once or twice? If a {\i stam mishna} carries authority because it represents {\i Rebbe}\u8217's final ruling, why should mere repetition amplify that authority? The same {\i mishna} appearing in two places should not count as two separate votes.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rav Nachman bar Yitzchak} therefore offers the true basis for the ruling: {\i ho\u8217'il ve\u8217'isnan bi\u8217'vchirasa kevasei} \u8212- since we have a {\i stam mishna} like {\i Rabbi Meir} in the chosen {\i masechet}, in the {\i masechet} of choice. That {\i masechet} is Eduyot. The {\i mishnayot} of Masechet Eduyot carry greater weight than {\i mishnayot} elsewhere, because Eduyot is a tractate of authentic, carefully preserved testimonies and traditions. A {\i stam mishna} in Eduyot therefore carries special authority that tips the balance in favor of {\i Rabbi Meir}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Authority Tension\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 There is a striking irony embedded in this ruling. Masechet Eduyot was taught, according to the {\i gemara}, precisely when the doors of the {\i beit midrash} were opened to all \u8212- when {\i Rabban Gamliel} was replaced as head and a more inclusive policy was adopted. The {\i mishna} records that on that day the benches had to be multiplied to accommodate the flood of new students. Even ordinary craftsmen \u8212- weavers, for instance \u8212- came forward and transmitted authentic traditions they had preserved. The very tractate whose {\i stamim} carry the highest halachic weight was compiled under maximally populist circumstances, admitting testimony from people who would not normally have been considered {\i talmidei chachamim}. Yet what they transmitted carries more authority, not less. This tension \u8212- between elitist and populist sources of authority \u8212- runs deep through the structure of {\i halacha}, and echoes in the ongoing question of how much weight to give communal practice against canonical sources, or canonical sources against communal practice.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is why we rule that {\i maaser sheni} is {\i mamon gavoah}, following {\i Rabbi Meir}, even though the natural reading of several {\i stam mishnayot} supports {\i Rabbi Yehuda}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Wandering Animal\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Animal Types\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 We now turn to a new topic introduced at the opening of 55a, drawn from a {\i mishna} in Shekalim. The {\i mishna} teaches: {\i behemah shenimtzes miYerushalayim leMigdal Eder} \u8212- an animal found wandering in the area between Jerusalem and Migdal Eder, approximately fifteen kilometers south of the city, and within a comparable radius in every direction. {\i Zcharim olos} \u8212- if it is a male, we presume it is an {\i olah}. {\i Nekeivos zivchei shelamim} \u8212- if it is a female, we presume it is a {\i shelamim}. The reasoning behind these presumptions is that in the vicinity of Jerusalem, where animals were brought constantly for {\i korbanot}, and where private individuals generally did not raise livestock within the city itself, a wandering animal is most likely a sacrificial animal.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Male Ambiguity\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} immediately interrupts with a question: it is true that all {\i olos} are male, but it does not follow that all males are {\i olos}. A {\i shelamim} can also be male. Why, then, when we find a male animal, do we not also consider the possibility that it might be a {\i shelamim}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Monetary Obligation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Oshaya} explains: {\i hacha bavo lachov bidmeihem askinan} \u8212- what the {\i mishna} is dealing with is not simple identification of the animal\u8217's status, but a situation in which a {\i tzaddik}, a conscientious person who found this animal, wishes to ensure that the {\i kedushah} is properly handled and is willing to incur personal monetary obligation to do so. The male has the possibility of being an {\i olah}, and the female can only be a {\i shelamim}. The question is what practically to do with such an animal \u8212- and that is what the {\i mishna} and the subsequent {\i gemara} discussion are working out.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Me\u8217'ilah B\u8217'Mezid\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rashi Dual Dedication\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Oshaya}\u8217's resolution of the {\i mishna} invokes the position of {\i Rabbi Meir}: {\i Rabbi Meir hi de\u8217'amar hekdesh b\u8217'mezid mitchalel} \u8212- it is {\i Rabbi Meir} who holds that {\i hekdesh} can be desacralized and transferred to {\i chullin} even through an intentional act. On {\i Rashi}\u8217's reading, the procedure works as follows. Since the male animal might be either an {\i olah} or a {\i shelamim}, the {\i tzaddik} takes two separate piles of money, each equal to the animal\u8217's value. He declares: if this animal is an {\i olah}, I am transferring its {\i kedushah} to pile A, and I am simultaneously dedicating pile B as a {\i shelamim} as a voluntary gift; if this animal is a {\i shelamim}, I am transferring its {\i kedushah} to pile B, and I am simultaneously dedicating pile A as an {\i olah} as a voluntary gift. The result is that one pile carries the transferred {\i kedushah} and the other carries his own voluntary dedication \u8212- one is an {\i olah} and one is a {\i shelamim} \u8212- and the animal itself is redeemed. Both the {\i olah} and {\i shelamim} obligations are thus fulfilled, covering whichever the animal actually was.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafot Theft\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tosafot} reads the mechanism differently, and while this reading is probably closer to the {\i pshat} of the {\i gemara}, it is striking enough that one can understand why {\i Rashi} sought an alternative. According to {\i Tosafot}, the {\i tzaddik} \u8212- precisely because he wishes to properly resolve the animal\u8217's status \u8212- takes the animal for himself. This is an act of {\i me\u8217'ilah}. He is fully aware of what he is doing; it is {\i b\u8217'mezid}. According to {\i Rabbi Meir}, {\i hekdesh b\u8217'mezid mitchalel} \u8212- this intentional act of {\i me\u8217'ilah} removes the {\i kedushah} from the animal and renders it {\i chullin}. The {\i tzaddik} then contributes both an {\i olah} and a {\i shelamim} to the {\i beit hamikdash} from his own funds, thus ensuring that the {\i beit hamikdash} receives its due regardless. He has, in effect, exposed himself to the transgression of {\i me\u8217'ilah} in order to resolve the animal\u8217's problematic status \u8212- hence the phrase {\i bavo lachov bidmeihem}, coming to incur an obligation commensurate with their value.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kedusha Removal\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} raises the obvious difficulty: even granting {\i Rabbi Meir}\u8217's position that {\i me\u8217'ilah b\u8217'mezid} can remove {\i kedushah}, that principle was articulated with respect to {\i hekdesh} funds \u8212- money or property owned by the {\i beit hamikdash}, which is {\i bedek habayis}. {\i Bedek habayis} is essentially the {\i beit hamikdash}\u8217's bank account: the institution owns the asset and can dispose of it as it sees fit. It is understandable that an act of misappropriation could sever that ownership relationship and render the object {\i chullin}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But an animal dedicated as a {\i korban} has {\i kedushat haguf} \u8212- intrinsic sanctity. Its {\i kedushah} does not consist merely in being owned by the {\i beit hamikdash}; rather, the {\i korban} itself, in its very identity, is sacred. Why would an act of {\i me\u8217'ilah}, even {\i b\u8217'mezid}, have the power to remove a {\i kedushah} that defines the object\u8217's essential nature? The question becomes: does {\i me\u8217'ilah b\u8217'mezid} extend to {\i kedushat haguf}?\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Kedushat HaGuf\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Multiple Me\u8217'ilot\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} brings a {\i mishna} that bears directly on this question. The {\i mishna} states: {\i ein moel achar moel b\u8217'mukkdashim ella b\u8217'vehema u\u8217've\u8217'klei shares bilvad} \u8212- one cannot do multiple acts of {\i me\u8217'ilah} on the same sanctified object, except in the case of an animal ({\i korban}) or the sacred vessels ({\i klei shares}). The reason one generally cannot transgress {\i me\u8217'ilah} twice on the same object is that once {\i me\u8217'ilah} has been committed, the object exits {\i kedushah} and becomes {\i chullin} \u8212- there is nothing sacred left to misuse a second time. The exception for animals and {\i klei shares} demonstrates that these objects, possessing {\i kedushat haguf}, retain their {\i kedushah} even after an act of {\i me\u8217'ilah}. Multiple individuals can ride upon a {\i korban} animal or drink from a sacred golden goblet, and each one commits {\i me\u8217'ilah}, because the {\i kedushah} of these objects is not extinguished by being misused.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Survival Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This {\i mishna} poses a sharp problem. If {\i kedushat haguf} survives even {\i me\u8217'ilah}, how can {\i me\u8217'ilah b\u8217'mezid} be said to remove the {\i kedushah} from this wandering animal? The very {\i mishna} just cited teaches that {\i kedushat haguf} persists through {\i me\u8217'ilah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Strength of Act\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} resolves this by distinguishing between the positions of {\i Rabbi Yehuda} and {\i Rabbi Meir}. {\i Rav amar Rabbi Yehuda hekdesh b\u8217'shogeg mitchalel kedushat haguf lo mitchalel} \u8212- {\i Rabbi Yehuda} holds that {\i hekdesh} goes out to {\i chullin} only {\i b\u8217'shogeg}, not {\i b\u8217'mezid}, and moreover, {\i kedushat haguf} never goes out to {\i chullin} through {\i me\u8217'ilah} at all. One then asks: why should {\i Rabbi Meir} disagree on this latter point? Even if {\i Rabbi Meir} extends the effect of {\i me\u8217'ilah} to {\i b\u8217'mezid}, why should that change the rule regarding {\i kedushat haguf}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} answers with a crucial conceptual distinction. According to {\i Rabbi Yehuda}, {\i me\u8217'ilah} operates only {\i b\u8217'shogeg}. An inadvertent misuse is, inherently, a weak act \u8212- one did not intend it, did not consciously strive to appropriate or to desacralize. Because the act is weak, it can sever a relationship of ownership ({\i kedushat damim}, the {\i beit hamikdash}\u8217's proprietary claim), but it cannot penetrate and negate a {\i kedushah} that defines the object\u8217's very identity. According to {\i Rabbi Yehuda}, the {\i mishna}\u8217's rule that {\i kedushat haguf} survives {\i me\u8217'ilah} is entirely coherent: {\i me\u8217'ilah b\u8217'shogeg} is simply not a strong enough act to dislodge something so fundamental.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But according to {\i Rabbi Meir}, {\i me\u8217'ilah} can occur {\i b\u8217'mezid} \u8212- and {\i me\u8217'ilah b\u8217'mezid} is qualitatively different. When a person consciously, deliberately intends to remove an object from the domain of the {\i beit hamikdash} and take it into his own possession \u8212- {\i hacha ka michaven la\u8217'apukeia lechullin}, here he is intending to bring it out to {\i chullin} \u8212- that is an act of a fundamentally stronger character. It is, in a sense, the most aggressive possible form of appropriation. And according to {\i Rabbi Meir}, an act of that strength is sufficient to negate even {\i kedushat haguf}. The intuition is compelling: if any act is going to change the status of something intrinsically holy, it would be a maximally intentional, deliberate effort to desacralize it, not an accidental stumble.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} further develops this: {\i amar lei hahu me\u8217'rabbanan, Rabbi Yaakov shemei, kal vachomer} \u8212- one of the rabbis, a figure named {\i Rabbi Yaakov}, argued from a {\i kal vachomer}: if {\i kodshei kodashim}, the most holy things, can be brought out to {\i chullin} through {\i me\u8217'ilah b\u8217'mezid} according to {\i Rabbi Meir}, then {\i kodashim kalim} \u8212- things of lesser sanctity, like a {\i shelamim}, which the owner himself will eat \u8212- should certainly be susceptible to losing their {\i kedushah}, since their sanctity is of a lower order and therefore more easily displaced. One might have thought the reverse: that a lower level of {\i kedushah} is more resistant to removal because it is less fragile. But the {\i kal vachomer} presses the other direction \u8212- lesser {\i kedushah} yields more readily, not less.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is confirmed in a {\i baraita}: {\i amar Rabbi Chama b\u8217'Rabbi Yaakov amar Rabbi Yose b\u8217'Rabbi Chanina, haya Rabbi Meir omer} \u8212- {\i Rabbi Meir} used to say: {\i hekdesh b\u8217'mezid mitchalel, b\u8217'shogeg ein mitchalel, echad kodshei kodashim v\u8217'echad kodashim kalim} \u8212- {\i hekdesh} goes out to {\i chullin} through intentional {\i me\u8217'ilah}, not through inadvertent {\i me\u8217'ilah}, and this applies equally to {\i kodshei kodashim} and to {\i kodashim kalim}. The {\i kal vachomer} is then stated explicitly.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sin Objection\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Yochanan} was astounded by the reading of {\i Rabbi Oshaya}: {\i V\u8217'chi omrim lo l\u8217'adam amod v\u8217'chata bishvil shetizkeh} \u8212- does one tell a person, \u8220"Go and sin so that things will work out properly with this animal\u8221"? Could the {\i mishna} actually be instructing someone to commit {\i me\u8217'ilah} deliberately as a matter of course?\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Blemish Solution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Male Procedure\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Yochanan} therefore offers an entirely different reading of the {\i mishna}, free of any intentional transgression. {\i Mamtin lo ad sheyista\u8217'eiv} \u8212- one simply waits until the animal develops a {\i mum}, a blemish. Once it has a {\i mum}, it can be redeemed without restriction. Having redeemed the blemished animal, one then brings two substitute animals and makes a conditional declaration: if this wandering animal is an {\i olah}, I am transferring its {\i kedushah} to animal A, and I am voluntarily dedicating animal B as a {\i shelamim}; if it is a {\i shelamim}, I am transferring its {\i kedushah} to animal B, and I am voluntarily dedicating animal A as an {\i olah}. One then brings both animals. The {\i tzaddik} is out the cost of two animals, but no transgression has occurred.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Three Animals\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} immediately challenges: why only two animals? {\i Dilma todah hi} \u8212- perhaps the animal is a {\i todah}, a thanksgiving offering, which is a subspecies of {\i shelamim} that can be brought by a male or female animal. One would then need a third animal to cover this possibility. {\i D\u8217'meisei nami todah} \u8212- fine, bring three animals. But {\i v\u8217'ha ba\u8217'ya lechem} \u8212- a {\i todah} requires bread loaves to accompany it. {\i D\u8217'meisei nami lechem} \u8212- bring the bread as well. If the animal turns out to be a {\i todah}, the {\i kedushah} transferred to the corresponding animal plus its bread; if not, the extra animal and bread were a voluntary {\i todah} dedication.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Age Requirement\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then asks: {\i v\u8217'dilma asham hu} \u8212- perhaps it is an {\i asham}, a guilt offering, which is male, presents a more serious problem because an {\i asham} cannot be brought as a voluntary {\i nedavah}. The {\i gemara} answers: {\i asham ben shtei shanim} \u8212- an {\i asham} must be two years old. Since the case in the {\i mishna} involves a one-year-old animal, the possibility of {\i asham} is excluded by age. The {\i gemara} further raises the possibility of {\i asham metzora} or {\i asham nazir}, both of which require one-year animals, but these are dismissed as uncommon enough to set aside \u8212- {\i lo shchiach}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Pesach and Bechor\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i V\u8217'dilma pesach hu} \u8212- perhaps it is a {\i pesach} offering, which is also one year and male. The {\i gemara} answers that {\i pesach bizmano}, in its proper season, {\i miz\u8217'harei zehirei bei} \u8212- people are careful with their {\i pesach} animals, and one need not be concerned about a stray {\i pesach} wandering unattended. {\i V\u8217'shelo bizmano} \u8212- and if we are past the time of {\i pesach}, a {\i pesach} that was not offered in its time reverts to the status of a {\i shelamim}, and {\i shelamim} is already covered.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i V\u8217'dilma bechor u\u8217'maaser ninhu} \u8212- perhaps it is a {\i bechor} or {\i maaser beheimah}, both of which are male and one year old. The {\i gemara} answers: {\i g\u8217'mai hilchasa l\u8217'michli\u8217'inhu b\u8217'muman} \u8212- the established {\i halacha} for both {\i bechor} and {\i maaser} is that once they develop a {\i mum}, the owners may eat them outside the {\i beit hamikdash} without further ceremony. Since one is already waiting for a {\i mum}, this scenario is automatically handled: if the animal turns out to be a {\i bechor} or {\i maaser}, the owner simply eats it once the {\i mum} appears. {\i Hacha nami b\u8217'muman mi\u8217'achli} \u8212- here too, one eats it upon the {\i mum}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Female Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} turns to the second part of the {\i mishna}: {\i nekeiva zivchei shelamim} \u8212- if it is a female, it is treated as a {\i shelamim}. The {\i gemara} initially questions why one cannot simply bring it directly \u8212- but the problem is {\i dilma todah hi}, it might be a {\i todah}, which is also female. Fine, one brings a {\i todah} as well and covers the bread requirement. But then: {\i v\u8217'dilma chatas hi} \u8212- perhaps it is a {\i chatat}, which is also female and which cannot be brought as a voluntary {\i nedavah}. If this animal might be a {\i chatat}, one cannot simply bring it as a {\i shelamim}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} notes that a {\i chatat} must be in its first year of life \u8212- {\i chatas bas shnata} \u8212- and accordingly, if the found female animal is two years old, the {\i chatat} possibility is eliminated. But if it is one year old, {\i ishtakach bas shnata mai} \u8212- what then?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Cell Confinement\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} cites a {\i baraita} in which {\i Chananya ben Chachinai} teaches that a one-year-old female goat \u8212- {\i ez bas shnata l\u8217'chatas} \u8212- evokes the verse\u8217's language for a {\i chatat}. The {\i gemara} immediately asks: but perhaps it is not a {\i chatat} at all \u8212- perhaps it is a {\i shelamim}? {\i Ella amar Abaye: k\u8217'chatas} \u8212- {\i Abaye} answers that it must be treated as a {\i chatat}. What does that mean in practice? {\i Konas\u8217'la l\u8217'chippa v\u8217'hi mesa mei\u8217'aleha} \u8212- one confines it in a cell and allows it to die on its own. There is no offering one can bring for it, since one cannot voluntarily bring a {\i chatat} that does not correspond to a specific obligation. Any other {\i kedushah} the animal might have \u8212- if it were a {\i shelamim} \u8212- can be transferred conditionally to substitute animals and dealt with through {\i olah} and {\i todah} dedications, allowing those to proceed, while the animal itself is left in confinement until it dies.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Kiddushin 56\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Me\u8217'ilah & Ma\u8217'aser Sheni\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Recap Me\u8217'ilah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 We pick up at the very bottom of 55b, where a {\i tanu rabbanan} opens a new section of the {\i gemara}. The preceding discussion had centered on a case of an animal found outside of Jerusalem whose identity as a particular type of {\i korban} was uncertain. That uncertainty served as an occasion to explore the position of Rabbi Meir, who holds that {\i me\u8217'ilah be\u8217'mezid} \u8212- intentional misappropriation of consecrated property \u8212- is halachically effective. The {\i gemara} had raised the possibility that according to Rabbi Meir, {\i me\u8217'ilah be\u8217'mezid} could operate even against something possessing {\i kedushat haguf}, intrinsic sanctity, since the power of a willful act might be sufficient to divest even a {\i korban} of its consecrated status. Under the ordinary rule, {\i me\u8217'ilah} does not remove {\i kedusha} from an object with intrinsic sanctity. The {\i gemara} further noted that this question would apply regardless of whether the animal in question was {\i kodshei kodashim} or {\i kodshei kallim}, with arguments running in both directions.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ma\u8217'aser Sheni Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 To step back and provide the broader framing: several days earlier, the {\i gemara} had addressed the foundational dispute over the status of {\i ma\u8217'aser sheni} \u8212- whether it is {\i mammon gavoah}, God\u8217's property, or {\i mammon hedyot}, the individual\u8217's own property encumbered with {\i kedusha}. The {\i gemara} concluded that by {\i ma\u8217'aser sheni} we rule like Rabbi Meir, who holds it is {\i mammon gavoah}, and that by {\i me\u8217'ilah} we rule against Rabbi Meir, rejecting the view that {\i me\u8217'ilah be\u8217'mezid} is halachically operative. Both issues, however, were explored in their own right regardless of the practical ruling. Now the {\i gemara} returns to the {\i ma\u8217'aser sheni} question and proceeds to examine several consequential and intricate {\i halachot} that arise from it.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Animal Purchase Ban\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rule: No Animal Purchase\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} cites a {\i baraita}: {\i ain lokchin behema b\u8217'dmei ma\u8217'aser sheni} \u8212- one may not purchase an animal with {\i ma\u8217'aser sheni} money. This prohibition applies outside of Jerusalem. Within Jerusalem, one may certainly purchase an animal and either slaughter it for its meat or bring it as a {\i korban}, since {\i ma\u8217'aser sheni} money is designated for food. The question is why one cannot purchase an animal outside of Jerusalem with that money.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rashi Explanation 1\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rashi offers two explanations. His first is that the only things one may bring to Jerusalem are the original fruit itself or the money to which it was transferred; one may not purchase other items outside and transport them in. According to this view, the animal purchase is invalid because the mechanism of {\i ma\u8217'aser sheni} transfer requires either the original produce or its monetary equivalent, not a further exchange.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafot Challenge\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot challenges Rashi\u8217's first explanation by pointing out that it is in fact permissible to transfer {\i ma\u8217'aser sheni} to money and then use that money to purchase food, which one then brings to Jerusalem in place of the original fruit. It is not the case that only the original fruit or the original money may be brought \u8212- other food purchased with that money is equally valid. If so, Tosafot cannot understand why purchasing an animal outside of Jerusalem should be inherently problematic, given that one could simply slaughter it or bring it as a {\i korban} upon arrival. Rashi\u8217's first rationale, Tosafot argues, is thus without basis.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rashi Explanation 2\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rashi therefore offers a second explanation: the problem is that an animal driven on foot to Jerusalem will lose value in transit. Unlike other goods that can be transported without deterioration, a sheep or cow walked along the road will become less healthy, less fat, and therefore worth less upon arrival. The {\i ma\u8217'aser sheni} money thus suffers a loss. This is a rabbinic concern \u8212- the practical devaluation of the sacred funds.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafot Explanation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot adds a third explanation, also a secondary rabbinic concern. If one purchases an animal with {\i ma\u8217'aser sheni} money and then delays bringing it to Jerusalem, the animal may give birth, and its offspring would also carry the status of {\i ma\u8217'aser sheni}. One could quickly find oneself responsible for entire flocks encumbered with {\i kedushat ma\u8217'aser sheni}, which creates obvious practical difficulties and potential for abuse. For one reason or another, then \u8212- whether the animal\u8217's devaluation during transport or the proliferation of {\i ma\u8217'aser sheni} offspring \u8212- the Sages prohibited purchasing animals outside Jerusalem with {\i ma\u8217'aser sheni} funds.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Unwitting Purchase\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shogeg: Sale Reversed\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} moves to the top of 56a: {\i ve\u8217'im lakach bishogeg} \u8212- if one purchased an animal unwittingly, not realizing he was using {\i ma\u8217'aser sheni} money, then {\i yachzeru damim limkomam}, the sale is reversed. The money is returned to the seller, the animal is returned to the buyer, and the transaction is undone.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mekach Taos Principle\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rashi explains that this reversal is grounded in the principle associated with Rabbi Yehuda: had the buyer known he was using {\i ma\u8217'aser sheni} money, he certainly would not have wanted to violate this rabbinic restriction. Since, had he known, he would not have agreed to the transaction, the sale is treated as a {\i mekach ta\u8217'os} \u8212- an erroneous transaction \u8212- and is therefore void. This principle was already encountered earlier in the {\i sugya}, and here it applies straightforwardly.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Mezid: Intent & Transfer\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mezid Rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now addresses the case of intentional purchase: {\i bemezid ta\u8217'aleh vetachel bamakom} \u8212- if he knew what he was doing and violated the prohibition anyway, the animal acquires {\i kedushat ma\u8217'aser sheni}, and he should bring it to Jerusalem and consume it there with that {\i kedusha}. What is done is done; the appropriate response is to move forward and treat the animal as having assumed the sanctity of the money used to purchase it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Yehuda Limit\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yehuda, however, qualifies this rule. He asks: {\i bameh devarim amurim} \u8212- when does this ruling apply? Only when the buyer was {\i bimitkaven velakach techila leshem shelamim} \u8212- when he intentionally purchased the animal with the conscious purpose of having its {\i kedusha} transfer, and with the plan to bring it to Jerusalem and eat it there with {\i kedushat ma\u8217'aser sheni}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shelamim Use Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The natural and obvious case Rabbi Yehuda envisions is the following. A person buys a sheep with {\i ma\u8217'aser sheni} money; the sheep takes on {\i kedushat ma\u8217'aser sheni}. He could simply slaughter it and eat the meat as {\i ma\u8217'aser sheni} food in Jerusalem. But since he is going to Jerusalem anyway, he takes the opportunity to bring it as a {\i shelamim}, thereby fulfilling his obligation to bring a {\i korban} while still retaining the ability to eat the meat \u8212- since a {\i shelamim} cannot be an {\i olah}, which would require burning the entire animal and preclude eating it. This double purpose is entirely appropriate, and in such a case Rabbi Yehuda\u8217's rule applies: proceed, bring the animal up, eat it in Jerusalem.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Profane Transfer Concern\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Problem Scenario\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yehuda continues: {\i aval bimitkavein lehotzi ma\u8217'os ma\u8217'aser sheni lechulin} \u8212- but what if the buyer\u8217's intention was not to transfer the {\i kedusha} to the animal but rather to spend the {\i ma\u8217'aser sheni} money and obtain a {\i chulin} animal in exchange? In this scenario, the buyer wishes to have steaks in his backyard rather than travel to Jerusalem, and he \u8220"sticks\u8221" the seller with the {\i ma\u8217'aser sheni} money. The seller, for his part, has agreed to this arrangement \u8212- perhaps because he is being offered more than he would ordinarily receive for the animal.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafot Reading\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot\u8217's reading of this passage \u8212- which is clearer than Rashi\u8217's \u8212- presents a contrast between two intentional scenarios. In the first, the buyer knowingly transfers the {\i kedusha} of the money to the animal; he intends to bring a {\i shelamim} or eat the animal in Jerusalem with {\i kedushat ma\u8217'aser sheni}, and the transaction functions as an intentional and legitimate transfer of {\i kedusha}. In the second, the buyer knowingly spends the {\i ma\u8217'aser sheni} money \u8212- {\i lehotzi}, to spend it \u8212- in exchange for a {\i chulin} animal, deliberately avoiding the {\i kedusha} obligation and leaving the seller holding the {\i ma\u8217'aser sheni} money without a corresponding transfer of {\i kedusha} to the purchased object.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The phrase {\i lehotzi ma\u8217'os ma\u8217'aser sheni lechulin} thus means: to spend the {\i ma\u8217'aser sheni} funds in a transaction designed to yield {\i chulin} goods, while the money retains its {\i kedusha} in the seller\u8217's possession. The buyer intends to avoid the {\i kedusha} obligations entirely, and the seller may be complicit or simply uninformed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Misuse Risk\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This scenario is deeply problematic. The {\i ma\u8217'aser sheni} money belongs in Jerusalem \u8212- it must be spent on food there, or its {\i kedusha} must transfer to something that will serve that purpose. By structuring the transaction as the buyer did, the money is diverted from its intended purpose. There is a real concern of {\i zilzul ma\u8217'aser sheni} \u8212- a degradation or misuse of the sacred funds. The seller may take the money and, thinking or hoping that the {\i kedusha} has been lifted, use it for ordinary purposes. The buyer has no intention of bringing anything to Jerusalem.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Reversal Solution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Accordingly, in this scenario \u8212- {\i bein shogeg bein mezid}, whether the buyer was {\i shogeg} or {\i mezid} in a relevant sense \u8212- the sale must be reversed. The {\i gemara} notes that {\i shogeg} here refers to a buyer who did not even realize he was using {\i ma\u8217'aser sheni} money at all, in which case {\i mekach ta\u8217'os} applies directly. The {\i mezid} case refers to a buyer who knew it was {\i ma\u8217'aser sheni} money and intentionally structured the deal to keep the animal as {\i chulin} while leaving the seller with the encumbered funds. In both cases, {\i yachazir damim limkomam} \u8212- the sale is reversed. The Rabbis do not approve of this arrangement, and the reversal of the sale is the remedy. The concern is clear: the money is being diverted from its sacred purpose, and the seller may well misuse it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This resolution is halachically sufficient, and the practical enforceability is a separate matter. The general principle is that by firmly establishing the halachic ruling, communal pressure and individual conscience play their role. Moreover, it is rarely a case of pure, calculating wickedness \u8212- most situations are more ambiguous, and the ruling creates the framework within which people are expected to act.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Kiddushin Exception\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kiddushin Valid\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now raises a comparison to the {\i mishnah} before us: {\i Rabbi Yehuda omer bemezid kideish} \u8212- if one intentionally used {\i ma\u8217'aser sheni} money to betroth a woman, the {\i kiddushin} is valid. Here, too, the {\i ma\u8217'aser sheni} money retains its {\i kedusha} in the woman\u8217's hands \u8212- there is nothing for the {\i kedusha} to transfer to, since a woman is not a food item that can be eaten in Jerusalem.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Why No Reversal?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This raises a pointed question. The woman is in an analogous position to the seller of the animal: she has received {\i ma\u8217'aser sheni} money, and in the back of her mind she might rationalize \u8212- as the animal seller might \u8212- that the {\i kedusha} has left the money and she can use it freely. The same concern of {\i zilzul ma\u8217'aser sheni} should apply. So why does the {\i gemara} not demand that the {\i kiddushin} be reversed, just as the animal sale is reversed? It is clearly a less drastic remedy to reverse a sale than to void a {\i kiddushin} \u8212- the latter would leave a woman thinking she is unmarried when she may not be, with all the attendant consequences for {\i ishut}. And yet, there is still a conceptual question: if the same misuse concern exists, why does it not generate at least a demand for reversal?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Woman Knows Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Elazar answers: {\i isha yoda\u8217'at she\u8217'ein me\u8217'os ma\u8217'aser sheni mischallin al yadah, v\u8217'ola v\u8217'ochla b\u8217'Yerushalayim} \u8212- the woman knows that {\i ma\u8217'aser sheni} money does not lose its {\i kedusha} through her receipt of it, and she therefore intends to go up to Jerusalem and spend it properly there. But this is not simply because she is a woman receiving {\i kiddushin} money \u8212- the key factor is that she is a {\i chavera}, a member of that scrupulous, learned subgroup that is expert in precisely these laws. The term {\i chaver} or {\i chavera} does not denote scholarly achievement per se but rather membership in a community distinguished by its careful practice of these {\i halachot}. Because she is a {\i chavera} and is {\i yoda\u8217'at} \u8212- she knows \u8212- we trust that the money will be used properly and do not demand reversal.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This answer is, in a sense, a limiting principle: the case of {\i kiddushin} in the {\i mishnah} is not a general rule but a specific case premised on the woman\u8217's knowledge. The real rule, applicable across all scenarios, is that wherever we fear the money will be misused, we demand reversal. The {\i kiddushin} case is bracketed off as exceptional not primarily because of the severity of undoing a marriage \u8212- though that is surely also a factor \u8212- but because of the presumption that a {\i chavera} will handle the funds properly.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Non-Food Purchases\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Challenge Posed\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Yirmiyah now challenges this framework. He raises the case of purchasing non-food items with {\i ma\u8217'aser sheni} money: {\i beheima tmei\u8217'a, avadim, v\u8217'karka\u8217'os} \u8212- a non-kosher animal, slaves, or land. In all of these cases, the {\i kedusha} of the {\i ma\u8217'aser sheni} money obviously cannot transfer, since {\i kedusha} can only transfer to food items. The money therefore retains its {\i kedushat ma\u8217'aser sheni} in the seller\u8217's hands.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Presumption of Knowledge\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Yirmiyah\u8217's point is this: if the buyer knows that {\i kedusha} cannot transfer to non-food items \u8212- and this should be common knowledge, since the {\i halacha} that {\i kedusha} transfers only to food is well established \u8212- then by the same logic we applied to the {\i chavera} who received {\i kiddushin} money, the seller should be presumed to know that the money retains its {\i kedusha} and should handle it accordingly. If that presumption of knowledge justified not reversing the {\i kiddushin}, it should equally justify not reversing the sale of a non-food item.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gemara Response\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} responds that in fact one may not purchase these non-food items with {\i ma\u8217'aser sheni} money \u8212- {\i ein lokchim beheima tmei\u8217'a avadim v\u8217'karka\u8217'os b\u8217'me\u8217'os ma\u8217'aser sheni afilu b\u8217'Yerushalayim}, not even in Jerusalem. However, if one did purchase such items, there is an alternative remedy rather than reversal: {\i v\u8217'im lakach yochal k\u8217'negdan}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Yochel K\u8217'Negdan\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Non-Food Remedy\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Yochal k\u8217'negdan} \u8212- the buyer must spend an equivalent sum on food and eat it in Jerusalem. He is not required to reverse the sale; instead, he personally undertakes the obligation that the {\i ma\u8217'aser sheni} money was supposed to fulfill. This alternative remedy applies when the sale of a non-food item cannot be reversed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Interpretation Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 There is a {\i machlokes} among the {\i ba\u8217'alei haTosafos} regarding the precise mechanics of {\i yochal k\u8217'negdan}. One view holds that when the buyer spends his own money in Jerusalem on food, the {\i kedusha} of the original {\i ma\u8217'aser sheni} money \u8212- still sitting with the seller \u8212- transfers onto the buyer\u8217's new expenditure, and the seller\u8217's money thereby becomes {\i chulin}. The other view holds that the seller\u8217's money remains {\i kodesh} and must still find its way to proper use; the buyer\u8217's {\i yochal k\u8217'negdan} expenditure is a {\i knas} visited upon him but does not automatically discharge the seller\u8217's {\i ma\u8217'aser sheni} obligation. Either way, the buyer must spend equivalent money and eat it in Jerusalem; the question of whether this action discharges the {\i kedusha} on the seller\u8217's funds remains the subject of dispute.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Decision Framework\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Solutions Differ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 At this point, the {\i gemara} confronts an apparent inconsistency. We seem to have different legal solutions for different types of objects: reversal for a kosher animal, {\i yochal k\u8217'negdan} for non-food items, and nothing at all for {\i kiddushin}. The {\i gemara} asks why these distinct solutions apply to these distinct object types.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Seller Presence Key\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Shmuel answers: {\i keshebarach} \u8212- the distinguishing factor is not the type of object at all, but whether the seller is present. If the seller has fled or is otherwise unavailable, it is impossible to reverse the sale. In that case, {\i yochal k\u8217'negdan} is the only available remedy. If the seller is present, reversal is possible and is the preferred remedy.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Decision Chart\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara}\u8217's framework thus yields the following decision structure. The first question is: does the recipient know that the {\i ma\u8217'aser sheni} money retains its {\i kedusha} and will use it properly? If yes \u8212- as in the case of the {\i chavera} who receives {\i kiddushin} money \u8212- no remedy is necessary and the transaction stands. If no \u8212- the normal case, in which we fear the money will be misused \u8212- then we proceed to the second question: is the seller present? If present, we reverse the sale. If not present, we apply {\i yochal k\u8217'negdan}. The differences between the {\i mishnah}\u8217's cases involving a kosher animal, a non-kosher animal, and {\i kiddushin} are not intrinsic to those categories but reflect these underlying circumstantial differences that happen to characterize each typical scenario.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Why Reverse?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Default Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} presses further. Given that both reversal and {\i yochal k\u8217'negdan} are available remedies when the seller is present, why is reversal treated as the default? Why not simply apply {\i yochal k\u8217'negdan} in all cases?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Fine the Seller\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} answers: {\i kansinan lemocher} \u8212- we impose a penalty on the seller. Reversal is the means by which this penalty is administered. When the seller is present, he is held accountable, and reversal \u8212- forcing him to undo a transaction he sought to complete \u8212- is the appropriate sanction.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Seller More Eager\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot explains why the seller is the one penalized rather than the buyer. If the remedy were {\i yochal k\u8217'negdan} rather than reversal, the seller would clearly prefer that outcome: he has already sold his animal or property, and the buyer is the one burdened with the obligation to spend equivalent money in Jerusalem. {\i Yochal k\u8217'negdan} is a {\i knas} on the buyer. Reversal, by contrast, is a {\i knas} on the seller, who is forced to undo the very transaction he was eager to complete. Moreover, there is a general presumption that the seller is always more eager to sell than the buyer is to buy \u8212- the seller wanted to be rid of his goods, and reversing the sale frustrates that interest. Thus, when the seller is present, reversal is the appropriate mechanism.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rectify Location\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Seller as Enabler\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 At this point the {\i gemara} introduces the famous principle: {\i lav achbara ganav ela chura ganav} \u8212- it is not the rat that steals, it is the hole it escapes through. The seller, by being available and willing to transact with {\i ma\u8217'aser sheni} money, provided the very opportunity that enabled the misuse. Without the seller\u8217's participation, the buyer could not have diverted the sacred funds. In this sense, the seller is the enabling condition, the \u8220"hole,\u8221" and bears responsibility accordingly.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Buyer More Culpable\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} itself, however, pushes back against this reasoning. After all, {\i ve\u8217'ee lav achbara chura mai ka\u8217'avad} \u8212- what good would the hole have done without the rat? The buyer is the one who actually possessed the {\i ma\u8217'aser sheni} money, who chose to spend it in this improper manner, who sought to obtain {\i chulin} goods while leaving the seller with encumbered funds. He is, if anything, the more culpable party \u8212- the one whose act we would most naturally identify as the {\i aveira}. Why then do we penalize the seller rather than the buyer?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rectify Not Punish\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} resolves this with the word {\i mistavra} \u8212- it stands to reason. Both the buyer and the seller are complicit; both parties participated in the problematic transaction, and arguments can be made for penalizing either. But the {\i gemara} shifts the frame: {\i kol heicha de\u8217'ika issura hatam kansinan} \u8212- wherever the problem currently resides, that is where we focus our intervention. And where is the problem? The {\i ma\u8217'aser sheni} money is now with the seller. That money has {\i kedusha}; it does not belong where it is. The goal is not to punish individuals but to rectify the situation \u8212- to return the money to a position where it can fulfill its sacred purpose. Since the money is in the seller\u8217's hands, we address that situation directly by reversing the sale and returning the money to the buyer, from whom it must then properly make its way to Jerusalem. The asymmetry of penalty is thus not really about culpability at all; it is about where the money is and what needs to be done about it.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Issurei Hana\u8217'ah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Six Categories\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 With the {\i ma\u8217'aser sheni} and {\i me\u8217'ilah} discussion concluded, the {\i gemara} now transitions to a new {\i mishnah}. We move from objects that are {\i kodesh} \u8212- sanctified \u8212- to a different category: {\i issurei hana\u8217'ah}, objects from which one is forbidden to derive any benefit. These are not objects possessing {\i kedusha} in the manner of {\i ma\u8217'aser sheni} or {\i hekdesh}, but objects that the Torah has placed entirely beyond the realm of permissible use.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Object Examples\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} enumerates several categories. {\i Orla} is fruit from the first three years of a tree\u8217's growth. {\i Kilai hakerem} refers specifically to the mixture of grape vine with other grain crops; a {\i kilayim} of two grain species without the vine would not render the produce forbidden, but the combination of vine with grain does. The {\i shor haniskal} is an ox sentenced to stoning because it gored a human being. The {\i eglah arufah} is the heifer whose neck is broken in the ritual performed when a murder victim is found outside a city. {\i Tziporei metzora} are the birds used in the purification process of the {\i metzora}. {\i Se\u8217'ar nazir} is the hair of the {\i nazir} that is shaved upon the completion of the {\i nezirut} period. {\i Peter chamor} is the firstborn of a donkey. {\i Basar bechalav} is meat and milk cooked together. And {\i chullin shenishchatu ba\u8217'azarah} are non-sanctified animals slaughtered in the {\i Beit HaMikdash}. All of these are {\i assurim behana\u8217'ah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kiddushin Invalid\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If one attempts to betroth a woman using any of these objects \u8212- {\i hamikadesh be\u8217'orla eino mekudeshet} \u8212- the {\i kiddushin} is invalid. The reason is that these objects are legally valueless: since one may not derive benefit from them in any recognized way, they carry no legal market value, and {\i kiddushin} requires transfer of an object worth at least a {\i perutah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 No Status Transfer\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A crucial distinction marks off {\i issurei hana\u8217'ah} from {\i kedushat ma\u8217'aser sheni}: {\i kedusha} can transfer. If I sell a {\i ma\u8217'aser sheni} item, the money I receive takes on the {\i kedusha}. With {\i issurei hana\u8217'ah}, no such transfer occurs. If one transgresses and sells a cheeseburger to another person for ten dollars, having violated the prohibition against deriving benefit from {\i basar bechalav}, the ten dollars received is entirely {\i mutar} \u8212- plain, ordinary money, free of any forbidden status. The {\i issur hana\u8217'ah} does not follow the proceeds. Therefore, if one uses that ten dollars to betroth a woman, the {\i kiddushin} is valid: the money is perfectly ordinary, and the {\i isur} on the original item does not encumber the sale proceeds.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Tosafot Discussion\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Market Value Q\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot raises a significant question on the {\i mishnah}\u8217's rule that {\i kiddushin} with {\i issurei hana\u8217'ah} is invalid. The assumption is that such objects are valueless. But Tosafot points out that {\i chazal} permit deriving benefit from {\i issurei hana\u8217'ah} in a non-standard manner \u8212- {\i shelo k\u8217'derech hana\u8217'aso}. For instance, olives that have not yet ripened can be used for medicinal purposes in a non-food application. Since there is some category of permissible use, however narrow, someone might be willing to pay something for the item. If so, the object does carry some market value, and {\i kiddushin} with it should in principle be valid.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Value Answer\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot\u8217's first answer is that the quantity in question simply does not reach {\i shaveh perutah}. Even if theoretically someone would pay something for the object because of its limited non-standard use, the amount of the object being used in the {\i kiddushin} is insufficient to constitute a {\i perutah}\u8217's worth of value via that restricted use. Only if one had enough of the substance to generate a full {\i perutah} of value through that unusual channel would {\i kiddushin} be potentially valid.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Awareness Answer\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot\u8217's second answer introduces a different principle. Even granting that enough of the substance is present to be worth a {\i perutah} through non-standard use, if the woman is unaware that her use of the object is restricted to this unusual mode \u8212- if she assumed she was receiving something she could use normally \u8212- then the {\i kiddushin} is void as a {\i mekach ta\u8217'os}. She never would have agreed to the {\i kiddushin} had she known she was receiving something with such a limited and atypical form of permissible use. This answer effectively transforms the {\i mishnah}\u8217's ruling from a categorical legal statement about the valuelessness of {\i issurei hana\u8217'ah} into a circumstantial rule dependent on the woman\u8217's awareness.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rashba: Lawful Valuation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Rashba offers what is likely the most straightforward {\i pshat} in the {\i mishnah}, and it cuts through both of Tosafot\u8217's answers. Tosafot\u8217's two answers effectively concede that if there is enough of the substance and if the woman is aware of the restricted use, one could marry a woman with {\i issurei hana\u8217'ah} \u8212- which is plainly not what the {\i mishnah} is saying. The Rashba therefore takes a more fundamental position: it is irrelevant what someone might actually pay for an {\i issur hana\u8217'ah} object. The law defines these objects as valueless. Since the Torah forbids any benefit that we would legally recognize as {\i hana\u8217'ah}, there is no legally cognizable value. Whatever a buyer might actually pay for such an object reflects a transaction between private parties that we do not prohibit \u8212- but it does not alter the legal classification of the object\u8217's value. As a matter of {\i halacha}, the object\u8217's value is zero. {\i Kiddushin} performed with a legally valueless object is invalid, regardless of what private market conditions might dictate.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Sources & Proofs\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Orlah Source\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now undertakes to trace the biblical sources for each of the {\i issurei hana\u8217'ah} listed in the {\i mishnah}. Beginning with {\i orla}: {\i ba\u8217'orlah minalan} \u8212- from where do we derive that {\i orla} is forbidden in benefit? A {\i beraita} is cited: the {\i pasuk} says {\i orlah lo ye\u8217'achel} \u8212- the uncircumcised fruit shall not be eaten. From this verse alone, I would know only that eating it is forbidden. How do I know that benefit is also prohibited \u8212- that one cannot use it for dyeing, or for fuel for a candle? The answer comes from the continuation of the verse: {\i va\u8217'araltem arlato} \u8212- make it entirely off-limits, as though it were uncircumcised. This language of total exclusion \u8212- {\i lirabot et kulam} \u8212- extends the prohibition to all forms of benefit.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kilai Source\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 For {\i kilai hakerem}: {\i bekilai hakerem minalan} \u8212- from where do we know this is forbidden in benefit? Chizkiyah cites the verse {\i pen tikdash} \u8212- lest it become sanctified. Chizkiyah reads this not as {\i tikdash} (become holy) but as {\i pen tukad esh} \u8212- lest it be deserving of burning by fire. This reading implies total prohibition: an object deserving to be burned is one from which no benefit may be derived.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Ashi Read\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Ashi proposes an alternative reading of {\i pen tikdash}: take it literally as {\i pen yihyeh kodesh} \u8212- lest it become like something holy. And since holy things \u8212- {\i hekdesh} \u8212- are forbidden in benefit, the same should apply to {\i kilai hakerem}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Objection\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} objects to Rav Ashi\u8217's reading. {\i Kedusha} is not a perfect analogy, because an object possessing {\i kedusha} can have that {\i kedusha} transferred to money, whereupon the original object becomes {\i chulin}. If {\i kilai hakerem} were truly analogous to {\i kodesh}, one might think that transferring its \u8220"status\u8221" to money would render the original produce permissible \u8212- which is clearly not the case.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Resolution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} therefore concludes: {\i ela k\u8217'deChizkiyah} \u8212- better to adopt Chizkiyah\u8217's {\i drasha} of {\i pen tukad esh} rather than the {\i kedusha} analogy. An object deserving to be burned is absolutely off-limits, and this resolves the difficulty raised against Rav Ashi\u8217's reading.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Shor Haniskal\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Neveilah Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 For the {\i shor haniskal}: {\i shor haniskal minalan} \u8212- from where do we know the stoned ox is forbidden in benefit? A {\i beraita} begins by noting that the verse commands {\i sakol yisakel hashor} \u8212- the ox shall certainly be stoned. Once stoned, the ox is a {\i neveilah}, an animal that did not undergo proper {\i shechita}. And a {\i neveilah} is already forbidden in eating.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Redundancy Q\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If so, why does the Torah add {\i velo ye\u8217'achel et besaro} \u8212- and its flesh shall not be eaten? We already know from the {\i neveilah} status that eating is forbidden. What does this additional prohibition accomplish?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Slaughter Teaching\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i beraita} explains: {\i she\u8217'im shechato le\u8217'achar shenigmar dino assur ba\u8217'achilah} \u8212- to teach that even if one slaughtered the ox properly after the death sentence was pronounced \u8212- thereby circumventing the {\i neveilah} status and producing what would otherwise be ordinary slaughtered meat \u8212- it is nevertheless forbidden to eat. The ruling of {\i shor haniskal} takes effect at the moment the {\i gmar din} is rendered; from that point forward, all the {\i halachot} of {\i shor haniskal} apply, including the eating prohibition, regardless of how the animal subsequently dies.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Benefit Source\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But that addresses the eating prohibition. For the forbidden benefit aspect: {\i talmud lomar u\u8217'va\u8217'al hashor naki} \u8212- the verse adds that the owner of the ox is {\i naki}, clean. The {\i gemara} cites Shimon ben Zoma\u8217's {\i drasha}: this is like the expression {\i yatza ploni naki minichasav} \u8212- that person was cleaned out of his possessions, he had no benefit from them at all. The phrase {\i ba\u8217'al hashor naki} thus teaches that the owner is \u8220"cleaned out\u8221" \u8212- he retains no benefit whatsoever from the ox, meaning all {\i hana\u8217'ah} from it is forbidden.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Lo Ye\u8217'achel Rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Standard Rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Eliezer articulates a general principle: {\i kol makom shene\u8217'emar lo ye\u8217'achel, lo tochal, lo tochlu} \u8212- whenever the Torah uses the formulation \u8220"shall not eat\u8221" in any of its conjugations, the verse includes {\i both} the prohibition on eating and the prohibition on deriving benefit: {\i echad issur achilah ve\u8217'echad issur hana\u8217'ah bemashma}. The standard biblical prohibition to not eat encompasses the full prohibition on benefit.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Exception Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The only exception is where the {\i pasuk} itself explicitly permits benefit. The example is {\i neveilah}: the Torah says one may not eat a {\i neveilah}, but then adds {\i lager asher bish\u8217'arecha t\u8217'tnena va\u8217'achalhah o machor l\u8217'nochri} \u8212- you may give it to the resident alien or sell it to a foreigner. This explicit permission to sell demonstrates that benefit is permitted. Absent such explicit permission, {\i lo ye\u8217'achel} includes the prohibition on {\i hana\u8217'ah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shor Application\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now applies this principle back to the {\i shor haniskal} and raises a difficulty. Given Rabbi Eliezer\u8217's rule that {\i lo ye\u8217'achel} always implies {\i issur hana\u8217'ah}, why did the {\i gemara} need the {\i drasha} of {\i ba\u8217'al hashor naki} to derive that {\i hana\u8217'ah} is forbidden? Let the extra {\i lo ye\u8217'achel et besaro} itself teach us the prohibition on {\i hana\u8217'ah}!\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The resolution: Rabbi Eliezer\u8217's principle applies when the eating prohibition itself was derived from the {\i lo ye\u8217'achel} of that very verse. But for the {\i shor haniskal}, the eating prohibition did not derive from {\i lo ye\u8217'achel et besaro} \u8212- it was already established from the {\i neveilah} status, which flows from {\i sakol yisakel}. Since the eating prohibition was independently derived from a different source, the {\i lo ye\u8217'achel} cannot serve double duty by additionally teaching {\i issur hana\u8217'ah}; the extra {\i lo ye\u8217'achel et besaro} must therefore be doing something else entirely. And what does it teach? {\i Af al gav deshachatei ke\u8217'ein basar assar} \u8212- even if the ox was slaughtered and has the character of ordinary meat, it remains forbidden. That is the {\i chiddush} of the phrase {\i et besaro}. The source for {\i issur hana\u8217'ah} must therefore come from elsewhere \u8212- and that source is {\i ba\u8217'al hashor naki}, which teaches that the owner is entirely cleaned out and retains no benefit whatsoever.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Kiddushin 57\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Shor Haniskal\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishna Overview\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i daf} before us \u8212- Kiddushin 57 \u8212- opens at the bottom of 56b, dealing with the {\i mishna} that lists items forbidden in benefit ({\i issur hana\u8217'ah}), and therefore if a man attempted to betroth a woman using any of them, she is not {\i mekudeshet}, since such items carry no legally transferable value. Among the cases discussed is the {\i shor haniskal} \u8212- the ox condemned to stoning because it killed a person \u8212- which remains forbidden in benefit even if it was subsequently {\i shechted}, rather than actually stoned.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mar Zutra Challenge\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} raises a challenge \u8212- {\i matkif lah} Mar Zutra: when the {\i Torah} says that even its flesh ({\i besoro}) is forbidden, perhaps that applies only where one performed something akin to stoning itself \u8212- for instance, where one sharpened a stone into a blade and used it to slaughter the animal, {\i damechzei kisekila}, it resembles stoning. But if one simply used an ordinary knife, perhaps that would render the animal permitted.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Slaughter Methods\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} responds on two grounds. First, {\i mi saki b\u8217'oraisa kesiv} \u8212- the {\i Torah} nowhere specifies a knife, and therefore there is no halakhic basis for distinguishing between one method of {\i shechita} and another; if one type renders the animal permitted, so should any other. Second, there is an explicit {\i braisa}: one may {\i shecht} with any implement \u8212- {\i bein betzur bein bezechuchis bein bekurmis shel kano} \u8212- whether with a rock, a piece of glass, or a reed splinter. The methods are legally equivalent, and the challenge collapses.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Et Particle Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Dual Prohibitions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now observes that both the prohibition of eating ({\i issur achila}) and the prohibition of benefit ({\i issur hana\u8217'ah}) are derived from the same phrase \u8212- {\i lo ye\u8217'achel es besoro}, its flesh shall not be eaten. Given that, what further need is there for the phrase {\i u\u8217'va\u8217'al hashor naki}, \u8220"and the owner of the ox shall be clean\u8221" \u8212- which has also been used to teach that the owner derives no benefit?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Skin Benefit\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The answer is that {\i u\u8217'va\u8217'al hashor naki} is needed to establish that one may not derive benefit even from the hide. Without it, one might have reasoned from {\i lo ye\u8217'achel besoro} \u8212- the word {\i besaro}, its flesh, is what is written \u8212- that the flesh is forbidden but the skin is permitted, and one could therefore produce leather from it. The phrase {\i u\u8217'va\u8217'al hashor naki} closes that possibility.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But what of those {\i tannaim} who deduce something else from {\i u\u8217'va\u8217'al hashor naki} \u8212- for instance, that one does not pay half-damages ({\i chatzi kofer}) or compensate for miscarried fetuses ({\i demei vlados}) in the case of a goring ox \u8212- and thus do not use that phrase to teach the prohibition on the skin? From where do they derive that the skin too is forbidden in benefit?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The answer is: from the word {\i es} \u8212- {\i es besoro} \u8212- the particle {\i es} coming to include {\i hamatfel lebesoro}, something that is secondary or attached to the flesh, namely the skin. And the opposing position \u8212- the one that does derive the skin prohibition from {\i u\u8217'va\u8217'al hashor naki} \u8212- does not employ the {\i et} particle hermeneutically at all.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shimon Ha\u8217'amsuni\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This introduces one of the most striking passages in the {\i gemara}. At the top of 57a, a famous {\i braisa} is cited: Shimon Ha\u8217'amsuni \u8212- and some say Nechemia Ha\u8217'amsuni \u8212- would systematically interpret every instance of the particle {\i es} in the {\i Torah} to indicate an inclusion, something additional being taught. This was his organizing principle across the entire {\i Torah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Keivan shehigia le\u8217'es Hashem Elokecha tira} \u8212- until he reached the verse commanding fear of God \u8212- {\i perash}, he withdrew. He could not bring himself to say that anything could be compared to or included alongside fear of God. His students immediately raised the obvious problem: {\i kol esin shedarashta mah tehei aleihem} \u8212- if this particular {\i es} teaches nothing, does that not undermine every other instance you have interpreted? Is the hermeneutical principle now invalidated retroactively?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 His response was remarkable: {\i keshem shekibalti sachar al hadrisha kach kibalti sachar al haprisha} \u8212- just as I received reward for the interpretations, so too I will receive reward for the retraction. There is a question of how far this concession actually goes. One reading is that he means: I still believe all those other interpretations are true; I simply cannot in good conscience force this one, and if my inability to answer here casts doubt in people\u8217's minds about my other teachings, so be it \u8212- my intellectual honesty demands no less. The other, more radical reading is that {\i sachar al haprisha} means he was willing to surrender all those teachings entirely, rather than compromise on a {\i drasha} he did not believe. That would make the gesture even more extraordinary \u8212- the full sacrifice of a lifetime\u8217's learning in service of {\i emes}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Akiva Resolution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Ad sheba Rabbi Akiva v\u8217'limed} \u8212- until Rabbi Akiva came and taught that {\i es Hashem Elokecha tira l\u8217'rabos talmidei chachamim}: the {\i es} particle in that verse comes to include {\i talmidei chachamim}, whose fear and awe bears at least an analogical relationship \u8212- obviously not equivalence \u8212- to the awe of God, inasmuch as they represent and transmit His {\i Torah}. One elegant reading of this episode is that it was precisely Shimon Ha\u8217'amsuni\u8217's act of {\i perisha}, his public withdrawal rather than offering a forced answer, that made possible Rabbi Akiva\u8217's insight: only a tradition that demonstrates such radical commitment to {\i emes} could produce {\i talmidei chachamim} genuinely worthy of being mentioned alongside the awe of God.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Egla/Metzora Analogy\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Egla Arufah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} proceeds to ask: from where do we know that the {\i egla arufa} \u8212- the heifer whose neck is broken as part of the unsolved murder ritual \u8212- is forbidden in benefit? The answer given in the name of the school of Rabbi Yannai is: {\i kapar kesiv bah k\u8217'kodshim} \u8212- the {\i Torah} uses the language of atonement ({\i kapar}) in connection with the {\i egla arufa}, and therefore it operates on the same principle as sacrifices, which are forbidden in benefit.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tzippurei Metzora\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Regarding the {\i tzippurei metzora} \u8212- the birds brought as part of the purification process for a {\i metzora} \u8212- the source is derived from a {\i braisa} of the school of Rabbi Yishmael. The {\i braisa} formulates a parallel structure: inside the {\i Beis Hamikdash}, the {\i Torah} mentions something that is {\i machshir} (changes the status of the {\i metzora}, namely the {\i asham} brought by the {\i metzora}) alongside something that is {\i mechaper} (provides atonement, meaning all other {\i korbanos}). The same pairing appears outside the {\i Beis Hamikdash}: something {\i machshir} (the birds of the {\i metzora}) alongside something {\i mechaper} (the {\i egla arufa}).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Parallel Structure\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The logical structure is a {\i gezeira shava} of principle: just as inside the {\i Beis Hamikdash} the {\i machshir} \u8212- the {\i metzora\u8217's asham} \u8212- is treated like the {\i mechaper} and is forbidden in benefit, so too outside the {\i Beis Hamikdash} the {\i machshir} \u8212- the {\i metzora\u8217's} birds \u8212- is treated like the {\i mechaper}, and since the {\i egla arufa} is forbidden in benefit, so too the birds are forbidden in benefit.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Metzora Birds Timeli (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yochanan/Lakish\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 An {\i amoraic} dispute now turns on the question of precisely {\i when} the {\i tzippurei metzora} become forbidden in benefit. Rabbi Yochanan holds: {\i mishe\u8217'as shechita} \u8212- from the moment of {\i shechita}. One bird is slaughtered and the other is dipped in its blood and water and released; the act of {\i shechita} is what triggers the prohibition. Reish Lakish holds: {\i mishe\u8217'as lekicha} \u8212- from the moment of purchase.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is worth noting \u8212- as {\i Rashi} and most {\i Rishonim} understand \u8212- that the sent bird also acquires forbidden status at some point, but ultimately becomes permitted once sent free. According to Reish Lakish, both birds become forbidden from the moment of purchase; the released bird subsequently regains its permitted status upon release. According to Rabbi Yochanan, both become forbidden from the moment of {\i shechita}, and the released bird then regains permitted status. {\i Rashi}\u8217's alternative reading \u8212- that according to Rabbi Yochanan only the slaughtered bird ever becomes forbidden while the sent bird was never forbidden \u8212- does not accord easily with the plain language of the {\i mishna}, which lists \u8220"the birds of the {\i metzora}\u8221" without distinction.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yochanan\u8217's reasoning is simply that {\i shechita hi d\u8217'asra lah} \u8212- it is the act of {\i shechita} that creates the forbidden status. Reish Lakish\u8217's reasoning is that we derive the prohibition of the {\i metzora} birds from {\i egla arufa}. The {\i egla arufa} is forbidden {\i michayim} \u8212- while still alive \u8212- just as sacrifices become forbidden while still alive. Therefore, the {\i metzora} birds are likewise forbidden while still alive, and since they are alive at the time of purchase, from that point the prohibition takes effect.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Egla Timeline\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yannai Memory\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now turns to the {\i egla arufa} itself: when precisely does it become forbidden? Rabbi Yannai says: {\i gvul shamati bah v\u8217'shachachti} \u8212- I recall that there is a significant point in the process that triggers the prohibition, but I no longer remember which point.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Wadi Descent\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The colleagues \u8212- {\i chavraya} \u8212- propose: {\i yeridasah lenachal eisan hi osartah} \u8212- it is the descent into the {\i nachal eitan}, the wadi, that renders it forbidden. This is a concrete, explicit moment described in the {\i Torah}, making it the natural candidate for the triggering event.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Consistency Test\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then asks: if the {\i egla arufa} is not forbidden from the moment of purchase, should we apply that reasoning consistently to the {\i metzora} birds \u8212- and say that they too are not forbidden from the time of purchase?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two Moments\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} rejects this: {\i hachi hashta? hasam is lei gvul achrina hacha leis lei gvul achrina} \u8212- the situations are not parallel. The {\i egla arufa} has its own distinct moment \u8212- the descent to the wadi \u8212- which is explicitly referenced in the {\i Torah}. The {\i metzora} birds have no such equivalent moment. And as {\i Rashi} points out, while the {\i Torah} says nothing analogous to the descent of the wadi by the birds, it does say explicitly that the birds must be purchased ({\i tikach}), thereby establishing the moment of acquisition as the operative trigger. Each has its own moment, but both become forbidden while still alive.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Metzora Birds Timeli (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Braisa Proof\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yochanan now challenges Reish Lakish from a {\i braisa}. The {\i braisa} expounds: {\i kol tzippor tehorah tocheilu lerabos es hameshulachas} \u8212- \u8220"every pure bird you may eat\u8221" includes the sent-out bird, teaching that it is permitted in benefit. And {\i vezeh asher lo socheilu meihem lerabos es hashechutah} \u8212- the extra formulation teaches that the slaughtered bird is forbidden in eating \u8212- and by extension, given the {\i mishna}, in benefit.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Now, Rabbi Yochanan presses: if according to Reish Lakish the bird was already forbidden from the time of purchase \u8212- {\i michayim asura} \u8212- why does the {\i braisa} derive the prohibition specifically from the moment of {\i shechita}? If it was already forbidden before {\i shechita}, why would the text focus on that moment?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Reish Lakish\u8217's response is that this teaching is necessary nonetheless: one might otherwise have thought that the {\i metzora} birds are like {\i kodashim}, where the animal begins forbidden while alive but then {\i shechita} comes and renders it permitted ({\i mashchita umachshira}). The {\i braisa} therefore teaches that no \u8212- the {\i shechita} does not change its status to permitted; the bird remains forbidden even after the ritual has been performed on it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Status Resolution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then brings another challenge to Reish Lakish, this time from a {\i mishna}: {\i shachat venimtzah tereifah} \u8212- if one of the {\i metzora} birds was slaughtered and found to be a {\i tereifah}, one takes a replacement pair for the second bird, {\i veharishonah muteres bahana\u8217'ah} \u8212- and the first bird (the one found to be {\i tereifah}) is permitted in benefit. Like any {\i tereifah}, it does not carry the forbidden status of the {\i metzora} birds, because ultimately it was not used.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The challenge to Reish Lakish is sharp: if the bird was forbidden from the time of purchase, it was already forbidden before being slaughtered. Why, then, when its {\i shechita} fails because it is a {\i tereifah}, would it revert to being permitted? It was already forbidden!\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} resolves this: the case here is where the {\i tereifah} defect was found {\i bevnei me\u8217'eha} \u8212- in its internal organs \u8212- meaning a congenital defect, an organ that was missing from birth. Such an animal was never fit to serve as one of the {\i metzora} birds at all, and therefore {\i kedushah} never took effect upon it \u8212- {\i delo chalah kedushah klal}. Even according to Reish Lakish, who holds that the prohibition takes effect at purchase, if at the very moment of purchase the bird was already a {\i tereifah} by virtue of a congenital defect, the {\i kedushah} never attached. This is different from a case where the animal became {\i tereifah} only later \u8212- whether at the moment of {\i shechita} or subsequently \u8212- in which case, since it was whole at the time of purchase, the prohibition would have taken effect then and would remain in force even if the {\i shechita} ultimately failed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Improper Shechita\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A further {\i tosefta} challenges Reish Lakish: if one performed the {\i shechita} without having the cedar wood, the hyssop, and the red string present \u8212- {\i shelo be\u8217'ezov ushelo be\u8217'erez ushelo bishni tola\u8217'as} \u8212- Rabbi Shimon rules that since it was slaughtered without fulfilling its commandment properly ({\i shelo kemitzvato}), the bird is permitted in benefit.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} explains the dispute: {\i ad kan lo pligi ela} \u8212- the entire disagreement turns on whether {\i shechita she\u8217'eina re\u8217'uya} \u8212- a {\i shechita} that is not properly constituted \u8212- counts as a {\i shechita} for purposes of conferring the forbidden status, or not. Crucially, both disputants agree that it is {\i shechita} that gives the bird its status; they disagree only about whether this particular defective {\i shechita} accomplishes that. One holds that {\i shechita she\u8217'eina re\u8217'uya shema shechita} \u8212- even an improper {\i shechita} is still called {\i shechita} and triggers the prohibition, since the animal was set aside ({\i huktza}) for the {\i mitzvah}. The other holds that {\i shechita she\u8217'eina re\u8217'uya lo shema shechita} \u8212- such a {\i shechita} does not count and therefore does not confer the status.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} concludes that this is a {\i tannaitic} dispute ({\i tannai hi}), and Reish Lakish aligns himself with the school of Rabbi Yishmael, which \u8212- as cited earlier \u8212- derives the birds\u8217' prohibition from {\i egla arufa} and therefore holds that they are forbidden {\i michayim}, from the time they are alive. That position belongs to Rabbi Yishmael\u8217's school, while those {\i tannaim} who ground the prohibition in {\i shechita} form the opposing view. The existence of that {\i tannaitic} disagreement means the challenge to Reish Lakish from the {\i tosefta} does not compel him.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Sent-Out Bird Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Living Animals\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now turns to a different question: is the sent-out bird permitted in benefit? The starting premise, offered in the name of Rabbi Yochanan citing Rabbi Shimon ben Yochai, is that {\i lo matzinu ba\u8217'alei chayim she\u8217'asurin} \u8212- we do not find that living animals acquire a status of {\i issur hana\u8217'ah}. The ordinary rule is that forbidden statuses attach when an animal dies or is slaughtered, not while it is still alive. On that basis, since the sent-out bird remains alive, it should be permitted.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Exceptions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Shmuel bar Rav Yitzchak immediately challenges this: what about an animal that was {\i muktza} or {\i ne\u8217'evad} \u8212- set aside for or actually worshiped as {\i avodah zarah}? These are living animals and they are forbidden. Rabbi Yirmiyah adds: what about an animal that performed {\i biah} in the presence of witnesses ({\i rovei\u8217'a venirba be\u8217'eidim})? That too is a living animal which the {\i Torah} requires be put to death \u8212- and presumably it is forbidden in benefit even while alive. One could equally have cited the {\i shor haniskal} from our own {\i mishna}, which is forbidden from the time of {\i g\u8217'mar din}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Most vs.\u160 ?All\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} therefore qualifies the principle. The formulation is adjusted: {\i lo matzinu rov ba\u8217'alei chayim she\u8217'asurin} \u8212- it is not that no living animal ever acquires forbidden status, but that {\i most} living animals do not. These exceptional cases exist, but they are exceptions. The operative assumption, where we have a choice between deeming the living bird permitted and deeming it forbidden, is that living animals ordinarily do not carry {\i issur hana\u8217'ah}, and that is why we default to permitting the sent-out bird.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Field Analogy\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yishmael\u8217's school provides a textual basis: the {\i Torah} says {\i v\u8217'shilach al pnei hasadeh} \u8212- and he shall send it out over the face of the field. {\i Mah sadeh mutar, af hai nami mutar} \u8212- just as a field is free for use, so too this bird is permitted.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Geographic Requirement\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara}, however, questions whether the word {\i sadeh} really comes to teach that the bird is permitted. Perhaps the term is needed for a different {\i braisa}: {\i sadeh} means you cannot stand in Yafo and throw it into the Mediterranean, nor stand at the edge of the wilderness and fling it into the desert, nor stand outside a city and send it back into the city. Rather, you must stand within a city and send it out beyond the city wall, with an open field beyond. The word {\i sadeh} teaches a geographic requirement for how and where the bird must be sent, not that it is permitted in benefit. The {\i gemara} replies: if that were all, the text would not need the definite article \u8212- {\i hasadeh}. The extra {\i hei} teaches two things: {\i shema mina tarti}, both the geographic requirement and the permissibility of the bird.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava Answer\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava offers a simpler and more elegant answer: {\i lo amra Torah shlach l\u8217'takalah} \u8212- the {\i Torah} does not issue a command that would create a stumbling block. If the sent-out bird were forbidden in benefit, then after the ceremony no one would know which specific bird it was, and someone might find it and eat it, unknowingly transgressing. The {\i Torah} would not design a commandment that predictably leads people into transgression.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Examples Series\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Nezir Hair\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} continues its survey of cases of {\i issur hana\u8217'ah}. Regarding the hair of a {\i nazir} \u8212- {\i bsar nazir} \u8212- the source is the verse {\i kadosh yihiyeh gadel pera sear rosho}, \u8220"holy shall be the growth of his hair.\u8221" From the word {\i kadosh}, one derives that the hair is forbidden in benefit.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might ask: perhaps {\i kodesh tofes es damav v\u8217'yotze l\u8217'chulin} \u8212- perhaps holiness works here the way it does with sanctified objects, where one can redeem them so that the money becomes holy and the object returns to ordinary status? The {\i gemara} answers: {\i mich\u8217'dinan kodesh kadosh k\u8217'rinan}. Though we read the word as {\i kodesh} for purposes of the {\i drasha}, the actual word written is {\i kadosh} \u8212- and here {\i Rashi} identifies a crucial distinction between a noun and an adjective. {\i Kodesh} is a noun, a status of sanctification that can in principle be transferred to money through redemption. {\i Kadosh} is an adjective, a descriptive quality inherent to the thing itself. An adjective cannot be redeemed away from the object it describes; it adheres to the thing and cannot be detached by transferring it to money. The hair of the {\i nazir} is intrinsically described as holy \u8212- it is not that holiness was assigned to it as a transferable status.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Peter Chamor\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Regarding the {\i peter chamor} \u8212- the firstborn donkey that was not redeemed \u8212- our {\i mishna} appears not to accord with the view of Rabbi Shimon. A {\i braisa} records a {\i tannaitic} dispute: Rabbi Yehuda holds that the {\i peter chamor} is forbidden in benefit; Rabbi Shimon permits it. The {\i mishna} reflects Rabbi Yehuda\u8217's position.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Nachman in the name of Rabbah bar Avuha clarifies: this dispute \u8212- and the {\i mishna}\u8217's ruling of forbidden benefit \u8212- applies specifically {\i l\u8217'achar arifa}, after the donkey\u8217's neck has been broken because the owner did not redeem it with a lamb. On that everyone agrees it is forbidden. The dispute apparently concerns its status prior to that point.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Basar B\u8217'Chalav\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 For {\i basar b\u8217'chalav}, the school of Rabbi Yishmael derives the {\i issur hana\u8217'ah} from the fact that the verse {\i lo tivashel gdi b\u8217'chalev imo} \u8212- do not cook a kid in its mother\u8217's milk \u8212- appears three times in the {\i Torah}. One occurrence teaches the prohibition of eating, one teaches the prohibition of cooking, and one teaches the prohibition of deriving benefit.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shimon Yehuda\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna}\u8217's ruling does not accord with the view of Rabbi Shimon Yehuda, who holds that {\i basar b\u8217'chalav} is forbidden in eating but permitted in benefit. His proof is from a juxtaposition: the {\i Torah} places {\i lo tivashel gdi b\u8217'chalev imo} adjacent to the verse {\i v\u8217'anshei kodesh tihiyun li}, \u8220"you shall be holy people to me,\u8221" followed immediately by {\i u\u8217'vasar basadeh tereifah lo tocheilu lakelev tashlichun oto} \u8212- do not eat meat of a {\i tereifah} in the field; throw it to the dog. The {\i tereifah} is forbidden in eating but permitted in benefit \u8212- you may throw it to a dog, meaning you may derive benefit from it. Since the passage about {\i basar b\u8217'chalav} is juxtaposed to the {\i tereifah}, we learn: {\i ma lehalan assur b\u8217'achila u\u8217'mutar b\u8217'hana\u8217'ah, af kan assur b\u8217'achila u\u8217'mutar b\u8217'hana\u8217'ah} \u8212- just as {\i tereifah} is forbidden to eat but permitted for benefit, so too {\i basar b\u8217'chalav}. The {\i halacha} does not follow this view; we rule {\i basar b\u8217'chalav} is forbidden even in benefit.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Chulin B\u8217'Azara\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Parallel Argument\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now arrives at what is arguably the most complex case in the {\i mishna}: {\i chulin shenishchatu b\u8217'azara}, ordinary ({\i chulin}) animals slaughtered in the {\i Beis Hamikdash}. The source for this prohibition is not immediately obvious, since no verse explicitly addresses it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yochanan citing Rabbi Meir offers a structural parallel: the {\i Torah} implies {\i shchot li besheli v\u8217'shelcha beshelcha} \u8212- slaughter My things in My place, and your things in your place. We know from the prohibition of {\i shchutei chutz} that {\i kodshim} must be slaughtered inside the {\i Beis Hamikdash}; slaughtering them outside renders them forbidden in benefit. By parallel, {\i chulin} must be slaughtered outside the {\i Beis Hamikdash}: {\i ma sheli beshelcha assur, af shelcha besheli assur} \u8212- just as My things [slaughtered] in your place are forbidden, so too your things [slaughtered] in My place are forbidden.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Karet Distinction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} immediately probes whether {\i karet} applies to {\i chulin b\u8217'azara} by the same analogy: {\i ma sheli beshelcha anush kares, af shelcha besheli anush kares?} The {\i gemara} rejects this by citing the verse {\i v\u8217'el petach ohel moed lo hevio l\u8217'hakriv korban l\u8217'Hashem v\u8217'nichrat} \u8212- one who fails to bring a {\i korban} to the {\i Beis Hamikdash} and instead slaughters it outside incurs {\i karet}. The verse specifies {\i korban} \u8212- a sacrifice. {\i Al korban anush kares v\u8217'ein al chulin shenishchatu b\u8217'azara anush kares}: {\i karet} applies only to a {\i korban} slaughtered outside, not to {\i chulin} slaughtered inside.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But this creates a difficulty for the original analogy: since {\i chulin b\u8217'azara} does not carry {\i karet} while {\i shchutei chutz} does, the parallel is imperfect \u8212- {\i ika l\u8217'mifrach} \u8212- the analogy can be challenged. One can argue that the severity of the {\i kodshim} case (which includes {\i karet}) warrants the {\i issur hana\u8217'ah} there, but the absence of {\i karet} for {\i chulin b\u8217'azara} means the parallel does not compel us to apply {\i issur hana\u8217'ah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Abaye Derivation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye therefore offers an independent derivation. The {\i Torah} uses the formulation {\i v\u8217'shachat\u8230 ? v\u8217'shachato\u8230 ? v\u8217'shachat oso} \u8212- three separate mentions of {\i shechita} in connection with {\i shelamim} (peace offerings). Given that a single mention suffices to teach the law, why the repetition?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Five-Step Logic\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Because the {\i Torah} also states {\i ki yirchak mimcha hamakom v\u8217'zavachta} \u8212- when the {\i Beis Hamikdash} is distant from you, you may slaughter and eat. The implication: {\i berichuk makom ata zove\u8217'ach v\u8217'ee ata zove\u8217'ach bemakom karov} \u8212- it is when you are far from the {\i Beis Hamikdash} that you slaughter {\i chulin}; you do not slaughter {\i chulin} when you are close to the {\i Beis Hamikdash}, meaning inside it. {\i Prats le-chulin shelo yishchatu ba-azarah} \u8212- this excludes {\i chulin} from being slaughtered in the {\i azarah}. But from this verse alone, one might have thought the prohibition applies only to {\i tmimim} \u8212- unblemished animals that are fit to be brought as {\i korbanos}. And even if one would extend it to animals with blemishes ({\i ba\u8217'alei mumim}), on the grounds that they belong to the same general category ({\i min hamachshir}), one might not extend it to wild animals ({\i chaya}), and certainly not to birds, since birds are not brought as {\i shelamim} and are slaughtered differently.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shechut Repetitions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The three-fold repetition of {\i shechita} by {\i shelamim} \u8212- {\i v\u8217'shachat, v\u8217'shachato, v\u8217'shachat oso} \u8212- comes to close each of these gaps progressively: only {\i shelamim} are slaughtered in the {\i azarah}, not {\i chulin}, not blemished animals, not {\i chaya}, and not birds. And the phrase {\i ki yirchak\u8230 ? v\u8217'zavachta v\u8217'achalta} \u8212- when far away, you shall slaughter and eat \u8212- implies further: {\i mah she\u8217'ata zove\u8217'ach berichuk makom ata ochel, v\u8217'ee ata ochel mah she\u8217'ata zove\u8217'ach bemakom karov} \u8212- what you slaughter far from the {\i Beis Hamikdash} you may eat; what you slaughter inside the {\i Beis Hamikdash} you may not eat. This establishes the prohibition on eating {\i chulin b\u8217'azara}. The same three-fold repetition applies again to extend this eating prohibition to all the same categories \u8212- blemished animals, wild animals, and birds.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tereifa Parallel\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Finally, the question remains: perhaps {\i chulin b\u8217'azara} is merely forbidden in eating, but one may derive benefit \u8212- for example by throwing it to dogs? Here the {\i gemara} derives from the verse {\i lakelev tashlichun oso}, referring to {\i tereifah}: {\i oso ata mashlich lakelev v\u8217'ee ata mashlich lakelev chulin shenishchatu ba-azarah} \u8212- it is the {\i tereifah} that you throw to dogs; you do not throw {\i chulin b\u8217'azara} to dogs. The juxtaposition with {\i tereifah} \u8212- which may be fed to dogs and thus is permitted in benefit \u8212- teaches by exclusion that {\i chulin b\u8217'azara} may not. That is how we derive that {\i chulin b\u8217'azara} is forbidden not only in eating but in benefit entirely.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Kiddushin 58\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Intro: Assur B\u8217'Hana\u8217'ah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Opening Context\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The daf opens in the midst of a discussion about objects that are {\i assur b\u8217'hana\u8217'ah} \u8212- forbidden to derive benefit from \u8212- and whether a man who attempts to betroth a woman using such an object achieves a valid {\i kiddushin}. The Gemara has been working through the relevant cases systematically, and the primary focus at this point is {\i chullin shenishchatu ba\u8217'azarah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chullin Ba\u8217'azarah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Chullin shenishchatu ba\u8217'azarah} refers to non-sanctified animals \u8212- ordinary {\i chullin} \u8212- that were slaughtered within the {\i Beis HaMikdash} courtyard. This act is prohibited, and the resulting meat is forbidden in benefit. The question is both how we know the act is prohibited and how we know the prohibition extends to {\i hana\u8217'ah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Pasuk Source\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara derives both dimensions from the {\i pasuk} that speaks of slaughtering and eating meat outside the {\i Beis HaMikdash}: {\i ki yirchak mimcha hamakom \u8212- vezavachta ve\u8217'achalta} \u8212- \u8220"when the place is distant from you, you shall slaughter and eat.\u8221" The implication is that if one is {\i within} the {\i Beis HaMikdash}, one may not slaughter and one may not eat. Additional {\i psukim} extend the prohibition to all types of animals slaughtered there \u8212- even blemished ones, even species that could never be brought as a {\i korban}. From this web of verses the Gemara establishes both the prohibition of the act and the forbidden status of the meat that results.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 R. Shimon vs.\u160 ?Chachamim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Braita: R. Shimon\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara cites a {\i braisa} that introduces a dissenting voice. {\i Hamkadesh b\u8217'peter chamor, b\u8217'basar bechalav, uvchullin shenishchatu ba\u8217'azarah} \u8212- one who betroths a woman using a firstborn donkey that has not yet had its neck broken, or with meat and milk cooked together, or with {\i chullin} slaughtered in the {\i azarah} \u8212- {\i Rabbi Shimon omer mekudeshet}: Rabbi Shimon holds that the betrothal is valid in all three cases. The {\i Chachamim}, by contrast, say she is not betrothed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Logic of Positions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Shimon\u8217's position in each case has a traceable basis. With respect to {\i peter chamor}, an earlier {\i braisa} already established that according to Rabbi Shimon, a firstborn donkey is not forbidden in benefit while still alive \u8212- only after its neck is broken does it take on a more restricted status, analogous to {\i eglah arufah}. The case here would concern the living donkey, where Rabbi Shimon is consistent with his own position. As for {\i basar bechalav}, a position attributed to Rabbi Shimon ben Yehuda \u8212- that meat and milk are permitted in benefit \u8212- was cited the previous day. Rashi notes that {\i Rabbi Shimon le\u8217'shitaso} implies this is the standard Rabbi Shimon, namely Rabbi Shimon bar Yochai, though some read his {\i girsa} as referring specifically to Rabbi Shimon ben Yehuda. Either way, there is a {\i tanna} who holds that {\i basar bechalav} is permitted in benefit.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Not Biblical\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The most striking element of the {\i braisa} is what it implies about {\i chullin shenishchatu ba\u8217'azarah}: if Rabbi Shimon rules that a woman is betrothed through such meat, the Gemara draws the conclusion that {\i chullin shenishchatu ba\u8217'azarah leRabbi Shimon lav de\u8217'oraisa} \u8212- for Rabbi Shimon, the prohibition is not biblical. As Rashi explains: {\i kasavar issuro lav de\u8217'oraisa, lo issur shechitah velo issur hana\u8217'ah} \u8212- Rabbi Shimon holds that neither the prohibition of the slaughter itself nor the prohibition of deriving benefit is of biblical force. Importantly, Tosafot notes in several places that saying the prohibition is not {\i de\u8217'oraisa} need not mean the act itself carries no biblical violation. It may mean specifically that the {\i status} of the meat produced \u8212- the consequence \u8212- is not biblically prohibited, even if the act of slaughtering it in the {\i azarah} might itself still be problematic on some level.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Lav De-Oraisa Issue\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rashi Explains\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rashi\u8217's comment establishes that when the Gemara says {\i lav de\u8217'oraisa}, it is making a claim about the resulting status of the meat: neither the prohibition against performing the slaughter nor the prohibition against deriving benefit from the outcome rises to a biblical level for Rabbi Shimon. The implication \u8212- that the prohibition is rabbinic \u8212- is what the Gemara acknowledges and what generates the next problem.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbinic Prohibition\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This raises a pointed question: if the meat of {\i chullin shenishchatu ba\u8217'azarah} is only rabbinically forbidden in benefit according to Rabbi Shimon, can it nevertheless be used for {\i kiddushin}? The question has two competing frameworks. On one view, from the Torah\u8217's perspective the meat retains real value \u8212- it is edible flesh from which benefit can in principle be derived \u8212- and a woman who receives it has received something of value, so the marriage should take effect. The Rabbis\u8217' prohibition is a superimposed restriction, but it does not alter the Torah-level reality of the object. On the other hand, one might argue that the Rabbis have the power to affect ownership and can declare an object effectively worthless in order to enforce their enactment, or alternatively that within an observant community, an object that no one is permitted to purchase or use has no practical monetary value \u8212- {\i de facto} rather than {\i de jure}. The prohibition\u8217's practical effect collapses the object\u8217's market value to zero, regardless of its theoretical worth.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafot Analysis\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot addresses this directly. The Gemara in the chapter {\i Kol Sha\u8217'ah} quotes a teaching of Rav: one who betroths a woman from the sixth hour onward on {\i erev Pesach} \u8212- when {\i chametz} becomes forbidden in benefit \u8212- {\i ein chosheshin le\u8217'kiddushin}, the {\i kiddushin} does not take effect. This applies even with the type of hard wheat ({\i chitei de\u8217'kurdenayta}) whose {\i chametz} status is only rabbinic \u8212- {\i de\u8217'einam assurim ela mi\u8217'de\u8217'rabanan}. This appears to be a clear ruling that a rabbinically forbidden object cannot effect {\i kiddushin}, even when the prohibition is only rabbinic. The question then is how to reconcile this with Rabbi Shimon\u8217's ruling that {\i chullin shenishchatu ba\u8217'azarah} \u8212- which is only rabbinically prohibited according to him \u8212- can nevertheless be used for {\i kiddushin}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Tam vs.\u160 ?Rashi\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tam Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot resolves the tension by distinguishing between two types of rabbinic prohibition, following the view of Rabbeinu Tam. The case of the hard wheat on {\i erev Pesach} \u8212- in the sixth hour \u8212- is technically rabbinic, but Rabbeinu Tam explains that it sits within the immediate ambit of a biblical prohibition. The wheat itself may be genuine {\i chametz}; the only reason the prohibition at that specific moment is rabbinic is that the sixth hour has not yet fully elapsed. In a matter of minutes, the very same wheat will be forbidden {\i de\u8217'oraisa}. That proximity to, and structural position within, a biblical category gives the rabbinic prohibition a weight it would not otherwise carry. The Rabbis, in extending the prohibition to the sixth hour, are extending a biblically established category \u8212- and that extension participates in the stringencies of the biblical category, including the effect of rendering the object valueless for {\i kiddushin}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rashi Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rashi, by contrast, reads the Gemara in {\i Pesachim} differently. On his reading, the wheat in question is not genuine {\i chametz de\u8217'oraisa} at all; the rabbinic prohibition there does not operate as an extension of the biblical category. Instead, the object itself is simply rabbinically prohibited \u8212- which might appear to make Rashi\u8217's position more permissive. Yet Tosafot acknowledges Rashi\u8217's reading and still must grapple with the question of how {\i chullin shenishchatu ba\u8217'azarah} differs.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Extension Doctrine\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot\u8217's resolution draws a structural distinction. When the Rabbis create a prohibition that is an {\i extension} of a biblical category \u8212- even if the particular case being prohibited is not itself biblical \u8212- the object is treated as carrying the full weight of the biblical prohibition, including being considered devoid of monetary value for {\i kiddushin}. By contrast, when the Rabbis create a prohibition that is entirely new, {\i whole cloth} \u8212- something that has no analogue or root in any biblical prohibition \u8212- the object is not stripped of its value, and a {\i kiddushin} effected with it would be binding. {\i Chullin shenishchatu ba\u8217'azarah}, on this analysis, is a standalone rabbinic category with no biblical counterpart, and therefore even if forbidden, it does not render the object worthless. The {\i chametz} of the sixth hour, however, is structurally part of the biblical {\i chametz} framework, and therefore carries biblical weight into the {\i kiddushin} analysis \u8212- even where the specific prohibition at that hour is only rabbinic.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Burning Challenge\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mar Yehuda Challenge\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Mar Yehuda encountered Rav Yosef and Shmuel bar Rabba \u8212- the son of Rabba bar bar Chana \u8212- standing at the entrance of Rabba\u8217's academy and posed a sharp challenge. The {\i braisa} just discussed attributes to Rabbi Shimon the position that {\i chullin shenishchatu ba\u8217'azarah} allows {\i kiddushin} \u8212- implying that the prohibition is not biblical. Yet another source also attributed to Rabbi Shimon states: {\i chullin shenishchatu ba\u8217'azarah yisarefu} \u8212- such meat must be burned. And not only that, but even a {\i chayah} \u8212- a deer or similar animal that could never be brought as a {\i korban} \u8212- must be burned if slaughtered in the {\i azarah}. Rashi explains the significance of the burning: ordinarily, things forbidden in benefit are buried, not burned. Burning is a special stringency reserved for disqualified {\i kodashim}. The reasoning is that when a {\i chullin} animal is slaughtered in the courtyard and cannot be eaten, observers will assume it is a disqualified sacrifice \u8212- and disqualified sacrifices are burned. The extension to a {\i chayah}, a species with no conceivable connection to any sacrifice, pushes this further: even there, the meat must be burned. This level of stringency suggests the prohibition is treated as fully biblical. How, then, can Rabbi Shimon simultaneously say the woman is betrothed when {\i kiddushin} is performed with such meat? Rav Yosef and Shmuel bar Rabba had no answer and were silenced.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava Responds\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 They went before Rava, who was dismissive \u8212- calling them troublemakers \u8212- suggesting he had already explained this. His resolution: {\i hacha b\u8217'mai askinan? kegon shenishchata venimtza trefa} \u8212- the case in which Rabbi Shimon permits {\i kiddushin} is where the animal was slaughtered and then discovered to be a {\i treifah}, or was known to be a {\i treifah} when slaughtered. This is not a standard case. In a standard case of {\i chullin shenishchatu ba\u8217'azarah}, Rabbi Shimon would agree that the meat is {\i assur b\u8217'hana\u8217'ah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shechita She\u8217'eina\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The key principle underlying Rava\u8217's resolution is {\i shechita she\u8217'eina re\u8217'uya} \u8212- a slaughter that does not render the meat permissible. This principle was introduced the previous day: if the slaughter does not achieve its purpose of permitting the meat for consumption \u8212- because the animal was a {\i treifah} \u8212- then halachically the act is not categorized as a valid {\i shechita}. It therefore does not trigger the prohibition of {\i chullin shenishchatu ba\u8217'azarah}, because that prohibition is predicated on an act of {\i shechita} having occurred, and a {\i shechita she\u8217'eina re\u8217'uya} is not such an act. The resolution is not an artificial narrowing of the case but is directly grounded in an explicit {\i braisa}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Treifah Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Known/Discovered Treifah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i braisa} addresses the case explicitly: {\i hashochet et hatrefa} \u8212- one who slaughters an animal known to be a {\i treifah} \u8212- and {\i vehen hashochet venimtza trefa} \u8212- one who slaughters and then finds the animal to be a {\i treifah} upon examining the organs \u8212- {\i vezeh vezeh chullin ba\u8217'azarah} \u8212- and both cases involve {\i chullin} slaughtered in the {\i azarah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Shimon vs.\u160 ?Chachamim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Shimon matir b\u8217'hana\u8217'ah} \u8212- Rabbi Shimon permits benefit \u8212- because the act constitutes {\i shechita she\u8217'eina re\u8217'uya}: since the animal was a {\i treifah}, the {\i shechita} did not accomplish its purpose of rendering the meat permissible, and therefore halachically no act of {\i chullin shenishchatu ba\u8217'azarah} occurred. The {\i Chachamim}, however, forbid benefit: {\i osrim} \u8212- they hold that physically the act of slaughter was performed, the animal was indeed killed through {\i shechita} in the {\i azarah}, and it does not matter that the animal was a {\i treifah}. The physical act triggers the prohibition regardless. This {\i braisa} confirms that Rava\u8217's {\i oikimta} is not an ad hoc narrowing but rather reflects an explicit controversy that maps precisely onto the case \u8212- and this is why Rava referred to it as something he had already established.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Money Transfer\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Status Transfer\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now pivots away from the entire list of {\i issurei hana\u8217'ah} to address a new question. What happens when one takes a forbidden object and sells it, thereby obtaining money? The act of sale was itself a transgression \u8212- but what is the status of the proceeds? The answer the Gemara establishes is that the status does {\i not} transfer. The meat and milk was {\i assur b\u8217'hana\u8217'ah}; one transgressed by converting it to money; but the money itself is fully one\u8217's own, unrestricted by any prohibition. One may use these proceeds to betroth a woman, and the {\i kiddushin} takes effect.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Avodah Zarah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara asks: how do we know the status does not transfer? The answer: {\i midigallei rachmana b\u8217'avodat kochavim} \u8212- because the Torah explicitly states by the case of an idol: {\i vehayita cherem kamohu} \u8212- \u8220"you shall be under the ban like it.\u8221" The phrase {\i kol she\u8217'ata mehaya mimenu} \u8212- anything that arises through it \u8212- acquires the status of the idol. So if one sells an idol, the money received carries the idol\u8217's forbidden status. This could suggest a general principle that selling any forbidden object transfers its {\i issur} to the proceeds. The Gemara rejects this: far from a general rule, the idol case is the exception.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two Verses Rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The exception is proven by the principle of {\i shnei ketuvim haba\u8217'im ke\u8217'echad}: when the Torah teaches the same rule in two separate cases, it is signaling that these cases are the {\i exceptions}, not examples of a general rule. The two cases are {\i avodah zarah} and {\i shevi\u8217'is} \u8212- Sabbatical year produce \u8212- where the Torah separately establishes that the forbidden status transfers to whatever is exchanged for it. Because both cases are stated explicitly, we conclude that the {\i rule} is the opposite: for all other {\i issurei hana\u8217'ah}, the status does {\i not} transfer to the proceeds of a sale.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Shemitah Transfers\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Redemption Model\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The basis for including {\i shevi\u8217'is} among the exceptions is a verse: {\i yovel hi, kodesh tihye lakhem} \u8212- \u8220"it is the Jubilee year, it shall be holy to you.\u8221" Just as something that is sanctified ({\i kodesh}) transfers its status to whatever it is redeemed into \u8212- and the original object is thereby desanctified \u8212- so too {\i shemitah} produce transfers its {\i kedushah} to whatever one obtains in exchange for it. One might then think that, as with {\i pidyon}, once the status transfers to the money, the original produce loses its {\i kedushah}. Against this the Torah says {\i tihye} \u8212- \u8220"it shall be\u8221" \u8212- {\i b\u8217'havayatah tehei}: it retains its sanctity. The original {\i shemitah} produce never exits its status.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Last Object Rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara illustrates through a chain of exchanges: if one takes {\i shemitah} fruit and exchanges it for meat, both the original fruit and the meat now carry {\i kedushat shevi\u8217'is} \u8212- meaning they must be consumed before the time of {\i biur} and cannot be used for other purposes. If one then exchanges the meat for fish, the meat exits the status and the fish take it on. Fish exchanged for wine: the fish exit, the wine enters. Wine exchanged for oil: the wine exits, the oil enters. The rule is {\i acharon acharon nitpas beshevi\u8217'is}: each successive exchange \u8212- the {\i last} object in the swap \u8212- carries the {\i shemitah} status. And {\i peri atzmo assur}: the original fruit itself always retains its status regardless.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Exception Parallels\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara\u8217's position that {\i shemitah} and {\i avodah zarah} are the two exceptions raises a certain conceptual question. {\i Shemitah} is not, at least at its core, an {\i issur hana\u8217'ah} in the standard sense \u8212- it is a {\i kedushah} status that governs how produce must be treated and consumed. It might seem more natural to include only {\i avodah zarah} as the single exception to the rule that status does not transfer, since {\i avodah zarah} is the paradigmatic case of an object forbidden in benefit. One could reasonably suggest that the analogy between {\i shemitah} and {\i avodah zarah} works because of the {\i biur} stage \u8212- after the time of {\i biur}, {\i shemitah} produce that has not been destroyed does become {\i assur b\u8217'hana\u8217'ah}, and perhaps it is from that angle that the Gemara makes the comparison. Regardless, the operative conclusion is clear: only by these two does the status transfer; for everything else, the proceeds of a sale are permitted.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Mishnah: Terumot\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Objects Listed\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The final {\i mishnah} of the chapter takes up a new category of objects that can be used for {\i kiddushin}: {\i hamekadesh b\u8217'terumos u\u8217'b\u8217'maasros u\u8217'b\u8217'matnos b\u8217'mei chatas u\u8217'b\u8217'eifer parah harei zo mekudeshes v\u8217'afilu Yisrael} \u8212- one who betroths with {\i terumah}, with {\i ma\u8217'aser}, with priestly gifts, with water used in the {\i parah adumah} purification process, or with the ashes of the {\i parah adumah} \u8212- the woman is betrothed, and this applies even if the man is a non-kohen.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Priest Gifts\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i matanot} referred to are the portions of a slaughtered animal that must be given to a {\i kohen} \u8212- the {\i zero\u8217'a}, the cheeks, and the maw \u8212- as obligated by the Torah. {\i Terumah} and {\i ma\u8217'aser} are the portions of one\u8217's grain that must be given to a {\i kohen} and {\i levi} respectively. The initial analysis suggests these cases are relatively straightforward: {\i ma\u8217'aser} and priestly gifts carry no inherent sanctity that would restrict their use, and {\i terumah}, even if the woman cannot eat it herself, could be resold to a {\i kohen}. On this surface reading, the {\i mishnah} seems obvious \u8212- if one possesses {\i terumah}, it has market value and can be given in {\i kiddushin}. The Gemara, however, will complicate this picture.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chatas/Parah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The more intriguing cases are the {\i mei chatas} \u8212- water that has been or is to be mixed with the ashes of the {\i parah adumah} \u8212- and the {\i eifer parah adumah} itself \u8212- the ashes of the red heifer used in the purification ritual. These raise genuine questions: are these objects {\i assur b\u8217'hana\u8217'ah}? Do the ashes of an animal used for an elaborate purification ritual carry any forbidden status if applied to a non-purification purpose? If one possesses this water or these ashes, can they be sold? And in what scenario does one use them to betroth a woman? The Gemara will address these questions directly.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Tovas Hana\u8217'ah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ulla\u8217's Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Ulla offers a teaching that reframes how the entire {\i mishnah} is to be understood: {\i tovas hana\u8217'ah einah mamon} \u8212- the benefit one receives from the ability to choose which {\i kohen} or {\i levi} to give an obligatory gift to is not considered monetary value. This statement implies that Ulla understands the {\i mishnah} to be teaching precisely the case where the man does not own the {\i terumah} outright but has merely separated it and must give it away \u8212- the interest Ulla is addressing is not ownership of the object but the privilege of deciding its recipient.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Separation Context\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The scenario Ulla envisions is {\i hamafrish terumah mitvalo} \u8212- a man who has separated {\i terumah} from his grain, which he is now obligated to give to a {\i kohen}. He cannot keep it himself. The {\i chiddush} of the {\i mishnah}, on Ulla\u8217's reading, is about what exactly this man gives the woman when he betroths her with this {\i terumah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Choice Benefit\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What the woman receives, on this reading, is not the {\i terumah} in a full proprietary sense \u8212- she too must ultimately give it to a {\i kohen} \u8212- but rather the right to choose {\i which} kohen receives it. That privilege of selection \u8212- {\i tovas hana\u8217'ah} \u8212- is what Ulla characterizes as {\i einah mamon}, not genuine monetary ownership. The {\i chiddush} of the {\i mishnah} would then be that despite the man\u8217's lack of full ownership in the object, the {\i kiddushin} takes effect. This makes the {\i mishnah} a genuinely novel ruling rather than an obvious case.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rav Abba Challenge\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Contradiction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Abba challenges Ulla. If {\i tovas hana\u8217'ah einah mamon} \u8212- if the right to choose the recipient is not monetary \u8212- then how can the {\i mishnah} rule that the woman is betrothed? {\i Kiddushin} requires the man to give the woman something of genuine monetary value, at minimum a {\i perutah}\u8217's worth. If the man has no real ownership stake in the {\i terumah}, what has he given her?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Easy Answer\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Ulla\u8217's response is that the {\i mishnah} need not be read in the case Rav Abba assumes. The easy reading \u8212- which Ulla rejects as too trivial \u8212- is that the man actually {\i owns} the {\i terumah}. A non-{\i kohen} can come to own {\i terumah} in several ways: he might have purchased it from {\i kohanim} as a commercial venture, he might have inherited it from a relative who was a {\i kohen}, or similar scenarios. If the man owns the {\i terumah} outright, the case is obvious \u8212- {\i terumah} has market value, it can be sold to {\i kohanim}, and using it for {\i kiddushin} is a straightforward application of giving the woman an object of value. This reading, Ulla argues, trivializes the {\i mishnah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rashi Critique\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rashi makes the point explicit: the reason we cannot be satisfied with the easy case is that it renders the {\i mishnah} unremarkable. Of course one can betroth with an object one owns, even if it is {\i terumah}. The {\i mishnah}\u8217's ruling demands a harder case to be interesting and to constitute a genuine {\i chiddush}. Therefore the {\i mishnah} must be speaking of a more complex scenario \u8212- which is what the Gemara proceeds to establish.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Inherited Tevel\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tevel Inheritance\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The harder case the Gemara identifies: a {\i Yisrael} who inherited {\i tevel} \u8212- grain from which {\i terumos u\u8217'maasros} have not yet been separated \u8212- from his maternal grandfather, who was a {\i kohen}. He himself is not a {\i kohen}, since {\i kehunah} follows the father\u8217's lineage. Nevertheless, because of his grandfather\u8217's status, there is a potential claim to the {\i terumah} embedded in the {\i tevel}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gifts Present\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The operative principle: {\i matanot shelo hurmu k\u8217'mi shehurmu damyan} \u8212- gifts that have not yet been separated are considered as though they have been separated. The terumah exists in potential within the {\i tevel}, and a {\i kohen} who owns {\i tevel} is considered, on this view, to have already staked his claim to the {\i terumah} portion even before the physical act of separation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Grandfather\u8217's Claim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Because the grandfather was a {\i kohen}, his claim to the {\i terumah} portion of his {\i tevel} was already established during his lifetime, on the principle that {\i matanot shelo hurmu k\u8217'mi shehurmu damyan}. When the grandson inherits this {\i tevel} and separates the {\i terumah}, he is not separating {\i terumah} that is owed to any other {\i kohen} \u8212- he is separating {\i terumah} that was already owned by his grandfather, which he has now inherited. He may keep it himself. This is the genuinely novel case of the {\i mishnah}: the grandson is considered an actual owner of the {\i terumah} \u8212- not through {\i tovas hana\u8217'ah}, but through the inherited status \u8212- and betrothing with it is therefore effective. Ulla\u8217's point, then, is preserved: {\i tovas hana\u8217'ah einah mamon}, and the {\i mishnah} is not speaking of a case of mere {\i tovas hana\u8217'ah} but of real ownership transmitted through the grandfather.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Chiyya to Huna\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Direct Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Chiyya bar Avin posed a direct question to Rav Huna: {\i tovas hana\u8217'ah mamon o eino mamon?} \u8212- is the benefit of choosing which recipient to give an obligatory gift to considered monetary value or not?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Huna Answers\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Huna pointed to the {\i mishnah}: {\i tanitua} \u8212- you have learned it. {\i Hamekadesh b\u8217'terumah u\u8217'v\u8217'ma\u8217'aser u\u8217'v\u8217'matnot b\u8217'mei chatas u\u8217'v\u8217'eifer parah harei zo mekudeshes v\u8217'afilu b\u8217'Yisrael} \u8212- he understood the {\i mishnah} as Ulla had understood it, that the case involves a man who has separated {\i terumah} and must give it to a {\i kohen}, and whose right to choose the recipient constitutes the value given to the woman. From the fact that she is betrothed, Rav Huna concluded that {\i tovas hana\u8217'ah mammona}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Clarification Issue\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Chiyya pressed back: but haven\u8217't you already explained the {\i mishnah} as the case of a {\i Yisrael} who inherited {\i tevalim} from his maternal grandfather the {\i kohen} \u8212- a case of genuine ownership, not mere {\i tovas hana\u8217'ah}? If the {\i mishnah} was explained away as a case of real ownership, it cannot serve as a proof that {\i tovas hana\u8217'ah} is {\i mamon}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav\u8217's Deflection\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The response: {\i hutzat} \u8212- that explanation, the maternal grandfather case, is excluded from serious consideration, or alternatively, it designates the speaker as following the approach of a scholar from {\i Hutzal}. The exchange becomes uncomfortable: Rav Chiyya took {\i hutzat} as an insult, implying he was not fit to be in the {\i beis midrash}. The speaker clarified that he meant {\i hutzat} in reference to the approach of Rav Assi of {\i Hutzal} \u8212- a buffering of the offense. But the underlying point stands: Rav Huna\u8217's answer is that the {\i mishnah} should be read in its primary sense as a case of {\i tovas hana\u8217'ah}, and that reading demonstrates {\i tovas hana\u8217'ah mammona}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Theft Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Theft Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara introduces a related dispute to probe the question of {\i tovas hana\u8217'ah} and monetary ownership further. If one steals {\i tevel} \u8212- grain from which {\i terumos u\u8217'maasros} have not been separated \u8212- from a non-{\i kohen}, how much must the thief repay? The owner would eventually be obligated to give away a portion: at minimum approximately 2% as {\i terumah} and 10% as {\i ma\u8217'aser}. Does the thief owe the full face value, or only the value of the portion the owner could actually retain?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Positions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi holds: {\i meshalem lo d\u8217'mei tevelo} \u8212- the thief pays the full value of the {\i tevel}. Rabbi Yose bar Yehuda holds: {\i ein meshalem lo ella d\u8217'mei chullin shebo} \u8212- the thief pays only the value of the {\i chulin} portion \u8212- approximately 88% of the total.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Benefit = Ownership?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara initially suggests this may reflect the debate about {\i tovas hana\u8217'ah}: Rabbi, who requires full payment, may hold that {\i tovas hana\u8217'ah mammona} \u8212- the owner\u8217's right to choose which {\i kohen} and {\i levi} receives the gifts constitutes genuine monetary ownership, and therefore the full value of the {\i tevel} belongs to him. Rabbi Yose bar Yehuda, who requires payment only for the {\i chullin} portion, may hold that {\i tovas hana\u8217'ah eino mamon} \u8212- the owner has no monetary stake in the portions destined for {\i kohen} and {\i levi}, and the thief owes nothing for them. Rashi\u8217's reading of this position is striking: it would mean that {\i tovas hana\u8217'ah mammona} implies full ownership of the face value of the object \u8212- not merely that the owner has {\i some} stake, but that he is considered the complete owner of those portions, able to demand their full market value.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Matanot Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Reframing\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then offers an alternative framework in which the dispute between Rabbi and Rabbi Yose bar Yehuda does not actually turn on the question of {\i tovas hana\u8217'ah} at all. Rather, all parties could agree that {\i tovas hana\u8217'ah eino mamon}. The dispute instead concerns the application of the principle {\i matanot shelo hurmu k\u8217'mi shehurmu damyan} \u8212- whether gifts that have not yet been separated are considered as though they already have been \u8212- to a specific factual scenario.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi: Pre-owned\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 On this reading, Rabbi holds that {\i matanot shelo hurmu k\u8217'mi shehurmu damyan} \u8212- the obligatory portions are considered already separated and already owned by the eventual recipient. The owner of the {\i tevel}, on this view, already fully owns the entire hundred bushels because the {\i terumah} and {\i ma\u8217'aser} portions within it are treated as though they had already been separated and allocated to him \u8212- that is, if his ancestor was a {\i kohen} who owned the grain, the {\i terumah} was already his. Hence the thief must pay the full value.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R.Y.B.Y: Not Pre-owned\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yose bar Yehuda disagrees: {\i matanot shelo hurmu} are {\i not} like {\i shehurmu} \u8212- the obligatory gifts have no legal existence until physically separated, and until that moment the owner has no claim to those portions. Therefore the thief owes only for the portions the owner actually owns \u8212- the {\i chullin} part.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Thief Penalty\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Penalty Rationale\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara ultimately rejects the interpretation that the dispute is about ownership \u8212- whether {\i tovas hana\u8217'ah} or {\i matanot} \u8212- and instead explains Rabbi\u8217's ruling as a rabbinic penalty. Even if technically the owner of the {\i tevel} has no monetary claim to the portions destined for {\i kohen} and {\i levi}, the Rabbis imposed a penalty on the thief: {\i kansuha rabbanan le\u8217'ganav} \u8212- they fined the thief \u8212- requiring payment of the full value.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Escape Logic\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The reasoning: if the thief only had to pay 88 dollars on 100 dollars\u8217' worth of stolen grain, he would escape any obligation to the {\i kohen} and {\i levi} whose portions he effectively consumed. Any {\i kohen} or {\i levi} who came to claim his share would be told: prove that you are the specific {\i kohen} or {\i levi} to whom this grain was going to be given. They cannot prove it, and the thief walks away without paying the full social debt his theft incurred. The Rabbis found it intolerable that a thief could steal a hundred dollars\u8217' worth of grain and walk away having paid only 88. Therefore, despite the owner\u8217's limited technical ownership of the obligatory portions, the thief is penalized and must pay the full value to the owner.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shmuel Alternative\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 An additional resolution: one might say that everyone agrees with Shmuel\u8217's principle \u8212- {\i chitah achas poteres es hakari} \u8212- even a single grain of {\i terumah} exempts the entire pile, meaning the owner theoretically could separate only the smallest possible amount of {\i terumah} and retain nearly the full 90% {\i chullin} portion. Under this view, the dispute is not about ownership at all, but about the proper calculation of repayment given different assumptions about how much the owner would actually give away in practice versus what he was technically obligated to give.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Chatas Service\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Payment Void\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now turns to the final cases in the {\i mishnah}: {\i mei chatas} and {\i eifer parah adumah} \u8212- the water and ashes used in the purification ritual of the {\i parah adumah}. The Gemara challenges from a {\i braisa}: {\i ha\u8217'notel sachar ladun \u8212- dinav bateilin} \u8212- one who accepts payment for rendering judgment voids his rulings. Similarly, {\i l\u8217'ha\u8217'id \u8212- eidaso beteilah} \u8212- a witness who accepts payment voids his testimony. And then the relevant case: {\i l\u8217'hazos u\u8217'l\u8217'kadesh \u8212- memav mei mearah v\u8217'eifro eifer mikleh} \u8212- one who accepts payment for performing the {\i hazayah} (sprinkling of the purification water) or for the {\i kiddush} (mixing the ashes into the water) \u8212- his water is considered like water drawn from a swamp (not {\i mayim chayim} as required) and his ashes are like ordinary cooking ashes. The ritual is entirely voided. The reason, as Tosafot explains, is that one may not accept payment for performing a {\i mitzvah}; doing so invalidates the act, at least rabbinically. This creates an obvious problem: how can the {\i mishnah} say one may betroth a woman with {\i mei chatas} or {\i eifer parah}, if accepting benefit from performing these acts voids the ritual and is itself prohibited?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Forbidden Acts\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye resolves the contradiction: {\i lo kashya \u8212- kan b\u8217'sachar hava\u8217'ah u\u8217'milui v\u8217'kan b\u8217'sachar haza\u8217'ah v\u8217'kiddush} \u8212- there is no difficulty. The {\i braisa} that voids the ritual refers to payment for the {\i mitzvah} acts themselves \u8212- {\i hazayah} (sprinkling) and {\i kiddush} (mixing the ashes with the water). These are the ritual acts that constitute the {\i mitzvah}, and one may not be compensated for performing them. Our {\i mishnah}, however, refers to something entirely different.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Permitted Service\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The permitted acts for which payment may be accepted \u8212- and with which {\i kiddushin} may be effected \u8212- are {\i hava\u8217'ah u\u8217'milui}: transporting the ashes and drawing the water. These are logistical services, not {\i mitzvah} acts. The {\i Beis HaMikdash} required people to carry ashes from one location to another and to draw water for use in the {\i parah adumah} process. These tasks are physical labor, not ritual acts, and one may legitimately charge for them. In whatever scenario the man provides this service to the woman rather than to the {\i Beis HaMikdash} directly \u8212- and she compensates him through {\i kiddushin} \u8212- the betrothal takes effect, because he has given her something of genuine value: his service.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Language Check\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye adds that this resolution is also {\i dika} from the language of the {\i mishnah} itself: {\i hacha b\u8217'mei chatas u\u8217'b\u8217'eifer parah} \u8212- the {\i mishnah} says \u8220"water\u8221" and \u8220"ashes\u8221" \u8212- referring to the objects transported and drawn, not to the acts of sprinkling or mixing. The contrasting {\i braisa} explicitly states {\i l\u8217'hazos u\u8217'l\u8217'kadesh} \u8212- \u8220"for sprinkling and for mixing\u8221" \u8212- highlighting that the prohibition targets the ritual acts themselves, while the {\i mishnah}\u8217's permissive ruling concerns the preparatory physical services. {\i Shema minah} \u8212- this distinction is confirmed by the precise language each source chooses to employ.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Hadran alach ha\u8217'ish mekadesh.}\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Kiddushin 59\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Agent Acts for Himse (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishnah Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The third {\i perek} opens with the following case: a man says to his friend, \u8220"Go and betroth a woman for me,\u8221" and the friend goes and betroths her for himself. The {\i mishnah} rules that she is married \u8212- {\i mekudeshet} \u8212- to the second man, the one who acted as the emissary. The act works, whatever its ethical dimensions.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafot Chiddush\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot immediately raises the obvious question: what is the {\i chiddush} here? Surely it is self-evident that if a man performs an act of {\i kiddushin} with his own money and in his own name, the marriage is valid. Tosafot answers by specifying the precise scenario the {\i mishnah} is addressing. At the moment of betrothal, the agent declared to the woman that so-and-so had sent him to betroth her to him \u8212- {\i Ploni shlachani l\u8217'kadeshach lo} \u8212- and then, in the very same breath, turned and said to her, {\i harei at mekudeshet li}, \u8220"You are betrothed to me.\u8221" In such a case, one might have thought that when he said {\i li} \u8212- \u8220"to me\u8221" \u8212- she could not tell whether he meant himself or whether he was speaking in the voice of the {\i meshaleach}, representing the one who sent him. If she were genuinely confused and believed she was being betrothed to the one who sent him, the marriage would be a {\i kiddushei ta\u8217'ut}. Tosafot therefore adds that the {\i mishnah} is teaching a case where {\i hi yada\u8217'at} \u8212- she knew \u8212- that he meant himself. But the deeper {\i chiddush} is the rejection of a {\i hava amina} that we might read {\i li} as spoken in the principal\u8217's voice: we do not say that. When the agent says {\i li}, he speaks in his own voice, not that of the one who appointed him.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A natural question arises: why does the {\i mishnah} require that she subjectively understood him? Generally in {\i halacha} we assess matters objectively \u8212- {\i dvarim shebalev einam dvarim} \u8212- we do not ask what she thought he meant, but what the words or circumstances objectively signify. The answer seems to be that the language here is genuinely ambiguous in context, given that the agent had just been speaking as a representative of the {\i meshaleach}. That objective ambiguity is precisely why the {\i mishnah} must specify the scenario in which the meaning was clear.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rama\u8217'ut Discussion\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Braita Rama\u8217'ut\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah}\u8217's ruling \u8212- that the marriage is valid \u8212- says nothing explicit about the moral dimension. A {\i braita} fills the gap: {\i mah she-asah asui}, what he did is done and stands, {\i ela she-nahag bah minhag rama\u8217'ut} \u8212- but he conducted himself with deceptive and faithless behavior toward the one who sent him.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is worth pausing over, because the ethical weight of the critique depends heavily on the specific circumstances. The {\i mishnah} uses the phrase {\i isha plonit} \u8212- a specific, named woman \u8212- which means the principal had a particular woman in mind, not merely a general mandate to find someone suitable. That makes the betrayal considerably more concrete. The agent was not simply stumbling upon a woman while on an errand; he was dispatched with a particular mission regarding a particular woman and then diverted that mission to his own benefit.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Even so, the situation is not entirely simple. If the first man and this specific woman had no existing relationship beyond the principal\u8217's expressed interest, one might wonder how severe the {\i rama\u8217'ut} truly is. The {\i gemara} will also raise the principle of {\i shema yikdemenu acher} \u8212- the fear that someone else will seize the opportunity \u8212- which suggests that urgency can sometimes change the calculus. And the woman\u8217's own preference, while not foregrounded in the {\i gemara}, is not entirely absent from the moral picture.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 As for the {\i mishnah} itself, the {\i gemara} notes that although it does not explicitly invoke the language of {\i rama\u8217'ut}, the word {\i halach} \u8212- \u8220"and he went\u8221" \u8212- carries a connotation of going off and doing something underhanded. The {\i tanna} of the {\i mishnah} encodes the moral critique in the choice of word, while the {\i braita} makes it explicit.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Agent Acts for Himse (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Friend vs.\u160 ?Agent\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then asks: why does the {\i mishnah} describe the one sent as {\i chavero} \u8212- his friend \u8212- while an earlier {\i mishnah} in the previous {\i perek} described a similar figure as {\i shlucho}, his agent? The {\i gemara} answers that each formulation carries its own {\i chiddush}, and both were necessary.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 In the present {\i mishnah}, which uses the language of {\i chavero}, the {\i chiddush} is this: one might have thought that only when you formally appoint an {\i agent} \u8212- someone you have explicitly commissioned, on whom you rely, from whom you expect full performance of the task \u8212- does his acting for himself constitute {\i rama\u8217'ut}. That is because {\i samcha da\u8217'atei}, you have placed full confidence in him; you feel you have discharged your responsibility. But if you merely ask a favor of a friend \u8212- if you said casually, \u8220"If you happen to be passing by and you get the chance, see if you can arrange this for me\u8221" \u8212- then perhaps there is less of a sense of binding obligation, and the friend who acts for himself would not be a {\i ramai}. The {\i mishnah} teaches that even in that case it is the wrong thing to do.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rashi clarifies the distinction between {\i chavero} and {\i shaliach}: the friend was asked in an incidental way \u8212- if you happen to find yourself near her, perhaps you could arrange this on my behalf \u8212- not sent on a formal mission. Nevertheless, the {\i mishnah} tells us, even that informal request creates enough of a moral obligation that diverting it to one\u8217's own benefit is considered {\i rama\u8217'ut}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The earlier {\i mishnah}, which uses {\i shlucho}, teaches a different {\i chiddush}: that if you send a formal agent to betroth a woman in a specific location, and he betroths her elsewhere, the {\i kiddushin} does not take effect. One might have thought that if you are sent on a mission, the specification of a location is merely {\i mareh makom} \u8212- a helpful pointer about where to find the woman \u8212- and that you retain latitude to act wherever you encounter her. The {\i mishnah} teaches otherwise: if a location was stipulated, only that location falls within the {\i shelichut}, and an act performed elsewhere is outside its scope entirely.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The broader point is that the nature of the relationship \u8212- whether the person is a paid agent on a formal commission or a friend asked a casual favor \u8212- can shape the moral weight of the betrayal and the scope of the {\i shelichut}, even if in all cases the person technically holds the legal power to perform the act.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rama\u8217'ut Discussion (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabin Chasida Story\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now records the story of Rabin Chasida \u8212- the righteous one, and presumably {\i chasida} here is not merely a family name \u8212- who went to betroth a woman on behalf of his son. His son had presumably made his father a {\i shaliach} for this purpose; Tosafot addresses the awkwardness that a son would be so bold as to ask his father to serve as his agent in this matter, and suggests the father may have volunteered. In any case, when Rabin Chasida began speaking with the woman, he decided to betroth her for himself.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} asks: does this not violate the {\i braita}\u8217's ruling that acting for oneself in such circumstances is {\i rama\u8217'ut}? The {\i gemara} answers: the situation was that the woman\u8217's family would not give her to the son \u8212- he was not {\i chashuv} enough for her \u8212- but they were pleased to give her to the father, who was a far more distinguished person. So the agent\u8217's taking her for himself was not, in that context, taking something away from his principal, because his principal could not have obtained it regardless.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Duty to Inform\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Even granting that the switch was substantively acceptable \u8212- because the family would not have agreed to the original match in any event \u8212- the {\i gemara} insists: {\i ibaya lei la-aduyei}, he should have informed his son before proceeding. The obligation to inform is more than a courtesy. It prevents the experience of betrayal that arises when someone discovers, without warning, that the person they trusted to act on their behalf has instead acted for himself. Even if the explanation that follows is entirely legitimate, the shock of the discovery is itself a form of harm. Moreover, informing the principal formally terminates the {\i shelichut}; the agent announces that the original mission cannot be fulfilled, and only then proceeds on his own behalf. Without that communication, the transition from acting as representative to acting for oneself happens invisibly, and that invisibility is its own form of {\i rama\u8217'ut}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shma Yikdemenu Acher\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} explains why Rabin Chasida did not stop to inform his son: {\i savar ad hacha ve-hacha ati inish achrini mekadesh lah} \u8212- he calculated that by the time he could send word to his son and wait, another suitor would come and betroth her first. The principle of {\i shema yikdemenu acher} \u8212- the concern that delay will cost the opportunity entirely \u8212- justified acting without prior notification. This woman was evidently sought after, and the window was narrow. Under those circumstances, the {\i gemara} accepts the failure to inform as excusable.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Land & Competition\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav & Land Deal\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The discussion of {\i rama\u8217'ut} extends naturally from marriage to commerce. Rabbah bar bar Chana gave money to Rav and said: go buy me this plot of land. Rav went and bought it for himself. The {\i gemara} again invokes the {\i braita}: is this not {\i minhag rama\u8217'ut}? And again the {\i gemara} answers that the land was owned by powerful men \u8212- {\i alimei} \u8212- who showed honor and deference to Rav, and who would sell the land to Rav but would not have sold it to Rabbah bar bar Chana, even at Rav\u8217's request. Only Rav himself could acquire it; the principal could not have obtained it through any means.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 And again: {\i ibaya lei la-aduyei} \u8212- Rav should nevertheless have informed Rabbah bar bar Chana of the situation before proceeding. The {\i gemara} answers in the same terms: {\i savar ad hacha ve-hacha ati inish achrini zavnin lah} \u8212- Rav calculated that by the time he sent word, someone else would purchase the land. This was hot property, the opportunity was fleeting, and the delay required to inform his principal would have cost him the acquisition entirely.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Abba Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A related but distinct case follows. Rav Giddel had been {\i mehapeich} \u8212- actively engaged in negotiating the purchase of \u8212- a certain plot of land. Rabbi Abba came along and purchased it. This is importantly different from the previous cases: Rabbi Abba was not a {\i shaliach} for Rav Giddel. He was simply another person, acting entirely for himself, with no prior relationship of agency or mission. Each man was, as it were, acting on his own behalf.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} does not apply the {\i braita}\u8217's critique of {\i rama\u8217'ut} to Rabbi Abba directly. The {\i gemara} reserves that critique specifically for situations where one was charged with acting on another\u8217's behalf. Here it is a case of open competition.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Giddel complained to Rabbi Zeira, who brought the matter to Rabbi Yitzchak Nafcha. Rabbi Yitzchak Nafcha waited until Rabbi Abba came to the pre-{\i Yom Tov} lectures \u8212- the {\i pirka d\u8217'yarcha kalla} \u8212- and confronted him indirectly. He posed the case: what do we say about a poor person who is in the process of trying to seize a piece of cake \u8212- {\i ani mehapech becharara} \u8212- and someone comes and snatches it from under his nose? Rabbi Abba answered immediately: {\i nikra rasha}, the one who takes it is called a {\i rasha}. Rabbi Yitzchak Nafcha then said: so why did you do exactly that?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Abba replied that he had not known Rav Giddel was in negotiations; the seller had not informed him. Rabbi Yitzchak Nafcha then said: in that case, now that you know, give the land to Rav Giddel. Rabbi Abba said he would not sell it \u8212- it was the first plot of land he had ever purchased, and divesting oneself of one\u8217's first property is a bad {\i siman}. But he offered to give it to Rav Giddel as a gift.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Giddel would not take the gift, for it is written {\i ve-sonei matanot yichyeh} \u8212- one who hates gifts will live; he would not accept what he could not earn himself. Rabbi Abba would not use the land, because Rav Giddel had been engaged in trying to purchase it and he had unwittingly cut him out. The result: {\i lo mar nachis lah velo mar nachis lah} \u8212- neither this one nor that one would enter the land, and it came to be called {\i ar\u8217'a de-rabbanan}, the land of the rabbis, as it was given over to the use of the {\i benei yeshiva}. A story of everyone trying to do the right thing, at the conclusion of a discussion about people doing the wrong thing.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafot: Metziah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The case of {\i ani mehapech becharara} raises a deeper question about when it is and is not appropriate to seize an opportunity that someone else had their eye on \u8212- in a context where no {\i shelichut} exists at all. Tosafot notes the tension with the {\i halacha} of {\i metzia}: the law of found objects seems to be that even if you spotted a lost object first, if I physically seize it before you do, it belongs to me. Why would seizing a cake or a plot of land be any different?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot, citing Rabbeinu Tam, offers an important distinction. The prohibition on {\i mehapech becharara} applies specifically in two types of cases: where a person is trying to profit from an ownerless or available thing and you compete with them for the same opportunity, as in the case of Rav Giddel and the land; or where a person is trying to purchase a particular item and you swoop in and buy it first. These are analogous to the case of Rav Giddel \u8212- {\i dumya de-Rav Giddel} \u8212- and are called {\i rasha} for the same reason: go and find another opportunity for yourself, rather than interfering with your fellow\u8217's engagement.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But if the object is a true {\i metziah} \u8212- a lost object on the street \u8212- then there is no equivalent alternative. There is only one such object, only one such opportunity. If you do not grab it, you will not find another. In that case, it is genuinely every man for himself, and there is no prohibition.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This maps onto an interesting parallel in the context of marriage. The very first {\i daf} of {\i Kiddushin} compared the act of finding a wife to finding a {\i metziah}. If a particular woman is unique \u8212- if there truly is no equivalent \u8212- then the zero-sum nature of the situation may change the moral analysis. By contrast, where the matter is purely commercial and similar opportunities exist, interfering with another person\u8217's active engagement is considered a form of {\i resha\u8217'a}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot extends this reasoning to a further case: the prohibition of {\i yored le-umanuto shel chaveiro} \u8212- entering into another person\u8217's trade or livelihood. If a person has cast nets for fish, one may not begin casting nets in the same area, even though the fish are ownerless {\i hefker}. The reason is that this is not a case of grabbing an isolated windfall opportunity; the other person has established a business, and you are interfering with that business. The relevant category is not {\i metziah} but {\i umanuto} \u8212- the ongoing livelihood of the other.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Employment Ethics\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot draws an explicit practical application from this analysis, citing a ruling of Rabbeinu Yitzchak: it is forbidden for a tutor to approach a homeowner who already employs a different tutor and offer to take the position. The existing tutor is already engaged, and the one seeking to displace him should go find his own employment elsewhere rather than interfering with that {\i parnassah} arrangement.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 However, if the homeowner himself indicates dissatisfaction and signals that he is looking for a replacement, then of course one may compete for the position. And, Tosafot adds, another homeowner may approach the already-employed tutor and try to hire him away \u8212- because the second homeowner can say: {\i eino ritzoni ela lazeh}, I want specifically this person, not just any tutor, since this one seems to me most likely to benefit my son. That rationale \u8212- I cannot simply find a functionally equivalent alternative \u8212- is precisely what removes the prohibition.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This parallels the logic applied to marriage: one cannot simply say to the person who wants a particular woman, \u8220"go find someone else,\u8221" because in the world of persons, each individual is unique. The zero-sum nature of the situation justifies acting in one\u8217's own interest even at another\u8217's loss \u8212- provided one is not acting in violation of a prior commitment or an existing agency relationship.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 After 30 Days\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Thirty-Day Condition\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} moves to a second case, which is joined by the word {\i ve-chen} \u8212- \u8220"and similarly.\u8221" A man says to a woman: {\i harei at mekudeshet li le-achar shloshim yom} \u8212- you are betrothed to me as of thirty days from now. If another man came and betrothed her within that thirty-day window, she is betrothed to the second man. The {\i mishnah} further distinguishes the case where the first man said {\i me-achshav u-le-achar shloshim yom} \u8212- from now and taking effect in thirty days \u8212- in which case if another man betroths her within that period, her status is {\i mekudeshet ve-eina mekudeshet}, doubtfully betrothed, because it is unclear how much the formula \u8220"from now\u8221" advances the effect of the {\i kiddushin}. In that state of uncertainty, a {\i bat Yisrael} betrothed to a {\i kohen} or a {\i bat kohen} betrothed to an {\i Yisrael} may not eat {\i terumah}, as the respective marital status is unresolved.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Money Consumed\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} asks: what is the law if no second man came and betrothed her within the thirty days, but the money \u8212- or ring \u8212- given at the time of the original {\i kiddushin} was consumed or spent before the thirty days elapsed? Does the {\i kiddushin} take effect at the end of the thirty-day period?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav & Shmuel\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav and Shmuel both rule: she is betrothed \u8212- {\i mekudeshet} \u8212- even though the money has been consumed. The {\i chiddush} of this ruling is striking, because ordinarily when a legal {\i kinyan} is structured to take effect after a period of time, some remnant of the original act of acquisition must still be present when that time arrives. If a person says \u8220"I will own your cow in thirty days\u8221" and draws the cow into his property, but on day thirty the cow is wandering in {\i reshut ha-rabbim}, the {\i kinyan} does not take effect. The original act must still have a surviving presence when the transaction is supposed to finalize.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Original Kesef\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} explains why the thirty-day {\i kiddushin} is different. {\i Hanei zuzei lav l\u8217'milveh damu ve-lav l\u8217'pikadon damu} \u8212- this money is neither a loan nor a deposit.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is not a {\i pikadon}, because if it were, the money would remain in the man\u8217's ownership, held in trust by the woman; loss of the money would be the man\u8217's loss, and no money remaining would mean nothing left with which to effect the {\i kiddushin}. But here the money was given into the woman\u8217's possession outright \u8212- if it is lost, it is her loss. That is not a {\i pikadon}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Nor is it a {\i milveh}, a loan. A loan is money transferred for the borrower to spend, with an obligation to repay an equivalent sum. One cannot effect {\i kiddushin} with a loan \u8212- {\i ein mekadesh be-milveh} \u8212- because there is nothing concrete with which to betroth; you are merely forgiving a debt. That too does not apply here.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What was given was {\i kesef kiddushin} from the very first moment \u8212- money delivered to the woman specifically in the capacity of {\i kiddushin}, with the understanding that it would take effect later. Because it was originally given with the full force of {\i kesef kiddushin}, the memory of that act is not erased even if the physical money no longer exists. The woman received it as {\i kiddushin}, and if the {\i kiddushin} does not go through, she would be obligated to return it; that lingering reality of the transaction is sufficient to keep the original act \u8220"present\u8221" in the relevant sense. One is not marrying her with money that no longer exists at the moment the thirty days arrive; one is marrying her with the money that was originally given to her as {\i kesef kiddushin}, and the fact that it no longer physically exists does not erase the significance of that original act.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Woman Retracts\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Yochanan\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now asks: what is the law if no second man came, and the woman herself retracted \u8212- {\i chazrah bah} \u8212- before the thirty days were up? Since the {\i kiddushin} has not yet taken effect, can she withdraw from it?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yochanan rules that she can retract \u8212- {\i chozeret}. The reasoning is {\i ati dibbur u-mevatel dibbur}: her words negate his words. Until the {\i kiddushin} takes effect, the entire transaction is still in the realm of speech and declaration; it has not been actualized. Words can therefore undo words.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Reish Lakish\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Reish Lakish disagrees: she cannot retract. {\i Lo ati dibbur u-mevatel ma\u8217'aseh} \u8212- words cannot negate an act. Although the {\i kiddushin} has not yet taken effect, the {\i ma\u8217'aseh kiddushin} \u8212- the physical act of handing the ring or money to the woman \u8212- has already been performed. That act cannot be undone by mere words.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Get Comparison\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yochanan challenges Reish Lakish from the law of {\i terumah}: if a householder appoints an agent to separate {\i terumah} on his behalf and then verbally revokes the appointment before the agent has acted, the {\i terumah} is invalid \u8212- {\i ein trumato trumah}. Here, both the appointment and the revocation were purely verbal \u8212- {\i dibbur u-mevatel dibbur} \u8212- and the revocation is effective. The same should apply in the case of {\i kiddushin}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Reish Lakish counters: the case of {\i kiddushin} is different, because {\i nesinas ma\u8217'ot le-yad ishah ke-ma\u8217'aseh dami} \u8212- the transfer of money into the woman\u8217's hands is equivalent to an act. There was a concrete physical transaction, not merely an exchange of words. Words can void words, but words cannot void an act.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Get Analysis\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yochanan presses further with the case of {\i get}: if a man sends a messenger to deliver a {\i get} to his wife, and then sends a second messenger after the first saying \u8220"the {\i get} I gave you is void,\u8221" it is indeed void \u8212- {\i harei ze batel}. Here, a physical {\i get} was placed in the agent\u8217's hand \u8212- an act was performed \u8212- yet a verbal declaration is sufficient to void the agency. This parallels the case of {\i kiddushin}, where money was placed in the woman\u8217's hand and she now verbally retracts.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Reish Lakish responds: there is a crucial distinction. {\i Kol kamma de-lo mata gitta le-yadah, dibbur hu} \u8212- as long as the {\i get} has not reached the wife\u8217's hand, the entire arrangement is still only words in the relevant sense. The act that transforms legal status \u8212- {\i gerushin} \u8212- has not yet taken place. Placing the {\i get} in the agent\u8217's hand is a preparatory step, not the actualization of the divorce. Since the status-changing act has not occurred, the transaction remains in an abstract state and words can still void it. But in the case of {\i kiddushin}, placing the ring in the woman\u8217's hand {\i is} the act of {\i kiddushin} \u8212- the {\i ma\u8217'aseh} was complete, even though the {\i kiddushin} has not yet taken effect. That completed act cannot be reversed by words alone.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Thought vs.\u160 ?Action\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Vessel Completion\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Reish Lakish himself now raises a challenge to Rabbi Yochanan from the laws of {\i tumah} and vessels. The {\i mishnah} teaches: {\i kol ha-kelim yordim lidei tumatan be-machshava} \u8212- all vessels acquire susceptibility to {\i tumah} through thought. When a craftsman is fashioning a vessel, the question of when the vessel is \u8220"complete\u8221" \u8212- and therefore susceptible to {\i tumah} \u8212- is determined by the craftsman\u8217's intention. If he decides in his mind that the vessel is finished, it is finished and can become {\i tamei} from that moment, even without any further physical action.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 However, {\i ve-ein olim mi-tumatan ela be-shinuy ma\u8217'aseh} \u8212- vessels do not lose their susceptibility to {\i tumah} except through a change in action \u8212- actually reworking the vessel. If a craftsman has already decided in his mind that the vessel is complete, he cannot simply think \u8220"I want to continue working on it\u8221" and thereby un-make the vessel\u8217's status as a complete vessel. He must actually go back and physically resume work on it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The principle derived from this: {\i ma\u8217'aseh motzi miyad ma\u8217'aseh u-miyad machshava} \u8212- an act can reverse either a prior act or a prior thought. {\i Machshava eina motzi\u8217'ah lo miyad ma\u8217'aseh ve-lo miyad machshava} \u8212- thought reverses neither a prior act nor even a prior thought. This seems to directly contradict Rabbi Yochanan\u8217's position: even thought cannot reverse thought, so certainly words should not be able to reverse {\i kiddushin}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Maaseh Motzi\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yochanan responds: the domain of {\i tumah} is sui generis. {\i Shani machshava de-tuma de-chi ma\u8217'aseh damya} \u8212- thought regarding {\i tumah} is treated as equivalent to an act. This is derived from the {\i drasha} of Rav Pappa, who noted the tension between the written and read forms of the verse {\i ki yutan}: water placed passively on seeds renders them susceptible to {\i tumah} provided the owner was {\i nicha lei} \u8212- pleased about it. The owner\u8217's positive disposition toward the water having touched the seeds is treated as though he himself had placed the water on them. Thought and will, in the domain of {\i tumah}, have the legal force of action.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Because thought in the {\i tumah} context functions as an act, when the craftsman decides in his mind that the vessel is finished, that is legally equivalent to performing an act of completion. And since thought-as-act cannot be reversed by mere thought, it takes a physical act of resuming work on the vessel to undo that status. This is entirely specific to {\i tumah}; it does not establish a general principle that thought cannot reverse thought, and therefore has no bearing on the {\i kiddushin} question, where Rabbi Yochanan holds that words \u8212- in a domain where there is no such equivalence to acts \u8212- can indeed reverse prior words.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Zavid Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} notes that Rav Zavid taught this entire dispute in the context of a different case. Rather than the case of conditional {\i kiddushin} taking effect after thirty days, he applied the debate to the following scenario: a woman appointed an agent to accept {\i kiddushin} on her behalf \u8212- {\i natna reshut li-shlucha le-kadsha} \u8212- and then went and betrothed herself before the agent acted. If she acted first, her own {\i kiddushin} is binding; if the agent acted first, his {\i kiddushin} on her behalf is binding.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Void Agent\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then asks: what if she did not betroth herself, but simply verbally retracted her appointment of the agent \u8212- {\i chazrah bah} \u8212- without performing any act? Can she void her agent with words?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yochanan says she can retract: {\i ati dibbur u-mevatel dibbur}. Reish Lakish says she cannot: {\i lo ati dibbur u-mevatel dibbur}. Reish Lakish\u8217's position here is, as noted, quite extreme: a woman could announce before witnesses that she is revoking her agent\u8217's authority, and yet the agent could theoretically proceed to marry her off. Tosafot grapples with this and attempts to limit the scope of Reish Lakish\u8217's ruling.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Terumah & Get\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Terumah Negation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yochanan challenges Reish Lakish from the {\i terumah} case: if one appointed an agent to separate {\i terumah} and verbally revoked the appointment before the agent acted, {\i ein trumato trumah} \u8212- the agent\u8217's {\i terumah} is invalid. This is a case where appointment was verbal and revocation was verbal, and the revocation was effective \u8212- {\i ati dibbur u-mevatel dibbur}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Response\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav responds on behalf of Reish Lakish: no \u8212- {\i hacha be-mai askinan ke-gon she-kadam ba\u8217'al ha-bayit ve-taram mi-kalav}, the case is where the householder himself went ahead and performed the act of taking {\i terumah} from his own vessels first. The revocation was not purely verbal; it was effected by an action \u8212- the householder taking {\i terumah} himself before the agent could act. This is a case of {\i ma\u8217'aseh mevatel shelichut}, an act terminating the agency, not words terminating the agency. Reish Lakish\u8217's principle \u8212- that words cannot void a prior arrangement \u8212- remains intact, because the effective revocation here was through action, not speech.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 By extension, a woman wishing to void her agent for {\i kiddushin} would have to perform an act \u8212- such as betrothing herself to someone else \u8212- not merely declare verbally that she revokes the agency.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Get Negation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yochanan then raises the {\i get} case again: a man sends a {\i shaliach} with a {\i get} for his wife and subsequently sends a second messenger to tell the first that the {\i get} is void \u8212- {\i batel hu}. This is purely verbal revocation of an agency that involved a physical act of handing over the {\i get}. Yet it is effective.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Get Contingent\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Reish Lakish\u8217's earlier answer applies here too: {\i kol kamma de-lo mata gitta le-yadah, dibbur hu} \u8212- as long as the {\i get} has not reached the wife\u8217's hand, the entire arrangement is still in the realm of the abstract and contingent. The act of {\i gerushin} has not been performed. The delivery of the {\i get} to the agent was preparatory, not the completion of any legal change in status. Before any status has changed, words can still void words.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hilcheta Ruling\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Despite this defense, the {\i gemara} concludes: {\i tiyuvta de-Reish Lakish tiyuvta} \u8212- Reish Lakish is refuted by the {\i get} case. And the {\i gemara} rules: {\i ve-hilcheta ke-vateih de-Rabbi Yochanan va-afilu be-kamaita} \u8212- the {\i halacha} follows Rabbi Yochanan, and not only in the case of voiding an agent with words, but even in the original case of thirty-day conditional {\i kiddushin}. Even there, where a physical act \u8212- the giving of the ring or money \u8212- has already been performed, since the {\i kiddushin} has not yet taken effect, the woman can retract with words before the thirty days elapse.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Kiddushin 60\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Voiding Pending Kiddushin\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Dibbur Mevattel Dibbur\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The daf opens by returning to the debate between Rabbi Yochanan and Reish Lakish concerning a case where a man designates that the effect of {\i kiddushin} will take place later \u8212- he gives the money now, but the {\i kiddushin} is not to take effect immediately. The question is whether such an arrangement can be voided through words alone, or whether some {\i ma\u8217'aseh} is required. The principle at stake is {\i ati dibbur u-mevattel dibbur} \u8212- can speech negate speech?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The underlying tension is that while Reish Lakish concedes that purely verbal acts can be negated verbally \u8212- appointing a {\i shaliach}, for instance, is a verbal act and can therefore be verbally revoked \u8212- the present case involves an actual {\i ma\u8217'aseh}: money was physically handed over as {\i kiddushin}. The question is to what degree that act is considered legally operative when its effect has been postponed, and whether it therefore falls into the category of {\i ma\u8217'aseh} or remains merely in the domain of {\i dibbur}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Reish Lakish\u8217's Versions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} presents two versions of Reish Lakish\u8217's position. In the first, he disputes only the case of the postponed monetary act. In the second, far more striking version, he disputes even the case of a woman who designated a {\i shaliach} to receive {\i kiddushin} on her behalf \u8212- arguing that she cannot verbally revoke the appointment. This position is difficult to the point of being untenable: it would mean a woman could be married against her will. Tosafot grapples with this reading, but the {\i Gemara} clearly concludes that it cannot be correct. If a woman designates a {\i shaliach}, she may verbally void the {\i shlichus}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hilchesa Ke-Rabbi Yochan\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} records a definitive contradiction to Reish Lakish \u8212- {\i tiuvta de-Reish Lakish tiuvta} \u8212- demonstrating that a {\i shaliach} appointment can indeed be verbally revoked. And then the {\i Gemara} goes further: {\i ve-hilchesa ke-Rabbi Yochanan ve-afilu be-kameita} \u8212- we rule like Rabbi Yochanan even in the first version of the dispute, even where money was physically given with the effect designated to take place thirty days later. Even that can be verbally voided before it takes effect.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} anticipates the objection: {\i shani nesinas mammon le-yad ha-ishah de-kama\u8217'aseh dami} \u8212- the actual giving of money into a woman\u8217's hands is like a real act. Nevertheless, the ruling stands: {\i afilu hachi ati dibbur u-mevattel dibbur}. As long as the {\i kiddushin} has not yet taken effect, words can void it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Status Change Analysis\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} explains why. The classic case in which {\i dibbur} cannot negate a {\i ma\u8217'aseh} involves a situation where a status change has already occurred \u8212- for instance, once an object has been rendered a finished vessel, it becomes susceptible to {\i tum\u8217'ah}, and a subsequent mental intention to resume work on it is irrelevant, because the status has already changed. Here, by contrast, although money was given, no halachic status has yet changed. The woman\u8217's status has not shifted; the man\u8217's status has not shifted; everything has been projected into the future. Since nothing has yet been concretized halachically, the entire arrangement remains in the domain of {\i dibbur}, and words suffice to void it.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Get vs.\u160 ?Shaliach Voiding\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Conflicting Piskei Din\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} immediately raises a contradiction: {\i kashya hilchesa a-hilchesa}. On one hand, we have just established that we rule like Rabbi Yochanan \u8212- an arrangement to take effect later can be verbally voided. On the other hand, we also hold {\i hilchesa ke-Rav Nachman}, that in the case of voiding a {\i shaliach ha-get}, we rule like Rav Nachman. What was that ruling?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The case, discussed in Gittin, is as follows: a man sent a {\i shaliach} to deliver a {\i get} to his wife, then chased down the {\i shaliach} and verbally revoked the {\i shlichus}. The question is whether, having voided the {\i shaliach}, he may reuse the {\i get} document itself. Rav Nachman says yes; Rav Sheshet says no. We rule like Rav Nachman. But how is this consistent with our ruling that a verbal voiding dissolves everything? If the writing of the {\i get} is analogous to giving money to the woman\u8217's hands \u8212- both are acts done now with effect designated for later \u8212- then just as the {\i kiddushin} is voided, the {\i get} should be voided as well, and could not be reused.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Get Remains Valid\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} resolves the contradiction directly: {\i nehi d\u8217'batlei mishum shlichus mishum get lo batlei} \u8212- yes, you voided the {\i shlichus}, but you did not void the {\i get} itself. The two are entirely separate. One might ask why: in both cases, has not the decisive act been done now, with effect pending?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The answer lies in a crucial distinction. When money is given for {\i kiddushin} with a postponed effect, nothing new has been created. The money remains money; the woman\u8217's status is unchanged; the man\u8217's status is unchanged. The act has been suspended in abstraction. But when a {\i get} is written, the act of writing creates something concrete here and now \u8212- this piece of paper acquires the halachic status of a {\i get}. That is a genuine {\i ma\u8217'aseh}, analogous to designating an object as {\i terumah} or rendering an item a completed vessel. The writing transforms the document\u8217's status in the present moment.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Maasah Analysis\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This distinction clarifies the ruling. Because the {\i get} document itself has undergone a status change \u8212- it has become a {\i get} \u8212- that status cannot be dissolved through words alone. The voiding of the {\i shaliach} affects only the agency, not the instrument. The {\i get} document remains fully valid, and may be reused. By contrast, the giving of {\i kiddushin} money with a postponed effect created no present status change whatsoever, and therefore {\i ati dibbur u-mevattel dibbur} applies in full. The two rulings are thus entirely consistent.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Me\u8217'achshav U\u8217'Le\u8217'achar Zm\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav: Safek Le\u8217'olam\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now turns to the precise case under discussion in the {\i Mishnah}: a man says {\i me-achshav u-le\u8217'achar sheloshim yom} \u8212- \u8220"as of now and after thirty days.\u8221" A second man then comes and betroths this woman before the thirty days have elapsed. What is the legal status of both {\i kiddushin}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav rules: {\i mekudeshet ve-einah mekudeshet le\u8217'olam} \u8212- she is betrothed and not betrothed, and this status of uncertainty is permanent. The {\i safek} \u8212- does she belong to man number one or man number two \u8212- never resolves. Even after day thirty arrives, the uncertainty endures.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shmuel: Tenai Logic\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Shmuel disagrees: {\i mekudeshet ve-einah mekudeshet ella ad sheloshim yom} \u8212- the uncertainty lasts only until day thirty. After that, {\i paka kiddushei sheni ve-gamri kiddushei rishon} \u8212- man number two\u8217's {\i kiddushin} lapses, and man number one\u8217's {\i kiddushin} takes full effect.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Shmuel\u8217's reasoning is that the phrase {\i me-achshav u-le\u8217'achar sheloshim yom} functions as a {\i tenai} \u8212- a condition. The meaning is: \u8220"You are betrothed to me as of now, provided that day thirty arrives\u8221" \u8212- or more precisely, provided that nothing intervenes to prevent it (neither party dies, neither reverses). When day thirty does arrive and the {\i tenai} is satisfied, the {\i kiddushin} takes effect retroactively from the moment it was given. During the intervening period, therefore, the {\i safek} is genuine \u8212- perhaps the {\i tenai} will not be satisfied, perhaps man number one will withdraw or die \u8212- so man number two\u8217's {\i kiddushin} is uncertain. But once day thirty arrives and the {\i tenai} is fulfilled, all uncertainty dissolves in favor of man number one.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Language Interpretation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav\u8217's position, by contrast, is that the phrase is genuinely ambiguous \u8212- not about subjective intent, but as a legal matter. The language could mean either: \u8220"as of now, with day thirty as a {\i tenai}\u8221" (Shmuel\u8217's reading), or: \u8220"not really as of now \u8212- when he added {\i u-le\u8217'achar sheloshim yom}, he effectively retracted the {\i me-achshav} and meant {\i le-achar sheloshim yom} alone.\u8221" On this reading, the two readings are mutually exclusive extremes \u8212- either entirely now (retroactively upon fulfillment of the {\i tenai}) or entirely later \u8212- and we cannot determine which was meant. Therefore the {\i safek} is permanent: {\i mekudeshet ve-einah mekudeshet le\u8217'olam}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} notes that this debate maps directly onto a {\i tannaitic} dispute. The {\i braita} records: {\i me-hayom u-le\u8217'achar mita get ve-eino get} \u8212- \u8220"from today and after I die, this is a {\i get} and is not a {\i get}\u8221" \u8212- {\i divrei Chachamim}. A man on his deathbed attempting to spare his wife from {\i yibum} says the {\i get} should take effect now, assuming he dies. The {\i Chachamim} hold it is uncertain \u8212- perhaps he meant {\i me-achshav} as a {\i tenai}, perhaps he retracted and meant {\i le-achar mita} only, in which case there is no valid {\i get}. {\i Rabbi} says: {\i kazeh get} \u8212- it is certainly a {\i get}, meaning Rabbi reads it definitively as {\i me-achshav}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav thus parallels the {\i Chachamim}; Shmuel parallels Rabbi. The {\i Gemara} explains why it is insufficient to simply state their positions as {\i psak} in one of the two cases: the contexts differ in a legally significant way. In {\i kiddushin}, the man is eager to create the relationship and might be presumed to prefer an earlier effective date; in {\i gittin}, the man is distancing himself and might be less eager, making the \u8220"later\u8221" reading more plausible. And in the {\i get} case, there is an additional pressure toward the immediate reading \u8212- because a {\i get le-achar mita} is legally impossible \u8212- that does not exist in {\i kiddushin}, where {\i kiddushin} taking effect after thirty days is perfectly valid. Hence both cases are independently necessary.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye then raises a further case under Rav\u8217's framework: {\i ba echad ve-amar lah harei at mekudeshes li me-hayom u-le\u8217'achar lamed yom, u-va acher ve-amar lah harei at mekudeshes li me-hayom u-le\u8217'achar esrim yom}, and a third man who says {\i me-hayom u-le\u8217'achar asara yamim}. The ruling: {\i mi-rishon u-mi-acharon tzricha get, mi-emtzai eina tzricha get}. She requires a {\i get} from man number one and from man number three, but not from man number two.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The logic is straightforward: {\i mima nafshach} \u8212- whatever you say. If all three statements constitute a {\i tenai}, then all three take effect retroactively as of the moment of {\i kiddushin}, and man number one, having acted first, wins. Man number two and man number three are both excluded; she needs only a {\i get} from man number one. If, conversely, all three statements mean the effect is genuinely delayed, then whoever\u8217's designated date arrives first prevails \u8212- that is man number three, whose ten-day period expires first. Man number one and man number two are excluded; she needs only a {\i get} from man number three. Either way, man number two \u8212- the {\i meatzai}, the middle one \u8212- is never the winner.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} asks: isn\u8217't this obvious? Why state it? The answer is this: one might have thought that the phrase {\i me-achshav u-le\u8217'achar zman} has no single fixed legal meaning, but rather that each individual speaker might have meant it differently \u8212- man number one meaning {\i me-achshav}, man number two meaning {\i le-achar zman}, and so forth. If that were so, any one of them could have been the operative {\i kiddushin}, and all three would require a {\i get}. The {\i Gemara} therefore teaches that this is not the case. A legal phrase has one fixed meaning, not a range of personal interpretations. We may not know yet which of two possible fixed meanings it carries, but it carries only one. Whatever the phrase means, it means the same thing for every person who uses it. This is a foundational principle: {\i devarim she-belev einam devarim} \u8212- subjective intent that departs from the objective legal meaning of a term is legally inert. The legal system demands fixed meanings for its operative language.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Three Kiddushin Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Case Setup\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having established that the phrase {\i me-achshav u-le\u8217'achar zman} carries a single fixed legal meaning \u8212- even if we remain uncertain what that meaning is \u8212- the {\i Gemara} also finds that the uncertainty itself has been resolved by Abaye\u8217's analysis. Rav\u8217's position is that the phrase is permanently ambiguous as between two binary alternatives: either entirely now or entirely later. Shmuel\u8217's position is that it unambiguously means {\i me-achshav} with a {\i tenai}, so that all uncertainty dissipates once day thirty arrives.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Get Requirement Logic\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The case of three sequential {\i kiddushin} \u8212- man number one designating effect after thirty days, man number two after twenty days, man number three after ten days \u8212- illustrates the consequences of Rav\u8217's view. Since the phrase is either-or, the same either-or applies identically to all three. If it means now for one, it means now for all; if it means later for one, it means later for all. This produces the clean {\i mima nafshach} analysis: the middle man is excluded under both possibilities.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Fixed Legal Meaning\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The deeper lesson is about the nature of legal language. One might have thought, as the {\i Gemara} entertains, that since we are ultimately trying to understand what the speaker intended, it is possible for different speakers to have meant different things by the same phrase. But a legal system cannot function on that basis. The same term must carry the same legal meaning regardless of the speaker\u8217's internal state. This principle aligns with {\i devarim she-belev einam devarim} \u8212- unspoken thoughts cannot alter the legal effect of spoken words. The contrast with {\i nedarim} and {\i nezirut} is instructive: those are one-sided obligations where the subjective intent of the obligating party carries greater weight, operating under the inverse principle of {\i ad she-piv ve-libo shavin}. But in bilateral transactions like {\i kiddushin}, legal objectivity is paramount: what the term means halachically is what governs, regardless of what any individual speaker may have privately intended.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Ongoing Process View\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shivura Metaphor\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now introduces a third interpretive framework, presented by Ulla in the name of Rabbi Yochanan: {\i afilu me\u8217'ah tofsim bah} \u8212- even a hundred men could be betrothed to this woman under {\i me-achshav u-le\u8217'achar sheloshim yom}. Rav Assi transmits the same ruling in Rabbi Yochanan\u8217's name.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is a striking statement. It does not mean \u8212- Rashi is explicit on this \u8212- that there is a {\i safek} as to which one of a hundred men married her, such that any one of them might have been the operative betrothal. Rather, it means literally that all hundred could be simultaneously betrothed to her. The mechanism is explained by Rav Mesharshiya son of Rav Ami: {\i shavtah nafshah ki-sharga d\u8217'livni} \u8212- she made herself like a row of bricks, {\i de-chol chad ve-chad ravcha le-chavreih shavik} \u8212- each brick leaving space for the next one alongside it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The brick-row metaphor captures a third reading of {\i me-achshav u-le\u8217'achar sheloshim yom} that is distinct from both Rav\u8217's and Shmuel\u8217's. This phrase does not mean either \u8220"now\u8221" (with a {\i tenai}) or \u8220"later\u8221" (with a retraction of {\i me-achshav}). It means that the {\i kiddushin} is a continuous process: it begins now and concludes after thirty days. It is not a binary event \u8212- a switch that is either on or off \u8212- but an ongoing, partially-actualized state. During the thirty-day period, the woman is not fully betrothed to man number one, but she is not unbetrothed either. The process is underway. When man number two arrives and says {\i me-achshav u-le\u8217'achar esrim yom}, she is not yet fully married, and so his {\i kiddushin} too can take hold. Just as bricks are laid in overlapping rows, each man\u8217's {\i kiddushin} begins before the previous one has fully concluded, and the partial availability of the woman at each moment allows the next {\i kiddushin} to attach.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Partial Marriage Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot raises what seems a natural objection: elsewhere we have established that if a man says \u8220"half of you is betrothed to me,\u8221" the {\i kiddushin} does not take effect, because a woman cannot be half-married. How, then, can Rabbi Yochanan say that all hundred men are betrothed to her \u8212- is she not fractionally married to each?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot\u8217's answer distinguishes the two cases precisely. In the \u8220"half of you\u8221" case, the man leaves over a portion of the woman herself \u8212- he does not marry the full person. That is the defect: {\i kiddushin} requires taking the whole person, and if you attempt to marry only half of her, nothing takes effect. Here, by contrast, each man marries her completely \u8212- {\i lo sheyar be-gufa ha-ishah kelum ela gemurin kulam} \u8212- he did not leave over any part of her person; he married her in full. It is only that the marriage has not yet fully actualized, that the process is still ongoing. He left over time, not personhood. Since the remainder is temporal rather than personal, his {\i kiddushin} is not defective \u8212- it simply has not yet completed. And because it has not yet completed, the next man finds enough unresolved space to enter.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Get L\u8217'achar Mita Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Get After Death\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} applies Rabbi Yochanan\u8217's framework \u8212- {\i kiddushin} as ongoing process \u8212- to the {\i get} case. The case of {\i me-hayom u-le\u8217'achar mita get ve-eino get}, a man on his deathbed who says \u8220"from today and after I die,\u8221" produces a different result under Rabbi Yochanan\u8217's model than under Rav\u8217's or Shmuel\u8217's.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If the {\i get} is an ongoing process that begins now and concludes after the man dies, then by the time the process concludes \u8212- at the man\u8217's death \u8212- it arrives at a moment when the {\i get} cannot take effect, because a {\i get} must take effect while the husband is alive. The process started, but did not complete. Therefore, according to Rabbi Yochanan, there is no {\i safek} here at all: this is simply not a valid {\i get}. The woman is a widow, not a divorc\u233 ?e.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The halachic consequence is significant. If she is a widow, {\i yibum} should apply. The {\i Mishnah} records: {\i choletzes ve-lo mityabemes} \u8212- she performs {\i chalitza} but does not undergo {\i yibum}. This is fine according to Rav, whose position generates genuine {\i safek} \u8212- perhaps the {\i get} was valid, perhaps not. It is also fine according to Shmuel, who aligns with the {\i Rabbanan} of the {\i braita}. But according to Rabbi Yochanan, there is no {\i safek} \u8212- she is definitively not divorced. Why, then, does she not simply undergo {\i yibum}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava proposes: {\i get lehotzi\u8217'a u-mita lehotzi\u8217'a} \u8212- the {\i get} removes her from the husband\u8217's domain, and death likewise removes her from that domain. Perhaps the {\i get} initiated ninety-eight percent of the severance, and the death completed the remaining two percent \u8212- {\i ma she-shiyyer get gamar mita}. Abaye rejects this reasoning: {\i get motzi\u8217'a me-reshut yavam, mita machneset le-reshut yavam} \u8212- a {\i get} removes her from the domain of a potential {\i yavam}; death brings her into that domain. The two operate in opposite directions. Death cannot complete the process of a {\i get}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Get Models Applied\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye therefore says: yes, according to Rabbi Yochanan, she is definitively not divorced. The ongoing process did not complete before the husband died, and therefore there is no {\i get} at all. She is a widow and should by rights undergo {\i yibum} \u8212- or {\i chalitza}. The reason we do not permit {\i yibum}, and instead require {\i chalitza}, is not intrinsic to this case.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gezeira M\u8217'shum Leshon\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The reason is a {\i gezeira}: {\i gezeira mishum me-hayom im mati harei zeh get} \u8212- a protective ordinance against confusing this case with a different one. If a man says \u8220"from today {\i if} I die\u8221" \u8212- {\i im mati} \u8212- the {\i get} is unambiguously valid. This is a clear {\i tenai}: the {\i get} takes effect now on the condition that he dies from this illness. According to Rabbi Yochanan, this would be a complete, valid {\i get}, and there would be no {\i yibum} at all. Were we to permit {\i yibum} in the case of {\i me-hayom u-le\u8217'achar mita} \u8212- where the {\i get} failed because the process did not complete \u8212- people might confuse the two cases and permit {\i yibum} even in the {\i im mati} case, where she is definitively divorced and {\i yibum} would be {\i arayot}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} then asks the reverse: since we require {\i chalitza} in the {\i me-hayom u-le\u8217'achar mita} case, should we not also require {\i chalitza} in the {\i me-hayom im mati} case \u8212- as a {\i gezeira} lest the cases be confused in the other direction? The answer: if we require {\i chalitza} in the {\i im mati} case, people will conclude that the {\i get} was doubtful, leading them to permit {\i yibum} there. And {\i yibum} in the {\i im mati} case, where the {\i get} is valid, would be catastrophic. The {\i Gemara} responds: so too with our {\i gezeira} \u8212- requiring {\i chalitza} in the {\i me-hayom u-le\u8217'achar mita} case might lead to {\i yibum}. But the {\i Gemara} dismisses this: {\i tityabbem ve-ein bekach kelum} \u8212- in the {\i me-hayom u-le\u8217'achar mita} case, {\i yibum} is in principle permitted; we merely said not to do it for reasons of confusion. If requiring {\i chalitza} there leads someone to do {\i yibum}, that is not a disaster, because {\i yibum} is fundamentally valid in that case. By contrast, permitting {\i yibum} in the {\i im mati} case \u8212- where she is divorced \u8212- would be genuinely catastrophic.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The upshot is a three-way framework for {\i me-achshav u-le\u8217'achar sheloshim yom}: Shmuel holds it is unambiguously a {\i tenai}, uncertainty resolving at day thirty; Rav holds it is permanently ambiguous between \u8220"entirely now\u8221" and \u8220"entirely later\u8221"; and Rabbi Yochanan, via Ulla, holds it is an ongoing process \u8212- beginning now and concluding later \u8212- with all the attendant consequences for {\i kiddushin} and {\i get}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Al Menas Conditionality\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishnah Cases\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} moves to the next {\i Mishnah}, which deals with {\i al menas} \u8212- conditional {\i kiddushin}. {\i Ha-omer le-ishah harei at mekudeshes li al menas she-etten lach masayim zuz} \u8212- \u8220"you are betrothed to me on the condition that I give you two hundred {\i zuz}.\u8221" The {\i Mishnah} rules: {\i harei zo mekudeshes ve-hu yitten} \u8212- she is betrothed, and he must give. If he stipulated {\i al menas she-etten lach mikan ve-ad sheloshim yom} \u8212- within thirty days \u8212- then {\i nasan lah be-soch sheloshim yom mekudeshes, ve-im lav einah mekudeshes}. Further cases: {\i al menas she-yesh li masayim zuz} \u8212- on the condition I have two hundred {\i zuz} \u8212- she is betrothed if he does indeed have them. {\i Al menas she-arech masayim zuz} \u8212- on the condition I show you two hundred {\i zuz} \u8212- she is betrothed if he shows her, provided {\i ve-im her\u8217'ah lah al ha-shulchan einah mekudeshes} \u8212- if he showed her money on the table (Rashi: belonging to a money-changer, not his own), she is not betrothed, because it is evident he did not mean to show her someone else\u8217's money.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tenai Timing Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The operative question the {\i Mishnah} leaves unresolved \u8212- and which is now the subject of an {\i amoraic} dispute \u8212- is the precise meaning of {\i ve-hu yitten}. Does it mean \u8220"as of now, provided that he eventually gives\u8221" (a {\i tenai}, taking effect retroactively from the moment of {\i kiddushin}), or \u8220"when he gives\u8221" (taking effect only at the moment the money is transferred)?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Huna rules: {\i ve-hu yitten \u8212- tenai havei, mekayem tna\u8217'eih ve-azil} \u8212- it is a {\i tenai}; he fulfills his condition whenever he fulfills it, but the {\i kiddushin} dates from now. Rav Yehuda rules: {\i lich\u8217'she-yitten \u8212- lich\u8217'yaiv havei kiddushin, hashta miha lo havei kiddushin} \u8212- only when he gives the money does the {\i kiddushin} take effect; as of now, it does not.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i nafka mina} is immediate: if she accepted {\i kiddushin} from a second man before the first gave the money. According to Rav Huna, she was already betrothed to man number one from the outset, so the second {\i kiddushin} is ineffective. According to Rav Yehuda, since no {\i kiddushin} yet exists, the second {\i kiddushin} is valid.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kiddushin vs.\u160 ?Geirushin\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The parallel {\i Mishnah} in {\i Gittin} presents the same question in the context of divorce. A man says: {\i harei zeh gittech al menas she-titni li masayim zuz} \u8212- \u8220"here is your {\i get}, on the condition you give me two hundred {\i zuz}.\u8221" The {\i Mishnah} rules: {\i harei zo megureshes ve-hi titten} \u8212- she is divorced as long as she gives the money. Rav Huna applies the same reasoning: {\i ve-hi titten \u8212- tenai havei}, it is a {\i tenai}, and upon fulfilling the {\i tenai} she is retroactively divorced from when the {\i get} was given. Rav Yehuda says: {\i lich\u8217'she-titten} \u8212- only when she gives.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i nafka mina} in the {\i get} case is the {\i Gemara}\u8217's focus: the {\i get} is torn or lost before she gives the money. According to Rav Huna, this is no problem \u8212- the {\i tenai} can be fulfilled later, and the effect retroacts; the {\i get} need not be physically present at the moment of fulfillment. According to Rav Yehuda, since the {\i kiddushin} or {\i get} only takes effect at the moment of the condition\u8217's fulfillment, the operative instrument \u8212- money or document \u8212- must still exist at that moment. A {\i get} that has been destroyed cannot take effect even if the condition is later satisfied.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} notes that both cases must be stated independently, because there are contextual reasons to presume different intentions in each. In {\i kiddushin}, the man is seeking to bring the woman closer, which suggests eagerness to have the {\i kiddushin} take effect immediately \u8212- favoring Rav Huna\u8217's reading. But one might argue that the woman\u8217's reluctance to ask for money she was promised ({\i de-ichasfa lah le-mitbahyei}) means she would insist the {\i kiddushin} not take effect until payment is received \u8212- perhaps favoring Rav Yehuda. In {\i gittin}, the man is distancing himself and might prefer a delayed effect; but one might argue that he is not embarrassed to demand payment ({\i de-ihu lo kasif lei le-mitbah}) and therefore does not need to hold the {\i get}\u8217's effect hostage to receipt of funds, again favoring Rav Huna\u8217's reading. Each case presents independent considerations, and therefore each must be stated.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Al Menas Proof Texts\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Get Validity Proof\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now marshals decisive evidence in favor of Rav Huna. The first {\i braita}: a man says {\i harei zeh gittech al menas she-titni li masayim zuz} \u8212- even if the {\i get} is torn or lost, {\i harei zo megureshes} \u8212- she is divorced, as long as she gives the money. She should not, however, remarry until she has done so \u8212- not because she is not yet divorced, but because she must ensure she can satisfy the {\i tenai}. This {\i braita} clearly treats the {\i get} as taking effect from the moment of giving, with the condition functioning retroactively \u8212- precisely Rav Huna\u8217's position.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A second {\i braita} reinforces this: a man gives his wife a {\i get al menas she-titni li masayim zuz} and then dies. {\i Natna ein zekuka le-yavam} \u8212- if she gave the money and fulfilled the {\i tenai}, she is divorced and not subject to {\i yibum}. {\i Lo natna zekuka le-yavam} \u8212- if she did not give it, the {\i tenai} was not fulfilled and she is a widow subject to {\i yibum}. Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel adds: she may give the money to his brother, his father, or another relative \u8212- the {\i tenai} can be satisfied even post-mortem, by giving to heirs. The debate between {\i tanna kamma} and Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel is only whether {\i li} \u8212- \u8220"to me\u8221" \u8212- includes heirs. But both agree that the {\i tenai} can be satisfied after the man\u8217's death, and the {\i get} then takes effect retroactively to when it was given. This is a clear proof that {\i al menas} functions as a {\i tenai} that retroacts \u8212- {\i dkulei alma miha tnai havei}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 These two {\i braitas} constitute a {\i tiuvta} \u8212- a decisive contradiction \u8212- to Rav Yehuda.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Consensus on Al Menas\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Yehuda deflects by arguing that both {\i braitas} may reflect only the position of Rabbi, who holds {\i kol ha-omer al menas ke-omer me-achshav dami} \u8212- whoever says \u8220"on the condition\u8221" is as if he said \u8220"as of now.\u8221" This is Rav Huna\u8217's position, attributed here to Rabbi. Perhaps, Rav Yehuda claims, the {\i Rabbanan} dispute Rabbi and hold that {\i al menas} means {\i le-achar zman}, and he rules like the {\i Rabbanan}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Zeira then reports a tradition that resolves the matter entirely. When he was in Bavel, he had understood this to be a dispute between Rabbi and the {\i Rabbanan} \u8212- Rabbi holding {\i me-achshav}, the {\i Rabbanan} disputing. But when he made {\i aliyah} to Eretz Yisrael, he found Rabbi Assi sitting and teaching in the name of Rabbi Yochanan: {\i ha-kol modim be-omer al menas ke-omer me-achshav dami} \u8212- everyone agrees that {\i al menas} means {\i me-achshav}. It is not Rabbi\u8217's lone opinion. There are no {\i Rabbanan} who dispute it. The claim that there exists a dissenting view regarding {\i al menas} has no basis.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Dispute Scope Limited\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The only genuine dispute, Rabbi Yochanan reports, is the case of {\i me-hayom u-le\u8217'achar mita} \u8212- and there, the debate is recorded in the {\i braita}: {\i get ve-eino get divrei Chachamim; Rabbi omer kazeh get}. That ambiguous construction \u8212- \u8220"from today and after my death\u8221" \u8212- is genuinely disputed. {\i Al menas} is not.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} then addresses Rav Yehuda\u8217's position directly: if the {\i Rabbanan} dispute Rabbi even regarding {\i al menas}, why does the {\i braita} record their debate specifically around {\i me-hayom u-le\u8217'achar mita}? Why not state the dispute regarding {\i al menas} and let us infer from there? The answer is {\i koach d-hetera adif} \u8212- it is preferable to demonstrate the force of the permissive ruling. Rabbi\u8217's position is strongest precisely in the hard case \u8212- {\i me-hayom u-le\u8217'achar mita}, where the language seems to push toward a later effect \u8212- and it is {\i koach d-hetera} to show that even there, Rabbi considers it a valid {\i get}. Conversely, demonstrating the force of the {\i Rabbanan}\u8217's restrictive view in the easy case of {\i al menas} would serve no such purpose.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The upshot is clear: {\i al menas} universally means {\i me-achshav}. Nobody disputes this. The only case in which there is a genuine {\i tannaitic} dispute about whether a designation means now or later is {\i me-hayom u-le\u8217'achar mita} \u8212- the ambiguous formulation that stands at the center of all the debates traversed in this {\i daf}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Kiddushin 61\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Al Menat Conditions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 From Now Until 30 Days\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i daf} opens toward the bottom of 61b, a little before the {\i mishnah}, picking up a theme established in the preceding discussion. There we had been dealing with a man who says \u8220"this is your {\i get} from now and after thirty days,\u8221" where a range of possibilities arose concerning whether the formula meant the {\i get} took effect immediately, whether there was ambiguity about the intended moment, or whether it described some ongoing mode of effectuation. The comparison was to {\i kiddushin}, not {\i get}, and the preceding {\i mishnah} had established the principle {\i kol ha\u8217'omer al menat ke\u8217'omer me\u8217'achshav damei}: whoever uses the {\i al menat} formulation is treated as though he said \u8220"from now.\u8221" That position is clearly associated with Rebbe, and while there is a minority voice suggesting the matter is subject to debate, the overwhelming weight of evidence treats it as a consensus ruling.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 We now turn to the next case: {\i al menat she\u8217'etayn lecha mi\u8217'kan v\u8217'ad shloshim yom} \u8212- \u8220"I am betrothing you on the condition that I give you two hundred {\i zuz} from now until thirty days.\u8221" He must give the money within thirty days, or the {\i kiddushin} does not take effect. The {\i Gemara} raises the possibility that this is so obvious it need not be stated \u8212- {\i peshita}. The response: one might have thought that the phrase \u8220"within thirty days\u8221" is not strictly part of the condition itself but is rather an expression of self-encouragement, a kind of personal pledge to act promptly, while the operative {\i tnai} remains only the giving of two hundred {\i zuz}. The {\i Gemara} therefore clarifies that the time limit is indeed part of the condition itself, and failure to perform within thirty days leaves her not {\i mekudeshet}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ownership of Zuz\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} then takes up: {\i al menat she\u8217'yesh li matayim zuz} \u8212- \u8220"on the condition that I have two hundred {\i zuz}.\u8221" If he does indeed have two hundred {\i zuz}, she is married. The plain reading of the {\i mishnah} implies that if he does not have it, she is definitely not {\i mekudeshet}. But this raises an immediate difficulty: how does one prove a negative? If you look in his wallet and find nothing, how can you be certain there is no money hidden elsewhere? A {\i braita} makes this explicit \u8212- {\i chashinan shema yesh lo}: we are concerned that perhaps he does have two hundred {\i zuz} tucked away somewhere.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} resolves the apparent contradiction: {\i lo kashya, ha b\u8217'kiddushei vadai, ha b\u8217'kiddushei safek} \u8212- the {\i mishnah} means she is not {\i definitely} married, while the {\i braita} clarifies she is not definitely {\i unmarried} either. When one cannot establish whether a {\i tnai} has been satisfied, the result is {\i safek mekudeshet} \u8212- a state of doubtful betrothal, which is of course the worst of both worlds. She cannot freely remarry without a {\i get}, yet she is not definitively bound to this man.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Showing Money\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} next addresses {\i al menat she\u8217'arech matayim zuz} \u8212- \u8220"on the condition that I show you two hundred {\i zuz}.\u8221" If he shows her the money, she is married. But the {\i Gemara} immediately adds: {\i tana lo niskaven ela l\u8217'harot mishelo} \u8212- the implicit meaning of \u8220"show you\u8221" is \u8220"show you that I have it,\u8221" specifically from his own funds. He cannot show her money belonging to others and count that as fulfillment of the condition.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is a significant interpretive point. Technically the words \u8220"show you\u8221" were satisfied \u8212- he showed her money. Yet the {\i Gemara} reads in an implicit qualification that he never explicitly stated: the money must be his own. This is not {\i devarim shebalev} \u8212- a subjective, unexpressed intention that the law generally disregards \u8212- because there is an objectively understood context that any normal person would appreciate. When someone says \u8220"I will show you two hundred {\i zuz}\u8221" in the context of betrothal, the universal understanding is that he is demonstrating his capacity to provide, not merely producing coins that happen to be nearby.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} then rules: {\i v\u8217'im her\u8217'ah al hashulchan einah mekudeshet} \u8212- if he showed her money on the table of a money-changer, she is not {\i mekudeshet}. {\i Peshita} \u8212- obviously. The case that actually requires the ruling is the following: {\i d\u8217'af al gav d\u8217'nakat damei b\u8217'iska} \u8212- he is holding money given to him to invest. Here there is a genuine {\i hava amina}: the money is not wholly unrelated to him; he has investment rights, will earn a share of the profits, and in a sense manages the funds. Nevertheless, since it was never his money to begin with \u8212- it was entrusted to him by others for investment purposes \u8212- showing it does not satisfy the condition. His demonstrating that he oversees a large investment portfolio does not constitute showing that he has two hundred {\i zuz} of his own.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Land as Condition\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The parallel case for land follows: {\i al menat she\u8217'yesh li beit kor afar} \u8212- on the condition that he has a plot of land measuring a {\i beit kor}, which {\i Rashi} defines as seventy-five thousand square {\i amot}. If he has it, she is {\i mekudeshet}; if he does not, she is not. When he specifies a location \u8212- {\i al menat she\u8217'yesh li b\u8217'makom ploni} \u8212- she is only married if the land is in that precise location. Land elsewhere does not satisfy the condition. The {\i Gemara} addresses the possibility that he might argue: the land is functionally equivalent, the only difference is distance, and he pledges to exert the extra effort in traveling to it \u8212- {\i ana tarchana u\u8217'maitina}. The {\i Gemara} rejects this: the location was part of the explicit condition, and he cannot discount what he stated clearly.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This raises a broader question that runs throughout this section: when does context allow us to {\i add} something unstated, and when does it allow us to {\i discount} something that was stated? When he says \u8220"show you\u8221" and we read in \u8220"mine,\u8221" we are supplementing an implicit understanding that nobody would contest. But when something was stated explicitly \u8212- a specific location, a specific time frame \u8212- context alone does not suffice to say he did not really mean it or that no one should care. There is a meaningful distinction between using context to fill a gap and using it to override an express term. The latter requires a far higher threshold, and even if the deviation seems trivial in practice, the condition as stated remains binding.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Showing Land\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} also takes up {\i al menat she\u8217'arecha beit kor afar} \u8212- on the condition that he shows her a {\i beit kor} of land \u8212- with exactly the parallel structure to the money case. {\i Tana: lo niskaven ela leharot mishelo} \u8212- when he says \u8220"show you,\u8221" he means show you mine. If he shows her land in a valley that he does not own, she is not {\i mekudeshet}. The operative {\i hava amina} here involves the case of a {\i distura} \u8212- a sharecropper. This is a stronger case than the money analogy, because the man never said \u8220"I\u8217'll show you my land\u8221" \u8212- he said \u8220"I\u8217'll show you a {\i beit kor} of land.\u8221" One might argue that a sharecropper demonstrating that he has means of agricultural support for the family has in substance fulfilled the promise. Yet the {\i Gemara} rules that even here, the implicit meaning of \u8220"show you\u8221" is that it must be land he fully owns.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What is striking is the degree to which we read the word \u8220"mine\u8221" into a statement that never used it. He said he would show her; we infer he meant to demonstrate that he possesses. And the inference is so powerful that even partial ownership \u8212- sharecropping rights, investment stakes \u8212- does not suffice. The {\i tnai} was for full ownership, even though those exact words were never spoken.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Beit Kor Definition\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hekdesh & Crevices\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having established that a {\i beit kor} is the relevant land measurement in the {\i kiddushin} case, the {\i Gemara} takes up a more fundamental question: what exactly constitutes a {\i beit kor}? The answer is less obvious than it first appears, because land is rarely perfectly flat, and the treatment of irregular topographical features \u8212- crevices and rocks \u8212- differs depending on the legal context in which the measurement arises.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} opens with the {\i hekdesh} case. When one sanctifies a field during the {\i yovel} period, the {\i Torah} establishes a fixed redemption rate: fifty {\i shekel kesef} for every plot of land on which one plants a {\i chomer} of barley seed \u8212- that is, for every {\i beit kor}. The relevant rule from the {\i mishnah} is: if the field contains crevices ten {\i tefachim} deep or rocks ten {\i tefachim} tall, they are not measured together with the field \u8212- {\i ein nimdadim imah}. If they are less than that, they are measured with it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rashi} understands {\i nimdadim imah} to mean that the measurement literally follows the contour of the terrain \u8212- up and over the rock, down into the crevice \u8212- so that the total measured area includes those features. Others understand it more simply: features below the threshold are treated as if they are flat and included in the overall area, while those above the threshold are subtracted from the total.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} asks: why does it matter whether the crevices and rocks are included in the measurement or not? Even if they are excluded from the main field and treated as separate sanctified spaces, the owner said \u8220"I am sanctifying this land,\u8221" and everything within the field\u8217's borders is in principle sanctified \u8212- either as part of the field or as its own unit. What is the {\i nafka mina}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The answer lies in the redemption structure. The fixed fifty {\i shekel} rate applies per {\i beit kor}. If the field was exactly a {\i beit kor} and you subtract the crevice, you no longer have a {\i beit kor} subject to the fixed rate \u8212- you have a slightly-less-than-{\i beit kor} field and a separate crevice that is not a field at all. The slightly-smaller field gets redeemed at the proportional rate (which follows from the {\i Torah}\u8217's use of the word {\i sadeh}, teaching that even partial plots are assessed proportionally). But the rock or water-filled crevice, as a non-plantable space, is not a {\i sadeh} and gets redeemed not at the standard rate but at market value.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Mar Ukva bar Chama} then specifies: the entire discussion of {\i hekdesh} concerns crevices filled with water \u8212- {\i nikaymei d\u8217'maya} \u8212- which are not plantable. The comparison with rocks supports this: just as rocks cannot be planted, we are dealing with features that are inherently non-agricultural.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Plantability Standard\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This introduces the governing principle for the {\i hekdesh} case: {\b plantability}. Whether a topographical feature is included in the {\i beit kor} or treated as a separate unit depends on whether it can be farmed. A crevice filled with water cannot be planted; if it is deep enough to constitute its own space (ten {\i tefachim}), it is not a {\i sadeh} and is excluded. If it is shallower, the {\i Gemara} explains: {\i hani nagnei d\u8217'ara mikru} \u8212- it is treated as a basin of the land, an integral topographical feature of an otherwise cultivable field. Similarly, a shorter rock is called {\i shidra d\u8217'ara} \u8212- the spine of the land \u8212- a natural feature within a plantable field, not a separate space.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The logic is coherent: a field need not be perfectly uniform in every square {\i amah} to be called a {\i sadeh}. Minor topographical variation is part of what a field is. But once a feature becomes deep or tall enough to constitute its own independent space \u8212- its own {\i reshut} \u8212- it is evaluated on its own terms. If those terms yield a non-plantable space, it falls outside the category of {\i sadeh} and is redeemed differently.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mechira Standard\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} then turns to the corresponding rule in {\i Bava Batra} for a sale \u8212- {\i mechira} \u8212- which, as anticipated, follows a meaningfully different standard. The {\i mishnah} there addresses the case where one says {\i beit kor afar ani mocher lach} \u8212- \u8220"I am selling you a {\i beit kor} of land\u8221" \u8212- and the field contains crevices ten {\i tefachim} deep or rocks ten {\i tefachim} tall. There too, they are {\i ein nimdadim imah} \u8212- not measured with the land, and the seller must make up the shortfall with additional flat land.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But {\i Ukva bar Chama} rules: {\i af al pi she\u8217'ein mele\u8217'im mayim} \u8212- even if the crevices are not filled with water. Unlike the {\i hekdesh} case, where plantability is the criterion, here the exclusion applies even to a dry, theoretically plantable crevice.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Pappa} explains the rationale: {\i lafi she\u8217'ein adam rotzeh sheyiten et ma\u8217'otav b\u8217'sadeh achat v\u8217'teira\u8217'eh lo kishnayim ushlosha mekomot} \u8212- a person does not want to spend money on what he understands to be one field and then find himself working two or three separate spaces. Even if the deep crevice is technically cultivable, it requires separate attention, separate plowing, separate harvesting \u8212- a distinct agricultural operation. The buyer was expecting to purchase a coherent, unified working unit, and a field fragmented by deep crevices does not deliver that. The standard here is not formal plantability but the {\b reasonable expectation of the buyer} \u8212- what a person of ordinary sense would have meant and understood when agreeing to purchase a {\i beit kor}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The underlying distinction between {\i hekdesh} and {\i mechira} is therefore conceptual. Redemption from {\i hekdesh} is governed by the {\i Torah}\u8217's formal definition of a {\i sadeh}: so long as the land as a whole qualifies as a field \u8212- meaning it is predominantly plantable \u8212- the technical definition controls. There is no \u8220"meeting of minds,\u8221" no subjective expectation to assess; the question is whether the land fits the Torah\u8217's category. A {\i mechira}, by contrast, is an agreement between two parties, and it is therefore governed by what each party reasonably understood the transaction to include. Economy of use, ease of harvest, and the coherence of the agricultural unit are all part of what the buyer was expecting to receive.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kiddushin Standard\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This sets up the {\i Gemara}\u8217's question regarding our case: {\i hacha mai} \u8212- which standard applies when a man says \u8220"I will show you a {\i beit kor}\u8221" as a condition of {\i kiddushin}? Do we compare it to {\i hekdesh} or to {\i mechira}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might argue that {\i kiddushin} resembles {\i mechira}, since it too involves a meeting of minds between two parties, and the woman might reasonably have expected a field that she could work as a coherent unit rather than one fractured by deep crevices. This argument mirrors the one the {\i Gemara} just made on behalf of the buyer: she could say she did not anticipate the extra difficulty. Nevertheless, the {\i Gemara} rules that as long as the crevice is not filled with water and is therefore plantable, the condition is satisfied. The man can say: {\i ana tarchana u\u8217'zarna u\u8217'maitina} \u8212- I will invest the extra effort, I will plant the crevice, I will bring in the yield. If the land is plantable \u8212- even if demanding \u8212- he has demonstrated that he has a {\i beit kor} of agricultural land to his name, and the {\i tnai} is met.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This result is instructive alongside what was said earlier about location. When the condition was not satisfied \u8212- when he claimed land in location A and the land was actually in location B \u8212- he could not say \u8220"don\u8217't worry, I\u8217'll do the extra traveling.\u8221" That is a case where the condition was genuinely unmet and he was proposing to compensate for its non-fulfillment. Here, by contrast, the question is definitional: {\i is} this a {\i beit kor} or not? Under the applicable standard \u8212- plantability \u8212- it is. He is not waiving or compensating for a failed condition; he is asserting that the condition has in fact been met under the correct measure, and that he will personally absorb whatever extra labor that entails.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Double Condition\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Meir Principle\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now moves to one of the most consequential debates in the laws of {\i tnai}: the doctrine of {\i Rabbi Meir} concerning the proper formulation of a conditional. {\i Rabbi Meir omer: kol tnai she\u8217'eino k\u8217'tnai bnei Gad u\u8217'vnei Reuven, eino tnai} \u8212- any condition that is not formulated like the condition that {\i Moshe} made with the tribes of Gad and Reuven is not a valid condition. If the condition is defective, it is simply ignored and the underlying act \u8212- the {\i kiddushin}, the sale, the {\i get} \u8212- takes effect unconditionally.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The biblical source is the negotiation in {\i Bemidbar} where {\i Moshe} tells the tribes of Gad and Reuven: if you go armed before your brothers, you will receive the land of {\i Gil\u8217'ad}. And it is written: {\i v\u8217'im lo ya\u8217'avru chalutzim} \u8212- and if the armed men do not cross over, then they will receive with you in the land of {\i Kena\u8217'an}. The positive side of the condition was stated, and then the negative side was explicitly stated as well. {\i Rabbi Meir} derives from this that a valid {\i tnai} requires the formulation of {\b both} consequences \u8212- the positive outcome if the condition is met and the negative outcome if it is not. This is the {\i tnai kaful}, the doubled condition.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Logic Explanation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The logical basis for this requirement is genuine and worth noting. In propositional logic, \u8220"if P then Q\u8221" does not entail \u8220"if not P then not Q.\u8221" What it entails is the contrapositive: \u8220"if not Q then not P.\u8221" The two are not equivalent. If one says \u8220"you are betrothed to me if I give you two hundred {\i zuz},\u8221" that statement alone does not logically imply that if he fails to give the money she is not betrothed. Strictly speaking, the non-fulfillment of the antecedent leaves the consequent indeterminate. Only by stating the negative explicitly \u8212- \u8220"and if I do not give you two hundred {\i zuz}, you are not betrothed\u8221" \u8212- does the condition operate symmetrically and unambiguously govern both outcomes.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is not how most people speak in ordinary conversation, but {\i Rabbi Meir} holds that when it comes to legal acts as powerful as {\i kiddushin} \u8212- where the {\i ma\u8217'aseh} carries enormous weight and its undoing requires extensive formulation \u8212- only a rigorously complete statement of the condition suffices to make the {\i ma\u8217'aseh} contingent.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Three Requirements\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rashi} identifies three requirements derived from the {\i tnai b\u8217'nei Gad u\u8217'vnei Reuven}, of which the {\i mishnah} places primary emphasis on the {\i tnai kaful}:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The first is {\i tnai kaful} \u8212- the double condition, as described. Both the positive and negative outcomes must be articulated.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The second is {\i tnai kodem l\u8217'ma\u8217'aseh} \u8212- the condition must be stated before the act. If he says \u8220"you are betrothed to me \u8212- if I give you two hundred {\i zuz},\u8221" the act ({\i kiddushin}) is stated first and the condition appended afterward. Since primacy has been given to the act, the condition is not strong enough to prevent it from taking effect. The underlying principle is that the {\i ma\u8217'aseh} is so powerful a legal event that in order for a condition to subordinate it completely, the conditionality must be established from the very outset. Stating the act first gives it an unqualified primacy that a subsequent condition cannot overcome.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The third requirement is {\i hein kodem l\u8217'lav} \u8212- the affirmative outcome must be stated before the negative. One must say \u8220"if I give you, you are betrothed; if I do not give you, you are not betrothed\u8221" \u8212- not the reverse. If one ends by stating the positive outcome, the act is again given the last word, imparting to it a kind of finality that undermines the conditionality. {\i Rabbi Meir} requires that the condition be framed so that the act\u8217's contingency is expressed in every way possible: it comes second, its positive outcome is stated first, and the negative consequence is made explicit.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The formula that satisfies all three: {\i im etayn lach matayim zuz, harei at mekudeshet li; v\u8217'im lo etayn lach, einech mekudeshet li} \u8212- \u8220"if I give you two hundred {\i zuz}, you are betrothed to me; and if I do not give you, you are not betrothed to me.\u8221" The {\i tnai} precedes the {\i ma\u8217'aseh}, the {\i hein} precedes the {\i lav}, and both sides are stated.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is notable that every case in the preceding {\i mishnayot} \u8212- formulated as {\i al menat she\u8217'etayn lach matayim zuz}, {\i titkadshi li} \u8212- places the act before the condition, violating {\i Rabbi Meir}\u8217's rule of {\i tnai kodem l\u8217'ma\u8217'aseh}, and none of them include a {\i tnai kaful}. Those {\i mishnayot} are not according to {\i Rabbi Meir}, and it is a legitimate question whether we {\i pasken} like him across all three requirements or only in some of them \u8212- particularly {\i tnai kaful}, which has stronger reason to be followed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Hanina Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Hanina ben Gamliel} disagrees with the premise that the doubled formulation in {\i Bemidbar} was necessary to teach the doctrine of {\i tnai kaful}. He holds: {\i tzarich hadavar l\u8217'omro} \u8212- there was a specific reason {\i Moshe} had to state the negative side, but it was not to establish a general principle requiring repetition. Had {\i Moshe} not stated \u8220"and if they do not cross over,\u8221" one might have inferred that the tribes would receive nothing at all \u8212- {\i afilu b\u8217'eretz Kena\u8217'an lo yinchalu}. The explicit statement was necessary not to articulate the consequence of non-fulfillment of the positive {\i tnai} (that was already implicit), but to inform them that they would nonetheless retain a portion in {\i Eretz Kena\u8217'an}. The repetition was doing positive work \u8212- granting a consolation benefit \u8212- not negative work establishing a rule of formulation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 On {\i Rabbi Hanina}\u8217's view, therefore, a single-sided {\i tnai} is perfectly valid. The non-fulfillment of a stated condition implicitly negates the act; explicit repetition is not required.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Pessukim Analysis\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} examines this dispute through a careful reading of the relevant {\i pesukim}. {\i Rabbi Meir} challenges {\i Rabbi Hanina}: if the purpose of the negative formulation was merely to grant them a portion in {\i Eretz Kena\u8217'an}, it would have sufficed to say {\i im lo ya\u8217'avru v\u8217'ne\u8217'echzu b\u8217'tochachem} \u8212- if they do not cross over, they will be settled among you. That alone would establish their entitlement to a share among the rest of {\i Bnei Yisrael}. Why does the verse need to add {\i b\u8217'eretz Kena\u8217'an}? The extra words, says {\i Rabbi Meir}, are limiting: they specify {\i only} the land of {\i Kena\u8217'an}, thereby explicitly negating the earlier promise of {\i Gil\u8217'ad}. This demonstrates that without such explicit negation, the original promise would have remained in effect \u8212- proving that a {\i tnai kaful} is necessary.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Hanina} responds: had the {\i Torah} not said {\i b\u8217'eretz Kena\u8217'an}, the word {\i b\u8217'tochachem} would have been understood to mean among you in {\i Gil\u8217'ad} \u8212- a shared portion in the Transjordanian territory \u8212- and would not have conveyed any entitlement to {\i Kena\u8217'an} at all. The phrase {\i b\u8217'eretz Kena\u8217'an} was therefore necessary to specify that they receive a portion in {\i Kena\u8217'an} proper, not merely a share in {\i Gil\u8217'ad}. The non-receipt of all of {\i Gil\u8217'ad} in the case of non-fulfillment was already obvious from the simple logic of the condition and required no statement.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Meir} counters: {\i b\u8217'tochachem kol heicha d\u8217'ato lachu mashma} \u8212- \u8220"among you\u8221" means wherever you have, encompassing both {\i Gil\u8217'ad} and {\i Kena\u8217'an}. Had it said only {\i im lo ya\u8217'avru v\u8217'ne\u8217'echzu b\u8217'tochachem}, one would have understood that they receive equal shares everywhere \u8212- in {\i Gil\u8217'ad} and {\i Kena\u8217'an} alike. {\i B\u8217'eretz Kena\u8217'an} is therefore restricting: they get {\i Kena\u8217'an} and not {\i Gil\u8217'ad}. That restriction constitutes an explicit rejection of the original promise of {\i Gil\u8217'ad}, and without it, the original {\i tnai} would not have been negated.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Meir vs Hanina\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The fundamental disagreement can now be sharpened. {\i Rabbi Meir} holds that without the explicit negation embedded in {\i b\u8217'eretz Kena\u8217'an}, the original promise of {\i Gil\u8217'ad} would have remained in force even if the condition went unfulfilled. The entire doubled formulation was therefore necessary to negate the original {\i tnai} \u8212- establishing the general rule of {\i tnai kaful}. {\i Rabbi Hanina ben Gamliel} holds that the negation of the original {\i tnai} was already implicit; the affirmative statement of the {\i tnai} is sufficient to generate, by implication, its negative consequence. The doubled language served the entirely different purpose of conferring a positive benefit \u8212- a portion in {\i Kena\u8217'an} \u8212- that would not otherwise have been granted.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Inheritance Parable\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 To illustrate his position, {\i Rabbi Hanina ben Gamliel} offers a {\i mashal}. A man on his deathbed distributes his estate among his sons. He says: Reuven shall inherit such-and-such a field; Shimon shall inherit such-and-such a field; and Levi, if he gives two hundred {\i zuz} to the estate, shall inherit field number three. If he had stopped there, one might have worried that Levi is disinherited entirely if he fails to give the money. The father therefore adds: if he does not give, he shall inherit together with his brothers among the remaining assets. {\i Mi garam lo l\u8217'yerash im echav b\u8217'shar nechasim? Kefelo garam lo} \u8212- what enabled him to share with his brothers in the remainder? The repetition \u8212- the doubled statement \u8212- did that. The parable\u8217's point: the doubled language was needed not to prevent the original promise from standing in the absence of fulfillment, but affirmatively to confer a benefit that would not otherwise have existed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Final Resolution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} notes a tension between the parable and the earlier language of the {\i mishnah} itself. The {\i mishnah} had stated that without the repetition, one might have inferred {\i she\u8217'afilu b\u8217'eretz Kena\u8217'an lo yinchalu} \u8212- that they would not have received {\i any} share in {\i Eretz Kena\u8217'an}. This suggests that {\i venachazu b\u8217'tochachem} alone would have given them a share in {\i Gil\u8217'ad} (not {\i Kena\u8217'an}), and only {\i b\u8217'eretz Kena\u8217'an} extended that to {\i Kena\u8217'an} as well. But the parable implies the repetition only helped with {\i shar nechasim} \u8212- the remainder \u8212- not with the primary promised asset.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} resolves the tension: {\i lo kashya \u8212- ha mikamei denamlei Rabbi Meir venachazu, ha levatar denamlei Rabbi Meir venachazu}. The answer turns on the phrase {\i venachazu} itself. Before the specification {\i b\u8217'eretz Kena\u8217'an}, the phrase {\i venachazu b\u8217'tochachem} already established, on its own, that they would receive a share in {\i Gil\u8217'ad}. Then {\i b\u8217'eretz Kena\u8217'an} added that they would also receive a share in {\i Kena\u8217'an}. For {\i Rabbi Hanina}, the logic of a single {\i tnai} already negated their exclusive claim to all of {\i Gil\u8217'ad} without any need for the doubled language; both subsequent phrases served only to specify and expand the positive benefits they would retain. For {\i Rabbi Meir}, this entire scaffolding was necessary precisely to overcome the force of the original unrepeated promise \u8212- which, without explicit negation, would have continued to entitle them to all of {\i Gil\u8217'ad}. The {\i sugyah} on the parsing of the {\i pesukim} is thus complete, and the {\i shiur} will continue tomorrow with the working out of {\i Rabbi Meir}\u8217's doctrine of {\i tnai kaful} in its practical applications.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Kiddushin 62\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Tnai Kaful Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Opening & Context\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The daf begins in the middle of 61b, picking up the debate between Rabbi Meir and Rabbi Chanina ben Gamliel concerning whether the requirement of a {\i tnai kaful} \u8212- a double condition \u8212- can be derived from the condition set by Bnei Gad and Bnei Reuven, and whether, absent an explicit statement of the negative clause, one should assume the act is binding even if the condition is not met.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Before entering the {\i sugyah} proper, it is worth noting the broader framing of the debate. Rabbi Meir\u8217's position is articulated in two distinct ways in the rabbinic literature. One formulation is the technical halachic principle: {\i kol tnai she-eino ke-tnai Bnei Gad u-Bnei Reuven eino tnai} \u8212- any condition that is not structured like the condition of Bnei Gad and Bnei Reuven is not a valid condition. That is a claim about {\i hilchos tna\u8217'im}, about what makes a stipulation legally operative in the context of a halakhic act. The second formulation, however, is purely linguistic: {\i miklal lav ee ata shomea hen u-miklal hen ee ata shomea lav} \u8212- from the positive you cannot infer the negative, and from the negative you cannot infer the positive. That principle has nothing to do with formal legal conditionality. It is a claim about how language works and how rigorously we should apply formal logic to ordinary speech. The {\i gemara\u8217's} strategy in the passages that follow \u8212- moving through a series of {\i psukim} from across the Torah \u8212- strongly suggests it is this second, linguistic dimension that is primarily at issue, even as Tosafot throughout the {\i sugyah} attempts to read everything through the narrower lens of {\i tnai kaful} in the technical halachic sense.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Bereishit Example\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} opens with a {\i bishlama} formulation: it makes sense according to Rabbi Meir that the verse in Bereishit 4 states, {\i im tetiv se\u8217'et v\u8217'im lo tetiv la-petach chatat rovetz} \u8212- if you do good you will be uplifted, and if you do not do good, sin crouches at the door. According to Rabbi Meir, who holds that you cannot infer the negative from the positive, it was necessary to state both sides explicitly.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then asks: and according to Rabbi Chanina ben Gamliel, who maintains that the negative is indeed implied by the positive, why did the verse need to state the negative side at all? The answer is: {\i itzterich} \u8212- it was necessary. Without the explicit negative clause, one might have thought that {\i im tetiv agra} \u8212- if you do good you are rewarded \u8212- and {\i im lo tetiv}, if you do not do good, {\i lo agra ve-lo dina} \u8212- you simply receive no reward, but no punishment either. In other words, Rabbi Chanina ben Gamliel\u8217's point is not that the negative clause is superfluous, but that it carries additional information beyond the mere negation of the positive. Without it, we would know only that one does not receive what was promised in the first clause; we would not know that the opposite consequence actively applies. This distinction \u8212- between the absence of reward and the imposition of punishment \u8212- is precisely what necessitates stating the reverse.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sotah Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} turns to a verse from the {\i sotah} passage: {\i im lo shachav ish otach v\u8217'im lo satit tuma tachat ishech hinaki} \u8212- if no man has lain with you and if you have not strayed from your husband, you will be cleansed. The verse does not state the converse \u8212- that if you did stray you will be punished. According to Rabbi Chanina ben Gamliel, this is the expected pattern: you need not state the flip side. The negative of the first clause implies only that you will not receive what the first clause promised, but does not by itself indicate a positive punishment.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But according to Rabbi Meir, who requires the double condition, why does the verse omit the explicit reverse? The {\i gemara} answers: {\i hinaki ketiv} \u8212- the word {\i hinaki} is written {\i chaser}, defectively, without the expected {\i yud}. Rabbi Tanchum teaches that this orthographic anomaly is intentional, allowing the verse to be read as a {\i drasha} of {\i chinaki} \u8212- you will be strangled. The flip side is thus hidden within the {\i pasuk} itself. The word that says \u8220"you will be cleansed\u8221" simultaneously implies \u8220"you will be strangled,\u8221" and Rabbi Meir can maintain that without such a device, the verse would have been deficient.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Chanina ben Gamliel, for his part, also explains why the verse required even the implicit suggestion of {\i chinaki}: without it, one might have thought that {\i im lo shachav} \u8212- if she is innocent, she is cleansed \u8212- but {\i im shachav}, if she committed adultery, {\i lo hinaki ve-lo chinaki} \u8212- she neither gets cleansed nor strangled, merely violating a prohibition without incurring the {\i sotah} punishment. The verse therefore encodes the punishment even within its conditional structure.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is worth pausing here. The {\i gemara}\u8217's movement through these verses \u8212- Bereishit, Vayikra, the {\i sotah} passage \u8212- makes clear that what is being examined is not only a formal rule of {\i tnai kaful} in the domain of {\i kiddushin} or {\i gittin}, but a broader question of how conditional language functions. When a verse says \u8220"if X then Y,\u8221" does the reader know what happens in the case of not-X? From a strict logical standpoint, the implication does not follow. From the standpoint of ordinary language, it often does. Rabbi Meir holds the stricter logical position; Rabbi Chanina ben Gamliel holds that ordinary language entitles one to infer the reverse, but only in the limited sense that the positive result of the first clause will not obtain \u8212- nothing more.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Abraham\u8217's Oath\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} next cites the episode of Avraham\u8217's oath to his servant in Bereishit 24. Avraham adjures his servant not to take a wife for his son Yitzchak from the daughters of Canaan, and instructs him to go to Avraham\u8217's homeland. He then says: {\i v\u8217'im lo taveh ha\u8217'isha lalechet acharecha v\u8217'nikita mishvuati} \u8212- and if the woman is unwilling to follow you, then you will be cleansed of my oath. And similarly, {\i v\u8217'im lo yitnu lach} \u8212- if they do not give her to you. According to Rabbi Meir, it makes sense that the verse spells out the condition under which the servant would be released from the oath, because without stating it explicitly one could not have inferred it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} notes that the passages being cited include both Avraham\u8217's original formulation and the servant\u8217's later repetition of it to Lavan, treating them as part of the same narrative unit.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot raises a pointed question: this is not really a {\i tnai} in the formal halachic sense at all. Avraham did not say \u8220"you are bound by the oath if X and released if not-X.\u8221" He issued a direct oath \u8212- you shall not take a wife from the daughters of Canaan. The case of {\i v\u8217'nikita mishvuati} \u8212- being released from the oath \u8212- is a separate matter, not a classically structured condition. Tosafot asks: {\i ma inyan zeh etzel machlokes} \u8212- what does this have to do with the dispute? If Avraham had not spelled out the conditions of release, the servant might simply have assumed he was bound by the oath unconditionally, whether he succeeded or not. The fact that the release had to be stated explicitly tells us something, but Tosafot is uncertain whether it tells us the same thing as the dispute about {\i tnai kaful}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara}\u8217's answer is that according to Rabbi Chanina ben Gamliel, even he concedes that the release conditions needed to be stated, because otherwise one might have assumed: if the parents are willing but the girl herself is not, bring her against her will. The verse therefore had to say {\i v\u8217'im lo taveh ha\u8217'isha} \u8212- if the woman herself is unwilling \u8212- to make clear that her refusal alone, independently of her parents\u8217' objection, is sufficient grounds for the servant\u8217's release. Similarly, {\i v\u8217'im lo yitnu lach} \u8212- even if she is willing but her family objects \u8212- that too suffices. Neither side\u8217's objection alone, without the other\u8217's, would have been assumed to constitute a legitimate out without explicit statement.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara}\u8217's handling of this passage makes it clear, again, that what is at stake is not primarily a technical rule about the structure of a halakhic {\i tnai}, but rather a question about what we can and cannot infer from the language of conditionality in its broadest sense.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Verse Support\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Blessings/Curses\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} continues to marshal biblical support for Rabbi Meir\u8217's position, turning to the {\i tochachah} passages in Vayikra. The verse states: {\i im bechukotai telechu} \u8212- if you follow my statutes, then all the blessings enumerated will come upon you. The Torah then separately states: {\i v\u8217'im bechukotai timasu} \u8212- but if you despise my statutes, then the curses will follow. According to Rabbi Meir, one cannot infer the curses from the mere absence of the blessings; the negative clause must be stated explicitly.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 According to Rabbi Chanina ben Gamliel: why state both? The {\i gemara} answers that without the explicit negative clause, one would have known only that failing to follow the {\i mitzvos} means you do not receive the promised {\i bracha} \u8212- but one would not know that something actively bad happens. The punishment is not implied by the mere absence of the reward. That is precisely why the verse must say it: {\i lo bracha v\u8217'lo klalah} \u8212- no blessing and no curse \u8212- is what one would have assumed; the explicit statement is necessary to convey that the curse actively follows.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The same analysis applies to the verses in Yeshayahu: {\i im tovu ushmartem tuv ha\u8217'aretz tocheilu} \u8212- if you desire and obey, you will eat of the good of the land; {\i v\u8217'im temanu u-meritem cherev tukhallu} \u8212- but if you rebel, you will be consumed by the sword. Again, without the second clause, one would have known only that the reward is withheld, not that a punishment is incurred.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 At this point the {\i gemara} briefly digresses to explain the phrase {\i cherev tukhallu} \u8212- \u8220"the sword will consume you\u8221" \u8212- which presents an unusual grammatical structure. Rava explains that this refers to {\i milcha galulanita nahama d\u8217'se\u8217'arei akusha u-vatzli} \u8212- coarse salt, hard barley bread, and onions. The teaching is that plain hard bread with salt and onions is as harmful to the body as swallowing swords, so that {\i cherev tukhallu} denotes eating food that tears up the body from within.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sotah Drasha\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} returns to the {\i sotah} passage in greater depth. The verse reads: {\i im lo shachav ish otach v\u8217'im lo satit tuma tachat ishech hinaki} \u8212- and you will be cleansed. The verse presents only the positive outcome of innocence; it does not explicitly state what happens if she did sin. This supports Rabbi Chanina ben Gamliel: you do not need to state the negative clause; you only need to specify the positive, and the implication is that failure to meet the condition means failure to receive what the positive clause promised \u8212- in this case, cleansing.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But according to Rabbi Meir, the verse should have spelled out {\i chinaki} \u8212- strangling \u8212- as the reverse. The {\i gemara} answers, as noted above, that {\i hinaki ketiv} \u8212- the word is written {\i chaser} \u8212- allowing for the {\i drasha} of {\i chinaki} to be embedded within the text. Rabbi Meir can thus maintain his position: the reverse is stated, just in an encoded form.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Chanina ben Gamliel then explains why even according to his view the hint was necessary: without it, one might have assumed that {\i im shachav} \u8212- if she did commit adultery \u8212- {\i lo hinaki v\u8217'lo chinaki} \u8212- neither cleansed nor punished in the specific way the {\i sotah} ceremony implies, but merely in violation of a general prohibition. The encoded word {\i chinaki} therefore adds: no, the specific {\i sotah} punishment does apply.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Parah Adumah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then cites the laws of {\i parah adumah} purification. The verse states: {\i hu yitchata bo bayom hashlishi u-vayom hashvi\u8217'i yithar} \u8212- he shall purify himself on the third day and on the seventh day, and he will be cleansed. The verse then adds: {\i v\u8217'im lo yitchata bayom hashlishi u-vayom hashvi\u8217'i lo yithar} \u8212- and if he does not do so on both the third and seventh days, he will not become pure. According to Rabbi Meir, it makes sense that the verse states both sides: without the negative clause, one could not infer that failure to do both invalidates the purification.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 According to Rabbi Chanina ben Gamliel: why must both sides be stated? The {\i gemara} answers that without the negative clause, one might have thought the requirement of day three and day seven is a {\i lechatchila} but not strictly necessary {\i bedi\u8217'eved}. Perhaps if one performed the purification only on day three or only on day seven, it would still be effective after the fact. The negative clause teaches that both days are indispensable: absence of either renders the purification invalid.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Heifer Details\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having cited the {\i parah adumah} verse in the context of the {\i tnai kaful} debate, the {\i gemara} pauses to expound on two additional phrases in those laws that might appear redundant. The verse {\i viyazeh ha-tahor al ha-tamei bayom hashlishi u-vayom hashvi\u8217'i} \u8212- the pure one shall sprinkle on the impure person on day three and on day seven \u8212- why does the Torah repeat this specification after already having stated it?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} explains: without this repetition, one might have thought that \u8220"day three\u8221" and \u8220"day seven\u8221" function merely to exclude day two and day six \u8212- that is, one cannot shorten the purification period below three and seven. But one might then have assumed that extending the period would be acceptable: doing the sprinkling on day three and day eight, for instance, would be fine since one is adding days of purification rather than subtracting them. The repetition teaches that there can be no variation \u8212- neither shorter nor longer \u8212- only precisely day three and day seven.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Similarly, another verse \u8212- {\i v\u8217'chiteo bayom hashvi\u8217'i} \u8212- why does it need to specify cleansing on the seventh day after already establishing the requirement? The {\i gemara} explains that without this verse one might have thought the full regimen of day three and day seven is necessary only for {\i kodashim} \u8212- sacred foods \u8212- but that for {\i terumah}, perhaps one day alone would suffice. The additional verse teaches that the full requirement applies uniformly.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This extended explication of the {\i parah adumah} passage is the {\i gemara}\u8217's own, not directly tied to the Rabbi Meir\u8211-Rabbi Chanina ben Gamliel dispute, but it follows naturally from the verse cited in that context. Its presence here reinforces the observation that the {\i gemara} is treating the {\i tnai kaful} debate not merely as a technical legal question but as an entry point into broader questions of how language and conditional structures operate across the full breadth of Torah.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Tnai Kaful Debate (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafot Analysis\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having worked through the {\i sugyah}, the {\i gemara} notes \u8212- and Tosafot presses \u8212- that it is not entirely obvious how these biblical examples relate to the formal dispute about {\i tnai kaful} in halakhic contexts. The {\i gemara} concludes its treatment of the {\i parah adumah} verse and the {\i tnai kaful} debate by returning to Tosafot\u8217's broader concern.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot asks a pointed question ({\i teima}): the verse {\i hu yitchata} \u8212- what does this have to do with the dispute between Rabbi Meir and Rabbi Chanina ben Gamliel? After all, in many places in the Torah it says {\i rachatz bamayim v\u8217'taher} \u8212- he shall wash in water and be purified \u8212- and there is no felt need to add \u8220"and if he does not wash, he will not be purified.\u8221" The act is described as achieving a result; its negative is obviously implied. So how does the {\i parah adumah} verse, which seems similarly descriptive, become relevant to the debate?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot answers: {\i d\u8217'lo dami} \u8212- these are not comparable. When the Torah says {\i rachatz bamayim v\u8217'taher}, it is phrased as a {\i mitzvah}, a command. Since God commanded this act, it is self-evident that failure to perform it means failure to achieve the result. There is no conditional reading possible; this is simply the mechanism by which the thing works. But the verse {\i hu yitchata bayom hashlishi} \u8212- \u8220"he shall purify himself on day three and on day seven, and he will be purified\u8221" \u8212- reads slightly differently. The phrasing, Tosafot observes, is more suggestive of a condition: {\i michdi ketiv hu yitchata \u8212- d\u8217'havei k\u8217'ilu ketiv im yitchata bo yithar} \u8212- it is as though the text said \u8220"if he purifies himself, he will be purified.\u8221" Had it said {\i yitchata v\u8217'yithar} in direct sequence, it would read as a command with a consequence; instead the formulation carries a conditional flavor. And it is precisely this conditional flavor that brings it within the orbit of the Rabbi Meir\u8211-Rabbi Chanina ben Gamliel dispute.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What Tosafot is doing here is distinguishing three categories. The first is the classic {\i tnai} structure: \u8220"if X then Y\u8221" \u8212- an explicit condition where one might ask whether Y obtains even in the absence of X. The second is the descriptive: \u8220"doing this act achieves this result.\u8221" There, obviously, the absence of the act means the result is not achieved; no inferential problem arises. The third is the command: \u8220"you must do X and Y will follow.\u8221" There too, as Tosafot notes, the logic is clear \u8212- it is a {\i mitzvah}, and not performing it obviously forfeits the result.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The middle, descriptive category is where the dispute becomes interesting. When a verse describes what an act accomplishes rather than commanding it or conditioning it, and when its phrasing tilts slightly toward the conditional, that is where Rabbi Meir insists the negative clause must be stated, and Rabbi Chanina ben Gamliel insists it is sufficiently implied. The {\i parah adumah} verse sits in this middle zone, which is why the {\i gemara} brings it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The larger picture that emerges from the full {\i sugyah} is this: Rabbi Meir\u8217's position is not merely a technical rule about the formal structure of {\i tna\u8217'im} in {\i kiddushin} and {\i gittin}. It reflects a sustained commitment to strict inferential discipline \u8212- a refusal to assume that the converse of a statement is implied by the statement itself. That principle has implications across legal acts, biblical narrative, divine promise, and ritual law. And that is precisely why the {\i gemara} ranges so far afield in illustrating it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 At the same time, it is worth noting that there remain substantial unresolved questions about Rabbi Meir\u8217's position: whether it applies as a general linguistic rule or only to formal halakhic {\i tna\u8217'im}; whether other requirements of {\i tna\u8217'im} \u8212- such as {\i tnai kodem l\u8217'ma\u8217'aseh} (the condition preceding the act) or {\i hen kodem la-lav} (the positive clause preceding the negative) \u8212- are accepted by Rabbi Meir, disputed, or simply not addressed in this {\i sugyah}. The {\i gemara} here focuses exclusively on {\i tnai kaful}; the full picture of {\i hilchos tna\u8217'im} remains to be assembled from elsewhere.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Error & Deception\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Unspoken Conditions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} presents a series of cases in which a man betroths a woman under a certain assumption that turns out to be mistaken. He thought she was a {\i kohenet}, she turns out to be a {\i leviya}. He thought she was a {\i leviya}, she is a {\i kohenet}. He thought she was poor and she is wealthy. He thought she was wealthy and she is poor. In all these cases, the ruling is {\i harei zu mekudeshet} \u8212- she is nonetheless betrothed, fully and validly. The reason given is {\i mipnei shelo hit\u8217'atu} \u8212- because she did not deceive him. He deceived himself. The assumption was in his mind, unspoken, and no explicit condition was attached to the {\i kiddushin}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is worth noting a small but telling point in the {\i mishnah}\u8217's language. The categories \u8212- {\i kohenet}, {\i leviya}, wealthy, poor \u8212- are presented symmetrically. One might assume a {\i kohenet} or a {\i leviya} is always preferred, but the {\i mishnah}\u8217's symmetry suggests otherwise: different men have different preferences and different concerns, and neither status is presumed superior to the other.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Devarim Shebaleiv\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The operative principle is {\i devarim she-b\u8217'lev einam devarim} \u8212- matters that remain in the heart, unexpressed, are not halakhically operative. A condition or assumption that is never articulated cannot render the act conditional. Since the man\u8217's belief about her status was never stated as part of the {\i kiddushin} transaction, it has no legal force, and the {\i kiddushin} stands.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Complexities\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah}\u8217's ruling, however, leaves significant questions unaddressed. The {\i mishnah} identifies two distinct elements: the absence of deception, and the absence of articulated conditions. But these can come apart in important ways, and the {\i gemara} does not fully explore the resulting complexity.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Consider a case where there was no explicit verbal deception, but it was perfectly clear from the context \u8212- from conversations with the {\i shadchan}, from statements made earlier in the courtship \u8212- that the man was operating under a specific assumption, and that assumption was objectively central to his decision. Could the absence of formal articulation at the moment of {\i kiddushin} truly be dispositive? Or are there circumstances where the contextual {\i da\u8217'at} of the parties is so clear that treating unexpressed assumptions as legally irrelevant would be a distortion?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Conversely, consider cases of active deception where no formal {\i tnai} was stated. If she told him she was a {\i kohenet} and she knew otherwise, and he relied on that representation, does the absence of an explicit condition at the moment of {\i kiddushin} insulate the transaction from challenge? Or does deception introduce a separate ground for voiding the act, even when the deceived party failed to articulate the relevant fact as a formal condition?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 These questions multiply further. If the deception concerned something the court would deem trivial \u8212- a matter that would not objectively affect a reasonable person\u8217's decision \u8212- does active deception still have legal weight? And what of timing: if a material representation was made at the outset of a courtship but not repeated or formally incorporated into the {\i kiddushin} act itself, how is that treated?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} offers no answers to these questions, and the {\i gemara} passes over them in pursuit of the next topic.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Agunah Issues\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The practical stakes of these questions are not hypothetical. The gap between stated conditions and operative assumptions bears directly on cases of {\i agunah}. Active deception \u8212- a man who misrepresented his employment, his history, or other material facts \u8212- might in principle constitute grounds for challenging the validity of the {\i kiddushin}, but whether that challenge succeeds depends on unresolved questions about the relationship between deception and the formal requirements of {\i tnai}. Similarly, the failure to disclose information that any reasonable person would have considered essential \u8212- whether concerning a history of abuse, or significant undisclosed facts about one\u8217's personal circumstances \u8212- raises the question of whether {\i devarim she-b\u8217'lev} truly governs even where the implicit understanding of both parties, and of society at large, would have been that the marriage was conditional on those facts. The {\i gemara} does not resolve any of this. What is clear is that the principle of {\i devarim she-b\u8217'lev einam devarim} is stated broadly, but its full scope and its interaction with deception, {\i da\u8217'as}, and contextual assumptions remain genuinely open.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Future Conditions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishna Examples\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} continues with a further series of cases, distinct from the error-and-deception cases above. A man says to a woman: you are betrothed to me {\i l\u8217'achar she-esgayer} \u8212- after I convert (he is currently not Jewish); {\i l\u8217'achar she-tisgyeri} \u8212- after you convert; {\i l\u8217'achar she-eshtachrer} \u8212- after I am freed (he is a slave); {\i l\u8217'achar she-tishtachreri} \u8212- after you are freed; {\i l\u8217'achar she-yamus ba\u8217'alech} \u8212- after your husband dies (she is currently married); {\i l\u8217'achar she-tamus achotecha} \u8212- after your sister dies (he is married to her sister); {\i l\u8217'achar she-yachaletz lach yevamech} \u8212- after your {\i yavam} performs {\i chalitzah} with you. In all these cases the ruling is {\i einah mekudeshes} \u8212- she is not betrothed. The reason, as the {\i gemara} will develop, is that these are instances of {\i davar shelo ba le\u8217'olam} \u8212- a thing not yet in existence \u8212- or more precisely, a transaction attempted on a party who is not yet in the legal state required for that transaction to take effect.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Unborn Child\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} adds a further case: {\i ha-omer la-chaveiro im yalda ishtecha nekeivah harei zu mekudeshes li} \u8212- if a man says to his fellow, \u8220"if your wife gives birth to a daughter, she is betrothed to me.\u8221" Here, the father is attempting to betroth a daughter who has not yet been born. This is a genuine {\i davar shelo ba le\u8217'olam}, and the ruling is {\i einah mekudeshes}. The {\i gemara} will note, however, that if the wife is already pregnant, the analysis may differ \u8212- and that qualification, drawn from a {\i baraita}, will be read back into the {\i mishnah} as a limiting condition.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Terumah Analogy\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rather than analyzing the first half of the {\i mishnah} \u8212- the cases of conversion, manumission, death of a spouse, and {\i chalitzah} \u8212- the {\i gemara} pivots almost immediately to the question of {\i davar shelo ba le\u8217'olam} as illuminated by an analogy from the laws of {\i terumah}. The {\i gemara} introduces a teaching: {\i ein tormin min ha-talush al ha-mechubar} \u8212- one may not separate {\i terumah} from harvested produce on behalf of produce still attached to the ground. And {\i bedi\u8217'eved}, if one did so, {\i ein terumato terumah} \u8212- it does not take effect.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} will use this {\i terumah} framework to develop the key principles governing conditional transactions involving things not yet in the necessary state.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Terumah Parallels\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Terumah Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Assi posed a question to Rabbi Yochanan: what if someone said, \u8220"the fruit of this patch \u8212- already harvested \u8212- shall be {\i terumah} on behalf of the fruit of that patch \u8212- still attached to the ground, {\i lichsheyitlashu} \u8212- once they are harvested\u8221"? And then they were in fact harvested. Does it take effect?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The question is whether one can perform an act now \u8212- designating {\i terumah} \u8212- that will take legal effect later, once the object reaches the state required for such an act to be meaningful. This is structurally analogous to the {\i kiddushin} cases in the {\i mishnah}: can a transaction be initiated now on an object or person not yet in the necessary legal state, to take effect when that state is eventually reached?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 There is a curious note in Rashi\u8217's commentary here. Rashi explains the question as asking whether the {\i terumah} designation would apply {\i lemafrea} \u8212- retroactively \u8212- once the produce is harvested. That reading is striking, and it sits uneasily with the rest of the {\i sugyah}, which consistently speaks of the transaction taking effect at the future moment when conditions are met, not retroactively from the time of the original designation. Rashi\u8217's formulation remains puzzling in this context.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kol Shebeyado\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yochanan answered: {\i kol she-beyado lav ke-mechusar ma\u8217'aseh dami} \u8212- anything that is within one\u8217's power to bring about is not regarded as lacking the necessary act. Since the produce belongs to this person and he can simply go and harvest it himself, it is not considered so absent from the world that the designation cannot work. The {\i kol she-beyado} principle thus carves out an exception to the general rule of {\i davar shelo ba le\u8217'olam}: where the necessary transformation is entirely within one\u8217's own control to effect, the transaction may be initiated now to take effect when that transformation occurs.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The principle has implications well beyond the immediate case. A related application \u8212- though not stated in this {\i sugya} itself \u8212- appears in discussions of selling goods one does not yet own: if the goods are readily available on the open market and the seller can trivially acquire them at will, some authorities understand this as a form of {\i kol she-beyado}, sufficient to allow the transaction to proceed, since the seller\u8217's ability to obtain the item is in practice unimpeded.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Geir & Shichrur\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now connects the {\i kol she-beyado} principle to the cases of the {\i mishnah}. Among the cases where the {\i mishnah} rules {\i einah mekudeshes} is the case where a man says, \u8220"you are betrothed to me after I convert.\u8221" One might ask: is conversion not {\i beyado} \u8212- within his control? He can simply choose to convert. If {\i kol she-beyado} makes the {\i terumah} designation effective, should it not similarly make this conditional {\i kiddushin} effective?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Geir Requirement\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} rejects this inference. {\i Ger nami lav beyado} \u8212- conversion is also not within his power in the relevant sense. The {\i gemara} cites Rabbi Chiyya bar Abba in the name of Rabbi Yochanan: {\i ger shetzarich shlosha} \u8212- a convert requires a {\i beit din} of three. The source is the verse {\i mishpat echad yihyeh lachem ka-ger ka-ezrach ha-aretz} \u8212- there shall be one law for you, for the convert as for the native-born \u8212- and since {\i mishpat} implies a judicial proceeding, conversion requires a formal court of three. Consequently, {\i mi yeimar de-mizdakku leh hani tlata} \u8212- who can guarantee that three judges will be convened and available? The outcome depends on others, not solely on the would-be convert. It is therefore not {\i beyado} in the sense the {\i kol she-beyado} principle requires.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara}\u8217's formulation here is deliberately neutral: it does not suggest that the {\i beit din} might reject the convert\u8217's sincerity or impose difficult requirements; it simply notes that convening a {\i beit din} involves other people, and is therefore not fully within one person\u8217's unilateral control.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Slave Woman Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Freed Slave\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Abba bar Memel challenges the {\i kol she-beyado} principle with a more pointed case. What if a master gives a {\i prutah} to his female slave \u8212- a slave he can free \u8212- and says to her: \u8220"you are betrothed to me after I free you\u8221"? Freeing her is entirely within his power; it is a unilateral act requiring no third party. If {\i kol she-beyado} is the operative principle, this should work, and the betrothal should take effect upon manumission. The {\i gemara} asks: {\i hachi nami de-havei kiddushin} \u8212- would it indeed be effective?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 One vs.\u160 ?Two-Sided\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} rejects the challenge. {\i Hachi hashta} \u8212- how can you compare the cases? The {\i terumah} case involves a unilateral designation; there is no other party whose participation is required. But {\i kiddushin} is a bilateral act \u8212- it requires not only the man\u8217's action but the woman\u8217's willing acceptance. The question then becomes: even if the master can unilaterally free the slave, can he unilaterally {\i mekadesh} her? {\i Beyado l\u8217'garshah} \u8212- yes, he can divorce her unilaterally; but {\i beyado l\u8217'kadshah} \u8212- can he marry her unilaterally? He cannot. {\i Kiddushin} requires her agreement, and her future self \u8212- the freed woman \u8212- is not yet a party to this transaction in the way required.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This point appears to have broad implications. If the {\i kol she-beyado} principle applies only to unilateral acts, then nearly all the {\i kiddushin} cases in the {\i mishnah} would be excluded from its scope by definition, since {\i kiddushin} is always bilateral. The principle retains its force in the {\i terumah} context and in analogous unilateral acts such as {\i get}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Behemah Problem\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} offers a second, deeper reason why the slave woman case fails. {\i Hatam meikara behemah hashta da\u8217'at acheret} \u8212- there, originally she was like an animal, and now she becomes a separate, autonomous person. This language is deeply troubling, and it requires careful interpretation. The {\i gemara} seems to be making a legal-philosophical point, not a statement about the slave woman\u8217's moral worth: as a slave, she is not a legally cognizable party to a {\i kiddushin} transaction at all. The transformation from slave to free person is not a modification of an already-present legal subject; it is the creation of an entirely new legal subject. This is not a matter of a condition not yet being met while the essential components are present \u8212- it is a case where one of the essential components of the transaction does not exist at all in the relevant legal sense.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The distinction being drawn resembles what classical philosophy would call the difference between essential and accidental attributes. A piece of fruit that has not yet ripened is still fruit; ripeness is something that will naturally accrue to it. But a slave, in the legal framework of the {\i gemara}, does not yet possess the legal personhood required to be a party to {\i kiddushin}. The transformation is total, not incremental. This, not merely the bilateral nature of {\i kiddushin}, is why the case fails even on the {\i kol she-beyado} analysis.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Divorce Condition\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two Rings\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Oshaya posed the following case: a man gives his wife two {\i prutot} \u8212- two rings. With the first he says {\i hitkadshi li hayom} \u8212- be betrothed to me today. With the second he says {\i hitkadshi li l\u8217'achar she-agareshech} \u8212- be betrothed to me after I divorce you. The first {\i kiddushin} is presumably valid; the question is whether the second takes effect upon divorce. The {\i gemara} notes that Rabbi Oshaya left this as an open question.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Why It Works\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} analyzes whether the principle developed above \u8212- that {\i kol she-beyado} in a unilateral act can suffice \u8212- resolves Rabbi Oshaya\u8217's question. The key observation is this: at the moment the man gives both rings, the woman is single. She is currently eligible to receive {\i kiddushin}. The first ring effects a valid {\i kiddushin} immediately. As for the second ring: since the man is giving it to a woman who is, at this moment, in the state where she can receive {\i kiddushin}, the act of handing the ring is not inherently void. He is saying: this ring\u8217's effect is to be postponed until after I divorce you. The components for {\i kiddushin} are present now \u8212- a single woman, a willing man, the {\i kesef} \u8212- and he is simply specifying a future time of effect.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Why It Fails\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But the {\i gemara} questions whether this actually resolves Rabbi Oshaya\u8217's question or simply pushes it forward. The apparent resolution depends on a distinction: here, the woman is currently single, so there is a valid platform for {\i kiddushin} to occur. Compare this to the case where a man says to his {\i wife} \u8212- while she is already married to him \u8212- \u8220"be betrothed to me after I divorce you.\u8221" In that case the act is categorically void: one cannot perform an act of {\i kiddushin} on a woman who is currently married, even to oneself. There is no valid platform for the act, and the fact that divorce is {\i beyado} cannot save it, because {\i kiddushin} also requires her agreement, and therefore is not purely {\i beyado}. The distinction thus stands: when the woman is single at the time the rings are given, the act has a valid foundation and can be directed at a future moment; when she is already married, no valid foundation exists.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Natural Events\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Grain Ripening\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having established the {\i kol she-beyado} principle and its limits, the {\i gemara} records an extension by Rabbi Elazar ben Yaakov that goes significantly further. The earlier ruling was that one cannot separate {\i terumah} from harvested produce on behalf of unharvested produce, but that saying {\i lichsheyitlashu} \u8212- once they are harvested \u8212- and having them subsequently harvested does work, because harvesting is {\i beyado}. Rabbi Elazar ben Yaakov adds: {\i afilu} \u8212- even if you did not say \u8220"once they are harvested\u8221" but only \u8220"once they reach a third of their ripeness\u8221" \u8212- {\i lichsheyaviu shelish} \u8212- and they subsequently ripened and were harvested, {\i devarav kayamin} \u8212- it takes effect.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is a significant extension. Ripening is not {\i beyado} \u8212- the owner cannot cause the grain to ripen by an act of will. Ripening is a natural process, dependent on sun, water, and time. Yet Rabbi Elazar ben Yaakov holds that this is sufficient. There are, it seems, degrees of {\i lo ba le\u8217'olam}. The most attenuated form \u8212- a future event that depends on nature running its normal course \u8212- is still sufficient to anchor a halakhic designation, as long as the natural outcome can be expected with reasonable confidence.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Standing Grain\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava qualifies Rabbi Elazar ben Yaakov\u8217's ruling: {\i lo amar Rabbi Elazar ben Yaakov ela be-shachas} \u8212- Rabbi Elazar ben Yaakov said this only regarding {\i shachas} \u8212- grain that is already standing, at the stage where it could be cut for fodder. At that stage, the grain is visibly present, physically substantial, and its ripening is the expected natural outcome of what is already clearly a developed plant. But {\i aval agam} \u8212- if the grain is still at the {\i agam} stage, the very early shoot stage \u8212- Rava holds it would not work. At that stage the thing is barely present at all, and the eventual outcome is too remote to anchor a halakhic act.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Young Shoots\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Yosef disagrees: {\i afilu ba-agam} \u8212- even at the earliest shoot stage, Rabbi Elazar ben Yaakov would hold the designation works. The {\i gemara} clarifies the meaning of {\i agam} by invoking the verse {\i ha-lachof k\u8217'agmon rosho} \u8212- will you bow your head like an {\i agmon}? \u8212- referring to a plant that droops or leans, like a young tender shoot. For Rav Yosef, even at that stage, as long as the natural outcome can be expected, the act is valid.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Both Rava and Rav Yosef agree, at a minimum, that it must be standing grain \u8212- {\i shachas} at minimum for Rava, even {\i agam} for Rav Yosef \u8212- and both are reading Rabbi Elazar ben Yaakov as extending beyond the {\i kol she-beyado} principle into a distinct category: natural, non-controllable processes that reliably produce a known outcome.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Pregnancy Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Unborn Daughter\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now applies this framework to the case at the end of the {\i mishnah}: a man who says to his fellow, \u8220"if your wife gives birth to a daughter, she is betrothed to me.\u8221" The ruling is {\i lo amar klum} \u8212- it has no effect. But Rabbi Chanina qualifies: {\i lo shanu ela she-ein ishto me\u8217'uberes} \u8212- this ruling applies only when the wife is not pregnant. But {\i aval ishto me\u8217'uberes} \u8212- if the wife is in fact pregnant \u8212- {\i devarav kayamin} \u8212- it takes effect.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The reasoning is clear in light of what has just been established. If the wife is pregnant, the birth of a daughter is the natural expected outcome \u8212- not something within anyone\u8217's control, but a natural process that, if it proceeds normally, will result in the existence of the very child being designated. This is structurally analogous to Rabbi Elazar ben Yaakov\u8217's case: the grain is not yet at the stage where it can be designated {\i terumah}, but because it will naturally reach that stage, the designation is effective.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Pregnancy Case (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Visible Pregnancy\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now debates whether Rabbi Chanina\u8217's ruling \u8212- that pregnancy suffices to make the betrothal of an unborn daughter effective \u8212- requires that the pregnancy be {\i hukar ubra} \u8212- visibly noticeable \u8212- or whether even a hidden, early pregnancy would suffice.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Following Rava\u8217's position on grain, one would say: just as standing grain ({\i shachas}) is required \u8212- the grain must be visibly present in a substantial form \u8212- so too the pregnancy must be {\i hukar ubra}, recognizable to the eye, for the betrothal to take effect. An early, invisible pregnancy is analogous to the {\i agam} stage \u8212- barely present, too remote from the expected outcome to anchor the transaction.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Following Rav Yosef, even an early, unrecognizable pregnancy might suffice, since the principle is simply whether the natural outcome can be expected to occur, not whether the thing is already visibly substantial.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Natural Expectation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} frames the positions accordingly: {\i im ke-Rava}, according to Rava, {\i keshehukkar ubra} \u8212- the pregnancy must be noticeable. {\i U-le-Rav Yosef}, according to Rav Yosef, {\i af al pi shelo hukar ubra} \u8212- even if the pregnancy is not yet recognizable, the betrothal takes effect, because the natural process, once initiated, is expected to reach its conclusion.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The underlying principle is thus the following hierarchy. First: a transaction on something entirely absent from the world, with no expected natural path to its existence, cannot take effect \u8212- this is the core of {\i davar shelo ba le\u8217'olam}. Second: if the thing can be brought into being by the person\u8217's own unilateral act, that is sufficient \u8212- this is {\i kol she-beyado}. Third: if the thing will come into being through a natural, expected process even without the person\u8217's intervention, that too may suffice \u8212- this is the extension of Rabbi Elazar ben Yaakov. And within this third category, there are degrees: how present must the thing already be for the expected outcome to be close enough to reality?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Certainty Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara}\u8217's final discussion raises the underlying question most sharply. The debate about visible versus invisible pregnancy \u8212- and about standing grain versus young shoots \u8212- is not purely technical. It reflects a deeper question about what we mean by \u8220"expected\u8221" and \u8220"natural\u8221" in halakhic reasoning: is the relevant standard what we know statistically or scientifically, or is it what we experience intuitively as certain? How confident must we be that the natural outcome will occur, and is that confidence assessed by objective probabilities or by experiential certainty?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is genuinely unresolved. The {\i machloket} between Rava and Rav Yosef may not simply be about the threshold of physical development required \u8212- it may also reflect different intuitions about how we assess the reliability of natural processes and how far we are willing to extend the logic of natural expectation into the halakhic domain.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Natural Events (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Field Types\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} closes with one further distinction within Rava\u8217's position. Rava had said that Rabbi Elazar ben Yaakov\u8217's ruling requires {\i shachas} \u8212- standing grain. But even among cases of standing grain, there is a further variable: is the field {\i beit ha-shelahim} \u8212- irrigated \u8212- or {\i beit ha-ba\u8217'al} \u8212- dependent on rainfall alone?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava holds that Rabbi Elazar ben Yaakov\u8217's leniency applies only to {\i shachas de-bei karkha} \u8212- grain from a rainfed field \u8212- because in such a field, the grain grows and ripens naturally as a matter of course, without any active human intervention required beyond what has already occurred. But {\i shachas de-bei shakya} \u8212- grain from an irrigated field \u8212- would not qualify according to Rava, because ripening there is contingent on continued human action: someone must continue to irrigate. The outcome is therefore more tentative, more dependent on choices yet to be made, and less assured as a natural consequence of what already exists.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Yosef, again the more expansive voice, holds {\i afilu be-shachas de-bei shakya} \u8212- even irrigated-field grain qualifies, because practically speaking, the irrigation will continue and the natural outcome will be reached. The theoretical contingency does not undermine the practical expectation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Both Rava and Rav Yosef agree, in this second version of the dispute, that the grain must at minimum be at the {\i shachas} stage. The disagreement is only about the degree of confidence required in the ultimate natural outcome \u8212- and whether that confidence is affected by the grain\u8217's dependence on continued irrigation. The range of the {\i machloket}, then, is not so much about the physical state of the grain as about the epistemology of natural expectation: what degree of certainty, and what kind of certainty, is sufficient to allow a halakhic act to reach across the present moment into a future whose occurrence is natural but not guaranteed.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Kiddushin 63\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Davar Shelo Ba L\u8217'olam\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Three Categories\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The sugya on this daf continues a discussion that began at the bottom of 62b, concerning the principle of {\i davar shelo ba l\u8217'olam} \u8212- a legal act performed in relation to something that has not yet come into existence. The preceding mishna established that if a man says to a woman, \u8220"You are married to me after you are divorced,\u8221" or \u8220"after your husband dies,\u8221" or \u8220"after I convert\u8221" or \u8220"after you convert,\u8221" the {\i kiddushin} does not take effect. The {\i gemara}, however, has been exploring whether there are circumstances in which such an act might nonetheless be valid \u8212- particularly where the missing component is {\i b\u8217'yado}, within the person\u8217's control, or where its coming into being is naturally anticipated.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is useful to distinguish three categories of {\i davar shelo ba l\u8217'olam}. The first is something not yet present that the person himself can bring about \u8212- {\i b\u8217'yado} \u8212- where even under normal rules some authorities allow the transaction to work. The second is something that will naturally occur if circumstances proceed as expected: grain will ripen, a pregnant woman will give birth. Though not {\i b\u8217'yado}, these are reasonably anticipated. The third and most extreme category encompasses things whose occurrence is genuinely uncertain and unpredictable \u8212- being freed from slavery, a husband dying, obtaining {\i chalitzah} from a {\i yavam}. These represent the hardest cases of {\i davar shelo ba l\u8217'olam}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Eliezer b. Yaakov\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} had been focused on the position of Rabbi Eliezer ben Yaakov, who recognizes validity even in the middle category \u8212- things that will happen naturally and as anticipated, though not within the actor\u8217's direct control. This position now serves as the launching point for Abaye\u8217's broader observation about the landscape of {\i tannaitic} opinion on the subject.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Three Tannaim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Abaye\u8217's Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Four lines from the bottom of 62b, the {\i gemara} records: {\i Amar Abaye} \u8212- Rabbi Eliezer ben Yaakov, Rabbi Meir, and Rabbi ({\i Yehuda HaNasi}) \u8212- {\i kulhu sevira lehu adam makneh davar shelo ba l\u8217'olam}, all three share the view that a person can transfer ownership or effect a legal transaction with respect to something or someone not yet in existence. Abaye characteristically identifies {\i tannaim} who, though not in identical cases, share a common legal disposition, and groups them together. The three positions are not identical, but they reflect a shared underlying approach.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Eliezer ben Yaakov\u8217's case has just been discussed: fruit not yet ripened, not {\i b\u8217'yado}, but naturally expected to come about. That is the most accessible form of {\i davar shelo ba l\u8217'olam}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi & Slave\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What is Rabbi\u8217's position? The {\i gemara} cites a {\i braita} (top of 63a): {\i lo tashgir eved el adonav} \u8212- you shall not hand over a slave to his master. Rabbi interprets this verse as referring to one who purchases a slave with the stipulation that he will free him. If the master then changes his mind, the community \u8212- the {\i beit din} \u8212- cannot return the slave to his master. The community must enforce and protect the slave\u8217's freedom.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Freeing Slave\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} asks: {\i heichi dami} \u8212- what exactly is the case? Rav Dimi bar Yitzchak explains: it is a case where the purchaser wrote to the slave before acquiring him, {\i lech-she-ekkachecha harei atzmecha knuya lach me-achshav} \u8212- \u8220"when I acquire you, you are hereby freed, as of now.\u8221" The transaction of liberation was performed before the purchaser actually owned the slave. Moreover, the outcome was not {\i b\u8217'yado} in any full sense \u8212- the seller was not necessarily going to complete the sale \u8212- and yet the act of liberation takes effect.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is precisely what Abaye uses to demonstrate Rabbi\u8217's position: the fact that Rabbi is willing to read the verse this way, choosing to understand {\i lo tashgir} as enforcing an actual, effective transaction, shows that Rabbi presupposes the legal validity of such an act. The {\i gemara} clarifies: it cannot be that the purchaser merely promised to free the slave, because a moral promise is not legally binding under {\i halacha}, and the Torah\u8217's command would then be protecting something legally insubstantial. Nor can it be that the master freed the slave outright and simply changed his mind, because the verse says \u8220"do not return the slave to his master\u8221" \u8212- implying that in some sense he remains a slave at the time the verse speaks. The only coherent reading, therefore, is that the act of liberation was executed against a not-yet-existing ownership, and the Torah commands us to honor it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Me\u8217'achshav\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot raises an important question about the word {\i me-achshav} \u8212- \u8220"as of now.\u8221" Two difficulties present themselves. First, all other cases of {\i davar shelo ba l\u8217'olam} in the {\i sugya} do not use this language; if anything, they use formulations pointing toward the future, such as {\i lich-she-yitlashu} \u8212- when they are picked. Second, logically, how can an act take effect {\i now} when the necessary components are not yet in place? The slave is not yet owned; a woman being offered {\i kiddushin} is still married to another man.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot offers two explanations. One is that {\i me-achshav} carries no independent legal force; it is simply a clarification of Rabbi\u8217's intent rather than a technically operative term. The other, more developed explanation draws on a general principle that when an act is intended to take effect in the future, something of that act \u8212- some remnant or trace \u8212- must still be operative at the moment it is supposed to take effect. So if one says \u8220"this item will be yours in thirty days,\u8221" there must be some continuing connection between the transaction and the object at the time it activates. Tosafot suggests that {\i me-achshav} serves to give the act of liberation sufficient force and permanence that it does not require such a remnant later. Even if the {\i shtar} of liberation were subsequently lost or torn, the act would still take effect \u8212- because the word {\i me-achshav} establishes it with the full force of a present act, even though it cannot literally take present effect.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This does not mean the {\i shtar} need not be given at all. The entire mechanism of {\i davar shelo ba l\u8217'olam} requires that the transactional instrument \u8212- the ring, the {\i shtar} \u8212- be given now, to a slave who belongs to another, or to a woman still married to another man. That is precisely the striking feature of the principle: you give the {\i shtar} to a slave owned by someone else, and it takes effect once the necessary circumstances align. But without {\i me-achshav}, the recipient would need to retain the {\i shtar} for the transaction to remain viable. With {\i me-achshav}, the act is sufficiently anchored that even if the {\i shtar} is lost, the liberation holds.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rabbi Meir Extreme\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Extreme Conditions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Meir is classically associated with the principle of {\i davar shelo ba l\u8217'olam}. The {\i gemara} cites a {\i braita}: {\i ha\u8217'omer l\u8217'isha harei at mekudeshet li l\u8217'achar she\u8217'isgayer l\u8217'achar she\u8217'tisgayri l\u8217'achar she\u8217'ishtachrer l\u8217'achar she\u8217'tishtachrari} \u8212- if he says to a woman, \u8220"You are married to me after I convert, after you convert, after I am freed from slavery, after you are freed from slavery.\u8221" None of these are {\i b\u8217'yado}, none are naturally anticipated occurrences. The {\i braita} continues: {\i l\u8217'achar sheyamus ba\u8217'aleich l\u8217'achar shetamus achoseich} \u8212- after your husband dies, after your sister dies (the latter presumably relevant where he had once been married to the sister). {\i L\u8217'achar sheyachalotz l\u8217'yevameich} \u8212- after your {\i yavam} performs {\i chalitzah}, a provision that itself presumes a {\i yevamah} cannot receive {\i kiddushin} while still awaiting {\i yibbum} or {\i chalitzah}, contrary to the way we rule.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Meir Dispute\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Eina mekudeshet} \u8212- the {\i tanna kamma} rules that none of this works. {\i Rabbi Meir omer mekudeshet} \u8212- Rabbi Meir says she is betrothed. This is a remarkable position. It is striking enough that the necessary components are entirely absent and their arrival uncertain. But consider further: a non-Jew proposing {\i kiddushin} is not even a legal participant in the institution of {\i kiddushin}, which is exclusively a Jewish framework. The institution of marriage recognized for non-Jews is a different legal structure entirely. Yet Rabbi Meir maintains that such an act is nonetheless legally operative. Similarly, effecting {\i kiddushin} to a woman who is still an {\i eishes ish} \u8212- still married to another man \u8212- goes to the very heart of what {\i kiddushin} is designed to establish. That these extreme cases should nonetheless work under the principle of {\i davar shelo ba l\u8217'olam} is, to say the least, difficult to absorb.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Afkeinhu Rabanan\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Yehuda HaNasi omer mekudeshet} \u8212- Rabbi (Yehuda HaNasi) agrees with Rabbi Meir: the {\i kiddushin} works in principle. However, the {\i gemara} asks: {\i uma ta\u8217'am amru eina mekudeshet} \u8212- why then did the Sages say it does not take effect? The answer is {\i mishum eivah} \u8212- because of enmity. Rashi explains this in reference to two of the cases: betrothal conditioned on the woman\u8217's husband dying, or on her sister dying. Recognizing such {\i kiddushin} would create deeply dangerous social tensions \u8212- a man waiting for, or perhaps arranging, the death of a husband or a sister-in-law. These cases therefore cannot be recognized, even if technically valid, as a matter of social policy. This reflects the principle of {\i afkinhu rabanan l\u8217'kiddushin} \u8212- the Sages retroactively annul the {\i kiddushin}. Rabbi agrees with Rabbi Meir as a matter of law, and departs only on grounds of social necessity in these two specific instances.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then asks: since Rabbi Yehuda HaNasi agrees with Rabbi Meir, why not add him to Abaye\u8217's list alongside Rabbi Meir? The {\i gemara} answers: {\i hainu Rabbi hainu Rabbi Yehuda HaNasi} \u8212- they are the same person; Rabbi was already cited in connection with the slave case above.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rabbi Akiva\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Woman\u8217's Vow\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} suggests adding Rabbi Akiva to the list of {\i tannaim} who accept {\i davar shelo ba l\u8217'olam}. A {\i braita} is cited: a woman declares {\i konam she\u8217'ani oseh le\u8217'picha} \u8212- a {\i konam} vow, functioning like {\i hekdesh}, forbidding her husband any benefit from her labor. Under the laws of the {\i ketubah}, a husband holds rights to his wife\u8217's labor; she is not generally in a position to sever those rights through a vow. {\i Rabbi Akiva omer yafer} \u8212- Rabbi Akiva rules that the husband should nonetheless annul the vow. Why? {\i Shema ta\u8217'adif alav yoter min ha\u8217'ra\u8217'uy lo} \u8212- perhaps she will produce more than the amount to which he is entitled. The {\i ketubah} obligates a certain measure of labor; any excess belongs to her. If she vows to forbid everything she produces, the vow might take hold over that excess which is genuinely hers to dispose of, and the husband\u8217's benefit from what he is entitled to might thereby be compromised, since one cannot easily distinguish which labor is the obligated portion and which is the surplus. Therefore, the husband should annul the vow to prevent it from affecting the extra that is hers.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot asks: why can the husband annul in the first place? The mechanism of a husband annulling a wife\u8217's vow applies to vows that affect the marital relationship. But if the surplus labor is entirely her own property with no marital obligation attached, why would her vow over it be subject to his annulment at all? Tosafot answers that in practice one cannot precisely calculate whether a given moment of labor is within the obligated portion or beyond it. The presence of the vow therefore intrudes on his access even to what he is entitled to, since it cannot be determined in real time whether any given unit of output falls on one side of the line or the other. On this basis, even the surplus is considered to fall within the ambit of the marital relationship, and the husband can annul.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hands Exist\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then raises a challenge to the very identification of Rabbi Akiva\u8217's position with {\i davar shelo ba l\u8217'olam}. The labor has not yet been performed; how can a vow forbid something that does not yet exist? {\i Ha itmar alah amar Rav Huna breih de\u8217'Rav Yehoshua} \u8212- it was already stated regarding this: {\i b\u8217'omeres yikdeshu yadai l\u8217'oseihen} \u8212- the vow is formulated as \u8220"may my hands be consecrated for what they produce.\u8221" The {\i neder} is not imposed on the as-yet-unproduced labor, but on the hands themselves \u8212- the hands as instruments for their eventual output. {\i Ve\u8217'yadayim itnhu b\u8217'olam} \u8212- and the hands are already in the world. There is therefore no {\i davar shelo ba l\u8217'olam}; the subject of the vow exists right now.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This prompts a further reflection: if one were to declare forbidden to oneself the fruit of a particular tree before those fruit have grown, would that be a {\i davar shelo ba l\u8217'olam}? The more apt formulation \u8212- and one that would avoid the problem \u8212- would be to direct the vow at the tree itself, in terms of its capacity to produce fruit, much as the woman directs the vow at her hands rather than at the as-yet-nonexistent produce. Such a vow would take hold of an existing entity. Whether a vow formulated over an entire class of objects, including those not yet in existence, raises similar questions, is left as an open matter.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Father\u8217's Approval\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Service Condition\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now turns to a new mishna. {\i Ha\u8217'omer l\u8217'isha harei at mekudeshet li al menat she\u8217'adaber alayich lashilton} \u8212- if a man says to a woman, \u8220"You are hereby married to me on the condition that I speak to the governor on your behalf,\u8221" or {\i ve\u8217'e\u8217'eseh imach k\u8217'poel} \u8212- \u8220"on the condition that I perform a day\u8217's labor for you.\u8221" If he followed through \u8212- {\i diber aleha lashilton} or {\i asah imah k\u8217'poel} \u8212- she is betrothed; if not, she is not. The mishna appears straightforward. Resh Lakish comments: {\i v\u8217'hu she\u8217'natan lah shaveh pruta} \u8212- and this applies only where he also gave her the value of a {\i pruta}. In other words, Resh Lakish reads the mishna as follows: the act of {\i kiddushin} was performed in the normal way \u8212- he gave her a ring or coin \u8212- and the service functions only as a {\i tnai}, a condition, not as the {\i kessef kiddushin} itself.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Fee for Service\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} challenges this: {\i u\u8217'vischar lo} \u8212- are you saying one cannot betroth a woman with the fee for a service? The {\i gemara} cites a {\i braita}: {\i bischar she\u8217'hirkavtich al hachamor} \u8212- with the fee for the donkey ride I gave you, or the wagon ride, or the ferry ride \u8212- {\i eina mekudeshet}. That does not work because the service was already rendered; the woman owes him money, and betrothing with that debt is {\i mekadesh b\u8217'milveh} \u8212- betrothing with a loan \u8212- which is ineffective since the money is already hers. But: {\i bischar she\u8217'oshivich al hachamor} \u8212- with the fee for the donkey ride I {\i will} give you \u8212- {\i mekudeshet}. He performs the service, she receives value that was not yet owed, and that value constitutes the {\i kesef kiddushin}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A second {\i braita} reinforces this: if a woman says to a man, {\i shevi imi b\u8217'tzavta ve\u8217'ekadesh lach} \u8212- sit with me and keep me company, and I will be betrothed to you for that \u8212- or {\i sechok lefanai}, perform a comedy routine; {\i rekod lefanai}, do a dance; {\i aseh kidmus hazeh}, construct some structure \u8212- {\i shamin}, one assesses the value. If the service is worth a {\i pruta}, she is betrothed; if not, she is not. This is striking also because the formulation here has the woman initiating the transaction \u8212- there is no statement of {\i harei at mekudeshet li} from the man; she herself declares {\i ekadesh lach}, and the betrothal follows from that.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} presses: perhaps in these {\i braitot} too, he had given her a ring and the service was merely a condition? The {\i gemara} dismisses this. In the first case, the language is explicitly {\i bischar} \u8212- with the fee. In the second, the {\i braita} says {\i shamin}, they assess the value \u8212- clearly the {\i kiddushin} is effected through the service itself and nothing else.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tiyuvta deResh Lakish} \u8212- this is a refutation of Resh Lakish\u8217's reading. But Resh Lakish deflects: the {\i tanna} of those {\i braitot} holds {\i einah lisechirut ela levasof} \u8212- the obligation for labor or service accrues only at the conclusion of the work. Under this view, when the service is complete, a discrete value exists that can be given as a gift to the woman and constitutes {\i kessef kiddushin}. But the {\i tanna} of our mishna holds {\i yeshnah lisechirut mitechilah ve\u8217'ad sof} \u8212- the obligation accrues incrementally from the very beginning of the service through its completion.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Accrual vs.\u160 ?End\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Under the view that payment accrues from beginning to end, every infinitesimal increment of service immediately generates a corresponding infinitesimal fraction of a {\i pruta} of obligation. No completed service of a {\i pruta}\u8217's worth is ever given as a unified act; instead, the value is parceled out across the duration in fractions too small to constitute {\i kessef kiddushin} at any given moment. Each fraction, as it accrues, immediately becomes a debt \u8212- {\i milveh} \u8212- and cannot then function as {\i kessef kiddushin}. The cumulative amount never coheres into a single transferable gift of a {\i pruta} because by the time each increment exists, it has already become her property as a debt.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This leads to the {\i gemara}\u8217's further question: {\i u\u8217'mai dukhkheih d\u8217'Resh Lakish} \u8212- what compelled Resh Lakish to read the mishna this way at all? Why not simply read the mishna as a case where the service is the {\i kessef kiddushin}? Rava answers: {\i matnisin kashya lei} \u8212- Resh Lakish was bothered by the plain language of the mishna. The mishna says {\i al menat she\u8217'adaber alayich lashilton} \u8212- on the condition that I speak to the governor. If the service were the {\i kessef kiddushin}, the mishna should have said \u8220"with the fee for my speaking to the governor.\u8221" The use of {\i al menat}, \u8220"on the condition,\u8221" is the consistent formulation throughout the preceding mishnayot wherever a condition is added to an independent act of {\i kiddushin}. Resh Lakish simply reads the {\i pshat} of the mishna: the service is a condition; the {\i kessef kiddushin} was given separately.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Silence Interpretation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The mishna continues: {\i al menat sheyirtze abba} \u8212- on the condition that Father agrees. If the father agrees, she is betrothed; if not, she is not. If the father dies, she is betrothed. If the son (the groom) dies, one instructs the father to say he did not consent, so that the girl will not be left as a widow \u8212- for her situation is far preferable if the {\i kiddushin} never took effect than if she is now an {\i almanah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} asks what {\i sheyirtze abba} \u8212- \u8220"that Father agrees\u8221" \u8212- actually means. If it means that the father must verbally affirm his consent, then in the case where the father dies without speaking, why is she betrothed? He never said yes. This forces a reinterpretation: the condition must mean {\i sheyishtok abba} \u8212- that the father is silent, that he does not object. If he dies, he never objected, and the {\i kiddushin} takes effect.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But now the {\i gemara} faces a difficulty with the end of the mishna. If silence constitutes acceptance, then when the son dies and the father is momentarily silent before we instruct him, has he not already implicitly accepted? Why can we still instruct him to object? The {\i gemara} therefore recasts the condition again: {\i al menat shelo yimche abba} \u8212- not that he is silent for a moment, but that he never actively objects. Silence alone does not seal the matter; only active objection terminates it. Under this reading: if the father verbally accepts, the {\i kiddushin} is firmly established \u8212- though one might think that if the condition is only \u8220"as long as he doesn\u8217't object,\u8221" he could theoretically object even after verbal acceptance. This is precisely what troubles the {\i gemara} about the {\i reisha}: how can verbal consent make the {\i kiddushin} definitively binding if the real condition is simply non-objection?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Yannai\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Yannai resolves the apparent inconsistency between the beginning and end of the mishna by positing that the two clauses describe different formulations. In the {\i reisha}, where verbal agreement renders her betrothed, the groom used the language {\i sheyishtok abba} \u8212- on the condition that the father is silent. Under that formulation, a moment of silence following the father\u8217's learning of the marriage seals the {\i kiddushin}. In the {\i seifa}, where active objection is what terminates it, the groom used the language {\i shelo yimche abba} \u8212- on the condition that the father not object. Under that formulation, verbal acceptance does not definitively close the matter; the condition remains open until the father actively objects or the timeframe expires.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Resh Lakish draws a methodological inference from Rav Yannai\u8217's willingness to read the beginning and end of the mishna as different scenarios: {\i dachkinan umokminan matnitin bitrei ta\u8217'amei ve\u8217'aliba dechad tana} \u8212- we would rather strain the mishna to present two different circumstances under one {\i tanna}\u8217's opinion than posit a hidden dispute between two {\i tannaim} expressed within a single anonymous mishna.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Thirty Days\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Yosef bar Ami proposes a simpler resolution: {\i le\u8217'olam chada ta\u8217'ama hu} \u8212- it is actually a single scenario throughout. The groom\u8217's formulation was {\i al menat shelo yimche abba mikan ve\u8217'ad shloshim yom} \u8212- on the condition that the father does not object within the next thirty days. A time limit resolves every difficulty. In the {\i reisha}, when the father verbally affirms consent, the {\i kiddushin} is established because no further objection is possible \u8212- or at minimum, the condition has been resolved favorably. The middle case, where the father dies: even within the thirty-day window, his death ensures he will never object before it expires, so she is immediately betrothed. The final case, where the son dies: the thirty-day clock is still running, and one may still instruct the father to object within that window, thereby preventing the {\i kiddushin} from taking effect.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Father\u8217's Ignorance\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Father Believed\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The mishna proceeds: {\i kidashti et biti ve\u8217'eini yodei lemi kidashtiya} \u8212- I married off my daughter, and I do not know to whom I married her. The underlying premise of this mishna \u8212- left implicit but relied upon by the {\i gemara} \u8212- is that a father is believed to claim that he married off his daughter, even without producing witnesses. This is derived from the {\i pesukim} about the father who says {\i et biti natati la\u8217'ish hazeh} \u8212- I gave my daughter to this man. Despite the fact that {\i kiddushin} normally requires witnesses, the father\u8217's testimony is credited by virtue of this special {\i ne\u8217'emanut}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If the father cannot identify the groom, the daughter is in a dire situation: she may not remarry since she might be an {\i eishes ish}. The mishna therefore says: if one man comes forward and says {\i ani kidashtiya} \u8212- I was the one who betrothed her \u8212- {\i ne\u8217'eman}, he is believed. This raises its own question: the Torah granted the father special {\i ne\u8217'emanut}; on what basis does a random claimant receive credibility?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Single Claimant\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav rules: {\i ne\u8217'eman liten get ve\u8217'eino ne\u8217'eman liknos} \u8212- the claimant is believed to give her a {\i get} and thereby free her to marry someone else, but he is not believed to marry her himself. The distinction rests on {\i ein adam chotei velo lo} \u8212- a person does not sin without personal benefit. If the man is lying, and we prohibit him from marrying her, he gains nothing. The only beneficiary of his false testimony would be some future husband, who might be committing adultery without knowing it. The claimant has no personal gain, and therefore we credit him. But {\i eino ne\u8217'eman liknos} \u8212- he cannot marry her himself \u8212- because {\i shema yitzro takpo}: if he has a vested personal interest, his {\i yetzer hara} may overpower him, and people do sometimes lie even about grave sins when they stand to benefit.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two Claimants\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Ashi disagrees: {\i af ne\u8217'eman liknos} \u8212- he is even believed to marry her. Two considerations support this: first, the sin would be severe and falls on himself; second, he would be afraid of being exposed as a liar, since the father might look at him and say the groom was of a different appearance entirely, or there may be other corroborating details that would give him away.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The mishna\u8217's case of two claimants: {\i shneihem notnim get} \u8212- both must give a {\i get}, since they cannot both be the husband, and one of them is certainly lying; to allow either to remain with her risks adultery. {\i Ve\u8217'im ratzu echad noten get ve\u8217'echad kones} \u8212- alternatively, if they choose, one may give a {\i get} and the other may marry her, on the grounds of {\i mimah nafshach}: whichever one is the true husband, the woman is covered. If the one who stays with her is the true husband, all is well; if it was the other, she is at least divorced from him first. The {\i gemara} notes this appears to contradict Rav, who would not allow the claimant to marry. Rav replies: {\i shani hatam dekivan de\u8217'ika achrina bahadei mirtat} \u8212- the case of two claimants is different, because each man knows the other is present, and the risk of being exposed as a liar in front of the other claimant and the father is much greater. This heightened social pressure provides sufficient deterrent that we credit the one who steps forward to marry. Rashi and Tosafot note that we treat the one willing to give the {\i get} as more likely to be the liar \u8212- a quasi-{\i Shlomo HaMelech} resolution \u8212- though the logic remains somewhat difficult.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Woman\u8217's Ignorance\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Woman\u8217's Claim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} states that Rav Ashi himself concedes the following case: {\i hi she\u8217'amrah nitkadashti ve\u8217'eini yoda\u8217'at lemi nitkadashti} \u8212- if a woman herself says, \u8220"I was betrothed and I do not know to whom,\u8221" and a man comes forward claiming {\i ani kidashtich} \u8212- I betrothed you \u8212- {\i eino ne\u8217'eman liknos}, he is not believed to marry her. This is a striking asymmetry: the father\u8217's claim generates a {\i ne\u8217'emanut} that can be extended to a male claimant; the woman\u8217's own claim does not generate an equivalent {\i ne\u8217'emanut} for a claimant.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Woman\u8217's Interest\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The reason given is: {\i mipnei shehi mechapa alav} \u8212- because she will cover for him. The logic is as follows: part of what renders the claimant credible in the father\u8217's case is the assumption that if he is lying, he risks being identified and exposed \u8212- the father will call him out. But when the woman herself is the one who said she was betrothed to an unknown person, and a man appears claiming to be that person, the woman has a powerful vested interest not to expose him. If she is not betrothed to this man, she remains bound to the unknown original {\i mekadesh}, cannot remarry, and faces a life as an {\i agunah}. The temptation to go along with the false claimant \u8212- or to genuinely convince herself that this must be the right person \u8212- is far stronger for the woman in her own predicament than for a father concerned for his daughter, however genuine that concern may be. Because the woman\u8217's {\i ne\u8217'emanut} cannot constrain the claimant in the same way the father\u8217's does, the claimant lacks the fear of exposure that otherwise supports his credibility, and we therefore do not credit him to marry her.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Kiddushin 64\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Father\u8217's Believability\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Capital Punishment Query\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara opens with a question \u8212- {\i ibaya lehu} \u8212- that naturally extends from the {\i mishnah} concerning a father\u8217's authority to marry off his minor daughter. The {\i mishnah} had established, grounded in the {\i pasuk} from the {\i motzi shem ra} case \u8212- {\i et biti natati la-ish hazeh}, \u8220"I gave my daughter to this man\u8221" \u8212- that a father is believed when he declares he has married off his daughter. This generates immediate practical consequences: if the father says he married her to Reuven and cannot remember, she may not freely marry another without a {\i get}. But the Gemara now presses further: {\i mahu liskol al yado} \u8212- does the father\u8217's testimony carry sufficient weight that a third party who commits adultery with this daughter could be executed on the basis of that testimony alone? That is, if the father declares she is married and Shimon, with witnesses to the act itself but with no independent witnesses to the marriage, has relations with her, would Shimon receive {\i skilah}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav: No Execution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav rules: {\i ein soklin} \u8212- no, one does not execute on the basis of the father\u8217's testimony. The Torah believed the father {\i l\u8217'issura}, to create a prohibition, to treat the daughter as married for purposes of restricting her behavior and requiring a {\i get} before she may remarry. But {\i l\u8217'ketala lo heimneih} \u8212- the Torah did not extend that credibility to the point of executing another person on the father\u8217's word.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Assi: Yes Execution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Assi disagrees and rules: {\i soklin} \u8212- one does execute. When the Torah granted the father believability through the verse {\i et biti natati la-ish hazeh}, it did so comprehensively: {\i l\u8217'chol milei heimneih rachmana l\u8217'av}, the Torah trusted the father in all matters, without reservation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Woman vs.\u160 ?Father\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Assi then offers a remarkable self-reflective comment. He acknowledges that his two rulings \u8212- the one taught previously and the one just stated \u8212- {\i me\u8217'afsan igra}, they shatter the rooftops, they sound internally contradictory. The teaching from the previous day\u8217's discussion was that if a woman says \u8220"I was married to someone but I cannot remember who,\u8221" and a man steps forward claiming to be that husband, we do not believe him \u8212- because we suspect the woman might collude with an unsuitable suitor who cannot be trusted to fear contradiction. By contrast, when the {\i father} says he married off his daughter to someone and cannot remember who, and a man steps forward claiming it was him, we {\i do} believe that man, because he fears being contradicted by the father. The father, after all, might well say: \u8220"I don\u8217't know who it was, but it certainly was not you.\u8221"\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Now Rav Assi articulates the tension: {\i makom sheba lichnos koneis} \u8212- in the scenario involving the father, even though we are willing to let the claimant live with the woman as her husband, implying that we treat the father\u8217's testimony as something less than absolute fact (since we readily accept the claimant\u8217's word), Rav Assi nevertheless rules that we would give {\i skilah} to some other man who commits adultery with her. And yet in the case where the woman herself testifies she was married \u8212- where we treat her testimony as weighty enough to refuse belief in any claimant who says he is the husband \u8212- we would {\i not} give {\i skilah} if someone were to commit adultery with her. This seems backwards: the woman\u8217's testimony about her own marriage is treated as more binding than the father\u8217's when it comes to refusing the claimant, yet less binding when it comes to capital consequences.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Resolving Contradiction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Assi himself resolves the apparent contradiction: {\i v\u8217'lo hee} \u8212- in truth, this is not a real problem. The Torah simply treated these two sources of testimony differently, each in its own domain. {\i L\u8217'av heimneih rachmana, l\u8217'didah lo heimneih} \u8212- the Torah trusted the father; it did not trust the woman. The father\u8217's declaration about his minor daughter is treated as fact for purposes of capital punishment. The woman\u8217's declaration about herself is sufficient to prohibit her remarriage without a {\i get}, but it is not treated as juridical fact sufficient for execution. Meanwhile, the rule about whether to believe a man who comes forward claiming to be the husband operates on entirely separate logic \u8212- the father\u8217's declaration creates a dynamic where the claimant fears contradiction, the woman\u8217's does not. These are independent considerations that do not produce a true contradiction.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Chisda Alternative\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Chisda rules: {\i echad zeh v\u8217'echad zeh ein soklin} \u8212- neither the father\u8217's testimony about his daughter\u8217's marriage nor the woman\u8217's testimony about herself is sufficient to impose capital punishment. And Rav Chisda here follows his broader principle, articulated elsewhere, that when the Torah grants an individual {\i ne\u8217'eman} status regarding personal matters of status, that believability is not equivalent to formal testimony by {\i edim}. It is a limited form of credibility.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Age & Sacrifice\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This principle is illustrated by a case Rav Chisda himself stated: if a father declares \u8220"my son is nine years and a day old\u8221" or \u8220"my daughter is three years and a day old,\u8221" these ages are halachically significant \u8212- a boy of nine years and a day who engages in intercourse with a married woman has effected a halachically cognizable act of intercourse, making the woman liable, though not the minor himself; and similarly for a girl of three years and a day. Rav Chisda ruled that the father is believed to establish these ages {\i lekorban} \u8212- to obligate an adult party in an unintentional act to bring a {\i korban} \u8212- but not {\i lemalkos} and not {\i le\u8217'onashim}, not for lashes and not for execution. A {\i baraita} is then cited in support of this position: it teaches the same formula regarding a father\u8217's declaration that his son is thirteen years and a day or his daughter is twelve years and a day, making them halachic adults. The father is believed {\i lenedarim ulcharamim ulehakdashot ule\u8217'erchin} \u8212- for all matters of vows, consecrations, and monetary obligations \u8212- but not for physical punishments. This confirms the approach of Rav Chisda.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Adolescence Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i baraita} thus closes this opening sugya. The father is {\i ne\u8217'eman} to establish the majority status of his children for purposes of vows and sanctification, and {\i ne\u8217'eman} to establish the married status of his daughter for purposes of creating a prohibition and requiring a {\i get} \u8212- but in neither case does the Torah\u8217's grant of believability rise to the level of imposing capital punishment or corporal punishment on others.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Marriage & Divorce\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Past Marriage Claim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now arrives at the official {\i daf} \u8212- 64 \u8212- and opens a new {\i mishnah}. The {\i mishnah} presents a series of cases in which a father makes declarations about his daughter, and the question in each case is whether he is {\i ne\u8217'eman}. The first case: {\i kidashtiha vegerashtiha keshehi katana veharei hi katana} \u8212- the father says he married off his daughter and subsequently accepted a {\i get} on her behalf, all of this occurring while she was a minor, and she is currently still a minor. In such a case he is {\i ne\u8217'eman}. The practical consequence is that she is now a divorced woman and therefore forbidden to marry a {\i kohen}. The second variation: the father makes the same claim \u8212- he married her and divorced her while she was a minor \u8212- but she is now an adult at the time of his declaration. In this case he is {\i eino ne\u8217'eman}, not believed, and therefore she is treated as never having been married and is fully permitted to a {\i kohen}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Captivity Claim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The next case: {\i nishbis uphdisiha} \u8212- the father says his daughter was taken captive and he redeemed her. This claim, if accepted, would render her forbidden to a {\i kohen} due to the presumption of rape during captivity \u8212- a concern whose painful contemporary resonance the teacher pauses to note. The {\i mishnah} rules: {\i bein ketana bein gedola eino ne\u8217'eman}, whether she is a minor or an adult, the father is not believed. She therefore remains fully permitted to a {\i kohen}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Present Capacity\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara asks: {\i mai shna reisha umai shna seifa} \u8212- what distinguishes the opening case, where he is believed, from the closing case, where he is not? The Gemara answers: {\i reisha biyado, seifa lav biyado} \u8212- in the first case, the matter is within his control; in the second, it is not. When his daughter is still a minor, it is within his power to marry her off and to receive a {\i get} on her behalf. The Torah believed him precisely because the act was something he could perform. By contrast, a daughter being taken captive is entirely beyond his control; he cannot cause that to happen. Therefore he has no {\i biyado} and he is not believed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Control Analysis\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This principle \u8212- that {\i biyado} is the criterion for {\i ne\u8217'eman} status \u8212- is understood, at least by Rashi, as a form of {\i mah li leshaker}: since he could have done the thing he claims, there is no motive to fabricate. Some {\i rishonim} frame it differently, as a structural principle that authority over a matter generates credibility about it, rather than as a psychological inference. Either way, {\i biyado} in the {\i reisha} is the basis for belief, and {\i lav biyado} in the {\i seifa} is the basis for disbelief.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chalal Marriage\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara immediately challenges this framework: is it truly the case that the father has no capacity to affect his daughter\u8217's status vis-\u224 ?-vis a {\i kohen}? {\i Veharei biyado lehasi\u8217'ah lechalal} \u8212- he could marry her off to a {\i chalal}, a man born of a forbidden union involving a {\i kohen} (for example, the son of a {\i kohen} who married a divorc\u233 ?e). Such a marriage is itself permitted, but the woman who marries a {\i chalal} becomes a {\i chalalah} and is thereby forbidden to {\i kohanim}. Since the father has the capacity to effect this result, he should \u8212- by the {\i biyado} logic \u8212- be believed about her captivity status, which concerns the same issue of {\i pesul} to {\i kehunah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chalal Resolution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara answers: {\i ha lo kashya, k\u8217'Rabbi Dostai ben Yehuda} \u8212- this is not difficult, because our {\i mishnah} follows the opinion of Rabbi Dostai ben Yehuda. Rabbi Dostai holds that {\i benos Yisrael mikveh tahara lechalalim} \u8212- a Jewish woman who is not herself a {\i chalalah} serves as a source of purification for the {\i chalal} she marries, meaning that neither the woman nor their children take on the {\i chalal} status. Therefore, according to Rabbi Dostai, marrying the daughter to a {\i chalal} does not make her a {\i chalalah} and does not affect her status for {\i kehunah} at all. The father thus has no ability to render her {\i pesulah} to a {\i kohen} through that route, and the {\i biyado} reasoning does not apply.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mamzer Marriage\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara presses further: {\i veharei biyado lehasi\u8217'ah l\u8217'mamzer} \u8212- but he could marry her off to a {\i mamzer}. A {\i bat Yisrael} is forbidden to marry a {\i mamzer}, but the {\i kiddushin} in such a case would nevertheless be binding, since a {\i mamzer}\u8217's forbiddenness is only a {\i lav} \u8212- a simple negative prohibition \u8212- and {\i kiddushin} takes effect even in such cases. The consequence of such a marriage would render her forbidden to a {\i kohen}. Even though the father would be transgressing in arranging this, the fact that the {\i kiddushin} is binding means it remains within his power \u8212- and thus he should be believed about her captivity status.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara responds: {\i k\u8217'Rabbi Akiva} \u8212- our {\i mishnah} follows Rabbi Akiva, who holds {\i ein kiddushin tofsin b\u8217'chiyuvei lavin}, that {\i kiddushin} does not take effect in any forbidden union, including those prohibited by a mere {\i lav}. Therefore, according to Rabbi Akiva, an attempt to marry the daughter to a {\i mamzer} would produce no valid {\i kiddushin}, the child of such a union would be a {\i mamzer}, and the father has effectively no power to change her status. {\i Biyado} is absent.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara continues probing. Could the father arrange a marriage of an {\i almanah} to a {\i Kohen Gadol}? Such a union is forbidden, and the child is a {\i chalal} but \u8212- according to Rabbi Simai\u8217's understanding of Rabbi Akiva \u8212- not a {\i mamzer}. Since it is not a {\i mamzer}-producing union, Rabbi Akiva would agree that the {\i kiddushin} is binding. This would mean the father {\i does} have a mechanism, at least in theory, to render his daughter forbidden to a {\i kohen} even under Rabbi Akiva\u8217's framework, and the {\i biyado} argument would re-emerge.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara resolves this by appealing to the view of Rabbi Yeshovav. Rabbi Yeshovav declared, {\i bavo venitzvach al Akiva ben Yosef} \u8212- let us come and cry out against Rabbi Akiva \u8212- arguing that Rabbi Akiva\u8217's position is that {\i kol she\u8217'ein lo biah b\u8217'Yisrael havlad mamzer}, any forbidden intercourse whatsoever, including the {\i issurei kehunah} cases, produces a {\i mamzer}. Under this view, Rabbi Akiva would hold that even the union of an {\i almanah} with a {\i Kohen Gadol} produces a {\i mamzer} and the {\i kiddushin} is void. Thus, the father has no avenue by which to render his daughter {\i pesulah} to a {\i kohen}, and his lack of {\i biyado} is complete.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 However, the Gemara notes a further complication: if Rabbi Yeshovav was specifically responding to Rabbi Simai\u8217's formulation \u8212- rather than making a free-standing claim about Rabbi Akiva\u8217's view \u8212- then Rabbi Yeshovav only disagrees with Rabbi Simai\u8217's example of {\i almanah} to {\i Kohen Gadol}, while perhaps conceding that an {\i issur asei} case (a prohibition derived from a positive commandment, such as a third-generation {\i Mitzri}) would still produce binding {\i kiddushin} even under Rabbi Akiva. In that event, even under the most restrictive reading, the father could theoretically marry his daughter to such a man and thereby render her forbidden to a {\i kohen}, and the {\i biyado} logic would resurface. The Gemara has thus reached a point where the {\i biyado} framework is under severe pressure.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Ashi Reframe\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 At this juncture, Rav Ashi intervenes to reject the entire {\i biyado} framework as an explanation for the {\i mishnah}. {\i V\u8217'tisbra} \u8212- is this really the correct reading? Do we truly believe that the reason the father is {\i ne\u8217'eman} in the {\i reisha} is because the matter is within his control? The objection is decisive on two fronts. First, {\i nehi d\u8217'biyado le-kadsha, biyado le-garsha}? \u8212- granted that he could initiate the marriage, but the divorce must be initiated by the husband, not the father. The father cannot compel a {\i get}. How then can {\i biyado} explain his believability about the divorce? Second, {\i ilu amar hai d\u8217'lo nicha begava mi matzi mekadesh leih lah} \u8212- even for the marriage itself, if the prospective groom were to say he is unwilling, the father could not force it through. The father is not in full control of either the marriage or the divorce. The {\i biyado} explanation therefore cannot be the true foundation of the {\i mishnah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Torah Source\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Ashi therefore offers an entirely different explanation: {\i reisha rachmana heimneih} \u8212- in the {\i reisha} of the {\i mishnah}, the father is believed because the Torah directly granted him that believability. This is demonstrated by the {\i pasuk} cited by Rav Huna in the name of Rav: the verse {\i et biti natati la-ish hazeh} \u8212- the father\u8217's declaration in the {\i motzi shem ra} narrative is treated by the Torah as possessing halachic weight in court. From here we derive that a father is {\i ne\u8217'eman} to establish that he married off his daughter. Furthermore, parsing the verse: {\i la-ish asrah} \u8212- by saying \u8220"to a man,\u8221" he creates a prohibition; {\i la-ish hazeh hitirah} \u8212- by saying \u8220"to {\i this} man,\u8221" he identifies the husband and thereby makes her permissible to him specifically. From that same verse, then, we derive both his believability to declare the marriage and his believability to delimit its scope \u8212- and by extension, to confirm the divorce that released her.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Limited Scope\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This {\i ne\u8217'emanut} is therefore inherent and textually grounded, not derived from any logic of control or {\i mah li leshaker}. And precisely because it is textually grounded in the context of marriage, it is also textually limited: {\i be-nisuin heimneih rachmana l\u8217'av, be-shvuyah lo heimneih} \u8212- the Torah believed the father with respect to matters of marriage and {\i kiddushin}; it did not extend that believability to matters of captivity. The father\u8217's declaration that his daughter was taken captive is thus simply outside the scope of the Torah\u8217's grant, regardless of any question of {\i biyado}. She therefore retains her full permitted status vis-\u224 ?-vis a {\i kohen}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Yibum Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sons Claim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} now turns to a different application of the {\i ne\u8217'eman} principle, this time concerning a dying man\u8217's deathbed declarations about his family structure, which have direct bearing on whether his widow will be subject to {\i yibum}. If a man says on his deathbed {\i yesh li banim} \u8212- I have sons \u8212- he is believed, and his widow is thereby exempt from {\i yibum}. Even if no one knew of these sons and the presumed status would otherwise obligate her in {\i yibum}, the husband\u8217's declaration on his deathbed establishes their existence.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Brothers Claim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Conversely, if a man says on his deathbed {\i yesh li achim} \u8212- I have brothers \u8212- when no brothers were previously known, he is {\i eino ne\u8217'eman}; he is not believed. His widow therefore remains in her prior status and does not become obligated in {\i yibum} or {\i chalitza} on the basis of his declaration.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Permit Not Forbid\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The pattern that emerges is: the husband is believed to permit \u8212- to free his wife from {\i yibum} \u8212- but not to forbid, not to impose upon her an obligation of {\i yibum} or {\i chalitza} that did not previously exist. The {\i mishnah} does not make explicit what the presumed status was before the husband spoke, nor does it explain the logical basis for this asymmetry, though one possible explanation involves {\i biyado}: he could have given her a {\i get} to free her from {\i yibum} entirely, and therefore when he speaks to that effect, he is believed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Not Rabbi Nathan\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara notes: {\i neima matnitin d\u8217'lo k\u8217'Rabbi Nathan} \u8212- let us say our {\i mishnah} does not follow Rabbi Nathan\u8217's position.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Contradictions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i baraita} is cited presenting a more complex scenario. At the time of {\i kiddushin}, the husband said \u8220"I have children\u8221" \u8212- she need not worry about {\i yibum}. At the time of death he said \u8220"I have no children.\u8221" Alternatively, at {\i kiddushin} he said \u8220"I have no brothers,\u8221" and at death he said \u8220"I have brothers.\u8221" In other words, his deathbed statement directly contradicts what he said at the time of marriage. This is a sharper case than our {\i mishnah}, where the husband simply makes a single declaration.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi\u8217's Rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi holds: {\i ne\u8217'eman lehatir ve\u8217'eino ne\u8217'eman le\u8217'asor} \u8212- even in this case of self-contradiction, one accepts the statement that permits and disregards the statement that forbids. The earlier statement at the time of marriage that exempted her from {\i yibum} is accepted; the later statement that would reimpose the obligation is rejected. This is more expansive than our {\i mishnah}, because here Rabbi maintains his position even in the face of explicit contradiction, accepting the permitting statement over the forbidding one regardless of which came first or last.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Nathan\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Nathan rules: {\i af ne\u8217'eman le\u8217'asor} \u8212- the husband is believed even to forbid, even to impose {\i yibum} obligation. The last statement he makes is credited with establishing the reality. This position appears incompatible with our {\i mishnah} at first glance, since our {\i mishnah} refuses to believe the husband when he says things that obligate {\i yibum}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava Harmony\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava attempts to show that Rabbi Nathan could still agree with our {\i mishnah}. His argument is based on the specific logic of the {\i baraita} case: {\i keivan debishat mita kahadar bei, eima kushta ka\u8217'amar} \u8212- since the husband reverses himself on his deathbed, one might reasonably infer that he is now telling the truth. He deceived his wife at the time of marriage, telling her what she wanted to hear in order to ensure she would agree to marry him. Now, lying on his deathbed, laden with guilt, he recants and speaks the truth. Rabbi Nathan, Rava argues, believes the deathbed statement precisely because this narrative of guilt-driven truth-telling is plausible. In our {\i mishnah}, by contrast, the husband simply makes a statement without any prior contradiction. The issue of {\i yibum} never arose at the time of marriage. There is no backdrop of guilt and no prior false assurance. Indeed, one might suspect the opposite \u8212- that the dying man wants to make his wife\u8217's life difficult, as it were a final act of spite. In that context, Rava suggests, even Rabbi Nathan might concede that the husband is not believed to impose the obligation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Abaye Objection\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye rejects Rava\u8217's harmonization. His objection runs in the opposite direction: {\i hashta, mah hatam de\u8217'kamarei lei le\u8217'diburei, amarta kushta ka\u8217'amar} \u8212- in the {\i baraita}\u8217's case, the husband contradicts his own prior statement, yet even there Rabbi Nathan accepts the deathbed declaration and imposes {\i yibum} obligation. Now in our {\i mishnah}, where the husband never made any prior statement and thus does not contradict himself, should we not {\i certainly} \u8212- {\i lo kol sheken} \u8212- accept his deathbed statement and worry about the {\i yibum} obligation? The fact that in the {\i baraita} there is a contradiction, which might otherwise undermine credibility, and Rabbi Nathan still credits the deathbed statement, should make it all the more compelling that in our {\i mishnah}, where there is no undermining contradiction, we credit his statement. Abaye concludes that the logic favors reading our {\i mishnah} as Rabbi\u8217's position, not Rabbi Nathan\u8217's, though the reasoning cuts sharply in either direction.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Unresolved\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 As the {\i daf} closes, a number of questions remain unresolved. What exactly was the presumed status of the widow before the husband spoke in each case of the {\i mishnah}? What is the precise logical basis \u8212- whether {\i biyado}, presumption of status, or some other principle \u8212- for the rule that the husband is believed to permit but not to forbid? How do Rabbi Nathan\u8217's various scenarios cohere into a single principled position? These questions will be taken up when Abaye rereads the {\i mishnah} in the continuation of the sugya, though, as the teacher notes, not all the difficulties will be fully resolved even then.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Kiddushin 64b\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Hazakah & Mah Li Leshaker\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishnah\u8217's Basic Rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara on 64b opens by completing its analysis of the Mishnah\u8217's rule that a husband is believed to free his wife from {\i yibbum} but not to bind her in it. The Mishnah had established this principle without fully explaining its rationale, and the Gemara \u8212- having already introduced the positions of Rabbi and Rabbi Natan in the preceding discussion \u8212- now presses toward a resolution that clarifies the underlying logic.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Abaye on Hazakah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye explains the Mishnah\u8217's position as follows: the husband is believed only insofar as his statement reinforces the existing {\i hazakah} \u8212- the presumptive status of his wife. Since we have no established presumption that the man has brothers or that he lacks sons, we begin by presuming she is not obligated in {\i yibbum}. If on his deathbed he says, \u8220"I have children,\u8221" he is saying something consistent with that presumption and he is believed. If, however, he says, \u8220"I have brothers,\u8221" he is attempting to overturn the status quo and make her {\i asur} to the world \u8212- and for that, his unilateral claim carries no weight. As the Gemara puts it: {\i lav kol kemeinei de\u8217'asar la akol alma} \u8212- it is not within his power to forbid her to everyone.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Mishnah\u8217's principle, then, is not precisely that he is believed to permit and not to forbid; it is that he is not believed to go against the existing {\i hazakah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Natan\u8217's Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i baraita} of Rabbi Natan presents a more complex scenario. There, it was established before the marriage that the husband did have brothers \u8212- there was a presumption ({\i hazakah}) that she would be obligated in {\i yibbum}. At the time of {\i kiddushin}, the husband said, \u8220"I have no brothers,\u8221" which went against that presumption. On his deathbed, he retracted and said that he does have brothers. Rabbi Natan holds that in this case, the deathbed statement is believed and she is bound in {\i yibbum}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mah Li Leshaker Logic\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The first question is why we believed him at the time of {\i kiddushin} when he said he had no brothers \u8212- a statement that contradicted the prevailing {\i hazakah}. The answer is {\i mah li leshaker}: why would he lie? One might argue he was trying to induce her to marry him by assuring her she would never be subject to {\i yibbum}. But this reasoning fails, because if he simply wanted to make that promise, he could have told her he would give her a {\i get} that would take effect a moment before his death. Since he had an alternative means of achieving the same result without misrepresenting his family situation, we say there is no sufficient motive to lie \u8212- and therefore we believe him, even against the {\i hazakah}. This {\i mah li leshaker} functions much like {\i migo}: because he could have made a more modest claim and achieved the same benefit, the fact that he made the stronger claim is treated as equivalent to the testimony of witnesses.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is, the Gemara acknowledges, a significant {\i chiddush}. Even where there was a pre-existing {\i hazakah} that she was obligated in {\i yibbum}, his statement at {\i kiddushin} was sufficient to override it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi\u8217's Perspective\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi holds that this first {\i mah li leshaker} was so powerful that it is treated {\i k\u8217'eidim dami} \u8212- as equivalent to the testimony of witnesses. And just as witness testimony would completely uproot the {\i hazakah}, so too his statement at {\i kiddushin} entirely erased the prior presumption and established a new one: she is now presumed to be free from {\i yibbum}. With that new {\i hazakah} firmly in place, his later deathbed statement \u8212- that he does have brothers \u8212- is merely an attempt to make a woman who is presumed {\i mutar} into someone {\i asur}. And for that, there is no {\i mah li leshaker}: there is no obvious alternative means by which he could have accomplished that goal on his deathbed. He cannot claim he \u8220"could have\u8221" bound her another way. Perhaps he simply wishes, in his final moments, to cause her anguish. Without a compelling counter-motive, his statement has no special credibility, and since she already has the {\i hazakah} of being free, he cannot overturn it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Natan Refined\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Natan disagrees with Rabbi\u8217's assessment of the first statement\u8217's power. He holds that {\i mah li leshaker} is only {\i k\u8217'chazakah dami} \u8212- as strong as a {\i hazakah}, not equivalent to witnesses. The first statement was sufficient to neutralize the pre-existing {\i hazakah} that she was obligated, but it did not completely erase that original presumption and replace it with a new one. The scales were tipped, but not so decisively. Consequently, when on his deathbed he goes back to reinforcing the original presumption that he has brothers, he is believed \u8212- because he is not creating a new {\i issur} from nothing; he is returning us to an underlying presumption that was only partially displaced.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Unified Understanding\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara uses this analysis to make an important clarifying point. Rabbi Natan\u8217's position had initially appeared to say that a husband is believed to make his wife obligated in {\i yibbum} \u8212- a sweeping power to bind her by his statement alone. The Gemara now shows that even Rabbi Natan would agree that a husband cannot, through a bare claim, take a woman who is presumed {\i mutar} and make her {\i asur}. Rabbi Natan only believes the husband when the statement reinforces a pre-existing {\i hazakah} that had been partially, but not fully, neutralized. Both Rabbi and Rabbi Natan concur that a husband\u8217's claim cannot independently create an obligation of {\i yibbum} where none was presumed to exist. Their dispute is narrow: how potently did the {\i mah li leshaker} at {\i kiddushin} shift the presumptive status, and can a deathbed retraction restore the original {\i hazakah}?\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Hamekadesh Et Bito\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Bogeret Exclusion\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Mishnah states: {\i hamekadesh et bito stam} \u8212- a man who betroths off his daughter without specifying which one. The rule is that {\i bogeret} daughters \u8212- those who are {\i gedolot}, over twelve and a half years old \u8212- are not included, because he has no legal authority to arrange their {\i kiddushin}. This seems obvious enough, and the Gemara will shortly ask what the {\i chiddush} could possibly be.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Plural vs.\u160 ?Actual\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Before reaching that question, the Gemara notes that the Mishnah uses the plural {\i bogrot}, which might suggest the man has multiple adult daughters. The Gemara clarifies: this is not a factual description of his family. The plural is idiomatic \u8212- it reflects how people generally speak in such cases. When people say that a father marrying off his daughter does not include the adult daughters, they express it in the plural as a category, not as a description of how many adult daughters this particular man actually has.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The real case of the Mishnah, the Gemara establishes, involves a father who has exactly one adult daughter and one underage daughter ({\i ketanah}). The point is not to address multiple adult daughters; it is to clarify which daughter is included in an unspecified betrothal. And the {\i chiddush}, the Gemara explains, lies in a specific circumstance: what if the adult daughter designated the father as her {\i shaliach} \u8212- her agent \u8212- to arrange her {\i kiddushin}? In that case, he would be legally empowered to marry off both daughters. Yet the Mishnah implies that even then, the unspecified betrothal is presumed to refer to the underage daughter, not the adult one.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shaliach & Priority\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 When the adult daughter has made the father her {\i shaliach}, one might have supposed that since he is authorized to act for her, and since a marriage of an adult woman is more intuitive \u8212- we might presume he intended the adult daughter. The Gemara rejects this: {\i lo shavik inish midi d\u8217'is lei hana\u8217'ei minei v\u8217'avid midi d\u8217'leis lei hana\u8217'ei minei} \u8212- a person does not abandon something that benefits him in favor of something that does not. When a father marries off his {\i ketanah}, he retains the {\i kesef kiddushin} \u8212- the ring or money given by the {\i mekadesh} \u8212- for himself. When he acts as {\i shaliach} for his adult daughter, the {\i kesef kiddushin} goes to her. He therefore has a financial incentive to be marrying off the {\i ketanah} rather than the {\i bogeret}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Father\u8217's Obligation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara presses further: what if even the adult daughter told the father that he may keep the {\i kesef kiddushin} for his efforts? Would we not then presume, absent any financial differential, that the {\i bogeret} is the intended bride? The Gemara answers that even so, the father\u8217's presumed intention remains the {\i ketanah}. The reason is not financial but moral and legal: {\i lo shavik inish mitzvah d\u8217'ramya alei v\u8217'avid mitzvah d\u8217'lo ramya alei} \u8212- a person does not set aside an obligation that rests upon him in order to fulfill one that does not.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Torah places the responsibility for an underage daughter squarely on her father. In the world the Talmud presumes, a daughter moves directly from {\i beit aviha} \u8212- her father\u8217's home \u8212- to {\i beit ba\u8217'alah} \u8212- her husband\u8217's home. There is no normative period of independent adult life without either a father\u8217's or a husband\u8217's protection. Once a girl reaches {\i bogeret} status, she acquires her own legal standing and can manage her own affairs; she is, in a sense, no longer fully under her father\u8217's jurisdiction. The {\i ketanah}, however, is entirely his responsibility. He is obligated to ensure that she transitions properly from his household into a marriage. That obligation weighs on him and constitutes the priority: he will, as it were, take care of his own obligations first.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two Daughter Groups\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Mishnah then addresses a more complex case. A man has two groups of daughters \u8212- one group from an earlier wife and a second group from a later wife. He says, \u8220"I betrothed my {\i gedolah} \u8212- my oldest,\u8221" but then cannot remember precisely whom he meant. The ambiguity the Mishnah describes is striking: did he mean the oldest of the older group? The oldest of the younger group \u8212- who is the oldest within that set? Or the youngest of the older group \u8212- who is nonetheless older than the oldest of the younger group?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Mishnah walks through the parallel case using the word {\i ketanah} \u8212- youngest \u8212- with the same structure.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Meir\u8217's Approach\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Meir holds that all daughters who could in any sense be called {\i gedolah} are potentially betrothed and therefore {\i asur} \u8212- all, that is, except for the very youngest of the younger group. Rabbi Meir\u8217's reasoning is that a person is willing to use a term loosely and allow themselves to enter into a state of ambiguity. If he remembers saying \u8220"{\i gedolah},\u8221" he may well have been using that label in a relative sense \u8212- anyone who is older than someone else might be called {\i gedolah}. As long as a daughter has someone younger than her, she can bear that label. Rabbi Meir does not expect precision in such a memory, and the result is that the uncertainty sweeps broadly.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Yosi\u8217's View\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yosi takes the opposite position: {\i kulan mutarot chutz min hagedolah shebagedolot} \u8212- they are all permitted except for the oldest of the oldest group. Rabbi Yosi reasons that a person does not get themselves into ambiguity ({\i lo machzik inish nafshei besafeka}). If what he remembers is the word {\i gedolah}, he must mean the most specific and unambiguous referent of that word \u8212- the daughter who is oldest in the clearest sense, the oldest of the oldest group. Anything less definite would not have registered in his memory under that label.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Pesach Comparison\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara notes that there is an apparently parallel debate elsewhere. If someone takes a {\i neder} forbidding himself from something {\i ad pnei HaPesach} \u8212- until the \u8220"face\u8221" of {\i Pesach} \u8212- what does that mean? The Mishnah cited there states: Rabbi Meir says {\i asur ad sheyagia} \u8212- forbidden until {\i Pesach} arrives; Rabbi Yosi says {\i asur ad sheyeitzei} \u8212- forbidden until {\i Pesach} departs. But this seems to be the reverse of their positions in our Mishnah. Here, Rabbi Meir holds that the term can refer to many things and thus extends the prohibition broadly; there, Rabbi Meir seems to take the narrower reading, and Rabbi Yosi the broader one.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara initially suggests that the names may have been accidentally reversed in transmission \u8212- {\i muchlefet hashita} \u8212- and that a {\i baraita} records the positions in the correct order consistent with our Mishnah.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Abaye\u8217's Precision\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye offers a fundamental clarification of the debate in our Mishnah. The dispute between Rabbi Meir and Rabbi Yosi, he says, applies specifically to the case of {\i shtei kitei banot} \u8212- two distinct groups of daughters. The crucial feature of two groups is that membership in the older group itself confers the label {\i gedolah} on every daughter within it. A daughter in the older group can be called {\i gedolah} not because she is the oldest individual, but because she belongs to the {\i kat hagedolah} \u8212- the older cohort. This is what allows the ambiguity to extend beyond a single individual.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 By contrast, if there is only one group of daughters \u8212- say, four daughters in a single family unit \u8212- then {\i gedolah} means the oldest and {\i ketanah} means the youngest, full stop. A middle daughter would be called by her name. The label {\i gedolah} or {\i ketanah} attaches unambiguously only to the extremes. In that case, there is no dispute: everyone agrees that {\i gedolah} means precisely the oldest, and {\i ketanah} precisely the youngest.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Three Daughters Test\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Adda bar Masna challenges Abaye: if the debate applies only when there are two groups, and within those groups the label {\i gedolah} attaches to everyone in the older cohort, then what if there are three daughters in the younger group rather than two? The middle one of the younger group would seem ambiguous \u8212- she could arguably be called {\i gedolah} relative to the youngest, but she cannot be the {\i gedolah} of the younger group in any straightforward sense. Should she then be {\i mutar}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye responds that the Mishnah is best read as presupposing exactly two daughters in the younger group. This is evident from the Mishnah\u8217's own language: it says that the father does not know whether he meant {\i gedolah shebakedolot}, {\i gedolah shebaketanot}, or {\i ketanah shebagedolot} \u8212- but says nothing about a middle daughter in either group. If there were three in the younger group, the Mishnah would have needed to mention the middle one as a case of ambiguity. Since it does not, the case must be that there are only two in each group.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Pesach Resolution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Returning to the {\i Pesach} case, Rav Huna son of Rav Yehoshua asks Rava: if the entire debate in the Mishnah rests on the two-group structure \u8212- which allows the label {\i gedolah} to attach to an entire cohort rather than a single individual \u8212- why does the {\i Pesach} case generate a parallel dispute? The days of {\i Pesach} are all in one group; there are not two cohorts of days. According to Abaye, only one day should clearly be called the {\i pnei haPesach}, just as only one daughter in a single group can unambiguously be called {\i gedolah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara resolves this by clarifying that the {\i Pesach} case involves an entirely different type of dispute. The debate there is not about whether a person enters into ambiguity or remembers things loosely \u8212- it is a purely linguistic question about the meaning of the word {\i pnei}. One view ({\i mar savar}) holds that {\i pnei} means \u8220"before\u8221" \u8212- {\i ad kamei Pischa} \u8212- and so the prohibition lasts until {\i Pesach} begins. The other view holds that {\i pnei} derives from {\i mifnei} \u8212- to turn away or pass \u8212- and so the prohibition lasts until {\i Pesach} has passed. The two Amoraim are therefore debating the semantic content of a single word, not the psychological question of how loosely people use descriptive labels or whether they tolerate ambiguity. Once this distinction is clear, the apparent parallel with the daughters case dissolves entirely, and the positions of Rabbi Meir and Rabbi Yosi in our Mishnah stand on their own terms without needing to be reversed.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 He Said / She Said\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 His Claim Alone\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Mishnah presents a series of cases in which the two parties to an alleged {\i kiddushin} give contradictory accounts. The first: a man says to a woman, {\i kidashtich} \u8212- \u8220"I betrothed you\u8221" \u8212- and she says, {\i lo kidashtani} \u8212- \u8220"you did not betroth me.\u8221" The result is asymmetric. He is {\i asur} to her relatives \u8212- he cannot marry her mother, her daughter, her sister \u8212- because he is bound by his own statement. She, however, is entirely {\i muteret}: she is not bound by his statement and may marry whomever she wishes, including his own relatives. One person\u8217's claim cannot impose a status upon another person.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Her Claim Alone\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The reverse: she says he married her, and he says he did not. Now she is {\i asurah} to his relatives \u8212- and, more immediately, she cannot marry any other man, since she is claiming she is already married. He, however, is {\i mutar} to her relatives, since he denies the marriage. She has bound herself by her own words; she has not bound him.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Different Targets\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Mishnah then introduces cases in which the two parties do not even agree on who was betrothed. He says, \u8220"I betrothed you,\u8221" and she says, \u8220"No \u8212- you betrothed my daughter.\u8221" Each party is bound only by their own statement. He is {\i asur} to the mother\u8217's relatives (since he claims he married her), but {\i mutar} to the daughter\u8217's relatives (since he makes no such claim about her). The mother is {\i muteret} to his relatives (since she does not claim to be married to him), and the daughter is also {\i muteret} to his relatives, because a mother\u8217's claim about her daughter\u8217's betrothal does not bind the daughter.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mother vs.\u160 ?Daughter\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The final case reverses the parties: he says, \u8220"I betrothed your daughter,\u8221" and she says, \u8220"No \u8212- you betrothed me.\u8221" Again, each is bound only by their own claim. He is {\i asur} to the daughter\u8217's relatives, {\i mutar} to the mother\u8217's relatives. She is {\i asurah} to his relatives because she claims the marriage, and the daughter remains {\i muteret} to his relatives since she said nothing.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The underlying principle throughout is simple: {\i you are bound by your own statement, and your statement cannot bind another person}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Why Believe Whom\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara asks why these cases need to be stated explicitly rather than derived from each other. The answer illuminates the asymmetry between male and female claims. When a man says he married a woman, it costs him relatively little: his only immediate {\i issur} is with respect to her close relatives. He remains free to marry other women; no sweeping prohibition falls upon him. Since his statement is so inexpensive, {\i gavra lo ikhpat lei u\u8217'mikrei amar} \u8212- men will make such statements casually \u8212- and we have no strong reason to extend its legal effect to bind anyone else.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 By contrast, when a woman says she is married to a man, she is making an extraordinarily consequential claim. She is effectively declaring herself {\i asurah} to every other man in the world. No one would make such a statement carelessly or frivolously. The inherent weight of her claim gives it credibility: {\i lo havi amra l\u8217'meisar nafsha b\u8217'krovateih} \u8212- she would not make that statement, imposing such a severe restriction upon herself, unless it were true. One might therefore argue that even the man should be bound \u8212- that at minimum he should not be permitted to marry her relatives, since she has given such a compelling self-incriminating statement. The Gemara concludes, however, that even this reasoning does not suffice to make something {\i asur} upon another person on the basis of one party\u8217's unilateral claim.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mother\u8217's No Power\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The case of the mother who claims that her daughter was betrothed raises a natural question: the Torah empowers a father to betroth off his underage daughter, but grants no such authority to the mother. Might we nonetheless say that rabbinically, the mother should be believed enough to impose some restriction upon her daughter? The Gemara states that no such rabbinic authority exists \u8212- the mother\u8217's claim does not bind the daughter even rabbinically. The {\i chiddush} of the Mishnah in including this case is precisely to tell us that we do not extend the father\u8217's Torah-level power to the mother on a rabbinic basis.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Pesach & Get\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then addresses the practical consequence in the case where she claims to be married and he denies it. Shmuel holds: {\i mevakshin mimenu litten get} \u8212- we ask him, as a matter of propriety and compassion, to give her a {\i get}. She has put herself in a bind by her own statement; she can now not marry anyone else. Even though he is not halakhically compelled to do anything, it is proper to request that he give her a {\i get} and thereby free her from her self-imposed status.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Request vs.\u160 ?Coercion\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav\u8217's position initially appears to be that we {\i force} him to give a {\i get} \u8212- {\i kofin}. The Gemara resolves the apparent contradiction between Rav and Shmuel by showing that they are not disputing but complementing one another. Shmuel\u8217's statement establishes that we request a {\i get} from him. Rav adds: {\i v\u8217'im natan get mei-atzmo} \u8212- if he gives the {\i get} on his own initiative, without being asked or pressured by the {\i beit din}, that act constitutes an implicit admission that he was in fact married to her. Having admitted the marriage through the voluntary {\i get}, we then {\i force} him to pay her {\i ketubah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The practical implication is notable: a man in this situation would be well advised to give the {\i get} only in response to the {\i beit din\u8217's} request, and not spontaneously. A voluntary {\i get}, given before any such request, reads as an admission of the marriage and opens him to a compelled {\i ketubah} payment. The Gemara presents this without editorializing, though the underlying logic is clear.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Witnesses Requirement\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Yehuda\u8217's Rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now turns to what is, in many respects, one of the most consequential {\i sugyot} in the entire tractate for contemporary {\i hilchos kiddushin}. Rav Yehuda states: {\i hamekadesh be\u8217'ed echad ein chosheshin lekiddushav} \u8212- if a man performs {\i kiddushin} with only one witness present, we have no concern that the {\i kiddushin} is binding. The marriage is without legal effect.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Constitutive vs.\u160 ?Evident\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This rule is clear in its practical import, but its conceptual basis is ambiguous. Does the requirement for two witnesses mean that their presence is necessary to {\i prove} the {\i kiddushin} occurred \u8212- so that if everyone already agrees it happened, one witness might suffice? Or does it mean that the presence of two kosher witnesses is {\i constitutive} of the {\i kiddushin} itself \u8212- without them, the act simply does not take effect as a {\i kiddushin}, regardless of how confident everyone is that it occurred? The test case is clear: both parties fully admit that {\i kiddushin} took place, and one witness corroborates them. We have no factual doubt whatsoever. Is this {\i kiddushin} binding?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Yehuda Uncertain\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Yehuda himself, when asked this precise question, could not resolve it. He vacillated \u8212- {\i lo pashat b\u8217'yadei} \u8212- and left the question open. Whether two witnesses are required as a constitutive element of {\i kiddushin} or merely as an evidentiary mechanism for proving it remained, for him, genuinely uncertain.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shmuel\u8217's Clear Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Nachman, citing Shmuel, provides an unambiguous answer: {\i hamekadesh be\u8217'ed echad ein chosheshin lekiddushav, va\u8217'afilu shneihem modim} \u8212- even if both parties fully admit to the {\i kiddushin}, without two witnesses present the {\i kiddushin} is not legally binding. Shmuel\u8217's position is that witnesses are not merely evidentiary \u8212- they are constitutive. Without them, the act of {\i kiddushin} does not take legal effect.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava\u8217's Challenge\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava challenges this from the very Mishnah we have been studying. The {\i he said / she said} cases in the Mishnah \u8212- which occupy a significant portion of the tractate \u8212- appear to operate without any mention of witnesses. A man says he betrothed a woman; she denies it; the Mishnah determines who is bound by what. If {\i kiddushin} requires two witnesses to be legally binding, the Mishnah\u8217's entire framework of claims and counter-claims should be irrelevant: without witnesses, no {\i kiddushin} occurred, end of discussion. The fact that the Mishnah engages extensively with whose statement binds whom seems to presuppose that {\i kiddushin} can be legally effective without two witnesses present and actively testifying.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 One Witness Present\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara resolves Rava\u8217's challenge by reinterpreting the Mishnah\u8217's scenario. The cases in the Mishnah do not presume an absence of witnesses. Rather, the Gemara reads the Mishnah as operating in a case where the claimant asserts that witnesses were present at the time \u8212- {\i kidashtich bifnei ploni u\u8217'ploni} \u8212- but those witnesses are not currently available to testify. They have, as the Gemara puts it, gone overseas ({\i halchu lahem limdinat hayam}). The legal question is then not whether {\i kiddushin} requires witnesses, but rather: given that someone claims witnesses were present, how much do we credit that claim? Each party is bound by their own statement regarding an alleged act they themselves assert was properly witnessed, even though those witnesses cannot currently be produced.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 All Cases Presume Witness\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This resolution has far-reaching implications. Every one of the Mishnah\u8217's cases \u8212- not just the current ones but all the earlier discussions about who is believed regarding marriage \u8212- must be understood as involving a claim that witnesses were present, even though those witnesses are not currently being called to testify. The Mishnah is not operating in a world without witnesses; it is operating in a world where witnesses existed at the time but are unavailable now. This is, the Gemara notes, a significant reinterpretation of the entire series of {\i mishnayos}, and it is precisely what Shmuel\u8217's position requires.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Seclusion as Witnesses\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then raises a further challenge from the debate between {\i Beit Shammai} and {\i Beit Hillel} regarding a man who divorced his wife and later spent the night with her at an inn. {\i Beit Hillel} holds that a second {\i get} is required \u8212- suggesting that the encounter may have constituted new {\i kiddushin}; {\i Beit Shammai} holds it does not. The Gemara initially considers whether this debate might hinge on the question of one witness versus two, with the positions of {\i Beit Hillel} and {\i Beit Shammai} mapping onto different views of evidentiary sufficiency.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 What Witnesses See\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara immediately recognizes that this reading does not hold up under scrutiny. If witnesses directly observed them having intercourse, {\i Beit Shammai} would have no grounds to say no second {\i get} is needed. And if there were no witnesses at all, {\i Beit Hillel} would have no basis to require one. The debate cannot be about the number of witnesses. It must concern something else.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara resolves this: the case involves witnesses who observed the couple entering into {\i yichud} \u8212- seclusion together \u8212- but who did not directly witness any sexual act. The legal and factual question is whether {\i eidei yichud} \u8212- witnesses to seclusion \u8212- are treated as {\i eidei biah} \u8212- witnesses to intercourse \u8212- and thereby constitute evidence of a new act of {\i kiddushin}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Beis Shammai\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Beit Shammai} holds: {\i lo amrinan hein hein eidei yichud hein hein eidei biah} \u8212- we do not treat witnesses to seclusion as equivalent to witnesses to intercourse. Seclusion alone, without prior sexual history between the parties, does not allow us to presume that intercourse occurred, and therefore no new {\i kiddushin} is created.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Beis Hillel\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Beit Hillel} holds the opposite: {\i amrinan hein hein eidei yichud hein hein eidei biah} \u8212- where the couple had previously been married and had engaged in intercourse, witnesses seeing them enter seclusion together are treated as if they had witnessed the sexual act itself, and this constitutes evidence of a new {\i kiddushin} requiring a new {\i get}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Consensus on Virgins\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Both {\i Beit Shammai} and {\i Beit Hillel} agree, however, that if the couple had been divorced after {\i eirusin} \u8212- betrothal without consummation \u8212- and had therefore never had sexual relations, then witnesses to seclusion are not treated as witnesses to intercourse, and no second {\i get} is required. The reasoning: {\i she\u8217'ein libo gas bah} \u8212- without the prior intimacy of a consummated marriage, we cannot presume that an incidental overnight encounter would naturally lead to intercourse. The debate between the two schools thus concerns not the requirement for witnesses, but the evidentiary and presumptive weight to be assigned to what those witnesses actually saw.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Kiddushin 65\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Chazakah & Yibum Belief\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Abaye\u8217's Clarification\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The daf opens mid-{\i sugya}, carrying over from the previous discussion on 64b. The {\i mishna} had established that a husband is believed when he says on his deathbed that he has sons or no brothers \u8212- statements that would free his wife from {\i yibum} \u8212- but is not believed when he says the opposite, claiming he has brothers or no sons, which would obligate her in {\i yibum}. The rule appeared to be that he is believed to free her but not to bind her, without an explicit rationale. The {\i gemara} then cited a {\i braita} quoting Rebbe to that effect \u8212- {\i ne\u8217'eman lehatir ve\u8217'eino ne\u8217'eman le\u8217'asor} \u8212- and contrasted it with the position of Rebbe Natan, who held that the husband is believed both ways, at least in the scenario where he had said the freeing statement at the time of {\i kiddushin} and retracted it on his deathbed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye now clarifies the underlying logic. Our {\i mishna}, he says, is dealing with a case where {\i de\u8217'lo muchzak lan b\u8217'achi v\u8217'lo muchzak lan bivani} \u8212- we have no established presumption that he has brothers or sons. Given this starting point, if he says he has children, he is believed, because his statement reinforces the {\i chazakah} \u8212- our baseline presumption that she is {\i mutar}, not obligated in {\i yibum}. If he says he has brothers, he is not believed \u8212- {\i lav kol kimeinei de\u8217'asar lah akoli alma} \u8212- it is not within his power to make her forbidden to the world on his say-so alone.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Status Quo Principle\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The essential principle that emerges is not that the husband is believed to produce a {\i heter} and disbelieved to produce an {\i issur} in the abstract. Rather, the {\i gemara} frames it in terms of the {\i chazakah}: a person is believed when his statement is consistent with the prevailing presumption, and is not believed when his statement contradicts it. If what he says reinforces her status of being {\i mutar}, of course we believe him. If she already has a {\i chazakah} of being {\i mutar} and he now claims she is {\i assur}, he is not believed \u8212- he does not have the power, through his bare assertion, to override an established presumption and render his wife obligated in {\i yibum}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i braita} involving Rebbe Natan addresses a different factual configuration: {\i de\u8217'muchzak lan b\u8217'achi v\u8217'lo muchzak lan bivani} \u8212- we had reason to presume he had brothers, though no sons were known. In this setting, the woman was presumed obligated in {\i yibum} before the marriage. He then said at the time of {\i kiddushin} that he had no brothers, and the {\i gemara} considers why we believed him then, even against the prevailing presumption.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rebbe Natan Reconciliation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The answer is {\i mah lo leshaker}: why would he lie? One might think he was lying simply to induce her to marry him, promising she would not be bound by {\i yibum}. But that reasoning fails, because {\i matsi patarna lach b\u8217'gita} \u8212- he could simply have promised to give her a {\i get} that would take effect before his death, rendering the {\i yibum} question moot regardless. Since he had an alternative means of achieving the same practical result, there is no compelling motive for the lie, and therefore we believe him at the time of {\i kiddushin}, even to the point of shifting the {\i chazakah} in her favor.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mah Lo Leshaker Logic\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is the critical juncture. The first {\i mah lo leshaker} \u8212- his statement at the time of {\i kiddushin} \u8212- was powerful enough that Rebbe holds it was {\i k\u8217'eidim dami}, like the testimony of witnesses, and {\i asu eidim akrei chazakah} \u8212- it completely uprooted the prior {\i chazakah}. She is now fully presumed to be free of any {\i yibum} obligation. Then, on his deathbed, he says the opposite: he has brothers after all. Now Rebbe and Rebbe Natan divide.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rebbe holds that the first {\i mah lo leshaker} was so powerful that it totally shifted the presumption. She is now presumed {\i mutar}, and he cannot overturn that new {\i chazakah} on his deathbed with a bare claim \u8212- especially since the {\i mah lo leshaker} logic does not apply in reverse. At the deathbed, why would he lie? One cannot say he had no alternative means to bind her; perhaps he is simply trying, in his final hours, to make her life difficult. There is no analogous argument eliminating his motive to lie, and therefore his retraction carries no weight against the established presumption.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rebbe Natan disagrees. He holds the first {\i mah lo leshaker} was {\i k\u8217'chazakah dami} \u8212- it was like a {\i chazakah}, sufficient to neutralize the prior presumption, but not sufficient to establish a new one in the opposite direction \u8212- {\i v\u8217'lo atei chazakah v\u8217'akrei chazakah l\u8217'gamrei}. The original presumption was not completely erased, only suspended. When, on his deathbed, he reverts to the original presumption, he is in effect reinforcing what was there to begin with, and Rebbe Natan believes him.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mah Lo Leshaker Context\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara}\u8217's larger move, as one steps back, is to neutralize what appeared to be an absolute position of Rebbe Natan \u8212- that the husband is simply believed to make his wife obligated in {\i yibum}. That is not what Rebbe Natan says at all. Even according to Rebbe Natan, if the woman has a full {\i chazakah} of being {\i mutar}, the husband is not believed on his bare word to make her {\i assur}. The only case where Rebbe Natan believes him is precisely because his deathbed statement is reinforcing \u8212- even if only partially \u8212- a pre-existing {\i chazakah} that was never fully extinguished. The {\i mishna} and Rebbe Natan are thus not fundamentally at odds.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The broader application of {\i mah lo leshaker} \u8212- which functions here similarly to {\i migo}, though the {\i rishonim} debate the precise relationship between the two concepts \u8212- is that it can shift a {\i chazakah} but only within limits defined by whether it truly eliminates a plausible motive to deceive. At the time of {\i kiddushin}, the logic is compelling. On the deathbed, it is not \u8212- and without it, the man\u8217's statement cannot prevail against whatever {\i chazakah} is in place.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Unspecified Daughters\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Adult vs.\u160 ?Underage\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now turns to a new {\i mishna} concerning cases where a father marries off his daughter without specifying which daughter he means. The {\i mishna} states that {\i hamakadesh et bito stam} \u8212- one who betroths his daughter in general, without designation \u8212- {\i ein babogres bechlal}: the statement does not encompass daughters who have already reached the age of {\i bogeres}, i.e., who are more than twelve and a half years old and fully mature, because the father no longer has the halachic authority to betroth an adult daughter. The {\i gemara} will probe what this is teaching us and then turn to the more complex cases.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} asks: if there are multiple underage daughters, are they all potentially betrothed by his unspecified statement? This raises the issue of {\i kiddushin she\u8217'einam mesurin l\u8217'viah} \u8212- a {\i kiddushin} that can never culminate in consummation, because the husband would never know which daughter he actually married, and any act with one might be a violation with the sister of his actual wife. The {\i gemara} resolves this by saying the {\i mishna} is discussing a case where the father has only one underage daughter and one adult daughter \u8212- {\i ein sham ela gedola u\u8217'ketana}. The use of the plural form referring to adult daughters is not an indication that this specific father has multiple adult daughters; it simply describes the general class of daughters in such matters.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Father\u8217's Priority\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But if there is only one underage daughter and one adult daughter, and the father is empowered to betroth both \u8212- since, as the {\i gemara} considers, the adult daughter may have appointed him as her {\i shaliach} \u8212- why assume the unspecified betrothal refers to the younger one rather than the older?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} offers two reasons. First, {\i lo shavik inish midi d\u8217'isleih hana\u8217'ah minei v\u8217'avid midi d\u8217'lo isleih hana\u8217'ah minei} \u8212- a person does not abandon what yields him benefit and act instead on what yields him none. When a father betroths his underage daughter, he retains the {\i kesef kiddushin}; when he betroths his adult daughter, the money belongs to her. Even if the adult daughter waived her share as compensation for his efforts as {\i shaliach}, a second consideration applies: {\i lo shavik inish mitzvah d\u8217'ramya alei v\u8217'avid mitzvah d\u8217'lo ramya alei} \u8212- he would not set aside an obligation incumbent upon him in favor of one that is not. While his daughter is a minor, she is under his guardianship and it is his responsibility \u8212- indeed his {\i mitzvah} \u8212- to ensure she is married and protected. The Torah envisions a girl passing directly from {\i beis aviha} to {\i beis ba\u8217'alah}; the rabbis carve out a space for the adult woman who has not yet married, but that scenario does not exist in the Torah\u8217's own framing. The father\u8217's presumptive priority is therefore to marry off his younger daughter, and the unspecified statement is read accordingly.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two Groups Ambiguity\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} then introduces the more elaborate case: a man had daughters from two wives \u8212- sequentially, not simultaneously \u8212- and thus has two groups of daughters, an older cohort and a younger cohort. He says he betrothed {\i biti hagedola} \u8212- his older or oldest daughter \u8212- and then cannot remember which daughter he meant. The {\i mishna} enumerates the possibilities: did he mean the oldest of the older group? The older of the younger group? Or the youngest of the older group, who is nonetheless older than the oldest of the younger group?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 According to Rabbi Meir, all of the daughters are {\i asuros} \u8212- forbidden to all other men until the matter is clarified \u8212- except for the youngest of the younger group, {\i chutz min haketana shebaketanos}, because no interpretation of the word {\i gedola} could possibly apply to her. According to Rabbi Yosi, all are {\i mutaros} except for the oldest of the oldest group, {\i chutz min hagedola shebagedolos}, because when a person uses the word {\i oldest} they mean it in its most precise and unambiguous sense.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The case is then presented in reverse: he said {\i biti haketana}, my younger or youngest daughter, and cannot remember which he meant. Rabbi Meir says all are forbidden except the oldest of the oldest group; Rabbi Yosi says all are permitted except the youngest of the youngest group.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Meir vs.\u160 ?Yosi\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} asks whether it is necessary to state both cases \u8212- the {\i gedola} case and the {\i ketana} case. It concludes that both are necessary. If we only knew the {\i gedola} case, one might think Rabbi Meir holds his position only there \u8212- because any daughter who is older than someone else can be called {\i gedola} \u8212- but that when the father says {\i ketana}, surely he means the unambiguous youngest, and even Rabbi Meir would agree with Rabbi Yosi. Conversely, if we only knew the {\i ketana} case, one might think Rabbi Yosi holds his position only there, but that for {\i gedola} \u8212- a more positive and freely used descriptor \u8212- even Rabbi Yosi might agree that it could refer to a wider range, aligning with Rabbi Meir. Therefore both cases must be stated, showing that they debate both.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The underlying question is whether people speak or remember in ways that create ambiguity. Rabbi Meir holds that they do \u8212- {\i machnis inish nafshei l\u8217'safeka} \u8212- a person will record a memory as \u8220"oldest\u8221" even if that word does not uniquely identify the intended person. Rabbi Yosi holds the opposite \u8212- {\i lo machnis inish nafshei l\u8217'safeka} \u8212- a person would not characterize their memory with a term that leaves them in a state of genuine doubt; if he remembers {\i oldest}, he means it in its most determinate sense.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Abaye\u8217's Two-Group Theory\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i braita} then presents an apparently reversed debate concerning {\i nedarim}: regarding the {\i neder} formulation {\i ad p\u8217'nei hapesach} \u8212- until the face of {\i Pesach} \u8212- Rabbi Meir says it means until {\i Pesach} arrives and Rabbi Yosi says it means until {\i Pesach} departs, which appears to be the exact inverse of their positions in the {\i mishna}. Rabbi Chanina bar Avdimi cites Rav: {\i muchlefet hashita} \u8212- the names are inverted in one source, and a {\i braita} indeed presents the {\i Pesach} positions consistently with the {\i mishna}, so that the same underlying logic governs in both cases.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye then provides an important clarification of the {\i mishna} itself: {\i machlokes bishtei kitei banot} \u8212- the dispute between Rabbi Meir and Rabbi Yosi applies specifically to the case where there are two distinct groups of daughters. In that situation, any daughter from the older group can be described as {\i gedola} because she belongs to {\i hakat ha-gedola}, the older set, and thus the label is not anchored solely to the single oldest individual. This is what opens the possibility, under Rabbi Meir\u8217's reading, that multiple daughters could fall under the description.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 By contrast, says Abaye, if there were only one group \u8212- say, four daughters from a single wife \u8212- {\i amtza\u8217'is bishmah kari lah}: the middle daughters would simply be called middle daughters. {\i Gedola} would refer unambiguously to the oldest, and {\i ketana} unambiguously to the youngest. The ambiguity Rabbi Meir identifies arises only because the two-group structure licenses a broader use of the term: she is {\i gedola} meaning she is from the older group, not that she is the single oldest individual.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Adda Challenge\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Adda bar Matna challenges Abaye: if his theory depends on the two-group structure, what happens if there are three daughters in the younger group rather than two? According to Abaye, the word {\i gedola} in the context of the younger group should attach only to the oldest daughter within that younger group, since she is the one who can be labeled {\i gedola} as its leading member. But then the {\i mishna} should permit the middle daughter of the younger group \u8212- why does it list her among the forbidden ones?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye\u8217's response is that the {\i mishna} is indeed speaking of a case where there are only two daughters per group \u8212- {\i ein sham ela gedola u-ketana} \u8212- and this is further confirmed by the internal logic of the {\i mishna} itself: if there were three daughters in the younger group, the {\i mishna} would have needed to mention ambiguity about the middle daughter of that group, yet it does not. The {\i mishna}\u8217's silence on that possibility is itself evidence that no such middle daughter exists in the case under discussion.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then presses: if that argument from silence works for the younger group, it should equally prove the absence of a middle daughter in the older group. The response is that the two situations are not parallel. In the older group, the {\i mishna} explicitly states that both the oldest and the youngest of that group are {\i assur} \u8212- so of course any middle daughter there is also {\i assur} and need not be separately listed. In the younger group, by contrast, the {\i mishna} says only the oldest daughter of that group is {\i assur}, implying that anyone younger within that group is {\i mutar} \u8212- so if a middle daughter existed, she would need to be mentioned as {\i assur}, and her absence from the list demonstrates she does not exist.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Pesach Neder Parallel\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Huna the son of Rav Yehoshua raises a challenge to Abaye from the {\i Pesach} {\i neder} parallel: the case of {\i ad p\u8217'nei hapesach} was presented as analogous to the daughters debate, but if Abaye is correct that the daughters debate turns on the two-group structure, the {\i Pesach} case does not fit that framework. The days of {\i Pesach} are one continuous sequence \u8212- one group \u8212- not two distinct groups. So why should any intermediate day be understood as a potential {\i face} of {\i Pesach} that creates ambiguity?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} responds that the {\i Pesach} debate is not actually about the same question at all. It is not a question of whether language creates ambiguity by being applicable to multiple members of a group; it is a linguistic dispute about the word {\i pnei} itself. One opinion holds {\i ad p\u8217'nei hapesach} means {\i ad kamei Pesach} \u8212- until before {\i Pesach}, i.e., until it arrives. The other holds it derives from {\i lipanot}, to turn away or depart \u8212- the Aramaic {\i mipanei}, to pass \u8212- meaning until {\i Pesach} has concluded. The debate is purely about the semantic range of the word {\i pnei}, not about the structure of groups or the breadth of ambiguous descriptors. The {\i Pesach} case and the daughters case thus involve distinct conceptual issues, and the apparent parallel between the two debates should not be overstated.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 He Said/She Said\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 He Says/She Denies\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now turns to a new {\i mishna} presenting a series of conflicting claims about whether a betrothal occurred. The core principle governing all the cases is that a person\u8217's statement binds himself but cannot, through his words alone, obligate another person.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 In the first case: a man says to a woman {\i kidashtich} \u8212- I betrothed you \u8212- and she says {\i lo kidashtani} \u8212- you did not betroth me. The rule is {\i hu assur bikrovoteha v\u8217'hi muteres bikrovav}: he is forbidden to marry her close female relatives \u8212- her mother, sister, daughter \u8212- since his own statement binds him, while she is free to marry his relatives, because his unilateral assertion cannot impose a prohibited status upon her.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 She Says/He Denies\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 In the reverse case: she says {\i kidashtani} \u8212- you betrothed me \u8212- and he says {\i lo kidashtich} \u8212- I did not. He is free to marry her relatives, since he made no claim. She, however, is forbidden to his relatives \u8212- and indeed, more basically, she is forbidden to any other man entirely, since she herself is asserting that she is married. Her own statement binds her, but it cannot impose any restriction upon him.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Daughter vs.\u160 ?Mother\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The third case: he says {\i kidashtich} to the mother, and she says {\i lo kidashta ela et biti} \u8212- you did not marry me, you married my daughter. He is forbidden to the mother\u8217's relatives \u8212- since he claimed the mother \u8212- but the mother is free to marry his relatives, since she does not claim to be married to him. He is permitted to marry the daughter\u8217's relatives, since he never claimed to have married her. And the daughter \u8212- {\i haktana muteres bikrovav} \u8212- the daughter is free to marry his relatives, because the mother\u8217's claim that the daughter is married to him does not bind the daughter.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mother vs.\u160 ?Daughter\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The fourth case is the mirror image: he says {\i kidashti et bitchich} \u8212- I married your daughter \u8212- and the mother says {\i lo kidashta ela oti} \u8212- no, you married me. He is forbidden to the daughter\u8217's relatives, since he claimed her. The daughter is free to marry his relatives, since no claim was made about her and she made none about herself. He is free to marry the mother\u8217's relatives, since he never claimed her. The mother is forbidden to his relatives, since she claims to be married to him.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Low Cost Principle\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} asks why it is necessary to state both the case where he makes a claim and the case where she makes a claim \u8212- are they not obvious? The response is that one might have thought differently in each direction. When the man says he married a woman, one could argue: {\i gavra lo ichpat lei u\u8217'mikrey amar} \u8212- for him, the cost of such a claim is so low that he might simply say it casually, without it being a sincere or reliable statement. After all, during {\i kiddushin} \u8212- as opposed to {\i nisu\u8217'in} \u8212- the betrothal does not immediately impose an obligation of {\i ketubah}, and the man\u8217's restriction is primarily limited to her close relatives. The {\i gemara} teaches us that even so, his statement is taken seriously and does bind him.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mother\u8217's Claim Invalid\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Regarding the case where the mother claims the daughter is married: one might have thought that just as the Torah grants the father authority to effect and attest to the {\i kiddushin} of his minor daughter \u8212- {\i heimnu rachmana l\u8217'av} \u8212- the rabbis might extend analogous, if lesser, authority to the mother rabbinically, so that her claim would bind the daughter at least on a rabbinic level. The {\i mishna} teaches that this is not so. The mother is simply not believed with respect to the marriage status of her daughter \u8212- not biblically and not rabbinically \u8212- and her statement creates no prohibition upon the daughter at all.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Get Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then records a dispute between Rav and Shmuel concerning the second case \u8212- where she claims he married her and he denies it. Shmuel says {\i m\u8217'vakshin mimenu litein get} \u8212- we ask him to give a {\i get} as a matter of good will, since her own statement has rendered her unable to marry any other man, and a {\i get} would release her. Rav adds: {\i im natan get mei\u8217'atzmo} \u8212- if he gives a {\i get} on his own initiative, unprompted by the court\u8217's request, {\i kofin oto litein ketubah} \u8212- we treat that act as an admission that they were married and compel him to pay her {\i ketubah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} initially reads these as conflicting opinions and asks how one can {\i force} him to give a {\i get}, since writing a {\i get} would make her his divorcee and prevent him from marrying her relatives \u8212- a concrete cost to him that he might legitimately resist. The resolution is that Rav and Shmuel are not contradicting each other but building upon one another \u8212- {\i sh\u8217'maita d\u8217'adu adadei} \u8212- Shmuel establishes that we ask, and Rav adds that if he gives spontaneously, that is taken as an admission, with the consequence that the {\i ketubah} becomes enforceable. The practical lesson that emerges is that a man in this situation would be well advised to wait for the court\u8217's formal request before giving the {\i get}, rather than acting voluntarily, so that his act of generosity is not construed as a legal admission.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Witnesses Requirement\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 One vs.\u160 ?Two Witnesses\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now arrives at what may be the most consequential legal question in the entire tractate, one that has taken sixty-four {\i dappim} to reach: whether the presence of kosher witnesses is constitutive of {\i kiddushin} \u8212- whether without such witnesses the act of {\i kiddushin} simply does not take effect as a matter of {\i halacha}, even if everyone present acknowledges that the betrothal occurred.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Yehuda states: {\i hamikadesh b\u8217'ed echad ein chosheshin lekiddushav} \u8212- if a man betroths a woman before only one witness, the {\i kiddushin} is not binding and we have no concern that it took effect.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Constitutive Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This formulation immediately prompts a question that was posed to Rav Yehuda himself: {\i shnayim hamodim mai} \u8212- what is the rule when both parties agree? The question is probing the theoretical foundation of the witness requirement. Is the requirement evidentiary \u8212- do we need witnesses to prove that the betrothal occurred? Or is it constitutive \u8212- do we need witnesses in order for the act of {\i kiddushin} to achieve legal standing at all, irrespective of whether anyone doubts what happened? If both parties agree, and a single witness corroborates them, there is no evidentiary question \u8212- the act is not in doubt. If the {\i kiddushin} is still invalid in that case, it must be because witnesses are constitutive. Rav Yehuda\u8217's answer on this point was {\i rafya bidei} \u8212- it was unresolved in his hand, and he could not give a decisive ruling.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The question is then resolved by Rav Nachman citing Shmuel: {\i hamikadesh b\u8217'ed echad ein chosheshin lekiddushav v\u8217'afilu shneihem modim} \u8212- even if both parties fully agree that the betrothal occurred, without two witnesses it is not binding. The requirement for witnesses is constitutive, not merely evidentiary.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava\u8217's Challenge\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava challenges this ruling from the {\i mishna} we have just studied \u8212- the {\i he said/she said} cases. That {\i mishna} discusses scenarios where one party claims a betrothal occurred and the other denies it, with no mention of witnesses at all. If witnesses are constitutive \u8212- if without two witnesses there is simply no {\i kiddushin} \u8212- then the {\i mishna} makes no sense. If witnesses were present, how is anyone {\i mutar}? The witnesses themselves establish the marriage. If witnesses were not present, how is anyone {\i assur}? There is no binding {\i kiddushin} without them.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It must therefore be the case, argues Rava, that the {\i mishna} is operating in a scenario with one witness \u8212- and with one witness combined with one party\u8217's admission, that combination is sufficient to generate at least a partial status. So it appears that one witness plus an admission does constitute {\i kiddushin}, which would contradict Rav Nachman\u8217's ruling in the name of Shmuel.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Witnesses Presumed\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} resolves this by re-reading the {\i mishna}: {\i kegon de\u8217'amar lah kidashtich bifnei ploni u\u8217'ploni vehalchu lahem lemedinas hayam} \u8212- the {\i mishna} is discussing a case where the man says \u8220"I betrothed you in the presence of so-and-so and so-and-so,\u8221" explicitly claiming that two witnesses were present, even though those witnesses are not here to testify now. The {\i mishna} does not depict a situation where there were objectively no witnesses; rather, it depicts a situation of contested claims where witnesses are alleged but inaccessible.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This reading has sweeping implications for everything that came before. All the {\i mishnayos} throughout the tractate that discuss who is believed about a betrothal \u8212- the father\u8217's claim about his daughter, the man\u8217's claim at the time of {\i kiddushin}, all the {\i he said/she said} cases \u8212- must be understood as cases where witnesses are claimed to have been present, even though they are not being produced at the moment. Without that background assumption, none of those cases are legally coherent. The {\i gemara} has therefore quietly transformed the interpretive framework for the entire preceding discussion.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Beis Shammai/Hillel\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then tests Rav Nachman\u8217's ruling against a {\i braita} about a man who divorced his wife and later shared lodgings with her at an inn. The question is whether we are concerned that they renewed their {\i kiddushin} through intercourse \u8212- {\i kiddushei biah}. Beis Shammai say no second {\i get} is required; Beis Hillel say one is.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} initially suggests that this debate might hinge on the testimony of a single witness, but quickly recognizes a much simpler explanation: {\i hacha b\u8217'mai askinan k\u8217'gon d\u8217'ika eidei yichud v\u8217'leika eidei biah} \u8212- the case involves witnesses to {\i yichud}, to the fact that the two entered seclusion together, but no witnesses to intercourse itself. The debate between Beis Shammai and Beis Hillel is not about the numerical sufficiency of witnesses but about the legal weight of {\i eidei yichud}: do witnesses to seclusion function as witnesses to intercourse \u8212- {\i hen hen eidei yichud hen hen eidei biah} \u8212- or not?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Beis Shammai hold that {\i eidei yichud} do not constitute {\i eidei biah}, and therefore no new {\i kiddushin} has been established. Beis Hillel hold that when the couple has a prior history of marital intercourse, witnesses to their entering seclusion are treated as equivalent to witnesses to intercourse, and a new {\i kiddushin} through {\i biah} has thereby taken effect, requiring a second {\i get}. Both schools agree, however, that if the couple had never consummated their original marriage \u8212- {\i niseis min ha\u8217'erusin} \u8212- {\i eidei yichud} would not be treated as {\i eidei biah}, because {\i ein libo gassah bah}: without the prior intimacy, we cannot presume they acted upon the seclusion.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} concludes that this entire {\i braita} has nothing to do with the question of one versus two witnesses, and therefore poses no challenge to Rav Nachman\u8217's ruling. The question of how many witnesses are required for {\i kiddushin} to be constitutive remains firmly established as requiring two \u8212- and the question of what those witnesses must have observed is a separate matter, one that the debate between Beis Hillel and Beis Shammai illuminates. That discussion will continue on the following {\i daf}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Kiddushin 65b\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Witnesses & Kiddushin\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 One Witness & Agreement\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The central question animating this sugya is whether witnesses play a merely evidentiary role in {\i kiddushin} \u8212- that is, whether they are needed only to prove that the betrothal occurred \u8212- or whether they are constitutive of the act itself, such that without witnesses, the {\i kiddushin} simply did not take place. A further dimension of this question concerns whether one witness might suffice when both parties acknowledge that the {\i kiddushin} occurred. If the couple themselves agree, one might reason that the evidentiary function is already satisfied by their mutual admission, rendering a second witness superfluous.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav & Rabbah Rulings\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} opens with a clear and emphatic ruling. In the name of Rav, Rav Yitzchak bar Shmuel bar Marta states: if a man performs {\i kiddushin} with only one witness, {\i ein chosheshin lekidushav} \u8212- there is no concern whatsoever that any {\i kiddushin} has taken place. Crucially, this applies {\i va\u8217'afilu shenehem modim} \u8212- even if both parties agree that the betrothal occurred. The mutual admission of husband and wife cannot substitute for the requirement of two witnesses.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbah bar Rav Huna then repeats this ruling, attributing it to {\i Bei Dina Rabba} \u8212- \u8220"the great court.\u8221" The {\i gemara} asks: who is this great court? The answer is Rav himself. An alternative version of the tradition has Rabbah bar Rav Huna citing Rav directly, in which case {\i Bei Dina Rabba} refers to Rebbi. In either formulation, an authority of the highest stature stands behind this position, and its force extends even to the rabbinic level \u8212- that is, it is not merely a {\i de\u8217'oraita} invalidation. This matters enormously in practice, because any requirement of a {\i get} carries grave consequences for the woman, and the rabbis were deeply reluctant to impose one without firm grounding.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two Men, Woman Bundle\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A challenge is raised from the following scenario: two men arrive from overseas together with a woman and a bundle of belongings. Each man claims the woman as his wife, the other man as his slave, and the bundle as his property. The woman, for her part, claims both men as her slaves and the bundle as hers. The ruling is that she requires two {\i gittin} and may collect her {\i ketubah} from the bundle.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The immediate question is why she should need a {\i get} from either man, given that he is merely making a claim without witnessed proof. The {\i gemara} investigates: if each man had witnesses to his marriage, her counterclaim would carry no weight against their testimony. It therefore concludes that the case must involve one witness per claimant \u8212- and since one witness for one man is effectively cancelled by one witness for the other, we are back to the position of mutual, unwitnessed claims. {\i Ela le-mishrei le-alma de-kulei alma lo peligi de-sharya} \u8212- to permit her to the general public, everyone agrees she is free. The requirement of two {\i gittin}, then, is not a genuine {\i halachic} demand but a practical mechanism: only if one of the men voluntarily gives a {\i get} would she be entitled to collect her {\i ketubah} from the contested bundle. The {\i chiddush} is that of Rabbi Meir, who holds that movable property ({\i metaltelin}) is subject to the {\i ketubah} lien. The case thus does not prove that one witness effects {\i kiddushin}; rather, the absence of any objective proof means that no {\i get} is truly required, and the question reduces entirely to the disposition of the assets.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A final practical note: the {\i gemara} is very reluctant, absent clear necessity, to declare that a woman requires a {\i get}, given the devastating consequences in practice \u8212- the contemporary reality of {\i agunah} being the most acute expression of that concern.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Proof vs.\u160 ?Constitutive\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kahana vs.\u160 ?Pappa\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now asks {\i mai havya alah} \u8212- what is the bottom line of all this analysis? Two positions emerge. Rav Kahana rules {\i ein chosheshin lekiddushin} \u8212- there is no concern at all; {\i kiddushin} performed with a single witness is entirely without effect. Rav Pappa rules {\i chosheshin lekiddushin} \u8212- he is still somewhat concerned, perhaps at the level of rabbinic stringency.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Ashi\u8217's Challenge\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Ashi presses Rav Kahana: what is the source for his confident ruling that a single witness effects no {\i kiddushin} whatsoever? The most natural source would be a {\i gezeirah shava}: the Torah uses the word {\i davar} in the phrase {\i al pi shenei edim yakum davar} \u8212- testimony is established by two witnesses \u8212- and the same word {\i davar} appears in the phrase {\i ki matza bah ervat davar}, which concerns marital and personal status matters. From this verbal analogy, one might derive that the two-witness requirement applies to {\i davar she-be-erva} \u8212- matters of personal status \u8212- just as it applies to monetary matters.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But here the challenge bites: by monetary matters, the rule is {\i hoda\u8217'at ba\u8217'al din keme\u8217'ah edim dami} \u8212- a litigant\u8217's own admission is equivalent to a hundred witnesses. The debt exists whether or not there were witnesses present; the witnesses serve only to prove it in court. If the need for two witnesses in {\i kiddushin} is derived from the monetary domain, why not draw the same inference \u8212- that if both parties admit the {\i kiddushin} occurred, their admission should function like witnesses, and the {\i kiddushin} should be valid?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Money vs.\u160 ?Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The answer given is fundamental: {\i hatam lo kachaiv l\u8217'achrinei hacha kachaiv l\u8217'achrinei} \u8212- there is a crucial difference. When a borrower admits a debt, he harms only himself. His admission is therefore fully effective. But when a husband and wife admit to a {\i kiddushin}, they are harming others. The husband\u8217's admission restricts the wife vis-\u224 ?-vis all other men; the wife\u8217's admission restricts the husband from her relatives; and other men in the world are now prohibited from marrying her under pain of adultery. Matters of personal status have societal ramifications that reach far beyond the admitting parties, and therefore personal admission \u8212- {\i hoda\u8217'at ba\u8217'al din} \u8212- cannot substitute for objective, external testimony.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Yet one might observe that this argument proves only that admission is insufficient as proof; it does not yet prove that witnesses are constitutive \u8212- that without them, the act did not occur at all. The gap between \u8220"we require a higher standard of proof\u8221" and \u8220"without witnesses, nothing happened\u8221" is a substantial one, and it is a point that troubles the {\i acharonim}. The {\i gemara} itself, they note, never fully bridges that gap.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Edei Beirur Kiyum\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This brings us to the conceptual heart of the discussion: the distinction between {\i edei beirur} and {\i edei kiyum}. {\i Edei beirur} \u8212- proof witnesses \u8212- serve only to establish in the eyes of the court that something happened. If everyone is honest and the fact is admitted, witnesses of this type are redundant. {\i Edei kiyum} \u8212- constitutive witnesses \u8212- do something altogether different: they make the act happen. Without their presence, the act is a legal nullity, regardless of what the parties intended or admit.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 By monetary matters, witnesses are {\i edei beirur}. The obligation exists independently of whether anyone witnessed it; the witnesses merely allow us to enforce it. By {\i kiddushin} and {\i gittin}, witnesses are {\i edei kiyum} \u8212- without them, the marriage or divorce did not occur.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 There is, however, a further debate among the {\i acharonim} about precisely how to conceptualize the {\i edei kiyum} function. One view holds that witnesses are still fundamentally evidentiary, but because {\i kiddushin} is {\i chav l\u8217'achrinei} \u8212- it impacts broader society \u8212- a society that cannot verify its occurrence cannot recognize it. In the absence of proof, the act did not, as a social-legal matter, happen. A second view holds that the witnesses do something categorically different: they formalize and give gravity to the act. Just as one cannot be executed without witnesses and {\i hatra\u8217'ah}, the weight and solemnity of {\i kiddushin} demands the formal presence of witnesses as a constitutive element of the act.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i nafka mina} between these two views is whether witnesses hidden behind a bush \u8212- unknown to the bride and groom \u8212- would suffice. If witnesses are fundamentally there to prove that the act occurred, then hidden witnesses accomplish exactly that. If, however, witnesses must be part of formalizing the act and impressing its gravity upon the parties, their concealment might be fatal. The {\i Ktzot} in {\i Choshen Mishpat} engages this question extensively, drawing on a {\i machloket Rishonim} about precisely this scenario.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Property Division\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Mar Zutra and Rav Adda Sava, the sons of Rav Mari bar Issur, divided their inherited property between themselves without witnesses. They came before Rav Ashi with a question: does {\i al pi shnei edim yakum davar} \u8212- the Torah\u8217's requirement of two witnesses \u8212- mean that the division is simply invalid without witnesses? Or is the entire function of witnesses in monetary matters only to prevent subsequent denial, and since neither party intends to back out, the division stands?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Abaye Liars Principle\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye answered them with the formulation that became the classic expression of the {\i edei beirur} principle: {\i lo ivru sahadei ella leshakrei} \u8212- witnesses were created ({\i nivre\u8217'u}) only for liars. The entire purpose of witnesses in monetary matters is to prevent dishonest parties from denying what occurred. In a world of honest parties who both affirm what happened, witnesses add nothing and are not required to validate the act. This is {\i edei beirur} in its purest form. And the implicit contrast with {\i kiddushin} and {\i gittin} could not be sharper: there, the witnesses are {\i edei kiyum}, and their absence means the act never happened, honest parties notwithstanding.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might draw an interesting parallel to {\i malkut} and capital punishment, which similarly require both witnesses and {\i hatra\u8217'ah}. There, too, {\i hoda\u8217'at ba\u8217'al din} does not work. Whether the witnesses and {\i hatra\u8217'ah} in those cases are there to establish guilt evidentiary or to constitute the particular character of a defiant, warned transgression is a parallel conceptual question \u8212- one that mirrors the {\i edei beirur/edei kiyum} debate and is left here as a worthy avenue for further study.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 One Witness & Silence\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Forbidden Fat Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye now introduces a series of cases in which a single witness is believed when the person he testifies against remains silent. The first: one witness says to a person, {\i achalta cheilev} \u8212- you ate forbidden fat ({\i chelev}) inadvertently. The person says nothing. The {\i gemara} rules: {\i ne\u8217'eman} \u8212- the witness is believed, and the person would be obligated to bring a {\i korban}. A supporting {\i baraita} confirms this: if the accused says {\i lo achalti} \u8212- I did not eat it \u8212- he is exempt. The {\i ta\u8217'ama de\u8217'amar lo} \u8212- the reason is because he denied it. But {\i ha ishtik meheiman} \u8212- if he was silent, the witness is believed and the {\i korban} is required.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Grain Purity Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye continues: one witness says to a person, {\i nitme\u8217'u taharotecha} \u8212- your grain has become {\i tamei}. The person is silent. The witness is believed, and the grain may not be eaten in a state of {\i tahara}. Again, a parallel {\i baraita} is cited: if one witness says the grain became {\i tamei} and the owner contradicts him, the grain is {\i tahor}. The reason is because he said otherwise; had he been silent, the single witness would have been believed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ox Bestiality Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A third case: one witness says {\i shorcha nirva} \u8212- your ox was used in an act of bestiality. The owner is silent. The witness is believed, and the ox is disqualified from being brought as a {\i korban}. Note that without two witnesses, the ox would not be stoned \u8212- the capital penalty for the animal requires two witnesses \u8212- but the prohibition on bringing it as a {\i korban} is triggered even by a single witness coupled with the owner\u8217's silence. A {\i baraita} confirms: animals used in sin or that killed a person {\i al pi ed echad} \u8212- based on one witness \u8212- or {\i al pi ba\u8217'alim} \u8212- based on the owner\u8217's admission \u8212- are disqualified from the altar. The {\i gemara} asks: what does {\i al pi ed echad} mean if the owner already admitted? It must mean the owner was silent, and silence functions as admission.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Three Cases Analysis\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then performs a {\i tzrichuta} \u8212- explaining why all three cases needed to be stated. In the {\i chelev} case, silence is clearly an admission: {\i dei lav dekimlei benafshei de-avad} \u8212- if he truly did not know he had eaten the forbidden fat, he would not remain silent, because the consequence of his silence is that he must bring a {\i korban}. And no one would bring a {\i korban} they were not certain they were obligated to bring, since that would constitute {\i chullin ba-azarah} \u8212- bringing non-sacred animals into the Temple precincts in a sacred context. His silence thus unmistakably signals acknowledgment.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 In the grain case, one might think the silence is not admission \u8212- perhaps he reasons he will simply eat the grain on a day when he is {\i tamei} and suffer no loss, making his silence meaningless. The case therefore needed to be stated to show that since he does lose access to the grain on days when he is {\i tahor}, his silence is indeed an admission.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 In the ox case, one might think silence means nothing \u8212- perhaps he never intended to bring the ox as a {\i korban} anyway, so he sustains no real loss by remaining silent. That case also needed to be stated.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafot Silence Distinct\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This {\i tzrichuta} leads Tosafot to a crucial interpretive conclusion: the operative legal mechanism in all three cases is not that the single witness is believed in his own right, but that the owner\u8217's silence is read as an admission. The witness functions as the prompt for an admission; it is the admission itself that carries legal weight. A critical {\i nafka mina} follows: if the owner says {\i eini yode\u8217'a} \u8212- I don\u8217't know \u8212- this is not silence, and therefore not admission, and the witness would not be believed. Similarly, if the witness testifies about something that occurred in the owner\u8217's absence \u8212- where silence cannot possibly constitute acknowledgment \u8212- the witness would likewise not be believed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot also raises a broader question: given the principle of {\i ed echad ne\u8217'eman be-issurin} \u8212- a single witness is ordinarily believed regarding matters of prohibited status \u8212- why do these cases require the owner\u8217's silence at all? The answer seems to lie in the fact that all three cases involve a direct economic consequence to the owner of the property in question. This is qualitatively different from the general rule about {\i issurin}, where the witness is speaking about the status of an item without directly threatening the owner\u8217's assets. When a witness\u8217's testimony would force the owner to incur a loss \u8212- sacrificing an animal, discarding grain, losing the use of an ox \u8212- additional indicia of reliability are required, and the owner\u8217's own silence provides that assurance.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Personal Status & Witnes\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Wife Adultery Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now poses the question that the preceding analysis has been building toward: {\i ishto zinta be-ed echad veshotek} \u8212- a single witness tells a man that his wife committed adultery, and the husband remains silent. What is the law?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 To understand the stakes, a preliminary distinction must be drawn. If the husband himself declares that his wife committed adultery, the consequence is that he is bound by his own statement and must divorce her \u8212- but since he is exercising what is always his unilateral right to divorce, this is not considered {\i chav l\u8217'achrinei} in the relevant sense. He must, however, pay the {\i ketubah}, since there is no objective proof of the transgression. Now: what if a single witness makes this claim and the husband says nothing?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The single witness cannot be believed in the ordinary sense for matters of personal status. The principle of {\i ed echad ne\u8217'eman be-issurin} does not apply to {\i davar she-be-erva} \u8212- matters concerning marital and sexual status \u8212- which require two witnesses even for purposes of proof, not merely for the constitutive function discussed above.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Abaye Rava Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye rules {\i ne\u8217'eman} \u8212- the witness is believed, and the husband would be required to divorce his wife. Rava rules {\i eino ne\u8217'eman} \u8212- {\i havay davar shebe\u8217'ervah ve\u8217'ein davar shebe\u8217'ervah pachos mishnayim}: this is a matter of {\i ervah}, and matters of {\i ervah} require a minimum of two witnesses.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot explains the debate in terms of the distinction developed in the preceding section. Abaye reads the husband\u8217's silence as an admission. Since the husband is always personally bound by his own admission regarding his wife\u8217's fidelity, and since his silence functions as admission, the result is that he must divorce her \u8212- not because the external witness is believed as a formal matter of {\i davar she-be-erva}, but because the husband himself has constructively admitted the fact. Rava, by contrast, holds that the legal mechanism here is not silence-as-admission but the credibility of the external witness, and that credibility is simply insufficient for {\i davar she-be-erva}. Without two witnesses, the testimony cannot establish the wife\u8217's transgression, and the husband has no obligation to act.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Blind Tanna Story\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye adduces a compelling source for his position. He notes: {\i dehava samya dehava mesader masneisa kamei deMar Shmuel} \u8212- there was a blind man who served as Shmuel\u8217's {\i tanna}, memorizing and reciting the Tannaitic traditions in the {\i yeshiva}. One day this man was running late. Shmuel sent a messenger to fetch him. The messenger took one route to his house while the {\i tanna} arrived by another, so they did not meet. In the meantime, the messenger \u8212- having arrived at the {\i tanna}\u8217's house in his absence \u8212- witnessed the man\u8217's wife engaged in an act of infidelity. When the messenger returned to Shmuel and reported what he had seen, the {\i tanna} came before Shmuel and asked: must I divorce my wife?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This case is structurally revealing. The husband was not present and therefore could not have been silent in any meaningful sense. There is no question of {\i shtikat ke-hoda\u8217'ah} here. The entire question is about the weight of a single, unopposed witness in a matter of personal status, absent any admission. Shmuel ruled: {\i im meheman lach} \u8212- if you trust him, {\i zil apkah} \u8212- go divorce her; {\i va\u8217'i lo lo tipik} \u8212- if not, do not divorce her.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye reads {\i im meheman lach} as meaning: if you know him to be reliable in the sense of not being a {\i gazlan} \u8212- that is, if there is no specific reason to doubt him \u8212- then you treat an unopposed single witness as sufficient to compel divorce. This would be a striking ruling: even absent any admission, an unopposed {\i ed echad} can operate in a matter of {\i davar she-be-erva}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava Rebuttal\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava reads Shmuel\u8217's ruling entirely differently. {\i Im meheman lach ke-bei trei zil apkah} \u8212- if you trust him {\i as much as} two witnesses, then you must divorce her. This is not about the ordinary credibility of a single witness in the absence of contradiction. It is about a special and exceptional personal trust, equivalent to ironclad certainty, that this specific person would never lie. In the absence of that extraordinary trust, the testimony of a single witness in a matter of {\i davar she-be-erva} carries no legal weight whatsoever. The woman\u8217's marital status remains unchanged, and no divorce is required.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 King Yannai Narrative\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yannai Feast Scheme\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye now seeks a further proof-text, this time from a striking historical {\i baraita} \u8212- a passage that has a parallel in Josephus and has attracted the attention of historians. The {\i baraita} recounts: King Yannai went to the region of Kochlit in the wilderness and conquered sixty cities. Upon his triumphant return, he was filled with great joy and summoned all the sages of Israel. He said to them: our ancestors, when they were engaged in building the {\i Beit HaMikdash}, ate salted foods ({\i meluchim}) in their poverty \u8212- let us likewise eat salted foods in memory of them. In a striking contrast, these humble foods were served upon golden tables. There is a {\i mishibud lige\u8217'ulah} quality to the moment \u8212- a deliberate juxtaposition of former deprivation and present glory.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 At the feast, one man was present who is described in almost biblical literary language as a {\i latz}, a mocker \u8212- evil and scheming. His name was Elazar ben Po\u8217'ira. He approached King Yannai and said: {\i libam shel Perushim alecha} \u8212- the hearts of the Pharisees, meaning the sages, are against you. They do not support you.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yehuda Challenge\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Yannai asked what he should do to test this claim. Elazar advised him: wear the {\i tzitz} \u8212- the golden headplate of the {\i Kohen Gadol} \u8212- and observe the reaction of the sages. (The {\i gemara} notes in passing the debate between Rashi and Tosafot about whether a {\i Kohen Gadol} is permitted to wear {\i bigdei kehunah} outside the {\i Beit HaMikdash}, and whether the {\i tzitz} \u8212- which the Torah describes as to be worn {\i tamid}, always \u8212- carries a special dispensation to be worn continuously. Neither question is the {\i halachic} focus here.)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One elder at the feast, named Yehuda ben Gedidya, spoke up \u8212- not very astutely, as it turned out: {\i rav lecha keter malchut} \u8212- the crown of kingship is sufficient for you, Yannai. {\i Hanach keter kehunah lezar\u8217'o shel Aharon} \u8212- leave the crown of {\i kehunah} for the descendants of Aaron. The reason he offered was that there had been a rumor that Yannai\u8217's mother had been taken captive at Modi\u8217'im. If true, this would have disqualified her from her {\i kohen} husband, rendering Yannai a {\i chalal} \u8212- someone of compromised priestly lineage \u8212- and unfit to serve as {\i Kohen Gadol}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mother Captivity Rumor\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i baraita} states: {\i vayivukash hadavar velo nimtza} \u8212- the matter was investigated and nothing was found to substantiate it. The rumor had not been established. Nevertheless, Yehuda ben Gedidya\u8217's remark caused enormous offense, and {\i vayibadlu chachmei Yisrael beza\u8217'am} \u8212- the sages of Israel were separated, driven out in the king\u8217's fury. Perhaps their silence at Yehuda\u8217's outburst was read as complicity.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sages Destruction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Elazar ben Po\u8217'ira then pressed his advantage, saying to Yannai: if this were merely an ordinary person ({\i hedyot she-be-Yisrael}), what was done to Yehuda ben Gedidya would be appropriate. But you are a king and {\i Kohen Gadol} \u8212- is this the treatment you deserve? (The parallel in Josephus fills in the gap: the sages had imposed lashes on Yehuda ben Gedidya for {\i motzi shem ra}, and Elazar was arguing that this trivial penalty demonstrated the sages\u8217' fundamental disrespect for the king\u8217's dignity.)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 When Yannai asked what he should do, Elazar replied: {\i romsem} \u8212- destroy them. Yannai asked: but what will become of the Torah? Elazar answered: {\i harei keruchah umunachat bekeren zavit} \u8212- it is rolled up and sitting in the corner. Any person who wishes to study may come and study. The Torah scroll is still there; kill the sages and the Torah survives.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Nachman bar Yitzchak observes: {\i miyad nizreka bo apikorsus} \u8212- immediately a spirit of {\i apikorsus} took hold of Yannai. What was his {\i apikorsus}? That he should have responded: {\i tinach Torah shebikhtav, Torah she-be-al peh mai} \u8212- granted, we still have the Written Torah; but what of the Oral Torah? Who will transmit it? By remaining silent at Elazar\u8217's answer, Yannai implicitly accepted the premise that the Written Torah alone suffices \u8212- a denial, in effect, of the entire tradition of {\i Torah she-be-al peh}. It is worth noting that {\i apikorsus} in the {\i gemara} in Sanhedrin is specifically defined as {\i mevazeh talmidei chachamim} \u8212- contempt for the sages and their authority \u8212- which makes its deployment here particularly pointed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shimon Restoration\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Miyad} \u8212- immediately \u8212- {\i vayahargu kol chachmei Yisrael} \u8212- all the sages of Israel were killed. {\i Vehaya ha-olam mishtomem} \u8212- the world was desolate and bereft. This continued until Shimon ben Shatach \u8212- who had been hidden away, reportedly because he was the brother of Yannai\u8217's wife \u8212- emerged and {\i hechzir et ha-Torah le-yoshnah}, restored the Torah to its former state. Some connect this restoration to the tradition that Shimon ben Shatach was instrumental in establishing primary Jewish education, ensuring that all children would be educated from a young age \u8212- a fitting counterpoint to the catastrophic loss described here.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Evidence Analysis\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now asks: what exactly was the evidentiary situation regarding the rumor about Yannai\u8217's mother? The {\i baraita} says it was investigated and dismissed. But what kind of investigation was this?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If two witnesses had originally testified that she was taken captive, and then two witnesses contradicted them, the result should be a standoff \u8212- {\i mai chazit disomkhat ahanei somkhat ahanei} \u8212- why do we credit one pair over the other? Rashi suggests that even in a two-against-two standoff, Yannai\u8217's status might remain in doubt, because while the mother had a {\i chezkat kashrut} going in her favor, Yannai himself \u8212- as the child whose status is the operative question \u8212- may never have had an established presumption of priestly fitness to fall back on. But this is itself a difficult position, since our intuition is that two witnesses against two witnesses should simply neutralize one another.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye\u8217's reading of the story is that the evidence was one witness against one witness or one witness unopposed \u8212- and since an unopposed single witness is believed even in matters of {\i davar she-be-erva}, the rumor initially gained traction until it was directly contradicted.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Unopposed Witness Conclu\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava rejects Abaye\u8217's reading of the story entirely. He maintains {\i le\u8217'olam trei vetrei} \u8212- it was in fact two against two. The reason the claim was dismissed rather than left in doubt is explained by reference to the principle of {\i edei hazamah}: the second pair of witnesses did not simply testify to a different version of events, but rather told the first pair {\i atem einkem yecholim} \u8212- you could not have been there to see what you claim, because you were elsewhere with us. When witnesses are subjected to {\i hazamah} \u8212- a direct challenge to their physical presence at the scene of the alleged event \u8212- their testimony is not merely contradicted but completely annulled. Therefore the original two witnesses were entirely discredited, and nothing remained of the accusation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Alternatively, Rava offers the explanation of Rabbi Yitzchak: {\i shifcha hachnisu tachteha} \u8212- a maidservant was substituted for Yannai\u8217's mother in captivity. The second pair of witnesses did not contradict the first pair but completed the account: yes, she was taken, but she was subsequently rescued and a maidservant was swapped in her place. In this reading, the testimony of the second pair does not cancel the first but resolves the situation into a positive outcome.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Abaye Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The debate concludes in a state of tension. Abaye holds the bold position that an unopposed single witness is believed even in personal status matters \u8212- {\i davar she-be-erva} \u8212- such that a husband confronted with such testimony and remaining silent would be compelled to divorce his wife. Rava holds that for {\i davar she-be-erva}, two witnesses are always required, whether for the constitutive function that creates the act of {\i kiddushin} or {\i gittin}, or for the evidentiary function of proving that a personal-status fact occurred. A single witness, even one who goes wholly unopposed, cannot satisfy that threshold. The {\i halacha} will follow Rava \u8212- as it nearly always does against Abaye \u8212- and the requirement of two witnesses for {\i davar she-be-erva} stands in full force. The discussion continues on the following {\i amud}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Kiddushin 66\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Witnesses in Kiddushin\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Central Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The daf opens in the middle of a sustained inquiry into one of the most fundamental questions in the laws of {\i kiddushin}: are witnesses merely required to {\i prove} that a marriage took place, or are they constitutive of the act itself \u8212- such that without witnesses, no {\i kiddushin} occurs at all? This distinction carries enormous practical weight. If witnesses serve only a probative function, then one might argue that where both parties openly acknowledge the marriage, their mutual admission substitutes for testimony. But if witnesses are constitutive \u8212- if their presence is what makes the act of {\i kiddushin} happen \u8212- then no degree of mutual consent can compensate for their absence.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara had been testing this question by examining various cases: a husband claiming marriage while his wife denies it (where are the witnesses?), and the dispute between Beis Shammai and Beis Hillel regarding a divorced couple discovered together in an inn (where witnesses to the seclusion raise the question of whether they stand in for witnesses to the act itself). Throughout, the thrust of the discussion has been that witnesses are not optional \u8212- without them, the {\i kiddushin} did not take place.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav\u8217's Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now presents a direct ruling, transmitted by Rav Yitzchak bar Shmuel bar Marta in the name of Rav: {\i ha-mekadesh be-ed echad ein chosheshin le-kiddushav} \u8212- one who performs {\i kiddushin} with only a single witness present need not be concerned; no {\i kiddushin} has occurred. Critically, the ruling adds {\i ve-afilu shneihem modim} \u8212- even if both parties, the man and the woman, mutually agree and acknowledge that the marriage took place, it makes no difference. Without two witnesses, nothing happened.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbah bar Rav Huna\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbah bar Rav Huna then offers a parallel formulation: one who performs {\i kiddushin} with only one witness \u8212- {\i Beidina Rabba amrei ein chosheshin le-kiddushav} \u8212- the great {\i beis din} rules there is no concern. The Gemara asks: who is this {\i Beidina Rabba}, this great {\i beis din}? The answer: Rav. That is, the earlier authority, Rav himself, is being identified as the source of this ruling, lending it still greater weight.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Textual Variants\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then records an alternative version of the transmission: {\i ve-ika de-amrei}, some say the chain runs differently \u8212- Rabbah bar Rav Huna said in the name of Rav that the great {\i beis din} rules there is no concern. In this reading, Rav is explicitly named, and the {\i Beidina Rabba} he references is identified not as Rav but as Rebbi \u8212- the Nasi, Rabbi Yehudah HaNasi. Either way, the ruling emerges with formidable authority: whether it is Rav himself or Rebbi standing behind it, the dominant voice in the Gemara is unambiguous that {\i kiddushin} performed with only one witness is of no legal effect whatsoever.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Two Men, One Woman\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 The Scenario\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now introduces a counter-challenge from Rav Achdevai bar Ami. Two men arrive from {\i medinas ha-yam} \u8212- from overseas \u8212- accompanied by a woman and a bundle of property. The first man claims: \u8220"This is my wife, this is my slave, and this is my luggage.\u8221" The second man claims: \u8220"This is my wife, that is my slave, and that is my bundle.\u8221" And the woman herself declares: \u8220"Both of you are my slaves, and the bundle is mine.\u8221" It is a remarkable scenario, made all the more pointed by the fact that no one simply says \u8220"three people arrived\u8221" \u8212- two people come {\i with} a woman, and she is listed alongside the luggage.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 The Ruling\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i halacha} in this case is: {\i tzerichah shnei gittin} \u8212- she requires a {\i get} from each of them \u8212- {\i ve-gomer kesuvahsah min ha-chavilah} \u8212- and she may collect the value of her {\i kesuvah} from the bundle. Each man\u8217's claim that she is his wife creates at least a conditional obligation: if he is to give her a {\i get}, he must pay her {\i kesuvah}, and {\i mima nafshach} she is entitled to collect that amount from the disputed bundle, since each man acknowledges the luggage came with the woman he claims to have married.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Case Clarification\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara immediately asks: {\i heichi dami} \u8212- what exactly is the case? If each man has witnesses establishing that the woman is his wife, how can her claim that both are merely her slaves have any standing at all? The presence of witnesses to {\i kiddushin} should overwhelm her counterclaim. The Gemara considers the possibility that each man has one witness \u8212- but then the witnesses neutralize one another, and we are back to square one. There is no objective basis for requiring her to obtain a {\i get} at all, since a man\u8217's bare claim that a woman is his wife does not, by itself, impose upon her the need for a {\i get}. Indeed, the Gemara confirms: {\i ela lemishrei le\u8217'alma dechulei alma lo peligi desharya} \u8212- she requires no {\i get} to remarry, as there is no objective halachic basis for such a requirement.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What then is the case actually saying? The scenario is best understood as one in which the claims are all a matter of he-said-she-said, with no standing witnesses available at the time of judgment. In such a case, no one can compel anyone to do anything. The only practical resolution is: if one of the men voluntarily gives her a {\i get} \u8212- why he would, given that he is not halachically required to, remains unclear, but if he does \u8212- only then would she be entitled to collect her {\i kesuvah} from the bundle.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is worth noting the contemporary weight of this discussion. The Gemara is extremely reluctant to say a woman requires a {\i get} without clear halachic basis, precisely because of the consequences for the woman: she would be {\i asurah le-kohen}, he would be {\i asur bi-krovah}, and the specter of {\i agunah} looms over any unnecessary imposition of {\i get} requirements.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Meir\u8217's Chiddush\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If no {\i get} is actually required and the entire scenario is contingent on voluntary action, what is the {\i chiddush}? The Gemara explains: the novel ruling here follows Rabbi Meir, {\i de-amar metalteli mishta\u8217'abdi le-kesuvah} \u8212- Rabbi Meir holds that movable property is subject to the lien of a {\i kesuvah}. The innovation is not about the {\i get} requirement or the witness question per se, but about the eligibility of the bundle \u8212- {\i metaltelin}, chattel \u8212- to serve as collateral for collecting a {\i kesuvah}. According to Rabbi Meir, one need not hold real estate to pay a {\i kesuvah}; movable goods suffice.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Abaye vs.\u160 ?Rava\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kahana & Papa\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now asks: {\i mai hava alah} \u8212- what is the practical upshot of all this discussion? Despite the weight of amora\u8217'ic opinion that two witnesses are required, it has proven difficult to adduce clear Tannaitic support for the rule. Rav Kahana therefore rules: {\i ein chosheshin le-kiddushin} \u8212- no concern whatsoever; {\i kiddushin} with one witness simply did not occur. Rav Papa, however, says: {\i chosheshin le-kiddushin} \u8212- he remains somewhat concerned. Even if the {\i kiddushin} may not be fully valid, Rav Papa is unwilling to declare it entirely without effect.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gezeirah Shavah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Ashi then challenges Rav Kahana directly: what is the source for your position? You must have derived it through a {\i gezeirah shavah} \u8212- a verbal analogy \u8212- between the word {\i davar} appearing in {\i al pi shnayim edim yakum davar} (the requirement for two witnesses in monetary cases) and the word {\i davar} in {\i ki matza bah ervat davar} (pertaining to personal status and {\i davar she\u8217'be\u8217'ervah}). The analogy imports the two-witness requirement from monetary law into the domain of personal status.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But this raises a powerful objection: in monetary matters, {\i hoda\u8217'as ba\u8217'al din ke-me\u8217'ah edim dami} \u8212- a litigant\u8217's own admission is equivalent to the testimony of a hundred witnesses. If I acknowledge that I owe someone money, the debt stands even without any witnesses to the original transaction. Witnesses in monetary law are {\i edei beirur} \u8212- their function is purely probative, to prevent lying and establish facts in court. If the parties are honest and admit the reality, no witnesses are needed. If this is the model from which the {\i kiddushin} requirement is derived, why should not the same logic apply there? If husband and wife both acknowledge the marriage, why should that admission not suffice?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Beirur vs.\u160 ?Kiyum\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara\u8217's response is sharp: {\i lo k\u8217'chayev l\u8217'achrinei hacha k\u8217'chayev l\u8217'achrinei} \u8212- there, in monetary law, when I admit a debt I am only harming myself. Here, when a man and woman admit to a marriage, they are imposing consequences on others. The woman\u8217's admission restricts her to this man; other men are now forbidden to her, and relations with them would constitute adultery. His admission restricts him from her relatives. The marriage, unlike a private debt, is not a transaction between two parties only \u8212- it ramifies throughout society.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This reasoning, however, only explains why personal admission is insufficient as a {\i standard of proof}. It does not yet prove that witnesses are {\i constitutive} \u8212- that without them the {\i kiddushin} literally did not occur. This gap is noted by the {\i acharonim} as something the Gemara never fully closes: we begin from monetary law where witnesses serve only to prove that something happened, and we arrive at the conclusion that in {\i kiddushin} and {\i gittin} the act does not even occur without witnesses \u8212- which is a far more radical claim.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 There is indeed a significant debate among {\i acharonim} about how to conceptualize this. One approach holds that even in {\i kiddushin} the witnesses are fundamentally {\i edei beirur} \u8212- their role is probative \u8212- but because the act {\i chav le-achrinei}, because it impacts all of society, an act that cannot be proven in the requisite manner is treated as if it did not happen. Another approach holds that witnesses are genuinely constitutive of the act: {\i kiddushin} is so weighty a transaction that it requires the formal presence of witnesses to give it legal reality, in the same way that some acts require a justice of the peace.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A practical {\i nafka mina} between these two approaches concerns the case of witnesses who were hidden \u8212- watching from concealment \u8212- without the bride and groom\u8217's knowledge. If witnesses are merely probative, it should not matter whether the parties knew they were present; the fact can be proven. But if witnesses are constitutive \u8212- if they formalize and give weight to the act \u8212- perhaps the parties must be aware of their presence. This question is in fact debated among the {\i rishonim}, and the {\i Ketzos ha-Choshen} in {\i Choshen Mishpat} is among the primary places where the competing views are laid out, alongside a {\i teshuvah} of the Rashba.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A practical corollary: the Rema in {\i Even HaEzer} notes that since the Gemara did not entirely reject the possibility that {\i kiddushin} with one witness and mutual admission might carry some weight, one should {\i lechatchila} take it seriously if the situation arises \u8212- particularly in cases involving potential {\i agunah} concerns. Rav Moshe\u8217's responsa address precisely this scenario (a case of a teenager who said {\i harei at mekudeshet li} before only one witness), and the answer is nuanced: we are somewhat concerned {\i lechatchila}, but not to the point of treating the woman as requiring a {\i get} in the face of serious hardship.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Witnesses & Monetary Law\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Asset Division\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now addresses a parallel case that further develops the conceptual framework. Mar Zutra and Rav Ada Saba, sons of Rav Mari bar Issur, had divided their assets \u8212- presumably a partnership or an inheritance \u8212- without any witnesses present. They came before Rav Ashi with a fundamental question: when the Torah says {\i al pi shnayim edim}, does that mean witnesses are necessary even to effectuate a valid division of assets? That is, if nobody wishes to back out and both parties acknowledge what was done, must the division be repeated with witnesses? Or were witnesses created only to prevent subsequent denial?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Abaye\u8217's Answer\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye answers with one of the most important and oft-cited principles in halachic literature: {\i lo ivru sahadei ela le-shakrei} \u8212- witnesses were created only for liars. The sole purpose of witnesses in monetary matters is to protect against dishonest denial. In a world of universal honesty, witnesses would be unnecessary. Since neither party here is interested in reneging, the division stands entirely without witnesses.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Framework\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This ruling crystallizes the distinction that underlies the entire sugya. In monetary law, witnesses are {\i edei beirur} \u8212- probative instruments designed to establish facts against potential denial. The act itself \u8212- the loan, the division, the sale \u8212- exists independently of whether witnesses were present. Hoda\u8217'as ba\u8217'al din works precisely because the underlying obligation is real regardless; witnesses merely allow us to prove it in court. {\i Edei kiyum}, by contrast, are constitutive: without them, the act itself has no legal existence.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This framework also raises fascinating parallels with capital law, where formal witnesses and {\i hatra\u8217'ah} \u8212- forewarning \u8212- are equally required. Are those requirements merely evidentiary \u8212- to establish that the act was truly willful \u8212- or are they part of what defines the gravity of the act itself? The question of whether witnesses and {\i hatra\u8217'ah} make an act {\i deserving} of punishment by constituting its character, or merely allow us to {\i prove} it, maps precisely onto the debate about {\i edei beirur} versus {\i edei kiyum} in the domain of {\i kiddushin}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Single Witness Cases\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Contaminated Terumah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now presents a series of cases in which Abaye rules that a single unopposed witness is believed. A single witness tells someone {\i nitme\u8217'u taharosecha} \u8212- your pure items, your {\i terumah} grain, have become {\i tamei} \u8212- and the owner is silent. Abaye rules: {\i ne\u8217'eman} \u8212- the witness is believed, and the grain may not be eaten. A supporting {\i baraisa} confirms: if the owner actively contradicts the witness \u8212- saying {\i lo nitme\u8217'u} \u8212- the grain is {\i tahor}. The reason the grain is {\i tahor} in the contradicted case is that he said no; the implication is that had he been silent, the witness would have been believed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Korban Obligation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye offers a second case: a single witness tells someone {\i achalta chelev} \u8212- you ate forbidden fat \u8212- {\i be-shogeg}, unintentionally, and the owner is silent. The witness is believed and the person must bring a {\i korban}. Here too the {\i baraisa} confirms: if the person says {\i lo achalti} \u8212- I did not eat \u8212- he is exempt. The implication is that silence would have obligated him.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Animal Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A third case: a single witness tells someone {\i shorcha nirva} \u8212- your ox was used in an act of bestiality \u8212- and the owner is silent. The witness is believed, and the ox is disqualified from use as a {\i korban}. A {\i baraisa} teaches that animals disqualified through sin \u8212- whether through bestiality ({\i nirva}) or through having killed a person ({\i shehemit}) \u8212- are disqualified {\i al pi ed echad} or {\i al pi baalim}, on the basis of a single witness or the owner\u8217's own admission. The Gemara notes: if the owner is admitting, that is the second category ({\i al pi baalim}). The case of a single witness must therefore be the case where the owner is simply silent \u8212- and silence suffices.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Why Three Cases\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara explains why all three cases are necessary: {\i tzricha}. Each case presents a scenario where one might have thought the silence is not a meaningful admission. In the {\i korban} case, silence seems clearly an admission \u8212- the owner would never remain silent if he did not know he had eaten {\i chelev}, because bringing an unnecessary {\i korban} would constitute {\i chullin ba-azarah}, which no one would risk. Hence his silence is unmistakably an acknowledgment. But one might have thought that in the {\i terumah} case, silence is not meaningful, since the owner might reason: even if the grain is {\i tamei}, I can eat it during my own days of {\i tum\u8217'ah} \u8212- no real loss. The Gemara counters: he does lose the ability to eat it while {\i tahor}. And one might think the ox case requires no admission at all, since the owner may not have been planning to bring this particular animal as a {\i korban} anyway \u8212- why would he care if it is disqualified? All three cases are needed to establish that in each context, the silence does constitute a meaningful admission.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafos Analysis\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafos draws from this {\i tzrichusa} a significant principle: what the Gemara is really crediting in these cases is not the weight of the single witness per se, but rather the owner\u8217's silence read as an admission. If the Gemara were simply recognizing the witness\u8217's credibility as a matter of {\i ed echad ne\u8217'eman be-issurin}, no {\i tzrichusa} would have been necessary. The reason we need all three cases is that each requires us to carefully establish that silence in that specific context is indeed an admission.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This yields an important {\i nafka mina}: if the owner says {\i I don\u8217't know} rather than remaining silent, the witness is not believed \u8212- because the admission that underlies the ruling is absent. Similarly, if the witness testifies about something that occurred while the owner was elsewhere and had no way of knowing about it, silence cannot be read as admission, and the witness\u8217's testimony alone does not suffice.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This analysis also intersects with the question of why {\i ed echad ne\u8217'eman be-issurin} does not simply operate here on its own. In these cases, the witness is telling someone something about their own property, effectively imposing a financial consequence \u8212- the grain must be discarded, the animal cannot be sold as a {\i korban}. That is more akin to a monetary imposition than to a simple status ruling, and it is not obvious that the ordinary rule of {\i ed echad ne\u8217'eman be-issurin} applies where the consequence is a direct loss to the owner. Tosafos introduces additional factors here as well, such as whether the person was in a position to have done something about the outcome \u8212- as in the case of an animal given to be slaughtered, where the {\i shochet} who declares it {\i treif} is more readily believed because he could have handled the {\i shechita} differently.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Wife & Adultery\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 The Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now introduces the central case of the broader discussion: {\i ishto zinsa be-ed echad ve-shotek} \u8212- a single witness testifies to a man that his wife committed adultery, and the man is silent. What is the law?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Before addressing the ruling, it is worth situating this within the broader framework. If a man himself declares that his wife committed adultery, the halachic consequence is not that the court objectively establishes adultery \u8212- his unilateral declaration cannot do that, as it is {\i chav le-achrinei}. Rather, he is bound by his own words and must divorce his wife, though he remains obligated to pay her {\i kesuvah} since there is no objective proof. By contrast, a single witness to adultery would not ordinarily be credited in matters of personal status \u8212- {\i davar she\u8217'be\u8217'ervah} requires two witnesses even to prove it. The question here combines these issues: a single witness speaks, the husband says nothing. What now?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Abaye vs.\u160 ?Rava\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye says: {\i ne\u8217'eman} \u8212- the witness is believed, and the husband must divorce his wife. Rava says: {\i eino ne\u8217'eman} \u8212- he is not believed. {\i Havei davar she\u8217'be\u8217'ervah ve-ein davar she\u8217'be\u8217'ervah pachos mi-shnayim} \u8212- this is a matter of {\i ervah}, and no matter of {\i ervah} can be established with fewer than two witnesses.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafos offers an elegant framework for understanding what is actually being disputed. Abaye and Rava agree that personal status matters require two witnesses for objective halachic effect. They disagree about what this case actually is. Abaye reads the husband\u8217's silence as an admission: he is not contesting the witness, which is tantamount to saying he himself acknowledges his wife committed adultery \u8212- and just as a man\u8217's own statement about his wife\u8217's adultery binds him to divorce her, so too here. Rava, by contrast, does not read silence as admission in this context. This case is about the credibility of an unopposed single witness in a matter of {\i davar she\u8217'be\u8217'ervah} \u8212- and such a witness simply cannot carry the requisite halachic weight, regardless of whether anyone contradicts him.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Reframing\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The distinction is sharp and consequential. For Abaye, the operative mechanism is {\i shtika ke-hoda\u8217'ah} \u8212- silence as admission \u8212- and the admission of a party binds him to consequences he would bear from his own declaration. For Rava, the operative question is the inherent credibility of the witness\u8217's testimony in a {\i davar she\u8217'be\u8217'ervah} matter, and a single witness \u8212- even unchallenged \u8212- cannot establish {\i davar she\u8217'be\u8217'ervah}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Blind Man Story\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 The Setup\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye now attempts to adduce a source for his position. He introduces a story: there was a blind scholar \u8212- a {\i tanna}, one who had memorized Tannaitic material and served as a teaching assistant in the {\i yeshiva} \u8212- who would regularly arrange {\i mishnayos} before Mar Shmuel. One day he was running late. Shmuel sent a messenger to fetch him. The messenger and the scholar took different paths and missed each other; the scholar arrived at the {\i yeshiva} and the {\i shiur} proceeded. When the messenger returned, he reported what he had seen: {\i ishta zanya} \u8212- the scholar\u8217's wife had been with another man.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shmuel\u8217's Ruling\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The scholar came before Shmuel and reported what the messenger had said. Shmuel told him: {\i im meiman lach, zil afkeih} \u8212- if you trust this person, go and divorce her. {\i Ve-i lo, lo tappeik} \u8212- and if not, do not divorce her.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Abaye\u8217's Proof\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye uses this story to prove his position. Note that this case cannot be explained by silence as admission: the husband was away, the witness reports what he saw, and the husband\u8217's lack of response is obviously not an admission \u8212- he had no way of knowing what happened. Yet Shmuel appears to rule that an unopposed single witness can obligate the husband to divorce his wife. This is precisely what Abaye holds: an unopposed {\i ed echad} carries sufficient weight in {\i davar she\u8217'be\u8217'ervah} to require divorce, even without any admission from the husband.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava\u8217's Reading\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava reads the story differently. When Shmuel said {\i im meiman lach} \u8212- if you trust him \u8212- he did not mean simply \u8220"if you know him to be an honest person and not a {\i gazlan}.\u8221" He meant: {\i im meiman lach ke-vei trei} \u8212- if you trust him to the degree that you would trust two witnesses \u8212- if you have such absolute, ironclad personal confidence in this individual that his word is equivalent to formal testimony. Only in that extraordinary case should you act on his report. This is not a general ruling about unopposed single witnesses; it is a ruling about a case of such unusual personal certainty that it approaches the weight of two witnesses. And even then, Rava would likely maintain, the husband would still need to pay the {\i kesuvah}, since there remains no objective testimony sufficient to establish adultery in court.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Yannai Story\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 The Feast\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye\u8217's broader proof is derived from a remarkable historical {\i baraisa} \u8212- one with a clear literary quality unusual in Tannaitic texts, and with a known parallel in Josephus. The story concerns King Yannai, one of the Hasmonean monarchs. He sent forces to {\i Kochalit} in the wilderness and conquered sixty cities there \u8212- an achievement he celebrated with great joy upon returning. He summoned all the sages of Israel and told them: our ancestors, in the days when they were preoccupied with building the {\i Beis HaMikdash}, ate salted fish \u8212- the food of poverty. Let us eat salted fish in their memory. Rashi understands this as a gesture of {\i me-shibbud li-ge\u8217'ulah}: a recognition of how far they had come, from grinding poverty to this moment of military triumph, with the food of the past served on tables of gold \u8212- {\i meluchim al shulchanot shel zahav} \u8212- a striking and deliberate contrast.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 The Insult\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 At this feast there was, as the {\i baraisa} describes in almost scriptural language, {\i ish echad latz lev ra u-vliya\u8217'al} \u8212- one person, a mocker, evil-hearted and worthless \u8212- {\i ve-Elazar ben Po\u8217'irah sh\u8217'mo} \u8212- and his name was Elazar ben Poira. He said to King Yannai: the hearts of the Pharisees \u8212- the {\i chachamim} \u8212- are against you; they do not support you. Yannai asked: what can I do to test this? Elazar told him: wear the {\i tzitz} \u8212- the golden headplate of the {\i Kohen Gadol} \u8212- and make them stand before you. (There is a dispute among {\i rishonim} here: Rashi permits wearing {\i bigdei kehuna} outside the {\i Beis HaMikdash} based on the principle that the Torah was not given to ministering angels \u8212- one cannot demand instant removal of sacred garments after completing the {\i avodah}. Tosafos restricts this leniency and suggests the {\i tzitz} may be an exception, as the verse states {\i ve-hayah al mitzcho tamid} \u8212- it shall be upon his forehead always.)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One elder at the feast, Yehudah ben Gedidya, then spoke \u8212- unwisely. He said to King Yannai: {\i rav lecha keter malchut} \u8212- the crown of kingship is enough for you; leave the crown of {\i kehuna} to the descendants of Aharon. His basis was a rumor: {\i imo nishbis be-Modi\u8217'in} \u8212- that Yannai\u8217's mother had been taken captive in Modi\u8217'in, which, if true, would have rendered her unfit as the wife of a {\i kohen} and Yannai himself a {\i chalal}, disqualified from priestly service and from the {\i kehuna gedolah}. The problem was that {\i vi-yuvakash ha-davar ve-lo nimtza} \u8212- the matter had been investigated and nothing had been found to substantiate the claim. Yehudah ben Gedidya\u8217's remark was thus not only politically reckless but factually without support.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 The Rumor\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The sages at the feast \u8212- {\i vayivadlu chachmei Yisrael be-za\u8217'am} \u8212- were driven out under the king\u8217's displeasure. Rashi explains this as their being expelled from the feast under Yannai\u8217's anger; the suggestion is also that their collective silence, their failure to defend Yannai or rebuke the offending speaker more forcefully, made them appear complicit in the insult. From Josephus\u8217's parallel account, we know that the sages in fact imposed lashes on the offender for {\i motzi shem ra} \u8212- defamation \u8212- but Elazar then argued to Yannai that the punishment was inadequate given the magnitude of the insult to a king and high priest.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 The Conspiracy\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Elazar ben Poira then said to Yannai: {\i im atah shome\u8217'a le-atzati} \u8212- if you follow my counsel \u8212- destroy the sages, wipe them out. Yannai asked: {\i ha-Torah mah te\u8217'ei aleiha} \u8212- but what will become of the Torah? Elazar replied: {\i harei krucha u-munachat be-keren zavis} \u8212- it is rolled up and sitting in the corner; {\i kol ha-rotzeh yilmod yavo ve-yilmod} \u8212- whoever wants to learn may come and learn. The written Torah remains; why do you need the sages?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Heresy & Restoration\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara records: Rav Nachman bar Yitzchak said that immediately an {\i apikorsus} \u8212- a heresy \u8212- entered into Yannai. What was his sin? {\i De-hava leih le-meimar} \u8212- he should have responded: {\i tenach Torah she-bichtav} \u8212- granted, the written Torah remains, {\i Torah she-be-al peh mah} \u8212- but what of the Oral Torah? Who will transmit it? By remaining silent in the face of Elazar\u8217's dismissal of the sages, Yannai tacitly accepted the premise that the written Torah alone suffices. Note that {\i apikorsus} in the Gemara \u8212- especially as it appears in Sanhedrin \u8212- specifically denotes {\i mevazeh talmidei chachamim}, contempt for Torah scholars. The term here is thus precise: Yannai\u8217's silence was a form of dismissing the indispensable role of the {\i chachamim}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i baraisa} continues: {\i u-tutzatz ha-ra\u8217'ah al yedei Elazar ben Po\u8217'irah} \u8212- the evil blossomed forth through Elazar ben Poira \u8212- {\i ve-yarequ kol chachmei Yisrael} \u8212- and all the sages of Israel were killed. {\i Ve-hayah ha-olam mishtomem} \u8212- the world was desolate. This continued {\i ad she-ba Shimon ben Shetach} \u8212- until Shimon ben Shetach came \u8212- who had been hidden away, reportedly under the protection of Yannai\u8217's own wife, who was his sister. {\i Ve-hechzir es ha-Torah le-yoshnah} \u8212- and he returned the Torah to its former glory. Some connect Shimon ben Shetach to the establishment of primary education for children throughout the land \u8212- an interesting counterpoint to this very story, in that the response to the near-annihilation of Torah scholarship was to ensure that Torah would never again be concentrated only in the hands of a small group of sages who could be wiped out in a single night.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Analysis of Story\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 The Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now turns to ask what this story proves. Abaye had invoked it as a source for his position that an unopposed single witness can be credited in matters of personal status. The question is: what was the nature of the evidence about Yannai\u8217's mother\u8217's captivity, and how exactly was it refuted?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Evidence Structure\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara considers the possibilities. If two witnesses had testified that she was taken captive, and two others testified she was not, then we have {\i bei trei u-bei trei} \u8212- two against two. What would that produce? In monetary matters, we say they neutralize one another and return to the status quo. But in personal status matters, the analysis is more complex.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rashi\u8217's View\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rashi\u8217's reading is that even if the two sets of witnesses neutralize each other in some sense, the child \u8212- Yannai \u8212- would remain in {\i safek}. The mother herself has a {\i chazkas kashrus} \u8212- a presumption of fitness \u8212- and so the conflicting testimony leaves her status intact. But Yannai as a child never had an established {\i chazakah} of priestly fitness in this particular context. Since the case before the court is about the {\i child\u8217's} status, and the child lacks an independent {\i chazakah} that could resolve the doubt, the result is that Yannai\u8217's status as a {\i kohen} would remain {\i be-safek}. The {\i baraisa} says the matter was investigated and nothing was found \u8212- Rashi takes this to mean that even in the two-against-two scenario there would have remained a real question, which is why the story is halachically meaningful.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gemara\u8217's Inference\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 From here the Gemara draws its inference for Abaye\u8217's position: {\i ela be-echad} \u8212- the initial testimony must have been by a single witness. And if the sole reason the case was dismissed is that one or two witnesses contradicted that single witness \u8212- {\i te\u8217'ima de-kamachshei lei bei trei} \u8212- then in the absence of that contradiction, {\i meheiman}, the single witness would have been believed. This is Abaye\u8217's proof: even in a {\i davar she\u8217'be\u8217'ervah} matter \u8212- the personal status of the king\u8217's mother, which carries enormous ramifications \u8212- an unopposed single witness would have been sufficient.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava\u8217's Counter\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava responds: {\i le-olam trei ve-trei} \u8212- in fact, it was two against two from the start. And the reason the case was completely dismissed \u8212- rather than leaving Yannai in {\i safek} \u8212- is because the second pair of witnesses employed {\i edei hazamah}, the mechanism of impeachment. Rather than contradicting the content of the first testimony, they said to the first witnesses: you could not have seen what you claim to have seen \u8212- you were elsewhere at that time. {\i Hazamah} does not merely neutralize; it entirely discredits and removes the first testimony from consideration. Rav Acha bar Minyumi is cited in a parallel context for precisely this principle. Thus the first testimony was not neutralized by equal counter-testimony; it was thrown out entirely, leaving nothing. This is why the {\i baraisa} says the matter was found to be without basis.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Alternative Explanation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then offers a further alternative: {\i ve-i ba\u8217'it eima, ki de-Rabbi Yitzchak} \u8212- if you prefer, explain it like Rabbi Yitzchak, who says {\i shifcha hichnisu tachteha} \u8212- a maidservant was substituted in her place. On this reading, the two sets of witnesses do not contradict each other at all. The first pair testified that Yannai\u8217's mother was taken captive \u8212- and they were correct. The second pair testified to the rest of the story: yes, she was initially taken, but she was then rescued, and a maidservant was substituted so that the captors would not notice. The second witnesses thus complete and supplement the first testimony rather than contradict it, and the combined account establishes that Yannai\u8217's mother had ultimately not been harmed in her personal status.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The daf concludes with the debate between Abaye and Rava unresolved in the text at this point \u8212- though of course, as the Gemara will make clear, the {\i halacha} follows Rava: in all matters of {\i davar she\u8217'be\u8217'ervah}, whether to constitute the act or to prove it, two witnesses are required, and even an unopposed single witness does not suffice. For Abaye to maintain that an unopposed {\i ed echad} is believed in such cases \u8212- with consequences as severe as compelling a man to divorce his wife \u8212- is, as the Gemara acknowledges, a striking and counterintuitive position, and its implications underscore just how much rides on the foundational distinction between {\i edei beirur} and {\i edei kiyum}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Kiddushin 66b\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Abaye-Rava Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 One vs.\u160 ?Two Witnesses\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The daf opens with a debate between Abaye and Rava concerning the evidentiary standards required in matters of personal status \u8212- specifically, whether one witness suffices or whether two are required. The question is not merely about the formal act of {\i kiddushin} or {\i gittin}, where it is already established that two witnesses are needed for the act to take effect, but about proof after the fact: can one witness, even an uncontested one, establish a fact bearing on personal status? Abaye holds that one witness would be enough in such circumstances. Rava disagrees, invoking the principle of {\i ein davar shebiervah pachot mishnayim} \u8212- no matter touching on {\i erva}, personal status, or sexual propriety can be established by fewer than two witnesses. According to Rava, this principle operates in both directions: two witnesses are required both to effectuate such an act and to prove that it occurred.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The scope of the discussion extends well beyond {\i gittin} and {\i kiddushin} proper. Among the examples discussed: whether a woman was taken captive, which would render her forbidden to a {\i kohen}, or whether a woman committed adultery, which would render her forbidden to her husband. In all such cases, Rava insists, the standard of proof remains two witnesses.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava\u8217's Principle\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara opens with {\i mina meimra} \u8212- where do we derive this? \u8212- and points to a {\i mishnah} (also found in the {\i Tosefta}). The citation is introduced with {\i tennan}, indicating a {\i mishnah}-level source.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mikvah Story\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} presents the following case: there was a {\i mikvah} in a place called Diskim \u8212- Rashi understands this as either a personal or a place name \u8212- located in Yavneh. The {\i mikvah} had stood under the presumption ({\i chazakah}) of being {\i shalem}, complete, containing the required forty {\i se\u8217'ah} of valid water. At some point, a {\i chacham} measured it and found it deficient. The question becomes: what is the status of everything done using this {\i mikvah} since the last time it was verified as complete?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Tarfon rules {\i metaher} \u8212- he considers everything done with the {\i mikvah} to have been valid. His reasoning rests on the principle of {\i chazakah}: the {\i mikvah} had an established status of completeness. A doubt about when exactly it became deficient does not override that status; we assume the {\i mikvah} remained {\i shalem} until the last possible moment, i.e., until the moment it was found to be lacking. As he articulates it: {\i mikvah zeh bechezkat shalem hu omed} \u8212- this {\i mikvah} stands in its presumption of completeness. One cannot retroactively undermine it on the basis of uncertainty.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Akiva rules {\i metamei} \u8212- he considers everything done since the last verification to be suspect. His reasoning is that there is a competing {\i chazakah} to consider: {\i adam zeh bechezkat tamei hu omed} \u8212- the person who immersed had a pre-immersion status of {\i tumah}. That status too is established. The question of whether the person became {\i tahor} depends entirely on whether the {\i mikvah} was {\i kasher} at the time of immersion. Since that is now uncertain, one cannot presume the person\u8217's status changed. The doubt cuts both ways, and the question becomes which {\i chazakah} prevails.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kohen Analogy\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Unable to resolve the competing {\i chazakot} directly, Rabbi Tarfon proposes an analogy: {\i mashal le\u8217'omed umakriv al gabbe hamizbeach} \u8212- imagine a {\i kohen} who has been standing and offering {\i korbanot}, and it then becomes known that he is a {\i ben gerushah} or {\i ben chalutzah}, i.e., a {\i chalal}. In that case, his {\i avodah} until that point is {\i kesheirah}. This is not merely a matter of presumption but a special {\i gezeirat hakatuv}, derived from a {\i pasuk}, that retroactive invalidation does not apply once the {\i avodah} was performed in ignorance. Rabbi Tarfon invokes this case to suggest a general policy consideration: just as one cannot issue recall notices invalidating years of priestly service, so too one cannot retroactively invalidate months of {\i taharot} performed using the {\i mikvah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 There is, as the lecturer acknowledges, a notable imprecision in the analogy. The {\i mikvah} case involves a {\i safek} \u8212- genuine uncertainty about when the deficiency arose \u8212- whereas the {\i kohen} case involves a situation where the {\i pesul} was present throughout, and we are simply applying a special legal rule that suspends retroactive invalidation. Nonetheless, Rabbi Tarfon\u8217's point has a certain policy logic: the societal consequences of wholesale retroactive invalidation support leaning toward the {\i chazakah} that the {\i mikvah} remained {\i kasher}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Baal Mum Analogy\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Akiva responds with a counter-analogy: {\i mashal l\u8217'ma hadavar domeh} \u8212- imagine instead a {\i kohen} offering {\i korbanot} who is discovered to have a {\i mum}, a bodily blemish. In that case, his {\i avodah} is retroactively {\i pesulah}. This is a striking contrast: one might have supposed that being a {\i chalal} \u8212- lacking the fundamental {\i kedushat kehunah} \u8212- is a more severe disqualification than having a {\i baal mum}, who in many respects retains {\i kedushat kehunah} (he shares in the {\i korbanot}, he is subject to the restrictions of {\i kohanim}). And yet the {\i halacha} is precisely the reverse: for the {\i chalal}, past {\i avodah} is valid; for the {\i baal mum}, it is not.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Akiva presses the point further. He offers a second distinction: {\i mikvah pesulah begufa, baal mum pasul begufo} \u8212- the {\i mikvah}\u8217's deficiency is intrinsic to itself, and the {\i baal mum}\u8217's deficiency is intrinsic to his own body. By contrast, the {\i ben gerushah} or {\i ben chalutzah} is {\i pesul me\u8217'acherim} \u8212- the disqualification comes from outside, from what his parents did. This structural parallel between the {\i mikvah} case and the {\i baal mum} case suggests that the {\i mikvah} should follow the rule of the {\i baal mum}, under which retroactive invalidation applies.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Tarfon then states the question openly: {\i nireh l\u8217'mi domeh} \u8212- let us determine which analogy is more apt. If the {\i mikvah} case more closely resembles {\i ben gerushah}, we apply that rule; if it more closely resembles {\i baal mum}, we apply that rule. Rabbi Akiva then proceeds to argue the comparison systematically: {\i mikvah pesulah beyachid, baal mum pasul beyachid} \u8212- the {\i mikvah}\u8217's deficiency can be established by a single person\u8217's testimony, and the {\i baal mum}\u8217's deficiency likewise. Whereas {\i ben gerushah} and {\i ben chalutzah} \u8212- matters of personal status touching on {\i erva} \u8212- require two witnesses. The {\i mikvah} thus structurally resembles the {\i baal mum}, not the {\i ben gerushah}. One therefore applies the {\i baal mum} rule, and retroactive invalidation follows.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Tarfon famously capitulates: {\i kol haparesh mimcha k\u8217'poresh min hachayim} \u8212- anyone who separates from you is as one who separates from life itself. The argument was conceded.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Contested Testimony\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now takes stock of the actual probative value of this exchange for Rava\u8217's principle. The question is: in what circumstance does the {\i baal mum} case require only one witness? The Gemara asks: {\i hai baal mum d\u8217'pesulah beyachid heichi dami?} \u8212- in what scenario does a single person\u8217's testimony suffice for a {\i baal mum}? If the accused is contesting the testimony, we would not ordinarily credit a single witness against a denial. It must therefore be the case \u8212- says Rava \u8212- that the scenario is one of uncontested testimony: {\i ela d\u8217'shatik}, the {\i kohen} is not disputing it. And yet even such an uncontested single witness does not suffice for {\i ben gerushah} or {\i ben chalutzah}. This demonstrates that in matters of personal status, even uncontested testimony from a single witness is insufficient. Two witnesses are required, and that is Rava\u8217's proof.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Uncontested Testimony\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye responds: {\i l\u8217'olam d\u8217'ka machish lei} \u8212- in fact, the {\i kohen} is contesting the testimony. So how does the single witness prevail? {\i D\u8217'amar lei shlach achvei} \u8212- because the witness can say: if you are telling the truth that you have no {\i mum}, strip and demonstrate it. The single witness is believed not because the testimony is uncontested in the ordinary sense, but because there is a mechanism for verification: the subject\u8217's refusal to demonstrate constitutes corroboration of the witness\u8217's claim.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This explains the second distinction Rabbi Akiva drew: {\i baal mum pesulah begufo} \u8212- the {\i mum} is on the person\u8217's body, and is therefore in principle demonstrable. The {\i mikvah}\u8217's deficiency is similarly intrinsic and demonstrable. Whereas {\i ben gerushah} and {\i ben chalutzah} \u8212- {\i pesulah me\u8217'acherim} \u8212- involve a fact about parentage that cannot be demonstrated physically. If a witness says this man is a {\i ben gerushah} and the {\i kohen} denies it, there is nothing to show. Therefore a single witness, even a contested one, does not suffice; two are required.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye\u8217's position thus becomes: the distinction is between contested and uncontested testimony, not between one witness and two in the context of personal status per se. When testimony is contested and cannot be demonstrated or refuted, two are needed \u8212- as by {\i ben gerushah}. When testimony can be verified by physical demonstration, one witness may suffice even in a contested setting \u8212- as by {\i baal mum}. But for personal status where no physical verification is possible, one witness \u8212- contested or not \u8212- is insufficient.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara concludes that the debate between Abaye and Rava on this point remains unresolved by this source alone. We do {\i posken} like Rava \u8212- {\i ein davar shebiervah pachot mishnayim} applies both to effectuating and to proving matters of personal status \u8212- but the logical proof from this passage is not airtight.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One practical extension of this debate is worth noting: the principle applies not only to cases of invalidation but also in situations where a person must prove something favorable \u8212- a woman proving she is divorced, a convert proving the fact of conversion. The standard of proof is accordingly robust.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kohen Ben Gerusha\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara closes out this section by establishing the underlying source for the rule that a {\i kohen} discovered to be a {\i ben gerushah} or {\i ben chalutzah} does not have his prior {\i avodah} retroactively invalidated.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Yehudah citing Shmuel derives it from the verse regarding Pinchas: {\i v\u8217'haytah lo u\u8217'l\u8217'zaro acharav berit kehunat olam} \u8212- it shall be for him and for his descendants after him a covenant of eternal priesthood \u8212- which is interpreted to apply {\i bein zera kasher bein zera pasul}, to both valid and disqualified offspring. Even a {\i chalal} retains enough of the {\i kehunat olam} to validate past {\i avodah} performed in ignorance.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The father of Shmuel derives it from {\i barech Hashem cheilo u\u8217'fo\u8217'al yadav tirtzeh} \u8212- bless his service-strength and may the work of his hands find favor \u8212- understanding {\i cheilo} as a wordplay on {\i chulin}, and reading the verse as: even if it turns out there is {\i chulin} in him \u8212- i.e., he is a {\i chalal} \u8212- his {\i avodah} was {\i tirtzeh}, found acceptable, until the point of discovery.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yannai derives it from {\i u\u8217'vata el hakohen asher yihyeh bayamim hahem} \u8212- you shall go to the {\i kohen} who is in your days. Obviously one can only go to the {\i kohen} available at the time; the verse thus validates the {\i avodah} of whichever {\i kohen} served during a given period, even if he was later discovered to be disqualified.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 For the {\i baal mum}, the Gemara inverts the derivation. Rav Yehudah citing Shmuel reads {\i lachen emor hineni noten lo et briti shalom} \u8212- the covenant of peace \u8212- as applying {\i kshehu shalem}, only when he is physically complete, unblemished. The word {\i shalom} in this verse is written with a {\i vav ketia}, a broken or truncated {\i vav}, as a scribal tradition indicating that the wholeness the verse describes is conditional on physical integrity. Once there is a {\i mum}, the condition fails, and past {\i avodah} is retroactively {\i pesulah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The lecturer acknowledges difficulty with the internal logic of this distinction: intuitively, a {\i chalal} \u8212- who lacks {\i kedushat kehunah} entirely \u8212- seems more fundamentally disqualified than a {\i baal mum}, who retains {\i kedushat kehunah} and participates in {\i korbanot}. And yet the {\i halacha} holds the {\i baal mum}\u8217's past {\i avodah} invalid while validating the {\i chalal}\u8217's. The best available explanation tracks the distinction between something demonstrable and present \u8212- the {\i mum} was on the body, in principle perceptible \u8212- versus something more abstract and genealogical, which could not have been known without information from others. But the lecturer notes that this explanation remains imperfect.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Yichus Rules\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 No Aveirah Rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The next {\i mishnah} opens the broader inquiry into {\i yichus} \u8212- genealogical status \u8212- which encompasses the questions of {\i kohen}, {\i levi}, {\i Yisrael}, {\i mamzer}, {\i chalal}, and related categories. The {\i mishnah} articulates a general principle: {\i kol makom sheyesh kiddushin v\u8217'ein aveirah, havalad holech achar hazachar} \u8212- wherever {\i kiddushin} is binding and no transgression occurred, the child\u8217's status follows the father.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Paternal Descent\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The illustrative case: a woman who is a {\i kohenet}, {\i leviyah}, or {\i Yisraelit} marries a man who is a {\i kohen}, {\i levi}, or {\i Yisrael}. The child follows the father\u8217's status in all cases, regardless of the mother\u8217's status. This is the straightforward application of paternal descent in a permitted marriage.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Aveirah Rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} continues: {\i v\u8217'kol makom sheyesh kiddushin v\u8217'yesh aveirah, havalad holech achar hapagum} \u8212- wherever {\i kiddushin} is binding but a transgression occurred, the child follows the disqualification. It does not matter whether the {\i pesul} comes from the father\u8217's side or the mother\u8217's side; the stigma passes to the child.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Forbidden Marriages\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The illustrative cases of forbidden {\i kohen} marriages: an {\i almanah} with a {\i kohen gadol}, or a {\i gerushah} or {\i chalutzah} with a {\i kohen hedyot}. In these unions {\i kiddushin} is technically binding (as these are {\i issurei lavin}, not {\i issurei karet}), but transgression has occurred. The child does not inherit the precise status of {\i gerushah} per se, but becomes a {\i chalal} \u8212- disqualified from priestly service and the associated privileges and restrictions. The concept is that the disqualifying status passes on in some form regardless.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mamzer Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Similarly, if a woman who is a {\i mamzeret} or {\i netinah} marries a {\i Yisrael} (or vice versa \u8212- a {\i mamzer} marrying a {\i bat Yisrael}), {\i kiddushin} is binding, but the child is a {\i mamzer}. The principle of {\i holech achar hapagum} applies directly: the child carries the disqualified status.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 No Kiddushin/Others Poss\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} then addresses a woman with whom {\i kiddushin} is not binding at all regarding this particular man, but who could be validly married to others: {\i havalad mamzer}. The child is a {\i mamzer}. The paradigm case here is a married woman \u8212- her husband could marry her, but she cannot create {\i kiddushin} with this other man. Any issue of such a union, being from {\i arayot}, is a {\i mamzer}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Matrilineal Descent\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The final category: {\i kol mi she\u8217'ein lah lo alav v\u8217'lo al acherim kiddushin, havalad kamosah} \u8212- any woman with whom no {\i kiddushin} is possible at all, with anyone, and the child follows the mother\u8217's status. The cases: a non-Jewish woman, or a slave. No Jew can contract valid {\i kiddushin} with either. The child accordingly is not a {\i mamzer} \u8212- that category presupposes a prohibited but potentially valid Jewish marital nexus \u8212- but is rather a non-Jew or a slave, following the mother.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is, as the Gemara will shortly elaborate upon, the foundational principle of matrilineal descent. Where no {\i kiddushin} can exist, the child\u8217's status is determined entirely by the mother.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rule Exceptions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shimon\u8217's Challenge\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara immediately challenges the {\i mishnah}\u8217's framing as a universal principle. Rabbi Shimon asks Rabbi Yochanan: {\i klala hu?} \u8212- is this really a complete and reliable rule?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ger-Mamzeret\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The challenge: consider a {\i ger} who married a {\i mamzeret}. According to some opinions, such a marriage is permitted \u8212- a {\i ger} is allowed to marry a {\i mamzeret}. If so, there is {\i kiddushin} and there is no {\i aveirah}, yet the child is still a {\i mamzer}. The child follows the {\i pagum} despite the absence of transgression \u8212- which contradicts the first clause of the {\i mishnah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i baraita} is cited: {\i ger shenasa mamzeret, havalad mamzer} \u8212- if a {\i ger} marries a {\i mamzeret}, the child is a {\i mamzer}. This is the position of Rabbi Yose.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Yehuda\u8217's View\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yochanan responds: {\i mi savart masnitin Rabbi Yose hi?} \u8212- who says our {\i mishnah} follows Rabbi Yose? The {\i mishnah} may reflect the view of Rabbi Yehudah, who holds that a {\i ger} is not permitted to marry a {\i mamzeret} in the first place. According to Rabbi Yehudah, such a union involves {\i kiddushin} with {\i aveirah}, and therefore the child following the {\i pagum} is entirely consistent with the {\i mishnah}\u8217's second clause. The apparent exception dissolves once we assign the {\i mishnah} to Rabbi Yehudah rather than Rabbi Yose.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kol Makom Inclusion\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then takes up the question of completeness. Even if the {\i mishnah}\u8217's rule is internally consistent, there are cases that fit the rule but are not listed as examples, and cases that are exceptions to the rule that are also unlisted. How do we account for both?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara answers with a dual hermeneutical move. The phrase {\i kol makom} \u8212- wherever \u8212- at the opening of each clause signals a general principle intended to include additional cases beyond those explicitly listed. The phrase {\i v\u8217'eizu zu} \u8212- and which is this? \u8212- following each list signals that the list is exhaustive for its particular formulation, thereby implicitly acknowledging that other cases may deviate from the rule.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara proceeds through candidate cases. A {\i chalal} who marries a {\i bat Yisrael}: {\i kiddushin} is binding, no {\i aveirah}, and the child follows the father \u8212- he is a {\i chalal}. This is an application of the rule not listed in the {\i mishnah}. A {\i Yisrael} who marries a {\i chalala}: {\i kiddushin} is binding, no {\i aveirah}, and the child follows the father \u8212- the child is {\i kasher}, not a {\i chalala}. This too is an application of the rule not listed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chalal Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara asks: if these are applications of the rule, why were they not listed explicitly? The answer is that they cannot be framed neatly within the {\i mishnah}\u8217's formulation. The {\i mishnah} lists cases of {\i kohenet}, {\i leviyah}, and {\i Yisraelit} marrying a {\i kohen}, {\i levi}, or {\i Yisrael} \u8212- implying that any of these women could marry any of these men. But a {\i chalala} cannot marry a {\i kohen}. Were the {\i mishnah} to include {\i v\u8217'chalala} in the list of women who married a {\i kohen}, {\i levi}, or {\i Yisrael}, it would imply that a {\i chalala} marrying a {\i kohen} is among the scenarios \u8212- which is forbidden. The {\i mishnah} cannot include the case without distorting its own framing. This is, as the Gemara acknowledges, a satisfying answer: literary considerations of economy and consistency preclude the explicit listing.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Framing Problem\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This observation generalizes. The {\i mishnah} is constructed as a tight, elegant set of rules. Cases that fit the rule but require syntactic contortions to include are simply absorbed into the scope of {\i kol makom} without being named. Cases that are exceptions are similarly handled by {\i eizu zu}, which marks the list as the intended application rather than an exhaustive catalogue of all possible cases. The strategy is: {\i tana kol makom l\u8217'atuyei} \u8212- the {\i tanna} uses {\i kol makom} to include unlisted applications; {\i tana eizu zu l\u8217'motei} \u8212- and uses {\i eizu zu} to exclude, or rather to acknowledge, unlisted exceptions.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mitzri Generations\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Another application of the rule is now introduced from a tradition of Rabba bar bar Chana citing Rabbi Yochanan: {\i Mitzri sheini shenasa Mitzrit rishona, benah shelishi havei} \u8212- a second-generation {\i Mitzri} who marries a first-generation {\i Mitzrit} produces a third-generation child. The background rule is that a {\i Mitzri} convert may not marry into the Jewish community until the third generation: {\i dor shelishi yavo lahem bikhal Hashem}. The question is how to count generations when two converts of different generational remove marry each other. The answer: the child\u8217's generation is counted by the father\u8217's status. A second-generation {\i Mitzri} father and a first-generation {\i Mitzrit} mother produces a third-generation child \u8212- following the father. This is another application of the rule of paternal descent in a permitted marriage with no {\i aveirah}. Why was it not listed? The {\i kol makom} of the {\i reisha} includes it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Converting Nations\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A tradition brought in the name of Ravin citing Rabbi Yochanan introduces a case that functions as an exception rather than an application: when members of non-Jewish nations convert, and two converts of different national backgrounds marry \u8212- for example, an {\i Edomi} convert and a {\i Mitzri} convert \u8212- one asks what status their child bears, given that different nations have different generational thresholds for full integration. The rule here, says Ravin, is {\i halach achar hapagam shebishneihem} \u8212- we follow whichever of the two parents carries the greater disqualification, the greater {\i pagum}. Even if no {\i aveirah} was committed in the marriage, the child follows the more disqualified parent. This is an exception to the rule that in a permitted marriage one follows the father. It is accounted for by {\i eizu zu l\u8217'motei} \u8212- the {\i eizu zu} formulation acknowledges the existence of such exceptions without enumerating them.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Category Resolution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now resolves how the various cases map onto the categories under Rabbi Yehudah versus Rabbi Yose. According to Rabbi Yehudah, who holds that a {\i ger} may not marry a {\i mamzeret}, the union of {\i ger} and {\i mamzeret} is one of {\i kiddushin v\u8217'yesh aveirah}, and the child following the {\i pagum} fits the second clause of the {\i mishnah}. The {\i kol makom} of the {\i reisha} includes the {\i chalala} and {\i Mitzri} cases as unlisted applications of paternal descent. The {\i eizu zu} of both clauses covers the exceptions \u8212- most notably the {\i umot shenitgayru} case of nations that converted.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 According to Rabbi Yose, who holds the {\i ger-mamzeret} union is permitted, that case is an exception: no {\i aveirah}, yet the child follows the {\i pagum}. It is covered by {\i eizu zu} as an unlisted exception. But the Gemara notes that under Rabbi Yose\u8217's reading, the {\i kol makom} of the {\i seifa} \u8212- the clause about {\i kiddushin v\u8217'yesh aveirah} and {\i holech achar hapagum} \u8212- has nothing additional to include. The Gemara deflects: {\i aidi d\u8217'tana reisha eizu zu, tana seifa eizu zu; aidi d\u8217'tana reisha kol makom, tana seifa kol makom} \u8212- the parallel structure of the {\i mishnah} requires that each clause employ both formulations, even if in a given clause one of them does no independent work. The literary symmetry of {\i kol makom} and {\i eizu zu} appearing in each half of the {\i mishnah} is itself sufficient justification for their inclusion, regardless of whether both are needed for substantive legal purposes in every case.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Non-Jewish Nations\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tribal Paternity\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara turns to the governing principle for non-Jews: {\i b\u8217'umos halach achar hazachar} \u8212- among the nations, one follows the father in determining tribal identity. The question of national or tribal membership for non-Jews is determined patrilineally.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Cana\u8217'anite Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara distinguishes between native {\i Kena\u8217'anim} \u8212- members of the seven nations \u8212- and non-native {\i Kena\u8217'anim}, those who happen to dwell in the land but whose fathers are not from the seven nations. The legal significance pertains to the laws of slavery: the Torah permits purchasing {\i Kena\u8217'ani} slaves from among the peoples dwelling in the land, but the application of this permission turns on the national identity of the person in question. The Gemara asks: from where do we know that a child born to a {\i Kena\u8217'ani} woman by a foreign father is not considered a {\i Kena\u8217'ani} for these purposes?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Slavery Proofs\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i talmud lomar}: {\i vegam mibnei hatoshavim hagarim imachem} \u8212- from among the children of the residents who dwell among you \u8212- you may acquire them as slaves. The verse distinguishes those who are resident sojourners in the land from native inhabitants. The Gemara understands this distinction as tracking the father\u8217's origin: if the father is foreign, the child, though born in the land, is not a native {\i Kena\u8217'ani}. The verse {\i mehem tiknu} \u8212- from them you may acquire \u8212- applies to such a child, and the implication is that the child\u8217's status is determined by the father\u8217's national identity.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Reverse Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The reverse case: a {\i Kena\u8217'ani} man who fathers a child with a non-{\i Kena\u8217'ani} woman. The verse {\i asher holidu b\u8217'artzechem} \u8212- who were born in your land \u8212- is read to indicate that being physically born in the land is not determinative; what matters is whether the father is from {\i Kena\u8217'an}. If the father is a {\i Kena\u8217'ani}, the child is considered to be living in {\i Kena\u8217'an} in the relevant sense, because the child\u8217's national identity follows the father. {\i Aval lo min hagarim b\u8217'artzechem} \u8212- but not from those who are merely dwelling in the land \u8212- distinguishes the case where the father is foreign: that child, though resident in the land, does not carry {\i Kena\u8217'ani} identity.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara acknowledges the circularity here: the {\i pesuk} does not explicitly mention the father as the determining factor; rather, the presumption that status follows the father is read into the verse, and then read back out of it as proof. Nevertheless, the result is consistent: for non-Jewish nations, tribal or national identity is determined patrilineally. The discussion of what occurs when such individuals convert \u8212- how to reckon the status of a child born to, for example, an {\i Edomi} convert and a {\i Moavi} convert \u8212- is deferred to the following session.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Kiddushin 67\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Two Witnesses Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 One vs.\u160 ?Two Witnesses\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The sugya opens with the dispute between Abaye and Rava concerning the principle of {\i ein davar she-be\u8217'erva pahot mi-shenayim} \u8212- that matters of personal status require no fewer than two witnesses. The question is not merely about the formal act of {\i kiddushin} or {\i get}, where two witnesses are needed to constitute the act itself, but about the evidentiary standard required to establish that such an act occurred, or to determine personal status more broadly. Rava holds that two witnesses are required both to effectuate and to prove matters of personal status. Abaye disagrees, maintaining that one uncontested witness suffices for proof even in such matters.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The stakes of this dispute extend well beyond the narrow domain of marriage and divorce. The same standard of proof applies, for instance, to questions of whether a woman was taken captive \u8212- which would render her forbidden to her {\i kohen} husband \u8212- or whether a woman committed adultery, which would render her forbidden to her husband entirely.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mikvah Case Source\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava introduces his proof from a {\i mishnah} (cited also in the {\i Tosefta}): {\i Amar Rabbi Shimon} \u8212- there was an incident involving a {\i megurah}, a cistern or body of water, in a place called Diskim (or, as Rashi notes, this may be a personal name rather than a place), situated in Yavneh. This {\i mikvah} had long stood with a {\i chezkat shelemah} \u8212- a presumption of having sufficient water. When it was eventually measured, it was found to be lacking the requisite forty {\i se\u8217'ah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The question that arises is: what is the status of all the purifications performed using this {\i mikvah} since the last time it was confirmed to be complete? The {\i mikvah} did not become deficient the very day after the prior measurement. So when did it lose its valid status, and what are the consequences for everything done in the interim?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mikvah Dispute\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Two {\i tanna\u8217'im} debate the question. Rabbi Tarfon rules leniently: {\i mikvah zeh be-hezkat shalem hu omed} \u8212- the {\i mikvah} stands with a presumption of being complete. He invokes the principle of {\i chazakah}: since the {\i mikvah} had a recognized valid status, one cannot retroactively undermine that status based on mere doubt about when it became deficient. The doubt does not override the {\i status quo} of the {\i mikvah} being {\i shalem}. This is the foundational logic of {\i chazakah}: we assume the prior status persists until we have definitive evidence that it changed, which means we push the change to the last possible moment \u8212- when the measurement was actually taken.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Akiva, however, rules stringently. He too invokes {\i chazakah}, but from the opposite direction: {\i adam zeh be-hezkat tame hu omed} \u8212- the person who immersed had a {\i status quo} of being {\i tameh} before entering the {\i mikvah}. Just as one cannot arbitrarily strip away the {\i mikvah\u8217's} status based on doubt, one equally cannot strip away the person\u8217's prior {\i tumah} status based on doubt. The fundamental question thus becomes a collision of two competing {\i chazakot}: the {\i chazakah} of the {\i mikvah} as valid versus the {\i chazakah} of the person as {\i tameh}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Tarfon then introduces a {\i mashal}: consider a {\i kohen} who was standing and offering {\i korbanot}, and it subsequently came to light that his mother had been divorced before marrying his father, making him a {\i ben grushah} and hence a {\i challal}. In that case, his {\i avodah} performed prior to the discovery is declared {\i ksherah} \u8212- valid. Now, admittedly, once we have this information, we know retroactively that he was a {\i challal} all along. Logically, one might expect his past {\i avodah} to be invalidated. Yet the {\i halacha} is that it is not, and the {\i gemara} will derive this from a {\i pasuk}. Rabbi Tarfon is invoking this case analogically, perhaps also gesturing toward the practical and societal consequences of the alternative \u8212- if a {\i kohen} at age seventy discovered he was a {\i ben grushah}, are we to retroactively recall and invalidate everything he performed over the preceding decades? There is reason, policy and otherwise, to affirm the prior status.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Comparative Arguments\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Akiva responds with a counter-analogy: consider a {\i kohen} who was offering {\i korbanot} and was subsequently discovered to have a {\i mum} \u8212- a bodily blemish. In that case, his {\i avodah} is {\i pesulah} \u8212- retroactively invalid. This is striking, because intuitively one might have thought the opposite: being a {\i ben grushah} or {\i ben chalutzah} is a more fundamental deficiency of status \u8212- it goes to the heart of one\u8217's {\i kehuna} \u8212- whereas a {\i ba\u8217'al mum} retains his {\i kedushat kehuna} in every other respect. Yet the {\i halacha} distinguishes sharply: the {\i ba\u8217'al mum} whose {\i avodah} is retroactively disqualified, and the {\i ben grushah} whose {\i avodah} is retroactively valid.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Akiva\u8217's point is structural: these two cases generate different legal consequences, so one must ask which case the {\i mikvah} scenario more closely resembles. And he argues that the {\i mikvah} case resembles the {\i ba\u8217'al mum} case: just as by a {\i ba\u8217'al mum} a single witness suffices to establish the disqualification, similarly, the deficiency of the {\i mikvah} can be established by one person \u8212- the deficiency is, so to speak, {\i pesulah bi-gufo}, present in the body of the thing itself. By contrast, the disqualification of a {\i ben grushah} or {\i ben chalutzah} \u8212- {\i psulo me-acherim} \u8212- derives from others, namely the conduct of the parents, and requires two witnesses.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Akiva then formalizes the comparison: {\i mikvah pasul bi-yachid u-va\u8217'al mum pasul bi-yachid} \u8212- the deficiency of the {\i mikvah} is established by one person and the {\i ba\u8217'al mum} is established by one person. But the {\i ben grushah} and {\i ben chalutzah} \u8212- {\i psulo bi-shnayim} \u8212- their disqualification requires two witnesses. This is precisely the reason the {\i gemara} introduced this entire passage: to prove that matters of {\i erva}, of personal status, require two witnesses. He additionally offers a second line of comparison: {\i mikvah pesulah be-gufah, ba\u8217'al mum pasul be-gufo} \u8212- the problem is inherent in the body of the {\i mikvah} or the person, whereas {\i ben grushah u-ven chalutzah} \u8212- {\i psulo me-acherim} \u8212- the problem originates externally, from what others did.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Tarfon, unable to counter this reasoning, concedes with characteristic rhetorical flourish: {\i kol ha-poreish mimcha ke-poreish min ha-chayim} \u8212- anyone who separates from you separates from life itself.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Contested Testimony\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then turns to interrogate the precise meaning of the {\i ba\u8217'al mum} case. It asks: {\i hai ba\u8217'al mum she-pesulah bi-yachid heichi dami?} \u8212- What exactly is the case where one witness suffices for a {\i ba\u8217'al mum}? If the {\i kohen} himself is contesting the claim \u8212- denying he has a blemish \u8212- would we really believe a single witness against him? Surely not. So presumably, says Rava, the case is where the {\i kohen} is not contesting the testimony \u8212- {\i de-shatik} \u8212- and one uncontested witness is believed. And yet, even in that scenario, by a {\i ben grushah} and {\i ben chalutzah} two witnesses are still required. This is Rava\u8217's proof: even uncontested testimony of a single witness is insufficient for personal status matters; two are always required.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye responds directly \u8212- and this is precisely the counterargument that presents itself naturally. Abaye says: {\i le\u8217'olam de-ka machish lei} \u8212- actually, we are dealing with a contested case. The {\i kohen} is denying it. But why, then, do we believe the single witness? Because the witness can say to the {\i kohen}: {\i shlach achvei} \u8212- strip and show us. If you deny having a blemish, demonstrate it. The single witness is believed even over the {\i kohen\u8217's} objection because the claim is objectively verifiable \u8212- the {\i ba\u8217'al mum} is {\i pesulah be-gufo}, the deficiency is on the body itself, and the {\i kohen\u8217's} refusal to demonstrate substantiates the witness\u8217's account.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is precisely why the comparison to {\i ben grushah} and {\i ben chalutzah} breaks down for Abaye. In that case, if the {\i kohen} contests a single witness\u8217's claim that he is a {\i ben grushah}, there is nothing to show, no demonstration possible. The status derives entirely from a historical fact about the parents \u8212- {\i psulo me-acherim} \u8212- and so a contested single witness cannot be believed, because there is no objective verification to demand. Hence, for contested testimony about personal status where there is nothing to demonstrate, two witnesses are required. But for Abaye, this is the reason for the two-witness requirement in the {\i ben grushah} case, not a general rule that personal status always requires two witnesses.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Personal Status Rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} does not definitively resolve the dispute at this juncture. Whether the {\i ba\u8217'al mum} case was contested or uncontested remains open \u8212- for if the {\i mum} is internal, there is nothing to demonstrate in any event, making it structurally more similar to an uncontested case, which would restore Rava\u8217's proof. The {\i gemara} then briefly addresses the underlying {\i halachic} sources for the distinction between {\i ben grushah} and {\i ba\u8217'al mum}. How do we know a {\i kohen} discovered to be a {\i ben grushah} does not have his past {\i avodah} invalidated? Rav Yehuda citing Shmuel derives it from the {\i pasuk} concerning Pinchas: {\i ve-haytah lo u-le-zaro acharav} \u8212- the covenant of {\i kehuna} extends to his descendants, {\i bein zera kasher bein zera pasul}, whether the offspring is fit or disqualified. The father of Shmuel derives it from the verse {\i bareich Hashem cheilo} \u8212- bless his strength \u8212- reading {\i cheilo} as referring even to {\i chullin she-bo}, those whose sanctity may be impaired, and {\i u-po\u8217'al yadav tirtzeh} as indicating acceptance of his service through the moment of discovery.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yochanan offers yet another source: {\i u-vata el ha-kohen asher yihyeh ba-yamim ha-hem} \u8212- you shall come to the {\i kohen} who will be in those days. This teaches that even if it turns out the {\i kohen} before whom you stood was later discovered to be unfit, the interaction was valid \u8212- {\i zeh kasher ve-nit-chalal}, he was fit and became disqualified thereafter.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Regarding the {\i ba\u8217'al mum}, Rav Yehuda citing Shmuel derives from the continuation of the Pinchas passage: {\i lachen emor hinni noten lo et briti shalom} \u8212- the covenant of {\i shalom} applies only {\i ke-shehu shalem}, when he is whole and unblemished, {\i ve-lo ke-shehu chaser}, not when he is lacking. The word {\i shalom} in the {\i pasuk} is written with a broken {\i vav} \u8212- a {\i vav keti\u8217'a} \u8212- as Rav Nachman notes, a scribal tradition attested in {\i Sifrei Torah}, with various traditions as to the precise form. This textual irregularity is the basis for reading {\i shalom} as {\i shalem}, and thereby limiting the grant of validity to an unblemished {\i kohen}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 On the conceptual level, this distinction remains genuinely puzzling. A {\i challal} has no {\i kedushat kehuna} at all \u8212- he is exempt from all its prohibitions and obligations \u8212- while a {\i ba\u8217'al mum} retains his sanctity fully, merely being unable to perform the {\i avodah}. Yet it is the {\i challal} whose prior {\i avodah} is retroactively valid and the {\i ba\u8217'al mum} whose prior {\i avodah} is retroactively disqualified. One possible line of reasoning, parallel to a point the {\i gemara} makes in another context, is that the {\i ba\u8217'al mum\u8217's} disqualification was present and objectively discoverable \u8212- it was there to be seen \u8212- whereas the {\i challal\u8217's} disqualification was a matter of hidden historical fact. That which was discoverable and observable carries a greater legal charge. But this remains difficult.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The practical ruling follows Rava \u8212- we generally rule {\i ein davar she-be\u8217'erva pahot mi-shenayim} even as a standard of proof. This has significant implications beyond invalidating personal status: a woman seeking to prove she is divorced, or a convert seeking to prove the validity of his conversion, faces the same evidentiary threshold. These remain live questions wherever status must be affirmatively established.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Genealogical Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Child Follows Father\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} opens with a tripartite framework governing {\i yichus} \u8212- genealogical status \u8212- based on the nature of the union between the parents.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The first principle: {\i kol makom she-yesh kiddushin ve-ein aveira, ha-velad holech achar ha-zachar} \u8212- wherever {\i kiddushin} is binding and there is no transgression, the child\u8217's status follows the father. The {\i mishnah} illustrates this with the most basic case: whether the mother is a {\i kohenet}, a {\i leviyah}, or a {\i yisraelit}, and the father is a {\i kohen}, {\i levi}, or {\i yisrael}, the child inherits the father\u8217's tribal and priestly status. A {\i kohen} father produces a {\i kohen} child regardless of the mother\u8217's background. This is the standard, unexceptional case of a valid and permitted marriage.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Child Follows Disqualifi\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The second principle: {\i kol makom she-yesh kiddushin ve-yesh aveira, ha-velad holech achar ha-pagum} \u8212- wherever {\i kiddushin} is binding but the union involves transgression, the child follows the disqualified party. The examples given are the forbidden unions of {\i kohanim}: an {\i almanah} with a {\i kohen gadol}, or a {\i grushah} or {\i chalutzah} with a {\i kohen hedyot}. The child born of such a union does not himself become a {\i kohen gadol} or a divorc\u233 ?e \u8212- rather, the child becomes a {\i challal}, stripped of the full sanctity of {\i kehuna}, which is the operative consequence of a forbidden {\i kohen} union. The conceptual principle is that the stigma of the forbidden relationship attaches to the offspring, regardless of whether the disqualified party is the father or the mother.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mamzer Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The third principle extends this logic to {\i mamzer} status: if a woman who is a {\i mamzeret} marries a {\i yisrael}, or a {\i yisrael} woman marries a {\i mamzer}, the child is a {\i mamzer}. The {\i kiddushin} in such unions is binding \u8212- since the prohibition is an {\i isur lav} and not an {\i isur karet} or punishable by death \u8212- but the child inherits the disqualified status of whichever parent bears it. This applies symmetrically regardless of whether the {\i mamzer} is the father or the mother.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Matrilineal Descent\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} then turns to two remaining categories defined by the woman\u8217's status. First: {\i kol mi she-ein lah alav kiddushin aval yesh lah al acherim kiddushin} \u8212- a woman with whom {\i kiddushin} is not binding for this particular man, but who can nonetheless contract valid marriage with others \u8212- {\i ha-velad mamzer}. This is the category of {\i arayot}, the forbidden incestuous and adulterous relations enumerated in the Torah. For instance, a married woman\u8217's union with someone other than her husband produces a {\i mamzer}, because {\i kiddushin} cannot take effect with the adulterer, but she remains capable of valid marriage with her husband.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Finally: {\i kol mi she-ein lah lo alav ve-lo al acherim kiddushin} \u8212- a woman who cannot contract valid marriage with anyone \u8212- {\i ha-velad kemotah}, the child follows the mother\u8217's status and is not a {\i mamzer}. The {\i mishnah} identifies this category as the {\i vlad shifcha ve-nochrit}, the child of a non-Jewish woman or a slave woman. Since no Jew can contract {\i kiddushin} with a non-Jewish woman or a slave, and since these women cannot marry any Jew, the child follows the mother entirely \u8212- and is either a non-Jew or a slave, not a {\i mamzer}. This is the {\i mishnah\u8217's} expression of matrilineal descent: where no Jewish {\i kiddushin} can attach to the mother in any circumstances, the child\u8217's status is defined entirely by her.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rule vs.\u160 ?Exception\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Challenge to Completeness\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} opens with Rabbi Shimon challenging Rabbi Yochanan: {\i klala hu?} \u8212- Is this general principle truly comprehensive? Does the rule {\i kol makom she-yesh kiddushin ve-ein aveira ha-velad holech achar ha-zachar} genuinely account for all cases?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Convert & Mamzeret\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Shimon immediately identifies a problem. {\i Harei ger she-nasa mamzeret} \u8212- consider a convert who married a {\i mamzeret}. According to one opinion, a {\i mamzeret} is permitted to marry a {\i ger}, making the {\i kiddushin} binding with no transgression. Yet even in that case, the child is a {\i mamzer} \u8212- {\i ha-velad holech achar ha-pagum}. Here we have a case of valid {\i kiddushin} with no {\i aveira}, yet the child does not follow the father; the child follows the stigmatized party. The {\i mishnah\u8217's} general rule is therefore violated.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i baraita} confirms: {\i ger she-nasa mamzeret \u8212- ha-velad mamzer}. This is the position of Rabbi Yosi. Rabbi Yochanan\u8217's response is that our {\i mishnah} need not be Rabbi Yosi\u8217's view. {\i Matnitin Rabbi Yehuda hi} \u8212- perhaps the {\i mishnah} reflects Rabbi Yehuda\u8217's position. Rabbi Yehuda holds that a {\i ger} is in fact {\i forbidden} to marry a {\i mamzeret} \u8212- {\i ger lo yisa mamzeret}. Under Rabbi Yehuda\u8217's view, the union involves transgression \u8212- {\i yesh kiddushin ve-yesh aveira} \u8212- and so the child following the {\i pagum} fits squarely within the {\i mishnah\u8217's} second rule. The apparent exception dissolves once we attribute the {\i mishnah} to Rabbi Yehuda.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Klal Inclusion\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But the {\i mishnah} does not list the {\i ger} and {\i mamzeret} case explicitly under the second rule, even according to Rabbi Yehuda. Why not? The {\i gemara} answers: {\i tanna kol makom le-atuyei} \u8212- the phrase {\i kol makom} functions as a {\i klal}, a general statement broad enough to include unlisted cases within the scope of the rule. The {\i mishnah} says {\i kol makom she-yesh kiddushin ve-yesh aveira} \u8212- wherever there is {\i kiddushin} and transgression \u8212- and that formulation is deliberately expansive, encompassing the {\i ger u-mamzeret} case without explicit mention.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Alternatively, even if the {\i mishnah} reflects Rabbi Yosi\u8217's view, the case of {\i ger u-mamzeret} would be an exception to the first rule, handled by the word {\i ve-eizo zu} in the {\i mishnah\u8217's} phrasing, as we shall see.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then raises a further unlisted case: {\i vaharei challal she-nasa bat Yisrael} \u8212- a {\i challal} who married a {\i bat Yisrael}. This union involves no transgression \u8212- a {\i challal} is not forbidden to a non-{\i kohen} woman \u8212- and {\i kiddushin} is fully binding. The child follows the father and is a {\i challal}. This is a straightforward application of the first rule, yet it is absent from the {\i mishnah\u8217's} list. Similarly, a {\i Yisrael} who married a {\i challala} \u8212- again, no transgression, binding {\i kiddushin}, and the child follows the father and is {\i kasher}. Why were these not listed?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} answers: {\i de-lo pasika lei} \u8212- the case cannot be stated cleanly given the {\i mishnah\u8217's} framing. If one tried to extend the list to include {\i challala}, one would write something like: {\i kohenet, leviyah, Yisraelit, ve-challala she-niset le-kohen, levi, ve-Yisrael} \u8212- but then one is implicitly including the possibility of a {\i challala} marrying a {\i kohen}, which is in fact {\i assur}. The case does not lend itself to the tidy phrasing the {\i mishnah} prefers, and so it was left out despite being a genuine application of the rule. The {\i klal} of {\i kol makom} is understood to encompass it nonetheless.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Eizehu Exceptions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Now the {\i gemara} examines the function of {\i eizehu} \u8212- the word {\i ve-eizo zu} that introduces the specific lists in the {\i mishnah}. Initially, there is an assumption that {\i ve-eizo zu} is exhaustive \u8212- that it confines the application of the rule to precisely those cases enumerated. If so, any unlisted application would be a problem for the {\i mishnah\u8217's} completeness. Jenna\u8217's observation here is apt: at this stage, the {\i gemara} is working with the premise that {\i ve-eizo zu} is limiting, before ultimately arriving at the resolution that {\i ve-eizo zu} serves a different function.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} develops the position that {\i ve-eizo zu} functions not only to define the scope of the rule but to flag exceptions \u8212- cases that fall outside the expected application of the rule. By saying \u8220"and which cases are these?\u8221" and then providing a list, the {\i mishnah} implicitly acknowledges that the list does not exhaust all possible applications; there are others not mentioned that nonetheless fall under the {\i klal}. At the same time, the specificity of the list implies that there are scenarios where one might have expected the rule to apply but where it does not. The word {\i eizehu} thus does double duty: it confirms that the rule applies to the listed cases, and it signals that certain unlisted cases are exceptions.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The overall framework that emerges is this: anything that is an application of the rule not explicitly listed is included under {\i kol makom}; anything that is an exception to the rule is signaled by {\i ve-eizo zu}. The two phrases together provide the {\i mishnah} with the flexibility to be both a general rule and a practically accurate guide.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Israelite & Challala\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Returning to the unlisted cases of {\i challal} and {\i Yisrael} \u8212- the {\i gemara} confirms that {\i tanna kol makom de-reisha le-atuyei}, the {\i kol makom} of the opening clause serves to include {\i Yisrael she-nasa challala} and {\i challal she-nasa bat Yisrael} as applications of the first rule, even though they were not explicitly enumerated. This is the {\i gemara\u8217's} formal resolution for those cases.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Framing Difficulty\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} returns to the question of why the {\i challal} case was not listed explicitly, even though it is a genuine application of the rule. The answer, as established, is that including {\i challala} in the list of women would require writing a formulation that also implies a {\i challala} could marry a {\i kohen} \u8212- {\i ve-challala le-kohen lo chazya} \u8212- which she cannot. The {\i mishnah} cannot phrase it in a single clean sentence without creating a false implication, and so it leaves the {\i challal} case to be derived from {\i kol makom}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Egyptian Generations\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} identifies another unlisted case: {\i Mitzri she-nasa Mitzrit} \u8212- an Egyptian convert who married a female Egyptian convert. The {\i halacha} is that an Egyptian convert may enter the community of Israel ({\i kahal Hashem}) only from the third generation: {\i dor shelishi yavo lahem bi-kehal Hashem}. The question is how generations are counted when the parents are of different generational standing within the Egyptian convert lineage.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabba bar bar Chana citing Rabbi Yochanan states: {\i Mitzri sheni she-nasa Mitzrit rishona \u8212- bena shelishi havei} \u8212- if a second-generation Egyptian convert married a first-generation Egyptian female convert, the child is a third-generation, and consequently permitted. Generations are counted by the father. This is another application of the rule that the child follows the father \u8212- {\i yesh kiddushin, ein aveira, ha-velad holech achar ha-zachar} \u8212- and it is included in the {\i klal} of {\i kol makom}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Non-Jewish Nations\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Ravin, citing Rabbi Yochanan, introduces a principle regarding non-Jews: {\i be-umot halech achar ha-zachar} \u8212- among the nations, the tribal identity of a child follows the father. If a man from one non-Jewish nation has a child with a woman from another nation, the child\u8217's nationality is determined by the father. This too is an unlisted application of the rule that status follows the male, and the {\i gemara} includes it under {\i kol makom de-reisha le-atuyei}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Converted Nations\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The same teaching of Ravin continues: {\i nitgayeru \u8212- halech achar ha-pagum she-be-shneihem} \u8212- if they converted, the child follows whichever parent bears the more disqualifying status. For instance, if an {\i Edomi} convert married a {\i Mitzri} convert \u8212- where the {\i Edomi} is restricted for two generations and the {\i Mitzri} for three \u8212- the child follows the more stringent status. This is a case of {\i yesh kiddushin, ein aveira} \u8212- two converts, permitted to marry one another \u8212- yet the child does not follow the father; it follows the {\i pagum}. This is an exception to the first rule, and the {\i gemara} includes it under the category of {\i eizehu} as an unlisted exception.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Klal & Eizehu Purpose\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now consolidates its analysis. According to Rabbi Yehuda \u8212- the likely author of the {\i mishnah} \u8212- the {\i ger u-mamzeret} case is an {\i aveira}, so it fits the second rule via the {\i kol makom} of the {\i seifa}. The {\i challal} and {\i Yisrael} cases are applications of the first rule also included in {\i kol makom}. The exceptions \u8212- the converted nations following the {\i pagum} despite no {\i aveira}, and possibly the {\i Mitzri} generation count depending on the ruling \u8212- are flagged by {\i eizehu}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 According to Rabbi Yosi, the {\i ger u-mamzeret} case is {\i muttar} and thus an exception to the first rule, included in {\i eizehu}. The converted nations\u8217' cases are likewise exceptions included there. But then the {\i kol makom} of the {\i seifa} \u8212- the general rule for when there is {\i kiddushin} and {\i aveira} \u8212- seems to have nothing left to include that is not already listed. The {\i gemara} resolves this by noting: {\i ayidi de-tana reisha eizo zu, tani seifa eizo zu} \u8212- since the {\i mishnah} uses {\i eizehu} in the first clause, it maintains the same structural parallel in the second clause, even if the second {\i eizehu} has no independent work to do in limiting or flagging exceptions there. Similarly, the {\i kol makom} of the {\i seifa} mirrors the {\i kol makom} of the {\i reisha} for structural reasons, even if \u8212- according to Rabbi Yosi \u8212- no additional cases need to be drawn in by the {\i seifa\u8217's kol makom}. The {\i mishnah\u8217's} symmetrical structure accounts for the repeated usage of both phrases.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Non-Jewish Tribal Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now examines the case of non-Jewish tribal identity in detail, returning to the teaching of Ravin. {\i Mai be-umot halech achar ha-zachar?} \u8212- What is the precise case where among non-Jews the child\u8217's tribal identity follows the father?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} cites a {\i baraita}: {\i minayin le-echad min ha-umot she-ba al Kena\u8217'anit ve-holid ben she-ata rashai liknoto be-eved?} \u8212- From where do we know that if a man from the nations (not one of the seven Canaanite nations) fathered a child with a {\i Kena\u8217'anit} woman, that child may be purchased as a slave and is not subject to the prohibition of destruction that applies to the seven nations? The {\i talmud lomar}: {\i ve-gam mi-benei ha-toshavim ha-garim imachem, me-hem tiknu} \u8212- from those who dwell as sojourners in your land, you may purchase. The {\i baraita} understands the distinction between \u8220"sojourners\u8221" and \u8220"native\u8221" to be defined by the father. If the father is from outside {\i Kena\u8217'an}, the child is not a {\i Kena\u8217'ani}, even if the mother is.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Conversely: if a {\i Kena\u8217'ani} man fathered a child with a non-{\i Kena\u8217'anit} woman \u8212- {\i yachol af eved she-ba al shifcha min ha-umot ve-holid ben she-ata rashai liknoto be-eved?} \u8212- perhaps that child is also purchasable as a non-{\i Kena\u8217'ani}? The {\i talmud lomar}: {\i asher holidu be-artzechem} \u8212- those born in your land, meaning those born into a foreign land whose father identifies them as belonging elsewhere. {\i Min ha-noladim be-artzechem \u8212- aval lo min ha-garim be-artzechem} \u8212- you may purchase those born here among sojourners, but not those who genuinely dwell in the land as part of the {\i Kena\u8217'ani} nations. And who belongs to the {\i Kena\u8217'ani} nations? Those whose father is {\i Kena\u8217'ani} \u8212- the child follows the father.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} acknowledges that what we have done here is read the assumption of paternal identity into the {\i pesukim} and then read it back out again as a proof \u8212- the {\i pesukim} speak of \u8220"born in your land\u8221" versus \u8220"sojourning in your land\u8221" without explicitly mentioning the father, and the determination of which category a child belongs to is being derived from the principle that follows the father. Nonetheless, this is the {\i tanna\u8217'im\u8217's} reading, and it establishes that for non-Jewish national identity, status is defined by the father.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The discussion of what happens when such individuals convert \u8212- for instance, an {\i Edomi} convert and a {\i Moavi} convert, or an {\i Ammoni} convert, each of whom carries different {\i halachic} consequences regarding entry into the Jewish community \u8212- will continue in the next {\i shiur}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Kiddushin 67b\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Converts & Lineage\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Restricted Nations\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The daf begins towards the end of 67b, with the final lines of the {\i mishnah} addressing the transmission of {\i yichus} \u8212- lineage status \u8212- in cases of marriage between individuals whose marital prospects are in some way restricted. The {\i mishnah} groups these cases by the nature of the relational status involved and how that status passes to children. Of particular concern here are converts from nations that carry restrictions on who they may marry into the broader Jewish community: the {\i mitzri} (Egyptian), the {\i amoni} (Ammonite), and the {\i moavi} (Moabite). These nations\u8217' converts, while permitted to marry {\i mamzerim} and other converts, face a higher bar before marrying into a community of fully accepted {\i yichus}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 3-Gen Barrier\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} states: {\i nitgaiyru halach achar hapgum shebishneihem} \u8212- if both converts, one must follow whichever of the two parties is the more {\i pagum}, the more restricted. The {\i gemara} immediately asks: {\i bemai} \u8212- what precise case is the {\i mishnah} describing? If we suggest it refers to an Egyptian man who married an Ammonite woman, there is no real competing problem: {\i amoni velo amonit} \u8212- the prohibition affecting Ammonites applies only to males. A female Ammonite convert faces no restriction whatsoever in marrying into the Jewish community. This is the same principle underlying the narrative of the Book of Ruth, where Ruth\u8217's Moabite origin posed no halachic obstacle to her marriage with Boaz, in keeping with the oral tradition that the exclusions of Ammon and Moab apply only to males.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} therefore concludes the {\i mishnah} must be discussing the reverse: a male Ammonite convert who married a female Egyptian convert. Here both parties carry restrictions, but they operate differently. The male Ammonite\u8217's restriction is permanent \u8212- his male descendants are perpetually barred from full integration. The female Egyptian convert, by contrast, transmits the {\i mitzri} status in a non-gendered fashion, such that her descendants face a three-generation barrier. So: {\i veim zachar havei, hai shadia batrei dideih} \u8212- if the couple has a male child, the child follows the father\u8217's status and is treated as an Ammonite, with the lifelong restriction; {\i veim nekeiva havei, shadia batrah didah} \u8212- if they have a daughter, she is treated as a second-generation Egyptian. This, then, is the meaning of {\i halach achar hapgum shebishneihem}: one does not simply take the worst of the two and apply it uniformly. Rather, the {\i yichus} transmitted depends on the child\u8217's gender, each child tracking the more restrictive parent according to their own status.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gender-Based Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This ruling raises a practical question about its modern application: does the three-generation barrier still apply to Egyptian converts today, long after {\i yetziat Mitzrayim}? The traditional understanding, at least in terms of {\i halacha}, is that the prohibition is not limited to the generations immediately following the Exodus. Whenever a {\i mitzri} converts, the three-generation restriction applies afresh. The arithmetic is significant: a convert\u8217's children are born Jewish but are second-generation {\i mitzri}; their children are third-generation {\i mitzri}; and only the grandchildren of the original convert are fully free of the restriction. Seventy-five years is a long time to carry that particular layer of {\i yichus} complexity.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Mamzerut Defined\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Modern Tech\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 At this point the {\i gemara} transitions to one of the central discussions in all of {\i shas} on the question of {\i mamzerus}: what is the precise link between a prohibited relationship and the status of {\i mamzer}? It is important to clarify at the outset that a child born out of wedlock between two people who are halachically permitted to marry each other is emphatically {\i not} a {\i mamzer}. {\i Mamzerut} is fundamentally tied \u8212- at least according to most {\i tanna\u8217'im} \u8212- to the prohibition on the relationship itself. The debate is over which level of prohibition suffices.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The question of advanced reproductive technologies is directly relevant here, since the status of {\i mamzer} requires an actual sexual relationship. A child conceived through in-vitro fertilization or artificial insemination, according to the majority view among {\i poskim}, would not be a {\i mamzer} even if the genetic parents could not halachically have relations. Rav Moshe Feinstein famously took the permissive position on such technologies, marshaling extensive {\i mekorot} to argue that the administering physician could not be considered the active male party in the halachic sense. The Satmar Rav held the opposite view, maintaining that artificial insemination constitutes a form of adulterous act. Most authorities today follow Rav Moshe\u8217's reasoning.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tannaim Dispute\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Among the {\i tanna\u8217'im}, the question of which prohibited relationships produce {\i mamzerut} is the subject of a three-way (or four-way) {\i machloket}. Rabbi Shimon HaTimni holds that {\i mamzerut} is produced only by {\i chayvei kraitot} \u8212- those forbidden unions punishable by {\i karet} or by {\i mitat beit din} (capital punishment). For these relationships, {\i kiddushin} does not take effect and the child is a {\i mamzer}. Rabbi Yehoshua takes an even narrower position: only those {\i arayot} that carry {\i mitat beit din} \u8212- actual capital punishment \u8212- create {\i mamzerut}; those punishable merely by {\i karet} do not.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Akiva View\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Akiva holds the most expansive view. His position, encountered already in {\i Yevamot} and elsewhere, is that {\i chayvei lavin} \u8212- any prohibited relationship carrying a negative commandment \u8212- also produces {\i mamzerut}, because {\i kiddushin} does not take effect in such cases. There are a small number of prohibited marriages that are {\i chayvei asei} or {\i issur asei} rather than {\i lavin}, but the vast majority involve some form of prohibition. Rabbi Akiva extends the principle of {\i lo tafsei bah kiddushin} accordingly.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Kiddushin Failure\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Divorce Passage\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} asks: {\i mena hanei milei} \u8212- what is the scriptural source for the rule that a certain category of prohibited union prevents {\i kiddushin} from taking effect, and that a child born from such a union is a {\i mamzer}? The {\i mishnah} had stated: {\i mi she-ein la alav kiddushin aval yesh la al acherim kiddushin \u8212- havlad mamzer} \u8212- a {\i mamzer} is the product of a union in which {\i this} man and {\i this} woman cannot be married, but the woman could in principle marry someone else.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} cites a teaching transmitted through a chain: Rabbi Chiyya bar Abba said in the name of Rabbi Yochanan, tracing back through Rabbi Yannai, and also through Rabbi Acha the son of Rava, tracing back to Rabbi Yossi Ha-Gelili. The proof text is the verse in the divorce passage: {\i ve-yatz\u8217'ah mi-beitah ve-halchah ve-haytah le-ish acher} \u8212- she may go and become the wife of another man. The {\i drasha}: {\i l\u8217'acherim v\u8217'lo likrovim} \u8212- she may marry \u8220"others,\u8221" but not the relatives of her ex-husband. This is not merely a statement that such a marriage would be prohibited; it implies that {\i kiddushin} would not take effect at all with those {\i krovim}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Abba Query\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Abba challenges this reading: {\i v\u8217'eima l\u8217'acher v\u8217'lo l\u8217'ven} \u8212- why not read the exclusion as applying only to the ex-husband\u8217's son, rather than to all relatives? The {\i gemara} responds: {\i ben b\u8217'hedya ketiv beih} \u8212- the son is already mentioned explicitly elsewhere, in the verse {\i lo yikach ish et eshet aviv} \u8212- a man may not marry his father\u8217's wife. And importantly, the term {\i lo yikach} \u8212- he shall not take \u8212- implies not only a prohibition but also an absence of valid {\i kiddushin}. So the verse using the word {\i acher} serves a distinct purpose: {\i shma mina l\u8217'acherim v\u8217'lo likrovim} \u8212- it broadens the exclusion to all relatives.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A further challenge is raised: perhaps both verses refer to the son, one teaching the prohibition {\i lechatchila} and the other teaching that even {\i bediavad} the {\i kiddushin} does not work. The {\i gemara} responds: {\i lechatchila m\u8217'achot isha nafka} \u8212- the prohibition itself is already derived from the case of {\i achot isha}, the wife\u8217's sister. That case becomes the paradigm for the entire category of {\i arayot} and for the principle that {\i kiddushin} does not take effect.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sister-in-Law\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The verse {\i acher} is therefore understood as teaching that a woman\u8217's {\i kiddushin} does not work with her ex-husband\u8217's relatives. The case of {\i achot isha} \u8212- a man\u8217's wife\u8217's sister \u8212- then becomes the {\i binyan av} (paradigmatic source) from which we extend to all {\i arayot}: just as {\i kiddushin} is impossible with one\u8217's wife\u8217's sister, so too with all forbidden relationships of equivalent or greater severity. This is also the classical source, from the opening of {\i Yevamot}, for the principle of {\i tzarat ervah} and the exemption from {\i yibum}. It is worth noting the tension at the heart of {\i Yevamot}: the wife\u8217's sister ({\i achot isha}) generates the paradigmatic prohibition on {\i yibum}, while the brother\u8217's wife ({\i eshet ach}) is precisely where the {\i mitzvah} of {\i yibum} is located \u8212- a tension that tells us much about how the Torah conceptualizes family structure and inter-clan relations.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Binyan Av Logic\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kal Vachomer\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 From the paradigm of {\i achot isha}, the {\i gemara} attempts a {\i kal vachomer}: {\i u-mah achot isha b\u8217'karet lo tikadesh, chayavei mitot beit din lo kol sheken} \u8212- if marriage to one\u8217's wife\u8217's sister, which carries the punishment of {\i karet}, renders {\i kiddushin} inoperative, then certainly relationships punishable by {\i mitat beit din} \u8212- the most severe category \u8212- should also preclude valid {\i kiddushin}. The {\i kal vachomer} appears straightforward at first glance.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Weaknesses\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 However, this {\i kal vachomer} is not airtight. One might posit that the severity of the death penalty actually reflects the fact that {\i kiddushin does} take effect \u8212- that is, the very reason the Torah requires capital punishment is because the {\i kiddushin} creates a real bond requiring more drastic severing. If so, the argument that a more severe prohibition must also preclude {\i kiddushin} is undermined: one could argue the opposite, that greater severity correlates with {\i kiddushin} being {\i tofes}. Without an independent intuition about which direction the {\i kal vachomer} should run, the argument is weakened.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A further challenge: perhaps both the {\i kal vachomer} source and its target refer only to {\i achot isha}, with one verse teaching the {\i lechatchila} prohibition and the other teaching the {\i bediavad} ineffectiveness of {\i kiddushin}. If so, the {\i kal vachomer} cannot be extended at all to other {\i arayot}. The {\i gemara} responds: {\i i hachi nami ashkhachan achot isha, she\u8217'ar arayot minalan} \u8212- we would then have established the rule only for {\i achot isha} itself, leaving all other {\i arayot} without a source.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Additionally, even granting the {\i binyan av} from {\i achot isha}, two cases pose a challenge: {\i eshet ish} and {\i eshet ach}. For {\i eshet ach} \u8212- the brother\u8217's wife \u8212- there is a {\i heter b\u8217'makom mitzvah}: the {\i mitzvah} of {\i yibum} creates an exception that applies to no other {\i ervah}. And {\i eshet ish} \u8212- the married woman \u8212- has a {\i heter b\u8217'chayei osran}: the prohibition can be removed during the lifetime of the one who created it, through divorce. That reduces the categorical severity of {\i eshet ish} as an {\i ervah}, making it a weaker paradigm and undermining the {\i binyan av}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Verbal Analogy\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yona \u8212- and the tradition also attributes this to Rav Huna the son of Rav Yehoshua \u8212- offers a different approach. The summary verse at the end of the {\i parasha} of {\i arayot} states: {\i kol asher ya\u8217'aseh mikol hato\u8217'evot ha\u8217'eleh venichretu} \u8212- whoever commits any of these {\i to\u8217'evot} shall be subject to {\i karet}. This groups all {\i arayot} together in a single category linked to {\i achot isha}: {\i hukshu kol ha\u8217'arayot kulehan l\u8217'achot isha}. Just as {\i kiddushin} does not take effect with {\i achot isha}, so too with all other {\i arayot} \u8212- {\i af kol ha\u8217'arayot kulam lo tafsi bah kiddushin}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is worth noting that {\i Tosafot} in their discussion of {\i mena hanei milei} addresses at length the relationship between the absence of {\i kiddushin} and the status of {\i mamzerut}, asking whether one necessarily causes the other, and concluding that they are tightly bound together \u8212- though the precise direction of causation is discussed extensively in {\i Yevamot} and elsewhere.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Exceptions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Niddah Problem\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The verbal analogy linking all {\i arayot} to {\i achot isha} immediately raises a major difficulty: {\i i hachi afilu niddah nami} \u8212- if we group all {\i arayot} together, then {\i niddah} should also be included, since the {\i Torah} lists the menstruant woman among the {\i arayot}. Yet everyone knows that a child born to a woman who was {\i niddah} at the time of conception is not a {\i mamzer}, and furthermore that {\i kiddushin} with an unmarried woman who happens to be {\i niddah} is fully valid. So {\i niddah} appears on the list of {\i arayot} but is categorically different.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This distinction is treated at length in related contexts. {\i Tosafot HaRosh} on {\i Yevamot} daf 2 confronts the question of whether a wife who was {\i niddah} at the time of her husband\u8217's death would create a {\i tzarat ervah} situation that exempts co-wives from {\i yibum}. {\i Tosafot HaRosh} concludes that {\i niddah} is not really called an {\i ervah} in the full halachic sense \u8212- even though she appears on the list. This parallels a {\i Tosafot} in {\i Sanhedrin} discussing why the laws of {\i yichud} do not apply with equal stringency between a husband and wife during {\i niddah} as they do with other {\i arayot}. Many {\i achronim}, particularly in the Brisker tradition \u8212- the Brisker Rav in his commentary on the {\i Rambam} \u8212- devote attention to the fundamental distinction between {\i niddah} and the other {\i arayot}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rashi} here clarifies the case: {\i im kidesh pnuyah b\u8217'imei niddutah} \u8212- what if a man betrothed an unmarried woman during her period of {\i niddah}? The {\i hava amina} would be that this {\i kiddushin} fails and any child would be a {\i mamzer}. As a side note, the separate question of {\i chupat niddah} \u8212- whether {\i nisuin} can take effect when the bride is {\i niddah} \u8212- is handled differently. Most {\i rishonim} do not view this as a major halachic obstacle, though the {\i Rambam} does quote an opinion that {\i nisuin} does not take hold during {\i niddah}. In practice, various arrangements are made to avoid having the couple in {\i yichud} before {\i niddah} concludes, though the practical logistics can become complex.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The resolution comes from {\i Abaye}, who states: {\i hakol modin b\u8217'va al haniddah v\u8217'al hasotah she\u8217'ein havlad mamzer} \u8212- everyone agrees that a child born following relations with a {\i niddah} or with a {\i sotah} is not a {\i mamzer}. This applies even to Rabbi Shimon HaTimni, who holds that any {\i chayav karet} prevents {\i kiddushin} \u8212- he nonetheless concedes that {\i niddah} is an exception. And it applies even to Rabbi Akiva, who holds that {\i chayvei lavin} produce {\i mamzerut} \u8212- he nonetheless concedes that {\i sotah} is an exception. A {\i sotah} \u8212- a woman who committed adultery \u8212- creates a prohibition ({\i issur lav}) on her husband resuming relations with her, but a child born from those subsequent relations is not a {\i mamzer}. (If the child were born from the adulterous relationship itself, that would raise separate questions about paternity, resolved in most cases through the {\i chazakah} of {\i rov beilos achar habaal} \u8212- the presumption that most of a married woman\u8217's intimacy is with her husband.)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Chizkiyah provides the scriptural basis for excluding {\i niddah}: {\i utehi niddatah alav} \u8212- the verse that describes how a man who has relations with a {\i niddah} acquires her {\i tumah} for seven days. The word {\i tehi} is read as a form of {\i havayah} (being/marriage), implying: {\i afilu bish\u8217'at niddutah tehei bah havayah} \u8212- even during her {\i niddah} state, the act of {\i havayah} (i.e., valid {\i kiddushin}) can apply to her. Hence {\i niddah} is carved out by explicit scriptural derivation from the general rule that {\i arayot} preclude {\i kiddushin}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hekkesh Rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This raises a methodological question: given that all {\i arayot} are grouped together by the summary verse, one could equally make the {\i hekkesh} to connect them all to {\i niddah} (permissive outcome) or to connect them all to {\i achot isha} (restrictive outcome). Why choose {\i achot isha} as the paradigm? The {\i gemara} answers with a principle: {\i kula bechumra lechumra makshinan} \u8212- when constructing a {\i hekkesh}, we always link to the more stringent outcome. This is worth noting as a methodological {\i klal} about how {\i drashot} operate, even though it has a somewhat arbitrary quality \u8212- it is presented as a rule of {\i drasha} rather than as an application of {\i safek deoraitah lechumra}, which belongs to a different domain entirely.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yevama Proof\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Acha bar Yaakov offers an alternative derivation via {\i kal vachomer}: {\i ma yevama shehi belav lo tafsi bah kiddushin, chayavei mita v\u8217'chayavei kraitot kol she-ken} \u8212- if a {\i yevama} (a deceased brother\u8217's childless widow), who is only an {\i issur lav}, cannot contract valid {\i kiddushin} with anyone other than the {\i yavam}, then certainly relationships punishable by death or {\i karet} should preclude {\i kiddushin} as well. The force of this argument relies on the principle of {\i yesh zika} \u8212- that the bond between the {\i yavam} and {\i yevama} begins automatically at the moment of the brother\u8217's death, creating something almost like a proto-{\i kiddushin} that prevents her from being acquired by anyone else. The {\i kal vachomer} thus runs from the least severe prohibition ({\i lav}) to the most severe.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two-Wives\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} challenges Rav Acha bar Yaakov\u8217's {\i kal vachomer}: if it holds, it would extend to {\i all} other {\i chayvei lavin} as well \u8212- a result that contradicts the accepted view that {\i chayvei lavin} do allow {\i kiddushin} to take effect. Rav Pappa resolves the issue with a direct proof: {\i chayevei lavin be-hedaya ktiv behu} \u8212- the {\i Torah} makes explicit that {\i chayvei lavin} do produce valid {\i kiddushin}. The source is the passage in {\i Parshat Ki Teitzei}: {\i ki tehiyena le-ish shtei nashim ha\u8217'achat ahuvah veha\u8217'achat snuah} \u8212- when a man has two wives, one beloved and one hated. The {\i gemara} asks: does God play favorites? Rather, {\i ahuvah ahuvah benisu\u8217'eha, snuah snuah benisu\u8217'eha} \u8212- one wife\u8217's marriage is \u8220"beloved,\u8221" meaning halachically favorable, while the other\u8217's is \u8220"hated,\u8221" meaning she is someone he is prohibited from marrying \u8212- yet the {\i kiddushin} works. This is direct scriptural proof that {\i chayvei lavin} are {\i tofsei bakiddushin}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 R. Akiva Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Issur Aseh\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Given that the two-wives passage proves {\i chayvei lavin} allow {\i kiddushin}, how does Rabbi Akiva \u8212- who holds that {\i chayvei lavin} do not allow {\i kiddushin} \u8212- interpret that verse? He must hold that it refers to {\i almana l\u8217'kohen gadol}: the case of a High Priest marrying a widow, which is not a {\i lav} in the ordinary sense. Here it is important to correct a possible misreading of the relevant categories. The prohibition on a {\i kohen gadol} marrying an {\i almana} is indeed a {\i lav} \u8212- it is listed explicitly in the verse {\i almana ugirusha vachalala zonah et eileh lo yikach}. What is an {\i issur asei}, rather than a {\i lav}, is the prohibition on a {\i kohen gadol} marrying a woman who has been sexually active but was not previously married ({\i beulah} who is not {\i almana} or {\i grushah}) \u8212- since the positive commandment {\i ki im betulah m\u8217'amav yikach isha} requires him to marry a {\i betulah}, and the prohibition on {\i beulah} is a {\i lav haba m\u8217'klal asei}, a prohibition derived from a positive commandment.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 For Rabbi Akiva, who maintains that {\i chayvei lavin} preclude {\i kiddushin}, the verse {\i ki tehiyena le-ish shtei nashim} must describe a case where one wife is halachically acceptable and the other is not \u8212- but the \u8220"not\u8221" must fall outside the category of ordinary {\i lavin} (since Rabbi Akiva holds those don\u8217't produce {\i kiddushin} at all). The only case that fits is {\i beulah l\u8217'kohen gadol}, an {\i issur asei}, because {\i issur asei} does allow {\i kiddushin} even according to Rabbi Akiva. The inheritance law in that passage \u8212- that the father cannot favor the son of the beloved wife over the firstborn son of the disfavored wife \u8212- would then apply to a {\i kohen gadol} who married one {\i betulah} and one {\i beulah}, producing children from both.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Simai\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This reading of Rabbi Akiva\u8217's position is associated with Rabbi Simai, whose opinion is cited in a {\i baraita}: {\i min hakol haya Rabbi Akiva oseh mamzer chutz m\u8217'almana l\u8217'kohen gadol} \u8212- from all prohibited unions, Rabbi Akiva holds the child is a {\i mamzer}, except for {\i almana l\u8217'kohen gadol}. In that exceptional case, the Torah says {\i lo yichalel} \u8212- he shall not desecrate \u8212- implying that the child of such a union becomes a {\i chalal}, not a {\i mamzer}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i rishonim} note that this reasoning should extend beyond the {\i kohen gadol} to a regular {\i kohen} who marries a {\i grushah}, since the underlying principle \u8212- that the Torah provides a distinct category for priestly offspring of prohibited unions \u8212- applies there as well. The child of a {\i kohen} who marries someone he is forbidden to marry does not become a {\i mamzer} but a {\i chalal}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chalal Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The distinction between {\i chalal} and {\i mamzer} is significant. A {\i mamzer} represents a drastic reduction in {\i yichus} \u8212- from whatever level the parents occupied, the child falls to the most restricted category. A {\i chalal}, by contrast, represents only one step down from {\i kohen} status; in practical terms, a {\i chalal} is comparable in many respects to a regular {\i Yisrael}. He cannot serve as a {\i kohen}, cannot eat {\i trumah}, and is cut off from the priestly lineage \u8212- but unlike a {\i mamzer}, a {\i chalal} is not restricted in whom he may marry. Interestingly, there is a path back: a {\i chalal}\u8217's daughter, if she marries a {\i kohen}, may produce sons who are fully qualified {\i kohanim} again. The priestly status can be recovered within one generation through the maternal line, which has no parallel for {\i mamzerim}. The only classical route out of {\i mamzerus} involved slavery \u8212- a mechanism no longer operative.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Shaveiv\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Shaveiv disagrees sharply with Rabbi Simai\u8217's limiting principle. He declares: {\i bo u\u8217'nitzvach al Akiva ben Yosef} \u8212- let us take Rabbi Akiva to task. In Rabbi Shaveiv\u8217's reading, Rabbi Akiva\u8217's position \u8212- that {\i kol she-ein lo biah b\u8217'Yisrael havlad mamzer}, every prohibited union produces a {\i mamzer} \u8212- is an almost absurdly sweeping rule that would create {\i mamzerim} in great numbers, since not everyone observes {\i halacha}. Rabbi Shaveiv\u8217's objection has the character of a {\i sevara} argument: the sheer breadth of the position makes it untenable. It is also notable that Rabbi Shaveiv does not grant Rabbi Simai\u8217's exception for {\i almana l\u8217'kohen gadol} \u8212- he holds that even that union produces a {\i mamzer}. The {\i gemara} notes the somewhat animated quality of this exchange; similar rhetorical intensity appears elsewhere, such as Reish Lakish\u8217's famous protest against Rabbi Yochanan regarding the {\i hekkesh havayah l\u8217'yetziah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Reconciling\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Passage Scope\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now reconciles the competing {\i drashot}. Those who accept Rabbi Akiva\u8217's position in the reading of Rabbi Simai \u8212- that only {\i chayvei lavin} (and above) preclude {\i kiddushin} according to Rabbi Akiva, with the single exception of {\i almana l\u8217'kohen gadol} \u8212- must account for the two-wives passage, which Rav Pappa argued demonstrates that {\i chayvei lavin} do allow {\i kiddushin}. For Rabbi Akiva, that passage must instead refer to {\i beulah l\u8217'kohen gadol}, which is an {\i issur asei}. For the {\i rabbanan} who disagree with Rabbi Akiva, the passage applies more broadly to {\i chayvei lavin} in general.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} works through the four operative categories: {\i mitat beit din}, {\i karet}, {\i lavin}, and {\i asei}. The alignment is as follows: Rabbi Akiva holds that for {\i mitat beit din}, {\i karet}, and {\i lavin}, there is no {\i kiddushin}; only {\i issur asei} allows {\i kiddushin}. Rabbi Shimon HaTimni holds that {\i mitat beit din} and {\i karet} preclude {\i kiddushin}, while {\i lavin} and {\i asei} allow it. Rabbi Yehoshua holds that only {\i mitat beit din} precludes {\i kiddushin}. Within these broad categories, various specific exceptions apply: {\i yevama} falls within {\i lavin} but is universally agreed to preclude {\i kiddushin} (based on {\i yesh zika}); {\i eshet ach} has a {\i heter} in the context of the {\i mitzvah} of {\i yibum}; {\i eshet ish} has a {\i heter} through divorce; and {\i niddah} and {\i sotah} are conceded exceptions even by the more stringent {\i tanna\u8217'im}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gender Issue\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A further challenge to reading the two-wives passage as referring to {\i arayot} arises from gender specificity. The passage describes {\i shtei nashim} \u8212- two wives \u8212- implying the scenario is one man with two women. But the case of {\i achot isha}, the paradigmatic {\i ervah}, would involve a man married to two sisters simultaneously \u8212- both women are equally prohibited to him (once he has married one of them). There is no way to configure {\i achot isha} as \u8220"one acceptable and one unacceptable\u8221" within the framework the two-wives passage describes. The passage therefore cannot be speaking about {\i arayot} at all; it must be speaking about a different category of prohibited marriage that still allows {\i kiddushin}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Beulah Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This leaves {\i beulah l\u8217'kohen gadol} as the only case compatible with Rabbi Akiva\u8217's reading of the passage. The verse {\i ki tehiyena le-ish shtei nashim} is set in a context that says {\i le-ish} \u8212- to a man \u8212- rather than {\i le-kohen}, which is the objection raised against Rabbi Akiva\u8217's reading. The {\i gemara} concludes that Rabbi Akiva\u8217's {\i drasha} is admittedly strained ({\i baal karkhakh shavkah likra dehavi dakhik umuki anafshei}) \u8212- his reading is difficult to fit naturally into the verse, and in this sense the {\i rabbanan}\u8217's position, which applies the verse more naturally to a broader range of cases, is to be preferred. The {\i rabbanan} apply the two-wives passage to {\i chayvei lavin} in general, establishing that those unions do produce valid {\i kiddushin}, while Rabbi Akiva is forced to restrict the verse to a single, highly specific case in order to maintain his position.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Slave & Goy\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shifcha Kiddushin\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} addressed a third category: cases where a woman cannot marry {\i this} man and also cannot marry any Jewish man \u8212- the {\i shifchah} (slave) and the {\i nochrit} (non-Jewish woman). The {\i gemara} now asks for the scriptural basis for the rule that the child\u8217's status follows the mother in these cases.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Regarding the {\i shifchah kenaanit}: Rav Huna derives from the verse {\i shevu lachem poh im hachamor} \u8212- Abraham\u8217's instruction to Eliezer his servant to stay behind with the donkey at the time of the {\i akeidah} \u8212- the principle that slaves are comparable in some respects to animals: {\i am hadomeh lachamor}. The {\i drasha} is that just as animals have no {\i kiddushin}, slaves \u8212- {\i shifchah kenaanit} specifically \u8212- have no {\i kiddushin} either: {\i v\u8217'shifchah lo tafsei bah kiddushin}. It is a striking and harsh {\i drasha} to deploy for so fundamental a {\i halacha}. The broader tendency to assimilate the legal status of non-Jews or slaves to that of animals in certain specific halachic contexts appears in other {\i sugyot} as well, such as the discussion in {\i Avodah Zarah} about whether a non-Jewish corpse transmits {\i tumah be\u8217'ohel}, where the verse {\i atem keruyim adam v\u8217'ein ovdei avodah zarah keruyim adam} is invoked in a {\i machloket tanna\u8217'im}. In each such case, the implication is precisely calibrated to the halachic question at hand rather than serving as a global statement.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shifcha Children\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having established that {\i kiddushin} does not take effect with a {\i shifchah kenaanit}, the {\i gemara} asks separately: {\i v\u8217'valdah kemotah minalan} \u8212- how do we know that the child of a {\i shifchah} follows the mother\u8217's status and remains a slave? The source is: {\i ha\u8217'ishah v\u8217'yeladeha tiyeh l\u8217'adoneha} \u8212- the woman and her children shall belong to her master. This verse addresses the scenario in {\i parshat mishpatim} where a master gives his {\i eved ivri} (Hebrew slave) a {\i shifchah kenaanit} as a wife. Any children born from that union remain with the master as slaves, even when the {\i eved ivri} goes free. The children follow the mother \u8212- the {\i shifchah} \u8212- and stay with her master.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Non-Jew Kiddushin\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The case of the {\i nochrit} \u8212- a non-Jewish woman \u8212- is handled next. The {\i gemara} cites: {\i lo titchaten bam} \u8212- you shall not intermarry with them. This establishes that {\i kiddushin} does not take effect between a Jewish man and a non-Jewish woman. One might ask whether this verse teaches the impossibility of {\i kiddushin} or merely a prohibition on an otherwise-valid act. The {\i gemara} here reads it as {\i lo tafsei bah kiddushin} \u8212- the {\i kiddushin} simply does not work. {\i Tosafot} raise the obvious difficulty: {\i lo titchaten bam} is explicitly addressed to the seven Canaanite nations ({\i zayin amamim}), not to all non-Jews. {\i Rashi} responds at length, distinguishing between the seven nations \u8212- who are described as deeply entrenched in their {\i avodah zarah} in a way categorically different from other peoples \u8212- and non-Jews outside the Land of Israel, about whom the {\i gemara} in {\i Chullin} says: {\i lav ovdei avodah kochavim hen, ela minhag avoteihem beyadeihem} \u8212- they are not true idolaters but merely following ancestral practice. {\i Rashi} uses this distinction to explain why the prohibition extends to all non-Jews despite the verse\u8217's specific context, and {\i Tosafot} deploy the same reasoning in a number of {\i sugyot} to argue for more lenient approaches to relations with Christians in medieval Europe.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Descent Rules\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having established that {\i kiddushin} is impossible with a non-Jewish woman, the {\i gemara} asks how we know the child follows the mother: {\i v\u8217'valdah kemotah minalan}? Rabbi Yochanan in the name of Rabbi Shimon ben Yochai derives this from the verse: {\i ki yasir et bincha me\u8217'acharei} \u8212- for he will turn your son away from following Me. The {\i drasha} is: {\i bincha habah mi-Yisre\u8217'elit karui bincha} \u8212- your son born from a Jewish woman is called \u8220"your son,\u8221" {\i v\u8217'ein bincha habah min ha-ovedet kochavim karui bincha} \u8212- but your son born from a non-Jewish woman is not called \u8220"your son.\u8221" The non-Jewish mother\u8217's child, even though his father is Jewish, is not halachically Jewish. {\i Ravina} adds: {\i shma mina ben bitcha habah min ha-oved kochavim karui bincha} \u8212- but the son of your daughter born to a non-Jewish man is called your son. That is, your grandchild from a Jewish daughter and a non-Jewish father is Jewish.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Ravina}\u8217's ruling prompts a question: does this not imply that {\i Ravina} holds the child of a non-Jewish man or a slave who has relations with a Jewish woman is a {\i mamzer}? The {\i gemara} responds: {\i nehi de-kasher, mamzer lo havi} \u8212- granted the child is not fully {\i kasher}, but he is certainly not a {\i mamzer}. Rather, he is called {\i pasul} \u8212- specifically {\i pasul l\u8217'kehunah}, disqualified from the priesthood. A Jewish woman who has a child with either a non-Jew or a slave produces a child who is Jewish but who carries this disqualification one generation forward.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yifat Toar\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} extends this discussion to provide the source for those {\i rabbanan} who do not interpret {\i lo titchaten bam} as applying to all non-Jewish nations (following Rabbi Shimon\u8217's principle of {\i darish ta\u8217'ama dikra}). For them, the proof that {\i kiddushin} does not take effect with a non-Jewish woman comes from the passage of {\i yifat to\u8217'ar} \u8212- the non-Jewish captive woman taken in war. The verse states: {\i v\u8217'achar ken tavo eleiha u-ve\u8217'altah} \u8212- afterward you may come to her and have marital relations. The word {\i achar} \u8212- afterward, following a waiting period of transformation \u8212- implies: {\i michlal d\u8217'meikara lo tafsi bah kiddushin} \u8212- that initially, before this process was completed, {\i kiddushin} with her was impossible. This establishes that in the original state of a non-Jewish woman, {\i kiddushin} is inoperative.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 From this, the {\i gemara} then derives the principle that the child follows the mother in all such cases. The verse {\i tihyenah le-ish v\u8217'yaldu lo} \u8212- when women are married to a man and bear him children \u8212- is read as establishing a link: {\i ke-heicha d\u8217'karinan beih \u8216'ki tihyenah\u8217' karinan beih \u8216'v\u8217'yaldu lo\u8217'} \u8212- only where valid marriage ({\i tihyenah}) applies does the attribution of children to the father ({\i yaldu lo}) follow. Where {\i kiddushin} cannot take effect \u8212- as with the {\i shifchah kenaanit} and the {\i nochrit} in her initial state \u8212- the children are attributed to and follow the mother, not the father. This is the final and comprehensive statement of the {\i mishnah}\u8217's rule: the child follows the legally recognized parent, and where only the mother can contract a Jewish legal status, the child\u8217's status is determined by hers.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Kiddushin 68\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Converts & Yichus\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Opening & Daf\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Today\u8217's daf is 68, though we begin at the very end of 67b, in the last three narrow lines of that amud.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishnah: Yichus Cases\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} under discussion addresses four distinct categories regarding {\i yichus} \u8212- lineage and personal status \u8212- examining how the status of children is transmitted when parties of differing legal standing attempt to marry one another. The {\i mishnah} uses rather striking descriptive language to organize these cases, grouping them by the nature of the union and the resulting {\i yichus} of the offspring.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Restricted Nations\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One of the cases treated by the {\i mishnah} concerns individuals who have converted from nations subject to special restrictions on their marital prospects. The nations in question are {\i Mitzri} (Egyptian), {\i Ammoni} (Ammonite), and {\i Moavi} (Moabite). These groups are permitted to marry converts and {\i mamzerim}, but face a higher standard before they may enter into the fully accepted Jewish community by marriage. The principle stated is {\i nitgayru, halach achar hapagum shebishneihem} \u8212- when both parties convert, one follows the most {\i pagum} (flawed or restricted) status between the two of them.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} immediately asks: {\i bimai} \u8212- what case exactly are we discussing? If we say it is a {\i Mitzri} man who married an {\i Amonit} woman, there is a problem: the restriction for Ammonites, as established by {\i Torah she-be\u8217'al peh} and as the background for the entire discussion in {\i Masechet Yadayim} and for much of {\i Sefer Rut}, applies only to males ({\i Amoni velo Amonit}). The female Ammonite is not restricted from marrying into the Jewish people at all \u8212- which is precisely why {\i Rut} was able to marry {\i Boaz} without any halachic difficulty. Therefore, if an Egyptian man married an Ammonite woman, there is no competing {\i pagum} on her side, and the formulation {\i hapagum shebishneihem} \u8212- the more restricted of the two \u8212- cannot apply.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rather, the {\i gemara} redirects: {\i ela be\u8217'Ammoni shenasa Mitzrit} \u8212- we are discussing a male Ammonite convert who married a female Egyptian convert. Here, the husband\u8217's restriction as an {\i Ammoni} is perpetual for male descendants, while the wife\u8217's restriction as a {\i Mitzrit} is not gender-specific and applies for three generations. The {\i gemara} then clarifies how the {\i mishnah}\u8217's rule operates: {\i ve\u8217'im zachar havei, hai shadia batrei didei} \u8212- if they have a male child, that child follows the father\u8217's status, meaning the child is treated as a male Ammonite convert, whose restriction is perpetual. {\i Ve\u8217'im nekeivah hava, shadia batar didah} \u8212- if they have a female child, that daughter follows the mother\u8217's status, treated as a second-generation {\i Mitzrit}. The children are thus allocated by gender to the respective parental status \u8212- not a simple \u8220"take the worst of both\u8221" rule, but a gender-based bifurcation of {\i yichus}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Three Generations\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might ask: does the three-generation restriction for an Egyptian convert date from {\i Matan Torah}, applying only to those three generations after the Exodus? The traditional understanding, however, as reflected in {\i halacha}, is that {\i mitzvot} are binding for all generations. Any {\i Mitzri} who converts at any time in history still faces a three-generation barrier before full integration into the mainstream Jewish community. The convert\u8217's children are born Jewish, but are second-generation {\i Mitzri}; their children are third-generation {\i Mitzri}; and only thereafter are the descendants fully unrestricted. That represents a span of perhaps seventy-five years \u8212- a significant ongoing tracking of {\i yichus}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Mamzerut & Kiddushin\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mamzer Definition\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} now introduces the third category: {\i kol she-ein lah alav kiddushin} \u8212- any woman for whom there is no {\i kiddushin} possible with this particular man, but who could in principle marry someone else \u8212- {\i ha-valad mamzer}. The child of such a union is a {\i mamzer}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is important to emphasize at the outset what {\i mamzerut} is and is not. A child born out of wedlock to two people who are halachically permitted to marry one another is not a {\i mamzer}. {\i Mamzerut} is fundamentally linked \u8212- at least according to most {\i tanna\u8217'im} \u8212- to the prohibition itself upon the marriage. The connection between the prohibited nature of the union and the {\i mamzer} status of the offspring is what the {\i gemara} now seeks to establish.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Four Schools\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 There is a four-way {\i machloket} among {\i tanna\u8217'im} on this question. {\i Rabbi Shimon HaTimni} holds that {\i mamzerut} arises specifically from {\i chayavei krisos} \u8212- those forbidden relationships ({\i arayot}) for which the Torah prescribes {\i karet} or {\i mitat beit din} (capital punishment). In those cases, the {\i kiddushin} does not take effect and the children are {\i mamzerim}. {\i Rabbi Akiva} takes a far more expansive position: {\i chayavei lavin} \u8212- any prohibited relationship backed by a negative commandment \u8212- likewise produces {\i mamzerut}, because {\i kiddushin} cannot take hold. {\i Rabbi Yehoshua} is actually narrower than {\i Rabbi Shimon HaTimni}, holding that only those {\i arayot} punishable with {\i mitat beit din} \u8212- actual capital crimes \u8212- generate {\i mamzerut}; {\i karet} alone is insufficient.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sexual Relation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 An important clarification: in order for a child to be a {\i mamzer}, there must be an actual sexual relationship. A child conceived through {\i in vitro} fertilization or artificial insemination, even from a prohibited union, would not be a {\i mamzer} in the same sense. This is precisely the axis of a famous dispute among {\i poskim} regarding advanced reproductive technologies. {\i Rav Moshe Feinstein} was the permissive opinion on artificial insemination and {\i in vitro} fertilization, marshaling many {\i mekorot} in his argument. The {\i Satmar Rav} was strongly opposed, maintaining that a doctor administering such procedures might be regarded as the active male party in the fertilization and thus the act could be considered adulterous. The consensus among contemporary authorities has generally followed {\i Rav Moshe}\u8217's reasoning.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Divorce Pasuk\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} asks: {\i mena hanei milei} \u8212- what is the source for the rule that prohibited unions produce {\i mamzerut}? {\i Rabbi Chiyya bar Abba} said in the name of {\i Rabbi Yochanan}, transmitted through {\i Rabbi Yannai} and {\i Rabbi Acha bereih de-Rabba} in the name of {\i Rabbi Yossi HaGlili}, that the source is the {\i pasuk} concerning divorce: {\i ve-yatzah mi-beisah ve-halchah ve-haysah le-ish acher} \u8212- the divorced woman leaves and may go become the wife of another man. The {\i drasha} derives: {\i le\u8217'acherim velo likrovim} \u8212- she may marry others, but not close relatives of her ex-husband. This teaches not merely a prohibition but a substantive legal consequence: the {\i kiddushin} would not take effect at all between a divorced woman and her ex-husband\u8217's relatives.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Abba Query\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Matkif lah Rabbi Abba} \u8212- Rabbi Abba challenges this derivation. Why not read the {\i pasuk} more narrowly: she may marry someone else, but specifically not this man\u8217's {\i son} \u8212- not {\i krovim} in general? The {\i gemara} answers: the prohibition against a son marrying his father\u8217's ex-wife is stated explicitly elsewhere \u8212- {\i lo yikach ish et eshet aviv} \u8212- and that {\i pasuk} already teaches that the {\i kiddushin} would not work in that case. Therefore the phrase {\i le-ish acher} must be serving an additional purpose, and from it we derive {\i le\u8217'acherim velo likrovim} \u8212- she may marry other people generally, but not his relatives.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then considers: perhaps both {\i pesukim} refer to the son, one teaching the {\i lechatchila} prohibition and the other the {\i bedi\u8217'eved} invalidity of any attempted {\i kiddushin}? The response is that we already know from {\i achot isha} \u8212- a wife\u8217's sister \u8212- that the prohibition itself is established. Therefore the second {\i pasuk} must be extending the rule of invalid {\i kiddushin} to {\i krovim} more broadly.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Achot Isha Paradigm\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Achot isha} \u8212- the wife\u8217's sister \u8212- becomes the paradigmatic case for the inability of {\i kiddushin} to take effect among {\i arayot}. This is the same {\i achot isha} that appears at the very opening of {\i Yevamot} and forms the basis for the exemption from {\i yibum} in the case of {\i tzarat ervah}. Here it serves as the foundation for the broader rule: just as one cannot legally marry his wife\u8217's sister \u8212- not merely that it is prohibited but that any attempted {\i kiddushin} is legally null \u8212- so too should the same apply across the spectrum of {\i arayot}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kal Vachomer\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 From {\i achot isha}, the {\i gemara} attempts a {\i kal vachomer}: if {\i achot isha}, which carries the punishment of {\i karet}, disables {\i kiddushin}, then {\i chayavei mitat beit din} \u8212- those {\i arayot} punishable with capital punishment, which are in some ways more severe \u8212- should certainly ({\i lo kol shekein}) also disable {\i kiddushin}. One questioner raises the point that {\i achot isha} becomes permitted after the death of the wife, which might seem to reduce the severity of that prohibition. Yet this observation applies specifically to the {\i yesh la heter b\u8217'makom mitzvah} discussion that follows and does not fully undermine the {\i kal vachomer} as presented here.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kal Vachomer Weak\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i kal vachomer} is not without its difficulties. One might argue that the reason the Torah prescribes a harsher punishment for certain {\i arayot} is precisely because {\i kiddushin does} take effect there \u8212- the severity of the violation is greater because a genuine legal bond was formed. That is, there is no clear intuition about whether it is more {\i chamur} that {\i kiddushin} should work or that it should not work. The relationship between severity of punishment and validity of {\i kiddushin} is not self-evident, and this undercuts the {\i kal vachomer}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Binyan Av\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} therefore pivots to a {\i binyan av} from {\i achot isha}: just as {\i achot isha} is distinguished by the fact that she is an {\i ervah}, the violation carries {\i karet}, and a {\i korban chatat} if done {\i be-shogeg} \u8212- and {\i kiddushin} does not take effect \u8212- so too any {\i ervah} sharing those same features (punishment of {\i karet}, {\i chatat} for inadvertent violation) should likewise disable {\i kiddushin}. The formulation is: {\i mah achot isha miyuchedet she-hi ervah ve-chayavin al zdonah karet ve-shigegata chatat lo tafsei bah kiddushin, af kol she-hi ervah ve-chayavin al zdonah karet ve-shigegata chatat lo tafsei bah kiddushin}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Exceptions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Two cases threaten to break this {\i binyan av}: {\i eshet ish} (a married woman) and {\i eshet ach} (a brother\u8217's wife). The {\i gemara} articulates why each is different.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Regarding {\i eshet ach}: the prohibition against a brother\u8217's wife is in a different category because there exists a situation in which marrying her is not merely permitted but obligatory \u8212- namely {\i yibum}, the levirate marriage commanded when a brother dies childless. This {\i heter b\u8217'makom mitzvah} marks {\i eshet ach} as qualitatively distinct from other {\i arayot} and prevents us from using the {\i binyan av} to conclude that {\i kiddushin} with her never takes effect.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Regarding {\i eshet ish}: this prohibition can be lifted during the lifetime of the one who created the {\i issur} \u8212- the husband \u8212- through divorce. This {\i yesh lah heter b\u8217'chayei osra} makes {\i eshet ish} a lesser level of {\i ervah} in one respect, since there is a mechanism for removal. Therefore the {\i binyan av} from {\i achot isha} \u8212- which has no such escape mechanism in the normal case \u8212- cannot straightforwardly extend to {\i eshet ish}. Notably, the {\i acharonim} observe that this entire analysis frames the problem of marrying a married woman in terms of {\i ervah} \u8212- the relational prohibition \u8212- rather than from the {\i kinyan} perspective of whether a woman can be legally acquired by two men simultaneously. This appears to stand in some tension with material treated elsewhere in the tractate and generates significant discussion among the {\i acharonim}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Yona Drasha\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Yona} \u8212- or as the {\i gemara} alternatively identifies him, {\i Rav Huna bereih de-Rav Yehoshua} \u8212- proposes an entirely different approach. Rather than a {\i binyan av}, he derives the rule from the concluding {\i pasuk} of the {\i parasha} of {\i arayot}: {\i kol asher ya\u8217'aseh mikol ha-to\u8217'evot ha-eleh ve-nichreitu} \u8212- whoever does any of these abominations shall receive {\i karet}. This {\i pasuk} creates a {\i hekesh} \u8212- a textual equation \u8212- linking all of the {\i arayot} together with {\i achot isha}: {\i hukshu kol ha-arayot kulan le-achot isha}. Just as {\i achot isha} disables {\i kiddushin}, so too, by virtue of this encompassing equation, all {\i arayot} similarly disable {\i kiddushin}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Niddah & Sotah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Niddah Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i binyan av} and the {\i hekesh} of {\i Rabbi Yona} both confront a striking difficulty: {\i i hachi afilu niddah nami} \u8212- if this logic holds, then it should also apply to {\i niddah}. A {\i niddah} \u8212- a menstruant woman \u8212- appears in the list of {\i arayot} and her violation carries {\i karet}. The {\i hava amina} would therefore be that if one performed {\i kiddushin} with an unmarried woman while she is a {\i niddah}, the {\i kiddushin} would not take effect and any child born of that relationship would be a {\i mamzer}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is a significant challenge. {\i Tosafot}, already in the context of the first {\i daf} of {\i Yevamot}, grapples with the same issue in a parallel form: if {\i niddah} is among the {\i arayot}, then if one of the {\i tzarot} of the deceased brother was in a state of {\i niddah} at the time of his death, she might count as a {\i tzarat ervah}, thereby exempting all the {\i co-wives} from {\i yibum}. {\i Tosafot} resolves this, and {\i Tosafot HaRosh} on {\i Yevamot daf 2} goes further, arguing that {\i niddah} is not fundamentally called an {\i ervah} in the same sense as the other {\i arayot}. A similar point is raised by the {\i Tosafot} in {\i Sanhedrin} regarding why the laws of {\i yichud} do not apply with equal stringency between married partners during {\i niddah} as they do in other {\i arayot} contexts. The {\i Brisker Rav}, in his commentary on the {\i Rambam}, and several other {\i acharonim} develop the distinction between {\i niddah} and other {\i arayot} into a fundamental categorical difference.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might also note that using {\i achot isha} as the paradigmatic {\i ervah} for this entire {\i drasha} invites a similar objection: {\i achot isha} becomes permitted upon the death of the wife, which arguably makes it less severe than other {\i arayot}. The {\i gemara} does address the analogous concern somewhat earlier, in the framework of {\i ein lah heter b\u8217'chayei osra} vs.\u160 ?{\i yesh lah heter b\u8217'chayei osra}, but {\i Rabbi Yona}\u8217's formulation reopens the same question and the {\i gemara} does not fully resolve it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The case of {\i kiddushin} with a {\i niddah} that {\i Rashi} addresses is {\i im kidesh pnuya bimei nidutah} \u8212- what if a man performed {\i kiddushin} with an unmarried woman during her period of {\i niddah}? The {\i hava amina} is that {\i kiddushin} would not take effect and the child would be a {\i mamzer}. Separately, there is also the question of {\i chuppat niddah} \u8212- what happens if the {\i nisuin} ceremony takes place while the woman is in {\i niddah}? Most {\i rishonim} do not view this as a fundamental problem for the {\i nisuin} itself. The {\i Rambam} cites an opinion that the {\i nisuin} does not take full effect and must be redone, though in practice this is addressed through various means \u8212- principally by ensuring that the couple avoid {\i yichud} until after {\i niddah} concludes.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Agreement Niddah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Amar Abaye}: {\i hakol modim beva\u8217'al ha-niddah ve-al ha-sotah she-ein ha-valad mamzer} \u8212- everyone agrees that in the case of {\i niddah} and {\i sotah}, the child is not a {\i mamzer}. This means that {\i Rabbi Shimon HaTimni}, despite holding that all {\i chayavei krisos} produce {\i mamzerut}, agrees that {\i niddah} is an exception. And {\i Rabbi Akiva}, despite his sweeping position regarding {\i chayavei lavin}, agrees that {\i sotah} is an exception. {\i Sotah} here refers to the situation where a woman has had an adulterous relationship and her husband subsequently has relations with her \u8212- that relationship is an {\i issur lav} for the husband, yet the child of that relationship is not a {\i mamzer}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 In cases where adultery has occurred and a child is born shortly thereafter, the {\i chazakah} of {\i rov beilos achar ha-ba\u8217'al} \u8212- that most marital relations are with the husband \u8212- generally serves to prevent the child from being declared a {\i mamzer}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chizkiyah Drasha\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Amar Chizkiyah}: the exemption of {\i niddah} is derived from a specific {\i pasuk}. The verse {\i u-tehi nidatah alav} describes the consequence for one who has relations with a {\i niddah} \u8212- he acquires the same seven-day impurity she carries. {\i Chizkiyah} derives from the word {\i u-tehi} \u8212- the root {\i lihyot}, which the Torah elsewhere uses to denote the establishment of marriage \u8212- that even during her period of {\i niddah}, {\i havayah} (a marriage bond) can be established. The {\i pasuk} thus explicitly permits {\i kiddushin} to take hold despite the prohibition of {\i niddah}, notwithstanding the {\i karet} penalty.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chumra Chumra Rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} asks: given that we have a {\i hekesh} connecting all {\i arayot} to one paradigm, why did we choose to connect them all to {\i achot isha} rather than to {\i niddah}? If we connected them to {\i niddah}, the result would be permissive \u8212- {\i kiddushin} could take effect even with {\i arayot}. We could construct an argument in either direction, so why favor the stricter outcome?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} answers: {\i kula b\u8217'chumra l\u8217'chumra makshinan} \u8212- when a {\i hekesh} can be drawn in either direction, we always draw it toward the stricter ({\i machmir}) option. This is presented as a methodological rule of {\i drashot}. The {\i gemara} does not appear to ground this in the general principle of {\i safek de\u8217'oraita l\u8217'chumra}; rather, it seems to be an independent rule governing how textual equations are resolved when there is a choice. The {\i gemara} suggests it as a rule worth flagging, though its consistent application across {\i Shas} is a question worth investigating further.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Acha Kal V.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rav Acha bar Yaakov} offers a different approach altogether: the exclusion of {\i niddah} can be derived through {\i kal vachomer} from {\i yevamah}. A {\i yevamah} \u8212- a widow required to undergo {\i yibum} \u8212- who goes and attempts to marry a stranger faces only an {\i issur lav}, the least severe category of prohibition, yet her {\i kiddushin} with that stranger does not take effect. This is because of the principle of {\i yesh zikah} \u8212- the bond between the {\i yavam} and {\i yevamah} begins automatically at the moment of the brother\u8217's death, creating something almost akin to a proto-{\i eshet ish}. If even an {\i issur lav} can disable {\i kiddushin}, then {\i chayavei mitat beit din} and {\i chayavei krisos} certainly should as well \u8212- {\i lo kol shekein}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then challenges: {\i i hachi she\u8217'ar chayavei lavin nami} \u8212- if this {\i kal vachomer} holds, then all other {\i chayavei lavin} should equally disable {\i kiddushin}, which would force us to rule like {\i Rabbi Akiva} that {\i kiddushin} never takes effect by {\i chayavei lavin} \u8212- a conclusion rejected by the {\i rabbanan}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two Wives Pasuk\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Amar Rav Papa}: {\i chayavei lavin} are explicitly proven to allow {\i kiddushin} to take hold from a {\i pasuk} in {\i Parshat Ki Teitzei} \u8212- {\i ki tihyena le-ish shtei nashim ha-achat ahuvah ve-ha-achat senuah} \u8212- when a man has two wives, one beloved and one hated. Since God is described as designating one wife as {\i ahuvah} and one as {\i senuah}, and we do not say that God plays favorites among permissible relationships, this must mean that the {\i senuah} wife represents a marriage that was halachically disfavored \u8212- i.e., a prohibited relationship \u8212- yet the {\i kiddushin} nonetheless took effect. The {\i pasuk} thus explicitly contemplates a case where {\i kiddushin} is valid despite a prohibition: {\i ahuvah b\u8217'nisueha} and {\i senuah b\u8217'nisueha} \u8212- one whose marriage is favored in the eyes of heaven and one whose marriage is not, yet both are married.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Akiva vs Rabbanan\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i rabbanan} \u8212- those who disagree with {\i Rabbi Akiva} \u8212- understand this {\i pasuk} as providing explicit proof that {\i kiddushin} is effective with {\i chayavei lavin}. {\i Rabbi Akiva}, however, holds that {\i kiddushin} never takes hold by {\i chayavei lavin}. To what situation, then, does he apply this {\i pasuk}? {\i Rabbi Akiva} must restrict it to the case of {\i almanah le-Kohen Gadol} \u8212- a widow marrying the High Priest \u8212- which involves not a {\i lav} but an {\i issur asei}. The High Priest is commanded {\i ki im betulah me-amav yikach ishah} \u8212- he must marry a virgin from his people. Marrying a widow therefore constitutes a {\i lav ha-ba mi-chlal asei}, a prohibition derived from a positive commandment, which is regarded as a lower-level prohibition. According to {\i Rabbi Akiva}, this is the only case in which {\i kiddushin} takes effect despite a prohibition \u8212- because it is merely an {\i issur asei}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Four Categories\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 At this point the full analytic structure comes into view. There are four categories of prohibited relationships: {\i issur asei} (positive commandment violation), {\i chayavei lavin} (negative commandment violations), {\i chayavei karet}, and {\i chayavei mitat beit din}. The positions of the {\i tanna\u8217'im} can be charted accordingly. {\i Rabbi Akiva} holds there is no valid {\i kiddushin} for {\i chayavei lavin}, {\i chayavei karet}, and {\i chayavei mitat beit din}, but {\i kiddushin} is effective for {\i issur asei}. {\i Rabbi Shimon HaTimni} holds that {\i kiddushin} is disabled for {\i chayavei karet} and {\i chayavei mitat beit din} only. {\i Rabbi Yehoshua} restricts this further to {\i chayavei mitat beit din} alone.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 In addition, various exceptions apply within these categories. {\i Yevamah}, despite being merely an {\i issur lav}, disables {\i kiddushin} \u8212- universally agreed upon. {\i Eshet ach} has a special {\i heter} in the context of the {\i mitzvah} of {\i yibum}. {\i Eshet ish} can be released from her status through divorce during the husband\u8217's lifetime. {\i Niddah}, despite carrying {\i karet}, is universally agreed to allow {\i kiddushin} to take effect. And {\i sotah} is universally agreed not to produce {\i mamzerut} even under {\i Rabbi Akiva}\u8217's expansive framework.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Simai\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i De-tani Rabbi Simai}: Rabbi Simai articulates what he understands {\i Rabbi Akiva}\u8217's full position to be. {\i Min hakol hayah Rabbi Akiva oseh mamzer chutz me-almanah le-Kohen Gadol} \u8212- {\i Rabbi Akiva} creates {\i mamzerut} from all prohibited relationships, with one exception: {\i almanah le-Kohen Gadol}. The reason is that the Torah explicitly uses the word {\i lo yechalel} \u8212- \u8220"he shall not profane\u8221" \u8212- indicating that in this case the result is {\i chillul}, a disqualification to {\i kehunah} status, producing a {\i chalal} rather than a {\i mamzer}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The category of {\i chalal} is significant. The child of a {\i kohen} who marries a woman he is forbidden to marry becomes a {\i chalal} \u8212- not a full {\i kohen}, but not a {\i mamzer} either. A {\i chalal} cannot serve in the Temple and cannot eat {\i terumah}, but may otherwise marry any Jewish woman he wishes. Furthermore, there is a path back: a {\i chalal}\u8217's daughter who marries a legitimate {\i kohen} can have sons who are {\i kohanim} once again. This contrasts sharply with {\i mamzerut}, from which there is essentially no exit \u8212- at least not in a world without slavery, where the one technical mechanism of exit no longer functions.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} also notes that this discussion extends naturally to a regular {\i kohen} who marries a {\i gerushah} (divorced woman). The child of such a union, on the same logic, would be a {\i chalal} \u8212- a step down from {\i kohen}, but not as catastrophic as {\i mamzer}. This distinction \u8212- that violations within the priestly framework produce {\i chalal} status rather than {\i mamzer} status \u8212- is important both practically and conceptually.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Shaveiv\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Shaveiv} disagrees sharply with {\i Rabbi Simai}\u8217's account of {\i Rabbi Akiva}\u8217's position. {\i De-amar bo unitzvach al Rabbi Akiva} \u8212- let us go and protest loudly at {\i Rabbi Akiva}, who said {\i kol she-ein lo biah be-Yisrael ha-valad mamzer} \u8212- anyone whom you are forbidden to have relations with, the child is a {\i mamzer}. {\i Rabbi Shaveiv} contends that {\i Rabbi Akiva}\u8217's position creates {\i mamzerut} even in the case of {\i almanah le-Kohen Gadol}, without the exception that {\i Rabbi Simai} introduced. The implication is that {\i Rabbi Akiva}\u8217's rule, as {\i Rabbi Shaveiv} understands it, is sweeping and unqualified \u8212- and {\i Rabbi Shaveiv} finds this alarming, as it dramatically expands the population of {\i mamzerim}. The {\i gemara} notes this objection carries an almost rhetorical, polemical character \u8212- this is a {\i beit midrash} in which strong disagreement is sometimes expressed with open exasperation, as we have seen elsewhere, such as {\i Rabbi Shimon ben Lakish}\u8217's famous outburst about the {\i hekesh} of {\i havayah le-yitziah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The question of how many {\i Kohanim Gedolim} exist at any given time is relevant here: the {\i Kohen Gadol} is a singular figure, and the case of {\i almanah le-Kohen Gadol} affects a vanishingly small number of people. It is therefore striking that this particular case becomes the crux of the dispute between {\i Rabbi Simai} and {\i Rabbi Shaveiv}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Reconciliation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then works through the reconciliation. If {\i Rabbi Shaveiv} intends to refute {\i Rabbi Simai}, his approach is coherent \u8212- he is arguing that even {\i almanah le-Kohen Gadol} produces {\i mamzerut} and that {\i Rabbi Simai}\u8217's exception is wrong. But if {\i Rabbi Shaveiv} is stating his own independent position, what is the {\i issur asei} case he would apply the {\i pasuk} of {\i ki tihyena} to?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The resolution involves a careful re-examination of which category the prohibition of {\i almanah le-Kohen Gadol} properly belongs to. According to {\i Rabbi Simai}, {\i Rabbi Akiva}\u8217's one exception applies to {\i almanah le-Kohen Gadol} \u8212- which involves a {\i chayavei lav}-level prohibition \u8212- and therefore the exception operates within {\i chayavei lavin}. But the {\i gemara} ultimately concludes that {\i Rabbi Simai}\u8217's exception is better understood as applying only to {\i issur asei}, not to {\i chayavei lavin} proper.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The deeper point about {\i issur asei} is that it is an extremely weak level of prohibition \u8212- {\i bitul asei} is the least severe form of violation in the rabbinic hierarchy \u8212- and accordingly the case of a {\i beulah le-Kohen Gadol}, a non-virgin who marries the High Priest, is the paradigm of {\i issur asei} here. Even {\i Rabbi Akiva} agrees that {\i kiddushin} takes effect in such a case and no {\i mamzerut} results. The {\i rabbanan}, for their part, apply {\i ki tihyena} broadly to {\i chayavei lavin}, establishing that {\i kiddushin} is effective across that entire category. The contrast between {\i Rabbi Akiva} and the {\i rabbanan} thus turns on whether {\i chayavei lavin} falls on the side of valid or invalid {\i kiddushin}, with {\i Rabbi Akiva} placing it in the invalid column and the {\i rabbanan} placing it in the valid column \u8212- and the {\i pasuk} of {\i ki tihyena} being interpreted accordingly.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i sotah} exception under {\i Rabbi Akiva} similarly remains in place: even according to {\i Rabbi Akiva}, {\i sotah} \u8212- the adulterous woman whose husband subsequently has relations with her \u8212- does not produce {\i mamzerut}, because the {\i issur} in her case is specifically constructed around the marital relationship itself and carries its own textual basis for exemption.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Shifcha & Nochrit\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 No Kiddushin Ever\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} delineates a third major category: {\i kol mi she-ein lah alav lo kiddushin ve-lo al acherim kiddushin ha-valad k\u8217'motah} \u8212- any woman for whom there is no possibility of {\i kiddushin} with this man and no possibility of {\i kiddushin} with any Jewish man. The two paradigmatic examples are {\i shifcha Kenaanit} (a Canaanite slave woman) and {\i nochrit} (a non-Jewish woman). In both cases the child follows the mother\u8217's status.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shifcha Donkey\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Amar Rav Huna}: the derivation that a {\i shifcha Kenaanit} cannot contract {\i kiddushin} is learned from the {\i pasuk} in {\i Bereishit} in which {\i Avraham}, on his way to the {\i Akeidah}, instructs {\i Eliezer} and his companion: {\i shevu lachem po im ha-chamor} \u8212- stay here with the donkey. The {\i midrash} reads {\i im ha-chamor} as {\i am ha-domeh la-chamor} \u8212- a people similar to a donkey. Since slaves do not have {\i kiddushin}, they are compared, in this {\i drasha}, to animals. This is a strikingly harsh comparison, and the {\i gemara} is clearly aware of the gravity of this formulation. It must be situated within a broader legal framework in which the status of an {\i eved Kenaani} (Canaanite slave) is treated in certain respects as analogous to property rather than as a fully autonomous legal person with respect to personal-status law. Similar {\i drashot} appear elsewhere \u8212- for example, the discussion in {\i Avodah Zarah} about whether a non-Jewish corpse conveys {\i tumah be-ohel}, derived from {\i adam ki yamut be-ohel} with the {\i drasha atem k\u8217'ruyim adam ve-ein ovdei avodah zarah k\u8217'ruyim adam} \u8212- and in each case the {\i gemara} uses a sharp formulation to establish a specific legal category.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mother\u8217's Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} establishes that {\i kiddushin} does not apply to a slave. But from where do we know that the child of a {\i shifcha Kenaanit} follows the mother\u8217's status? From the {\i pasuk}: {\i ha-ishah vi-yladeha tihyeh le-adoneha} \u8212- the woman and her children shall belong to her master. This verse appears in the context of an {\i eved Ivri} (Hebrew slave) who marries a {\i shifcha Kenaanit} that his master provided for him; when the {\i eved Ivri} goes free, his children from the {\i shifcha Kenaanit} remain with the master as slaves. The explicit language of the {\i pasuk} establishes that the children follow the mother\u8217's status \u8212- they remain enslaved because she is a slave. The economic logic here is plain: the capacity to have one\u8217's slaves procreate was, within the institution of slavery, a means of generating additional slaves without capture or purchase.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Non-Jew Marriage\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Nochrit minalan} \u8212- how do we know that a non-Jewish woman cannot contract {\i kiddushin} with a Jewish man and that her children follow her status? {\i Amar kra lo titchaten bam} \u8212- you shall not intermarry with them. The {\i gemara} derives from {\i lo titchaten bam} that {\i kiddushin} cannot take effect with non-Jews.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 However, {\i Tosafot} raises an objection: the {\i pasuk} of {\i lo titchaten bam} is specifically addressed to the seven Canaanite nations \u8212- the {\i Zayin Amamim} \u8212- for whom there is a distinct and heightened prohibition rooted in their extreme devotion to idolatry. {\i Rashi} in a long comment further down in the {\i sugya} \u8212- when he discusses {\i Rabbi Shimon ben Yochai}\u8217's approach of {\i darish ta\u8217'ama de-kra} \u8212- distinguishes between these seven nations and other non-Jews precisely on this basis, citing the {\i gemara} in {\i Chullin}: {\i umot she-b\u8217'chutz la-aretz lav ovdei avodat kochavim heim, ela minhag avoteihem b\u8217'yadeihem} \u8212- non-Jews outside of {\i Eretz Yisrael} are not considered genuine idol worshippers; they simply follow inherited custom. The seven Canaanite nations, by contrast, were deeply and genuinely committed to {\i avodah zarah}, which is the underlying reason for the heightened prohibition against marrying them. {\i Tosafot} leverages this distinction in several places to permit closer commercial and social relations with medieval European Christians.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Severs Jewishness\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Amar Rabbi Yochanan mishum Rabbi Shimon ben Yochai}: the derivation for the children\u8217's status follows the mother is taken from {\i ki yasir et bincha me-acharei} \u8212- for he will turn your son away from Me. The {\i pasuk} addresses the case of a Jewish man who marries a non-Jewish woman: the non-Jewish father-in-law will lead the Jewish son astray into idolatry. From this the {\i gemara} derives: {\i bincha ha-ba mi-Yisraelit karuy bincha} \u8212- your son born from a Jewish woman is called your son, {\i ve-ein bincha ha-ba min ha-ovedet kochavim karuy bincha} \u8212- but your son born from a non-Jewish woman is not called your son. One generation down, the grandchildren of a Jewish man through a non-Jewish woman are no longer recognized as his children within the Jewish people.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Yishmael} draws a further inference: {\i ben bitcha ha-ba min ha-oved kochavim karuy bincha} \u8212- the son of your daughter born from a non-Jewish man is called your son. That is, the grandchildren of a Jewish woman, even through a non-Jewish father, are still Jewish. The {\i gemara} then notes that {\i Ravina} holds that a child born from a non-Jewish man or a slave who had relations with a Jewish woman is not fully {\i kasher} \u8212- {\i nehi de-kasher lo havi, mamzer lo havi} \u8212- meaning the child is Jewish but carries a disqualification ({\i pasul}), specifically being {\i pasul le-kehunah}, ineligible for the priesthood. The child is neither fully unrestricted nor a {\i mamzer}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 All Nations\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} presses the point that {\i lo titchaten bam} applies specifically to the seven Canaanite nations. {\i She\u8217'ar umot minalan} \u8212- what is the source for extending the rule to all other nations? {\i Amar kra ki yasir et bincha lerabot kol ha-mesirim} \u8212- the phrase {\i ki yasir}, \u8220"for he will lead astray,\u8221" is understood to encompass anyone who would draw a Jewish child away from the Jewish people. Any non-Jewish woman who marries a Jewish man creates the same risk, and therefore the same legal consequence \u8212- invalid {\i kiddushin} and children following the mother \u8212- extends to all nations.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Drasha vs Pshat\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Hanicha le-Rabbi Shimon de-darish ta\u8217'ama de-kra} \u8212- this extension works according to {\i Rabbi Shimon ben Yochai} because he applies {\i darish ta\u8217'ama de-kra}, interpreting the contextual purpose and meaning of the verse and applying it broadly to all cases that share the same underlying rationale. {\i Ela le-Rabbanan} \u8212- the {\i rabbanan}, however, do not employ {\i darish ta\u8217'ama de-kra}; they do not generalize from the contextual reason of a verse to expand its legal application. They therefore require a separate source.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yefat Toar\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 For the {\i rabbanan}, the source is the {\i pasuk} describing {\i yefat to\u8217'ar} \u8212- the captive woman taken in war \u8212- which states {\i ve-achar ken tavo eleha u-ve\u8217'altah}, \u8220"and only afterward may you come to her and marry her.\u8221" The phrase {\i ve-achar ken} \u8212- only after a certain process \u8212- implies that initially, before this process is completed, {\i kiddushin} cannot take effect with her. {\i Michlal de-mei-ikara lo tafsei bah kiddushin} \u8212- it is implicit that at the outset she was not subject to {\i kiddushin}. This establishes, for the {\i rabbanan}, that {\i kiddushin} does not apply to a non-Jewish woman.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Concluding Rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 How do we then know that the child follows the mother? From the {\i pasuk}: {\i ki tihyena le-ish ve-yaldu lo} \u8212- when a man\u8217's wives give birth to him children. {\i Ke-heicha de-karinan bei ki tihyena karinan bei ve-yaldu lo} \u8212- wherever we apply the rule of {\i ki tihyena} (i.e., wherever a valid marriage bond exists), we also apply {\i ve-yaldu lo} (the children are attributed to the father). And wherever we cannot apply {\i ki tihyena} \u8212- wherever {\i kiddushin} does not take effect \u8212- the children follow the mother instead.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This concluding principle unifies the entire discussion: {\i kiddushin} validity and paternal attribution of children are two sides of the same coin. Wherever {\i kiddushin} works, children follow the father. Wherever it does not, children follow the mother. This is the foundational rule underlying the {\i mishnah}\u8217's entire framework of {\i yichus} in these cases.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Kiddushin 69\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Matrilineal Descent\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Non-Jewish Mother\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The daf opens in the middle of an extremely important discussion rooted in the final point of the preceding {\i mishnah}: that the child of a {\i shifcha} \u8212- a slave woman \u8212- is also a slave, derived from the {\i pasuk} {\i ha\u8217'isha veyeladeha tihyeh le\u8217'adoneha} (\u8220"the woman and her children shall belong to her master\u8221"). From there, the {\i Gemara} moves to the {\i halacha} that the child of a non-Jewish woman and a Jewish man is not Jewish. The {\i mishnah} does not explicitly address the reverse \u8212- a Jewish mother and a non-Jewish father \u8212- but the {\i Gemara} here is the locus classicus for the entire principle of matrilineal descent.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} begins with the question: {\i nochrit minalan} \u8212- how do we know, in the case of a non-Jewish woman, that the child is not Jewish? The answer is {\i amar kra: lo titchaten bam} \u8212- the Torah commands not to intermarry with them, the context being the seven Canaanite nations. From this prohibition we derive that {\i kiddushin} does not take effect. But {\i vevalda kamota minalan} \u8212- how do we know that the child\u8217's status follows the mother?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rashi\u8217's Interpretation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Amar Rabbi Yochanan mishum Rabbi Shimon bar Yochai}: the verse states {\i ki yasir et bincha me\u8217'acharai} \u8212- \u8220"for he will cause your son to stray from Me.\u8221" From this we derive: {\i bincha haba miyisraelit karui bincha} \u8212- your child born from a Jewish woman is called your son, {\i ve\u8217'ein bincha haba min ha\u8217'avodat kochavim karui bincha ela binah} \u8212- but your child born from a non-Jewish woman is not called your son; rather, he is called her son.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The difficulty is obvious: how does one derive all this from that {\i pasuk}? The surrounding verses state {\i bitcha lo titein livno uvito lo tikach livnecha} \u8212- do not give your daughter to their son and do not take their daughter for your son \u8212- and the reason given is \u8220"for he will cause your son to stray from Me.\u8221" But this is grammatically awkward. If we are talking about giving your daughter to their son, the verse should have said that he \u8212- the non-Jewish son-in-law \u8212- will cause {\i your daughter} to stray. And if we are talking about taking their daughter for your son, it should have warned that {\i she} will cause your son to stray. The verse, as written, says \u8220"he will cause your son to stray\u8221" \u8212- which fits neither case straightforwardly.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rashi resolves this by reading the verse as follows: the prohibition against giving your daughter to their son is because {\i he} \u8212- the non-Jewish son-in-law \u8212- will cause {\i your grandson} to stray. In other words, the \u8220"son\u8221" referred to is not your own son but your daughter\u8217's son, your grandson. This is significant: it implies that the child of your Jewish daughter and her non-Jewish husband is still {\i your} grandson, still Jewish \u8212- and therefore the concern is that this non-Jewish husband will lead that Jewish grandchild away from God. The reverse inference is then drawn: if a Jewish man fathers a child with a non-Jewish woman, that child is called {\i her} child, not his, and is therefore not Jewish.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbeinu Tam\u8217's View\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbeinu Tam is troubled by the interpretive leap of reading \u8220"your son\u8221" as \u8220"your grandson,\u8221" since the grandson has not been mentioned in the text. He therefore reads the verse differently. According to Rabbeinu Tam, \u8220"he\u8221" who will cause \u8220"your son\u8221" to stray refers not to any future child of the union but to the non-Jewish father-in-law himself. The case in view is your son marrying their daughter, and the warning is that {\i he} \u8212- the father-in-law \u8212- will pull your son away from Jewish practice. What the verse thereby teaches about the status of the offspring is that, under this reading, \u8220"turning your son away\u8221" implies that the children of that union will be non-Jewish \u8212- the entire family line is being drawn away. Rashi\u8217's reading remains somewhat simpler: the word {\i bincha} \u8212- your son \u8212- means your grandson through your daughter, which establishes that the child of a Jewish mother and non-Jewish father is still Jewish.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Yochanan\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Returning to the {\i Gemara}\u8217's derivation: the principle stated by Rabbi Yochanan in the name of Rabbi Shimon bar Yochai is that {\i bincha haba miyisrael karui bincha} \u8212- a child born of a Jewish woman is called your child \u8212- {\i ve\u8217'ein bincha haba min ha\u8217'ovedet kochavim karui bincha ella benah} \u8212- but the child of a non-Jewish woman is not your son but hers. However one parses the grammatical problem in {\i ki yasir et bincha}, it is from this difficulty that the {\i Gemara} derives the principle of matrilineal descent. It is worth noting that this is not the most airtight proof; the entire edifice rests on a subtle grammatical anomaly.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ravina\u8217's Proof\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Amar Ravina shema mina}: Ravina draws an explicit conclusion from this teaching \u8212- {\i ben bitcha haba mi\u8217'oved kochavim karui bincha} \u8212- the son of your daughter, even if born from a non-Jewish man, is still called your son, and thus is Jewish. This appears to be a complete restatement of what Rabbi Yochanan already said, and {\i Tosafos} attempts to distinguish them, but functionally both are making the same point: a child of a Jewish mother and non-Jewish father is Jewish. This principle, it bears repeating, was not stated in the {\i mishnah} itself, which addressed only the case of a non-Jewish mother.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mamzer Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} immediately raises a concern: {\i neima kasavar Ravina oved kochavim ve\u8217'eved haba al bat Yisrael havlad mamzer} \u8212- perhaps Ravina holds that the child of a non-Jewish man or slave who fathers a child with a Jewish woman is a {\i mamzer}? The reasoning is familiar: we know that when {\i kiddushin} cannot take effect between two parties, the child of their union is a {\i mamzer}. Since {\i kiddushin} is clearly not binding between a Jewish woman and a non-Jewish man, why isn\u8217't the child a {\i mamzer}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This would actually create a strange dynamic: saying the child is non-Jewish is in one sense the more lenient outcome, because the child could simply convert and carry no stigma. Saying the child is Jewish immediately raises the specter of {\i mamzer} status.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Psul Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} resolves this: {\i nehi de\u8217'kasher lo havei mamzer lo havei} \u8212- Ravina said neither that the child is fully {\i kasher} nor that the child is a {\i mamzer}. As Rashi explains, {\i mamzer} status arises from a union where both parties could, in principle, marry someone else within {\i halakha} but are forbidden to each other \u8212- as in adultery or incest. In the case of a Jewish woman and a non-Jewish man, the non-Jewish man has no valid {\i halachic} marriage at all within our framework. This is a categorically different type of forbidden relationship. Therefore: the child is Jewish, but carries a {\i psul} \u8212- a defective status that falls short of full {\i kashrut} without rising to the severity of {\i mamzerut}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What does this {\i psul} mean practically? Two areas are worth noting. First, there are contexts in the {\i Gemara} where the status of a person with a Jewish mother and non-Jewish father is considered alongside the status of a {\i geir}, not because such a person needs conversion \u8212- they do not \u8212- but because there is a partial analogy in that their Jewish identity has, as it were, a non-Jewish biographical component. Second, and perhaps more immediately practical, there is a significant debate among the {\i poskim} \u8212- addressed in another {\i Gemara} and not fully spelled out here \u8212- about whether a {\i kohen} may marry a woman born of a Jewish mother and non-Jewish father. This {\i psul} may give rise to at least a {\i de\u8217'rabbanan} concern in that context. The {\i Gemara} here does not spell out all the ramifications, but these are the two areas where this intermediate status carries genuine {\i halachic} weight.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Seven Nations\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 All Non-Jews Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having established the principle, the {\i Gemara} now asks: {\i hahu beshivah goyim ketiv, she\u8217'ar umos minalan} \u8212- that {\i pasuk} of {\i lo titchaten bam}, with its attendant consequences for the status of the child, is written in the context of the seven Canaanite nations. How do we know to apply the same rules to all non-Jews?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} answers: {\i amar kra ki yasir et bincha} \u8212- the verse explains the reason for the prohibition as \u8220"he will cause your son to stray,\u8221" {\i l\u8217'rabos kol hamisirim} \u8212- which comes to include all those who would cause such straying. Since the rationale \u8212- that the non-Jewish spouse will lead the child away from Jewish identity \u8212- applies equally to any non-Jew, the verse\u8217's scope is not limited to the seven nations.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Shimon Method\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But the {\i Gemara} immediately challenges this: {\i hani l\u8217'Rabbi Shimon d\u8217'daresh ta\u8217'ama dikra} \u8212- this works well for Rabbi Shimon, who is willing to use the reason of a verse to redefine its parameters. Rabbi Shimon\u8217's method, {\i darshan ta\u8217'ama dikra}, allows the inferred rationale to expand or contract the application of a law. A classic illustration of this principle is the prohibition of {\i lo tachvol beged almanah} \u8212- one may not take a widow\u8217's garment as collateral. Rabbi Shimon reads this as applying only to cases where taking the garment would create a problematic situation \u8212- such as repeatedly entering a widow\u8217's home alone to return the garment each day, raising suspicion \u8212- but not to a rich widow, because the rationale does not apply there. Crucially, the rationale itself may vary: even when two authorities agree that {\i darshan ta\u8217'ama dikra} is legitimate, they may disagree entirely about what the {\i ta\u8217'ama dikra} is.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbanan Objection\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Rabbanan}, however, do not use the reason of a verse to redefine its parameters. This does not mean they deny that {\i mitzvot} have reasons; it means they refuse to allow their own assumption of a reason to override or extend the plain scope of the verse. So {\i ella l\u8217'Rabbanan mai ta\u8217'ama} \u8212- how do the {\i Rabbanan} extend the law beyond the seven Canaanite nations?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rashi\u8217's Resolution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rashi\u8217's approach is that the very inclusion of the phrase {\i ki yasir et bincha} in the verse is what provides the extension \u8212- for {\i everyone}, including the {\i Rabbanan}. Had the verse not stated this reason, one might indeed have confined the prohibition and its consequences to the seven nations. But because the Torah itself explicitly provided the rationale, that explicit statement is itself teaching that wherever the rationale applies \u8212- any non-Jew who might lead the child astray \u8212- the law applies as well. This is not a case of the {\i Rabbanan} inferring an unstated reason and then using it to redefine the {\i mitzvah}; rather, the Torah stated the reason explicitly, and that explicit statement carries its own exegetical force as a {\i ribui} \u8212- an expansion. {\i Tosafos} takes a somewhat different approach, but the upshot is the same.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Yefat Toar Source\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yefat Toar Intro\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now introduces a second textual basis for the law of matrilineal descent, drawn from a completely different part of the Torah \u8212- the {\i yefat to\u8217'ar}, the captive woman taken in war. This case, by definition, involves non-Canaanite nations, since the laws of {\i yefat to\u8217'ar} apply specifically to wars waged against nations {\i outside} the land. The verse there states {\i v\u8217'acharei ken tavo eleha u\u8217'va\u8217'alta} \u8212- only after the prescribed mourning process does the marriage become valid.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kiddushin Bindable\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 From here the {\i Gemara} derives a general principle: {\i kiddushin} must be capable of taking binding effect for the child\u8217's status to follow the father. The mechanism is as follows: where {\i kiddushin} can bind, the children are \u8220"his\u8221"; where it cannot, they are not.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ki Tiheyena L\u8217'Ish\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The textual peg for this is the verse {\i ki tiheyena l\u8217'ish shtei nashim v\u8217'yaldu lo} \u8212- if a man has two wives and they bear him children. The {\i Gemara} reads this as: {\i kol heicha d\u8217'karinan ki tiheyena} \u8212- anywhere that the woman can truly be said to \u8220"belong to a man\u8221" in the full legal sense of {\i kiddushin} \u8212- {\i karinan beih v\u8217'yaldu lo} \u8212- the children are considered born to him and follow his status.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Non-Jewish Mother\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Conversely, {\i v\u8217'kol heicha d\u8217'lo karinan beih ki tiheyena} \u8212- in any case where there is no Jewish man for whom this woman\u8217's {\i kiddushin} would be binding, {\i lo karinan beih v\u8217'yaldu lo} \u8212- the children are not attributed to the father. This is not merely a case where {\i kiddushin} is forbidden but where it is categorically incapable of taking effect with {\i any} Jewish man. In such a case, the children are not his and are not Jewish.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Jewish Mother Inference\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Notably, this derivation, like the one from {\i ki yasir et bincha}, establishes only the case of the non-Jewish mother: where {\i kiddushin} cannot bind, the child is not Jewish. The converse \u8212- that the child of a Jewish mother and non-Jewish father {\i is} Jewish \u8212- is not explicitly derived from this verse; that principle rests on the earlier {\i drasha} of Rabbi Yochanan.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Servant Woman\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Redundancy Challenge\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might ask: if we have already derived from {\i ki tiheyena l\u8217'ish} that where {\i kiddushin} cannot bind, the child follows the mother \u8212- this should cover the case of a {\i shifcha} as well, since no Jewish man can contract a valid {\i kiddushin} with a {\i shifcha}. If so, why do we need the separate verse {\i ha\u8217'isha veyeladeha tihyeh le\u8217'adoneha} \u8212- \u8220"the woman and her children shall belong to her master\u8221"? We have already established the principle from elsewhere.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Braita Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} answers: {\i lichdetanya} \u8212- as was taught in a {\i braita} (on 67a). The verse is needed for a specific case: what if a master says to his female slave, {\i harei at bat chorin} \u8212- you are hereby a free woman \u8212- {\i u\u8217'vladech eved} \u8212- but your unborn child, still in the womb, will remain a slave? Can a master split the freedom of the mother from the slave-status of the fetus?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Split Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Havlad kamota, divrei Rabbi Yossi HaGlili} \u8212- Rabbi Yossi HaGlili rules that you cannot split them: if the mother is freed, the child is automatically freed as well. {\i Va\u8217'chachamim omrim d\u8217'varav kayamin} \u8212- the {\i chachamim} say his words stand and the split is valid.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Verse Unity\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Amar Rava aliba d\u8217'Rabbi Yossi HaGlili}: what is the textual basis for Rabbi Yossi HaGlili\u8217's position? The verse {\i ha\u8217'isha veyeladeha tihyeh le\u8217'adoneha} \u8212- the woman {\i and her children} shall belong to her master \u8212- teaches that mother and child are bound together: when the mother is the master\u8217's slave, the children are the master\u8217's slaves; but you cannot create a situation where the mother is no longer your slave while the unborn child is. The mother and child are a unified legal unit. This is what required the verse: the general principle of matrilineal descent was already derivable from {\i ki tiheyena l\u8217'ish}, but the specific rule that you cannot sever the legal bond between mother and fetus in this way required the verse {\i ha\u8217'isha veyeladeha} explicitly.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rabbi Tarfon\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Proposal Structure\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} concludes the {\i perek} with a striking proposal: {\i Rabbi Tarfon omer yakhol ani letaher et hamamzerim} \u8212- Rabbi Tarfon says he can \u8220"purify\u8221" {\i mamzerim} \u8212- not retroactively remove the status, but arrange that their children will not be {\i mamzerim}. The mechanism is rooted in what was just established. Since the child of a {\i shifcha} is an {\i eved} and follows the mother, Rabbi Tarfon proposes: let the {\i mamzer} marry a {\i shifcha kena\u8217'anit}. Their children will be slaves \u8212- {\i vlad eved} \u8212- following the mother. As slaves, they are outside the category of {\i mamzerut}, which applies only within Jewish status. Then free the children. The freed children are Jewish and not {\i mamzerim}. Problem solved.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Eliezer\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Eliezer omer harei zeh eved mamzer} \u8212- Rabbi Eliezer disagrees sharply. The {\i mamzer} status passes through regardless; the child is worse off than before, now an {\i eved mamzer}. The reasoning of Rabbi Eliezer, as Rabbi Elazar explains, is derived from the verse {\i lo yavo lo bikhal Hashem} \u8212- the word {\i lo} (meaning \u8220"to him,\u8221" the {\i mamzer}) teaches that the stigma always follows: {\i halach achar psulo}, any future generation traces back to the original {\i mamzer} regardless of the mother\u8217's status. The word {\i lo} is read as overriding the ordinary rules of lineage \u8212- just as normally we follow the father ({\i l\u8217'mishpechotam l\u8217'veit avotam}), yet the verse by {\i mamzer} says even where you would normally not transmit the {\i psul}, {\i lo} tells you the {\i psul} persists.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rabbi Tarfon (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 L\u8217'Chatchila Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A question is raised: when Rabbi Tarfon proposes this solution, is he saying it is permitted {\i l\u8217'chatchila} \u8212- that a {\i mamzer} may proactively seek out a {\i shifcha} to marry \u8212- or only {\i b\u8217'di\u8217'eved}, meaning that if it happened, the children would not be {\i mamzerim}, but one may not initiate the arrangement? There is an underlying {\i issur} for a {\i Yisrael} to marry a {\i shifcha}. A {\i mamzer} may marry a {\i giyoret}, since there is no prohibition for a {\i Yisrael} to marry a {\i giyoret}, but marrying a {\i shifcha} is a different matter.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gender Difference\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} notes an asymmetry between the male and female {\i mamzer}. The {\i mishnah} notes that Rabbi Tarfon\u8217's interlocutors observed: {\i tiharta es hazacharim v\u8217'lo tiharta es hanekevos} \u8212- this solution works for males but not for females. A {\i mamzeret} who is a female cannot marry a male {\i eved} and achieve the same result, because the child of a {\i mamzeret} and a male {\i eved} would follow the mother \u8212- but the mother is Jewish and a {\i mamzeret}, so the child would be Jewish and a {\i mamzer}. The solution only works when the {\i mamzer} is male and the {\i shifcha} is female, because then the child follows the mother \u8212- the {\i shifcha} \u8212- and is an {\i eved}, breaking the Jewish status entirely, after which the children can be freed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rashi explains the practical reason why the solution was unavailable to a female {\i mamzeret}: she could not go off anonymously and enter into such an arrangement without her family \u8212- fathers, brothers \u8212- knowing and intervening. A male could, in principle, travel elsewhere and arrange a union with a {\i shifcha} without attracting attention. But this logic presupposes the arrangement is not entirely above-board, suggesting it might only work {\i b\u8217'di\u8217'eved} \u8212- after the fact.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Simlai Proof\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} brings a proof from a story: the host of Rabbi Simlai \u8212- {\i ushpizchanei de-Rabbi Simlai} \u8212- was a {\i mamzer}. Rabbi Simlai said to him, {\i ei akdamtach tihartinhu l\u8217'vanecha} \u8212- had I known you earlier, I could have advised you how to arrange that your children would not be {\i mamzerim}. The {\i Gemara} reasons: {\i ei amart bishlama l\u8217'chatchila shapir} \u8212- if it is permitted {\i l\u8217'chatchila}, Rabbi Simlai\u8217's statement is perfectly coherent; he would have given proactive advice. {\i Ela ei amart di\u8217'eved} \u8212- but if marrying a {\i shifcha} is forbidden and only works {\i b\u8217'di\u8217'eved}, how could Rabbi Simlai say he would have {\i advised} someone to do something prohibited?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} considers a workaround: Rabbi Simlai would have advised the man to go steal something, be sold as an {\i eved Ivri}, and since an {\i eved Ivri} is permitted to marry a {\i shifcha kena\u8217'anit}, the {\i issur} would be circumvented. Rashi notes the obvious problem \u8212- stealing is forbidden \u8212- but the {\i Gemara} rejects this solution on independent grounds: {\i uv\u8217'shnei de-Rabbi Simlai eved Ivri mi havai?} The laws of {\i eved Ivri} only apply when {\i yovel} is in force, which was not the case in Rabbi Simlai\u8217's time.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Perpetuation Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The disagreement between Rabbi Eliezer and the {\i Rabbanan} thus comes into full relief. Rabbi Eliezer holds that the {\i mamzer} status perpetuates through any union, even with a {\i shifcha}, based on {\i lo yavo lo bikhal Hashem} \u8212- {\i halach achar psulo}, the stigma always follows. The {\i Rabbanan} counter that {\i kol vlad shebimei shifcha kena\u8217'anit kevlad bimei behema dami} \u8212- the child of a non-Jewish slave woman is like the offspring of an animal, in the sense that it completely severs any prior lineage-based identity. Just as an animal\u8217's offspring has no tribal or family affiliation, the child of a {\i shifcha} exits the entire framework of Jewish lineage, including {\i mamzerut}. Once that break is complete, there is nothing for the {\i mamzer} stigma to attach to.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The distinction is important: we are not merely saying that the child follows the mother\u8217's status rather than the father\u8217's \u8212- which would be the normal matrilineal principle operating within a Jewish framework. Here, because the mother is entirely outside the Jewish legal order, the child\u8217's very connection to the father\u8217's status is severed at a more fundamental level. This is why the {\i Rabbanan}\u8217's position is not simply \u8220"the child follows the mother\u8217's slave status\u8221" but the stronger claim that the bond to the father\u8217's Jewish identity \u8212- including its {\i psul} \u8212- is broken entirely.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Halacha Conclusion\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Amar Rav Yehuda amar Shmuel halacha k\u8217'Rabbi Tarfon} \u8212- the {\i halacha} follows Rabbi Tarfon, and as established, it is {\i muttar l\u8217'chatchila}. A male {\i mamzer} is permitted, not merely {\i b\u8217'di\u8217'eved}, to marry a {\i shifcha} with the intention of freeing the children and resolving the {\i mamzerut}. Indeed, Rav Moshe Feinstein has a {\i teshuvah} in which he considers implementing this in practice as a solution for a {\i mamzer}, with all the {\i halachos} attending such an arrangement \u8212- the {\i kiddushin} would not be effected with the {\i shifcha}, she is {\i koneh} as a {\i shifcha}, the same {\i halachos} of {\i hilchos niddah} apply since a {\i shifcha kena\u8217'anit}\u8217's obligations in {\i mitzvos} are equivalent to a woman\u8217's, and the children may be freed immediately upon birth without waiting for the mother to be freed.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Ten Yuchasin\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Categories List\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The next {\i mishnah} opens a new {\i perek} continuing the discussion of {\i yuchasin} \u8212- lineage categories \u8212- and their consequences for marriage eligibility. {\i Asara yuchasin alu miBavel} \u8212- ten categories of lineage came up from Babylon with Ezra. The ten are: {\i kohanei, leviyei, yisraeli} \u8212- Kohanim, Levi\u8217'im, Yisraelim \u8212- {\i challalim} (children of a Kohen from a forbidden union for {\i kehuna}, who lack {\i kedushat kehuna}), {\i geirim} (converts), {\i charurei} (freed slaves), {\i mamzeirim}, {\i nesinim} (Gibeonites, subject to David HaMelech\u8217's {\i gezera} prohibiting intermarriage within the Jewish nation), {\i shtuki}, and {\i asufi}. All of these categories were known and identified among those who made {\i aliyah} with Ezra.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 First Tier\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Kohanei, leviyei, va\u8217'yisraeli mutarin lavo ze baze} \u8212- Kohanim, Levi\u8217'im, and Yisraelim may all intermarry freely with one another. This group constitutes the first tier.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Second Tier\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Levi, yisraeli, challali, geirei, ve\u8217'charurei mutarin lavo ze baze} \u8212- Levi\u8217'im, Yisraelim, {\i challalim}, {\i geirim}, and freed slaves may all intermarry with one another. The reason Kohanim are excluded: a Kohen may not marry a {\i chalalah}, and by extension is prohibited from marrying a {\i gerusha}, a {\i zonah}, and categories assimilated thereto. A {\i geir} and an {\i eved meshuchrar}, having presumably had a sexual life outside of marriage before their entry into full Jewish status, are placed by {\i halakha} into the category analogous to {\i zonah} for purposes of {\i kehuna} marriage prohibitions. Hence this second tier excludes Kohanim, but all others in it may intermarry freely.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Third Tier\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Geyri ve-charurei im mamzer ve-netini, shtuki va-asufi, kulam mutarin lavo ze baze} \u8212- converts and freed slaves may marry {\i mamzerim}, {\i nesinim}, {\i shtuki}, and {\i asufi}, and all of these may intermarry with one another. The rationale is rooted in the verse {\i lo yavo mamzer bikhal Hashem} (and similarly {\i lo yavo lo bikhal Hashem} by the descendants of {\i mamzerim}): the inner circle of {\i kehal Hashem} \u8212- as understood to exclude those who are not Jewish by birth \u8212- means that {\i geirim} and {\i meshuchrarim}, despite being fully Jewish, are considered outside the innermost {\i kehal Hashem} in this restricted sense, and therefore may marry {\i mamzerim}. This is a striking {\i halacha} and not without theological complexity, given the competing {\i pasuk} of {\i mishpat echad yihyeh lachem kager kaezrach ha\u8217'aretz}. But it is the operative {\i halakha}, and it actually functions as a {\i kula} in practice for these categories.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shtuki Definition\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Ve-eilu hem shtuki} \u8212- who is a {\i shtuki}? It is one who knows who his mother is but does not know his father. The etymology is from {\i shtak} \u8212- silence or \u8220"be quiet\u8221" \u8212- because when the child asks about his father, the mother says nothing, or perhaps tells the child not to ask, or redirects him from the man he might have identified as his father. Rashi notes an alternative reading: the mother may have said {\i shh} to the child when he pointed at the man currently married to her, indicating that person is not his father.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Asufi Definition\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Asufi} is {\i kol sheneesaf min hashuk} \u8212- whoever was gathered in from the marketplace. This is the foundling, the child left in a basket and collected, who does not know either parent.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Safek Mamzer\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Both the {\i shtuki} and {\i asufi} are treated as {\i safek mamzerim}. One might wonder: in the case of a {\i shtuki}, the mother is not a married woman \u8212- there is no accusation of adultery. How could the child be a {\i mamzer} at all, since {\i mamzerut} requires a union forbidden under penalty of {\i karet} or death? The answer is that the father could have been a {\i mamzer} himself \u8212- and this is indeed the primary {\i choshesh}, not incest, which would be a far rarer concern.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The general principle of {\i yuchasin} operates differently from other areas of {\i halakha}. In other domains, we often rely on {\i rov} \u8212- the statistical majority \u8212- to resolve doubts. Here, we are far more demanding about certainty of lineage. Once a {\i safek mamzer} status is assigned, the individual is considered a {\i safek mamzer} and may not marry a {\i Yisrael} \u8212- but may marry a {\i vadai mamzer} or a {\i geir}. Interestingly, what appears to be a stringency \u8212- calling someone a {\i safek mamzer} \u8212- operates simultaneously as a {\i kula}: it opens the door to marriages with {\i mamzerim} and {\i geirim} that would otherwise be unavailable if the person were simply considered a full {\i Yisrael}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The further asymmetry is worth noting: a {\i safek mamzer} cannot marry a full {\i Yisrael}, but nor can two {\i safek mamzerim} marry each other without difficulty, since one might be a {\i mamzer} and the other not. In practice, they may marry {\i geirim} and {\i mamzerim} of either sex.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Abba Shaul\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Abba Shaul haya korei l\u8217'shtuki beduki} \u8212- Abba Shaul would call the {\i shtuki} not {\i shtuki} but {\i beduki} \u8212- from {\i bedika}, investigation. Rather than accepting the silence as definitive, Abba Shaul\u8217's name for this person suggests that the proper response to uncertainty is inquiry and investigation. The {\i Gemara} will elaborate on what that means in practice.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Babylonian Ascent\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Aliyah Meaning\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} states {\i asara yuchasin alu miBavel} \u8212- ten categories of lineage {\i came up} from Babylon. The {\i Gemara} asks: {\i mai irya d\u8217'tani alu miBavel} \u8212- why does the {\i tanna} use the language of \u8220"came up\u8221"? Why is traveling from Babylon to Eretz Yisrael described as going {\i up}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Eretz Yisrael Higher\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The answer: {\i milta gavita kamashma lan} \u8212- it is telling us something about the relative sanctity and elevation of Eretz Yisrael. As the {\i braita} derives from the verse {\i v\u8217'kamta v\u8217'alita el hamakom asher yivchar Hashem Elokecha} \u8212- \u8220"you shall rise and go up to the place that God has chosen\u8221" \u8212- we learn that the {\i Beit HaMikdash} is higher than all of Eretz Yisrael, and Eretz Yisrael is higher than all other lands. The verse from {\i Yirmiyahu} further confirms this: God says that people will no longer praise Him solely as the One who brought Israel up from Egypt, but as the One who brought them up from all the northern lands and all the places to which they had been scattered \u8212- {\i asher he\u8217'ela}, \u8220"who brought them up\u8221" \u8212- meaning all those other lands require going {\i up} to reach Eretz Yisrael.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Elazar\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But why specifically mention that they came from {\i Bavel}? {\i Mesayei lei l\u8217'Rebbi Elazar} \u8212- the {\i mishnah}\u8217's phrasing supports the view of Rabbi Elazar: {\i lo ala Ezra miBavel ad she\u8217'asah k\u8217'solet nekiyah v\u8217'ala} \u8212- Ezra did not make {\i aliyah} from Babylon until he had made it like sifted flour \u8212- {\i solet nekiyah} \u8212- and then went up. That is, Ezra cleaned out Babylon of all those of questionable lineage before departing, so that what remained in Babylon was the refined, identified population, and what he brought to Eretz Yisrael was equally identifiable and categorized.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Abbayyei Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Abaye amar alu me-aleihem} \u8212- Abaye holds that they went up on their own initiative. According to Abaye, Ezra identified the lineage categories of the various families in Babylon; once identified as {\i challal}, {\i mamzer}, {\i nasin}, and so on, those individuals found their social position in Babylon untenable and chose on their own to join Ezra on his {\i aliyah}. Ezra did not force them out; he simply made their status known, and self-interest did the rest.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rava amar he-alum tnan} \u8212- Rava holds that the {\i mishnah}\u8217's language, {\i alu} (they went up), implies they were {\i brought up} \u8212- that Ezra actively compelled them to leave, against their will if necessary. Ezra controlled the entire process of identification and removal.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Removal Mechanism\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A further layer of disagreement: {\i iy ba\u8217'is eima d\u8217'kulei alma is lehu d\u8217'Rabbi Elazar} \u8212- one can argue that everyone agrees with Rabbi Elazar\u8217's essential point, that Ezra\u8217's agenda was to clean out Babylon. The dispute is only about the {\i mechanism}: {\i mar savar afrushei afrashunihu, m\u8217'nafsheihu siliku} \u8212- one view is that Ezra merely separated and identified them, and they departed of their own accord; {\i u-mar savar b\u8217'al korcha asikinuhu} \u8212- the other view is that he physically compelled their departure.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sifted Flour\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Kol ha\u8217'aratzot issah l\u8217'Eretz Yisrael, v\u8217'Eretz Yisrael issah l\u8217'Bavel} \u8212- Rav Yehuda in the name of Shmuel articulates the hierarchy: all lands are like {\i issah} (unkneaded dough \u8212- mixed, undifferentiated) relative to Eretz Yisrael, and Eretz Yisrael itself is like {\i issah} relative to Babylon. Babylon, having been purged of those of questionable lineage by Ezra, is the most refined \u8212- {\i solet nekiyah}, sifted flour. Eretz Yisrael, while more refined than other lands, still has admixture. All other lands are the most mixed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This makes intuitive sense according to Abaye\u8217's view: since those who went up did so voluntarily after being identified, the process in Eretz Yisrael was less controlled. Ezra could not maintain the same oversight once they had gone up, and over time the clarity of lineage in Eretz Yisrael became diluted again. Babylon, from which they had been removed, remained clean.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Later Generations\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} addresses the tension in Rava\u8217's view: if Ezra forcibly controlled the entire process, why isn\u8217't Eretz Yisrael also {\i solet nekiyah}? {\i Midei yad\u8217'inhu l\u8217'hahu dara} \u8212- in {\i that} generation, everyone knew everyone\u8217's identity. But {\i l\u8217'dara achrina lo yadi} \u8212- in subsequent generations, the clarity was lost. Over time, the categories blurred in Eretz Yisrael even though they had been crisply identified when those people arrived with Ezra. Babylon, having been emptied entirely of those categories, remained permanently clean \u8212- there was no one left there of questionable status to create confusion. Eretz Yisrael absorbed these people, and once the generation that knew them personally had passed, confusion set in again.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} also addresses a difficulty with Rava\u8217's view from the book of Ezra itself: when Ezra encamped at the river Ahava and investigated the people with him, he found that among the {\i kohanim} and {\i levi\u8217'im}, there were those who could not serve in the {\i Beit HaMikdash}. If Ezra had been in full control of identifying everyone from the outset, how did this come as a surprise? The answer: {\i nehi de-izhar bi-psulim} \u8212- Ezra had indeed identified who among the people bore stigmatized {\i yuchasin} statuses ({\i mamzer}, {\i nasin}, etc.), but what he discovered at the river was a different matter entirely \u8212- Kohanim and Levi\u8217'im who, while not bearing those general {\i psul} categories, were disqualified specifically from Temple service. That was a separate inquiry he had not yet completed. The two categories of disqualification are distinct, and discovering one does not mean the other had been addressed.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Kiddushin 70\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Return from Exile\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Opening & Location\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Today\u8217's daf is daf 70. We pick up in the middle of 69b, approximately fifteen lines down in the wide lines, at the word {\i minalan} \u8212- \u8220"from where do we know?\u8221" \u8212- where the text begins its discussion of {\i Kohani Leviei VeYisraeli}, the various groups that came up with Ezra from the Babylonian exile.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Three Groups\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} enumerates the different groups that returned with Ezra, and the {\i gemara} now proceeds to demonstrate that most of what the {\i mishnah} records is grounded in scriptural testimony. In this sense, the {\i mishnah} reflects a fairly accurate historical record, even if not every detail is made explicit in the verses. The {\i gemara} asks: {\i minalan disliku} \u8212- how do we know that members of the {\i kohanim}, {\i levi\u8217'im}, and {\i Yisrael} all came up with Ezra?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Scriptural Proof\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The answer comes from the verse: {\i Vayeshvu hakohanim vehaleviim umin ha\u8217'am vehameshorerim vehasho\u8217'arim vehanetinim be\u8217'areihem vechol Yisrael be\u8217'areihem} \u8212- \u8220"And the {\i kohanim} and the {\i levi\u8217'im} and from the people and the {\i meshorerim} and the {\i sho\u8217'arim}\u8221" \u8212- understood to refer to the {\i levi\u8217'im} who perform the singing or instrumental music and those who guard the gates \u8212- \u8220"and the {\i netinim} in their cities, and all of Israel in their cities.\u8221" The verse thus attests to {\i kohen}, {\i levi}, and {\i Yisrael}. The {\i netinim} \u8212- the Gibeonites \u8212- appear here as well, though their discussion comes later in the {\i mishnah}. Moving forward.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Chalalim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gedola Chazaka\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} turns to {\i chalalim}, {\i geirim}, and {\i charurim} \u8212- disqualified {\i kohanim}, converts, and freed slaves. How do we know {\i chalalim} \u8212- {\i kohanim} born from forbidden unions who therefore lack {\i kedushat kehuna} \u8212- came up with Ezra? A {\i braita} is cited: Rabbi Yosi says, {\i gedola chazaka} \u8212- the principle of presumed status is great \u8212- as we learn from the following passage in Ezra.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 No Genealogy\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The verse reads: {\i Umibnei hakohanim bnei Chavayah bnei haKotz bnei Barzillai asher lakach mibnot Barzillai haGil\u8217'adi ishah vayikarei al shemam} \u8212- these are the priestly families that went up from the exile, the sons of Chavayah, Kotz, and Barzillai, named after Barzillai the Gileadite because they had married his daughters and were identified by that name even though they were really his daughters\u8217' children. The verse continues: {\i Eleh bikshu ksavam hamityachasim velo nimtze\u8217'u} \u8212- these sought their records of lineage and could not find them. The word {\i yichus} appears here directly: they could not produce documentation to establish themselves as legitimate {\i kohanim}. As a result, {\i vayig\u8217'alu min hakehuna} \u8212- they were pushed out from the {\i kehuna}, though the word {\i vayig\u8217'alu} is read with the sense of being removed or rejected rather than redeemed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kodshei Kodashim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Vayomer HaTirshatha lahem asher lo yochlu mikodesh hakodashim ad amod kohen le\u8217'urim vetumim} \u8212- Nehemiah, who is called by the title {\i HaTirshatha}, said to them: since you cannot document your {\i kehuna}, you may no longer eat from the {\i kodshei hakodashim} \u8212- from the sacrificial offerings \u8212- until a {\i kohen gadol} arises with an {\i urim vetumim}. Since there was no {\i urim vetumim} in the Second {\i Beit Hamikdash}, Rashi understands this to mean, in effect, until the coming of Mashiach. They cannot prove their {\i yichus}, and so they cannot eat from the {\i korbanot}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Terumah Exemption\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But the {\i gemara} then introduces the principle of {\i gedola chazaka}. He said to them: {\i harei atem bechezkatchem} \u8212- you retain your presumed status. The question is: what were you eating in exile? {\i Bekodshei hagvul} \u8212- you were eating things sanctified outside the {\i Mikdash}, meaning {\i terumah}, which is what you had been eating until now. And so, {\i af kan nami bekodshei hagvul} \u8212- here too you may continue eating {\i terumah}; your {\i chazaka} is retained.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is the {\i chiddush} of Rabbi Yosi: although these {\i kohanim} could not document their {\i kehuna} and could not eat from the {\i korbanot}, they could continue eating {\i terumah} on the basis of their established presumptive status. There had been no contrary evidence disproving their {\i kehuna} \u8212- only an absence of supporting documentation. All {\i kohanim} today lack documentation of their lineage, yet we extend them the same {\i chazaka}. The distinction between {\i korbanot} and {\i terumah} here is notable and will be developed further.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Terumah Jurisdiction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A complication arises, however. There is an opinion \u8212- {\i ma\u8217'alin mitrumah le\u8217'yichus} \u8212- that one who is seen eating {\i terumah} is thereby presumed to hold full {\i yichus}, including fitness to bring {\i korbanot} in the {\i Beit Hamikdash}. If we allow these {\i kohanim} to eat {\i terumah} now that they are in Eretz Yisrael, observers may presume that they are fully qualified {\i kohanim} and draw incorrect inferences about their status vis-\u224 ?-vis the {\i Beit Hamikdash}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} answers: {\i shani hasam de-rei\u8217'a chezkateihu} \u8212- their case is different, because their {\i chazaka} has been weakened. Rashi explains that since a public declaration was made distinguishing those permitted to bring {\i korbanot} from those who are not, the distinction is already known. People understand that these particular {\i kohanim} are eating {\i terumah} on a different basis and will not draw the wrong conclusion. Tosafot adds that since it was publicly known that they had sought their genealogical records and failed to find them, no one would be confused into thinking they held full {\i yichus}. Either way, the historical event attached a clear and known label: they eat {\i terumah} but may not be {\i makriv korbanot}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This still raises the question: what exactly is {\i gedola chazaka}? A {\i chazaka} simply means that the status quo continues in the absence of contrary evidence \u8212- so what is so great about this one? The {\i gemara} answers: {\i demeikara achal biterumah derabbanan ulevesof achal biterumah de\u8217'oraita} \u8212- initially, in exile, {\i terumah} was only rabbinic in force, since {\i terumah} outside Eretz Yisrael has no biblical standing. But now that they are coming into Eretz Yisrael, their {\i chazaka} as {\i kohanim} in the domain of {\i terumah} carries over into {\i terumah de\u8217'oraita} as well. That is genuinely remarkable: within the world of {\i terumah}, once a {\i chazaka} of {\i kehuna} is established, it applies across all levels of {\i terumah} obligation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 An alternative answer is then offered: {\i ve\u8217'ibba\u8217'it eima le\u8217'olam hasam nami bederabbanan achal uved\u8217'oraita lo achal} \u8212- perhaps they may only eat {\i terumah derabbanan} even in Eretz Yisrael, meaning produce for which the biblical obligation of {\i terumah} does not apply. According to Rashi, {\i terumah de\u8217'oraita} applies only to grain, oil, and wine, so any other produce would remain {\i terumah derabbanan}. Under this reading, they are limited to {\i terumah derabbanan} in Eretz Yisrael as well, and there is no concern about the {\i ma\u8217'alin mitrumah le\u8217'yichus} problem, since that inference applies only when one is observed eating {\i terumah de\u8217'oraita}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But then what is {\i gedola chazaka}? The {\i gemara} replies: when they were in exile, there was no risk that eating {\i terumah derabbanan} there would ever lead to someone eating {\i terumah de\u8217'oraita}, because there was simply no {\i terumah de\u8217'oraita} outside Eretz Yisrael. But now, {\i af al gav de\u8217'ika lemigzar mishum terumah de\u8217'oraita} \u8212- even though one might say rabbinically we ought to prevent them from eating even the {\i terumah derabbanan} of fruits in Eretz Yisrael, lest observers see them eating it and assume they may also eat {\i terumah de\u8217'oraita} of grain \u8212- the {\i chazaka} overrides that rabbinic concern. We do not allow a speculative rabbinic hedge to erode an established {\i chazaka}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} also addresses a textual difficulty. The verse says {\i lo yochlu mikodshei hakodashim} \u8212- they shall not eat from {\i kodshei hakodashim}. Does this not imply that they {\i may} eat everything else, including {\i terumah de\u8217'oraita}? The {\i gemara} resolves this: the prohibition is against anything called {\i kodesh} or {\i kodashim} \u8212- which encompasses {\i terumah} as well \u8212- and even the lesser category of {\i korbanot} such as the {\i chazeh veshok}, the priest\u8217's portion of the {\i shelamim}, called {\i trumat hakodashim} in the verse that states a {\i bat kohen} who marries a non-{\i kohen} may not eat it. So according to the restrictive reading, they are barred from {\i terumah de\u8217'oraita} and from {\i korbanot} of all levels, but may continue eating {\i terumah derabbanan}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chazaka Strength\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might ask: is this not anomalous? The same person is simultaneously a {\i kohen} for the purposes of {\i terumah} and not a {\i kohen} for the purposes of {\i korbanot}. The entire point of Rabbi Yosi\u8217's teaching is precisely that \u8212- {\i gedola chazaka}: a {\i chazaka} established within a defined domain is operative within that domain even when it cannot be extended to an adjacent one. The {\i chazaka} was established through the practice of eating {\i terumah}; there is no parallel practice in the realm of {\i korbanot} that would generate a corresponding {\i chazaka} there.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This principle has important practical analogues. A {\i kohen} today has established a {\i chazaka} of {\i kehuna} through his ongoing observance of the laws of {\i tumah} \u8212- avoiding cemeteries, hospitals, and the like \u8212- in a way that he has not necessarily established through his marriage conduct. Accordingly, when a leniency is sought in a {\i tumah} case on the grounds that \u8220"perhaps he is not really a {\i kohen},\u8221" such an argument carries much less weight, because his actions have established him as a {\i kohen} in that domain. In the world of marriage, by contrast, no comparable practice has built up the same {\i chazaka}, so there may be somewhat more room to introduce doubt. The point, as the {\i gemara} illustrates here, is that {\i chazaka} operates through the lens of a particular legal domain, and one cannot automatically transfer it from one domain to another.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It also bears noting what kind of {\i chalalim} the {\i gemara} has actually established here. These are not definitively identified {\i chalalim} \u8212- they are {\i safek chalalim}, {\i kohanim} who could not prove they had a clean priestly lineage and may therefore have been {\i chalalim}. They were restricted from the {\i korbanot}, permitted to continue with {\i terumah}, and it is this group \u8212- those unable to establish their genealogical fitness \u8212- that represents the {\i chalalim} who came up with Ezra.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Gerim & Slaves\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gerim & Freedmen\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} turns to {\i geirim} and {\i charurim} \u8212- converts and freed slaves. How do we know they came up with Ezra? Rav Chisda says, from the verse at the top of 70a: {\i vechol hanivdal mitum\u8217'at goyei ha\u8217'aretz aleihem} \u8212- all those who separated themselves from the impurity of the nations of the land joined them. These were people who had previously been non-Jews, and their joining the community could only come about through conversion or manumission.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The question of {\i mamzeirim} follows. The {\i gemara} cites the verse: {\i vayishma Sanbalat haChoroni veTuviyah ha\u8217'eved ha\u8217'Ammoni} \u8212- Sanbalat and Tuviyah the Ammonite slave heard. The verse then states: {\i ki rabbim biYehudah ba\u8217'alei shevuah lo} \u8212- many in Judah had taken an oath to this Tuviyah. {\i Ki chatan hu liShechaniah ben Arach} \u8212- because he was a son-in-law to Shechaniah ben Arach, a Jew. So this slave had married a Jewish woman. And further: {\i viYehochanan beno lakach et bat Meshullam ben Berechiah} \u8212- and Tuviyah\u8217's son Yehochanan had also married the daughter of another Jewish man. These slaves, then, had married Jewish women and presumably had children from those unions \u8212- children who would have been {\i mamzeirim}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Intermarriage Issue\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But the {\i gemara} challenges this on several grounds. First, is a child born of a slave and a {\i bat Yisrael} really a {\i mamzer}? The {\i gemara} acknowledges that this holds only according to the opinion that such a child is a {\i mamzer} \u8212- on the grounds that {\i kiddushin} cannot take effect between them, and a child of a union where {\i kiddushin} is invalid is a {\i mamzer}. We do not rule that way; the accepted {\i halacha} is that such a child is {\i kasher}, not a {\i mamzer}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mamzeirim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Even setting that aside, the {\i gemara} raises further objections. How do we know they actually had children from these marriages? And even if they did, how do we know those children were born in Babylon and then came up to Eretz Yisrael? The verses being cited describe the situation {\i in Judah} \u8212- {\i biYehudah} \u8212- meaning those marriages may have taken place in the land itself. You cannot use those verses to prove that {\i mamzeirim} from Babylon made the journey with Ezra\u8217's group.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} therefore derives the point from a different source entirely.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Tel Melach Groups\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Genealogy Proof\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} turns to the verse: {\i ve\u8217'eleh ha\u8217'olim miTel Melach, Tel Charsha, Keruv, Addon ve\u8217'Immer} \u8212- these are those who came up from Tel Melach, Tel Charsha, and other locations. {\i Velo yachlu lehagid beit avotam vezar\u8217'am im Yisrael hem} \u8212- they could not say who their paternal household was, or, as Rashi explains, even from whom they were descended on their mother\u8217's side. They could not prove they were Jews at all. So already from the plain meaning of the verse, an entire contingent of the returning exiles could not establish their Israelite identity. They did not claim to be non-Jews; they simply had no way to prove they were Jewish.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tel Melach Derasha\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Now the {\i gemara} offers its {\i derasha}. {\i Tel Melach} \u8212- the name means \u8220"mound of salt.\u8221" These are people whose deeds resembled those of Sodom, which was overturned into a region of salt. According to certain {\i midrashim}, Sodom was associated with adultery; and so those compared to Sodom had committed adultery, which means their children were {\i mamzeirim}. So on the level of {\i derasha}, the first group \u8212- those from Tel Melach \u8212- represents {\i mamzeirim}, children of adulterous unions who cannot demonstrate legitimate Jewish descent.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shtukim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tel Charsha} \u8212- {\i charsha} evokes {\i cheresh}, silence. This refers to someone who calls out \u8220"Abba, Abba!\u8221" \u8212- who calls for his father \u8212- and his mother silences him. She cannot or will not reveal who the father is. That is a {\i shtuki} \u8212- someone whose paternity cannot be established, though his mother is known to be Jewish.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Asufim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The verse continues: {\i velo yachlu lehagid beit avotam vezar\u8217'am im Yisrael hem} \u8212- they could not even establish whether they were from Israel. {\i Zehu ha\u8217'asufi} \u8212- this is the foundling, {\i shene\u8217'esaf min hashuk}, one gathered from the marketplace. A {\i shtuki} does not know his father but knows he is Jewish through his mother. The {\i asufi} does not even know who his mother was, and so cannot establish Jewish identity at all.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i netinim}, it should be noted, appeared explicitly in an earlier verse in the passage, so their presence in the returning group is already accounted for. With these derivations, the {\i gemara} has now covered the complete list from the {\i mishnah}: {\i kohanim}, {\i levi\u8217'im}, {\i Yisrael}, {\i chalalim}, {\i geirim}, {\i charurim}, {\i mamzeirim}, {\i shtukim}, {\i asufim}, and {\i netinim}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Intermarriage Ethics\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Leopard Metaphor\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now transitions into a more {\i aggadic} register, directly confronting the social reality described by the {\i mishnah}. Rabbi Abbahu expounds the phrase {\i Keruv Addon ve\u8217'Immer} from the same verse. {\i Amar Adon} \u8212- the Master, God, said: {\i yehu Yisrael lefanai chashuvim ke-Keruv} \u8212- Israel should be as precious to Me as the cherubim, as the angels. {\i Vehem samu atzmam ke-namer} \u8212- but they made themselves like the leopard, like an untrustworthy, spotted animal. They committed adultery and intermarried inappropriately.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 An alternative version of the same {\i derasha} is also recorded: even though they made themselves like the leopard and acted in this disloyal way, {\i hen chashuvim lefanai ke-Keruv} \u8212- they are still precious before Me as the cherubim. God\u8217's love for Israel is not extinguished by their failures. Two readings of the same words, two very different theological endpoints.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Unequal Marriage\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabba bar bar Chana then cites a teaching with sharp moral force: {\i kol hanosei ishah she\u8217'einah hogenet lo} \u8212- anyone who marries a woman who is not fitting for him \u8212- here the context makes clear that \u8220"fitting\u8221" refers to social and genealogical standing, {\i yichus} \u8212- it is as though he plowed the entire world and sowed it with salt. The imagery is of destruction: you plow the earth to prepare it for seeds, then pour in salt to render it permanently barren. The marriage produces not growth but ruin. The {\i gemara} finds this reading in the same verse: {\i ve\u8217'eleh ha\u8217'olim miTel Melach} \u8212- {\i charsha}, the plowing; {\i melach}, the salt.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Marrying for Money\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabba bar Rav Adda citing Rav then extends this: {\i kol hanosei ishah leshum mamon} \u8212- anyone who marries a woman for money, that is, who accepts a lower {\i yichus} in exchange for wealth, {\i havyu lo banim she\u8217'einam hogenin} \u8212- will have children who are unworthy. The verse cited: {\i baHashem bagadu ki banim zarim yaladu} \u8212- they betrayed God because they bore foreign children. The word {\i zarim} points to the estrangement from proper lineage and character that results.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might think the wealth at least is preserved even if the children turn out poorly. Not so. {\i Atah yochlem chodesh et chelkeihem} \u8212- in a month their portion will be consumed. And lest one imagine only the man\u8217's portion is lost: {\i chelkeihem} \u8212- both their portions, his and hers. And lest one think the destruction takes a long time: {\i chodesh} \u8212- a month. Rav Nachman bar Yitzchak explains: one month comes in and another goes out \u8212- in a single month cycle, everything is gone.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Divine Judgment\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The teaching escalates further. Rabba bar Rav Adda citing Rav Sala citing Rav Hamnuna: {\i kol hanosei ishah she\u8217'einah hogenet lo, Eliyahu kofato veHakadosh Baruch Hu rotze\u8217'ahu} \u8212- whoever marries a woman who is not fitting for him, Eliyahu binds him to the post and God Himself administers the lashes. A {\i braita} adds: on all such people, Eliyahu writes and God seals \u8212- {\i oi lo leposel et zar\u8217'o uvehapogem et mishpachto ulehanosei ishah she\u8217'einah hogenet lo} \u8212- woe to him who stigmatizes his offspring and degrades his family and marries a woman not fitting for him.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The choice of Eliyahu as the one who writes this writ is significant. Eliyahu is alive in heaven; he is the one who tells God, when asked what he is doing there, that he alone has remained faithful. He is the guardian of fidelity to the covenant. That it is Eliyahu who writes and God who signs reflects a cosmic seriousness attached to this offense.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kol Haposel\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} continues: {\i vechol haposel pasul ve\u8217'eino medaber bishvacho le\u8217'olam} \u8212- and anyone who stigmatizes others is himself invalid, and never speaks in praise of others. This appears to be part of the writ Eliyahu writes, or perhaps it is the {\i gemara}\u8217's own observation \u8212- in either case, the one who goes around declaring others {\i pasul} is himself {\i pasul}, and is the sort of person who only ever criticizes, never praises. And Shmuel sharpens this into the maxim: {\i bemumo posel} \u8212- when someone invalidates another, it is because that very flaw is his own. The classic formulation that emerges is {\i kol haposel bemumo posel}: whoever disqualifies others shares their disqualification.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A reasonable question arises: does this mean that rabbis who investigate {\i yichus} and declare certain families {\i pesulim} are thereby themselves suspect? The answer is clearly not. The teaching addresses the layperson who spreads unfounded slurs, not the rabbinic authority who investigates and adjudicates. Ezra himself identified and categorized all these groups \u8212- such inquiry is legitimate and necessary. The {\i gemara} is concerned with the world in which status-anxiety is high and defamation is easy: someone who casually calls others {\i pasul} without evidence is revealing something about himself. But that is altogether different from the structured process of communal genealogical investigation.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Nehardea Visitor\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Butcher Incident\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now tells a vivid story that will wind its way back to {\i kol haposel bemumo posel}. A certain man from Nehardea came to Pumbedisa and went to the butcher to purchase meat. He was told to wait \u8212- they needed first to serve the attendant of Rav Yehuda bar Yechezkel. The visitor was outraged. He demanded to know who this \u8220"Yehuda bar Sheviskel\u8221" was to take precedence over him \u8212- mocking the name {\i Yechezkel} by substituting a derogatory term, which Rashi suggests may also carry the meaning of a glutton. When the people reported this to Rav Yehuda, Rav Yehuda placed the man in {\i nidui} \u8212- excommunication \u8212- for showing disrespect toward a {\i talmid chacham}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 They then told Rav Yehuda something further: this man is in the habit of calling people {\i avdi} \u8212- slaves \u8212- whenever he is offended. On the basis of this, Rav Yehuda proclaimed: this man is a slave. This declaration rests directly on the principle of {\i kol haposel bemumo posel}: if he constantly calls others slaves, it is because that stigma is his own.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Court Summons\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The visitor, now placed in {\i nidui} and declared a slave, was not prepared to let the matter rest. He summoned Rav Yehuda to stand before Rav Nachman\u8217's court in Nehardea. A writ of subpoena \u8212- a {\i pitka de\u8217'azmana} \u8212- was delivered to Rav Yehuda.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Huna\u8217's Advice\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Yehuda brought this subpoena to Rav Huna and asked: {\i eizal o lo eizal} \u8212- should I go or not? Rav Huna replied that as a matter of principle, Rav Yehuda had no need to go \u8212- {\i mishum degavra raba at} \u8212- because Rav Yehuda was a greater authority than Rav Nachman, and arguably Rav Nachman was outside his proper jurisdiction. However, Rav Nachman was a son-in-law of the house of the {\i nasi}, and given the honor owed to that family, Rav Huna advised: go. The whole episode had begun as a dispute about respect for authority and precedence, and now that tension was being played out on a larger stage.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yehuda Arrives\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Yehuda arrived and found Rav Nachman building a {\i ma\u8217'aka} \u8212- a fence. Immediately, Rav Yehuda challenged him: does the Master not know the teaching of Rav Huna bar Idi citing Shmuel, that once a person is appointed {\i parnas al hatzibur} \u8212- a communal leader \u8212- it is forbidden for him to perform labor in the presence of three people? Rav Yehuda, famously a devoted transmitter of Shmuel\u8217's teachings, was wasting no time in demonstrating that Rav Nachman was not only of lower stature but was actively violating a ruling Rav Yehuda knew firsthand. Rav Nachman replied that he was only repairing a small break, minimizing what he was doing.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Terminology Dispute\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But Rav Yehuda immediately pivoted to something else. The word Rav Nachman had used for \u8220"fence\u8221" was {\i gundirisa} \u8212- an unusual, elevated-sounding term. Rav Yehuda asked: what is wrong with the Torah\u8217's word {\i ma\u8217'aka}, or the rabbinic term {\i mechitza}? Why use this strange word?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Nachman then invited Rav Yehuda to sit, using the word {\i karpisa} for stool. Rav Yehuda responded: why not use {\i safsal}, which is the rabbinic term, or {\i itztaba}, which is what people commonly say? Rav Nachman offered fruit, calling it {\i estrunga}. Rav Yehuda shot back: Shmuel himself taught that anyone who says {\i estrunga} has a third of haughtiness in him \u8212- use either {\i esrog}, as the rabbis say, or {\i esroga}, as people say. Rav Nachman then offered a drink using the word {\i anvaga}. Rav Yehuda asked: why not {\i ispargos}, the rabbinic term, or {\i anfak}, the common word?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The pattern is consistent: Rav Yehuda is showing that at every turn, Rav Nachman is using inflated, affected language \u8212- putting on airs \u8212- while simultaneously conducting himself in ways unbecoming of his claimed authority. One listener suggested the opposite reading, that Rav Nachman was using common vernacular. But the {\i gemara}\u8217's logic moves clearly in the other direction: Shmuel\u8217's statement that {\i estrunga} reflects haughtiness confirms that the vocabulary was the problem, not the simplicity.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Women\u8217's Service\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Nachman then called for his daughter {\i Dunag} to pour wine for them. Rav Yehuda stopped him: Shmuel explicitly taught that one may not make use of a woman\u8217's services. Rav Nachman protested \u8212- she is a minor! Rav Yehuda replied: Shmuel said explicitly, {\i bein gedolah bein ketannah} \u8212- whether a grown woman or a minor.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Nachman then tried a different tack. Perhaps Rav Yehuda would like to send his respects to Rav Nachman\u8217's wife, Yalta? Rav Yehuda replied: Shmuel taught {\i kol be\u8217'ishah ervah} \u8212- the voice of a woman is {\i ervah}. If she responds to my greeting I will hear her voice. Rav Nachman then suggested it could be done through an intermediary. Rav Yehuda: Shmuel taught one does not ask after the welfare of a woman even through an intermediary. Through her husband then? Rav Yehuda: Shmuel taught one does not ask after a woman\u8217's welfare at all, {\i klal} \u8212- categorically.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This last ruling prompted a challenge from Tosafot: the {\i malachim} asked {\i Ayeh Sarah ishtecha} \u8212- where is Sarah your wife? Tosafot\u8217's distinction, which the lecturer acknowledges is not entirely clear, appears to be between asking how a woman is doing ({\i she\u8217'ilat shalom}) and actively dispatching a message of good wishes \u8212- the former being permitted, the latter being what Shmuel prohibits. However this distinction is parsed, the {\i gemara} treats {\i kol be\u8217'ishah ervah} here as referring to the speaking voice. The {\i rishonim} note that this is not absolute: in a context where men and women are accustomed to speaking with one another, the speaking voice is not categorically {\i ervah} \u8212- which underscores the broader principle that these categories are contextually sensitive.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yalta\u8217's Intervention\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 At this point Yalta, Rav Nachman\u8217's wife, sent a message to her husband: dismiss the case and let this man go \u8212- {\i delo nishvach kishar amei ha\u8217'aretz} \u8212- otherwise Rav Yehuda would reduce Rav Nachman to the level of an ordinary {\i am ha\u8217'aretz}. Rav Yehuda was making Rav Nachman look incompetent at every exchange, and Yalta was sharp enough to see that this needed to end.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Nachman\u8217's Capitulation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Nachman now attempted to recover. He asked Rav Yehuda, {\i mai shayata demar hacha} \u8212- what brings you here? Rav Yehuda was taken aback: you sent me a subpoena! He produced the writ from his jacket and showed it. Rav Nachman responded: {\i hashta deshuta demar lo gamirna} \u8212- I cannot even follow your ordinary conversation, you are so far above me; do you think I would ever send you a subpoena to a {\i beis din}? {\i Chas veshalom}, I would never presume to do such a thing!\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Nonetheless, since Rav Yehuda had already made the journey, Rav Nachman said they should hear the case \u8212- {\i ki heichi delo lemru machanfe rabbanan ahadadi} \u8212- so people should not say that rabbis merely flatter and show favoritism to one another. Rav Nachman asked: why did you put this man in {\i nidui}? Because he caused pain to a messenger of the rabbis. Why not lashes? Rav responded to similar offenses with lashes; Rav Yehuda explained that {\i nidui} is actually a more significant and lasting censure. Why did you declare him a slave? {\i Deragil dekari inshi avdei} \u8212- because he habitually calls others slaves. {\i Vetani kol haposel pasul ve\u8217'eino medaber bishvacha le\u8217'olam, ve\u8217'amar Shmuel bemumo posel} \u8212- and the teaching of Shmuel is: when someone disqualifies others, it is because that flaw is his own.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Nachman pressed back: Shmuel\u8217's teaching, one might say, means only to be {\i suspect} of such a person \u8212- not to {\i proclaim} him a slave outright. Is actually declaring someone a slave not taking things too far?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Hasmonean Claim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 While they were still debating this very point, the litigant himself stepped forward and said to Rav Yehuda: you had the audacity to call me a slave? {\i De\u8217'atina mibeit Chashmonai malka} \u8212- I descend from the royal Hasmonean house!\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Slave Verdict\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Yehuda replied without hesitation: Shmuel taught \u8212- {\i kol de\u8217'amar mibeit Chashmonai ka\u8217'atina avda hu} \u8212- anyone who claims descent from the Hasmonean house is a slave. The entire Hasmonean line was wiped out; the one who assumed their name and perpetuated it was Herod \u8212- {\i Hordos} \u8212- who was a slave. So by claiming Hasmonean descent, the man has only confirmed the verdict. The story will continue tomorrow, but Rav Yehuda has, in every exchange, thoroughly demonstrated his authority over Rav Nachman.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Kiddushin 71\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Chashmonai Testimony\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Slave Declaration\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The daf begins in the middle of 71b, six lines down from the top of the wide text, at the word {\i Chashmonai}. We are in the midst of a narrative involving Rav Yehuda and Rav Huna, centered on a case of {\i din Torah} that arose when a certain man came to Pumbedita, insulted Rav Yehuda\u8217's attendant, and was himself known to speak disparagingly of others \u8212- calling people slaves. In response, Rav Yehuda declared this man to be a slave. The broader backdrop of this story is the principle of {\i kol haposel bemumo posel} \u8212- one who invalidates others does so because of his own defect.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 As established in the previous daf, Rav Huna challenged Rav Yehuda\u8217's declaration, arguing that even if Shmuel taught {\i kol haposel bemumo posel}, this teaching should at most make one {\i choshesh} \u8212- cautiously alert \u8212- but not empower a formal declaration of slavery. At that very moment, the man in question entered the room and declared indignantly that he was a descendant of {\i Beit Chashmonai}. Rav Yehuda seized on this: precisely as I said! Because Shmuel himself taught that anyone who claims descent from {\i Beit Chashmonai} is a slave, since the original Hasmonean dynasty was entirely killed off, and it was only Herod \u8212- an Edomite slave \u8212- who remained and identified with that lineage. Therefore, any contemporary claim to Hasmonean descent is itself evidence of slave ancestry.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kol Haposel Teaching\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now raises a pointed challenge to Rav Yehuda\u8217's reliance on this teaching. Rav Huna said to him: do you not hold by the principle that Rav Abba cited in the name of Rav, who cited it in the name of Rav Huna \u8212- who cited it in the name of Rav? This is already somewhat striking: Rav Huna is quoting a teaching transmitted in his own name, from Rav, the classic {\i bar plugta} of Shmuel. The teaching states: {\i kol talmid chacham shemoreh halacha uva} \u8212- any {\i talmid chacham} who advances a new legal ruling, {\i im kodem maaseh amra shomin lo} \u8212- if he taught it before the relevant case arose, he is believed. But {\i im lav ein shomin lo} \u8212- if he produces the ruling only at the moment when the case conveniently arises before him, we are somewhat suspicious of it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is a remarkable piece of Talmudic skepticism. The Gemara acknowledges that a scholar might fabricate a tradition in the name of his teacher precisely to respond to a case at hand. In our story, the suspicion is compounded: Rav Yehuda was not merely citing a teaching that happened to be relevant \u8212- he was invoking it specifically to vindicate his own prior ruling. He declared the man a slave, and now he produces a teaching of Shmuel that justifies that declaration. One might fairly ask: why have we never heard this teaching before?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Matna Confirms\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 To this challenge, Rav Yehuda responded: {\i ha\u8217'ika Rav Masna dekay kivasay} \u8212- Rav Matna holds like me. Rav Matna can corroborate that this is indeed a teaching of Shmuel. It then happened that Rav Matna, who had not been in Nehardea \u8212- the location of these proceedings \u8212- for the past thirteen years, arrived that very day. {\i Hahu yoma} \u8212- by pure coincidence, on that exact day, he came to Nehardea.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Yehuda then had to prompt Rav Matna without leading him, and he did so elegantly: do you remember what Shmuel taught {\i ki kachada} \u8212- on that occasion when he was stepping onto the ferry, one foot still on the bank and one foot already on the vessel? The memorable physical circumstance made the teaching verifiable and distinctive. Rav Matna confirmed: {\i hachi amar Shmuel} \u8212- Shmuel said: {\i kol d\u8217'omar midebeis Chashmonai moga chasina avda hu} \u8212- anyone who says he descends from the royal house of the Hasmoneans is a slave.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Woman on Roof\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Matna then provided the reason for Shmuel\u8217's ruling. He recalled that after Herod\u8217's violent overthrow of the Hasmonean house, only one young woman remained from the original dynasty. When she saw what Herod had done \u8212- that the entire family had been slaughtered \u8212- she went up to the roof, {\i v\u8217'ramya kala}, and called out: let it be known that anyone who claims descent from the house of {\i Chashmonai} is a slave, for there is no one left. And then, {\i nafla m\u8217'igra umesa} \u8212- she fell from the roof and died. With this, the entire Hasmonean lineage was extinguished, and Herod \u8212- the Edomite slave \u8212- was the only one who identified himself with that heritage.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Yehuda had now been vindicated on all fronts.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ketuvot Torn Up\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Achrizu alei d\u8217'avda hu} \u8212- they announced about this man that he was indeed a slave, exactly as Rav Yehuda had originally declared. The consequences were severe: {\i hahu yoma akran kama ksuva beNardaa} \u8212- on that day, many {\i ketubot} were torn up in Nehardea.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rashi explains that the members of this man\u8217's family, having now been identified as slaves, could not have contracted valid {\i kiddushin}, and so those marriages required invalidation. There is an obvious problem, however: the man\u8217's testimony applies only to himself \u8212- there is no independent, objective evidence that implicates his family members. How could their marriages be invalidated on the basis of his own admission? One commentator resolves this by suggesting that the {\i ketubot} were torn up for a different reason: he had served as a signatory on those documents, and now that he had been declared {\i pasul}, his testimony was rendered invalid, requiring the documents to be redrawn. Yet the narrative suggests something more sweeping.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 As Rav Yehuda departed Nehardea to return to Pumbedita, the families whose marriages had been disrupted ran after him intending to stone him \u8212- literally, stones were in their hands. He turned to them and said: {\i ei shtiku shtiku} \u8212- if you stay silent, stay silent. But if you continue making a fuss, {\i maglina alaichu} \u8212- I will reveal something about you that is even worse. What could be worse than being declared slaves? Shmuel, he said, also taught: {\i tarti zarayasa ika beNardaa} \u8212- there are two families in Nehardea, one called the house of the dove and one called the house of the raven \u8212- {\i v\u8217'simanach tamei tamei tahor tahor} \u8212- the impure family bears the name of the impure bird, and the pure family bears the name of the pure bird. Those descended from the raven are of invalid lineage \u8212- {\i mamzerim}, slaves, or the like.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 How exactly this threat made things worse if they were already declared slaves is somewhat unclear from the text; perhaps the formal {\i halachic} ruling against the wider family had not yet been made and remained merely a suspicion. Regardless, the message landed. {\i Shadu alahu rigma} \u8212- they threw down the stones they had intended to hurl at him, and so many stones fell into the {\i Nehar Malka} that they formed a dam in the river.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Invalid Families\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Announced Invalidities\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The foregoing story raises an important question: if Rav Yehuda knew about the invalidity of these Nehardean families, why had he been withholding the information? We will return shortly to the principle of {\i mishpacha keivan shenitm\u8217'ah nitm\u8217'ah} \u8212- once a family\u8217's lineage has been submerged, one leaves it submerged. That principle stands in tension with what we have just witnessed. In the present story, the declaration of slavery came only as a response to the man\u8217's own behavior \u8212- {\i kol haposel bemumo posel} \u8212- and it happened to be borne out. Nevertheless, the Gemara now pivots in the opposite direction, moving into a series of proactive public announcements about people and places of problematic lineage.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Machriz Rav Yehuda} \u8212- Rav Yehuda publicly announced, according to one textual tradition in Pumbedita and according to another in Nehardea: {\i Ada veYonatan avdi} \u8212- these two individuals are slaves; {\i Yehuda bar Pappa mamzer} \u8212- this person is a {\i mamzer}; {\i Bati bar Tuvia berama srucha deshakil gitei} \u8212- this wealthy man, universally assumed to be of excellent {\i yichus}, was actually offered a {\i shtar shichrur}, a deed of manumission, but refused to accept it because of his pride in his high social standing. Nonetheless, he is halachically a slave. Here Rav Yehuda is not merely responding to a case \u8212- he is proactively labeling individuals as {\i pasul}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava\u8217's Declarations\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Machriz Rava beMechuza} \u8212- Rava similarly made public announcements in Mechuza, his own city: {\i Belai Danai Talai Malai Zagai kulam lepsul} \u8212- these families are all of invalid status, whether {\i mamzer}, {\i avdus}, or some other form of disqualification. Rav Yehuda further identified whole towns: {\i Gvai} is a corruption of {\i Givonai} \u8212- the inhabitants are really {\i Givonim}, of problematic lineage. {\i Durnunisa} is {\i Darei Netinai} \u8212- the village of {\i Netinim}. The {\i Netinim} and the {\i Givonim} are the same. And Rav Yosef added that a suburb of Pumbedita, {\i Beikvei}, was entirely composed of slaves.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 These broad geographic labelings are deeply troubling, moving beyond the identification of individuals to the invalidation of entire communities. The Gemara draws a parallel to the actions of Ezra, who similarly identified and sorted the {\i psulim} when he returned from Bavel to Eretz Yisrael.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Slave Intermarriage\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Yehuda said in the name of Shmuel: {\i arba meos avadim, ve\u8217'amrei lei arba alafim avadim hayu lo lePishchar ben Imar} \u8212- four hundred slaves, and some say four thousand slaves, belonged to a man named Pishchar ben Imar. {\i Vekulam nitmu bikehuna} \u8212- all of them intermarried among {\i kohanim}. Rashi explains that as slaves of a {\i kohen}, they were permitted to eat {\i terumah} and were thus able to pass themselves off as {\i kohanim}, eventually intermarrying into priestly families. The practical consequence is alarming: a {\i kohen} we encounter today might actually descend from one of these slaves. But how far does that suspicion extend \u8212- should we be suspicious of every {\i kohen}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara offers a suggested marker: {\i vekol kohen sheyesh bo azus panim eino ela mehem} \u8212- any {\i kohen} who displays brazenness is probably from among these slaves and is not a legitimate {\i kohen}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Abaye\u8217's Pushback\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye pushes back on this, first noting practically that all of these slave-descended pseudo-{\i kohanim} can be found in a particular position of authority in a specific locale \u8212- they are identifiable. But he then challenges the underlying principle directly. This view, that brazenness is a marker of illegitimate priestly descent, {\i pliga d\u8217'Rabbi Elazar} \u8212- contradicts the position of Rabbi Elazar. Rabbi Elazar taught: {\i im ra\u8217'isa kohen b\u8217'azus metzach al taharher acharav} \u8212- if you see a {\i kohen} who acts with brazenness, do not suspect thereby that he is an illegitimate {\i kohen}. The proof is from the verse {\i v\u8217'amcha k\u8217'mrivai kohen} \u8212- your people are like those who contend with a {\i kohen}, which indicates that a {\i kohen} can be contentious; it takes two parties to have a quarrel. So brazenness alone cannot be taken as evidence of disqualified lineage.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 We find ourselves, then, in a state of tension: a great deal of labeling and suspecting, with only a small degree of pushback thus far \u8212- though the pushback will grow considerably.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Aggada on Lineage\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Divine Presence & Yichus\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now turns to {\i aggadic} material bearing on the themes of genealogy and the Divine Presence. Rav Avin bar Rav Adda said in the name of Rav: {\i kol hanosei ishah she\u8217'eino hogenes lo} \u8212- anyone who marries a woman of lesser lineage than himself, {\i kshe\u8217'hakadosh baruch hu mashreh shechinaso maid al kol hashvatim v\u8217'ein maid alav} \u8212- when God causes His {\i Shechina} to dwell among the people, He testifies on behalf of all the {\i shevatim} but will not testify about or identify with that family. The verse cited is {\i shivtei Yah edus l\u8217'Yisrael} \u8212- the tribes of God, testimony to Israel. The {\i Shechina} gives testimony about Israel only when they are truly {\i shivtei Yah}, when the tribal and family structures are properly maintained and not adulterated.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A subtle question arises: does \u8220"adulterated\u8221" here refer to marrying someone of inferior but still halachically valid status \u8212- in which case this {\i aggadah} is making a sweeping statement about social hierarchy \u8212- or does it refer to marrying someone of formally invalid status, a {\i mamzer} or slave, who would genuinely disqualify the family line? Given the immediate context of the discussion about {\i mamzeirim} and {\i avdus}, the latter reading seems more likely, but the text does not foreclose the former.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shechina Dwelling\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Chama in the name of Rabbi Chanina sharpens this further: {\i kshe\u8217'hakadosh baruch hu mashreh shechinaso, ein mashreh ela al mishpachos meyuchasos shebeYisrael} \u8212- when God causes His {\i Shechina} to rest, it rests only upon families of distinguished lineage within Israel. The verse is from Jeremiah: {\i ba\u8217'eis hahi neum Hashem eheyeh l\u8217'Elokim l\u8217'chol mishpachos Yisrael} \u8212- at that time, says God, I will be a God to all the {\i mishpachos} of Israel. Notably, the verse does not say {\i l\u8217'chol Yisrael} but {\i l\u8217'chol mishpachos} \u8212- to all the families, meaning those who have preserved their family distinctions and {\i yichus}. This is a stronger statement than the previous one; here the {\i Shechina} does not merely withhold its testimony, but actively does not dwell except upon families of recognized lineage.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Native vs Convert\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbah bar Rav Huna draws a distinction between a native-born Israelite and a {\i ger}: {\i zo ma\u8217'alah yesh bein Yisrael l\u8217'geirim} \u8212- there is a level of superiority that the native-born Jew holds over the convert. When Scripture speaks about Israel, it says {\i v\u8217'hayisi lahem l\u8217'Elokim v\u8217'heimah yihyu li l\u8217'am} \u8212- I will be for them a God, and they will be for Me a nation. The relationship with God is stated first; it preexists and draws Israel near. When Scripture addresses converts, it says: {\i mi hu zeh arav es libo l\u8217'higeshal elai v\u8217'hayisem li l\u8217'am v\u8217'anochi eheyeh lachem l\u8217'Elokim} \u8212- whoever stirs his heart to come close to Me, you will be for Me a nation and I will be for you a God. Here the initiative must come from the convert; the divine relationship follows rather than precedes their approach.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rashi reads this as descriptive: the native-born Jew is born into an existing covenantal relationship that draws him naturally toward God, while the {\i ger} must first generate the motivation from within himself. One might fairly read this as an extraordinary tribute to the spiritual achievement of the convert \u8212- the initiative required is immense. However, the Gemara\u8217's framing is plainly hierarchical, and it cannot easily be read otherwise.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Converts as Scabs\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Chelbo makes a striking and harsh statement: {\i kashim gerim le\u8217'Yisrael kesappachat} \u8212- converts are as difficult for Israel as a scab. The verse is {\i venilva hager aleihem venispachu al beit Yaakov} \u8212- the stranger will cleave to them and be added onto the house of Jacob. The word {\i venispachu} is connected exegetically to {\i sappachat}, the skin affliction mentioned among the forms of {\i tzara\u8217'at}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What does this mean? Rashi, working within the context of tractate Yevamot, explains that converts are sometimes insufficiently conversant with the tradition and may lead others astray. Tosafot, however, connects the statement to the immediately preceding discussion: if the {\i Shechina} dwells only upon {\i mishpachos meyuchasos}, then the presence of {\i geirim} among Israel \u8212- who do not have the same chain of {\i yichus} \u8212- diminishes that quality. This is an extremely harsh reading of the concept of {\i yichus}, one that operates not merely as a halachic category but as a marker of social and spiritual hierarchy.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot then cites, though without attribution, an interpretation by one Rav Avraham Hager \u8212- a man who was himself a convert yet whose interpretation made its way into Tosafot \u8212- who read the statement in precisely the opposite spirit: because converts are new to the tradition and approach it with passion rather than complacency, they put to shame the native-born Jews who are not so {\i medakdek bemitzvot}. This is a beautiful inversion, and Tosafot records it seriously.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 There is no question that this entire sugyah on {\i yichus} sits in considerable tension with a more egalitarian ethos, and equally with other Talmudic passages that speak in glowing terms about {\i geirim} and invoke the commandments of {\i ve\u8217'ahavtem et hager} and {\i kager ka\u8217'ezrach}. Both currents are present in the Talmud, and the tension between them is real.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Keep It Hidden\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Money Purifies\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Continuing at the bottom of amud bet, Rabbi Chama bar Chanina teaches: {\i kshe\u8217'hakadosh baruch hu metaher shvatim} \u8212- when God comes to purify the tribes, {\i shivto shel Levi metaher techila} \u8212- He begins with the tribe of Levi. The prooftext is from Malachi: {\i veyashev metzaref umetaher kesef vetiher es bnei Levi v\u8217'zikak osam k\u8217'zahav v\u8217'chakesef} \u8212- He will sit as one who smelts and purifies silver, and He will purify the sons of Levi, refining them like gold and silver, and they will then be able to bring offerings to God in righteousness.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yehoshua ben Levi, however, turns this passage on its head: {\i kesef metaher mamzeirim} \u8212- it is money that purifies {\i mamzeirim}. This is a striking inversion of the imagery. The verse\u8217's metaphor of silver purification is reread not as an analogy for genealogical purification but as a literal reference to wealth. Rashi explains the reference: wealthy individuals \u8212- for the sake of money \u8212- married into the tribe of Levi despite having invalid status, and the point Rabbi Yehoshua ben Levi is making is not that money literally confers kashrut, but rather that once people have intermarried, the genealogical record is absorbed and everything becomes {\i kasher} by virtue of being submerged. That is the operative mechanism of purification \u8212- not the money itself, but the intermingling that money enabled.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Ri\u8221"f, however, reads {\i kesef metaher mamzeirim} more literally and more cleverly: the halachic mechanism by which a {\i mamzer} can produce non-{\i mamzer} offspring is through marriage to a {\i shifcha} \u8212- a female slave \u8212- whose child, being a slave, can then be freed and thereby exits the status of {\i mamzer} entirely. The money involved is the purchase price of the slave. This is an elegant halachic reading.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mixed Family Rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yitzchak draws out the implication of the verse\u8217's closing phrase \u8212- {\i v\u8217'hayu l\u8217'Hashem magishei mincha bitzdaka}, they will bring offerings to God in righteousness: {\i tzadaka asa hakadosh baruch hu im Yisrael} \u8212- God performed an act of charity for Israel \u8212- {\i shemishpacha shenitme\u8217'ah nitme\u8217'ah} \u8212- that once a family has been submerged into the general population, it remains submerged. This is the principle of {\i mishpacha shenitme\u8217'ah nitme\u8217'ah}, written with an {\i ayin}, meaning merged rather than defiled. God\u8217's act of righteousness consists precisely in not requiring Israel to investigate and uproot every genealogical impurity that has been absorbed into the community. If people present themselves as {\i Levi\u8217'im} or {\i Yisraelim}, and their intermarriage has made it impossible to know otherwise, they are accepted as such.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 With this, the Gemara begins to shift explicitly against the mode of proactive identification that characterized the earlier part of the {\i sugya}. The two tendencies \u8212- publicly labeling {\i psulim} on the one hand, and accepting the status quo on the other \u8212- have now both been articulated, and the latter is gaining ground.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Boundaries & Assumpt (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shmuel\u8217's Teaching\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara introduces a teaching of Rav Yehuda in the name of Shmuel that returns to the geographic framework established earlier in the {\i perek}: {\i kol ha\u8217'aratzos issa l\u8217'Eretz Yisrael} \u8212- all other lands are like {\i issa}, dough, compared to Eretz Yisrael. The contrast drawn earlier was that when Ezra departed from Bavel, he left it like {\i solet nekiyah} \u8212- refined, sifted flour, with the bran entirely removed \u8212- meaning that all those of problematic lineage had departed with him, and what remained in Bavel was pure. All other lands lack this quality; they are like dough, mixed and unpurified. But {\i v\u8217'Eretz Yisrael issa l\u8217'Bavel} \u8212- Eretz Yisrael is itself like dough compared to Bavel. Even Eretz Yisrael, which contains {\i psulim} in known and recorded form, has a less certain pedigree than Bavel overall.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 In the time of Rabbi, the question arose whether to reverse this ranking \u8212- to say that Bavel had perhaps deteriorated over time and should be considered {\i issa} relative to Eretz Yisrael. Rabbi responded sharply: {\i kotzim atem matilim li bein einai} \u8212- you are placing thorns in my eyes. Since Rabbi was descended from Hillel, and Hillel came from Bavel, any downgrading of Babylonian {\i yichus} would undermine Rabbi\u8217's own genealogical standing and his claim of descent from the house of David. Accordingly, he deferred the matter to Rabbi Chanina bar Chama, who engaged those raising the challenge and reported: {\i kach mikubalni mi\u8217'Rabbi Yishmael be\u8217'Rabbi Yossi she\u8217'amar mishum aviv} \u8212- this is the tradition I received from Rabbi Yishmael in the name of his father: all lands are dough compared to Eretz Yisrael, and Eretz Yisrael is dough compared to Bavel. The tradition was thus confirmed independently, which was important precisely because Rabbi himself could not have reported it without appearing self-interested.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Diversion Strategy\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A parallel episode occurred in the time of Rabbi Pinchas. When the same attempt arose \u8212- to reclassify Bavel as inferior to Eretz Yisrael \u8212- Rabbi Pinchas devised a remarkable stratagem. He told his servants: when I say two things in the {\i beit hamidrash}, rush me out immediately, carry me out on my stretcher and run. He went in and announced: {\i ein shechita l\u8217'of min haTorah} \u8212- the requirement of ritual slaughter for birds is not Biblically mandated; it is only rabbinic. This is a provocative ruling that does not represent the final {\i halacha}, but it was sufficiently surprising to throw the assembled scholars into a state of confusion and discussion. While they were processing this startling claim, he quickly added: {\i kol ha\u8217'aratzot issa l\u8217'Eretz Yisrael v\u8217'Eretz Yisrael issa l\u8217'Bavel} \u8212- and then his servants ran him out on the stretcher before anyone could ask follow-up questions.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The scholars pursued him but could not catch up. When they sat back down and began investigating which families in Eretz Yisrael might be problematic \u8212- since Rabbi Pinchas had implied that there were some {\i psulim} there \u8212- they found themselves approaching dangerous territory. {\i Ad she\u8217'higiu l\u8217'sakana} \u8212- until they reached a point of danger: Rashi explains that they began discovering that some very prominent families might have questionable {\i yichus}, at which point they realized it was better to stop looking. {\i U\u8217'pirshu} \u8212- and they ceased.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Keep It Hidden (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Yochanan Stays Sil\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yochanan stated, taking an oath by the {\i Beit HaMikdash}: {\i b\u8217'yadeinu hu} \u8212- it is within our capacity to identify certain families of invalid lineage \u8212- {\i aval mah e\u8217'eseh she\u8217'gdolei hador nitme\u8217'u bo} \u8212- but what can I do, since great figures of this generation have already been intermingled with those families? To reveal the invalidity would call into question the {\i yichus} of major communal figures. And so Rabbi Yochanan kept silent. This is the clearest expression yet of the principle of {\i mishpacha shenitme\u8217'ah nitme\u8217'ah}: even when knowledge exists, the decision is made not to deploy it. Rabbi Yochanan {\i savar lah k\u8217'Rabbi Yitzchak} \u8212- held in accordance with Rabbi Yitzchak, who taught explicitly: {\i mishpacha shenitmeah nitmeah} \u8212- once a family has been merged in, it stays merged in.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Forced Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye notes a corroborating {\i baraita} from tractate Eduyot: there was a family called {\i Beit Hatzerifah} on the far side of the Jordan which was actually of valid lineage, but a powerful man named Ben Tziyon used his authority \u8212- {\i b\u8217'zroa}, with force \u8212- to get everyone to treat them as {\i pasul}. Conversely, there was another family that was genuinely {\i pasul}, and Ben Tziyon again used his power to force people to treat them as {\i kasher}. The {\i Mishnah} then states: cases like these, {\i Eliyahu ba l\u8217'tamei u\u8217'l\u8217'taher l\u8217'rachek u\u8217'l\u8217'karev} \u8212- Eliyahu will come to set right, declaring the unjustly invalidated to be valid and the unjustly validated to be {\i pasul}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara comments: this {\i mishnah} about Eliyahu refers specifically to {\i d\u8217'yedian} \u8212- situations that are known to be problematic and have been handled improperly. But {\i aval mishpacha shenitmeah} \u8212- when a family\u8217's invalid lineage has genuinely been absorbed and lost to public knowledge \u8212- {\i nitmeah}, it stays that way, and no rectification is expected.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Secret Traditions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i baraita} then teaches: {\i oda cheres haysa v\u8217'lo ratzu chachamim l\u8217'galosa} \u8212- there was another invalid family that the Sages did not wish to publicize. Nevertheless, the Sages transmitted this information to their children and students {\i pa\u8217'am achas b\u8217'shavua} \u8212- once every seven years; some say twice every seven years, so that some record would survive until the time of Eliyahu HaNavi.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Nachman bar Yitzchak rules that the preferable position is once every seven years \u8212- the less spoken about such matters, the better. The preference for restraint is reinforced by a {\i baraita}: if a person says, \u8220"I hereby take upon myself {\i nezirut} on the condition that I will not reveal genealogical problems,\u8221" we say {\i yehei nazir v\u8217'lo yigaleh mishpachos} \u8212- let him be a {\i nazir} and not reveal the family information. Rather than allowing him to choose between the {\i nezirut} and disclosure, we direct him to accept the {\i nezirut} and remain silent.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 At this juncture, the Gemara draws an illuminating parallel. Rabbah bar Bar Chana said in the name of Rabbi Yochanan: the four-letter Divine name \u8212- {\i shem ben arba otiyos} \u8212- the Sages transmitted to their students {\i pa\u8217'am achas b\u8217'shavua}, once every seven years; some say twice every seven years. Rav Nachman bar Yitzchak again prefers the position of once every seven years, deriving support from the verse {\i zeh shemi le\u8217'olam} \u8212- this is My name forever \u8212- which is written {\i le\u8217'aleim}, suggesting it should remain hidden. The parallel is pointed: just as the transmission of God\u8217's secret name is hedged with restraint and solemnity, so too the transmission of genealogical secrets about invalid families.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava once sought to teach the four-letter name publicly to his students. A certain elder stopped him by quoting {\i le\u8217'aleim ksiv} \u8212- it is written to be concealed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Avina raises an apparent contradiction: one verse says {\i zeh shemi le\u8217'olam} \u8212- this is My name forever (which we read {\i le\u8217'illum}, hidden), while another says {\i zeh zikhri} \u8212- this is how I am to be remembered. God\u8217's response, as it were, is: {\i lo kshe\u8217'ani nikhtav ani nikra} \u8212- I am not read the way I am written. I am written with the {\i yud-keh-vav-keh} but read as {\i Adon}. The written name is for concealment; the spoken name is for public invocation. The name itself \u8212- {\i shemi} \u8212- is {\i le\u8217'aleim}, hidden. The name by which I am addressed \u8212- {\i zikhri} \u8212- is known.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then describes a progression. Originally, the twelve-letter name of God was transmitted to everyone. Once those who were insufficiently guarded became more numerous and the name began to be misused, it was transmitted only to the {\i tzenu\u8217'in shebekehuna} \u8212- the particularly modest among the {\i kohanim}. These would weave the name into their melodic recitation during {\i Birkat Kohanim}, blending it into the singing so that the other {\i kohanim} \u8212- who were simultaneously stretching out the four-letter name \u8212- could not discern it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Tarfon testified personally: once, when he accompanied his maternal uncle to the {\i duchan} for {\i Birkat Kohanim}, he tilted his ear toward the {\i kohen gadol} \u8212- or perhaps, given the chronological difficulties with this testimony, toward a senior {\i kohen} \u8212- and overheard him weaving in the twelve-letter name during the melody.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The forty-two letter name \u8212- still more sublime and potent \u8212- was transmitted only under stringent conditions: the recipient must be {\i tzanua} \u8212- modest and trustworthy with secrets \u8212- {\i v\u8217'anav} \u8212- humble \u8212- {\i v\u8217'omed bechatzi yamav} \u8212- having reached at least the midpoint of life (approximately 35, assuming a lifespan of 70) \u8212- {\i v\u8217'eino ko\u8217'es} \u8212- not given to anger \u8212- {\i v\u8217'eino mishtaker} \u8212- not prone to intoxication \u8212- and {\i v\u8217'eino ma\u8217'amid al midotav} \u8212- one who does not press his claims insistently when wronged. The reason for these requirements, as Rashi explains, is that such a name carries enormous power to do damage, and a person who cannot control his temper or his drinking might use it to curse another. The reward for those who know the name and guard it with purity is that they become {\i ahuv lemala} \u8212- beloved above \u8212- {\i venichmad lemata} \u8212- cherished below \u8212- and {\i nochal shenei olamim}, inheriting both this world and the world to come.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Boundaries & Assumpt (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ze\u8217'iri\u8217's Avoidance\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Shmuel said in the name of an elder: {\i Bavel bechezkat ksheira omedet ad sheyivada lecha bameh nifsala} \u8212- Bavel stands in the presumption of being kasher unless you know specifically on what basis a given person became {\i pasul}. By contrast, other lands \u8212- {\i bechezkat pasul hein omdot ad sheyivada lecha bamah nichsharah} \u8212- stand in the presumption of invalidity unless you know how the person became {\i kasher}. Eretz Yisrael occupies a middle position: {\i muchzak lepasul pasul, muchzak lekasher kasher} \u8212- if known to be {\i pasul}, they are {\i pasul}; if known to be {\i kasher}, they are {\i kasher}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This last formulation seems internally contradictory \u8212- it establishes no default. The Gemara asks: {\i hagufei kashya} \u8212- is this not self-contradictory? The statement {\i muchzak lepasul pasul} implies that absent that knowledge the person is {\i kasher}, making {\i kasher} the default; yet {\i muchzak lekasher kasher} implies the opposite. Rav Huna bar Tachalifa resolves this in the name of Rav: {\i kan lehasi\u8217'o isha kan lehotzi isha miyado} \u8212- one clause speaks to permitting him to marry, and the other speaks to compelling a divorce after the fact. Before marriage to someone of distinguished lineage, a man from Eretz Yisrael must establish his {\i yichus}; but once married, only demonstrated {\i psul} can undo it. Rashi and Tosafot both connect this to the specific case of marrying into a family of elevated status, not to ordinary marriages \u8212- a normal person is not required to prove his lineage before marrying.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Yosef adds: {\i kol sheyichaso beVavel masi\u8217'in lo isha} \u8212- anyone whose lineage identifies him as Babylonian, one may marry him off to a woman of good {\i yichus}. However, {\i ve\u8217'idna de\u8217'ika rama\u8217'ei chaishinan} \u8212- now that there are deceivers who can learn to speak Babylonian even without having Babylonian origins, we are suspicious and this linguistic marker can no longer be relied upon.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now relates the story of Ze\u8217'iri, who persistently avoided Rabbi Yochanan \u8212- because Rabbi Yochanan wished to give him his daughter in marriage. Rabbi Yochanan was from Eretz Yisrael; Ze\u8217'iri was from Bavel. One day, they were traveling together and came to a stream. Ze\u8217'iri, as the devoted student, carried Rabbi Yochanan on his shoulders through the water. Rabbi Yochanan, frustrated by the persistent evasion, said to him: our Torah of Eretz Yisrael is good enough for you \u8212- {\i oraitin kesheira} \u8212- but our daughters are not? What is your reasoning? Are you relying on the {\i Mishnah} that teaches that ten categories of lineage made the ascent from Bavel with Ezra \u8212- {\i kohanim}, {\i Levi\u8217'im}, {\i Yisraelim}, and so forth \u8212- and inferring therefrom that Bavel is genealogically purer than Eretz Yisrael?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But one cannot infer this from the {\i Mishnah}, Rabbi Yochanan argues. {\i Atu Kahanei Levi\u8217'ei ve-Yisra\u8217'ei kulu siliku?} Just because {\i psulim} came with Ezra, the {\i Mishnah} also states that {\i kohanim}, {\i levi\u8217'im}, and {\i yisraelim} came with him \u8212- but that does not mean they all left Bavel. There are still {\i kohanim}, {\i levi\u8217'im}, and {\i yisraelim} in Bavel. And correspondingly, {\i psulim} also remain in Bavel. You are no better than we are.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara notes that Rabbi Yochanan had in that moment forgotten the teaching of Rabbi Elazar: {\i lo ala Ezra miBavel ad she\u8217'asah k\u8217'soles nekiyah v\u8217'alah} \u8212- Ezra did not ascend from Bavel until he had made it like refined flour, meaning he specifically removed all the {\i psulim}, even if he did not remove all the legitimate Israelites.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Ulla came to Pumbedita and visited Rav Yehuda. He observed that Rav Yehuda\u8217's son Rabbi Yitzchak was already grown but had not yet married. Rav Yehuda explained: {\i mi yadana me\u8217'heicha yinasiv} \u8212- I do not know where to marry him off, because the question of {\i yichus} is so fraught. Ulla pushed back vigorously: {\i atu anan mi yad\u8217'inan me\u8217'heicha kasina?} \u8212- Do we even know where we ourselves come from? Perhaps we descend from women who were violated during the {\i Churban HaBayis}, as the verse in Lamentations attests: {\i nashim b\u8217'Tzion inu b\u8217'sulos b\u8217'arei Yehuda}. Rav Yehuda responded: but such a union would still produce a {\i kasher} child halachically. Ulla countered: what about other disqualifying unions that occurred among the first Temple generation \u8212- the Gemara cites the verse {\i ha\u8217'shochvim al mitos shen u\u8217'sruchim al arsosam}, which Rabbi Abbahu interprets not as a reference to mere indecency but as describing widespread wife-swapping \u8212- eating and drinking together, pushing beds together, exchanging wives \u8212- {\i u\u8217'masrichim arsoseihem b\u8217'shichvas zera she\u8217'eina shelahem}, and thereby filling their beds with semen that was not theirs. Given such pervasive adultery in the generation of the first Temple, there are certainly {\i mamzeirim} embedded in many Jewish family trees. So how does Rav Yehuda know that anyone is pure enough for his son?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Silence as Yichus\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Yehuda acknowledged the point and asked: so what should I do if I still want to find a suitable match? The answer given is: {\i zil basar shtikusa} \u8212- follow the families that are quiet, that do not argue and pick fights. Rashi explains this as based on the premise \u8212- whether understood genetically or sociologically \u8212- that families of compromised lineage tend toward contentiousness, either as a character trait passed down through their ancestry or as a result of having historically been marginalized by society, cultivating a defensive and argumentative culture.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The people of Eretz Yisrael used a similar method of investigation: {\i ki matzu bei srei bahadi hadadei} \u8212- when two families were in dispute, they would watch to see which one was first willing to be silent, to step back and not press the fight. {\i Hai meyuchas tefei} \u8212- the one more willing to stop fighting has the better {\i yichus}. What begins as an index of {\i yichus} becomes, practically speaking, a direct focus on {\i middos}. The inquiry about lineage has been quietly displaced by an inquiry about character.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav states: {\i shetikutei deBavel hainu yichusa} \u8212- the silence of Bavel is itself its {\i yichus}. The willingness to not fight is not merely a sign of good lineage; it is what the lineage actually means in practice. When challenged that Rav himself went to investigate a certain family, the Gemara clarifies: he did not investigate their genealogical records; he investigated whether they were {\i shtiki} \u8212- non-argumentative. That was his method of verification.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Yehuda said in the name of Rav: {\i im ra\u8217'ita shenei bnei adam shemitgarim zeh ba\u8217'zeh, shemetz pasul yesh be\u8217'echad mehen} \u8212- if you see two people perpetually feuding, there is a scintilla of invalidity in one of them, {\i v\u8217'ein manichin oto lidabek bechavertah} \u8212- and from Heaven, they are made to quarrel so that the families should not intermarry. It is worth noting that this logic works better if the concern is something like a hereditary disease \u8212- both families are carriers and should not intermingle \u8212- than if the concern is purely about lineage status, where the problem would apply equally to any marriage partner. Nevertheless, the principle stands: contentiousness is treated as a sign, if not a symptom, of underlying genealogical trouble.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Bavel Bri\u8217'ah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Pappa savya said in the name of Rav: {\i Bavel bri\u8217'ah} \u8212- Bavel is healthy, meaning its {\i yichus} is sound. {\i Meshan mitah} \u8212- this neighboring region is dead, meaning virtually all its inhabitants are of suspect lineage \u8212- {\i mamzeirim} and the like. {\i Madai mi\u8217'idi cholah} \u8212- Media is sick, meaning mostly {\i kasher} but with some {\i psulim} present. {\i Ve\u8217'Eilam hi goseset} \u8212- Elam is like one in the final stages of dying, meaning mostly {\i pasul} with some {\i kasher} present. The difference between {\i cholah} and {\i goseset} is simply probabilistic: most sick people live, while most of those on their deathbed die.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Geographic Limits\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 East/West Limits\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now turns to the question of the precise geographic boundaries of Bavel \u8212- a necessary question given that Bavel has been established as the gold standard of {\i yichus}. {\i Ad heichan hi Bavel?} Rav says: until the {\i Nahar Azak} on the east. Shmuel says: until the {\i Nahar Yoni}. Rashi identifies this as the discussion of the eastern boundary. The primary territory of Bavel is understood to be bounded by the Tigris and the Euphrates \u8212- {\i bein shenei neharot}, between the two rivers \u8212- but the question here is how far the recognized region extends beyond those two rivers in each cardinal direction.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Northern Limits\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Going north along the Tigris: Rav says until {\i Bagda} and {\i Avna}. Shmuel says until {\i Mushchani} \u8212- but not including {\i Mushchani} itself. Rabbi Chiyya bar Abba then says in the name of Shmuel: {\i Mushchani harei hi keGolah} \u8212- Mushchani is like {\i Golah}. In this context, {\i Golah} is not exile in the pejorative sense but rather a name for Pumbedita, the city most closely associated with purity of {\i yichus}. So Mushchani is treated as having the status of Pumbedita \u8212- genuine Babylonian {\i yichus}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Euphrates Limits\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Going north along the Euphrates: Rav says until {\i Akra deTulbanki}. Shmuel says until the bridge of {\i Perat}. Rabbi Yochanan says until the crossing of {\i Gizma}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye, and some say Rav Yosef, cursed Rav\u8217's position for extending the boundary too far north. One might ask: why curse Rav and not Shmuel, whose position went even farther north? The answer given is that the curse applied to Rav {\i and certainly} to Shmuel \u8212- the curser was criticizing those who extended the valid boundary too far, and Shmuel\u8217's more expansive position was included by implication.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Alternatively, there is no contradiction at all: when Shmuel made his ruling, the bridge of {\i Perat} was actually located further south than its current position. The Persians subsequently moved it northward. {\i Ha\u8217'idna hu de\u8217'illyuyei Parsa\u8217'ei} \u8212- it is only now that the Persians have situated it further north, but Shmuel\u8217's original designation referred to the lower location, which was actually less expansive than Rav\u8217's.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye asked Rav Yosef: what about the western side of the Euphrates \u8212- how far east into that territory does the Bavel zone extend? Rav Yosef understood the question to be about the town of {\i Biram}, located to the east of the {\i Perat}. He answered: {\i meyuchasei dePumbedita miBiram nasvei} \u8212- people of distinguished {\i yichus} from Pumbedita used to marry women from {\i Biram}. So {\i Biram} is fully within the acceptable zone.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Papa states: {\i kemachloket leyuchasin kach machloket legittin} \u8212- the same disagreement that governs the parameters of Bavel for purposes of {\i yuchasin} also governs the parameters of Bavel for {\i gittin}, specifically regarding whether a messenger delivering a {\i get} from Bavel to Eretz Yisrael must declare {\i bifanai nechtam} \u8212- it was signed in my presence. Since Bavel was as conversant in {\i halacha} as Eretz Yisrael, this declaration was unnecessary within those borders.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Yosef disagrees: the debate applies only to {\i yuchasin}. For {\i gittin}, everyone agrees the boundary is the second lake of the bridge \u8212- {\i Arva Tinyana deGishra} \u8212- a known, specific location.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chavil Yama\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rami bar Abba teaches: {\i Chavil Yama techla deBavel} \u8212- {\i Chavil Yama} is the {\i techeiles}, the finest quality, of Bavel. And within {\i Chavil Yama}, the cities of {\i Shunya} and {\i Guvya} are the cr\u232 ?me de la cr\u232 ?me. Ravina adds: even {\i Tzitzura} is included. A {\i baraita} corroborates: Chanan ben Pinchas said the same \u8212- {\i Chavil Yama techla deBavel}, and {\i Shunya, Guvya, and Tzitzura} are the finest within it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Papa then introduces a contemporary qualification: {\i ve\u8217'ha\u8217'idna arevu behu Kutai} \u8212- in his time, {\i Kutim} \u8212- Samaritans \u8212- had intermingled there, diminishing its {\i yichus}. The Gemara immediately challenges this: {\i velo hi} \u8212- it is not so. The Gemara explains that a certain individual wanted to marry a woman from that region and they refused him, and he then began raising the claim of Samaritan infiltration. Rashi notes that it is very difficult to attribute to Rav Papa the {\i motzi la\u8217'az} of slandering an entire community out of pique at a personal rejection. Rashi therefore prefers the reading that it was a {\i Kuti} who sought to marry there and was refused, and the episode led to some concern about Samaritan presence \u8212- but not substantiated enough to genuinely compromise the {\i yichus} of {\i Chavil Yama}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shot Mishat\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara concludes with the location of {\i Chavil Yama} itself: {\i Zu Prat DeBorsi} \u8212- it is the region of {\i Prat DeBorsi}. A man who declared himself to be from {\i Shot Mishat} prompted Rav Yitzchak Nafcha to clarify: {\i agra d\u8217'amshat Shot Mishat bein hanaharot omedet} \u8212- the advantage of {\i Shot Mishat} is that it stands between the rivers, between the Tigris and the Euphrates, and is therefore classic Bavel with good {\i yichus}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then asks: what exactly constitutes {\i bein hanaharot} \u8212- between the rivers? Abaye said in the name of Rav Chama bar Ukva, who said in the name of Rabbi Yose in the name of Rabbi Chanina: {\i bein hanaharot kegolah leyuchsin} \u8212- being between the rivers is like {\i Golah}, like Pumbedita, for purposes of {\i yuchasin}. Any place between the Tigris and the Euphrates has this elevated status.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yochanan specifies: the region of {\i bein hanaharot} begins from {\i Dikra} going northward, and extends until the crossing of {\i Gizma}. But Abaye adds: {\i retzu\u8217'ah nafka} \u8212- there is a strip that extends beyond, reaching further north and including {\i Shot Mishat} within the zone of {\i bein hanaharot}. Even though {\i Shot Mishat} lies north of {\i Dikra}, the strip extension brings it within the territory of Bavel and grants it the status of {\i bein hanaharot} for purposes of {\i yuchasin}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Kiddushin 72\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Chalazone Nihavnad\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Ika\u8217's Claim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The sugya opens with a statement transmitted through a chain of authorities: {\i amar Rav Ika bar Avin amar Rav Chananel amar Rav} \u8212- Chalazone Nihavnad, a city located in Iran that exists to this day, has the status of {\i gola} with respect to {\i yuchsin}. That is, it enjoys the same presumption of genealogical purity as Pumbedita, the paradigmatic {\i meyuchas} community of Babylonian Jewry.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Before proceeding, it is worth recalling the broader context in which this statement appears. The preceding dappim engaged at length with the question of {\i yuchsin} \u8212- genealogical fitness for marriage \u8212- examining which lands could be relied upon, which families could not, and how far the concern for {\i yichus} could be pressed in practice. Bavel was identified as superior to {\i chutz la\u8217'aretz}, while Eretz Yisrael occupied an intermediate position. Yet the {\i Gemara} simultaneously pushed back against the entire enterprise, arguing that once a family has become intermingled with {\i psulim}, there is no way to identify it, and one should neither ask nor tell. Even the role of Eliyahu HaNavi at the end of days was reinterpreted: rather than exposing families of problematic lineage that have already been absorbed into the community, he will address only those cases that remain conspicuously and publicly unresolved. In other words, the {\i Gemara} has repeatedly and powerfully challenged the practical relevance of {\i yichus} as a halakhic category \u8212- even while nominally maintaining the structure of the {\i Mishnah\u8217's} concern. Against that backdrop, the claim that a specific Iranian city qualifies as {\i meyuchas} like {\i gola} is itself notable, and the {\i Gemara} immediately scrutinizes it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Abaye\u8217's Challenge\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Amar lei Abaye: lo tetzitzu lei} \u8212- do not rely on what Rav Ika bar Avin said. The reason: {\i yevama hi d\u8217'nafla lei hatam} \u8212- he had a {\i yevama}, a sister-in-law whom he was obligated to marry, living in that very city. His claim that Chalazone Nihavnad is {\i meyuchas} is therefore self-interested and cannot be trusted.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This picks up a theme the {\i Gemara} developed previously, in which Rav Pappa made a negative claim about a certain place \u8212- asserting it had Samaritans intermingled within it \u8212- and the {\i Gemara} suggested this was motivated by the fact that he had been refused permission to marry a woman from there, making it look more like slander born of resentment than an objective report. {\i Tosafot} notes that it was not actually Rav Pappa himself but someone else, yet the pattern is clear. In that earlier case, one could not trust a negative claim about a place\u8217's {\i yichus} because the claimant had a personal grievance. Here, conversely, one cannot trust a positive claim because the claimant stands to benefit from it. Taken together, these cases leave the reliability of any testimony about a community\u8217's {\i yichus} deeply in question \u8212- whether the claim is favorable or unfavorable, some personal motive may be driving it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might ask: even if Rav Ika had a personal interest, he was transmitting in the name of Rav Chananel, who transmitted in the name of Rav \u8212- surely those earlier authorities had no such interest? The {\i Gemara} addresses this directly. Rav Ika responded: {\i atu didi?} \u8212- are you accusing me of fabricating this? I said it in the name of Rav Chananel, and Rav Chananel has no {\i yevama} there; he was not {\i nogeia ba-davar}. So they went and asked Rav Chananel directly. Rav Chananel replied: {\i hachi amar Rav} \u8212- this is indeed what Rav said, that Chalazone Nihavnad is like {\i gola} for {\i yuchsin}. The chain thus traces back to Rav himself \u8212- but it now emerges that Rav was the one who had the {\i yevama} there. The very source of the tradition is compromised by a personal interest.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The episode is a striking illustration of how the {\i Gemara} refuses to allow {\i yichus} claims to pass unchallenged. Every positive or negative assessment of a community\u8217's genealogical standing is potentially tainted by the claimant\u8217's circumstances.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ten Tribes Connection\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i U-pligi d\u8217'Rav Abba bar Kahana} \u8212- this disputes the position of Rav Abba bar Kahana, who independently addressed the question of Chalazone Nihavnad by grounding it in a biblical text. Rav Abba bar Kahana cited the verse describing where the Ten Tribes were settled after the Assyrian exile under Sancherev: {\i va-yanchim ba-Chalach u-va-Chavor nehar Gozan ve-arei Madai} \u8212- and he settled them in Chalach, Chavor, the river of Gozan, and the cities of Madai. He then identified each toponym: {\i Chalach zo Chalazone} \u8212- Chalach is Chalazone; {\i Chavor zo Hadyav} \u8212- Chavor is Hadyav; {\i nehar Gozan ze Ginzak} \u8212- the river Gozan is Ginzak; and {\i arei Madai ze Chemdan ve-chaveroteha} \u8212- the cities of Madai are Chemdan and its neighboring cities. An alternative tradition identifies the last location not as Chemdan but as {\i Nahavnad ve-chaveroteha} \u8212- Nihavnad and its neighbors.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The implication is significant. Chalazone and Nihavnad \u8212- the very city whose {\i meyuchas} status was being claimed \u8212- are identified as places where the Ten Tribes were exiled and settled. Since the Ten Tribes are understood to have intermingled with the surrounding populations and lost their distinct Jewish identity, these locations cannot be presumed genealogically pure. On the contrary, according to Rav Abba bar Kahana, they are precisely the kind of places one should view with suspicion regarding {\i yuchsin}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} then asks about the neighboring cities of Nihavnad mentioned by Shmuel: {\i krach Moshchi, Chutzki, ve-Rumki}. Rabbi Yochanan adds: {\i ve-kulam lipsol} \u8212- all of these are cities problematic for {\i yuchsin}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Border Cities\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} now introduces a further complication from the book of Daniel. The verse {\i ve-tlat alin be-pumeih bein shinaiha} \u8212- three leaves were in its mouth, between its teeth \u8212- is expounded by Rabbi Yochanan: {\i zu Chalazone, Hadyav, ve-Natzivin} \u8212- these are the three cities, Chalazone, Hadyav, and Natzivin. {\i She-pe\u8217'amim bole\u8217'an u-fe\u8217'amim poltetan} \u8212- sometimes they are swallowed up by the Persian Empire and sometimes they are expelled from it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The image is vivid: these cities sit on the border, sometimes absorbed within the empire\u8217's control and sometimes outside it, perpetually shifting between jurisdictions, like territory that alternates between two powers. The analogy to border regions that have historically moved between empires \u8212- absorbed one year, expelled the next \u8212- makes the point concrete. Their unstable political status is directly connected to their uncertain {\i yuchsin} status: when a community\u8217's identity and boundaries are permanently in flux, its genealogical records cannot be relied upon.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} then proceeds with the vision of the bear-like beast, identified by a {\i baraita} as referring to the Persians \u8212- {\i she-ochlim ve-shotim ke-dov, u-muse\u8217'arim ke-dov, ve-ein lahem menuchah ke-dov} \u8212- who eat and drink like a bear, are as hairy as a bear, and have no rest like a bear. Rabbi Ami, when he would see a Persian riding, would say: there goes a traveling bear. It is worth noting \u8212- as the scholar Yaakov Elman has observed \u8212- that this particular set of pejoratives (hairy, fat, bear-like) was itself a Persian slur directed against others; the {\i Gemara} here applies it back against the Persians themselves, an ironic inversion captured by the principle {\i kol ha-posel b\u8217'mumo posel}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav asked Levi to describe the Persians through a {\i mashal}. Levi said they resemble the troops of the house of David \u8212- meaning they are valorous and powerful. The Chaverin, a nation that came to dominate parts of the region after the Persians and proved far more oppressive toward Jews, are like {\i malachei chabalah} \u8212- angels of destruction. The Ishmaelites are compared to {\i se\u8217'irim shel beit ha-kisei} \u8212- the demons of the outhouse. And the Torah scholars of Bavel? They are like the ministering angels \u8212- {\i malachei ha-shareit} \u8212- dressed in white, wrapped in their garments, their appearance distinguished and luminous. Rashi elaborates, citing other {\i Gemarot} that compare the {\i talmidei chachamim} of Bavel to the vision in Yechezkel of the angel {\i ish lavush badim}, clothed in linen \u8212- because they too would sit wrapped in {\i sedinim ha-metzuyatzin}, fringed linen garments, like heavenly figures.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rebbi\u8217's Prophecy\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Humanya\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Ki hava nicha nafshia d\u8217'Rabbi amar} \u8212- as Rebbi lay dying, he was moved to prophetic utterance, and this is what he declared. The first statement: {\i Humanya ika b\u8217'Bavel kulah Amonai} \u8212- there is a place in Bavel called Humanya, and it is entirely populated by Ammonites. The halakhic relevance is implicit: the prohibition of {\i lo yavo Amoni} applies to the male \u8212- {\i Ammoni v\u8217'lo Ammonit} \u8212- so the concern, while not all-encompassing, is nonetheless a real {\i yuchsin} issue.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 That Rebbi chose to devote his final moments to enumerating these genealogical problems is itself striking. Rebbi was the great champion of Babylonian Jewry\u8217's honor \u8212- it was he who resisted the claim that Bavel was inferior to Eretz Yisrael in terms of {\i yichus}. And yet here, on his deathbed, he is cataloguing problems specifically in Bavel. The answer may be that precisely because he otherwise defended Bavel\u8217's honor, this transmission had special weight \u8212- he was not slandering a community out of bias, but passing down information he felt obligated to disclose at the end of his life, information that might otherwise be lost.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Misgarya\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Misgarya ika b\u8217'Bavel kulah d\u8217'mamzeira hi} \u8212- there is a place in Bavel called Misgarya that is entirely populated by {\i mamzerim}. Again, the focus is on Bavel specifically \u8212- the very land whose genealogical standing Rebbi had defended throughout his life.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Birka\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Birka ika b\u8217'Bavel, shenei achim yesh sham ha-machlifim neshotehem zeh la-zeh} \u8212- there is a place called Birka in Bavel, where two brothers live who swap their wives with one another. The consequence is that all children born of these unions are {\i mamzerim}, since a married woman who cohabits with another man produces illegitimate offspring. Rebbi identifies Birka as a community bearing this taint.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Birta deSatya\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Birta deSatya ika b\u8217'Bavel, hayom saru me-acharei ha-Makom} \u8212- there is a place in Bavel called Birta deSatya, meaning something like the city that strays, and Rebbi declares: today they strayed from God. What occurred? {\i De-akfei peira be-khifrei be-Shabta} \u8212- fish rose to the surface of the pond on {\i Shabbat}, and the townspeople could not resist the opportunity: {\i va-azlu ve-tzadu lehu be-Shabta} \u8212- they went and caught the fish on {\i Shabbat}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The obvious question arises: why does Rebbi include this? The other items in his deathbed disclosure concern permanent {\i yuchsin} problems \u8212- communities with {\i mamzerim} or {\i Ammonites} intermingled. A single {\i Shabbat} violation, whatever its gravity, would not seem to leave a lasting genealogical stain. What is the relevance?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} continues: {\i ve-shamteinun Rav Akhi berei de-Rabbi Yoshiya} \u8212- Rav Akhi the son of Rabbi Yoshiya put them in {\i cherem} on account of what they had done. But the consequence of this severe communal excommunication was {\i ve-ishtamad} \u8212- they all abandoned the faith. The lesson embedded here is itself a warning: a {\i cherem} applied to an entire city, based on what may have been prophetic knowledge rather than direct halakhic adjudication, can be counterproductive to the point of catastrophe. The {\i Gemara} records the outcome in a single phrase, without further editorial comment, leaving the weight of the result to speak for itself.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Akra de-Agma\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Akra de-Agma ika b\u8217'Bavel, Adda bar Ahava yesh bah} \u8212- there is a place in Bavel called Akra de-Agma, and in it lives Adda bar Ahava. {\i Hayom yoshev be-cheiko shel Avraham} \u8212- today he sits in the lap of Avraham. Rashi suggests this might mean he has died and ascended to the world of the righteous, but notes the difficulty that Rav Adda bar Ahava was a much later figure than Rebbi. An alternative reading offered by Rashi is that today was his {\i bris milah} \u8212- he was just born, and is thus literally in the embrace of Avraham, as the sandek holds the infant. {\i Tosafot} proposes that there may have been two figures with the same name. The precise resolution remains uncertain, but the significance of the mention is clear: Rebbi wanted to note that in this place, a figure of great spiritual stature was present.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Succession of Tzadikim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Hayom nolad Rav Yehuda b\u8217'Bavel} \u8212- today, Rav Yehuda was born in Bavel. This statement becomes the pivot for a teaching about the continuity of {\i tzadikim}: {\i keshe-mes Rabbi Akiva nolad Rebbe} \u8212- when Rabbi Akiva died, Rebbi was born; {\i keshe-mes Rebbe nolad Rav Yehuda} \u8212- when Rebbi died, Rav Yehuda was born; {\i keshe-mes Rav Yehuda nolad Rava} \u8212- when Rav Yehuda died, Rava was born; {\i keshe-mes Rava nolad Rav Ashi} \u8212- when Rava died, Rav Ashi was born. The {\i Rambam\u8217's} text had the formulation {\i ad she-lo mes Rava nolad Rav Ashi} \u8212- before Rava died, Rav Ashi was already born \u8212- suggesting the succession is not merely sequential but overlapping.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Le-lamedakh she-ein tzadik niftar min ha-olam ad she-nivra tzadik kamosohu} \u8212- to teach you that a righteous person does not depart from this world until another like him has already been created to take his place. The prooftext is {\i ve-zarach ha-shemesh u-va ha-shemesh} \u8212- the sun rises and the sun sets \u8212- read as a statement that before one sun sets, the next has already risen. This is supported by the story of Eli and Shmuel: {\i ad she-lo kavsah shimsho shel Eli zarchah shimsho shel Shmuel ha-Ramati} \u8212- before Eli\u8217's light was extinguished, Shmuel\u8217's had already begun to shine, as indicated by the verse {\i ve-ner Elokim terem yichbeh u-Shmuel shochev} \u8212- the lamp of God had not yet gone out when Shmuel was lying in the sanctuary, already receiving his prophetic calling.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might pause over the choice of the word {\i tzadik}. The chain being traced is not merely a chain of righteous individuals in a general sense, but of generational halakhic leadership \u8212- great {\i rabbinic} figures whose absence would leave a community without guidance. The proof from Eli and Shmuel is instructive in this regard, as Shmuel was Eli\u8217's own student. The verse speaks to an overlap, not merely a succession, and the concern is that no generation be left without its guiding light.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} then cites: {\i tziva Hashem l\u8217'Yaakov sevivo tzarav} \u8212- God has commanded that Yaakov\u8217's oppressors always surround him. Rabbi Yehuda explains: {\i kegon Humanya l\u8217'pum Nahara} \u8212- just as Humanya surrounds Pum Nahara, a place inhabited by Jews, closing the circle back to Rebbi\u8217's opening statement about Humanya and its Ammonite population.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Pelatya ben Benaya\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Good Version\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} cites a verse from Yechezkel: {\i va-yehi k\u8217'hinavi u-Pelatya ben Benaya met} \u8212- and it was as I prophesied that Pelatya ben Benaya died, and I fell upon my face and cried out with a great voice, saying, \u8220"Ah, Lord God!\u8221" This is interpreted as a dispute between Rav and Shmuel. One says the prophet\u8217's outcry was {\i l\u8217'tova} \u8212- mourning the death of a righteous man. The other says it was {\i l\u8217'ra\u8217'ah} \u8212- distress that an evil man died too peacefully.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The positive interpretation unfolds as follows. The governor of Mishan \u8212- and it is worth noting that Mishan is one of the places previously identified as having problematic {\i yuchsin} \u8212- was the son-in-law of Nevuchadnetzar. After Nevuchadnetzar destroyed the {\i Beis HaMikdash} and took the Jews captive, the governor sent a message demanding his share of the captives: {\i mikulei hai shivya d\u8217'ayeisis lach reisha d\u8217'varta lan} \u8212- from all those captives you brought, give us the leadership of the settlement, our portion. Nevuchadnetzar wanted to comply and send Jewish captives to Mishan. Pelatya ben Benaya intervened and said to Nevuchadnetzar: {\i anan d\u8217'chashvinan neiku mikameich hacha} \u8212- we Israelites, who are important, should remain here with you, the great king; {\i v\u8217'avdin neizlu l\u8217'hasam} \u8212- let our slaves go there. By this maneuver, he ensured that the Jewish captives stayed in Bavel rather than being sent to Mishan.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Why was this a kindness? Rashi explains it on two grounds. First, Bavel afforded the Jews far better conditions than Mishan would have. Second, it is generally worse to be a servant of a servant \u8212- {\i avadim l\u8217'avadim} \u8212- subject to someone who is himself subordinate to a greater power, than to be in the service of the ultimate sovereign. The prophet\u8217's anguished cry was therefore a lament: {\i mi she-asa tova b\u8217'Yisrael yamut b\u8217'chatzi yamav?} \u8212- someone who did such good for Israel should die in the prime of his life?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Bad Version\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The negative interpretation draws on a different passage in Yechezkel. The prophet is brought to the eastern gate of the {\i Beis HaMikdash}, where he sees twenty-five men, and among them he identifies {\i Yaazanya ben Azur ve-Pelatya ben Benaya, sarei ha-am} \u8212- officers of the nation. He is then brought to the inner courtyard, where again there are approximately twenty-five men \u8212- {\i achoreihem el heichal Hashem u-fneihem kedma} \u8212- their backs were toward the sanctuary and their faces toward the east.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} asks: when the verse says their faces were toward the east, is it not obvious that their backs were toward the west? What is the need to specify {\i achoreihem el heichal Hashem}? {\i Melamed she-hayu mafri\u8217'in atzman u-mitrizin atzman klapei ma\u8217'alah} \u8212- this teaches that they would expose and relieve themselves in a manner directed toward God, as an act of deliberate desecration and contempt toward the {\i Heichal}. The prophet\u8217's outcry of anguish was therefore not a lament but rather an expression of outrage: {\i mi she-asa ra\u8217'ah zot b\u8217'Yisrael yamut al mitato?} \u8212- someone who committed such desecration against Israel should die peacefully in his bed?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shmuel\u8217's Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tistayem b\u8217'Shmuel d\u8217'amar l\u8217'ra\u8217'ah} \u8212- the {\i Gemara} proposes to identify Shmuel as the one who holds the negative version. The reasoning: {\i d\u8217'amar Rabbi Chiya bar Avin amar Shmuel, Moshchani harei hi k\u8217'gola l\u8217'yuchsin} \u8212- Shmuel says Moshchani is like {\i gola} for {\i yuchsin}. And separately: {\i Mishan lo chashu lah, lo mishum avdut v\u8217'lo mishum mamzerut, ela kohanim she-hayu bah lo hikpidu al gerushot} \u8212- regarding Mishan, there is no concern for slave status or {\i mamzerut}; the only issue is that the {\i kohanim} there did not guard themselves from marrying divorced women, creating {\i chalalim}. This is exclusively a {\i kehuna} concern, not a general {\i yuchsin} problem.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Now, if according to the positive version, what was sent to Mishan were slaves \u8212- not Jews \u8212- and Shmuel holds that there are no slave-status concerns in Mishan, this would seem to be no problem at all. But the {\i Gemara} notes that Shmuel has a principle: {\i ha-mafkir avdo yatza l\u8217'cheirut v\u8217'eino tzarich get shichrur} \u8212- one who renounces ownership of his slave, the slave goes free without requiring a formal writ of manumission. If the Jewish masters were taken captive and their slaves along with them, the masters\u8217' despair of ever regaining their slaves constitutes an implicit renunciation of ownership. {\i She-ne\u8217'emar kol eved ish miknat kasef} \u8212- the verse says \u8220"slave of a man,\u8221" meaning only when the {\i ish} maintains his ownership is the person considered a slave; once effective ownership lapses, the slave is freed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} therefore concludes: {\i l\u8217'olam eima lach Shmuel amar l\u8217'tova} \u8212- you can maintain that Shmuel holds the positive version after all. Even according to Shmuel, slaves were sent to Mishan; and even according to Shmuel, those individuals ceased to be slaves once ownership was effectively abandoned. Therefore Shmuel\u8217's statement that Mishan has no slave-status concern is entirely consistent with the positive reading of Pelatya\u8217's intervention.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This resolution carries an implicit but striking observation about the scope of the {\i yuchsin} concern. The {\i Gemara} is essentially saying that even freed slaves \u8212- individuals who, in the previous chapter of the {\i Mishnah}, are ranked lower than {\i yisrael} in the hierarchy of marriageability \u8212- are treated as having no meaningful {\i yuchsin} issue once they are technically free. The concern for {\i yichus} is thus narrowly halakhic, not a broader social or cultural judgment about genealogical quality.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rabbi Meir vs.\u160 ?Chachamim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Meir\u8217's Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Amar Rav Yehuda amar Shmuel: zu divrei Rabbi Meir} \u8212- Rav Yehuda said in the name of Shmuel: the entire framework we have been working with \u8212- the graduated hierarchy of Bavel as superior to {\i chutz la\u8217'aretz} for {\i yuchsin}, with all its distinctions and concerns \u8212- represents only the position of Rabbi Meir. It is not the consensus view.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is a remarkable statement. Everything the {\i Gemara} has developed across the preceding dappim about the superiority of Bavel, the problems of other lands, and the careful distinctions between communities \u8212- all of it, according to this formulation, reflects the singular opinion of one Tanna. And this statement comes, pointedly, from Rav Yehuda in the name of Shmuel \u8212- the very authority who elsewhere was responsible for many of the most pointed warnings about {\i yuchsin} problems in specific places.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chachamim\u8217's View\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Ve-chachamim omrim kol ha\u8217'aratzot b\u8217'chezkas kashrus hein omdot} \u8212- the Chachamim say: all lands stand under a presumption of genealogical fitness. This is a sweeping counter-position. Rather than elaborate hierarchies of {\i yuchsin} quality between different lands, the Chachamim apply the standard principle of {\i chazakah} \u8212- the ordinary legal presumption that things are as they appear and as they have been \u8212- to the question of lineage. Why should a person\u8217's fitness for marriage be treated differently from any other matter of personal status, where we simply follow the {\i chazakah}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Amemar vs.\u160 ?Rav Ashi\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Amemar sharei lei l\u8217'Rav Huna bar Nasan l\u8217'minsav itesa mechoza\u8217'ah} \u8212- Ameimar permitted Rav Huna bar Nasan to marry a woman from Chozaya, a place outside of Bavel and therefore in {\i chutz la\u8217'aretz}. Rav Ashi challenged him: {\i mah da\u8217'atach?} \u8212- on what basis? You are relying on the statement of Rav Yehuda in the name of Shmuel that this whole framework is only Rabbi Meir\u8217's view. But the houses of Rav Kahana, Rav Pappa, and Rav Zevid did not transmit the tradition in these terms \u8212- they did not frame it as a dispute between Rabbi Meir and the Chachamim, but as a settled and unanimous concern.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Version Disputes\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Despite Rav Ashi\u8217's objection, Ameimar did not accept it. The {\i Gemara} records: {\i afilu hachi lo kibel minei} \u8212- even so, he did not accept from him, {\i mishum d\u8217'shmi\u8217'a lei me-Rav Zevid d\u8217'Naharda\u8217'ah} \u8212- because he had heard it from Rav Zevid of Nehardea, a different Rav Zevid from the one cited earlier, that this framework of {\i yuchsin} concerns was indeed only Rabbi Meir\u8217's position.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The competing attributions here are themselves revealing. The same essential dispute \u8212- whether concern for {\i yuchsin} across lands is a universal position or merely Rabbi Meir\u8217's minority view \u8212- is being transmitted differently by different {\i batei midrash}. The houses of Rav Kahana, Rav Pappa, and Rav Zevid (one version) did not transmit the framing as a {\i machloket}; Rav Zevid of Nehardea (another version) did. This mirrors a broader pattern in the {\i Gemara\u8217's} treatment of {\i yuchsin} throughout these pages: even on the question of how much to worry about {\i yuchsin}, there is irresolvable uncertainty about what anyone actually held.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is also worth noting the internal tension in attributing the limiting formulation \u8212- \u8220"this is only Rabbi Meir\u8221" \u8212- to Rav Yehuda amar Shmuel. Throughout the preceding discussion, it has consistently been Rav Yehuda amar Shmuel who transmitted the most pointed statements about {\i yuchsin} problems: this place is filled with {\i mamzerim}, that place is problematic, and so on. Yet here it is the same authority who provides the most powerful tool for dismissing the entire apparatus \u8212- it is only Rabbi Meir. One possible resolution is that {\i la matni hachi} \u8212- the tradition that this is a dispute of Rabbi Meir versus Chachamim \u8212- was itself contested, and different {\i amoraim} disputed whether Rav Yehuda amar Shmuel actually said it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One further observation the {\i Gemara} invites: is Rabbi Meir\u8217's concern for {\i yuchsin} consistent with his general halakhic disposition of {\i chaish l\u8217'miyuta} \u8212- being attentive to small minorities and minority possibilities? If so, the Chachamim\u8217's counter-position \u8212- that everyone has a {\i chezkas kashrus} \u8212- represents simply the standard application of {\i chazakah}, without any heightened vigilance toward the remote possibility of genealogical disqualification.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Future Purification\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Yosi\u8217's View\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tanu Rabbanan: mamzerei ve-netinei tehorim le\u8217'atid lavo, divrei Rabbi Yosi} \u8212- our Rabbis taught: {\i mamzerim} and {\i netinim} will become pure in the future, in the words of Rabbi Yosi. This is a significant claim about the eschatological resolution of personal status issues. {\i Netinim} \u8212- descendants of the Gibeonites who were made Temple servants by David \u8212- are disqualified from full membership in the Jewish community, their status a product of circumstances rather than of any sin. And {\i mamzerim}, however they came to be, bear their status indefinitely. Rabbi Yosi maintains that in the future, both will be purified.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The prooftext is Yechezkel\u8217's vision of the messianic era: {\i ve-zarakti aleichem mayim tehorim u-tehartem} \u8212- I will sprinkle pure water upon you and you shall become pure. If God is going to purify Israel from all impurity in the future, that purification must include the removal of these personal status stigmas as well.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Meir\u8217's Response\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Meir disagrees. {\i Keshe-hu omer mi-kol tumoteichem u-mi-kol gilluleichem} \u8212- when the verse says \u8220"from all your impurities and from all your idolatries,\u8221" that specifies the source of the purification: sin and ritual defilement. {\i Ve-lo min ha-mamzerut} \u8212- but not from personal status issues. The word {\i tumah} refers to moral and ritual contamination; the status of {\i mamzer} is a halakhic category of personal identity, not a species of ritual impurity, and the verse does not promise its erasure.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yosi responds: {\i keshe-hu omer ateher etchem} \u8212- but then the verse continues with the separate phrase \u8220"I will purify you,\u8221" which is a second, broader statement of purification. {\i Hevei omer af min ha-mamzerut} \u8212- this additional formulation includes even the removal of {\i mamzerut}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Bishlama le-Rabbi Meir}, the verse {\i ve-yashav mamzer be-Ashdod} is well-understood \u8212- in the future, {\i mamzerim} will be settled in Ashdod, meaning they will be identified and separated, retaining their distinct status even in messianic times. But according to Rabbi Yosi, {\i mai ve-yashav mamzer be-Ashdod?} Rav Yosef offers a {\i targum}: {\i ve-yateivun beit Yisrael le-ruchatzan be-arahon, de-havu damu beh le-nuchrain} \u8212- the house of Israel will dwell in security in their own land, they who had been like strangers. The {\i mamzer} here is a metaphor for Israel\u8217's own experience of exile and marginalization \u8212- the nation that was itself like a rejected outsider, dwelling in a land not its own, will now return and settle securely. The implication is that just as Israel\u8217's status as a stranger is reversed in the messianic era, so too is the {\i mamzer\u8217's} status of exclusion reversed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 There is something poignant in this reading. The {\i Gemara} is drawing on the profound parallel between the {\i mamzer\u8217's} condition \u8212- excluded, marginalized, unable to marry fully into the community \u8212- and the Jewish people\u8217's condition in exile. Both are described by the same word, and both find their resolution in the same future moment.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Future Purification (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yechezkel\u8217's Vision\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The previous discussion of Yechezkel\u8217's prophecy continues. Rabbi Yosi\u8217's reading of {\i ve-yashav mamzer be-Ashdod}, rendered by Rav Yosef\u8217's {\i targum} as speaking of Israel\u8217's homecoming and security after generations of displacement, frames the entire messianic purification as encompassing the reversal of every form of exclusion \u8212- including the status of {\i mamzer}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Halacha K\u8217'Rabbi Yosi\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Amar Rav Yehuda amar Shmuel: halacha k\u8217'Rabbi Yosi} \u8212- Rav Yehuda in the name of Shmuel rules that the {\i halacha} follows Rabbi Yosi. Again this is Rav Yehuda amar Shmuel \u8212- the same voice that previously said the entire {\i yuchsin} framework is only Rabbi Meir\u8217's view, and the same voice behind so much of the {\i yuchsin} discussion across these pages. The package is consistent: we are not particularly concerned about {\i yuchsin} in the present, and in the future it will all be set right.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The ruling is {\i halacha k\u8217'Rabbi Yosi} even though the subject matter is eschatological \u8212- a statement about what will be true {\i le\u8217'atid lavo}. The use of the term {\i halacha} in this context is itself striking.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Yosef\u8217's Statement\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Amar Rav Yosef: ilmale d\u8217'amar Rav Yehuda amar Shmuel halacha k\u8217'Rabbi Yosi, hava ati Eliyahu u-mafik minnan tzvarne tzvarne kolarin} \u8212- Rav Yosef said: were it not for the fact that Rav Yehuda in the name of Shmuel ruled that the {\i halacha} follows Rabbi Yosi, Eliyahu would have come and extracted from us, group by group, great numbers of people \u8212- identifying the {\i psulim} among us and expelling them from the community.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is a breathtaking statement. The ruling {\i halacha k\u8217'Rabbi Yosi} is understood not merely as a practical legal decision about the present, but as a decision that effectively reshapes what Eliyahu will do when he comes. Before this ruling, the plan \u8212- as one reading of the verses suggested \u8212- was that Eliyahu would come and expose all the hidden {\i psulim}, performing a massive rectification through exclusion. By ruling {\i halacha k\u8217'Rabbi Yosi}, the tradition has changed the trajectory: the future rectification will be one of inclusion and purification, not identification and expulsion.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is reminiscent of the principle {\i lo bashamayim hi} \u8212- the Torah is not in heaven \u8212- but even more radical, because here the {\i halacha} is not merely overriding a heavenly voice in a present dispute, but actively determining what the heavenly plan will look like in the future. As one participant noted, this is an extraordinary use of the word {\i halacha} in the context of a question about messianic times \u8212- and the audacity of it is clearly the point.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Marriage Status Rules\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ger and Mamzeret\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having engaged in an extended multi-layered dismantling of the practical concern for {\i yuchsin} \u8212- through arguments about unknowability, through limiting it to Rabbi Meir\u8217's minority position, through appeal to future purification \u8212- the {\i Gemara} now pivots decisively to concrete, specific {\i halachot} about marriage between identified individuals of known status. The previous discussion has effectively cleared the ground; now we address what the rules actually are when the relevant facts are known.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tanu Rabbanan: ger nosei mamzeret, divrei Rabbi Yosi} \u8212- a {\i ger} may marry a {\i mamzeret}, in the words of Rabbi Yosi. {\i Rabbi Yehuda omer: ger lo yisa mamzeret} \u8212- Rabbi Yehuda says a {\i ger} may not marry a {\i mamzeret}. The pivot of the dispute is the verse {\i lo yavo mamzer bi-khal Hashem} \u8212- a {\i mamzer} may not enter the assembly of God. Does that prohibition encompass {\i gerim} as well? Is a {\i ger} included in the {\i kahal} that a {\i mamzer} cannot enter?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chamisha Kahale\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Gemara} grounds the dispute in a {\i derashah} on the word {\i kahal} and its multiple occurrences in the Torah. {\i Chamisha kahale ketivi} \u8212- the word {\i kahal} appears five times in relevant contexts. Rashi enumerates: {\i lo yavo mamzer bi-khal Hashem} (once); {\i lo yavo lo bi-khal Hashem} (again, emphasizing the double exclusion); {\i lo yavo Amoni u-Moavi bi-khal Hashem} (concerning Ammonites and Moabites); and further instances. Each use of {\i kahal} is taken to include an additional category of Jews within the prohibited assembly: one {\i kahal} for {\i kohanim}, one for {\i leviyim}, and one for {\i yisraelim} \u8212- establishing that the prohibition on a {\i mamzer} marrying into the {\i kahal} applies not only to the inner priestly circles but to the full range of Jewish lineage.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Vadai vs.\u160 ?Safek\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The remaining two uses of the word {\i kahal} teach that: {\i v\u8217'chad l\u8217'mishri mamzer b\u8217'shtuki} \u8212- one use permits a {\i mamzer} to marry a {\i shtuki} (a person of unknown paternity, a doubtful {\i mamzer}); {\i v\u8217'chad l\u8217'mishri shtuki b\u8217'yisrael} \u8212- and one use permits a {\i shtuki} to marry into the full Jewish community. The reasoning is that {\i bi-khal Hashem} means {\i kahal vadai} \u8212- a {\i definite} assembly, a person of {\i definite} Jewish fitness. {\i Kahal safek} \u8212- a person whose status is uncertain \u8212- is not encompassed by the prohibition. Therefore {\i lo yavo mamzer bi-khal Hashem} means only a {\i definite mamzer} may not enter; a person of {\i doubtful mamzer} status ({\i shtuki}) may marry a {\i yisrael}. Correspondingly, the prohibition bars a {\i mamzer} only from a {\i definite kahal}; he may marry a {\i shtuki}, who is only a {\i safek kahal}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The practical significance is substantial: {\i mid\u8217'oraita}, a {\i shtuki} may marry into a {\i kohen}, {\i levi}, or {\i yisrael} family. The {\i mishnah\u8217's} stricter treatment of {\i shtuki} is {\i mid\u8217'rabbanan}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gerim Outside Kahal\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Kahal gerim lo ikra kahal} \u8212- converts were never referred to as {\i kahal} in the Torah. Therefore, the prohibition of {\i lo yavo mamzer bi-khal Hashem} does not apply to them as the protected community. A {\i mamzer} may marry a {\i giyoret}, and a {\i ger} may marry a {\i mamzeret}, because {\i gerim} were not included in the Torah\u8217's designation of {\i kahal Hashem}. This is Rabbi Yosi\u8217's position.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 One Law Argument\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yehuda disagrees and sources his position in a different verse: {\i ha-kahal, chuka achat lachem v\u8217'la-ger ha-gar} \u8212- the assembly: one law for you and for the {\i ger} who dwells among you. This verse explicitly places the {\i ger} within the {\i kahal}, meaning that when the Torah says a {\i mamzer} cannot enter {\i kahal Hashem}, the {\i ger} is included in that {\i kahal}. Accordingly, a {\i mamzer} cannot marry a {\i giyoret}, and a {\i ger} cannot marry a {\i mamzeret}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yosi responds: {\i chuka achat hefsik ha-inyan} \u8212- the phrase {\i chuka achat} (\u8220"one law\u8221") interrupts the passage and is a separate statement. The verse says \u8220"one law for you and for the {\i ger}\u8221" \u8212- meaning certain laws apply equally to both \u8212- but it does not mean the {\i ger} is identified as part of the {\i kahal} in the genealogical sense.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Marriage to Kohenet\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Echad ger v\u8217'echad eved meshuchrar v\u8217'chalal mutarin b\u8217'kohenet} \u8212- a {\i ger}, a freed slave, and a {\i chalal} are all permitted to marry a {\i kohenet} (the daughter of a {\i kohen}). This is uncontested. The Torah\u8217's marriage restrictions on {\i kehuna} apply to {\i kohanim} as men \u8212- they may not marry a {\i giyoret}, a {\i chalalah}, a divorced woman, or a {\i zonah}. There is no corresponding restriction on which men a {\i kohenet} may marry. Since a {\i ger}, freed slave, and {\i chalal} each have the same {\i yisrael} status for purposes of marriage eligibility, they may marry any woman a {\i yisrael} may marry, including a {\i bat kohen}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Women\u8217's Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Mesayei lei l\u8217'Rav, d\u8217'amar Rav Yehuda amar Rav: hitir kesherot l\u8217'hinasei l\u8217'psulim} \u8212- this supports Rav\u8217's position that {\i kesherot} \u8212- women of fit status \u8212- are not restricted from marrying men of {\i pasul} categories. The entire structure of marriage prohibitions in {\i kehuna} is directed at the {\i kohanic} male. A woman, regardless of her father\u8217's lineage, bears no independent restriction based on whom she marries, as long as the marriage itself is permitted from his side.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Zeira Stoned\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Darash Zeira b\u8217'Mechuza: ger mutar b\u8217'mamzeret} \u8212- Rabbi Zeira stood up and publicly taught in Mechuza that a {\i ger} is permitted to marry a {\i mamzeret}, in accordance with Rabbi Yosi. {\i Ragmuhu kulei alma b\u8217'etrogaihu} \u8212- everyone pelted him with their {\i etrogim}. The incident apparently occurred during {\i Sukkot}. Rashi explains that the audience was not actually offended by the permissive ruling itself \u8212- being told that something is {\i mutar} is hardly an insult. What offended them was the underlying {\i derashah}: Rabbi Zeira\u8217's teaching implied that {\i gerim} were never called {\i kahal}, that they stand outside the primary designation of the Jewish assembly. For a community with a large {\i ger} population, this was experienced as a statement of exclusion and diminishment of identity, not as halakhic permission.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava\u8217's Strategy\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Amar Rava: mi ika d\u8217'darash milta ki hai b\u8217'duchta d\u8217'shchichei giyorei?} \u8212- who would be so careless as to teach this in a place full of converts? Rava himself then taught in Mechuza. He began with: {\i ger mutar b\u8217'kohenet} \u8212- a {\i ger} may marry a {\i kohenet}. The community was delighted \u8212- {\i tnu b\u8217'shirai}, they clothed him in silk garments \u8212- for this ruling affirmed the {\i ger\u8217's} full status and his access to the most distinguished women of the Jewish community. Then Rava added: {\i ger mutar b\u8217'mamzeret} \u8212- a {\i ger} may also marry a {\i mamzeret}. The community objected that he had undermined his first teaching.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava replied: {\i lo d\u8217'tava l\u8217'chu avdi l\u8217'chu} \u8212- no, I am doing you good. {\i Ei bei meihacha nasiv v\u8217'ei bei meihacha nasiv} \u8212- if he wishes, he may marry from here (a {\i kohenet}), and if he wishes, from there (a {\i mamzeret}). The point Rava was making was not that the second ruling is offensive, but that it should be understood as an expansion of opportunity, not as a statement of diminishment. The pedagogical lesson is clear: the same halakhic content, presented in a different sequence and framing, can be received as affirming rather than insulting. One begins by establishing the {\i ger\u8217's} full dignity and standing, and then presents the broader range of his legal options.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Final Halacha\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Hilkhata: ger mutar b\u8217'kohenet u-mutar b\u8217'mamzeret} \u8212- the practical ruling is that a {\i ger} is permitted with a {\i kohenet} and permitted with a {\i mamzeret}. {\i Mutar b\u8217'kohenet d\u8217'huzharu kesherot l\u8217'hinasei l\u8217'psulim} \u8212- the permission with a {\i kohenet} reflects the rule that women of fit status are not restricted from marrying men of {\i pasul} category; the restriction of {\i kehuna} runs only to the {\i kohanic} male. {\i U-mutar b\u8217'mamzeret k\u8217'Rabbi Yosi} \u8212- the permission with a {\i mamzeret} follows Rabbi Yosi\u8217's ruling, which is the {\i halacha l\u8217'maaseh}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Kiddushin 73\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Safek D\u8217'Oraita Framework\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Safek in Psak\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Before entering the {\i gemara} itself, it is worth pausing on one of the most far-reaching and central {\i sugyos} in all of practical {\i psak}: the question of {\i safek} \u8212- doubt \u8212- and how halachic authorities navigate it. This question bears on {\i machlokes}, on cases of genuine uncertainty, and on a vast range of applied {\i halacha}. The most important foundational source is the Rambam, and understanding his position is indispensable for what the {\i gemara} on this {\i daf} is doing.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rambam on Safek\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Rambam, at the end of the ninth {\i perek} of {\i Hilchos Tumas Meis}, states what he calls a {\i davar yadua} \u8212- a known fact: {\i she-kol elu ha-tumaos v\u8217'kayotzei bahen she-hein mishum safek hare hein shel divreihem} \u8212- any {\i tumah} concern that arises from a case of doubt is itself only rabbinic in origin. And he continues: {\i v\u8217'ein t\u8217'me\u8217'in min ha-Torah ela mi she-nitma b\u8217'tumah vada\u8217'is} \u8212- the only biblical-level {\i tumah} is one that is certain. He extends this principle across every domain of {\i halacha}: {\i b\u8217'tumaos bein b\u8217'machalos asuros bein b\u8217'arayos v\u8217'shabbasos} \u8212- {\i tumah v\u8217'taharah}, forbidden foods, {\i arayos}, and {\i Shabbos} alike \u8212- {\i ein chosheshin lahem ela mi-divrei sofrim}. According to the Rambam, any case of doubt immediately reduces the prohibition to a rabbinic level.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is worth noting where these general rules about {\i safek} find their primary halachic articulation: in the laws of {\i tumah v\u8217'taharah}. This is because a principle derived from {\i Sotah} \u8212- {\i safek tumah b\u8217'reshus harabbim tahor}, that a doubtful impurity in a public space is treated as pure \u8212- is applied throughout. The Rambam defines {\i reshus harabbim} for {\i tumah} purposes differently than for {\i hilchos Shabbos}; here, even three people constitute a {\i reshus harabbim}. In {\i reshus hayachid}, by contrast, {\i safek tumah} is {\i tamei}. Both of these rulings operate at the biblical level. The Rambam\u8217's broader point, however, is that beyond these specific {\i tumah} rules, in all other areas of {\i halacha} \u8212- including the gravest categories of {\i issur} \u8212- doubt itself carries only rabbinic force. The implication that immediately follows is that one would never bring a {\i korban} for any such doubtful case, since the doubt does not constitute a biblical violation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rashba vs Rambam\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 That observation precisely is the ground of the {\i Rashba}\u8217's challenge. In his {\i Toras HaBayis} \u8212- the fourth {\i bayis}, first {\i shaar} \u8212- the {\i Rashba} argues at length against what he takes to be the Rambam\u8217's position. His objection: we know that when someone has a {\i safek} about whether he violated a prohibition that would normally require a {\i korban chatas}, he must bring an {\i asham talui}. The very institution of {\i asham talui} demonstrates that the Torah itself is concerned with cases of doubt. The Torah, in the {\i Rashba}\u8217's view, does not treat {\i safek} as automatically rabbinic.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Rashba} further believes that the Rambam\u8217's source for his broad principle is precisely the {\i gemara} before us \u8212- and we will see shortly why. The debate reduces to whether the {\i gemara}\u8217's treatment of {\i safek mamzer} is a model for all other cases of doubt in the Torah, or whether it is a unique exception. The {\i Rashba} takes the latter position, maintaining that {\i safek mamzer} is a special case and that the Torah\u8217's general rule remains that {\i safek d\u8217'Oraita l\u8217'chumra} \u8212- biblical-level doubts must be treated stringently \u8212- and this principle is itself biblical, not merely rabbinic.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Machlokes & Authority\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Rambam presents a second, distinct {\i halacha} about how to rule when there is a {\i machlokes}. In {\i Hilchos Mamrim}, at the end of the first {\i perek}, the Rambam rules: {\i shnei chachamim o shnei batei dinin she-necheleku} \u8212- if two sages or two courts disagree \u8212- {\i bizman shelo bizman haSanhedrin}, in a time when there is no centralized authority (as has been the case for some seventeen hundred years), or even in a time when there is a {\i Sanhedrin} but the matter has not yet been adjudicated before it, then whether the dispute spans one generation or many generations successively, if {\i im ein lekha yodea lehikan hadin noteh} \u8212- if the decisor cannot determine which position the law favors \u8212- {\i beshel Torah halakh ahar hamahmir}, in matters of biblical law one must be stringent, and {\i beshel soferim halakh ahar hamekel}, in matters of rabbinic law one must be lenient.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Rambam\u8217's point is that post-{\i Sanhedrin}, there is no longer any structural rule for resolving {\i machlokes}. The only operative principle is: leniency for rabbinic matters, stringency for biblical matters. This {\i Rambam} is much debated, but it is essential to distinguish it clearly from the question of {\i safek}. The question of what to do when a case itself is in doubt is separate from the question of how to rule when authorities disagree. The two are often conflated, but they are distinct {\i halachos}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chumra vs Kula\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A further complexity arises when applying the rule of {\i beshel Torah halakh ahar hamahmir}. There are cases \u8212- {\i shiurim} being a prime example \u8212- where the very same ruling that is stringent in one application produces leniency in another. Almost any {\i halachic she\u8217'eilah} can be analyzed where the {\i machmir} position and the {\i meikil} position each simultaneously generate {\i kulot} and {\i chumros} depending on the specific case. This is a critical part of the {\i halachic} calculus: whether a given ruling is genuinely stringent or genuinely lenient must be evaluated across its full range of applications, not only in isolation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The famous formulation of Rav Chaim Brisker captures this well. When asked why he was so lenient in {\i hilchos Shabbos}, he replied: I am not lenient in {\i hilchos Shabbos} \u8212- I am stringent in {\i pikuach nefesh}. His tendency to permit even arguably Rabbinic violations when there was any medical concern was, in his framing, a {\i chumra} in the domain of preserving life \u8212- which is itself a biblical obligation \u8212- not a {\i kula} in {\i Shabbos}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Shtuki Kosher D\u8217'Oraita\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shtuki Kasher\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 We are now at the second wide line on 73a. {\i Amar Rava: devar Torah shtuki kasher.} Rava rules that at the biblical level, a {\i shtuki} \u8212- a child whose mother is known but whose father is unidentified \u8212- is {\i kasher} for marriage into the Jewish people. The {\i mishna} had already discussed the category of {\i shtuki}, who may be a {\i mamzer} or a {\i chalal} without our knowing. Yet Rava\u8217's opening claim is that this concern is not biblical in origin. We now need to understand why.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rov & Extraction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Mai ta\u8217'ama?} \u8212- what is the reason? {\i Rov kesherim etzlah} \u8212- the majority of men who had any relationship with this woman are {\i kesherim lah}: they are not her relatives, not {\i mamzerim}, not {\i netinim}. They are, in other words, men whose children would be fully {\i kasher}. And {\i umi\u8217'ut pesulim etzlah} \u8212- only a minority are {\i pesulim} to her.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Now, even if the man who fathered the child came from among those {\i pesulim}, we can apply the principle of {\i kol d\u8217'parish m\u8217'ruba parish}: whenever a person or thing is extracted from its original locale, we follow the {\i rov}. That is, if the man left his community and came to be with this woman, he separated himself from the majority \u8212- and since the majority of men are {\i kesherim}, we follow that majority and presume the child is {\i kasher}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might ask: why invoke {\i kol d\u8217'parish m\u8217'ruba parish} at all? Is this not simply the standard application of {\i rov}? The {\i gemara} here addresses one of the central distinctions in the entire topic: the difference between going after the majority of a fixed group versus going after a majority when the individual has left his setting.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kavuah Distinction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Mah amart dilma azlah ihi legabeihu? Havah lei kavuah.} If she left her location and went to him, then the man is stationary \u8212- {\i kavuah} \u8212- and a different principle applies: {\i kol kavuah k\u8217'mechetza al mechetza dami}. When the relevant party is fixed in place, we treat the doubt as fifty-fifty rather than as a case governed by the majority.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} thus distinguishes two scenarios. If the man came to her \u8212- or if both of them went to a third location \u8212- then {\i kol d\u8217'parish m\u8217'ruba parish} applies and we follow the {\i rov}. But if she went to him, his being stationary invokes {\i kavuah} and the doubt becomes an even split. This contrast between {\i kol d\u8217'parish m\u8217'ruba parish} and {\i kavuah} \u8212- between a mobile extraction and a fixed party \u8212- is one of the core distinctions in the {\i sugya} of {\i rov}, debated extensively by the {\i Rishonim} and {\i Acharonim}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mamzer Safek Allowed\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Even granting that the case of {\i kavuah} would leave us with a fifty-fifty doubt, there is a further biblical principle that resolves the matter in favor of {\i kashrut}. {\i HaTorah amra lo yavo mamzer} \u8212- the Torah says a {\i mamzer} may not enter the assembly of Israel. But {\i mamzer vadai hu d\u8217'lo yavo} \u8212- only a {\i certain} mamzer is excluded. {\i Ha mamzer safek yavo b\u8217'kahal} \u8212- a {\i safek mamzer} may enter and marry into the Jewish people.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 From this the {\i gemara} draws a further inference: {\i b\u8217'kahal vadai hu d\u8217'lo yavo, ha b\u8217'kahal safek yavo} \u8212- a {\i vadai mamzer} is excluded only from a {\i vadai} Jewish community, but he may marry someone whose {\i kashrut} is itself {\i b\u8217'safek}. Both conclusions follow from the {\i drasha} on the verse: a {\i safek mamzer} may marry a perfectly {\i kasher} Jew, and a {\i vadai mamzer} may marry a person whose {\i kashrut} is uncertain.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is precisely here that the {\i Rashba} locates the Rambam\u8217's proof. According to the {\i Rashba}\u8217's reading of the Rambam, the Torah\u8217's permission for a {\i safek mamzer} to marry is the model for the Rambam\u8217's general rule that {\i safek} reduces all prohibitions to the rabbinic level. The {\i Rashba} himself reads this as a {\i chiddush} unique to the law of {\i mamzer} \u8212- a special biblical exception \u8212- while maintaining that {\i safek d\u8217'Oraita l\u8217'chumra} remains a biblical rule everywhere else.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbis\u8217' Yuchsin Upgrade\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If {\i shtuki} is {\i kasher} at the biblical level, why did the Rabbis restrict him? {\i Gezeira shema yisa achoto me\u8217'aviv} \u8212- a {\i shtuki} does not know who his father is, and so he might unknowingly marry his paternal sister. This is the first proposed reason for the rabbinic prohibition.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gezeira Rejection\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} immediately challenges this reasoning. {\i Ela me\u8217'ata shtuki shtukit lo yisa shema yisa achoto me\u8217'aviv} \u8212- if we are worried that a {\i shtuki} might marry his paternal sister, then he should be forbidden to marry even another {\i shtukis}, a female {\i shtuki}, since they might share the same father even with different mothers. And one could extend this further: {\i bat shtukit lo yisa shema yisa achoto me\u8217'aviv} \u8212- he should not be permitted to marry even the daughter of a {\i shtukit}, since conceivably her father might also be his father.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} rejects each extension: {\i kol ki hani mezaneu v\u8217'azlei?} \u8212- are we really to suppose that the same man fathered every {\i shtuki} in town? And {\i lo shechiach} \u8212- such a scenario is so rare and implausible that we do not build legislation around it. {\i Hacha nami lo shechiach} \u8212- the case of {\i bat shtukit} is equally implausible. The {\i gezeira} argument for each of these extensions fails for the same reason.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 With all these proposed {\i gezeiros} rejected, the {\i gemara} concludes: {\i ela ma\u8217'ala asu b\u8217'yuchsin} \u8212- the prohibition on {\i shtuki} is not grounded in any single compelling logical necessity but reflects a rabbinic decision to establish heightened standards in matters of {\i yuchsin}, lineage. The {\i Maharsha} at the beginning of this {\i perek} compares the ten levels of lineage that the Rabbis instituted to the ten levels of {\i kedusha} in {\i Eretz Yisrael} enumerated in the first {\i perek} of {\i Keilim} \u8212- from the land itself inward to the {\i Beis HaMikdash}. Just as those gradations of sanctity are maintained and protected, so too the Rabbis established tiers of {\i yuchsin} as a way of heightening the community\u8217's awareness and protection of familial status. The Rambam himself, in his {\i Perush HaMishnah}, traces his own ancestry back many generations, listing rabbis and {\i dayanim} who preceded him in Spain \u8212- a testament to the care families ought to take in preserving knowledge of their lineage.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Asufi Kosher D\u8217'Oraita\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Asufi Kasher\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Having completed the {\i sugya} of {\i shtuki}, Rava turns to the parallel case: {\i devar Torah asufi kasher} \u8212- at the biblical level, an {\i asufi} is likewise {\i kasher}. An {\i asufi} \u8212- literally one who has been gathered up \u8212- is a foundling of entirely unknown parentage, discovered abandoned in a public place. Unlike the {\i shtuki}, whose mother is known, the {\i asufi}\u8217's lineage is completely unidentified. Rava maintains that even this more severe case of unknown parentage does not render the child {\i pasul} at the Torah level.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Three-Fold Doubt\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Mai ta\u8217'ama? Eshet ish bi\u8217'ba\u8217'ala talya.} The reasoning begins with the observation that if the mother was a married woman, it is overwhelmingly likely that her husband is the child\u8217's father. A married woman\u8217's child would therefore be fully {\i kasher}. The vast majority of women who might abandon a child are married women whose children are presumptively legitimate.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What, then, of the remaining cases? {\i Mi\u8217'ut arusot umi\u8217'ut shehalach ba\u8217'aleihen limdinat hayam} \u8212- the {\i gemara} identifies two smaller categories: {\i arusot}, women who are {\i mekudeshet} but whose marriages have not been consummated, whose child would be a {\i mamzer} if fathered by another man; and {\i nesuot} whose husbands had gone abroad for an extended period, such that any child they bore would also be a {\i mamzer}. To these two categories we add {\i pnuyah} \u8212- an unmarried woman, whose child would be entirely {\i kasher} regardless of the father \u8212- and the case of {\i demachmat ra\u8217'avon}, a woman compelled by famine to abandon a child she could not support.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Keivan de\u8217'ika pnuyah ve\u8217'ika nami demachmat ra\u8217'avon, hava palga u\u8217'palga.} Because there are two plausible scenarios that would produce a {\i kasher} child and two that would produce a {\i mamzer}, the {\i gemara} treats this as a fifty-fifty doubt. One might object that the incidence rates of each scenario are not necessarily equal, but the {\i gemara}\u8217's point is that there is genuine doubt on both sides \u8212- enough to constitute a {\i safek}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is worth appreciating what the practice of abandoning a child in the street meant in the ancient world. It was not equivalent to modern adoption; an infant placed in a public thoroughfare faced severe risk of death unless discovered quickly. Yet in years of famine, parents who felt unable to provide for a newborn and wished to preserve resources for surviving children made this choice \u8212- not an excusable one, but an understandable one given the desperation of circumstances. Notably, Rav Chaim Brisker was known as a kind of address for such cases in his own community: parents who could not care for a child knew that the rabbi\u8217's doorstep was where to turn, and Rav Chaim reputedly checked his front step throughout the night to ensure no abandoned child was left unattended.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Asufi as Safek\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The same biblical argument that resolved {\i shtuki} resolves {\i asufi} as well. {\i HaTorah amra lo yavo mamzer b\u8217'kahal Hashem} \u8212- and {\i mamzer vadai hu d\u8217'lo yavo, ha mamzer safek yavo}. Since the worst-case scenario for an {\i asufi} is that he is a {\i safek mamzer}, and the Torah explicitly permits a {\i safek mamzer} to marry into the Jewish people, the {\i asufi} is {\i kasher} at the biblical level. Even the {\i kavuah} concern does not generate anything worse than a fifty-fifty doubt \u8212- and a fifty-fifty doubt, as the {\i gemara} has shown, still falls within the category of {\i safek mamzer} who may marry a {\i kasher} Jew.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Asufi Prohibition\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Why then did the Rabbis prohibit the {\i asufi} from marrying into an established Jewish family? {\i Shema yisa achoto me\u8217'aviv bein me\u8217'imo} \u8212- perhaps he will unknowingly marry his paternal or maternal sister, since both parents are unknown. {\i V\u8217'chi hanei shadei v\u8217'azlei?} \u8212- and are we to suppose that all the abandoned children in a city come from the same man and woman? Once again, the {\i gezeira} is extended to its logical extremes and each extension is rejected as implausible: {\i lo shechiach, hacha nami lo shechiach}. And once again, the {\i gemara} concludes: {\i ela ma\u8217'ala asu b\u8217'yuchsin} \u8212- the Rabbis elevated the standards of lineage beyond what the Torah strictly demands. The {\i asufi}, like the {\i shtuki}, is {\i kasher} d\u8217'Oraita, but the Rabbis, as a matter of {\i yuchsin} policy, restricted his marriage options.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is a notable moment in the {\i sugya}. The acknowledgment that these restrictions are rabbinic \u8212- not biblical \u8212- creates real {\i halachic} flexibility. Once a prohibition is classified as rabbinic, a further {\i safek} about whether the category applies at all is sufficient, under {\i safek d\u8217'rabbanan l\u8217'kula}, to resolve matters leniently. The {\i amoraim} in the {\i sugya} that follows will work hard to narrow the {\i asufi} category precisely in this spirit.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Regarding contemporary analogues: the question of sperm donation has generated significant {\i halachic} discussion. Rav Moshe Feinstein, in the first volume of {\i Igros Moshe}, rules leniently, arguing that {\i mamzerus} cannot be created through a medical procedure that does not constitute an act of sexual intercourse. The Torah\u8217's prohibition and the {\i halachic} category of {\i mamzer} are predicated on a prohibited sexual act, and a clinical procedure does not meet that threshold. The Satmar Rav reportedly took the opposing position, and the dispute was fierce. Today, however, the mainstream {\i poskim} \u8212- including in the {\i Chareidi} world \u8212- generally follow Rav Moshe\u8217's position, since the alternative is logically untenable: if the parents committed no prohibited {\i act}, the mechanism for creating {\i mamzerus} is absent. Rav Moshe supports his ruling with {\i gemaros} that discuss conception occurring without intercourse \u8212- cases the {\i gemara} itself apparently contemplated. Related but distinct questions arise around egg donation, where the {\i halachic} identity of the mother becomes a separate dispute, and where the concern shifts from {\i mamzerus} to {\i arayos} for the resulting child.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Signs Negating Asufi\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Circumcision Evidence\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now turns to a critical practical narrowing of the {\i asufi} category. {\i Amar Rav Abba bar Huna: matzu mahul, ein bo mishum asufi} \u8212- if the foundling was found to have been circumcised, he does not carry the {\i halachic} status of {\i asufi}. The reasoning is that parents who kept the child for eight days, arranged for a {\i mohel}, and performed the {\i mila} cannot be said to have abandoned the child in a spirit of utter neglect. The very act of {\i mila} demonstrates parental care, and a child whose parents exercised such care is presumed to come from a {\i kasher} family. It is worth noting that this ruling does not depend on the assumption that only Jews performed {\i mila} in that environment \u8212- the point is that the parents\u8217' ongoing involvement, evidenced by the {\i mila}, removes the presumption of abandonment that defines {\i asufi} status. The {\i rishonim} already raised the question of whether parents of a {\i mamzer} would be willing to provide such care, and the answer embedded in this {\i halacha} is that the stigma and shame attending a {\i mamzer} are precisely what would lead such parents to avoid investing in the child \u8212- making the presence of {\i mila} an indicator of {\i kasher} parentage.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Limb Care Signs\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Similarly, {\i meshofeyhu} \u8212- if the child\u8217's limbs had been straightened. Newborns in antiquity were sometimes bound to frames to correct bow-leggedness or otherwise align their limbs, a practice Rashi notes continued as recently as the early twentieth century. {\i Rashi} explains {\i eivarav mesukanim umeyusharim} \u8212- his limbs are adjusted and straightened. If the child shows evidence of this kind of physical care, {\i ein bo mishum asufi}, he is not classified as an {\i asufi}, because once again the parents are demonstrably not people who simply cast the child away.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Anointing & Amulets\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Further signs of parental care: {\i mashuach u\u8217'mahul v\u8217'kachul} \u8212- if the child has been anointed with oil or had eye shadow applied, practices done for infants in that period. {\i Rami chamra tali pitka} \u8212- if the child was bound with medicinal preparations or had an amulet hung about him \u8212- {\i tali kemeia} \u8212- or carried some other protective charm, {\i ein bo mishum asufi}. The logic is consistent: the parents provided for the child\u8217's health and protection, which is incompatible with the profile of total abandonment that defines {\i asufi}. A child found with a vaccination record in a modern setting might be understood analogously \u8212- the parents were clearly engaged in the child\u8217's care.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tree Placement\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tali b\u8217'diklei} \u8212- if the child was found hanging in a palm tree, suspended in a basket or swaddled, the {\i gemara} rules as follows: {\i ei matya leh chaya, yesh bo mishum asufi} \u8212- if it is a location where an animal could reach him, he is an {\i asufi}, because the parents placed him there for him to be consumed. {\i V\u8217'im lav, ein bo mishum asufi} \u8212- if the location was one where no animal could reach him, the parents placed him there to protect him, not to abandon him to death, and he is not an {\i asufi}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A variant case involves a {\i sardita} \u8212- a sorb tree, which Rashi identifies using a term meaning a tree known to attract demonic forces when close to a settled area. {\i Sardita semicha l\u8217'mata, yesh bo mishum asufi} \u8212- if the sorb tree was close to town, the child is an {\i asufi} because the proximity of such a tree to populated areas renders it dangerous on account of the demonic presences said to frequent inhabited spaces. {\i V\u8217'im lav, ein bo mishum asufi} \u8212- if the tree was far from town, the child is not an {\i asufi}. Rava offers an alternative explanation: the criterion is simply whether there are many people about \u8212- {\i mishum de\u8217'nefishi} \u8212- which is in effect the same standard as the synagogue case that follows.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Synagogue & Public\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Bei knishta smicha lemata, ve\u8217'shichichi bei rabim, ein bo mishum asufi} \u8212- a child found in a {\i shul} that is close to the town and regularly frequented by many people is not an {\i asufi}, because placing a child there is a way of ensuring he will be found and cared for. It is worth noting that in Babylonia, the {\i shul} was typically located outside the city \u8212- a feature of Persian rule or of the practical convenience for people traveling to work \u8212- and indeed some of the conventions of Babylonian {\i davening} reflect this extra-urban placement. {\i V\u8217'i lo, yesh bo mishum asufi} \u8212- if the {\i shul} was not close to town and few people passed through, then leaving the child there is tantamount to abandonment and the child is an {\i asufi}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Non-Asufi Assumption\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The thrust of this entire {\i sugya} is a consistent movement away from classifying children as {\i asufi}. The {\i mishna}\u8217's category of {\i asufi} is narrowed by the {\i amora\u8217'im} at every turn. Whatever grounds can be found to say the child is {\i not} an {\i asufi} are embraced. The principle is that {\i asufi} means truly abandoned \u8212- not merely found in the street, but found in circumstances indicating total neglect, with no signs of parental investment whatsoever. Any evidence of care \u8212- physical, medical, ritual, or positional \u8212- removes the classification. The {\i gezeira} that motivated the rabbinic prohibition in the first place ({\i ma\u8217'ala asu b\u8217'yuchsin}) was itself acknowledged to be not strictly logical; and so the {\i amora\u8217'im} are appropriately reluctant to extend it into cases where any reasonable argument for non-{\i asufi} status exists. A {\i safek} about whether the child is an {\i asufi} at all, once the prohibition is rabbinic, resolves leniently.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Pit & River Tests\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Hai pira de\u8217'sufla, yesh bo mishum asufi} \u8212- a ditch used for storing harvested dates, or for storing date pits for animals to lick (since the residual sugar attracts goats and cattle), is a location where a child left there would be an {\i asufi}. {\i Chitzra de\u8217'nahara, ein bo mishum asufi} \u8212- the bank of a river, by the docks where people pass, does not confer {\i asufi} status, because the parents placed the child there to be found. {\i Pashrei} \u8212- but in another section of the riverbank, further from populated areas and traffic \u8212- {\i yesh bo mishum asufi}, the child is an {\i asufi} because discovery there is unlikely.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The case of Moshe Rabbeinu naturally comes to mind: found on the bank of the Nile in the middle of Mitzrayim. Of course, Moshe had a {\i mashgiach} \u8212- his sister Miriam stood watch. The {\i midrashim} in the beginning of {\i Sotah} elaborate on Miriam\u8217's role: she had prophesied Moshe\u8217's birth, she confronted her father about his decision, and she watched over the infant with dedication. The {\i mefarshim} debate what exactly it was she later took issue with \u8212- whether it concerned whom Moshe married or whether it involved his separation from his wife \u8212- but throughout the narrative Miriam\u8217's protective watchfulness over her younger brother is evident.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Street Edges\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Ve\u8217'tzidei reshus harabim, ein bo mishum asufi} \u8212- the edges of the public thoroughfare do not constitute {\i reshus harabim} in the full sense for purposes of {\i asufi}. As we know from the first {\i perek} of {\i Masechet Shabbat}, the sidewalk-like edges alongside storefronts do not share the full status of the {\i reshus harabim} that occupies the middle of a sixteen-{\i amah}-wide street; they carry at most the status of {\i karmelis}. Here too, a child placed at the edge of a public street \u8212- against a wall, near a storefront \u8212- was placed in a protected and visible location. The parents were trying to ensure he would be found, not leaving him to be trampled. {\i Yesh bo mishum asufi} only if the child was found in the actual middle of the street, where leaving him constitutes abandonment to death.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Famine & Reclamation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Famine Leniency\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Amar Rava: uvshanei raavon, ein bo mishum asufi} \u8212- Rava rules that during years of famine, a child found in the marketplace does not carry {\i asufi} status at all. The reasoning is that a family driven by famine to give up a child is not a family that has abandoned the child in the morally and {\i halachically} relevant sense. However desperate and inexcusable the act, there is a comprehensible motivation \u8212- preserving resources for surviving children \u8212- that distinguishes this from straightforward abandonment. The presumption, even for a child found in the street during a famine, is that his parents are a {\i kasher} family acting under impossible circumstances.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Le\u8217'Altar Principle\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This raises the question of how Rava\u8217's ruling on famine years relates to an earlier opinion. {\i Ella ki itmar d\u8217'Rava aha d\u8217'amar Rav Yehuda bar Zavda amar Rav: kol zman shebashuk aviv ve\u8217'imo ne\u8217'emanim alav} \u8212- as long as the child is still in the marketplace, his parents are believed if they appear and claim him. {\i Ne\u8217'esaf min hashuk ein ne\u8217'emanim alav} \u8212- once the child has been taken in from the marketplace, the parents are no longer believed. The principle underlying this ruling is {\i le\u8217'altar}: the parents are believed only immediately, while the situation is still unfolding and no {\i shem} has attached to the child.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shem Asufi Timing\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Mai ta\u8217'ama? Amar Rava: ho\u8217'il veyatza alav shem asufi} \u8212- once the child has been publicly identified as an {\i asufi}, that name and status attach irreversibly. It is analogous to {\i yatza kol} \u8212- once the name has gone out, it cannot be undone. The parents who arrive after the fact and wish to reclaim the child cannot undo the {\i shem asufi} that has already spread.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Famine Exception\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Ve\u8217'amar Rava: uvshanei raavon af al pi shene\u8217'esaf min hashuk aviv ve\u8217'imo ne\u8217'emanim alav} \u8212- but in a year of famine, Rava rules that even after the child has been taken in from the marketplace, the parents may appear and reclaim him, and they will be believed. The {\i shem asufi} that would normally be irreversible does not permanently attach during famine years. The {\i gemara} clarifies that Rava\u8217's ruling addresses precisely the case where {\i ne\u8217'esaf min hashuk} has already occurred \u8212- in the normal case that is the point of irreversibility, but in a famine year the parents retain the ability to reclaim the child even afterward. How long afterward this extends \u8212- whether days, weeks, or longer \u8212- is left open, but the short-term claim is clearly valid.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This has poignant resonance with the realities of post-Holocaust life, where parents and children separated by catastrophe sought to reunite years later, generating real {\i she\u8217'eilos} in {\i arayos} when people who had grown close discovered they were related. One thinks also of the story of Trotsky\u8217's grandson, himself {\i halachically} Jewish, who discovered that his sister had survived Stalin\u8217's purges and flew to Moscow to be reunited with her as she was dying of cancer \u8212- a lifetime after their separation.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Three Ne\u8217'emanim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Le\u8217'Altar Witnesses\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Amar Rav Chisda: shlosha ne\u8217'emanim le\u8217'altar} \u8212- there are three parties who are believed {\i le\u8217'altar}, in the moment, but only in the moment. After the fact, their testimony or claim is no longer accepted. These three are: {\i asufi} \u8212- the case of the foundling\u8217's parents, as just discussed \u8212- {\i chaya}, the midwife, and {\i poteret chavroseha}, a woman who exempts her fellow women from a {\i halachic} concern.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Midwife & Bechor\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Chaya ne\u8217'emenes lomar zeh yatza rishon vezeh yatza sheini} \u8212- the midwife is believed to testify which of two children emerged first and which second. The practical significance of this testimony is primarily in a case of twin boys, where the question of {\i bechor} \u8212- firstborn status \u8212- determines who receives the double portion of inheritance. The narrative of Tamar and Yehuda, where the midwife played an active role in identifying which twin was born first, illustrates exactly this concern.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Midwife Conditions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Uvameh devarim amurim shelo yatzsa vechazra} \u8212- the midwife is believed only if she did not leave the birthing room and return. {\i Aval yatzsa vechazra eina ne\u8217'emenes} \u8212- if she left and came back, the chain of observation is broken and she is no longer believed, since even a professional midwife could become confused when newborns \u8212- who look remarkably similar to one another \u8212- are involved and she had a gap in her supervision.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Eliezer Strict\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Eliezer omer huchzeka al omda ne\u8217'emenes} \u8212- Rabbi Eliezer is even more stringent: the midwife is believed only if she remained sitting on the birthing stool itself throughout. {\i Ve\u8217'im lav eina ne\u8217'emenes} \u8212- if she even turned her head or got up, she is no longer believed. {\i Mai beinaihu? Ika beinaihu de\u8217'ahadar apei} \u8212- the practical difference between Rabbi Eliezer and the {\i Tanna Kamma} is a case where the midwife turned her face away from the birthing woman. According to the {\i Tanna Kamma}, who requires only that she not leave the room, turning her face would not disqualify her; according to Rabbi Eliezer, who requires she remain on the birthing stool, even this would disqualify her testimony.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Poteret Chavroseha\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Poteret chavroseha mai hi?} \u8212- what is the case of a woman who exempts her fellow women? {\i Detnan: shalosh nashim shehayu yeshenos bemitah achas, nimtza dam tachas achas meihen} \u8212- three women are sleeping in the same bed and blood is found underneath one of them. {\i Kulan temeios} \u8212- all three are rendered {\i tamei} by reason of {\i safek tumah birshus hayachid}, since any one of them might be the source of the blood. However, {\i badka achas meihen venimtzas teme\u8217'ah} \u8212- if one of them performs a {\i bedika} and is found to be {\i tamei}, {\i hi teme\u8217'ah vekulan tehoros} \u8212- she is {\i niddah} and the other two are exempt. This woman\u8217's {\i bedika} \u8220"exempts her friends\u8221" \u8212- she is the {\i poteret chavroseha}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ra\u8217'avad\u8217's Burden\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Ra\u8217'avad}, in {\i Ba\u8217'alei Hanefesh}, discusses the varying thresholds of proof required for {\i bedikos} in the laws of {\i niddah}. He observes that the burden of proof in this case \u8212- {\i poteret chavroseha} \u8212- is actually the lowest of all the {\i bedika} scenarios. What suffices here is {\i kinuach}, a mere wiping, rather than a full internal {\i bedika}. Even this minimal examination, according to the {\i Ra\u8217'avad}, is sufficient to establish that this woman is the source of the blood and thereby release the other two women from their {\i safek tumah}. The contrast between the minimal threshold here and the more demanding standards required in other {\i bedika} contexts is a point the {\i Ra\u8217'avad} uses to illuminate the graduated structure of {\i halachic} proof in the laws of {\i niddah}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Kiddushin 75\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Lineage & Safek Mamzer\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Opening & Overview\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The daf under discussion is 75b, and we pick up at the bottom of 74b. Before diving into the new material, it is worth pausing to survey the broad sweep of what the Gemara has accomplished in these pages. The Mishnah famously lists ten distinct gradations of {\i yichus}, but much of what the Gemara has done is to narrow and minimize the scope of the concerns they generate. First, the Gemara asserted that no one truly knows their lineage with certainty \u8212- we may all be descendants of {\i mamzerim} for all we know, and once a family\u8217's identity has become lost and intermixed, we leave it alone. Second, when the discussion turned to the {\i asufi} and the {\i shtuki} \u8212- individuals whose father, or both parents, are unknown, making them {\i safek mamzerim} \u8212- the Gemara proceeded to restrict even those categories dramatically.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Scope Minimization\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara established that the category of {\i asufi} applies only to a child left to die, and that any sign of care having been extended to an abandoned child removes it from that classification entirely. Similarly, the category of {\i shtuki} was subjected to careful delimitation. The overall trajectory of the Gemara, then, has been to reassure us that the population of individuals genuinely subject to these concerns is quite small.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava\u8217's Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Into this context came Rava\u8217's pivotal ruling: {\i mamzer vaday amra rachmana velo mamzer safek} \u8212- the Torah forbids a definite {\i mamzer} from entering the congregation, but a doubtful {\i mamzer} is not Biblically prohibited at all. One might have expected the Gemara to immediately add that rabbinic legislation nonetheless restricts the {\i safek mamzer}. Instead, the Gemara frames the stringency differently.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ma\u8217'alah Asu Beyuchsin\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rather than framing the concern as a rabbinic prohibition, the Gemara describes it as {\i ma\u8217'alah asu beyuchsin} \u8212- a higher standard that was adopted specifically in matters of marriage and lineage. This framing is significant: it suggests that the weight of the {\i mamzer} prohibition is not what is driving the concern, neither Biblically nor even rabbinically in the usual sense. It is a separate category of elevated standard. This has practical implications for questions about how vigorously one must investigate questionable lineage, and how much leniency remains available.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mishnah: Asurin Bazeh\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The most recent Mishnah \u8212- found at the top of 74a \u8212- states: {\i kol ha\u8217'asurin lavo bakahal mutarin lavo zeh bazeh}, that all those who cannot marry into the {\i kahal} \u8212- the paradigmatic case being the {\i mamzer}, the {\i nasin}, and the {\i safek mamzer} \u8212- may intermarry with one another. There followed a seemingly contradictory line from Rabbi Yehuda forbidding this. The Gemara spent much of yesterday\u8217's discussion untangling that debate, ultimately concluding that Rabbi Yehuda was not broadly forbidding intermarriage among those of disqualified status. Rather, his statement concerned converts of Ammonite, Moabite, or Edomite origin who, though barred from marrying into general {\i Klal Yisrael}, are permitted to marry a {\i mamzer}. Even Rabbi Yehuda, who forbids a {\i ger} from marrying a {\i mamzer} in the ordinary case, concedes that such a {\i ger} \u8212- who cannot marry into the general Jewish community \u8212- may marry a {\i mamzer}. Thus the principle holds: {\i mamzerim} and {\i safek mamzerim} may marry one another, and the {\i halacha} is that a {\i ger} may marry a {\i mamzer}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Eliezer\u8217's View\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 With that backdrop, the Gemara arrives at the position of Rabbi Eliezer, which presents a genuine and severe challenge for the {\i safek mamzer}. Rabbi Eliezer holds: {\i vaday bevaday mutar} \u8212- a definite {\i mamzer} may marry another definite {\i mamzer} \u8212- but a definite {\i mamzer} may not marry a {\i safek mamzer}, and a {\i safek mamzer} may not marry another {\i safek mamzer}. The logic follows rigorously from treating the doubt as a genuine {\i safek}: a {\i safek mamzer} might not be a {\i mamzer} at all, so marrying a definite {\i mamzer} is problematic; and two {\i safek mamzerim} cannot marry one another because one might be a {\i mamzer} while the other is not. One is immediately compelled to ask: whom, then, can a {\i safek mamzer} marry? The answer would be a {\i ger}. And according to the position that a {\i mamzer} cannot marry a {\i ger}, the answer becomes essentially no one. This is a stark and troubling conclusion when treated as a genuine {\i safek} and the normal rules of doubt are applied without relief. The approach of {\i ma\u8217'alah asu beyuchsin}, by contrast, offers more flexibility \u8212- the {\i safek} is not treated as a real {\i safek} in the ordinary legal sense, but rather as a separate elevated standard, allowing for greater leniency in practice.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Forbidden Relations\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Baraita: Forbidden Partn\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now introduces a {\i baraita}. The text, five lines from the bottom of 74b, three lines before the line becomes wide, begins: {\i tanu rabbanan}. The {\i baraita} enumerates a list of men \u8212- a {\i ger Amoni u-Moavi, Mitzri v\u8217'Edomi}, a {\i Kuti}, a {\i nasin}, a {\i challal}, and a {\i mamzer} \u8212- and states that any of these, being at least nine years old such that their act of intercourse is legally consequential, who had intercourse with a {\i kohenet}, a {\i leviyah}, or a {\i bat Yisrael}, have invalidated that woman \u8212- {\i pasluha}. This is because any such forbidden act of intercourse effectively transforms the woman into a {\i challalah} and renders her forbidden to a {\i kohen}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The converts listed \u8212- Ammonite, Moabite, Egyptian, Edomite \u8212- cannot marry into {\i Yisrael} and their intercourse with a Jewish woman is therefore forbidden. The {\i Kuti}, or Samaritan, represents a category we will return to shortly, whose status as a genuine convert is in question. The {\i nasin} also cannot marry into the general Jewish population. The {\i challal} is included because even though intercourse with him is not itself forbidden to a Jewish woman, it transforms her into a {\i challalah}. The {\i mamzer} rounds out the list \u8212- whether the {\i mamzerut} stems from {\i arayot} or from {\i eshet ish}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tanna Kamma\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Tanna Kamma} holds that any forbidden act of intercourse \u8212- {\i bi\u8217'ato ba\u8217'aveira} \u8212- invalidates the woman and renders her a {\i challalah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Yosi\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yosi qualifies this position: {\i kol she-zaro pasul posel, v\u8217'chol she-ein zaro pasul eino posel}. Not every forbidden act of intercourse transforms the woman into a {\i challalah} \u8212- only an act where the resulting child would be {\i pasul}, disqualified. If the intercourse is forbidden but the child\u8217's status is unaffected, the woman is not transformed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel frames his position differently: {\i kol she-ata nosei bito, ata nosei almanato} \u8212- whoever\u8217's daughter a {\i kohen} may marry, that {\i kohen} may also marry the widow of the man in question. If you cannot marry his daughter, you cannot marry his widow. The logic, of course, is that the marital scenario in which a widowed woman becomes available to a {\i kohen} would occur if her husband died. The statement thus sets up a criterion: the daughter\u8217's status governs the mother\u8217's.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mitzri Sheini Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What then is the practical difference between the {\i Tanna Kamma} and Rabbi Yosi? On the surface, every category listed \u8212- {\i Amoni}, {\i Moavi}, {\i Mitzri}, {\i Adomi}, {\i Kuti}, {\i nasin}, {\i challal}, {\i mamzer} \u8212- seems to produce children whose status is equally disqualified. The Gemara asks, {\i mai ika beinayhu}, and answers: Rabbi Yochanan identifies the case of a {\i Mitzri sheini}, a second-generation Egyptian convert. According to the Torah, {\i dor shelishi yavo lahem bikhal Hashem} \u8212- the third generation of Egyptian converts may marry into the congregation. So a second-generation Egyptian who has intercourse with a Jewish woman produces a third-generation child, who is entirely permitted. According to the {\i Tanna Kamma}, the act of intercourse was forbidden and so the woman becomes a {\i challalah} regardless. According to Rabbi Yosi, since the child is permitted \u8212- he is a {\i Mitzri shelishi} \u8212- the woman does not become a {\i challalah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kohen Gadol Ba\u8217'Almanah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Both Rabbi Yosi and the {\i Tanna Kamma} derive their respective positions from the same source: the verse concerning the {\i Kohen Gadol} who has intercourse with a widow, regarding which the Torah states {\i velo yechalel zaro be\u8217'amav}. The Gemara notes that the word {\i yechalel} \u8212- with its double {\i lamed} \u8212- implies two acts of {\i chillul}: the {\i Kohen Gadol} renders the woman a {\i challalah}, and the child becomes a {\i challal}. The child becomes a {\i challal} as a derivative consequence of the mother having been transformed into a {\i challalah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Three Position Analysis\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What is the {\i nafka mina} between these three positions? Schematically: the {\i Tanna Kamma} requires only that the intercourse was forbidden \u8212- {\i bi\u8217'ato ba\u8217'aveira} \u8212- to render the woman a {\i challalah}. Rabbi Yosi adds a second criterion: not only must the intercourse be forbidden, but the {\i other} status involved \u8212- be it {\i Mitzri}, {\i Adomi}, or {\i mamzer} \u8212- must pass to the child. If the status does not pass to the child, the woman is not a {\i challalah}, even if the act itself was forbidden. The {\i Mitzri sheini} case illustrates this: since the child emerges as a {\i Mitzri shelishi} and is permitted to marry into the congregation, there is no status passing to the child, and according to Rabbi Yosi the woman is not transformed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel introduces a third criterion: the disqualifying status must pass not merely to a child, but specifically to a daughter. The {\i nafka mina} between Rabbi Yosi and Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel is identified by Ulla: the case of a {\i ger Ammoni} or {\i Moavi} who has intercourse with a Jewish woman, producing a daughter. The verse teaches {\i Ammoni v\u8217'lo Ammonit, Moavi v\u8217'lo Moavit} \u8212- only the male Ammonites and Moabites are excluded from the congregation; female descendants are fully permitted to marry. Therefore: according to the {\i Tanna Kamma}, the act was forbidden, so the woman becomes a {\i challalah}. According to Rabbi Yosi, the status passes to the son, so the woman becomes a {\i challalah}. According to Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel, since the daughter is not disqualified, the woman is not a {\i challalah}. Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel requires that the paradigm from {\i Kohen Gadol be\u8217'almanah} be matched fully \u8212- and in that paradigm, {\i all} children, sons and daughters alike, become {\i chalalim}. Only when that full correspondence holds does the woman\u8217's status shift.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The three-way comparison may be stated thus: the {\i Tanna Kamma} requires forbidden intercourse alone. Rabbi Yosi adds that the disqualifying status must pass to any child. Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel adds further that it must pass to daughters as well as sons, requiring that the {\i kohen gadol be\u8217'almanah} paradigm be matched at every point \u8212- forbidden intercourse and universal transmission of status.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Derivative Safek & Kutim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Almanas Issa\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Chisda now introduces a point of consensus across all three positions: {\i hakol modim be\u8217'Almanas Issa she\u8217'psula le\u8217'kehuna} \u8212- everyone agrees that a widow of uncertain lineage, the {\i Almanas Issa}, is forbidden to {\i kehunah}. The case is that a woman had intercourse with a {\i safek challal} \u8212- a man whose status as a {\i challal} is doubtful. Even though the intercourse itself was not forbidden (a {\i challal} may marry any Jewish woman), the {\i challal} status, when it is definite, passes from the man to the woman. If the man is a {\i safek challal}, the woman becomes a {\i safek challalah}, and she is forbidden to a {\i kohen}. Rav Chisda observes that even Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel \u8212- the most lenient of the three {\i tannaim} \u8212- would agree to this ruling. His statement excluding certain cases from the prohibition comes to exclude specific categories, not the {\i Almanas Issa} case. That case, with its {\i safek challalah} status extending to the daughter as a {\i safek challalah}, falls squarely within the principle that if you cannot marry the daughter, you cannot marry the widow.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sefek Sefeka\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then cites a {\i Mishnah}: {\i he\u8217'id Rabbi Yehoshua ve\u8217'Rabbi Yehuda ben Beteira al Almanas Issa she\u8217'kesheira le\u8217'kehuna} \u8212- Rabbi Yehoshua and Rabbi Yehuda ben Beteira testified that the widow of an {\i issa} is permitted to marry a {\i kohen}. The case, as the Gemara explains, is that a woman was married to a {\i safek challal}, and upon his death she is a widow who is now a {\i safek challalah}. Why would these authorities permit her to a {\i kohen}? The Gemara\u8217's answer is {\i havai sefek sefeka ve\u8217'sefek sefeka le\u8217'kula} \u8212- it is a double doubt, and a compound doubt is resolved leniently.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 As Rashi notes, this is not technically a {\i sefek sefeka} in the classic sense \u8212- there is really only one underlying question, namely whether her husband was a {\i challal}. If he was, she becomes a {\i challalah} and her daughter becomes a {\i challalah}. But because the second-order status \u8212- her own {\i challalah} status \u8212- is a derivative of the first-order doubt, and because applying that first-order doubt one degree removed does produce what has the character of a compound or diluted doubt, the authorities here are lenient. One might call it not a doubled doubt but a {\i derivative} doubt \u8212- a doubtful status transmitted from a doubtful status. According to the opinion of Rabbi Yehoshua and Rabbi Yehuda ben Beteira, that derivativeness is sufficient to permit her.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This brief excursus into the laws of {\i chalalut} represents something of a detour \u8212- the focus is on {\i kehunah} concerns rather than the broader {\i yichus} questions \u8212- but both belong to the larger fabric of lineage {\i halacha}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Returning now to Rabbi Eliezer\u8217's position, which creates a genuine difficulty for the {\i safek mamzer}: unlike the broader Gemara which uses the framework of {\i ma\u8217'alah asu beyuchsin} to soften the concern, Rabbi Eliezer treats the {\i safek mamzer} as a genuine {\i safek} with full legal consequences. As noted, Rav Yehuda reports in the name of Rav: {\i halacha k\u8217'Rabbi Eliezer}. When this ruling was brought before Shmuel, Shmuel responded by citing Hillel\u8217's tradition: {\i asarah yuchsin alu miBavel ve-chulam mutarin lavo zeh bazeh} \u8212- ten categories of {\i yichus} came up from Babylonia, and they are all permitted to intermarry with one another. Rashi clarifies that this means the {\i pesulim} among them may intermarry. The point is that ruling like Rabbi Eliezer creates a new stringency where a {\i safek mamzer} suddenly cannot marry a {\i vaday mamzer}, undercutting this established tradition.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then notes an internal contradiction \u8212- {\i rami deRav aDeRav ve-rami deShmuel aDeShmuel} \u8212- both Rav and Shmuel appear to contradict themselves. The contradiction arises from a separate case: a woman who was betrothed ({\i arusah}) \u8212- living in her father\u8217's home, with no marital cohabitation yet having taken place \u8212- who became pregnant. The question is the status of the child. On one reading, Rav says the child is a {\i mamzer} and Shmuel says the child is a {\i shtuki}. But if that is their positions, they conflict with what each said previously about whether a {\i safek mamzer} may marry another {\i mamzer}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara resolves this by reversing their positions: {\i ipoch} \u8212- Rav says the child is a {\i shtuki} (a {\i safek mamzer} who therefore cannot marry a {\i mamzeret}), and Shmuel says the child is a {\i mamzer} (meaning, by that term, a definite enough status that we permit him to marry a {\i mamzeret}). Now both authorities are internally consistent: Rav holds that a {\i safek mamzer} cannot marry a {\i vaday mamzer}, and Shmuel holds that even a {\i safek mamzer} may. The oral nature of this tradition \u8212- {\i amar}, he said \u8212- makes a reversal of attribution entirely plausible.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara asks: {\i tarti lamah li} \u8212- why do we need both cases? Could one case not have taught the other? The answer is {\i tzricha}: each case contains a unique argument that might have led us to think the authority in question would rule differently in that scenario. Regarding the case of the {\i safek mamzer} generally \u8212- a child born to a single woman whose father is unknown \u8212- Rav would say the child cannot marry a {\i mamzeret}, because most men around such a woman are {\i kesherim}, making it quite likely the child is also {\i kasher}, and thus a genuine {\i safek} as to the child\u8217's {\i mamzer} status prevents marriage to a {\i mamzeret}. One might have thought Rav would be lenient in the case of the betrothed woman, since there is a particular man \u8212- her betrothed \u8212- to whom we can attribute paternity, and the possibility that it is his legitimate child makes the {\i safek} significant. But the Gemara teaches that even there, Rav insists on the stringency. Conversely, Shmuel\u8217's leniency in permitting the {\i safek mamzer} to marry a {\i mamzeret} might seem inapplicable in the case of the {\i arusah}, since the vast majority of men in the world \u8212- {\i rov pesulim atzlah} \u8212- would make the child a {\i mamzer}; only the betrothed husband would not. One might have thought Shmuel would be stringent there. But the Gemara teaches he is lenient even in that case.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then offers an alternative reading \u8212- {\i ve-iba\u8217'it eima lo teipoch} \u8212- in which the positions are not reversed at all. On this reading, both Rav and Shmuel agree the child of the {\i arusah} is a {\i safek mamzer} who cannot marry a {\i Yisrael}. The debate between them concerns a different question entirely. Rav says {\i mamzer} simply to convey that the child is forbidden to marry a {\i bat Yisrael}. Shmuel calls him a {\i shtuki}, not meaning something different about his {\i mamzer} status, but adding a separate point: {\i she\u8217'meshatkin oto mi\u8217'nichsei aviv} \u8212- the child is silenced from claiming any portion in his putative father\u8217's estate. Even if the child seized some of the property before the matter was adjudicated, he would have no rightful claim, because we do not know that the betrothed husband is his father at all.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A third possibility: Shmuel calls him a {\i shtuki} because the case calls for investigation \u8212- {\i beduki}. The mother should be asked, and if she says {\i le\u8217'kasher nivalti} \u8212- that the intercourse was with a permitted man, her betrothed \u8212- she is believed, in accordance with the ruling of Rabban Gamliel in {\i Ketubot} that such a woman\u8217's testimony about herself is credible. Shmuel had already ruled {\i halacha k\u8217'Rabban Gamliel} in a parallel case involving a single pregnant woman who identified the father. The Gemara notes that this ruling needs to be stated in both contexts, since in the case of the betrothed woman most men who could have been the father would produce a {\i mamzer} child, and one might have thought the mother\u8217's testimony would not be believed there.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Regarding the {\i halacha}: Tosafot states that we rule like Rabbi Eliezer, since Rav rules accordingly and we follow Rav over Shmuel in ritual prohibitions. The Rambam and the {\i Shulchan Aruch} concur. The ruling is therefore harsh: a {\i safek mamzer} may not marry a {\i vaday Yisrael}, may not marry a {\i vaday mamzer}, and may not marry another {\i safek mamzer} \u8212- since in the last case, one might be a {\i mamzer} and the other might not. The only remaining permissible partner for a {\i safek mamzer} is a {\i ger}. This is a severe outcome for anyone who actually falls within that category. The practical impact is mitigated, however, by the narrowing of the categories themselves: the trust given to a mother\u8217's testimony removes many cases from uncertainty, and the restricted definition of {\i asufi} removes many others. So while the ruling for a genuine {\i safek mamzer} is indeed austere, the scope of who actually falls into that category has been substantially diminished.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kutim Lo Yisa Kutit\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now introduces a new matter. The text states: {\i v\u8217'chein Rabbi Eliezer v\u8217'chein Rabbi Elazar omer Kuti lo yisa Kutit} \u8212- a Samaritan may not marry a Samaritan woman. This is striking: not only is a {\i Kuti} restricted from marrying into the general Jewish population, but apparently {\i Kutim} cannot marry one another either. The immediate question, of course, is whom a {\i Kuti} is supposed to marry. We will need to understand the status of the {\i Kutim} before we can understand why that restriction applies.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ger Achar Asara Dorot\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Yosef explains: {\i asa\u8217'uha k\u8217'ger l\u8217'achar asara dorot} \u8212- the Rabbis treated the {\i Kutim} as having the status of a {\i ger} after ten generations. This is based on a {\i baraita}: {\i ger ad asara dorot mutar b\u8217'mamzeret mikan ve\u8217'eilach assur b\u8217'mamzeret} \u8212- a {\i ger} within ten generations of his conversion may marry a {\i mamzeret}, but beyond that he may not. Some say the criterion is not a precise count of ten generations but rather until the name of idolatry \u8212- the non-Jewish identity \u8212- is forgotten from him. The Rambam understands this to mean until the fact that he descends from converts is forgotten.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This ruling contains several important {\i halachot}. First, it establishes that not only the {\i ger} himself but descendants of {\i gerim} retain the status of {\i ger} for the purposes of marriage to a {\i mamzer}. Second \u8212- and this is the critical implication for the {\i Kutim} case \u8212- once enough generations have passed and the {\i ger} identity is effectively forgotten, the individual can no longer benefit from the {\i kula} of {\i ger} status. At that point he must be treated as a {\i Yisrael le\u8217'chumra}, and a {\i Yisrael} may not marry a {\i mamzer}. One might therefore expect that if the {\i Kutim} have been around for so many generations, their {\i ger} status is effectively forgotten, and they should be treated as {\i Yisrael} \u8212- which would explain why they cannot marry {\i mamzerim}. But this still does not explain why they cannot marry {\i one another}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One line of reasoning runs as follows: if we assume that {\i Kutim} are {\i safek mamzerim} \u8212- because of concerns about their adherence to the laws of {\i gittin v\u8217'kiddushin} \u8212- then Rabbi Eliezer\u8217's rule that a {\i safek mamzer} cannot marry another {\i safek mamzer} would explain the prohibition. The problem with this reasoning, as the Gemara points out, is that even {\i safek mamzerim} may marry a {\i ger}, and if all {\i Kutim} are descendants of {\i gerim}, then any {\i Kuti} marrying another {\i Kuti} is marrying a {\i ger} \u8212- which should be perfectly permissible for a {\i safek mamzer}. Rav Yosef\u8217's answer is that because of the passage of so many generations, the {\i kula} of {\i ger} status no longer applies, and so they cannot rely on the {\i ger} dispensation. Their {\i gerus} is old enough that it is no longer operative for leniency, but their {\i kuti} identity is not forgotten enough to treat them as pure {\i Yisrael}. This asymmetric situation is precisely what prohibits {\i Kuti} from marrying {\i Kutit}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Abaye\u8217's Challenge\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye challenges Rav Yosef: {\i mi dami} \u8212- how can you compare these cases? In the case of the {\i ger achar asara dorot}, the problem is clear: {\i ger yashan u\u8217'mamzeret chadasha} \u8212- you have an old {\i ger} whose {\i ger} identity has faded into the past, and a {\i mamzeret} whose {\i mamzer} identity is current and well-known. People will see what appears to be a {\i bar Yisrael} marrying a {\i mamzeret}, which is a visible violation that cannot be tolerated.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But by the {\i Kutim}, argues Abaye, the situation is symmetrical: {\i idi v\u8217'idi ke\u8217'haddi hanu} \u8212- both elements travel together. The {\i kuti} label carries with it both the {\i ger} identity and whatever {\i mamzer} concern attaches to that group. If you say so many generations have passed that the {\i ger} label is forgotten and therefore we cannot apply the {\i kula}, then for the same reason the {\i mamzer} concern associated with being a {\i kuti} should also be forgotten. You cannot selectively apply the stringency of the forgotten {\i ger} status while retaining the stringency of an unresolved {\i mamzer} concern. If the identity is old enough to lose the {\i kula}, it should be old enough to lose the {\i chumra} as well. This challenge leaves the Gemara without a satisfying resolution to the question of why {\i Kutim} cannot marry one another under Rav Yosef\u8217's framework.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Kutim Status Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gerei Arayot\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 When Rav Dimi came from Eretz Yisrael, he reported a different tradition: Rabbi Eliezer holds that {\i Kutim gerei arayot hein} \u8212- the Samaritans are converts motivated by lions, meaning they converted not out of sincere conviction but out of fear of the lions that were ravaging the land (as described in the Book of Kings). Such a conversion is not genuine, and therefore {\i Kutim} are to be treated as non-Jews. This position is attributed to Rabbi Yishmael. With this framing, the prohibition on {\i Kutim} marrying one another begins to take on a different character: if they are non-Jews, the concern is not about {\i mamzer} status in the internal sense, but about the intermingling of Jews and non-Jews among the {\i Kuti} population.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Akiva\u8217's View\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now introduces a second principle that Rabbi Eliezer combines with the above: {\i Rabbi Yishmael savar lah k\u8217'Rabbi Akiva de\u8217'amar oved kochavim ve\u8217'eved ha\u8217'ba al bat Yisrael havlad mamzer}. Rabbi Akiva holds that when a non-Jew or slave has intercourse with a Jewish woman, the child is a {\i mamzer}. If we accept both that (a) {\i Kutim} are non-Jews, and (b) Jewish women intermarried with {\i Kutim}, then the children born of those unions are {\i mamzerim} according to Rabbi Akiva. This explains the concern: the {\i Kuti} population consists of non-Jews intermixed with Jewish {\i mamzerim}, and because of that mixture, {\i Kutim} cannot marry one another \u8212- a Jew among them would be marrying a non-Jew, and a Jewish {\i mamzer} would be marrying a non-{\i mamzer} or non-Jew.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Matrilineal Descent\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 At this point the Gemara opens a parenthetical of startling significance. If the child of a Jewish woman and a non-Jewish man would be a {\i mamzer} according to Rabbi Akiva \u8212- rather than the {\i halacha} as we know it, which says the child follows the mother and is simply a Jew \u8212- then one might ask: what does our mainstream tradition say about such a child? The Gemara says the child is {\i kasher}. But one reading among the {\i Ba\u8217'alei HaTosafot} interprets {\i kasher} not to mean \u8220"Jewish\u8221" but rather \u8220"non-{\i mamzer}.\u8221" On this reading, the child of a non-Jewish father and a Jewish mother is a non-Jew who is free of {\i mamzer} status, meaning he has the capacity to convert and thereby become {\i kasher} \u8212- better than a {\i mamzer} who is permanently disqualified, but not a Jew by birth.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This reading, if accepted, would imply that matrilineal descent \u8212- the principle that any child of a Jewish mother is Jewish regardless of the father\u8217's origin \u8212- is not universally accepted in the Talmudic discussion. Rather, on this one reading, Jewish status requires both parents to be Jewish. The child of a non-Jewish father would be either a {\i mamzer} (Rabbi Akiva\u8217's view) or a non-Jew (the mainstream view on this reading). The importance of mentioning this reading is that it becomes relevant to the {\i Kuti} question: if we say {\i Kutim} are non-Jews and the children of Jewish mothers with {\i Kutim} are not {\i mamzerim} but simply non-Jews, then the {\i Kuti} population is Jews mixed with non-Jews \u8212- which is still a problem, but not because of {\i mamzerut}. This is the direction that explains why we need Rabbi Akiva\u8217's position: the only way the children of {\i Kutim} with Jewish women are {\i mamzerim} \u8212- as opposed to merely non-Jews \u8212- is to follow Rabbi Akiva. And the only way invoking {\i mamzerut} matters to the {\i Kutim} problem is if you also think the alternative is Jewish status via matrilineal descent.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Needless to say, this reading of a {\i Rishon} is not normative, and we do not rule that way. The concept of matrilineal descent is bedrock {\i halacha}. But the discussion surfaces here in the Gemara\u8217's attempt to account for Rabbi Eliezer\u8217's position on {\i Kutim}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Position Alignment\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now asks: does Rabbi Yishmael really hold like Rabbi Akiva on this point? The challenge comes from a ruling of Rabbi Yochanan in the name of Rabbi Yishmael: {\i minaayin l\u8217'oved kochavim ve\u8217'eved sheba al hakohenet ve\u8217'al haleviyah ve\u8217'al bat Yisrael she\u8217'pasluha} \u8212- how do we know that a non-Jew or slave who had intercourse with a Jewish woman invalidated her for marriage to a {\i kohen}? The answer is derived from a verse: {\i uvas kohen ki tihyeh almanah ugerusha ve\u8217'zera ein lah} \u8212- the daughter of a {\i kohen} who is widowed or divorced with no children returns to her father\u8217's house. The Gemara derives: she returns to her father\u8217's house only if she was widowed or divorced from a man with whom {\i almanut} and {\i gerushin} \u8212- widowhood and divorce \u8212- are halachically possible. {\i Yatza oved kochavim ve\u8217'eved} \u8212- excluded is a non-Jew or slave, with whom no valid {\i kiddushin} can occur and hence no {\i almanut} or {\i gerushin}. Therefore, intercourse with such a man invalidates her.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara points out the contradiction: if Rabbi Yishmael held like Rabbi Akiva that the child of a non-Jewish man and a Jewish woman is a {\i mamzer}, no {\i drasha} would be needed to derive that the mother is invalidated. Of course a mother whose intercourse produced a {\i mamzer} is invalidated. The very fact that Rabbi Yishmael needs the {\i pasuk} to derive her invalidation demonstrates that he does {\i not} hold the child is a {\i mamzer}. Clearly Rabbi Yishmael did not accept Rabbi Akiva\u8217's position that the child is a {\i mamzer}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ervah Requirement\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara therefore reframes the claim: it is not Rabbi Yishmael but Rabbi Eliezer himself who holds both positions simultaneously \u8212- {\i savar lah k\u8217'Rabbi Yishmael de\u8217'amar Kutim gerei arayot} and {\i savar k\u8217'Rabbi Akiva de\u8217'amar oved kochavim ve\u8217'eved ha\u8217'ba al bat Yisrael ha\u8217'velad mamzer}. Rabbi Eliezer holds that {\i Kutim} are non-Jews and also that children of non-Jewish men and Jewish women are {\i mamzerim}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But the Gemara immediately challenges this: how can Rabbi Eliezer hold like Rabbi Akiva on {\i mamzerut} from a non-Jewish father? The Gemara cites Rabbi Eliezer\u8217's own ruling elsewhere: {\i af al pi she-necheleku Beit Shammai u-Veit Hillel be\u8217'tzarot} \u8212- even in the famous disputes between Beit Shammai and Beit Hillel regarding co-wives and the obligation of {\i yibbum} or {\i chalitzah} \u8212- despite all those disputes which might theoretically put some children\u8217's status in question \u8212- {\i modim be\u8217'elu she-ein mamzer ela mi she-issuro issur ervah ve\u8217'anush karet}. A child is not a {\i mamzer} unless the prohibition violated was an {\i issur ervah} punishable by {\i karet}. A mere {\i lav} \u8212- a simple prohibition without {\i karet} \u8212- does not produce a {\i mamzer}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is the crucial point. The prohibition on a non-Jewish man having intercourse with a Jewish woman is not an {\i issur karet}. It is not an {\i ervah} in the Talmudic sense. Therefore, according to Rabbi Eliezer\u8217's own principle, the child of such a union would {\i not} be a {\i mamzer}. Rabbi Eliezer cannot hold both that {\i mamzerut} requires an {\i issur karet} and that the child of a non-Jewish father and Jewish mother is a {\i mamzer} \u8212- these positions are incompatible.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Unresolved Question\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This leaves the Gemara \u8212- and us \u8212- without a satisfying account of why, according to Rabbi Eliezer, {\i Kutim} cannot marry one another. The first explanation \u8212- that they are {\i safek mamzerim} who cannot benefit from {\i ger} status after so many generations \u8212- was challenged by Abaye\u8217's objection that you cannot selectively discard the {\i ger kula} while retaining the {\i mamzer chumra}. The second explanation \u8212- that they are non-Jews mixed with Jewish {\i mamzerim} \u8212- collapses because Rabbi Eliezer himself holds that the child of a non-Jewish father and Jewish mother is not a {\i mamzer}, since that intercourse is not an {\i issur karet}. So if {\i Kutim} are non-Jews and their children with Jewish women are not {\i mamzerim}, the concern is simply one of Jews intermingled with non-Jews \u8212- a serious concern, but one that would explain a prohibition on {\i Kutim} marrying Jews, not necessarily on {\i Kutim} marrying one another.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara ends here with the question unresolved: what exactly is Rabbi Eliezer\u8217's reason for prohibiting a {\i Kuti} from marrying a {\i Kutit}? We will return to find the answer tomorrow.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Kiddushin 76\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Kutim Marriage Prohibiti\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Three Tradition Versions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The sugya opens on 76b still working to understand the {\i braisa} of Rabbi Eliezer \u8212- or, more precisely, the baraita associated with his school \u8212- and the Gemara introduces another possibility through a chain of transmission: {\i ki ata Ravin amar Rabbi Chiya bar Abba amar Rabbi Yochanan}, with two alternative versions of the chain ({\i ve\u8217'amri lah amar Rabbi Abba bar Zavda amar Rabbi Chanina}; {\i ve\u8217'amri lah amar Rabbi Yaakov bar Idi amar Rabbi Yehoshua ben Levi}). Three possible chains of transmission for one tradition. The {\i davar} that the tradition actually addresses is itself a subject of {\i machlokes Rishonim}: whether it concerns the {\i issur} for {\i Kutim} to marry Jews, or specifically {\i Kuti beKutit} \u8212- a {\i Kuti} marrying another {\i Kuti}. The Ritva brings both possibilities and explains how all three opinions might be read as bearing on {\i Kuti beKutit}, which is the {\i braisa} originally under discussion. The explanation he ultimately accepts \u8212- and the more likely reading \u8212- is that the first two opinions concern {\i Kuti beYisrael}, while only the third opinion addresses {\i Kuti beKutit}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Yishmael: Invalid Con\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The first opinion in this tradition is that of Rabbi Yishmael: {\i Kutim gerei arayos hein} \u8212- the Samaritans are invalid converts, their conversion did not take. The Ramban has much to say about this, though a full treatment belongs in {\i Yevamos}, where we hold that {\i gerei arayos} are in fact valid {\i geirim}. Why {\i gerei arayos} appears here as a defective category is a question the Ramban wrestles with at length.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Disqualified Priests\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The tradition also addresses {\i ha-Kohanim she-nitme\u8217'u bahen} \u8212- the {\i Kohanim} who lived and became intermingled among the Samaritans. According to Rabbi Yishmael, {\i Kohanim psulim hayu}, they were disqualified priests, as derived from the verse {\i vayaasu lahem miktzosam Kohanei bamos} (they made for themselves priests of the high places from among their number). Rabbah bar bar Chana in the name of Rabbi Yochanan explains the {\i drasha}: {\i miktzosam} is read as a play on {\i kotz} (thorn), yielding {\i min ha-kotzim sheba\u8217'am} \u8212- the corrupt elements of the nation. {\i Umeshum hachi paslinhu} \u8212- that is why they are {\i pasul}. This was not the only context in which priests maintained {\i bamot}; Sefer Melachim records such practices widely. But according to {\i Chazal}, doing so in an official capacity was already prohibited at that point.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Akiva: Valid Converts\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Akiva represents the second opinion: {\i Kutim gerei emes hein} \u8212- the Samaritans actually underwent a valid conversion. And {\i ha-Kohanim she-nitme\u8217'u bahen Kohanim kesherim hayu} \u8212- the {\i Kohanim} who dwelt among them were good, valid {\i Kohanim}. The same verse ({\i vayaasu lahem miktzosam Kohanei bamos}) is cited, but Rabbah bar bar Chana in the name of Rabbi Yochanan reads it differently: {\i min ha-bechirim sheba\u8217'am} \u8212- from the finest of the nation, not the thorns. So {\i miktzosam} is read as {\i miktzas} \u8212- a portion, the choice ones. The {\i bamot} themselves did not {\i posul} the {\i Kohanim} in either reading; the question is only how the verse describes them.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Yet if the Samaritans are valid converts and the {\i Kohanim} among them were fit, {\i ve\u8217'ela mipnei mah asrum} \u8212- why are they forbidden in marriage? The answer: {\i mipnei she-hayu meyabmim es ha-arusos u-fotrim es ha-nesuos} \u8212- because they would perform {\i yibum} with a woman who had only been {\i arusah} (betrothed) and release from {\i yibum} a woman who had been fully married through {\i nisuin} \u8212- the opposite of normative halakha.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rashi already notes at the top of 76a: {\i u-fotrim es ha-nesuos min ha-chalitza}, and observes that {\i u-le-Rabbi Akiva bnei chayavei lavin mamzeirim heim} \u8212- according to Rabbi Akiva\u8217's well-known {\i shita} that a forbidden union arising from an {\i issur lav} (as distinct from an {\i issur kares}) produces a {\i mamzer}, this aberrant {\i yibum} practice generates {\i mamzeirim}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kuti Yibum Practices\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i drasha} the {\i Kutim} used to arrive at this unusual practice: they derived it from the {\i pasuk} in the Torah \u8212- {\i ki yeshvu achim yachdav u-mes echad mehem u-ven ein lo, lo sihiyeh eshes ha-mes ha-chutza l\u8217'ish zar, yevamah yavo aleha} (Devarim 25:5). The {\i Kutim} focused on the phrase {\i lo sihiyeh eshes ha-mes ha-chutza l\u8217'ish zar} \u8212- she who sat outside (i.e., who had not yet entered the marital home) should not go to a stranger: {\i heicha de-nafka chutzah hi, lo sihiyeh l\u8217'ish zar} \u8212- she who went out, who was only {\i arusah}, requires {\i yibum}. But {\i aval hacha de-lo yatva chutzah} \u8212- the woman who did not sit outside, who was fully married, {\i tihyeh l\u8217'ish zar} \u8212- she may go to another man, she needs no {\i yibum}. This is the inverse of what we hold. And it is halakhically problematic, particularly given Rabbi Akiva\u8217's position that relations in violation of a {\i lav} produce {\i mamzerim}, which is what grounds the {\i pesul} in this case.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Third Opinion: Halakhic\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Ve-yesh omrim} \u8212- the third opinion in these three traditions \u8212- gives a different explanation: {\i lefi she-ein beki\u8217'in be-dikdukei mitzvos} \u8212- that the Samaritans are not careful about the details of the commandments. This formulation initially strikes one as unusually broad; after all, many people are not expert in the details of every {\i mitzvah} without being thereby considered {\i pesulim}. The specific content of what they are not {\i baki} in will require further clarification.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Elazar & Matzah Proof\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara asks: who is this anonymous {\i yesh omrim}? {\i Amar Rav Iddi bar Avin \u8212- ve-Rabbi Elazar hi}. The text before us actually reads {\i Rabbi Eliezer}, which is the reading preserved through the Maharshal. The Rashash \u8212- Rabbi Shmuel Strashun \u8212- emends the text to {\i Rabbi Elazar}, which makes better sense in this {\i sugya}; the Oz VeHadar edition notes this variant on the margin.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The proof: {\i de-tanya, matzat Kutim muteret, ve-adam yotzei bah yedei chovato be-Pesach} \u8212- a {\i braisa} teaches that {\i matzah} made by a Samaritan is permitted on {\i Pesach} and one fulfills one\u8217's obligation with it, even though other items may be permitted on {\i Pesach} without enabling {\i yetzias yedei chovah}. {\i Ve-Rabbi Elazar oser} \u8212- Rabbi Elazar forbids it. The {\i braisa} explains his reasoning: {\i lefi she-ein beki\u8217'in be-dikdukei mitzvos}. This is the same formulation as the {\i yesh omrim}, identifying Rabbi Elazar as its author. Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel then adds: {\i kol mitzvah she-hecheziku bah Kutim harbeh medakdekim bah yoter mi-Yisrael} \u8212- any commandment they took upon themselves, they observed with greater care than Jews did. A striking observation, and arguably true of certain kinds of religious dedication even today.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then asks: {\i ve-la-hacha, mai ein beki\u8217'in?} What specifically are the {\i Kutim} not expert in that creates a {\i pesul} for {\i Kuti be-Kutit}? Knowing or not knowing the laws of {\i matzah} says nothing about their {\i yichus}. The answer: {\i lefi she-ein beki\u8217'in be-torat kiddushin ve-geirushin} \u8212- they do not know the laws of marriage and divorce properly. They may think someone was properly divorced when no valid {\i get} was given, or that a marriage did not require a {\i get}, producing {\i safek mamzerut} in subsequent generations. Rabbi Elazar already holds that {\i safek be-safek} \u8212- multiple layers of doubt \u8212- is a serious problem, which explains why he would rule that even {\i Kuti be-Kuti} is prohibited.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might wonder why a tradition about {\i dikdukei mitzvos} refers only to the specific domain of {\i ishus}. It would perhaps have been more natural for the tradition to say {\i she-ein yodim tiv gittin ve-kiddushin}. But the text transmits what it transmits \u8212- this is clearly a widely-circulated tradition, as evidenced by its three separate chains of transmission \u8212- and the Gemara explains it as applying specifically to the realm of {\i ishus}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kiddushin & Gittin Issues\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 In summary, the three opinions in the tradition concerning the {\i Kutim} reflect three distinct roots of the {\i pesul}: according to Rabbi Yishmael, they are {\i gerei arayos} and their conversion was never valid; according to Rabbi Akiva, they are valid converts but their aberrant {\i yibum} practices generate {\i mamzerim} under his {\i shita}; and according to the {\i yesh omrim} identified as Rabbi Elazar, they lack mastery of the laws of {\i kiddushin ve-geirushin}, producing {\i safek mamzerim} across generations.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Mamzer & Slave Causes\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mamzer Admixture\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Beyond the three opinions in the tradition, the Gemara presents two additional {\i Amoraic} explanations for the {\i Kutim}\u8217's disqualified status. {\i Amar Rav Nachman amar Rabbah bar Avuha}: {\i mamzer mei-achoso u-mamzer mei-eishes ish nit\u8217'arvu bahen} \u8212- a {\i mamzer} born from incest and a {\i mamzer} born from adultery became mixed into the Samaritan community, creating {\i mamzer} contamination throughout.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Why Both Mamzer Types\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara asks: {\i mai kamashma lan} \u8212- what is this {\i Amora} teaching us? Perhaps he intends to establish that {\i mamzerut} arises from {\i chayavei krisos} (rather than from {\i chayavei lavin} or {\i misas beis din}). If so, {\i lama li tarti} \u8212- why mention both? Why both the product of incest ({\i achoso}) and the product of adultery ({\i eishes ish})? One such case would suffice to establish the principle. The answer: {\i ma\u8217'aseh she-hayah kach hayah} \u8212- this is historical fact. Both actually occurred. A product of incest and a product of adultery each independently became part of the Samaritan community, and therefore both are stated. History is not reshaped for the sake of logical economy.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Slave Admixture\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Ve-Rava amar}: a different explanation \u8212- {\i eved ve-shifchah nit\u8217'arvu bahen} \u8212- a male slave and a female slave became intermingled with the Samaritan community. {\i Issura mishum mai? Mishum shifchah} \u8212- the core halakhic problem is the {\i shifchah}: a free person who has a child with a female slave produces offspring who appear free but are not, generating {\i mamzerut}-type complications downstream. {\i Lama li tarti} \u8212- again, why mention both the {\i eved} and the {\i shifchah} if only the {\i shifchah} creates the {\i issur}? {\i Ma\u8217'aseh she-hayah kach hayah} \u8212- same answer. Both events actually occurred in this community. Historical fact stands as reported.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Bedika for Priestly Marr\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Bedika Rationale\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 With the Samaritan {\i sugya} concluded, the Gemara introduces a new {\i Mishnah} dealing with {\i bedika} \u8212- genealogical investigation \u8212- which is no longer practiced in the form described here. We do have {\i chezkat kashrut} as a baseline, and the {\i shitas chachamim} that emerges in the {\i sugya} holds that everyone stands in that presumption. The rationale for the {\i bedika} requirement as described in the {\i Mishnah} is cited in the Ritva\u8217's name from the Yerushalmi, brought by the Ramban: {\i zo knas she-kansu} \u8212- this is an enactment imposed as a kind of {\i knas}, a penalty, {\i kedei she-yehei adam davek be-shivto u-ve-mishpachto} \u8212- so that a person would cleave to his tribe and to his family. The Ritva explains: {\i be-din hu she-lo yehu tzrichin bedika klal} \u8212- logically, no one should need to investigate {\i yichus} issues at all, neither {\i psulei kehunah} nor {\i psulei mamzerut}. But {\i Chazal} enacted the requirement to encourage closeness to one\u8217's defined family and tribal community.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot disagree with this framing. For the Yerushalmi\u8217's explanation to work, the {\i Mishnah} must address both {\i psulei kehunah} and {\i psulei kahal} (i.e., {\i mamzerut}) \u8212- which is how the Ramban and his students understood it. Tosafot maintain that the {\i Mishnah} concerns only {\i psulei kehunah}, and they explain the entire {\i sugya} along different lines, as we shall see.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Presumption & Investigat\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The baseline presumption underlying all of this is {\i chezkat kashrut} \u8212- everyone stands in the presumption of fitness. The Rambam rules accordingly in {\i Issurei Biah} 19: {\i kol mishpachot be-chezkat kashrut hen omdot, mutar li-sa mehem le-chatchila} \u8212- one may marry from any family without prior investigation. The requirement to investigate is triggered only when a {\i ir\u8217'ur} \u8212- a challenge \u8212- has been raised. Specifically, the Rambam rules based on the Yerushalmi that if two witnesses testify that a {\i mamzer} or {\i chalal} or someone with {\i avdut} status has become intermixed in a particular family ({\i mishpacha kotzim}), that family becomes suspect and one may not marry from it until completing the {\i bedika} of the four mothers, which is eight ancestors.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Four Mothers Requirement\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Mishnah} states: {\i ha-nose isha Kehenet tzarich livdok achareha arba imahot she-hein shemonah} \u8212- one who marries a {\i Kohenet} must investigate four mothers, which are eight. The structure of this requirement traces back through four distinct maternal-and-maternal-grandfather pairs. Beginning from the woman herself: her mother and her mother\u8217's mother ({\i ima ve-em ima}); her maternal grandfather\u8217's mother ({\i em avi ima} and {\i ima} of that line); her father\u8217's mother ({\i em aviha ve-ima}); and her paternal grandfather\u8217's mother ({\i em avi aviha ve-ima}). Four pairs \u8212- {\i arba she-hein shemonah}. The investigative burden this would impose is staggering by any modern standard; the disruption of European Jewish record-keeping in the twentieth century alone would render such investigation impossible for virtually everyone.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Leviya & Yisraelit\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Mishnah} continues: {\i Leviyah ve-Yisraelit mosifin aleihen od achat} \u8212- for a {\i Leviyah} or an ordinary Israelite woman, one adds yet another unit of investigation beyond the baseline. The {\i Mishnah} has two textual traditions: {\i Leviyah ve-Yisraelit} (as in Rashi and Tosafot) and {\i Leviyim ve-Yisraelim} (as in the Sephardic texts), and this variant drives the underlying dispute about what the {\i Mishnah} addresses. On the Tosafot reading ({\i Leviyah ve-Yisraelit}), the added investigation reflects the fact that non-{\i Kohen} families are less careful about genealogical record-keeping that bears on {\i kehunah} fitness; a {\i Kohen} marrying a {\i Leviyah} or {\i Yisraelit} must therefore check further to ensure his children will not be {\i psulim le-kehunah}. On the Sephardic reading ({\i Leviyim ve-Yisraelim}), the {\i Mishnah} extends to {\i psulei kahal} as well \u8212- {\i mamzerut} itself \u8212- and the added requirement reflects the historically lesser care that non-priestly families took with their {\i yichus}: {\i de-chein sagi yoter be-levi\u8217'im ve-yisre\u8217'elim min ha-Kohanim, lefi she-ein medakdekim kol kach be-yichusan ve-einam medabkim be-mishpachoteihem}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Exceptions to Bedik (III)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Temple Service Exemptions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Mishnah} then lists categories that exempt one from further investigation: {\i ein bodkin lo min ha-mizbe\u8217'ach u-lema\u8217'lah} \u8212- one need not investigate beyond a {\i Kohen} who performed {\i avodah} at the altar; {\i ve-lo min ha-duchan u-lema\u8217'lah} \u8212- nor beyond a {\i Levi} who served on the {\i duchan}; {\i ve-lo min ha-Sanhedrin u-lema\u8217'lah} \u8212- nor beyond a member of a {\i Sanhedrin}. The Gemara explains each: a {\i Kohen} who served at the {\i mizbe\u8217'ach} \u8212- {\i i lav de-badkuhu, lo havu maskei lei} \u8212- had the {\i Kohen} not been checked first, he would never have been permitted to ascend to begin with. The same logic applies to the {\i duchan}: {\i de-amar mar she-sham hayu yoshvim meyuchasei kehunah u-meyuchasei leviyah} \u8212- only those with proper {\i yichus} in {\i kehunah} and {\i leviyah} would have been seated there. The investigation was already performed; finding such an ancestor means the checking has already been done.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The case of {\i Sanhedrin} is more conceptually complex. The intuitive question is: why should serving on the {\i Sanhedrin} establish {\i yichus}? Judicial appointment is presumably based on intellectual capacity, not lineage. {\i Amar Rabbi Yehoshua ben Levi} \u8212- or more precisely, the principle transmitted through him \u8212- is explicated by {\i Rav Yosef}: {\i tani Rav Yosef, keshem she-beis din menukim be-tzedek, kach menukim mi-kol mum} \u8212- just as the court must be clean in justice, so too they must be clean of any blemish. This is not merely an observation; it establishes a halakhic standard that membership on the court itself constitutes evidence that the relevant {\i bedika} was already performed.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Women Checked, Not M (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Insult & Yichus\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara raises a pointed question: {\i mai shena be-nashei badki u-mai shena be-gavrei lo badki} \u8212- why does the {\i Mishnah} speak only of investigating women and not men? The answer draws on a social observation. When women quarrel with one another ({\i dechi nashei de-mintzu bahadi hadadei}), they accuse each other of specific immoral acts \u8212- improper personal conduct. If such an accusation is true, the {\i yichus} problem it creates (in subsequent generations) would be known, because the conduct itself generates {\i kola} \u8212- public rumor. When men quarrel ({\i gavra de-nitzei bahadi hadadi}), they attack each other\u8217's {\i yichus} \u8212- your father, your mother were not who you think they were. If that accusation has any basis, it too generates {\i kola}. So in both cases, if there truly is a {\i yichus} problem, it will have been broadcast already. The asymmetry is that a woman\u8217's insult attacks her adversary\u8217's personal behavior, not her genealogy \u8212- the genealogical problem for the children remains concealed. A man\u8217's insult attacks genealogy directly, making the problem public. The Ran on the Rif explains this distinction clearly, and it is somewhat peculiar but it is what the Gemara says.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Bedika Upon Challen (III)ge\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chazaka Default\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The implicit question remains: why is {\i bedika} not automatically required regardless of any challenge? The answer is the {\i chezkat kashrut} \u8212- all families stand in a presumption of fitness. {\i Kol mishpachot be-chezkat kashrut hen omdot}. This is the {\i shitas chachamim} that forms the halakhic baseline. The investigation is triggered not by the mere act of marriage but by a specific {\i ir\u8217'ur} \u8212- a challenge to status.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Bedika Upon Challen (III)ge\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav\u8217's Lenient View\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara notes that women do not check men in any event. Beyond the {\i kola} argument, this is supported by Rav\u8217's ruling, cited as {\i amar Rav Yehuda amar Rav}: {\i lo huzharu kesherot le-hinasei li-psulim} \u8212- fit women were not warned against marrying men who are {\i psulim le-kehunah}. There is no {\i issur} for a {\i Yisraelit} to marry a {\i Kohen} who has a {\i pesul kehunah} in his background. If there is no prohibition, there is correspondingly no rabbinic obligation to investigate.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Women Checked, Not M (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 No Obligation for Women\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 On Rav\u8217's view, a woman faces no {\i issur} in marrying a {\i Kohen} who may be {\i pasul le-kehunah}, and so the entire framework of investigation does not apply to her in examining a prospective husband. The {\i Mishnah}\u8217's silence about women checking men is therefore explicable even without the {\i kola} argument: there is simply nothing to investigate for, because she incurs no prohibition. This is a significant leniency in Rav\u8217's position, though it is a difficult one to reconcile with the general spirit of the {\i knas} as the Ramban understood it \u8212- which is one reason the Ramban\u8217's approach generates its own difficulties.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Bedika Variants\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Twelve-Generation Variant\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara records a variant tradition: {\i Rav Adda bar Abba tani arba imot she-hein shtem esrei} \u8212- Rav Adda bar Abba has a version in which the four mothers yield not eight but twelve. On each node, one additional maternal ancestor is added: {\i al kol zu mosif lidok od em le-ma\u8217'alah}. Another version goes further: {\i tana matnitin tani arba imot she-hein shesh esrei} \u8212- four which are sixteen. The practical question of how one actually conducts such an investigation \u8212- what form of testimony is required, how one verifies a claim generation by generation \u8212- is not addressed here, and remains genuinely unclear. What is clear is that this is a rabbinic enactment, entirely {\i divrei chachamim}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara works through how Rav Adda bar Abba\u8217's version relates to the {\i Mishnah}. His version ({\i arba she-hein shtem esrei}) fits neatly with the {\i Mishnah}\u8217's language of {\i od achat} by the {\i Leviyah u-vat Yisrael} \u8212- one additional unit beyond the base of eight, which when added produces twelve. But the other variant ({\i arba she-hein shesh esrei}) appears to conflict with the {\i Mishnah}. The Gemara resolves this: {\i mai od achat? Zuga echad} \u8212- {\i od achat} does not mean one single ancestor but one additional {\i pair} \u8212- {\i ima ve-em ima} \u8212- yielding sixteen rather than twelve. Thus both versions can be harmonized with the {\i Mishnah}\u8217's language if read appropriately.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Meir vs.\u160 ?Sages\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Amar Rav Yehuda amar Rav}: the entire {\i Mishnah} as we have it \u8212- with all its variants of the investigation requirement \u8212- represents the position of {\i Rabbi Meir}. {\i Ve-chachamim omrim: kol mishpachot be-chezkat kashrut hen omdot} \u8212- the Sages hold that all families stand in the presumption of fitness and no automatic investigation is required. The {\i halacha} follows the Sages.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Bedika Upon Challen (III)ge\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Investigation on Ir\u8217'ur\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This creates an apparent difficulty: {\i Rav Chama bar Gurya amar Rav} states that the {\i Mishnah} \u8212- which we attributed to Rabbi Meir \u8212- actually describes the rule {\i ke-she-korei lo ir\u8217'ur}, when a challenge has been raised. But if that is what the {\i Mishnah} means, then even the Sages would agree that investigation is required once a challenge is raised; {\i ve-chachamim modeh le-Rabbi Meir} in that circumstance.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 There are two ways to resolve the tension between the two statements of Rav. The first: they are simply mutually exclusive \u8212- {\i matni ha, lo matni ha} \u8212- one cannot hold both simultaneously. The second, preferred version: {\i amar Rav Yehuda amar Rav} in its fuller formulation is {\i zo divrei Rabbi Meir, ve-chachamim omrim kol mishpachot be-chezkat kashrut hen omdot}; and the separate statement of {\i Rav Chama bar Gurya amar Rav} establishes that {\i im koreh alav ir\u8217'ur, tzarich livdok} \u8212- even the Sages concede that once a challenge is raised, investigation is mandatory. On this reading, both statements of Rav cohere: the baseline is {\i chezkat kashrut} per the Sages, but {\i ir\u8217'ur} from two witnesses triggers the full {\i bedika} of four mothers (eight ancestors). The {\i ir\u8217'ur} in question is testimony from two people that a {\i mamzer}, {\i chalal}, or person of {\i avdut} status has become intermixed in the family. This is how the Rambam rules, and it reflects his application of {\i halacha ke-batrai}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Exceptions to Bedik (III)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Court Purity Standard\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Returning to the {\i Mishnah}\u8217's exemptions: {\i ve-lo min ha-Sanhedrin u-lema\u8217'lah} \u8212- no investigation is required once one finds a {\i Sanhedrin} ancestor. The Gemara establishes the textual basis for this. {\i Mareimar} cites {\i Shir ha-Shirim} 4:7: {\i kulach yafah ra\u8217'ayati u-mum ein bach} \u8212- \u8220"You are entirely beautiful, my beloved, and there is no blemish in you\u8221" \u8212- read as referring to the {\i zekenim} of Israel: they are beautiful and bear no blemish. One might object that the verse refers to a physical blemish. Therefore {\i Rav Acha bar Yaakov} adds a second proof: the verse {\i ve-hityatzvu sham imach} (Bamidbar 11:16) \u8212- \u8220"and they shall stand there with you\u8221" \u8212- from which we derive {\i imach be-domin lach}: they must be {\i domeh le-Moshe} in being free of any {\i yichus} defect.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Yichus in Public Lif (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Domim Lecha Principle\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i drasha} of {\i domim lach} is a requirement that {\i Sanhedrin} members resemble Moshe \u8212- not in intellect alone, but in {\i yichus}. Rashi explains that this requirement applied to the {\i Sanhedrin she-be-ota sha\u8217'ah} specifically because the {\i shechina} rested upon the {\i zekenim} by virtue of the prophetic experience at that moment, and so the particular resonance of being {\i domeh le-Moshe} was contextually acute. The Gemara then brings {\i Rav Nachman\u8217's} verse: {\i ve-hakel me-alecha ve-nas\u8217'u itach} (from Yitro\u8217's advice in Shemot) \u8212- {\i ve-nas\u8217'u itach be-domin lach} \u8212- the requirement to resemble Moshe emanates even from Yitro\u8217's original counsel and is not limited to the singular prophetic moment. This returns the requirement to its ongoing, {\i le-dorot} application.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Dayan Categories\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Mishnah} then mentions {\i shotrei rabbim} \u8212- lower-level public officials or judges. The Gemara clarifies: this refers to {\i dayanim be-alma}, ordinary judges who are not the twenty-three of {\i Sanhedrin ketana}. The Gemara then asks: {\i lemeimra de-lo mokminan mi-psulim} \u8212- does this imply that we do not appoint {\i psulim} as {\i dayanim}? But a {\i braisa} states: {\i hakol kesherim ladun dinei mamonot, ve-ein hakol kesherim ladun dinei nefashot} \u8212- everyone is eligible to judge monetary cases; not everyone is eligible for capital cases. So {\i psulim} can be {\i dayanim} for {\i dinei mamonot} \u8212- how do we reconcile this with the {\i Mishnah}\u8217's requirement of {\i yichus} for {\i shotrei rabbim}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rav Yehuda} explains: {\i hakol} in that {\i braisa} comes to include even a {\i mamzer} \u8212- a {\i mamzer} can serve as a {\i dayan} for {\i dinei mamonot}. The apparent contradiction is resolved by {\i Abaye}: the {\i shotrei rabbim} referenced in the {\i Mishnah} refers specifically to judges in {\i Yerushalayim}, who had a special standard of {\i yichus}, not to all {\i dayanim be-mamonot} everywhere. {\i Ve-chein tani Rabbi Shimon bar Zevida be-kidushei d\u8217'bei Levi be-Yerushalayim} \u8212- a tradition from the academy of Levi confirms that {\i Yerushalayim} was extraordinarily careful about {\i yichus}, a standard beyond what the general rule would require.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Exceptions to Bedik (III)a\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Municipal Officials\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Mishnah} extends the exemption to {\i gabai tzedaka} \u8212- charity collectors \u8212- and those who served in similar municipal roles. The Gemara asks why their past service establishes {\i yichus}. The answer: {\i keivan de-minatzei bahadi inshi} \u8212- since they are constantly fighting with people. Their role requires them to pressure people to give, even on {\i erev Shabbat}, and people resist and quarrel. And as we saw earlier, when men quarrel they attack each other\u8217's {\i yichus}. If the {\i gabai tzedaka} had any genealogical defect, {\i iplugi iplugi kala} \u8212- it would have come out in the course of those disputes.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Public Dispute Revelation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is important to appreciate that {\i gabai tzedaka} were not merely volunteers collecting donations. The Rambam rules, based on the Yerushalmi, that {\i tzedaka} was a genuine tax: one could have one\u8217's property seized for failure to give, and the {\i beis din} could impose lashes. The {\i gabai tzedaka} had real coercive authority and therefore generated real resentment \u8212- which is precisely why their disputes would reliably expose any hidden {\i yichus} problems. They are more analogous to tax collectors than to fundraisers, and the disputes they generated were correspondingly adversarial.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Yichus in Public Lif (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mekrev Achecha Standard\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A related episode: {\i ushpizchanie de-Rav Adda bar Ahava giyora hava} \u8212- the host of Rav Adda bar Ahava was a {\i ger}. {\i Ve-hava kemintzi ihu ve-Rav Bibi} \u8212- he and Rav Bibi came into dispute over which of them would hold the position of authority in the town ({\i shraruta de-mata}). They came before Rav Yosef, who ruled based on the verse {\i som tasim alecha melech mi-kerev achecha} (Devarim 17:15): {\i kol mesimot she-ata mesim, lo yehe ela mi-kerev achecha} \u8212- any public appointment must come from among one\u8217's people.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Adda bar Abba responded: does this not apply even when {\i imo mi-Yisrael} \u8212- even if the {\i ger}\u8217's mother is Jewish? The implication would be that having a Jewish mother counts as {\i mi-kerev achecha}. Rav Yosef disagreed: {\i imo mi-Yisrael, mi-kerev achecha karina bei} \u8212- yes, if his mother is Jewish, he is indeed called {\i mi-kerev achecha}. Accordingly, Rav Bibi \u8212- a {\i gavra rabba} \u8212- was assigned oversight of {\i milei de-shmaya}, the religious affairs of the community, and the {\i ger} was assigned {\i milei de-mata}, the civic matters. {\i Abaye} draws a separate lesson from the episode: one who hosts a {\i talmid chacham} should choose someone like Rav Adda bar Abba, who knows how to argue on behalf of his host\u8217's merit.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ger Restrictions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then notes a regional contrast: {\i u-ve-ma\u8217'arava, afilu reish korei lo mokmei minaihu} \u8212- in the West, in Eretz Yisrael, they would not appoint a {\i ger} even to the position of {\i reish korei}, the official in charge of weights and measures in the market. {\i Be-Neharde\u8217'a afilu reish gargouta lo mokmei minaihu} \u8212- in Neharde\u8217'a, they would not appoint a {\i ger} even to oversee the town\u8217's irrigation works. Different communities applied the {\i mi-kerev achecha} principle with varying stringency, ranging from excluding {\i gerim} from all public authority to permitting them in purely civic or non-judicial roles.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rambam & Ger Problem\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rambam\u8217's Requirement\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The formal halakhic crystallization of the {\i yichus} requirement for {\i Sanhedrin} membership appears in the Rambam, {\i Sanhedrin} chapter 2, {\i halacha} 1. One might expect the Rambam to require only that {\i Sanhedrin} members be {\i chachamim u-nevonim}, wise and discerning \u8212- indeed he says this, and adds that they must be {\i muflagim be-chochmat ha-Torah}, of broad knowledge in {\i Torah}, {\i ba\u8217'alei de\u8217'a meruba}, possessing wide understanding, and even knowledgeable in adjacent fields: {\i refua, cheshbon, tekufot u-mazalot, itztagninut, ve-darchei ha-me\u8217'onenim ve-kosemim u-mechashefim ve-havlei avoda zara} \u8212- medicine, mathematics, astronomy, astrology, and the methods of sorcerers and idolaters, {\i kedei she-yihyu yodim ladun otam}. The {\i Kesef Mishneh} explains the astronomical requirement: the {\i Sanhedrin} must perform {\i ibur shana} and {\i kiddush ha-chodesh}, which require genuine astronomical competence. But the Rambam then adds: {\i ve-ein ma\u8217'amidin be-Sanhedrin\u8230 ? ela Kohanim, Levi\u8217'im, ve-Yisraelim ha-meyuchasim ha-ra\u8217'uyim le-hasi li-kehunah} \u8212- one may only appoint to the {\i Sanhedrin} those of proper {\i yichus}, specifically those whose daughters are fit to marry {\i Kohanim}. The verse cited is {\i ve-hityatzvu sham imach}, yielding {\i domim lach be-chochma u-ve-yira u-ve-yachas}. The source for {\i yira} is not apparent from this {\i sugya} itself.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gerim in Sanhedrin\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This formulation creates an immediate and serious historical difficulty. In {\i Pirkei Avot}, Shemaya and Avtalyon appear as links in the chain of transmission from Sinai. The Gemara in {\i Sanhedrin} identifies them as {\i gerim}. Rabbi Akiva likewise is described elsewhere as descending from a {\i ger} family, and he lacked {\i zechut avot} \u8212- ancestral merit \u8212- in a way that implies his family did not have generations of Jewish lineage. Yet Shemaya and Avtalyon manifestly served on and led the {\i Sanhedrin}. How can the Rambam\u8217's requirement be reconciled with this historical fact?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two Sanhedrin Functions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Tumim} \u8212- Rabbi Yehonatan Eibeschutz in his commentary on {\i Choshen Mishpat}, {\i siman} 7, {\i sif katan} 1 \u8212- addresses this directly. His resolution is that the {\i Sanhedrin Gedolah} bears two distinct functions. The first encompasses the full range of judicial authority: {\i dinei nefashot}, judgment of a {\i Zaken Mamre}, appointments of kings, and all capital and constitutional matters. For this function, {\i gerim} are unfit \u8212- {\i pesulim} \u8212- and cannot participate. The second function is to serve as the {\i ikar Torah she-ba\u8217'al peh}, the institutional repository and authority for oral law. In this capacity, as the Rambam articulates in {\i Hilchot Mamrim} chapter 1, {\i halacha} 1: {\i Beis Din ha-Gadol she-bi-Yerushalayim heim ikar Torah she-ba\u8217'al peh ve-heim amudei ha-hora\u8217'ah, u-me-hem chok u-mishpat yotzei le-chol Yisrael} \u8212- they are the pillars of legal teaching for all of Israel. For this role, {\i gerim} are not only permitted but may be the most qualified, as was the case with Shemaya and Avtalyon. On the {\i Tumim}\u8217's understanding, when the {\i Sanhedrin Gedolah} convened for capital proceedings, Shemaya and Avtalyon would step aside, replaced by others with the required {\i yichus}, while the bench was maintained at its full number through substitution \u8212- analogous to the modern practice of courts sitting with temporary or substitute judges.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Soloveitchik\u8217's Two Roles\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Soloveitchik advances a structurally similar but conceptually more elegant distinction, as formulated and transmitted by Rav Aharon Lichtenstein. The {\i Sanhedrin Gedolah} has two aspects: the {\i hilchati-torani} function and the {\i mishpati-tekesi} function. The {\i hilchati-torani} function \u8212- determining what the Torah requires, ruling on novel halakhic questions, preserving and transmitting the {\i mesorah} \u8212- is a matter of pure learning and {\i yiras Shamayim}. For this function, {\i yichus} is irrelevant. The principle that {\i afilu mamzer talmid chacham gadol mi-Kohen Gadol am ha-aretz} applies here without qualification. The {\i mishpati-tekesi} function \u8212- appointing a king, establishing courts throughout the land, judging entire tribes, trying a {\i navi sheker}, as enumerated in {\i Sanhedrin} chapter 5, {\i halacha} 1 \u8212- is a different matter entirely. In this role, the {\i Sanhedrin Gedolah} functions as {\i nitzigei Klal Yisrael}, the representative body of the Jewish people. It is this representative capacity that demands proper {\i yichus}: one who represents {\i Klal Yisrael} must be {\i mi-kerev achecha} in the fullest sense. Shemaya and Avtalyon were the supreme halakhic authorities of their generation \u8212- the {\i gedolim she-be-dor} \u8212- for the {\i hilchati-torani} aspect. For the {\i mishpati-tekesi} aspect, their {\i ger} status made them unfit, and Rav Soloveitchik suggests that these two functions may not even require the same body: one could have a separate group of seventy-one constituted specifically for the formal representational-governmental role, distinct from those sitting as the halakhic council, both legitimately called {\i Sanhedrin Gedolah} yet composed of entirely different people, each wearing, so to speak, a different hat.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Kiddushin 77\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Gerim & Authority\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ger in Authority\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The present daf begins at the top of 77a, though our shiur opened by returning briefly to yesterday\u8217's material, which carried weighty halachic implications regarding {\i gerim}. The central teaching from the previous daf concerned the prohibition against appointing a {\i ger} to a position of authority. The Gemara derives this from the verse {\i som tasim alecha melech mikerev achicha} \u8212- \u8220"you shall surely place upon yourself a king from among your brethren\u8221" \u8212- understanding {\i mikerev achicha} as excluding a {\i ger} from such a role. The analogy to the American constitutional requirement that a president be a \u8220"naturally born citizen\u8221" is apt, though even that phrase has proven surprisingly contested in modern legal discourse.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Moshe Serarah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Rambam extends this principle further, deriving from a {\i drasha} \u8212- apparently sourced in a lost {\i Midrash Halacha} \u8212- the phrase {\i melech velo malkah}, that the prohibition applies equally to women. This gives rise to contemporary discussions about women and {\i serarah}. The Young Israel\u8217's standing policy that neither women nor converts may serve as presidents of a synagogue congregation drew considerable attention when publicized, with the public finding the restriction on {\i gerim} more surprising than that on women \u8212- a telling reversal of contemporary sensibilities.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One of the most important responsa on this topic is a {\i teshuvah} of Rav Moshe Feinstein, addressing whether a {\i ger} may serve as a {\i rosh yeshiva}. Rav Moshe opens his responsum with a striking methodological framing: on the one hand, the Gemara prohibits a {\i ger} from holding positions of {\i serarah}; on the other hand, the Torah commands {\i ahavat hager} \u8212- love of the convert \u8212- and legislates {\i mishpat echad yihyeh lachem kager k\u8217'ezrach ha\u8217'aretz}, that one law shall apply equally to the native-born and the convert alike. Rav Moshe argues that this obligation to love the {\i ger} and treat him equally compels us to understand the {\i issur} of {\i serarah} as narrowly as possible. From that premise, he proceeds to argue that a {\i rosh yeshiva} does not constitute a position of {\i serarah} in the relevant halakhic sense. His broader principle is that positions people enter voluntarily \u8212- elected positions or those within institutions one chooses to join \u8212- are not the kind of imposed coercive authority the Gemara has in mind. The Gemara\u8217's concern is with authority imposed from above, not with roles entered into by mutual consent.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ger Identity Descent\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A further observation from yesterday\u8217's Gemara bears directly on our current discussions of {\i yichus}. The Gemara had recorded a case in which the resolution was that the individual in question \u8212- referred to as a {\i ger} \u8212- could serve in a position of authority because {\i imo miyisrael havay}, his mother was Jewish. This raises an obvious and pointed question: if his mother was Jewish, why is he called a {\i ger} at all?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One possibility is that his father was a {\i ger}, and the Gemara earlier established that {\i ger} identity \u8212- at least for certain purposes, such as the {\i issur} involving {\i mamzer} \u8212- can extend through generations even among descendants of {\i gerim}. Perhaps the Gemara uses the term {\i ger} even for someone whose mother is {\i miyisrael} because his father was a {\i ger}, and that identity lingers in some form. A more radical possibility, however, is that the Gemara is using the term {\i ger} loosely, to denote someone whose parentage is not entirely of Jewish birth \u8212- not in the technical sense of one who underwent conversion, but in the broader sense of having some non-Jewish parentage. This reading would imply that even partial non-Jewish paternal lineage suffices to invoke certain {\i ger}-related concerns. There is indeed a position among the {\i Rishonim} \u8212- admittedly not the accepted {\i halacha} \u8212- that when the father is not Jewish but the mother is, the child actually requires conversion. We do not rule this way; matrilineal descent suffices. But the Gemara\u8217's use of the term {\i ger} for someone whose mother is Jewish remains genuinely puzzling and raises real questions about what {\i ger} identity means across generations.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Bibi Division\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 In the end, the Gemara resolved the case by dividing responsibilities: Rav Bibi was appointed to oversee {\i milei d\u8217'shmaya} \u8212- matters of a religious character \u8212- while the {\i ger} was given responsibility for {\i milei d\u8217'alma}, the more mundane civic affairs. There is a parallel Gemara that describes the proper internal division of household responsibilities along similar lines, with the wife governing the {\i milei d\u8217'alma} and the husband the {\i milei d\u8217'shmaya}. As the joke goes: some husbands have been waiting for decades for their wives to tell them what constitutes a {\i milei d\u8217'shmaya}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Yichus Registers\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Register Witnesses\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Picking up approximately ten lines from the bottom of 76b, the Gemara continues its discussion of {\i yichus} \u8212- at what point and in what circumstances lineage must be verified. Rabbi Yosei states that one need not investigate the lineage of anyone listed in the register of {\i arkhei hayeshana}. Rashi explains that {\i yeshana} is the name of a town near Tzippori. The register in question, according to Rashi, was one of judges rather than witnesses \u8212- though the functional point is the same: only individuals whose {\i yichus} had been verified would have been admitted to such a register. The Gemara explains the rationale with the phrase {\i dayka u\u8217'machtmi} \u8212- they were careful before accepting people as signatories, so anyone found in that register can be trusted to have already been cleared.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 David\u8217's Troops\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Chanina ben Antigonus adds another category: {\i istratya shel melech}, someone listed in the king\u8217's military register, is also presumed to have verified lineage. The Gemara, in the name of Rav Yehuda citing Shmuel, specifies that this refers to {\i be\u8217'chaylot shel beit David} \u8212- the troops of King David. Service in David\u8217's army was restricted to those of distinguished {\i yichus}. This recalls a historical pattern in which military officership was a hereditary, aristocratic position, with the nobility occupying command roles by virtue of lineage.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Veyityachsam\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Yosef identifies the scriptural basis: the verse {\i veyityachsam batzava bamilchama} \u8212- \u8220"and they were affiliated by lineage in the army, in the war.\u8221" The very word {\i yichus} appears in this context, denoting the formal genealogical identification of soldiers in David\u8217's military.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Merit Ancestors\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara, in the name of Rav Yehuda citing Rav, explains the reason: {\i kedei shetehei zechutan vezechut avoteihem mesayatun} \u8212- so that both their own merit and the merit of their forefathers would come to their aid in battle. The underlying logic is that military success is not solely a function of physical prowess and tactical skill; divine assistance matters, and one who brings with him the accumulated {\i zechut} of righteous ancestors is a more potent asset on the battlefield.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Non-Israelite Warriors\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This raises an immediate difficulty from Tanach itself. Several of David\u8217's most celebrated warriors appear to be of non-Israelite descent. Tzelek is identified as {\i ha-Ammoni} \u8212- does this not indicate he descended from Ammon? The Gemara answers: no, it means he {\i dwelt} in Ammon. Similarly, Uriah is called {\i ha-Chiti} \u8212- the Gemara answers that this too denotes his place of residence, not his ethnic origin. But then there is Itai {\i ha-Gitti}, identified as from Gat. Should we say the same \u8212- that he merely lived there? The Gemara cannot, because Rav Nachman explicitly states that Itai {\i ha-Gitti} came and annulled the idol worship of a certain locality \u8212- a detail Rashi explains as indicating that he had himself been an idolater, establishing that he genuinely descended from Gat and was not originally Jewish.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 There is also the tradition that David had four hundred young men \u8212- not biological children, as Rashi clarifies, but soldiers in his retinue \u8212- all of whom were {\i benei yifat to\u8217'ar}, descendants of women taken captive in war. These men would cut their hair in styles associated with non-Jewish practice and sat in golden wagons, leading the armies into battle. The Gemara resolves the contradiction by limiting their role: {\i de-azli leva\u8217'utei alma} \u8212- they went to the battlefield not to fight but to strike fear into the enemy. The actual combatants were the soldiers of pure lineage; these men served as a kind of psychological vanguard. Whether this resolution is fully satisfying is another matter \u8212- the Gemara has its answer, even if the evidence from Tanach pulls in multiple directions.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Bat Chalal Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chalal Definition\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 We arrive now at the {\i Mishna} at the top of 77a. The subject is the status of a {\i chalal} and those descended from him. By way of introduction: the Torah explicitly prohibits a {\i kohen} from marrying certain categories of women. The verse states {\i isha zona vechalala lo yikachu ve-isha gerusha me-isha lo yikachu} \u8212- a {\i kohen} may not marry a {\i zonah}, a {\i chalala}, or a {\i gerusha}. A {\i gerusha} requires no elaboration. The definitions of {\i zonah} and {\i chalala}, however, demand careful examination.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i chalala} is defined in two related ways. First, she is a woman born from a union that is forbidden specifically to a {\i kohen} \u8212- for example, the child of a {\i kohen} and a {\i gerusha}, or a {\i kohen} and a {\i chalala}, or a {\i kohen} and a {\i zonah}. Second, there is the question of whether the {\i chalal} or {\i chalala} status, once established, passes to subsequent generations through the {\i chalal} himself, even in cases where no forbidden act of intercourse has occurred. A {\i zonah}, for our purposes, is the product of a forbidden act of intercourse that is not unique to {\i kohanim} \u8212- such as an act of {\i arayot} \u8212- or the offspring of a union involving a {\i mamzer}; we will return to the {\i zonah} category in the Gemara.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chalal L\u8217'Olam\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Mishna} states: {\i bat chalal zachar pesulah min hakehunah le-olam} \u8212- the daughter of a male {\i chalal} is forever forbidden to the {\i kehunah}. The operative word is {\i le-olam} \u8212- forever, across generations. This requires explanation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If a {\i chalal} has a child and it is a son, that son is likewise a {\i chalal}, yet his being a {\i chalal} in the {\i Yisrael} context carries no practical consequence on its own. He functions as a carrier \u8212- analogous, as it were, to a recessive genetic trait. The male line continues to carry the status without it manifesting. But when the line finally produces a daughter, she becomes a {\i chalala}, forbidden to a {\i kohen}. That is what {\i le-olam} means: however many generations of sons intervene, the status persists through them, waiting to manifest in the first daughter.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 L\u8217'Olam Mechanism\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The mechanism is striking in its asymmetry: males carry the status but do not personally suffer its prohibition in any meaningful way (since being a {\i chalal} matters for a {\i Yisrael} mainly in the sense of not having {\i kedushat kehunah} to begin with); females receive the status and are forbidden to {\i kohanim}, but do not transmit it further. Males transmit without manifesting; females manifest without transmitting. Once the status reaches a daughter, it ends with her \u8212- she does not pass it on.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yisrael Chalala\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Mishna} continues: {\i Yisrael shenasa chalala bito kesherah lakehunah} \u8212- if a {\i Yisrael} marries a {\i chalala}, the daughter is fit for {\i kehunah}. The status does not pass through the mother. Transmission is exclusively patrilineal. This is not a case of a forbidden act of intercourse \u8212- a {\i Yisrael} is entirely permitted to marry a {\i chalala} \u8212- and since the father has no {\i chalal} status, the daughter acquires none. The mother\u8217's status simply does not descend.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Parallel Chalal Cases\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Yehuda Ger\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Mishna} now introduces a parallel set of laws concerning {\i gerim}. Rabbi Yehuda states: {\i bat ger zachar} \u8212- the daughter of a male {\i ger} who has a child with a {\i Yisraelit} woman \u8212- is like the daughter of a {\i chalal} in this respect: her status renders her forbidden to a {\i kohen}, like a {\i giyoret}. This is a striking ruling. A man who converted and subsequently married a fully Jewish woman has a daughter who, according to Rabbi Yehuda, cannot marry a {\i kohen}. The {\i ger} status, transmitted through the male line, gives the child a {\i giyoret} classification sufficient to disqualify her from {\i kehunah}. This is consistent, by the way, with the earlier Gemara where a person whose mother was Jewish was still referred to as a {\i ger} \u8212- apparently because the father was a {\i ger}, that identity can persist. And if a {\i kohen} had married this woman, he would need to divorce her.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Eliezer Ger\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Eliezer ben Yaakov disagrees. He holds that for the purposes of {\i kehunah}, the {\i ger} status does not pass down if either parent was born Jewish. A {\i Yisrael} who married a {\i giyoret} \u8212- the daughter is fit. A {\i ger} who married a {\i bat Yisrael} \u8212- the daughter is fit. The {\i ger} or {\i giyoret} status is not transmitted to the next generation unless {\i both} parents are {\i gerim}. The one case where it does pass down: {\i ger shenasa giyoret bito pesulah lakehunah} \u8212- the daughter of a {\i ger} and a {\i giyoret} is forbidden to a {\i kohen}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is worth noting here that the ordinary reason given for why a {\i giyoret} is forbidden to a {\i kohen} \u8212- concerns about her conduct prior to conversion \u8212- would obviously be irrelevant to the daughter of {\i gerim}. The application of the prohibition to second-generation descendants of {\i gerim} is therefore much more about a continuing identity status than about any behavioral concern, and the question of where precisely this is derived from the {\i psukim} becomes important.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Imo Miyisrael\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara adds a clarifying statement: {\i echad ger ve-echad avadim meshucharim afilu ad asarah dorot ad shetehei imo miyisrael} \u8212- whether a {\i ger} or a freed slave, even through ten generations, the disqualification persists {\i ad shetehei imo miyisrael} \u8212- until the mother is born Jewish. Here again the phrase {\i imo miyisrael} recurs, echoing the earlier discussion. There is a slight tension, however: the {\i Mishna} had indicated that either parent being born Jewish suffices to break the transmission \u8212- yet this formulation mentions only the mother, not the father. The asymmetry is not fully resolved here.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Yosei Ger\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yosei takes the most lenient position: even the child of a {\i ger} and a {\i giyoret} is fit. The {\i ger} status, in his view, does not transmit at all \u8212- it applies only to the first-generation convert himself and does not descend to any subsequent generation under any circumstances.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Gezeirah Shavah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Amitzri Adomi\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now asks: what does {\i le-olam} \u8212- the ruling that the {\i chalal} status persists indefinitely through the male line \u8212- come to teach? One might have thought the transmission of this status should be limited, analogous to the cases of {\i Mitzri} and {\i Adomi}, where the prohibition lapses after three generations. Perhaps the {\i chalal} status should similarly expire. The Gemara instructs us that this inference is incorrect \u8212- {\i le-olam} means precisely what it says, with no generational cutoff.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Velo Yechalel\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then asks: from where is the transmission of the {\i chalal} status derived? Rabbi Yochanan cites Rabbi Shimon (or Rabbi Yishmael): the verse states {\i velo yechalel zaro be-amav} \u8212- \u8220"he shall not defile his offspring among his people.\u8221" This verse appears in the context of the {\i Kohen Gadol} and the {\i almanah}, and it establishes the foundational principle that the offspring of a {\i kohen} who violates the marriage prohibitions becomes a {\i chalal}. From {\i velo yechalel zaro be-amav} we learn that the child of such a union is {\i chalal}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tumah Gezeirah Shavah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then introduces a {\i gezeirah shavah}: just as the {\i issur} of {\i tumah} for a {\i kohen} focuses on men \u8212- {\i velo yitama ba\u8217'al be-amav} uses the masculine \u8212- so too the transmission of {\i chalal} status focuses on men. The {\i chalal} status is carried through the male line. The same way the laws of {\i tumah} are stated in the masculine and emphasize the male, so too the {\i chalal} status passes down through the father.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Zaro Both Genders\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But does the word {\i zaro} \u8212- \u8220"his offspring\u8221" \u8212- in {\i velo yechalel zaro be-amav} not include daughters as well? The Gemara confronts a potential objection: if the emphasis is on men, perhaps the status only ever passes to sons and never to daughters. To this the Gemara responds: {\i mi ketiv beno? zaro ketiv} \u8212- the verse does not say \u8220"his son,\u8221" it says \u8220"his offspring.\u8221" {\i Zaro} encompasses both sons and daughters. The one who {\i receives} the status can be either a son or a daughter. That is what we learn from {\i zaro}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A further question: once the status has been transmitted to a son, how do we know it continues to a second generation \u8212- specifically, does the son\u8217's daughter also receive the status? Perhaps {\i zaro} refers only to the immediate child. The Gemara responds by pointing to the possessive force of {\i zaro}: the offspring is likened to the father, and therefore {\i mahu bito pesulah af beno bito pesulah} \u8212- just as the father\u8217's daughter receives the {\i chalal} status, so too the son\u8217's daughter receives it, since the son, being a {\i chalal} himself, is now in the position of the father.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Male Carriers Only\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But then one might ask: if {\i zaro} includes daughters, why doesn\u8217't the daughter also transmit the status to her children? The Gemara answers: this is precisely what the {\i gezeirah shavah} comes to teach. The emphasis on the male, derived from {\i tumah}, establishes that while both sons and daughters {\i receive} the status from a {\i chalal} father, only males {\i transmit} it. The {\i gezeirah shavah} narrows the carrier role to men alone. The daughter receives the {\i chalala} status and is thereby forbidden to a {\i kohen}, but she does not pass it to her own children. The chain runs through sons indefinitely, manifesting in daughters but terminating there.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Parallel Repetition\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Redundancy Challenge\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara raises a question about the structure of the {\i Mishna} itself. The {\i Mishna} states in its opening clause {\i bat chalal zachar pesulah min hakehunah le-olam}, and then later states {\i chalal shenasa bat yisrael bito pesulah lakehunah}. But this third clause \u8212- that the daughter of a male {\i chalal} who married a {\i bat Yisrael} is forbidden \u8212- seems to repeat what was already established in the first clause. The {\i Mishna} has already told us that!\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Parallel Structure\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara explains: {\i aidi d\u8217'tana reisha yisrael shenasa chalalah, tana nami seifa chalal shenasa bat yisrael} \u8212- since the {\i Mishna} needed to teach the second case, that a {\i Yisrael} who married a {\i chalala} has a fit daughter, it formulated a parallel clause with the reverse scenario: a {\i chalal} who married a {\i bat Yisrael} has a disqualified daughter. The parallel literary structure accounts for what would otherwise appear redundant. The {\i Mishna} wanted two clean parallel cases \u8212- {\i Yisrael} with {\i chalala} on one side, {\i chalal} with {\i bat Yisrael} on the other \u8212- even at the cost of apparent redundancy with the opening clause.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rabbi Dostai\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mikveh Metaphor\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now notes that the {\i Mishna}\u8217's ruling stands in contrast to the opinion of Rabbi Dostai bar Rabbi Yehuda, cited in a {\i baraita}: {\i keshem shebnei Yisrael mikveh tahara l\u8217'chalalot, kach bnot Yisrael mikveh tahara l\u8217'chalalim} \u8212- just as Jewish men function as a {\i mikveh} of purification for {\i chalalot}, so Jewish women function as a {\i mikveh} of purification for {\i chalalim}. The {\i mikveh} imagery is admittedly more intuitive in the second half: the child grows within the woman, so she as the containing environment \u8220"purifies\u8221" the child that emerges. Applied to men, the image is less natural, but the halakhic point is symmetric.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 One Parent Kasher\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Dostai\u8217's position is that it does not matter whether the {\i chalal} is the father or the mother \u8212- as long as one parent is not a {\i chalal}, the child is fit. If the father is a {\i Yisrael} and the mother is a {\i chalala}, the child is fit. If the father is a {\i chalal} and the mother is a {\i bat Yisrael}, the child is equally fit. The {\i Mishna}, however, does not rule this way; it holds that when the father is the {\i chalal}, the status passes to the child regardless of the mother\u8217's status.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Woman Becomes Chalala\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Woman Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i baraita} raises a further question: the verse {\i velo yechalel zaro} speaks of {\i his offspring} \u8212- from this we know the child of the forbidden act becomes a {\i chalal}. But where do we learn that the woman herself, who participated in the forbidden act of intercourse with the {\i kohen}, also becomes a {\i chalala}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kal V\u8217'Chomer\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara proposes a {\i kal vachomer}: if the child \u8212- who committed no sin \u8212- becomes a {\i chalal}, then surely the woman who participated in the transgression should become a {\i chalala} as well.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kohen Exception\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara immediately challenges this {\i kal vachomer} with a counterexample: let the {\i kohen} himself prove it. The {\i kohen} committed the forbidden act of intercourse and yet does not become a {\i chalal} as a result \u8212- his {\i kehunah} status is unaffected. If the active transgressor (the {\i kohen}) does not lose status, perhaps the prohibition does not attach to the guilty parties at all, only to the innocent child.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Status Change\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara responds by distinguishing the {\i kohen}\u8217's situation from the woman\u8217's. A {\i kohen}, once born with the status of {\i kasher kohen}, can never lose his {\i kehunah} \u8212- his status is immutable in that direction. The woman, by contrast, has a halakhic status that is demonstrably changeable \u8212- a woman can begin life with a perfectly fit status and acquire a disqualifying one through subsequent events, as we see with {\i almanah}, {\i gerusha}, and {\i zonah}. Since we know that a woman\u8217's {\i kehunah}-related status can change, and since the child\u8217's becoming a {\i chalal} demonstrates that this act produces defilement, the {\i kal vachomer} holds: the woman too is defiled by the act and becomes a {\i chalala}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rabbi Dostai (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 B\u8217'Amav One Genre\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 An alternative derivation is also offered. The verse {\i velo yechalel zaro be-amav} itself can be read as limiting the defilement: {\i be-am echad hu d\u8217'meichel} \u8212- he only defiles when the act involves one \u8220"nation,\u8221" meaning two people of the same halakhic type, i.e., both {\i chalal} and {\i chalala}. {\i Bishnei amamim} \u8212- if the two parties are of different types, one being a {\i chalal} and the other not, {\i eino meichel} \u8212- the status does not pass to the child. This is the basis of Rabbi Dostai bar Rabbi Yehuda\u8217's view: the defilement of offspring requires a homogeneous union; a mixed union \u8212- one {\i chalal} parent and one who is not \u8212- is purified by the non-defiled parent.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Chalala Definition\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Born Forbidden\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now turns to a broader {\i baraita} addressing the definition of {\i chalala}. {\i Tanu Rabbanan}: {\i eizo hi chalalah? kol she-noldah min hapsulim} \u8212- who is a {\i chalala}? Anyone born from the disqualified ones. The Gemara immediately asks what {\i psulim} means here.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kohen-Specific\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If {\i psulim} means simply any woman forbidden to a particular {\i kohen} for whatever reason, the scope would be too broad. A man who divorced his wife, she subsequently married another man, and then was divorced from him \u8212- if the first husband, who was a {\i kohen}, took her back, that act is forbidden because she is now {\i pesulah lo}, but it is not a prohibition unique to {\i kohanim}. The prohibition of {\i machazir gerushato} applies to all Israelites alike.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Machzir Grusha\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Yet we know that the children of such a union are {\i kesherim} \u8212- from the verse {\i to\u8217'evah hi}: {\i hi to\u8217'evah}, the woman herself is forbidden, {\i ve-ein baneha to\u8217'avin}, but the prohibition does not extend to her children. This proves that a {\i chalala} is not simply the product of any forbidden sexual act involving a {\i kohen}, but specifically of those acts prohibited to {\i kohanim} as {\i kohanim}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Psul Kehuna\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Yehuda therefore reformulates: {\i eizo hi chalalah? kol she-noldah mi-psul kehunah} \u8212- a {\i chalala} is anyone born from a woman who is specifically forbidden to a {\i kohen} by reason of her status vis-\u224 ?-vis the {\i kehunah}. The disqualification must be one unique to {\i kohanim}, not a general prohibition that happens to apply to a {\i kohen} as well.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara raises an objection: if we define {\i chalala} as one who is {\i born} from such a union, what about the woman herself \u8212- the {\i almanah} or {\i gerusha} who has intercourse with a {\i kohen}? She is not born from anything; she becomes a {\i chalala} through the act. And we established above that she does become a {\i chalala}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Never Kasher\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava responds with a more precise formulation: {\i eizo hi chalalah muzkeret?} \u8212- what is the {\i chalala} explicitly mentioned in the {\i pasuk}? The verse {\i velo yechalel zaro be-amav} describes a child {\i she-lo haytah she\u8217'at hakosher klal} \u8212- one who was never {\i kasher} to begin with, who was born already disqualified. That is the {\i chalala} the Torah refers to directly: {\i kol she-noldah mi-psul kehunah} \u8212- anyone born from a union forbidden by the laws of {\i kehunah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Basic Pshat\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Yitzchak bar Avin adds a final clarification: {\i eizo hi chalalah she-ikarah mi-divrei Torah?} \u8212- what is the {\i chalala} whose status is derived from the plain meaning of the Torah, without rabbinic interpretation? {\i Ve-ein tzerichah le-faresh mi-divrei sofrim} \u8212- it requires no supplementary rabbinic derivation. The answer is the same: anyone born from a woman who is specifically forbidden to a {\i kohen} by the laws of {\i kehunah}. The {\i pasuk} states explicitly \u8212- do not take an {\i almanah}, {\i gerusha}, {\i chalala}, or {\i zonah} \u8212- {\i velo yechalel zaro be-amav} \u8212- and if you do, the child will be a {\i chalal}. The plain {\i pshat} of the {\i pasuk} requires nothing beyond this.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Forbidden Statuses\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Four Statuses\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now addresses a new {\i baraita} dealing with the layering of multiple forbidden statuses. The case concerns a {\i kohen} who has intercourse with a woman who is simultaneously \u8212- or who becomes in sequence \u8212- an {\i almanah}, {\i gerusha}, {\i chalala}, and {\i zonah}. How many sets of lashes does he receive? And does the order in which she acquired these statuses matter?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Three Widow\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i baraita} begins with a simpler case: a woman who is a widow three times over \u8212- {\i almanah almanah almanah} \u8212- if a {\i kohen} has intercourse with her, he receives only one set of lashes. The Gemara works to identify what this case actually means. It cannot mean he had intercourse with three different widows in succession, for those are separate bodies and separate acts, yielding obvious multiple liability. And if he had intercourse with a single woman three times \u8212- did the interposing warnings between each act define them as separate transgressions? The precedent from the {\i nazir} who drank wine repeatedly is invoked: only if a separate warning preceded each act does each act constitute an independent transgression. The actual case of {\i almanah almanah almanah} turns out to be a single woman who was widowed by three consecutive husbands. She is triply an {\i almanah}, but she is one person and one act \u8212- so only one transgression is incurred.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Separate Acts\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The broader question is then: when a woman carries four distinct forbidden statuses simultaneously, can a single act of intercourse with a {\i kohen} generate liability for all four simultaneously? In principle, {\i issur chal al issur} \u8212- one forbidden status layering atop another \u8212- is a halakhic mechanism that can produce multiple liabilities from a single act. But this mechanism is not universally applicable.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Issur Mosif\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava establishes the governing principle: this {\i tanna} does not hold the general rule of {\i issur chal al issur} \u8212- that any forbidden status can be imposed upon a pre-existing one. However, the {\i tanna} does accept {\i issur mosif} \u8212- a status that {\i broadens} the scope of the prohibition beyond what the prior status covered can be layered on top of it. And this is why the order of the statuses matters.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Order Broadens\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Working through the sequence: a woman who begins as an {\i almanah} is forbidden to a {\i Kohen Gadol} but permitted to a regular {\i kohen}. If she then becomes a {\i gerusha}, the scope of the prohibition expands \u8212- now she is forbidden to any {\i kohen}, not just the {\i Kohen Gadol}. Since this is an {\i issur mosif} \u8212- a broadening \u8212- the {\i gerusha} prohibition layers atop the {\i almanah} prohibition, and the {\i Kohen Gadol} who has intercourse with her is liable for both. She is still at this point permitted to eat {\i terumah}, assuming she is a {\i bat kohen}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 When she then becomes a {\i chalala} \u8212- perhaps because a {\i kohen} had intercourse with her \u8212- the scope broadens again: now she is forbidden from eating {\i terumah} as well, a new dimension of prohibition previously absent. This broadening allows the {\i chalala} status to layer atop the {\i gerusha} status, which had layered atop the {\i almanah} status. All three are now simultaneously operative.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Zonah Scope\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The final stage \u8212- {\i zonah} \u8212- presents a difficulty. She is already fully forbidden to {\i kohanim} in every respect. In what sense does becoming a {\i zonah} broaden the prohibition further? Rav Chana bar Katina explains: {\i v\u8217'shem znut poseil b\u8217'Yisrael} \u8212- there is a form of {\i znut} that disqualifies a woman even in the context of her marriage to a {\i Yisrael}. Adultery renders a woman forbidden to her husband, a prohibition applicable to all of Israel, not just to {\i kohanim}. Although this particular woman\u8217's {\i zonah} status does not render her forbidden to anyone she was not already forbidden to, the conceptual scope of {\i znut} as a category extends to all Israel \u8212- and this broader conceptual reach qualifies it as an {\i issur mosif} sufficient to allow it to layer atop the prior statuses. Done in this precise order \u8212- {\i almanah}, {\i gerusha}, {\i chalala}, {\i zonah} \u8212- all four statuses are operative simultaneously, and a {\i kohen} who has intercourse with her incurs liability for each. In any other order, the broadening mechanism does not apply, and only a single transgression is incurred.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Kiddushin 78\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Issur Chal al Issur\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chalal/Zonah/Almanah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The daf opens in the midst of a discussion concerning the statuses of {\i chalalah}, {\i zonah}, and {\i almanah} as they pertain to a {\i kohen}. The preceding sugya had explored the possibility that a single woman might simultaneously bear all of these designations \u8212- {\i chalalah}, {\i zonah}, {\i almanah}, and {\i grushah} \u8212- and whether each status could take effect sequentially even under the principle of {\i ein issur chal al issur}, the rule that once a person or object is already forbidden, a new and distinct prohibition cannot take effect upon it. The answer, as we had seen, was that this could be possible when each successive prohibition expands the scope of the original one.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Braita: Kohen Gadol\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i tana} taught the following before Rav Sheshet: {\i kol shehu b\u8217'yikach harei hu b\u8217'lo yikach} \u8212- any woman who could theoretically have been eligible for a {\i kohen gadol} to marry had she been a {\i betulah} is now subject to the prohibitions of {\i lo yikach} enumerated in the verse: {\i almanah}, {\i grushah}, {\i chalalah}, and {\i zonah}. Conversely, {\i kol she\u8217'eino b\u8217'yikach eino b\u8217'lo yikach} \u8212- a woman who could not have been married by a {\i kohen gadol} even had she been a {\i betulah} and free of any prior marital status, is not further forbidden by virtue of being an {\i almanah} or {\i grushah}. The operative exclusion the {\i braita} has in mind is: {\i prat l\u8217'Kohen Gadol sheba al achoto almanah} \u8212- this would exclude the case of his sister who happens to be a widow. Since he could not marry her even if she were a virgin, by virtue of the {\i issur} of {\i achot}, the subsequent status of {\i almanah} or {\i grushah} cannot take effect upon her from his perspective \u8212- which is essentially the application of {\i ein issur chal al issur}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Sheshet/Rabbi Shimon\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Sheshet responded to the {\i tana} and asked: whose teaching is this? It must be that of Rabbi Shimon, {\i d\u8217'amar ein issur chal al issur} \u8212- who holds that a prohibition cannot take effect upon an already-forbidden object or person. Since she is already forbidden to him as his sister, she cannot become additionally forbidden as an {\i almanah} or {\i grushah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Neveilah/Yom Kippur\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara cites the classical {\i braita} in support of this attribution: {\i Rabbi Shimon omer ha\u8217'ochel neveilah b\u8217'Yom HaKippurim patur} \u8212- one who eats {\i neveilah} on Yom Kippur is exempt, because {\i ein issur chal al issur}. It is worth pausing here, because this example is actually quite striking. The standard cases of {\i issur chal al issur} involve multiple statuses applied to the same object \u8212- the food itself is forbidden. But on Yom Kippur, the prohibition of fasting applies to the {\i person}, not to the food. The {\i neveilah} is a forbidden object; Yom Kippur is an obligation that rests on the individual. It is therefore fascinating that Rabbi Shimon applies {\i ein issur chal al issur} even across these two distinct planes.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Issur Chamur/Kal\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then considers: perhaps the {\i braita} actually reflects the position of the {\i Rabbanan} rather than Rabbi Shimon? After all, the {\i Rabbanan} generally say {\i issur chal al issur} \u8212- that a prohibition does take effect upon a pre-existing prohibition. Under that view, the sister of a {\i kohen} who is also an {\i almanah} would be forbidden to him under both designations. Yet the Gemara rejects this: {\i afilu teima Rabbanan} \u8212- even according to the {\i Rabbanan}, {\i ki amri Rabbanan issur chal al issur hanei mili issur chamur al issur kal} \u8212- when the {\i Rabbanan} say one {\i issur} takes effect on top of another, that applies when the second prohibition is weightier or at least equal in severity. What they would not say is that a lighter {\i issur} ({\i issur kal}) takes effect upon a heavier one ({\i issur chamur}). The {\i issur} of {\i almanah} is a simple {\i lav}, whereas the {\i issur} of a sister carries the weight of {\i kares}. Accordingly, even the {\i Rabbanan} would concede that the {\i almanah} status cannot take effect upon what is already a {\i kares}-level prohibition.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sister Cannot Add Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A second version ({\i ika d\u8217'amri}) holds that Rav Sheshet\u8217's response was precisely this point \u8212- that the {\i braita} reflects the view of the {\i Rabbanan}, who generally permit {\i issur chal al issur}, but who nonetheless would agree that a lighter prohibition cannot take effect upon a heavier one. The {\i braita} thus comes to spell out the limiting principle: the {\i Rabbanan}\u8217's rule applies only when the subsequent prohibition is of equal or greater severity than the first.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kehunah Shani\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might have thought, however, that {\i issur kehunah} operates differently \u8212- that since {\i kohanim} carry a uniquely elevated set of marital prohibitions, the Torah may have intended to extend the reach of those prohibitions even beyond the normal parameters of {\i issur chal al issur}. The Gemara dismisses this as well: {\i ka mashma lan} \u8212- the {\i braita} teaches us that this is not the case. Even a {\i kohen}\u8217's special status does not endow the {\i issur kehunah} with the capacity to take effect on top of an existing {\i issur kares}. The {\i issur kehunah} is always a {\i lav}, and a {\i lav} cannot override a pre-existing {\i kares}-level prohibition simply by virtue of the {\i kohen}\u8217's heightened obligations.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Zonah vs.\u160 ?Chalalah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Pappa Query\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now turns to a fundamental question posed by Rav Pappa to Abaye: {\i Yisrael ha-ba al achoto} \u8212- if a non-{\i kohen} has sexual relations with his sister, the act is certainly {\i assur}, and such a woman becomes a {\i zonah} through an act of forbidden sex not connected to {\i kehunah}. The question is: {\i chalalah mashvi lah o lo mashvi lah?} \u8212- does this act also confer upon her the status of {\i chalalah}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 To situate the question properly: a {\i zonah} is a woman who becomes forbidden through an act of illicit sexual relations, a category that applies even outside the world of {\i kehunah}. A {\i chalalah}, by contrast, would seem to be a status created either by birth \u8212- the daughter of a {\i chalal} \u8212- or through an act of sexual relations that is specifically forbidden to a {\i kohen}. For example, a {\i grushah} who has relations with a {\i kohen} becomes a {\i chalalah}, having already been forbidden to him; or an {\i almanah} who has relations with a {\i kohen gadol} becomes a {\i chalalah} as well, now forbidden to all {\i kohanim}. So the question becomes: is the category of {\i chalalah} confined to {\i issurei kehunah} \u8212- prohibitions specific to {\i kohanim} \u8212- or might it extend to any severe act of forbidden relations, such as the {\i issur kares} of a sister?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kal Vechomer\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara considers whether one might construct a {\i kal vachomer}: {\i mi amrinan kal v\u8217'chomer me\u8217'chayavei lavin} \u8212- since a woman can become a {\i chalalah} from relations that are prohibited by a simple {\i lav}, such as a {\i kohen gadol} with an {\i almanah}, certainly relations prohibited by {\i kares}, such as a sister, should create {\i chalalah} status as well. The {\i kal vachomer} has a superficial appeal, yet it is also structurally awkward, since it conflates two qualitatively different categories \u8212- relations that are forbidden specifically to {\i kohanim} versus relations that are universally forbidden.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Abaye\u8217's Answer\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye responds definitively: {\i ein chalalah ela mi\u8217'issur kehunah bilvad} \u8212- the status of {\i chalalah} applies exclusively to prohibitions that are specific to {\i kohanim}. The concept of {\i chalalah} is rooted in the desecration of {\i kehunah} \u8212- {\i yichalel} \u8212- and therefore it is bounded by the world of {\i issurei kehunah}. Acts that are forbidden generally, even if weightier, belong to the category of {\i zonah}, not {\i chalalah}. Greater severity does not translate into {\i chalalah} status; the determinative factor is the specific relationship to {\i kehunah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava\u8217's Source\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava then asks: what is the textual source for this principle? He brings a {\i braita}: the {\i braita} asks why the Torah needs to write {\i grushah} in the passage concerning the {\i kohen gadol} \u8212- {\i lo yomar grushah b\u8217'kohen gadol v\u8217'tasi b\u8217'kal vachomer mi\u8217'kohen hedyot}. Since a normal {\i kohen} is already forbidden to marry a {\i grushah}, one could have derived through a {\i kal vachomer} that a {\i kohen gadol}, who has additional marital restrictions, is certainly forbidden. Why, then, does the verse enumerate {\i grushah} explicitly in the {\i kohen gadol}\u8217's passage?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The verse concerning the {\i kohen gadol} lists: {\i almanah, grushah, v\u8217'chalalah, zonah es eileh lo yikach}. Three of these \u8212- {\i grushah}, {\i chalalah}, {\i zonah} \u8212- are already forbidden to a regular {\i kohen}. The only genuinely new addition is {\i almanah}. So why repeat the others?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Grushah Enumeration\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 By the regular {\i kohen}, the Torah separates {\i grushah} syntactically from {\i chalalah} and {\i zonah}: it says {\i ishah chalalah v\u8217'zonah lo yikachu} and then, separately, {\i v\u8217'ishah grushah me\u8217'ishah lo yikachu}. This separation teaches that each designation \u8212- {\i grushah}, {\i chalalah}, {\i zonah} \u8212- constitutes its own independent prohibition, for which separate {\i malkus} may be administered. The same principle \u8212- that each {\i shem} stands independently \u8212- applies equally to the {\i kohen gadol}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 By the {\i kohen gadol}, then, the repetition of {\i grushah} serves not merely to reiterate a known prohibition, but to signal that just as {\i grushah} is separated from {\i chalalah} and {\i zonah} by the regular {\i kohen} to indicate independent prohibitions, so too each status in the {\i kohen gadol}\u8217's verse is an independent {\i lav}, including the newly added {\i almanah}: {\i kach almanah chaluka mi\u8217'grushah v\u8217'chalalah v\u8217'zonah b\u8217'Kohen Gadol} \u8212- the {\i almanah} is to be treated as a distinct {\i lav} in its own right, distinct from the other enumerated statuses.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chalalah Context\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i braita} then asks: why does the verse by the {\i kohen gadol} repeat {\i chalalah}? The answer: {\i ein chalalah ela mi\u8217'issur kehunah} \u8212- the repetition of {\i chalalah} in the context of {\i almanah} and {\i grushah}, which are both specifically {\i issurei kehunah}, places {\i chalalah} squarely within that same domain. As Rashi explains, the proximity of {\i chalalah} to {\i almanah} and {\i grushah} in the verse is contextually determinative: {\i chalalah} belongs to the same category as those prohibitions, i.e., prohibitions that pertain specifically to {\i kohanim}. The {\i zonah} status, by contrast, can arise from relations that are universally forbidden and that apply to non-{\i kohanim} as well.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Zonah Linkage\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i braita} continues: {\i zona lama ne\u8217'emra} \u8212- why does the verse by the {\i kohen gadol} mention {\i zonah} at all? The answer: {\i ne\u8217'emar kan zona v\u8217'ne\u8217'emar lehalan zona} \u8212- because the word {\i zona} appears in both passages, a textual linkage ({\i gezeirah shavah} or analogy) is created. By the {\i kohen gadol}, the verse states {\i v\u8217'lo yechalel zaro} \u8212- he shall not desecrate his offspring. This concept of {\i yechalel zaro}, that the child born of a forbidden union is a {\i chalal}, had not been stated explicitly by the regular {\i kohen}. The repetition of {\i zona} in both passages links them and teaches that the concept of {\i lo yechalel zaro} applies to a regular {\i kohen} as well.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Ashi Conclusion\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Ashi draws the practical conclusion from all of the foregoing: {\i Kohen haba al achoso, zona mashvi lah, chalalah lo mashvi lah} \u8212- a {\i kohen} who has relations with his sister renders her a {\i zonah} (since the act is an illicit union) but does not render her a {\i chalalah} (since it is not an {\i issur kehunah}). However, {\i chozar u-va aleha} \u8212- if subsequently another {\i kohen} has relations with her now that she bears the status of {\i zonah}, that second act does constitute a forbidden union specific to {\i kehunah}, since {\i zonah} is among the women a {\i kohen} is prohibited from. That second act would therefore render her a {\i chalalah}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Lo Yikach/Lo Yechallel\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Yehudah Two Lashes\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now introduces a major principle through the statement of Rav Yehudah: {\i Kohen Gadol b\u8217'almanah lokeh shtayim} \u8212- a {\i kohen gadol} who has relations with an {\i almanah} receives two sets of lashes: {\i achas mishum lo yikach ve\u8217'achas mishum lo yechalel} \u8212- one for violating the prohibition of {\i lo yikach} (the prohibition against taking a forbidden woman in marriage) and one for violating {\i lo yechalel} (the prohibition of desecrating, understood here as rendering the woman a {\i chalalah}). Until now, the discussion had focused on {\i lo yikach} and the role of the act of relations in fulfilling or transgressing the marital prohibition. Rav Yehudah introduces the idea that {\i lo yechalel zaro} \u8212- normally read as forbidding the desecration of one\u8217's offspring \u8212- also constitutes a standalone prohibition on the {\i act of sex} itself, distinct from and in addition to the prohibition of {\i lo yikach}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might note that this potentially yields not two but three prohibitions: {\i lo yikach}, {\i lo yechalel} for rendering her a {\i chalalah}, and {\i lo yechalel zaro} for rendering the offspring a {\i chalal}. The Gemara addresses this: the statement of Rav Yehudah involves a case of {\i shelo gamar bi\u8217'aso} \u8212- where there was no completion of the sexual act in the full halakhic sense. Without {\i gmar bi\u8217'ah} \u8212- full completion \u8212- there is no concern that a child would be conceived, and therefore the third prohibition of {\i lo yechalel zaro} in the sense of the offspring does not apply. This is a remarkable halakhic move: rather than reading {\i lo yechalel zaro} as a prohibition triggered only upon the birth (or even conception) of an actual {\i chalal} child, the Gemara reads it as a prohibition structured around the act of relations itself \u8212- specifically, an act that has the character of one that could lead to conception.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Lo Yechallel Zaro\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 With {\i gmar bi\u8217'ah}, then, all three prohibitions would potentially apply. Without it, only two.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gamur Biah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 As noted, the operative concept of {\i gmar bi\u8217'ah} is determinative in distinguishing between a case where {\i lo yechalel zaro} is engaged and one where it is not. The Gemara\u8217's assumption here is that {\i lo yechalel zaro}, as a prohibition, attaches to the act of relations understood as reproductive \u8212- i.e., complete intercourse \u8212- rather than to the actual birth of a {\i chalal} child.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Almanah/Grushah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava then raises a challenge: the {\i braita} teaches that if a {\i kohen gadol} has relations with a woman who is simultaneously an {\i almanah} and a {\i grushah}, he receives lashes for {\i shnei shemos} \u8212- two distinct designations. If we take this at face value, it sounds like he receives exactly two sets of lashes. But according to Rav Yehudah\u8217's principle, each act that renders her a {\i chalalah} should generate an additional set of lashes for {\i lo yechalel}. So there ought to be more than two.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two Shemot\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara resolves this: {\i lo, shnei shemos al zeh u\u8217'shnei shemos al zeh} \u8212- no, the {\i braita} means two {\i shemot} for each of the two categories. For the {\i almanah} designation, he incurs lashes for {\i almanah} and for {\i lo yechalel}; for the {\i grushah} designation, he incurs lashes for {\i grushah} and for {\i lo yechalel}. Even though the woman is rendered a {\i chalalah} only once by the single act, since each prohibition \u8212- {\i almanah} and {\i grushah} \u8212- independently carries the potential to render her a {\i chalalah}, each one independently triggers the {\i lo yechalel} prohibition as well. The result is that for each {\i shem}, there are always two sets of lashes.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Grushah/Chalutzah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then challenges this reading from the {\i seifa} of the same {\i braita}: {\i grushah va\u8217'chalutzah eino chayav ela achas} \u8212- if the woman is a {\i grushah} and a {\i chalutzah} rather than an {\i almanah} and a {\i grushah}, he is liable for only {\i achat} \u8212- one. This sounds like a single set of lashes, which would contradict the structure of two-per-{\i shem} that was just established. The Gemara resolves: {\i hachi ka\u8217'amar, eino chayav ela al achas} \u8212- the {\i braita} means he is liable only for one {\i category} (since {\i chalutzah} is {\i derabanan}), but within that one category the lashes are still two sets. The {\i braita} says {\i achat} in contrast to the two full categories of the {\i almanah}/{\i grushah} case, not in contrast to the doubled lashes within a single category.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Notably, this leads the Gemara to affirm that {\i chalutzah} is {\i derabanan}. A {\i braita} does derive {\i chalutzah} from the verse {\i v\u8217'ishah grushah me\u8217'ishah} via the extra {\i vav}, but the Gemara dismisses this as a mere {\i asmachta} \u8212- a textual anchor for a rabbinic ruling rather than a genuine biblical derivation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Each Shemot\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 To summarize the structure: each {\i shem} \u8212- each independent prohibited designation \u8212- carries with it two sets of lashes: one for the {\i shem} itself and one for the {\i lo yechalel} that attaches to any act by a {\i kohen} (or specifically a {\i kohen gadol}, as we will see) that renders a woman a {\i chalalah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rashi/Rambam\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rashi reads this entire {\i sugya} as applying to all {\i kohanim}, not only to the {\i kohen gadol}. Whenever a {\i kohen} has forbidden relations with a woman, and that act renders her a {\i chalalah}, the {\i kohen} incurs an additional set of lashes for {\i lo yechalel}, since each act of forbidden relations by a {\i kohen} with an otherwise-eligible woman creates {\i chalalah} status as a byproduct of the violation. On Rashi\u8217's reading, this is a general feature of the {\i issur kehunah} that applies regardless of whether the {\i kohen} is a {\i kohen gadol} or a {\i kohen hedyot}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rambam reads it otherwise. The {\i psukim} of {\i lo yechalel} are stated specifically in the context of the {\i kohen gadol}, and there are compelling reasons to limit the rule to that case. First, the textual locus of {\i lo yechalel} is the {\i kohen gadol} passage. Second, and perhaps more consequentially: by any prohibition applicable to a regular {\i kohen} \u8212- {\i grushah}, {\i zonah}, etc. \u8212- the woman was already forbidden before the act; rendering her additionally a {\i chalalah} adds a new status but does not materially expand her forbidden territory. By contrast, a {\i kohen gadol} who has relations with an {\i almanah} transforms a woman who was previously permissible to all {\i kohanim} into one who is now forbidden to all {\i kohanim}. The act of desecration has concrete and expansive effect specifically in that case. For the Rambam, therefore, the prohibition of {\i lo yechalel} as an independent {\i lav} triggered by sex alone applies only to a {\i kohen gadol}, not to a {\i kohen hedyot}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Marriage vs.\u160 ?Sex\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Abaye Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara now turns to what is perhaps the most consequential structural question of the {\i sugya}: where precisely does the prohibition of the {\i kohen} reside \u8212- in the act of marriage, in the act of relations, or in both? Abaye states: {\i kidesh lokeh, ba\u8217'al lokeh} \u8212- when he betroths (performs {\i kiddushin}), he receives lashes; when he has relations, he receives lashes. {\i Kidesh lokeh mishum lo yikach, ba\u8217'al lokeh mishum lo yechalel} \u8212- the {\i kiddushin} itself violates {\i lo yikach}, and the subsequent sexual relations violate {\i lo yechalel}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rashi Interpretation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rashi qualifies this significantly. He does not read Abaye as saying that the act of {\i kiddushin} itself, as a formal legal transaction, triggers {\i malkus}. Throughout the {\i sugya}, the focus has always been on the act of relations as the site of transgression \u8212- indeed, the Gemara elsewhere established that a {\i kohen} who repeats the act with the same forbidden woman receives lashes each time, demonstrating that it is the sex and not the betrothal that is the operative violation. What Abaye means, on Rashi\u8217's reading, is conceptual: when {\i kiddushin} is followed by sex, one set of lashes is attributed conceptually to the {\i lo yikach} of the {\i kiddushin}, and a second to the {\i lo yechalel} of the relations.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava disagrees: {\i ba\u8217'al lokeh, lo ba\u8217'al eino lokeh} \u8212- he receives lashes only when he actually has relations; without relations, no lashes are incurred for {\i kiddushin} alone. The reason: {\i mishum d\u8217'ksiv lo yikach v\u8217'lo yechalel, mah ta\u8217'am lo yikach? mishum lo yechalel} \u8212- the verse writes {\i lo yikach} and {\i lo yechalel} in juxtaposition, and the implication is that the reason for {\i lo yikach} is precisely because it leads to {\i lo yechalel}. The prohibition of marriage, therefore, is operative only when it is in fact followed by sexual relations. {\i Lo yikach} that does not lead to {\i lo yechalel} \u8212- i.e., {\i kiddushin} without subsequent sex \u8212- does not trigger lashes.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Machzir Gerushah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A further refinement: Abaye concedes that in the case of {\i machzir gerushato} \u8212- a man who remarries his divorced wife after she has been married to another man \u8212- even he agrees that {\i kiddushin} alone does not trigger lashes. The reason is that the verse in that case says {\i lakachtah lihyos lo l\u8217'ishah} \u8212- the Torah frames the prohibition around the act of taking her to be a wife in the full relational sense, i.e., including the sexual consummation. Without the sex, the prohibition is not complete. Abaye\u8217's position that {\i kiddushin} by itself is a violation applies to the standard {\i issurei kehunah}, not to {\i machzir gerushato}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sex Alone\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava, however, concedes in the opposite direction for the case of a {\i kohen gadol} with an {\i almanah}: {\i u\u8217'modeh Rava b\u8217'Kohen Gadol b\u8217'almanah she\u8217'im ba\u8217'al v\u8217'lo kidesh she\u8217'loke} \u8212- even Rava agrees that if the {\i kohen gadol} had relations without any prior {\i kiddushin}, he still receives lashes, because the verse says {\i v\u8217'lo yechalel zaro b\u8217'amav} \u8212- and he did in fact desecrate, having rendered her a {\i chalalah} through the act itself.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Both Abaye and Rava agree, however, that by {\i machzir gerushato}, if he had relations without {\i kiddushin}, no lashes apply \u8212- {\i derech likuchim asra Torah} \u8212- the Torah\u8217's prohibition in that case is framed specifically around the act of taking (i.e., marriage), not the act of relations standing alone.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Abaye/Rava Summary\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 To restate the positions clearly: for {\i machzir gerushato}, both Abaye and Rava agree that marriage {\i and} sex are both required \u8212- neither alone suffices. For the {\i kohen gadol} with an {\i almanah}, both agree that sex alone transgresses {\i lo yechalel}. The debate between Abaye and Rava concerns {\i kiddushin} without sex in the standard {\i issurei kehunah} context: Abaye says this independently violates {\i lo yikach}; Rava says it does not, since {\i lo yikach} is only operative when it leads to {\i lo yechalel}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rambam Limitation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 For the Rambam, as noted above, this entire framework \u8212- in which sex alone, without {\i kiddushin}, constitutes a violation through {\i lo yechalel} \u8212- is limited to a {\i kohen gadol}. A {\i kohen hedyot} always requires the context of {\i lo yikach}; the prohibition is grounded in the act of marriage, and sex divorced from the marital framework, while generating {\i chalalah} status and being otherwise {\i assur}, does not constitute a separate biblical transgression of {\i lo yechalel} by a {\i kohen hedyot}. This distinction is practically significant in cases where {\i poskim} grapple with a {\i kohen} who has had relations with a {\i grushah} without formal {\i kiddushin}: according to the Rambam, there is no biblical transgression in that scenario, even as the children would be {\i challalim} and she would become a {\i chalalah}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Ger\u8217's Daughter\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Yehudah Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i sugya} now transitions to a {\i braita} concerning the status of a {\i ger}\u8217's daughter. Rabbi Yehudah holds: {\i bas ger zachar kebas chalal} \u8212- the daughter of a male convert is like the daughter of a male {\i chalal} and is therefore a {\i chalalah}, disqualified for {\i kohanim}. This is a far-reaching position, and it must be noted at the outset that we do not rule according to Rabbi Yehudah here.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Logic Argument\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yehudah supports this with what appears to be a logical derivation: {\i mah challal sheba mitipa kesheira bito pesulah} \u8212- a {\i chalal}, who was born from a technically valid (\u8220"kosher\u8221") drop of seed yet is disqualified because of the forbidden nature of his parents\u8217' union, has a daughter who is {\i pesulah}. {\i Ger sheba mitipa pesulah} \u8212- a {\i ger}, who was born from seed that is \u8220"invalid\u8221" in the sense that it preceded his joining the Jewish people, {\i eino din shebito pesulah} \u8212- should certainly have a {\i pesulah} daughter. The argument seems to be that if the {\i chalal}\u8217's flawed status (arising from a sin) makes his daughter invalid, then the {\i ger}\u8217's even more fundamental prior status of non-Jewishness should equally or more strongly disqualify his daughter.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gemarah Challenge\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara challenges this immediately: {\i mah lechallal shekein yetziraso ba\u8217'aveira} \u8212- what makes a {\i chalal} different is precisely that his very creation ({\i yetzira}) came through sin \u8212- through a forbidden act of relations between a {\i kohen} and a woman forbidden to him. Can we derive anything from this about a {\i ger}, who was not \u8220"created in sin\u8221" in any such sense?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then invokes a counterexample: {\i kohen gadol be\u8217'almanah yochiach} \u8212- look at the case of the {\i chalalah} who comes from a {\i kohen gadol}\u8217's union with an {\i almanah}. There, {\i ein yetzirasa ba\u8217'aveira} \u8212- her becoming a {\i chalalah} does not involve her being created through sin in the sense of birth; she herself is made a {\i chalalah} through an act of forbidden sex. So the category of {\i chalalah} is not limited to cases of creation through sin.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two Ways Chalalah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This establishes that a woman becomes a {\i chalalah} in one of two ways: either by being born from a {\i chalal} (and thus \u8220"created\u8221" in proximity to sin), or by herself participating in an act of forbidden relations specific to {\i kehunah} (and thus through an act of sin). In neither of these two cases is the {\i ger} implicated in any obvious way.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Common Denominator\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara attempts to construct a common denominator ({\i tzad hashaveh}) by combining both ways of becoming {\i chalalah}: the {\i chalal} (whose daughter becomes a {\i chalalah}) and the {\i almanah} who had relations with a {\i kohen gadol} (who herself becomes a {\i chalalah}) \u8212- what do they share? {\i She\u8217'einan b\u8217'rov hakahal} \u8212- neither is representative of the majority of the population. They occupy some marginal or exceptional status. And therefore, one might argue, a {\i ger} \u8212- who is also not part of the normative majority \u8212- should similarly disqualify his daughter.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Too Broad\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara is dissatisfied with this: the commonality is far too broad and generic to be halakhically meaningful. Almost any exceptional population could be swept in on such a standard.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Aveira Element\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A more refined common denominator is then proposed: what the {\i chalal} and the {\i almanah}-made-{\i chalalah} actually share is the presence of {\i aveira} \u8212- transgression \u8212- in some form. The {\i chalal} owes his status to the sin of his parents\u8217' union; the woman owes her {\i chalalah} status to the forbidden act she participated in. There is, in each case, an element of sin underlying the disqualification.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mitzri Rishon\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 But this too has a flaw. The Gemara counters with the case of {\i Mitzri rishon yochiach} \u8212- a first-generation Egyptian can serve as a counterexample. His son (a second-generation Egyptian) is forbidden (to the community, not only to {\i kohanim}), yet there was no act of sin involved in the child\u8217's birth. The Gemara goes through the familiar dialectic of {\i chalal yochiach} and {\i chozar hadin} \u8212- each proposed counterexample is met with a counter-counterexample, and the logical circle continues without yielding a clean principle. The attempt to establish {\i ger} as part of the category through a {\i tzad hashaveh} ultimately fails to produce a coherent, satisfying derivation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Final Application\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Despite the failure to produce a logically compelling argument, the Gemara notes: {\i l\u8217'Rabbi Yehudah ger nami posel b\u8217'vi\u8217'ato} \u8212- Rabbi Yehudah extends his position even to hold that the act of a {\i ger} having relations with a woman would render her a {\i chalalah}, just as he holds that the {\i ger}\u8217's daughter is a {\i chalalah}. His position is internally consistent: once one accepts that {\i ger} status is categorically disqualifying for {\i kehunah} purposes, all of its implications follow, including that a {\i ger} cannot make a woman suitable for {\i kehunah} through his relations with her. Yet the difficulty of the logic is, as noted, something that the Gemara itself presses without resolution. We do not rule like Rabbi Yehudah.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Alternate Positions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Eliezer\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Eliezer ben Yaakov offers a more circumscribed position: only the offspring of a {\i ger} and a {\i giyoret} together are disqualified from {\i kehunah}. If only one parent was a convert and the other was a natural-born Jew, the daughter is permitted to a {\i kohen}. The reasoning flows naturally: a {\i giyoret} herself is disqualified from {\i kehunah}; a daughter born of both a {\i ger} and a {\i giyoret} is similarly disqualified. But if one parent provides naturally-born Jewish status, that parent\u8217's lineage is sufficient.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Shimon Age\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Shimon ben Yochai takes a different approach entirely, focusing on age of conversion rather than parentage: {\i giyoret p\u8217'chutah mibat shalosh shanim v\u8217'yom echad kesherah l\u8217'kehunah} \u8212- a female convert who converted before the age of three years and one day is {\i kesherah} for {\i kehunah}. His concern, as will become clear, is not with lineage per se but with the presumption that she may have had sexual relations while not Jewish \u8212- a presumption that does not apply to a child converted under the age of three.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Midianite Source\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The textual basis invoked for Rabbi Shimon\u8217's position is the verse regarding the women of Midian: {\i v\u8217'chol hataf banashim hachayu lachem} \u8212- and all the young girls, keep alive for yourselves. This implies that the surviving girls were kept for the purpose of marriage, including marriage to Pinchas, who was a {\i kohen}. Since Pinchas is explicitly included among those who could take them ({\i b\u8217'lo Pinchas hayu imahem}), young girls from among non-Jewish nations, when they are below the age of three, are deemed suitable for {\i kehunah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbanan Alternative\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Rabbanan} reject this reading of the verse: {\i v\u8217'rabbanan, hachayu lachem l\u8217'avadim ul\u8217'shfachot} \u8212- they say the verse means the girls were kept as servants and maidservants, not as potential wives. The verse therefore cannot serve as a source for permitting young {\i giyorot} to {\i kohanim}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yechezkel Pasuk\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 All of these positions \u8212- Rabbi Yehudah, Rabbi Eliezer ben Yaakov, Rabbi Shimon ben Yochai, and the {\i Rabbanan} \u8212- are derived from a single verse in Yechezkel: {\i almanah u\u8217'grushah lo yikchu lahem l\u8217'nashim ki im betulot mizera beit Yisrael} \u8212- widows and divorc\u233 ?es they shall not take as wives, but only virgins from the seed of the house of Israel. This verse, notably, reads like a description of the {\i kohen gadol}\u8217's restrictions (since the regular {\i kohen} may marry an {\i almanah}), yet it is read here as the textual anchor for the broader question of who qualifies as {\i beit Yisrael} for purposes of {\i kehunah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Zera Beit Yisrael\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yehudah reads {\i mizera beit Yisrael} with the emphasis on {\i zera} \u8212- seed \u8212- in a patrilineal sense: {\i ad d\u8217'ika kol zera miYisrael} \u8212- as long as there is fully Jewish male seed, meaning the father must be a natural-born Jew. A {\i ger}, regardless of the mother\u8217's status, lacks this {\i zera Yisrael} in the paternal line, and therefore his daughter is disqualified. Conversely, a Jewish father and a {\i giyoret} mother would produce a {\i kesherah} daughter, since the seed (father\u8217's side) is fully Jewish. This is a far more coherent derivation than the attempted {\i tzad hashaveh} above, and it grounds the disqualification not in any problematization of the {\i ger} himself but in a criterion of patrilineal Jewish origin.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Miktzat Zera\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Eliezer ben Yaakov reads {\i mizera} more leniently: {\i va\u8217'afilu miktzat zera} \u8212- even part of the seed being from {\i beit Yisrael} suffices. As long as one parent \u8212- mother or father \u8212- is a natural-born Jew, the requirement is met. Only when both parents are converts does the daughter fail to qualify.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Yosei\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yosei understands {\i mizera beit Yisrael} temporally: {\i mishenizre\u8217'u beYisrael} \u8212- as long as she was conceived among Jews, i.e., while her parents were already Jewish. On this reading, even the daughter of two converts is permitted, since both parents were Jewish at the time of her conception. The only woman truly disqualified is one who was herself born or conceived before her parents converted \u8212- i.e., the {\i giyoret} herself.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Core Issue\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This divergence of opinion reveals the fundamental halakhic question underlying the entire {\i sugya}: is the disqualification of a {\i giyoret} from {\i kehunah} rooted in a concern about {\i zenut} \u8212- the presumption that she may have had forbidden sexual relations while not Jewish, the position associated with Rabbi Shimon bar Yochai? Or is it rooted in a separate criterion of {\i mizera beit Yisrael} \u8212- Jewish lineage or Jewish-conceived origin \u8212- which has nothing to do with sexual history and might well be {\i derabanan} since the source is a verse in Yechezkel rather than the Torah itself? This is a question of considerable practical importance, since the two approaches would generate different distinctions: the {\i zenut}-based approach lends itself to an age-of-conversion analysis (since a child under three cannot have had relations), while the {\i zera Yisrael} approach generates lineage-based distinctions regardless of age.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Betuloteha\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Shimon bar Yochai advances his position further: {\i mishenizre\u8217'u betuloha beYisrael} \u8212- the disqualifying concern is that she lost her {\i betulot} \u8212- her virginity \u8212- while not Jewish. The age of three is the operative threshold because it is understood that the hymen is regenerated up to that age; a girl converted before age three, even if something had occurred, would not have lost her virginity in a halakhically meaningful sense. Accordingly, only a {\i giyoret} who converted at age three or older might be presumed to have had relations as a non-Jew and is therefore disqualified. One who converted younger than three is {\i kesherah} for {\i kehunah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i sugya} thus closes with a rich set of positions on the question of who qualifies as {\i beit Yisrael} for purposes of {\i kehunah}: whether the criterion is entirely patrilineal natural-born Jewish seed (Rabbi Yehudah), partially Jewish seed from either parent (Rabbi Eliezer ben Yaakov), Jewish conception regardless of convert status (Rabbi Yosei), or rather a concern with sexual history resolved by an age threshold (Rabbi Shimon bar Yochai). The normative ruling follows none of the most restrictive positions: a {\i giyoret} conceived after her parents\u8217' conversion is {\i kesherah}, in accordance with Rabbi Yosei\u8217's principle that {\i mishenizre\u8217'u beYisrael} is the operative standard.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Kiddushin 79\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Kohein & Forbidden Women\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chalalah vs.\u160 ?Zonah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Today\u8217's daf is 79, and we begin where we left off at the very bottom of 78a, dealing with the issue of the women who are forbidden to a {\i kohein}. Yesterday\u8217's discussion established several critical distinctions: first, the difference between the definition of a {\i chalalah} and a {\i zonah}; and second, the crucial question of whether the prohibition is one of marriage or of sexual relations.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} makes clear regarding the {\i kohein gadol} that the prohibition of {\i lo yikach} is violated by marriage followed by sex, while according to Abaye marriage alone constitutes that prohibition. The prohibition of {\i lo yechalel} is triggered by sex alone. In the case of {\i machzir grushaso} \u8212- which is unrelated to {\i kehunah} specifically \u8212- it is marriage culminating in sex that constitutes the violation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kohein Hedyot Dispute\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A question the {\i gemara} never directly resolves is what applies to a normal {\i kohein hedyot}. The Ravad and Rashi hold that a {\i kohein hedyot} is governed by the same rules as the {\i kohein gadol}, and that there is a prohibition of sexual relations alone. The Rambam, by contrast, holds that for a {\i kohein hedyot} the prohibition is marriage alone \u8212- there is no freestanding prohibition of sexual relations.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This distinction carries significant practical weight. When a {\i kohein} and a {\i giyores} or a {\i kohein} and a {\i gerushah} wish to marry, and no avenue exists to establish that the woman was not truly a {\i gerushah} or that the {\i kohein} is not truly a {\i kohein}, the Rambam\u8217's position offers a meaningful, if incomplete, consideration: according to him, without marriage they would not be in violation of the core prohibition \u8212- though the children would still be {\i chalalim}. This is because when a {\i kohein} has sexual relations with a woman forbidden to him as a {\i kohein}, the woman becomes a {\i chalalah} and the children become {\i chalalim}, and that consequence applies even to a {\i kohein hedyot}. So while not a full resolution, it is at least noteworthy that even as the children bear the status of {\i chalalim}, the Rambam\u8217's position forecloses a prohibition absent marriage.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Giyores Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 We then turned to the prohibition of a {\i giyores}, which is nowhere stated explicitly in the {\i Torah}. Accordingly, the {\i gemara} derives it from the {\i pasuk} in Yechezkel that states the {\i kohanim} may only marry {\i besulos mi-zera beit yisrael} \u8212- virgins of Jewish descent. According to several {\i rishonim}, this derivation renders the prohibition {\i d\u8217'rabbanan} or {\i divrei sofrim}, not {\i d\u8217'oraisa}. According to others, the {\i pasuk} in Yechezkel reveals the {\i Torah}\u8217's own definition of {\i zonah}, thereby making the prohibition {\i d\u8217'oraisa} \u8212- though, as noted, it is somewhat paradoxical to categorize a convert as a {\i zonah}, since that category seems linked to the concept of sexual activity.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Giyores Positions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Four tannaitic positions emerge. Rabbi Yehuda holds that the disqualification stems from a kind of stigmatic status attached to having originated as a non-Jew, and this can even be transmitted paternally \u8212- such that the daughter of a male {\i ger} is also {\i pesulah} to a {\i kohein}. Rabbi Eliezer ben Yaakov holds that if one parent is a born Jew there is no problem, but if both parents are {\i geirim}, the disqualification passes down. Rabbi Yossi holds that the prohibition attaches only to the woman who actually converted herself \u8212- a child of {\i geirim} is not disqualified. Rabbi Shimon bar Yochai, the most lenient position, holds that it is only a problem if the woman converted at the age of three or older; under that age it is not a problem.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 De\u8217'oraisa/Derabbanan\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i nafka mina} between the {\i d\u8217'oraisa} and {\i d\u8217'rabbanan} positions is significant in multiple directions. If the prohibition is only {\i d\u8217'rabbanan}, many {\i poskim} hold that children of such a union between a {\i kohein} and a {\i giyores} would not be {\i chalalim}, since a rabbinic category of {\i chalal} has no clear precedent (though one might note the interesting question raised by a {\i chalutzah}\u8217's child). On the other hand, if the prohibition is {\i d\u8217'rabbanan}, it rests entirely and absolutely on the {\i pasuk} in Yechezkel concerning Jewish descent \u8212- end of story. Whereas if we understand the {\i pasuk} as elucidating the {\i Torah}\u8217's category of {\i zonah}, then it becomes somewhat linked to a concept of sexual activity, which potentially opens different analytical avenues. Indeed, a Rashi in Yevamot appears to suggest \u8212- against everything else we have said \u8212- that a {\i giyores} is only problematic if she actually had sexual relations as a non-Jewish woman.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Modern Applications\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This framework raises interesting contemporary questions that some {\i poskim} have begun to explore, albeit without formal written responsa. Consider a child adopted as an infant into a {\i frum} Jewish home and converted at a very young age \u8212- where the entire premise that being raised as a non-Jew correlates with premarital sexual activity seems inapplicable. Could such a case be distinguished within the {\i zonah} framework? However, our {\i gemara} itself offers no support for an approach grounded in concern about actual sexual behavior. All the rules are derived from the {\i pasuk} in Yechezkel, and the very fact that even a woman who converted {\i in utero} \u8212- having been conceived as a non-Jew when her pregnant mother converted \u8212- is still categorized as forbidden to a {\i kohein} according to many {\i rishonim} (on the basis of the phrase {\i nizre\u8217'ah b\u8217'yisrael}, meaning conceived as a Jew), leaves no room in our {\i sugya} for that line of reasoning. Still, the {\i rishonim} who frame the prohibition as ultimately implicating the {\i zonah} category do introduce at least a conceptual linkage to sexual conduct, and that is at least worth noting as one explores the outer parameters.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Yechezkel Pasuk\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Pasuk Division\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 We now turn to the final line of 78a and the opening of 78b. Rav Nachman poses a challenge to Rava: the {\i pasuk} in Yechezkel appears to address two different categories of {\i kohanim} simultaneously \u8212- its opening addresses the {\i kohein gadol} (prohibiting marriage with a widow, an {\i almanah}), while its conclusion, {\i ki im besulos mi-zera beit yisrael}, is the source we have been using for the prohibition applicable to a {\i kohein hedyot} regarding a {\i giyores}. How can a single {\i pasuk} address two different groups of people?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shmuel Precedent\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} responds affirmatively: yes, a {\i pasuk} can be written in this fashion, with one part addressing one group and another part addressing another. As precedent, the {\i gemara} cites the story of Shmuel, where the verse reads: {\i ve-ner Elokim terem yichbe ve-Shmuel shochev be-heichal Hashem} \u8212- \u8220"the lamp of God had not yet been extinguished, and Shmuel was lying in the sanctuary of God.\u8221" But this is problematic, since no one is permitted to sit \u8212- let alone lie down \u8212- in the {\i azarah}, the Temple courtyard, except for kings of the house of David. The {\i gemara} therefore reconfigures the verse: the phrase {\i be-heichal Hashem} is detached from the second clause and reattached to the first \u8212- the lamp of God had not yet been extinguished {\i in the sanctuary of God}, and Shmuel was lying in his own place, outside the {\i azarah}. This demonstrates that a {\i pasuk} can be divided, with its internal elements redistributed, to resolve a textual difficulty.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Almana Interpretation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Applying this principle to the Yechezkel {\i pasuk}, the {\i gemara} reads: {\i ve-ha\u8217'almana asher tihye almana mi-kohein yikchu} \u8212- a widow who is a widow from a {\i kohein} they may marry. This immediately raises a question: does this mean a {\i kohein hedyot} may marry the widow of a {\i kohein} but not the widow of a {\i yisrael}? The {\i gemara} clarifies: {\i mikohein yikchu} means {\i mi-she\u8217'ar kohanim yikchu} \u8212- all the other {\i kohanim} (the {\i kohanim hedyot}) may marry an {\i almanah}. The structure of the {\i pasuk} is therefore: the opening prohibits the {\i kohein gadol} from marrying an {\i almanah} or a {\i gerushah}; the middle section \u8212- {\i ki im besulos mi-zera beit yisrael} \u8212- applies the requirement of Jewish lineage to the {\i kohein hedyot}; and the final clause permits all other {\i kohanim} to marry a widow.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is precisely why there were those who sought to suppress Sefer Yechezkel \u8212- because its plain meaning contradicts the {\i Torah}, and we are reading it in a way designed to reconcile the contradiction.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Widow-Daughter Rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A {\i baraita} is then cited in support of this reading: {\i mikohein yikchu, mi-she\u8217'ar kohanim yikchu}. Rabbi Yehuda, however, reads the phrase differently: {\i min ha-masi\u8217'im li-khunah yikchu} \u8212- a {\i kohein hedyot} may marry a widow from men who are eligible to marry their daughters to {\i kohanim}. The class of men who cannot give their daughters to a {\i kohein} includes a {\i ger} \u8212- this follows Rabbi Yehuda\u8217's own position that the daughter of a male {\i ger} is like the daughter of a {\i chalal} and is {\i pesulah} to a {\i kohein}. Accordingly, the widow of a {\i ger} also may not marry a {\i kohein}, since his wife too assumes a disqualified status through him. The operative principle stated explicitly in the {\i baraita} is: {\i kol she-atah nosei bito atah nosei almanato} \u8212- whoever\u8217's daughter you may marry, you may marry his widow; whoever\u8217's daughter you may not marry, you may not marry his widow. This is how we reconcile the Yechezkel {\i pasuk} with the {\i Torah}\u8217's law, and from this {\i pasuk} we derive the prohibition of a {\i kohein} marrying a {\i giyores}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Giyores Ruling\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Halacha k\u8217'Rabbi Yossi\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now states the definitive ruling. Rav Hamnuna in the name of Ulla declares: {\i halacha ke-Rabbi Yose} \u8212- we rule like Rabbi Yossi. A child of converts, even of two converts, is therefore not disqualified; the prohibition attaches only to the woman who herself converted. Rabbah bar bar Chana similarly declares: {\i halacha ke-Rabbi Yose}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Post-Destruction Practice\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 That said, the {\i gemara} immediately qualifies: {\i u-mi-yom she-charav Beit ha-Mikdash nahagu kohanim silsul be-atzman ke-Rabbi Eliezer ben Yaakov} \u8212- from the day the {\i Beit ha-Mikdash} was destroyed, the {\i kohanim} adopted a higher level of stringency for themselves in the matter of lineage, and they conducted themselves in practice like Rabbi Eliezer ben Yaakov. It is striking that they adopted this elevated standard specifically after the destruction rather than during the period of Temple service. One might have expected greater stringency when they were actively serving \u8212- but perhaps this represents a defiant affirmation of {\i kedushah} in the absence of the {\i Mikdash}: we have lost the Temple, but we will not allow our sanctity to diminish.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Prospective/Retroactive\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Nachman further refines the practical ruling: if someone comes to ask a {\i she\u8217'elah} before the fact, one rules like Rabbi Eliezer ben Yaakov \u8212- a {\i kohein} should not marry the daughter of two converts. But {\i be-di\u8217'avad}, once the couple is already married, one rules like Rabbi Yossi \u8212- the {\i kiddushin} is valid and we do not require them to separate. These are among the more challenging cases in {\i halacha} today, and they arise with some regularity.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Mamzer Claims\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Father Not Believed\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} transitions to a new {\i mishnah}: {\i ha-omer beni zeh mamzer} \u8212- if a man declares that his son is a {\i mamzer}, he is not believed. This follows from two considerations. The first is the principle of {\i ein adam meisim atzmo rasha} \u8212- a person cannot testify to his own wickedness. The second, and perhaps more analytically primary reason, is that he is in effect testifying about someone who may not legally be his son at all. If the mother is known and is his wife, and she bears no familial relationship to him, then the only way a {\i mamzer} status would arise is if someone else fathered the child \u8212- in which case, he has less standing to testify about this child than any other individual, since the child would not be \u8220"his son\u8221" in the relevant sense.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Legal Basis\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} elaborates: this is partly {\i ein adam meisim atzmo rasha} and partly the principle that a single witness \u8212- an {\i ed echad} \u8212- is insufficient for matters of personal status. A claim of {\i mamzerut} constitutes a {\i davar she-be-ervah}, which requires no fewer than two witnesses. Even someone who is epistemically positioned to know \u8212- as the mother certainly is \u8212- cannot establish the status through her testimony alone, both because she is a single witness and because she is a woman.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Parents Not Believed\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} states that even if both the mother and the father agree and jointly declare the child to be a {\i mamzer}, they are still not believed. This is a {\i chiddush}. The {\i mishnah} goes further: {\i ve-afilu sheneihem al ubar she-be-me\u8217'eha} \u8212- even if they make this declaration while the child is still in the womb, they are not believed. The {\i gemara} explains the structure of this ruling: {\i lo mibaya} he is not believed \u8212- for how does a father reliably know enough to establish that he is not the biological father? But even she, who clearly does know who the father is, is not believed, because it remains a {\i davar she-be-ervah} requiring two witnesses, and she is but one. And not only in a case where the child has been born with an established presumption of legitimacy \u8212- {\i chazakah de-kashrut} \u8212- but even where the child is still in utero and no such presumption has yet attached, they are still not believed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Yehuda\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yehuda dissents and holds that the father {\i is} believed. The source is the word {\i yakir} in the {\i pasuk}: {\i ki et ha-bechor ben ha-snuah yakir latet lo pi shenayim} \u8212- the father shall \u8220"recognize\u8221" his firstborn son to give him a double portion. The {\i gemara} interprets: {\i yakirenu le-acheirim} \u8212- he may identify him to others; even if the world does not know who the firstborn son is, the father is believed to declare it. From here Rabbi Yehuda extends: just as the father is believed to identify his firstborn son, so too is he believed to say of his son, {\i zeh ben gerushah} or {\i zeh ben chalutzah} \u8212- this son\u8217's mother was a {\i gerushah} or a {\i chalutzah}, making the child a {\i chalal}. And by extension, Rabbi Yehuda would apparently grant similar credibility to a declaration of {\i mamzerut} \u8212- though the {\i gemara} acknowledges that claiming {\i mamzerut} by attributing paternity to another man is a considerably larger {\i chiddush}, since the father is then not even testifying about his own child. The {\i chachamim} reject Rabbi Yehuda\u8217's extension: they grant credibility to the father only for the purpose of {\i bechor} recognition, and no further.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Migo Argument\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Nachman bar Yitzchak then raises a sharp question: the position of Rabbi Yehuda is well-grounded \u8212- the word {\i yakir} constitutes a {\i chiddush} that the father is believed to identify the status of his child. But according to the {\i rabbanan}, who accept the father\u8217's testimony only for purposes of firstborn recognition, why does a {\i pasuk} need to tell us that? It should be self-evident from a {\i migo} argument: since the father, if he wished, could simply give this child a double portion as a gift, he should be believed to declare him a {\i bechor} and thereby grant him a double portion through inheritance, since he could have achieved the same result without any declaration at all.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Firstborn/Migo\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Future Earnings\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} resolves the {\i migo} argument as follows: a gift can only operate on property the father currently possesses. But the declaration of {\i bechor} takes effect on everything the father will earn over the remainder of his life \u8212- potentially decades of future accumulation. A gift given today could not capture future property; only the {\i pasuk} of {\i yakir} empowers a statement made years in advance to govern all subsequent acquisitions. Thus the {\i pasuk} is necessary.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then notes a further complication: according to Rabbi Meir, who holds that {\i adam makneh davar she-lo ba l\u8217'olam} \u8212- a person can transfer ownership over things not yet in existence \u8212- why would a {\i pasuk} be needed even for future earnings? One could simply give a gift today that takes effect on all future property. The {\i gemara} answers: {\i she-naflu ki-she-hu goses} \u8212- because the declaration covers even property that falls to the father when he is in his death throes ({\i goses}). A gift designated to take effect on property received at the moment of {\i goses} would not be effective, and therefore the {\i pasuk} is still necessary.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Goses Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This introduces a remarkable dispute between Rashi and Tosafot. Rashi understands that a {\i goses} \u8212- a person in the final throes of death, as distinct from a {\i shichiv mera} who is merely bedridden and gravely ill \u8212- has lost a certain dimension of legal agency. Though killing a {\i goses} remains murder, and they retain full personhood, Rashi treats them as analogous in some respects to a minor ({\i katan}): present as a human being but lacking full legal standing to execute transactions. Tosafot vigorously disputes this on the basis of other {\i gemarot}, and argues instead that the issue is purely practical: a {\i goses} typically can no longer speak. Therefore, even if one accepts Rabbi Meir\u8217's principle that {\i adam makneh davar she-lo ba l\u8217'olam}, such a transaction would require the capacity to perform the act at the time it takes effect \u8212- and a {\i goses} who cannot speak or signal cannot physically complete the transaction at that moment. The legal power is present, according to Tosafot, but the physical capacity is absent. This is a meaningful distinction: one might have thought that legal authority is sufficient even without physical capacity at the moment of effect, but Tosafot holds that {\i makneh davar she-lo ba l\u8217'olam} requires that one be physically able to execute the act at the time it becomes operative.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Shaliach Priority\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Father\u8217's Override\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} now takes up the law of agency in {\i kiddushin}: {\i mi she-natan reshus li-shlucho le-kadesh et bito} \u8212- a father who appoints a {\i shaliach} to betroth his daughter, and then himself goes and betroths her. If the father acted first, his act negates the {\i shlichus} and his {\i kiddushin} is binding. If the {\i shaliach} acted first, the {\i shlichus} was still operative at that time and the {\i shaliach}\u8217's {\i kiddushin} is binding and the father\u8217's is not.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Uncertainty Resolution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If it is unknown who acted first, both men must give a {\i get}, since either might be the true husband. And if they agree to a practical resolution, one gives a {\i get} and the other may then marry her \u8212- because either the husband has given the {\i get} and the other is now contracting a new marriage, or the non-husband has given an extraneous {\i get} and the actual husband is now marrying her. (It is worth noting parenthetically, as one student observed, that the option of one giving a {\i get} and the other marrying her would not be available if the man in question is a {\i kohein}, who may not marry a woman who has received a {\i get}.)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Woman\u8217's Override\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishnah} then presents the parallel case: a woman who appoints a {\i shaliach} to accept {\i kiddushin} on her behalf, and then herself goes and accepts {\i kiddushin} directly. If she acted first, her {\i kiddushin} is binding. A potential objection presents itself: by actively seeking to betroth herself before her {\i shaliach} has acted, has she not implicitly revoked the {\i shlichus}? The {\i mishnah} answers no \u8212- she has not revoked the {\i shaliach} merely by pursuing her own {\i kiddushin}, and until she actually succeeds in her own act, the {\i shaliach} retains authority. If the {\i shaliach} acted first, his act is binding even after she has already been trying to arrange her own {\i kiddushin}, and her subsequent act is too late.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Father\u8217's Reasoning\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} explains why we need to state both cases: had we only stated the father\u8217's case, one might have thought the reason the father\u8217's act prevails over the {\i shaliach}\u8217's (when the father acts first) is because {\i gavra kim leh be-yuchsin} \u8212- a father knows who is {\i meyuchas} for his daughter, and when he finds such a man and betroths his daughter himself, he is acting with genuine conviction, thereby definitively negating the {\i shlichus}. One might then have thought that in the woman\u8217's case, since her male {\i shaliach} presumably knows pedigree better than she does, her own act would never truly override the {\i shaliach} \u8212- she might be understood as acting only as a backup in case the {\i shaliach} fails, and she would always defer to whatever match her {\i shaliach} secures.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Woman\u8217's Reasoning\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Conversely, had we only stated the woman\u8217's case, one might have explained the rule by noting that {\i itata daika u-minsava} \u8212- a woman pays careful attention to whom she is marrying, and when she acts herself it reflects a genuine and definitive choice. One might then have concluded that the father, who will not have to live with his daughter\u8217's husband and may not particularly care which eligible man she marries, is perhaps also acting only as a backup, not intending to definitively override the {\i shaliach}. The {\i mishnah} therefore teaches both cases to make clear that each acts definitively when acting first, for the reasons specific to each.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Bogeres Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Scenario Setup\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now introduces a major dispute between Rav and Shmuel that will occupy the remainder of the {\i daf}. The case: a father betrothed his daughter while traveling on the road, and she betrothed herself in the city. We now observe her and find she is a {\i bogeres} \u8212- a fully mature young woman past the {\i naarah} stage. The critical question is: what was her status at the time of the respective acts of {\i kiddushin}? If she was a {\i naarah} when they acted, the father had legal authority over her {\i kiddushin} and his act is binding; if she was already a {\i bogeres}, she is legally independent and her act is binding, while the father\u8217's is not.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav holds: {\i harei hi bogeres lefaneinu} \u8212- she is a {\i bogeres} before us now. We therefore assume she was a {\i bogeres} at the time the acts of {\i kiddushin} were performed, which means the father had no authority, and her {\i kiddushin} is the binding one.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shmuel Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Shmuel holds: {\i chaishinan le-kiddushei sheneihem} \u8212- we are concerned about the validity of both betrothals. She began her life as a {\i ketana} and a {\i naarah}; we know she is now a {\i bogeres}, but we cannot determine at which point during the relevant period the transition occurred. Therefore, we do not know whose {\i kiddushin} was binding, and she requires a {\i get} from both men.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Six-Month Window\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} presses on the precise timing. If these acts occurred during the six-month window following her attainment of {\i naarah} status (marked by the appearance of two pubic hairs), how could Rav rule that she was already a {\i bogeres}? We generally presume that physical {\i bogrut} does not occur during those first six months. And if the acts occurred after the six months, why would Shmuel treat it as a {\i safek} \u8212- given that Shmuel himself rules that the maximum interval between {\i naarah} and {\i bogeres} is six months and no more? Once six months have passed, she is definitively a {\i bogeres}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} resolves: {\i lo tzricha d-kadish be-hahu yoma d\u8217'mashlim shisha} \u8212- the case is that both acts of {\i kiddushin} occurred on the very day the six months were completing, the day on which {\i bogrut} is expected to arrive. By the end of that day, Shmuel agrees she is definitely a {\i bogeres}; but within that day, the precise moment of transition is unknown. The father may have acted in the morning, before maturity arrived; she may have acted later, or vice versa. Rav holds that since she is a {\i bogeres} at the end of that day, we assume she was a {\i bogeres} at the beginning of the day \u8212- at the time the father acted \u8212- and the father\u8217's {\i kiddushin} was therefore invalid. Shmuel holds that we assume the signs of maturity arrived only at the moment we observe them, and that until then she retained her prior status of {\i naarah}, which means the father\u8217's {\i kiddushin} may well have been binding.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Mikvah/Chavit\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mikvah Validity\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now links this dispute to a classic tannaitic debate about a {\i mikvah} that was measured and found to be lacking \u8212- containing less than forty {\i se\u8217'ah}. We know it started with forty {\i se\u8217'ah}, but it has now been found deficient. The question is: what is the status of all the dunkings ({\i tevilos}) that relied on this {\i mikvah} in the past? The {\i baraita} states that all purifications performed upon it retroactively are {\i tamei} \u8212- whether they occurred in a {\i reshut ha-yachid} or in a {\i reshut ha-rabim}. Ordinarily, a {\i safek} of {\i tumah} in a {\i reshut ha-rabim} is ruled {\i tahor}, but here the ruling is {\i vadai tamei}, suggesting a definitive retroactive determination of invalidity consistent with Rav\u8217's approach.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tarti Le\u8217're\u8217'usa\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} probes how Shmuel can be reconciled with this ruling. The answer lies in the principle of {\i tarti le-rei\u8217'usa} \u8212- two negative factors versus one. In the {\i mikvah} case, you have two competing characterizations of the {\i mikvah}: its past status as full and valid, and its present status as deficient. Those two statuses face off and in some sense neutralize each other \u8212- the past known validity and the present known deficiency cancel each other out. What then determines the outcome? The independent {\i chazakah} of the person or vessel: they were {\i tamei} before immersion and that prior status reasserts itself in the absence of a proven valid {\i mikvah}. Here there are thus two factors working against the person\u8217's claim of {\i taharah}: the {\i mikvah}\u8217's current deficiency (neutralizing its past validity) and the person\u8217's own prior {\i tumah}. That is {\i tarti le-rei\u8217'usa} \u8212- two adverse factors \u8212- in contrast to the {\i bogeres} case, where there is only one adverse factor ({\i chada le-rei\u8217'usa}): her current {\i bogeres} status faces off against her prior {\i naarah} status, but there is no independent {\i chazakah} of any other party to break the tie, leaving it as a genuine {\i safek}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chavit Analogy\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A further analogy is introduced: the case of a {\i chavit} \u8212- a wine vessel. Someone had a cask of wine and was systematically designating portions of it as {\i terumah} for wine he was pressing. Later, the wine in the cask was found to have turned to vinegar ({\i chometz}). When did the transformation occur? Any {\i terumah} designated while the wine was already {\i chometz} is invalid, since {\i terumah} must be wine. The {\i baraita} states that the first three days after checking are definitely one status and thereafter it is a {\i safek} \u8212- with a tannaitic dispute about which way the three days cut. For our purposes, the key point is that this is ruled a {\i safek}, not {\i vadai} \u8212- in contrast to the {\i mikvah} case. And yet one might have thought it should be {\i vadai} by analogy to the {\i mikvah}, because the grain was definitely {\i tevel} (untithed) \u8212- just as the person was definitely {\i tamei} before the {\i mikvah}, the grain was definitely {\i tevel} before the wine became {\i terumah}. Why then is the {\i chavit} case only a {\i safek}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Shimon\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} answers that the ruling of {\i safek} in the {\i chavit} case reflects the view of Rabbi Shimon, and Rabbi Shimon applies the same logic to the {\i mikvah}: {\i d\u8217'gabbei mikvah nami safeka mashvi lei} \u8212- he also treats the {\i mikvah} case as a {\i safek}, not {\i vadai}. The {\i baraita} that treats the {\i mikvah} as {\i vadai tamei} \u8212- even in a {\i reshut ha-rabim} \u8212- reflects the view of the {\i rabbanan}, not Rabbi Shimon. Rabbi Shimon holds {\i safek tumah bi-reshut ha-yachid} is {\i safek}, and in {\i reshut ha-rabim} it is {\i tahor}. For the {\i rabbanan}, the {\i mikvah} case is {\i vadai} (driven by the {\i chazakah} of {\i tumah}), and consistently the {\i chavit} case would also be {\i vadai} (driven by the {\i chazakah} of {\i tevel}). For Rabbi Shimon, both cases are {\i safek}. The two cases are in fact structurally parallel \u8212- same rule applies to both \u8212- and the apparent contradiction between them dissolves once we recognize that different tannaitic authorities govern each ruling. In the {\i bogeres} case, there is no independent {\i chazakah} of a third element to tip the balance, and therefore regardless of whose view one follows, it remains a {\i safek} \u8212- hence Shmuel\u8217's ruling of {\i chaishinan le-kiddushei sheneihem}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Mi Motzi Ruling\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Deathbed Gift Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} introduces another analogy: {\i mi motzi mi-yad mi} \u8212- who takes from whom? The case is a person who gives away all his property as a deathbed gift ({\i matnat shechiv mera}) and then recovers. Such a gift is ordinarily void \u8212- it was given in anticipation of death, and since he recovered, the gift lapses. Now he is walking around healthy and claims he gave the gift from his deathbed and is therefore entitled to reclaim the property. The recipients counter that he was healthy when he gave it, and the gift stands. Who bears the burden of proof?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Yaakov View\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yaakov says: {\i hu motzi mi-yadam be-lo ra\u8217'ayah} \u8212- he can take the property back from them without proof, and they cannot take it from him without proof. The underlying reasoning: the property began under his ownership and retains a {\i chazakah} of being his. They must prove their entitlement; he benefits from the status quo.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Natan View\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Natan says the opposite: {\i barri hu alav le-havi ra\u8217'ayah} \u8212- if he is now healthy, the burden is on him to prove he was on his deathbed. His current healthy status serves as evidence of his past healthy status, and the recipients accordingly are presumed to have received a valid gift. Conversely, if he is now on his deathbed ({\i shechiv mera}), then they bear the burden of proving he was healthy when he gave the gift.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} considers: does this dispute map onto the dispute of Rav and Shmuel? Perhaps Rav follows Rabbi Natan \u8212- current status projects backward \u8212- and Shmuel follows Rabbi Yaakov \u8212- you do not project current status backward, and the property retains its {\i chazakah} of belonging to the original owner.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Reconciliation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} rejects this mapping. Rav can say: {\i ana d\u8217'amri afilu ke-Rabbi Yaakov} \u8212- I can rule like Rav even while agreeing with Rabbi Yaakov. Rabbi Yaakov ignores his current healthy status because the property has an independent {\i chazakah} of belonging to him \u8212- that {\i chazakah} tips the balance. But in the {\i bogeres} case, her body was actively in the process of changing; there is no fixed {\i chazakah} of the body remaining as it was, precisely because transformation was underway. Therefore Rav is entitled to say she was already a {\i bogeres} in the morning, even while agreeing with Rabbi Yaakov that one does not blindly project current status backward when an independent {\i chazakah} of property exists. Shmuel, symmetrically, can say: {\i ana d\u8217'amri afilu ke-Rabbi Natan} \u8212- I can follow Shmuel even while agreeing with Rabbi Natan. Rabbi Natan projects current healthy status backward because {\i chulei alma be-chezkat bri\u8217'im kaimei} \u8212- the world is presumptively healthy; if you claim you were on your deathbed, that is an extraordinary claim that requires proof. But in the {\i bogeres} case, it is not at all extraordinary that she was a {\i naarah} yesterday \u8212- that is precisely what she was \u8212- and the question is simply when within that day the transition occurred. That is a genuine {\i safek}, not analogous to an implausible claim of terminal illness. The two disputes therefore do not map onto each other.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Baraita Reconcile\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Dual Baraitas\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now confronts the fact that two {\i baraitas} appear to reflect this very dispute: one states she is definitively a {\i bogeres} ({\i harei hi bogeres lefaneinu}) \u8212- the father\u8217's {\i kiddushin} is invalid \u8212- and another states {\i chaishinan le-kiddushei sheneihem} \u8212- both {\i kiddushin} are in doubt and she needs a {\i get} from both men.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Harmonization Try\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The first attempted resolution: perhaps one {\i baraita} follows Rav and the other follows Shmuel, and there is simply a {\i machlokes} between the {\i tannaim} that mirrors the {\i machlokes} between the {\i amoraim}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Claim Distinction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} proposes an alternative: both {\i baraitas} actually follow Shmuel, and the difference lies in whether the woman herself makes a claim. When she affirmatively claims that she became a {\i bogeres} earlier in the day \u8212- {\i mekacheshes} \u8212- even Shmuel might concede that we credit her claim, and thus her {\i kiddushin} is binding. When she makes no such claim, Shmuel maintains that it is a {\i safek} and both men must give a {\i get}. Under this reading, Rav and Shmuel are not in fundamental disagreement \u8212- Rav addresses the case where she makes a claim, Shmuel addresses the case where she does not.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Yosef Incident\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then demonstrates that Rav and Shmuel do in fact disagree. Rav Yosef son of Rav Menashya of Devil ruled in practice like Rav \u8212- he treated her as definitively a {\i bogeres}, invalidating the father\u8217's {\i kiddushin} and recognizing hers. Shmuel became furious.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shmuel Rebuke\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Shmuel\u8217's rebuke is pointed: {\i kulei alma kailei leih be-kava zuta, ve-hai mi-derabbanan kailei leih be-kava raba} \u8212- everyone else measures out their wisdom in a small measure, and this student measured his out in a large one? The implicit message: normal rabbinic authorities, confronted with this uncertain situation, would recognize the genuine {\i safek} and treat it accordingly \u8212- requiring {\i gittin} from both men. To rule definitively that she was a {\i bogeres} in the morning and dismiss the father\u8217's {\i kiddushin} out of hand requires a level of halachic mastery and confidence that this student did not possess. Shmuel was teaching him that the appropriate posture for a jurist of his standing was caution and the recognition of {\i safek}, not bold retroactive determination. The very fact that Shmuel erupted demonstrates that he and Rav genuinely disagreed, since if they agreed in the case of a woman making a claim, Shmuel would have had no basis for anger if that was indeed the scenario in question.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Final Halacha\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} closes with competing reports: Mar Zutra tells Rav Ashi that the {\i halacha} follows Shmuel; Rav Ashi himself says the {\i halacha} follows Rav \u8212- and the {\i gemara}\u8217's final declaration is {\i ve-hilcheta ke-vatei de-Rav}, we rule like Rav. As a practical matter, then, we treat her as definitively a {\i bogeres} and do not require {\i gittin} from both men. However, the {\i gemara}\u8217's closing lines are somewhat unclear in terms of their precise scope \u8212- there are questions of {\i girsa}, of exactly how to read the final passage \u8212- and it is possible the ruling applies specifically when she makes an affirmative claim of earlier maturity, which would provide even stronger grounds for ruling like Rav. The broader ruling, that we follow Rav even without such a claim, is the most likely reading of our text as it stands.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Kiddushin 80\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Kinship & Yichus\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Overseas Return\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Today\u8217's daf is 80, and we pick up at the {\i mishna} at the bottom of 79b, moving to a somewhat different topic. The present {\i perek} has been devoted largely to questions of {\i ne\u8217'emanus} and {\i yesod yuchasin}, while ranging across several classic {\i kiddushin} themes. The most proximate {\i mishna} concerned a man who appoints a {\i shaliach} to betroth his daughter and the question of whose {\i kiddushin} takes effect first. Now we return to a theme from two {\i mishnayos} earlier \u8212- someone testifying about the status of his own family and the question of whether he is believed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} reads: {\i mi she-yatzah hu ve-ishto li-medinas ha-yam} \u8212- a man and his wife went to a faraway place. {\i U-va hu ve-ishto u-vanav} \u8212- and he returned after many years with his wife and children. {\i Ve-amar ishah she-yatzta imi li-medinas ha-yam harey hi zo} \u8212- this is the same wife I left with, he declares, perhaps twenty years later when no one recognizes her anymore. {\i Ve-elu baneha} \u8212- and these are the children she bore overseas. {\i Eino tzarich le-havi rayah lo al ha-ishah ve-lo al ha-banim} \u8212- he need not bring any proof regarding his wife or the children.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rashi explains that the {\i chiddush} here is twofold. First, since her acceptable {\i yichus} was established at the time of the marriage here, before they departed, he need not re-establish it now. Second, since he left with a wife and has returned with a wife, he is believed to say it is the same woman. And crucially, he need not bring any proof that these are her children \u8212- since she was known to be of acceptable {\i yichus}, the children inherit that status. The {\i gemara} will explain that the basis for presuming these are her children is that the children are {\i koruch achareha}, literally wrapped around her, clinging to her in a manner that makes clear she is mothering them.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Three Scenarios\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i mishna} then addresses a second scenario: {\i meisah} \u8212- suppose he says his wife died overseas. {\i Ve-elu baneha} \u8212- but these are the children she bore. In that case, {\i meivi rayah al ha-banim ve-eino meivi rayah al ha-ishah} \u8212- he must bring proof that these children are indeed his wife\u8217's children, though he need not bring proof about the wife\u8217's own {\i yichus}, since that was established before they departed. Once he proves these are her children, their {\i yichus} follows from hers.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A third scenario: {\i ishah nasasi bi-medinas ha-yam} \u8212- he left as a single man and returns saying he married a woman overseas. {\i Ve-harey hi zo ve-elu baneha} \u8212- this is the woman I married and these are her children. Here, {\i meivi rayah al ha-ishah ve-eino meivi rayah al ha-banim} \u8212- he must bring proof about the woman\u8217's {\i yichus}, since we have no prior knowledge of who she is, but the children\u8217's identity is presumed from the behavioral evidence of their attachment to her. However, if she has died and he has only his word that these were her children, he must prove the children\u8217's identity as well, since the behavioral presumption is no longer available.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The structure is thus consistent: the presence of the children together with the woman, when they exhibit the attachment of {\i koruch achareha}, creates a presumption that she is their mother. When that behavioral evidence is absent, their identity must be proven independently. And the question of the woman\u8217's {\i yichus} depends entirely on whether she was known here before departure or married abroad.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kerucha Achareiha\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Amar Rabah bar Rav Huna: ve-kulan d\u8217'kruchin achareha} \u8212- in all these cases where the children are presumed to be hers while she is present, the operative principle is that they are literally intertwined with her, wrapped around her, conducting themselves toward her as young children do toward a mother. This behavioral reality creates the presumption of maternity.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might ask whether the man\u8217's claim plays any role, or whether the behavior of {\i kruchin achareha} alone is sufficient even without his identification. This is genuinely uncertain. Perhaps his framing lowers the behavioral threshold required, or perhaps the observed behavior standing alone would suffice regardless of who makes the claim. And if the question is raised from the woman\u8217's side \u8212- if she were to claim these are her children while exhibiting the same mothering behavior \u8212- it is not immediately obvious why that should carry less weight, though the matter remains open.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Small vs Adult\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i baraita} refines the principle further: {\i meivi rayah al ha-gedolim ve-ein tzarich l\u8217'havi rayah al ha-ketanim} \u8212- even if he is present with children, if the children are adults, their attachment to this woman does not create the same clear presumption. With small children, the behavior is unmistakable \u8212- they are being nursed, held, clung to. With adults, the relationship is more ambiguous; one could be a stepmother, a guardian, or simply a long-term acquaintance who has taken over care. The {\i gemara} limits the presumption of maternity from {\i kerucha achareha} to small children, for whom the behavior is sufficiently distinctive.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Of course, with adults one might simply ask them directly \u8212- which raises the interesting question of what evidentiary weight their own testimony would carry. But the principle articulated here is that the behavioral evidence of {\i koruch achareha}, in its classic form, is specific to small children.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two Wives Rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Bameh devarim amurim \u8212- b\u8217'ishah achat} \u8212- this entire framework applies when the man makes a claim about one woman. {\i Aval bishtei nashim} \u8212- but if he claims he married two women overseas, or if he left with two women and returned with only one, then even the {\i kerucha achareha} of small children is not sufficient. {\i Meivi rayah al ha-ishah ve-al ha-banim, al ha-gedolim ve-al ha-ketanim} \u8212- he must prove the woman\u8217's identity and must prove the identity of both the adult and the small children.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The reason is that once there are two wives in the picture, a woman mothering children who cling to her proves nothing definitive, since she might naturally have taken in her co-wife\u8217's children after that woman\u8217's death. The {\i kerucha achareha} presumption thus fails when there is an alternative plausible explanation for the observed behavior.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might note the apparent issue of {\i hapeh she-asar} \u8212- the man could simply have claimed he married only one woman. The fact that he volunteers the existence of a second wife might seem to lend him some credibility by that principle. The matter may depend on whether it was publicly known that he left with two women, in which case {\i hapeh she-asar} would not apply.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 RL vs RY Dispute\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now focuses on a fundamental question: what standard of proof does the {\i kerucha achareha} presumption actually achieve? {\i Amar Resh Lakish: lo shanu ela be-kodshei ha-gvul} \u8212- this applies only for matters of sanctified things outside the Temple precincts, meaning {\i terumah}. If the man is a {\i kohen}, the behavioral presumption is sufficient to establish that these are his children and his wife\u8217's children, permitting them to eat {\i terumah}. {\i Aval le-yuchasin lo} \u8212- but for {\i yuchasin} purposes \u8212- for example, marrying a daughter off to a {\i kohen} \u8212- the higher standard of investigation we have been discussing throughout the {\i perek} would still be required, and mere behavioral presumption would not suffice.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Ve-Rabbi Yochanan amar: afilu le-yuchasin} \u8212- Rabbi Yochanan maintains that even for {\i yuchasin} purposes, the {\i kerucha achareha} presumption is evidentially adequate. {\i Ve-azal Rabbi Yochanan le-ta\u8217'ameih} \u8212- and Rabbi Yochanan follows his own reasoning here, for as we are about to see, he holds that a properly established {\i chazakah} based on observed behavior constitutes a very high standard of proof, sufficient even for the most weighty legal determinations.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Chazaka as Evidence\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chazaka Definition\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Before examining the specific dispute, it is worth pausing on the concept of {\i chazakah} itself, because the word is used in two quite different senses in rabbinic literature. One usage refers to {\i status quo} \u8212- the prior established state of an object or person that we presume to continue absent evidence of change. So, for instance, a {\i mikvah} that previously had a valid amount of water is presumed to retain that status, or a vessel that had been {\i tamei} retains the {\i chazakah} of {\i tumah}. A {\i na\u8217'arah} retains the {\i chazakah} of being a {\i na\u8217'arah} until there is evidence she has become a {\i bogeres}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The second usage \u8212- and this is the {\i chazakah} at stake in the coming discussion \u8212- refers to a presumption of facts derived from observed behavior. {\i Chazakah ein ishah me-izah paneiha bifnei ba\u8217'alah} \u8212- there is a presumption that a woman would not be brazen enough to falsely claim to her husband that he had divorced her. On the basis of certain presumptions about how people behave, we infer certain realities, treating those inferred facts as established for legal purposes. The {\i kerucha achareha} is precisely this kind of {\i chazakah}: children who cling to a woman and are mothered by her are presumed to be hers, because the behavioral evidence creates a presumption about the underlying fact.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Lashes & Death\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What level of legal consequence can such a {\i chazakah} support? This is the stunning claim of Rabbi Yochanan: {\i de-amar Rabbi Chiyya bar Abba amar Rabbi Yochanan \u8212- malkin al ha-chazakos, ve-soklin ve-sorfin al ha-chazakos, ve-ein sorfin terumah al ha-chazakah}. You give {\i malkus} \u8212- lashes \u8212- based on a {\i chazakah}. You would even stone or burn someone \u8212- administer capital punishment \u8212- based on a {\i chazakah}. Yet you would not burn {\i terumah} that is presumed {\i tamei} on the basis of a {\i chazakah}. This is immediately striking, if not outright baffling: the evidentiary standard appears higher for burning grain than for executing a human being.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Niddah Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} illustrates the principle of {\i malkin al ha-chazakos} with an example from Rav Yehuda: {\i huchzekah niddah bi-shchenoteiha} \u8212- if a woman has been established among her neighbors as being a {\i niddah}, through her conduct, what she has said to friends, perhaps the garments she wears, and there now exists a presumption in her social circle that she is in a state of {\i niddah} and will be going to the {\i mikvah} in a few days \u8212- then {\i ba\u8217'alah lokeh aleiha mishum niddah} \u8212- if her husband is witnessed having relations with her during this period, he receives lashes on account of transgressing the prohibition of relations with a {\i niddah}. No one has physically examined her garments or conducted a {\i bedikah}. The presumption that arose from her observed behavior is treated as establishing the underlying fact.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Incest Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The capital punishment application is illustrated by a teaching of Rabbah bar Avuna: {\i ish ve-ishah tinok ve-tinoket she-gidelu be-toch ha-bayit} \u8212- a man, a woman, a young boy, and a young girl who all grow up together in one household. No one witnessed a marriage ceremony, no one was present at the births, no documentation exists. But {\i niskaalin zeh al zeh ve-nisrafin zeh al zeh} \u8212- if the presumed father were to have relations with the presumed daughter, or the presumed son with the presumed mother, these would be prosecuted as incest with the applicable capital penalties, even though the family relationships rest entirely on presumption developed through communal perception.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is a significant legal claim. The community perceives them as a family; that perception, established over time and through consistent behavior, is treated as constituting legal fact for all {\i halachic} purposes \u8212- including the most severe ones.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Facts vs Acts\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A critical clarification, however: the {\i chazakah} establishes presumed {\i facts}, not presumed {\i acts}. The standard of proof required to establish that a transgression was actually committed remains unchanged \u8212- it always requires two witnesses to the act itself. The {\i gemara} famously discusses the case of a man running after another with a knife, who then enters a barn; witnesses arrive moments later to find the victim mortally wounded, the pursuer standing over him with a blood-dripping knife. This is not sufficient for conviction, because no one directly witnessed the act of murder. The evidentiary requirement for the commission of a sin is direct witness testimony; it cannot be replaced by inference, however compelling.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What {\i chazakah} establishes is the background reality \u8212- who the parties are, what status an object or person holds \u8212- and these presumptions, once established through communal perception and behavior, are treated as fact for purposes of all downstream {\i halachot}, including those that carry capital consequences. This is not so strange when one considers that society cannot demand proof of every foundational fact. Who is your father? What is your status? These are things established through communal perception, and it would be impossible to function otherwise.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A parallel principle: {\i ed echad ne\u8217'eman be-issurin}. The Rambam rules that if one witness testifies that a piece of meat before you is {\i chelev} \u8212- forbidden fat \u8212- the meat\u8217's status is thereby established. If someone then knowingly eats it in violation, that person would receive {\i malkus}, even though only one witness established the status of the meat. The {\i chazakah} of status need not meet the two-witness standard required for proving the act. The Rambam adds, however, that {\i ed echad ne\u8217'eman be-issurin} supports {\i malkus} but not {\i mitah} \u8212- capital punishment. The reason the {\i chazakah} in the family-in-the-household case supports capital punishment is not because one person has asserted it, but because a society-wide perception has developed over time that constitutes something qualitatively more robust \u8212- approaching the status of established fact.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Doubt Concepts\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Practical Reality\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Underlying all of this is a practical reality that the {\i halacha} must confront: society cannot require proof of every foundational fact. Who is one\u8217's father, what is one\u8217's status, what is the nature of an ongoing social relationship \u8212- these are things known through communal perception and behavioral evidence. If halachic decision-making required documentary or testimonial proof for every such fact, normal life would become impossible. The framework of {\i chazakah}, {\i rov}, {\i safek}, and related concepts exists precisely to navigate this reality \u8212- to allow decisions to be made with appropriate confidence even in the absence of certainty.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 One Witness\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Rambam\u8217's ruling on {\i ed echad ne\u8217'eman be-issurin} illustrates this further. A single witness establishes the status of an object or a situation, and once that status is established, subsequent transgressions committed in the face of that established status are prosecutable. The distinction, as already noted, is between establishing the {\i status} \u8212- where one witness or even communal presumption can suffice \u8212- and proving the {\i act}, which always requires the full standard of direct two-witness testimony.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Chazaka as Evidence (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mother-Son Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} brings a remarkable narrative to illustrate these principles: {\i amar Rabbi Shimon ben Pazi amar Rabbi Yehoshua ben Levi mishum Bar Kappara \u8212- maaseh be-ishah she-ba\u8217'ah li-Yerushalayim, ve-tinok murkav lah al ktefeiha} \u8212- a woman came to Jerusalem with an infant on her shoulders. {\i Ve-higdelto} \u8212- she raised him. After he grew up, {\i u-va aleha} \u8212- they had sexual relations. {\i Ve-hevi\u8217'um le-beit din ve-saklum} \u8212- they were brought to court and executed. And the basis for the execution was {\i lo mipnei she-binah vadai} \u8212- not because anyone had proof that this was her son \u8212- {\i ela mipnei she-korekh achareha} \u8212- but because of the principle of {\i korekh achareha}. The way he had been raised clinging to her, the way she had mothered him from infancy, created the presumption that she was his mother.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Notably, in this case neither the man nor the woman was making any claim. The husband was not testifying. The presumption arose entirely from observed behavior, and it was sufficient to support a capital conviction. This answers directly the earlier question of whether {\i kerucha achareha} requires the man\u8217's framing claim or whether the behavior alone suffices. Here, the behavior alone was the basis for the legal determination.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Terumah Anomaly\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This brings the {\i gemara} back to the anomaly that was flagged at the outset: {\i ve-ein sorfin terumah al ha-chazakot}. If a {\i chazakah} is robust enough to execute human beings, why is it not sufficient to burn {\i terumah} that is presumed {\i tamei}? The {\i gemara} attributes conflicting positions to Resh Lakish and Rabbi Yochanan: {\i d\u8217'amar Reish Lakish sorfin al ha-chazakot} \u8212- Resh Lakish says you would burn {\i terumah} on a {\i chazakah} \u8212- {\i Rabbi Yochanan amar ein sorfin} \u8212- Rabbi Yochanan says you would not. {\i Ve-azdu le-ta\u8217'amayhu} \u8212- they follow their respective reasonings, as we shall see.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Infant & Dough (III)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Setup\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The operative case is {\i tinok she-nimtza be-tzad ha-issah u-batzek be-yado} \u8212- an infant found beside a dough mass with a piece of dough in his hand. Following Tosafot\u8217's reading: what we do not know for certain is whether the infant reached into the larger dough mass himself and took the piece, or whether some adult gave him a piece. The {\i tinok} himself is {\i tamei} \u8212- according to Tosafot, because he has been held and nursed by his mother who is {\i tamei} as a {\i niddah} or after childbirth. The question is whether the larger dough mass has become {\i tamei} as a result of the infant\u8217's touch.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rashi reads the scenario slightly differently: we know the infant touched the dough, but we do not know whether the infant himself is {\i tamei}, which is presumed on the basis of his contact with his {\i tamei} mother. Either way, one element of the situation is known and one is presumed. And the question being posed is: may the larger dough mass be presumed {\i tamei}?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 RM vs Chachamim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Meir metaher} \u8212- Rabbi Meir rules the dough is {\i tahor}. {\i Ve-Chachamim metamin} \u8212- and the {\i Chachamim} rule it is {\i tamei}. The {\i Chachamim}\u8217's reasoning is stated in the {\i mishna} itself: {\i mipnei she-darko shel tinok le-tape\u8217'ach} \u8212- because it is the nature of an infant to pat and touch things. An infant will reach out and touch whatever is within reach; if he has a piece of dough in his hand, the presumption is that he took it by touching the larger mass.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Infant Nature\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The word {\i le-tape\u8217'ach} captures the behavioral reality of small children \u8212- they reach, grab, and pat at objects around them. Rashi interprets this somewhat differently, suggesting that the infant\u8217's habit of playing in dirt and touching impure things is what establishes that he became {\i tamei}. But the fundamental principle is the same: we infer from the observed nature of infant behavior what likely occurred.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Miut Logic\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Mai ta\u8217'ama de-Rabbi Meir?} \u8212- What is Rabbi Meir\u8217's reasoning for ruling the dough {\i tahor}? {\i Kasavar: rov tinokot metapchin u-miut ein metapchin} \u8212- it is true that the majority of infants pat and touch things, but a minority do not. {\i Ve-ika chezkat tahara} \u8212- and the dough has a {\i chazakah} of being {\i tahor}, meaning its prior established status is one of {\i taharah}. {\i U-smoch miuta le-chazakah ve-itrei lei rova} \u8212- you combine the minority possibility that the infant did not touch the dough together with the {\i chazakah} of the dough\u8217's prior status, and this combination counteracts the {\i rov}. Because the {\i rov} is counteracted, the result is a {\i safek}. And since this is a case where there is no one present capable of being asked \u8212- the infant cannot respond to questions, and this is the category of {\i ein bo da\u8217'at lishael} \u8212- {\i safek} in this situation would yield {\i tahor}, or at minimum {\i safek} in the public domain ({\i reshus ha-rabim}) is {\i tahor}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is Rabbi Meir\u8217's characteristic position that {\i chaishishan le-miuta} \u8212- we give weight to the minority possibility and cannot simply ignore it because the majority points the other way. As one might put it: when you are the one in a million, the denominator does not matter.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chachamim Rov\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i Chachamim} take a different view: {\i miuta ke-man de-leita dami} \u8212- the minority possibility is treated as nonexistent. Therefore we have {\i rov} \u8212- the majority of infants touch \u8212- against {\i chazakah} \u8212- the prior status of the dough \u8212- and {\i rova adif} \u8212- {\i rov} trumps {\i chazakah}. The result is that the dough is presumed {\i tamei}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 RL Burning\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Amar Reish Lakish mishum Rabbi Oshaya: zo hi she-sorfin aleha et ha-terumah} \u8212- this is the scenario in which you would burn the {\i terumah}. And here the reversal becomes apparent: this is the same Resh Lakish who, in the earlier discussion about {\i kerucha achareha}, held that behavioral {\i chazakah} is insufficient for {\i yuchasin} \u8212- meaning he sets a higher evidentiary bar in that context. Yet here he says that in this {\i tinok} case you would burn {\i terumah}. And Rabbi Yochanan \u8212- who holds {\i chazakah} is good enough even for {\i yuchasin} and for capital punishment \u8212- would {\i not} burn {\i terumah} in this case.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Doubt Concepts (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Chullin Framework\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The systematic treatment of all these categories \u8212- {\i rov}, {\i chazakah}, {\i safek}, {\i karov}, {\i miut}, {\i matzui ve-eino matzui} \u8212- is found in the first {\i perek} of Chullin, which addresses directly the question of {\i ruba ve-chazakah, eizehu adif?} \u8212- which of these presumptions takes precedence over which. That discussion provides the broader conceptual architecture for navigating cases of doubt, and the cases here on Kiddushin 80 are important data points within that larger framework.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Reconciliation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Inversion\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The inversion of positions between Resh Lakish and Rabbi Yochanan is initially puzzling. Rabbi Yochanan, who holds that {\i chazakah} supports capital punishment and {\i yuchasin}, says we do {\i not} burn {\i terumah} in the {\i tinok} case. Resh Lakish, who holds that {\i chazakah} does {\i not} suffice for {\i yuchasin}, says we {\i would} burn {\i terumah} in the {\i tinok} case. The positions appear to cross, but the resolution clarifies something fundamental about what kind of {\i chazakah} is actually being discussed in each case.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Quality Diff\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The resolution is that this is not a question of higher versus lower evidentiary standards \u8212- as if burning {\i terumah} requires {\i more} proof than executing a person, which would be absurd. Rather, we are dealing with {\i qualitatively different types of presumption} in each case. Rabbi Yochanan\u8217's statement \u8212- {\i ein zo chazakah she-sorfin} \u8212- means precisely this: {\i this particular} presumption is not the kind sufficient to burn {\i terumah}. It does not mean {\i chazakah} as a category is insufficient; it means this instance does not qualify as the relevant kind.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Perception Type\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i chazakah} involved in capital cases \u8212- a family presumed to be a family over years of communal life, a woman presumed to be a {\i niddah} based on things she has said and done before her neighbors \u8212- is a {\i chazakah} of established communal perception. A status has been created, perceived, and maintained in public awareness. The {\i tinok} case is categorically different: there is no established perception, no status that has crystallized in communal consciousness. There is simply a question mark over a specific act \u8212- did the child touch the dough? This is closer in nature to asking whether a transgression occurred, which is the domain of direct testimony, not of {\i chazakah}. Applying the label {\i chazakah} to both obscures this qualitative distinction. The {\i chazakah} of {\i niddah} status among neighbors and the {\i chazakah} of a family\u8217's relationships are genuinely established presumptions of fact. The question of whether this specific child touched this specific dough on this specific occasion is not that kind of {\i chazakah}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Infant & Dough (III)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rodents & Frogs\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} then asks: what would be a {\i chazakah} sufficient for Rabbi Yochanan to burn {\i terumah}? {\i Ke-de-tanya: issah be-toch ha-bayit, u-shratzim ve-tzfardaim mitaplim sham} \u8212- you have dough in a house where {\i shratzim} \u8212- impure rodents \u8212- and frogs are hopping about. The {\i shratzim} would render the dough {\i tamei} if they touched it; the frogs would not. {\i Ve-nimtzu chatichot be-issah} \u8212- and you find pieces \u8212- a little leg, for instance \u8212- in the dough. The question is whether the piece came from a {\i shratz} or a frog. {\i Im rov shratzim tamei; im rov tzfardaim tehorah} \u8212- if the majority of the creatures present are {\i shratzim}, the dough is {\i tamei}; if the majority are frogs, the dough is {\i tahor}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Here the decision is made by {\i rov}, and something has definitely touched the dough \u8212- the question is only the identity of what touched it. This is a meaningfully different scenario from the {\i tinok} case: the act of contact is certain, and what remains uncertain is the classification of the agent. One resolves that by {\i rov}. In the {\i tinok} case, by contrast, even the act of contact was not certain. This distinction \u8212- between uncertainty about {\i whether} contact occurred and uncertainty about the {\i identity} of what made contact \u8212- is one of the key axes separating the cases.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Reconciliation (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 No Questions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tanya ke-vateih de-Rabbi Yochanan: shnei devarim ein bahem da\u8217'at lishael} \u8212- there are two things whose relevant party has no {\i da\u8217'at lishael}, no capacity to respond to questions, and yet are treated stringently. The principle that {\i safek tumah be-reshus ha-yachid} is {\i tamei} \u8212- that doubt in a private space is ruled {\i tamei} \u8212- is derived from the case of the {\i sotah}, where the woman was alone with the man, present and capable of being asked. The {\i chachamim} extended this principle to ordinary cases of {\i tumah}, but maintained the limiting condition: it applies only when someone with {\i da\u8217'at lishael} was present. If no such person was present, technically the case should be {\i safek tahor}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Nevertheless, in two exceptional cases, the {\i chachamim} ruled {\i tamei} even though no one with {\i da\u8217'at lishael} was present: first, the {\i tinok} \u8212- the infant who cannot respond to questions; second, {\i issah be-toch ha-bayit ve-tarnegolin u-mashkin tme\u8217'im sham} \u8212- dough in a house with chickens and {\i tamei} liquid present, where you find {\i nikurim} \u8212- pecking marks \u8212- in the dough. Did the chicken have the {\i tamei} liquid in its beak when it pecked? {\i Tolin lo ochlin ve-lo sorfin} \u8212- the dough is left in suspension: it is not eaten but it is not burned. Rabbi Yehoshua ben Levi notes that even though technically these should have been {\i safek tahor} since no one with {\i da\u8217'at lishael} was present, the {\i chachamim} were {\i machmir} and ruled {\i tamei}, but stopped short of requiring the {\i terumah} to be burned.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 These cases thus represent a middle category: more stringent than a pure {\i safek tahor}, but not stringent enough to justify burning. And this is exactly where Rabbi Yochanan\u8217's position places them \u8212- they are not the kind of robust, established {\i chazakah} that would justify burning {\i terumah}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Infant & Dough (III)\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Clear Liquid\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Amar Rabbi Yochanan: lo shanu ela be-mashkin tzelulim she-be-bavu\u8217'ah shel tinok nikar bah, aval mashkin achurin lo} \u8212- Rabbi Yochanan adds a further distinction within the chicken-and-liquid case. If the liquid is clear enough that one could see an infant\u8217's face reflected in it, then the following logic applies: had the chicken actually had this liquid in its beak, we would expect to see visible residue or evidence in the dough where it pecked. Since we do not see it, there is inferential reason to think the liquid was not present in the beak. But if the liquid is cloudy, this inference is unavailable, because cloudy liquid would not leave the same visible trace. The precise mechanism of this distinction \u8212- exactly how the absorption or residue differs between clear and cloudy liquids in the dough \u8212- is not entirely transparent from the text, but the general thrust is that clear liquid provides an evidentiary avenue by which the absence of visible residue can serve as circumstantial evidence that contact did not occur. Cloudy liquid does not provide that avenue.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Modern Evidence\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Written Docs\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One broader question raised by all this is the status of written documentation as proof. What constitutes a legitimate {\i shtar} halachically? Today we would point to a birth certificate, but the question of whether a birth certificate constitutes a halachically valid {\i shtar} is genuinely open.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 DNA & Fingerprints\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The same question arises with greater force regarding forensic evidence. When DNA analysis or fingerprinting was first introduced in secular legal systems, it was widely viewed with skepticism \u8212- the intuition was that nothing could be meaningful as proof without an eyewitness. Halacha never developed a framework for non-direct testimony of this kind. There is no halachic category that straightforwardly accommodates forensic evidence. One might try to argue it into the framework of {\i chazakah} \u8212- a sufficiently robust and reproducible scientific presumption \u8212- but this requires careful analysis of what type of {\i chazakah} is involved.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is important to note that DNA evidence, for instance, can function in two distinct ways: to establish {\i facts} about identity \u8212- who is someone\u8217's father, who was present at a scene \u8212- and to prove the commission of an {\i act}. The former category is closer to the terrain of {\i chazakah} and established status, where halacha has always allowed presumptions and inferences. The latter \u8212- using DNA to prove that someone committed a murder or a sexual crime \u8212- runs into the classical requirement of direct witness testimony for the act itself.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Facts vs Acts\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This distinction \u8212- between proving {\i facts} and proving {\i acts} \u8212- is central and must not be collapsed. Halacha has always permitted robust inference to establish the factual background: who someone is, what their status is, what the state of an object is. The {\i chazakah} of community perception, {\i rov}, {\i ed echad be-issurin} \u8212- all of these operate in this domain. But the act of transgression itself has always required direct testimony. Forensic evidence used to establish facts about identity or status sits in a different category than forensic evidence used to prove that a specific act of murder or assault occurred, and any halachic analysis of modern evidentiary tools must attend carefully to this distinction.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Eyewitness Irony\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The irony, of course, is that the modern empirical sciences have thoroughly documented the unreliability of eyewitness testimony. Memory is reconstructive, subject to suggestion, distorted by stress and emotion. DNA evidence, by contrast, when properly collected and analyzed, achieves a level of certainty that no eyewitness can match. Halacha committed itself to direct eyewitness testimony as the gold standard for proving acts precisely because of a commitment to the highest standard of certainty \u8212- and yet the standard it has demanded turns out, empirically, to be among the least reliable forms of human knowledge. This is not a trivial irony.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Yichud Intro\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Why Here\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 We now turn to the final {\i mishnayot} of {\i Masechet Kiddushin}, which address the laws of {\i yichud} \u8212- the prohibition on a man and woman being in seclusion together \u8212- and represent a significant shift in topic. One could perhaps construct a connection to {\i yichus}, since forbidden seclusion might lead to relationships with forbidden consequences, but that is not the stated concern. The connection is more naturally to the general themes of {\i Masechet Kiddushin} \u8212- the nature of the marital bond and the boundaries of permitted and forbidden relationships. The broader question of sex outside of marriage, incidentally, is itself a {\i machloket rishonim}: the Rambam holds it is forbidden, while the Ramban holds that a committed, monogamous relationship with a woman who is not a {\i niddah} is not strictly prohibited, given the institution of {\i pilegesh} described in the Torah.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Primary Cases\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The primary cases to which {\i yichud} applies are a married woman and a single woman who is a {\i niddah}. It does not apply to a husband and wife sharing a space even when she is a {\i niddah} \u8212- they are permitted to share a bedroom. The concern is with an unrelated man in seclusion with a married woman, or with a single woman who is in a state of {\i niddah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Historical Mikvah\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 In the time of the {\i gemara}, single women would go to the {\i mikvah} for {\i taharah} purposes generally, not only before marriage \u8212- people immersed for any state of {\i tumah}. Therefore a single woman could readily not be in a state of {\i niddah} at any given time. The prohibition of {\i yichud} with a single woman who was not a {\i niddah} was accordingly a much later {\i takanah}. The primary historical prohibition was with married women and with women who were {\i niddah}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Yichud Mishna\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 One Man Rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Lo yityached adam im shtei nashim} \u8212- a man should not be in seclusion with two women. This is the {\i tanna kamma}\u8217's position. Even though a third party is present, if that third party is a woman, it is insufficient to prevent {\i yichud}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two Men Rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Aval ishah achas mityachedes im shnei anashim} \u8212- but one woman may be in seclusion with two men. The presence of a second man is sufficient to prevent {\i yichud}, whereas the presence of a second woman is not sufficient to permit a man to be alone with women. This asymmetry is striking and will be explained by the {\i gemara}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Shimon\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Rabbi Shimon omer af ish echad mityached im shtei nashim} \u8212- Rabbi Shimon holds that even one man with two women is permissible. His position is that any third party, regardless of sex, removes the concern of {\i yichud}, as long as there are three people present. The assumption is that three people will not all collude in transgression \u8212- one of them will object and prevent it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Wife Present\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Following Tosafot\u8217's reading of the text, a separate principle follows: {\i u-vizman she-ishto imo yashen imahem be-pundeki mipnei she-ishto meshamarto} \u8212- if a man\u8217's wife is with him, he may sleep at an inn in the company of other women, because his wife watches over him. The presence of one\u8217's wife functions as an independent mechanism that removes {\i yichud} concerns, distinct from the question of how many third parties are present.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Close Relatives\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Mityached adam im imo ve-im bito} \u8212- a man may be in seclusion with his mother or his daughter. {\i Ve-yashen imahem be-keiruv basar} \u8212- and may even sleep in close physical proximity with them while they are small children. {\i Ve-im higdilu \u8212- zo yeshena bi-kesutah ve-zeh yashen bi-kesuto} \u8212- once the child is grown, they may still share a sleeping space but must be clothed. The practical realities of households with limited beds made some such ruling necessary, and obviously {\i yichud} cannot be applied between a father and his young child. The {\i gemara}\u8217's ruling maintains the permissibility of the relationship while imposing a modest standard of modesty once the children have grown.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Adopted Children\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A genuinely difficult contemporary application arises with adopted children. Since halachically they do not bear the status of biological children, some {\i poskim} have maintained that the usual leniencies regarding {\i yichud} and {\i negiyah} do not apply to adopted children of the opposite sex from the adoptive parent. This position raises serious concerns about the wellbeing of children who need to grow up in a normal family environment. Fortunately, other {\i poskim} rule otherwise and permit the normal warmth and physical closeness of parent-child relationships for adopted children.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Yichud Concepts\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rape or Sex\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Three conceptual questions underlie the laws of {\i yichud} and deserve to be placed on the table at the outset. The first: is {\i yichud} a concern specifically about rape, about consensual sex, or about both? The general position of the {\i poskim} is that it encompasses both concerns. This is consistent with the {\i gemarot} on the subject, which do not distinguish between coerced and consensual transgression.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Male vs Female\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The second question: is the concern directed only toward male initiation, or equally toward female initiation? The {\i mishna} and {\i gemara} discuss everything from the male perspective \u8212- {\i lo yityached adam} \u8212- but that does not necessarily mean women are excluded as potential initiators. This question has practical implications.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Locked Door\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One scenario where the distinction matters is a locked door between a man and a woman sharing a building or apartment. If the concern is purely about male initiation, some argue that a locked door from the woman\u8217's side \u8212- to which the man has no key \u8212- removes the concern, since the man cannot initiate the situation. If the concern equally encompasses female initiation, such a door would be irrelevant.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tzitz Eliezer\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Tzitz Eliezer disagrees with the gender-specific reading and maintains that he cannot find any basis in the sources for treating {\i yichud} as a concern only about men and not about women. He holds that the concern applies equally. At the same time, he is lenient about the locked door regardless, on the grounds that people in separate rooms are not in a situation of mutual temptation \u8212- the concern of {\i yichud} is about avoiding temptation, not about making transgression physically impossible. If they are genuinely in separate rooms, the fact that a door {\i could} be opened does not mean they are in a situation of temptation. This points to the third underlying question: whether the goal of {\i yichud} is to make transgression impossible, or simply to avoid situations where temptation becomes difficult to resist. The Tzitz Eliezer\u8217's position is clearly the latter: the {\i halacha} presumes people are not planning to sin; what it guards against is being placed in circumstances where temptation might overcome good intentions.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Yichud Gemara\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Women Light\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} asks why the {\i tanna kamma} rules differently for a man with two women versus a woman with two men. {\i Tana devei Eliyahu}: {\i ho\u8217'il ve-nashim da\u8217'atan kalot aleihen} \u8212- since women are more susceptible to being persuaded or seduced. The presumption is that one man might be able to seduce two women, either by persuading both or by seducing one while the other fails to object. This is the underlying rationale for the {\i tanna kamma}\u8217's asymmetric ruling.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Male Strength\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 By contrast, if there are two men present, male moral strength \u8212- or at minimum the willingness of men to object and intervene \u8212- is assumed to prevent the situation. One of the two men will object and prevent transgression. The {\i gemara}\u8217's framing here is not identical to the question of whether the concern is about male versus female initiation; it is about who is more likely to serve as an effective social check on the transgression of the other party.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Three Women\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If a single man with two women is prohibited, what about one man with three women? Would more women eventually suffice to prevent {\i yichud}, on the logic that surely not all of them would acquiesce? This is discussed among the {\i poskim}, and the question is whether the {\i tanna kamma}\u8217's concern is categorical \u8212- any number of women with one man is problematic \u8212- or whether it is threshold-dependent.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Torah Source\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Amar Rabbi Yochanan mishum Rabbi Yishmael \u8212- remez le-yichud min ha-Torah minayin?} \u8212- where in the Torah is there a basis for the laws of {\i yichud}? {\i Ki yesit-cha achicha ben imcha} \u8212- when your brother, your mother\u8217's son, seduces you \u8212- and this verse concerns seduction to {\i avodah zarah}. The {\i derashah} proceeds: {\i ve-chi ben em masis, ben av ein masis?} \u8212- would only your mother\u8217's son seduce you to {\i avodah zarah} and not your father\u8217's son? The distinction implies a scenario in which {\i ben em} \u8212- the son of one\u8217's mother \u8212- is the natural companion for seclusion: {\i ben im mityached im imo} \u8212- a son may be secluded with his mother. And from this it follows that {\i asur lehityached im kol arayos she-ba-Torah} \u8212- all other forbidden relationships are subject to the prohibition of {\i yichud}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 D\u8217'Oraita Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This {\i derashah} is admittedly far-fetched as a reading of the verse, and most people would reasonably classify it as an {\i asmachta} \u8212- a rabbinic enactment with a hinted textual support rather than a true biblical derivation. Yet another {\i gemara} states {\i yichud d\u8217'oraisa hi} \u8212- that {\i yichud} is Torah law \u8212- and the overwhelming majority of {\i poskim} treat it as a genuine {\i din} of Torah origin, even if the textual basis is presented as a {\i remez}. The one case treated as unambiguously rabbinic is {\i yichud im penuyah} \u8212- seclusion with a single woman \u8212- which the {\i gemara} elsewhere characterizes as a later {\i takanah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Single Woman\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The prohibition of {\i yichud} with a single woman was enacted later than the other cases, for the reason noted earlier: in the period when single women would regularly immerse in the {\i mikvah}, they would not necessarily be in a state of {\i niddah}, and the concern of {\i yichud} therefore did not attach to them in the same way it did to married women or women known to be {\i niddah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Abaye Reading\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Amar Abaye: lo mibaya ka\u8217'amar} \u8212- Abaye offers the simple reading of why the verse specifies one\u8217's brother {\i on the mother\u8217's side} rather than the father\u8217's side. The verse is speaking {\i a fortiori}. The case it might not have had to mention is the one it does mention.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Inheritance Logic\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Lo mibaya ben av d\u8217'sani lei} \u8212- it goes without saying that you would not listen to your brother on your father\u8217's side trying to lead you to {\i avodah zarah}, because your father\u8217's son is competing with you for your father\u8217's inheritance. He has a motive to see you destroyed \u8212- to get you executed for {\i avodah zarah} and thereby remove you from the picture. Naturally you would be suspicious of his counsel and disinclined to follow it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Seduction Warn\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Ela afilu ben em d\u8217'lo sani lei} \u8212- but your brother on your mother\u8217's side has no such conflict with you. He shares no inheritance with you from your father. You cannot assume he hates you or has ulterior motives. You might think his counsel is offered in good faith \u8212- {\i eima tzeis lei} \u8212- you might be inclined to listen to him. {\i Ka mashma lan} \u8212- therefore the Torah specifically warns you: even this brother, well-intentioned as he may appear, must not be heeded when he urges you toward {\i avodah zarah}. The seduction of someone close to you who seems to mean well is precisely the most dangerous kind.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 We will continue tomorrow with the further parameters of {\i yichud} \u8212- one man with two women as opposed to one woman with two men.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Kiddushin 81\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Aninus & Yichud\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tanna Kamma v. Abba Shaul\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The daf opens with a question about whether the {\i mishnah}\u8217's ruling on {\i yichud} is consistent with the position of Abba Shaul. The {\i mishnah} distinguishes between two men and one woman \u8212- which does not constitute {\i yichud} \u8212- and two women and one man \u8212- which does. The {\i gemara} asks: does this reflect a disagreement with Abba Shaul?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The source for the question is a {\i braisa}: {\i kol shloshim yom yotzei b\u8217'cheik v\u8217'nikbar b\u8217'isha achat u-shnei anashim} \u8212- an infant who dies within the first thirty days is taken out not on a bier but carried in one\u8217's arms, and the burial takes place with only one woman and two men. This {\i halacha} reflects a more restrained practice in the face of high infant mortality, keeping the mourning low-key. Abba Shaul adds: {\i af ish echad u-shtei nashim} \u8212- one man and two women would likewise be acceptable. On its face, this seems to conflict with the {\i tanna kamma} of the {\i mishnah}, who holds that one man and two women constitutes {\i yichud}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} resolves the tension: {\i afilu teima Abba Shaul} \u8212- even Abba Shaul can agree with the general rule that one man and two women is problematic. In this specific case, however, {\i b\u8217'shaas aninus tevir yitzrei} \u8212- at the moment of {\i aninus}, the acute shock of bereavement before burial, a person\u8217's sexual desire is subdued. Since all three individuals accompanying the infant are presumably immediate family, the concern for {\i yetzer hara} is diminished, and the parameters of {\i yichud} may be understood differently.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tevir Yitzrei in Aninus\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If that is so, why do the {\i rabbanan} maintain the ordinary laws of {\i yichud} even in a situation of {\i aninus}? The {\i gemara} answers: {\i rabbanan savri la k\u8217'Rebbi Yitzchak} \u8212- the {\i rabbanan} follow Rabbi Yitzchak\u8217's position.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Yitzchak\u8217's Response\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yitzchak cites the verse {\i mah yitonen adam chai gever al chatav} \u8212- why should a living man bewail, why should a man complain over his sins? The {\i gemara} reads this as teaching that {\i afilu b\u8217'shaas aninuto, yitzro mitgaber alav} \u8212- even in one\u8217's darkest hour of tragedy, the {\i yetzer} is still active and must be fought. As long as a person is alive, {\i gever al chatav} \u8212- he must be a {\i gibbor} and master his desire for sin. The {\i rabbanan} therefore do not relax the laws of {\i yichud} even during {\i aninus}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abba Shaul, however, disputes this reading of the verse entirely. He contends: {\i mi ktiv hachi? me-chata\u8217'av midotav ktiv} \u8212- the verse does not say {\i me-chata\u8217'av} (his sins) but {\i midotav} (his attributes or lot). The meaning, on Abba Shaul\u8217's reading, is: {\i mah yitonen adam al midosav? vechi gavar al chata\u8217'av?} \u8212- How can a man complain about what befalls him in life? Has he ever truly conquered his sins? {\i Dayo chayim she-natati lo} \u8212- he should simply be grateful to be alive given how much he has sinned. The verse thus has nothing to do with mastery over lust in a moment of mourning.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Woman at Funeral Story\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i rabbanan} respond with an additional argument: {\i kahahu maaseh d\u8217'hahi iteta d\u8217'hava uvda v\u8217'afikteh} \u8212- there was an actual incident involving a woman in which they took her out. Rashi explains that this was a ruse: individuals wished to leave the city for a sexual encounter and staged a pretend infant\u8217's funeral as cover. Therefore, even if one were to concede that a genuine {\i aninus} context reduces sexual desire, the formal laws of {\i yichud} cannot be relaxed, because one can never be certain what people\u8217's true intentions are.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Formal vs.\u160 ?Contextual\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Formalism Discussion\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This discussion opens a broader and recurring question about {\i yichud}: to what degree does {\i halacha} respond to context, and to what degree does it apply formal rules uniformly? In many areas of {\i halacha}, the principle is {\i lo plug} \u8212- no distinctions are drawn based on individual circumstances. The classic example is the {\i gezeirah} against reading by candlelight on {\i Shabbat} lest one tilt the flame: we do not say \u8220"but I have a large-print book\u8221" or \u8220"I am particularly careful around fire.\u8221" The rule applies across the board.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Yet the laws of {\i yichud} seem to operate differently. The original distinctions in the {\i mishnah} \u8212- two men versus two women, husband present versus absent \u8212- were themselves calibrated to the {\i gemara}\u8217's understanding of when the {\i yetzer hara} is more or less operative. So the question becomes: when we encounter a particular scenario, do we look at that context and ask whether the underlying concern actually applies, or do we say the formal rule governs regardless?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Elderly Woman Scenario\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Consider a concrete case: a fifteen-year-old boy is alone in the house with a ninety-year-old woman. Is this {\i assur} because of {\i yichud}? One approach says yes \u8212- a man and a woman, end of story. Another approach looks at the contextual reality and concludes that no one is genuinely concerned about sexual activity taking place. Which approach is correct?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This becomes an ongoing question threading through the rest of the {\i daf}. It arises directly from the present discussion about whether {\i aninus} modifies the concern.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Vulnerability & Temptati\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One additional distinction is worth noting: some of the cases we are discussing involve the protection of a vulnerable party \u8212- a power differential, or the risk of exploitation \u8212- while others are about temptation between consenting adults. Both categories are addressed within the same formal framework of {\i yichud}, without explicit differentiation between them. That is itself a striking feature of these laws.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The argument for formalism is strengthened, paradoxically, by the very contextual reality of the {\i gemara}\u8217's own story \u8212- {\i v\u8217'hahi uvda}, the incident where what appeared to be a funeral was actually a sexual liaison. If context itself can be fabricated, then perhaps that is precisely the reason to maintain formal rules regardless of apparent circumstances. The power of the {\i yetzer hara} is strong enough, and the capacity for self-deception and deception of others great enough, that clear, uniform rules are actually the contextually appropriate response. This is also why institutions \u8212- including Jewish ones \u8212- require unambiguous guidelines: not because everyone is assumed to be planning to sin, but because {\i halacha} recognizes that situations can develop in ways no one initially intended or desired.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Conditions for Yichud\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kasherim Requirement\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} turns to another limiting principle: {\i aval isha achat} \u8212- a single woman with two men. Rav Yehuda said in the name of Rav: {\i lo shanu ela b\u8217'ksherim} \u8212- this rule that two men suffice applies only when those men are {\i kasherim}, men of upstanding moral character.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ten Men Story\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Aval b\u8217'pritzim} \u8212- if the men are of licentious character, {\i afilu b\u8217'asara nami lo} \u8212- even ten men would not be sufficient. The {\i gemara} cites an incident \u8212- {\i hahu ma\u8217'aseh v\u8217'hotziu asara b\u8217'mita} \u8212- in which ten men took a woman out on a bed. Rashi explains: {\i bechezkas shemeisa, d\u8217'kilkilu ima v\u8217'ishes ish haita} \u8212- they proceeded under the pretense that she had died, and she was in fact a married woman. Like the earlier story of the staged infant funeral, this was a scheme to leave the city under cover of a pretended burial. Whether the woman was a willing participant is a serious question, but in any case, when individuals are {\i prutzim}, the presence of additional men provides no deterrent.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Collective Complicity\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Yosef explains why: {\i de-mechabrei bei asara ve-gandei kishura ve-lo michsafei mihadei} \u8212- when ten people are together in something, they have no shame before one another. He compares it to ten people stealing a beam together: each person needs all the others to carry it, and collective involvement eliminates the inhibition that comes from being observed. When everyone is equally implicated, no one serves as a check on anyone else.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sotah Escorts\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} attempts to bring a proof for Rav Yehuda\u8217's distinction from the case of the {\i sotah}. When a husband suspected his wife of adultery and she was to be brought before the {\i beit din} in Jerusalem, two Torah scholars were assigned to accompany them on the journey: {\i shema yavo aleha baderech} \u8212- lest the husband have relations with her on the way, which would nullify the effect of the {\i sotah} waters.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} asks: why specifically Torah scholars? Ordinary people could equally prevent {\i yichud}. The answer is: {\i shani talmidei chachamim deyadei le\u8217'as-hudei beih} \u8212- Torah scholars are needed not simply to prevent {\i yichud} but to be able to warn the husband, and to testify if he violated the prohibition. The two are required to serve as witnesses; they are not required to be Torah scholars merely to prevent seclusion.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 City vs.\u160 ?Journey\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A further distinction: Rav Yehuda said in the name of Rav, {\i lo shanu ela b\u8217'ir} \u8212- the rule that two men suffice applies only in a city. {\i Aval baderech, ad d\u8217'ika tlata} \u8212- on a journey, three men are required. The reason: {\i shema yitzaterech echad mehem lehastin venimtza echad mityached im ha\u8217'erva} \u8212- one of the two men may need to step off the road, and the remaining man and woman would be left alone together.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} again examines the {\i sotah} case as a potential proof. Two scholars accompany the couple on the road to Jerusalem \u8212- {\i trei v\u8217'ihu ha tlata}, two plus the husband makes three. Why three? The answer is that the two scholars are needed as witnesses, not to prevent {\i yichud}; one additional man would have sufficed for that purpose alone. The two and their status as Torah scholars serve independent functions unrelated to the general rule about journeys.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Yetzer Hara Stories\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava Flees Woman\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} now turns to a series of narratives that illustrate the power of the {\i yetzer hara}. Rava and Rav Yehuda were walking together on the road when a woman walked ahead of them. Rava said to Rav Yehuda: {\i dal karecha mikamei Gehenom} \u8212- lift your legs, hurry, get in front of her, so as not to be walking in her proximity, lest they be tempted and fall into {\i gehennom}. This passage clearly frames the concern from the male perspective: the woman as the object of temptation, the {\i yetzer hara} as a male problem.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Yehuda Challenges\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Yehuda responded with a challenge: {\i uvmai d\u8217'amart biksherim shapir dami} \u8212- according to your own ruling that two {\i kasherim} men may be with one woman without concern, we are two {\i kasherim} men and there is one woman; why are you worried? Rava replied: {\i mi yeimar d\u8217'biksherim kegon ana ve-at} \u8212- who says that men like you and me qualify as {\i kasherim} in the relevant sense?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 R. Chanina Standard\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} asks: well, what standard of {\i kasherim} would suffice? {\i K\u8217'gon Rabbi Chanina bar Pappi v\u8217'chaveraiv} \u8212- men like Rabbi Chanina bar Pappi and his peers, who were great {\i chasidim}. Ordinary people, even distinguished {\i amoraim} like Rava and Rav Yehuda, cannot assume they meet that standard.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is a striking passage. On one reading it sounds like an extra {\i chumra} beyond the letter of the law. Yet the Rambam takes it seriously as {\i halacha}, ruling that since no one today can be considered a {\i kasher} in the relevant sense, the normal rule permitting two men with one woman no longer applies without qualification \u8212- and in a generation where no one is fully {\i kasher}, even larger numbers may not suffice for {\i prutzim}. In practice, however, we do rule that we are considered {\i kasherim} and the ordinary rules apply.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This raises a further contemporary question: how does one define {\i parutz} today? Someone who has casual one-night stands is clearly {\i parutz}. But what of someone who is not observant but has intimate relations only within committed long-term relationships? Does that person count for purposes of these rules? The {\i gemara} does not give a precise definition, and the question remains genuinely open.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Enforcement of Yichud\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Lashes without Assumption\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav rules: {\i malkin al ha-yichud v\u8217'ein osrin al ha-yichud} \u8212- one who violates {\i yichud} receives lashes, but the woman is not thereby rendered forbidden. Even if the woman is married, we do not presume that intercourse occurred. The transgression of {\i yichud} is enforced \u8212- publicly and seriously \u8212- but the enormous legal consequences of presuming adultery (which would require the woman to leave her husband) are not triggered. Tosafot adds that even in the case of an unmarried woman, we do not presume intercourse occurred in a way that would render her forbidden to a {\i kohen}. The lashes here are rabbinic rather than biblical in character, consistent with Rashi\u8217's reading that the prohibition of {\i yichud} is derived by way of {\i asmachta}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 No Lashes for Married\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Ashi qualifies: {\i lo amrinan ela be-yichud penuyah} \u8212- lashes are given only when the seclusion involved an unmarried woman. {\i Aval be-yichud eshet ish, lo} \u8212- if the woman is married, we do not give lashes at all. The reason: {\i she-lo tehei motzi la\u8217'az al baneha} \u8212- even if we intend only to punish the transgression of {\i yichud} and not to impute adultery, the public will draw its own conclusions. Once people hear that a man and a married woman received lashes for {\i yichud}, questions will inevitably arise about whether her children are {\i mamzerim}. To prevent that inference from taking root, we do not give lashes in such cases at all.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Mar Zutra\u8217's Method\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Mar Zutra proposed a solution: give lashes {\i and} make a public announcement \u8212- {\i malkei u-makhriz} \u8212- clarifying that no inference of adultery should be drawn and that her children\u8217's status is unaffected. Rav Nachman of Parhatya suggested this approach to Rav Ashi. Rav Ashi rejected it: {\i ika de-shama be-ha ve-lo shama be-ha} \u8212- some people will hear about the lashes and miss the clarifying announcement. Moreover, an announcement that \u8220"we know she did not commit adultery\u8221" is not something one can actually assert with authority; one can only say \u8220"draw no conclusions\u8221" \u8212- and that is unlikely to prevent people from doing exactly that.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Bad Reputation Lashes\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A further ruling from Rav: {\i malkin al lo tovah ha-shmu\u8217'ah} \u8212- lashes are given not only for a proven {\i yichud} violation but also when someone has a bad reputation regarding sexual matters, even without direct evidence of seclusion. The proof-text is {\i al banai ki lo tovah ha-shmu\u8217'ah} \u8212- \u8220"do not, my son, for the report is not good.\u8221" The language {\i al} carries the sense of prohibition: one is forbidden to conduct oneself in a manner that generates a bad reputation. If one does, even absent substantive evidence, one has transgressed something. Mar Zutra would place a yoke on the neck of such a person \u8212- a form of public shaming \u8212- and announce this verse over him, apparently as an alternative to lashes proper.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Major Exceptions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Husband in City\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava rules: {\i ba\u8217'alah ba-ir ein chosheshin mishum yichud} \u8212- if the woman\u8217's husband is in the city, there is no concern of {\i yichud}, because she is always apprehensive that he might appear unexpectedly. This is notably asymmetric: in the {\i mishnah}, the equivalent protection for a man is only operative if his wife is physically present with him \u8212- {\i be-zman she-ishto imo}. The asymmetry reflects the different power dynamics of the rabbinic household: a wife is more fearful of her husband\u8217's surprise appearance than a husband is of his wife\u8217's.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Several nuances arise within this exception. If the husband is in the city but does not know where his wife is, presumably the fear of his arrival does not operate \u8212- she cannot be said to be deterred by a husband who has no idea of her whereabouts. Conversely, if the husband knows where she is \u8212- say, working late at a professional setting \u8212- but it is the kind of location he would never plausibly drop by at that hour, is that still {\i ba\u8217'ala ba-ir}? These are live {\i halachic} questions.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Petach Patuach\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Yosef rules: {\i petach patuach l\u8217'reshut ha-rabim ein chosheshin mishum yichud} \u8212- an open doorway to the public thoroughfare eliminates the concern of {\i yichud}, because passersby may look in or enter at any time. Questions arise about the precise parameters: must the door actually be open, or does unlocked suffice? Would a window through which the interior is visible serve the same function even if the door is closed? What constitutes sufficient openness to the public? These questions have significant practical implications.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Ladder Removal Story\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Bibi came to the house of Rav Yosef. After they ate together upstairs, Rav Yosef descended and instructed his household to remove the step-ladder from beneath Rav Bibi \u8212- {\i shkulu darga mitutei d\u8217'Bibi} \u8212- so that Rav Bibi could not come down to where Rav Yosef\u8217's wife was. The {\i gemara} asks: but Rav Yosef was in the city; does not the rule of {\i ba\u8217'ala ba-ir} apply? The {\i gemara} answers: {\i shani Rav Bibi d\u8217'shushvinisei havai} \u8212- Rav Bibi was different because he was Rav Yosef\u8217's {\i shushvin}, his very close intimate companion \u8212- and indeed Rashi specifies that she was closely bonded with him as well.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shushvinin Closeness\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The principle emerging here is that a {\i shushvin} \u8212- an intimate friend, associated particularly with the groom\u8217's circle \u8212- may feel sufficiently comfortable to allow himself liberties he would not otherwise take. Not sinister or calculated liberties, but the relaxation of the ordinary social distance that normally deters indiscretion. One sits closer, remains longer, interacts more freely \u8212- and one thing, the {\i gemara} implies, can lead to another. Additionally, the very closeness of the friendship may blunt the deterrent effect of {\i ba\u8217'ala ba-ir}: if she is so comfortable with the guest, she may be less vigilant about her husband\u8217's potential arrival. This explains Rav Yosef\u8217's precaution, even though his presence in the city would ordinarily be sufficient.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Inner/Outer Rooms\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Kahana rules on a case of two rooms \u8212- an inner room and an outer room \u8212- with men and women situated in separate spaces. The ruling has two parts: {\i anashim mibachutz v\u8217'nashim mibifnim ein chosheshin mishum yichud} \u8212- men outside and women inside, no concern; {\i anashim mibifnim v\u8217'nashim mibachutz chosheshin mishum yichud} \u8212- men inside and women outside, there is concern.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rashi explains the reasoning. When men are outside and women are inside, the worry would be that a man might enter the inner room where the women are. But his companions outside would notice and question why he would go into a room where he has no business. The social pressure prevents it. Furthermore, even if a woman were to come out to the outer room where the men are, it would not create {\i yichud} because one woman with multiple men is not {\i yichud}. The reverse arrangement is more dangerous: a man might slip into the inner room on the pretext of needing to go out, and no one in the outer room would notice or have reason to stop him, because he would not be going into a space clearly designated for the other sex.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i braisa}, however, has the concern reversed. The {\i braisa} reasons that when men are inside, a man who goes into the outer room with women will still be deterred, because he knows someone else may exit the inner room at any moment. But when men are outside, a man who slips into the inner room is not afraid of being observed \u8212- no one in the outer room has business entering the inner room, so no one will come and discover him.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye concludes: {\i anan na\u8217'avid le-chumra} \u8212- since Rav Kahana and the {\i braisa} disagree about which direction poses the greater risk, we treat both as problematic.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rashi vs.\u160 ?Rambam\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This ruling marks a significant fork in interpretation. Rashi\u8217's reading, as explained above, understands the concern as a {\i gezeirah} \u8212- we prohibit a present situation because it might develop into {\i yichud}. Right now, with men in one room and women in the other, there is no {\i yichud}; the question is whether we forbid the arrangement because it could lead to {\i yichud}. According to this approach, certain configurations must be avoided even when {\i yichud} does not yet exist.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Rambam and the Shulchan Aruch read the case differently. For them, the question is not whether a currently permissible situation might become {\i yichud}, but whether the situation as it actually stands \u8212- once someone has entered the other room \u8212- constitutes {\i yichud} or not. The presence of others who might cross between rooms may function as something akin to {\i ba\u8217'ala ba-ir}, creating a deterrent that means even his actual presence with the women does not rise to {\i yichud}. The practical difference between the two readings is substantial: according to Rashi, we may forbid arrangements that are not currently {\i yichud} on the grounds they might become so; according to the Rambam and Shulchan Aruch, only an actually existing {\i yichud} situation triggers the prohibition.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sister Sleepover Example\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This disagreement has direct practical application. Consider the common case: a sister needs a place to stay for the night and will sleep in her brother\u8217's room while his roommates occupy the other rooms. Is that permitted? According to the Rambam and Shulchan Aruch, as long as everyone retires to their own rooms before going to sleep \u8212- no {\i yichud} exists at that moment \u8212- there is no prohibition, even though one could imagine a scenario in which someone wanders the apartment in the middle of the night and an encounter occurs. The Tzitz Eliezer supports this position. Many other {\i poskim}, however, invoking something like Rashi\u8217's approach, hold that one may not permit a situation so easily capable of becoming {\i yichud}. The question turns precisely on whether Rashi\u8217's {\i gezeirah} framework or the Rambam\u8217's current-state analysis governs.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Gender Separation (III)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Abaye\u8217's Pots\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abaye, when men and women gathered together \u8212- for a {\i derasha} or a {\i chupah}, as Rashi explains the context \u8212- would arrange pots between the two groups to maintain separation, so that anyone attempting to cross from one side to the other would trip over them and make noise. The concern was that individual men or women might slip over to the other side, potentially creating situations of {\i yichud}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rav Amram Story (III)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Captive Women Setup\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A group of captive women who had been redeemed were brought to Nehardea and placed in the house of Rav Amram Chasida for safekeeping until they could return to their homes. They were housed in the attic. The step-ladder was removed \u8212- {\i ashkulu darga mikamaya} \u8212- so they would be protected in their own space and no one could ascend to be with them.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Gender Separation (III)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rava\u8217's Reeds\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rava used reeds rather than pots to create separation between men and women at public gatherings.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rav Amram Story (III)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Amram\u8217's Temptation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 As one of the captive women moved across the upper story, sunlight shone through the skylight and illuminated her, and Rav Amram was overcome with desire. He then seized the step-ladder \u8212- {\i d\u8217'la hayu yachlin be\u8217'asara l\u8217'midalya}, so heavy that ten men could not have moved it \u8212- and moved it by himself alone, the way Yaakov Avinu moved the stone. He climbed the steps toward the woman\u8217's quarters. When he reached the halfway point \u8212- {\i ki mata l\u8217'palga d\u8217'darga} \u8212- he stopped himself, held himself firm, and cried out: {\i nura be\u8217'Amram} \u8212- fire in Amram\u8217's house! The {\i talmidei chachamim} came running, and when they saw there was no fire, they understood what had transpired and told him he had shamed himself. He replied: {\i mutav techsefnu be\u8217'Amram be\u8217'alma hadein, v\u8217'lo techsefnu minei b\u8217'alma d\u8217'atei} \u8212- better that Amram be shamed in this world than that you be ashamed of him in the world to come.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 He then imposed an oath on his {\i yetzer hara} that it should depart from him. It left him {\i ke\u8217'amuda denura} \u8212- like a pillar of fire. He addressed it: {\i chazi de\u8217'at nura ve\u8217'ana bisra, ve\u8217'ana adifa minach} \u8212- you are fire and I am flesh, yet I am greater than you.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This story echoes the notion of {\i gera b\u8217'chata\u8217'av} \u8212- mastery over one\u8217's sins. It simultaneously demonstrates the terrifying power of the {\i yetzer hara} \u8212- capable of enabling a man to move what ten men cannot \u8212- and the possibility of human mastery over it. The narrative is not primarily a {\i halachic} ruling about separate rooms; it is an {\i aggadic} illustration of why these laws exist and what they are guarding against.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Gender Separation (III)\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Regalim Concern\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Avin teaches: {\i saqva d\u8217'shata rigla} \u8212- the weakness of the year is the {\i regalim}, the pilgrimage festivals. That is the time when large gatherings of men and women come together to hear {\i derashot} and celebrate, and when the risk of individuals slipping away together into {\i yichud} is greatest.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Rav Amram Story (III)\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Fire Alarm Strategy\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The device of crying fire \u8212- {\i nura be\u8217'Amram} \u8212- served to transform private temptation into a public crisis, trading the possibility of future shame in the world to come for immediate earthly embarrassment. The choice was deliberate: public shame now, not hidden sin later.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Amram\u8217's Mastery\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Amram\u8217's declaration \u8212- {\i chazi de\u8217'at nura ve\u8217'ana bisra, ve\u8217'ana adifa minach} \u8212- you are flame, I am flesh, yet I am greater \u8212- is the culminating statement of the story. It recalls the earlier framing of {\i gera b\u8217'chata\u8217'av}, the idea that a human being can exercise genuine mastery over the {\i yetzer hara}, even when that {\i yetzer} burns with the intensity of fire.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Sages vs.\u160 ?Yetzer\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Meir Mocks\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Meir used to mock those who gave in to sexual sin \u8212- {\i hava mitlotzetz be\u8217'ovrei aveira}. One day the {\i satan} \u8212- whom Rashi identifies with the {\i yetzer hara} \u8212- appeared to him as a woman on the far bank of a river. There was no boat crossing available. Rav Meir grabbed a rope spanning the river and shimmied across. When he reached the midpoint, the {\i yetzer hara} released him and said: had they not announced in heaven \u8220"Take care of Rav Meir and his Torah,\u8221" I would have valued your life at two {\i ma\u8217'ei} \u8212- you were nearly mine. For all your mocking of sinners, here you were, halfway across a river.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Plimo\u8217's Story\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Plimo would declare each day: {\i gira b\u8217'einei d\u8217'Satan} \u8212- arrows into the eyes of the {\i satan}. One {\i Erev Yom Kippur}, the {\i yetzer hara} appeared to him as a poor man calling at the door. Plimo brought him bread. The poor man complained that everyone else was inside at the {\i seudah mafseket} while he was left outside. Plimo brought him inside. The man complained he was eating apart from the family. Plimo brought him to the table. There the guest sat \u8212- covered in boils and sores, behaving repulsively, popping his blisters, spitting phlegm into his cup. When Plimo rebuked him, the guest acted as though he had died. People cried out: {\i Plimo katal gavra} \u8212- Plimo has killed a man! Plimo fled and hid in the outhouse outside the city.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Meir\u8217's River Rope\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The story of Rav Meir and the river rope belongs to the same pattern as Rav Amram\u8217's story: someone who mocked sinners was himself nearly brought down by the very temptation he scorned. The {\i yetzer hara}\u8217's message is consistent \u8212- those who think themselves immune are the most vulnerable.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Akiva\u8217's Palm Tree\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Akiva similarly used to mock those who yielded to sexual desire. One day the {\i satan} appeared to him as a woman at the top of a palm tree. He began climbing toward her. When he reached the midpoint, the {\i yetzer hara} released him and delivered the same warning: had they not announced in heaven to protect Rabbi Akiva and his Torah, his life would have been worth two {\i ma\u8217'ei}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Escalating Seduction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i yetzer hara}, in Plimo\u8217's story, arrives as a poor man, escalates his demands step by step \u8212- from bread at the door, to food inside, to a seat at the table \u8212- all under the guise of legitimate needs. The Maharsha notes the parallel to an {\i aggadata} in which the {\i yetzer hara} begins as a casual visitor, then a guest, then the master of the house. Each small accommodation leads to the next, and the total result is the complete surrender of one\u8217's home and self. The bread, the wine, the seat at the table \u8212- all of it carries metaphorical weight about how desire enters and establishes residence through incremental compromise.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yetzer Reveals Self\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i yetzer hara} followed Plimo to the outhouse, fell before him, and revealed itself: this is who I am. It then asked: {\i mai ta\u8217'ama amart hachi?} \u8212- why did you go about declaring arrows into my eyes? What did you think you were accomplishing?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 God\u8217's Rebuke\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Leima mar Rachmana niga\u8217'ar bei b\u8217'Satan} \u8212- rather than say \u8220"arrows into the eyes of the Satan,\u8221" one should say: may God rebuke the Satan. The distinction is significant. Trying to destroy the {\i yetzer} is counterproductive \u8212- that is the lesson the {\i yetzer} itself delivers. The {\i yetzer} is part of human nature, created by God; it cannot simply be annihilated. The appropriate prayer is not for its destruction but for divine assistance in restraining and redirecting it. This also connects to the psychiatric observation that denying natural desire entirely, rather than finding licit channels for it, tends to make one more rather than less vulnerable to its power \u8212- as the following story will demonstrate still more starkly.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 R. Chiya Bar Ashi\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Years Without Intimacy\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Chiya bar Ashi had the practice, every time he performed {\i tachanun} \u8212- falling on his face in prayer \u8212- of saying: {\i HaRachaman yatzileinu miyetzer hara} \u8212- may God save us from the evil inclination. One day his wife overheard him and reflected: it has been many years since we have had relations \u8212- and yet he still prays to be saved from the {\i yetzer hara}? She resolved to investigate.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Disguise as Charuta\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One day, as Rabbi Chiya bar Ashi was studying in the garden, his wife adorned herself and altered her appearance so that he would not recognize her. She walked back and forth before him. He took notice and asked: {\i man at?} \u8212- who are you? She answered: {\i ana Charuta} \u8212- I am Charuta, a famously known prostitute, just returned to town. He propositioned her for sex.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Pomegranate Test\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 She said to him: {\i aiti nihalai l\u8217'hach rumna d\u8217'reish tzutzita} \u8212- bring me that pomegranate from the top of that tree. He leaped up, climbed the tree, and brought it to her. The sexual imagery of the pomegranate at the tree\u8217's summit, and the act of scaling it, is unmistakable.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Oven Suicide\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 When Rabbi Chiya bar Ashi returned home, his wife was firing up the oven. He climbed into it \u8212- {\i salik v\u8217'ka yativ b\u8217'gaveih} \u8212- overwhelmed by guilt, intent on self-destruction. The inversion of the Yehuda and Tamar story is difficult to miss: Tamar was to be thrown into the fire, condemned for the very act that Yehuda\u8217's uncontrolled desire had caused; here it is Rabbi Chiya bar Ashi who throws himself in, condemned by his own.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Intent to Sin\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 His wife asked what had happened. He confessed: he had been with a prostitute \u8212- {\i hachi v\u8217'hachi hava ma\u8217'aseh}. She revealed herself: {\i ana havai} \u8212- that was me. He did not believe her until she produced confirming signs \u8212- again echoing Yehuda and Tamar, where the signs established the truth of an unexpected identity. Even after she revealed herself and he realized no actual sin had been committed, he remained inconsolable: {\i ana miha l\u8217'isura ikvanna} \u8212- even so, I had intended to sin. The intention was sufficient to condemn him in his own eyes.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Lifelong Fasting\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 He fasted for the rest of his days \u8212- {\i kol yomav d\u8217'oto tzadik hava mit\u8217'aneh} \u8212- until he died through self-denial. The story is a direct continuation of the lesson from Plimo\u8217's encounter with the {\i yetzer hara}. Rabbi Chiya bar Ashi, by denying himself intimacy with his wife \u8212- presumably in an effort to achieve a higher spiritual state \u8212- made himself far more vulnerable to temptation, not less. The licit channel for sexual desire was closed off; the desire itself remained. The {\i yetzer hara}\u8217's point to Plimo applies here with full force: do not try to destroy the desire; find the proper, permitted expression for it. Refusing one\u8217's wife does not eliminate the {\i yetzer hara} \u8212- it merely displaces it.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Intent & Objective Deed\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Nazirite Vow Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i gemara} closes with a cluster of teachings about the relationship between conscious intent and objective deed. The {\i braisa} cites the verse {\i ishah heferam va-Hashem yislach lah} \u8212- her husband annulled her vows and God will forgive her. The question is: if the husband annulled the vow, why does the woman need divine forgiveness? The case is that of a woman who took a {\i nazir} vow and whose husband annulled it without her knowledge. She then drank wine and became {\i tamei} \u8212- consciously believing herself to be sinning, because she did not know the vow had been annulled.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Forgiveness for Intent\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Objectively, she committed no sin: the vow was already void. But because she acted with the conscious intention of sinning, divine forgiveness is still required. The subjective intention to sin creates culpability even when the objective act is permissible.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Akiva\u8217's Tears\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Akiva would weep when he reached the following {\i pasuk}: {\i u-mah mi she-nitkavein le-echol basar chazir ve-alah be-yado basar taleh, amerah Torah tzrichah kaparah u-selichah} \u8212- someone who intended to eat pork and accidentally ate kosher meat \u8212- the Torah says even this person requires atonement and forgiveness. If one who intended to sin but did not actually do so still needs forgiveness, all the more so one who intended to sin and succeeded.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Kal V\u8217'Chomer\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\i kal v\u8217'chomer} follows: {\i mi she-nitkavein le-echol basar chazir ve-alah be-yado basar chazir \u8212- al achat kamah ve-chamah} \u8212- someone who intended to sin and succeeded in sinning requires all the more forgiveness. The conscious intent to transgress, combined with the actual transgression, demands the greatest degree of {\i kaparah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Akiva\u8217's Tears\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Akiva further weeps over the reverse case: {\i mah mi she-nitkavein le-echol shuman ve-alah be-yado chelev} \u8212- someone who intended to eat kosher fat and accidentally ate {\i chelev}, forbidden fat. Even though the person had no intention of sinning, the Torah says {\i ve-lo yada ve-ashem ve-nasa avono} \u8212- he does not know, yet is guilty, and bears the burden of his sin. If someone who sought to do right and accidentally sinned still bears guilt, then {\i al achat kamah ve-chamah} \u8212- someone who sought to sin and succeeded bears guilt all the more.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Issi\u8217's Ruling\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Issi ben Yehuda adds a final, devastating observation: {\i ve-lo yada ve-ashem ve-nasa avono \u8212- al davar zeh yidagu kol ha-devuyim} \u8212- all those who grieve and wail should wail over this: that even one who sinned without any intention whatsoever still bears the burden of the sin. The Torah assigns culpability not only to deliberate sin but even to inadvertent violation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Taken together, these teachings close the {\i daf} with a powerful warning about the situations one allows oneself to enter. Whether the objective act is sinful, whether the subjective intent was sinful, or whether one only tried to sin without succeeding \u8212- all carry moral and spiritual weight. The laws of {\i yichud}, with all their nuance and complexity, are ultimately an effort to keep people from entering situations in which any one of these three forms of culpability might arise.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Kiddushin 82\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Yichud w/ Relatives\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Parent-Child Sleeping\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 We pick up in the middle of 81b \u8212- roughly twenty to twenty-five lines down \u8212- where the Mishnah states: {\i mityached adam im imo}, a man may be in {\i yichud} together with his mother, and a father with his daughter. The Mishnah continues that they may even sleep together in the same bed without nightclothes, until the children become older \u8212- though the Mishnah does not specify exactly what \u8220"older\u8221" means.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rav Assi vs.\u160 ?Shmuel\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then examines other familial relationships. Rav Yehuda said in the name of Rav Assi: {\i mityached adam im achoto} \u8212- a man may even be in {\i yichud} together with his adult sister. This is striking, since a sister falls squarely within the major category of {\i arayot} in the Torah. One might explain this leniency on the grounds that the {\i yetzer hara} for incest is presumed weaker \u8212- and as Freud observed, you do not need taboos about something that nobody is doing. Alternatively, it may simply be an acknowledgment of domestic reality: brothers and sisters grow up in the same household, and it is not feasible to require them to vacate the premises whenever a parent steps out. The Gemara understands the permission as stemming from either a lesser concern about sexual temptation or from practical necessity.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Assi adds that regarding a mother-son or father-daughter relationship, the leniency is even greater \u8212- they are permitted not merely {\i yichud} but actual cohabitation in the same dwelling. The precise distinction between living with one\u8217's mother versus living with one\u8217's sister once one is an independent householder ({\i baal habayit}) is not entirely spelled out, though the Gemara seems to assume that marriage or bar mitzvah marks a relevant threshold.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 When Rav Yehuda transmitted Rav Assi\u8217's ruling before Shmuel, however, Shmuel objected sharply: {\i asur l\u8217'hityached im kol arayot shebaTorah} \u8212- it is forbidden to be in {\i yichud} with any of the forbidden relations enumerated in the Torah. Shmuel\u8217's position apparently includes even one\u8217's mother and daughter, which creates obvious practical difficulties regarding living arrangements. Moreover, Shmuel extends the prohibition even further: {\i ve\u8217'afilu im behemah} \u8212- one may not even be in {\i yichud} with an animal.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Baraita on Witnesses\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then tests Shmuel\u8217's position against the Mishnah. The Mishnah explicitly permits {\i yichud} between parent and child and even allows them to sleep skin to skin \u8212- a clear contradiction of Shmuel\u8217's blanket prohibition. The Gemara presents Shmuel\u8217's response: you cite one source in my favor, but there is also a Baraita, which teaches that for one\u8217's sister, mother-in-law, and all other {\i arayot} of the Torah, one may not be in {\i yichud} with them unless witnesses are present. This Baraita is consistent with the Mishnah \u8212- which permits only the parent-child exception \u8212- and simultaneously contradicts Rav Assi, who permitted {\i yichud} with a sister. Thus Rav Assi\u8217's position conflicts with the Baraita, while Shmuel\u8217's position conflicts with the Mishnah. Better, the Gemara notes, to be against a Baraita than against a Mishnah.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tannaitic Positions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara resolves the tension by declaring: {\i ela tannai hi} \u8212- this is simply a dispute among Tannaim. Three positions emerge: (1) a lenient view, consistent with Rav Assi, that even non-parental relatives such as a sister present no {\i yichud} concern; (2) a middle position, that of the Mishnah and Baraita, permitting {\i yichud} only for parent-child relationships while prohibiting it for other relatives; and (3) Shmuel\u8217's strict position, prohibiting {\i yichud} with any {\i arayot} whatsoever, including one\u8217's own mother or daughter.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 To illustrate the strictest position, the Gemara cites a Baraita in which Rabbi Meir said to the members of his household: \u8220"Be careful with me regarding my daughter\u8221" \u8212- meaning, never leave me alone in the same room with her. Rabbi Tarfon similarly said: \u8220"Be careful with me regarding my daughter-in-law.\u8221" A certain student mocked this stringency, suggesting that these great Sages surely had no such {\i yetzer hara}. The Gemara records that not many days passed before that same student himself committed a sexual transgression with his own mother-in-law. The principle {\i kol haposel bemumo posel} \u8212- one who imputes a fault to others betrays his own fault \u8212- is apt here. The power of the {\i yetzer hara} for sexual sin is not something to be dismissed, and those who mock others\u8217' caution most loudly may be the least honest with themselves.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Amoraim\u8217's Stringencies\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Regarding Shmuel\u8217's ruling that even {\i yichud} with an animal is prohibited, the Gemara records that several Amoraim adopted similarly stringent practices. Abaye would clear all animals from the field before sitting down to learn. Rav Sheshet would have them moved to the other side of the bridge or river. The Gemara then recounts that Rav Chana mi-Neharde\u8217'a once visited Rav Kahana in Fum Nahara and found him sitting and learning with a cow standing right in front of him. Rav Chana challenged: \u8220"Does the Master not hold that even with an animal one should not be in {\i yichud}?\u8221" Rav Kahana replied: {\i lav adaitai} \u8212- \u8220"I wasn\u8217't thinking about it.\u8221" This is not without irony: precisely because he was absorbed in learning and distracted, the concern had not occurred to him \u8212- yet someone felt it necessary to point it out.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One should note that these scenarios are quite extreme. In an open field where passersby can wander through at any moment, it is debatable whether {\i yichud} even applies \u8212- after all, a space open to the public ({\i petach patuach lerashut harabbim}) is generally not considered {\i yichud}. Indeed, in the very case of Rav Kahana, someone did come by.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tosafot\u8217's Ruling\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tosafot addresses the question of how we rule in practice. Regarding the cases of Abaye, Rav Sheshet, and Rav Chana, Tosafot writes: {\i nireh d\u8217'hanei amoraye chumra b\u8217'alma ka avdi} \u8212- it appears that these Amoraim were merely adopting an extra stringency. The operative ruling follows the Tannaitic position that {\i lo nechshadu Yisrael al mishkav zachar velo al habehemah} \u8212- Jews are not suspected of relations with another man or with an animal \u8212- and therefore the concern Shmuel expressed about animals is not a formal prohibition binding on all. We rule like the Mishnah that parent-child {\i yichud} is permitted. As for the question of other relatives \u8212- a sister, grandmother, or the like \u8212- those remain subjects of debate among the {\i poskim}, and practical questions should be addressed accordingly.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Physical Maturity Limits\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Age Thresholds Debate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Returning to the Mishnah\u8217's statement that parent and child may sleep together even without nightclothes until the child becomes \u8220"older,\u8221" the Gemara asks: {\i vekhamah} \u8212- how old is older? Rava bar Abba said in the name of Rav Assi: a girl ({\i tinokes}) once she reaches nine years old and one day, and a boy ({\i tinok}) once he reaches twelve years and one day. The asymmetry is notable \u8212- a daughter\u8217's threshold is earlier than a son\u8217's. One way to read this is that we are more concerned about a father taking advantage of a daughter than a mother of a son, which connects to broader questions about gendered power in sexual matters.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A second version \u8212- {\i ika d\u8217'amri} \u8212- sets both thresholds at the standard ages of {\i bar} and {\i bat mitzvah}: twelve years and a day for a girl, thirteen years and a day for a boy. This parallel formulation is linked to the onset of halachic adulthood and its associated physical maturity.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Physical Development Sig\u8230 ?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara adds a qualification: even once these ages are reached, the prohibition on sleeping unclothed together applies only when the child has in fact reached physical maturity \u8212- when the breasts have developed and pubic hair has appeared, as described with reference to a verse in Yechezkel. If those signs of maturity have not yet appeared, the concern is not triggered, even at those ages.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One might ask: why not simply rely on the signs of physical maturity alone, regardless of age? And further, the Gemara\u8217's description of maturity is framed entirely in terms of the daughter\u8217's body \u8212- which reflects a shift to an exclusively male perspective. No parallel description of the son\u8217's physical development is offered, despite the earlier parallel discussion of mother-son sleeping arrangements.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Embarrassment Standard\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Raffin bar Pappa said in the name of Rav Chisda: the Gemara\u8217's ruling applies specifically to a case where the daughter would not be embarrassed to stand naked before her father. But if she {\i would} be embarrassed \u8212- {\i aval bosha la\u8217'amod lefanav aruma assur}. Why? {\i Yetzer ala bah} \u8212- the {\i yetzer} has risen within her; she now understands what it means to be unclothed before a member of the opposite sex. Once that awareness exists, even apart from any formal age threshold or physical sign, the concern is triggered. Notably, the concern here is entirely about consensual sexual awareness. There is no mention of the risk of coercion or abuse \u8212- the prohibition kicks in when {\i she} experiences the {\i yetzer}, not when {\i he} might harm her.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Modern Concerns\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This section of Gemara can be read through two very different lenses. One reading appreciates the Gemara\u8217's capacity to acknowledge domestic reality without excessive vigilance: in the vast majority of cases, a parent and child sleeping together is entirely innocent, and the Gemara reflects that. Another reading notices what the Gemara does not address: the documented reality of parental abuse of children, particularly fathers of daughters. The Gemara seems unconcerned with coercion or rape as a category of risk here \u8212- the entire framework presupposes a concern about willing participants. The question of whether the halachic system should have legislated more protectively in this space \u8212- as it did in some areas (like {\i yichud} with non-relatives) but not here \u8212- is worth sitting with honestly.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Boundary Violations\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Grandfather Lap Case\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara recounts: Rav Acha bar Abba came to the home of his son-in-law Rav Chisda. He took his granddaughter and sat her on his lap \u8212- {\i oseiv beichnafeih}, placing her in his cloak. His son-in-law challenged him.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Marriage Status Issue\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The son-in-law said: {\i lo savar lah mar de\u8217'eishes ish} \u8212- does the Master not realize that she is a married woman? The granddaughter, young as she was \u8212- apparently a minor, {\i ktana} \u8212- had already been betrothed. Rav Acha responded by going on the offensive: {\i avarta lach adei-Rav} \u8212- you have violated the ruling of Rav, who taught (as cited earlier in the second {\i perek} of this masechta) that a father may not betroth his minor daughter until she grows up and can say, \u8220"I want this particular man.\u8221" You have married her off against Rav\u8217's ruling \u8212- so do not lecture me.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shmuel\u8217's Services Ban\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The son-in-law replied: you, too, are violating a teaching of Shmuel \u8212- {\i de\u8217'amar Shmuel, ein mishtamshin be\u8217'isha}, one may not make use of the services of a woman. Shmuel\u8217's principle is that a man should not have a woman attend to his personal domestic needs, even when there appears to be nothing sexual about the arrangement. Here, the granddaughter is not exactly serving him in that sense, but the same principle applies: even seemingly innocent physical proximity to women should be avoided. Shmuel, true to form throughout this tractate, is maximally stringent \u8212- no animals, no relatives, and now no female domestic attendants.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This raises a question: at what age does {\i yichud} or physical proximity with a young girl become a concern? The son-in-law appears to have raised the issue specifically because she was married, suggesting that without that status, sitting a young granddaughter on one\u8217's lap would not have been considered problematic. The precise age threshold for these concerns \u8212- whether it follows the age of {\i biah} (nine for a girl), {\i bat mitzvah}, or the signs of physical maturity discussed above \u8212- remains an open question.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Sake of Heaven\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rav Acha responded: {\i ana k\u8217'idach de\u8217'Shmuel sevira li} \u8212- I follow this other teaching of Shmuel. For Shmuel also taught: {\i hakol leshem shamayim} \u8212- when one\u8217's intentions are entirely for the sake of heaven, there is no cause for concern. This creates a remarkable tension within Shmuel\u8217's own corpus: on one side, sweeping prohibitions against any proximity to women or {\i arayot}; on the other, a principle that pure intent renders such proximity permissible.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rashi on Intent\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rashi explains {\i hakol leshem shamayim} in this context: {\i ve\u8217'ein da\u8217'ati achareiha le\u8217'chibas ishus} \u8212- my intention is not in any way sexual; {\i ela le\u8217'chibas kurva} \u8212- it is the love of kinship, {\i vela\u8217'asos koras ruach le\u8217'imah k\u8217'she\u8217'ani mechabev es bitah} \u8212- and to give pleasure to her mother by showing how much I cherish her daughter. A grandfather embracing his granddaughter is an expression of familial love, not sexuality.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is an important observation because it challenges the binary that sometimes appears in {\i halachic} discourse \u8212- as if there are only two kinds of touch: neutral/transactional and sexual. Rashi here acknowledges a third category: warm, intimate, loving touch that carries no sexual charge. The question then becomes whether {\i hakol leshem shamayim} is a principle available to all, or only to those who are genuinely {\i tzaddikim gemurim} with complete confidence that no {\i hirhur} will follow.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Touch & Intent\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Neutral Touch Status\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This Gemara opens a broader question: is there a formal {\i issur de\u8217'rabbanan} on neutral touch \u8212- a handshake, a casual greeting \u8212- between an unmarried man and a woman he is not married to? The case of a married woman who is a {\i niddah} is different: any touch there carries the weight of {\i issur arayot}. But what of neutral physical contact in ordinary social contexts?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Handshake Cases\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A number of {\i rishonim} and {\i poskim} hold that if the touch is genuinely non-sexual, there is no technical {\i issur de\u8217'rabbanan} \u8212- the prohibition, if any, is entirely contingent on the intent and likely consequence of the contact. A relevant contemporary case arose in Switzerland, where Muslim boys refused to shake their female teacher\u8217's hand. The school initially accommodated them consistently \u8212- neither male nor female teacher would receive a handshake. When this became publicly known, there was widespread outcry, and the court ruled that the students must shake the teacher\u8217's hand. The intersection of cultural norms, religious conscience, and civil law in that case mirrors the {\i halachic} complexity of this question.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two Approaches\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Two distinct approaches emerge from the {\i rishonim} and {\i poskim}. One approach holds that there is a formal {\i issur de\u8217'rabbanan} on any physical contact with an {\i ervah}, regardless of intent, though it may be overridden in cases of {\i kevod habriyot} \u8212- for instance, in a business context where refusing to shake hands would cause significant embarrassment. Under this approach, the practice of waiting for the woman to extend her hand first is sometimes invoked, though in many situations {\i kevod habriyot} may itself require the man to initiate.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The second approach, grounded in this Gemara and parallel sources, holds that where the touch is genuinely neutral \u8212- carrying no sexual charge for either party \u8212- there is no formal prohibition at all. The permission is not an override; there is simply nothing to override. Under this view, the question is entirely case-by-case, evaluated honestly based on the nature of the contact and the realistic likelihood of {\i hirhurim}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Self-Deception Risk\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The vulnerability in the second approach is precisely the problem illustrated by the student who mocked Rabbi Tarfon. The very mechanism of denial \u8212- \u8220"I have no such {\i yetzer hara}, this concerns me not at all\u8221" \u8212- is the most dangerous posture one can adopt. The Gemara\u8217's recurring theme is that sexual temptation is more powerful and more subtle than people acknowledge, and self-assessment in this domain is notoriously unreliable. One can balance these two teachings of Shmuel \u8212- the sweeping formal prohibitions on one hand, and {\i hakol leshem shamayim} on the other \u8212- either by saying the formal rules always apply as defaults and only genuine special cases may invoke the principle of pure intent; or by saying there are no formal rules at all, only an obligation to exercise honest personal judgment. Either way, the risk of self-deception looms large.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Multiple People Yichud\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Two Women Rule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara records that Rava taught: a man may be in {\i yichud} with two {\i yevamot} \u8212- sisters-in-law \u8212- or with two {\i tzarot} (co-wives), or with a woman and her mother-in-law, or with a woman and her stepdaughter. The common thread in all these cases is mutual animosity: the two women despise one another, and therefore each functions as an effective watchdog on the other\u8217's behavior. If a man were to attempt to seduce one of them, the other would immediately expose him, since she has every incentive to ruin his reputation. The general rule is that one man cannot be alone with two women \u8212- because he might seduce both, or seduce one while the other remains silent. But where the two women hate each other, that mutual hostility renders any misconduct effectively impossible to conceal.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Yevamot & Tzarot\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The specific examples \u8212- {\i shtei yevamot} and {\i shtei tzarot} \u8212- are particularly apt. Two sisters-in-law are in tension because if one husband dies, levirate marriage ({\i yibbum}) may make the survivor a co-wife to the other. Co-wives are structurally rivals, and their animosity is presumed. A woman and her mother-in-law, and a woman and her stepdaughter, are similarly natural adversaries whose mutual surveillance creates a reliable deterrent.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Woman & Child\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara adds another case: a woman together with a young child who understands what sexual relations are but would not herself willingly participate \u8212- {\i sheyoda\u8217'at ta\u8217'am biah ve\u8217'ein moseret atzmah l\u8217'biah}. Rashi explains: {\i sheyoda\u8217'at ta\u8217'am biah} \u8212- she understands enough about what sex is that she would report something inappropriate if she witnessed it \u8212- {\i sheteda l\u8217'saper devarim bashuk}, she will blurt it out in the marketplace. Children have no discretion; they will innocently repeat anything they have seen. But {\i einah moseret atzmah l\u8217'biah} \u8212- she will not willingly participate in the act herself, because she has not yet developed a sexual inclination: {\i mitoch kotan adayin lo lavsha yetzer}. The child\u8217's involuntary transparency thus makes the situation safer than two adult women whose silence might be secured.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Child as Witness\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The child functions, in other words, as an involuntary watchdog \u8212- not a formal witness in the legal sense, but an unavoidable deterrent. It is worth noting that the Gemara\u8217's entire analysis here assumes the concern is about consensual relations. The observation that the child lacks the inclination to participate ({\i einah moseret atzmah l\u8217'biah}) implicitly sets aside any concern about coercion or abuse \u8212- an absence that modern readers will find striking.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Unmarried Teachers\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Teacher Restrictions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Mishnah states: {\i lo yilamed adam ravak sofrim} \u8212- an unmarried man ({\i ravak}, meaning never-married) should not serve as a school teacher. The grammar is unusual \u8212- literally \u8220"he should not teach scribes\u8221" \u8212- but the meaning is that he should not be a {\i melamed}, a teacher of young children. The Mishnah adds a parallel case: {\i velo telamed isha sofrim} \u8212- a woman likewise should not be a school teacher. This is particularly striking given that the overwhelming majority of primary school teachers today are women.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Eliezer Extension\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Eliezer extends the prohibition: {\i af mi she\u8217'ein lo isha lo yilamed sofrim} \u8212- even a man who currently lacks a wife, whether through divorce or widowhood, may not serve as a school teacher. One might have argued that a man who has previously been married and has had an outlet for his sexuality is no longer at risk \u8212- but Rabbi Eliezer rejects this reasoning. The conclusion is that under all these positions, the only person who may serve as a school teacher is a married man currently living with his wife.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A Baraita further refines Rabbi Eliezer\u8217's position: {\i af mi sheyesh lo ve\u8217'einah sheruya etzlo lo yilamed soferim} \u8212- even a man who is married but whose wife is temporarily away (traveling, visiting family, etc.) may not teach. Even that temporary separation creates sufficient concern that he should take leave from teaching while she is absent.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Shepherds & Animals\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Mishnah also records Rabbi Yehuda\u8217's ruling: {\i lo yir\u8217'eh ravak behemah} \u8212- an unmarried man should not be a shepherd, and {\i velo yishnu shenei ravakim betallit achat} \u8212- two unmarried men should not sleep under the same blanket. The {\i chachamim}, however, permit these, and a Baraita supports them: {\i lo nechshadu Yisrael al mishkav zachar velo al habehemah} \u8212- Israel is not suspected of relations with another male or with an animal. The {\i chachamim}\u8217's position is that since Jews are presumed not to engage in these acts, there is no basis for the restrictions Rabbi Yehuda imposes.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This statement \u8212- {\i lo nechshadu Yisrael al mishkav zachar} \u8212- carries significant conceptual weight. It reflects that {\i Chazal} treated sex between two men as so utterly foreign to the Jewish norm that they saw no need to legislate {\i yichud} rules for it, just as they saw no need for such rules regarding animals. This creates genuinely complex questions for contemporary {\i halachic} discussion: if a gay man is known to be sexually active with other men, does the formal {\i yichud} framework now apply? Some {\i poskim} argue that once the concern is real for a particular individual, formal rules do kick in; others maintain that {\i Chazal} never legislated in this area at all, and therefore the only applicable framework is the general principle of not exposing oneself to temptation \u8212- not a formal {\i issur}, but basic {\i halachic} responsibility.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Related questions arise as well: does the absence of formal legislation mean a gay man may be in {\i yichud} with a woman without concern? These are live questions debated in contemporary {\i posekim}, with views on multiple sides.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Parent Attraction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara asks: what precisely is the concern that an unmarried male teacher poses? One might assume it is about the children \u8212- but the Gemara immediately dismisses this: we have just established that {\i lo nechshadu Yisrael al mishkav zachar}, so the children in {\i cheder} (who are all boys) cannot be the concern. The Gemara answers: {\i ela ravak mishum immahot deyenokei} \u8212- the concern is the mothers who bring the children to school. An unmarried male teacher will have daily, intimate, emotionally charged conversations with these mothers \u8212- about their children\u8217's progress, their struggles, their needs \u8212- and such sustained personal contact creates temptation. Similarly, {\i ve\u8217'ishah mishum avot deyenokei} \u8212- an unmarried female teacher poses the same risk vis-\u224 ?-vis the fathers.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Gender Asymmetry\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This raises an important question: why is a {\i married} man a permissible teacher, but a {\i married} woman is not? The Mishnah says an unmarried woman cannot teach \u8212- not a married one who lacks a wife, but {\i any} woman. The logic for the male teacher is clear: a married man is less susceptible to the temptation presented by the mothers, or less likely to pursue it, whether because he is sexually satisfied at home or because he fears his wife\u8217's reaction. But why does marriage provide no equivalent protection for a female teacher? Why is the married female teacher still considered a risk to the fathers who come to collect their children?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The most plausible reading is that the Mishnah\u8217's primary concern is the vulnerability of the male party \u8212- the fathers \u8212- rather than the female teacher\u8217's inclinations. We seem to be more worried about the fathers than about the married male teacher. The Mishnah may not have fully incorporated later Amoraic rulings such as {\i ba\u8217'alah ba\u8217'ir} (the principle that a woman is deterred by concern for her absent husband) \u8212- and even if it had, that principle addresses the woman\u8217's behavior, not the fathers\u8217' temptation. The underlying structure reflects the patriarchal framework of the society in which these rules were formed.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Women-Focused Trades\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rashi-Rambam\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Before proceeding to the Mishnah\u8217's next case, the earlier question of whether three women together eliminate {\i yichud} deserves resolution. Rashi reads the coming Mishnah as built on the premise that as a general rule, one man together with three women does {\i not} constitute {\i yichud}. It is only for the person whose trade involves constant contact with women \u8212- a dressmaker, a jeweler \u8212- that the normal leniency of three women is removed, because familiarity has lowered his inhibitions. Rambam and Tosafot, by contrast, hold that two women, three women, and any larger number of women are equally subject to {\i yichud} with a single man, unless a second man is present. Under this reading, the Mishnah is simply affirming that even the professional who deals with women daily must observe the standard {\i halachot} and ensure another man is present.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The practical ruling among Ashkenazim generally follows Rashi: one man with three women does not constitute {\i yichud}. Some authorities, however, follow the Rambam.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Business with Women\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Mishnah states: {\i kol she\u8217'asakav im hanashim lo yityached im hanashim} \u8212- anyone whose business involves women should not be in {\i yichud} with women. The examples that follow in the Gemara include jewelers and goldsmiths, weavers and embroiderers, those who clean hand-mills, traveling perfumers and fragrance vendors, tailors, barbers (since mothers bring children to barbers), launderers, bloodletters, bathhouse attendants, and tanners. This is a broad list of tradespeople whose professional work puts them in sustained personal contact with women. Regarding some of these \u8212- particularly the goldsmith and jeweler who adjusts rings and bracelets, and the tailor who fits garments \u8212- the nature of the work itself involves physical proximity of an intimate kind.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara adds that neither a king nor a {\i kohen gadol} should be appointed from among those who practice these lowly trades \u8212- not because their practitioners are technically disqualified ({\i pasul}) as witnesses or sinners, but because {\i dezilu umnasaihu} \u8212- the trades themselves are considered socially degraded. In a patriarchal society, occupations requiring constant interaction with and attendance upon women carried a certain stigma.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Avoid Women-Trades\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Mishnah concludes: {\i velo yilamed adam b\u8217'no umanut hanashim} \u8212- a man should not teach his son a trade that deals primarily with women, because of the ongoing exposure to temptation that such work entails. This sets up the Mishnah\u8217's positive teaching, which follows immediately.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Clean Livelihood\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rabbi Meir Teaching\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Meir teaches: {\i l\u8217'olam yilamed adam b\u8217'no umanut nekiyah v\u8217'kalah} \u8212- a man should always teach his son a clean and easy trade \u8212- one that does not require him to soil his hands, that is not physically exhausting, and that does not put him in uncomfortable social situations. Why not choose a more demanding and lucrative trade? Rabbi Meir answers: {\i veyitpallel l\u8217'mi sheha\u8217'osher vehanechasim shelo} \u8212- one should pray to the One to Whom all wealth and possessions belong, because {\i she\u8217'ein umanut she\u8217'ein bah aniyut ve\u8217'ashirut} \u8212- there is no trade that lacks both rich and poor practitioners. You can find stockbrokers who earn millions and stockbrokers who barely survive. Therefore {\i shelo aniyut min ha\u8217'umanut velo ashirut min ha\u8217'umanut} \u8212- poverty and wealth are not functions of one\u8217's profession; {\i ela hakol lefi zechuto} \u8212- it is all according to one\u8217's merit. Tosafot interestingly interprets {\i zechut} here as referring to one\u8217's {\i mazel}, based on a parallel Gemara, though ordinarily {\i zechut} and {\i mazel} are quite distinct concepts.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Lottery Parable\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The point is captured by the familiar parable of the man who prays his whole life, keeps all the {\i mitzvot}, and cannot understand why he has never won the lottery \u8212- only to hear a heavenly voice respond: \u8220"Idiot, buy a ticket.\u8221" One obviously cannot wait for money to fall from heaven without working. Rabbi Meir is saying: yes, you must work; but choose a job that will not exhaust you or entangle you in spiritual danger, that will leave you time to learn {\i Torah} \u8212- for that, as the Maharsha explains, is the implicit point \u8212- and trust that God controls the financial outcome. The size of one\u8217's income is not determined by how demanding one\u8217's profession is.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Animal Sustenance\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Shimon ben Elazar asks: have you ever seen an animal or a bird that has a profession? {\i Vehem mitparnesim shelo b\u8217'tza\u8217'ar} \u8212- they are sustained without effort. {\i Vehalo lo nivreu ela leshamsheni} \u8212- and these animals were created merely to serve me. {\i Ve\u8217'ani nivrati leshamesh et koni} \u8212- I was created for a higher purpose, to serve my Creator. Does it not stand to reason, then, that I should be sustained without effort, if even beings of lower purpose are provided for effortlessly?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The answer: {\i ela she\u8217'hare\u8217'oti ma\u8217'asai vekippachti et parnasati} \u8212- because I have corrupted my deeds and thereby forfeited that easy sustenance. The obligation to labor is the consequence of the primordial sin \u8212- {\i beze\u8217'at apecha tochal lechem} \u8212- not the natural order of creation. Rabbi Shimon ben Elazar appears to be reinforcing Rabbi Meir\u8217's message: do not imagine that professional choice is what determines your income. The world as it was meant to be required no effort for sustenance; the effort we now expend is the consequence of sin. So work, yes \u8212- but focus on leading a righteous life, pray to God, and do not overinvest your energies in professional advancement at the expense of {\i Torah} and {\i mitzvot}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Disreputable Trades\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Robber Trades\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Abba Guryon of Tzaidon taught in the name of Abba Gurya: {\i lo yilamed adam et b\u8217'no chammar gamal kaddar sappan ro\u8217'eh v\u8217'chanvani} \u8212- a man should not teach his son to be a donkey driver, a camel driver, a wagon driver, a sailor, a shepherd, or a storekeeper, {\i she\u8217'umanutan umanut listim} \u8212- because their trade is the trade of robbers. Rashi explains: those who drive animals and travel on long journeys graze their animals in others\u8217' fields and collect firewood from others\u8217' land. The storekeeper cheats with weights and measures.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Refined Judgment\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Yehuda then offers a more nuanced reading in the name of Abba Guryon: {\i hachammarim rubban resha\u8217'im} \u8212- donkey drivers, most of them are wicked, since they regularly trespass and appropriate from others. {\i Vegamalim rubban ksheirim} \u8212- but camel drivers, most of them are upright. Why? First, because they travel through the desert, where there is little opportunity to steal from others. Second, as Rashi notes, the constant exposure to mortal danger makes them more pious \u8212- when one\u8217's life is perpetually at risk, one prays more sincerely and lives more carefully. {\i Hasapanim rubban chasidim} \u8212- and sailors are mostly genuinely righteous, for the dangers of the sea are even more acute and their dependence on God more immediately felt.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Maharsha reconciles the two statements by arguing that the original condemnation of all these trades as the work of robbers ({\i listim}) applied specifically to shepherds and storekeepers, who have the most structural opportunity for theft. The camel drivers and sailors were included in the list not because they rob but because their professions take them far from home for extended periods \u8212- which carries the concerns about instability, absence from family, and separation from {\i Torah} life that Rabbi Meir also addressed. They are discouraged not as criminals but as impractical for a life of Jewish normalcy.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Doctors to Gehinnom\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Tov shebi-rofin l\u8217'Gehennom} \u8212- the best of the doctors go to {\i Gehinnom}. Rashi offers two explanations: first, the excellent doctor takes such pride in his own vitality and health that he does not humble his heart before God \u8212- unlike the sailors, who are constantly face to face with death and therefore pray earnestly. Second, the best doctor sometimes causes death through negligence or overconfidence, and at other times has the ability to treat a poor patient but withholds care. The Maharsha\u8217's reading is particularly pointed: {\i tov shebi-rofin} \u8212- the doctor who considers himself the {\i best} is the one destined for {\i Gehinnom}. It is the physician who will not consult colleagues, who trusts his own judgment above all, who is most dangerous. The arrogance of thinking oneself the best of doctors is itself the spiritual flaw that sends one to {\i Gehinnom}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Butcher as Amalek\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Vehakasher shebatabbachim shutfo shel Amalek} \u8212- and the most apparently upright of butchers is a partner with Amalek. Rashi suggests this refers to giving people {\i treifa} meat rather than lose the {\i parnassah}. But the identification with Amalek points toward something deeper: the butcher who appears pious but channels a redirected bloodlust. The association with Amalek \u8212- the archetype of gratuitous violence \u8212- implies that even the {\i kosher} butcher participates in a culture of bloodshed, however redirected it may be through the structures of {\i shechita}.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Torah Over Trade\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Nehorai\u8217's Position\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Nehorai declares: {\i maniach ani kol umanut sheba\u8217'olam ve\u8217'einey melameid et bni ela Torah} \u8212- I would set aside all the professions in the world and teach my son nothing but {\i Torah}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Maharsha\u8217's Reading\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Maharsha clarifies that Rabbi Nehorai is not arguing that a profession is unnecessary or that one should simply wait for sustenance to arrive miraculously. Rather, he is reinforcing Rabbi Meir\u8217's emphasis: yes, one acquires some profession, but the entirety of one\u8217's educational priority \u8212- the focus of what one teaches and prepares one\u8217's son for \u8212- should be {\i Torah}. One invests minimally in professional training and maximally in {\i Torah} formation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Torah\u8217's Rewards\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The reason is practical as much as theological: {\i she\u8217'adam ochel mis\u8217'chara ba\u8217'olam ha-zeh vehakeren kayemet lo l\u8217'olam ha-ba} \u8212- a person enjoys the rewards of {\i Torah} in this world and the principal remains for {\i olam ha-ba}. By contrast, every other profession fails in the end: {\i keshe\u8217'adam ba lidei choli o lidei zikna o lidei yisurim ve\u8217'eino yachol la\u8217'asok bim\u8217'lachto, harei hu meit bara\u8217'av} \u8212- when illness, old age, or suffering strikes and a person can no longer practice his trade, he starves. {\i Aval Torah einah khen} \u8212- but {\i Torah} is not like that. It {\i mishmarto mikol ra b\u8217'na\u8217'aruto} \u8212- protects a person from all harm in youth (and presumably from sexual temptation as well, which has been the recurring theme of this {\i perek}), {\i venotenet lo acharit vetikva b\u8217'ziknuto} \u8212- and gives him hope and meaning in old age.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Biblical Support\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Two prooftexts are adduced. For youth: {\i vekovei Hashem yachalifu koach ya\u8217'alu ever kanesharim} \u8212- those who hope in God will renew their strength, they will soar on wings like eagles. For old age: {\i od yenuvun b\u8217'seivah, dashanim vera\u8217'ananim yihiyu} \u8212- they will still be fruitful in old age, still full of sap and vigor. Torah learners never become obsolete.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Aggadic Expansion\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Abraham Prooftext\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara then brings: {\i vekhen hu omer b\u8217'Avraham Avinu} \u8212- Abraham our forefather fulfilled the entire {\i Torah} before it was given, as it says, {\i eikev asher shama Avraham b\u8217'koli vayishmor mishmarti mitzvotai chukotai vetorotai} \u8212- because Abraham hearkened to My voice and observed My safeguards, My commandments, My statutes, and My teachings. This prooftext appears to be something of a gloss \u8212- it is not immediately obvious how Abraham\u8217's precocious {\i Torah} observance connects to the preceding argument about {\i Torah}\u8217's rewards in youth and old age.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Tannaitic Authenticity\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Indeed, some scholars argue that the entirety of the section from Rabbi Meir onward may be a later {\i aggadic} addition \u8212- perhaps a Baraita or redactional gloss \u8212- rather than part of the original Mishnah itself. The real conclusion of the {\i perek}, on this view, was {\i kol she\u8217'asakav im nashim lo yityached im hanashim}, and possibly the line immediately following about not teaching one\u8217's son a women\u8217's trade. The {\i aggadic} material from Rabbi Meir through Rabbi Nehorai, along with the Abraham prooftext at the end, would then represent a later accretion that organically closed the tractate on a broader thematic note.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Life Organization\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Whether or not the entire section is a later addition, its thematic fitness as a conclusion to Masechet Kiddushin is undeniable. This tractate is often thought of as being simply about the laws of betrothal. But a broader view reveals something more comprehensive: Kiddushin opens with the paradigm of a father betrothing his daughter \u8212- {\i kiddushin} as the primal act of establishing a family unit \u8212- and proceeds through {\i kinyanim}, the acquisition structures of Jewish law, to the question of parental obligations toward children. And what are those obligations? To marry off one\u8217's child, to teach him {\i Torah}, and to teach him a trade. Masechet Kiddushin turns out to be, on this reading, a guide to organizing a Jewish life: marriage, {\i Torah}, and {\i parnassah} \u8212- the three pillars.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Three Obligations\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The final {\i aggadic} section thus makes sense as the natural culmination of the tractate. Having established the {\i halachot} of marriage and family formation, and having devoted much of this last {\i perek} to the dangers of sexual temptation in professional contexts, the Mishnah closes by asking: what kind of work should you do, and how do you balance the obligations of {\i parnassah} and {\i Torah}? The answer \u8212- choose a clean and easy trade, pray to God for wealth, and focus your deepest educational energies on {\i Torah} \u8212- integrates the tractate\u8217's practical and spiritual concerns into a single unified vision of how to live.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Disreputable Trades (II)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Bloodletter Traits\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A Baraita lists ten characteristics attributed to bloodletters: {\i mehalach al tzido} \u8212- he walks with an erect, prideful bearing; {\i verucho gassa} \u8212- he is haughty; {\i veniklat veyoshev} \u8212- when he sits, he reclines with exaggerated ease, displaying his arrogance; {\i ve\u8217'eino tzara} \u8212- he is stingy. Rashi explains the stinginess: a bloodletter is always being hosted and fed wherever he practices, so he grows accustomed to never spending his own money. {\i Ve\u8217'eino ra\u8217'ah} \u8212- he has an evil eye against others; he dislikes seeing healthy people, since healthy people are bad for business. {\i Ochel harbeh umotzi kima} \u8212- he eats abundantly (from the fine meals to which he is always treated) but produces very little waste, because, Rashi notes, the rich food \u8212- fatty meats, white bread \u8212- has that effect.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Most significantly: {\i vechashud al ha\u8217'arayot} \u8212- he is suspect of sexual sin. Rashi explains: {\i mito shehanashim tzrichot lo ve\u8217'asakav imahem} \u8212- because women need his services and his work is with them. The bloodletter is in a position of power over vulnerable people, and that power creates opportunity. This is the dynamic of abuse that recurs throughout this {\i perek}: the professional who has structured access to women, who is seen as an authority, who meets with them in relatively private circumstances. The remaining traits \u8212- {\i ve\u8217'al hagezel}, he is prone to theft through overcharging, and {\i al shefichut damim}, he is careless with life \u8212- complete the picture of a professional whose work breeds dangerous moral habits.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Necessary Work\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rebbe on Trades\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rebbe teaches: {\i ein lecha umanut she\u8217'overet min ha\u8217'olam} \u8212- there is no profession that disappears from the world. Every trade has its role; someone will always need to do every kind of work. {\i Ashrei mi she\u8217'roeh et horav be\u8217'umanut me\u8217'ula} \u8212- praised is the person who sees his parents engaged in a refined profession. {\i Oy lo lemi she\u8217'roeh et horav be\u8217'umanut pguma} \u8212- woe to the one who sees his parents in a degraded trade. Rebbe \u8212- who was the {\i nasi} and a man of considerable means \u8212- may have in mind something different from Rabbi Meir\u8217's \u8220"clean and easy trade.\u8221" Whether Rebbe means social prestige, moral cleanliness, or simply not the disreputable trades catalogued in this {\i perek} is not entirely clear.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Perfumer & Tanner\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Iy efshar la\u8217'olam belo basam uvelo borski} \u8212- the world cannot exist without a perfumer or a tanner. Both are necessary. But {\i ashrei mi she\u8217'umanuto basam} \u8212- praised is the one whose trade is perfumery, {\i oy lo lemi she\u8217'umanuto borski} \u8212- and woe to the one whose trade is tanning. Someone must be a tanner \u8212- the world cannot function otherwise \u8212- but you personally should aspire to be the perfumer.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Final Gender Statement\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Mishnah closes with a statement of breathtaking scope and undeniable provocation: {\i iy efshar la\u8217'olam belo zacharim uvelo nekevot} \u8212- the world cannot exist without males and females. {\i Ashrei mi shebanav zacharim} \u8212- praised is the one whose children are male. {\i Oy lo lemi shebanav nekevot} \u8212- woe to the one whose children are female.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Dowry Reading\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The most charitable and probably most historically accurate reading of this statement concerns economics, not ontology. In the world of the Talmud, daughters required dowries and were not expected to earn a living independently. A father with daughters faced the burden of providing for their marriages and had no expectation that they would support him in old age. A father with sons, by contrast, had financial assets who would provide for the household, carry on his trade, and support him in his later years. The statement is about the financial anxiety of raising daughters in a patriarchal economic structure \u8212- along the lines of Tevye in {\i Fiddler on the Roof}, who has five daughters and no sons, and whose central preoccupation is marrying them off. It is emphatically not a statement about the intrinsic worth of daughters, and should not be read as one.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Masechet Closure\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Meir Restated\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Gemara restates Rabbi Meir\u8217's teaching: {\i l\u8217'olam yilamed adam livno umanut nekiyah v\u8217'kalah veyevakesh rachamim lemi she\u8217'ha\u8217'osher vehanechasim shelo} \u8212- always teach your son a clean and easy trade, and pray for mercy to the One to Whom wealth and possessions belong. {\i She\u8217'ein aniyut min ha\u8217'umanut ve\u8217'ein ashirut min ha\u8217'umanut ela lemi she\u8217'ha\u8217'osher shelo} \u8212- poverty and wealth are not functions of one\u8217's trade but of God\u8217's will \u8212- {\i shenemar li hakesef veli hazahav neum Hashem Tzevaot} \u8212- as it says, \u8220"Mine is the silver and Mine the gold, says the Lord of Hosts.\u8221"\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Elazar Restated\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rabbi Shimon ben Elazar\u8217's teaching is then restated: {\i miyami lo raiti tzvi kayatz, ve\u8217'ari sabal, veshual chanvani} \u8212- never have I seen a deer who dried figs for a living, or a lion who was a porter, or a fox who was a storekeeper. {\i Vehem mitparnesim belo tza\u8217'ar} \u8212- and they are provided for without effort. {\i Vehem lo nivreu ela leshamshani, va\u8217'ani nivrati leshamesh et koni} \u8212- they were created only to serve me, and I was created to serve my Creator. Does it not follow that I should be sustained without effort? {\i Ela she\u8217'hareti et ma\u8217'asai vekipachti et parnasati} \u8212- only because I have corrupted my deeds have I forfeited that effortless sustenance \u8212- {\i shenemar avonotechem hitu} \u8212- as it says, \u8220"Your sins have deflected these things.\u8221" One wonders, in closing this tractate with a return to the primal consequences of sin, whether the echo of {\i Gan Eden} is intentional \u8212- the perfect marriage of {\i Gan Eden}, the effortless sustenance of {\i Gan Eden}, both lost through that first transgression, and {\i Masechet Kiddushin} in some sense tracking the long human effort to reconstruct a sanctified life within those fallen conditions.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Nehorai Final\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A final Baraita records Rabbi Nehorai\u8217's teaching once more: {\i maniach ani kol umanut sheba\u8217'olam ve\u8217'einey melamed et bni ela Torah, shekol umanut sheba\u8217'olam eina omedet lo ela bimei yalduto} \u8212- I set aside all the professions of the world and teach my son nothing but {\i Torah}, because every other profession serves him only in his youth. {\i Bimei ziknuto harei hu mutal bara\u8217'av} \u8212- in old age, he will starve if all he has is his trade. {\i Aval Torah einah khen} \u8212- but {\i Torah} is not like that. {\i Omedet lo la\u8217'adam be\u8217'et yalduto venotenet lo acharit vetikva be\u8217'et ziknuto} \u8212- it sustains him in youth and gives him hope in old age. {\i Be\u8217'et yalduto ma hu omer? Vekovei Hashem yachalifu koach ya\u8217'alu ever kanesharim} \u8212- in youth the verse says: \u8220"Those who hope in God will renew their strength, they will soar on wings like eagles.\u8221" {\i Beziknuto ma hu omer? Od yenuvun besivah, dashanim vera\u8217'ananim yihiyu} \u8212- in old age: \u8220"They will still be fruitful in old age, still full of sap and still fresh.\u8221"\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Completion\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Hadran alach asarah yuchasin} \u8212- we have completed the final {\i perek} of Masechet Kiddushin. The formal {\i siyum} will await a proper {\i minyan}, but the learning is done. Tomorrow we begin Bava Kamma. {\i Yasher koach} to all.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
}
